if(typeof BODIES_ORIG!=="undefined") BODIES_ORIG["gb-580"]="1. The Pickwickians 2. The first Day’s Journey, and the first Evening’s Adventures; with their Consequences 3. A new Acquaintance--The Stroller’s Tale--A disagreeable Interruption, and an unpleasant Encounter 4. A Field Day and Bivouac--More new Friends--An Invitation to the Country 5. A short one--Showing, among other Matters, how Mr. Pickwick undertook to drive, and Mr. Winkle to ride, and how they both did it 6. An old-fashioned Card-party--The Clergyman’s verses--The Story of the Convict’s Return 7. How Mr. Winkle, instead of shooting at the Pigeon and killing the Crow, shot at the Crow and wounded the Pigeon; how the Dingley Dell Cricket Club played All-Muggleton, and how All- Muggleton dined at the Dingley Dell Expense; with other interesting and instructive Matters 8. Strongly illustrative of the Position, that the Course of True Love is not a Railway 9. A Discovery and a Chase 10. Clearing up all Doubts (if any existed) of the Disinterestedness of Mr. A. Jingle’s Character 11. Involving another Journey, and an Antiquarian Discovery; Recording Mr. Pickwick’s Determination to be present at an Election; and containing a Manuscript of the old Clergyman’s 12. Descriptive of a very important Proceeding on the Part of Mr. Pickwick; no less an Epoch in his Life, than in this History 13. Some Account of Eatanswill; of the State of Parties therein; and of the Election of a Member to serve in Parliament for that ancient, loyal, and patriotic Borough 14. Comprising a brief Description of the Company at the Peacock assembled; and a Tale told by a Bagman 15. In which is given a faithful Portraiture of two distinguished Persons; and an accurate Description of a public Breakfast in their House and Grounds: which public Breakfast leads to the Recognition of an old Acquaintance, and the Commencement of anothe r Chapter 16. Too full of Adventure to be briefly described 17. Showing that an Attack of Rheumatism, in some Cases, acts as a Quickener to inventive Genius 18. Briefly illustrative of two Points; first, the Power of Hysterics, and, secondly, the Force of Circumstances 19. A pleasant Day with an unpleasant Termination 20. Showing how Dodson and Fogg were Men of Business, and their Clerks Men of pleasure; and how an affecting Interview took place between Mr. Weller and his long-lost Parent; showing also what Choice Spirits assembled at the Magpie and Stump, and what a C apital Chapter the next one will be 21. In which the old Man launches forth into his favourite Theme, and relates a Story about a queer Client 22. Mr. Pickwick journeys to Ipswich and meets with a romantic Adventure with a middle-aged Lady in yellow Curl-papers 23. In which Mr. Samuel Weller begins to devote his Energies to the Return Match between himself and Mr. Trotter 24. Wherein Mr. Peter Magnus grows jealous, and the middle-aged Lady apprehensive, which brings the Pickwickians within the Grasp of the Law 25. Showing, among a Variety of pleasant Matters, how majestic and impartial Mr. Nupkins was; and how Mr. Weller returned Mr. Job Trotter’s Shuttlecock as heavily as it came- -With another Matter, which will be found in its Place 26. Which contains a brief Account of the Progress of the Action of Bardell against Pickwick 27. Samuel Weller makes a Pilgrimage to Dorking, and beholds his Mother-in-law 28. A good-humoured Christmas Chapter, containing an Account of a Wedding, and some other Sports beside: which although in their Way even as good Customs as Marriage itself, are not quite so religiously kept up, in these degenerate Times 29. The Story of the Goblins who stole a Sexton 30. How the Pickwickians made and cultivated the Acquaintance of a Couple of nice young Men belonging to one of the liberal Professions; how they disported themselves on the Ice; and how their Visit came to a Conclusion 31. Which is all about the Law, and sundry Great Authorities learned therein 32. Describes, far more fully than the Court Newsman ever did, a Bachelor’s Party, given by Mr. Bob Sawyer at his Lodgings in the Borough 33. Mr. Weller the elder delivers some Critical Sentiments respecting Literary Composition; and, assisted by his Son Samuel, pays a small Instalment of Retaliation to the Account of the Reverend Gentleman with the Red Nose 34. Is wholly devoted to a full and faithful Report of the memorable Trial of Bardell against Pickwick 35. In which Mr. Pickwick thinks he had better go to Bath; and goes accordingly 36. The chief Features of which will be found to be an authentic Version of the Legend of Prince Bladud, and a most extraordinary Calamity that befell Mr. Winkle 37. Honourably accounts for Mr. Weller’s Absence, by describing a Soiree to which he was invited and went; also relates how he was intrusted by Mr. Pickwick with a Private Mission of Delicacy and Importance 38. How Mr. Winkle, when he stepped out of the Frying-pan, walked gently and comfortably into the Fire 39. Mr. Samuel Weller, being intrusted with a Mission of Love, proceeds to execute it; with what Success will hereinafter appear 40. Introduces Mr. Pickwick to a new and not uninteresting Scene in the great Drama of Life 41. What befell Mr. Pickwick when he got into the Fleet; what Prisoners he saw there; and how he passed the Night 42. Illustrative, like the preceding one, of the old Proverb, that Adversity brings a Man acquainted with strange Bedfellows--Likewise containing Mr. Pickwick’s extraordinary and startling Announcement to Mr. Samuel Weller 43. Showing how Mr. Samuel Weller got into Difficulties 44. Treats of divers little Matters which occurred in the Fleet, and of Mr. Winkle’s mysterious Behaviour; and shows how the poor Chancery Prisoner obtained his Release at last 45. Descriptive of an affecting Interview between Mr. Samuel Weller and a Family Party. Mr. Pickwick makes a Tour of the diminutive World he inhabits, and resolves to mix with it, in Future, as little as possible 46. Records a touching Act of delicate Feeling not unmixed with Pleasantry, achieved and performed by Messrs. Dodson and Fogg 47. Is chiefly devoted to Matters of Business, and the temporal Advantage of Dodson and Fogg--Mr. Winkle reappears under extraordinary Circumstances--Mr. Pickwick’s Benevolence proves stronger than his Obstinacy 48. Relates how Mr. Pickwick, with the Assistance of Samuel Weller, essayed to soften the Heart of Mr. Benjamin Allen, and to mollify the Wrath of Mr. Robert Sawyer 49. Containing the Story of the Bagman’s Uncle 50. How Mr. Pickwick sped upon his Mission, and how he was reinforced in the Outset by a most unexpected Auxiliary 51. In which Mr. Pickwick encounters an old Acquaintance--To which fortunate Circumstance the Reader is mainly indebted for Matter of thrilling Interest herein set down, concerning two great Public Men of Might and Power 52. Involving a serious Change in the Weller Family, and the untimely Downfall of Mr. Stiggins 53. Comprising the final Exit of Mr. Jingle and Job Trotter, with a great Morning of business in Gray’s Inn Square--Concluding with a Double Knock at Mr. Perker’s Door 54. Containing some Particulars relative to the Double Knock, and other Matters: among which certain interesting Disclosures relative to Mr. Snodgrass and a Young Lady are by no Means irrelevant to this History 55. Mr. Solomon Pell, assisted by a Select Committee of Coachmen, arranges the affairs of the elder Mr. Weller 56. An important Conference takes place between Mr. Pickwick and Samuel Weller, at which his Parent assists--An old Gentleman in a snuff-coloured Suit arrives unexpectedly 57. In which the Pickwick Club is finally dissolved, and everything concluded to the Satisfaction of Everybody\n\n# CHAPTER I. THE PICKWICKIANS\n\nThe first ray of light which illumines the gloom, and converts into a dazzling brilliancy that obscurity in which the earlier history of the public career of the immortal Pickwick would appear to be involved, is derived from the perusal of the following entry in the Transactions of the Pickwick Club, which the editor of these papers feels the highest pleasure in laying before his readers, as a proof of the careful attention, indefatigable assiduity, and nice discrimination, with which his search among the multifarious documents confided to him has been conducted.\n\n‘May 12, 1827. Joseph Smiggers, Esq., P.V.P.M.P.C. [Perpetual Vice- President--Member Pickwick Club], presiding. The following resolutions unanimously agreed to:--\n\n‘That this Association has heard read, with feelings of unmingled satisfaction, and unqualified approval, the paper communicated by Samuel Pickwick, Esq., G.C.M.P.C. [General Chairman--Member Pickwick Club], entitled “Speculations on the Source of the Hampstead Ponds, with some Observations on the Theory of Tittlebats;” and that this Association does hereby return its warmest thanks to the said Samuel Pickwick, Esq., G.C.M.P.C., for the same.\n\n‘That while this Association is deeply sensible of the advantages which must accrue to the cause of science, from the production to which they have just adverted--no less than from the unwearied researches of Samuel Pickwick, Esq., G.C.M.P.C., in Hornsey, Highgate, Brixton, and Camberwell--they cannot but entertain a lively sense of the inestimable benefits which must inevitably result from carrying the speculations of that learned man into a wider field, from extending his travels, and, consequently, enlarging his sphere of observation, to the advancement of knowledge, and the diffusion of learning.\n\n‘That, with the view just mentioned, this Association has taken into its serious consideration a proposal, emanating from the aforesaid, Samuel Pickwick, Esq., G.C.M.P.C., and three other Pickwickians hereinafter named, for forming a new branch of United Pickwickians, under the title of The Corresponding Society of the Pickwick Club.\n\n‘That the said proposal has received the sanction and approval of this Association.\n\n‘That the Corresponding Society of the Pickwick Club is therefore hereby constituted; and that Samuel Pickwick, Esq., G.C.M.P.C., Tracy Tupman, Esq., M.P.C., Augustus Snodgrass, Esq., M.P.C., and Nathaniel Winkle, Esq., M.P.C., are hereby nominated and appointed members of the same; and that they be requested to forward, from time to time, authenticated accounts of their journeys and investigations, of their observations of character and manners, and of the whole of their adventures, together with all tales and papers to which local scenery or associations may give rise, to the Pickwick Club, stationed in London.\n\n‘That this Association cordially recognises the principle of every member of the Corresponding Society defraying his own travelling expenses; and that it sees no objection whatever to the members of the said society pursuing their inquiries for any length of time they please, upon the same terms.\n\n‘That the members of the aforesaid Corresponding Society be, and are hereby informed, that their proposal to pay the postage of their letters, and the carriage of their parcels, has been deliberated upon by this Association: that this Association considers such proposal worthy of the great minds from which it emanated, and that it hereby signifies its perfect acquiescence therein.’\n\nA casual observer, adds the secretary, to whose notes we are indebted for the following account--a casual observer might possibly have remarked nothing extraordinary in the bald head, and circular spectacles, which were intently turned towards his (the secretary’s) face, during the reading of the above resolutions: to those who knew that the gigantic brain of Pickwick was working beneath that forehead, and that the beaming eyes of Pickwick were twinkling behind those glasses, the sight was indeed an interesting one. There sat the man who had traced to their source the mighty ponds of Hampstead, and agitated the scientific world with his Theory of Tittlebats, as calm and unmoved as the deep waters of the one on a frosty day, or as a solitary specimen of the other in the inmost recesses of an earthen jar. And how much more interesting did the spectacle become, when, starting into full life and animation, as a simultaneous call for ‘Pickwick’ burst from his followers, that illustrious man slowly mounted into the Windsor chair, on which he had been previously seated, and addressed the club himself had founded. What a study for an artist did that exciting scene present! The eloquent Pickwick, with one hand gracefully concealed behind his coat tails, and the other waving in air to assist his glowing declamation; his elevated position revealing those tights and gaiters, which, had they clothed an ordinary man, might have passed without observation, but which, when Pickwick clothed them--if we may use the expression--inspired involuntary awe and respect; surrounded by the men who had volunteered to share the perils of his travels, and who were destined to participate in the glories of his discoveries. On his right sat Mr. Tracy Tupman--the too susceptible Tupman, who to the wisdom and experience of maturer years superadded the enthusiasm and ardour of a boy in the most interesting and pardonable of human weaknesses--love. Time and feeding had expanded that once romantic form; the black silk waistcoat had become more and more developed; inch by inch had the gold watch-chain beneath it disappeared from within the range of Tupman’s vision; and gradually had the capacious chin encroached upon the borders of the white cravat: but the soul of Tupman had known no change-- admiration of the fair sex was still its ruling passion. On the left of his great leader sat the poetic Snodgrass, and near him again the sporting Winkle; the former poetically enveloped in a mysterious blue cloak with a canine-skin collar, and the latter communicating additional lustre to a new green shooting-coat, plaid neckerchief, and closely- fitted drabs.\n\nMr. Pickwick’s oration upon this occasion, together with the debate thereon, is entered on the Transactions of the Club. Both bear a strong affinity to the discussions of other celebrated bodies; and, as it is always interesting to trace a resemblance between the proceedings of great men, we transfer the entry to these pages.\n\n‘Mr. Pickwick observed (says the secretary) that fame was dear to the heart of every man. Poetic fame was dear to the heart of his friend Snodgrass; the fame of conquest was equally dear to his friend Tupman; and the desire of earning fame in the sports of the field, the air, and the water was uppermost in the breast of his friend Winkle. He (Mr. Pickwick) would not deny that he was influenced by human passions and human feelings (cheers)--possibly by human weaknesses (loud cries of “No”); but this he would say, that if ever the fire of self-importance broke out in his bosom, the desire to benefit the human race in preference effectually quenched it. The praise of mankind was his swing; philanthropy was his insurance office. (Vehement cheering.) He had felt some pride--he acknowledged it freely, and let his enemies make the most of it--he had felt some pride when he presented his Tittlebatian Theory to the world; it might be celebrated or it might not. (A cry of “It is,” and great cheering.) He would take the assertion of that honourable Pickwickian whose voice he had just heard--it was celebrated; but if the fame of that treatise were to extend to the farthest confines of the known world, the pride with which he should reflect on the authorship of that production would be as nothing compared with the pride with which he looked around him, on this, the proudest moment of his existence. (Cheers.) He was a humble individual. (“No, no.”) Still he could not but feel that they had selected him for a service of great honour, and of some danger. Travelling was in a troubled state, and the minds of coachmen were unsettled. Let them look abroad and contemplate the scenes which were enacting around them. Stage-coaches were upsetting in all directions, horses were bolting, boats were overturning, and boilers were bursting. (Cheers--a voice “No.”) No! (Cheers.) Let that honourable Pickwickian who cried “No” so loudly come forward and deny it, if he could. (Cheers.) Who was it that cried “No”? (Enthusiastic cheering.) Was it some vain and disappointed man--he would not say haberdasher (loud cheers)--who, jealous of the praise which had been--perhaps undeservedly--bestowed on his (Mr. Pickwick’s) researches, and smarting under the censure which had been heaped upon his own feeble attempts at rivalry, now took this vile and calumnious mode of--\n\n‘MR. BLOTTON (of Aldgate) rose to order. Did the honourable Pickwickian allude to him? (Cries of “Order,” “Chair,” “Yes,” “No,” “Go on,” “Leave off,” etc.)\n\n‘MR. PICKWICK would not put up to be put down by clamour. He had alluded to the honourable gentleman. (Great excitement.)\n\n‘MR. BLOTTON would only say then, that he repelled the hon. gent.’s false and scurrilous accusation, with profound contempt. (Great cheering.) The hon. gent. was a humbug. (Immense confusion, and loud cries of “Chair,” and “Order.”)\n\n‘Mr. A. SNODGRASS rose to order. He threw himself upon the chair. (Hear.) He wished to know whether this disgraceful contest between two members of that club should be allowed to continue. (Hear, hear.)\n\n‘The CHAIRMAN was quite sure the hon. Pickwickian would withdraw the expression he had just made use of.\n\n‘MR. BLOTTON, with all possible respect for the chair, was quite sure he would not.\n\n‘The CHAIRMAN felt it his imperative duty to demand of the honourable gentleman, whether he had used the expression which had just escaped him in a common sense.\n\n‘MR. BLOTTON had no hesitation in saying that he had not--he had used the word in its Pickwickian sense. (Hear, hear.) He was bound to acknowledge that, personally, he entertained the highest regard and esteem for the honourable gentleman; he had merely considered him a humbug in a Pickwickian point of view. (Hear, hear.)\n\n‘MR. PICKWICK felt much gratified by the fair, candid, and full explanation of his honourable friend. He begged it to be at once understood, that his own observations had been merely intended to bear a Pickwickian construction. (Cheers.)’\n\nHere the entry terminates, as we have no doubt the debate did also, after arriving at such a highly satisfactory and intelligible point. We have no official statement of the facts which the reader will find recorded in the next chapter, but they have been carefully collated from letters and other MS. authorities, so unquestionably genuine as to justify their narration in a connected form.\n\nCHAPTER II. THE FIRST DAY’S JOURNEY, AND THE FIRST EVENING’S ADVENTURES; WITH THEIR CONSEQUENCES\nThat punctual servant of all work, the sun, had just risen, and begun to strike a light on the morning of the thirteenth of May, one thousand eight hundred and twenty-seven, when Mr. Samuel Pickwick burst like another sun from his slumbers, threw open his chamber window, and looked out upon the world beneath. Goswell Street was at his feet, Goswell Street was on his right hand--as far as the eye could reach, Goswell Street extended on his left; and the opposite side of Goswell Street was over the way. ‘Such,’ thought Mr. Pickwick, ‘are the narrow views of those philosophers who, content with examining the things that lie before them, look not to the truths which are hidden beyond. As well might I be content to gaze on Goswell Street for ever, without one effort to penetrate to the hidden countries which on every side surround it.’ And having given vent to this beautiful reflection, Mr. Pickwick proceeded to put himself into his clothes, and his clothes into his portmanteau. Great men are seldom over scrupulous in the arrangement of their attire; the operation of shaving, dressing, and coffee-imbibing was soon performed; and, in another hour, Mr. Pickwick, with his portmanteau in his hand, his telescope in his greatcoat pocket, and his note-book in his waistcoat, ready for the reception of any discoveries worthy of being noted down, had arrived at the coach-stand in St. Martin’s-le-Grand.\n\n‘Cab!’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Here you are, sir,’ shouted a strange specimen of the human race, in a sackcloth coat, and apron of the same, who, with a brass label and number round his neck, looked as if he were catalogued in some collection of rarities. This was the waterman. ‘Here you are, sir. Now, then, fust cab!’ And the first cab having been fetched from the public- house, where he had been smoking his first pipe, Mr. Pickwick and his portmanteau were thrown into the vehicle.\n\n‘Golden Cross,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Only a bob’s vorth, Tommy,’ cried the driver sulkily, for the information of his friend the waterman, as the cab drove off.\n\n‘How old is that horse, my friend?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick, rubbing his nose with the shilling he had reserved for the fare.\n\n‘Forty-two,’ replied the driver, eyeing him askant.\n\n‘What!’ ejaculated Mr. Pickwick, laying his hand upon his note-book. The driver reiterated his former statement. Mr. Pickwick looked very hard at the man’s face, but his features were immovable, so he noted down the fact forthwith.\n\n‘And how long do you keep him out at a time?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick, searching for further information.\n\n‘Two or three veeks,’ replied the man.\n\n‘Weeks!’ said Mr. Pickwick in astonishment, and out came the note-book again.\n\n‘He lives at Pentonwil when he’s at home,’ observed the driver coolly, ‘but we seldom takes him home, on account of his weakness.’\n\n‘On account of his weakness!’ reiterated the perplexed Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘He always falls down when he’s took out o’ the cab,’ continued the driver, ‘but when he’s in it, we bears him up werry tight, and takes him in werry short, so as he can’t werry well fall down; and we’ve got a pair o’ precious large wheels on, so ven he does move, they run after him, and he must go on--he can’t help it.’\n\nMr. Pickwick entered every word of this statement in his note-book, with the view of communicating it to the club, as a singular instance of the tenacity of life in horses under trying circumstances. The entry was scarcely completed when they reached the Golden Cross. Down jumped the driver, and out got Mr. Pickwick. Mr. Tupman, Mr. Snodgrass, and Mr. Winkle, who had been anxiously waiting the arrival of their illustrious leader, crowded to welcome him.\n\n‘Here’s your fare,’ said Mr. Pickwick, holding out the shilling to the driver.\n\nWhat was the learned man’s astonishment, when that unaccountable person flung the money on the pavement, and requested in figurative terms to be allowed the pleasure of fighting him (Mr. Pickwick) for the amount!\n\n‘You are mad,’ said Mr. Snodgrass.\n\n‘Or drunk,’ said Mr. Winkle.\n\n‘Or both,’ said Mr. Tupman.\n\n‘Come on!’ said the cab-driver, sparring away like clockwork. ‘Come on-- all four on you.’\n\n‘Here’s a lark!’ shouted half a dozen hackney coachmen. ‘Go to vork, Sam!--and they crowded with great glee round the party.\n\n‘What’s the row, Sam?’ inquired one gentleman in black calico sleeves.\n\n‘Row!’ replied the cabman, ‘what did he want my number for?’\n\n‘I didn’t want your number,’ said the astonished Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘What did you take it for, then?’ inquired the cabman.\n\n‘I didn’t take it,’ said Mr. Pickwick indignantly.\n\n‘Would anybody believe,’ continued the cab-driver, appealing to the crowd, ‘would anybody believe as an informer’ud go about in a man’s cab, not only takin’ down his number, but ev’ry word he says into the bargain’ (a light flashed upon Mr. Pickwick--it was the note-book).\n\n‘Did he though?’ inquired another cabman.\n\n‘Yes, did he,’ replied the first; ‘and then arter aggerawatin’ me to assault him, gets three witnesses here to prove it. But I’ll give it him, if I’ve six months for it. Come on!’ and the cabman dashed his hat upon the ground, with a reckless disregard of his own private property, and knocked Mr. Pickwick’s spectacles off, and followed up the attack with a blow on Mr. Pickwick’s nose, and another on Mr. Pickwick’s chest, and a third in Mr. Snodgrass’s eye, and a fourth, by way of variety, in Mr. Tupman’s waistcoat, and then danced into the road, and then back again to the pavement, and finally dashed the whole temporary supply of breath out of Mr. Winkle’s body; and all in half a dozen seconds.\n\n‘Where’s an officer?’ said Mr. Snodgrass.\n\n‘Put ‘em under the pump,’ suggested a hot-pieman.\n\n‘You shall smart for this,’ gasped Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Informers!’ shouted the crowd.\n\n‘Come on,’ cried the cabman, who had been sparring without cessation the whole time.\n\nThe mob hitherto had been passive spectators of the scene, but as the intelligence of the Pickwickians being informers was spread among them, they began to canvass with considerable vivacity the propriety of enforcing the heated pastry-vendor’s proposition: and there is no saying what acts of personal aggression they might have committed, had not the affray been unexpectedly terminated by the interposition of a new-comer.\n\n‘What’s the fun?’ said a rather tall, thin, young man, in a green coat, emerging suddenly from the coach-yard.\n\n‘Informers!’ shouted the crowd again.\n\n‘We are not,’ roared Mr. Pickwick, in a tone which, to any dispassionate listener, carried conviction with it.\n\n‘Ain’t you, though--ain’t you?’ said the young man, appealing to Mr. Pickwick, and making his way through the crowd by the infallible process of elbowing the countenances of its component members.\n\nThat learned man in a few hurried words explained the real state of the case.\n\n‘Come along, then,’ said he of the green coat, lugging Mr. Pickwick after him by main force, and talking the whole way. Here, No. 924, take your fare, and take yourself off--respectable gentleman--know him well-- none of your nonsense--this way, sir--where’s your friends?--all a mistake, I see--never mind--accidents will happen--best regulated families--never say die--down upon your luck--Pull him up--Put that in his pipe--like the flavour--damned rascals.’ And with a lengthened string of similar broken sentences, delivered with extraordinary volubility, the stranger led the way to the traveller’s waiting-room, whither he was closely followed by Mr. Pickwick and his disciples.\n\n‘Here, waiter!’ shouted the stranger, ringing the bell with tremendous violence, ‘glasses round--brandy-and-water, hot and strong, and sweet, and plenty,--eye damaged, Sir? Waiter! raw beef-steak for the gentleman’s eye--nothing like raw beef-steak for a bruise, sir; cold lamp-post very good, but lamp-post inconvenient--damned odd standing in the open street half an hour, with your eye against a lamp-post--eh,-- very good--ha! ha!’ And the stranger, without stopping to take breath, swallowed at a draught full half a pint of the reeking brandy-and-water, and flung himself into a chair with as much ease as if nothing uncommon had occurred.\n\nWhile his three companions were busily engaged in proffering their thanks to their new acquaintance, Mr. Pickwick had leisure to examine his costume and appearance.\n\nHe was about the middle height, but the thinness of his body, and the length of his legs, gave him the appearance of being much taller. The green coat had been a smart dress garment in the days of swallow-tails, but had evidently in those times adorned a much shorter man than the stranger, for the soiled and faded sleeves scarcely reached to his wrists. It was buttoned closely up to his chin, at the imminent hazard of splitting the back; and an old stock, without a vestige of shirt collar, ornamented his neck. His scanty black trousers displayed here and there those shiny patches which bespeak long service, and were strapped very tightly over a pair of patched and mended shoes, as if to conceal the dirty white stockings, which were nevertheless distinctly visible. His long, black hair escaped in negligent waves from beneath each side of his old pinched-up hat; and glimpses of his bare wrists might be observed between the tops of his gloves and the cuffs of his coat sleeves. His face was thin and haggard; but an indescribable air of jaunty impudence and perfect self-possession pervaded the whole man.\n\nSuch was the individual on whom Mr. Pickwick gazed through his spectacles (which he had fortunately recovered), and to whom he proceeded, when his friends had exhausted themselves, to return in chosen terms his warmest thanks for his recent assistance.\n\n‘Never mind,’ said the stranger, cutting the address very short, ‘said enough--no more; smart chap that cabman--handled his fives well; but if I’d been your friend in the green jemmy--damn me--punch his head,--‘cod I would,--pig’s whisper--pieman too,--no gammon.’\n\nThis coherent speech was interrupted by the entrance of the Rochester coachman, to announce that ‘the Commodore’ was on the point of starting.\n\n‘Commodore!’ said the stranger, starting up, ‘my coach--place booked,-- one outside--leave you to pay for the brandy-and-water,--want change for a five,--bad silver--Brummagem buttons--won’t do--no go--eh?’ and he shook his head most knowingly.\n\nNow it so happened that Mr. Pickwick and his three companions had resolved to make Rochester their first halting-place too; and having intimated to their new-found acquaintance that they were journeying to the same city, they agreed to occupy the seat at the back of the coach, where they could all sit together.\n\n‘Up with you,’ said the stranger, assisting Mr. Pickwick on to the roof with so much precipitation as to impair the gravity of that gentleman’s deportment very materially.\n\n‘Any luggage, Sir?’ inquired the coachman.\n\n‘Who--I? Brown paper parcel here, that’s all--other luggage gone by water--packing-cases, nailed up--big as houses--heavy, heavy, damned heavy,’ replied the stranger, as he forced into his pocket as much as he could of the brown paper parcel, which presented most suspicious indications of containing one shirt and a handkerchief.\n\n‘Heads, heads--take care of your heads!’ cried the loquacious stranger, as they came out under the low archway, which in those days formed the entrance to the coach-yard. ‘Terrible place--dangerous work--other day-- five children--mother--tall lady, eating sandwiches--forgot the arch-- crash--knock--children look round--mother’s head off--sandwich in her hand--no mouth to put it in--head of a family off--shocking, shocking! Looking at Whitehall, sir?--fine place--little window--somebody else’s head off there, eh, sir?--he didn’t keep a sharp look-out enough either- -eh, Sir, eh?’\n\n‘I am ruminating,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘on the strange mutability of human affairs.’\n\n‘Ah! I see--in at the palace door one day, out at the window the next. Philosopher, Sir?’\n\n‘An observer of human nature, Sir,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Ah, so am I. Most people are when they’ve little to do and less to get. Poet, Sir?’\n\n‘My friend Mr. Snodgrass has a strong poetic turn,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘So have I,’ said the stranger. ‘Epic poem--ten thousand lines-- revolution of July--composed it on the spot--Mars by day, Apollo by night--bang the field-piece, twang the lyre.’\n\n‘You were present at that glorious scene, sir?’ said Mr. Snodgrass.\n\n‘Present! think I was;* fired a musket--fired with an idea--rushed into wine shop--wrote it down--back again--whiz, bang--another idea--wine shop again--pen and ink--back again--cut and slash--noble time, Sir. Sportsman, sir?’ abruptly turning to Mr. Winkle.\n* A remarkable instance of the prophetic force of Mr. Jingle’s imagination; this dialogue occurring in the year 1827, and the Revolution in 1830.\n\n‘A little, Sir,’ replied that gentleman.\n\n‘Fine pursuit, sir--fine pursuit.--Dogs, Sir?’\n\n‘Not just now,’ said Mr. Winkle.\n\n‘Ah! you should keep dogs--fine animals--sagacious creatures--dog of my own once--pointer--surprising instinct--out shooting one day--entering inclosure--whistled--dog stopped--whistled again--Ponto--no go; stock still--called him--Ponto, Ponto--wouldn’t move--dog transfixed--staring at a board--looked up, saw an inscription--“Gamekeeper has orders to shoot all dogs found in this inclosure”--wouldn’t pass it--wonderful dog--valuable dog that--very.’\n\n‘Singular circumstance that,’ said Mr. Pickwick. ‘Will you allow me to make a note of it?’\n\n‘Certainly, Sir, certainly--hundred more anecdotes of the same animal.-- Fine girl, Sir’ (to Mr. Tracy Tupman, who had been bestowing sundry anti-Pickwickian glances on a young lady by the roadside).\n\n‘Very!’ said Mr. Tupman.\n\n‘English girls not so fine as Spanish--noble creatures--jet hair--black eyes--lovely forms--sweet creatures--beautiful.’\n\n‘You have been in Spain, sir?’ said Mr. Tracy Tupman.\n\n‘Lived there--ages.’\n\n‘Many conquests, sir?’ inquired Mr. Tupman.\n\n‘Conquests! Thousands. Don Bolaro Fizzgig--grandee--only daughter--Donna Christina--splendid creature--loved me to distraction--jealous father-- high-souled daughter--handsome Englishman--Donna Christina in despair-- prussic acid--stomach pump in my portmanteau--operation performed--old Bolaro in ecstasies--consent to our union--join hands and floods of tears--romantic story--very.’\n\n‘Is the lady in England now, sir?’ inquired Mr. Tupman, on whom the description of her charms had produced a powerful impression.\n\n‘Dead, sir--dead,’ said the stranger, applying to his right eye the brief remnant of a very old cambric handkerchief. ‘Never recovered the stomach pump--undermined constitution--fell a victim.’\n\n‘And her father?’ inquired the poetic Snodgrass.\n\n‘Remorse and misery,’ replied the stranger. ‘Sudden disappearance--talk of the whole city--search made everywhere without success--public fountain in the great square suddenly ceased playing--weeks elapsed-- still a stoppage--workmen employed to clean it--water drawn off--father- in-law discovered sticking head first in the main pipe, with a full confession in his right boot--took him out, and the fountain played away again, as well as ever.’\n\n‘Will you allow me to note that little romance down, Sir?’ said Mr. Snodgrass, deeply affected.\n\n‘Certainly, Sir, certainly--fifty more if you like to hear ‘em--strange life mine--rather curious history--not extraordinary, but singular.’\n\nIn this strain, with an occasional glass of ale, by way of parenthesis, when the coach changed horses, did the stranger proceed, until they reached Rochester bridge, by which time the note-books, both of Mr. Pickwick and Mr. Snodgrass, were completely filled with selections from his adventures.\n\n‘Magnificent ruin!’ said Mr. Augustus Snodgrass, with all the poetic fervour that distinguished him, when they came in sight of the fine old castle.\n\n‘What a study for an antiquarian!’ were the very words which fell from Mr. Pickwick’s mouth, as he applied his telescope to his eye.\n\n‘Ah! fine place,’ said the stranger, ‘glorious pile--frowning walls-- tottering arches--dark nooks--crumbling staircases--old cathedral too-- earthy smell--pilgrims’ feet wore away the old steps--little Saxon doors--confessionals like money-takers’ boxes at theatres--queer customers those monks--popes, and lord treasurers, and all sorts of old fellows, with great red faces, and broken noses, turning up every day-- buff jerkins too--match-locks--sarcophagus--fine place--old legends too- -strange stories: capital;’ and the stranger continued to soliloquise until they reached the Bull Inn, in the High Street, where the coach stopped.\n\n‘Do you remain here, Sir?’ inquired Mr. Nathaniel Winkle.\n\n‘Here--not I--but you’d better--good house--nice beds--Wright’s next house, dear--very dear--half-a-crown in the bill if you look at the waiter--charge you more if you dine at a friend’s than they would if you dined in the coffee-room--rum fellows--very.’\n\nMr. Winkle turned to Mr. Pickwick, and murmured a few words; a whisper passed from Mr. Pickwick to Mr. Snodgrass, from Mr. Snodgrass to Mr. Tupman, and nods of assent were exchanged. Mr. Pickwick addressed the stranger.\n\n‘You rendered us a very important service this morning, sir,’ said he, ‘will you allow us to offer a slight mark of our gratitude by begging the favour of your company at dinner?’\n\n‘Great pleasure--not presume to dictate, but broiled fowl and mushrooms- -capital thing! What time?’\n\n‘Let me see,’ replied Mr. Pickwick, referring to his watch, ‘it is now nearly three. Shall we say five?’\n\n‘Suit me excellently,’ said the stranger, ‘five precisely--till then-- care of yourselves;’ and lifting the pinched-up hat a few inches from his head, and carelessly replacing it very much on one side, the stranger, with half the brown paper parcel sticking out of his pocket, walked briskly up the yard, and turned into the High Street.\n\n‘Evidently a traveller in many countries, and a close observer of men and things,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘I should like to see his poem,’ said Mr. Snodgrass.\n\n‘I should like to have seen that dog,’ said Mr. Winkle.\n\nMr. Tupman said nothing; but he thought of Donna Christina, the stomach pump, and the fountain; and his eyes filled with tears.\n\nA private sitting-room having been engaged, bedrooms inspected, and dinner ordered, the party walked out to view the city and adjoining neighbourhood.\n\nWe do not find, from a careful perusal of Mr. Pickwick’s notes of the four towns, Stroud, Rochester, Chatham, and Brompton, that his impressions of their appearance differ in any material point from those of other travellers who have gone over the same ground. His general description is easily abridged.\n\n‘The principal productions of these towns,’ says Mr. Pickwick, ‘appear to be soldiers, sailors, Jews, chalk, shrimps, officers, and dockyard men. The commodities chiefly exposed for sale in the public streets are marine stores, hard-bake, apples, flat-fish, and oysters. The streets present a lively and animated appearance, occasioned chiefly by the conviviality of the military. It is truly delightful to a philanthropic mind to see these gallant men staggering along under the influence of an overflow both of animal and ardent spirits; more especially when we remember that the following them about, and jesting with them, affords a cheap and innocent amusement for the boy population. Nothing,’ adds Mr. Pickwick, ‘can exceed their good-humour. It was but the day before my arrival that one of them had been most grossly insulted in the house of a publican. The barmaid had positively refused to draw him any more liquor; in return for which he had (merely in playfulness) drawn his bayonet, and wounded the girl in the shoulder. And yet this fine fellow was the very first to go down to the house next morning and express his readiness to overlook the matter, and forget what had occurred!\n\n‘The consumption of tobacco in these towns,’ continues Mr. Pickwick, ‘must be very great, and the smell which pervades the streets must be exceedingly delicious to those who are extremely fond of smoking. A superficial traveller might object to the dirt, which is their leading characteristic; but to those who view it as an indication of traffic and commercial prosperity, it is truly gratifying.’\n\nPunctual to five o’clock came the stranger, and shortly afterwards the dinner. He had divested himself of his brown paper parcel, but had made no alteration in his attire, and was, if possible, more loquacious than ever.\n\n‘What’s that?’ he inquired, as the waiter removed one of the covers.\n\n‘Soles, Sir.’\n\n‘Soles--ah!--capital fish--all come from London-stage-coach proprietors get up political dinners--carriage of soles--dozens of baskets--cunning fellows. Glass of wine, Sir.’\n\n‘With pleasure,’ said Mr. Pickwick; and the stranger took wine, first with him, and then with Mr. Snodgrass, and then with Mr. Tupman, and then with Mr. Winkle, and then with the whole party together, almost as rapidly as he talked.\n\n‘Devil of a mess on the staircase, waiter,’ said the stranger. ‘Forms going up--carpenters coming down--lamps, glasses, harps. What’s going forward?’\n\n‘Ball, Sir,’ said the waiter.\n\n‘Assembly, eh?’\n\n‘No, Sir, not assembly, Sir. Ball for the benefit of a charity, Sir.’\n\n‘Many fine women in this town, do you know, Sir?’ inquired Mr. Tupman, with great interest.\n\n‘Splendid--capital. Kent, sir--everybody knows Kent--apples, cherries, hops, and women. Glass of wine, Sir!’\n\n‘With great pleasure,’ replied Mr. Tupman. The stranger filled, and emptied.\n\n‘I should very much like to go,’ said Mr. Tupman, resuming the subject of the ball, ‘very much.’\n\n‘Tickets at the bar, Sir,’ interposed the waiter; ‘half-a-guinea each, Sir.’\n\nMr. Tupman again expressed an earnest wish to be present at the festivity; but meeting with no response in the darkened eye of Mr. Snodgrass, or the abstracted gaze of Mr. Pickwick, he applied himself with great interest to the port wine and dessert, which had just been placed on the table. The waiter withdrew, and the party were left to enjoy the cosy couple of hours succeeding dinner.\n\n‘Beg your pardon, sir,’ said the stranger, ‘bottle stands--pass it round--way of the sun--through the button-hole--no heeltaps,’ and he emptied his glass, which he had filled about two minutes before, and poured out another, with the air of a man who was used to it.\n\nThe wine was passed, and a fresh supply ordered. The visitor talked, the Pickwickians listened. Mr. Tupman felt every moment more disposed for the ball. Mr. Pickwick’s countenance glowed with an expression of universal philanthropy, and Mr. Winkle and Mr. Snodgrass fell fast asleep.\n\n‘They’re beginning upstairs,’ said the stranger--‘hear the company-- fiddles tuning--now the harp--there they go.’ The various sounds which found their way downstairs announced the commencement of the first quadrille.\n\n‘How I should like to go,’ said Mr. Tupman again.\n\n‘So should I,’ said the stranger--‘confounded luggage,--heavy smacks-- nothing to go in--odd, ain’t it?’\n\nNow general benevolence was one of the leading features of the Pickwickian theory, and no one was more remarkable for the zealous manner in which he observed so noble a principle than Mr. Tracy Tupman. The number of instances recorded on the Transactions of the Society, in which that excellent man referred objects of charity to the houses of other members for left-off garments or pecuniary relief is almost incredible.\n\n‘I should be very happy to lend you a change of apparel for the purpose,’ said Mr. Tracy Tupman, ‘but you are rather slim, and I am--’\n\n‘Rather fat--grown-up Bacchus--cut the leaves--dismounted from the tub, and adopted kersey, eh?--not double distilled, but double milled--ha! ha! pass the wine.’\n\nWhether Mr. Tupman was somewhat indignant at the peremptory tone in which he was desired to pass the wine which the stranger passed so quickly away, or whether he felt very properly scandalised at an influential member of the Pickwick Club being ignominiously compared to a dismounted Bacchus, is a fact not yet completely ascertained. He passed the wine, coughed twice, and looked at the stranger for several seconds with a stern intensity; as that individual, however, appeared perfectly collected, and quite calm under his searching glance, he gradually relaxed, and reverted to the subject of the ball.\n\n‘I was about to observe, Sir,’ he said, ‘that though my apparel would be too large, a suit of my friend Mr. Winkle’s would, perhaps, fit you better.’\n\nThe stranger took Mr. Winkle’s measure with his eye, and that feature glistened with satisfaction as he said, ‘Just the thing.’\n\nMr. Tupman looked round him. The wine, which had exerted its somniferous influence over Mr. Snodgrass and Mr. Winkle, had stolen upon the senses of Mr. Pickwick. That gentleman had gradually passed through the various stages which precede the lethargy produced by dinner, and its consequences. He had undergone the ordinary transitions from the height of conviviality to the depth of misery, and from the depth of misery to the height of conviviality. Like a gas-lamp in the street, with the wind in the pipe, he had exhibited for a moment an unnatural brilliancy, then sank so low as to be scarcely discernible; after a short interval, he had burst out again, to enlighten for a moment; then flickered with an uncertain, staggering sort of light, and then gone out altogether. His head was sunk upon his bosom, and perpetual snoring, with a partial choke occasionally, were the only audible indications of the great man’s presence.\n\nThe temptation to be present at the ball, and to form his first impressions of the beauty of the Kentish ladies, was strong upon Mr. Tupman. The temptation to take the stranger with him was equally great. He was wholly unacquainted with the place and its inhabitants, and the stranger seemed to possess as great a knowledge of both as if he had lived there from his infancy. Mr. Winkle was asleep, and Mr. Tupman had had sufficient experience in such matters to know that the moment he awoke he would, in the ordinary course of nature, roll heavily to bed. He was undecided. ‘Fill your glass, and pass the wine,’ said the indefatigable visitor.\n\nMr. Tupman did as he was requested; and the additional stimulus of the last glass settled his determination.\n\n‘Winkle’s bedroom is inside mine,’ said Mr. Tupman; ‘I couldn’t make him understand what I wanted, if I woke him now, but I know he has a dress- suit in a carpet bag; and supposing you wore it to the ball, and took it off when we returned, I could replace it without troubling him at all about the matter.’\n\n‘Capital,’ said the stranger, ‘famous plan--damned odd situation-- fourteen coats in the packing-cases, and obliged to wear another man’s-- very good notion, that--very.’\n\n‘We must purchase our tickets,’ said Mr. Tupman.\n\n‘Not worth while splitting a guinea,’ said the stranger, ‘toss who shall pay for both--I call; you spin--first time--woman--woman--bewitching woman,’ and down came the sovereign with the dragon (called by courtesy a woman) uppermost.\n\nMr. Tupman rang the bell, purchased the tickets, and ordered chamber candlesticks. In another quarter of an hour the stranger was completely arrayed in a full suit of Mr. Nathaniel Winkle’s.\n\n‘It’s a new coat,’ said Mr. Tupman, as the stranger surveyed himself with great complacency in a cheval glass; ‘the first that’s been made with our club button,’ and he called his companions’ attention to the large gilt button which displayed a bust of Mr. Pickwick in the centre, and the letters ‘P. C.’ on either side.\n\n‘“P. C.”’ said the stranger--‘queer set out--old fellow’s likeness, and “P. C.”--What does “P. C.” stand for--Peculiar Coat, eh?’ Mr. Tupman, with rising indignation and great importance, explained the mystic device.\n\n‘Rather short in the waist, ain’t it?’ said the stranger, screwing himself round to catch a glimpse in the glass of the waist buttons, which were half-way up his back. ‘Like a general postman’s coat--queer coats those--made by contract--no measuring--mysterious dispensations of Providence--all the short men get long coats--all the long men short ones.’ Running on in this way, Mr. Tupman’s new companion adjusted his dress, or rather the dress of Mr. Winkle; and, accompanied by Mr. Tupman, ascended the staircase leading to the ballroom.\n\n‘What names, sir?’ said the man at the door. Mr. Tracy Tupman was stepping forward to announce his own titles, when the stranger prevented him.\n\n‘No names at all;’ and then he whispered Mr. Tupman, ‘names won’t do-- not known--very good names in their way, but not great ones--capital names for a small party, but won’t make an impression in public assemblies--incog. the thing--gentlemen from London--distinguished foreigners--anything.’ The door was thrown open, and Mr. Tracy Tupman and the stranger entered the ballroom.\n\nIt was a long room, with crimson-covered benches, and wax candles in glass chandeliers. The musicians were securely confined in an elevated den, and quadrilles were being systematically got through by two or three sets of dancers. Two card-tables were made up in the adjoining card-room, and two pair of old ladies, and a corresponding number of stout gentlemen, were executing whist therein.\n\nThe finale concluded, the dancers promenaded the room, and Mr. Tupman and his companion stationed themselves in a corner to observe the company.\n\n‘Charming women,’ said Mr. Tupman.\n\n‘Wait a minute,’ said the stranger, ‘fun presently--nobs not come yet-- queer place--dockyard people of upper rank don’t know dockyard people of lower rank--dockyard people of lower rank don’t know small gentry--small gentry don’t know tradespeople--commissioner don’t know anybody.’\n\n‘Who’s that little boy with the light hair and pink eyes, in a fancy dress?’ inquired Mr. Tupman.\n\n‘Hush, pray--pink eyes--fancy dress--little boy--nonsense--ensign 97th-- Honourable Wilmot Snipe--great family--Snipes--very.’\n\n‘Sir Thomas Clubber, Lady Clubber, and the Misses Clubber!’ shouted the man at the door in a stentorian voice. A great sensation was created throughout the room by the entrance of a tall gentleman in a blue coat and bright buttons, a large lady in blue satin, and two young ladies, on a similar scale, in fashionably-made dresses of the same hue.\n\n‘Commissioner--head of the yard--great man--remarkably great man,’ whispered the stranger in Mr. Tupman’s ear, as the charitable committee ushered Sir Thomas Clubber and family to the top of the room. The Honourable Wilmot Snipe, and other distinguished gentlemen crowded to render homage to the Misses Clubber; and Sir Thomas Clubber stood bolt upright, and looked majestically over his black kerchief at the assembled company.\n\n‘Mr. Smithie, Mrs. Smithie, and the Misses Smithie,’ was the next announcement.\n\n‘What’s Mr. Smithie?’ inquired Mr. Tracy Tupman.\n\n‘Something in the yard,’ replied the stranger. Mr. Smithie bowed deferentially to Sir Thomas Clubber; and Sir Thomas Clubber acknowledged the salute with conscious condescension. Lady Clubber took a telescopic view of Mrs. Smithie and family through her eye-glass and Mrs. Smithie stared in her turn at Mrs. Somebody-else, whose husband was not in the dockyard at all.\n\n‘Colonel Bulder, Mrs. Colonel Bulder, and Miss Bulder,’ were the next arrivals.\n\n‘Head of the garrison,’ said the stranger, in reply to Mr. Tupman’s inquiring look.\n\nMiss Bulder was warmly welcomed by the Misses Clubber; the greeting between Mrs. Colonel Bulder and Lady Clubber was of the most affectionate description; Colonel Bulder and Sir Thomas Clubber exchanged snuff-boxes, and looked very much like a pair of Alexander Selkirks--‘Monarchs of all they surveyed.’\n\nWhile the aristocracy of the place--the Bulders, and Clubbers, and Snipes--were thus preserving their dignity at the upper end of the room, the other classes of society were imitating their example in other parts of it. The less aristocratic officers of the 97th devoted themselves to the families of the less important functionaries from the dockyard. The solicitors’ wives, and the wine-merchant’s wife, headed another grade (the brewer’s wife visited the Bulders); and Mrs. Tomlinson, the post- office keeper, seemed by mutual consent to have been chosen the leader of the trade party.\n\nOne of the most popular personages, in his own circle, present, was a little fat man, with a ring of upright black hair round his head, and an extensive bald plain on the top of it--Doctor Slammer, surgeon to the 97th. The doctor took snuff with everybody, chatted with everybody, laughed, danced, made jokes, played whist, did everything, and was everywhere. To these pursuits, multifarious as they were, the little doctor added a more important one than any--he was indefatigable in paying the most unremitting and devoted attention to a little old widow, whose rich dress and profusion of ornament bespoke her a most desirable addition to a limited income.\n\nUpon the doctor, and the widow, the eyes of both Mr. Tupman and his companion had been fixed for some time, when the stranger broke silence.\n\n‘Lots of money--old girl--pompous doctor--not a bad idea--good fun,’ were the intelligible sentences which issued from his lips. Mr. Tupman looked inquisitively in his face.\n\n‘I’ll dance with the widow,’ said the stranger.\n\n‘Who is she?’ inquired Mr. Tupman.\n\n‘Don’t know--never saw her in all my life--cut out the doctor--here goes.’ And the stranger forthwith crossed the room; and, leaning against a mantel-piece, commenced gazing with an air of respectful and melancholy admiration on the fat countenance of the little old lady. Mr. Tupman looked on, in mute astonishment. The stranger progressed rapidly; the little doctor danced with another lady; the widow dropped her fan; the stranger picked it up, and presented it--a smile--a bow--a curtsey-- a few words of conversation. The stranger walked boldly up to, and returned with, the master of the ceremonies; a little introductory pantomime; and the stranger and Mrs. Budger took their places in a quadrille.\n\nThe surprise of Mr. Tupman at this summary proceeding, great as it was, was immeasurably exceeded by the astonishment of the doctor. The stranger was young, and the widow was flattered. The doctor’s attentions were unheeded by the widow; and the doctor’s indignation was wholly lost on his imperturbable rival. Doctor Slammer was paralysed. He, Doctor Slammer, of the 97th, to be extinguished in a moment, by a man whom nobody had ever seen before, and whom nobody knew even now! Doctor Slammer--Doctor Slammer of the 97th rejected! Impossible! It could not be! Yes, it was; there they were. What! introducing his friend! Could he believe his eyes! He looked again, and was under the painful necessity of admitting the veracity of his optics; Mrs. Budger was dancing with Mr. Tracy Tupman; there was no mistaking the fact. There was the widow before him, bouncing bodily here and there, with unwonted vigour; and Mr. Tracy Tupman hopping about, with a face expressive of the most intense solemnity, dancing (as a good many people do) as if a quadrille were not a thing to be laughed at, but a severe trial to the feelings, which it requires inflexible resolution to encounter.\n\nSilently and patiently did the doctor bear all this, and all the handings of negus, and watching for glasses, and darting for biscuits, and coquetting, that ensued; but, a few seconds after the stranger had disappeared to lead Mrs. Budger to her carriage, he darted swiftly from the room with every particle of his hitherto-bottled-up indignation effervescing, from all parts of his countenance, in a perspiration of passion.\n\nThe stranger was returning, and Mr. Tupman was beside him. He spoke in a low tone, and laughed. The little doctor thirsted for his life. He was exulting. He had triumphed.\n\n‘Sir!’ said the doctor, in an awful voice, producing a card, and retiring into an angle of the passage, ‘my name is Slammer, Doctor Slammer, sir--97th Regiment--Chatham Barracks--my card, Sir, my card.’ He would have added more, but his indignation choked him.\n\n‘Ah!’ replied the stranger coolly, ‘Slammer--much obliged--polite attention--not ill now, Slammer--but when I am--knock you up.’\n\n‘You--you’re a shuffler, sir,’ gasped the furious doctor, ‘a poltroon--a coward--a liar--a--a--will nothing induce you to give me your card, sir!’\n\n‘Oh! I see,’ said the stranger, half aside, ‘negus too strong here-- liberal landlord--very foolish--very--lemonade much better--hot rooms-- elderly gentlemen--suffer for it in the morning--cruel--cruel;’ and he moved on a step or two.\n\n‘You are stopping in this house, Sir,’ said the indignant little man; ‘you are intoxicated now, Sir; you shall hear from me in the morning, sir. I shall find you out, sir; I shall find you out.’\n\n‘Rather you found me out than found me at home,’ replied the unmoved stranger.\n\nDoctor Slammer looked unutterable ferocity, as he fixed his hat on his head with an indignant knock; and the stranger and Mr. Tupman ascended to the bedroom of the latter to restore the borrowed plumage to the unconscious Winkle.\n\nThat gentleman was fast asleep; the restoration was soon made. The stranger was extremely jocose; and Mr. Tracy Tupman, being quite bewildered with wine, negus, lights, and ladies, thought the whole affair was an exquisite joke. His new friend departed; and, after experiencing some slight difficulty in finding the orifice in his nightcap, originally intended for the reception of his head, and finally overturning his candlestick in his struggles to put it on, Mr. Tracy Tupman managed to get into bed by a series of complicated evolutions, and shortly afterwards sank into repose.\n\nSeven o’clock had hardly ceased striking on the following morning, when Mr. Pickwick’s comprehensive mind was aroused from the state of unconsciousness, in which slumber had plunged it, by a loud knocking at his chamber door.\n\n‘Who’s there?’ said Mr. Pickwick, starting up in bed.\n\n‘Boots, sir.’\n\n‘What do you want?’\n\n‘Please, sir, can you tell me which gentleman of your party wears a bright blue dress-coat, with a gilt button with “P. C.” on it?’\n\n‘It’s been given out to brush,’ thought Mr. Pickwick, ‘and the man has forgotten whom it belongs to.’\n\nMr. Winkle,’ he called out, ‘next room but two, on the right hand.’\n\n‘Thank’ee, sir,’ said the Boots, and away he went.\n\n‘What’s the matter?’ cried Mr. Tupman, as a loud knocking at his door roused him from his oblivious repose.\n\n‘Can I speak to Mr. Winkle, sir?’ replied Boots from the outside.\n\n‘Winkle--Winkle!’ shouted Mr. Tupman, calling into the inner room.\n\n‘Hollo!’ replied a faint voice from within the bed-clothes.\n\n‘You’re wanted--some one at the door;’ and, having exerted himself to articulate thus much, Mr. Tracy Tupman turned round and fell fast asleep again.\n\n‘Wanted!’ said Mr. Winkle, hastily jumping out of bed, and putting on a few articles of clothing; ‘wanted! at this distance from town--who on earth can want me?’\n\n‘Gentleman in the coffee-room, sir,’ replied the Boots, as Mr. Winkle opened the door and confronted him; ‘gentleman says he’ll not detain you a moment, Sir, but he can take no denial.’\n\n‘Very odd!’ said Mr. Winkle; ‘I’ll be down directly.’\n\nHe hurriedly wrapped himself in a travelling-shawl and dressing-gown, and proceeded downstairs. An old woman and a couple of waiters were cleaning the coffee-room, and an officer in undress uniform was looking out of the window. He turned round as Mr. Winkle entered, and made a stiff inclination of the head. Having ordered the attendants to retire, and closed the door very carefully, he said, ‘Mr. Winkle, I presume?’\n\n‘My name is Winkle, sir.’\n\n‘You will not be surprised, sir, when I inform you that I have called here this morning on behalf of my friend, Doctor Slammer, of the 97th.’\n\n‘Doctor Slammer!’ said Mr. Winkle.\n\n‘Doctor Slammer. He begged me to express his opinion that your conduct of last evening was of a description which no gentleman could endure; and’ (he added) ‘which no one gentleman would pursue towards another.’\n\nMr. Winkle’s astonishment was too real, and too evident, to escape the observation of Doctor Slammer’s friend; he therefore proceeded--\n\n‘My friend, Doctor Slammer, requested me to add, that he was firmly persuaded you were intoxicated during a portion of the evening, and possibly unconscious of the extent of the insult you were guilty of. He commissioned me to say, that should this be pleaded as an excuse for your behaviour, he will consent to accept a written apology, to be penned by you, from my dictation.’\n\n‘A written apology!’ repeated Mr. Winkle, in the most emphatic tone of amazement possible.\n\n‘Of course you know the alternative,’ replied the visitor coolly.\n\n‘Were you intrusted with this message to me by name?’ inquired Mr. Winkle, whose intellects were hopelessly confused by this extraordinary conversation.\n\n‘I was not present myself,’ replied the visitor, ‘and in consequence of your firm refusal to give your card to Doctor Slammer, I was desired by that gentleman to identify the wearer of a very uncommon coat--a bright blue dress-coat, with a gilt button displaying a bust, and the letters “P. C.”’\n\nMr. Winkle actually staggered with astonishment as he heard his own costume thus minutely described. Doctor Slammer’s friend proceeded:-- ‘From the inquiries I made at the bar, just now, I was convinced that the owner of the coat in question arrived here, with three gentlemen, yesterday afternoon. I immediately sent up to the gentleman who was described as appearing the head of the party, and he at once referred me to you.’\n\nIf the principal tower of Rochester Castle had suddenly walked from its foundation, and stationed itself opposite the coffee-room window, Mr. Winkle’s surprise would have been as nothing compared with the profound astonishment with which he had heard this address. His first impression was that his coat had been stolen. ‘Will you allow me to detain you one moment?’ said he.\n\n‘Certainly,’ replied the unwelcome visitor.\n\nMr. Winkle ran hastily upstairs, and with a trembling hand opened the bag. There was the coat in its usual place, but exhibiting, on a close inspection, evident tokens of having been worn on the preceding night.\n‘It must be so,’ said Mr. Winkle, letting the coat fall from his hands. ‘I took too much wine after dinner, and have a very vague recollection of walking about the streets, and smoking a cigar afterwards. The fact is, I was very drunk;--I must have changed my coat--gone somewhere--and insulted somebody--I have no doubt of it; and this message is the terrible consequence.’ Saying which, Mr. Winkle retraced his steps in the direction of the coffee-room, with the gloomy and dreadful resolve of accepting the challenge of the warlike Doctor Slammer, and abiding by the worst consequences that might ensue.\n\nTo this determination Mr. Winkle was urged by a variety of considerations, the first of which was his reputation with the club. He had always been looked up to as a high authority on all matters of amusement and dexterity, whether offensive, defensive, or inoffensive; and if, on this very first occasion of being put to the test, he shrunk back from the trial, beneath his leader’s eye, his name and standing were lost for ever. Besides, he remembered to have heard it frequently surmised by the uninitiated in such matters that by an understood arrangement between the seconds, the pistols were seldom loaded with ball; and, furthermore, he reflected that if he applied to Mr. Snodgrass to act as his second, and depicted the danger in glowing terms, that gentleman might possibly communicate the intelligence to Mr. Pickwick, who would certainly lose no time in transmitting it to the local authorities, and thus prevent the killing or maiming of his follower.\nSuch were his thoughts when he returned to the coffee-room, and intimated his intention of accepting the doctor’s challenge.\n\n‘Will you refer me to a friend, to arrange the time and place of meeting?’ said the officer.\n\n‘Quite unnecessary,’ replied Mr. Winkle; ‘name them to me, and I can procure the attendance of a friend afterwards.’\n\n‘Shall we say--sunset this evening?’ inquired the officer, in a careless tone.\n\n‘Very good,’ replied Mr. Winkle, thinking in his heart it was very bad.\n\n‘You know Fort Pitt?’\n\n‘Yes; I saw it yesterday.’\n\n‘If you will take the trouble to turn into the field which borders the trench, take the foot-path to the left when you arrive at an angle of the fortification, and keep straight on, till you see me, I will precede you to a secluded place, where the affair can be conducted without fear of interruption.’\n\n‘Fear of interruption!’ thought Mr. Winkle.\n\n‘Nothing more to arrange, I think,’ said the officer.\n\n‘I am not aware of anything more,’ replied Mr. Winkle. ‘Good-morning.’\n\n‘Good-morning;’ and the officer whistled a lively air as he strode away.\n\nThat morning’s breakfast passed heavily off. Mr. Tupman was not in a condition to rise, after the unwonted dissipation of the previous night; Mr. Snodgrass appeared to labour under a poetical depression of spirits; and even Mr. Pickwick evinced an unusual attachment to silence and soda- water. Mr. Winkle eagerly watched his opportunity: it was not long wanting. Mr. Snodgrass proposed a visit to the castle, and as Mr. Winkle was the only other member of the party disposed to walk, they went out together.\n\n‘Snodgrass,’ said Mr. Winkle, when they had turned out of the public street.’Snodgrass, my dear fellow, can I rely upon your secrecy?’ As he said this, he most devoutly and earnestly hoped he could not.\n\n‘You can,’ replied Mr. Snodgrass. ‘Hear me swear--’\n\n‘No, no,’ interrupted Winkle, terrified at the idea of his companion’s unconsciously pledging himself not to give information; ‘don’t swear, don’t swear; it’s quite unnecessary.’\n\nMr. Snodgrass dropped the hand which he had, in the spirit of poesy, raised towards the clouds as he made the above appeal, and assumed an attitude of attention.\n\n‘I want your assistance, my dear fellow, in an affair of honour,’ said Mr. Winkle.\n\n‘You shall have it,’ replied Mr. Snodgrass, clasping his friend’s hand.\n\n‘With a doctor--Doctor Slammer, of the 97th,’ said Mr. Winkle, wishing to make the matter appear as solemn as possible; ‘an affair with an officer, seconded by another officer, at sunset this evening, in a lonely field beyond Fort Pitt.’\n\n‘I will attend you,’ said Mr. Snodgrass.\n\nHe was astonished, but by no means dismayed. It is extraordinary how cool any party but the principal can be in such cases. Mr. Winkle had forgotten this. He had judged of his friend’s feelings by his own.\n\n‘The consequences may be dreadful,’ said Mr. Winkle.\n\n‘I hope not,’ said Mr. Snodgrass.\n\n‘The doctor, I believe, is a very good shot,’ said Mr. Winkle.\n\n‘Most of these military men are,’ observed Mr. Snodgrass calmly; ‘but so are you, ain’t you?’\n\nMr. Winkle replied in the affirmative; and perceiving that he had not alarmed his companion sufficiently, changed his ground.\n\n‘Snodgrass,’ he said, in a voice tremulous with emotion, ‘if I fall, you will find in a packet which I shall place in your hands a note for my-- for my father.’\n\nThis attack was a failure also. Mr. Snodgrass was affected, but he undertook the delivery of the note as readily as if he had been a twopenny postman.\n\n‘If I fall,’ said Mr. Winkle, ‘or if the doctor falls, you, my dear friend, will be tried as an accessory before the fact. Shall I involve my friend in transportation--possibly for life!’\n\nMr. Snodgrass winced a little at this, but his heroism was invincible. ‘In the cause of friendship,’ he fervently exclaimed, ‘I would brave all dangers.’\n\nHow Mr. Winkle cursed his companion’s devoted friendship internally, as they walked silently along, side by side, for some minutes, each immersed in his own meditations! The morning was wearing away; he grew desperate.\n\n‘Snodgrass,’ he said, stopping suddenly, ‘do not let me be balked in this matter--do not give information to the local authorities--do not obtain the assistance of several peace officers, to take either me or Doctor Slammer, of the 97th Regiment, at present quartered in Chatham Barracks, into custody, and thus prevent this duel!--I say, do not.’\n\nMr. Snodgrass seized his friend’s hand warmly, as he enthusiastically replied, ‘Not for worlds!’\n\nA thrill passed over Mr. Winkle’s frame as the conviction that he had nothing to hope from his friend’s fears, and that he was destined to become an animated target, rushed forcibly upon him.\n\nThe state of the case having been formally explained to Mr. Snodgrass, and a case of satisfactory pistols, with the satisfactory accompaniments of powder, ball, and caps, having been hired from a manufacturer in Rochester, the two friends returned to their inn; Mr. Winkle to ruminate on the approaching struggle, and Mr. Snodgrass to arrange the weapons of war, and put them into proper order for immediate use.\n\nIt was a dull and heavy evening when they again sallied forth on their awkward errand. Mr. Winkle was muffled up in a huge cloak to escape observation, and Mr. Snodgrass bore under his the instruments of destruction.\n\n‘Have you got everything?’ said Mr. Winkle, in an agitated tone.\n\n‘Everything,’ replied Mr. Snodgrass; ‘plenty of ammunition, in case the shots don’t take effect. There’s a quarter of a pound of powder in the case, and I have got two newspapers in my pocket for the loadings.’\n\nThese were instances of friendship for which any man might reasonably feel most grateful. The presumption is, that the gratitude of Mr. Winkle was too powerful for utterance, as he said nothing, but continued to walk on--rather slowly.\n\n‘We are in excellent time,’ said Mr. Snodgrass, as they climbed the fence of the first field; ‘the sun is just going down.’ Mr. Winkle looked up at the declining orb and painfully thought of the probability of his ‘going down’ himself, before long.\n\n‘There’s the officer,’ exclaimed Mr. Winkle, after a few minutes walking.\n\n‘Where?’ said Mr. Snodgrass.\n\n‘There--the gentleman in the blue cloak.’ Mr. Snodgrass looked in the direction indicated by the forefinger of his friend, and observed a figure, muffled up, as he had described. The officer evinced his consciousness of their presence by slightly beckoning with his hand; and the two friends followed him at a little distance, as he walked away.\n\nThe evening grew more dull every moment, and a melancholy wind sounded through the deserted fields, like a distant giant whistling for his house-dog. The sadness of the scene imparted a sombre tinge to the feelings of Mr. Winkle. He started as they passed the angle of the trench--it looked like a colossal grave.\n\nThe officer turned suddenly from the path, and after climbing a paling, and scaling a hedge, entered a secluded field. Two gentlemen were waiting in it; one was a little, fat man, with black hair; and the other--a portly personage in a braided surtout--was sitting with perfect equanimity on a camp-stool.\n\n‘The other party, and a surgeon, I suppose,’ said Mr. Snodgrass; ‘take a drop of brandy.’ Mr. Winkle seized the wicker bottle which his friend proffered, and took a lengthened pull at the exhilarating liquid.\n\n‘My friend, Sir, Mr. Snodgrass,’ said Mr. Winkle, as the officer approached. Doctor Slammer’s friend bowed, and produced a case similar to that which Mr. Snodgrass carried.\n\n‘We have nothing further to say, Sir, I think,’ he coldly remarked, as he opened the case; ‘an apology has been resolutely declined.’\n\n‘Nothing, Sir,’ said Mr. Snodgrass, who began to feel rather uncomfortable himself.\n\n‘Will you step forward?’ said the officer.\n\n‘Certainly,’ replied Mr. Snodgrass. The ground was measured, and preliminaries arranged.\n\n‘You will find these better than your own,’ said the opposite second, producing his pistols. ‘You saw me load them. Do you object to use them?’\n\n‘Certainly not,’ replied Mr. Snodgrass. The offer relieved him from considerable embarrassment, for his previous notions of loading a pistol were rather vague and undefined.\n\n‘We may place our men, then, I think,’ observed the officer, with as much indifference as if the principals were chess-men, and the seconds players.\n\n‘I think we may,’ replied Mr. Snodgrass; who would have assented to any proposition, because he knew nothing about the matter. The officer crossed to Doctor Slammer, and Mr. Snodgrass went up to Mr. Winkle.\n\n‘It’s all ready,’ said he, offering the pistol. ‘Give me your cloak.’\n\n‘You have got the packet, my dear fellow,’ said poor Winkle.\n\n‘All right,’ said Mr. Snodgrass. ‘Be steady, and wing him.’\n\nIt occurred to Mr. Winkle that this advice was very like that which bystanders invariably give to the smallest boy in a street fight, namely, ‘Go in, and win’--an admirable thing to recommend, if you only know how to do it. He took off his cloak, however, in silence--it always took a long time to undo that cloak--and accepted the pistol. The seconds retired, the gentleman on the camp-stool did the same, and the belligerents approached each other.\n\nMr. Winkle was always remarkable for extreme humanity. It is conjectured that his unwillingness to hurt a fellow-creature intentionally was the cause of his shutting his eyes when he arrived at the fatal spot; and that the circumstance of his eyes being closed, prevented his observing the very extraordinary and unaccountable demeanour of Doctor Slammer. That gentleman started, stared, retreated, rubbed his eyes, stared again, and, finally, shouted, ‘Stop, stop!’\n\n‘What’s all this?’ said Doctor Slammer, as his friend and Mr. Snodgrass came running up; ‘that’s not the man.’\n\n‘Not the man!’ said Doctor Slammer’s second.\n\n‘Not the man!’ said Mr. Snodgrass.\n\n‘Not the man!’ said the gentleman with the camp-stool in his hand.\n\n‘Certainly not,’ replied the little doctor. ‘That’s not the person who insulted me last night.’\n\n‘Very extraordinary!’ exclaimed the officer.\n\n‘Very,’ said the gentleman with the camp-stool. ‘The only question is, whether the gentleman, being on the ground, must not be considered, as a matter of form, to be the individual who insulted our friend, Doctor Slammer, yesterday evening, whether he is really that individual or not;’ and having delivered this suggestion, with a very sage and mysterious air, the man with the camp-stool took a large pinch of snuff, and looked profoundly round, with the air of an authority in such matters.\n\nNow Mr. Winkle had opened his eyes, and his ears too, when he heard his adversary call out for a cessation of hostilities; and perceiving by what he had afterwards said that there was, beyond all question, some mistake in the matter, he at once foresaw the increase of reputation he should inevitably acquire by concealing the real motive of his coming out; he therefore stepped boldly forward, and said--\n\n‘I am not the person. I know it.’\n\n‘Then, that,’ said the man with the camp-stool, ‘is an affront to Doctor Slammer, and a sufficient reason for proceeding immediately.’\n\n‘Pray be quiet, Payne,’ said the doctor’s second. ‘Why did you not communicate this fact to me this morning, Sir?’\n\n‘To be sure--to be sure,’ said the man with the camp-stool indignantly.\n\n‘I entreat you to be quiet, Payne,’ said the other. ‘May I repeat my question, Sir?’\n\n‘Because, Sir,’ replied Mr. Winkle, who had had time to deliberate upon his answer, ‘because, Sir, you described an intoxicated and ungentlemanly person as wearing a coat which I have the honour, not only to wear but to have invented--the proposed uniform, Sir, of the Pickwick Club in London. The honour of that uniform I feel bound to maintain, and I therefore, without inquiry, accepted the challenge which you offered me.’\n\n‘My dear Sir,’ said the good-humoured little doctor advancing with extended hand, ‘I honour your gallantry. Permit me to say, Sir, that I highly admire your conduct, and extremely regret having caused you the inconvenience of this meeting, to no purpose.’\n\n‘I beg you won’t mention it, Sir,’ said Mr. Winkle.\n\n‘I shall feel proud of your acquaintance, Sir,’ said the little doctor.\n\n‘It will afford me the greatest pleasure to know you, sir,’ replied Mr. Winkle. Thereupon the doctor and Mr. Winkle shook hands, and then Mr. Winkle and Lieutenant Tappleton (the doctor’s second), and then Mr. Winkle and the man with the camp-stool, and, finally, Mr. Winkle and Mr. Snodgrass--the last-named gentleman in an excess of admiration at the noble conduct of his heroic friend.\n\n‘I think we may adjourn,’ said Lieutenant Tappleton.\n\n‘Certainly,’ added the doctor.\n\n‘Unless,’ interposed the man with the camp-stool, ‘unless Mr. Winkle feels himself aggrieved by the challenge; in which case, I submit, he has a right to satisfaction.’\n\nMr. Winkle, with great self-denial, expressed himself quite satisfied already.\n\n‘Or possibly,’ said the man with the camp-stool, ‘the gentleman’s second may feel himself affronted with some observations which fell from me at an early period of this meeting; if so, I shall be happy to give him satisfaction immediately.’\n\nMr. Snodgrass hastily professed himself very much obliged with the handsome offer of the gentleman who had spoken last, which he was only induced to decline by his entire contentment with the whole proceedings. The two seconds adjusted the cases, and the whole party left the ground in a much more lively manner than they had proceeded to it.\n\n‘Do you remain long here?’ inquired Doctor Slammer of Mr. Winkle, as they walked on most amicably together.\n\n‘I think we shall leave here the day after to-morrow,’ was the reply.\n\n‘I trust I shall have the pleasure of seeing you and your friend at my rooms, and of spending a pleasant evening with you, after this awkward mistake,’ said the little doctor; ‘are you disengaged this evening?’\n\n‘We have some friends here,’ replied Mr. Winkle, ‘and I should not like to leave them to-night. Perhaps you and your friend will join us at the Bull.’\n\n‘With great pleasure,’ said the little doctor; ‘will ten o’clock be too late to look in for half an hour?’\n\n‘Oh dear, no,’ said Mr. Winkle. ‘I shall be most happy to introduce you to my friends, Mr. Pickwick and Mr. Tupman.’\n\n‘It will give me great pleasure, I am sure,’ replied Doctor Slammer, little suspecting who Mr. Tupman was.\n\n‘You will be sure to come?’ said Mr. Snodgrass.\n\n‘Oh, certainly.’\n\nBy this time they had reached the road. Cordial farewells were exchanged, and the party separated. Doctor Slammer and his friends repaired to the barracks, and Mr. Winkle, accompanied by Mr. Snodgrass, returned to their inn.\n\nCHAPTER III. A NEW ACQUAINTANCE--THE STROLLER’S TALE--A DISAGREEABLE INTERRUPTION, AND AN UNPLEASANT ENCOUNTER\nMr. Pickwick had felt some apprehensions in consequence of the unusual absence of his two friends, which their mysterious behaviour during the whole morning had by no means tended to diminish. It was, therefore, with more than ordinary pleasure that he rose to greet them when they again entered; and with more than ordinary interest that he inquired what had occurred to detain them from his society. In reply to his questions on this point, Mr. Snodgrass was about to offer an historical account of the circumstances just now detailed, when he was suddenly checked by observing that there were present, not only Mr. Tupman and their stage-coach companion of the preceding day, but another stranger of equally singular appearance. It was a careworn-looking man, whose sallow face, and deeply-sunken eyes, were rendered still more striking than Nature had made them, by the straight black hair which hung in matted disorder half-way down his face. His eyes were almost unnaturally bright and piercing; his cheek-bones were high and prominent; and his jaws were so long and lank, that an observer would have supposed that he was drawing the flesh of his face in, for a moment, by some contraction of the muscles, if his half-opened mouth and immovable expression had not announced that it was his ordinary appearance. Round his neck he wore a green shawl, with the large ends straggling over his chest, and making their appearance occasionally beneath the worn button-holes of his old waistcoat. His upper garment was a long black surtout; and below it he wore wide drab trousers, and large boots, running rapidly to seed.\n\nIt was on this uncouth-looking person that Mr. Winkle’s eye rested, and it was towards him that Mr. Pickwick extended his hand when he said, ‘A friend of our friend’s here. We discovered this morning that our friend was connected with the theatre in this place, though he is not desirous to have it generally known, and this gentleman is a member of the same profession. He was about to favour us with a little anecdote connected with it, when you entered.’\n\n‘Lots of anecdote,’ said the green-coated stranger of the day before, advancing to Mr. Winkle and speaking in a low and confidential tone. ‘Rum fellow--does the heavy business--no actor--strange man--all sorts of miseries--Dismal Jemmy, we call him on the circuit.’ Mr. Winkle and Mr. Snodgrass politely welcomed the gentleman, elegantly designated as ‘Dismal Jemmy’; and calling for brandy-and-water, in imitation of the remainder of the company, seated themselves at the table.\n\n‘Now sir,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘will you oblige us by proceeding with what you were going to relate?’\n\nThe dismal individual took a dirty roll of paper from his pocket, and turning to Mr. Snodgrass, who had just taken out his note-book, said in a hollow voice, perfectly in keeping with his outward man--‘Are you the poet?’\n\n‘I--I do a little in that way,’ replied Mr. Snodgrass, rather taken aback by the abruptness of the question.\n\n‘Ah! poetry makes life what light and music do the stage--strip the one of the false embellishments, and the other of its illusions, and what is there real in either to live or care for?’\n\n‘Very true, Sir,’ replied Mr. Snodgrass.\n\n‘To be before the footlights,’ continued the dismal man, ‘is like sitting at a grand court show, and admiring the silken dresses of the gaudy throng; to be behind them is to be the people who make that finery, uncared for and unknown, and left to sink or swim, to starve or live, as fortune wills it.’\n\n‘Certainly,’ said Mr. Snodgrass: for the sunken eye of the dismal man rested on him, and he felt it necessary to say something.\n\n‘Go on, Jemmy,’ said the Spanish traveller, ‘like black-eyed Susan--all in the Downs--no croaking--speak out--look lively.’\n\n‘Will you make another glass before you begin, Sir?’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\nThe dismal man took the hint, and having mixed a glass of brandy-and- water, and slowly swallowed half of it, opened the roll of paper and proceeded, partly to read, and partly to relate, the following incident, which we find recorded on the Transactions of the Club as ‘The Stroller’s Tale.’\n\n# THE STROLLER’S TALE\n\n‘There is nothing of the marvellous in what I am going to relate,’ said the dismal man; ‘there is nothing even uncommon in it. Want and sickness are too common in many stations of life to deserve more notice than is usually bestowed on the most ordinary vicissitudes of human nature. I have thrown these few notes together, because the subject of them was well known to me for many years. I traced his progress downwards, step by step, until at last he reached that excess of destitution from which he never rose again.\n\n‘The man of whom I speak was a low pantomime actor; and, like many people of his class, an habitual drunkard. In his better days, before he had become enfeebled by dissipation and emaciated by disease, he had been in the receipt of a good salary, which, if he had been careful and prudent, he might have continued to receive for some years--not many; because these men either die early, or by unnaturally taxing their bodily energies, lose, prematurely, those physical powers on which alone they can depend for subsistence. His besetting sin gained so fast upon him, however, that it was found impossible to employ him in the situations in which he really was useful to the theatre. The public- house had a fascination for him which he could not resist. Neglected disease and hopeless poverty were as certain to be his portion as death itself, if he persevered in the same course; yet he did persevere, and the result may be guessed. He could obtain no engagement, and he wanted bread.\n\n‘Everybody who is at all acquainted with theatrical matters knows what a host of shabby, poverty-stricken men hang about the stage of a large establishment--not regularly engaged actors, but ballet people, procession men, tumblers, and so forth, who are taken on during the run of a pantomime, or an Easter piece, and are then discharged, until the production of some heavy spectacle occasions a new demand for their services. To this mode of life the man was compelled to resort; and taking the chair every night, at some low theatrical house, at once put him in possession of a few more shillings weekly, and enabled him to gratify his old propensity. Even this resource shortly failed him; his irregularities were too great to admit of his earning the wretched pittance he might thus have procured, and he was actually reduced to a state bordering on starvation, only procuring a trifle occasionally by borrowing it of some old companion, or by obtaining an appearance at one or other of the commonest of the minor theatres; and when he did earn anything it was spent in the old way.\n\n‘About this time, and when he had been existing for upwards of a year no one knew how, I had a short engagement at one of the theatres on the Surrey side of the water, and here I saw this man, whom I had lost sight of for some time; for I had been travelling in the provinces, and he had been skulking in the lanes and alleys of London. I was dressed to leave the house, and was crossing the stage on my way out, when he tapped me on the shoulder. Never shall I forget the repulsive sight that met my eye when I turned round. He was dressed for the pantomimes in all the absurdity of a clown’s costume. The spectral figures in the Dance of Death, the most frightful shapes that the ablest painter ever portrayed on canvas, never presented an appearance half so ghastly. His bloated body and shrunken legs--their deformity enhanced a hundredfold by the fantastic dress--the glassy eyes, contrasting fearfully with the thick white paint with which the face was besmeared; the grotesquely- ornamented head, trembling with paralysis, and the long skinny hands, rubbed with white chalk--all gave him a hideous and unnatural appearance, of which no description could convey an adequate idea, and which, to this day, I shudder to think of. His voice was hollow and tremulous as he took me aside, and in broken words recounted a long catalogue of sickness and privations, terminating as usual with an urgent request for the loan of a trifling sum of money. I put a few shillings in his hand, and as I turned away I heard the roar of laughter which followed his first tumble on the stage.\n\n‘A few nights afterwards, a boy put a dirty scrap of paper in my hand, on which were scrawled a few words in pencil, intimating that the man was dangerously ill, and begging me, after the performance, to see him at his lodgings in some street--I forget the name of it now--at no great distance from the theatre. I promised to comply, as soon as I could get away; and after the curtain fell, sallied forth on my melancholy errand.\n\n‘It was late, for I had been playing in the last piece; and, as it was a benefit night, the performances had been protracted to an unusual length. It was a dark, cold night, with a chill, damp wind, which blew the rain heavily against the windows and house-fronts. Pools of water had collected in the narrow and little-frequented streets, and as many of the thinly-scattered oil-lamps had been blown out by the violence of the wind, the walk was not only a comfortless, but most uncertain one. I had fortunately taken the right course, however, and succeeded, after a little difficulty, in finding the house to which I had been directed--a coal-shed, with one storey above it, in the back room of which lay the object of my search.\n\n‘A wretched-looking woman, the man’s wife, met me on the stairs, and, telling me that he had just fallen into a kind of doze, led me softly in, and placed a chair for me at the bedside. The sick man was lying with his face turned towards the wall; and as he took no heed of my presence, I had leisure to observe the place in which I found myself.\n\n‘He was lying on an old bedstead, which turned up during the day. The tattered remains of a checked curtain were drawn round the bed’s head, to exclude the wind, which, however, made its way into the comfortless room through the numerous chinks in the door, and blew it to and fro every instant. There was a low cinder fire in a rusty, unfixed grate; and an old three-cornered stained table, with some medicine bottles, a broken glass, and a few other domestic articles, was drawn out before it. A little child was sleeping on a temporary bed which had been made for it on the floor, and the woman sat on a chair by its side. There were a couple of shelves, with a few plates and cups and saucers; and a pair of stage shoes and a couple of foils hung beneath them. With the exception of little heaps of rags and bundles which had been carelessly thrown into the corners of the room, these were the only things in the apartment.\n\n‘I had had time to note these little particulars, and to mark the heavy breathing and feverish startings of the sick man, before he was aware of my presence. In the restless attempts to procure some easy resting-place for his head, he tossed his hand out of the bed, and it fell on mine. He started up, and stared eagerly in my face.\n\n‘“Mr. Hutley, John,” said his wife; “Mr. Hutley, that you sent for to- night, you know.”\n\n‘“Ah!” said the invalid, passing his hand across his forehead; “Hutley-- Hutley--let me see.” He seemed endeavouring to collect his thoughts for a few seconds, and then grasping me tightly by the wrist said, “Don’t leave me--don’t leave me, old fellow. She’ll murder me; I know she will.”\n\n‘“Has he been long so?” said I, addressing his weeping wife.\n\n‘“Since yesterday night,” she replied. “John, John, don’t you know me?”\n\n‘“Don’t let her come near me,” said the man, with a shudder, as she stooped over him. “Drive her away; I can’t bear her near me.” He stared wildly at her, with a look of deadly apprehension, and then whispered in my ear, “I beat her, Jem; I beat her yesterday, and many times before. I have starved her and the boy too; and now I am weak and helpless, Jem, she’ll murder me for it; I know she will. If you’d seen her cry, as I have, you’d know it too. Keep her off.” He relaxed his grasp, and sank back exhausted on the pillow.\n\n‘I knew but too well what all this meant. If I could have entertained any doubt of it, for an instant, one glance at the woman’s pale face and wasted form would have sufficiently explained the real state of the case. “You had better stand aside,” said I to the poor creature. “You can do him no good. Perhaps he will be calmer, if he does not see you.” She retired out of the man’s sight. He opened his eyes after a few seconds, and looked anxiously round.\n\n‘“Is she gone?” he eagerly inquired.\n\n‘“Yes--yes,” said I; “she shall not hurt you.”\n\n‘“I’ll tell you what, Jem,” said the man, in a low voice, “she does hurt me. There’s something in her eyes wakes such a dreadful fear in my heart, that it drives me mad. All last night, her large, staring eyes and pale face were close to mine; wherever I turned, they turned; and whenever I started up from my sleep, she was at the bedside looking at me.” He drew me closer to him, as he said in a deep alarmed whisper, “Jem, she must be an evil spirit--a devil! Hush! I know she is. If she had been a woman she would have died long ago. No woman could have borne what she has.”\n\n‘I sickened at the thought of the long course of cruelty and neglect which must have occurred to produce such an impression on such a man. I could say nothing in reply; for who could offer hope, or consolation, to the abject being before me?\n\n‘I sat there for upwards of two hours, during which time he tossed about, murmuring exclamations of pain or impatience, restlessly throwing his arms here and there, and turning constantly from side to side. At length he fell into that state of partial unconsciousness, in which the mind wanders uneasily from scene to scene, and from place to place, without the control of reason, but still without being able to divest itself of an indescribable sense of present suffering. Finding from his incoherent wanderings that this was the case, and knowing that in all probability the fever would not grow immediately worse, I left him, promising his miserable wife that I would repeat my visit next evening, and, if necessary, sit up with the patient during the night.\n\n‘I kept my promise. The last four-and-twenty hours had produced a frightful alteration. The eyes, though deeply sunk and heavy, shone with a lustre frightful to behold. The lips were parched, and cracked in many places; the hard, dry skin glowed with a burning heat; and there was an almost unearthly air of wild anxiety in the man’s face, indicating even more strongly the ravages of the disease. The fever was at its height.\n\n‘I took the seat I had occupied the night before, and there I sat for hours, listening to sounds which must strike deep to the heart of the most callous among human beings--the awful ravings of a dying man. From what I had heard of the medical attendant’s opinion, I knew there was no hope for him: I was sitting by his death-bed. I saw the wasted limbs-- which a few hours before had been distorted for the amusement of a boisterous gallery, writhing under the tortures of a burning fever--I heard the clown’s shrill laugh, blending with the low murmurings of the dying man.\n\n‘It is a touching thing to hear the mind reverting to the ordinary occupations and pursuits of health, when the body lies before you weak and helpless; but when those occupations are of a character the most strongly opposed to anything we associate with grave and solemn ideas, the impression produced is infinitely more powerful. The theatre and the public-house were the chief themes of the wretched man’s wanderings. It was evening, he fancied; he had a part to play that night; it was late, and he must leave home instantly. Why did they hold him, and prevent his going?--he should lose the money--he must go. No! they would not let him. He hid his face in his burning hands, and feebly bemoaned his own weakness, and the cruelty of his persecutors. A short pause, and he shouted out a few doggerel rhymes--the last he had ever learned. He rose in bed, drew up his withered limbs, and rolled about in uncouth positions; he was acting--he was at the theatre. A minute’s silence, and he murmured the burden of some roaring song. He had reached the old house at last--how hot the room was. He had been ill, very ill, but he was well now, and happy. Fill up his glass. Who was that, that dashed it from his lips? It was the same persecutor that had followed him before. He fell back upon his pillow and moaned aloud. A short period of oblivion, and he was wandering through a tedious maze of low-arched rooms--so low, sometimes, that he must creep upon his hands and knees to make his way along; it was close and dark, and every way he turned, some obstacle impeded his progress. There were insects, too, hideous crawling things, with eyes that stared upon him, and filled the very air around, glistening horribly amidst the thick darkness of the place. The walls and ceiling were alive with reptiles--the vault expanded to an enormous size--frightful figures flitted to and fro--and the faces of men he knew, rendered hideous by gibing and mouthing, peered out from among them; they were searing him with heated irons, and binding his head with cords till the blood started; and he struggled madly for life.\n\n‘At the close of one of these paroxysms, when I had with great difficulty held him down in his bed, he sank into what appeared to be a slumber. Overpowered with watching and exertion, I had closed my eyes for a few minutes, when I felt a violent clutch on my shoulder. I awoke instantly. He had raised himself up, so as to seat himself in bed--a dreadful change had come over his face, but consciousness had returned, for he evidently knew me. The child, who had been long since disturbed by his ravings, rose from its little bed, and ran towards its father, screaming with fright--the mother hastily caught it in her arms, lest he should injure it in the violence of his insanity; but, terrified by the alteration of his features, stood transfixed by the bedside. He grasped my shoulder convulsively, and, striking his breast with the other hand, made a desperate attempt to articulate. It was unavailing; he extended his arm towards them, and made another violent effort. There was a rattling noise in the throat--a glare of the eye--a short stifled groan- -and he fell back--dead!’\n\nIt would afford us the highest gratification to be enabled to record Mr. Pickwick’s opinion of the foregoing anecdote. We have little doubt that we should have been enabled to present it to our readers, but for a most unfortunate occurrence.\n\nMr. Pickwick had replaced on the table the glass which, during the last few sentences of the tale, he had retained in his hand; and had just made up his mind to speak--indeed, we have the authority of Mr. Snodgrass’s note-book for stating, that he had actually opened his mouth--when the waiter entered the room, and said--\n\n‘Some gentlemen, Sir.’\n\nIt has been conjectured that Mr. Pickwick was on the point of delivering some remarks which would have enlightened the world, if not the Thames, when he was thus interrupted; for he gazed sternly on the waiter’s countenance, and then looked round on the company generally, as if seeking for information relative to the new-comers.\n\n‘Oh!’ said Mr. Winkle, rising, ‘some friends of mine--show them in. Very pleasant fellows,’ added Mr. Winkle, after the waiter had retired-- ‘officers of the 97th, whose acquaintance I made rather oddly this morning. You will like them very much.’\n\nMr. Pickwick’s equanimity was at once restored. The waiter returned, and ushered three gentlemen into the room.\n\n‘Lieutenant Tappleton,’ said Mr. Winkle, ‘Lieutenant Tappleton, Mr. Pickwick--Doctor Payne, Mr. Pickwick--Mr. Snodgrass you have seen before, my friend Mr. Tupman, Doctor Payne--Doctor Slammer, Mr. Pickwick--Mr. Tupman, Doctor Slam--’\n\nHere Mr. Winkle suddenly paused; for strong emotion was visible on the countenance both of Mr. Tupman and the doctor.\n\n‘I have met this gentleman before,’ said the Doctor, with marked emphasis.\n\n‘Indeed!’ said Mr. Winkle.\n\n‘And--and that person, too, if I am not mistaken,’ said the doctor, bestowing a scrutinising glance on the green-coated stranger. ‘I think I gave that person a very pressing invitation last night, which he thought proper to decline.’ Saying which the doctor scowled magnanimously on the stranger, and whispered his friend Lieutenant Tappleton.\n\n‘You don’t say so,’ said that gentleman, at the conclusion of the whisper.\n\n‘I do, indeed,’ replied Doctor Slammer.\n\n‘You are bound to kick him on the spot,’ murmured the owner of the camp- stool, with great importance.\n\n‘Do be quiet, Payne,’ interposed the lieutenant. ‘Will you allow me to ask you, sir,’ he said, addressing Mr. Pickwick, who was considerably mystified by this very unpolite by-play--‘will you allow me to ask you, Sir, whether that person belongs to your party?’\n\n‘No, Sir,’ replied Mr. Pickwick, ‘he is a guest of ours.’\n\n‘He is a member of your club, or I am mistaken?’ said the lieutenant inquiringly.\n\n‘Certainly not,’ responded Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘And never wears your club-button?’ said the lieutenant.\n\n‘No--never!’ replied the astonished Mr. Pickwick.\n\nLieutenant Tappleton turned round to his friend Doctor Slammer, with a scarcely perceptible shrug of the shoulder, as if implying some doubt of the accuracy of his recollection. The little doctor looked wrathful, but confounded; and Mr. Payne gazed with a ferocious aspect on the beaming countenance of the unconscious Pickwick.\n\n‘Sir,’ said the doctor, suddenly addressing Mr. Tupman, in a tone which made that gentleman start as perceptibly as if a pin had been cunningly inserted in the calf of his leg, ‘you were at the ball here last night!’\n\nMr. Tupman gasped a faint affirmative, looking very hard at Mr. Pickwick all the while.\n\n‘That person was your companion,’ said the doctor, pointing to the still unmoved stranger.\n\nMr. Tupman admitted the fact.\n\n‘Now, sir,’ said the doctor to the stranger, ‘I ask you once again, in the presence of these gentlemen, whether you choose to give me your card, and to receive the treatment of a gentleman; or whether you impose upon me the necessity of personally chastising you on the spot?’\n\n‘Stay, sir,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘I really cannot allow this matter to go any further without some explanation. Tupman, recount the circumstances.’\n\nMr. Tupman, thus solemnly adjured, stated the case in a few words; touched slightly on the borrowing of the coat; expatiated largely on its having been done ‘after dinner’; wound up with a little penitence on his own account; and left the stranger to clear himself as best he could.\n\nHe was apparently about to proceed to do so, when Lieutenant Tappleton, who had been eyeing him with great curiosity, said with considerable scorn, ‘Haven’t I seen you at the theatre, Sir?’\n\n‘Certainly,’ replied the unabashed stranger.\n\n‘He is a strolling actor!’ said the lieutenant contemptuously, turning to Doctor Slammer.--‘He acts in the piece that the officers of the 52nd get up at the Rochester Theatre to-morrow night. You cannot proceed in this affair, Slammer--impossible!’\n\n‘Quite!’ said the dignified Payne.\n\n‘Sorry to have placed you in this disagreeable situation,’ said Lieutenant Tappleton, addressing Mr. Pickwick; ‘allow me to suggest, that the best way of avoiding a recurrence of such scenes in future will be to be more select in the choice of your companions. Good-evening, Sir!’ and the lieutenant bounced out of the room.\n\n‘And allow me to say, Sir,’ said the irascible Doctor Payne, ‘that if I had been Tappleton, or if I had been Slammer, I would have pulled your nose, Sir, and the nose of every man in this company. I would, sir-- every man. Payne is my name, sir--Doctor Payne of the 43rd. Good- evening, Sir.’ Having concluded this speech, and uttered the last three words in a loud key, he stalked majestically after his friend, closely followed by Doctor Slammer, who said nothing, but contented himself by withering the company with a look.\n\nRising rage and extreme bewilderment had swelled the noble breast of Mr. Pickwick, almost to the bursting of his waistcoat, during the delivery of the above defiance. He stood transfixed to the spot, gazing on vacancy. The closing of the door recalled him to himself. He rushed forward with fury in his looks, and fire in his eye. His hand was upon the lock of the door; in another instant it would have been on the throat of Doctor Payne of the 43rd, had not Mr. Snodgrass seized his revered leader by the coat tail, and dragged him backwards.\n\n‘Restrain him,’ cried Mr. Snodgrass; ‘Winkle, Tupman--he must not peril his distinguished life in such a cause as this.’\n\n‘Let me go,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Hold him tight,’ shouted Mr. Snodgrass; and by the united efforts of the whole company, Mr. Pickwick was forced into an arm-chair.\n\n‘Leave him alone,’ said the green-coated stranger; ‘brandy-and-water-- jolly old gentleman--lots of pluck--swallow this--ah!--capital stuff.’ Having previously tested the virtues of a bumper, which had been mixed by the dismal man, the stranger applied the glass to Mr. Pickwick’s mouth; and the remainder of its contents rapidly disappeared.\n\nThere was a short pause; the brandy-and-water had done its work; the amiable countenance of Mr. Pickwick was fast recovering its customary expression.\n\n‘They are not worth your notice,’ said the dismal man.\n\n‘You are right, sir,’ replied Mr. Pickwick, ‘they are not. I am ashamed to have been betrayed into this warmth of feeling. Draw your chair up to the table, Sir.’\n\nThe dismal man readily complied; a circle was again formed round the table, and harmony once more prevailed. Some lingering irritability appeared to find a resting-place in Mr. Winkle’s bosom, occasioned possibly by the temporary abstraction of his coat--though it is scarcely reasonable to suppose that so slight a circumstance can have excited even a passing feeling of anger in a Pickwickian’s breast. With this exception, their good-humour was completely restored; and the evening concluded with the conviviality with which it had begun.\n\nCHAPTER IV. A FIELD DAY AND BIVOUAC--MORE NEW FRIENDS--AN INVITATION TO THE COUNTRY\nMany authors entertain, not only a foolish, but a really dishonest objection to acknowledge the sources whence they derive much valuable information. We have no such feeling. We are merely endeavouring to discharge, in an upright manner, the responsible duties of our editorial functions; and whatever ambition we might have felt under other circumstances to lay claim to the authorship of these adventures, a regard for truth forbids us to do more than claim the merit of their judicious arrangement and impartial narration. The Pickwick papers are our New River Head; and we may be compared to the New River Company. The labours of others have raised for us an immense reservoir of important facts. We merely lay them on, and communicate them, in a clear and gentle stream, through the medium of these pages, to a world thirsting for Pickwickian knowledge.\n\nActing in this spirit, and resolutely proceeding on our determination to avow our obligations to the authorities we have consulted, we frankly say, that to the note-book of Mr. Snodgrass are we indebted for the particulars recorded in this and the succeeding chapter--particulars which, now that we have disburdened our consciences, we shall proceed to detail without further comment.\n\nThe whole population of Rochester and the adjoining towns rose from their beds at an early hour of the following morning, in a state of the utmost bustle and excitement. A grand review was to take place upon the lines. The manoeuvres of half a dozen regiments were to be inspected by the eagle eye of the commander-in-chief; temporary fortifications had been erected, the citadel was to be attacked and taken, and a mine was to be sprung.\n\nMr. Pickwick was, as our readers may have gathered from the slight extract we gave from his description of Chatham, an enthusiastic admirer of the army. Nothing could have been more delightful to him--nothing could have harmonised so well with the peculiar feeling of each of his companions--as this sight. Accordingly they were soon afoot, and walking in the direction of the scene of action, towards which crowds of people were already pouring from a variety of quarters.\n\nThe appearance of everything on the lines denoted that the approaching ceremony was one of the utmost grandeur and importance. There were sentries posted to keep the ground for the troops, and servants on the batteries keeping places for the ladies, and sergeants running to and fro, with vellum-covered books under their arms, and Colonel Bulder, in full military uniform, on horseback, galloping first to one place and then to another, and backing his horse among the people, and prancing, and curvetting, and shouting in a most alarming manner, and making himself very hoarse in the voice, and very red in the face, without any assignable cause or reason whatever. Officers were running backwards and forwards, first communicating with Colonel Bulder, and then ordering the sergeants, and then running away altogether; and even the very privates themselves looked from behind their glazed stocks with an air of mysterious solemnity, which sufficiently bespoke the special nature of the occasion.\n\nMr. Pickwick and his three companions stationed themselves in the front of the crowd, and patiently awaited the commencement of the proceedings. The throng was increasing every moment; and the efforts they were compelled to make, to retain the position they had gained, sufficiently occupied their attention during the two hours that ensued. At one time there was a sudden pressure from behind, and then Mr. Pickwick was jerked forward for several yards, with a degree of speed and elasticity highly inconsistent with the general gravity of his demeanour; at another moment there was a request to ‘keep back’ from the front, and then the butt-end of a musket was either dropped upon Mr. Pickwick’s toe, to remind him of the demand, or thrust into his chest, to insure its being complied with. Then some facetious gentlemen on the left, after pressing sideways in a body, and squeezing Mr. Snodgrass into the very last extreme of human torture, would request to know ‘vere he vos a shovin’ to’; and when Mr. Winkle had done expressing his excessive indignation at witnessing this unprovoked assault, some person behind would knock his hat over his eyes, and beg the favour of his putting his head in his pocket. These, and other practical witticisms, coupled with the unaccountable absence of Mr. Tupman (who had suddenly disappeared, and was nowhere to be found), rendered their situation upon the whole rather more uncomfortable than pleasing or desirable.\n\nAt length that low roar of many voices ran through the crowd which usually announces the arrival of whatever they have been waiting for. All eyes were turned in the direction of the sally-port. A few moments of eager expectation, and colours were seen fluttering gaily in the air, arms glistened brightly in the sun, column after column poured on to the plain. The troops halted and formed; the word of command rang through the line; there was a general clash of muskets as arms were presented; and the commander-in-chief, attended by Colonel Bulder and numerous officers, cantered to the front. The military bands struck up altogether; the horses stood upon two legs each, cantered backwards, and whisked their tails about in all directions; the dogs barked, the mob screamed, the troops recovered, and nothing was to be seen on either side, as far as the eye could reach, but a long perspective of red coats and white trousers, fixed and motionless.\n\nMr. Pickwick had been so fully occupied in falling about, and disentangling himself, miraculously, from between the legs of horses, that he had not enjoyed sufficient leisure to observe the scene before him, until it assumed the appearance we have just described. When he was at last enabled to stand firmly on his legs, his gratification and delight were unbounded.\n\n‘Can anything be finer or more delightful?’ he inquired of Mr. Winkle.\n\n‘Nothing,’ replied that gentleman, who had had a short man standing on each of his feet for the quarter of an hour immediately preceding.\n\n‘It is indeed a noble and a brilliant sight,’ said Mr. Snodgrass, in whose bosom a blaze of poetry was rapidly bursting forth, ‘to see the gallant defenders of their country drawn up in brilliant array before its peaceful citizens; their faces beaming--not with warlike ferocity, but with civilised gentleness; their eyes flashing--not with the rude fire of rapine or revenge, but with the soft light of humanity and intelligence.’\n\nMr. Pickwick fully entered into the spirit of this eulogium, but he could not exactly re-echo its terms; for the soft light of intelligence burned rather feebly in the eyes of the warriors, inasmuch as the command ‘eyes front’ had been given, and all the spectator saw before him was several thousand pair of optics, staring straight forward, wholly divested of any expression whatever.\n\n‘We are in a capital situation now,’ said Mr. Pickwick, looking round him. The crowd had gradually dispersed in their immediate vicinity, and they were nearly alone.\n\n‘Capital!’ echoed both Mr. Snodgrass and Mr. Winkle.\n\n‘What are they doing now?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick, adjusting his spectacles.\n\n‘I--I--rather think,’ said Mr. Winkle, changing colour--‘I rather think they’re going to fire.’\n\n‘Nonsense,’ said Mr. Pickwick hastily.\n\n‘I--I--really think they are,’ urged Mr. Snodgrass, somewhat alarmed.\n\n‘Impossible,’ replied Mr. Pickwick. He had hardly uttered the word, when the whole half-dozen regiments levelled their muskets as if they had but one common object, and that object the Pickwickians, and burst forth with the most awful and tremendous discharge that ever shook the earth to its centres, or an elderly gentleman off his.\n\nIt was in this trying situation, exposed to a galling fire of blank cartridges, and harassed by the operations of the military, a fresh body of whom had begun to fall in on the opposite side, that Mr. Pickwick displayed that perfect coolness and self-possession, which are the indispensable accompaniments of a great mind. He seized Mr. Winkle by the arm, and placing himself between that gentleman and Mr. Snodgrass, earnestly besought them to remember that beyond the possibility of being rendered deaf by the noise, there was no immediate danger to be apprehended from the firing.\n\n‘But--but--suppose some of the men should happen to have ball cartridges by mistake,’ remonstrated Mr. Winkle, pallid at the supposition he was himself conjuring up. ‘I heard something whistle through the air now--so sharp; close to my ear.’\n\n‘We had better throw ourselves on our faces, hadn’t we?’ said Mr. Snodgrass.\n\n‘No, no--it’s over now,’ said Mr. Pickwick. His lip might quiver, and his cheek might blanch, but no expression of fear or concern escaped the lips of that immortal man.\n\nMr. Pickwick was right--the firing ceased; but he had scarcely time to congratulate himself on the accuracy of his opinion, when a quick movement was visible in the line; the hoarse shout of the word of command ran along it, and before either of the party could form a guess at the meaning of this new manoeuvre, the whole of the half-dozen regiments, with fixed bayonets, charged at double-quick time down upon the very spot on which Mr. Pickwick and his friends were stationed.\n\nMan is but mortal; and there is a point beyond which human courage cannot extend. Mr. Pickwick gazed through his spectacles for an instant on the advancing mass, and then fairly turned his back and--we will not say fled; firstly, because it is an ignoble term, and, secondly, because Mr. Pickwick’s figure was by no means adapted for that mode of retreat-- he trotted away, at as quick a rate as his legs would convey him; so quickly, indeed, that he did not perceive the awkwardness of his situation, to the full extent, until too late.\n\nThe opposite troops, whose falling-in had perplexed Mr. Pickwick a few seconds before, were drawn up to repel the mimic attack of the sham besiegers of the citadel; and the consequence was that Mr. Pickwick and his two companions found themselves suddenly inclosed between two lines of great length, the one advancing at a rapid pace, and the other firmly waiting the collision in hostile array.\n\n‘Hoi!’ shouted the officers of the advancing line.\n\n‘Get out of the way!’ cried the officers of the stationary one.\n\n‘Where are we to go to?’ screamed the agitated Pickwickians.\n\n‘Hoi--hoi--hoi!’ was the only reply. There was a moment of intense bewilderment, a heavy tramp of footsteps, a violent concussion, a smothered laugh; the half-dozen regiments were half a thousand yards off, and the soles of Mr. Pickwick’s boots were elevated in air.\n\nMr. Snodgrass and Mr. Winkle had each performed a compulsory somerset with remarkable agility, when the first object that met the eyes of the latter as he sat on the ground, staunching with a yellow silk handkerchief the stream of life which issued from his nose, was his venerated leader at some distance off, running after his own hat, which was gambolling playfully away in perspective.\n\nThere are very few moments in a man’s existence when he experiences so much ludicrous distress, or meets with so little charitable commiseration, as when he is in pursuit of his own hat. A vast deal of coolness, and a peculiar degree of judgment, are requisite in catching a hat. A man must not be precipitate, or he runs over it; he must not rush into the opposite extreme, or he loses it altogether. The best way is to keep gently up with the object of pursuit, to be wary and cautious, to watch your opportunity well, get gradually before it, then make a rapid dive, seize it by the crown, and stick it firmly on your head; smiling pleasantly all the time, as if you thought it as good a joke as anybody else.\n\nThere was a fine gentle wind, and Mr. Pickwick’s hat rolled sportively before it. The wind puffed, and Mr. Pickwick puffed, and the hat rolled over and over as merrily as a lively porpoise in a strong tide: and on it might have rolled, far beyond Mr. Pickwick’s reach, had not its course been providentially stopped, just as that gentleman was on the point of resigning it to its fate.\n\nMr. Pickwick, we say, was completely exhausted, and about to give up the chase, when the hat was blown with some violence against the wheel of a carriage, which was drawn up in a line with half a dozen other vehicles on the spot to which his steps had been directed. Mr. Pickwick, perceiving his advantage, darted briskly forward, secured his property, planted it on his head, and paused to take breath. He had not been stationary half a minute, when he heard his own name eagerly pronounced by a voice, which he at once recognised as Mr. Tupman’s, and, looking upwards, he beheld a sight which filled him with surprise and pleasure.\n\nIn an open barouche, the horses of which had been taken out, the better to accommodate it to the crowded place, stood a stout old gentleman, in a blue coat and bright buttons, corduroy breeches and top-boots, two young ladies in scarfs and feathers, a young gentleman apparently enamoured of one of the young ladies in scarfs and feathers, a lady of doubtful age, probably the aunt of the aforesaid, and Mr. Tupman, as easy and unconcerned as if he had belonged to the family from the first moments of his infancy. Fastened up behind the barouche was a hamper of spacious dimensions--one of those hampers which always awakens in a contemplative mind associations connected with cold fowls, tongues, and bottles of wine--and on the box sat a fat and red-faced boy, in a state of somnolency, whom no speculative observer could have regarded for an instant without setting down as the official dispenser of the contents of the before-mentioned hamper, when the proper time for their consumption should arrive.\n\nMr. Pickwick had bestowed a hasty glance on these interesting objects, when he was again greeted by his faithful disciple.\n\n‘Pickwick--Pickwick,’ said Mr. Tupman; ‘come up here. Make haste.’\n\n‘Come along, Sir. Pray, come up,’ said the stout gentleman. ‘Joe!--damn that boy, he’s gone to sleep again.--Joe, let down the steps.’ The fat boy rolled slowly off the box, let down the steps, and held the carriage door invitingly open. Mr. Snodgrass and Mr. Winkle came up at the moment.\n\n‘Room for you all, gentlemen,’ said the stout man. ‘Two inside, and one out. Joe, make room for one of these gentlemen on the box. Now, Sir, come along;’ and the stout gentleman extended his arm, and pulled first Mr. Pickwick, and then Mr. Snodgrass, into the barouche by main force. Mr. Winkle mounted to the box, the fat boy waddled to the same perch, and fell fast asleep instantly.\n\n‘Well, gentlemen,’ said the stout man, ‘very glad to see you. Know you very well, gentlemen, though you mayn’t remember me. I spent some ev’nin’s at your club last winter--picked up my friend Mr. Tupman here this morning, and very glad I was to see him. Well, Sir, and how are you? You do look uncommon well, to be sure.’\n\nMr. Pickwick acknowledged the compliment, and cordially shook hands with the stout gentleman in the top-boots.\n\n‘Well, and how are you, sir?’ said the stout gentleman, addressing Mr. Snodgrass with paternal anxiety. ‘Charming, eh? Well, that’s right-- that’s right. And how are you, sir (to Mr. Winkle)? Well, I am glad to hear you say you are well; very glad I am, to be sure. My daughters, gentlemen--my gals these are; and that’s my sister, Miss Rachael Wardle. She’s a Miss, she is; and yet she ain’t a Miss--eh, Sir, eh?’ And the stout gentleman playfully inserted his elbow between the ribs of Mr. Pickwick, and laughed very heartily.\n\n‘Lor, brother!’ said Miss Wardle, with a deprecating smile.\n\n‘True, true,’ said the stout gentleman; ‘no one can deny it. Gentlemen, I beg your pardon; this is my friend Mr. Trundle. And now you all know each other, let’s be comfortable and happy, and see what’s going forward; that’s what I say.’ So the stout gentleman put on his spectacles, and Mr. Pickwick pulled out his glass, and everybody stood up in the carriage, and looked over somebody else’s shoulder at the evolutions of the military.\n\nAstounding evolutions they were, one rank firing over the heads of another rank, and then running away; and then the other rank firing over the heads of another rank, and running away in their turn; and then forming squares, with officers in the centre; and then descending the trench on one side with scaling-ladders, and ascending it on the other again by the same means; and knocking down barricades of baskets, and behaving in the most gallant manner possible. Then there was such a ramming down of the contents of enormous guns on the battery, with instruments like magnified mops; such a preparation before they were let off, and such an awful noise when they did go, that the air resounded with the screams of ladies. The young Misses Wardle were so frightened, that Mr. Trundle was actually obliged to hold one of them up in the carriage, while Mr. Snodgrass supported the other; and Mr. Wardle’s sister suffered under such a dreadful state of nervous alarm, that Mr. Tupman found it indispensably necessary to put his arm round her waist, to keep her up at all. Everybody was excited, except the fat boy, and he slept as soundly as if the roaring of cannon were his ordinary lullaby.\n\n‘Joe, Joe!’ said the stout gentleman, when the citadel was taken, and the besiegers and besieged sat down to dinner. ‘Damn that boy, he’s gone to sleep again. Be good enough to pinch him, sir--in the leg, if you please; nothing else wakes him--thank you. Undo the hamper, Joe.’\n\nThe fat boy, who had been effectually roused by the compression of a portion of his leg between the finger and thumb of Mr. Winkle, rolled off the box once again, and proceeded to unpack the hamper with more expedition than could have been expected from his previous inactivity.\n\n‘Now we must sit close,’ said the stout gentleman. After a great many jokes about squeezing the ladies’ sleeves, and a vast quantity of blushing at sundry jocose proposals, that the ladies should sit in the gentlemen’s laps, the whole party were stowed down in the barouche; and the stout gentleman proceeded to hand the things from the fat boy (who had mounted up behind for the purpose) into the carriage.\n\n‘Now, Joe, knives and forks.’ The knives and forks were handed in, and the ladies and gentlemen inside, and Mr. Winkle on the box, were each furnished with those useful instruments.\n\n‘Plates, Joe, plates.’ A similar process employed in the distribution of the crockery.\n\n‘Now, Joe, the fowls. Damn that boy; he’s gone to sleep again. Joe! Joe!’ (Sundry taps on the head with a stick, and the fat boy, with some difficulty, roused from his lethargy.) ‘Come, hand in the eatables.’\n\nThere was something in the sound of the last word which roused the unctuous boy. He jumped up, and the leaden eyes which twinkled behind his mountainous cheeks leered horribly upon the food as he unpacked it from the basket.\n\n‘Now make haste,’ said Mr. Wardle; for the fat boy was hanging fondly over a capon, which he seemed wholly unable to part with. The boy sighed deeply, and, bestowing an ardent gaze upon its plumpness, unwillingly consigned it to his master.\n\n‘That’s right--look sharp. Now the tongue--now the pigeon pie. Take care of that veal and ham--mind the lobsters--take the salad out of the cloth--give me the dressing.’ Such were the hurried orders which issued from the lips of Mr. Wardle, as he handed in the different articles described, and placed dishes in everybody’s hands, and on everybody’s knees, in endless number.\n\n‘Now ain’t this capital?’ inquired that jolly personage, when the work of destruction had commenced.\n\n‘Capital!’ said Mr. Winkle, who was carving a fowl on the box.\n\n‘Glass of wine?’\n\n‘With the greatest pleasure.’\n\n‘You’d better have a bottle to yourself up there, hadn’t you?’\n\n‘You’re very good.’\n\n‘Joe!’\n\n‘Yes, Sir.’ (He wasn’t asleep this time, having just succeeded in abstracting a veal patty.)\n\n‘Bottle of wine to the gentleman on the box. Glad to see you, Sir.’\n\n‘Thank’ee.’ Mr. Winkle emptied his glass, and placed the bottle on the coach-box, by his side.\n\n‘Will you permit me to have the pleasure, Sir?’ said Mr. Trundle to Mr. Winkle.\n\n‘With great pleasure,’ replied Mr. Winkle to Mr. Trundle, and then the two gentlemen took wine, after which they took a glass of wine round, ladies and all.\n\n‘How dear Emily is flirting with the strange gentleman,’ whispered the spinster aunt, with true spinster-aunt-like envy, to her brother, Mr. Wardle.\n\n‘Oh! I don’t know,’ said the jolly old gentleman; ‘all very natural, I dare say--nothing unusual. Mr. Pickwick, some wine, Sir?’ Mr. Pickwick, who had been deeply investigating the interior of the pigeon-pie, readily assented.\n\n‘Emily, my dear,’ said the spinster aunt, with a patronising air, ‘don’t talk so loud, love.’\n\n‘Lor, aunt!’\n\n‘Aunt and the little old gentleman want to have it all to themselves, I think,’ whispered Miss Isabella Wardle to her sister Emily. The young ladies laughed very heartily, and the old one tried to look amiable, but couldn’t manage it.\n\n‘Young girls have such spirits,’ said Miss Wardle to Mr. Tupman, with an air of gentle commiseration, as if animal spirits were contraband, and their possession without a permit a high crime and misdemeanour.\n\n‘Oh, they have,’ replied Mr. Tupman, not exactly making the sort of reply that was expected from him. ‘It’s quite delightful.’\n\n‘Hem!’ said Miss Wardle, rather dubiously.\n\n‘Will you permit me?’ said Mr. Tupman, in his blandest manner, touching the enchanting Rachael’s wrist with one hand, and gently elevating the bottle with the other. ‘Will you permit me?’\n\n‘Oh, sir!’ Mr. Tupman looked most impressive; and Rachael expressed her fear that more guns were going off, in which case, of course, she should have required support again.\n\n‘Do you think my dear nieces pretty?’ whispered their affectionate aunt to Mr. Tupman.\n\n‘I should, if their aunt wasn’t here,’ replied the ready Pickwickian, with a passionate glance.\n\n‘Oh, you naughty man--but really, if their complexions were a little better, don’t you think they would be nice-looking girls--by candlelight?’\n\n‘Yes; I think they would,’ said Mr. Tupman, with an air of indifference.\n\n‘Oh, you quiz--I know what you were going to say.’\n\n‘What?’ inquired Mr. Tupman, who had not precisely made up his mind to say anything at all.\n\n‘You were going to say that Isabel stoops--I know you were--you men are such observers. Well, so she does; it can’t be denied; and, certainly, if there is one thing more than another that makes a girl look ugly it is stooping. I often tell her that when she gets a little older she’ll be quite frightful. Well, you are a quiz!’\n\nMr. Tupman had no objection to earning the reputation at so cheap a rate: so he looked very knowing, and smiled mysteriously.\n\n‘What a sarcastic smile,’ said the admiring Rachael; ‘I declare I’m quite afraid of you.’\n\n‘Afraid of me!’\n\n‘Oh, you can’t disguise anything from me--I know what that smile means very well.’\n\n‘What?’ said Mr. Tupman, who had not the slightest notion himself.\n\n‘You mean,’ said the amiable aunt, sinking her voice still lower--‘you mean, that you don’t think Isabella’s stooping is as bad as Emily’s boldness. Well, she is bold! You cannot think how wretched it makes me sometimes--I’m sure I cry about it for hours together--my dear brother is so good, and so unsuspicious, that he never sees it; if he did, I’m quite certain it would break his heart. I wish I could think it was only manner--I hope it may be--’ (Here the affectionate relative heaved a deep sigh, and shook her head despondingly).\n\n‘I’m sure aunt’s talking about us,’ whispered Miss Emily Wardle to her sister--‘I’m quite certain of it--she looks so malicious.’\n\n‘Is she?’ replied Isabella.--‘Hem! aunt, dear!’\n\n‘Yes, my dear love!’\n\n‘I’m so afraid you’ll catch cold, aunt--have a silk handkerchief to tie round your dear old head--you really should take care of yourself-- consider your age!’\n\nHowever well deserved this piece of retaliation might have been, it was as vindictive a one as could well have been resorted to. There is no guessing in what form of reply the aunt’s indignation would have vented itself, had not Mr. Wardle unconsciously changed the subject, by calling emphatically for Joe.\n\n‘Damn that boy,’ said the old gentleman, ‘he’s gone to sleep again.’\n\n‘Very extraordinary boy, that,’ said Mr. Pickwick; ‘does he always sleep in this way?’\n\n‘Sleep!’ said the old gentleman, ‘he’s always asleep. Goes on errands fast asleep, and snores as he waits at table.’\n\n‘How very odd!’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Ah! odd indeed,’ returned the old gentleman; ‘I’m proud of that boy-- wouldn’t part with him on any account--he’s a natural curiosity! Here, Joe--Joe--take these things away, and open another bottle--d’ye hear?’\n\nThe fat boy rose, opened his eyes, swallowed the huge piece of pie he had been in the act of masticating when he last fell asleep, and slowly obeyed his master’s orders--gloating languidly over the remains of the feast, as he removed the plates, and deposited them in the hamper. The fresh bottle was produced, and speedily emptied: the hamper was made fast in its old place--the fat boy once more mounted the box--the spectacles and pocket-glass were again adjusted--and the evolutions of the military recommenced. There was a great fizzing and banging of guns, and starting of ladies--and then a mine was sprung, to the gratification of everybody--and when the mine had gone off, the military and the company followed its example, and went off too.\n\n‘Now, mind,’ said the old gentleman, as he shook hands with Mr. Pickwick at the conclusion of a conversation which had been carried on at intervals, during the conclusion of the proceedings, ‘we shall see you all to-morrow.’\n\n‘Most certainly,’ replied Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘You have got the address?’\n\n‘Manor Farm, Dingley Dell,’ said Mr. Pickwick, consulting his pocket- book.\n\n‘That’s it,’ said the old gentleman. ‘I don’t let you off, mind, under a week; and undertake that you shall see everything worth seeing. If you’ve come down for a country life, come to me, and I’ll give you plenty of it. Joe--damn that boy, he’s gone to sleep again--Joe, help Tom put in the horses.’\n\nThe horses were put in--the driver mounted--the fat boy clambered up by his side--farewells were exchanged--and the carriage rattled off. As the Pickwickians turned round to take a last glimpse of it, the setting sun cast a rich glow on the faces of their entertainers, and fell upon the form of the fat boy. His head was sunk upon his bosom; and he slumbered again.\n\nCHAPTER V. A SHORT ONE--SHOWING, AMONG OTHER MATTERS, HOW Mr. PICKWICK UNDERTOOK TO DRIVE, AND MR. WINKLE TO RIDE, AND HOW THEY BOTH DID IT\nBright and pleasant was the sky, balmy the air, and beautiful the appearance of every object around, as Mr. Pickwick leaned over the balustrades of Rochester Bridge, contemplating nature, and waiting for breakfast. The scene was indeed one which might well have charmed a far less reflective mind, than that to which it was presented.\n\nOn the left of the spectator lay the ruined wall, broken in many places, and in some, overhanging the narrow beach below in rude and heavy masses. Huge knots of seaweed hung upon the jagged and pointed stones, trembling in every breath of wind; and the green ivy clung mournfully round the dark and ruined battlements. Behind it rose the ancient castle, its towers roofless, and its massive walls crumbling away, but telling us proudly of its old might and strength, as when, seven hundred years ago, it rang with the clash of arms, or resounded with the noise of feasting and revelry. On either side, the banks of the Medway, covered with cornfields and pastures, with here and there a windmill, or a distant church, stretched away as far as the eye could see, presenting a rich and varied landscape, rendered more beautiful by the changing shadows which passed swiftly across it as the thin and half-formed clouds skimmed away in the light of the morning sun. The river, reflecting the clear blue of the sky, glistened and sparkled as it flowed noiselessly on; and the oars of the fishermen dipped into the water with a clear and liquid sound, as their heavy but picturesque boats glided slowly down the stream.\n\nMr. Pickwick was roused from the agreeable reverie into which he had been led by the objects before him, by a deep sigh, and a touch on his shoulder. He turned round: and the dismal man was at his side.\n\n‘Contemplating the scene?’ inquired the dismal man.\n\n‘I was,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘And congratulating yourself on being up so soon?’\n\nMr. Pickwick nodded assent.\n\n‘Ah! people need to rise early, to see the sun in all his splendour, for his brightness seldom lasts the day through. The morning of day and the morning of life are but too much alike.’\n\n‘You speak truly, sir,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘How common the saying,’ continued the dismal man, ‘“The morning’s too fine to last.” How well might it be applied to our everyday existence. God! what would I forfeit to have the days of my childhood restored, or to be able to forget them for ever!’\n\n‘You have seen much trouble, sir,’ said Mr. Pickwick compassionately.\n\n‘I have,’ said the dismal man hurriedly; ‘I have. More than those who see me now would believe possible.’ He paused for an instant, and then said abruptly--\n\n‘Did it ever strike you, on such a morning as this, that drowning would be happiness and peace?’\n\n‘God bless me, no!’ replied Mr. Pickwick, edging a little from the balustrade, as the possibility of the dismal man’s tipping him over, by way of experiment, occurred to him rather forcibly.\n\n‘I have thought so, often,’ said the dismal man, without noticing the action. ‘The calm, cool water seems to me to murmur an invitation to repose and rest. A bound, a splash, a brief struggle; there is an eddy for an instant, it gradually subsides into a gentle ripple; the waters have closed above your head, and the world has closed upon your miseries and misfortunes for ever.’ The sunken eye of the dismal man flashed brightly as he spoke, but the momentary excitement quickly subsided; and he turned calmly away, as he said--\n\n‘There--enough of that. I wish to see you on another subject. You invited me to read that paper, the night before last, and listened attentively while I did so.’\n\n‘I did,’ replied Mr. Pickwick; ‘and I certainly thought--’\n\n‘I asked for no opinion,’ said the dismal man, interrupting him, ‘and I want none. You are travelling for amusement and instruction. Suppose I forward you a curious manuscript--observe, not curious because wild or improbable, but curious as a leaf from the romance of real life--would you communicate it to the club, of which you have spoken so frequently?’\n\n‘Certainly,’ replied Mr. Pickwick, ‘if you wished it; and it would be entered on their transactions.’\n\n‘You shall have it,’ replied the dismal man. ‘Your address;’ and, Mr. Pickwick having communicated their probable route, the dismal man carefully noted it down in a greasy pocket-book, and, resisting Mr. Pickwick’s pressing invitation to breakfast, left that gentleman at his inn, and walked slowly away.\n\nMr. Pickwick found that his three companions had risen, and were waiting his arrival to commence breakfast, which was ready laid in tempting display. They sat down to the meal; and broiled ham, eggs, tea, coffee and sundries, began to disappear with a rapidity which at once bore testimony to the excellence of the fare, and the appetites of its consumers.\n\n‘Now, about Manor Farm,’ said Mr. Pickwick. ‘How shall we go?’\n\n‘We had better consult the waiter, perhaps,’ said Mr. Tupman; and the waiter was summoned accordingly.\n\n‘Dingley Dell, gentlemen--fifteen miles, gentlemen--cross road--post- chaise, sir?’\n\n‘Post-chaise won’t hold more than two,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘True, sir--beg your pardon, sir.--Very nice four-wheel chaise, sir-- seat for two behind--one in front for the gentleman that drives--oh! beg your pardon, sir--that’ll only hold three.’\n\n‘What’s to be done?’ said Mr. Snodgrass.\n\n‘Perhaps one of the gentlemen would like to ride, sir?’ suggested the waiter, looking towards Mr. Winkle; ‘very good saddle-horses, sir--any of Mr. Wardle’s men coming to Rochester, bring ‘em back, Sir.’\n\n‘The very thing,’ said Mr. Pickwick. ‘Winkle, will you go on horseback?’\n\nNow Mr. Winkle did entertain considerable misgivings in the very lowest recesses of his own heart, relative to his equestrian skill; but, as he would not have them even suspected, on any account, he at once replied with great hardihood, ‘Certainly. I should enjoy it of all things.’\n\nMr. Winkle had rushed upon his fate; there was no resource.\n\n‘Let them be at the door by eleven,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Very well, sir,’ replied the waiter.\n\nThe waiter retired; the breakfast concluded; and the travellers ascended to their respective bedrooms, to prepare a change of clothing, to take with them on their approaching expedition.\n\nMr. Pickwick had made his preliminary arrangements, and was looking over the coffee-room blinds at the passengers in the street, when the waiter entered, and announced that the chaise was ready--an announcement which the vehicle itself confirmed, by forthwith appearing before the coffee- room blinds aforesaid.\n\nIt was a curious little green box on four wheels, with a low place like a wine-bin for two behind, and an elevated perch for one in front, drawn by an immense brown horse, displaying great symmetry of bone. An hostler stood near, holding by the bridle another immense horse--apparently a near relative of the animal in the chaise--ready saddled for Mr. Winkle.\n\n‘Bless my soul!’ said Mr. Pickwick, as they stood upon the pavement while the coats were being put in. ‘Bless my soul! who’s to drive? I never thought of that.’\n\n‘Oh! you, of course,’ said Mr. Tupman.\n\n‘Of course,’ said Mr. Snodgrass.\n\n‘I!’ exclaimed Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Not the slightest fear, Sir,’ interposed the hostler. ‘Warrant him quiet, Sir; a hinfant in arms might drive him.’\n\n‘He don’t shy, does he?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Shy, sir?-he wouldn’t shy if he was to meet a vagin-load of monkeys with their tails burned off.’\n\nThe last recommendation was indisputable. Mr. Tupman and Mr. Snodgrass got into the bin; Mr. Pickwick ascended to his perch, and deposited his feet on a floor-clothed shelf, erected beneath it for that purpose.\n\n‘Now, shiny Villiam,’ said the hostler to the deputy hostler, ‘give the gen’lm’n the ribbons.’\n\nShiny Villiam’--so called, probably, from his sleek hair and oily countenance--placed the reins in Mr. Pickwick’s left hand; and the upper hostler thrust a whip into his right.\n\n‘Wo-o!’ cried Mr. Pickwick, as the tall quadruped evinced a decided inclination to back into the coffee-room window.\n\n‘Wo-o!’ echoed Mr. Tupman and Mr. Snodgrass, from the bin.\n\n‘Only his playfulness, gen’lm’n,’ said the head hostler encouragingly; ‘jist kitch hold on him, Villiam.’ The deputy restrained the animal’s impetuosity, and the principal ran to assist Mr. Winkle in mounting.\n\n‘T’other side, sir, if you please.’\n\n‘Blowed if the gen’lm’n worn’t a-gettin’ up on the wrong side,’ whispered a grinning post-boy to the inexpressibly gratified waiter.\n\nMr. Winkle, thus instructed, climbed into his saddle, with about as much difficulty as he would have experienced in getting up the side of a first-rate man-of-war.\n\n‘All right?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick, with an inward presentiment that it was all wrong.\n\n‘All right,’ replied Mr. Winkle faintly.\n\n‘Let ‘em go,’ cried the hostler.--‘Hold him in, sir;’ and away went the chaise, and the saddle-horse, with Mr. Pickwick on the box of the one, and Mr. Winkle on the back of the other, to the delight and gratification of the whole inn-yard.\n\n‘What makes him go sideways?’ said Mr. Snodgrass in the bin, to Mr. Winkle in the saddle.\n\n‘I can’t imagine,’ replied Mr. Winkle. His horse was drifting up the street in the most mysterious manner--side first, with his head towards one side of the way, and his tail towards the other.\n\nMr. Pickwick had no leisure to observe either this or any other particular, the whole of his faculties being concentrated in the management of the animal attached to the chaise, who displayed various peculiarities, highly interesting to a bystander, but by no means equally amusing to any one seated behind him. Besides constantly jerking his head up, in a very unpleasant and uncomfortable manner, and tugging at the reins to an extent which rendered it a matter of great difficulty for Mr. Pickwick to hold them, he had a singular propensity for darting suddenly every now and then to the side of the road, then stopping short, and then rushing forward for some minutes, at a speed which it was wholly impossible to control.\n\n‘What can he mean by this?’ said Mr. Snodgrass, when the horse had executed this manoeuvre for the twentieth time.\n\n‘I don’t know,’ replied Mr. Tupman; ‘it looks very like shying, don’t it?’ Mr. Snodgrass was about to reply, when he was interrupted by a shout from Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Woo!’ said that gentleman; ‘I have dropped my whip.’\n\n‘Winkle,’ said Mr. Snodgrass, as the equestrian came trotting up on the tall horse, with his hat over his ears, and shaking all over, as if he would shake to pieces, with the violence of the exercise, ‘pick up the whip, there’s a good fellow.’ Mr. Winkle pulled at the bridle of the tall horse till he was black in the face; and having at length succeeded in stopping him, dismounted, handed the whip to Mr. Pickwick, and grasping the reins, prepared to remount.\n\nNow whether the tall horse, in the natural playfulness of his disposition, was desirous of having a little innocent recreation with Mr. Winkle, or whether it occurred to him that he could perform the journey as much to his own satisfaction without a rider as with one, are points upon which, of course, we can arrive at no definite and distinct conclusion. By whatever motives the animal was actuated, certain it is that Mr. Winkle had no sooner touched the reins, than he slipped them over his head, and darted backwards to their full length.\n\n‘Poor fellow,’ said Mr. Winkle soothingly--‘poor fellow--good old horse.’ The ‘poor fellow’ was proof against flattery; the more Mr. Winkle tried to get nearer him, the more he sidled away; and, notwithstanding all kinds of coaxing and wheedling, there were Mr. Winkle and the horse going round and round each other for ten minutes, at the end of which time each was at precisely the same distance from the other as when they first commenced--an unsatisfactory sort of thing under any circumstances, but particularly so in a lonely road, where no assistance can be procured.\n\n‘What am I to do?’ shouted Mr. Winkle, after the dodging had been prolonged for a considerable time. ‘What am I to do? I can’t get on him.’\n\n‘You had better lead him till we come to a turnpike,’ replied Mr. Pickwick from the chaise.\n\n‘But he won’t come!’ roared Mr. Winkle. ‘Do come and hold him.’\n\nMr. Pickwick was the very personation of kindness and humanity: he threw the reins on the horse’s back, and having descended from his seat, carefully drew the chaise into the hedge, lest anything should come along the road, and stepped back to the assistance of his distressed companion, leaving Mr. Tupman and Mr. Snodgrass in the vehicle.\n\nThe horse no sooner beheld Mr. Pickwick advancing towards him with the chaise whip in his hand, than he exchanged the rotary motion in which he had previously indulged, for a retrograde movement of so very determined a character, that it at once drew Mr. Winkle, who was still at the end of the bridle, at a rather quicker rate than fast walking, in the direction from which they had just come. Mr. Pickwick ran to his assistance, but the faster Mr. Pickwick ran forward, the faster the horse ran backward. There was a great scraping of feet, and kicking up of the dust; and at last Mr. Winkle, his arms being nearly pulled out of their sockets, fairly let go his hold. The horse paused, stared, shook his head, turned round, and quietly trotted home to Rochester, leaving Mr. Winkle and Mr. Pickwick gazing on each other with countenances of blank dismay. A rattling noise at a little distance attracted their attention. They looked up.\n\n‘Bless my soul!’ exclaimed the agonised Mr. Pickwick; ‘there’s the other horse running away!’\n\nIt was but too true. The animal was startled by the noise, and the reins were on his back. The results may be guessed. He tore off with the four- wheeled chaise behind him, and Mr. Tupman and Mr. Snodgrass in the four- wheeled chaise. The heat was a short one. Mr. Tupman threw himself into the hedge, Mr. Snodgrass followed his example, the horse dashed the four--wheeled chaise against a wooden bridge, separated the wheels from the body, and the bin from the perch; and finally stood stock still to gaze upon the ruin he had made.\n\nThe first care of the two unspilt friends was to extricate their unfortunate companions from their bed of quickset--a process which gave them the unspeakable satisfaction of discovering that they had sustained no injury, beyond sundry rents in their garments, and various lacerations from the brambles. The next thing to be done was to unharness the horse. This complicated process having been effected, the party walked slowly forward, leading the horse among them, and abandoning the chaise to its fate.\n\nAn hour’s walk brought the travellers to a little road-side public- house, with two elm-trees, a horse trough, and a signpost, in front; one or two deformed hay-ricks behind, a kitchen garden at the side, and rotten sheds and mouldering outhouses jumbled in strange confusion all about it. A red-headed man was working in the garden; and to him Mr. Pickwick called lustily, ‘Hollo there!’\n\nThe red-headed man raised his body, shaded his eyes with his hand, and stared, long and coolly, at Mr. Pickwick and his companions.\n\n‘Hollo there!’ repeated Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Hollo!’ was the red-headed man’s reply.\n\n‘How far is it to Dingley Dell?’\n\n‘Better er seven mile.’\n\n‘Is it a good road?’\n\n‘No, ‘tain’t.’ Having uttered this brief reply, and apparently satisfied himself with another scrutiny, the red-headed man resumed his work. ‘We want to put this horse up here,’ said Mr. Pickwick; ‘I suppose we can, can’t we?’\n\nWant to put that ere horse up, do ee?’ repeated the red-headed man, leaning on his spade.\n\n‘Of course,’ replied Mr. Pickwick, who had by this time advanced, horse in hand, to the garden rails.\n\n‘Missus’--roared the man with the red head, emerging from the garden, and looking very hard at the horse--‘missus!’\n\nA tall, bony woman--straight all the way down--in a coarse, blue pelisse, with the waist an inch or two below her arm-pits, responded to the call.\n\n‘Can we put this horse up here, my good woman?’ said Mr. Tupman, advancing, and speaking in his most seductive tones. The woman looked very hard at the whole party; and the red-headed man whispered something in her ear.\n\n‘No,’ replied the woman, after a little consideration, ‘I’m afeerd on it.’\n\n‘Afraid!’ exclaimed Mr. Pickwick, ‘what’s the woman afraid of?’\n\n‘It got us in trouble last time,’ said the woman, turning into the house; ‘I woan’t have nothin’ to say to ‘un.’\n\n‘Most extraordinary thing I have ever met with in my life,’ said the astonished Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘I--I--really believe,’ whispered Mr. Winkle, as his friends gathered round him, ‘that they think we have come by this horse in some dishonest manner.’\n\n‘What!’ exclaimed Mr. Pickwick, in a storm of indignation. Mr. Winkle modestly repeated his suggestion.\n\n‘Hollo, you fellow,’ said the angry Mr. Pickwick, ‘do you think we stole the horse?’\n\n‘I’m sure ye did,’ replied the red-headed man, with a grin which agitated his countenance from one auricular organ to the other. Saying which he turned into the house and banged the door after him.\n\n‘It’s like a dream,’ ejaculated Mr. Pickwick, ‘a hideous dream. The idea of a man’s walking about all day with a dreadful horse that he can’t get rid of!’ The depressed Pickwickians turned moodily away, with the tall quadruped, for which they all felt the most unmitigated disgust, following slowly at their heels.\n\nIt was late in the afternoon when the four friends and their four-footed companion turned into the lane leading to Manor Farm; and even when they were so near their place of destination, the pleasure they would otherwise have experienced was materially damped as they reflected on the singularity of their appearance, and the absurdity of their situation. Torn clothes, lacerated faces, dusty shoes, exhausted looks, and, above all, the horse. Oh, how Mr. Pickwick cursed that horse: he had eyed the noble animal from time to time with looks expressive of hatred and revenge; more than once he had calculated the probable amount of the expense he would incur by cutting his throat; and now the temptation to destroy him, or to cast him loose upon the world, rushed upon his mind with tenfold force. He was roused from a meditation on these dire imaginings by the sudden appearance of two figures at a turn of the lane. It was Mr. Wardle, and his faithful attendant, the fat boy.\n\n‘Why, where have you been?’ said the hospitable old gentleman; ‘I’ve been waiting for you all day. Well, you do look tired. What! Scratches! Not hurt, I hope--eh? Well, I am glad to hear that--very. So you’ve been spilt, eh? Never mind. Common accident in these parts. Joe--he’s asleep again!--Joe, take that horse from the gentlemen, and lead it into the stable.’\n\nThe fat boy sauntered heavily behind them with the animal; and the old gentleman, condoling with his guests in homely phrase on so much of the day’s adventures as they thought proper to communicate, led the way to the kitchen.\n\n‘We’ll have you put to rights here,’ said the old gentleman, ‘and then I’ll introduce you to the people in the parlour. Emma, bring out the cherry brandy; now, Jane, a needle and thread here; towels and water, Mary. Come, girls, bustle about.’\n\nThree or four buxom girls speedily dispersed in search of the different articles in requisition, while a couple of large-headed, circular- visaged males rose from their seats in the chimney-corner (for although it was a May evening their attachment to the wood fire appeared as cordial as if it were Christmas), and dived into some obscure recesses, from which they speedily produced a bottle of blacking, and some half- dozen brushes.\n\n‘Bustle!’ said the old gentleman again, but the admonition was quite unnecessary, for one of the girls poured out the cherry brandy, and another brought in the towels, and one of the men suddenly seizing Mr. Pickwick by the leg, at imminent hazard of throwing him off his balance, brushed away at his boot till his corns were red-hot; while the other shampooed Mr. Winkle with a heavy clothes-brush, indulging, during the operation, in that hissing sound which hostlers are wont to produce when engaged in rubbing down a horse.\n\nMr. Snodgrass, having concluded his ablutions, took a survey of the room, while standing with his back to the fire, sipping his cherry brandy with heartfelt satisfaction. He describes it as a large apartment, with a red brick floor and a capacious chimney; the ceiling garnished with hams, sides of bacon, and ropes of onions. The walls were decorated with several hunting-whips, two or three bridles, a saddle, and an old rusty blunderbuss, with an inscription below it, intimating that it was ‘Loaded’--as it had been, on the same authority, for half a century at least. An old eight-day clock, of solemn and sedate demeanour, ticked gravely in one corner; and a silver watch, of equal antiquity, dangled from one of the many hooks which ornamented the dresser.\n\n‘Ready?’ said the old gentleman inquiringly, when his guests had been washed, mended, brushed, and brandied.\n\n‘Quite,’ replied Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Come along, then;’ and the party having traversed several dark passages, and being joined by Mr. Tupman, who had lingered behind to snatch a kiss from Emma, for which he had been duly rewarded with sundry pushings and scratchings, arrived at the parlour door.\n\n‘Welcome,’ said their hospitable host, throwing it open and stepping forward to announce them, ‘welcome, gentlemen, to Manor Farm.’\n\nCHAPTER VI. AN OLD-FASHIONED CARD-PARTY--THE CLERGYMAN’S VERSES--THE STORY OF THE CONVICT’S RETURN\nSeveral guests who were assembled in the old parlour rose to greet Mr. Pickwick and his friends upon their entrance; and during the performance of the ceremony of introduction, with all due formalities, Mr. Pickwick had leisure to observe the appearance, and speculate upon the characters and pursuits, of the persons by whom he was surrounded--a habit in which he, in common with many other great men, delighted to indulge.\n\nA very old lady, in a lofty cap and faded silk gown--no less a personage than Mr. Wardle’s mother--occupied the post of honour on the right-hand corner of the chimney-piece; and various certificates of her having been brought up in the way she should go when young, and of her not having departed from it when old, ornamented the walls, in the form of samplers of ancient date, worsted landscapes of equal antiquity, and crimson silk tea-kettle holders of a more modern period. The aunt, the two young ladies, and Mr. Wardle, each vying with the other in paying zealous and unremitting attentions to the old lady, crowded round her easy-chair, one holding her ear-trumpet, another an orange, and a third a smelling- bottle, while a fourth was busily engaged in patting and punching the pillows which were arranged for her support. On the opposite side sat a bald-headed old gentleman, with a good-humoured, benevolent face--the clergyman of Dingley Dell; and next him sat his wife, a stout, blooming old lady, who looked as if she were well skilled, not only in the art and mystery of manufacturing home-made cordials greatly to other people’s satisfaction, but of tasting them occasionally very much to her own. A little hard-headed, Ripstone pippin-faced man, was conversing with a fat old gentleman in one corner; and two or three more old gentlemen, and two or three more old ladies, sat bolt upright and motionless on their chairs, staring very hard at Mr. Pickwick and his fellow-voyagers.\n\n‘Mr. Pickwick, mother,’ said Mr. Wardle, at the very top of his voice.\n\n‘Ah!’ said the old lady, shaking her head; ‘I can’t hear you.’\n\n‘Mr. Pickwick, grandma!’ screamed both the young ladies together.\n\n‘Ah!’ exclaimed the old lady. ‘Well, it don’t much matter. He don’t care for an old ‘ooman like me, I dare say.’\n\n‘I assure you, ma’am,’ said Mr. Pickwick, grasping the old lady’s hand, and speaking so loud that the exertion imparted a crimson hue to his benevolent countenance--‘I assure you, ma’am, that nothing delights me more than to see a lady of your time of life heading so fine a family, and looking so young and well.’\n\n‘Ah!’ said the old lady, after a short pause: ‘it’s all very fine, I dare say; but I can’t hear him.’\n\n‘Grandma’s rather put out now,’ said Miss Isabella Wardle, in a low tone; ‘but she’ll talk to you presently.’\n\nMr. Pickwick nodded his readiness to humour the infirmities of age, and entered into a general conversation with the other members of the circle.\n\n‘Delightful situation this,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Delightful!’ echoed Messrs. Snodgrass, Tupman, and Winkle.\n\n‘Well, I think it is,’ said Mr. Wardle.\n\n‘There ain’t a better spot o’ ground in all Kent, sir,’ said the hard- headed man with the pippin--face; ‘there ain’t indeed, sir--I’m sure there ain’t, Sir.’ The hard-headed man looked triumphantly round, as if he had been very much contradicted by somebody, but had got the better of him at last.\n\n‘There ain’t a better spot o’ ground in all Kent,’ said the hard-headed man again, after a pause.\n\n‘’Cept Mullins’s Meadows,’ observed the fat man solemnly.\n\n‘Mullins’s Meadows!’ ejaculated the other, with profound contempt.\n\n‘Ah, Mullins’s Meadows,’ repeated the fat man.\n\n‘Reg’lar good land that,’ interposed another fat man.\n\n‘And so it is, sure-ly,’ said a third fat man.\n\n‘Everybody knows that,’ said the corpulent host.\n\nThe hard-headed man looked dubiously round, but finding himself in a minority, assumed a compassionate air and said no more.\n\n‘What are they talking about?’ inquired the old lady of one of her granddaughters, in a very audible voice; for, like many deaf people, she never seemed to calculate on the possibility of other persons hearing what she said herself.\n\n‘About the land, grandma.’\n\n‘What about the land?--Nothing the matter, is there?’\n\n‘No, no. Mr. Miller was saying our land was better than Mullins’s Meadows.’\n\n‘How should he know anything about it?’ inquired the old lady indignantly. ‘Miller’s a conceited coxcomb, and you may tell him I said so.’ Saying which, the old lady, quite unconscious that she had spoken above a whisper, drew herself up, and looked carving-knives at the hard- headed delinquent.\n\n‘Come, come,’ said the bustling host, with a natural anxiety to change the conversation, ‘what say you to a rubber, Mr. Pickwick?’\n\n‘I should like it of all things,’ replied that gentleman; ‘but pray don’t make up one on my account.’\n\n‘Oh, I assure you, mother’s very fond of a rubber,’ said Mr. Wardle; ‘ain’t you, mother?’\n\nThe old lady, who was much less deaf on this subject than on any other, replied in the affirmative.\n\n‘Joe, Joe!’ said the gentleman; ‘Joe--damn that--oh, here he is; put out the card-tables.’\n\nThe lethargic youth contrived without any additional rousing to set out two card-tables; the one for Pope Joan, and the other for whist. The whist-players were Mr. Pickwick and the old lady, Mr. Miller and the fat gentleman. The round game comprised the rest of the company.\n\nThe rubber was conducted with all that gravity of deportment and sedateness of demeanour which befit the pursuit entitled ‘whist’--a solemn observance, to which, as it appears to us, the title of ‘game’ has been very irreverently and ignominiously applied. The round-game table, on the other hand, was so boisterously merry as materially to interrupt the contemplations of Mr. Miller, who, not being quite so much absorbed as he ought to have been, contrived to commit various high crimes and misdemeanours, which excited the wrath of the fat gentleman to a very great extent, and called forth the good-humour of the old lady in a proportionate degree.\n\n‘There!’ said the criminal Miller triumphantly, as he took up the odd trick at the conclusion of a hand; ‘that could not have been played better, I flatter myself; impossible to have made another trick!’\n\n‘Miller ought to have trumped the diamond, oughtn’t he, Sir?’ said the old lady.\n\nMr. Pickwick nodded assent.\n\n‘Ought I, though?’ said the unfortunate, with a doubtful appeal to his partner.\n\n‘You ought, Sir,’ said the fat gentleman, in an awful voice.\n\n‘Very sorry,’ said the crestfallen Miller.\n\n‘Much use that,’ growled the fat gentleman.\n\n‘Two by honours--makes us eight,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Another hand. ‘Can you one?’ inquired the old lady.\n\n‘I can,’ replied Mr. Pickwick. ‘Double, single, and the rub.’\n\n‘Never was such luck,’ said Mr. Miller.\n\n‘Never was such cards,’ said the fat gentleman.\n\nA solemn silence; Mr. Pickwick humorous, the old lady serious, the fat gentleman captious, and Mr. Miller timorous.\n\n‘Another double,’ said the old lady, triumphantly making a memorandum of the circumstance, by placing one sixpence and a battered halfpenny under the candlestick.\n\n‘A double, sir,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Quite aware of the fact, Sir,’ replied the fat gentleman sharply.\n\nAnother game, with a similar result, was followed by a revoke from the unlucky Miller; on which the fat gentleman burst into a state of high personal excitement which lasted until the conclusion of the game, when he retired into a corner, and remained perfectly mute for one hour and twenty-seven minutes; at the end of which time he emerged from his retirement, and offered Mr. Pickwick a pinch of snuff with the air of a man who had made up his mind to a Christian forgiveness of injuries sustained. The old lady’s hearing decidedly improved and the unlucky Miller felt as much out of his element as a dolphin in a sentry-box.\n\nMeanwhile the round game proceeded right merrily. Isabella Wardle and Mr. Trundle ‘went partners,’ and Emily Wardle and Mr. Snodgrass did the same; and even Mr. Tupman and the spinster aunt established a joint- stock company of fish and flattery. Old Mr. Wardle was in the very height of his jollity; and he was so funny in his management of the board, and the old ladies were so sharp after their winnings, that the whole table was in a perpetual roar of merriment and laughter. There was one old lady who always had about half a dozen cards to pay for, at which everybody laughed, regularly every round; and when the old lady looked cross at having to pay, they laughed louder than ever; on which the old lady’s face gradually brightened up, till at last she laughed louder than any of them, Then, when the spinster aunt got ‘matrimony,’ the young ladies laughed afresh, and the Spinster aunt seemed disposed to be pettish; till, feeling Mr. Tupman squeezing her hand under the table, she brightened up too, and looked rather knowing, as if matrimony in reality were not quite so far off as some people thought for; whereupon everybody laughed again, and especially old Mr. Wardle, who enjoyed a joke as much as the youngest. As to Mr. Snodgrass, he did nothing but whisper poetical sentiments into his partner’s ear, which made one old gentleman facetiously sly, about partnerships at cards and partnerships for life, and caused the aforesaid old gentleman to make some remarks thereupon, accompanied with divers winks and chuckles, which made the company very merry and the old gentleman’s wife especially so. And Mr. Winkle came out with jokes which are very well known in town, but are not all known in the country; and as everybody laughed at them very heartily, and said they were very capital, Mr. Winkle was in a state of great honour and glory. And the benevolent clergyman looked pleasantly on; for the happy faces which surrounded the table made the good old man feel happy too; and though the merriment was rather boisterous, still it came from the heart and not from the lips; and this is the right sort of merriment, after all.\n\nThe evening glided swiftly away, in these cheerful recreations; and when the substantial though homely supper had been despatched, and the little party formed a social circle round the fire, Mr. Pickwick thought he had never felt so happy in his life, and at no time so much disposed to enjoy, and make the most of, the passing moment.\n\n‘Now this,’ said the hospitable host, who was sitting in great state next the old lady’s arm-chair, with her hand fast clasped in his--‘this is just what I like--the happiest moments of my life have been passed at this old fireside; and I am so attached to it, that I keep up a blazing fire here every evening, until it actually grows too hot to bear it. Why, my poor old mother, here, used to sit before this fireplace upon that little stool when she was a girl; didn’t you, mother?’\n\nThe tear which starts unbidden to the eye when the recollection of old times and the happiness of many years ago is suddenly recalled, stole down the old lady’s face as she shook her head with a melancholy smile.\n\n‘You must excuse my talking about this old place, Mr. Pickwick,’ resumed the host, after a short pause, ‘for I love it dearly, and know no other- -the old houses and fields seem like living friends to me; and so does our little church with the ivy, about which, by the bye, our excellent friend there made a song when he first came amongst us. Mr. Snodgrass, have you anything in your glass?’\n\n‘Plenty, thank you,’ replied that gentleman, whose poetic curiosity had been greatly excited by the last observation of his entertainer. ‘I beg your pardon, but you were talking about the song of the Ivy.’\n\n‘You must ask our friend opposite about that,’ said the host knowingly, indicating the clergyman by a nod of his head.\n\n‘May I say that I should like to hear you repeat it, sir?’ said Mr. Snodgrass.\n\n‘Why, really,’ replied the clergyman, ‘it’s a very slight affair; and the only excuse I have for having ever perpetrated it is, that I was a young man at the time. Such as it is, however, you shall hear it, if you wish.’\n\nA murmur of curiosity was of course the reply; and the old gentleman proceeded to recite, with the aid of sundry promptings from his wife, the lines in question. ‘I call them,’ said he,\n\n# THE IVY GREEN\n\nOh, a dainty plant is the Ivy green, That creepeth o’er ruins old! Of right choice food are his meals, I ween, In his cell so lone and cold. The wall must be crumbled, the stone decayed, To pleasure his dainty whim; And the mouldering dust that years have made, Is a merry meal for him. Creeping where no life is seen, A rare old plant is the Ivy green.\n\nFast he stealeth on, though he wears no wings, And a staunch old heart has he. How closely he twineth, how tight he clings To his friend the huge Oak Tree! And slily he traileth along the ground, And his leaves he gently waves, As he joyously hugs and crawleth round The rich mould of dead men’s graves. Creeping where grim death has been, A rare old plant is the Ivy green.\n\nWhole ages have fled and their works decayed, And nations have scattered been; But the stout old Ivy shall never fade, From its hale and hearty green. The brave old plant in its lonely days, Shall fatten upon the past; For the stateliest building man can raise, Is the Ivy’s food at last. Creeping on where time has been, A rare old plant is the Ivy green.\n\nWhile the old gentleman repeated these lines a second time, to enable Mr. Snodgrass to note them down, Mr. Pickwick perused the lineaments of his face with an expression of great interest. The old gentleman having concluded his dictation, and Mr. Snodgrass having returned his note-book to his pocket, Mr. Pickwick said--\n\n‘Excuse me, sir, for making the remark on so short an acquaintance; but a gentleman like yourself cannot fail, I should think, to have observed many scenes and incidents worth recording, in the course of your experience as a minister of the Gospel.’\n\n‘I have witnessed some certainly,’ replied the old gentleman, ‘but the incidents and characters have been of a homely and ordinary nature, my sphere of action being so very limited.’\n\n‘You did make some notes, I think, about John Edmunds, did you not?’ inquired Mr. Wardle, who appeared very desirous to draw his friend out, for the edification of his new visitors.\n\nThe old gentleman slightly nodded his head in token of assent, and was proceeding to change the subject, when Mr. Pickwick said--\n\n‘I beg your pardon, sir, but pray, if I may venture to inquire, who was John Edmunds?’\n\n‘The very thing I was about to ask,’ said Mr. Snodgrass eagerly.\n\n‘You are fairly in for it,’ said the jolly host. ‘You must satisfy the curiosity of these gentlemen, sooner or later; so you had better take advantage of this favourable opportunity, and do so at once.’\n\nThe old gentleman smiled good-humouredly as he drew his chair forward-- the remainder of the party drew their chairs closer together, especially Mr. Tupman and the spinster aunt, who were possibly rather hard of hearing; and the old lady’s ear-trumpet having been duly adjusted, and Mr. Miller (who had fallen asleep during the recital of the verses) roused from his slumbers by an admonitory pinch, administered beneath the table by his ex-partner the solemn fat man, the old gentleman, without further preface, commenced the following tale, to which we have taken the liberty of prefixing the title of\n\n# THE CONVICT’S RETURN\n\n‘When I first settled in this village,’ said the old gentleman, ‘which is now just five-and-twenty years ago, the most notorious person among my parishioners was a man of the name of Edmunds, who leased a small farm near this spot. He was a morose, savage-hearted, bad man; idle and dissolute in his habits; cruel and ferocious in his disposition. Beyond the few lazy and reckless vagabonds with whom he sauntered away his time in the fields, or sotted in the ale-house, he had not a single friend or acquaintance; no one cared to speak to the man whom many feared, and every one detested--and Edmunds was shunned by all.\n\n‘This man had a wife and one son, who, when I first came here, was about twelve years old. Of the acuteness of that woman’s sufferings, of the gentle and enduring manner in which she bore them, of the agony of solicitude with which she reared that boy, no one can form an adequate conception. Heaven forgive me the supposition, if it be an uncharitable one, but I do firmly and in my soul believe, that the man systematically tried for many years to break her heart; but she bore it all for her child’s sake, and, however strange it may seem to many, for his father’s too; for brute as he was, and cruelly as he had treated her, she had loved him once; and the recollection of what he had been to her, awakened feelings of forbearance and meekness under suffering in her bosom, to which all God’s creatures, but women, are strangers.\n\n‘They were poor--they could not be otherwise when the man pursued such courses; but the woman’s unceasing and unwearied exertions, early and late, morning, noon, and night, kept them above actual want. These exertions were but ill repaid. People who passed the spot in the evening--sometimes at a late hour of the night--reported that they had heard the moans and sobs of a woman in distress, and the sound of blows; and more than once, when it was past midnight, the boy knocked softly at the door of a neighbour’s house, whither he had been sent, to escape the drunken fury of his unnatural father.\n\n‘During the whole of this time, and when the poor creature often bore about her marks of ill-usage and violence which she could not wholly conceal, she was a constant attendant at our little church. Regularly every Sunday, morning and afternoon, she occupied the same seat with the boy at her side; and though they were both poorly dressed--much more so than many of their neighbours who were in a lower station--they were always neat and clean. Every one had a friendly nod and a kind word for “poor Mrs. Edmunds”; and sometimes, when she stopped to exchange a few words with a neighbour at the conclusion of the service in the little row of elm-trees which leads to the church porch, or lingered behind to gaze with a mother’s pride and fondness upon her healthy boy, as he sported before her with some little companions, her careworn face would lighten up with an expression of heartfelt gratitude; and she would look, if not cheerful and happy, at least tranquil and contented.\n\n‘Five or six years passed away; the boy had become a robust and well- grown youth. The time that had strengthened the child’s slight frame and knit his weak limbs into the strength of manhood had bowed his mother’s form, and enfeebled her steps; but the arm that should have supported her was no longer locked in hers; the face that should have cheered her, no more looked upon her own. She occupied her old seat, but there was a vacant one beside her. The Bible was kept as carefully as ever, the places were found and folded down as they used to be: but there was no one to read it with her; and the tears fell thick and fast upon the book, and blotted the words from her eyes. Neighbours were as kind as they were wont to be of old, but she shunned their greetings with averted head. There was no lingering among the old elm-trees now--no cheering anticipations of happiness yet in store. The desolate woman drew her bonnet closer over her face, and walked hurriedly away.\n\n‘Shall I tell you that the young man, who, looking back to the earliest of his childhood’s days to which memory and consciousness extended, and carrying his recollection down to that moment, could remember nothing which was not in some way connected with a long series of voluntary privations suffered by his mother for his sake, with ill-usage, and insult, and violence, and all endured for him--shall I tell you, that he, with a reckless disregard for her breaking heart, and a sullen, wilful forgetfulness of all she had done and borne for him, had linked himself with depraved and abandoned men, and was madly pursuing a headlong career, which must bring death to him, and shame to her? Alas for human nature! You have anticipated it long since.\n\n‘The measure of the unhappy woman’s misery and misfortune was about to be completed. Numerous offences had been committed in the neighbourhood; the perpetrators remained undiscovered, and their boldness increased. A robbery of a daring and aggravated nature occasioned a vigilance of pursuit, and a strictness of search, they had not calculated on. Young Edmunds was suspected, with three companions. He was apprehended-- committed--tried--condemned--to die.\n\n‘The wild and piercing shriek from a woman’s voice, which resounded through the court when the solemn sentence was pronounced, rings in my ears at this moment. That cry struck a terror to the culprit’s heart, which trial, condemnation--the approach of death itself, had failed to awaken. The lips which had been compressed in dogged sullenness throughout, quivered and parted involuntarily; the face turned ashy pale as the cold perspiration broke forth from every pore; the sturdy limbs of the felon trembled, and he staggered in the dock.\n\n‘In the first transports of her mental anguish, the suffering mother threw herself on her knees at my feet, and fervently sought the Almighty Being who had hitherto supported her in all her troubles to release her from a world of woe and misery, and to spare the life of her only child. A burst of grief, and a violent struggle, such as I hope I may never have to witness again, succeeded. I knew that her heart was breaking from that hour; but I never once heard complaint or murmur escape her lips.\n\n‘It was a piteous spectacle to see that woman in the prison-yard from day to day, eagerly and fervently attempting, by affection and entreaty, to soften the hard heart of her obdurate son. It was in vain. He remained moody, obstinate, and unmoved. Not even the unlooked-for commutation of his sentence to transportation for fourteen years, softened for an instant the sullen hardihood of his demeanour.\n\n‘But the spirit of resignation and endurance that had so long upheld her, was unable to contend against bodily weakness and infirmity. She fell sick. She dragged her tottering limbs from the bed to visit her son once more, but her strength failed her, and she sank powerless on the ground.\n\n‘And now the boasted coldness and indifference of the young man were tested indeed; and the retribution that fell heavily upon him nearly drove him mad. A day passed away and his mother was not there; another flew by, and she came not near him; a third evening arrived, and yet he had not seen her--, and in four-and-twenty hours he was to be separated from her, perhaps for ever. Oh! how the long-forgotten thoughts of former days rushed upon his mind, as he almost ran up and down the narrow yard--as if intelligence would arrive the sooner for his hurrying--and how bitterly a sense of his helplessness and desolation rushed upon him, when he heard the truth! His mother, the only parent he had ever known, lay ill--it might be, dying--within one mile of the ground he stood on; were he free and unfettered, a few minutes would place him by her side. He rushed to the gate, and grasping the iron rails with the energy of desperation, shook it till it rang again, and threw himself against the thick wall as if to force a passage through the stone; but the strong building mocked his feeble efforts, and he beat his hands together and wept like a child.\n\n‘I bore the mother’s forgiveness and blessing to her son in prison; and I carried the solemn assurance of repentance, and his fervent supplication for pardon, to her sick-bed. I heard, with pity and compassion, the repentant man devise a thousand little plans for her comfort and support when he returned; but I knew that many months before he could reach his place of destination, his mother would be no longer of this world.\n\n‘He was removed by night. A few weeks afterwards the poor woman’s soul took its flight, I confidently hope, and solemnly believe, to a place of eternal happiness and rest. I performed the burial service over her remains. She lies in our little churchyard. There is no stone at her grave’s head. Her sorrows were known to man; her virtues to God.\n\n‘It had been arranged previously to the convict’s departure, that he should write to his mother as soon as he could obtain permission, and that the letter should be addressed to me. The father had positively refused to see his son from the moment of his apprehension; and it was a matter of indifference to him whether he lived or died. Many years passed over without any intelligence of him; and when more than half his term of transportation had expired, and I had received no letter, I concluded him to be dead, as, indeed, I almost hoped he might be.\n\n‘Edmunds, however, had been sent a considerable distance up the country on his arrival at the settlement; and to this circumstance, perhaps, may be attributed the fact, that though several letters were despatched, none of them ever reached my hands. He remained in the same place during the whole fourteen years. At the expiration of the term, steadily adhering to his old resolution and the pledge he gave his mother, he made his way back to England amidst innumerable difficulties, and returned, on foot, to his native place.\n\n‘On a fine Sunday evening, in the month of August, John Edmunds set foot in the village he had left with shame and disgrace seventeen years before. His nearest way lay through the churchyard. The man’s heart swelled as he crossed the stile. The tall old elms, through whose branches the declining sun cast here and there a rich ray of light upon the shady part, awakened the associations of his earliest days. He pictured himself as he was then, clinging to his mother’s hand, and walking peacefully to church. He remembered how he used to look up into her pale face; and how her eyes would sometimes fill with tears as she gazed upon his features--tears which fell hot upon his forehead as she stooped to kiss him, and made him weep too, although he little knew then what bitter tears hers were. He thought how often he had run merrily down that path with some childish playfellow, looking back, ever and again, to catch his mother’s smile, or hear her gentle voice; and then a veil seemed lifted from his memory, and words of kindness unrequited, and warnings despised, and promises broken, thronged upon his recollection till his heart failed him, and he could bear it no longer.\n\n‘He entered the church. The evening service was concluded and the congregation had dispersed, but it was not yet closed. His steps echoed through the low building with a hollow sound, and he almost feared to be alone, it was so still and quiet. He looked round him. Nothing was changed. The place seemed smaller than it used to be; but there were the old monuments on which he had gazed with childish awe a thousand times; the little pulpit with its faded cushion; the Communion table before which he had so often repeated the Commandments he had reverenced as a child, and forgotten as a man. He approached the old seat; it looked cold and desolate. The cushion had been removed, and the Bible was not there. Perhaps his mother now occupied a poorer seat, or possibly she had grown infirm and could not reach the church alone. He dared not think of what he feared. A cold feeling crept over him, and he trembled violently as he turned away. ‘An old man entered the porch just as he reached it. Edmunds started back, for he knew him well; many a time he had watched him digging graves in the churchyard. What would he say to the returned convict?\n\n‘The old man raised his eyes to the stranger’s face, bade him “good- evening,” and walked slowly on. He had forgotten him.\n\n‘He walked down the hill, and through the village. The weather was warm, and the people were sitting at their doors, or strolling in their little gardens as he passed, enjoying the serenity of the evening, and their rest from labour. Many a look was turned towards him, and many a doubtful glance he cast on either side to see whether any knew and shunned him. There were strange faces in almost every house; in some he recognised the burly form of some old schoolfellow--a boy when he last saw him--surrounded by a troop of merry children; in others he saw, seated in an easy-chair at a cottage door, a feeble and infirm old man, whom he only remembered as a hale and hearty labourer; but they had all forgotten him, and he passed on unknown.\n\n‘The last soft light of the setting sun had fallen on the earth, casting a rich glow on the yellow corn sheaves, and lengthening the shadows of the orchard trees, as he stood before the old house--the home of his infancy--to which his heart had yearned with an intensity of affection not to be described, through long and weary years of captivity and sorrow. The paling was low, though he well remembered the time that it had seemed a high wall to him; and he looked over into the old garden. There were more seeds and gayer flowers than there used to be, but there were the old trees still--the very tree under which he had lain a thousand times when tired of playing in the sun, and felt the soft, mild sleep of happy boyhood steal gently upon him. There were voices within the house. He listened, but they fell strangely upon his ear; he knew them not. They were merry too; and he well knew that his poor old mother could not be cheerful, and he away. The door opened, and a group of little children bounded out, shouting and romping. The father, with a little boy in his arms, appeared at the door, and they crowded round him, clapping their tiny hands, and dragging him out, to join their joyous sports. The convict thought on the many times he had shrunk from his father’s sight in that very place. He remembered how often he had buried his trembling head beneath the bedclothes, and heard the harsh word, and the hard stripe, and his mother’s wailing; and though the man sobbed aloud with agony of mind as he left the spot, his fist was clenched, and his teeth were set, in a fierce and deadly passion.\n\n‘And such was the return to which he had looked through the weary perspective of many years, and for which he had undergone so much suffering! No face of welcome, no look of forgiveness, no house to receive, no hand to help him--and this too in the old village. What was his loneliness in the wild, thick woods, where man was never seen, to this!\n\n‘He felt that in the distant land of his bondage and infamy, he had thought of his native place as it was when he left it; and not as it would be when he returned. The sad reality struck coldly at his heart, and his spirit sank within him. He had not courage to make inquiries, or to present himself to the only person who was likely to receive him with kindness and compassion. He walked slowly on; and shunning the roadside like a guilty man, turned into a meadow he well remembered; and covering his face with his hands, threw himself upon the grass.\n\n‘He had not observed that a man was lying on the bank beside him; his garments rustled as he turned round to steal a look at the new-comer; and Edmunds raised his head.\n\n‘The man had moved into a sitting posture. His body was much bent, and his face was wrinkled and yellow. His dress denoted him an inmate of the workhouse: he had the appearance of being very old, but it looked more the effect of dissipation or disease, than the length of years. He was staring hard at the stranger, and though his eyes were lustreless and heavy at first, they appeared to glow with an unnatural and alarmed expression after they had been fixed upon him for a short time, until they seemed to be starting from their sockets. Edmunds gradually raised himself to his knees, and looked more and more earnestly on the old man’s face. They gazed upon each other in silence.\n\n‘The old man was ghastly pale. He shuddered and tottered to his feet. Edmunds sprang to his. He stepped back a pace or two. Edmunds advanced.\n\n‘“Let me hear you speak,” said the convict, in a thick, broken voice.\n\n‘“Stand off!” cried the old man, with a dreadful oath. The convict drew closer to him.\n\n‘“Stand off!” shrieked the old man. Furious with terror, he raised his stick, and struck Edmunds a heavy blow across the face.\n\n‘“Father--devil!” murmured the convict between his set teeth. He rushed wildly forward, and clenched the old man by the throat--but he was his father; and his arm fell powerless by his side.\n\n‘The old man uttered a loud yell which rang through the lonely fields like the howl of an evil spirit. His face turned black, the gore rushed from his mouth and nose, and dyed the grass a deep, dark red, as he staggered and fell. He had ruptured a blood-vessel, and he was a dead man before his son could raise him.\n\n‘In that corner of the churchyard,’ said the old gentleman, after a silence of a few moments, ‘in that corner of the churchyard of which I have before spoken, there lies buried a man who was in my employment for three years after this event, and who was truly contrite, penitent, and humbled, if ever man was. No one save myself knew in that man’s lifetime who he was, or whence he came--it was John Edmunds, the returned convict.’\n\nCHAPTER VII. HOW MR. WINKLE, INSTEAD OF SHOOTING AT THE PIGEON AND KILLING THE CROW, SHOT AT THE CROW AND WOUNDED THE PIGEON; HOW THE DINGLEY DELL CRICKET CLUB PLAYED ALL-MUGGLETON, AND HOW ALL-MUGGLETON DINED AT THE DINGLEY DELL EXPENSE; WITH OTHER INTERESTING AND INSTRUCTIVE MATTERS\nThe fatiguing adventures of the day or the somniferous influence of the clergyman’s tale operated so strongly on the drowsy tendencies of Mr. Pickwick, that in less than five minutes after he had been shown to his comfortable bedroom he fell into a sound and dreamless sleep, from which he was only awakened by the morning sun darting his bright beams reproachfully into the apartment. Mr. Pickwick was no sluggard, and he sprang like an ardent warrior from his tent-bedstead.\n\n‘Pleasant, pleasant country,’ sighed the enthusiastic gentleman, as he opened his lattice window. ‘Who could live to gaze from day to day on bricks and slates who had once felt the influence of a scene like this? Who could continue to exist where there are no cows but the cows on the chimney-pots; nothing redolent of Pan but pan-tiles; no crop but stone crop? Who could bear to drag out a life in such a spot? Who, I ask, could endure it?’ and, having cross-examined solitude after the most approved precedents, at considerable length, Mr. Pickwick thrust his head out of the lattice and looked around him.\n\nThe rich, sweet smell of the hay-ricks rose to his chamber window; the hundred perfumes of the little flower-garden beneath scented the air around; the deep-green meadows shone in the morning dew that glistened on every leaf as it trembled in the gentle air; and the birds sang as if every sparkling drop were to them a fountain of inspiration. Mr. Pickwick fell into an enchanting and delicious reverie.\n\n‘Hollo!’ was the sound that roused him.\n\nHe looked to the right, but he saw nobody; his eyes wandered to the left, and pierced the prospect; he stared into the sky, but he wasn’t wanted there; and then he did what a common mind would have done at once--looked into the garden, and there saw Mr. Wardle.\n\n‘How are you?’ said the good-humoured individual, out of breath with his own anticipations of pleasure.’Beautiful morning, ain’t it? Glad to see you up so early. Make haste down, and come out. I’ll wait for you here.’\n\nMr. Pickwick needed no second invitation. Ten minutes sufficed for the completion of his toilet, and at the expiration of that time he was by the old gentleman’s side.\n\n‘Hollo!’ said Mr. Pickwick in his turn, seeing that his companion was armed with a gun, and that another lay ready on the grass; ‘what’s going forward?’\n\n‘Why, your friend and I,’ replied the host, ‘are going out rook-shooting before breakfast. He’s a very good shot, ain’t he?’\n\n‘I’ve heard him say he’s a capital one,’ replied Mr. Pickwick, ‘but I never saw him aim at anything.’\n\n‘Well,’ said the host, ‘I wish he’d come. Joe--Joe!’\n\nThe fat boy, who under the exciting influence of the morning did not appear to be more than three parts and a fraction asleep, emerged from the house.\n\n‘Go up, and call the gentleman, and tell him he’ll find me and Mr. Pickwick in the rookery. Show the gentleman the way there; d’ye hear?’\n\nThe boy departed to execute his commission; and the host, carrying both guns like a second Robinson Crusoe, led the way from the garden.\n\n‘This is the place,’ said the old gentleman, pausing after a few minutes walking, in an avenue of trees. The information was unnecessary; for the incessant cawing of the unconscious rooks sufficiently indicated their whereabouts.\n\nThe old gentleman laid one gun on the ground, and loaded the other.\n\n‘Here they are,’ said Mr. Pickwick; and, as he spoke, the forms of Mr. Tupman, Mr. Snodgrass, and Mr. Winkle appeared in the distance. The fat boy, not being quite certain which gentleman he was directed to call, had with peculiar sagacity, and to prevent the possibility of any mistake, called them all.\n\n‘Come along,’ shouted the old gentleman, addressing Mr. Winkle; ‘a keen hand like you ought to have been up long ago, even to such poor work as this.’\n\nMr. Winkle responded with a forced smile, and took up the spare gun with an expression of countenance which a metaphysical rook, impressed with a foreboding of his approaching death by violence, may be supposed to assume. It might have been keenness, but it looked remarkably like misery.\n\nThe old gentleman nodded; and two ragged boys who had been marshalled to the spot under the direction of the infant Lambert, forthwith commenced climbing up two of the trees.\n\n‘What are these lads for?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick abruptly. He was rather alarmed; for he was not quite certain but that the distress of the agricultural interest, about which he had often heard a great deal, might have compelled the small boys attached to the soil to earn a precarious and hazardous subsistence by making marks of themselves for inexperienced sportsmen.\n\n‘Only to start the game,’ replied Mr. Wardle, laughing.\n\n‘To what?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Why, in plain English, to frighten the rooks.’\n\n‘Oh, is that all?’\n\n‘You are satisfied?’\n\n‘Quite.’\n\n‘Very well. Shall I begin?’\n\n‘If you please,’ said Mr. Winkle, glad of any respite.\n\n‘Stand aside, then. Now for it.’\n\nThe boy shouted, and shook a branch with a nest on it. Half a dozen young rooks in violent conversation, flew out to ask what the matter was. The old gentleman fired by way of reply. Down fell one bird, and off flew the others.\n\n‘Take him up, Joe,’ said the old gentleman.\n\nThere was a smile upon the youth’s face as he advanced. Indistinct visions of rook-pie floated through his imagination. He laughed as he retired with the bird--it was a plump one.\n\n‘Now, Mr. Winkle,’ said the host, reloading his own gun. ‘Fire away.’\n\nMr. Winkle advanced, and levelled his gun. Mr. Pickwick and his friends cowered involuntarily to escape damage from the heavy fall of rooks, which they felt quite certain would be occasioned by the devastating barrel of their friend. There was a solemn pause--a shout--a flapping of wings--a faint click.\n\n‘Hollo!’ said the old gentleman.\n\n‘Won’t it go?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Missed fire,’ said Mr. Winkle, who was very pale--probably from disappointment.\n\n‘Odd,’ said the old gentleman, taking the gun. ‘Never knew one of them miss fire before. Why, I don’t see anything of the cap.’\n\nBless my soul!’ said Mr. Winkle, ‘I declare I forgot the cap!’\n\nThe slight omission was rectified. Mr. Pickwick crouched again. Mr. Winkle stepped forward with an air of determination and resolution; and Mr. Tupman looked out from behind a tree. The boy shouted; four birds flew out. Mr. Winkle fired. There was a scream as of an individual--not a rook--in corporal anguish. Mr. Tupman had saved the lives of innumerable unoffending birds by receiving a portion of the charge in his left arm.\n\nTo describe the confusion that ensued would be impossible. To tell how Mr. Pickwick in the first transports of emotion called Mr. Winkle ‘Wretch!’ how Mr. Tupman lay prostrate on the ground; and how Mr. Winkle knelt horror-stricken beside him; how Mr. Tupman called distractedly upon some feminine Christian name, and then opened first one eye, and then the other, and then fell back and shut them both--all this would be as difficult to describe in detail, as it would be to depict the gradual recovering of the unfortunate individual, the binding up of his arm with pocket-handkerchiefs, and the conveying him back by slow degrees supported by the arms of his anxious friends.\n\nThey drew near the house. The ladies were at the garden gate, waiting for their arrival and their breakfast. The spinster aunt appeared; she smiled, and beckoned them to walk quicker. ‘Twas evident she knew not of the disaster. Poor thing! there are times when ignorance is bliss indeed.\n\nThey approached nearer.\n\n‘Why, what is the matter with the little old gentleman?’ said Isabella Wardle. The spinster aunt heeded not the remark; she thought it applied to Mr. Pickwick. In her eyes Tracy Tupman was a youth; she viewed his years through a diminishing glass.\n\n‘Don’t be frightened,’ called out the old host, fearful of alarming his daughters. The little party had crowded so completely round Mr. Tupman, that they could not yet clearly discern the nature of the accident.\n\n‘Don’t be frightened,’ said the host.\n\n‘What’s the matter?’ screamed the ladies.\n\n‘Mr. Tupman has met with a little accident; that’s all.’\n\nThe spinster aunt uttered a piercing scream, burst into an hysteric laugh, and fell backwards in the arms of her nieces.\n\n‘Throw some cold water over her,’ said the old gentleman.\n\n‘No, no,’ murmured the spinster aunt; ‘I am better now. Bella, Emily--a surgeon! Is he wounded?--Is he dead?--Is he--Ha, ha, ha!’ Here the spinster aunt burst into fit number two, of hysteric laughter interspersed with screams.\n\n‘Calm yourself,’ said Mr. Tupman, affected almost to tears by this expression of sympathy with his sufferings. ‘Dear, dear madam, calm yourself.’\n\n‘It is his voice!’ exclaimed the spinster aunt; and strong symptoms of fit number three developed themselves forthwith.\n\n‘Do not agitate yourself, I entreat you, dearest madam,’ said Mr. Tupman soothingly. ‘I am very little hurt, I assure you.’\n\n‘Then you are not dead!’ ejaculated the hysterical lady. ‘Oh, say you are not dead!’\n\n‘Don’t be a fool, Rachael,’ interposed Mr. Wardle, rather more roughly than was consistent with the poetic nature of the scene. ‘What the devil’s the use of his saying he isn’t dead?’\n\n‘No, no, I am not,’ said Mr. Tupman. ‘I require no assistance but yours. Let me lean on your arm.’ He added, in a whisper, ‘Oh, Miss Rachael!’ The agitated female advanced, and offered her arm. They turned into the breakfast parlour. Mr. Tracy Tupman gently pressed her hand to his lips, and sank upon the sofa.\n\n‘Are you faint?’ inquired the anxious Rachael.\n\n‘No,’ said Mr. Tupman. ‘It is nothing. I shall be better presently.’ He closed his eyes.\n\n‘He sleeps,’ murmured the spinster aunt. (His organs of vision had been closed nearly twenty seconds.) ‘Dear--dear--Mr. Tupman!’\n\nMr. Tupman jumped up--‘Oh, say those words again!’ he exclaimed.\n\nThe lady started. ‘Surely you did not hear them!’ she said bashfully.\n\n‘Oh, yes, I did!’ replied Mr. Tupman; ‘repeat them. If you would have me recover, repeat them.’\n\nHush!’ said the lady. ‘My brother.’ Mr. Tracy Tupman resumed his former position; and Mr. Wardle, accompanied by a surgeon, entered the room.\n\nThe arm was examined, the wound dressed, and pronounced to be a very slight one; and the minds of the company having been thus satisfied, they proceeded to satisfy their appetites with countenances to which an expression of cheerfulness was again restored. Mr. Pickwick alone was silent and reserved. Doubt and distrust were exhibited in his countenance. His confidence in Mr. Winkle had been shaken--greatly shaken--by the proceedings of the morning.\n\n‘Are you a cricketer?’ inquired Mr. Wardle of the marksman.\n\nAt any other time, Mr. Winkle would have replied in the affirmative. He felt the delicacy of his situation, and modestly replied, ‘No.’\n\n‘Are you, sir?’ inquired Mr. Snodgrass.\n\n‘I was once upon a time,’ replied the host; ‘but I have given it up now. I subscribe to the club here, but I don’t play.’\n\n‘The grand match is played to-day, I believe,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘It is,’ replied the host. ‘Of course you would like to see it.’\n\n‘I, sir,’ replied Mr. Pickwick, ‘am delighted to view any sports which may be safely indulged in, and in which the impotent effects of unskilful people do not endanger human life.’ Mr. Pickwick paused, and looked steadily on Mr. Winkle, who quailed beneath his leader’s searching glance. The great man withdrew his eyes after a few minutes, and added: ‘Shall we be justified in leaving our wounded friend to the care of the ladies?’\n\n‘You cannot leave me in better hands,’ said Mr. Tupman.\n\n‘Quite impossible,’ said Mr. Snodgrass.\n\nIt was therefore settled that Mr. Tupman should be left at home in charge of the females; and that the remainder of the guests, under the guidance of Mr. Wardle, should proceed to the spot where was to be held that trial of skill, which had roused all Muggleton from its torpor, and inoculated Dingley Dell with a fever of excitement.\n\nAs their walk, which was not above two miles long, lay through shady lanes and sequestered footpaths, and as their conversation turned upon the delightful scenery by which they were on every side surrounded, Mr. Pickwick was almost inclined to regret the expedition they had used, when he found himself in the main street of the town of Muggleton.\n\nEverybody whose genius has a topographical bent knows perfectly well that Muggleton is a corporate town, with a mayor, burgesses, and freemen; and anybody who has consulted the addresses of the mayor to the freemen, or the freemen to the mayor, or both to the corporation, or all three to Parliament, will learn from thence what they ought to have known before, that Muggleton is an ancient and loyal borough, mingling a zealous advocacy of Christian principles with a devoted attachment to commercial rights; in demonstration whereof, the mayor, corporation, and other inhabitants, have presented at divers times, no fewer than one thousand four hundred and twenty petitions against the continuance of negro slavery abroad, and an equal number against any interference with the factory system at home; sixty-eight in favour of the sale of livings in the Church, and eighty-six for abolishing Sunday trading in the street.\n\nMr. Pickwick stood in the principal street of this illustrious town, and gazed with an air of curiosity, not unmixed with interest, on the objects around him. There was an open square for the market-place; and in the centre of it, a large inn with a sign-post in front, displaying an object very common in art, but rarely met with in nature--to wit, a blue lion, with three bow legs in the air, balancing himself on the extreme point of the centre claw of his fourth foot. There were, within sight, an auctioneer’s and fire-agency office, a corn-factor’s, a linen- draper’s, a saddler’s, a distiller’s, a grocer’s, and a shoe-shop--the last-mentioned warehouse being also appropriated to the diffusion of hats, bonnets, wearing apparel, cotton umbrellas, and useful knowledge. There was a red brick house with a small paved courtyard in front, which anybody might have known belonged to the attorney; and there was, moreover, another red brick house with Venetian blinds, and a large brass door-plate with a very legible announcement that it belonged to the surgeon. A few boys were making their way to the cricket-field; and two or three shopkeepers who were standing at their doors looked as if they should like to be making their way to the same spot, as indeed to all appearance they might have done, without losing any great amount of custom thereby. Mr. Pickwick having paused to make these observations, to be noted down at a more convenient period, hastened to rejoin his friends, who had turned out of the main street, and were already within sight of the field of battle.\n\nThe wickets were pitched, and so were a couple of marquees for the rest and refreshment of the contending parties. The game had not yet commenced. Two or three Dingley Dellers, and All-Muggletonians, were amusing themselves with a majestic air by throwing the ball carelessly from hand to hand; and several other gentlemen dressed like them, in straw hats, flannel jackets, and white trousers--a costume in which they looked very much like amateur stone-masons--were sprinkled about the tents, towards one of which Mr. Wardle conducted the party.\n\nSeveral dozen of ‘How-are-you’s?’ hailed the old gentleman’s arrival; and a general raising of the straw hats, and bending forward of the flannel jackets, followed his introduction of his guests as gentlemen from London, who were extremely anxious to witness the proceedings of the day, with which, he had no doubt, they would be greatly delighted.\n\n‘You had better step into the marquee, I think, Sir,’ said one very stout gentleman, whose body and legs looked like half a gigantic roll of flannel, elevated on a couple of inflated pillow-cases.\n\n‘You’ll find it much pleasanter, Sir,’ urged another stout gentleman, who strongly resembled the other half of the roll of flannel aforesaid.\n\n‘You’re very good,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘This way,’ said the first speaker; ‘they notch in here--it’s the best place in the whole field;’ and the cricketer, panting on before, preceded them to the tent.\n\n‘Capital game--smart sport--fine exercise--very,’ were the words which fell upon Mr. Pickwick’s ear as he entered the tent; and the first object that met his eyes was his green-coated friend of the Rochester coach, holding forth, to the no small delight and edification of a select circle of the chosen of All-Muggleton. His dress was slightly improved, and he wore boots; but there was no mistaking him.\n\nThe stranger recognised his friends immediately; and, darting forward and seizing Mr. Pickwick by the hand, dragged him to a seat with his usual impetuosity, talking all the while as if the whole of the arrangements were under his especial patronage and direction.\n\n‘This way--this way--capital fun--lots of beer--hogsheads; rounds of beef--bullocks; mustard--cart-loads; glorious day--down with you--make yourself at home--glad to see you--very.’\n\nMr. Pickwick sat down as he was bid, and Mr. Winkle and Mr. Snodgrass also complied with the directions of their mysterious friend. Mr. Wardle looked on in silent wonder.\n\n‘Mr. Wardle--a friend of mine,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Friend of yours!--My dear sir, how are you?--Friend of my friend’s-- give me your hand, sir’--and the stranger grasped Mr. Wardle’s hand with all the fervour of a close intimacy of many years, and then stepped back a pace or two as if to take a full survey of his face and figure, and then shook hands with him again, if possible, more warmly than before.\n\n‘Well; and how came you here?’ said Mr. Pickwick, with a smile in which benevolence struggled with surprise.\n\n‘Come,’ replied the stranger--‘stopping at Crown--Crown at Muggleton-- met a party--flannel jackets--white trousers--anchovy sandwiches-- devilled kidney--splendid fellows--glorious.’\n\nMr. Pickwick was sufficiently versed in the stranger’s system of stenography to infer from this rapid and disjointed communication that he had, somehow or other, contracted an acquaintance with the All- Muggletons, which he had converted, by a process peculiar to himself, into that extent of good-fellowship on which a general invitation may be easily founded. His curiosity was therefore satisfied, and putting on his spectacles he prepared himself to watch the play which was just commencing.\n\nAll-Muggleton had the first innings; and the interest became intense when Mr. Dumkins and Mr. Podder, two of the most renowned members of that most distinguished club, walked, bat in hand, to their respective wickets. Mr. Luffey, the highest ornament of Dingley Dell, was pitched to bowl against the redoubtable Dumkins, and Mr. Struggles was selected to do the same kind office for the hitherto unconquered Podder. Several players were stationed, to ‘look out,’ in different parts of the field, and each fixed himself into the proper attitude by placing one hand on each knee, and stooping very much as if he were ‘making a back’ for some beginner at leap-frog. All the regular players do this sort of thing;-- indeed it is generally supposed that it is quite impossible to look out properly in any other position.\n\nThe umpires were stationed behind the wickets; the scorers were prepared to notch the runs; a breathless silence ensued. Mr. Luffey retired a few paces behind the wicket of the passive Podder, and applied the ball to his right eye for several seconds. Dumkins confidently awaited its coming with his eyes fixed on the motions of Luffey.\n\n‘Play!’ suddenly cried the bowler. The ball flew from his hand straight and swift towards the centre stump of the wicket. The wary Dumkins was on the alert: it fell upon the tip of the bat, and bounded far away over the heads of the scouts, who had just stooped low enough to let it fly over them.\n\n‘Run--run--another.--Now, then throw her up--up with her--stop there-- another--no--yes--no--throw her up, throw her up!’--Such were the shouts which followed the stroke; and at the conclusion of which All-Muggleton had scored two. Nor was Podder behindhand in earning laurels wherewith to garnish himself and Muggleton. He blocked the doubtful balls, missed the bad ones, took the good ones, and sent them flying to all parts of the field. The scouts were hot and tired; the bowlers were changed and bowled till their arms ached; but Dumkins and Podder remained unconquered. Did an elderly gentleman essay to stop the progress of the ball, it rolled between his legs or slipped between his fingers. Did a slim gentleman try to catch it, it struck him on the nose, and bounded pleasantly off with redoubled violence, while the slim gentleman’s eyes filled with water, and his form writhed with anguish. Was it thrown straight up to the wicket, Dumkins had reached it before the ball. In short, when Dumkins was caught out, and Podder stumped out, All- Muggleton had notched some fifty-four, while the score of the Dingley Dellers was as blank as their faces. The advantage was too great to be recovered. In vain did the eager Luffey, and the enthusiastic Struggles, do all that skill and experience could suggest, to regain the ground Dingley Dell had lost in the contest--it was of no avail; and in an early period of the winning game Dingley Dell gave in, and allowed the superior prowess of All-Muggleton.\n\nThe stranger, meanwhile, had been eating, drinking, and talking, without cessation. At every good stroke he expressed his satisfaction and approval of the player in a most condescending and patronising manner, which could not fail to have been highly gratifying to the party concerned; while at every bad attempt at a catch, and every failure to stop the ball, he launched his personal displeasure at the head of the devoted individual in such denunciations as--‘Ah, ah!--stupid’--‘Now, butter-fingers’--‘Muff’--‘Humbug’--and so forth--ejaculations which seemed to establish him in the opinion of all around, as a most excellent and undeniable judge of the whole art and mystery of the noble game of cricket.\n\n‘Capital game--well played--some strokes admirable,’ said the stranger, as both sides crowded into the tent, at the conclusion of the game.\n\n‘You have played it, sir?’ inquired Mr. Wardle, who had been much amused by his loquacity.\n\n‘Played it! Think I have--thousands of times--not here--West Indies-- exciting thing--hot work--very.’ ‘It must be rather a warm pursuit in such a climate,’ observed Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Warm!--red hot--scorching--glowing. Played a match once--single wicket- -friend the colonel--Sir Thomas Blazo--who should get the greatest number of runs.--Won the toss--first innings--seven o’clock A.M.--six natives to look out--went in; kept in--heat intense--natives all fainted--taken away--fresh half-dozen ordered--fainted also--Blazo bowling--supported by two natives--couldn’t bowl me out--fainted too-- cleared away the colonel--wouldn’t give in--faithful attendant--Quanko Samba--last man left--sun so hot, bat in blisters, ball scorched brown-- five hundred and seventy runs--rather exhausted--Quanko mustered up last remaining strength--bowled me out--had a bath, and went out to dinner.’\n\n‘And what became of what’s-his-name, Sir?’ inquired an old gentleman.\n‘Blazo?’\n\n‘No--the other gentleman.’\n\nQuanko Samba?’\n\n‘Yes, sir.’\n\n‘Poor Quanko--never recovered it--bowled on, on my account--bowled off, on his own--died, sir.’ Here the stranger buried his countenance in a brown jug, but whether to hide his emotion or imbibe its contents, we cannot distinctly affirm. We only know that he paused suddenly, drew a long and deep breath, and looked anxiously on, as two of the principal members of the Dingley Dell club approached Mr. Pickwick, and said--\n\n‘We are about to partake of a plain dinner at the Blue Lion, Sir; we hope you and your friends will join us.’\n\nOf course,’ said Mr. Wardle, ‘among our friends we include Mr.--;’ and he looked towards the stranger.\n\n‘Jingle,’ said that versatile gentleman, taking the hint at once. ‘Jingle--Alfred Jingle, Esq., of No Hall, Nowhere.’\n\n‘I shall be very happy, I am sure,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘So shall I,’ said Mr. Alfred Jingle, drawing one arm through Mr. Pickwick’s, and another through Mr. Wardle’s, as he whispered confidentially in the ear of the former gentleman:--\n\n‘Devilish good dinner--cold, but capital--peeped into the room this morning--fowls and pies, and all that sort of thing--pleasant fellows these--well behaved, too--very.’\n\nThere being no further preliminaries to arrange, the company straggled into the town in little knots of twos and threes; and within a quarter of an hour were all seated in the great room of the Blue Lion Inn, Muggleton--Mr. Dumkins acting as chairman, and Mr. Luffey officiating as vice.\n\nThere was a vast deal of talking and rattling of knives and forks, and plates; a great running about of three ponderous-headed waiters, and a rapid disappearance of the substantial viands on the table; to each and every of which item of confusion, the facetious Mr. Jingle lent the aid of half-a-dozen ordinary men at least. When everybody had eaten as much as possible, the cloth was removed, bottles, glasses, and dessert were placed on the table; and the waiters withdrew to ‘clear away,’ or in other words, to appropriate to their own private use and emolument whatever remnants of the eatables and drinkables they could contrive to lay their hands on.\n\nAmidst the general hum of mirth and conversation that ensued, there was a little man with a puffy Say-nothing-to-me,-or-I’ll-contradict-you sort of countenance, who remained very quiet; occasionally looking round him when the conversation slackened, as if he contemplated putting in something very weighty; and now and then bursting into a short cough of inexpressible grandeur. At length, during a moment of comparative silence, the little man called out in a very loud, solemn voice,--\n\n‘Mr. Luffey!’\n\nEverybody was hushed into a profound stillness as the individual addressed, replied--\n\n‘Sir!’\n\n‘I wish to address a few words to you, Sir, if you will entreat the gentlemen to fill their glasses.’\n\nMr. Jingle uttered a patronising ‘Hear, hear,’ which was responded to by the remainder of the company; and the glasses having been filled, the vice-president assumed an air of wisdom in a state of profound attention; and said--\n\n‘Mr. Staple.’\n\n‘Sir,’ said the little man, rising, ‘I wish to address what I have to say to you and not to our worthy chairman, because our worthy chairman is in some measure--I may say in a great degree--the subject of what I have to say, or I may say to--to--’\n\n‘State,’ suggested Mr. Jingle.\n\n‘Yes, to state,’ said the little man, ‘I thank my honourable friend, if he will allow me to call him so (four hears and one certainly from Mr. Jingle), for the suggestion. Sir, I am a Deller--a Dingley Deller (cheers). I cannot lay claim to the honour of forming an item in the population of Muggleton; nor, Sir, I will frankly admit, do I covet that honour: and I will tell you why, Sir (hear); to Muggleton I will readily concede all these honours and distinctions to which it can fairly lay claim--they are too numerous and too well known to require aid or recapitulation from me. But, sir, while we remember that Muggleton has given birth to a Dumkins and a Podder, let us never forget that Dingley Dell can boast a Luffey and a Struggles. (Vociferous cheering.) Let me not be considered as wishing to detract from the merits of the former gentlemen. Sir, I envy them the luxury of their own feelings on this occasion. (Cheers.) Every gentleman who hears me, is probably acquainted with the reply made by an individual, who--to use an ordinary figure of speech--“hung out” in a tub, to the emperor Alexander:--“if I were not Diogenes,” said he, “I would be Alexander.” I can well imagine these gentlemen to say, “If I were not Dumkins I would be Luffey; if I were not Podder I would be Struggles.” (Enthusiasm.) But, gentlemen of Muggleton, is it in cricket alone that your fellow-townsmen stand pre- eminent? Have you never heard of Dumkins and determination? Have you never been taught to associate Podder with property? (Great applause.) Have you never, when struggling for your rights, your liberties, and your privileges, been reduced, if only for an instant, to misgiving and despair? And when you have been thus depressed, has not the name of Dumkins laid afresh within your breast the fire which had just gone out; and has not a word from that man lighted it again as brightly as if it had never expired? (Great cheering.) Gentlemen, I beg to surround with a rich halo of enthusiastic cheering the united names of “Dumkins and Podder.”’\n\nHere the little man ceased, and here the company commenced a raising of voices, and thumping of tables, which lasted with little intermission during the remainder of the evening. Other toasts were drunk. Mr. Luffey and Mr. Struggles, Mr. Pickwick and Mr. Jingle, were, each in his turn, the subject of unqualified eulogium; and each in due course returned thanks for the honour.\n\nEnthusiastic as we are in the noble cause to which we have devoted ourselves, we should have felt a sensation of pride which we cannot express, and a consciousness of having done something to merit immortality of which we are now deprived, could we have laid the faintest outline on these addresses before our ardent readers. Mr. Snodgrass, as usual, took a great mass of notes, which would no doubt have afforded most useful and valuable information, had not the burning eloquence of the words or the feverish influence of the wine made that gentleman’s hand so extremely unsteady, as to render his writing nearly unintelligible, and his style wholly so. By dint of patient investigation, we have been enabled to trace some characters bearing a faint resemblance to the names of the speakers; and we can only discern an entry of a song (supposed to have been sung by Mr. Jingle), in which the words ‘bowl’ ‘sparkling’ ‘ruby’ ‘bright’ and ‘wine’ are frequently repeated at short intervals. We fancy, too, that we can discern at the very end of the notes, some indistinct reference to ‘broiled bones’; and then the words ‘cold’ ‘without’ occur: but as any hypothesis we could found upon them must necessarily rest upon mere conjecture, we are not disposed to indulge in any of the speculations to which they may give rise.\n\nWe will therefore return to Mr. Tupman; merely adding that within some few minutes before twelve o’clock that night, the convocation of worthies of Dingley Dell and Muggleton were heard to sing, with great feeling and emphasis, the beautiful and pathetic national air of\n\n‘We won’t go home till morning, We won’t go home till morning, We won’t go home till morning, Till daylight doth appear.’\n\nCHAPTER VIII. STRONGLY ILLUSTRATIVE OF THE POSITION, THAT THE COURSE OF TRUE LOVE IS NOT A RAILWAY\nThe quiet seclusion of Dingley Dell, the presence of so many of the gentler sex, and the solicitude and anxiety they evinced in his behalf, were all favourable to the growth and development of those softer feelings which nature had implanted deep in the bosom of Mr. Tracy Tupman, and which now appeared destined to centre in one lovely object. The young ladies were pretty, their manners winning, their dispositions unexceptionable; but there was a dignity in the air, a touch-me-not- ishness in the walk, a majesty in the eye, of the spinster aunt, to which, at their time of life, they could lay no claim, which distinguished her from any female on whom Mr. Tupman had ever gazed. That there was something kindred in their nature, something congenial in their souls, something mysteriously sympathetic in their bosoms, was evident. Her name was the first that rose to Mr. Tupman’s lips as he lay wounded on the grass; and her hysteric laughter was the first sound that fell upon his ear when he was supported to the house. But had her agitation arisen from an amiable and feminine sensibility which would have been equally irrepressible in any case; or had it been called forth by a more ardent and passionate feeling, which he, of all men living, could alone awaken? These were the doubts which racked his brain as he lay extended on the sofa; these were the doubts which he determined should be at once and for ever resolved.\n\nIt was evening. Isabella and Emily had strolled out with Mr. Trundle; the deaf old lady had fallen asleep in her chair; the snoring of the fat boy, penetrated in a low and monotonous sound from the distant kitchen; the buxom servants were lounging at the side door, enjoying the pleasantness of the hour, and the delights of a flirtation, on first principles, with certain unwieldy animals attached to the farm; and there sat the interesting pair, uncared for by all, caring for none, and dreaming only of themselves; there they sat, in short, like a pair of carefully-folded kid gloves--bound up in each other.\n\n‘I have forgotten my flowers,’ said the spinster aunt.\n\n‘Water them now,’ said Mr. Tupman, in accents of persuasion.\n\n‘You will take cold in the evening air,’ urged the spinster aunt affectionately.\n\n‘No, no,’ said Mr. Tupman, rising; ‘it will do me good. Let me accompany you.’\n\nThe lady paused to adjust the sling in which the left arm of the youth was placed, and taking his right arm led him to the garden.\n\nThere was a bower at the farther end, with honeysuckle, jessamine, and creeping plants--one of those sweet retreats which humane men erect for the accommodation of spiders.\n\nThe spinster aunt took up a large watering-pot which lay in one corner, and was about to leave the arbour. Mr. Tupman detained her, and drew her to a seat beside him.\n\n‘Miss Wardle!’ said he.\n\nThe spinster aunt trembled, till some pebbles which had accidentally found their way into the large watering-pot shook like an infant’s rattle.\n\n‘Miss Wardle,’ said Mr. Tupman, ‘you are an angel.’\n\n‘Mr. Tupman!’ exclaimed Rachael, blushing as red as the watering-pot itself.\n\n‘Nay,’ said the eloquent Pickwickian--‘I know it but too well.’\n\n‘All women are angels, they say,’ murmured the lady playfully.\n\n‘Then what can you be; or to what, without presumption, can I compare you?’ replied Mr. Tupman. ‘Where was the woman ever seen who resembled you? Where else could I hope to find so rare a combination of excellence and beauty? Where else could I seek to--Oh!’ Here Mr. Tupman paused, and pressed the hand which clasped the handle of the happy watering-pot.\n\nThe lady turned aside her head. ‘Men are such deceivers,’ she softly whispered.\n\n‘They are, they are,’ ejaculated Mr. Tupman; ‘but not all men. There lives at least one being who can never change--one being who would be content to devote his whole existence to your happiness--who lives but in your eyes--who breathes but in your smiles--who bears the heavy burden of life itself only for you.’\n\n‘Could such an individual be found--’ said the lady.\n\n‘But he can be found,’ said the ardent Mr. Tupman, interposing. ‘He is found. He is here, Miss Wardle.’ And ere the lady was aware of his intention, Mr. Tupman had sunk upon his knees at her feet.\n\n‘Mr. Tupman, rise,’ said Rachael.\n\n‘Never!’ was the valorous reply. ‘Oh, Rachael!’ He seized her passive hand, and the watering-pot fell to the ground as he pressed it to his lips.--‘Oh, Rachael! say you love me.’\n\n‘Mr. Tupman,’ said the spinster aunt, with averted head, ‘I can hardly speak the words; but--but--you are not wholly indifferent to me.’\n\nMr. Tupman no sooner heard this avowal, than he proceeded to do what his enthusiastic emotions prompted, and what, for aught we know (for we are but little acquainted with such matters), people so circumstanced always do. He jumped up, and, throwing his arm round the neck of the spinster aunt, imprinted upon her lips numerous kisses, which after a due show of struggling and resistance, she received so passively, that there is no telling how many more Mr. Tupman might have bestowed, if the lady had not given a very unaffected start, and exclaimed in an affrighted tone--\n\n‘Mr. Tupman, we are observed!--we are discovered!’\n\nMr. Tupman looked round. There was the fat boy, perfectly motionless, with his large circular eyes staring into the arbour, but without the slightest expression on his face that the most expert physiognomist could have referred to astonishment, curiosity, or any other known passion that agitates the human breast. Mr. Tupman gazed on the fat boy, and the fat boy stared at him; and the longer Mr. Tupman observed the utter vacancy of the fat boy’s countenance, the more convinced he became that he either did not know, or did not understand, anything that had been going forward. Under this impression, he said with great firmness--\n\n‘What do you want here, Sir?’\n\n‘Supper’s ready, sir,’ was the prompt reply.\n\n‘Have you just come here, sir?’ inquired Mr. Tupman, with a piercing look.\n\n‘Just,’ replied the fat boy.\n\nMr. Tupman looked at him very hard again; but there was not a wink in his eye, or a curve in his face.\n\nMr. Tupman took the arm of the spinster aunt, and walked towards the house; the fat boy followed behind.\n\n‘He knows nothing of what has happened,’ he whispered.\n\n‘Nothing,’ said the spinster aunt.\n\nThere was a sound behind them, as of an imperfectly suppressed chuckle. Mr. Tupman turned sharply round. No; it could not have been the fat boy; there was not a gleam of mirth, or anything but feeding in his whole visage.\n\n‘He must have been fast asleep,’ whispered Mr. Tupman.\n\n‘I have not the least doubt of it,’ replied the spinster aunt.\n\nThey both laughed heartily.\n\nMr. Tupman was wrong. The fat boy, for once, had not been fast asleep. He was awake--wide awake--to what had been going forward.\n\nThe supper passed off without any attempt at a general conversation. The old lady had gone to bed; Isabella Wardle devoted herself exclusively to Mr. Trundle; the spinster’s attentions were reserved for Mr. Tupman; and Emily’s thoughts appeared to be engrossed by some distant object-- possibly they were with the absent Snodgrass.\n\nEleven--twelve--one o’clock had struck, and the gentlemen had not arrived. Consternation sat on every face. Could they have been waylaid and robbed? Should they send men and lanterns in every direction by which they could be supposed likely to have travelled home? or should they--Hark! there they were. What could have made them so late? A strange voice, too! To whom could it belong? They rushed into the kitchen, whither the truants had repaired, and at once obtained rather more than a glimmering of the real state of the case.\n\nMr. Pickwick, with his hands in his pockets and his hat cocked completely over his left eye, was leaning against the dresser, shaking his head from side to side, and producing a constant succession of the blandest and most benevolent smiles without being moved thereunto by any discernible cause or pretence whatsoever; old Mr. Wardle, with a highly- inflamed countenance, was grasping the hand of a strange gentleman muttering protestations of eternal friendship; Mr. Winkle, supporting himself by the eight-day clock, was feebly invoking destruction upon the head of any member of the family who should suggest the propriety of his retiring for the night; and Mr. Snodgrass had sunk into a chair, with an expression of the most abject and hopeless misery that the human mind can imagine, portrayed in every lineament of his expressive face.\n\n‘Is anything the matter?’ inquired the three ladies.\n\n‘Nothing the matter,’ replied Mr. Pickwick. ‘We--we’re--all right.--I say, Wardle, we’re all right, ain’t we?’\n\n‘I should think so,’ replied the jolly host.--‘My dears, here’s my friend Mr. Jingle--Mr. Pickwick’s friend, Mr. Jingle, come ‘pon--little visit.’\n\n‘Is anything the matter with Mr. Snodgrass, Sir?’ inquired Emily, with great anxiety.\n\n‘Nothing the matter, ma’am,’ replied the stranger. ‘Cricket dinner-- glorious party--capital songs--old port--claret--good--very good--wine, ma’am--wine.’\n\n‘It wasn’t the wine,’ murmured Mr. Snodgrass, in a broken voice. ‘It was the salmon.’ (Somehow or other, it never is the wine, in these cases.)\n\n‘Hadn’t they better go to bed, ma’am?’ inquired Emma. ‘Two of the boys will carry the gentlemen upstairs.’\n\n‘I won’t go to bed,’ said Mr. Winkle firmly.\n\n‘No living boy shall carry me,’ said Mr. Pickwick stoutly; and he went on smiling as before.\n\n‘Hurrah!’ gasped Mr. Winkle faintly.\n\n‘Hurrah!’ echoed Mr. Pickwick, taking off his hat and dashing it on the floor, and insanely casting his spectacles into the middle of the kitchen. At this humorous feat he laughed outright.\n\n‘Let’s--have--‘nother--bottle,’ cried Mr. Winkle, commencing in a very loud key, and ending in a very faint one. His head dropped upon his breast; and, muttering his invincible determination not to go to his bed, and a sanguinary regret that he had not ‘done for old Tupman’ in the morning, he fell fast asleep; in which condition he was borne to his apartment by two young giants under the personal superintendence of the fat boy, to whose protecting care Mr. Snodgrass shortly afterwards confided his own person, Mr. Pickwick accepted the proffered arm of Mr. Tupman and quietly disappeared, smiling more than ever; and Mr. Wardle, after taking as affectionate a leave of the whole family as if he were ordered for immediate execution, consigned to Mr. Trundle the honour of conveying him upstairs, and retired, with a very futile attempt to look impressively solemn and dignified.\n\n‘What a shocking scene!’ said the spinster aunt.\n\n‘Dis-gusting!’ ejaculated both the young ladies.\n\n‘Dreadful--dreadful!’ said Jingle, looking very grave: he was about a bottle and a half ahead of any of his companions. ‘Horrid spectacle-- very!’\n\n‘What a nice man!’ whispered the spinster aunt to Mr. Tupman.\n\n‘Good-looking, too!’ whispered Emily Wardle.\n\n‘Oh, decidedly,’ observed the spinster aunt.\n\nMr. Tupman thought of the widow at Rochester, and his mind was troubled. The succeeding half-hour’s conversation was not of a nature to calm his perturbed spirit. The new visitor was very talkative, and the number of his anecdotes was only to be exceeded by the extent of his politeness. Mr. Tupman felt that as Jingle’s popularity increased, he (Tupman) retired further into the shade. His laughter was forced--his merriment feigned; and when at last he laid his aching temples between the sheets, he thought, with horrid delight, on the satisfaction it would afford him to have Jingle’s head at that moment between the feather bed and the mattress.\n\nThe indefatigable stranger rose betimes next morning, and, although his companions remained in bed overpowered with the dissipation of the previous night, exerted himself most successfully to promote the hilarity of the breakfast-table. So successful were his efforts, that even the deaf old lady insisted on having one or two of his best jokes retailed through the trumpet; and even she condescended to observe to the spinster aunt, that ‘He’ (meaning Jingle) ‘was an impudent young fellow:’ a sentiment in which all her relations then and there present thoroughly coincided.\n\nIt was the old lady’s habit on the fine summer mornings to repair to the arbour in which Mr. Tupman had already signalised himself, in form and manner following: first, the fat boy fetched from a peg behind the old lady’s bedroom door, a close black satin bonnet, a warm cotton shawl, and a thick stick with a capacious handle; and the old lady, having put on the bonnet and shawl at her leisure, would lean one hand on the stick and the other on the fat boy’s shoulder, and walk leisurely to the arbour, where the fat boy would leave her to enjoy the fresh air for the space of half an hour; at the expiration of which time he would return and reconduct her to the house.\n\nThe old lady was very precise and very particular; and as this ceremony had been observed for three successive summers without the slightest deviation from the accustomed form, she was not a little surprised on this particular morning to see the fat boy, instead of leaving the arbour, walk a few paces out of it, look carefully round him in every direction, and return towards her with great stealth and an air of the most profound mystery.\n\nThe old lady was timorous--most old ladies are--and her first impression was that the bloated lad was about to do her some grievous bodily harm with the view of possessing himself of her loose coin. She would have cried for assistance, but age and infirmity had long ago deprived her of the power of screaming; she, therefore, watched his motions with feelings of intense horror which were in no degree diminished by his coming close up to her, and shouting in her ear in an agitated, and as it seemed to her, a threatening tone--\n\n‘Missus!’\n\nNow it so happened that Mr. Jingle was walking in the garden close to the arbour at that moment. He too heard the shouts of ‘Missus,’ and stopped to hear more. There were three reasons for his doing so. In the first place, he was idle and curious; secondly, he was by no means scrupulous; thirdly, and lastly, he was concealed from view by some flowering shrubs. So there he stood, and there he listened.\n\n‘Missus!’ shouted the fat boy.\n\n‘Well, Joe,’ said the trembling old lady. ‘I’m sure I have been a good mistress to you, Joe. You have invariably been treated very kindly. You have never had too much to do; and you have always had enough to eat.’\n\nThis last was an appeal to the fat boy’s most sensitive feelings. He seemed touched, as he replied emphatically--\n\n‘I knows I has.’\n\n‘Then what can you want to do now?’ said the old lady, gaining courage.\n\n‘I wants to make your flesh creep,’ replied the boy.\n\nThis sounded like a very bloodthirsty mode of showing one’s gratitude; and as the old lady did not precisely understand the process by which such a result was to be attained, all her former horrors returned.\n\n‘What do you think I see in this very arbour last night?’ inquired the boy.\n\n‘Bless us! What?’ exclaimed the old lady, alarmed at the solemn manner of the corpulent youth.\n\n‘The strange gentleman--him as had his arm hurt--a-kissin’ and huggin’-- ‘\n\n‘Who, Joe? None of the servants, I hope.’\n\nWorser than that,’ roared the fat boy, in the old lady’s ear.\n\n‘Not one of my grandda’aters?’\n\n‘Worser than that.’\n\n‘Worse than that, Joe!’ said the old lady, who had thought this the extreme limit of human atrocity. ‘Who was it, Joe? I insist upon knowing.’\n\nThe fat boy looked cautiously round, and having concluded his survey, shouted in the old lady’s ear--\n\n‘Miss Rachael.’\n\n‘What!’ said the old lady, in a shrill tone. ‘Speak louder.’\n\n‘Miss Rachael,’ roared the fat boy.\n\n‘My da’ater!’\n\nThe train of nods which the fat boy gave by way of assent, communicated a blanc-mange like motion to his fat cheeks.\n\n‘And she suffered him!’ exclaimed the old lady. A grin stole over the fat boy’s features as he said--\n\n‘I see her a-kissin’ of him agin.’\n\nIf Mr. Jingle, from his place of concealment, could have beheld the expression which the old lady’s face assumed at this communication, the probability is that a sudden burst of laughter would have betrayed his close vicinity to the summer-house. He listened attentively. Fragments of angry sentences such as, ‘Without my permission!’--‘At her time of life’--‘Miserable old ‘ooman like me’--‘Might have waited till I was dead,’ and so forth, reached his ears; and then he heard the heels of the fat boy’s boots crunching the gravel, as he retired and left the old lady alone.\n\nIt was a remarkable coincidence perhaps, but it was nevertheless a fact, that Mr. Jingle within five minutes of his arrival at Manor Farm on the preceding night, had inwardly resolved to lay siege to the heart of the spinster aunt, without delay. He had observation enough to see, that his off-hand manner was by no means disagreeable to the fair object of his attack; and he had more than a strong suspicion that she possessed that most desirable of all requisites, a small independence. The imperative necessity of ousting his rival by some means or other, flashed quickly upon him, and he immediately resolved to adopt certain proceedings tending to that end and object, without a moment’s delay. Fielding tells us that man is fire, and woman tow, and the Prince of Darkness sets a light to ‘em. Mr. Jingle knew that young men, to spinster aunts, are as lighted gas to gunpowder, and he determined to essay the effect of an explosion without loss of time.\n\nFull of reflections upon this important decision, he crept from his place of concealment, and, under cover of the shrubs before mentioned, approached the house. Fortune seemed determined to favour his design. Mr. Tupman and the rest of the gentlemen left the garden by the side gate just as he obtained a view of it; and the young ladies, he knew, had walked out alone, soon after breakfast. The coast was clear.\n\nThe breakfast-parlour door was partially open. He peeped in. The spinster aunt was knitting. He coughed; she looked up and smiled. Hesitation formed no part of Mr. Alfred Jingle’s character. He laid his finger on his lips mysteriously, walked in, and closed the door.\n\n‘Miss Wardle,’ said Mr. Jingle, with affected earnestness, ‘forgive intrusion--short acquaintance--no time for ceremony--all discovered.’\n\n‘Sir!’ said the spinster aunt, rather astonished by the unexpected apparition and somewhat doubtful of Mr. Jingle’s sanity.\n\n‘Hush!’ said Mr. Jingle, in a stage-whisper--‘Large boy--dumpling face-- round eyes--rascal!’ Here he shook his head expressively, and the spinster aunt trembled with agitation.\n\n‘I presume you allude to Joseph, Sir?’ said the lady, making an effort to appear composed.\n\n‘Yes, ma’am--damn that Joe!--treacherous dog, Joe--told the old lady-- old lady furious--wild--raving--arbour--Tupman--kissing and hugging--all that sort of thing--eh, ma’am--eh?’\n\n‘Mr. Jingle,’ said the spinster aunt, ‘if you come here, Sir, to insult me--’\n\n‘Not at all--by no means,’ replied the unabashed Mr. Jingle--‘overheard the tale--came to warn you of your danger--tender my services--prevent the hubbub. Never mind--think it an insult--leave the room’--and he turned, as if to carry the threat into execution.\n\n‘What shall I do!’ said the poor spinster, bursting into tears. ‘My brother will be furious.’\n\n‘Of course he will,’ said Mr. Jingle pausing--‘outrageous.’\n\nOh, Mr. Jingle, what can I say!’ exclaimed the spinster aunt, in another flood of despair.\n\n‘Say he dreamt it,’ replied Mr. Jingle coolly.\n\nA ray of comfort darted across the mind of the spinster aunt at this suggestion. Mr. Jingle perceived it, and followed up his advantage.\n\n‘Pooh, pooh!--nothing more easy--blackguard boy--lovely woman--fat boy horsewhipped--you believed--end of the matter--all comfortable.’\n\nWhether the probability of escaping from the consequences of this ill- timed discovery was delightful to the spinster’s feelings, or whether the hearing herself described as a ‘lovely woman’ softened the asperity of her grief, we know not. She blushed slightly, and cast a grateful look on Mr. Jingle.\n\nThat insinuating gentleman sighed deeply, fixed his eyes on the spinster aunt’s face for a couple of minutes, started melodramatically, and suddenly withdrew them.\n\n‘You seem unhappy, Mr. Jingle,’ said the lady, in a plaintive voice. ‘May I show my gratitude for your kind interference, by inquiring into the cause, with a view, if possible, to its removal?’\n\n‘Ha!’ exclaimed Mr. Jingle, with another start--‘removal! remove my unhappiness, and your love bestowed upon a man who is insensible to the blessing--who even now contemplates a design upon the affections of the niece of the creature who--but no; he is my friend; I will not expose his vices. Miss Wardle--farewell!’ At the conclusion of this address, the most consecutive he was ever known to utter, Mr. Jingle applied to his eyes the remnant of a handkerchief before noticed, and turned towards the door.\n\n‘Stay, Mr. Jingle!’ said the spinster aunt emphatically. ‘You have made an allusion to Mr. Tupman--explain it.’\n\n‘Never!’ exclaimed Jingle, with a professional (i.e., theatrical) air. ‘Never!’ and, by way of showing that he had no desire to be questioned further, he drew a chair close to that of the spinster aunt and sat down.\n\n‘Mr. Jingle,’ said the aunt, ‘I entreat--I implore you, if there is any dreadful mystery connected with Mr. Tupman, reveal it.’\n\n‘Can I,’ said Mr. Jingle, fixing his eyes on the aunt’s face--‘can I see--lovely creature--sacrificed at the shrine--heartless avarice!’ He appeared to be struggling with various conflicting emotions for a few seconds, and then said in a low voice-- ‘Tupman only wants your money.’\n\n‘The wretch!’ exclaimed the spinster, with energetic indignation. (Mr. Jingle’s doubts were resolved. She had money.)\n\n‘More than that,’ said Jingle--‘loves another.’\n\n‘Another!’ ejaculated the spinster. ‘Who?’\n\nShort girl--black eyes--niece Emily.’\n\nThere was a pause.\n\nNow, if there was one individual in the whole world, of whom the spinster aunt entertained a mortal and deep-rooted jealousy, it was this identical niece. The colour rushed over her face and neck, and she tossed her head in silence with an air of ineffable contempt. At last, biting her thin lips, and bridling up, she said--\n\n‘It can’t be. I won’t believe it.’\n\n‘Watch ‘em,’ said Jingle.\n\n‘I will,’ said the aunt.\n\n‘Watch his looks.’\n\n‘I will.’\n‘His whispers.’\n\n‘I will.’\n\n‘He’ll sit next her at table.’\n\n‘Let him.’\n\n‘He’ll flatter her.’\n\n‘Let him.’\n\n‘He’ll pay her every possible attention.’\n\n‘Let him.’\n\n‘And he’ll cut you.’\n\n‘Cut me!’ screamed the spinster aunt. ‘he cut me; will he!’ and she trembled with rage and disappointment.\n\n‘You will convince yourself?’ said Jingle.\n\n‘I will.’\n\n‘You’ll show your spirit?’\n\n‘I will.’\n\nYou’ll not have him afterwards?’\n‘Never.’\n\n‘You’ll take somebody else?’\n\nYes.’\n\n‘You shall.’\n\nMr. Jingle fell on his knees, remained thereupon for five minutes thereafter; and rose the accepted lover of the spinster aunt-- conditionally upon Mr. Tupman’s perjury being made clear and manifest.\n\nThe burden of proof lay with Mr. Alfred Jingle; and he produced his evidence that very day at dinner. The spinster aunt could hardly believe her eyes. Mr. Tracy Tupman was established at Emily’s side, ogling, whispering, and smiling, in opposition to Mr. Snodgrass. Not a word, not a look, not a glance, did he bestow upon his heart’s pride of the evening before.\n\n‘Damn that boy!’ thought old Mr. Wardle to himself.--He had heard the story from his mother. ‘Damn that boy! He must have been asleep. It’s all imagination.’\n\n‘Traitor!’ thought the spinster aunt. ‘Dear Mr. Jingle was not deceiving me. Ugh! how I hate the wretch!’\n\nThe following conversation may serve to explain to our readers this apparently unaccountable alteration of deportment on the part of Mr. Tracy Tupman.\n\nThe time was evening; the scene the garden. There were two figures walking in a side path; one was rather short and stout; the other tall and slim. They were Mr. Tupman and Mr. Jingle. The stout figure commenced the dialogue.\n\n‘How did I do it?’ he inquired.\n\n‘Splendid--capital--couldn’t act better myself--you must repeat the part to-morrow--every evening till further notice.’\n\n‘Does Rachael still wish it?’\n\n‘Of course--she don’t like it--but must be done--avert suspicion--afraid of her brother--says there’s no help for it--only a few days more--when old folks blinded--crown your happiness.’\n\n‘Any message?’\n\n‘Love--best love--kindest regards--unalterable affection. Can I say anything for you?’\n\n‘My dear fellow,’ replied the unsuspicious Mr. Tupman, fervently grasping his ‘friend’s’ hand--‘carry my best love--say how hard I find it to dissemble--say anything that’s kind: but add how sensible I am of the necessity of the suggestion she made to me, through you, this morning. Say I applaud her wisdom and admire her discretion.’\n\nI will. Anything more?’\n\n‘Nothing, only add how ardently I long for the time when I may call her mine, and all dissimulation may be unnecessary.’\n\n‘Certainly, certainly. Anything more?’\n\n‘Oh, my friend!’ said poor Mr. Tupman, again grasping the hand of his companion, ‘receive my warmest thanks for your disinterested kindness; and forgive me if I have ever, even in thought, done you the injustice of supposing that you could stand in my way. My dear friend, can I ever repay you?’\n\n‘Don’t talk of it,’ replied Mr. Jingle. He stopped short, as if suddenly recollecting something, and said--‘By the bye--can’t spare ten pounds, can you?--very particular purpose--pay you in three days.’\n\n‘I dare say I can,’ replied Mr. Tupman, in the fulness of his heart. ‘Three days, you say?’\n\n‘Only three days--all over then--no more difficulties.’ Mr. Tupman counted the money into his companion’s hand, and he dropped it piece by piece into his pocket, as they walked towards the house.\n\n‘Be careful,’ said Mr. Jingle--‘not a look.’\n\n‘Not a wink,’ said Mr. Tupman.\n\n‘Not a syllable.’\n\n‘Not a whisper.’\n\n‘All your attentions to the niece--rather rude, than otherwise, to the aunt--only way of deceiving the old ones.’\n\n‘I’ll take care,’ said Mr. Tupman aloud.\n\n‘And I’ll take care,’ said Mr. Jingle internally; and they entered the house.\n\nThe scene of that afternoon was repeated that evening, and on the three afternoons and evenings next ensuing. On the fourth, the host was in high spirits, for he had satisfied himself that there was no ground for the charge against Mr. Tupman. So was Mr. Tupman, for Mr. Jingle had told him that his affair would soon be brought to a crisis. So was Mr. Pickwick, for he was seldom otherwise. So was not Mr. Snodgrass, for he had grown jealous of Mr. Tupman. So was the old lady, for she had been winning at whist. So were Mr. Jingle and Miss Wardle, for reasons of sufficient importance in this eventful history to be narrated in another chapter.\n\n# CHAPTER IX. A DISCOVERY AND A CHASE\n\nThe supper was ready laid, the chairs were drawn round the table, bottles, jugs, and glasses were arranged upon the sideboard, and everything betokened the approach of the most convivial period in the whole four-and-twenty hours.\n\n‘Where’s Rachael?’ said Mr. Wardle.\n\n‘Ay, and Jingle?’ added Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Dear me,’ said the host, ‘I wonder I haven’t missed him before. Why, I don’t think I’ve heard his voice for two hours at least. Emily, my dear, ring the bell.’\n\nThe bell was rung, and the fat boy appeared.\n\n‘Where’s Miss Rachael?’ He couldn’t say.\n\n‘Where’s Mr. Jingle, then?’ He didn’t know. Everybody looked surprised. It was late--past eleven o’clock. Mr. Tupman laughed in his sleeve. They were loitering somewhere, talking about him. Ha, ha! capital notion that--funny.\n\n‘Never mind,’ said Wardle, after a short pause. ‘They’ll turn up presently, I dare say. I never wait supper for anybody.’\n\n‘Excellent rule, that,’ said Mr. Pickwick--‘admirable.’\n\n‘Pray, sit down,’ said the host.\n\n‘Certainly’ said Mr. Pickwick; and down they sat.\n\nThere was a gigantic round of cold beef on the table, and Mr. Pickwick was supplied with a plentiful portion of it. He had raised his fork to his lips, and was on the very point of opening his mouth for the reception of a piece of beef, when the hum of many voices suddenly arose in the kitchen. He paused, and laid down his fork. Mr. Wardle paused too, and insensibly released his hold of the carving-knife, which remained inserted in the beef. He looked at Mr. Pickwick. Mr. Pickwick looked at him.\n\nHeavy footsteps were heard in the passage; the parlour door was suddenly burst open; and the man who had cleaned Mr. Pickwick’s boots on his first arrival, rushed into the room, followed by the fat boy and all the domestics.\n\n‘What the devil’s the meaning of this?’ exclaimed the host.\n\n‘The kitchen chimney ain’t a-fire, is it, Emma?’ inquired the old lady.\n\n‘Lor, grandma! No,’ screamed both the young ladies.\n\n‘What’s the matter?’ roared the master of the house.\n\nThe man gasped for breath, and faintly ejaculated--\n\n‘They ha’ gone, mas’r!--gone right clean off, Sir!’ (At this juncture Mr. Tupman was observed to lay down his knife and fork, and to turn very pale.)\n\n‘Who’s gone?’ said Mr. Wardle fiercely.\n\n‘Mus’r Jingle and Miss Rachael, in a po’-chay, from Blue Lion, Muggleton. I was there; but I couldn’t stop ‘em; so I run off to tell ‘ee.’\n\n‘I paid his expenses!’ said Mr. Tupman, jumping up frantically. ‘He’s got ten pounds of mine!--stop him!--he’s swindled me!--I won’t bear it!- -I’ll have justice, Pickwick!--I won’t stand it!’ and with sundry incoherent exclamations of the like nature, the unhappy gentleman spun round and round the apartment, in a transport of frenzy.\n\n‘Lord preserve us!’ ejaculated Mr. Pickwick, eyeing the extraordinary gestures of his friend with terrified surprise. ‘He’s gone mad! What shall we do?’\n\nDo!’ said the stout old host, who regarded only the last words of the sentence. ‘Put the horse in the gig! I’ll get a chaise at the Lion, and follow ‘em instantly. Where?’--he exclaimed, as the man ran out to execute the commission--‘where’s that villain, Joe?’\n\n‘Here I am! but I hain’t a willin,’ replied a voice. It was the fat boy’s.\n\n‘Let me get at him, Pickwick,’ cried Wardle, as he rushed at the ill- starred youth. ‘He was bribed by that scoundrel, Jingle, to put me on a wrong scent, by telling a cock-and-bull story of my sister and your friend Tupman!’ (Here Mr. Tupman sank into a chair.) ‘Let me get at him!’\n\n‘Don’t let him!’ screamed all the women, above whose exclamations the blubbering of the fat boy was distinctly audible.\n\n‘I won’t be held!’ cried the old man. ‘Mr. Winkle, take your hands off. Mr. Pickwick, let me go, sir!’\n\nIt was a beautiful sight, in that moment of turmoil and confusion, to behold the placid and philosophical expression of Mr. Pickwick’s face, albeit somewhat flushed with exertion, as he stood with his arms firmly clasped round the extensive waist of their corpulent host, thus restraining the impetuosity of his passion, while the fat boy was scratched, and pulled, and pushed from the room by all the females congregated therein. He had no sooner released his hold, than the man entered to announce that the gig was ready.\n\n‘Don’t let him go alone!’ screamed the females. ‘He’ll kill somebody!’\n\n‘I’ll go with him,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘You’re a good fellow, Pickwick,’ said the host, grasping his hand. ‘Emma, give Mr. Pickwick a shawl to tie round his neck--make haste. Look after your grandmother, girls; she has fainted away. Now then, are you ready?’\n\nMr. Pickwick’s mouth and chin having been hastily enveloped in a large shawl, his hat having been put on his head, and his greatcoat thrown over his arm, he replied in the affirmative.\n\nThey jumped into the gig. ‘Give her her head, Tom,’ cried the host; and away they went, down the narrow lanes; jolting in and out of the cart- ruts, and bumping up against the hedges on either side, as if they would go to pieces every moment.\n\n‘How much are they ahead?’ shouted Wardle, as they drove up to the door of the Blue Lion, round which a little crowd had collected, late as it was.\n\n‘Not above three-quarters of an hour,’ was everybody’s reply.\n\n‘Chaise-and-four directly!--out with ‘em! Put up the gig afterwards.’\n\n‘Now, boys!’ cried the landlord--‘chaise-and-four out--make haste--look alive there!’\n\nAway ran the hostlers and the boys. The lanterns glimmered, as the men ran to and fro; the horses’ hoofs clattered on the uneven paving of the yard; the chaise rumbled as it was drawn out of the coach-house; and all was noise and bustle.\n\n‘Now then!--is that chaise coming out to-night?’ cried Wardle.\n\n‘Coming down the yard now, Sir,’ replied the hostler.\n\nOut came the chaise--in went the horses--on sprang the boys--in got the travellers.\n\n‘Mind--the seven-mile stage in less than half an hour!’ shouted Wardle.\n\n‘Off with you!’\n\nThe boys applied whip and spur, the waiters shouted, the hostlers cheered, and away they went, fast and furiously.\n\n‘Pretty situation,’ thought Mr. Pickwick, when he had had a moment’s time for reflection. ‘Pretty situation for the general chairman of the Pickwick Club. Damp chaise--strange horses--fifteen miles an hour--and twelve o’clock at night!’\n\nFor the first three or four miles, not a word was spoken by either of the gentlemen, each being too much immersed in his own reflections to address any observations to his companion. When they had gone over that much ground, however, and the horses getting thoroughly warmed began to do their work in really good style, Mr. Pickwick became too much exhilarated with the rapidity of the motion, to remain any longer perfectly mute.\n\n‘We’re sure to catch them, I think,’ said he.\n\n‘Hope so,’ replied his companion.\n\n‘Fine night,’ said Mr. Pickwick, looking up at the moon, which was shining brightly.\n\n‘So much the worse,’ returned Wardle; ‘for they’ll have had all the advantage of the moonlight to get the start of us, and we shall lose it. It will have gone down in another hour.’\n\n‘It will be rather unpleasant going at this rate in the dark, won’t it?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘I dare say it will,’ replied his friend dryly.\n\nMr. Pickwick’s temporary excitement began to sober down a little, as he reflected upon the inconveniences and dangers of the expedition in which he had so thoughtlessly embarked. He was roused by a loud shouting of the post-boy on the leader.\n\n‘Yo-yo-yo-yo-yoe!’ went the first boy.\n\n‘Yo-yo-yo-yoe!’ went the second.\n\n‘Yo-yo-yo-yoe!’ chimed in old Wardle himself, most lustily, with his head and half his body out of the coach window.\n\n‘Yo-yo-yo-yoe!’ shouted Mr. Pickwick, taking up the burden of the cry, though he had not the slightest notion of its meaning or object. And amidst the yo-yoing of the whole four, the chaise stopped.\n\n‘What’s the matter?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘There’s a gate here,’ replied old Wardle. ‘We shall hear something of the fugitives.’\n\nAfter a lapse of five minutes, consumed in incessant knocking and shouting, an old man in his shirt and trousers emerged from the turnpike-house, and opened the gate.\n\n‘How long is it since a post-chaise went through here?’ inquired Mr. Wardle.\n\n‘How long?’\n‘Ah!’\n\n‘Why, I don’t rightly know. It worn’t a long time ago, nor it worn’t a short time ago--just between the two, perhaps.’\n\n‘Has any chaise been by at all?’\n\n‘Oh, yes, there’s been a chay by.’\n\n‘How long ago, my friend,’ interposed Mr. Pickwick; ‘an hour?’\n\n‘Ah, I dare say it might be,’ replied the man.\n\n‘Or two hours?’ inquired the post--boy on the wheeler.\n\n‘Well, I shouldn’t wonder if it was,’ returned the old man doubtfully.\n\n‘Drive on, boys,’ cried the testy old gentleman; ‘don’t waste any more time with that old idiot!’\n\n‘Idiot!’ exclaimed the old man with a grin, as he stood in the middle of the road with the gate half-closed, watching the chaise which rapidly diminished in the increasing distance. ‘No--not much o’ that either; you’ve lost ten minutes here, and gone away as wise as you came, arter all. If every man on the line as has a guinea give him, earns it half as well, you won’t catch t’other chay this side Mich’lmas, old short-and- fat.’ And with another prolonged grin, the old man closed the gate, re- entered his house, and bolted the door after him.\n\nMeanwhile the chaise proceeded, without any slackening of pace, towards the conclusion of the stage. The moon, as Wardle had foretold, was rapidly on the wane; large tiers of dark, heavy clouds, which had been gradually overspreading the sky for some time past, now formed one black mass overhead; and large drops of rain which pattered every now and then against the windows of the chaise, seemed to warn the travellers of the rapid approach of a stormy night. The wind, too, which was directly against them, swept in furious gusts down the narrow road, and howled dismally through the trees which skirted the pathway. Mr. Pickwick drew his coat closer about him, coiled himself more snugly up into the corner of the chaise, and fell into a sound sleep, from which he was only awakened by the stopping of the vehicle, the sound of the hostler’s bell, and a loud cry of ‘Horses on directly!’\n\nBut here another delay occurred. The boys were sleeping with such mysterious soundness, that it took five minutes a-piece to wake them. The hostler had somehow or other mislaid the key of the stable, and even when that was found, two sleepy helpers put the wrong harness on the wrong horses, and the whole process of harnessing had to be gone through afresh. Had Mr. Pickwick been alone, these multiplied obstacles would have completely put an end to the pursuit at once, but old Wardle was not to be so easily daunted; and he laid about him with such hearty good-will, cuffing this man, and pushing that; strapping a buckle here, and taking in a link there, that the chaise was ready in a much shorter time than could reasonably have been expected, under so many difficulties.\n\nThey resumed their journey; and certainly the prospect before them was by no means encouraging. The stage was fifteen miles long, the night was dark, the wind high, and the rain pouring in torrents. It was impossible to make any great way against such obstacles united; it was hard upon one o’clock already; and nearly two hours were consumed in getting to the end of the stage. Here, however, an object presented itself, which rekindled their hopes, and reanimated their drooping spirits.\n\n‘When did this chaise come in?’ cried old Wardle, leaping out of his own vehicle, and pointing to one covered with wet mud, which was standing in the yard.\n\n‘Not a quarter of an hour ago, sir,’ replied the hostler, to whom the question was addressed.\n\n‘Lady and gentleman?’ inquired Wardle, almost breathless with impatience.\n\n‘Yes, sir.’\n\n‘Tall gentleman--dress-coat--long legs--thin body?’\n\n‘Yes, sir.’\n\n‘Elderly lady--thin face--rather skinny--eh?’\n\n‘Yes, sir.’\n\n‘By heavens, it’s the couple, Pickwick,’ exclaimed the old gentleman.\n\n‘Would have been here before,’ said the hostler, ‘but they broke a trace.’\n\n‘’Tis them!’ said Wardle, ‘it is, by Jove! Chaise-and-four instantly! We shall catch them yet before they reach the next stage. A guinea a-piece, boys-be alive there--bustle about--there’s good fellows.’\n\nAnd with such admonitions as these, the old gentleman ran up and down the yard, and bustled to and fro, in a state of excitement which communicated itself to Mr. Pickwick also; and under the influence of which, that gentleman got himself into complicated entanglements with harness, and mixed up with horses and wheels of chaises, in the most surprising manner, firmly believing that by so doing he was materially forwarding the preparations for their resuming their journey.\n\n‘Jump in--jump in!’ cried old Wardle, climbing into the chaise, pulling up the steps, and slamming the door after him. ‘Come along! Make haste!’ And before Mr. Pickwick knew precisely what he was about, he felt himself forced in at the other door, by one pull from the old gentleman and one push from the hostler; and off they were again.\n\n‘Ah! we are moving now,’ said the old gentleman exultingly. They were indeed, as was sufficiently testified to Mr. Pickwick, by his constant collision either with the hard wood-work of the chaise, or the body of his companion.\n\n‘Hold up!’ said the stout old Mr. Wardle, as Mr. Pickwick dived head foremost into his capacious waistcoat.\n\n‘I never did feel such a jolting in my life,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Never mind,’ replied his companion, ‘it will soon be over. Steady, steady.’\n\nMr. Pickwick planted himself into his own corner, as firmly as he could; and on whirled the chaise faster than ever.\n\nThey had travelled in this way about three miles, when Mr. Wardle, who had been looking out of the Window for two or three minutes, suddenly drew in his face, covered with splashes, and exclaimed in breathless eagerness--\n\n‘Here they are!’\n\nMr. Pickwick thrust his head out of his window. Yes: there was a chaise- and-four, a short distance before them, dashing along at full gallop.\n\n‘Go on, go on,’ almost shrieked the old gentleman. ‘Two guineas a-piece, boys--don’t let ‘em gain on us--keep it up--keep it up.’\n\nThe horses in the first chaise started on at their utmost speed; and those in Mr. Wardle’s galloped furiously behind them.\n\n‘I see his head,’ exclaimed the choleric old man; ‘damme, I see his head.’\n\n‘So do I’ said Mr. Pickwick; ‘that’s he.’\n\nMr. Pickwick was not mistaken. The countenance of Mr. Jingle, completely coated with mud thrown up by the wheels, was plainly discernible at the window of his chaise; and the motion of his arm, which was waving violently towards the postillions, denoted that he was encouraging them to increased exertion.\n\nThe interest was intense. Fields, trees, and hedges, seemed to rush past them with the velocity of a whirlwind, so rapid was the pace at which they tore along. They were close by the side of the first chaise. Jingle’s voice could be plainly heard, even above the din of the wheels, urging on the boys. Old Mr. Wardle foamed with rage and excitement. He roared out scoundrels and villains by the dozen, clenched his fist and shook it expressively at the object of his indignation; but Mr. Jingle only answered with a contemptuous smile, and replied to his menaces by a shout of triumph, as his horses, answering the increased application of whip and spur, broke into a faster gallop, and left the pursuers behind.\n\nMr. Pickwick had just drawn in his head, and Mr. Wardle, exhausted with shouting, had done the same, when a tremendous jolt threw them forward against the front of the vehicle. There was a sudden bump--a loud crash- -away rolled a wheel, and over went the chaise.\n\nAfter a very few seconds of bewilderment and confusion, in which nothing but the plunging of horses, and breaking of glass could be made out, Mr. Pickwick felt himself violently pulled out from among the ruins of the chaise; and as soon as he had gained his feet, extricated his head from the skirts of his greatcoat, which materially impeded the usefulness of his spectacles, the full disaster of the case met his view.\n\nOld Mr. Wardle without a hat, and his clothes torn in several places, stood by his side, and the fragments of the chaise lay scattered at their feet. The post-boys, who had succeeded in cutting the traces, were standing, disfigured with mud and disordered by hard riding, by the horses’ heads. About a hundred yards in advance was the other chaise, which had pulled up on hearing the crash. The postillions, each with a broad grin convulsing his countenance, were viewing the adverse party from their saddles, and Mr. Jingle was contemplating the wreck from the coach window, with evident satisfaction. The day was just breaking, and the whole scene was rendered perfectly visible by the grey light of the morning.\n\n‘Hollo!’ shouted the shameless Jingle, ‘anybody damaged?--elderly gentlemen--no light weights--dangerous work--very.’\n\n‘You’re a rascal,’ roared Wardle.\n\n‘Ha! ha!’ replied Jingle; and then he added, with a knowing wink, and a jerk of the thumb towards the interior of the chaise--‘I say--she’s very well--desires her compliments--begs you won’t trouble yourself--love to Tuppy--won’t you get up behind?--drive on, boys.’\n\nThe postillions resumed their proper attitudes, and away rattled the chaise, Mr. Jingle fluttering in derision a white handkerchief from the coach window.\n\nNothing in the whole adventure, not even the upset, had disturbed the calm and equable current of Mr. Pickwick’s temper. The villainy, however, which could first borrow money of his faithful follower, and then abbreviate his name to ‘Tuppy,’ was more than he could patiently bear. He drew his breath hard, and coloured up to the very tips of his spectacles, as he said, slowly and emphatically--\n\n‘If ever I meet that man again, I’ll--’\n\n‘Yes, yes,’ interrupted Wardle, ‘that’s all very well; but while we stand talking here, they’ll get their licence, and be married in London.’\n\nMr. Pickwick paused, bottled up his vengeance, and corked it down. ‘How far is it to the next stage?’ inquired Mr. Wardle, of one of the boys.\n\n‘Six mile, ain’t it, Tom?’\n\n‘Rayther better.’\n\n‘Rayther better nor six mile, Sir.’\n\n‘Can’t be helped,’ said Wardle, ‘we must walk it, Pickwick.’\n\n‘No help for it,’ replied that truly great man.\n\nSo sending forward one of the boys on horseback, to procure a fresh chaise and horses, and leaving the other behind to take care of the broken one, Mr. Pickwick and Mr. Wardle set manfully forward on the walk, first tying their shawls round their necks, and slouching down their hats to escape as much as possible from the deluge of rain, which after a slight cessation had again begun to pour heavily down.\n\nCHAPTER X. CLEARING UP ALL DOUBTS (IF ANY EXISTED) OF THE DISINTERESTEDNESS OF MR. A. JINGLE’S CHARACTER\nThere are in London several old inns, once the headquarters of celebrated coaches in the days when coaches performed their journeys in a graver and more solemn manner than they do in these times; but which have now degenerated into little more than the abiding and booking- places of country wagons. The reader would look in vain for any of these ancient hostelries, among the Golden Crosses and Bull and Mouths, which rear their stately fronts in the improved streets of London. If he would light upon any of these old places, he must direct his steps to the obscurer quarters of the town, and there in some secluded nooks he will find several, still standing with a kind of gloomy sturdiness, amidst the modern innovations which surround them.\n\nIn the Borough especially, there still remain some half-dozen old inns, which have preserved their external features unchanged, and which have escaped alike the rage for public improvement and the encroachments of private speculation. Great, rambling queer old places they are, with galleries, and passages, and staircases, wide enough and antiquated enough to furnish materials for a hundred ghost stories, supposing we should ever be reduced to the lamentable necessity of inventing any, and that the world should exist long enough to exhaust the innumerable veracious legends connected with old London Bridge, and its adjacent neighbourhood on the Surrey side.\n\nIt was in the yard of one of these inns--of no less celebrated a one than the White Hart--that a man was busily employed in brushing the dirt off a pair of boots, early on the morning succeeding the events narrated in the last chapter. He was habited in a coarse, striped waistcoat, with black calico sleeves, and blue glass buttons; drab breeches and leggings. A bright red handkerchief was wound in a very loose and unstudied style round his neck, and an old white hat was carelessly thrown on one side of his head. There were two rows of boots before him, one cleaned and the other dirty, and at every addition he made to the clean row, he paused from his work, and contemplated its results with evident satisfaction.\n\nThe yard presented none of that bustle and activity which are the usual characteristics of a large coach inn. Three or four lumbering wagons, each with a pile of goods beneath its ample canopy, about the height of the second-floor window of an ordinary house, were stowed away beneath a lofty roof which extended over one end of the yard; and another, which was probably to commence its journey that morning, was drawn out into the open space. A double tier of bedroom galleries, with old clumsy balustrades, ran round two sides of the straggling area, and a double row of bells to correspond, sheltered from the weather by a little sloping roof, hung over the door leading to the bar and coffee-room. Two or three gigs and chaise-carts were wheeled up under different little sheds and pent-houses; and the occasional heavy tread of a cart-horse, or rattling of a chain at the farther end of the yard, announced to anybody who cared about the matter, that the stable lay in that direction. When we add that a few boys in smock-frocks were lying asleep on heavy packages, wool-packs, and other articles that were scattered about on heaps of straw, we have described as fully as need be the general appearance of the yard of the White Hart Inn, High Street, Borough, on the particular morning in question.\n\nA loud ringing of one of the bells was followed by the appearance of a smart chambermaid in the upper sleeping gallery, who, after tapping at one of the doors, and receiving a request from within, called over the balustrades--\n\n‘Sam!’\n\n‘Hollo,’ replied the man with the white hat.\n\n‘Number twenty-two wants his boots.’\n\n‘Ask number twenty-two, vether he’ll have ‘em now, or vait till he gets ‘em,’ was the reply.\n\n‘Come, don’t be a fool, Sam,’ said the girl coaxingly, ‘the gentleman wants his boots directly.’\n\n‘Well, you are a nice young ‘ooman for a musical party, you are,’ said the boot-cleaner. ‘Look at these here boots--eleven pair o’ boots; and one shoe as belongs to number six, with the wooden leg. The eleven boots is to be called at half-past eight and the shoe at nine. Who’s number twenty-two, that’s to put all the others out? No, no; reg’lar rotation, as Jack Ketch said, ven he tied the men up. Sorry to keep you a-waitin’, Sir, but I’ll attend to you directly.’\n\nSaying which, the man in the white hat set to work upon a top-boot with increased assiduity.\n\nThere was another loud ring; and the bustling old landlady of the White Hart made her appearance in the opposite gallery.\n\n‘Sam,’ cried the landlady, ‘where’s that lazy, idle--why, Sam--oh, there you are; why don’t you answer?’\n\n‘Vouldn’t be gen-teel to answer, till you’d done talking,’ replied Sam gruffly.\n\n‘Here, clean these shoes for number seventeen directly, and take ‘em to private sitting-room, number five, first floor.’\n\nThe landlady flung a pair of lady’s shoes into the yard, and bustled away.\n\n‘Number five,’ said Sam, as he picked up the shoes, and taking a piece of chalk from his pocket, made a memorandum of their destination on the soles--‘Lady’s shoes and private sittin’-room! I suppose she didn’t come in the vagin.’\n\n‘She came in early this morning,’ cried the girl, who was still leaning over the railing of the gallery, ‘with a gentleman in a hackney-coach, and it’s him as wants his boots, and you’d better do ‘em, that’s all about it.’\n\n‘Vy didn’t you say so before,’ said Sam, with great indignation, singling out the boots in question from the heap before him. ‘For all I know’d he was one o’ the regular threepennies. Private room! and a lady too! If he’s anything of a gen’l’m’n, he’s vurth a shillin’ a day, let alone the arrands.’\n\nStimulated by this inspiring reflection, Mr. Samuel brushed away with such hearty good-will, that in a few minutes the boots and shoes, with a polish which would have struck envy to the soul of the amiable Mr. Warren (for they used Day & Martin at the White Hart), had arrived at the door of number five.\n\n‘Come in,’ said a man’s voice, in reply to Sam’s rap at the door. Sam made his best bow, and stepped into the presence of a lady and gentleman seated at breakfast. Having officiously deposited the gentleman’s boots right and left at his feet, and the lady’s shoes right and left at hers, he backed towards the door.\n\n‘Boots,’ said the gentleman.\n\n‘Sir,’ said Sam, closing the door, and keeping his hand on the knob of the lock.\n\n‘Do you know--what’s a-name--Doctors’ Commons?’\n\n‘Yes, Sir.’\n\n‘Where is it?’\n\n‘Paul’s Churchyard, Sir; low archway on the carriage side, bookseller’s at one corner, hotel on the other, and two porters in the middle as touts for licences.’\n\n‘Touts for licences!’ said the gentleman.\n\n‘Touts for licences,’ replied Sam. ‘Two coves in vhite aprons--touches their hats ven you walk in--“Licence, Sir, licence?” Queer sort, them, and their mas’rs, too, sir--Old Bailey Proctors--and no mistake.’\n\n‘What do they do?’ inquired the gentleman.\n\n‘Do! You, Sir! That ain’t the worst on it, neither. They puts things into old gen’l’m’n’s heads as they never dreamed of. My father, Sir, wos a coachman. A widower he wos, and fat enough for anything--uncommon fat, to be sure. His missus dies, and leaves him four hundred pound. Down he goes to the Commons, to see the lawyer and draw the blunt--very smart-- top boots on--nosegay in his button-hole--broad-brimmed tile--green shawl--quite the gen’l’m’n. Goes through the archvay, thinking how he should inwest the money--up comes the touter, touches his hat--“Licence, Sir, licence?”--“What’s that?” says my father.--“Licence, Sir,” says he.--“What licence?” says my father.--“Marriage licence,” says the touter.--“Dash my veskit,” says my father, “I never thought o’ that.”-- “I think you wants one, Sir,” says the touter. My father pulls up, and thinks a bit--“No,” says he, “damme, I’m too old, b’sides, I’m a many sizes too large,” says he.--“Not a bit on it, Sir,” says the touter.-- “Think not?” says my father.--“I’m sure not,” says he; “we married a gen’l’m’n twice your size, last Monday.”--“Did you, though?” said my father.--“To be sure, we did,” says the touter, “you’re a babby to him-- this way, sir--this way!”--and sure enough my father walks arter him, like a tame monkey behind a horgan, into a little back office, vere a teller sat among dirty papers, and tin boxes, making believe he was busy. “Pray take a seat, vile I makes out the affidavit, Sir,” says the lawyer.--“Thank’ee, Sir,” says my father, and down he sat, and stared with all his eyes, and his mouth vide open, at the names on the boxes. “What’s your name, Sir,” says the lawyer.--“Tony Weller,” says my father.--“Parish?” says the lawyer. “Belle Savage,” says my father; for he stopped there wen he drove up, and he know’d nothing about parishes, he didn’t.--“And what’s the lady’s name?” says the lawyer. My father was struck all of a heap. “Blessed if I know,” says he.--“Not know!” says the lawyer.--“No more nor you do,” says my father; “can’t I put that in arterwards?”--“Impossible!” says the lawyer.--“Wery well,” says my father, after he’d thought a moment, “put down Mrs. Clarke.”--“What Clarke?” says the lawyer, dipping his pen in the ink.--“Susan Clarke, Markis o’ Granby, Dorking,” says my father; “she’ll have me, if I ask. I des-say--I never said nothing to her, but she’ll have me, I know.” The licence was made out, and she did have him, and what’s more she’s got him now; and I never had any of the four hundred pound, worse luck. Beg your pardon, sir,’ said Sam, when he had concluded, ‘but wen I gets on this here grievance, I runs on like a new barrow with the wheel greased.’ Having said which, and having paused for an instant to see whether he was wanted for anything more, Sam left the room.\n\n‘Half-past nine--just the time--off at once;’ said the gentleman, whom we need hardly introduce as Mr. Jingle.\n\n‘Time--for what?’ said the spinster aunt coquettishly.\n\n‘Licence, dearest of angels--give notice at the church--call you mine, to-morrow’--said Mr. Jingle, and he squeezed the spinster aunt’s hand.\n\n‘The licence!’ said Rachael, blushing.\n\n‘The licence,’ repeated Mr. Jingle--\n\n‘In hurry, post-haste for a licence, In hurry, ding dong I come back.’\n\n‘How you run on,’ said Rachael.\n\n‘Run on--nothing to the hours, days, weeks, months, years, when we’re united--run on--they’ll fly on--bolt--mizzle--steam-engine--thousand- horse power--nothing to it.’\n\n‘Can’t--can’t we be married before to-morrow morning?’ inquired Rachael.\n\n‘Impossible--can’t be--notice at the church--leave the licence to-day-- ceremony come off to-morrow.’\n\nI am so terrified, lest my brother should discover us!’ said Rachael.\n\n‘Discover--nonsense--too much shaken by the break-down--besides--extreme caution--gave up the post-chaise--walked on--took a hackney-coach--came to the Borough--last place in the world that he’d look in--ha! ha!-- capital notion that--very.’\n\n‘Don’t be long,’ said the spinster affectionately, as Mr. Jingle stuck the pinched-up hat on his head.\n\n‘Long away from you?--Cruel charmer,’ and Mr. Jingle skipped playfully up to the spinster aunt, imprinted a chaste kiss upon her lips, and danced out of the room.\n\n‘Dear man!’ said the spinster, as the door closed after him.\n\n‘Rum old girl,’ said Mr. Jingle, as he walked down the passage.\n\nIt is painful to reflect upon the perfidy of our species; and we will not, therefore, pursue the thread of Mr. Jingle’s meditations, as he wended his way to Doctors’ Commons. It will be sufficient for our purpose to relate, that escaping the snares of the dragons in white aprons, who guard the entrance to that enchanted region, he reached the vicar-general’s office in safety and having procured a highly flattering address on parchment, from the Archbishop of Canterbury, to his ‘trusty and well-beloved Alfred Jingle and Rachael Wardle, greeting,’ he carefully deposited the mystic document in his pocket, and retraced his steps in triumph to the Borough.\n\nHe was yet on his way to the White Hart, when two plump gentleman and one thin one entered the yard, and looked round in search of some authorised person of whom they could make a few inquiries. Mr. Samuel Weller happened to be at that moment engaged in burnishing a pair of painted tops, the personal property of a farmer who was refreshing himself with a slight lunch of two or three pounds of cold beef and a pot or two of porter, after the fatigues of the Borough market; and to him the thin gentleman straightway advanced.\n\n‘My friend,’ said the thin gentleman.\n\n‘You’re one o’ the adwice gratis order,’ thought Sam, ‘or you wouldn’t be so wery fond o’ me all at once.’ But he only said--‘Well, Sir.’\n\n‘My friend,’ said the thin gentleman, with a conciliatory hem--‘have you got many people stopping here now? Pretty busy. Eh?’\n\nSam stole a look at the inquirer. He was a little high-dried man, with a dark squeezed-up face, and small, restless, black eyes, that kept winking and twinkling on each side of his little inquisitive nose, as if they were playing a perpetual game of peep-bo with that feature. He was dressed all in black, with boots as shiny as his eyes, a low white neckcloth, and a clean shirt with a frill to it. A gold watch-chain, and seals, depended from his fob. He carried his black kid gloves in his hands, and not ON them; and as he spoke, thrust his wrists beneath his coat tails, with the air of a man who was in the habit of propounding some regular posers.\n\n‘Pretty busy, eh?’ said the little man.\n\n‘Oh, wery well, Sir,’ replied Sam, ‘we shan’t be bankrupts, and we shan’t make our fort’ns. We eats our biled mutton without capers, and don’t care for horse-radish ven ve can get beef.’\n\n‘Ah,’ said the little man, ‘you’re a wag, ain’t you?’\n\n‘My eldest brother was troubled with that complaint,’ said Sam; ‘it may be catching--I used to sleep with him.’\n\n‘This is a curious old house of yours,’ said the little man, looking round him.\n\n‘If you’d sent word you was a-coming, we’d ha’ had it repaired;’ replied the imperturbable Sam.\n\nThe little man seemed rather baffled by these several repulses, and a short consultation took place between him and the two plump gentlemen. At its conclusion, the little man took a pinch of snuff from an oblong silver box, and was apparently on the point of renewing the conversation, when one of the plump gentlemen, who in addition to a benevolent countenance, possessed a pair of spectacles, and a pair of black gaiters, interfered--\n\n‘The fact of the matter is,’ said the benevolent gentleman, ‘that my friend here (pointing to the other plump gentleman) will give you half a guinea, if you’ll answer one or two--’\n\n‘Now, my dear sir--my dear Sir,’ said the little man, ‘pray, allow me-- my dear Sir, the very first principle to be observed in these cases, is this: if you place the matter in the hands of a professional man, you must in no way interfere in the progress of the business; you must repose implicit confidence in him. Really, Mr.--’ He turned to the other plump gentleman, and said, ‘I forget your friend’s name.’\n\n‘Pickwick,’ said Mr. Wardle, for it was no other than that jolly personage.\n\n‘Ah, Pickwick--really Mr. Pickwick, my dear Sir, excuse me--I shall be happy to receive any private suggestions of yours, as AMICUS CURIAE, but you must see the impropriety of your interfering with my conduct in this case, with such an AD CAPTANDUM argument as the offer of half a guinea. Really, my dear Sir, really;’ and the little man took an argumentative pinch of snuff, and looked very profound.\n\n‘My only wish, Sir,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘was to bring this very unpleasant matter to as speedy a close as possible.’\n\n‘Quite right--quite right,’ said the little man.\n\n‘With which view,’ continued Mr. Pickwick, ‘I made use of the argument which my experience of men has taught me is the most likely to succeed in any case.’\n\n‘Ay, ay,’ said the little man, ‘very good, very good, indeed; but you should have suggested it to me. My dear sir, I’m quite certain you cannot be ignorant of the extent of confidence which must be placed in professional men. If any authority can be necessary on such a point, my dear sir, let me refer you to the well-known case in Barnwell and--’\n\n‘Never mind George Barnwell,’ interrupted Sam, who had remained a wondering listener during this short colloquy; ‘everybody knows what sort of a case his was, tho’ it’s always been my opinion, mind you, that the young ‘ooman deserved scragging a precious sight more than he did. Hows’ever, that’s neither here nor there. You want me to accept of half a guinea. Wery well, I’m agreeable: I can’t say no fairer than that, can I, sir?’ (Mr. Pickwick smiled.) Then the next question is, what the devil do you want with me, as the man said, wen he see the ghost?’\n\n‘We want to know--’ said Mr. Wardle.\n\n‘Now, my dear sir--my dear sir,’ interposed the busy little man.\n\nMr. Wardle shrugged his shoulders, and was silent.\n\n‘We want to know,’ said the little man solemnly; ‘and we ask the question of you, in order that we may not awaken apprehensions inside-- we want to know who you’ve got in this house at present?’\n\n‘Who there is in the house!’ said Sam, in whose mind the inmates were always represented by that particular article of their costume, which came under his immediate superintendence. ‘There’s a vooden leg in number six; there’s a pair of Hessians in thirteen; there’s two pair of halves in the commercial; there’s these here painted tops in the snuggery inside the bar; and five more tops in the coffee-room.’\n\n‘Nothing more?’ said the little man.\n\n‘Stop a bit,’ replied Sam, suddenly recollecting himself. ‘Yes; there’s a pair of Vellingtons a good deal worn, and a pair o’ lady’s shoes, in number five.’\n\n‘What sort of shoes?’ hastily inquired Wardle, who, together with Mr. Pickwick, had been lost in bewilderment at the singular catalogue of visitors.\n\n‘Country make,’ replied Sam.\n\n‘Any maker’s name?’\n\n‘Brown.’\n‘Where of?’\n\n‘Muggleton.\n\n‘It is them,’ exclaimed Wardle. ‘By heavens, we’ve found them.’\n\n‘Hush!’ said Sam. ‘The Vellingtons has gone to Doctors’ Commons.’\n\n‘No,’ said the little man.\n\n‘Yes, for a licence.’\n\n‘We’re in time,’ exclaimed Wardle. ‘Show us the room; not a moment is to be lost.’\n\n‘Pray, my dear sir--pray,’ said the little man; ‘caution, caution.’ He drew from his pocket a red silk purse, and looked very hard at Sam as he drew out a sovereign.\n\nSam grinned expressively.\n\n‘Show us into the room at once, without announcing us,’ said the little man, ‘and it’s yours.’\n\nSam threw the painted tops into a corner, and led the way through a dark passage, and up a wide staircase. He paused at the end of a second passage, and held out his hand.\n\n‘Here it is,’ whispered the attorney, as he deposited the money on the hand of their guide.\n\nThe man stepped forward for a few paces, followed by the two friends and their legal adviser. He stopped at a door.\n\n‘Is this the room?’ murmured the little gentleman.\n\nSam nodded assent.\n\nOld Wardle opened the door; and the whole three walked into the room just as Mr. Jingle, who had that moment returned, had produced the licence to the spinster aunt.\n\nThe spinster uttered a loud shriek, and throwing herself into a chair, covered her face with her hands. Mr. Jingle crumpled up the licence, and thrust it into his coat pocket. The unwelcome visitors advanced into the middle of the room.\n\n‘You--you are a nice rascal, arn’t you?’ exclaimed Wardle, breathless with passion.\n\n‘My dear Sir, my dear sir,’ said the little man, laying his hat on the table, ‘pray, consider--pray. Defamation of character: action for damages. Calm yourself, my dear sir, pray--’\n\n‘How dare you drag my sister from my house?’ said the old man.\n\nAy--ay--very good,’ said the little gentleman, ‘you may ask that. How dare you, sir?--eh, sir?’\n\n‘Who the devil are you?’ inquired Mr. Jingle, in so fierce a tone, that the little gentleman involuntarily fell back a step or two.\n\n‘Who is he, you scoundrel,’ interposed Wardle. ‘He’s my lawyer, Mr. Perker, of Gray’s Inn. Perker, I’ll have this fellow prosecuted-- indicted--I’ll--I’ll--I’ll ruin him. And you,’ continued Mr. Wardle, turning abruptly round to his sister--‘you, Rachael, at a time of life when you ought to know better, what do you mean by running away with a vagabond, disgracing your family, and making yourself miserable? Get on your bonnet and come back. Call a hackney-coach there, directly, and bring this lady’s bill, d’ye hear--d’ye hear?’\n\nCert’nly, Sir,’ replied Sam, who had answered Wardle’s violent ringing of the bell with a degree of celerity which must have appeared marvellous to anybody who didn’t know that his eye had been applied to the outside of the keyhole during the whole interview.\n\n‘Get on your bonnet,’ repeated Wardle.\n\n‘Do nothing of the kind,’ said Jingle. ‘Leave the room, Sir--no business here--lady’s free to act as she pleases--more than one-and-twenty.’\n\n‘More than one-and-twenty!’ ejaculated Wardle contemptuously. ‘More than one-and-forty!’\n\n‘I ain’t,’ said the spinster aunt, her indignation getting the better of her determination to faint.\n\n‘You are,’ replied Wardle; ‘you’re fifty if you’re an hour.’\n\nHere the spinster aunt uttered a loud shriek, and became senseless.\n\n‘A glass of water,’ said the humane Mr. Pickwick, summoning the landlady.\n\n‘A glass of water!’ said the passionate Wardle. ‘Bring a bucket, and throw it all over her; it’ll do her good, and she richly deserves it.’\n\n‘Ugh, you brute!’ ejaculated the kind-hearted landlady. ‘Poor dear.’ And with sundry ejaculations of ‘Come now, there’s a dear--drink a little of this--it’ll do you good--don’t give way so--there’s a love,’ etc. etc., the landlady, assisted by a chambermaid, proceeded to vinegar the forehead, beat the hands, titillate the nose, and unlace the stays of the spinster aunt, and to administer such other restoratives as are usually applied by compassionate females to ladies who are endeavouring to ferment themselves into hysterics.\n\n‘Coach is ready, Sir,’ said Sam, appearing at the door.\n\n‘Come along,’ cried Wardle. ‘I’ll carry her downstairs.’\n\nAt this proposition, the hysterics came on with redoubled violence.\n\nThe landlady was about to enter a very violent protest against this proceeding, and had already given vent to an indignant inquiry whether Mr. Wardle considered himself a lord of the creation, when Mr. Jingle interposed--\n\n‘Boots,’ said he, ‘get me an officer.’\n\n‘Stay, stay,’ said little Mr. Perker. ‘Consider, Sir, consider.’\n\n‘I’ll not consider,’ replied Jingle. ‘She’s her own mistress--see who dares to take her away--unless she wishes it.’\n\n‘I won’t be taken away,’ murmured the spinster aunt. ‘I don’t wish it.’ (Here there was a frightful relapse.)\n\n‘My dear Sir,’ said the little man, in a low tone, taking Mr. Wardle and Mr. Pickwick apart--‘my dear Sir, we’re in a very awkward situation. It’s a distressing case--very; I never knew one more so; but really, my dear sir, really we have no power to control this lady’s actions. I warned you before we came, my dear sir, that there was nothing to look to but a compromise.’\n\nThere was a short pause.\n\n‘What kind of compromise would you recommend?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Why, my dear Sir, our friend’s in an unpleasant position--very much so. We must be content to suffer some pecuniary loss.’\n\n‘I’ll suffer any, rather than submit to this disgrace, and let her, fool as she is, be made miserable for life,’ said Wardle.\n\n‘I rather think it can be done,’ said the bustling little man. ‘Mr. Jingle, will you step with us into the next room for a moment?’\n\nMr. Jingle assented, and the quartette walked into an empty apartment.\n\n‘Now, sir,’ said the little man, as he carefully closed the door, ‘is there no way of accommodating this matter--step this way, sir, for a moment--into this window, Sir, where we can be alone--there, sir, there, pray sit down, sir. Now, my dear Sir, between you and I, we know very well, my dear Sir, that you have run off with this lady for the sake of her money. Don’t frown, Sir, don’t frown; I say, between you and I, we know it. We are both men of the world, and WE know very well that our friends here, are not--eh?’\n\nMr. Jingle’s face gradually relaxed; and something distantly resembling a wink quivered for an instant in his left eye.\n\n‘Very good, very good,’ said the little man, observing the impression he had made. ‘Now, the fact is, that beyond a few hundreds, the lady has little or nothing till the death of her mother--fine old lady, my dear Sir.’\n\n‘Old,’ said Mr. Jingle briefly but emphatically.\n\n‘Why, yes,’ said the attorney, with a slight cough. ‘You are right, my dear Sir, she is rather old. She comes of an old family though, my dear Sir; old in every sense of the word. The founder of that family came into Kent when Julius Caesar invaded Britain;--only one member of it, since, who hasn’t lived to eighty-five, and he was beheaded by one of the Henrys. The old lady is not seventy-three now, my dear Sir.’ The little man paused, and took a pinch of snuff.\n\n‘Well,’ cried Mr. Jingle.\n\n‘Well, my dear sir--you don’t take snuff!--ah! so much the better-- expensive habit--well, my dear Sir, you’re a fine young man, man of the world--able to push your fortune, if you had capital, eh?’\n\n‘Well,’ said Mr. Jingle again.\n\n‘Do you comprehend me?’\n\n‘Not quite.’\n\n‘Don’t you think--now, my dear Sir, I put it to you don’t you think-- that fifty pounds and liberty would be better than Miss Wardle and expectation?’\n\n‘Won’t do--not half enough!’ said Mr. Jingle, rising.\n\n‘Nay, nay, my dear Sir,’ remonstrated the little attorney, seizing him by the button. ‘Good round sum--a man like you could treble it in no time--great deal to be done with fifty pounds, my dear Sir.’\n\n‘More to be done with a hundred and fifty,’ replied Mr. Jingle coolly.\n\n‘Well, my dear Sir, we won’t waste time in splitting straws,’ resumed the little man, ‘say--say--seventy.’\n\nWon’t do,’ said Mr. Jingle.\n\n‘Don’t go away, my dear sir--pray don’t hurry,’ said the little man. ‘Eighty; come: I’ll write you a cheque at once.’\n\n‘Won’t do,’ said Mr. Jingle.\n\n‘Well, my dear Sir, well,’ said the little man, still detaining him; ‘just tell me what will do.’\n\n‘Expensive affair,’ said Mr. Jingle. ‘Money out of pocket--posting, nine pounds; licence, three--that’s twelve--compensation, a hundred--hundred and twelve--breach of honour--and loss of the lady--’\n\n‘Yes, my dear Sir, yes,’ said the little man, with a knowing look, ‘never mind the last two items. That’s a hundred and twelve--say a hundred--come.’\n\n‘And twenty,’ said Mr. Jingle.\n\n‘Come, come, I’ll write you a cheque,’ said the little man; and down he sat at the table for that purpose.\n\n‘I’ll make it payable the day after to-morrow,’ said the little man, with a look towards Mr. Wardle; ‘and we can get the lady away, meanwhile.’ Mr. Wardle sullenly nodded assent.\n\n‘A hundred,’ said the little man.\n\n‘And twenty,’ said Mr. Jingle.\n\n‘My dear Sir,’ remonstrated the little man.\n\n‘Give it him,’ interposed Mr. Wardle, ‘and let him go.’\n\nThe cheque was written by the little gentleman, and pocketed by Mr. Jingle.\n\n‘Now, leave this house instantly!’ said Wardle, starting up.\n\n‘My dear Sir,’ urged the little man.\n\n‘And mind,’ said Mr. Wardle, ‘that nothing should have induced me to make this compromise--not even a regard for my family--if I had not known that the moment you got any money in that pocket of yours, you’d go to the devil faster, if possible, than you would without it--’\n\n‘My dear sir,’ urged the little man again.\n\n‘Be quiet, Perker,’ resumed Wardle. ‘Leave the room, Sir.’\n\n‘Off directly,’ said the unabashed Jingle. ‘Bye bye, Pickwick.’\n\nIf any dispassionate spectator could have beheld the countenance of the illustrious man, whose name forms the leading feature of the title of this work, during the latter part of this conversation, he would have been almost induced to wonder that the indignant fire which flashed from his eyes did not melt the glasses of his spectacles--so majestic was his wrath. His nostrils dilated, and his fists clenched involuntarily, as he heard himself addressed by the villain. But he restrained himself again- -he did not pulverise him.\n\n‘Here,’ continued the hardened traitor, tossing the licence at Mr. Pickwick’s feet; ‘get the name altered--take home the lady--do for Tuppy.’\n\nMr. Pickwick was a philosopher, but philosophers are only men in armour, after all. The shaft had reached him, penetrated through his philosophical harness, to his very heart. In the frenzy of his rage, he hurled the inkstand madly forward, and followed it up himself. But Mr. Jingle had disappeared, and he found himself caught in the arms of Sam.\n\n‘Hollo,’ said that eccentric functionary, ‘furniter’s cheap where you come from, Sir. Self-acting ink, that ‘ere; it’s wrote your mark upon the wall, old gen’l’m’n. Hold still, Sir; wot’s the use o’ runnin’ arter a man as has made his lucky, and got to t’other end of the Borough by this time?’\n\nMr. Pickwick’s mind, like those of all truly great men, was open to conviction. He was a quick and powerful reasoner; and a moment’s reflection sufficed to remind him of the impotency of his rage. It subsided as quickly as it had been roused. He panted for breath, and looked benignantly round upon his friends.\n\nShall we tell the lamentations that ensued when Miss Wardle found herself deserted by the faithless Jingle? Shall we extract Mr. Pickwick’s masterly description of that heartrending scene? His note- book, blotted with the tears of sympathising humanity, lies open before us; one word, and it is in the printer’s hands. But, no! we will be resolute! We will not wring the public bosom, with the delineation of such suffering!\n\nSlowly and sadly did the two friends and the deserted lady return next day in the Muggleton heavy coach. Dimly and darkly had the sombre shadows of a summer’s night fallen upon all around, when they again reached Dingley Dell, and stood within the entrance to Manor Farm.\n\nCHAPTER XI. INVOLVING ANOTHER JOURNEY, AND AN ANTIQUARIAN DISCOVERY; RECORDING MR. PICKWICK’S DETERMINATION TO BE PRESENT AT AN ELECTION; AND CONTAINING A MANUSCRIPT OF THE OLD CLERGYMAN’S\nA night of quiet and repose in the profound silence of Dingley Dell, and an hour’s breathing of its fresh and fragrant air on the ensuing morning, completely recovered Mr. Pickwick from the effects of his late fatigue of body and anxiety of mind. That illustrious man had been separated from his friends and followers for two whole days; and it was with a degree of pleasure and delight, which no common imagination can adequately conceive, that he stepped forward to greet Mr. Winkle and Mr. Snodgrass, as he encountered those gentlemen on his return from his early walk. The pleasure was mutual; for who could ever gaze on Mr. Pickwick’s beaming face without experiencing the sensation? But still a cloud seemed to hang over his companions which that great man could not but be sensible of, and was wholly at a loss to account for. There was a mysterious air about them both, as unusual as it was alarming.\n\n‘And how,’ said Mr. Pickwick, when he had grasped his followers by the hand, and exchanged warm salutations of welcome--‘how is Tupman?’\n\nMr. Winkle, to whom the question was more peculiarly addressed, made no reply. He turned away his head, and appeared absorbed in melancholy reflection.\n\n‘Snodgrass,’ said Mr. Pickwick earnestly, ‘how is our friend--he is not ill?’\n\n‘No,’ replied Mr. Snodgrass; and a tear trembled on his sentimental eyelid, like a rain-drop on a window-frame--‘no; he is not ill.’\n\nMr. Pickwick stopped, and gazed on each of his friends in turn.\n\n‘Winkle--Snodgrass,’ said Mr. Pickwick; ‘what does this mean? Where is our friend? What has happened? Speak--I conjure, I entreat--nay, I command you, speak.’\n\nThere was a solemnity--a dignity--in Mr. Pickwick’s manner, not to be withstood.\n\n‘He is gone,’ said Mr. Snodgrass.\n\n‘Gone!’ exclaimed Mr. Pickwick. ‘Gone!’\n\n‘Gone,’ repeated Mr. Snodgrass.\n\n‘Where!’ ejaculated Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘We can only guess, from that communication,’ replied Mr. Snodgrass, taking a letter from his pocket, and placing it in his friend’s hand. ‘Yesterday morning, when a letter was received from Mr. Wardle, stating that you would be home with his sister at night, the melancholy which had hung over our friend during the whole of the previous day, was observed to increase. He shortly afterwards disappeared: he was missing during the whole day, and in the evening this letter was brought by the hostler from the Crown, at Muggleton. It had been left in his charge in the morning, with a strict injunction that it should not be delivered until night.’\n\nMr. Pickwick opened the epistle. It was in his friend’s hand-writing, and these were its contents:--\n\n‘MY DEAR PICKWICK,--You, my dear friend, are placed far beyond the reach of many mortal frailties and weaknesses which ordinary people cannot overcome. You do not know what it is, at one blow, to be deserted by a lovely and fascinating creature, and to fall a victim to the artifices of a villain, who had the grin of cunning beneath the mask of friendship. I hope you never may.\n\n‘Any letter addressed to me at the Leather Bottle, Cobham, Kent, will be forwarded--supposing I still exist. I hasten from the sight of that world, which has become odious to me. Should I hasten from it altogether, pity--forgive me. Life, my dear Pickwick, has become insupportable to me. The spirit which burns within us, is a porter’s knot, on which to rest the heavy load of worldly cares and troubles; and when that spirit fails us, the burden is too heavy to be borne. We sink beneath it. You may tell Rachael--Ah, that name!--\n\n‘We must leave this place directly,’ said Mr. Pickwick, as he refolded the note. ‘It would not have been decent for us to remain here, under any circumstances, after what has happened; and now we are bound to follow in search of our friend.’ And so saying, he led the way to the house.\n\nHis intention was rapidly communicated. The entreaties to remain were pressing, but Mr. Pickwick was inflexible. Business, he said, required his immediate attendance.\n\nThe old clergyman was present.\n\n‘You are not really going?’ said he, taking Mr. Pickwick aside.\n\nMr. Pickwick reiterated his former determination.\n\n‘Then here,’ said the old gentleman, ‘is a little manuscript, which I had hoped to have the pleasure of reading to you myself. I found it on the death of a friend of mine--a medical man, engaged in our county lunatic asylum--among a variety of papers, which I had the option of destroying or preserving, as I thought proper. I can hardly believe that the manuscript is genuine, though it certainly is not in my friend’s hand. However, whether it be the genuine production of a maniac, or founded upon the ravings of some unhappy being (which I think more probable), read it, and judge for yourself.’\n\nMr. Pickwick received the manuscript, and parted from the benevolent old gentleman with many expressions of good-will and esteem.\n\nIt was a more difficult task to take leave of the inmates of Manor Farm, from whom they had received so much hospitality and kindness. Mr. Pickwick kissed the young ladies--we were going to say, as if they were his own daughters, only, as he might possibly have infused a little more warmth into the salutation, the comparison would not be quite appropriate--hugged the old lady with filial cordiality; and patted the rosy cheeks of the female servants in a most patriarchal manner, as he slipped into the hands of each some more substantial expression of his approval. The exchange of cordialities with their fine old host and Mr. Trundle was even more hearty and prolonged; and it was not until Mr. Snodgrass had been several times called for, and at last emerged from a dark passage followed soon after by Emily (whose bright eyes looked unusually dim), that the three friends were enabled to tear themselves from their friendly entertainers. Many a backward look they gave at the farm, as they walked slowly away; and many a kiss did Mr. Snodgrass waft in the air, in acknowledgment of something very like a lady’s handkerchief, which was waved from one of the upper windows, until a turn of the lane hid the old house from their sight.\n\nAt Muggleton they procured a conveyance to Rochester. By the time they reached the last-named place, the violence of their grief had sufficiently abated to admit of their making a very excellent early dinner; and having procured the necessary information relative to the road, the three friends set forward again in the afternoon to walk to Cobham.\n\nA delightful walk it was; for it was a pleasant afternoon in June, and their way lay through a deep and shady wood, cooled by the light wind which gently rustled the thick foliage, and enlivened by the songs of the birds that perched upon the boughs. The ivy and the moss crept in thick clusters over the old trees, and the soft green turf overspread the ground like a silken mat. They emerged upon an open park, with an ancient hall, displaying the quaint and picturesque architecture of Elizabeth’s time. Long vistas of stately oaks and elm trees appeared on every side; large herds of deer were cropping the fresh grass; and occasionally a startled hare scoured along the ground, with the speed of the shadows thrown by the light clouds which swept across a sunny landscape like a passing breath of summer.\n\n‘If this,’ said Mr. Pickwick, looking about him--‘if this were the place to which all who are troubled with our friend’s complaint came, I fancy their old attachment to this world would very soon return.’\n\n‘I think so too,’ said Mr. Winkle.\n\n‘And really,’ added Mr. Pickwick, after half an hour’s walking had brought them to the village, ‘really, for a misanthrope’s choice, this is one of the prettiest and most desirable places of residence I ever met with.’\n\nIn this opinion also, both Mr. Winkle and Mr. Snodgrass expressed their concurrence; and having been directed to the Leather Bottle, a clean and commodious village ale-house, the three travellers entered, and at once inquired for a gentleman of the name of Tupman.\n\n‘Show the gentlemen into the parlour, Tom,’ said the landlady.\n\nA stout country lad opened a door at the end of the passage, and the three friends entered a long, low-roofed room, furnished with a large number of high-backed leather-cushioned chairs, of fantastic shapes, and embellished with a great variety of old portraits and roughly-coloured prints of some antiquity. At the upper end of the room was a table, with a white cloth upon it, well covered with a roast fowl, bacon, ale, and et ceteras; and at the table sat Mr. Tupman, looking as unlike a man who had taken his leave of the world, as possible.\n\nOn the entrance of his friends, that gentleman laid down his knife and fork, and with a mournful air advanced to meet them.\n\n‘I did not expect to see you here,’ he said, as he grasped Mr. Pickwick’s hand. ‘It’s very kind.’\n\n‘Ah!’ said Mr. Pickwick, sitting down, and wiping from his forehead the perspiration which the walk had engendered. ‘Finish your dinner, and walk out with me. I wish to speak to you alone.’\n\nMr. Tupman did as he was desired; and Mr. Pickwick having refreshed himself with a copious draught of ale, waited his friend’s leisure. The dinner was quickly despatched, and they walked out together.\n\nFor half an hour, their forms might have been seen pacing the churchyard to and fro, while Mr. Pickwick was engaged in combating his companion’s resolution. Any repetition of his arguments would be useless; for what language could convey to them that energy and force which their great originator’s manner communicated? Whether Mr. Tupman was already tired of retirement, or whether he was wholly unable to resist the eloquent appeal which was made to him, matters not, he did not resist it at last.\n\n‘It mattered little to him,’ he said, ‘where he dragged out the miserable remainder of his days; and since his friend laid so much stress upon his humble companionship, he was willing to share his adventures.’\n\nMr. Pickwick smiled; they shook hands, and walked back to rejoin their companions.\n\nIt was at this moment that Mr. Pickwick made that immortal discovery, which has been the pride and boast of his friends, and the envy of every antiquarian in this or any other country. They had passed the door of their inn, and walked a little way down the village, before they recollected the precise spot in which it stood. As they turned back, Mr. Pickwick’s eye fell upon a small broken stone, partially buried in the ground, in front of a cottage door. He paused.\n\n‘This is very strange,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘What is strange?’ inquired Mr. Tupman, staring eagerly at every object near him, but the right one. ‘God bless me, what’s the matter?’\n\nThis last was an ejaculation of irrepressible astonishment, occasioned by seeing Mr. Pickwick, in his enthusiasm for discovery, fall on his knees before the little stone, and commence wiping the dust off it with his pocket-handkerchief.\n\n‘There is an inscription here,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Is it possible?’ said Mr. Tupman.\n\n‘I can discern,’ continued Mr. Pickwick, rubbing away with all his might, and gazing intently through his spectacles--‘I can discern a cross, and a 13, and then a T. This is important,’ continued Mr. Pickwick, starting up. ‘This is some very old inscription, existing perhaps long before the ancient alms-houses in this place. It must not be lost.’\n\nHe tapped at the cottage door. A labouring man opened it.\n\n‘Do you know how this stone came here, my friend?’ inquired the benevolent Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘No, I doan’t, Sir,’ replied the man civilly. ‘It was here long afore I was born, or any on us.’\n\nMr. Pickwick glanced triumphantly at his companion.\n\n‘You--you--are not particularly attached to it, I dare say,’ said Mr. Pickwick, trembling with anxiety. ‘You wouldn’t mind selling it, now?’\n\n‘Ah! but who’d buy it?’ inquired the man, with an expression of face which he probably meant to be very cunning.\n\n‘I’ll give you ten shillings for it, at once,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘if you would take it up for me.’\n\nThe astonishment of the village may be easily imagined, when (the little stone having been raised with one wrench of a spade) Mr. Pickwick, by dint of great personal exertion, bore it with his own hands to the inn, and after having carefully washed it, deposited it on the table.\n\nThe exultation and joy of the Pickwickians knew no bounds, when their patience and assiduity, their washing and scraping, were crowned with success. The stone was uneven and broken, and the letters were straggling and irregular, but the following fragment of an inscription was clearly to be deciphered:--\n\nMr. Pickwick’s eyes sparkled with delight, as he sat and gloated over the treasure he had discovered. He had attained one of the greatest objects of his ambition. In a county known to abound in the remains of the early ages; in a village in which there still existed some memorials of the olden time, he--he, the chairman of the Pickwick Club--had discovered a strange and curious inscription of unquestionable antiquity, which had wholly escaped the observation of the many learned men who had preceded him. He could hardly trust the evidence of his senses.\n\n‘This--this,’ said he, ‘determines me. We return to town to-morrow.’\n\n‘To-morrow!’ exclaimed his admiring followers.\n\n‘To-morrow,’ said Mr. Pickwick. ‘This treasure must be at once deposited where it can be thoroughly investigated and properly understood. I have another reason for this step. In a few days, an election is to take place for the borough of Eatanswill, at which Mr. Perker, a gentleman whom I lately met, is the agent of one of the candidates. We will behold, and minutely examine, a scene so interesting to every Englishman.’\n\n‘We will,’ was the animated cry of three voices.\n\nMr. Pickwick looked round him. The attachment and fervour of his followers lighted up a glow of enthusiasm within him. He was their leader, and he felt it.\n\n‘Let us celebrate this happy meeting with a convivial glass,’ said he. This proposition, like the other, was received with unanimous applause. Having himself deposited the important stone in a small deal box, purchased from the landlady for the purpose, he placed himself in an arm-chair, at the head of the table; and the evening was devoted to festivity and conversation.\n\nIt was past eleven o’clock--a late hour for the little village of Cobham--when Mr. Pickwick retired to the bedroom which had been prepared for his reception. He threw open the lattice window, and setting his light upon the table, fell into a train of meditation on the hurried events of the two preceding days.\n\nThe hour and the place were both favourable to contemplation; Mr. Pickwick was roused by the church clock striking twelve. The first stroke of the hour sounded solemnly in his ear, but when the bell ceased the stillness seemed insupportable--he almost felt as if he had lost a companion. He was nervous and excited; and hastily undressing himself and placing his light in the chimney, got into bed.\n\nEvery one has experienced that disagreeable state of mind, in which a sensation of bodily weariness in vain contends against an inability to sleep. It was Mr. Pickwick’s condition at this moment: he tossed first on one side and then on the other; and perseveringly closed his eyes as if to coax himself to slumber. It was of no use. Whether it was the unwonted exertion he had undergone, or the heat, or the brandy-and- water, or the strange bed--whatever it was, his thoughts kept reverting very uncomfortably to the grim pictures downstairs, and the old stories to which they had given rise in the course of the evening. After half an hour’s tumbling about, he came to the unsatisfactory conclusion, that it was of no use trying to sleep; so he got up and partially dressed himself. Anything, he thought, was better than lying there fancying all kinds of horrors. He looked out of the window--it was very dark. He walked about the room--it was very lonely.\n\nHe had taken a few turns from the door to the window, and from the window to the door, when the clergyman’s manuscript for the first time entered his head. It was a good thought. If it failed to interest him, it might send him to sleep. He took it from his coat pocket, and drawing a small table towards his bedside, trimmed the light, put on his spectacles, and composed himself to read. It was a strange handwriting, and the paper was much soiled and blotted. The title gave him a sudden start, too; and he could not avoid casting a wistful glance round the room. Reflecting on the absurdity of giving way to such feelings, however, he trimmed the light again, and read as follows:--\n\n# A MADMAN’S MANUSCRIPT\n\n‘Yes!--a madman’s! How that word would have struck to my heart, many years ago! How it would have roused the terror that used to come upon me sometimes, sending the blood hissing and tingling through my veins, till the cold dew of fear stood in large drops upon my skin, and my knees knocked together with fright! I like it now though. It’s a fine name. Show me the monarch whose angry frown was ever feared like the glare of a madman’s eye--whose cord and axe were ever half so sure as a madman’s gripe. Ho! ho! It’s a grand thing to be mad! to be peeped at like a wild lion through the iron bars--to gnash one’s teeth and howl, through the long still night, to the merry ring of a heavy chain and to roll and twine among the straw, transported with such brave music. Hurrah for the madhouse! Oh, it’s a rare place!\n\n‘I remember days when I was afraid of being mad; when I used to start from my sleep, and fall upon my knees, and pray to be spared from the curse of my race; when I rushed from the sight of merriment or happiness, to hide myself in some lonely place, and spend the weary hours in watching the progress of the fever that was to consume my brain. I knew that madness was mixed up with my very blood, and the marrow of my bones! that one generation had passed away without the pestilence appearing among them, and that I was the first in whom it would revive. I knew it must be so: that so it always had been, and so it ever would be: and when I cowered in some obscure corner of a crowded room, and saw men whisper, and point, and turn their eyes towards me, I knew they were telling each other of the doomed madman; and I slunk away again to mope in solitude.\n\n‘I did this for years; long, long years they were. The nights here are long sometimes--very long; but they are nothing to the restless nights, and dreadful dreams I had at that time. It makes me cold to remember them. Large dusky forms with sly and jeering faces crouched in the corners of the room, and bent over my bed at night, tempting me to madness. They told me in low whispers, that the floor of the old house in which my father died, was stained with his own blood, shed by his own hand in raging madness. I drove my fingers into my ears, but they screamed into my head till the room rang with it, that in one generation before him the madness slumbered, but that his grandfather had lived for years with his hands fettered to the ground, to prevent his tearing himself to pieces. I knew they told the truth--I knew it well. I had found it out years before, though they had tried to keep it from me. Ha! ha! I was too cunning for them, madman as they thought me.\n\n‘At last it came upon me, and I wondered how I could ever have feared it. I could go into the world now, and laugh and shout with the best among them. I knew I was mad, but they did not even suspect it. How I used to hug myself with delight, when I thought of the fine trick I was playing them after their old pointing and leering, when I was not mad, but only dreading that I might one day become so! And how I used to laugh for joy, when I was alone, and thought how well I kept my secret, and how quickly my kind friends would have fallen from me, if they had known the truth. I could have screamed with ecstasy when I dined alone with some fine roaring fellow, to think how pale he would have turned, and how fast he would have run, if he had known that the dear friend who sat close to him, sharpening a bright, glittering knife, was a madman with all the power, and half the will, to plunge it in his heart. Oh, it was a merry life!\n\n‘Riches became mine, wealth poured in upon me, and I rioted in pleasures enhanced a thousandfold to me by the consciousness of my well-kept secret. I inherited an estate. The law--the eagle-eyed law itself--had been deceived, and had handed over disputed thousands to a madman’s hands. Where was the wit of the sharp-sighted men of sound mind? Where the dexterity of the lawyers, eager to discover a flaw? The madman’s cunning had overreached them all.\n\n‘I had money. How I was courted! I spent it profusely. How I was praised! How those three proud, overbearing brothers humbled themselves before me! The old, white-headed father, too--such deference--such respect--such devoted friendship--he worshipped me! The old man had a daughter, and the young men a sister; and all the five were poor. I was rich; and when I married the girl, I saw a smile of triumph play upon the faces of her needy relatives, as they thought of their well-planned scheme, and their fine prize. It was for me to smile. To smile! To laugh outright, and tear my hair, and roll upon the ground with shrieks of merriment. They little thought they had married her to a madman.\n\n‘Stay. If they had known it, would they have saved her? A sister’s happiness against her husband’s gold. The lightest feather I blow into the air, against the gay chain that ornaments my body!\n\n‘In one thing I was deceived with all my cunning. If I had not been mad- -for though we madmen are sharp-witted enough, we get bewildered sometimes--I should have known that the girl would rather have been placed, stiff and cold in a dull leaden coffin, than borne an envied bride to my rich, glittering house. I should have known that her heart was with the dark-eyed boy whose name I once heard her breathe in her troubled sleep; and that she had been sacrificed to me, to relieve the poverty of the old, white-headed man and the haughty brothers.\n\n‘I don’t remember forms or faces now, but I know the girl was beautiful. I know she was; for in the bright moonlight nights, when I start up from my sleep, and all is quiet about me, I see, standing still and motionless in one corner of this cell, a slight and wasted figure with long black hair, which, streaming down her back, stirs with no earthly wind, and eyes that fix their gaze on me, and never wink or close. Hush! the blood chills at my heart as I write it down--that form is her’s; the face is very pale, and the eyes are glassy bright; but I know them well. That figure never moves; it never frowns and mouths as others do, that fill this place sometimes; but it is much more dreadful to me, even than the spirits that tempted me many years ago--it comes fresh from the grave; and is so very death-like.\n\n‘For nearly a year I saw that face grow paler; for nearly a year I saw the tears steal down the mournful cheeks, and never knew the cause. I found it out at last though. They could not keep it from me long. She had never liked me; I had never thought she did: she despised my wealth, and hated the splendour in which she lived; but I had not expected that. She loved another. This I had never thought of. Strange feelings came over me, and thoughts, forced upon me by some secret power, whirled round and round my brain. I did not hate her, though I hated the boy she still wept for. I pitied--yes, I pitied--the wretched life to which her cold and selfish relations had doomed her. I knew that she could not live long; but the thought that before her death she might give birth to some ill-fated being, destined to hand down madness to its offspring, determined me. I resolved to kill her.\n\n‘For many weeks I thought of poison, and then of drowning, and then of fire. A fine sight, the grand house in flames, and the madman’s wife smouldering away to cinders. Think of the jest of a large reward, too, and of some sane man swinging in the wind for a deed he never did, and all through a madman’s cunning! I thought often of this, but I gave it up at last. Oh! the pleasure of stropping the razor day after day, feeling the sharp edge, and thinking of the gash one stroke of its thin, bright edge would make!\n\n‘At last the old spirits who had been with me so often before whispered in my ear that the time was come, and thrust the open razor into my hand. I grasped it firmly, rose softly from the bed, and leaned over my sleeping wife. Her face was buried in her hands. I withdrew them softly, and they fell listlessly on her bosom. She had been weeping; for the traces of the tears were still wet upon her cheek. Her face was calm and placid; and even as I looked upon it, a tranquil smile lighted up her pale features. I laid my hand softly on her shoulder. She started--it was only a passing dream. I leaned forward again. She screamed, and woke.\n\n‘One motion of my hand, and she would never again have uttered cry or sound. But I was startled, and drew back. Her eyes were fixed on mine. I knew not how it was, but they cowed and frightened me; and I quailed beneath them. She rose from the bed, still gazing fixedly and steadily on me. I trembled; the razor was in my hand, but I could not move. She made towards the door. As she neared it, she turned, and withdrew her eyes from my face. The spell was broken. I bounded forward, and clutched her by the arm. Uttering shriek upon shriek, she sank upon the ground.\n\n‘Now I could have killed her without a struggle; but the house was alarmed. I heard the tread of footsteps on the stairs. I replaced the razor in its usual drawer, unfastened the door, and called loudly for assistance.\n\n‘They came, and raised her, and placed her on the bed. She lay bereft of animation for hours; and when life, look, and speech returned, her senses had deserted her, and she raved wildly and furiously.\n\n‘Doctors were called in--great men who rolled up to my door in easy carriages, with fine horses and gaudy servants. They were at her bedside for weeks. They had a great meeting and consulted together in low and solemn voices in another room. One, the cleverest and most celebrated among them, took me aside, and bidding me prepare for the worst, told me--me, the madman!--that my wife was mad. He stood close beside me at an open window, his eyes looking in my face, and his hand laid upon my arm. With one effort, I could have hurled him into the street beneath. It would have been rare sport to have done it; but my secret was at stake, and I let him go. A few days after, they told me I must place her under some restraint: I must provide a keeper for her. I! I went into the open fields where none could hear me, and laughed till the air resounded with my shouts!\n\n‘She died next day. The white-headed old man followed her to the grave, and the proud brothers dropped a tear over the insensible corpse of her whose sufferings they had regarded in her lifetime with muscles of iron. All this was food for my secret mirth, and I laughed behind the white handkerchief which I held up to my face, as we rode home, till the tears came into my eyes.\n\n‘But though I had carried my object and killed her, I was restless and disturbed, and I felt that before long my secret must be known. I could not hide the wild mirth and joy which boiled within me, and made me when I was alone, at home, jump up and beat my hands together, and dance round and round, and roar aloud. When I went out, and saw the busy crowds hurrying about the streets; or to the theatre, and heard the sound of music, and beheld the people dancing, I felt such glee, that I could have rushed among them, and torn them to pieces limb from limb, and howled in transport. But I ground my teeth, and struck my feet upon the floor, and drove my sharp nails into my hands. I kept it down; and no one knew I was a madman yet.\n\n‘I remember--though it’s one of the last things I can remember: for now I mix up realities with my dreams, and having so much to do, and being always hurried here, have no time to separate the two, from some strange confusion in which they get involved--I remember how I let it out at last. Ha! ha! I think I see their frightened looks now, and feel the ease with which I flung them from me, and dashed my clenched fist into their white faces, and then flew like the wind, and left them screaming and shouting far behind. The strength of a giant comes upon me when I think of it. There--see how this iron bar bends beneath my furious wrench. I could snap it like a twig, only there are long galleries here with many doors--I don’t think I could find my way along them; and even if I could, I know there are iron gates below which they keep locked and barred. They know what a clever madman I have been, and they are proud to have me here, to show.\n\n‘Let me see: yes, I had been out. It was late at night when I reached home, and found the proudest of the three proud brothers waiting to see me--urgent business he said: I recollect it well. I hated that man with all a madman’s hate. Many and many a time had my fingers longed to tear him. They told me he was there. I ran swiftly upstairs. He had a word to say to me. I dismissed the servants. It was late, and we were alone together--for the first time.\n\n‘I kept my eyes carefully from him at first, for I knew what he little thought--and I gloried in the knowledge--that the light of madness gleamed from them like fire. We sat in silence for a few minutes. He spoke at last. My recent dissipation, and strange remarks, made so soon after his sister’s death, were an insult to her memory. Coupling together many circumstances which had at first escaped his observation, he thought I had not treated her well. He wished to know whether he was right in inferring that I meant to cast a reproach upon her memory, and a disrespect upon her family. It was due to the uniform he wore, to demand this explanation.\n\n‘This man had a commission in the army--a commission, purchased with my money, and his sister’s misery! This was the man who had been foremost in the plot to ensnare me, and grasp my wealth. This was the man who had been the main instrument in forcing his sister to wed me; well knowing that her heart was given to that puling boy. Due to his uniform! The livery of his degradation! I turned my eyes upon him--I could not help it--but I spoke not a word.\n\n‘I saw the sudden change that came upon him beneath my gaze. He was a bold man, but the colour faded from his face, and he drew back his chair. I dragged mine nearer to him; and I laughed--I was very merry then--I saw him shudder. I felt the madness rising within me. He was afraid of me.\n\n‘“You were very fond of your sister when she was alive,” I said.-- “Very.”\n\n‘He looked uneasily round him, and I saw his hand grasp the back of his chair; but he said nothing.\n\n‘“You villain,” said I, “I found you out: I discovered your hellish plots against me; I know her heart was fixed on some one else before you compelled her to marry me. I know it--I know it.”\n\n‘He jumped suddenly from his chair, brandished it aloft, and bid me stand back--for I took care to be getting closer to him all the time I spoke.\n\n‘I screamed rather than talked, for I felt tumultuous passions eddying through my veins, and the old spirits whispering and taunting me to tear his heart out.\n\n‘“Damn you,” said I, starting up, and rushing upon him; “I killed her. I am a madman. Down with you. Blood, blood! I will have it!”\n\n‘I turned aside with one blow the chair he hurled at me in his terror, and closed with him; and with a heavy crash we rolled upon the floor together.\n\n‘It was a fine struggle that; for he was a tall, strong man, fighting for his life; and I, a powerful madman, thirsting to destroy him. I knew no strength could equal mine, and I was right. Right again, though a madman! His struggles grew fainter. I knelt upon his chest, and clasped his brawny throat firmly with both hands. His face grew purple; his eyes were starting from his head, and with protruded tongue, he seemed to mock me. I squeezed the tighter.\n\n‘The door was suddenly burst open with a loud noise, and a crowd of people rushed forward, crying aloud to each other to secure the madman.\n\n‘My secret was out; and my only struggle now was for liberty and freedom. I gained my feet before a hand was on me, threw myself among my assailants, and cleared my way with my strong arm, as if I bore a hatchet in my hand, and hewed them down before me. I gained the door, dropped over the banisters, and in an instant was in the street.\n\n‘Straight and swift I ran, and no one dared to stop me. I heard the noise of the feet behind, and redoubled my speed. It grew fainter and fainter in the distance, and at length died away altogether; but on I bounded, through marsh and rivulet, over fence and wall, with a wild shout which was taken up by the strange beings that flocked around me on every side, and swelled the sound, till it pierced the air. I was borne upon the arms of demons who swept along upon the wind, and bore down bank and hedge before them, and spun me round and round with a rustle and a speed that made my head swim, until at last they threw me from them with a violent shock, and I fell heavily upon the earth. When I woke I found myself here--here in this gray cell, where the sunlight seldom comes, and the moon steals in, in rays which only serve to show the dark shadows about me, and that silent figure in its old corner. When I lie awake, I can sometimes hear strange shrieks and cries from distant parts of this large place. What they are, I know not; but they neither come from that pale form, nor does it regard them. For from the first shades of dusk till the earliest light of morning, it still stands motionless in the same place, listening to the music of my iron chain, and watching my gambols on my straw bed.’\n\nAt the end of the manuscript was written, in another hand, this note:--\n\n[The unhappy man whose ravings are recorded above, was a melancholy instance of the baneful results of energies misdirected in early life, and excesses prolonged until their consequences could never be repaired. The thoughtless riot, dissipation, and debauchery of his younger days produced fever and delirium. The first effects of the latter was the strange delusion, founded upon a well-known medical theory, strongly contended for by some, and as strongly contested by others, that an hereditary madness existed in his family. This produced a settled gloom, which in time developed a morbid insanity, and finally terminated in raving madness. There is every reason to believe that the events he detailed, though distorted in the description by his diseased imagination, really happened. It is only matter of wonder to those who were acquainted with the vices of his early career, that his passions, when no longer controlled by reason, did not lead him to the commission of still more frightful deeds.]\n\nMr. Pickwick’s candle was just expiring in the socket, as he concluded the perusal of the old clergyman’s manuscript; and when the light went suddenly out, without any previous flicker by way of warning, it communicated a very considerable start to his excited frame. Hastily throwing off such articles of clothing as he had put on when he rose from his uneasy bed, and casting a fearful glance around, he once more scrambled hastily between the sheets, and soon fell fast asleep.\n\nThe sun was shining brilliantly into his chamber, when he awoke, and the morning was far advanced. The gloom which had oppressed him on the previous night had disappeared with the dark shadows which shrouded the landscape, and his thoughts and feelings were as light and gay as the morning itself. After a hearty breakfast, the four gentlemen sallied forth to walk to Gravesend, followed by a man bearing the stone in its deal box. They reached the town about one o’clock (their luggage they had directed to be forwarded to the city, from Rochester), and being fortunate enough to secure places on the outside of a coach, arrived in London in sound health and spirits, on that same afternoon.\n\nThe next three or four days were occupied with the preparations which were necessary for their journey to the borough of Eatanswill. As any references to that most important undertaking demands a separate chapter, we may devote the few lines which remain at the close of this, to narrate, with great brevity, the history of the antiquarian discovery.\n\nIt appears from the Transactions of the Club, then, that Mr. Pickwick lectured upon the discovery at a General Club Meeting, convened on the night succeeding their return, and entered into a variety of ingenious and erudite speculations on the meaning of the inscription. It also appears that a skilful artist executed a faithful delineation of the curiosity, which was engraven on stone, and presented to the Royal Antiquarian Society, and other learned bodies: that heart-burnings and jealousies without number were created by rival controversies which were penned upon the subject; and that Mr. Pickwick himself wrote a pamphlet, containing ninety-six pages of very small print, and twenty-seven different readings of the inscription: that three old gentlemen cut off their eldest sons with a shilling a-piece for presuming to doubt the antiquity of the fragment; and that one enthusiastic individual cut himself off prematurely, in despair at being unable to fathom its meaning: that Mr. Pickwick was elected an honorary member of seventeen native and foreign societies, for making the discovery: that none of the seventeen could make anything of it; but that all the seventeen agreed it was very extraordinary.\n\nMr. Blotton, indeed--and the name will be doomed to the undying contempt of those who cultivate the mysterious and the sublime--Mr. Blotton, we say, with the doubt and cavilling peculiar to vulgar minds, presumed to state a view of the case, as degrading as ridiculous. Mr. Blotton, with a mean desire to tarnish the lustre of the immortal name of Pickwick, actually undertook a journey to Cobham in person, and on his return, sarcastically observed in an oration at the club, that he had seen the man from whom the stone was purchased; that the man presumed the stone to be ancient, but solemnly denied the antiquity of the inscription-- inasmuch as he represented it to have been rudely carved by himself in an idle mood, and to display letters intended to bear neither more or less than the simple construction of--‘BILL STUMPS, HIS MARK’; and that Mr. Stumps, being little in the habit of original composition, and more accustomed to be guided by the sound of words than by the strict rules of orthography, had omitted the concluding ‘L’ of his Christian name.\n\nThe Pickwick Club (as might have been expected from so enlightened an institution) received this statement with the contempt it deserved, expelled the presumptuous and ill-conditioned Blotton from the society, and voted Mr. Pickwick a pair of gold spectacles, in token of their confidence and approbation: in return for which, Mr. Pickwick caused a portrait of himself to be painted, and hung up in the club room.\n\nMr. Blotton was ejected but not conquered. He also wrote a pamphlet, addressed to the seventeen learned societies, native and foreign, containing a repetition of the statement he had already made, and rather more than half intimating his opinion that the seventeen learned societies were so many ‘humbugs.’ Hereupon, the virtuous indignation of the seventeen learned societies being roused, several fresh pamphlets appeared; the foreign learned societies corresponded with the native learned societies; the native learned societies translated the pamphlets of the foreign learned societies into English; the foreign learned societies translated the pamphlets of the native learned societies into all sorts of languages; and thus commenced that celebrated scientific discussion so well known to all men, as the Pickwick controversy.\n\nBut this base attempt to injure Mr. Pickwick recoiled upon the head of its calumnious author. The seventeen learned societies unanimously voted the presumptuous Blotton an ignorant meddler, and forthwith set to work upon more treatises than ever. And to this day the stone remains, an illegible monument of Mr. Pickwick’s greatness, and a lasting trophy to the littleness of his enemies.\n\nCHAPTER XII. DESCRIPTIVE OF A VERY IMPORTANT PROCEEDING ON THE PART OF MR. PICKWICK; NO LESS AN EPOCH IN HIS LIFE, THAN IN THIS HISTORY\nMr. Pickwick’s apartments in Goswell Street, although on a limited scale, were not only of a very neat and comfortable description, but peculiarly adapted for the residence of a man of his genius and observation. His sitting-room was the first-floor front, his bedroom the second-floor front; and thus, whether he were sitting at his desk in his parlour, or standing before the dressing-glass in his dormitory, he had an equal opportunity of contemplating human nature in all the numerous phases it exhibits, in that not more populous than popular thoroughfare. His landlady, Mrs. Bardell--the relict and sole executrix of a deceased custom-house officer--was a comely woman of bustling manners and agreeable appearance, with a natural genius for cooking, improved by study and long practice, into an exquisite talent. There were no children, no servants, no fowls. The only other inmates of the house were a large man and a small boy; the first a lodger, the second a production of Mrs. Bardell’s. The large man was always home precisely at ten o’clock at night, at which hour he regularly condensed himself into the limits of a dwarfish French bedstead in the back parlour; and the infantine sports and gymnastic exercises of Master Bardell were exclusively confined to the neighbouring pavements and gutters. Cleanliness and quiet reigned throughout the house; and in it Mr. Pickwick’s will was law.\n\nTo any one acquainted with these points of the domestic economy of the establishment, and conversant with the admirable regulation of Mr. Pickwick’s mind, his appearance and behaviour on the morning previous to that which had been fixed upon for the journey to Eatanswill would have been most mysterious and unaccountable. He paced the room to and fro with hurried steps, popped his head out of the window at intervals of about three minutes each, constantly referred to his watch, and exhibited many other manifestations of impatience very unusual with him. It was evident that something of great importance was in contemplation, but what that something was, not even Mrs. Bardell had been enabled to discover.\n\n‘Mrs. Bardell,’ said Mr. Pickwick, at last, as that amiable female approached the termination of a prolonged dusting of the apartment.\n\n‘Sir,’ said Mrs. Bardell.\n\n‘Your little boy is a very long time gone.’\n\n‘Why it’s a good long way to the Borough, sir,’ remonstrated Mrs. Bardell.\n\n‘Ah,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘very true; so it is.’ Mr. Pickwick relapsed into silence, and Mrs. Bardell resumed her dusting.\n\n‘Mrs. Bardell,’ said Mr. Pickwick, at the expiration of a few minutes.\n\n‘Sir,’ said Mrs. Bardell again.\n\n‘Do you think it a much greater expense to keep two people, than to keep one?’\n\n‘La, Mr. Pickwick,’ said Mrs. Bardell, colouring up to the very border of her cap, as she fancied she observed a species of matrimonial twinkle in the eyes of her lodger; ‘La, Mr. Pickwick, what a question!’\n\n‘Well, but do you?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘That depends,’ said Mrs. Bardell, approaching the duster very near to Mr. Pickwick’s elbow which was planted on the table. ‘That depends a good deal upon the person, you know, Mr. Pickwick; and whether it’s a saving and careful person, sir.’\n\n‘That’s very true,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘but the person I have in my eye (here he looked very hard at Mrs. Bardell) I think possesses these qualities; and has, moreover, a considerable knowledge of the world, and a great deal of sharpness, Mrs. Bardell, which may be of material use to me.’\n\n‘La, Mr. Pickwick,’ said Mrs. Bardell, the crimson rising to her cap- border again.\n\n‘I do,’ said Mr. Pickwick, growing energetic, as was his wont in speaking of a subject which interested him--‘I do, indeed; and to tell you the truth, Mrs. Bardell, I have made up my mind.’\n\n‘Dear me, sir,’ exclaimed Mrs. Bardell.\n\n‘You’ll think it very strange now,’ said the amiable Mr. Pickwick, with a good-humoured glance at his companion, ‘that I never consulted you about this matter, and never even mentioned it, till I sent your little boy out this morning--eh?’\n\nMrs. Bardell could only reply by a look. She had long worshipped Mr. Pickwick at a distance, but here she was, all at once, raised to a pinnacle to which her wildest and most extravagant hopes had never dared to aspire. Mr. Pickwick was going to propose--a deliberate plan, too-- sent her little boy to the Borough, to get him out of the way--how thoughtful--how considerate!\n\n‘Well,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘what do you think?’\n\n‘Oh, Mr. Pickwick,’ said Mrs. Bardell, trembling with agitation, ‘you’re very kind, sir.’\n\n‘It’ll save you a good deal of trouble, won’t it?’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Oh, I never thought anything of the trouble, sir,’ replied Mrs. Bardell; ‘and, of course, I should take more trouble to please you then, than ever; but it is so kind of you, Mr. Pickwick, to have so much consideration for my loneliness.’\n\n‘Ah, to be sure,’ said Mr. Pickwick; ‘I never thought of that. When I am in town, you’ll always have somebody to sit with you. To be sure, so you will.’\n\n‘I am sure I ought to be a very happy woman,’ said Mrs. Bardell.\n\n‘And your little boy--’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Bless his heart!’ interposed Mrs. Bardell, with a maternal sob.\n\n‘He, too, will have a companion,’ resumed Mr. Pickwick, ‘a lively one, who’ll teach him, I’ll be bound, more tricks in a week than he would ever learn in a year.’ And Mr. Pickwick smiled placidly.\n\n‘Oh, you dear--’ said Mrs. Bardell.\n\nMr. Pickwick started.\n\n‘Oh, you kind, good, playful dear,’ said Mrs. Bardell; and without more ado, she rose from her chair, and flung her arms round Mr. Pickwick’s neck, with a cataract of tears and a chorus of sobs.\n\n‘Bless my soul,’ cried the astonished Mr. Pickwick; ‘Mrs. Bardell, my good woman--dear me, what a situation--pray consider.--Mrs. Bardell, don’t--if anybody should come--’\n\n‘Oh, let them come,’ exclaimed Mrs. Bardell frantically; ‘I’ll never leave you--dear, kind, good soul;’ and, with these words, Mrs. Bardell clung the tighter.\n\n‘Mercy upon me,’ said Mr. Pickwick, struggling violently, ‘I hear somebody coming up the stairs. Don’t, don’t, there’s a good creature, don’t.’ But entreaty and remonstrance were alike unavailing; for Mrs. Bardell had fainted in Mr. Pickwick’s arms; and before he could gain time to deposit her on a chair, Master Bardell entered the room, ushering in Mr. Tupman, Mr. Winkle, and Mr. Snodgrass.\n\nMr. Pickwick was struck motionless and speechless. He stood with his lovely burden in his arms, gazing vacantly on the countenances of his friends, without the slightest attempt at recognition or explanation. They, in their turn, stared at him; and Master Bardell, in his turn, stared at everybody.\n\nThe astonishment of the Pickwickians was so absorbing, and the perplexity of Mr. Pickwick was so extreme, that they might have remained in exactly the same relative situations until the suspended animation of the lady was restored, had it not been for a most beautiful and touching expression of filial affection on the part of her youthful son. Clad in a tight suit of corduroy, spangled with brass buttons of a very considerable size, he at first stood at the door astounded and uncertain; but by degrees, the impression that his mother must have suffered some personal damage pervaded his partially developed mind, and considering Mr. Pickwick as the aggressor, he set up an appalling and semi-earthly kind of howling, and butting forward with his head, commenced assailing that immortal gentleman about the back and legs, with such blows and pinches as the strength of his arm, and the violence of his excitement, allowed.\n\n‘Take this little villain away,’ said the agonised Mr. Pickwick, ‘he’s mad.’\n\n‘What is the matter?’ said the three tongue-tied Pickwickians.\n\n‘I don’t know,’ replied Mr. Pickwick pettishly. ‘Take away the boy.’ (Here Mr. Winkle carried the interesting boy, screaming and struggling, to the farther end of the apartment.) ‘Now help me, lead this woman downstairs.’\n\n‘Oh, I am better now,’ said Mrs. Bardell faintly.\n\n‘Let me lead you downstairs,’ said the ever-gallant Mr. Tupman.\n\n‘Thank you, sir--thank you;’ exclaimed Mrs. Bardell hysterically. And downstairs she was led accordingly, accompanied by her affectionate son.\n\n‘I cannot conceive,’ said Mr. Pickwick when his friend returned--‘I cannot conceive what has been the matter with that woman. I had merely announced to her my intention of keeping a man-servant, when she fell into the extraordinary paroxysm in which you found her. Very extraordinary thing.’\n\n‘Very,’ said his three friends.\n\n‘Placed me in such an extremely awkward situation,’ continued Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Very,’ was the reply of his followers, as they coughed slightly, and looked dubiously at each other.\n\nThis behaviour was not lost upon Mr. Pickwick. He remarked their incredulity. They evidently suspected him.\n\n‘There is a man in the passage now,’ said Mr. Tupman.\n\n‘It’s the man I spoke to you about,’ said Mr. Pickwick; ‘I sent for him to the Borough this morning. Have the goodness to call him up, Snodgrass.’\n\nMr. Snodgrass did as he was desired; and Mr. Samuel Weller forthwith presented himself.\n\n‘Oh--you remember me, I suppose?’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘I should think so,’ replied Sam, with a patronising wink. ‘Queer start that ‘ere, but he was one too many for you, warn’t he? Up to snuff and a pinch or two over--eh?’\n\n‘Never mind that matter now,’ said Mr. Pickwick hastily; ‘I want to speak to you about something else. Sit down.’\n\n‘Thank’ee, sir,’ said Sam. And down he sat without further bidding, having previously deposited his old white hat on the landing outside the door. ‘’Tain’t a wery good ‘un to look at,’ said Sam, ‘but it’s an astonishin’ ‘un to wear; and afore the brim went, it was a wery handsome tile. Hows’ever it’s lighter without it, that’s one thing, and every hole lets in some air, that’s another--wentilation gossamer I calls it.’ On the delivery of this sentiment, Mr. Weller smiled agreeably upon the assembled Pickwickians.\n\n‘Now with regard to the matter on which I, with the concurrence of these gentlemen, sent for you,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘That’s the pint, sir,’ interposed Sam; ‘out vith it, as the father said to his child, when he swallowed a farden.’\n\n‘We want to know, in the first place,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘whether you have any reason to be discontented with your present situation.’\n\n‘Afore I answers that ‘ere question, gen’l’m’n,’ replied Mr. Weller, ‘I should like to know, in the first place, whether you’re a-goin’ to purwide me with a better?’\n\nA sunbeam of placid benevolence played on Mr. Pickwick’s features as he said, ‘I have half made up my mind to engage you myself.’\n\n‘Have you, though?’ said Sam.\n\nMr. Pickwick nodded in the affirmative.\n\n‘Wages?’ inquired Sam.\n\n‘Twelve pounds a year,’ replied Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Clothes?’\n\n‘Two suits.’\n\n‘Work?’\n\n‘To attend upon me; and travel about with me and these gentlemen here.’\n\n‘Take the bill down,’ said Sam emphatically. ‘I’m let to a single gentleman, and the terms is agreed upon.’\n\n‘You accept the situation?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Cert’nly,’ replied Sam. ‘If the clothes fits me half as well as the place, they’ll do.’\n\n‘You can get a character of course?’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Ask the landlady o’ the White Hart about that, Sir,’ replied Sam.\n\n‘Can you come this evening?’\n\n‘I’ll get into the clothes this minute, if they’re here,’ said Sam, with great alacrity.\n\n‘Call at eight this evening,’ said Mr. Pickwick; ‘and if the inquiries are satisfactory, they shall be provided.’\n\nWith the single exception of one amiable indiscretion, in which an assistant housemaid had equally participated, the history of Mr. Weller’s conduct was so very blameless, that Mr. Pickwick felt fully justified in closing the engagement that very evening. With the promptness and energy which characterised not only the public proceedings, but all the private actions of this extraordinary man, he at once led his new attendant to one of those convenient emporiums where gentlemen’s new and second-hand clothes are provided, and the troublesome and inconvenient formality of measurement dispensed with; and before night had closed in, Mr. Weller was furnished with a grey coat with the P. C. button, a black hat with a cockade to it, a pink striped waistcoat, light breeches and gaiters, and a variety of other necessaries, too numerous to recapitulate.\n\n‘Well,’ said that suddenly-transformed individual, as he took his seat on the outside of the Eatanswill coach next morning; ‘I wonder whether I’m meant to be a footman, or a groom, or a gamekeeper, or a seedsman. I looks like a sort of compo of every one on ‘em. Never mind; there’s a change of air, plenty to see, and little to do; and all this suits my complaint uncommon; so long life to the Pickvicks, says I!’\n\nCHAPTER XIII. SOME ACCOUNT OF EATANSWILL; OF THE STATE OF PARTIES THEREIN; AND OF THE ELECTION OF A MEMBER TO SERVE IN PARLIAMENT FOR THAT ANCIENT, LOYAL, AND PATRIOTIC BOROUGH\n\nWe will frankly acknowledge that, up to the period of our being first immersed in the voluminous papers of the Pickwick Club, we had never heard of Eatanswill; we will with equal candour admit that we have in vain searched for proof of the actual existence of such a place at the present day. Knowing the deep reliance to be placed on every note and statement of Mr. Pickwick’s, and not presuming to set up our recollection against the recorded declarations of that great man, we have consulted every authority, bearing upon the subject, to which we could possibly refer. We have traced every name in schedules A and B, without meeting with that of Eatanswill; we have minutely examined every corner of the pocket county maps issued for the benefit of society by our distinguished publishers, and the same result has attended our investigation. We are therefore led to believe that Mr. Pickwick, with that anxious desire to abstain from giving offence to any, and with those delicate feelings for which all who knew him well know he was so eminently remarkable, purposely substituted a fictitious designation, for the real name of the place in which his observations were made. We are confirmed in this belief by a little circumstance, apparently slight and trivial in itself, but when considered in this point of view, not undeserving of notice. In Mr. Pickwick’s note-book, we can just trace an entry of the fact, that the places of himself and followers were booked by the Norwich coach; but this entry was afterwards lined through, as if for the purpose of concealing even the direction in which the borough is situated. We will not, therefore, hazard a guess upon the subject, but will at once proceed with this history, content with the materials which its characters have provided for us.\n\nIt appears, then, that the Eatanswill people, like the people of many other small towns, considered themselves of the utmost and most mighty importance, and that every man in Eatanswill, conscious of the weight that attached to his example, felt himself bound to unite, heart and soul, with one of the two great parties that divided the town--the Blues and the Buffs. Now the Blues lost no opportunity of opposing the Buffs, and the Buffs lost no opportunity of opposing the Blues; and the consequence was, that whenever the Buffs and Blues met together at public meeting, town-hall, fair, or market, disputes and high words arose between them. With these dissensions it is almost superfluous to say that everything in Eatanswill was made a party question. If the Buffs proposed to new skylight the market-place, the Blues got up public meetings, and denounced the proceeding; if the Blues proposed the erection of an additional pump in the High Street, the Buffs rose as one man and stood aghast at the enormity. There were Blue shops and Buff shops, Blue inns and Buff inns--there was a Blue aisle and a Buff aisle in the very church itself.\n\nOf course it was essentially and indispensably necessary that each of these powerful parties should have its chosen organ and representative: and, accordingly, there were two newspapers in the town--the Eatanswill Gazette and the Eatanswill Independent; the former advocating Blue principles, and the latter conducted on grounds decidedly Buff. Fine newspapers they were. Such leading articles, and such spirited attacks!- -’Our worthless contemporary, the Gazette’--‘That disgraceful and dastardly journal, the Independent’--‘That false and scurrilous print, the Independent’--‘That vile and slanderous calumniator, the Gazette;’ these, and other spirit-stirring denunciations, were strewn plentifully over the columns of each, in every number, and excited feelings of the most intense delight and indignation in the bosoms of the townspeople.\n\nMr. Pickwick, with his usual foresight and sagacity, had chosen a peculiarly desirable moment for his visit to the borough. Never was such a contest known. The Honourable Samuel Slumkey, of Slumkey Hall, was the Blue candidate; and Horatio Fizkin, Esq., of Fizkin Lodge, near Eatanswill, had been prevailed upon by his friends to stand forward on the Buff interest. The Gazette warned the electors of Eatanswill that the eyes not only of England, but of the whole civilised world, were upon them; and the Independent imperatively demanded to know, whether the constituency of Eatanswill were the grand fellows they had always taken them for, or base and servile tools, undeserving alike of the name of Englishmen and the blessings of freedom. Never had such a commotion agitated the town before.\n\nIt was late in the evening when Mr. Pickwick and his companions, assisted by Sam, dismounted from the roof of the Eatanswill coach. Large blue silk flags were flying from the windows of the Town Arms Inn, and bills were posted in every sash, intimating, in gigantic letters, that the Honourable Samuel Slumkey’s committee sat there daily. A crowd of idlers were assembled in the road, looking at a hoarse man in the balcony, who was apparently talking himself very red in the face in Mr. Slumkey’s behalf; but the force and point of whose arguments were somewhat impaired by the perpetual beating of four large drums which Mr. Fizkin’s committee had stationed at the street corner. There was a busy little man beside him, though, who took off his hat at intervals and motioned to the people to cheer, which they regularly did, most enthusiastically; and as the red-faced gentleman went on talking till he was redder in the face than ever, it seemed to answer his purpose quite as well as if anybody had heard him.\n\nThe Pickwickians had no sooner dismounted than they were surrounded by a branch mob of the honest and independent, who forthwith set up three deafening cheers, which being responded to by the main body (for it’s not at all necessary for a crowd to know what they are cheering about), swelled into a tremendous roar of triumph, which stopped even the red- faced man in the balcony.\n\n‘Hurrah!’ shouted the mob, in conclusion.\n\n‘One cheer more,’ screamed the little fugleman in the balcony, and out shouted the mob again, as if lungs were cast-iron, with steel works.\n\n‘Slumkey for ever!’ roared the honest and independent.\n\n‘Slumkey for ever!’ echoed Mr. Pickwick, taking off his hat.\n\n‘No Fizkin!’ roared the crowd.\n\n‘Certainly not!’ shouted Mr. Pickwick. ‘Hurrah!’ And then there was another roaring, like that of a whole menagerie when the elephant has rung the bell for the cold meat.\n\n‘Who is Slumkey?’ whispered Mr. Tupman.\n\n‘I don’t know,’ replied Mr. Pickwick, in the same tone. ‘Hush. Don’t ask any questions. It’s always best on these occasions to do what the mob do.’\n\n‘But suppose there are two mobs?’ suggested Mr. Snodgrass.\n\n‘Shout with the largest,’ replied Mr. Pickwick.\n\nVolumes could not have said more.\n\nThey entered the house, the crowd opening right and left to let them pass, and cheering vociferously. The first object of consideration was to secure quarters for the night.\n\n‘Can we have beds here?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick, summoning the waiter.\n\n‘Don’t know, Sir,’ replied the man; ‘afraid we’re full, sir--I’ll inquire, Sir.’ Away he went for that purpose, and presently returned, to ask whether the gentleman were ‘Blue.’\n\nAs neither Mr. Pickwick nor his companions took any vital interest in the cause of either candidate, the question was rather a difficult one to answer. In this dilemma Mr. Pickwick bethought himself of his new friend, Mr. Perker.\n\n‘Do you know a gentleman of the name of Perker?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Certainly, Sir; Honourable Mr. Samuel Slumkey’s agent.’\n\n‘He is Blue, I think?’\n‘Oh, yes, Sir.’\n\n‘Then we are Blue,’ said Mr. Pickwick; but observing that the man looked rather doubtful at this accommodating announcement, he gave him his card, and desired him to present it to Mr. Perker forthwith, if he should happen to be in the house. The waiter retired; and reappearing almost immediately with a request that Mr. Pickwick would follow him, led the way to a large room on the first floor, where, seated at a long table covered with books and papers, was Mr. Perker.\n\n‘Ah--ah, my dear Sir,’ said the little man, advancing to meet him; ‘very happy to see you, my dear Sir, very. Pray sit down. So you have carried your intention into effect. You have come down here to see an election-- eh?’\n\nMr. Pickwick replied in the affirmative.\n\n‘Spirited contest, my dear sir,’ said the little man.\n\n‘I’m delighted to hear it,’ said Mr. Pickwick, rubbing his hands. ‘I like to see sturdy patriotism, on whatever side it is called forth--and so it’s a spirited contest?’\n\n‘Oh, yes,’ said the little man, ‘very much so indeed. We have opened all the public-houses in the place, and left our adversary nothing but the beer-shops--masterly stroke of policy that, my dear Sir, eh?’ The little man smiled complacently, and took a large pinch of snuff.\n\n‘And what are the probabilities as to the result of the contest?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Why, doubtful, my dear Sir; rather doubtful as yet,’ replied the little man. ‘Fizkin’s people have got three-and-thirty voters in the lock-up coach-house at the White Hart.’\n\n‘In the coach-house!’ said Mr. Pickwick, considerably astonished by this second stroke of policy.\n\n‘They keep ‘em locked up there till they want ‘em,’ resumed the little man. ‘The effect of that is, you see, to prevent our getting at them; and even if we could, it would be of no use, for they keep them very drunk on purpose. Smart fellow Fizkin’s agent--very smart fellow indeed.’\n\nMr. Pickwick stared, but said nothing.\n\n‘We are pretty confident, though,’ said Mr. Perker, sinking his voice almost to a whisper. ‘We had a little tea-party here, last night--five- and-forty women, my dear sir--and gave every one of ‘em a green parasol when she went away.’\n\n‘A parasol!’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Fact, my dear Sir, fact. Five-and-forty green parasols, at seven and sixpence a-piece. All women like finery--extraordinary the effect of those parasols. Secured all their husbands, and half their brothers-- beats stockings, and flannel, and all that sort of thing hollow. My idea, my dear Sir, entirely. Hail, rain, or sunshine, you can’t walk half a dozen yards up the street, without encountering half a dozen green parasols.’\n\nHere the little man indulged in a convulsion of mirth, which was only checked by the entrance of a third party.\n\nThis was a tall, thin man, with a sandy-coloured head inclined to baldness, and a face in which solemn importance was blended with a look of unfathomable profundity. He was dressed in a long brown surtout, with a black cloth waistcoat, and drab trousers. A double eyeglass dangled at his waistcoat; and on his head he wore a very low-crowned hat with a broad brim. The new-comer was introduced to Mr. Pickwick as Mr. Pott, the editor of the Eatanswill Gazette. After a few preliminary remarks, Mr. Pott turned round to Mr. Pickwick, and said with solemnity--\n\n‘This contest excites great interest in the metropolis, sir?’\n\n‘I believe it does,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘To which I have reason to know,’ said Pott, looking towards Mr. Perker for corroboration--‘to which I have reason to know that my article of last Saturday in some degree contributed.’\n\n‘Not the least doubt of it,’ said the little man.\n\n‘The press is a mighty engine, sir,’ said Pott.\n\nMr. Pickwick yielded his fullest assent to the proposition.\n\n‘But I trust, sir,’ said Pott, ‘that I have never abused the enormous power I wield. I trust, sir, that I have never pointed the noble instrument which is placed in my hands, against the sacred bosom of private life, or the tender breast of individual reputation; I trust, sir, that I have devoted my energies to--to endeavours--humble they may be, humble I know they are--to instil those principles of--which--are--’\n\nHere the editor of the Eatanswill Gazette, appearing to ramble, Mr. Pickwick came to his relief, and said--\n\n‘Certainly.’\n\n‘And what, Sir,’ said Pott--‘what, Sir, let me ask you as an impartial man, is the state of the public mind in London, with reference to my contest with the Independent?’\n\n‘Greatly excited, no doubt,’ interposed Mr. Perker, with a look of slyness which was very likely accidental.\n\n‘The contest,’ said Pott, ‘shall be prolonged so long as I have health and strength, and that portion of talent with which I am gifted. From that contest, Sir, although it may unsettle men’s minds and excite their feelings, and render them incapable for the discharge of the everyday duties of ordinary life; from that contest, sir, I will never shrink, till I have set my heel upon the Eatanswill Independent. I wish the people of London, and the people of this country to know, sir, that they may rely upon me--that I will not desert them, that I am resolved to stand by them, Sir, to the last.’\n\nYour conduct is most noble, Sir,’ said Mr. Pickwick; and he grasped the hand of the magnanimous Pott.\n\n‘You are, sir, I perceive, a man of sense and talent,’ said Mr. Pott, almost breathless with the vehemence of his patriotic declaration. ‘I am most happy, sir, to make the acquaintance of such a man.’\n\n‘And I,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘feel deeply honoured by this expression of your opinion. Allow me, sir, to introduce you to my fellow-travellers, the other corresponding members of the club I am proud to have founded.’\n\n‘I shall be delighted,’ said Mr. Pott.\n\nMr. Pickwick withdrew, and returning with his friends, presented them in due form to the editor of the Eatanswill Gazette.\n\n‘Now, my dear Pott,’ said little Mr. Perker, ‘the question is, what are we to do with our friends here?’\n\n‘We can stop in this house, I suppose,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Not a spare bed in the house, my dear sir--not a single bed.’\n\n‘Extremely awkward,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Very,’ said his fellow-voyagers.\n\n‘I have an idea upon this subject,’ said Mr. Pott, ‘which I think may be very successfully adopted. They have two beds at the Peacock, and I can boldly say, on behalf of Mrs. Pott, that she will be delighted to accommodate Mr. Pickwick and any one of his friends, if the other two gentlemen and their servant do not object to shifting, as they best can, at the Peacock.’\n\nAfter repeated pressings on the part of Mr. Pott, and repeated protestations on that of Mr. Pickwick that he could not think of incommoding or troubling his amiable wife, it was decided that it was the only feasible arrangement that could be made. So it was made; and after dinner together at the Town Arms, the friends separated, Mr. Tupman and Mr. Snodgrass repairing to the Peacock, and Mr. Pickwick and Mr. Winkle proceeding to the mansion of Mr. Pott; it having been previously arranged that they should all reassemble at the Town Arms in the morning, and accompany the Honourable Samuel Slumkey’s procession to the place of nomination.\n\nMr. Pott’s domestic circle was limited to himself and his wife. All men whom mighty genius has raised to a proud eminence in the world, have usually some little weakness which appears the more conspicuous from the contrast it presents to their general character. If Mr. Pott had a weakness, it was, perhaps, that he was rather too submissive to the somewhat contemptuous control and sway of his wife. We do not feel justified in laying any particular stress upon the fact, because on the present occasion all Mrs. Pott’s most winning ways were brought into requisition to receive the two gentlemen.\n\n‘My dear,’ said Mr. Pott, ‘Mr. Pickwick--Mr. Pickwick of London.’\n\nMrs. Pott received Mr. Pickwick’s paternal grasp of the hand with enchanting sweetness; and Mr. Winkle, who had not been announced at all, sidled and bowed, unnoticed, in an obscure corner.\n\n‘P. my dear’--said Mrs. Pott.\n\n‘My life,’ said Mr. Pott.\n\n‘Pray introduce the other gentleman.’\n\n‘I beg a thousand pardons,’ said Mr. Pott. ‘Permit me, Mrs. Pott, Mr.--’\n\n‘Winkle,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Winkle,’ echoed Mr. Pott; and the ceremony of introduction was complete.\n\n‘We owe you many apologies, ma’am,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘for disturbing your domestic arrangements at so short a notice.’\n\n‘I beg you won’t mention it, sir,’ replied the feminine Pott, with vivacity. ‘It is a high treat to me, I assure you, to see any new faces; living as I do, from day to day, and week to week, in this dull place, and seeing nobody.’\n\n‘Nobody, my dear!’ exclaimed Mr. Pott archly.\n\n‘Nobody but you,’ retorted Mrs. Pott, with asperity.\n\n‘You see, Mr. Pickwick,’ said the host in explanation of his wife’s lament, ‘that we are in some measure cut off from many enjoyments and pleasures of which we might otherwise partake. My public station, as editor of the Eatanswill Gazette, the position which that paper holds in the country, my constant immersion in the vortex of politics--’\n\n‘P. my dear--’ interposed Mrs. Pott.\n\n‘My life--’ said the editor.\n\n‘I wish, my dear, you would endeavour to find some topic of conversation in which these gentlemen might take some rational interest.’\n\n‘But, my love,’ said Mr. Pott, with great humility, ‘Mr. Pickwick does take an interest in it.’\n\n‘It’s well for him if he can,’ said Mrs. Pott emphatically; ‘I am wearied out of my life with your politics, and quarrels with the Independent, and nonsense. I am quite astonished, P., at your making such an exhibition of your absurdity.’\n\n‘But, my dear--’ said Mr. Pott.\n\n‘Oh, nonsense, don’t talk to me,’ said Mrs. Pott. ‘Do you play ecarte, Sir?’\n\n‘I shall be very happy to learn under your tuition,’ replied Mr. Winkle.\n\n‘Well, then, draw that little table into this window, and let me get out of hearing of those prosy politics.’\n\n‘Jane,’ said Mr. Pott, to the servant who brought in candles, ‘go down into the office, and bring me up the file of the Gazette for eighteen hundred and twenty-six. I’ll read you,’ added the editor, turning to Mr. Pickwick--‘I’ll just read you a few of the leaders I wrote at that time upon the Buff job of appointing a new tollman to the turnpike here; I rather think they’ll amuse you.’\n\n‘I should like to hear them very much indeed,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\nUp came the file, and down sat the editor, with Mr. Pickwick at his side.\n\nWe have in vain pored over the leaves of Mr. Pickwick’s note-book, in the hope of meeting with a general summary of these beautiful compositions. We have every reason to believe that he was perfectly enraptured with the vigour and freshness of the style; indeed Mr. Winkle has recorded the fact that his eyes were closed, as if with excess of pleasure, during the whole time of their perusal.\n\nThe announcement of supper put a stop both to the game of ecarte, and the recapitulation of the beauties of the Eatanswill Gazette. Mrs. Pott was in the highest spirits and the most agreeable humour. Mr. Winkle had already made considerable progress in her good opinion, and she did not hesitate to inform him, confidentially, that Mr. Pickwick was ‘a delightful old dear.’ These terms convey a familiarity of expression, in which few of those who were intimately acquainted with that colossal-minded man, would have presumed to indulge. We have preserved them, nevertheless, as affording at once a touching and a convincing proof of the estimation in which he was held by every class of society, and the ease with which he made his way to their hearts and feelings.\n\nIt was a late hour of the night--long after Mr. Tupman and Mr. Snodgrass had fallen asleep in the inmost recesses of the Peacock--when the two friends retired to rest. Slumber soon fell upon the senses of Mr. Winkle, but his feelings had been excited, and his admiration roused; and for many hours after sleep had rendered him insensible to earthly objects, the face and figure of the agreeable Mrs. Pott presented themselves again and again to his wandering imagination.\n\nThe noise and bustle which ushered in the morning were sufficient to dispel from the mind of the most romantic visionary in existence, any associations but those which were immediately connected with the rapidly-approaching election. The beating of drums, the blowing of horns and trumpets, the shouting of men, and tramping of horses, echoed and re-echoed through the streets from the earliest dawn of day; and an occasional fight between the light skirmishers of either party at once enlivened the preparations, and agreeably diversified their character.\n\n‘Well, Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick, as his valet appeared at his bedroom door, just as he was concluding his toilet; ‘all alive to-day, I suppose?’\n\n‘Reg’lar game, sir,’ replied Mr. Weller; ‘our people’s a-collecting down at the Town Arms, and they’re a-hollering themselves hoarse already.’\n\n‘Ah,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘do they seem devoted to their party, Sam?’\n\n‘Never see such dewotion in my life, Sir.’\n\n‘Energetic, eh?’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Uncommon,’ replied Sam; ‘I never see men eat and drink so much afore. I wonder they ain’t afeer’d o’ bustin’.’\n\n‘That’s the mistaken kindness of the gentry here,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Wery likely,’ replied Sam briefly.\n\n‘Fine, fresh, hearty fellows they seem,’ said Mr. Pickwick, glancing from the window.\n\n‘Wery fresh,’ replied Sam; ‘me and the two waiters at the Peacock has been a-pumpin’ over the independent woters as supped there last night.’\n\n‘Pumping over independent voters!’ exclaimed Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Yes,’ said his attendant, ‘every man slept vere he fell down; we dragged ‘em out, one by one, this mornin’, and put ‘em under the pump, and they’re in reg’lar fine order now. Shillin’ a head the committee paid for that ‘ere job.’\n\n‘Can such things be!’ exclaimed the astonished Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Lord bless your heart, sir,’ said Sam, ‘why where was you half baptised?--that’s nothin’, that ain’t.’\n\n‘Nothing?’said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Nothin’ at all, Sir,’ replied his attendant. ‘The night afore the last day o’ the last election here, the opposite party bribed the barmaid at the Town Arms, to hocus the brandy-and-water of fourteen unpolled electors as was a-stoppin’ in the house.’\n\n‘What do you mean by “hocussing” brandy-and-water?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Puttin’ laud’num in it,’ replied Sam. ‘Blessed if she didn’t send ‘em all to sleep till twelve hours arter the election was over. They took one man up to the booth, in a truck, fast asleep, by way of experiment, but it was no go--they wouldn’t poll him; so they brought him back, and put him to bed again.’\n\nStrange practices, these,’ said Mr. Pickwick; half speaking to himself and half addressing Sam.\n\n‘Not half so strange as a miraculous circumstance as happened to my own father, at an election time, in this wery place, Sir,’ replied Sam.\n\n‘What was that?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Why, he drove a coach down here once,’ said Sam; ‘’lection time came on, and he was engaged by vun party to bring down woters from London. Night afore he was going to drive up, committee on t’ other side sends for him quietly, and away he goes vith the messenger, who shows him in;- -large room--lots of gen’l’m’n--heaps of papers, pens and ink, and all that ‘ere. “Ah, Mr. Weller,” says the gen’l’m’n in the chair, “glad to see you, sir; how are you?”--“Wery well, thank ‘ee, Sir,” says my father; “I hope you’re pretty middlin,” says he.--“Pretty well, thank’ee, Sir,” says the gen’l’m’n; “sit down, Mr. Weller--pray sit down, sir.” So my father sits down, and he and the gen’l’m’n looks wery hard at each other. “You don’t remember me?” said the gen’l’m’n.--“Can’t say I do,” says my father.--“Oh, I know you,” says the gen’l’m’n: “know’d you when you was a boy,” says he.--“Well, I don’t remember you,” says my father.--“That’s wery odd,” says the gen’l’m’n.”--“Wery,” says my father.--“You must have a bad mem’ry, Mr. Weller,” says the gen’l’m’n.--“Well, it is a wery bad ‘un,” says my father.--“I thought so,” says the gen’l’m’n. So then they pours him out a glass of wine, and gammons him about his driving, and gets him into a reg’lar good humour, and at last shoves a twenty-pound note into his hand. “It’s a wery bad road between this and London,” says the gen’l’m’n.--“Here and there it is a heavy road,” says my father.--” ‘Specially near the canal, I think,” says the gen’l’m’n.--“Nasty bit that ‘ere,” says my father.-- “Well, Mr. Weller,” says the gen’l’m’n, “you’re a wery good whip, and can do what you like with your horses, we know. We’re all wery fond o’ you, Mr. Weller, so in case you should have an accident when you’re bringing these here woters down, and should tip ‘em over into the canal vithout hurtin’ of ‘em, this is for yourself,” says he.--“Gen’l’m’n, you’re wery kind,” says my father, “and I’ll drink your health in another glass of wine,” says he; vich he did, and then buttons up the money, and bows himself out. You wouldn’t believe, sir,’ continued Sam, with a look of inexpressible impudence at his master, ‘that on the wery day as he came down with them woters, his coach was upset on that ‘ere wery spot, and ev’ry man on ‘em was turned into the canal.’\n\n‘And got out again?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick hastily.\n\n‘Why,’ replied Sam very slowly, ‘I rather think one old gen’l’m’n was missin’; I know his hat was found, but I ain’t quite certain whether his head was in it or not. But what I look at is the hex-traordinary and wonderful coincidence, that arter what that gen’l’m’n said, my father’s coach should be upset in that wery place, and on that wery day!’\n\n‘It is, no doubt, a very extraordinary circumstance indeed,’ said Mr. Pickwick. ‘But brush my hat, Sam, for I hear Mr. Winkle calling me to breakfast.’\n\nWith these words Mr. Pickwick descended to the parlour, where he found breakfast laid, and the family already assembled. The meal was hastily despatched; each of the gentlemen’s hats was decorated with an enormous blue favour, made up by the fair hands of Mrs. Pott herself; and as Mr. Winkle had undertaken to escort that lady to a house-top, in the immediate vicinity of the hustings, Mr. Pickwick and Mr. Pott repaired alone to the Town Arms, from the back window of which, one of Mr. Slumkey’s committee was addressing six small boys and one girl, whom he dignified, at every second sentence, with the imposing title of ‘Men of Eatanswill,’ whereat the six small boys aforesaid cheered prodigiously.\n\nThe stable-yard exhibited unequivocal symptoms of the glory and strength of the Eatanswill Blues. There was a regular army of blue flags, some with one handle, and some with two, exhibiting appropriate devices, in golden characters four feet high, and stout in proportion. There was a grand band of trumpets, bassoons, and drums, marshalled four abreast, and earning their money, if ever men did, especially the drum-beaters, who were very muscular. There were bodies of constables with blue staves, twenty committee-men with blue scarfs, and a mob of voters with blue cockades. There were electors on horseback and electors afoot. There was an open carriage-and-four, for the Honourable Samuel Slumkey; and there were four carriage-and-pair, for his friends and supporters; and the flags were rustling, and the band was playing, and the constables were swearing, and the twenty committee-men were squabbling, and the mob were shouting, and the horses were backing, and the post- boys perspiring; and everybody, and everything, then and there assembled, was for the special use, behoof, honour, and renown, of the Honourable Samuel Slumkey, of Slumkey Hall, one of the candidates for the representation of the borough of Eatanswill, in the Commons House of Parliament of the United Kingdom.\n\nLoud and long were the cheers, and mighty was the rustling of one of the blue flags, with ‘Liberty of the Press’ inscribed thereon, when the sandy head of Mr. Pott was discerned in one of the windows, by the mob beneath; and tremendous was the enthusiasm when the Honourable Samuel Slumkey himself, in top-boots, and a blue neckerchief, advanced and seized the hand of the said Pott, and melodramatically testified by gestures to the crowd, his ineffaceable obligations to the Eatanswill Gazette.\n\n‘Is everything ready?’ said the Honourable Samuel Slumkey to Mr. Perker.\n\n‘Everything, my dear Sir,’ was the little man’s reply.\n\n‘Nothing has been omitted, I hope?’ said the Honourable Samuel Slumkey.\n\n‘Nothing has been left undone, my dear sir--nothing whatever. There are twenty washed men at the street door for you to shake hands with; and six children in arms that you’re to pat on the head, and inquire the age of; be particular about the children, my dear sir--it has always a great effect, that sort of thing.’\n\n‘I’ll take care,’ said the Honourable Samuel Slumkey.\n\n‘And, perhaps, my dear Sir,’ said the cautious little man, ‘perhaps if you could--I don’t mean to say it’s indispensable--but if you could manage to kiss one of ‘em, it would produce a very great impression on the crowd.’\n\n‘Wouldn’t it have as good an effect if the proposer or seconder did that?’ said the Honourable Samuel Slumkey.\n\n‘Why, I am afraid it wouldn’t,’ replied the agent; ‘if it were done by yourself, my dear Sir, I think it would make you very popular.’\n\n‘Very well,’ said the Honourable Samuel Slumkey, with a resigned air, ‘then it must be done. That’s all.’\n\n‘Arrange the procession,’ cried the twenty committee-men.\n\nAmidst the cheers of the assembled throng, the band, and the constables, and the committee-men, and the voters, and the horsemen, and the carriages, took their places--each of the two-horse vehicles being closely packed with as many gentlemen as could manage to stand upright in it; and that assigned to Mr. Perker, containing Mr. Pickwick, Mr. Tupman, Mr. Snodgrass, and about half a dozen of the committee besides.\n\nThere was a moment of awful suspense as the procession waited for the Honourable Samuel Slumkey to step into his carriage. Suddenly the crowd set up a great cheering.\n\n‘He has come out,’ said little Mr. Perker, greatly excited; the more so as their position did not enable them to see what was going forward.\n\nAnother cheer, much louder.\n\n‘He has shaken hands with the men,’ cried the little agent.\n\nAnother cheer, far more vehement.\n\n‘He has patted the babies on the head,’ said Mr. Perker, trembling with anxiety.\n\nA roar of applause that rent the air.\n\n‘He has kissed one of ‘em!’ exclaimed the delighted little man.\n\nA second roar.\n\n‘He has kissed another,’ gasped the excited manager.\n\nA third roar.\n\n‘He’s kissing ‘em all!’ screamed the enthusiastic little gentleman, and hailed by the deafening shouts of the multitude, the procession moved on.\n\nHow or by what means it became mixed up with the other procession, and how it was ever extricated from the confusion consequent thereupon, is more than we can undertake to describe, inasmuch as Mr. Pickwick’s hat was knocked over his eyes, nose, and mouth, by one poke of a Buff flag- staff, very early in the proceedings. He describes himself as being surrounded on every side, when he could catch a glimpse of the scene, by angry and ferocious countenances, by a vast cloud of dust, and by a dense crowd of combatants. He represents himself as being forced from the carriage by some unseen power, and being personally engaged in a pugilistic encounter; but with whom, or how, or why, he is wholly unable to state. He then felt himself forced up some wooden steps by the persons from behind; and on removing his hat, found himself surrounded by his friends, in the very front of the left hand side of the hustings. The right was reserved for the Buff party, and the centre for the mayor and his officers; one of whom--the fat crier of Eatanswill--was ringing an enormous bell, by way of commanding silence, while Mr. Horatio Fizkin, and the Honourable Samuel Slumkey, with their hands upon their hearts, were bowing with the utmost affability to the troubled sea of heads that inundated the open space in front; and from whence arose a storm of groans, and shouts, and yells, and hootings, that would have done honour to an earthquake.\n\n‘There’s Winkle,’ said Mr. Tupman, pulling his friend by the sleeve.\n\n‘Where!’ said Mr. Pickwick, putting on his spectacles, which he had fortunately kept in his pocket hitherto.\n\n‘There,’ said Mr. Tupman, ‘on the top of that house.’ And there, sure enough, in the leaden gutter of a tiled roof, were Mr. Winkle and Mrs. Pott, comfortably seated in a couple of chairs, waving their handkerchiefs in token of recognition--a compliment which Mr. Pickwick returned by kissing his hand to the lady.\n\nThe proceedings had not yet commenced; and as an inactive crowd is generally disposed to be jocose, this very innocent action was sufficient to awaken their facetiousness.\n\n‘Oh, you wicked old rascal,’ cried one voice, ‘looking arter the girls, are you?’\n\n‘Oh, you wenerable sinner,’ cried another.\n\n‘Putting on his spectacles to look at a married ‘ooman!’ said a third.\n\n‘I see him a-winkin’ at her, with his wicked old eye,’ shouted a fourth.\n\n‘Look arter your wife, Pott,’ bellowed a fifth--and then there was a roar of laughter.\n\nAs these taunts were accompanied with invidious comparisons between Mr. Pickwick and an aged ram, and several witticisms of the like nature; and as they moreover rather tended to convey reflections upon the honour of an innocent lady, Mr. Pickwick’s indignation was excessive; but as silence was proclaimed at the moment, he contented himself by scorching the mob with a look of pity for their misguided minds, at which they laughed more boisterously than ever.\n\n‘Silence!’ roared the mayor’s attendants.\n\n‘Whiffin, proclaim silence,’ said the mayor, with an air of pomp befitting his lofty station. In obedience to this command the crier performed another concerto on the bell, whereupon a gentleman in the crowd called out ‘Muffins’; which occasioned another laugh.\n\n‘Gentlemen,’ said the mayor, at as loud a pitch as he could possibly force his voice to--‘gentlemen. Brother electors of the borough of Eatanswill. We are met here to-day for the purpose of choosing a representative in the room of our late--’\n\nHere the mayor was interrupted by a voice in the crowd.\n\n‘Suc-cess to the mayor!’ cried the voice, ‘and may he never desert the nail and sarspan business, as he got his money by.’\n\nThis allusion to the professional pursuits of the orator was received with a storm of delight, which, with a bell-accompaniment, rendered the remainder of his speech inaudible, with the exception of the concluding sentence, in which he thanked the meeting for the patient attention with which they heard him throughout--an expression of gratitude which elicited another burst of mirth, of about a quarter of an hour’s duration.\n\nNext, a tall, thin gentleman, in a very stiff white neckerchief, after being repeatedly desired by the crowd to ‘send a boy home, to ask whether he hadn’t left his voice under the pillow,’ begged to nominate a fit and proper person to represent them in Parliament. And when he said it was Horatio Fizkin, Esquire, of Fizkin Lodge, near Eatanswill, the Fizkinites applauded, and the Slumkeyites groaned, so long, and so loudly, that both he and the seconder might have sung comic songs in lieu of speaking, without anybody’s being a bit the wiser.\n\nThe friends of Horatio Fizkin, Esquire, having had their innings, a little choleric, pink-faced man stood forward to propose another fit and proper person to represent the electors of Eatanswill in Parliament; and very swimmingly the pink-faced gentleman would have got on, if he had not been rather too choleric to entertain a sufficient perception of the fun of the crowd. But after a very few sentences of figurative eloquence, the pink-faced gentleman got from denouncing those who interrupted him in the mob, to exchanging defiances with the gentlemen on the hustings; whereupon arose an uproar which reduced him to the necessity of expressing his feelings by serious pantomime, which he did, and then left the stage to his seconder, who delivered a written speech of half an hour’s length, and wouldn’t be stopped, because he had sent it all to the Eatanswill Gazette, and the Eatanswill Gazette had already printed it, every word.\n\nThen Horatio Fizkin, Esquire, of Fizkin Lodge, near Eatanswill, presented himself for the purpose of addressing the electors; which he no sooner did, than the band employed by the Honourable Samuel Slumkey, commenced performing with a power to which their strength in the morning was a trifle; in return for which, the Buff crowd belaboured the heads and shoulders of the Blue crowd; on which the Blue crowd endeavoured to dispossess themselves of their very unpleasant neighbours the Buff crowd; and a scene of struggling, and pushing, and fighting, succeeded, to which we can no more do justice than the mayor could, although he issued imperative orders to twelve constables to seize the ringleaders, who might amount in number to two hundred and fifty, or thereabouts. At all these encounters, Horatio Fizkin, Esquire, of Fizkin Lodge, and his friends, waxed fierce and furious; until at last Horatio Fizkin, Esquire, of Fizkin Lodge, begged to ask his opponent, the Honourable Samuel Slumkey, of Slumkey Hall, whether that band played by his consent; which question the Honourable Samuel Slumkey declining to answer, Horatio Fizkin, Esquire, of Fizkin Lodge, shook his fist in the countenance of the Honourable Samuel Slumkey, of Slumkey Hall; upon which the Honourable Samuel Slumkey, his blood being up, defied Horatio Fizkin, Esquire, to mortal combat. At this violation of all known rules and precedents of order, the mayor commanded another fantasia on the bell, and declared that he would bring before himself, both Horatio Fizkin, Esquire, of Fizkin Lodge, and the Honourable Samuel Slumkey, of Slumkey Hall, and bind them over to keep the peace. Upon this terrific denunciation, the supporters of the two candidates interfered, and after the friends of each party had quarrelled in pairs, for three-quarters of an hour, Horatio Fizkin, Esquire, touched his hat to the Honourable Samuel Slumkey; the Honourable Samuel Slumkey touched his to Horatio Fizkin, Esquire; the band was stopped; the crowd were partially quieted; and Horatio Fizkin, Esquire, was permitted to proceed.\n\nThe speeches of the two candidates, though differing in every other respect, afforded a beautiful tribute to the merit and high worth of the electors of Eatanswill. Both expressed their opinion that a more independent, a more enlightened, a more public-spirited, a more noble- minded, a more disinterested set of men than those who had promised to vote for him, never existed on earth; each darkly hinted his suspicions that the electors in the opposite interest had certain swinish and besotted infirmities which rendered them unfit for the exercise of the important duties they were called upon to discharge. Fizkin expressed his readiness to do anything he was wanted: Slumkey, his determination to do nothing that was asked of him. Both said that the trade, the manufactures, the commerce, the prosperity of Eatanswill, would ever be dearer to their hearts than any earthly object; and each had it in his power to state, with the utmost confidence, that he was the man who would eventually be returned.\n\nThere was a show of hands; the mayor decided in favour of the Honourable Samuel Slumkey, of Slumkey Hall. Horatio Fizkin, Esquire, of Fizkin Lodge, demanded a poll, and a poll was fixed accordingly. Then a vote of thanks was moved to the mayor for his able conduct in the chair; and the mayor, devoutly wishing that he had had a chair to display his able conduct in (for he had been standing during the whole proceedings), returned thanks. The processions reformed, the carriages rolled slowly through the crowd, and its members screeched and shouted after them as their feelings or caprice dictated.\n\nDuring the whole time of the polling, the town was in a perpetual fever of excitement. Everything was conducted on the most liberal and delightful scale. Excisable articles were remarkably cheap at all the public-houses; and spring vans paraded the streets for the accommodation of voters who were seized with any temporary dizziness in the head--an epidemic which prevailed among the electors, during the contest, to a most alarming extent, and under the influence of which they might frequently be seen lying on the pavements in a state of utter insensibility. A small body of electors remained unpolled on the very last day. They were calculating and reflecting persons, who had not yet been convinced by the arguments of either party, although they had frequent conferences with each. One hour before the close of the poll, Mr. Perker solicited the honour of a private interview with these intelligent, these noble, these patriotic men. It was granted. His arguments were brief but satisfactory. They went in a body to the poll; and when they returned, the Honourable Samuel Slumkey, of Slumkey Hall, was returned also.\n\nCHAPTER XIV. COMPRISING A BRIEF DESCRIPTION OF THE COMPANY AT THE PEACOCK ASSEMBLED; AND A TALE TOLD BY A BAGMAN\nIt is pleasant to turn from contemplating the strife and turmoil of political existence, to the peaceful repose of private life. Although in reality no great partisan of either side, Mr. Pickwick was sufficiently fired with Mr. Pott’s enthusiasm, to apply his whole time and attention to the proceedings, of which the last chapter affords a description compiled from his own memoranda. Nor while he was thus occupied was Mr. Winkle idle, his whole time being devoted to pleasant walks and short country excursions with Mrs. Pott, who never failed, when such an opportunity presented itself, to seek some relief from the tedious monotony she so constantly complained of. The two gentlemen being thus completely domesticated in the editor’s house, Mr. Tupman and Mr. Snodgrass were in a great measure cast upon their own resources. Taking but little interest in public affairs, they beguiled their time chiefly with such amusements as the Peacock afforded, which were limited to a bagatelle-board in the first floor, and a sequestered skittle-ground in the back yard. In the science and nicety of both these recreations, which are far more abstruse than ordinary men suppose, they were gradually initiated by Mr. Weller, who possessed a perfect knowledge of such pastimes. Thus, notwithstanding that they were in a great measure deprived of the comfort and advantage of Mr. Pickwick’s society, they were still enabled to beguile the time, and to prevent its hanging heavily on their hands.\n\nIt was in the evening, however, that the Peacock presented attractions which enabled the two friends to resist even the invitations of the gifted, though prosy, Pott. It was in the evening that the ‘commercial room’ was filled with a social circle, whose characters and manners it was the delight of Mr. Tupman to observe; whose sayings and doings it was the habit of Mr. Snodgrass to note down.\n\nMost people know what sort of places commercial rooms usually are. That of the Peacock differed in no material respect from the generality of such apartments; that is to say, it was a large, bare-looking room, the furniture of which had no doubt been better when it was newer, with a spacious table in the centre, and a variety of smaller dittos in the corners; an extensive assortment of variously shaped chairs, and an old Turkey carpet, bearing about the same relative proportion to the size of the room, as a lady’s pocket-handkerchief might to the floor of a watch- box. The walls were garnished with one or two large maps; and several weather-beaten rough greatcoats, with complicated capes, dangled from a long row of pegs in one corner. The mantel-shelf was ornamented with a wooden inkstand, containing one stump of a pen and half a wafer; a road- book and directory; a county history minus the cover; and the mortal remains of a trout in a glass coffin. The atmosphere was redolent of tobacco-smoke, the fumes of which had communicated a rather dingy hue to the whole room, and more especially to the dusty red curtains which shaded the windows. On the sideboard a variety of miscellaneous articles were huddled together, the most conspicuous of which were some very cloudy fish-sauce cruets, a couple of driving-boxes, two or three whips, and as many travelling shawls, a tray of knives and forks, and the mustard.\n\nHere it was that Mr. Tupman and Mr. Snodgrass were seated on the evening after the conclusion of the election, with several other temporary inmates of the house, smoking and drinking.\n\n‘Well, gents,’ said a stout, hale personage of about forty, with only one eye--a very bright black eye, which twinkled with a roguish expression of fun and good-humour, ‘our noble selves, gents. I always propose that toast to the company, and drink Mary to myself. Eh, Mary!’\n\n‘Get along with you, you wretch,’ said the hand-maiden, obviously not ill-pleased with the compliment, however.\n\n‘Don’t go away, Mary,’ said the black-eyed man.\n\n‘Let me alone, imperence,’ said the young lady.\n\n‘Never mind,’ said the one-eyed man, calling after the girl as she left the room. ‘I’ll step out by and by, Mary. Keep your spirits up, dear.’ Here he went through the not very difficult process of winking upon the company with his solitary eye, to the enthusiastic delight of an elderly personage with a dirty face and a clay pipe.\n\n‘Rum creeters is women,’ said the dirty-faced man, after a pause.\n\n‘Ah! no mistake about that,’ said a very red-faced man, behind a cigar.\n\nAfter this little bit of philosophy there was another pause.\n\n‘There’s rummer things than women in this world though, mind you,’ said the man with the black eye, slowly filling a large Dutch pipe, with a most capacious bowl.\n\n‘Are you married?’ inquired the dirty-faced man.\n\n‘Can’t say I am.’\n\n‘I thought not.’ Here the dirty-faced man fell into ecstasies of mirth at his own retort, in which he was joined by a man of bland voice and placid countenance, who always made it a point to agree with everybody.\n\n‘Women, after all, gentlemen,’ said the enthusiastic Mr. Snodgrass, ‘are the great props and comforts of our existence.’\n\n‘So they are,’ said the placid gentleman.\n\n‘When they’re in a good humour,’ interposed the dirty-faced man.\n\n‘And that’s very true,’ said the placid one.\n\n‘I repudiate that qualification,’ said Mr. Snodgrass, whose thoughts were fast reverting to Emily Wardle. ‘I repudiate it with disdain--with indignation. Show me the man who says anything against women, as women, and I boldly declare he is not a man.’ And Mr. Snodgrass took his cigar from his mouth, and struck the table violently with his clenched fist.\n\n‘That’s good sound argument,’ said the placid man.\n\n‘Containing a position which I deny,’ interrupted he of the dirty countenance.\n\n‘And there’s certainly a very great deal of truth in what you observe too, Sir,’ said the placid gentleman.\n\n‘Your health, Sir,’ said the bagman with the lonely eye, bestowing an approving nod on Mr. Snodgrass.\n\nMr. Snodgrass acknowledged the compliment.\n\n‘I always like to hear a good argument,’ continued the bagman, ‘a sharp one, like this: it’s very improving; but this little argument about women brought to my mind a story I have heard an old uncle of mine tell, the recollection of which, just now, made me say there were rummer things than women to be met with, sometimes.’\n\n‘I should like to hear that same story,’ said the red-faced man with the cigar.\n\n‘Should you?’ was the only reply of the bagman, who continued to smoke with great vehemence.\n\n‘So should I,’ said Mr. Tupman, speaking for the first time. He was always anxious to increase his stock of experience.\n\n‘Should you? Well then, I’ll tell it. No, I won’t. I know you won’t believe it,’ said the man with the roguish eye, making that organ look more roguish than ever. ‘If you say it’s true, of course I shall,’ said Mr. Tupman.\n\n‘Well, upon that understanding I’ll tell you,’ replied the traveller. ‘Did you ever hear of the great commercial house of Bilson & Slum? But it doesn’t matter though, whether you did or not, because they retired from business long since. It’s eighty years ago, since the circumstance happened to a traveller for that house, but he was a particular friend of my uncle’s; and my uncle told the story to me. It’s a queer name; but he used to call it\n\nand he used to tell it, something in this way.\n\n‘One winter’s evening, about five o’clock, just as it began to grow dusk, a man in a gig might have been seen urging his tired horse along the road which leads across Marlborough Downs, in the direction of Bristol. I say he might have been seen, and I have no doubt he would have been, if anybody but a blind man had happened to pass that way; but the weather was so bad, and the night so cold and wet, that nothing was out but the water, and so the traveller jogged along in the middle of the road, lonesome and dreary enough. If any bagman of that day could have caught sight of the little neck-or-nothing sort of gig, with a clay-coloured body and red wheels, and the vixenish, ill tempered, fast- going bay mare, that looked like a cross between a butcher’s horse and a twopenny post-office pony, he would have known at once, that this traveller could have been no other than Tom Smart, of the great house of Bilson and Slum, Cateaton Street, City. However, as there was no bagman to look on, nobody knew anything at all about the matter; and so Tom Smart and his clay-coloured gig with the red wheels, and the vixenish mare with the fast pace, went on together, keeping the secret among them, and nobody was a bit the wiser.\n\n‘There are many pleasanter places even in this dreary world, than Marlborough Downs when it blows hard; and if you throw in beside, a gloomy winter’s evening, a miry and sloppy road, and a pelting fall of heavy rain, and try the effect, by way of experiment, in your own proper person, you will experience the full force of this observation.\n\n‘The wind blew--not up the road or down it, though that’s bad enough, but sheer across it, sending the rain slanting down like the lines they used to rule in the copy-books at school, to make the boys slope well. For a moment it would die away, and the traveller would begin to delude himself into the belief that, exhausted with its previous fury, it had quietly laid itself down to rest, when, whoo! he could hear it growling and whistling in the distance, and on it would come rushing over the hill-tops, and sweeping along the plain, gathering sound and strength as it drew nearer, until it dashed with a heavy gust against horse and man, driving the sharp rain into their ears, and its cold damp breath into their very bones; and past them it would scour, far, far away, with a stunning roar, as if in ridicule of their weakness, and triumphant in the consciousness of its own strength and power.\n\n‘The bay mare splashed away, through the mud and water, with drooping ears; now and then tossing her head as if to express her disgust at this very ungentlemanly behaviour of the elements, but keeping a good pace notwithstanding, until a gust of wind, more furious than any that had yet assailed them, caused her to stop suddenly and plant her four feet firmly against the ground, to prevent her being blown over. It’s a special mercy that she did this, for if she had been blown over, the vixenish mare was so light, and the gig was so light, and Tom Smart such a light weight into the bargain, that they must infallibly have all gone rolling over and over together, until they reached the confines of earth, or until the wind fell; and in either case the probability is, that neither the vixenish mare, nor the clay-coloured gig with the red wheels, nor Tom Smart, would ever have been fit for service again.\n\n‘“Well, damn my straps and whiskers,” says Tom Smart (Tom sometimes had an unpleasant knack of swearing)--“damn my straps and whiskers,” says Tom, “if this ain’t pleasant, blow me!”\n\n‘You’ll very likely ask me why, as Tom Smart had been pretty well blown already, he expressed this wish to be submitted to the same process again. I can’t say--all I know is, that Tom Smart said so--or at least he always told my uncle he said so, and it’s just the same thing.\n\n“‘Blow me,” says Tom Smart; and the mare neighed as if she were precisely of the same opinion.\n\n“‘Cheer up, old girl,” said Tom, patting the bay mare on the neck with the end of his whip. “It won’t do pushing on, such a night as this; the first house we come to we’ll put up at, so the faster you go the sooner it’s over. Soho, old girl--gently--gently.”\n\n‘Whether the vixenish mare was sufficiently well acquainted with the tones of Tom’s voice to comprehend his meaning, or whether she found it colder standing still than moving on, of course I can’t say. But I can say that Tom had no sooner finished speaking, than she pricked up her ears, and started forward at a speed which made the clay-coloured gig rattle until you would have supposed every one of the red spokes were going to fly out on the turf of Marlborough Downs; and even Tom, whip as he was, couldn’t stop or check her pace, until she drew up of her own accord, before a roadside inn on the right-hand side of the way, about half a quarter of a mile from the end of the Downs.\n\n‘Tom cast a hasty glance at the upper part of the house as he threw the reins to the hostler, and stuck the whip in the box. It was a strange old place, built of a kind of shingle, inlaid, as it were, with cross- beams, with gabled-topped windows projecting completely over the pathway, and a low door with a dark porch, and a couple of steep steps leading down into the house, instead of the modern fashion of half a dozen shallow ones leading up to it. It was a comfortable-looking place though, for there was a strong, cheerful light in the bar window, which shed a bright ray across the road, and even lighted up the hedge on the other side; and there was a red flickering light in the opposite window, one moment but faintly discernible, and the next gleaming strongly through the drawn curtains, which intimated that a rousing fire was blazing within. Marking these little evidences with the eye of an experienced traveller, Tom dismounted with as much agility as his half- frozen limbs would permit, and entered the house.\n\n‘In less than five minutes’ time, Tom was ensconced in the room opposite the bar--the very room where he had imagined the fire blazing--before a substantial, matter-of-fact, roaring fire, composed of something short of a bushel of coals, and wood enough to make half a dozen decent gooseberry bushes, piled half-way up the chimney, and roaring and crackling with a sound that of itself would have warmed the heart of any reasonable man. This was comfortable, but this was not all; for a smartly-dressed girl, with a bright eye and a neat ankle, was laying a very clean white cloth on the table; and as Tom sat with his slippered feet on the fender, and his back to the open door, he saw a charming prospect of the bar reflected in the glass over the chimney-piece, with delightful rows of green bottles and gold labels, together with jars of pickles and preserves, and cheeses and boiled hams, and rounds of beef, arranged on shelves in the most tempting and delicious array. Well, this was comfortable too; but even this was not all--for in the bar, seated at tea at the nicest possible little table, drawn close up before the brightest possible little fire, was a buxom widow of somewhere about eight-and-forty or thereabouts, with a face as comfortable as the bar, who was evidently the landlady of the house, and the supreme ruler over all these agreeable possessions. There was only one drawback to the beauty of the whole picture, and that was a tall man--a very tall man-- in a brown coat and bright basket buttons, and black whiskers and wavy black hair, who was seated at tea with the widow, and who it required no great penetration to discover was in a fair way of persuading her to be a widow no longer, but to confer upon him the privilege of sitting down in that bar, for and during the whole remainder of the term of his natural life.\n\n‘Tom Smart was by no means of an irritable or envious disposition, but somehow or other the tall man with the brown coat and the bright basket buttons did rouse what little gall he had in his composition, and did make him feel extremely indignant, the more especially as he could now and then observe, from his seat before the glass, certain little affectionate familiarities passing between the tall man and the widow, which sufficiently denoted that the tall man was as high in favour as he was in size. Tom was fond of hot punch--I may venture to say he was very fond of hot punch--and after he had seen the vixenish mare well fed and well littered down, and had eaten every bit of the nice little hot dinner which the widow tossed up for him with her own hands, he just ordered a tumbler of it by way of experiment. Now, if there was one thing in the whole range of domestic art, which the widow could manufacture better than another, it was this identical article; and the first tumbler was adapted to Tom Smart’s taste with such peculiar nicety, that he ordered a second with the least possible delay. Hot punch is a pleasant thing, gentlemen--an extremely pleasant thing under any circumstances--but in that snug old parlour, before the roaring fire, with the wind blowing outside till every timber in the old house creaked again, Tom Smart found it perfectly delightful. He ordered another tumbler, and then another--I am not quite certain whether he didn’t order another after that--but the more he drank of the hot punch, the more he thought of the tall man.\n\n‘“Confound his impudence!” said Tom to himself, “what business has he in that snug bar? Such an ugly villain too!” said Tom. “If the widow had any taste, she might surely pick up some better fellow than that.” Here Tom’s eye wandered from the glass on the chimney-piece to the glass on the table; and as he felt himself becoming gradually sentimental, he emptied the fourth tumbler of punch and ordered a fifth.\n\n‘Tom Smart, gentlemen, had always been very much attached to the public line. It had been long his ambition to stand in a bar of his own, in a green coat, knee-cords, and tops. He had a great notion of taking the chair at convivial dinners, and he had often thought how well he could preside in a room of his own in the talking way, and what a capital example he could set to his customers in the drinking department. All these things passed rapidly through Tom’s mind as he sat drinking the hot punch by the roaring fire, and he felt very justly and properly indignant that the tall man should be in a fair way of keeping such an excellent house, while he, Tom Smart, was as far off from it as ever. So, after deliberating over the two last tumblers, whether he hadn’t a perfect right to pick a quarrel with the tall man for having contrived to get into the good graces of the buxom widow, Tom Smart at last arrived at the satisfactory conclusion that he was a very ill-used and persecuted individual, and had better go to bed.\n\n‘Up a wide and ancient staircase the smart girl preceded Tom, shading the chamber candle with her hand, to protect it from the currents of air which in such a rambling old place might have found plenty of room to disport themselves in, without blowing the candle out, but which did blow it out nevertheless--thus affording Tom’s enemies an opportunity of asserting that it was he, and not the wind, who extinguished the candle, and that while he pretended to be blowing it alight again, he was in fact kissing the girl. Be this as it may, another light was obtained, and Tom was conducted through a maze of rooms, and a labyrinth of passages, to the apartment which had been prepared for his reception, where the girl bade him good-night and left him alone.\n\n‘It was a good large room with big closets, and a bed which might have served for a whole boarding-school, to say nothing of a couple of oaken presses that would have held the baggage of a small army; but what struck Tom’s fancy most was a strange, grim-looking, high backed chair, carved in the most fantastic manner, with a flowered damask cushion, and the round knobs at the bottom of the legs carefully tied up in red cloth, as if it had got the gout in its toes. Of any other queer chair, Tom would only have thought it was a queer chair, and there would have been an end of the matter; but there was something about this particular chair, and yet he couldn’t tell what it was, so odd and so unlike any other piece of furniture he had ever seen, that it seemed to fascinate him. He sat down before the fire, and stared at the old chair for half an hour.--Damn the chair, it was such a strange old thing, he couldn’t take his eyes off it.\n\n‘“Well,” said Tom, slowly undressing himself, and staring at the old chair all the while, which stood with a mysterious aspect by the bedside, “I never saw such a rum concern as that in my days. Very odd,” said Tom, who had got rather sage with the hot punch--“very odd.” Tom shook his head with an air of profound wisdom, and looked at the chair again. He couldn’t make anything of it though, so he got into bed, covered himself up warm, and fell asleep.\n\n‘In about half an hour, Tom woke up with a start, from a confused dream of tall men and tumblers of punch; and the first object that presented itself to his waking imagination was the queer chair.\n\n‘“I won’t look at it any more,” said Tom to himself, and he squeezed his eyelids together, and tried to persuade himself he was going to sleep again. No use; nothing but queer chairs danced before his eyes, kicking up their legs, jumping over each other’s backs, and playing all kinds of antics.\n\n“‘I may as well see one real chair, as two or three complete sets of false ones,” said Tom, bringing out his head from under the bedclothes. There it was, plainly discernible by the light of the fire, looking as provoking as ever.\n\n‘Tom gazed at the chair; and, suddenly as he looked at it, a most extraordinary change seemed to come over it. The carving of the back gradually assumed the lineaments and expression of an old, shrivelled human face; the damask cushion became an antique, flapped waistcoat; the round knobs grew into a couple of feet, encased in red cloth slippers; and the whole chair looked like a very ugly old man, of the previous century, with his arms akimbo. Tom sat up in bed, and rubbed his eyes to dispel the illusion. No. The chair was an ugly old gentleman; and what was more, he was winking at Tom Smart.\n\n‘Tom was naturally a headlong, careless sort of dog, and he had had five tumblers of hot punch into the bargain; so, although he was a little startled at first, he began to grow rather indignant when he saw the old gentleman winking and leering at him with such an impudent air. At length he resolved that he wouldn’t stand it; and as the old face still kept winking away as fast as ever, Tom said, in a very angry tone--\n\n‘“What the devil are you winking at me for?”\n\n‘“Because I like it, Tom Smart,” said the chair; or the old gentleman, whichever you like to call him. He stopped winking though, when Tom spoke, and began grinning like a superannuated monkey.\n\n‘“How do you know my name, old nut-cracker face?” inquired Tom Smart, rather staggered; though he pretended to carry it off so well.\n\n‘“Come, come, Tom,” said the old gentleman, “that’s not the way to address solid Spanish mahogany. Damme, you couldn’t treat me with less respect if I was veneered.” When the old gentleman said this, he looked so fierce that Tom began to grow frightened.\n\n‘“I didn’t mean to treat you with any disrespect, Sir,” said Tom, in a much humbler tone than he had spoken in at first.\n\n‘“Well, well,” said the old fellow, “perhaps not--perhaps not. Tom--”\n\n‘“Sir--”\n\n‘“I know everything about you, Tom; everything. You’re very poor, Tom.”\n\n‘“I certainly am,” said Tom Smart. “But how came you to know that?”\n\n‘“Never mind that,” said the old gentleman; “you’re much too fond of punch, Tom.”\n\n‘Tom Smart was just on the point of protesting that he hadn’t tasted a drop since his last birthday, but when his eye encountered that of the old gentleman he looked so knowing that Tom blushed, and was silent.\n\n‘“Tom,” said the old gentleman, “the widow’s a fine woman--remarkably fine woman--eh, Tom?” Here the old fellow screwed up his eyes, cocked up one of his wasted little legs, and looked altogether so unpleasantly amorous, that Tom was quite disgusted with the levity of his behaviour-- at his time of life, too!\n\n‘“I am her guardian, Tom,” said the old gentleman.\n\n‘“Are you?” inquired Tom Smart.\n\n‘“I knew her mother, Tom,” said the old fellow: “and her grandmother. She was very fond of me--made me this waistcoat, Tom.”\n\n‘“Did she?” said Tom Smart.\n\n‘“And these shoes,” said the old fellow, lifting up one of the red cloth mufflers; “but don’t mention it, Tom. I shouldn’t like to have it known that she was so much attached to me. It might occasion some unpleasantness in the family.” When the old rascal said this, he looked so extremely impertinent, that, as Tom Smart afterwards declared, he could have sat upon him without remorse.\n\n‘“I have been a great favourite among the women in my time, Tom,” said the profligate old debauchee; “hundreds of fine women have sat in my lap for hours together. What do you think of that, you dog, eh!” The old gentleman was proceeding to recount some other exploits of his youth, when he was seized with such a violent fit of creaking that he was unable to proceed.\n\n‘“Just serves you right, old boy,” thought Tom Smart; but he didn’t say anything.\n\n‘“Ah!” said the old fellow, “I am a good deal troubled with this now. I am getting old, Tom, and have lost nearly all my nails. I have had an operation performed, too--a small piece let into my back--and I found it a severe trial, Tom.”\n\n‘“I dare say you did, Sir,” said Tom Smart.\n\n‘“However,” said the old gentleman, “that’s not the point. Tom! I want you to marry the widow.”\n\n‘“Me, Sir!” said Tom.\n\n‘“You,” said the old gentleman.\n\n‘“Bless your reverend locks,” said Tom (he had a few scattered horse- hairs left)--“bless your reverend locks, she wouldn’t have me.” And Tom sighed involuntarily, as he thought of the bar.\n\n‘“Wouldn’t she?” said the old gentleman firmly.\n\n‘“No, no,” said Tom; “there’s somebody else in the wind. A tall man--a confoundedly tall man--with black whiskers.”\n\n‘“Tom,” said the old gentleman; “she will never have him.”\n\n‘“Won’t she?” said Tom. “If you stood in the bar, old gentleman, you’d tell another story.”\n\n‘“Pooh, pooh,” said the old gentleman. “I know all about that.”\n\n‘“About what?” said Tom.\n\n‘“The kissing behind the door, and all that sort of thing, Tom,” said the old gentleman. And here he gave another impudent look, which made Tom very wroth, because as you all know, gentlemen, to hear an old fellow, who ought to know better, talking about these things, is very unpleasant--nothing more so.\n\n‘“I know all about that, Tom,” said the old gentleman. “I have seen it done very often in my time, Tom, between more people than I should like to mention to you; but it never came to anything after all.”\n\n‘“You must have seen some queer things,” said Tom, with an inquisitive look.\n\n‘“You may say that, Tom,” replied the old fellow, with a very complicated wink. “I am the last of my family, Tom,” said the old gentleman, with a melancholy sigh.\n\n‘“Was it a large one?” inquired Tom Smart.\n\n‘“There were twelve of us, Tom,” said the old gentleman; “fine, straight-backed, handsome fellows as you’d wish to see. None of your modern abortions--all with arms, and with a degree of polish, though I say it that should not, which it would have done your heart good to behold.”\n\n‘“And what’s become of the others, Sir?” asked Tom Smart--\n\n‘The old gentleman applied his elbow to his eye as he replied, “Gone, Tom, gone. We had hard service, Tom, and they hadn’t all my constitution. They got rheumatic about the legs and arms, and went into kitchens and other hospitals; and one of ‘em, with long service and hard usage, positively lost his senses--he got so crazy that he was obliged to be burnt. Shocking thing that, Tom.”\n\n‘“Dreadful!” said Tom Smart.\n\n‘The old fellow paused for a few minutes, apparently struggling with his feelings of emotion, and then said--\n\n‘“However, Tom, I am wandering from the point. This tall man, Tom, is a rascally adventurer. The moment he married the widow, he would sell off all the furniture, and run away. What would be the consequence? She would be deserted and reduced to ruin, and I should catch my death of cold in some broker’s shop.”\n\n‘“Yes, but--”\n\n‘“Don’t interrupt me,” said the old gentleman. “Of you, Tom, I entertain a very different opinion; for I well know that if you once settled yourself in a public-house, you would never leave it, as long as there was anything to drink within its walls.”\n\n‘“I am very much obliged to you for your good opinion, Sir,” said Tom Smart.\n\n‘“Therefore,” resumed the old gentleman, in a dictatorial tone, “you shall have her, and he shall not.”\n\n‘“What is to prevent it?” said Tom Smart eagerly.\n\n‘“This disclosure,” replied the old gentleman; “he is already married.”\n\n‘“How can I prove it?” said Tom, starting half out of bed.\n\n‘The old gentleman untucked his arm from his side, and having pointed to one of the oaken presses, immediately replaced it, in its old position.\n\n‘“He little thinks,” said the old gentleman, “that in the right-hand pocket of a pair of trousers in that press, he has left a letter, entreating him to return to his disconsolate wife, with six--mark me, Tom--six babes, and all of them small ones.”\n\n‘As the old gentleman solemnly uttered these words, his features grew less and less distinct, and his figure more shadowy. A film came over Tom Smart’s eyes. The old man seemed gradually blending into the chair, the damask waistcoat to resolve into a cushion, the red slippers to shrink into little red cloth bags. The light faded gently away, and Tom Smart fell back on his pillow, and dropped asleep.\n\n‘Morning aroused Tom from the lethargic slumber, into which he had fallen on the disappearance of the old man. He sat up in bed, and for some minutes vainly endeavoured to recall the events of the preceding night. Suddenly they rushed upon him. He looked at the chair; it was a fantastic and grim-looking piece of furniture, certainly, but it must have been a remarkably ingenious and lively imagination, that could have discovered any resemblance between it and an old man.\n\n‘“How are you, old boy?” said Tom. He was bolder in the daylight--most men are.\n\n‘The chair remained motionless, and spoke not a word.\n\n‘“Miserable morning,” said Tom. No. The chair would not be drawn into conversation.\n\n‘“Which press did you point to?--you can tell me that,” said Tom. Devil a word, gentlemen, the chair would say.\n\n‘“It’s not much trouble to open it, anyhow,” said Tom, getting out of bed very deliberately. He walked up to one of the presses. The key was in the lock; he turned it, and opened the door. There was a pair of trousers there. He put his hand into the pocket, and drew forth the identical letter the old gentleman had described!\n\n‘“Queer sort of thing, this,” said Tom Smart, looking first at the chair and then at the press, and then at the letter, and then at the chair again. “Very queer,” said Tom. But, as there was nothing in either, to lessen the queerness, he thought he might as well dress himself, and settle the tall man’s business at once--just to put him out of his misery.\n\n‘Tom surveyed the rooms he passed through, on his way downstairs, with the scrutinising eye of a landlord; thinking it not impossible, that before long, they and their contents would be his property. The tall man was standing in the snug little bar, with his hands behind him, quite at home. He grinned vacantly at Tom. A casual observer might have supposed he did it, only to show his white teeth; but Tom Smart thought that a consciousness of triumph was passing through the place where the tall man’s mind would have been, if he had had any. Tom laughed in his face; and summoned the landlady.\n\n‘“Good-morning ma’am,” said Tom Smart, closing the door of the little parlour as the widow entered.\n\n‘“Good-morning, Sir,” said the widow. “What will you take for breakfast, sir?”\n\n‘Tom was thinking how he should open the case, so he made no answer.\n\n‘“There’s a very nice ham,” said the widow, “and a beautiful cold larded fowl. Shall I send ‘em in, Sir?”\n\n‘These words roused Tom from his reflections. His admiration of the widow increased as she spoke. Thoughtful creature! Comfortable provider!\n\n‘“Who is that gentleman in the bar, ma’am?” inquired Tom.\n\n‘“His name is Jinkins, Sir,” said the widow, slightly blushing.\n\n‘“He’s a tall man,” said Tom.\n\n‘“He is a very fine man, Sir,” replied the widow, “and a very nice gentleman.”\n\n‘“Ah!” said Tom.\n\n‘“Is there anything more you want, Sir?” inquired the widow, rather puzzled by Tom’s manner.\n\n‘“Why, yes,” said Tom. “My dear ma’am, will you have the kindness to sit down for one moment?”\n\n‘The widow looked much amazed, but she sat down, and Tom sat down too, close beside her. I don’t know how it happened, gentlemen--indeed my uncle used to tell me that Tom Smart said he didn’t know how it happened either--but somehow or other the palm of Tom’s hand fell upon the back of the widow’s hand, and remained there while he spoke.\n\n‘“My dear ma’am,” said Tom Smart--he had always a great notion of committing the amiable--“my dear ma’am, you deserve a very excellent husband--you do indeed.”\n\n‘“Lor, Sir!” said the widow--as well she might; Tom’s mode of commencing the conversation being rather unusual, not to say startling; the fact of his never having set eyes upon her before the previous night being taken into consideration. “Lor, Sir!”\n\n‘“I scorn to flatter, my dear ma’am,” said Tom Smart. “You deserve a very admirable husband, and whoever he is, he’ll be a very lucky man.” As Tom said this, his eye involuntarily wandered from the widow’s face to the comfort around him.\n\n‘The widow looked more puzzled than ever, and made an effort to rise. Tom gently pressed her hand, as if to detain her, and she kept her seat. Widows, gentlemen, are not usually timorous, as my uncle used to say.\n\n‘“I am sure I am very much obliged to you, Sir, for your good opinion,” said the buxom landlady, half laughing; “and if ever I marry again--”\n\n’”If,” said Tom Smart, looking very shrewdly out of the right-hand corner of his left eye. “If--”\n\n“Well,” said the widow, laughing outright this time, “when I do, I hope I shall have as good a husband as you describe.”\n\n‘“Jinkins, to wit,” said Tom.\n\n‘“Lor, sir!” exclaimed the widow.\n\n‘“Oh, don’t tell me,” said Tom, “I know him.”\n\n‘“I am sure nobody who knows him, knows anything bad of him,” said the widow, bridling up at the mysterious air with which Tom had spoken.\n\n‘“Hem!” said Tom Smart.\n\n‘The widow began to think it was high time to cry, so she took out her handkerchief, and inquired whether Tom wished to insult her, whether he thought it like a gentleman to take away the character of another gentleman behind his back, why, if he had got anything to say, he didn’t say it to the man, like a man, instead of terrifying a poor weak woman in that way; and so forth.\n\n‘“I’ll say it to him fast enough,” said Tom, “only I want you to hear it first.”\n\n‘“What is it?” inquired the widow, looking intently in Tom’s countenance.\n\n‘“I’ll astonish you,” said Tom, putting his hand in his pocket.\n\n‘“If it is, that he wants money,” said the widow, “I know that already, and you needn’t trouble yourself.” ‘“Pooh, nonsense, that’s nothing,” said Tom Smart, “I want money. ‘Tain’t that.”\n\n‘“Oh, dear, what can it be?” exclaimed the poor widow.\n\n‘“Don’t be frightened,” said Tom Smart. He slowly drew forth the letter, and unfolded it. “You won’t scream?” said Tom doubtfully.\n\n‘“No, no,” replied the widow; “let me see it.”\n\n‘“You won’t go fainting away, or any of that nonsense?” said Tom.\n\n‘“No, no,” returned the widow hastily.\n\n‘“And don’t run out, and blow him up,” said Tom; “because I’ll do all that for you. You had better not exert yourself.”\n\n‘“Well, well,” said the widow, “let me see it.”\n\n‘“I will,” replied Tom Smart; and, with these words, he placed the letter in the widow’s hand.\n\n‘Gentlemen, I have heard my uncle say, that Tom Smart said the widow’s lamentations when she heard the disclosure would have pierced a heart of stone. Tom was certainly very tender-hearted, but they pierced his, to the very core. The widow rocked herself to and fro, and wrung her hands.\n\n‘“Oh, the deception and villainy of the man!” said the widow.\n\n‘“Frightful, my dear ma’am; but compose yourself,” said Tom Smart.\n\n‘“Oh, I can’t compose myself,” shrieked the widow. “I shall never find anyone else I can love so much!”\n\n‘“Oh, yes you will, my dear soul,” said Tom Smart, letting fall a shower of the largest-sized tears, in pity for the widow’s misfortunes. Tom Smart, in the energy of his compassion, had put his arm round the widow’s waist; and the widow, in a passion of grief, had clasped Tom’s hand. She looked up in Tom’s face, and smiled through her tears. Tom looked down in hers, and smiled through his.\n\n‘I never could find out, gentlemen, whether Tom did or did not kiss the widow at that particular moment. He used to tell my uncle he didn’t, but I have my doubts about it. Between ourselves, gentlemen, I rather think he did.\n\n‘At all events, Tom kicked the very tall man out at the front door half an hour later, and married the widow a month after. And he used to drive about the country, with the clay-coloured gig with the red wheels, and the vixenish mare with the fast pace, till he gave up business many years afterwards, and went to France with his wife; and then the old house was pulled down.’\n\n‘Will you allow me to ask you,’ said the inquisitive old gentleman, ‘what became of the chair?’\n\n‘Why,’ replied the one-eyed bagman, ‘it was observed to creak very much on the day of the wedding; but Tom Smart couldn’t say for certain whether it was with pleasure or bodily infirmity. He rather thought it was the latter, though, for it never spoke afterwards.’\n\n‘Everybody believed the story, didn’t they?’ said the dirty-faced man, refilling his pipe.\n\n‘Except Tom’s enemies,’ replied the bagman. ‘Some of ‘em said Tom invented it altogether; and others said he was drunk and fancied it, and got hold of the wrong trousers by mistake before he went to bed. But nobody ever minded what they said.’\n\n‘Tom Smart said it was all true?’\n\n‘Every word.’\n\n‘And your uncle?’\n\n‘Every letter.’\n\n‘They must have been very nice men, both of ‘em,’ said the dirty-faced man.\n\n‘Yes, they were,’ replied the bagman; ‘very nice men indeed!’\n\nCHAPTER XV. IN WHICH IS GIVEN A FAITHFUL PORTRAITURE OF TWO DISTINGUISHED PERSONS; AND AN ACCURATE DESCRIPTION OF A PUBLIC BREAKFAST IN THEIR HOUSE AND GROUNDS: WHICH PUBLIC BREAKFAST LEADS TO THE RECOGNITION OF AN OLD ACQUAINTANCE, AND THE COMMENCEMENT OF ANOTHER CHAPTER\nMr. Pickwick’s conscience had been somewhat reproaching him for his recent neglect of his friends at the Peacock; and he was just on the point of walking forth in quest of them, on the third morning after the election had terminated, when his faithful valet put into his hand a card, on which was engraved the following inscription:--\n\nMrs. Leo Hunter THE DEN. EATANSWILL.\n\n‘Person’s a-waitin’,’ said Sam, epigrammatically.\n\n‘Does the person want me, Sam?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘He wants you partickler; and no one else ‘ll do, as the devil’s private secretary said ven he fetched avay Doctor Faustus,’ replied Mr. Weller.\n\n‘He. Is it a gentleman?’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘A wery good imitation o’ one, if it ain’t,’ replied Mr. Weller.\n\n‘But this is a lady’s card,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Given me by a gen’l’m’n, howsoever,’ replied Sam, ‘and he’s a-waitin’ in the drawing-room--said he’d rather wait all day, than not see you.’\n\nMr. Pickwick, on hearing this determination, descended to the drawing- room, where sat a grave man, who started up on his entrance, and said, with an air of profound respect:--\n\n‘Mr. Pickwick, I presume?’\n\n‘The same.’\n\n‘Allow me, Sir, the honour of grasping your hand. Permit me, Sir, to shake it,’ said the grave man.\n\n‘Certainly,’ said Mr. Pickwick. The stranger shook the extended hand, and then continued--\n\n‘We have heard of your fame, sir. The noise of your antiquarian discussion has reached the ears of Mrs. Leo Hunter--my wife, sir; I am Mr. Leo Hunter’--the stranger paused, as if he expected that Mr. Pickwick would be overcome by the disclosure; but seeing that he remained perfectly calm, proceeded--\n\n‘My wife, sir--Mrs. Leo Hunter--is proud to number among her acquaintance all those who have rendered themselves celebrated by their works and talents. Permit me, sir, to place in a conspicuous part of the list the name of Mr. Pickwick, and his brother-members of the club that derives its name from him.’\n\n‘I shall be extremely happy to make the acquaintance of such a lady, sir,’ replied Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘You shall make it, sir,’ said the grave man. ‘To-morrow morning, sir, we give a public breakfast--a fete champetre--to a great number of those who have rendered themselves celebrated by their works and talents. Permit Mrs. Leo Hunter, Sir, to have the gratification of seeing you at the Den.’\n\n‘With great pleasure,’ replied Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Mrs. Leo Hunter has many of these breakfasts, Sir,’ resumed the new acquaintance--‘“feasts of reason,” sir, “and flows of soul,” as somebody who wrote a sonnet to Mrs. Leo Hunter on her breakfasts, feelingly and originally observed.’\n\n‘Was he celebrated for his works and talents?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘He was Sir,’ replied the grave man, ‘all Mrs. Leo Hunter’s acquaintances are; it is her ambition, sir, to have no other acquaintance.’\n\n‘It is a very noble ambition,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘When I inform Mrs. Leo Hunter, that that remark fell from your lips, sir, she will indeed be proud,’ said the grave man. ‘You have a gentleman in your train, who has produced some beautiful little poems, I think, sir.’\n\n‘My friend Mr. Snodgrass has a great taste for poetry,’ replied Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘So has Mrs. Leo Hunter, Sir. She dotes on poetry, sir. She adores it; I may say that her whole soul and mind are wound up, and entwined with it. She has produced some delightful pieces, herself, sir. You may have met with her “Ode to an Expiring Frog,” sir.’\n\n‘I don’t think I have,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘You astonish me, Sir,’ said Mr. Leo Hunter. ‘It created an immense sensation. It was signed with an “L” and eight stars, and appeared originally in a lady’s magazine. It commenced--\n\n‘“Can I view thee panting, lying On thy stomach, without sighing; Can I unmoved see thee dying On a log Expiring frog!”’\n\n‘Beautiful!’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Fine,’ said Mr. Leo Hunter; ‘so simple.’\n\n‘Very,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘The next verse is still more touching. Shall I repeat it?’\n\n‘If you please,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘It runs thus,’ said the grave man, still more gravely.\n\n‘“Say, have fiends in shape of boys, With wild halloo, and brutal noise, Hunted thee from marshy joys, With a dog, Expiring frog!”’\n\n‘Finely expressed,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘All point, Sir,’ said Mr. Leo Hunter; ‘but you shall hear Mrs. Leo Hunter repeat it. She can do justice to it, Sir. She will repeat it, in character, Sir, to-morrow morning.’\n\n‘In character!’\n\n‘As Minerva. But I forgot--it’s a fancy-dress dejeune.’\n\n‘Dear me,’ said Mr. Pickwick, glancing at his own figure--‘I can’t possibly--’\n\n‘Can’t, sir; can’t!’ exclaimed Mr. Leo Hunter. ‘Solomon Lucas, the Jew in the High Street, has thousands of fancy-dresses. Consider, Sir, how many appropriate characters are open for your selection. Plato, Zeno, Epicurus, Pythagoras--all founders of clubs.’\n\n‘I know that,’ said Mr. Pickwick; ‘but as I cannot put myself in competition with those great men, I cannot presume to wear their dresses.’\n\nThe grave man considered deeply, for a few seconds, and then said--\n\n‘On reflection, Sir, I don’t know whether it would not afford Mrs. Leo Hunter greater pleasure, if her guests saw a gentleman of your celebrity in his own costume, rather than in an assumed one. I may venture to promise an exception in your case, sir--yes, I am quite certain that, on behalf of Mrs. Leo Hunter, I may venture to do so.’\n\n‘In that case,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘I shall have great pleasure in coming.’\n\n‘But I waste your time, Sir,’ said the grave man, as if suddenly recollecting himself. ‘I know its value, sir. I will not detain you. I may tell Mrs. Leo Hunter, then, that she may confidently expect you and your distinguished friends? Good-morning, Sir, I am proud to have beheld so eminent a personage--not a step sir; not a word.’ And without giving Mr. Pickwick time to offer remonstrance or denial, Mr. Leo Hunter stalked gravely away.\n\nMr. Pickwick took up his hat, and repaired to the Peacock, but Mr. Winkle had conveyed the intelligence of the fancy-ball there, before him.\n\n‘Mrs. Pott’s going,’ were the first words with which he saluted his leader.\n\n‘Is she?’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘As Apollo,’ replied Winkle. ‘Only Pott objects to the tunic.’\n\n‘He is right. He is quite right,’ said Mr. Pickwick emphatically.\n\n‘Yes; so she’s going to wear a white satin gown with gold spangles.’\n\n‘They’ll hardly know what she’s meant for; will they?’ inquired Mr. Snodgrass.\n\n‘Of course they will,’ replied Mr. Winkle indignantly. ‘They’ll see her lyre, won’t they?’\n\n‘True; I forgot that,’ said Mr. Snodgrass.\n\n‘I shall go as a bandit,’ interposed Mr. Tupman.\n\n‘What!’ said Mr. Pickwick, with a sudden start.\n\n‘As a bandit,’ repeated Mr. Tupman, mildly.\n\n‘You don’t mean to say,’ said Mr. Pickwick, gazing with solemn sternness at his friend--‘you don’t mean to say, Mr. Tupman, that it is your intention to put yourself into a green velvet jacket, with a two-inch tail?’\n\n‘Such is my intention, Sir,’ replied Mr. Tupman warmly. ‘And why not, sir?’\n\n‘Because, Sir,’ said Mr. Pickwick, considerably excited--‘because you are too old, Sir.’\n\n‘Too old!’ exclaimed Mr. Tupman.\n\n‘And if any further ground of objection be wanting,’ continued Mr. Pickwick, ‘you are too fat, sir.’\n\n‘Sir,’ said Mr. Tupman, his face suffused with a crimson glow, ‘this is an insult.’\n\n‘Sir,’ replied Mr. Pickwick, in the same tone, ‘it is not half the insult to you, that your appearance in my presence in a green velvet jacket, with a two-inch tail, would be to me.’\n\n‘Sir,’ said Mr. Tupman, ‘you’re a fellow.’\n\n‘Sir,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘you’re another!’\n\nMr. Tupman advanced a step or two, and glared at Mr. Pickwick. Mr. Pickwick returned the glare, concentrated into a focus by means of his spectacles, and breathed a bold defiance. Mr. Snodgrass and Mr. Winkle looked on, petrified at beholding such a scene between two such men.\n\n‘Sir,’ said Mr. Tupman, after a short pause, speaking in a low, deep voice, ‘you have called me old.’\n\n‘I have,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘And fat.’\n\n‘I reiterate the charge.’\n\n‘And a fellow.’\n‘So you are!’\n\nThere was a fearful pause.\n\n‘My attachment to your person, sir,’ said Mr. Tupman, speaking in a voice tremulous with emotion, and tucking up his wristbands meanwhile, ‘is great--very great--but upon that person, I must take summary vengeance.’\n\n‘Come on, Sir!’ replied Mr. Pickwick. Stimulated by the exciting nature of the dialogue, the heroic man actually threw himself into a paralytic attitude, confidently supposed by the two bystanders to have been intended as a posture of defence.\n\n‘What!’ exclaimed Mr. Snodgrass, suddenly recovering the power of speech, of which intense astonishment had previously bereft him, and rushing between the two, at the imminent hazard of receiving an application on the temple from each--‘what! Mr. Pickwick, with the eyes of the world upon you! Mr. Tupman! who, in common with us all, derives a lustre from his undying name! For shame, gentlemen; for shame.’\n\nThe unwonted lines which momentary passion had ruled in Mr. Pickwick’s clear and open brow, gradually melted away, as his young friend spoke, like the marks of a black-lead pencil beneath the softening influence of india-rubber. His countenance had resumed its usual benign expression, ere he concluded.\n\n‘I have been hasty,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘very hasty. Tupman; your hand.’\n\nThe dark shadow passed from Mr. Tupman’s face, as he warmly grasped the hand of his friend.\n\n‘I have been hasty, too,’ said he.\n\n‘No, no,’ interrupted Mr. Pickwick, ‘the fault was mine. You will wear the green velvet jacket?’\n\n‘No, no,’ replied Mr. Tupman.\n\n‘To oblige me, you will,’ resumed Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Well, well, I will,’ said Mr. Tupman.\n\nIt was accordingly settled that Mr. Tupman, Mr. Winkle, and Mr. Snodgrass, should all wear fancy-dresses. Thus Mr. Pickwick was led by the very warmth of his own good feelings to give his consent to a proceeding from which his better judgment would have recoiled--a more striking illustration of his amiable character could hardly have been conceived, even if the events recorded in these pages had been wholly imaginary.\n\nMr. Leo Hunter had not exaggerated the resources of Mr. Solomon Lucas. His wardrobe was extensive--very extensive--not strictly classical perhaps, not quite new, nor did it contain any one garment made precisely after the fashion of any age or time, but everything was more or less spangled; and what can be prettier than spangles! It may be objected that they are not adapted to the daylight, but everybody knows that they would glitter if there were lamps; and nothing can be clearer than that if people give fancy-balls in the day-time, and the dresses do not show quite as well as they would by night, the fault lies solely with the people who give the fancy-balls, and is in no wise chargeable on the spangles. Such was the convincing reasoning of Mr. Solomon Lucas; and influenced by such arguments did Mr. Tupman, Mr. Winkle, and Mr. Snodgrass engage to array themselves in costumes which his taste and experience induced him to recommend as admirably suited to the occasion.\n\nA carriage was hired from the Town Arms, for the accommodation of the Pickwickians, and a chariot was ordered from the same repository, for the purpose of conveying Mr. and Mrs. Pott to Mrs. Leo Hunter’s grounds, which Mr. Pott, as a delicate acknowledgment of having received an invitation, had already confidently predicted in the Eatanswill Gazette ‘would present a scene of varied and delicious enchantment--a bewildering coruscation of beauty and talent--a lavish and prodigal display of hospitality--above all, a degree of splendour softened by the most exquisite taste; and adornment refined with perfect harmony and the chastest good keeping--compared with which, the fabled gorgeousness of Eastern fairyland itself would appear to be clothed in as many dark and murky colours, as must be the mind of the splenetic and unmanly being who could presume to taint with the venom of his envy, the preparations made by the virtuous and highly distinguished lady at whose shrine this humble tribute of admiration was offered.’ This last was a piece of biting sarcasm against the Independent, who, in consequence of not having been invited at all, had been, through four numbers, affecting to sneer at the whole affair, in his very largest type, with all the adjectives in capital letters.\n\nThe morning came: it was a pleasant sight to behold Mr. Tupman in full brigand’s costume, with a very tight jacket, sitting like a pincushion over his back and shoulders, the upper portion of his legs incased in the velvet shorts, and the lower part thereof swathed in the complicated bandages to which all brigands are peculiarly attached. It was pleasing to see his open and ingenuous countenance, well mustachioed and corked, looking out from an open shirt collar; and to contemplate the sugar-loaf hat, decorated with ribbons of all colours, which he was compelled to carry on his knee, inasmuch as no known conveyance with a top to it, would admit of any man’s carrying it between his head and the roof. Equally humorous and agreeable was the appearance of Mr. Snodgrass in blue satin trunks and cloak, white silk tights and shoes, and Grecian helmet, which everybody knows (and if they do not, Mr. Solomon Lucas did) to have been the regular, authentic, everyday costume of a troubadour, from the earliest ages down to the time of their final disappearance from the face of the earth. All this was pleasant, but this was as nothing compared with the shouting of the populace when the carriage drew up, behind Mr. Pott’s chariot, which chariot itself drew up at Mr. Pott’s door, which door itself opened, and displayed the great Pott accoutred as a Russian officer of justice, with a tremendous knout in his hand--tastefully typical of the stern and mighty power of the Eatanswill Gazette, and the fearful lashings it bestowed on public offenders.\n\n‘Bravo!’ shouted Mr. Tupman and Mr. Snodgrass from the passage, when they beheld the walking allegory.\n\n‘Bravo!’ Mr. Pickwick was heard to exclaim, from the passage.\n\n‘Hoo-roar Pott!’ shouted the populace. Amid these salutations, Mr. Pott, smiling with that kind of bland dignity which sufficiently testified that he felt his power, and knew how to exert it, got into the chariot.\n\nThen there emerged from the house, Mrs. Pott, who would have looked very like Apollo if she hadn’t had a gown on, conducted by Mr. Winkle, who, in his light-red coat could not possibly have been mistaken for anything but a sportsman, if he had not borne an equal resemblance to a general postman. Last of all came Mr. Pickwick, whom the boys applauded as loud as anybody, probably under the impression that his tights and gaiters were some remnants of the dark ages; and then the two vehicles proceeded towards Mrs. Leo Hunter’s; Mr. Weller (who was to assist in waiting) being stationed on the box of that in which his master was seated.\n\nEvery one of the men, women, boys, girls, and babies, who were assembled to see the visitors in their fancy-dresses, screamed with delight and ecstasy, when Mr. Pickwick, with the brigand on one arm, and the troubadour on the other, walked solemnly up the entrance. Never were such shouts heard as those which greeted Mr. Tupman’s efforts to fix the sugar-loaf hat on his head, by way of entering the garden in style.\n\nThe preparations were on the most delightful scale; fully realising the prophetic Pott’s anticipations about the gorgeousness of Eastern fairyland, and at once affording a sufficient contradiction to the malignant statements of the reptile Independent. The grounds were more than an acre and a quarter in extent, and they were filled with people! Never was such a blaze of beauty, and fashion, and literature. There was the young lady who ‘did’ the poetry in the Eatanswill Gazette, in the garb of a sultana, leaning upon the arm of the young gentleman who ‘did’ the review department, and who was appropriately habited in a field- marshal’s uniform--the boots excepted. There were hosts of these geniuses, and any reasonable person would have thought it honour enough to meet them. But more than these, there were half a dozen lions from London--authors, real authors, who had written whole books, and printed them afterwards--and here you might see ‘em, walking about, like ordinary men, smiling, and talking--aye, and talking pretty considerable nonsense too, no doubt with the benign intention of rendering themselves intelligible to the common people about them. Moreover, there was a band of music in pasteboard caps; four something-ean singers in the costume of their country, and a dozen hired waiters in the costume of their country--and very dirty costume too. And above all, there was Mrs. Leo Hunter in the character of Minerva, receiving the company, and overflowing with pride and gratification at the notion of having called such distinguished individuals together.\n\n‘Mr. Pickwick, ma’am,’ said a servant, as that gentleman approached the presiding goddess, with his hat in his hand, and the brigand and troubadour on either arm.\n\n‘What! Where!’ exclaimed Mrs. Leo Hunter, starting up, in an affected rapture of surprise.\n\n‘Here,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Is it possible that I have really the gratification of beholding Mr. Pickwick himself!’ ejaculated Mrs. Leo Hunter.\n\n‘No other, ma’am,’ replied Mr. Pickwick, bowing very low. ‘Permit me to introduce my friends--Mr. Tupman--Mr. Winkle--Mr. Snodgrass--to the authoress of “The Expiring Frog.”’\n\nVery few people but those who have tried it, know what a difficult process it is to bow in green velvet smalls, and a tight jacket, and high-crowned hat; or in blue satin trunks and white silks, or knee-cords and top-boots that were never made for the wearer, and have been fixed upon him without the remotest reference to the comparative dimensions of himself and the suit. Never were such distortions as Mr. Tupman’s frame underwent in his efforts to appear easy and graceful--never was such ingenious posturing, as his fancy-dressed friends exhibited.\n\n‘Mr. Pickwick,’ said Mrs. Leo Hunter, ‘I must make you promise not to stir from my side the whole day. There are hundreds of people here, that I must positively introduce you to.’\n\n‘You are very kind, ma’am,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘In the first place, here are my little girls; I had almost forgotten them,’ said Minerva, carelessly pointing towards a couple of full-grown young ladies, of whom one might be about twenty, and the other a year or two older, and who were dressed in very juvenile costumes--whether to make them look young, or their mamma younger, Mr. Pickwick does not distinctly inform us.\n\n‘They are very beautiful,’ said Mr. Pickwick, as the juveniles turned away, after being presented.\n\n‘They are very like their mamma, Sir,’ said Mr. Pott, majestically.\n\n‘Oh, you naughty man,’ exclaimed Mrs. Leo Hunter, playfully tapping the editor’s arm with her fan (Minerva with a fan!).\n\n‘Why now, my dear Mrs. Hunter,’ said Mr. Pott, who was trumpeter in ordinary at the Den, ‘you know that when your picture was in the exhibition of the Royal Academy, last year, everybody inquired whether it was intended for you, or your youngest daughter; for you were so much alike that there was no telling the difference between you.’\n\n‘Well, and if they did, why need you repeat it, before strangers?’ said Mrs. Leo Hunter, bestowing another tap on the slumbering lion of the Eatanswill Gazette.\n\n‘Count, count,’ screamed Mrs. Leo Hunter to a well-whiskered individual in a foreign uniform, who was passing by.\n\n‘Ah! you want me?’ said the count, turning back.\n\n‘I want to introduce two very clever people to each other,’ said Mrs. Leo Hunter. ‘Mr. Pickwick, I have great pleasure in introducing you to Count Smorltork.’ She added in a hurried whisper to Mr. Pickwick--‘The famous foreigner--gathering materials for his great work on England-- hem!--Count Smorltork, Mr. Pickwick.’\n\nMr. Pickwick saluted the count with all the reverence due to so great a man, and the count drew forth a set of tablets.\n\n‘What you say, Mrs. Hunt?’ inquired the count, smiling graciously on the gratified Mrs. Leo Hunter, ‘Pig Vig or Big Vig--what you call--lawyer-- eh? I see--that is it. Big Vig’--and the count was proceeding to enter Mr. Pickwick in his tablets, as a gentleman of the long robe, who derived his name from the profession to which he belonged, when Mrs. Leo Hunter interposed.\n\n‘No, no, count,’ said the lady, ‘Pick-wick.’\n\n‘Ah, ah, I see,’ replied the count. ‘Peek--christian name; Weeks-- surname; good, ver good. Peek Weeks. How you do, Weeks?’\n\n‘Quite well, I thank you,’ replied Mr. Pickwick, with all his usual affability. ‘Have you been long in England?’\n\n‘Long--ver long time--fortnight--more.’\n\n‘Do you stay here long?’\n\n‘One week.’\n\n‘You will have enough to do,’ said Mr. Pickwick smiling, ‘to gather all the materials you want in that time.’\n\n‘Eh, they are gathered,’ said the count.\n\n‘Indeed!’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘They are here,’ added the count, tapping his forehead significantly. ‘Large book at home--full of notes--music, picture, science, potry, poltic; all tings.’\n\n‘The word politics, sir,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘comprises in itself, a difficult study of no inconsiderable magnitude.’\n\n‘Ah!’ said the count, drawing out the tablets again, ‘ver good--fine words to begin a chapter. Chapter forty-seven. Poltics. The word poltic surprises by himself--’ And down went Mr. Pickwick’s remark, in Count Smorltork’s tablets, with such variations and additions as the count’s exuberant fancy suggested, or his imperfect knowledge of the language occasioned.\n\n‘Count,’ said Mrs. Leo Hunter.\n\n‘Mrs. Hunt,’ replied the count.\n\n‘This is Mr. Snodgrass, a friend of Mr. Pickwick’s, and a poet.’\n\n‘Stop,’ exclaimed the count, bringing out the tablets once more. ‘Head, potry--chapter, literary friends--name, Snowgrass; ver good. Introduced to Snowgrass--great poet, friend of Peek Weeks--by Mrs. Hunt, which wrote other sweet poem--what is that name?--Fog--Perspiring Fog--ver good--ver good indeed.’ And the count put up his tablets, and with sundry bows and acknowledgments walked away, thoroughly satisfied that he had made the most important and valuable additions to his stock of information.\n\n‘Wonderful man, Count Smorltork,’ said Mrs. Leo Hunter.\n\n‘Sound philosopher,’ said Mr. Pott.\n\n‘Clear-headed, strong-minded person,’ added Mr. Snodgrass.\n\nA chorus of bystanders took up the shout of Count Smorltork’s praise, shook their heads sagely, and unanimously cried, ‘Very!’\n\nAs the enthusiasm in Count Smorltork’s favour ran very high, his praises might have been sung until the end of the festivities, if the four something-ean singers had not ranged themselves in front of a small apple-tree, to look picturesque, and commenced singing their national songs, which appeared by no means difficult of execution, inasmuch as the grand secret seemed to be, that three of the something-ean singers should grunt, while the fourth howled. This interesting performance having concluded amidst the loud plaudits of the whole company, a boy forthwith proceeded to entangle himself with the rails of a chair, and to jump over it, and crawl under it, and fall down with it, and do everything but sit upon it, and then to make a cravat of his legs, and tie them round his neck, and then to illustrate the ease with which a human being can be made to look like a magnified toad--all which feats yielded high delight and satisfaction to the assembled spectators. After which, the voice of Mrs. Pott was heard to chirp faintly forth, something which courtesy interpreted into a song, which was all very classical, and strictly in character, because Apollo was himself a composer, and composers can very seldom sing their own music or anybody else’s, either. This was succeeded by Mrs. Leo Hunter’s recitation of her far-famed ‘Ode to an Expiring Frog,’ which was encored once, and would have been encored twice, if the major part of the guests, who thought it was high time to get something to eat, had not said that it was perfectly shameful to take advantage of Mrs. Hunter’s good nature. So although Mrs. Leo Hunter professed her perfect willingness to recite the ode again, her kind and considerate friends wouldn’t hear of it on any account; and the refreshment room being thrown open, all the people who had ever been there before, scrambled in with all possible despatch- -Mrs. Leo Hunter’s usual course of proceedings being, to issue cards for a hundred, and breakfast for fifty, or in other words to feed only the very particular lions, and let the smaller animals take care of themselves.\n\n‘Where is Mr. Pott?’ said Mrs. Leo Hunter, as she placed the aforesaid lions around her.\n\n‘Here I am,’ said the editor, from the remotest end of the room; far beyond all hope of food, unless something was done for him by the hostess.\n\n‘Won’t you come up here?’\n\n‘Oh, pray don’t mind him,’ said Mrs. Pott, in the most obliging voice-- ‘you give yourself a great deal of unnecessary trouble, Mrs. Hunter. You’ll do very well there, won’t you--dear?’\n\n‘Certainly--love,’ replied the unhappy Pott, with a grim smile. Alas for the knout! The nervous arm that wielded it, with such a gigantic force on public characters, was paralysed beneath the glance of the imperious Mrs. Pott.\n\nMrs. Leo Hunter looked round her in triumph. Count Smorltork was busily engaged in taking notes of the contents of the dishes; Mr. Tupman was doing the honours of the lobster salad to several lionesses, with a degree of grace which no brigand ever exhibited before; Mr. Snodgrass having cut out the young gentleman who cut up the books for the Eatanswill Gazette, was engaged in an impassioned argument with the young lady who did the poetry; and Mr. Pickwick was making himself universally agreeable. Nothing seemed wanting to render the select circle complete, when Mr. Leo Hunter--whose department on these occasions, was to stand about in doorways, and talk to the less important people--suddenly called out--\n\n‘My dear; here’s Mr. Charles Fitz-Marshall.’\n\n‘Oh dear,’ said Mrs. Leo Hunter, ‘how anxiously I have been expecting him. Pray make room, to let Mr. Fitz-Marshall pass. Tell Mr. Fitz- Marshall, my dear, to come up to me directly, to be scolded for coming so late.’\n\n‘Coming, my dear ma’am,’ cried a voice, ‘as quick as I can--crowds of people--full room--hard work--very.’\n\nMr. Pickwick’s knife and fork fell from his hand. He stared across the table at Mr. Tupman, who had dropped his knife and fork, and was looking as if he were about to sink into the ground without further notice.\n\n‘Ah!’ cried the voice, as its owner pushed his way among the last five- and-twenty Turks, officers, cavaliers, and Charles the Seconds, that remained between him and the table, ‘regular mangle--Baker’s patent--not a crease in my coat, after all this squeezing--might have “got up my linen” as I came along--ha! ha! not a bad idea, that--queer thing to have it mangled when it’s upon one, though--trying process--very.’\n\nWith these broken words, a young man dressed as a naval officer made his way up to the table, and presented to the astonished Pickwickians the identical form and features of Mr. Alfred Jingle.\n\nThe offender had barely time to take Mrs. Leo Hunter’s proffered hand, when his eyes encountered the indignant orbs of Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Hollo!’ said Jingle. ‘Quite forgot--no directions to postillion--give ‘em at once--back in a minute.’\n\n‘The servant, or Mr. Hunter will do it in a moment, Mr. Fitz-Marshall,’ said Mrs. Leo Hunter.\n\n‘No, no--I’ll do it--shan’t be long--back in no time,’ replied Jingle. With these words he disappeared among the crowd.\n\n‘Will you allow me to ask you, ma’am,’ said the excited Mr. Pickwick, rising from his seat, ‘who that young man is, and where he resides?’\n\n‘He is a gentleman of fortune, Mr. Pickwick,’ said Mrs. Leo Hunter, ‘to whom I very much want to introduce you. The count will be delighted with him.’\n\n‘Yes, yes,’ said Mr. Pickwick hastily. ‘His residence--’\n\n‘Is at present at the Angel at Bury.’\n\n‘At Bury?’\n\n‘At Bury St. Edmunds, not many miles from here. But dear me, Mr. Pickwick, you are not going to leave us; surely Mr. Pickwick you cannot think of going so soon?’\n\nBut long before Mrs. Leo Hunter had finished speaking, Mr. Pickwick had plunged through the throng, and reached the garden, whither he was shortly afterwards joined by Mr. Tupman, who had followed his friend closely.\n\n‘It’s of no use,’ said Mr. Tupman. ‘He has gone.’\n\n‘I know it,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘and I will follow him.’\n\n‘Follow him! Where?’ inquired Mr. Tupman.\n\n‘To the Angel at Bury,’ replied Mr. Pickwick, speaking very quickly. ‘How do we know whom he is deceiving there? He deceived a worthy man once, and we were the innocent cause. He shall not do it again, if I can help it; I’ll expose him! Sam! Where’s my servant?’\n\n‘Here you are, Sir,’ said Mr. Weller, emerging from a sequestered spot, where he had been engaged in discussing a bottle of Madeira, which he had abstracted from the breakfast-table an hour or two before. ‘Here’s your servant, Sir. Proud o’ the title, as the living skellinton said, ven they show’d him.’\n\n‘Follow me instantly,’ said Mr. Pickwick. ‘Tupman, if I stay at Bury, you can join me there, when I write. Till then, good-bye!’\n\nRemonstrances were useless. Mr. Pickwick was roused, and his mind was made up. Mr. Tupman returned to his companions; and in another hour had drowned all present recollection of Mr. Alfred Jingle, or Mr. Charles Fitz-Marshall, in an exhilarating quadrille and a bottle of champagne. By that time, Mr. Pickwick and Sam Weller, perched on the outside of a stage-coach, were every succeeding minute placing a less and less distance between themselves and the good old town of Bury St. Edmunds.\n\n# CHAPTER XVI. TOO FULL OF ADVENTURE TO BE BRIEFLY DESCRIBED\n\nThere is no month in the whole year in which nature wears a more beautiful appearance than in the month of August. Spring has many beauties, and May is a fresh and blooming month, but the charms of this time of year are enhanced by their contrast with the winter season. August has no such advantage. It comes when we remember nothing but clear skies, green fields, and sweet-smelling flowers--when the recollection of snow, and ice, and bleak winds, has faded from our minds as completely as they have disappeared from the earth--and yet what a pleasant time it is! Orchards and cornfields ring with the hum of labour; trees bend beneath the thick clusters of rich fruit which bow their branches to the ground; and the corn, piled in graceful sheaves, or waving in every light breath that sweeps above it, as if it wooed the sickle, tinges the landscape with a golden hue. A mellow softness appears to hang over the whole earth; the influence of the season seems to extend itself to the very wagon, whose slow motion across the well- reaped field is perceptible only to the eye, but strikes with no harsh sound upon the ear.\n\nAs the coach rolls swiftly past the fields and orchards which skirt the road, groups of women and children, piling the fruit in sieves, or gathering the scattered ears of corn, pause for an instant from their labour, and shading the sun-burned face with a still browner hand, gaze upon the passengers with curious eyes, while some stout urchin, too small to work, but too mischievous to be left at home, scrambles over the side of the basket in which he has been deposited for security, and kicks and screams with delight. The reaper stops in his work, and stands with folded arms, looking at the vehicle as it whirls past; and the rough cart-horses bestow a sleepy glance upon the smart coach team, which says as plainly as a horse’s glance can, ‘It’s all very fine to look at, but slow going, over a heavy field, is better than warm work like that, upon a dusty road, after all.’ You cast a look behind you, as you turn a corner of the road. The women and children have resumed their labour; the reaper once more stoops to his work; the cart-horses have moved on; and all are again in motion.\n\nThe influence of a scene like this, was not lost upon the well-regulated mind of Mr. Pickwick. Intent upon the resolution he had formed, of exposing the real character of the nefarious Jingle, in any quarter in which he might be pursuing his fraudulent designs, he sat at first taciturn and contemplative, brooding over the means by which his purpose could be best attained. By degrees his attention grew more and more attracted by the objects around him; and at last he derived as much enjoyment from the ride, as if it had been undertaken for the pleasantest reason in the world.\n\n‘Delightful prospect, Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Beats the chimbley-pots, Sir,’ replied Mr. Weller, touching his hat.\n\n‘I suppose you have hardly seen anything but chimney-pots and bricks and mortar all your life, Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick, smiling.\n\n‘I worn’t always a boots, sir,’ said Mr. Weller, with a shake of the head. ‘I wos a vaginer’s boy, once.’\n\n‘When was that?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘When I wos first pitched neck and crop into the world, to play at leap- frog with its troubles,’ replied Sam. ‘I wos a carrier’s boy at startin’; then a vaginer’s, then a helper, then a boots. Now I’m a gen’l’m’n’s servant. I shall be a gen’l’m’n myself one of these days, perhaps, with a pipe in my mouth, and a summer-house in the back-garden. Who knows? I shouldn’t be surprised for one.’\n\n‘You are quite a philosopher, Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘It runs in the family, I b’lieve, sir,’ replied Mr. Weller. ‘My father’s wery much in that line now. If my mother-in-law blows him up, he whistles. She flies in a passion, and breaks his pipe; he steps out, and gets another. Then she screams wery loud, and falls into ‘sterics; and he smokes wery comfortably till she comes to agin. That’s philosophy, Sir, ain’t it?’\n\n‘A very good substitute for it, at all events,’ replied Mr. Pickwick, laughing. ‘It must have been of great service to you, in the course of your rambling life, Sam.’\n\n‘Service, sir,’ exclaimed Sam. ‘You may say that. Arter I run away from the carrier, and afore I took up with the vaginer, I had unfurnished lodgin’s for a fortnight.’\n\n‘Unfurnished lodgings?’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Yes--the dry arches of Waterloo Bridge. Fine sleeping-place--vithin ten minutes’ walk of all the public offices--only if there is any objection to it, it is that the sitivation’s rayther too airy. I see some queer sights there.’\n\nAh, I suppose you did,’ said Mr. Pickwick, with an air of considerable interest.\n\n‘Sights, sir,’ resumed Mr. Weller, ‘as ‘ud penetrate your benevolent heart, and come out on the other side. You don’t see the reg’lar wagrants there; trust ‘em, they knows better than that. Young beggars, male and female, as hasn’t made a rise in their profession, takes up their quarters there sometimes; but it’s generally the worn-out, starving, houseless creeturs as roll themselves in the dark corners o’ them lonesome places--poor creeturs as ain’t up to the twopenny rope.’\n\n‘And pray, Sam, what is the twopenny rope?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘The twopenny rope, sir,’ replied Mr. Weller, ‘is just a cheap lodgin’ house, where the beds is twopence a night.’\n\n‘What do they call a bed a rope for?’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Bless your innocence, sir, that ain’t it,’ replied Sam. ‘Ven the lady and gen’l’m’n as keeps the hot-el first begun business, they used to make the beds on the floor; but this wouldn’t do at no price, ‘cos instead o’ taking a moderate twopenn’orth o’ sleep, the lodgers used to lie there half the day. So now they has two ropes, ‘bout six foot apart, and three from the floor, which goes right down the room; and the beds are made of slips of coarse sacking, stretched across ‘em.’\n\n‘Well,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Well,’ said Mr. Weller, ‘the adwantage o’ the plan’s hobvious. At six o’clock every mornin’ they let’s go the ropes at one end, and down falls the lodgers. Consequence is, that being thoroughly waked, they get up wery quietly, and walk away!’\n\n‘Beg your pardon, sir,’ said Sam, suddenly breaking off in his loquacious discourse. ‘Is this Bury St. Edmunds?’\n\n‘It is,’ replied Mr. Pickwick.\n\nThe coach rattled through the well-paved streets of a handsome little town, of thriving and cleanly appearance, and stopped before a large inn situated in a wide open street, nearly facing the old abbey.\n\n‘And this,’ said Mr. Pickwick, looking up. ‘Is the Angel! We alight here, Sam. But some caution is necessary. Order a private room, and do not mention my name. You understand.’\n\n‘Right as a trivet, sir,’ replied Mr. Weller, with a wink of intelligence; and having dragged Mr. Pickwick’s portmanteau from the hind boot, into which it had been hastily thrown when they joined the coach at Eatanswill, Mr. Weller disappeared on his errand. A private room was speedily engaged; and into it Mr. Pickwick was ushered without delay.\n\n‘Now, Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘the first thing to be done is to--’\n\nOrder dinner, Sir,’ interposed Mr. Weller. ‘It’s wery late, sir.’\n\n‘Ah, so it is,’ said Mr. Pickwick, looking at his watch. ‘You are right, Sam.’\n\n‘And if I might adwise, Sir,’ added Mr. Weller, ‘I’d just have a good night’s rest arterwards, and not begin inquiring arter this here deep ‘un till the mornin’. There’s nothin’ so refreshen’ as sleep, sir, as the servant girl said afore she drank the egg-cupful of laudanum.’\n\n‘I think you are right, Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick. ‘But I must first ascertain that he is in the house, and not likely to go away.’\n\n‘Leave that to me, Sir,’ said Sam. ‘Let me order you a snug little dinner, and make my inquiries below while it’s a-getting ready; I could worm ev’ry secret out O’ the boots’s heart, in five minutes, Sir.’\n\nDo so,’ said Mr. Pickwick; and Mr. Weller at once retired.\n\nIn half an hour, Mr. Pickwick was seated at a very satisfactory dinner; and in three-quarters Mr. Weller returned with the intelligence that Mr. Charles Fitz-Marshall had ordered his private room to be retained for him, until further notice. He was going to spend the evening at some private house in the neighbourhood, had ordered the boots to sit up until his return, and had taken his servant with him.\n\n‘Now, sir,’ argued Mr. Weller, when he had concluded his report, ‘if I can get a talk with this here servant in the mornin’, he’ll tell me all his master’s concerns.’\n\n‘How do you know that?’ interposed Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Bless your heart, sir, servants always do,’ replied Mr. Weller.\n\n‘Oh, ah, I forgot that,’ said Mr. Pickwick. ‘Well.’\n\n‘Then you can arrange what’s best to be done, sir, and we can act accordingly.’\n\nAs it appeared that this was the best arrangement that could be made, it was finally agreed upon. Mr. Weller, by his master’s permission, retired to spend the evening in his own way; and was shortly afterwards elected, by the unanimous voice of the assembled company, into the taproom chair, in which honourable post he acquitted himself so much to the satisfaction of the gentlemen-frequenters, that their roars of laughter and approbation penetrated to Mr. Pickwick’s bedroom, and shortened the term of his natural rest by at least three hours.\n\nEarly on the ensuing morning, Mr. Weller was dispelling all the feverish remains of the previous evening’s conviviality, through the instrumentality of a halfpenny shower-bath (having induced a young gentleman attached to the stable department, by the offer of that coin, to pump over his head and face, until he was perfectly restored), when he was attracted by the appearance of a young fellow in mulberry- coloured livery, who was sitting on a bench in the yard, reading what appeared to be a hymn-book, with an air of deep abstraction, but who occasionally stole a glance at the individual under the pump, as if he took some interest in his proceedings, nevertheless.\n\n‘You’re a rum ‘un to look at, you are!’ thought Mr. Weller, the first time his eyes encountered the glance of the stranger in the mulberry suit, who had a large, sallow, ugly face, very sunken eyes, and a gigantic head, from which depended a quantity of lank black hair. ‘You’re a rum ‘un!’ thought Mr. Weller; and thinking this, he went on washing himself, and thought no more about him.\n\nStill the man kept glancing from his hymn-book to Sam, and from Sam to his hymn-book, as if he wanted to open a conversation. So at last, Sam, by way of giving him an opportunity, said with a familiar nod--\n\n‘How are you, governor?’\n\n‘I am happy to say, I am pretty well, Sir,’ said the man, speaking with great deliberation, and closing the book. ‘I hope you are the same, Sir?’\n\n‘Why, if I felt less like a walking brandy-bottle I shouldn’t be quite so staggery this mornin’,’ replied Sam. ‘Are you stoppin’ in this house, old ‘un?’\n\nThe mulberry man replied in the affirmative.\n\n‘How was it you worn’t one of us, last night?’ inquired Sam, scrubbing his face with the towel. ‘You seem one of the jolly sort--looks as conwivial as a live trout in a lime basket,’ added Mr. Weller, in an undertone.\n\n‘I was out last night with my master,’ replied the stranger.\n\n‘What’s his name?’ inquired Mr. Weller, colouring up very red with sudden excitement, and the friction of the towel combined.\n\n‘Fitz-Marshall,’ said the mulberry man.\n\n‘Give us your hand,’ said Mr. Weller, advancing; ‘I should like to know you. I like your appearance, old fellow.’\n\n‘Well, that is very strange,’ said the mulberry man, with great simplicity of manner. ‘I like yours so much, that I wanted to speak to you, from the very first moment I saw you under the pump.’\n\nDid you though?’\n\n‘Upon my word. Now, isn’t that curious?’\n\n‘Wery sing’ler,’ said Sam, inwardly congratulating himself upon the softness of the stranger. ‘What’s your name, my patriarch?’\n\n‘Job.’\n\n‘And a wery good name it is; only one I know that ain’t got a nickname to it. What’s the other name?’\n\n‘Trotter,’ said the stranger. ‘What is yours?’\n\nSam bore in mind his master’s caution, and replied--\n\n‘My name’s Walker; my master’s name’s Wilkins. Will you take a drop o’ somethin’ this mornin’, Mr. Trotter?’\n\nMr. Trotter acquiesced in this agreeable proposal; and having deposited his book in his coat pocket, accompanied Mr. Weller to the tap, where they were soon occupied in discussing an exhilarating compound, formed by mixing together, in a pewter vessel, certain quantities of British Hollands and the fragrant essence of the clove.\n\n‘And what sort of a place have you got?’ inquired Sam, as he filled his companion’s glass, for the second time.\n\n‘Bad,’ said Job, smacking his lips, ‘very bad.’\n\n‘You don’t mean that?’ said Sam.\n\n‘I do, indeed. Worse than that, my master’s going to be married.’\n\n‘No.’\n\n‘Yes; and worse than that, too, he’s going to run away with an immense rich heiress, from boarding-school.’\n\n‘What a dragon!’ said Sam, refilling his companion’s glass. ‘It’s some boarding-school in this town, I suppose, ain’t it?’ Now, although this question was put in the most careless tone imaginable, Mr. Job Trotter plainly showed by gestures that he perceived his new friend’s anxiety to draw forth an answer to it. He emptied his glass, looked mysteriously at his companion, winked both of his small eyes, one after the other, and finally made a motion with his arm, as if he were working an imaginary pump-handle; thereby intimating that he (Mr. Trotter) considered himself as undergoing the process of being pumped by Mr. Samuel Weller.\n\n‘No, no,’ said Mr. Trotter, in conclusion, ‘that’s not to be told to everybody. That is a secret--a great secret, Mr. Walker.’ As the mulberry man said this, he turned his glass upside down, by way of reminding his companion that he had nothing left wherewith to slake his thirst. Sam observed the hint; and feeling the delicate manner in which it was conveyed, ordered the pewter vessel to be refilled, whereat the small eyes of the mulberry man glistened.\n\n‘And so it’s a secret?’ said Sam.\n\n‘I should rather suspect it was,’ said the mulberry man, sipping his liquor, with a complacent face.\n\n‘I suppose your mas’r’s wery rich?’ said Sam.\n\nMr. Trotter smiled, and holding his glass in his left hand, gave four distinct slaps on the pockets of his mulberry indescribables with his right, as if to intimate that his master might have done the same without alarming anybody much by the chinking of coin.\n\n‘Ah,’ said Sam, ‘that’s the game, is it?’\n\nThe mulberry man nodded significantly.\n\n‘Well, and don’t you think, old feller,’ remonstrated Mr. Weller, ‘that if you let your master take in this here young lady, you’re a precious rascal?’\n\n‘I know that,’ said Job Trotter, turning upon his companion a countenance of deep contrition, and groaning slightly, ‘I know that, and that’s what it is that preys upon my mind. But what am I to do?’\n\n‘Do!’ said Sam; ‘di-wulge to the missis, and give up your master.’\n\n‘Who’d believe me?’ replied Job Trotter. ‘The young lady’s considered the very picture of innocence and discretion. She’d deny it, and so would my master. Who’d believe me? I should lose my place, and get indicted for a conspiracy, or some such thing; that’s all I should take by my motion.’\n\n‘There’s somethin’ in that,’ said Sam, ruminating; ‘there’s somethin’ in that.’\n\n‘If I knew any respectable gentleman who would take the matter up,’ continued Mr. Trotter. ‘I might have some hope of preventing the elopement; but there’s the same difficulty, Mr. Walker, just the same. I know no gentleman in this strange place; and ten to one if I did, whether he would believe my story.’\n\n‘Come this way,’ said Sam, suddenly jumping up, and grasping the mulberry man by the arm. ‘My mas’r’s the man you want, I see.’ And after a slight resistance on the part of Job Trotter, Sam led his newly-found friend to the apartment of Mr. Pickwick, to whom he presented him, together with a brief summary of the dialogue we have just repeated.\n\n‘I am very sorry to betray my master, sir,’ said Job Trotter, applying to his eyes a pink checked pocket-handkerchief about six inches square.\n\n‘The feeling does you a great deal of honour,’ replied Mr. Pickwick; ‘but it is your duty, nevertheless.’\n\n‘I know it is my duty, Sir,’ replied Job, with great emotion. ‘We should all try to discharge our duty, Sir, and I humbly endeavour to discharge mine, Sir; but it is a hard trial to betray a master, Sir, whose clothes you wear, and whose bread you eat, even though he is a scoundrel, Sir.’\n\n‘You are a very good fellow,’ said Mr. Pickwick, much affected; ‘an honest fellow.’\n\n‘Come, come,’ interposed Sam, who had witnessed Mr. Trotter’s tears with considerable impatience, ‘blow this ‘ere water-cart bis’ness. It won’t do no good, this won’t.’\n\n‘Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick reproachfully. ‘I am sorry to find that you have so little respect for this young man’s feelings.’\n\n‘His feelin’s is all wery well, Sir,’ replied Mr. Weller; ‘and as they’re so wery fine, and it’s a pity he should lose ‘em, I think he’d better keep ‘em in his own buzzum, than let ‘em ewaporate in hot water, ‘specially as they do no good. Tears never yet wound up a clock, or worked a steam ingin’. The next time you go out to a smoking party, young fellow, fill your pipe with that ‘ere reflection; and for the present just put that bit of pink gingham into your pocket. ‘Tain’t so handsome that you need keep waving it about, as if you was a tight-rope dancer.’\n\n‘My man is in the right,’ said Mr. Pickwick, accosting Job, ‘although his mode of expressing his opinion is somewhat homely, and occasionally incomprehensible.’\n\n‘He is, sir, very right,’ said Mr. Trotter, ‘and I will give way no longer.’\n\nVery well,’ said Mr. Pickwick. ‘Now, where is this boarding-school?’\n\n‘It is a large, old, red brick house, just outside the town, Sir,’ replied Job Trotter.\n\n‘And when,’ said Mr. Pickwick--‘when is this villainous design to be carried into execution--when is this elopement to take place?’\n\n‘To-night, Sir,’ replied Job.\n\n‘To-night!’ exclaimed Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘This very night, sir,’ replied Job Trotter. ‘That is what alarms me so much.’\n\n‘Instant measures must be taken,’ said Mr. Pickwick. ‘I will see the lady who keeps the establishment immediately.’\n\n‘I beg your pardon, Sir,’ said Job, ‘but that course of proceeding will never do.’\n\n‘Why not?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘My master, sir, is a very artful man.’\n\n‘I know he is,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘And he has so wound himself round the old lady’s heart, Sir,’ resumed Job, ‘that she would believe nothing to his prejudice, if you went down on your bare knees, and swore it; especially as you have no proof but the word of a servant, who, for anything she knows (and my master would be sure to say so), was discharged for some fault, and does this in revenge.’\n\n‘What had better be done, then?’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Nothing but taking him in the very act of eloping, will convince the old lady, sir,’ replied Job.\n\n‘All them old cats will run their heads agin milestones,’ observed Mr. Weller, in a parenthesis.\n\n‘But this taking him in the very act of elopement, would be a very difficult thing to accomplish, I fear,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘I don’t know, sir,’ said Mr. Trotter, after a few moments’ reflection. ‘I think it might be very easily done.’\n\n‘How?’ was Mr. Pickwick’s inquiry.\n\n‘Why,’ replied Mr. Trotter, ‘my master and I, being in the confidence of the two servants, will be secreted in the kitchen at ten o’clock. When the family have retired to rest, we shall come out of the kitchen, and the young lady out of her bedroom. A post-chaise will be waiting, and away we go.’\n\n‘Well?’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Well, sir, I have been thinking that if you were in waiting in the garden behind, alone--’\n\n‘Alone,’ said Mr. Pickwick. ‘Why alone?’\n\n‘I thought it very natural,’ replied Job, ‘that the old lady wouldn’t like such an unpleasant discovery to be made before more persons than can possibly be helped. The young lady, too, sir--consider her feelings.’\n\n‘You are very right,’ said Mr. Pickwick. ‘The consideration evinces your delicacy of feeling. Go on; you are very right.’\n\n‘Well, sir, I have been thinking that if you were waiting in the back garden alone, and I was to let you in, at the door which opens into it, from the end of the passage, at exactly half-past eleven o’clock, you would be just in the very moment of time to assist me in frustrating the designs of this bad man, by whom I have been unfortunately ensnared.’ Here Mr. Trotter sighed deeply.\n\n‘Don’t distress yourself on that account,’ said Mr. Pickwick; ‘if he had one grain of the delicacy of feeling which distinguishes you, humble as your station is, I should have some hopes of him.’\n\nJob Trotter bowed low; and in spite of Mr. Weller’s previous remonstrance, the tears again rose to his eyes.\n\n‘I never see such a feller,’ said Sam, ‘Blessed if I don’t think he’s got a main in his head as is always turned on.’\n\n‘Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick, with great severity, ‘hold your tongue.’\n\n‘Wery well, sir,’ replied Mr. Weller.\n\n‘I don’t like this plan,’ said Mr. Pickwick, after deep meditation. ‘Why cannot I communicate with the young lady’s friends?’\n\n‘Because they live one hundred miles from here, sir,’ responded Job Trotter.\n\n‘That’s a clincher,’ said Mr. Weller, aside.\n\n‘Then this garden,’ resumed Mr. Pickwick. ‘How am I to get into it?’\n\n‘The wall is very low, sir, and your servant will give you a leg up.’\n\nMy servant will give me a leg up,’ repeated Mr. Pickwick mechanically. ‘You will be sure to be near this door that you speak of?’\n\n‘You cannot mistake it, Sir; it’s the only one that opens into the garden. Tap at it when you hear the clock strike, and I will open it instantly.’\n\n‘I don’t like the plan,’ said Mr. Pickwick; ‘but as I see no other, and as the happiness of this young lady’s whole life is at stake, I adopt it. I shall be sure to be there.’\n\nThus, for the second time, did Mr. Pickwick’s innate good-feeling involve him in an enterprise from which he would most willingly have stood aloof.\n\n‘What is the name of the house?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Westgate House, Sir. You turn a little to the right when you get to the end of the town; it stands by itself, some little distance off the high road, with the name on a brass plate on the gate.’\n\n‘I know it,’ said Mr. Pickwick. ‘I observed it once before, when I was in this town. You may depend upon me.’\n\nMr. Trotter made another bow, and turned to depart, when Mr. Pickwick thrust a guinea into his hand.\n\n‘You’re a fine fellow,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘and I admire your goodness of heart. No thanks. Remember--eleven o’clock.’\n\n‘There is no fear of my forgetting it, sir,’ replied Job Trotter. With these words he left the room, followed by Sam.\n\n‘I say,’ said the latter, ‘not a bad notion that ‘ere crying. I’d cry like a rain-water spout in a shower on such good terms. How do you do it?’\n\n‘It comes from the heart, Mr. Walker,’ replied Job solemnly. ‘Good- morning, sir.’\n\n‘You’re a soft customer, you are; we’ve got it all out o’ you, anyhow,’ thought Mr. Weller, as Job walked away.\n\nWe cannot state the precise nature of the thoughts which passed through Mr. Trotter’s mind, because we don’t know what they were.\n\nThe day wore on, evening came, and at a little before ten o’clock Sam Weller reported that Mr. Jingle and Job had gone out together, that their luggage was packed up, and that they had ordered a chaise. The plot was evidently in execution, as Mr. Trotter had foretold.\n\nHalf-past ten o’clock arrived, and it was time for Mr. Pickwick to issue forth on his delicate errand. Resisting Sam’s tender of his greatcoat, in order that he might have no encumbrance in scaling the wall, he set forth, followed by his attendant.\n\nThere was a bright moon, but it was behind the clouds. It was a fine dry night, but it was most uncommonly dark. Paths, hedges, fields, houses, and trees, were enveloped in one deep shade. The atmosphere was hot and sultry, the summer lightning quivered faintly on the verge of the horizon, and was the only sight that varied the dull gloom in which everything was wrapped--sound there was none, except the distant barking of some restless house-dog.\n\nThey found the house, read the brass plate, walked round the wall, and stopped at that portion of it which divided them from the bottom of the garden.\n\n‘You will return to the inn, Sam, when you have assisted me over,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Wery well, Sir.’\n\n‘And you will sit up, till I return.’\n\n‘Cert’nly, Sir.’\n\n‘Take hold of my leg; and, when I say “Over,” raise me gently.’\n\n‘All right, sir.’\n\nHaving settled these preliminaries, Mr. Pickwick grasped the top of the wall, and gave the word ‘Over,’ which was literally obeyed. Whether his body partook in some degree of the elasticity of his mind, or whether Mr. Weller’s notions of a gentle push were of a somewhat rougher description than Mr. Pickwick’s, the immediate effect of his assistance was to jerk that immortal gentleman completely over the wall on to the bed beneath, where, after crushing three gooseberry-bushes and a rose- tree, he finally alighted at full length.\n\n‘You ha’n’t hurt yourself, I hope, Sir?’ said Sam, in a loud whisper, as soon as he had recovered from the surprise consequent upon the mysterious disappearance of his master.\n\n‘I have not hurt myself, Sam, certainly,’ replied Mr. Pickwick, from the other side of the wall, ‘but I rather think that you have hurt me.’\n\n‘I hope not, Sir,’ said Sam.\n\n‘Never mind,’ said Mr. Pickwick, rising, ‘it’s nothing but a few scratches. Go away, or we shall be overheard.’\n\n‘Good-bye, Sir.’\n\n‘Good-bye.’\n\nWith stealthy steps Sam Weller departed, leaving Mr. Pickwick alone in the garden.\n\nLights occasionally appeared in the different windows of the house, or glanced from the staircases, as if the inmates were retiring to rest. Not caring to go too near the door, until the appointed time, Mr. Pickwick crouched into an angle of the wall, and awaited its arrival.\n\nIt was a situation which might well have depressed the spirits of many a man. Mr. Pickwick, however, felt neither depression nor misgiving. He knew that his purpose was in the main a good one, and he placed implicit reliance on the high-minded Job. It was dull, certainly; not to say dreary; but a contemplative man can always employ himself in meditation. Mr. Pickwick had meditated himself into a doze, when he was roused by the chimes of the neighbouring church ringing out the hour--half-past eleven.\n\n‘That’s the time,’ thought Mr. Pickwick, getting cautiously on his feet. He looked up at the house. The lights had disappeared, and the shutters were closed--all in bed, no doubt. He walked on tiptoe to the door, and gave a gentle tap. Two or three minutes passing without any reply, he gave another tap rather louder, and then another rather louder than that.\n\nAt length the sound of feet was audible upon the stairs, and then the light of a candle shone through the keyhole of the door. There was a good deal of unchaining and unbolting, and the door was slowly opened.\n\nNow the door opened outwards; and as the door opened wider and wider, Mr. Pickwick receded behind it, more and more. What was his astonishment when he just peeped out, by way of caution, to see that the person who had opened it was--not Job Trotter, but a servant-girl with a candle in her hand! Mr. Pickwick drew in his head again, with the swiftness displayed by that admirable melodramatic performer, Punch, when he lies in wait for the flat-headed comedian with the tin box of music.\n\n‘It must have been the cat, Sarah,’ said the girl, addressing herself to some one in the house. ‘Puss, puss, puss,--tit, tit, tit.’\n\nBut no animal being decoyed by these blandishments, the girl slowly closed the door, and re-fastened it; leaving Mr. Pickwick drawn up straight against the wall.\n\n‘This is very curious,’ thought Mr. Pickwick. ‘They are sitting up beyond their usual hour, I suppose. Extremely unfortunate, that they should have chosen this night, of all others, for such a purpose-- exceedingly.’ And with these thoughts, Mr. Pickwick cautiously retired to the angle of the wall in which he had been before ensconced; waiting until such time as he might deem it safe to repeat the signal.\n\nHe had not been here five minutes, when a vivid flash of lightning was followed by a loud peal of thunder that crashed and rolled away in the distance with a terrific noise--then came another flash of lightning, brighter than the other, and a second peal of thunder louder than the first; and then down came the rain, with a force and fury that swept everything before it.\n\nMr. Pickwick was perfectly aware that a tree is a very dangerous neighbour in a thunderstorm. He had a tree on his right, a tree on his left, a third before him, and a fourth behind. If he remained where he was, he might fall the victim of an accident; if he showed himself in the centre of the garden, he might be consigned to a constable. Once or twice he tried to scale the wall, but having no other legs this time, than those with which Nature had furnished him, the only effect of his struggles was to inflict a variety of very unpleasant gratings on his knees and shins, and to throw him into a state of the most profuse perspiration.\n\n‘What a dreadful situation,’ said Mr. Pickwick, pausing to wipe his brow after this exercise. He looked up at the house--all was dark. They must be gone to bed now. He would try the signal again.\n\nHe walked on tiptoe across the moist gravel, and tapped at the door. He held his breath, and listened at the key-hole. No reply: very odd. Another knock. He listened again. There was a low whispering inside, and then a voice cried--\n\n‘Who’s there?’\n\n‘That’s not Job,’ thought Mr. Pickwick, hastily drawing himself straight up against the wall again. ‘It’s a woman.’\n\nHe had scarcely had time to form this conclusion, when a window above stairs was thrown up, and three or four female voices repeated the query--‘Who’s there?’\n\nMr. Pickwick dared not move hand or foot. It was clear that the whole establishment was roused. He made up his mind to remain where he was, until the alarm had subsided; and then by a supernatural effort, to get over the wall, or perish in the attempt.\n\nLike all Mr. Pickwick’s determinations, this was the best that could be made under the circumstances; but, unfortunately, it was founded upon the assumption that they would not venture to open the door again. What was his discomfiture, when he heard the chain and bolts withdrawn, and saw the door slowly opening, wider and wider! He retreated into the corner, step by step; but do what he would, the interposition of his own person, prevented its being opened to its utmost width.\n\n‘Who’s there?’ screamed a numerous chorus of treble voices from the staircase inside, consisting of the spinster lady of the establishment, three teachers, five female servants, and thirty boarders, all half- dressed and in a forest of curl-papers.\n\nOf course Mr. Pickwick didn’t say who was there: and then the burden of the chorus changed into--‘Lor! I am so frightened.’\n\n‘Cook,’ said the lady abbess, who took care to be on the top stair, the very last of the group--‘cook, why don’t you go a little way into the garden?’\n\nPlease, ma’am, I don’t like,’ responded the cook.\n\n‘Lor, what a stupid thing that cook is!’ said the thirty boarders.\n\n‘Cook,’ said the lady abbess, with great dignity; ‘don’t answer me, if you please. I insist upon your looking into the garden immediately.’\n\nHere the cook began to cry, and the housemaid said it was ‘a shame!’ for which partisanship she received a month’s warning on the spot.\n\n‘Do you hear, cook?’ said the lady abbess, stamping her foot impatiently.\n\n‘Don’t you hear your missis, cook?’ said the three teachers.\n\n‘What an impudent thing that cook is!’ said the thirty boarders.\n\nThe unfortunate cook, thus strongly urged, advanced a step or two, and holding her candle just where it prevented her from seeing at all, declared there was nothing there, and it must have been the wind. The door was just going to be closed in consequence, when an inquisitive boarder, who had been peeping between the hinges, set up a fearful screaming, which called back the cook and housemaid, and all the more adventurous, in no time.\n\n‘What is the matter with Miss Smithers?’ said the lady abbess, as the aforesaid Miss Smithers proceeded to go into hysterics of four young lady power.\n\n‘Lor, Miss Smithers, dear,’ said the other nine-and-twenty boarders.\n\n‘Oh, the man--the man--behind the door!’ screamed Miss Smithers.\n\nThe lady abbess no sooner heard this appalling cry, than she retreated to her own bedroom, double-locked the door, and fainted away comfortably. The boarders, and the teachers, and the servants, fell back upon the stairs, and upon each other; and never was such a screaming, and fainting, and struggling beheld. In the midst of the tumult, Mr. Pickwick emerged from his concealment, and presented himself amongst them.\n\n‘Ladies--dear ladies,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Oh. he says we’re dear,’ cried the oldest and ugliest teacher. ‘Oh, the wretch!’\n\n‘Ladies,’ roared Mr. Pickwick, rendered desperate by the danger of his situation. ‘Hear me. I am no robber. I want the lady of the house.’\n\n‘Oh, what a ferocious monster!’ screamed another teacher. ‘He wants Miss Tomkins.’\n\nHere there was a general scream.\n\n‘Ring the alarm bell, somebody!’ cried a dozen voices.\n\n‘Don’t--don’t,’ shouted Mr. Pickwick. ‘Look at me. Do I look like a robber! My dear ladies--you may bind me hand and leg, or lock me up in a closet, if you like. Only hear what I have got to say--only hear me.’\n\n‘How did you come in our garden?’ faltered the housemaid.\n\n‘Call the lady of the house, and I’ll tell her everything,’ said Mr. Pickwick, exerting his lungs to the utmost pitch. ‘Call her--only be quiet, and call her, and you shall hear everything.’\n\nIt might have been Mr. Pickwick’s appearance, or it might have been his manner, or it might have been the temptation--irresistible to a female mind--of hearing something at present enveloped in mystery, that reduced the more reasonable portion of the establishment (some four individuals) to a state of comparative quiet. By them it was proposed, as a test of Mr. Pickwick’s sincerity, that he should immediately submit to personal restraint; and that gentleman having consented to hold a conference with Miss Tomkins, from the interior of a closet in which the day boarders hung their bonnets and sandwich-bags, he at once stepped into it, of his own accord, and was securely locked in. This revived the others; and Miss Tomkins having been brought to, and brought down, the conference began.\n\n‘What did you do in my garden, man?’ said Miss Tomkins, in a faint voice.\n\n‘I came to warn you that one of your young ladies was going to elope to- night,’ replied Mr. Pickwick, from the interior of the closet.\n\n‘Elope!’ exclaimed Miss Tomkins, the three teachers, the thirty boarders, and the five servants. ‘Who with?’\n\nYour friend, Mr. Charles Fitz-Marshall.’\n\n‘My friend! I don’t know any such person.’\n\n‘Well, Mr. Jingle, then.’\n\n‘I never heard the name in my life.’\n\n‘Then, I have been deceived, and deluded,’ said Mr. Pickwick. ‘I have been the victim of a conspiracy--a foul and base conspiracy. Send to the Angel, my dear ma’am, if you don’t believe me. Send to the Angel for Mr. Pickwick’s manservant, I implore you, ma’am.’\n\n‘He must be respectable--he keeps a manservant,’ said Miss Tomkins to the writing and ciphering governess.\n\n‘It’s my opinion, Miss Tomkins,’ said the writing and ciphering governess, ‘that his manservant keeps him, I think he’s a madman, Miss Tomkins, and the other’s his keeper.’\n\n‘I think you are very right, Miss Gwynn,’ responded Miss Tomkins. ‘Let two of the servants repair to the Angel, and let the others remain here, to protect us.’\n\nSo two of the servants were despatched to the Angel in search of Mr. Samuel Weller; and the remaining three stopped behind to protect Miss Tomkins, and the three teachers, and the thirty boarders. And Mr. Pickwick sat down in the closet, beneath a grove of sandwich-bags, and awaited the return of the messengers, with all the philosophy and fortitude he could summon to his aid.\n\nAn hour and a half elapsed before they came back, and when they did come, Mr. Pickwick recognised, in addition to the voice of Mr. Samuel Weller, two other voices, the tones of which struck familiarly on his ear; but whose they were, he could not for the life of him call to mind.\n\nA very brief conversation ensued. The door was unlocked. Mr. Pickwick stepped out of the closet, and found himself in the presence of the whole establishment of Westgate House, Mr Samuel Weller, and--old Wardle, and his destined son-in-law, Mr. Trundle!\n\n‘My dear friend,’ said Mr. Pickwick, running forward and grasping Wardle’s hand, ‘my dear friend, pray, for Heaven’s sake, explain to this lady the unfortunate and dreadful situation in which I am placed. You must have heard it from my servant; say, at all events, my dear fellow, that I am neither a robber nor a madman.’\n\n‘I have said so, my dear friend. I have said so already,’ replied Mr. Wardle, shaking the right hand of his friend, while Mr. Trundle shook the left.\n\n‘And whoever says, or has said, he is,’ interposed Mr. Weller, stepping forward, ‘says that which is not the truth, but so far from it, on the contrary, quite the rewerse. And if there’s any number o’ men on these here premises as has said so, I shall be wery happy to give ‘em all a wery convincing proof o’ their being mistaken, in this here wery room, if these wery respectable ladies ‘ll have the goodness to retire, and order ‘em up, one at a time.’ Having delivered this defiance with great volubility, Mr. Weller struck his open palm emphatically with his clenched fist, and winked pleasantly on Miss Tomkins, the intensity of whose horror at his supposing it within the bounds of possibility that there could be any men on the premises of Westgate House Establishment for Young Ladies, it is impossible to describe.\n\nMr. Pickwick’s explanation having already been partially made, was soon concluded. But neither in the course of his walk home with his friends, nor afterwards when seated before a blazing fire at the supper he so much needed, could a single observation be drawn from him. He seemed bewildered and amazed. Once, and only once, he turned round to Mr. Wardle, and said--\n\n‘How did you come here?’\n\n‘Trundle and I came down here, for some good shooting on the first,’ replied Wardle. ‘We arrived to-night, and were astonished to hear from your servant that you were here too. But I am glad you are,’ said the old fellow, slapping him on the back--‘I am glad you are. We shall have a jovial party on the first, and we’ll give Winkle another chance--eh, old boy?’\n\nMr. Pickwick made no reply, he did not even ask after his friends at Dingley Dell, and shortly afterwards retired for the night, desiring Sam to fetch his candle when he rung.\n\nThe bell did ring in due course, and Mr. Weller presented himself.\n\n‘Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick, looking out from under the bed-clothes.\n\n‘Sir,’ said Mr. Weller.\n\nMr. Pickwick paused, and Mr. Weller snuffed the candle.\n\n‘Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick again, as if with a desperate effort.\n\n‘Sir,’ said Mr. Weller, once more.\n\n‘Where is that Trotter?’\n\n‘Job, sir?’\n‘Yes.\n\n‘Gone, sir.’\n\n‘With his master, I suppose?’\n\n‘Friend or master, or whatever he is, he’s gone with him,’ replied Mr. Weller. ‘There’s a pair on ‘em, sir.’\n\n‘Jingle suspected my design, and set that fellow on you, with this story, I suppose?’ said Mr. Pickwick, half choking.\n\n‘Just that, sir,’ replied Mr. Weller.\n\n‘It was all false, of course?’\n\n‘All, sir,’ replied Mr. Weller. ‘Reg’lar do, sir; artful dodge.’\n\n‘I don’t think he’ll escape us quite so easily the next time, Sam!’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘I don’t think he will, Sir.’\n\n‘Whenever I meet that Jingle again, wherever it is,’ said Mr. Pickwick, raising himself in bed, and indenting his pillow with a tremendous blow, ‘I’ll inflict personal chastisement on him, in addition to the exposure he so richly merits. I will, or my name is not Pickwick.’\n\n‘And venever I catches hold o’ that there melan-cholly chap with the black hair,’ said Sam, ‘if I don’t bring some real water into his eyes, for once in a way, my name ain’t Weller. Good-night, Sir!’\n\nCHAPTER XVII. SHOWING THAT AN ATTACK OF RHEUMATISM, IN SOME CASES, ACTS AS A QUICKENER TO INVENTIVE GENIUS\nThe constitution of Mr. Pickwick, though able to sustain a very considerable amount of exertion and fatigue, was not proof against such a combination of attacks as he had undergone on the memorable night, recorded in the last chapter. The process of being washed in the night air, and rough-dried in a closet, is as dangerous as it is peculiar. Mr. Pickwick was laid up with an attack of rheumatism.\n\nBut although the bodily powers of the great man were thus impaired, his mental energies retained their pristine vigour. His spirits were elastic; his good-humour was restored. Even the vexation consequent upon his recent adventure had vanished from his mind; and he could join in the hearty laughter, which any allusion to it excited in Mr. Wardle, without anger and without embarrassment. Nay, more. During the two days Mr. Pickwick was confined to bed, Sam was his constant attendant. On the first, he endeavoured to amuse his master by anecdote and conversation; on the second, Mr. Pickwick demanded his writing-desk, and pen and ink, and was deeply engaged during the whole day. On the third, being able to sit up in his bedchamber, he despatched his valet with a message to Mr. Wardle and Mr. Trundle, intimating that if they would take their wine there, that evening, they would greatly oblige him. The invitation was most willingly accepted; and when they were seated over their wine, Mr. Pickwick, with sundry blushes, produced the following little tale, as having been ‘edited’ by himself, during his recent indisposition, from his notes of Mr. Weller’s unsophisticated recital.\n\n# THE PARISH CLERK A TALE OF TRUE LOVE\n\n‘Once upon a time, in a very small country town, at a considerable distance from London, there lived a little man named Nathaniel Pipkin, who was the parish clerk of the little town, and lived in a little house in the little High Street, within ten minutes’ walk from the little church; and who was to be found every day, from nine till four, teaching a little learning to the little boys. Nathaniel Pipkin was a harmless, inoffensive, good-natured being, with a turned-up nose, and rather turned-in legs, a cast in his eye, and a halt in his gait; and he divided his time between the church and his school, verily believing that there existed not, on the face of the earth, so clever a man as the curate, so imposing an apartment as the vestry-room, or so well-ordered a seminary as his own. Once, and only once, in his life, Nathaniel Pipkin had seen a bishop--a real bishop, with his arms in lawn sleeves, and his head in a wig. He had seen him walk, and heard him talk, at a confirmation, on which momentous occasion Nathaniel Pipkin was so overcome with reverence and awe, when the aforesaid bishop laid his hand on his head, that he fainted right clean away, and was borne out of church in the arms of the beadle.\n\n‘This was a great event, a tremendous era, in Nathaniel Pipkin’s life, and it was the only one that had ever occurred to ruffle the smooth current of his quiet existence, when happening one fine afternoon, in a fit of mental abstraction, to raise his eyes from the slate on which he was devising some tremendous problem in compound addition for an offending urchin to solve, they suddenly rested on the blooming countenance of Maria Lobbs, the only daughter of old Lobbs, the great saddler over the way. Now, the eyes of Mr. Pipkin had rested on the pretty face of Maria Lobbs many a time and oft before, at church and elsewhere; but the eyes of Maria Lobbs had never looked so bright, the cheeks of Maria Lobbs had never looked so ruddy, as upon this particular occasion. No wonder then, that Nathaniel Pipkin was unable to take his eyes from the countenance of Miss Lobbs; no wonder that Miss Lobbs, finding herself stared at by a young man, withdrew her head from the window out of which she had been peeping, and shut the casement and pulled down the blind; no wonder that Nathaniel Pipkin, immediately thereafter, fell upon the young urchin who had previously offended, and cuffed and knocked him about to his heart’s content. All this was very natural, and there’s nothing at all to wonder at about it.\n\n‘It is matter of wonder, though, that anyone of Mr. Nathaniel Pipkin’s retiring disposition, nervous temperament, and most particularly diminutive income, should from this day forth, have dared to aspire to the hand and heart of the only daughter of the fiery old Lobbs--of old Lobbs, the great saddler, who could have bought up the whole village at one stroke of his pen, and never felt the outlay--old Lobbs, who was well known to have heaps of money, invested in the bank at the nearest market town--who was reported to have countless and inexhaustible treasures hoarded up in the little iron safe with the big keyhole, over the chimney-piece in the back parlour--and who, it was well known, on festive occasions garnished his board with a real silver teapot, cream- ewer, and sugar-basin, which he was wont, in the pride of his heart, to boast should be his daughter’s property when she found a man to her mind. I repeat it, to be matter of profound astonishment and intense wonder, that Nathaniel Pipkin should have had the temerity to cast his eyes in this direction. But love is blind; and Nathaniel had a cast in his eye; and perhaps these two circumstances, taken together, prevented his seeing the matter in its proper light.\n\n‘Now, if old Lobbs had entertained the most remote or distant idea of the state of the affections of Nathaniel Pipkin, he would just have razed the school-room to the ground, or exterminated its master from the surface of the earth, or committed some other outrage and atrocity of an equally ferocious and violent description; for he was a terrible old fellow, was Lobbs, when his pride was injured, or his blood was up. Swear! Such trains of oaths would come rolling and pealing over the way, sometimes, when he was denouncing the idleness of the bony apprentice with the thin legs, that Nathaniel Pipkin would shake in his shoes with horror, and the hair of the pupils’ heads would stand on end with fright.\n\n‘Well! Day after day, when school was over, and the pupils gone, did Nathaniel Pipkin sit himself down at the front window, and, while he feigned to be reading a book, throw sidelong glances over the way in search of the bright eyes of Maria Lobbs; and he hadn’t sat there many days, before the bright eyes appeared at an upper window, apparently deeply engaged in reading too. This was delightful, and gladdening to the heart of Nathaniel Pipkin. It was something to sit there for hours together, and look upon that pretty face when the eyes were cast down; but when Maria Lobbs began to raise her eyes from her book, and dart their rays in the direction of Nathaniel Pipkin, his delight and admiration were perfectly boundless. At last, one day when he knew old Lobbs was out, Nathaniel Pipkin had the temerity to kiss his hand to Maria Lobbs; and Maria Lobbs, instead of shutting the window, and pulling down the blind, kissed hers to him, and smiled. Upon which Nathaniel Pipkin determined, that, come what might, he would develop the state of his feelings, without further delay.\n\n‘A prettier foot, a gayer heart, a more dimpled face, or a smarter form, never bounded so lightly over the earth they graced, as did those of Maria Lobbs, the old saddler’s daughter. There was a roguish twinkle in her sparkling eyes, that would have made its way to far less susceptible bosoms than that of Nathaniel Pipkin; and there was such a joyous sound in her merry laugh, that the sternest misanthrope must have smiled to hear it. Even old Lobbs himself, in the very height of his ferocity, couldn’t resist the coaxing of his pretty daughter; and when she, and her cousin Kate--an arch, impudent-looking, bewitching little person-- made a dead set upon the old man together, as, to say the truth, they very often did, he could have refused them nothing, even had they asked for a portion of the countless and inexhaustible treasures, which were hidden from the light, in the iron safe.\n\n‘Nathaniel Pipkin’s heart beat high within him, when he saw this enticing little couple some hundred yards before him one summer’s evening, in the very field in which he had many a time strolled about till night-time, and pondered on the beauty of Maria Lobbs. But though he had often thought then, how briskly he would walk up to Maria Lobbs and tell her of his passion if he could only meet her, he felt, now that she was unexpectedly before him, all the blood in his body mounting to his face, manifestly to the great detriment of his legs, which, deprived of their usual portion, trembled beneath him. When they stopped to gather a hedge flower, or listen to a bird, Nathaniel Pipkin stopped too, and pretended to be absorbed in meditation, as indeed he really was; for he was thinking what on earth he should ever do, when they turned back, as they inevitably must in time, and meet him face to face. But though he was afraid to make up to them, he couldn’t bear to lose sight of them; so when they walked faster he walked faster, when they lingered he lingered, and when they stopped he stopped; and so they might have gone on, until the darkness prevented them, if Kate had not looked slyly back, and encouragingly beckoned Nathaniel to advance. There was something in Kate’s manner that was not to be resisted, and so Nathaniel Pipkin complied with the invitation; and after a great deal of blushing on his part, and immoderate laughter on that of the wicked little cousin, Nathaniel Pipkin went down on his knees on the dewy grass, and declared his resolution to remain there for ever, unless he were permitted to rise the accepted lover of Maria Lobbs. Upon this, the merry laughter of Miss Lobbs rang through the calm evening air--without seeming to disturb it, though; it had such a pleasant sound--and the wicked little cousin laughed more immoderately than before, and Nathaniel Pipkin blushed deeper than ever. At length, Maria Lobbs being more strenuously urged by the love-worn little man, turned away her head, and whispered her cousin to say, or at all events Kate did say, that she felt much honoured by Mr. Pipkin’s addresses; that her hand and heart were at her father’s disposal; but that nobody could be insensible to Mr. Pipkin’s merits. As all this was said with much gravity, and as Nathaniel Pipkin walked home with Maria Lobbs, and struggled for a kiss at parting, he went to bed a happy man, and dreamed all night long, of softening old Lobbs, opening the strong box, and marrying Maria.\n\nThe next day, Nathaniel Pipkin saw old Lobbs go out upon his old gray pony, and after a great many signs at the window from the wicked little cousin, the object and meaning of which he could by no means understand, the bony apprentice with the thin legs came over to say that his master wasn’t coming home all night, and that the ladies expected Mr. Pipkin to tea, at six o’clock precisely. How the lessons were got through that day, neither Nathaniel Pipkin nor his pupils knew any more than you do; but they were got through somehow, and, after the boys had gone, Nathaniel Pipkin took till full six o’clock to dress himself to his satisfaction. Not that it took long to select the garments he should wear, inasmuch as he had no choice about the matter; but the putting of them on to the best advantage, and the touching of them up previously, was a task of no inconsiderable difficulty or importance.\n\n‘There was a very snug little party, consisting of Maria Lobbs and her cousin Kate, and three or four romping, good-humoured, rosy-cheeked girls. Nathaniel Pipkin had ocular demonstration of the fact, that the rumours of old Lobbs’s treasures were not exaggerated. There were the real solid silver teapot, cream-ewer, and sugar-basin, on the table, and real silver spoons to stir the tea with, and real china cups to drink it out of, and plates of the same, to hold the cakes and toast in. The only eye-sore in the whole place was another cousin of Maria Lobbs’s, and a brother of Kate, whom Maria Lobbs called “Henry,” and who seemed to keep Maria Lobbs all to himself, up in one corner of the table. It’s a delightful thing to see affection in families, but it may be carried rather too far, and Nathaniel Pipkin could not help thinking that Maria Lobbs must be very particularly fond of her relations, if she paid as much attention to all of them as to this individual cousin. After tea, too, when the wicked little cousin proposed a game at blind man’s buff, it somehow or other happened that Nathaniel Pipkin was nearly always blind, and whenever he laid his hand upon the male cousin, he was sure to find that Maria Lobbs was not far off. And though the wicked little cousin and the other girls pinched him, and pulled his hair, and pushed chairs in his way, and all sorts of things, Maria Lobbs never seemed to come near him at all; and once--once--Nathaniel Pipkin could have sworn he heard the sound of a kiss, followed by a faint remonstrance from Maria Lobbs, and a half-suppressed laugh from her female friends. All this was odd--very odd--and there is no saying what Nathaniel Pipkin might or might not have done, in consequence, if his thoughts had not been suddenly directed into a new channel.\n\n‘The circumstance which directed his thoughts into a new channel was a loud knocking at the street door, and the person who made this loud knocking at the street door was no other than old Lobbs himself, who had unexpectedly returned, and was hammering away, like a coffin-maker; for he wanted his supper. The alarming intelligence was no sooner communicated by the bony apprentice with the thin legs, than the girls tripped upstairs to Maria Lobbs’s bedroom, and the male cousin and Nathaniel Pipkin were thrust into a couple of closets in the sitting- room, for want of any better places of concealment; and when Maria Lobbs and the wicked little cousin had stowed them away, and put the room to rights, they opened the street door to old Lobbs, who had never left off knocking since he first began.\n\n‘Now it did unfortunately happen that old Lobbs being very hungry was monstrous cross. Nathaniel Pipkin could hear him growling away like an old mastiff with a sore throat; and whenever the unfortunate apprentice with the thin legs came into the room, so surely did old Lobbs commence swearing at him in a most Saracenic and ferocious manner, though apparently with no other end or object than that of easing his bosom by the discharge of a few superfluous oaths. At length some supper, which had been warming up, was placed on the table, and then old Lobbs fell to, in regular style; and having made clear work of it in no time, kissed his daughter, and demanded his pipe.\n\n‘Nature had placed Nathaniel Pipkin’s knees in very close juxtaposition, but when he heard old Lobbs demand his pipe, they knocked together, as if they were going to reduce each other to powder; for, depending from a couple of hooks, in the very closet in which he stood, was a large, brown-stemmed, silver-bowled pipe, which pipe he himself had seen in the mouth of old Lobbs, regularly every afternoon and evening, for the last five years. The two girls went downstairs for the pipe, and upstairs for the pipe, and everywhere but where they knew the pipe was, and old Lobbs stormed away meanwhile, in the most wonderful manner. At last he thought of the closet, and walked up to it. It was of no use a little man like Nathaniel Pipkin pulling the door inwards, when a great strong fellow like old Lobbs was pulling it outwards. Old Lobbs gave it one tug, and open it flew, disclosing Nathaniel Pipkin standing bolt upright inside, and shaking with apprehension from head to foot. Bless us! what an appalling look old Lobbs gave him, as he dragged him out by the collar, and held him at arm’s length.\n\n‘“Why, what the devil do you want here?” said old Lobbs, in a fearful voice.\n\n‘Nathaniel Pipkin could make no reply, so old Lobbs shook him backwards and forwards, for two or three minutes, by way of arranging his ideas for him.\n\n‘“What do you want here?” roared Lobbs; “I suppose you have come after my daughter, now!”\n\n‘Old Lobbs merely said this as a sneer: for he did not believe that mortal presumption could have carried Nathaniel Pipkin so far. What was his indignation, when that poor man replied--\n\n‘“Yes, I did, Mr. Lobbs, I did come after your daughter. I love her, Mr. Lobbs.”\n\n‘“Why, you snivelling, wry-faced, puny villain,” gasped old Lobbs, paralysed by the atrocious confession; “what do you mean by that? Say this to my face! Damme, I’ll throttle you!”\n\n‘It is by no means improbable that old Lobbs would have carried his threat into execution, in the excess of his rage, if his arm had not been stayed by a very unexpected apparition: to wit, the male cousin, who, stepping out of his closet, and walking up to old Lobbs, said--\n\n‘“I cannot allow this harmless person, Sir, who has been asked here, in some girlish frolic, to take upon himself, in a very noble manner, the fault (if fault it is) which I am guilty of, and am ready to avow. I love your daughter, sir; and I came here for the purpose of meeting her.”\n\n‘Old Lobbs opened his eyes very wide at this, but not wider than Nathaniel Pipkin.\n\n‘“You did?” said Lobbs, at last finding breath to speak.\n\n‘“I did.”\n\n‘“And I forbade you this house, long ago.”\n\n‘“You did, or I should not have been here, clandestinely, to-night.”\n\n‘I am sorry to record it of old Lobbs, but I think he would have struck the cousin, if his pretty daughter, with her bright eyes swimming in tears, had not clung to his arm.\n\n‘“Don’t stop him, Maria,” said the young man; “if he has the will to strike me, let him. I would not hurt a hair of his gray head, for the riches of the world.”\n\n‘The old man cast down his eyes at this reproof, and they met those of his daughter. I have hinted once or twice before, that they were very bright eyes, and, though they were tearful now, their influence was by no means lessened. Old Lobbs turned his head away, as if to avoid being persuaded by them, when, as fortune would have it, he encountered the face of the wicked little cousin, who, half afraid for her brother, and half laughing at Nathaniel Pipkin, presented as bewitching an expression of countenance, with a touch of slyness in it, too, as any man, old or young, need look upon. She drew her arm coaxingly through the old man’s, and whispered something in his ear; and do what he would, old Lobbs couldn’t help breaking out into a smile, while a tear stole down his cheek at the same time.\n\n‘Five minutes after this, the girls were brought down from the bedroom with a great deal of giggling and modesty; and while the young people were making themselves perfectly happy, old Lobbs got down the pipe, and smoked it; and it was a remarkable circumstance about that particular pipe of tobacco, that it was the most soothing and delightful one he ever smoked.\n\n‘Nathaniel Pipkin thought it best to keep his own counsel, and by so doing gradually rose into high favour with old Lobbs, who taught him to smoke in time; and they used to sit out in the garden on the fine evenings, for many years afterwards, smoking and drinking in great state. He soon recovered the effects of his attachment, for we find his name in the parish register, as a witness to the marriage of Maria Lobbs to her cousin; and it also appears, by reference to other documents, that on the night of the wedding he was incarcerated in the village cage, for having, in a state of extreme intoxication, committed sundry excesses in the streets, in all of which he was aided and abetted by the bony apprentice with the thin legs.’\n\nCHAPTER XVIII. BRIEFLY ILLUSTRATIVE OF TWO POINTS; FIRST, THE POWER OF HYSTERICS, AND, SECONDLY, THE FORCE OF CIRCUMSTANCES\nFor two days after the dejeune at Mrs. Hunter’s, the Pickwickians remained at Eatanswill, anxiously awaiting the arrival of some intelligence from their revered leader. Mr. Tupman and Mr. Snodgrass were once again left to their own means of amusement; for Mr. Winkle, in compliance with a most pressing invitation, continued to reside at Mr. Pott’s house, and to devote his time to the companionship of his amiable lady. Nor was the occasional society of Mr. Pott himself wanting to complete their felicity. Deeply immersed in the intensity of his speculations for the public weal and the destruction of the Independent, it was not the habit of that great man to descend from his mental pinnacle to the humble level of ordinary minds. On this occasion, however, and as if expressly in compliment to any follower of Mr. Pickwick’s, he unbent, relaxed, stepped down from his pedestal, and walked upon the ground, benignly adapting his remarks to the comprehension of the herd, and seeming in outward form, if not in spirit, to be one of them.\n\nSuch having been the demeanour of this celebrated public character towards Mr. Winkle, it will be readily imagined that considerable surprise was depicted on the countenance of the latter gentleman, when, as he was sitting alone in the breakfast-room, the door was hastily thrown open, and as hastily closed, on the entrance of Mr. Pott, who, stalking majestically towards him, and thrusting aside his proffered hand, ground his teeth, as if to put a sharper edge on what he was about to utter, and exclaimed, in a saw-like voice--\n\n‘Serpent!’\n\n‘Sir!’ exclaimed Mr. Winkle, starting from his chair.\n\n‘Serpent, Sir,’ repeated Mr. Pott, raising his voice, and then suddenly depressing it: ‘I said, serpent, sir--make the most of it.’\n\nWhen you have parted with a man at two o’clock in the morning, on terms of the utmost good-fellowship, and he meets you again, at half-past nine, and greets you as a serpent, it is not unreasonable to conclude that something of an unpleasant nature has occurred meanwhile. So Mr. Winkle thought. He returned Mr. Pott’s gaze of stone, and in compliance with that gentleman’s request, proceeded to make the most he could of the ‘serpent.’ The most, however, was nothing at all; so, after a profound silence of some minutes’ duration, he said,--\n\n‘Serpent, Sir! Serpent, Mr. Pott! What can you mean, Sir?--this is pleasantry.’\n\n‘Pleasantry, sir!’ exclaimed Pott, with a motion of the hand, indicative of a strong desire to hurl the Britannia metal teapot at the head of the visitor. ‘Pleasantry, sir!--But--no, I will be calm; I will be calm, Sir;’ in proof of his calmness, Mr. Pott flung himself into a chair, and foamed at the mouth.\n\n‘My dear sir,’ interposed Mr. Winkle.\n\n‘DEAR Sir!’ replied Pott. ‘How dare you address me, as dear Sir, Sir? How dare you look me in the face and do it, sir?’\n\n‘Well, Sir, if you come to that,’ responded Mr. Winkle, ‘how dare you look me in the face, and call me a serpent, sir?’\n\n‘Because you are one,’ replied Mr. Pott.\n\n‘Prove it, Sir,’ said Mr. Winkle warmly. ‘Prove it.’\n\nA malignant scowl passed over the profound face of the editor, as he drew from his pocket the Independent of that morning; and laying his finger on a particular paragraph, threw the journal across the table to Mr. Winkle.\n\nThat gentleman took it up, and read as follows:--\n\n‘Our obscure and filthy contemporary, in some disgusting observations on the recent election for this borough, has presumed to violate the hallowed sanctity of private life, and to refer in a manner not to be misunderstood, to the personal affairs of our late candidate--aye, and notwithstanding his base defeat, we will add, our future member, Mr. Fizkin. What does our dastardly contemporary mean? What would the ruffian say, if we, setting at naught, like him, the decencies of social intercourse, were to raise the curtain which happily conceals His private life from general ridicule, not to say from general execration? What, if we were even to point out, and comment on, facts and circumstances, which are publicly notorious, and beheld by every one but our mole-eyed contemporary--what if we were to print the following effusion, which we received while we were writing the commencement of this article, from a talented fellow-townsman and correspondent?\n\n‘“Oh Pott! if you’d known How false she’d have grown, When you heard the marriage bells tinkle; You’d have done then, I vow, What you cannot help now,\n\n‘What,’ said Mr. Pott solemnly--‘what rhymes to “tinkle,” villain?’\n\n‘What rhymes to tinkle?’ said Mrs. Pott, whose entrance at the moment forestalled the reply. ‘What rhymes to tinkle? Why, Winkle, I should conceive.’ Saying this, Mrs. Pott smiled sweetly on the disturbed Pickwickian, and extended her hand towards him. The agitated young man would have accepted it, in his confusion, had not Pott indignantly interposed.\n\n‘Back, ma’am--back!’ said the editor. ‘Take his hand before my very face!’\n\n‘Mr. P.!’ said his astonished lady.\n\n‘Wretched woman, look here,’ exclaimed the husband. ‘Look here, ma’am-- “Lines to a Brass Pot.” “Brass Pot”; that’s me, ma’am. “False she’d have grown”; that’s you, ma’am--you.’ With this ebullition of rage, which was not unaccompanied with something like a tremble, at the expression of his wife’s face, Mr. Pott dashed the current number of the Eatanswill Independent at her feet.\n\n‘Upon my word, Sir,’ said the astonished Mrs. Pott, stooping to pick up the paper. ‘Upon my word, Sir!’\n\nMr. Pott winced beneath the contemptuous gaze of his wife. He had made a desperate struggle to screw up his courage, but it was fast coming unscrewed again.\n\nThere appears nothing very tremendous in this little sentence, ‘Upon my word, sir,’ when it comes to be read; but the tone of voice in which it was delivered, and the look that accompanied it, both seeming to bear reference to some revenge to be thereafter visited upon the head of Pott, produced their effect upon him. The most unskilful observer could have detected in his troubled countenance, a readiness to resign his Wellington boots to any efficient substitute who would have consented to stand in them at that moment.\n\nMrs. Pott read the paragraph, uttered a loud shriek, and threw herself at full length on the hearth-rug, screaming, and tapping it with the heels of her shoes, in a manner which could leave no doubt of the propriety of her feelings on the occasion.\n\n‘My dear,’ said the terrified Pott, ‘I didn’t say I believed it;--I--’ but the unfortunate man’s voice was drowned in the screaming of his partner.\n\n‘Mrs. Pott, let me entreat you, my dear ma’am, to compose yourself,’ said Mr. Winkle; but the shrieks and tappings were louder, and more frequent than ever.\n\n‘My dear,’ said Mr. Pott, ‘I’m very sorry. If you won’t consider your own health, consider me, my dear. We shall have a crowd round the house.’ But the more strenuously Mr. Pott entreated, the more vehemently the screams poured forth.\n\nVery fortunately, however, attached to Mrs. Pott’s person was a bodyguard of one, a young lady whose ostensible employment was to preside over her toilet, but who rendered herself useful in a variety of ways, and in none more so than in the particular department of constantly aiding and abetting her mistress in every wish and inclination opposed to the desires of the unhappy Pott. The screams reached this young lady’s ears in due course, and brought her into the room with a speed which threatened to derange, materially, the very exquisite arrangement of her cap and ringlets.\n\n‘Oh, my dear, dear mistress!’ exclaimed the bodyguard, kneeling frantically by the side of the prostrate Mrs. Pott. ‘Oh, my dear mistress, what is the matter?’\n\n‘Your master--your brutal master,’ murmured the patient.\n\nPott was evidently giving way.\n\n‘It’s a shame,’ said the bodyguard reproachfully. ‘I know he’ll be the death on you, ma’am. Poor dear thing!’\n\nHe gave way more. The opposite party followed up the attack.\n\n‘Oh, don’t leave me--don’t leave me, Goodwin,’ murmured Mrs. Pott, clutching at the wrist of the said Goodwin with an hysteric jerk. ‘You’re the only person that’s kind to me, Goodwin.’\n\nAt this affecting appeal, Goodwin got up a little domestic tragedy of her own, and shed tears copiously.\n\n‘Never, ma’am--never,’ said Goodwin. ‘Oh, sir, you should be careful-- you should indeed; you don’t know what harm you may do missis; you’ll be sorry for it one day, I know--I’ve always said so.’\n\nThe unlucky Pott looked timidly on, but said nothing.\n\n‘Goodwin,’ said Mrs. Pott, in a soft voice.\n\n‘Ma’am,’ said Goodwin.\n\n‘If you only knew how I have loved that man--’\n\nDon’t distress yourself by recollecting it, ma’am,’ said the bodyguard.\n\nPott looked very frightened. It was time to finish him.\n\n‘And now,’ sobbed Mrs. Pott, ‘now, after all, to be treated in this way; to be reproached and insulted in the presence of a third party, and that party almost a stranger. But I will not submit to it! Goodwin,’ continued Mrs. Pott, raising herself in the arms of her attendant, ‘my brother, the lieutenant, shall interfere. I’ll be separated, Goodwin!’\n\n‘It would certainly serve him right, ma’am,’ said Goodwin.\n\nWhatever thoughts the threat of a separation might have awakened in Mr. Pott’s mind, he forbore to give utterance to them, and contented himself by saying, with great humility:--\n\n‘My dear, will you hear me?’\n\nA fresh train of sobs was the only reply, as Mrs. Pott grew more hysterical, requested to be informed why she was ever born, and required sundry other pieces of information of a similar description.\n\n‘My dear,’ remonstrated Mr. Pott, ‘do not give way to these sensitive feelings. I never believed that the paragraph had any foundation, my dear--impossible. I was only angry, my dear--I may say outrageous--with the Independent people for daring to insert it; that’s all.’ Mr. Pott cast an imploring look at the innocent cause of the mischief, as if to entreat him to say nothing about the serpent.\n\n‘And what steps, sir, do you mean to take to obtain redress?’ inquired Mr. Winkle, gaining courage as he saw Pott losing it.\n\n‘Oh, Goodwin,’ observed Mrs. Pott, ‘does he mean to horsewhip the editor of the Independent--does he, Goodwin?’\n\n‘Hush, hush, ma’am; pray keep yourself quiet,’ replied the bodyguard. ‘I dare say he will, if you wish it, ma’am.’\n\n‘Certainly,’ said Pott, as his wife evinced decided symptoms of going off again. ‘Of course I shall.’\n\n‘When, Goodwin--when?’ said Mrs. Pott, still undecided about the going off.\n\n‘Immediately, of course,’ said Mr. Pott; ‘before the day is out.’\n\n‘Oh, Goodwin,’ resumed Mrs. Pott, ‘it’s the only way of meeting the slander, and setting me right with the world.’\n\n‘Certainly, ma’am,’ replied Goodwin. ‘No man as is a man, ma’am, could refuse to do it.’\n\nSo, as the hysterics were still hovering about, Mr. Pott said once more that he would do it; but Mrs. Pott was so overcome at the bare idea of having ever been suspected, that she was half a dozen times on the very verge of a relapse, and most unquestionably would have gone off, had it not been for the indefatigable efforts of the assiduous Goodwin, and repeated entreaties for pardon from the conquered Pott; and finally, when that unhappy individual had been frightened and snubbed down to his proper level, Mrs. Pott recovered, and they went to breakfast.\n\n‘You will not allow this base newspaper slander to shorten your stay here, Mr. Winkle?’ said Mrs. Pott, smiling through the traces of her tears.\n\n‘I hope not,’ said Mr. Pott, actuated, as he spoke, by a wish that his visitor would choke himself with the morsel of dry toast which he was raising to his lips at the moment, and so terminate his stay effectually.\n\n‘I hope not.’\n‘You are very good,’ said Mr. Winkle; ‘but a letter has been received from Mr. Pickwick--so I learn by a note from Mr. Tupman, which was brought up to my bedroom door, this morning--in which he requests us to join him at Bury to-day; and we are to leave by the coach at noon.’\n\n‘But you will come back?’ said Mrs. Pott.\n\n‘Oh, certainly,’ replied Mr. Winkle.\n\n‘You are quite sure?’ said Mrs. Pott, stealing a tender look at her visitor.\n\n‘Quite,’ responded Mr. Winkle.\n\nThe breakfast passed off in silence, for each of the party was brooding over his, or her, own personal grievances. Mrs. Pott was regretting the loss of a beau; Mr. Pott his rash pledge to horsewhip the Independent; Mr. Winkle his having innocently placed himself in so awkward a situation. Noon approached, and after many adieux and promises to return, he tore himself away.\n\n‘If he ever comes back, I’ll poison him,’ thought Mr. Pott, as he turned into the little back office where he prepared his thunderbolts.\n\n‘If I ever do come back, and mix myself up with these people again,’ thought Mr. Winkle, as he wended his way to the Peacock, ‘I shall deserve to be horsewhipped myself--that’s all.’\n\nHis friends were ready, the coach was nearly so, and in half an hour they were proceeding on their journey, along the road over which Mr. Pickwick and Sam had so recently travelled, and of which, as we have already said something, we do not feel called upon to extract Mr. Snodgrass’s poetical and beautiful description.\n\nMr. Weller was standing at the door of the Angel, ready to receive them, and by that gentleman they were ushered to the apartment of Mr. Pickwick, where, to the no small surprise of Mr. Winkle and Mr. Snodgrass, and the no small embarrassment of Mr. Tupman, they found old Wardle and Trundle.\n\n‘How are you?’ said the old man, grasping Mr. Tupman’s hand. ‘Don’t hang back, or look sentimental about it; it can’t be helped, old fellow. For her sake, I wish you’d had her; for your own, I’m very glad you have not. A young fellow like you will do better one of these days, eh?’ With this conclusion, Wardle slapped Mr. Tupman on the back, and laughed heartily.\n\n‘Well, and how are you, my fine fellows?’ said the old gentleman, shaking hands with Mr. Winkle and Mr. Snodgrass at the same time. ‘I have just been telling Pickwick that we must have you all down at Christmas. We’re going to have a wedding--a real wedding this time.’\n\n‘A wedding!’ exclaimed Mr. Snodgrass, turning very pale.\n\n‘Yes, a wedding. But don’t be frightened,’ said the good-humoured old man; ‘it’s only Trundle there, and Bella.’\n\n‘Oh, is that all?’ said Mr. Snodgrass, relieved from a painful doubt which had fallen heavily on his breast. ‘Give you joy, Sir. How is Joe?’\n\n‘Very well,’ replied the old gentleman. ‘Sleepy as ever.’\n\n‘And your mother, and the clergyman, and all of ‘em?’\n\n‘Quite well.’\n\n‘Where,’ said Mr. Tupman, with an effort--‘where is--she, Sir?’ and he turned away his head, and covered his eyes with his hand.\n\n‘She!’ said the old gentleman, with a knowing shake of the head. ‘Do you mean my single relative--eh?’\n\nMr. Tupman, by a nod, intimated that his question applied to the disappointed Rachael.\n\n‘Oh, she’s gone away,’ said the old gentleman. ‘She’s living at a relation’s, far enough off. She couldn’t bear to see the girls, so I let her go. But come! Here’s the dinner. You must be hungry after your ride. I am, without any ride at all; so let us fall to.’\n\nAmple justice was done to the meal; and when they were seated round the table, after it had been disposed of, Mr. Pickwick, to the intense horror and indignation of his followers, related the adventure he had undergone, and the success which had attended the base artifices of the diabolical Jingle.\n\n‘And the attack of rheumatism which I caught in that garden,’ said Mr. Pickwick, in conclusion, ‘renders me lame at this moment.’\n\n‘I, too, have had something of an adventure,’ said Mr. Winkle, with a smile; and, at the request of Mr. Pickwick, he detailed the malicious libel of the Eatanswill Independent, and the consequent excitement of their friend, the editor.\n\nMr. Pickwick’s brow darkened during the recital. His friends observed it, and, when Mr. Winkle had concluded, maintained a profound silence. Mr. Pickwick struck the table emphatically with his clenched fist, and spoke as follows:--\n\n‘Is it not a wonderful circumstance,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘that we seem destined to enter no man’s house without involving him in some degree of trouble? Does it not, I ask, bespeak the indiscretion, or, worse than that, the blackness of heart--that I should say so!--of my followers, that, beneath whatever roof they locate, they disturb the peace of mind and happiness of some confiding female? Is it not, I say--’\n\nMr. Pickwick would in all probability have gone on for some time, had not the entrance of Sam, with a letter, caused him to break off in his eloquent discourse. He passed his handkerchief across his forehead, took off his spectacles, wiped them, and put them on again; and his voice had recovered its wonted softness of tone when he said--\n\n‘What have you there, Sam?’\n\n‘Called at the post-office just now, and found this here letter, as has laid there for two days,’ replied Mr. Weller. ‘It’s sealed vith a vafer, and directed in round hand.’\n\n‘I don’t know this hand,’ said Mr. Pickwick, opening the letter. ‘Mercy on us! what’s this? It must be a jest; it--it--can’t be true.’\n\n‘What’s the matter?’ was the general inquiry.\n\n‘Nobody dead, is there?’ said Wardle, alarmed at the horror in Mr. Pickwick’s countenance.\n\nMr. Pickwick made no reply, but, pushing the letter across the table, and desiring Mr. Tupman to read it aloud, fell back in his chair with a look of vacant astonishment quite alarming to behold.\n\nMr. Tupman, with a trembling voice, read the letter, of which the following is a copy:--\n\nFreeman’s Court, Cornhill, August 28th, 1827.\n\nBardell against Pickwick.\n\nSir,\nHaving been instructed by Mrs. Martha Bardell to commence an action against you for a breach of promise of marriage, for which the plaintiff lays her damages at fifteen hundred pounds, we beg to inform you that a writ has been issued against you in this suit in the Court of Common Pleas; and request to know, by return of post, the name of your attorney in London, who will accept service thereof.\n\nWe are, Sir, Your obedient servants, Dodson & Fogg.\n\nMr. Samuel Pickwick.\n\nThere was something so impressive in the mute astonishment with which each man regarded his neighbour, and every man regarded Mr. Pickwick, that all seemed afraid to speak. The silence was at length broken by Mr. Tupman.\n\n‘Dodson and Fogg,’ he repeated mechanically.\n\n‘Bardell and Pickwick,’ said Mr. Snodgrass, musing.\n\n‘Peace of mind and happiness of confiding females,’ murmured Mr. Winkle, with an air of abstraction.\n\n‘It’s a conspiracy,’ said Mr. Pickwick, at length recovering the power of speech; ‘a base conspiracy between these two grasping attorneys, Dodson and Fogg. Mrs. Bardell would never do it;--she hasn’t the heart to do it;--she hasn’t the case to do it. Ridiculous--ridiculous.’\n\nOf her heart,’ said Wardle, with a smile, ‘you should certainly be the best judge. I don’t wish to discourage you, but I should certainly say that, of her case, Dodson and Fogg are far better judges than any of us can be.’\n\n‘It’s a vile attempt to extort money,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘I hope it is,’ said Wardle, with a short, dry cough.\n\n‘Who ever heard me address her in any way but that in which a lodger would address his landlady?’ continued Mr. Pickwick, with great vehemence. ‘Who ever saw me with her? Not even my friends here--’\n\n‘Except on one occasion,’ said Mr. Tupman.\n\nMr. Pickwick changed colour.\n\n‘Ah,’ said Mr. Wardle. ‘Well, that’s important. There was nothing suspicious then, I suppose?’\n\nMr. Tupman glanced timidly at his leader. ‘Why,’ said he, ‘there was nothing suspicious; but--I don’t know how it happened, mind--she certainly was reclining in his arms.’\n\n‘Gracious powers!’ ejaculated Mr. Pickwick, as the recollection of the scene in question struck forcibly upon him; ‘what a dreadful instance of the force of circumstances! So she was--so she was.’\n\n‘And our friend was soothing her anguish,’ said Mr. Winkle, rather maliciously.\n\n‘So I was,’ said Mr. Pickwick. ‘I don’t deny it. So I was.’\n\n‘Hollo!’ said Wardle; ‘for a case in which there’s nothing suspicious, this looks rather queer--eh, Pickwick? Ah, sly dog--sly dog!’ and he laughed till the glasses on the sideboard rang again.\n\n‘What a dreadful conjunction of appearances!’ exclaimed Mr. Pickwick, resting his chin upon his hands. ‘Winkle--Tupman--I beg your pardon for the observations I made just now. We are all the victims of circumstances, and I the greatest.’ With this apology Mr. Pickwick buried his head in his hands, and ruminated; while Wardle measured out a regular circle of nods and winks, addressed to the other members of the company.\n\n‘I’ll have it explained, though,’ said Mr. Pickwick, raising his head and hammering the table. ‘I’ll see this Dodson and Fogg! I’ll go to London to-morrow.’\n\n‘Not to-morrow,’ said Wardle; ‘you’re too lame.’\n\n‘Well, then, next day.’\n\n‘Next day is the first of September, and you’re pledged to ride out with us, as far as Sir Geoffrey Manning’s grounds at all events, and to meet us at lunch, if you don’t take the field.’\n\n‘Well, then, the day after,’ said Mr. Pickwick; ‘Thursday.--Sam!’\n\n‘Sir,’ replied Mr. Weller.\n\n‘Take two places outside to London, on Thursday morning, for yourself and me.’\n\n‘Wery well, Sir.’\n\nMr. Weller left the room, and departed slowly on his errand, with his hands in his pocket and his eyes fixed on the ground.\n\n‘Rum feller, the hemperor,’ said Mr. Weller, as he walked slowly up the street. ‘Think o’ his makin’ up to that ‘ere Mrs. Bardell--vith a little boy, too! Always the vay vith these here old ‘uns howsoever, as is such steady goers to look at. I didn’t think he’d ha’ done it, though--I didn’t think he’d ha’ done it!’ Moralising in this strain, Mr. Samuel Weller bent his steps towards the booking-office.\n\n# CHAPTER XIX. A PLEASANT DAY WITH AN UNPLEASANT TERMINATION\n\nThe birds, who, happily for their own peace of mind and personal comfort, were in blissful ignorance of the preparations which had been making to astonish them, on the first of September, hailed it, no doubt, as one of the pleasantest mornings they had seen that season. Many a young partridge who strutted complacently among the stubble, with all the finicking coxcombry of youth, and many an older one who watched his levity out of his little round eye, with the contemptuous air of a bird of wisdom and experience, alike unconscious of their approaching doom, basked in the fresh morning air with lively and blithesome feelings, and a few hours afterwards were laid low upon the earth. But we grow affecting: let us proceed.\n\nIn plain commonplace matter-of-fact, then, it was a fine morning--so fine that you would scarcely have believed that the few months of an English summer had yet flown by. Hedges, fields, and trees, hill and moorland, presented to the eye their ever-varying shades of deep rich green; scarce a leaf had fallen, scarce a sprinkle of yellow mingled with the hues of summer, warned you that autumn had begun. The sky was cloudless; the sun shone out bright and warm; the songs of birds, the hum of myriads of summer insects, filled the air; and the cottage gardens, crowded with flowers of every rich and beautiful tint, sparkled, in the heavy dew, like beds of glittering jewels. Everything bore the stamp of summer, and none of its beautiful colour had yet faded from the die.\n\nSuch was the morning, when an open carriage, in which were three Pickwickians (Mr. Snodgrass having preferred to remain at home), Mr. Wardle, and Mr. Trundle, with Sam Weller on the box beside the driver, pulled up by a gate at the roadside, before which stood a tall, raw- boned gamekeeper, and a half-booted, leather-legginged boy, each bearing a bag of capacious dimensions, and accompanied by a brace of pointers.\n\n‘I say,’ whispered Mr. Winkle to Wardle, as the man let down the steps, ‘they don’t suppose we’re going to kill game enough to fill those bags, do they?’\n\n‘Fill them!’ exclaimed old Wardle. ‘Bless you, yes! You shall fill one, and I the other; and when we’ve done with them, the pockets of our shooting-jackets will hold as much more.’\n\nMr. Winkle dismounted without saying anything in reply to this observation; but he thought within himself, that if the party remained in the open air, till he had filled one of the bags, they stood a considerable chance of catching colds in their heads.\n\n‘Hi, Juno, lass-hi, old girl; down, Daph, down,’ said Wardle, caressing the dogs. ‘Sir Geoffrey still in Scotland, of course, Martin?’\n\nThe tall gamekeeper replied in the affirmative, and looked with some surprise from Mr. Winkle, who was holding his gun as if he wished his coat pocket to save him the trouble of pulling the trigger, to Mr. Tupman, who was holding his as if he was afraid of it--as there is no earthly reason to doubt he really was.\n\n‘My friends are not much in the way of this sort of thing yet, Martin,’ said Wardle, noticing the look. ‘Live and learn, you know. They’ll be good shots one of these days. I beg my friend Winkle’s pardon, though; he has had some practice.’\n\nMr. Winkle smiled feebly over his blue neckerchief in acknowledgment of the compliment, and got himself so mysteriously entangled with his gun, in his modest confusion, that if the piece had been loaded, he must inevitably have shot himself dead upon the spot.\n\n‘You mustn’t handle your piece in that ‘ere way, when you come to have the charge in it, Sir,’ said the tall gamekeeper gruffly; ‘or I’m damned if you won’t make cold meat of some on us.’\n\nMr. Winkle, thus admonished, abruptly altered his position, and in so doing, contrived to bring the barrel into pretty smart contact with Mr. Weller’s head.\n\n‘Hollo!’ said Sam, picking up his hat, which had been knocked off, and rubbing his temple. ‘Hollo, sir! if you comes it this vay, you’ll fill one o’ them bags, and something to spare, at one fire.’\n\nHere the leather-legginged boy laughed very heartily, and then tried to look as if it was somebody else, whereat Mr. Winkle frowned majestically.\n\n‘Where did you tell the boy to meet us with the snack, Martin?’ inquired Wardle.\n\n‘Side of One-tree Hill, at twelve o’clock, Sir.’\n\n‘That’s not Sir Geoffrey’s land, is it?’\n\n‘No, Sir; but it’s close by it. It’s Captain Boldwig’s land; but there’ll be nobody to interrupt us, and there’s a fine bit of turf there.’\n\n‘Very well,’ said old Wardle. ‘Now the sooner we’re off the better. Will you join us at twelve, then, Pickwick?’\n\nMr. Pickwick was particularly desirous to view the sport, the more especially as he was rather anxious in respect of Mr. Winkle’s life and limbs. On so inviting a morning, too, it was very tantalising to turn back, and leave his friends to enjoy themselves. It was, therefore, with a very rueful air that he replied--\n\n‘Why, I suppose I must.’\n\n‘Ain’t the gentleman a shot, Sir?’ inquired the long gamekeeper.\n\n‘No,’ replied Wardle; ‘and he’s lame besides.’\n\n‘I should very much like to go,’ said Mr. Pickwick--‘very much.’\n\nThere was a short pause of commiseration.\n\n‘There’s a barrow t’other side the hedge,’ said the boy. ‘If the gentleman’s servant would wheel along the paths, he could keep nigh us, and we could lift it over the stiles, and that.’\n\n‘The wery thing,’ said Mr. Weller, who was a party interested, inasmuch as he ardently longed to see the sport. ‘The wery thing. Well said, Smallcheek; I’ll have it out in a minute.’\n\nBut here a difficulty arose. The long gamekeeper resolutely protested against the introduction into a shooting party, of a gentleman in a barrow, as a gross violation of all established rules and precedents.\n\nIt was a great objection, but not an insurmountable one. The gamekeeper having been coaxed and feed, and having, moreover, eased his mind by ‘punching’ the head of the inventive youth who had first suggested the use of the machine, Mr. Pickwick was placed in it, and off the party set; Wardle and the long gamekeeper leading the way, and Mr. Pickwick in the barrow, propelled by Sam, bringing up the rear.\n\n‘Stop, Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick, when they had got half across the first field.\n\n‘What’s the matter now?’ said Wardle.\n\n‘I won’t suffer this barrow to be moved another step,’ said Mr. Pickwick, resolutely, ‘unless Winkle carries that gun of his in a different manner.’\n\n‘How am I to carry it?’ said the wretched Winkle.\n\n‘Carry it with the muzzle to the ground,’ replied Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘It’s so unsportsmanlike,’ reasoned Winkle.\n\n‘I don’t care whether it’s unsportsmanlike or not,’ replied Mr. Pickwick; ‘I am not going to be shot in a wheel-barrow, for the sake of appearances, to please anybody.’\n\n‘I know the gentleman’ll put that ‘ere charge into somebody afore he’s done,’ growled the long man.\n\n‘Well, well--I don’t mind,’ said poor Winkle, turning his gun-stock uppermost--‘there.’\n\n‘Anythin’ for a quiet life,’ said Mr. Weller; and on they went again.\n\n‘Stop!’ said Mr. Pickwick, after they had gone a few yards farther.\n\n‘What now?’ said Wardle.\n\n‘That gun of Tupman’s is not safe: I know it isn’t,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Eh? What! not safe?’ said Mr. Tupman, in a tone of great alarm.\n\n‘Not as you are carrying it,’ said Mr. Pickwick. ‘I am very sorry to make any further objection, but I cannot consent to go on, unless you carry it as Winkle does his.’\n\n‘I think you had better, sir,’ said the long gamekeeper, ‘or you’re quite as likely to lodge the charge in yourself as in anything else.’\n\nMr. Tupman, with the most obliging haste, placed his piece in the position required, and the party moved on again; the two amateurs marching with reversed arms, like a couple of privates at a royal funeral.\n\nThe dogs suddenly came to a dead stop, and the party advancing stealthily a single pace, stopped too.\n\n‘What’s the matter with the dogs’ legs?’ whispered Mr. Winkle. ‘How queer they’re standing.’\n\n‘Hush, can’t you?’ replied Wardle softly. ‘Don’t you see, they’re making a point?’\n\n‘Making a point!’ said Mr. Winkle, staring about him, as if he expected to discover some particular beauty in the landscape, which the sagacious animals were calling special attention to. ‘Making a point! What are they pointing at?’\n\n‘Keep your eyes open,’ said Wardle, not heeding the question in the excitement of the moment. ‘Now then.’\n\nThere was a sharp whirring noise, that made Mr. Winkle start back as if he had been shot himself. Bang, bang, went a couple of guns--the smoke swept quickly away over the field, and curled into the air.\n\n‘Where are they!’ said Mr. Winkle, in a state of the highest excitement, turning round and round in all directions. ‘Where are they? Tell me when to fire. Where are they--where are they?’\n\n‘Where are they!’ said Wardle, taking up a brace of birds which the dogs had deposited at his feet. ‘Why, here they are.’\n\n‘No, no; I mean the others,’ said the bewildered Winkle.\n\n‘Far enough off, by this time,’ replied Wardle, coolly reloading his gun.\n\n‘We shall very likely be up with another covey in five minutes,’ said the long gamekeeper. ‘If the gentleman begins to fire now, perhaps he’ll just get the shot out of the barrel by the time they rise.’\n\n‘Ha! ha! ha!’ roared Mr. Weller.\n\n‘Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick, compassionating his follower’s confusion and embarrassment.\n\n‘Don’t laugh.’\n\n‘Certainly not, Sir.’ So, by way of indemnification, Mr. Weller contorted his features from behind the wheel-barrow, for the exclusive amusement of the boy with the leggings, who thereupon burst into a boisterous laugh, and was summarily cuffed by the long gamekeeper, who wanted a pretext for turning round, to hide his own merriment.\n\n‘Bravo, old fellow!’ said Wardle to Mr. Tupman; ‘you fired that time, at all events.’\n\n‘Oh, yes,’ replied Mr. Tupman, with conscious pride. ‘I let it off.’\n\n‘Well done. You’ll hit something next time, if you look sharp. Very easy, ain’t it?’\n\n‘Yes, it’s very easy,’ said Mr. Tupman. ‘How it hurts one’s shoulder, though. It nearly knocked me backwards. I had no idea these small firearms kicked so.’\n\n‘Ah,’ said the old gentleman, smiling, ‘you’ll get used to it in time. Now then--all ready--all right with the barrow there?’\n\n‘All right, Sir,’ replied Mr. Weller.\n\n‘Come along, then.’\n\n‘Hold hard, Sir,’ said Sam, raising the barrow.\n\n‘Aye, aye,’ replied Mr. Pickwick; and on they went, as briskly as need be.\n\n‘Keep that barrow back now,’ cried Wardle, when it had been hoisted over a stile into another field, and Mr. Pickwick had been deposited in it once more.\n\n‘All right, sir,’ replied Mr. Weller, pausing.\n\n‘Now, Winkle,’ said the old gentleman, ‘follow me softly, and don’t be too late this time.’\n\n‘Never fear,’ said Mr. Winkle. ‘Are they pointing?’\n\n‘No, no; not now. Quietly now, quietly.’ On they crept, and very quietly they would have advanced, if Mr. Winkle, in the performance of some very intricate evolutions with his gun, had not accidentally fired, at the most critical moment, over the boy’s head, exactly in the very spot where the tall man’s brain would have been, had he been there instead.\n\n‘Why, what on earth did you do that for?’ said old Wardle, as the birds flew unharmed away.\n\n‘I never saw such a gun in my life,’ replied poor Mr. Winkle, looking at the lock, as if that would do any good. ‘It goes off of its own accord. It will do it.’\n\n‘Will do it!’ echoed Wardle, with something of irritation in his manner. ‘I wish it would kill something of its own accord.’\n\n‘It’ll do that afore long, Sir,’ observed the tall man, in a low, prophetic voice.\n\n‘What do you mean by that observation, Sir?’ inquired Mr. Winkle, angrily.\n\n‘Never mind, Sir, never mind,’ replied the long gamekeeper; ‘I’ve no family myself, sir; and this here boy’s mother will get something handsome from Sir Geoffrey, if he’s killed on his land. Load again, Sir, load again.’\n\n‘Take away his gun,’ cried Mr. Pickwick from the barrow, horror-stricken at the long man’s dark insinuations. ‘Take away his gun, do you hear, somebody?’\n\nNobody, however, volunteered to obey the command; and Mr. Winkle, after darting a rebellious glance at Mr. Pickwick, reloaded his gun, and proceeded onwards with the rest.\n\nWe are bound, on the authority of Mr. Pickwick, to state, that Mr. Tupman’s mode of proceeding evinced far more of prudence and deliberation, than that adopted by Mr. Winkle. Still, this by no means detracts from the great authority of the latter gentleman, on all matters connected with the field; because, as Mr. Pickwick beautifully observes, it has somehow or other happened, from time immemorial, that many of the best and ablest philosophers, who have been perfect lights of science in matters of theory, have been wholly unable to reduce them to practice.\n\nMr. Tupman’s process, like many of our most sublime discoveries, was extremely simple. With the quickness and penetration of a man of genius, he had at once observed that the two great points to be attained were-- first, to discharge his piece without injury to himself, and, secondly, to do so, without danger to the bystanders--obviously, the best thing to do, after surmounting the difficulty of firing at all, was to shut his eyes firmly, and fire into the air.\n\nOn one occasion, after performing this feat, Mr. Tupman, on opening his eyes, beheld a plump partridge in the act of falling, wounded, to the ground. He was on the point of congratulating Mr. Wardle on his invariable success, when that gentleman advanced towards him, and grasped him warmly by the hand.\n\n‘Tupman,’ said the old gentleman, ‘you singled out that particular bird?’\n\n‘No,’ said Mr. Tupman--‘no.’\n\n‘You did,’ said Wardle. ‘I saw you do it--I observed you pick him out--I noticed you, as you raised your piece to take aim; and I will say this, that the best shot in existence could not have done it more beautifully. You are an older hand at this than I thought you, Tupman; you have been out before.’\n\nIt was in vain for Mr. Tupman to protest, with a smile of self-denial, that he never had. The very smile was taken as evidence to the contrary; and from that time forth his reputation was established. It is not the only reputation that has been acquired as easily, nor are such fortunate circumstances confined to partridge-shooting.\n\nMeanwhile, Mr. Winkle flashed, and blazed, and smoked away, without producing any material results worthy of being noted down; sometimes expending his charge in mid-air, and at others sending it skimming along so near the surface of the ground as to place the lives of the two dogs on a rather uncertain and precarious tenure. As a display of fancy- shooting, it was extremely varied and curious; as an exhibition of firing with any precise object, it was, upon the whole, perhaps a failure. It is an established axiom, that ‘every bullet has its billet.’ If it apply in an equal degree to shot, those of Mr. Winkle were unfortunate foundlings, deprived of their natural rights, cast loose upon the world, and billeted nowhere.\n\n‘Well,’ said Wardle, walking up to the side of the barrow, and wiping the streams of perspiration from his jolly red face; ‘smoking day, isn’t it?’\n\n‘It is, indeed,’ replied Mr. Pickwick. The sun is tremendously hot, even to me. I don’t know how you must feel it.’\n\n‘Why,’ said the old gentleman, ‘pretty hot. It’s past twelve, though. You see that green hill there?’\n\n‘Certainly.’\n\n‘That’s the place where we are to lunch; and, by Jove, there’s the boy with the basket, punctual as clockwork!’\n\n‘So he is,’ said Mr. Pickwick, brightening up. ‘Good boy, that. I’ll give him a shilling, presently. Now, then, Sam, wheel away.’\n\n‘Hold on, sir,’ said Mr. Weller, invigorated with the prospect of refreshments. ‘Out of the vay, young leathers. If you walley my precious life don’t upset me, as the gen’l’m’n said to the driver when they was a-carryin’ him to Tyburn.’ And quickening his pace to a sharp run, Mr. Weller wheeled his master nimbly to the green hill, shot him dexterously out by the very side of the basket, and proceeded to unpack it with the utmost despatch.\n\n‘Weal pie,’ said Mr. Weller, soliloquising, as he arranged the eatables on the grass. ‘Wery good thing is weal pie, when you know the lady as made it, and is quite sure it ain’t kittens; and arter all though, where’s the odds, when they’re so like weal that the wery piemen themselves don’t know the difference?’\n\n‘Don’t they, Sam?’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Not they, sir,’ replied Mr. Weller, touching his hat. ‘I lodged in the same house vith a pieman once, sir, and a wery nice man he was--reg’lar clever chap, too--make pies out o’ anything, he could. “What a number o’ cats you keep, Mr. Brooks,” says I, when I’d got intimate with him. “Ah,” says he, “I do--a good many,” says he, “You must be wery fond o’ cats,” says I. “Other people is,” says he, a-winkin’ at me; “they ain’t in season till the winter though,” says he. “Not in season!” says I. “No,” says he, “fruits is in, cats is out.” “Why, what do you mean?” says I. “Mean!” says he. “That I’ll never be a party to the combination o’ the butchers, to keep up the price o’ meat,” says he. “Mr. Weller,” says he, a-squeezing my hand wery hard, and vispering in my ear--“don’t mention this here agin--but it’s the seasonin’ as does it. They’re all made o’ them noble animals,” says he, a-pointin’ to a wery nice little tabby kitten, “and I seasons ‘em for beefsteak, weal or kidney, ‘cording to the demand. And more than that,” says he, “I can make a weal a beef- steak, or a beef-steak a kidney, or any one on ‘em a mutton, at a minute’s notice, just as the market changes, and appetites wary!”’\n\n‘He must have been a very ingenious young man, that, Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick, with a slight shudder.\n\n‘Just was, sir,’ replied Mr. Weller, continuing his occupation of emptying the basket, ‘and the pies was beautiful. Tongue--, well that’s a wery good thing when it ain’t a woman’s. Bread--knuckle o’ ham, reg’lar picter--cold beef in slices, wery good. What’s in them stone jars, young touch-and-go?’\n\n‘Beer in this one,’ replied the boy, taking from his shoulder a couple of large stone bottles, fastened together by a leathern strap--‘cold punch in t’other.’\n\n‘And a wery good notion of a lunch it is, take it altogether,’ said Mr. Weller, surveying his arrangement of the repast with great satisfaction. ‘Now, gen’l’m’n, “fall on,” as the English said to the French when they fixed bagginets.’\n\nIt needed no second invitation to induce the party to yield full justice to the meal; and as little pressing did it require to induce Mr. Weller, the long gamekeeper, and the two boys, to station themselves on the grass, at a little distance, and do good execution upon a decent proportion of the viands. An old oak afforded a pleasant shelter to the group, and a rich prospect of arable and meadow land, intersected with luxuriant hedges, and richly ornamented with wood, lay spread out before them.\n\n‘This is delightful--thoroughly delightful!’ said Mr. Pickwick; the skin of whose expressive countenance was rapidly peeling off, with exposure to the sun.\n\n‘So it is--so it is, old fellow,’ replied Wardle. ‘Come; a glass of punch!’\n\n‘With great pleasure,’ said Mr. Pickwick; the satisfaction of whose countenance, after drinking it, bore testimony to the sincerity of the reply.\n\n‘Good,’ said Mr. Pickwick, smacking his lips. ‘Very good. I’ll take another. Cool; very cool. Come, gentlemen,’ continued Mr. Pickwick, still retaining his hold upon the jar, ‘a toast. Our friends at Dingley Dell.’\n\nThe toast was drunk with loud acclamations.\n\n‘I’ll tell you what I shall do, to get up my shooting again,’ said Mr. Winkle, who was eating bread and ham with a pocket-knife. ‘I’ll put a stuffed partridge on the top of a post, and practise at it, beginning at a short distance, and lengthening it by degrees. I understand it’s capital practice.’\n\n‘I know a gen’l’man, Sir,’ said Mr. Weller, ‘as did that, and begun at two yards; but he never tried it on agin; for he blowed the bird right clean away at the first fire, and nobody ever seed a feather on him arterwards.’\n\n‘Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Sir,’ replied Mr. Weller.\n\n‘Have the goodness to reserve your anecdotes till they are called for.’\n\n‘Cert’nly, sir.’\n\nHere Mr. Weller winked the eye which was not concealed by the beer-can he was raising to his lips, with such exquisite facetiousness, that the two boys went into spontaneous convulsions, and even the long man condescended to smile.\n\n‘Well, that certainly is most capital cold punch,’ said Mr. Pickwick, looking earnestly at the stone bottle; ‘and the day is extremely warm, and--Tupman, my dear friend, a glass of punch?’\n\n‘With the greatest delight,’ replied Mr. Tupman; and having drank that glass, Mr. Pickwick took another, just to see whether there was any orange peel in the punch, because orange peel always disagreed with him; and finding that there was not, Mr. Pickwick took another glass to the health of their absent friend, and then felt himself imperatively called upon to propose another in honour of the punch-compounder, unknown.\n\nThis constant succession of glasses produced considerable effect upon Mr. Pickwick; his countenance beamed with the most sunny smiles, laughter played around his lips, and good-humoured merriment twinkled in his eye. Yielding by degrees to the influence of the exciting liquid, rendered more so by the heat, Mr. Pickwick expressed a strong desire to recollect a song which he had heard in his infancy, and the attempt proving abortive, sought to stimulate his memory with more glasses of punch, which appeared to have quite a contrary effect; for, from forgetting the words of the song, he began to forget how to articulate any words at all; and finally, after rising to his legs to address the company in an eloquent speech, he fell into the barrow, and fast asleep, simultaneously.\n\nThe basket having been repacked, and it being found perfectly impossible to awaken Mr. Pickwick from his torpor, some discussion took place whether it would be better for Mr. Weller to wheel his master back again, or to leave him where he was, until they should all be ready to return. The latter course was at length decided on; and as the further expedition was not to exceed an hour’s duration, and as Mr. Weller begged very hard to be one of the party, it was determined to leave Mr. Pickwick asleep in the barrow, and to call for him on their return. So away they went, leaving Mr. Pickwick snoring most comfortably in the shade.\n\nThat Mr. Pickwick would have continued to snore in the shade until his friends came back, or, in default thereof, until the shades of evening had fallen on the landscape, there appears no reasonable cause to doubt; always supposing that he had been suffered to remain there in peace. But he was not suffered to remain there in peace. And this was what prevented him.\n\nCaptain Boldwig was a little fierce man in a stiff black neckerchief and blue surtout, who, when he did condescend to walk about his property, did it in company with a thick rattan stick with a brass ferrule, and a gardener and sub-gardener with meek faces, to whom (the gardeners, not the stick) Captain Boldwig gave his orders with all due grandeur and ferocity; for Captain Boldwig’s wife’s sister had married a marquis, and the captain’s house was a villa, and his land ‘grounds,’ and it was all very high, and mighty, and great.\n\nMr. Pickwick had not been asleep half an hour when little Captain Boldwig, followed by the two gardeners, came striding along as fast as his size and importance would let him; and when he came near the oak tree, Captain Boldwig paused and drew a long breath, and looked at the prospect as if he thought the prospect ought to be highly gratified at having him to take notice of it; and then he struck the ground emphatically with his stick, and summoned the head-gardener.\n\n‘Hunt,’ said Captain Boldwig.\n\n‘Yes, Sir,’ said the gardener.\n\n‘Roll this place to-morrow morning--do you hear, Hunt?’\n\n‘Yes, Sir.’\n\n‘And take care that you keep this place in good order--do you hear, Hunt?’\n\n‘Yes, Sir.’\n\n‘And remind me to have a board done about trespassers, and spring guns, and all that sort of thing, to keep the common people out. Do you hear, Hunt; do you hear?’\n\n‘I’ll not forget it, Sir.’\n\n‘I beg your pardon, Sir,’ said the other man, advancing, with his hand to his hat.\n\n‘Well, Wilkins, what’s the matter with you?’ said Captain Boldwig.\n\n‘I beg your pardon, sir--but I think there have been trespassers here to-day.’\n\n‘Ha!’ said the captain, scowling around him.\n\n‘Yes, sir--they have been dining here, I think, sir.’\n\n‘Why, damn their audacity, so they have,’ said Captain Boldwig, as the crumbs and fragments that were strewn upon the grass met his eye. ‘They have actually been devouring their food here. I wish I had the vagabonds here!’ said the captain, clenching the thick stick.\n\n‘I wish I had the vagabonds here,’ said the captain wrathfully.\n\n‘Beg your pardon, sir,’ said Wilkins, ‘but--’\n\n‘But what? Eh?’ roared the captain; and following the timid glance of Wilkins, his eyes encountered the wheel-barrow and Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Who are you, you rascal?’ said the captain, administering several pokes to Mr. Pickwick’s body with the thick stick. ‘What’s your name?’\n\n‘Cold punch,’ murmured Mr. Pickwick, as he sank to sleep again.\n\n‘What?’ demanded Captain Boldwig.\n\nNo reply.\n\n‘What did he say his name was?’ asked the captain.\n\n‘Punch, I think, sir,’ replied Wilkins.\n\n‘That’s his impudence--that’s his confounded impudence,’ said Captain Boldwig. ‘He’s only feigning to be asleep now,’ said the captain, in a high passion. ‘He’s drunk; he’s a drunken plebeian. Wheel him away, Wilkins, wheel him away directly.’\n\nWhere shall I wheel him to, sir?’ inquired Wilkins, with great timidity.\n\n‘Wheel him to the devil,’ replied Captain Boldwig.\n\n‘Very well, sir,’ said Wilkins.\n\n‘Stay,’ said the captain.\n\nWilkins stopped accordingly.\n\n‘Wheel him,’ said the captain--‘wheel him to the pound; and let us see whether he calls himself Punch when he comes to himself. He shall not bully me--he shall not bully me. Wheel him away.’\n\nAway Mr. Pickwick was wheeled in compliance with this imperious mandate; and the great Captain Boldwig, swelling with indignation, proceeded on his walk.\n\nInexpressible was the astonishment of the little party when they returned, to find that Mr. Pickwick had disappeared, and taken the wheel-barrow with him. It was the most mysterious and unaccountable thing that was ever heard of. For a lame man to have got upon his legs without any previous notice, and walked off, would have been most extraordinary; but when it came to his wheeling a heavy barrow before him, by way of amusement, it grew positively miraculous. They searched every nook and corner round, together and separately; they shouted, whistled, laughed, called--and all with the same result. Mr. Pickwick was not to be found. After some hours of fruitless search, they arrived at the unwelcome conclusion that they must go home without him.\n\nMeanwhile Mr. Pickwick had been wheeled to the Pound, and safely deposited therein, fast asleep in the wheel-barrow, to the immeasurable delight and satisfaction not only of all the boys in the village, but three-fourths of the whole population, who had gathered round, in expectation of his waking. If their most intense gratification had been awakened by seeing him wheeled in, how many hundredfold was their joy increased when, after a few indistinct cries of ‘Sam!’ he sat up in the barrow, and gazed with indescribable astonishment on the faces before him.\n\nA general shout was of course the signal of his having woke up; and his involuntary inquiry of ‘What’s the matter?’ occasioned another, louder than the first, if possible.\n\n‘Here’s a game!’ roared the populace.\n\n‘Where am I?’ exclaimed Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘In the pound,’ replied the mob.\n\n‘How came I here? What was I doing? Where was I brought from?’\n\nBoldwig! Captain Boldwig!’ was the only reply.\n\n‘Let me out,’ cried Mr. Pickwick. ‘Where’s my servant? Where are my friends?’\n\n‘You ain’t got no friends. Hurrah!’ Then there came a turnip, then a potato, and then an egg; with a few other little tokens of the playful disposition of the many-headed.\n\nHow long this scene might have lasted, or how much Mr. Pickwick might have suffered, no one can tell, had not a carriage, which was driving swiftly by, suddenly pulled up, from whence there descended old Wardle and Sam Weller, the former of whom, in far less time than it takes to write it, if not to read it, had made his way to Mr. Pickwick’s side, and placed him in the vehicle, just as the latter had concluded the third and last round of a single combat with the town-beadle.\n\n‘Run to the justice’s!’ cried a dozen voices.\n\n‘Ah, run avay,’ said Mr. Weller, jumping up on the box. ‘Give my compliments--Mr. Veller’s compliments--to the justice, and tell him I’ve spiled his beadle, and that, if he’ll swear in a new ‘un, I’ll come back again to-morrow and spile him. Drive on, old feller.’\n\n‘I’ll give directions for the commencement of an action for false imprisonment against this Captain Boldwig, directly I get to London,’ said Mr. Pickwick, as soon as the carriage turned out of the town.\n\n‘We were trespassing, it seems,’ said Wardle.\n\n‘I don’t care,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘I’ll bring the action.’\n\n‘No, you won’t,’ said Wardle.\n\n‘I will, by--’ But as there was a humorous expression in Wardle’s face, Mr. Pickwick checked himself, and said, ‘Why not?’\n\n‘Because,’ said old Wardle, half-bursting with laughter, ‘because they might turn on some of us, and say we had taken too much cold punch.’\n\nDo what he would, a smile would come into Mr. Pickwick’s face; the smile extended into a laugh; the laugh into a roar; the roar became general. So, to keep up their good-humour, they stopped at the first roadside tavern they came to, and ordered a glass of brandy-and-water all round, with a magnum of extra strength for Mr. Samuel Weller.\n\nCHAPTER XX. SHOWING HOW DODSON AND FOGG WERE MEN OF BUSINESS, AND THEIR CLERKS MEN OF PLEASURE; AND HOW AN AFFECTING INTERVIEW TOOK PLACE BETWEEN MR. WELLER AND HIS LONG-LOST PARENT; SHOWING ALSO WHAT CHOICE SPIRITS ASSEMBLED AT THE MAGPIE AND STUMP, AND WHAT A CAPITAL CHAPTER THE NEXT ONE WILL BE\nIn the ground-floor front of a dingy house, at the very farthest end of Freeman’s Court, Cornhill, sat the four clerks of Messrs. Dodson & Fogg, two of his Majesty’s attorneys of the courts of King’s Bench and Common Pleas at Westminster, and solicitors of the High Court of Chancery--the aforesaid clerks catching as favourable glimpses of heaven’s light and heaven’s sun, in the course of their daily labours, as a man might hope to do, were he placed at the bottom of a reasonably deep well; and without the opportunity of perceiving the stars in the day-time, which the latter secluded situation affords.\n\nThe clerks’ office of Messrs. Dodson & Fogg was a dark, mouldy, earthy- smelling room, with a high wainscotted partition to screen the clerks from the vulgar gaze, a couple of old wooden chairs, a very loud-ticking clock, an almanac, an umbrella-stand, a row of hat-pegs, and a few shelves, on which were deposited several ticketed bundles of dirty papers, some old deal boxes with paper labels, and sundry decayed stone ink bottles of various shapes and sizes. There was a glass door leading into the passage which formed the entrance to the court, and on the outer side of this glass door, Mr. Pickwick, closely followed by Sam Weller, presented himself on the Friday morning succeeding the occurrence of which a faithful narration is given in the last chapter.\n\n‘Come in, can’t you!’ cried a voice from behind the partition, in reply to Mr. Pickwick’s gentle tap at the door. And Mr. Pickwick and Sam entered accordingly.\n\n‘Mr. Dodson or Mr. Fogg at home, sir?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick, gently, advancing, hat in hand, towards the partition.\n\n‘Mr. Dodson ain’t at home, and Mr. Fogg’s particularly engaged,’ replied the voice; and at the same time the head to which the voice belonged, with a pen behind its ear, looked over the partition, and at Mr. Pickwick.\n\nIt was a ragged head, the sandy hair of which, scrupulously parted on one side, and flattened down with pomatum, was twisted into little semi- circular tails round a flat face ornamented with a pair of small eyes, and garnished with a very dirty shirt collar, and a rusty black stock.\n\n‘Mr. Dodson ain’t at home, and Mr. Fogg’s particularly engaged,’ said the man to whom the head belonged.\n\n‘When will Mr. Dodson be back, sir?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Can’t say.’\n\n‘Will it be long before Mr. Fogg is disengaged, Sir?’\n\n‘Don’t know.’\n\nHere the man proceeded to mend his pen with great deliberation, while another clerk, who was mixing a Seidlitz powder, under cover of the lid of his desk, laughed approvingly.\n\n‘I think I’ll wait,’ said Mr. Pickwick. There was no reply; so Mr. Pickwick sat down unbidden, and listened to the loud ticking of the clock and the murmured conversation of the clerks.\n\n‘That was a game, wasn’t it?’ said one of the gentlemen, in a brown coat and brass buttons, inky drabs, and bluchers, at the conclusion of some inaudible relation of his previous evening’s adventures.\n\n‘Devilish good--devilish good,’ said the Seidlitz-powder man.\n\n‘Tom Cummins was in the chair,’ said the man with the brown coat. ‘It was half-past four when I got to Somers Town, and then I was so uncommon lushy, that I couldn’t find the place where the latch-key went in, and was obliged to knock up the old ‘ooman. I say, I wonder what old Fogg ‘ud say, if he knew it. I should get the sack, I s’pose--eh?’\n\nAt this humorous notion, all the clerks laughed in concert.\n\n‘There was such a game with Fogg here, this mornin’,’ said the man in the brown coat, ‘while Jack was upstairs sorting the papers, and you two were gone to the stamp-office. Fogg was down here, opening the letters when that chap as we issued the writ against at Camberwell, you know, came in--what’s his name again?’\n\n‘Ramsey,’ said the clerk who had spoken to Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Ah, Ramsey--a precious seedy-looking customer. “Well, sir,” says old Fogg, looking at him very fierce--you know his way--“well, Sir, have you come to settle?” “Yes, I have, sir,” said Ramsey, putting his hand in his pocket, and bringing out the money, “the debt’s two pound ten, and the costs three pound five, and here it is, Sir;” and he sighed like bricks, as he lugged out the money, done up in a bit of blotting-paper. Old Fogg looked first at the money, and then at him, and then he coughed in his rum way, so that I knew something was coming. “You don’t know there’s a declaration filed, which increases the costs materially, I suppose,” said Fogg. “You don’t say that, sir,” said Ramsey, starting back; “the time was only out last night, Sir.” “I do say it, though,” said Fogg, “my clerk’s just gone to file it. Hasn’t Mr. Jackson gone to file that declaration in Bullman and Ramsey, Mr. Wicks?” Of course I said yes, and then Fogg coughed again, and looked at Ramsey. “My God!” said Ramsey; “and here have I nearly driven myself mad, scraping this money together, and all to no purpose.” “None at all,” said Fogg coolly; “so you had better go back and scrape some more together, and bring it here in time.” “I can’t get it, by God!” said Ramsey, striking the desk with his fist. “Don’t bully me, sir,” said Fogg, getting into a passion on purpose. “I am not bullying you, sir,” said Ramsey. “You are,” said Fogg; “get out, sir; get out of this office, Sir, and come back, Sir, when you know how to behave yourself.” Well, Ramsey tried to speak, but Fogg wouldn’t let him, so he put the money in his pocket, and sneaked out. The door was scarcely shut, when old Fogg turned round to me, with a sweet smile on his face, and drew the declaration out of his coat pocket. “Here, Wicks,” says Fogg, “take a cab, and go down to the Temple as quick as you can, and file that. The costs are quite safe, for he’s a steady man with a large family, at a salary of five-and-twenty shillings a week, and if he gives us a warrant of attorney, as he must in the end, I know his employers will see it paid; so we may as well get all we can get out of him, Mr. Wicks; it’s a Christian act to do it, Mr. Wicks, for with his large family and small income, he’ll be all the better for a good lesson against getting into debt--won’t he, Mr. Wicks, won’t he?”-- and he smiled so good-naturedly as he went away, that it was delightful to see him. He is a capital man of business,’ said Wicks, in a tone of the deepest admiration, ‘capital, isn’t he?’\n\nThe other three cordially subscribed to this opinion, and the anecdote afforded the most unlimited satisfaction.\n\n‘Nice men these here, Sir,’ whispered Mr. Weller to his master; ‘wery nice notion of fun they has, Sir.’\n\nMr. Pickwick nodded assent, and coughed to attract the attention of the young gentlemen behind the partition, who, having now relaxed their minds by a little conversation among themselves, condescended to take some notice of the stranger.\n\n‘I wonder whether Fogg’s disengaged now?’ said Jackson.\n\n‘I’ll see,’ said Wicks, dismounting leisurely from his stool. ‘What name shall I tell Mr. Fogg?’\n\n‘Pickwick,’ replied the illustrious subject of these memoirs.\n\nMr. Jackson departed upstairs on his errand, and immediately returned with a message that Mr. Fogg would see Mr. Pickwick in five minutes; and having delivered it, returned again to his desk.\n\n‘What did he say his name was?’ whispered Wicks.\n\n‘Pickwick,’ replied Jackson; ‘it’s the defendant in Bardell and Pickwick.’\n\nA sudden scraping of feet, mingled with the sound of suppressed laughter, was heard from behind the partition.\n\n‘They’re a-twiggin’ of you, Sir,’ whispered Mr. Weller.\n\n‘Twigging of me, Sam!’ replied Mr. Pickwick; ‘what do you mean by twigging me?’\n\nMr. Weller replied by pointing with his thumb over his shoulder, and Mr. Pickwick, on looking up, became sensible of the pleasing fact, that all the four clerks, with countenances expressive of the utmost amusement, and with their heads thrust over the wooden screen, were minutely inspecting the figure and general appearance of the supposed trifler with female hearts, and disturber of female happiness. On his looking up, the row of heads suddenly disappeared, and the sound of pens travelling at a furious rate over paper, immediately succeeded.\n\nA sudden ring at the bell which hung in the office, summoned Mr. Jackson to the apartment of Fogg, from whence he came back to say that he (Fogg) was ready to see Mr. Pickwick if he would step upstairs.\n\nUpstairs Mr. Pickwick did step accordingly, leaving Sam Weller below. The room door of the one-pair back, bore inscribed in legible characters the imposing words, ‘Mr. Fogg’; and, having tapped thereat, and been desired to come in, Jackson ushered Mr. Pickwick into the presence.\n\n‘Is Mr. Dodson in?’ inquired Mr. Fogg.\n\n‘Just come in, Sir,’ replied Jackson.\n\n‘Ask him to step here.’\n\n‘Yes, sir.’ Exit Jackson.\n\n‘Take a seat, sir,’ said Fogg; ‘there is the paper, sir; my partner will be here directly, and we can converse about this matter, sir.’\n\nMr. Pickwick took a seat and the paper, but, instead of reading the latter, peeped over the top of it, and took a survey of the man of business, who was an elderly, pimply-faced, vegetable-diet sort of man, in a black coat, dark mixture trousers, and small black gaiters; a kind of being who seemed to be an essential part of the desk at which he was writing, and to have as much thought or feeling.\n\nAfter a few minutes’ silence, Mr. Dodson, a plump, portly, stern-looking man, with a loud voice, appeared; and the conversation commenced.\n\n‘This is Mr. Pickwick,’ said Fogg.\n\n‘Ah! You are the defendant, Sir, in Bardell and Pickwick?’ said Dodson.\n\n‘I am, sir,’ replied Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Well, sir,’ said Dodson, ‘and what do you propose?’\n\n‘Ah!’ said Fogg, thrusting his hands into his trousers’ pockets, and throwing himself back in his chair, ‘what do you propose, Mr Pickwick?’\n\n‘Hush, Fogg,’ said Dodson, ‘let me hear what Mr. Pickwick has to say.’\n\n‘I came, gentlemen,’ said Mr. Pickwick, gazing placidly on the two partners, ‘I came here, gentlemen, to express the surprise with which I received your letter of the other day, and to inquire what grounds of action you can have against me.’\n\n‘Grounds of--’ Fogg had ejaculated this much, when he was stopped by Dodson.\n\n‘Mr. Fogg,’ said Dodson, ‘I am going to speak.’\n\nI beg your pardon, Mr. Dodson,’ said Fogg.\n\n‘For the grounds of action, sir,’ continued Dodson, with moral elevation in his air, ‘you will consult your own conscience and your own feelings. We, Sir, we, are guided entirely by the statement of our client. That statement, Sir, may be true, or it may be false; it may be credible, or it may be incredible; but, if it be true, and if it be credible, I do not hesitate to say, Sir, that our grounds of action, Sir, are strong, and not to be shaken. You may be an unfortunate man, Sir, or you may be a designing one; but if I were called upon, as a juryman upon my oath, Sir, to express an opinion of your conduct, Sir, I do not hesitate to assert that I should have but one opinion about it.’ Here Dodson drew himself up, with an air of offended virtue, and looked at Fogg, who thrust his hands farther in his pockets, and nodding his head sagely, said, in a tone of the fullest concurrence, ‘Most certainly.’\n\n‘Well, Sir,’ said Mr. Pickwick, with considerable pain depicted in his countenance, ‘you will permit me to assure you that I am a most unfortunate man, so far as this case is concerned.’\n\n‘I hope you are, Sir,’ replied Dodson; ‘I trust you may be, Sir. If you are really innocent of what is laid to your charge, you are more unfortunate than I had believed any man could possibly be. What do you say, Mr. Fogg?’\n\n‘I say precisely what you say,’ replied Fogg, with a smile of incredulity.\n\n‘The writ, Sir, which commences the action,’ continued Dodson, ‘was issued regularly. Mr. Fogg, where is the Praecipe book?’\n\n‘Here it is,’ said Fogg, handing over a square book, with a parchment cover.\n\n‘Here is the entry,’ resumed Dodson. ‘“Middlesex, Capias MARTHA BARDELL, WIDOW, v. SAMUEL PICKWICK. Damages £1500. Dodson & Fogg for the plaintiff, Aug. 28, 1827.” All regular, Sir; perfectly.’ Dodson coughed and looked at Fogg, who said ‘Perfectly,’ also. And then they both looked at Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘I am to understand, then,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘that it really is your intention to proceed with this action?’\n\n‘Understand, sir!--that you certainly may,’ replied Dodson, with something as near a smile as his importance would allow.\n\n‘And that the damages are actually laid at fifteen hundred pounds?’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘To which understanding you may add my assurance, that if we could have prevailed upon our client, they would have been laid at treble the amount, sir,’ replied Dodson.\n\n‘I believe Mrs. Bardell specially said, however,’ observed Fogg, glancing at Dodson, ‘that she would not compromise for a farthing less.’\n\n‘Unquestionably,’ replied Dodson sternly. For the action was only just begun; and it wouldn’t have done to let Mr. Pickwick compromise it then, even if he had been so disposed.\n\n‘As you offer no terms, sir,’ said Dodson, displaying a slip of parchment in his right hand, and affectionately pressing a paper copy of it, on Mr. Pickwick with his left, ‘I had better serve you with a copy of this writ, sir. Here is the original, sir.’\n\n‘Very well, gentlemen, very well,’ said Mr. Pickwick, rising in person and wrath at the same time; ‘you shall hear from my solicitor, gentlemen.’\n\n‘We shall be very happy to do so,’ said Fogg, rubbing his hands.\n\n‘Very,’ said Dodson, opening the door.\n\n‘And before I go, gentlemen,’ said the excited Mr. Pickwick, turning round on the landing, ‘permit me to say, that of all the disgraceful and rascally proceedings--’\n\n‘Stay, sir, stay,’ interposed Dodson, with great politeness. ‘Mr. Jackson! Mr. Wicks!’\n\n‘Sir,’ said the two clerks, appearing at the bottom of the stairs.\n\n‘I merely want you to hear what this gentleman says,’ replied Dodson. ‘Pray, go on, sir--disgraceful and rascally proceedings, I think you said?’\n\n‘I did,’ said Mr. Pickwick, thoroughly roused. ‘I said, Sir, that of all the disgraceful and rascally proceedings that ever were attempted, this is the most so. I repeat it, sir.’\n\n‘You hear that, Mr. Wicks,’ said Dodson.\n\n‘You won’t forget these expressions, Mr. Jackson?’ said Fogg.\n\n‘Perhaps you would like to call us swindlers, sir,’ said Dodson. ‘Pray do, Sir, if you feel disposed; now pray do, Sir.’\n\n‘I do,’ said Mr. Pickwick. ‘You are swindlers.’\n\n‘Very good,’ said Dodson. ‘You can hear down there, I hope, Mr. Wicks?’\n\n‘Oh, yes, Sir,’ said Wicks.\n\n‘You had better come up a step or two higher, if you can’t,’ added Mr. Fogg. ‘Go on, Sir; do go on. You had better call us thieves, Sir; or perhaps You would like to assault one of us. Pray do it, Sir, if you would; we will not make the smallest resistance. Pray do it, Sir.’\n\nAs Fogg put himself very temptingly within the reach of Mr. Pickwick’s clenched fist, there is little doubt that that gentleman would have complied with his earnest entreaty, but for the interposition of Sam, who, hearing the dispute, emerged from the office, mounted the stairs, and seized his master by the arm.\n\n‘You just come away,’ said Mr. Weller. ‘Battledore and shuttlecock’s a wery good game, vhen you ain’t the shuttlecock and two lawyers the battledores, in which case it gets too excitin’ to be pleasant. Come avay, Sir. If you want to ease your mind by blowing up somebody, come out into the court and blow up me; but it’s rayther too expensive work to be carried on here.’\n\nAnd without the slightest ceremony, Mr. Weller hauled his master down the stairs, and down the court, and having safely deposited him in Cornhill, fell behind, prepared to follow whithersoever he should lead.\n\nMr. Pickwick walked on abstractedly, crossed opposite the Mansion House, and bent his steps up Cheapside. Sam began to wonder where they were going, when his master turned round, and said--\n\n‘Sam, I will go immediately to Mr. Perker’s.’\n\n‘That’s just exactly the wery place vere you ought to have gone last night, Sir,’ replied Mr. Weller.\n\n‘I think it is, Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘I know it is,’ said Mr. Weller.\n\n‘Well, well, Sam,’ replied Mr. Pickwick, ‘we will go there at once; but first, as I have been rather ruffled, I should like a glass of brandy- and-water warm, Sam. Where can I have it, Sam?’\n\nMr. Weller’s knowledge of London was extensive and peculiar. He replied, without the slightest consideration--\n\n‘Second court on the right hand side--last house but vun on the same side the vay--take the box as stands in the first fireplace, ‘cos there ain’t no leg in the middle o’ the table, which all the others has, and it’s wery inconvenient.’\n\nMr. Pickwick observed his valet’s directions implicitly, and bidding Sam follow him, entered the tavern he had pointed out, where the hot brandy- and-water was speedily placed before him; while Mr. Weller, seated at a respectful distance, though at the same table with his master, was accommodated with a pint of porter.\n\nThe room was one of a very homely description, and was apparently under the especial patronage of stage-coachmen; for several gentleman, who had all the appearance of belonging to that learned profession, were drinking and smoking in the different boxes. Among the number was one stout, red-faced, elderly man, in particular, seated in an opposite box, who attracted Mr. Pickwick’s attention. The stout man was smoking with great vehemence, but between every half-dozen puffs, he took his pipe from his mouth, and looked first at Mr. Weller and then at Mr. Pickwick. Then, he would bury in a quart pot, as much of his countenance as the dimensions of the quart pot admitted of its receiving, and take another look at Sam and Mr. Pickwick. Then he would take another half-dozen puffs with an air of profound meditation and look at them again. At last the stout man, putting up his legs on the seat, and leaning his back against the wall, began to puff at his pipe without leaving off at all, and to stare through the smoke at the new-comers, as if he had made up his mind to see the most he could of them.\n\nAt first the evolutions of the stout man had escaped Mr. Weller’s observation, but by degrees, as he saw Mr. Pickwick’s eyes every now and then turning towards him, he began to gaze in the same direction, at the same time shading his eyes with his hand, as if he partially recognised the object before him, and wished to make quite sure of its identity. His doubts were speedily dispelled, however; for the stout man having blown a thick cloud from his pipe, a hoarse voice, like some strange effort of ventriloquism, emerged from beneath the capacious shawls which muffled his throat and chest, and slowly uttered these sounds--‘Wy, Sammy!’\n\n‘Who’s that, Sam?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Why, I wouldn’t ha’ believed it, Sir,’ replied Mr. Weller, with astonished eyes. ‘It’s the old ‘un.’\n\n‘Old one,’ said Mr. Pickwick. ‘What old one?’\n\n‘My father, sir,’ replied Mr. Weller. ‘How are you, my ancient?’ And with this beautiful ebullition of filial affection, Mr. Weller made room on the seat beside him, for the stout man, who advanced pipe in mouth and pot in hand, to greet him.\n\n‘Wy, Sammy,’ said the father, ‘I ha’n’t seen you, for two year and better.’\n\n‘Nor more you have, old codger,’ replied the son. ‘How’s mother-in-law?’\n\n‘Wy, I’ll tell you what, Sammy,’ said Mr. Weller, senior, with much solemnity in his manner; ‘there never was a nicer woman as a widder, than that ‘ere second wentur o’ mine--a sweet creetur she was, Sammy; all I can say on her now, is, that as she was such an uncommon pleasant widder, it’s a great pity she ever changed her condition. She don’t act as a vife, Sammy.’\n\nDon’t she, though?’ inquired Mr. Weller, junior.\n\nThe elder Mr. Weller shook his head, as he replied with a sigh, ‘I’ve done it once too often, Sammy; I’ve done it once too often. Take example by your father, my boy, and be wery careful o’ widders all your life, ‘specially if they’ve kept a public-house, Sammy.’ Having delivered this parental advice with great pathos, Mr. Weller, senior, refilled his pipe from a tin box he carried in his pocket; and, lighting his fresh pipe from the ashes of the old One, commenced smoking at a great rate.\n\n‘Beg your pardon, sir,’ he said, renewing the subject, and addressing Mr. Pickwick, after a considerable pause, ‘nothin’ personal, I hope, sir; I hope you ha’n’t got a widder, sir.’\n\n‘Not I,’ replied Mr. Pickwick, laughing; and while Mr. Pickwick laughed, Sam Weller informed his parent in a whisper, of the relation in which he stood towards that gentleman.\n\n‘Beg your pardon, sir,’ said Mr. Weller, senior, taking off his hat, ‘I hope you’ve no fault to find with Sammy, Sir?’\n\n‘None whatever,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Wery glad to hear it, sir,’ replied the old man; ‘I took a good deal o’ pains with his eddication, sir; let him run in the streets when he was wery young, and shift for hisself. It’s the only way to make a boy sharp, sir.’\n\n‘Rather a dangerous process, I should imagine,’ said Mr. Pickwick, with a smile.\n\n‘And not a wery sure one, neither,’ added Mr. Weller; ‘I got reg’larly done the other day.’\n\n‘No!’ said his father.\n\n‘I did,’ said the son; and he proceeded to relate, in as few words as possible, how he had fallen a ready dupe to the stratagems of Job Trotter.\n\nMr. Weller, senior, listened to the tale with the most profound attention, and, at its termination, said--\n\n‘Worn’t one o’ these chaps slim and tall, with long hair, and the gift o’ the gab wery gallopin’?’\n\nMr. Pickwick did not quite understand the last item of description, but, comprehending the first, said ‘Yes,’ at a venture.\n\n‘T’ other’s a black-haired chap in mulberry livery, with a wery large head?’\n\n‘Yes, yes, he is,’ said Mr. Pickwick and Sam, with great earnestness.\n\n‘Then I know where they are, and that’s all about it,’ said Mr. Weller; ‘they’re at Ipswich, safe enough, them two.’\n\n‘No!’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Fact,’ said Mr. Weller, ‘and I’ll tell you how I know it. I work an Ipswich coach now and then for a friend o’ mine. I worked down the wery day arter the night as you caught the rheumatic, and at the Black Boy at Chelmsford--the wery place they’d come to--I took ‘em up, right through to Ipswich, where the man-servant--him in the mulberries--told me they was a-goin’ to put up for a long time.’\n\n‘I’ll follow him,’ said Mr. Pickwick; ‘we may as well see Ipswich as any other place. I’ll follow him.’\n\n‘You’re quite certain it was them, governor?’ inquired Mr. Weller, junior.\n\n‘Quite, Sammy, quite,’ replied his father, ‘for their appearance is wery sing’ler; besides that ‘ere, I wondered to see the gen’l’m’n so formiliar with his servant; and, more than that, as they sat in the front, right behind the box, I heerd ‘em laughing and saying how they’d done old Fireworks.’\n\n‘Old who?’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Old Fireworks, Sir; by which, I’ve no doubt, they meant you, Sir.’\n\nThere is nothing positively vile or atrocious in the appellation of ‘old Fireworks,’ but still it is by no means a respectful or flattering designation. The recollection of all the wrongs he had sustained at Jingle’s hands, had crowded on Mr. Pickwick’s mind, the moment Mr. Weller began to speak; it wanted but a feather to turn the scale, and ‘old Fireworks’ did it.\n\n‘I’ll follow him,’ said Mr. Pickwick, with an emphatic blow on the table.\n\n‘I shall work down to Ipswich the day arter to-morrow, Sir,’ said Mr. Weller the elder, ‘from the Bull in Whitechapel; and if you really mean to go, you’d better go with me.’\n\n‘So we had,’ said Mr. Pickwick; ‘very true; I can write to Bury, and tell them to meet me at Ipswich. We will go with you. But don’t hurry away, Mr. Weller; won’t you take anything?’\n\n‘You’re wery good, Sir,’ replied Mr. W., stopping short;--‘perhaps a small glass of brandy to drink your health, and success to Sammy, Sir, wouldn’t be amiss.’\n\n‘Certainly not,’ replied Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘A glass of brandy here!’ The brandy was brought; and Mr. Weller, after pulling his hair to Mr. Pickwick, and nodding to Sam, jerked it down his capacious throat as if it had been a small thimbleful.\n\n‘Well done, father,’ said Sam, ‘take care, old fellow, or you’ll have a touch of your old complaint, the gout.’\n\n‘I’ve found a sov’rin’ cure for that, Sammy,’ said Mr. Weller, setting down the glass.\n\n‘A sovereign cure for the gout,’ said Mr. Pickwick, hastily producing his note-book--‘what is it?’\n\n‘The gout, Sir,’ replied Mr. Weller, ‘the gout is a complaint as arises from too much ease and comfort. If ever you’re attacked with the gout, sir, jist you marry a widder as has got a good loud woice, with a decent notion of usin’ it, and you’ll never have the gout agin. It’s a capital prescription, sir. I takes it reg’lar, and I can warrant it to drive away any illness as is caused by too much jollity.’ Having imparted this valuable secret, Mr. Weller drained his glass once more, produced a laboured wink, sighed deeply, and slowly retired.\n\n‘Well, what do you think of what your father says, Sam?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick, with a smile.\n\n‘Think, Sir!’ replied Mr. Weller; ‘why, I think he’s the wictim o’ connubiality, as Blue Beard’s domestic chaplain said, vith a tear of pity, ven he buried him.’\n\nThere was no replying to this very apposite conclusion, and, therefore, Mr. Pickwick, after settling the reckoning, resumed his walk to Gray’s Inn. By the time he reached its secluded groves, however, eight o’clock had struck, and the unbroken stream of gentlemen in muddy high-lows, soiled white hats, and rusty apparel, who were pouring towards the different avenues of egress, warned him that the majority of the offices had closed for that day.\n\nAfter climbing two pairs of steep and dirty stairs, he found his anticipations were realised. Mr. Perker’s ‘outer door’ was closed; and the dead silence which followed Mr. Weller’s repeated kicks thereat, announced that the officials had retired from business for the night.\n\n‘This is pleasant, Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick; ‘I shouldn’t lose an hour in seeing him; I shall not be able to get one wink of sleep to-night, I know, unless I have the satisfaction of reflecting that I have confided this matter to a professional man.’\n\n‘Here’s an old ‘ooman comin’ upstairs, sir,’ replied Mr. Weller; ‘p’raps she knows where we can find somebody. Hollo, old lady, vere’s Mr. Perker’s people?’\n\n‘Mr. Perker’s people,’ said a thin, miserable-looking old woman, stopping to recover breath after the ascent of the staircase--‘Mr. Perker’s people’s gone, and I’m a-goin’ to do the office out.’\n\nAre you Mr. Perker’s servant?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘I am Mr. Perker’s laundress,’ replied the woman.\n\n‘Ah,’ said Mr. Pickwick, half aside to Sam, ‘it’s a curious circumstance, Sam, that they call the old women in these inns, laundresses. I wonder what’s that for?’\n\n‘’Cos they has a mortal awersion to washing anythin’, I suppose, Sir,’ replied Mr. Weller.\n\n‘I shouldn’t wonder,’ said Mr. Pickwick, looking at the old woman, whose appearance, as well as the condition of the office, which she had by this time opened, indicated a rooted antipathy to the application of soap and water; ‘do you know where I can find Mr. Perker, my good woman?’\n\n‘No, I don’t,’ replied the old woman gruffly; ‘he’s out o’ town now.’\n\n‘That’s unfortunate,’ said Mr. Pickwick; ‘where’s his clerk? Do you know?’\n\n‘Yes, I know where he is, but he won’t thank me for telling you,’ replied the laundress.\n\n‘I have very particular business with him,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Won’t it do in the morning?’ said the woman.\n\n‘Not so well,’ replied Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Well,’ said the old woman, ‘if it was anything very particular, I was to say where he was, so I suppose there’s no harm in telling. If you just go to the Magpie and Stump, and ask at the bar for Mr. Lowten, they’ll show you in to him, and he’s Mr. Perker’s clerk.’\n\nWith this direction, and having been furthermore informed that the hostelry in question was situated in a court, happy in the double advantage of being in the vicinity of Clare Market, and closely approximating to the back of New Inn, Mr. Pickwick and Sam descended the rickety staircase in safety, and issued forth in quest of the Magpie and Stump.\n\nThis favoured tavern, sacred to the evening orgies of Mr. Lowten and his companions, was what ordinary people would designate a public-house. That the landlord was a man of money-making turn was sufficiently testified by the fact of a small bulkhead beneath the tap-room window, in size and shape not unlike a sedan-chair, being underlet to a mender of shoes: and that he was a being of a philanthropic mind was evident from the protection he afforded to a pieman, who vended his delicacies without fear of interruption, on the very door-step. In the lower windows, which were decorated with curtains of a saffron hue, dangled two or three printed cards, bearing reference to Devonshire cider and Dantzic spruce, while a large blackboard, announcing in white letters to an enlightened public, that there were 500,000 barrels of double stout in the cellars of the establishment, left the mind in a state of not unpleasing doubt and uncertainty as to the precise direction in the bowels of the earth, in which this mighty cavern might be supposed to extend. When we add that the weather-beaten signboard bore the half- obliterated semblance of a magpie intently eyeing a crooked streak of brown paint, which the neighbours had been taught from infancy to consider as the ‘stump,’ we have said all that need be said of the exterior of the edifice.\n\nOn Mr. Pickwick’s presenting himself at the bar, an elderly female emerged from behind the screen therein, and presented herself before him.\n\n‘Is Mr. Lowten here, ma’am?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Yes, he is, Sir,’ replied the landlady. ‘Here, Charley, show the gentleman in to Mr. Lowten.’\n\n‘The gen’l’m’n can’t go in just now,’ said a shambling pot-boy, with a red head, ‘cos’ Mr. Lowten’s a-singin’ a comic song, and he’ll put him out. He’ll be done directly, Sir.’\n\nThe red-headed pot-boy had scarcely finished speaking, when a most unanimous hammering of tables, and jingling of glasses, announced that the song had that instant terminated; and Mr. Pickwick, after desiring Sam to solace himself in the tap, suffered himself to be conducted into the presence of Mr. Lowten.\n\nAt the announcement of ‘A gentleman to speak to you, Sir,’ a puffy-faced young man, who filled the chair at the head of the table, looked with some surprise in the direction from whence the voice proceeded; and the surprise seemed to be by no means diminished, when his eyes rested on an individual whom he had never seen before.\n\n‘I beg your pardon, Sir,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘and I am very sorry to disturb the other gentlemen, too, but I come on very particular business; and if you will suffer me to detain you at this end of the room for five minutes, I shall be very much obliged to you.’\n\nThe puffy-faced young man rose, and drawing a chair close to Mr. Pickwick in an obscure corner of the room, listened attentively to his tale of woe.\n\n‘Ah,’ he said, when Mr. Pickwick had concluded, ‘Dodson and Fogg--sharp practice theirs--capital men of business, Dodson and Fogg, sir.’\n\nMr. Pickwick admitted the sharp practice of Dodson and Fogg, and Lowten resumed.\n\n‘Perker ain’t in town, and he won’t be, neither, before the end of next week; but if you want the action defended, and will leave the copy with me, I can do all that’s needful till he comes back.’\n\n‘That’s exactly what I came here for,’ said Mr. Pickwick, handing over the document. ‘If anything particular occurs, you can write to me at the post-office, Ipswich.’\n\n‘That’s all right,’ replied Mr. Perker’s clerk; and then seeing Mr. Pickwick’s eye wandering curiously towards the table, he added, ‘will you join us, for half an hour or so? We are capital company here to- night. There’s Samkin and Green’s managing-clerk, and Smithers and Price’s chancery, and Pimkin and Thomas’s out o’ doors--sings a capital song, he does--and Jack Bamber, and ever so many more. You’re come out of the country, I suppose. Would you like to join us?’\n\nMr. Pickwick could not resist so tempting an opportunity of studying human nature. He suffered himself to be led to the table, where, after having been introduced to the company in due form, he was accommodated with a seat near the chairman and called for a glass of his favourite beverage.\n\nA profound silence, quite contrary to Mr. Pickwick’s expectation, succeeded.\n\n‘You don’t find this sort of thing disagreeable, I hope, sir?’ said his right hand neighbour, a gentleman in a checked shirt and Mosaic studs, with a cigar in his mouth.\n\n‘Not in the least,’ replied Mr. Pickwick; ‘I like it very much, although I am no smoker myself.’\n\n‘I should be very sorry to say I wasn’t,’ interposed another gentleman on the opposite side of the table. ‘It’s board and lodgings to me, is smoke.’\n\nMr. Pickwick glanced at the speaker, and thought that if it were washing too, it would be all the better.\n\nHere there was another pause. Mr. Pickwick was a stranger, and his coming had evidently cast a damp upon the party.\n\n‘Mr. Grundy’s going to oblige the company with a song,’ said the chairman.\n\n‘No, he ain’t,’ said Mr. Grundy.\n\n‘Why not?’ said the chairman.\n\n‘Because he can’t,’ said Mr. Grundy.\n\n‘You had better say he won’t,’ replied the chairman.\n\n‘Well, then, he won’t,’ retorted Mr. Grundy. Mr. Grundy’s positive refusal to gratify the company occasioned another silence.\n\n‘Won’t anybody enliven us?’ said the chairman, despondingly.\n\n‘Why don’t you enliven us yourself, Mr. Chairman?’ said a young man with a whisker, a squint, and an open shirt collar (dirty), from the bottom of the table.\n\n‘Hear! hear!’ said the smoking gentleman, in the Mosaic jewellery.\n\n‘Because I only know one song, and I have sung it already, and it’s a fine of “glasses round” to sing the same song twice in a night,’ replied the chairman.\n\nThis was an unanswerable reply, and silence prevailed again.\n\n‘I have been to-night, gentlemen,’ said Mr. Pickwick, hoping to start a subject which all the company could take a part in discussing, ‘I have been to-night, in a place which you all know very well, doubtless, but which I have not been in for some years, and know very little of; I mean Gray’s Inn, gentlemen. Curious little nooks in a great place, like London, these old inns are.’\n\n‘By Jove!’ said the chairman, whispering across the table to Mr. Pickwick, ‘you have hit upon something that one of us, at least, would talk upon for ever. You’ll draw old Jack Bamber out; he was never heard to talk about anything else but the inns, and he has lived alone in them till he’s half crazy.’\n\nThe individual to whom Lowten alluded, was a little, yellow, high- shouldered man, whose countenance, from his habit of stooping forward when silent, Mr. Pickwick had not observed before. He wondered, though, when the old man raised his shrivelled face, and bent his gray eye upon him, with a keen inquiring look, that such remarkable features could have escaped his attention for a moment. There was a fixed grim smile perpetually on his countenance; he leaned his chin on a long, skinny hand, with nails of extraordinary length; and as he inclined his head to one side, and looked keenly out from beneath his ragged gray eyebrows, there was a strange, wild slyness in his leer, quite repulsive to behold.\n\nThis was the figure that now started forward, and burst into an animated torrent of words. As this chapter has been a long one, however, and as the old man was a remarkable personage, it will be more respectful to him, and more convenient to us, to let him speak for himself in a fresh one.\n\nCHAPTER XXI. IN WHICH THE OLD MAN LAUNCHES FORTH INTO HIS FAVOURITE THEME, AND RELATES A STORY ABOUT A QUEER CLIENT\n\n‘Aha!’ said the old man, a brief description of whose manner and appearance concluded the last chapter, ‘aha! who was talking about the inns?’\n\n‘I was, Sir,’ replied Mr. Pickwick--‘I was observing what singular old places they are.’\n‘You!’ said the old man contemptuously. ‘What do you know of the time when young men shut themselves up in those lonely rooms, and read and read, hour after hour, and night after night, till their reason wandered beneath their midnight studies; till their mental powers were exhausted; till morning’s light brought no freshness or health to them; and they sank beneath the unnatural devotion of their youthful energies to their dry old books? Coming down to a later time, and a very different day, what do you know of the gradual sinking beneath consumption, or the quick wasting of fever--the grand results of “life” and dissipation--which men have undergone in these same rooms? How many vain pleaders for mercy, do you think, have turned away heart-sick from the lawyer’s office, to find a resting-place in the Thames, or a refuge in the jail? They are no ordinary houses, those. There is not a panel in the old wainscotting, but what, if it were endowed with the powers of speech and memory, could start from the wall, and tell its tale of horror--the romance of life, Sir, the romance of life! Common-place as they may seem now, I tell you they are strange old places, and I would rather hear many a legend with a terrific-sounding name, than the true history of one old set of chambers.’\n\nThere was something so odd in the old man’s sudden energy, and the subject which had called it forth, that Mr. Pickwick was prepared with no observation in reply; and the old man checking his impetuosity, and resuming the leer, which had disappeared during his previous excitement, said--\n\n‘Look at them in another light--their most common-place and least romantic. What fine places of slow torture they are! Think of the needy man who has spent his all, beggared himself, and pinched his friends, to enter the profession, which is destined never to yield him a morsel of bread. The waiting--the hope--the disappointment--the fear--the misery-- the poverty--the blight on his hopes, and end to his career--the suicide perhaps, or the shabby, slipshod drunkard. Am I not right about them?’ And the old man rubbed his hands, and leered as if in delight at having found another point of view in which to place his favourite subject.\n\nMr. Pickwick eyed the old man with great curiosity, and the remainder of the company smiled, and looked on in silence.\n\n‘Talk of your German universities,’ said the little old man. ‘Pooh, pooh! there’s romance enough at home without going half a mile for it; only people never think of it.’\n\n‘I never thought of the romance of this particular subject before, certainly,’ said Mr. Pickwick, laughing.\n\n‘To be sure you didn’t,’ said the little old man; ‘of course not. As a friend of mine used to say to me, “What is there in chambers in particular?” “Queer old places,” said I. “Not at all,” said he. “Lonely,” said I. “Not a bit of it,” said he. He died one morning of apoplexy, as he was going to open his outer door. Fell with his head in his own letter-box, and there he lay for eighteen months. Everybody thought he’d gone out of town.’\n\n‘And how was he found out at last?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘The benchers determined to have his door broken open, as he hadn’t paid any rent for two years. So they did. Forced the lock; and a very dusty skeleton in a blue coat, black knee-shorts, and silks, fell forward in the arms of the porter who opened the door. Queer, that. Rather, perhaps; rather, eh?’ The little old man put his head more on one side, and rubbed his hands with unspeakable glee.\n\n‘I know another case,’ said the little old man, when his chuckles had in some degree subsided. ‘It occurred in Clifford’s Inn. Tenant of a top set--bad character--shut himself up in his bedroom closet, and took a dose of arsenic. The steward thought he had run away: opened the door, and put a bill up. Another man came, took the chambers, furnished them, and went to live there. Somehow or other he couldn’t sleep--always restless and uncomfortable. “Odd,” says he. “I’ll make the other room my bedchamber, and this my sitting-room.” He made the change, and slept very well at night, but suddenly found that, somehow, he couldn’t read in the evening: he got nervous and uncomfortable, and used to be always snuffing his candles and staring about him. “I can’t make this out,” said he, when he came home from the play one night, and was drinking a glass of cold grog, with his back to the wall, in order that he mightn’t be able to fancy there was any one behind him--“I can’t make it out,” said he; and just then his eyes rested on the little closet that had been always locked up, and a shudder ran through his whole frame from top to toe. “I have felt this strange feeling before,” said he, “I cannot help thinking there’s something wrong about that closet.” He made a strong effort, plucked up his courage, shivered the lock with a blow or two of the poker, opened the door, and there, sure enough, standing bolt upright in the corner, was the last tenant, with a little bottle clasped firmly in his hand, and his face--well!’ As the little old man concluded, he looked round on the attentive faces of his wondering auditory with a smile of grim delight.\n\n‘What strange things these are you tell us of, Sir,’ said Mr. Pickwick, minutely scanning the old man’s countenance, by the aid of his glasses.\n\n‘Strange!’ said the little old man. ‘Nonsense; you think them strange, because you know nothing about it. They are funny, but not uncommon.’\n\n‘Funny!’ exclaimed Mr. Pickwick involuntarily.\n\n‘Yes, funny, are they not?’ replied the little old man, with a diabolical leer; and then, without pausing for an answer, he continued--\n\n‘I knew another man--let me see--forty years ago now--who took an old, damp, rotten set of chambers, in one of the most ancient inns, that had been shut up and empty for years and years before. There were lots of old women’s stories about the place, and it certainly was very far from being a cheerful one; but he was poor, and the rooms were cheap, and that would have been quite a sufficient reason for him, if they had been ten times worse than they really were. He was obliged to take some mouldering fixtures that were on the place, and, among the rest, was a great lumbering wooden press for papers, with large glass doors, and a green curtain inside; a pretty useless thing for him, for he had no papers to put in it; and as to his clothes, he carried them about with him, and that wasn’t very hard work, either. Well, he had moved in all his furniture--it wasn’t quite a truck-full--and had sprinkled it about the room, so as to make the four chairs look as much like a dozen as possible, and was sitting down before the fire at night, drinking the first glass of two gallons of whisky he had ordered on credit, wondering whether it would ever be paid for, and if so, in how many years’ time, when his eyes encountered the glass doors of the wooden press. “Ah,” says he, “if I hadn’t been obliged to take that ugly article at the old broker’s valuation, I might have got something comfortable for the money. I’ll tell you what it is, old fellow,” he said, speaking aloud to the press, having nothing else to speak to, “if it wouldn’t cost more to break up your old carcass, than it would ever be worth afterward, I’d have a fire out of you in less than no time.” He had hardly spoken the words, when a sound resembling a faint groan, appeared to issue from the interior of the case. It startled him at first, but thinking, on a moment’s reflection, that it must be some young fellow in the next chamber, who had been dining out, he put his feet on the fender, and raised the poker to stir the fire. At that moment, the sound was repeated; and one of the glass doors slowly opening, disclosed a pale and emaciated figure in soiled and worn apparel, standing erect in the press. The figure was tall and thin, and the countenance expressive of care and anxiety; but there was something in the hue of the skin, and gaunt and unearthly appearance of the whole form, which no being of this world was ever seen to wear. “Who are you?” said the new tenant, turning very pale; poising the poker in his hand, however, and taking a very decent aim at the countenance of the figure. “Who are you?” “Don’t throw that poker at me,” replied the form; “if you hurled it with ever so sure an aim, it would pass through me, without resistance, and expend its force on the wood behind. I am a spirit.” “And pray, what do you want here?” faltered the tenant. “In this room,” replied the apparition, “my worldly ruin was worked, and I and my children beggared. In this press, the papers in a long, long suit, which accumulated for years, were deposited. In this room, when I had died of grief, and long-deferred hope, two wily harpies divided the wealth for which I had contested during a wretched existence, and of which, at last, not one farthing was left for my unhappy descendants. I terrified them from the spot, and since that day have prowled by night--the only period at which I can revisit the earth--about the scenes of my long-protracted misery. This apartment is mine: leave it to me.” “If you insist upon making your appearance here,” said the tenant, who had had time to collect his presence of mind during this prosy statement of the ghost’s, “I shall give up possession with the greatest pleasure; but I should like to ask you one question, if you will allow me.” “Say on,” said the apparition sternly. “Well,” said the tenant, “I don’t apply the observation personally to you, because it is equally applicable to most of the ghosts I ever heard of; but it does appear to me somewhat inconsistent, that when you have an opportunity of visiting the fairest spots of earth--for I suppose space is nothing to you--you should always return exactly to the very places where you have been most miserable.” “Egad, that’s very true; I never thought of that before,” said the ghost. “You see, Sir,” pursued the tenant, “this is a very uncomfortable room. From the appearance of that press, I should be disposed to say that it is not wholly free from bugs; and I really think you might find much more comfortable quarters: to say nothing of the climate of London, which is extremely disagreeable.” “You are very right, Sir,” said the ghost politely, “it never struck me till now; I’ll try change of air directly”--and, in fact, he began to vanish as he spoke; his legs, indeed, had quite disappeared. “And if, Sir,” said the tenant, calling after him, “if you would have the goodness to suggest to the other ladies and gentlemen who are now engaged in haunting old empty houses, that they might be much more comfortable elsewhere, you will confer a very great benefit on society.” “I will,” replied the ghost; “we must be dull fellows--very dull fellows, indeed; I can’t imagine how we can have been so stupid.” With these words, the spirit disappeared; and what is rather remarkable,’ added the old man, with a shrewd look round the table, ‘he never came back again.’\n\n‘That ain’t bad, if it’s true,’ said the man in the Mosaic studs, lighting a fresh cigar.\n\n‘If!’ exclaimed the old man, with a look of excessive contempt. ‘I suppose,’ he added, turning to Lowten, ‘he’ll say next, that my story about the queer client we had, when I was in an attorney’s office, is not true either--I shouldn’t wonder.’\n\n‘I shan’t venture to say anything at all about it, seeing that I never heard the story,’ observed the owner of the Mosaic decorations.\n\n‘I wish you would repeat it, Sir,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Ah, do,’ said Lowten, ‘nobody has heard it but me, and I have nearly forgotten it.’\n\nThe old man looked round the table, and leered more horribly than ever, as if in triumph, at the attention which was depicted in every face. Then rubbing his chin with his hand, and looking up to the ceiling as if to recall the circumstances to his memory, he began as follows:--\n\n# THE OLD MAN’S TALE ABOUT THE QUEER CLIENT\n\n‘It matters little,’ said the old man, ‘where, or how, I picked up this brief history. If I were to relate it in the order in which it reached me, I should commence in the middle, and when I had arrived at the conclusion, go back for a beginning. It is enough for me to say that some of its circumstances passed before my own eyes; for the remainder I know them to have happened, and there are some persons yet living, who will remember them but too well.\n\n‘In the Borough High Street, near St. George’s Church, and on the same side of the way, stands, as most people know, the smallest of our debtors’ prisons, the Marshalsea. Although in later times it has been a very different place from the sink of filth and dirt it once was, even its improved condition holds out but little temptation to the extravagant, or consolation to the improvident. The condemned felon has as good a yard for air and exercise in Newgate, as the insolvent debtor in the Marshalsea Prison. [Better. But this is past, in a better age, and the prison exists no longer.]\n\n‘It may be my fancy, or it may be that I cannot separate the place from the old recollections associated with it, but this part of London I cannot bear. The street is broad, the shops are spacious, the noise of passing vehicles, the footsteps of a perpetual stream of people--all the busy sounds of traffic, resound in it from morn to midnight; but the streets around are mean and close; poverty and debauchery lie festering in the crowded alleys; want and misfortune are pent up in the narrow prison; an air of gloom and dreariness seems, in my eyes at least, to hang about the scene, and to impart to it a squalid and sickly hue.\n\n‘Many eyes, that have long since been closed in the grave, have looked round upon that scene lightly enough, when entering the gate of the old Marshalsea Prison for the first time; for despair seldom comes with the first severe shock of misfortune. A man has confidence in untried friends, he remembers the many offers of service so freely made by his boon companions when he wanted them not; he has hope--the hope of happy inexperience--and however he may bend beneath the first shock, it springs up in his bosom, and flourishes there for a brief space, until it droops beneath the blight of disappointment and neglect. How soon have those same eyes, deeply sunken in the head, glared from faces wasted with famine, and sallow from confinement, in days when it was no figure of speech to say that debtors rotted in prison, with no hope of release, and no prospect of liberty! The atrocity in its full extent no longer exists, but there is enough of it left to give rise to occurrences that make the heart bleed.\n\n‘Twenty years ago, that pavement was worn with the footsteps of a mother and child, who, day by day, so surely as the morning came, presented themselves at the prison gate; often after a night of restless misery and anxious thoughts, were they there, a full hour too soon, and then the young mother turning meekly away, would lead the child to the old bridge, and raising him in her arms to show him the glistening water, tinted with the light of the morning’s sun, and stirring with all the bustling preparations for business and pleasure that the river presented at that early hour, endeavour to interest his thoughts in the objects before him. But she would quickly set him down, and hiding her face in her shawl, give vent to the tears that blinded her; for no expression of interest or amusement lighted up his thin and sickly face. His recollections were few enough, but they were all of one kind--all connected with the poverty and misery of his parents. Hour after hour had he sat on his mother’s knee, and with childish sympathy watched the tears that stole down her face, and then crept quietly away into some dark corner, and sobbed himself to sleep. The hard realities of the world, with many of its worst privations--hunger and thirst, and cold and want--had all come home to him, from the first dawnings of reason; and though the form of childhood was there, its light heart, its merry laugh, and sparkling eyes were wanting.\n\n‘The father and mother looked on upon this, and upon each other, with thoughts of agony they dared not breathe in words. The healthy, strong- made man, who could have borne almost any fatigue of active exertion, was wasting beneath the close confinement and unhealthy atmosphere of a crowded prison. The slight and delicate woman was sinking beneath the combined effects of bodily and mental illness. The child’s young heart was breaking.\n\n‘Winter came, and with it weeks of cold and heavy rain. The poor girl had removed to a wretched apartment close to the spot of her husband’s imprisonment; and though the change had been rendered necessary by their increasing poverty, she was happier now, for she was nearer him. For two months, she and her little companion watched the opening of the gate as usual. One day she failed to come, for the first time. Another morning arrived, and she came alone. The child was dead.\n\n‘They little know, who coldly talk of the poor man’s bereavements, as a happy release from pain to the departed, and a merciful relief from expense to the survivor--they little know, I say, what the agony of those bereavements is. A silent look of affection and regard when all other eyes are turned coldly away--the consciousness that we possess the sympathy and affection of one being when all others have deserted us--is a hold, a stay, a comfort, in the deepest affliction, which no wealth could purchase, or power bestow. The child had sat at his parents’ feet for hours together, with his little hands patiently folded in each other, and his thin wan face raised towards them. They had seen him pine away, from day to day; and though his brief existence had been a joyless one, and he was now removed to that peace and rest which, child as he was, he had never known in this world, they were his parents, and his loss sank deep into their souls.\n\n‘It was plain to those who looked upon the mother’s altered face, that death must soon close the scene of her adversity and trial. Her husband’s fellow-prisoners shrank from obtruding on his grief and misery, and left to himself alone, the small room he had previously occupied in common with two companions. She shared it with him; and lingering on without pain, but without hope, her life ebbed slowly away.\n\n‘She had fainted one evening in her husband’s arms, and he had borne her to the open window, to revive her with the air, when the light of the moon falling full upon her face, showed him a change upon her features, which made him stagger beneath her weight, like a helpless infant.\n\n‘“Set me down, George,” she said faintly. He did so, and seating himself beside her, covered his face with his hands, and burst into tears.\n\n‘“It is very hard to leave you, George,” she said; “but it is God’s will, and you must bear it for my sake. Oh! how I thank Him for having taken our boy! He is happy, and in heaven now. What would he have done here, without his mother!”\n\n‘“You shall not die, Mary, you shall not die;” said the husband, starting up. He paced hurriedly to and fro, striking his head with his clenched fists; then reseating himself beside her, and supporting her in his arms, added more calmly, “Rouse yourself, my dear girl. Pray, pray do. You will revive yet.”\n\n‘“Never again, George; never again,” said the dying woman. “Let them lay me by my poor boy now, but promise me, that if ever you leave this dreadful place, and should grow rich, you will have us removed to some quiet country churchyard, a long, long way off--very far from here-- where we can rest in peace. Dear George, promise me you will.”\n\n‘“I do, I do,” said the man, throwing himself passionately on his knees before her. “Speak to me, Mary, another word; one look--but one!”\n\n‘He ceased to speak: for the arm that clasped his neck grew stiff and heavy. A deep sigh escaped from the wasted form before him; the lips moved, and a smile played upon the face; but the lips were pallid, and the smile faded into a rigid and ghastly stare. He was alone in the world.\n\n‘That night, in the silence and desolation of his miserable room, the wretched man knelt down by the dead body of his wife, and called on God to witness a terrible oath, that from that hour, he devoted himself to revenge her death and that of his child; that thenceforth to the last moment of his life, his whole energies should be directed to this one object; that his revenge should be protracted and terrible; that his hatred should be undying and inextinguishable; and should hunt its object through the world.\n\n‘The deepest despair, and passion scarcely human, had made such fierce ravages on his face and form, in that one night, that his companions in misfortune shrank affrighted from him as he passed by. His eyes were bloodshot and heavy, his face a deadly white, and his body bent as if with age. He had bitten his under lip nearly through in the violence of his mental suffering, and the blood which had flowed from the wound had trickled down his chin, and stained his shirt and neckerchief. No tear, or sound of complaint escaped him; but the unsettled look, and disordered haste with which he paced up and down the yard, denoted the fever which was burning within.\n\n‘It was necessary that his wife’s body should be removed from the prison, without delay. He received the communication with perfect calmness, and acquiesced in its propriety. Nearly all the inmates of the prison had assembled to witness its removal; they fell back on either side when the widower appeared; he walked hurriedly forward, and stationed himself, alone, in a little railed area close to the lodge gate, from whence the crowd, with an instinctive feeling of delicacy, had retired. The rude coffin was borne slowly forward on men’s shoulders. A dead silence pervaded the throng, broken only by the audible lamentations of the women, and the shuffling steps of the bearers on the stone pavement. They reached the spot where the bereaved husband stood: and stopped. He laid his hand upon the coffin, and mechanically adjusting the pall with which it was covered, motioned them onward. The turnkeys in the prison lobby took off their hats as it passed through, and in another moment the heavy gate closed behind it. He looked vacantly upon the crowd, and fell heavily to the ground.\n\n‘Although for many weeks after this, he was watched, night and day, in the wildest ravings of fever, neither the consciousness of his loss, nor the recollection of the vow he had made, ever left him for a moment. Scenes changed before his eyes, place succeeded place, and event followed event, in all the hurry of delirium; but they were all connected in some way with the great object of his mind. He was sailing over a boundless expanse of sea, with a blood-red sky above, and the angry waters, lashed into fury beneath, boiling and eddying up, on every side. There was another vessel before them, toiling and labouring in the howling storm; her canvas fluttering in ribbons from the mast, and her deck thronged with figures who were lashed to the sides, over which huge waves every instant burst, sweeping away some devoted creatures into the foaming sea. Onward they bore, amidst the roaring mass of water, with a speed and force which nothing could resist; and striking the stem of the foremost vessel, crushed her beneath their keel. From the huge whirlpool which the sinking wreck occasioned, arose a shriek so loud and shrill-- the death-cry of a hundred drowning creatures, blended into one fierce yell--that it rung far above the war-cry of the elements, and echoed, and re-echoed till it seemed to pierce air, sky, and ocean. But what was that--that old gray head that rose above the water’s surface, and with looks of agony, and screams for aid, buffeted with the waves! One look, and he had sprung from the vessel’s side, and with vigorous strokes was swimming towards it. He reached it; he was close upon it. They were his features. The old man saw him coming, and vainly strove to elude his grasp. But he clasped him tight, and dragged him beneath the water. Down, down with him, fifty fathoms down; his struggles grew fainter and fainter, until they wholly ceased. He was dead; he had killed him, and had kept his oath.\n\n‘He was traversing the scorching sands of a mighty desert, barefoot and alone. The sand choked and blinded him; its fine thin grains entered the very pores of his skin, and irritated him almost to madness. Gigantic masses of the same material, carried forward by the wind, and shone through by the burning sun, stalked in the distance like pillars of living fire. The bones of men, who had perished in the dreary waste, lay scattered at his feet; a fearful light fell on everything around; so far as the eye could reach, nothing but objects of dread and horror presented themselves. Vainly striving to utter a cry of terror, with his tongue cleaving to his mouth, he rushed madly forward. Armed with supernatural strength, he waded through the sand, until, exhausted with fatigue and thirst, he fell senseless on the earth. What fragrant coolness revived him; what gushing sound was that? Water! It was indeed a well; and the clear fresh stream was running at his feet. He drank deeply of it, and throwing his aching limbs upon the bank, sank into a delicious trance. The sound of approaching footsteps roused him. An old gray-headed man tottered forward to slake his burning thirst. It was he again! He wound his arms round the old man’s body, and held him back. He struggled, and shrieked for water--for but one drop of water to save his life! But he held the old man firmly, and watched his agonies with greedy eyes; and when his lifeless head fell forward on his bosom, he rolled the corpse from him with his feet.\n\n‘When the fever left him, and consciousness returned, he awoke to find himself rich and free, to hear that the parent who would have let him die in jail--would! who had let those who were far dearer to him than his own existence die of want, and sickness of heart that medicine cannot cure--had been found dead in his bed of down. He had had all the heart to leave his son a beggar, but proud even of his health and strength, had put off the act till it was too late, and now might gnash his teeth in the other world, at the thought of the wealth his remissness had left him. He awoke to this, and he awoke to more. To recollect the purpose for which he lived, and to remember that his enemy was his wife’s own father--the man who had cast him into prison, and who, when his daughter and her child sued at his feet for mercy, had spurned them from his door. Oh, how he cursed the weakness that prevented him from being up, and active, in his scheme of vengeance!\n\n‘He caused himself to be carried from the scene of his loss and misery, and conveyed to a quiet residence on the sea-coast; not in the hope of recovering his peace of mind or happiness, for both were fled for ever; but to restore his prostrate energies, and meditate on his darling object. And here, some evil spirit cast in his way the opportunity for his first, most horrible revenge.\n\n‘It was summer-time; and wrapped in his gloomy thoughts, he would issue from his solitary lodgings early in the evening, and wandering along a narrow path beneath the cliffs, to a wild and lonely spot that had struck his fancy in his ramblings, seat himself on some fallen fragment of the rock, and burying his face in his hands, remain there for hours-- sometimes until night had completely closed in, and the long shadows of the frowning cliffs above his head cast a thick, black darkness on every object near him.\n\n‘He was seated here, one calm evening, in his old position, now and then raising his head to watch the flight of a sea-gull, or carry his eye along the glorious crimson path, which, commencing in the middle of the ocean, seemed to lead to its very verge where the sun was setting, when the profound stillness of the spot was broken by a loud cry for help; he listened, doubtful of his having heard aright, when the cry was repeated with even greater vehemence than before, and, starting to his feet, he hastened in the direction whence it proceeded.\n\n‘The tale told itself at once: some scattered garments lay on the beach; a human head was just visible above the waves at a little distance from the shore; and an old man, wringing his hands in agony, was running to and fro, shrieking for assistance. The invalid, whose strength was now sufficiently restored, threw off his coat, and rushed towards the sea, with the intention of plunging in, and dragging the drowning man ashore.\n\n‘“Hasten here, Sir, in God’s name; help, help, sir, for the love of Heaven. He is my son, Sir, my only son!” said the old man frantically, as he advanced to meet him. “My only son, Sir, and he is dying before his father’s eyes!”\n\n‘At the first word the old man uttered, the stranger checked himself in his career, and, folding his arms, stood perfectly motionless.\n\n‘“Great God!” exclaimed the old man, recoiling, “Heyling!”\n\n‘The stranger smiled, and was silent.\n\n‘“Heyling!” said the old man wildly; “my boy, Heyling, my dear boy, look, look!” Gasping for breath, the miserable father pointed to the spot where the young man was struggling for life.\n\n‘“Hark!” said the old man. “He cries once more. He is alive yet. Heyling, save him, save him!”\n\n‘The stranger smiled again, and remained immovable as a statue.\n\n‘“I have wronged you,” shrieked the old man, falling on his knees, and clasping his hands together. “Be revenged; take my all, my life; cast me into the water at your feet, and, if human nature can repress a struggle, I will die, without stirring hand or foot. Do it, Heyling, do it, but save my boy; he is so young, Heyling, so young to die!”\n\n‘“Listen,” said the stranger, grasping the old man fiercely by the wrist; “I will have life for life, and here is one. My child died, before his father’s eyes, a far more agonising and painful death than that young slanderer of his sister’s worth is meeting while I speak. You laughed--laughed in your daughter’s face, where death had already set his hand--at our sufferings, then. What think you of them now! See there, see there!”\n\n‘As the stranger spoke, he pointed to the sea. A faint cry died away upon its surface; the last powerful struggle of the dying man agitated the rippling waves for a few seconds; and the spot where he had gone down into his early grave, was undistinguishable from the surrounding water.\n\n‘Three years had elapsed, when a gentleman alighted from a private carriage at the door of a London attorney, then well known as a man of no great nicety in his professional dealings, and requested a private interview on business of importance. Although evidently not past the prime of life, his face was pale, haggard, and dejected; and it did not require the acute perception of the man of business, to discern at a glance, that disease or suffering had done more to work a change in his appearance, than the mere hand of time could have accomplished in twice the period of his whole life.\n\n‘“I wish you to undertake some legal business for me,” said the stranger.\n\n‘The attorney bowed obsequiously, and glanced at a large packet which the gentleman carried in his hand. His visitor observed the look, and proceeded.\n\n‘“It is no common business,” said he; “nor have these papers reached my hands without long trouble and great expense.”\n\n‘The attorney cast a still more anxious look at the packet; and his visitor, untying the string that bound it, disclosed a quantity of promissory notes, with copies of deeds, and other documents.\n\n‘“Upon these papers,” said the client, “the man whose name they bear, has raised, as you will see, large sums of money, for years past. There was a tacit understanding between him and the men into whose hands they originally went--and from whom I have by degrees purchased the whole, for treble and quadruple their nominal value--that these loans should be from time to time renewed, until a given period had elapsed. Such an understanding is nowhere expressed. He has sustained many losses of late; and these obligations accumulating upon him at once, would crush him to the earth.”\n\n‘“The whole amount is many thousands of pounds,” said the attorney, looking over the papers.\n\n‘“It is,” said the client.\n\n‘“What are we to do?” inquired the man of business.\n\n‘“Do!” replied the client, with sudden vehemence. “Put every engine of the law in force, every trick that ingenuity can devise and rascality execute; fair means and foul; the open oppression of the law, aided by all the craft of its most ingenious practitioners. I would have him die a harassing and lingering death. Ruin him, seize and sell his lands and goods, drive him from house and home, and drag him forth a beggar in his old age, to die in a common jail.”\n\n‘“But the costs, my dear Sir, the costs of all this,” reasoned the attorney, when he had recovered from his momentary surprise. “If the defendant be a man of straw, who is to pay the costs, Sir?”\n\n‘“Name any sum,” said the stranger, his hand trembling so violently with excitement, that he could scarcely hold the pen he seized as he spoke-- “any sum, and it is yours. Don’t be afraid to name it, man. I shall not think it dear, if you gain my object.”\n\n‘The attorney named a large sum, at hazard, as the advance he should require to secure himself against the possibility of loss; but more with the view of ascertaining how far his client was really disposed to go, than with any idea that he would comply with the demand. The stranger wrote a cheque upon his banker, for the whole amount, and left him.\n\n‘The draft was duly honoured, and the attorney, finding that his strange client might be safely relied upon, commenced his work in earnest. For more than two years afterwards, Mr. Heyling would sit whole days together, in the office, poring over the papers as they accumulated, and reading again and again, his eyes gleaming with joy, the letters of remonstrance, the prayers for a little delay, the representations of the certain ruin in which the opposite party must be involved, which poured in, as suit after suit, and process after process, was commenced. To all applications for a brief indulgence, there was but one reply--the money must be paid. Land, house, furniture, each in its turn, was taken under some one of the numerous executions which were issued; and the old man himself would have been immured in prison had he not escaped the vigilance of the officers, and fled.\n\n‘The implacable animosity of Heyling, so far from being satiated by the success of his persecution, increased a hundredfold with the ruin he inflicted. On being informed of the old man’s flight, his fury was unbounded. He gnashed his teeth with rage, tore the hair from his head, and assailed with horrid imprecations the men who had been intrusted with the writ. He was only restored to comparative calmness by repeated assurances of the certainty of discovering the fugitive. Agents were sent in quest of him, in all directions; every stratagem that could be invented was resorted to, for the purpose of discovering his place of retreat; but it was all in vain. Half a year had passed over, and he was still undiscovered.\n\n‘At length late one night, Heyling, of whom nothing had been seen for many weeks before, appeared at his attorney’s private residence, and sent up word that a gentleman wished to see him instantly. Before the attorney, who had recognised his voice from above stairs, could order the servant to admit him, he had rushed up the staircase, and entered the drawing-room pale and breathless. Having closed the door, to prevent being overheard, he sank into a chair, and said, in a low voice--\n\n‘“Hush! I have found him at last.”\n\n‘“No!” said the attorney. “Well done, my dear sir, well done.”\n\n‘“He lies concealed in a wretched lodging in Camden Town,” said Heyling. “Perhaps it is as well we did lose sight of him, for he has been living alone there, in the most abject misery, all the time, and he is poor--very poor.”\n\n‘“Very good,” said the attorney. “You will have the caption made to- morrow, of course?”\n\n‘“Yes,” replied Heyling. “Stay! No! The next day. You are surprised at my wishing to postpone it,” he added, with a ghastly smile; “but I had forgotten. The next day is an anniversary in his life: let it be done then.”\n\n‘“Very good,” said the attorney. “Will you write down instructions for the officer?”\n\n‘“No; let him meet me here, at eight in the evening, and I will accompany him myself.”\n\n‘They met on the appointed night, and, hiring a hackney-coach, directed the driver to stop at that corner of the old Pancras Road, at which stands the parish workhouse. By the time they alighted there, it was quite dark; and, proceeding by the dead wall in front of the Veterinary Hospital, they entered a small by-street, which is, or was at that time, called Little College Street, and which, whatever it may be now, was in those days a desolate place enough, surrounded by little else than fields and ditches.\n\n‘Having drawn the travelling-cap he had on half over his face, and muffled himself in his cloak, Heyling stopped before the meanest-looking house in the street, and knocked gently at the door. It was at once opened by a woman, who dropped a curtsey of recognition, and Heyling, whispering the officer to remain below, crept gently upstairs, and, opening the door of the front room, entered at once.\n\n‘The object of his search and his unrelenting animosity, now a decrepit old man, was seated at a bare deal table, on which stood a miserable candle. He started on the entrance of the stranger, and rose feebly to his feet.\n\n‘“What now, what now?” said the old man. “What fresh misery is this? What do you want here?”\n\n‘“A word with you,” replied Heyling. As he spoke, he seated himself at the other end of the table, and, throwing off his cloak and cap, disclosed his features.\n\n‘The old man seemed instantly deprived of speech. He fell backward in his chair, and, clasping his hands together, gazed on the apparition with a mingled look of abhorrence and fear.\n\n‘“This day six years,” said Heyling, “I claimed the life you owed me for my child’s. Beside the lifeless form of your daughter, old man, I swore to live a life of revenge. I have never swerved from my purpose for a moment’s space; but if I had, one thought of her uncomplaining, suffering look, as she drooped away, or of the starving face of our innocent child, would have nerved me to my task. My first act of requital you well remember: this is my last.”\n\n‘The old man shivered, and his hands dropped powerless by his side.\n\n‘“I leave England to-morrow,” said Heyling, after a moment’s pause. “To- night I consign you to the living death to which you devoted her--a hopeless prison--”\n\n‘He raised his eyes to the old man’s countenance, and paused. He lifted the light to his face, set it gently down, and left the apartment.\n\n‘“You had better see to the old man,” he said to the woman, as he opened the door, and motioned the officer to follow him into the street. “I think he is ill.” The woman closed the door, ran hastily upstairs, and found him lifeless.\n\n‘Beneath a plain gravestone, in one of the most peaceful and secluded churchyards in Kent, where wild flowers mingle with the grass, and the soft landscape around forms the fairest spot in the garden of England, lie the bones of the young mother and her gentle child. But the ashes of the father do not mingle with theirs; nor, from that night forward, did the attorney ever gain the remotest clue to the subsequent history of his queer client.’\n\nAs the old man concluded his tale, he advanced to a peg in one corner, and taking down his hat and coat, put them on with great deliberation; and, without saying another word, walked slowly away. As the gentleman with the Mosaic studs had fallen asleep, and the major part of the company were deeply occupied in the humorous process of dropping melted tallow-grease into his brandy-and-water, Mr. Pickwick departed unnoticed, and having settled his own score, and that of Mr. Weller, issued forth, in company with that gentleman, from beneath the portal of the Magpie and Stump.\n\nCHAPTER XXII. MR. PICKWICK JOURNEYS TO IPSWICH AND MEETS WITH A ROMANTIC ADVENTURE WITH A MIDDLE-AGED LADY IN YELLOW CURL-PAPERS\n\nThat ‘ere your governor’s luggage, Sammy?’ inquired Mr. Weller of his affectionate son, as he entered the yard of the Bull Inn, Whitechapel, with a travelling-bag and a small portmanteau.\n‘You might ha’ made a worser guess than that, old feller,’ replied Mr. Weller the younger, setting down his burden in the yard, and sitting himself down upon it afterwards. ‘The governor hisself’ll be down here presently.’\n\n‘He’s a-cabbin’ it, I suppose?’ said the father.\n\n‘Yes, he’s a havin’ two mile o’ danger at eight-pence,’ responded the son. ‘How’s mother-in-law this mornin’?’\n\n‘Queer, Sammy, queer,’ replied the elder Mr. Weller, with impressive gravity. ‘She’s been gettin’ rayther in the Methodistical order lately, Sammy; and she is uncommon pious, to be sure. She’s too good a creetur for me, Sammy. I feel I don’t deserve her.’\n\n‘Ah,’ said Mr. Samuel. ‘that’s wery self-denyin’ o’ you.’\n\n‘Wery,’ replied his parent, with a sigh. ‘She’s got hold o’ some inwention for grown-up people being born again, Sammy--the new birth, I think they calls it. I should wery much like to see that system in haction, Sammy. I should wery much like to see your mother-in-law born again. Wouldn’t I put her out to nurse!’\n\n‘What do you think them women does t’other day,’ continued Mr. Weller, after a short pause, during which he had significantly struck the side of his nose with his forefinger some half-dozen times. ‘What do you think they does, t’other day, Sammy?’\n\n‘Don’t know,’ replied Sam, ‘what?’\n\n‘Goes and gets up a grand tea drinkin’ for a feller they calls their shepherd,’ said Mr. Weller. ‘I was a-standing starin’ in at the pictur shop down at our place, when I sees a little bill about it; “tickets half-a-crown. All applications to be made to the committee. Secretary, Mrs. Weller”; and when I got home there was the committee a-sittin’ in our back parlour. Fourteen women; I wish you could ha’ heard ‘em, Sammy. There they was, a-passin’ resolutions, and wotin’ supplies, and all sorts o’ games. Well, what with your mother-in-law a-worrying me to go, and what with my looking for’ard to seein’ some queer starts if I did, I put my name down for a ticket; at six o’clock on the Friday evenin’ I dresses myself out wery smart, and off I goes with the old ‘ooman, and up we walks into a fust-floor where there was tea-things for thirty, and a whole lot o’ women as begins whisperin’ to one another, and lookin’ at me, as if they’d never seen a rayther stout gen’l’m’n of eight-and-fifty afore. By and by, there comes a great bustle downstairs, and a lanky chap with a red nose and a white neckcloth rushes up, and sings out, “Here’s the shepherd a-coming to wisit his faithful flock;” and in comes a fat chap in black, vith a great white face, a-smilin’ avay like clockwork. Such goin’s on, Sammy! “The kiss of peace,” says the shepherd; and then he kissed the women all round, and ven he’d done, the man vith the red nose began. I was just a-thinkin’ whether I hadn’t better begin too--‘specially as there was a wery nice lady a-sittin’ next me--ven in comes the tea, and your mother-in-law, as had been makin’ the kettle bile downstairs. At it they went, tooth and nail. Such a precious loud hymn, Sammy, while the tea was a brewing; such a grace, such eatin’ and drinkin’! I wish you could ha’ seen the shepherd walkin’ into the ham and muffins. I never see such a chap to eat and drink-- never. The red-nosed man warn’t by no means the sort of person you’d like to grub by contract, but he was nothin’ to the shepherd. Well; arter the tea was over, they sang another hymn, and then the shepherd began to preach: and wery well he did it, considerin’ how heavy them muffins must have lied on his chest. Presently he pulls up, all of a sudden, and hollers out, “Where is the sinner; where is the mis’rable sinner?” Upon which, all the women looked at me, and began to groan as if they was a-dying. I thought it was rather sing’ler, but howsoever, I says nothing. Presently he pulls up again, and lookin’ wery hard at me, says, “Where is the sinner; where is the mis’rable sinner?” and all the women groans again, ten times louder than afore. I got rather savage at this, so I takes a step or two for’ard and says, “My friend,” says I, “did you apply that ‘ere obserwation to me?” ‘Stead of beggin’ my pardon as any gen’l’m’n would ha’ done, he got more abusive than ever:--called me a wessel, Sammy--a wessel of wrath--and all sorts o’ names. So my blood being reg’larly up, I first gave him two or three for himself, and then two or three more to hand over to the man with the red nose, and walked off. I wish you could ha’ heard how the women screamed, Sammy, ven they picked up the shepherd from underneath the table--Hollo! here’s the governor, the size of life.’\n\nAs Mr. Weller spoke, Mr. Pickwick dismounted from a cab, and entered the yard.\n\n‘Fine mornin’, Sir,’ said Mr. Weller, senior.\n\n‘Beautiful indeed,’ replied Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Beautiful indeed,’ echoes a red-haired man with an inquisitive nose and green spectacles, who had unpacked himself from a cab at the same moment as Mr. Pickwick. ‘Going to Ipswich, Sir?’\n\n‘I am,’ replied Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Extraordinary coincidence. So am I.’\n\nMr. Pickwick bowed.\n\n‘Going outside?’ said the red-haired man.\n\nMr. Pickwick bowed again.\n\n‘Bless my soul, how remarkable--I am going outside, too,’ said the red- haired man; ‘we are positively going together.’ And the red-haired man, who was an important-looking, sharp-nosed, mysterious-spoken personage, with a bird-like habit of giving his head a jerk every time he said anything, smiled as if he had made one of the strangest discoveries that ever fell to the lot of human wisdom.\n\n‘I am happy in the prospect of your company, Sir,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Ah,’ said the new-comer, ‘it’s a good thing for both of us, isn’t it? Company, you see--company--is--is--it’s a very different thing from solitude--ain’t it?’\n\n‘There’s no denying that ‘ere,’ said Mr. Weller, joining in the conversation, with an affable smile. ‘That’s what I call a self-evident proposition, as the dog’s-meat man said, when the housemaid told him he warn’t a gentleman.’\n\n‘Ah,’ said the red-haired man, surveying Mr. Weller from head to foot with a supercilious look. ‘Friend of yours, sir?’\n\n‘Not exactly a friend,’ replied Mr. Pickwick, in a low tone. ‘The fact is, he is my servant, but I allow him to take a good many liberties; for, between ourselves, I flatter myself he is an original, and I am rather proud of him.’\n\n‘Ah,’ said the red-haired man, ‘that, you see, is a matter of taste. I am not fond of anything original; I don’t like it; don’t see the necessity for it. What’s your name, sir?’\n\n‘Here is my card, sir,’ replied Mr. Pickwick, much amused by the abruptness of the question, and the singular manner of the stranger.\n\n‘Ah,’ said the red-haired man, placing the card in his pocket-book, ‘Pickwick; very good. I like to know a man’s name, it saves so much trouble. That’s my card, sir. Magnus, you will perceive, sir--Magnus is my name. It’s rather a good name, I think, sir.’\n\n‘A very good name, indeed,’ said Mr. Pickwick, wholly unable to repress a smile.\n\n‘Yes, I think it is,’ resumed Mr. Magnus. ‘There’s a good name before it, too, you will observe. Permit me, sir--if you hold the card a little slanting, this way, you catch the light upon the up-stroke. There--Peter Magnus--sounds well, I think, sir.’\n\n‘Very,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Curious circumstance about those initials, sir,’ said Mr. Magnus. ‘You will observe--P.M.--post meridian. In hasty notes to intimate acquaintance, I sometimes sign myself “Afternoon.” It amuses my friends very much, Mr. Pickwick.’\n\n‘It is calculated to afford them the highest gratification, I should conceive,’ said Mr. Pickwick, rather envying the ease with which Mr. Magnus’s friends were entertained.\n\n‘Now, gen’l’m’n,’ said the hostler, ‘coach is ready, if you please.’\n\n‘Is all my luggage in?’ inquired Mr. Magnus.\n\n‘All right, sir.’\n\n‘Is the red bag in?’\n\n‘All right, Sir.’\n\n‘And the striped bag?’\n\n‘Fore boot, Sir.’\n\n‘And the brown-paper parcel?’\n\n‘Under the seat, Sir.’\n\n‘And the leather hat-box?’\n\n‘They’re all in, Sir.’\n\n‘Now, will you get up?’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Excuse me,’ replied Magnus, standing on the wheel. ‘Excuse me, Mr. Pickwick. I cannot consent to get up, in this state of uncertainty. I am quite satisfied from that man’s manner, that the leather hat-box is not in.’\n\nThe solemn protestations of the hostler being wholly unavailing, the leather hat-box was obliged to be raked up from the lowest depth of the boot, to satisfy him that it had been safely packed; and after he had been assured on this head, he felt a solemn presentiment, first, that the red bag was mislaid, and next that the striped bag had been stolen, and then that the brown-paper parcel ‘had come untied.’ At length when he had received ocular demonstration of the groundless nature of each and every of these suspicions, he consented to climb up to the roof of the coach, observing that now he had taken everything off his mind, he felt quite comfortable and happy.\n\n‘You’re given to nervousness, ain’t you, Sir?’ inquired Mr. Weller, senior, eyeing the stranger askance, as he mounted to his place.\n\n‘Yes; I always am rather about these little matters,’ said the stranger, ‘but I am all right now--quite right.’\n\n‘Well, that’s a blessin’, said Mr. Weller. ‘Sammy, help your master up to the box; t’other leg, Sir, that’s it; give us your hand, Sir. Up with you. You was a lighter weight when you was a boy, sir.’\n\nTrue enough, that, Mr. Weller,’ said the breathless Mr. Pickwick good- humouredly, as he took his seat on the box beside him.\n\n‘Jump up in front, Sammy,’ said Mr. Weller. ‘Now Villam, run ‘em out. Take care o’ the archvay, gen’l’m’n. “Heads,” as the pieman says. That’ll do, Villam. Let ‘em alone.’ And away went the coach up Whitechapel, to the admiration of the whole population of that pretty densely populated quarter.\n\n‘Not a wery nice neighbourhood, this, Sir,’ said Sam, with a touch of the hat, which always preceded his entering into conversation with his master.\n\n‘It is not indeed, Sam,’ replied Mr. Pickwick, surveying the crowded and filthy street through which they were passing.\n\n‘It’s a wery remarkable circumstance, Sir,’ said Sam, ‘that poverty and oysters always seem to go together.’\n\n‘I don’t understand you, Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘What I mean, sir,’ said Sam, ‘is, that the poorer a place is, the greater call there seems to be for oysters. Look here, sir; here’s a oyster-stall to every half-dozen houses. The street’s lined vith ‘em. Blessed if I don’t think that ven a man’s wery poor, he rushes out of his lodgings, and eats oysters in reg’lar desperation.’\n\n‘To be sure he does,’ said Mr. Weller, senior; ‘and it’s just the same vith pickled salmon!’\n\n‘Those are two very remarkable facts, which never occurred to me before,’ said Mr. Pickwick. ‘The very first place we stop at, I’ll make a note of them.’\n\nBy this time they had reached the turnpike at Mile End; a profound silence prevailed until they had got two or three miles farther on, when Mr. Weller, senior, turning suddenly to Mr. Pickwick, said--\n\n‘Wery queer life is a pike-keeper’s, sir.’\n\n‘A what?’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘A pike-keeper.’\n\n‘What do you mean by a pike-keeper?’ inquired Mr. Peter Magnus.\n\n‘The old ‘un means a turnpike-keeper, gen’l’m’n,’ observed Mr. Samuel Weller, in explanation.\n\n‘Oh,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘I see. Yes; very curious life. Very uncomfortable.’\n\n‘They’re all on ‘em men as has met vith some disappointment in life,’ said Mr. Weller, senior.\n\n‘Ay, ay,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Yes. Consequence of vich, they retires from the world, and shuts themselves up in pikes; partly with the view of being solitary, and partly to rewenge themselves on mankind by takin’ tolls.’\n\n‘Dear me,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘I never knew that before.’\n\n‘Fact, Sir,’ said Mr. Weller; ‘if they was gen’l’m’n, you’d call ‘em misanthropes, but as it is, they only takes to pike-keepin’.’\n\nWith such conversation, possessing the inestimable charm of blending amusement with instruction, did Mr. Weller beguile the tediousness of the journey, during the greater part of the day. Topics of conversation were never wanting, for even when any pause occurred in Mr. Weller’s loquacity, it was abundantly supplied by the desire evinced by Mr. Magnus to make himself acquainted with the whole of the personal history of his fellow-travellers, and his loudly-expressed anxiety at every stage, respecting the safety and well-being of the two bags, the leather hat-box, and the brown-paper parcel.\n\nIn the main street of Ipswich, on the left-hand side of the way, a short distance after you have passed through the open space fronting the Town Hall, stands an inn known far and wide by the appellation of the Great White Horse, rendered the more conspicuous by a stone statue of some rampacious animal with flowing mane and tail, distantly resembling an insane cart-horse, which is elevated above the principal door. The Great White Horse is famous in the neighbourhood, in the same degree as a prize ox, or a county-paper-chronicled turnip, or unwieldy pig--for its enormous size. Never was such labyrinths of uncarpeted passages, such clusters of mouldy, ill-lighted rooms, such huge numbers of small dens for eating or sleeping in, beneath any one roof, as are collected together between the four walls of the Great White Horse at Ipswich.\n\nIt was at the door of this overgrown tavern that the London coach stopped, at the same hour every evening; and it was from this same London coach that Mr. Pickwick, Sam Weller, and Mr. Peter Magnus dismounted, on the particular evening to which this chapter of our history bears reference.\n\n‘Do you stop here, sir?’ inquired Mr. Peter Magnus, when the striped bag, and the red bag, and the brown-paper parcel, and the leather hat- box, had all been deposited in the passage. ‘Do you stop here, sir?’\n\n‘I do,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Dear me,’ said Mr. Magnus, ‘I never knew anything like these extraordinary coincidences. Why, I stop here too. I hope we dine together?’\n\n‘With pleasure,’ replied Mr. Pickwick. ‘I am not quite certain whether I have any friends here or not, though. Is there any gentleman of the name of Tupman here, waiter?’\n\nA corpulent man, with a fortnight’s napkin under his arm, and coeval stockings on his legs, slowly desisted from his occupation of staring down the street, on this question being put to him by Mr. Pickwick; and, after minutely inspecting that gentleman’s appearance, from the crown of his hat to the lowest button of his gaiters, replied emphatically--\n\n‘Nor any gentleman of the name of Snodgrass?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘No!’\n\n‘Nor Winkle?’\n\n‘No!’\n‘My friends have not arrived to-day, Sir,’ said Mr. Pickwick. ‘We will dine alone, then. Show us a private room, waiter.’\n\nOn this request being preferred, the corpulent man condescended to order the boots to bring in the gentlemen’s luggage; and preceding them down a long, dark passage, ushered them into a large, badly-furnished apartment, with a dirty grate, in which a small fire was making a wretched attempt to be cheerful, but was fast sinking beneath the dispiriting influence of the place. After the lapse of an hour, a bit of fish and a steak was served up to the travellers, and when the dinner was cleared away, Mr. Pickwick and Mr. Peter Magnus drew their chairs up to the fire, and having ordered a bottle of the worst possible port wine, at the highest possible price, for the good of the house, drank brandy-and-water for their own.\n\nMr. Peter Magnus was naturally of a very communicative disposition, and the brandy-and-water operated with wonderful effect in warming into life the deepest hidden secrets of his bosom. After sundry accounts of himself, his family, his connections, his friends, his jokes, his business, and his brothers (most talkative men have a great deal to say about their brothers), Mr. Peter Magnus took a view of Mr. Pickwick through his coloured spectacles for several minutes, and then said, with an air of modesty--\n\n‘And what do you think--what do you think, Mr. Pickwick--I have come down here for?’\n\n‘Upon my word,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘it is wholly impossible for me to guess; on business, perhaps.’\n\n‘Partly right, Sir,’ replied Mr. Peter Magnus, ‘but partly wrong at the same time; try again, Mr. Pickwick.’\n\n‘Really,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘I must throw myself on your mercy, to tell me or not, as you may think best; for I should never guess, if I were to try all night.’\n\n‘Why, then, he-he-he!’ said Mr. Peter Magnus, with a bashful titter, ‘what should you think, Mr. Pickwick, if I had come down here to make a proposal, Sir, eh? He, he, he!’\n\n‘Think! That you are very likely to succeed,’ replied Mr. Pickwick, with one of his beaming smiles.\n\n‘Ah!’ said Mr. Magnus. ‘But do you really think so, Mr. Pickwick? Do you, though?’\n\n‘Certainly,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘No; but you’re joking, though.’\n\n‘I am not, indeed.’\n\n‘Why, then,’ said Mr. Magnus, ‘to let you into a little secret, I think so too. I don’t mind telling you, Mr. Pickwick, although I’m dreadful jealous by nature--horrid--that the lady is in this house.’ Here Mr. Magnus took off his spectacles, on purpose to wink, and then put them on again.\n\n‘That’s what you were running out of the room for, before dinner, then, so often,’ said Mr. Pickwick archly.\n\n‘Hush! Yes, you’re right, that was it; not such a fool as to see her, though.’\n‘No!’\n\n‘No; wouldn’t do, you know, after having just come off a journey. Wait till to-morrow, sir; double the chance then. Mr. Pickwick, Sir, there is a suit of clothes in that bag, and a hat in that box, which, I expect, in the effect they will produce, will be invaluable to me, sir.’\n\n‘Indeed!’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Yes; you must have observed my anxiety about them to-day. I do not believe that such another suit of clothes, and such a hat, could be bought for money, Mr. Pickwick.’\n\nMr. Pickwick congratulated the fortunate owner of the irresistible garments on their acquisition; and Mr. Peter Magnus remained a few moments apparently absorbed in contemplation.\n\n‘She’s a fine creature,’ said Mr. Magnus.\n\n‘Is she?’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Very,’ said Mr. Magnus. ‘Very. She lives about twenty miles from here, Mr. Pickwick. I heard she would be here to-night and all to-morrow forenoon, and came down to seize the opportunity. I think an inn is a good sort of a place to propose to a single woman in, Mr. Pickwick. She is more likely to feel the loneliness of her situation in travelling, perhaps, than she would be at home. What do you think, Mr. Pickwick?’\n\n‘I think it is very probable,’ replied that gentleman.\n\n‘I beg your pardon, Mr. Pickwick,’ said Mr. Peter Magnus, ‘but I am naturally rather curious; what may you have come down here for?’\n\n‘On a far less pleasant errand, Sir,’ replied Mr. Pickwick, the colour mounting to his face at the recollection. ‘I have come down here, Sir, to expose the treachery and falsehood of an individual, upon whose truth and honour I placed implicit reliance.’\n\n‘Dear me,’ said Mr. Peter Magnus, ‘that’s very unpleasant. It is a lady, I presume? Eh? ah! Sly, Mr. Pickwick, sly. Well, Mr. Pickwick, sir, I wouldn’t probe your feelings for the world. Painful subjects, these, sir, very painful. Don’t mind me, Mr. Pickwick, if you wish to give vent to your feelings. I know what it is to be jilted, Sir; I have endured that sort of thing three or four times.’\n\n‘I am much obliged to you, for your condolence on what you presume to be my melancholy case,’ said Mr. Pickwick, winding up his watch, and laying it on the table, ‘but--’\n\n‘No, no,’ said Mr. Peter Magnus, ‘not a word more; it’s a painful subject. I see, I see. What’s the time, Mr. Pickwick?’\n\nPast twelve.’\n\n‘Dear me, it’s time to go to bed. It will never do, sitting here. I shall be pale to-morrow, Mr. Pickwick.’\n\nAt the bare notion of such a calamity, Mr. Peter Magnus rang the bell for the chambermaid; and the striped bag, the red bag, the leathern hat- box, and the brown-paper parcel, having been conveyed to his bedroom, he retired in company with a japanned candlestick, to one side of the house, while Mr. Pickwick, and another japanned candlestick, were conducted through a multitude of tortuous windings, to another.\n\n‘This is your room, sir,’ said the chambermaid.\n\n‘Very well,’ replied Mr. Pickwick, looking round him. It was a tolerably large double-bedded room, with a fire; upon the whole, a more comfortable-looking apartment than Mr. Pickwick’s short experience of the accommodations of the Great White Horse had led him to expect.\n\n‘Nobody sleeps in the other bed, of course,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Very good. Tell my servant to bring me up some hot water at half-past eight in the morning, and that I shall not want him any more to-night.’\n\n‘Yes, Sir,’ and bidding Mr. Pickwick good-night, the chambermaid retired, and left him alone.\n\nMr. Pickwick sat himself down in a chair before the fire, and fell into a train of rambling meditations. First he thought of his friends, and wondered when they would join him; then his mind reverted to Mrs. Martha Bardell; and from that lady it wandered, by a natural process, to the dingy counting-house of Dodson & Fogg. From Dodson & Fogg’s it flew off at a tangent, to the very centre of the history of the queer client; and then it came back to the Great White Horse at Ipswich, with sufficient clearness to convince Mr. Pickwick that he was falling asleep. So he roused himself, and began to undress, when he recollected he had left his watch on the table downstairs.\n\nNow this watch was a special favourite with Mr. Pickwick, having been carried about, beneath the shadow of his waistcoat, for a greater number of years than we feel called upon to state at present. The possibility of going to sleep, unless it were ticking gently beneath his pillow, or in the watch-pocket over his head, had never entered Mr. Pickwick’s brain. So as it was pretty late now, and he was unwilling to ring his bell at that hour of the night, he slipped on his coat, of which he had just divested himself, and taking the japanned candlestick in his hand, walked quietly downstairs.\n\nThe more stairs Mr. Pickwick went down, the more stairs there seemed to be to descend, and again and again, when Mr. Pickwick got into some narrow passage, and began to congratulate himself on having gained the ground-floor, did another flight of stairs appear before his astonished eyes. At last he reached a stone hall, which he remembered to have seen when he entered the house. Passage after passage did he explore; room after room did he peep into; at length, as he was on the point of giving up the search in despair, he opened the door of the identical room in which he had spent the evening, and beheld his missing property on the table.\n\nMr. Pickwick seized the watch in triumph, and proceeded to retrace his steps to his bedchamber. If his progress downward had been attended with difficulties and uncertainty, his journey back was infinitely more perplexing. Rows of doors, garnished with boots of every shape, make, and size, branched off in every possible direction. A dozen times did he softly turn the handle of some bedroom door which resembled his own, when a gruff cry from within of ‘Who the devil’s that?’ or ‘What do you want here?’ caused him to steal away, on tiptoe, with a perfectly marvellous celerity. He was reduced to the verge of despair, when an open door attracted his attention. He peeped in. Right at last! There were the two beds, whose situation he perfectly remembered, and the fire still burning. His candle, not a long one when he first received it, had flickered away in the drafts of air through which he had passed and sank into the socket as he closed the door after him. ‘No matter,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘I can undress myself just as well by the light of the fire.’\n\nThe bedsteads stood one on each side of the door; and on the inner side of each was a little path, terminating in a rush-bottomed chair, just wide enough to admit of a person’s getting into or out of bed, on that side, if he or she thought proper. Having carefully drawn the curtains of his bed on the outside, Mr. Pickwick sat down on the rush-bottomed chair, and leisurely divested himself of his shoes and gaiters. He then took off and folded up his coat, waistcoat, and neckcloth, and slowly drawing on his tasselled nightcap, secured it firmly on his head, by tying beneath his chin the strings which he always had attached to that article of dress. It was at this moment that the absurdity of his recent bewilderment struck upon his mind. Throwing himself back in the rush- bottomed chair, Mr. Pickwick laughed to himself so heartily, that it would have been quite delightful to any man of well-constituted mind to have watched the smiles that expanded his amiable features as they shone forth from beneath the nightcap.\n\n‘It is the best idea,’ said Mr. Pickwick to himself, smiling till he almost cracked the nightcap strings--‘it is the best idea, my losing myself in this place, and wandering about these staircases, that I ever heard of. Droll, droll, very droll.’ Here Mr. Pickwick smiled again, a broader smile than before, and was about to continue the process of undressing, in the best possible humour, when he was suddenly stopped by a most unexpected interruption: to wit, the entrance into the room of some person with a candle, who, after locking the door, advanced to the dressing-table, and set down the light upon it.\n\nThe smile that played on Mr. Pickwick’s features was instantaneously lost in a look of the most unbounded and wonder-stricken surprise. The person, whoever it was, had come in so suddenly and with so little noise, that Mr. Pickwick had had no time to call out, or oppose their entrance. Who could it be? A robber? Some evil-minded person who had seen him come upstairs with a handsome watch in his hand, perhaps. What was he to do?\n\nThe only way in which Mr. Pickwick could catch a glimpse of his mysterious visitor with the least danger of being seen himself, was by creeping on to the bed, and peeping out from between the curtains on the opposite side. To this manoeuvre he accordingly resorted. Keeping the curtains carefully closed with his hand, so that nothing more of him could be seen than his face and nightcap, and putting on his spectacles, he mustered up courage and looked out.\n\nMr. Pickwick almost fainted with horror and dismay. Standing before the dressing-glass was a middle-aged lady, in yellow curl-papers, busily engaged in brushing what ladies call their ‘back-hair.’ However the unconscious middle-aged lady came into that room, it was quite clear that she contemplated remaining there for the night; for she had brought a rushlight and shade with her, which, with praiseworthy precaution against fire, she had stationed in a basin on the floor, where it was glimmering away, like a gigantic lighthouse in a particularly small piece of water.\n\n‘Bless my soul!’ thought Mr. Pickwick, ‘what a dreadful thing!’\n\n‘Hem!’ said the lady; and in went Mr. Pickwick’s head with automaton- like rapidity.\n\n‘I never met with anything so awful as this,’ thought poor Mr. Pickwick, the cold perspiration starting in drops upon his nightcap. ‘Never. This is fearful.’\n\nIt was quite impossible to resist the urgent desire to see what was going forward. So out went Mr. Pickwick’s head again. The prospect was worse than before. The middle-aged lady had finished arranging her hair; had carefully enveloped it in a muslin nightcap with a small plaited border; and was gazing pensively on the fire.\n\n‘This matter is growing alarming,’ reasoned Mr. Pickwick with himself. ‘I can’t allow things to go on in this way. By the self-possession of that lady, it is clear to me that I must have come into the wrong room. If I call out she’ll alarm the house; but if I remain here the consequences will be still more frightful.’\n\nMr. Pickwick, it is quite unnecessary to say, was one of the most modest and delicate-minded of mortals. The very idea of exhibiting his nightcap to a lady overpowered him, but he had tied those confounded strings in a knot, and, do what he would, he couldn’t get it off. The disclosure must be made. There was only one other way of doing it. He shrunk behind the curtains, and called out very loudly--\n\n‘Ha-hum!’\n\nThat the lady started at this unexpected sound was evident, by her falling up against the rushlight shade; that she persuaded herself it must have been the effect of imagination was equally clear, for when Mr. Pickwick, under the impression that she had fainted away stone-dead with fright, ventured to peep out again, she was gazing pensively on the fire as before.\n\n‘Most extraordinary female this,’ thought Mr. Pickwick, popping in again. ‘Ha-hum!’\n\nThese last sounds, so like those in which, as legends inform us, the ferocious giant Blunderbore was in the habit of expressing his opinion that it was time to lay the cloth, were too distinctly audible to be again mistaken for the workings of fancy.\n\n‘Gracious Heaven!’ said the middle-aged lady, ‘what’s that?’\n\n‘It’s--it’s--only a gentleman, ma’am,’ said Mr. Pickwick, from behind the curtains.\n\n‘A gentleman!’ said the lady, with a terrific scream.\n\n‘It’s all over!’ thought Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘A strange man!’ shrieked the lady. Another instant and the house would be alarmed. Her garments rustled as she rushed towards the door.\n\n‘Ma’am,’ said Mr. Pickwick, thrusting out his head in the extremity of his desperation, ‘ma’am!’\n\nNow, although Mr. Pickwick was not actuated by any definite object in putting out his head, it was instantaneously productive of a good effect. The lady, as we have already stated, was near the door. She must pass it, to reach the staircase, and she would most undoubtedly have done so by this time, had not the sudden apparition of Mr. Pickwick’s nightcap driven her back into the remotest corner of the apartment, where she stood staring wildly at Mr. Pickwick, while Mr. Pickwick in his turn stared wildly at her.\n\n‘Wretch,’ said the lady, covering her eyes with her hands, ‘what do you want here?’\n\n‘Nothing, ma’am; nothing whatever, ma’am,’ said Mr. Pickwick earnestly.\n\n‘Nothing!’ said the lady, looking up.\n\n‘Nothing, ma’am, upon my honour,’ said Mr. Pickwick, nodding his head so energetically, that the tassel of his nightcap danced again. ‘I am almost ready to sink, ma’am, beneath the confusion of addressing a lady in my nightcap (here the lady hastily snatched off hers), but I can’t get it off, ma’am (here Mr. Pickwick gave it a tremendous tug, in proof of the statement). It is evident to me, ma’am, now, that I have mistaken this bedroom for my own. I had not been here five minutes, ma’am, when you suddenly entered it.’\n\n‘If this improbable story be really true, Sir,’ said the lady, sobbing violently, ‘you will leave it instantly.’\n\n‘I will, ma’am, with the greatest pleasure,’ replied Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Instantly, sir,’ said the lady.\n\n‘Certainly, ma’am,’ interposed Mr. Pickwick, very quickly. ‘Certainly, ma’am. I--I--am very sorry, ma’am,’ said Mr. Pickwick, making his appearance at the bottom of the bed, ‘to have been the innocent occasion of this alarm and emotion; deeply sorry, ma’am.’\n\nThe lady pointed to the door. One excellent quality of Mr. Pickwick’s character was beautifully displayed at this moment, under the most trying circumstances. Although he had hastily put on his hat over his nightcap, after the manner of the old patrol; although he carried his shoes and gaiters in his hand, and his coat and waistcoat over his arm; nothing could subdue his native politeness.\n\n‘I am exceedingly sorry, ma’am,’ said Mr. Pickwick, bowing very low.\n\n‘If you are, Sir, you will at once leave the room,’ said the lady.\n\n‘Immediately, ma’am; this instant, ma’am,’ said Mr. Pickwick, opening the door, and dropping both his shoes with a crash in so doing.\n\n‘I trust, ma’am,’ resumed Mr. Pickwick, gathering up his shoes, and turning round to bow again--‘I trust, ma’am, that my unblemished character, and the devoted respect I entertain for your sex, will plead as some slight excuse for this--’ But before Mr. Pickwick could conclude the sentence, the lady had thrust him into the passage, and locked and bolted the door behind him.\n\nWhatever grounds of self-congratulation Mr. Pickwick might have for having escaped so quietly from his late awkward situation, his present position was by no means enviable. He was alone, in an open passage, in a strange house in the middle of the night, half dressed; it was not to be supposed that he could find his way in perfect darkness to a room which he had been wholly unable to discover with a light, and if he made the slightest noise in his fruitless attempts to do so, he stood every chance of being shot at, and perhaps killed, by some wakeful traveller. He had no resource but to remain where he was until daylight appeared. So after groping his way a few paces down the passage, and, to his infinite alarm, stumbling over several pairs of boots in so doing, Mr. Pickwick crouched into a little recess in the wall, to wait for morning, as philosophically as he might.\n\nHe was not destined, however, to undergo this additional trial of patience; for he had not been long ensconced in his present concealment when, to his unspeakable horror, a man, bearing a light, appeared at the end of the passage. His horror was suddenly converted into joy, however, when he recognised the form of his faithful attendant. It was indeed Mr. Samuel Weller, who after sitting up thus late, in conversation with the boots, who was sitting up for the mail, was now about to retire to rest.\n\n‘Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick, suddenly appearing before him, ‘where’s my bedroom?’\n\nMr. Weller stared at his master with the most emphatic surprise; and it was not until the question had been repeated three several times, that he turned round, and led the way to the long-sought apartment.\n\n‘Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick, as he got into bed, ‘I have made one of the most extraordinary mistakes to-night, that ever were heard of.’\n\n‘Wery likely, Sir,’ replied Mr. Weller drily.\n\n‘But of this I am determined, Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick; ‘that if I were to stop in this house for six months, I would never trust myself about it, alone, again.’\n\n‘That’s the wery prudentest resolution as you could come to, Sir,’ replied Mr. Weller. ‘You rayther want somebody to look arter you, Sir, when your judgment goes out a wisitin’.’\n\n‘What do you mean by that, Sam?’ said Mr. Pickwick. He raised himself in bed, and extended his hand, as if he were about to say something more; but suddenly checking himself, turned round, and bade his valet ‘Good- night.’\n\n‘Good-night, Sir,’ replied Mr. Weller. He paused when he got outside the door--shook his head--walked on--stopped--snuffed the candle--shook his head again--and finally proceeded slowly to his chamber, apparently buried in the profoundest meditation.\n\nCHAPTER XXIII. IN WHICH MR. SAMUEL WELLER BEGINS TO DEVOTE HIS ENERGIES TO THE RETURN MATCH BETWEEN HIMSELF AND MR. TROTTER\nIn a small room in the vicinity of the stableyard, betimes in the morning, which was ushered in by Mr. Pickwick’s adventure with the middle--aged lady in the yellow curl-papers, sat Mr. Weller, senior, preparing himself for his journey to London. He was sitting in an excellent attitude for having his portrait taken; and here it is.\n\nIt is very possible that at some earlier period of his career, Mr. Weller’s profile might have presented a bold and determined outline. His face, however, had expanded under the influence of good living, and a disposition remarkable for resignation; and its bold, fleshy curves had so far extended beyond the limits originally assigned them, that unless you took a full view of his countenance in front, it was difficult to distinguish more than the extreme tip of a very rubicund nose. His chin, from the same cause, had acquired the grave and imposing form which is generally described by prefixing the word ‘double’ to that expressive feature; and his complexion exhibited that peculiarly mottled combination of colours which is only to be seen in gentlemen of his profession, and in underdone roast beef. Round his neck he wore a crimson travelling-shawl, which merged into his chin by such imperceptible gradations, that it was difficult to distinguish the folds of the one, from the folds of the other. Over this, he mounted a long waistcoat of a broad pink-striped pattern, and over that again, a wide- skirted green coat, ornamented with large brass buttons, whereof the two which garnished the waist, were so far apart, that no man had ever beheld them both at the same time. His hair, which was short, sleek, and black, was just visible beneath the capacious brim of a low-crowned brown hat. His legs were encased in knee-cord breeches, and painted top- boots; and a copper watch-chain, terminating in one seal, and a key of the same material, dangled loosely from his capacious waistband.\n\nWe have said that Mr. Weller was engaged in preparing for his journey to London--he was taking sustenance, in fact. On the table before him, stood a pot of ale, a cold round of beef, and a very respectable-looking loaf, to each of which he distributed his favours in turn, with the most rigid impartiality. He had just cut a mighty slice from the latter, when the footsteps of somebody entering the room, caused him to raise his head; and he beheld his son.\n\n‘Mornin’, Sammy!’ said the father.\n\nThe son walked up to the pot of ale, and nodding significantly to his parent, took a long draught by way of reply.\n\n‘Wery good power o’ suction, Sammy,’ said Mr. Weller the elder, looking into the pot, when his first-born had set it down half empty. ‘You’d ha’ made an uncommon fine oyster, Sammy, if you’d been born in that station o’ life.’\n\n‘Yes, I des-say, I should ha’ managed to pick up a respectable livin’,’ replied Sam applying himself to the cold beef, with considerable vigour.\n\n‘I’m wery sorry, Sammy,’ said the elder Mr. Weller, shaking up the ale, by describing small circles with the pot, preparatory to drinking. ‘I’m wery sorry, Sammy, to hear from your lips, as you let yourself be gammoned by that ‘ere mulberry man. I always thought, up to three days ago, that the names of Veller and gammon could never come into contract, Sammy, never.’\n\n‘Always exceptin’ the case of a widder, of course,’ said Sam.\n\n‘Widders, Sammy,’ replied Mr. Weller, slightly changing colour. ‘Widders are ‘ceptions to ev’ry rule. I have heerd how many ordinary women one widder’s equal to in pint o’ comin’ over you. I think it’s five-and- twenty, but I don’t rightly know vether it ain’t more.’\n\n‘Well; that’s pretty well,’ said Sam.\n\n‘Besides,’ continued Mr. Weller, not noticing the interruption, ‘that’s a wery different thing. You know what the counsel said, Sammy, as defended the gen’l’m’n as beat his wife with the poker, venever he got jolly. “And arter all, my Lord,” says he, “it’s a amiable weakness.” So I says respectin’ widders, Sammy, and so you’ll say, ven you gets as old as me.’\n\n‘I ought to ha’ know’d better, I know,’ said Sam.\n\n‘Ought to ha’ know’d better!’ repeated Mr. Weller, striking the table with his fist. ‘Ought to ha’ know’d better! why, I know a young ‘un as hasn’t had half nor quarter your eddication--as hasn’t slept about the markets, no, not six months--who’d ha’ scorned to be let in, in such a vay; scorned it, Sammy.’ In the excitement of feeling produced by this agonising reflection, Mr. Weller rang the bell, and ordered an additional pint of ale.\n\n‘Well, it’s no use talking about it now,’ said Sam. ‘It’s over, and can’t be helped, and that’s one consolation, as they always says in Turkey, ven they cuts the wrong man’s head off. It’s my innings now, gov’nor, and as soon as I catches hold o’ this ‘ere Trotter, I’ll have a good ‘un.’\n\n‘I hope you will, Sammy. I hope you will,’ returned Mr. Weller. ‘Here’s your health, Sammy, and may you speedily vipe off the disgrace as you’ve inflicted on the family name.’ In honour of this toast Mr. Weller imbibed at a draught, at least two-thirds of a newly-arrived pint, and handed it over to his son, to dispose of the remainder, which he instantaneously did.\n\n‘And now, Sammy,’ said Mr. Weller, consulting a large double-faced silver watch that hung at the end of the copper chain. ‘Now it’s time I was up at the office to get my vay-bill and see the coach loaded; for coaches, Sammy, is like guns--they requires to be loaded with wery great care, afore they go off.’\n\nAt this parental and professional joke, Mr. Weller, junior, smiled a filial smile. His revered parent continued in a solemn tone--\n\n‘I’m a-goin’ to leave you, Samivel, my boy, and there’s no telling ven I shall see you again. Your mother-in-law may ha’ been too much for me, or a thousand things may have happened by the time you next hears any news o’ the celebrated Mr. Veller o’ the Bell Savage. The family name depends wery much upon you, Samivel, and I hope you’ll do wot’s right by it. Upon all little pints o’ breedin’, I know I may trust you as vell as if it was my own self. So I’ve only this here one little bit of adwice to give you. If ever you gets to up’ards o’ fifty, and feels disposed to go a-marryin’ anybody--no matter who--jist you shut yourself up in your own room, if you’ve got one, and pison yourself off hand. Hangin’s wulgar, so don’t you have nothin’ to say to that. Pison yourself, Samivel, my boy, pison yourself, and you’ll be glad on it arterwards.’ With these affecting words, Mr. Weller looked steadfastly on his son, and turning slowly upon his heel, disappeared from his sight.\n\nIn the contemplative mood which these words had awakened, Mr. Samuel Weller walked forth from the Great White Horse when his father had left him; and bending his steps towards St. Clement’s Church, endeavoured to dissipate his melancholy, by strolling among its ancient precincts. He had loitered about, for some time, when he found himself in a retired spot--a kind of courtyard of venerable appearance--which he discovered had no other outlet than the turning by which he had entered. He was about retracing his steps, when he was suddenly transfixed to the spot by a sudden appearance; and the mode and manner of this appearance, we now proceed to relate.\n\nMr. Samuel Weller had been staring up at the old brick houses now and then, in his deep abstraction, bestowing a wink upon some healthy- looking servant girl as she drew up a blind, or threw open a bedroom window, when the green gate of a garden at the bottom of the yard opened, and a man having emerged therefrom, closed the green gate very carefully after him, and walked briskly towards the very spot where Mr. Weller was standing.\n\nNow, taking this, as an isolated fact, unaccompanied by any attendant circumstances, there was nothing very extraordinary in it; because in many parts of the world men do come out of gardens, close green gates after them, and even walk briskly away, without attracting any particular share of public observation. It is clear, therefore, that there must have been something in the man, or in his manner, or both, to attract Mr. Weller’s particular notice. Whether there was, or not, we must leave the reader to determine, when we have faithfully recorded the behaviour of the individual in question.\n\nWhen the man had shut the green gate after him, he walked, as we have said twice already, with a brisk pace up the courtyard; but he no sooner caught sight of Mr. Weller than he faltered, and stopped, as if uncertain, for the moment, what course to adopt. As the green gate was closed behind him, and there was no other outlet but the one in front, however, he was not long in perceiving that he must pass Mr. Samuel Weller to get away. He therefore resumed his brisk pace, and advanced, staring straight before him. The most extraordinary thing about the man was, that he was contorting his face into the most fearful and astonishing grimaces that ever were beheld. Nature’s handiwork never was disguised with such extraordinary artificial carving, as the man had overlaid his countenance with in one moment.\n\n‘Well!’ said Mr. Weller to himself, as the man approached. ‘This is wery odd. I could ha’ swore it was him.’\n\nUp came the man, and his face became more frightfully distorted than ever, as he drew nearer.\n\n‘I could take my oath to that ‘ere black hair and mulberry suit,’ said Mr. Weller; ‘only I never see such a face as that afore.’\n\nAs Mr. Weller said this, the man’s features assumed an unearthly twinge, perfectly hideous. He was obliged to pass very near Sam, however, and the scrutinising glance of that gentleman enabled him to detect, under all these appalling twists of feature, something too like the small eyes of Mr. Job Trotter to be easily mistaken.\n\n‘Hollo, you Sir!’ shouted Sam fiercely.\n\nThe stranger stopped.\n\n‘Hollo!’ repeated Sam, still more gruffly.\n\nThe man with the horrible face looked, with the greatest surprise, up the court, and down the court, and in at the windows of the houses-- everywhere but at Sam Weller--and took another step forward, when he was brought to again by another shout.\n\n‘Hollo, you sir!’ said Sam, for the third time.\n\nThere was no pretending to mistake where the voice came from now, so the stranger, having no other resource, at last looked Sam Weller full in the face.\n\n‘It won’t do, Job Trotter,’ said Sam. ‘Come! None o’ that ‘ere nonsense. You ain’t so wery ‘andsome that you can afford to throw avay many o’ your good looks. Bring them ‘ere eyes o’ yourn back into their proper places, or I’ll knock ‘em out of your head. D’ye hear?’\n\nAs Mr. Weller appeared fully disposed to act up to the spirit of this address, Mr. Trotter gradually allowed his face to resume its natural expression; and then giving a start of joy, exclaimed, ‘What do I see? Mr. Walker!’\n\n‘Ah,’ replied Sam. ‘You’re wery glad to see me, ain’t you?’\n\n‘Glad!’ exclaimed Job Trotter; ‘Oh, Mr. Walker, if you had but known how I have looked forward to this meeting! It is too much, Mr. Walker; I cannot bear it, indeed I cannot.’ And with these words, Mr. Trotter burst into a regular inundation of tears, and, flinging his arms around those of Mr. Weller, embraced him closely, in an ecstasy of joy.\n\n‘Get off!’ cried Sam, indignant at this process, and vainly endeavouring to extricate himself from the grasp of his enthusiastic acquaintance. ‘Get off, I tell you. What are you crying over me for, you portable engine?’\n\n‘Because I am so glad to see you,’ replied Job Trotter, gradually releasing Mr. Weller, as the first symptoms of his pugnacity disappeared. ‘Oh, Mr. Walker, this is too much.’\n\n‘Too much!’ echoed Sam, ‘I think it is too much--rayther! Now, what have you got to say to me, eh?’\n\nMr. Trotter made no reply; for the little pink pocket-handkerchief was in full force.\n\n‘What have you got to say to me, afore I knock your head off?’ repeated Mr. Weller, in a threatening manner.\n\n‘Eh!’ said Mr. Trotter, with a look of virtuous surprise.\n\n‘What have you got to say to me?’\n\n‘I, Mr. Walker!’\n\n‘Don’t call me Valker; my name’s Veller; you know that vell enough. What have you got to say to me?’\n\n‘Bless you, Mr. Walker--Weller, I mean--a great many things, if you will come away somewhere, where we can talk comfortably. If you knew how I have looked for you, Mr. Weller--’\n\n‘Wery hard, indeed, I s’pose?’ said Sam drily.\n\n‘Very, very, Sir,’ replied Mr. Trotter, without moving a muscle of his face. ‘But shake hands, Mr. Weller.’\n\nSam eyed his companion for a few seconds, and then, as if actuated by a sudden impulse, complied with his request.\n\n‘How,’ said Job Trotter, as they walked away, ‘how is your dear, good master? Oh, he is a worthy gentleman, Mr. Weller! I hope he didn’t catch cold, that dreadful night, Sir.’\n\nThere was a momentary look of deep slyness in Job Trotter’s eye, as he said this, which ran a thrill through Mr. Weller’s clenched fist, as he burned with a desire to make a demonstration on his ribs. Sam constrained himself, however, and replied that his master was extremely well.\n\n‘Oh, I am so glad,’ replied Mr. Trotter; ‘is he here?’\n\n‘Is yourn?’ asked Sam, by way of reply.\n\n‘Oh, yes, he is here, and I grieve to say, Mr. Weller, he is going on worse than ever.’\n\n‘Ah, ah!’ said Sam.\n\n‘Oh, shocking--terrible!’\n\n‘At a boarding-school?’ said Sam.\n\n‘No, not at a boarding-school,’ replied Job Trotter, with the same sly look which Sam had noticed before; ‘not at a boarding-school.’\n\n‘At the house with the green gate?’ said Sam, eyeing his companion closely.\n\n‘No, no--oh, not there,’ replied Job, with a quickness very unusual to him, ‘not there.’\n\n‘What was you a-doin’ there?’ asked Sam, with a sharp glance. ‘Got inside the gate by accident, perhaps?’\n\n‘Why, Mr. Weller,’ replied Job, ‘I don’t mind telling you my little secrets, because, you know, we took such a fancy for each other when we first met. You recollect how pleasant we were that morning?’\n\n‘Oh, yes,’ said Sam, impatiently. ‘I remember. Well?’\n\n‘Well,’ replied Job, speaking with great precision, and in the low tone of a man who communicates an important secret; ‘in that house with the green gate, Mr. Weller, they keep a good many servants.’\n\n‘So I should think, from the look on it,’ interposed Sam.\n\n‘Yes,’ continued Mr. Trotter, ‘and one of them is a cook, who has saved up a little money, Mr. Weller, and is desirous, if she can establish herself in life, to open a little shop in the chandlery way, you see.’\n\nYes.’\n‘Yes, Mr. Weller. Well, Sir, I met her at a chapel that I go to; a very neat little chapel in this town, Mr. Weller, where they sing the number four collection of hymns, which I generally carry about with me, in a little book, which you may perhaps have seen in my hand--and I got a little intimate with her, Mr. Weller, and from that, an acquaintance sprung up between us, and I may venture to say, Mr. Weller, that I am to be the chandler.’\n\n‘Ah, and a wery amiable chandler you’ll make,’ replied Sam, eyeing Job with a side look of intense dislike.\n\n‘The great advantage of this, Mr. Weller,’ continued Job, his eyes filling with tears as he spoke, ‘will be, that I shall be able to leave my present disgraceful service with that bad man, and to devote myself to a better and more virtuous life; more like the way in which I was brought up, Mr. Weller.’\n\n‘You must ha’ been wery nicely brought up,’ said Sam.\n\n‘Oh, very, Mr. Weller, very,’ replied Job. At the recollection of the purity of his youthful days, Mr. Trotter pulled forth the pink handkerchief, and wept copiously.\n\n‘You must ha’ been an uncommon nice boy, to go to school vith,’ said Sam.\n\n‘I was, sir,’ replied Job, heaving a deep sigh; ‘I was the idol of the place.’\n\n‘Ah,’ said Sam, ‘I don’t wonder at it. What a comfort you must ha’ been to your blessed mother.’\n\nAt these words, Mr. Job Trotter inserted an end of the pink handkerchief into the corner of each eye, one after the other, and began to weep copiously.\n\n‘Wot’s the matter with the man,’ said Sam, indignantly. ‘Chelsea water- works is nothin’ to you. What are you melting vith now? The consciousness o’ willainy?’\n\n‘I cannot keep my feelings down, Mr. Weller,’ said Job, after a short pause. ‘To think that my master should have suspected the conversation I had with yours, and so dragged me away in a post-chaise, and after persuading the sweet young lady to say she knew nothing of him, and bribing the school-mistress to do the same, deserted her for a better speculation! Oh! Mr. Weller, it makes me shudder.’\n\n‘Oh, that was the vay, was it?’ said Mr. Weller.\n\n‘To be sure it was,’ replied Job.\n\n‘Vell,’ said Sam, as they had now arrived near the hotel, ‘I vant to have a little bit o’ talk with you, Job; so if you’re not partickler engaged, I should like to see you at the Great White Horse to-night, somewheres about eight o’clock.’\n\n‘I shall be sure to come,’ said Job.\n\n‘Yes, you’d better,’ replied Sam, with a very meaning look, ‘or else I shall perhaps be askin’ arter you, at the other side of the green gate, and then I might cut you out, you know.’\n\n‘I shall be sure to be with you, sir,’ said Mr. Trotter; and wringing Sam’s hand with the utmost fervour, he walked away.\n\n‘Take care, Job Trotter, take care,’ said Sam, looking after him, ‘or I shall be one too many for you this time. I shall, indeed.’ Having uttered this soliloquy, and looked after Job till he was to be seen no more, Mr. Weller made the best of his way to his master’s bedroom.\n\n‘It’s all in training, Sir,’ said Sam.\n\n‘What’s in training, Sam?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘I’ve found ‘em out, Sir,’ said Sam.\n\n‘Found out who?’\n\n‘That ‘ere queer customer, and the melan-cholly chap with the black hair.’\n\n‘Impossible, Sam!’ said Mr. Pickwick, with the greatest energy. ‘Where are they, Sam: where are they?’\n\n‘Hush, hush!’ replied Mr. Weller; and as he assisted Mr. Pickwick to dress, he detailed the plan of action on which he proposed to enter.\n\n‘But when is this to be done, Sam?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘All in good time, Sir,’ replied Sam.\n\nWhether it was done in good time, or not, will be seen hereafter.\n\nCHAPTER XXIV. WHEREIN MR. PETER MAGNUS GROWS JEALOUS, AND THE MIDDLE- AGED LADY APPREHENSIVE, WHICH BRINGS THE PICKWICKIANS WITHIN THE GRASP OF THE LAW\nWhen Mr. Pickwick descended to the room in which he and Mr. Peter Magnus had spent the preceding evening, he found that gentleman with the major part of the contents of the two bags, the leathern hat-box, and the brown-paper parcel, displaying to all possible advantage on his person, while he himself was pacing up and down the room in a state of the utmost excitement and agitation.\n\n‘Good-morning, Sir,’ said Mr. Peter Magnus. ‘What do you think of this, Sir?’\n\n‘Very effective indeed,’ replied Mr. Pickwick, surveying the garments of Mr. Peter Magnus with a good-natured smile.\n\n‘Yes, I think it’ll do,’ said Mr. Magnus. ‘Mr. Pickwick, Sir, I have sent up my card.’\n\n‘Have you?’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘And the waiter brought back word, that she would see me at eleven--at eleven, Sir; it only wants a quarter now.’\n\n‘Very near the time,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Yes, it is rather near,’ replied Mr. Magnus, ‘rather too near to be pleasant--eh! Mr. Pickwick, sir?’\n\n‘Confidence is a great thing in these cases,’ observed Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘I believe it is, Sir,’ said Mr. Peter Magnus. ‘I am very confident, Sir. Really, Mr. Pickwick, I do not see why a man should feel any fear in such a case as this, sir. What is it, Sir? There’s nothing to be ashamed of; it’s a matter of mutual accommodation, nothing more. Husband on one side, wife on the other. That’s my view of the matter, Mr. Pickwick.’\n\n‘It is a very philosophical one,’ replied Mr. Pickwick. ‘But breakfast is waiting, Mr. Magnus. Come.’\n\nDown they sat to breakfast, but it was evident, notwithstanding the boasting of Mr. Peter Magnus, that he laboured under a very considerable degree of nervousness, of which loss of appetite, a propensity to upset the tea-things, a spectral attempt at drollery, and an irresistible inclination to look at the clock, every other second, were among the principal symptoms.\n\n‘He-he-he,’ tittered Mr. Magnus, affecting cheerfulness, and gasping with agitation. ‘It only wants two minutes, Mr. Pickwick. Am I pale, Sir?’\n\n‘Not very,’ replied Mr. Pickwick.\n\nThere was a brief pause.\n\n‘I beg your pardon, Mr. Pickwick; but have you ever done this sort of thing in your time?’ said Mr. Magnus.\n\n‘You mean proposing?’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Yes.’\n\n‘Never,’ said Mr. Pickwick, with great energy, ‘never.’\n\n‘You have no idea, then, how it’s best to begin?’ said Mr. Magnus.\n\n‘Why,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘I may have formed some ideas upon the subject, but, as I have never submitted them to the test of experience, I should be sorry if you were induced to regulate your proceedings by them.’\n\n‘I should feel very much obliged to you, for any advice, Sir,’ said Mr. Magnus, taking another look at the clock, the hand of which was verging on the five minutes past.\n\n‘Well, sir,’ said Mr. Pickwick, with the profound solemnity with which that great man could, when he pleased, render his remarks so deeply impressive. ‘I should commence, sir, with a tribute to the lady’s beauty and excellent qualities; from them, Sir, I should diverge to my own unworthiness.’\n\n‘Very good,’ said Mr. Magnus.\n\n‘Unworthiness for her only, mind, sir,’ resumed Mr. Pickwick; ‘for to show that I was not wholly unworthy, sir, I should take a brief review of my past life, and present condition. I should argue, by analogy, that to anybody else, I must be a very desirable object. I should then expatiate on the warmth of my love, and the depth of my devotion. Perhaps I might then be tempted to seize her hand.’\n\n‘Yes, I see,’ said Mr. Magnus; ‘that would be a very great point.’\n\n‘I should then, Sir,’ continued Mr. Pickwick, growing warmer as the subject presented itself in more glowing colours before him--‘I should then, Sir, come to the plain and simple question, “Will you have me?” I think I am justified in assuming that upon this, she would turn away her head.’\n\n‘You think that may be taken for granted?’ said Mr. Magnus; ‘because, if she did not do that at the right place, it would be embarrassing.’\n\n‘I think she would,’ said Mr. Pickwick. ‘Upon this, sir, I should squeeze her hand, and I think--I think, Mr. Magnus--that after I had done that, supposing there was no refusal, I should gently draw away the handkerchief, which my slight knowledge of human nature leads me to suppose the lady would be applying to her eyes at the moment, and steal a respectful kiss. I think I should kiss her, Mr. Magnus; and at this particular point, I am decidedly of opinion that if the lady were going to take me at all, she would murmur into my ears a bashful acceptance.’\n\nMr. Magnus started; gazed on Mr. Pickwick’s intelligent face, for a short time in silence; and then (the dial pointing to the ten minutes past) shook him warmly by the hand, and rushed desperately from the room.\n\nMr. Pickwick had taken a few strides to and fro; and the small hand of the clock following the latter part of his example, had arrived at the figure which indicates the half-hour, when the door suddenly opened. He turned round to meet Mr. Peter Magnus, and encountered, in his stead, the joyous face of Mr. Tupman, the serene countenance of Mr. Winkle, and the intellectual lineaments of Mr. Snodgrass.\n\nAs Mr. Pickwick greeted them, Mr. Peter Magnus tripped into the room.\n\n‘My friends, the gentleman I was speaking of--Mr. Magnus,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Your servant, gentlemen,’ said Mr. Magnus, evidently in a high state of excitement; ‘Mr. Pickwick, allow me to speak to you one moment, sir.’\n\nAs he said this, Mr. Magnus harnessed his forefinger to Mr. Pickwick’s buttonhole, and, drawing him to a window recess, said--\n\n‘Congratulate me, Mr. Pickwick; I followed your advice to the very letter.’\n\n‘And it was all correct, was it?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘It was, Sir. Could not possibly have been better,’ replied Mr. Magnus. ‘Mr. Pickwick, she is mine.’\n\n‘I congratulate you, with all my heart,’ replied Mr. Pickwick, warmly shaking his new friend by the hand.\n\n‘You must see her. Sir,’ said Mr. Magnus; ‘this way, if you please. Excuse us for one instant, gentlemen.’ Hurrying on in this way, Mr. Peter Magnus drew Mr. Pickwick from the room. He paused at the next door in the passage, and tapped gently thereat.\n\n‘Come in,’ said a female voice. And in they went.\n\n‘Miss Witherfield,’ said Mr. Magnus, ‘allow me to introduce my very particular friend, Mr. Pickwick. Mr. Pickwick, I beg to make you known to Miss Witherfield.’\n\nThe lady was at the upper end of the room. As Mr. Pickwick bowed, he took his spectacles from his waistcoat pocket, and put them on; a process which he had no sooner gone through, than, uttering an exclamation of surprise, Mr. Pickwick retreated several paces, and the lady, with a half-suppressed scream, hid her face in her hands, and dropped into a chair; whereupon Mr. Peter Magnus was stricken motionless on the spot, and gazed from one to the other, with a countenance expressive of the extremities of horror and surprise.\n\nThis certainly was, to all appearance, very unaccountable behaviour; but the fact is, that Mr. Pickwick no sooner put on his spectacles, than he at once recognised in the future Mrs. Magnus the lady into whose room he had so unwarrantably intruded on the previous night; and the spectacles had no sooner crossed Mr. Pickwick’s nose, than the lady at once identified the countenance which she had seen surrounded by all the horrors of a nightcap. So the lady screamed, and Mr. Pickwick started.\n\n‘Mr. Pickwick!’ exclaimed Mr. Magnus, lost in astonishment, ‘what is the meaning of this, Sir? What is the meaning of it, Sir?’ added Mr. Magnus, in a threatening, and a louder tone.\n\n‘Sir,’ said Mr. Pickwick, somewhat indignant at the very sudden manner in which Mr. Peter Magnus had conjugated himself into the imperative mood, ‘I decline answering that question.’\n\n‘You decline it, Sir?’ said Mr. Magnus.\n\n‘I do, Sir,’ replied Mr. Pickwick; ‘I object to say anything which may compromise that lady, or awaken unpleasant recollections in her breast, without her consent and permission.’\n\n‘Miss Witherfield,’ said Mr. Peter Magnus, ‘do you know this person?’\n\n‘Know him!’ repeated the middle-aged lady, hesitating.\n\n‘Yes, know him, ma’am; I said know him,’ replied Mr. Magnus, with ferocity.\n\n‘I have seen him,’ replied the middle-aged lady.\n\n‘Where?’ inquired Mr. Magnus, ‘where?’\n\n‘That,’ said the middle-aged lady, rising from her seat, and averting her head--‘that I would not reveal for worlds.’\n\n‘I understand you, ma’am,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘and respect your delicacy; it shall never be revealed by me depend upon it.’\n\n‘Upon my word, ma’am,’ said Mr. Magnus, ‘considering the situation in which I am placed with regard to yourself, you carry this matter off with tolerable coolness--tolerable coolness, ma’am.’\n\n‘Cruel Mr. Magnus!’ said the middle-aged lady; here she wept very copiously indeed.\n\n‘Address your observations to me, sir,’ interposed Mr. Pickwick; ‘I alone am to blame, if anybody be.’\n\n‘Oh! you alone are to blame, are you, sir?’ said Mr. Magnus; ‘I--I--see through this, sir. You repent of your determination now, do you?’\n\n‘My determination!’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Your determination, Sir. Oh! don’t stare at me, Sir,’ said Mr. Magnus; ‘I recollect your words last night, Sir. You came down here, sir, to expose the treachery and falsehood of an individual on whose truth and honour you had placed implicit reliance--eh?’ Here Mr. Peter Magnus indulged in a prolonged sneer; and taking off his green spectacles-- which he probably found superfluous in his fit of jealousy--rolled his little eyes about, in a manner frightful to behold.\n\n‘Eh?’ said Mr. Magnus; and then he repeated the sneer with increased effect. ‘But you shall answer it, Sir.’\n\n‘Answer what?’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Never mind, sir,’ replied Mr. Magnus, striding up and down the room. ‘Never mind.’\n\nThere must be something very comprehensive in this phrase of ‘Never mind,’ for we do not recollect to have ever witnessed a quarrel in the street, at a theatre, public room, or elsewhere, in which it has not been the standard reply to all belligerent inquiries. ‘Do you call yourself a gentleman, sir?’--‘Never mind, sir.’\n\nDid I offer to say anything to the young woman, sir?’--‘Never mind, sir.’\n\nDo you want your head knocked up against that wall, sir?’--‘Never mind, sir.’ It is observable, too, that there would appear to be some hidden taunt in this universal ‘Never mind,’ which rouses more indignation in the bosom of the individual addressed, than the most lavish abuse could possibly awaken.\n\nWe do not mean to assert that the application of this brevity to himself, struck exactly that indignation to Mr. Pickwick’s soul, which it would infallibly have roused in a vulgar breast. We merely record the fact that Mr. Pickwick opened the room door, and abruptly called out, ‘Tupman, come here!’\n\nMr. Tupman immediately presented himself, with a look of very considerable surprise.\n\n‘Tupman,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘a secret of some delicacy, in which that lady is concerned, is the cause of a difference which has just arisen between this gentleman and myself. When I assure him, in your presence, that it has no relation to himself, and is not in any way connected with his affairs, I need hardly beg you to take notice that if he continue to dispute it, he expresses a doubt of my veracity, which I shall consider extremely insulting.’ As Mr. Pickwick said this, he looked encyclopedias at Mr. Peter Magnus.\n\nMr. Pickwick’s upright and honourable bearing, coupled with that force and energy of speech which so eminently distinguished him, would have carried conviction to any reasonable mind; but, unfortunately, at that particular moment, the mind of Mr. Peter Magnus was in anything but reasonable order. Consequently, instead of receiving Mr. Pickwick’s explanation as he ought to have done, he forthwith proceeded to work himself into a red-hot, scorching, consuming passion, and to talk about what was due to his own feelings, and all that sort of thing; adding force to his declamation by striding to and fro, and pulling his hair-- amusements which he would vary occasionally, by shaking his fist in Mr. Pickwick’s philanthropic countenance.\n\nMr. Pickwick, in his turn, conscious of his own innocence and rectitude, and irritated by having unfortunately involved the middle-aged lady in such an unpleasant affair, was not so quietly disposed as was his wont. The consequence was, that words ran high, and voices higher; and at length Mr. Magnus told Mr. Pickwick he should hear from him; to which Mr. Pickwick replied, with laudable politeness, that the sooner he heard from him the better; whereupon the middle-aged lady rushed in terror from the room, out of which Mr. Tupman dragged Mr. Pickwick, leaving Mr. Peter Magnus to himself and meditation.\n\nIf the middle-aged lady had mingled much with the busy world, or had profited at all by the manners and customs of those who make the laws and set the fashions, she would have known that this sort of ferocity is the most harmless thing in nature; but as she had lived for the most part in the country, and never read the parliamentary debates, she was little versed in these particular refinements of civilised life. Accordingly, when she had gained her bedchamber, bolted herself in, and began to meditate on the scene she had just witnessed, the most terrific pictures of slaughter and destruction presented themselves to her imagination; among which, a full-length portrait of Mr. Peter Magnus borne home by four men, with the embellishment of a whole barrelful of bullets in his left side, was among the very least. The more the middle- aged lady meditated, the more terrified she became; and at length she determined to repair to the house of the principal magistrate of the town, and request him to secure the persons of Mr. Pickwick and Mr. Tupman without delay.\n\nTo this decision the middle-aged lady was impelled by a variety of considerations, the chief of which was the incontestable proof it would afford of her devotion to Mr. Peter Magnus, and her anxiety for his safety. She was too well acquainted with his jealous temperament to venture the slightest allusion to the real cause of her agitation on beholding Mr. Pickwick; and she trusted to her own influence and power of persuasion with the little man, to quell his boisterous jealousy, supposing that Mr. Pickwick were removed, and no fresh quarrel could arise. Filled with these reflections, the middle-aged lady arrayed herself in her bonnet and shawl, and repaired to the mayor’s dwelling straightway.\n\nNow George Nupkins, Esquire, the principal magistrate aforesaid, was as grand a personage as the fastest walker would find out, between sunrise and sunset, on the twenty-first of June, which being, according to the almanacs, the longest day in the whole year, would naturally afford him the longest period for his search. On this particular morning, Mr. Nupkins was in a state of the utmost excitement and irritation, for there had been a rebellion in the town; all the day-scholars at the largest day-school had conspired to break the windows of an obnoxious apple-seller, and had hooted the beadle and pelted the constabulary--an elderly gentleman in top-boots, who had been called out to repress the tumult, and who had been a peace-officer, man and boy, for half a century at least. And Mr. Nupkins was sitting in his easy-chair, frowning with majesty, and boiling with rage, when a lady was announced on pressing, private, and particular business. Mr. Nupkins looked calmly terrible, and commanded that the lady should be shown in; which command, like all the mandates of emperors, and magistrates, and other great potentates of the earth, was forthwith obeyed; and Miss Witherfield, interestingly agitated, was ushered in accordingly.\n\n‘Muzzle!’ said the magistrate.\n\nMuzzle was an undersized footman, with a long body and short legs.\n\n‘Muzzle!’\n\nYes, your Worship.’\n\n‘Place a chair, and leave the room.’\n\n‘Yes, your Worship.’\n\n‘Now, ma’am, will you state your business?’ said the magistrate.\n\n‘It is of a very painful kind, Sir,’ said Miss Witherfield.\n\n‘Very likely, ma’am,’ said the magistrate. ‘Compose your feelings, ma’am.’ Here Mr. Nupkins looked benignant. ‘And then tell me what legal business brings you here, ma’am.’ Here the magistrate triumphed over the man; and he looked stern again.\n\n‘It is very distressing to me, Sir, to give this information,’ said Miss Witherfield, ‘but I fear a duel is going to be fought here.’\n\n‘Here, ma’am?’ said the magistrate. ‘Where, ma’am?’\n\n‘In Ipswich.’\n\nIn Ipswich, ma’am! A duel in Ipswich!’ said the magistrate, perfectly aghast at the notion. ‘Impossible, ma’am; nothing of the kind can be contemplated in this town, I am persuaded. Bless my soul, ma’am, are you aware of the activity of our local magistracy? Do you happen to have heard, ma’am, that I rushed into a prize-ring on the fourth of May last, attended by only sixty special constables; and, at the hazard of falling a sacrifice to the angry passions of an infuriated multitude, prohibited a pugilistic contest between the Middlesex Dumpling and the Suffolk Bantam? A duel in Ipswich, ma’am? I don’t think--I do not think,’ said the magistrate, reasoning with himself, ‘that any two men can have had the hardihood to plan such a breach of the peace, in this town.’\n\n‘My information is, unfortunately, but too correct,’ said the middle- aged lady; ‘I was present at the quarrel.’\n\n‘It’s a most extraordinary thing,’ said the astounded magistrate. ‘Muzzle!’\n\n‘Yes, your Worship.’\n\n‘Send Mr. Jinks here, directly! Instantly.’\n\n‘Yes, your Worship.’\n\nMuzzle retired; and a pale, sharp-nosed, half-fed, shabbily-clad clerk, of middle age, entered the room.\n\n‘Mr. Jinks,’ said the magistrate. ‘Mr. Jinks.’\n\n‘Sir,’ said Mr. Jinks.\n\n‘This lady, Mr. Jinks, has come here, to give information of an intended duel in this town.’\n\nMr. Jinks, not knowing exactly what to do, smiled a dependent’s smile.\n\n‘What are you laughing at, Mr. Jinks?’ said the magistrate.\n\nMr. Jinks looked serious instantly.\n\n‘Mr. Jinks,’ said the magistrate, ‘you’re a fool.’\n\nMr. Jinks looked humbly at the great man, and bit the top of his pen.\n\n‘You may see something very comical in this information, Sir--but I can tell you this, Mr. Jinks, that you have very little to laugh at,’ said the magistrate.\n\nThe hungry-looking Jinks sighed, as if he were quite aware of the fact of his having very little indeed to be merry about; and, being ordered to take the lady’s information, shambled to a seat, and proceeded to write it down.\n\n‘This man, Pickwick, is the principal, I understand?’ said the magistrate, when the statement was finished.\n\n‘He is,’ said the middle-aged lady.\n\n‘And the other rioter--what’s his name, Mr. Jinks?’\n\n‘Tupman, Sir.’\n\nTupman is the second?’\n\n‘Yes.’\n\n‘The other principal, you say, has absconded, ma’am?’\n\n‘Yes,’ replied Miss Witherfield, with a short cough.\n\n‘Very well,’ said the magistrate. ‘These are two cut-throats from London, who have come down here to destroy his Majesty’s population, thinking that at this distance from the capital, the arm of the law is weak and paralysed. They shall be made an example of. Draw up the warrants, Mr. Jinks. Muzzle!’\n\n‘Yes, your Worship.’\n\n‘Is Grummer downstairs?’\n\n‘Yes, your Worship.’\n\n‘Send him up.’\n\nThe obsequious Muzzle retired, and presently returned, introducing the elderly gentleman in the top-boots, who was chiefly remarkable for a bottle-nose, a hoarse voice, a snuff-coloured surtout, and a wandering eye.\n\n‘Grummer,’ said the magistrate.\n\n‘Your Wash-up.’\n\n‘Is the town quiet now?’\n\n‘Pretty well, your Wash-up,’ replied Grummer. ‘Pop’lar feeling has in a measure subsided, consekens o’ the boys having dispersed to cricket.’\n\n‘Nothing but vigorous measures will do in these times, Grummer,’ said the magistrate, in a determined manner. ‘If the authority of the king’s officers is set at naught, we must have the riot act read. If the civil power cannot protect these windows, Grummer, the military must protect the civil power, and the windows too. I believe that is a maxim of the constitution, Mr. Jinks?’\n\nCertainly, sir,’ said Jinks.\n\n‘Very good,’ said the magistrate, signing the warrants. ‘Grummer, you will bring these persons before me, this afternoon. You will find them at the Great White Horse. You recollect the case of the Middlesex Dumpling and the Suffolk Bantam, Grummer?’\n\nMr. Grummer intimated, by a retrospective shake of the head, that he should never forget it--as indeed it was not likely he would, so long as it continued to be cited daily.\n\n‘This is even more unconstitutional,’ said the magistrate; ‘this is even a greater breach of the peace, and a grosser infringement of his Majesty’s prerogative. I believe duelling is one of his Majesty’s most undoubted prerogatives, Mr. Jinks?’\n\n‘Expressly stipulated in Magna Charta, sir,’ said Mr. Jinks.\n\n‘One of the brightest jewels in the British crown, wrung from his Majesty by the barons, I believe, Mr. Jinks?’ said the magistrate.\n\n‘Just so, Sir,’ replied Mr. Jinks.\n\n‘Very well,’ said the magistrate, drawing himself up proudly, ‘it shall not be violated in this portion of his dominions. Grummer, procure assistance, and execute these warrants with as little delay as possible. Muzzle!’\n\n‘Yes, your Worship.’\n\n‘Show the lady out.’\n\nMiss Witherfield retired, deeply impressed with the magistrate’s learning and research; Mr. Nupkins retired to lunch; Mr. Jinks retired within himself--that being the only retirement he had, except the sofa- bedstead in the small parlour which was occupied by his landlady’s family in the daytime--and Mr. Grummer retired, to wipe out, by his mode of discharging his present commission, the insult which had been fastened upon himself, and the other representative of his Majesty--the beadle--in the course of the morning.\n\nWhile these resolute and determined preparations for the conservation of the king’s peace were pending, Mr. Pickwick and his friends, wholly unconscious of the mighty events in progress, had sat quietly down to dinner; and very talkative and companionable they all were. Mr. Pickwick was in the very act of relating his adventure of the preceding night, to the great amusement of his followers, Mr. Tupman especially, when the door opened, and a somewhat forbidding countenance peeped into the room. The eyes in the forbidding countenance looked very earnestly at Mr. Pickwick, for several seconds, and were to all appearance satisfied with their investigation; for the body to which the forbidding countenance belonged, slowly brought itself into the apartment, and presented the form of an elderly individual in top-boots--not to keep the reader any longer in suspense, in short, the eyes were the wandering eyes of Mr. Grummer, and the body was the body of the same gentleman.\n\nMr. Grummer’s mode of proceeding was professional, but peculiar. His first act was to bolt the door on the inside; his second, to polish his head and countenance very carefully with a cotton handkerchief; his third, to place his hat, with the cotton handkerchief in it, on the nearest chair; and his fourth, to produce from the breast-pocket of his coat a short truncheon, surmounted by a brazen crown, with which he beckoned to Mr. Pickwick with a grave and ghost-like air.\n\nMr. Snodgrass was the first to break the astonished silence. He looked steadily at Mr. Grummer for a brief space, and then said emphatically, ‘This is a private room, Sir. A private room.’\n\nMr. Grummer shook his head, and replied, ‘No room’s private to his Majesty when the street door’s once passed. That’s law. Some people maintains that an Englishman’s house is his castle. That’s gammon.’\n\nThe Pickwickians gazed on each other with wondering eyes.\n\n‘Which is Mr. Tupman?’ inquired Mr. Grummer. He had an intuitive perception of Mr. Pickwick; he knew him at once.\n\n‘My name’s Tupman,’ said that gentleman.\n\n‘My name’s Law,’ said Mr. Grummer.\n\n‘What?’ said Mr. Tupman.\n\n‘Law,’ replied Mr. Grummer--‘Law, civil power, and exekative; them’s my titles; here’s my authority. Blank Tupman, blank Pickwick--against the peace of our sufferin’ lord the king--stattit in the case made and purwided--and all regular. I apprehend you Pickwick! Tupman--the aforesaid.’\n\n‘What do you mean by this insolence?’ said Mr. Tupman, starting up; ‘leave the room!’\n\n‘Hollo,’ said Mr. Grummer, retreating very expeditiously to the door, and opening it an inch or two, ‘Dubbley.’\n\n‘Well,’ said a deep voice from the passage.\n\n‘Come for’ard, Dubbley.’\n\nAt the word of command, a dirty-faced man, something over six feet high, and stout in proportion, squeezed himself through the half-open door (making his face very red in the process), and entered the room.\n\n‘Is the other specials outside, Dubbley?’ inquired Mr. Grummer.\n\nMr. Dubbley, who was a man of few words, nodded assent.\n\n‘Order in the diwision under your charge, Dubbley,’ said Mr. Grummer.\n\nMr. Dubbley did as he was desired; and half a dozen men, each with a short truncheon and a brass crown, flocked into the room. Mr. Grummer pocketed his staff, and looked at Mr. Dubbley; Mr. Dubbley pocketed his staff and looked at the division; the division pocketed their staves and looked at Messrs. Tupman and Pickwick.\n\nMr. Pickwick and his followers rose as one man.\n\n‘What is the meaning of this atrocious intrusion upon my privacy?’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Who dares apprehend me?’ said Mr. Tupman.\n\n‘What do you want here, scoundrels?’ said Mr. Snodgrass.\n\nMr. Winkle said nothing, but he fixed his eyes on Grummer, and bestowed a look upon him, which, if he had had any feeling, must have pierced his brain. As it was, however, it had no visible effect on him whatever.\n\nWhen the executive perceived that Mr. Pickwick and his friends were disposed to resist the authority of the law, they very significantly turned up their coat sleeves, as if knocking them down in the first instance, and taking them up afterwards, were a mere professional act which had only to be thought of to be done, as a matter of course. This demonstration was not lost upon Mr. Pickwick. He conferred a few moments with Mr. Tupman apart, and then signified his readiness to proceed to the mayor’s residence, merely begging the parties then and there assembled, to take notice, that it was his firm intention to resent this monstrous invasion of his privileges as an Englishman, the instant he was at liberty; whereat the parties then and there assembled laughed very heartily, with the single exception of Mr. Grummer, who seemed to consider that any slight cast upon the divine right of magistrates was a species of blasphemy not to be tolerated.\n\nBut when Mr. Pickwick had signified his readiness to bow to the laws of his country, and just when the waiters, and hostlers, and chambermaids, and post-boys, who had anticipated a delightful commotion from his threatened obstinacy, began to turn away, disappointed and disgusted, a difficulty arose which had not been foreseen. With every sentiment of veneration for the constituted authorities, Mr. Pickwick resolutely protested against making his appearance in the public streets, surrounded and guarded by the officers of justice, like a common criminal. Mr. Grummer, in the then disturbed state of public feeling (for it was half-holiday, and the boys had not yet gone home), as resolutely protested against walking on the opposite side of the way, and taking Mr. Pickwick’s parole that he would go straight to the magistrate’s; and both Mr. Pickwick and Mr. Tupman as strenuously objected to the expense of a post-coach, which was the only respectable conveyance that could be obtained. The dispute ran high, and the dilemma lasted long; and just as the executive were on the point of overcoming Mr. Pickwick’s objection to walking to the magistrate’s, by the trite expedient of carrying him thither, it was recollected that there stood in the inn yard, an old sedan-chair, which, having been originally built for a gouty gentleman with funded property, would hold Mr. Pickwick and Mr. Tupman, at least as conveniently as a modern post-chaise. The chair was hired, and brought into the hall; Mr. Pickwick and Mr. Tupman squeezed themselves inside, and pulled down the blinds; a couple of chairmen were speedily found; and the procession started in grand order. The specials surrounded the body of the vehicle; Mr. Grummer and Mr. Dubbley marched triumphantly in front; Mr. Snodgrass and Mr. Winkle walked arm-in-arm behind; and the unsoaped of Ipswich brought up the rear.\n\nThe shopkeepers of the town, although they had a very indistinct notion of the nature of the offence, could not but be much edified and gratified by this spectacle. Here was the strong arm of the law, coming down with twenty gold-beater force, upon two offenders from the metropolis itself; the mighty engine was directed by their own magistrate, and worked by their own officers; and both the criminals, by their united efforts, were securely shut up, in the narrow compass of one sedan-chair. Many were the expressions of approval and admiration which greeted Mr. Grummer, as he headed the cavalcade, staff in hand; loud and long were the shouts raised by the unsoaped; and amidst these united testimonials of public approbation, the procession moved slowly and majestically along.\n\nMr. Weller, habited in his morning jacket, with the black calico sleeves, was returning in a rather desponding state from an unsuccessful survey of the mysterious house with the green gate, when, raising his eyes, he beheld a crowd pouring down the street, surrounding an object which had very much the appearance of a sedan-chair. Willing to divert his thoughts from the failure of his enterprise, he stepped aside to see the crowd pass; and finding that they were cheering away, very much to their own satisfaction, forthwith began (by way of raising his spirits) to cheer too, with all his might and main.\n\nMr. Grummer passed, and Mr. Dubbley passed, and the sedan passed, and the bodyguard of specials passed, and Sam was still responding to the enthusiastic cheers of the mob, and waving his hat about as if he were in the very last extreme of the wildest joy (though, of course, he had not the faintest idea of the matter in hand), when he was suddenly stopped by the unexpected appearance of Mr. Winkle and Mr. Snodgrass.\n\n‘What’s the row, gen’l’m’n? ’cried Sam. ‘Who have they got in this here watch-box in mournin’?’\n\nBoth gentlemen replied together, but their words were lost in the tumult.\n\n‘Who is it?’ cried Sam again.\n\nOnce more was a joint reply returned; and, though the words were inaudible, Sam saw by the motion of the two pairs of lips that they had uttered the magic word ‘Pickwick.’\n\nThis was enough. In another minute Mr. Weller had made his way through the crowd, stopped the chairmen, and confronted the portly Grummer.\n\n‘Hollo, old gen’l’m’n!’ said Sam. ‘Who have you got in this here conweyance?’\n\n‘Stand back,’ said Mr. Grummer, whose dignity, like the dignity of a great many other men, had been wondrously augmented by a little popularity.\n\n‘Knock him down, if he don’t,’ said Mr. Dubbley.\n\n‘I’m wery much obliged to you, old gen’l’m’n,’ replied Sam, ‘for consulting my conwenience, and I’m still more obliged to the other gen’l’m’n, who looks as if he’d just escaped from a giant’s carrywan, for his wery ‘andsome suggestion; but I should prefer your givin’ me a answer to my question, if it’s all the same to you.--How are you, Sir?’ This last observation was addressed with a patronising air to Mr. Pickwick, who was peeping through the front window.\n\nMr. Grummer, perfectly speechless with indignation, dragged the truncheon with the brass crown from its particular pocket, and flourished it before Sam’s eyes.\n\n‘Ah,’ said Sam, ‘it’s wery pretty, ‘specially the crown, which is uncommon like the real one.’\n\n‘Stand back!’ said the outraged Mr. Grummer. By way of adding force to the command, he thrust the brass emblem of royalty into Sam’s neckcloth with one hand, and seized Sam’s collar with the other--a compliment which Mr. Weller returned by knocking him down out of hand, having previously with the utmost consideration, knocked down a chairman for him to lie upon.\n\nWhether Mr. Winkle was seized with a temporary attack of that species of insanity which originates in a sense of injury, or animated by this display of Mr. Weller’s valour, is uncertain; but certain it is, that he no sooner saw Mr. Grummer fall than he made a terrific onslaught on a small boy who stood next him; whereupon Mr. Snodgrass, in a truly Christian spirit, and in order that he might take no one unawares, announced in a very loud tone that he was going to begin, and proceeded to take off his coat with the utmost deliberation. He was immediately surrounded and secured; and it is but common justice both to him and Mr. Winkle to say, that they did not make the slightest attempt to rescue either themselves or Mr. Weller; who, after a most vigorous resistance, was overpowered by numbers and taken prisoner. The procession then reformed; the chairmen resumed their stations; and the march was re- commenced.\n\nMr. Pickwick’s indignation during the whole of this proceeding was beyond all bounds. He could just see Sam upsetting the specials, and flying about in every direction; and that was all he could see, for the sedan doors wouldn’t open, and the blinds wouldn’t pull up. At length, with the assistance of Mr. Tupman, he managed to push open the roof; and mounting on the seat, and steadying himself as well as he could, by placing his hand on that gentleman’s shoulder, Mr. Pickwick proceeded to address the multitude; to dwell upon the unjustifiable manner in which he had been treated; and to call upon them to take notice that his servant had been first assaulted. In this order they reached the magistrate’s house; the chairmen trotting, the prisoners following, Mr. Pickwick oratorising, and the crowd shouting.\n\nCHAPTER XXV. SHOWING, AMONG A VARIETY OF PLEASANT MATTERS, HOW MAJESTIC AND IMPARTIAL MR. NUPKINS WAS; AND HOW MR. WELLER RETURNED MR. JOB TROTTER’S SHUTTLECOCK AS HEAVILY AS IT CAME--WITH ANOTHER MATTER, WHICH WILL BE FOUND IN ITS PLACE\nViolent was Mr. Weller’s indignation as he was borne along; numerous were the allusions to the personal appearance and demeanour of Mr. Grummer and his companion; and valorous were the defiances to any six of the gentlemen present, in which he vented his dissatisfaction. Mr. Snodgrass and Mr. Winkle listened with gloomy respect to the torrent of eloquence which their leader poured forth from the sedan-chair, and the rapid course of which not all Mr. Tupman’s earnest entreaties to have the lid of the vehicle closed, were able to check for an instant. But Mr. Weller’s anger quickly gave way to curiosity when the procession turned down the identical courtyard in which he had met with the runaway Job Trotter; and curiosity was exchanged for a feeling of the most gleeful astonishment, when the all-important Mr. Grummer, commanding the sedan-bearers to halt, advanced with dignified and portentous steps to the very green gate from which Job Trotter had emerged, and gave a mighty pull at the bell-handle which hung at the side thereof. The ring was answered by a very smart and pretty-faced servant-girl, who, after holding up her hands in astonishment at the rebellious appearance of the prisoners, and the impassioned language of Mr. Pickwick, summoned Mr. Muzzle. Mr. Muzzle opened one half of the carriage gate, to admit the sedan, the captured ones, and the specials; and immediately slammed it in the faces of the mob, who, indignant at being excluded, and anxious to see what followed, relieved their feelings by kicking at the gate and ringing the bell, for an hour or two afterwards. In this amusement they all took part by turns, except three or four fortunate individuals, who, having discovered a grating in the gate, which commanded a view of nothing, stared through it with the indefatigable perseverance with which people will flatten their noses against the front windows of a chemist’s shop, when a drunken man, who has been run over by a dog-cart in the street, is undergoing a surgical inspection in the back-parlour.\n\nAt the foot of a flight of steps, leading to the house door, which was guarded on either side by an American aloe in a green tub, the sedan- chair stopped. Mr. Pickwick and his friends were conducted into the hall, whence, having been previously announced by Muzzle, and ordered in by Mr. Nupkins, they were ushered into the worshipful presence of that public-spirited officer.\n\nThe scene was an impressive one, well calculated to strike terror to the hearts of culprits, and to impress them with an adequate idea of the stern majesty of the law. In front of a big book-case, in a big chair, behind a big table, and before a big volume, sat Mr. Nupkins, looking a full size larger than any one of them, big as they were. The table was adorned with piles of papers; and above the farther end of it, appeared the head and shoulders of Mr. Jinks, who was busily engaged in looking as busy as possible. The party having all entered, Muzzle carefully closed the door, and placed himself behind his master’s chair to await his orders. Mr. Nupkins threw himself back with thrilling solemnity, and scrutinised the faces of his unwilling visitors.\n\n‘Now, Grummer, who is that person?’ said Mr. Nupkins, pointing to Mr. Pickwick, who, as the spokesman of his friends, stood hat in hand, bowing with the utmost politeness and respect.\n\n‘This here’s Pickvick, your Wash-up,’ said Grummer.\n\n‘Come, none o’ that ‘ere, old Strike-a-light,’ interposed Mr. Weller, elbowing himself into the front rank. ‘Beg your pardon, sir, but this here officer o’ yourn in the gambooge tops, ‘ull never earn a decent livin’ as a master o’ the ceremonies any vere. This here, sir’ continued Mr. Weller, thrusting Grummer aside, and addressing the magistrate with pleasant familiarity, ‘this here is S. Pickvick, Esquire; this here’s Mr. Tupman; that ‘ere’s Mr. Snodgrass; and farder on, next him on the t’other side, Mr. Winkle--all wery nice gen’l’m’n, Sir, as you’ll be wery happy to have the acquaintance on; so the sooner you commits these here officers o’ yourn to the tread-mill for a month or two, the sooner we shall begin to be on a pleasant understanding. Business first, pleasure arterwards, as King Richard the Third said when he stabbed the t’other king in the Tower, afore he smothered the babbies.’\n\nAt the conclusion of this address, Mr. Weller brushed his hat with his right elbow, and nodded benignly to Jinks, who had heard him throughout with unspeakable awe.\n\n‘Who is this man, Grummer?’ said the magistrate.\n\n‘Wery desp’rate ch’racter, your Wash-up,’ replied Grummer. ‘He attempted to rescue the prisoners, and assaulted the officers; so we took him into custody, and brought him here.’\n\n‘You did quite right,’ replied the magistrate. ‘He is evidently a desperate ruffian.’\n\n‘He is my servant, Sir,’ said Mr. Pickwick angrily.\n\n‘Oh! he is your servant, is he?’ said Mr. Nupkins. ‘A conspiracy to defeat the ends of justice, and murder its officers. Pickwick’s servant. Put that down, Mr. Jinks.’\n\nMr. Jinks did so.\n\n‘What’s your name, fellow?’ thundered Mr. Nupkins.\n\n‘Veller,’ replied Sam.\n\n‘A very good name for the Newgate Calendar,’ said Mr. Nupkins.\n\nThis was a joke; so Jinks, Grummer, Dubbley, all the specials, and Muzzle, went into fits of laughter of five minutes’ duration.\n\n‘Put down his name, Mr. Jinks,’ said the magistrate.\n\n‘Two L’s, old feller,’ said Sam.\n\nHere an unfortunate special laughed again, whereupon the magistrate threatened to commit him instantly. It is a dangerous thing to laugh at the wrong man, in these cases.\n\n‘Where do you live?’ said the magistrate.\n\n‘Vere ever I can,’ replied Sam.\n\n‘Put down that, Mr. Jinks,’ said the magistrate, who was fast rising into a rage.\n\n‘Score it under,’ said Sam.\n\n‘He is a vagabond, Mr. Jinks,’ said the magistrate. ‘He is a vagabond on his own statement,--is he not, Mr. Jinks?’\n\n‘Certainly, Sir.’\n\n‘Then I’ll commit him--I’ll commit him as such,’ said Mr. Nupkins.\n\n‘This is a wery impartial country for justice, ‘said Sam.’ There ain’t a magistrate goin’ as don’t commit himself twice as he commits other people.’\n\nAt this sally another special laughed, and then tried to look so supernaturally solemn, that the magistrate detected him immediately.\n\n‘Grummer,’ said Mr. Nupkins, reddening with passion, ‘how dare you select such an inefficient and disreputable person for a special constable, as that man? How dare you do it, Sir?’\n\n‘I am very sorry, your Wash-up,’ stammered Grummer.\n\n‘Very sorry!’ said the furious magistrate. ‘You shall repent of this neglect of duty, Mr. Grummer; you shall be made an example of. Take that fellow’s staff away. He’s drunk. You’re drunk, fellow.’\n\n‘I am not drunk, your Worship,’ said the man.\n\n‘You are drunk,’ returned the magistrate. ‘How dare you say you are not drunk, Sir, when I say you are? Doesn’t he smell of spirits, Grummer?’\n\n‘Horrid, your Wash-up,’ replied Grummer, who had a vague impression that there was a smell of rum somewhere.\n\n‘I knew he did,’ said Mr. Nupkins. ‘I saw he was drunk when he first came into the room, by his excited eye. Did you observe his excited eye, Mr. Jinks?’\n\n‘Certainly, Sir.’\n\n‘I haven’t touched a drop of spirits this morning,’ said the man, who was as sober a fellow as need be.\n\n‘How dare you tell me a falsehood?’ said Mr. Nupkins. ‘Isn’t he drunk at this moment, Mr. Jinks?’\n\n‘Certainly, Sir,’ replied Jinks.\n\n‘Mr. Jinks,’ said the magistrate, ‘I shall commit that man for contempt. Make out his committal, Mr. Jinks.’\n\nAnd committed the special would have been, only Jinks, who was the magistrate’s adviser (having had a legal education of three years in a country attorney’s office), whispered the magistrate that he thought it wouldn’t do; so the magistrate made a speech, and said, that in consideration of the special’s family, he would merely reprimand and discharge him. Accordingly, the special was abused, vehemently, for a quarter of an hour, and sent about his business; and Grummer, Dubbley, Muzzle, and all the other specials, murmured their admiration of the magnanimity of Mr. Nupkins.\n\n‘Now, Mr. Jinks,’ said the magistrate, ‘swear Grummer.’\n\nGrummer was sworn directly; but as Grummer wandered, and Mr. Nupkins’s dinner was nearly ready, Mr. Nupkins cut the matter short, by putting leading questions to Grummer, which Grummer answered as nearly in the affirmative as he could. So the examination went off, all very smooth and comfortable, and two assaults were proved against Mr. Weller, and a threat against Mr. Winkle, and a push against Mr. Snodgrass. When all this was done to the magistrate’s satisfaction, the magistrate and Mr. Jinks consulted in whispers.\n\nThe consultation having lasted about ten minutes, Mr. Jinks retired to his end of the table; and the magistrate, with a preparatory cough, drew himself up in his chair, and was proceeding to commence his address, when Mr. Pickwick interposed.\n\n‘I beg your pardon, sir, for interrupting you,’ said Mr. Pickwick; ‘but before you proceed to express, and act upon, any opinion you may have formed on the statements which have been made here, I must claim my right to be heard so far as I am personally concerned.’\n\n‘Hold your tongue, Sir,’ said the magistrate peremptorily.\n\n‘I must submit to you, Sir--’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Hold your tongue, sir,’ interposed the magistrate, ‘or I shall order an officer to remove you.’\n\n‘You may order your officers to do whatever you please, Sir,’ said Mr. Pickwick; ‘and I have no doubt, from the specimen I have had of the subordination preserved amongst them, that whatever you order, they will execute, Sir; but I shall take the liberty, Sir, of claiming my right to be heard, until I am removed by force.’\n\n‘Pickvick and principle!’ exclaimed Mr. Weller, in a very audible voice.\n\n‘Sam, be quiet,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Dumb as a drum vith a hole in it, Sir,’ replied Sam.\n\nMr. Nupkins looked at Mr. Pickwick with a gaze of intense astonishment, at his displaying such unwonted temerity; and was apparently about to return a very angry reply, when Mr. Jinks pulled him by the sleeve, and whispered something in his ear. To this, the magistrate returned a half- audible answer, and then the whispering was renewed. Jinks was evidently remonstrating.\n\nAt length the magistrate, gulping down, with a very bad grace, his disinclination to hear anything more, turned to Mr. Pickwick, and said sharply, ‘What do you want to say?’\n\n‘First,’ said Mr. Pickwick, sending a look through his spectacles, under which even Nupkins quailed, ‘first, I wish to know what I and my friend have been brought here for?’\n\n‘Must I tell him?’ whispered the magistrate to Jinks.\n\n‘I think you had better, sir,’ whispered Jinks to the magistrate.\n\n‘An information has been sworn before me,’ said the magistrate, ‘that it is apprehended you are going to fight a duel, and that the other man, Tupman, is your aider and abettor in it. Therefore--eh, Mr. Jinks?’\n\n‘Certainly, sir.’\n\n‘Therefore, I call upon you both, to--I think that’s the course, Mr. Jinks?’\n\n‘Certainly, Sir.’\n\n‘To--to--what, Mr. Jinks?’ said the magistrate pettishly.\n\n‘To find bail, sir.’\n\n‘Yes. Therefore, I call upon you both--as I was about to say when I was interrupted by my clerk--to find bail.’\n\nGood bail,’ whispered Mr. Jinks.\n\n‘I shall require good bail,’ said the magistrate.\n\n‘Town’s-people,’ whispered Jinks.\n\n‘They must be townspeople,’ said the magistrate.\n\n‘Fifty pounds each,’ whispered Jinks, ‘and householders, of course.’\n\n‘I shall require two sureties of fifty pounds each,’ said the magistrate aloud, with great dignity, ‘and they must be householders, of course.’\n\n‘But bless my heart, Sir,’ said Mr. Pickwick, who, together with Mr. Tupman, was all amazement and indignation; ‘we are perfect strangers in this town. I have as little knowledge of any householders here, as I have intention of fighting a duel with anybody.’\n\n‘I dare say,’ replied the magistrate, ‘I dare say--don’t you, Mr. Jinks?’\n\n‘Certainly, Sir.’\n\n‘Have you anything more to say?’ inquired the magistrate.\n\nMr. Pickwick had a great deal more to say, which he would no doubt have said, very little to his own advantage, or the magistrate’s satisfaction, if he had not, the moment he ceased speaking, been pulled by the sleeve by Mr. Weller, with whom he was immediately engaged in so earnest a conversation, that he suffered the magistrate’s inquiry to pass wholly unnoticed. Mr. Nupkins was not the man to ask a question of the kind twice over; and so, with another preparatory cough, he proceeded, amidst the reverential and admiring silence of the constables, to pronounce his decision.\n\nHe should fine Weller two pounds for the first assault, and three pounds for the second. He should fine Winkle two pounds, and Snodgrass one pound, besides requiring them to enter into their own recognisances to keep the peace towards all his Majesty’s subjects, and especially towards his liege servant, Daniel Grummer. Pickwick and Tupman he had already held to bail.\n\nImmediately on the magistrate ceasing to speak, Mr. Pickwick, with a smile mantling on his again good-humoured countenance, stepped forward, and said--\n\n‘I beg the magistrate’s pardon, but may I request a few minutes’ private conversation with him, on a matter of deep importance to himself?’\n\n‘What?’ said the magistrate. Mr. Pickwick repeated his request.\n\n‘This is a most extraordinary request,’ said the magistrate. ‘A private interview?’\n\n‘A private interview,’ replied Mr. Pickwick firmly; ‘only, as a part of the information which I wish to communicate is derived from my servant, I should wish him to be present.’\n\nThe magistrate looked at Mr. Jinks; Mr. Jinks looked at the magistrate; the officers looked at each other in amazement. Mr. Nupkins turned suddenly pale. Could the man Weller, in a moment of remorse, have divulged some secret conspiracy for his assassination? It was a dreadful thought. He was a public man; and he turned paler, as he thought of Julius Caesar and Mr. Perceval.\n\nThe magistrate looked at Mr. Pickwick again, and beckoned Mr. Jinks.\n\n‘What do you think of this request, Mr. Jinks?’ murmured Mr. Nupkins.\n\nMr. Jinks, who didn’t exactly know what to think of it, and was afraid he might offend, smiled feebly, after a dubious fashion, and, screwing up the corners of his mouth, shook his head slowly from side to side.\n\n‘Mr. Jinks,’ said the magistrate gravely, ‘you are an ass.’\n\nAt this little expression of opinion, Mr. Jinks smiled again--rather more feebly than before--and edged himself, by degrees, back into his own corner.\n\nMr. Nupkins debated the matter within himself for a few seconds, and then, rising from his chair, and requesting Mr. Pickwick and Sam to follow him, led the way into a small room which opened into the justice- parlour. Desiring Mr. Pickwick to walk to the upper end of the little apartment, and holding his hand upon the half-closed door, that he might be able to effect an immediate escape, in case there was the least tendency to a display of hostilities, Mr. Nupkins expressed his readiness to hear the communication, whatever it might be.\n\n‘I will come to the point at once, sir,’ said Mr. Pickwick; ‘it affects yourself and your credit materially. I have every reason to believe, Sir, that you are harbouring in your house a gross impostor!’\n\n‘Two,’ interrupted Sam. ‘Mulberry agin all natur, for tears and willainny!’\n\n‘Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘if I am to render myself intelligible to this gentleman, I must beg you to control your feelings.’\n\n‘Wery sorry, Sir,’ replied Mr. Weller; ‘but when I think o’ that ‘ere Job, I can’t help opening the walve a inch or two.’\n\n‘In one word, Sir,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘is my servant right in suspecting that a certain Captain Fitz-Marshall is in the habit of visiting here? Because,’ added Mr. Pickwick, as he saw that Mr. Nupkins was about to offer a very indignant interruption, ‘because if he be, I know that person to be a--’\n\n‘Hush, hush,’ said Mr. Nupkins, closing the door. ‘Know him to be what, Sir?’\n\n‘An unprincipled adventurer--a dishonourable character--a man who preys upon society, and makes easily-deceived people his dupes, Sir; his absurd, his foolish, his wretched dupes, Sir,’ said the excited Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Dear me,’ said Mr. Nupkins, turning very red, and altering his whole manner directly. ‘Dear me, Mr.--’\n\n‘Pickvick,’ said Sam.\n\n‘Pickwick,’ said the magistrate, ‘dear me, Mr. Pickwick--pray take a seat--you cannot mean this? Captain Fitz-Marshall!’\n\n‘Don’t call him a cap’en,’ said Sam, ‘nor Fitz-Marshall neither; he ain’t neither one nor t’other. He’s a strolling actor, he is, and his name’s Jingle; and if ever there was a wolf in a mulberry suit, that ‘ere Job Trotter’s him.’\n\n‘It is very true, Sir,’ said Mr. Pickwick, replying to the magistrate’s look of amazement; ‘my only business in this town, is to expose the person of whom we now speak.’\n\nMr. Pickwick proceeded to pour into the horror-stricken ear of Mr. Nupkins, an abridged account of all Mr. Jingle’s atrocities. He related how he had first met him; how he had eloped with Miss Wardle; how he had cheerfully resigned the lady for a pecuniary consideration; how he had entrapped himself into a lady’s boarding-school at midnight; and how he (Mr. Pickwick) now felt it his duty to expose his assumption of his present name and rank.\n\nAs the narrative proceeded, all the warm blood in the body of Mr. Nupkins tingled up into the very tips of his ears. He had picked up the captain at a neighbouring race-course. Charmed with his long list of aristocratic acquaintance, his extensive travel, and his fashionable demeanour, Mrs. Nupkins and Miss Nupkins had exhibited Captain Fitz- Marshall, and quoted Captain Fitz-Marshall, and hurled Captain Fitz- Marshall at the devoted heads of their select circle of acquaintance, until their bosom friends, Mrs. Porkenham and the Misses Porkenhams, and Mr. Sidney Porkenham, were ready to burst with jealousy and despair. And now, to hear, after all, that he was a needy adventurer, a strolling player, and if not a swindler, something so very like it, that it was hard to tell the difference! Heavens! what would the Porkenhams say! What would be the triumph of Mr. Sidney Porkenham when he found that his addresses had been slighted for such a rival! How should he, Nupkins, meet the eye of old Porkenham at the next quarter-sessions! And what a handle would it be for the opposition magisterial party if the story got abroad!\n\n‘But after all,’ said Mr. Nupkins, brightening for a moment, after a long pause; ‘after all, this is a mere statement. Captain Fitz-Marshall is a man of very engaging manners, and, I dare say, has many enemies. What proof have you of the truth of these representations?’\n\n‘Confront me with him,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘that is all I ask, and all I require. Confront him with me and my friends here; you will want no further proof.’\n\n‘Why,’ said Mr. Nupkins, ‘that might be very easily done, for he will be here to-night, and then there would be no occasion to make the matter public, just--just--for the young man’s own sake, you know. I--I--should like to consult Mrs. Nupkins on the propriety of the step, in the first instance, though. At all events, Mr. Pickwick, we must despatch this legal business before we can do anything else. Pray step back into the next room.’\n\nInto the next room they went.\n\n‘Grummer,’ said the magistrate, in an awful voice.\n\n‘Your Wash-up,’ replied Grummer, with the smile of a favourite.\n\n‘Come, come, Sir,’ said the magistrate sternly, ‘don’t let me see any of this levity here. It is very unbecoming, and I can assure you that you have very little to smile at. Was the account you gave me just now strictly true? Now be careful, sir!’\n\nYour Wash-up,’ stammered Grummer, ‘I-’\n\n‘Oh, you are confused, are you?’ said the magistrate. ‘Mr. Jinks, you observe this confusion?’\n\n‘Certainly, Sir,’ replied Jinks.\n\n‘Now,’ said the magistrate, ‘repeat your statement, Grummer, and again I warn you to be careful. Mr. Jinks, take his words down.’\n\nThe unfortunate Grummer proceeded to re-state his complaint, but, what between Mr. Jinks’s taking down his words, and the magistrate’s taking them up, his natural tendency to rambling, and his extreme confusion, he managed to get involved, in something under three minutes, in such a mass of entanglement and contradiction, that Mr. Nupkins at once declared he didn’t believe him. So the fines were remitted, and Mr. Jinks found a couple of bail in no time. And all these solemn proceedings having been satisfactorily concluded, Mr. Grummer was ignominiously ordered out--an awful instance of the instability of human greatness, and the uncertain tenure of great men’s favour.\n\nMrs. Nupkins was a majestic female in a pink gauze turban and a light brown wig. Miss Nupkins possessed all her mamma’s haughtiness without the turban, and all her ill-nature without the wig; and whenever the exercise of these two amiable qualities involved mother and daughter in some unpleasant dilemma, as they not infrequently did, they both concurred in laying the blame on the shoulders of Mr. Nupkins. Accordingly, when Mr. Nupkins sought Mrs. Nupkins, and detailed the communication which had been made by Mr. Pickwick, Mrs. Nupkins suddenly recollected that she had always expected something of the kind; that she had always said it would be so; that her advice was never taken; that she really did not know what Mr. Nupkins supposed she was; and so forth.\n\n‘The idea!’ said Miss Nupkins, forcing a tear of very scanty proportions into the corner of each eye; ‘the idea of my being made such a fool of!’\n\n‘Ah! you may thank your papa, my dear,’ said Mrs. Nupkins; ‘how I have implored and begged that man to inquire into the captain’s family connections; how I have urged and entreated him to take some decisive step! I am quite certain nobody would believe it--quite.’\n\n‘But, my dear,’ said Mr. Nupkins.\n\n‘Don’t talk to me, you aggravating thing, don’t!’ said Mrs. Nupkins.\n\n‘My love,’ said Mr. Nupkins, ‘you professed yourself very fond of Captain Fitz-Marshall. You have constantly asked him here, my dear, and you have lost no opportunity of introducing him elsewhere.’\n\n‘Didn’t I say so, Henrietta?’ cried Mrs. Nupkins, appealing to her daughter with the air of a much-injured female. ‘Didn’t I say that your papa would turn round and lay all this at my door? Didn’t I say so?’ Here Mrs. Nupkins sobbed.\n\n‘Oh, pa!’ remonstrated Miss Nupkins. And here she sobbed too.\n\n‘Isn’t it too much, when he has brought all this disgrace and ridicule upon us, to taunt me with being the cause of it?’ exclaimed Mrs. Nupkins.\n\n‘How can we ever show ourselves in society!’ said Miss Nupkins.\n\n‘How can we face the Porkenhams?’ cried Mrs. Nupkins.\n\n‘Or the Griggs!’ cried Miss Nupkins.\n\n‘Or the Slummintowkens!’ cried Mrs. Nupkins. ‘But what does your papa care! What is it to him!’ At this dreadful reflection, Mrs. Nupkins wept mental anguish, and Miss Nupkins followed on the same side.\n\nMrs. Nupkins’s tears continued to gush forth, with great velocity, until she had gained a little time to think the matter over; when she decided, in her own mind, that the best thing to do would be to ask Mr. Pickwick and his friends to remain until the captain’s arrival, and then to give Mr. Pickwick the opportunity he sought. If it appeared that he had spoken truly, the captain could be turned out of the house without noising the matter abroad, and they could easily account to the Porkenhams for his disappearance, by saying that he had been appointed, through the Court influence of his family, to the governor-generalship of Sierra Leone, of Saugur Point, or any other of those salubrious climates which enchant Europeans so much, that when they once get there, they can hardly ever prevail upon themselves to come back again.\n\nWhen Mrs. Nupkins dried up her tears, Miss Nupkins dried up hers, and Mr. Nupkins was very glad to settle the matter as Mrs. Nupkins had proposed. So Mr. Pickwick and his friends, having washed off all marks of their late encounter, were introduced to the ladies, and soon afterwards to their dinner; and Mr. Weller, whom the magistrate, with his peculiar sagacity, had discovered in half an hour to be one of the finest fellows alive, was consigned to the care and guardianship of Mr. Muzzle, who was specially enjoined to take him below, and make much of him.\n\n‘How de do, sir?’ said Mr. Muzzle, as he conducted Mr. Weller down the kitchen stairs.\n\n‘Why, no considerable change has taken place in the state of my system, since I see you cocked up behind your governor’s chair in the parlour, a little vile ago,’ replied Sam.\n\n‘You will excuse my not taking more notice of you then,’ said Mr. Muzzle. ‘You see, master hadn’t introduced us, then. Lord, how fond he is of you, Mr. Weller, to be sure!’\n\n‘Ah!’ said Sam, ‘what a pleasant chap he is!’\n\n‘Ain’t he?’ replied Mr. Muzzle.\n\n‘So much humour,’ said Sam.\n\n‘And such a man to speak,’ said Mr. Muzzle. ‘How his ideas flow, don’t they?’\n\n‘Wonderful,’ replied Sam; ‘they comes a-pouring out, knocking each other’s heads so fast, that they seems to stun one another; you hardly know what he’s arter, do you?’\n\nThat’s the great merit of his style of speaking,’ rejoined Mr. Muzzle. ‘Take care of the last step, Mr. Weller. Would you like to wash your hands, sir, before we join the ladies? Here’s a sink, with the water laid on, Sir, and a clean jack towel behind the door.’\n\n‘Ah! perhaps I may as well have a rinse,’ replied Mr. Weller, applying plenty of yellow soap to the towel, and rubbing away till his face shone again. ‘How many ladies are there?’\n\n‘Only two in our kitchen,’ said Mr. Muzzle; ‘cook and ‘ouse-maid. We keep a boy to do the dirty work, and a gal besides, but they dine in the wash’us.’\n\n‘Oh, they dines in the wash’us, do they?’ said Mr. Weller.\n\n‘Yes,’ replied Mr. Muzzle, ‘we tried ‘em at our table when they first come, but we couldn’t keep ‘em. The gal’s manners is dreadful vulgar; and the boy breathes so very hard while he’s eating, that we found it impossible to sit at table with him.’\n\n‘Young grampus!’ said Mr. Weller.\n\n‘Oh, dreadful,’ rejoined Mr. Muzzle; ‘but that is the worst of country service, Mr. Weller; the juniors is always so very savage. This way, sir, if you please, this way.’\n\nPreceding Mr. Weller, with the utmost politeness, Mr. Muzzle conducted him into the kitchen.\n\n‘Mary,’ said Mr. Muzzle to the pretty servant-girl, ‘this is Mr. Weller; a gentleman as master has sent down, to be made as comfortable as possible.’\n\n‘And your master’s a knowin’ hand, and has just sent me to the right place,’ said Mr. Weller, with a glance of admiration at Mary. ‘If I wos master o’ this here house, I should alvays find the materials for comfort vere Mary wos.’\n\nLor, Mr. Weller!’ said Mary blushing.\n\n‘Well, I never!’ ejaculated the cook.\n\n‘Bless me, cook, I forgot you,’ said Mr. Muzzle. ‘Mr. Weller, let me introduce you.’\n\n‘How are you, ma’am?’ said Mr. Weller. ‘Wery glad to see you, indeed, and hope our acquaintance may be a long ‘un, as the gen’l’m’n said to the fi’ pun’ note.’\n\nWhen this ceremony of introduction had been gone through, the cook and Mary retired into the back kitchen to titter, for ten minutes; then returning, all giggles and blushes, they sat down to dinner.\n\nMr. Weller’s easy manners and conversational powers had such irresistible influence with his new friends, that before the dinner was half over, they were on a footing of perfect intimacy, and in possession of a full account of the delinquency of Job Trotter.\n\n‘I never could a-bear that Job,’ said Mary.\n\n‘No more you never ought to, my dear,’ replied Mr. Weller.\n\n‘Why not?’ inquired Mary.\n\n‘’Cos ugliness and svindlin’ never ought to be formiliar with elegance and wirtew,’ replied Mr. Weller. ‘Ought they, Mr. Muzzle?’\n\n‘Not by no means,’ replied that gentleman.\n\nHere Mary laughed, and said the cook had made her; and the cook laughed, and said she hadn’t.\n\n‘I ha’n’t got a glass,’ said Mary.\n\n‘Drink with me, my dear,’ said Mr. Weller. ‘Put your lips to this here tumbler, and then I can kiss you by deputy.’\n\n‘For shame, Mr. Weller!’ said Mary.\n\n‘What’s a shame, my dear?’\n\n‘Talkin’ in that way.’\n\n‘Nonsense; it ain’t no harm. It’s natur; ain’t it, cook?’\n\n‘Don’t ask me, imperence,’ replied the cook, in a high state of delight; and hereupon the cook and Mary laughed again, till what between the beer, and the cold meat, and the laughter combined, the latter young lady was brought to the verge of choking--an alarming crisis from which she was only recovered by sundry pats on the back, and other necessary attentions, most delicately administered by Mr. Samuel Weller.\n\nIn the midst of all this jollity and conviviality, a loud ring was heard at the garden gate, to which the young gentleman who took his meals in the wash-house, immediately responded. Mr. Weller was in the height of his attentions to the pretty house-maid; Mr. Muzzle was busy doing the honours of the table; and the cook had just paused to laugh, in the very act of raising a huge morsel to her lips; when the kitchen door opened, and in walked Mr. Job Trotter.\n\nWe have said in walked Mr. Job Trotter, but the statement is not distinguished by our usual scrupulous adherence to fact. The door opened and Mr. Trotter appeared. He would have walked in, and was in the very act of doing so, indeed, when catching sight of Mr. Weller, he involuntarily shrank back a pace or two, and stood gazing on the unexpected scene before him, perfectly motionless with amazement and terror.\n\n‘Here he is!’ said Sam, rising with great glee. ‘Why we were that wery moment a-speaking o’ you. How are you? Where have you been? Come in.’\n\nLaying his hand on the mulberry collar of the unresisting Job, Mr. Weller dragged him into the kitchen; and, locking the door, handed the key to Mr. Muzzle, who very coolly buttoned it up in a side pocket.\n\n‘Well, here’s a game!’ cried Sam. ‘Only think o’ my master havin’ the pleasure o’ meeting yourn upstairs, and me havin’ the joy o’ meetin’ you down here. How are you gettin’ on, and how is the chandlery bis’ness likely to do? Well, I am so glad to see you. How happy you look. It’s quite a treat to see you; ain’t it, Mr. Muzzle?’\n\n‘Quite,’ said Mr. Muzzle.\n\n‘So cheerful he is!’ said Sam.\n\n‘In such good spirits!’ said Muzzle.\n\n‘And so glad to see us--that makes it so much more comfortable,’ said Sam. ‘Sit down; sit down.’\n\nMr. Trotter suffered himself to be forced into a chair by the fireside. He cast his small eyes, first on Mr. Weller, and then on Mr. Muzzle, but said nothing.\n\n‘Well, now,’ said Sam, ‘afore these here ladies, I should jest like to ask you, as a sort of curiosity, whether you don’t consider yourself as nice and well-behaved a young gen’l’m’n, as ever used a pink check pocket-handkerchief, and the number four collection?’\n\n‘And as was ever a-going to be married to a cook,’ said that lady indignantly. ‘The willin!’\n\n‘And leave off his evil ways, and set up in the chandlery line arterwards,’ said the housemaid.\n\n‘Now, I’ll tell you what it is, young man,’ said Mr. Muzzle solemnly, enraged at the last two allusions, ‘this here lady (pointing to the cook) keeps company with me; and when you presume, Sir, to talk of keeping chandlers’ shops with her, you injure me in one of the most delicatest points in which one man can injure another. Do you understand that, Sir?’\n\nHere Mr. Muzzle, who had a great notion of his eloquence, in which he imitated his master, paused for a reply.\n\nBut Mr. Trotter made no reply. So Mr. Muzzle proceeded in a solemn manner--\n\n‘It’s very probable, sir, that you won’t be wanted upstairs for several minutes, Sir, because my master is at this moment particularly engaged in settling the hash of your master, Sir; and therefore you’ll have leisure, Sir, for a little private talk with me, Sir. Do you understand that, Sir?’\n\nMr. Muzzle again paused for a reply; and again Mr. Trotter disappointed him.\n\n‘Well, then,’ said Mr. Muzzle, ‘I’m very sorry to have to explain myself before ladies, but the urgency of the case will be my excuse. The back kitchen’s empty, Sir. If you will step in there, Sir, Mr. Weller will see fair, and we can have mutual satisfaction till the bell rings. Follow me, Sir!’\n\nAs Mr. Muzzle uttered these words, he took a step or two towards the door; and, by way of saving time, began to pull off his coat as he walked along.\n\nNow, the cook no sooner heard the concluding words of this desperate challenge, and saw Mr. Muzzle about to put it into execution, than she uttered a loud and piercing shriek; and rushing on Mr. Job Trotter, who rose from his chair on the instant, tore and buffeted his large flat face, with an energy peculiar to excited females, and twining her hands in his long black hair, tore therefrom about enough to make five or six dozen of the very largest-sized mourning-rings. Having accomplished this feat with all the ardour which her devoted love for Mr. Muzzle inspired, she staggered back; and being a lady of very excitable and delicate feelings, she instantly fell under the dresser, and fainted away.\n\nAt this moment, the bell rang.\n\n‘That’s for you, Job Trotter,’ said Sam; and before Mr. Trotter could offer remonstrance or reply--even before he had time to stanch the wounds inflicted by the insensible lady--Sam seized one arm and Mr. Muzzle the other, and one pulling before, and the other pushing behind, they conveyed him upstairs, and into the parlour.\n\nIt was an impressive tableau. Alfred Jingle, Esquire, alias Captain Fitz-Marshall, was standing near the door with his hat in his hand, and a smile on his face, wholly unmoved by his very unpleasant situation. Confronting him, stood Mr. Pickwick, who had evidently been inculcating some high moral lesson; for his left hand was beneath his coat tail, and his right extended in air, as was his wont when delivering himself of an impressive address. At a little distance, stood Mr. Tupman with indignant countenance, carefully held back by his two younger friends; at the farther end of the room were Mr. Nupkins, Mrs. Nupkins, and Miss Nupkins, gloomily grand and savagely vexed.\n\n‘What prevents me,’ said Mr. Nupkins, with magisterial dignity, as Job was brought in--‘what prevents me from detaining these men as rogues and impostors? It is a foolish mercy. What prevents me?’\n\n‘Pride, old fellow, pride,’ replied Jingle, quite at his ease. ‘Wouldn’t do--no go--caught a captain, eh?--ha! ha! very good--husband for daughter--biter bit--make it public--not for worlds--look stupid--very!’\n\n‘Wretch,’ said Mr. Nupkins, ‘we scorn your base insinuations.’\n\n‘I always hated him,’ added Henrietta.\n\n‘Oh, of course,’ said Jingle. ‘Tall young man--old lover--Sidney Porkenham--rich--fine fellow--not so rich as captain, though, eh?--turn him away--off with him--anything for captain--nothing like captain anywhere--all the girls--raving mad--eh, Job, eh?’\n\nHere Mr. Jingle laughed very heartily; and Job, rubbing his hands with delight, uttered the first sound he had given vent to since he entered the house--a low, noiseless chuckle, which seemed to intimate that he enjoyed his laugh too much, to let any of it escape in sound.\n\n‘Mr. Nupkins,’ said the elder lady,’ this is not a fit conversation for the servants to overhear. Let these wretches be removed.’\n\n‘Certainly, my dear,’ Said Mr. Nupkins. ‘Muzzle!’\n\n‘Your Worship.’\n\n‘Open the front door.’\n\n‘Yes, your Worship.’\n\n‘Leave the house!’ said Mr. Nupkins, waving his hand emphatically.\n\nJingle smiled, and moved towards the door.\n\n‘Stay!’ said Mr. Pickwick. Jingle stopped.\n\n‘I might,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘have taken a much greater revenge for the treatment I have experienced at your hands, and that of your hypocritical friend there.’\n\nJob Trotter bowed with great politeness, and laid his hand upon his heart.\n\n‘I say,’ said Mr. Pickwick, growing gradually angry, ‘that I might have taken a greater revenge, but I content myself with exposing you, which I consider a duty I owe to society. This is a leniency, Sir, which I hope you will remember.’\n\nWhen Mr. Pickwick arrived at this point, Job Trotter, with facetious gravity, applied his hand to his ear, as if desirous not to lose a syllable he uttered.\n\n‘And I have only to add, sir,’ said Mr. Pickwick, now thoroughly angry, ‘that I consider you a rascal, and a--a--ruffian--and--and worse than any man I ever saw, or heard of, except that pious and sanctified vagabond in the mulberry livery.’\n\n‘Ha! ha!’ said Jingle, ‘good fellow, Pickwick--fine heart--stout old boy--but must not be passionate--bad thing, very--bye, bye--see you again some day--keep up your spirits--now, Job--trot!’\n\nWith these words, Mr. Jingle stuck on his hat in his old fashion, and strode out of the room. Job Trotter paused, looked round, smiled and then with a bow of mock solemnity to Mr. Pickwick, and a wink to Mr. Weller, the audacious slyness of which baffles all description, followed the footsteps of his hopeful master.\n\n‘Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick, as Mr. Weller was following.\n\n‘Sir.’\nStay here.’\n\nMr. Weller seemed uncertain.\n\n‘Stay here,’ repeated Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Mayn’t I polish that ‘ere Job off, in the front garden?’ said Mr. Weller.\n\n‘Certainly not,’ replied Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Mayn’t I kick him out o’ the gate, Sir?’ said Mr. Weller.\n\n‘Not on any account,’ replied his master.\n\nFor the first time since his engagement, Mr. Weller looked, for a moment, discontented and unhappy. But his countenance immediately cleared up; for the wily Mr. Muzzle, by concealing himself behind the street door, and rushing violently out, at the right instant, contrived with great dexterity to overturn both Mr. Jingle and his attendant, down the flight of steps, into the American aloe tubs that stood beneath.\n\n‘Having discharged my duty, Sir,’ said Mr. Pickwick to Mr. Nupkins, ‘I will, with my friends, bid you farewell. While we thank you for such hospitality as we have received, permit me to assure you, in our joint names, that we should not have accepted it, or have consented to extricate ourselves in this way, from our previous dilemma, had we not been impelled by a strong sense of duty. We return to London to-morrow. Your secret is safe with us.’\n\nHaving thus entered his protest against their treatment of the morning, Mr. Pickwick bowed low to the ladies, and notwithstanding the solicitations of the family, left the room with his friends.\n\n‘Get your hat, Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘It’s below stairs, Sir,’ said Sam, and he ran down after it.\n\nNow, there was nobody in the kitchen, but the pretty housemaid; and as Sam’s hat was mislaid, he had to look for it, and the pretty housemaid lighted him. They had to look all over the place for the hat. The pretty housemaid, in her anxiety to find it, went down on her knees, and turned over all the things that were heaped together in a little corner by the door. It was an awkward corner. You couldn’t get at it without shutting the door first.\n\n‘Here it is,’ said the pretty housemaid. ‘This is it, ain’t it?’\n\n‘Let me look,’ said Sam.\n\nThe pretty housemaid had stood the candle on the floor; and, as it gave a very dim light, Sam was obliged to go down on his knees before he could see whether it really was his own hat or not. It was a remarkably small corner, and so--it was nobody’s fault but the man’s who built the house--Sam and the pretty housemaid were necessarily very close together.\n\n‘Yes, this is it,’ said Sam. ‘Good-bye!’\n\n‘Good-bye!’ said the pretty housemaid.\n\n‘Good-bye!’ said Sam; and as he said it, he dropped the hat that had cost so much trouble in looking for.\n\n‘How awkward you are,’ said the pretty housemaid. ‘You’ll lose it again, if you don’t take care.’\n\nSo just to prevent his losing it again, she put it on for him.\n\nWhether it was that the pretty housemaid’s face looked prettier still, when it was raised towards Sam’s, or whether it was the accidental consequence of their being so near to each other, is matter of uncertainty to this day; but Sam kissed her.\n\n‘You don’t mean to say you did that on purpose,’ said the pretty housemaid, blushing.\n\n‘No, I didn’t then,’ said Sam; ‘but I will now.’\nSo he kissed her again.\n\n‘Sam!’ said Mr. Pickwick, calling over the banisters.\n\n‘Coming, Sir,’ replied Sam, running upstairs.\n\n‘How long you have been!’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘There was something behind the door, Sir, which perwented our getting it open, for ever so long, Sir,’ replied Sam.\n\nAnd this was the first passage of Mr. Weller’s first love.\n\nCHAPTER XXVI. WHICH CONTAINS A BRIEF ACCOUNT OF THE PROGRESS OF THE ACTION OF BARDELL AGAINST PICKWICK\nHaving accomplished the main end and object of his journey, by the exposure of Jingle, Mr. Pickwick resolved on immediately returning to London, with the view of becoming acquainted with the proceedings which had been taken against him, in the meantime, by Messrs. Dodson and Fogg. Acting upon this resolution with all the energy and decision of his character, he mounted to the back seat of the first coach which left Ipswich on the morning after the memorable occurrences detailed at length in the two preceding chapters; and accompanied by his three friends, and Mr. Samuel Weller, arrived in the metropolis, in perfect health and safety, the same evening.\n\nHere the friends, for a short time, separated. Messrs. Tupman, Winkle, and Snodgrass repaired to their several homes to make such preparations as might be requisite for their forthcoming visit to Dingley Dell; and Mr. Pickwick and Sam took up their present abode in very good, old- fashioned, and comfortable quarters, to wit, the George and Vulture Tavern and Hotel, George Yard, Lombard Street.\n\nMr. Pickwick had dined, finished his second pint of particular port, pulled his silk handkerchief over his head, put his feet on the fender, and thrown himself back in an easy-chair, when the entrance of Mr. Weller with his carpet-bag, aroused him from his tranquil meditation.\n\n‘Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Sir,’ said Mr. Weller.\n\n‘I have just been thinking, Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘that having left a good many things at Mrs. Bardell’s, in Goswell Street, I ought to arrange for taking them away, before I leave town again.’\n\n‘Wery good, sir,’ replied Mr. Weller.\n\n‘I could send them to Mr. Tupman’s, for the present, Sam,’ continued Mr. Pickwick, ‘but before we take them away, it is necessary that they should be looked up, and put together. I wish you would step up to Goswell Street, Sam, and arrange about it.’\n\n‘At once, Sir?’ inquired Mr. Weller.\n\n‘At once,’ replied Mr. Pickwick. ‘And stay, Sam,’ added Mr. Pickwick, pulling out his purse, ‘there is some rent to pay. The quarter is not due till Christmas, but you may pay it, and have done with it. A month’s notice terminates my tenancy. Here it is, written out. Give it, and tell Mrs. Bardell she may put a bill up, as soon as she likes.’\n\n‘Wery good, sir,’ replied Mr. Weller; ‘anythin’ more, sir?’\n\n‘Nothing more, Sam.’\n\nMr. Weller stepped slowly to the door, as if he expected something more; slowly opened it, slowly stepped out, and had slowly closed it within a couple of inches, when Mr. Pickwick called out--\n\n‘Sam.’\n\n‘Yes, sir,’ said Mr. Weller, stepping quickly back, and closing the door behind him.\n\n‘I have no objection, Sam, to your endeavouring to ascertain how Mrs. Bardell herself seems disposed towards me, and whether it is really probable that this vile and groundless action is to be carried to extremity. I say I do not object to you doing this, if you wish it, Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\nSam gave a short nod of intelligence, and left the room. Mr. Pickwick drew the silk handkerchief once more over his head, And composed himself for a nap. Mr. Weller promptly walked forth, to execute his commission.\n\nIt was nearly nine o’clock when he reached Goswell Street. A couple of candles were burning in the little front parlour, and a couple of caps were reflected on the window-blind. Mrs. Bardell had got company.\n\nMr. Weller knocked at the door, and after a pretty long interval-- occupied by the party without, in whistling a tune, and by the party within, in persuading a refractory flat candle to allow itself to be lighted--a pair of small boots pattered over the floor-cloth, and Master Bardell presented himself.\n\n‘Well, young townskip,’ said Sam, ‘how’s mother?’\n\n‘She’s pretty well,’ replied Master Bardell, ‘so am I.’\n\n‘Well, that’s a mercy,’ said Sam; ‘tell her I want to speak to her, will you, my hinfant fernomenon?’\n\nMaster Bardell, thus adjured, placed the refractory flat candle on the bottom stair, and vanished into the front parlour with his message.\n\nThe two caps, reflected on the window-blind, were the respective head- dresses of a couple of Mrs. Bardell’s most particular acquaintance, who had just stepped in, to have a quiet cup of tea, and a little warm supper of a couple of sets of pettitoes and some toasted cheese. The cheese was simmering and browning away, most delightfully, in a little Dutch oven before the fire; the pettitoes were getting on deliciously in a little tin saucepan on the hob; and Mrs. Bardell and her two friends were getting on very well, also, in a little quiet conversation about and concerning all their particular friends and acquaintance; when Master Bardell came back from answering the door, and delivered the message intrusted to him by Mr. Samuel Weller.\n\n‘Mr. Pickwick’s servant!’ said Mrs. Bardell, turning pale.\n\n‘Bless my soul!’ said Mrs. Cluppins.\n\n‘Well, I raly would not ha’ believed it, unless I had ha’ happened to ha’ been here!’ said Mrs. Sanders.\n\nMrs. Cluppins was a little, brisk, busy-looking woman; Mrs. Sanders was a big, fat, heavy-faced personage; and the two were the company.\n\nMrs. Bardell felt it proper to be agitated; and as none of the three exactly knew whether under existing circumstances, any communication, otherwise than through Dodson & Fogg, ought to be held with Mr. Pickwick’s servant, they were all rather taken by surprise. In this state of indecision, obviously the first thing to be done, was to thump the boy for finding Mr. Weller at the door. So his mother thumped him, and he cried melodiously.\n\n‘Hold your noise--do--you naughty creetur!’ said Mrs. Bardell.\n\n‘Yes; don’t worrit your poor mother,’ said Mrs. Sanders.\n\n‘She’s quite enough to worrit her, as it is, without you, Tommy,’ said Mrs. Cluppins, with sympathising resignation.\n\n‘Ah! worse luck, poor lamb!’ said Mrs. Sanders.\n\nAt all which moral reflections, Master Bardell howled the louder.\n\n‘Now, what shall I do?’ said Mrs. Bardell to Mrs. Cluppins.\n\n‘I think you ought to see him,’ replied Mrs. Cluppins. ‘But on no account without a witness.’\n\n‘I think two witnesses would be more lawful,’ said Mrs. Sanders, who, like the other friend, was bursting with curiosity.\n\n‘Perhaps he’d better come in here,’ said Mrs. Bardell.\n\n‘To be sure,’ replied Mrs. Cluppins, eagerly catching at the idea; ‘walk in, young man; and shut the street door first, please.’\n\nMr. Weller immediately took the hint; and presenting himself in the parlour, explained his business to Mrs. Bardell thus--\n\n‘Wery sorry to ‘casion any personal inconwenience, ma’am, as the housebreaker said to the old lady when he put her on the fire; but as me and my governor ‘s only jest come to town, and is jest going away agin, it can’t be helped, you see.’\n\n‘Of course, the young man can’t help the faults of his master,’ said Mrs. Cluppins, much struck by Mr. Weller’s appearance and conversation.\n\n‘Certainly not,’ chimed in Mrs. Sanders, who, from certain wistful glances at the little tin saucepan, seemed to be engaged in a mental calculation of the probable extent of the pettitoes, in the event of Sam’s being asked to stop to supper.\n\n‘So all I’ve come about, is jest this here,’ said Sam, disregarding the interruption; ‘first, to give my governor’s notice--there it is. Secondly, to pay the rent--here it is. Thirdly, to say as all his things is to be put together, and give to anybody as we sends for ‘em. Fourthly, that you may let the place as soon as you like--and that’s all.’\n\n‘Whatever has happened,’ said Mrs. Bardell, ‘I always have said, and always will say, that in every respect but one, Mr. Pickwick has always behaved himself like a perfect gentleman. His money always as good as the bank--always.’\n\nAs Mrs. Bardell said this, she applied her handkerchief to her eyes, and went out of the room to get the receipt.\n\nSam well knew that he had only to remain quiet, and the women were sure to talk; so he looked alternately at the tin saucepan, the toasted cheese, the wall, and the ceiling, in profound silence.\n\n‘Poor dear!’ said Mrs. Cluppins.\n\n‘Ah, poor thing!’ replied Mrs. Sanders.\n\nSam said nothing. He saw they were coming to the subject.\n\n‘I raly cannot contain myself,’ said Mrs. Cluppins, ‘when I think of such perjury. I don’t wish to say anything to make you uncomfortable, young man, but your master’s an old brute, and I wish I had him here to tell him so.’\n\nI wish you had,’ said Sam.\n\n‘To see how dreadful she takes on, going moping about, and taking no pleasure in nothing, except when her friends comes in, out of charity, to sit with her, and make her comfortable,’ resumed Mrs. Cluppins, glancing at the tin saucepan and the Dutch oven, ‘it’s shocking!’\n\n‘Barbareous,’ said Mrs. Sanders.\n\n‘And your master, young man! A gentleman with money, as could never feel the expense of a wife, no more than nothing,’ continued Mrs. Cluppins, with great volubility; ‘why there ain’t the faintest shade of an excuse for his behaviour! Why don’t he marry her?’\n\n‘Ah,’ said Sam, ‘to be sure; that’s the question.’\n\n‘Question, indeed,’ retorted Mrs. Cluppins, ‘she’d question him, if she’d my spirit. Hows’ever, there is law for us women, mis’rable creeturs as they’d make us, if they could; and that your master will find out, young man, to his cost, afore he’s six months older.’\n\nAt this consolatory reflection, Mrs. Cluppins bridled up, and smiled at Mrs. Sanders, who smiled back again.\n\n‘The action’s going on, and no mistake,’ thought Sam, as Mrs. Bardell re-entered with the receipt.\n\n‘Here’s the receipt, Mr. Weller,’ said Mrs. Bardell, ‘and here’s the change, and I hope you’ll take a little drop of something to keep the cold out, if it’s only for old acquaintance’ sake, Mr. Weller.’\n\nSam saw the advantage he should gain, and at once acquiesced; whereupon Mrs. Bardell produced, from a small closet, a black bottle and a wine- glass; and so great was her abstraction, in her deep mental affliction, that, after filling Mr. Weller’s glass, she brought out three more wine- glasses, and filled them too.\n\n‘Lauk, Mrs. Bardell,’ said Mrs. Cluppins, ‘see what you’ve been and done!’\n\n‘Well, that is a good one!’ ejaculated Mrs. Sanders.\n\n‘Ah, my poor head!’ said Mrs. Bardell, with a faint smile.\n\nSam understood all this, of course, so he said at once, that he never could drink before supper, unless a lady drank with him. A great deal of laughter ensued, and Mrs. Sanders volunteered to humour him, so she took a slight sip out of her glass. Then Sam said it must go all round, so they all took a slight sip. Then little Mrs. Cluppins proposed as a toast, ‘Success to Bardell agin Pickwick’; and then the ladies emptied their glasses in honour of the sentiment, and got very talkative directly.\n\n‘I suppose you’ve heard what’s going forward, Mr. Weller?’ said Mrs. Bardell.\n\n‘I’ve heerd somethin’ on it,’ replied Sam.\n\n‘It’s a terrible thing to be dragged before the public, in that way, Mr. Weller,’ said Mrs. Bardell; ‘but I see now, that it’s the only thing I ought to do, and my lawyers, Mr. Dodson and Fogg, tell me that, with the evidence as we shall call, we must succeed. I don’t know what I should do, Mr. Weller, if I didn’t.’\n\nThe mere idea of Mrs. Bardell’s failing in her action, affected Mrs. Sanders so deeply, that she was under the necessity of refilling and re- emptying her glass immediately; feeling, as she said afterwards, that if she hadn’t had the presence of mind to do so, she must have dropped.\n\n‘Ven is it expected to come on?’ inquired Sam.\n\n‘Either in February or March,’ replied Mrs. Bardell.\n\n‘What a number of witnesses there’ll be, won’t there?’ said Mrs. Cluppins.\n\n‘Ah! won’t there!’ replied Mrs. Sanders.\n\n‘And won’t Mr. Dodson and Fogg be wild if the plaintiff shouldn’t get it?’ added Mrs. Cluppins, ‘when they do it all on speculation!’\n\n‘Ah! won’t they!’ said Mrs. Sanders.\n\n‘But the plaintiff must get it,’ resumed Mrs. Cluppins.\n\n‘I hope so,’ said Mrs. Bardell.\n\n‘Oh, there can’t be any doubt about it,’ rejoined Mrs. Sanders.\n\n‘Vell,’ said Sam, rising and setting down his glass, ‘all I can say is, that I vish you may get it.’\n\n‘Thank’ee, Mr. Weller,’ said Mrs. Bardell fervently.\n\n‘And of them Dodson and Foggs, as does these sort o’ things on spec,’ continued Mr. Weller, ‘as vell as for the other kind and gen’rous people o’ the same purfession, as sets people by the ears, free gratis for nothin’, and sets their clerks to work to find out little disputes among their neighbours and acquaintances as vants settlin’ by means of lawsuits--all I can say o’ them is, that I vish they had the reward I’d give ‘em.’\n\n‘Ah, I wish they had the reward that every kind and generous heart would be inclined to bestow upon them!’ said the gratified Mrs. Bardell.\n\n‘Amen to that,’ replied Sam, ‘and a fat and happy liven’ they’d get out of it! Wish you good-night, ladies.’\n\nTo the great relief of Mrs. Sanders, Sam was allowed to depart without any reference, on the part of the hostess, to the pettitoes and toasted cheese; to which the ladies, with such juvenile assistance as Master Bardell could afford, soon afterwards rendered the amplest justice-- indeed they wholly vanished before their strenuous exertions.\n\nMr. Weller wended his way back to the George and Vulture, and faithfully recounted to his master, such indications of the sharp practice of Dodson & Fogg, as he had contrived to pick up in his visit to Mrs. Bardell’s. An interview with Mr. Perker, next day, more than confirmed Mr. Weller’s statement; and Mr. Pickwick was fain to prepare for his Christmas visit to Dingley Dell, with the pleasant anticipation that some two or three months afterwards, an action brought against him for damages sustained by reason of a breach of promise of marriage, would be publicly tried in the Court of Common Pleas; the plaintiff having all the advantages derivable, not only from the force of circumstances, but from the sharp practice of Dodson & Fogg to boot.\n\nCHAPTER XXVII. SAMUEL WELLER MAKES A PILGRIMAGE TO DORKING, AND BEHOLDS HIS MOTHER-IN-LAW\nThere still remaining an interval of two days before the time agreed upon for the departure of the Pickwickians to Dingley Dell, Mr. Weller sat himself down in a back room at the George and Vulture, after eating an early dinner, to muse on the best way of disposing of his time. It was a remarkably fine day; and he had not turned the matter over in his mind ten minutes, when he was suddenly stricken filial and affectionate; and it occurred to him so strongly that he ought to go down and see his father, and pay his duty to his mother-in-law, that he was lost in astonishment at his own remissness in never thinking of this moral obligation before. Anxious to atone for his past neglect without another hour’s delay, he straightway walked upstairs to Mr. Pickwick, and requested leave of absence for this laudable purpose.\n\n‘Certainly, Sam, certainly,’ said Mr. Pickwick, his eyes glistening with delight at this manifestation of filial feeling on the part of his attendant; ‘certainly, Sam.’\n\nMr. Weller made a grateful bow.\n\n‘I am very glad to see that you have so high a sense of your duties as a son, Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘I always had, sir,’ replied Mr. Weller.\n\n‘That’s a very gratifying reflection, Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick approvingly.\n\n‘Wery, Sir,’ replied Mr. Weller; ‘if ever I wanted anythin’ o’ my father, I always asked for it in a wery ‘spectful and obligin’ manner. If he didn’t give it me, I took it, for fear I should be led to do anythin’ wrong, through not havin’ it. I saved him a world o’ trouble this vay, Sir.’\n\n‘That’s not precisely what I meant, Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick, shaking his head, with a slight smile.\n\n‘All good feelin’, sir--the wery best intentions, as the gen’l’m’n said ven he run away from his wife ‘cos she seemed unhappy with him,’ replied Mr. Weller.\n\n‘You may go, Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Thank’ee, Sir,’ replied Mr. Weller; and having made his best bow, and put on his best clothes, Sam planted himself on the top of the Arundel coach, and journeyed on to Dorking.\n\nThe Marquis of Granby, in Mrs. Weller’s time, was quite a model of a roadside public-house of the better class--just large enough to be convenient, and small enough to be snug. On the opposite side of the road was a large sign-board on a high post, representing the head and shoulders of a gentleman with an apoplectic countenance, in a red coat with deep blue facings, and a touch of the same blue over his three- cornered hat, for a sky. Over that again were a pair of flags; beneath the last button of his coat were a couple of cannon; and the whole formed an expressive and undoubted likeness of the Marquis of Granby of glorious memory. The bar window displayed a choice collection of geranium plants, and a well-dusted row of spirit phials. The open shutters bore a variety of golden inscriptions, eulogistic of good beds and neat wines; and the choice group of countrymen and hostlers lounging about the stable door and horse-trough, afforded presumptive proof of the excellent quality of the ale and spirits which were sold within. Sam Weller paused, when he dismounted from the coach, to note all these little indications of a thriving business, with the eye of an experienced traveller; and having done so, stepped in at once, highly satisfied with everything he had observed.\n\n‘Now, then!’ said a shrill female voice the instant Sam thrust his head in at the door, ‘what do you want, young man?’\n\nSam looked round in the direction whence the voice proceeded. It came from a rather stout lady of comfortable appearance, who was seated beside the fireplace in the bar, blowing the fire to make the kettle boil for tea. She was not alone; for on the other side of the fireplace, sitting bolt upright in a high-backed chair, was a man in threadbare black clothes, with a back almost as long and stiff as that of the chair itself, who caught Sam’s most particular and especial attention at once.\n\nHe was a prim-faced, red-nosed man, with a long, thin countenance, and a semi-rattlesnake sort of eye--rather sharp, but decidedly bad. He wore very short trousers, and black cotton stockings, which, like the rest of his apparel, were particularly rusty. His looks were starched, but his white neckerchief was not, and its long limp ends straggled over his closely-buttoned waistcoat in a very uncouth and unpicturesque fashion. A pair of old, worn, beaver gloves, a broad-brimmed hat, and a faded green umbrella, with plenty of whalebone sticking through the bottom, as if to counterbalance the want of a handle at the top, lay on a chair beside him; and, being disposed in a very tidy and careful manner, seemed to imply that the red-nosed man, whoever he was, had no intention of going away in a hurry.\n\nTo do the red-nosed man justice, he would have been very far from wise if he had entertained any such intention; for, to judge from all appearances, he must have been possessed of a most desirable circle of acquaintance, if he could have reasonably expected to be more comfortable anywhere else. The fire was blazing brightly under the influence of the bellows, and the kettle was singing gaily under the influence of both. A small tray of tea-things was arranged on the table; a plate of hot buttered toast was gently simmering before the fire; and the red-nosed man himself was busily engaged in converting a large slice of bread into the same agreeable edible, through the instrumentality of a long brass toasting-fork. Beside him stood a glass of reeking hot pine-apple rum-and-water, with a slice of lemon in it; and every time the red-nosed man stopped to bring the round of toast to his eye, with the view of ascertaining how it got on, he imbibed a drop or two of the hot pine-apple rum-and-water, and smiled upon the rather stout lady, as she blew the fire.\n\nSam was so lost in the contemplation of this comfortable scene, that he suffered the first inquiry of the rather stout lady to pass unheeded. It was not until it had been twice repeated, each time in a shriller tone, that he became conscious of the impropriety of his behaviour.\n\n‘Governor in?’ inquired Sam, in reply to the question.\n\n‘No, he isn’t,’ replied Mrs. Weller; for the rather stout lady was no other than the quondam relict and sole executrix of the dead-and-gone Mr. Clarke; ‘no, he isn’t, and I don’t expect him, either.’\n\n‘I suppose he’s drivin’ up to-day?’ said Sam.\n\n‘He may be, or he may not,’ replied Mrs. Weller, buttering the round of toast which the red-nosed man had just finished. ‘I don’t know, and, what’s more, I don’t care.--Ask a blessin’, Mr. Stiggins.’\n\nThe red-nosed man did as he was desired, and instantly commenced on the toast with fierce voracity.\n\nThe appearance of the red-nosed man had induced Sam, at first sight, to more than half suspect that he was the deputy-shepherd of whom his estimable parent had spoken. The moment he saw him eat, all doubt on the subject was removed, and he perceived at once that if he purposed to take up his temporary quarters where he was, he must make his footing good without delay. He therefore commenced proceedings by putting his arm over the half-door of the bar, coolly unbolting it, and leisurely walking in.\n\n‘Mother-in-law,’ said Sam, ‘how are you?’\n\n‘Why, I do believe he is a Weller!’ said Mrs. W., raising her eyes to Sam’s face, with no very gratified expression of countenance.\n\n‘I rayther think he is,’ said the imperturbable Sam; ‘and I hope this here reverend gen’l’m’n ‘ll excuse me saying that I wish I was the Weller as owns you, mother-in-law.’\n\nThis was a double-barrelled compliment. It implied that Mrs. Weller was a most agreeable female, and also that Mr. Stiggins had a clerical appearance. It made a visible impression at once; and Sam followed up his advantage by kissing his mother-in-law.\n\n‘Get along with you!’ said Mrs. Weller, pushing him away.\n\n‘For shame, young man!’ said the gentleman with the red nose.\n\n‘No offence, sir, no offence,’ replied Sam; ‘you’re wery right, though; it ain’t the right sort o’ thing, ven mothers-in-law is young and good- looking, is it, Sir?’\n\n‘It’s all vanity,’ said Mr. Stiggins.\n\n‘Ah, so it is,’ said Mrs. Weller, setting her cap to rights.\n\nSam thought it was, too, but he held his peace.\n\nThe deputy-shepherd seemed by no means best pleased with Sam’s arrival; and when the first effervescence of the compliment had subsided, even Mrs. Weller looked as if she could have spared him without the smallest inconvenience. However, there he was; and as he couldn’t be decently turned out, they all three sat down to tea.\n\n‘And how’s father?’ said Sam.\n\nAt this inquiry, Mrs. Weller raised her hands, and turned up her eyes, as if the subject were too painful to be alluded to.\n\nMr. Stiggins groaned.\n\n‘What’s the matter with that ‘ere gen’l’m’n?’ inquired Sam.\n\n‘He’s shocked at the way your father goes on in,’ replied Mrs. Weller.\n\n‘Oh, he is, is he?’ said Sam.\n\n‘And with too good reason,’ added Mrs. Weller gravely.\n\nMr. Stiggins took up a fresh piece of toast, and groaned heavily.\n\n‘He is a dreadful reprobate,’ said Mrs. Weller.\n\n‘A man of wrath!’ exclaimed Mr. Stiggins. He took a large semi-circular bite out of the toast, and groaned again.\n\nSam felt very strongly disposed to give the reverend Mr. Stiggins something to groan for, but he repressed his inclination, and merely asked, ‘What’s the old ‘un up to now?’\n\n‘Up to, indeed!’ said Mrs. Weller, ‘Oh, he has a hard heart. Night after night does this excellent man--don’t frown, Mr. Stiggins; I will say you are an excellent man--come and sit here, for hours together, and it has not the least effect upon him.’\n\nWell, that is odd,’ said Sam; ‘it ‘ud have a wery considerable effect upon me, if I wos in his place; I know that.’\n\n‘The fact is, my young friend,’ said Mr. Stiggins solemnly, ‘he has an obderrate bosom. Oh, my young friend, who else could have resisted the pleading of sixteen of our fairest sisters, and withstood their exhortations to subscribe to our noble society for providing the infant negroes in the West Indies with flannel waistcoats and moral pocket- handkerchiefs?’\n\n‘What’s a moral pocket-ankercher?’ said Sam; ‘I never see one o’ them articles o’ furniter.’\n\n‘Those which combine amusement With instruction, my young friend,’ replied Mr. Stiggins, ‘blending select tales with wood-cuts.’\n\n‘Oh, I know,’ said Sam; ‘them as hangs up in the linen-drapers’ shops, with beggars’ petitions and all that ‘ere upon ‘em?’\n\nMr. Stiggins began a third round of toast, and nodded assent.\n\n‘And he wouldn’t be persuaded by the ladies, wouldn’t he?’ said Sam.\n\n‘Sat and smoked his pipe, and said the infant negroes were--what did he say the infant negroes were?’ said Mrs. Weller.\n\n‘Little humbugs,’ replied Mr. Stiggins, deeply affected.\n\n‘Said the infant negroes were little humbugs,’ repeated Mrs. Weller. And they both groaned at the atrocious conduct of the elder Mr. Weller.\n\nA great many more iniquities of a similar nature might have been disclosed, only the toast being all eaten, the tea having got very weak, and Sam holding out no indications of meaning to go, Mr. Stiggins suddenly recollected that he had a most pressing appointment with the shepherd, and took himself off accordingly.\n\nThe tea-things had been scarcely put away, and the hearth swept up, when the London coach deposited Mr. Weller, senior, at the door; his legs deposited him in the bar; and his eyes showed him his son.\n\n‘What, Sammy!’ exclaimed the father.\n\n‘What, old Nobs!’ ejaculated the son. And they shook hands heartily.\n\n‘Wery glad to see you, Sammy,’ said the elder Mr. Weller, ‘though how you’ve managed to get over your mother-in-law, is a mystery to me. I only vish you’d write me out the receipt, that’s all.’\n\n‘Hush!’ said Sam, ‘she’s at home, old feller.’\n\nShe ain’t vithin hearin’,’ replied Mr. Weller; ‘she always goes and blows up, downstairs, for a couple of hours arter tea; so we’ll just give ourselves a damp, Sammy.’\n\nSaying this, Mr. Weller mixed two glasses of spirits-and-water, and produced a couple of pipes. The father and son sitting down opposite each other; Sam on one side of the fire, in the high-backed chair, and Mr. Weller, senior, on the other, in an easy ditto, they proceeded to enjoy themselves with all due gravity.\n\n‘Anybody been here, Sammy?’ asked Mr. Weller, senior, dryly, after a long silence.\n\nSam nodded an expressive assent.\n\n‘Red-nosed chap?’ inquired Mr. Weller.\n\nSam nodded again.\n\n‘Amiable man that ‘ere, Sammy,’ said Mr. Weller, smoking violently.\n\n‘Seems so,’ observed Sam.\n\n‘Good hand at accounts,’ said Mr. Weller. ‘Is he?’ said Sam.\n\n‘Borrows eighteenpence on Monday, and comes on Tuesday for a shillin’ to make it up half-a-crown; calls again on Vensday for another half-crown to make it five shillin’s; and goes on, doubling, till he gets it up to a five pund note in no time, like them sums in the ‘rithmetic book ‘bout the nails in the horse’s shoes, Sammy.’\n\nSam intimated by a nod that he recollected the problem alluded to by his parent.\n\n‘So you vouldn’t subscribe to the flannel veskits?’ said Sam, after another interval of smoking.\n\n‘Cert’nly not,’ replied Mr. Weller; ‘what’s the good o’ flannel veskits to the young niggers abroad? But I’ll tell you what it is, Sammy,’ said Mr. Weller, lowering his voice, and bending across the fireplace; ‘I’d come down wery handsome towards strait veskits for some people at home.’\n\nAs Mr. Weller said this, he slowly recovered his former position, and winked at his first-born, in a profound manner.\n\n‘It cert’nly seems a queer start to send out pocket-’ankerchers to people as don’t know the use on ‘em,’ observed Sam.\n\n‘They’re alvays a-doin’ some gammon of that sort, Sammy,’ replied his father. ‘T’other Sunday I wos walkin’ up the road, wen who should I see, a-standin’ at a chapel door, with a blue soup-plate in her hand, but your mother-in-law! I werily believe there was change for a couple o’ suv’rins in it, then, Sammy, all in ha’pence; and as the people come out, they rattled the pennies in it, till you’d ha’ thought that no mortal plate as ever was baked, could ha’ stood the wear and tear. What d’ye think it was all for?’\n\n‘For another tea-drinkin’, perhaps,’ said Sam.\n\n‘Not a bit on it,’ replied the father; ‘for the shepherd’s water-rate, Sammy.’\n\n‘The shepherd’s water-rate!’ said Sam.\n\n‘Ay,’ replied Mr. Weller, ‘there was three quarters owin’, and the shepherd hadn’t paid a farden, not he--perhaps it might be on account that the water warn’t o’ much use to him, for it’s wery little o’ that tap he drinks, Sammy, wery; he knows a trick worth a good half-dozen of that, he does. Hows’ever, it warn’t paid, and so they cuts the water off. Down goes the shepherd to chapel, gives out as he’s a persecuted saint, and says he hopes the heart of the turncock as cut the water off, ‘ll be softened, and turned in the right vay, but he rayther thinks he’s booked for somethin’ uncomfortable. Upon this, the women calls a meetin’, sings a hymn, wotes your mother-in-law into the chair, wolunteers a collection next Sunday, and hands it all over to the shepherd. And if he ain’t got enough out on ‘em, Sammy, to make him free of the water company for life,’ said Mr. Weller, in conclusion, ‘I’m one Dutchman, and you’re another, and that’s all about it.’\n\nMr. Weller smoked for some minutes in silence, and then resumed--\n\n‘The worst o’ these here shepherds is, my boy, that they reg’larly turns the heads of all the young ladies, about here. Lord bless their little hearts, they thinks it’s all right, and don’t know no better; but they’re the wictims o’ gammon, Samivel, they’re the wictims o’ gammon.’\n\n‘I s’pose they are,’ said Sam.\n\n‘Nothin’ else,’ said Mr. Weller, shaking his head gravely; ‘and wot aggrawates me, Samivel, is to see ‘em a-wastin’ all their time and labour in making clothes for copper-coloured people as don’t want ‘em, and taking no notice of flesh-coloured Christians as do. If I’d my vay, Samivel, I’d just stick some o’ these here lazy shepherds behind a heavy wheelbarrow, and run ‘em up and down a fourteen-inch-wide plank all day. That ‘ud shake the nonsense out of ‘em, if anythin’ vould.’\n\nMr. Weller, having delivered this gentle recipe with strong emphasis, eked out by a variety of nods and contortions of the eye, emptied his glass at a draught, and knocked the ashes out of his pipe, with native dignity.\n\nHe was engaged in this operation, when a shrill voice was heard in the passage.\n\n‘Here’s your dear relation, Sammy,’ said Mr. Weller; and Mrs. W. hurried into the room.\n\n‘Oh, you’ve come back, have you!’ said Mrs. Weller.\n\n‘Yes, my dear,’ replied Mr. Weller, filling a fresh pipe.\n\n‘Has Mr. Stiggins been back?’ said Mrs. Weller.\n\n‘No, my dear, he hasn’t,’ replied Mr. Weller, lighting the pipe by the ingenious process of holding to the bowl thereof, between the tongs, a red-hot coal from the adjacent fire; and what’s more, my dear, I shall manage to surwive it, if he don’t come back at all.’\n\n‘Ugh, you wretch!’ said Mrs. Weller.\n\n‘Thank’ee, my love,’ said Mr. Weller.\n\n‘Come, come, father,’ said Sam, ‘none o’ these little lovin’s afore strangers. Here’s the reverend gen’l’m’n a-comin’ in now.’\n\nAt this announcement, Mrs. Weller hastily wiped off the tears which she had just begun to force on; and Mr. W. drew his chair sullenly into the chimney-corner.\n\nMr. Stiggins was easily prevailed on to take another glass of the hot pine-apple rum-and-water, and a second, and a third, and then to refresh himself with a slight supper, previous to beginning again. He sat on the same side as Mr. Weller, senior; and every time he could contrive to do so, unseen by his wife, that gentleman indicated to his son the hidden emotions of his bosom, by shaking his fist over the deputy-shepherd’s head; a process which afforded his son the most unmingled delight and satisfaction, the more especially as Mr. Stiggins went on, quietly drinking the hot pine-apple rum-and-water, wholly unconscious of what was going forward.\n\nThe major part of the conversation was confined to Mrs. Weller and the reverend Mr. Stiggins; and the topics principally descanted on, were the virtues of the shepherd, the worthiness of his flock, and the high crimes and misdemeanours of everybody beside--dissertations which the elder Mr. Weller occasionally interrupted by half-suppressed references to a gentleman of the name of Walker, and other running commentaries of the same kind.\n\nAt length Mr. Stiggins, with several most indubitable symptoms of having quite as much pine-apple rum-and-water about him as he could comfortably accommodate, took his hat, and his leave; and Sam was, immediately afterwards, shown to bed by his father. The respectable old gentleman wrung his hand fervently, and seemed disposed to address some observation to his son; but on Mrs. Weller advancing towards him, he appeared to relinquish that intention, and abruptly bade him good-night.\n\nSam was up betimes next day, and having partaken of a hasty breakfast, prepared to return to London. He had scarcely set foot without the house, when his father stood before him.\n\n‘Goin’, Sammy?’ inquired Mr. Weller.\n\n‘Off at once,’ replied Sam.\n\n‘I vish you could muffle that ‘ere Stiggins, and take him vith you,’ said Mr. Weller.\n\n‘I am ashamed on you!’ said Sam reproachfully; ‘what do you let him show his red nose in the Markis o’ Granby at all, for?’\n\nMr. Weller the elder fixed on his son an earnest look, and replied, ‘’Cause I’m a married man, Samivel, ‘cause I’m a married man. Ven you’re a married man, Samivel, you’ll understand a good many things as you don’t understand now; but vether it’s worth while goin’ through so much, to learn so little, as the charity-boy said ven he got to the end of the alphabet, is a matter o’ taste. I rayther think it isn’t.’\n\nWell,’ said Sam, ‘good-bye.’\n\n‘Tar, tar, Sammy,’ replied his father.\n\n‘I’ve only got to say this here,’ said Sam, stopping short, ‘that if I was the properiator o’ the Markis o’ Granby, and that ‘ere Stiggins came and made toast in my bar, I’d--’\n\n‘What?’ interposed Mr. Weller, with great anxiety. ‘What?’\n\n‘Pison his rum-and-water,’ said Sam.\n\n‘No!’ said Mr. Weller, shaking his son eagerly by the hand, ‘would you raly, Sammy-would you, though?’\n\n‘I would,’ said Sam. ‘I wouldn’t be too hard upon him at first. I’d drop him in the water-butt, and put the lid on; and if I found he was insensible to kindness, I’d try the other persvasion.’\n\nThe elder Mr. Weller bestowed a look of deep, unspeakable admiration on his son, and, having once more grasped his hand, walked slowly away, revolving in his mind the numerous reflections to which his advice had given rise.\n\nSam looked after him, until he turned a corner of the road; and then set forward on his walk to London. He meditated at first, on the probable consequences of his own advice, and the likelihood of his father’s adopting it. He dismissed the subject from his mind, however, with the consolatory reflection that time alone would show; and this is the reflection we would impress upon the reader.\n\nCHAPTER XXVIII. A GOOD-HUMOURED CHRISTMAS CHAPTER, CONTAINING AN ACCOUNT OF A WEDDING, AND SOME OTHER SPORTS BESIDE: WHICH ALTHOUGH IN THEIR WAY, EVEN AS GOOD CUSTOMS AS MARRIAGE ITSELF, ARE NOT QUITE SO RELIGIOUSLY KEPT UP, IN THESE DEGENERATE TIMES\n\nAs brisk as bees, if not altogether as light as fairies, did the four Pickwickians assemble on the morning of the twenty-second day of December, in the year of grace in which these, their faithfully-recorded adventures, were undertaken and accomplished. Christmas was close at hand, in all his bluff and hearty honesty; it was the season of hospitality, merriment, and open-heartedness; the old year was preparing, like an ancient philosopher, to call his friends around him, and amidst the sound of feasting and revelry to pass gently and calmly away. Gay and merry was the time; and right gay and merry were at least four of the numerous hearts that were gladdened by its coming.\n\nAnd numerous indeed are the hearts to which Christmas brings a brief season of happiness and enjoyment. How many families, whose members have been dispersed and scattered far and wide, in the restless struggles of life, are then reunited, and meet once again in that happy state of companionship and mutual goodwill, which is a source of such pure and unalloyed delight; and one so incompatible with the cares and sorrows of the world, that the religious belief of the most civilised nations, and the rude traditions of the roughest savages, alike number it among the first joys of a future condition of existence, provided for the blessed and happy! How many old recollections, and how many dormant sympathies, does Christmas time awaken!\n\nWe write these words now, many miles distant from the spot at which, year after year, we met on that day, a merry and joyous circle. Many of the hearts that throbbed so gaily then, have ceased to beat; many of the looks that shone so brightly then, have ceased to glow; the hands we grasped, have grown cold; the eyes we sought, have hid their lustre in the grave; and yet the old house, the room, the merry voices and smiling faces, the jest, the laugh, the most minute and trivial circumstances connected with those happy meetings, crowd upon our mind at each recurrence of the season, as if the last assemblage had been but yesterday! Happy, happy Christmas, that can win us back to the delusions of our childish days; that can recall to the old man the pleasures of his youth; that can transport the sailor and the traveller, thousands of miles away, back to his own fireside and his quiet home!\n\nBut we are so taken up and occupied with the good qualities of this saint Christmas, that we are keeping Mr. Pickwick and his friends waiting in the cold on the outside of the Muggleton coach, which they have just attained, well wrapped up in great-coats, shawls, and comforters. The portmanteaus and carpet-bags have been stowed away, and Mr. Weller and the guard are endeavouring to insinuate into the fore- boot a huge cod-fish several sizes too large for it--which is snugly packed up, in a long brown basket, with a layer of straw over the top, and which has been left to the last, in order that he may repose in safety on the half-dozen barrels of real native oysters, all the property of Mr. Pickwick, which have been arranged in regular order at the bottom of the receptacle. The interest displayed in Mr. Pickwick’s countenance is most intense, as Mr. Weller and the guard try to squeeze the cod-fish into the boot, first head first, and then tail first, and then top upward, and then bottom upward, and then side-ways, and then long-ways, all of which artifices the implacable cod-fish sturdily resists, until the guard accidentally hits him in the very middle of the basket, whereupon he suddenly disappears into the boot, and with him, the head and shoulders of the guard himself, who, not calculating upon so sudden a cessation of the passive resistance of the cod-fish, experiences a very unexpected shock, to the unsmotherable delight of all the porters and bystanders. Upon this, Mr. Pickwick smiles with great good-humour, and drawing a shilling from his waistcoat pocket, begs the guard, as he picks himself out of the boot, to drink his health in a glass of hot brandy-and-water; at which the guard smiles too, and Messrs. Snodgrass, Winkle, and Tupman, all smile in company. The guard and Mr. Weller disappear for five minutes, most probably to get the hot brandy-and-water, for they smell very strongly of it, when they return, the coachman mounts to the box, Mr. Weller jumps up behind, the Pickwickians pull their coats round their legs and their shawls over their noses, the helpers pull the horse-cloths off, the coachman shouts out a cheery ‘All right,’ and away they go.\n\nThey have rumbled through the streets, and jolted over the stones, and at length reach the wide and open country. The wheels skim over the hard and frosty ground; and the horses, bursting into a canter at a smart crack of the whip, step along the road as if the load behind them-- coach, passengers, cod-fish, oyster-barrels, and all--were but a feather at their heels. They have descended a gentle slope, and enter upon a level, as compact and dry as a solid block of marble, two miles long. Another crack of the whip, and on they speed, at a smart gallop, the horses tossing their heads and rattling the harness, as if in exhilaration at the rapidity of the motion; while the coachman, holding whip and reins in one hand, takes off his hat with the other, and resting it on his knees, pulls out his handkerchief, and wipes his forehead, partly because he has a habit of doing it, and partly because it’s as well to show the passengers how cool he is, and what an easy thing it is to drive four-in-hand, when you have had as much practice as he has. Having done this very leisurely (otherwise the effect would be materially impaired), he replaces his handkerchief, pulls on his hat, adjusts his gloves, squares his elbows, cracks the whip again, and on they speed, more merrily than before.\n\nA few small houses, scattered on either side of the road, betoken the entrance to some town or village. The lively notes of the guard’s key- bugle vibrate in the clear cold air, and wake up the old gentleman inside, who, carefully letting down the window-sash half-way, and standing sentry over the air, takes a short peep out, and then carefully pulling it up again, informs the other inside that they’re going to change directly; on which the other inside wakes himself up, and determines to postpone his next nap until after the stoppage. Again the bugle sounds lustily forth, and rouses the cottager’s wife and children, who peep out at the house door, and watch the coach till it turns the corner, when they once more crouch round the blazing fire, and throw on another log of wood against father comes home; while father himself, a full mile off, has just exchanged a friendly nod with the coachman, and turned round to take a good long stare at the vehicle as it whirls away.\n\nAnd now the bugle plays a lively air as the coach rattles through the ill-paved streets of a country town; and the coachman, undoing the buckle which keeps his ribands together, prepares to throw them off the moment he stops. Mr. Pickwick emerges from his coat collar, and looks about him with great curiosity; perceiving which, the coachman informs Mr. Pickwick of the name of the town, and tells him it was market-day yesterday, both of which pieces of information Mr. Pickwick retails to his fellow-passengers; whereupon they emerge from their coat collars too, and look about them also. Mr. Winkle, who sits at the extreme edge, with one leg dangling in the air, is nearly precipitated into the street, as the coach twists round the sharp corner by the cheesemonger’s shop, and turns into the market-place; and before Mr. Snodgrass, who sits next to him, has recovered from his alarm, they pull up at the inn yard where the fresh horses, with cloths on, are already waiting. The coachman throws down the reins and gets down himself, and the other outside passengers drop down also; except those who have no great confidence in their ability to get up again; and they remain where they are, and stamp their feet against the coach to warm them--looking, with longing eyes and red noses, at the bright fire in the inn bar, and the sprigs of holly with red berries which ornament the window.\n\nBut the guard has delivered at the corn-dealer’s shop, the brown paper packet he took out of the little pouch which hangs over his shoulder by a leathern strap; and has seen the horses carefully put to; and has thrown on the pavement the saddle which was brought from London on the coach roof; and has assisted in the conference between the coachman and the hostler about the gray mare that hurt her off fore-leg last Tuesday; and he and Mr. Weller are all right behind, and the coachman is all right in front, and the old gentleman inside, who has kept the window down full two inches all this time, has pulled it up again, and the cloths are off, and they are all ready for starting, except the ‘two stout gentlemen,’ whom the coachman inquires after with some impatience. Hereupon the coachman, and the guard, and Sam Weller, and Mr. Winkle, and Mr. Snodgrass, and all the hostlers, and every one of the idlers, who are more in number than all the others put together, shout for the missing gentlemen as loud as they can bawl. A distant response is heard from the yard, and Mr. Pickwick and Mr. Tupman come running down it, quite out of breath, for they have been having a glass of ale a-piece, and Mr. Pickwick’s fingers are so cold that he has been full five minutes before he could find the sixpence to pay for it. The coachman shouts an admonitory ‘Now then, gen’l’m’n,’ the guard re-echoes it; the old gentleman inside thinks it a very extraordinary thing that people will get down when they know there isn’t time for it; Mr. Pickwick struggles up on one side, Mr. Tupman on the other; Mr. Winkle cries ‘All right’; and off they start. Shawls are pulled up, coat collars are readjusted, the pavement ceases, the houses disappear; and they are once again dashing along the open road, with the fresh clear air blowing in their faces, and gladdening their very hearts within them.\n\nSuch was the progress of Mr. Pickwick and his friends by the Muggleton Telegraph, on their way to Dingley Dell; and at three o’clock that afternoon they all stood high and dry, safe and sound, hale and hearty, upon the steps of the Blue Lion, having taken on the road quite enough of ale and brandy, to enable them to bid defiance to the frost that was binding up the earth in its iron fetters, and weaving its beautiful network upon the trees and hedges. Mr. Pickwick was busily engaged in counting the barrels of oysters and superintending the disinterment of the cod-fish, when he felt himself gently pulled by the skirts of the coat. Looking round, he discovered that the individual who resorted to this mode of catching his attention was no other than Mr. Wardle’s favourite page, better known to the readers of this unvarnished history, by the distinguishing appellation of the fat boy.\n\n‘Aha!’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Aha!’ said the fat boy.\n\nAs he said it, he glanced from the cod-fish to the oyster-barrels, and chuckled joyously. He was fatter than ever.\n\n‘Well, you look rosy enough, my young friend,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘I’ve been asleep, right in front of the taproom fire,’ replied the fat boy, who had heated himself to the colour of a new chimney-pot, in the course of an hour’s nap. ‘Master sent me over with the chay-cart, to carry your luggage up to the house. He’d ha’ sent some saddle-horses, but he thought you’d rather walk, being a cold day.’\n\n‘Yes, yes,’ said Mr. Pickwick hastily, for he remembered how they had travelled over nearly the same ground on a previous occasion. ‘Yes, we would rather walk. Here, Sam!’\n\n‘Sir,’ said Mr. Weller.\n\n‘Help Mr. Wardle’s servant to put the packages into the cart, and then ride on with him. We will walk forward at once.’\n\nHaving given this direction, and settled with the coachman, Mr. Pickwick and his three friends struck into the footpath across the fields, and walked briskly away, leaving Mr. Weller and the fat boy confronted together for the first time. Sam looked at the fat boy with great astonishment, but without saying a word; and began to stow the luggage rapidly away in the cart, while the fat boy stood quietly by, and seemed to think it a very interesting sort of thing to see Mr. Weller working by himself.\n\n‘There,’ said Sam, throwing in the last carpet-bag, ‘there they are!’\n\n‘Yes,’ said the fat boy, in a very satisfied tone, ‘there they are.’\n\n‘Vell, young twenty stun,’ said Sam, ‘you’re a nice specimen of a prize boy, you are!’\n\nThank’ee,’ said the fat boy.\n\n‘You ain’t got nothin’ on your mind as makes you fret yourself, have you?’ inquired Sam.\n\n‘Not as I knows on,’ replied the fat boy.\n\n‘I should rayther ha’ thought, to look at you, that you was a-labourin’ under an unrequited attachment to some young ‘ooman,’ said Sam.\n\nThe fat boy shook his head.\n\n‘Vell,’ said Sam, ‘I am glad to hear it. Do you ever drink anythin’?’\n\n‘I likes eating better,’ replied the boy.\n\n‘Ah,’ said Sam, ‘I should ha’ s’posed that; but what I mean is, should you like a drop of anythin’ as’d warm you? but I s’pose you never was cold, with all them elastic fixtures, was you?’\n\n‘Sometimes,’ replied the boy; ‘and I likes a drop of something, when it’s good.’\n\n‘Oh, you do, do you?’ said Sam, ‘come this way, then!’\n\nThe Blue Lion tap was soon gained, and the fat boy swallowed a glass of liquor without so much as winking--a feat which considerably advanced him in Mr. Weller’s good opinion. Mr. Weller having transacted a similar piece of business on his own account, they got into the cart.\n\n‘Can you drive?’ said the fat boy.\n\n‘I should rayther think so,’ replied Sam.\n\n‘There, then,’ said the fat boy, putting the reins in his hand, and pointing up a lane, ‘it’s as straight as you can go; you can’t miss it.’\n\nWith these words, the fat boy laid himself affectionately down by the side of the cod-fish, and, placing an oyster-barrel under his head for a pillow, fell asleep instantaneously.\n\n‘Well,’ said Sam, ‘of all the cool boys ever I set my eyes on, this here young gen’l’m’n is the coolest. Come, wake up, young dropsy!’\n\nBut as young dropsy evinced no symptoms of returning animation, Sam Weller sat himself down in front of the cart, and starting the old horse with a jerk of the rein, jogged steadily on, towards the Manor Farm.\n\nMeanwhile, Mr. Pickwick and his friends having walked their blood into active circulation, proceeded cheerfully on. The paths were hard; the grass was crisp and frosty; the air had a fine, dry, bracing coldness; and the rapid approach of the gray twilight (slate-coloured is a better term in frosty weather) made them look forward with pleasant anticipation to the comforts which awaited them at their hospitable entertainer’s. It was the sort of afternoon that might induce a couple of elderly gentlemen, in a lonely field, to take off their greatcoats and play at leap-frog in pure lightness of heart and gaiety; and we firmly believe that had Mr. Tupman at that moment proffered ‘a back,’ Mr. Pickwick would have accepted his offer with the utmost avidity.\n\nHowever, Mr. Tupman did not volunteer any such accommodation, and the friends walked on, conversing merrily. As they turned into a lane they had to cross, the sound of many voices burst upon their ears; and before they had even had time to form a guess to whom they belonged, they walked into the very centre of the party who were expecting their arrival--a fact which was first notified to the Pickwickians, by the loud ‘Hurrah,’ which burst from old Wardle’s lips, when they appeared in sight.\n\nFirst, there was Wardle himself, looking, if that were possible, more jolly than ever; then there were Bella and her faithful Trundle; and, lastly, there were Emily and some eight or ten young ladies, who had all come down to the wedding, which was to take place next day, and who were in as happy and important a state as young ladies usually are, on such momentous occasions; and they were, one and all, startling the fields and lanes, far and wide, with their frolic and laughter.\n\nThe ceremony of introduction, under such circumstances, was very soon performed, or we should rather say that the introduction was soon over, without any ceremony at all. In two minutes thereafter, Mr. Pickwick was joking with the young ladies who wouldn’t come over the stile while he looked--or who, having pretty feet and unexceptionable ankles, preferred standing on the top rail for five minutes or so, declaring that they were too frightened to move--with as much ease and absence of reserve or constraint, as if he had known them for life. It is worthy of remark, too, that Mr. Snodgrass offered Emily far more assistance than the absolute terrors of the stile (although it was full three feet high, and had only a couple of stepping-stones) would seem to require; while one black-eyed young lady in a very nice little pair of boots with fur round the top, was observed to scream very loudly, when Mr. Winkle offered to help her over.\n\nAll this was very snug and pleasant. And when the difficulties of the stile were at last surmounted, and they once more entered on the open field, old Wardle informed Mr. Pickwick how they had all been down in a body to inspect the furniture and fittings-up of the house, which the young couple were to tenant, after the Christmas holidays; at which communication Bella and Trundle both coloured up, as red as the fat boy after the taproom fire; and the young lady with the black eyes and the fur round the boots, whispered something in Emily’s ear, and then glanced archly at Mr. Snodgrass; to which Emily responded that she was a foolish girl, but turned very red, notwithstanding; and Mr. Snodgrass, who was as modest as all great geniuses usually are, felt the crimson rising to the crown of his head, and devoutly wished, in the inmost recesses of his own heart, that the young lady aforesaid, with her black eyes, and her archness, and her boots with the fur round the top, were all comfortably deposited in the adjacent county.\n\nBut if they were social and happy outside the house, what was the warmth and cordiality of their reception when they reached the farm! The very servants grinned with pleasure at sight of Mr. Pickwick; and Emma bestowed a half-demure, half-impudent, and all-pretty look of recognition, on Mr. Tupman, which was enough to make the statue of Bonaparte in the passage, unfold his arms, and clasp her within them.\n\nThe old lady was seated with customary state in the front parlour, but she was rather cross, and, by consequence, most particularly deaf. She never went out herself, and like a great many other old ladies of the same stamp, she was apt to consider it an act of domestic treason, if anybody else took the liberty of doing what she couldn’t. So, bless her old soul, she sat as upright as she could, in her great chair, and looked as fierce as might be--and that was benevolent after all.\n\n‘Mother,’ said Wardle, ‘Mr. Pickwick. You recollect him?’\n\n‘Never mind,’ replied the old lady, with great dignity. ‘Don’t trouble Mr. Pickwick about an old creetur like me. Nobody cares about me now, and it’s very nat’ral they shouldn’t.’ Here the old lady tossed her head, and smoothed down her lavender-coloured silk dress with trembling hands.\n\n‘Come, come, ma’am,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘I can’t let you cut an old friend in this way. I have come down expressly to have a long talk, and another rubber with you; and we’ll show these boys and girls how to dance a minuet, before they’re eight-and-forty hours older.’\n\nThe old lady was rapidly giving way, but she did not like to do it all at once; so she only said, ‘Ah! I can’t hear him!’\n\n‘Nonsense, mother,’ said Wardle. ‘Come, come, don’t be cross, there’s a good soul. Recollect Bella; come, you must keep her spirits up, poor girl.’\n\nThe good old lady heard this, for her lip quivered as her son said it. But age has its little infirmities of temper, and she was not quite brought round yet. So, she smoothed down the lavender-coloured dress again, and turning to Mr. Pickwick said, ‘Ah, Mr. Pickwick, young people was very different, when I was a girl.’\n\n‘No doubt of that, ma’am,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘and that’s the reason why I would make much of the few that have any traces of the old stock’--and saying this, Mr. Pickwick gently pulled Bella towards him, and bestowing a kiss upon her forehead, bade her sit down on the little stool at her grandmother’s feet. Whether the expression of her countenance, as it was raised towards the old lady’s face, called up a thought of old times, or whether the old lady was touched by Mr. Pickwick’s affectionate good- nature, or whatever was the cause, she was fairly melted; so she threw herself on her granddaughter’s neck, and all the little ill-humour evaporated in a gush of silent tears.\n\nA happy party they were, that night. Sedate and solemn were the score of rubbers in which Mr. Pickwick and the old lady played together; uproarious was the mirth of the round table. Long after the ladies had retired, did the hot elder wine, well qualified with brandy and spice, go round, and round, and round again; and sound was the sleep and pleasant were the dreams that followed. It is a remarkable fact that those of Mr. Snodgrass bore constant reference to Emily Wardle; and that the principal figure in Mr. Winkle’s visions was a young lady with black eyes, and arch smile, and a pair of remarkably nice boots with fur round the tops.\n\nMr. Pickwick was awakened early in the morning, by a hum of voices and a pattering of feet, sufficient to rouse even the fat boy from his heavy slumbers. He sat up in bed and listened. The female servants and female visitors were running constantly to and fro; and there were such multitudinous demands for hot water, such repeated outcries for needles and thread, and so many half-suppressed entreaties of ‘Oh, do come and tie me, there’s a dear!’ that Mr. Pickwick in his innocence began to imagine that something dreadful must have occurred--when he grew more awake, and remembered the wedding. The occasion being an important one, he dressed himself with peculiar care, and descended to the breakfast- room.\n\nThere were all the female servants in a bran new uniform of pink muslin gowns with white bows in their caps, running about the house in a state of excitement and agitation which it would be impossible to describe. The old lady was dressed out in a brocaded gown, which had not seen the light for twenty years, saving and excepting such truant rays as had stolen through the chinks in the box in which it had been laid by, during the whole time. Mr. Trundle was in high feather and spirits, but a little nervous withal. The hearty old landlord was trying to look very cheerful and unconcerned, but failing signally in the attempt. All the girls were in tears and white muslin, except a select two or three, who were being honoured with a private view of the bride and bridesmaids, upstairs. All the Pickwickians were in most blooming array; and there was a terrific roaring on the grass in front of the house, occasioned by all the men, boys, and hobbledehoys attached to the farm, each of whom had got a white bow in his button-hole, and all of whom were cheering with might and main; being incited thereto, and stimulated therein by the precept and example of Mr. Samuel Weller, who had managed to become mighty popular already, and was as much at home as if he had been born on the land.\n\nA wedding is a licensed subject to joke upon, but there really is no great joke in the matter after all;--we speak merely of the ceremony, and beg it to be distinctly understood that we indulge in no hidden sarcasm upon a married life. Mixed up with the pleasure and joy of the occasion, are the many regrets at quitting home, the tears of parting between parent and child, the consciousness of leaving the dearest and kindest friends of the happiest portion of human life, to encounter its cares and troubles with others still untried and little known--natural feelings which we would not render this chapter mournful by describing, and which we should be still more unwilling to be supposed to ridicule.\n\nLet us briefly say, then, that the ceremony was performed by the old clergyman, in the parish church of Dingley Dell, and that Mr. Pickwick’s name is attached to the register, still preserved in the vestry thereof; that the young lady with the black eyes signed her name in a very unsteady and tremulous manner; that Emily’s signature, as the other bridesmaid, is nearly illegible; that it all went off in very admirable style; that the young ladies generally thought it far less shocking than they had expected; and that although the owner of the black eyes and the arch smile informed Mr. Wardle that she was sure she could never submit to anything so dreadful, we have the very best reasons for thinking she was mistaken. To all this, we may add, that Mr. Pickwick was the first who saluted the bride, and that in so doing he threw over her neck a rich gold watch and chain, which no mortal eyes but the jeweller’s had ever beheld before. Then, the old church bell rang as gaily as it could, and they all returned to breakfast.\n\n‘Vere does the mince-pies go, young opium-eater?’ said Mr. Weller to the fat boy, as he assisted in laying out such articles of consumption as had not been duly arranged on the previous night.\n\nThe fat boy pointed to the destination of the pies.\n\n‘Wery good,’ said Sam, ‘stick a bit o’ Christmas in ‘em. T’other dish opposite. There; now we look compact and comfortable, as the father said ven he cut his little boy’s head off, to cure him o’ squintin’.’\n\nAs Mr. Weller made the comparison, he fell back a step or two, to give full effect to it, and surveyed the preparations with the utmost satisfaction.\n\n‘Wardle,’ said Mr. Pickwick, almost as soon as they were all seated, ‘a glass of wine in honour of this happy occasion!’\n\n‘I shall be delighted, my boy,’ said Wardle. ‘Joe--damn that boy, he’s gone to sleep.’\n\nNo, I ain’t, sir,’ replied the fat boy, starting up from a remote corner, where, like the patron saint of fat boys--the immortal Horner-- he had been devouring a Christmas pie, though not with the coolness and deliberation which characterised that young gentleman’s proceedings.\n\n‘Fill Mr. Pickwick’s glass.’\n\n‘Yes, sir.’\n\nThe fat boy filled Mr. Pickwick’s glass, and then retired behind his master’s chair, from whence he watched the play of the knives and forks, and the progress of the choice morsels from the dishes to the mouths of the company, with a kind of dark and gloomy joy that was most impressive.\n\n‘God bless you, old fellow!’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Same to you, my boy,’ replied Wardle; and they pledged each other, heartily.\n\n‘Mrs. Wardle,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘we old folks must have a glass of wine together, in honour of this joyful event.’\n\nThe old lady was in a state of great grandeur just then, for she was sitting at the top of the table in the brocaded gown, with her newly- married granddaughter on one side, and Mr. Pickwick on the other, to do the carving. Mr. Pickwick had not spoken in a very loud tone, but she understood him at once, and drank off a full glass of wine to his long life and happiness; after which the worthy old soul launched forth into a minute and particular account of her own wedding, with a dissertation on the fashion of wearing high-heeled shoes, and some particulars concerning the life and adventures of the beautiful Lady Tollimglower, deceased; at all of which the old lady herself laughed very heartily indeed, and so did the young ladies too, for they were wondering among themselves what on earth grandma was talking about. When they laughed, the old lady laughed ten times more heartily, and said that these always had been considered capital stories, which caused them all to laugh again, and put the old lady into the very best of humours. Then the cake was cut, and passed through the ring; the young ladies saved pieces to put under their pillows to dream of their future husbands on; and a great deal of blushing and merriment was thereby occasioned.\n\n‘Mr. Miller,’ said Mr. Pickwick to his old acquaintance, the hard-headed gentleman, ‘a glass of wine?’\n\n‘With great satisfaction, Mr. Pickwick,’ replied the hard-headed gentleman solemnly.\n\n‘You’ll take me in?’ said the benevolent old clergyman.\n\n‘And me,’ interposed his wife.\n\n‘And me, and me,’ said a couple of poor relations at the bottom of the table, who had eaten and drunk very heartily, and laughed at everything.\n\nMr. Pickwick expressed his heartfelt delight at every additional suggestion; and his eyes beamed with hilarity and cheerfulness.\n\n‘Ladies and gentlemen,’ said Mr. Pickwick, suddenly rising.\n\n‘Hear, hear! Hear, hear! Hear, hear!’ cried Mr. Weller, in the excitement of his feelings.\n\n‘Call in all the servants,’ cried old Wardle, interposing to prevent the public rebuke which Mr. Weller would otherwise most indubitably have received from his master. ‘Give them a glass of wine each to drink the toast in. Now, Pickwick.’\n\nAmidst the silence of the company, the whispering of the women-servants, and the awkward embarrassment of the men, Mr. Pickwick proceeded--\n\n‘Ladies and gentlemen--no, I won’t say ladies and gentlemen, I’ll call you my friends, my dear friends, if the ladies will allow me to take so great a liberty--’\n\nHere Mr. Pickwick was interrupted by immense applause from the ladies, echoed by the gentlemen, during which the owner of the eyes was distinctly heard to state that she could kiss that dear Mr. Pickwick. Whereupon Mr. Winkle gallantly inquired if it couldn’t be done by deputy: to which the young lady with the black eyes replied ‘Go away,’ and accompanied the request with a look which said as plainly as a look could do, ‘if you can.’\n\n‘My dear friends,’ resumed Mr. Pickwick, ‘I am going to propose the health of the bride and bridegroom--God bless ‘em (cheers and tears). My young friend, Trundle, I believe to be a very excellent and manly fellow; and his wife I know to be a very amiable and lovely girl, well qualified to transfer to another sphere of action the happiness which for twenty years she has diffused around her, in her father’s house. (Here, the fat boy burst forth into stentorian blubberings, and was led forth by the coat collar, by Mr. Weller.) I wish,’ added Mr. Pickwick-- ‘I wish I was young enough to be her sister’s husband (cheers), but, failing that, I am happy to be old enough to be her father; for, being so, I shall not be suspected of any latent designs when I say, that I admire, esteem, and love them both (cheers and sobs). The bride’s father, our good friend there, is a noble person, and I am proud to know him (great uproar). He is a kind, excellent, independent-spirited, fine- hearted, hospitable, liberal man (enthusiastic shouts from the poor relations, at all the adjectives; and especially at the two last). That his daughter may enjoy all the happiness, even he can desire; and that he may derive from the contemplation of her felicity all the gratification of heart and peace of mind which he so well deserves, is, I am persuaded, our united wish. So, let us drink their healths, and wish them prolonged life, and every blessing!’\n\nMr. Pickwick concluded amidst a whirlwind of applause; and once more were the lungs of the supernumeraries, under Mr. Weller’s command, brought into active and efficient operation. Mr. Wardle proposed Mr. Pickwick; Mr. Pickwick proposed the old lady. Mr. Snodgrass proposed Mr. Wardle; Mr. Wardle proposed Mr. Snodgrass. One of the poor relations proposed Mr. Tupman, and the other poor relation proposed Mr. Winkle; all was happiness and festivity, until the mysterious disappearance of both the poor relations beneath the table, warned the party that it was time to adjourn.\n\nAt dinner they met again, after a five-and-twenty mile walk, undertaken by the males at Wardle’s recommendation, to get rid of the effects of the wine at breakfast. The poor relations had kept in bed all day, with the view of attaining the same happy consummation, but, as they had been unsuccessful, they stopped there. Mr. Weller kept the domestics in a state of perpetual hilarity; and the fat boy divided his time into small alternate allotments of eating and sleeping.\n\nThe dinner was as hearty an affair as the breakfast, and was quite as noisy, without the tears. Then came the dessert and some more toasts. Then came the tea and coffee; and then, the ball.\n\nThe best sitting-room at Manor Farm was a good, long, dark-panelled room with a high chimney-piece, and a capacious chimney, up which you could have driven one of the new patent cabs, wheels and all. At the upper end of the room, seated in a shady bower of holly and evergreens were the two best fiddlers, and the only harp, in all Muggleton. In all sorts of recesses, and on all kinds of brackets, stood massive old silver candlesticks with four branches each. The carpet was up, the candles burned bright, the fire blazed and crackled on the hearth, and merry voices and light-hearted laughter rang through the room. If any of the old English yeomen had turned into fairies when they died, it was just the place in which they would have held their revels.\n\nIf anything could have added to the interest of this agreeable scene, it would have been the remarkable fact of Mr. Pickwick’s appearing without his gaiters, for the first time within the memory of his oldest friends.\n\n‘You mean to dance?’ said Wardle.\n\n‘Of course I do,’ replied Mr. Pickwick. ‘Don’t you see I am dressed for the purpose?’ Mr. Pickwick called attention to his speckled silk stockings, and smartly tied pumps.\n\n‘You in silk stockings!’ exclaimed Mr. Tupman jocosely.\n\n‘And why not, sir--why not?’ said Mr. Pickwick, turning warmly upon him.\n\n‘Oh, of course there is no reason why you shouldn’t wear them,’ responded Mr. Tupman.\n\n‘I imagine not, sir--I imagine not,’ said Mr. Pickwick, in a very peremptory tone.\n\nMr. Tupman had contemplated a laugh, but he found it was a serious matter; so he looked grave, and said they were a pretty pattern.\n\n‘I hope they are,’ said Mr. Pickwick, fixing his eyes upon his friend. ‘You see nothing extraordinary in the stockings, as stockings, I trust, Sir?’\n\n‘Certainly not. Oh, certainly not,’ replied Mr. Tupman. He walked away; and Mr. Pickwick’s countenance resumed its customary benign expression.\n\n‘We are all ready, I believe,’ said Mr. Pickwick, who was stationed with the old lady at the top of the dance, and had already made four false starts, in his excessive anxiety to commence.\n\n‘Then begin at once,’ said Wardle. ‘Now!’\n\nUp struck the two fiddles and the one harp, and off went Mr. Pickwick into hands across, when there was a general clapping of hands, and a cry of ‘Stop, stop!’\n\n‘What’s the matter?’ said Mr. Pickwick, who was only brought to, by the fiddles and harp desisting, and could have been stopped by no other earthly power, if the house had been on fire.\n\n‘Where’s Arabella Allen?’ cried a dozen voices.\n\n‘And Winkle?’ added Mr. Tupman.\n\n‘Here we are!’ exclaimed that gentleman, emerging with his pretty companion from the corner; as he did so, it would have been hard to tell which was the redder in the face, he or the young lady with the black eyes.\n\n‘What an extraordinary thing it is, Winkle,’ said Mr. Pickwick, rather pettishly, ‘that you couldn’t have taken your place before.’\n\n‘Not at all extraordinary,’ said Mr. Winkle.\n\n‘Well,’ said Mr. Pickwick, with a very expressive smile, as his eyes rested on Arabella, ‘well, I don’t know that it was extraordinary, either, after all.’\n\nHowever, there was no time to think more about the matter, for the fiddles and harp began in real earnest. Away went Mr. Pickwick--hands across--down the middle to the very end of the room, and half-way up the chimney, back again to the door--poussette everywhere--loud stamp on the ground--ready for the next couple--off again--all the figure over once more--another stamp to beat out the time--next couple, and the next, and the next again--never was such going; at last, after they had reached the bottom of the dance, and full fourteen couple after the old lady had retired in an exhausted state, and the clergyman’s wife had been substituted in her stead, did that gentleman, when there was no demand whatever on his exertions, keep perpetually dancing in his place, to keep time to the music, smiling on his partner all the while with a blandness of demeanour which baffles all description.\n\nLong before Mr. Pickwick was weary of dancing, the newly-married couple had retired from the scene. There was a glorious supper downstairs, notwithstanding, and a good long sitting after it; and when Mr. Pickwick awoke, late the next morning, he had a confused recollection of having, severally and confidentially, invited somewhere about five-and-forty people to dine with him at the George and Vulture, the very first time they came to London; which Mr. Pickwick rightly considered a pretty certain indication of his having taken something besides exercise, on the previous night.\n\n‘And so your family has games in the kitchen to-night, my dear, has they?’ inquired Sam of Emma.\n\n‘Yes, Mr. Weller,’ replied Emma; ‘we always have on Christmas Eve. Master wouldn’t neglect to keep it up on any account.’\n\n‘Your master’s a wery pretty notion of keeping anythin’ up, my dear,’ said Mr. Weller; ‘I never see such a sensible sort of man as he is, or such a reg’lar gen’l’m’n.’\n\nOh, that he is!’ said the fat boy, joining in the conversation; ‘don’t he breed nice pork!’ The fat youth gave a semi-cannibalic leer at Mr. Weller, as he thought of the roast legs and gravy.\n\n‘Oh, you’ve woke up, at last, have you?’ said Sam.\n\nThe fat boy nodded.\n\n‘I’ll tell you what it is, young boa-constructer,’ said Mr. Weller impressively; ‘if you don’t sleep a little less, and exercise a little more, wen you comes to be a man you’ll lay yourself open to the same sort of personal inconwenience as was inflicted on the old gen’l’m’n as wore the pigtail.’\n\n‘What did they do to him?’ inquired the fat boy, in a faltering voice.\n\n‘I’m a-going to tell you,’ replied Mr. Weller; ‘he was one o’ the largest patterns as was ever turned out--reg’lar fat man, as hadn’t caught a glimpse of his own shoes for five-and-forty year.’\n\n‘Lor!’ exclaimed Emma.\n\n‘No, that he hadn’t, my dear,’ said Mr. Weller; ‘and if you’d put an exact model of his own legs on the dinin’-table afore him, he wouldn’t ha’ known ‘em. Well, he always walks to his office with a wery handsome gold watch-chain hanging out, about a foot and a quarter, and a gold watch in his fob pocket as was worth--I’m afraid to say how much, but as much as a watch can be--a large, heavy, round manufacter, as stout for a watch, as he was for a man, and with a big face in proportion. “You’d better not carry that ‘ere watch,” says the old gen’l’m’n’s friends, “you’ll be robbed on it,” says they. “Shall I?” says he. “Yes, you will,” says they. “Well,” says he, “I should like to see the thief as could get this here watch out, for I’m blessed if I ever can, it’s such a tight fit,” says he, “and wenever I vants to know what’s o’clock, I’m obliged to stare into the bakers’ shops,” he says. Well, then he laughs as hearty as if he was a-goin’ to pieces, and out he walks agin with his powdered head and pigtail, and rolls down the Strand with the chain hangin’ out furder than ever, and the great round watch almost bustin’ through his gray kersey smalls. There warn’t a pickpocket in all London as didn’t take a pull at that chain, but the chain ‘ud never break, and the watch ‘ud never come out, so they soon got tired of dragging such a heavy old gen’l’m’n along the pavement, and he’d go home and laugh till the pigtail wibrated like the penderlum of a Dutch clock. At last, one day the old gen’l’m’n was a-rollin’ along, and he sees a pickpocket as he know’d by sight, a-coming up, arm in arm with a little boy with a wery large head. “Here’s a game,” says the old gen’l’m’n to himself, “they’re a-goin’ to have another try, but it won’t do!” So he begins a- chucklin’ wery hearty, wen, all of a sudden, the little boy leaves hold of the pickpocket’s arm, and rushes head foremost straight into the old gen’l’m’n’s stomach, and for a moment doubles him right up with the pain. “Murder!” says the old gen’l’m’n. “All right, Sir,” says the pickpocket, a-wisperin’ in his ear. And wen he come straight agin, the watch and chain was gone, and what’s worse than that, the old gen’l’m’n’s digestion was all wrong ever afterwards, to the wery last day of his life; so just you look about you, young feller, and take care you don’t get too fat.’\n\nAs Mr. Weller concluded this moral tale, with which the fat boy appeared much affected, they all three repaired to the large kitchen, in which the family were by this time assembled, according to annual custom on Christmas Eve, observed by old Wardle’s forefathers from time immemorial.\n\nFrom the centre of the ceiling of this kitchen, old Wardle had just suspended, with his own hands, a huge branch of mistletoe, and this same branch of mistletoe instantaneously gave rise to a scene of general and most delightful struggling and confusion; in the midst of which, Mr. Pickwick, with a gallantry that would have done honour to a descendant of Lady Tollimglower herself, took the old lady by the hand, led her beneath the mystic branch, and saluted her in all courtesy and decorum. The old lady submitted to this piece of practical politeness with all the dignity which befitted so important and serious a solemnity, but the younger ladies, not being so thoroughly imbued with a superstitious veneration for the custom, or imagining that the value of a salute is very much enhanced if it cost a little trouble to obtain it, screamed and struggled, and ran into corners, and threatened and remonstrated, and did everything but leave the room, until some of the less adventurous gentlemen were on the point of desisting, when they all at once found it useless to resist any longer, and submitted to be kissed with a good grace. Mr. Winkle kissed the young lady with the black eyes, and Mr. Snodgrass kissed Emily; and Mr. Weller, not being particular about the form of being under the mistletoe, kissed Emma and the other female servants, just as he caught them. As to the poor relations, they kissed everybody, not even excepting the plainer portions of the young lady visitors, who, in their excessive confusion, ran right under the mistletoe, as soon as it was hung up, without knowing it! Wardle stood with his back to the fire, surveying the whole scene, with the utmost satisfaction; and the fat boy took the opportunity of appropriating to his own use, and summarily devouring, a particularly fine mince-pie, that had been carefully put by, for somebody else.\n\nNow, the screaming had subsided, and faces were in a glow, and curls in a tangle, and Mr. Pickwick, after kissing the old lady as before mentioned, was standing under the mistletoe, looking with a very pleased countenance on all that was passing around him, when the young lady with the black eyes, after a little whispering with the other young ladies, made a sudden dart forward, and, putting her arm round Mr. Pickwick’s neck, saluted him affectionately on the left cheek; and before Mr. Pickwick distinctly knew what was the matter, he was surrounded by the whole body, and kissed by every one of them.\n\nIt was a pleasant thing to see Mr. Pickwick in the centre of the group, now pulled this way, and then that, and first kissed on the chin, and then on the nose, and then on the spectacles, and to hear the peals of laughter which were raised on every side; but it was a still more pleasant thing to see Mr. Pickwick, blinded shortly afterwards with a silk handkerchief, falling up against the wall, and scrambling into corners, and going through all the mysteries of blind-man’s buff, with the utmost relish for the game, until at last he caught one of the poor relations, and then had to evade the blind-man himself, which he did with a nimbleness and agility that elicited the admiration and applause of all beholders. The poor relations caught the people who they thought would like it, and, when the game flagged, got caught themselves. When they all tired of blind-man’s buff, there was a great game at snap- dragon, and when fingers enough were burned with that, and all the raisins were gone, they sat down by the huge fire of blazing logs to a substantial supper, and a mighty bowl of wassail, something smaller than an ordinary wash-house copper, in which the hot apples were hissing and bubbling with a rich look, and a jolly sound, that were perfectly irresistible.\n\n‘This,’ said Mr. Pickwick, looking round him, ‘this is, indeed, comfort.’ ‘Our invariable custom,’ replied Mr. Wardle. ‘Everybody sits down with us on Christmas Eve, as you see them now--servants and all; and here we wait, until the clock strikes twelve, to usher Christmas in, and beguile the time with forfeits and old stories. Trundle, my boy, rake up the fire.’\n\nUp flew the bright sparks in myriads as the logs were stirred. The deep red blaze sent forth a rich glow, that penetrated into the farthest corner of the room, and cast its cheerful tint on every face.\n\n‘Come,’ said Wardle, ‘a song--a Christmas song! I’ll give you one, in default of a better.’\n\n‘Bravo!’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Fill up,’ cried Wardle. ‘It will be two hours, good, before you see the bottom of the bowl through the deep rich colour of the wassail; fill up all round, and now for the song.’\n\nThus saying, the merry old gentleman, in a good, round, sturdy voice, commenced without more ado--\n\n# A CHRISTMAS CAROL\n\n‘I care not for Spring; on his fickle wing Let the blossoms and buds be borne; He woos them amain with his treacherous rain, And he scatters them ere the morn. An inconstant elf, he knows not himself, Nor his own changing mind an hour, He’ll smile in your face, and, with wry grimace, He’ll wither your youngest flower.\n\n‘Let the Summer sun to his bright home run, He shall never be sought by me; When he’s dimmed by a cloud I can laugh aloud And care not how sulky he be! For his darling child is the madness wild That sports in fierce fever’s train; And when love is too strong, it don’t last long, As many have found to their pain.\n\n‘A mild harvest night, by the tranquil light Of the modest and gentle moon, Has a far sweeter sheen for me, I ween, Than the broad and unblushing noon. But every leaf awakens my grief, As it lieth beneath the tree; So let Autumn air be never so fair, It by no means agrees with me.\n\n‘But my song I troll out, for Christmas Stout, The hearty, the true, and the bold; A bumper I drain, and with might and main Give three cheers for this Christmas old! We’ll usher him in with a merry din That shall gladden his joyous heart, And we’ll keep him up, while there’s bite or sup, And in fellowship good, we’ll part. ‘In his fine honest pride, he scorns to hide One jot of his hard-weather scars; They’re no disgrace, for there’s much the same trace On the cheeks of our bravest tars. Then again I sing till the roof doth ring And it echoes from wall to wall--To the stout old wight, fair welcome to-night, As the King of the Seasons all!’\n\nThis song was tumultuously applauded--for friends and dependents make a capital audience--and the poor relations, especially, were in perfect ecstasies of rapture. Again was the fire replenished, and again went the wassail round.\n\n‘How it snows!’ said one of the men, in a low tone.\n\n‘Snows, does it?’ said Wardle.\n\n‘Rough, cold night, Sir,’ replied the man; ‘and there’s a wind got up, that drifts it across the fields, in a thick white cloud.’\n\n‘What does Jem say?’ inquired the old lady. ‘There ain’t anything the matter, is there?’\n\n‘No, no, mother,’ replied Wardle; ‘he says there’s a snowdrift, and a wind that’s piercing cold. I should know that, by the way it rumbles in the chimney.’\n\n‘Ah!’ said the old lady, ‘there was just such a wind, and just such a fall of snow, a good many years back, I recollect--just five years before your poor father died. It was a Christmas Eve, too; and I remember that on that very night he told us the story about the goblins that carried away old Gabriel Grub.’\n\n‘The story about what?’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Oh, nothing, nothing,’ replied Wardle. ‘About an old sexton, that the good people down here suppose to have been carried away by goblins.’\n\n‘Suppose!’ ejaculated the old lady. ‘Is there anybody hardy enough to disbelieve it? Suppose! Haven’t you heard ever since you were a child, that he was carried away by the goblins, and don’t you know he was?’\n\n‘Very well, mother, he was, if you like,’ said Wardle laughing. ‘He was carried away by goblins, Pickwick; and there’s an end of the matter.’\n\n‘No, no,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘not an end of it, I assure you; for I must hear how, and why, and all about it.’\n\nWardle smiled, as every head was bent forward to hear, and filling out the wassail with no stinted hand, nodded a health to Mr. Pickwick, and began as follows--\n\nBut bless our editorial heart, what a long chapter we have been betrayed into! We had quite forgotten all such petty restrictions as chapters, we solemnly declare. So here goes, to give the goblin a fair start in a new one. A clear stage and no favour for the goblins, ladies and gentlemen, if you please.\n\n# CHAPTER XXIX. THE STORY OF THE GOBLINS WHO STOLE A SEXTON\n\n‘In an old abbey town, down in this part of the country, a long, long while ago--so long, that the story must be a true one, because our great-grandfathers implicitly believed it--there officiated as sexton and grave-digger in the churchyard, one Gabriel Grub. It by no means follows that because a man is a sexton, and constantly surrounded by the emblems of mortality, therefore he should be a morose and melancholy man; your undertakers are the merriest fellows in the world; and I once had the honour of being on intimate terms with a mute, who in private life, and off duty, was as comical and jocose a little fellow as ever chirped out a devil-may-care song, without a hitch in his memory, or drained off a good stiff glass without stopping for breath. But notwithstanding these precedents to the contrary, Gabriel Grub was an ill-conditioned, cross-grained, surly fellow--a morose and lonely man, who consorted with nobody but himself, and an old wicker bottle which fitted into his large deep waistcoat pocket--and who eyed each merry face, as it passed him by, with such a deep scowl of malice and ill- humour, as it was difficult to meet without feeling something the worse for.\n\n‘A little before twilight, one Christmas Eve, Gabriel shouldered his spade, lighted his lantern, and betook himself towards the old churchyard; for he had got a grave to finish by next morning, and, feeling very low, he thought it might raise his spirits, perhaps, if he went on with his work at once. As he went his way, up the ancient street, he saw the cheerful light of the blazing fires gleam through the old casements, and heard the loud laugh and the cheerful shouts of those who were assembled around them; he marked the bustling preparations for next day’s cheer, and smelled the numerous savoury odours consequent thereupon, as they steamed up from the kitchen windows in clouds. All this was gall and wormwood to the heart of Gabriel Grub; and when groups of children bounded out of the houses, tripped across the road, and were met, before they could knock at the opposite door, by half a dozen curly-headed little rascals who crowded round them as they flocked upstairs to spend the evening in their Christmas games, Gabriel smiled grimly, and clutched the handle of his spade with a firmer grasp, as he thought of measles, scarlet fever, thrush, whooping-cough, and a good many other sources of consolation besides.\n\n‘In this happy frame of mind, Gabriel strode along, returning a short, sullen growl to the good-humoured greetings of such of his neighbours as now and then passed him, until he turned into the dark lane which led to the churchyard. Now, Gabriel had been looking forward to reaching the dark lane, because it was, generally speaking, a nice, gloomy, mournful place, into which the townspeople did not much care to go, except in broad daylight, and when the sun was shining; consequently, he was not a little indignant to hear a young urchin roaring out some jolly song about a merry Christmas, in this very sanctuary which had been called Coffin Lane ever since the days of the old abbey, and the time of the shaven-headed monks. As Gabriel walked on, and the voice drew nearer, he found it proceeded from a small boy, who was hurrying along, to join one of the little parties in the old street, and who, partly to keep himself company, and partly to prepare himself for the occasion, was shouting out the song at the highest pitch of his lungs. So Gabriel waited until the boy came up, and then dodged him into a corner, and rapped him over the head with his lantern five or six times, just to teach him to modulate his voice. And as the boy hurried away with his hand to his head, singing quite a different sort of tune, Gabriel Grub chuckled very heartily to himself, and entered the churchyard, locking the gate behind him.\n\n‘He took off his coat, set down his lantern, and getting into the unfinished grave, worked at it for an hour or so with right good-will. But the earth was hardened with the frost, and it was no very easy matter to break it up, and shovel it out; and although there was a moon, it was a very young one, and shed little light upon the grave, which was in the shadow of the church. At any other time, these obstacles would have made Gabriel Grub very moody and miserable, but he was so well pleased with having stopped the small boy’s singing, that he took little heed of the scanty progress he had made, and looked down into the grave, when he had finished work for the night, with grim satisfaction, murmuring as he gathered up his things--\n\nBrave lodgings for one, brave lodgings for one, A few feet of cold earth, when life is done; A stone at the head, a stone at the feet, A rich, juicy meal for the worms to eat; Rank grass overhead, and damp clay around, Brave lodgings for one, these, in holy ground!\n\n‘“Ho! ho!” laughed Gabriel Grub, as he sat himself down on a flat tombstone which was a favourite resting-place of his, and drew forth his wicker bottle. “A coffin at Christmas! A Christmas box! Ho! ho! ho!”\n\n‘“Ho! ho! ho!” repeated a voice which sounded close behind him.\n\n‘Gabriel paused, in some alarm, in the act of raising the wicker bottle to his lips, and looked round. The bottom of the oldest grave about him was not more still and quiet than the churchyard in the pale moonlight. The cold hoar frost glistened on the tombstones, and sparkled like rows of gems, among the stone carvings of the old church. The snow lay hard and crisp upon the ground; and spread over the thickly-strewn mounds of earth, so white and smooth a cover that it seemed as if corpses lay there, hidden only by their winding sheets. Not the faintest rustle broke the profound tranquillity of the solemn scene. Sound itself appeared to be frozen up, all was so cold and still.\n\n‘“It was the echoes,” said Gabriel Grub, raising the bottle to his lips again.\n\n‘“It was not,” said a deep voice.\n\n‘Gabriel started up, and stood rooted to the spot with astonishment and terror; for his eyes rested on a form that made his blood run cold.\n\n‘Seated on an upright tombstone, close to him, was a strange, unearthly figure, whom Gabriel felt at once, was no being of this world. His long, fantastic legs which might have reached the ground, were cocked up, and crossed after a quaint, fantastic fashion; his sinewy arms were bare; and his hands rested on his knees. On his short, round body, he wore a close covering, ornamented with small slashes; a short cloak dangled at his back; the collar was cut into curious peaks, which served the goblin in lieu of ruff or neckerchief; and his shoes curled up at his toes into long points. On his head, he wore a broad-brimmed sugar-loaf hat, garnished with a single feather. The hat was covered with the white frost; and the goblin looked as if he had sat on the same tombstone very comfortably, for two or three hundred years. He was sitting perfectly still; his tongue was put out, as if in derision; and he was grinning at Gabriel Grub with such a grin as only a goblin could call up.\n\n‘“It was not the echoes,” said the goblin.\n\n‘Gabriel Grub was paralysed, and could make no reply.\n\n‘“What do you do here on Christmas Eve?” said the goblin sternly.\n\n‘“I came to dig a grave, Sir,” stammered Gabriel Grub.\n\n‘“What man wanders among graves and churchyards on such a night as this?” cried the goblin.\n\n‘“Gabriel Grub! Gabriel Grub!” screamed a wild chorus of voices that seemed to fill the churchyard. Gabriel looked fearfully round--nothing was to be seen.\n\n‘“What have you got in that bottle?” said the goblin.\n\n‘“Hollands, sir,” replied the sexton, trembling more than ever; for he had bought it of the smugglers, and he thought that perhaps his questioner might be in the excise department of the goblins.\n\n‘“Who drinks Hollands alone, and in a churchyard, on such a night as this?” said the goblin.\n\n‘“Gabriel Grub! Gabriel Grub!” exclaimed the wild voices again.\n\n‘The goblin leered maliciously at the terrified sexton, and then raising his voice, exclaimed--\n\n‘“And who, then, is our fair and lawful prize?”\n\n‘To this inquiry the invisible chorus replied, in a strain that sounded like the voices of many choristers singing to the mighty swell of the old church organ--a strain that seemed borne to the sexton’s ears upon a wild wind, and to die away as it passed onward; but the burden of the reply was still the same, “Gabriel Grub! Gabriel Grub!”\n\n‘The goblin grinned a broader grin than before, as he said, “Well, Gabriel, what do you say to this?”\n\n‘The sexton gasped for breath.\n\n‘“What do you think of this, Gabriel?” said the goblin, kicking up his feet in the air on either side of the tombstone, and looking at the turned-up points with as much complacency as if he had been contemplating the most fashionable pair of Wellingtons in all Bond Street.\n\n‘“It’s--it’s--very curious, Sir,” replied the sexton, half dead with fright; “very curious, and very pretty, but I think I’ll go back and finish my work, Sir, if you please.”\n\n‘“Work!” said the goblin, “what work?”\n\n‘“The grave, Sir; making the grave,” stammered the sexton.\n\n‘“Oh, the grave, eh?” said the goblin; “who makes graves at a time when all other men are merry, and takes a pleasure in it?”\n\n‘Again the mysterious voices replied, “Gabriel Grub! Gabriel Grub!”\n\n‘“I am afraid my friends want you, Gabriel,” said the goblin, thrusting his tongue farther into his cheek than ever--and a most astonishing tongue it was--“I’m afraid my friends want you, Gabriel,” said the goblin.\n\n‘“Under favour, Sir,” replied the horror-stricken sexton, “I don’t think they can, Sir; they don’t know me, Sir; I don’t think the gentlemen have ever seen me, Sir.”\n\n‘“Oh, yes, they have,” replied the goblin; “we know the man with the sulky face and grim scowl, that came down the street to-night, throwing his evil looks at the children, and grasping his burying-spade the tighter. We know the man who struck the boy in the envious malice of his heart, because the boy could be merry, and he could not. We know him, we know him.”\n\n‘Here, the goblin gave a loud, shrill laugh, which the echoes returned twentyfold; and throwing his legs up in the air, stood upon his head, or rather upon the very point of his sugar-loaf hat, on the narrow edge of the tombstone, whence he threw a Somerset with extraordinary agility, right to the sexton’s feet, at which he planted himself in the attitude in which tailors generally sit upon the shop-board.\n\n‘“I--I--am afraid I must leave you, Sir,” said the sexton, making an effort to move.\n\n‘“Leave us!” said the goblin, “Gabriel Grub going to leave us. Ho! ho! ho!”\n\n‘As the goblin laughed, the sexton observed, for one instant, a brilliant illumination within the windows of the church, as if the whole building were lighted up; it disappeared, the organ pealed forth a lively air, and whole troops of goblins, the very counterpart of the first one, poured into the churchyard, and began playing at leap-frog with the tombstones, never stopping for an instant to take breath, but “overing” the highest among them, one after the other, with the most marvellous dexterity. The first goblin was a most astonishing leaper, and none of the others could come near him; even in the extremity of his terror the sexton could not help observing, that while his friends were content to leap over the common-sized gravestones, the first one took the family vaults, iron railings and all, with as much ease as if they had been so many street-posts.\n\n‘At last the game reached to a most exciting pitch; the organ played quicker and quicker, and the goblins leaped faster and faster, coiling themselves up, rolling head over heels upon the ground, and bounding over the tombstones like footballs. The sexton’s brain whirled round with the rapidity of the motion he beheld, and his legs reeled beneath him, as the spirits flew before his eyes; when the goblin king, suddenly darting towards him, laid his hand upon his collar, and sank with him through the earth.\n\n‘When Gabriel Grub had had time to fetch his breath, which the rapidity of his descent had for the moment taken away, he found himself in what appeared to be a large cavern, surrounded on all sides by crowds of goblins, ugly and grim; in the centre of the room, on an elevated seat, was stationed his friend of the churchyard; and close behind him stood Gabriel Grub himself, without power of motion.\n\n‘“Cold to-night,” said the king of the goblins, “very cold. A glass of something warm here!”\n\n‘At this command, half a dozen officious goblins, with a perpetual smile upon their faces, whom Gabriel Grub imagined to be courtiers, on that account, hastily disappeared, and presently returned with a goblet of liquid fire, which they presented to the king.\n\n‘“Ah!” cried the goblin, whose cheeks and throat were transparent, as he tossed down the flame, “this warms one, indeed! Bring a bumper of the same, for Mr. Grub.”\n\n‘It was in vain for the unfortunate sexton to protest that he was not in the habit of taking anything warm at night; one of the goblins held him while another poured the blazing liquid down his throat; the whole assembly screeched with laughter, as he coughed and choked, and wiped away the tears which gushed plentifully from his eyes, after swallowing the burning draught.\n\n‘“And now,” said the king, fantastically poking the taper corner of his sugar-loaf hat into the sexton’s eye, and thereby occasioning him the most exquisite pain; “and now, show the man of misery and gloom, a few of the pictures from our own great storehouse!”\n\n‘As the goblin said this, a thick cloud which obscured the remoter end of the cavern rolled gradually away, and disclosed, apparently at a great distance, a small and scantily furnished, but neat and clean apartment. A crowd of little children were gathered round a bright fire, clinging to their mother’s gown, and gambolling around her chair. The mother occasionally rose, and drew aside the window-curtain, as if to look for some expected object; a frugal meal was ready spread upon the table; and an elbow chair was placed near the fire. A knock was heard at the door; the mother opened it, and the children crowded round her, and clapped their hands for joy, as their father entered. He was wet and weary, and shook the snow from his garments, as the children crowded round him, and seizing his cloak, hat, stick, and gloves, with busy zeal, ran with them from the room. Then, as he sat down to his meal before the fire, the children climbed about his knee, and the mother sat by his side, and all seemed happiness and comfort.\n\n‘But a change came upon the view, almost imperceptibly. The scene was altered to a small bedroom, where the fairest and youngest child lay dying; the roses had fled from his cheek, and the light from his eye; and even as the sexton looked upon him with an interest he had never felt or known before, he died. His young brothers and sisters crowded round his little bed, and seized his tiny hand, so cold and heavy; but they shrank back from its touch, and looked with awe on his infant face; for calm and tranquil as it was, and sleeping in rest and peace as the beautiful child seemed to be, they saw that he was dead, and they knew that he was an angel looking down upon, and blessing them, from a bright and happy Heaven.\n\n‘Again the light cloud passed across the picture, and again the subject changed. The father and mother were old and helpless now, and the number of those about them was diminished more than half; but content and cheerfulness sat on every face, and beamed in every eye, as they crowded round the fireside, and told and listened to old stories of earlier and bygone days. Slowly and peacefully, the father sank into the grave, and, soon after, the sharer of all his cares and troubles followed him to a place of rest. The few who yet survived them, kneeled by their tomb, and watered the green turf which covered it with their tears; then rose, and turned away, sadly and mournfully, but not with bitter cries, or despairing lamentations, for they knew that they should one day meet again; and once more they mixed with the busy world, and their content and cheerfulness were restored. The cloud settled upon the picture, and concealed it from the sexton’s view.\n\n‘“What do you think of that?” said the goblin, turning his large face towards Gabriel Grub.\n\n‘Gabriel murmured out something about its being very pretty, and looked somewhat ashamed, as the goblin bent his fiery eyes upon him.\n\n‘“You a miserable man!” said the goblin, in a tone of excessive contempt. “You!” He appeared disposed to add more, but indignation choked his utterance, so he lifted up one of his very pliable legs, and, flourishing it above his head a little, to insure his aim, administered a good sound kick to Gabriel Grub; immediately after which, all the goblins in waiting crowded round the wretched sexton, and kicked him without mercy, according to the established and invariable custom of courtiers upon earth, who kick whom royalty kicks, and hug whom royalty hugs.\n\n‘“Show him some more!” said the king of the goblins.\n\n‘At these words, the cloud was dispelled, and a rich and beautiful landscape was disclosed to view--there is just such another, to this day, within half a mile of the old abbey town. The sun shone from out the clear blue sky, the water sparkled beneath his rays, and the trees looked greener, and the flowers more gay, beneath its cheering influence. The water rippled on with a pleasant sound, the trees rustled in the light wind that murmured among their leaves, the birds sang upon the boughs, and the lark carolled on high her welcome to the morning. Yes, it was morning; the bright, balmy morning of summer; the minutest leaf, the smallest blade of grass, was instinct with life. The ant crept forth to her daily toil, the butterfly fluttered and basked in the warm rays of the sun; myriads of insects spread their transparent wings, and revelled in their brief but happy existence. Man walked forth, elated with the scene; and all was brightness and splendour.\n\n’”You a miserable man!” said the king of the goblins, in a more contemptuous tone than before. And again the king of the goblins gave his leg a flourish; again it descended on the shoulders of the sexton; and again the attendant goblins imitated the example of their chief.\n\n‘Many a time the cloud went and came, and many a lesson it taught to Gabriel Grub, who, although his shoulders smarted with pain from the frequent applications of the goblins’ feet thereunto, looked on with an interest that nothing could diminish. He saw that men who worked hard, and earned their scanty bread with lives of labour, were cheerful and happy; and that to the most ignorant, the sweet face of Nature was a never-failing source of cheerfulness and joy. He saw those who had been delicately nurtured, and tenderly brought up, cheerful under privations, and superior to suffering, that would have crushed many of a rougher grain, because they bore within their own bosoms the materials of happiness, contentment, and peace. He saw that women, the tenderest and most fragile of all God’s creatures, were the oftenest superior to sorrow, adversity, and distress; and he saw that it was because they bore, in their own hearts, an inexhaustible well-spring of affection and devotion. Above all, he saw that men like himself, who snarled at the mirth and cheerfulness of others, were the foulest weeds on the fair surface of the earth; and setting all the good of the world against the evil, he came to the conclusion that it was a very decent and respectable sort of world after all. No sooner had he formed it, than the cloud which had closed over the last picture, seemed to settle on his senses, and lull him to repose. One by one, the goblins faded from his sight; and, as the last one disappeared, he sank to sleep.\n\n‘The day had broken when Gabriel Grub awoke, and found himself lying at full length on the flat gravestone in the churchyard, with the wicker bottle lying empty by his side, and his coat, spade, and lantern, all well whitened by the last night’s frost, scattered on the ground. The stone on which he had first seen the goblin seated, stood bolt upright before him, and the grave at which he had worked, the night before, was not far off. At first, he began to doubt the reality of his adventures, but the acute pain in his shoulders when he attempted to rise, assured him that the kicking of the goblins was certainly not ideal. He was staggered again, by observing no traces of footsteps in the snow on which the goblins had played at leap-frog with the gravestones, but he speedily accounted for this circumstance when he remembered that, being spirits, they would leave no visible impression behind them. So, Gabriel Grub got on his feet as well as he could, for the pain in his back; and, brushing the frost off his coat, put it on, and turned his face towards the town.\n\n‘But he was an altered man, and he could not bear the thought of returning to a place where his repentance would be scoffed at, and his reformation disbelieved. He hesitated for a few moments; and then turned away to wander where he might, and seek his bread elsewhere.\n\n‘The lantern, the spade, and the wicker bottle were found, that day, in the churchyard. There were a great many speculations about the sexton’s fate, at first, but it was speedily determined that he had been carried away by the goblins; and there were not wanting some very credible witnesses who had distinctly seen him whisked through the air on the back of a chestnut horse blind of one eye, with the hind-quarters of a lion, and the tail of a bear. At length all this was devoutly believed; and the new sexton used to exhibit to the curious, for a trifling emolument, a good-sized piece of the church weathercock which had been accidentally kicked off by the aforesaid horse in his aerial flight, and picked up by himself in the churchyard, a year or two afterwards.\n\n‘Unfortunately, these stories were somewhat disturbed by the unlooked- for reappearance of Gabriel Grub himself, some ten years afterwards, a ragged, contented, rheumatic old man. He told his story to the clergyman, and also to the mayor; and in course of time it began to be received as a matter of history, in which form it has continued down to this very day. The believers in the weathercock tale, having misplaced their confidence once, were not easily prevailed upon to part with it again, so they looked as wise as they could, shrugged their shoulders, touched their foreheads, and murmured something about Gabriel Grub having drunk all the Hollands, and then fallen asleep on the flat tombstone; and they affected to explain what he supposed he had witnessed in the goblin’s cavern, by saying that he had seen the world, and grown wiser. But this opinion, which was by no means a popular one at any time, gradually died off; and be the matter how it may, as Gabriel Grub was afflicted with rheumatism to the end of his days, this story has at least one moral, if it teach no better one--and that is, that if a man turn sulky and drink by himself at Christmas time, he may make up his mind to be not a bit the better for it: let the spirits be never so good, or let them be even as many degrees beyond proof, as those which Gabriel Grub saw in the goblin’s cavern.’\n\nCHAPTER XXX. HOW THE PICKWICKIANS MADE AND CULTIVATED THE ACQUAINTANCE OF A COUPLE OF NICE YOUNG MEN BELONGING TO ONE OF THE LIBERAL PROFESSIONS; HOW THEY DISPORTED THEMSELVES ON THE ICE; AND HOW THEIR VISIT CAME TO A CONCLUSION\nWell, Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick, as that favoured servitor entered his bed-chamber, with his warm water, on the morning of Christmas Day, ‘still frosty?’\n\n‘Water in the wash-hand basin’s a mask o’ ice, Sir,’ responded Sam.\n\n‘Severe weather, Sam,’ observed Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Fine time for them as is well wropped up, as the Polar bear said to himself, ven he was practising his skating,’ replied Mr. Weller.\n\n‘I shall be down in a quarter of an hour, Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick, untying his nightcap.\n\n‘Wery good, sir,’ replied Sam. ‘There’s a couple o’ sawbones downstairs.’\n\n‘A couple of what!’ exclaimed Mr. Pickwick, sitting up in bed.\n\n‘A couple o’ sawbones,’ said Sam.\n\n‘What’s a sawbones?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick, not quite certain whether it was a live animal, or something to eat.\n\n‘What! Don’t you know what a sawbones is, sir?’ inquired Mr. Weller. ‘I thought everybody know’d as a sawbones was a surgeon.’\n\n‘Oh, a surgeon, eh?’ said Mr. Pickwick, with a smile.\n\n‘Just that, sir,’ replied Sam. ‘These here ones as is below, though, ain’t reg’lar thoroughbred sawbones; they’re only in trainin’.’\n\nIn other words they’re medical students, I suppose?’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\nSam Weller nodded assent.\n\n‘I am glad of it,’ said Mr. Pickwick, casting his nightcap energetically on the counterpane. ‘They are fine fellows--very fine fellows; with judgments matured by observation and reflection; and tastes refined by reading and study. I am very glad of it.’\n\n‘They’re a-smokin’ cigars by the kitchen fire,’ said Sam.\n\n‘Ah!’ observed Mr. Pickwick, rubbing his hands, ‘overflowing with kindly feelings and animal spirits. Just what I like to see.’\n\nAnd one on ‘em,’ said Sam, not noticing his master’s interruption, ‘one on ‘em’s got his legs on the table, and is a-drinking brandy neat, vile the t’other one--him in the barnacles--has got a barrel o’ oysters atween his knees, which he’s a-openin’ like steam, and as fast as he eats ‘em, he takes a aim vith the shells at young dropsy, who’s a sittin’ down fast asleep, in the chimbley corner.’\n\n‘Eccentricities of genius, Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick. ‘You may retire.’\n\nSam did retire accordingly. Mr. Pickwick at the expiration of the quarter of an hour, went down to breakfast.\n\n‘Here he is at last!’ said old Mr. Wardle. ‘Pickwick, this is Miss Allen’s brother, Mr. Benjamin Allen. Ben we call him, and so may you, if you like. This gentleman is his very particular friend, Mr.--’\n\n‘Mr. Bob Sawyer,’ interposed Mr. Benjamin Allen; whereupon Mr. Bob Sawyer and Mr. Benjamin Allen laughed in concert.\n\nMr. Pickwick bowed to Bob Sawyer, and Bob Sawyer bowed to Mr. Pickwick. Bob and his very particular friend then applied themselves most assiduously to the eatables before them; and Mr. Pickwick had an opportunity of glancing at them both.\n\nMr. Benjamin Allen was a coarse, stout, thick-set young man, with black hair cut rather short, and a white face cut rather long. He was embellished with spectacles, and wore a white neckerchief. Below his single-breasted black surtout, which was buttoned up to his chin, appeared the usual number of pepper-and-salt coloured legs, terminating in a pair of imperfectly polished boots. Although his coat was short in the sleeves, it disclosed no vestige of a linen wristband; and although there was quite enough of his face to admit of the encroachment of a shirt collar, it was not graced by the smallest approach to that appendage. He presented, altogether, rather a mildewy appearance, and emitted a fragrant odour of full-flavoured Cubas.\n\nMr. Bob Sawyer, who was habited in a coarse, blue coat, which, without being either a greatcoat or a surtout, partook of the nature and qualities of both, had about him that sort of slovenly smartness, and swaggering gait, which is peculiar to young gentlemen who smoke in the streets by day, shout and scream in the same by night, call waiters by their Christian names, and do various other acts and deeds of an equally facetious description. He wore a pair of plaid trousers, and a large, rough, double-breasted waistcoat; out of doors, he carried a thick stick with a big top. He eschewed gloves, and looked, upon the whole, something like a dissipated Robinson Crusoe.\n\nSuch were the two worthies to whom Mr. Pickwick was introduced, as he took his seat at the breakfast-table on Christmas morning.\n\n‘Splendid morning, gentlemen,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\nMr. Bob Sawyer slightly nodded his assent to the proposition, and asked Mr. Benjamin Allen for the mustard.\n\n‘Have you come far this morning, gentlemen?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Blue Lion at Muggleton,’ briefly responded Mr. Allen.\n\n‘You should have joined us last night,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘So we should,’ replied Bob Sawyer, ‘but the brandy was too good to leave in a hurry; wasn’t it, Ben?’\n\n‘Certainly,’ said Mr. Benjamin Allen; ‘and the cigars were not bad, or the pork-chops either; were they, Bob?’\n\n‘Decidedly not,’ said Bob. The particular friends resumed their attack upon the breakfast, more freely than before, as if the recollection of last night’s supper had imparted a new relish to the meal.\n\n‘Peg away, Bob,’ said Mr. Allen, to his companion, encouragingly.\n\n‘So I do,’ replied Bob Sawyer. And so, to do him justice, he did.\n\n‘Nothing like dissecting, to give one an appetite,’ said Mr. Bob Sawyer, looking round the table.\n\nMr. Pickwick slightly shuddered.\n\n‘By the bye, Bob,’ said Mr. Allen, ‘have you finished that leg yet?’\n\n‘Nearly,’ replied Sawyer, helping himself to half a fowl as he spoke. ‘It’s a very muscular one for a child’s.’\n\nIs it?’ inquired Mr. Allen carelessly.\n\n‘Very,’ said Bob Sawyer, with his mouth full.\n\n‘I’ve put my name down for an arm at our place,’ said Mr. Allen. ‘We’re clubbing for a subject, and the list is nearly full, only we can’t get hold of any fellow that wants a head. I wish you’d take it.’\n\n‘No,’ replied ‘Bob Sawyer; ‘can’t afford expensive luxuries.’\n\n‘Nonsense!’ said Allen.\n\n‘Can’t, indeed,’ rejoined Bob Sawyer, ‘I wouldn’t mind a brain, but I couldn’t stand a whole head.’\n\nHush, hush, gentlemen, pray,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘I hear the ladies.’\n\nAs Mr. Pickwick spoke, the ladies, gallantly escorted by Messrs. Snodgrass, Winkle, and Tupman, returned from an early walk.\n\n‘Why, Ben!’ said Arabella, in a tone which expressed more surprise than pleasure at the sight of her brother.\n\n‘Come to take you home to-morrow,’ replied Benjamin.\n\nMr. Winkle turned pale.\n\n‘Don’t you see Bob Sawyer, Arabella?’ inquired Mr. Benjamin Allen, somewhat reproachfully. Arabella gracefully held out her hand, in acknowledgment of Bob Sawyer’s presence. A thrill of hatred struck to Mr. Winkle’s heart, as Bob Sawyer inflicted on the proffered hand a perceptible squeeze.\n\n‘Ben, dear!’ said Arabella, blushing; ‘have--have--you been introduced to Mr. Winkle?’\n\n‘I have not been, but I shall be very happy to be, Arabella,’ replied her brother gravely. Here Mr. Allen bowed grimly to Mr. Winkle, while Mr. Winkle and Mr. Bob Sawyer glanced mutual distrust out of the corners of their eyes.\n\nThe arrival of the two new visitors, and the consequent check upon Mr. Winkle and the young lady with the fur round her boots, would in all probability have proved a very unpleasant interruption to the hilarity of the party, had not the cheerfulness of Mr. Pickwick, and the good humour of the host, been exerted to the very utmost for the common weal. Mr. Winkle gradually insinuated himself into the good graces of Mr. Benjamin Allen, and even joined in a friendly conversation with Mr. Bob Sawyer; who, enlivened with the brandy, and the breakfast, and the talking, gradually ripened into a state of extreme facetiousness, and related with much glee an agreeable anecdote, about the removal of a tumour on some gentleman’s head, which he illustrated by means of an oyster-knife and a half-quartern loaf, to the great edification of the assembled company. Then the whole train went to church, where Mr. Benjamin Allen fell fast asleep; while Mr. Bob Sawyer abstracted his thoughts from worldly matters, by the ingenious process of carving his name on the seat of the pew, in corpulent letters of four inches long.\n\n‘Now,’ said Wardle, after a substantial lunch, with the agreeable items of strong beer and cherry-brandy, had been done ample justice to, ‘what say you to an hour on the ice? We shall have plenty of time.’\n\n‘Capital!’ said Mr. Benjamin Allen.\n\n‘Prime!’ ejaculated Mr. Bob Sawyer.\n\n‘You skate, of course, Winkle?’ said Wardle.\n\n‘Ye-yes; oh, yes,’ replied Mr. Winkle. ‘I--I--am rather out of practice.’\n\n‘Oh, do skate, Mr. Winkle,’ said Arabella. ‘I like to see it so much.’\n\n‘Oh, it is so graceful,’ said another young lady.\n\nA third young lady said it was elegant, and a fourth expressed her opinion that it was ‘swan-like.’\n\n‘I should be very happy, I’m sure,’ said Mr. Winkle, reddening; ‘but I have no skates.’\n\nThis objection was at once overruled. Trundle had a couple of pair, and the fat boy announced that there were half a dozen more downstairs; whereat Mr. Winkle expressed exquisite delight, and looked exquisitely uncomfortable.\n\nOld Wardle led the way to a pretty large sheet of ice; and the fat boy and Mr. Weller, having shovelled and swept away the snow which had fallen on it during the night, Mr. Bob Sawyer adjusted his skates with a dexterity which to Mr. Winkle was perfectly marvellous, and described circles with his left leg, and cut figures of eight, and inscribed upon the ice, without once stopping for breath, a great many other pleasant and astonishing devices, to the excessive satisfaction of Mr. Pickwick, Mr. Tupman, and the ladies; which reached a pitch of positive enthusiasm, when old Wardle and Benjamin Allen, assisted by the aforesaid Bob Sawyer, performed some mystic evolutions, which they called a reel.\n\nAll this time, Mr. Winkle, with his face and hands blue with the cold, had been forcing a gimlet into the sole of his feet, and putting his skates on, with the points behind, and getting the straps into a very complicated and entangled state, with the assistance of Mr. Snodgrass, who knew rather less about skates than a Hindoo. At length, however, with the assistance of Mr. Weller, the unfortunate skates were firmly screwed and buckled on, and Mr. Winkle was raised to his feet.\n\n‘Now, then, Sir,’ said Sam, in an encouraging tone; ‘off vith you, and show ‘em how to do it.’\n\n‘Stop, Sam, stop!’ said Mr. Winkle, trembling violently, and clutching hold of Sam’s arms with the grasp of a drowning man. ‘How slippery it is, Sam!’\n\n‘Not an uncommon thing upon ice, Sir,’ replied Mr. Weller. ‘Hold up, Sir!’\n\nThis last observation of Mr. Weller’s bore reference to a demonstration Mr. Winkle made at the instant, of a frantic desire to throw his feet in the air, and dash the back of his head on the ice.\n\n‘These--these--are very awkward skates; ain’t they, Sam?’ inquired Mr. Winkle, staggering.\n\n‘I’m afeerd there’s a orkard gen’l’m’n in ‘em, Sir,’ replied Sam.\n\n‘Now, Winkle,’ cried Mr. Pickwick, quite unconscious that there was anything the matter. ‘Come; the ladies are all anxiety.’\n\n‘Yes, yes,’ replied Mr. Winkle, with a ghastly smile. ‘I’m coming.’\n\n‘Just a-goin’ to begin,’ said Sam, endeavouring to disengage himself. ‘Now, Sir, start off!’\n\n‘Stop an instant, Sam,’ gasped Mr. Winkle, clinging most affectionately to Mr. Weller. ‘I find I’ve got a couple of coats at home that I don’t want, Sam. You may have them, Sam.’\n\n‘Thank’ee, Sir,’ replied Mr. Weller.\n\n‘Never mind touching your hat, Sam,’ said Mr. Winkle hastily. ‘You needn’t take your hand away to do that. I meant to have given you five shillings this morning for a Christmas box, Sam. I’ll give it you this afternoon, Sam.’\n\n‘You’re wery good, sir,’ replied Mr. Weller.\n\n‘Just hold me at first, Sam; will you?’ said Mr. Winkle. ‘There--that’s right. I shall soon get in the way of it, Sam. Not too fast, Sam; not too fast.’\n\nMr. Winkle, stooping forward, with his body half doubled up, was being assisted over the ice by Mr. Weller, in a very singular and un-swan-like manner, when Mr. Pickwick most innocently shouted from the opposite bank--\n\n‘Sam!’\n‘Sir?’\n\n‘Here. I want you.’\n\n‘Let go, Sir,’ said Sam. ‘Don’t you hear the governor a-callin’? Let go, sir.’\n\nWith a violent effort, Mr. Weller disengaged himself from the grasp of the agonised Pickwickian, and, in so doing, administered a considerable impetus to the unhappy Mr. Winkle. With an accuracy which no degree of dexterity or practice could have insured, that unfortunate gentleman bore swiftly down into the centre of the reel, at the very moment when Mr. Bob Sawyer was performing a flourish of unparalleled beauty. Mr. Winkle struck wildly against him, and with a loud crash they both fell heavily down. Mr. Pickwick ran to the spot. Bob Sawyer had risen to his feet, but Mr. Winkle was far too wise to do anything of the kind, in skates. He was seated on the ice, making spasmodic efforts to smile; but anguish was depicted on every lineament of his countenance.\n\n‘Are you hurt?’ inquired Mr. Benjamin Allen, with great anxiety.\n\n‘Not much,’ said Mr. Winkle, rubbing his back very hard.\n\n‘I wish you’d let me bleed you,’ said Mr. Benjamin, with great eagerness.\n\n‘No, thank you,’ replied Mr. Winkle hurriedly.\n\n‘I really think you had better,’ said Allen.\n\n‘Thank you,’ replied Mr. Winkle; ‘I’d rather not.’\n\n‘What do you think, Mr. Pickwick?’ inquired Bob Sawyer.\n\nMr. Pickwick was excited and indignant. He beckoned to Mr. Weller, and said in a stern voice, ‘Take his skates off.’\n\n‘No; but really I had scarcely begun,’ remonstrated Mr. Winkle.\n\n‘Take his skates off,’ repeated Mr. Pickwick firmly.\n\nThe command was not to be resisted. Mr. Winkle allowed Sam to obey it, in silence.\n\n‘Lift him up,’ said Mr. Pickwick. Sam assisted him to rise.\n\nMr. Pickwick retired a few paces apart from the bystanders; and, beckoning his friend to approach, fixed a searching look upon him, and uttered in a low, but distinct and emphatic tone, these remarkable words--\n\n‘You’re a humbug, sir.’\n\nA what?’ said Mr. Winkle, starting.\n\n‘A humbug, Sir. I will speak plainer, if you wish it. An impostor, sir.’\n\nWith those words, Mr. Pickwick turned slowly on his heel, and rejoined his friends.\n\nWhile Mr. Pickwick was delivering himself of the sentiment just recorded, Mr. Weller and the fat boy, having by their joint endeavours cut out a slide, were exercising themselves thereupon, in a very masterly and brilliant manner. Sam Weller, in particular, was displaying that beautiful feat of fancy-sliding which is currently denominated ‘knocking at the cobbler’s door,’ and which is achieved by skimming over the ice on one foot, and occasionally giving a postman’s knock upon it with the other. It was a good long slide, and there was something in the motion which Mr. Pickwick, who was very cold with standing still, could not help envying.\n\n‘It looks a nice warm exercise that, doesn’t it?’ he inquired of Wardle, when that gentleman was thoroughly out of breath, by reason of the indefatigable manner in which he had converted his legs into a pair of compasses, and drawn complicated problems on the ice.\n\n‘Ah, it does, indeed,’ replied Wardle. ‘Do you slide?’\n\n‘I used to do so, on the gutters, when I was a boy,’ replied Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Try it now,’ said Wardle.\n\n‘Oh, do, please, Mr. Pickwick!’ cried all the ladies.\n\n‘I should be very happy to afford you any amusement,’ replied Mr. Pickwick, ‘but I haven’t done such a thing these thirty years.’\n\n‘Pooh! pooh! Nonsense!’ said Wardle, dragging off his skates with the impetuosity which characterised all his proceedings. ‘Here; I’ll keep you company; come along!’ And away went the good-tempered old fellow down the slide, with a rapidity which came very close upon Mr. Weller, and beat the fat boy all to nothing.\n\nMr. Pickwick paused, considered, pulled off his gloves and put them in his hat; took two or three short runs, baulked himself as often, and at last took another run, and went slowly and gravely down the slide, with his feet about a yard and a quarter apart, amidst the gratified shouts of all the spectators.\n\n‘Keep the pot a-bilin’, Sir!’ said Sam; and down went Wardle again, and then Mr. Pickwick, and then Sam, and then Mr. Winkle, and then Mr. Bob Sawyer, and then the fat boy, and then Mr. Snodgrass, following closely upon each other’s heels, and running after each other with as much eagerness as if their future prospects in life depended on their expedition.\n\nIt was the most intensely interesting thing, to observe the manner in which Mr. Pickwick performed his share in the ceremony; to watch the torture of anxiety with which he viewed the person behind, gaining upon him at the imminent hazard of tripping him up; to see him gradually expend the painful force he had put on at first, and turn slowly round on the slide, with his face towards the point from which he had started; to contemplate the playful smile which mantled on his face when he had accomplished the distance, and the eagerness with which he turned round when he had done so, and ran after his predecessor, his black gaiters tripping pleasantly through the snow, and his eyes beaming cheerfulness and gladness through his spectacles. And when he was knocked down (which happened upon the average every third round), it was the most invigorating sight that can possibly be imagined, to behold him gather up his hat, gloves, and handkerchief, with a glowing countenance, and resume his station in the rank, with an ardour and enthusiasm that nothing Could abate.\n\nThe sport was at its height, the sliding was at the quickest, the laughter was at the loudest, when a sharp smart crack was heard. There was a quick rush towards the bank, a wild scream from the ladies, and a shout from Mr. Tupman. A large mass of ice disappeared; the water bubbled up over it; Mr. Pickwick’s hat, gloves, and handkerchief were floating on the surface; and this was all of Mr. Pickwick that anybody could see.\n\nDismay and anguish were depicted on every countenance; the males turned pale, and the females fainted; Mr. Snodgrass and Mr. Winkle grasped each other by the hand, and gazed at the spot where their leader had gone down, with frenzied eagerness; while Mr. Tupman, by way of rendering the promptest assistance, and at the same time conveying to any persons who might be within hearing, the clearest possible notion of the catastrophe, ran off across the country at his utmost speed, screaming ‘Fire!’ with all his might.\n\nIt was at this moment, when old Wardle and Sam Weller were approaching the hole with cautious steps, and Mr. Benjamin Allen was holding a hurried consultation with Mr. Bob Sawyer on the advisability of bleeding the company generally, as an improving little bit of professional practice--it was at this very moment, that a face, head, and shoulders, emerged from beneath the water, and disclosed the features and spectacles of Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Keep yourself up for an instant--for only one instant!’ bawled Mr. Snodgrass.\n\n‘Yes, do; let me implore you--for my sake!’ roared Mr. Winkle, deeply affected. The adjuration was rather unnecessary; the probability being, that if Mr. Pickwick had declined to keep himself up for anybody else’s sake, it would have occurred to him that he might as well do so, for his own.\n\n‘Do you feel the bottom there, old fellow?’ said Wardle.\n\n‘Yes, certainly,’ replied Mr. Pickwick, wringing the water from his head and face, and gasping for breath. ‘I fell upon my back. I couldn’t get on my feet at first.’\n\nThe clay upon so much of Mr. Pickwick’s coat as was yet visible, bore testimony to the accuracy of this statement; and as the fears of the spectators were still further relieved by the fat boy’s suddenly recollecting that the water was nowhere more than five feet deep, prodigies of valour were performed to get him out. After a vast quantity of splashing, and cracking, and struggling, Mr. Pickwick was at length fairly extricated from his unpleasant position, and once more stood on dry land.\n\n‘Oh, he’ll catch his death of cold,’ said Emily.\n\n‘Dear old thing!’ said Arabella. ‘Let me wrap this shawl round you, Mr. Pickwick.’\n\n‘Ah, that’s the best thing you can do,’ said Wardle; ‘and when you’ve got it on, run home as fast as your legs can carry you, and jump into bed directly.’\n\nA dozen shawls were offered on the instant. Three or four of the thickest having been selected, Mr. Pickwick was wrapped up, and started off, under the guidance of Mr. Weller; presenting the singular phenomenon of an elderly gentleman, dripping wet, and without a hat, with his arms bound down to his sides, skimming over the ground, without any clearly-defined purpose, at the rate of six good English miles an hour.\n\nBut Mr. Pickwick cared not for appearances in such an extreme case, and urged on by Sam Weller, he kept at the very top of his speed until he reached the door of Manor Farm, where Mr. Tupman had arrived some five minutes before, and had frightened the old lady into palpitations of the heart by impressing her with the unalterable conviction that the kitchen chimney was on fire--a calamity which always presented itself in glowing colours to the old lady’s mind, when anybody about her evinced the smallest agitation.\n\nMr. Pickwick paused not an instant until he was snug in bed. Sam Weller lighted a blazing fire in the room, and took up his dinner; a bowl of punch was carried up afterwards, and a grand carouse held in honour of his safety. Old Wardle would not hear of his rising, so they made the bed the chair, and Mr. Pickwick presided. A second and a third bowl were ordered in; and when Mr. Pickwick awoke next morning, there was not a symptom of rheumatism about him; which proves, as Mr. Bob Sawyer very justly observed, that there is nothing like hot punch in such cases; and that if ever hot punch did fail to act as a preventive, it was merely because the patient fell into the vulgar error of not taking enough of it.\n\nThe jovial party broke up next morning. Breakings-up are capital things in our school-days, but in after life they are painful enough. Death, self-interest, and fortune’s changes, are every day breaking up many a happy group, and scattering them far and wide; and the boys and girls never come back again. We do not mean to say that it was exactly the case in this particular instance; all we wish to inform the reader is, that the different members of the party dispersed to their several homes; that Mr. Pickwick and his friends once more took their seats on the top of the Muggleton coach; and that Arabella Allen repaired to her place of destination, wherever it might have been--we dare say Mr. Winkle knew, but we confess we don’t--under the care and guardianship of her brother Benjamin, and his most intimate and particular friend, Mr. Bob Sawyer.\n\nBefore they separated, however, that gentleman and Mr. Benjamin Allen drew Mr. Pickwick aside with an air of some mystery; and Mr. Bob Sawyer, thrusting his forefinger between two of Mr. Pickwick’s ribs, and thereby displaying his native drollery, and his knowledge of the anatomy of the human frame, at one and the same time, inquired--\n\n‘I say, old boy, where do you hang out?’ Mr. Pickwick replied that he was at present suspended at the George and Vulture.\n\n‘I wish you’d come and see me,’ said Bob Sawyer.\n\n‘Nothing would give me greater pleasure,’ replied Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘There’s my lodgings,’ said Mr. Bob Sawyer, producing a card. ‘Lant Street, Borough; it’s near Guy’s, and handy for me, you know. Little distance after you’ve passed St. George’s Church--turns out of the High Street on the right hand side the way.’\n\n‘I shall find it,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Come on Thursday fortnight, and bring the other chaps with you,’ said Mr. Bob Sawyer; ‘I’m going to have a few medical fellows that night.’\n\nMr. Pickwick expressed the pleasure it would afford him to meet the medical fellows; and after Mr. Bob Sawyer had informed him that he meant to be very cosy, and that his friend Ben was to be one of the party, they shook hands and separated.\n\nWe feel that in this place we lay ourself open to the inquiry whether Mr. Winkle was whispering, during this brief conversation, to Arabella Allen; and if so, what he said; and furthermore, whether Mr. Snodgrass was conversing apart with Emily Wardle; and if so, what he said. To this, we reply, that whatever they might have said to the ladies, they said nothing at all to Mr. Pickwick or Mr. Tupman for eight-and-twenty miles, and that they sighed very often, refused ale and brandy, and looked gloomy. If our observant lady readers can deduce any satisfactory inferences from these facts, we beg them by all means to do so.\n\nCHAPTER XXXI. WHICH IS ALL ABOUT THE LAW, AND SUNDRY GREAT AUTHORITIES LEARNED THEREIN\nScattered about, in various holes and corners of the Temple, are certain dark and dirty chambers, in and out of which, all the morning in vacation, and half the evening too in term time, there may be seen constantly hurrying with bundles of papers under their arms, and protruding from their pockets, an almost uninterrupted succession of lawyers’ clerks. There are several grades of lawyers’ clerks. There is the articled clerk, who has paid a premium, and is an attorney in perspective, who runs a tailor’s bill, receives invitations to parties, knows a family in Gower Street, and another in Tavistock Square; who goes out of town every long vacation to see his father, who keeps live horses innumerable; and who is, in short, the very aristocrat of clerks. There is the salaried clerk--out of door, or in door, as the case may be--who devotes the major part of his thirty shillings a week to his Personal pleasure and adornments, repairs half-price to the Adelphi Theatre at least three times a week, dissipates majestically at the cider cellars afterwards, and is a dirty caricature of the fashion which expired six months ago. There is the middle-aged copying clerk, with a large family, who is always shabby, and often drunk. And there are the office lads in their first surtouts, who feel a befitting contempt for boys at day-schools, club as they go home at night, for saveloys and porter, and think there’s nothing like ‘life.’ There are varieties of the genus, too numerous to recapitulate, but however numerous they may be, they are all to be seen, at certain regulated business hours, hurrying to and from the places we have just mentioned.\n\nThese sequestered nooks are the public offices of the legal profession, where writs are issued, judgments signed, declarations filed, and numerous other ingenious machines put in motion for the torture and torment of His Majesty’s liege subjects, and the comfort and emolument of the practitioners of the law. They are, for the most part, low- roofed, mouldy rooms, where innumerable rolls of parchment, which have been perspiring in secret for the last century, send forth an agreeable odour, which is mingled by day with the scent of the dry-rot, and by night with the various exhalations which arise from damp cloaks, festering umbrellas, and the coarsest tallow candles.\n\nAbout half-past seven o’clock in the evening, some ten days or a fortnight after Mr. Pickwick and his friends returned to London, there hurried into one of these offices, an individual in a brown coat and brass buttons, whose long hair was scrupulously twisted round the rim of his napless hat, and whose soiled drab trousers were so tightly strapped over his Blucher boots, that his knees threatened every moment to start from their concealment. He produced from his coat pockets a long and narrow strip of parchment, on which the presiding functionary impressed an illegible black stamp. He then drew forth four scraps of paper, of similar dimensions, each containing a printed copy of the strip of parchment with blanks for a name; and having filled up the blanks, put all the five documents in his pocket, and hurried away.\n\nThe man in the brown coat, with the cabalistic documents in his pocket, was no other than our old acquaintance Mr. Jackson, of the house of Dodson & Fogg, Freeman’s Court, Cornhill. Instead of returning to the office whence he came, however, he bent his steps direct to Sun Court, and walking straight into the George and Vulture, demanded to know whether one Mr. Pickwick was within.\n\n‘Call Mr. Pickwick’s servant, Tom,’ said the barmaid of the George and Vulture.\n\n‘Don’t trouble yourself,’ said Mr. Jackson. ‘I’ve come on business. If you’ll show me Mr. Pickwick’s room I’ll step up myself.’\n\n‘What name, Sir?’ said the waiter.\n\n‘Jackson,’ replied the clerk.\n\nThe waiter stepped upstairs to announce Mr. Jackson; but Mr. Jackson saved him the trouble by following close at his heels, and walking into the apartment before he could articulate a syllable.\n\nMr. Pickwick had, that day, invited his three friends to dinner; they were all seated round the fire, drinking their wine, when Mr. Jackson presented himself, as above described.\n\n‘How de do, sir?’ said Mr. Jackson, nodding to Mr. Pickwick.\n\nThat gentleman bowed, and looked somewhat surprised, for the physiognomy of Mr. Jackson dwelt not in his recollection.\n\n‘I have called from Dodson and Fogg’s,’ said Mr. Jackson, in an explanatory tone.\n\nMr. Pickwick roused at the name. ‘I refer you to my attorney, Sir; Mr. Perker, of Gray’s Inn,’ said he. ‘Waiter, show this gentleman out.’\n\n‘Beg your pardon, Mr. Pickwick,’ said Jackson, deliberately depositing his hat on the floor, and drawing from his pocket the strip of parchment. ‘But personal service, by clerk or agent, in these cases, you know, Mr. Pickwick--nothing like caution, sir, in all legal forms--eh?’\n\nHere Mr. Jackson cast his eye on the parchment; and, resting his hands on the table, and looking round with a winning and persuasive smile, said, ‘Now, come; don’t let’s have no words about such a little matter as this. Which of you gentlemen’s name’s Snodgrass?’\n\nAt this inquiry, Mr. Snodgrass gave such a very undisguised and palpable start, that no further reply was needed.\n\n‘Ah! I thought so,’ said Mr. Jackson, more affably than before. ‘I’ve a little something to trouble you with, Sir.’\n\n‘Me!’ exclaimed Mr. Snodgrass.\n\n‘It’s only a subpoena in Bardell and Pickwick on behalf of the plaintiff,’ replied Jackson, singling out one of the slips of paper, and producing a shilling from his waistcoat pocket. ‘It’ll come on, in the settens after Term: fourteenth of Febooary, we expect; we’ve marked it a special jury cause, and it’s only ten down the paper. That’s yours, Mr. Snodgrass.’ As Jackson said this, he presented the parchment before the eyes of Mr. Snodgrass, and slipped the paper and the shilling into his hand.\n\nMr. Tupman had witnessed this process in silent astonishment, when Jackson, turning sharply upon him, said--\n\n‘I think I ain’t mistaken when I say your name’s Tupman, am I?’\n\nMr. Tupman looked at Mr. Pickwick; but, perceiving no encouragement in that gentleman’s widely-opened eyes to deny his name, said--\n\n‘Yes, my name is Tupman, Sir.’\n\n‘And that other gentleman’s Mr. Winkle, I think?’ said Jackson. Mr. Winkle faltered out a reply in the affirmative; and both gentlemen were forthwith invested with a slip of paper, and a shilling each, by the dexterous Mr. Jackson.\n\n‘Now,’ said Jackson, ‘I’m afraid you’ll think me rather troublesome, but I want somebody else, if it ain’t inconvenient. I have Samuel Weller’s name here, Mr. Pickwick.’\n\n‘Send my servant here, waiter,’ said Mr. Pickwick. The waiter retired, considerably astonished, and Mr. Pickwick motioned Jackson to a seat.\n\nThere was a painful pause, which was at length broken by the innocent defendant.\n\n‘I suppose, Sir,’ said Mr. Pickwick, his indignation rising while he spoke--‘I suppose, Sir, that it is the intention of your employers to seek to criminate me upon the testimony of my own friends?’\n\nMr. Jackson struck his forefinger several times against the left side of his nose, to intimate that he was not there to disclose the secrets of the prison house, and playfully rejoined--\n\n‘Not knowin’, can’t say.’\n\n‘For what other reason, Sir,’ pursued Mr. Pickwick, ‘are these subpoenas served upon them, if not for this?’\n\n‘Very good plant, Mr. Pickwick,’ replied Jackson, slowly shaking his head. ‘But it won’t do. No harm in trying, but there’s little to be got out of me.’\n\nHere Mr. Jackson smiled once more upon the company, and, applying his left thumb to the tip of his nose, worked a visionary coffee-mill with his right hand, thereby performing a very graceful piece of pantomime (then much in vogue, but now, unhappily, almost obsolete) which was familiarly denominated ‘taking a grinder.’\n\n‘No, no, Mr. Pickwick,’ said Jackson, in conclusion; ‘Perker’s people must guess what we’ve served these subpoenas for. If they can’t, they must wait till the action comes on, and then they’ll find out.’\n\nMr. Pickwick bestowed a look of excessive disgust on his unwelcome visitor, and would probably have hurled some tremendous anathema at the heads of Messrs. Dodson & Fogg, had not Sam’s entrance at the instant interrupted him.\n\n‘Samuel Weller?’ said Mr. Jackson, inquiringly.\n\n‘Vun o’ the truest things as you’ve said for many a long year,’ replied Sam, in a most composed manner.\n\n‘Here’s a subpoena for you, Mr. Weller,’ said Jackson.\n\n‘What’s that in English?’ inquired Sam.\n\n‘Here’s the original,’ said Jackson, declining the required explanation.\n\n‘Which?’ said Sam.\n\n‘This,’ replied Jackson, shaking the parchment.\n\n‘Oh, that’s the ‘rig’nal, is it?’ said Sam. ‘Well, I’m wery glad I’ve seen the ‘rig’nal, ‘cos it’s a gratifyin’ sort o’ thing, and eases vun’s mind so much.’\n\n‘And here’s the shilling,’ said Jackson. ‘It’s from Dodson and Fogg’s.’\n\n‘And it’s uncommon handsome o’ Dodson and Fogg, as knows so little of me, to come down vith a present,’ said Sam. ‘I feel it as a wery high compliment, sir; it’s a wery honorable thing to them, as they knows how to reward merit werever they meets it. Besides which, it’s affectin’ to one’s feelin’s.’\n\nAs Mr. Weller said this, he inflicted a little friction on his right eyelid, with the sleeve of his coat, after the most approved manner of actors when they are in domestic pathetics.\n\nMr. Jackson seemed rather puzzled by Sam’s proceedings; but, as he had served the subpoenas, and had nothing more to say, he made a feint of putting on the one glove which he usually carried in his hand, for the sake of appearances; and returned to the office to report progress.\n\nMr. Pickwick slept little that night; his memory had received a very disagreeable refresher on the subject of Mrs. Bardell’s action. He breakfasted betimes next morning, and, desiring Sam to accompany him, set forth towards Gray’s Inn Square.\n\n‘Sam!’ said Mr. Pickwick, looking round, when they got to the end of Cheapside.\n\n‘Sir?’ said Sam, stepping up to his master.\n\n‘Which way?’\n\nUp Newgate Street.’\n\nMr. Pickwick did not turn round immediately, but looked vacantly in Sam’s face for a few seconds, and heaved a deep sigh.\n\n‘What’s the matter, sir?’ inquired Sam.\n\n‘This action, Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘is expected to come on, on the fourteenth of next month.’\n\nRemarkable coincidence that ‘ere, sir,’ replied Sam.\n\n‘Why remarkable, Sam?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Walentine’s day, sir,’ responded Sam; ‘reg’lar good day for a breach o’ promise trial.’\n\nMr. Weller’s smile awakened no gleam of mirth in his master’s countenance. Mr. Pickwick turned abruptly round, and led the way in silence.\n\nThey had walked some distance, Mr. Pickwick trotting on before, plunged in profound meditation, and Sam following behind, with a countenance expressive of the most enviable and easy defiance of everything and everybody, when the latter, who was always especially anxious to impart to his master any exclusive information he possessed, quickened his pace until he was close at Mr. Pickwick’s heels; and, pointing up at a house they were passing, said--\n\n‘Wery nice pork-shop that ‘ere, sir.’\n\n‘Yes, it seems so,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Celebrated sassage factory,’ said Sam.\n\n‘Is it?’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Is it!’ reiterated Sam, with some indignation; ‘I should rayther think it was. Why, sir, bless your innocent eyebrows, that’s where the mysterious disappearance of a ‘spectable tradesman took place four years ago.’\n\n‘You don’t mean to say he was burked, Sam?’ said Mr. Pickwick, looking hastily round.\n\n‘No, I don’t indeed, sir,’ replied Mr. Weller, ‘I wish I did; far worse than that. He was the master o’ that ‘ere shop, sir, and the inwentor o’ the patent-never-leavin’-off sassage steam-ingin, as ‘ud swaller up a pavin’ stone if you put it too near, and grind it into sassages as easy as if it was a tender young babby. Wery proud o’ that machine he was, as it was nat’ral he should be, and he’d stand down in the celler a-lookin’ at it wen it was in full play, till he got quite melancholy with joy. A wery happy man he’d ha’ been, Sir, in the procession o’ that ‘ere ingin and two more lovely hinfants besides, if it hadn’t been for his wife, who was a most owdacious wixin. She was always a-follerin’ him about, and dinnin’ in his ears, till at last he couldn’t stand it no longer. “I’ll tell you what it is, my dear,” he says one day; “if you persewere in this here sort of amusement,” he says, “I’m blessed if I don’t go away to ‘Merriker; and that’s all about it.” “You’re a idle willin,” says she, “and I wish the ‘Merrikins joy of their bargain.” Arter which she keeps on abusin’ of him for half an hour, and then runs into the little parlour behind the shop, sets to a-screamin’, says he’ll be the death on her, and falls in a fit, which lasts for three good hours--one o’ them fits wich is all screamin’ and kickin’. Well, next mornin’, the husband was missin’. He hadn’t taken nothin’ from the till--hadn’t even put on his greatcoat--so it was quite clear he warn’t gone to ‘Merriker. Didn’t come back next day; didn’t come back next week; missis had bills printed, sayin’ that, if he’d come back, he should be forgiven everythin’ (which was very liberal, seein’ that he hadn’t done nothin’ at all); the canals was dragged, and for two months arterwards, wenever a body turned up, it was carried, as a reg’lar thing, straight off to the sassage shop. Hows’ever, none on ‘em answered; so they gave out that he’d run away, and she kep’ on the bis’ness. One Saturday night, a little, thin, old gen’l’m’n comes into the shop in a great passion and says, “Are you the missis o’ this here shop?” “Yes, I am,” says she. “Well, ma’am,” says he, “then I’ve just looked in to say that me and my family ain’t a-goin’ to be choked for nothin’; and more than that, ma’am,” he says, “you’ll allow me to observe that as you don’t use the primest parts of the meat in the manafacter o’ sassages, I’d think you’d find beef come nearly as cheap as buttons.” “As buttons, Sir!” says she. “Buttons, ma’am,” says the little, old gentleman, unfolding a bit of paper, and showin’ twenty or thirty halves o’ buttons. “Nice seasonin’ for sassages, is trousers’ buttons, ma’am.” “They’re my husband’s buttons!” says the widder beginnin’ to faint, “What!” screams the little old gen’l’m’n, turnin’ wery pale. “I see it all,” says the widder; “in a fit of temporary insanity he rashly converted hisself into sassages!” And so he had, Sir,’ said Mr. Weller, looking steadily into Mr. Pickwick’s horror-stricken countenance, ‘or else he’d been draw’d into the ingin; but however that might ha’ been, the little, old gen’l’m’n, who had been remarkably partial to sassages all his life, rushed out o’ the shop in a wild state, and was never heerd on arterwards!’\n\nThe relation of this affecting incident of private life brought master and man to Mr. Perker’s chambers. Lowten, holding the door half open, was in conversation with a rustily-clad, miserable-looking man, in boots without toes and gloves without fingers. There were traces of privation and suffering--almost of despair--in his lank and care-worn countenance; he felt his poverty, for he shrank to the dark side of the staircase as Mr. Pickwick approached.\n\n‘It’s very unfortunate,’ said the stranger, with a sigh.\n\n‘Very,’ said Lowten, scribbling his name on the doorpost with his pen, and rubbing it out again with the feather. ‘Will you leave a message for him?’\n\n‘When do you think he’ll be back?’ inquired the stranger.\n\n‘Quite uncertain,’ replied Lowten, winking at Mr. Pickwick, as the stranger cast his eyes towards the ground.\n\n‘You don’t think it would be of any use my waiting for him?’ said the stranger, looking wistfully into the office.\n\n‘Oh, no, I’m sure it wouldn’t,’ replied the clerk, moving a little more into the centre of the doorway. ‘He’s certain not to be back this week, and it’s a chance whether he will be next; for when Perker once gets out of town, he’s never in a hurry to come back again.’\n\n‘Out of town!’ said Mr. Pickwick; ‘dear me, how unfortunate!’\n\n‘Don’t go away, Mr. Pickwick,’ said Lowten, ‘I’ve got a letter for you.’ The stranger, seeming to hesitate, once more looked towards the ground, and the clerk winked slyly at Mr. Pickwick, as if to intimate that some exquisite piece of humour was going forward, though what it was Mr. Pickwick could not for the life of him divine.\n\n‘Step in, Mr. Pickwick,’ said Lowten. ‘Well, will you leave a message, Mr. Watty, or will you call again?’\n\n‘Ask him to be so kind as to leave out word what has been done in my business,’ said the man; ‘for God’s sake don’t neglect it, Mr. Lowten.’\n\n‘No, no; I won’t forget it,’ replied the clerk. ‘Walk in, Mr. Pickwick. Good-morning, Mr. Watty; it’s a fine day for walking, isn’t it?’ Seeing that the stranger still lingered, he beckoned Sam Weller to follow his master in, and shut the door in his face.\n\n‘There never was such a pestering bankrupt as that since the world began, I do believe!’ said Lowten, throwing down his pen with the air of an injured man. ‘His affairs haven’t been in Chancery quite four years yet, and I’m d----d if he don’t come worrying here twice a week. Step this way, Mr. Pickwick. Perker is in, and he’ll see you, I know. Devilish cold,’ he added pettishly, ‘standing at that door, wasting one’s time with such seedy vagabonds!’ Having very vehemently stirred a particularly large fire with a particularly small poker, the clerk led the way to his principal’s private room, and announced Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Ah, my dear Sir,’ said little Mr. Perker, bustling up from his chair. ‘Well, my dear sir, and what’s the news about your matter, eh? Anything more about our friends in Freeman’s Court? They’ve not been sleeping, I know that. Ah, they’re very smart fellows; very smart, indeed.’\n\nAs the little man concluded, he took an emphatic pinch of snuff, as a tribute to the smartness of Messrs. Dodson and Fogg.\n\n‘They are great scoundrels,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Aye, aye,’ said the little man; ‘that’s a matter of opinion, you know, and we won’t dispute about terms; because of course you can’t be expected to view these subjects with a professional eye. Well, we’ve done everything that’s necessary. I have retained Serjeant Snubbin.’\n\n‘Is he a good man?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Good man!’ replied Perker; ‘bless your heart and soul, my dear Sir, Serjeant Snubbin is at the very top of his profession. Gets treble the business of any man in court--engaged in every case. You needn’t mention it abroad; but we say--we of the profession--that Serjeant Snubbin leads the court by the nose.’\n\nThe little man took another pinch of snuff as he made this communication, and nodded mysteriously to Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘They have subpoenaed my three friends,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Ah! of course they would,’ replied Perker. ‘Important witnesses; saw you in a delicate situation.’\n\n‘But she fainted of her own accord,’ said Mr. Pickwick. ‘She threw herself into my arms.’\n\n‘Very likely, my dear Sir,’ replied Perker; ‘very likely and very natural. Nothing more so, my dear Sir, nothing. But who’s to prove it?’\n\n‘They have subpoenaed my servant, too,’ said Mr. Pickwick, quitting the other point; for there Mr. Perker’s question had somewhat staggered him.\n\n‘Sam?’ said Perker.\n\nMr. Pickwick replied in the affirmative.\n\n‘Of course, my dear Sir; of course. I knew they would. I could have told you that, a month ago. You know, my dear Sir, if you will take the management of your affairs into your own hands after entrusting them to your solicitor, you must also take the consequences.’ Here Mr. Perker drew himself up with conscious dignity, and brushed some stray grains of snuff from his shirt frill.\n\n‘And what do they want him to prove?’ asked Mr. Pickwick, after two or three minutes’ silence.\n\n‘That you sent him up to the plaintiff ‘s to make some offer of a compromise, I suppose,’ replied Perker. ‘It don’t matter much, though; I don’t think many counsel could get a great deal out of him.’\n\n‘I don’t think they could,’ said Mr. Pickwick, smiling, despite his vexation, at the idea of Sam’s appearance as a witness. ‘What course do we pursue?’\n\n‘We have only one to adopt, my dear Sir,’ replied Perker; ‘cross-examine the witnesses; trust to Snubbin’s eloquence; throw dust in the eyes of the judge; throw ourselves on the jury.’\n\n‘And suppose the verdict is against me?’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\nMr. Perker smiled, took a very long pinch of snuff, stirred the fire, shrugged his shoulders, and remained expressively silent.\n\n‘You mean that in that case I must pay the damages?’ said Mr. Pickwick, who had watched this telegraphic answer with considerable sternness.\n\nPerker gave the fire another very unnecessary poke, and said, ‘I am afraid so.’\n\n‘Then I beg to announce to you my unalterable determination to pay no damages whatever,’ said Mr. Pickwick, most emphatically. ‘None, Perker. Not a pound, not a penny of my money, shall find its way into the pockets of Dodson and Fogg. That is my deliberate and irrevocable determination.’ Mr. Pickwick gave a heavy blow on the table before him, in confirmation of the irrevocability of his intention.\n\n‘Very well, my dear Sir, very well,’ said Perker. ‘You know best, of course.’\n\n‘Of course,’ replied Mr. Pickwick hastily. ‘Where does Serjeant Snubbin live?’\n\nIn Lincoln’s Inn Old Square,’ replied Perker.\n\n‘I should like to see him,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘See Serjeant Snubbin, my dear Sir!’ rejoined Perker, in utter amazement. ‘Pooh, pooh, my dear Sir, impossible. See Serjeant Snubbin! Bless you, my dear Sir, such a thing was never heard of, without a consultation fee being previously paid, and a consultation fixed. It couldn’t be done, my dear Sir; it couldn’t be done.’\n\nMr. Pickwick, however, had made up his mind not only that it could be done, but that it should be done; and the consequence was, that within ten minutes after he had received the assurance that the thing was impossible, he was conducted by his solicitor into the outer office of the great Serjeant Snubbin himself.\n\nIt was an uncarpeted room of tolerable dimensions, with a large writing- table drawn up near the fire, the baize top of which had long since lost all claim to its original hue of green, and had gradually grown gray with dust and age, except where all traces of its natural colour were obliterated by ink-stains. Upon the table were numerous little bundles of papers tied with red tape; and behind it, sat an elderly clerk, whose sleek appearance and heavy gold watch-chain presented imposing indications of the extensive and lucrative practice of Mr. Serjeant Snubbin.\n\n‘Is the Serjeant in his room, Mr. Mallard?’ inquired Perker, offering his box with all imaginable courtesy.\n\n‘Yes, he is,’ was the reply, ‘but he’s very busy. Look here; not an opinion given yet, on any one of these cases; and an expedition fee paid with all of ‘em.’ The clerk smiled as he said this, and inhaled the pinch of snuff with a zest which seemed to be compounded of a fondness for snuff and a relish for fees.\n\n‘Something like practice that,’ said Perker.\n\n‘Yes,’ said the barrister’s clerk, producing his own box, and offering it with the greatest cordiality; ‘and the best of it is, that as nobody alive except myself can read the serjeant’s writing, they are obliged to wait for the opinions, when he has given them, till I have copied ‘em, ha-ha-ha!’\n\n‘Which makes good for we know who, besides the serjeant, and draws a little more out of the clients, eh?’ said Perker; ‘Ha, ha, ha!’ At this the serjeant’s clerk laughed again--not a noisy boisterous laugh, but a silent, internal chuckle, which Mr. Pickwick disliked to hear. When a man bleeds inwardly, it is a dangerous thing for himself; but when he laughs inwardly, it bodes no good to other people.\n\n‘You haven’t made me out that little list of the fees that I’m in your debt, have you?’ said Perker.\n\n‘No, I have not,’ replied the clerk.\n\n‘I wish you would,’ said Perker. ‘Let me have them, and I’ll send you a cheque. But I suppose you’re too busy pocketing the ready money, to think of the debtors, eh? ha, ha, ha!’ This sally seemed to tickle the clerk amazingly, and he once more enjoyed a little quiet laugh to himself.\n\n‘But, Mr. Mallard, my dear friend,’ said Perker, suddenly recovering his gravity, and drawing the great man’s great man into a Corner, by the lappel of his coat; ‘you must persuade the Serjeant to see me, and my client here.’\n\n‘Come, come,’ said the clerk, ‘that’s not bad either. See the Serjeant! come, that’s too absurd.’ Notwithstanding the absurdity of the proposal, however, the clerk allowed himself to be gently drawn beyond the hearing of Mr. Pickwick; and after a short conversation conducted in whispers, walked softly down a little dark passage, and disappeared into the legal luminary’s sanctum, whence he shortly returned on tiptoe, and informed Mr. Perker and Mr. Pickwick that the Serjeant had been prevailed upon, in violation of all established rules and customs, to admit them at once.\n\nMr. Serjeant Snubbins was a lantern-faced, sallow-complexioned man, of about five-and-forty, or--as the novels say--he might be fifty. He had that dull-looking, boiled eye which is often to be seen in the heads of people who have applied themselves during many years to a weary and laborious course of study; and which would have been sufficient, without the additional eyeglass which dangled from a broad black riband round his neck, to warn a stranger that he was very near-sighted. His hair was thin and weak, which was partly attributable to his having never devoted much time to its arrangement, and partly to his having worn for five- and-twenty years the forensic wig which hung on a block beside him. The marks of hairpowder on his coat-collar, and the ill-washed and worse tied white neckerchief round his throat, showed that he had not found leisure since he left the court to make any alteration in his dress; while the slovenly style of the remainder of his costume warranted the inference that his personal appearance would not have been very much improved if he had. Books of practice, heaps of papers, and opened letters, were scattered over the table, without any attempt at order or arrangement; the furniture of the room was old and rickety; the doors of the book-case were rotting in their hinges; the dust flew out from the carpet in little clouds at every step; the blinds were yellow with age and dirt; the state of everything in the room showed, with a clearness not to be mistaken, that Mr. Serjeant Snubbin was far too much occupied with his professional pursuits to take any great heed or regard of his personal comforts.\n\nThe Serjeant was writing when his clients entered; he bowed abstractedly when Mr. Pickwick was introduced by his solicitor; and then, motioning them to a seat, put his pen carefully in the inkstand, nursed his left leg, and waited to be spoken to.\n\n‘Mr. Pickwick is the defendant in Bardell and Pickwick, Serjeant Snubbin,’ said Perker.\n\n‘I am retained in that, am I?’ said the Serjeant.\n\n‘You are, Sir,’ replied Perker.\n\nThe Serjeant nodded his head, and waited for something else.\n\n‘Mr. Pickwick was anxious to call upon you, Serjeant Snubbin,’ said Perker, ‘to state to you, before you entered upon the case, that he denies there being any ground or pretence whatever for the action against him; and that unless he came into court with clean hands, and without the most conscientious conviction that he was right in resisting the plaintiff’s demand, he would not be there at all. I believe I state your views correctly; do I not, my dear Sir?’ said the little man, turning to Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Quite so,’ replied that gentleman.\n\nMr. Serjeant Snubbin unfolded his glasses, raised them to his eyes; and, after looking at Mr. Pickwick for a few seconds with great curiosity, turned to Mr. Perker, and said, smiling slightly as he spoke--\n\n‘Has Mr. Pickwick a strong case?’\n\nThe attorney shrugged his shoulders.\n\n‘Do you propose calling witnesses?’\n\n‘No.’\nThe smile on the Serjeant’s countenance became more defined; he rocked his leg with increased violence; and, throwing himself back in his easy- chair, coughed dubiously.\n\nThese tokens of the Serjeant’s presentiments on the subject, slight as they were, were not lost on Mr. Pickwick. He settled the spectacles, through which he had attentively regarded such demonstrations of the barrister’s feelings as he had permitted himself to exhibit, more firmly on his nose; and said with great energy, and in utter disregard of all Mr. Perker’s admonitory winkings and frownings--\n\n‘My wishing to wait upon you, for such a purpose as this, Sir, appears, I have no doubt, to a gentleman who sees so much of these matters as you must necessarily do, a very extraordinary circumstance.’\n\nThe Serjeant tried to look gravely at the fire, but the smile came back again.\n\n‘Gentlemen of your profession, Sir,’ continued Mr. Pickwick, ‘see the worst side of human nature. All its disputes, all its ill-will and bad blood, rise up before you. You know from your experience of juries (I mean no disparagement to you, or them) how much depends upon effect; and you are apt to attribute to others, a desire to use, for purposes of deception and self-interest, the very instruments which you, in pure honesty and honour of purpose, and with a laudable desire to do your utmost for your client, know the temper and worth of so well, from constantly employing them yourselves. I really believe that to this circumstance may be attributed the vulgar but very general notion of your being, as a body, suspicious, distrustful, and over-cautious. Conscious as I am, sir, of the disadvantage of making such a declaration to you, under such circumstances, I have come here, because I wish you distinctly to understand, as my friend Mr. Perker has said, that I am innocent of the falsehood laid to my charge; and although I am very well aware of the inestimable value of your assistance, Sir, I must beg to add, that unless you sincerely believe this, I would rather be deprived of the aid of your talents than have the advantage of them.’\n\nLong before the close of this address, which we are bound to say was of a very prosy character for Mr. Pickwick, the Serjeant had relapsed into a state of abstraction. After some minutes, however, during which he had reassumed his pen, he appeared to be again aware of the presence of his clients; raising his head from the paper, he said, rather snappishly--\n\n‘Who is with me in this case?’\n\n‘Mr. Phunky, Serjeant Snubbin,’ replied the attorney.\n\n‘Phunky--Phunky,’ said the Serjeant, ‘I never heard the name before. He must be a very young man.’\n\n‘Yes, he is a very young man,’ replied the attorney. ‘He was only called the other day. Let me see--he has not been at the Bar eight years yet.’\n\n‘Ah, I thought not,’ said the Serjeant, in that sort of pitying tone in which ordinary folks would speak of a very helpless little child. ‘Mr. Mallard, send round to Mr.--Mr.--’\n\nPhunky’s--Holborn Court, Gray’s Inn,’ interposed Perker. (Holborn Court, by the bye, is South Square now.)--‘Mr. Phunky, and say I should be glad if he’d step here, a moment.’\n\nMr. Mallard departed to execute his commission; and Serjeant Snubbin relapsed into abstraction until Mr. Phunky himself was introduced.\n\nAlthough an infant barrister, he was a full-grown man. He had a very nervous manner, and a painful hesitation in his speech; it did not appear to be a natural defect, but seemed rather the result of timidity, arising from the consciousness of being ‘kept down’ by want of means, or interest, or connection, or impudence, as the case might be. He was overawed by the Serjeant, and profoundly courteous to the attorney.\n\n‘I have not had the pleasure of seeing you before, Mr. Phunky,’ said Serjeant Snubbin, with haughty condescension.\n\nMr. Phunky bowed. He had had the pleasure of seeing the Serjeant, and of envying him too, with all a poor man’s envy, for eight years and a quarter.\n\n‘You are with me in this case, I understand?’ said the Serjeant.\n\nIf Mr. Phunky had been a rich man, he would have instantly sent for his clerk to remind him; if he had been a wise one, he would have applied his forefinger to his forehead, and endeavoured to recollect, whether, in the multiplicity of his engagements, he had undertaken this one or not; but as he was neither rich nor wise (in this sense, at all events) he turned red, and bowed.\n\n‘Have you read the papers, Mr. Phunky?’ inquired the Serjeant.\n\nHere again, Mr. Phunky should have professed to have forgotten all about the merits of the case; but as he had read such papers as had been laid before him in the course of the action, and had thought of nothing else, waking or sleeping, throughout the two months during which he had been retained as Mr. Serjeant Snubbin’s junior, he turned a deeper red and bowed again.\n\n‘This is Mr. Pickwick,’ said the Serjeant, waving his pen in the direction in which that gentleman was standing.\n\nMr. Phunky bowed to Mr. Pickwick, with a reverence which a first client must ever awaken; and again inclined his head towards his leader.\n\n‘Perhaps you will take Mr. Pickwick away,’ said the Serjeant, ‘and--and- -and--hear anything Mr. Pickwick may wish to communicate. We shall have a consultation, of course.’ With that hint that he had been interrupted quite long enough, Mr. Serjeant Snubbin, who had been gradually growing more and more abstracted, applied his glass to his eyes for an instant, bowed slightly round, and was once more deeply immersed in the case before him, which arose out of an interminable lawsuit, originating in the act of an individual, deceased a century or so ago, who had stopped up a pathway leading from some place which nobody ever came from, to some other place which nobody ever went to.\n\nMr. Phunky would not hear of passing through any door until Mr. Pickwick and his solicitor had passed through before him, so it was some time before they got into the Square; and when they did reach it, they walked up and down, and held a long conference, the result of which was, that it was a very difficult matter to say how the verdict would go; that nobody could presume to calculate on the issue of an action; that it was very lucky they had prevented the other party from getting Serjeant Snubbin; and other topics of doubt and consolation, common in such a position of affairs.\n\nMr. Weller was then roused by his master from a sweet sleep of an hour’s duration; and, bidding adieu to Lowten, they returned to the city.\n\nCHAPTER XXXII. DESCRIBES, FAR MORE FULLY THAN THE COURT NEWSMAN EVER DID, A BACHELOR’S PARTY, GIVEN BY MR. BOB SAWYER AT HIS LODGINGS IN THE BOROUGH\nThere is a repose about Lant Street, in the Borough, which sheds a gentle melancholy upon the soul. There are always a good many houses to let in the street: it is a by-street too, and its dulness is soothing. A house in Lant Street would not come within the denomination of a first- rate residence, in the strict acceptation of the term; but it is a most desirable spot nevertheless. If a man wished to abstract himself from the world--to remove himself from within the reach of temptation--to place himself beyond the possibility of any inducement to look out of the window--we should recommend him by all means go to Lant Street.\n\nIn this happy retreat are colonised a few clear-starchers, a sprinkling of journeymen bookbinders, one or two prison agents for the Insolvent Court, several small housekeepers who are employed in the Docks, a handful of mantua-makers, and a seasoning of jobbing tailors. The majority of the inhabitants either direct their energies to the letting of furnished apartments, or devote themselves to the healthful and invigorating pursuit of mangling. The chief features in the still life of the street are green shutters, lodging-bills, brass door-plates, and bell-handles; the principal specimens of animated nature, the pot-boy, the muffin youth, and the baked-potato man. The population is migratory, usually disappearing on the verge of quarter-day, and generally by night. His Majesty’s revenues are seldom collected in this happy valley; the rents are dubious; and the water communication is very frequently cut off.\n\nMr. Bob Sawyer embellished one side of the fire, in his first-floor front, early on the evening for which he had invited Mr. Pickwick, and Mr. Ben Allen the other. The preparations for the reception of visitors appeared to be completed. The umbrellas in the passage had been heaped into the little corner outside the back-parlour door; the bonnet and shawl of the landlady’s servant had been removed from the bannisters; there were not more than two pairs of pattens on the street-door mat; and a kitchen candle, with a very long snuff, burned cheerfully on the ledge of the staircase window. Mr. Bob Sawyer had himself purchased the spirits at a wine vaults in High Street, and had returned home preceding the bearer thereof, to preclude the possibility of their delivery at the wrong house. The punch was ready-made in a red pan in the bedroom; a little table, covered with a green baize cloth, had been borrowed from the parlour, to play at cards on; and the glasses of the establishment, together with those which had been borrowed for the occasion from the public-house, were all drawn up in a tray, which was deposited on the landing outside the door.\n\nNotwithstanding the highly satisfactory nature of all these arrangements, there was a cloud on the countenance of Mr. Bob Sawyer, as he sat by the fireside. There was a sympathising expression, too, in the features of Mr. Ben Allen, as he gazed intently on the coals, and a tone of melancholy in his voice, as he said, after a long silence--\n\n‘Well, it is unlucky she should have taken it in her head to turn sour, just on this occasion. She might at least have waited till to-morrow.’\n\n‘That’s her malevolence--that’s her malevolence,’ returned Mr. Bob Sawyer vehemently. ‘She says that if I can afford to give a party I ought to be able to pay her confounded “little bill.”’\n\nHow long has it been running?’ inquired Mr. Ben Allen. A bill, by the bye, is the most extraordinary locomotive engine that the genius of man ever produced. It would keep on running during the longest lifetime, without ever once stopping of its own accord.\n\n‘Only a quarter, and a month or so,’ replied Mr. Bob Sawyer.\n\nBen Allen coughed hopelessly, and directed a searching look between the two top bars of the stove.\n\n‘It’ll be a deuced unpleasant thing if she takes it into her head to let out, when those fellows are here, won’t it?’ said Mr. Ben Allen at length.\n\n‘Horrible,’ replied Bob Sawyer, ‘horrible.’\n\nA low tap was heard at the room door. Mr. Bob Sawyer looked expressively at his friend, and bade the tapper come in; whereupon a dirty, slipshod girl in black cotton stockings, who might have passed for the neglected daughter of a superannuated dustman in very reduced circumstances, thrust in her head, and said--\n\n‘Please, Mister Sawyer, Missis Raddle wants to speak to you.’\n\nBefore Mr. Bob Sawyer could return any answer, the girl suddenly disappeared with a jerk, as if somebody had given her a violent pull behind; this mysterious exit was no sooner accomplished, than there was another tap at the door--a smart, pointed tap, which seemed to say, ‘Here I am, and in I’m coming.’\n\nMr. Bob Sawyer glanced at his friend with a look of abject apprehension, and once more cried, ‘Come in.’\n\nThe permission was not at all necessary, for, before Mr. Bob Sawyer had uttered the words, a little, fierce woman bounced into the room, all in a tremble with passion, and pale with rage.\n\n‘Now, Mr. Sawyer,’ said the little, fierce woman, trying to appear very calm, ‘if you’ll have the kindness to settle that little bill of mine I’ll thank you, because I’ve got my rent to pay this afternoon, and my landlord’s a-waiting below now.’ Here the little woman rubbed her hands, and looked steadily over Mr. Bob Sawyer’s head, at the wall behind him.\n\n‘I am very sorry to put you to any inconvenience, Mrs. Raddle,’ said Bob Sawyer deferentially, ‘but--’\n\n‘Oh, it isn’t any inconvenience,’ replied the little woman, with a shrill titter. ‘I didn’t want it particular before to-day; leastways, as it has to go to my landlord directly, it was as well for you to keep it as me. You promised me this afternoon, Mr. Sawyer, and every gentleman as has ever lived here, has kept his word, Sir, as of course anybody as calls himself a gentleman does.’ Mrs. Raddle tossed her head, bit her lips, rubbed her hands harder, and looked at the wall more steadily than ever. It was plain to see, as Mr. Bob Sawyer remarked in a style of Eastern allegory on a subsequent occasion, that she was ‘getting the steam up.’\n\n‘I am very sorry, Mrs. Raddle,’ said Bob Sawyer, with all imaginable humility, ‘but the fact is, that I have been disappointed in the City to-day.’--Extraordinary place that City. An astonishing number of men always are getting disappointed there.\n\n‘Well, Mr. Sawyer,’ said Mrs. Raddle, planting herself firmly on a purple cauliflower in the Kidderminster carpet, ‘and what’s that to me, Sir?’\n\n‘I--I--have no doubt, Mrs. Raddle,’ said Bob Sawyer, blinking this last question, ‘that before the middle of next week we shall be able to set ourselves quite square, and go on, on a better system, afterwards.’\n\nThis was all Mrs. Raddle wanted. She had bustled up to the apartment of the unlucky Bob Sawyer, so bent upon going into a passion, that, in all probability, payment would have rather disappointed her than otherwise. She was in excellent order for a little relaxation of the kind, having just exchanged a few introductory compliments with Mr. R. in the front kitchen.\n\n‘Do you suppose, Mr. Sawyer,’ said Mrs. Raddle, elevating her voice for the information of the neighbours--‘do you suppose that I’m a-going day after day to let a fellar occupy my lodgings as never thinks of paying his rent, nor even the very money laid out for the fresh butter and lump sugar that’s bought for his breakfast, and the very milk that’s took in, at the street door? Do you suppose a hard-working and industrious woman as has lived in this street for twenty year (ten year over the way, and nine year and three-quarters in this very house) has nothing else to do but to work herself to death after a parcel of lazy idle fellars, that are always smoking and drinking, and lounging, when they ought to be glad to turn their hands to anything that would help ‘em to pay their bills? Do you--’\n\n‘My good soul,’ interposed Mr. Benjamin Allen soothingly.\n\n‘Have the goodness to keep your observashuns to yourself, Sir, I beg,’ said Mrs. Raddle, suddenly arresting the rapid torrent of her speech, and addressing the third party with impressive slowness and solemnity. ‘I am not aweer, Sir, that you have any right to address your conversation to me. I don’t think I let these apartments to you, Sir.’\n\n‘No, you certainly did not,’ said Mr. Benjamin Allen.\n\n‘Very good, Sir,’ responded Mrs. Raddle, with lofty politeness. ‘Then p’raps, Sir, you’ll confine yourself to breaking the arms and legs of the poor people in the hospitals, and keep yourself to yourself, Sir, or there may be some persons here as will make you, Sir.’\n\n‘But you are such an unreasonable woman,’ remonstrated Mr. Benjamin Allen.\n\n‘I beg your parding, young man,’ said Mrs. Raddle, in a cold perspiration of anger. ‘But will you have the goodness just to call me that again, sir?’\n\n‘I didn’t make use of the word in any invidious sense, ma’am,’ replied Mr. Benjamin Allen, growing somewhat uneasy on his own account.\n\n‘I beg your parding, young man,’ demanded Mrs. Raddle, in a louder and more imperative tone. ‘But who do you call a woman? Did you make that remark to me, sir?’\n\n‘Why, bless my heart!’ said Mr. Benjamin Allen.\n\n‘Did you apply that name to me, I ask of you, sir?’ interrupted Mrs. Raddle, with intense fierceness, throwing the door wide open.\n\n‘Why, of course I did,’ replied Mr. Benjamin Allen.\n\n‘Yes, of course you did,’ said Mrs. Raddle, backing gradually to the door, and raising her voice to its loudest pitch, for the special behoof of Mr. Raddle in the kitchen. ‘Yes, of course you did! And everybody knows that they may safely insult me in my own ‘ouse while my husband sits sleeping downstairs, and taking no more notice than if I was a dog in the streets. He ought to be ashamed of himself (here Mrs. Raddle sobbed) to allow his wife to be treated in this way by a parcel of young cutters and carvers of live people’s bodies, that disgraces the lodgings (another sob), and leaving her exposed to all manner of abuse; a base, faint-hearted, timorous wretch, that’s afraid to come upstairs, and face the ruffinly creatures--that’s afraid--that’s afraid to come!’ Mrs. Raddle paused to listen whether the repetition of the taunt had roused her better half; and finding that it had not been successful, proceeded to descend the stairs with sobs innumerable; when there came a loud double knock at the street door; whereupon she burst into an hysterical fit of weeping, accompanied with dismal moans, which was prolonged until the knock had been repeated six times, when, in an uncontrollable burst of mental agony, she threw down all the umbrellas, and disappeared into the back parlour, closing the door after her with an awful crash.\n\n‘Does Mr. Sawyer live here?’ said Mr. Pickwick, when the door was opened.\n\n‘Yes,’ said the girl, ‘first floor. It’s the door straight afore you, when you gets to the top of the stairs.’ Having given this instruction, the handmaid, who had been brought up among the aboriginal inhabitants of Southwark, disappeared, with the candle in her hand, down the kitchen stairs, perfectly satisfied that she had done everything that could possibly be required of her under the circumstances.\n\nMr. Snodgrass, who entered last, secured the street door, after several ineffectual efforts, by putting up the chain; and the friends stumbled upstairs, where they were received by Mr. Bob Sawyer, who had been afraid to go down, lest he should be waylaid by Mrs. Raddle.\n\n‘How are you?’ said the discomfited student. ‘Glad to see you--take care of the glasses.’ This caution was addressed to Mr. Pickwick, who had put his hat in the tray.\n\n‘Dear me,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘I beg your pardon.’\n\n‘Don’t mention it, don’t mention it,’ said Bob Sawyer. ‘I’m rather confined for room here, but you must put up with all that, when you come to see a young bachelor. Walk in. You’ve seen this gentleman before, I think?’ Mr. Pickwick shook hands with Mr. Benjamin Allen, and his friends followed his example. They had scarcely taken their seats when there was another double knock.\n\n‘I hope that’s Jack Hopkins!’ said Mr. Bob Sawyer. ‘Hush. Yes, it is. Come up, Jack; come up.’\n\nA heavy footstep was heard upon the stairs, and Jack Hopkins presented himself. He wore a black velvet waistcoat, with thunder-and-lightning buttons; and a blue striped shirt, with a white false collar.\n\n‘You’re late, Jack?’ said Mr. Benjamin Allen.\n\n‘Been detained at Bartholomew’s,’ replied Hopkins.\n\n‘Anything new?’\n\n‘No, nothing particular. Rather a good accident brought into the casualty ward.’\n\n‘What was that, sir?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Only a man fallen out of a four pair of stairs’ window; but it’s a very fair case indeed.’\n\n‘Do you mean that the patient is in a fair way to recover?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘No,’ replied Mr. Hopkins carelessly. ‘No, I should rather say he wouldn’t. There must be a splendid operation, though, to-morrow-- magnificent sight if Slasher does it.’\n\n‘You consider Mr. Slasher a good operator?’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Best alive,’ replied Hopkins. ‘Took a boy’s leg out of the socket last week--boy ate five apples and a gingerbread cake--exactly two minutes after it was all over, boy said he wouldn’t lie there to be made game of, and he’d tell his mother if they didn’t begin.’\n\n‘Dear me!’ said Mr. Pickwick, astonished.\n\n‘Pooh! That’s nothing, that ain’t,’ said Jack Hopkins. ‘Is it, Bob?’\n\n‘Nothing at all,’ replied Mr. Bob Sawyer.\n\n‘By the bye, Bob,’ said Hopkins, with a scarcely perceptible glance at Mr. Pickwick’s attentive face, ‘we had a curious accident last night. A child was brought in, who had swallowed a necklace.’\n\n‘Swallowed what, Sir?’ interrupted Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘A necklace,’ replied Jack Hopkins. ‘Not all at once, you know, that would be too much--you couldn’t swallow that, if the child did--eh, Mr. Pickwick? ha, ha!’ Mr. Hopkins appeared highly gratified with his own pleasantry, and continued--‘No, the way was this. Child’s parents were poor people who lived in a court. Child’s eldest sister bought a necklace--common necklace, made of large black wooden beads. Child being fond of toys, cribbed the necklace, hid it, played with it, cut the string, and swallowed a bead. Child thought it capital fun, went back next day, and swallowed another bead.’\n\n‘Bless my heart,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘what a dreadful thing! I beg your pardon, Sir. Go on.’\n\n‘Next day, child swallowed two beads; the day after that, he treated himself to three, and so on, till in a week’s time he had got through the necklace--five-and-twenty beads in all. The sister, who was an industrious girl, and seldom treated herself to a bit of finery, cried her eyes out, at the loss of the necklace; looked high and low for it; but, I needn’t say, didn’t find it. A few days afterwards, the family were at dinner--baked shoulder of mutton, and potatoes under it--the child, who wasn’t hungry, was playing about the room, when suddenly there was heard a devil of a noise, like a small hailstorm. “Don’t do that, my boy,” said the father. “I ain’t a-doin’ nothing,” said the child. “Well, don’t do it again,” said the father. There was a short silence, and then the noise began again, worse than ever. “If you don’t mind what I say, my boy,” said the father, “you’ll find yourself in bed, in something less than a pig’s whisper.” He gave the child a shake to make him obedient, and such a rattling ensued as nobody ever heard before. “Why, damme, it’s in the child!” said the father, “he’s got the croup in the wrong place!” “No, I haven’t, father,” said the child, beginning to cry, “it’s the necklace; I swallowed it, father.”--The father caught the child up, and ran with him to the hospital; the beads in the boy’s stomach rattling all the way with the jolting; and the people looking up in the air, and down in the cellars, to see where the unusual sound came from. He’s in the hospital now,’ said Jack Hopkins, ‘and he makes such a devil of a noise when he walks about, that they’re obliged to muffle him in a watchman’s coat, for fear he should wake the patients.’\n\n‘That’s the most extraordinary case I ever heard of,’ said Mr. Pickwick, with an emphatic blow on the table.\n\n‘Oh, that’s nothing,’ said Jack Hopkins. ‘Is it, Bob?’\n\n‘Certainly not,’ replied Bob Sawyer.\n\n‘Very singular things occur in our profession, I can assure you, Sir,’ said Hopkins.\n\n‘So I should be disposed to imagine,’ replied Mr. Pickwick.\n\nAnother knock at the door announced a large-headed young man in a black wig, who brought with him a scorbutic youth in a long stock. The next comer was a gentleman in a shirt emblazoned with pink anchors, who was closely followed by a pale youth with a plated watchguard. The arrival of a prim personage in clean linen and cloth boots rendered the party complete. The little table with the green baize cover was wheeled out; the first instalment of punch was brought in, in a white jug; and the succeeding three hours were devoted to Vingt-et-un at sixpence a dozen, which was only once interrupted by a slight dispute between the scorbutic youth and the gentleman with the pink anchors; in the course of which, the scorbutic youth intimated a burning desire to pull the nose of the gentleman with the emblems of hope; in reply to which, that individual expressed his decided unwillingness to accept of any ‘sauce’ on gratuitous terms, either from the irascible young gentleman with the scorbutic countenance, or any other person who was ornamented with a head.\n\nWhen the last ‘natural’ had been declared, and the profit and loss account of fish and sixpences adjusted, to the satisfaction of all parties, Mr. Bob Sawyer rang for supper, and the visitors squeezed themselves into corners while it was getting ready.\n\nIt was not so easily got ready as some people may imagine. First of all, it was necessary to awaken the girl, who had fallen asleep with her face on the kitchen table; this took a little time, and, even when she did answer the bell, another quarter of an hour was consumed in fruitless endeavours to impart to her a faint and distant glimmering of reason. The man to whom the order for the oysters had been sent, had not been told to open them; it is a very difficult thing to open an oyster with a limp knife and a two-pronged fork; and very little was done in this way. Very little of the beef was done either; and the ham (which was also from the German-sausage shop round the corner) was in a similar predicament. However, there was plenty of porter in a tin can; and the cheese went a great way, for it was very strong. So upon the whole, perhaps, the supper was quite as good as such matters usually are.\n\nAfter supper, another jug of punch was put upon the table, together with a paper of cigars, and a couple of bottles of spirits. Then there was an awful pause; and this awful pause was occasioned by a very common occurrence in this sort of place, but a very embarrassing one notwithstanding.\n\nThe fact is, the girl was washing the glasses. The establishment boasted four: we do not record the circumstance as at all derogatory to Mrs. Raddle, for there never was a lodging-house yet, that was not short of glasses. The landlady’s glasses were little, thin, blown-glass tumblers, and those which had been borrowed from the public-house were great, dropsical, bloated articles, each supported on a huge gouty leg. This would have been in itself sufficient to have possessed the company with the real state of affairs; but the young woman of all work had prevented the possibility of any misconception arising in the mind of any gentleman upon the subject, by forcibly dragging every man’s glass away, long before he had finished his beer, and audibly stating, despite the winks and interruptions of Mr. Bob Sawyer, that it was to be conveyed downstairs, and washed forthwith.\n\nIt is a very ill wind that blows nobody any good. The prim man in the cloth boots, who had been unsuccessfully attempting to make a joke during the whole time the round game lasted, saw his opportunity, and availed himself of it. The instant the glasses disappeared, he commenced a long story about a great public character, whose name he had forgotten, making a particularly happy reply to another eminent and illustrious individual whom he had never been able to identify. He enlarged at some length and with great minuteness upon divers collateral circumstances, distantly connected with the anecdote in hand, but for the life of him he couldn’t recollect at that precise moment what the anecdote was, although he had been in the habit of telling the story with great applause for the last ten years.\n\n‘Dear me,’ said the prim man in the cloth boots, ‘it is a very extraordinary circumstance.’\n\n‘I am sorry you have forgotten it,’ said Mr. Bob Sawyer, glancing eagerly at the door, as he thought he heard the noise of glasses jingling; ‘very sorry.’\n\n‘So am I,’ responded the prim man, ‘because I know it would have afforded so much amusement. Never mind; I dare say I shall manage to recollect it, in the course of half an hour or so.’\n\nThe prim man arrived at this point just as the glasses came back, when Mr. Bob Sawyer, who had been absorbed in attention during the whole time, said he should very much like to hear the end of it, for, so far as it went, it was, without exception, the very best story he had ever heard.\n\nThe sight of the tumblers restored Bob Sawyer to a degree of equanimity which he had not possessed since his interview with his landlady. His face brightened up, and he began to feel quite convivial.\n\n‘Now, Betsy,’ said Mr. Bob Sawyer, with great suavity, and dispersing, at the same time, the tumultuous little mob of glasses the girl had collected in the centre of the table--‘now, Betsy, the warm water; be brisk, there’s a good girl.’\n\n‘You can’t have no warm water,’ replied Betsy.\n\n‘No warm water!’ exclaimed Mr. Bob Sawyer.\n\n‘No,’ said the girl, with a shake of the head which expressed a more decided negative than the most copious language could have conveyed. ‘Missis Raddle said you warn’t to have none.’\n\nThe surprise depicted on the countenances of his guests imparted new courage to the host.\n\n‘Bring up the warm water instantly--instantly!’ said Mr. Bob Sawyer, with desperate sternness.\n\n‘No. I can’t,’ replied the girl; ‘Missis Raddle raked out the kitchen fire afore she went to bed, and locked up the kittle.’\n\n‘Oh, never mind; never mind. Pray don’t disturb yourself about such a trifle,’ said Mr. Pickwick, observing the conflict of Bob Sawyer’s passions, as depicted in his countenance, ‘cold water will do very well.’\n\n‘Oh, admirably,’ said Mr. Benjamin Allen.\n\n‘My landlady is subject to some slight attacks of mental derangement,’ remarked Bob Sawyer, with a ghastly smile; ‘I fear I must give her warning.’\n\n‘No, don’t,’ said Ben Allen.\n\n‘I fear I must,’ said Bob, with heroic firmness. ‘I’ll pay her what I owe her, and give her warning to-morrow morning.’ Poor fellow! how devoutly he wished he could!\n\nMr. Bob Sawyer’s heart-sickening attempts to rally under this last blow, communicated a dispiriting influence to the company, the greater part of whom, with the view of raising their spirits, attached themselves with extra cordiality to the cold brandy-and-water, the first perceptible effects of which were displayed in a renewal of hostilities between the scorbutic youth and the gentleman in the shirt. The belligerents vented their feelings of mutual contempt, for some time, in a variety of frownings and snortings, until at last the scorbutic youth felt it necessary to come to a more explicit understanding on the matter; when the following clear understanding took place.\n\n‘Sawyer,’ said the scorbutic youth, in a loud voice.\n\n‘Well, Noddy,’ replied Mr. Bob Sawyer.\n\n‘I should be very sorry, Sawyer,’ said Mr. Noddy, ‘to create any unpleasantness at any friend’s table, and much less at yours, Sawyer-- very; but I must take this opportunity of informing Mr. Gunter that he is no gentleman.’\n\n‘And I should be very sorry, Sawyer, to create any disturbance in the street in which you reside,’ said Mr. Gunter, ‘but I’m afraid I shall be under the necessity of alarming the neighbours by throwing the person who has just spoken, out o’ window.’\n\n‘What do you mean by that, sir?’ inquired Mr. Noddy.\n\n‘What I say, Sir,’ replied Mr. Gunter.\n\n‘I should like to see you do it, Sir,’ said Mr. Noddy.\n\n‘You shall feel me do it in half a minute, Sir,’ replied Mr. Gunter.\n\n‘I request that you’ll favour me with your card, Sir,’ said Mr. Noddy.\n\n‘I’ll do nothing of the kind, Sir,’ replied Mr. Gunter.\n\n‘Why not, Sir?’ inquired Mr. Noddy.\n\n‘Because you’ll stick it up over your chimney-piece, and delude your visitors into the false belief that a gentleman has been to see you, Sir,’ replied Mr. Gunter.\n\n‘Sir, a friend of mine shall wait on you in the morning,’ said Mr. Noddy.\n\n‘Sir, I’m very much obliged to you for the caution, and I’ll leave particular directions with the servant to lock up the spoons,’ replied Mr. Gunter.\n\nAt this point the remainder of the guests interposed, and remonstrated with both parties on the impropriety of their conduct; on which Mr. Noddy begged to state that his father was quite as respectable as Mr. Gunter’s father; to which Mr. Gunter replied that his father was to the full as respectable as Mr. Noddy’s father, and that his father’s son was as good a man as Mr. Noddy, any day in the week. As this announcement seemed the prelude to a recommencement of the dispute, there was another interference on the part of the company; and a vast quantity of talking and clamouring ensued, in the course of which Mr. Noddy gradually allowed his feelings to overpower him, and professed that he had ever entertained a devoted personal attachment towards Mr. Gunter. To this Mr. Gunter replied that, upon the whole, he rather preferred Mr. Noddy to his own brother; on hearing which admission, Mr. Noddy magnanimously rose from his seat, and proffered his hand to Mr. Gunter. Mr. Gunter grasped it with affecting fervour; and everybody said that the whole dispute had been conducted in a manner which was highly honourable to both parties concerned.\n\n‘Now,’ said Jack Hopkins, ‘just to set us going again, Bob, I don’t mind singing a song.’ And Hopkins, incited thereto by tumultuous applause, plunged himself at once into ‘The King, God bless him,’ which he sang as loud as he could, to a novel air, compounded of the ‘Bay of Biscay,’ and ‘A Frog he would.’ The chorus was the essence of the song; and, as each gentleman sang it to the tune he knew best, the effect was very striking indeed.\n\nIt was at the end of the chorus to the first verse, that Mr. Pickwick held up his hand in a listening attitude, and said, as soon as silence was restored--\n\n‘Hush! I beg your pardon. I thought I heard somebody calling from upstairs.’\n\nA profound silence immediately ensued; and Mr. Bob Sawyer was observed to turn pale.\n\n‘I think I hear it now,’ said Mr. Pickwick. ‘Have the goodness to open the door.’\n\nThe door was no sooner opened than all doubt on the subject was removed.\n\n‘Mr. Sawyer! Mr. Sawyer!’ screamed a voice from the two-pair landing.\n\n‘It’s my landlady,’ said Bob Sawyer, looking round him with great dismay. ‘Yes, Mrs. Raddle.’\n\n‘What do you mean by this, Mr. Sawyer?’ replied the voice, with great shrillness and rapidity of utterance. ‘Ain’t it enough to be swindled out of one’s rent, and money lent out of pocket besides, and abused and insulted by your friends that dares to call themselves men, without having the house turned out of the window, and noise enough made to bring the fire-engines here, at two o’clock in the morning?--Turn them wretches away.’\n\n‘You ought to be ashamed of yourselves,’ said the voice of Mr. Raddle, which appeared to proceed from beneath some distant bed-clothes.\n\n‘Ashamed of themselves!’ said Mrs. Raddle. ‘Why don’t you go down and knock ‘em every one downstairs? You would if you was a man.’\n\nI should if I was a dozen men, my dear,’ replied Mr. Raddle pacifically, ‘but they have the advantage of me in numbers, my dear.’\n\n‘Ugh, you coward!’ replied Mrs. Raddle, with supreme contempt. ‘Do you mean to turn them wretches out, or not, Mr. Sawyer?’\n\n‘They’re going, Mrs. Raddle, they’re going,’ said the miserable Bob. ‘I am afraid you’d better go,’ said Mr. Bob Sawyer to his friends. ‘I thought you were making too much noise.’\n\n‘It’s a very unfortunate thing,’ said the prim man. ‘Just as we were getting so comfortable too!’ The prim man was just beginning to have a dawning recollection of the story he had forgotten.\n\n‘It’s hardly to be borne,’ said the prim man, looking round. ‘Hardly to be borne, is it?’\n\n‘Not to be endured,’ replied Jack Hopkins; ‘let’s have the other verse, Bob. Come, here goes!’\n\n‘No, no, Jack, don’t,’ interposed Bob Sawyer; ‘it’s a capital song, but I am afraid we had better not have the other verse. They are very violent people, the people of the house.’\n\n‘Shall I step upstairs, and pitch into the landlord?’ inquired Hopkins, ‘or keep on ringing the bell, or go and groan on the staircase? You may command me, Bob.’\n\n‘I am very much indebted to you for your friendship and good-nature, Hopkins,’ said the wretched Mr. Bob Sawyer, ‘but I think the best plan to avoid any further dispute is for us to break up at once.’\n\n‘Now, Mr. Sawyer,’ screamed the shrill voice of Mrs. Raddle, ‘are them brutes going?’\n\n‘They’re only looking for their hats, Mrs. Raddle,’ said Bob; ‘they are going directly.’\n\n‘Going!’ said Mrs. Raddle, thrusting her nightcap over the banisters just as Mr. Pickwick, followed by Mr. Tupman, emerged from the sitting- room. ‘Going! what did they ever come for?’\n\n‘My dear ma’am,’ remonstrated Mr. Pickwick, looking up.\n\n‘Get along with you, old wretch!’ replied Mrs. Raddle, hastily withdrawing the nightcap. ‘Old enough to be his grandfather, you willin! You’re worse than any of ‘em.’\n\nMr. Pickwick found it in vain to protest his innocence, so hurried downstairs into the street, whither he was closely followed by Mr. Tupman, Mr. Winkle, and Mr. Snodgrass. Mr. Ben Allen, who was dismally depressed with spirits and agitation, accompanied them as far as London Bridge, and in the course of the walk confided to Mr. Winkle, as an especially eligible person to intrust the secret to, that he was resolved to cut the throat of any gentleman, except Mr. Bob Sawyer, who should aspire to the affections of his sister Arabella. Having expressed his determination to perform this painful duty of a brother with proper firmness, he burst into tears, knocked his hat over his eyes, and, making the best of his way back, knocked double knocks at the door of the Borough Market office, and took short naps on the steps alternately, until daybreak, under the firm impression that he lived there, and had forgotten the key.\n\nThe visitors having all departed, in compliance with the rather pressing request of Mrs. Raddle, the luckless Mr. Bob Sawyer was left alone, to meditate on the probable events of to-morrow, and the pleasures of the evening.\n\nCHAPTER XXXIII. MR. WELLER THE ELDER DELIVERS SOME CRITICAL SENTIMENTS RESPECTING LITERARY COMPOSITION; AND, ASSISTED BY HIS SON SAMUEL, PAYS A SMALL INSTALMENT OF RETALIATION TO THE ACCOUNT OF THE REVEREND GENTLEMAN WITH THE RED NOSE\nThe morning of the thirteenth of February, which the readers of this authentic narrative know, as well as we do, to have been the day immediately preceding that which was appointed for the trial of Mrs. Bardell’s action, was a busy time for Mr. Samuel Weller, who was perpetually engaged in travelling from the George and Vulture to Mr. Perker’s chambers and back again, from and between the hours of nine o’clock in the morning and two in the afternoon, both inclusive. Not that there was anything whatever to be done, for the consultation had taken place, and the course of proceeding to be adopted, had been finally determined on; but Mr. Pickwick being in a most extreme state of excitement, persevered in constantly sending small notes to his attorney, merely containing the inquiry, ‘Dear Perker. Is all going on well?’ to which Mr. Perker invariably forwarded the reply, ‘Dear Pickwick. As well as possible’; the fact being, as we have already hinted, that there was nothing whatever to go on, either well or ill, until the sitting of the court on the following morning.\n\nBut people who go voluntarily to law, or are taken forcibly there, for the first time, may be allowed to labour under some temporary irritation and anxiety; and Sam, with a due allowance for the frailties of human nature, obeyed all his master’s behests with that imperturbable good- humour and unruffable composure which formed one of his most striking and amiable characteristics.\n\nSam had solaced himself with a most agreeable little dinner, and was waiting at the bar for the glass of warm mixture in which Mr. Pickwick had requested him to drown the fatigues of his morning’s walks, when a young boy of about three feet high, or thereabouts, in a hairy cap and fustian overalls, whose garb bespoke a laudable ambition to attain in time the elevation of an hostler, entered the passage of the George and Vulture, and looked first up the stairs, and then along the passage, and then into the bar, as if in search of somebody to whom he bore a commission; whereupon the barmaid, conceiving it not improbable that the said commission might be directed to the tea or table spoons of the establishment, accosted the boy with--\n\n‘Now, young man, what do you want?’\n\n‘Is there anybody here, named Sam?’ inquired the youth, in a loud voice of treble quality.\n\n‘What’s the t’other name?’ said Sam Weller, looking round.\n\n‘How should I know?’ briskly replied the young gentleman below the hairy cap.\n\n‘You’re a sharp boy, you are,’ said Mr. Weller; ‘only I wouldn’t show that wery fine edge too much, if I was you, in case anybody took it off. What do you mean by comin’ to a hot-el, and asking arter Sam, vith as much politeness as a vild Indian?’\n\n‘’Cos an old gen’l’m’n told me to,’ replied the boy.\n\n‘What old gen’l’m’n?’ inquired Sam, with deep disdain.\n\n‘Him as drives a Ipswich coach, and uses our parlour,’ rejoined the boy. ‘He told me yesterday mornin’ to come to the George and Wultur this arternoon, and ask for Sam.’\n\n‘It’s my father, my dear,’ said Mr. Weller, turning with an explanatory air to the young lady in the bar; ‘blessed if I think he hardly knows wot my other name is. Well, young brockiley sprout, wot then?’\n\n‘Why then,’ said the boy, ‘you was to come to him at six o’clock to our ‘ouse, ‘cos he wants to see you--Blue Boar, Leaden’all Markit. Shall I say you’re comin’?’\n\n‘You may wenture on that ‘ere statement, Sir,’ replied Sam. And thus empowered, the young gentleman walked away, awakening all the echoes in George Yard as he did so, with several chaste and extremely correct imitations of a drover’s whistle, delivered in a tone of peculiar richness and volume.\n\nMr. Weller having obtained leave of absence from Mr. Pickwick, who, in his then state of excitement and worry, was by no means displeased at being left alone, set forth, long before the appointed hour, and having plenty of time at his disposal, sauntered down as far as the Mansion House, where he paused and contemplated, with a face of great calmness and philosophy, the numerous cads and drivers of short stages who assemble near that famous place of resort, to the great terror and confusion of the old-lady population of these realms. Having loitered here, for half an hour or so, Mr. Weller turned, and began wending his way towards Leadenhall Market, through a variety of by-streets and courts. As he was sauntering away his spare time, and stopped to look at almost every object that met his gaze, it is by no means surprising that Mr. Weller should have paused before a small stationer’s and print- seller’s window; but without further explanation it does appear surprising that his eyes should have no sooner rested on certain pictures which were exposed for sale therein, than he gave a sudden start, smote his right leg with great vehemence, and exclaimed, with energy, ‘if it hadn’t been for this, I should ha’ forgot all about it, till it was too late!’\n\nThe particular picture on which Sam Weller’s eyes were fixed, as he said this, was a highly-coloured representation of a couple of human hearts skewered together with an arrow, cooking before a cheerful fire, while a male and female cannibal in modern attire, the gentleman being clad in a blue coat and white trousers, and the lady in a deep red pelisse with a parasol of the same, were approaching the meal with hungry eyes, up a serpentine gravel path leading thereunto. A decidedly indelicate young gentleman, in a pair of wings and nothing else, was depicted as superintending the cooking; a representation of the spire of the church in Langham Place, London, appeared in the distance; and the whole formed a ‘valentine,’ of which, as a written inscription in the window testified, there was a large assortment within, which the shopkeeper pledged himself to dispose of, to his countrymen generally, at the reduced rate of one-and-sixpence each.\n\n‘I should ha’ forgot it; I should certainly ha’ forgot it!’ said Sam; so saying, he at once stepped into the stationer’s shop, and requested to be served with a sheet of the best gilt-edged letter-paper, and a hard- nibbed pen which could be warranted not to splutter. These articles having been promptly supplied, he walked on direct towards Leadenhall Market at a good round pace, very different from his recent lingering one. Looking round him, he there beheld a signboard on which the painter’s art had delineated something remotely resembling a cerulean elephant with an aquiline nose in lieu of trunk. Rightly conjecturing that this was the Blue Boar himself, he stepped into the house, and inquired concerning his parent.\n\n‘He won’t be here this three-quarters of an hour or more,’ said the young lady who superintended the domestic arrangements of the Blue Boar.\n\n‘Wery good, my dear,’ replied Sam. ‘Let me have nine-penn’oth o’ brandy- and-water luke, and the inkstand, will you, miss?’\n\nThe brandy-and-water luke, and the inkstand, having been carried into the little parlour, and the young lady having carefully flattened down the coals to prevent their blazing, and carried away the poker to preclude the possibility of the fire being stirred, without the full privity and concurrence of the Blue Boar being first had and obtained, Sam Weller sat himself down in a box near the stove, and pulled out the sheet of gilt-edged letter-paper, and the hard-nibbed pen. Then looking carefully at the pen to see that there were no hairs in it, and dusting down the table, so that there might be no crumbs of bread under the paper, Sam tucked up the cuffs of his coat, squared his elbows, and composed himself to write.\n\nTo ladies and gentlemen who are not in the habit of devoting themselves practically to the science of penmanship, writing a letter is no very easy task; it being always considered necessary in such cases for the writer to recline his head on his left arm, so as to place his eyes as nearly as possible on a level with the paper, and, while glancing sideways at the letters he is constructing, to form with his tongue imaginary characters to correspond. These motions, although unquestionably of the greatest assistance to original composition, retard in some degree the progress of the writer; and Sam had unconsciously been a full hour and a half writing words in small text, smearing out wrong letters with his little finger, and putting in new ones which required going over very often to render them visible through the old blots, when he was roused by the opening of the door and the entrance of his parent.\n\n‘Vell, Sammy,’ said the father.\n\n‘Vell, my Prooshan Blue,’ responded the son, laying down his pen. ‘What’s the last bulletin about mother-in-law?’\n\n‘Mrs. Veller passed a very good night, but is uncommon perwerse, and unpleasant this mornin’. Signed upon oath, Tony Veller, Esquire. That’s the last vun as was issued, Sammy,’ replied Mr. Weller, untying his shawl.\n\n‘No better yet?’ inquired Sam.\n\n‘All the symptoms aggerawated,’ replied Mr. Weller, shaking his head. ‘But wot’s that, you’re a-doin’ of? Pursuit of knowledge under difficulties, Sammy?’\n\n‘I’ve done now,’ said Sam, with slight embarrassment; ‘I’ve been a- writin’.’\n\n‘So I see,’ replied Mr. Weller. ‘Not to any young ‘ooman, I hope, Sammy?’\n\n‘Why, it’s no use a-sayin’ it ain’t,’ replied Sam; ‘it’s a walentine.’\n\n‘A what!’ exclaimed Mr. Weller, apparently horror-stricken by the word.\n\n‘A walentine,’ replied Sam.\n\n‘Samivel, Samivel,’ said Mr. Weller, in reproachful accents, ‘I didn’t think you’d ha’ done it. Arter the warnin’ you’ve had o’ your father’s wicious propensities; arter all I’ve said to you upon this here wery subject; arter actiwally seein’ and bein’ in the company o’ your own mother-in-law, vich I should ha’ thought wos a moral lesson as no man could never ha’ forgotten to his dyin’ day! I didn’t think you’d ha’ done it, Sammy, I didn’t think you’d ha’ done it!’ These reflections were too much for the good old man. He raised Sam’s tumbler to his lips and drank off its contents.\n\n‘Wot’s the matter now?’ said Sam.\n\n‘Nev’r mind, Sammy,’ replied Mr. Weller, ‘it’ll be a wery agonisin’ trial to me at my time of life, but I’m pretty tough, that’s vun consolation, as the wery old turkey remarked wen the farmer said he wos afeerd he should be obliged to kill him for the London market.’\n\n‘Wot’ll be a trial?’ inquired Sam.\n\n‘To see you married, Sammy--to see you a dilluded wictim, and thinkin’ in your innocence that it’s all wery capital,’ replied Mr. Weller. ‘It’s a dreadful trial to a father’s feelin’s, that ‘ere, Sammy--’\n\n‘Nonsense,’ said Sam. ‘I ain’t a-goin’ to get married, don’t you fret yourself about that; I know you’re a judge of these things. Order in your pipe and I’ll read you the letter. There!’\n\nWe cannot distinctly say whether it was the prospect of the pipe, or the consolatory reflection that a fatal disposition to get married ran in the family, and couldn’t be helped, which calmed Mr. Weller’s feelings, and caused his grief to subside. We should be rather disposed to say that the result was attained by combining the two sources of consolation, for he repeated the second in a low tone, very frequently; ringing the bell meanwhile, to order in the first. He then divested himself of his upper coat; and lighting the pipe and placing himself in front of the fire with his back towards it, so that he could feel its full heat, and recline against the mantel-piece at the same time, turned towards Sam, and, with a countenance greatly mollified by the softening influence of tobacco, requested him to ‘fire away.’\n\nSam dipped his pen into the ink to be ready for any corrections, and began with a very theatrical air--\n\n‘“Lovely--“’\n\n‘Stop,’ said Mr. Weller, ringing the bell. ‘A double glass o’ the inwariable, my dear.’\n\n‘Very well, Sir,’ replied the girl; who with great quickness appeared, vanished, returned, and disappeared.\n\n‘They seem to know your ways here,’ observed Sam.\n\n‘Yes,’ replied his father, ‘I’ve been here before, in my time. Go on, Sammy.’\n\n‘“Lovely creetur,”’ repeated Sam.\n\n‘’Tain’t in poetry, is it?’ interposed his father.\n\n‘No, no,’ replied Sam.\n\n‘Wery glad to hear it,’ said Mr. Weller. ‘Poetry’s unnat’ral; no man ever talked poetry ‘cept a beadle on boxin’-day, or Warren’s blackin’, or Rowland’s oil, or some of them low fellows; never you let yourself down to talk poetry, my boy. Begin agin, Sammy.’\n\nMr. Weller resumed his pipe with critical solemnity, and Sam once more commenced, and read as follows:\n\n‘“Lovely creetur I feel myself a damned--“’\n\nThat ain’t proper,’ said Mr. Weller, taking his pipe from his mouth.\n\n‘No; it ain’t “damned,”’ observed Sam, holding the letter up to the light, ‘it’s “shamed,” there’s a blot there--“I feel myself ashamed.”’\n\n‘Wery good,’ said Mr. Weller. ‘Go on.’\n\n‘Feel myself ashamed, and completely cir--’ I forget what this here word is,’ said Sam, scratching his head with the pen, in vain attempts to remember.\n\n‘Why don’t you look at it, then?’ inquired Mr. Weller.\n\n‘So I am a-lookin’ at it,’ replied Sam, ‘but there’s another blot. Here’s a “c,” and a “i,” and a “d.”’\n\n‘Circumwented, p’raps,’ suggested Mr. Weller.\n\n‘No, it ain’t that,’ said Sam, ‘“circumscribed”; that’s it.’\n\n‘That ain’t as good a word as “circumwented,” Sammy,’ said Mr. Weller gravely.\n\n‘Think not?’ said Sam.\n\n‘Nothin’ like it,’ replied his father.\n\n‘But don’t you think it means more?’ inquired Sam.\n\n‘Vell p’raps it’s a more tenderer word,’ said Mr. Weller, after a few moments’ reflection. ‘Go on, Sammy.’\n\n‘“Feel myself ashamed and completely circumscribed in a-dressin’ of you, for you are a nice gal and nothin’ but it.”’\n\n‘That’s a wery pretty sentiment,’ said the elder Mr. Weller, removing his pipe to make way for the remark.\n\n‘Yes, I think it is rayther good,’ observed Sam, highly flattered.\n\n‘Wot I like in that ‘ere style of writin’,’ said the elder Mr. Weller, ‘is, that there ain’t no callin’ names in it--no Wenuses, nor nothin’ o’ that kind. Wot’s the good o’ callin’ a young ‘ooman a Wenus or a angel, Sammy?’\n\n‘Ah! what, indeed?’ replied Sam.\n\n‘You might jist as well call her a griffin, or a unicorn, or a king’s arms at once, which is wery well known to be a collection o’ fabulous animals,’ added Mr. Weller.\n\n‘Just as well,’ replied Sam.\n\n‘Drive on, Sammy,’ said Mr. Weller.\n\nSam complied with the request, and proceeded as follows; his father continuing to smoke, with a mixed expression of wisdom and complacency, which was particularly edifying.\n\n‘“Afore I see you, I thought all women was alike.”’\n\n‘So they are,’ observed the elder Mr. Weller parenthetically.\n\n‘“But now,”’ continued Sam, ‘“now I find what a reg’lar soft-headed, inkred’lous turnip I must ha’ been; for there ain’t nobody like you, though I like you better than nothin’ at all.” I thought it best to make that rayther strong,’ said Sam, looking up.\n\nMr. Weller nodded approvingly, and Sam resumed.\n\n‘“So I take the privilidge of the day, Mary, my dear--as the gen’l’m’n in difficulties did, ven he valked out of a Sunday--to tell you that the first and only time I see you, your likeness was took on my hart in much quicker time and brighter colours than ever a likeness was took by the profeel macheen (wich p’raps you may have heerd on Mary my dear) altho it does finish a portrait and put the frame and glass on complete, with a hook at the end to hang it up by, and all in two minutes and a quarter.”’\n\n‘I am afeerd that werges on the poetical, Sammy,’ said Mr. Weller dubiously.\n\n‘No, it don’t,’ replied Sam, reading on very quickly, to avoid contesting the point--\n\n‘“Except of me Mary my dear as your walentine and think over what I’ve said.--My dear Mary I will now conclude.” That’s all,’ said Sam.\n\n‘That’s rather a Sudden pull-up, ain’t it, Sammy?’ inquired Mr. Weller.\n\n‘Not a bit on it,’ said Sam; ‘she’ll vish there wos more, and that’s the great art o’ letter-writin’.’\n\n‘Well,’ said Mr. Weller, ‘there’s somethin’ in that; and I wish your mother-in-law ‘ud only conduct her conwersation on the same gen-teel principle. Ain’t you a-goin’ to sign it?’\n\n‘That’s the difficulty,’ said Sam; ‘I don’t know what to sign it.’\n\n‘Sign it--“Veller”,’ said the oldest surviving proprietor of that name.\n\n‘Won’t do,’ said Sam. ‘Never sign a walentine with your own name.’\n\n‘Sign it “Pickwick,” then,’ said Mr. Weller; ‘it’s a wery good name, and a easy one to spell.’\n\nThe wery thing,’ said Sam. ‘I could end with a werse; what do you think?’\n\n‘I don’t like it, Sam,’ rejoined Mr. Weller. ‘I never know’d a respectable coachman as wrote poetry, ‘cept one, as made an affectin’ copy o’ werses the night afore he was hung for a highway robbery; and he wos only a Cambervell man, so even that’s no rule.’\n\nBut Sam was not to be dissuaded from the poetical idea that had occurred to him, so he signed the letter--\n\n‘Your love-sick Pickwick.’\n\nAnd having folded it, in a very intricate manner, squeezed a downhill direction in one corner: ‘To Mary, Housemaid, at Mr. Nupkins’s, Mayor’s, Ipswich, Suffolk’; and put it into his pocket, wafered, and ready for the general post. This important business having been transacted, Mr. Weller the elder proceeded to open that, on which he had summoned his son.\n\n‘The first matter relates to your governor, Sammy,’ said Mr. Weller. ‘He’s a-goin’ to be tried to-morrow, ain’t he?’\n\n‘The trial’s a-comin’ on,’ replied Sam.\n\n‘Vell,’ said Mr. Weller, ‘Now I s’pose he’ll want to call some witnesses to speak to his character, or p’rhaps to prove a alleybi. I’ve been a- turnin’ the bis’ness over in my mind, and he may make his-self easy, Sammy. I’ve got some friends as’ll do either for him, but my adwice ‘ud be this here--never mind the character, and stick to the alleybi. Nothing like a alleybi, Sammy, nothing.’ Mr. Weller looked very profound as he delivered this legal opinion; and burying his nose in his tumbler, winked over the top thereof, at his astonished son.\n\n‘Why, what do you mean?’ said Sam; ‘you don’t think he’s a-goin’ to be tried at the Old Bailey, do you?’\n\n‘That ain’t no part of the present consideration, Sammy,’ replied Mr. Weller. ‘Verever he’s a-goin’ to be tried, my boy, a alleybi’s the thing to get him off. Ve got Tom Vildspark off that ‘ere manslaughter, with a alleybi, ven all the big vigs to a man said as nothing couldn’t save him. And my ‘pinion is, Sammy, that if your governor don’t prove a alleybi, he’ll be what the Italians call reg’larly flummoxed, and that’s all about it.’\n\nAs the elder Mr. Weller entertained a firm and unalterable conviction that the Old Bailey was the supreme court of judicature in this country, and that its rules and forms of proceeding regulated and controlled the practice of all other courts of justice whatsoever, he totally disregarded the assurances and arguments of his son, tending to show that the alibi was inadmissible; and vehemently protested that Mr. Pickwick was being ‘wictimised.’ Finding that it was of no use to discuss the matter further, Sam changed the subject, and inquired what the second topic was, on which his revered parent wished to consult him.\n\n‘That’s a pint o’ domestic policy, Sammy,’ said Mr. Weller. ‘This here Stiggins--’\n\n‘Red-nosed man?’ inquired Sam.\n\n‘The wery same,’ replied Mr. Weller. ‘This here red-nosed man, Sammy, wisits your mother-in-law vith a kindness and constancy I never see equalled. He’s sitch a friend o’ the family, Sammy, that wen he’s avay from us, he can’t be comfortable unless he has somethin’ to remember us by.’\n\n‘And I’d give him somethin’ as ‘ud turpentine and beeswax his memory for the next ten years or so, if I wos you,’ interposed Sam.\n\n‘Stop a minute,’ said Mr. Weller; ‘I wos a-going to say, he always brings now, a flat bottle as holds about a pint and a half, and fills it vith the pine-apple rum afore he goes avay.’\n\n‘And empties it afore he comes back, I s’pose?’ said Sam.\n\n‘Clean!’ replied Mr. Weller; ‘never leaves nothin’ in it but the cork and the smell; trust him for that, Sammy. Now, these here fellows, my boy, are a-goin’ to-night to get up the monthly meetin’ o’ the Brick Lane Branch o’ the United Grand Junction Ebenezer Temperance Association. Your mother-in-law wos a-goin’, Sammy, but she’s got the rheumatics, and can’t; and I, Sammy--I’ve got the two tickets as wos sent her.’ Mr. Weller communicated this secret with great glee, and winked so indefatigably after doing so, that Sam began to think he must have got the Tic Doloureux in his right eyelid.\n\n‘Well?’ said that young gentleman.\n\n‘Well,’ continued his progenitor, looking round him very cautiously, ‘you and I’ll go, punctiwal to the time. The deputy-shepherd won’t, Sammy; the deputy-shepherd won’t.’ Here Mr. Weller was seized with a paroxysm of chuckles, which gradually terminated in as near an approach to a choke as an elderly gentleman can, with safety, sustain.\n\n‘Well, I never see sitch an old ghost in all my born days,’ exclaimed Sam, rubbing the old gentleman’s back, hard enough to set him on fire with the friction. ‘What are you a-laughin’ at, corpilence?’\n\n‘Hush! Sammy,’ said Mr. Weller, looking round him with increased caution, and speaking in a whisper. ‘Two friends o’ mine, as works the Oxford Road, and is up to all kinds o’ games, has got the deputy- shepherd safe in tow, Sammy; and ven he does come to the Ebenezer Junction (vich he’s sure to do: for they’ll see him to the door, and shove him in, if necessary), he’ll be as far gone in rum-and-water, as ever he wos at the Markis o’ Granby, Dorkin’, and that’s not sayin’ a little neither.’ And with this, Mr. Weller once more laughed immoderately, and once more relapsed into a state of partial suffocation, in consequence.\n\nNothing could have been more in accordance with Sam Weller’s feelings than the projected exposure of the real propensities and qualities of the red-nosed man; and it being very near the appointed hour of meeting, the father and son took their way at once to Brick Lane, Sam not forgetting to drop his letter into a general post-office as they walked along.\n\nThe monthly meetings of the Brick Lane Branch of the United Grand Junction Ebenezer Temperance Association were held in a large room, pleasantly and airily situated at the top of a safe and commodious ladder. The president was the straight-walking Mr. Anthony Humm, a converted fireman, now a schoolmaster, and occasionally an itinerant preacher; and the secretary was Mr. Jonas Mudge, chandler’s shopkeeper, an enthusiastic and disinterested vessel, who sold tea to the members. Previous to the commencement of business, the ladies sat upon forms, and drank tea, till such time as they considered it expedient to leave off; and a large wooden money-box was conspicuously placed upon the green baize cloth of the business-table, behind which the secretary stood, and acknowledged, with a gracious smile, every addition to the rich vein of copper which lay concealed within.\n\nOn this particular occasion the women drank tea to a most alarming extent; greatly to the horror of Mr. Weller, senior, who, utterly regardless of all Sam’s admonitory nudgings, stared about him in every direction with the most undisguised astonishment.\n\n‘Sammy,’ whispered Mr. Weller, ‘if some o’ these here people don’t want tappin’ to-morrow mornin’, I ain’t your father, and that’s wot it is. Why, this here old lady next me is a-drowndin’ herself in tea.’\n\nBe quiet, can’t you?’ murmured Sam.\n\n‘Sam,’ whispered Mr. Weller, a moment afterwards, in a tone of deep agitation, ‘mark my vords, my boy. If that ‘ere secretary fellow keeps on for only five minutes more, he’ll blow hisself up with toast and water.’\n\n‘Well, let him, if he likes,’ replied Sam; ‘it ain’t no bis’ness o’ yourn.’\n\n‘If this here lasts much longer, Sammy,’ said Mr. Weller, in the same low voice, ‘I shall feel it my duty, as a human bein’, to rise and address the cheer. There’s a young ‘ooman on the next form but two, as has drunk nine breakfast cups and a half; and she’s a-swellin’ wisibly before my wery eyes.’\n\nThere is little doubt that Mr. Weller would have carried his benevolent intention into immediate execution, if a great noise, occasioned by putting up the cups and saucers, had not very fortunately announced that the tea-drinking was over. The crockery having been removed, the table with the green baize cover was carried out into the centre of the room, and the business of the evening was commenced by a little emphatic man, with a bald head and drab shorts, who suddenly rushed up the ladder, at the imminent peril of snapping the two little legs incased in the drab shorts, and said--\n\n‘Ladies and gentlemen, I move our excellent brother, Mr. Anthony Humm, into the chair.’\n\nThe ladies waved a choice selection of pocket-handkerchiefs at this proposition; and the impetuous little man literally moved Mr. Humm into the chair, by taking him by the shoulders and thrusting him into a mahogany-frame which had once represented that article of furniture. The waving of handkerchiefs was renewed; and Mr. Humm, who was a sleek, white-faced man, in a perpetual perspiration, bowed meekly, to the great admiration of the females, and formally took his seat. Silence was then proclaimed by the little man in the drab shorts, and Mr. Humm rose and said--That, with the permission of his Brick Lane Branch brothers and sisters, then and there present, the secretary would read the report of the Brick Lane Branch committee; a proposition which was again received with a demonstration of pocket-handkerchiefs.\n\nThe secretary having sneezed in a very impressive manner, and the cough which always seizes an assembly, when anything particular is going to be done, having been duly performed, the following document was read:\n\n‘REPORT OF THE COMMITTEE OF THE BRICK LANE BRANCH OF THE UNITED GRAND JUNCTION EBENEZER TEMPERANCE ASSOCIATION\n\n‘Your committee have pursued their grateful labours during the past month, and have the unspeakable pleasure of reporting the following additional cases of converts to Temperance.\n\n‘H. Walker, tailor, wife, and two children. When in better circumstances, owns to having been in the constant habit of drinking ale and beer; says he is not certain whether he did not twice a week, for twenty years, taste “dog’s nose,” which your committee find upon inquiry, to be compounded of warm porter, moist sugar, gin, and nutmeg (a groan, and ‘So it is!’ from an elderly female). Is now out of work and penniless; thinks it must be the porter (cheers) or the loss of the use of his right hand; is not certain which, but thinks it very likely that, if he had drunk nothing but water all his life, his fellow-workman would never have stuck a rusty needle in him, and thereby occasioned his accident (tremendous cheering). Has nothing but cold water to drink, and never feels thirsty (great applause).\n\n‘Betsy Martin, widow, one child, and one eye. Goes out charing and washing, by the day; never had more than one eye, but knows her mother drank bottled stout, and shouldn’t wonder if that caused it (immense cheering). Thinks it not impossible that if she had always abstained from spirits she might have had two eyes by this time (tremendous applause). Used, at every place she went to, to have eighteen-pence a day, a pint of porter, and a glass of spirits; but since she became a member of the Brick Lane Branch, has always demanded three-and-sixpence (the announcement of this most interesting fact was received with deafening enthusiasm).\n\n‘Henry Beller was for many years toast-master at various corporation dinners, during which time he drank a great deal of foreign wine; may sometimes have carried a bottle or two home with him; is not quite certain of that, but is sure if he did, that he drank the contents. Feels very low and melancholy, is very feverish, and has a constant thirst upon him; thinks it must be the wine he used to drink (cheers). Is out of employ now; and never touches a drop of foreign wine by any chance (tremendous plaudits).\n\n‘Thomas Burton is purveyor of cat’s meat to the Lord Mayor and Sheriffs, and several members of the Common Council (the announcement of this gentleman’s name was received with breathless interest). Has a wooden leg; finds a wooden leg expensive, going over the stones; used to wear second-hand wooden legs, and drink a glass of hot gin-and-water regularly every night--sometimes two (deep sighs). Found the second-hand wooden legs split and rot very quickly; is firmly persuaded that their constitution was undermined by the gin-and-water (prolonged cheering). Buys new wooden legs now, and drinks nothing but water and weak tea. The new legs last twice as long as the others used to do, and he attributes this solely to his temperate habits (triumphant cheers).’\n\nAnthony Humm now moved that the assembly do regale itself with a song. With a view to their rational and moral enjoyment, Brother Mordlin had adapted the beautiful words of ‘Who hasn’t heard of a Jolly Young Waterman?’ to the tune of the Old Hundredth, which he would request them to join him in singing (great applause). He might take that opportunity of expressing his firm persuasion that the late Mr. Dibdin, seeing the errors of his former life, had written that song to show the advantages of abstinence. It was a temperance song (whirlwinds of cheers). The neatness of the young man’s attire, the dexterity of his feathering, the enviable state of mind which enabled him in the beautiful words of the poet, to\n\n‘Row along, thinking of nothing at all,’\n\nall combined to prove that he must have been a water-drinker (cheers). Oh, what a state of virtuous jollity! (rapturous cheering). And what was the young man’s reward? Let all young men present mark this:\n\n‘The maidens all flocked to his boat so readily.’\n\n(Loud cheers, in which the ladies joined.) What a bright example! The sisterhood, the maidens, flocking round the young waterman, and urging him along the stream of duty and of temperance. But, was it the maidens of humble life only, who soothed, consoled, and supported him? No!\n\n‘He was always first oars with the fine city ladies.’\n\n(Immense cheering.) The soft sex to a man--he begged pardon, to a female--rallied round the young waterman, and turned with disgust from the drinker of spirits (cheers). The Brick Lane Branch brothers were watermen (cheers and laughter). That room was their boat; that audience were the maidens; and he (Mr. Anthony Humm), however unworthily, was ‘first oars’ (unbounded applause).\n\n‘Wot does he mean by the soft sex, Sammy?’ inquired Mr. Weller, in a whisper.\n\n‘The womin,’ said Sam, in the same tone.\n\n‘He ain’t far out there, Sammy,’ replied Mr. Weller; ‘they must be a soft sex--a wery soft sex, indeed--if they let themselves be gammoned by such fellers as him.’\n\nAny further observations from the indignant old gentleman were cut short by the announcement of the song, which Mr. Anthony Humm gave out two lines at a time, for the information of such of his hearers as were unacquainted with the legend. While it was being sung, the little man with the drab shorts disappeared; he returned immediately on its conclusion, and whispered Mr. Anthony Humm, with a face of the deepest importance.\n\n‘My friends,’ said Mr. Humm, holding up his hand in a deprecatory manner, to bespeak the silence of such of the stout old ladies as were yet a line or two behind; ‘my friends, a delegate from the Dorking Branch of our society, Brother Stiggins, attends below.’\n\nOut came the pocket-handkerchiefs again, in greater force than ever; for Mr. Stiggins was excessively popular among the female constituency of Brick Lane.\n\n‘He may approach, I think,’ said Mr. Humm, looking round him, with a fat smile. ‘Brother Tadger, let him come forth and greet us.’\n\nThe little man in the drab shorts who answered to the name of Brother Tadger, bustled down the ladder with great speed, and was immediately afterwards heard tumbling up with the Reverend Mr. Stiggins.\n\n‘He’s a-comin’, Sammy,’ whispered Mr. Weller, purple in the countenance with suppressed laughter.\n\n‘Don’t say nothin’ to me,’ replied Sam, ‘for I can’t bear it. He’s close to the door. I hear him a-knockin’ his head again the lath and plaster now.’\n\nAs Sam Weller spoke, the little door flew open, and Brother Tadger appeared, closely followed by the Reverend Mr. Stiggins, who no sooner entered, than there was a great clapping of hands, and stamping of feet, and flourishing of handkerchiefs; to all of which manifestations of delight, Brother Stiggins returned no other acknowledgment than staring with a wild eye, and a fixed smile, at the extreme top of the wick of the candle on the table, swaying his body to and fro, meanwhile, in a very unsteady and uncertain manner.\n\n‘Are you unwell, Brother Stiggins?’ whispered Mr. Anthony Humm.\n\n‘I am all right, Sir,’ replied Mr. Stiggins, in a tone in which ferocity was blended with an extreme thickness of utterance; ‘I am all right, Sir.’\n\n‘Oh, very well,’ rejoined Mr. Anthony Humm, retreating a few paces.\n\n‘I believe no man here has ventured to say that I am not all right, Sir?’ said Mr. Stiggins.\n\n‘Oh, certainly not,’ said Mr. Humm.\n\n‘I should advise him not to, Sir; I should advise him not,’ said Mr. Stiggins.\n\nBy this time the audience were perfectly silent, and waited with some anxiety for the resumption of business.\n\n‘Will you address the meeting, brother?’ said Mr. Humm, with a smile of invitation.\n\n‘No, sir,’ rejoined Mr. Stiggins; ‘No, sir. I will not, sir.’\n\nThe meeting looked at each other with raised eyelids; and a murmur of astonishment ran through the room.\n\n‘It’s my opinion, sir,’ said Mr. Stiggins, unbuttoning his coat, and speaking very loudly--‘it’s my opinion, sir, that this meeting is drunk, sir. Brother Tadger, sir!’ said Mr. Stiggins, suddenly increasing in ferocity, and turning sharp round on the little man in the drab shorts, ‘you are drunk, sir!’ With this, Mr. Stiggins, entertaining a praiseworthy desire to promote the sobriety of the meeting, and to exclude therefrom all improper characters, hit Brother Tadger on the summit of the nose with such unerring aim, that the drab shorts disappeared like a flash of lightning. Brother Tadger had been knocked, head first, down the ladder.\n\nUpon this, the women set up a loud and dismal screaming; and rushing in small parties before their favourite brothers, flung their arms around them to preserve them from danger. An instance of affection, which had nearly proved fatal to Humm, who, being extremely popular, was all but suffocated, by the crowd of female devotees that hung about his neck, and heaped caresses upon him. The greater part of the lights were quickly put out, and nothing but noise and confusion resounded on all sides.\n\n‘Now, Sammy,’ said Mr. Weller, taking off his greatcoat with much deliberation, ‘just you step out, and fetch in a watchman.’\n\n‘And wot are you a-goin’ to do, the while?’ inquired Sam.\n\n‘Never you mind me, Sammy,’ replied the old gentleman; ‘I shall ockipy myself in havin’ a small settlement with that ‘ere Stiggins.’ Before Sam could interfere to prevent it, his heroic parent had penetrated into a remote corner of the room, and attacked the Reverend Mr. Stiggins with manual dexterity.\n\n‘Come off!’ said Sam.\n\n‘Come on!’ cried Mr. Weller; and without further invitation he gave the Reverend Mr. Stiggins a preliminary tap on the head, and began dancing round him in a buoyant and cork-like manner, which in a gentleman at his time of life was a perfect marvel to behold.\n\nFinding all remonstrances unavailing, Sam pulled his hat firmly on, threw his father’s coat over his arm, and taking the old man round the waist, forcibly dragged him down the ladder, and into the street; never releasing his hold, or permitting him to stop, until they reached the corner. As they gained it, they could hear the shouts of the populace, who were witnessing the removal of the Reverend Mr. Stiggins to strong lodgings for the night, and could hear the noise occasioned by the dispersion in various directions of the members of the Brick Lane Branch of the United Grand Junction Ebenezer Temperance Association.\n\nCHAPTER XXXIV. IS WHOLLY DEVOTED TO A FULL AND FAITHFUL REPORT OF THE MEMORABLE TRIAL OF BARDELL AGAINST PICKWICK\nI wonder what the foreman of the jury, whoever he’ll be, has got for breakfast,’ said Mr. Snodgrass, by way of keeping up a conversation on the eventful morning of the fourteenth of February.\n\n‘Ah!’ said Perker, ‘I hope he’s got a good one.’\n\nWhy so?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Highly important--very important, my dear Sir,’ replied Perker. ‘A good, contented, well-breakfasted juryman is a capital thing to get hold of. Discontented or hungry jurymen, my dear sir, always find for the plaintiff.’\n\n‘Bless my heart,’ said Mr. Pickwick, looking very blank, ‘what do they do that for?’\n\n‘Why, I don’t know,’ replied the little man coolly; ‘saves time, I suppose. If it’s near dinner-time, the foreman takes out his watch when the jury has retired, and says, “Dear me, gentlemen, ten minutes to five, I declare! I dine at five, gentlemen.” “So do I,” says everybody else, except two men who ought to have dined at three and seem more than half disposed to stand out in consequence. The foreman smiles, and puts up his watch:--“Well, gentlemen, what do we say, plaintiff or defendant, gentlemen? I rather think, so far as I am concerned, gentlemen,--I say, I rather think--but don’t let that influence you--I rather think the plaintiff’s the man.” Upon this, two or three other men are sure to say that they think so too--as of course they do; and then they get on very unanimously and comfortably. Ten minutes past nine!’ said the little man, looking at his watch. ‘Time we were off, my dear sir; breach of promise trial-court is generally full in such cases. You had better ring for a coach, my dear sir, or we shall be rather late.’\n\nMr. Pickwick immediately rang the bell, and a coach having been procured, the four Pickwickians and Mr. Perker ensconced themselves therein, and drove to Guildhall; Sam Weller, Mr. Lowten, and the blue bag, following in a cab.\n\n‘Lowten,’ said Perker, when they reached the outer hall of the court, ‘put Mr. Pickwick’s friends in the students’ box; Mr. Pickwick himself had better sit by me. This way, my dear sir, this way.’ Taking Mr. Pickwick by the coat sleeve, the little man led him to the low seat just beneath the desks of the King’s Counsel, which is constructed for the convenience of attorneys, who from that spot can whisper into the ear of the leading counsel in the case, any instructions that may be necessary during the progress of the trial. The occupants of this seat are invisible to the great body of spectators, inasmuch as they sit on a much lower level than either the barristers or the audience, whose seats are raised above the floor. Of course they have their backs to both, and their faces towards the judge.\n\n‘That’s the witness-box, I suppose?’ said Mr. Pickwick, pointing to a kind of pulpit, with a brass rail, on his left hand.\n\n‘That’s the witness-box, my dear sir,’ replied Perker, disinterring a quantity of papers from the blue bag, which Lowten had just deposited at his feet.\n\n‘And that,’ said Mr. Pickwick, pointing to a couple of enclosed seats on his right, ‘that’s where the jurymen sit, is it not?’\n\n‘The identical place, my dear Sir,’ replied Perker, tapping the lid of his snuff-box.\n\nMr. Pickwick stood up in a state of great agitation, and took a glance at the court. There were already a pretty large sprinkling of spectators in the gallery, and a numerous muster of gentlemen in wigs, in the barristers’ seats, who presented, as a body, all that pleasing and extensive variety of nose and whisker for which the Bar of England is so justly celebrated. Such of the gentlemen as had a brief to carry, carried it in as conspicuous a manner as possible, and occasionally scratched their noses therewith, to impress the fact more strongly on the observation of the spectators. Other gentlemen, who had no briefs to show, carried under their arms goodly octavos, with a red label behind, and that under-done-pie-crust-coloured cover, which is technically known as ‘law calf.’ Others, who had neither briefs nor books, thrust their hands into their pockets, and looked as wise as they conveniently could; others, again, moved here and there with great restlessness and earnestness of manner, content to awaken thereby the admiration and astonishment of the uninitiated strangers. The whole, to the great wonderment of Mr. Pickwick, were divided into little groups, who were chatting and discussing the news of the day in the most unfeeling manner possible--just as if no trial at all were coming on.\n\nA bow from Mr. Phunky, as he entered, and took his seat behind the row appropriated to the King’s Counsel, attracted Mr. Pickwick’s attention; and he had scarcely returned it, when Mr. Serjeant Snubbin appeared, followed by Mr. Mallard, who half hid the Serjeant behind a large crimson bag, which he placed on his table, and, after shaking hands with Perker, withdrew. Then there entered two or three more Serjeants; and among them, one with a fat body and a red face, who nodded in a friendly manner to Mr. Serjeant Snubbin, and said it was a fine morning.\n\n‘Who’s that red-faced man, who said it was a fine morning, and nodded to our counsel?’ whispered Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Mr. Serjeant Buzfuz,’ replied Perker. ‘He’s opposed to us; he leads on the other side. That gentleman behind him is Mr. Skimpin, his junior.’\n\nMr. Pickwick was on the point of inquiring, with great abhorrence of the man’s cold-blooded villainy, how Mr. Serjeant Buzfuz, who was counsel for the opposite party, dared to presume to tell Mr. Serjeant Snubbin, who was counsel for him, that it was a fine morning, when he was interrupted by a general rising of the barristers, and a loud cry of ‘Silence!’ from the officers of the court. Looking round, he found that this was caused by the entrance of the judge.\n\nMr. Justice Stareleigh (who sat in the absence of the Chief Justice, occasioned by indisposition) was a most particularly short man, and so fat, that he seemed all face and waistcoat. He rolled in, upon two little turned legs, and having bobbed gravely to the Bar, who bobbed gravely to him, put his little legs underneath his table, and his little three-cornered hat upon it; and when Mr. Justice Stareleigh had done this, all you could see of him was two queer little eyes, one broad pink face, and somewhere about half of a big and very comical-looking wig.\n\nThe judge had no sooner taken his seat, than the officer on the floor of the court called out ‘Silence!’ in a commanding tone, upon which another officer in the gallery cried ‘Silence!’ in an angry manner, whereupon three or four more ushers shouted ‘Silence!’ in a voice of indignant remonstrance. This being done, a gentleman in black, who sat below the judge, proceeded to call over the names of the jury; and after a great deal of bawling, it was discovered that only ten special jurymen were present. Upon this, Mr. Serjeant Buzfuz prayed a tales; the gentleman in black then proceeded to press into the special jury, two of the common jurymen; and a greengrocer and a chemist were caught directly.\n\n‘Answer to your names, gentlemen, that you may be sworn,’ said the gentleman in black. ‘Richard Upwitch.’\n\n‘Here,’ said the greengrocer.\n\n‘Thomas Groffin.’\n\n‘Here,’ said the chemist.\n\n‘Take the book, gentlemen. You shall well and truly try--’\n\n‘I beg this court’s pardon,’ said the chemist, who was a tall, thin, yellow-visaged man, ‘but I hope this court will excuse my attendance.’\n\n‘On what grounds, Sir?’ said Mr. Justice Stareleigh.\n\n‘I have no assistant, my Lord,’ said the chemist.\n\n‘I can’t help that, Sir,’ replied Mr. Justice Stareleigh. ‘You should hire one.’\n\n‘I can’t afford it, my Lord,’ rejoined the chemist.\n\n‘Then you ought to be able to afford it, Sir,’ said the judge, reddening; for Mr. Justice Stareleigh’s temper bordered on the irritable, and brooked not contradiction.\n\n‘I know I ought to do, if I got on as well as I deserved; but I don’t, my Lord,’ answered the chemist.\n\n‘Swear the gentleman,’ said the judge peremptorily.\n\nThe officer had got no further than the ‘You shall well and truly try,’ when he was again interrupted by the chemist.\n\n‘I am to be sworn, my Lord, am I?’ said the chemist.\n\n‘Certainly, sir,’ replied the testy little judge.\n\n‘Very well, my Lord,’ replied the chemist, in a resigned manner. ‘Then there’ll be murder before this trial’s over; that’s all. Swear me, if you please, Sir;’ and sworn the chemist was, before the judge could find words to utter.\n\n‘I merely wanted to observe, my Lord,’ said the chemist, taking his seat with great deliberation, ‘that I’ve left nobody but an errand-boy in my shop. He is a very nice boy, my Lord, but he is not acquainted with drugs; and I know that the prevailing impression on his mind is, that Epsom salts means oxalic acid; and syrup of senna, laudanum. That’s all, my Lord.’ With this, the tall chemist composed himself into a comfortable attitude, and, assuming a pleasant expression of countenance, appeared to have prepared himself for the worst.\n\nMr. Pickwick was regarding the chemist with feelings of the deepest horror, when a slight sensation was perceptible in the body of the court; and immediately afterwards Mrs. Bardell, supported by Mrs. Cluppins, was led in, and placed, in a drooping state, at the other end of the seat on which Mr. Pickwick sat. An extra-sized umbrella was then handed in by Mr. Dodson, and a pair of pattens by Mr. Fogg, each of whom had prepared a most sympathising and melancholy face for the occasion. Mrs. Sanders then appeared, leading in Master Bardell. At sight of her child, Mrs. Bardell started; suddenly recollecting herself, she kissed him in a frantic manner; then relapsing into a state of hysterical imbecility, the good lady requested to be informed where she was. In reply to this, Mrs. Cluppins and Mrs. Sanders turned their heads away and wept, while Messrs. Dodson and Fogg entreated the plaintiff to compose herself. Serjeant Buzfuz rubbed his eyes very hard with a large white handkerchief, and gave an appealing look towards the jury, while the judge was visibly affected, and several of the beholders tried to cough down their emotion.\n\n‘Very good notion that indeed,’ whispered Perker to Mr. Pickwick. ‘Capital fellows those Dodson and Fogg; excellent ideas of effect, my dear Sir, excellent.’\n\nAs Perker spoke, Mrs. Bardell began to recover by slow degrees, while Mrs. Cluppins, after a careful survey of Master Bardell’s buttons and the button-holes to which they severally belonged, placed him on the floor of the court in front of his mother--a commanding position in which he could not fail to awaken the full commiseration and sympathy of both judge and jury. This was not done without considerable opposition, and many tears, on the part of the young gentleman himself, who had certain inward misgivings that the placing him within the full glare of the judge’s eye was only a formal prelude to his being immediately ordered away for instant execution, or for transportation beyond the seas, during the whole term of his natural life, at the very least.\n\n‘Bardell and Pickwick,’ cried the gentleman in black, calling on the case, which stood first on the list.\n\n‘I am for the plaintiff, my Lord,’ said Mr. Serjeant Buzfuz.\n\n‘Who is with you, Brother Buzfuz?’ said the judge. Mr. Skimpin bowed, to intimate that he was.\n\n‘I appear for the defendant, my Lord,’ said Mr. Serjeant Snubbin.\n\n‘Anybody with you, Brother Snubbin?’ inquired the court.\n\n‘Mr. Phunky, my Lord,’ replied Serjeant Snubbin.\n\n‘Serjeant Buzfuz and Mr. Skimpin for the plaintiff,’ said the judge, writing down the names in his note-book, and reading as he wrote; ‘for the defendant, Serjeant Snubbin and Mr. Monkey.’\n\n‘Beg your Lordship’s pardon, Phunky.’\n\n‘Oh, very good,’ said the judge; ‘I never had the pleasure of hearing the gentleman’s name before.’ Here Mr. Phunky bowed and smiled, and the judge bowed and smiled too, and then Mr. Phunky, blushing into the very whites of his eyes, tried to look as if he didn’t know that everybody was gazing at him, a thing which no man ever succeeded in doing yet, or in all reasonable probability, ever will.\n\n‘Go on,’ said the judge.\n\nThe ushers again called silence, and Mr. Skimpin proceeded to ‘open the case’; and the case appeared to have very little inside it when he had opened it, for he kept such particulars as he knew, completely to himself, and sat down, after a lapse of three minutes, leaving the jury in precisely the same advanced stage of wisdom as they were in before.\nSerjeant Buzfuz then rose with all the majesty and dignity which the grave nature of the proceedings demanded, and having whispered to Dodson, and conferred briefly with Fogg, pulled his gown over his shoulders, settled his wig, and addressed the jury.\n\nSerjeant Buzfuz began by saying, that never, in the whole course of his professional experience--never, from the very first moment of his applying himself to the study and practice of the law--had he approached a case with feelings of such deep emotion, or with such a heavy sense of the responsibility imposed upon him--a responsibility, he would say, which he could never have supported, were he not buoyed up and sustained by a conviction so strong, that it amounted to positive certainty that the cause of truth and justice, or, in other words, the cause of his much-injured and most oppressed client, must prevail with the high- minded and intelligent dozen of men whom he now saw in that box before him.\n\nCounsel usually begin in this way, because it puts the jury on the very best terms with themselves, and makes them think what sharp fellows they must be. A visible effect was produced immediately, several jurymen beginning to take voluminous notes with the utmost eagerness.\n\n‘You have heard from my learned friend, gentlemen,’ continued Serjeant Buzfuz, well knowing that, from the learned friend alluded to, the gentlemen of the jury had heard just nothing at all--‘you have heard from my learned friend, gentlemen, that this is an action for a breach of promise of marriage, in which the damages are laid at £1,500. But you have not heard from my learned friend, inasmuch as it did not come within my learned friend’s province to tell you, what are the facts and circumstances of the case. Those facts and circumstances, gentlemen, you shall hear detailed by me, and proved by the unimpeachable female whom I will place in that box before you.’\n\nHere, Mr. Serjeant Buzfuz, with a tremendous emphasis on the word ‘box,’ smote his table with a mighty sound, and glanced at Dodson and Fogg, who nodded admiration of the Serjeant, and indignant defiance of the defendant.\n\n‘The plaintiff, gentlemen,’ continued Serjeant Buzfuz, in a soft and melancholy voice, ‘the plaintiff is a widow; yes, gentlemen, a widow. The late Mr. Bardell, after enjoying, for many years, the esteem and confidence of his sovereign, as one of the guardians of his royal revenues, glided almost imperceptibly from the world, to seek elsewhere for that repose and peace which a custom-house can never afford.’\n\nAt this pathetic description of the decease of Mr. Bardell, who had been knocked on the head with a quart-pot in a public-house cellar, the learned serjeant’s voice faltered, and he proceeded, with emotion--\n\n‘Some time before his death, he had stamped his likeness upon a little boy. With this little boy, the only pledge of her departed exciseman, Mrs. Bardell shrank from the world, and courted the retirement and tranquillity of Goswell Street; and here she placed in her front parlour window a written placard, bearing this inscription--“Apartments furnished for a single gentleman. Inquire within.”’ Here Serjeant Buzfuz paused, while several gentlemen of the jury took a note of the document.\n\n‘There is no date to that, is there?’ inquired a juror.\n\n‘There is no date, gentlemen,’ replied Serjeant Buzfuz; ‘but I am instructed to say that it was put in the plaintiff’s parlour window just this time three years. I entreat the attention of the jury to the wording of this document--“Apartments furnished for a single gentleman”! Mrs. Bardell’s opinions of the opposite sex, gentlemen, were derived from a long contemplation of the inestimable qualities of her lost husband. She had no fear, she had no distrust, she had no suspicion; all was confidence and reliance. “Mr. Bardell,” said the widow--“Mr. Bardell was a man of honour, Mr. Bardell was a man of his word, Mr. Bardell was no deceiver, Mr. Bardell was once a single gentleman himself; to single gentlemen I look for protection, for assistance, for comfort, and for consolation; in single gentlemen I shall perpetually see something to remind me of what Mr. Bardell was when he first won my young and untried affections; to a single gentleman, then, shall my lodgings be let.” Actuated by this beautiful and touching impulse (among the best impulses of our imperfect nature, gentlemen), the lonely and desolate widow dried her tears, furnished her first floor, caught her innocent boy to her maternal bosom, and put the bill up in her parlour window. Did it remain there long? No. The serpent was on the watch, the train was laid, the mine was preparing, the sapper and miner was at work. Before the bill had been in the parlour window three days--three days, gentlemen--a being, erect upon two legs, and bearing all the outward semblance of a man, and not of a monster, knocked at the door of Mrs. Bardell’s house. He inquired within--he took the lodgings; and on the very next day he entered into possession of them. This man was Pickwick--Pickwick, the defendant.’\n\nSerjeant Buzfuz, who had proceeded with such volubility that his face was perfectly crimson, here paused for breath. The silence awoke Mr. Justice Stareleigh, who immediately wrote down something with a pen without any ink in it, and looked unusually profound, to impress the jury with the belief that he always thought most deeply with his eyes shut. Serjeant Buzfuz proceeded--\n\n‘Of this man Pickwick I will say little; the subject presents but few attractions; and I, gentlemen, am not the man, nor are you, gentlemen, the men, to delight in the contemplation of revolting heartlessness, and of systematic villainy.’\n\nHere Mr. Pickwick, who had been writhing in silence for some time, gave a violent start, as if some vague idea of assaulting Serjeant Buzfuz, in the august presence of justice and law, suggested itself to his mind. An admonitory gesture from Perker restrained him, and he listened to the learned gentleman’s continuation with a look of indignation, which contrasted forcibly with the admiring faces of Mrs. Cluppins and Mrs. Sanders.\n\n‘I say systematic villainy, gentlemen,’ said Serjeant Buzfuz, looking through Mr. Pickwick, and talking at him; ‘and when I say systematic villainy, let me tell the defendant Pickwick, if he be in court, as I am informed he is, that it would have been more decent in him, more becoming, in better judgment, and in better taste, if he had stopped away. Let me tell him, gentlemen, that any gestures of dissent or disapprobation in which he may indulge in this court will not go down with you; that you will know how to value and how to appreciate them; and let me tell him further, as my Lord will tell you, gentlemen, that a counsel, in the discharge of his duty to his client, is neither to be intimidated nor bullied, nor put down; and that any attempt to do either the one or the other, or the first, or the last, will recoil on the head of the attempter, be he plaintiff or be he defendant, be his name Pickwick, or Noakes, or Stoakes, or Stiles, or Brown, or Thompson.’\n\nThis little divergence from the subject in hand, had, of course, the intended effect of turning all eyes to Mr. Pickwick. Serjeant Buzfuz, having partially recovered from the state of moral elevation into which he had lashed himself, resumed--\n\n‘I shall show you, gentlemen, that for two years, Pickwick continued to reside constantly, and without interruption or intermission, at Mrs. Bardell’s house. I shall show you that Mrs. Bardell, during the whole of that time, waited on him, attended to his comforts, cooked his meals, looked out his linen for the washerwoman when it went abroad, darned, aired, and prepared it for wear, when it came home, and, in short, enjoyed his fullest trust and confidence. I shall show you that, on many occasions, he gave halfpence, and on some occasions even sixpences, to her little boy; and I shall prove to you, by a witness whose testimony it will be impossible for my learned friend to weaken or controvert, that on one occasion he patted the boy on the head, and, after inquiring whether he had won any “alley tors” or “commoneys” lately (both of which I understand to be a particular species of marbles much prized by the youth of this town), made use of this remarkable expression, “How should you like to have another father?” I shall prove to you, gentlemen, that about a year ago, Pickwick suddenly began to absent himself from home, during long intervals, as if with the intention of gradually breaking off from my client; but I shall show you also, that his resolution was not at that time sufficiently strong, or that his better feelings conquered, if better feelings he has, or that the charms and accomplishments of my client prevailed against his unmanly intentions, by proving to you, that on one occasion, when he returned from the country, he distinctly and in terms, offered her marriage: previously, however, taking special care that there would be no witness to their solemn contract; and I am in a situation to prove to you, on the testimony of three of his own friends--most unwilling witnesses, gentlemen--most unwilling witnesses--that on that morning he was discovered by them holding the plaintiff in his arms, and soothing her agitation by his caresses and endearments.’\n\nA visible impression was produced upon the auditors by this part of the learned Serjeant’s address. Drawing forth two very small scraps of paper, he proceeded--\n\n‘And now, gentlemen, but one word more. Two letters have passed between these parties, letters which are admitted to be in the handwriting of the defendant, and which speak volumes, indeed. The letters, too, bespeak the character of the man. They are not open, fervent, eloquent epistles, breathing nothing but the language of affectionate attachment. They are covert, sly, underhanded communications, but, fortunately, far more conclusive than if couched in the most glowing language and the most poetic imagery--letters that must be viewed with a cautious and suspicious eye--letters that were evidently intended at the time, by Pickwick, to mislead and delude any third parties into whose hands they might fall. Let me read the first: “Garraways, twelve o’clock. Dear Mrs. B.--Chops and tomato sauce. Yours, Pickwick.” Gentlemen, what does this mean? Chops and tomato sauce. Yours, Pickwick! Chops! Gracious heavens! and tomato sauce! Gentlemen, is the happiness of a sensitive and confiding female to be trifled away, by such shallow artifices as these? The next has no date whatever, which is in itself suspicious. “Dear Mrs. B., I shall not be at home till to-morrow. Slow coach.” And then follows this very remarkable expression. “Don’t trouble yourself about the warming-pan.” The warming-pan! Why, gentlemen, who does trouble himself about a warming-pan? When was the peace of mind of man or woman broken or disturbed by a warming-pan, which is in itself a harmless, a useful, and I will add, gentlemen, a comforting article of domestic furniture? Why is Mrs. Bardell so earnestly entreated not to agitate herself about this warming-pan, unless (as is no doubt the case) it is a mere cover for hidden fire--a mere substitute for some endearing word or promise, agreeably to a preconcerted system of correspondence, artfully contrived by Pickwick with a view to his contemplated desertion, and which I am not in a condition to explain? And what does this allusion to the slow coach mean? For aught I know, it may be a reference to Pickwick himself, who has most unquestionably been a criminally slow coach during the whole of this transaction, but whose speed will now be very unexpectedly accelerated, and whose wheels, gentlemen, as he will find to his cost, will very soon be greased by you!’\n\nMr. Serjeant Buzfuz paused in this place, to see whether the jury smiled at his joke; but as nobody took it but the greengrocer, whose sensitiveness on the subject was very probably occasioned by his having subjected a chaise-cart to the process in question on that identical morning, the learned Serjeant considered it advisable to undergo a slight relapse into the dismals before he concluded.\n\n‘But enough of this, gentlemen,’ said Mr. Serjeant Buzfuz, ‘it is difficult to smile with an aching heart; it is ill jesting when our deepest sympathies are awakened. My client’s hopes and prospects are ruined, and it is no figure of speech to say that her occupation is gone indeed. The bill is down--but there is no tenant. Eligible single gentlemen pass and repass--but there is no invitation for to inquire within or without. All is gloom and silence in the house; even the voice of the child is hushed; his infant sports are disregarded when his mother weeps; his “alley tors” and his “commoneys” are alike neglected; he forgets the long familiar cry of “knuckle down,” and at tip-cheese, or odd and even, his hand is out. But Pickwick, gentlemen, Pickwick, the ruthless destroyer of this domestic oasis in the desert of Goswell Street--Pickwick who has choked up the well, and thrown ashes on the sward--Pickwick, who comes before you to-day with his heartless tomato sauce and warming-pans--Pickwick still rears his head with unblushing effrontery, and gazes without a sigh on the ruin he has made. Damages, gentlemen--heavy damages is the only punishment with which you can visit him; the only recompense you can award to my client. And for those damages she now appeals to an enlightened, a high-minded, a right- feeling, a conscientious, a dispassionate, a sympathising, a contemplative jury of her civilised countrymen.’ With this beautiful peroration, Mr. Serjeant Buzfuz sat down, and Mr. Justice Stareleigh woke up.\n\n‘Call Elizabeth Cluppins,’ said Serjeant Buzfuz, rising a minute afterwards, with renewed vigour.\n\nThe nearest usher called for Elizabeth Tuppins; another one, at a little distance off, demanded Elizabeth Jupkins; and a third rushed in a breathless state into King Street, and screamed for Elizabeth Muffins till he was hoarse.\n\nMeanwhile Mrs. Cluppins, with the combined assistance of Mrs. Bardell, Mrs. Sanders, Mr. Dodson, and Mr. Fogg, was hoisted into the witness- box; and when she was safely perched on the top step, Mrs. Bardell stood on the bottom one, with the pocket-handkerchief and pattens in one hand, and a glass bottle that might hold about a quarter of a pint of smelling-salts in the other, ready for any emergency. Mrs. Sanders, whose eyes were intently fixed on the judge’s face, planted herself close by, with the large umbrella, keeping her right thumb pressed on the spring with an earnest countenance, as if she were fully prepared to put it up at a moment’s notice.\n\n‘Mrs. Cluppins,’ said Serjeant Buzfuz, ‘pray compose yourself, ma’am.’ Of course, directly Mrs. Cluppins was desired to compose herself, she sobbed with increased vehemence, and gave divers alarming manifestations of an approaching fainting fit, or, as she afterwards said, of her feelings being too many for her.\n\n‘Do you recollect, Mrs. Cluppins,’ said Serjeant Buzfuz, after a few unimportant questions--‘do you recollect being in Mrs. Bardell’s back one pair of stairs, on one particular morning in July last, when she was dusting Pickwick’s apartment?’\n\n‘Yes, my Lord and jury, I do,’ replied Mrs. Cluppins.\n\n‘Mr. Pickwick’s sitting-room was the first-floor front, I believe?’\n\n‘Yes, it were, Sir,’ replied Mrs. Cluppins.\n\n‘What were you doing in the back room, ma’am?’ inquired the little judge.\n\n‘My Lord and jury,’ said Mrs. Cluppins, with interesting agitation, ‘I will not deceive you.’\n\n‘You had better not, ma’am,’ said the little judge.\n\n‘I was there,’ resumed Mrs. Cluppins, ‘unbeknown to Mrs. Bardell; I had been out with a little basket, gentlemen, to buy three pound of red kidney pertaties, which was three pound tuppence ha’penny, when I see Mrs. Bardell’s street door on the jar.’\n\n‘On the what?’ exclaimed the little judge.\n\n‘Partly open, my Lord,’ said Serjeant Snubbin.\n\n‘She said on the jar,’ said the little judge, with a cunning look.\n\n‘It’s all the same, my Lord,’ said Serjeant Snubbin. The little judge looked doubtful, and said he’d make a note of it. Mrs. Cluppins then resumed--\n\n‘I walked in, gentlemen, just to say good-mornin’, and went, in a permiscuous manner, upstairs, and into the back room. Gentlemen, there was the sound of voices in the front room, and--’\n\n‘And you listened, I believe, Mrs. Cluppins?’ said Serjeant Buzfuz.\n\n‘Beggin’ your pardon, Sir,’ replied Mrs. Cluppins, in a majestic manner, ‘I would scorn the haction. The voices was very loud, Sir, and forced themselves upon my ear.’\n\n‘Well, Mrs. Cluppins, you were not listening, but you heard the voices. Was one of those voices Pickwick’s?’\n\n‘Yes, it were, Sir.’ And Mrs. Cluppins, after distinctly stating that Mr. Pickwick addressed himself to Mrs. Bardell, repeated by slow degrees, and by dint of many questions, the conversation with which our readers are already acquainted.\n\nThe jury looked suspicious, and Mr. Serjeant Buzfuz smiled as he sat down. They looked positively awful when Serjeant Snubbin intimated that he should not cross-examine the witness, for Mr. Pickwick wished it to be distinctly stated that it was due to her to say, that her account was in substance correct.\n\nMrs. Cluppins having once broken the ice, thought it a favourable opportunity for entering into a short dissertation on her own domestic affairs; so she straightway proceeded to inform the court that she was the mother of eight children at that present speaking, and that she entertained confident expectations of presenting Mr. Cluppins with a ninth, somewhere about that day six months. At this interesting point, the little judge interposed most irascibly; and the effect of the interposition was, that both the worthy lady and Mrs. Sanders were politely taken out of court, under the escort of Mr. Jackson, without further parley.\n\n‘Nathaniel Winkle!’ said Mr. Skimpin.\n\n‘Here!’ replied a feeble voice. Mr. Winkle entered the witness-box, and having been duly sworn, bowed to the judge with considerable deference.\n\n‘Don’t look at me, Sir,’ said the judge sharply, in acknowledgment of the salute; ‘look at the jury.’\n\nMr. Winkle obeyed the mandate, and looked at the place where he thought it most probable the jury might be; for seeing anything in his then state of intellectual complication was wholly out of the question.\n\nMr. Winkle was then examined by Mr. Skimpin, who, being a promising young man of two or three-and-forty, was of course anxious to confuse a witness who was notoriously predisposed in favour of the other side, as much as he could.\n\n‘Now, Sir,’ said Mr. Skimpin, ‘have the goodness to let his Lordship know what your name is, will you?’ and Mr. Skimpin inclined his head on one side to listen with great sharpness to the answer, and glanced at the jury meanwhile, as if to imply that he rather expected Mr. Winkle’s natural taste for perjury would induce him to give some name which did not belong to him.\n\n‘Winkle,’ replied the witness.\n\n‘What’s your Christian name, Sir?’ angrily inquired the little judge.\n\n‘Nathaniel, Sir.’\n\n‘Daniel--any other name?’\n\n‘Nathaniel, sir--my Lord, I mean.’\n\n‘Nathaniel Daniel, or Daniel Nathaniel?’\n\n‘No, my Lord, only Nathaniel--not Daniel at all.’\n\n‘What did you tell me it was Daniel for, then, sir?’ inquired the judge.\n\n‘I didn’t, my Lord,’ replied Mr. Winkle.\n\n‘You did, Sir,’ replied the judge, with a severe frown. ‘How could I have got Daniel on my notes, unless you told me so, Sir?’\n\nThis argument was, of course, unanswerable.\n\n‘Mr. Winkle has rather a short memory, my Lord,’ interposed Mr. Skimpin, with another glance at the jury. ‘We shall find means to refresh it before we have quite done with him, I dare say.’\n\n‘You had better be careful, Sir,’ said the little judge, with a sinister look at the witness.\n\nPoor Mr. Winkle bowed, and endeavoured to feign an easiness of manner, which, in his then state of confusion, gave him rather the air of a disconcerted pickpocket.\n\n‘Now, Mr. Winkle,’ said Mr. Skimpin, ‘attend to me, if you please, Sir; and let me recommend you, for your own sake, to bear in mind his Lordship’s injunctions to be careful. I believe you are a particular friend of Mr. Pickwick, the defendant, are you not?’\n\n‘I have known Mr. Pickwick now, as well as I recollect at this moment, nearly--’\n\n‘Pray, Mr. Winkle, do not evade the question. Are you, or are you not, a particular friend of the defendant’s?’\n\n‘I was just about to say, that--’\n\n‘Will you, or will you not, answer my question, Sir?’\n\nIf you don’t answer the question, you’ll be committed, Sir,’ interposed the little judge, looking over his note-book.\n\n‘Come, Sir,’ said Mr. Skimpin, ‘yes or no, if you please.’\n\n‘Yes, I am,’ replied Mr. Winkle.\n\n‘Yes, you are. And why couldn’t you say that at once, Sir? Perhaps you know the plaintiff too? Eh, Mr. Winkle?’\n\n‘I don’t know her; I’ve seen her.’\n\n‘Oh, you don’t know her, but you’ve seen her? Now, have the goodness to tell the gentlemen of the jury what you mean by that, Mr. Winkle.’\n\n‘I mean that I am not intimate with her, but I have seen her when I went to call on Mr. Pickwick, in Goswell Street.’\n\n‘How often have you seen her, Sir?’\n\n‘How often?’\n\n‘Yes, Mr. Winkle, how often? I’ll repeat the question for you a dozen times, if you require it, Sir.’ And the learned gentleman, with a firm and steady frown, placed his hands on his hips, and smiled suspiciously to the jury.\n\nOn this question there arose the edifying brow-beating, customary on such points. First of all, Mr. Winkle said it was quite impossible for him to say how many times he had seen Mrs. Bardell. Then he was asked if he had seen her twenty times, to which he replied, ‘Certainly--more than that.’ Then he was asked whether he hadn’t seen her a hundred times-- whether he couldn’t swear that he had seen her more than fifty times-- whether he didn’t know that he had seen her at least seventy-five times, and so forth; the satisfactory conclusion which was arrived at, at last, being, that he had better take care of himself, and mind what he was about. The witness having been by these means reduced to the requisite ebb of nervous perplexity, the examination was continued as follows--\n\n‘Pray, Mr. Winkle, do you remember calling on the defendant Pickwick at these apartments in the plaintiff’s house in Goswell Street, on one particular morning, in the month of July last?’\n\n‘Yes, I do.’\n\n‘Were you accompanied on that occasion by a friend of the name of Tupman, and another by the name of Snodgrass?’\n\n‘Yes, I was.’\n\n‘Are they here?’\n\nYes, they are,’ replied Mr. Winkle, looking very earnestly towards the spot where his friends were stationed.\n\n‘Pray attend to me, Mr. Winkle, and never mind your friends,’ said Mr. Skimpin, with another expressive look at the jury. ‘They must tell their stories without any previous consultation with you, if none has yet taken place (another look at the jury). Now, Sir, tell the gentlemen of the jury what you saw on entering the defendant’s room, on this particular morning. Come; out with it, Sir; we must have it, sooner or later.’\n\n‘The defendant, Mr. Pickwick, was holding the plaintiff in his arms, with his hands clasping her waist,’ replied Mr. Winkle with natural hesitation, ‘and the plaintiff appeared to have fainted away.’\n\n‘Did you hear the defendant say anything?’\n\n‘I heard him call Mrs. Bardell a good creature, and I heard him ask her to compose herself, for what a situation it was, if anybody should come, or words to that effect.’\n\n‘Now, Mr. Winkle, I have only one more question to ask you, and I beg you to bear in mind his Lordship’s caution. Will you undertake to swear that Pickwick, the defendant, did not say on the occasion in question-- “My dear Mrs. Bardell, you’re a good creature; compose yourself to this situation, for to this situation you must come,” or words to that effect?’\n\n‘I--I didn’t understand him so, certainly,’ said Mr. Winkle, astounded on this ingenious dove-tailing of the few words he had heard. ‘I was on the staircase, and couldn’t hear distinctly; the impression on my mind is--’\n\n‘The gentlemen of the jury want none of the impressions on your mind, Mr. Winkle, which I fear would be of little service to honest, straightforward men,’ interposed Mr. Skimpin. ‘You were on the staircase, and didn’t distinctly hear; but you will not swear that Pickwick did not make use of the expressions I have quoted? Do I understand that?’\n\n‘No, I will not,’ replied Mr. Winkle; and down sat Mr. Skimpin with a triumphant countenance.\n\nMr. Pickwick’s case had not gone off in so particularly happy a manner, up to this point, that it could very well afford to have any additional suspicion cast upon it. But as it could afford to be placed in a rather better light, if possible, Mr. Phunky rose for the purpose of getting something important out of Mr. Winkle in cross-examination. Whether he did get anything important out of him, will immediately appear.\n\n‘I believe, Mr. Winkle,’ said Mr. Phunky, ‘that Mr. Pickwick is not a young man?’\n\n‘Oh, no,’ replied Mr. Winkle; ‘old enough to be my father.’\n\n‘You have told my learned friend that you have known Mr. Pickwick a long time. Had you ever any reason to suppose or believe that he was about to be married?’\n\n‘Oh, no; certainly not;’ replied Mr. Winkle with so much eagerness, that Mr. Phunky ought to have got him out of the box with all possible dispatch. Lawyers hold that there are two kinds of particularly bad witnesses--a reluctant witness, and a too-willing witness; it was Mr. Winkle’s fate to figure in both characters.\n\n‘I will even go further than this, Mr. Winkle,’ continued Mr. Phunky, in a most smooth and complacent manner. ‘Did you ever see anything in Mr. Pickwick’s manner and conduct towards the opposite sex, to induce you to believe that he ever contemplated matrimony of late years, in any case?’\n\n‘Oh, no; certainly not,’ replied Mr. Winkle.\n\n‘Has his behaviour, when females have been in the case, always been that of a man, who, having attained a pretty advanced period of life, content with his own occupations and amusements, treats them only as a father might his daughters?’\n\n‘Not the least doubt of it,’ replied Mr. Winkle, in the fulness of his heart. ‘That is--yes--oh, yes--certainly.’\n\n‘You have never known anything in his behaviour towards Mrs. Bardell, or any other female, in the least degree suspicious?’ said Mr. Phunky, preparing to sit down; for Serjeant Snubbin was winking at him.\n\n‘N-n-no,’ replied Mr. Winkle, ‘except on one trifling occasion, which, I have no doubt, might be easily explained.’\n\nNow, if the unfortunate Mr. Phunky had sat down when Serjeant Snubbin had winked at him, or if Serjeant Buzfuz had stopped this irregular cross-examination at the outset (which he knew better than to do; observing Mr. Winkle’s anxiety, and well knowing it would, in all probability, lead to something serviceable to him), this unfortunate admission would not have been elicited. The moment the words fell from Mr. Winkle’s lips, Mr. Phunky sat down, and Serjeant Snubbin rather hastily told him he might leave the box, which Mr. Winkle prepared to do with great readiness, when Serjeant Buzfuz stopped him.\n\n‘Stay, Mr. Winkle, stay!’ said Serjeant Buzfuz, ‘will your Lordship have the goodness to ask him, what this one instance of suspicious behaviour towards females on the part of this gentleman, who is old enough to be his father, was?’\n\n‘You hear what the learned counsel says, Sir,’ observed the judge, turning to the miserable and agonised Mr. Winkle. ‘Describe the occasion to which you refer.’\n\n‘My Lord,’ said Mr. Winkle, trembling with anxiety, ‘I--I’d rather not.’\n\n‘Perhaps so,’ said the little judge; ‘but you must.’\n\nAmid the profound silence of the whole court, Mr. Winkle faltered out, that the trifling circumstance of suspicion was Mr. Pickwick’s being found in a lady’s sleeping-apartment at midnight; which had terminated, he believed, in the breaking off of the projected marriage of the lady in question, and had led, he knew, to the whole party being forcibly carried before George Nupkins, Esq., magistrate and justice of the peace, for the borough of Ipswich!\n\n‘You may leave the box, Sir,’ said Serjeant Snubbin. Mr. Winkle did leave the box, and rushed with delirious haste to the George and Vulture, where he was discovered some hours after, by the waiter, groaning in a hollow and dismal manner, with his head buried beneath the sofa cushions.\n\nTracy Tupman, and Augustus Snodgrass, were severally called into the box; both corroborated the testimony of their unhappy friend; and each was driven to the verge of desperation by excessive badgering.\n\nSusannah Sanders was then called, and examined by Serjeant Buzfuz, and cross-examined by Serjeant Snubbin. Had always said and believed that Pickwick would marry Mrs. Bardell; knew that Mrs. Bardell’s being engaged to Pickwick was the current topic of conversation in the neighbourhood, after the fainting in July; had been told it herself by Mrs. Mudberry which kept a mangle, and Mrs. Bunkin which clear-starched, but did not see either Mrs. Mudberry or Mrs. Bunkin in court. Had heard Pickwick ask the little boy how he should like to have another father. Did not know that Mrs. Bardell was at that time keeping company with the baker, but did know that the baker was then a single man and is now married. Couldn’t swear that Mrs. Bardell was not very fond of the baker, but should think that the baker was not very fond of Mrs. Bardell, or he wouldn’t have married somebody else. Thought Mrs. Bardell fainted away on the morning in July, because Pickwick asked her to name the day: knew that she (witness) fainted away stone dead when Mr. Sanders asked her to name the day, and believed that everybody as called herself a lady would do the same, under similar circumstances. Heard Pickwick ask the boy the question about the marbles, but upon her oath did not know the difference between an ‘alley tor’ and a ‘commoney.’\n\nBy the court.--During the period of her keeping company with Mr. Sanders, had received love letters, like other ladies. In the course of their correspondence Mr. Sanders had often called her a ‘duck,’ but never ‘chops,’ nor yet ‘tomato sauce.’ He was particularly fond of ducks. Perhaps if he had been as fond of chops and tomato sauce, he might have called her that, as a term of affection.\n\nSerjeant Buzfuz now rose with more importance than he had yet exhibited, if that were possible, and vociferated; ‘Call Samuel Weller.’\n\nIt was quite unnecessary to call Samuel Weller; for Samuel Weller stepped briskly into the box the instant his name was pronounced; and placing his hat on the floor, and his arms on the rail, took a bird’s- eye view of the Bar, and a comprehensive survey of the Bench, with a remarkably cheerful and lively aspect.\n\n‘What’s your name, sir?’ inquired the judge.\n\n‘Sam Weller, my Lord,’ replied that gentleman.\n\n‘Do you spell it with a “V” or a “W”?’ inquired the judge.\n\n‘That depends upon the taste and fancy of the speller, my Lord,’ replied Sam; ‘I never had occasion to spell it more than once or twice in my life, but I spells it with a “V.”’\n\nHere a voice in the gallery exclaimed aloud, ‘Quite right too, Samivel, quite right. Put it down a “we,” my Lord, put it down a “we.”’\n\nWho is that, who dares address the court?’ said the little judge, looking up. ‘Usher.’\n\n‘Yes, my Lord.’\n\n‘Bring that person here instantly.’\n\n‘Yes, my Lord.’\n\nBut as the usher didn’t find the person, he didn’t bring him; and, after a great commotion, all the people who had got up to look for the culprit, sat down again. The little judge turned to the witness as soon as his indignation would allow him to speak, and said--\n\n‘Do you know who that was, sir?’\n\n‘I rayther suspect it was my father, my lord,’ replied Sam.\n\n‘Do you see him here now?’ said the judge.\n\n‘No, I don’t, my Lord,’ replied Sam, staring right up into the lantern at the roof of the court.\n\n‘If you could have pointed him out, I would have committed him instantly,’ said the judge. Sam bowed his acknowledgments and turned, with unimpaired cheerfulness of countenance, towards Serjeant Buzfuz.\n\n‘Now, Mr. Weller,’ said Serjeant Buzfuz.\n\n‘Now, sir,’ replied Sam.\n\n‘I believe you are in the service of Mr. Pickwick, the defendant in this case? Speak up, if you please, Mr. Weller.’\n\n‘I mean to speak up, Sir,’ replied Sam; ‘I am in the service o’ that ‘ere gen’l’man, and a wery good service it is.’\n\n‘Little to do, and plenty to get, I suppose?’ said Serjeant Buzfuz, with jocularity.\n\n‘Oh, quite enough to get, Sir, as the soldier said ven they ordered him three hundred and fifty lashes,’ replied Sam.\n\n‘You must not tell us what the soldier, or any other man, said, Sir,’ interposed the judge; ‘it’s not evidence.’\n\n‘Wery good, my Lord,’ replied Sam.\n\n‘Do you recollect anything particular happening on the morning when you were first engaged by the defendant; eh, Mr. Weller?’ said Serjeant Buzfuz.\n\n‘Yes, I do, sir,’ replied Sam.\n\n‘Have the goodness to tell the jury what it was.’\n\n‘I had a reg’lar new fit out o’ clothes that mornin’, gen’l’men of the jury,’ said Sam, ‘and that was a wery partickler and uncommon circumstance vith me in those days.’\n\nHereupon there was a general laugh; and the little judge, looking with an angry countenance over his desk, said, ‘You had better be careful, Sir.’\n\n‘So Mr. Pickwick said at the time, my Lord,’ replied Sam; ‘and I was wery careful o’ that ‘ere suit o’ clothes; wery careful indeed, my Lord.’\n\nThe judge looked sternly at Sam for full two minutes, but Sam’s features were so perfectly calm and serene that the judge said nothing, and motioned Serjeant Buzfuz to proceed.\n\n‘Do you mean to tell me, Mr. Weller,’ said Serjeant Buzfuz, folding his arms emphatically, and turning half-round to the jury, as if in mute assurance that he would bother the witness yet--‘do you mean to tell me, Mr. Weller, that you saw nothing of this fainting on the part of the plaintiff in the arms of the defendant, which you have heard described by the witnesses?’\n\nCertainly not,’ replied Sam; ‘I was in the passage till they called me up, and then the old lady was not there.’\n\n‘Now, attend, Mr. Weller,’ said Serjeant Buzfuz, dipping a large pen into the inkstand before him, for the purpose of frightening Sam with a show of taking down his answer. ‘You were in the passage, and yet saw nothing of what was going forward. Have you a pair of eyes, Mr. Weller?’\n\n‘Yes, I have a pair of eyes,’ replied Sam, ‘and that’s just it. If they wos a pair o’ patent double million magnifyin’ gas microscopes of hextra power, p’raps I might be able to see through a flight o’ stairs and a deal door; but bein’ only eyes, you see, my wision ‘s limited.’\n\nAt this answer, which was delivered without the slightest appearance of irritation, and with the most complete simplicity and equanimity of manner, the spectators tittered, the little judge smiled, and Serjeant Buzfuz looked particularly foolish. After a short consultation with Dodson & Fogg, the learned Serjeant again turned towards Sam, and said, with a painful effort to conceal his vexation, ‘Now, Mr. Weller, I’ll ask you a question on another point, if you please.’\n\n‘If you please, Sir,’ rejoined Sam, with the utmost good-humour.\n\n‘Do you remember going up to Mrs. Bardell’s house, one night in November last?’\n\nOh, yes, wery well.’\n\n‘Oh, you do remember that, Mr. Weller,’ said Serjeant Buzfuz, recovering his spirits; ‘I thought we should get at something at last.’\n\n‘I rayther thought that, too, sir,’ replied Sam; and at this the spectators tittered again.\n\n‘Well; I suppose you went up to have a little talk about this trial--eh, Mr. Weller?’ said Serjeant Buzfuz, looking knowingly at the jury.\n\n‘I went up to pay the rent; but we did get a-talkin’ about the trial,’ replied Sam.\n\n‘Oh, you did get a-talking about the trial,’ said Serjeant Buzfuz, brightening up with the anticipation of some important discovery. ‘Now, what passed about the trial; will you have the goodness to tell us, Mr. Weller’?’\n\n‘Vith all the pleasure in life, sir,’ replied Sam. ‘Arter a few unimportant obserwations from the two wirtuous females as has been examined here to-day, the ladies gets into a very great state o’ admiration at the honourable conduct of Mr. Dodson and Fogg--them two gen’l’men as is settin’ near you now.’ This, of course, drew general attention to Dodson & Fogg, who looked as virtuous as possible.\n\n‘The attorneys for the plaintiff,’ said Mr. Serjeant Buzfuz. ‘Well! They spoke in high praise of the honourable conduct of Messrs. Dodson and Fogg, the attorneys for the plaintiff, did they?’\n\n‘Yes,’ said Sam, ‘they said what a wery gen’rous thing it was o’ them to have taken up the case on spec, and to charge nothing at all for costs, unless they got ‘em out of Mr. Pickwick.’\n\nAt this very unexpected reply, the spectators tittered again, and Dodson & Fogg, turning very red, leaned over to Serjeant Buzfuz, and in a hurried manner whispered something in his ear.\n\n‘You are quite right,’ said Serjeant Buzfuz aloud, with affected composure. ‘It’s perfectly useless, my Lord, attempting to get at any evidence through the impenetrable stupidity of this witness. I will not trouble the court by asking him any more questions. Stand down, sir.’\n\n‘Would any other gen’l’man like to ask me anythin’?’ inquired Sam, taking up his hat, and looking round most deliberately.\n\n‘Not I, Mr. Weller, thank you,’ said Serjeant Snubbin, laughing.\n\n‘You may go down, sir,’ said Serjeant Buzfuz, waving his hand impatiently. Sam went down accordingly, after doing Messrs. Dodson & Fogg’s case as much harm as he conveniently could, and saying just as little respecting Mr. Pickwick as might be, which was precisely the object he had had in view all along.\n\n‘I have no objection to admit, my Lord,’ said Serjeant Snubbin, ‘if it will save the examination of another witness, that Mr. Pickwick has retired from business, and is a gentleman of considerable independent property.’\n\n‘Very well,’ said Serjeant Buzfuz, putting in the two letters to be read, ‘then that’s my case, my Lord.’\n\nSerjeant Snubbin then addressed the jury on behalf of the defendant; and a very long and a very emphatic address he delivered, in which he bestowed the highest possible eulogiums on the conduct and character of Mr. Pickwick; but inasmuch as our readers are far better able to form a correct estimate of that gentleman’s merits and deserts, than Serjeant Snubbin could possibly be, we do not feel called upon to enter at any length into the learned gentleman’s observations. He attempted to show that the letters which had been exhibited, merely related to Mr. Pickwick’s dinner, or to the preparations for receiving him in his apartments on his return from some country excursion. It is sufficient to add in general terms, that he did the best he could for Mr. Pickwick; and the best, as everybody knows, on the infallible authority of the old adage, could do no more.\n\nMr. Justice Stareleigh summed up, in the old-established and most approved form. He read as much of his notes to the jury as he could decipher on so short a notice, and made running-comments on the evidence as he went along. If Mrs. Bardell were right, it was perfectly clear that Mr. Pickwick was wrong, and if they thought the evidence of Mrs. Cluppins worthy of credence they would believe it, and, if they didn’t, why, they wouldn’t. If they were satisfied that a breach of promise of marriage had been committed they would find for the plaintiff with such damages as they thought proper; and if, on the other hand, it appeared to them that no promise of marriage had ever been given, they would find for the defendant with no damages at all. The jury then retired to their private room to talk the matter over, and the judge retired to his private room, to refresh himself with a mutton chop and a glass of sherry.\n\nAn anxious quarter of a hour elapsed; the jury came back; the judge was fetched in. Mr. Pickwick put on his spectacles, and gazed at the foreman with an agitated countenance and a quickly-beating heart.\n\n‘Gentlemen,’ said the individual in black, ‘are you all agreed upon your verdict?’\n\n‘We are,’ replied the foreman.\n\n‘Do you find for the plaintiff, gentlemen, or for the defendant?’\n\nFor the plaintiff.’\n\n‘With what damages, gentlemen?’\n\n‘Seven hundred and fifty pounds.’\n\nMr. Pickwick took off his spectacles, carefully wiped the glasses, folded them into their case, and put them in his pocket; then, having\n\ndrawn on his gloves with great nicety, and stared at the foreman all the while, he mechanically followed Mr. Perker and the blue bag out of court.\n\nThey stopped in a side room while Perker paid the court fees; and here, Mr. Pickwick was joined by his friends. Here, too, he encountered Messrs. Dodson & Fogg, rubbing their hands with every token of outward satisfaction.\n\n‘Well, gentlemen,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Well, Sir,’ said Dodson, for self and partner.\n\n‘You imagine you’ll get your costs, don’t you, gentlemen?’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\nFogg said they thought it rather probable. Dodson smiled, and said they’d try.\n\n‘You may try, and try, and try again, Messrs. Dodson and Fogg,’ said Mr. Pickwick vehemently, ‘but not one farthing of costs or damages do you ever get from me, if I spend the rest of my existence in a debtor’s prison.’\n\n‘Ha! ha!’ laughed Dodson. ‘You’ll think better of that, before next term, Mr. Pickwick.’\n\n‘He, he, he! We’ll soon see about that, Mr. Pickwick,’ grinned Fogg.\n\nSpeechless with indignation, Mr. Pickwick allowed himself to be led by his solicitor and friends to the door, and there assisted into a hackney-coach, which had been fetched for the purpose, by the ever- watchful Sam Weller.\n\nSam had put up the steps, and was preparing to jump upon the box, when he felt himself gently touched on the shoulder; and, looking round, his father stood before him. The old gentleman’s countenance wore a mournful expression, as he shook his head gravely, and said, in warning accents--\n\n‘I know’d what ‘ud come o’ this here mode o’ doin’ bisness. Oh, Sammy, Sammy, vy worn’t there a alleybi!’\n\nCHAPTER XXXV. IN WHICH MR. PICKWICK THINKS HE HAD BETTER GO TO BATH; AND GOES ACCORDINGLY\nBut surely, my dear sir,’ said little Perker, as he stood in Mr. Pickwick’s apartment on the morning after the trial, ‘surely you don’t really mean--really and seriously now, and irritation apart--that you won’t pay these costs and damages?’\n\n‘Not one halfpenny,’ said Mr. Pickwick firmly; ‘not one halfpenny.’\n\n‘Hooroar for the principle, as the money-lender said ven he vouldn’t renew the bill,’ observed Mr. Weller, who was clearing away the breakfast-things.\n\n‘Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘have the goodness to step downstairs.’\n\n‘Cert’nly, sir,’ replied Mr. Weller; and acting on Mr. Pickwick’s gentle hint, Sam retired.\n\n‘No, Perker,’ said Mr. Pickwick, with great seriousness of manner, ‘my friends here have endeavoured to dissuade me from this determination, but without avail. I shall employ myself as usual, until the opposite party have the power of issuing a legal process of execution against me; and if they are vile enough to avail themselves of it, and to arrest my person, I shall yield myself up with perfect cheerfulness and content of heart. When can they do this?’\n\n‘They can issue execution, my dear Sir, for the amount of the damages and taxed costs, next term,’ replied Perker, ‘just two months hence, my dear sir.’\n\n‘Very good,’ said Mr. Pickwick. ‘Until that time, my dear fellow, let me hear no more of the matter. And now,’ continued Mr. Pickwick, looking round on his friends with a good-humoured smile, and a sparkle in the eye which no spectacles could dim or conceal, ‘the only question is, Where shall we go next?’\n\nMr. Tupman and Mr. Snodgrass were too much affected by their friend’s heroism to offer any reply. Mr. Winkle had not yet sufficiently recovered the recollection of his evidence at the trial, to make any observation on any subject, so Mr. Pickwick paused in vain.\n\n‘Well,’ said that gentleman, ‘if you leave me to suggest our destination, I say Bath. I think none of us have ever been there.’\n\nNobody had; and as the proposition was warmly seconded by Perker, who considered it extremely probable that if Mr. Pickwick saw a little change and gaiety he would be inclined to think better of his determination, and worse of a debtor’s prison, it was carried unanimously; and Sam was at once despatched to the White Horse Cellar, to take five places by the half-past seven o’clock coach, next morning.\n\nThere were just two places to be had inside, and just three to be had out; so Sam Weller booked for them all, and having exchanged a few compliments with the booking-office clerk on the subject of a pewter half-crown which was tendered him as a portion of his ‘change,’ walked back to the George and Vulture, where he was pretty busily employed until bed-time in reducing clothes and linen into the smallest possible compass, and exerting his mechanical genius in constructing a variety of ingenious devices for keeping the lids on boxes which had neither locks nor hinges.\n\nThe next was a very unpropitious morning for a journey--muggy, damp, and drizzly. The horses in the stages that were going out, and had come through the city, were smoking so, that the outside passengers were invisible. The newspaper-sellers looked moist, and smelled mouldy; the wet ran off the hats of the orange-vendors as they thrust their heads into the coach windows, and diluted the insides in a refreshing manner. The Jews with the fifty-bladed penknives shut them up in despair; the men with the pocket-books made pocket-books of them. Watch-guards and toasting-forks were alike at a discount, and pencil-cases and sponges were a drug in the market.\n\nLeaving Sam Weller to rescue the luggage from the seven or eight porters who flung themselves savagely upon it, the moment the coach stopped, and finding that they were about twenty minutes too early, Mr. Pickwick and his friends went for shelter into the travellers’ room--the last resource of human dejection.\n\nThe travellers’ room at the White Horse Cellar is of course uncomfortable; it would be no travellers’ room if it were not. It is the right-hand parlour, into which an aspiring kitchen fireplace appears to have walked, accompanied by a rebellious poker, tongs, and shovel. It is divided into boxes, for the solitary confinement of travellers, and is furnished with a clock, a looking-glass, and a live waiter, which latter article is kept in a small kennel for washing glasses, in a corner of the apartment.\n\nOne of these boxes was occupied, on this particular occasion, by a stern-eyed man of about five-and-forty, who had a bald and glossy forehead, with a good deal of black hair at the sides and back of his head, and large black whiskers. He was buttoned up to the chin in a brown coat; and had a large sealskin travelling-cap, and a greatcoat and cloak, lying on the seat beside him. He looked up from his breakfast as Mr. Pickwick entered, with a fierce and peremptory air, which was very dignified; and, having scrutinised that gentleman and his companions to his entire satisfaction, hummed a tune, in a manner which seemed to say that he rather suspected somebody wanted to take advantage of him, but it wouldn’t do.\n\n‘Waiter,’ said the gentleman with the whiskers.\n\n‘Sir?’ replied a man with a dirty complexion, and a towel of the same, emerging from the kennel before mentioned.\n\n‘Some more toast.’\n\n‘Yes, sir.’\n\n‘Buttered toast, mind,’ said the gentleman fiercely.\n\n‘Directly, sir,’ replied the waiter.\n\nThe gentleman with the whiskers hummed a tune in the same manner as before, and pending the arrival of the toast, advanced to the front of the fire, and, taking his coat tails under his arms, looked at his boots and ruminated.\n\n‘I wonder whereabouts in Bath this coach puts up,’ said Mr. Pickwick, mildly addressing Mr. Winkle.\n\n‘Hum--eh--what’s that?’ said the strange man.\n\n‘I made an observation to my friend, sir,’ replied Mr. Pickwick, always ready to enter into conversation. ‘I wondered at what house the Bath coach put up. Perhaps you can inform me.’\n\nAre you going to Bath?’ said the strange man.\n\n‘I am, sir,’ replied Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘And those other gentlemen?’\n\n‘They are going also,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Not inside--I’ll be damned if you’re going inside,’ said the strange man.\n\n‘Not all of us,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘No, not all of you,’ said the strange man emphatically. ‘I’ve taken two places. If they try to squeeze six people into an infernal box that only holds four, I’ll take a post-chaise and bring an action. I’ve paid my fare. It won’t do; I told the clerk when I took my places that it wouldn’t do. I know these things have been done. I know they are done every day; but I never was done, and I never will be. Those who know me best, best know it; crush me!’ Here the fierce gentleman rang the bell with great violence, and told the waiter he’d better bring the toast in five seconds, or he’d know the reason why.\n\n‘My good sir,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘you will allow me to observe that this is a very unnecessary display of excitement. I have only taken places inside for two.’\n\n‘I am glad to hear it,’ said the fierce man. ‘I withdraw my expressions. I tender an apology. There’s my card. Give me your acquaintance.’\n\n‘With great pleasure, Sir,’ replied Mr. Pickwick. ‘We are to be fellow- travellers, and I hope we shall find each other’s society mutually agreeable.’\n\n‘I hope we shall,’ said the fierce gentleman. ‘I know we shall. I like your looks; they please me. Gentlemen, your hands and names. Know me.’\n\nOf course, an interchange of friendly salutations followed this gracious speech; and the fierce gentleman immediately proceeded to inform the friends, in the same short, abrupt, jerking sentences, that his name was Dowler; that he was going to Bath on pleasure; that he was formerly in the army; that he had now set up in business as a gentleman; that he lived upon the profits; and that the individual for whom the second place was taken, was a personage no less illustrious than Mrs. Dowler, his lady wife.\n\n‘She’s a fine woman,’ said Mr. Dowler. ‘I am proud of her. I have reason.’\n\n‘I hope I shall have the pleasure of judging,’ said Mr. Pickwick, with a smile.\n\n‘You shall,’ replied Dowler. ‘She shall know you. She shall esteem you. I courted her under singular circumstances. I won her through a rash vow. Thus. I saw her; I loved her; I proposed; she refused me.--“You love another?”--“Spare my blushes.”--“I know him.”--“You do.”--“Very good; if he remains here, I’ll skin him.”’\n\n‘Lord bless me!’ exclaimed Mr. Pickwick involuntarily.\n\n‘Did you skin the gentleman, Sir?’ inquired Mr. Winkle, with a very pale face.\n\n‘I wrote him a note, I said it was a painful thing. And so it was.’\n\n‘Certainly,’ interposed Mr. Winkle.\n\n‘I said I had pledged my word as a gentleman to skin him. My character was at stake. I had no alternative. As an officer in His Majesty’s service, I was bound to skin him. I regretted the necessity, but it must be done. He was open to conviction. He saw that the rules of the service were imperative. He fled. I married her. Here’s the coach. That’s her head.’\n\nAs Mr. Dowler concluded, he pointed to a stage which had just driven up, from the open window of which a rather pretty face in a bright blue bonnet was looking among the crowd on the pavement, most probably for the rash man himself. Mr. Dowler paid his bill, and hurried out with his travelling cap, coat, and cloak; and Mr. Pickwick and his friends followed to secure their places.\n\nMr. Tupman and Mr. Snodgrass had seated themselves at the back part of the coach; Mr. Winkle had got inside; and Mr. Pickwick was preparing to follow him, when Sam Weller came up to his master, and whispering in his ear, begged to speak to him, with an air of the deepest mystery.\n\n‘Well, Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘what’s the matter now?’\n\n‘Here’s rayther a rum go, sir,’ replied Sam.\n\n‘What?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘This here, Sir,’ rejoined Sam. ‘I’m wery much afeerd, sir, that the properiator o’ this here coach is a playin’ some imperence vith us.’\n\n‘How is that, Sam?’ said Mr. Pickwick; ‘aren’t the names down on the way-bill?’\n\n‘The names is not only down on the vay-bill, Sir,’ replied Sam, ‘but they’ve painted vun on ‘em up, on the door o’ the coach.’ As Sam spoke, he pointed to that part of the coach door on which the proprietor’s name usually appears; and there, sure enough, in gilt letters of a goodly size, was the magic name of Pickwick!\n\n‘Dear me,’ exclaimed Mr. Pickwick, quite staggered by the coincidence; ‘what a very extraordinary thing!’\n\n‘Yes, but that ain’t all,’ said Sam, again directing his master’s attention to the coach door; ‘not content vith writin’ up “Pick-wick,” they puts “Moses” afore it, vich I call addin’ insult to injury, as the parrot said ven they not only took him from his native land, but made him talk the English langwidge arterwards.’\n\n‘It’s odd enough, certainly, Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick; ‘but if we stand talking here, we shall lose our places.’\n\n‘Wot, ain’t nothin’ to be done in consequence, sir?’ exclaimed Sam, perfectly aghast at the coolness with which Mr. Pickwick prepared to ensconce himself inside.\n\n‘Done!’ said Mr. Pickwick. ‘What should be done?’\n\nAin’t nobody to be whopped for takin’ this here liberty, sir?’ said Mr. Weller, who had expected that at least he would have been commissioned to challenge the guard and the coachman to a pugilistic encounter on the spot.\n\n‘Certainly not,’ replied Mr. Pickwick eagerly; ‘not on any account. Jump up to your seat directly.’\n\n‘I am wery much afeered,’ muttered Sam to himself, as he turned away, ‘that somethin’ queer’s come over the governor, or he’d never ha’ stood this so quiet. I hope that ‘ere trial hasn’t broke his spirit, but it looks bad, wery bad.’ Mr. Weller shook his head gravely; and it is worthy of remark, as an illustration of the manner in which he took this circumstance to heart, that he did not speak another word until the coach reached the Kensington turnpike. Which was so long a time for him to remain taciturn, that the fact may be considered wholly unprecedented.\n\nNothing worthy of special mention occurred during the journey. Mr. Dowler related a variety of anecdotes, all illustrative of his own personal prowess and desperation, and appealed to Mrs. Dowler in corroboration thereof; when Mrs. Dowler invariably brought in, in the form of an appendix, some remarkable fact or circumstance which Mr. Dowler had forgotten, or had perhaps through modesty, omitted; for the addenda in every instance went to show that Mr. Dowler was even a more wonderful fellow than he made himself out to be. Mr. Pickwick and Mr. Winkle listened with great admiration, and at intervals conversed with Mrs. Dowler, who was a very agreeable and fascinating person. So, what between Mr. Dowler’s stories, and Mrs. Dowler’s charms, and Mr. Pickwick’s good-humour, and Mr. Winkle’s good listening, the insides contrived to be very companionable all the way.\n\nThe outsides did as outsides always do. They were very cheerful and talkative at the beginning of every stage, and very dismal and sleepy in the middle, and very bright and wakeful again towards the end. There was one young gentleman in an India-rubber cloak, who smoked cigars all day; and there was another young gentleman in a parody upon a greatcoat, who lighted a good many, and feeling obviously unsettled after the second whiff, threw them away when he thought nobody was looking at him. There was a third young man on the box who wished to be learned in cattle; and an old one behind, who was familiar with farming. There was a constant succession of Christian names in smock-frocks and white coats, who were invited to have a ‘lift’ by the guard, and who knew every horse and hostler on the road and off it; and there was a dinner which would have been cheap at half-a-crown a mouth, if any moderate number of mouths could have eaten it in the time. And at seven o’clock P.M. Mr. Pickwick and his friends, and Mr. Dowler and his wife, respectively retired to their private sitting-rooms at the White Hart Hotel, opposite the Great Pump Room, Bath, where the waiters, from their costume, might be mistaken for Westminster boys, only they destroy the illusion by behaving themselves much better.\n\nBreakfast had scarcely been cleared away on the succeeding morning, when a waiter brought in Mr. Dowler’s card, with a request to be allowed permission to introduce a friend. Mr. Dowler at once followed up the delivery of the card, by bringing himself and the friend also.\n\nThe friend was a charming young man of not much more than fifty, dressed in a very bright blue coat with resplendent buttons, black trousers, and the thinnest possible pair of highly-polished boots. A gold eye-glass was suspended from his neck by a short, broad, black ribbon; a gold snuff-box was lightly clasped in his left hand; gold rings innumerable glittered on his fingers; and a large diamond pin set in gold glistened in his shirt frill. He had a gold watch, and a gold curb chain with large gold seals; and he carried a pliant ebony cane with a gold top. His linen was of the very whitest, finest, and stiffest; his wig of the glossiest, blackest, and curliest. His snuff was princes’ mixture; his scent bouquet du roi. His features were contracted into a perpetual smile; and his teeth were in such perfect order that it was difficult at a small distance to tell the real from the false.\n\n‘Mr. Pickwick,’ said Mr. Dowler; ‘my friend, Angelo Cyrus Bantam, Esquire, M.C.; Bantam; Mr. Pickwick. Know each other.’\n\n‘Welcome to Ba--ath, Sir. This is indeed an acquisition. Most welcome to Ba--ath, sir. It is long--very long, Mr. Pickwick, since you drank the waters. It appears an age, Mr. Pickwick. Re-markable!’\n\nSuch were the expressions with which Angelo Cyrus Bantam, Esquire, M.C., took Mr. Pickwick’s hand; retaining it in his, meantime, and shrugging up his shoulders with a constant succession of bows, as if he really could not make up his mind to the trial of letting it go again.\n\n‘It is a very long time since I drank the waters, certainly,’ replied Mr. Pickwick; ‘for, to the best of my knowledge, I was never here before.’\n\n‘Never in Ba--ath, Mr. Pickwick!’ exclaimed the Grand Master, letting the hand fall in astonishment. ‘Never in Ba--ath! He! he! Mr. Pickwick, you are a wag. Not bad, not bad. Good, good. He! he! he! Re-markable!’\n\n‘To my shame, I must say that I am perfectly serious,’ rejoined Mr. Pickwick. ‘I really never was here before.’\n\n‘Oh, I see,’ exclaimed the Grand Master, looking extremely pleased; ‘yes, yes--good, good--better and better. You are the gentleman of whom we have heard. Yes; we know you, Mr. Pickwick; we know you.’\n\n‘The reports of the trial in those confounded papers,’ thought Mr. Pickwick. ‘They have heard all about me.’\n\nYou are the gentleman residing on Clapham Green,’ resumed Bantam, ‘who lost the use of his limbs from imprudently taking cold after port wine; who could not be moved in consequence of acute suffering, and who had the water from the king’s bath bottled at one hundred and three degrees, and sent by wagon to his bedroom in town, where he bathed, sneezed, and the same day recovered. Very remarkable!’\n\nMr. Pickwick acknowledged the compliment which the supposition implied, but had the self-denial to repudiate it, notwithstanding; and taking advantage of a moment’s silence on the part of the M.C., begged to introduce his friends, Mr. Tupman, Mr. Winkle, and Mr. Snodgrass. An introduction which overwhelmed the M.C. with delight and honour.\n\n‘Bantam,’ said Mr. Dowler, ‘Mr. Pickwick and his friends are strangers. They must put their names down. Where’s the book?’\n\n‘The register of the distinguished visitors in Ba--ath will be at the Pump Room this morning at two o’clock,’ replied the M.C. ‘Will you guide our friends to that splendid building, and enable me to procure their autographs?’\n\n‘I will,’ rejoined Dowler. ‘This is a long call. It’s time to go. I shall be here again in an hour. Come.’\n\n‘This is a ball-night,’ said the M.C., again taking Mr. Pickwick’s hand, as he rose to go. ‘The ball-nights in Ba--ath are moments snatched from paradise; rendered bewitching by music, beauty, elegance, fashion, etiquette, and--and--above all, by the absence of tradespeople, who are quite inconsistent with paradise, and who have an amalgamation of themselves at the Guildhall every fortnight, which is, to say the least, remarkable. Good-bye, good-bye!’ and protesting all the way downstairs that he was most satisfied, and most delighted, and most overpowered, and most flattered, Angelo Cyrus Bantam, Esquire, M.C., stepped into a very elegant chariot that waited at the door, and rattled off.\n\nAt the appointed hour, Mr. Pickwick and his friends, escorted by Dowler, repaired to the Assembly Rooms, and wrote their names down in the book-- an instance of condescension at which Angelo Bantam was even more overpowered than before. Tickets of admission to that evening’s assembly were to have been prepared for the whole party, but as they were not ready, Mr. Pickwick undertook, despite all the protestations to the contrary of Angelo Bantam, to send Sam for them at four o’clock in the afternoon, to the M.C.’s house in Queen Square. Having taken a short walk through the city, and arrived at the unanimous conclusion that Park Street was very much like the perpendicular streets a man sees in a dream, which he cannot get up for the life of him, they returned to the White Hart, and despatched Sam on the errand to which his master had pledged him.\n\nSam Weller put on his hat in a very easy and graceful manner, and, thrusting his hands in his waistcoat pockets, walked with great deliberation to Queen Square, whistling as he went along, several of the most popular airs of the day, as arranged with entirely new movements for that noble instrument the organ, either mouth or barrel. Arriving at the number in Queen Square to which he had been directed, he left off whistling and gave a cheerful knock, which was instantaneously answered by a powdered-headed footman in gorgeous livery, and of symmetrical stature.\n\n‘Is this here Mr. Bantam’s, old feller?’ inquired Sam Weller, nothing abashed by the blaze of splendour which burst upon his sight in the person of the powdered-headed footman with the gorgeous livery.\n\n‘Why, young man?’ was the haughty inquiry of the powdered-headed footman.\n\n‘’Cos if it is, jist you step in to him with that ‘ere card, and say Mr. Veller’s a-waitin’, will you?’ said Sam. And saying it, he very coolly walked into the hall, and sat down.\n\nThe powdered-headed footman slammed the door very hard, and scowled very grandly; but both the slam and the scowl were lost upon Sam, who was regarding a mahogany umbrella-stand with every outward token of critical approval.\n\nApparently his master’s reception of the card had impressed the powdered-headed footman in Sam’s favour, for when he came back from delivering it, he smiled in a friendly manner, and said that the answer would be ready directly.\n\n‘Wery good,’ said Sam. ‘Tell the old gen’l’m’n not to put himself in a perspiration. No hurry, six-foot. I’ve had my dinner.’\n\n‘You dine early, sir,’ said the powdered-headed footman.\n\n‘I find I gets on better at supper when I does,’ replied Sam.\n\n‘Have you been long in Bath, sir?’ inquired the powdered-headed footman. ‘I have not had the pleasure of hearing of you before.’\n\n‘I haven’t created any wery surprisin’ sensation here, as yet,’ rejoined Sam, ‘for me and the other fash’nables only come last night.’\n\n‘Nice place, Sir,’ said the powdered-headed footman.\n\n‘Seems so,’ observed Sam.\n\n‘Pleasant society, sir,’ remarked the powdered-headed footman. ‘Very agreeable servants, sir.’\n\n‘I should think they wos,’ replied Sam. ‘Affable, unaffected, say- nothin’-to-nobody sorts o’ fellers.’\n\n‘Oh, very much so, indeed, sir,’ said the powdered-headed footman, taking Sam’s remarks as a high compliment. ‘Very much so indeed. Do you do anything in this way, Sir?’ inquired the tall footman, producing a small snuff-box with a fox’s head on the top of it.\n\n‘Not without sneezing,’ replied Sam.\n\n‘Why, it is difficult, sir, I confess,’ said the tall footman. ‘It may be done by degrees, Sir. Coffee is the best practice. I carried coffee, Sir, for a long time. It looks very like rappee, sir.’\n\nHere, a sharp peal at the bell reduced the powdered-headed footman to the ignominious necessity of putting the fox’s head in his pocket, and hastening with a humble countenance to Mr. Bantam’s ‘study.’ By the bye, who ever knew a man who never read or wrote either, who hadn’t got some small back parlour which he would call a study!\n\n‘There is the answer, sir,’ said the powdered-headed footman. ‘I’m afraid you’ll find it inconveniently large.’\n\n‘Don’t mention it,’ said Sam, taking a letter with a small enclosure. ‘It’s just possible as exhausted natur’ may manage to surwive it.’\n\n‘I hope we shall meet again, Sir,’ said the powdered-headed footman, rubbing his hands, and following Sam out to the door-step.\n\n‘You are wery obligin’, sir,’ replied Sam. ‘Now, don’t allow yourself to be fatigued beyond your powers; there’s a amiable bein’. Consider what you owe to society, and don’t let yourself be injured by too much work. For the sake o’ your feller-creeturs, keep yourself as quiet as you can; only think what a loss you would be!’ With these pathetic words, Sam Weller departed.\n\n‘A very singular young man that,’ said the powdered-headed footman, looking after Mr. Weller, with a countenance which clearly showed he could make nothing of him.\n\nSam said nothing at all. He winked, shook his head, smiled, winked again; and, with an expression of countenance which seemed to denote that he was greatly amused with something or other, walked merrily away.\n\nAt precisely twenty minutes before eight o’clock that night, Angelo Cyrus Bantam, Esq., the Master of the Ceremonies, emerged from his chariot at the door of the Assembly Rooms in the same wig, the same teeth, the same eye-glass, the same watch and seals, the same rings, the same shirt-pin, and the same cane. The only observable alterations in his appearance were, that he wore a brighter blue coat, with a white silk lining, black tights, black silk stockings, and pumps, and a white waistcoat, and was, if possible, just a thought more scented.\n\nThus attired, the Master of the Ceremonies, in strict discharge of the important duties of his all-important office, planted himself in the room to receive the company.\n\nBath being full, the company, and the sixpences for tea, poured in, in shoals. In the ballroom, the long card-room, the octagonal card-room, the staircases, and the passages, the hum of many voices, and the sound of many feet, were perfectly bewildering. Dresses rustled, feathers waved, lights shone, and jewels sparkled. There was the music--not of the quadrille band, for it had not yet commenced; but the music of soft, tiny footsteps, with now and then a clear, merry laugh--low and gentle, but very pleasant to hear in a female voice, whether in Bath or elsewhere. Brilliant eyes, lighted up with pleasurable expectation, gleamed from every side; and, look where you would, some exquisite form glided gracefully through the throng, and was no sooner lost, than it was replaced by another as dainty and bewitching.\n\nIn the tea-room, and hovering round the card-tables, were a vast number of queer old ladies, and decrepit old gentlemen, discussing all the small talk and scandal of the day, with a relish and gusto which sufficiently bespoke the intensity of the pleasure they derived from the occupation. Mingled with these groups, were three or four match-making mammas, appearing to be wholly absorbed by the conversation in which they were taking part, but failing not from time to time to cast an anxious sidelong glance upon their daughters, who, remembering the maternal injunction to make the best use of their youth, had already commenced incipient flirtations in the mislaying scarves, putting on gloves, setting down cups, and so forth; slight matters apparently, but which may be turned to surprisingly good account by expert practitioners.\n\nLounging near the doors, and in remote corners, were various knots of silly young men, displaying various varieties of puppyism and stupidity; amusing all sensible people near them with their folly and conceit; and happily thinking themselves the objects of general admiration--a wise and merciful dispensation which no good man will quarrel with.\n\nAnd lastly, seated on some of the back benches, where they had already taken up their positions for the evening, were divers unmarried ladies past their grand climacteric, who, not dancing because there were no partners for them, and not playing cards lest they should be set down as irretrievably single, were in the favourable situation of being able to abuse everybody without reflecting on themselves. In short, they could abuse everybody, because everybody was there. It was a scene of gaiety, glitter, and show; of richly-dressed people, handsome mirrors, chalked floors, girandoles and wax-candles; and in all parts of the scene, gliding from spot to spot in silent softness, bowing obsequiously to this party, nodding familiarly to that, and smiling complacently on all, was the sprucely-attired person of Angelo Cyrus Bantam, Esquire, the Master of the Ceremonies.\n\n‘Stop in the tea-room. Take your sixpenn’orth. Then lay on hot water, and call it tea. Drink it,’ said Mr. Dowler, in a loud voice, directing Mr. Pickwick, who advanced at the head of the little party, with Mrs. Dowler on his arm. Into the tea-room Mr. Pickwick turned; and catching sight of him, Mr. Bantam corkscrewed his way through the crowd and welcomed him with ecstasy.\n\n‘My dear Sir, I am highly honoured. Ba--ath is favoured. Mrs. Dowler, you embellish the rooms. I congratulate you on your feathers. Re- markable!’\n\n‘Anybody here?’ inquired Dowler suspiciously.\n\n‘Anybody! The elite of Ba--ath. Mr. Pickwick, do you see the old lady in the gauze turban?’\n\n‘The fat old lady?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick innocently.\n\n‘Hush, my dear sir--nobody’s fat or old in Ba--ath. That’s the Dowager Lady Snuphanuph.’\n\n‘Is it, indeed?’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘No less a person, I assure you,’ said the Master of the Ceremonies. ‘Hush. Draw a little nearer, Mr. Pickwick. You see the splendidly- dressed young man coming this way?’\n\n‘The one with the long hair, and the particularly small forehead?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘The same. The richest young man in Ba--ath at this moment. Young Lord Mutanhed.’\n\n‘You don’t say so?’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Yes. You’ll hear his voice in a moment, Mr. Pickwick. He’ll speak to me. The other gentleman with him, in the red under-waistcoat and dark moustache, is the Honourable Mr. Crushton, his bosom friend. How do you do, my Lord?’\n\n‘Veway hot, Bantam,’ said his Lordship.\n\n‘It is very warm, my Lord,’ replied the M.C.\n\n‘Confounded,’ assented the Honourable Mr. Crushton.\n\n‘Have you seen his Lordship’s mail-cart, Bantam?’ inquired the Honourable Mr. Crushton, after a short pause, during which young Lord Mutanhed had been endeavouring to stare Mr. Pickwick out of countenance, and Mr. Crushton had been reflecting what subject his Lordship could talk about best.\n\n‘Dear me, no,’ replied the M.C. ‘A mail-cart! What an excellent idea. Re-markable!’\n\n‘Gwacious heavens!’ said his Lordship, ‘I thought evewebody had seen the new mail-cart; it’s the neatest, pwettiest, gwacefullest thing that ever wan upon wheels. Painted wed, with a cweam piebald.’\n\n‘With a real box for the letters, and all complete,’ said the Honourable Mr. Crushton.\n\n‘And a little seat in fwont, with an iwon wail, for the dwiver,’ added his Lordship. ‘I dwove it over to Bwistol the other morning, in a cwimson coat, with two servants widing a quarter of a mile behind; and confound me if the people didn’t wush out of their cottages, and awest my pwogwess, to know if I wasn’t the post. Glorwious--glorwious!’\n\nAt this anecdote his Lordship laughed very heartily, as did the listeners, of course. Then, drawing his arm through that of the obsequious Mr. Crushton, Lord Mutanhed walked away.\n\n‘Delightful young man, his Lordship,’ said the Master of the Ceremonies.\n\n‘So I should think,’ rejoined Mr. Pickwick drily.\n\nThe dancing having commenced, the necessary introductions having been made, and all preliminaries arranged, Angelo Bantam rejoined Mr. Pickwick, and led him into the card-room.\n\nJust at the very moment of their entrance, the Dowager Lady Snuphanuph and two other ladies of an ancient and whist-like appearance, were hovering over an unoccupied card-table; and they no sooner set eyes upon Mr. Pickwick under the convoy of Angelo Bantam, than they exchanged glances with each other, seeing that he was precisely the very person they wanted, to make up the rubber.\n\n‘My dear Bantam,’ said the Dowager Lady Snuphanuph coaxingly, ‘find us some nice creature to make up this table; there’s a good soul.’ Mr. Pickwick happened to be looking another way at the moment, so her Ladyship nodded her head towards him, and frowned expressively.\n\n‘My friend Mr. Pickwick, my Lady, will be most happy, I am sure, remarkably so,’ said the M.C., taking the hint. ‘Mr. Pickwick, Lady Snuphanuph--Mrs. Colonel Wugsby--Miss Bolo.’\n\nMr. Pickwick bowed to each of the ladies, and, finding escape impossible, cut. Mr. Pickwick and Miss Bolo against Lady Snuphanuph and Mrs. Colonel Wugsby.\n\nAs the trump card was turned up, at the commencement of the second deal, two young ladies hurried into the room, and took their stations on either side of Mrs. Colonel Wugsby’s chair, where they waited patiently until the hand was over.\n\n‘Now, Jane,’ said Mrs. Colonel Wugsby, turning to one of the girls, ‘what is it?’\n\nI came to ask, ma, whether I might dance with the youngest Mr. Crawley,’ whispered the prettier and younger of the two.\n\n‘Good God, Jane, how can you think of such things?’ replied the mamma indignantly. ‘Haven’t you repeatedly heard that his father has eight hundred a year, which dies with him? I am ashamed of you. Not on any account.’\n\n‘Ma,’ whispered the other, who was much older than her sister, and very insipid and artificial, ‘Lord Mutanhed has been introduced to me. I said I thought I wasn’t engaged, ma.’\n\n‘You’re a sweet pet, my love,’ replied Mrs. Colonel Wugsby, tapping her daughter’s cheek with her fan, ‘and are always to be trusted. He’s immensely rich, my dear. Bless you!’ With these words Mrs. Colonel Wugsby kissed her eldest daughter most affectionately, and frowning in a warning manner upon the other, sorted her cards.\n\nPoor Mr. Pickwick! he had never played with three thorough-paced female card-players before. They were so desperately sharp, that they quite frightened him. If he played a wrong card, Miss Bolo looked a small armoury of daggers; if he stopped to consider which was the right one, Lady Snuphanuph would throw herself back in her chair, and smile with a mingled glance of impatience and pity to Mrs. Colonel Wugsby, at which Mrs. Colonel Wugsby would shrug up her shoulders, and cough, as much as to say she wondered whether he ever would begin. Then, at the end of every hand, Miss Bolo would inquire with a dismal countenance and reproachful sigh, why Mr. Pickwick had not returned that diamond, or led the club, or roughed the spade, or finessed the heart, or led through the honour, or brought out the ace, or played up to the king, or some such thing; and in reply to all these grave charges, Mr. Pickwick would be wholly unable to plead any justification whatever, having by this time forgotten all about the game. People came and looked on, too, which made Mr. Pickwick nervous. Besides all this, there was a great deal of distracting conversation near the table, between Angelo Bantam and the two Misses Matinter, who, being single and singular, paid great court to the Master of the Ceremonies, in the hope of getting a stray partner now and then. All these things, combined with the noises and interruptions of constant comings in and goings out, made Mr. Pickwick play rather badly; the cards were against him, also; and when they left off at ten minutes past eleven, Miss Bolo rose from the table considerably agitated, and went straight home, in a flood of tears and a sedan-chair.\n\nBeing joined by his friends, who one and all protested that they had scarcely ever spent a more pleasant evening, Mr. Pickwick accompanied them to the White Hart, and having soothed his feelings with something hot, went to bed, and to sleep, almost simultaneously.\n\nCHAPTER XXXVI. THE CHIEF FEATURES OF WHICH WILL BE FOUND TO BE AN AUTHENTIC VERSION OF THE LEGEND OF PRINCE BLADUD, AND A MOST EXTRAORDINARY CALAMITY THAT BEFELL MR. WINKLE\nAs Mr. Pickwick contemplated a stay of at least two months in Bath, he deemed it advisable to take private lodgings for himself and friends for that period; and as a favourable opportunity offered for their securing, on moderate terms, the upper portion of a house in the Royal Crescent, which was larger than they required, Mr. and Mrs. Dowler offered to relieve them of a bedroom and sitting-room. This proposition was at once accepted, and in three days’ time they were all located in their new abode, when Mr. Pickwick began to drink the waters with the utmost assiduity. Mr. Pickwick took them systematically. He drank a quarter of a pint before breakfast, and then walked up a hill; and another quarter of a pint after breakfast, and then walked down a hill; and, after every fresh quarter of a pint, Mr. Pickwick declared, in the most solemn and emphatic terms, that he felt a great deal better; whereat his friends were very much delighted, though they had not been previously aware that there was anything the matter with him.\n\nThe Great Pump Room is a spacious saloon, ornamented with Corinthian pillars, and a music-gallery, and a Tompion clock, and a statue of Nash, and a golden inscription, to which all the water-drinkers should attend, for it appeals to them in the cause of a deserving charity. There is a large bar with a marble vase, out of which the pumper gets the water; and there are a number of yellow-looking tumblers, out of which the company get it; and it is a most edifying and satisfactory sight to behold the perseverance and gravity with which they swallow it. There are baths near at hand, in which a part of the company wash themselves; and a band plays afterwards, to congratulate the remainder on their having done so. There is another pump room, into which infirm ladies and gentlemen are wheeled, in such an astonishing variety of chairs and chaises, that any adventurous individual who goes in with the regular number of toes, is in imminent danger of coming out without them; and there is a third, into which the quiet people go, for it is less noisy than either. There is an immensity of promenading, on crutches and off, with sticks and without, and a great deal of conversation, and liveliness, and pleasantry.\n\nEvery morning, the regular water-drinkers, Mr. Pickwick among the number, met each other in the pump room, took their quarter of a pint, and walked constitutionally. At the afternoon’s promenade, Lord Mutanhed, and the Honourable Mr. Crushton, the Dowager Lady Snuphanuph, Mrs. Colonel Wugsby, and all the great people, and all the morning water-drinkers, met in grand assemblage. After this, they walked out, or drove out, or were pushed out in bath-chairs, and met one another again. After this, the gentlemen went to the reading-rooms, and met divisions of the mass. After this, they went home. If it were theatre-night, perhaps they met at the theatre; if it were assembly-night, they met at the rooms; and if it were neither, they met the next day. A very pleasant routine, with perhaps a slight tinge of sameness.\n\nMr. Pickwick was sitting up by himself, after a day spent in this manner, making entries in his journal, his friends having retired to bed, when he was roused by a gentle tap at the room door.\n\n‘Beg your pardon, Sir,’ said Mrs. Craddock, the landlady, peeping in; ‘but did you want anything more, sir?’\n\n‘Nothing more, ma’am,’ replied Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘My young girl is gone to bed, Sir,’ said Mrs. Craddock; ‘and Mr. Dowler is good enough to say that he’ll sit up for Mrs. Dowler, as the party isn’t expected to be over till late; so I was thinking that if you wanted nothing more, Mr. Pickwick, I would go to bed.’\n\n‘By all means, ma’am,’ replied Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Wish you good-night, Sir,’ said Mrs. Craddock.\n\n‘Good-night, ma’am,’ rejoined Mr. Pickwick.\n\nMrs. Craddock closed the door, and Mr. Pickwick resumed his writing.\n\nIn half an hour’s time the entries were concluded. Mr. Pickwick carefully rubbed the last page on the blotting-paper, shut up the book, wiped his pen on the bottom of the inside of his coat tail, and opened the drawer of the inkstand to put it carefully away. There were a couple of sheets of writing-paper, pretty closely written over, in the inkstand drawer, and they were folded so, that the title, which was in a good round hand, was fully disclosed to him. Seeing from this, that it was no private document; and as it seemed to relate to Bath, and was very short: Mr. Pick-wick unfolded it, lighted his bedroom candle that it might burn up well by the time he finished; and drawing his chair nearer the fire, read as follows--\n\n# THE TRUE LEGEND OF PRINCE BLADUD\n\n‘Less than two hundred years ago, on one of the public baths in this city, there appeared an inscription in honour of its mighty founder, the renowned Prince Bladud. That inscription is now erased.\n\n‘For many hundred years before that time, there had been handed down, from age to age, an old legend, that the illustrious prince being afflicted with leprosy, on his return from reaping a rich harvest of knowledge in Athens, shunned the court of his royal father, and consorted moodily with husbandman and pigs. Among the herd (so said the legend) was a pig of grave and solemn countenance, with whom the prince had a fellow-feeling--for he too was wise--a pig of thoughtful and reserved demeanour; an animal superior to his fellows, whose grunt was terrible, and whose bite was sharp. The young prince sighed deeply as he looked upon the countenance of the majestic swine; he thought of his royal father, and his eyes were bedewed with tears.\n\n‘This sagacious pig was fond of bathing in rich, moist mud. Not in summer, as common pigs do now, to cool themselves, and did even in those distant ages (which is a proof that the light of civilisation had already begun to dawn, though feebly), but in the cold, sharp days of winter. His coat was ever so sleek, and his complexion so clear, that the prince resolved to essay the purifying qualities of the same water that his friend resorted to. He made the trial. Beneath that black mud, bubbled the hot springs of Bath. He washed, and was cured. Hastening to his father’s court, he paid his best respects, and returning quickly hither, founded this city and its famous baths.\n\n‘He sought the pig with all the ardour of their early friendship--but, alas! the waters had been his death. He had imprudently taken a bath at too high a temperature, and the natural philosopher was no more! He was succeeded by Pliny, who also fell a victim to his thirst for knowledge.\n\n‘This was the legend. Listen to the true one.\n\n‘A great many centuries since, there flourished, in great state, the famous and renowned Lud Hudibras, king of Britain. He was a mighty monarch. The earth shook when he walked--he was so very stout. His people basked in the light of his countenance--it was so red and glowing. He was, indeed, every inch a king. And there were a good many inches of him, too, for although he was not very tall, he was a remarkable size round, and the inches that he wanted in height, he made up in circumference. If any degenerate monarch of modern times could be in any way compared with him, I should say the venerable King Cole would be that illustrious potentate.\n\n‘This good king had a queen, who eighteen years before, had had a son, who was called Bladud. He was sent to a preparatory seminary in his father’s dominions until he was ten years old, and was then despatched, in charge of a trusty messenger, to a finishing school at Athens; and as there was no extra charge for remaining during the holidays, and no notice required previous to the removal of a pupil, there he remained for eight long years, at the expiration of which time, the king his father sent the lord chamberlain over, to settle the bill, and to bring him home; which, the lord chamberlain doing, was received with shouts, and pensioned immediately.\n\n‘When King Lud saw the prince his son, and found he had grown up such a fine young man, he perceived what a grand thing it would be to have him married without delay, so that his children might be the means of perpetuating the glorious race of Lud, down to the very latest ages of the world. With this view, he sent a special embassy, composed of great noblemen who had nothing particular to do, and wanted lucrative employment, to a neighbouring king, and demanded his fair daughter in marriage for his son; stating at the same time that he was anxious to be on the most affectionate terms with his brother and friend, but that if they couldn’t agree in arranging this marriage, he should be under the unpleasant necessity of invading his kingdom and putting his eyes out. To this, the other king (who was the weaker of the two) replied that he was very much obliged to his friend and brother for all his goodness and magnanimity, and that his daughter was quite ready to be married, whenever Prince Bladud liked to come and fetch her.\n\n‘This answer no sooner reached Britain, than the whole nation was transported with joy. Nothing was heard, on all sides, but the sounds of feasting and revelry--except the chinking of money as it was paid in by the people to the collector of the royal treasures, to defray the expenses of the happy ceremony. It was upon this occasion that King Lud, seated on the top of his throne in full council, rose, in the exuberance of his feelings, and commanded the lord chief justice to order in the richest wines and the court minstrels--an act of graciousness which has been, through the ignorance of traditionary historians, attributed to King Cole, in those celebrated lines in which his Majesty is represented as\n\nCalling for his pipe, and calling for his pot, And calling for his fiddlers three.\n\nWhich is an obvious injustice to the memory of King Lud, and a dishonest exaltation of the virtues of King Cole.\n\n‘But, in the midst of all this festivity and rejoicing, there was one individual present, who tasted not when the sparkling wines were poured forth, and who danced not, when the minstrels played. This was no other than Prince Bladud himself, in honour of whose happiness a whole people were, at that very moment, straining alike their throats and purse- strings. The truth was, that the prince, forgetting the undoubted right of the minister for foreign affairs to fall in love on his behalf, had, contrary to every precedent of policy and diplomacy, already fallen in love on his own account, and privately contracted himself unto the fair daughter of a noble Athenian.\n\n‘Here we have a striking example of one of the manifold advantages of civilisation and refinement. If the prince had lived in later days, he might at once have married the object of his father’s choice, and then set himself seriously to work, to relieve himself of the burden which rested heavily upon him. He might have endeavoured to break her heart by a systematic course of insult and neglect; or, if the spirit of her sex, and a proud consciousness of her many wrongs had upheld her under this ill-treatment, he might have sought to take her life, and so get rid of her effectually. But neither mode of relief suggested itself to Prince Bladud; so he solicited a private audience, and told his father.\n\n‘It is an old prerogative of kings to govern everything but their passions. King Lud flew into a frightful rage, tossed his crown up to the ceiling, and caught it again--for in those days kings kept their crowns on their heads, and not in the Tower--stamped the ground, rapped his forehead, wondered why his own flesh and blood rebelled against him, and, finally, calling in his guards, ordered the prince away to instant Confinement in a lofty turret; a course of treatment which the kings of old very generally pursued towards their sons, when their matrimonial inclinations did not happen to point to the same quarter as their own.\n\n‘When Prince Bladud had been shut up in the lofty turret for the greater part of a year, with no better prospect before his bodily eyes than a stone wall, or before his mental vision than prolonged imprisonment, he naturally began to ruminate on a plan of escape, which, after months of preparation, he managed to accomplish; considerately leaving his dinner- knife in the heart of his jailer, lest the poor fellow (who had a family) should be considered privy to his flight, and punished accordingly by the infuriated king.\n\n‘The monarch was frantic at the loss of his son. He knew not on whom to vent his grief and wrath, until fortunately bethinking himself of the lord chamberlain who had brought him home, he struck off his pension and his head together.\n\n‘Meanwhile, the young prince, effectually disguised, wandered on foot through his father’s dominions, cheered and supported in all his hardships by sweet thoughts of the Athenian maid, who was the innocent cause of his weary trials. One day he stopped to rest in a country village; and seeing that there were gay dances going forward on the green, and gay faces passing to and fro, ventured to inquire of a reveller who stood near him, the reason for this rejoicing.\n\n‘“Know you not, O stranger,” was the reply, “of the recent proclamation of our gracious king?”\n\n‘“Proclamation! No. What proclamation?” rejoined the prince--for he had travelled along the by and little-frequented ways, and knew nothing of what had passed upon the public roads, such as they were.\n\n‘“Why,” replied the peasant, “the foreign lady that our prince wished to wed, is married to a foreign noble of her own country, and the king proclaims the fact, and a great public festival besides; for now, of course, Prince Bladud will come back and marry the lady his father chose, who they say is as beautiful as the noonday sun. Your health, sir. God save the king!”\n\n‘The prince remained to hear no more. He fled from the spot, and plunged into the thickest recesses of a neighbouring wood. On, on, he wandered, night and day; beneath the blazing sun, and the cold pale moon; through the dry heat of noon, and the damp cold of night; in the gray light of morn, and the red glare of eve. So heedless was he of time or object, that being bound for Athens, he wandered as far out of his way as Bath.\n\n‘There was no city where Bath stands, then. There was no vestige of human habitation, or sign of man’s resort, to bear the name; but there was the same noble country, the same broad expanse of hill and dale, the same beautiful channel stealing on, far away, the same lofty mountains which, like the troubles of life, viewed at a distance, and partially obscured by the bright mist of its morning, lose their ruggedness and asperity, and seem all ease and softness. Moved by the gentle beauty of the scene, the prince sank upon the green turf, and bathed his swollen feet in his tears.\n\n‘“Oh!” said the unhappy Bladud, clasping his hands, and mournfully raising his eyes towards the sky, “would that my wanderings might end here! Would that these grateful tears with which I now mourn hope misplaced, and love despised, might flow in peace for ever!”\n\n‘The wish was heard. It was in the time of the heathen deities, who used occasionally to take people at their words, with a promptness, in some cases, extremely awkward. The ground opened beneath the prince’s feet; he sank into the chasm; and instantaneously it closed upon his head for ever, save where his hot tears welled up through the earth, and where they have continued to gush forth ever since.\n\n‘It is observable that, to this day, large numbers of elderly ladies and gentlemen who have been disappointed in procuring partners, and almost as many young ones who are anxious to obtain them, repair annually to Bath to drink the waters, from which they derive much strength and comfort. This is most complimentary to the virtue of Prince Bladud’s tears, and strongly corroborative of the veracity of this legend.’\n\nMr. Pickwick yawned several times when he had arrived at the end of this little manuscript, carefully refolded, and replaced it in the inkstand drawer, and then, with a countenance expressive of the utmost weariness, lighted his chamber candle, and went upstairs to bed.\n\nHe stopped at Mr. Dowler’s door, according to custom, and knocked to say good-night.\n\n‘Ah!’ said Dowler, ‘going to bed? I wish I was. Dismal night. Windy; isn’t it?’\n\n‘Very,’ said Mr. Pickwick. ‘Good-night.’\n‘Good-night.’\n\nMr. Pickwick went to his bedchamber, and Mr. Dowler resumed his seat before the fire, in fulfilment of his rash promise to sit up till his wife came home.\n\nThere are few things more worrying than sitting up for somebody, especially if that somebody be at a party. You cannot help thinking how quickly the time passes with them, which drags so heavily with you; and the more you think of this, the more your hopes of their speedy arrival decline. Clocks tick so loud, too, when you are sitting up alone, and you seem as if you had an under-garment of cobwebs on. First, something tickles your right knee, and then the same sensation irritates your left. You have no sooner changed your position, than it comes again in the arms; when you have fidgeted your limbs into all sorts of queer shapes, you have a sudden relapse in the nose, which you rub as if to rub it off--as there is no doubt you would, if you could. Eyes, too, are mere personal inconveniences; and the wick of one candle gets an inch and a half long, while you are snuffing the other. These, and various other little nervous annoyances, render sitting up for a length of time after everybody else has gone to bed, anything but a cheerful amusement.\n\nThis was just Mr. Dowler’s opinion, as he sat before the fire, and felt honestly indignant with all the inhuman people at the party who were keeping him up. He was not put into better humour either, by the reflection that he had taken it into his head, early in the evening, to think he had got an ache there, and so stopped at home. At length, after several droppings asleep, and fallings forward towards the bars, and catchings backward soon enough to prevent being branded in the face, Mr. Dowler made up his mind that he would throw himself on the bed in the back room and think--not sleep, of course.\n\n‘I’m a heavy sleeper,’ said Mr. Dowler, as he flung himself on the bed. ‘I must keep awake. I suppose I shall hear a knock here. Yes. I thought so. I can hear the watchman. There he goes. Fainter now, though. A little fainter. He’s turning the corner. Ah!’ When Mr. Dowler arrived at this point, he turned the corner at which he had been long hesitating, and fell fast asleep.\n\nJust as the clock struck three, there was blown into the crescent a sedan-chair with Mrs. Dowler inside, borne by one short, fat chairman, and one long, thin one, who had had much ado to keep their bodies perpendicular: to say nothing of the chair. But on that high ground, and in the crescent, which the wind swept round and round as if it were going to tear the paving stones up, its fury was tremendous. They were very glad to set the chair down, and give a good round loud double-knock at the street door.\n\nThey waited some time, but nobody came.\n\n‘Servants is in the arms o’ Porpus, I think,’ said the short chairman, warming his hands at the attendant link-boy’s torch.\n\n‘I wish he’d give ‘em a squeeze and wake ‘em,’ observed the long one.\n\n‘Knock again, will you, if you please,’ cried Mrs. Dowler from the chair. ‘Knock two or three times, if you please.’\n\nThe short man was quite willing to get the job over, as soon as possible; so he stood on the step, and gave four or five most startling double-knocks, of eight or ten knocks a-piece, while the long man went into the road, and looked up at the windows for a light.\n\nNobody came. It was all as silent and dark as ever.\n\n‘Dear me!’ said Mrs. Dowler. ‘You must knock again, if you please.’\n\nThere ain’t a bell, is there, ma’am?’ said the short chairman.\n\n‘Yes, there is,’ interposed the link-boy, ‘I’ve been a-ringing at it ever so long.’\n\n‘It’s only a handle,’ said Mrs. Dowler, ‘the wire’s broken.’\n\n‘I wish the servants’ heads wos,’ growled the long man.\n\n‘I must trouble you to knock again, if you please,’ said Mrs. Dowler, with the utmost politeness.\n\nThe short man did knock again several times, without producing the smallest effect. The tall man, growing very impatient, then relieved him, and kept on perpetually knocking double-knocks of two loud knocks each, like an insane postman.\n\nAt length Mr. Winkle began to dream that he was at a club, and that the members being very refractory, the chairman was obliged to hammer the table a good deal to preserve order; then he had a confused notion of an auction room where there were no bidders, and the auctioneer was buying everything in; and ultimately he began to think it just within the bounds of possibility that somebody might be knocking at the street door. To make quite certain, however, he remained quiet in bed for ten minutes or so, and listened; and when he had counted two or three-and- thirty knocks, he felt quite satisfied, and gave himself a great deal of credit for being so wakeful.\n\n‘Rap rap-rap rap-rap rap-ra, ra, ra, ra, ra, rap!’ went the knocker.\n\nMr. Winkle jumped out of bed, wondering very much what could possibly be the matter, and hastily putting on his stockings and slippers, folded his dressing-gown round him, lighted a flat candle from the rush-light that was burning in the fireplace, and hurried downstairs.\n\n‘Here’s somebody comin’ at last, ma’am,’ said the short chairman.\n\n‘I wish I wos behind him vith a bradawl,’ muttered the long one.\n\n‘Who’s there?’ cried Mr. Winkle, undoing the chain.\n\n‘Don’t stop to ask questions, cast-iron head,’ replied the long man, with great disgust, taking it for granted that the inquirer was a footman; ‘but open the door.’\n\n‘Come, look sharp, timber eyelids,’ added the other encouragingly.\n\nMr. Winkle, being half asleep, obeyed the command mechanically, opened the door a little, and peeped out. The first thing he saw, was the red glare of the link-boy’s torch. Startled by the sudden fear that the house might be on fire, he hastily threw the door wide open, and holding the candle above his head, stared eagerly before him, not quite certain whether what he saw was a sedan-chair or a fire-engine. At this instant there came a violent gust of wind; the light was blown out; Mr. Winkle felt himself irresistibly impelled on to the steps; and the door blew to, with a loud crash.\n\n‘Well, young man, now you have done it!’ said the short chairman.\n\nMr. Winkle, catching sight of a lady’s face at the window of the sedan, turned hastily round, plied the knocker with all his might and main, and called frantically upon the chairman to take the chair away again.\n\n‘Take it away, take it away,’ cried Mr. Winkle. ‘Here’s somebody coming out of another house; put me into the chair. Hide me! Do something with me!’\n\nAll this time he was shivering with cold; and every time he raised his hand to the knocker, the wind took the dressing-gown in a most unpleasant manner.\n\n‘The people are coming down the crescent now. There are ladies with ‘em; cover me up with something. Stand before me!’ roared Mr. Winkle. But the chairmen were too much exhausted with laughing to afford him the slightest assistance, and the ladies were every moment approaching nearer and nearer.\n\nMr. Winkle gave a last hopeless knock; the ladies were only a few doors off. He threw away the extinguished candle, which, all this time he had held above his head, and fairly bolted into the sedan-chair where Mrs. Dowler was.\n\nNow, Mrs. Craddock had heard the knocking and the voices at last; and, only waiting to put something smarter on her head than her nightcap, ran down into the front drawing-room to make sure that it was the right party. Throwing up the window-sash as Mr. Winkle was rushing into the chair, she no sooner caught sight of what was going forward below, than she raised a vehement and dismal shriek, and implored Mr. Dowler to get up directly, for his wife was running away with another gentleman.\n\nUpon this, Mr. Dowler bounced off the bed as abruptly as an India-rubber ball, and rushing into the front room, arrived at one window just as Mr. Pickwick threw up the other, when the first object that met the gaze of both, was Mr. Winkle bolting into the sedan-chair.\n\n‘Watchman,’ shouted Dowler furiously, ‘stop him--hold him--keep him tight--shut him in, till I come down. I’ll cut his throat--give me a knife--from ear to ear, Mrs. Craddock--I will!’ And breaking from the shrieking landlady, and from Mr. Pickwick, the indignant husband seized a small supper-knife, and tore into the street.\n\nBut Mr. Winkle didn’t wait for him. He no sooner heard the horrible threat of the valorous Dowler, than he bounced out of the sedan, quite as quickly as he had bounced in, and throwing off his slippers into the road, took to his heels and tore round the crescent, hotly pursued by Dowler and the watchman. He kept ahead; the door was open as he came round the second time; he rushed in, slammed it in Dowler’s face, mounted to his bedroom, locked the door, piled a wash-hand-stand, chest of drawers, and a table against it, and packed up a few necessaries ready for flight with the first ray of morning.\n\nDowler came up to the outside of the door; avowed, through the keyhole, his steadfast determination of cutting Mr. Winkle’s throat next day; and, after a great confusion of voices in the drawing-room, amidst which that of Mr. Pickwick was distinctly heard endeavouring to make peace, the inmates dispersed to their several bed-chambers, and all was quiet once more.\n\nIt is not unlikely that the inquiry may be made, where Mr. Weller was, all this time? We will state where he was, in the next chapter.\n\nCHAPTER XXXVII. HONOURABLY ACCOUNTS FOR MR. WELLER’S ABSENCE, BY DESCRIBING A SOIREE TO WHICH HE WAS INVITED AND WENT; ALSO RELATES HOW HE WAS ENTRUSTED BY MR. PICKWICK WITH A PRIVATE MISSION OF DELICACY AND IMPORTANCE\nMr. Weller,’ said Mrs. Craddock, upon the morning of this very eventful day, ‘here’s a letter for you.’\n\n‘Wery odd that,’ said Sam; ‘I’m afeerd there must be somethin’ the matter, for I don’t recollect any gen’l’m’n in my circle of acquaintance as is capable o’ writin’ one.’\n\n‘Perhaps something uncommon has taken place,’ observed Mrs. Craddock.\n\n‘It must be somethin’ wery uncommon indeed, as could perduce a letter out o’ any friend o’ mine,’ replied Sam, shaking his head dubiously; ‘nothin’ less than a nat’ral conwulsion, as the young gen’l’m’n observed ven he wos took with fits. It can’t be from the gov’ner,’ said Sam, looking at the direction. ‘He always prints, I know, ‘cos he learnt writin’ from the large bills in the booking-offices. It’s a wery strange thing now, where this here letter can ha’ come from.’\n\nAs Sam said this, he did what a great many people do when they are uncertain about the writer of a note--looked at the seal, and then at the front, and then at the back, and then at the sides, and then at the superscription; and, as a last resource, thought perhaps he might as well look at the inside, and try to find out from that.\n\n‘It’s wrote on gilt-edged paper,’ said Sam, as he unfolded it, ‘and sealed in bronze vax vith the top of a door key. Now for it.’ And, with a very grave face, Mr. Weller slowly read as follows--\n\n‘A select company of the Bath footmen presents their compliments to Mr. Weller, and requests the pleasure of his company this evening, to a friendly swarry, consisting of a boiled leg of mutton with the usual trimmings. The swarry to be on table at half-past nine o’clock punctually.’\n\nThis was inclosed in another note, which ran thus--\n\n‘Mr. John Smauker, the gentleman who had the pleasure of meeting Mr. Weller at the house of their mutual acquaintance, Mr. Bantam, a few days since, begs to inclose Mr. Weller the herewith invitation. If Mr. Weller will call on Mr. John Smauker at nine o’clock, Mr. John Smauker will have the pleasure of introducing Mr. Weller.\n\n(Signed) ‘John Smauker.’\n\nThe envelope was directed to blank Weller, Esq., at Mr. Pickwick’s; and in a parenthesis, in the left hand corner, were the words ‘airy bell,’ as an instruction to the bearer.\n\n‘Vell,’ said Sam, ‘this is comin’ it rayther powerful, this is. I never heerd a biled leg o’ mutton called a swarry afore. I wonder wot they’d call a roast one.’\n\nHowever, without waiting to debate the point, Sam at once betook himself into the presence of Mr. Pickwick, and requested leave of absence for that evening, which was readily granted. With this permission and the street-door key, Sam Weller issued forth a little before the appointed time, and strolled leisurely towards Queen Square, which he no sooner gained than he had the satisfaction of beholding Mr. John Smauker leaning his powdered head against a lamp-post at a short distance off, smoking a cigar through an amber tube.\n\n‘How do you do, Mr. Weller?’ said Mr. John Smauker, raising his hat gracefully with one hand, while he gently waved the other in a condescending manner. ‘How do you do, Sir?’\n\n‘Why, reasonably conwalessent,’ replied Sam. ‘How do you find yourself, my dear feller?’\n\n‘Only so so,’ said Mr. John Smauker.\n\n‘Ah, you’ve been a-workin’ too hard,’ observed Sam. ‘I was fearful you would; it won’t do, you know; you must not give way to that ‘ere uncompromisin’ spirit o’ yourn.’\n\n‘It’s not so much that, Mr. Weller,’ replied Mr. John Smauker, ‘as bad wine; I’m afraid I’ve been dissipating.’\n\n‘Oh! that’s it, is it?’ said Sam; ‘that’s a wery bad complaint, that.’\n\n‘And yet the temptation, you see, Mr. Weller,’ observed Mr. John Smauker.\n\n‘Ah, to be sure,’ said Sam.\n\n‘Plunged into the very vortex of society, you know, Mr. Weller,’ said Mr. John Smauker, with a sigh.\n\n‘Dreadful, indeed!’ rejoined Sam.\n\n‘But it’s always the way,’ said Mr. John Smauker; ‘if your destiny leads you into public life, and public station, you must expect to be subjected to temptations which other people is free from, Mr. Weller.’\n\n‘Precisely what my uncle said, ven he vent into the public line,’ remarked Sam, ‘and wery right the old gen’l’m’n wos, for he drank hisself to death in somethin’ less than a quarter.’\n\nMr. John Smauker looked deeply indignant at any parallel being drawn between himself and the deceased gentleman in question; but, as Sam’s face was in the most immovable state of calmness, he thought better of it, and looked affable again.\n\n‘Perhaps we had better be walking,’ said Mr. Smauker, consulting a copper timepiece which dwelt at the bottom of a deep watch-pocket, and was raised to the surface by means of a black string, with a copper key at the other end.\n\n‘P’raps we had,’ replied Sam, ‘or they’ll overdo the swarry, and that’ll spile it.’\n\n‘Have you drank the waters, Mr. Weller?’ inquired his companion, as they walked towards High Street.\n\n‘Once,’ replied Sam.\n\n‘What did you think of ‘em, Sir?’\n\n‘I thought they was particklery unpleasant,’ replied Sam.\n\n‘Ah,’ said Mr. John Smauker, ‘you disliked the killibeate taste, perhaps?’\n\n‘I don’t know much about that ‘ere,’ said Sam. ‘I thought they’d a wery strong flavour o’ warm flat irons.’\n\n‘That is the killibeate, Mr. Weller,’ observed Mr. John Smauker contemptuously.\n\n‘Well, if it is, it’s a wery inexpressive word, that’s all,’ said Sam. ‘It may be, but I ain’t much in the chimical line myself, so I can’t say.’ And here, to the great horror of Mr. John Smauker, Sam Weller began to whistle.\n\n‘I beg your pardon, Mr. Weller,’ said Mr. John Smauker, agonised at the exceeding ungenteel sound, ‘will you take my arm?’\n\n‘Thank’ee, you’re wery good, but I won’t deprive you of it,’ replied Sam. ‘I’ve rayther a way o’ putting my hands in my pockets, if it’s all the same to you.’ As Sam said this, he suited the action to the word, and whistled far louder than before.\n\n‘This way,’ said his new friend, apparently much relieved as they turned down a by-street; ‘we shall soon be there.’\n\n‘Shall we?’ said Sam, quite unmoved by the announcement of his close vicinity to the select footmen of Bath.\n\n‘Yes,’ said Mr. John Smauker. ‘Don’t be alarmed, Mr. Weller.’\n\n‘Oh, no,’ said Sam.\n\n‘You’ll see some very handsome uniforms, Mr. Weller,’ continued Mr. John Smauker; ‘and perhaps you’ll find some of the gentlemen rather high at first, you know, but they’ll soon come round.’\n\n‘That’s wery kind on ‘em,’ replied Sam.\n\n‘And you know,’ resumed Mr. John Smauker, with an air of sublime protection--‘you know, as you’re a stranger, perhaps, they’ll be rather hard upon you at first.’\n\n‘They won’t be wery cruel, though, will they?’ inquired Sam.\n\n‘No, no,’ replied Mr. John Smauker, pulling forth the fox’s head, and taking a gentlemanly pinch. ‘There are some funny dogs among us, and they will have their joke, you know; but you mustn’t mind ‘em, you mustn’t mind ‘em.’\n\n‘I’ll try and bear up agin such a reg’lar knock down o’ talent,’ replied Sam.\n\n‘That’s right,’ said Mr. John Smauker, putting forth his fox’s head, and elevating his own; ‘I’ll stand by you.’\n\nBy this time they had reached a small greengrocer’s shop, which Mr. John Smauker entered, followed by Sam, who, the moment he got behind him, relapsed into a series of the very broadest and most unmitigated grins, and manifested other demonstrations of being in a highly enviable state of inward merriment.\n\nCrossing the greengrocer’s shop, and putting their hats on the stairs in the little passage behind it, they walked into a small parlour; and here the full splendour of the scene burst upon Mr. Weller’s view.\n\nA couple of tables were put together in the middle of the parlour, covered with three or four cloths of different ages and dates of washing, arranged to look as much like one as the circumstances of the case would allow. Upon these were laid knives and forks for six or eight people. Some of the knife handles were green, others red, and a few yellow; and as all the forks were black, the combination of colours was exceedingly striking. Plates for a corresponding number of guests were warming behind the fender; and the guests themselves were warming before it: the chief and most important of whom appeared to be a stoutish gentleman in a bright crimson coat with long tails, vividly red breeches, and a cocked hat, who was standing with his back to the fire, and had apparently just entered, for besides retaining his cocked hat on his head, he carried in his hand a high stick, such as gentlemen of his profession usually elevate in a sloping position over the roofs of carriages.\n\n‘Smauker, my lad, your fin,’ said the gentleman with the cocked hat.\n\nMr. Smauker dovetailed the top joint of his right-hand little finger into that of the gentleman with the cocked hat, and said he was charmed to see him looking so well.\n\n‘Well, they tell me I am looking pretty blooming,’ said the man with the cocked hat, ‘and it’s a wonder, too. I’ve been following our old woman about, two hours a day, for the last fortnight; and if a constant contemplation of the manner in which she hooks-and-eyes that infernal lavender-coloured old gown of hers behind, isn’t enough to throw anybody into a low state of despondency for life, stop my quarter’s salary.’\n\nAt this, the assembled selections laughed very heartily; and one gentleman in a yellow waistcoat, with a coach-trimming border, whispered a neighbour in green-foil smalls, that Tuckle was in spirits to-night.\n\n‘By the bye,’ said Mr. Tuckle, ‘Smauker, my boy, you--’ The remainder of the sentence was forwarded into Mr. John Smauker’s ear, by whisper.\n\n‘Oh, dear me, I quite forgot,’ said Mr. John Smauker. ‘Gentlemen, my friend Mr. Weller.’\n\n‘Sorry to keep the fire off you, Weller,’ said Mr. Tuckle, with a familiar nod. ‘Hope you’re not cold, Weller.’\n\n‘Not by no means, Blazes,’ replied Sam. ‘It ‘ud be a wery chilly subject as felt cold wen you stood opposite. You’d save coals if they put you behind the fender in the waitin’-room at a public office, you would.’\n\nAs this retort appeared to convey rather a personal allusion to Mr. Tuckle’s crimson livery, that gentleman looked majestic for a few seconds, but gradually edging away from the fire, broke into a forced smile, and said it wasn’t bad.\n\n‘Wery much obliged for your good opinion, sir,’ replied Sam. ‘We shall get on by degrees, I des-say. We’ll try a better one by and bye.’\n\nAt this point the conversation was interrupted by the arrival of a gentleman in orange-coloured plush, accompanied by another selection in purple cloth, with a great extent of stocking. The new-comers having been welcomed by the old ones, Mr. Tuckle put the question that supper be ordered in, which was carried unanimously.\n\nThe greengrocer and his wife then arranged upon the table a boiled leg of mutton, hot, with caper sauce, turnips, and potatoes. Mr. Tuckle took the chair, and was supported at the other end of the board by the gentleman in orange plush. The greengrocer put on a pair of wash-leather gloves to hand the plates with, and stationed himself behind Mr. Tuckle’s chair.\n\n‘Harris,’ said Mr. Tuckle, in a commanding tone.\n\n‘Sir,’ said the greengrocer.\n\n‘Have you got your gloves on?’\n\nYes, Sir.’\n\n‘Then take the kiver off.’\n\n‘Yes, Sir.’\n\nThe greengrocer did as he was told, with a show of great humility, and obsequiously handed Mr. Tuckle the carving-knife; in doing which, he accidentally gaped.\n\n‘What do you mean by that, Sir?’ said Mr. Tuckle, with great asperity.\n\n‘I beg your pardon, Sir,’ replied the crestfallen greengrocer, ‘I didn’t mean to do it, Sir; I was up very late last night, Sir.’\n\n‘I tell you what my opinion of you is, Harris,’ said Mr. Tuckle, with a most impressive air, ‘you’re a wulgar beast.’\n\n‘I hope, gentlemen,’ said Harris, ‘that you won’t be severe with me, gentlemen. I am very much obliged to you indeed, gentlemen, for your patronage, and also for your recommendations, gentlemen, whenever additional assistance in waiting is required. I hope, gentlemen, I give satisfaction.’\n\n‘No, you don’t, Sir,’ said Mr. Tuckle. ‘Very far from it, Sir.’\n\n‘We consider you an inattentive reskel,’ said the gentleman in the orange plush.\n\n‘And a low thief,’ added the gentleman in the green-foil smalls.\n\n‘And an unreclaimable blaygaird,’ added the gentleman in purple.\n\nThe poor greengrocer bowed very humbly while these little epithets were bestowed upon him, in the true spirit of the very smallest tyranny; and when everybody had said something to show his superiority, Mr. Tuckle proceeded to carve the leg of mutton, and to help the company.\n\nThis important business of the evening had hardly commenced, when the door was thrown briskly open, and another gentleman in a light-blue suit, and leaden buttons, made his appearance.\n\n‘Against the rules,’ said Mr. Tuckle. ‘Too late, too late.’\n\n‘No, no; positively I couldn’t help it,’ said the gentleman in blue. ‘I appeal to the company. An affair of gallantry now, an appointment at the theayter.’\n\n‘Oh, that indeed,’ said the gentleman in the orange plush.\n\n‘Yes; raly now, honour bright,’ said the man in blue. ‘I made a promese to fetch our youngest daughter at half-past ten, and she is such an uncauminly fine gal, that I raly hadn’t the ‘art to disappint her. No offence to the present company, Sir, but a petticut, sir--a petticut, Sir, is irrevokeable.’\n\n‘I begin to suspect there’s something in that quarter,’ said Tuckle, as the new-comer took his seat next Sam, ‘I’ve remarked, once or twice, that she leans very heavy on your shoulder when she gets in and out of the carriage.’\n\n‘Oh, raly, raly, Tuckle, you shouldn’t,’ said the man in blue. ‘It’s not fair. I may have said to one or two friends that she wos a very divine creechure, and had refused one or two offers without any hobvus cause, but--no, no, no, indeed, Tuckle--before strangers, too--it’s not right-- you shouldn’t. Delicacy, my dear friend, delicacy!’ And the man in blue, pulling up his neckerchief, and adjusting his coat cuffs, nodded and frowned as if there were more behind, which he could say if he liked, but was bound in honour to suppress.\n\nThe man in blue being a light-haired, stiff-necked, free and easy sort of footman, with a swaggering air and pert face, had attracted Mr. Weller’s special attention at first, but when he began to come out in this way, Sam felt more than ever disposed to cultivate his acquaintance; so he launched himself into the conversation at once, with characteristic independence.\n\n‘Your health, Sir,’ said Sam. ‘I like your conversation much. I think it’s wery pretty.’\n\nAt this the man in blue smiled, as if it were a compliment he was well used to; but looked approvingly on Sam at the same time, and said he hoped he should be better acquainted with him, for without any flattery at all he seemed to have the makings of a very nice fellow about him, and to be just the man after his own heart.\n\n‘You’re wery good, sir,’ said Sam. ‘What a lucky feller you are!’\n\n‘How do you mean?’ inquired the gentleman in blue.\n\n‘That ‘ere young lady,’ replied Sam. ‘She knows wot’s wot, she does. Ah! I see.’ Mr. Weller closed one eye, and shook his head from side to side, in a manner which was highly gratifying to the personal vanity of the gentleman in blue.\n\n‘I’m afraid you’re a cunning fellow, Mr. Weller,’ said that individual.\n\n‘No, no,’ said Sam. ‘I leave all that ‘ere to you. It’s a great deal more in your way than mine, as the gen’l’m’n on the right side o’ the garden vall said to the man on the wrong un, ven the mad bull vos a- comin’ up the lane.’\n\n‘Well, well, Mr. Weller,’ said the gentleman in blue, ‘I think she has remarked my air and manner, Mr. Weller.’\n\n‘I should think she couldn’t wery well be off o’ that,’ said Sam.\n\n‘Have you any little thing of that kind in hand, sir?’ inquired the favoured gentleman in blue, drawing a toothpick from his waistcoat pocket.\n\n‘Not exactly,’ said Sam. ‘There’s no daughters at my place, else o’ course I should ha’ made up to vun on ‘em. As it is, I don’t think I can do with anythin’ under a female markis. I might keep up with a young ‘ooman o’ large property as hadn’t a title, if she made wery fierce love to me. Not else.’\n\n‘Of course not, Mr. Weller,’ said the gentleman in blue, ‘one can’t be troubled, you know; and we know, Mr. Weller--we, who are men of the world--that a good uniform must work its way with the women, sooner or later. In fact, that’s the only thing, between you and me, that makes the service worth entering into.’\n\n‘Just so,’ said Sam. ‘That’s it, o’ course.’\n\nWhen this confidential dialogue had gone thus far, glasses were placed round, and every gentleman ordered what he liked best, before the public-house shut up. The gentleman in blue, and the man in orange, who were the chief exquisites of the party, ordered ‘cold shrub and water,’ but with the others, gin-and-water, sweet, appeared to be the favourite beverage. Sam called the greengrocer a ‘desp’rate willin,’ and ordered a large bowl of punch--two circumstances which seemed to raise him very much in the opinion of the selections.\n\n‘Gentlemen,’ said the man in blue, with an air of the most consummate dandyism, ‘I’ll give you the ladies; come.’\n\n‘Hear, hear!’ said Sam. ‘The young mississes.’\n\nHere there was a loud cry of ‘Order,’ and Mr. John Smauker, as the gentleman who had introduced Mr. Weller into that company, begged to inform him that the word he had just made use of, was unparliamentary.\n\n‘Which word was that ‘ere, Sir?’ inquired Sam.\n\n‘Mississes, Sir,’ replied Mr. John Smauker, with an alarming frown. ‘We don’t recognise such distinctions here.’\n\n‘Oh, wery good,’ said Sam; ‘then I’ll amend the obserwation and call ‘em the dear creeturs, if Blazes vill allow me.’\n\nSome doubt appeared to exist in the mind of the gentleman in the green- foil smalls, whether the chairman could be legally appealed to, as ‘Blazes,’ but as the company seemed more disposed to stand upon their own rights than his, the question was not raised. The man with the cocked hat breathed short, and looked long at Sam, but apparently thought it as well to say nothing, in case he should get the worst of it. After a short silence, a gentleman in an embroidered coat reaching down to his heels, and a waistcoat of the same which kept one half of his legs warm, stirred his gin-and-water with great energy, and putting himself upon his feet, all at once by a violent effort, said he was desirous of offering a few remarks to the company, whereupon the person in the cocked hat had no doubt that the company would be very happy to hear any remarks that the man in the long coat might wish to offer.\n\n‘I feel a great delicacy, gentlemen, in coming for’ard,’ said the man in the long coat, ‘having the misforchune to be a coachman, and being only admitted as a honorary member of these agreeable swarrys, but I do feel myself bound, gentlemen--drove into a corner, if I may use the expression--to make known an afflicting circumstance which has come to my knowledge; which has happened I may say within the soap of my everyday contemplation. Gentlemen, our friend Mr. Whiffers (everybody looked at the individual in orange), our friend Mr. Whiffers has resigned.’\n\nUniversal astonishment fell upon the hearers. Each gentleman looked in his neighbour’s face, and then transferred his glance to the upstanding coachman.\n\n‘You may well be sapparised, gentlemen,’ said the coachman. ‘I will not wenchure to state the reasons of this irrepairabel loss to the service, but I will beg Mr. Whiffers to state them himself, for the improvement and imitation of his admiring friends.’\n\nThe suggestion being loudly approved of, Mr. Whiffers explained. He said he certainly could have wished to have continued to hold the appointment he had just resigned. The uniform was extremely rich and expensive, the females of the family was most agreeable, and the duties of the situation was not, he was bound to say, too heavy; the principal service that was required of him, being, that he should look out of the hall window as much as possible, in company with another gentleman, who had also resigned. He could have wished to have spared that company the painful and disgusting detail on which he was about to enter, but as the explanation had been demanded of him, he had no alternative but to state, boldly and distinctly, that he had been required to eat cold meat.\n\nIt is impossible to conceive the disgust which this avowal awakened in the bosoms of the hearers. Loud cries of ‘Shame,’ mingled with groans and hisses, prevailed for a quarter of an hour.\n\nMr. Whiffers then added that he feared a portion of this outrage might be traced to his own forbearing and accommodating disposition. He had a distinct recollection of having once consented to eat salt butter, and he had, moreover, on an occasion of sudden sickness in the house, so far forgotten himself as to carry a coal-scuttle up to the second floor. He trusted he had not lowered himself in the good opinion of his friends by this frank confession of his faults; and he hoped the promptness with which he had resented the last unmanly outrage on his feelings, to which he had referred, would reinstate him in their good opinion, if he had.\n\nMr. Whiffers’s address was responded to, with a shout of admiration, and the health of the interesting martyr was drunk in a most enthusiastic manner; for this, the martyr returned thanks, and proposed their visitor, Mr. Weller--a gentleman whom he had not the pleasure of an intimate acquaintance with, but who was the friend of Mr. John Smauker, which was a sufficient letter of recommendation to any society of gentlemen whatever, or wherever. On this account, he should have been disposed to have given Mr. Weller’s health with all the honours, if his friends had been drinking wine; but as they were taking spirits by way of a change, and as it might be inconvenient to empty a tumbler at every toast, he should propose that the honours be understood.\n\nAt the conclusion of this speech, everybody took a sip in honour of Sam; and Sam having ladled out, and drunk, two full glasses of punch in honour of himself, returned thanks in a neat speech.\n\n‘Wery much obliged to you, old fellers,’ said Sam, ladling away at the punch in the most unembarrassed manner possible, ‘for this here compliment; which, comin’ from sich a quarter, is wery overvelmin’. I’ve heered a good deal on you as a body, but I will say, that I never thought you was sich uncommon nice men as I find you air. I only hope you’ll take care o’ yourselves, and not compromise nothin’ o’ your dignity, which is a wery charmin’ thing to see, when one’s out a- walkin’, and has always made me wery happy to look at, ever since I was a boy about half as high as the brass-headed stick o’ my wery respectable friend, Blazes, there. As to the wictim of oppression in the suit o’ brimstone, all I can say of him, is, that I hope he’ll get jist as good a berth as he deserves; in vitch case it’s wery little cold swarry as ever he’ll be troubled with agin.’\n\nHere Sam sat down with a pleasant smile, and his speech having been vociferously applauded, the company broke up.\n\n‘Wy, you don’t mean to say you’re a-goin’ old feller?’ said Sam Weller to his friend, Mr. John Smauker.\n\n‘I must, indeed,’ said Mr. Smauker; ‘I promised Bantam.’\n\n‘Oh, wery well,’ said Sam; ‘that’s another thing. P’raps he’d resign if you disappinted him. You ain’t a-goin’, Blazes?’\n\n‘Yes, I am,’ said the man with the cocked hat.\n\n‘Wot, and leave three-quarters of a bowl of punch behind you!’ said Sam; ‘nonsense, set down agin.’\n\nMr. Tuckle was not proof against this invitation. He laid aside the cocked hat and stick which he had just taken up, and said he would have one glass, for good fellowship’s sake.\n\nAs the gentleman in blue went home the same way as Mr. Tuckle, he was prevailed upon to stop too. When the punch was about half gone, Sam ordered in some oysters from the green-grocer’s shop; and the effect of both was so extremely exhilarating, that Mr. Tuckle, dressed out with the cocked hat and stick, danced the frog hornpipe among the shells on the table, while the gentleman in blue played an accompaniment upon an ingenious musical instrument formed of a hair-comb upon a curl-paper. At last, when the punch was all gone, and the night nearly so, they sallied forth to see each other home. Mr. Tuckle no sooner got into the open air, than he was seized with a sudden desire to lie on the curbstone; Sam thought it would be a pity to contradict him, and so let him have his own way. As the cocked hat would have been spoiled if left there, Sam very considerately flattened it down on the head of the gentleman in blue, and putting the big stick in his hand, propped him up against his own street-door, rang the bell, and walked quietly home.\n\nAt a much earlier hour next morning than his usual time of rising, Mr. Pickwick walked downstairs completely dressed, and rang the bell.\n\n‘Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick, when Mr. Weller appeared in reply to the summons, ‘shut the door.’\n\nMr. Weller did so.\n\n‘There was an unfortunate occurrence here, last night, Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘which gave Mr. Winkle some cause to apprehend violence from Mr. Dowler.’\n\n‘So I’ve heerd from the old lady downstairs, Sir,’ replied Sam.\n\n‘And I’m sorry to say, Sam,’ continued Mr. Pickwick, with a most perplexed countenance, ‘that in dread of this violence, Mr. Winkle has gone away.’\n\n‘Gone avay!’ said Sam.\n\n‘Left the house early this morning, without the slightest previous communication with me,’ replied Mr. Pickwick. ‘And is gone, I know not where.’\n\n‘He should ha’ stopped and fought it out, Sir,’ replied Sam contemptuously. ‘It wouldn’t take much to settle that ‘ere Dowler, Sir.’\n\n‘Well, Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘I may have my doubts of his great bravery and determination also. But however that may be, Mr. Winkle is gone. He must be found, Sam. Found and brought back to me.’\n\nAnd s’pose he won’t come back, Sir?’ said Sam.\n\n‘He must be made, Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Who’s to do it, Sir?’ inquired Sam, with a smile.\n\n‘You,’ replied Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Wery good, Sir.’\n\nWith these words Mr. Weller left the room, and immediately afterwards was heard to shut the street door. In two hours’ time he returned with so much coolness as if he had been despatched on the most ordinary message possible, and brought the information that an individual, in every respect answering Mr. Winkle’s description, had gone over to Bristol that morning, by the branch coach from the Royal Hotel.\n\n‘Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick, grasping his hand, ‘you’re a capital fellow; an invaluable fellow. You must follow him, Sam.’\n\n‘Cert’nly, Sir,’ replied Mr. Weller.\n\n‘The instant you discover him, write to me immediately, Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick. ‘If he attempts to run away from you, knock him down, or lock him up. You have my full authority, Sam.’\n\n‘I’ll be wery careful, sir,’ rejoined Sam.\n\n‘You’ll tell him,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘that I am highly excited, highly displeased, and naturally indignant, at the very extraordinary course he has thought proper to pursue.’\n\n‘I will, Sir,’ replied Sam.\n\n‘You’ll tell him,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘that if he does not come back to this very house, with you, he will come back with me, for I will come and fetch him.’\n\n‘I’ll mention that ‘ere, Sir,’ rejoined Sam.\n\n‘You think you can find him, Sam?’ said Mr. Pickwick, looking earnestly in his face.\n\n‘Oh, I’ll find him if he’s anyvere,’ rejoined Sam, with great confidence.\n\n‘Very well,’ said Mr. Pickwick. ‘Then the sooner you go the better.’\n\nWith these instructions, Mr. Pickwick placed a sum of money in the hands of his faithful servitor, and ordered him to start for Bristol immediately, in pursuit of the fugitive.\n\nSam put a few necessaries in a carpet-bag, and was ready for starting. He stopped when he had got to the end of the passage, and walking quietly back, thrust his head in at the parlour door.\n\n‘Sir,’ whispered Sam.\n\n‘Well, Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘I fully understands my instructions, do I, Sir?’ inquired Sam.\n\n‘I hope so,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘It’s reg’larly understood about the knockin’ down, is it, Sir?’ inquired Sam.\n\n‘Perfectly,’ replied Pickwick. ‘Thoroughly. Do what you think necessary. You have my orders.’\n\nSam gave a nod of intelligence, and withdrawing his head from the door, set forth on his pilgrimage with a light heart.\n\nCHAPTER XXXVIII. HOW MR. WINKLE, WHEN HE STEPPED OUT OF THE FRYING-PAN, WALKED GENTLY AND COMFORTABLY INTO THE FIRE\nThe ill-starred gentleman who had been the unfortunate cause of the unusual noise and disturbance which alarmed the inhabitants of the Royal Crescent in manner and form already described, after passing a night of great confusion and anxiety, left the roof beneath which his friends still slumbered, bound he knew not whither. The excellent and considerate feelings which prompted Mr. Winkle to take this step can never be too highly appreciated or too warmly extolled. ‘If,’ reasoned Mr. Winkle with himself--‘if this Dowler attempts (as I have no doubt he will) to carry into execution his threat of personal violence against myself, it will be incumbent on me to call him out. He has a wife; that wife is attached to, and dependent on him. Heavens! If I should kill him in the blindness of my wrath, what would be my feelings ever afterwards!’ This painful consideration operated so powerfully on the feelings of the humane young man, as to cause his knees to knock together, and his countenance to exhibit alarming manifestations of inward emotion. Impelled by such reflections, he grasped his carpet-bag, and creeping stealthily downstairs, shut the detestable street door with as little noise as possible, and walked off. Bending his steps towards the Royal Hotel, he found a coach on the point of starting for Bristol, and, thinking Bristol as good a place for his purpose as any other he could go to, he mounted the box, and reached his place of destination in such time as the pair of horses, who went the whole stage and back again, twice a day or more, could be reasonably supposed to arrive there.\n\nHe took up his quarters at the Bush, and designing to postpone any communication by letter with Mr. Pickwick until it was probable that Mr. Dowler’s wrath might have in some degree evaporated, walked forth to view the city, which struck him as being a shade more dirty than any place he had ever seen. Having inspected the docks and shipping, and viewed the cathedral, he inquired his way to Clifton, and being directed thither, took the route which was pointed out to him. But as the pavements of Bristol are not the widest or cleanest upon earth, so its streets are not altogether the straightest or least intricate; and Mr. Winkle, being greatly puzzled by their manifold windings and twistings, looked about him for a decent shop in which he could apply afresh for counsel and instruction.\n\nHis eye fell upon a newly-painted tenement which had been recently converted into something between a shop and a private house, and which a red lamp, projecting over the fanlight of the street door, would have sufficiently announced as the residence of a medical practitioner, even if the word ‘Surgery’ had not been inscribed in golden characters on a wainscot ground, above the window of what, in times bygone, had been the front parlour. Thinking this an eligible place wherein to make his inquiries, Mr. Winkle stepped into the little shop where the gilt- labelled drawers and bottles were; and finding nobody there, knocked with a half-crown on the counter, to attract the attention of anybody who might happen to be in the back parlour, which he judged to be the innermost and peculiar sanctum of the establishment, from the repetition of the word surgery on the door--painted in white letters this time, by way of taking off the monotony.\n\nAt the first knock, a sound, as of persons fencing with fire-irons, which had until now been very audible, suddenly ceased; at the second, a studious-looking young gentleman in green spectacles, with a very large book in his hand, glided quietly into the shop, and stepping behind the counter, requested to know the visitor’s pleasure.\n\n‘I am sorry to trouble you, Sir,’ said Mr. Winkle, ‘but will you have the goodness to direct me to--’\n\n‘Ha! ha! ha!’ roared the studious young gentleman, throwing the large book up into the air, and catching it with great dexterity at the very moment when it threatened to smash to atoms all the bottles on the counter. ‘Here’s a start!’\n\nThere was, without doubt; for Mr. Winkle was so very much astonished at the extraordinary behaviour of the medical gentleman, that he involuntarily retreated towards the door, and looked very much disturbed at his strange reception.\n\n‘What, don’t you know me?’ said the medical gentleman.\n\nMr. Winkle murmured, in reply, that he had not that pleasure.\n\n‘Why, then,’ said the medical gentleman, ‘there are hopes for me yet; I may attend half the old women in Bristol, if I’ve decent luck. Get out, you mouldy old villain, get out!’ With this adjuration, which was addressed to the large book, the medical gentleman kicked the volume with remarkable agility to the farther end of the shop, and, pulling off his green spectacles, grinned the identical grin of Robert Sawyer, Esquire, formerly of Guy’s Hospital in the Borough, with a private residence in Lant Street.\n\n‘You don’t mean to say you weren’t down upon me?’ said Mr. Bob Sawyer, shaking Mr. Winkle’s hand with friendly warmth.\n\n‘Upon my word I was not,’ replied Mr. Winkle, returning his pressure.\n\n‘I wonder you didn’t see the name,’ said Bob Sawyer, calling his friend’s attention to the outer door, on which, in the same white paint, were traced the words ‘Sawyer, late Nockemorf.’\n\n‘It never caught my eye,’ returned Mr. Winkle.\n\n‘Lord, if I had known who you were, I should have rushed out, and caught you in my arms,’ said Bob Sawyer; ‘but upon my life, I thought you were the King’s-taxes.’\n\n‘No!’ said Mr. Winkle.\n\n‘I did, indeed,’ responded Bob Sawyer, ‘and I was just going to say that I wasn’t at home, but if you’d leave a message I’d be sure to give it to myself; for he don’t know me; no more does the Lighting and Paving. I think the Church-rates guesses who I am, and I know the Water-works does, because I drew a tooth of his when I first came down here. But come in, come in!’ Chattering in this way, Mr. Bob Sawyer pushed Mr. Winkle into the back room, where, amusing himself by boring little circular caverns in the chimney-piece with a red-hot poker, sat no less a person than Mr. Benjamin Allen.\n\n‘Well!’ said Mr. Winkle. ‘This is indeed a pleasure I did not expect. What a very nice place you have here!’\n\n‘Pretty well, pretty well,’ replied Bob Sawyer. ‘I passed, soon after that precious party, and my friends came down with the needful for this business; so I put on a black suit of clothes, and a pair of spectacles, and came here to look as solemn as I could.’\n\n‘And a very snug little business you have, no doubt?’ said Mr. Winkle knowingly.\n\n‘Very,’ replied Bob Sawyer. ‘So snug, that at the end of a few years you might put all the profits in a wine-glass, and cover ‘em over with a gooseberry leaf.’\n\nYou cannot surely mean that?’ said Mr. Winkle. ‘The stock itself--’\n\nDummies, my dear boy,’ said Bob Sawyer; ‘half the drawers have nothing in ‘em, and the other half don’t open.’\n\n‘Nonsense!’ said Mr. Winkle.\n\n‘Fact--honour!’ returned Bob Sawyer, stepping out into the shop, and demonstrating the veracity of the assertion by divers hard pulls at the little gilt knobs on the counterfeit drawers. ‘Hardly anything real in the shop but the leeches, and they are second-hand.’\n\n‘I shouldn’t have thought it!’ exclaimed Mr. Winkle, much surprised.\n\n‘I hope not,’ replied Bob Sawyer, ‘else where’s the use of appearances, eh? But what will you take? Do as we do? That’s right. Ben, my fine fellow, put your hand into the cupboard, and bring out the patent digester.’\n\nMr. Benjamin Allen smiled his readiness, and produced from the closet at his elbow a black bottle half full of brandy.\n\n‘You don’t take water, of course?’ said Bob Sawyer.\n\n‘Thank you,’ replied Mr. Winkle. ‘It’s rather early. I should like to qualify it, if you have no objection.’\n\n‘None in the least, if you can reconcile it to your conscience,’ replied Bob Sawyer, tossing off, as he spoke, a glass of the liquor with great relish. ‘Ben, the pipkin!’\n\nMr. Benjamin Allen drew forth, from the same hiding-place, a small brass pipkin, which Bob Sawyer observed he prided himself upon, particularly because it looked so business-like. The water in the professional pipkin having been made to boil, in course of time, by various little shovelfuls of coal, which Mr. Bob Sawyer took out of a practicable window-seat, labelled ‘Soda Water,’ Mr. Winkle adulterated his brandy; and the conversation was becoming general, when it was interrupted by the entrance into the shop of a boy, in a sober gray livery and a gold- laced hat, with a small covered basket under his arm, whom Mr. Bob Sawyer immediately hailed with, ‘Tom, you vagabond, come here.’\n\nThe boy presented himself accordingly.\n\n‘You’ve been stopping to “over” all the posts in Bristol, you idle young scamp!’ said Mr. Bob Sawyer.\n\n‘No, sir, I haven’t,’ replied the boy.\n\n‘You had better not!’ said Mr. Bob Sawyer, with a threatening aspect. ‘Who do you suppose will ever employ a professional man, when they see his boy playing at marbles in the gutter, or flying the garter in the horse-road? Have you no feeling for your profession, you groveller? Did you leave all the medicine?’\n\nYes, Sir.’\n\n‘The powders for the child, at the large house with the new family, and the pills to be taken four times a day at the ill-tempered old gentleman’s with the gouty leg?’\n\n‘Yes, sir.’\n\n‘Then shut the door, and mind the shop.’\n\n‘Come,’ said Mr. Winkle, as the boy retired, ‘things are not quite so bad as you would have me believe, either. There is some medicine to be sent out.’\n\nMr. Bob Sawyer peeped into the shop to see that no stranger was within hearing, and leaning forward to Mr. Winkle, said, in a low tone--\n\n‘He leaves it all at the wrong houses.’\n\nMr. Winkle looked perplexed, and Bob Sawyer and his friend laughed.\n\n‘Don’t you see?’ said Bob. ‘He goes up to a house, rings the area bell, pokes a packet of medicine without a direction into the servant’s hand, and walks off. Servant takes it into the dining-parlour; master opens it, and reads the label: “Draught to be taken at bedtime--pills as before--lotion as usual--the powder. From Sawyer’s, late Nockemorf’s. Physicians’ prescriptions carefully prepared,” and all the rest of it. Shows it to his wife--she reads the label; it goes down to the servants- -they read the label. Next day, boy calls: “Very sorry--his mistake-- immense business--great many parcels to deliver--Mr. Sawyer’s compliments--late Nockemorf.” The name gets known, and that’s the thing, my boy, in the medical way. Bless your heart, old fellow, it’s better than all the advertising in the world. We have got one four-ounce bottle that’s been to half the houses in Bristol, and hasn’t done yet.’\n\n‘Dear me, I see,’ observed Mr. Winkle; ‘what an excellent plan!’\n\n‘Oh, Ben and I have hit upon a dozen such,’ replied Bob Sawyer, with great glee. ‘The lamplighter has eighteenpence a week to pull the night- bell for ten minutes every time he comes round; and my boy always rushes into the church just before the psalms, when the people have got nothing to do but look about ‘em, and calls me out, with horror and dismay depicted on his countenance. “Bless my soul,” everybody says, “somebody taken suddenly ill! Sawyer, late Nockemorf, sent for. What a business that young man has!”’\n\nAt the termination of this disclosure of some of the mysteries of medicine, Mr. Bob Sawyer and his friend, Ben Allen, threw themselves back in their respective chairs, and laughed boisterously. When they had enjoyed the joke to their heart’s content, the discourse changed to topics in which Mr. Winkle was more immediately interested.\n\nWe think we have hinted elsewhere, that Mr. Benjamin Allen had a way of becoming sentimental after brandy. The case is not a peculiar one, as we ourself can testify, having, on a few occasions, had to deal with patients who have been afflicted in a similar manner. At this precise period of his existence, Mr. Benjamin Allen had perhaps a greater predisposition to maudlinism than he had ever known before; the cause of which malady was briefly this. He had been staying nearly three weeks with Mr. Bob Sawyer; Mr. Bob Sawyer was not remarkable for temperance, nor was Mr. Benjamin Allen for the ownership of a very strong head; the consequence was that, during the whole space of time just mentioned, Mr. Benjamin Allen had been wavering between intoxication partial, and intoxication complete.\n\n‘My dear friend,’ said Mr. Ben Allen, taking advantage of Mr. Bob Sawyer’s temporary absence behind the counter, whither he had retired to dispense some of the second-hand leeches, previously referred to; ‘my dear friend, I am very miserable.’\n\nMr. Winkle professed his heartfelt regret to hear it, and begged to know whether he could do anything to alleviate the sorrows of the suffering student.\n\n‘Nothing, my dear boy, nothing,’ said Ben. ‘You recollect Arabella, Winkle? My sister Arabella--a little girl, Winkle, with black eyes--when we were down at Wardle’s? I don’t know whether you happened to notice her--a nice little girl, Winkle. Perhaps my features may recall her countenance to your recollection?’\n\nMr. Winkle required nothing to recall the charming Arabella to his mind; and it was rather fortunate he did not, for the features of her brother Benjamin would unquestionably have proved but an indifferent refresher to his memory. He answered, with as much calmness as he could assume, that he perfectly remembered the young lady referred to, and sincerely trusted she was in good health.\n\n‘Our friend Bob is a delightful fellow, Winkle,’ was the only reply of Mr. Ben Allen.\n\n‘Very,’ said Mr. Winkle, not much relishing this close connection of the two names.\n\n‘I designed ‘em for each other; they were made for each other, sent into the world for each other, born for each other, Winkle,’ said Mr. Ben Allen, setting down his glass with emphasis. ‘There’s a special destiny in the matter, my dear sir; there’s only five years’ difference between ‘em, and both their birthdays are in August.’\n\nMr. Winkle was too anxious to hear what was to follow to express much wonderment at this extraordinary coincidence, marvellous as it was; so Mr. Ben Allen, after a tear or two, went on to say that, notwithstanding all his esteem and respect and veneration for his friend, Arabella had unaccountably and undutifully evinced the most determined antipathy to his person.\n\n‘And I think,’ said Mr. Ben Allen, in conclusion. ‘I think there’s a prior attachment.’\n\n‘Have you any idea who the object of it might be?’ asked Mr. Winkle, with great trepidation.\n\nMr. Ben Allen seized the poker, flourished it in a warlike manner above his head, inflicted a savage blow on an imaginary skull, and wound up by saying, in a very expressive manner, that he only wished he could guess; that was all.\n\n‘I’d show him what I thought of him,’ said Mr. Ben Allen. And round went the poker again, more fiercely than before.\n\nAll this was, of course, very soothing to the feelings of Mr. Winkle, who remained silent for a few minutes; but at length mustered up resolution to inquire whether Miss Allen was in Kent.\n\n‘No, no,’ said Mr. Ben Allen, laying aside the poker, and looking very cunning; ‘I didn’t think Wardle’s exactly the place for a headstrong girl; so, as I am her natural protector and guardian, our parents being dead, I have brought her down into this part of the country to spend a few months at an old aunt’s, in a nice, dull, close place. I think that will cure her, my boy. If it doesn’t, I’ll take her abroad for a little while, and see what that’ll do.’\n\n‘Oh, the aunt’s is in Bristol, is it?’ faltered Mr. Winkle.\n\n‘No, no, not in Bristol,’ replied Mr. Ben Allen, jerking his thumb over his right shoulder; ‘over that way--down there. But, hush, here’s Bob. Not a word, my dear friend, not a word.’\n\nShort as this conversation was, it roused in Mr. Winkle the highest degree of excitement and anxiety. The suspected prior attachment rankled in his heart. Could he be the object of it? Could it be for him that the fair Arabella had looked scornfully on the sprightly Bob Sawyer, or had he a successful rival? He determined to see her, cost what it might; but here an insurmountable objection presented itself, for whether the explanatory ‘over that way,’ and ‘down there,’ of Mr. Ben Allen, meant three miles off, or thirty, or three hundred, he could in no wise guess.\n\nBut he had no opportunity of pondering over his love just then, for Bob Sawyer’s return was the immediate precursor of the arrival of a meat-pie from the baker’s, of which that gentleman insisted on his staying to partake. The cloth was laid by an occasional charwoman, who officiated in the capacity of Mr. Bob Sawyer’s housekeeper; and a third knife and fork having been borrowed from the mother of the boy in the gray livery (for Mr. Sawyer’s domestic arrangements were as yet conducted on a limited scale), they sat down to dinner; the beer being served up, as Mr. Sawyer remarked, ‘in its native pewter.’\n\nAfter dinner, Mr. Bob Sawyer ordered in the largest mortar in the shop, and proceeded to brew a reeking jorum of rum-punch therein, stirring up and amalgamating the materials with a pestle in a very creditable and apothecary-like manner. Mr. Sawyer, being a bachelor, had only one tumbler in the house, which was assigned to Mr. Winkle as a compliment to the visitor, Mr. Ben Allen being accommodated with a funnel with a cork in the narrow end, and Bob Sawyer contented himself with one of those wide-lipped crystal vessels inscribed with a variety of cabalistic characters, in which chemists are wont to measure out their liquid drugs in compounding prescriptions. These preliminaries adjusted, the punch was tasted, and pronounced excellent; and it having been arranged that Bob Sawyer and Ben Allen should be considered at liberty to fill twice to Mr. Winkle’s once, they started fair, with great satisfaction and good-fellowship.\n\nThere was no singing, because Mr. Bob Sawyer said it wouldn’t look professional; but to make amends for this deprivation there was so much talking and laughing that it might have been heard, and very likely was, at the end of the street. Which conversation materially lightened the hours and improved the mind of Mr. Bob Sawyer’s boy, who, instead of devoting the evening to his ordinary occupation of writing his name on the counter, and rubbing it out again, peeped through the glass door, and thus listened and looked on at the same time.\n\nThe mirth of Mr. Bob Sawyer was rapidly ripening into the furious, Mr. Ben Allen was fast relapsing into the sentimental, and the punch had well-nigh disappeared altogether, when the boy hastily running in, announced that a young woman had just come over, to say that Sawyer late Nockemorf was wanted directly, a couple of streets off. This broke up the party. Mr. Bob Sawyer, understanding the message, after some twenty repetitions, tied a wet cloth round his head to sober himself, and, having partially succeeded, put on his green spectacles and issued forth. Resisting all entreaties to stay till he came back, and finding it quite impossible to engage Mr. Ben Allen in any intelligible conversation on the subject nearest his heart, or indeed on any other, Mr. Winkle took his departure, and returned to the Bush.\n\nThe anxiety of his mind, and the numerous meditations which Arabella had awakened, prevented his share of the mortar of punch producing that effect upon him which it would have had under other circumstances. So, after taking a glass of soda-water and brandy at the bar, he turned into the coffee-room, dispirited rather than elevated by the occurrences of the evening.\n\nSitting in front of the fire, with his back towards him, was a tallish gentleman in a greatcoat: the only other occupant of the room. It was rather a cool evening for the season of the year, and the gentleman drew his chair aside to afford the new-comer a sight of the fire. What were Mr. Winkle’s feelings when, in doing so, he disclosed to view the face and figure of the vindictive and sanguinary Dowler!\n\nMr. Winkle’s first impulse was to give a violent pull at the nearest bell-handle, but that unfortunately happened to be immediately behind Mr. Dowler’s head. He had made one step towards it, before he checked himself. As he did so, Mr. Dowler very hastily drew back.\n\n‘Mr. Winkle, Sir. Be calm. Don’t strike me. I won’t bear it. A blow! Never!’ said Mr. Dowler, looking meeker than Mr. Winkle had expected in a gentleman of his ferocity.\n\n‘A blow, Sir?’ stammered Mr. Winkle.\n\n‘A blow, Sir,’ replied Dowler. ‘Compose your feelings. Sit down. Hear me.’\n\n‘Sir,’ said Mr. Winkle, trembling from head to foot, ‘before I consent to sit down beside, or opposite you, without the presence of a waiter, I must be secured by some further understanding. You used a threat against me last night, Sir, a dreadful threat, Sir.’ Here Mr. Winkle turned very pale indeed, and stopped short.\n\n‘I did,’ said Dowler, with a countenance almost as white as Mr. Winkle’s. ‘Circumstances were suspicious. They have been explained. I respect your bravery. Your feeling is upright. Conscious innocence. There’s my hand. Grasp it.’\n\n‘Really, Sir,’ said Mr. Winkle, hesitating whether to give his hand or not, and almost fearing that it was demanded in order that he might be taken at an advantage, ‘really, Sir, I--’\n\n‘I know what you mean,’ interposed Dowler. ‘You feel aggrieved. Very natural. So should I. I was wrong. I beg your pardon. Be friendly. Forgive me.’ With this, Dowler fairly forced his hand upon Mr. Winkle, and shaking it with the utmost vehemence, declared he was a fellow of extreme spirit, and he had a higher opinion of him than ever.\n\n‘Now,’ said Dowler, ‘sit down. Relate it all. How did you find me? When did you follow? Be frank. Tell me.’\n\n‘It’s quite accidental,’ replied Mr. Winkle, greatly perplexed by the curious and unexpected nature of the interview. ‘Quite.’\n\n‘Glad of it,’ said Dowler. ‘I woke this morning. I had forgotten my threat. I laughed at the accident. I felt friendly. I said so.’\n\n‘To whom?’ inquired Mr. Winkle.\n\n‘To Mrs. Dowler. “You made a vow,” said she. “I did,” said I. “It was a rash one,” said she. “It was,” said I. “I’ll apologise. Where is he?”’\n\n‘Who?’ inquired Mr. Winkle.\n\n‘You,’ replied Dowler. ‘I went downstairs. You were not to be found. Pickwick looked gloomy. Shook his head. Hoped no violence would be committed. I saw it all. You felt yourself insulted. You had gone, for a friend perhaps. Possibly for pistols. “High spirit,” said I. “I admire him.”’\n\nMr. Winkle coughed, and beginning to see how the land lay, assumed a look of importance.\n\n‘I left a note for you,’ resumed Dowler. ‘I said I was sorry. So I was. Pressing business called me here. You were not satisfied. You followed. You required a verbal explanation. You were right. It’s all over now. My business is finished. I go back to-morrow. Join me.’\n\nAs Dowler progressed in his explanation, Mr. Winkle’s countenance grew more and more dignified. The mysterious nature of the commencement of their conversation was explained; Mr. Dowler had as great an objection to duelling as himself; in short, this blustering and awful personage was one of the most egregious cowards in existence, and interpreting Mr. Winkle’s absence through the medium of his own fears, had taken the same step as himself, and prudently retired until all excitement of feeling should have subsided.\n\nAs the real state of the case dawned upon Mr. Winkle’s mind, he looked very terrible, and said he was perfectly satisfied; but at the same time, said so with an air that left Mr. Dowler no alternative but to infer that if he had not been, something most horrible and destructive must inevitably have occurred. Mr. Dowler appeared to be impressed with a becoming sense of Mr. Winkle’s magnanimity and condescension; and the two belligerents parted for the night, with many protestations of eternal friendship.\n\nAbout half-past twelve o’clock, when Mr. Winkle had been revelling some twenty minutes in the full luxury of his first sleep, he was suddenly awakened by a loud knocking at his chamber door, which, being repeated with increased vehemence, caused him to start up in bed, and inquire who was there, and what the matter was.\n\n‘Please, Sir, here’s a young man which says he must see you directly,’ responded the voice of the chambermaid.\n\n‘A young man!’ exclaimed Mr. Winkle.\n\n‘No mistake about that ‘ere, Sir,’ replied another voice through the keyhole; ‘and if that wery same interestin’ young creetur ain’t let in vithout delay, it’s wery possible as his legs vill enter afore his countenance.’ The young man gave a gentle kick at one of the lower panels of the door, after he had given utterance to this hint, as if to add force and point to the remark.\n\n‘Is that you, Sam?’ inquired Mr. Winkle, springing out of bed.\n\n‘Quite unpossible to identify any gen’l’m’n vith any degree o’ mental satisfaction, vithout lookin’ at him, Sir,’ replied the voice dogmatically.\n\nMr. Winkle, not much doubting who the young man was, unlocked the door; which he had no sooner done than Mr. Samuel Weller entered with great precipitation, and carefully relocking it on the inside, deliberately put the key in his waistcoat pocket; and, after surveying Mr. Winkle from head to foot, said--\n\n‘You’re a wery humorous young gen’l’m’n, you air, Sir!’\n\n‘What do you mean by this conduct, Sam?’ inquired Mr. Winkle indignantly. ‘Get out, sir, this instant. What do you mean, Sir?’\n\n‘What do I mean,’ retorted Sam; ‘come, Sir, this is rayther too rich, as the young lady said when she remonstrated with the pastry-cook, arter he’d sold her a pork pie as had got nothin’ but fat inside. What do I mean! Well, that ain’t a bad ‘un, that ain’t.’\n\n‘Unlock that door, and leave this room immediately, Sir,’ said Mr. Winkle.\n\n‘I shall leave this here room, sir, just precisely at the wery same moment as you leaves it,’ responded Sam, speaking in a forcible manner, and seating himself with perfect gravity. ‘If I find it necessary to carry you away, pick-a-back, o’ course I shall leave it the least bit o’ time possible afore you; but allow me to express a hope as you won’t reduce me to extremities; in saying wich, I merely quote wot the nobleman said to the fractious pennywinkle, ven he vouldn’t come out of his shell by means of a pin, and he conseqvently began to be afeered that he should be obliged to crack him in the parlour door.’ At the end of this address, which was unusually lengthy for him, Mr. Weller planted his hands on his knees, and looked full in Mr. Winkle’s face, with an expression of countenance which showed that he had not the remotest intention of being trifled with.\n\n‘You’re a amiably-disposed young man, Sir, I don’t think,’ resumed Mr. Weller, in a tone of moral reproof, ‘to go inwolving our precious governor in all sorts o’ fanteegs, wen he’s made up his mind to go through everythink for principle. You’re far worse nor Dodson, Sir; and as for Fogg, I consider him a born angel to you!’ Mr. Weller having accompanied this last sentiment with an emphatic slap on each knee, folded his arms with a look of great disgust, and threw himself back in his chair, as if awaiting the criminal’s defence.\n\n‘My good fellow,’ said Mr. Winkle, extending his hand--his teeth chattering all the time he spoke, for he had been standing, during the whole of Mr. Weller’s lecture, in his night-gear--‘my good fellow, I respect your attachment to my excellent friend, and I am very sorry indeed to have added to his causes for disquiet. There, Sam, there!’\n\n‘Well,’ said Sam, rather sulkily, but giving the proffered hand a respectful shake at the same time--‘well, so you ought to be, and I am very glad to find you air; for, if I can help it, I won’t have him put upon by nobody, and that’s all about it.’\n\n‘Certainly not, Sam,’ said Mr. Winkle. ‘There! Now go to bed, Sam, and we’ll talk further about this in the morning.’\n\n‘I’m wery sorry,’ said Sam, ‘but I can’t go to bed.’\n\n‘Not go to bed!’ repeated Mr. Winkle.\n\n‘No,’ said Sam, shaking his head. ‘Can’t be done.’\n\n‘You don’t mean to say you’re going back to-night, Sam?’ urged Mr. Winkle, greatly surprised.\n\n‘Not unless you particklerly wish it,’ replied Sam; ‘but I mustn’t leave this here room. The governor’s orders wos peremptory.’\n\n‘Nonsense, Sam,’ said Mr. Winkle, ‘I must stop here two or three days; and more than that, Sam, you must stop here too, to assist me in gaining an interview with a young lady--Miss Allen, Sam; you remember her--whom I must and will see before I leave Bristol.’\n\nBut in reply to each of these positions, Sam shook his head with great firmness, and energetically replied, ‘It can’t be done.’\n\nAfter a great deal of argument and representation on the part of Mr. Winkle, however, and a full disclosure of what had passed in the interview with Dowler, Sam began to waver; and at length a compromise was effected, of which the following were the main and principal conditions:--\n\nThat Sam should retire, and leave Mr. Winkle in the undisturbed possession of his apartment, on the condition that he had permission to lock the door on the outside, and carry off the key; provided always, that in the event of an alarm of fire, or other dangerous contingency, the door should be instantly unlocked. That a letter should be written to Mr. Pickwick early next morning, and forwarded per Dowler, requesting his consent to Sam and Mr. Winkle’s remaining at Bristol, for the purpose and with the object already assigned, and begging an answer by the next coach--, if favourable, the aforesaid parties to remain accordingly, and if not, to return to Bath immediately on the receipt thereof. And, lastly, that Mr. Winkle should be understood as distinctly pledging himself not to resort to the window, fireplace, or other surreptitious mode of escape in the meanwhile. These stipulations having been concluded, Sam locked the door and departed.\n\nHe had nearly got downstairs, when he stopped, and drew the key from his pocket.\n\n‘I quite forgot about the knockin’ down,’ said Sam, half turning back. ‘The governor distinctly said it was to be done. Amazin’ stupid o’ me, that ‘ere! Never mind,’ said Sam, brightening up, ‘it’s easily done to- morrow, anyvays.’\n\nApparently much consoled by this reflection, Mr. Weller once more deposited the key in his pocket, and descending the remainder of the stairs without any fresh visitations of conscience, was soon, in common with the other inmates of the house, buried in profound repose.\n\nCHAPTER XXXIX. MR. SAMUEL WELLER, BEING INTRUSTED WITH A MISSION OF LOVE, PROCEEDS TO EXECUTE IT; WITH WHAT SUCCESS WILL HEREINAFTER APPEAR\nDuring the whole of next day, Sam kept Mr. Winkle steadily in sight, fully determined not to take his eyes off him for one instant, until he should receive express instructions from the fountain-head. However disagreeable Sam’s very close watch and great vigilance were to Mr. Winkle, he thought it better to bear with them, than, by any act of violent opposition, to hazard being carried away by force, which Mr. Weller more than once strongly hinted was the line of conduct that a strict sense of duty prompted him to pursue. There is little reason to doubt that Sam would very speedily have quieted his scruples, by bearing Mr. Winkle back to Bath, bound hand and foot, had not Mr. Pickwick’s prompt attention to the note, which Dowler had undertaken to deliver, forestalled any such proceeding. In short, at eight o’clock in the evening, Mr. Pickwick himself walked into the coffee-room of the Bush Tavern, and told Sam with a smile, to his very great relief, that he had done quite right, and it was unnecessary for him to mount guard any longer.\n\n‘I thought it better to come myself,’ said Mr. Pickwick, addressing Mr. Winkle, as Sam disencumbered him of his great-coat and travelling-shawl, ‘to ascertain, before I gave my consent to Sam’s employment in this matter, that you are quite in earnest and serious, with respect to this young lady.’\n\n‘Serious, from my heart--from my soul!’ returned Mr. Winkle, with great energy.\n\n‘Remember,’ said Mr. Pickwick, with beaming eyes, ‘we met her at our excellent and hospitable friend’s, Winkle. It would be an ill return to tamper lightly, and without due consideration, with this young lady’s affections. I’ll not allow that, sir. I’ll not allow it.’\n\n‘I have no such intention, indeed,’ exclaimed Mr. Winkle warmly. ‘I have considered the matter well, for a long time, and I feel that my happiness is bound up in her.’\n\n‘That’s wot we call tying it up in a small parcel, sir,’ interposed Mr. Weller, with an agreeable smile.\n\nMr. Winkle looked somewhat stern at this interruption, and Mr. Pickwick angrily requested his attendant not to jest with one of the best feelings of our nature; to which Sam replied, ‘That he wouldn’t, if he was aware on it; but there were so many on ‘em, that he hardly know’d which was the best ones wen he heerd ‘em mentioned.’\n\nMr. Winkle then recounted what had passed between himself and Mr. Ben Allen, relative to Arabella; stated that his object was to gain an interview with the young lady, and make a formal disclosure of his passion; and declared his conviction, founded on certain dark hints and mutterings of the aforesaid Ben, that, wherever she was at present immured, it was somewhere near the Downs. And this was his whole stock of knowledge or suspicion on the subject.\n\nWith this very slight clue to guide him, it was determined that Mr. Weller should start next morning on an expedition of discovery; it was also arranged that Mr. Pickwick and Mr. Winkle, who were less confident of their powers, should parade the town meanwhile, and accidentally drop in upon Mr. Bob Sawyer in the course of the day, in the hope of seeing or hearing something of the young lady’s whereabouts.\n\nAccordingly, next morning, Sam Weller issued forth upon his quest, in no way daunted by the very discouraging prospect before him; and away he walked, up one street and down another--we were going to say, up one hill and down another, only it’s all uphill at Clifton--without meeting with anything or anybody that tended to throw the faintest light on the matter in hand. Many were the colloquies into which Sam entered with grooms who were airing horses on roads, and nursemaids who were airing children in lanes; but nothing could Sam elicit from either the first- mentioned or the last, which bore the slightest reference to the object of his artfully-prosecuted inquiries. There were a great many young ladies in a great many houses, the greater part whereof were shrewdly suspected by the male and female domestics to be deeply attached to somebody, or perfectly ready to become so, if opportunity afforded. But as none among these young ladies was Miss Arabella Allen, the information left Sam at exactly the old point of wisdom at which he had stood before.\n\nSam struggled across the Downs against a good high wind, wondering whether it was always necessary to hold your hat on with both hands in that part of the country, and came to a shady by-place, about which were sprinkled several little villas of quiet and secluded appearance. Outside a stable door at the bottom of a long back lane without a thoroughfare, a groom in undress was idling about, apparently persuading himself that he was doing something with a spade and a wheel-barrow. We may remark, in this place, that we have scarcely ever seen a groom near a stable, in his lazy moments, who has not been, to a greater or less extent, the victim of this singular delusion.\n\nSam thought he might as well talk to this groom as to any one else, especially as he was very tired with walking, and there was a good large stone just opposite the wheel-barrow; so he strolled down the lane, and, seating himself on the stone, opened a conversation with the ease and freedom for which he was remarkable.\n\n‘Mornin’, old friend,’ said Sam.\n\n‘Arternoon, you mean,’ replied the groom, casting a surly look at Sam.\n\n‘You’re wery right, old friend,’ said Sam; ‘I do mean arternoon. How are you?’\n\n‘Why, I don’t find myself much the better for seeing of you,’ replied the ill-tempered groom.\n\n‘That’s wery odd--that is,’ said Sam, ‘for you look so uncommon cheerful, and seem altogether so lively, that it does vun’s heart good to see you.’\n\nThe surly groom looked surlier still at this, but not sufficiently so to produce any effect upon Sam, who immediately inquired, with a countenance of great anxiety, whether his master’s name was not Walker.\n\n‘No, it ain’t,’ said the groom.\n\n‘Nor Brown, I s’pose?’ said Sam.\n\n‘No, it ain’t.’\n\n‘Nor Vilson?’\n\n‘No; nor that either,’ said the groom.\n\n‘Vell,’ replied Sam, ‘then I’m mistaken, and he hasn’t got the honour o’ my acquaintance, which I thought he had. Don’t wait here out o’ compliment to me,’ said Sam, as the groom wheeled in the barrow, and prepared to shut the gate. ‘Ease afore ceremony, old boy; I’ll excuse you.’\n\n‘I’d knock your head off for half-a-crown,’ said the surly groom, bolting one half of the gate.\n\n‘Couldn’t afford to have it done on those terms,’ rejoined Sam. ‘It ‘ud be worth a life’s board wages at least, to you, and ‘ud be cheap at that. Make my compliments indoors. Tell ‘em not to vait dinner for me, and say they needn’t mind puttin’ any by, for it’ll be cold afore I come in.’\n\nIn reply to this, the groom waxing very wroth, muttered a desire to damage somebody’s person; but disappeared without carrying it into execution, slamming the door angrily after him, and wholly unheeding Sam’s affectionate request, that he would leave him a lock of his hair before he went.\n\nSam continued to sit on the large stone, meditating upon what was best to be done, and revolving in his mind a plan for knocking at all the doors within five miles of Bristol, taking them at a hundred and fifty or two hundred a day, and endeavouring to find Miss Arabella by that expedient, when accident all of a sudden threw in his way what he might have sat there for a twelvemonth and yet not found without it.\n\nInto the lane where he sat, there opened three or four garden gates, belonging to as many houses, which though detached from each other, were only separated by their gardens. As these were large and long, and well planted with trees, the houses were not only at some distance off, but the greater part of them were nearly concealed from view. Sam was sitting with his eyes fixed upon the dust-heap outside the next gate to that by which the groom had disappeared, profoundly turning over in his mind the difficulties of his present undertaking, when the gate opened, and a female servant came out into the lane to shake some bedside carpets.\n\nSam was so very busy with his own thoughts, that it is probable he would have taken no more notice of the young woman than just raising his head and remarking that she had a very neat and pretty figure, if his feelings of gallantry had not been most strongly roused by observing that she had no one to help her, and that the carpets seemed too heavy for her single strength. Mr. Weller was a gentleman of great gallantry in his own way, and he no sooner remarked this circumstance than he hastily rose from the large stone, and advanced towards her.\n\n‘My dear,’ said Sam, sliding up with an air of great respect, ‘you’ll spile that wery pretty figure out o’ all perportion if you shake them carpets by yourself. Let me help you.’\n\nThe young lady, who had been coyly affecting not to know that a gentleman was so near, turned round as Sam spoke--no doubt (indeed she said so, afterwards) to decline this offer from a perfect stranger--when instead of speaking, she started back, and uttered a half-suppressed scream. Sam was scarcely less staggered, for in the countenance of the well-shaped female servant, he beheld the very features of his valentine, the pretty housemaid from Mr. Nupkins’s.\n\n‘Wy, Mary, my dear!’ said Sam.\n\n‘Lauk, Mr. Weller,’ said Mary, ‘how you do frighten one!’\n\nSam made no verbal answer to this complaint, nor can we precisely say what reply he did make. We merely know that after a short pause Mary said, ‘Lor, do adun, Mr. Weller!’ and that his hat had fallen off a few moments before--from both of which tokens we should be disposed to infer that one kiss, or more, had passed between the parties.\n\n‘Why, how did you come here?’ said Mary, when the conversation to which this interruption had been offered, was resumed.\n\n‘O’ course I came to look arter you, my darlin’,’ replied Mr. Weller; for once permitting his passion to get the better of his veracity.\n\n‘And how did you know I was here?’ inquired Mary. ‘Who could have told you that I took another service at Ipswich, and that they afterwards moved all the way here? Who could have told you that, Mr. Weller?’\n\n‘Ah, to be sure,’ said Sam, with a cunning look, ‘that’s the pint. Who could ha’ told me?’\n\n‘It wasn’t Mr. Muzzle, was it?’ inquired Mary.\n\n‘Oh, no.’ replied Sam, with a solemn shake of the head, ‘it warn’t him.’\n\n‘It must have been the cook,’ said Mary.\n\n‘O’ course it must,’ said Sam.\n\n‘Well, I never heard the like of that!’ exclaimed Mary.\n\n‘No more did I,’ said Sam. ‘But Mary, my dear’--here Sam’s manner grew extremely affectionate--‘Mary, my dear, I’ve got another affair in hand as is wery pressin’. There’s one o’ my governor’s friends--Mr. Winkle, you remember him?’\n\n‘Him in the green coat?’ said Mary. ‘Oh, yes, I remember him.’\n\n‘Well,’ said Sam, ‘he’s in a horrid state o’ love; reg’larly comfoozled, and done over vith it.’\n\n‘Lor!’ interposed Mary.\n\n‘Yes,’ said Sam; ‘but that’s nothin’ if we could find out the young ‘ooman;’ and here Sam, with many digressions upon the personal beauty of Mary, and the unspeakable tortures he had experienced since he last saw her, gave a faithful account of Mr. Winkle’s present predicament.\n\n‘Well,’ said Mary, ‘I never did!’\n\n‘O’ course not,’ said Sam, ‘and nobody never did, nor never vill neither; and here am I a-walkin’ about like the wandering Jew--a sportin’ character you have perhaps heerd on Mary, my dear, as vos alvays doin’ a match agin’ time, and never vent to sleep--looking arter this here Miss Arabella Allen.’\n\n‘Miss who?’ said Mary, in great astonishment.\n\n‘Miss Arabella Allen,’ said Sam.\n\n‘Goodness gracious!’ said Mary, pointing to the garden door which the sulky groom had locked after him. ‘Why, it’s that very house; she’s been living there these six weeks. Their upper house-maid, which is lady’s- maid too, told me all about it over the wash-house palin’s before the family was out of bed, one mornin’.’\n\n‘Wot, the wery next door to you?’ said Sam.\n\n‘The very next,’ replied Mary.\n\nMr. Weller was so deeply overcome on receiving this intelligence that he found it absolutely necessary to cling to his fair informant for support; and divers little love passages had passed between them, before he was sufficiently collected to return to the subject.\n\n‘Vell,’ said Sam at length, ‘if this don’t beat cock-fightin’ nothin’ never vill, as the lord mayor said, ven the chief secretary o’ state proposed his missis’s health arter dinner. That wery next house! Wy, I’ve got a message to her as I’ve been a-trying all day to deliver.’\n\n‘Ah,’ said Mary, ‘but you can’t deliver it now, because she only walks in the garden in the evening, and then only for a very little time; she never goes out, without the old lady.’\n\nSam ruminated for a few moments, and finally hit upon the following plan of operations; that he should return just at dusk--the time at which Arabella invariably took her walk--and, being admitted by Mary into the garden of the house to which she belonged, would contrive to scramble up the wall, beneath the overhanging boughs of a large pear-tree, which would effectually screen him from observation; would there deliver his message, and arrange, if possible, an interview on behalf of Mr. Winkle for the ensuing evening at the same hour. Having made this arrangement with great despatch, he assisted Mary in the long-deferred occupation of shaking the carpets.\n\nIt is not half as innocent a thing as it looks, that shaking little pieces of carpet--at least, there may be no great harm in the shaking, but the folding is a very insidious process. So long as the shaking lasts, and the two parties are kept the carpet’s length apart, it is as innocent an amusement as can well be devised; but when the folding begins, and the distance between them gets gradually lessened from one half its former length to a quarter, and then to an eighth, and then to a sixteenth, and then to a thirty-second, if the carpet be long enough, it becomes dangerous. We do not know, to a nicety, how many pieces of carpet were folded in this instance, but we can venture to state that as many pieces as there were, so many times did Sam kiss the pretty housemaid.\n\nMr. Weller regaled himself with moderation at the nearest tavern until it was nearly dusk, and then returned to the lane without the thoroughfare. Having been admitted into the garden by Mary, and having received from that lady sundry admonitions concerning the safety of his limbs and neck, Sam mounted into the pear-tree, to wait until Arabella should come into sight.\n\nHe waited so long without this anxiously-expected event occurring, that he began to think it was not going to take place at all, when he heard light footsteps upon the gravel, and immediately afterwards beheld Arabella walking pensively down the garden. As soon as she came nearly below the tree, Sam began, by way of gently indicating his presence, to make sundry diabolical noises similar to those which would probably be natural to a person of middle age who had been afflicted with a combination of inflammatory sore throat, croup, and whooping-cough, from his earliest infancy.\n\nUpon this, the young lady cast a hurried glance towards the spot whence the dreadful sounds proceeded; and her previous alarm being not at all diminished when she saw a man among the branches, she would most certainly have decamped, and alarmed the house, had not fear fortunately deprived her of the power of moving, and caused her to sink down on a garden seat, which happened by good luck to be near at hand.\n\n‘She’s a-goin’ off,’ soliloquised Sam in great perplexity. ‘Wot a thing it is, as these here young creeturs will go a-faintin’ avay just ven they oughtn’t to. Here, young ‘ooman, Miss Sawbones, Mrs. Vinkle, don’t!’\n\nWhether it was the magic of Mr. Winkle’s name, or the coolness of the open air, or some recollection of Mr. Weller’s voice, that revived Arabella, matters not. She raised her head and languidly inquired, ‘Who’s that, and what do you want?’\n\n‘Hush,’ said Sam, swinging himself on to the wall, and crouching there in as small a compass as he could reduce himself to, ‘only me, miss, only me.’\n\n‘Mr. Pickwick’s servant!’ said Arabella earnestly.\n\n‘The wery same, miss,’ replied Sam. ‘Here’s Mr. Vinkle reg’larly sewed up vith desperation, miss.’\n\n‘Ah!’ said Arabella, drawing nearer the wall.\n\n‘Ah, indeed,’ said Sam. ‘Ve thought ve should ha’ been obliged to strait-veskit him last night; he’s been a-ravin’ all day; and he says if he can’t see you afore to-morrow night’s over, he vishes he may be somethin’ unpleasanted if he don’t drownd hisself.’\n\n‘Oh, no, no, Mr. Weller!’ said Arabella, clasping her hands.\n\n‘That’s wot he says, miss,’ replied Sam coolly. ‘He’s a man of his word, and it’s my opinion he’ll do it, miss. He’s heerd all about you from the sawbones in barnacles.’\n\n‘From my brother!’ said Arabella, having some faint recognition of Sam’s description.\n\n‘I don’t rightly know which is your brother, miss,’ replied Sam. ‘Is it the dirtiest vun o’ the two?’\n\n‘Yes, yes, Mr. Weller,’ returned Arabella, ‘go on. Make haste, pray.’\n\n‘Well, miss,’ said Sam, ‘he’s heerd all about it from him; and it’s the gov’nor’s opinion that if you don’t see him wery quick, the sawbones as we’ve been a-speakin’ on, ‘ull get as much extra lead in his head as’ll rayther damage the dewelopment o’ the orgins if they ever put it in spirits artervards.’\n\n‘Oh, what can I do to prevent these dreadful quarrels!’ exclaimed Arabella.\n\n‘It’s the suspicion of a priory ‘tachment as is the cause of it all,’ replied Sam. ‘You’d better see him, miss.’\n\n‘But how?--where?’ cried Arabella. ‘I dare not leave the house alone. My brother is so unkind, so unreasonable! I know how strange my talking thus to you may appear, Mr. Weller, but I am very, very unhappy--’ and here poor Arabella wept so bitterly that Sam grew chivalrous.\n\n‘It may seem wery strange talkin’ to me about these here affairs, miss,’ said Sam, with great vehemence; ‘but all I can say is, that I’m not only ready but villin’ to do anythin’ as’ll make matters agreeable; and if chuckin’ either o’ them sawboneses out o’ winder ‘ull do it, I’m the man.’ As Sam Weller said this, he tucked up his wristbands, at the imminent hazard of falling off the wall in so doing, to intimate his readiness to set to work immediately.\n\nFlattering as these professions of good feeling were, Arabella resolutely declined (most unaccountably, as Sam thought) to avail herself of them. For some time she strenuously refused to grant Mr. Winkle the interview Sam had so pathetically requested; but at length, when the conversation threatened to be interrupted by the unwelcome arrival of a third party, she hurriedly gave him to understand, with many professions of gratitude, that it was barely possible she might be in the garden an hour later, next evening. Sam understood this perfectly well; and Arabella, bestowing upon him one of her sweetest smiles, tripped gracefully away, leaving Mr. Weller in a state of very great admiration of her charms, both personal and mental.\n\nHaving descended in safety from the wall, and not forgotten to devote a few moments to his own particular business in the same department, Mr. Weller then made the best of his way back to the Bush, where his prolonged absence had occasioned much speculation and some alarm.\n\n‘We must be careful,’ said Mr. Pickwick, after listening attentively to Sam’s tale, ‘not for our sakes, but for that of the young lady. We must be very cautious.’\n\n‘We!’ said Mr. Winkle, with marked emphasis.\n\nMr. Pickwick’s momentary look of indignation at the tone of this remark, subsided into his characteristic expression of benevolence, as he replied--\n\n‘We, Sir! I shall accompany you.’\n\n‘You!’ said Mr. Winkle.\n\n‘I,’ replied Mr. Pickwick mildly. ‘In affording you this interview, the young lady has taken a natural, perhaps, but still a very imprudent step. If I am present at the meeting--a mutual friend, who is old enough to be the father of both parties--the voice of calumny can never be raised against her hereafter.’\n\nMr. Pickwick’s eyes lightened with honest exultation at his own foresight, as he spoke thus. Mr. Winkle was touched by this little trait of his delicate respect for the young protegee of his friend, and took his hand with a feeling of regard, akin to veneration.\n\n‘You SHALL go,’ said Mr. Winkle.\n\n‘I will,’ said Mr. Pickwick. ‘Sam, have my greatcoat and shawl ready, and order a conveyance to be at the door to-morrow evening, rather earlier than is absolutely necessary, in order that we may be in good time.’\n\nMr. Weller touched his hat, as an earnest of his obedience, and withdrew to make all needful preparations for the expedition.\n\nThe coach was punctual to the time appointed; and Mr. Weller, after duly installing Mr. Pickwick and Mr. Winkle inside, took his seat on the box by the driver. They alighted, as had been agreed on, about a quarter of a mile from the place of rendezvous, and desiring the coachman to await their return, proceeded the remaining distance on foot.\n\nIt was at this stage of the undertaking that Mr. Pickwick, with many smiles and various other indications of great self-satisfaction, produced from one of his coat pockets a dark lantern, with which he had specially provided himself for the occasion, and the great mechanical beauty of which he proceeded to explain to Mr. Winkle, as they walked along, to the no small surprise of the few stragglers they met.\n\n‘I should have been the better for something of this kind, in my last garden expedition, at night; eh, Sam?’ said Mr. Pickwick, looking good- humouredly round at his follower, who was trudging behind.\n\n‘Wery nice things, if they’re managed properly, Sir,’ replied Mr. Weller; ‘but wen you don’t want to be seen, I think they’re more useful arter the candle’s gone out, than wen it’s alight.’\n\nMr. Pickwick appeared struck by Sam’s remarks, for he put the lantern into his pocket again, and they walked on in silence.\n\n‘Down here, Sir,’ said Sam. ‘Let me lead the way. This is the lane, Sir.’\n\nDown the lane they went, and dark enough it was. Mr. Pickwick brought out the lantern, once or twice, as they groped their way along, and threw a very brilliant little tunnel of light before them, about a foot in diameter. It was very pretty to look at, but seemed to have the effect of rendering surrounding objects rather darker than before.\n\nAt length they arrived at the large stone. Here Sam recommended his master and Mr. Winkle to seat themselves, while he reconnoitred, and ascertained whether Mary was yet in waiting.\n\nAfter an absence of five or ten minutes, Sam returned to say that the gate was opened, and all quiet. Following him with stealthy tread, Mr. Pickwick and Mr. Winkle soon found themselves in the garden. Here everybody said, ‘Hush!’ a good many times; and that being done, no one seemed to have any very distinct apprehension of what was to be done next.\n\n‘Is Miss Allen in the garden yet, Mary?’ inquired Mr. Winkle, much agitated.\n\n‘I don’t know, sir,’ replied the pretty housemaid. ‘The best thing to be done, sir, will be for Mr. Weller to give you a hoist up into the tree, and perhaps Mr. Pickwick will have the goodness to see that nobody comes up the lane, while I watch at the other end of the garden. Goodness gracious, what’s that?’\n\n‘That ‘ere blessed lantern ‘ull be the death on us all,’ exclaimed Sam peevishly. ‘Take care wot you’re a-doin’ on, sir; you’re a-sendin’ a blaze o’ light, right into the back parlour winder.’\n\n‘Dear me!’ said Mr. Pickwick, turning hastily aside, ‘I didn’t mean to do that.’\n\n‘Now, it’s in the next house, sir,’ remonstrated Sam.\n\n‘Bless my heart!’ exclaimed Mr. Pickwick, turning round again.\n\n‘Now, it’s in the stable, and they’ll think the place is afire,’ said Sam. ‘Shut it up, sir, can’t you?’\n\n‘It’s the most extraordinary lantern I ever met with, in all my life!’ exclaimed Mr. Pickwick, greatly bewildered by the effects he had so unintentionally produced. ‘I never saw such a powerful reflector.’\n\n‘It’ll be vun too powerful for us, if you keep blazin’ avay in that manner, sir,’ replied Sam, as Mr. Pickwick, after various unsuccessful efforts, managed to close the slide. ‘There’s the young lady’s footsteps. Now, Mr. Winkle, sir, up vith you.’\n\n‘Stop, stop!’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘I must speak to her first. Help me up, Sam.’\n\n‘Gently, Sir,’ said Sam, planting his head against the wall, and making a platform of his back. ‘Step atop o’ that ‘ere flower-pot, Sir. Now then, up vith you.’\n\n‘I’m afraid I shall hurt you, Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Never mind me, Sir,’ replied Sam. ‘Lend him a hand, Mr. Winkle, sir. Steady, sir, steady! That’s the time o’ day!’\n\nAs Sam spoke, Mr. Pickwick, by exertions almost supernatural in a gentleman of his years and weight, contrived to get upon Sam’s back; and Sam gently raising himself up, and Mr. Pickwick holding on fast by the top of the wall, while Mr. Winkle clasped him tight by the legs, they contrived by these means to bring his spectacles just above the level of the coping.\n\n‘My dear,’ said Mr. Pickwick, looking over the wall, and catching sight of Arabella, on the other side, ‘don’t be frightened, my dear, it’s only me.’ ‘Oh, pray go away, Mr. Pickwick,’ said Arabella. ‘Tell them all to go away. I am so dreadfully frightened. Dear, dear Mr. Pickwick, don’t stop there. You’ll fall down and kill yourself, I know you will.’\n\n‘Now, pray don’t alarm yourself, my dear,’ said Mr. Pickwick soothingly. ‘There is not the least cause for fear, I assure you. Stand firm, Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick, looking down.\n\n‘All right, sir,’ replied Mr. Weller. ‘Don’t be longer than you can conweniently help, sir. You’re rayther heavy.’\n\n‘Only another moment, Sam,’ replied Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘I merely wished you to know, my dear, that I should not have allowed my young friend to see you in this clandestine way, if the situation in which you are placed had left him any alternative; and, lest the impropriety of this step should cause you any uneasiness, my love, it may be a satisfaction to you, to know that I am present. That’s all, my dear.’\n\n‘Indeed, Mr. Pickwick, I am very much obliged to you for your kindness and consideration,’ replied Arabella, drying her tears with her handkerchief. She would probably have said much more, had not Mr. Pickwick’s head disappeared with great swiftness, in consequence of a false step on Sam’s shoulder which brought him suddenly to the ground. He was up again in an instant however; and bidding Mr. Winkle make haste and get the interview over, ran out into the lane to keep watch, with all the courage and ardour of youth. Mr. Winkle himself, inspired by the occasion, was on the wall in a moment, merely pausing to request Sam to be careful of his master.\n\n‘I’ll take care on him, sir,’ replied Sam. ‘Leave him to me.’\n\n‘Where is he? What’s he doing, Sam?’ inquired Mr. Winkle.\n\n‘Bless his old gaiters,’ rejoined Sam, looking out at the garden door. ‘He’s a-keepin’ guard in the lane vith that ‘ere dark lantern, like a amiable Guy Fawkes! I never see such a fine creetur in my days. Blessed if I don’t think his heart must ha’ been born five-and-twenty year arter his body, at least!’\n\nMr. Winkle stayed not to hear the encomium upon his friend. He had dropped from the wall; thrown himself at Arabella’s feet; and by this time was pleading the sincerity of his passion with an eloquence worthy even of Mr. Pickwick himself.\n\nWhile these things were going on in the open air, an elderly gentleman of scientific attainments was seated in his library, two or three houses off, writing a philosophical treatise, and ever and anon moistening his clay and his labours with a glass of claret from a venerable-looking bottle which stood by his side. In the agonies of composition, the elderly gentleman looked sometimes at the carpet, sometimes at the ceiling, and sometimes at the wall; and when neither carpet, ceiling, nor wall afforded the requisite degree of inspiration, he looked out of the window.\n\nIn one of these pauses of invention, the scientific gentleman was gazing abstractedly on the thick darkness outside, when he was very much surprised by observing a most brilliant light glide through the air, at a short distance above the ground, and almost instantaneously vanish. After a short time the phenomenon was repeated, not once or twice, but several times; at last the scientific gentleman, laying down his pen, began to consider to what natural causes these appearances were to be assigned.\n\nThey were not meteors; they were too low. They were not glow-worms; they were too high. They were not will-o’-the-wisps; they were not fireflies; they were not fireworks. What could they be? Some extraordinary and wonderful phenomenon of nature, which no philosopher had ever seen before; something which it had been reserved for him alone to discover, and which he should immortalise his name by chronicling for the benefit of posterity. Full of this idea, the scientific gentleman seized his pen again, and committed to paper sundry notes of these unparalleled appearances, with the date, day, hour, minute, and precise second at which they were visible: all of which were to form the data of a voluminous treatise of great research and deep learning, which should astonish all the atmospherical wiseacres that ever drew breath in any part of the civilised globe.\n\nHe threw himself back in his easy-chair, wrapped in contemplations of his future greatness. The mysterious light appeared more brilliantly than before, dancing, to all appearance, up and down the lane, crossing from side to side, and moving in an orbit as eccentric as comets themselves.\n\nThe scientific gentleman was a bachelor. He had no wife to call in and astonish, so he rang the bell for his servant.\n\n‘Pruffle,’ said the scientific gentleman, ‘there is something very extraordinary in the air to-night? Did you see that?’ said the scientific gentleman, pointing out of the window, as the light again became visible.\n\n‘Yes, I did, Sir.’\n\n‘What do you think of it, Pruffle?’\n\n‘Think of it, Sir?’\n\n‘Yes. You have been bred up in this country. What should you say was the cause for those lights, now?’\n\nThe scientific gentleman smilingly anticipated Pruffle’s reply that he could assign no cause for them at all. Pruffle meditated.\n\n‘I should say it was thieves, Sir,’ said Pruffle at length.\n\n‘You’re a fool, and may go downstairs,’ said the scientific gentleman.\n\n‘Thank you, Sir,’ said Pruffle. And down he went.\n\nBut the scientific gentleman could not rest under the idea of the ingenious treatise he had projected being lost to the world, which must inevitably be the case if the speculation of the ingenious Mr. Pruffle were not stifled in its birth. He put on his hat and walked quickly down the garden, determined to investigate the matter to the very bottom.\n\nNow, shortly before the scientific gentleman walked out into the garden, Mr. Pickwick had run down the lane as fast as he could, to convey a false alarm that somebody was coming that way; occasionally drawing back the slide of the dark lantern to keep himself from the ditch. The alarm was no sooner given, than Mr. Winkle scrambled back over the wall, and Arabella ran into the house; the garden gate was shut, and the three adventurers were making the best of their way down the lane, when they were startled by the scientific gentleman unlocking his garden gate.\n\n‘Hold hard,’ whispered Sam, who was, of course, the first of the party. ‘Show a light for just vun second, Sir.’\n\nMr. Pickwick did as he was desired, and Sam, seeing a man’s head peeping out very cautiously within half a yard of his own, gave it a gentle tap with his clenched fist, which knocked it, with a hollow sound, against the gate. Having performed this feat with great suddenness and dexterity, Mr. Weller caught Mr. Pickwick up on his back, and followed Mr. Winkle down the lane at a pace which, considering the burden he carried, was perfectly astonishing.\n\n‘Have you got your vind back agin, Sir,’ inquired Sam, when they had reached the end.\n\n‘Quite. Quite, now,’ replied Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Then come along, Sir,’ said Sam, setting his master on his feet again. ‘Come betveen us, sir. Not half a mile to run. Think you’re vinnin’ a cup, sir. Now for it.’\n\nThus encouraged, Mr. Pickwick made the very best use of his legs. It may be confidently stated that a pair of black gaiters never got over the ground in better style than did those of Mr. Pickwick on this memorable occasion.\n\nThe coach was waiting, the horses were fresh, the roads were good, and the driver was willing. The whole party arrived in safety at the Bush before Mr. Pickwick had recovered his breath.\n\n‘In with you at once, sir,’ said Sam, as he helped his master out. ‘Don’t stop a second in the street, arter that ‘ere exercise. Beg your pardon, sir,’ continued Sam, touching his hat as Mr. Winkle descended, ‘hope there warn’t a priory ‘tachment, sir?’\n\nMr. Winkle grasped his humble friend by the hand, and whispered in his ear, ‘It’s all right, Sam; quite right.’ Upon which Mr. Weller struck three distinct blows upon his nose in token of intelligence, smiled, winked, and proceeded to put the steps up, with a countenance expressive of lively satisfaction.\n\nAs to the scientific gentleman, he demonstrated, in a masterly treatise, that these wonderful lights were the effect of electricity; and clearly proved the same by detailing how a flash of fire danced before his eyes when he put his head out of the gate, and how he received a shock which stunned him for a quarter of an hour afterwards; which demonstration delighted all the scientific associations beyond measure, and caused him to be considered a light of science ever afterwards.\n\nCHAPTER XL. INTRODUCES MR. PICKWICK TO A NEW AND NOT UNINTERESTING SCENE IN THE GREAT DRAMA OF LIFE\nThe remainder of the period which Mr. Pickwick had assigned as the duration of the stay at Bath passed over without the occurrence of anything material. Trinity term commenced. On the expiration of its first week, Mr. Pickwick and his friends returned to London; and the former gentleman, attended of course by Sam, straightway repaired to his old quarters at the George and Vulture.\n\nOn the third morning after their arrival, just as all the clocks in the city were striking nine individually, and somewhere about nine hundred and ninety-nine collectively, Sam was taking the air in George Yard, when a queer sort of fresh-painted vehicle drove up, out of which there jumped with great agility, throwing the reins to a stout man who sat beside him, a queer sort of gentleman, who seemed made for the vehicle, and the vehicle for him.\n\nThe vehicle was not exactly a gig, neither was it a stanhope. It was not what is currently denominated a dog-cart, neither was it a taxed cart, nor a chaise-cart, nor a guillotined cabriolet; and yet it had something of the character of each and every of these machines. It was painted a bright yellow, with the shafts and wheels picked out in black; and the driver sat in the orthodox sporting style, on cushions piled about two feet above the rail. The horse was a bay, a well-looking animal enough; but with something of a flash and dog-fighting air about him, nevertheless, which accorded both with the vehicle and his master.\n\nThe master himself was a man of about forty, with black hair, and carefully combed whiskers. He was dressed in a particularly gorgeous manner, with plenty of articles of jewellery about him--all about three sizes larger than those which are usually worn by gentlemen--and a rough greatcoat to crown the whole. Into one pocket of this greatcoat, he thrust his left hand the moment he dismounted, while from the other he drew forth, with his right, a very bright and glaring silk handkerchief, with which he whisked a speck or two of dust from his boots, and then, crumpling it in his hand, swaggered up the court.\n\nIt had not escaped Sam’s attention that, when this person dismounted, a shabby-looking man in a brown greatcoat shorn of divers buttons, who had been previously slinking about, on the opposite side of the way, crossed over, and remained stationary close by. Having something more than a suspicion of the object of the gentleman’s visit, Sam preceded him to the George and Vulture, and, turning sharp round, planted himself in the centre of the doorway.\n\n‘Now, my fine fellow!’ said the man in the rough coat, in an imperious tone, attempting at the same time to push his way past.\n\n‘Now, Sir, wot’s the matter?’ replied Sam, returning the push with compound interest.\n\n‘Come, none of this, my man; this won’t do with me,’ said the owner of the rough coat, raising his voice, and turning white. ‘Here, Smouch!’\n\n‘Well, wot’s amiss here?’ growled the man in the brown coat, who had been gradually sneaking up the court during this short dialogue.\n\n‘Only some insolence of this young man’s,’ said the principal, giving Sam another push.\n\n‘Come, none o’ this gammon,’ growled Smouch, giving him another, and a harder one.\n\nThis last push had the effect which it was intended by the experienced Mr. Smouch to produce; for while Sam, anxious to return the compliment, was grinding that gentleman’s body against the door-post, the principal crept past, and made his way to the bar, whither Sam, after bandying a few epithetical remarks with Mr. Smouch, followed at once.\n\n‘Good-morning, my dear,’ said the principal, addressing the young lady at the bar, with Botany Bay ease, and New South Wales gentility; ‘which is Mr. Pickwick’s room, my dear?’\n\n‘Show him up,’ said the barmaid to a waiter, without deigning another look at the exquisite, in reply to his inquiry.\n\nThe waiter led the way upstairs as he was desired, and the man in the rough coat followed, with Sam behind him, who, in his progress up the staircase, indulged in sundry gestures indicative of supreme contempt and defiance, to the unspeakable gratification of the servants and other lookers-on. Mr. Smouch, who was troubled with a hoarse cough, remained below, and expectorated in the passage.\n\nMr. Pickwick was fast asleep in bed, when his early visitor, followed by Sam, entered the room. The noise they made, in so doing, awoke him.\n\n‘Shaving-water, Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick, from within the curtains.\n\n‘Shave you directly, Mr. Pickwick,’ said the visitor, drawing one of them back from the bed’s head. ‘I’ve got an execution against you, at the suit of Bardell.--Here’s the warrant.--Common Pleas.--Here’s my card. I suppose you’ll come over to my house.’ Giving Mr. Pickwick a friendly tap on the shoulder, the sheriff’s officer (for such he was) threw his card on the counterpane, and pulled a gold toothpick from his waistcoat pocket.\n\n‘Namby’s the name,’ said the sheriff’s deputy, as Mr. Pickwick took his spectacles from under the pillow, and put them on, to read the card. ‘Namby, Bell Alley, Coleman Street.’\n\nAt this point, Sam Weller, who had had his eyes fixed hitherto on Mr. Namby’s shining beaver, interfered.\n\n‘Are you a Quaker?’ said Sam.\n\n‘I’ll let you know I am, before I’ve done with you,’ replied the indignant officer. ‘I’ll teach you manners, my fine fellow, one of these fine mornings.’\n\n‘Thank’ee,’ said Sam. ‘I’ll do the same to you. Take your hat off.’ With this, Mr. Weller, in the most dexterous manner, knocked Mr. Namby’s hat to the other side of the room, with such violence, that he had very nearly caused him to swallow the gold toothpick into the bargain.\n\n‘Observe this, Mr. Pickwick,’ said the disconcerted officer, gasping for breath. ‘I’ve been assaulted in the execution of my dooty by your servant in your chamber. I’m in bodily fear. I call you to witness this.’\n\n‘Don’t witness nothin’, Sir,’ interposed Sam. ‘Shut your eyes up tight, Sir. I’d pitch him out o’ winder, only he couldn’t fall far enough, ‘cause o’ the leads outside.’\n\n‘Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick, in an angry voice, as his attendant made various demonstrations of hostilities, ‘if you say another word, or offer the slightest interference with this person, I discharge you that instant.’\n\n‘But, Sir!’ said Sam.\n\n‘Hold your tongue,’ interposed Mr. Pickwick. ‘Take that hat up again.’\n\nBut this Sam flatly and positively refused to do; and, after he had been severely reprimanded by his master, the officer, being in a hurry, condescended to pick it up himself, venting a great variety of threats against Sam meanwhile, which that gentleman received with perfect composure, merely observing that if Mr. Namby would have the goodness to put his hat on again, he would knock it into the latter end of next week. Mr. Namby, perhaps thinking that such a process might be productive of inconvenience to himself, declined to offer the temptation, and, soon after, called up Smouch. Having informed him that the capture was made, and that he was to wait for the prisoner until he should have finished dressing, Namby then swaggered out, and drove away. Smouch, requesting Mr. Pickwick in a surly manner ‘to be as alive as he could, for it was a busy time,’ drew up a chair by the door and sat there, until he had finished dressing. Sam was then despatched for a hackney-coach, and in it the triumvirate proceeded to Coleman Street. It was fortunate the distance was short; for Mr. Smouch, besides possessing no very enchanting conversational powers, was rendered a decidedly unpleasant companion in a limited space, by the physical weakness to which we have elsewhere adverted.\n\nThe coach having turned into a very narrow and dark street, stopped before a house with iron bars to all the windows; the door-posts of which were graced by the name and title of ‘Namby, Officer to the Sheriffs of London’; the inner gate having been opened by a gentleman who might have passed for a neglected twin-brother of Mr. Smouch, and who was endowed with a large key for the purpose, Mr. Pickwick was shown into the ‘coffee-room.’\n\nThis coffee-room was a front parlour, the principal features of which were fresh sand and stale tobacco smoke. Mr. Pickwick bowed to the three persons who were seated in it when he entered; and having despatched Sam for Perker, withdrew into an obscure corner, and looked thence with some curiosity upon his new companions.\n\nOne of these was a mere boy of nineteen or twenty, who, though it was yet barely ten o’clock, was drinking gin-and-water, and smoking a cigar- -amusements to which, judging from his inflamed countenance, he had devoted himself pretty constantly for the last year or two of his life. Opposite him, engaged in stirring the fire with the toe of his right boot, was a coarse, vulgar young man of about thirty, with a sallow face and harsh voice; evidently possessed of that knowledge of the world, and captivating freedom of manner, which is to be acquired in public-house parlours, and at low billiard tables. The third tenant of the apartment was a middle-aged man in a very old suit of black, who looked pale and haggard, and paced up and down the room incessantly; stopping, now and then, to look with great anxiety out of the window as if he expected somebody, and then resuming his walk.\n\n‘You’d better have the loan of my razor this morning, Mr. Ayresleigh,’ said the man who was stirring the fire, tipping the wink to his friend the boy.\n\n‘Thank you, no, I shan’t want it; I expect I shall be out, in the course of an hour or so,’ replied the other in a hurried manner. Then, walking again up to the window, and once more returning disappointed, he sighed deeply, and left the room; upon which the other two burst into a loud laugh.\n\n‘Well, I never saw such a game as that,’ said the gentleman who had offered the razor, whose name appeared to be Price. ‘Never!’ Mr. Price confirmed the assertion with an oath, and then laughed again, when of course the boy (who thought his companion one of the most dashing fellows alive) laughed also.\n\n‘You’d hardly think, would you now,’ said Price, turning towards Mr. Pickwick, ‘that that chap’s been here a week yesterday, and never once shaved himself yet, because he feels so certain he’s going out in half an hour’s time, thinks he may as well put it off till he gets home?’\n\n‘Poor man!’ said Mr. Pickwick. ‘Are his chances of getting out of his difficulties really so great?’\n\n‘Chances be d----d,’ replied Price; ‘he hasn’t half the ghost of one. I wouldn’t give that for his chance of walking about the streets this time ten years.’ With this, Mr. Price snapped his fingers contemptuously, and rang the bell.\n\n‘Give me a sheet of paper, Crookey,’ said Mr. Price to the attendant, who in dress and general appearance looked something between a bankrupt glazier, and a drover in a state of insolvency; ‘and a glass of brandy- and-water, Crookey, d’ye hear? I’m going to write to my father, and I must have a stimulant, or I shan’t be able to pitch it strong enough into the old boy.’ At this facetious speech, the young boy, it is almost needless to say, was fairly convulsed.\n\n‘That’s right,’ said Mr. Price. ‘Never say die. All fun, ain’t it?’\n\n‘Prime!’ said the young gentleman.\n\n‘You’ve got some spirit about you, you have,’ said Price. ‘You’ve seen something of life.’\n\n‘I rather think I have!’ replied the boy. He had looked at it through the dirty panes of glass in a bar door.\n\nMr. Pickwick, feeling not a little disgusted with this dialogue, as well as with the air and manner of the two beings by whom it had been carried on, was about to inquire whether he could not be accommodated with a private sitting-room, when two or three strangers of genteel appearance entered, at sight of whom the boy threw his cigar into the fire, and whispering to Mr. Price that they had come to ‘make it all right’ for him, joined them at a table in the farther end of the room.\n\nIt would appear, however, that matters were not going to be made all right quite so speedily as the young gentleman anticipated; for a very long conversation ensued, of which Mr. Pickwick could not avoid hearing certain angry fragments regarding dissolute conduct, and repeated forgiveness. At last, there were very distinct allusions made by the oldest gentleman of the party to one Whitecross Street, at which the young gentleman, notwithstanding his primeness and his spirit, and his knowledge of life into the bargain, reclined his head upon the table, and howled dismally.\n\nVery much satisfied with this sudden bringing down of the youth’s valour, and this effectual lowering of his tone, Mr. Pickwick rang the bell, and was shown, at his own request, into a private room furnished with a carpet, table, chairs, sideboard and sofa, and ornamented with a looking-glass, and various old prints. Here he had the advantage of hearing Mrs. Namby’s performance on a square piano overhead, while the breakfast was getting ready; when it came, Mr. Perker came too.\n\n‘Aha, my dear sir,’ said the little man, ‘nailed at last, eh? Come, come, I’m not sorry for it either, because now you’ll see the absurdity of this conduct. I’ve noted down the amount of the taxed costs and damages for which the ca-sa was issued, and we had better settle at once and lose no time. Namby is come home by this time, I dare say. What say you, my dear sir? Shall I draw a cheque, or will you?’ The little man rubbed his hands with affected cheerfulness as he said this, but glancing at Mr. Pickwick’s countenance, could not forbear at the same time casting a desponding look towards Sam Weller.\n\n‘Perker,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘let me hear no more of this, I beg. I see no advantage in staying here, so I shall go to prison to-night.’\n\n‘You can’t go to Whitecross Street, my dear Sir,’ said Perker. ‘Impossible! There are sixty beds in a ward; and the bolt’s on, sixteen hours out of the four-and-twenty.’\n\n‘I would rather go to some other place of confinement if I can,’ said Mr. Pickwick. ‘If not, I must make the best I can of that.’\n\n‘You can go to the Fleet, my dear Sir, if you’re determined to go somewhere,’ said Perker.\n\n‘That’ll do,’ said Mr. Pickwick. ‘I’ll go there directly I have finished my breakfast.’\n\n‘Stop, stop, my dear Sir; not the least occasion for being in such a violent hurry to get into a place that most other men are as eager to get out of,’ said the good-natured little attorney. ‘We must have a habeas-corpus. There’ll be no judge at chambers till four o’clock this afternoon. You must wait till then.’\n\n‘Very good,’ said Mr. Pickwick, with unmoved patience. ‘Then we will have a chop here, at two. See about it, Sam, and tell them to be punctual.’\n\nMr. Pickwick remaining firm, despite all the remonstrances and arguments of Perker, the chops appeared and disappeared in due course; he was then put into another hackney coach, and carried off to Chancery Lane, after waiting half an hour or so for Mr. Namby, who had a select dinner-party and could on no account be disturbed before.\n\nThere were two judges in attendance at Serjeant’s Inn--one King’s Bench, and one Common Pleas--and a great deal of business appeared to be transacting before them, if the number of lawyer’s clerks who were hurrying in and out with bundles of papers, afforded any test. When they reached the low archway which forms the entrance to the inn, Perker was detained a few moments parlaying with the coachman about the fare and the change; and Mr. Pickwick, stepping to one side to be out of the way of the stream of people that were pouring in and out, looked about him with some curiosity.\n\nThe people that attracted his attention most, were three or four men of shabby-genteel appearance, who touched their hats to many of the attorneys who passed, and seemed to have some business there, the nature of which Mr. Pickwick could not divine. They were curious-looking fellows. One was a slim and rather lame man in rusty black, and a white neckerchief; another was a stout, burly person, dressed in the same apparel, with a great reddish-black cloth round his neck; a third was a little weazen, drunken-looking body, with a pimply face. They were loitering about, with their hands behind them, and now and then with an anxious countenance whispered something in the ear of some of the gentlemen with papers, as they hurried by. Mr. Pickwick remembered to have very often observed them lounging under the archway when he had been walking past; and his curiosity was quite excited to know to what branch of the profession these dingy-looking loungers could possibly belong.\n\nHe was about to propound the question to Namby, who kept close beside him, sucking a large gold ring on his little finger, when Perker bustled up, and observing that there was no time to lose, led the way into the inn. As Mr. Pickwick followed, the lame man stepped up to him, and civilly touching his hat, held out a written card, which Mr. Pickwick, not wishing to hurt the man’s feelings by refusing, courteously accepted and deposited in his waistcoat pocket.\n\n‘Now,’ said Perker, turning round before he entered one of the offices, to see that his companions were close behind him. ‘In here, my dear sir. Hallo, what do you want?’\n\nThis last question was addressed to the lame man, who, unobserved by Mr. Pickwick, made one of the party. In reply to it, the lame man touched his hat again, with all imaginable politeness, and motioned towards Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘No, no,’ said Perker, with a smile. ‘We don’t want you, my dear friend, we don’t want you.’\n\n‘I beg your pardon, sir,’ said the lame man. ‘The gentleman took my card. I hope you will employ me, sir. The gentleman nodded to me. I’ll be judged by the gentleman himself. You nodded to me, sir?’\n\n‘Pooh, pooh, nonsense. You didn’t nod to anybody, Pickwick? A mistake, a mistake,’ said Perker.\n\n‘The gentleman handed me his card,’ replied Mr. Pickwick, producing it from his waistcoat pocket. ‘I accepted it, as the gentleman seemed to wish it--in fact I had some curiosity to look at it when I should be at leisure. I--’\n\nThe little attorney burst into a loud laugh, and returning the card to the lame man, informing him it was all a mistake, whispered to Mr. Pickwick as the man turned away in dudgeon, that he was only a bail.\n\n‘A what!’ exclaimed Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘A bail,’ replied Perker.\n‘A bail!’\n\nYes, my dear sir--half a dozen of ‘em here. Bail you to any amount, and only charge half a crown. Curious trade, isn’t it?’ said Perker, regaling himself with a pinch of snuff.\n\n‘What! Am I to understand that these men earn a livelihood by waiting about here, to perjure themselves before the judges of the land, at the rate of half a crown a crime?’ exclaimed Mr. Pickwick, quite aghast at the disclosure.\n\n‘Why, I don’t exactly know about perjury, my dear sir,’ replied the little gentleman. ‘Harsh word, my dear sir, very harsh word indeed. It’s a legal fiction, my dear sir, nothing more.’ Saying which, the attorney shrugged his shoulders, smiled, took a second pinch of snuff, and led the way into the office of the judge’s clerk.\n\nThis was a room of specially dirty appearance, with a very low ceiling and old panelled walls; and so badly lighted, that although it was broad day outside, great tallow candles were burning on the desks. At one end, was a door leading to the judge’s private apartment, round which were congregated a crowd of attorneys and managing clerks, who were called in, in the order in which their respective appointments stood upon the file. Every time this door was opened to let a party out, the next party made a violent rush to get in; and, as in addition to the numerous dialogues which passed between the gentlemen who were waiting to see the judge, a variety of personal squabbles ensued between the greater part of those who had seen him, there was as much noise as could well be raised in an apartment of such confined dimensions.\n\nNor were the conversations of these gentlemen the only sounds that broke upon the ear. Standing on a box behind a wooden bar at another end of the room was a clerk in spectacles who was ‘taking the affidavits’; large batches of which were, from time to time, carried into the private room by another clerk for the judge’s signature. There were a large number of attorneys’ clerks to be sworn, and it being a moral impossibility to swear them all at once, the struggles of these gentlemen to reach the clerk in spectacles, were like those of a crowd to get in at the pit door of a theatre when Gracious Majesty honours it with its presence. Another functionary, from time to time, exercised his lungs in calling over the names of those who had been sworn, for the purpose of restoring to them their affidavits after they had been signed by the judge, which gave rise to a few more scuffles; and all these things going on at the same time, occasioned as much bustle as the most active and excitable person could desire to behold. There were yet another class of persons--those who were waiting to attend summonses their employers had taken out, which it was optional to the attorney on the opposite side to attend or not--and whose business it was, from time to time, to cry out the opposite attorney’s name; to make certain that he was not in attendance without their knowledge.\n\nFor example. Leaning against the wall, close beside the seat Mr. Pickwick had taken, was an office-lad of fourteen, with a tenor voice; near him a common-law clerk with a bass one.\n\nA clerk hurried in with a bundle of papers, and stared about him.\n\n‘Sniggle and Blink,’ cried the tenor.\n\n‘Porkin and Snob,’ growled the bass.\n\n‘Stumpy and Deacon,’ said the new-comer.\n\nNobody answered; the next man who came in, was bailed by the whole three; and he in his turn shouted for another firm; and then somebody else roared in a loud voice for another; and so forth.\n\nAll this time, the man in the spectacles was hard at work, swearing the clerks; the oath being invariably administered, without any effort at punctuation, and usually in the following terms:--\n\n‘Take the book in your right hand this is your name and hand-writing you swear that the contents of this your affidavit are true so help you God a shilling you must get change I haven’t got it.’\n\n‘Well, Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘I suppose they are getting the Habeas- corpus ready?’\n\n‘Yes,’ said Sam, ‘and I vish they’d bring out the have-his-carcase. It’s wery unpleasant keepin’ us vaitin’ here. I’d ha’ got half a dozen have- his-carcases ready, pack’d up and all, by this time.’\n\nWhat sort of cumbrous and unmanageable machine, Sam Weller imagined a habeas-corpus to be, does not appear; for Perker, at that moment, walked up and took Mr. Pickwick away.\n\nThe usual forms having been gone through, the body of Samuel Pickwick was soon afterwards confided to the custody of the tipstaff, to be by him taken to the warden of the Fleet Prison, and there detained until the amount of the damages and costs in the action of Bardell against Pickwick was fully paid and satisfied.\n\n‘And that,’ said Mr. Pickwick, laughing, ‘will be a very long time. Sam, call another hackney-coach. Perker, my dear friend, good-bye.’\n\n‘I shall go with you, and see you safe there,’ said Perker.\n\n‘Indeed,’ replied Mr. Pickwick, ‘I would rather go without any other attendant than Sam. As soon as I get settled, I will write and let you know, and I shall expect you immediately. Until then, good-bye.’\n\nAs Mr. Pickwick said this, he got into the coach which had by this time arrived, followed by the tipstaff. Sam having stationed himself on the box, it rolled away.\n\n‘A most extraordinary man that!’ said Perker, as he stopped to pull on his gloves.\n\n‘What a bankrupt he’d make, Sir,’ observed Mr. Lowten, who was standing near. ‘How he would bother the commissioners! He’d set ‘em at defiance if they talked of committing him, Sir.’\n\nThe attorney did not appear very much delighted with his clerk’s professional estimate of Mr. Pickwick’s character, for he walked away without deigning any reply.\n\nThe hackney-coach jolted along Fleet Street, as hackney-coaches usually do. The horses ‘went better’, the driver said, when they had anything before them (they must have gone at a most extraordinary pace when there was nothing), and so the vehicle kept behind a cart; when the cart stopped, it stopped; and when the cart went on again, it did the same. Mr. Pickwick sat opposite the tipstaff; and the tipstaff sat with his hat between his knees, whistling a tune, and looking out of the coach window.\n\nTime performs wonders. By the powerful old gentleman’s aid, even a hackney-coach gets over half a mile of ground. They stopped at length, and Mr. Pickwick alighted at the gate of the Fleet.\n\nThe tipstaff, just looking over his shoulder to see that his charge was following close at his heels, preceded Mr. Pickwick into the prison; turning to the left, after they had entered, they passed through an open door into a lobby, from which a heavy gate, opposite to that by which they had entered, and which was guarded by a stout turnkey with the key in his hand, led at once into the interior of the prison.\n\nHere they stopped, while the tipstaff delivered his papers; and here Mr. Pickwick was apprised that he would remain, until he had undergone the ceremony, known to the initiated as ‘sitting for your portrait.’\n\n‘Sitting for my portrait?’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Having your likeness taken, sir,’ replied the stout turnkey.\n\n‘We’re capital hands at likenesses here. Take ‘em in no time, and always exact. Walk in, sir, and make yourself at home.’\n\nMr. Pickwick complied with the invitation, and sat himself down; when Mr. Weller, who stationed himself at the back of the chair, whispered that the sitting was merely another term for undergoing an inspection by the different turnkeys, in order that they might know prisoners from visitors.\n\n‘Well, Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘then I wish the artists would come. This is rather a public place.’\n\n‘They von’t be long, Sir, I des-say,’ replied Sam. ‘There’s a Dutch clock, sir.’\n\n‘So I see,’ observed Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘And a bird-cage, sir,’ says Sam. ‘Veels vithin veels, a prison in a prison. Ain’t it, Sir?’\n\nAs Mr. Weller made this philosophical remark, Mr. Pickwick was aware that his sitting had commenced. The stout turnkey having been relieved from the lock, sat down, and looked at him carelessly, from time to time, while a long thin man who had relieved him, thrust his hands beneath his coat tails, and planting himself opposite, took a good long view of him. A third rather surly-looking gentleman, who had apparently been disturbed at his tea, for he was disposing of the last remnant of a crust and butter when he came in, stationed himself close to Mr. Pickwick; and, resting his hands on his hips, inspected him narrowly; while two others mixed with the group, and studied his features with most intent and thoughtful faces. Mr. Pickwick winced a good deal under the operation, and appeared to sit very uneasily in his chair; but he made no remark to anybody while it was being performed, not even to Sam, who reclined upon the back of the chair, reflecting, partly on the situation of his master, and partly on the great satisfaction it would have afforded him to make a fierce assault upon all the turnkeys there assembled, one after the other, if it were lawful and peaceable so to do.\n\nAt length the likeness was completed, and Mr. Pickwick was informed that he might now proceed into the prison.\n\n‘Where am I to sleep to-night?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Why, I don’t rightly know about to-night,’ replied the stout turnkey. ‘You’ll be chummed on somebody to-morrow, and then you’ll be all snug and comfortable. The first night’s generally rather unsettled, but you’ll be set all squares to-morrow.’\n\nAfter some discussion, it was discovered that one of the turnkeys had a bed to let, which Mr. Pickwick could have for that night. He gladly agreed to hire it.\n\n‘If you’ll come with me, I’ll show it you at once,’ said the man. ‘It ain’t a large ‘un; but it’s an out-and-outer to sleep in. This way, sir.’\n\nThey passed through the inner gate, and descended a short flight of steps. The key was turned after them; and Mr. Pickwick found himself, for the first time in his life, within the walls of a debtors’ prison.\n\nCHAPTER XLI. WHAT BEFELL MR. PICKWICK WHEN HE GOT INTO THE FLEET; WHAT PRISONERS HE SAW THERE, AND HOW HE PASSED THE NIGHT\nMr. Tom Roker, the gentleman who had accompanied Mr. Pickwick into the prison, turned sharp round to the right when he got to the bottom of the little flight of steps, and led the way, through an iron gate which stood open, and up another short flight of steps, into a long narrow gallery, dirty and low, paved with stone, and very dimly lighted by a window at each remote end.\n\n‘This,’ said the gentleman, thrusting his hands into his pockets, and looking carelessly over his shoulder to Mr. Pickwick--‘this here is the hall flight.’\n\n‘Oh,’ replied Mr. Pickwick, looking down a dark and filthy staircase, which appeared to lead to a range of damp and gloomy stone vaults, beneath the ground, ‘and those, I suppose, are the little cellars where the prisoners keep their small quantities of coals. Unpleasant places to have to go down to; but very convenient, I dare say.’\n\n‘Yes, I shouldn’t wonder if they was convenient,’ replied the gentleman, ‘seeing that a few people live there, pretty snug. That’s the Fair, that is.’\n\n‘My friend,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘you don’t really mean to say that human beings live down in those wretched dungeons?’\n\n‘Don’t I?’ replied Mr. Roker, with indignant astonishment; ‘why shouldn’t I?’\n\n‘Live!--live down there!’ exclaimed Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Live down there! Yes, and die down there, too, very often!’ replied Mr. Roker; ‘and what of that? Who’s got to say anything agin it? Live down there! Yes, and a wery good place it is to live in, ain’t it?’\n\nAs Roker turned somewhat fiercely upon Mr. Pickwick in saying this, and moreover muttered in an excited fashion certain unpleasant invocations concerning his own eyes, limbs, and circulating fluids, the latter gentleman deemed it advisable to pursue the discourse no further. Mr. Roker then proceeded to mount another staircase, as dirty as that which led to the place which has just been the subject of discussion, in which ascent he was closely followed by Mr. Pickwick and Sam.\n\n‘There,’ said Mr. Roker, pausing for breath when they reached another gallery of the same dimensions as the one below, ‘this is the coffee- room flight; the one above’s the third, and the one above that’s the top; and the room where you’re a-going to sleep to-night is the warden’s room, and it’s this way--come on.’ Having said all this in a breath, Mr. Roker mounted another flight of stairs with Mr. Pickwick and Sam Weller following at his heels.\n\nThese staircases received light from sundry windows placed at some little distance above the floor, and looking into a gravelled area bounded by a high brick wall, with iron chevaux-de-frise at the top. This area, it appeared from Mr. Roker’s statement, was the racket- ground; and it further appeared, on the testimony of the same gentleman, that there was a smaller area in that portion of the prison which was nearest Farringdon Street, denominated and called ‘the Painted Ground,’ from the fact of its walls having once displayed the semblance of various men-of-war in full sail, and other artistical effects achieved in bygone times by some imprisoned draughtsman in his leisure hours.\n\nHaving communicated this piece of information, apparently more for the purpose of discharging his bosom of an important fact, than with any specific view of enlightening Mr. Pickwick, the guide, having at length reached another gallery, led the way into a small passage at the extreme end, opened a door, and disclosed an apartment of an appearance by no means inviting, containing eight or nine iron bedsteads.\n\n‘There,’ said Mr. Roker, holding the door open, and looking triumphantly round at Mr. Pickwick, ‘there’s a room!’\n\nMr. Pickwick’s face, however, betokened such a very trifling portion of satisfaction at the appearance of his lodging, that Mr. Roker looked, for a reciprocity of feeling, into the countenance of Samuel Weller, who, until now, had observed a dignified silence.\n\n‘There’s a room, young man,’ observed Mr. Roker.\n\n‘I see it,’ replied Sam, with a placid nod of the head.\n\n‘You wouldn’t think to find such a room as this in the Farringdon Hotel, would you?’ said Mr. Roker, with a complacent smile.\n\nTo this Mr. Weller replied with an easy and unstudied closing of one eye; which might be considered to mean, either that he would have thought it, or that he would not have thought it, or that he had never thought anything at all about it, as the observer’s imagination suggested. Having executed this feat, and reopened his eye, Mr. Weller proceeded to inquire which was the individual bedstead that Mr. Roker had so flatteringly described as an out-and-outer to sleep in.\n\n‘That’s it,’ replied Mr. Roker, pointing to a very rusty one in a corner. ‘It would make any one go to sleep, that bedstead would, whether they wanted to or not.’\n\n‘I should think,’ said Sam, eyeing the piece of furniture in question with a look of excessive disgust--‘I should think poppies was nothing to it.’\n\n‘Nothing at all,’ said Mr. Roker.\n\n‘And I s’pose,’ said Sam, with a sidelong glance at his master, as if to see whether there were any symptoms of his determination being shaken by what passed, ‘I s’pose the other gen’l’men as sleeps here are gen’l’men.’\n\n‘Nothing but it,’ said Mr. Roker. ‘One of ‘em takes his twelve pints of ale a day, and never leaves off smoking even at his meals.’\n\n‘He must be a first-rater,’ said Sam.\n\n‘A1,’ replied Mr. Roker.\n\nNothing daunted, even by this intelligence, Mr. Pickwick smilingly announced his determination to test the powers of the narcotic bedstead for that night; and Mr. Roker, after informing him that he could retire to rest at whatever hour he thought proper, without any further notice or formality, walked off, leaving him standing with Sam in the gallery.\n\nIt was getting dark; that is to say, a few gas jets were kindled in this place which was never light, by way of compliment to the evening, which had set in outside. As it was rather warm, some of the tenants of the numerous little rooms which opened into the gallery on either hand, had set their doors ajar. Mr. Pickwick peeped into them as he passed along, with great curiosity and interest. Here, four or five great hulking fellows, just visible through a cloud of tobacco smoke, were engaged in noisy and riotous conversation over half-emptied pots of beer, or playing at all-fours with a very greasy pack of cards. In the adjoining room, some solitary tenant might be seen poring, by the light of a feeble tallow candle, over a bundle of soiled and tattered papers, yellow with dust and dropping to pieces from age, writing, for the hundredth time, some lengthened statement of his grievances, for the perusal of some great man whose eyes it would never reach, or whose heart it would never touch. In a third, a man, with his wife and a whole crowd of children, might be seen making up a scanty bed on the ground, or upon a few chairs, for the younger ones to pass the night in. And in a fourth, and a fifth, and a sixth, and a seventh, the noise, and the beer, and the tobacco smoke, and the cards, all came over again in greater force than before.\n\nIn the galleries themselves, and more especially on the stair-cases, there lingered a great number of people, who came there, some because their rooms were empty and lonesome, others because their rooms were full and hot; the greater part because they were restless and uncomfortable, and not possessed of the secret of exactly knowing what to do with themselves. There were many classes of people here, from the labouring man in his fustian jacket, to the broken-down spendthrift in his shawl dressing-gown, most appropriately out at elbows; but there was the same air about them all--a kind of listless, jail-bird, careless swagger, a vagabondish who’s-afraid sort of bearing, which is wholly indescribable in words, but which any man can understand in one moment if he wish, by setting foot in the nearest debtors’ prison, and looking at the very first group of people he sees there, with the same interest as Mr. Pickwick did.\n\n‘It strikes me, Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick, leaning over the iron rail at the stair-head, ‘it strikes me, Sam, that imprisonment for debt is scarcely any punishment at all.’\n\n‘Think not, sir?’ inquired Mr. Weller.\n\n‘You see how these fellows drink, and smoke, and roar,’ replied Mr. Pickwick. ‘It’s quite impossible that they can mind it much.’\n\n‘Ah, that’s just the wery thing, Sir,’ rejoined Sam, ‘they don’t mind it; it’s a reg’lar holiday to them--all porter and skittles. It’s the t’other vuns as gets done over vith this sort o’ thing; them down- hearted fellers as can’t svig avay at the beer, nor play at skittles neither; them as vould pay if they could, and gets low by being boxed up. I’ll tell you wot it is, sir; them as is always a-idlin’ in public- houses it don’t damage at all, and them as is alvays a-workin’ wen they can, it damages too much. “It’s unekal,” as my father used to say wen his grog worn’t made half-and-half: “it’s unekal, and that’s the fault on it.”’\n\n‘I think you’re right, Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick, after a few moments’ reflection, ‘quite right.’\n\n‘P’raps, now and then, there’s some honest people as likes it,’ observed Mr. Weller, in a ruminative tone, ‘but I never heerd o’ one as I can call to mind, ‘cept the little dirty-faced man in the brown coat; and that was force of habit.’\n\n‘And who was he?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Wy, that’s just the wery point as nobody never know’d,’ replied Sam.\n\n‘But what did he do?’\n\n‘Wy, he did wot many men as has been much better know’d has done in their time, Sir,’ replied Sam, ‘he run a match agin the constable, and vun it.’\n\n‘In other words, I suppose,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘he got into debt.’\n\n‘Just that, Sir,’ replied Sam, ‘and in course o’ time he come here in consekens. It warn’t much--execution for nine pound nothin’, multiplied by five for costs; but hows’ever here he stopped for seventeen year. If he got any wrinkles in his face, they were stopped up vith the dirt, for both the dirty face and the brown coat wos just the same at the end o’ that time as they wos at the beginnin’. He wos a wery peaceful, inoffendin’ little creetur, and wos alvays a-bustlin’ about for somebody, or playin’ rackets and never vinnin’; till at last the turnkeys they got quite fond on him, and he wos in the lodge ev’ry night, a-chattering vith ‘em, and tellin’ stories, and all that ‘ere. Vun night he wos in there as usual, along vith a wery old friend of his, as wos on the lock, ven he says all of a sudden, “I ain’t seen the market outside, Bill,” he says (Fleet Market wos there at that time)--“I ain’t seen the market outside, Bill,” he says, “for seventeen year.” “I know you ain’t,” says the turnkey, smoking his pipe. “I should like to see it for a minit, Bill,” he says. “Wery probable,” says the turnkey, smoking his pipe wery fierce, and making believe he warn’t up to wot the little man wanted. “Bill,” says the little man, more abrupt than afore, “I’ve got the fancy in my head. Let me see the public streets once more afore I die; and if I ain’t struck with apoplexy, I’ll be back in five minits by the clock.” “And wot ‘ud become o’ me if you wos struck with apoplexy?” said the turnkey. “Wy,” says the little creetur, “whoever found me, ‘ud bring me home, for I’ve got my card in my pocket, Bill,” he says, “No. 20, Coffee-room Flight”: and that wos true, sure enough, for wen he wanted to make the acquaintance of any new-comer, he used to pull out a little limp card vith them words on it and nothin’ else; in consideration of vich, he vos alvays called Number Tventy. The turnkey takes a fixed look at him, and at last he says in a solemn manner, “Tventy,” he says, “I’ll trust you; you Won’t get your old friend into trouble.” “No, my boy; I hope I’ve somethin’ better behind here,” says the little man; and as he said it he hit his little vesket wery hard, and then a tear started out o’ each eye, which wos wery extraordinary, for it wos supposed as water never touched his face. He shook the turnkey by the hand; out he vent--’\n\n‘And never came back again,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Wrong for vunce, sir,’ replied Mr. Weller, ‘for back he come, two minits afore the time, a-bilin’ with rage, sayin’ how he’d been nearly run over by a hackney-coach that he warn’t used to it; and he was blowed if he wouldn’t write to the lord mayor. They got him pacified at last; and for five years arter that, he never even so much as peeped out o’ the lodge gate.’\n\n‘At the expiration of that time he died, I suppose,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘No, he didn’t, Sir,’ replied Sam. ‘He got a curiosity to go and taste the beer at a new public-house over the way, and it wos such a wery nice parlour, that he took it into his head to go there every night, which he did for a long time, always comin’ back reg’lar about a quarter of an hour afore the gate shut, which was all wery snug and comfortable. At last he began to get so precious jolly, that he used to forget how the time vent, or care nothin’ at all about it, and he went on gettin’ later and later, till vun night his old friend wos just a-shuttin’ the gate-- had turned the key in fact--wen he come up. “Hold hard, Bill,” he says. “Wot, ain’t you come home yet, Tventy?” says the turnkey, “I thought you wos in, long ago.” “No, I wasn’t,” says the little man, with a smile. “Well, then, I’ll tell you wot it is, my friend,” says the turnkey, openin’ the gate wery slow and sulky, “it’s my ‘pinion as you’ve got into bad company o’ late, which I’m wery sorry to see. Now, I don’t wish to do nothing harsh,” he says, “but if you can’t confine yourself to steady circles, and find your vay back at reg’lar hours, as sure as you’re a-standin’ there, I’ll shut you out altogether!” The little man was seized vith a wiolent fit o’ tremblin’, and never vent outside the prison walls artervards!’\n\nAs Sam concluded, Mr. Pickwick slowly retraced his steps downstairs. After a few thoughtful turns in the Painted Ground, which, as it was now dark, was nearly deserted, he intimated to Mr. Weller that he thought it high time for him to withdraw for the night; requesting him to seek a bed in some adjacent public-house, and return early in the morning, to make arrangements for the removal of his master’s wardrobe from the George and Vulture. This request Mr. Samuel Weller prepared to obey, with as good a grace as he could assume, but with a very considerable show of reluctance nevertheless. He even went so far as to essay sundry ineffectual hints regarding the expediency of stretching himself on the gravel for that night; but finding Mr. Pickwick obstinately deaf to any such suggestions, finally withdrew.\n\nThere is no disguising the fact that Mr. Pickwick felt very low-spirited and uncomfortable--not for lack of society, for the prison was very full, and a bottle of wine would at once have purchased the utmost good- fellowship of a few choice spirits, without any more formal ceremony of introduction; but he was alone in the coarse, vulgar crowd, and felt the depression of spirits and sinking of heart, naturally consequent on the reflection that he was cooped and caged up, without a prospect of liberation. As to the idea of releasing himself by ministering to the sharpness of Dodson & Fogg, it never for an instant entered his thoughts.\n\nIn this frame of mind he turned again into the coffee-room gallery, and walked slowly to and fro. The place was intolerably dirty, and the smell of tobacco smoke perfectly suffocating. There was a perpetual slamming and banging of doors as the people went in and out; and the noise of their voices and footsteps echoed and re-echoed through the passages constantly. A young woman, with a child in her arms, who seemed scarcely able to crawl, from emaciation and misery, was walking up and down the passage in conversation with her husband, who had no other place to see her in. As they passed Mr. Pickwick, he could hear the female sob bitterly; and once she burst into such a passion of grief, that she was compelled to lean against the wall for support, while the man took the child in his arms, and tried to soothe her.\n\nMr. Pickwick’s heart was really too full to bear it, and he went upstairs to bed.\n\nNow, although the warder’s room was a very uncomfortable one (being, in every point of decoration and convenience, several hundred degrees inferior to the common infirmary of a county jail), it had at present the merit of being wholly deserted save by Mr. Pickwick himself. So, he sat down at the foot of his little iron bedstead, and began to wonder how much a year the warder made out of the dirty room. Having satisfied himself, by mathematical calculation, that the apartment was about equal in annual value to the freehold of a small street in the suburbs of London, he took to wondering what possible temptation could have induced a dingy-looking fly that was crawling over his pantaloons, to come into a close prison, when he had the choice of so many airy situations--a course of meditation which led him to the irresistible conclusion that the insect was insane. After settling this point, he began to be conscious that he was getting sleepy; whereupon he took his nightcap out of the pocket in which he had had the precaution to stow it in the morning, and, leisurely undressing himself, got into bed and fell asleep.\n\n‘Bravo! Heel over toe--cut and shuffle--pay away at it, Zephyr! I’m smothered if the opera house isn’t your proper hemisphere. Keep it up! Hooray!’ These expressions, delivered in a most boisterous tone, and accompanied with loud peals of laughter, roused Mr. Pickwick from one of those sound slumbers which, lasting in reality some half-hour, seem to the sleeper to have been protracted for three weeks or a month.\n\nThe voice had no sooner ceased than the room was shaken with such violence that the windows rattled in their frames, and the bedsteads trembled again. Mr. Pickwick started up, and remained for some minutes fixed in mute astonishment at the scene before him.\n\nOn the floor of the room, a man in a broad-skirted green coat, with corduroy knee-smalls and gray cotton stockings, was performing the most popular steps of a hornpipe, with a slang and burlesque caricature of grace and lightness, which, combined with the very appropriate character of his costume, was inexpressibly absurd. Another man, evidently very drunk, who had probably been tumbled into bed by his companions, was sitting up between the sheets, warbling as much as he could recollect of a comic song, with the most intensely sentimental feeling and expression; while a third, seated on one of the bedsteads, was applauding both performers with the air of a profound connoisseur, and encouraging them by such ebullitions of feeling as had already roused Mr. Pickwick from his sleep.\n\nThis last man was an admirable specimen of a class of gentry which never can be seen in full perfection but in such places--they may be met with, in an imperfect state, occasionally about stable-yards and Public- houses; but they never attain their full bloom except in these hot-beds, which would almost seem to be considerately provided by the legislature for the sole purpose of rearing them.\n\nHe was a tall fellow, with an olive complexion, long dark hair, and very thick bushy whiskers meeting under his chin. He wore no neckerchief, as he had been playing rackets all day, and his open shirt collar displayed their full luxuriance. On his head he wore one of the common eighteenpenny French skull-caps, with a gaudy tassel dangling therefrom, very happily in keeping with a common fustian coat. His legs, which, being long, were afflicted with weakness, graced a pair of Oxford- mixture trousers, made to show the full symmetry of those limbs. Being somewhat negligently braced, however, and, moreover, but imperfectly buttoned, they fell in a series of not the most graceful folds over a pair of shoes sufficiently down at heel to display a pair of very soiled white stockings. There was a rakish, vagabond smartness, and a kind of boastful rascality, about the whole man, that was worth a mine of gold.\n\nThis figure was the first to perceive that Mr. Pickwick was looking on; upon which he winked to the Zephyr, and entreated him, with mock gravity, not to wake the gentleman.\n\n‘Why, bless the gentleman’s honest heart and soul!’ said the Zephyr, turning round and affecting the extremity of surprise; ‘the gentleman is awake. Hem, Shakespeare! How do you do, Sir? How is Mary and Sarah, sir? and the dear old lady at home, Sir? Will you have the kindness to put my compliments into the first little parcel you’re sending that way, sir, and say that I would have sent ‘em before, only I was afraid they might be broken in the wagon, sir?’\n\n‘Don’t overwhelm the gentlemen with ordinary civilities when you see he’s anxious to have something to drink,’ said the gentleman with the whiskers, with a jocose air. ‘Why don’t you ask the gentleman what he’ll take?’\n\n‘Dear me, I quite forgot,’ replied the other. ‘What will you take, sir? Will you take port wine, sir, or sherry wine, sir? I can recommend the ale, sir; or perhaps you’d like to taste the porter, sir? Allow me to have the felicity of hanging up your nightcap, Sir.’\n\nWith this, the speaker snatched that article of dress from Mr. Pickwick’s head, and fixed it in a twinkling on that of the drunken man, who, firmly impressed with the belief that he was delighting a numerous assembly, continued to hammer away at the comic song in the most melancholy strains imaginable.\n\nTaking a man’s nightcap from his brow by violent means, and adjusting it on the head of an unknown gentleman, of dirty exterior, however ingenious a witticism in itself, is unquestionably one of those which come under the denomination of practical jokes. Viewing the matter precisely in this light, Mr. Pickwick, without the slightest intimation of his purpose, sprang vigorously out of bed, struck the Zephyr so smart a blow in the chest as to deprive him of a considerable portion of the commodity which sometimes bears his name, and then, recapturing his nightcap, boldly placed himself in an attitude of defence.\n\n‘Now,’ said Mr. Pickwick, gasping no less from excitement than from the expenditure of so much energy, ‘come on--both of you--both of you!’ With this liberal invitation the worthy gentleman communicated a revolving motion to his clenched fists, by way of appalling his antagonists with a display of science.\n\nIt might have been Mr. Pickwick’s very unexpected gallantry, or it might have been the complicated manner in which he had got himself out of bed, and fallen all in a mass upon the hornpipe man, that touched his adversaries. Touched they were; for, instead of then and there making an attempt to commit man-slaughter, as Mr. Pickwick implicitly believed they would have done, they paused, stared at each other a short time, and finally laughed outright.\n\n‘Well, you’re a trump, and I like you all the better for it,’ said the Zephyr. ‘Now jump into bed again, or you’ll catch the rheumatics. No malice, I hope?’ said the man, extending a hand the size of the yellow clump of fingers which sometimes swings over a glover’s door.\n\n‘Certainly not,’ said Mr. Pickwick, with great alacrity; for, now that the excitement was over, he began to feel rather cool about the legs.\n\n‘Allow me the H-onour,’ said the gentleman with the whiskers, presenting his dexter hand, and aspirating the h.\n\n‘With much pleasure, sir,’ said Mr. Pickwick; and having executed a very long and solemn shake, he got into bed again.\n\n‘My name is Smangle, sir,’ said the man with the whiskers.\n\n‘Oh,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Mine is Mivins,’ said the man in the stockings.\n\n‘I am delighted to hear it, sir,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Hem,’ coughed Mr. Smangle.\n\n‘Did you speak, sir?’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘No, I did not, sir,’ said Mr. Smangle.\n\nAll this was very genteel and pleasant; and, to make matters still more comfortable, Mr. Smangle assured Mr. Pickwick a great many more times that he entertained a very high respect for the feelings of a gentleman; which sentiment, indeed, did him infinite credit, as he could be in no wise supposed to understand them.\n\n‘Are you going through the court, sir?’ inquired Mr. Smangle.\n\n‘Through the what?’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Through the court--Portugal Street--the Court for Relief of--you know.’\n\n‘Oh, no,’ replied Mr. Pickwick. ‘No, I am not.’\n\n‘Going out, perhaps?’ suggested Mr. Mivins.\n\n‘I fear not,’ replied Mr. Pickwick. ‘I refuse to pay some damages, and am here in consequence.’\n\n‘Ah,’ said Mr. Smangle, ‘paper has been my ruin.’\n\n‘A stationer, I presume, Sir?’ said Mr. Pickwick innocently.\n\n‘Stationer! No, no; confound and curse me! Not so low as that. No trade. When I say paper, I mean bills.’\n\n‘Oh, you use the word in that sense. I see,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Damme! A gentleman must expect reverses,’ said Smangle. ‘What of that? Here am I in the Fleet Prison. Well; good. What then? I’m none the worse for that, am I?’\n\n‘Not a bit,’ replied Mr. Mivins. And he was quite right; for, so far from Mr. Smangle being any the worse for it, he was something the better, inasmuch as to qualify himself for the place, he had attained gratuitous possession of certain articles of jewellery, which, long before that, had found their way to the pawnbroker’s.\n\n‘Well; but come,’ said Mr. Smangle; ‘this is dry work. Let’s rinse our mouths with a drop of burnt sherry; the last-comer shall stand it, Mivins shall fetch it, and I’ll help to drink it. That’s a fair and gentlemanlike division of labour, anyhow. Curse me!’\n\nUnwilling to hazard another quarrel, Mr. Pickwick gladly assented to the proposition, and consigned the money to Mr. Mivins, who, as it was nearly eleven o’clock, lost no time in repairing to the coffee-room on his errand.\n\n‘I say,’ whispered Smangle, the moment his friend had left the room; ‘what did you give him?’\n\n‘Half a sovereign,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘He’s a devilish pleasant gentlemanly dog,’ said Mr. Smangle;--‘infernal pleasant. I don’t know anybody more so; but--’ Here Mr. Smangle stopped short, and shook his head dubiously.\n\n‘You don’t think there is any probability of his appropriating the money to his own use?’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Oh, no! Mind, I don’t say that; I expressly say that he’s a devilish gentlemanly fellow,’ said Mr. Smangle. ‘But I think, perhaps, if somebody went down, just to see that he didn’t dip his beak into the jug by accident, or make some confounded mistake in losing the money as he came upstairs, it would be as well. Here, you sir, just run downstairs, and look after that gentleman, will you?’\n\nThis request was addressed to a little timid-looking, nervous man, whose appearance bespoke great poverty, and who had been crouching on his bedstead all this while, apparently stupefied by the novelty of his situation.\n\n‘You know where the coffee-room is,’ said Smangle; ‘just run down, and tell that gentleman you’ve come to help him up with the jug. Or--stop-- I’ll tell you what--I’ll tell you how we’ll do him,’ said Smangle, with a cunning look.\n\n‘How?’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Send down word that he’s to spend the change in cigars. Capital thought. Run and tell him that; d’ye hear? They shan’t be wasted,’ continued Smangle, turning to Mr. Pickwick. ‘I’ll smoke ‘em.’\n\nThis manoeuvring was so exceedingly ingenious and, withal, performed with such immovable composure and coolness, that Mr. Pickwick would have had no wish to disturb it, even if he had had the power. In a short time Mr. Mivins returned, bearing the sherry, which Mr. Smangle dispensed in two little cracked mugs; considerately remarking, with reference to himself, that a gentleman must not be particular under such circumstances, and that, for his part, he was not too proud to drink out of the jug. In which, to show his sincerity, he forthwith pledged the company in a draught which half emptied it.\n\nAn excellent understanding having been by these means promoted, Mr. Smangle proceeded to entertain his hearers with a relation of divers romantic adventures in which he had been from time to time engaged, involving various interesting anecdotes of a thoroughbred horse, and a magnificent Jewess, both of surpassing beauty, and much coveted by the nobility and gentry of these kingdoms.\n\nLong before these elegant extracts from the biography of a gentleman were concluded, Mr. Mivins had betaken himself to bed, and had set in snoring for the night, leaving the timid stranger and Mr. Pickwick to the full benefit of Mr. Smangle’s experiences.\n\nNor were the two last-named gentlemen as much edified as they might have been by the moving passages narrated. Mr. Pickwick had been in a state of slumber for some time, when he had a faint perception of the drunken man bursting out afresh with the comic song, and receiving from Mr. Smangle a gentle intimation, through the medium of the water-jug, that his audience was not musically disposed. Mr. Pickwick then once again dropped off to sleep, with a confused consciousness that Mr. Smangle was still engaged in relating a long story, the chief point of which appeared to be that, on some occasion particularly stated and set forth, he had ‘done’ a bill and a gentleman at the same time.\n\nCHAPTER XLII. ILLUSTRATIVE, LIKE THE PRECEDING ONE, OF THE OLD PROVERB, THAT ADVERSITY BRINGS A MAN ACQUAINTED WITH STRANGE BEDFELLOWS-- LIKEWISE CONTAINING MR. PICKWICK’S EXTRAORDINARY AND STARTLING ANNOUNCEMENT TO MR. SAMUEL WELLER\nWhen Mr. Pickwick opened his eyes next morning, the first object upon which they rested was Samuel Weller, seated upon a small black portmanteau, intently regarding, apparently in a condition of profound abstraction, the stately figure of the dashing Mr. Smangle; while Mr. Smangle himself, who was already partially dressed, was seated on his bedstead, occupied in the desperately hopeless attempt of staring Mr. Weller out of countenance. We say desperately hopeless, because Sam, with a comprehensive gaze which took in Mr. Smangle’s cap, feet, head, face, legs, and whiskers, all at the same time, continued to look steadily on, with every demonstration of lively satisfaction, but with no more regard to Mr. Smangle’s personal sentiments on the subject than he would have displayed had he been inspecting a wooden statue, or a straw-embowelled Guy Fawkes.\n\n‘Well; will you know me again?’ said Mr. Smangle, with a frown.\n\n‘I’d svear to you anyveres, Sir,’ replied Sam cheerfully.\n\n‘Don’t be impertinent to a gentleman, Sir,’ said Mr. Smangle.\n\n‘Not on no account,’ replied Sam. ‘If you’ll tell me wen he wakes, I’ll be upon the wery best extra-super behaviour!’ This observation, having a remote tendency to imply that Mr. Smangle was no gentleman, kindled his ire.\n\n‘Mivins!’ said Mr. Smangle, with a passionate air.\n\n‘What’s the office?’ replied that gentleman from his couch.\n\n‘Who the devil is this fellow?’\n\n‘’Gad,’ said Mr. Mivins, looking lazily out from under the bed-clothes, ‘I ought to ask you that. Hasn’t he any business here?’\n\n‘No,’ replied Mr. Smangle.\n\n‘Then knock him downstairs, and tell him not to presume to get up till I come and kick him,’ rejoined Mr. Mivins; with this prompt advice that excellent gentleman again betook himself to slumber.\n\nThe conversation exhibiting these unequivocal symptoms of verging on the personal, Mr. Pickwick deemed it a fit point at which to interpose.\n\n‘Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Sir,’ rejoined that gentleman.\n\n‘Has anything new occurred since last night?’\n\n‘Nothin’ partickler, sir,’ replied Sam, glancing at Mr. Smangle’s whiskers; ‘the late prewailance of a close and confined atmosphere has been rayther favourable to the growth of veeds, of an alarmin’ and sangvinary natur; but vith that ‘ere exception things is quiet enough.’\n\n‘I shall get up,’ said Mr. Pickwick; ‘give me some clean things.’\n\nWhatever hostile intentions Mr. Smangle might have entertained, his thoughts were speedily diverted by the unpacking of the portmanteau; the contents of which appeared to impress him at once with a most favourable opinion, not only of Mr. Pickwick, but of Sam also, who, he took an early opportunity of declaring in a tone of voice loud enough for that eccentric personage to overhear, was a regular thoroughbred original, and consequently the very man after his own heart. As to Mr. Pickwick, the affection he conceived for him knew no limits.\n\n‘Now is there anything I can do for you, my dear Sir?’ said Smangle.\n\n‘Nothing that I am aware of, I am obliged to you,’ replied Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘No linen that you want sent to the washerwoman’s? I know a delightful washerwoman outside, that comes for my things twice a week; and, by Jove!--how devilish lucky!--this is the day she calls. Shall I put any of those little things up with mine? Don’t say anything about the trouble. Confound and curse it! if one gentleman under a cloud is not to put himself a little out of the way to assist another gentleman in the same condition, what’s human nature?’\n\nThus spake Mr. Smangle, edging himself meanwhile as near as possible to the portmanteau, and beaming forth looks of the most fervent and disinterested friendship.\n\n‘There’s nothing you want to give out for the man to brush, my dear creature, is there?’ resumed Smangle.\n\n‘Nothin’ whatever, my fine feller,’ rejoined Sam, taking the reply into his own mouth. ‘P’raps if vun of us wos to brush, without troubling the man, it ‘ud be more agreeable for all parties, as the schoolmaster said when the young gentleman objected to being flogged by the butler.’\n\n‘And there’s nothing I can send in my little box to the washer-woman’s, is there?’ said Smangle, turning from Sam to Mr. Pickwick, with an air of some discomfiture.\n\n‘Nothin’ whatever, Sir,’ retorted Sam; ‘I’m afeered the little box must be chock full o’ your own as it is.’\n\nThis speech was accompanied with such a very expressive look at that particular portion of Mr. Smangle’s attire, by the appearance of which the skill of laundresses in getting up gentlemen’s linen is generally tested, that he was fain to turn upon his heel, and, for the present at any rate, to give up all design on Mr. Pickwick’s purse and wardrobe. He accordingly retired in dudgeon to the racket-ground, where he made a light and whole-some breakfast on a couple of the cigars which had been purchased on the previous night.\n\nMr. Mivins, who was no smoker, and whose account for small articles of chandlery had also reached down to the bottom of the slate, and been ‘carried over’ to the other side, remained in bed, and, in his own words, ‘took it out in sleep.’\n\nAfter breakfasting in a small closet attached to the coffee-room, which bore the imposing title of the Snuggery, the temporary inmate of which, in consideration of a small additional charge, had the unspeakable advantage of overhearing all the conversation in the coffee-room aforesaid; and, after despatching Mr. Weller on some necessary errands, Mr. Pickwick repaired to the lodge, to consult Mr. Roker concerning his future accommodation.\n\n‘Accommodation, eh?’ said that gentleman, consulting a large book. ‘Plenty of that, Mr. Pickwick. Your chummage ticket will be on twenty- seven, in the third.’\n\n‘Oh,’ said Mr. Pickwick. ‘My what, did you say?’\n\n‘Your chummage ticket,’ replied Mr. Roker; ‘you’re up to that?’\n\n‘Not quite,’ replied Mr. Pickwick, with a smile.\n\n‘Why,’ said Mr. Roker, ‘it’s as plain as Salisbury. You’ll have a chummage ticket upon twenty-seven in the third, and them as is in the room will be your chums.’\n\n‘Are there many of them?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick dubiously.\n\n‘Three,’ replied Mr. Roker.\n\nMr. Pickwick coughed.\n\n‘One of ‘em’s a parson,’ said Mr. Roker, filling up a little piece of paper as he spoke; ‘another’s a butcher.’\n\n‘Eh?’ exclaimed Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘A butcher,’ repeated Mr. Roker, giving the nib of his pen a tap on the desk to cure it of a disinclination to mark. ‘What a thorough-paced goer he used to be sure-ly! You remember Tom Martin, Neddy?’ said Roker, appealing to another man in the lodge, who was paring the mud off his shoes with a five-and-twenty-bladed pocket-knife.\n\n‘I should think so,’ replied the party addressed, with a strong emphasis on the personal pronoun.\n\n‘Bless my dear eyes!’ said Mr. Roker, shaking his head slowly from side to side, and gazing abstractedly out of the grated windows before him, as if he were fondly recalling some peaceful scene of his early youth; ‘it seems but yesterday that he whopped the coal-heaver down Fox-under- the-Hill by the wharf there. I think I can see him now, a-coming up the Strand between the two street-keepers, a little sobered by the bruising, with a patch o’ winegar and brown paper over his right eyelid, and that ‘ere lovely bulldog, as pinned the little boy arterwards, a-following at his heels. What a rum thing time is, ain’t it, Neddy?’\n\nThe gentleman to whom these observations were addressed, who appeared of a taciturn and thoughtful cast, merely echoed the inquiry; Mr. Roker, shaking off the poetical and gloomy train of thought into which he had been betrayed, descended to the common business of life, and resumed his pen.\n\n‘Do you know what the third gentlemen is?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick, not very much gratified by this description of his future associates.\n\n‘What is that Simpson, Neddy?’ said Mr. Roker, turning to his companion.\n\n‘What Simpson?’ said Neddy.\n\n‘Why, him in twenty-seven in the third, that this gentleman’s going to be chummed on.’\n\n‘Oh, him!’ replied Neddy; ‘he’s nothing exactly. He was a horse chaunter: he’s a leg now.’\n\n‘Ah, so I thought,’ rejoined Mr. Roker, closing the book, and placing the small piece of paper in Mr. Pickwick’s hands. ‘That’s the ticket, sir.’\n\nVery much perplexed by this summary disposition of this person, Mr. Pickwick walked back into the prison, revolving in his mind what he had better do. Convinced, however, that before he took any other steps it would be advisable to see, and hold personal converse with, the three gentlemen with whom it was proposed to quarter him, he made the best of his way to the third flight.\n\nAfter groping about in the gallery for some time, attempting in the dim light to decipher the numbers on the different doors, he at length appealed to a pot-boy, who happened to be pursuing his morning occupation of gleaning for pewter.\n\n‘Which is twenty-seven, my good fellow?’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Five doors farther on,’ replied the pot-boy. ‘There’s the likeness of a man being hung, and smoking the while, chalked outside the door.’\n\nGuided by this direction, Mr. Pickwick proceeded slowly along the gallery until he encountered the ‘portrait of a gentleman,’ above described, upon whose countenance he tapped, with the knuckle of his forefinger--gently at first, and then audibly. After repeating this process several times without effect, he ventured to open the door and peep in.\n\nThere was only one man in the room, and he was leaning out of window as far as he could without overbalancing himself, endeavouring, with great perseverance, to spit upon the crown of the hat of a personal friend on the parade below. As neither speaking, coughing, sneezing, knocking, nor any other ordinary mode of attracting attention, made this person aware of the presence of a visitor, Mr. Pickwick, after some delay, stepped up to the window, and pulled him gently by the coat tail. The individual brought in his head and shoulders with great swiftness, and surveying Mr. Pickwick from head to foot, demanded in a surly tone what the-- something beginning with a capital H--he wanted.\n\n‘I believe,’ said Mr. Pickwick, consulting his ticket--‘I believe this is twenty-seven in the third?’\n\n‘Well?’ replied the gentleman.\n\n‘I have come here in consequence of receiving this bit of paper,’ rejoined Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Hand it over,’ said the gentleman.\n\nMr. Pickwick complied.\n\n‘I think Roker might have chummed you somewhere else,’ said Mr. Simpson (for it was the leg), after a very discontented sort of a pause.\n\nMr. Pickwick thought so also; but, under all the circumstances, he considered it a matter of sound policy to be silent.\n\nMr. Simpson mused for a few moments after this, and then, thrusting his head out of the window, gave a shrill whistle, and pronounced some word aloud, several times. What the word was, Mr. Pickwick could not distinguish; but he rather inferred that it must be some nickname which distinguished Mr. Martin, from the fact of a great number of gentlemen on the ground below, immediately proceeding to cry ‘Butcher!’ in imitation of the tone in which that useful class of society are wont, diurnally, to make their presence known at area railings.\n\nSubsequent occurrences confirmed the accuracy of Mr. Pickwick’s impression; for, in a few seconds, a gentleman, prematurely broad for his years, clothed in a professional blue jean frock and top-boots with circular toes, entered the room nearly out of breath, closely followed by another gentleman in very shabby black, and a sealskin cap. The latter gentleman, who fastened his coat all the way up to his chin by means of a pin and a button alternately, had a very coarse red face, and looked like a drunken chaplain; which, indeed, he was.\n\nThese two gentlemen having by turns perused Mr. Pickwick’s billet, the one expressed his opinion that it was ‘a rig,’ and the other his conviction that it was ‘a go.’ Having recorded their feelings in these very intelligible terms, they looked at Mr. Pickwick and each other in awkward silence.\n\n‘It’s an aggravating thing, just as we got the beds so snug,’ said the chaplain, looking at three dirty mattresses, each rolled up in a blanket; which occupied one corner of the room during the day, and formed a kind of slab, on which were placed an old cracked basin, ewer, and soap-dish, of common yellow earthenware, with a blue flower--‘very aggravating.’\n\nMr. Martin expressed the same opinion in rather stronger terms; Mr. Simpson, after having let a variety of expletive adjectives loose upon society without any substantive to accompany them, tucked up his sleeves, and began to wash the greens for dinner.\n\nWhile this was going on, Mr. Pickwick had been eyeing the room, which was filthily dirty, and smelt intolerably close. There was no vestige of either carpet, curtain, or blind. There was not even a closet in it. Unquestionably there were but few things to put away, if there had been one; but, however few in number, or small in individual amount, still, remnants of loaves and pieces of cheese, and damp towels, and scrags of meat, and articles of wearing apparel, and mutilated crockery, and bellows without nozzles, and toasting-forks without prongs, do present somewhat of an uncomfortable appearance when they are scattered about the floor of a small apartment, which is the common sitting and sleeping room of three idle men.\n\n‘I suppose this can be managed somehow,’ said the butcher, after a pretty long silence. ‘What will you take to go out?’\n\nI beg your pardon,’ replied Mr. Pickwick. ‘What did you say? I hardly understand you.’\n\n‘What will you take to be paid out?’ said the butcher. ‘The regular chummage is two-and-six. Will you take three bob?’\n\n‘And a bender,’ suggested the clerical gentleman.\n\n‘Well, I don’t mind that; it’s only twopence a piece more,’ said Mr. Martin. ‘What do you say, now? We’ll pay you out for three-and-sixpence a week. Come!’\n\n‘And stand a gallon of beer down,’ chimed in Mr. Simpson. ‘There!’\n\n‘And drink it on the spot,’ said the chaplain. ‘Now!’\n\n‘I really am so wholly ignorant of the rules of this place,’ returned Mr. Pickwick, ‘that I do not yet comprehend you. Can I live anywhere else? I thought I could not.’\n\nAt this inquiry Mr. Martin looked, with a countenance of excessive surprise, at his two friends, and then each gentleman pointed with his right thumb over his left shoulder. This action imperfectly described in words by the very feeble term of ‘over the left,’ when performed by any number of ladies or gentlemen who are accustomed to act in unison, has a very graceful and airy effect; its expression is one of light and playful sarcasm.\n\n‘Can you!’ repeated Mr. Martin, with a smile of pity.\n\n‘Well, if I knew as little of life as that, I’d eat my hat and swallow the buckle whole,’ said the clerical gentleman.\n\n‘So would I,’ added the sporting one solemnly.\n\nAfter this introductory preface, the three chums informed Mr. Pickwick, in a breath, that money was, in the Fleet, just what money was out of it; that it would instantly procure him almost anything he desired; and that, supposing he had it, and had no objection to spend it, if he only signified his wish to have a room to himself, he might take possession of one, furnished and fitted to boot, in half an hour’s time.\n\nWith this the parties separated, very much to their common satisfaction; Mr. Pickwick once more retracing his steps to the lodge, and the three companions adjourning to the coffee-room, there to spend the five shillings which the clerical gentleman had, with admirable prudence and foresight, borrowed of him for the purpose.\n\n‘I knowed it!’ said Mr. Roker, with a chuckle, when Mr. Pickwick stated the object with which he had returned. ‘Didn’t I say so, Neddy?’\n\nThe philosophical owner of the universal penknife growled an affirmative.\n\n‘I knowed you’d want a room for yourself, bless you!’ said Mr. Roker. ‘Let me see. You’ll want some furniture. You’ll hire that of me, I suppose? That’s the reg’lar thing.’\n\n‘With great pleasure,’ replied Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘There’s a capital room up in the coffee-room flight, that belongs to a Chancery prisoner,’ said Mr. Roker. ‘It’ll stand you in a pound a week. I suppose you don’t mind that?’\n\n‘Not at all,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Just step there with me,’ said Roker, taking up his hat with great alacrity; ‘the matter’s settled in five minutes. Lord! why didn’t you say at first that you was willing to come down handsome?’\n\nThe matter was soon arranged, as the turnkey had foretold. The Chancery prisoner had been there long enough to have lost his friends, fortune, home, and happiness, and to have acquired the right of having a room to himself. As he laboured, however, under the inconvenience of often wanting a morsel of bread, he eagerly listened to Mr. Pickwick’s proposal to rent the apartment, and readily covenanted and agreed to yield him up the sole and undisturbed possession thereof, in consideration of the weekly payment of twenty shillings; from which fund he furthermore contracted to pay out any person or persons that might be chummed upon it.\n\nAs they struck the bargain, Mr. Pickwick surveyed him with a painful interest. He was a tall, gaunt, cadaverous man, in an old greatcoat and slippers, with sunken cheeks, and a restless, eager eye. His lips were bloodless, and his bones sharp and thin. God help him! the iron teeth of confinement and privation had been slowly filing him down for twenty years.\n\n‘And where will you live meanwhile, Sir?’ said Mr. Pickwick, as he laid the amount of the first week’s rent, in advance, on the tottering table.\n\nThe man gathered up the money with a trembling hand, and replied that he didn’t know yet; he must go and see where he could move his bed to.\n\n‘I am afraid, sir,’ said Mr. Pickwick, laying his hand gently and compassionately on his arm--‘I am afraid you will have to live in some noisy, crowded place. Now, pray, consider this room your own when you want quiet, or when any of your friends come to see you.’\n\n‘Friends!’ interposed the man, in a voice which rattled in his throat. ‘if I lay dead at the bottom of the deepest mine in the world; tight screwed down and soldered in my coffin; rotting in the dark and filthy ditch that drags its slime along, beneath the foundations of this prison; I could not be more forgotten or unheeded than I am here. I am a dead man; dead to society, without the pity they bestow on those whose souls have passed to judgment. Friends to see me! My God! I have sunk, from the prime of life into old age, in this place, and there is not one to raise his hand above my bed when I lie dead upon it, and say, “It is a blessing he is gone!”’\n\nThe excitement, which had cast an unwonted light over the man’s face, while he spoke, subsided as he concluded; and pressing his withered hands together in a hasty and disordered manner, he shuffled from the room.\n\n‘Rides rather rusty,’ said Mr. Roker, with a smile. ‘Ah! they’re like the elephants. They feel it now and then, and it makes ‘em wild!’\n\nHaving made this deeply-sympathising remark, Mr. Roker entered upon his arrangements with such expedition, that in a short time the room was furnished with a carpet, six chairs, a table, a sofa bedstead, a tea- kettle, and various small articles, on hire, at the very reasonable rate of seven-and-twenty shillings and sixpence per week.\n\n‘Now, is there anything more we can do for you?’ inquired Mr. Roker, looking round with great satisfaction, and gaily chinking the first week’s hire in his closed fist.\n\n‘Why, yes,’ said Mr. Pickwick, who had been musing deeply for some time. ‘Are there any people here who run on errands, and so forth?’\n\n‘Outside, do you mean?’ inquired Mr. Roker.\n\n‘Yes. I mean who are able to go outside. Not prisoners.’\n\n‘Yes, there is,’ said Roker. ‘There’s an unfortunate devil, who has got a friend on the poor side, that’s glad to do anything of that sort. He’s been running odd jobs, and that, for the last two months. Shall I send him?’\n\n‘If you please,’ rejoined Mr. Pickwick. ‘Stay; no. The poor side, you say? I should like to see it. I’ll go to him myself.’\n\nThe poor side of a debtor’s prison is, as its name imports, that in which the most miserable and abject class of debtors are confined. A prisoner having declared upon the poor side, pays neither rent nor chummage. His fees, upon entering and leaving the jail, are reduced in amount, and he becomes entitled to a share of some small quantities of food: to provide which, a few charitable persons have, from time to time, left trifling legacies in their wills. Most of our readers will remember, that, until within a very few years past, there was a kind of iron cage in the wall of the Fleet Prison, within which was posted some man of hungry looks, who, from time to time, rattled a money-box, and exclaimed in a mournful voice, ‘Pray, remember the poor debtors; pray remember the poor debtors.’ The receipts of this box, when there were any, were divided among the poor prisoners; and the men on the poor side relieved each other in this degrading office.\n\nAlthough this custom has been abolished, and the cage is now boarded up, the miserable and destitute condition of these unhappy persons remains the same. We no longer suffer them to appeal at the prison gates to the charity and compassion of the passersby; but we still leave unblotted the leaves of our statute book, for the reverence and admiration of succeeding ages, the just and wholesome law which declares that the sturdy felon shall be fed and clothed, and that the penniless debtor shall be left to die of starvation and nakedness. This is no fiction. Not a week passes over our head, but, in every one of our prisons for debt, some of these men must inevitably expire in the slow agonies of want, if they were not relieved by their fellow-prisoners.\n\nTurning these things in his mind, as he mounted the narrow staircase at the foot of which Roker had left him, Mr. Pickwick gradually worked himself to the boiling-over point; and so excited was he with his reflections on this subject, that he had burst into the room to which he had been directed, before he had any distinct recollection, either of the place in which he was, or of the object of his visit.\n\nThe general aspect of the room recalled him to himself at once; but he had no sooner cast his eye on the figure of a man who was brooding over the dusty fire, than, letting his hat fall on the floor, he stood perfectly fixed and immovable with astonishment.\n\nYes; in tattered garments, and without a coat; his common calico shirt, yellow and in rags; his hair hanging over his face; his features changed with suffering, and pinched with famine--there sat Mr. Alfred Jingle; his head resting on his hands, his eyes fixed upon the fire, and his whole appearance denoting misery and dejection!\n\nNear him, leaning listlessly against the wall, stood a strong-built countryman, flicking with a worn-out hunting-whip the top-boot that adorned his right foot; his left being thrust into an old slipper. Horses, dogs, and drink had brought him there, pell-mell. There was a rusty spur on the solitary boot, which he occasionally jerked into the empty air, at the same time giving the boot a smart blow, and muttering some of the sounds by which a sportsman encourages his horse. He was riding, in imagination, some desperate steeplechase at that moment. Poor wretch! He never rode a match on the swiftest animal in his costly stud, with half the speed at which he had torn along the course that ended in the Fleet.\n\nOn the opposite side of the room an old man was seated on a small wooden box, with his eyes riveted on the floor, and his face settled into an expression of the deepest and most hopeless despair. A young girl--his little grand-daughter--was hanging about him, endeavouring, with a thousand childish devices, to engage his attention; but the old man neither saw nor heard her. The voice that had been music to him, and the eyes that had been light, fell coldly on his senses. His limbs were shaking with disease, and the palsy had fastened on his mind.\n\nThere were two or three other men in the room, congregated in a little knot, and noiselessly talking among themselves. There was a lean and haggard woman, too--a prisoner’s wife--who was watering, with great solicitude, the wretched stump of a dried-up, withered plant, which, it was plain to see, could never send forth a green leaf again--too true an emblem, perhaps, of the office she had come there to discharge.\n\nSuch were the objects which presented themselves to Mr. Pickwick’s view, as he looked round him in amazement. The noise of some one stumbling hastily into the room, roused him. Turning his eyes towards the door, they encountered the new-comer; and in him, through his rags and dirt, he recognised the familiar features of Mr. Job Trotter.\n\n‘Mr. Pickwick!’ exclaimed Job aloud.\n\n‘Eh?’ said Jingle, starting from his seat.\n\n‘Mr ----! So it is--queer place--strange things--serves me right--very.’ Mr. Jingle thrust his hands into the place where his trousers pockets used to be, and, dropping his chin upon his breast, sank back into his chair.\n\nMr. Pickwick was affected; the two men looked so very miserable. The sharp, involuntary glance Jingle had cast at a small piece of raw loin of mutton, which Job had brought in with him, said more of their reduced state than two hours’ explanation could have done. Mr. Pickwick looked mildly at Jingle, and said--\n\n‘I should like to speak to you in private. Will you step out for an instant?’\n\n‘Certainly,’ said Jingle, rising hastily. ‘Can’t step far--no danger of overwalking yourself here--spike park--grounds pretty--romantic, but not extensive--open for public inspection--family always in town-- housekeeper desperately careful--very.’\n\n‘You have forgotten your coat,’ said Mr. Pickwick, as they walked out to the staircase, and closed the door after them.\n\n‘Eh?’ said Jingle. ‘Spout--dear relation--uncle Tom--couldn’t help it-- must eat, you know. Wants of nature--and all that.’\n\n‘What do you mean?’\n\n‘Gone, my dear sir--last coat--can’t help it. Lived on a pair of boots, whole fortnight. Silk umbrella--ivory handle--week--fact--honour--ask Job--knows it.’\n\n‘Lived for three weeks upon a pair of boots, and a silk umbrella with an ivory handle!’ exclaimed Mr. Pickwick, who had only heard of such things in shipwrecks or read of them in Constable’s Miscellany.\n\n‘True,’ said Jingle, nodding his head. ‘Pawnbroker’s shop--duplicates here--small sums--mere nothing--all rascals.’\n\n‘Oh,’ said Mr. Pickwick, much relieved by this explanation; ‘I understand you. You have pawned your wardrobe.’\n\n‘Everything--Job’s too--all shirts gone--never mind--saves washing. Nothing soon--lie in bed--starve--die--inquest--little bone-house--poor prisoner--common necessaries--hush it up--gentlemen of the jury-- warden’s tradesmen--keep it snug--natural death--coroner’s order-- workhouse funeral--serve him right--all over--drop the curtain.’\n\nJingle delivered this singular summary of his prospects in life, with his accustomed volubility, and with various twitches of the countenance to counterfeit smiles. Mr. Pickwick easily perceived that his recklessness was assumed, and looking him full, but not unkindly, in the face, saw that his eyes were moist with tears.\n\n‘Good fellow,’ said Jingle, pressing his hand, and turning his head away. ‘Ungrateful dog--boyish to cry--can’t help it--bad fever--weak-- ill--hungry. Deserved it all--but suffered much--very.’ Wholly unable to keep up appearances any longer, and perhaps rendered worse by the effort he had made, the dejected stroller sat down on the stairs, and, covering his face with his hands, sobbed like a child.\n\n‘Come, come,’ said Mr. Pickwick, with considerable emotion, ‘we will see what can be done, when I know all about the matter. Here, Job; where is that fellow?’\n\n‘Here, sir,’ replied Job, presenting himself on the staircase. We have described him, by the bye, as having deeply-sunken eyes, in the best of times. In his present state of want and distress, he looked as if those features had gone out of town altogether.\n\n‘Here, sir,’ cried Job.\n\n‘Come here, sir,’ said Mr. Pickwick, trying to look stern, with four large tears running down his waistcoat. ‘Take that, sir.’\n\nTake what? In the ordinary acceptation of such language, it should have been a blow. As the world runs, it ought to have been a sound, hearty cuff; for Mr. Pickwick had been duped, deceived, and wronged by the destitute outcast who was now wholly in his power. Must we tell the truth? It was something from Mr. Pickwick’s waistcoat pocket, which chinked as it was given into Job’s hand, and the giving of which, somehow or other imparted a sparkle to the eye, and a swelling to the heart, of our excellent old friend, as he hurried away.\n\nSam had returned when Mr. Pickwick reached his own room, and was inspecting the arrangements that had been made for his comfort, with a kind of grim satisfaction which was very pleasant to look upon. Having a decided objection to his master’s being there at all, Mr. Weller appeared to consider it a high moral duty not to appear too much pleased with anything that was done, said, suggested, or proposed.\n\n‘Well, Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Well, sir,’ replied Mr. Weller.\n\n‘Pretty comfortable now, eh, Sam?’\n\n‘Pretty vell, sir,’ responded Sam, looking round him in a disparaging manner.\n\n‘Have you seen Mr. Tupman and our other friends?’\n\n‘Yes, I have seen ‘em, sir, and they’re a-comin’ to-morrow, and wos wery much surprised to hear they warn’t to come to-day,’ replied Sam.\n\n‘You have brought the things I wanted?’\n\nMr. Weller in reply pointed to various packages which he had arranged, as neatly as he could, in a corner of the room.\n\n‘Very well, Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick, after a little hesitation; ‘listen to what I am going to say, Sam.’\n\n‘Cert’nly, Sir,’ rejoined Mr. Weller; ‘fire away, Sir.’\n\n‘I have felt from the first, Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick, with much solemnity, ‘that this is not the place to bring a young man to.’\n\n‘Nor an old ‘un neither, Sir,’ observed Mr. Weller.\n\n‘You’re quite right, Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick; ‘but old men may come here through their own heedlessness and unsuspicion, and young men may be brought here by the selfishness of those they serve. It is better for those young men, in every point of view, that they should not remain here. Do you understand me, Sam?’\n\n‘Vy no, Sir, I do not,’ replied Mr. Weller doggedly.\n\n‘Try, Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Vell, sir,’ rejoined Sam, after a short pause, ‘I think I see your drift; and if I do see your drift, it’s my ‘pinion that you’re a-comin’ it a great deal too strong, as the mail-coachman said to the snowstorm, ven it overtook him.’\n\n‘I see you comprehend me, Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick. ‘Independently of my wish that you should not be idling about a place like this, for years to come, I feel that for a debtor in the Fleet to be attended by his manservant is a monstrous absurdity. Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘for a time you must leave me.’\n\n‘Oh, for a time, eh, sir?’ rejoined Mr. Weller rather sarcastically.\n\n‘Yes, for the time that I remain here,’ said Mr. Pickwick. ‘Your wages I shall continue to pay. Any one of my three friends will be happy to take you, were it only out of respect to me. And if I ever do leave this place, Sam,’ added Mr. Pickwick, with assumed cheerfulness--‘if I do, I pledge you my word that you shall return to me instantly.’\n\n‘Now I’ll tell you wot it is, Sir,’ said Mr. Weller, in a grave and solemn voice. ‘This here sort o’ thing won’t do at all, so don’t let’s hear no more about it.’\n\nI am serious, and resolved, Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘You air, air you, sir?’ inquired Mr. Weller firmly. ‘Wery good, Sir; then so am I.’\n\nThus speaking, Mr. Weller fixed his hat on his head with great precision, and abruptly left the room.\n\n‘Sam!’ cried Mr. Pickwick, calling after him, ‘Sam! Here!’\n\nBut the long gallery ceased to re-echo the sound of footsteps. Sam Weller was gone.\n\n# CHAPTER XLIII. SHOWING HOW MR. SAMUEL WELLER GOT INTO DIFFICULTIES\n\nIn a lofty room, ill-lighted and worse ventilated, situated in Portugal Street, Lincoln’s Inn Fields, there sit nearly the whole year round, one, two, three, or four gentlemen in wigs, as the case may be, with little writing-desks before them, constructed after the fashion of those used by the judges of the land, barring the French polish. There is a box of barristers on their right hand; there is an enclosure of insolvent debtors on their left; and there is an inclined plane of most especially dirty faces in their front. These gentlemen are the Commissioners of the Insolvent Court, and the place in which they sit, is the Insolvent Court itself.\n\nIt is, and has been, time out of mind, the remarkable fate of this court to be, somehow or other, held and understood, by the general consent of all the destitute shabby-genteel people in London, as their common resort, and place of daily refuge. It is always full. The steams of beer and spirits perpetually ascend to the ceiling, and, being condensed by the heat, roll down the walls like rain; there are more old suits of clothes in it at one time, than will be offered for sale in all Houndsditch in a twelvemonth; more unwashed skins and grizzly beards than all the pumps and shaving-shops between Tyburn and Whitechapel could render decent, between sunrise and sunset.\n\nIt must not be supposed that any of these people have the least shadow of business in, or the remotest connection with, the place they so indefatigably attend. If they had, it would be no matter of surprise, and the singularity of the thing would cease. Some of them sleep during the greater part of the sitting; others carry small portable dinners wrapped in pocket-handkerchiefs or sticking out of their worn-out pockets, and munch and listen with equal relish; but no one among them was ever known to have the slightest personal interest in any case that was ever brought forward. Whatever they do, there they sit from the first moment to the last. When it is heavy, rainy weather, they all come in, wet through; and at such times the vapours of the court are like those of a fungus-pit.\n\nA casual visitor might suppose this place to be a temple dedicated to the Genius of Seediness. There is not a messenger or process-server attached to it, who wears a coat that was made for him; not a tolerably fresh, or wholesome-looking man in the whole establishment, except a little white-headed apple-faced tipstaff, and even he, like an ill- conditioned cherry preserved in brandy, seems to have artificially dried and withered up into a state of preservation to which he can lay no natural claim. The very barristers’ wigs are ill-powdered, and their curls lack crispness.\n\nBut the attorneys, who sit at a large bare table below the commissioners, are, after all, the greatest curiosities. The professional establishment of the more opulent of these gentlemen, consists of a blue bag and a boy; generally a youth of the Jewish persuasion. They have no fixed offices, their legal business being transacted in the parlours of public-houses, or the yards of prisons, whither they repair in crowds, and canvass for customers after the manner of omnibus cads. They are of a greasy and mildewed appearance; and if they can be said to have any vices at all, perhaps drinking and cheating are the most conspicuous among them. Their residences are usually on the outskirts of ‘the Rules,’ chiefly lying within a circle of one mile from the obelisk in St. George’s Fields. Their looks are not prepossessing, and their manners are peculiar.\n\nMr. Solomon Pell, one of this learned body, was a fat, flabby, pale man, in a surtout which looked green one minute, and brown the next, with a velvet collar of the same chameleon tints. His forehead was narrow, his face wide, his head large, and his nose all on one side, as if Nature, indignant with the propensities she observed in him in his birth, had given it an angry tweak which it had never recovered. Being short-necked and asthmatic, however, he respired principally through this feature; so, perhaps, what it wanted in ornament, it made up in usefulness.\n\n‘I’m sure to bring him through it,’ said Mr. Pell.\n\n‘Are you, though?’ replied the person to whom the assurance was pledged.\n\n‘Certain sure,’ replied Pell; ‘but if he’d gone to any irregular practitioner, mind you, I wouldn’t have answered for the consequences.’\n\n‘Ah!’ said the other, with open mouth.\n\n‘No, that I wouldn’t,’ said Mr. Pell; and he pursed up his lips, frowned, and shook his head mysteriously.\n\nNow, the place where this discourse occurred was the public-house just opposite to the Insolvent Court; and the person with whom it was held was no other than the elder Mr. Weller, who had come there, to comfort and console a friend, whose petition to be discharged under the act, was to be that day heard, and whose attorney he was at that moment consulting.\n\n‘And vere is George?’ inquired the old gentleman.\n\nMr. Pell jerked his head in the direction of a back parlour, whither Mr. Weller at once repairing, was immediately greeted in the warmest and most flattering manner by some half-dozen of his professional brethren, in token of their gratification at his arrival. The insolvent gentleman, who had contracted a speculative but imprudent passion for horsing long stages, which had led to his present embarrassments, looked extremely well, and was soothing the excitement of his feelings with shrimps and porter.\n\nThe salutation between Mr. Weller and his friends was strictly confined to the freemasonry of the craft; consisting of a jerking round of the right wrist, and a tossing of the little finger into the air at the same time. We once knew two famous coachmen (they are dead now, poor fellows) who were twins, and between whom an unaffected and devoted attachment existed. They passed each other on the Dover road, every day, for twenty-four years, never exchanging any other greeting than this; and yet, when one died, the other pined away, and soon afterwards followed him!\n\n‘Vell, George,’ said Mr. Weller senior, taking off his upper coat, and seating himself with his accustomed gravity. ‘How is it? All right behind, and full inside?’\n\n‘All right, old feller,’ replied the embarrassed gentleman.\n\n‘Is the gray mare made over to anybody?’ inquired Mr. Weller anxiously.\n\nGeorge nodded in the affirmative.\n\n‘Vell, that’s all right,’ said Mr. Weller. ‘Coach taken care on, also?’\n\n‘Con-signed in a safe quarter,’ replied George, wringing the heads off half a dozen shrimps, and swallowing them without any more ado.\n\n‘Wery good, wery good,’ said Mr. Weller. ‘Alvays see to the drag ven you go downhill. Is the vay-bill all clear and straight for’erd?’\n\n‘The schedule, sir,’ said Pell, guessing at Mr. Weller’s meaning, ‘the schedule is as plain and satisfactory as pen and ink can make it.’\n\nMr. Weller nodded in a manner which bespoke his inward approval of these arrangements; and then, turning to Mr. Pell, said, pointing to his friend George--\n\n‘Ven do you take his cloths off?’\n\n‘Why,’ replied Mr. Pell, ‘he stands third on the opposed list, and I should think it would be his turn in about half an hour. I told my clerk to come over and tell us when there was a chance.’\n\nMr. Weller surveyed the attorney from head to foot with great admiration, and said emphatically--\n\n‘And what’ll you take, sir?’\n\n‘Why, really,’ replied Mr. Pell, ‘you’re very--. Upon my word and honour, I’m not in the habit of--. It’s so very early in the morning, that, actually, I am almost--. Well, you may bring me threepenn’orth of rum, my dear.’\n\nThe officiating damsel, who had anticipated the order before it was given, set the glass of spirits before Pell, and retired.\n\n‘Gentlemen,’ said Mr. Pell, looking round upon the company, ‘success to your friend! I don’t like to boast, gentlemen; it’s not my way; but I can’t help saying, that, if your friend hadn’t been fortunate enough to fall into hands that--But I won’t say what I was going to say. Gentlemen, my service to you.’ Having emptied the glass in a twinkling, Mr. Pell smacked his lips, and looked complacently round on the assembled coachmen, who evidently regarded him as a species of divinity.\n\n‘Let me see,’ said the legal authority. ‘What was I a-saying, gentlemen?’\n\n‘I think you was remarkin’ as you wouldn’t have no objection to another o’ the same, Sir,’ said Mr. Weller, with grave facetiousness.\n\n‘Ha, ha!’ laughed Mr. Pell. ‘Not bad, not bad. A professional man, too! At this time of the morning, it would be rather too good a--Well, I don’t know, my dear--you may do that again, if you please. Hem!’\n\nThis last sound was a solemn and dignified cough, in which Mr. Pell, observing an indecent tendency to mirth in some of his auditors, considered it due to himself to indulge.\n\n‘The late Lord Chancellor, gentlemen, was very fond of me,’ said Mr. Pell.\n\n‘And wery creditable in him, too,’ interposed Mr. Weller.\n\n‘Hear, hear,’ assented Mr. Pell’s client. ‘Why shouldn’t he be?\n\n‘Ah! Why, indeed!’ said a very red-faced man, who had said nothing yet, and who looked extremely unlikely to say anything more. ‘Why shouldn’t he?’\n\nA murmur of assent ran through the company.\n\n‘I remember, gentlemen,’ said Mr. Pell, ‘dining with him on one occasion; there was only us two, but everything as splendid as if twenty people had been expected--the great seal on a dumb-waiter at his right hand, and a man in a bag-wig and suit of armour guarding the mace with a drawn sword and silk stockings--which is perpetually done, gentlemen, night and day; when he said, “Pell,” he said, “no false delicacy, Pell. You’re a man of talent; you can get anybody through the Insolvent Court, Pell; and your country should be proud of you.” Those were his very words. “My Lord,” I said, “you flatter me.”--“Pell,” he said, “if I do, I’m damned.”’\n\n‘Did he say that?’ inquired Mr. Weller.\n\n‘He did,’ replied Pell.\n\n‘Vell, then,’ said Mr. Weller, ‘I say Parliament ought to ha’ took it up; and if he’d been a poor man, they would ha’ done it.’\n\n‘But, my dear friend,’ argued Mr. Pell, ‘it was in confidence.’\n\n‘In what?’ said Mr. Weller.\n\n‘In confidence.’\n\n‘Oh! wery good,’ replied Mr. Weller, after a little reflection. ‘If he damned hisself in confidence, o’ course that was another thing.’\n\n‘Of course it was,’ said Mr. Pell. ‘The distinction’s obvious, you will perceive.’\n\n‘Alters the case entirely,’ said Mr. Weller. ‘Go on, Sir.’\n\nNo, I will not go on, Sir,’ said Mr. Pell, in a low and serious tone. ‘You have reminded me, Sir, that this conversation was private--private and confidential, gentlemen. Gentlemen, I am a professional man. It may be that I am a good deal looked up to, in my profession--it may be that I am not. Most people know. I say nothing. Observations have already been made, in this room, injurious to the reputation of my noble friend. You will excuse me, gentlemen; I was imprudent. I feel that I have no right to mention this matter without his concurrence. Thank you, Sir; thank you.’ Thus delivering himself, Mr. Pell thrust his hands into his pockets, and, frowning grimly around, rattled three halfpence with terrible determination.\n\nThis virtuous resolution had scarcely been formed, when the boy and the blue bag, who were inseparable companions, rushed violently into the room, and said (at least the boy did, for the blue bag took no part in the announcement) that the case was coming on directly. The intelligence was no sooner received than the whole party hurried across the street, and began to fight their way into court--a preparatory ceremony, which has been calculated to occupy, in ordinary cases, from twenty-five minutes to thirty.\n\nMr. Weller, being stout, cast himself at once into the crowd, with the desperate hope of ultimately turning up in some place which would suit him. His success was not quite equal to his expectations; for having neglected to take his hat off, it was knocked over his eyes by some unseen person, upon whose toes he had alighted with considerable force. Apparently this individual regretted his impetuosity immediately afterwards, for, muttering an indistinct exclamation of surprise, he dragged the old man out into the hall, and, after a violent struggle, released his head and face.\n\n‘Samivel!’ exclaimed Mr. Weller, when he was thus enabled to behold his rescuer.\n\nSam nodded.\n‘You’re a dutiful and affectionate little boy, you are, ain’t you,’ said Mr. Weller, ‘to come a-bonnetin’ your father in his old age?’\n\n‘How should I know who you wos?’ responded the son. ‘Do you s’pose I wos to tell you by the weight o’ your foot?’\n\n‘Vell, that’s wery true, Sammy,’ replied Mr. Weller, mollified at once; ‘but wot are you a-doin’ on here? Your gov’nor can’t do no good here, Sammy. They won’t pass that werdick, they won’t pass it, Sammy.’ And Mr. Weller shook his head with legal solemnity.\n\n‘Wot a perwerse old file it is!’ exclaimed Sam, ‘always a-goin’ on about werdicks and alleybis and that. Who said anything about the werdick?’\n\nMr. Weller made no reply, but once more shook his head most learnedly.\n\n‘Leave off rattlin’ that ‘ere nob o’ yourn, if you don’t want it to come off the springs altogether,’ said Sam impatiently, ‘and behave reasonable. I vent all the vay down to the Markis o’ Granby, arter you, last night.’\n\n‘Did you see the Marchioness o’ Granby, Sammy?’ inquired Mr. Weller, with a sigh.\n\n‘Yes, I did,’ replied Sam.\n\n‘How wos the dear creetur a-lookin’?’\n\n‘Wery queer,’ said Sam. ‘I think she’s a-injurin’ herself gradivally vith too much o’ that ‘ere pine-apple rum, and other strong medicines of the same natur.’\n\n‘You don’t mean that, Sammy?’ said the senior earnestly.\n\n‘I do, indeed,’ replied the junior.\n\nMr. Weller seized his son’s hand, clasped it, and let it fall. There was an expression on his countenance in doing so--not of dismay or apprehension, but partaking more of the sweet and gentle character of hope. A gleam of resignation, and even of cheerfulness, passed over his face too, as he slowly said, ‘I ain’t quite certain, Sammy; I wouldn’t like to say I wos altogether positive, in case of any subsekent disappointment, but I rayther think, my boy, I rayther think, that the shepherd’s got the liver complaint!’\n\n‘Does he look bad?’ inquired Sam.\n\n‘He’s uncommon pale,’ replied his father, ‘’cept about the nose, which is redder than ever. His appetite is wery so-so, but he imbibes wonderful.’\n\nSome thoughts of the rum appeared to obtrude themselves on Mr. Weller’s mind, as he said this; for he looked gloomy and thoughtful; but he very shortly recovered, as was testified by a perfect alphabet of winks, in which he was only wont to indulge when particularly pleased.\n\n‘Vell, now,’ said Sam, ‘about my affair. Just open them ears o’ yourn, and don’t say nothin’ till I’ve done.’ With this preface, Sam related, as succinctly as he could, the last memorable conversation he had had with Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Stop there by himself, poor creetur!’ exclaimed the elder Mr. Weller, ‘without nobody to take his part! It can’t be done, Samivel, it can’t be done.’\n\n‘O’ course it can’t,’ asserted Sam: ‘I know’d that, afore I came.’\n\nWhy, they’ll eat him up alive, Sammy,’ exclaimed Mr. Weller.\n\nSam nodded his concurrence in the opinion.\n\n‘He goes in rayther raw, Sammy,’ said Mr. Weller metaphorically, ‘and he’ll come out, done so ex-ceedin’ brown, that his most formiliar friends won’t know him. Roast pigeon’s nothin’ to it, Sammy.’\n\nAgain Sam Weller nodded.\n\n‘It oughtn’t to be, Samivel,’ said Mr. Weller gravely.\n\n‘It mustn’t be,’ said Sam.\n\n‘Cert’nly not,’ said Mr. Weller.\n\n‘Vell now,’ said Sam, ‘you’ve been a-prophecyin’ away, wery fine, like a red-faced Nixon, as the sixpenny books gives picters on.’\n\n‘Who wos he, Sammy?’ inquired Mr. Weller.\n\n‘Never mind who he was,’ retorted Sam; ‘he warn’t a coachman; that’s enough for you.’\n\nI know’d a ostler o’ that name,’ said Mr. Weller, musing.\n\n‘It warn’t him,’ said Sam. ‘This here gen’l’m’n was a prophet.’\n\n‘Wot’s a prophet?’ inquired Mr. Weller, looking sternly on his son.\n\n‘Wy, a man as tells what’s a-goin’ to happen,’ replied Sam.\n\n‘I wish I’d know’d him, Sammy,’ said Mr. Weller. ‘P’raps he might ha’ throw’d a small light on that ‘ere liver complaint as we wos a-speakin’ on, just now. Hows’ever, if he’s dead, and ain’t left the bisness to nobody, there’s an end on it. Go on, Sammy,’ said Mr. Weller, with a sigh.\n\n‘Well,’ said Sam, ‘you’ve been a-prophecyin’ avay about wot’ll happen to the gov’ner if he’s left alone. Don’t you see any way o’ takin’ care on him?’\n\n‘No, I don’t, Sammy,’ said Mr. Weller, with a reflective visage.\n\n‘No vay at all?’ inquired Sam.\n\n‘No vay,’ said Mr. Weller, ‘unless’--and a gleam of intelligence lighted up his countenance as he sank his voice to a whisper, and applied his mouth to the ear of his offspring--‘unless it is getting him out in a turn-up bedstead, unbeknown to the turnkeys, Sammy, or dressin’ him up like a old ‘ooman vith a green wail.’\n\nSam Weller received both of these suggestions with unexpected contempt, and again propounded his question.\n\n‘No,’ said the old gentleman; ‘if he von’t let you stop there, I see no vay at all. It’s no thoroughfare, Sammy, no thoroughfare.’\n\n‘Well, then, I’ll tell you wot it is,’ said Sam, ‘I’ll trouble you for the loan of five-and-twenty pound.’\n\n‘Wot good’ll that do?’ inquired Mr. Weller.\n\n‘Never mind,’ replied Sam. ‘P’raps you may ask for it five minits arterwards; p’raps I may say I von’t pay, and cut up rough. You von’t think o’ arrestin’ your own son for the money, and sendin’ him off to the Fleet, will you, you unnat’ral wagabone?’\n\nAt this reply of Sam’s, the father and son exchanged a complete code of telegraph nods and gestures, after which, the elder Mr. Weller sat himself down on a stone step and laughed till he was purple.\n\n‘Wot a old image it is!’ exclaimed Sam, indignant at this loss of time. ‘What are you a-settin’ down there for, con-wertin’ your face into a street-door knocker, wen there’s so much to be done. Where’s the money?’\n\n‘In the boot, Sammy, in the boot,’ replied Mr. Weller, composing his features. ‘Hold my hat, Sammy.’\n\nHaving divested himself of this encumbrance, Mr. Weller gave his body a sudden wrench to one side, and by a dexterous twist, contrived to get his right hand into a most capacious pocket, from whence, after a great deal of panting and exertion, he extricated a pocket-book of the large octavo size, fastened by a huge leathern strap. From this ledger he drew forth a couple of whiplashes, three or four buckles, a little sample-bag of corn, and, finally, a small roll of very dirty bank-notes, from which he selected the required amount, which he handed over to Sam.\n\n‘And now, Sammy,’ said the old gentleman, when the whip-lashes, and the buckles, and the samples, had been all put back, and the book once more deposited at the bottom of the same pocket, ‘now, Sammy, I know a gen’l’m’n here, as’ll do the rest o’ the bisness for us, in no time--a limb o’ the law, Sammy, as has got brains like the frogs, dispersed all over his body, and reachin’ to the wery tips of his fingers; a friend of the Lord Chancellorship’s, Sammy, who’d only have to tell him what he wanted, and he’d lock you up for life, if that wos all.’\n\n‘I say,’ said Sam, ‘none o’ that.’\n\n‘None o’ wot?’ inquired Mr. Weller.\n\n‘Wy, none o’ them unconstitootional ways o’ doin’ it,’ retorted Sam. ‘The have-his-carcass, next to the perpetual motion, is vun of the blessedest things as wos ever made. I’ve read that ‘ere in the newspapers wery of’en.’\n\n‘Well, wot’s that got to do vith it?’ inquired Mr. Weller.\n\n‘Just this here,’ said Sam, ‘that I’ll patronise the inwention, and go in, that vay. No visperin’s to the Chancellorship--I don’t like the notion. It mayn’t be altogether safe, vith reference to gettin’ out agin.’\n\nDeferring to his son’s feeling upon this point, Mr. Weller at once sought the erudite Solomon Pell, and acquainted him with his desire to issue a writ, instantly, for the sum of twenty-five pounds, and costs of process; to be executed without delay upon the body of one Samuel Weller; the charges thereby incurred, to be paid in advance to Solomon Pell.\n\nThe attorney was in high glee, for the embarrassed coach-horser was ordered to be discharged forthwith. He highly approved of Sam’s attachment to his master; declared that it strongly reminded him of his own feelings of devotion to his friend, the Chancellor; and at once led the elder Mr. Weller down to the Temple, to swear the affidavit of debt, which the boy, with the assistance of the blue bag, had drawn up on the spot.\n\nMeanwhile, Sam, having been formally introduced to the whitewashed gentleman and his friends, as the offspring of Mr. Weller, of the Belle Savage, was treated with marked distinction, and invited to regale himself with them in honour of the occasion--an invitation which he was by no means backward in accepting.\n\nThe mirth of gentlemen of this class is of a grave and quiet character, usually; but the present instance was one of peculiar festivity, and they relaxed in proportion. After some rather tumultuous toasting of the Chief Commissioner and Mr. Solomon Pell, who had that day displayed such transcendent abilities, a mottled-faced gentleman in a blue shawl proposed that somebody should sing a song. The obvious suggestion was, that the mottled-faced gentleman, being anxious for a song, should sing it himself; but this the mottled-faced gentleman sturdily, and somewhat offensively, declined to do. Upon which, as is not unusual in such cases, a rather angry colloquy ensued.\n\n‘Gentlemen,’ said the coach-horser, ‘rather than disturb the harmony of this delightful occasion, perhaps Mr. Samuel Weller will oblige the company.’\n\n‘Raly, gentlemen,’ said Sam, ‘I’m not wery much in the habit o’ singin’ without the instrument; but anythin’ for a quiet life, as the man said wen he took the sitivation at the lighthouse.’\n\nWith this prelude, Mr. Samuel Weller burst at once into the following wild and beautiful legend, which, under the impression that it is not generally known, we take the liberty of quoting. We would beg to call particular attention to the monosyllable at the end of the second and fourth lines, which not only enables the singer to take breath at those points, but greatly assists the metre.\n\n# I\n\nBold Turpin vunce, on Hounslow Heath, His bold mare Bess bestrode--er; Ven there he see’d the Bishop’s coach A-coming along the road--er. So he gallops close to the ‘orse’s legs, And he claps his head vithin; And the Bishop says, ‘Sure as eggs is eggs, This here’s the bold Turpin!’\n\nAnd the Bishop says, ‘Sure as eggs is eggs, This here’s the bold Turpin!’\n\n# II\n\nSays Turpin, ‘You shall eat your words, With a sarse of leaden bul-- let;’ So he puts a pistol to his mouth, And he fires it down his gul-- let. The coachman he not likin’ the job, Set off at full gal-lop, But Dick put a couple of balls in his nob, And perwailed on him to stop.\n\nCHORUS (sarcastically)\n\nBut Dick put a couple of balls in his nob, And perwailed on him to stop.\n\n‘I maintain that that ‘ere song’s personal to the cloth,’ said the mottled-faced gentleman, interrupting it at this point. ‘I demand the name o’ that coachman.’\n\n‘Nobody know’d,’ replied Sam. ‘He hadn’t got his card in his pocket.’\n\n‘I object to the introduction o’ politics,’ said the mottled-faced gentleman. ‘I submit that, in the present company, that ‘ere song’s political; and, wot’s much the same, that it ain’t true. I say that that coachman did not run away; but that he died game--game as pheasants; and I won’t hear nothin’ said to the contrairey.’\n\nAs the mottled-faced gentleman spoke with great energy and determination, and as the opinions of the company seemed divided on the subject, it threatened to give rise to fresh altercation, when Mr. Weller and Mr. Pell most opportunely arrived.\n\n‘All right, Sammy,’ said Mr. Weller.\n\n‘The officer will be here at four o’clock,’ said Mr. Pell. ‘I suppose you won’t run away meanwhile, eh? Ha! ha!’\n\n‘P’raps my cruel pa ‘ull relent afore then,’ replied Sam, with a broad grin.\n\n‘Not I,’ said the elder Mr. Weller.\n\n‘Do,’ said Sam.\n‘Not on no account,’ replied the inexorable creditor.\n\n‘I’ll give bills for the amount, at sixpence a month,’ said Sam.\n\n‘I won’t take ‘em,’ said Mr. Weller.\n\n‘Ha, ha, ha! very good, very good,’ said Mr. Solomon Pell, who was making out his little bill of costs; ‘a very amusing incident indeed! Benjamin, copy that.’ And Mr. Pell smiled again, as he called Mr. Weller’s attention to the amount.\n\n‘Thank you, thank you,’ said the professional gentleman, taking up another of the greasy notes as Mr. Weller took it from the pocket-book. ‘Three ten and one ten is five. Much obliged to you, Mr. Weller. Your son is a most deserving young man, very much so indeed, Sir. It’s a very pleasant trait in a young man’s character, very much so,’ added Mr. Pell, smiling smoothly round, as he buttoned up the money.\n\n‘Wot a game it is!’ said the elder Mr. Weller, with a chuckle. ‘A reg’lar prodigy son!’\n\n‘Prodigal--prodigal son, Sir,’ suggested Mr. Pell, mildly.\n\n‘Never mind, Sir,’ said Mr. Weller, with dignity. ‘I know wot’s o’clock, Sir. Wen I don’t, I’ll ask you, Sir.’\n\nBy the time the officer arrived, Sam had made himself so extremely popular, that the congregated gentlemen determined to see him to prison in a body. So off they set; the plaintiff and defendant walking arm in arm, the officer in front, and eight stout coachmen bringing up the rear. At Serjeant’s Inn Coffee-house the whole party halted to refresh, and, the legal arrangements being completed, the procession moved on again.\n\nSome little commotion was occasioned in Fleet Street, by the pleasantry of the eight gentlemen in the flank, who persevered in walking four abreast; it was also found necessary to leave the mottled-faced gentleman behind, to fight a ticket-porter, it being arranged that his friends should call for him as they came back. Nothing but these little incidents occurred on the way. When they reached the gate of the Fleet, the cavalcade, taking the time from the plaintiff, gave three tremendous cheers for the defendant, and, after having shaken hands all round, left him.\n\nSam, having been formally delivered into the warder’s custody, to the intense astonishment of Roker, and to the evident emotion of even the phlegmatic Neddy, passed at once into the prison, walked straight to his master’s room, and knocked at the door.\n\n‘Come in,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\nSam appeared, pulled off his hat, and smiled.\n\n‘Ah, Sam, my good lad!’ said Mr. Pickwick, evidently delighted to see his humble friend again; ‘I had no intention of hurting your feelings yesterday, my faithful fellow, by what I said. Put down your hat, Sam, and let me explain my meaning, a little more at length.’\n\n‘Won’t presently do, sir?’ inquired Sam.\n\n‘Certainly,’ said Mr. Pickwick; ‘but why not now?’\n\n‘I’d rayther not now, sir,’ rejoined Sam.\n\n‘Why?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘’Cause--’ said Sam, hesitating.\n\n‘Because of what?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick, alarmed at his follower’s manner. ‘Speak out, Sam.’\n\n‘’Cause,’ rejoined Sam--‘’cause I’ve got a little bisness as I want to do.’\n\n‘What business?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick, surprised at Sam’s confused manner.\n\n‘Nothin’ partickler, Sir,’ replied Sam.\n\n‘Oh, if it’s nothing particular,’ said Mr. Pickwick, with a smile, ‘you can speak with me first.’\n\n‘I think I’d better see arter it at once,’ said Sam, still hesitating.\n\nMr. Pickwick looked amazed, but said nothing.\n\n‘The fact is--’ said Sam, stopping short.\n\n‘Well!’ said Mr. Pickwick. ‘Speak out, Sam.’\n\n‘Why, the fact is,’ said Sam, with a desperate effort, ‘perhaps I’d better see arter my bed afore I do anythin’ else.’\n\n‘Your bed!’ exclaimed Mr. Pickwick, in astonishment.\n\n‘Yes, my bed, Sir,’ replied Sam, ‘I’m a prisoner. I was arrested this here wery arternoon for debt.’\n\n‘You arrested for debt!’ exclaimed Mr. Pickwick, sinking into a chair.\n\n‘Yes, for debt, Sir,’ replied Sam. ‘And the man as puts me in, ‘ull never let me out till you go yourself.’\n\n‘Bless my heart and soul!’ ejaculated Mr. Pickwick. ‘What do you mean?’\n\n‘Wot I say, Sir,’ rejoined Sam. ‘If it’s forty years to come, I shall be a prisoner, and I’m very glad on it; and if it had been Newgate, it would ha’ been just the same. Now the murder’s out, and, damme, there’s an end on it!’\n\nWith these words, which he repeated with great emphasis and violence, Sam Weller dashed his hat upon the ground, in a most unusual state of excitement; and then, folding his arms, looked firmly and fixedly in his master’s face.\n\nCHAPTER XLIV. TREATS OF DIVERS LITTLE MATTERS WHICH OCCURRED IN THE FLEET, AND OF MR. WINKLE’S MYSTERIOUS BEHAVIOUR; AND SHOWS HOW THE POOR CHANCERY PRISONER OBTAINED HIS RELEASE AT LAST\nMr. Pickwick felt a great deal too much touched by the warmth of Sam’s attachment, to be able to exhibit any manifestation of anger or displeasure at the precipitate course he had adopted, in voluntarily consigning himself to a debtor’s prison for an indefinite period. The only point on which he persevered in demanding an explanation, was, the name of Sam’s detaining creditor; but this Mr. Weller as perseveringly withheld.\n\n‘It ain’t o’ no use, sir,’ said Sam, again and again; ‘he’s a malicious, bad-disposed, vorldly-minded, spiteful, windictive creetur, with a hard heart as there ain’t no soft’nin’, as the wirtuous clergyman remarked of the old gen’l’m’n with the dropsy, ven he said, that upon the whole he thought he’d rayther leave his property to his vife than build a chapel vith it.’\n\n‘But consider, Sam,’ Mr. Pickwick remonstrated, ‘the sum is so small that it can very easily be paid; and having made up my mind that you shall stop with me, you should recollect how much more useful you would be, if you could go outside the walls.’\n\nWery much obliged to you, sir,’ replied Mr. Weller gravely; ‘but I’d rayther not.’\n\n‘Rather not do what, Sam?’\n\n‘Wy, I’d rayther not let myself down to ask a favour o’ this here unremorseful enemy.’\n\n‘But it is no favour asking him to take his money, Sam,’ reasoned Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Beg your pardon, sir,’ rejoined Sam, ‘but it ‘ud be a wery great favour to pay it, and he don’t deserve none; that’s where it is, sir.’\n\nHere Mr. Pickwick, rubbing his nose with an air of some vexation, Mr. Weller thought it prudent to change the theme of the discourse.\n\n‘I takes my determination on principle, Sir,’ remarked Sam, ‘and you takes yours on the same ground; wich puts me in mind o’ the man as killed his-self on principle, wich o’ course you’ve heerd on, Sir.’ Mr. Weller paused when he arrived at this point, and cast a comical look at his master out of the corners of his eyes.\n\n‘There is no “of course” in the case, Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick, gradually breaking into a smile, in spite of the uneasiness which Sam’s obstinacy had given him. ‘The fame of the gentleman in question, never reached my ears.’\n\n‘No, sir!’ exclaimed Mr. Weller. ‘You astonish me, Sir; he wos a clerk in a gov’ment office, sir.’\n\n‘Was he?’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Yes, he wos, Sir,’ rejoined Mr. Weller; ‘and a wery pleasant gen’l’m’n too--one o’ the precise and tidy sort, as puts their feet in little India-rubber fire-buckets wen it’s wet weather, and never has no other bosom friends but hare-skins; he saved up his money on principle, wore a clean shirt ev’ry day on principle; never spoke to none of his relations on principle, ‘fear they shou’d want to borrow money of him; and wos altogether, in fact, an uncommon agreeable character. He had his hair cut on principle vunce a fortnight, and contracted for his clothes on the economic principle--three suits a year, and send back the old uns. Being a wery reg’lar gen’l’m’n, he din’d ev’ry day at the same place, where it was one-and-nine to cut off the joint, and a wery good one-and- nine’s worth he used to cut, as the landlord often said, with the tears a-tricklin’ down his face, let alone the way he used to poke the fire in the vinter time, which wos a dead loss o’ four-pence ha’penny a day, to say nothin’ at all o’ the aggrawation o’ seein’ him do it. So uncommon grand with it too! “Post arter the next gen’l’m’n,” he sings out ev’ry day ven he comes in. “See arter the TIMES, Thomas; let me look at the MORNIN’ HERALD, when it’s out o’ hand; don’t forget to bespeak the CHRONICLE; and just bring the ‘TIZER, vill you:” and then he’d set vith his eyes fixed on the clock, and rush out, just a quarter of a minit ‘fore the time to waylay the boy as wos a-comin’ in with the evenin’ paper, which he’d read with sich intense interest and persewerance as worked the other customers up to the wery confines o’ desperation and insanity, ‘specially one i-rascible old gen’l’m’n as the vaiter wos always obliged to keep a sharp eye on, at sich times, fear he should be tempted to commit some rash act with the carving-knife. Vell, Sir, here he’d stop, occupyin’ the best place for three hours, and never takin’ nothin’ arter his dinner, but sleep, and then he’d go away to a coffee- house a few streets off, and have a small pot o’ coffee and four crumpets, arter wich he’d walk home to Kensington and go to bed. One night he wos took very ill; sends for a doctor; doctor comes in a green fly, with a kind o’ Robinson Crusoe set o’ steps, as he could let down wen he got out, and pull up arter him wen he got in, to perwent the necessity o’ the coachman’s gettin’ down, and thereby undeceivin’ the public by lettin’ ‘em see that it wos only a livery coat as he’d got on, and not the trousers to match. “Wot’s the matter?” says the doctor. “Wery ill,” says the patient. “Wot have you been a-eatin’ on?” says the doctor. “Roast weal,” says the patient. “Wot’s the last thing you dewoured?” says the doctor. “Crumpets,” says the patient. “That’s it!” says the doctor. “I’ll send you a box of pills directly, and don’t you never take no more of ‘em,” he says. “No more o’ wot?” says the patient- -“pills?” “No; crumpets,” says the doctor. “Wy?” says the patient, starting up in bed; “I’ve eat four crumpets, ev’ry night for fifteen year, on principle.” “Well, then, you’d better leave ‘em off, on principle,” says the doctor. “Crumpets is not wholesome, Sir,” says the doctor, wery fierce. “But they’re so cheap,” says the patient, comin’ down a little, “and so wery fillin’ at the price.” “They’d be dear to you, at any price; dear if you wos paid to eat ‘em,” says the doctor. “Four crumpets a night,” he says, “vill do your business in six months!” The patient looks him full in the face, and turns it over in his mind for a long time, and at last he says, “Are you sure o’ that ‘ere, Sir?” “I’ll stake my professional reputation on it,” says the doctor. “How many crumpets, at a sittin’, do you think ‘ud kill me off at once?” says the patient. “I don’t know,” says the doctor. “Do you think half-a-crown’s wurth ‘ud do it?” says the patient. “I think it might,” says the doctor. “Three shillins’ wurth ‘ud be sure to do it, I s’pose?” says the patient. “Certainly,” says the doctor. “Wery good,” says the patient; “good-night.” Next mornin’ he gets up, has a fire lit, orders in three shillins’ wurth o’ crumpets, toasts ‘em all, eats ‘em all, and blows his brains out.’\n\n‘What did he do that for?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick abruptly; for he was considerably startled by this tragical termination of the narrative.\n\n‘Wot did he do it for, Sir?’ reiterated Sam. ‘Wy, in support of his great principle that crumpets wos wholesome, and to show that he wouldn’t be put out of his way for nobody!’\n\nWith such like shiftings and changings of the discourse, did Mr. Weller meet his master’s questioning on the night of his taking up his residence in the Fleet. Finding all gentle remonstrance useless, Mr. Pickwick at length yielded a reluctant consent to his taking lodgings by the week, of a bald-headed cobbler, who rented a small slip room in one of the upper galleries. To this humble apartment Mr. Weller moved a mattress and bedding, which he hired of Mr. Roker; and, by the time he lay down upon it at night, was as much at home as if he had been bred in the prison, and his whole family had vegetated therein for three generations.\n\n‘Do you always smoke arter you goes to bed, old cock?’ inquired Mr. Weller of his landlord, when they had both retired for the night.\n\n‘Yes, I does, young bantam,’ replied the cobbler.\n\n‘Will you allow me to in-quire wy you make up your bed under that ‘ere deal table?’ said Sam.\n\n‘’Cause I was always used to a four-poster afore I came here, and I find the legs of the table answer just as well,’ replied the cobbler.\n\n‘You’re a character, sir,’ said Sam.\n\n‘I haven’t got anything of the kind belonging to me,’ rejoined the cobbler, shaking his head; ‘and if you want to meet with a good one, I’m afraid you’ll find some difficulty in suiting yourself at this register office.’\n\nThe above short dialogue took place as Mr. Weller lay extended on his mattress at one end of the room, and the cobbler on his, at the other; the apartment being illumined by the light of a rush-candle, and the cobbler’s pipe, which was glowing below the table, like a red-hot coal. The conversation, brief as it was, predisposed Mr. Weller strongly in his landlord’s favour; and, raising himself on his elbow, he took a more lengthened survey of his appearance than he had yet had either time or inclination to make.\n\nHe was a sallow man--all cobblers are; and had a strong bristly beard-- all cobblers have. His face was a queer, good-tempered, crooked-featured piece of workmanship, ornamented with a couple of eyes that must have worn a very joyous expression at one time, for they sparkled yet. The man was sixty, by years, and Heaven knows how old by imprisonment, so that his having any look approaching to mirth or contentment, was singular enough. He was a little man, and, being half doubled up as he lay in bed, looked about as long as he ought to have been without his legs. He had a great red pipe in his mouth, and was smoking, and staring at the rush-light, in a state of enviable placidity.\n\n‘Have you been here long?’ inquired Sam, breaking the silence which had lasted for some time.\n\n‘Twelve year,’ replied the cobbler, biting the end of his pipe as he spoke.\n\n‘Contempt?’ inquired Sam.\n\nThe cobbler nodded.\n\n‘Well, then,’ said Sam, with some sternness, ‘wot do you persevere in bein’ obstinit for, vastin’ your precious life away, in this here magnified pound? Wy don’t you give in, and tell the Chancellorship that you’re wery sorry for makin’ his court contemptible, and you won’t do so no more?’\n\nThe cobbler put his pipe in the corner of his mouth, while he smiled, and then brought it back to its old place again; but said nothing.\n\n‘Wy don’t you?’ said Sam, urging his question strenuously.\n\n‘Ah,’ said the cobbler, ‘you don’t quite understand these matters. What do you suppose ruined me, now?’\n\n‘Wy,’ said Sam, trimming the rush-light, ‘I s’pose the beginnin’ wos, that you got into debt, eh?’\n\n‘Never owed a farden,’ said the cobbler; ‘try again.’\n\n‘Well, perhaps,’ said Sam, ‘you bought houses, wich is delicate English for goin’ mad; or took to buildin’, wich is a medical term for bein’ incurable.’\n\nThe cobbler shook his head and said, ‘Try again.’\n\n‘You didn’t go to law, I hope?’ said Sam suspiciously.\n\n‘Never in my life,’ replied the cobbler. ‘The fact is, I was ruined by having money left me.’\n\n‘Come, come,’ said Sam, ‘that von’t do. I wish some rich enemy ‘ud try to vork my destruction in that ‘ere vay. I’d let him.’\n\n‘Oh, I dare say you don’t believe it,’ said the cobbler, quietly smoking his pipe. ‘I wouldn’t if I was you; but it’s true for all that.’\n\n‘How wos it?’ inquired Sam, half induced to believe the fact already, by the look the cobbler gave him.\n\n‘Just this,’ replied the cobbler; ‘an old gentleman that I worked for, down in the country, and a humble relation of whose I married--she’s dead, God bless her, and thank Him for it!--was seized with a fit and went off.’\n\n‘Where?’ inquired Sam, who was growing sleepy after the numerous events of the day.\n\n‘How should I know where he went?’ said the cobbler, speaking through his nose in an intense enjoyment of his pipe. ‘He went off dead.’\n\n‘Oh, that indeed,’ said Sam. ‘Well?’\n\n‘Well,’ said the cobbler, ‘he left five thousand pound behind him.’\n\n‘And wery gen-teel in him so to do,’ said Sam.\n\n‘One of which,’ continued the cobbler, ‘he left to me, ‘cause I married his relation, you see.’\n\n‘Wery good,’ murmured Sam.\n\n‘And being surrounded by a great number of nieces and nevys, as was always quarrelling and fighting among themselves for the property, he makes me his executor, and leaves the rest to me in trust, to divide it among ‘em as the will prowided.’\n\n‘Wot do you mean by leavin’ it on trust?’ inquired Sam, waking up a little. ‘If it ain’t ready-money, were’s the use on it?’\n\n‘It’s a law term, that’s all,’ said the cobbler.\n\n‘I don’t think that,’ said Sam, shaking his head. ‘There’s wery little trust at that shop. Hows’ever, go on.’\n\nWell,’ said the cobbler, ‘when I was going to take out a probate of the will, the nieces and nevys, who was desperately disappointed at not getting all the money, enters a caveat against it.’\n\nWhat’s that?’ inquired Sam.\n\n‘A legal instrument, which is as much as to say, it’s no go,’ replied the cobbler.\n\n‘I see,’ said Sam, ‘a sort of brother-in-law o’ the have-his-carcass. Well.’\n\n‘But,’ continued the cobbler, ‘finding that they couldn’t agree among themselves, and consequently couldn’t get up a case against the will, they withdrew the caveat, and I paid all the legacies. I’d hardly done it, when one nevy brings an action to set the will aside. The case comes on, some months afterwards, afore a deaf old gentleman, in a back room somewhere down by Paul’s Churchyard; and arter four counsels had taken a day a-piece to bother him regularly, he takes a week or two to consider, and read the evidence in six volumes, and then gives his judgment that how the testator was not quite right in his head, and I must pay all the money back again, and all the costs. I appealed; the case come on before three or four very sleepy gentlemen, who had heard it all before in the other court, where they’re lawyers without work; the only difference being, that, there, they’re called doctors, and in the other place delegates, if you understand that; and they very dutifully confirmed the decision of the old gentleman below. After that, we went into Chancery, where we are still, and where I shall always be. My lawyers have had all my thousand pound long ago; and what between the estate, as they call it, and the costs, I’m here for ten thousand, and shall stop here, till I die, mending shoes. Some gentlemen have talked of bringing it before Parliament, and I dare say would have done it, only they hadn’t time to come to me, and I hadn’t power to go to them, and they got tired of my long letters, and dropped the business. And this is God’s truth, without one word of suppression or exaggeration, as fifty people, both in this place and out of it, very well know.’\n\nThe cobbler paused to ascertain what effect his story had produced on Sam; but finding that he had dropped asleep, knocked the ashes out of his pipe, sighed, put it down, drew the bed-clothes over his head, and went to sleep, too.\n\nMr. Pickwick was sitting at breakfast, alone, next morning (Sam being busily engaged in the cobbler’s room, polishing his master’s shoes and brushing the black gaiters) when there came a knock at the door, which, before Mr. Pickwick could cry ‘Come in!’ was followed by the appearance of a head of hair and a cotton-velvet cap, both of which articles of dress he had no difficulty in recognising as the personal property of Mr. Smangle.\n\n‘How are you?’ said that worthy, accompanying the inquiry with a score or two of nods; ‘I say--do you expect anybody this morning? Three men-- devilish gentlemanly fellows--have been asking after you downstairs, and knocking at every door on the hall flight; for which they’ve been most infernally blown up by the collegians that had the trouble of opening ‘em.’\n\n‘Dear me! How very foolish of them,’ said Mr. Pickwick, rising. ‘Yes; I have no doubt they are some friends whom I rather expected to see, yesterday.’\n\n‘Friends of yours!’ exclaimed Smangle, seizing Mr. Pickwick by the hand. ‘Say no more. Curse me, they’re friends of mine from this minute, and friends of Mivins’s, too. Infernal pleasant, gentlemanly dog, Mivins, isn’t he?’ said Smangle, with great feeling.\n\n‘I know so little of the gentleman,’ said Mr. Pickwick, hesitating, ‘that I--’\n\n‘I know you do,’ interrupted Smangle, clasping Mr. Pickwick by the shoulder. ‘You shall know him better. You’ll be delighted with him. That man, Sir,’ said Smangle, with a solemn countenance, ‘has comic powers that would do honour to Drury Lane Theatre.’\n\n‘Has he indeed?’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Ah, by Jove he has!’ replied Smangle. ‘Hear him come the four cats in the wheel-barrow--four distinct cats, sir, I pledge you my honour. Now you know that’s infernal clever! Damme, you can’t help liking a man, when you see these traits about him. He’s only one fault--that little failing I mentioned to you, you know.’\n\nAs Mr. Smangle shook his head in a confidential and sympathising manner at this juncture, Mr. Pickwick felt that he was expected to say something, so he said, ‘Ah!’ and looked restlessly at the door.\n\n‘Ah!’ echoed Mr. Smangle, with a long-drawn sigh. ‘He’s delightful company, that man is, sir. I don’t know better company anywhere; but he has that one drawback. If the ghost of his grandfather, Sir, was to rise before him this minute, he’d ask him for the loan of his acceptance on an eightpenny stamp.’\n\nDear me!’ exclaimed Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Yes,’ added Mr. Smangle; ‘and if he’d the power of raising him again, he would, in two months and three days from this time, to renew the bill!’\n\n‘Those are very remarkable traits,’ said Mr. Pickwick; ‘but I’m afraid that while we are talking here, my friends may be in a state of great perplexity at not finding me.’\n\n‘I’ll show ‘em the way,’ said Smangle, making for the door. ‘Good-day. I won’t disturb you while they’re here, you know. By the bye--’\n\nAs Smangle pronounced the last three words, he stopped suddenly, reclosed the door which he had opened, and, walking softly back to Mr. Pickwick, stepped close up to him on tiptoe, and said, in a very soft whisper--\n\n‘You couldn’t make it convenient to lend me half-a-crown till the latter end of next week, could you?’\n\nMr. Pickwick could scarcely forbear smiling, but managing to preserve his gravity, he drew forth the coin, and placed it in Mr. Smangle’s palm; upon which, that gentleman, with many nods and winks, implying profound mystery, disappeared in quest of the three strangers, with whom he presently returned; and having coughed thrice, and nodded as many times, as an assurance to Mr. Pickwick that he would not forget to pay, he shook hands all round, in an engaging manner, and at length took himself off.\n\n‘My dear friends,’ said Mr. Pickwick, shaking hands alternately with Mr. Tupman, Mr. Winkle, and Mr. Snodgrass, who were the three visitors in question, ‘I am delighted to see you.’\n\nThe triumvirate were much affected. Mr. Tupman shook his head deploringly, Mr. Snodgrass drew forth his handkerchief, with undisguised emotion; and Mr. Winkle retired to the window, and sniffed aloud.\n\n‘Mornin’, gen’l’m’n,’ said Sam, entering at the moment with the shoes and gaiters. ‘Avay vith melincholly, as the little boy said ven his schoolmissus died. Velcome to the college, gen’l’m’n.’\n\n‘This foolish fellow,’ said Mr. Pickwick, tapping Sam on the head as he knelt down to button up his master’s gaiters--‘this foolish fellow has got himself arrested, in order to be near me.’\n\n‘What!’ exclaimed the three friends.\n\n‘Yes, gen’l’m’n,’ said Sam, ‘I’m a--stand steady, sir, if you please-- I’m a prisoner, gen’l’m’n. Con-fined, as the lady said.’\n\n‘A prisoner!’ exclaimed Mr. Winkle, with unaccountable vehemence.\n\n‘Hollo, sir!’ responded Sam, looking up. ‘Wot’s the matter, Sir?’\n\n‘I had hoped, Sam, that--Nothing, nothing,’ said Mr. Winkle precipitately.\n\nThere was something so very abrupt and unsettled in Mr. Winkle’s manner, that Mr. Pickwick involuntarily looked at his two friends for an explanation.\n\n‘We don’t know,’ said Mr. Tupman, answering this mute appeal aloud. ‘He has been much excited for two days past, and his whole demeanour very unlike what it usually is. We feared there must be something the matter, but he resolutely denies it.’\n\n‘No, no,’ said Mr. Winkle, colouring beneath Mr. Pickwick’s gaze; ‘there is really nothing. I assure you there is nothing, my dear sir. It will be necessary for me to leave town, for a short time, on private business, and I had hoped to have prevailed upon you to allow Sam to accompany me.’\n\nMr. Pickwick looked more astonished than before.\n\n‘I think,’ faltered Mr. Winkle, ‘that Sam would have had no objection to do so; but, of course, his being a prisoner here, renders it impossible. So I must go alone.’\n\nAs Mr. Winkle said these words, Mr. Pickwick felt, with some astonishment, that Sam’s fingers were trembling at the gaiters, as if he were rather surprised or startled. Sam looked up at Mr. Winkle, too, when he had finished speaking; and though the glance they exchanged was instantaneous, they seemed to understand each other.\n\n‘Do you know anything of this, Sam?’ said Mr. Pickwick sharply.\n\n‘No, I don’t, sir,’ replied Mr. Weller, beginning to button with extraordinary assiduity.\n\n‘Are you sure, Sam?’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Wy, sir,’ responded Mr. Weller; ‘I’m sure so far, that I’ve never heerd anythin’ on the subject afore this moment. If I makes any guess about it,’ added Sam, looking at Mr. Winkle, ‘I haven’t got any right to say what it is, ‘fear it should be a wrong ‘un.’\n\n‘I have no right to make any further inquiry into the private affairs of a friend, however intimate a friend,’ said Mr. Pickwick, after a short silence; ‘at present let me merely say, that I do not understand this at all. There. We have had quite enough of the subject.’\n\nThus expressing himself, Mr. Pickwick led the conversation to different topics, and Mr. Winkle gradually appeared more at ease, though still very far from being completely so. They had all so much to converse about, that the morning very quickly passed away; and when, at three o’clock, Mr. Weller produced upon the little dining-table, a roast leg of mutton and an enormous meat-pie, with sundry dishes of vegetables, and pots of porter, which stood upon the chairs or the sofa bedstead, or where they could, everybody felt disposed to do justice to the meal, notwithstanding that the meat had been purchased, and dressed, and the pie made, and baked, at the prison cookery hard by.\n\nTo these succeeded a bottle or two of very good wine, for which a messenger was despatched by Mr. Pickwick to the Horn Coffee-house, in Doctors’ Commons. The bottle or two, indeed, might be more properly described as a bottle or six, for by the time it was drunk, and tea over, the bell began to ring for strangers to withdraw.\n\nBut, if Mr. Winkle’s behaviour had been unaccountable in the morning, it became perfectly unearthly and solemn when, under the influence of his feelings, and his share of the bottle or six, he prepared to take leave of his friend. He lingered behind, until Mr. Tupman and Mr. Snodgrass had disappeared, and then fervently clenched Mr. Pickwick’s hand, with an expression of face in which deep and mighty resolve was fearfully blended with the very concentrated essence of gloom.\n\n‘Good-night, my dear Sir!’ said Mr. Winkle between his set teeth.\n\n‘Bless you, my dear fellow!’ replied the warm-hearted Mr. Pickwick, as he returned the pressure of his young friend’s hand.\n\n‘Now then!’ cried Mr. Tupman from the gallery.\n\n‘Yes, yes, directly,’ replied Mr. Winkle. ‘Good-night!’\n\n‘Good-night,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\nThere was another good-night, and another, and half a dozen more after that, and still Mr. Winkle had fast hold of his friend’s hand, and was looking into his face with the same strange expression.\n\n‘Is anything the matter?’ said Mr. Pickwick at last, when his arm was quite sore with shaking.\n\n‘Nothing,’ said Mr. Winkle.\n\n‘Well then, good-night,’ said Mr. Pickwick, attempting to disengage his hand.\n\n‘My friend, my benefactor, my honoured companion,’ murmured Mr. Winkle, catching at his wrist. ‘Do not judge me harshly; do not, when you hear that, driven to extremity by hopeless obstacles, I--’\n\n‘Now then,’ said Mr. Tupman, reappearing at the door. ‘Are you coming, or are we to be locked in?’\n\n‘Yes, yes, I am ready,’ replied Mr. Winkle. And with a violent effort he tore himself away.\n\nAs Mr. Pickwick was gazing down the passage after them in silent astonishment, Sam Weller appeared at the stair-head, and whispered for one moment in Mr. Winkle’s ear.\n\n‘Oh, certainly, depend upon me,’ said that gentleman aloud.\n\n‘Thank’ee, sir. You won’t forget, sir?’ said Sam.\n\n‘Of course not,’ replied Mr. Winkle.\n\n‘Wish you luck, Sir,’ said Sam, touching his hat. ‘I should very much liked to ha’ joined you, Sir; but the gov’nor, o’ course, is paramount.’\n\n‘It is very much to your credit that you remain here,’ said Mr. Winkle. With these words they disappeared down the stairs.\n\n‘Very extraordinary,’ said Mr. Pickwick, going back into his room, and seating himself at the table in a musing attitude. ‘What can that young man be going to do?’\n\nHe had sat ruminating about the matter for some time, when the voice of Roker, the turnkey, demanded whether he might come in.\n\n‘By all means,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘I’ve brought you a softer pillow, Sir,’ said Mr. Roker, ‘instead of the temporary one you had last night.’\n\n‘Thank you,’ said Mr. Pickwick. ‘Will you take a glass of wine?’\n\n‘You’re wery good, Sir,’ replied Mr. Roker, accepting the proffered glass. ‘Yours, sir.’\n\n‘Thank you,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘I’m sorry to say that your landlord’s wery bad to-night, Sir,’ said Roker, setting down the glass, and inspecting the lining of his hat preparatory to putting it on again.\n\n‘What! The Chancery prisoner!’ exclaimed Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘He won’t be a Chancery prisoner wery long, Sir,’ replied Roker, turning his hat round, so as to get the maker’s name right side upwards, as he looked into it.\n\n‘You make my blood run cold,’ said Mr. Pickwick. ‘What do you mean?’\n\n‘He’s been consumptive for a long time past,’ said Mr. Roker, ‘and he’s taken wery bad in the breath to-night. The doctor said, six months ago, that nothing but change of air could save him.’\n\n‘Great Heaven!’ exclaimed Mr. Pickwick; ‘has this man been slowly murdered by the law for six months?’\n\n‘I don’t know about that,’ replied Roker, weighing the hat by the brim in both hands. ‘I suppose he’d have been took the same, wherever he was. He went into the infirmary, this morning; the doctor says his strength is to be kept up as much as possible; and the warden’s sent him wine and broth and that, from his own house. It’s not the warden’s fault, you know, sir.’\n\n‘Of course not,’ replied Mr. Pickwick hastily.\n\n‘I’m afraid, however,’ said Roker, shaking his head, ‘that it’s all up with him. I offered Neddy two six-penn’orths to one upon it just now, but he wouldn’t take it, and quite right. Thank’ee, Sir. Good-night, sir.’\n\n‘Stay,’ said Mr. Pickwick earnestly. ‘Where is this infirmary?’\n\n‘Just over where you slept, sir,’ replied Roker. ‘I’ll show you, if you like to come.’ Mr. Pickwick snatched up his hat without speaking, and followed at once.\n\nThe turnkey led the way in silence; and gently raising the latch of the room door, motioned Mr. Pickwick to enter. It was a large, bare, desolate room, with a number of stump bedsteads made of iron, on one of which lay stretched the shadow of a man--wan, pale, and ghastly. His breathing was hard and thick, and he moaned painfully as it came and went. At the bedside sat a short old man in a cobbler’s apron, who, by the aid of a pair of horn spectacles, was reading from the Bible aloud. It was the fortunate legatee.\n\nThe sick man laid his hand upon his attendant’s arm, and motioned him to stop. He closed the book, and laid it on the bed.\n\n‘Open the window,’ said the sick man.\n\nHe did so. The noise of carriages and carts, the rattle of wheels, the cries of men and boys, all the busy sounds of a mighty multitude instinct with life and occupation, blended into one deep murmur, floated into the room. Above the hoarse loud hum, arose, from time to time, a boisterous laugh; or a scrap of some jingling song, shouted forth, by one of the giddy crowd, would strike upon the ear, for an instant, and then be lost amidst the roar of voices and the tramp of footsteps; the breaking of the billows of the restless sea of life, that rolled heavily on, without. These are melancholy sounds to a quiet listener at any time; but how melancholy to the watcher by the bed of death!\n\n‘There is no air here,’ said the man faintly. ‘The place pollutes it. It was fresh round about, when I walked there, years ago; but it grows hot and heavy in passing these walls. I cannot breathe it.’\n\n‘We have breathed it together, for a long time,’ said the old man. ‘Come, come.’\n\nThere was a short silence, during which the two spectators approached the bed. The sick man drew a hand of his old fellow-prisoner towards him, and pressing it affectionately between both his own, retained it in his grasp.\n\n‘I hope,’ he gasped after a while, so faintly that they bent their ears close over the bed to catch the half-formed sounds his pale lips gave vent to--‘I hope my merciful Judge will bear in mind my heavy punishment on earth. Twenty years, my friend, twenty years in this hideous grave! My heart broke when my child died, and I could not even kiss him in his little coffin. My loneliness since then, in all this noise and riot, has been very dreadful. May God forgive me! He has seen my solitary, lingering death.’\n\nHe folded his hands, and murmuring something more they could not hear, fell into a sleep--only a sleep at first, for they saw him smile.\n\nThey whispered together for a little time, and the turnkey, stooping over the pillow, drew hastily back. ‘He has got his discharge, by G--!’ said the man.\n\nHe had. But he had grown so like death in life, that they knew not when he died.\n\nCHAPTER XLV. DESCRIPTIVE OF AN AFFECTING INTERVIEW BETWEEN MR. SAMUEL WELLER AND A FAMILY PARTY. MR. PICKWICK MAKES A TOUR OF THE DIMINUTIVE WORLD HE INHABITS, AND RESOLVES TO MIX WITH IT, IN FUTURE, AS LITTLE AS POSSIBLE\nA few mornings after his incarceration, Mr. Samuel Weller, having arranged his master’s room with all possible care, and seen him comfortably seated over his books and papers, withdrew to employ himself for an hour or two to come, as he best could. It was a fine morning, and it occurred to Sam that a pint of porter in the open air would lighten his next quarter of an hour or so, as well as any little amusement in which he could indulge.\n\nHaving arrived at this conclusion, he betook himself to the tap. Having purchased the beer, and obtained, moreover, the day-but-one-before- yesterday’s paper, he repaired to the skittle-ground, and seating himself on a bench, proceeded to enjoy himself in a very sedate and methodical manner.\n\nFirst of all, he took a refreshing draught of the beer, and then he looked up at a window, and bestowed a platonic wink on a young lady who was peeling potatoes thereat. Then he opened the paper, and folded it so as to get the police reports outwards; and this being a vexatious and difficult thing to do, when there is any wind stirring, he took another draught of the beer when he had accomplished it. Then, he read two lines of the paper, and stopped short to look at a couple of men who were finishing a game at rackets, which, being concluded, he cried out ‘wery good,’ in an approving manner, and looked round upon the spectators, to ascertain whether their sentiments coincided with his own. This involved the necessity of looking up at the windows also; and as the young lady was still there, it was an act of common politeness to wink again, and to drink to her good health in dumb show, in another draught of the beer, which Sam did; and having frowned hideously upon a small boy who had noted this latter proceeding with open eyes, he threw one leg over the other, and, holding the newspaper in both hands, began to read in real earnest.\n\nHe had hardly composed himself into the needful state of abstraction, when he thought he heard his own name proclaimed in some distant passage. Nor was he mistaken, for it quickly passed from mouth to mouth, and in a few seconds the air teemed with shouts of ‘Weller!’\n\nHere!’ roared Sam, in a stentorian voice. ‘Wot’s the matter? Who wants him? Has an express come to say that his country house is afire?’\n\n‘Somebody wants you in the hall,’ said a man who was standing by.\n\n‘Just mind that ‘ere paper and the pot, old feller, will you?’ said Sam. ‘I’m a-comin’. Blessed, if they was a-callin’ me to the bar, they couldn’t make more noise about it!’\n\nAccompanying these words with a gentle rap on the head of the young gentleman before noticed, who, unconscious of his close vicinity to the person in request, was screaming ‘Weller!’ with all his might, Sam hastened across the ground, and ran up the steps into the hall. Here, the first object that met his eyes was his beloved father sitting on a bottom stair, with his hat in his hand, shouting out ‘Weller!’ in his very loudest tone, at half-minute intervals.\n\n‘Wot are you a-roarin’ at?’ said Sam impetuously, when the old gentleman had discharged himself of another shout; ‘making yourself so precious hot that you looks like a aggrawated glass-blower. Wot’s the matter?’\n\n‘Aha!’ replied the old gentleman, ‘I began to be afeerd that you’d gone for a walk round the Regency Park, Sammy.’\n\n‘Come,’ said Sam, ‘none o’ them taunts agin the wictim o’ avarice, and come off that ‘ere step. Wot are you a-settin’ down there for? I don’t live there.’\n\n‘I’ve got such a game for you, Sammy,’ said the elder Mr. Weller, rising.\n\n‘Stop a minit,’ said Sam, ‘you’re all vite behind.’\n\n‘That’s right, Sammy, rub it off,’ said Mr. Weller, as his son dusted him. ‘It might look personal here, if a man walked about with vitevash on his clothes, eh, Sammy?’\n\nAs Mr. Weller exhibited in this place unequivocal symptoms of an approaching fit of chuckling, Sam interposed to stop it.\n\n‘Keep quiet, do,’ said Sam, ‘there never vos such a old picter-card born. Wot are you bustin’ vith, now?’\n\n‘Sammy,’ said Mr. Weller, wiping his forehead, ‘I’m afeerd that vun o’ these days I shall laugh myself into a appleplexy, my boy.’\n\n‘Vell, then, wot do you do it for?’ said Sam. ‘Now, then, wot have you got to say?’\n\n‘Who do you think’s come here with me, Samivel?’ said Mr. Weller, drawing back a pace or two, pursing up his mouth, and extending his eyebrows.\n\n‘Pell?’ said Sam.\n\nMr. Weller shook his head, and his red cheeks expanded with the laughter that was endeavouring to find a vent.\n\n‘Mottled-faced man, p’raps?’ asked Sam.\n\nAgain Mr. Weller shook his head.\n\n‘Who then?’asked Sam.\n\n‘Your mother-in-law,’ said Mr. Weller; and it was lucky he did say it, or his cheeks must inevitably have cracked, from their most unnatural distension.\n\n‘Your mother-in-law, Sammy,’ said Mr. Weller, ‘and the red-nosed man, my boy; and the red-nosed man. Ho! ho! ho!’\n\nWith this, Mr. Weller launched into convulsions of laughter, while Sam regarded him with a broad grin gradually over-spreading his whole countenance.\n\n‘They’ve come to have a little serious talk with you, Samivel,’ said Mr. Weller, wiping his eyes. ‘Don’t let out nothin’ about the unnat’ral creditor, Sammy.’\n\n‘Wot, don’t they know who it is?’ inquired Sam.\n\n‘Not a bit on it,’ replied his father.\n\n‘Vere are they?’ said Sam, reciprocating all the old gentleman’s grins.\n\n‘In the snuggery,’ rejoined Mr. Weller. ‘Catch the red-nosed man a-goin’ anyvere but vere the liquors is; not he, Samivel, not he. Ve’d a wery pleasant ride along the road from the Markis this mornin’, Sammy,’ said Mr. Weller, when he felt himself equal to the task of speaking in an articulate manner. ‘I drove the old piebald in that ‘ere little chay- cart as belonged to your mother-in-law’s first wenter, into vich a harm- cheer wos lifted for the shepherd; and I’m blessed,’ said Mr. Weller, with a look of deep scorn--‘I’m blessed if they didn’t bring a portable flight o’ steps out into the road a-front o’ our door for him, to get up by.’\n\n‘You don’t mean that?’ said Sam.\n\n‘I do mean that, Sammy,’ replied his father, ‘and I vish you could ha’ seen how tight he held on by the sides wen he did get up, as if he wos afeerd o’ being precipitayted down full six foot, and dashed into a million hatoms. He tumbled in at last, however, and avay ve vent; and I rayther think--I say I rayther think, Samivel--that he found his-self a little jolted ven ve turned the corners.’\n\n‘Wot, I s’pose you happened to drive up agin a post or two?’ said Sam.\n\n‘I’m afeerd,’ replied Mr. Weller, in a rapture of winks--‘I’m afeerd I took vun or two on ‘em, Sammy; he wos a-flyin’ out o’ the arm-cheer all the way.’\n\nHere the old gentleman shook his head from side to side, and was seized with a hoarse internal rumbling, accompanied with a violent swelling of the countenance, and a sudden increase in the breadth of all his features; symptoms which alarmed his son not a little.\n\n‘Don’t be frightened, Sammy, don’t be frightened,’ said the old gentleman, when by dint of much struggling, and various convulsive stamps upon the ground, he had recovered his voice. ‘It’s only a kind o’ quiet laugh as I’m a-tryin’ to come, Sammy.’\n\n‘Well, if that’s wot it is,’ said Sam, ‘you’d better not try to come it agin. You’ll find it rayther a dangerous inwention.’\n\n‘Don’t you like it, Sammy?’ inquired the old gentleman.\n\n‘Not at all,’ replied Sam.\n\n‘Well,’ said Mr. Weller, with the tears still running down his cheeks, ‘it ‘ud ha’ been a wery great accommodation to me if I could ha’ done it, and ‘ud ha’ saved a good many vords atween your mother-in-law and me, sometimes; but I’m afeerd you’re right, Sammy, it’s too much in the appleplexy line--a deal too much, Samivel.’\n\nThis conversation brought them to the door of the snuggery, into which Sam--pausing for an instant to look over his shoulder, and cast a sly leer at his respected progenitor, who was still giggling behind--at once led the way.\n\n‘Mother-in-law,’ said Sam, politely saluting the lady, ‘wery much obliged to you for this here wisit.--Shepherd, how air you?’\n\n‘Oh, Samuel!’ said Mrs. Weller. ‘This is dreadful.’\n\n‘Not a bit on it, mum,’ replied Sam.--‘Is it, shepherd?’\n\nMr. Stiggins raised his hands, and turned up his eyes, until the whites- -or rather the yellows--were alone visible; but made no reply in words.\n\n‘Is this here gen’l’m’n troubled with any painful complaint?’ said Sam, looking to his mother-in-law for explanation.\n\n‘The good man is grieved to see you here, Samuel,’ replied Mrs. Weller.\n\n‘Oh, that’s it, is it?’ said Sam. ‘I was afeerd, from his manner, that he might ha’ forgotten to take pepper vith that ‘ere last cowcumber he eat. Set down, Sir, ve make no extra charge for settin’ down, as the king remarked wen he blowed up his ministers.’\n\n‘Young man,’ said Mr. Stiggins ostentatiously, ‘I fear you are not softened by imprisonment.’\n\n‘Beg your pardon, Sir,’ replied Sam; ‘wot wos you graciously pleased to hobserve?’\n\n‘I apprehend, young man, that your nature is no softer for this chastening,’ said Mr. Stiggins, in a loud voice.\n\n‘Sir,’ replied Sam, ‘you’re wery kind to say so. I hope my natur is NOT a soft vun, Sir. Wery much obliged to you for your good opinion, Sir.’\n\nAt this point of the conversation, a sound, indecorously approaching to a laugh, was heard to proceed from the chair in which the elder Mr. Weller was seated; upon which Mrs. Weller, on a hasty consideration of all the circumstances of the case, considered it her bounden duty to become gradually hysterical.\n\n‘Weller,’ said Mrs. W. (the old gentleman was seated in a corner); ‘Weller! Come forth.’\n\n‘Wery much obleeged to you, my dear,’ replied Mr. Weller; ‘but I’m quite comfortable vere I am.’\n\nUpon this, Mrs. Weller burst into tears.\n\n‘Wot’s gone wrong, mum?’ said Sam.\n\n‘Oh, Samuel!’ replied Mrs. Weller, ‘your father makes me wretched. Will nothing do him good?’\n\n‘Do you hear this here?’ said Sam. ‘Lady vants to know vether nothin’ ‘ull do you good.’\n\n‘Wery much indebted to Mrs. Weller for her po-lite inquiries, Sammy,’ replied the old gentleman. ‘I think a pipe vould benefit me a good deal. Could I be accommodated, Sammy?’\n\nHere Mrs. Weller let fall some more tears, and Mr. Stiggins groaned.\n\n‘Hollo! Here’s this unfortunate gen’l’m’n took ill agin,’ said Sam, looking round. ‘Vere do you feel it now, sir?’\n\n‘In the same place, young man,’ rejoined Mr. Stiggins, ‘in the same place.’\n\n‘Vere may that be, Sir?’ inquired Sam, with great outward simplicity.\n\n‘In the buzzim, young man,’ replied Mr. Stiggins, placing his umbrella on his waistcoat.\n\nAt this affecting reply, Mrs. Weller, being wholly unable to suppress her feelings, sobbed aloud, and stated her conviction that the red-nosed man was a saint; whereupon Mr. Weller, senior, ventured to suggest, in an undertone, that he must be the representative of the united parishes of St. Simon Without and St. Walker Within.\n\n‘I’m afeered, mum,’ said Sam, ‘that this here gen’l’m’n, with the twist in his countenance, feels rather thirsty, with the melancholy spectacle afore him. Is it the case, mum?’\n\nThe worthy lady looked at Mr. Stiggins for a reply; that gentleman, with many rollings of the eye, clenched his throat with his right hand, and mimicked the act of swallowing, to intimate that he was athirst.\n\n‘I am afraid, Samuel, that his feelings have made him so indeed,’ said Mrs. Weller mournfully.\n\n‘Wot’s your usual tap, sir?’ replied Sam.\n\n‘Oh, my dear young friend,’ replied Mr. Stiggins, ‘all taps is vanities!’\n\n‘Too true, too true, indeed,’ said Mrs. Weller, murmuring a groan, and shaking her head assentingly.\n\n‘Well,’ said Sam, ‘I des-say they may be, sir; but wich is your partickler wanity? Wich wanity do you like the flavour on best, sir?’\n\n‘Oh, my dear young friend,’ replied Mr. Stiggins, ‘I despise them all. If,’ said Mr. Stiggins--‘if there is any one of them less odious than another, it is the liquor called rum. Warm, my dear young friend, with three lumps of sugar to the tumbler.’\n\n‘Wery sorry to say, sir,’ said Sam, ‘that they don’t allow that particular wanity to be sold in this here establishment.’\n\n‘Oh, the hardness of heart of these inveterate men!’ ejaculated Mr. Stiggins. ‘Oh, the accursed cruelty of these inhuman persecutors!’\n\nWith these words, Mr. Stiggins again cast up his eyes, and rapped his breast with his umbrella; and it is but justice to the reverend gentleman to say, that his indignation appeared very real and unfeigned indeed.\n\nAfter Mrs. Weller and the red-nosed gentleman had commented on this inhuman usage in a very forcible manner, and had vented a variety of pious and holy execrations against its authors, the latter recommended a bottle of port wine, warmed with a little water, spice, and sugar, as being grateful to the stomach, and savouring less of vanity than many other compounds. It was accordingly ordered to be prepared, and pending its preparation the red-nosed man and Mrs. Weller looked at the elder W. and groaned.\n\n‘Well, Sammy,’ said the gentleman, ‘I hope you’ll find your spirits rose by this here lively wisit. Wery cheerful and improvin’ conwersation, ain’t it, Sammy?’\n\n‘You’re a reprobate,’ replied Sam; ‘and I desire you won’t address no more o’ them ungraceful remarks to me.’\n\nSo far from being edified by this very proper reply, the elder Mr. Weller at once relapsed into a broad grin; and this inexorable conduct causing the lady and Mr. Stiggins to close their eyes, and rock themselves to and fro on their chairs, in a troubled manner, he furthermore indulged in several acts of pantomime, indicative of a desire to pummel and wring the nose of the aforesaid Stiggins, the performance of which, appeared to afford him great mental relief. The old gentleman very narrowly escaped detection in one instance; for Mr. Stiggins happening to give a start on the arrival of the negus, brought his head in smart contact with the clenched fist with which Mr. Weller had been describing imaginary fireworks in the air, within two inches of his ear, for some minutes.\n\n‘Wot are you a-reachin’ out, your hand for the tumbler in that ‘ere sawage way for?’ said Sam, with great promptitude. ‘Don’t you see you’ve hit the gen’l’m’n?’\n\n‘I didn’t go to do it, Sammy,’ said Mr. Weller, in some degree abashed by the very unexpected occurrence of the incident.\n\n‘Try an in’ard application, sir,’ said Sam, as the red-nosed gentleman rubbed his head with a rueful visage. ‘Wot do you think o’ that, for a go o’ wanity, warm, Sir?’\n\nMr. Stiggins made no verbal answer, but his manner was expressive. He tasted the contents of the glass which Sam had placed in his hand, put his umbrella on the floor, and tasted it again, passing his hand placidly across his stomach twice or thrice; he then drank the whole at a breath, and smacking his lips, held out the tumbler for more.\n\nNor was Mrs. Weller behind-hand in doing justice to the composition. The good lady began by protesting that she couldn’t touch a drop--then took a small drop--then a large drop--then a great many drops; and her feelings being of the nature of those substances which are powerfully affected by the application of strong waters, she dropped a tear with every drop of negus, and so got on, melting the feelings down, until at length she had arrived at a very pathetic and decent pitch of misery.\n\nThe elder Mr. Weller observed these signs and tokens with many manifestations of disgust, and when, after a second jug of the same, Mr. Stiggins began to sigh in a dismal manner, he plainly evinced his disapprobation of the whole proceedings, by sundry incoherent ramblings of speech, among which frequent angry repetitions of the word ‘gammon’ were alone distinguishable to the ear.\n\n‘I’ll tell you wot it is, Samivel, my boy,’ whispered the old gentleman into his son’s ear, after a long and steadfast contemplation of his lady and Mr. Stiggins; ‘I think there must be somethin’ wrong in your mother- in-law’s inside, as vell as in that o’ the red-nosed man.’\n\n‘Wot do you mean?’ said Sam.\n\n‘I mean this here, Sammy,’ replied the old gentleman, ‘that wot they drink, don’t seem no nourishment to ‘em; it all turns to warm water, and comes a-pourin’ out o’ their eyes. ‘Pend upon it, Sammy, it’s a constitootional infirmity.’\n\nMr. Weller delivered this scientific opinion with many confirmatory frowns and nods; which, Mrs. Weller remarking, and concluding that they bore some disparaging reference either to herself or to Mr. Stiggins, or to both, was on the point of becoming infinitely worse, when Mr. Stiggins, getting on his legs as well as he could, proceeded to deliver an edifying discourse for the benefit of the company, but more especially of Mr. Samuel, whom he adjured in moving terms to be upon his guard in that sink of iniquity into which he was cast; to abstain from all hypocrisy and pride of heart; and to take in all things exact pattern and copy by him (Stiggins), in which case he might calculate on arriving, sooner or later at the comfortable conclusion, that, like him, he was a most estimable and blameless character, and that all his acquaintances and friends were hopelessly abandoned and profligate wretches. Which consideration, he said, could not but afford him the liveliest satisfaction.\n\nHe furthermore conjured him to avoid, above all things, the vice of intoxication, which he likened unto the filthy habits of swine, and to those poisonous and baleful drugs which being chewed in the mouth, are said to filch away the memory. At this point of his discourse, the reverend and red-nosed gentleman became singularly incoherent, and staggering to and fro in the excitement of his eloquence, was fain to catch at the back of a chair to preserve his perpendicular.\n\nMr. Stiggins did not desire his hearers to be upon their guard against those false prophets and wretched mockers of religion, who, without sense to expound its first doctrines, or hearts to feel its first principles, are more dangerous members of society than the common criminal; imposing, as they necessarily do, upon the weakest and worst informed, casting scorn and contempt on what should be held most sacred, and bringing into partial disrepute large bodies of virtuous and well- conducted persons of many excellent sects and persuasions. But as he leaned over the back of the chair for a considerable time, and closing one eye, winked a good deal with the other, it is presumed that he thought all this, but kept it to himself.\n\nDuring the delivery of the oration, Mrs. Weller sobbed and wept at the end of the paragraphs; while Sam, sitting cross-legged on a chair and resting his arms on the top rail, regarded the speaker with great suavity and blandness of demeanour; occasionally bestowing a look of recognition on the old gentleman, who was delighted at the beginning, and went to sleep about half-way.\n\n‘Brayvo; wery pretty!’ said Sam, when the red-nosed man having finished, pulled his worn gloves on, thereby thrusting his fingers through the broken tops till the knuckles were disclosed to view. ‘Wery pretty.’\n\n‘I hope it may do you good, Samuel,’ said Mrs. Weller solemnly.\n\n‘I think it vill, mum,’ replied Sam.\n\n‘I wish I could hope that it would do your father good,’ said Mrs. Weller.\n\n‘Thank’ee, my dear,’ said Mr. Weller, senior. ‘How do you find yourself arter it, my love?’\n\n‘Scoffer!’ exclaimed Mrs. Weller.\n\n‘Benighted man!’ said the Reverend Mr. Stiggins.\n\n‘If I don’t get no better light than that ‘ere moonshine o’ yourn, my worthy creetur,’ said the elder Mr. Weller, ‘it’s wery likely as I shall continey to be a night coach till I’m took off the road altogether. Now, Mrs. We, if the piebald stands at livery much longer, he’ll stand at nothin’ as we go back, and p’raps that ‘ere harm-cheer ‘ull be tipped over into some hedge or another, with the shepherd in it.’\n\nAt this supposition, the Reverend Mr. Stiggins, in evident consternation, gathered up his hat and umbrella, and proposed an immediate departure, to which Mrs. Weller assented. Sam walked with them to the lodge gate, and took a dutiful leave.\n\n‘A-do, Samivel,’ said the old gentleman.\n\n‘Wot’s a-do?’ inquired Sammy.\n\n‘Well, good-bye, then,’ said the old gentleman.\n\n‘Oh, that’s wot you’re aimin’ at, is it?’ said Sam. ‘Good-bye!’\n\n‘Sammy,’ whispered Mr. Weller, looking cautiously round; ‘my duty to your gov’nor, and tell him if he thinks better o’ this here bis’ness, to com-moonicate vith me. Me and a cab’net-maker has dewised a plan for gettin’ him out. A pianner, Samivel--a pianner!’ said Mr. Weller, striking his son on the chest with the back of his hand, and falling back a step or two.\n\n‘Wot do you mean?’ said Sam.\n\n‘A pianner-forty, Samivel,’ rejoined Mr. Weller, in a still more mysterious manner, ‘as he can have on hire; vun as von’t play, Sammy.’\n\n‘And wot ‘ud be the good o’ that?’ said Sam.\n\n‘Let him send to my friend, the cabinet-maker, to fetch it back, Sammy,’ replied Mr. Weller. ‘Are you avake, now?’\n\n‘No,’ rejoined Sam.\n\n‘There ain’t no vurks in it,’ whispered his father. ‘It ‘ull hold him easy, vith his hat and shoes on, and breathe through the legs, vich his holler. Have a passage ready taken for ‘Merriker. The ‘Merrikin gov’ment will never give him up, ven vunce they find as he’s got money to spend, Sammy. Let the gov’nor stop there, till Mrs. Bardell’s dead, or Mr. Dodson and Fogg’s hung (wich last ewent I think is the most likely to happen first, Sammy), and then let him come back and write a book about the ‘Merrikins as’ll pay all his expenses and more, if he blows ‘em up enough.’\n\nMr. Weller delivered this hurried abstract of his plot with great vehemence of whisper; and then, as if fearful of weakening the effect of the tremendous communication by any further dialogue, he gave the coachman’s salute, and vanished.\n\nSam had scarcely recovered his usual composure of countenance, which had been greatly disturbed by the secret communication of his respected relative, when Mr. Pickwick accosted him.\n\n‘Sam,’ said that gentleman.\n\n‘Sir,’ replied Mr. Weller.\n\n‘I am going for a walk round the prison, and I wish you to attend me. I see a prisoner we know coming this way, Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick, smiling.\n\n‘Wich, Sir?’ inquired Mr. Weller; ‘the gen’l’m’n vith the head o’ hair, or the interestin’ captive in the stockin’s?’\n\n‘Neither,’ rejoined Mr. Pickwick. ‘He is an older friend of yours, Sam.’\n\n‘O’ mine, Sir?’ exclaimed Mr. Weller.\n\n‘You recollect the gentleman very well, I dare say, Sam,’ replied Mr. Pickwick, ‘or else you are more unmindful of your old acquaintances than I think you are. Hush! not a word, Sam; not a syllable. Here he is.’\n\nAs Mr. Pickwick spoke, Jingle walked up. He looked less miserable than before, being clad in a half-worn suit of clothes, which, with Mr. Pickwick’s assistance, had been released from the pawnbroker’s. He wore clean linen too, and had had his hair cut. He was very pale and thin, however; and as he crept slowly up, leaning on a stick, it was easy to see that he had suffered severely from illness and want, and was still very weak. He took off his hat as Mr. Pickwick saluted him, and seemed much humbled and abashed at the sight of Sam Weller.\n\nFollowing close at his heels, came Mr. Job Trotter, in the catalogue of whose vices, want of faith and attachment to his companion could at all events find no place. He was still ragged and squalid, but his face was not quite so hollow as on his first meeting with Mr. Pickwick, a few days before. As he took off his hat to our benevolent old friend, he murmured some broken expressions of gratitude, and muttered something about having been saved from starving.\n\n‘Well, well,’ said Mr. Pickwick, impatiently interrupting him, ‘you can follow with Sam. I want to speak to you, Mr. Jingle. Can you walk without his arm?’\n\n‘Certainly, sir--all ready--not too fast--legs shaky--head queer--round and round--earthquaky sort of feeling--very.’\n\n‘Here, give me your arm,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘No, no,’ replied Jingle; ‘won’t indeed--rather not.’\n\n‘Nonsense,’ said Mr. Pickwick; ‘lean upon me, I desire, Sir.’\n\nSeeing that he was confused and agitated, and uncertain what to do, Mr. Pickwick cut the matter short by drawing the invalided stroller’s arm through his, and leading him away, without saying another word about it.\n\nDuring the whole of this time the countenance of Mr. Samuel Weller had exhibited an expression of the most overwhelming and absorbing astonishment that the imagination can portray. After looking from Job to Jingle, and from Jingle to Job in profound silence, he softly ejaculated the words, ‘Well, I am damn’d!’ which he repeated at least a score of times; after which exertion, he appeared wholly bereft of speech, and again cast his eyes, first upon the one and then upon the other, in mute perplexity and bewilderment.\n\n‘Now, Sam!’ said Mr. Pickwick, looking back.\n\n‘I’m a-comin’, sir,’ replied Mr. Weller, mechanically following his master; and still he lifted not his eyes from Mr. Job Trotter, who walked at his side in silence.\n\nJob kept his eyes fixed on the ground for some time. Sam, with his glued to Job’s countenance, ran up against the people who were walking about, and fell over little children, and stumbled against steps and railings, without appearing at all sensible of it, until Job, looking stealthily up, said--\n\n‘How do you do, Mr. Weller?’\n\n‘It is him!’ exclaimed Sam; and having established Job’s identity beyond all doubt, he smote his leg, and vented his feelings in a long, shrill whistle.\n\n‘Things has altered with me, sir,’ said Job.\n\n‘I should think they had,’ exclaimed Mr. Weller, surveying his companion’s rags with undisguised wonder. ‘This is rayther a change for the worse, Mr. Trotter, as the gen’l’m’n said, wen he got two doubtful shillin’s and sixpenn’orth o’ pocket-pieces for a good half-crown.’\n\n‘It is indeed,’ replied Job, shaking his head. ‘There is no deception now, Mr. Weller. Tears,’ said Job, with a look of momentary slyness-- ‘tears are not the only proofs of distress, nor the best ones.’\n\n‘No, they ain’t,’ replied Sam expressively.\n\n‘They may be put on, Mr. Weller,’ said Job.\n\n‘I know they may,’ said Sam; ‘some people, indeed, has ‘em always ready laid on, and can pull out the plug wenever they likes.’\n\n‘Yes,’ replied Job; ‘but these sort of things are not so easily counterfeited, Mr. Weller, and it is a more painful process to get them up.’ As he spoke, he pointed to his sallow, sunken cheeks, and, drawing up his coat sleeve, disclosed an arm which looked as if the bone could be broken at a touch, so sharp and brittle did it appear, beneath its thin covering of flesh.\n\n‘Wot have you been a-doin’ to yourself?’ said Sam, recoiling.\n\n‘Nothing,’ replied Job.\n\n‘Nothin’!’ echoed Sam.\n\n‘I have been doin’ nothing for many weeks past,’ said Job; and eating and drinking almost as little.’\n\nSam took one comprehensive glance at Mr. Trotter’s thin face and wretched apparel; and then, seizing him by the arm, commenced dragging him away with great violence.\n\n‘Where are you going, Mr. Weller?’ said Job, vainly struggling in the powerful grasp of his old enemy.\n\n‘Come on,’ said Sam; ‘come on!’ He deigned no further explanation till they reached the tap, and then called for a pot of porter, which was speedily produced.\n\n‘Now,’ said Sam, ‘drink that up, ev’ry drop on it, and then turn the pot upside down, to let me see as you’ve took the medicine.’\n\n‘But, my dear Mr. Weller,’ remonstrated Job.\n\n‘Down vith it!’ said Sam peremptorily.\n\nThus admonished, Mr. Trotter raised the pot to his lips, and, by gentle and almost imperceptible degrees, tilted it into the air. He paused once, and only once, to draw a long breath, but without raising his face from the vessel, which, in a few moments thereafter, he held out at arm’s length, bottom upward. Nothing fell upon the ground but a few particles of froth, which slowly detached themselves from the rim, and trickled lazily down.\n\n‘Well done!’ said Sam. ‘How do you find yourself arter it?’\n\n‘Better, Sir. I think I am better,’ responded Job.\n\n‘O’ course you air,’ said Sam argumentatively. ‘It’s like puttin’ gas in a balloon. I can see with the naked eye that you gets stouter under the operation. Wot do you say to another o’ the same dimensions?’\n\n‘I would rather not, I am much obliged to you, Sir,’ replied Job--‘much rather not.’\n\n‘Vell, then, wot do you say to some wittles?’ inquired Sam.\n\n‘Thanks to your worthy governor, Sir,’ said Mr. Trotter, ‘we have half a leg of mutton, baked, at a quarter before three, with the potatoes under it to save boiling.’\n\n‘Wot! Has he been a-purwidin’ for you?’ asked Sam emphatically.\n\n‘He has, Sir,’ replied Job. ‘More than that, Mr. Weller; my master being very ill, he got us a room--we were in a kennel before--and paid for it, Sir; and come to look at us, at night, when nobody should know. Mr. Weller,’ said Job, with real tears in his eyes, for once, ‘I could serve that gentleman till I fell down dead at his feet.’\n\n‘I say!’ said Sam, ‘I’ll trouble you, my friend! None o’ that!’\n\nJob Trotter looked amazed.\n\n‘None o’ that, I say, young feller,’ repeated Sam firmly. ‘No man serves him but me. And now we’re upon it, I’ll let you into another secret besides that,’ said Sam, as he paid for the beer. ‘I never heerd, mind you, or read of in story-books, nor see in picters, any angel in tights and gaiters--not even in spectacles, as I remember, though that may ha’ been done for anythin’ I know to the contrairey--but mark my vords, Job Trotter, he’s a reg’lar thoroughbred angel for all that; and let me see the man as wenturs to tell me he knows a better vun.’ With this defiance, Mr. Weller buttoned up his change in a side pocket, and, with many confirmatory nods and gestures by the way, proceeded in search of the subject of discourse.\n\nThey found Mr. Pickwick, in company with Jingle, talking very earnestly, and not bestowing a look on the groups who were congregated on the racket-ground; they were very motley groups too, and worth the looking at, if it were only in idle curiosity.\n\n‘Well,’ said Mr. Pickwick, as Sam and his companion drew nigh, ‘you will see how your health becomes, and think about it meanwhile. Make the statement out for me when you feel yourself equal to the task, and I will discuss the subject with you when I have considered it. Now, go to your room. You are tired, and not strong enough to be out long.’\n\nMr. Alfred Jingle, without one spark of his old animation--with nothing even of the dismal gaiety which he had assumed when Mr. Pickwick first stumbled on him in his misery--bowed low without speaking, and, motioning to Job not to follow him just yet, crept slowly away.\n\n‘Curious scene this, is it not, Sam?’ said Mr. Pickwick, looking good- humouredly round.\n\n‘Wery much so, Sir,’ replied Sam. ‘Wonders ‘ull never cease,’ added Sam, speaking to himself. ‘I’m wery much mistaken if that ‘ere Jingle worn’t a-doin somethin’ in the water-cart way!’\n\nThe area formed by the wall in that part of the Fleet in which Mr. Pickwick stood was just wide enough to make a good racket-court; one side being formed, of course, by the wall itself, and the other by that portion of the prison which looked (or rather would have looked, but for the wall) towards St. Paul’s Cathedral. Sauntering or sitting about, in every possible attitude of listless idleness, were a great number of debtors, the major part of whom were waiting in prison until their day of ‘going up’ before the Insolvent Court should arrive; while others had been remanded for various terms, which they were idling away as they best could. Some were shabby, some were smart, many dirty, a few clean; but there they all lounged, and loitered, and slunk about with as little spirit or purpose as the beasts in a menagerie.\n\nLolling from the windows which commanded a view of this promenade were a number of persons, some in noisy conversation with their acquaintance below, others playing at ball with some adventurous throwers outside, others looking on at the racket-players, or watching the boys as they cried the game. Dirty, slipshod women passed and repassed, on their way to the cooking-house in one corner of the yard; children screamed, and fought, and played together, in another; the tumbling of the skittles, and the shouts of the players, mingled perpetually with these and a hundred other sounds; and all was noise and tumult--save in a little miserable shed a few yards off, where lay, all quiet and ghastly, the body of the Chancery prisoner who had died the night before, awaiting the mockery of an inquest. The body! It is the lawyer’s term for the restless, whirling mass of cares and anxieties, affections, hopes, and griefs, that make up the living man. The law had his body; and there it lay, clothed in grave-clothes, an awful witness to its tender mercy.\n\n‘Would you like to see a whistling-shop, Sir?’ inquired Job Trotter.\n\n‘What do you mean?’ was Mr. Pickwick’s counter inquiry.\n\n‘A vistlin’ shop, Sir,’ interposed Mr. Weller.\n\n‘What is that, Sam?--A bird-fancier’s?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Bless your heart, no, Sir,’ replied Job; ‘a whistling-shop, Sir, is where they sell spirits.’ Mr. Job Trotter briefly explained here, that all persons, being prohibited under heavy penalties from conveying spirits into debtors’ prisons, and such commodities being highly prized by the ladies and gentlemen confined therein, it had occurred to some speculative turnkey to connive, for certain lucrative considerations, at two or three prisoners retailing the favourite article of gin, for their own profit and advantage.\n\n‘This plan, you see, Sir, has been gradually introduced into all the prisons for debt,’ said Mr. Trotter.\n\n‘And it has this wery great advantage,’ said Sam, ‘that the turnkeys takes wery good care to seize hold o’ ev’rybody but them as pays ‘em, that attempts the willainy, and wen it gets in the papers they’re applauded for their wigilance; so it cuts two ways--frightens other people from the trade, and elewates their own characters.’\n\n‘Exactly so, Mr. Weller,’ observed Job.\n\n‘Well, but are these rooms never searched to ascertain whether any spirits are concealed in them?’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Cert’nly they are, Sir,’ replied Sam; ‘but the turnkeys knows beforehand, and gives the word to the wistlers, and you may wistle for it wen you go to look.’\n\nBy this time, Job had tapped at a door, which was opened by a gentleman with an uncombed head, who bolted it after them when they had walked in, and grinned; upon which Job grinned, and Sam also; whereupon Mr. Pickwick, thinking it might be expected of him, kept on smiling to the end of the interview.\n\nThe gentleman with the uncombed head appeared quite satisfied with this mute announcement of their business, and, producing a flat stone bottle, which might hold about a couple of quarts, from beneath his bedstead, filled out three glasses of gin, which Job Trotter and Sam disposed of in a most workmanlike manner.\n\n‘Any more?’ said the whistling gentleman.\n\n‘No more,’ replied Job Trotter.\n\nMr. Pickwick paid, the door was unbolted, and out they came; the uncombed gentleman bestowing a friendly nod upon Mr. Roker, who happened to be passing at the moment.\n\nFrom this spot, Mr. Pickwick wandered along all the galleries, up and down all the staircases, and once again round the whole area of the yard. The great body of the prison population appeared to be Mivins, and Smangle, and the parson, and the butcher, and the leg, over and over, and over again. There were the same squalor, the same turmoil and noise, the same general characteristics, in every corner; in the best and the worst alike. The whole place seemed restless and troubled; and the people were crowding and flitting to and fro, like the shadows in an uneasy dream.\n\n‘I have seen enough,’ said Mr. Pickwick, as he threw himself into a chair in his little apartment. ‘My head aches with these scenes, and my heart too. Henceforth I will be a prisoner in my own room.’\n\nAnd Mr. Pickwick steadfastly adhered to this determination. For three long months he remained shut up, all day; only stealing out at night to breathe the air, when the greater part of his fellow-prisoners were in bed or carousing in their rooms. His health was beginning to suffer from the closeness of the confinement, but neither the often-repeated entreaties of Perker and his friends, nor the still more frequently- repeated warnings and admonitions of Mr. Samuel Weller, could induce him to alter one jot of his inflexible resolution.\n\nCHAPTER XLVI. RECORDS A TOUCHING ACT OF DELICATE FEELING, NOT UNMIXED WITH PLEASANTRY, ACHIEVED AND PERFORMED BY Messrs. DODSON AND FOGG\nIt was within a week of the close of the month of July, that a hackney cabriolet, number unrecorded, was seen to proceed at a rapid pace up Goswell Street; three people were squeezed into it besides the driver, who sat in his own particular little dickey at the side; over the apron were hung two shawls, belonging to two small vixenish-looking ladies under the apron; between whom, compressed into a very small compass, was stowed away, a gentleman of heavy and subdued demeanour, who, whenever he ventured to make an observation, was snapped up short by one of the vixenish ladies before-mentioned. Lastly, the two vixenish ladies and the heavy gentleman were giving the driver contradictory directions, all tending to the one point, that he should stop at Mrs. Bardell’s door; which the heavy gentleman, in direct opposition to, and defiance of, the vixenish ladies, contended was a green door and not a yellow one.\n\n‘Stop at the house with a green door, driver,’ said the heavy gentleman.\n\n‘Oh! You perwerse creetur!’ exclaimed one of the vixenish ladies. ‘Drive to the ‘ouse with the yellow door, cabmin.’\n\nUpon this the cabman, who in a sudden effort to pull up at the house with the green door, had pulled the horse up so high that he nearly pulled him backward into the cabriolet, let the animal’s fore-legs down to the ground again, and paused.\n\n‘Now vere am I to pull up?’ inquired the driver. ‘Settle it among yourselves. All I ask is, vere?’\n\nHere the contest was renewed with increased violence; and the horse being troubled with a fly on his nose, the cabman humanely employed his leisure in lashing him about on the head, on the counter-irritation principle.\n\n‘Most wotes carries the day!’ said one of the vixenish ladies at length. ‘The ‘ouse with the yellow door, cabman.’\n\nBut after the cabriolet had dashed up, in splendid style, to the house with the yellow door, ‘making,’ as one of the vixenish ladies triumphantly said, ‘acterrally more noise than if one had come in one’s own carriage,’ and after the driver had dismounted to assist the ladies in getting out, the small round head of Master Thomas Bardell was thrust out of the one-pair window of a house with a red door, a few numbers off.\n\n‘Aggrawatin’ thing!’ said the vixenish lady last-mentioned, darting a withering glance at the heavy gentleman.\n\n‘My dear, it’s not my fault,’ said the gentleman.\n\n‘Don’t talk to me, you creetur, don’t,’ retorted the lady. ‘The house with the red door, cabmin. Oh! If ever a woman was troubled with a ruffinly creetur, that takes a pride and a pleasure in disgracing his wife on every possible occasion afore strangers, I am that woman!’\n\n‘You ought to be ashamed of yourself, Raddle,’ said the other little woman, who was no other than Mrs. Cluppins.\n\n‘What have I been a-doing of?’ asked Mr. Raddle.\n\n‘Don’t talk to me, don’t, you brute, for fear I should be perwoked to forgit my sect and strike you!’ said Mrs. Raddle.\n\nWhile this dialogue was going on, the driver was most ignominiously leading the horse, by the bridle, up to the house with the red door, which Master Bardell had already opened. Here was a mean and low way of arriving at a friend’s house! No dashing up, with all the fire and fury of the animal; no jumping down of the driver; no loud knocking at the door; no opening of the apron with a crash at the very last moment, for fear of the ladies sitting in a draught; and then the man handing the shawls out, afterwards, as if he were a private coachman! The whole edge of the thing had been taken off--it was flatter than walking.\n\n‘Well, Tommy,’ said Mrs. Cluppins, ‘how’s your poor dear mother?’\n\n‘Oh, she’s very well,’ replied Master Bardell. ‘She’s in the front parlour, all ready. I’m ready too, I am.’ Here Master Bardell put his hands in his pockets, and jumped off and on the bottom step of the door.\n\n‘Is anybody else a-goin’, Tommy?’ said Mrs. Cluppins, arranging her pelerine.\n\n‘Mrs. Sanders is going, she is,’ replied Tommy; ‘I’m going too, I am.’\n\n‘Drat the boy,’ said little Mrs. Cluppins. ‘He thinks of nobody but himself. Here, Tommy, dear.’\n\n‘Well,’ said Master Bardell.\n\n‘Who else is a-goin’, lovey?’ said Mrs. Cluppins, in an insinuating manner.\n\n‘Oh! Mrs. Rogers is a-goin’,’ replied Master Bardell, opening his eyes very wide as he delivered the intelligence.\n\n‘What? The lady as has taken the lodgings!’ ejaculated Mrs. Cluppins.\n\nMaster Bardell put his hands deeper down into his pockets, and nodded exactly thirty-five times, to imply that it was the lady-lodger, and no other.\n\n‘Bless us!’ said Mrs. Cluppins. ‘It’s quite a party!’\n\n‘Ah, if you knew what was in the cupboard, you’d say so,’ replied Master Bardell.\n\n‘What is there, Tommy?’ said Mrs. Cluppins coaxingly. ‘You’ll tell me, Tommy, I know.’\n\nNo, I won’t,’ replied Master Bardell, shaking his head, and applying himself to the bottom step again.\n\n‘Drat the child!’ muttered Mrs. Cluppins. ‘What a prowokin’ little wretch it is! Come, Tommy, tell your dear Cluppy.’\n\n‘Mother said I wasn’t to,’ rejoined Master Bardell, ‘I’m a-goin’ to have some, I am.’ Cheered by this prospect, the precocious boy applied himself to his infantile treadmill, with increased vigour.\n\nThe above examination of a child of tender years took place while Mr. and Mrs. Raddle and the cab-driver were having an altercation concerning the fare, which, terminating at this point in favour of the cabman, Mrs. Raddle came up tottering.\n\n‘Lauk, Mary Ann! what’s the matter?’ said Mrs. Cluppins.\n\n‘It’s put me all over in such a tremble, Betsy,’ replied Mrs. Raddle. ‘Raddle ain’t like a man; he leaves everythink to me.’\n\nThis was scarcely fair upon the unfortunate Mr. Raddle, who had been thrust aside by his good lady in the commencement of the dispute, and peremptorily commanded to hold his tongue. He had no opportunity of defending himself, however, for Mrs. Raddle gave unequivocal signs of fainting; which, being perceived from the parlour window, Mrs. Bardell, Mrs. Sanders, the lodger, and the lodger’s servant, darted precipitately out, and conveyed her into the house, all talking at the same time, and giving utterance to various expressions of pity and condolence, as if she were one of the most suffering mortals on earth. Being conveyed into the front parlour, she was there deposited on a sofa; and the lady from the first floor running up to the first floor, returned with a bottle of sal-volatile, which, holding Mrs. Raddle tight round the neck, she applied in all womanly kindness and pity to her nose, until that lady with many plunges and struggles was fain to declare herself decidedly better.\n\n‘Ah, poor thing!’ said Mrs. Rogers, ‘I know what her feelin’s is, too well.’\n\nAh, poor thing! so do I,’ said Mrs. Sanders; and then all the ladies moaned in unison, and said they knew what it was, and they pitied her from their hearts, they did. Even the lodger’s little servant, who was thirteen years old and three feet high, murmured her sympathy.\n\n‘But what’s been the matter?’ said Mrs. Bardell.\n\n‘Ah, what has decomposed you, ma’am?’ inquired Mrs. Rogers.\n\n‘I have been a good deal flurried,’ replied Mrs. Raddle, in a reproachful manner. Thereupon the ladies cast indignant glances at Mr. Raddle.\n\n‘Why, the fact is,’ said that unhappy gentleman, stepping forward, ‘when we alighted at this door, a dispute arose with the driver of the cabrioily--’ A loud scream from his wife, at the mention of this word, rendered all further explanation inaudible.\n\n‘You’d better leave us to bring her round, Raddle,’ said Mrs. Cluppins. ‘She’ll never get better as long as you’re here.’\n\nAll the ladies concurred in this opinion; so Mr. Raddle was pushed out of the room, and requested to give himself an airing in the back yard. Which he did for about a quarter of an hour, when Mrs. Bardell announced to him with a solemn face that he might come in now, but that he must be very careful how he behaved towards his wife. She knew he didn’t mean to be unkind; but Mary Ann was very far from strong, and, if he didn’t take care, he might lose her when he least expected it, which would be a very dreadful reflection for him afterwards; and so on. All this, Mr. Raddle heard with great submission, and presently returned to the parlour in a most lamb-like manner.\n\n‘Why, Mrs. Rogers, ma’am,’ said Mrs. Bardell, ‘you’ve never been introduced, I declare! Mr. Raddle, ma’am; Mrs. Cluppins, ma’am; Mrs. Raddle, ma’am.’\n\n‘Which is Mrs. Cluppins’s sister,’ suggested Mrs. Sanders.\n\n‘Oh, indeed!’ said Mrs. Rogers graciously; for she was the lodger, and her servant was in waiting, so she was more gracious than intimate, in right of her position. ‘Oh, indeed!’\n\nMrs. Raddle smiled sweetly, Mr. Raddle bowed, and Mrs. Cluppins said, ‘she was sure she was very happy to have an opportunity of being known to a lady which she had heerd so much in favour of, as Mrs. Rogers.’ A compliment which the last-named lady acknowledged with graceful condescension.\n\n‘Well, Mr. Raddle,’ said Mrs. Bardell; ‘I’m sure you ought to feel very much honoured at you and Tommy being the only gentlemen to escort so many ladies all the way to the Spaniards, at Hampstead. Don’t you think he ought, Mrs. Rogers, ma’am?’\n\nOh, certainly, ma’am,’ replied Mrs. Rogers; after whom all the other ladies responded, ‘Oh, certainly.’\n\n‘Of course I feel it, ma’am,’ said Mr. Raddle, rubbing his hands, and evincing a slight tendency to brighten up a little. ‘Indeed, to tell you the truth, I said, as we was a-coming along in the cabrioily--’\n\nAt the recapitulation of the word which awakened so many painful recollections, Mrs. Raddle applied her handkerchief to her eyes again, and uttered a half-suppressed scream; so that Mrs. Bardell frowned upon Mr. Raddle, to intimate that he had better not say anything more, and desired Mrs. Rogers’s servant, with an air, to ‘put the wine on.’\n\nThis was the signal for displaying the hidden treasures of the closet, which comprised sundry plates of oranges and biscuits, and a bottle of old crusted port--that at one-and-nine--with another of the celebrated East India sherry at fourteen-pence, which were all produced in honour of the lodger, and afforded unlimited satisfaction to everybody. After great consternation had been excited in the mind of Mrs. Cluppins, by an attempt on the part of Tommy to recount how he had been cross-examined regarding the cupboard then in action (which was fortunately nipped in the bud by his imbibing half a glass of the old crusted ‘the wrong way,’ and thereby endangering his life for some seconds), the party walked forth in quest of a Hampstead stage. This was soon found, and in a couple of hours they all arrived safely in the Spaniards Tea-gardens, where the luckless Mr. Raddle’s very first act nearly occasioned his good lady a relapse; it being neither more nor less than to order tea for seven, whereas (as the ladies one and all remarked), what could have been easier than for Tommy to have drank out of anybody’s cup--or everybody’s, if that was all--when the waiter wasn’t looking, which would have saved one head of tea, and the tea just as good!\n\nHowever, there was no help for it, and the tea-tray came, with seven cups and saucers, and bread-and-butter on the same scale. Mrs. Bardell was unanimously voted into the chair, and Mrs. Rogers being stationed on her right hand, and Mrs. Raddle on her left, the meal proceeded with great merriment and success.\n\n‘How sweet the country is, to be sure!’ sighed Mrs. Rogers; ‘I almost wish I lived in it always.’\n\n‘Oh, you wouldn’t like that, ma’am,’ replied Mrs. Bardell, rather hastily; for it was not at all advisable, with reference to the lodgings, to encourage such notions; ‘you wouldn’t like it, ma’am.’\n\n‘Oh! I should think you was a deal too lively and sought after, to be content with the country, ma’am,’ said little Mrs. Cluppins.\n\n‘Perhaps I am, ma’am. Perhaps I am,’ sighed the first-floor lodger.\n\n‘For lone people as have got nobody to care for them, or take care of them, or as have been hurt in their mind, or that kind of thing,’ observed Mr. Raddle, plucking up a little cheerfulness, and looking round, ‘the country is all very well. The country for a wounded spirit, they say.’\n\nNow, of all things in the world that the unfortunate man could have said, any would have been preferable to this. Of course Mrs. Bardell burst into tears, and requested to be led from the table instantly; upon which the affectionate child began to cry too, most dismally.\n\n‘Would anybody believe, ma’am,’ exclaimed Mrs. Raddle, turning fiercely to the first-floor lodger, ‘that a woman could be married to such a unmanly creetur, which can tamper with a woman’s feelings as he does, every hour in the day, ma’am?’\n\n‘My dear,’ remonstrated Mr. Raddle, ‘I didn’t mean anything, my dear.’\n\n‘You didn’t mean!’ repeated Mrs. Raddle, with great scorn and contempt. ‘Go away. I can’t bear the sight on you, you brute.’\n\n‘You must not flurry yourself, Mary Ann,’ interposed Mrs. Cluppins. ‘You really must consider yourself, my dear, which you never do. Now go away, Raddle, there’s a good soul, or you’ll only aggravate her.’\n\n‘You had better take your tea by yourself, Sir, indeed,’ said Mrs. Rogers, again applying the smelling-bottle.\n\nMrs. Sanders, who, according to custom, was very busy with the bread- and-butter, expressed the same opinion, and Mr. Raddle quietly retired.\n\nAfter this, there was a great hoisting up of Master Bardell, who was rather a large size for hugging, into his mother’s arms, in which operation he got his boots in the tea-board, and occasioned some confusion among the cups and saucers. But that description of fainting fits, which is contagious among ladies, seldom lasts long; so when he had been well kissed, and a little cried over, Mrs. Bardell recovered, set him down again, wondering how she could have been so foolish, and poured out some more tea.\n\nIt was at this moment, that the sound of approaching wheels was heard, and that the ladies, looking up, saw a hackney-coach stop at the garden gate.\n\n‘More company!’ said Mrs. Sanders.\n\n‘It’s a gentleman,’ said Mrs. Raddle.\n\n‘Well, if it ain’t Mr. Jackson, the young man from Dodson and Fogg’s!’ cried Mrs. Bardell. ‘Why, gracious! Surely Mr. Pickwick can’t have paid the damages.’\n\n‘Or hoffered marriage!’ said Mrs. Cluppins.\n\n‘Dear me, how slow the gentleman is,’ exclaimed Mrs. Rogers. ‘Why doesn’t he make haste!’\n\nAs the lady spoke these words, Mr. Jackson turned from the coach where he had been addressing some observations to a shabby man in black leggings, who had just emerged from the vehicle with a thick ash stick in his hand, and made his way to the place where the ladies were seated; winding his hair round the brim of his hat, as he came along.\n\n‘Is anything the matter? Has anything taken place, Mr. Jackson?’ said Mrs. Bardell eagerly.\n\n‘Nothing whatever, ma’am,’ replied Mr. Jackson. ‘How de do, ladies? I have to ask pardon, ladies, for intruding--but the law, ladies--the law.’ With this apology Mr. Jackson smiled, made a comprehensive bow, and gave his hair another wind. Mrs. Rogers whispered Mrs. Raddle that he was really an elegant young man.\n\n‘I called in Goswell Street,’ resumed Mr. Jackson, ‘and hearing that you were here, from the slavey, took a coach and came on. Our people want you down in the city directly, Mrs. Bardell.’\n\n‘Lor!’ ejaculated that lady, starting at the sudden nature of the communication.\n\n‘Yes,’ said Mr. Jackson, biting his lip. ‘It’s very important and pressing business, which can’t be postponed on any account. Indeed, Dodson expressly said so to me, and so did Fogg. I’ve kept the coach on purpose for you to go back in.’\n\n‘How very strange!’ exclaimed Mrs. Bardell.\n\nThe ladies agreed that it was very strange, but were unanimously of opinion that it must be very important, or Dodson & Fogg would never have sent; and further, that the business being urgent, she ought to repair to Dodson & Fogg’s without any delay.\n\nThere was a certain degree of pride and importance about being wanted by one’s lawyers in such a monstrous hurry, that was by no means displeasing to Mrs. Bardell, especially as it might be reasonably supposed to enhance her consequence in the eyes of the first-floor lodger. She simpered a little, affected extreme vexation and hesitation, and at last arrived at the conclusion that she supposed she must go.\n\n‘But won’t you refresh yourself after your walk, Mr. Jackson?’ said Mrs. Bardell persuasively.\n\n‘Why, really there ain’t much time to lose,’ replied Jackson; ‘and I’ve got a friend here,’ he continued, looking towards the man with the ash stick.\n\n‘Oh, ask your friend to come here, Sir,’ said Mrs. Bardell. ‘Pray ask your friend here, Sir.’\n\n‘Why, thank’ee, I’d rather not,’ said Mr. Jackson, with some embarrassment of manner. ‘He’s not much used to ladies’ society, and it makes him bashful. If you’ll order the waiter to deliver him anything short, he won’t drink it off at once, won’t he!--only try him!’ Mr. Jackson’s fingers wandered playfully round his nose at this portion of his discourse, to warn his hearers that he was speaking ironically.\n\nThe waiter was at once despatched to the bashful gentleman, and the bashful gentleman took something; Mr. Jackson also took something, and the ladies took something, for hospitality’s sake. Mr. Jackson then said he was afraid it was time to go; upon which, Mrs. Sanders, Mrs. Cluppins, and Tommy (who it was arranged should accompany Mrs. Bardell, leaving the others to Mr. Raddle’s protection), got into the coach.\n\n‘Isaac,’ said Jackson, as Mrs. Bardell prepared to get in, looking up at the man with the ash stick, who was seated on the box, smoking a cigar.\n\n‘Well?’\n\n‘This is Mrs. Bardell.’\n\n‘Oh, I know’d that long ago,’ said the man.\n\nMrs. Bardell got in, Mr. Jackson got in after her, and away they drove. Mrs. Bardell could not help ruminating on what Mr. Jackson’s friend had said. Shrewd creatures, those lawyers. Lord bless us, how they find people out!\n\n‘Sad thing about these costs of our people’s, ain’t it,’ said Jackson, when Mrs. Cluppins and Mrs. Sanders had fallen asleep; ‘your bill of costs, I mean.’\n\n‘I’m very sorry they can’t get them,’ replied Mrs. Bardell. ‘But if you law gentlemen do these things on speculation, why you must get a loss now and then, you know.’\n\n‘You gave them a cognovit for the amount of your costs, after the trial, I’m told!’ said Jackson.\n\n‘Yes. Just as a matter of form,’ replied Mrs. Bardell.\n\n‘Certainly,’ replied Jackson drily. ‘Quite a matter of form. Quite.’\n\nOn they drove, and Mrs. Bardell fell asleep. She was awakened, after some time, by the stopping of the coach.\n\n‘Bless us!’ said the lady. ‘Are we at Freeman’s Court?’\n\n‘We’re not going quite so far,’ replied Jackson. ‘Have the goodness to step out.’\n\nMrs. Bardell, not yet thoroughly awake, complied. It was a curious place: a large wall, with a gate in the middle, and a gas-light burning inside.\n\n‘Now, ladies,’ cried the man with the ash stick, looking into the coach, and shaking Mrs. Sanders to wake her, ‘Come!’ Rousing her friend, Mrs. Sanders alighted. Mrs. Bardell, leaning on Jackson’s arm, and leading Tommy by the hand, had already entered the porch. They followed.\n\nThe room they turned into was even more odd-looking than the porch. Such a number of men standing about! And they stared so!\n\n‘What place is this?’ inquired Mrs. Bardell, pausing.\n\n‘Only one of our public offices,’ replied Jackson, hurrying her through a door, and looking round to see that the other women were following. ‘Look sharp, Isaac!’\n\n‘Safe and sound,’ replied the man with the ash stick. The door swung heavily after them, and they descended a small flight of steps.\n\n‘Here we are at last. All right and tight, Mrs. Bardell!’ said Jackson, looking exultingly round.\n\n‘What do you mean?’ said Mrs. Bardell, with a palpitating heart.\n\n‘Just this,’ replied Jackson, drawing her a little on one side; ‘don’t be frightened, Mrs. Bardell. There never was a more delicate man than Dodson, ma’am, or a more humane man than Fogg. It was their duty in the way of business, to take you in execution for them costs; but they were anxious to spare your feelings as much as they could. What a comfort it must be, to you, to think how it’s been done! This is the Fleet, ma’am. Wish you good-night, Mrs. Bardell. Good-night, Tommy!’\n\nAs Jackson hurried away in company with the man with the ash stick another man, with a key in his hand, who had been looking on, led the bewildered female to a second short flight of steps leading to a doorway. Mrs. Bardell screamed violently; Tommy roared; Mrs. Cluppins shrunk within herself; and Mrs. Sanders made off, without more ado. For there stood the injured Mr. Pickwick, taking his nightly allowance of air; and beside him leant Samuel Weller, who, seeing Mrs. Bardell, took his hat off with mock reverence, while his master turned indignantly on his heel.\n\n‘Don’t bother the woman,’ said the turnkey to Weller; ‘she’s just come in.’\n\n‘A prisoner!’ said Sam, quickly replacing his hat. ‘Who’s the plaintives? What for? Speak up, old feller.’\n\n‘Dodson and Fogg,’ replied the man; ‘execution on cognovit for costs.’\n\n‘Here, Job, Job!’ shouted Sam, dashing into the passage. ‘Run to Mr. Perker’s, Job. I want him directly. I see some good in this. Here’s a game. Hooray! vere’s the gov’nor?’\n\nBut there was no reply to these inquiries, for Job had started furiously off, the instant he received his commission, and Mrs. Bardell had fainted in real downright earnest.\n\nCHAPTER XLVII. IS CHIEFLY DEVOTED TO MATTERS OF BUSINESS, AND THE TEMPORAL ADVANTAGE OF DODSON AND FOGG--MR. WINKLE REAPPEARS UNDER EXTRAORDINARY CIRCUMSTANCES--MR. PICKWICK’S BENEVOLENCE PROVES STRONGER THAN HIS OBSTINACY\nJob Trotter, abating nothing of his speed, ran up Holborn, sometimes in the middle of the road, sometimes on the pavement, sometimes in the gutter, as the chances of getting along varied with the press of men, women, children, and coaches, in each division of the thoroughfare, and, regardless of all obstacles stopped not for an instant until he reached the gate of Gray’s Inn. Notwithstanding all the expedition he had used, however, the gate had been closed a good half-hour when he reached it, and by the time he had discovered Mr. Perker’s laundress, who lived with a married daughter, who had bestowed her hand upon a non-resident waiter, who occupied the one-pair of some number in some street closely adjoining to some brewery somewhere behind Gray’s Inn Lane, it was within fifteen minutes of closing the prison for the night. Mr. Lowten had still to be ferreted out from the back parlour of the Magpie and Stump; and Job had scarcely accomplished this object, and communicated Sam Weller’s message, when the clock struck ten.\n\n‘There,’ said Lowten, ‘it’s too late now. You can’t get in to-night; you’ve got the key of the street, my friend.’\n\n‘Never mind me,’ replied Job. ‘I can sleep anywhere. But won’t it be better to see Mr. Perker to-night, so that we may be there, the first thing in the morning?’\n\n‘Why,’ responded Lowten, after a little consideration, ‘if it was in anybody else’s case, Perker wouldn’t be best pleased at my going up to his house; but as it’s Mr. Pickwick’s, I think I may venture to take a cab and charge it to the office.’ Deciding on this line of conduct, Mr. Lowten took up his hat, and begging the assembled company to appoint a deputy-chairman during his temporary absence, led the way to the nearest coach-stand. Summoning the cab of most promising appearance, he directed the driver to repair to Montague Place, Russell Square.\n\nMr. Perker had had a dinner-party that day, as was testified by the appearance of lights in the drawing-room windows, the sound of an improved grand piano, and an improvable cabinet voice issuing therefrom, and a rather overpowering smell of meat which pervaded the steps and entry. In fact, a couple of very good country agencies happening to come up to town, at the same time, an agreeable little party had been got together to meet them, comprising Mr. Snicks, the Life Office Secretary, Mr. Prosee, the eminent counsel, three solicitors, one commissioner of bankrupts, a special pleader from the Temple, a small-eyed peremptory young gentleman, his pupil, who had written a lively book about the law of demises, with a vast quantity of marginal notes and references; and several other eminent and distinguished personages. From this society, little Mr. Perker detached himself, on his clerk being announced in a whisper; and repairing to the dining-room, there found Mr. Lowten and Job Trotter looking very dim and shadowy by the light of a kitchen candle, which the gentleman who condescended to appear in plush shorts and cottons for a quarterly stipend, had, with a becoming contempt for the clerk and all things appertaining to ‘the office,’ placed upon the table.\n\n‘Now, Lowten,’ said little Mr. Perker, shutting the door, ‘what’s the matter? No important letter come in a parcel, is there?’\n\n‘No, Sir,’ replied Lowten. ‘This is a messenger from Mr. Pickwick, Sir.’\n\n‘From Pickwick, eh?’ said the little man, turning quickly to Job. ‘Well, what is it?’\n\n‘Dodson and Fogg have taken Mrs. Bardell in execution for her costs, Sir,’ said Job.\n\n‘No!’ exclaimed Perker, putting his hands in his pockets, and reclining against the sideboard.\n\n‘Yes,’ said Job. ‘It seems they got a cognovit out of her, for the amount of ‘em, directly after the trial.’\n\n‘By Jove!’ said Perker, taking both hands out of his pockets, and striking the knuckles of his right against the palm of his left, emphatically, ‘those are the cleverest scamps I ever had anything to do with!’\n\n‘The sharpest practitioners I ever knew, Sir,’ observed Lowten.\n\n‘Sharp!’ echoed Perker. ‘There’s no knowing where to have them.’\n\n‘Very true, Sir, there is not,’ replied Lowten; and then, both master and man pondered for a few seconds, with animated countenances, as if they were reflecting upon one of the most beautiful and ingenious discoveries that the intellect of man had ever made. When they had in some measure recovered from their trance of admiration, Job Trotter discharged himself of the rest of his commission. Perker nodded his head thoughtfully, and pulled out his watch.\n\n‘At ten precisely, I will be there,’ said the little man. ‘Sam is quite right. Tell him so. Will you take a glass of wine, Lowten?’\n\nNo, thank you, Sir.’\n\n‘You mean yes, I think,’ said the little man, turning to the sideboard for a decanter and glasses.\n\nAs Lowten did mean yes, he said no more on the subject, but inquired of Job, in an audible whisper, whether the portrait of Perker, which hung opposite the fireplace, wasn’t a wonderful likeness, to which Job of course replied that it was. The wine being by this time poured out, Lowten drank to Mrs. Perker and the children, and Job to Perker. The gentleman in the plush shorts and cottons considering it no part of his duty to show the people from the office out, consistently declined to answer the bell, and they showed themselves out. The attorney betook himself to his drawing-room, the clerk to the Magpie and Stump, and Job to Covent Garden Market to spend the night in a vegetable basket.\n\nPunctually at the appointed hour next morning, the good-humoured little attorney tapped at Mr. Pickwick’s door, which was opened with great alacrity by Sam Weller.\n\n‘Mr. Perker, sir,’ said Sam, announcing the visitor to Mr. Pickwick, who was sitting at the window in a thoughtful attitude. ‘Wery glad you’ve looked in accidentally, Sir. I rather think the gov’nor wants to have a word and a half with you, Sir.’\n\nPerker bestowed a look of intelligence on Sam, intimating that he understood he was not to say he had been sent for; and beckoning him to approach, whispered briefly in his ear.\n\n‘You don’t mean that ‘ere, Sir?’ said Sam, starting back in excessive surprise.\n\nPerker nodded and smiled.\n\nMr. Samuel Weller looked at the little lawyer, then at Mr. Pickwick, then at the ceiling, then at Perker again; grinned, laughed outright, and finally, catching up his hat from the carpet, without further explanation, disappeared.\n\n‘What does this mean?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick, looking at Perker with astonishment. ‘What has put Sam into this extraordinary state?’\n\n‘Oh, nothing, nothing,’ replied Perker. ‘Come, my dear Sir, draw up your chair to the table. I have a good deal to say to you.’\n\n‘What papers are those?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick, as the little man deposited on the table a small bundle of documents tied with red tape.\n\n‘The papers in Bardell and Pickwick,’ replied Perker, undoing the knot with his teeth.\n\nMr. Pickwick grated the legs of his chair against the ground; and throwing himself into it, folded his hands and looked sternly--if Mr. Pickwick ever could look sternly--at his legal friend.\n\n‘You don’t like to hear the name of the cause?’ said the little man, still busying himself with the knot.\n\n‘No, I do not indeed,’ replied Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Sorry for that,’ resumed Perker, ‘because it will form the subject of our conversation.’\n\n‘I would rather that the subject should be never mentioned between us, Perker,’ interposed Mr. Pickwick hastily.\n\n‘Pooh, pooh, my dear Sir,’ said the little man, untying the bundle, and glancing eagerly at Mr. Pickwick out of the corners of his eyes. ‘It must be mentioned. I have come here on purpose. Now, are you ready to hear what I have to say, my dear Sir? No hurry; if you are not, I can wait. I have this morning’s paper here. Your time shall be mine. There!’ Hereupon, the little man threw one leg over the other, and made a show of beginning to read with great composure and application.\n\n‘Well, well,’ said Mr. Pickwick, with a sigh, but softening into a smile at the same time. ‘Say what you have to say; it’s the old story, I suppose?’\n\n‘With a difference, my dear Sir; with a difference,’ rejoined Perker, deliberately folding up the paper and putting it into his pocket again. ‘Mrs. Bardell, the plaintiff in the action, is within these walls, Sir.’\n\n‘I know it,’ was Mr. Pickwick’s reply.\n\n‘Very good,’ retorted Perker. ‘And you know how she comes here, I suppose; I mean on what grounds, and at whose suit?’\n\n‘Yes; at least I have heard Sam’s account of the matter,’ said Mr. Pickwick, with affected carelessness.\n\n‘Sam’s account of the matter,’ replied Perker, ‘is, I will venture to say, a perfectly correct one. Well now, my dear Sir, the first question I have to ask, is, whether this woman is to remain here?’\n\n‘To remain here!’ echoed Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘To remain here, my dear Sir,’ rejoined Perker, leaning back in his chair and looking steadily at his client.\n\n‘How can you ask me?’ said that gentleman. ‘It rests with Dodson and Fogg; you know that very well.’\n\n‘I know nothing of the kind,’ retorted Perker firmly. ‘It does not rest with Dodson and Fogg; you know the men, my dear Sir, as well as I do. It rests solely, wholly, and entirely with you.’\n\n‘With me!’ ejaculated Mr. Pickwick, rising nervously from his chair, and reseating himself directly afterwards.\n\nThe little man gave a double-knock on the lid of his snuff-box, opened it, took a great pinch, shut it up again, and repeated the words, ‘With you.’\n\n‘I say, my dear Sir,’ resumed the little man, who seemed to gather confidence from the snuff--‘I say, that her speedy liberation or perpetual imprisonment rests with you, and with you alone. Hear me out, my dear Sir, if you please, and do not be so very energetic, for it will only put you into a perspiration and do no good whatever. I say,’ continued Perker, checking off each position on a different finger, as he laid it down--‘I say that nobody but you can rescue her from this den of wretchedness; and that you can only do that, by paying the costs of this suit--both of plaintive and defendant--into the hands of these Freeman Court sharks. Now pray be quiet, my dear sir.’\n\nMr. Pickwick, whose face had been undergoing most surprising changes during this speech, and was evidently on the verge of a strong burst of indignation, calmed his wrath as well as he could. Perker, strengthening his argumentative powers with another pinch of snuff, proceeded--\n\n‘I have seen the woman, this morning. By paying the costs, you can obtain a full release and discharge from the damages; and further--this I know is a far greater object of consideration with you, my dear sir--a voluntary statement, under her hand, in the form of a letter to me, that this business was, from the very first, fomented, and encouraged, and brought about, by these men, Dodson and Fogg; that she deeply regrets ever having been the instrument of annoyance or injury to you; and that she entreats me to intercede with you, and implore your pardon.’\n\n‘If I pay her costs for her,’ said Mr. Pickwick indignantly. ‘A valuable document, indeed!’\n\n‘No “if” in the case, my dear Sir,’ said Perker triumphantly. ‘There is the very letter I speak of. Brought to my office by another woman at nine o’clock this morning, before I had set foot in this place, or held any communication with Mrs. Bardell, upon my honour.’ Selecting the letter from the bundle, the little lawyer laid it at Mr. Pickwick’s elbow, and took snuff for two consecutive minutes, without winking.\n\n‘Is this all you have to say to me?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick mildly.\n\n‘Not quite,’ replied Perker. ‘I cannot undertake to say, at this moment, whether the wording of the cognovit, the nature of the ostensible consideration, and the proof we can get together about the whole conduct of the suit, will be sufficient to justify an indictment for conspiracy. I fear not, my dear Sir; they are too clever for that, I doubt. I do mean to say, however, that the whole facts, taken together, will be sufficient to justify you, in the minds of all reasonable men. And now, my dear Sir, I put it to you. This one hundred and fifty pounds, or whatever it may be--take it in round numbers--is nothing to you. A jury had decided against you; well, their verdict is wrong, but still they decided as they thought right, and it is against you. You have now an opportunity, on easy terms, of placing yourself in a much higher position than you ever could, by remaining here; which would only be imputed, by people who didn’t know you, to sheer dogged, wrongheaded, brutal obstinacy; nothing else, my dear Sir, believe me. Can you hesitate to avail yourself of it, when it restores you to your friends, your old pursuits, your health and amusements; when it liberates your faithful and attached servant, whom you otherwise doom to imprisonment for the whole of your life; and above all, when it enables you to take the very magnanimous revenge--which I know, my dear sir, is one after your own heart--of releasing this woman from a scene of misery and debauchery, to which no man should ever be consigned, if I had my will, but the infliction of which on any woman, is even more frightful and barbarous. Now I ask you, my dear sir, not only as your legal adviser, but as your very true friend, will you let slip the occasion of attaining all these objects, and doing all this good, for the paltry consideration of a few pounds finding their way into the pockets of a couple of rascals, to whom it makes no manner of difference, except that the more they gain, the more they’ll seek, and so the sooner be led into some piece of knavery that must end in a crash? I have put these considerations to you, my dear Sir, very feebly and imperfectly, but I ask you to think of them. Turn them over in your mind as long as you please. I wait here most patiently for your answer.’\n\nBefore Mr. Pickwick could reply, before Mr. Perker had taken one twentieth part of the snuff with which so unusually long an address imperatively required to be followed up, there was a low murmuring of voices outside, and then a hesitating knock at the door.\n\n‘Dear, dear,’ exclaimed Mr. Pickwick, who had been evidently roused by his friend’s appeal; ‘what an annoyance that door is! Who is that?’\n\n‘Me, Sir,’ replied Sam Weller, putting in his head.\n\n‘I can’t speak to you just now, Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick. ‘I am engaged at this moment, Sam.’\n\n‘Beg your pardon, Sir,’ rejoined Mr. Weller. ‘But here’s a lady here, Sir, as says she’s somethin’ wery partickler to disclose.’\n\n‘I can’t see any lady,’ replied Mr. Pickwick, whose mind was filled with visions of Mrs. Bardell.\n\n‘I wouldn’t make too sure o’ that, Sir,’ urged Mr. Weller, shaking his head. ‘If you know’d who was near, sir, I rayther think you’d change your note; as the hawk remarked to himself vith a cheerful laugh, ven he heerd the robin-redbreast a-singin’ round the corner.’\n\n‘Who is it?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Will you see her, Sir?’ asked Mr. Weller, holding the door in his hand as if he had some curious live animal on the other side.\n\n‘I suppose I must,’ said Mr. Pickwick, looking at Perker.\n\n‘Well then, all in to begin!’ cried Sam. ‘Sound the gong, draw up the curtain, and enter the two conspiraytors.’\n\nAs Sam Weller spoke, he threw the door open, and there rushed tumultuously into the room, Mr. Nathaniel Winkle, leading after him by the hand, the identical young lady who at Dingley Dell had worn the boots with the fur round the tops, and who, now a very pleasing compound of blushes and confusion, and lilac silk, and a smart bonnet, and a rich lace veil, looked prettier than ever.\n\n‘Miss Arabella Allen!’ exclaimed Mr. Pickwick, rising from his chair.\n\n‘No,’ replied Mr. Winkle, dropping on his knees. ‘Mrs. Winkle. Pardon, my dear friend, pardon!’\n\nMr. Pickwick could scarcely believe the evidence of his senses, and perhaps would not have done so, but for the corroborative testimony afforded by the smiling countenance of Perker, and the bodily presence, in the background, of Sam and the pretty housemaid; who appeared to contemplate the proceedings with the liveliest satisfaction.\n\n‘Oh, Mr. Pickwick!’ said Arabella, in a low voice, as if alarmed at the silence. ‘Can you forgive my imprudence?’\n\nMr. Pickwick returned no verbal response to this appeal; but he took off his spectacles in great haste, and seizing both the young lady’s hands in his, kissed her a great number of times--perhaps a greater number than was absolutely necessary--and then, still retaining one of her hands, told Mr. Winkle he was an audacious young dog, and bade him get up. This, Mr. Winkle, who had been for some seconds scratching his nose with the brim of his hat, in a penitent manner, did; whereupon Mr. Pickwick slapped him on the back several times, and then shook hands heartily with Perker, who, not to be behind-hand in the compliments of the occasion, saluted both the bride and the pretty housemaid with right good-will, and, having wrung Mr. Winkle’s hand most cordially, wound up his demonstrations of joy by taking snuff enough to set any half-dozen men with ordinarily-constructed noses, a-sneezing for life.\n\n‘Why, my dear girl,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘how has all this come about? Come! Sit down, and let me hear it all. How well she looks, doesn’t she, Perker?’ added Mr. Pickwick, surveying Arabella’s face with a look of as much pride and exultation, as if she had been his daughter.\n\n‘Delightful, my dear Sir,’ replied the little man. ‘If I were not a married man myself, I should be disposed to envy you, you dog.’ Thus expressing himself, the little lawyer gave Mr. Winkle a poke in the chest, which that gentleman reciprocated; after which they both laughed very loudly, but not so loudly as Mr. Samuel Weller, who had just relieved his feelings by kissing the pretty housemaid under cover of the cupboard door.\n\n‘I can never be grateful enough to you, Sam, I am sure,’ said Arabella, with the sweetest smile imaginable. ‘I shall not forget your exertions in the garden at Clifton.’\n\n‘Don’t say nothin’ wotever about it, ma’am,’ replied Sam. ‘I only assisted natur, ma’am; as the doctor said to the boy’s mother, after he’d bled him to death.’\n\n‘Mary, my dear, sit down,’ said Mr. Pickwick, cutting short these compliments. ‘Now then; how long have you been married, eh?’\n\nArabella looked bashfully at her lord and master, who replied, ‘Only three days.’\n\n‘Only three days, eh?’ said Mr. Pickwick. ‘Why, what have you been doing these three months?’\n\n‘Ah, to be sure!’ interposed Perker; ‘come, account for this idleness. You see Mr. Pickwick’s only astonishment is, that it wasn’t all over, months ago.’\n\n‘Why the fact is,’ replied Mr. Winkle, looking at his blushing young wife, ‘that I could not persuade Bella to run away, for a long time. And when I had persuaded her, it was a long time more before we could find an opportunity. Mary had to give a month’s warning, too, before she could leave her place next door, and we couldn’t possibly have done it without her assistance.’\n\nUpon my word,’ exclaimed Mr. Pickwick, who by this time had resumed his spectacles, and was looking from Arabella to Winkle, and from Winkle to Arabella, with as much delight depicted in his countenance as warmheartedness and kindly feeling can communicate to the human face-- ‘upon my word! you seem to have been very systematic in your proceedings. And is your brother acquainted with all this, my dear?’\n\n‘Oh, no, no,’ replied Arabella, changing colour. ‘Dear Mr. Pickwick, he must only know it from you--from your lips alone. He is so violent, so prejudiced, and has been so--so anxious in behalf of his friend, Mr. Sawyer,’ added Arabella, looking down, ‘that I fear the consequences dreadfully.’\n\n‘Ah, to be sure,’ said Perker gravely. ‘You must take this matter in hand for them, my dear sir. These young men will respect you, when they would listen to nobody else. You must prevent mischief, my dear Sir. Hot blood, hot blood.’ And the little man took a warning pinch, and shook his head doubtfully.\n\n‘You forget, my love,’ said Mr. Pickwick gently, ‘you forget that I am a prisoner.’\n\n‘No, indeed I do not, my dear Sir,’ replied Arabella. ‘I never have forgotten it. I have never ceased to think how great your sufferings must have been in this shocking place. But I hoped that what no consideration for yourself would induce you to do, a regard to our happiness might. If my brother hears of this, first, from you, I feel certain we shall be reconciled. He is my only relation in the world, Mr. Pickwick, and unless you plead for me, I fear I have lost even him. I have done wrong, very, very wrong, I know.’ Here poor Arabella hid her face in her handkerchief, and wept bitterly.\n\nMr. Pickwick’s nature was a good deal worked upon, by these same tears; but when Mrs. Winkle, drying her eyes, took to coaxing and entreating in the sweetest tones of a very sweet voice, he became particularly restless, and evidently undecided how to act, as was evinced by sundry nervous rubbings of his spectacle-glasses, nose, tights, head, and gaiters.\n\nTaking advantage of these symptoms of indecision, Mr. Perker (to whom, it appeared, the young couple had driven straight that morning) urged with legal point and shrewdness that Mr. Winkle, senior, was still unacquainted with the important rise in life’s flight of steps which his son had taken; that the future expectations of the said son depended entirely upon the said Winkle, senior, continuing to regard him with undiminished feelings of affection and attachment, which it was very unlikely he would, if this great event were long kept a secret from him; that Mr. Pickwick, repairing to Bristol to seek Mr. Allen, might, with equal reason, repair to Birmingham to seek Mr. Winkle, senior; lastly, that Mr. Winkle, senior, had good right and title to consider Mr. Pickwick as in some degree the guardian and adviser of his son, and that it consequently behoved that gentleman, and was indeed due to his personal character, to acquaint the aforesaid Winkle, senior, personally, and by word of mouth, with the whole circumstances of the case, and with the share he had taken in the transaction.\n\nMr. Tupman and Mr. Snodgrass arrived, most opportunely, in this stage of the pleadings, and as it was necessary to explain to them all that had occurred, together with the various reasons pro and con, the whole of the arguments were gone over again, after which everybody urged every argument in his own way, and at his own length. And, at last, Mr. Pickwick, fairly argued and remonstrated out of all his resolutions, and being in imminent danger of being argued and remonstrated out of his wits, caught Arabella in his arms, and declaring that she was a very amiable creature, and that he didn’t know how it was, but he had always been very fond of her from the first, said he could never find it in his heart to stand in the way of young people’s happiness, and they might do with him as they pleased.\n\nMr. Weller’s first act, on hearing this concession, was to despatch Job Trotter to the illustrious Mr. Pell, with an authority to deliver to the bearer the formal discharge which his prudent parent had had the foresight to leave in the hands of that learned gentleman, in case it should be, at any time, required on an emergency; his next proceeding was, to invest his whole stock of ready-money in the purchase of five- and-twenty gallons of mild porter, which he himself dispensed on the racket-ground to everybody who would partake of it; this done, he hurra’d in divers parts of the building until he lost his voice, and then quietly relapsed into his usual collected and philosophical condition.\n\nAt three o’clock that afternoon, Mr. Pickwick took a last look at his little room, and made his way, as well as he could, through the throng of debtors who pressed eagerly forward to shake him by the hand, until he reached the lodge steps. He turned here, to look about him, and his eye lightened as he did so. In all the crowd of wan, emaciated faces, he saw not one which was not happier for his sympathy and charity.\n\n‘Perker,’ said Mr. Pickwick, beckoning one young man towards him, ‘this is Mr. Jingle, whom I spoke to you about.’\n\n‘Very good, my dear Sir,’ replied Perker, looking hard at Jingle. ‘You will see me again, young man, to-morrow. I hope you may live to remember and feel deeply, what I shall have to communicate, Sir.’\n\nJingle bowed respectfully, trembled very much as he took Mr. Pickwick’s proffered hand, and withdrew.\n\n‘Job you know, I think?’ said Mr. Pickwick, presenting that gentleman.\n\n‘I know the rascal,’ replied Perker good-humouredly. ‘See after your friend, and be in the way to-morrow at one. Do you hear? Now, is there anything more?’\n\n‘Nothing,’ rejoined Mr. Pickwick. ‘You have delivered the little parcel I gave you for your old landlord, Sam?’\n\n‘I have, Sir,’ replied Sam. ‘He bust out a-cryin’, Sir, and said you wos wery gen’rous and thoughtful, and he only wished you could have him innockilated for a gallopin’ consumption, for his old friend as had lived here so long wos dead, and he’d noweres to look for another.’\n\nPoor fellow, poor fellow!’ said Mr. Pickwick. ‘God bless you, my friends!’\n\nAs Mr. Pickwick uttered this adieu, the crowd raised a loud shout. Many among them were pressing forward to shake him by the hand again, when he drew his arm through Perker’s, and hurried from the prison, far more sad and melancholy, for the moment, than when he had first entered it. Alas! how many sad and unhappy beings had he left behind!\n\nA happy evening was that for at least one party in the George and Vulture; and light and cheerful were two of the hearts that emerged from its hospitable door next morning. The owners thereof were Mr. Pickwick and Sam Weller, the former of whom was speedily deposited inside a comfortable post-coach, with a little dickey behind, in which the latter mounted with great agility.\n\n‘Sir,’ called out Mr. Weller to his master.\n\n‘Well, Sam,’ replied Mr. Pickwick, thrusting his head out of the window.\n\n‘I wish them horses had been three months and better in the Fleet, Sir.’\n\n‘Why, Sam?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Wy, Sir,’ exclaimed Mr. Weller, rubbing his hands, ‘how they would go if they had been!’\n\nCHAPTER XLVIII. RELATES HOW MR. PICKWICK, WITH THE ASSISTANCE OF SAMUEL WELLER, ESSAYED TO SOFTEN THE HEART OF MR. BENJAMIN ALLEN, AND TO MOLLIFY THE WRATH OF MR. ROBERT SAWYER\nMr. Ben Allen and Mr. Bob Sawyer sat together in the little surgery behind the shop, discussing minced veal and future prospects, when the discourse, not unnaturally, turned upon the practice acquired by Bob the aforesaid, and his present chances of deriving a competent independence from the honourable profession to which he had devoted himself.\n\n‘Which, I think,’ observed Mr. Bob Sawyer, pursuing the thread of the subject--‘which, I think, Ben, are rather dubious.’\n\n‘What’s rather dubious?’ inquired Mr. Ben Allen, at the same time sharpening his intellect with a draught of beer. ‘What’s dubious?’\n\n‘Why, the chances,’ responded Mr. Bob Sawyer.\n\n‘I forgot,’ said Mr. Ben Allen. ‘The beer has reminded me that I forgot, Bob--yes; they are dubious.’\n\n‘It’s wonderful how the poor people patronise me,’ said Mr. Bob Sawyer reflectively. ‘They knock me up, at all hours of the night; they take medicine to an extent which I should have conceived impossible; they put on blisters and leeches with a perseverance worthy of a better cause; they make additions to their families, in a manner which is quite awful. Six of those last-named little promissory notes, all due on the same day, Ben, and all intrusted to me!’\n\n‘It’s very gratifying, isn’t it?’ said Mr. Ben Allen, holding his plate for some more minced veal.\n\n‘Oh, very,’ replied Bob; ‘only not quite so much so as the confidence of patients with a shilling or two to spare would be. This business was capitally described in the advertisement, Ben. It is a practice, a very extensive practice--and that’s all.’\n\n‘Bob,’ said Mr. Ben Allen, laying down his knife and fork, and fixing his eyes on the visage of his friend, ‘Bob, I’ll tell you what it is.’\n\n‘What is it?’ inquired Mr. Bob Sawyer.\n\n‘You must make yourself, with as little delay as possible, master of Arabella’s one thousand pounds.’\n\n‘Three per cent. consolidated bank annuities, now standing in her name in the book or books of the governor and company of the Bank of England,’ added Bob Sawyer, in legal phraseology.\n\n‘Exactly so,’ said Ben. ‘She has it when she comes of age, or marries. She wants a year of coming of age, and if you plucked up a spirit she needn’t want a month of being married.’\n\n‘She’s a very charming and delightful creature,’ quoth Mr. Robert Sawyer, in reply; ‘and has only one fault that I know of, Ben. It happens, unfortunately, that that single blemish is a want of taste. She don’t like me.’\n\n‘It’s my opinion that she don’t know what she does like,’ said Mr. Ben Allen contemptuously.\n\n‘Perhaps not,’ remarked Mr. Bob Sawyer. ‘But it’s my opinion that she does know what she doesn’t like, and that’s of more importance.’\n\n‘I wish,’ said Mr. Ben Allen, setting his teeth together, and speaking more like a savage warrior who fed on raw wolf’s flesh which he carved with his fingers, than a peaceable young gentleman who ate minced veal with a knife and fork--‘I wish I knew whether any rascal really has been tampering with her, and attempting to engage her affections. I think I should assassinate him, Bob.’\n\n‘I’d put a bullet in him, if I found him out,’ said Mr. Sawyer, stopping in the course of a long draught of beer, and looking malignantly out of the porter pot. ‘If that didn’t do his business, I’d extract it afterwards, and kill him that way.’\n\nMr. Benjamin Allen gazed abstractedly on his friend for some minutes in silence, and then said--\n\n‘You have never proposed to her, point-blank, Bob?’\n\n‘No. Because I saw it would be of no use,’ replied Mr. Robert Sawyer.\n\n‘You shall do it, before you are twenty-four hours older,’ retorted Ben, with desperate calmness. ‘She shall have you, or I’ll know the reason why. I’ll exert my authority.’\n\n‘Well,’ said Mr. Bob Sawyer, ‘we shall see.’\n\n‘We shall see, my friend,’ replied Mr. Ben Allen fiercely. He paused for a few seconds, and added in a voice broken by emotion, ‘You have loved her from a child, my friend. You loved her when we were boys at school together, and, even then, she was wayward and slighted your young feelings. Do you recollect, with all the eagerness of a child’s love, one day pressing upon her acceptance, two small caraway-seed biscuits and one sweet apple, neatly folded into a circular parcel with the leaf of a copy-book?’\n\n‘I do,’ replied Bob Sawyer.\n\n‘She slighted that, I think?’ said Ben Allen.\n\n‘She did,’ rejoined Bob. ‘She said I had kept the parcel so long in the pockets of my corduroys, that the apple was unpleasantly warm.’\n\n‘I remember,’ said Mr. Allen gloomily. ‘Upon which we ate it ourselves, in alternate bites.’\n\nBob Sawyer intimated his recollection of the circumstance last alluded to, by a melancholy frown; and the two friends remained for some time absorbed, each in his own meditations.\n\nWhile these observations were being exchanged between Mr. Bob Sawyer and Mr. Benjamin Allen; and while the boy in the gray livery, marvelling at the unwonted prolongation of the dinner, cast an anxious look, from time to time, towards the glass door, distracted by inward misgivings regarding the amount of minced veal which would be ultimately reserved for his individual cravings; there rolled soberly on through the streets of Bristol, a private fly, painted of a sad green colour, drawn by a chubby sort of brown horse, and driven by a surly-looking man with his legs dressed like the legs of a groom, and his body attired in the coat of a coachman. Such appearances are common to many vehicles belonging to, and maintained by, old ladies of economic habits; and in this vehicle sat an old lady who was its mistress and proprietor.\n\n‘Martin!’ said the old lady, calling to the surly man, out of the front window.\n\n‘Well?’ said the surly man, touching his hat to the old lady.\n\n‘Mr. Sawyer’s,’ said the old lady.\n\n‘I was going there,’ said the surly man.\n\nThe old lady nodded the satisfaction which this proof of the surly man’s foresight imparted to her feelings; and the surly man giving a smart lash to the chubby horse, they all repaired to Mr. Bob Sawyer’s together.\n\n‘Martin!’ said the old lady, when the fly stopped at the door of Mr. Robert Sawyer, late Nockemorf.\n\n‘Well?’ said Martin.\n\n‘Ask the lad to step out, and mind the horse.’\n\n‘I’m going to mind the horse myself,’ said Martin, laying his whip on the roof of the fly.\n\n‘I can’t permit it, on any account,’ said the old lady; ‘your testimony will be very important, and I must take you into the house with me. You must not stir from my side during the whole interview. Do you hear?’\n\n‘I hear,’ replied Martin.\n\n‘Well; what are you stopping for?’\n\n‘Nothing,’ replied Martin. So saying, the surly man leisurely descended from the wheel, on which he had been poising himself on the tops of the toes of his right foot, and having summoned the boy in the gray livery, opened the coach door, flung down the steps, and thrusting in a hand enveloped in a dark wash-leather glove, pulled out the old lady with as much unconcern in his manner as if she were a bandbox.\n\n‘Dear me!’ exclaimed the old lady. ‘I am so flurried, now I have got here, Martin, that I’m all in a tremble.’\n\nMr. Martin coughed behind the dark wash-leather gloves, but expressed no sympathy; so the old lady, composing herself, trotted up Mr. Bob Sawyer’s steps, and Mr. Martin followed. Immediately on the old lady’s entering the shop, Mr. Benjamin Allen and Mr. Bob Sawyer, who had been putting the spirits-and-water out of sight, and upsetting nauseous drugs to take off the smell of the tobacco smoke, issued hastily forth in a transport of pleasure and affection.\n\n‘My dear aunt,’ exclaimed Mr. Ben Allen, ‘how kind of you to look in upon us! Mr. Sawyer, aunt; my friend Mr. Bob Sawyer whom I have spoken to you about, regarding--you know, aunt.’ And here Mr. Ben Allen, who was not at the moment extraordinarily sober, added the word ‘Arabella,’ in what was meant to be a whisper, but which was an especially audible and distinct tone of speech which nobody could avoid hearing, if anybody were so disposed.\n\n‘My dear Benjamin,’ said the old lady, struggling with a great shortness of breath, and trembling from head to foot, ‘don’t be alarmed, my dear, but I think I had better speak to Mr. Sawyer, alone, for a moment. Only for one moment.’\n\n‘Bob,’ said Mr. Allen, ‘will you take my aunt into the surgery?’\n\n‘Certainly,’ responded Bob, in a most professional voice. ‘Step this way, my dear ma’am. Don’t be frightened, ma’am. We shall be able to set you to rights in a very short time, I have no doubt, ma’am. Here, my dear ma’am. Now then!’ With this, Mr. Bob Sawyer having handed the old lady to a chair, shut the door, drew another chair close to her, and waited to hear detailed the symptoms of some disorder from which he saw in perspective a long train of profits and advantages.\n\nThe first thing the old lady did, was to shake her head a great many times, and began to cry.\n\n‘Nervous,’ said Bob Sawyer complacently. ‘Camphor-julep and water three times a day, and composing draught at night.’\n\n‘I don’t know how to begin, Mr. Sawyer,’ said the old lady. ‘It is so very painful and distressing.’\n\n‘You need not begin, ma’am,’ rejoined Mr. Bob Sawyer. ‘I can anticipate all you would say. The head is in fault.’\n\n‘I should be very sorry to think it was the heart,’ said the old lady, with a slight groan.\n\n‘Not the slightest danger of that, ma’am,’ replied Bob Sawyer. ‘The stomach is the primary cause.’\n\n‘Mr. Sawyer!’ exclaimed the old lady, starting.\n\n‘Not the least doubt of it, ma’am,’ rejoined Bob, looking wondrous wise. ‘Medicine, in time, my dear ma’am, would have prevented it all.’\n\n‘Mr. Sawyer,’ said the old lady, more flurried than before, ‘this conduct is either great impertinence to one in my situation, Sir, or it arises from your not understanding the object of my visit. If it had been in the power of medicine, or any foresight I could have used, to prevent what has occurred, I should certainly have done so. I had better see my nephew at once,’ said the old lady, twirling her reticule indignantly, and rising as she spoke.\n\n‘Stop a moment, ma’am,’ said Bob Sawyer; ‘I’m afraid I have not understood you. What is the matter, ma’am?’\n\n‘My niece, Mr. Sawyer,’ said the old lady: ‘your friend’s sister.’\n\n‘Yes, ma’am,’ said Bob, all impatience; for the old lady, although much agitated, spoke with the most tantalising deliberation, as old ladies often do. ‘Yes, ma’am.’\n\n‘Left my home, Mr. Sawyer, three days ago, on a pretended visit to my sister, another aunt of hers, who keeps the large boarding-school, just beyond the third mile-stone, where there is a very large laburnum-tree and an oak gate,’ said the old lady, stopping in this place to dry her eyes.\n\n‘Oh, devil take the laburnum-tree, ma’am!’ said Bob, quite forgetting his professional dignity in his anxiety. ‘Get on a little faster; put a little more steam on, ma’am, pray.’\n\n‘This morning,’ said the old lady slowly--‘this morning, she--’\n\n‘She came back, ma’am, I suppose,’ said Bob, with great animation. ‘Did she come back?’\n\n‘No, she did not; she wrote,’ replied the old lady.\n\n‘What did she say?’ inquired Bob eagerly.\n\n‘She said, Mr. Sawyer,’ replied the old lady--‘and it is this I want to prepare Benjamin’s mind for, gently and by degrees; she said that she was--I have got the letter in my pocket, Mr. Sawyer, but my glasses are in the carriage, and I should only waste your time if I attempted to point out the passage to you, without them; she said, in short, Mr. Sawyer, that she was married.’\n\nWhat!’ said, or rather shouted, Mr. Bob Sawyer.\n\n‘Married,’ repeated the old lady.\n\nMr. Bob Sawyer stopped to hear no more; but darting from the surgery into the outer shop, cried in a stentorian voice, ‘Ben, my boy, she’s bolted!’\n\nMr. Ben Allen, who had been slumbering behind the counter, with his head half a foot or so below his knees, no sooner heard this appalling communication, than he made a precipitate rush at Mr. Martin, and, twisting his hand in the neck-cloth of that taciturn servitor, expressed an obliging intention of choking him where he stood. This intention, with a promptitude often the effect of desperation, he at once commenced carrying into execution, with much vigour and surgical skill.\n\nMr. Martin, who was a man of few words and possessed but little power of eloquence or persuasion, submitted to this operation with a very calm and agreeable expression of countenance, for some seconds; finding, however, that it threatened speedily to lead to a result which would place it beyond his power to claim any wages, board or otherwise, in all time to come, he muttered an inarticulate remonstrance and felled Mr. Benjamin Allen to the ground. As that gentleman had his hands entangled in his cravat, he had no alternative but to follow him to the floor. There they both lay struggling, when the shop door opened, and the party was increased by the arrival of two most unexpected visitors, to wit, Mr. Pickwick and Mr. Samuel Weller.\n\nThe impression at once produced on Mr. Weller’s mind by what he saw, was, that Mr. Martin was hired by the establishment of Sawyer, late Nockemorf, to take strong medicine, or to go into fits and be experimentalised upon, or to swallow poison now and then with the view of testing the efficacy of some new antidotes, or to do something or other to promote the great science of medicine, and gratify the ardent spirit of inquiry burning in the bosoms of its two young professors. So, without presuming to interfere, Sam stood perfectly still, and looked on, as if he were mightily interested in the result of the then pending experiment. Not so, Mr. Pickwick. He at once threw himself on the astonished combatants, with his accustomed energy, and loudly called upon the bystanders to interpose.\n\nThis roused Mr. Bob Sawyer, who had been hitherto quite paralysed by the frenzy of his companion. With that gentleman’s assistance, Mr. Pickwick raised Ben Allen to his feet. Mr. Martin finding himself alone on the floor, got up, and looked about him.\n\n‘Mr. Allen,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘what is the matter, Sir?’\n\n‘Never mind, Sir!’ replied Mr. Allen, with haughty defiance.\n\n‘What is it?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick, looking at Bob Sawyer. ‘Is he unwell?’\n\nBefore Bob could reply, Mr. Ben Allen seized Mr. Pickwick by the hand, and murmured, in sorrowful accents, ‘My sister, my dear Sir; my sister.’\n\n‘Oh, is that all!’ said Mr. Pickwick. ‘We shall easily arrange that matter, I hope. Your sister is safe and well, and I am here, my dear Sir, to--’\n\n‘Sorry to do anythin’ as may cause an interruption to such wery pleasant proceedin’s, as the king said wen he dissolved the parliament,’ interposed Mr. Weller, who had been peeping through the glass door; ‘but there’s another experiment here, sir. Here’s a wenerable old lady a-- lyin’ on the carpet waitin’ for dissection, or galwinism, or some other rewivin’ and scientific inwention.’\n\n‘I forgot,’ exclaimed Mr. Ben Allen. ‘It is my aunt.’\n\n‘Dear me!’ said Mr. Pickwick. ‘Poor lady! Gently Sam, gently.’\n\n‘Strange sitivation for one o’ the family,’ observed Sam Weller, hoisting the aunt into a chair. ‘Now depitty sawbones, bring out the wollatilly!’\n\nThe latter observation was addressed to the boy in gray, who, having handed over the fly to the care of the street-keeper, had come back to see what all the noise was about. Between the boy in gray, and Mr. Bob Sawyer, and Mr. Benjamin Allen (who having frightened his aunt into a fainting fit, was affectionately solicitous for her recovery) the old lady was at length restored to consciousness; then Mr. Ben Allen, turning with a puzzled countenance to Mr. Pickwick, asked him what he was about to say, when he had been so alarmingly interrupted.\n\n‘We are all friends here, I presume?’ said Mr. Pickwick, clearing his voice, and looking towards the man of few words with the surly countenance, who drove the fly with the chubby horse.\n\nThis reminded Mr. Bob Sawyer that the boy in gray was looking on, with eyes wide open, and greedy ears. The incipient chemist having been lifted up by his coat collar, and dropped outside the door, Bob Sawyer assured Mr. Pickwick that he might speak without reserve.\n\n‘Your sister, my dear Sir,’ said Mr. Pickwick, turning to Benjamin Allen, ‘is in London; well and happy.’\n\n‘Her happiness is no object to me, sir,’ said Benjamin Allen, with a flourish of the hand.\n\n‘Her husband is an object to me, Sir,’ said Bob Sawyer. ‘He shall be an object to me, sir, at twelve paces, and a pretty object I’ll make of him, sir--a mean-spirited scoundrel!’ This, as it stood, was a very pretty denunciation, and magnanimous withal; but Mr. Bob Sawyer rather weakened its effect, by winding up with some general observations concerning the punching of heads and knocking out of eyes, which were commonplace by comparison.\n\n‘Stay, sir,’ said Mr. Pickwick; ‘before you apply those epithets to the gentleman in question, consider, dispassionately, the extent of his fault, and above all remember that he is a friend of mine.’\n\n‘What!’ said Mr. Bob Sawyer. ‘His name!’ cried Ben Allen. ‘His name!’\n\n‘Mr. Nathaniel Winkle,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\nMr. Benjamin Allen deliberately crushed his spectacles beneath the heel of his boot, and having picked up the pieces, and put them into three separate pockets, folded his arms, bit his lips, and looked in a threatening manner at the bland features of Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Then it’s you, is it, Sir, who have encouraged and brought about this match?’ inquired Mr. Benjamin Allen at length.\n\n‘And it’s this gentleman’s servant, I suppose,’ interrupted the old lady, ‘who has been skulking about my house, and endeavouring to entrap my servants to conspire against their mistress.--Martin!’\n\n‘Well?’ said the surly man, coming forward.\n\n‘Is that the young man you saw in the lane, whom you told me about, this morning?’\n\nMr. Martin, who, as it has already appeared, was a man of few words, looked at Sam Weller, nodded his head, and growled forth, ‘That’s the man.’ Mr. Weller, who was never proud, gave a smile of friendly recognition as his eyes encountered those of the surly groom, and admitted in courteous terms, that he had ‘knowed him afore.’\n\n‘And this is the faithful creature,’ exclaimed Mr. Ben Allen, ‘whom I had nearly suffocated!--Mr. Pickwick, how dare you allow your fellow to be employed in the abduction of my sister? I demand that you explain this matter, sir.’\n\n‘Explain it, sir!’ cried Bob Sawyer fiercely.\n\n‘It’s a conspiracy,’ said Ben Allen.\n\n‘A regular plant,’ added Mr. Bob Sawyer.\n\n‘A disgraceful imposition,’ observed the old lady.\n\n‘Nothing but a do,’ remarked Martin.\n\n‘Pray hear me,’ urged Mr. Pickwick, as Mr. Ben Allen fell into a chair that patients were bled in, and gave way to his pocket-handkerchief. ‘I have rendered no assistance in this matter, beyond being present at one interview between the young people which I could not prevent, and from which I conceived my presence would remove any slight colouring of impropriety that it might otherwise have had; this is the whole share I have had in the transaction, and I had no suspicion that an immediate marriage was even contemplated. Though, mind,’ added Mr. Pickwick, hastily checking himself--‘mind, I do not say I should have prevented it, if I had known that it was intended.’\n\n‘You hear that, all of you; you hear that?’ said Mr. Benjamin Allen.\n\n‘I hope they do,’ mildly observed Mr. Pickwick, looking round, ‘and,’ added that gentleman, his colour mounting as he spoke, ‘I hope they hear this, Sir, also. That from what has been stated to me, sir, I assert that you were by no means justified in attempting to force your sister’s inclinations as you did, and that you should rather have endeavoured by your kindness and forbearance to have supplied the place of other nearer relations whom she had never known, from a child. As regards my young friend, I must beg to add, that in every point of worldly advantage he is, at least, on an equal footing with yourself, if not on a much better one, and that unless I hear this question discussed with becoming temper and moderation, I decline hearing any more said upon the subject.’\n\n‘I wish to make a wery few remarks in addition to wot has been put for’ard by the honourable gen’l’m’n as has jist give over,’ said Mr. Weller, stepping forth, ‘wich is this here: a indiwidual in company has called me a feller.’\n\n‘That has nothing whatever to do with the matter, Sam,’ interposed Mr. Pickwick. ‘Pray hold your tongue.’\n\n‘I ain’t a-goin’ to say nothin’ on that ‘ere pint, sir,’ replied Sam, ‘but merely this here. P’raps that gen’l’m’n may think as there wos a priory ‘tachment; but there worn’t nothin’ o’ the sort, for the young lady said in the wery beginnin’ o’ the keepin’ company, that she couldn’t abide him. Nobody’s cut him out, and it ‘ud ha’ been jist the wery same for him if the young lady had never seen Mr. Vinkle. That’s what I wished to say, sir, and I hope I’ve now made that ‘ere gen’l’m’n’s mind easy.\n\nA short pause followed these consolatory remarks of Mr. Weller. Then Mr. Ben Allen rising from his chair, protested that he would never see Arabella’s face again; while Mr. Bob Sawyer, despite Sam’s flattering assurance, vowed dreadful vengeance on the happy bridegroom.\n\nBut, just when matters were at their height, and threatening to remain so, Mr. Pickwick found a powerful assistant in the old lady, who, evidently much struck by the mode in which he had advocated her niece’s cause, ventured to approach Mr. Benjamin Allen with a few comforting reflections, of which the chief were, that after all, perhaps, it was well it was no worse; the least said the soonest mended, and upon her word she did not know that it was so very bad after all; what was over couldn’t be begun, and what couldn’t be cured must be endured; with various other assurances of the like novel and strengthening description. To all of these, Mr. Benjamin Allen replied that he meant no disrespect to his aunt, or anybody there, but if it were all the same to them, and they would allow him to have his own way, he would rather have the pleasure of hating his sister till death, and after it.\n\nAt length, when this determination had been announced half a hundred times, the old lady suddenly bridling up and looking very majestic, wished to know what she had done that no respect was to be paid to her years or station, and that she should be obliged to beg and pray, in that way, of her own nephew, whom she remembered about five-and-twenty years before he was born, and whom she had known, personally, when he hadn’t a tooth in his head; to say nothing of her presence on the first occasion of his having his hair cut, and assistance at numerous other times and ceremonies during his babyhood, of sufficient importance to found a claim upon his affection, obedience, and sympathies, for ever.\n\nWhile the good lady was bestowing this objurgation on Mr. Ben Allen, Bob Sawyer and Mr. Pickwick had retired in close conversation to the inner room, where Mr. Sawyer was observed to apply himself several times to the mouth of a black bottle, under the influence of which, his features gradually assumed a cheerful and even jovial expression. And at last he emerged from the room, bottle in hand, and, remarking that he was very sorry to say he had been making a fool of himself, begged to propose the health and happiness of Mr. and Mrs. Winkle, whose felicity, so far from envying, he would be the first to congratulate them upon. Hearing this, Mr. Ben Allen suddenly arose from his chair, and, seizing the black bottle, drank the toast so heartily, that, the liquor being strong, he became nearly as black in the face as the bottle. Finally, the black bottle went round till it was empty, and there was so much shaking of hands and interchanging of compliments, that even the metal-visaged Mr. Martin condescended to smile.\n\n‘And now,’ said Bob Sawyer, rubbing his hands, ‘we’ll have a jolly night.’\n\n‘I am sorry,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘that I must return to my inn. I have not been accustomed to fatigue lately, and my journey has tired me exceedingly.’\n\n‘You’ll take some tea, Mr. Pickwick?’ said the old lady, with irresistible sweetness.\n\n‘Thank you, I would rather not,’ replied that gentleman. The truth is, that the old lady’s evidently increasing admiration was Mr. Pickwick’s principal inducement for going away. He thought of Mrs. Bardell; and every glance of the old lady’s eyes threw him into a cold perspiration.\n\nAs Mr. Pickwick could by no means be prevailed upon to stay, it was arranged at once, on his own proposition, that Mr. Benjamin Allen should accompany him on his journey to the elder Mr. Winkle’s, and that the coach should be at the door, at nine o’clock next morning. He then took his leave, and, followed by Samuel Weller, repaired to the Bush. It is worthy of remark, that Mr. Martin’s face was horribly convulsed as he shook hands with Sam at parting, and that he gave vent to a smile and an oath simultaneously; from which tokens it has been inferred by those who were best acquainted with that gentleman’s peculiarities, that he expressed himself much pleased with Mr. Weller’s society, and requested the honour of his further acquaintance.\n\n‘Shall I order a private room, Sir?’ inquired Sam, when they reached the Bush.\n\n‘Why, no, Sam,’ replied Mr. Pickwick; ‘as I dined in the coffee-room, and shall go to bed soon, it is hardly worth while. See who there is in the travellers’ room, Sam.’\n\nMr. Weller departed on his errand, and presently returned to say that there was only a gentleman with one eye; and that he and the landlord were drinking a bowl of bishop together.\n\n‘I will join them,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘He’s a queer customer, the vun-eyed vun, sir,’ observed Mr. Weller, as he led the way. ‘He’s a-gammonin’ that ‘ere landlord, he is, sir, till he don’t rightly know wether he’s a-standing on the soles of his boots or the crown of his hat.’\n\nThe individual to whom this observation referred, was sitting at the upper end of the room when Mr. Pickwick entered, and was smoking a large Dutch pipe, with his eye intently fixed on the round face of the landlord; a jolly-looking old personage, to whom he had recently been relating some tale of wonder, as was testified by sundry disjointed exclamations of, ‘Well, I wouldn’t have believed it! The strangest thing I ever heard! Couldn’t have supposed it possible!’ and other expressions of astonishment which burst spontaneously from his lips, as he returned the fixed gaze of the one-eyed man.\n\n‘Servant, sir,’ said the one-eyed man to Mr. Pickwick. ‘Fine night, sir.’\n\n‘Very much so indeed,’ replied Mr. Pickwick, as the waiter placed a small decanter of brandy, and some hot water before him.\n\nWhile Mr. Pickwick was mixing his brandy-and-water, the one-eyed man looked round at him earnestly, from time to time, and at length said--\n\n‘I think I’ve seen you before.’\n\n‘I don’t recollect you,’ rejoined Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘I dare say not,’ said the one-eyed man. ‘You didn’t know me, but I knew two friends of yours that were stopping at the Peacock at Eatanswill, at the time of the election.’\n\n‘Oh, indeed!’ exclaimed Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Yes,’ rejoined the one-eyed man. ‘I mentioned a little circumstance to them about a friend of mine of the name of Tom Smart. Perhaps you’ve heard them speak of it.’\n\n‘Often,’ rejoined Mr. Pickwick, smiling. ‘He was your uncle, I think?’\n\n‘No, no; only a friend of my uncle’s,’ replied the one-eyed man.\n\n‘He was a wonderful man, that uncle of yours, though,’ remarked the landlord shaking his head.\n\n‘Well, I think he was; I think I may say he was,’ answered the one-eyed man. ‘I could tell you a story about that same uncle, gentlemen, that would rather surprise you.’\n\n‘Could you?’ said Mr. Pickwick. ‘Let us hear it, by all means.’\n\nThe one-eyed bagman ladled out a glass of negus from the bowl, and drank it; smoked a long whiff out of the Dutch pipe; and then, calling to Sam Weller who was lingering near the door, that he needn’t go away unless he wanted to, because the story was no secret, fixed his eye upon the landlord’s, and proceeded, in the words of the next chapter.\n\n# CHAPTER XLIX. CONTAINING THE STORY OF THE BAGMAN’S UNCLE\n\nMy uncle, gentlemen,’ said the bagman, ‘was one of the merriest, pleasantest, cleverest fellows, that ever lived. I wish you had known him, gentlemen. On second thoughts, gentlemen, I don’t wish you had known him, for if you had, you would have been all, by this time, in the ordinary course of nature, if not dead, at all events so near it, as to have taken to stopping at home and giving up company, which would have deprived me of the inestimable pleasure of addressing you at this moment. Gentlemen, I wish your fathers and mothers had known my uncle. They would have been amazingly fond of him, especially your respectable mothers; I know they would. If any two of his numerous virtues predominated over the many that adorned his character, I should say they were his mixed punch and his after-supper song. Excuse my dwelling on these melancholy recollections of departed worth; you won’t see a man like my uncle every day in the week.\n\n‘I have always considered it a great point in my uncle’s character, gentlemen, that he was the intimate friend and companion of Tom Smart, of the great house of Bilson and Slum, Cateaton Street, City. My uncle collected for Tiggin and Welps, but for a long time he went pretty near the same journey as Tom; and the very first night they met, my uncle took a fancy for Tom, and Tom took a fancy for my uncle. They made a bet of a new hat before they had known each other half an hour, who should brew the best quart of punch and drink it the quickest. My uncle was judged to have won the making, but Tom Smart beat him in the drinking by about half a salt-spoonful. They took another quart apiece to drink each other’s health in, and were staunch friends ever afterwards. There’s a destiny in these things, gentlemen; we can’t help it.\n\n‘In personal appearance, my uncle was a trifle shorter than the middle size; he was a thought stouter too, than the ordinary run of people, and perhaps his face might be a shade redder. He had the jolliest face you ever saw, gentleman: something like Punch, with a handsome nose and chin; his eyes were always twinkling and sparkling with good-humour; and a smile--not one of your unmeaning wooden grins, but a real, merry, hearty, good-tempered smile--was perpetually on his countenance. He was pitched out of his gig once, and knocked, head first, against a milestone. There he lay, stunned, and so cut about the face with some gravel which had been heaped up alongside it, that, to use my uncle’s own strong expression, if his mother could have revisited the earth, she wouldn’t have known him. Indeed, when I come to think of the matter, gentlemen, I feel pretty sure she wouldn’t, for she died when my uncle was two years and seven months old, and I think it’s very likely that, even without the gravel, his top-boots would have puzzled the good lady not a little; to say nothing of his jolly red face. However, there he lay, and I have heard my uncle say, many a time, that the man said who picked him up that he was smiling as merrily as if he had tumbled out for a treat, and that after they had bled him, the first faint glimmerings of returning animation, were his jumping up in bed, bursting out into a loud laugh, kissing the young woman who held the basin, and demanding a mutton chop and a pickled walnut. He was very fond of pickled walnuts, gentlemen. He said he always found that, taken without vinegar, they relished the beer.\n\n‘My uncle’s great journey was in the fall of the leaf, at which time he collected debts, and took orders, in the north; going from London to Edinburgh, from Edinburgh to Glasgow, from Glasgow back to Edinburgh, and thence to London by the smack. You are to understand that his second visit to Edinburgh was for his own pleasure. He used to go back for a week, just to look up his old friends; and what with breakfasting with this one, lunching with that, dining with the third, and supping with another, a pretty tight week he used to make of it. I don’t know whether any of you, gentlemen, ever partook of a real substantial hospitable Scotch breakfast, and then went out to a slight lunch of a bushel of oysters, a dozen or so of bottled ale, and a noggin or two of whiskey to close up with. If you ever did, you will agree with me that it requires a pretty strong head to go out to dinner and supper afterwards.\n\n‘But bless your hearts and eyebrows, all this sort of thing was nothing to my uncle! He was so well seasoned, that it was mere child’s play. I have heard him say that he could see the Dundee people out, any day, and walk home afterwards without staggering; and yet the Dundee people have as strong heads and as strong punch, gentlemen, as you are likely to meet with, between the poles. I have heard of a Glasgow man and a Dundee man drinking against each other for fifteen hours at a sitting. They were both suffocated, as nearly as could be ascertained, at the same moment, but with this trifling exception, gentlemen, they were not a bit the worse for it.\n\n‘One night, within four-and-twenty hours of the time when he had settled to take shipping for London, my uncle supped at the house of a very old friend of his, a Bailie Mac something and four syllables after it, who lived in the old town of Edinburgh. There were the bailie’s wife, and the bailie’s three daughters, and the bailie’s grown-up son, and three or four stout, bushy eye-browed, canny, old Scotch fellows, that the bailie had got together to do honour to my uncle, and help to make merry. It was a glorious supper. There was kippered salmon, and Finnan haddocks, and a lamb’s head, and a haggis--a celebrated Scotch dish, gentlemen, which my uncle used to say always looked to him, when it came to table, very much like a Cupid’s stomach--and a great many other things besides, that I forget the names of, but very good things, notwithstanding. The lassies were pretty and agreeable; the bailie’s wife was one of the best creatures that ever lived; and my uncle was in thoroughly good cue. The consequence of which was, that the young ladies tittered and giggled, and the old lady laughed out loud, and the bailie and the other old fellows roared till they were red in the face, the whole mortal time. I don’t quite recollect how many tumblers of whiskey- toddy each man drank after supper; but this I know, that about one o’clock in the morning, the bailie’s grown-up son became insensible while attempting the first verse of “Willie brewed a peck o’ maut”; and he having been, for half an hour before, the only other man visible above the mahogany, it occurred to my uncle that it was almost time to think about going, especially as drinking had set in at seven o’clock, in order that he might get home at a decent hour. But, thinking it might not be quite polite to go just then, my uncle voted himself into the chair, mixed another glass, rose to propose his own health, addressed himself in a neat and complimentary speech, and drank the toast with great enthusiasm. Still nobody woke; so my uncle took a little drop more--neat this time, to prevent the toddy from disagreeing with him-- and, laying violent hands on his hat, sallied forth into the street.\n\n‘It was a wild, gusty night when my uncle closed the bailie’s door, and settling his hat firmly on his head to prevent the wind from taking it, thrust his hands into his pockets, and looking upward, took a short survey of the state of the weather. The clouds were drifting over the moon at their giddiest speed; at one time wholly obscuring her; at another, suffering her to burst forth in full splendour and shed her light on all the objects around; anon, driving over her again, with increased velocity, and shrouding everything in darkness. “Really, this won’t do,” said my uncle, addressing himself to the weather, as if he felt himself personally offended. “This is not at all the kind of thing for my voyage. It will not do at any price,” said my uncle, very impressively. Having repeated this, several times, he recovered his balance with some difficulty--for he was rather giddy with looking up into the sky so long--and walked merrily on.\n\n‘The bailie’s house was in the Canongate, and my uncle was going to the other end of Leith Walk, rather better than a mile’s journey. On either side of him, there shot up against the dark sky, tall, gaunt, straggling houses, with time-stained fronts, and windows that seemed to have shared the lot of eyes in mortals, and to have grown dim and sunken with age. Six, seven, eight storey high, were the houses; storey piled upon storey, as children build with cards--throwing their dark shadows over the roughly paved road, and making the dark night darker. A few oil lamps were scattered at long distances, but they only served to mark the dirty entrance to some narrow close, or to show where a common stair communicated, by steep and intricate windings, with the various flats above. Glancing at all these things with the air of a man who had seen them too often before, to think them worthy of much notice now, my uncle walked up the middle of the street, with a thumb in each waistcoat pocket, indulging from time to time in various snatches of song, chanted forth with such good-will and spirit, that the quiet honest folk started from their first sleep and lay trembling in bed till the sound died away in the distance; when, satisfying themselves that it was only some drunken ne’er-do-weel finding his way home, they covered themselves up warm and fell asleep again.\n\n‘I am particular in describing how my uncle walked up the middle of the street, with his thumbs in his waistcoat pockets, gentlemen, because, as he often used to say (and with great reason too) there is nothing at all extraordinary in this story, unless you distinctly understand at the beginning, that he was not by any means of a marvellous or romantic turn.\n\n‘Gentlemen, my uncle walked on with his thumbs in his waistcoat pockets, taking the middle of the street to himself, and singing, now a verse of a love song, and then a verse of a drinking one, and when he was tired of both, whistling melodiously, until he reached the North Bridge, which, at this point, connects the old and new towns of Edinburgh. Here he stopped for a minute, to look at the strange, irregular clusters of lights piled one above the other, and twinkling afar off so high, that they looked like stars, gleaming from the castle walls on the one side and the Calton Hill on the other, as if they illuminated veritable castles in the air; while the old picturesque town slept heavily on, in gloom and darkness below: its palace and chapel of Holyrood, guarded day and night, as a friend of my uncle’s used to say, by old Arthur’s Seat, towering, surly and dark, like some gruff genius, over the ancient city he has watched so long. I say, gentlemen, my uncle stopped here, for a minute, to look about him; and then, paying a compliment to the weather, which had a little cleared up, though the moon was sinking, walked on again, as royally as before; keeping the middle of the road with great dignity, and looking as if he would very much like to meet with somebody who would dispute possession of it with him. There was nobody at all disposed to contest the point, as it happened; and so, on he went, with his thumbs in his waistcoat pockets, like a lamb.\n\n‘When my uncle reached the end of Leith Walk, he had to cross a pretty large piece of waste ground which separated him from a short street which he had to turn down to go direct to his lodging. Now, in this piece of waste ground, there was, at that time, an enclosure belonging to some wheelwright who contracted with the Post Office for the purchase of old, worn-out mail coaches; and my uncle, being very fond of coaches, old, young, or middle-aged, all at once took it into his head to step out of his road for no other purpose than to peep between the palings at these mails--about a dozen of which he remembered to have seen, crowded together in a very forlorn and dismantled state, inside. My uncle was a very enthusiastic, emphatic sort of person, gentlemen; so, finding that he could not obtain a good peep between the palings he got over them, and sitting himself quietly down on an old axle-tree, began to contemplate the mail coaches with a deal of gravity.\n\n‘There might be a dozen of them, or there might be more--my uncle was never quite certain on this point, and being a man of very scrupulous veracity about numbers, didn’t like to say--but there they stood, all huddled together in the most desolate condition imaginable. The doors had been torn from their hinges and removed; the linings had been stripped off, only a shred hanging here and there by a rusty nail; the lamps were gone, the poles had long since vanished, the ironwork was rusty, the paint was worn away; the wind whistled through the chinks in the bare woodwork; and the rain, which had collected on the roofs, fell, drop by drop, into the insides with a hollow and melancholy sound. They were the decaying skeletons of departed mails, and in that lonely place, at that time of night, they looked chill and dismal.\n\n‘My uncle rested his head upon his hands, and thought of the busy, bustling people who had rattled about, years before, in the old coaches, and were now as silent and changed; he thought of the numbers of people to whom one of these crazy, mouldering vehicles had borne, night after night, for many years, and through all weathers, the anxiously expected intelligence, the eagerly looked-for remittance, the promised assurance of health and safety, the sudden announcement of sickness and death. The merchant, the lover, the wife, the widow, the mother, the school-boy, the very child who tottered to the door at the postman’s knock--how had they all looked forward to the arrival of the old coach. And where were they all now?\n\n‘Gentlemen, my uncle used to say that he thought all this at the time, but I rather suspect he learned it out of some book afterwards, for he distinctly stated that he fell into a kind of doze, as he sat on the old axle-tree looking at the decayed mail coaches, and that he was suddenly awakened by some deep church bell striking two. Now, my uncle was never a fast thinker, and if he had thought all these things, I am quite certain it would have taken him till full half-past two o’clock at the very least. I am, therefore, decidedly of opinion, gentlemen, that my uncle fell into a kind of doze, without having thought about anything at all.\n\n‘Be this as it may, a church bell struck two. My uncle woke, rubbed his eyes, and jumped up in astonishment.\n\n‘In one instant, after the clock struck two, the whole of this deserted and quiet spot had become a scene of most extraordinary life and animation. The mail coach doors were on their hinges, the lining was replaced, the ironwork was as good as new, the paint was restored, the lamps were alight; cushions and greatcoats were on every coach-box, porters were thrusting parcels into every boot, guards were stowing away letter-bags, hostlers were dashing pails of water against the renovated wheels; numbers of men were pushing about, fixing poles into every coach; passengers arrived, portmanteaus were handed up, horses were put to; in short, it was perfectly clear that every mail there, was to be off directly. Gentlemen, my uncle opened his eyes so wide at all this, that, to the very last moment of his life, he used to wonder how it fell out that he had ever been able to shut ‘em again.\n\n‘“Now then!” said a voice, as my uncle felt a hand on his shoulder, “you’re booked for one inside. You’d better get in.”\n\n‘“I booked!” said my uncle, turning round.\n\n‘“Yes, certainly.”\n\n‘My uncle, gentlemen, could say nothing, he was so very much astonished. The queerest thing of all was that although there was such a crowd of persons, and although fresh faces were pouring in, every moment, there was no telling where they came from. They seemed to start up, in some strange manner, from the ground, or the air, and disappear in the same way. When a porter had put his luggage in the coach, and received his fare, he turned round and was gone; and before my uncle had well begun to wonder what had become of him, half a dozen fresh ones started up, and staggered along under the weight of parcels, which seemed big enough to crush them. The passengers were all dressed so oddly too! Large, broad-skirted laced coats, with great cuffs and no collars; and wigs, gentlemen--great formal wigs with a tie behind. My uncle could make nothing of it.\n\n‘“Now, are you going to get in?” said the person who had addressed my uncle before. He was dressed as a mail guard, with a wig on his head and most enormous cuffs to his coat, and had a lantern in one hand, and a huge blunderbuss in the other, which he was going to stow away in his little arm-chest. “are you going to get in, Jack Martin?” said the guard, holding the lantern to my uncle’s face.\n\n‘“Hollo!” said my uncle, falling back a step or two. “That’s familiar!”\n\n‘“It’s so on the way-bill,” said the guard.\n\n‘“Isn’t there a ‘Mister’ before it?” said my uncle. For he felt, gentlemen, that for a guard he didn’t know, to call him Jack Martin, was a liberty which the Post Office wouldn’t have sanctioned if they had known it.\n\n‘“No, there is not,” rejoined the guard coolly.\n\n‘“Is the fare paid?” inquired my uncle.\n\n‘“Of course it is,” rejoined the guard.\n\n‘“It is, is it?” said my uncle. “Then here goes! Which coach?”\n\n‘“This,” said the guard, pointing to an old-fashioned Edinburgh and London mail, which had the steps down and the door open. “Stop! Here are the other passengers. Let them get in first.”\n\n‘As the guard spoke, there all at once appeared, right in front of my uncle, a young gentleman in a powdered wig, and a sky-blue coat trimmed with silver, made very full and broad in the skirts, which were lined with buckram. Tiggin and Welps were in the printed calico and waistcoat piece line, gentlemen, so my uncle knew all the materials at once. He wore knee breeches, and a kind of leggings rolled up over his silk stockings, and shoes with buckles; he had ruffles at his wrists, a three-cornered hat on his head, and a long taper sword by his side. The flaps of his waist-coat came half-way down his thighs, and the ends of his cravat reached to his waist. He stalked gravely to the coach door, pulled off his hat, and held it above his head at arm’s length, cocking his little finger in the air at the same time, as some affected people do, when they take a cup of tea. Then he drew his feet together, and made a low, grave bow, and then put out his left hand. My uncle was just going to step forward, and shake it heartily, when he perceived that these attentions were directed, not towards him, but to a young lady who just then appeared at the foot of the steps, attired in an old-fashioned green velvet dress with a long waist and stomacher. She had no bonnet on her head, gentlemen, which was muffled in a black silk hood, but she looked round for an instant as she prepared to get into the coach, and such a beautiful face as she disclosed, my uncle had never seen--not even in a picture. She got into the coach, holding up her dress with one hand; and as my uncle always said with a round oath, when he told the story, he wouldn’t have believed it possible that legs and feet could have been brought to such a state of perfection unless he had seen them with his own eyes.\n\n‘But, in this one glimpse of the beautiful face, my uncle saw that the young lady cast an imploring look upon him, and that she appeared terrified and distressed. He noticed, too, that the young fellow in the powdered wig, notwithstanding his show of gallantry, which was all very fine and grand, clasped her tight by the wrist when she got in, and followed himself immediately afterwards. An uncommonly ill-looking fellow, in a close brown wig, and a plum-coloured suit, wearing a very large sword, and boots up to his hips, belonged to the party; and when he sat himself down next to the young lady, who shrank into a corner at his approach, my uncle was confirmed in his original impression that something dark and mysterious was going forward, or, as he always said himself, that “there was a screw loose somewhere.” It’s quite surprising how quickly he made up his mind to help the lady at any peril, if she needed any help.\n\n‘“Death and lightning!” exclaimed the young gentleman, laying his hand upon his sword as my uncle entered the coach.\n\n‘“Blood and thunder!” roared the other gentleman. With this, he whipped his sword out, and made a lunge at my uncle without further ceremony. My uncle had no weapon about him, but with great dexterity he snatched the ill-looking gentleman’s three-cornered hat from his head, and, receiving the point of his sword right through the crown, squeezed the sides together, and held it tight.\n\n‘“Pink him behind!” cried the ill-looking gentleman to his companion, as he struggled to regain his sword.\n\n‘“He had better not,” cried my uncle, displaying the heel of one of his shoes, in a threatening manner. “I’ll kick his brains out, if he has any--, or fracture his skull if he hasn’t.” Exerting all his strength, at this moment, my uncle wrenched the ill-looking man’s sword from his grasp, and flung it clean out of the coach window, upon which the younger gentleman vociferated, “Death and lightning!” again, and laid his hand upon the hilt of his sword, in a very fierce manner, but didn’t draw it. Perhaps, gentlemen, as my uncle used to say with a smile, perhaps he was afraid of alarming the lady.\n\n‘“Now, gentlemen,” said my uncle, taking his seat deliberately, “I don’t want to have any death, with or without lightning, in a lady’s presence, and we have had quite blood and thundering enough for one journey; so, if you please, we’ll sit in our places like quiet insides. Here, guard, pick up that gentleman’s carving-knife.”\n\n‘As quickly as my uncle said the words, the guard appeared at the coach window, with the gentleman’s sword in his hand. He held up his lantern, and looked earnestly in my uncle’s face, as he handed it in, when, by its light, my uncle saw, to his great surprise, that an immense crowd of mail-coach guards swarmed round the window, every one of whom had his eyes earnestly fixed upon him too. He had never seen such a sea of white faces, red bodies, and earnest eyes, in all his born days.\n\n‘“This is the strangest sort of thing I ever had anything to do with,” thought my uncle; “allow me to return you your hat, sir.”\n\n‘The ill-looking gentleman received his three-cornered hat in silence, looked at the hole in the middle with an inquiring air, and finally stuck it on the top of his wig with a solemnity the effect of which was a trifle impaired by his sneezing violently at the moment, and jerking it off again.\n\n‘“All right!” cried the guard with the lantern, mounting into his little seat behind. Away they went. My uncle peeped out of the coach window as they emerged from the yard, and observed that the other mails, with coachmen, guards, horses, and passengers, complete, were driving round and round in circles, at a slow trot of about five miles an hour. My uncle burned with indignation, gentlemen. As a commercial man, he felt that the mail-bags were not to be trifled with, and he resolved to memorialise the Post Office on the subject, the very instant he reached London.\n\n‘At present, however, his thoughts were occupied with the young lady who sat in the farthest corner of the coach, with her face muffled closely in her hood; the gentleman with the sky-blue coat sitting opposite to her; the other man in the plum-coloured suit, by her side; and both watching her intently. If she so much as rustled the folds of her hood, he could hear the ill-looking man clap his hand upon his sword, and could tell by the other’s breathing (it was so dark he couldn’t see his face) that he was looking as big as if he were going to devour her at a mouthful. This roused my uncle more and more, and he resolved, come what might, to see the end of it. He had a great admiration for bright eyes, and sweet faces, and pretty legs and feet; in short, he was fond of the whole sex. It runs in our family, gentleman--so am I.\n\n‘Many were the devices which my uncle practised, to attract the lady’s attention, or at all events, to engage the mysterious gentlemen in conversation. They were all in vain; the gentlemen wouldn’t talk, and the lady didn’t dare. He thrust his head out of the coach window at intervals, and bawled out to know why they didn’t go faster. But he called till he was hoarse; nobody paid the least attention to him. He leaned back in the coach, and thought of the beautiful face, and the feet and legs. This answered better; it whiled away the time, and kept him from wondering where he was going, and how it was that he found himself in such an odd situation. Not that this would have worried him much, anyway--he was a mighty free and easy, roving, devil-may-care sort of person, was my uncle, gentlemen.\n\n‘All of a sudden the coach stopped. “Hollo!” said my uncle, “what’s in the wind now?”\n\n‘“Alight here,” said the guard, letting down the steps.\n\n‘“Here!” cried my uncle.\n\n‘“Here,” rejoined the guard.\n\n‘“I’ll do nothing of the sort,” said my uncle.\n\n‘“Very well, then stop where you are,” said the guard.\n\n‘“I will,” said my uncle.\n\n‘“Do,” said the guard.\n\n‘The passengers had regarded this colloquy with great attention, and, finding that my uncle was determined not to alight, the younger man squeezed past him, to hand the lady out. At this moment, the ill-looking man was inspecting the hole in the crown of his three-cornered hat. As the young lady brushed past, she dropped one of her gloves into my uncle’s hand, and softly whispered, with her lips so close to his face that he felt her warm breath on his nose, the single word “Help!” Gentlemen, my uncle leaped out of the coach at once, with such violence that it rocked on the springs again.\n\n‘“Oh! you’ve thought better of it, have you?” said the guard, when he saw my uncle standing on the ground.\n\n‘My uncle looked at the guard for a few seconds, in some doubt whether it wouldn’t be better to wrench his blunderbuss from him, fire it in the face of the man with the big sword, knock the rest of the company over the head with the stock, snatch up the young lady, and go off in the smoke. On second thoughts, however, he abandoned this plan, as being a shade too melodramatic in the execution, and followed the two mysterious men, who, keeping the lady between them, were now entering an old house in front of which the coach had stopped. They turned into the passage, and my uncle followed.\n\n‘Of all the ruinous and desolate places my uncle had ever beheld, this was the most so. It looked as if it had once been a large house of entertainment; but the roof had fallen in, in many places, and the stairs were steep, rugged, and broken. There was a huge fireplace in the room into which they walked, and the chimney was blackened with smoke; but no warm blaze lighted it up now. The white feathery dust of burned wood was still strewed over the hearth, but the stove was cold, and all was dark and gloomy.\n\n‘“Well,” said my uncle, as he looked about him, “a mail travelling at the rate of six miles and a half an hour, and stopping for an indefinite time at such a hole as this, is rather an irregular sort of proceeding, I fancy. This shall be made known. I’ll write to the papers.”\n\n‘My uncle said this in a pretty loud voice, and in an open, unreserved sort of manner, with the view of engaging the two strangers in conversation if he could. But, neither of them took any more notice of him than whispering to each other, and scowling at him as they did so. The lady was at the farther end of the room, and once she ventured to wave her hand, as if beseeching my uncle’s assistance.\n\n‘At length the two strangers advanced a little, and the conversation began in earnest.\n\n‘“You don’t know this is a private room, I suppose, fellow?” said the gentleman in sky-blue.\n\n‘“No, I do not, fellow,” rejoined my uncle. “Only, if this is a private room specially ordered for the occasion, I should think the public room must be a very comfortable one;” with this, my uncle sat himself down in a high-backed chair, and took such an accurate measure of the gentleman, with his eyes, that Tiggin and Welps could have supplied him with printed calico for a suit, and not an inch too much or too little, from that estimate alone.\n\n‘“Quit this room,” said both men together, grasping their swords.\n\n‘“Eh?” said my uncle, not at all appearing to comprehend their meaning.\n\n‘“Quit the room, or you are a dead man,” said the ill-looking fellow with the large sword, drawing it at the same time and flourishing it in the air.\n\n‘“Down with him!” cried the gentleman in sky-blue, drawing his sword also, and falling back two or three yards. “Down with him!” The lady gave a loud scream.\n\n‘Now, my uncle was always remarkable for great boldness, and great presence of mind. All the time that he had appeared so indifferent to what was going on, he had been looking slily about for some missile or weapon of defence, and at the very instant when the swords were drawn, he espied, standing in the chimney-corner, an old basket-hilted rapier in a rusty scabbard. At one bound, my uncle caught it in his hand, drew it, flourished it gallantly above his head, called aloud to the lady to keep out of the way, hurled the chair at the man in sky-blue, and the scabbard at the man in plum-colour, and taking advantage of the confusion, fell upon them both, pell-mell.\n\n‘Gentlemen, there is an old story--none the worse for being true-- regarding a fine young Irish gentleman, who being asked if he could play the fiddle, replied he had no doubt he could, but he couldn’t exactly say, for certain, because he had never tried. This is not inapplicable to my uncle and his fencing. He had never had a sword in his hand before, except once when he played Richard the Third at a private theatre, upon which occasion it was arranged with Richmond that he was to be run through, from behind, without showing fight at all. But here he was, cutting and slashing with two experienced swordsman, thrusting, and guarding, and poking, and slicing, and acquitting himself in the most manful and dexterous manner possible, although up to that time he had never been aware that he had the least notion of the science. It only shows how true the old saying is, that a man never knows what he can do till he tries, gentlemen.\n\n‘The noise of the combat was terrific; each of the three combatants swearing like troopers, and their swords clashing with as much noise as if all the knives and steels in Newport market were rattling together, at the same time. When it was at its very height, the lady (to encourage my uncle most probably) withdrew her hood entirely from her face, and disclosed a countenance of such dazzling beauty, that he would have fought against fifty men, to win one smile from it and die. He had done wonders before, but now he began to powder away like a raving mad giant.\n\n‘At this very moment, the gentleman in sky-blue turning round, and seeing the young lady with her face uncovered, vented an exclamation of rage and jealousy, and, turning his weapon against her beautiful bosom, pointed a thrust at her heart, which caused my uncle to utter a cry of apprehension that made the building ring. The lady stepped lightly aside, and snatching the young man’s sword from his hand, before he had recovered his balance, drove him to the wall, and running it through him, and the panelling, up to the very hilt, pinned him there, hard and fast. It was a splendid example. My uncle, with a loud shout of triumph, and a strength that was irresistible, made his adversary retreat in the same direction, and plunging the old rapier into the very centre of a large red flower in the pattern of his waistcoat, nailed him beside his friend; there they both stood, gentlemen, jerking their arms and legs about in agony, like the toy-shop figures that are moved by a piece of pack-thread. My uncle always said, afterwards, that this was one of the surest means he knew of, for disposing of an enemy; but it was liable to one objection on the ground of expense, inasmuch as it involved the loss of a sword for every man disabled.\n\n‘“The mail, the mail!” cried the lady, running up to my uncle and throwing her beautiful arms round his neck; “we may yet escape.”\n\n‘“May!” cried my uncle; “why, my dear, there’s nobody else to kill, is there?” My uncle was rather disappointed, gentlemen, for he thought a little quiet bit of love-making would be agreeable after the slaughtering, if it were only to change the subject.\n\n‘“We have not an instant to lose here,” said the young lady. “He (pointing to the young gentleman in sky-blue) is the only son of the powerful Marquess of Filletoville.”\n\n‘“Well then, my dear, I’m afraid he’ll never come to the title,” said my uncle, looking coolly at the young gentleman as he stood fixed up against the wall, in the cockchafer fashion that I have described. “You have cut off the entail, my love.”\n\n‘“I have been torn from my home and my friends by these villains,” said the young lady, her features glowing with indignation. “That wretch would have married me by violence in another hour.”\n\n‘“Confound his impudence!” said my uncle, bestowing a very contemptuous look on the dying heir of Filletoville.\n\n‘“As you may guess from what you have seen,” said the young lady, “the party were prepared to murder me if I appealed to any one for assistance. If their accomplices find us here, we are lost. Two minutes hence may be too late. The mail!” With these words, overpowered by her feelings, and the exertion of sticking the young Marquess of Filletoville, she sank into my uncle’s arms. My uncle caught her up, and bore her to the house door. There stood the mail, with four long-tailed, flowing-maned, black horses, ready harnessed; but no coachman, no guard, no hostler even, at the horses’ heads.\n\n‘Gentlemen, I hope I do no injustice to my uncle’s memory, when I express my opinion, that although he was a bachelor, he had held some ladies in his arms before this time; I believe, indeed, that he had rather a habit of kissing barmaids; and I know, that in one or two instances, he had been seen by credible witnesses, to hug a landlady in a very perceptible manner. I mention the circumstance, to show what a very uncommon sort of person this beautiful young lady must have been, to have affected my uncle in the way she did; he used to say, that as her long dark hair trailed over his arm, and her beautiful dark eyes fixed themselves upon his face when she recovered, he felt so strange and nervous that his legs trembled beneath him. But who can look in a sweet, soft pair of dark eyes, without feeling queer? I can’t, gentlemen. I am afraid to look at some eyes I know, and that’s the truth of it.\n\n‘“You will never leave me,” murmured the young lady.\n\n‘“Never,” said my uncle. And he meant it too.\n\n‘“My dear preserver!” exclaimed the young lady. “My dear, kind, brave preserver!”\n\n‘“Don’t,” said my uncle, interrupting her.\n\n‘“‘Why?” inquired the young lady.\n\n‘“Because your mouth looks so beautiful when you speak,” rejoined my uncle, “that I’m afraid I shall be rude enough to kiss it.”\n\n‘The young lady put up her hand as if to caution my uncle not to do so, and said--No, she didn’t say anything--she smiled. When you are looking at a pair of the most delicious lips in the world, and see them gently break into a roguish smile--if you are very near them, and nobody else by--you cannot better testify your admiration of their beautiful form and colour than by kissing them at once. My uncle did so, and I honour him for it.\n\n‘“Hark!” cried the young lady, starting. “The noise of wheels, and horses!”\n\n‘“So it is,” said my uncle, listening. He had a good ear for wheels, and the trampling of hoofs; but there appeared to be so many horses and carriages rattling towards them, from a distance, that it was impossible to form a guess at their number. The sound was like that of fifty brakes, with six blood cattle in each.\n\n‘“We are pursued!” cried the young lady, clasping her hands. “We are pursued. I have no hope but in you!”\n\n‘There was such an expression of terror in her beautiful face, that my uncle made up his mind at once. He lifted her into the coach, told her not to be frightened, pressed his lips to hers once more, and then advising her to draw up the window to keep the cold air out, mounted to the box.\n\n‘“Stay, love,” cried the young lady.\n\n‘“What’s the matter?” said my uncle, from the coach-box.\n\n‘“I want to speak to you,” said the young lady; “only a word. Only one word, dearest.”\n\n‘“Must I get down?” inquired my uncle. The lady made no answer, but she smiled again. Such a smile, gentlemen! It beat the other one, all to nothing. My uncle descended from his perch in a twinkling.\n\n‘“What is it, my dear?” said my uncle, looking in at the coach window. The lady happened to bend forward at the same time, and my uncle thought she looked more beautiful than she had done yet. He was very close to her just then, gentlemen, so he really ought to know.\n\n‘“What is it, my dear?” said my uncle.\n\n‘“Will you never love any one but me--never marry any one beside?” said the young lady.\n\n‘My uncle swore a great oath that he never would marry anybody else, and the young lady drew in her head, and pulled up the window. He jumped upon the box, squared his elbows, adjusted the ribands, seized the whip which lay on the roof, gave one flick to the off leader, and away went the four long-tailed, flowing-maned black horses, at fifteen good English miles an hour, with the old mail-coach behind them. Whew! How they tore along!\n\n‘The noise behind grew louder. The faster the old mail went, the faster came the pursuers--men, horses, dogs, were leagued in the pursuit. The noise was frightful, but, above all, rose the voice of the young lady, urging my uncle on, and shrieking, “Faster! Faster!”\n\n‘They whirled past the dark trees, as feathers would be swept before a hurricane. Houses, gates, churches, haystacks, objects of every kind they shot by, with a velocity and noise like roaring waters suddenly let loose. But still the noise of pursuit grew louder, and still my uncle could hear the young lady wildly screaming, “Faster! Faster!”\n\n‘My uncle plied whip and rein, and the horses flew onward till they were white with foam; and yet the noise behind increased; and yet the young lady cried, “Faster! Faster!” My uncle gave a loud stamp on the boot in the energy of the moment, and--found that it was gray morning, and he was sitting in the wheelwright’s yard, on the box of an old Edinburgh mail, shivering with the cold and wet and stamping his feet to warm them! He got down, and looked eagerly inside for the beautiful young lady. Alas! There was neither door nor seat to the coach. It was a mere shell.\n\n‘Of course, my uncle knew very well that there was some mystery in the matter, and that everything had passed exactly as he used to relate it. He remained staunch to the great oath he had sworn to the beautiful young lady, refusing several eligible landladies on her account, and dying a bachelor at last. He always said what a curious thing it was that he should have found out, by such a mere accident as his clambering over the palings, that the ghosts of mail-coaches and horses, guards, coachmen, and passengers, were in the habit of making journeys regularly every night. He used to add, that he believed he was the only living person who had ever been taken as a passenger on one of these excursions. And I think he was right, gentlemen--at least I never heard of any other.’\n\n‘I wonder what these ghosts of mail-coaches carry in their bags,’ said the landlord, who had listened to the whole story with profound attention.\n\n‘The dead letters, of course,’ said the bagman.\n\n‘Oh, ah! To be sure,’ rejoined the landlord. ‘I never thought of that.’\n\nCHAPTER L. HOW MR. PICKWICK SPED UPON HIS MISSION, AND HOW HE WAS REINFORCED IN THE OUTSET BY A MOST UNEXPECTED AUXILIARY\nThe horses were put to, punctually at a quarter before nine next morning, and Mr. Pickwick and Sam Weller having each taken his seat, the one inside and the other out, the postillion was duly directed to repair in the first instance to Mr. Bob Sawyer’s house, for the purpose of taking up Mr. Benjamin Allen.\n\nIt was with feelings of no small astonishment, when the carriage drew up before the door with the red lamp, and the very legible inscription of ‘Sawyer, late Nockemorf,’ that Mr. Pickwick saw, on popping his head out of the coach window, the boy in the gray livery very busily employed in putting up the shutters--the which, being an unusual and an unbusinesslike proceeding at that hour of the morning, at once suggested to his mind two inferences: the one, that some good friend and patient of Mr. Bob Sawyer’s was dead; the other, that Mr. Bob Sawyer himself was bankrupt.\n\n‘What is the matter?’ said Mr. Pickwick to the boy.\n\n‘Nothing’s the matter, Sir,’ replied the boy, expanding his mouth to the whole breadth of his countenance.\n\n‘All right, all right!’ cried Bob Sawyer, suddenly appearing at the door, with a small leathern knapsack, limp and dirty, in one hand, and a rough coat and shawl thrown over the other arm. ‘I’m going, old fellow.’\n\n‘You!’ exclaimed Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Yes,’ replied Bob Sawyer, ‘and a regular expedition we’ll make of it. Here, Sam! Look out!’ Thus briefly bespeaking Mr. Weller’s attention, Mr. Bob Sawyer jerked the leathern knapsack into the dickey, where it was immediately stowed away, under the seat, by Sam, who regarded the proceeding with great admiration. This done, Mr. Bob Sawyer, with the assistance of the boy, forcibly worked himself into the rough coat, which was a few sizes too small for him, and then advancing to the coach window, thrust in his head, and laughed boisterously.\n\n‘What a start it is, isn’t it?’ cried Bob, wiping the tears out of his eyes, with one of the cuffs of the rough coat.\n\n‘My dear Sir,’ said Mr. Pickwick, with some embarrassment, ‘I had no idea of your accompanying us.’\n\n‘No, that’s just the very thing,’ replied Bob, seizing Mr. Pickwick by the lappel of his coat. ‘That’s the joke.’\n\n‘Oh, that’s the joke, is it?’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Of course,’ replied Bob. ‘It’s the whole point of the thing, you know-- that, and leaving the business to take care of itself, as it seems to have made up its mind not to take care of me.’ With this explanation of the phenomenon of the shutters, Mr. Bob Sawyer pointed to the shop, and relapsed into an ecstasy of mirth.\n\n‘Bless me, you are surely not mad enough to think of leaving your patients without anybody to attend them!’ remonstrated Mr. Pickwick in a very serious tone.\n\n‘Why not?’ asked Bob, in reply. ‘I shall save by it, you know. None of them ever pay. Besides,’ said Bob, lowering his voice to a confidential whisper, ‘they will be all the better for it; for, being nearly out of drugs, and not able to increase my account just now, I should have been obliged to give them calomel all round, and it would have been certain to have disagreed with some of them. So it’s all for the best.’\n\nThere was a philosophy and a strength of reasoning about this reply, which Mr. Pickwick was not prepared for. He paused a few moments, and added, less firmly than before--\n\n‘But this chaise, my young friend, will only hold two; and I am pledged to Mr. Allen.’\n\n‘Don’t think of me for a minute,’ replied Bob. ‘I’ve arranged it all; Sam and I will share the dickey between us. Look here. This little bill is to be wafered on the shop door: “Sawyer, late Nockemorf. Inquire of Mrs. Cripps over the way.” Mrs. Cripps is my boy’s mother. “Mr. Sawyer’s very sorry,” says Mrs. Cripps, “couldn’t help it--fetched away early this morning to a consultation of the very first surgeons in the country--couldn’t do without him--would have him at any price-- tremendous operation.” The fact is,’ said Bob, in conclusion, ‘it’ll do me more good than otherwise, I expect. If it gets into one of the local papers, it will be the making of me. Here’s Ben; now then, jump in!’\n\nWith these hurried words, Mr. Bob Sawyer pushed the postboy on one side, jerked his friend into the vehicle, slammed the door, put up the steps, wafered the bill on the street door, locked it, put the key in his pocket, jumped into the dickey, gave the word for starting, and did the whole with such extraordinary precipitation, that before Mr. Pickwick had well begun to consider whether Mr. Bob Sawyer ought to go or not, they were rolling away, with Mr. Bob Sawyer thoroughly established as part and parcel of the equipage.\n\nSo long as their progress was confined to the streets of Bristol, the facetious Bob kept his professional green spectacles on, and conducted himself with becoming steadiness and gravity of demeanour; merely giving utterance to divers verbal witticisms for the exclusive behoof and entertainment of Mr. Samuel Weller. But when they emerged on the open road, he threw off his green spectacles and his gravity together, and performed a great variety of practical jokes, which were calculated to attract the attention of the passersby, and to render the carriage and those it contained objects of more than ordinary curiosity; the least conspicuous among these feats being a most vociferous imitation of a key-bugle, and the ostentatious display of a crimson silk pocket- handkerchief attached to a walking-stick, which was occasionally waved in the air with various gestures indicative of supremacy and defiance.\n\n‘I wonder,’ said Mr. Pickwick, stopping in the midst of a most sedate conversation with Ben Allen, bearing reference to the numerous good qualities of Mr. Winkle and his sister--‘I wonder what all the people we pass, can see in us to make them stare so.’\n\n‘It’s a neat turn-out,’ replied Ben Allen, with something of pride in his tone. ‘They’re not used to see this sort of thing, every day, I dare say.’\n\n‘Possibly,’ replied Mr. Pickwick. ‘It may be so. Perhaps it is.’\n\nMr. Pickwick might very probably have reasoned himself into the belief that it really was, had he not, just then happening to look out of the coach window, observed that the looks of the passengers betokened anything but respectful astonishment, and that various telegraphic communications appeared to be passing between them and some persons outside the vehicle, whereupon it occurred to him that these demonstrations might be, in some remote degree, referable to the humorous deportment of Mr. Robert Sawyer.\n\n‘I hope,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘that our volatile friend is committing no absurdities in that dickey behind.’\n\n‘Oh dear, no,’ replied Ben Allen. ‘Except when he’s elevated, Bob’s the quietest creature breathing.’\n\nHere a prolonged imitation of a key-bugle broke upon the ear, succeeded by cheers and screams, all of which evidently proceeded from the throat and lungs of the quietest creature breathing, or in plainer designation, of Mr. Bob Sawyer himself.\n\nMr. Pickwick and Mr. Ben Allen looked expressively at each other, and the former gentleman taking off his hat, and leaning out of the coach window until nearly the whole of his waistcoat was outside it, was at length enabled to catch a glimpse of his facetious friend.\n\nMr. Bob Sawyer was seated, not in the dickey, but on the roof of the chaise, with his legs as far asunder as they would conveniently go, wearing Mr. Samuel Weller’s hat on one side of his head, and bearing, in one hand, a most enormous sandwich, while, in the other, he supported a goodly-sized case-bottle, to both of which he applied himself with intense relish, varying the monotony of the occupation by an occasional howl, or the interchange of some lively badinage with any passing stranger. The crimson flag was carefully tied in an erect position to the rail of the dickey; and Mr. Samuel Weller, decorated with Bob Sawyer’s hat, was seated in the centre thereof, discussing a twin sandwich, with an animated countenance, the expression of which betokened his entire and perfect approval of the whole arrangement.\n\nThis was enough to irritate a gentleman with Mr. Pickwick’s sense of propriety, but it was not the whole extent of the aggravation, for a stage-coach full, inside and out, was meeting them at the moment, and the astonishment of the passengers was very palpably evinced. The congratulations of an Irish family, too, who were keeping up with the chaise, and begging all the time, were of rather a boisterous description, especially those of its male head, who appeared to consider the display as part and parcel of some political or other procession of triumph.\n\n‘Mr. Sawyer!’ cried Mr. Pickwick, in a state of great excitement, ‘Mr. Sawyer, Sir!’\n\n‘Hollo!’ responded that gentleman, looking over the side of the chaise with all the coolness in life.\n\n‘Are you mad, sir?’ demanded Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Not a bit of it,’ replied Bob; ‘only cheerful.’\n\n‘Cheerful, sir!’ ejaculated Mr. Pickwick. ‘Take down that scandalous red handkerchief, I beg. I insist, Sir. Sam, take it down.’\n\nBefore Sam could interpose, Mr. Bob Sawyer gracefully struck his colours, and having put them in his pocket, nodded in a courteous manner to Mr. Pickwick, wiped the mouth of the case-bottle, and applied it to his own, thereby informing him, without any unnecessary waste of words, that he devoted that draught to wishing him all manner of happiness and prosperity. Having done this, Bob replaced the cork with great care, and looking benignantly down on Mr. Pickwick, took a large bite out of the sandwich, and smiled.\n\n‘Come,’ said Mr. Pickwick, whose momentary anger was not quite proof against Bob’s immovable self-possession, ‘pray let us have no more of this absurdity.’\n\n‘No, no,’ replied Bob, once more exchanging hats with Mr. Weller; ‘I didn’t mean to do it, only I got so enlivened with the ride that I couldn’t help it.’\n\n‘Think of the look of the thing,’ expostulated Mr. Pickwick; ‘have some regard to appearances.’\n\n‘Oh, certainly,’ said Bob, ‘it’s not the sort of thing at all. All over, governor.’\n\nSatisfied with this assurance, Mr. Pickwick once more drew his head into the chaise and pulled up the glass; but he had scarcely resumed the conversation which Mr. Bob Sawyer had interrupted, when he was somewhat startled by the apparition of a small dark body, of an oblong form, on the outside of the window, which gave sundry taps against it, as if impatient of admission.\n\n‘What’s this?’ exclaimed Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘It looks like a case-bottle;’ remarked Ben Allen, eyeing the object in question through his spectacles with some interest; ‘I rather think it belongs to Bob.’\n\nThe impression was perfectly accurate; for Mr. Bob Sawyer, having attached the case-bottle to the end of the walking-stick, was battering the window with it, in token of his wish, that his friends inside would partake of its contents, in all good-fellowship and harmony.\n\n‘What’s to be done?’ said Mr. Pickwick, looking at the bottle. ‘This proceeding is more absurd than the other.’\n\n‘I think it would be best to take it in,’ replied Mr. Ben Allen; ‘it would serve him right to take it in and keep it, wouldn’t it?’\n\n‘It would,’ said Mr. Pickwick; ‘shall I?’\n\n‘I think it the most proper course we could possibly adopt,’ replied Ben.\n\nThis advice quite coinciding with his own opinion, Mr. Pickwick gently let down the window and disengaged the bottle from the stick; upon which the latter was drawn up, and Mr. Bob Sawyer was heard to laugh heartily.\n\n‘What a merry dog it is!’ said Mr. Pickwick, looking round at his companion, with the bottle in his hand.\n\n‘He is,’ said Mr. Allen.\n\n‘You cannot possibly be angry with him,’ remarked Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Quite out of the question,’ observed Benjamin Allen.\n\nDuring this short interchange of sentiments, Mr. Pickwick had, in an abstracted mood, uncorked the bottle.\n\n‘What is it?’ inquired Ben Allen carelessly.\n\n‘I don’t know,’ replied Mr. Pickwick, with equal carelessness. ‘It smells, I think, like milk-punch.’\n\nOh, indeed?’ said Ben.\n\n‘I think so,’ rejoined Mr. Pickwick, very properly guarding himself against the possibility of stating an untruth; ‘mind, I could not undertake to say certainly, without tasting it.’\n\n‘You had better do so,’ said Ben; ‘we may as well know what it is.’\n\n‘Do you think so?’ replied Mr. Pickwick. ‘Well; if you are curious to know, of course I have no objection.’\n\nEver willing to sacrifice his own feelings to the wishes of his friend, Mr. Pickwick at once took a pretty long taste.\n\n‘What is it?’ inquired Ben Allen, interrupting him with some impatience.\n\n‘Curious,’ said Mr. Pickwick, smacking his lips, ‘I hardly know, now. Oh, yes!’ said Mr. Pickwick, after a second taste. ‘It is punch.’\n\nMr. Ben Allen looked at Mr. Pickwick; Mr. Pickwick looked at Mr. Ben Allen; Mr. Ben Allen smiled; Mr. Pickwick did not.\n\n‘It would serve him right,’ said the last-named gentleman, with some severity--‘it would serve him right to drink it every drop.’\n\n‘The very thing that occurred to me,’ said Ben Allen.\n\n‘Is it, indeed?’ rejoined Mr. Pickwick. ‘Then here’s his health!’ With these words, that excellent person took a most energetic pull at the bottle, and handed it to Ben Allen, who was not slow to imitate his example. The smiles became mutual, and the milk-punch was gradually and cheerfully disposed of.\n\n‘After all,’ said Mr. Pickwick, as he drained the last drop, ‘his pranks are really very amusing; very entertaining indeed.’\n\n‘You may say that,’ rejoined Mr. Ben Allen. In proof of Bob Sawyer’s being one of the funniest fellows alive, he proceeded to entertain Mr. Pickwick with a long and circumstantial account how that gentleman once drank himself into a fever and got his head shaved; the relation of which pleasant and agreeable history was only stopped by the stoppage of the chaise at the Bell at Berkeley Heath, to change horses.\n\n‘I say! We’re going to dine here, aren’t we?’ said Bob, looking in at the window.\n\n‘Dine!’ said Mr. Pickwick. ‘Why, we have only come nineteen miles, and have eighty-seven and a half to go.’\n\n‘Just the reason why we should take something to enable us to bear up against the fatigue,’ remonstrated Mr. Bob Sawyer.\n\n‘Oh, it’s quite impossible to dine at half-past eleven o’clock in the day,’ replied Mr. Pickwick, looking at his watch.\n\n‘So it is,’ rejoined Bob, ‘lunch is the very thing. Hollo, you sir! Lunch for three, directly; and keep the horses back for a quarter of an hour. Tell them to put everything they have cold, on the table, and some bottled ale, and let us taste your very best Madeira.’ Issuing these orders with monstrous importance and bustle, Mr. Bob Sawyer at once hurried into the house to superintend the arrangements; in less than five minutes he returned and declared them to be excellent.\n\nThe quality of the lunch fully justified the eulogium which Bob had pronounced, and very great justice was done to it, not only by that gentleman, but Mr. Ben Allen and Mr. Pickwick also. Under the auspices of the three, the bottled ale and the Madeira were promptly disposed of; and when (the horses being once more put to) they resumed their seats, with the case-bottle full of the best substitute for milk-punch that could be procured on so short a notice, the key-bugle sounded, and the red flag waved, without the slightest opposition on Mr. Pickwick’s part.\n\nAt the Hop Pole at Tewkesbury, they stopped to dine; upon which occasion there was more bottled ale, with some more Madeira, and some port besides; and here the case-bottle was replenished for the fourth time. Under the influence of these combined stimulants, Mr. Pickwick and Mr. Ben Allen fell fast asleep for thirty miles, while Bob and Mr. Weller sang duets in the dickey.\n\nIt was quite dark when Mr. Pickwick roused himself sufficiently to look out of the window. The straggling cottages by the road-side, the dingy hue of every object visible, the murky atmosphere, the paths of cinders and brick-dust, the deep-red glow of furnace fires in the distance, the volumes of dense smoke issuing heavily forth from high toppling chimneys, blackening and obscuring everything around; the glare of distant lights, the ponderous wagons which toiled along the road, laden with clashing rods of iron, or piled with heavy goods--all betokened their rapid approach to the great working town of Birmingham.\n\nAs they rattled through the narrow thoroughfares leading to the heart of the turmoil, the sights and sounds of earnest occupation struck more forcibly on the senses. The streets were thronged with working people. The hum of labour resounded from every house; lights gleamed from the long casement windows in the attic storeys, and the whirl of wheels and noise of machinery shook the trembling walls. The fires, whose lurid, sullen light had been visible for miles, blazed fiercely up, in the great works and factories of the town. The din of hammers, the rushing of steam, and the dead heavy clanking of engines, was the harsh music which arose from every quarter.\n\nThe postboy was driving briskly through the open streets, and past the handsome and well-lighted shops that intervene between the outskirts of the town and the Old Royal Hotel, before Mr. Pickwick had begun to consider the very difficult and delicate nature of the commission which had carried him thither.\n\nThe delicate nature of this commission, and the difficulty of executing it in a satisfactory manner, were by no means lessened by the voluntary companionship of Mr. Bob Sawyer. Truth to tell, Mr. Pickwick felt that his presence on the occasion, however considerate and gratifying, was by no means an honour he would willingly have sought; in fact, he would cheerfully have given a reasonable sum of money to have had Mr. Bob Sawyer removed to any place at not less than fifty miles’ distance, without delay.\n\nMr. Pickwick had never held any personal communication with Mr. Winkle, senior, although he had once or twice corresponded with him by letter, and returned satisfactory answers to his inquiries concerning the moral character and behaviour of his son; he felt nervously sensible that to wait upon him, for the first time, attended by Bob Sawyer and Ben Allen, both slightly fuddled, was not the most ingenious and likely means that could have been hit upon to prepossess him in his favour.\n\n‘However,’ said Mr. Pickwick, endeavouring to reassure himself, ‘I must do the best I can. I must see him to-night, for I faithfully promised to do so. If they persist in accompanying me, I must make the interview as brief as possible, and be content that, for their own sakes, they will not expose themselves.’\n\nAs he comforted himself with these reflections, the chaise stopped at the door of the Old Royal. Ben Allen having been partially awakened from a stupendous sleep, and dragged out by the collar by Mr. Samuel Weller, Mr. Pickwick was enabled to alight. They were shown to a comfortable apartment, and Mr. Pickwick at once propounded a question to the waiter concerning the whereabout of Mr. Winkle’s residence.\n\n‘Close by, Sir,’ said the waiter, ‘not above five hundred yards, Sir. Mr. Winkle is a wharfinger, Sir, at the canal, sir. Private residence is not--oh dear, no, sir, not five hundred yards, sir.’ Here the waiter blew a candle out, and made a feint of lighting it again, in order to afford Mr. Pickwick an opportunity of asking any further questions, if he felt so disposed.\n\n‘Take anything now, Sir?’ said the waiter, lighting the candle in desperation at Mr. Pickwick’s silence. ‘Tea or coffee, Sir? Dinner, sir?’\n\n‘Nothing now.’\n‘Very good, sir. Like to order supper, Sir?’\n\n‘Not just now.’\n\n‘Very good, Sir.’ Here, he walked slowly to the door, and then stopping short, turned round and said, with great suavity--\n\n‘Shall I send the chambermaid, gentlemen?’\n\n‘You may if you please,’ replied Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘If you please, sir.’\n\n‘And bring some soda-water,’ said Bob Sawyer.\n\n‘Soda-water, Sir! Yes, Sir.’ With his mind apparently relieved from an overwhelming weight, by having at last got an order for something, the waiter imperceptibly melted away. Waiters never walk or run. They have a peculiar and mysterious power of skimming out of rooms, which other mortals possess not.\n\nSome slight symptoms of vitality having been awakened in Mr. Ben Allen by the soda-water, he suffered himself to be prevailed upon to wash his face and hands, and to submit to be brushed by Sam. Mr. Pickwick and Bob Sawyer having also repaired the disorder which the journey had made in their apparel, the three started forth, arm in arm, to Mr. Winkle’s; Bob Sawyer impregnating the atmosphere with tobacco smoke as he walked along.\n\nAbout a quarter of a mile off, in a quiet, substantial-looking street, stood an old red brick house with three steps before the door, and a brass plate upon it, bearing, in fat Roman capitals, the words, ‘Mr. Winkle.’ The steps were very white, and the bricks were very red, and the house was very clean; and here stood Mr. Pickwick, Mr. Benjamin Allen, and Mr. Bob Sawyer, as the clock struck ten.\n\nA smart servant-girl answered the knock, and started on beholding the three strangers.\n\n‘Is Mr. Winkle at home, my dear?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘He is just going to supper, Sir,’ replied the girl.\n\n‘Give him that card if you please,’ rejoined Mr. Pickwick. ‘Say I am sorry to trouble him at so late an hour; but I am anxious to see him to- night, and have only just arrived.’\n\nThe girl looked timidly at Mr. Bob Sawyer, who was expressing his admiration of her personal charms by a variety of wonderful grimaces; and casting an eye at the hats and greatcoats which hung in the passage, called another girl to mind the door while she went upstairs. The sentinel was speedily relieved; for the girl returned immediately, and begging pardon of the gentlemen for leaving them in the street, ushered them into a floor-clothed back parlour, half office and half dressing room, in which the principal useful and ornamental articles of furniture were a desk, a wash-hand stand and shaving-glass, a boot-rack and boot- jack, a high stool, four chairs, a table, and an old eight-day clock. Over the mantelpiece were the sunken doors of an iron safe, while a couple of hanging shelves for books, an almanac, and several files of dusty papers, decorated the walls.\n\n‘Very sorry to leave you standing at the door, Sir,’ said the girl, lighting a lamp, and addressing Mr. Pickwick with a winning smile, ‘but you was quite strangers to me; and we have such a many trampers that only come to see what they can lay their hands on, that really--’\n\n‘There is not the least occasion for any apology, my dear,’ said Mr. Pickwick good-humouredly.\n\n‘Not the slightest, my love,’ said Bob Sawyer, playfully stretching forth his arms, and skipping from side to side, as if to prevent the young lady’s leaving the room.\n\nThe young lady was not at all softened by these allurements, for she at once expressed her opinion, that Mr. Bob Sawyer was an ‘odous creetur;’ and, on his becoming rather more pressing in his attentions, imprinted her fair fingers upon his face, and bounced out of the room with many expressions of aversion and contempt.\n\nDeprived of the young lady’s society, Mr. Bob Sawyer proceeded to divert himself by peeping into the desk, looking into all the table drawers, feigning to pick the lock of the iron safe, turning the almanac with its face to the wall, trying on the boots of Mr. Winkle, senior, over his own, and making several other humorous experiments upon the furniture, all of which afforded Mr. Pickwick unspeakable horror and agony, and yielded Mr. Bob Sawyer proportionate delight.\n\nAt length the door opened, and a little old gentleman in a snuff- coloured suit, with a head and face the precise counterpart of those belonging to Mr. Winkle, junior, excepting that he was rather bald, trotted into the room with Mr. Pickwick’s card in one hand, and a silver candlestick in the other.\n\n‘Mr. Pickwick, sir, how do you do?’ said Winkle the elder, putting down the candlestick and proffering his hand. ‘Hope I see you well, sir. Glad to see you. Be seated, Mr. Pickwick, I beg, Sir. This gentleman is--’\n\n‘My friend, Mr. Sawyer,’ interposed Mr. Pickwick, ‘your son’s friend.’\n\n‘Oh,’ said Mr. Winkle the elder, looking rather grimly at Bob. ‘I hope you are well, sir.’\n\n‘Right as a trivet, sir,’ replied Bob Sawyer.\n\n‘This other gentleman,’ cried Mr. Pickwick, ‘is, as you will see when you have read the letter with which I am intrusted, a very near relative, or I should rather say a very particular friend of your son’s. His name is Allen.’\n\n‘That gentleman?’ inquired Mr. Winkle, pointing with the card towards Ben Allen, who had fallen asleep in an attitude which left nothing of him visible but his spine and his coat collar.\n\nMr. Pickwick was on the point of replying to the question, and reciting Mr. Benjamin Allen’s name and honourable distinctions at full length, when the sprightly Mr. Bob Sawyer, with a view of rousing his friend to a sense of his situation, inflicted a startling pinch upon the fleshly part of his arm, which caused him to jump up with a shriek. Suddenly aware that he was in the presence of a stranger, Mr. Ben Allen advanced and, shaking Mr. Winkle most affectionately by both hands for about five minutes, murmured, in some half-intelligible fragments of sentences, the great delight he felt in seeing him, and a hospitable inquiry whether he felt disposed to take anything after his walk, or would prefer waiting ‘till dinner-time;’ which done, he sat down and gazed about him with a petrified stare, as if he had not the remotest idea where he was, which indeed he had not.\n\nAll this was most embarrassing to Mr. Pickwick, the more especially as Mr. Winkle, senior, evinced palpable astonishment at the eccentric--not to say extraordinary--behaviour of his two companions. To bring the matter to an issue at once, he drew a letter from his pocket, and presenting it to Mr. Winkle, senior, said--\n\n‘This letter, Sir, is from your son. You will see, by its contents, that on your favourable and fatherly consideration of it, depend his future happiness and welfare. Will you oblige me by giving it the calmest and coolest perusal, and by discussing the subject afterwards with me, in the tone and spirit in which alone it ought to be discussed? You may judge of the importance of your decision to your son, and his intense anxiety upon the subject, by my waiting upon you, without any previous warning, at so late an hour; and,’ added Mr. Pickwick, glancing slightly at his two companions--‘and under such unfavourable circumstances.’\n\nWith this prelude, Mr. Pickwick placed four closely-written sides of extra superfine wire-wove penitence in the hands of the astounded Mr. Winkle, senior. Then reseating himself in his chair, he watched his looks and manner: anxiously, it is true, but with the open front of a gentleman who feels he has taken no part which he need excuse or palliate.\n\nThe old wharfinger turned the letter over, looked at the front, back, and sides, made a microscopic examination of the fat little boy on the seal, raised his eyes to Mr. Pickwick’s face, and then, seating himself on the high stool, and drawing the lamp closer to him, broke the wax, unfolded the epistle, and lifting it to the light, prepared to read.\n\nJust at this moment, Mr. Bob Sawyer, whose wit had lain dormant for some minutes, placed his hands on his knees, and made a face after the portraits of the late Mr. Grimaldi, as clown. It so happened that Mr. Winkle, senior, instead of being deeply engaged in reading the letter, as Mr. Bob Sawyer thought, chanced to be looking over the top of it at no less a person than Mr. Bob Sawyer himself; rightly conjecturing that the face aforesaid was made in ridicule and derision of his own person, he fixed his eyes on Bob with such expressive sternness, that the late Mr. Grimaldi’s lineaments gradually resolved themselves into a very fine expression of humility and confusion.\n\n‘Did you speak, Sir?’ inquired Mr. Winkle, senior, after an awful silence.\n\n‘No, sir,’ replied Bob, With no remains of the clown about him, save and except the extreme redness of his cheeks.\n\n‘You are sure you did not, sir?’ said Mr. Winkle, senior.\n\n‘Oh dear, yes, sir, quite,’ replied Bob.\n\n‘I thought you did, Sir,’ replied the old gentleman, with indignant emphasis. ‘Perhaps you looked at me, sir?’\n\n‘Oh, no! sir, not at all,’ replied Bob, with extreme civility.\n\n‘I am very glad to hear it, sir,’ said Mr. Winkle, senior. Having frowned upon the abashed Bob with great magnificence, the old gentleman again brought the letter to the light, and began to read it seriously.\n\nMr. Pickwick eyed him intently as he turned from the bottom line of the first page to the top line of the second, and from the bottom of the second to the top of the third, and from the bottom of the third to the top of the fourth; but not the slightest alteration of countenance afforded a clue to the feelings with which he received the announcement of his son’s marriage, which Mr. Pickwick knew was in the very first half-dozen lines.\n\nHe read the letter to the last word, folded it again with all the carefulness and precision of a man of business, and, just when Mr. Pickwick expected some great outbreak of feeling, dipped a pen in the ink-stand, and said, as quietly as if he were speaking on the most ordinary counting-house topic--\n\n‘What is Nathaniel’s address, Mr. Pickwick?’\n\n‘The George and Vulture, at present,’ replied that gentleman.\n\n‘George and Vulture. Where is that?’\n\n‘George Yard, Lombard Street.’\n\n‘In the city?’\n\n‘Yes.’\nThe old gentleman methodically indorsed the address on the back of the letter; and then, placing it in the desk, which he locked, said, as he got off the stool and put the bunch of keys in his pocket--\n\n‘I suppose there is nothing else which need detain us, Mr. Pickwick?’\n\n‘Nothing else, my dear Sir!’ observed that warm-hearted person in indignant amazement. ‘Nothing else! Have you no opinion to express on this momentous event in our young friend’s life? No assurance to convey to him, through me, of the continuance of your affection and protection? Nothing to say which will cheer and sustain him, and the anxious girl who looks to him for comfort and support? My dear Sir, consider.’\n\n‘I will consider,’ replied the old gentleman. ‘I have nothing to say just now. I am a man of business, Mr. Pickwick. I never commit myself hastily in any affair, and from what I see of this, I by no means like the appearance of it. A thousand pounds is not much, Mr. Pickwick.’\n\n‘You’re very right, Sir,’ interposed Ben Allen, just awake enough to know that he had spent his thousand pounds without the smallest difficulty. ‘You’re an intelligent man. Bob, he’s a very knowing fellow this.’\n\n‘I am very happy to find that you do me the justice to make the admission, sir,’ said Mr. Winkle, senior, looking contemptuously at Ben Allen, who was shaking his head profoundly. ‘The fact is, Mr. Pickwick, that when I gave my son a roving license for a year or so, to see something of men and manners (which he has done under your auspices), so that he might not enter life a mere boarding-school milk-sop to be gulled by everybody, I never bargained for this. He knows that very well, so if I withdraw my countenance from him on this account, he has no call to be surprised. He shall hear from me, Mr. Pickwick. Good- night, sir.--Margaret, open the door.’\n\nAll this time, Bob Sawyer had been nudging Mr. Ben Allen to say something on the right side; Ben accordingly now burst, without the slightest preliminary notice, into a brief but impassioned piece of eloquence.\n\n‘Sir,’ said Mr. Ben Allen, staring at the old gentleman, out of a pair of very dim and languid eyes, and working his right arm vehemently up and down, ‘you--you ought to be ashamed of yourself.’\n\n‘As the lady’s brother, of course you are an excellent judge of the question,’ retorted Mr. Winkle, senior. ‘There; that’s enough. Pray say no more, Mr. Pickwick. Good-night, gentlemen!’\n\nWith these words the old gentleman took up the candle-stick and opening the room door, politely motioned towards the passage.\n\n‘You will regret this, Sir,’ said Mr. Pickwick, setting his teeth close together to keep down his choler; for he felt how important the effect might prove to his young friend.\n\n‘I am at present of a different opinion,’ calmly replied Mr. Winkle, senior. ‘Once again, gentlemen, I wish you a good-night.’\n\nMr. Pickwick walked with angry strides into the street. Mr. Bob Sawyer, completely quelled by the decision of the old gentleman’s manner, took the same course. Mr. Ben Allen’s hat rolled down the steps immediately afterwards, and Mr. Ben Allen’s body followed it directly. The whole party went silent and supperless to bed; and Mr. Pickwick thought, just before he fell asleep, that if he had known Mr. Winkle, senior, had been quite so much of a man of business, it was extremely probable he might never have waited upon him, on such an errand.\n\nCHAPTER LI. IN WHICH MR. PICKWICK ENCOUNTERS AN OLD ACQUAINTANCE--TO WHICH FORTUNATE CIRCUMSTANCE THE READER IS MAINLY INDEBTED FOR MATTER OF THRILLING INTEREST HEREIN SET DOWN, CONCERNING TWO GREAT PUBLIC MEN OF MIGHT AND POWER\nThe morning which broke upon Mr. Pickwick’s sight at eight o’clock, was not at all calculated to elevate his spirits, or to lessen the depression which the unlooked-for result of his embassy inspired. The sky was dark and gloomy, the air was damp and raw, the streets were wet and sloppy. The smoke hung sluggishly above the chimney-tops as if it lacked the courage to rise, and the rain came slowly and doggedly down, as if it had not even the spirit to pour. A game-cock in the stableyard, deprived of every spark of his accustomed animation, balanced himself dismally on one leg in a corner; a donkey, moping with drooping head under the narrow roof of an outhouse, appeared from his meditative and miserable countenance to be contemplating suicide. In the street, umbrellas were the only things to be seen, and the clicking of pattens and splashing of rain-drops were the only sounds to be heard.\n\nThe breakfast was interrupted by very little conversation; even Mr. Bob Sawyer felt the influence of the weather, and the previous day’s excitement. In his own expressive language he was ‘floored.’ So was Mr. Ben Allen. So was Mr. Pickwick.\n\nIn protracted expectation of the weather clearing up, the last evening paper from London was read and re-read with an intensity of interest only known in cases of extreme destitution; every inch of the carpet was walked over with similar perseverance; the windows were looked out of, often enough to justify the imposition of an additional duty upon them; all kinds of topics of conversation were started, and failed; and at length Mr. Pickwick, when noon had arrived, without a change for the better, rang the bell resolutely, and ordered out the chaise.\n\nAlthough the roads were miry, and the drizzling rain came down harder than it had done yet, and although the mud and wet splashed in at the open windows of the carriage to such an extent that the discomfort was almost as great to the pair of insides as to the pair of outsides, still there was something in the motion, and the sense of being up and doing, which was so infinitely superior to being pent in a dull room, looking at the dull rain dripping into a dull street, that they all agreed, on starting, that the change was a great improvement, and wondered how they could possibly have delayed making it as long as they had done.\n\nWhen they stopped to change at Coventry, the steam ascended from the horses in such clouds as wholly to obscure the hostler, whose voice was however heard to declare from the mist, that he expected the first gold medal from the Humane Society on their next distribution of rewards, for taking the postboy’s hat off; the water descending from the brim of which, the invisible gentleman declared, must have drowned him (the postboy), but for his great presence of mind in tearing it promptly from his head, and drying the gasping man’s countenance with a wisp of straw.\n\n‘This is pleasant,’ said Bob Sawyer, turning up his coat collar, and pulling the shawl over his mouth to concentrate the fumes of a glass of brandy just swallowed.\n\n‘Wery,’ replied Sam composedly.\n\n‘You don’t seem to mind it,’ observed Bob.\n\n‘Vy, I don’t exactly see no good my mindin’ on it ‘ud do, sir,’ replied Sam.\n\n‘That’s an unanswerable reason, anyhow,’ said Bob.\n\n‘Yes, sir,’ rejoined Mr. Weller. ‘Wotever is, is right, as the young nobleman sweetly remarked wen they put him down in the pension list ‘cos his mother’s uncle’s vife’s grandfather vunce lit the king’s pipe vith a portable tinder-box.’\n\nNot a bad notion that, Sam,’ said Mr. Bob Sawyer approvingly.\n\n‘Just wot the young nobleman said ev’ry quarter-day arterwards for the rest of his life,’ replied Mr. Weller.\n\n‘Wos you ever called in,’ inquired Sam, glancing at the driver, after a short silence, and lowering his voice to a mysterious whisper--‘wos you ever called in, when you wos ‘prentice to a sawbones, to wisit a postboy.’\n\n‘I don’t remember that I ever was,’ replied Bob Sawyer.\n\n‘You never see a postboy in that ‘ere hospital as you walked (as they says o’ the ghosts), did you?’ demanded Sam.\n\n‘No,’ replied Bob Sawyer. ‘I don’t think I ever did.’\n\n‘Never know’d a churchyard were there wos a postboy’s tombstone, or see a dead postboy, did you?’ inquired Sam, pursuing his catechism.\n\n‘No,’ rejoined Bob, ‘I never did.’\n\n‘No!’ rejoined Sam triumphantly. ‘Nor never vill; and there’s another thing that no man never see, and that’s a dead donkey. No man never see a dead donkey ‘cept the gen’l’m’n in the black silk smalls as know’d the young ‘ooman as kep’ a goat; and that wos a French donkey, so wery likely he warn’t wun o’ the reg’lar breed.’\n\n‘Well, what has that got to do with the postboys?’ asked Bob Sawyer.\n\n‘This here,’ replied Sam. ‘Without goin’ so far as to as-sert, as some wery sensible people do, that postboys and donkeys is both immortal, wot I say is this: that wenever they feels theirselves gettin’ stiff and past their work, they just rides off together, wun postboy to a pair in the usual way; wot becomes on ‘em nobody knows, but it’s wery probable as they starts avay to take their pleasure in some other vorld, for there ain’t a man alive as ever see either a donkey or a postboy a- takin’ his pleasure in this!’\n\nExpatiating upon this learned and remarkable theory, and citing many curious statistical and other facts in its support, Sam Weller beguiled the time until they reached Dunchurch, where a dry postboy and fresh horses were procured; the next stage was Daventry, and the next Towcester; and at the end of each stage it rained harder than it had done at the beginning.\n\n‘I say,’ remonstrated Bob Sawyer, looking in at the coach window, as they pulled up before the door of the Saracen’s Head, Towcester, ‘this won’t do, you know.’\n\n‘Bless me!’ said Mr. Pickwick, just awakening from a nap, ‘I’m afraid you’re wet.’\n\n‘Oh, you are, are you?’ returned Bob. ‘Yes, I am, a little that way, Uncomfortably damp, perhaps.’\n\nBob did look dampish, inasmuch as the rain was streaming from his neck, elbows, cuffs, skirts, and knees; and his whole apparel shone so with the wet, that it might have been mistaken for a full suit of prepared oilskin.\n\n‘I am rather wet,’ said Bob, giving himself a shake and casting a little hydraulic shower around, like a Newfoundland dog just emerged from the water.\n\n‘I think it’s quite impossible to go on to-night,’ interposed Ben.\n\n‘Out of the question, sir,’ remarked Sam Weller, coming to assist in the conference; ‘it’s a cruelty to animals, sir, to ask ‘em to do it. There’s beds here, sir,’ said Sam, addressing his master, ‘everything clean and comfortable. Wery good little dinner, sir, they can get ready in half an hour--pair of fowls, sir, and a weal cutlet; French beans, ‘taturs, tart, and tidiness. You’d better stop vere you are, sir, if I might recommend. Take adwice, sir, as the doctor said.’\n\nThe host of the Saracen’s Head opportunely appeared at this moment, to confirm Mr. Weller’s statement relative to the accommodations of the establishment, and to back his entreaties with a variety of dismal conjectures regarding the state of the roads, the doubt of fresh horses being to be had at the next stage, the dead certainty of its raining all night, the equally mortal certainty of its clearing up in the morning, and other topics of inducement familiar to innkeepers.\n\n‘Well,’ said Mr. Pickwick; ‘but I must send a letter to London by some conveyance, so that it may be delivered the very first thing in the morning, or I must go forwards at all hazards.’\n\nThe landlord smiled his delight. Nothing could be easier than for the gentleman to inclose a letter in a sheet of brown paper, and send it on, either by the mail or the night coach from Birmingham. If the gentleman were particularly anxious to have it left as soon as possible, he might write outside, ‘To be delivered immediately,’ which was sure to be attended to; or ‘Pay the bearer half-a-crown extra for instant delivery,’ which was surer still.\n\n‘Very well,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘then we will stop here.’\n\n‘Lights in the Sun, John; make up the fire; the gentlemen are wet!’ cried the landlord. ‘This way, gentlemen; don’t trouble yourselves about the postboy now, sir. I’ll send him to you when you ring for him, sir. Now, John, the candles.’\n\nThe candles were brought, the fire was stirred up, and a fresh log of wood thrown on. In ten minutes’ time, a waiter was laying the cloth for dinner, the curtains were drawn, the fire was blazing brightly, and everything looked (as everything always does, in all decent English inns) as if the travellers had been expected, and their comforts prepared, for days beforehand.\n\nMr. Pickwick sat down at a side table, and hastily indited a note to Mr. Winkle, merely informing him that he was detained by stress of weather, but would certainly be in London next day; until when he deferred any account of his proceedings. This note was hastily made into a parcel, and despatched to the bar per Mr. Samuel Weller.\n\nSam left it with the landlady, and was returning to pull his master’s boots off, after drying himself by the kitchen fire, when glancing casually through a half-opened door, he was arrested by the sight of a gentleman with a sandy head who had a large bundle of newspapers lying on the table before him, and was perusing the leading article of one with a settled sneer which curled up his nose and all other features into a majestic expression of haughty contempt.\n\n‘Hollo!’ said Sam, ‘I ought to know that ‘ere head and them features; the eyeglass, too, and the broad-brimmed tile! Eatansvill to vit, or I’m a Roman.’\n\nSam was taken with a troublesome cough, at once, for the purpose of attracting the gentleman’s attention; the gentleman starting at the sound, raised his head and his eyeglass, and disclosed to view the profound and thoughtful features of Mr. Pott, of the Eatanswill Gazette.\n\n‘Beggin’ your pardon, sir,’ said Sam, advancing with a bow, ‘my master’s here, Mr. Pott.’\n\n‘Hush! hush!’ cried Pott, drawing Sam into the room, and closing the door, with a countenance of mysterious dread and apprehension.\n\n‘Wot’s the matter, Sir?’ inquired Sam, looking vacantly about him.\n\n‘Not a whisper of my name,’ replied Pott; ‘this is a buff neighbourhood. If the excited and irritable populace knew I was here, I should be torn to pieces.’\n\n‘No! Vould you, sir?’ inquired Sam.\n\n‘I should be the victim of their fury,’ replied Pott. ‘Now young man, what of your master?’\n\n‘He’s a-stopping here to-night on his vay to town, with a couple of friends,’ replied Sam.\n\n‘Is Mr. Winkle one of them?’ inquired Pott, with a slight frown.\n\n‘No, Sir. Mr. Vinkle stops at home now,’ rejoined Sam. ‘He’s married.’\n\n‘Married!’ exclaimed Pott, with frightful vehemence. He stopped, smiled darkly, and added, in a low, vindictive tone, ‘It serves him right!’\n\nHaving given vent to this cruel ebullition of deadly malice and cold- blooded triumph over a fallen enemy, Mr. Pott inquired whether Mr. Pickwick’s friends were ‘blue?’ Receiving a most satisfactory answer in the affirmative from Sam, who knew as much about the matter as Pott himself, he consented to accompany him to Mr. Pickwick’s room, where a hearty welcome awaited him, and an agreement to club their dinners together was at once made and ratified.\n\n‘And how are matters going on in Eatanswill?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick, when Pott had taken a seat near the fire, and the whole party had got their wet boots off, and dry slippers on. ‘Is the Independent still in being?’\n\n‘The Independent, sir,’ replied Pott, ‘is still dragging on a wretched and lingering career. Abhorred and despised by even the few who are cognisant of its miserable and disgraceful existence, stifled by the very filth it so profusely scatters, rendered deaf and blind by the exhalations of its own slime, the obscene journal, happily unconscious of its degraded state, is rapidly sinking beneath that treacherous mud which, while it seems to give it a firm standing with the low and debased classes of society, is nevertheless rising above its detested head, and will speedily engulf it for ever.’\n\nHaving delivered this manifesto (which formed a portion of his last week’s leader) with vehement articulation, the editor paused to take breath, and looked majestically at Bob Sawyer.\n\n‘You are a young man, sir,’ said Pott.\n\nMr. Bob Sawyer nodded.\n\n‘So are you, sir,’ said Pott, addressing Mr. Ben Allen.\n\nBen admitted the soft impeachment.\n\n‘And are both deeply imbued with those blue principles, which, so long as I live, I have pledged myself to the people of these kingdoms to support and to maintain?’ suggested Pott.\n\n‘Why, I don’t exactly know about that,’ replied Bob Sawyer. ‘I am--’\n\n‘Not buff, Mr. Pickwick,’ interrupted Pott, drawing back his chair, ‘your friend is not buff, sir?’\n\n‘No, no,’ rejoined Bob, ‘I’m a kind of plaid at present; a compound of all sorts of colours.’\n\n‘A waverer,’ said Pott solemnly, ‘a waverer. I should like to show you a series of eight articles, Sir, that have appeared in the Eatanswill Gazette. I think I may venture to say that you would not be long in establishing your opinions on a firm and solid blue basis, sir.’\n\nI dare say I should turn very blue, long before I got to the end of them,’ responded Bob.\n\nMr. Pott looked dubiously at Bob Sawyer for some seconds, and, turning to Mr. Pickwick, said--\n\n‘You have seen the literary articles which have appeared at intervals in the Eatanswill Gazette in the course of the last three months, and which have excited such general--I may say such universal--attention and admiration?’\n\n‘Why,’ replied Mr. Pickwick, slightly embarrassed by the question, ‘the fact is, I have been so much engaged in other ways, that I really have not had an opportunity of perusing them.’\n\n‘You should do so, Sir,’ said Pott, with a severe countenance.\n\n‘I will,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘They appeared in the form of a copious review of a work on Chinese metaphysics, Sir,’ said Pott.\n\n‘Oh,’ observed Mr. Pickwick; ‘from your pen, I hope?’\n\n‘From the pen of my critic, Sir,’ rejoined Pott, with dignity.\n\n‘An abstruse subject, I should conceive,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Very, Sir,’ responded Pott, looking intensely sage. ‘He crammed for it, to use a technical but expressive term; he read up for the subject, at my desire, in the “Encyclopaedia Britannica.”’\n\n‘Indeed!’ said Mr. Pickwick; ‘I was not aware that that valuable work contained any information respecting Chinese metaphysics.’\n\n‘He read, Sir,’ rejoined Pott, laying his hand on Mr. Pickwick’s knee, and looking round with a smile of intellectual superiority--‘he read for metaphysics under the letter M, and for China under the letter C, and combined his information, Sir!’\n\nMr. Pott’s features assumed so much additional grandeur at the recollection of the power and research displayed in the learned effusions in question, that some minutes elapsed before Mr. Pickwick felt emboldened to renew the conversation; at length, as the editor’s countenance gradually relaxed into its customary expression of moral supremacy, he ventured to resume the discourse by asking--\n\n‘Is it fair to inquire what great object has brought you so far from home?’\n\n‘That object which actuates and animates me in all my gigantic labours, Sir,’ replied Pott, with a calm smile: ‘my country’s good.’\n\nI supposed it was some public mission,’ observed Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Yes, Sir,’ resumed Pott, ‘it is.’ Here, bending towards Mr. Pickwick, he whispered in a deep, hollow voice, ‘A Buff ball, Sir, will take place in Birmingham to-morrow evening.’\n\n‘God bless me!’ exclaimed Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Yes, Sir, and supper,’ added Pott.\n‘You don’t say so!’ ejaculated Mr. Pickwick.\n\nPott nodded portentously.\n\nNow, although Mr. Pickwick feigned to stand aghast at this disclosure, he was so little versed in local politics that he was unable to form an adequate comprehension of the importance of the dire conspiracy it referred to; observing which, Mr. Pott, drawing forth the last number of the Eatanswill Gazette, and referring to the same, delivered himself of the following paragraph:--\n\n# HOLE-AND-CORNER BUFFERY.\n\n‘A reptile contemporary has recently sweltered forth his black venom in the vain and hopeless attempt of sullying the fair name of our distinguished and excellent representative, the Honourable Mr. Slumkey-- that Slumkey whom we, long before he gained his present noble and exalted position, predicted would one day be, as he now is, at once his country’s brightest honour, and her proudest boast: alike her bold defender and her honest pride--our reptile contemporary, we say, has made himself merry, at the expense of a superbly embossed plated coal- scuttle, which has been presented to that glorious man by his enraptured constituents, and towards the purchase of which, the nameless wretch insinuates, the Honourable Mr. Slumkey himself contributed, through a confidential friend of his butler’s, more than three-fourths of the whole sum subscribed. Why, does not the crawling creature see, that even if this be the fact, the Honourable Mr. Slumkey only appears in a still more amiable and radiant light than before, if that be possible? Does not even his obtuseness perceive that this amiable and touching desire to carry out the wishes of the constituent body, must for ever endear him to the hearts and souls of such of his fellow townsmen as are not worse than swine; or, in other words, who are not as debased as our contemporary himself? But such is the wretched trickery of hole-and- corner Buffery! These are not its only artifices. Treason is abroad. We boldly state, now that we are goaded to the disclosure, and we throw ourselves on the country and its constables for protection--we boldly state that secret preparations are at this moment in progress for a Buff ball; which is to be held in a Buff town, in the very heart and centre of a Buff population; which is to be conducted by a Buff master of the ceremonies; which is to be attended by four ultra Buff members of Parliament, and the admission to which, is to be by Buff tickets! Does our fiendish contemporary wince? Let him writhe, in impotent malice, as we pen the words, We will be there.’\n\n‘There, Sir,’ said Pott, folding up the paper quite exhausted, ‘that is the state of the case!’\n\nThe landlord and waiter entering at the moment with dinner, caused Mr. Pott to lay his finger on his lips, in token that he considered his life in Mr. Pickwick’s hands, and depended on his secrecy. Messrs. Bob Sawyer and Benjamin Allen, who had irreverently fallen asleep during the reading of the quotation from the Eatanswill Gazette, and the discussion which followed it, were roused by the mere whispering of the talismanic word ‘Dinner’ in their ears; and to dinner they went with good digestion waiting on appetite, and health on both, and a waiter on all three.\n\nIn the course of the dinner and the sitting which succeeded it, Mr. Pott descending, for a few moments, to domestic topics, informed Mr. Pickwick that the air of Eatanswill not agreeing with his lady, she was then engaged in making a tour of different fashionable watering-places with a view to the recovery of her wonted health and spirits; this was a delicate veiling of the fact that Mrs. Pott, acting upon her often- repeated threat of separation, had, in virtue of an arrangement negotiated by her brother, the lieutenant, and concluded by Mr. Pott, permanently retired with the faithful bodyguard upon one moiety or half part of the annual income and profits arising from the editorship and sale of the Eatanswill Gazette.\n\nWhile the great Mr. Pott was dwelling upon this and other matters, enlivening the conversation from time to time with various extracts from his own lucubrations, a stern stranger, calling from the window of a stage-coach, outward bound, which halted at the inn to deliver packages, requested to know whether if he stopped short on his journey and remained there for the night, he could be furnished with the necessary accommodation of a bed and bedstead.\n\n‘Certainly, sir,’ replied the landlord.\n\n‘I can, can I?’ inquired the stranger, who seemed habitually suspicious in look and manner.\n\n‘No doubt of it, Sir,’ replied the landlord.\n\n‘Good,’ said the stranger. ‘Coachman, I get down here. Guard, my carpet- bag!’\n\nBidding the other passengers good-night, in a rather snappish manner, the stranger alighted. He was a shortish gentleman, with very stiff black hair cut in the porcupine or blacking-brush style, and standing stiff and straight all over his head; his aspect was pompous and threatening; his manner was peremptory; his eyes were sharp and restless; and his whole bearing bespoke a feeling of great confidence in himself, and a consciousness of immeasurable superiority over all other people.\n\nThis gentleman was shown into the room originally assigned to the patriotic Mr. Pott; and the waiter remarked, in dumb astonishment at the singular coincidence, that he had no sooner lighted the candles than the gentleman, diving into his hat, drew forth a newspaper, and began to read it with the very same expression of indignant scorn, which, upon the majestic features of Pott, had paralysed his energies an hour before. The man observed too, that, whereas Mr. Pott’s scorn had been roused by a newspaper headed the Eatanswill Independent, this gentleman’s withering contempt was awakened by a newspaper entitled the Eatanswill Gazette.\n\n‘Send the landlord,’ said the stranger.\n\n‘Yes, sir,’ rejoined the waiter.\n\nThe landlord was sent, and came.\n\n‘Are you the landlord?’ inquired the gentleman.\n\n‘I am sir,’ replied the landlord.\n\n‘Do you know me?’ demanded the gentleman.\n\n‘I have not had that pleasure, Sir,’ rejoined the landlord.\n\n‘My name is Slurk,’ said the gentleman.\n\nThe landlord slightly inclined his head.\n\n‘Slurk, sir,’ repeated the gentleman haughtily. ‘Do you know me now, man?’\n\nThe landlord scratched his head, looked at the ceiling, and at the stranger, and smiled feebly.\n\n‘Do you know me, man?’ inquired the stranger angrily.\n\nThe landlord made a strong effort, and at length replied, ‘Well, Sir, I do not know you.’\n\n‘Great Heaven!’ said the stranger, dashing his clenched fist upon the table. ‘And this is popularity!’\n\nThe landlord took a step or two towards the door; the stranger fixing his eyes upon him, resumed.\n\n‘This,’ said the stranger--‘this is gratitude for years of labour and study in behalf of the masses. I alight wet and weary; no enthusiastic crowds press forward to greet their champion; the church bells are silent; the very name elicits no responsive feeling in their torpid bosoms. It is enough,’ said the agitated Mr. Slurk, pacing to and fro, ‘to curdle the ink in one’s pen, and induce one to abandon their cause for ever.’\n\n‘Did you say brandy-and-water, Sir?’ said the landlord, venturing a hint.\n\n‘Rum,’ said Mr. Slurk, turning fiercely upon him. ‘Have you got a fire anywhere?’\n\n‘We can light one directly, Sir,’ said the landlord.\n\n‘Which will throw out no heat until it is bed-time,’ interrupted Mr. Slurk. ‘Is there anybody in the kitchen?’\n\nNot a soul. There was a beautiful fire. Everybody had gone, and the house door was closed for the night.\n\n‘I will drink my rum-and-water,’ said Mr. Slurk, ‘by the kitchen fire.’ So, gathering up his hat and newspaper, he stalked solemnly behind the landlord to that humble apartment, and throwing himself on a settle by the fireside, resumed his countenance of scorn, and began to read and drink in silent dignity.\n\nNow, some demon of discord, flying over the Saracen’s Head at that moment, on casting down his eyes in mere idle curiosity, happened to behold Slurk established comfortably by the kitchen fire, and Pott slightly elevated with wine in another room; upon which the malicious demon, darting down into the last-mentioned apartment with inconceivable rapidity, passed at once into the head of Mr. Bob Sawyer, and prompted him for his (the demon’s) own evil purpose to speak as follows:--\n\n‘I say, we’ve let the fire out. It’s uncommonly cold after the rain, isn’t it?’\n\n‘It really is,’ replied Mr. Pickwick, shivering.\n\n‘It wouldn’t be a bad notion to have a cigar by the kitchen fire, would it?’ said Bob Sawyer, still prompted by the demon aforesaid.\n\n‘It would be particularly comfortable, I think,’ replied Mr. Pickwick. ‘Mr. Pott, what do you say?’\n\nMr. Pott yielded a ready assent; and all four travellers, each with his glass in his hand, at once betook themselves to the kitchen, with Sam Weller heading the procession to show them the way.\n\nThe stranger was still reading; he looked up and started. Mr. Pott started.\n\n‘What’s the matter?’ whispered Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘That reptile!’ replied Pott.\n\n‘What reptile?’ said Mr. Pickwick, looking about him for fear he should tread on some overgrown black beetle, or dropsical spider.\n\n‘That reptile,’ whispered Pott, catching Mr. Pickwick by the arm, and pointing towards the stranger. ‘That reptile Slurk, of the Independent!’\n\n‘Perhaps we had better retire,’ whispered Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Never, Sir,’ rejoined Pott, pot-valiant in a double sense--‘never.’ With these words, Mr. Pott took up his position on an opposite settle, and selecting one from a little bundle of newspapers, began to read against his enemy.\n\nMr. Pott, of course read the Independent, and Mr. Slurk, of course, read the Gazette; and each gentleman audibly expressed his contempt at the other’s compositions by bitter laughs and sarcastic sniffs; whence they proceeded to more open expressions of opinion, such as ‘absurd,’ ‘wretched,’ ‘atrocity,’ ‘humbug,’ ‘knavery’, ‘dirt,’ ‘filth,’ ‘slime,’ ‘ditch-water,’ and other critical remarks of the like nature.\n\nBoth Mr. Bob Sawyer and Mr. Ben Allen had beheld these symptoms of rivalry and hatred, with a degree of delight which imparted great additional relish to the cigars at which they were puffing most vigorously. The moment they began to flag, the mischievous Mr. Bob Sawyer, addressing Slurk with great politeness, said--\n\n‘Will you allow me to look at your paper, Sir, when you have quite done with it?’\n\n‘You will find very little to repay you for your trouble in this contemptible thing, sir,’ replied Slurk, bestowing a Satanic frown on Pott.\n\n‘You shall have this presently,’ said Pott, looking up, pale with rage, and quivering in his speech, from the same cause. ‘Ha! ha! you will be amused with this fellow’s audacity.’\n\nTerrible emphasis was laid upon ‘thing’ and ‘fellow’; and the faces of both editors began to glow with defiance.\n\n‘The ribaldry of this miserable man is despicably disgusting,’ said Pott, pretending to address Bob Sawyer, and scowling upon Slurk.\n\nHere, Mr. Slurk laughed very heartily, and folding up the paper so as to get at a fresh column conveniently, said, that the blockhead really amused him.\n\n‘What an impudent blunderer this fellow is,’ said Pott, turning from pink to crimson.\n\n‘Did you ever read any of this man’s foolery, Sir?’ inquired Slurk of Bob Sawyer.\n\n‘Never,’ replied Bob; ‘is it very bad?’\n\n‘Oh, shocking! shocking!’ rejoined Slurk.\n\n‘Really! Dear me, this is too atrocious!’ exclaimed Pott, at this juncture; still feigning to be absorbed in his reading.\n\n‘If you can wade through a few sentences of malice, meanness, falsehood, perjury, treachery, and cant,’ said Slurk, handing the paper to Bob, ‘you will, perhaps, be somewhat repaid by a laugh at the style of this ungrammatical twaddler.’\n\n‘What’s that you said, Sir?’ inquired Mr. Pott, looking up, trembling all over with passion.\n\n‘What’s that to you, sir?’ replied Slurk.\n\n‘Ungrammatical twaddler, was it, sir?’ said Pott.\n\n‘Yes, sir, it was,’ replied Slurk; ‘and blue bore, Sir, if you like that better; ha! ha!’\n\nMr. Pott retorted not a word at this jocose insult, but deliberately folded up his copy of the Independent, flattened it carefully down, crushed it beneath his boot, spat upon it with great ceremony, and flung it into the fire.\n\n‘There, sir,’ said Pott, retreating from the stove, ‘and that’s the way I would serve the viper who produces it, if I were not, fortunately for him, restrained by the laws of my country.’\n\n‘Serve him so, sir!’ cried Slurk, starting up. ‘Those laws shall never be appealed to by him, sir, in such a case. Serve him so, sir!’\n\n‘Hear! hear!’ said Bob Sawyer.\n\n‘Nothing can be fairer,’ observed Mr. Ben Allen.\n\n‘Serve him so, sir!’ reiterated Slurk, in a loud voice.\n\nMr. Pott darted a look of contempt, which might have withered an anchor.\n\n‘Serve him so, sir!’ reiterated Slurk, in a louder voice than before.\n\n‘I will not, sir,’ rejoined Pott.\n\n‘Oh, you won’t, won’t you, sir?’ said Mr. Slurk, in a taunting manner; ‘you hear this, gentlemen! He won’t; not that he’s afraid--, oh, no! he won’t. Ha! ha!’\n\n‘I consider you, sir,’ said Mr. Pott, moved by this sarcasm, ‘I consider you a viper. I look upon you, sir, as a man who has placed himself beyond the pale of society, by his most audacious, disgraceful, and abominable public conduct. I view you, sir, personally and politically, in no other light than as a most unparalleled and unmitigated viper.’\n\nThe indignant Independent did not wait to hear the end of this personal denunciation; for, catching up his carpet-bag, which was well stuffed with movables, he swung it in the air as Pott turned away, and, letting it fall with a circular sweep on his head, just at that particular angle of the bag where a good thick hairbrush happened to be packed, caused a sharp crash to be heard throughout the kitchen, and brought him at once to the ground.\n\n‘Gentlemen,’ cried Mr. Pickwick, as Pott started up and seized the fire- shovel--‘gentlemen! Consider, for Heaven’s sake--help--Sam--here--pray, gentlemen--interfere, somebody.’\n\nUttering these incoherent exclamations, Mr. Pickwick rushed between the infuriated combatants just in time to receive the carpet-bag on one side of his body, and the fire-shovel on the other. Whether the representatives of the public feeling of Eatanswill were blinded by animosity, or (being both acute reasoners) saw the advantage of having a third party between them to bear all the blows, certain it is that they paid not the slightest attention to Mr. Pickwick, but defying each other with great spirit, plied the carpet-bag and the fire-shovel most fearlessly. Mr. Pickwick would unquestionably have suffered severely for his humane interference, if Mr. Weller, attracted by his master’s cries, had not rushed in at the moment, and, snatching up a meal-sack, effectually stopped the conflict by drawing it over the head and shoulders of the mighty Pott, and clasping him tight round the shoulders.\n\n‘Take away that ‘ere bag from the t’other madman,’ said Sam to Ben Allen and Bob Sawyer, who had done nothing but dodge round the group, each with a tortoise-shell lancet in his hand, ready to bleed the first man stunned. ‘Give it up, you wretched little creetur, or I’ll smother you in it.’\n\nAwed by these threats, and quite out of breath, the Independent suffered himself to be disarmed; and Mr. Weller, removing the extinguisher from Pott, set him free with a caution.\n\n‘You take yourselves off to bed quietly,’ said Sam, ‘or I’ll put you both in it, and let you fight it out vith the mouth tied, as I vould a dozen sich, if they played these games. And you have the goodness to come this here way, sir, if you please.’\n\nThus addressing his master, Sam took him by the arm, and led him off, while the rival editors were severally removed to their beds by the landlord, under the inspection of Mr. Bob Sawyer and Mr. Benjamin Allen; breathing, as they went away, many sanguinary threats, and making vague appointments for mortal combat next day. When they came to think it over, however, it occurred to them that they could do it much better in print, so they recommenced deadly hostilities without delay; and all Eatanswill rung with their boldness--on paper.\n\nThey had taken themselves off in separate coaches, early next morning, before the other travellers were stirring; and the weather having now cleared up, the chaise companions once more turned their faces to London.\n\nCHAPTER LII. INVOLVING A SERIOUS CHANGE IN THE WELLER FAMILY, AND THE UNTIMELY DOWNFALL OF MR. STIGGINS\nConsidering it a matter of delicacy to abstain from introducing either Bob Sawyer or Ben Allen to the young couple, until they were fully prepared to expect them, and wishing to spare Arabella’s feelings as much as possible, Mr. Pickwick proposed that he and Sam should alight in the neighbourhood of the George and Vulture, and that the two young men should for the present take up their quarters elsewhere. To this they very readily agreed, and the proposition was accordingly acted upon; Mr. Ben Allen and Mr. Bob Sawyer betaking themselves to a sequestered pot- shop on the remotest confines of the Borough, behind the bar door of which their names had in other days very often appeared at the head of long and complex calculations worked in white chalk.\n\n‘Dear me, Mr. Weller,’ said the pretty housemaid, meeting Sam at the door.\n\n‘Dear me I vish it vos, my dear,’ replied Sam, dropping behind, to let his master get out of hearing. ‘Wot a sweet-lookin’ creetur you are, Mary!’\n\n‘Lor’, Mr. Weller, what nonsense you do talk!’ said Mary. ‘Oh! don’t, Mr. Weller.’\n\n‘Don’t what, my dear?’ said Sam.\n\n‘Why, that,’ replied the pretty housemaid. ‘Lor, do get along with you.’ Thus admonishing him, the pretty housemaid pushed Sam against the wall, declaring that he had tumbled her cap, and put her hair quite out of curl.\n\n‘And prevented what I was going to say, besides,’ added Mary. ‘There’s a letter been waiting here for you four days; you hadn’t gone away, half an hour, when it came; and more than that, it’s got “immediate,” on the outside.’\n\n‘Vere is it, my love?’ inquired Sam.\n\n‘I took care of it, for you, or I dare say it would have been lost long before this,’ replied Mary. ‘There, take it; it’s more than you deserve.’\n\nWith these words, after many pretty little coquettish doubts and fears, and wishes that she might not have lost it, Mary produced the letter from behind the nicest little muslin tucker possible, and handed it to Sam, who thereupon kissed it with much gallantry and devotion.\n\n‘My goodness me!’ said Mary, adjusting the tucker, and feigning unconsciousness, ‘you seem to have grown very fond of it all at once.’\n\nTo this Mr. Weller only replied by a wink, the intense meaning of which no description could convey the faintest idea of; and, sitting himself down beside Mary on a window-seat, opened the letter and glanced at the contents.\n\n‘Hollo!’ exclaimed Sam, ‘wot’s all this?’\n\n‘Nothing the matter, I hope?’ said Mary, peeping over his shoulder.\n\n‘Bless them eyes o’ yourn!’ said Sam, looking up.\n\n‘Never mind my eyes; you had much better read your letter,’ said the pretty housemaid; and as she said so, she made the eyes twinkle with such slyness and beauty that they were perfectly irresistible.\n\nSam refreshed himself with a kiss, and read as follows:--\n\n‘I am wery sorry to have the pleasure of being a Bear of ill news your Mother in law cort cold consekens of imprudently settin too long on the damp grass in the rain a hearin of a shepherd who warnt able to leave off till late at night owen to his having vound his-self up vith brandy and vater and not being able to stop his-self till he got a little sober which took a many hours to do the doctor says that if she’d svallo’d varm brandy and vater artervards insted of afore she mightn’t have been no vus her veels wos immedetly greased and everythink done to set her agoin as could be inwented your father had hopes as she vould have vorked round as usual but just as she wos a turnen the corner my boy she took the wrong road and vent down hill vith a welocity you never see and notvithstandin that the drag wos put on drectly by the medikel man it wornt of no use at all for she paid the last pike at twenty minutes afore six o’clock yesterday evenin havin done the jouney wery much under the reglar time vich praps was partly owen to her haven taken in wery little luggage by the vay your father says that if you vill come and see me Sammy he vill take it as a wery great favor for I am wery lonely Samivel N. B. he vill have it spelt that vay vich I say ant right and as there is sich a many things to settle he is sure your guvner wont object of course he vill not Sammy for I knows him better so he sends his dooty in which I join and am Samivel infernally yours\n\n‘Wot a incomprehensible letter,’ said Sam; ‘who’s to know wot it means, vith all this he-ing and I-ing! It ain’t my father’s writin’, ‘cept this here signater in print letters; that’s his.’\n\n‘Perhaps he got somebody to write it for him, and signed it himself afterwards,’ said the pretty housemaid.\n\n‘Stop a minit,’ replied Sam, running over the letter again, and pausing here and there, to reflect, as he did so. ‘You’ve hit it. The gen’l’m’n as wrote it wos a-tellin’ all about the misfortun’ in a proper vay, and then my father comes a-lookin’ over him, and complicates the whole concern by puttin’ his oar in. That’s just the wery sort o’ thing he’d do. You’re right, Mary, my dear.’\n\nHaving satisfied himself on this point, Sam read the letter all over, once more, and, appearing to form a clear notion of its contents for the first time, ejaculated thoughtfully, as he folded it up--\n\n‘And so the poor creetur’s dead! I’m sorry for it. She warn’t a bad- disposed ‘ooman, if them shepherds had let her alone. I’m wery sorry for it.’\n\nMr. Weller uttered these words in so serious a manner, that the pretty housemaid cast down her eyes and looked very grave.\n\n‘Hows’ever,’ said Sam, putting the letter in his pocket with a gentle sigh, ‘it wos to be--and wos, as the old lady said arter she’d married the footman. Can’t be helped now, can it, Mary?’\n\nMary shook her head, and sighed too.\n\n‘I must apply to the hemperor for leave of absence,’ said Sam.\n\nMary sighed again--the letter was so very affecting.\n\n‘Good-bye!’ said Sam.\n\n‘Good-bye,’ rejoined the pretty housemaid, turning her head away.\n\n‘Well, shake hands, won’t you?’ said Sam.\n\nThe pretty housemaid put out a hand which, although it was a housemaid’s, was a very small one, and rose to go.\n\n‘I shan’t be wery long avay,’ said Sam.\n\n‘You’re always away,’ said Mary, giving her head the slightest possible toss in the air. ‘You no sooner come, Mr. Weller, than you go again.’\n\nMr. Weller drew the household beauty closer to him, and entered upon a whispering conversation, which had not proceeded far, when she turned her face round and condescended to look at him again. When they parted, it was somehow or other indispensably necessary for her to go to her room, and arrange the cap and curls before she could think of presenting herself to her mistress; which preparatory ceremony she went off to perform, bestowing many nods and smiles on Sam over the banisters as she tripped upstairs.\n\n‘I shan’t be avay more than a day, or two, Sir, at the furthest,’ said Sam, when he had communicated to Mr. Pickwick the intelligence of his father’s loss.\n\n‘As long as may be necessary, Sam,’ replied Mr. Pickwick, ‘you have my full permission to remain.’\nSam bowed.\n\n‘You will tell your father, Sam, that if I can be of any assistance to him in his present situation, I shall be most willing and ready to lend him any aid in my power,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Thank’ee, sir,’ rejoined Sam. ‘I’ll mention it, sir.’\n\nAnd with some expressions of mutual good-will and interest, master and man separated.\n\nIt was just seven o’clock when Samuel Weller, alighting from the box of a stage-coach which passed through Dorking, stood within a few hundred yards of the Marquis of Granby. It was a cold, dull evening; the little street looked dreary and dismal; and the mahogany countenance of the noble and gallant marquis seemed to wear a more sad and melancholy expression than it was wont to do, as it swung to and fro, creaking mournfully in the wind. The blinds were pulled down, and the shutters partly closed; of the knot of loungers that usually collected about the door, not one was to be seen; the place was silent and desolate.\n\nSeeing nobody of whom he could ask any preliminary questions, Sam walked softly in, and glancing round, he quickly recognised his parent in the distance.\n\nThe widower was seated at a small round table in the little room behind the bar, smoking a pipe, with his eyes intently fixed upon the fire. The funeral had evidently taken place that day, for attached to his hat, which he still retained on his head, was a hatband measuring about a yard and a half in length, which hung over the top rail of the chair and streamed negligently down. Mr. Weller was in a very abstracted and contemplative mood. Notwithstanding that Sam called him by name several times, he still continued to smoke with the same fixed and quiet countenance, and was only roused ultimately by his son’s placing the palm of his hand on his shoulder.\n\n‘Sammy,’ said Mr. Weller, ‘you’re welcome.’\n\n‘I’ve been a-callin’ to you half a dozen times,’ said Sam, hanging his hat on a peg, ‘but you didn’t hear me.’\n\n‘No, Sammy,’ replied Mr. Weller, again looking thoughtfully at the fire. ‘I was in a referee, Sammy.’\n\n‘Wot about?’ inquired Sam, drawing his chair up to the fire.\n\n‘In a referee, Sammy,’ replied the elder Mr. Weller, ‘regarding her, Samivel.’ Here Mr. Weller jerked his head in the direction of Dorking churchyard, in mute explanation that his words referred to the late Mrs. Weller.\n\n‘I wos a-thinkin’, Sammy,’ said Mr. Weller, eyeing his son, with great earnestness, over his pipe, as if to assure him that however extraordinary and incredible the declaration might appear, it was nevertheless calmly and deliberately uttered. ‘I wos a-thinkin’, Sammy, that upon the whole I wos wery sorry she wos gone.’\n\n‘Vell, and so you ought to be,’ replied Sam.\n\nMr. Weller nodded his acquiescence in the sentiment, and again fastening his eyes on the fire, shrouded himself in a cloud, and mused deeply.\n\n‘Those wos wery sensible observations as she made, Sammy,’ said Mr. Weller, driving the smoke away with his hand, after a long silence.\n\n‘Wot observations?’ inquired Sam.\n\n‘Them as she made, arter she was took ill,’ replied the old gentleman.\n\n‘Wot was they?’\n\n‘Somethin’ to this here effect. “Veller,” she says, “I’m afeered I’ve not done by you quite wot I ought to have done; you’re a wery kind- hearted man, and I might ha’ made your home more comfortabler. I begin to see now,” she says, “ven it’s too late, that if a married ‘ooman vishes to be religious, she should begin vith dischargin’ her dooties at home, and makin’ them as is about her cheerful and happy, and that vile she goes to church, or chapel, or wot not, at all proper times, she should be wery careful not to con-wert this sort o’ thing into a excuse for idleness or self-indulgence. I have done this,” she says, “and I’ve vasted time and substance on them as has done it more than me; but I hope ven I’m gone, Veller, that you’ll think on me as I wos afore I know’d them people, and as I raly wos by natur.” ‘“Susan,” says I--I wos took up wery short by this, Samivel; I von’t deny it, my boy--“Susan,” I says, “you’ve been a wery good vife to me, altogether; don’t say nothin’ at all about it; keep a good heart, my dear; and you’ll live to see me punch that ‘ere Stiggins’s head yet.” She smiled at this, Samivel,’ said the old gentleman, stifling a sigh with his pipe, ‘but she died arter all!’\n\n‘Vell,’ said Sam, venturing to offer a little homely consolation, after the lapse of three or four minutes, consumed by the old gentleman in slowly shaking his head from side to side, and solemnly smoking, ‘vell, gov’nor, ve must all come to it, one day or another.’\n\n‘So we must, Sammy,’ said Mr. Weller the elder.\n\n‘There’s a Providence in it all,’ said Sam.\n\n‘O’ course there is,’ replied his father, with a nod of grave approval. ‘Wot ‘ud become of the undertakers vithout it, Sammy?’\n\nLost in the immense field of conjecture opened by this reflection, the elder Mr. Weller laid his pipe on the table, and stirred the fire with a meditative visage.\n\nWhile the old gentleman was thus engaged, a very buxom-looking cook, dressed in mourning, who had been bustling about, in the bar, glided into the room, and bestowing many smirks of recognition upon Sam, silently stationed herself at the back of his father’s chair, and announced her presence by a slight cough, the which, being disregarded, was followed by a louder one.\n\n‘Hollo!’ said the elder Mr. Weller, dropping the poker as he looked round, and hastily drew his chair away. ‘Wot’s the matter now?’\n\n‘Have a cup of tea, there’s a good soul,’ replied the buxom female coaxingly.\n\n‘I von’t,’ replied Mr. Weller, in a somewhat boisterous manner. ‘I’ll see you--’ Mr. Weller hastily checked himself, and added in a low tone, ‘furder fust.’\n\n‘Oh, dear, dear! How adwersity does change people!’ said the lady, looking upwards.\n\n‘It’s the only thing ‘twixt this and the doctor as shall change my condition,’ muttered Mr. Weller.\n\n‘I really never saw a man so cross,’ said the buxom female.\n\n‘Never mind. It’s all for my own good; vich is the reflection vith vich the penitent school-boy comforted his feelin’s ven they flogged him,’ rejoined the old gentleman.\n\nThe buxom female shook her head with a compassionate and sympathising air; and, appealing to Sam, inquired whether his father really ought not to make an effort to keep up, and not give way to that lowness of spirits.\n\n‘You see, Mr. Samuel,’ said the buxom female, ‘as I was telling him yesterday, he will feel lonely, he can’t expect but what he should, sir, but he should keep up a good heart, because, dear me, I’m sure we all pity his loss, and are ready to do anything for him; and there’s no situation in life so bad, Mr. Samuel, that it can’t be mended. Which is what a very worthy person said to me when my husband died.’ Here the speaker, putting her hand before her mouth, coughed again, and looked affectionately at the elder Mr. Weller.\n\n‘As I don’t rekvire any o’ your conversation just now, mum, vill you have the goodness to re-tire?’ inquired Mr. Weller, in a grave and steady voice.\n\n‘Well, Mr. Weller,’ said the buxom female, ‘I’m sure I only spoke to you out of kindness.’\n\n‘Wery likely, mum,’ replied Mr. Weller. ‘Samivel, show the lady out, and shut the door after her.’\n\nThis hint was not lost upon the buxom female; for she at once left the room, and slammed the door behind her, upon which Mr. Weller, senior, falling back in his chair in a violent perspiration, said--\n\n‘Sammy, if I wos to stop here alone vun week--only vun week, my boy-- that ‘ere ‘ooman ‘ud marry me by force and wiolence afore it was over.’\n\n‘Wot! is she so wery fond on you?’ inquired Sam.\n\n‘Fond!’ replied his father. ‘I can’t keep her avay from me. If I was locked up in a fireproof chest vith a patent Brahmin, she’d find means to get at me, Sammy.’\n\n‘Wot a thing it is to be so sought arter!’ observed Sam, smiling.\n\n‘I don’t take no pride out on it, Sammy,’ replied Mr. Weller, poking the fire vehemently, ‘it’s a horrid sitiwation. I’m actiwally drove out o’ house and home by it. The breath was scarcely out o’ your poor mother- in-law’s body, ven vun old ‘ooman sends me a pot o’ jam, and another a pot o’ jelly, and another brews a blessed large jug o’ camomile-tea, vich she brings in vith her own hands.’ Mr. Weller paused with an aspect of intense disgust, and looking round, added in a whisper, ‘They wos all widders, Sammy, all on ‘em, ‘cept the camomile-tea vun, as wos a single young lady o’ fifty-three.’\n\nSam gave a comical look in reply, and the old gentleman having broken an obstinate lump of coal, with a countenance expressive of as much earnestness and malice as if it had been the head of one of the widows last-mentioned, said:\n\n‘In short, Sammy, I feel that I ain’t safe anyveres but on the box.’\n\n‘How are you safer there than anyveres else?’ interrupted Sam.\n\n‘’Cos a coachman’s a privileged indiwidual,’ replied Mr. Weller, looking fixedly at his son. ‘’Cos a coachman may do vithout suspicion wot other men may not; ‘cos a coachman may be on the wery amicablest terms with eighty mile o’ females, and yet nobody think that he ever means to marry any vun among ‘em. And wot other man can say the same, Sammy?’\n\n‘Vell, there’s somethin’ in that,’ said Sam.\n\n‘If your gov’nor had been a coachman,’ reasoned Mr. Weller, ‘do you s’pose as that ‘ere jury ‘ud ever ha’ conwicted him, s’posin’ it possible as the matter could ha’ gone to that extremity? They dustn’t ha’ done it.’\n\n‘Wy not?’ said Sam, rather disparagingly.\n\n‘Wy not!’ rejoined Mr. Weller; ‘’cos it ‘ud ha’ gone agin their consciences. A reg’lar coachman’s a sort o’ con-nectin’ link betwixt singleness and matrimony, and every practicable man knows it.’\n\n‘Wot! You mean, they’re gen’ral favorites, and nobody takes adwantage on ‘em, p’raps?’ said Sam.\n\nHis father nodded.\n\n‘How it ever come to that ‘ere pass,’ resumed the parent Weller, ‘I can’t say. Wy it is that long-stage coachmen possess such insiniwations, and is alvays looked up to--a-dored I may say--by ev’ry young ‘ooman in ev’ry town he vurks through, I don’t know. I only know that so it is. It’s a regulation of natur--a dispensary, as your poor mother-in-law used to say.’\n\n‘A dispensation,’ said Sam, correcting the old gentleman.\n\n‘Wery good, Samivel, a dispensation if you like it better,’ returned Mr. Weller; ‘I call it a dispensary, and it’s always writ up so, at the places vere they gives you physic for nothin’ in your own bottles; that’s all.’\n\nWith these words, Mr. Weller refilled and relighted his pipe, and once more summoning up a meditative expression of countenance, continued as follows--\n\n‘Therefore, my boy, as I do not see the adwisability o’ stoppin here to be married vether I vant to or not, and as at the same time I do not vish to separate myself from them interestin’ members o’ society altogether, I have come to the determination o’ driving the Safety, and puttin’ up vunce more at the Bell Savage, vich is my nat’ral born element, Sammy.’\n\n‘And wot’s to become o’ the bis’ness?’ inquired Sam.\n\n‘The bis’ness, Samivel,’ replied the old gentleman, ‘good-vill, stock, and fixters, vill be sold by private contract; and out o’ the money, two hundred pound, agreeable to a rekvest o’ your mother-in-law’s to me, a little afore she died, vill be invested in your name in--What do you call them things agin?’\n\n‘Wot things?’ inquired Sam.\n\n‘Them things as is always a-goin’ up and down, in the city.’\n\n‘Omnibuses?’ suggested Sam.\n\n‘Nonsense,’ replied Mr. Weller. ‘Them things as is alvays a- fluctooatin’, and gettin’ theirselves inwolved somehow or another vith the national debt, and the chequers bill; and all that.’\n\n‘Oh! the funds,’ said Sam.\n\n‘Ah!’ rejoined Mr. Weller, ‘the funs; two hundred pounds o’ the money is to be inwested for you, Samivel, in the funs; four and a half per cent. reduced counsels, Sammy.’\n\n‘Wery kind o’ the old lady to think o’ me,’ said Sam, ‘and I’m wery much obliged to her.’\n\n‘The rest will be inwested in my name,’ continued the elder Mr. Weller; ‘and wen I’m took off the road, it’ll come to you, so take care you don’t spend it all at vunst, my boy, and mind that no widder gets a inklin’ o’ your fortun’, or you’re done.’\n\nHaving delivered this warning, Mr. Weller resumed his pipe with a more serene countenance; the disclosure of these matters appearing to have eased his mind considerably.\n\n‘Somebody’s a-tappin’ at the door,’ said Sam.\n\n‘Let ‘em tap,’ replied his father, with dignity.\n\nSam acted upon the direction. There was another tap, and another, and then a long row of taps; upon which Sam inquired why the tapper was not admitted.\n\n‘Hush,’ whispered Mr. Weller, with apprehensive looks, ‘don’t take no notice on ‘em, Sammy, it’s vun o’ the widders, p’raps.’\n\nNo notice being taken of the taps, the unseen visitor, after a short lapse, ventured to open the door and peep in. It was no female head that was thrust in at the partially-opened door, but the long black locks and red face of Mr. Stiggins. Mr. Weller’s pipe fell from his hands.\n\nThe reverend gentleman gradually opened the door by almost imperceptible degrees, until the aperture was just wide enough to admit of the passage of his lank body, when he glided into the room and closed it after him, with great care and gentleness. Turning towards Sam, and raising his hands and eyes in token of the unspeakable sorrow with which he regarded the calamity that had befallen the family, he carried the high-backed chair to his old corner by the fire, and, seating himself on the very edge, drew forth a brown pocket-handkerchief, and applied the same to his optics.\n\nWhile this was going forward, the elder Mr. Weller sat back in his chair, with his eyes wide open, his hands planted on his knees, and his whole countenance expressive of absorbing and overwhelming astonishment. Sam sat opposite him in perfect silence, waiting, with eager curiosity, for the termination of the scene.\n\nMr. Stiggins kept the brown pocket-handkerchief before his eyes for some minutes, moaning decently meanwhile, and then, mastering his feelings by a strong effort, put it in his pocket and buttoned it up. After this, he stirred the fire; after that, he rubbed his hands and looked at Sam.\n\n‘Oh, my young friend,’ said Mr. Stiggins, breaking the silence, in a very low voice, ‘here’s a sorrowful affliction!’\n\nSam nodded very slightly.\n\n‘For the man of wrath, too!’ added Mr. Stiggins; ‘it makes a vessel’s heart bleed!’\n\nMr. Weller was overheard by his son to murmur something relative to making a vessel’s nose bleed; but Mr. Stiggins heard him not.\n\n‘Do you know, young man,’ whispered Mr. Stiggins, drawing his chair closer to Sam, ‘whether she has left Emanuel anything?’\n\n‘Who’s he?’ inquired Sam.\n\n‘The chapel,’ replied Mr. Stiggins; ‘our chapel; our fold, Mr. Samuel.’\n\n‘She hasn’t left the fold nothin’, nor the shepherd nothin’, nor the animals nothin’,’ said Sam decisively; ‘nor the dogs neither.’\n\nMr. Stiggins looked slily at Sam; glanced at the old gentleman, who was sitting with his eyes closed, as if asleep; and drawing his chair still nearer, said--\n\n‘Nothing for me, Mr. Samuel?’\n\nSam shook his head.\n\n‘I think there’s something,’ said Stiggins, turning as pale as he could turn. ‘Consider, Mr. Samuel; no little token?’\n\n‘Not so much as the vorth o’ that ‘ere old umberella o’ yourn,’ replied Sam.\n\n‘Perhaps,’ said Mr. Stiggins hesitatingly, after a few moments’ deep thought, ‘perhaps she recommended me to the care of the man of wrath, Mr. Samuel?’\n\n‘I think that’s wery likely, from what he said,’ rejoined Sam; ‘he wos a-speakin’ about you, jist now.’\n\n‘Was he, though?’ exclaimed Stiggins, brightening up. ‘Ah! He’s changed, I dare say. We might live very comfortably together now, Mr. Samuel, eh? I could take care of his property when you are away--good care, you see.’\n\nHeaving a long-drawn sigh, Mr. Stiggins paused for a response. Sam nodded, and Mr. Weller the elder gave vent to an extraordinary sound, which, being neither a groan, nor a grunt, nor a gasp, nor a growl, seemed to partake in some degree of the character of all four.\n\nMr. Stiggins, encouraged by this sound, which he understood to betoken remorse or repentance, looked about him, rubbed his hands, wept, smiled, wept again, and then, walking softly across the room to a well- remembered shelf in one corner, took down a tumbler, and with great deliberation put four lumps of sugar in it. Having got thus far, he looked about him again, and sighed grievously; with that, he walked softly into the bar, and presently returning with the tumbler half full of pine-apple rum, advanced to the kettle which was singing gaily on the hob, mixed his grog, stirred it, sipped it, sat down, and taking a long and hearty pull at the rum-and-water, stopped for breath.\n\nThe elder Mr. Weller, who still continued to make various strange and uncouth attempts to appear asleep, offered not a single word during these proceedings; but when Stiggins stopped for breath, he darted upon him, and snatching the tumbler from his hand, threw the remainder of the rum-and-water in his face, and the glass itself into the grate. Then, seizing the reverend gentleman firmly by the collar, he suddenly fell to kicking him most furiously, accompanying every application of his top- boot to Mr. Stiggins’s person, with sundry violent and incoherent anathemas upon his limbs, eyes, and body.\n\n‘Sammy,’ said Mr. Weller, ‘put my hat on tight for me.’\n\nSam dutifully adjusted the hat with the long hatband more firmly on his father’s head, and the old gentleman, resuming his kicking with greater agility than before, tumbled with Mr. Stiggins through the bar, and through the passage, out at the front door, and so into the street--the kicking continuing the whole way, and increasing in vehemence, rather than diminishing, every time the top-boot was lifted.\n\nIt was a beautiful and exhilarating sight to see the red-nosed man writhing in Mr. Weller’s grasp, and his whole frame quivering with anguish as kick followed kick in rapid succession; it was a still more exciting spectacle to behold Mr. Weller, after a powerful struggle, immersing Mr. Stiggins’s head in a horse-trough full of water, and holding it there, until he was half suffocated.\n\n‘There!’ said Mr. Weller, throwing all his energy into one most complicated kick, as he at length permitted Mr. Stiggins to withdraw his head from the trough, ‘send any vun o’ them lazy shepherds here, and I’ll pound him to a jelly first, and drownd him artervards! Sammy, help me in, and fill me a small glass of brandy. I’m out o’ breath, my boy.’\n\nCHAPTER LIII. COMPRISING THE FINAL EXIT OF MR. JINGLE AND JOB TROTTER, WITH A GREAT MORNING OF BUSINESS IN GRAY’S INN SQUARE--CONCLUDING WITH A DOUBLE KNOCK AT MR. PERKER’S DOOR\nWhen Arabella, after some gentle preparation and many assurances that there was not the least occasion for being low-spirited, was at length made acquainted by Mr. Pickwick with the unsatisfactory result of his visit to Birmingham, she burst into tears, and sobbing aloud, lamented in moving terms that she should have been the unhappy cause of any estrangement between a father and his son.\n\n‘My dear girl,’ said Mr. Pickwick kindly, ‘it is no fault of yours. It was impossible to foresee that the old gentleman would be so strongly prepossessed against his son’s marriage, you know. I am sure,’ added Mr. Pickwick, glancing at her pretty face, ‘he can have very little idea of the pleasure he denies himself.’\n\n‘Oh, my dear Mr. Pickwick,’ said Arabella, ‘what shall we do, if he continues to be angry with us?’\n\n‘Why, wait patiently, my dear, until he thinks better of it,’ replied Mr. Pickwick cheerfully.\n\n‘But, dear Mr. Pickwick, what is to become of Nathaniel if his father withdraws his assistance?’ urged Arabella.\n\n‘In that case, my love,’ rejoined Mr. Pickwick, ‘I will venture to prophesy that he will find some other friend who will not be backward in helping him to start in the world.’\n\nThe significance of this reply was not so well disguised by Mr. Pickwick but that Arabella understood it. So, throwing her arms round his neck, and kissing him affectionately, she sobbed louder than before.\n\n‘Come, come,’ said Mr. Pickwick taking her hand, ‘we will wait here a few days longer, and see whether he writes or takes any other notice of your husband’s communication. If not, I have thought of half a dozen plans, any one of which would make you happy at once. There, my dear, there!’\n\nWith these words, Mr. Pickwick gently pressed Arabella’s hand, and bade her dry her eyes, and not distress her husband. Upon which, Arabella, who was one of the best little creatures alive, put her handkerchief in her reticule, and by the time Mr. Winkle joined them, exhibited in full lustre the same beaming smiles and sparkling eyes that had originally captivated him.\n\n‘This is a distressing predicament for these young people,’ thought Mr. Pickwick, as he dressed himself next morning. ‘I’ll walk up to Perker’s, and consult him about the matter.’\n\nAs Mr. Pickwick was further prompted to betake himself to Gray’s Inn Square by an anxious desire to come to a pecuniary settlement with the kind-hearted little attorney without further delay, he made a hurried breakfast, and executed his intention so speedily, that ten o’clock had not struck when he reached Gray’s Inn.\n\nIt still wanted ten minutes to the hour when he had ascended the staircase on which Perker’s chambers were. The clerks had not arrived yet, and he beguiled the time by looking out of the staircase window.\n\nThe healthy light of a fine October morning made even the dingy old houses brighten up a little; some of the dusty windows actually looking almost cheerful as the sun’s rays gleamed upon them. Clerk after clerk hastened into the square by one or other of the entrances, and looking up at the Hall clock, accelerated or decreased his rate of walking according to the time at which his office hours nominally commenced; the half-past nine o’clock people suddenly becoming very brisk, and the ten o’clock gentlemen falling into a pace of most aristocratic slowness. The clock struck ten, and clerks poured in faster than ever, each one in a greater perspiration than his predecessor. The noise of unlocking and opening doors echoed and re-echoed on every side; heads appeared as if by magic in every window; the porters took up their stations for the day; the slipshod laundresses hurried off; the postman ran from house to house; and the whole legal hive was in a bustle.\n\n‘You’re early, Mr. Pickwick,’ said a voice behind him.\n\n‘Ah, Mr. Lowten,’ replied that gentleman, looking round, and recognising his old acquaintance.\n\n‘Precious warm walking, isn’t it?’ said Lowten, drawing a Bramah key from his pocket, with a small plug therein, to keep the dust out.\n\n‘You appear to feel it so,’ rejoined Mr. Pickwick, smiling at the clerk, who was literally red-hot.\n\n‘I’ve come along, rather, I can tell you,’ replied Lowten. ‘It went the half hour as I came through the Polygon. I’m here before him, though, so I don’t mind.’\n\nComforting himself with this reflection, Mr. Lowten extracted the plug from the door-key; having opened the door, replugged and repocketed his Bramah, and picked up the letters which the postman had dropped through the box, he ushered Mr. Pickwick into the office. Here, in the twinkling of an eye, he divested himself of his coat, put on a threadbare garment, which he took out of a desk, hung up his hat, pulled forth a few sheets of cartridge and blotting-paper in alternate layers, and, sticking a pen behind his ear, rubbed his hands with an air of great satisfaction.\n\n‘There, you see, Mr. Pickwick,’ he said, ‘now I’m complete. I’ve got my office coat on, and my pad out, and let him come as soon as he likes. You haven’t got a pinch of snuff about you, have you?’\n\n‘No, I have not,’ replied Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘I’m sorry for it,’ said Lowten. ‘Never mind. I’ll run out presently, and get a bottle of soda. Don’t I look rather queer about the eyes, Mr. Pickwick?’\n\nThe individual appealed to, surveyed Mr. Lowten’s eyes from a distance, and expressed his opinion that no unusual queerness was perceptible in those features.\n\n‘I’m glad of it,’ said Lowten. ‘We were keeping it up pretty tolerably at the Stump last night, and I’m rather out of sorts this morning. Perker’s been about that business of yours, by the bye.’\n\n‘What business?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick. ‘Mrs. Bardell’s costs?’\n\n‘No, I don’t mean that,’ replied Mr. Lowten. ‘About getting that customer that we paid the ten shillings in the pound to the bill- discounter for, on your account--to get him out of the Fleet, you know-- about getting him to Demerara.’\n\n‘Oh, Mr. Jingle,’ said Mr. Pickwick hastily. ‘Yes. Well?’\n\n‘Well, it’s all arranged,’ said Lowten, mending his pen. ‘The agent at Liverpool said he had been obliged to you many times when you were in business, and he would be glad to take him on your recommendation.’\n\n‘That’s well,’ said Mr. Pickwick. ‘I am delighted to hear it.’\n\n‘But I say,’ resumed Lowten, scraping the back of the pen preparatory to making a fresh split, ‘what a soft chap that other is!’\n\n‘Which other?’\n\n‘Why, that servant, or friend, or whatever he is; you know, Trotter.’\n\n‘Ah!’ said Mr. Pickwick, with a smile. ‘I always thought him the reverse.’\n\n‘Well, and so did I, from what little I saw of him,’ replied Lowten, ‘it only shows how one may be deceived. What do you think of his going to Demerara, too?’\n\n‘What! And giving up what was offered him here!’ exclaimed Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Treating Perker’s offer of eighteen bob a week, and a rise if he behaved himself, like dirt,’ replied Lowten. ‘He said he must go along with the other one, and so they persuaded Perker to write again, and they’ve got him something on the same estate; not near so good, Perker says, as a convict would get in New South Wales, if he appeared at his trial in a new suit of clothes.’\n\n‘Foolish fellow,’ said Mr. Pickwick, with glistening eyes. ‘Foolish fellow.’\n\n‘Oh, it’s worse than foolish; it’s downright sneaking, you know,’ replied Lowten, nibbing the pen with a contemptuous face. ‘He says that he’s the only friend he ever had, and he’s attached to him, and all that. Friendship’s a very good thing in its way--we are all very friendly and comfortable at the Stump, for instance, over our grog, where every man pays for himself; but damn hurting yourself for anybody else, you know! No man should have more than two attachments--the first, to number one, and the second to the ladies; that’s what I say--ha! ha!’ Mr. Lowten concluded with a loud laugh, half in jocularity, and half in derision, which was prematurely cut short by the sound of Perker’s footsteps on the stairs, at the first approach of which, he vaulted on his stool with an agility most remarkable, and wrote intensely.\n\nThe greeting between Mr. Pickwick and his professional adviser was warm and cordial; the client was scarcely ensconced in the attorney’s arm- chair, however, when a knock was heard at the door, and a voice inquired whether Mr. Perker was within.\n\n‘Hark!’ said Perker, ‘that’s one of our vagabond friends--Jingle himself, my dear Sir. Will you see him?’\n\n‘What do you think?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick, hesitating.\n\n‘Yes, I think you had better. Here, you Sir, what’s your name, walk in, will you?’\n\nIn compliance with this unceremonious invitation, Jingle and Job walked into the room, but, seeing Mr. Pickwick, stopped short in some confusion.\n\n‘Well,’ said Perker, ‘don’t you know that gentleman?’\n\n‘Good reason to,’ replied Mr. Jingle, stepping forward. ‘Mr. Pickwick-- deepest obligations--life preserver--made a man of me--you shall never repent it, Sir.’\n\n‘I am happy to hear you say so,’ said Mr. Pickwick. ‘You look much better.’\n\n‘Thanks to you, sir--great change--Majesty’s Fleet--unwholesome place-- very,’ said Jingle, shaking his head. He was decently and cleanly dressed, and so was Job, who stood bolt upright behind him, staring at Mr. Pickwick with a visage of iron.\n\n‘When do they go to Liverpool?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick, half aside to Perker.\n\n‘This evening, Sir, at seven o’clock,’ said Job, taking one step forward. ‘By the heavy coach from the city, Sir.’\n\n‘Are your places taken?’\n\n‘They are, sir,’ replied Job.\n\n‘You have fully made up your mind to go?’\n\n‘I have sir,’ answered Job.\n\n‘With regard to such an outfit as was indispensable for Jingle,’ said Perker, addressing Mr. Pickwick aloud. ‘I have taken upon myself to make an arrangement for the deduction of a small sum from his quarterly salary, which, being made only for one year, and regularly remitted, will provide for that expense. I entirely disapprove of your doing anything for him, my dear sir, which is not dependent on his own exertions and good conduct.’\n\n‘Certainly,’ interposed Jingle, with great firmness. ‘Clear head--man of the world--quite right--perfectly.’\n\n‘By compounding with his creditor, releasing his clothes from the pawnbroker’s, relieving him in prison, and paying for his passage,’ continued Perker, without noticing Jingle’s observation, ‘you have already lost upwards of fifty pounds.’\n\n‘Not lost,’ said Jingle hastily, ‘Pay it all--stick to business--cash up--every farthing. Yellow fever, perhaps--can’t help that--if not--’ Here Mr. Jingle paused, and striking the crown of his hat with great violence, passed his hand over his eyes, and sat down.\n\n‘He means to say,’ said Job, advancing a few paces, ‘that if he is not carried off by the fever, he will pay the money back again. If he lives, he will, Mr. Pickwick. I will see it done. I know he will, Sir,’ said Job, with energy. ‘I could undertake to swear it.’\n\n‘Well, well,’ said Mr. Pickwick, who had been bestowing a score or two of frowns upon Perker, to stop his summary of benefits conferred, which the little attorney obstinately disregarded, ‘you must be careful not to play any more desperate cricket matches, Mr. Jingle, or to renew your acquaintance with Sir Thomas Blazo, and I have little doubt of your preserving your health.’\n\nMr. Jingle smiled at this sally, but looked rather foolish notwithstanding; so Mr. Pickwick changed the subject by saying--\n\n‘You don’t happen to know, do you, what has become of another friend of yours--a more humble one, whom I saw at Rochester?’\n\n‘Dismal Jemmy?’ inquired Jingle.\n\n‘Yes.’\n\nJingle shook his head.\n\n‘Clever rascal--queer fellow, hoaxing genius--Job’s brother.’\n\n‘Job’s brother!’ exclaimed Mr. Pickwick. ‘Well, now I look at him closely, there is a likeness.’\n\n‘We were always considered like each other, Sir,’ said Job, with a cunning look just lurking in the corners of his eyes, ‘only I was really of a serious nature, and he never was. He emigrated to America, Sir, in consequence of being too much sought after here, to be comfortable; and has never been heard of since.’\n\n‘That accounts for my not having received the “page from the romance of real life,” which he promised me one morning when he appeared to be contemplating suicide on Rochester Bridge, I suppose,’ said Mr. Pickwick, smiling. ‘I need not inquire whether his dismal behaviour was natural or assumed.’\n\n‘He could assume anything, Sir,’ said Job. ‘You may consider yourself very fortunate in having escaped him so easily. On intimate terms he would have been even a more dangerous acquaintance than--’ Job looked at Jingle, hesitated, and finally added, ‘than--than-myself even.’\n\n‘A hopeful family yours, Mr. Trotter,’ said Perker, sealing a letter which he had just finished writing.\n\n‘Yes, Sir,’ replied Job. ‘Very much so.’\n\n‘Well,’ said the little man, laughing, ‘I hope you are going to disgrace it. Deliver this letter to the agent when you reach Liverpool, and let me advise you, gentlemen, not to be too knowing in the West Indies. If you throw away this chance, you will both richly deserve to be hanged, as I sincerely trust you will be. And now you had better leave Mr. Pickwick and me alone, for we have other matters to talk over, and time is precious.’ As Perker said this, he looked towards the door, with an evident desire to render the leave-taking as brief as possible.\n\nIt was brief enough on Mr. Jingle’s part. He thanked the little attorney in a few hurried words for the kindness and promptitude with which he had rendered his assistance, and, turning to his benefactor, stood for a few seconds as if irresolute what to say or how to act. Job Trotter relieved his perplexity; for, with a humble and grateful bow to Mr. Pickwick, he took his friend gently by the arm, and led him away.\n\n‘A worthy couple!’ said Perker, as the door closed behind them.\n\n‘I hope they may become so,’ replied Mr. Pickwick. ‘What do you think? Is there any chance of their permanent reformation?’\n\nPerker shrugged his shoulders doubtfully, but observing Mr. Pickwick’s anxious and disappointed look, rejoined--\n\n‘Of course there is a chance. I hope it may prove a good one. They are unquestionably penitent now; but then, you know, they have the recollection of very recent suffering fresh upon them. What they may become, when that fades away, is a problem that neither you nor I can solve. However, my dear Sir,’ added Perker, laying his hand on Mr. Pickwick’s shoulder, ‘your object is equally honourable, whatever the result is. Whether that species of benevolence which is so very cautious and long-sighted that it is seldom exercised at all, lest its owner should be imposed upon, and so wounded in his self-love, be real charity or a worldly counterfeit, I leave to wiser heads than mine to determine. But if those two fellows were to commit a burglary to-morrow, my opinion of this action would be equally high.’\n\nWith these remarks, which were delivered in a much more animated and earnest manner than is usual in legal gentlemen, Perker drew his chair to his desk, and listened to Mr. Pickwick’s recital of old Mr. Winkle’s obstinacy.\n\n‘Give him a week,’ said Perker, nodding his head prophetically.\n\n‘Do you think he will come round?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘I think he will,’ rejoined Perker. ‘If not, we must try the young lady’s persuasion; and that is what anybody but you would have done at first.’\n\nMr. Perker was taking a pinch of snuff with various grotesque contractions of countenance, eulogistic of the persuasive powers appertaining unto young ladies, when the murmur of inquiry and answer was heard in the outer office, and Lowten tapped at the door.\n\n‘Come in!’ cried the little man.\n\nThe clerk came in, and shut the door after him, with great mystery.\n\n‘What’s the matter?’ inquired Perker.\n\n‘You’re wanted, Sir.’\n\n‘Who wants me?’\n\nLowten looked at Mr. Pickwick, and coughed.\n\n‘Who wants me? Can’t you speak, Mr. Lowten?’\n\n‘Why, sir,’ replied Lowten, ‘it’s Dodson; and Fogg is with him.’\n\n‘Bless my life!’ said the little man, looking at his watch, ‘I appointed them to be here at half-past eleven, to settle that matter of yours, Pickwick. I gave them an undertaking on which they sent down your discharge; it’s very awkward, my dear Sir; what will you do? Would you like to step into the next room?’\n\nThe next room being the identical room in which Messrs. Dodson & Fogg were, Mr. Pickwick replied that he would remain where he was: the more especially as Messrs. Dodson & Fogg ought to be ashamed to look him in the face, instead of his being ashamed to see them. Which latter circumstance he begged Mr. Perker to note, with a glowing countenance and many marks of indignation.\n\n‘Very well, my dear Sir, very well,’ replied Perker, ‘I can only say that if you expect either Dodson or Fogg to exhibit any symptom of shame or confusion at having to look you, or anybody else, in the face, you are the most sanguine man in your expectations that I ever met with. Show them in, Mr. Lowten.’\n\nMr. Lowten disappeared with a grin, and immediately returned ushering in the firm, in due form of precedence--Dodson first, and Fogg afterwards.\n\n‘You have seen Mr. Pickwick, I believe?’ said Perker to Dodson, inclining his pen in the direction where that gentleman was seated.\n\n‘How do you do, Mr. Pickwick?’ said Dodson, in a loud voice.\n\n‘Dear me,’ cried Fogg, ‘how do you do, Mr. Pickwick? I hope you are well, Sir. I thought I knew the face,’ said Fogg, drawing up a chair, and looking round him with a smile.\n\nMr. Pickwick bent his head very slightly, in answer to these salutations, and, seeing Fogg pull a bundle of papers from his coat pocket, rose and walked to the window.\n\n‘There’s no occasion for Mr. Pickwick to move, Mr. Perker,’ said Fogg, untying the red tape which encircled the little bundle, and smiling again more sweetly than before. ‘Mr. Pickwick is pretty well acquainted with these proceedings. There are no secrets between us, I think. He! he! he!’\n\n‘Not many, I think,’ said Dodson. ‘Ha! ha! ha!’ Then both the partners laughed together--pleasantly and cheerfully, as men who are going to receive money often do.\n\n‘We shall make Mr. Pickwick pay for peeping,’ said Fogg, with considerable native humour, as he unfolded his papers. ‘The amount of the taxed costs is one hundred and thirty-three, six, four, Mr. Perker.’\n\nThere was a great comparing of papers, and turning over of leaves, by Fogg and Perker, after this statement of profit and loss. Meanwhile, Dodson said, in an affable manner, to Mr. Pickwick--\n\n‘I don’t think you are looking quite so stout as when I had the pleasure of seeing you last, Mr. Pickwick.’\n\n‘Possibly not, Sir,’ replied Mr. Pickwick, who had been flashing forth looks of fierce indignation, without producing the smallest effect on either of the sharp practitioners; ‘I believe I am not, Sir. I have been persecuted and annoyed by scoundrels of late, Sir.’\n\nPerker coughed violently, and asked Mr. Pickwick whether he wouldn’t like to look at the morning paper. To which inquiry Mr. Pickwick returned a most decided negative.\n\n‘True,’ said Dodson, ‘I dare say you have been annoyed in the Fleet; there are some odd gentry there. Whereabouts were your apartments, Mr. Pickwick?’\n\n‘My one room,’ replied that much-injured gentleman, ‘was on the coffee- room flight.’\n\n‘Oh, indeed!’ said Dodson. ‘I believe that is a very pleasant part of the establishment.’\n\n‘Very,’ replied Mr. Pickwick drily.\n\nThere was a coolness about all this, which, to a gentleman of an excitable temperament, had, under the circumstances, rather an exasperating tendency. Mr. Pickwick restrained his wrath by gigantic efforts; but when Perker wrote a cheque for the whole amount, and Fogg deposited it in a small pocket-book, with a triumphant smile playing over his pimply features, which communicated itself likewise to the stern countenance of Dodson, he felt the blood in his cheeks tingling with indignation.\n\n‘Now, Mr. Dodson,’ said Fogg, putting up the pocket-book and drawing on his gloves, ‘I am at your service.’\n\n‘Very good,’ said Dodson, rising; ‘I am quite ready.’\n\n‘I am very happy,’ said Fogg, softened by the cheque, ‘to have had the pleasure of making Mr. Pickwick’s acquaintance. I hope you don’t think quite so ill of us, Mr. Pickwick, as when we first had the pleasure of seeing you.’\n\n‘I hope not,’ said Dodson, with the high tone of calumniated virtue. ‘Mr. Pickwick now knows us better, I trust; whatever your opinion of gentlemen of our profession may be, I beg to assure you, sir, that I bear no ill-will or vindictive feeling towards you for the sentiments you thought proper to express in our office in Freeman’s Court, Cornhill, on the occasion to which my partner has referred.’\n\n‘Oh, no, no; nor I,’ said Fogg, in a most forgiving manner.\n\n‘Our conduct, Sir,’ said Dodson, ‘will speak for itself, and justify itself, I hope, upon every occasion. We have been in the profession some years, Mr. Pickwick, and have been honoured with the confidence of many excellent clients. I wish you good-morning, Sir.’\n\n‘Good-morning, Mr. Pickwick,’ said Fogg. So saying, he put his umbrella under his arm, drew off his right glove, and extended the hand of reconciliation to that most indignant gentleman; who, thereupon, thrust his hands beneath his coat tails, and eyed the attorney with looks of scornful amazement.\n\n‘Lowten!’ cried Perker, at this moment. ‘Open the door.’\n\n‘Wait one instant,’ said Mr. Pickwick. ‘Perker, I will speak.’\n\n‘My dear Sir, pray let the matter rest where it is,’ said the little attorney, who had been in a state of nervous apprehension during the whole interview; ‘Mr. Pickwick, I beg--’\n\n‘I will not be put down, Sir,’ replied Mr. Pickwick hastily. ‘Mr. Dodson, you have addressed some remarks to me.’\n\nDodson turned round, bent his head meekly, and smiled.\n\n‘Some remarks to me,’ repeated Mr. Pickwick, almost breathless; ‘and your partner has tendered me his hand, and you have both assumed a tone of forgiveness and high-mindedness, which is an extent of impudence that I was not prepared for, even in you.’\n\n‘What, sir!’ exclaimed Dodson.\n\n‘What, sir!’ reiterated Fogg.\n\n‘Do you know that I have been the victim of your plots and conspiracies?’ continued Mr. Pickwick. ‘Do you know that I am the man whom you have been imprisoning and robbing? Do you know that you were the attorneys for the plaintiff, in Bardell and Pickwick?’\n\n‘Yes, sir, we do know it,’ replied Dodson.\n\n‘Of course we know it, Sir,’ rejoined Fogg, slapping his pocket--perhaps by accident.\n\n‘I see that you recollect it with satisfaction,’ said Mr. Pickwick, attempting to call up a sneer for the first time in his life, and failing most signally in so doing. ‘Although I have long been anxious to tell you, in plain terms, what my opinion of you is, I should have let even this opportunity pass, in deference to my friend Perker’s wishes, but for the unwarrantable tone you have assumed, and your insolent familiarity. I say insolent familiarity, sir,’ said Mr. Pickwick, turning upon Fogg with a fierceness of gesture which caused that person to retreat towards the door with great expedition.\n\n‘Take care, Sir,’ said Dodson, who, though he was the biggest man of the party, had prudently entrenched himself behind Fogg, and was speaking over his head with a very pale face. ‘Let him assault you, Mr. Fogg; don’t return it on any account.’\n\n‘No, no, I won’t return it,’ said Fogg, falling back a little more as he spoke; to the evident relief of his partner, who by these means was gradually getting into the outer office.\n\n‘You are,’ continued Mr. Pickwick, resuming the thread of his discourse- -’you are a well-matched pair of mean, rascally, pettifogging robbers.’\n\n‘Well,’ interposed Perker, ‘is that all?’\n\n‘It is all summed up in that,’ rejoined Mr. Pickwick; ‘they are mean, rascally, pettifogging robbers.’\n\n‘There!’ said Perker, in a most conciliatory tone. ‘My dear sirs, he has said all he has to say. Now pray go. Lowten, is that door open?’\n\nMr. Lowten, with a distant giggle, replied in the affirmative.\n\n‘There, there--good-morning--good-morning--now pray, my dear sirs--Mr. Lowten, the door!’ cried the little man, pushing Dodson & Fogg, nothing loath, out of the office; ‘this way, my dear sirs--now pray don’t prolong this--Dear me--Mr. Lowten--the door, sir--why don’t you attend?’\n\n‘If there’s law in England, sir,’ said Dodson, looking towards Mr. Pickwick, as he put on his hat, ‘you shall smart for this.’\n\n‘You are a couple of mean--’\n\n‘Remember, sir, you pay dearly for this,’ said Fogg.\n\n‘--Rascally, pettifogging robbers!’ continued Mr. Pickwick, taking not the least notice of the threats that were addressed to him.\n\n‘Robbers!’ cried Mr. Pickwick, running to the stair-head, as the two attorneys descended.\n\n‘Robbers!’ shouted Mr. Pickwick, breaking from Lowten and Perker, and thrusting his head out of the staircase window.\n\nWhen Mr. Pickwick drew in his head again, his countenance was smiling and placid; and, walking quietly back into the office, he declared that he had now removed a great weight from his mind, and that he felt perfectly comfortable and happy.\n\nPerker said nothing at all until he had emptied his snuff-box, and sent Lowten out to fill it, when he was seized with a fit of laughing, which lasted five minutes; at the expiration of which time he said that he supposed he ought to be very angry, but he couldn’t think of the business seriously yet--when he could, he would be.\n\n‘Well, now,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘let me have a settlement with you.’\n\nOf the same kind as the last?’ inquired Perker, with another laugh.\n\n‘Not exactly,’ rejoined Mr. Pickwick, drawing out his pocket-book, and shaking the little man heartily by the hand, ‘I only mean a pecuniary settlement. You have done me many acts of kindness that I can never repay, and have no wish to repay, for I prefer continuing the obligation.’\n\nWith this preface, the two friends dived into some very complicated accounts and vouchers, which, having been duly displayed and gone through by Perker, were at once discharged by Mr. Pickwick with many professions of esteem and friendship.\n\nThey had no sooner arrived at this point, than a most violent and startling knocking was heard at the door; it was not an ordinary double- knock, but a constant and uninterrupted succession of the loudest single raps, as if the knocker were endowed with the perpetual motion, or the person outside had forgotten to leave off.\n\n‘Dear me, what’s that?’ exclaimed Perker, starting.\n\n‘I think it is a knock at the door,’ said Mr. Pickwick, as if there could be the smallest doubt of the fact.\n\nThe knocker made a more energetic reply than words could have yielded, for it continued to hammer with surprising force and noise, without a moment’s cessation.\n\n‘Dear me!’ said Perker, ringing his bell, ‘we shall alarm the inn. Mr. Lowten, don’t you hear a knock?’\n\n‘I’ll answer the door in one moment, Sir,’ replied the clerk.\n\nThe knocker appeared to hear the response, and to assert that it was quite impossible he could wait so long. It made a stupendous uproar.\n\n‘It’s quite dreadful,’ said Mr. Pickwick, stopping his ears.\n\n‘Make haste, Mr. Lowten,’ Perker called out; ‘we shall have the panels beaten in.’\n\nMr. Lowten, who was washing his hands in a dark closet, hurried to the door, and turning the handle, beheld the appearance which is described in the next chapter.\n\nCHAPTER LIV. CONTAINING SOME PARTICULARS RELATIVE TO THE DOUBLE KNOCK, AND OTHER MATTERS: AMONG WHICH CERTAIN INTERESTING DISCLOSURES RELATIVE TO MR. SNODGRASS AND A YOUNG LADY ARE BY NO MEANS IRRELEVANT TO THIS HISTORY\nThe object that presented itself to the eyes of the astonished clerk, was a boy--a wonderfully fat boy--habited as a serving lad, standing upright on the mat, with his eyes closed as if in sleep. He had never seen such a fat boy, in or out of a travelling caravan; and this, coupled with the calmness and repose of his appearance, so very different from what was reasonably to have been expected of the inflicter of such knocks, smote him with wonder.\n\n‘What’s the matter?’ inquired the clerk.\n\nThe extraordinary boy replied not a word; but he nodded once, and seemed, to the clerk’s imagination, to snore feebly.\n\n‘Where do you come from?’ inquired the clerk.\n\nThe boy made no sign. He breathed heavily, but in all other respects was motionless.\n\nThe clerk repeated the question thrice, and receiving no answer, prepared to shut the door, when the boy suddenly opened his eyes, winked several times, sneezed once, and raised his hand as if to repeat the knocking. Finding the door open, he stared about him with astonishment, and at length fixed his eyes on Mr. Lowten’s face.\n\n‘What the devil do you knock in that way for?’ inquired the clerk angrily.\n\n‘Which way?’ said the boy, in a slow and sleepy voice.\n\n‘Why, like forty hackney-coachmen,’ replied the clerk.\n\n‘Because master said, I wasn’t to leave off knocking till they opened the door, for fear I should go to sleep,’ said the boy.\n\n‘Well,’ said the clerk, ‘what message have you brought?’\n\n‘He’s downstairs,’ rejoined the boy.\n\n‘Who?’\n\n‘Master. He wants to know whether you’re at home.’\n\nMr. Lowten bethought himself, at this juncture, of looking out of the window. Seeing an open carriage with a hearty old gentleman in it, looking up very anxiously, he ventured to beckon him; on which, the old gentleman jumped out directly.\n\n‘That’s your master in the carriage, I suppose?’ said Lowten.\n\nThe boy nodded.\n\nAll further inquiries were superseded by the appearance of old Wardle, who, running upstairs and just recognising Lowten, passed at once into Mr. Perker’s room.\n\n‘Pickwick!’ said the old gentleman. ‘Your hand, my boy! Why have I never heard until the day before yesterday of your suffering yourself to be cooped up in jail? And why did you let him do it, Perker?’\n\n‘I couldn’t help it, my dear Sir,’ replied Perker, with a smile and a pinch of snuff; ‘you know how obstinate he is?’\n\n‘Of course I do; of course I do,’ replied the old gentleman. ‘I am heartily glad to see him, notwithstanding. I will not lose sight of him again, in a hurry.’\n\nWith these words, Wardle shook Mr. Pickwick’s hand once more, and, having done the same by Perker, threw himself into an arm-chair, his jolly red face shining again with smiles and health.\n\n‘Well!’ said Wardle. ‘Here are pretty goings on--a pinch of your snuff, Perker, my boy--never were such times, eh?’\n\n‘What do you mean?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Mean!’ replied Wardle. ‘Why, I think the girls are all running mad; that’s no news, you’ll say? Perhaps it’s not; but it’s true, for all that.’\n\n‘You have not come up to London, of all places in the world, to tell us that, my dear Sir, have you?’ inquired Perker.\n\n‘No, not altogether,’ replied Wardle; ‘though it was the main cause of my coming. How’s Arabella?’\n\n‘Very well,’ replied Mr. Pickwick, ‘and will be delighted to see you, I am sure.’\n\n‘Black-eyed little jilt!’ replied Wardle. ‘I had a great idea of marrying her myself, one of these odd days. But I am glad of it too, very glad.’\n\n‘How did the intelligence reach you?’ asked Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Oh, it came to my girls, of course,’ replied Wardle. ‘Arabella wrote, the day before yesterday, to say she had made a stolen match without her husband’s father’s consent, and so you had gone down to get it when his refusing it couldn’t prevent the match, and all the rest of it. I thought it a very good time to say something serious to my girls; so I said what a dreadful thing it was that children should marry without their parents’ consent, and so forth; but, bless your hearts, I couldn’t make the least impression upon them. They thought it such a much more dreadful thing that there should have been a wedding without bridesmaids, that I might as well have preached to Joe himself.’\n\nHere the old gentleman stopped to laugh; and having done so to his heart’s content, presently resumed--\n\n‘But this is not the best of it, it seems. This is only half the love- making and plotting that have been going forward. We have been walking on mines for the last six months, and they’re sprung at last.’\n\n‘What do you mean?’ exclaimed Mr. Pickwick, turning pale; ‘no other secret marriage, I hope?’\n\n‘No, no,’ replied old Wardle; ‘not so bad as that; no.’\n\n‘What then?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick; ‘am I interested in it?’\n\n‘Shall I answer that question, Perker?’ said Wardle.\n\n‘If you don’t commit yourself by doing so, my dear Sir.’\n\n‘Well then, you are,’ said Wardle.\n\n‘How?’ asked Mr. Pickwick anxiously. ‘In what way?’\n\n‘Really,’ replied Wardle, ‘you’re such a fiery sort of a young fellow that I am almost afraid to tell you; but, however, if Perker will sit between us to prevent mischief, I’ll venture.’\n\nHaving closed the room door, and fortified himself with another application to Perker’s snuff-box, the old gentleman proceeded with his great disclosure in these words--\n\n‘The fact is, that my daughter Bella--Bella, who married young Trundle, you know.’\n\n‘Yes, yes, we know,’ said Mr. Pickwick impatiently.\n\n‘Don’t alarm me at the very beginning. My daughter Bella--Emily having gone to bed with a headache after she had read Arabella’s letter to me-- sat herself down by my side the other evening, and began to talk over this marriage affair. “Well, pa,” she says, “what do you think of it?” “Why, my dear,” I said, “I suppose it’s all very well; I hope it’s for the best.” I answered in this way because I was sitting before the fire at the time, drinking my grog rather thoughtfully, and I knew my throwing in an undecided word now and then, would induce her to continue talking. Both my girls are pictures of their dear mother, and as I grow old I like to sit with only them by me; for their voices and looks carry me back to the happiest period of my life, and make me, for the moment, as young as I used to be then, though not quite so light-hearted. “It’s quite a marriage of affection, pa,” said Bella, after a short silence. “Yes, my dear,” said I, “but such marriages do not always turn out the happiest.”’\n\n‘I question that, mind!’ interposed Mr. Pickwick warmly.\n\n‘Very good,’ responded Wardle, ‘question anything you like when it’s your turn to speak, but don’t interrupt me.’\n\n‘I beg your pardon,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Granted,’ replied Wardle. ‘“I am sorry to hear you express your opinion against marriages of affection, pa,” said Bella, colouring a little. “I was wrong; I ought not to have said so, my dear, either,” said I, patting her cheek as kindly as a rough old fellow like me could pat it, “for your mother’s was one, and so was yours.” “It’s not that I meant, pa,” said Bella. “The fact is, pa, I wanted to speak to you about Emily.”’\n\nMr. Pickwick started.\n\n‘What’s the matter now?’ inquired Wardle, stopping in his narrative.\n\n‘Nothing,’ replied Mr. Pickwick. ‘Pray go on.’\n\n‘I never could spin out a story,’ said Wardle abruptly. ‘It must come out, sooner or later, and it’ll save us all a great deal of time if it comes at once. The long and the short of it is, then, that Bella at last mustered up courage to tell me that Emily was very unhappy; that she and your young friend Snodgrass had been in constant correspondence and communication ever since last Christmas; that she had very dutifully made up her mind to run away with him, in laudable imitation of her old friend and school-fellow; but that having some compunctions of conscience on the subject, inasmuch as I had always been rather kindly disposed to both of them, they had thought it better in the first instance to pay me the compliment of asking whether I would have any objection to their being married in the usual matter-of-fact manner. There now, Mr. Pickwick, if you can make it convenient to reduce your eyes to their usual size again, and to let me hear what you think we ought to do, I shall feel rather obliged to you!’\n\nThe testy manner in which the hearty old gentleman uttered this last sentence was not wholly unwarranted; for Mr. Pickwick’s face had settled down into an expression of blank amazement and perplexity, quite curious to behold.\n\n‘Snodgrass!--since last Christmas!’ were the first broken words that issued from the lips of the confounded gentleman.\n\n‘Since last Christmas,’ replied Wardle; ‘that’s plain enough, and very bad spectacles we must have worn, not to have discovered it before.’\n\n‘I don’t understand it,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ruminating; ‘I cannot really understand it.’\n\n‘It’s easy enough to understand it,’ replied the choleric old gentleman. ‘If you had been a younger man, you would have been in the secret long ago; and besides,’ added Wardle, after a moment’s hesitation, ‘the truth is, that, knowing nothing of this matter, I have rather pressed Emily for four or five months past, to receive favourably (if she could; I would never attempt to force a girl’s inclinations) the addresses of a young gentleman down in our neighbourhood. I have no doubt that, girl- like, to enhance her own value and increase the ardour of Mr. Snodgrass, she has represented this matter in very glowing colours, and that they have both arrived at the conclusion that they are a terribly-persecuted pair of unfortunates, and have no resource but clandestine matrimony, or charcoal. Now the question is, what’s to be done?’\n\n‘What have you done?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick.\n‘I!’\n\n‘I mean what did you do when your married daughter told you this?’\n\n‘Oh, I made a fool of myself of course,’ rejoined Wardle.\n\n‘Just so,’ interposed Perker, who had accompanied this dialogue with sundry twitchings of his watch-chain, vindictive rubbings of his nose, and other symptoms of impatience. ‘That’s very natural; but how?’\n\n‘I went into a great passion and frightened my mother into a fit,’ said Wardle.\n\n‘That was judicious,’ remarked Perker; ‘and what else?’\n\n‘I fretted and fumed all next day, and raised a great disturbance,’ rejoined the old gentleman. ‘At last I got tired of rendering myself unpleasant and making everybody miserable; so I hired a carriage at Muggleton, and, putting my own horses in it, came up to town, under pretence of bringing Emily to see Arabella.’\n\n‘Miss Wardle is with you, then?’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘To be sure she is,’ replied Wardle. ‘She is at Osborne’s Hotel in the Adelphi at this moment, unless your enterprising friend has run away with her since I came out this morning.’\n\n‘You are reconciled then?’ said Perker.\n\n‘Not a bit of it,’ answered Wardle; ‘she has been crying and moping ever since, except last night, between tea and supper, when she made a great parade of writing a letter that I pretended to take no notice of.’\n\n‘You want my advice in this matter, I suppose?’ said Perker, looking from the musing face of Mr. Pickwick to the eager countenance of Wardle, and taking several consecutive pinches of his favourite stimulant.\n\n‘I suppose so,’ said Wardle, looking at Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Certainly,’ replied that gentleman.\n\n‘Well then,’ said Perker, rising and pushing his chair back, ‘my advice is, that you both walk away together, or ride away, or get away by some means or other, for I’m tired of you, and just talk this matter over between you. If you have not settled it by the next time I see you, I’ll tell you what to do.’\n\n‘This is satisfactory,’ said Wardle, hardly knowing whether to smile or be offended.\n\n‘Pooh, pooh, my dear Sir,’ returned Perker. ‘I know you both a great deal better than you know yourselves. You have settled it already, to all intents and purposes.’\n\nThus expressing himself, the little gentleman poked his snuff-box first into the chest of Mr. Pickwick, and then into the waistcoat of Mr. Wardle, upon which they all three laughed, especially the two last-named gentlemen, who at once shook hands again, without any obvious or particular reason.\n\n‘You dine with me to-day,’ said Wardle to Perker, as he showed them out.\n\n‘Can’t promise, my dear Sir, can’t promise,’ replied Perker. ‘I’ll look in, in the evening, at all events.’\n\n‘I shall expect you at five,’ said Wardle. ‘Now, Joe!’ And Joe having been at length awakened, the two friends departed in Mr. Wardle’s carriage, which in common humanity had a dickey behind for the fat boy, who, if there had been a footboard instead, would have rolled off and killed himself in his very first nap.\n\nDriving to the George and Vulture, they found that Arabella and her maid had sent for a hackney-coach immediately on the receipt of a short note from Emily announcing her arrival in town, and had proceeded straight to the Adelphi. As Wardle had business to transact in the city, they sent the carriage and the fat boy to his hotel, with the information that he and Mr. Pickwick would return together to dinner at five o’clock.\n\nCharged with this message, the fat boy returned, slumbering as peaceably in his dickey, over the stones, as if it had been a down bed on watch springs. By some extraordinary miracle he awoke of his own accord, when the coach stopped, and giving himself a good shake to stir up his faculties, went upstairs to execute his commission.\n\nNow, whether the shake had jumbled the fat boy’s faculties together, instead of arranging them in proper order, or had roused such a quantity of new ideas within him as to render him oblivious of ordinary forms and ceremonies, or (which is also possible) had proved unsuccessful in preventing his falling asleep as he ascended the stairs, it is an undoubted fact that he walked into the sitting-room without previously knocking at the door; and so beheld a gentleman with his arms clasping his young mistress’s waist, sitting very lovingly by her side on a sofa, while Arabella and her pretty handmaid feigned to be absorbed in looking out of a window at the other end of the room. At the sight of this phenomenon, the fat boy uttered an interjection, the ladies a scream, and the gentleman an oath, almost simultaneously.\n\n‘Wretched creature, what do you want here?’ said the gentleman, who it is needless to say was Mr. Snodgrass.\n\nTo this the fat boy, considerably terrified, briefly responded, ‘Missis.’\n\n‘What do you want me for,’ inquired Emily, turning her head aside, ‘you stupid creature?’\n\n‘Master and Mr. Pickwick is a-going to dine here at five,’ replied the fat boy.\n\n‘Leave the room!’ said Mr. Snodgrass, glaring upon the bewildered youth.\n\n‘No, no, no,’ added Emily hastily. ‘Bella, dear, advise me.’\n\nUpon this, Emily and Mr. Snodgrass, and Arabella and Mary, crowded into a corner, and conversed earnestly in whispers for some minutes, during which the fat boy dozed.\n\n‘Joe,’ said Arabella, at length, looking round with a most bewitching smile, ‘how do you do, Joe?’\n\n‘Joe,’ said Emily, ‘you’re a very good boy; I won’t forget you, Joe.’\n\n‘Joe,’ said Mr. Snodgrass, advancing to the astonished youth, and seizing his hand, ‘I didn’t know you before. There’s five shillings for you, Joe!”\n\n‘I’ll owe you five, Joe,’ said Arabella, ‘for old acquaintance sake, you know;’ and another most captivating smile was bestowed upon the corpulent intruder.\n\nThe fat boy’s perception being slow, he looked rather puzzled at first to account for this sudden prepossession in his favour, and stared about him in a very alarming manner. At length his broad face began to show symptoms of a grin of proportionately broad dimensions; and then, thrusting half-a-crown into each of his pockets, and a hand and wrist after it, he burst into a horse laugh: being for the first and only time in his existence.\n\n‘He understands us, I see,’ said Arabella.\n\n‘He had better have something to eat, immediately,’ remarked Emily.\n\nThe fat boy almost laughed again when he heard this suggestion. Mary, after a little more whispering, tripped forth from the group and said--\n\n‘I am going to dine with you to-day, sir, if you have no objection.’\n\n‘This way,’ said the fat boy eagerly. ‘There is such a jolly meat-pie!’\n\nWith these words, the fat boy led the way downstairs; his pretty companion captivating all the waiters and angering all the chambermaids as she followed him to the eating-room.\n\nThere was the meat-pie of which the youth had spoken so feelingly, and there were, moreover, a steak, and a dish of potatoes, and a pot of porter.\n\n‘Sit down,’ said the fat boy. ‘Oh, my eye, how prime! I am so hungry.’\n\nHaving apostrophised his eye, in a species of rapture, five or six times, the youth took the head of the little table, and Mary seated herself at the bottom.\n\n‘Will you have some of this?’ said the fat boy, plunging into the pie up to the very ferules of the knife and fork.\n\n‘A little, if you please,’ replied Mary.\n\nThe fat boy assisted Mary to a little, and himself to a great deal, and was just going to begin eating when he suddenly laid down his knife and fork, leaned forward in his chair, and letting his hands, with the knife and fork in them, fall on his knees, said, very slowly--\n\n‘I say! How nice you look!’\n\nThis was said in an admiring manner, and was, so far, gratifying; but still there was enough of the cannibal in the young gentleman’s eyes to render the compliment a double one.\n\n‘Dear me, Joseph,’ said Mary, affecting to blush, ‘what do you mean?’\n\nThe fat boy, gradually recovering his former position, replied with a heavy sigh, and, remaining thoughtful for a few moments, drank a long draught of the porter. Having achieved this feat, he sighed again, and applied himself assiduously to the pie.\n\n‘What a nice young lady Miss Emily is!’ said Mary, after a long silence.\n\nThe fat boy had by this time finished the pie. He fixed his eyes on Mary, and replied--\n\n‘I knows a nicerer.’\n\n‘Indeed!’ said Mary.\n\n‘Yes, indeed!’ replied the fat boy, with unwonted vivacity.\n\n‘What’s her name?’ inquired Mary.\n\n‘What’s yours?’\n\n‘Mary.’\n\n‘So’s hers,’ said the fat boy. ‘You’re her.’ The boy grinned to add point to the compliment, and put his eyes into something between a squint and a cast, which there is reason to believe he intended for an ogle.\n\n‘You mustn’t talk to me in that way,’ said Mary; ‘you don’t mean it.’\n\n‘Don’t I, though?’ replied the fat boy. ‘I say?’\n\n‘Well?’\n\n‘Are you going to come here regular?’\n\n‘No,’ rejoined Mary, shaking her head, ‘I’m going away again to-night. Why?’\n\n‘Oh,’ said the fat boy, in a tone of strong feeling; ‘how we should have enjoyed ourselves at meals, if you had been!’\n\n‘I might come here sometimes, perhaps, to see you,’ said Mary, plaiting the table-cloth in assumed coyness, ‘if you would do me a favour.’\n\nThe fat boy looked from the pie-dish to the steak, as if he thought a favour must be in a manner connected with something to eat; and then took out one of the half-crowns and glanced at it nervously.\n\n‘Don’t you understand me?’ said Mary, looking slily in his fat face.\n\nAgain he looked at the half-crown, and said faintly, ‘No.’\n\n‘The ladies want you not to say anything to the old gentleman about the young gentleman having been upstairs; and I want you too.’\n\n‘Is that all?’ said the fat boy, evidently very much relieved, as he pocketed the half-crown again. ‘Of course I ain’t a-going to.’\n\n‘You see,’ said Mary, ‘Mr. Snodgrass is very fond of Miss Emily, and Miss Emily’s very fond of him, and if you were to tell about it, the old gentleman would carry you all away miles into the country, where you’d see nobody.’\n\n‘No, no, I won’t tell,’ said the fat boy stoutly.\n\n‘That’s a dear,’ said Mary. ‘Now it’s time I went upstairs, and got my lady ready for dinner.’\n\n‘Don’t go yet,’ urged the fat boy.\n\n‘I must,’ replied Mary. ‘Good-bye, for the present.’\n\nThe fat boy, with elephantine playfulness, stretched out his arms to ravish a kiss; but as it required no great agility to elude him, his fair enslaver had vanished before he closed them again; upon which the apathetic youth ate a pound or so of steak with a sentimental countenance, and fell fast asleep.\n\nThere was so much to say upstairs, and there were so many plans to concert for elopement and matrimony in the event of old Wardle continuing to be cruel, that it wanted only half an hour of dinner when Mr. Snodgrass took his final adieu. The ladies ran to Emily’s bedroom to dress, and the lover, taking up his hat, walked out of the room. He had scarcely got outside the door, when he heard Wardle’s voice talking loudly, and looking over the banisters beheld him, followed by some other gentlemen, coming straight upstairs. Knowing nothing of the house, Mr. Snodgrass in his confusion stepped hastily back into the room he had just quitted, and passing thence into an inner apartment (Mr. Wardle’s bedchamber), closed the door softly, just as the persons he had caught a glimpse of entered the sitting-room. These were Mr. Wardle, Mr. Pickwick, Mr. Nathaniel Winkle, and Mr. Benjamin Allen, whom he had no difficulty in recognising by their voices.\n\n‘Very lucky I had the presence of mind to avoid them,’ thought Mr. Snodgrass with a smile, and walking on tiptoe to another door near the bedside; ‘this opens into the same passage, and I can walk quietly and comfortably away.’\n\nThere was only one obstacle to his walking quietly and comfortably away, which was that the door was locked and the key gone.\n\n‘Let us have some of your best wine to-day, waiter,’ said old Wardle, rubbing his hands.\n\n‘You shall have some of the very best, sir,’ replied the waiter.\n\n‘Let the ladies know we have come in.’\n\n‘Yes, Sir.’\n\nDevoutly and ardently did Mr. Snodgrass wish that the ladies could know he had come in. He ventured once to whisper, ‘Waiter!’ through the keyhole, but the probability of the wrong waiter coming to his relief, flashed upon his mind, together with a sense of the strong resemblance between his own situation and that in which another gentleman had been recently found in a neighbouring hotel (an account of whose misfortunes had appeared under the head of ‘Police’ in that morning’s paper), he sat himself on a portmanteau, and trembled violently.\n\n‘We won’t wait a minute for Perker,’ said Wardle, looking at his watch; ‘he is always exact. He will be here, in time, if he means to come; and if he does not, it’s of no use waiting. Ha! Arabella!’\n\n‘My sister!’ exclaimed Mr. Benjamin Allen, folding her in a most romantic embrace.\n\n‘Oh, Ben, dear, how you do smell of tobacco,’ said Arabella, rather overcome by this mark of affection.\n\n‘Do I?’ said Mr. Benjamin Allen. ‘Do I, Bella? Well, perhaps I do.’\n\nPerhaps he did, having just left a pleasant little smoking-party of twelve medical students, in a small back parlour with a large fire.\n\n‘But I am delighted to see you,’ said Mr. Ben Allen. ‘Bless you, Bella!’\n\n‘There,’ said Arabella, bending forward to kiss her brother; ‘don’t take hold of me again, Ben, dear, because you tumble me so.’\n\nAt this point of the reconciliation, Mr. Ben Allen allowed his feelings and the cigars and porter to overcome him, and looked round upon the beholders with damp spectacles.\n\n‘Is nothing to be said to me?’ cried Wardle, with open arms.\n\n‘A great deal,’ whispered Arabella, as she received the old gentleman’s hearty caress and congratulation. ‘You are a hard-hearted, unfeeling, cruel monster.’\n\n‘You are a little rebel,’ replied Wardle, in the same tone, ‘and I am afraid I shall be obliged to forbid you the house. People like you, who get married in spite of everybody, ought not to be let loose on society. But come!’ added the old gentleman aloud, ‘here’s the dinner; you shall sit by me. Joe; why, damn the boy, he’s awake!’\n\nTo the great distress of his master, the fat boy was indeed in a state of remarkable vigilance, his eyes being wide open, and looking as if they intended to remain so. There was an alacrity in his manner, too, which was equally unaccountable; every time his eyes met those of Emily or Arabella, he smirked and grinned; once, Wardle could have sworn, he saw him wink.\n\nThis alteration in the fat boy’s demeanour originated in his increased sense of his own importance, and the dignity he acquired from having been taken into the confidence of the young ladies; and the smirks, and grins, and winks were so many condescending assurances that they might depend upon his fidelity. As these tokens were rather calculated to awaken suspicion than allay it, and were somewhat embarrassing besides, they were occasionally answered by a frown or shake of the head from Arabella, which the fat boy, considering as hints to be on his guard, expressed his perfect understanding of, by smirking, grinning, and winking, with redoubled assiduity.\n\n‘Joe,’ said Mr. Wardle, after an unsuccessful search in all his pockets, ‘is my snuff-box on the sofa?’\n\n‘No, sir,’ replied the fat boy.\n\n‘Oh, I recollect; I left it on my dressing-table this morning,’ said Wardle. ‘Run into the next room and fetch it.’\n\nThe fat boy went into the next room; and, having been absent about a minute, returned with the snuff-box, and the palest face that ever a fat boy wore.\n\n‘What’s the matter with the boy?’ exclaimed Wardle.\n\n‘Nothen’s the matter with me,’ replied Joe nervously.\n\n‘Have you been seeing any spirits?’ inquired the old gentleman.\n\n‘Or taking any?’ added Ben Allen.\n\n‘I think you’re right,’ whispered Wardle across the table. ‘He is intoxicated, I’m sure.’\n\nBen Allen replied that he thought he was; and, as that gentleman had seen a vast deal of the disease in question, Wardle was confirmed in an impression which had been hovering about his mind for half an hour, and at once arrived at the conclusion that the fat boy was drunk.\n\n‘Just keep your eye upon him for a few minutes,’ murmured Wardle. ‘We shall soon find out whether he is or not.’\n\nThe unfortunate youth had only interchanged a dozen words with Mr. Snodgrass, that gentleman having implored him to make a private appeal to some friend to release him, and then pushed him out with the snuff- box, lest his prolonged absence should lead to a discovery. He ruminated a little with a most disturbed expression of face, and left the room in search of Mary.\n\nBut Mary had gone home after dressing her mistress, and the fat boy came back again more disturbed than before.\n\nWardle and Mr. Ben Allen exchanged glances.\n\n‘Joe!’ said Wardle.\n\n‘Yes, sir.’\n\n‘What did you go away for?’\n\nThe fat boy looked hopelessly in the face of everybody at table, and stammered out that he didn’t know.\n\n‘Oh,’ said Wardle, ‘you don’t know, eh? Take this cheese to Mr. Pickwick.’\n\nNow, Mr. Pickwick being in the very best health and spirits, had been making himself perfectly delightful all dinner-time, and was at this moment engaged in an energetic conversation with Emily and Mr. Winkle; bowing his head, courteously, in the emphasis of his discourse, gently waving his left hand to lend force to his observations, and all glowing with placid smiles. He took a piece of cheese from the plate, and was on the point of turning round to renew the conversation, when the fat boy, stooping so as to bring his head on a level with that of Mr. Pickwick, pointed with his thumb over his shoulder, and made the most horrible and hideous face that was ever seen out of a Christmas pantomime.\n\n‘Dear me!’ said Mr. Pickwick, starting, ‘what a very--Eh?’ He stopped, for the fat boy had drawn himself up, and was, or pretended to be, fast asleep.\n\n‘What’s the matter?’ inquired Wardle.\n\n‘This is such an extremely singular lad!’ replied Mr. Pickwick, looking uneasily at the boy. ‘It seems an odd thing to say, but upon my word I am afraid that, at times, he is a little deranged.’\n\n‘Oh! Mr. Pickwick, pray don’t say so,’ cried Emily and Arabella, both at once.\n\n‘I am not certain, of course,’ said Mr. Pickwick, amidst profound silence and looks of general dismay; ‘but his manner to me this moment really was very alarming. Oh!’ ejaculated Mr. Pickwick, suddenly jumping up with a short scream. ‘I beg your pardon, ladies, but at that moment he ran some sharp instrument into my leg. Really, he is not safe.’\n\n‘He’s drunk,’ roared old Wardle passionately. ‘Ring the bell! Call the waiters! He’s drunk.’\n\n‘I ain’t,’ said the fat boy, falling on his knees as his master seized him by the collar. ‘I ain’t drunk.’\n\n‘Then you’re mad; that’s worse. Call the waiters,’ said the old gentleman.\n\n‘I ain’t mad; I’m sensible,’ rejoined the fat boy, beginning to cry.\n\n‘Then, what the devil did you run sharp instruments into Mr. Pickwick’s legs for?’ inquired Wardle angrily.\n\n‘He wouldn’t look at me,’ replied the boy. ‘I wanted to speak to him.’\n\n‘What did you want to say?’ asked half a dozen voices at once.\n\nThe fat boy gasped, looked at the bedroom door, gasped again, and wiped two tears away with the knuckle of each of his forefingers.\n\n‘What did you want to say?’ demanded Wardle, shaking him.\n\n‘Stop!’ said Mr. Pickwick; ‘allow me. What did you wish to communicate to me, my poor boy?’\n\n‘I want to whisper to you,’ replied the fat boy.\n\n‘You want to bite his ear off, I suppose,’ said Wardle. ‘Don’t come near him; he’s vicious; ring the bell, and let him be taken downstairs.’\n\nJust as Mr. Winkle caught the bell-rope in his hand, it was arrested by a general expression of astonishment; the captive lover, his face burning with confusion, suddenly walked in from the bedroom, and made a comprehensive bow to the company.\n\n‘Hollo!’ cried Wardle, releasing the fat boy’s collar, and staggering back. ‘What’s this?’\n\n‘I have been concealed in the next room, sir, since you returned,’ explained Mr. Snodgrass.\n\n‘Emily, my girl,’ said Wardle reproachfully, ‘I detest meanness and deceit; this is unjustifiable and indelicate in the highest degree. I don’t deserve this at your hands, Emily, indeed!’\n\n‘Dear papa,’ said Emily, ‘Arabella knows--everybody here knows--Joe knows--that I was no party to this concealment. Augustus, for Heaven’s sake, explain it!’\n\nMr. Snodgrass, who had only waited for a hearing, at once recounted how he had been placed in his then distressing predicament; how the fear of giving rise to domestic dissensions had alone prompted him to avoid Mr. Wardle on his entrance; how he merely meant to depart by another door, but, finding it locked, had been compelled to stay against his will. It was a painful situation to be placed in; but he now regretted it the less, inasmuch as it afforded him an opportunity of acknowledging, before their mutual friends, that he loved Mr. Wardle’s daughter deeply and sincerely; that he was proud to avow that the feeling was mutual; and that if thousands of miles were placed between them, or oceans rolled their waters, he could never for an instant forget those happy days, when first--et cetera, et cetera.\n\nHaving delivered himself to this effect, Mr. Snodgrass bowed again, looked into the crown of his hat, and stepped towards the door.\n\n‘Stop!’ shouted Wardle. ‘Why, in the name of all that’s--’\n\n‘Inflammable,’ mildly suggested Mr. Pickwick, who thought something worse was coming.\n\n‘Well--that’s inflammable,’ said Wardle, adopting the substitute; ‘couldn’t you say all this to me in the first instance?’\n\n‘Or confide in me?’ added Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Dear, dear,’ said Arabella, taking up the defence, ‘what is the use of asking all that now, especially when you know you had set your covetous old heart on a richer son-in-law, and are so wild and fierce besides, that everybody is afraid of you, except me? Shake hands with him, and order him some dinner, for goodness gracious’ sake, for he looks half starved; and pray have your wine up at once, for you’ll not be tolerable until you have taken two bottles at least.’\n\nThe worthy old gentleman pulled Arabella’s ear, kissed her without the smallest scruple, kissed his daughter also with great affection, and shook Mr. Snodgrass warmly by the hand.\n\n‘She is right on one point at all events,’ said the old gentleman cheerfully. ‘Ring for the wine!’\n\nThe wine came, and Perker came upstairs at the same moment. Mr. Snodgrass had dinner at a side table, and, when he had despatched it, drew his chair next Emily, without the smallest opposition on the old gentleman’s part.\n\nThe evening was excellent. Little Mr. Perker came out wonderfully, told various comic stories, and sang a serious song which was almost as funny as the anecdotes. Arabella was very charming, Mr. Wardle very jovial, Mr. Pickwick very harmonious, Mr. Ben Allen very uproarious, the lovers very silent, Mr. Winkle very talkative, and all of them very happy.\n\nCHAPTER LV. MR. SOLOMON PELL, ASSISTED BY A SELECT COMMITTEE OF COACHMEN, ARRANGES THE AFFAIRS OF THE ELDER MR. WELLER\nSamivel,’ said Mr. Weller, accosting his son on the morning after the funeral, ‘I’ve found it, Sammy. I thought it wos there.’\n\n‘Thought wot wos there?’ inquired Sam.\n\n‘Your mother-in-law’s vill, Sammy,’ replied Mr. Weller. ‘In wirtue o’ vich, them arrangements is to be made as I told you on, last night, respectin’ the funs.’\n\n‘Wot, didn’t she tell you were it wos?’ inquired Sam.\n\n‘Not a bit on it, Sammy,’ replied Mr. Weller. ‘We wos a adjestin’ our little differences, and I wos a-cheerin’ her spirits and bearin’ her up, so that I forgot to ask anythin’ about it. I don’t know as I should ha’ done it, indeed, if I had remembered it,’ added Mr. Weller, ‘for it’s a rum sort o’ thing, Sammy, to go a-hankerin’ arter anybody’s property, ven you’re assistin’ ‘em in illness. It’s like helping an outside passenger up, ven he’s been pitched off a coach, and puttin’ your hand in his pocket, vile you ask him, vith a sigh, how he finds his-self, Sammy.’\n\nWith this figurative illustration of his meaning, Mr. Weller unclasped his pocket-book, and drew forth a dirty sheet of letter-paper, on which were inscribed various characters crowded together in remarkable confusion.\n\n‘This here is the dockyment, Sammy,’ said Mr. Weller. ‘I found it in the little black tea-pot, on the top shelf o’ the bar closet. She used to keep bank-notes there, ‘fore she vos married, Samivel. I’ve seen her take the lid off, to pay a bill, many and many a time. Poor creetur, she might ha’ filled all the tea-pots in the house vith vills, and not have inconwenienced herself neither, for she took wery little of anythin’ in that vay lately, ‘cept on the temperance nights, ven they just laid a foundation o’ tea to put the spirits atop on!’\n\n‘What does it say?’ inquired Sam.\n\n‘Jist vot I told you, my boy,’ rejoined his parent. ‘Two hundred pound vurth o’ reduced counsels to my son-in-law, Samivel, and all the rest o’ my property, of ev’ry kind and description votsoever, to my husband, Mr. Tony Veller, who I appint as my sole eggzekiter.’\n\n‘That’s all, is it?’ said Sam.\n\n‘That’s all,’ replied Mr. Weller. ‘And I s’pose as it’s all right and satisfactory to you and me as is the only parties interested, ve may as vell put this bit o’ paper into the fire.’\n\n‘Wot are you a-doin’ on, you lunatic?’ said Sam, snatching the paper away, as his parent, in all innocence, stirred the fire preparatory to suiting the action to the word. ‘You’re a nice eggzekiter, you are.’\n\n‘Vy not?’ inquired Mr. Weller, looking sternly round, with the poker in his hand.\n\n‘Vy not?’ exclaimed Sam. ‘’Cos it must be proved, and probated, and swore to, and all manner o’ formalities.’\n\n‘You don’t mean that?’ said Mr. Weller, laying down the poker.\n\nSam buttoned the will carefully in a side pocket; intimating by a look, meanwhile, that he did mean it, and very seriously too.\n\n‘Then I’ll tell you wot it is,’ said Mr. Weller, after a short meditation, ‘this is a case for that ‘ere confidential pal o’ the Chancellorship’s. Pell must look into this, Sammy. He’s the man for a difficult question at law. Ve’ll have this here brought afore the Solvent Court, directly, Samivel.’\n\n‘I never did see such a addle-headed old creetur!’ exclaimed Sam irritably; ‘Old Baileys, and Solvent Courts, and alleybis, and ev’ry species o’ gammon alvays a-runnin’ through his brain. You’d better get your out o’ door clothes on, and come to town about this bisness, than stand a-preachin’ there about wot you don’t understand nothin’ on.’\n\n‘Wery good, Sammy,’ replied Mr. Weller, ‘I’m quite agreeable to anythin’ as vill hexpedite business, Sammy. But mind this here, my boy, nobody but Pell--nobody but Pell as a legal adwiser.’\n\n‘I don’t want anybody else,’ replied Sam. ‘Now, are you a-comin’?’\n\n‘Vait a minit, Sammy,’ replied Mr. Weller, who, having tied his shawl with the aid of a small glass that hung in the window, was now, by dint of the most wonderful exertions, struggling into his upper garments. ‘Vait a minit’ Sammy; ven you grow as old as your father, you von’t get into your veskit quite as easy as you do now, my boy.’\n\n‘If I couldn’t get into it easier than that, I’m blessed if I’d vear vun at all,’ rejoined his son.\n\n‘You think so now,’ said Mr. Weller, with the gravity of age, ‘but you’ll find that as you get vider, you’ll get viser. Vidth and visdom, Sammy, alvays grows together.’\n\nAs Mr. Weller delivered this infallible maxim--the result of many years’ personal experience and observation--he contrived, by a dexterous twist of his body, to get the bottom button of his coat to perform its office. Having paused a few seconds to recover breath, he brushed his hat with his elbow, and declared himself ready.\n\n‘As four heads is better than two, Sammy,’ said Mr. Weller, as they drove along the London Road in the chaise-cart, ‘and as all this here property is a wery great temptation to a legal gen’l’m’n, ve’ll take a couple o’ friends o’ mine vith us, as’ll be wery soon down upon him if he comes anythin’ irreg’lar; two o’ them as saw you to the Fleet that day. They’re the wery best judges,’ added Mr. Weller, in a half-whisper- -’the wery best judges of a horse, you ever know’d.’\n\n‘And of a lawyer too?’ inquired Sam.\n\n‘The man as can form a ackerate judgment of a animal, can form a ackerate judgment of anythin’,’ replied his father, so dogmatically, that Sam did not attempt to controvert the position.\n\nIn pursuance of this notable resolution, the services of the mottled- faced gentleman and of two other very fat coachmen--selected by Mr. Weller, probably, with a view to their width and consequent wisdom--were put into requisition; and this assistance having been secured, the party proceeded to the public-house in Portugal Street, whence a messenger was despatched to the Insolvent Court over the way, requiring Mr. Solomon Pell’s immediate attendance.\n\nThe messenger fortunately found Mr. Solomon Pell in court, regaling himself, business being rather slack, with a cold collation of an Abernethy biscuit and a saveloy. The message was no sooner whispered in his ear than he thrust them in his pocket among various professional documents, and hurried over the way with such alacrity that he reached the parlour before the messenger had even emancipated himself from the court.\n\n‘Gentlemen,’ said Mr. Pell, touching his hat, ‘my service to you all. I don’t say it to flatter you, gentlemen, but there are not five other men in the world, that I’d have come out of that court for, to-day.’\n\n‘So busy, eh?’ said Sam.\n\n‘Busy!’ replied Pell; ‘I’m completely sewn up, as my friend the late Lord Chancellor many a time used to say to me, gentlemen, when he came out from hearing appeals in the House of Lords. Poor fellow; he was very susceptible to fatigue; he used to feel those appeals uncommonly. I actually thought more than once that he’d have sunk under ‘em; I did, indeed.’\n\nHere Mr. Pell shook his head and paused; on which, the elder Mr. Weller, nudging his neighbour, as begging him to mark the attorney’s high connections, asked whether the duties in question produced any permanent ill effects on the constitution of his noble friend.\n\n‘I don’t think he ever quite recovered them,’ replied Pell; ‘in fact I’m sure he never did. “Pell,” he used to say to me many a time, “how the blazes you can stand the head-work you do, is a mystery to me.”--“Well,” I used to answer, “I hardly know how I do it, upon my life.”--“Pell,” he’d add, sighing, and looking at me with a little envy--friendly envy, you know, gentlemen, mere friendly envy; I never minded it--“Pell, you’re a wonder; a wonder.” Ah! you’d have liked him very much if you had known him, gentlemen. Bring me three-penn’orth of rum, my dear.’\n\nAddressing this latter remark to the waitress, in a tone of subdued grief, Mr. Pell sighed, looked at his shoes and the ceiling; and, the rum having by that time arrived, drank it up.\n\n‘However,’ said Pell, drawing a chair to the table, ‘a professional man has no right to think of his private friendships when his legal assistance is wanted. By the bye, gentlemen, since I saw you here before, we have had to weep over a very melancholy occurrence.’\n\nMr. Pell drew out a pocket-handkerchief, when he came to the word weep, but he made no further use of it than to wipe away a slight tinge of rum which hung upon his upper lip.\n\n‘I saw it in the ADVERTISER, Mr. Weller,’ continued Pell. ‘Bless my soul, not more than fifty-two! Dear me--only think.’\n\nThese indications of a musing spirit were addressed to the mottled-faced man, whose eyes Mr. Pell had accidentally caught; on which, the mottled- faced man, whose apprehension of matters in general was of a foggy nature, moved uneasily in his seat, and opined that, indeed, so far as that went, there was no saying how things was brought about; which observation, involving one of those subtle propositions which it is difficult to encounter in argument, was controverted by nobody.\n\n‘I have heard it remarked that she was a very fine woman, Mr. Weller,’ said Pell, in a sympathising manner.\n\n‘Yes, sir, she wos,’ replied the elder Mr. Weller, not much relishing this mode of discussing the subject, and yet thinking that the attorney, from his long intimacy with the late Lord Chancellor, must know best on all matters of polite breeding. ‘She wos a wery fine ‘ooman, sir, ven I first know’d her. She wos a widder, sir, at that time.’\n\n‘Now, it’s curious,’ said Pell, looking round with a sorrowful smile; ‘Mrs. Pell was a widow.’\n\n‘That’s very extraordinary,’ said the mottled-faced man.\n\n‘Well, it is a curious coincidence,’ said Pell.\n\n‘Not at all,’ gruffly remarked the elder Mr. Weller. ‘More widders is married than single wimin.’\n\n‘Very good, very good,’ said Pell, ‘you’re quite right, Mr. Weller. Mrs. Pell was a very elegant and accomplished woman; her manners were the theme of universal admiration in our neighbourhood. I was proud to see that woman dance; there was something so firm and dignified, and yet natural, in her motion. Her cutting, gentlemen, was simplicity itself. Ah! well, well! Excuse my asking the question, Mr. Samuel,’ continued the attorney in a lower voice, ‘was your mother-in-law tall?’\n\n‘Not wery,’ replied Sam.\n\n‘Mrs. Pell was a tall figure,’ said Pell, ‘a splendid woman, with a noble shape, and a nose, gentlemen, formed to command and be majestic. She was very much attached to me--very much--highly connected, too. Her mother’s brother, gentlemen, failed for eight hundred pounds, as a law stationer.’\n\n‘Vell,’ said Mr. Weller, who had grown rather restless during this discussion, ‘vith regard to bis’ness.’\n\nThe word was music to Pell’s ears. He had been revolving in his mind whether any business was to be transacted, or whether he had been merely invited to partake of a glass of brandy-and-water, or a bowl of punch, or any similar professional compliment, and now the doubt was set at rest without his appearing at all eager for its solution. His eyes glistened as he laid his hat on the table, and said--\n\n‘What is the business upon which--um? Either of these gentlemen wish to go through the court? We require an arrest; a friendly arrest will do, you know; we are all friends here, I suppose?’\n\n‘Give me the dockyment, Sammy,’ said Mr. Weller, taking the will from his son, who appeared to enjoy the interview amazingly. ‘Wot we rekvire, sir, is a probe o’ this here.’\n\n‘Probate, my dear Sir, probate,’ said Pell.\n\n‘Well, sir,’ replied Mr. Weller sharply, ‘probe and probe it, is wery much the same; if you don’t understand wot I mean, sir, I des-say I can find them as does.’\n\n‘No offence, I hope, Mr. Weller,’ said Pell meekly. ‘You are the executor, I see,’ he added, casting his eyes over the paper.\n\n‘I am, sir,’ replied Mr. Weller.\n\n‘These other gentlemen, I presume, are legatees, are they?’ inquired Pell, with a congratulatory smile.\n\n‘Sammy is a leg-at-ease,’ replied Mr. Weller; ‘these other gen’l’m’n is friends o’ mine, just come to see fair; a kind of umpires.’\n\n‘Oh!’ said Pell, ‘very good. I have no objections, I’m sure. I shall want a matter of five pound of you before I begin, ha! ha! ha!’\n\nIt being decided by the committee that the five pound might be advanced, Mr. Weller produced that sum; after which, a long consultation about nothing particular took place, in the course whereof Mr. Pell demonstrated to the perfect satisfaction of the gentlemen who saw fair, that unless the management of the business had been intrusted to him, it must all have gone wrong, for reasons not clearly made out, but no doubt sufficient. This important point being despatched, Mr. Pell refreshed himself with three chops, and liquids both malt and spirituous, at the expense of the estate; and then they all went away to Doctors’ Commons.\n\nThe next day there was another visit to Doctors’ Commons, and a great to-do with an attesting hostler, who, being inebriated, declined swearing anything but profane oaths, to the great scandal of a proctor and surrogate. Next week, there were more visits to Doctors’ Commons, and there was a visit to the Legacy Duty Office besides, and there were treaties entered into, for the disposal of the lease and business, and ratifications of the same, and inventories to be made out, and lunches to be taken, and dinners to be eaten, and so many profitable things to be done, and such a mass of papers accumulated that Mr. Solomon Pell, and the boy, and the blue bag to boot, all got so stout that scarcely anybody would have known them for the same man, boy, and bag, that had loitered about Portugal Street, a few days before.\n\nAt length all these weighty matters being arranged, a day was fixed for selling out and transferring the stock, and of waiting with that view upon Wilkins Flasher, Esquire, stock-broker, of somewhere near the bank, who had been recommended by Mr. Solomon Pell for the purpose.\n\nIt was a kind of festive occasion, and the parties were attired accordingly. Mr. Weller’s tops were newly cleaned, and his dress was arranged with peculiar care; the mottled-faced gentleman wore at his button-hole a full-sized dahlia with several leaves; and the coats of his two friends were adorned with nosegays of laurel and other evergreens. All three were habited in strict holiday costume; that is to say, they were wrapped up to the chins, and wore as many clothes as possible, which is, and has been, a stage-coachman’s idea of full dress ever since stage-coaches were invented.\n\nMr. Pell was waiting at the usual place of meeting at the appointed time; even he wore a pair of gloves and a clean shirt, much frayed at the collar and wristbands by frequent washings.\n\n‘A quarter to two,’ said Pell, looking at the parlour clock. ‘If we are with Mr. Flasher at a quarter past, we shall just hit the best time.’\n\n‘What should you say to a drop o’ beer, gen’l’m’n?’ suggested the mottled-faced man.\n\n‘And a little bit o’ cold beef,’ said the second coachman.\n\n‘Or a oyster,’ added the third, who was a hoarse gentleman, supported by very round legs.\n\n‘Hear, hear!’ said Pell; ‘to congratulate Mr. Weller, on his coming into possession of his property, eh? Ha! ha!’\n\n‘I’m quite agreeable, gen’l’m’n,’ answered Mr. Weller. ‘Sammy, pull the bell.’\n\nSammy complied; and the porter, cold beef, and oysters being promptly produced, the lunch was done ample justice to. Where everybody took so active a part, it is almost invidious to make a distinction; but if one individual evinced greater powers than another, it was the coachman with the hoarse voice, who took an imperial pint of vinegar with his oysters, without betraying the least emotion.\n\n‘Mr. Pell, Sir,’ said the elder Mr. Weller, stirring a glass of brandy- and-water, of which one was placed before every gentleman when the oyster shells were removed--‘Mr. Pell, Sir, it wos my intention to have proposed the funs on this occasion, but Samivel has vispered to me--’\n\nHere Mr. Samuel Weller, who had silently eaten his oysters with tranquil smiles, cried, ‘Hear!’ in a very loud voice.\n\n‘--Has vispered to me,’ resumed his father, ‘that it vould be better to dewote the liquor to vishin’ you success and prosperity, and thankin’ you for the manner in which you’ve brought this here business through. Here’s your health, sir.’\n\n‘Hold hard there,’ interposed the mottled-faced gentleman, with sudden energy; ‘your eyes on me, gen’l’m’n!’\n\nSaying this, the mottled-faced gentleman rose, as did the other gentlemen. The mottled-faced gentleman reviewed the company, and slowly lifted his hand, upon which every man (including him of the mottled countenance) drew a long breath, and lifted his tumbler to his lips. In one instant, the mottled-faced gentleman depressed his hand again, and every glass was set down empty. It is impossible to describe the thrilling effect produced by this striking ceremony. At once dignified, solemn, and impressive, it combined every element of grandeur.\n\n‘Well, gentlemen,’ said Mr. Pell, ‘all I can say is, that such marks of confidence must be very gratifying to a professional man. I don’t wish to say anything that might appear egotistical, gentlemen, but I’m very glad, for your own sakes, that you came to me; that’s all. If you had gone to any low member of the profession, it’s my firm conviction, and I assure you of it as a fact, that you would have found yourselves in Queer Street before this. I could have wished my noble friend had been alive to have seen my management of this case. I don’t say it out of pride, but I think--However, gentlemen, I won’t trouble you with that. I’m generally to be found here, gentlemen, but if I’m not here, or over the way, that’s my address. You’ll find my terms very cheap and reasonable, and no man attends more to his clients than I do, and I hope I know a little of my profession besides. If you have any opportunity of recommending me to any of your friends, gentlemen, I shall be very much obliged to you, and so will they too, when they come to know me. Your healths, gentlemen.’\n\nWith this expression of his feelings, Mr. Solomon Pell laid three small written cards before Mr. Weller’s friends, and, looking at the clock again, feared it was time to be walking. Upon this hint Mr. Weller settled the bill, and, issuing forth, the executor, legatee, attorney, and umpires, directed their steps towards the city.\n\nThe office of Wilkins Flasher, Esquire, of the Stock Exchange, was in a first floor up a court behind the Bank of England; the house of Wilkins Flasher, Esquire, was at Brixton, Surrey; the horse and stanhope of Wilkins Flasher, Esquire, were at an adjacent livery stable; the groom of Wilkins Flasher, Esquire, was on his way to the West End to deliver some game; the clerk of Wilkins Flasher, Esquire, had gone to his dinner; and so Wilkins Flasher, Esquire, himself, cried, ‘Come in,’ when Mr. Pell and his companions knocked at the counting-house door.\n\n‘Good-morning, Sir,’ said Pell, bowing obsequiously. ‘We want to make a little transfer, if you please.’\n\n‘Oh, just come in, will you?’ said Mr. Flasher. ‘Sit down a minute; I’ll attend to you directly.’\n\n‘Thank you, Sir,’ said Pell, ‘there’s no hurry. Take a chair, Mr. Weller.’\n\nMr. Weller took a chair, and Sam took a box, and the umpires took what they could get, and looked at the almanac and one or two papers which were wafered against the wall, with as much open-eyed reverence as if they had been the finest efforts of the old masters.\n\n‘Well, I’ll bet you half a dozen of claret on it; come!’ said Wilkins Flasher, Esquire, resuming the conversation to which Mr. Pell’s entrance had caused a momentary interruption.\n\nThis was addressed to a very smart young gentleman who wore his hat on his right whisker, and was lounging over the desk, killing flies with a ruler. Wilkins Flasher, Esquire, was balancing himself on two legs of an office stool, spearing a wafer-box with a penknife, which he dropped every now and then with great dexterity into the very centre of a small red wafer that was stuck outside. Both gentlemen had very open waistcoats and very rolling collars, and very small boots, and very big rings, and very little watches, and very large guard-chains, and symmetrical inexpressibles, and scented pocket-handkerchiefs.\n\n‘I never bet half a dozen!’ said the other gentleman. ‘I’ll take a dozen.’\n\n‘Done, Simmery, done!’ said Wilkins Flasher, Esquire.\n\n‘P. P., mind,’ observed the other.\n\n‘Of course,’ replied Wilkins Flasher, Esquire. Wilkins Flasher, Esquire, entered it in a little book, with a gold pencil-case, and the other gentleman entered it also, in another little book with another gold pencil-case.\n\n‘I see there’s a notice up this morning about Boffer,’ observed Mr. Simmery. ‘Poor devil, he’s expelled the house!’\n\n‘I’ll bet you ten guineas to five, he cuts his throat,’ said Wilkins Flasher, Esquire.\n\n‘Done,’ replied Mr. Simmery.\n\n‘Stop! I bar,’ said Wilkins Flasher, Esquire, thoughtfully. ‘Perhaps he may hang himself.’\n\n‘Very good,’ rejoined Mr. Simmery, pulling out the gold pencil-case again. ‘I’ve no objection to take you that way. Say, makes away with himself.’\n\n‘Kills himself, in fact,’ said Wilkins Flasher, Esquire.\n\n‘Just so,’ replied Mr. Simmery, putting it down. ‘“Flasher--ten guineas to five, Boffer kills himself.” Within what time shall we say?’\n\n‘A fortnight?’ suggested Wilkins Flasher, Esquire.\n\n‘Con-found it, no,’ rejoined Mr. Simmery, stopping for an instant to smash a fly with the ruler. ‘Say a week.’\n\n‘Split the difference,’ said Wilkins Flasher, Esquire. ‘Make it ten days.’\n\n‘Well; ten days,’ rejoined Mr. Simmery.\n\nSo it was entered down on the little books that Boffer was to kill himself within ten days, or Wilkins Flasher, Esquire, was to hand over to Frank Simmery, Esquire, the sum of ten guineas; and that if Boffer did kill himself within that time, Frank Simmery, Esquire, would pay to Wilkins Flasher, Esquire, five guineas, instead.\n\n‘I’m very sorry he has failed,’ said Wilkins Flasher, Esquire. ‘Capital dinners he gave.’\n\n‘Fine port he had too,’ remarked Mr. Simmery. ‘We are going to send our butler to the sale to-morrow, to pick up some of that sixty-four.’\n\n‘The devil you are!’ said Wilkins Flasher, Esquire. ‘My man’s going too. Five guineas my man outbids your man.’\n\n‘Done.’\n\nAnother entry was made in the little books, with the gold pencil-cases; and Mr. Simmery, having by this time killed all the flies and taken all the bets, strolled away to the Stock Exchange to see what was going forward.\n\nWilkins Flasher, Esquire, now condescended to receive Mr. Solomon Pell’s instructions, and having filled up some printed forms, requested the party to follow him to the bank, which they did: Mr. Weller and his three friends staring at all they beheld in unbounded astonishment, and Sam encountering everything with a coolness which nothing could disturb.\n\nCrossing a courtyard which was all noise and bustle, and passing a couple of porters who seemed dressed to match the red fire engine which was wheeled away into a corner, they passed into an office where their business was to be transacted, and where Pell and Mr. Flasher left them standing for a few moments, while they went upstairs into the Will Office.\n\n‘Wot place is this here?’ whispered the mottled-faced gentleman to the elder Mr. Weller.\n\n‘Counsel’s Office,’ replied the executor in a whisper.\n\n‘Wot are them gen’l’men a-settin’ behind the counters?’ asked the hoarse coachman.\n\n‘Reduced counsels, I s’pose,’ replied Mr. Weller. ‘Ain’t they the reduced counsels, Samivel?’\n\n‘Wy, you don’t suppose the reduced counsels is alive, do you?’ inquired Sam, with some disdain.\n\n‘How should I know?’ retorted Mr. Weller; ‘I thought they looked wery like it. Wot are they, then?’\n\n‘Clerks,’ replied Sam.\n\n‘Wot are they all a-eatin’ ham sangwidges for?’ inquired his father.\n\n‘’Cos it’s in their dooty, I suppose,’ replied Sam, ‘it’s a part o’ the system; they’re alvays a-doin’ it here, all day long!’\n\nMr. Weller and his friends had scarcely had a moment to reflect upon this singular regulation as connected with the monetary system of the country, when they were rejoined by Pell and Wilkins Flasher, Esquire, who led them to a part of the counter above which was a round blackboard with a large ‘W.’ on it.\n\n‘Wot’s that for, Sir?’ inquired Mr. Weller, directing Pell’s attention to the target in question.\n\n‘The first letter of the name of the deceased,’ replied Pell.\n\n‘I say,’ said Mr. Weller, turning round to the umpires, there’s somethin’ wrong here. We’s our letter--this won’t do.’\n\nThe referees at once gave it as their decided opinion that the business could not be legally proceeded with, under the letter W., and in all probability it would have stood over for one day at least, had it not been for the prompt, though, at first sight, undutiful behaviour of Sam, who, seizing his father by the skirt of the coat, dragged him to the counter, and pinned him there, until he had affixed his signature to a couple of instruments; which, from Mr. Weller’s habit of printing, was a work of so much labour and time, that the officiating clerk peeled and ate three Ribstone pippins while it was performing.\n\nAs the elder Mr. Weller insisted on selling out his portion forthwith, they proceeded from the bank to the gate of the Stock Exchange, to which Wilkins Flasher, Esquire, after a short absence, returned with a cheque on Smith, Payne, & Smith, for five hundred and thirty pounds; that being the money to which Mr. Weller, at the market price of the day, was entitled, in consideration of the balance of the second Mrs. Weller’s funded savings. Sam’s two hundred pounds stood transferred to his name, and Wilkins Flasher, Esquire, having been paid his commission, dropped the money carelessly into his coat pocket, and lounged back to his office.\n\nMr. Weller was at first obstinately determined on cashing the cheque in nothing but sovereigns; but it being represented by the umpires that by so doing he must incur the expense of a small sack to carry them home in, he consented to receive the amount in five-pound notes.\n\n‘My son,’ said Mr. Weller, as they came out of the banking-house--‘my son and me has a wery partickler engagement this arternoon, and I should like to have this here bis’ness settled out of hand, so let’s jest go straight avay someveres, vere ve can hordit the accounts.’\n\nA quiet room was soon found, and the accounts were produced and audited. Mr. Pell’s bill was taxed by Sam, and some charges were disallowed by the umpires; but, notwithstanding Mr. Pell’s declaration, accompanied with many solemn asseverations that they were really too hard upon him, it was by very many degrees the best professional job he had ever had, and one on which he boarded, lodged, and washed, for six months afterwards.\n\nThe umpires having partaken of a dram, shook hands and departed, as they had to drive out of town that night. Mr. Solomon Pell, finding that nothing more was going forward, either in the eating or drinking way, took a friendly leave, and Sam and his father were left alone.\n\n‘There!’ said Mr. Weller, thrusting his pocket-book in his side pocket. ‘Vith the bills for the lease, and that, there’s eleven hundred and eighty pound here. Now, Samivel, my boy, turn the horses’ heads to the George and Wulter!’\n\nCHAPTER LVI. AN IMPORTANT CONFERENCE TAKES PLACE BETWEEN MR. PICKWICK AND SAMUEL WELLER, AT WHICH HIS PARENT ASSISTS--AN OLD GENTLEMAN IN A SNUFF-COLOURED SUIT ARRIVES UNEXPECTEDLY\nMr. Pickwick was sitting alone, musing over many things, and thinking among other considerations how he could best provide for the young couple whose present unsettled condition was matter of constant regret and anxiety to him, when Mary stepped lightly into the room, and, advancing to the table, said, rather hastily--\n\n‘Oh, if you please, Sir, Samuel is downstairs, and he says may his father see you?’\n\n‘Surely,’ replied Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Thank you, Sir,’ said Mary, tripping towards the door again.\n\n‘Sam has not been here long, has he?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Oh, no, Sir,’ replied Mary eagerly. ‘He has only just come home. He is not going to ask you for any more leave, Sir, he says.’\n\nMary might have been conscious that she had communicated this last intelligence with more warmth than seemed actually necessary, or she might have observed the good-humoured smile with which Mr. Pickwick regarded her, when she had finished speaking. She certainly held down her head, and examined the corner of a very smart little apron, with more closeness than there appeared any absolute occasion for.\n\n‘Tell them they can come up at once, by all means,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\nMary, apparently much relieved, hurried away with her message.\n\nMr. Pickwick took two or three turns up and down the room; and, rubbing his chin with his left hand as he did so, appeared lost in thought.\n\n‘Well, well,’ said Mr. Pickwick, at length in a kind but somewhat melancholy tone, ‘it is the best way in which I could reward him for his attachment and fidelity; let it be so, in Heaven’s name. It is the fate of a lonely old man, that those about him should form new and different attachments and leave him. I have no right to expect that it should be otherwise with me. No, no,’ added Mr. Pickwick more cheerfully, ‘it would be selfish and ungrateful. I ought to be happy to have an opportunity of providing for him so well. I am. Of course I am.’\n\nMr. Pickwick had been so absorbed in these reflections, that a knock at the door was three or four times repeated before he heard it. Hastily seating himself, and calling up his accustomed pleasant looks, he gave the required permission, and Sam Weller entered, followed by his father.\n\n‘Glad to see you back again, Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick. ‘How do you do, Mr. Weller?’\n\n‘Wery hearty, thank’ee, sir,’ replied the widower; ‘hope I see you well, sir.’\n\n‘Quite, I thank you,’ replied Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘I wanted to have a little bit o’ conwersation with you, sir,’ said Mr. Weller, ‘if you could spare me five minits or so, sir.’\n\n‘Certainly,’ replied Mr. Pickwick. ‘Sam, give your father a chair.’\n\n‘Thank’ee, Samivel, I’ve got a cheer here,’ said Mr. Weller, bringing one forward as he spoke; ‘uncommon fine day it’s been, sir,’ added the old gentleman, laying his hat on the floor as he sat himself down.\n\n‘Remarkably so, indeed,’ replied Mr. Pickwick. ‘Very seasonable.’\n\n‘Seasonablest veather I ever see, sir,’ rejoined Mr. Weller. Here, the old gentleman was seized with a violent fit of coughing, which, being terminated, he nodded his head and winked and made several supplicatory and threatening gestures to his son, all of which Sam Weller steadily abstained from seeing.\n\nMr. Pickwick, perceiving that there was some embarrassment on the old gentleman’s part, affected to be engaged in cutting the leaves of a book that lay beside him, and waited patiently until Mr. Weller should arrive at the object of his visit.\n\n‘I never see sich a aggrawatin’ boy as you are, Samivel,’ said Mr. Weller, looking indignantly at his son; ‘never in all my born days.’\n\n‘What is he doing, Mr. Weller?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘He von’t begin, sir,’ rejoined Mr. Weller; ‘he knows I ain’t ekal to ex-pressin’ myself ven there’s anythin’ partickler to be done, and yet he’ll stand and see me a-settin’ here taking up your walable time, and makin’ a reg’lar spectacle o’ myself, rayther than help me out vith a syllable. It ain’t filial conduct, Samivel,’ said Mr. Weller, wiping his forehead; ‘wery far from it.’\n\n‘You said you’d speak,’ replied Sam; ‘how should I know you wos done up at the wery beginnin’?’\n\n‘You might ha’ seen I warn’t able to start,’ rejoined his father; ‘I’m on the wrong side of the road, and backin’ into the palin’s, and all manner of unpleasantness, and yet you von’t put out a hand to help me. I’m ashamed on you, Samivel.’\n\n‘The fact is, Sir,’ said Sam, with a slight bow, ‘the gov’nor’s been a- drawin’ his money.’\n\n‘Wery good, Samivel, wery good,’ said Mr. Weller, nodding his head with a satisfied air, ‘I didn’t mean to speak harsh to you, Sammy. Wery good. That’s the vay to begin. Come to the pint at once. Wery good indeed, Samivel.’\n\nMr. Weller nodded his head an extraordinary number of times, in the excess of his gratification, and waited in a listening attitude for Sam to resume his statement.\n\n‘You may sit down, Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick, apprehending that the interview was likely to prove rather longer than he had expected.\n\nSam bowed again and sat down; his father looking round, he continued--\n\n‘The gov’nor, sir, has drawn out five hundred and thirty pound.’\n\n‘Reduced counsels,’ interposed Mr. Weller, senior, in an undertone.\n\n‘It don’t much matter vether it’s reduced counsels, or wot not,’ said Sam; ‘five hundred and thirty pounds is the sum, ain’t it?’\n\n‘All right, Samivel,’ replied Mr. Weller.\n\n‘To vich sum, he has added for the house and bisness--’\n\n‘Lease, good-vill, stock, and fixters,’ interposed Mr. Weller.\n\n‘As much as makes it,’ continued Sam, ‘altogether, eleven hundred and eighty pound.’\n\n‘Indeed!’ said Mr. Pickwick. ‘I am delighted to hear it. I congratulate you, Mr. Weller, on having done so well.’\n\n‘Vait a minit, Sir,’ said Mr. Weller, raising his hand in a deprecatory manner. ‘Get on, Samivel.’\n\n‘This here money,’ said Sam, with a little hesitation, ‘he’s anxious to put someveres, vere he knows it’ll be safe, and I’m wery anxious too, for if he keeps it, he’ll go a-lendin’ it to somebody, or inwestin’ property in horses, or droppin’ his pocket-book down an airy, or makin’ a Egyptian mummy of his-self in some vay or another.’\n\n‘Wery good, Samivel,’ observed Mr. Weller, in as complacent a manner as if Sam had been passing the highest eulogiums on his prudence and foresight. ‘Wery good.’\n\n‘For vich reasons,’ continued Sam, plucking nervously at the brim of his hat--‘for vich reasons, he’s drawn it out to-day, and come here vith me to say, leastvays to offer, or in other vords--’\n\n‘To say this here,’ said the elder Mr. Weller impatiently, ‘that it ain’t o’ no use to me. I’m a-goin’ to vork a coach reg’lar, and ha’n’t got noveres to keep it in, unless I vos to pay the guard for takin’ care on it, or to put it in vun o’ the coach pockets, vich ‘ud be a temptation to the insides. If you’ll take care on it for me, sir, I shall be wery much obliged to you. P’raps,’ said Mr. Weller, walking up to Mr. Pickwick and whispering in his ear--‘p’raps it’ll go a little vay towards the expenses o’ that ‘ere conwiction. All I say is, just you keep it till I ask you for it again.’ With these words, Mr. Weller placed the pocket-book in Mr. Pickwick’s hands, caught up his hat, and ran out of the room with a celerity scarcely to be expected from so corpulent a subject.\n\n‘Stop him, Sam!’ exclaimed Mr. Pickwick earnestly. ‘Overtake him; bring him back instantly! Mr. Weller--here--come back!’\n\nSam saw that his master’s injunctions were not to be disobeyed; and, catching his father by the arm as he was descending the stairs, dragged him back by main force.\n\n‘My good friend,’ said Mr. Pickwick, taking the old man by the hand, ‘your honest confidence overpowers me.’\n\n‘I don’t see no occasion for nothin’ o’ the kind, Sir,’ replied Mr. Weller obstinately.\n\n‘I assure you, my good friend, I have more money than I can ever need; far more than a man at my age can ever live to spend,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘No man knows how much he can spend, till he tries,’ observed Mr. Weller.\n\n‘Perhaps not,’ replied Mr. Pickwick; ‘but as I have no intention of trying any such experiments, I am not likely to come to want. I must beg you to take this back, Mr. Weller.’\n\nWery well,’ said Mr. Weller, with a discontented look. ‘Mark my vords, Sammy, I’ll do somethin’ desperate vith this here property; somethin’ desperate!’\n\n‘You’d better not,’ replied Sam.\n\nMr. Weller reflected for a short time, and then, buttoning up his coat with great determination, said--\n\n‘I’ll keep a pike.’\n\n‘Wot!’ exclaimed Sam.\n\n‘A pike!’ rejoined Mr. Weller, through his set teeth; ‘I’ll keep a pike. Say good-bye to your father, Samivel. I dewote the remainder of my days to a pike.’\n\nThis threat was such an awful one, and Mr. Weller, besides appearing fully resolved to carry it into execution, seemed so deeply mortified by Mr. Pickwick’s refusal, that that gentleman, after a short reflection, said--\n\n‘Well, well, Mr. Weller, I will keep your money. I can do more good with it, perhaps, than you can.’\n\n‘Just the wery thing, to be sure,’ said Mr. Weller, brightening up; ‘o’ course you can, sir.’\n\n‘Say no more about it,’ said Mr. Pickwick, locking the pocket-book in his desk; ‘I am heartily obliged to you, my good friend. Now sit down again. I want to ask your advice.’\n\nThe internal laughter occasioned by the triumphant success of his visit, which had convulsed not only Mr. Weller’s face, but his arms, legs, and body also, during the locking up of the pocket-book, suddenly gave place to the most dignified gravity as he heard these words.\n\n‘Wait outside a few minutes, Sam, will you?’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\nSam immediately withdrew.\n\nMr. Weller looked uncommonly wise and very much amazed, when Mr. Pickwick opened the discourse by saying--\n\n‘You are not an advocate for matrimony, I think, Mr. Weller?’\n\nMr. Weller shook his head. He was wholly unable to speak; vague thoughts of some wicked widow having been successful in her designs on Mr. Pickwick, choked his utterance.\n\n‘Did you happen to see a young girl downstairs when you came in just now with your son?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Yes. I see a young gal,’ replied Mr. Weller shortly.\n\n‘What did you think of her, now? Candidly, Mr. Weller, what did you think of her?’\n\n‘I thought she wos wery plump, and vell made,’ said Mr. Weller, with a critical air.\n\n‘So she is,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘so she is. What did you think of her manners, from what you saw of her?’\n\n‘Wery pleasant,’ rejoined Mr. Weller. ‘Wery pleasant and comformable.’\n\nThe precise meaning which Mr. Weller attached to this last-mentioned adjective, did not appear; but, as it was evident from the tone in which he used it that it was a favourable expression, Mr. Pickwick was as well satisfied as if he had been thoroughly enlightened on the subject.\n\n‘I take a great interest in her, Mr. Weller,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\nMr. Weller coughed.\n\n‘I mean an interest in her doing well,’ resumed Mr. Pickwick; ‘a desire that she may be comfortable and prosperous. You understand?’\n\n‘Wery clearly,’ replied Mr. Weller, who understood nothing yet.\n\n‘That young person,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘is attached to your son.’\n\n‘To Samivel Veller!’ exclaimed the parent.\n\n‘Yes,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘It’s nat’ral,’ said Mr. Weller, after some consideration, ‘nat’ral, but rayther alarmin’. Sammy must be careful.’\n\n‘How do you mean?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Wery careful that he don’t say nothin’ to her,’ responded Mr. Weller. ‘Wery careful that he ain’t led avay, in a innocent moment, to say anythin’ as may lead to a conwiction for breach. You’re never safe vith ‘em, Mr. Pickwick, ven they vunce has designs on you; there’s no knowin’ vere to have ‘em; and vile you’re a-considering of it, they have you. I wos married fust, that vay myself, Sir, and Sammy wos the consekens o’ the manoover.’\n\n‘You give me no great encouragement to conclude what I have to say,’ observed Mr. Pickwick, ‘but I had better do so at once. This young person is not only attached to your son, Mr. Weller, but your son is attached to her.’\n\n‘Vell,’ said Mr. Weller, ‘this here’s a pretty sort o’ thing to come to a father’s ears, this is!’\n\n‘I have observed them on several occasions,’ said Mr. Pickwick, making no comment on Mr. Weller’s last remark; ‘and entertain no doubt at all about it. Supposing I were desirous of establishing them comfortably as man and wife in some little business or situation, where they might hope to obtain a decent living, what should you think of it, Mr. Weller?’\n\nAt first, Mr. Weller received with wry faces a proposition involving the marriage of anybody in whom he took an interest; but, as Mr. Pickwick argued the point with him, and laid great stress on the fact that Mary was not a widow, he gradually became more tractable. Mr. Pickwick had great influence over him, and he had been much struck with Mary’s appearance; having, in fact, bestowed several very unfatherly winks upon her, already. At length he said that it was not for him to oppose Mr. Pickwick’s inclination, and that he would be very happy to yield to his advice; upon which, Mr. Pickwick joyfully took him at his word, and called Sam back into the room.\n\n‘Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick, clearing his throat, ‘your father and I have been having some conversation about you.’\n\n‘About you, Samivel,’ said Mr. Weller, in a patronising and impressive voice.\n\n‘I am not so blind, Sam, as not to have seen, a long time since, that you entertain something more than a friendly feeling towards Mrs. Winkle’s maid,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘You hear this, Samivel?’ said Mr. Weller, in the same judicial form of speech as before.\n\n‘I hope, Sir,’ said Sam, addressing his master, ‘I hope there’s no harm in a young man takin’ notice of a young ‘ooman as is undeniably good- looking and well-conducted.’\n\n‘Certainly not,’ said Mr. Pickwick.\n\n‘Not by no means,’ acquiesced Mr. Weller, affably but magisterially.\n\n‘So far from thinking there is anything wrong in conduct so natural,’ resumed Mr. Pickwick, ‘it is my wish to assist and promote your wishes in this respect. With this view, I have had a little conversation with your father; and finding that he is of my opinion--’\n\n‘The lady not bein’ a widder,’ interposed Mr. Weller in explanation.\n\n‘The lady not being a widow,’ said Mr. Pickwick, smiling. ‘I wish to free you from the restraint which your present position imposes upon you, and to mark my sense of your fidelity and many excellent qualities, by enabling you to marry this girl at once, and to earn an independent livelihood for yourself and family. I shall be proud, Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick, whose voice had faltered a little hitherto, but now resumed its customary tone, ‘proud and happy to make your future prospects in life my grateful and peculiar care.’\n\nThere was a profound silence for a short time, and then Sam said, in a low, husky sort of voice, but firmly withal--\n\n‘I’m very much obliged to you for your goodness, Sir, as is only like yourself; but it can’t be done.’\n\n‘Can’t be done!’ ejaculated Mr. Pickwick in astonishment.\n\n‘Samivel!’ said Mr. Weller, with dignity.\n\n‘I say it can’t be done,’ repeated Sam in a louder key. ‘Wot’s to become of you, Sir?’\n\n‘My good fellow,’ replied Mr. Pickwick, ‘the recent changes among my friends will alter my mode of life in future, entirely; besides, I am growing older, and want repose and quiet. My rambles, Sam, are over.’\n\n‘How do I know that ‘ere, sir?’ argued Sam. ‘You think so now! S’pose you wos to change your mind, vich is not unlikely, for you’ve the spirit o’ five-and-twenty in you still, what ‘ud become on you vithout me? It can’t be done, Sir, it can’t be done.’\n\n‘Wery good, Samivel, there’s a good deal in that,’ said Mr. Weller encouragingly.\n\n‘I speak after long deliberation, Sam, and with the certainty that I shall keep my word,’ said Mr. Pickwick, shaking his head. ‘New scenes have closed upon me; my rambles are at an end.’\n\n‘Wery good,’ rejoined Sam. ‘Then, that’s the wery best reason wy you should alvays have somebody by you as understands you, to keep you up and make you comfortable. If you vant a more polished sort o’ feller, vell and good, have him; but vages or no vages, notice or no notice, board or no board, lodgin’ or no lodgin’, Sam Veller, as you took from the old inn in the Borough, sticks by you, come what may; and let ev’rythin’ and ev’rybody do their wery fiercest, nothin’ shall ever perwent it!’\n\nAt the close of this declaration, which Sam made with great emotion, the elder Mr. Weller rose from his chair, and, forgetting all considerations of time, place, or propriety, waved his hat above his head, and gave three vehement cheers.\n\n‘My good fellow,’ said Mr. Pickwick, when Mr. Weller had sat down again, rather abashed at his own enthusiasm, ‘you are bound to consider the young woman also.’\n\n‘I do consider the young ‘ooman, Sir,’ said Sam. ‘I have considered the young ‘ooman. I’ve spoke to her. I’ve told her how I’m sitivated; she’s ready to vait till I’m ready, and I believe she vill. If she don’t, she’s not the young ‘ooman I take her for, and I give her up vith readiness. You’ve know’d me afore, Sir. My mind’s made up, and nothin’ can ever alter it.’\n\nWho could combat this resolution? Not Mr. Pickwick. He derived, at that moment, more pride and luxury of feeling from the disinterested attachment of his humble friends, than ten thousand protestations from the greatest men living could have awakened in his heart.\n\nWhile this conversation was passing in Mr. Pickwick’s room, a little old gentleman in a suit of snuff-coloured clothes, followed by a porter carrying a small portmanteau, presented himself below; and, after securing a bed for the night, inquired of the waiter whether one Mrs. Winkle was staying there, to which question the waiter of course responded in the affirmative.\n\n‘Is she alone?’ inquired the old gentleman.\n\n‘I believe she is, Sir,’ replied the waiter; ‘I can call her own maid, Sir, if you--’\n\n‘No, I don’t want her,’ said the old gentleman quickly. ‘Show me to her room without announcing me.’\n\n‘Eh, Sir?’ said the waiter.\n\n‘Are you deaf?’ inquired the little old gentleman.\n\n‘No, sir.’\n\n‘Then listen, if you please. Can you hear me now?’\n\n‘Yes, Sir.’\n\n‘That’s well. Show me to Mrs. Winkle’s room, without announcing me.’\n\nAs the little old gentleman uttered this command, he slipped five shillings into the waiter’s hand, and looked steadily at him.\n\n‘Really, sir,’ said the waiter, ‘I don’t know, sir, whether--’\n\n‘Ah! you’ll do it, I see,’ said the little old gentleman. ‘You had better do it at once. It will save time.’\n\nThere was something so very cool and collected in the gentleman’s manner, that the waiter put the five shillings in his pocket, and led him upstairs without another word.\n\n‘This is the room, is it?’ said the gentleman. ‘You may go.’\n\nThe waiter complied, wondering much who the gentleman could be, and what he wanted; the little old gentleman, waiting till he was out of sight, tapped at the door.\n\n‘Come in,’ said Arabella.\n\n‘Um, a pretty voice, at any rate,’ murmured the little old gentleman; ‘but that’s nothing.’ As he said this, he opened the door and walked in. Arabella, who was sitting at work, rose on beholding a stranger--a little confused--but by no means ungracefully so.\n\n‘Pray don’t rise, ma’am,’ said the unknown, walking in, and closing the door after him. ‘Mrs. Winkle, I believe?’\n\nArabella inclined her head.\n\n‘Mrs. Nathaniel Winkle, who married the son of the old man at Birmingham?’ said the stranger, eyeing Arabella with visible curiosity.\n\nAgain Arabella inclined her head, and looked uneasily round, as if uncertain whether to call for assistance.\n\n‘I surprise you, I see, ma’am,’ said the old gentleman.\n\n‘Rather, I confess,’ replied Arabella, wondering more and more.\n\n‘I’ll take a chair, if you’ll allow me, ma’am,’ said the stranger.\n\nHe took one; and drawing a spectacle-case from his pocket, leisurely pulled out a pair of spectacles, which he adjusted on his nose.\n\n‘You don’t know me, ma’am?’ he said, looking so intently at Arabella that she began to feel alarmed.\n\n‘No, sir,’ she replied timidly.\n\n‘No,’ said the gentleman, nursing his left leg; ‘I don’t know how you should. You know my name, though, ma’am.’\n\n‘Do I?’ said Arabella, trembling, though she scarcely knew why. ‘May I ask what it is?’\n\n‘Presently, ma’am, presently,’ said the stranger, not having yet removed his eyes from her countenance. ‘You have been recently married, ma’am?’\n\n‘I have,’ replied Arabella, in a scarcely audible tone, laying aside her work, and becoming greatly agitated as a thought, that had occurred to her before, struck more forcibly upon her mind.\n\n‘Without having represented to your husband the propriety of first consulting his father, on whom he is dependent, I think?’ said the stranger.\n\nArabella applied her handkerchief to her eyes.\n\n‘Without an endeavour, even, to ascertain, by some indirect appeal, what were the old man’s sentiments on a point in which he would naturally feel much interested?’ said the stranger.\n\n‘I cannot deny it, Sir,’ said Arabella.\n\n‘And without having sufficient property of your own to afford your husband any permanent assistance in exchange for the worldly advantages which you knew he would have gained if he had married agreeably to his father’s wishes?’ said the old gentleman. ‘This is what boys and girls call disinterested affection, till they have boys and girls of their own, and then they see it in a rougher and very different light!’\n\nArabella’s tears flowed fast, as she pleaded in extenuation that she was young and inexperienced; that her attachment had alone induced her to take the step to which she had resorted; and that she had been deprived of the counsel and guidance of her parents almost from infancy.\n\n‘It was wrong,’ said the old gentleman in a milder tone, ‘very wrong. It was romantic, unbusinesslike, foolish.’\n\n‘It was my fault; all my fault, Sir,’ replied poor Arabella, weeping.\n\n‘Nonsense,’ said the old gentleman; ‘it was not your fault that he fell in love with you, I suppose? Yes it was, though,’ said the old gentleman, looking rather slily at Arabella. ‘It was your fault. He couldn’t help it.’\n\nThis little compliment, or the little gentleman’s odd way of paying it, or his altered manner--so much kinder than it was, at first--or all three together, forced a smile from Arabella in the midst of her tears.\n\n‘Where’s your husband?’ inquired the old gentleman, abruptly; stopping a smile which was just coming over his own face.\n\n‘I expect him every instant, sir,’ said Arabella. ‘I persuaded him to take a walk this morning. He is very low and wretched at not having heard from his father.’\n\n‘Low, is he?’ said the old gentlemen. ‘Serve him right!’\n\n‘He feels it on my account, I am afraid,’ said Arabella; ‘and indeed, Sir, I feel it deeply on his. I have been the sole means of bringing him to his present condition.’\n\n‘Don’t mind it on his account, my dear,’ said the old gentleman. ‘It serves him right. I am glad of it--actually glad of it, as far as he is concerned.’\n\nThe words were scarcely out of the old gentleman’s lips, when footsteps were heard ascending the stairs, which he and Arabella seemed both to recognise at the same moment. The little gentleman turned pale; and, making a strong effort to appear composed, stood up, as Mr. Winkle entered the room.\n\n‘Father!’ cried Mr. Winkle, recoiling in amazement.\n\n‘Yes, sir,’ replied the little old gentleman. ‘Well, Sir, what have you got to say to me?’\n\nMr. Winkle remained silent.\n\n‘You are ashamed of yourself, I hope, Sir?’ said the old gentleman.\n\nStill Mr. Winkle said nothing.\n\n‘Are you ashamed of yourself, Sir, or are you not?’ inquired the old gentleman.\n\n‘No, Sir,’ replied Mr. Winkle, drawing Arabella’s arm through his. ‘I am not ashamed of myself, or of my wife either.’\n\n‘Upon my word!’ cried the old gentleman ironically.\n\n‘I am very sorry to have done anything which has lessened your affection for me, Sir,’ said Mr. Winkle; ‘but I will say, at the same time, that I have no reason to be ashamed of having this lady for my wife, nor you of having her for a daughter.’\n\n‘Give me your hand, Nat,’ said the old gentleman, in an altered voice. ‘Kiss me, my love. You are a very charming little daughter-in-law after all!’\n\nIn a few minutes’ time Mr. Winkle went in search of Mr. Pickwick, and returning with that gentleman, presented him to his father, whereupon they shook hands for five minutes incessantly.\n\n‘Mr. Pickwick, I thank you most heartily for all your kindness to my son,’ said old Mr. Winkle, in a bluff, straightforward way. ‘I am a hasty fellow, and when I saw you last, I was vexed and taken by surprise. I have judged for myself now, and am more than satisfied. Shall I make any more apologies, Mr. Pickwick?’\n\n‘Not one,’ replied that gentleman. ‘You have done the only thing wanting to complete my happiness.’\n\nHereupon there was another shaking of hands for five minutes longer, accompanied by a great number of complimentary speeches, which, besides being complimentary, had the additional and very novel recommendation of being sincere.\n\nSam had dutifully seen his father to the Belle Sauvage, when, on returning, he encountered the fat boy in the court, who had been charged with the delivery of a note from Emily Wardle.\n\n‘I say,’ said Joe, who was unusually loquacious, ‘what a pretty girl Mary is, isn’t she? I am so fond of her, I am!’\n\nMr. Weller made no verbal remark in reply; but eyeing the fat boy for a moment, quite transfixed at his presumption, led him by the collar to the corner, and dismissed him with a harmless but ceremonious kick. After which, he walked home, whistling.\n\nCHAPTER LVII. IN WHICH THE PICKWICK CLUB IS FINALLY DISSOLVED, AND EVERYTHING CONCLUDED TO THE SATISFACTION OF EVERYBODY\nFor a whole week after the happy arrival of Mr. Winkle from Birmingham, Mr. Pickwick and Sam Weller were from home all day long, only returning just in time for dinner, and then wearing an air of mystery and importance quite foreign to their natures. It was evident that very grave and eventful proceedings were on foot; but various surmises were afloat, respecting their precise character. Some (among whom was Mr. Tupman) were disposed to think that Mr. Pickwick contemplated a matrimonial alliance; but this idea the ladies most strenuously repudiated. Others rather inclined to the belief that he had projected some distant tour, and was at present occupied in effecting the preliminary arrangements; but this again was stoutly denied by Sam himself, who had unequivocally stated, when cross-examined by Mary, that no new journeys were to be undertaken. At length, when the brains of the whole party had been racked for six long days, by unavailing speculation, it was unanimously resolved that Mr. Pickwick should be called upon to explain his conduct, and to state distinctly why he had thus absented himself from the society of his admiring friends.\n\nWith this view, Mr. Wardle invited the full circle to dinner at the Adelphi; and the decanters having been thrice sent round, opened the business.\n\n‘We are all anxious to know,’ said the old gentleman, ‘what we have done to offend you, and to induce you to desert us and devote yourself to these solitary walks.’\n\n‘Are you?’ said Mr. Pickwick. ‘It is singular enough that I had intended to volunteer a full explanation this very day; so, if you will give me another glass of wine, I will satisfy your curiosity.’\n\nThe decanters passed from hand to hand with unwonted briskness, and Mr. Pickwick, looking round on the faces of his friends with a cheerful smile, proceeded--\n\n‘All the changes that have taken place among us,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘I mean the marriage that has taken place, and the marriage that WILL take place, with the changes they involve, rendered it necessary for me to think, soberly and at once, upon my future plans. I determined on retiring to some quiet, pretty neighbourhood in the vicinity of London; I saw a house which exactly suited my fancy; I have taken it and furnished it. It is fully prepared for my reception, and I intend entering upon it at once, trusting that I may yet live to spend many quiet years in peaceful retirement, cheered through life by the society of my friends, and followed in death by their affectionate remembrance.’\n\nHere Mr. Pickwick paused, and a low murmur ran round the table.\n\n‘The house I have taken,’ said Mr. Pickwick, ‘is at Dulwich. It has a large garden, and is situated in one of the most pleasant spots near London. It has been fitted up with every attention to substantial comfort; perhaps to a little elegance besides; but of that you shall judge for yourselves. Sam accompanies me there. I have engaged, on Perker’s representation, a housekeeper--a very old one--and such other servants as she thinks I shall require. I propose to consecrate this little retreat, by having a ceremony in which I take a great interest, performed there. I wish, if my friend Wardle entertains no objection, that his daughter should be married from my new house, on the day I take possession of it. The happiness of young people,’ said Mr. Pickwick, a little moved, ‘has ever been the chief pleasure of my life. It will warm my heart to witness the happiness of those friends who are dearest to me, beneath my own roof.’\n\nMr. Pickwick paused again: Emily and Arabella sobbed audibly.\n\n‘I have communicated, both personally and by letter, with the club,’ resumed Mr. Pickwick, ‘acquainting them with my intention. During our long absence, it has suffered much from internal dissentions; and the withdrawal of my name, coupled with this and other circumstances, has occasioned its dissolution. The Pickwick Club exists no longer.\n\n‘I shall never regret,’ said Mr. Pickwick in a low voice, ‘I shall never regret having devoted the greater part of two years to mixing with different varieties and shades of human character, frivolous as my pursuit of novelty may have appeared to many. Nearly the whole of my previous life having been devoted to business and the pursuit of wealth, numerous scenes of which I had no previous conception have dawned upon me--I hope to the enlargement of my mind, and the improvement of my understanding. If I have done but little good, I trust I have done less harm, and that none of my adventures will be other than a source of amusing and pleasant recollection to me in the decline of life. God bless you all!’\n\nWith these words, Mr. Pickwick filled and drained a bumper with a trembling hand; and his eyes moistened as his friends rose with one accord, and pledged him from their hearts.\n\nThere were few preparatory arrangements to be made for the marriage of Mr. Snodgrass. As he had neither father nor mother, and had been in his minority a ward of Mr. Pickwick’s, that gentleman was perfectly well acquainted with his possessions and prospects. His account of both was quite satisfactory to Wardle--as almost any other account would have been, for the good old gentleman was overflowing with hilarity and kindness--and a handsome portion having been bestowed upon Emily, the marriage was fixed to take place on the fourth day from that time--the suddenness of which preparations reduced three dressmakers and a tailor to the extreme verge of insanity.\n\nGetting post-horses to the carriage, old Wardle started off, next day, to bring his mother back to town. Communicating his intelligence to the old lady with characteristic impetuosity, she instantly fainted away; but being promptly revived, ordered the brocaded silk gown to be packed up forthwith, and proceeded to relate some circumstances of a similar nature attending the marriage of the eldest daughter of Lady Tollimglower, deceased, which occupied three hours in the recital, and were not half finished at last.\n\nMrs. Trundle had to be informed of all the mighty preparations that were making in London; and, being in a delicate state of health, was informed thereof through Mr. Trundle, lest the news should be too much for her; but it was not too much for her, inasmuch as she at once wrote off to Muggleton, to order a new cap and a black satin gown, and moreover avowed her determination of being present at the ceremony. Hereupon, Mr. Trundle called in the doctor, and the doctor said Mrs. Trundle ought to know best how she felt herself, to which Mrs. Trundle replied that she felt herself quite equal to it, and that she had made up her mind to go; upon which the doctor, who was a wise and discreet doctor, and knew what was good for himself, as well as for other people, said that perhaps if Mrs. Trundle stopped at home, she might hurt herself more by fretting, than by going, so perhaps she had better go. And she did go; the doctor with great attention sending in half a dozen of medicine, to be drunk upon the road.\n\nIn addition to these points of distraction, Wardle was intrusted with two small letters to two small young ladies who were to act as bridesmaids; upon the receipt of which, the two young ladies were driven to despair by having no ‘things’ ready for so important an occasion, and no time to make them in--a circumstance which appeared to afford the two worthy papas of the two small young ladies rather a feeling of satisfaction than otherwise. However, old frocks were trimmed, and new bonnets made, and the young ladies looked as well as could possibly have been expected of them. And as they cried at the subsequent ceremony in the proper places, and trembled at the right times, they acquitted themselves to the admiration of all beholders.\n\nHow the two poor relations ever reached London--whether they walked, or got behind coaches, or procured lifts in wagons, or carried each other by turns--is uncertain; but there they were, before Wardle; and the very first people that knocked at the door of Mr. Pickwick’s house, on the bridal morning, were the two poor relations, all smiles and shirt collar.\n\nThey were welcomed heartily though, for riches or poverty had no influence on Mr. Pickwick; the new servants were all alacrity and readiness; Sam was in a most unrivalled state of high spirits and excitement; Mary was glowing with beauty and smart ribands.\n\nThe bridegroom, who had been staying at the house for two or three days previous, sallied forth gallantly to Dulwich Church to meet the bride, attended by Mr. Pickwick, Ben Allen, Bob Sawyer, and Mr. Tupman; with Sam Weller outside, having at his button-hole a white favour, the gift of his lady-love, and clad in a new and gorgeous suit of livery invented for the occasion. They were met by the Wardles, and the Winkles, and the bride and bridesmaids, and the Trundles; and the ceremony having been performed, the coaches rattled back to Mr. Pickwick’s to breakfast, where little Mr. Perker already awaited them.\n\nHere, all the light clouds of the more solemn part of the proceedings passed away; every face shone forth joyously; and nothing was to be heard but congratulations and commendations. Everything was so beautiful! The lawn in front, the garden behind, the miniature conservatory, the dining-room, the drawing-room, the bedrooms, the smoking-room, and, above all, the study, with its pictures and easy- chairs, and odd cabinets, and queer tables, and books out of number, with a large cheerful window opening upon a pleasant lawn and commanding a pretty landscape, dotted here and there with little houses almost hidden by the trees; and then the curtains, and the carpets, and the chairs, and the sofas! Everything was so beautiful, so compact, so neat, and in such exquisite taste, said everybody, that there really was no deciding what to admire most.\n\nAnd in the midst of all this, stood Mr. Pickwick, his countenance lighted up with smiles, which the heart of no man, woman, or child, could resist: himself the happiest of the group: shaking hands, over and over again, with the same people, and when his own hands were not so employed, rubbing them with pleasure: turning round in a different direction at every fresh expression of gratification or curiosity, and inspiring everybody with his looks of gladness and delight.\n\nBreakfast is announced. Mr. Pickwick leads the old lady (who has been very eloquent on the subject of Lady Tollimglower) to the top of a long table; Wardle takes the bottom; the friends arrange themselves on either side; Sam takes his station behind his master’s chair; the laughter and talking cease; Mr. Pickwick, having said grace, pauses for an instant and looks round him. As he does so, the tears roll down his cheeks, in the fullness of his joy.\n\nLet us leave our old friend in one of those moments of unmixed happiness, of which, if we seek them, there are ever some, to cheer our transitory existence here. There are dark shadows on the earth, but its lights are stronger in the contrast. Some men, like bats or owls, have better eyes for the darkness than for the light. We, who have no such optical powers, are better pleased to take our last parting look at the visionary companions of many solitary hours, when the brief sunshine of the world is blazing full upon them.\n\nIt is the fate of most men who mingle with the world, and attain even the prime of life, to make many real friends, and lose them in the course of nature. It is the fate of all authors or chroniclers to create imaginary friends, and lose them in the course of art. Nor is this the full extent of their misfortunes; for they are required to furnish an account of them besides.\n\nIn compliance with this custom--unquestionably a bad one--we subjoin a few biographical words, in relation to the party at Mr. Pickwick’s assembled.\n\nMr. and Mrs. Winkle, being fully received into favour by the old gentleman, were shortly afterwards installed in a newly-built house, not half a mile from Mr. Pickwick’s. Mr. Winkle, being engaged in the city as agent or town correspondent of his father, exchanged his old costume for the ordinary dress of Englishmen, and presented all the external appearance of a civilised Christian ever afterwards.\n\nMr. and Mrs. Snodgrass settled at Dingley Dell, where they purchased and cultivated a small farm, more for occupation than profit. Mr. Snodgrass, being occasionally abstracted and melancholy, is to this day reputed a great poet among his friends and acquaintance, although we do not find that he has ever written anything to encourage the belief. There are many celebrated characters, literary, philosophical, and otherwise, who hold a high reputation on a similar tenure.\n\nMr. Tupman, when his friends married, and Mr. Pickwick settled, took lodgings at Richmond, where he has ever since resided. He walks constantly on the terrace during the summer months, with a youthful and jaunty air, which has rendered him the admiration of the numerous elderly ladies of single condition, who reside in the vicinity. He has never proposed again.\n\nMr. Bob Sawyer, having previously passed through the Gazette, passed over to Bengal, accompanied by Mr. Benjamin Allen; both gentlemen having received surgical appointments from the East India Company. They each had the yellow fever fourteen times, and then resolved to try a little abstinence; since which period, they have been doing well. Mrs. Bardell let lodgings to many conversable single gentlemen, with great profit, but never brought any more actions for breach of promise of marriage. Her attorneys, Messrs. Dodson & Fogg, continue in business, from which they realise a large income, and in which they are universally considered among the sharpest of the sharp.\n\nSam Weller kept his word, and remained unmarried, for two years. The old housekeeper dying at the end of that time, Mr. Pickwick promoted Mary to the situation, on condition of her marrying Mr. Weller at once, which she did without a murmur. From the circumstance of two sturdy little boys having been repeatedly seen at the gate of the back garden, there is reason to suppose that Sam has some family.\n\nThe elder Mr. Weller drove a coach for twelve months, but being afflicted with the gout, was compelled to retire. The contents of the pocket-book had been so well invested for him, however, by Mr. Pickwick, that he had a handsome independence to retire on, upon which he still lives at an excellent public-house near Shooter’s Hill, where he is quite reverenced as an oracle, boasting very much of his intimacy with Mr. Pickwick, and retaining a most unconquerable aversion to widows.\n\nMr. Pickwick himself continued to reside in his new house, employing his leisure hours in arranging the memoranda which he afterwards presented to the secretary of the once famous club, or in hearing Sam Weller read aloud, with such remarks as suggested themselves to his mind, which never failed to afford Mr. Pickwick great amusement. He was much troubled at first, by the numerous applications made to him by Mr. Snodgrass, Mr. Winkle, and Mr. Trundle, to act as godfather to their offspring; but he has become used to it now, and officiates as a matter of course. He never had occasion to regret his bounty to Mr. Jingle; for both that person and Job Trotter became, in time, worthy members of society, although they have always steadily objected to return to the scenes of their old haunts and temptations. Mr. Pickwick is somewhat infirm now; but he retains all his former juvenility of spirit, and may still be frequently seen, contemplating the pictures in the Dulwich Gallery, or enjoying a walk about the pleasant neighbourhood on a fine day. He is known by all the poor people about, who never fail to take their hats off, as he passes, with great respect. The children idolise him, and so indeed does the whole neighbourhood. Every year he repairs to a large family merry-making at Mr. Wardle’s; on this, as on all other occasions, he is invariably attended by the faithful Sam, between whom and his master there exists a steady and reciprocal attachment which nothing but death will terminate.";
if(typeof BODIES_TRANS!=="undefined") BODIES_TRANS["gb-580"]="1. 픽윅 회원들 2. 첫째 날의 여정과 첫째 날 저녁의 모험, 그리고 그 결과 3. 새로운 친구—떠돌이 배우의 이야기—불쾌한 방해와 언짢은 만남 4. 야외 훈련일과 야영—더 많은 새 친구들—시골로의 초대 5. 짧은 장—여러 일들 가운데서도 픽윅 씨가 마차 모는 일을 맡고 윈클 씨가 말 타는 일을 맡은 사연과, 두 사람이 그것을 어떻게 해냈는지를 보여 주는 장 6. 구식 카드놀이 모임—목사의 시구—귀환한 죄수의 이야기 7. 윈클 씨가 비둘기를 쏘아 까마귀를 죽이는 대신 까마귀를 쏘아 비둘기를 다치게 한 사연, 딩글리 델 크리켓 클럽이 올머글턴과 시합한 사연, 그리고 올머글턴이 딩글리 델의 비용으로 만찬을 든 사연, 그 밖의 흥미롭고 유익한 일들 8. 참된 사랑의 길이 결코 철길처럼 곧지 않다는 명제를 강력히 예증하는 장 9. 발견과 추격 10. 에이 징글 씨의 인격이 사심 없음에 관한 모든 의혹을(있었다 하더라도) 말끔히 풀어 주는 장 11. 또 다른 여정과 골동품 발견을 다루고, 픽윅 씨가 선거에 참석하기로 결심한 일을 기록하며, 늙은 목사의 원고를 담은 장 12. 픽윅 씨 편에서 있었던 매우 중요한 처사를 묘사하는 장이니, 그의 생애에서뿐 아니라 이 역사에서도 한 시대를 긋는 일이다 13. 이턴스윌에 관한 약간의 기록, 그곳 정당들의 상태, 그리고 그 유서 깊고 충성스럽고 애국적인 자치구를 위해 의회에 봉직할 의원을 뽑는 선거에 관하여 14. 공작 여관에 모인 일행에 대한 간략한 묘사와 어느 외판원이 들려준 이야기를 담은 장 15. 두 명사에 대한 충실한 초상과, 그들의 저택과 정원에서 열린 공개 조찬에 대한 정확한 묘사가 주어지는 장이니, 그 공개 조찬은 옛 친구를 알아보는 일과 또 다른 장의 시작으로 이어진다 16. 간략히 묘사하기에는 모험이 너무도 가득한 장 17. 류머티즘의 발작이 어떤 경우에는 창의적 천재성을 촉진하는 작용을 한다는 것을 보여 주는 장 18. 두 가지 점을 간략히 예증하는 장이니, 첫째는 히스테리의 힘이고, 둘째는 정황의 위력이다 19. 유쾌한 하루와 그 불쾌한 결말 20. 도드슨과 포그가 어떻게 일에 밝은 자들이고 그들의 사무원들은 어떻게 노는 데 밝은 자들인지, 그리고 웰러 씨와 오래 잃었던 그의 아버지 사이에 어떻게 감동적인 상봉이 이루어졌는지를 보여 주며, 또한 매그파이 앤드 스텀프에 어떤 별난 무리가 모였는지, 다음 장이 얼마나 훌륭한 장이 될 것인지를 보여 주는 장 21. 늙은이가 그가 좋아하는 화제에 빠져들어 어느 괴상한 의뢰인에 관한 이야기를 들려주는 장 22. 픽윅 씨가 입스위치로 여행하여 노란 컬페이퍼를 만 중년 부인과 낭만적인 모험을 겪는 장 23. 새뮤얼 웰러 씨가 자신과 트로터 씨 사이의 설욕전에 정력을 쏟기 시작하는 장 24. 피터 매그너스 씨가 질투하고 중년 부인이 불안해하여 픽윅 회원들이 법의 손아귀에 들어가게 되는 장 25. 여러 가지 유쾌한 일들 가운데서도 너프킨스 씨가 얼마나 위엄 있고 공정했는지, 그리고 웰러 씨가 잡 트로터 씨의 셔틀콕을 날아온 만큼이나 묵직하게 되받아쳤는지를 보여 주는 장—그리고 제자리에서 밝혀질 또 다른 일을 함께 담은 장 26. 바델 대 픽윅 소송의 진행에 대한 간략한 기록을 담은 장 27. 새뮤얼 웰러가 도킹으로 순례를 떠나 그의 새어머니를 만나는 장 28. 결혼식에 관한 기록과 그 밖의 몇 가지 놀이를 담은 유쾌한 크리스마스 장이니, 그 놀이들은 그 나름으로 결혼 자체만큼이나 좋은 풍습이지만, 이 타락한 시대에는 그만큼 경건하게 지켜지지는 않는다 29. 교회지기를 훔쳐 간 도깨비들의 이야기 30. 픽윅 회원들이 어느 자유직에 속한 멋진 두 청년과 어떻게 알게 되어 사귀었는지, 어떻게 얼음 위에서 즐겼는지, 그리고 그들의 방문이 어떻게 끝났는지를 다룬 장 31. 온통 법과 그에 정통한 여러 위대한 권위자들에 관한 장 32. 보브 소여 씨가 자치구의 자기 하숙집에서 베푼 총각 파티를, 궁정 기자가 일찍이 했던 것보다 훨씬 더 충실하게 묘사하는 장 33. 웰러 영감이 문학 작품에 관한 몇 가지 비평적 견해를 펼치고, 아들 새뮤얼의 도움을 받아 빨간 코의 목사 양반의 계정에 약간의 보복 할부금을 치르는 장 34. 바델 대 픽윅의 기억할 만한 재판을 온전하고 충실하게 보고하는 데 전적으로 바친 장 35. 픽윅 씨가 배스로 가는 편이 낫겠다고 생각하여 그에 따라 가는 장 36. 그 주요 내용이 블래더드 왕자 전설의 믿을 만한 판본과, 윈클 씨에게 닥친 지극히 기이한 재난임이 밝혀질 장 37. 웰러 씨가 초대받아 참석한 야회를 묘사함으로써 그의 부재를 명예롭게 해명하고, 또한 그가 픽윅 씨로부터 미묘하고 중요한 비밀 임무를 어떻게 위임받았는지를 이야기하는 장 38. 윈클 씨가 프라이팬에서 빠져나오자마자 어떻게 부드럽고 편안하게 불 속으로 걸어 들어갔는지를 다룬 장 39. 새뮤얼 웰러 씨가 사랑의 임무를 위임받아 그것을 수행하러 나서는 장이니, 그 성과가 어떠했는지는 이후에 밝혀질 것이다 40. 픽윅 씨를 인생이라는 위대한 연극의 새롭고도 결코 흥미롭지 않다 할 수 없는 장면으로 안내하는 장 41. 픽윅 씨가 플리트에 들어갔을 때 어떤 일이 닥쳤는지, 그곳에서 어떤 죄수들을 보았는지, 그리고 그날 밤을 어떻게 보냈는지를 다룬 장 42. 앞 장과 마찬가지로, 역경이 사람을 낯선 잠자리 동무들과 사귀게 한다는 옛 속담을 예증하는 장—또한 픽윅 씨가 새뮤얼 웰러 씨에게 한 놀랍고도 충격적인 선언을 담은 장 43. 새뮤얼 웰러 씨가 어떻게 곤경에 빠졌는지를 보여 주는 장 44. 플리트에서 일어난 여러 가지 소소한 일들과 윈클 씨의 알 수 없는 행동을 다루고, 가엾은 형평법원 죄수가 어떻게 마침내 석방되었는지를 보여 주는 장 45. 새뮤얼 웰러 씨와 한 가족 일행 사이의 감동적인 상봉을 묘사하는 장. 픽윅 씨가 자신이 사는 이 작디작은 세계를 두루 돌아보고, 앞으로는 그것과 되도록 적게 어울리리라 결심하는 장 46. 도드슨과 포그 두 분이 이룩하고 행한, 익살과 뒤섞이지 않은 것은 아닌, 섬세한 감정의 갸륵한 행위를 기록하는 장 47. 주로 사무적인 일들과 도드슨과 포그의 현세적 이득에 바친 장—윈클 씨가 기이한 정황 아래 다시 나타나고—픽윅 씨의 자비심이 그의 고집보다 더 강함이 드러나는 장 48. 픽윅 씨가 새뮤얼 웰러의 도움을 받아 어떻게 벤저민 앨런 씨의 마음을 누그러뜨리고 로버트 소여 씨의 분노를 가라앉히려 애썼는지를 이야기하는 장 49. 외판원의 삼촌 이야기를 담은 장 50. 픽윅 씨가 그의 임무를 어떻게 서둘러 수행했는지, 그리고 그가 출발하자마자 어떻게 더없이 뜻밖의 조력자에 의해 힘을 얻게 되었는지를 다룬 장 51. 픽윅 씨가 옛 친구와 마주치는 장—독자는 바로 그 다행스러운 정황 덕분에, 여기에 적힌 두 위대한 권력자에 관한 짜릿하게 흥미로운 이야기를 주로 얻게 된다 52. 웰러 가에 일어난 심각한 변화와 스티긴스 씨의 때 이른 몰락을 다룬 장 53. 징글 씨와 잡 트로터의 마지막 퇴장, 그레이즈 인 광장에서 벌어진 분주한 위대한 아침나절을 포함하는 장—퍼커 씨의 문을 두 번 두드리는 소리로 끝맺는 장 54. 그 두 번의 노크에 관련된 몇 가지 자세한 사정과 그 밖의 일들을 담은 장이니, 그 가운데 스노드그래스 씨와 한 젊은 숙녀에 관한 흥미로운 폭로들은 결코 이 역사와 무관하지 않다 55. 솔로몬 펠 씨가 마부들로 구성된 특별 위원회의 도움을 받아 웰러 영감의 일을 처리하는 장 56. 픽윅 씨와 새뮤얼 웰러 사이에 중요한 회담이 벌어지고 그의 아버지가 거드는 장—코담배 빛깔의 옷을 입은 노신사가 뜻밖에 도착하는 장 57. 픽윅 클럽이 마침내 해산되고 모든 일이 모두를 만족시키는 가운데 마무리되는 장\n픽윅 클럽 유고집\n\n# CHAPTER I. THE PICKWICKIANS\n\n불멸의 픽윅이 걸어온 공적 생애의 초기 역사를 뒤덮은 듯한 그 어둠을 밝히고, 그 모호함을 눈부신 광채로 바꾸어 놓는 첫 줄기 빛은, 픽윅 클럽 의사록에 실린 다음 기록을 정독함으로써 얻어진다. 이 기록을 독자 앞에 내놓으면서 본 문서의 편집자는 더없는 기쁨을 느끼는바, 그에게 맡겨진 갖가지 문서들 사이에서 행해진 그의 탐색이 얼마나 세심한 주의와 지칠 줄 모르는 근면, 그리고 정밀한 분별로 이루어졌는지를 입증하는 증거로서 그러하다.\n\n'1827년 5월 12일. 조지프 스미거스 귀하, 픽윅 클럽 종신 부회장 겸 회원(P.V.P.M.P.C. — 종신 부회장, 픽윅 클럽 회원) 사회. 다음 결의안들이 만장일치로 가결되었음.\n\n'본 협회는, 새뮤얼 픽윅 귀하, 픽윅 클럽 총의장 겸 회원(G.C.M.P.C. — 총의장, 픽윅 클럽 회원)께서 전달하신 「햄프스테드 연못들의 수원(水源)에 관한 고찰, 그리고 가시고기 이론에 관한 약간의 소견」이라는 제목의 논문이 낭독되는 것을, 섞임 없는 만족과 무조건적인 찬동의 마음으로 청취하였음. 또한 본 협회는 이에 대하여 상기 새뮤얼 픽윅 귀하, 총의장 겸 회원께 가장 따뜻한 감사의 뜻을 표하는 바임.\n\n'본 협회는, 방금 언급한 그 저작으로부터 — 또한 새뮤얼 픽윅 귀하, 총의장 겸 회원께서 혼지, 하이게이트, 브릭스턴, 캠버웰에서 행하신 지칠 줄 모르는 연구로부터 — 학문의 대의에 반드시 돌아올 이익을 깊이 인식하는 동시에, 그 박식한 분의 고찰을 더 넓은 영역으로 확대하고, 그분의 여행을 넓혀, 따라서 그 관찰의 범위를 확장함으로써, 지식의 진보와 학문의 보급에 반드시 그리고 불가피하게 따라올 헤아릴 수 없는 이익에 대하여 생생한 감각을 품지 않을 수 없음.\n\n'방금 말한 견지에서, 본 협회는 상기 새뮤얼 픽윅 귀하, 총의장 겸 회원과 이후에 명시될 세 명의 다른 픽윅 회원으로부터 나온 제안, 곧 픽윅 클럽 통신협회라는 명칭 아래 연합 픽윅 회원의 새 지부를 결성하자는 제안을 진지하게 고려하였음.\n\n'상기 제안은 본 협회의 인가와 승인을 받았음.\n\n'따라서 이에 픽윅 클럽 통신협회를 구성하는 바이며, 새뮤얼 픽윅 귀하, 총의장 겸 회원, 트레이시 텁먼 귀하, 회원, 오거스터스 스노드그래스 귀하, 회원, 그리고 너새니얼 윈클 귀하, 회원을 이에 그 협회의 회원으로 지명하고 임명하는 바임. 또한 이들에게 요청하건대, 그들의 여행과 조사, 인물과 풍습에 대한 관찰, 그리고 그들의 모든 모험에 관한 확증된 기록을, 그 지역의 풍경이나 연고에서 비롯될 수 있는 모든 이야기와 문서와 더불어, 런던에 자리한 픽윅 클럽으로 수시로 보내 주기를 바라는 바임.\n\n'본 협회는 통신협회의 모든 회원이 자신의 여비를 스스로 부담한다는 원칙을 진심으로 인정하며, 상기 협회의 회원들이 동일한 조건으로 원하는 어떠한 기간 동안에도 그들의 탐구를 계속하는 데 대해 어떠한 이의도 없음.\n\n'상기 통신협회의 회원들에게 다음과 같이 알리며 또한 이에 통지하는 바임. 곧 그들의 편지 우편료와 소포 운송료를 스스로 지불하겠다는 제안은 본 협회에서 심의되었음. 본 협회는 그러한 제안이 그것을 내놓은 위대한 정신에 걸맞은 것이라 여기며, 이에 그에 대한 완전한 동의를 표명하는 바임.'\n\n비망록 작성자는 덧붙이기를 — 다음에 이어지는 기록은 그의 메모에 힘입은 것이다 — 무심한 관찰자라면, 위 결의안들이 낭독되는 동안 그(비망록 작성자)의 얼굴을 향해 골똘히 돌려져 있던 그 대머리와 둥근 안경에서 어쩌면 아무런 비범함도 알아채지 못했을 것이다. 그러나 픽윅의 거대한 두뇌가 그 이마 아래에서 움직이고 있으며, 픽윅의 빛나는 두 눈이 그 안경 너머에서 반짝이고 있음을 아는 이들에게, 그 광경은 참으로 흥미로운 것이었다. 햄프스테드의 그 웅대한 연못들을 그 수원까지 추적하고, 가시고기 이론으로 학계를 들썩이게 한 바로 그 사람이 거기 앉아 있었으니, 서리 내린 날 그 연못 가운데 하나의 깊은 물처럼, 혹은 흙으로 빚은 항아리의 가장 깊숙한 곳에 들어 있는 그 가시고기 한 마리의 표본처럼 고요하고 미동도 없었다. 그리고 그 광경은 얼마나 더 흥미로워졌던가 — 추종자들로부터 일제히 \"픽윅\"을 부르는 소리가 터져 나오자, 그 저명한 인물이 온전한 생기와 활력으로 불쑥 살아나, 앞서 앉아 있던 윈저식 의자 위로 천천히 올라서서, 그 자신이 창립한 클럽을 향해 연설하기 시작했을 때 말이다. 그 짜릿한 장면은 화가에게 얼마나 좋은 연구 거리가 되었으랴! 웅변하는 픽윅, 한 손은 우아하게 연미복 자락 뒤로 감추고, 다른 한 손은 그의 열렬한 웅변을 거들고자 허공에 흔들면서. 그 높아진 자리는 저 타이츠와 각반을 드러냈는데, 그것들이 평범한 사람의 몸에 걸쳐졌더라면 아무 주목도 받지 못하고 지나쳤을지 모르나, 픽윅이 그것들을 — 이런 표현을 써도 된다면 — 입혔을 때는 저절로 솟는 경외와 존경을 불러일으켰다. 그의 둘레에는 그의 여행이 안고 있는 위험을 기꺼이 함께 나누겠다고 자원한, 그리고 그의 발견의 영광에 동참할 운명을 지닌 사람들이 둘러서 있었다. 그의 오른편에는 트레이시 텁먼 씨가 앉아 있었다 — 너무도 마음 약한 텁먼, 무르익은 나이의 지혜와 경험에 더하여, 인간의 가장 흥미롭고도 용서받을 만한 약점인 사랑에 있어서는 소년의 열정과 정열까지 겸비한 사람이었다. 세월과 포식은 한때 낭만적이던 그 몸매를 불려 놓았다. 검은 비단 조끼는 점점 더 불룩해졌고, 그 아래 금시곗줄은 한 치 한 치 텁먼의 시야 밖으로 사라져 갔으며, 그 넉넉한 턱은 차츰 흰 크라바트의 경계를 잠식해 들어갔다. 그러나 텁먼의 영혼만은 아무 변화도 모른 채였으니 — 여성에 대한 흠모가 여전히 그를 지배하는 정념이었다. 그의 위대한 지도자 왼편에는 시인 기질의 스노드그래스가 앉아 있었고, 그 곁에는 또 사냥을 즐기는 윈클이 있었다. 전자는 개가죽 깃을 단 신비로운 푸른 망토에 시적으로 감싸여 있었고, 후자는 새로 산 초록빛 사냥 코트와 격자무늬 목도리, 그리고 몸에 꼭 맞는 황갈색 바지에 한층 더한 광채를 더하고 있었다.\n\n이 자리에서 픽윅 씨가 행한 연설과 그에 뒤이은 토론은 클럽 의사록에 기록되어 있다. 둘 다 다른 저명한 단체들의 토의와 강한 유사성을 띠는데, 위대한 인물들의 의사 진행 사이에서 닮은 점을 더듬어 보는 일은 언제나 흥미로운 법이므로, 우리는 그 기록을 이 지면에 옮겨 싣는다.\n\n'픽윅 씨는 관찰하기를(이라고 비망록 작성자는 적는다) 명성이란 모든 사람의 마음에 소중한 것이라 하였다. 시적 명성은 그의 벗 스노드그래스의 마음에 소중하고, 정복의 명성은 그의 벗 텁먼에게 똑같이 소중하며, 들과 하늘과 물에서 벌이는 스포츠로 명성을 얻고자 하는 욕망은 그의 벗 윈클의 가슴속에 가장 높이 자리하고 있다 하였다. 그(픽윅 씨) 자신도 인간적인 정념과 인간적인 감정에 좌우된다는 것을(환호) — 어쩌면 인간적인 약점에까지 좌우된다는 것을(요란하게 \"아니오\"를 외치는 소리) 부인하지는 않겠으나, 다만 이것만은 말하겠노라 하였다. 곧 만일 자만이라는 불길이 그의 가슴속에 일찍이 타오른 적이 있다면, 무엇보다 인류에게 이바지하고자 하는 욕망이 그것을 어김없이 꺼 버렸노라고. 인류의 칭송이 그가 매달리는 그네요, 박애가 그의 보험사무소라 하였다.(맹렬한 환호) 그는 얼마간 자부심을 느낀 적이 있었다 — 그는 이를 거리낌 없이 인정하며, 적들이 그것으로 마음껏 떠들도록 내버려 두겠노라 하였다 — 그는 자신의 가시고기 이론을 세상에 내놓았을 때 얼마간 자부심을 느꼈다 하였으니, 그것이 유명할 수도 있고 아닐 수도 있다 하였다.(\"유명합니다\"라는 외침과 큰 환호) 방금 그 목소리를 들은 그 명예로운 픽윅 회원의 주장을 받아들이겠노라 — 그것은 유명하다 — 그러나 설령 그 논저의 명성이 알려진 세계의 가장 먼 끝까지 뻗어 나간다 하더라도, 그 저작의 저술에 대해 그가 느낄 자부심은, 그의 생애에서 가장 자랑스러운 이 순간에 자기 둘레를 둘러보며 느끼는 자부심에 비하면 아무것도 아니라 하였다.(환호) 그는 보잘것없는 한 개인이다.(\"아니오, 아니오\") 그럼에도 그는 그들이 큰 영예와 더불어 얼마간의 위험이 따르는 임무를 위해 자신을 뽑아 주었음을 느끼지 않을 수 없다 하였다. 여행은 어지러운 상태에 놓여 있고, 마부들의 마음은 안정을 잃었다 하였다. 사방을 둘러보아 자기들 주위에서 벌어지고 있는 광경들을 헤아려 보라 하였다. 역마차들이 사방에서 뒤집히고, 말들이 날뛰며, 배들이 전복되고, 보일러들이 터지고 있다 하였다.(환호 — \"아니오\"라는 목소리) 아니라고요! (환호) 그토록 큰 소리로 \"아니오\"를 외친 그 명예로운 픽윅 회원더러 앞으로 나와, 할 수 있거든 그것을 부인해 보라 하였다.(환호) \"아니오\"를 외친 자가 누구인가? (열광적인 환호) 그것은 어떤 헛되고 좌절한 사내 — 그는 잡화상이라고는 말하지 않겠다 하였다(요란한 환호) — 그(픽윅 씨)의 연구에 어쩌면 분에 넘치게 주어진 칭송을 시샘하고, 자신의 변변찮은 경쟁 시도에 쏟아진 비난에 따끔하게 쓰라림을 느낀 나머지, 이제 이런 비열하고 중상모략적인 방식으로 —\n\n'블로턴 씨(올드게이트 출신)가 의사 진행 발언을 위해 일어섰다. 그 명예로운 픽윅 회원은 자기를 두고 말한 것인가? (\"질서를 지키시오\", \"의장\", \"그렇소\", \"아니오\", \"계속하시오\", \"그만두시오\" 등의 외침)\n\n'픽윅 씨는 떠들썩한 소리에 짓눌려 주저앉을 사람이 아니라 하였다. 그는 그 명예로운 신사를 두고 말한 것이라 하였다.(크게 술렁임)\n\n'블로턴 씨는 그렇다면 다만 이것만 말하겠노라 하였으니, 곧 그 명예로운 신사의 거짓되고 천박한 비난을 깊은 경멸로 물리치노라 하였다.(큰 환호) 그 명예로운 신사는 사기꾼이라 하였다.(극심한 혼란과 \"의장\", \"질서\"를 외치는 요란한 소리)\n\n'에이 스노드그래스 씨가 의사 진행 발언을 위해 일어섰다. 그는 의장에게 호소하였다.(경청) 그는 그 클럽의 두 회원 사이의 이 수치스러운 다툼이 계속되도록 허용되어야 하는지 알고 싶다 하였다.(옳소, 옳소)\n\n'의장은 그 명예로운 픽윅 회원이 방금 사용한 표현을 틀림없이 철회하리라 확신하였다.\n\n'블로턴 씨는 의장에 대해 더없는 경의를 표하면서도, 자신은 결코 그러지 않으리라 확신한다 하였다.\n\n'의장은 그 명예로운 신사에게, 방금 그의 입에서 새어 나온 표현을 통상적인 의미로 사용한 것인지 묻는 것이 자신의 절대적인 의무라 여겼다.\n\n'블로턴 씨는 주저 없이 말하기를, 그렇지 않다 — 자신은 그 말을 픽윅적인 의미로 사용한 것이라 하였다.(옳소, 옳소) 그는 개인적으로는 그 명예로운 신사에 대해 더없이 높은 존경과 경의를 품고 있음을 마땅히 인정하노라 하였으며, 다만 픽윅적인 관점에서 그를 사기꾼으로 여겼을 뿐이라 하였다.(옳소, 옳소)\n\n'픽윅 씨는 그 명예로운 벗의 공정하고 솔직하며 충분한 해명에 크게 흡족함을 느꼈다 하였다. 그는 자신의 발언 또한 단지 픽윅적인 해석을 띠도록 의도된 것임을 즉시 양해해 주기를 바란다 하였다.(환호)'\n\n여기서 기록은 끝나는데, 그토록 더없이 만족스럽고 명료한 지점에 이른 뒤였으니, 토론 또한 그러했으리라는 데 우리는 의심을 두지 않는다. 독자가 다음 장에서 보게 될 사실들에 관해서는 어떠한 공식 진술도 우리에게 없으나, 그것들은 편지와 그 밖의 필사 전거들로부터 세심하게 대조된 것이며, 그 진위가 너무도 확실하여 그것들을 하나로 이어진 형태로 서술하는 일을 정당하게 해 준다.\n\n만물을 부지런히 거드는 시종, 곧 태양이 막 떠올라 1827년 5월 13일 아침에 빛을 비추기 시작했을 때, 새뮤얼 픽윅 씨는 또 하나의 태양처럼 잠에서 솟구쳐 침실 창문을 활짝 열어젖히고 아래 세상을 내려다보았다. 고스웰 거리가 그의 발밑에 있었고, 고스웰 거리가 그의 오른편에 있었으며—눈이 닿는 데까지 고스웰 거리가 그의 왼편으로 뻗어 있었고, 고스웰 거리의 맞은편은 길 건너에 있었다. '눈앞에 놓인 것들을 살피는 데 만족하고 그 너머에 감추어진 진리는 들여다보지 않는 철학자들의 시야란,' 픽윅 씨는 생각했다, '이처럼 좁은 것이다. 사방에서 고스웰 거리를 둘러싸고 있는 저 감추어진 나라들을 꿰뚫어 보려는 노력 한 번 없이, 영원토록 고스웰 거리만 바라보고 만족할 수도 있으리라.' 이 아름다운 사색을 토로한 뒤, 픽윅 씨는 옷을 몸에 꿰고, 또 옷가지들을 여행 가방에 꿰어 넣기 시작했다. 위인은 의복을 정돈하는 데 좀처럼 지나치게 까다롭지 않은 법이다. 면도하고, 옷을 입고, 커피를 들이켜는 일이 곧 끝났다. 그리고 다시 한 시간 뒤, 픽윅 씨는 한 손에 여행 가방을 들고, 외투 주머니에 망원경을 넣고, 적어 둘 만한 어떤 발견이든 기록할 채비로 조끼에 수첩을 꽂은 채, 세인트 마틴스르그랜드의 마차 정거장에 다다라 있었다.\n\n\"마차!\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"여기 있습니다, 손님.\" 자루천으로 만든 외투와 같은 천의 앞치마를 두른, 인류 가운데 기묘한 표본 하나가 외쳤다. 그는 목에 놋쇠 표찰과 번호를 걸고 있어, 마치 무슨 진기품 수집 목록에라도 올라 있는 것처럼 보였다. 이 사람은 물지기였다. \"여기 있습니다, 손님. 자, 그럼, 첫 번째 마차!\" 그리고 첫 번째 마차의 마부가 첫 파이프를 피우고 있던 선술집에서 불려 나오자, 픽윅 씨와 그의 여행 가방은 마차 안으로 던져 넣어졌다.\n\n\"골든 크로스로.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"겨우 1실링짜리 손님이로구먼, 토미.\" 마차가 출발하자 마부가 친구인 물지기에게 알려 주려는 듯 시무룩하게 외쳤다.\n\n\"이 말은 나이가 몇이오, 친구?\" 픽윅 씨가 차비로 떼어 둔 1실링으로 코를 문지르며 물었다.\n\n\"마흔둘이오.\" 마부가 그를 곁눈질하며 대답했다.\n\n\"뭐라고요!\" 픽윅 씨가 수첩에 손을 얹으며 소리쳤다. 마부는 앞서 한 말을 다시 한 번 되풀이했다. 픽윅 씨가 그 사내의 얼굴을 뚫어지게 바라보았으나, 그 표정은 미동도 없었으므로, 그는 곧바로 그 사실을 적어 두었다.\n\n\"그러면 한 번 끌고 나오면 얼마나 오래 부리오?\" 픽윅 씨가 더 많은 정보를 캐며 물었다.\n\n\"두세 주일이오.\" 사내가 대답했다.\n\n\"주일이라고요!\" 픽윅 씨가 놀라 말했고, 다시 수첩이 나왔다.\n\n\"집에 있을 때는 펜턴빌에서 지내지요.\" 마부가 태연하게 말했다, \"하지만 우리는 좀처럼 이 녀석을 집에 데려가지 않소, 약해 빠진 탓에.\"\n\n\"약해 빠진 탓이라고요!\" 어리둥절해진 픽윅 씨가 되뇌었다.\n\n\"이 녀석은 마차에서 풀려나기만 하면 늘 쓰러져 버린답니다.\" 마부가 말을 이었다, \"하지만 마차에 매여 있을 때는 우리가 아주 바짝 떠받쳐 주고, 또 고삐를 아주 짧게 잡아 주니까, 좀처럼 쓰러질 수가 없지요. 게다가 우리한테는 아주 큼지막한 바퀴 한 쌍이 달려 있으니, 녀석이 일단 움직이기만 하면 그 바퀴가 뒤따라 굴러오는 통에, 녀석은 나아가는 수밖에 없소—어쩔 도리가 없지요.\"\n\n픽윅 씨는 이 진술을 한 마디도 빠짐없이 수첩에 적어 넣었으니, 고된 처지에 놓인 말의 끈질긴 생명력을 보여 주는 진기한 사례로 클럽에 전할 작정이었다. 그 기록을 채 마치기도 전에 그들은 골든 크로스에 다다랐다. 마부가 뛰어내렸고, 픽윅 씨가 내렸다. 자기네 저명한 지도자가 도착하기를 애타게 기다리고 있던 터프먼 씨, 스노드그래스 씨, 그리고 윈클 씨가 그를 맞이하려 몰려들었다.\n\n\"여기 차비요.\" 픽윅 씨가 마부에게 1실링을 내밀며 말했다.\n\n그 까닭 모를 인간이 돈을 길바닥에 내동댕이치더니, 그(픽윅 씨)와 그 액수를 걸고 한바탕 붙어 볼 즐거움을 허락해 달라고 비유적인 말투로 청했을 때, 그 학식 높은 분의 놀라움이란 어떠했겠는가!\n\n\"미쳤군.\" 스노드그래스 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"아니면 취했거나.\" 윈클 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"아니면 둘 다거나.\" 터프먼 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"덤벼!\" 마부가 태엽 인형처럼 주먹을 휘두르며 말했다. \"덤벼—넷 다 덤비라고.\"\n\n\"여기 구경거리 났다!\" 삯마차 마부 대여섯이 외쳤다. \"한판 붙어, 샘!\" 그러고는 신이 나서 일행 주위로 우르르 몰려들었다.\n\n\"무슨 소동이야, 샘?\" 검은 옥양목 소매를 단 신사 한 사람이 물었다.\n\n\"소동!\" 마부가 대답했다. \"이자가 내 번호는 왜 적어 가는 거냐고?\"\n\n\"난 당신 번호를 적은 적이 없소.\" 깜짝 놀란 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 왜 적어 갔소?\" 마부가 물었다.\n\n\"난 적은 적 없다니까.\" 픽윅 씨가 분개하여 말했다.\n\n\"누가 믿겠소,\" 마부가 군중에게 호소하며 말을 이었다, \"어느 밀고꾼이 남의 마차에 올라타서는 번호를 적어 가는 것도 모자라, 덤으로 사람이 하는 말까지 한 마디도 빠짐없이 받아 적었다는 걸 누가 믿겠냐고요.\" (그제야 픽윅 씨의 머리에 번쩍 떠오르는 것이 있었으니—바로 수첩이었다.)\n\n\"정말 그랬어?\" 다른 마부가 물었다.\n\n\"그래, 그랬다니까.\" 처음 마부가 대답했다. \"게다가 날 약 올려서 자기를 치게 만들어 놓고는, 여기 증인을 셋이나 세워 증명하려 들잖아. 하지만 여섯 달 콩밥을 먹는 한이 있어도 한 방 먹여 주겠어. 덤벼!\" 그러고는 마부가 제 사유 재산은 안중에도 없다는 듯이 모자를 땅바닥에 내동댕이치더니, 픽윅 씨의 안경을 후려쳐 떨어뜨리고, 이어 픽윅 씨의 코에 한 방, 픽윅 씨의 가슴에 또 한 방, 스노드그래스 씨의 눈에 세 번째 방, 그리고 변화를 주느라 터프먼 씨의 조끼에 네 번째 방을 날린 다음, 길 한복판으로 춤추듯 뛰어나갔다가 다시 보도로 돌아와, 마침내 윈클 씨의 몸에 잠시 채워져 있던 숨을 통째로 후려쳐 빼 버렸다. 그 모든 것이 겨우 대여섯 초 만의 일이었다.\n\n\"순경은 어디 있어?\" 스노드그래스 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"펌프 밑에다 처박아 버려.\" 뜨거운 파이를 파는 사람이 제안했다.\n\n\"이 일로 톡톡히 대가를 치를 거요.\" 픽윅 씨가 헐떡이며 말했다.\n\n\"밀고꾼이다!\" 군중이 외쳤다.\n\n\"덤벼.\" 그동안 쉴 새 없이 주먹을 휘두르던 마부가 외쳤다.\n\n여태껏 무리는 그 장면을 가만히 구경만 하고 있었으나, 픽윅 회원들이 밀고꾼이라는 소식이 그들 사이에 퍼지자, 그 흥분한 파이 장수의 제안을 실행에 옮기는 것이 옳은지 어떤지를 자못 활기차게 의논하기 시작했다. 그리고 만일 새로 나타난 한 인물의 끼어듦으로 이 난투극이 뜻밖에 끝나지 않았더라면, 그들이 어떤 폭행을 저질렀을지는 알 수 없는 노릇이었다.\n\n\"무슨 재미난 일이오?\" 초록색 외투를 입은, 다소 키가 크고 마른 청년 하나가 마차 마당에서 불쑥 나타나며 말했다.\n\n\"밀고꾼이오!\" 군중이 다시 외쳤다.\n\n\"우리는 그렇지 않소.\" 픽윅 씨가 사심 없는 청자라면 누구든 믿을 만한 어조로 우렁차게 말했다.\n\n\"아니라고요, 정말?—정말 아니에요?\" 청년이 픽윅 씨에게 호소하듯 말하며, 무리를 이룬 자들의 얼굴을 팔꿈치로 헤집는다는 어김없는 방법으로 군중을 뚫고 나아갔다.\n\n그 학식 높은 분은 몇 마디 다급한 말로 일의 실상을 설명했다.\n\n\"그럼, 이리 오시오.\" 초록 외투의 사내가 픽윅 씨를 억지로 끌어당기며, 가는 내내 떠들어 댔다. \"이봐, 924호, 차비 받고 썩 꺼져—훌륭한 신사분이야—내가 잘 아는 분이지—허튼소리 집어치워—이쪽이오, 손님—친구분들은 어디 계시오?—다 오해였소, 알겠소—신경 쓰지 마시오—사고란 일어나기 마련이지—아무리 점잖은 집안에서도—기죽지 마시오—운이 나빴을 뿐—저놈을 혼내 주시오—저놈 콧대를 꺾어 놔야지—본때를 보여 줘야 해—빌어먹을 악당 같으니.\" 이런 식의 끊어진 문장들을 길게 늘어놓으며, 그것도 놀라운 속도로 쏟아 내며, 낯선 사내는 여행객 대기실로 앞장서 갔고, 픽윅 씨와 그의 제자들이 바짝 뒤따랐다.\n\n\"여기, 종업원!\" 낯선 사내가 종을 무섭게 흔들어 대며 외쳤다. \"잔을 죽 돌려—브랜디에 물 타서, 뜨겁고 진하게, 달게, 넉넉히—눈을 다치셨소, 손님? 종업원! 이 신사분 눈에 댈 날고기 비프스테이크 가져와—멍에는 날고기 비프스테이크만 한 게 없지요, 손님. 차가운 가로등 기둥도 아주 좋지만, 가로등 기둥은 불편하거든—거리 한복판에 반 시간이나 눈을 가로등에 댄 채 서 있는 건 빌어먹게 우스운 꼴이지—어, 그렇잖소—하! 하!\" 그러고는 낯선 사내가 숨 돌릴 새도 없이 김이 무럭무럭 나는 브랜디 물을 단숨에 반 파인트나 들이켜고는, 무슨 별일도 없었다는 듯 태연히 의자에 몸을 던졌다.\n\n그의 세 동료가 새로 사귄 이에게 고마움을 표하느라 분주한 동안, 픽윅 씨는 그의 옷차림과 풍모를 살펴볼 여유가 있었다.\n\n그는 중간쯤 되는 키였으나, 몸이 마른 데다 다리가 길어 훨씬 더 커 보였다. 초록 외투는 연미복이 유행하던 시절에는 멋들어진 정장이었겠으나, 그 시절에는 분명 이 낯선 사내보다 훨씬 키가 작은 사람을 꾸며 주었을 것이니, 더럽고 빛바랜 소매가 손목에도 채 닿지 않았기 때문이다. 외투는 등판이 쩍 갈라질 위험을 무릅쓰고 턱밑까지 단단히 단추를 채워 놓았고, 셔츠 깃의 자취라곤 없는 낡은 목띠가 그의 목을 장식하고 있었다. 그의 변변찮은 검은 바지에는 군데군데 오랜 봉사를 말해 주는 반들거리는 자국이 드러나 있었고, 그것이 기우고 또 기운 한 켤레 구두 위로 아주 팽팽하게 끈으로 졸라매여 있었으니, 마치 더러운 흰 양말을 감추려는 듯했으나, 그래도 그 양말은 또렷이 드러나 보였다. 그의 길고 검은 머리카락은 낡고 찌그러진 모자 양옆 아래로 아무렇게나 물결치며 삐져나와 있었고, 장갑 끝과 외투 소맷부리 사이로 그의 맨손목이 언뜻언뜻 보였다. 그의 얼굴은 야위고 초췌했으나, 형언할 수 없는 멋들어진 뻔뻔함과 완벽한 침착함의 분위기가 그 사람 전체에 배어 있었다.\n\n픽윅 씨가 (다행히도 되찾은) 안경 너머로 바라본 인물이 바로 이러했으며, 친구들이 인사치레를 다 마치자 픽윅 씨는 곧 골라 뽑은 말로 그가 방금 베푼 도움에 더없이 따뜻한 감사를 표하기 시작했다.\n\n\"신경 쓰지 마시오.\" 낯선 사내가 인사말을 짤막하게 잘라 버리며 말했다. \"충분히 하셨소—그만하면 됐어. 그 마부 녀석 제법 야무지더군—주먹을 곧잘 썼어. 하지만 내가 만일 댁의 초록 외투를 걸친 친구였다면—빌어먹을—그놈 대갈통을 한 방 갈겨 줬을 거요—암, 그랬고말고—한순간에 끝내 버렸지—파이 장수 녀석도—농담이 아니오.\"\n\n이 조리 있는 연설은 '코모도어호'가 막 출발하려 한다고 알리러 들어온 로체스터행 마차의 마부 때문에 중단되었다.\n\n\"코모도어!\" 낯선 사내가 벌떡 일어서며 말했다. \"내가 탈 마차요—자리는 예약해 뒀지—바깥자리 하나—브랜디 물값은 댁이 치르도록 맡기겠소—5파운드 거슬러 받아야 하는데—불량 은화라—버밍엄제 단추 같은 가짜—안 통해—어림없지—어떻소?\" 그러고는 다 안다는 듯 고개를 절레절레 흔들었다.\n\n그런데 마침 픽윅 씨와 그의 세 동료도 로체스터를 첫 기착지로 삼기로 정한 터였다. 그래서 새로 사귄 이에게 자기들도 같은 도시로 가는 길이라고 알린 뒤, 그들은 다 함께 앉을 수 있는 마차 뒤쪽 자리를 차지하기로 합의했다.\n\n\"자, 올라가시오.\" 낯선 사내가 픽윅 씨를 지붕 위로 부축해 올리면서, 어찌나 급하게 서둘렀던지 그 신사의 위엄 있는 거동을 적잖이 해치고 말았다.\n\n\"짐이 있으십니까, 손님?\" 마부가 물었다.\n\n\"누구—나 말이오? 여기 갈색 종이 꾸러미 하나, 그게 전부요—나머지 짐은 배편으로 보냈지—못질해 봉한 포장 상자들—집채만 한 것들이오—무겁고, 무겁고, 빌어먹게 무겁지.\" 낯선 사내가 대답하며, 갈색 종이 꾸러미를 되도록 많이 주머니에 우겨 넣었는데, 그 꾸러미는 셔츠 한 장과 손수건 한 장이 들어 있을 뿐임을 자못 수상쩍게 드러내고 있었다.\n\n\"머리, 머리—머리 조심하시오!\" 그 시절 마차 마당의 입구를 이루고 있던 낮은 아치 아래로 나오면서 수다스러운 낯선 사내가 외쳤다. \"끔찍한 곳이지—위험한 일이오—얼마 전에도—아이 다섯에—어머니가—키 큰 부인이, 샌드위치를 먹으면서—아치를 깜박했지—와장창—쾅—아이들이 돌아보니—어머니 머리가 떨어져 나갔어—손에는 샌드위치를 쥔 채로—넣을 입이 없어진 거지—한 집안의 가장이 머리가 날아간 거요—끔찍해, 끔찍해! 화이트홀을 보고 계시오, 손님?—훌륭한 곳이지—조그만 창문 하나—거기서도 어떤 분 머리가 날아갔지, 안 그렇소, 손님?—그분도 충분히 정신을 바짝 차리지 못했던 거요—어떻소, 손님, 어떻소?\"\n* 화이트홀: 찰스 1세가 처형된 곳을 가리킨다.\n\n\"인간사의 기이한 무상함을 곰곰 생각하던 참이오.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"아! 알겠소—오늘은 궁전 문으로 들어가고, 내일은 창문으로 나가는 거지. 철학자시오, 손님?\"\n\n\"인간 본성을 관찰하는 사람이오.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"아, 나도 그렇소. 할 일은 적고 벌이는 더 적을 때면 사람들 대개가 그렇게 되지요. 시인이시오, 손님?\"\n\n\"내 친구 스노드그래스 씨가 시적인 기질이 다분하지요.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"나도 그렇소.\" 낯선 사내가 말했다. \"서사시—만 행짜리—칠월 혁명을—현장에서 지었지—낮에는 마르스요 밤에는 아폴로—대포를 쾅 쏘고, 수금을 뚱땅 켜고.\"\n* 이것은 징글 씨의 상상력이 지닌 예언적 힘을 보여 주는 놀라운 사례이니, 이 대화는 1827년에 일어난 일이고 혁명은 1830년에 일어났다.\n\n\"그 영광스러운 장면에 직접 계셨소, 손님?\" 스노드그래스 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"있었냐고! 있고말고. 머스킷 총을 쏘았지—한 가지 착상에 불이 붙어서 말이오—포도주 가게로 뛰어들어가—적어 두고—다시 뛰쳐나와—휙, 쾅—또 한 가지 착상—다시 포도주 가게로—펜과 잉크—다시 뛰쳐나와—베고 또 찌르고—굉장한 시절이었소, 손님. 사냥꾼이시오, 손님?\" 그가 불쑥 윈클 씨에게 돌아서며 물었다.\n\n\"조금 합니다, 손님.\" 그 신사가 대답했다.\n\n\"훌륭한 소일거리지요, 손님—훌륭한 소일거리고말고요—개는 기르시오, 손님?\"\n\n\"지금은 없습니다.\" 윈클 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"아! 개를 길러야 하오—멋진 짐승들이지—영리한 녀석들이오—나도 한때 개를 한 마리 길렀는데—포인터였소—놀라운 본능을 지녔지—어느 날 사냥을 나갔는데—울타리 친 땅에 들어서서—휘파람을 불었지—개가 멈춰 섰소—다시 휘파람을 불었는데—'폰토'—꿈쩍도 않더군. 돌처럼 굳어서—불렀지—'폰토, 폰토'—움직이지를 않는 거요—개가 못 박힌 듯이—어떤 푯말을 노려보고 있길래—올려다보니, 거기 이런 글귀가 적혀 있더군—'사냥터지기에게 이 울타리 안에서 발견되는 개는 모두 쏘아 죽이라는 명령이 내려져 있음'—그 앞을 지나가려 하질 않았소—기특한 개였지—값진 개였소, 그게—참으로 그랬소.\"\n\n\"그것 참 진기한 일이군요.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"제가 그걸 적어 두어도 되겠습니까?\"\n\n\"물론이오, 손님, 물론이지요—같은 짐승에 관한 일화가 백 가지는 더 있소—고운 아가씨군요, 손님\" (그는 트레이시 터프먼 씨에게 말했는데, 터프먼 씨는 길가의 한 젊은 숙녀에게 여러 차례 픽윅답지 않은 추파를 던지고 있던 참이었다).\n\n\"참으로 곱군요!\" 터프먼 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"영국 아가씨들은 스페인 아가씨들만큼 곱지는 못하오—참으로 고귀한 여인들이지—칠흑 같은 머리에—검은 눈동자에—어여쁜 자태—사랑스러운 여인들—아름답소.\"\n\n\"스페인에 가 보셨소, 손님?\" 트레이시 터프먼 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"거기서 살았소—오랜 세월을.\"\n\n\"많은 여인을 정복하셨소, 손님?\" 터프먼 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"정복이라! 수천이오. 돈 볼라로 피즈긱—대귀족이지—외동딸이 있었는데—도냐 크리스티나—눈부신 여인이었소—나를 미치도록 사랑했지—질투심 많은 아버지—기개 높은 딸—잘생긴 영국인—절망에 빠진 도냐 크리스티나—청산가리를—내 여행 가방에 위세척기가 있어서—시술을 했지—늙은 볼라로가 황홀경에 빠져—우리 결합을 승낙했소—두 손을 맞잡고 눈물을 펑펑 쏟았지—낭만적인 이야기요—참으로.\"\n\n\"그 부인은 지금 영국에 있소, 손님?\" 그녀의 매력을 묘사한 말이 강렬한 인상을 남긴 터프먼 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"죽었소, 손님—죽었지.\" 낯선 사내가 아주 낡은 삼베 손수건의 얼마 남지 않은 자투리를 오른쪽 눈에 갖다 대며 말했다. \"위세척기에서 끝내 회복하지 못했소—체질이 망가져서—희생되고 말았지.\"\n\n\"그러면 그녀의 아버지는요?\" 시적인 스노드그래스가 물었다.\n\n\"회한과 비참뿐이었소.\" 낯선 사내가 대답했다. \"갑작스러운 실종—온 도시가 떠들썩했지—샅샅이 찾았지만 허사였소—큰 광장의 공공 분수가 별안간 물을 뿜기를 멈추더니—몇 주가 지나도—여전히 막힌 채였소—인부들을 불러 청소를 시켰지—물을 다 빼냈더니—장인 어른이 본관 파이프에 머리부터 처박힌 채 발견되었소, 오른쪽 장화 속에는 전부를 자백한 글을 넣어 둔 채로—끌어내자, 분수가 예전처럼 멀쩡히 다시 물을 뿜더군.\"\n\n\"그 짤막한 사연을 적어 두어도 되겠습니까, 손님?\" 깊이 감동한 스노드그래스 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"물론이오, 손님, 물론이지요—듣고 싶으시면 쉰 가지는 더 있소—내 삶이 참 기이하지—꽤나 별난 내력이오—대단치는 않아도, 진기하지요.\"\n\n이런 식으로, 마차가 말을 바꿀 때면 그 사이사이 에일 한 잔씩을 곁들여 가며, 낯선 사내는 이야기를 계속해 나갔으니, 그들이 로체스터 다리에 다다랐을 무렵에는 픽윅 씨와 스노드그래스 씨의 수첩이 둘 다 그의 모험담에서 골라 뽑은 내용으로 빼곡히 채워져 있었다.\n\n\"장엄한 폐허로다!\" 그 멋진 옛 성이 시야에 들어오자, 오거스터스 스노드그래스 씨가 그를 돋보이게 하는 온갖 시적 열정을 담아 말했다.\n\n\"호고가에게 얼마나 좋은 연구 거리인가!\" 픽윅 씨가 망원경을 눈에 갖다 대며 입에서 떨어뜨린 말이 바로 이것이었다.\n\n\"아! 훌륭한 곳이오.\" 낯선 사내가 말했다. \"장엄한 건축물이지—험상궂은 성벽—허물어져 가는 아치들—어두운 구석들—부서져 내리는 계단들—오래된 대성당도 있고—흙냄새가 나지—순례자들의 발길이 옛 계단을 닳게 했소—작은 색슨식 문들—극장 매표소 상자 같은 고해실들—그 수도사들 참 별난 손님들이었지—교황이며, 재무 대신이며, 온갖 부류의 늙은이들이, 시뻘건 큰 얼굴에 부러진 코를 하고서, 날마다 나타났지—무두질한 가죽 조끼며—화승총이며—석관이며—훌륭한 곳이오—오래된 전설들도 있지—기이한 이야기들이오. 일품이지.\" 그러고는 낯선 사내가 마차가 멈춰 선 하이 가의 불 여관에 다다를 때까지 혼잣말을 이어 갔다.\n\n\"여기 묵으시오, 손님?\" 너새니얼 윈클 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"여기—나는 아니오—하지만 댁들은 묵는 게 좋겠소—좋은 집이오—침대도 훌륭하고—라이트 여관은 바로 옆집인데, 비싸오—아주 비싸지—종업원을 쳐다보기만 해도 계산서에 반 크라운이 붙소—친구 집에서 식사를 하면 커피실에서 먹는 것보다 더 받지—괴상한 작자들이오—참으로.\"\n\n윈클 씨가 픽윅 씨에게 돌아서서 몇 마디 중얼거렸다. 픽윅 씨에게서 스노드그래스 씨에게로, 스노드그래스 씨에게서 터프먼 씨에게로 속삭임이 오갔고, 동의하는 끄덕임이 오갔다. 픽윅 씨가 낯선 사내에게 말을 건넸다.\n\n\"오늘 아침 당신은 우리에게 아주 중요한 도움을 베풀어 주셨소, 손님.\" 그가 말했다, \"우리의 작은 감사의 표시로, 만찬에 함께해 주시는 영광을 청해도 되겠습니까?\"\n\n\"더없는 기쁨이오—이래라저래라 할 처지는 아니지만, 구운 닭에 버섯이면—일품이지! 몇 시요?\"\n\n\"어디 봅시다.\" 픽윅 씨가 회중시계를 보며 대답했다. \"지금이 거의 세 시군요. 다섯 시로 할까요?\"\n\n\"내게 더없이 좋소.\" 낯선 사내가 말했다. \"정각 다섯 시에—그때까지—몸조심들 하시오.\" 그러고는 찌그러진 모자를 머리에서 몇 인치 들어 올렸다가 아무렇게나 한쪽으로 잔뜩 기울여 다시 얹은 다음, 낯선 사내는 갈색 종이 꾸러미를 주머니에서 반쯤 삐죽 내민 채, 마당을 활기차게 걸어 올라가 하이 가로 접어들었다.\n\n\"여러 나라를 두루 다닌 여행객이자, 사람과 사물을 면밀히 관찰하는 사람임이 분명해.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그분의 시를 보고 싶군요.\" 스노드그래스 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그 개를 봤더라면 좋았을 텐데요.\" 윈클 씨가 말했다.\n\n터프먼 씨는 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 그러나 그는 도냐 크리스티나와, 위세척기와, 분수를 생각했다. 그리고 그의 눈에는 눈물이 그렁그렁 고였다.\n\n전용 응접실을 잡고, 침실들을 살펴보고, 만찬을 주문한 뒤, 일행은 도시와 인근 지역을 구경하러 걸어 나갔다.\n\n스트라우드, 로체스터, 채텀, 브롬프턴, 이 네 도시에 관한 픽윅 씨의 기록을 꼼꼼히 통독해 보아도, 그곳들의 외관에 대한 그의 인상이 같은 곳을 두루 다닌 다른 여행객들의 인상과 어떤 중요한 점에서도 다르다는 사실은 찾아볼 수 없다. 그의 전반적인 묘사는 쉽게 간추릴 수 있다.\n\n\"이 도시들의 주요 산물은,\" 픽윅 씨는 말한다, \"군인, 선원, 유대인, 백악, 새우, 장교, 그리고 조선소 인부인 듯하다. 큰길가에 주로 내놓고 파는 상품은 선박 용품, 캐러멜 과자, 사과, 넙치류, 그리고 굴이다. 거리는 활기차고 생기 넘치는 모습을 띠는데, 이는 주로 군인들의 흥겨움에서 비롯된다. 박애의 정신을 지닌 사람에게는, 이 늠름한 사내들이 동물적 혈기와 독한 술이 넘쳐흐른 까닭에 비틀거리며 걸어가는 모습을 보는 것이 참으로 즐거운 일이다. 더욱이 그들을 졸졸 따라다니며 놀려 대는 것이 소년 무리에게 값싸고 순진한 오락거리가 됨을 떠올린다면 더욱 그러하다. 그들의 더없는 호인다움은,\" 픽윅 씨는 덧붙인다, \"무엇과도 견줄 수 없다. 내가 도착하기 바로 전날에도, 그들 가운데 한 사람이 어느 선술집에서 더없이 무례한 모욕을 당한 일이 있었다. 술집 아가씨가 그에게 술을 더 따라 주기를 딱 잘라 거절했던 것이다. 그 보답으로 그는 (그저 장난삼아) 총검을 뽑아 그 아가씨의 어깨에 상처를 입혔다. 그런데도 이 훌륭한 사내는 이튿날 아침 그 집에 제일 먼저 내려가, 그 일을 너그러이 눈감아 주고 일어난 일을 잊을 용의가 있노라고 기꺼이 표명했던 것이다!\n\n\"이 도시들의 담배 소비량은,\" 픽윅 씨는 말을 잇는다, \"어마어마함이 틀림없으며, 거리에 배어 있는 그 냄새는 흡연을 극히 좋아하는 사람들에게는 더없이 향기로울 것이 분명하다. 피상적인 여행객이라면 이곳의 두드러진 특징인 불결함에 대해 반감을 품을지도 모르나, 그것을 교역과 상업적 번영의 징표로 보는 사람들에게는 참으로 흐뭇한 일이다.\"\n\n다섯 시 정각에 낯선 사내가 왔고, 곧이어 만찬이 나왔다. 그는 갈색 종이 꾸러미는 벗어 놓았으나, 옷차림에는 아무런 변화가 없었으며, 가능하다면 전보다 한층 더 수다스러웠다.\n\n\"이건 뭐요?\" 종업원이 덮개 하나를 치우자 그가 물었다.\n\n\"가자미입니다, 손님.\"\n\n\"가자미라—아!—일품 생선이지—다 런던에서 오는 거요—역마차 업자들이 정치 만찬을 여느라—가자미 운송이지—바구니가 수십 개씩—교활한 작자들이오. 포도주 한잔하시지요, 손님.\"\n\n\"기꺼이 하지요.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. 그러자 낯선 사내는 먼저 그와 한 잔, 그다음 스노드그래스 씨와 한 잔, 그다음 터프먼 씨와 한 잔, 그다음 윈클 씨와 한 잔, 그다음 일행 전체와 다 함께 한 잔을, 그가 떠들어 대는 만큼이나 거의 빠른 속도로 들이켰다.\n\n\"계단이 지독하게 어수선하군, 종업원.\" 낯선 사내가 말했다. \"긴 의자들은 올라가고—목수들은 내려오고—등이며, 유리잔이며, 하프며. 무슨 일이 벌어지는 거요?\"\n\n\"무도회입니다, 손님.\" 종업원이 말했다.\n\n\"친목회 말이오?\"\n\n\"아니요, 손님, 친목회가 아닙니다, 손님. 자선 기금을 마련하는 무도회입니다, 손님.\"\n\n\"이 도시에 고운 여인들이 많소, 혹시 아시오, 손님?\" 터프먼 씨가 큰 관심을 보이며 물었다.\n\n\"눈부시지요—일품이오. 켄트요, 손님—켄트는 누구나 알지요—사과며, 버찌며, 홉이며, 그리고 여인들. 포도주 한잔하시지요, 손님!\"\n\n\"기꺼이 하지요.\" 터프먼 씨가 대답했다. 낯선 사내가 잔을 채우고, 또 비웠다.\n\n\"저도 꼭 가 보고 싶군요.\" 터프먼 씨가 무도회 이야기를 다시 꺼내며 말했다, \"꼭이요.\"\n\n\"입장권은 카운터에서 파십니다, 손님.\" 종업원이 끼어들었다. \"한 장에 반 기니입니다, 손님.\"\n\n터프먼 씨가 그 흥겨운 자리에 꼭 참석하고 싶다는 간절한 뜻을 다시 한 번 내비쳤으나, 스노드그래스 씨의 흐릿해진 눈에서도, 픽윅 씨의 멍한 시선에서도 아무런 호응을 얻지 못하자, 막 식탁에 차려진 포트와인과 후식에 큰 관심을 쏟았다. 종업원이 물러갔고, 일행은 만찬 뒤에 이어지는 아늑한 두어 시간을 즐기도록 남겨졌다.\n\n\"실례하오, 손님.\" 낯선 사내가 말했다, \"병이 멈춰 있구려—죽 돌리시오—해가 도는 방향으로—단춧구멍을 거쳐서—한 방울도 남기지 말고.\" 그러고는 두어 분 전에 채워 두었던 잔을 비우고는, 이런 일에 익숙한 사람다운 태도로 또 한 잔을 따랐다.\n\n포도주가 돌았고, 새 병이 주문되었다. 손님은 떠들어 댔고, 픽윅 회원들은 귀를 기울였다. 터프먼 씨는 시간이 갈수록 점점 더 무도회에 마음이 기울었다. 픽윅 씨의 얼굴은 만인을 향한 박애의 표정으로 빛났고, 윈클 씨와 스노드그래스 씨는 곤히 잠들어 버렸다.\n\n\"위층에서 시작하는군.\" 낯선 사내가 말했다. \"사람들 소리가 들리오—바이올린을 조율하는데—이제 하프요—자, 시작이오.\" 아래층까지 흘러내려 온 갖가지 소리가 첫 카드리유의 시작을 알리고 있었다.\n\n\"아, 정말 가 보고 싶군요.\" 터프먼 씨가 다시 말했다.\n\n\"나도 그렇소.\" 낯선 사내가 말했다, \"빌어먹을 짐짝이—무거운 화물선에—입고 갈 게 없으니—고약하지 않소?\"\n\n그런데 만인을 향한 자비심이야말로 픽윅 사상의 으뜸가는 특징 가운데 하나였고, 그 고귀한 원칙을 그토록 열성적으로 받드는 데 트레이시 터프먼 씨보다 더 두드러진 사람도 없었다. 그 훌륭한 분이 자선의 대상들에게 입던 옷가지나 금전적 도움을 얻도록 다른 회원들의 집으로 안내해 준 사례가 클럽 회보에 기록된 것만 해도 거의 믿기 어려울 만큼 많았다.\n\n\"그 일에 쓰시도록 옷 한 벌 기꺼이 빌려 드리고 싶습니다만,\" 트레이시 터프먼 씨가 말했다, \"당신은 다소 호리호리하시고, 저는—\"\n\n\"다소 뚱뚱하시지—다 자란 바쿠스—포도 잎은 벗어 던지고—술통에서 내려와 거친 모직을 걸치셨군, 어떻소?—두 번 증류한 게 아니라 두 번 압착한 거지—하! 하! 포도주 좀 돌리시오.\"\n\n터프먼 씨가, 낯선 사내가 그토록 재빨리 비워 버리는 포도주를 어서 돌리라고 명령조로 요구받은 데에 다소 분개했는지, 아니면 픽윅 클럽의 영향력 있는 회원이 술통에서 내려온 바쿠스에 망신스럽게 견주어진 데에 지당하게도 발끈했는지는 아직 완전히 밝혀지지 않은 사실이다. 그는 포도주를 돌리고, 두 번 헛기침을 하고는, 몇 초 동안 낯선 사내를 매서운 강렬함으로 노려보았다. 그러나 그 인물이 그의 살피는 시선 아래서도 더없이 침착하고 아주 태연해 보였으므로, 그는 차츰 누그러져 다시 무도회 이야기로 돌아갔다.\n\n\"제가 말씀드리려던 것은요, 손님,\" 그가 말했다, \"제 옷은 너무 클 테지만, 제 친구 윈클 씨의 옷 한 벌이라면 어쩌면 더 잘 맞으실 것 같다는 겁니다.\"\n\n낯선 사내가 눈으로 윈클 씨의 치수를 가늠하더니, 그 눈이 만족스러움으로 반짝이며 말했다, \"안성맞춤이오.\"\n\n터프먼 씨가 주위를 둘러보았다. 스노드그래스 씨와 윈클 씨에게 잠을 부르는 위력을 떨쳤던 포도주가, 어느새 픽윅 씨의 의식 속으로도 스며들어 있었다. 그 신사는 만찬과 그 여파로 빚어지는 혼곤함에 앞서 거치는 여러 단계를 차츰 지나온 터였다. 그는 흥겨움의 절정에서 비참함의 나락으로, 그리고 비참함의 나락에서 다시 흥겨움의 절정으로 옮아가는 흔한 변천을 겪었다. 마치 파이프에 바람이 든 거리의 가스등처럼, 그는 한순간 부자연스러운 광채를 내뿜었다가, 이내 거의 알아볼 수 없을 만큼 사그라들었고, 잠시 뒤 다시 확 타올라 한순간 주위를 밝혔다가, 흔들리며 비틀거리는 듯한 불안정한 불빛으로 가물거리다가, 마침내 아예 꺼져 버렸다. 그의 머리는 가슴 위로 푹 떨어졌고, 이따금 부분적으로 컥컥 막히는 소리를 곁들인 끊임없는 코골이만이 이 위인이 거기 있음을 알려 주는 유일한 청각적 표시였다.\n\n무도회에 참석하여 켄트 숙녀들의 아름다움에 대한 첫인상을 얻어 보고 싶은 유혹이 터프먼 씨를 강하게 사로잡았다. 낯선 사내를 함께 데려가고 싶은 유혹도 그에 못지않게 컸다. 그는 이곳과 이곳 사람들을 전혀 알지 못했는데, 낯선 사내는 어릴 때부터 여기서 살아온 사람처럼 그 둘 모두를 훤히 꿰고 있는 듯했다. 윈클 씨는 잠들어 있었고, 터프먼 씨는 이런 일에 충분한 경험이 있어, 그가 깨어나는 순간 자연의 흔한 순리대로 묵직하게 침대로 굴러갈 것임을 알고 있었다. 그는 마음을 정하지 못했다. \"잔을 채워 죽 돌리시오.\" 지칠 줄 모르는 손님이 말했다.\n\n터프먼 씨는 요청받은 대로 했다. 그리고 마지막 잔이 더해 준 자극이 그의 결심을 굳혀 주었다.\n\n\"윈클의 침실이 제 방 안쪽에 붙어 있습니다.\" 터프먼 씨가 말했다. \"지금 그를 깨운들 제가 원하는 바를 이해시킬 수가 없겠지만, 그가 융단 가방 속에 정장 한 벌을 넣어 둔 건 압니다. 그러니 당신이 그걸 무도회에 입고 갔다가 돌아와서 벗어 놓으면, 제가 그를 조금도 성가시게 하지 않고 도로 갖다 둘 수 있을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"일품이오.\" 낯선 사내가 말했다, \"기막힌 계획이지—빌어먹게 묘한 처지군—포장 상자 속에 외투가 열네 벌이나 있는데, 남의 옷을 빌려 입어야 하다니—아주 좋은 생각이오, 그게—참으로.\"\n\n\"입장권을 사야 합니다.\" 터프먼 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"1기니를 쪼갤 것까지야.\" 낯선 사내가 말했다, \"누가 둘 값을 다 낼지 동전을 던집시다—내가 부르겠소. 댁이 던지시오—단번에—여자—여자—매혹적인 여자라.\" 그러자 용(예의상 여자라 부르는 그것)이 위로 오게 동전이 떨어졌다.\n\n* 당시 소버린 금화 뒷면에는 용을 무찌르는 성 게오르기우스가 새겨져 있었는데, 이를 농으로 '여자(앞면의 여왕상)'라 부른 것이다.\n터프먼 씨가 종을 울려 입장권을 사고, 침실용 촛대를 주문했다. 다시 십오 분 뒤, 낯선 사내는 너새니얼 윈클 씨의 정장 한 벌을 완벽하게 차려입고 있었다.\n\n\"새 외투입니다.\" 낯선 사내가 체경 앞에서 자못 흡족하게 제 모습을 살피는 동안 터프먼 씨가 말했다. \"우리 클럽 단추를 달아 처음 지은 것이지요.\" 그러면서 그는 동료들의 주의를, 한가운데 픽윅 씨의 흉상이 새겨지고 양옆에 'P. C.'라는 글자가 박힌 커다란 금박 단추로 이끌었다.\n\n\"'P. C.'라.\" 낯선 사내가 말했다. \"괴상한 차림이군—노친네 초상에다—'P. C.'까지—'P. C.'가 무슨 뜻이오—'괴상한 외투(Peculiar Coat)'요, 어떻소?\" 터프먼 씨가 치미는 분노와 큰 자부심을 안고 그 신비로운 표장을 설명해 주었다.\n\n\"허리가 좀 짧지 않소?\" 낯선 사내가 등 한가운데까지 올라와 붙은 허리 단추를 거울로 흘끗 보려고 몸을 비틀며 말했다. \"관리 우편배달부 외투 같군—그런 외투들 참 괴상하지—도급으로 짓거든—치수도 안 재고—신의 알 수 없는 섭리지—키 작은 사람은 죄다 긴 외투를 받고—키 큰 사람은 죄다 짧은 걸 받으니.\" 이런 식으로 떠들어 대면서, 터프먼 씨의 새 동료는 자기 옷을, 아니 차라리 윈클 씨의 옷을 매만진 뒤, 터프먼 씨를 대동하고 무도회장으로 통하는 계단을 올라갔다.\n\n\"성함이 어찌 되십니까, 손님?\" 문가에 선 사내가 말했다. 트레이시 터프먼 씨가 자기 직함을 알리려 앞으로 나서려는데, 낯선 사내가 그를 막았다.\n\n\"이름은 아예 대지 마시오.\" 그러고는 터프먼 씨에게 속삭였다, \"이름은 안 통하오—알려지지 않았으니—그런대로 괜찮은 이름이긴 하지만, 대단한 이름은 아니지—작은 모임에서는 일품인 이름이지만, 공개 집회에서는 인상을 못 남기지—익명이 상책이오—런던에서 온 신사들—저명한 외국 인사들—뭐든 좋소.\" 문이 활짝 열렸고, 트레이시 터프먼 씨와 낯선 사내가 무도회장으로 들어섰다.\n\n그곳은 진홍색 천을 씌운 긴 의자들과, 유리 샹들리에에 꽂힌 밀랍 양초들이 있는 기다란 방이었다. 악사들은 높직한 우리 안에 단단히 갇혀 있었고, 두세 무리의 무용수들이 카드리유를 차례차례 추어 나가고 있었다. 옆에 딸린 카드방에는 카드 탁자 둘이 차려져, 늙은 부인 두 쌍과 그에 걸맞은 수의 살집 좋은 신사들이 휘스트를 치고 있었다.\n\n피날레가 끝나자 무용수들이 방 안을 거닐었고, 터프먼 씨와 그의 동료는 한쪽 구석에 자리를 잡고 사람들을 살펴보았다.\n\n\"매혹적인 여인들이군요.\" 터프먼 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"잠깐 기다려 보시오.\" 낯선 사내가 말했다, \"곧 재미난 구경거리가 생길 거요—거물들이 아직 안 왔거든—괴상한 곳이지—조선소의 상류층 사람들은 조선소의 하류층 사람들을 모른 척하고—조선소의 하류층 사람들은 하급 귀족을 모른 척하고—하급 귀족은 상인들을 모른 척하고—감독관은 아무도 모른 척하지.\"\n\n\"저기 머리칼이 옅고 눈이 분홍빛인, 별난 옷을 차려입은 어린 소년은 누구요?\" 터프먼 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"쉿, 제발—분홍빛 눈에—별난 옷에—어린 소년이라니—당치도 않소—제97연대 소위요—존경하올 윌멋 스나이프—대단한 집안이지—스나이프 가문—참으로.\"\n\n\"토머스 클러버 경, 클러버 부인, 그리고 클러버 양들께서 입장하십니다!\" 문가의 사내가 우렁찬 목소리로 외쳤다. 푸른 외투에 번쩍이는 단추를 단 키 큰 신사 하나와, 푸른 새틴을 걸친 덩치 큰 부인 하나와, 같은 빛깔의 유행하는 옷을 차려입은 비슷한 체격의 젊은 숙녀 둘이 입장하자, 온 방 안에 일대 소동이 일었다.\n\n\"감독관이오—조선소장이지—거물이오—놀랄 만한 거물이지.\" 자선 위원회가 토머스 클러버 경과 그 가족을 방 윗자리로 안내하는 동안, 낯선 사내가 터프먼 씨의 귀에 대고 속삭였다. 존경하올 윌멋 스나이프와 그 밖의 저명한 신사들이 클러버 양들에게 경의를 표하려 몰려들었고, 토머스 클러버 경은 꼿꼿이 똑바로 서서, 검은 목수건 너머로 모인 사람들을 위엄 있게 굽어보았다.\n\n\"스미시 씨, 스미시 부인, 그리고 스미시 양들.\" 다음 입장 소개였다.\n\n\"스미시 씨는 뭐 하는 분이오?\" 트레이시 터프먼 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"조선소에서 뭔가 하는 사람이오.\" 낯선 사내가 대답했다. 스미시 씨가 토머스 클러버 경에게 공손히 절했고, 토머스 클러버 경은 의식적인 겸양으로 그 인사에 답했다. 클러버 부인이 안경 너머로 스미시 부인과 그 가족을 망원경 들여다보듯 훑어보았고, 스미시 부인은 또 그 나름으로, 남편이 조선소와는 아무 상관도 없는 어느 딴 부인을 빤히 쳐다보았다.\n\n\"불더 대령, 불더 대령 부인, 그리고 불더 양.\" 다음 도착이었다.\n\n\"수비대장이오.\" 터프먼 씨의 묻는 듯한 눈길에 답하여 낯선 사내가 말했다.\n\n불더 양은 클러버 양들의 따뜻한 환영을 받았고, 불더 대령 부인과 클러버 부인 사이의 인사는 더없이 다정한 것이었으며, 불더 대령과 토머스 클러버 경은 코담뱃갑을 주고받으며, 마치 한 쌍의 알렉산더 셀커크처럼—'굽어보는 모든 것의 군주'처럼—보였다.\n* 알렉산더 셀커크: 로빈슨 크루소의 모델이 된 무인도 표류자로, 카우퍼의 시 한 구절에서 인용한 것이다.\n\n그곳의 귀족층—불더 가, 클러버 가, 스나이프 가—이 방 윗자리에서 이렇듯 위엄을 지키는 동안, 다른 계층의 사람들도 방 다른 곳에서 그들을 본받고 있었다. 제97연대의 덜 귀족적인 장교들은 조선소의 덜 중요한 관리들의 가족에게 헌신했다. 변호사들의 부인들과 포도주 상인의 부인이 또 다른 등급을 이끌었고 (양조업자의 부인은 불더 가를 방문했다), 우체국장인 톰린슨 부인은 만장일치로 상인 패거리의 우두머리로 추대된 듯했다.\n\n그 자리에서, 제 패거리 안에서 가장 인기 있는 인물 가운데 하나는, 머리 둘레에 뻣뻣한 검은 머리칼이 고리처럼 둘려 있고 정수리에는 널찍한 대머리 벌판이 펼쳐진 자그마한 뚱뚱보였으니—제97연대 군의관 슬래머 박사였다. 박사는 누구하고나 코담배를 나누고, 누구하고나 잡담을 나누고, 웃고, 춤추고, 농담을 하고, 휘스트를 치고, 무엇이든 다 하고, 어디에나 있었다. 이토록 잡다한 활동에 더하여, 그 자그마한 박사는 그 무엇보다 중요한 일을 하나 더 보태고 있었으니—그는 자그마한 늙은 과부 하나에게 더없이 끊임없고 헌신적인 정성을 쏟는 데 지칠 줄을 몰랐던 것이다. 그 과부의 값진 옷차림과 넘쳐 나는 장신구는 그녀가 빠듯한 수입에 더없이 탐나는 보탬이 될 사람임을 말해 주고 있었다.\n\n터프먼 씨와 그의 동료의 눈은 한동안 그 박사와 과부에게 못 박혀 있었는데, 그때 낯선 사내가 침묵을 깼다.\n\n\"돈이 많군—늙은 아가씨라—거드름 피우는 박사라—나쁘지 않은 생각이지—재미있겠어.\" 그의 입에서 흘러나온 알아들을 만한 문장이 이것이었다. 터프먼 씨가 캐묻듯 그의 얼굴을 들여다보았다.\n\n\"내가 저 과부와 춤을 추겠소.\" 낯선 사내가 말했다.\n\n\"저 여자가 누구요?\" 터프먼 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"모르오—생전 처음 보는 여자요—저 박사 놈을 따돌리고—자, 갑니다.\" 그러더니 낯선 사내는 곧장 방을 가로질러 가, 벽난로 선반에 기대선 채, 그 자그마한 늙은 부인의 통통한 얼굴을 공손하고도 우수에 잠긴 흠모의 태도로 바라보기 시작했다. 터프먼 씨는 말없이 놀라며 지켜보았다. 낯선 사내는 빠르게 일을 진척시켰다. 자그마한 박사가 다른 부인과 춤을 추는 사이, 과부가 부채를 떨어뜨렸고, 낯선 사내가 그것을 주워 건넸다—미소 한 번—절 한 번—답례의 인사 한 번—몇 마디 대화. 낯선 사내가 대담하게 무도회 진행자에게로 걸어가, 그를 데리고 돌아왔다. 짤막한 소개의 무언극이 있은 뒤, 낯선 사내와 버저 부인이 카드리유 대형 속에 자리를 잡았다.\n\n이 일사천리의 진행에 터프먼 씨가 느낀 놀라움도 컸지만, 박사의 경악은 그보다 이루 헤아릴 수 없이 더 컸다. 낯선 사내는 젊었고, 과부는 우쭐해 있었다. 박사의 정성은 과부에게 안중에도 없었고, 박사의 분노는 그 태연한 연적에게 아무 효과도 없이 사그라졌다. 슬래머 박사는 망연자실했다. 자기, 곧 제97연대의 슬래머 박사가, 여태껏 아무도 본 적이 없고 지금조차 아무도 모르는 사내에게, 한순간에 꺼져 버리다니! 슬래머 박사가—제97연대의 슬래머 박사가 퇴짜를 맞다니! 있을 수 없는 일이었다! 그럴 리가 없었다! 그런데 사실이었다. 저기 두 사람이 있지 않은가. 뭐라고! 자기 친구를 소개하다니! 제 눈을 믿을 수 있단 말인가! 그는 다시 보았고, 제 눈의 정확함을 인정해야 하는 고통스러운 처지에 놓였다. 버저 부인이 트레이시 터프먼 씨와 춤을 추고 있었으니, 그 사실에는 오해의 여지가 없었다. 과부가 바로 그의 눈앞에서 평소답지 않은 기운으로 온몸을 이리저리 들썩이고 있었고, 트레이시 터프먼 씨가 더없이 진지한 표정을 한 얼굴로 (적잖은 사람들이 그러하듯이) 마치 카드리유가 웃어넘길 거리가 아니라 흔들림 없는 결의로 맞서야 할 가혹한 시련이라도 되는 듯이 춤을 추며 깡충깡충 뛰어다니고 있었다.\n\n박사는 이 모든 것을, 그리고 뒤이어 벌어진 온갖 니거스 술 건네기며, 잔 챙기기며, 비스킷 가져다 바치기며, 시시덕거림을, 말없이 참을성 있게 견뎌 냈다. 그러나 낯선 사내가 버저 부인을 마차까지 바래다주려고 사라진 지 몇 초 뒤, 그는 여태껏 병 속에 가둬 두었던 분노가 한 톨도 빠짐없이 부글부글 끓어올라, 격정의 진땀이 되어 얼굴 구석구석에서 배어 나오는 채로, 쏜살같이 방을 빠져나갔다.\n* 니거스: 포도주에 더운물, 설탕, 향료를 탄 음료.\n\n낯선 사내가 돌아오고 있었고, 터프먼 씨가 그 곁에 있었다. 그는 낮은 목소리로 무어라 말하며 웃었다. 자그마한 박사는 그의 목숨에 굶주려 있었다. 그는 의기양양했다. 그는 승리한 것이었다.\n\n\"이보시오!\" 박사가 무시무시한 목소리로 말하며 명함 한 장을 꺼내 들고, 복도 한쪽 구석으로 물러서며 말했다, \"내 이름은 슬래머요, 슬래머 박사요, 손님—제97연대요—채텀 병영이오—내 명함이오, 손님, 내 명함.\" 그는 더 보태고 싶었으나, 분노가 그의 목을 틀어막았다.\n\n\"아!\" 낯선 사내가 태연히 대답했다, \"슬래머라—고맙소—정중한 호의로구려—지금은 아프지 않소, 슬래머—하지만 아프게 되면—댁을 부르리다.\"\n\n\"댁은—댁은 발뺌하는 자요, 손님.\" 격분한 박사가 헐떡이며 말했다, \"비겁자에—겁쟁이에—거짓말쟁이에—그—그—무엇으로도 댁의 명함을 내놓게 할 수 없단 말이오, 손님!\"\n\n\"오! 알겠소.\" 낯선 사내가 반쯤 혼잣말로 말했다, \"여기 니거스가 너무 독하군—인심 좋은 주인장이지—참 어리석은 일이오—참으로—레모네이드가 훨씬 낫지—더운 방에—나이 지긋한 양반들이라—아침이면 그 대가를 치르지—가혹해—가혹하구려.\" 그러고는 한두 걸음 옮겨 갔다.\n\n\"댁은 이 집에 묵고 있소, 손님.\" 분개한 자그마한 사내가 말했다. \"지금 댁은 취해 있소, 손님. 아침에 내 소식을 듣게 될 거요, 손님. 댁을 기어이 찾아내고 말겠소, 손님. 기어이 찾아내고 말겠소.\"\n\n\"댁이 날 집에서 찾아내느니 차라리 알아채는 편이 낫겠구려.\" 꿈쩍도 않는 낯선 사내가 대답했다.\n\n* 'find me out'에는 '찾아내다'와 '정체를 간파하다'라는 두 뜻이 있어, 낯선 사내가 말장난으로 받아친 것이다.\n슬래머 박사는 분개하여 모자를 탁 내리쳐 머리에 눌러쓰며 말할 수 없는 흉포함을 드러냈다. 그리고 낯선 사내와 터프먼 씨는, 아무것도 모르는 윈클에게 빌린 깃털을 도로 돌려주려고, 후자의 침실로 올라갔다.\n\n그 신사는 곤히 잠들어 있었다. 옷은 곧 제자리에 돌려놓았다. 낯선 사내는 더없이 익살스러웠고, 트레이시 터프먼 씨는 포도주와 니거스, 불빛, 그리고 숙녀들에 완전히 얼이 빠져, 이 모든 일을 더없이 절묘한 농담쯤으로 여겼다. 그의 새 친구가 떠났다. 그리고 트레이시 터프먼 씨는, 본래 제 머리를 넣게 되어 있는 잠자리 모자의 구멍을 찾는 데 약간의 어려움을 겪은 끝에, 마침내 그것을 쓰려고 안간힘을 쓰다가 촛대를 넘어뜨린 다음, 갖가지 복잡한 곡예를 거쳐 가까스로 침대로 기어들어, 곧 잠 속으로 가라앉았다.\n\n이튿날 아침 일곱 시를 알리는 종소리가 채 멎기도 전에, 픽윅 씨의 포괄적인 정신은 침실 문을 요란하게 두드리는 소리에, 잠이 빠뜨려 놓았던 무의식 상태에서 깨어났다.\n\n\"누구요?\" 픽윅 씨가 침대에서 벌떡 일어나며 말했다.\n\n\"구두닦이입니다, 손님.\"\n\n\"무슨 일이오?\"\n\n\"황송하지만, 손님, 일행 중에 어느 신사분이 'P. C.'라는 금박 단추가 달린 밝은 푸른색 정장 외투를 입으시는지 말씀해 주시겠습니까?\"\n\n'손질하라고 내놓았는데,' 픽윅 씨는 생각했다, '저 사람이 그게 누구 것인지 잊었나 보군.'\n\n\"윈클 씨요.\" 그가 큰 소리로 외쳤다, \"오른편으로 두 칸 건너 다음 방이오.\"\n\n\"고맙습니다, 손님.\" 구두닦이가 말하고는 가 버렸다.\n\n\"무슨 일이야?\" 문을 요란하게 두드리는 소리가 망각의 잠에서 그를 깨우자, 터프먼 씨가 외쳤다.\n\n\"윈클 씨와 말씀 좀 나눌 수 있겠습니까, 손님?\" 구두닦이가 밖에서 대답했다.\n\n\"윈클—윈클!\" 터프먼 씨가 안쪽 방을 향해 외쳤다.\n\n\"이봐요!\" 이불 속에서 가냘픈 목소리가 대답했다.\n\n\"누가 찾아—문 앞에 누가 왔어.\" 그러고는 이만큼 분명히 말하느라 안간힘을 쓴 트레이시 터프먼 씨는 돌아누워 다시 곤히 잠들어 버렸다.\n\n\"찾는다고!\" 윈클 씨가 황급히 침대에서 뛰어나와 옷가지 몇 개를 걸치며 말했다. \"찾는다고! 도시에서 이렇게 멀리 떨어진 데서—대체 누가 날 찾는단 말인가?\"\n\n\"커피실에 어떤 신사분이 계십니다, 손님.\" 윈클 씨가 문을 열고 마주서자 구두닦이가 대답했다. \"그 신사분 말씀이, 잠시도 붙들지 않겠지만 거절은 절대 받아들일 수 없다십니다.\"\n\n\"참 이상하군!\" 윈클 씨가 말했다. \"곧 내려가겠소.\"\n\n그는 여행용 숄과 실내복을 황급히 두르고 아래층으로 내려갔다. 노파 한 사람과 종업원 두 사람이 커피실을 치우고 있었고, 평상복 차림의 장교 하나가 창밖을 내다보고 있었다. 그는 윈클 씨가 들어서자 돌아서서 뻣뻣하게 고개를 까딱했다. 그러고는 시중드는 이들에게 물러가라 이르고 문을 아주 조심스레 닫은 뒤 말했다, \"윈클 씨가 맞으십니까?\"\n\n\"제 이름이 윈클입니다, 손님.\"\n\n\"제가 오늘 아침 제 친구인 제97연대의 슬래머 박사를 대신하여 이곳에 찾아왔다고 말씀드려도, 손님께서는 놀라지 않으시겠지요.\"\n\n\"슬래머 박사라고요!\" 윈클 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"슬래머 박사입니다. 그가 제게 청하기를, 어젯밤 손님의 행동은 어떤 신사도 견딜 수 없는, 그리고\" (그가 덧붙였다) \"어떤 신사도 다른 신사에게 결코 하지 않을 종류의 것이었다는 그의 견해를 전해 달라고 하더군요.\"\n\n윈클 씨의 놀라움은 너무도 진실하고 너무도 분명하여 슬래머 박사의 친구의 눈을 피할 수가 없었다. 그래서 그는 말을 이었다—\n\n\"제 친구 슬래머 박사가 덧붙여 달라 청하기를, 그는 손님께서 어젯밤 한동안 취해 계셨고, 어쩌면 자신이 저지른 모욕의 정도를 의식하지 못하셨으리라고 굳게 믿는다고 합니다. 만일 이것을 손님의 행동에 대한 변명으로 내세우신다면, 제가 불러 드리는 대로 손님께서 손수 쓰신 서면 사과를 받아들이는 데 동의하겠노라고 그가 제게 말해 달라 하더군요.\"\n\n\"서면 사과라고요!\" 윈클 씨가 더없이 강한 경악의 어조로 되뇌었다.\n\n\"물론 그 대안이 무엇인지는 아시겠지요.\" 방문객이 태연하게 대답했다.\n\n\"이 전갈을 제 이름을 지목하여 받으셨소?\" 이 기이한 대화에 머리가 절망적으로 뒤죽박죽이 된 윈클 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"저는 그 자리에 직접 있지 않았습니다.\" 방문객이 대답했다. \"그리고 손님께서 슬래머 박사에게 명함을 내놓기를 한사코 거절하신 까닭에, 저는 그 신사로부터 아주 보기 드문 외투를 입은 사람을 식별해 달라는 부탁을 받았습니다—흉상과 'P. C.'라는 글자가 박힌 금박 단추가 달린, 밝은 푸른색 정장 외투를 말입니다.\"\n\n윈클 씨는 자신의 옷차림이 이토록 세세히 묘사되는 것을 듣고는 정말로 놀라 비틀거렸다. 슬래머 박사의 친구가 말을 이었다—\n\"방금 카운터에서 알아본 바로, 문제의 그 외투 주인이 어제 오후 세 신사와 함께 이곳에 도착했음을 확신하게 되었습니다. 저는 즉시 일행의 우두머리로 보인다는 신사에게 사람을 올려보냈고, 그 신사는 곧바로 저를 손님께로 안내해 주었습니다.\"\n\n만일 로체스터 성의 본루가 별안간 그 토대에서 걸어 나와 커피실 창문 맞은편에 떡 버티고 섰다 한들, 윈클 씨의 놀라움은 그가 이 말을 들으며 느낀 깊디깊은 경악에 비하면 아무것도 아니었을 것이다. 그의 첫 느낌은 자신의 외투를 도둑맞았다는 것이었다. \"잠시만 기다려 주시겠소?\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"물론이지요.\" 달갑잖은 방문객이 대답했다.\n\n윈클 씨는 황급히 위층으로 달려가, 떨리는 손으로 가방을 열었다. 외투는 늘 두던 자리에 있었으나, 가까이 들여다보니 지난밤 입었던 명백한 흔적을 드러내고 있었다. \"그렇구나.\" 윈클 씨가 외투를 손에서 떨어뜨리며 말했다. \"내가 만찬 뒤에 술을 너무 많이 마셔서, 거리를 쏘다니다 그 뒤에 시가를 피운 기억이 아주 어렴풋이 나는군. 사실, 나는 잔뜩 취해 있었어—외투를 바꿔 입은 게 틀림없고—어딘가에 가서—누군가를 모욕한 거야—틀림없어. 그리고 이 전갈이 그 끔찍한 결과인 거지.\" 이렇게 말하며 윈클 씨는, 호전적인 슬래머 박사의 결투 신청을 받아들이고 뒤따를지 모를 최악의 결과를 감수하리라는 음울하고 무시무시한 결심을 안고, 커피실 쪽으로 발길을 되돌렸다.\n\n윈클 씨가 이 결심에 이르게 된 데에는 여러 가지 고려가 작용했는데, 그 첫째는 클럽에서의 그의 명성이었다. 그는 공격이든 방어든 무해한 것이든, 온갖 오락과 기예에 관한 한 늘 높은 권위자로 추앙받아 온 터였다. 그러니 만일 시험대에 오르는 바로 이 첫 기회에, 그것도 지도자가 지켜보는 가운데 그가 시련 앞에서 움츠러든다면, 그의 이름과 지위는 영영 잃고 마는 것이었다. 게다가 그는, 이런 일에 어두운 사람들이 흔히 추측하는 말, 곧 입회인들 사이의 묵계로 권총에 실탄을 재는 일은 좀처럼 없다는 말을 자주 들은 것을 떠올렸다. 더욱이 그는, 만일 스노드그래스 씨에게 입회인이 되어 달라 청하면서 그 위험을 생생한 말로 그려 보인다면, 그 신사가 어쩌면 그 소식을 픽윅 씨에게 알릴 것이고, 픽윅 씨는 분명 지체 없이 그것을 지방 당국에 전하여, 자기 추종자가 살해되거나 불구가 되는 것을 막아 줄지도 모른다고 생각했다. 이런 것들이 그가 커피실로 돌아와 박사의 결투 신청을 받아들이겠다는 뜻을 내비쳤을 때 품고 있던 생각이었다.\n\n\"만날 시간과 장소를 정할 입회인을 제게 소개해 주시겠소?\" 장교가 말했다.\n\n\"전혀 그럴 필요 없소.\" 윈클 씨가 대답했다. \"그것들을 제게 일러 주시면, 입회인의 참석은 제가 나중에 마련하겠소.\"\n\n\"그럼—오늘 저녁 해 질 녘으로 할까요?\" 장교가 무심한 어조로 물었다.\n\n\"좋소.\" 윈클 씨가 속으로는 아주 나쁘다고 여기면서 대답했다.\n\n\"피트 요새는 아시오?\"\n\n\"예, 어제 봤소.\"\n\n\"수고스럽겠지만, 참호와 맞닿은 들판으로 들어서서, 요새의 한 모퉁이에 이르거든 왼편 오솔길을 잡아, 저를 보실 때까지 곧장 나아오시면, 제가 앞장서서 방해받을 염려 없이 일을 치를 수 있는 외진 곳으로 안내하겠습니다.\"\n\n'방해받을 염려라!' 윈클 씨는 생각했다.\n\n\"더 정할 것은 없는 듯하군요.\" 장교가 말했다.\n\n\"더 생각나는 것은 없소.\" 윈클 씨가 대답했다. \"그럼, 안녕히.\"\n\n\"안녕히 가시오.\" 그러고는 장교가 경쾌한 곡조를 휘파람으로 불며 성큼성큼 걸어갔다.\n\n그날 아침 식사는 무겁게 흘러갔다. 터프먼 씨는 지난밤의 평소답지 않은 방탕 끝에 일어날 형편이 못 되었고, 스노드그래스 씨는 시적인 의기소침에 시달리는 듯했으며, 픽윅 씨조차 평소답지 않게 침묵과 소다수에 애착을 보였다. 윈클 씨는 열심히 기회를 엿보았는데, 그것은 오래 기다릴 것도 없었다. 스노드그래스 씨가 성을 구경하러 가자고 제안했고, 일행 가운데 걸을 마음이 있는 사람은 윈클 씨뿐이었으므로, 둘은 함께 나갔다.\n\n\"스노드그래스.\" 큰길을 벗어나자 윈클 씨가 말했다. \"스노드그래스, 이보게, 자네의 입이 무거우리라 믿어도 되겠나?\" 이렇게 말하면서, 그는 더없이 간절하고 진심으로 그러지 못하기를 바랐다.\n\n\"믿어도 되네.\" 스노드그래스 씨가 대답했다. \"내 맹세를 들어 보게—\"\n\n\"아니, 아니.\" 윈클이, 제 동료가 무심코 발설하지 않겠노라 스스로 다짐하지나 않을까 겁이 나서 가로막았다. \"맹세는 말게, 맹세는 말아—그럴 필요 전혀 없네.\"\n\n스노드그래스 씨는 위와 같이 호소하면서 시정에 겨워 구름을 향해 치켜들었던 손을 내리고, 경청하는 자세를 취했다.\n\n\"내게 자네 도움이 필요하네, 이보게, 명예에 관한 일에서.\" 윈클 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"도와주고말고.\" 스노드그래스 씨가 친구의 손을 꽉 잡으며 대답했다.\n\n\"어느 박사와의 일이야—제97연대의 슬래머 박사 말일세.\" 윈클 씨가 일을 되도록 엄숙하게 보이려 애쓰며 말했다. \"한 장교와의 일이고, 또 다른 장교가 그의 입회인이지. 오늘 저녁 해 질 녘에, 피트 요새 너머 외딴 들판에서 말이야.\"\n\n\"내 자네와 함께하겠네.\" 스노드그래스 씨가 말했다.\n\n그는 놀라기는 했으나 결코 겁먹지 않았다. 이런 경우에 당사자가 아닌 쪽은 어찌 그리 태연할 수 있는지 참으로 놀라운 노릇이다. 윈클 씨는 이것을 깜박했던 것이다. 그는 제 친구의 심정을 제 심정에 빗대어 헤아렸던 것이다.\n\n\"그 결과가 끔찍할지도 모르네.\" 윈클 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇지 않기를 바라네.\" 스노드그래스 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그 박사는 명사수라던데.\" 윈클 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그런 군인들이야 대개 그렇지.\" 스노드그래스 씨가 차분히 말했다. \"하지만 자네도 그렇지 않은가?\"\n\n윈클 씨가 그렇다고 대답했다. 그리고 제 동료를 충분히 겁먹게 하지 못했음을 알아채고는, 방향을 바꾸었다.\n\n\"스노드그래스.\" 그가 감정에 떨리는 목소리로 말했다. \"만일 내가 쓰러지거든, 내가 자네 손에 맡길 꾸러미 속에서, 내—내 아버지께 드리는 편지를 발견하게 될 걸세.\"\n\n이 공략 또한 실패였다. 스노드그래스 씨는 마음이 움직이기는 했으나, 마치 2펜스짜리 우편배달부라도 되는 양 선뜻 그 편지를 전해 주겠노라 맡고 나섰다.\n\n\"만일 내가 쓰러지거나,\" 윈클 씨가 말했다, \"혹은 박사가 쓰러지면, 이보게 친구, 자네는 사전 종범으로 재판을 받게 될 걸세. 내가 친구를 유형에—어쩌면 종신 유형에 끌어들여야 한단 말인가!\"\n\n스노드그래스 씨가 이 말에 조금 움찔했으나, 그의 영웅심은 천하무적이었다. \"우정을 위해서라면,\" 그가 열렬히 외쳤다, \"내 어떤 위험도 무릅쓰겠네.\"\n\n그들이 한동안 나란히 말없이 걸으며 저마다 제 사색에 잠겨 있는 동안, 윈클 씨는 제 동료의 헌신적인 우정을 속으로 얼마나 저주했던가! 아침은 자꾸 지나가고 있었다. 그는 절박해졌다.\n\n\"스노드그래스.\" 그가 우뚝 멈춰 서며 말했다. \"이 일에서 내가 좌절되게 하지 말아 주게—지방 당국에 알리지 말고—여러 치안관의 도움을 얻어, 나든 현재 채텀 병영에 주둔 중인 제97연대의 슬래머 박사든 붙잡아 가두게 하여, 이 결투를 막는 짓일랑 하지 말아 주게!—제발, 그러지 말게.\"\n\n스노드그래스 씨는 친구의 손을 따뜻하게 움켜쥐고는 열정적으로 대답했다, \"천하를 준대도 그러지 않겠네!\"\n\n제 친구의 두려움에서 바랄 것이 아무것도 없으며, 자신은 살아 있는 과녁이 될 운명임을 깨닫는 확신이 거세게 밀려들자, 윈클 씨의 온몸에 전율이 스쳐 지나갔다.\n\n스노드그래스 씨에게 사정이 정식으로 설명되고, 화약과 탄알과 뇌관이라는 흡족한 부속품을 갖춘 흡족한 권총 한 벌이 로체스터의 어느 제조업자에게서 빌려진 뒤, 두 친구는 여관으로 돌아왔다. 윈클 씨는 다가올 싸움을 곰곰 생각하기 위해, 스노드그래스 씨는 전쟁 무기를 손질하여 당장이라도 쓸 수 있도록 제대로 갖추어 두기 위해서였다.\n\n그들이 다시 그 거북한 임무를 띠고 나선 것은 흐리고 무거운 저녁나절이었다. 윈클 씨는 남의 눈을 피하려 커다란 망토로 몸을 칭칭 감쌌고, 스노드그래스 씨는 제 망토 밑에 파멸의 도구를 품고 있었다.\n\n\"다 챙겼나?\" 윈클 씨가 동요한 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"다 챙겼네.\" 스노드그래스 씨가 대답했다. \"탄약도 넉넉히 챙겼지, 사격이 빗나갈 경우를 대비해서 말이야. 통에 화약이 4분의 1파운드 들어 있고, 장전할 때 쓰려고 신문지도 두 장 주머니에 넣어 왔네.\"\n\n이것들은 어느 누구라도 마땅히 더없이 고마워할 만한 우정의 사례였다. 윈클 씨의 감사가 너무도 벅차 차마 말로 표현할 수 없었으리라 짐작되는바, 그는 아무 말도 없이 그저 걸음을 계속할 뿐이었다—그것도 다소 느릿느릿.\n\n\"우리 시간이 아주 넉넉하군.\" 첫 번째 들판의 울타리를 넘으며 스노드그래스 씨가 말했다. \"해가 막 지려는 참이야.\" 윈클 씨가 기울어 가는 해를 올려다보며, 머지않아 자신도 '기울어' 버릴지 모른다는 가능성을 고통스레 떠올렸다.\n\n\"저기 장교가 있군.\" 몇 분쯤 걷고 나서 윈클 씨가 외쳤다.\n\n\"어디?\" 스노드그래스 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"저기—저 푸른 망토를 두른 신사 말이야.\" 스노드그래스 씨가 친구의 집게손가락이 가리키는 쪽을 바라보니, 그가 묘사한 대로 몸을 칭칭 감싼 사람 하나가 눈에 들어왔다. 장교는 손짓을 살짝 함으로써 그들의 존재를 알아챘음을 내비쳤고, 두 친구는 그가 걸어가자 조금 떨어져 그를 뒤따랐다.\n\n저녁은 시시각각 더욱 흐려졌고, 인적 없는 들판으로 구슬픈 바람이 윙윙 울려 퍼졌으니, 마치 멀리 있는 거인이 제 집 지키는 개를 부르려고 휘파람을 부는 듯했다. 그 풍경의 서글픔이 윈클 씨의 심정에 침울한 빛깔을 드리웠다. 그는 참호의 모퉁이를 지날 때 흠칫했으니—그것이 거대한 무덤처럼 보였던 것이다.\n\n장교가 오솔길에서 별안간 벗어나, 말뚝 울타리를 넘고 산울타리를 기어 넘은 끝에, 외진 들판으로 들어섰다. 그곳에는 두 신사가 기다리고 있었으니, 하나는 검은 머리의 자그마한 뚱뚱보였고, 다른 하나는—장식 끈을 두른 프록코트를 입은 풍채 좋은 인물로—야영용 걸상에 더없이 평온하게 앉아 있었다.\n\n\"상대편이로군, 그리고 저쪽은 외과의인가 보네.\" 스노드그래스 씨가 말했다. \"브랜디 한 모금 들게나.\" 윈클 씨가 친구가 내민 고리버들 술병을 움켜쥐고, 그 기운을 돋우는 액체를 길게 한 모금 들이켰다.\n\n\"제 친구입니다, 손님, 스노드그래스 씨예요.\" 장교가 다가오자 윈클 씨가 말했다. 슬래머 박사의 친구가 절하고는, 스노드그래스 씨가 들고 있는 것과 비슷한 상자를 꺼내 보였다.\n\n\"더 드릴 말씀은 없는 것 같군요, 손님.\" 그가 상자를 열며 차갑게 말했다. \"사과는 단호히 거절되었으니까요.\"\n\n\"없습니다, 손님.\" 슬슬 거북해지기 시작한 스노드그래스 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"앞으로 나와 주시겠습니까?\" 장교가 말했다.\n\n\"물론이지요.\" 스노드그래스 씨가 대답했다. 거리를 재고, 사전 절차가 준비되었다.\n\n\"이것이 그쪽 것보다 나을 겁니다.\" 상대편 입회인이 제 권총을 꺼내 보이며 말했다. \"제가 장전하는 걸 보셨지요. 이것을 쓰는 데 이의 없으십니까?\"\n\n\"전혀 없습니다.\" 스노드그래스 씨가 대답했다. 이 제안 덕분에 그는 적잖은 곤혹에서 벗어났으니, 권총을 장전하는 일에 대한 그의 이전 지식이란 자못 흐릿하고 막연했기 때문이다.\n\n\"그럼 우리 두 사람을 세워도 될 듯하군요.\" 장교가, 마치 당사자들은 장기 말이고 입회인들은 두는 사람이기라도 한 듯 무심한 태도로 말했다.\n\n\"그래도 될 것 같습니다.\" 스노드그래스 씨가 대답했다. 그는 이 일에 관해 아무것도 몰랐으므로 어떤 제안에든 동의했을 것이다. 장교는 슬래머 박사에게로 건너갔고, 스노드그래스 씨는 윈클 씨에게로 다가갔다.\n\n\"다 준비됐네.\" 그가 권총을 내밀며 말했다. \"망토를 이리 주게.\"\n\n\"꾸러미는 자네가 가졌지, 이보게.\" 가엾은 윈클이 말했다.\n\n\"걱정 말게.\" 스노드그래스 씨가 말했다. \"침착하게, 한 방 먹여 주게.\"\n\n윈클 씨는 이 조언이, 길거리 싸움에서 구경꾼들이 가장 작은 소년에게 한결같이 던지는 말, 곧 '덤벼서 이겨라' 하는 말과 꼭 닮았다는 생각이 들었다—할 줄만 안다면야 권할 만한 훌륭한 말이렷다. 그러나 그는 말없이 망토를 벗었으니—그 망토를 벗는 데는 늘 한참이 걸렸다—그러고는 권총을 받아 들었다. 입회인들이 물러갔고, 야영용 걸상에 앉아 있던 신사도 똑같이 물러갔으며, 두 교전자가 서로에게 다가섰다.\n\n윈클 씨는 언제나 지극한 인정으로 유명한 사람이었다. 그가 동족을 고의로 다치게 하기를 꺼린 까닭에 운명의 자리에 다다랐을 때 두 눈을 감아 버렸으리라는 것, 그리고 그렇게 눈을 감고 있던 사정 때문에 슬래머 박사의 매우 기이하고 영문 모를 거동을 보지 못했으리라는 것이 추측된다. 그 신사는 흠칫 놀라 빤히 바라보더니 뒷걸음질을 치고 눈을 비비고는 다시 빤히 바라보다가, 마침내 이렇게 외쳤다. \"멈춰요, 멈춰!\"\n\n\"이게 다 무슨 일이오?\" 슬래머 박사가 자기 친구와 스노드그래스 씨가 달려오자 말했다. \"저 사람이 아니오.\"\n\n\"저 사람이 아니라니!\" 슬래머 박사의 입회인이 말했다.\n\n\"저 사람이 아니라니!\" 스노드그래스 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"저 사람이 아니라니!\" 손에 야영 의자를 든 신사가 말했다.\n\n\"분명히 아니오.\" 작은 박사가 대답했다. \"저 사람은 어젯밤 나를 모욕한 사람이 아니오.\"\n\n\"참으로 기이하군!\" 장교가 소리쳤다.\n\n\"참으로.\" 야영 의자를 든 신사가 말했다. \"다만 문제는, 저 신사가 결투장에 나와 있는 이상, 그가 실제로 그 사람이든 아니든, 형식상으로 어제저녁 우리 친구 슬래머 박사를 모욕한 당사자로 간주되어야 하지 않겠는가 하는 것이오.\" 그리고 이 제안을 매우 현명하고 그윽한 태도로 내놓은 뒤, 야영 의자를 든 사내는 코담배를 한 줌 크게 집어 들이켜고는, 이런 문제에 정통한 권위자다운 태도로 주위를 깊이 둘러보았다.\n\n이제 윈클 씨는 상대편이 적대 행위를 중지하라고 외치는 소리를 들었을 때 두 눈을, 그리고 두 귀까지도 활짝 열었다. 그리고 그 뒤에 오간 말로 미루어 이 일에 무언가 착오가 있다는 것을 의심할 여지 없이 알아차리자, 그는 자기가 나온 진짜 동기를 숨김으로써 반드시 얻게 될 명성의 증대를 즉시 내다보았다. 그리하여 그는 당당히 앞으로 나서서 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"제가 그 사람이 아닙니다. 저도 압니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면, 그것은,\" 야영 의자를 든 사내가 말했다. \"슬래머 박사에 대한 모욕이며, 즉각 진행할 충분한 이유가 되오.\"\n\n\"제발 조용히 하시오, 페인.\" 박사의 입회인이 말했다. \"선생, 어째서 오늘 아침에 이 사실을 나에게 알리지 않으셨소?\"\n\n\"옳거니, 옳거니.\" 야영 의자를 든 사내가 분개하여 말했다.\n\n\"제발 좀 조용히 해 주시오, 페인.\" 다른 이가 말했다. \"선생, 내 질문을 다시 해도 되겠소?\"\n\n\"그것은, 선생,\" 그동안 자기 대답을 곰곰이 헤아릴 시간이 있었던 윈클 씨가 대답했다. \"그것은, 선생, 선생께서 술 취하고 신사답지 못한 사람이 어떤 외투를 입고 있더라고 묘사하셨는데, 그 외투는 제가 입는 영광을 가졌을 뿐 아니라 고안해 낸 영광까지 가진 것이기 때문입니다. 곧 런던 픽윅 클럽의 제정 예정 제복이지요, 선생. 저는 그 제복의 명예를 지켜야 한다고 느꼈고, 그래서 더 묻지도 않고 선생께서 내미신 결투를 받아들인 것입니다.\"\n\n\"이보시오, 선생,\" 사람 좋은 작은 박사가 손을 내밀며 다가와 말했다. \"나는 선생의 의기를 존경하오. 한 말씀 드려도 된다면, 선생, 나는 선생의 처신을 크게 흠모하며, 아무 보람도 없이 이 만남의 불편을 끼쳐 드린 것을 몹시 유감스럽게 여기오.\"\n\n\"부디 그런 말씀 마십시오, 선생.\" 윈클 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"선생과 알게 된 것을 자랑스럽게 여기겠소, 선생.\" 작은 박사가 말했다.\n\n\"선생을 알게 되어 더없는 기쁨이옵니다, 선생.\" 윈클 씨가 대답했다. 그리하여 박사와 윈클 씨가 악수를 나누고, 그다음에는 윈클 씨와 태플턴 중위(박사의 입회인)가, 그다음에는 윈클 씨와 야영 의자를 든 사내가, 마지막으로 윈클 씨와 스노드그래스 씨가 악수를 나누었으니, 마지막에 든 그 신사는 영웅적인 친구의 고귀한 처신에 감탄해 마지않는 모습이었다.\n\n\"이제 그만 자리를 파해도 되겠소이다.\" 태플턴 중위가 말했다.\n\n\"물론이오.\" 박사가 덧붙였다.\n\n\"다만,\" 야영 의자를 든 사내가 끼어들었다. \"다만 윈클 씨가 이 결투 신청으로 모욕을 당했다고 느끼지 않는다면 말이오. 그렇게 느낀다면, 내 생각에 그분은 만족을 요구할 권리가 있소.\"\n\n윈클 씨는 대단한 극기심을 발휘하여 이미 충분히 만족하고 있노라고 밝혔다.\n\n\"혹은 어쩌면,\" 야영 의자를 든 사내가 말했다. \"이 만남의 초입에서 내 입에서 나온 어떤 말 때문에 저 신사의 입회인이 모욕을 느꼈을지도 모르오. 만일 그렇다면, 나는 기꺼이 그분에게 즉시 만족을 드리리다.\"\n\n스노드그래스 씨는 방금 말한 신사의 너그러운 제안에 매우 감사한다고 황급히 밝히면서, 다만 모든 진행에 전적으로 만족하기에 사양할 수밖에 없노라고 했다. 두 입회인은 권총집을 챙겼고, 일행은 올 때보다 훨씬 활기찬 모습으로 결투장을 떠났다.\n\n\"여기 오래 머무르시오?\" 슬래머 박사가 윈클 씨와 더없이 다정하게 나란히 걸으며 물었다.\n\n\"모레쯤 이곳을 떠날 듯합니다.\" 하는 대답이었다.\n\n\"이 어색한 착오를 겪은 마당이니, 선생과 선생의 친구분을 내 거처에서 뵙고 즐거운 저녁을 함께 보내는 기쁨을 누렸으면 하오.\" 작은 박사가 말했다. \"오늘 저녁은 한가하시오?\"\n\n\"여기 친구들이 몇 있어서,\" 윈클 씨가 대답했다. \"오늘 밤은 그들을 두고 자리를 비우고 싶지 않습니다. 차라리 선생과 선생의 친구분이 불 여관으로 와서 저희와 함께하시지요.\"\n\n\"기꺼이 그러리다.\" 작은 박사가 말했다. \"열 시에 들러 반 시간쯤 머무는 것이 너무 늦겠소?\"\n\n\"아, 천만에요.\" 윈클 씨가 말했다. \"제 친구들인 픽윅 씨와 텁먼 씨에게 선생을 소개하게 되어 더없이 기쁘겠습니다.\"\n\n\"틀림없이 큰 기쁨이 되겠소이다.\" 슬래머 박사가 대답했으니, 텁먼 씨가 누구인지는 조금도 짐작하지 못한 채였다.\n\n\"꼭 오시는 거죠?\" 스노드그래스 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"아, 물론이오.\"\n\n이때쯤 그들은 큰길에 다다랐다. 다정한 작별 인사가 오갔고, 일행은 헤어졌다. 슬래머 박사와 그의 친구들은 병영으로 돌아갔고, 윈클 씨는 스노드그래스 씨와 함께 그들의 여관으로 돌아갔다.\n\n픽윅 씨는 두 친구가 평소와 달리 자리를 비운 탓에 적잖이 불안한 마음을 품고 있었는데, 아침 내내 이어진 그들의 알 수 없는 행동은 그 불안을 누그러뜨리는 데 조금도 도움이 되지 못했다. 그러므로 그들이 다시 들어왔을 때 그가 일어나 맞이한 것은 여느 때보다 더한 기쁨에서였고, 무슨 일이 있어 자기와의 자리를 이토록 늦추었는지 물은 것도 여느 때보다 더한 관심에서였다. 이 점에 관한 그의 질문에 답하여, 스노드그래스 씨는 방금 자세히 이야기된 정황을 역사적으로 설명하려 했으나, 좌중에 터프먼 씨와 전날의 역마차 동행뿐 아니라 그에 못지않게 기이한 풍모를 한 또 다른 낯선 이가 있음을 알아채고는 불현듯 말을 멈추었다. 근심에 찌든 듯한 사내였는데, 누런 얼굴과 깊이 꺼진 두 눈은 곧고 검은 머리카락이 헝클어진 채 얼굴 절반까지 늘어진 탓에 자연이 만들어 놓은 것보다 한층 더 두드러져 보였다. 그의 눈은 거의 부자연스러울 만큼 밝고 날카로웠고, 광대뼈는 높이 솟아 도드라졌으며, 턱은 어찌나 길고 야위었던지, 만일 반쯤 벌어진 입과 미동 없는 표정이 그것이 그의 본래 모습임을 알려 주지 않았더라면, 보는 이는 그가 잠시 근육을 수축시켜 얼굴 살을 안으로 끌어당기고 있는 줄로 여겼을 것이다. 목에는 초록빛 숄을 둘렀는데, 그 큰 양 끝이 가슴께로 어수선하게 흘러내려 낡은 조끼의 해진 단춧구멍 아래로 이따금 모습을 드러냈다. 겉옷은 검은빛의 긴 프록코트였고, 그 아래로는 헐렁한 칙칙한 빛깔의 바지와 빠르게 망가져 가는 큼직한 부츠를 신고 있었다.\n\n윈클 씨의 시선이 머문 것은 바로 이 거칠어 보이는 인물이었고, 픽윅 씨가 손을 내민 것도 바로 그를 향해서였다. 픽윅 씨는 말했다. \"여기 우리 친구의 친구분일세. 오늘 아침에 우리는 이 친구가 이곳 극장과 연이 있다는 걸 알게 되었네. 본인은 널리 알려지길 바라지 않지만 말이야. 그리고 이 신사분도 같은 직종에 몸담은 분일세. 자네가 들어왔을 때 마침 그와 관련된 짤막한 일화를 들려주려던 참이었네.\"\n\n\"일화라면 산더미죠.\" 전날의 초록 코트를 입은 낯선 이가 윈클 씨에게 다가와 나직하고 은밀한 어조로 말했다. \"괴짜라오—비극 같은 무게 있는 역을 맡지요—배우는 못 되고—이상한 사람이오—온갖 비참을 다 겪었지요—우리 순회 패거리에선 '음울한 제미'라고 부른다오.\" 윈클 씨와 스노드그래스 씨는 '음울한 제미'라고 우아하게 지칭된 신사를 정중히 맞이했고, 좌중의 나머지를 본떠 브랜디 워터를 청한 뒤 탁자에 자리를 잡았다.\n\n\"자, 선생.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"들려주시려던 이야기를 마저 해 주시는 호의를 베풀어 주시겠소?\"\n\n음울한 사내는 주머니에서 지저분한 종이 두루마리를 꺼내고는, 마침 수첩을 꺼내 든 스노드그래스 씨를 돌아보며, 그의 겉모습과 완벽히 어울리는 공허한 목소리로 말했다. \"당신이 시인이오?\"\n\n\"저—저는 그 방면에 약간 손을 대지요.\" 스노드그래스 씨가 질문의 느닷없음에 다소 당황하여 대답했다.\n\n\"아! 시가 인생에 해 주는 것은 조명과 음악이 무대에 해 주는 것과 같소—한쪽에서 거짓 장식을 벗기고, 다른 쪽에서 환영을 벗겨 내면, 그 어느 쪽에 살아 있고 마음 쓸 만한 무엇이 남겠소?\"\n\n\"참으로 옳은 말씀이오, 선생.\" 스노드그래스 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"각광 앞에 선다는 건,\" 음울한 사내가 말을 이었다. \"성대한 궁정 행사 자리에 앉아 화려한 무리의 비단옷을 감탄하며 바라보는 것과 같소. 그 뒤에 선다는 건, 아무도 돌보지 않고 알지도 못하는 채로 그 화려함을 만들어 내는 사람들이 되는 것이지요. 운명이 정하는 대로 가라앉든 헤엄치든, 굶든 살든 내버려진 사람들 말이오.\"\n\n\"그렇고말고요.\" 스노드그래스 씨가 말했다. 음울한 사내의 꺼진 눈이 그에게 머물러 있어, 무어라도 말해야 할 것 같았기 때문이다.\n\n\"계속하게, 제미.\" 스페인 여행자가 말했다. \"'검은 눈의 수전'처럼 말이야—다운스 한복판에서—꿍얼대지 말고—툭 터놓고—활기차게.\"\n\n\"시작하기 전에 한 잔 더 만드시겠소, 선생?\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n음울한 사내는 그 말귀를 알아듣고, 브랜디 워터를 한 잔 섞어 천천히 절반쯤 들이켠 뒤, 종이 두루마리를 펼쳐 다음의 사건을 반쯤은 읽고 반쯤은 이야기하는 식으로 들려주었으니, 이는 클럽 회의록에 '떠돌이 배우의 이야기'라는 제목으로 기록되어 있는 그대로다.\n떠돌이 배우의 이야기\n\n# THE STROLLER’S TALE\n\n\"제가 이제 들려드릴 이야기에는 경이로운 구석이라곤 없습니다.\" 음울한 사내가 말했다. \"심지어 별스러운 데도 없지요. 궁핍과 병고는 여러 처지의 삶에서 너무도 흔한 것이어서, 인간 본성의 가장 평범한 부침에 으레 베풀어지는 정도 이상의 주목을 받을 만하지 않습니다. 제가 이 몇 가지 기록을 한데 엮은 까닭은, 그 이야기의 당사자를 여러 해 동안 잘 알고 지냈기 때문입니다. 저는 그가 한 걸음 한 걸음 추락해 가는 과정을 좇았고, 마침내 그는 다시는 헤어나지 못할 극도의 궁핍에 이르고 말았습니다.\n\n\"제가 말하는 사내는 천한 무언극 배우였습니다. 그리고 그 부류의 많은 이들이 그렇듯, 상습적인 술꾼이었지요. 형편이 나았던 시절, 방탕으로 쇠약해지고 병으로 야위기 전에는 제법 두둑한 보수를 받았습니다. 신중하고 분별 있게 굴었더라면 몇 해는—그리 오래는 아니더라도—계속 받았을 것입니다. 이런 사내들은 일찍 죽거나, 아니면 제 몸의 기력을 부자연스럽게 혹사하여 호구지책으로 의지할 유일한 밑천인 체력을 너무 일찍 잃어버리기 때문이지요. 그러나 그의 고질적인 악습이 너무도 빠르게 그를 사로잡은 탓에, 그가 정말로 극장에 쓸모 있던 자리에조차 그를 부리기가 불가능해지고 말았습니다. 선술집은 그가 도무지 뿌리칠 수 없는 매혹을 지니고 있었지요. 같은 길을 고집한다면, 방치된 병과 가망 없는 가난이 죽음 그 자체만큼이나 확실히 그의 몫이 될 터였습니다. 그런데도 그는 끝내 고집했고, 그 결과야 짐작이 가실 겁니다. 그는 아무 일자리도 얻지 못했고, 빵에 굶주렸습니다.\n\n\"극장 사정을 조금이라도 아는 사람이라면, 큰 극장의 무대 언저리에 얼마나 많은 초라하고 가난에 찌든 사내들이 어슬렁거리는지 알 것입니다. 정식으로 고용된 배우가 아니라, 발레 인원이며 행렬 인원이며 곡예꾼 따위 말이지요. 그들은 무언극이나 부활절 연극이 상연되는 동안에만 고용되었다가 곧 해고되고, 이윽고 어떤 대형 볼거리가 제작되어 그들의 손이 새로 필요해질 때까지 기다리는 신세입니다. 그 사내도 이런 생활 방식에 매달릴 수밖에 없었습니다. 밤마다 어느 천한 극장 패거리에서 사회를 보는 일은 그에게 주당 몇 실링을 더 쥐여 주는 동시에, 그가 오랜 버릇을 채울 수 있게 해 주었지요. 이 밑천마저도 곧 그를 저버렸습니다. 그의 무절제가 너무 심해, 그렇게 마련할 수 있었을 그 보잘것없는 푼돈조차 벌 수 없었던 것입니다. 그리하여 그는 실제로 굶주림 직전의 처지로 전락했고, 어쩌다 옛 동료에게서 푼돈을 빌리거나, 가장 천한 군소 극장 가운데 어느 한 곳에 출연 자리를 얻어 가까스로 약간을 손에 넣을 뿐이었습니다. 그리고 무얼 좀 벌더라도, 그것은 늘 하던 식으로 탕진되었지요.\n\n\"이 무렵, 그가 어떻게 일 년 넘게 연명해 왔는지 아무도 모르던 그때에, 저는 강 건너 서리 쪽의 어느 극장에서 짧게 일하게 되었고, 거기서 한동안 소식을 모르던 이 사내를 보았습니다. 저는 지방을 돌며 순회 공연을 다녔고, 그는 런던의 골목과 뒷길에 숨어 지내고 있었던 것이지요. 저는 극장을 나서려고 옷을 갈아입은 차림으로 나가는 길에 무대를 가로지르고 있었는데, 그가 제 어깨를 톡톡 두드렸습니다. 제가 돌아섰을 때 눈에 들어온 그 역겨운 광경을 저는 결코 잊지 못할 것입니다. 그는 광대 의상의 온갖 우스꽝스러움을 다 갖춰 무언극용으로 분장하고 있었지요. '죽음의 춤'에 등장하는 유령 같은 형상들이며, 가장 유능한 화가가 일찍이 화폭에 그려 낸 가장 무시무시한 형체들도, 그 절반만큼도 소름 끼치는 모습을 보여 준 적이 없었습니다. 부어오른 몸뚱이와 쪼그라든 다리—그 기괴한 의상이 그 흉함을 백 배는 부풀려 놓았지요—얼굴에 두껍게 처바른 흰 분과 무섭게 대조를 이루는 흐리멍덩한 두 눈, 마비로 떨리는 우스꽝스럽게 치장된 머리, 그리고 흰 분필을 문질러 댄 길고 앙상한 두 손—이 모든 것이 그에게 끔찍하고 부자연스러운 모습을 부여했으니, 어떤 묘사로도 그 적확한 인상을 전할 수 없을 것이며, 지금까지도 떠올리면 몸서리가 쳐집니다. 그가 저를 한쪽으로 데려가며 띄엄띄엄한 말로 병고와 궁핍의 긴 목록을 늘어놓을 때, 그의 목소리는 공허하고 떨렸습니다. 그러더니 늘 그렇듯 약간의 돈을 빌려 달라는 간절한 청으로 끝맺었지요. 저는 그의 손에 몇 실링을 쥐여 주었고, 돌아서는 순간 그가 무대에서 처음 곤두박질치자 뒤따라 터지는 웃음의 함성을 들었습니다.\n\n\"며칠 밤이 지난 뒤, 한 소년이 제 손에 지저분한 종잇조각을 쥐여 주었는데, 거기엔 연필로 몇 마디가 휘갈겨져 있었습니다. 그 사내가 위독하다는 것, 그리고 공연이 끝나거든 극장에서 그리 멀지 않은 어느 거리—이제 그 이름은 잊었습니다—에 있는 그의 하숙으로 와서 그를 봐 달라고 간청하는 내용이었지요. 저는 빠져나오는 대로 가겠노라 약속했고, 막이 내린 뒤 그 우울한 심부름 길에 나섰습니다.\n\n\"마지막 연극에 출연했던 터라 시각이 늦었고, 게다가 자선 공연의 밤이어서 공연이 평소보다 길게 늘어졌던 것입니다. 어둡고 추운 밤이었으며, 으슬으슬 축축한 바람이 빗줄기를 창문과 집 정면에 사납게 들이쳤지요. 좁고 인적 드문 거리에는 빗물 웅덩이가 고여 있었고, 띄엄띄엄 흩어진 기름 등불도 거센 바람에 많이 꺼져 버려, 그 길은 음산할 뿐 아니라 갈피를 잡기조차 어려웠습니다. 그래도 다행히 저는 옳은 방향을 잡았고, 약간의 고생 끝에 일러 준 집을 찾아내는 데 성공했습니다. 그 집은 석탄 창고였고 그 위로 한 층이 있었는데, 그 위층 뒷방에 제가 찾던 이가 누워 있었지요.\n\n\"그 사내의 아내인, 비참한 몰골의 여인이 계단에서 저를 맞이하며, 그가 방금 일종의 선잠에 빠졌다고 일러 주고는 저를 조용히 안으로 이끌어 침대맡에 의자를 놓아 주었습니다. 병든 사내는 얼굴을 벽 쪽으로 돌린 채 누워 있었고, 제가 와 있다는 데 아무 신경을 쓰지 않았기에, 저는 제가 들어선 그곳을 찬찬히 살펴볼 겨를이 있었습니다.\n\n\"그는 낮이면 접어 올려 두는 낡은 침대 틀 위에 누워 있었습니다. 격자무늬 휘장의 누덕누덕 해진 잔해가 바람을 막으려 침대 머리맡에 둘러쳐져 있었으나, 바람은 문에 난 무수한 틈으로 그 안락이라곤 없는 방으로 비집고 들어와 그 휘장을 순간순간 이리저리 나부끼게 했습니다. 녹슨, 붙박이지 않은 화격자에는 낮은 잿불이 타고 있었고, 그 앞으로는 얼룩진 낡은 삼각 탁자가 끌려 나와 있었는데, 그 위에는 약병 몇 개와 깨진 유리잔, 그리고 몇 가지 살림 물건이 놓여 있었지요. 어린아이 하나가 바닥에 임시로 마련된 잠자리에서 자고 있었고, 여인은 그 곁의 의자에 앉아 있었습니다. 선반 두 개에는 접시 몇 개와 찻잔과 받침이 놓여 있었으며, 그 아래에는 무대용 신발 한 켤레와 펜싱용 검 두 자루가 걸려 있었습니다. 방 구석구석에 아무렇게나 던져 놓은 누더기와 보따리 무더기를 빼고는, 이것이 그 방에 있는 전부였습니다.\n\n\"저는 이 자잘한 것들을 눈여겨보고, 그 병든 사내의 거친 숨소리와 열에 들떠 흠칫거리는 모습을 살필 만큼의 시간이 있었습니다. 그가 제가 와 있음을 알아채기 전까지요. 머리를 편히 둘 자리를 찾으려고 안절부절 애쓰던 그는 손을 침대 밖으로 내던졌고, 그 손이 제 손 위에 떨어졌습니다. 그는 화들짝 일어나 제 얼굴을 골똘히 들여다보았습니다.\n\n\"'허틀리 씨예요, 존.' 그의 아내가 말했습니다. '오늘 밤에 당신이 부르러 보냈던 허틀리 씨 말이에요, 알잖아요.'\n\n\"'아!' 환자가 이마를 손으로 쓸며 말했습니다. '허틀리—허틀리—어디 보자.' 그는 몇 초간 생각을 추스르려 애쓰는 듯하더니, 제 손목을 꽉 움켜쥐며 말했습니다. '날 떠나지 마—날 떠나지 마요, 친구. 저 여자가 날 죽일 거요. 죽일 게 분명해.'\n\n\"'이런 지 오래되었소?' 제가 흐느끼는 그의 아내에게 물었습니다.\n\n\"'어젯밤부터요.' 그녀가 대답했습니다. '존, 존, 나 몰라보겠어요?'\n\n\"'저 여자를 내게 가까이 오지 못하게 해요.' 사내가 그녀가 자기 위로 몸을 굽히자 몸서리치며 말했습니다. '저 여자를 쫓아내요. 곁에 두는 걸 견딜 수가 없어.' 그는 죽음 같은 두려움의 눈빛으로 그녀를 사납게 노려보더니, 제 귀에 대고 속삭였습니다. '내가 저 여자를 때렸어, 젬. 어제도 때렸고, 그 전에도 여러 번 때렸지. 저 여자도 아이도 굶겼고. 그런데 이제 내가 약해지고 무력해졌으니, 젬, 저 여자가 그 앙갚음으로 날 죽일 거야. 분명히 그럴 거야. 자네가 나처럼 저 여자가 우는 걸 봤더라면, 자네도 알 거야. 저 여자를 떼어 놔 줘.' 그는 움켜쥔 손을 늦추고는 기진하여 베개에 다시 쓰러졌습니다.\n\n\"저는 이 모든 것이 무엇을 뜻하는지 너무도 잘 알았습니다. 설령 잠시라도 의심을 품었다 한들, 그 여인의 핼쑥한 얼굴과 야윈 몸을 한 번 보는 것만으로도 그 실상을 충분히 알 수 있었을 겁니다. '비켜서 계시는 게 낫겠습니다.' 제가 그 가엾은 여인에게 말했습니다. '당신은 그에게 도움이 되지 못합니다. 당신을 보지 않으면 좀 진정될지도 모르지요.' 그녀는 사내의 시야 밖으로 물러났습니다. 그는 몇 초 뒤 눈을 뜨고 불안하게 사방을 둘러보았습니다.\n\n\"'저 여자 갔소?' 그가 다급히 물었습니다.\n\n\"'네—네.' 제가 말했습니다. '저분이 당신을 해치지 못할 겁니다.'\n\n\"'내 말 좀 들어 봐, 젬.' 사내가 나직한 목소리로 말했습니다. '저 여자가 정말로 날 해친단 말이야. 저 여자 눈에는 뭔가가 있어서, 내 가슴에 그토록 끔찍한 두려움을 깨우니, 그게 날 미치게 만들어. 어젯밤 내내, 저 크고 부릅뜬 눈과 핼쑥한 얼굴이 내 얼굴 바로 곁에 붙어 있었어. 내가 어디로 돌아눕든 그것들도 돌아눕고, 잠결에 화들짝 깰 때마다 저 여자가 침대맡에서 날 들여다보고 있었지.' 그는 저를 더 가까이 끌어당기며, 깊고 겁에 질린 속삭임으로 말했습니다. '젬, 저 여자는 악령이 틀림없어—악마야! 쉿! 분명히 그래. 저 여자가 사람이었다면 벌써 옛날에 죽었을 거야. 어떤 여자도 저 여자가 견뎌 낸 걸 견디지 못했을 테니까.'\n\n\"그런 사내에게 그런 인상을 남기기까지 얼마나 길고 잔혹한 학대와 방치가 있었을지를 생각하니, 저는 속이 메스꺼워졌습니다. 저는 아무 대꾸도 할 수 없었습니다. 제 앞에 놓인 이 비참한 존재에게 누가 희망이나 위안을 건넬 수 있겠습니까?\n\n\"저는 거기 두 시간 넘게 앉아 있었는데, 그동안 그는 고통이나 초조의 신음을 중얼거리며 몸을 뒤척였고, 안절부절 두 팔을 이리저리 내던지며 끊임없이 옆으로 돌아누웠습니다. 마침내 그는 부분적인 무의식 상태에 빠졌는데, 그 상태에서는 정신이 이성의 통제 없이 이 장면에서 저 장면으로, 이곳에서 저곳으로 불안하게 떠돌면서도, 여전히 형언할 수 없는 현재의 고통 감각만은 떨쳐 내지 못합니다. 그의 두서없는 헛소리에서 사정이 그러함을 알아챈 데다, 십중팔구 열이 당장 더 악화되지는 않으리라는 것을 알았기에, 저는 그를 떠나며 그 비참한 아내에게 이튿날 저녁 다시 와서, 필요하다면 밤새 환자 곁을 지키겠노라 약속했습니다.\n\n\"저는 약속을 지켰습니다. 지난 스물네 시간이 무서운 변화를 빚어 놓았더군요. 두 눈은 깊이 꺼지고 무거워졌으나, 보기에 섬뜩한 광채로 빛나고 있었습니다. 입술은 바싹 마르고 여러 군데 갈라졌으며, 딱딱하고 메마른 살갗은 타는 듯한 열기로 달아올라 있었고, 사내의 얼굴에는 거의 이 세상 것 같지 않은 격렬한 불안의 기색이 어려 있어, 병이 갉아먹은 자취를 한층 더 강하게 드러냈습니다. 열은 절정에 달해 있었지요.\n\n\"저는 전날 밤 앉았던 자리에 앉아, 몇 시간이고 거기 앉아, 인간 가운데 가장 무딘 자의 가슴까지도 깊이 찔러 들 소리를—죽어 가는 사내의 끔찍한 헛소리를 들었습니다. 진료한 의사의 소견을 전해 들은 바로, 저는 그에게 아무 가망이 없음을 알았습니다. 저는 그의 임종 침상 곁에 앉아 있었던 것이지요. 저는 그 여윈 사지를 보았습니다—불과 몇 시간 전만 해도 소란스러운 객석을 즐겁게 하려고 일그러뜨려졌던 그 사지가, 이제 타는 듯한 열의 고문 아래 뒤틀리고 있었지요—저는 죽어 가는 사내의 나직한 웅얼거림에 뒤섞이는, 광대의 날카로운 웃음소리를 들었습니다.\n\n\"몸이 약하고 무력하게 눈앞에 누워 있는데 정신은 건강하던 시절의 평범한 일거리와 본분으로 되돌아가는 것을 듣는 일은 가슴을 저미는 일입니다. 그러나 그 일거리들이 진지하고 엄숙한 관념과 우리가 연결 짓는 모든 것과 가장 강하게 어긋나는 성질의 것일 때, 거기서 빚어지는 인상은 한없이 더 강렬합니다. 극장과 선술집이 그 비참한 사내의 헛소리에서 주된 주제였습니다. 저녁이라고 그는 상상했지요. 그날 밤에 맡은 배역이 있고, 시간이 늦었으니 당장 집을 나서야 한다고요. 어째서 사람들이 그를 붙잡아 못 가게 하는가?—돈을 못 받게 될 텐데—가야만 하는데. 안 돼! 그들이 그를 놓아주지 않아. 그는 타는 듯한 두 손에 얼굴을 묻고, 제 약함과 자기를 핍박하는 자들의 잔인함을 가냘프게 한탄했습니다. 잠시 멈추는가 싶더니, 그는 졸렬한 운율의 시구 몇 줄을—그가 마지막으로 익혔던 시구를—외쳐 댔습니다. 그는 침대에서 일어나 시든 사지를 끌어모으고는 거칠고 우스꽝스러운 자세로 뒹굴었습니다. 연기를 하고 있었던 것이지요—극장에 있었던 것입니다. 일 분간의 침묵이 흐르고, 그는 어떤 떠들썩한 노래의 후렴을 흥얼거렸습니다. 마침내 그는 그 오래된 선술집에 닿았던 것입니다—방 안이 어찌 이리 더운가. 그는 아팠습니다, 몹시 아팠지요, 하지만 이제는 멀쩡하고 행복합니다. 그의 잔을 채워라. 그의 입술에서 잔을 쳐서 떨어뜨린 게 누구인가? 전에도 그를 따라다니던 바로 그 핍박자였습니다. 그는 베개에 다시 쓰러져 큰 소리로 신음했습니다. 잠깐의 망각이 흐르고, 그는 천장이 낮은 방들이 이어진 지긋지긋한 미로를 헤매고 있었습니다—어떤 데는 어찌나 낮은지 손과 무릎으로 기어야만 길을 나아갈 수 있었지요. 답답하고 어두웠으며, 어디로 돌아서든 무언가 장애물이 그의 앞길을 막았습니다. 벌레들도 있었지요, 그를 노려보는 눈을 가진 흉측하게 기어 다니는 것들이, 사방의 공기를 가득 채우며 그곳의 짙은 어둠 속에서 끔찍하게 번득였습니다. 벽과 천장이 파충류로 우글거렸고—그 토굴은 거대한 크기로 부풀었으며—무시무시한 형상들이 이리저리 휙휙 날아다녔고—그가 아는 사람들의 얼굴이, 비웃고 입을 일그러뜨려 흉측해진 채 그것들 사이로 내다보았습니다. 그들은 달군 쇠로 그를 지지고, 피가 솟구칠 때까지 그의 머리를 밧줄로 동여맸으며—그는 살려고 미친 듯이 몸부림쳤습니다.\n\n\"이런 발작 가운데 하나가 끝날 무렵, 제가 무척 애를 써서 그를 침대에 눌러 뉘었을 때, 그는 잠인 듯한 상태로 빠져들었습니다. 지켜보느라, 또 힘쓰느라 진이 빠진 저는 몇 분간 눈을 감았는데, 그때 누군가 제 어깨를 사납게 움켜쥐는 것을 느꼈습니다. 저는 곧바로 깨어났지요. 그는 몸을 일으켜 침대에 앉은 자세였고—그의 얼굴에는 무서운 변화가 닥쳐 있었으나, 의식이 돌아와 있었으니, 분명 저를 알아보았기 때문입니다. 그의 헛소리에 진작부터 잠을 설치던 아이가 제 작은 잠자리에서 일어나, 겁에 질려 비명을 지르며 아버지에게 달려갔습니다—어머니는 광기의 발작 속에서 그가 아이를 해칠까 봐 황급히 아이를 품에 끌어안았으나, 그의 일그러진 얼굴에 겁을 먹어 침대맡에 못 박힌 듯 서 있었습니다. 그는 제 어깨를 발작적으로 움켜쥐고, 다른 손으로 제 가슴을 치며, 필사적으로 무어라 말하려 했습니다. 헛일이었지요. 그는 그들을 향해 팔을 뻗고 또 한 번 사납게 안간힘을 썼습니다. 목구멍에서 그렁거리는 소리가 났고—두 눈이 번쩍였으며—짧게 막힌 신음이 새어 나오더니—그는 뒤로 쓰러졌습니다—숨이 끊긴 채로!\"\n\n앞의 일화에 대한 픽윅 씨의 견해를 기록할 수 있다면 우리로서는 더없는 만족일 것이다. 더없이 불행한 사건만 아니었더라면 그것을 독자들께 제시할 수 있었으리라는 데 우리는 별 의심이 없다.\n\n픽윅 씨는 이야기의 마지막 몇 문장 동안 손에 쥐고 있던 잔을 탁자에 도로 내려놓고는, 막 입을 떼기로 마음먹은 참이었다—실은, 그가 실제로 입을 벌렸다고 단언할 근거를 우리는 스노드그래스 씨의 수첩에서 얻고 있다—그때 급사가 방으로 들어와 말했다.\n\n\"손님들이 오셨습니다, 선생님.\"\n\n이렇게 방해받았을 때 픽윅 씨가 막 어떤 발언을 내놓으려던 참이었으리라는 추측이 있는데, 그 발언은 템스강까지는 아니더라도 세상을 밝혀 주었을 것이다. 그는 급사의 얼굴을 준엄하게 쏘아본 뒤, 새로 온 이들에 관한 정보를 구하기라도 하듯 좌중을 두루 둘러보았기 때문이다.\n\n\"오!\" 윈클 씨가 일어서며 말했다. \"내 친구들일세—들여보내게. 아주 유쾌한 친구들이지요.\" 급사가 물러간 뒤 윈클 씨가 덧붙였다. \"제97연대 장교들인데, 오늘 아침 제법 묘하게 알게 되었답니다. 다들 무척 마음에 드실 겁니다.\"\n\n픽윅 씨의 평정심은 곧바로 회복되었다. 급사가 돌아와 세 신사를 방으로 안내해 들였다.\n\n\"태플턴 중위입니다.\" 윈클 씨가 말했다. \"태플턴 중위, 이쪽은 픽윅 씨—페인 군의관, 이쪽은 픽윅 씨—스노드그래스 씨는 전에 만나셨고, 제 친구 터프먼 씨, 이쪽은 페인 군의관—슬래머 군의관, 이쪽은 픽윅 씨—터프먼 씨, 이쪽은 슬래머 군의—\"\n\n여기서 윈클 씨는 불현듯 말을 멈추었다. 터프먼 씨와 군의관 양쪽의 얼굴에 강한 감정이 역력했기 때문이다.\n\n\"나는 이 신사분을 전에 만난 적이 있소.\" 군의관이 또렷이 힘을 주어 말했다.\n\n\"그렇소이까!\" 윈클 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그리고—그리고 저자도, 내가 잘못 본 게 아니라면 말이오.\" 군의관이 초록 코트를 입은 낯선 이에게 살피는 듯한 눈길을 던지며 말했다. \"내 생각엔 어젯밤 저자에게 아주 절박한 초대를 한 것 같은데, 저자는 그걸 마다하는 게 옳다고 여겼지요.\" 그렇게 말하면서 군의관은 그 낯선 이를 도량 있게 노려보고는, 친구인 태플턴 중위에게 무언가를 속삭였다.\n\n\"설마 그럴 리가.\" 그 신사가 속삭임이 끝나자 말했다.\n\n\"정말이라니까.\" 슬래머 군의관이 대답했다.\n\n\"당장 저자를 걷어차 줘야 하네.\" 야영 의자의 주인이 자못 거드름을 피우며 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"좀 조용히 하게, 페인.\" 중위가 끼어들었다. \"선생, 한 가지 여쭤도 되겠습니까.\" 그는 이 무척이나 무례한 곁다리 연극에 적잖이 어리둥절해진 픽윅 씨를 향해 말했다. \"선생, 저 사람이 댁의 일행에 속하는지 여쭤도 되겠습니까?\"\n\n\"아니오, 선생.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다. \"저분은 우리의 손님이오.\"\n\n\"그럼 댁네 클럽 회원이로군요, 내가 잘못 안 게 아니라면?\" 중위가 캐묻듯 말했다.\n\n\"전혀 아니오.\" 픽윅 씨가 응했다.\n\n\"그리고 댁네 클럽 단추도 한 번도 단 적이 없고요?\" 중위가 말했다.\n\n\"없소—한 번도!\" 어리둥절해진 픽윅 씨가 대답했다.\n\n태플턴 중위는 자기 기억의 정확성에 대한 의심을 내비치기라도 하듯, 거의 알아채기 힘들 만큼 어깨를 으쓱하며 친구인 슬래머 군의관 쪽으로 돌아섰다. 키 작은 군의관은 노기를 띠었으나 당혹스러운 표정이었고, 페인 씨는 아무것도 모르는 픽윅의 환한 얼굴을 사나운 기색으로 노려보았다.\n\n\"선생.\" 군의관이 불쑥 터프먼 씨에게 말을 건넸는데, 그 어조는 마치 종아리에 핀을 교묘히 찔러 넣기라도 한 듯 그 신사를 눈에 띄게 움찔하게 했다. \"댁은 어젯밤 여기 무도회에 있었지요!\"\n\n터프먼 씨는 내내 픽윅 씨를 몹시 뚫어지게 바라보며, 가냘프게 그렇다고 헐떡이듯 답했다.\n\n\"저 사람이 댁의 동행이었소.\" 군의관이 여전히 미동도 없는 낯선 이를 가리키며 말했다.\n\n터프먼 씨는 그 사실을 인정했다.\n\n\"자, 선생.\" 군의관이 낯선 이에게 말했다. \"이 신사분들 앞에서 다시 한번 묻겠소. 댁은 내게 명함을 건네고 신사로서의 대우를 받겠소, 아니면 내가 이 자리에서 직접 댁을 응징할 수밖에 없게 만들겠소?\"\n\n\"잠깐, 선생.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"나는 정말이지 이 일이 약간의 해명 없이 더 진행되도록 둘 수가 없소. 터프먼, 사정을 이야기해 보게.\"\n\n이처럼 엄숙히 종용받은 터프먼 씨는 몇 마디로 그 사정을 진술했다. 코트를 빌린 일은 슬쩍 건드리고, 그것이 '저녁 식사 후'에 일어났다는 점은 길게 부연했으며, 자기 책임에 대해 약간의 뉘우침으로 마무리한 뒤, 낯선 이가 알아서 변명하도록 내버려 두었다.\n\n낯선 이가 막 그렇게 하려는 참이었는데, 그를 큰 호기심으로 훑어보던 태플턴 중위가 자못 경멸조로 말했다. \"내가 댁을 극장에서 본 적이 있지 않소, 선생?\"\n\n\"그렇소.\" 낯선 이가 주눅 들지 않고 대답했다.\n\n\"이자는 떠돌이 배우라네!\" 중위가 슬래머 군의관을 돌아보며 경멸조로 말했다. \"내일 밤 제52연대 장교들이 로체스터 극장에서 올리는 연극에 출연하는 자야. 슬래머, 이 일을 더 진행할 수는 없네—불가능해!\"\n\n\"전혀!\" 위엄을 차린 페인이 말했다.\n\n\"이런 불쾌한 처지에 빠뜨려 드려 유감입니다.\" 태플턴 중위가 픽윅 씨에게 말했다. \"앞으로 이런 장면이 되풀이되는 것을 피하는 최선의 길은, 동행을 고르는 데 좀 더 신중을 기하시는 것임을 일러 드리지요. 안녕히 계시오, 선생!\" 그러고는 중위는 방에서 펄쩍 뛰쳐나갔다.\n\n\"그리고 한마디 하겠소, 선생.\" 성마른 페인 군의관이 말했다. \"만일 내가 태플턴이었거나, 내가 슬래머였더라면, 댁의 코를, 그리고 이 자리에 있는 모든 사람의 코를 비틀어 놓았을 거요. 그랬을 거요, 선생—한 사람도 빠짐없이. 내 이름은 페인이오, 선생—제43연대 페인 군의관이지. 안녕히 계시오, 선생.\" 이 연설을 마치고 마지막 세 마디를 큰 소리로 내뱉은 뒤, 그는 친구의 뒤를 위풍당당하게 성큼성큼 따라 나갔고, 슬래머 군의관이 바짝 뒤따랐는데, 그는 아무 말도 하지 않고 그저 한 차례 눈길로 좌중을 짓눌러 버리는 것으로 만족했다.\n\n위의 도전적 언사가 쏟아지는 동안, 치미는 분노와 극도의 당혹감이 픽윅 씨의 고결한 가슴을 부풀려, 조끼가 터질 지경에 이르게 했다. 그는 그 자리에 못 박힌 듯 서서 허공을 응시했다. 문이 닫히는 소리가 그를 제정신으로 돌려놓았다. 그는 얼굴에 격분을, 눈에 불을 담고 앞으로 달려 나갔다. 그의 손이 문고리에 닿았다. 한순간만 더 있었더라면 그것은 제43연대 페인 군의관의 멱살에 닿았을 터인데, 스노드그래스 씨가 자기 존경하는 지도자의 코트 자락을 붙잡아 뒤로 끌어당기지 않았더라면 말이다.\n\n\"저분을 말리게.\" 스노드그래스 씨가 외쳤다. \"윈클, 터프먼—이런 명분 따위에 저분의 고귀한 목숨을 걸게 해서는 안 되네.\"\n\n\"놓아 주게.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"단단히 붙잡게.\" 스노드그래스 씨가 소리쳤다. 그리하여 좌중 전원의 합심한 노력으로 픽윅 씨는 안락의자에 떠밀려 앉혀졌다.\n\n\"내버려 두시오.\" 초록 코트를 입은 낯선 이가 말했다. \"브랜디 워터—유쾌한 노신사—배짱도 두둑하시고—이걸 들이켜시오—아!—기막힌 물건이지.\" 음울한 사내가 섞어 놓은 가득 찬 잔의 효능을 미리 시험해 본 뒤, 낯선 이는 그 잔을 픽윅 씨의 입에 갖다 댔고, 잔에 남은 것은 순식간에 사라졌다.\n\n잠깐의 멈춤이 흘렀다. 브랜디 워터가 제 몫을 해냈고, 픽윅 씨의 온화한 얼굴은 빠르게 평소의 표정을 되찾아 갔다.\n\n\"저들은 당신이 마음 쓸 가치도 없는 자들입니다.\" 음울한 사내가 말했다.\n\n\"옳은 말씀이오, 선생.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다. \"가치도 없지요. 이런 격앙된 감정에 휩쓸린 것이 부끄럽소. 의자를 탁자 쪽으로 끌어당기시오, 선생.\"\n\n음울한 사내는 선뜻 응했다. 다시 탁자를 둘러싸고 둥글게 자리가 잡혔고, 화기애애함이 또 한 번 자리를 지배했다. 어딘가 가시지 않은 짜증이 윈클 씨의 가슴속에 머물 자리를 찾은 듯했는데, 아마도 그의 코트가 잠시 빌려진 일 탓이리라—하긴 그토록 사소한 일이 픽윅 회원의 가슴에 스치는 분노의 감정이나마 불러일으켰으리라고 보기는 좀처럼 합당치 않지만 말이다. 이 한 가지를 빼고는, 그들의 좋은 기분은 온전히 회복되었고, 그날 저녁은 시작될 때와 같은 흥겨움으로 마무리되었다.\n\n많은 저자들은 그들이 귀중한 정보를 길어 올린 출처를 밝히는 일에 어리석을 뿐만 아니라 실로 정직하지 못한 거부감을 품는다. 우리는 그런 감정이 없다. 우리는 다만 편집자로서의 책임 있는 직무를 올곧게 수행하려 애쓸 따름이다. 다른 정황이었더라면 이 모험담들의 저작권을 주장하고 싶은 야심을 어느 정도 느꼈을지 모르나, 진실에 대한 존중이 우리로 하여금 그것들을 분별 있게 배열하고 공정하게 서술한 공로를 주장하는 것 이상은 하지 못하게 막는다. 픽윅 페이퍼스는 우리의 뉴 리버 헤드이며, 우리는 뉴 리버 회사에 견줄 만하다. 다른 이들의 노고가 우리를 위해 중요한 사실들의 거대한 저수지를 쌓아 올렸다. 우리는 그저 그것들을 끌어다가, 맑고 부드러운 물줄기로, 이 지면이라는 매개를 통해, 픽윅적 지식에 목말라하는 세상에 흘려보낼 뿐이다.\n\n이러한 정신에 따라, 우리가 참고한 권위들에 진 빚을 밝히겠다는 결심을 단호히 밀고 나가면서, 우리는 솔직히 말한다. 이 장과 다음 장에 기록된 세부 사실들은 스노드그래스 씨의 수첩에 빚진 것이라고. 이제 양심의 짐을 벗었으니, 우리는 더 이상의 부연 없이 그 세부 사실들을 상세히 풀어 나가기로 한다.\n\n로체스터와 그 인근 마을의 주민 전체가 이튿날 아침 이른 시각에 더없는 부산함과 흥분 속에서 자리를 박차고 일어났다. 진지에서 성대한 열병식이 열릴 예정이었다. 여섯 개 연대의 기동훈련을 총사령관의 매서운 눈이 사열할 것이었으며, 임시 요새가 세워졌고, 성채가 공격받아 함락될 것이었으며, 지뢰가 터질 것이었다.\n\n픽윅 씨는, 우리가 그의 채텀 묘사에서 인용한 짤막한 발췌를 통해 독자들이 짐작했을지 모르나, 군대를 열렬히 흠모하는 사람이었다. 이 광경만큼 그를 기쁘게 할 수 있는 것은 없었으며, 그의 동행들 각자가 지닌 독특한 감정과 이토록 잘 어우러질 수 있는 것도 없었다. 그리하여 그들은 곧 길을 나서, 작전이 벌어질 무대를 향해 걸어갔는데, 그쪽으로는 이미 사방에서 사람들이 무리 지어 쏟아져 들어오고 있었다.\n\n진지에서 모든 것의 모습은 다가오는 의식이 더없이 웅장하고 중대한 것임을 말해 주고 있었다. 군대를 위해 자리를 지키려고 보초들이 배치되어 있었고, 포대 위에는 부인들의 자리를 맡아 두는 하인들이 있었으며, 양피지로 표지를 씌운 책을 옆구리에 낀 하사관들이 이리저리 뛰어다녔고, 정장 군복을 차려입은 불더 대령은 말 위에 올라 처음에는 이쪽으로, 다음에는 저쪽으로 질주하며, 사람들 사이로 말을 뒤로 몰고, 앞발을 들게 하고, 껑충껑충 뛰게 하며, 더없이 놀라운 태도로 고함을 질러 대 목소리가 몹시 쉬고 얼굴이 몹시 벌게졌으나, 거기에는 어떤 짐작할 만한 까닭이나 이유도 전혀 없었다. 장교들은 앞뒤로 뛰어다니며, 처음에는 불더 대령과 의논하고, 다음에는 하사관들에게 명령하고, 그다음에는 죄다 달아나 버렸으며, 심지어 사병들조차 광택 나는 옷깃 너머로 신비로운 엄숙함을 풍기며 바라보고 있었으니, 그것은 이 행사의 특별한 성격을 충분히 드러내 주었다.\n\n픽윅 씨와 그의 세 동행은 군중의 맨 앞에 자리를 잡고, 행사가 시작되기를 참을성 있게 기다렸다. 군중은 시시각각 불어났으며, 그들이 차지한 자리를 지키려고 어쩔 수 없이 들여야 했던 노력은 그 뒤로 이어진 두 시간 동안 그들의 주의를 충분히 사로잡았다. 한번은 뒤에서 갑자기 밀어붙이는 힘이 가해져, 픽윅 씨가 그의 전반적인 점잖은 태도와는 도무지 어울리지 않는 속도와 탄력으로 몇 야드 앞으로 홱 떠밀려 나갔다. 또 한번은 앞쪽에서 \"물러서라\"는 요구가 있었는데, 그러면 머스킷 총의 개머리판이 픽윅 씨의 발가락 위로 떨어져 그 요구를 일깨워 주거나, 아니면 그의 가슴팍에 들이밀려 그 요구가 반드시 지켜지도록 만들었다. 그러더니 왼편의 어떤 익살스러운 신사들이 한 무리로 옆에서 밀어붙여 스노드그래스 씨를 인간이 견딜 수 있는 고문의 극한까지 짓눌러 놓고는, \"워디로 떠미는 거유?\"라고 알고 싶어 했다. 그리고 윈클 씨가 이 까닭 없는 폭행을 목격한 데 대한 극심한 분노를 다 표하고 나면, 뒤의 누군가가 그의 모자를 눈 위까지 푹 눌러 씌우고는 머리를 호주머니에 넣어 주십사 청하는 것이었다. 이런 것들과 그 밖의 짓궂은 익살들이, 터프먼 씨가 영문 모르게 사라져 버린 일과 (그는 갑자기 자취를 감추어 어디서도 찾을 수 없었다) 맞물려, 그들의 처지를 전체적으로 보아 즐겁거나 바람직하다기보다는 오히려 더 불편한 것으로 만들었다.\n\n마침내 무언가 기다리던 것이 도착했음을 알리는, 여러 목소리의 그 낮은 함성이 군중 사이로 퍼져 나갔다. 모든 눈이 출격문 쪽으로 향했다. 간절한 기대의 몇 순간이 지나자, 군기들이 공중에서 흥겹게 펄럭이는 것이 보였고, 무기들이 햇빛 속에서 환하게 번쩍였으며, 종대가 잇따라 평원으로 쏟아져 나왔다. 군대가 멈춰 서서 대형을 갖추었고, 명령이 대열을 따라 울려 퍼졌으며, 받들어총을 하느라 머스킷 총들이 일제히 철컥하는 소리를 냈다. 그리고 불더 대령과 수많은 장교를 거느린 총사령관이 가벼운 구보로 앞으로 나왔다. 군악대들이 일제히 연주를 시작했고, 말들은 저마다 두 다리로 서서 뒷걸음치며 사방으로 꼬리를 휘둘러 댔으며, 개들은 짖고, 군중은 비명을 지르고, 군대는 자세를 가다듬었으며, 눈길이 닿는 한 어느 쪽을 보아도 보이는 것은 붉은 군복과 흰 바지가 고정된 채 미동도 없이 길게 늘어선 광경뿐이었다.\n\n픽윅 씨는 여기저기 넘어지며, 그리고 말들의 다리 사이에서 기적적으로 몸을 빼내느라 너무도 분주했던 터라, 눈앞의 광경이 우리가 방금 묘사한 모습을 갖추게 될 때까지는 그것을 관찰할 여유를 충분히 누리지 못했다. 마침내 두 다리로 굳건히 설 수 있게 되었을 때, 그의 만족과 기쁨은 끝이 없었다.\n\n\"이보다 더 멋지고 더 즐거운 것이 어디 있겠소?\" 그가 윈클 씨에게 물었다.\n\n\"없지요.\" 그 신사가 대답했는데, 그는 바로 앞선 십오 분 동안 두 발을 각각 키 작은 남자에게 하나씩 밟히고 있던 터였다.\n\n\"참으로 고결하고 찬란한 광경입니다.\" 스노드그래스 씨가 말했는데, 그의 가슴속에서는 시의 불길이 빠르게 타오르고 있었다. \"조국의 용맹한 수호자들이 평화로운 시민들 앞에 찬란하게 정렬한 모습을 보다니요. 그들의 얼굴은 빛나고 있습니다—호전적인 사나움이 아니라 문명화된 온화함으로 말입니다. 그들의 눈은 번뜩이고 있습니다—약탈이나 복수의 거친 불꽃이 아니라 인간애와 지성의 부드러운 빛으로 말입니다.\"\n\n픽윅 씨는 이 찬사의 정신에 충분히 공감했으나, 그 표현을 그대로 되받아 외칠 수는 없었다. 왜냐하면 지성의 부드러운 빛은 그 전사들의 눈 속에서 다소 희미하게 타오르고 있었기 때문이다. \"앞을 보라\"는 명령이 떨어진 터라, 구경꾼의 눈앞에 보이는 것이라곤 정면을 똑바로 응시하는, 어떤 표정도 전혀 깃들지 않은 수천 쌍의 눈동자뿐이었던 것이다.\n\n\"우리는 이제 아주 좋은 자리에 있군요.\" 픽윅 씨가 주위를 둘러보며 말했다. 그들 바로 가까이에 있던 군중이 차츰 흩어져, 그들은 거의 그들끼리만 남아 있었다.\n\n\"아주 좋군요!\" 스노드그래스 씨와 윈클 씨가 입을 모아 따라 했다.\n\n\"저들이 지금 뭘 하는 거지요?\" 픽윅 씨가 안경을 고쳐 쓰며 물었다.\n\n\"제—제 생각엔 말이죠.\" 윈클 씨가 안색이 변하며 말했다. \"제 생각엔 발포할 것 같습니다.\"\n\n\"말도 안 되는 소리.\" 픽윅 씨가 다급히 말했다.\n\n\"저—저는 정말로 그럴 것 같습니다.\" 스노드그래스 씨가 다소 겁먹은 채 거들었다.\n\n\"그럴 리가 없소.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다. 그가 그 말을 채 내뱉기도 전에, 여섯 개 연대 전체가 마치 단 하나의 공통된 목표를 가진 듯 머스킷 총을 겨누었으니, 그 목표란 바로 픽윅 회원들이었고, 일찍이 대지를 그 중심까지 뒤흔든 적도, 또는 노신사를 그 중심에서 떨어뜨린 적도 없을 만큼 더없이 무시무시하고 어마어마한 일제 사격을 터뜨렸다.\n\n바로 이 시련의 상황에서, 공포탄의 따가운 사격에 노출되고, 반대편에서 새로운 한 무리가 정렬하기 시작한 군대의 작전에 시달리는 가운데, 픽윅 씨는 위대한 정신에 없어서는 안 될 동반자인 그 완벽한 침착함과 자제력을 보여 주었다. 그는 윈클 씨의 팔을 붙잡고, 그 신사와 스노드그래스 씨 사이에 자신을 세운 뒤, 소음 때문에 귀가 먹먹해질 가능성을 넘어서는, 발포로 인한 어떤 임박한 위험도 염려할 것이 없음을 기억하라고 간곡히 당부했다.\n\n\"하지만—하지만—혹시 누군가 실수로 실탄을 가지고 있다면 어쩌지요.\" 윈클 씨가 스스로 떠올린 그 상상에 새파랗게 질려 항변했다. \"방금 무언가 공중을 가르고 휙 지나가는 소리를 들었거든요—아주 날카롭게—제 귀 바로 옆을요.\"\n\n\"우리 얼굴을 땅에 대고 엎드리는 게 낫지 않을까요?\" 스노드그래스 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 아니오—이제 끝났소.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. 그의 입술은 떨렸을지 모르고 그의 뺨은 창백해졌을지 모르나, 그 불멸의 인물의 입술에서는 두려움이나 근심의 기색이라곤 한마디도 새어 나오지 않았다.\n\n픽윅 씨가 옳았다—발포가 그쳤다. 그러나 그가 자신의 견해가 정확했음을 자축할 겨를도 거의 갖기 전에, 대열 안에서 재빠른 움직임이 보였다. 명령의 쉰 외침이 대열을 따라 퍼져 나갔고, 일행 중 누구도 이 새로운 기동의 의미를 미처 짐작하기도 전에, 여섯 개 연대 전체가 총검을 꽂은 채 속보로 픽윅 씨와 그의 친구들이 서 있는 바로 그 지점을 향해 돌격해 내려왔다.\n\n인간은 한낱 죽을 운명일 뿐이며, 인간의 용기가 미칠 수 없는 한계점이라는 것이 있다. 픽윅 씨는 안경 너머로 한순간 밀려드는 무리를 응시하더니, 그러고는 완전히 등을 돌리고는—달아났다고는 하지 않겠다. 첫째로 그것은 비천한 표현이기 때문이요, 둘째로 픽윅 씨의 몸매는 결코 그런 식의 후퇴에 적합하지 않았기 때문이다—그는 다리가 옮겨 주는 한 빠른 속도로 종종걸음을 쳤으니, 실로 어찌나 빨랐던지 자신이 처한 상황의 난처함을 그 온전한 크기로 깨달았을 때는 이미 너무 늦은 뒤였다.\n\n몇 초 전 그 정렬로 픽윅 씨를 어리둥절하게 했던 반대편 군대는, 성채를 거짓으로 포위한 가짜 공격자들의 모의 공격을 격퇴하려고 대형을 갖춘 것이었다. 그리하여 픽윅 씨와 그의 두 동행은 매우 길게 늘어선 두 대열 사이에 별안간 갇혀 버린 자신들을 발견하게 되었으니, 한쪽은 빠른 속도로 진격해 오고 있었고, 다른 한쪽은 적대적인 대형을 이룬 채 충돌을 굳건히 기다리고 있었다.\n\n\"어이!\" 진격해 오는 대열의 장교들이 고함쳤다.\n\n\"비켜라!\" 멈춰 서 있는 대열의 장교들이 외쳤다.\n\n\"우리더러 어디로 가란 말이오?\" 당황한 픽윅 회원들이 비명을 질렀다.\n\n\"어이—어이—어이!\" 그것이 유일한 대답이었다. 한순간 극심한 당혹이 있었고, 묵직한 발소리, 격렬한 충돌, 숨죽인 웃음소리가 있었다. 여섯 개 연대는 반 마일 저편에 있었고, 픽윅 씨의 장화 밑창은 공중에 치켜올려져 있었다.\n\n스노드그래스 씨와 윈클 씨는 저마다 놀라운 민첩함으로 강제적인 공중제비를 한 차례씩 넘었는데, 후자가 땅바닥에 주저앉아 노란 비단 손수건으로 코에서 흘러나오는 생명의 물줄기를 막으며 눈에 처음 들어온 광경은, 저만치 떨어진 곳에서 자신의 모자를 쫓아 달려가는 그들의 존경하는 지도자의 모습이었으니, 그 모자는 저 멀리로 장난스럽게 깡충거리며 달아나고 있었다.\n\n사람의 일생에서 자신의 모자를 쫓아갈 때만큼 우스꽝스러운 곤경을 그토록 많이 겪거나, 그토록 적은 자비로운 동정을 받는 순간도 드물다. 모자를 붙잡는 데는 엄청난 침착함과 남다른 정도의 판단력이 필요하다. 사람은 성급해서는 안 되니, 그러면 모자를 밟고 넘어가 버린다. 또 그 반대의 극단으로 달려들어서도 안 되니, 그러면 모자를 아주 놓쳐 버린다. 가장 좋은 방법은 쫓는 대상과 부드럽게 보조를 맞추며, 빈틈없이 신중하게 기회를 잘 엿보아, 차츰 그 앞으로 나아간 다음, 재빨리 몸을 날려 모자의 우묵한 꼭대기를 붙잡아 머리에 단단히 눌러쓰는 것이다. 그러면서도 줄곧 즐겁게 미소를 지어, 마치 그것이 누구에게나 그렇듯 자신에게도 썩 좋은 농담이라고 여기는 듯 보이는 것이다.\n\n부드럽고 산뜻한 바람이 불었고, 픽윅 씨의 모자는 그 앞에서 장난스럽게 굴러갔다. 바람이 푹푹 불었고, 픽윅 씨도 헉헉거렸으며, 모자는 거센 물살 속의 활기찬 돌고래처럼 흥겹게 데굴데굴 굴러갔다. 그리고 그 신사가 모자를 운명에 내맡기려던 바로 그 찰나에 섭리의 손길로 그 진로가 막히지 않았더라면, 모자는 픽윅 씨의 손이 닿지 않는 저 멀리까지 계속 굴러갔을 것이다.\n\n우리가 말하건대, 픽윅 씨는 완전히 기진맥진하여 추격을 막 포기하려던 참이었는데, 그때 모자가 어느 정도 세차게 날려 가 어느 마차의 바퀴에 부딪혔으니, 그 마차는 그의 발걸음이 향하던 바로 그 자리에 다른 여섯 대의 탈것과 한 줄로 세워져 있었다. 픽윅 씨는 자신에게 유리해진 형국을 알아채고는 잽싸게 앞으로 달려 나가, 자기 소유물을 확보하여 머리에 얹고는, 숨을 돌리려고 멈춰 섰다. 그가 가만히 멈춰 선 지 채 삼십 초도 되지 않았을 때, 어떤 목소리가 그의 이름을 간절히 부르는 것을 들었는데, 그는 그것이 터프먼 씨의 목소리임을 즉시 알아차렸다. 위를 올려다보니 그는 놀라움과 기쁨으로 가득 찬 광경을 보게 되었다.\n\n지붕 없는 사륜마차 한 대—붐비는 장소에 더 잘 맞추려고 말들을 떼어 낸 마차—안에 서 있는 것은, 푸른 외투에 번쩍이는 단추를 달고 코듀로이 반바지에 목 긴 장화를 신은 뚱뚱한 노신사와, 숄을 두르고 깃털을 단 두 젊은 숙녀와, 숄을 두르고 깃털을 단 그 젊은 숙녀들 중 한 명에게 반한 듯한 한 젊은 신사와, 나이를 가늠하기 어려운, 아마도 앞서 말한 숙녀들의 고모인 듯한 부인과, 그리고 터프먼 씨였는데, 터프먼 씨는 마치 갓난아기 시절부터 그 가족의 일원이었던 것처럼 편안하고 태평스러웠다. 사륜마차 뒤에 묶여 있는 것은 넉넉한 크기의 바구니였으니—사색에 잠긴 마음에 차가운 닭고기와 혓바닥 고기와 포도주병에 얽힌 연상을 언제나 불러일으키는 그런 바구니들 가운데 하나였다—그리고 마부석에는 뚱뚱하고 얼굴이 벌건 소년이 졸음에 겨운 상태로 앉아 있었으니, 사색을 즐기는 관찰자라면 누구든 그 소년을, 앞서 말한 바구니 속 내용물을 소비할 적절한 때가 오면 그것을 나누어 줄 공식 분배자라고 단번에 단정 짓지 않고서는 한순간도 바라볼 수 없었을 것이다.\n\n픽윅 씨가 이 흥미로운 대상들에 황급히 한 번 눈길을 던졌을 때, 그의 충실한 제자가 다시 그를 향해 인사를 건넸다.\n\n\"픽윅—픽윅.\" 터프먼 씨가 말했다. \"이리 올라오시오. 어서요.\"\n\n\"이리 오시지요, 선생. 어서 올라오십시오.\" 뚱뚱한 신사가 말했다. \"조!—저 녀석 망할, 또 잠들어 버렸군.—조, 발판 좀 내려라.\" 뚱뚱한 소년이 마부석에서 느릿느릿 굴러 내려와 발판을 내리고는 마차 문을 손짓하듯 활짝 열어 놓았다. 바로 그 순간 스노드그래스 씨와 윈클 씨가 다가왔다.\n\n\"여러분 모두 탈 자리가 있소이다.\" 뚱뚱한 사내가 말했다. \"둘은 안에, 하나는 밖에 타시오. 조, 이분들 중 한 분을 위해 마부석에 자리를 만들어라. 자, 선생, 이리 오시오.\" 그러고는 뚱뚱한 신사가 팔을 뻗어, 먼저 픽윅 씨를, 다음에 스노드그래스 씨를 힘으로 끌어당겨 사륜마차 안으로 들였다. 윈클 씨는 마부석에 올라탔고, 뚱뚱한 소년은 똑같은 자리로 뒤뚱뒤뚱 올라가더니 곧바로 깊이 잠들어 버렸다.\n\n\"자, 여러분.\" 뚱뚱한 사내가 말했다. \"만나 뵙게 되어 무척 반갑소이다. 여러분이 나를 기억하지 못하실지 몰라도, 나는 여러분을 아주 잘 알지요. 지난겨울에 여러분의 클럽에서 몇 차례 저녁을 보냈소이다—오늘 아침에 여기 내 친구 터프먼 씨를 만났는데, 그를 보게 되어 어찌나 반가웠는지 모른다오. 그래, 선생, 어떻게 지내시오? 참으로 보기 드물게 좋아 보이시는구려, 정말로.\"\n\n픽윅 씨는 그 찬사를 받아들이고, 목 긴 장화를 신은 뚱뚱한 신사와 다정하게 악수를 나누었다.\n\n\"자, 그래 댁은 어떻소, 선생?\" 뚱뚱한 신사가 아버지 같은 근심을 담아 스노드그래스 씨에게 말을 건넸다. \"아주 좋으시다고? 그래요, 그거 잘됐소—잘됐어. 그리고 댁은 어떻소, 선생 (윈클 씨에게)? 거참, 좋다고 하시니 반갑소이다. 정말로 무척 반가워요, 암. 여러분, 내 딸들이오—이 애들이 내 딸내미들이지요. 그리고 저쪽은 내 누이, 레이철 와들 양이오. 이 사람은 미스인데—그러면서도 미스가 아니지요—그렇잖소, 선생, 안 그렇소?\" 그러고는 뚱뚱한 신사가 장난스럽게 팔꿈치를 픽윅 씨의 갈비뼈 사이로 찔러 넣으며 아주 호탕하게 웃었다.\n\n\"어머, 오라버니도!\" 와들 양이 못마땅한 듯 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"맞지, 맞아.\" 뚱뚱한 신사가 말했다. \"아무도 그걸 부정할 순 없지. 여러분, 실례했소이다. 이쪽은 내 친구 트런들 씨요. 자, 이제 다들 서로 알게 되었으니, 편안하고 즐겁게 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 구경합시다. 내 말이 그 말이오.\" 그러고는 뚱뚱한 신사가 안경을 쓰자, 픽윅 씨도 자기 안경을 꺼냈고, 모두가 마차 안에서 일어서서 서로의 어깨 너머로 군대의 기동을 바라보았다.\n\n그것은 놀라운 기동이었으니, 한 열이 다른 열의 머리 너머로 발포하고는 달아나고, 그러면 다른 열이 또 다른 열의 머리 너머로 발포하고는 그 차례로 달아나고, 그러고는 장교들을 가운데 두고 방진을 이루고, 그러고는 한쪽에서 공성 사다리를 타고 참호로 내려갔다가 같은 수단으로 다시 다른 쪽에서 올라오고, 바구니로 만든 방책을 쓰러뜨리고, 더없이 용맹스럽게 처신하는 것이었다. 그러더니 포대 위의 거대한 대포들의 내용물을 거대하게 확대한 대걸레 같은 도구로 쑤셔 넣는 일이 있었고, 발사하기 전에 어찌나 준비가 요란했던지, 또 막상 발사할 때는 어찌나 무시무시한 소리가 났던지, 공중이 부인들의 비명으로 울려 퍼졌다. 어린 와들 양들은 어찌나 겁에 질렸던지, 트런들 씨가 실제로 마차 안에서 한 명을 부축해 주어야 했고, 그동안 스노드그래스 씨가 다른 한 명을 떠받쳤다. 그리고 와들 씨의 누이는 어찌나 끔찍한 신경성 경악에 시달렸던지, 터프먼 씨는 그녀가 도무지 쓰러지지 않게 하려면 그녀의 허리에 팔을 두르는 것이 절대로 필요하다는 것을 깨달았다. 모두가 흥분했으나, 뚱뚱한 소년만은 예외였으니, 그는 대포의 포효가 마치 평소의 자장가라도 되는 듯 곤히 잠들어 있었다.\n\n\"조, 조!\" 성채가 함락되고 포위한 자들과 포위당한 자들이 식사를 하려고 자리에 앉았을 때 뚱뚱한 신사가 말했다. \"저 녀석 망할, 또 잠들어 버렸군. 선생, 미안하지만 저 녀석 좀 꼬집어 주시겠소—다리를요, 괜찮으시다면. 다른 건 저 녀석을 깨우질 못한다오—고맙소이다. 조, 바구니를 풀어라.\"\n\n윈클 씨의 엄지와 검지 사이에 다리의 한 부분이 눌리는 바람에 효과적으로 깨어난 뚱뚱한 소년은 다시 한번 마부석에서 굴러 내려와, 앞서의 굼뜬 모습으로는 예상할 수 없었을 만큼 빠르게 바구니 푸는 일에 착수했다.\n\n\"이제 바싹 붙어 앉아야겠소.\" 뚱뚱한 신사가 말했다. 숙녀들의 소매를 짓누른다는 둥 갖가지 농담이 오가고, 숙녀들이 신사들 무릎에 앉아야 한다는 여러 익살스러운 제안에 잔뜩 얼굴이 붉어진 끝에, 일행 전체가 사륜마차 안에 차곡차곡 자리 잡았다. 그러고 나서 뚱뚱한 신사는 뚱뚱한 소년에게서 (이를 위해 소년은 뒤쪽에 올라타 있었다) 물건들을 받아 마차 안으로 건네주기 시작했다.\n\n\"자, 조, 칼하고 포크.\" 칼과 포크가 건네져, 안에 있는 숙녀와 신사들, 그리고 마부석의 윈클 씨가 저마다 그 유용한 도구들을 받았다.\n\n\"접시다, 조, 접시.\" 그릇을 나누어 주는 데도 비슷한 절차가 쓰였다.\n\n\"자, 조, 닭고기. 저 녀석 망할, 또 잠들어 버렸군. 조! 조!\" (막대기로 머리를 몇 차례 톡톡 두드리자, 뚱뚱한 소년이 다소 힘겹게 무기력에서 깨어났다.) \"자, 먹을거리를 건네라.\"\n\n마지막 단어의 울림에는 그 기름진 소년을 일깨우는 무언가가 있었다. 그는 벌떡 일어났고, 산더미 같은 뺨 뒤에서 반짝이는 납빛 눈동자가 그가 바구니에서 음식을 풀어낼 때 그것을 향해 끔찍하게 곁눈질을 했다.\n\n\"자, 어서 서둘러라.\" 와들 씨가 말했다. 뚱뚱한 소년이 거세한 수탉 한 마리 위로 애틋하게 몸을 숙인 채 도무지 그것과 헤어지지 못하는 듯했기 때문이다. 소년은 깊이 한숨을 쉬고는, 그 통통한 살집에 뜨거운 시선을 던지더니, 마지못해 그것을 주인에게 넘겨주었다.\n\n\"옳지—눈치껏 빨리 해라. 자 이제 혓바닥 고기—이제 비둘기 파이. 그 송아지 고기랑 햄 조심하고—바닷가재 조심하고—샐러드는 보자기에서 꺼내고—드레싱은 나한테 다오.\" 와들 씨가 묘사된 여러 가지 음식을 건네주며, 그리고 끝도 없이 많은 접시를 모두의 손에, 또 모두의 무릎에 올려놓으며 입에서 쏟아 낸 다급한 명령들이 이러했다.\n\n\"자, 이거 아주 근사하지 않소?\" 파괴의 작업이 시작되자 그 유쾌한 인물이 물었다.\n\n\"근사하군요!\" 마부석에서 닭고기를 썰고 있던 윈클 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"포도주 한잔 하시겠소?\"\n\n\"더없이 기쁘게 마시지요.\"\n\n\"거기서는 아예 한 병 통째로 가지시는 게 낫지 않겠소?\"\n\n\"참으로 친절하시군요.\"\n\n\"조!\"\n\n\"예, 선생.\" (이번에는 잠들어 있지 않았으니, 마침 송아지 고기 파이 한 조각을 슬쩍 빼내는 데 성공한 참이었기 때문이다.)\n\n\"마부석의 신사분께 포도주 한 병. 만나서 반갑소이다, 선생.\"\n\n\"고맙습니다.\" 윈클 씨는 잔을 비우고 병을 자기 옆 마부석 위에 놓았다.\n\n\"제게 그 영광을 허락해 주시겠습니까, 선생?\" 트런들 씨가 윈클 씨에게 말했다.\n\n\"기꺼이 그러지요.\" 윈클 씨가 트런들 씨에게 대답했고, 그러고 나서 두 신사가 함께 술을 들었으며, 그 뒤에는 숙녀들까지 모두 돌아가며 한 잔씩 들었다.\n\n\"가여운 에밀리가 낯선 신사하고 어찌나 시시덕거리는지.\" 노처녀 고모가 진정한 노처녀 고모다운 시샘을 담아 오라버니 와들 씨에게 속삭였다.\n\n\"오! 글쎄다.\" 유쾌한 노신사가 말했다. \"지극히 자연스러운 일이지 싶다—별난 일도 아니고. 픽윅 씨, 포도주 좀 드시겠소, 선생?\" 비둘기 파이 속을 깊이 탐구하고 있던 픽윅 씨가 선뜻 응했다.\n\n\"에밀리, 얘야.\" 노처녀 고모가 거들먹거리는 태도로 말했다. \"그렇게 큰 소리로 떠들지 말렴, 아가.\"\n\n\"어머, 고모도!\"\n\n\"고모하고 저 자그마한 노신사는 자기들끼리만 다 차지하고 싶은 모양이야, 내 보기엔.\" 이저벨라 와들 양이 동생 에밀리에게 속삭였다. 젊은 숙녀들은 아주 호탕하게 웃었고, 나이 든 쪽은 상냥하게 보이려 애썼으나 그러지 못했다.\n\n\"젊은 처녀들은 원기가 어찌나 넘치는지.\" 와들 양이 부드러운 동정의 기색을 띠고 터프먼 씨에게 말했으니, 마치 혈기란 금지된 밀수품이고 허가 없이 그것을 지니는 일은 중대한 범죄이자 비행이라도 되는 듯했다.\n\n\"오, 그렇지요.\" 터프먼 씨가 자기에게 기대된 종류의 대답을 정확히 하지는 못한 채 응수했다. \"참으로 즐거운 일이지요.\"\n\n\"흠!\" 와들 양이 다소 미심쩍은 듯 말했다.\n\n\"제가 좀 따라 드려도 될까요?\" 터프먼 씨가 더없이 부드러운 태도로, 한 손으로 매혹적인 레이철의 손목을 건드리고 다른 손으로는 살며시 병을 들어 올리며 말했다. \"허락해 주시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"어머나, 선생!\" 터프먼 씨는 더없이 인상적인 표정을 지었고, 레이철은 혹시 대포가 더 발사되는 게 아닐까 두렵다는 마음을 내비쳤으니, 그렇게 되면 당연히 그녀는 다시 부축을 받아야 할 터였다.\n\n\"내 사랑스러운 조카들이 예쁘다고 생각하세요?\" 그 다정한 고모가 터프먼 씨에게 속삭였다.\n\n\"그렇게 생각하겠지요, 그 애들의 고모님께서 여기 안 계신다면 말입니다.\" 발 빠른 픽윅 회원이 정열적인 눈길을 던지며 대답했다.\n\n\"어머, 짓궂으셔라—하지만 정말이지, 저 애들 안색이 조금만 더 좋다면, 촛불 아래서는—제법 예쁜 처녀들이라고 생각하지 않으세요?\"\n\n\"그렇지요. 그러리라 생각합니다.\" 터프먼 씨가 무심한 태도로 말했다.\n\n\"어머, 짓궂은 양반—무슨 말씀을 하려던 건지 다 알아요.\"\n\n\"무엇을요?\" 터프먼 씨가 물었는데, 그는 사실 무슨 말이든 할 작정을 정확히 정해 둔 것은 아니었다.\n\n\"이저벨이 구부정하다고 말씀하시려던 거죠—다 알아요—당신네 남자들은 어찌나 관찰력이 좋은지. 그래요, 그 애가 그렇긴 해요. 부정할 수 없죠. 그리고 확실히, 처녀를 못나 보이게 하는 게 무엇보다 하나 있다면 그건 바로 구부정한 자세예요. 저는 그 애한테 자주 말하지요, 좀 더 나이가 들면 아주 흉해 보일 거라고요. 어머, 정말 짓궂은 양반이셔!\"\n\n터프먼 씨는 그토록 싼값에 그런 평판을 얻는 데 이의가 없었다. 그래서 그는 무척이나 사정을 다 안다는 듯한 표정을 짓고는 알 수 없게 미소 지었다.\n\n\"어쩌면 그렇게 비꼬는 미소를 짓는지.\" 흠모에 찬 레이철이 말했다. \"정말이지 당신이 두려울 지경이에요.\"\n\n\"제가 두렵다고요!\"\n\n\"오, 당신은 제게 아무것도 감추지 못해요—저 미소가 무슨 뜻인지 아주 잘 알거든요.\"\n\n\"무슨 뜻인데요?\" 터프먼 씨가 말했는데, 정작 그 자신은 조금도 짐작이 가지 않았다.\n\n\"당신 뜻은요.\" 상냥한 고모가 목소리를 한층 더 낮추며 말했다. \"이저벨라의 구부정함이 에밀리의 그 대담함만큼 나쁘진 않다는 거죠. 글쎄, 그 애는 정말 대담하다니까요! 그게 가끔 저를 얼마나 비참하게 만드는지 당신은 모르실 거예요—정말 몇 시간이고 그 일로 울곤 한답니다—내 사랑하는 오라버니는 너무도 선량하고 의심할 줄을 몰라서, 그걸 도무지 보지 못하시지요. 만약 보셨더라면, 분명코 가슴이 찢어지셨을 거예요. 그게 그저 태도일 뿐이라고 생각할 수 있다면 좋으련만—제발 그렇기를 바라요—\" (여기서 그 다정한 친척은 깊은 한숨을 내쉬며 낙담한 듯 고개를 가로저었다.)\n\n\"고모가 분명 우리 얘기를 하고 있어.\" 에밀리 와들 양이 언니에게 속삭였다. \"틀림없어—저렇게 심술궂은 표정을 짓는 걸 보면.\"\n\n\"그럴까?\" 이저벨라가 대답했다. \"흠! 고모, 고모도!\"\n\n\"왜 그러니, 내 사랑?\"\n\n\"고모가 감기 드실까 봐 너무 걱정돼요—비단 손수건을 그 사랑스러운 늙은 머리에 둘러매세요—정말로 몸조심하셔야 해요—나이를 생각하셔야죠!\"\n\n이 보복이 아무리 받아 마땅한 것이었다 해도, 그것은 동원할 수 있는 가장 앙심에 찬 보복이었다. 와들 씨가 무심코 조를 힘주어 부르는 바람에 화제를 바꾸지 않았더라면, 고모의 분노가 어떤 형태의 대꾸로 터져 나왔을지는 짐작도 할 수 없는 일이었다.\n\n\"저 녀석 망할.\" 노신사가 말했다. \"또 잠들어 버렸군.\"\n\n\"참 별난 소년이로군요.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"저 애는 늘 저렇게 자나요?\"\n\n\"잔다고요!\" 노신사가 말했다. \"저 녀석은 늘 잠들어 있지요. 심부름도 곤히 잠든 채 다니고, 식탁에서 시중들면서 코를 곤다니까요.\"\n\n\"참으로 이상하군요!\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"아! 이상하다마다요.\" 노신사가 응수했다. \"나는 저 녀석이 자랑스럽소—무슨 일이 있어도 저 녀석을 내놓지 않을 거요—저건 자연이 만든 진귀한 물건이지요! 자, 조—조—이것들 좀 치우고, 또 한 병 따라—알겠느냐?\"\n\n뚱뚱한 소년은 일어나서 눈을 뜨고, 지난번 잠들었을 때 마침 씹고 있던 거대한 파이 조각을 꿀꺽 삼키고는, 천천히 주인의 명령에 따랐다—접시들을 치워 바구니에 담으면서, 잔치의 남은 음식 위로 나른하게 게걸스러운 눈길을 흘리면서. 새 병이 나와 순식간에 비워졌고, 바구니는 원래 자리에 단단히 묶였으며, 뚱뚱한 소년은 다시 한번 마부석에 올랐고, 안경과 휴대용 안경이 다시 고쳐 쓰였으며, 군대의 기동이 다시 시작되었다. 대포가 요란하게 쉬익거리며 쾅쾅 터졌고, 부인들이 화들짝 놀랐다—그러고 나서 모두를 흡족하게 하며 지뢰가 터졌고—지뢰가 터지고 나자 군대와 일행도 그 본을 따라, 자리를 떴다.\n\n\"자, 잊지 마시오.\" 노신사가, 행사가 끝나 가는 동안 틈틈이 이어 온 대화의 마무리에서 픽윅 씨와 악수를 나누며 말했다. \"내일 여러분 모두를 뵙기로 한 거요.\"\n\n\"그야 물론이지요.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"주소는 받아 두셨소?\"\n\n\"매너 농장, 딩글리 델.\" 픽윅 씨가 자기 수첩을 들여다보며 말했다.\n\n\"바로 그거요.\" 노신사가 말했다. \"잊지 마시오, 일주일은 안 채우면 못 보내드리오. 그리고 볼 만한 것은 죄다 보여 드리겠다고 약속하지요. 시골 생활을 맛보러 내려오신 거라면, 내게로 오시오, 내 그걸 실컷 맛보게 해 드리리다. 조—저 녀석 망할, 또 잠들어 버렸군—조, 톰이 말 매는 것 좀 도와라.\"\n\n말들이 매여졌고—마부가 올라탔고—뚱뚱한 소년이 그 옆으로 기어 올라갔고—작별 인사가 오갔고—마차가 덜커덩거리며 떠나갔다. 픽윅 회원들이 마차를 마지막으로 한 번 더 보려고 돌아섰을 때, 지는 해가 그들을 환대해 준 이들의 얼굴에 풍요로운 노을빛을 드리웠고, 그 빛은 뚱뚱한 소년의 모습 위로도 떨어졌다. 그의 머리는 가슴팍에 푹 파묻혀 있었으니, 그는 또다시 잠들어 있었다.\n\n하늘은 밝고 쾌청했으며, 공기는 향긋했고, 주위의 모든 사물은 아름다운 모습을 띠고 있었다. 픽윅 씨는 로체스터 다리의 난간에 몸을 기댄 채 자연을 바라보며 아침 식사를 기다리고 있었다. 그 광경은 실로 그것을 바라보는 이보다 훨씬 사색에 덜 잠긴 마음이라도 능히 매료시킬 만한 것이었다.\n\n바라보는 이의 왼편에는 여러 군데가 무너진 폐허의 성벽이 놓여 있었고, 어떤 곳에서는 거칠고 육중한 덩어리째 아래쪽의 좁은 모래톱 위로 비스듬히 드리워져 있었다. 들쭉날쭉 뾰족한 돌들 위에는 거대한 해초 무더기가 매달려 바람이 불 때마다 떨었고, 푸른 담쟁이덩굴은 어둡고 황폐한 흉벽 둘레를 구슬프게 휘감고 있었다. 그 뒤로는 고색창연한 성이 솟아 있었으니, 탑들은 지붕이 사라졌고 육중한 벽은 허물어져 가고 있었으나, 칠백 년 전 무기 부딪치는 소리로 울리고 잔치와 흥청거림의 소음으로 메아리치던 그 옛 위세와 힘을 자랑스레 우리에게 들려주고 있었다. 양편으로는 옥수수밭과 목초지로 뒤덮인 메드웨이 강의 강둑이 군데군데 풍차나 멀리 교회를 곁들인 채 눈길이 닿는 데까지 뻗어 있었고, 풍요롭고 변화무쌍한 풍경을 펼쳐 보였으니, 그것은 아침 햇살 속에 얇고 채 여물지 않은 구름들이 스쳐 가며 그 위로 재빠르게 지나가는 변화하는 그림자들로 한층 더 아름다워졌다. 강물은 맑고 푸른 하늘빛을 비추며 소리 없이 흐르는 동안 반짝이고 빛났고, 어부들의 노는 맑고 투명한 소리를 내며 물속에 잠겼으니, 그들의 묵직하면서도 그림 같은 배들은 천천히 강을 따라 미끄러져 내려가고 있었다.\n\n픽윅 씨는 눈앞의 사물들이 이끌어 낸 그 흐뭇한 몽상에서, 깊은 한숨 소리와 어깨를 두드리는 손길에 정신이 들었다. 그는 돌아보았다. 그 침울한 사내가 그의 곁에 서 있었다.\n\n\"경치를 감상하시는 중이오?\" 침울한 사내가 물었다.\n\n\"그렇소.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"그리고 이렇게 일찍 일어난 걸 스스로 축하하고 계셨겠지요?\"\n\n픽윅 씨는 그렇다는 듯 고개를 끄덕였다.\n\n\"아! 사람들은 해가 온전한 광휘를 떨치는 모습을 보려면 일찍 일어나야 하지요. 그 밝음은 하루 종일 가는 일이 드무니까요. 하루의 아침과 인생의 아침은 너무도 닮았소이다.\"\n\n\"옳은 말씀이오.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"얼마나 흔한 말이오.\" 침울한 사내가 이어 말했다. \"'아침이 너무 화창해서 오래갈 리 없다'는 말 말이오. 그것을 우리네 나날의 삶에 얼마나 잘 들어맞게 쓸 수 있겠소. 하느님! 어린 시절을 되돌릴 수만 있다면, 아니 그 시절을 영영 잊을 수만 있다면 나는 무엇인들 내놓지 못하겠소!\"\n\n\"많은 고초를 겪으셨군요, 선생.\" 픽윅 씨가 측은한 마음으로 말했다.\n\n\"그렇소.\" 침울한 사내가 황급히 말했다. \"그렇소. 지금 나를 보는 이들이 가능하다고 믿을 수 있는 것보다 훨씬 더 많이.\" 그는 잠시 말을 멈추었다가, 이윽고 불쑥 내뱉었다.\n\n\"이런 아침에, 물에 빠져 죽는 것이 곧 행복이요 평화이리라는 생각이 든 적이 있소?\"\n\n\"맙소사, 천만에!\" 픽윅 씨가 대답하며 난간에서 조금 비켜섰으니, 침울한 사내가 실험 삼아 자기를 떠밀어 떨어뜨릴 수도 있겠다는 가능성이 자못 강하게 떠올랐던 것이다.\n\n\"나는 종종 그런 생각을 했소.\" 침울한 사내가 그 동작을 알아채지 못한 채 말했다. \"고요하고 차가운 물이 내게는 안식과 휴식으로의 초대를 속삭이는 것만 같았소. 한번 뛰어들고, 첨벙 소리가 나고, 잠깐 발버둥치면, 한순간 소용돌이가 일다가 차츰 잔잔한 물결로 가라앉지요. 물은 당신의 머리 위로 닫히고, 세상은 당신의 고통과 불운 위로 영영 닫히는 것이오.\" 침울한 사내의 움푹 꺼진 눈은 그가 말하는 동안 환히 번득였으나, 그 순간의 흥분은 이내 가라앉았다. 그러더니 그는 차분하게 몸을 돌리며 말했다.\n\n\"자, 그 이야기는 그만하면 됐소. 다른 일로 당신을 보고 싶었던 거요. 당신은 그제 밤에 내게 그 원고를 읽어 보라고 청했고, 내가 읽는 동안 주의 깊게 들어 주었소.\"\n\n\"그랬지요.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다. \"그리고 분명히 이런 생각이 들었는데—\"\n\n\"나는 의견을 청하지 않았소.\" 침울한 사내가 그의 말을 가로막으며 말했다. \"그리고 의견 따위는 바라지도 않소. 당신은 즐거움과 배움을 위해 여행하고 있소. 만일 내가 당신에게 어떤 진기한 원고를 보낸다고 칩시다—잘 들으시오, 황당하거나 있을 법하지 않아서 진기한 게 아니라, 실제 삶의 로맨스에서 떼어 낸 한 장이기에 진기한 것이오—그것을 당신이 그토록 자주 입에 올린 그 클럽에 전해 주겠소?\"\n\n\"물론이오.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다. \"당신이 원하신다면 말이오. 그리고 그것은 클럽의 회보에 기록될 것이오.\"\n\n\"그것을 받게 될 거요.\" 침울한 사내가 대답했다. \"당신 주소를 말해 주시오.\" 그리하여 픽윅 씨가 그들의 예정된 노정을 일러 주자, 침울한 사내는 기름때 묻은 수첩에 그것을 꼼꼼히 적어 두고는, 함께 아침 식사를 하자는 픽윅 씨의 간곡한 권유를 뿌리치고 그를 여관에 남겨 둔 채 천천히 걸어가 버렸다.\n\n픽윅 씨는 세 동행이 이미 일어나 그가 오기를 기다리며 아침 식사를 시작하려는 참임을 알았으니, 식사는 먹음직스럽게 차려져 있었다. 그들은 식탁에 둘러앉았고, 구운 햄과 달걀, 차, 커피, 그 밖의 여러 가지가 빠른 속도로 사라지기 시작했으니, 그것은 음식의 훌륭함과 먹는 이들의 식욕을 동시에 증언하는 것이었다.\n\n\"자, 매너 농장 말인데.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"어떻게 가는 게 좋겠소?\"\n\n\"아마 종업원에게 물어보는 게 낫겠소.\" 터프먼 씨가 말했다. 그래서 종업원이 불려 왔다.\n\n\"딩글리 델 말씀이군요, 여러분—십오 마일입니다, 여러분—샛길이지요—역마차로 하시겠습니까, 손님?\"\n\n\"역마차는 둘밖에 못 타지 않소.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"맞습니다, 손님—실례했습니다, 손님.—아주 멋진 사륜 경마차가 있습니다, 손님—뒤에 두 분 자리가 있고요—앞에 모는 분 자리가 하나 있습죠—아! 죄송합니다, 손님—그건 세 분밖에 못 탑니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 어쩐다?\" 스노드그래스 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"혹시 한 분이 말을 타고 싶으실지요, 손님?\" 종업원이 윈클 씨를 바라보며 제안했다. \"아주 좋은 승용마가 있습니다, 손님—워들 씨 댁 일꾼 누구든 로체스터에 오는 길에 데려가시면 되돌려 보내 주실 겁니다, 손님.\"\n\n\"바로 그거요.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"윈클, 말을 타고 가시겠소?\"\n\n그런데 윈클 씨는 자기 마음 가장 깊은 구석에서 자신의 승마 솜씨에 관해 상당한 불안을 품고 있었다. 그러나 그것을 무슨 일이 있어도 남이 눈치채게 할 수는 없었기에, 그는 곧장 대단히 대담하게 대답했다. \"물론이오. 무엇보다도 그게 좋겠소.\"\n\n윈클 씨는 자기 운명을 향해 돌진하고 있었다. 달리 길이 없었다.\n\n\"열한 시까지 문 앞에 대 주시오.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"알겠습니다, 손님.\" 종업원이 대답했다.\n\n종업원은 물러갔고, 아침 식사는 끝났다. 여행자들은 다가오는 원정에 가지고 갈 갈아입을 옷을 챙기러 각자의 침실로 올라갔다.\n\n픽윅 씨는 사전 준비를 마치고 커피실의 차양 너머로 거리의 행인들을 내다보고 있었는데, 그때 종업원이 들어와 마차가 준비되었다고 알렸다. 이 알림은 곧 앞서 말한 커피실 차양 앞에 마차 자체가 나타남으로써 확인되었다.\n\n그것은 네 바퀴 달린 진기한 작은 초록색 상자 같은 것이었으니, 뒤에는 두 사람이 탈 술통 보관함 같은 낮은 자리가 있고, 앞에는 한 사람이 앉을 높이 솟은 자리가 있었으며, 뼈대가 매우 균형 잡힌 거대한 갈색 말이 끌고 있었다. 마부 하나가 곁에 서서 또 한 마리의 거대한 말—마차에 매인 짐승과 가까운 친척인 듯한—의 고삐를 쥐고 있었는데, 그 말은 윈클 씨를 위해 안장을 얹은 채 준비되어 있었다.\n\n\"이거 야단났군!\" 외투들을 싣는 동안 보도에 서 있던 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"야단났어! 누가 마차를 몬담? 그건 미처 생각도 못 했는데.\"\n\n\"아! 당연히 당신이지요.\" 터프먼 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"당연하지요.\" 스노드그래스 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"내가!\" 픽윅 씨가 소리쳤다.\n\n\"전혀 걱정 마십시오, 손님.\" 마부가 끼어들었다. \"이놈은 얌전하기로 보장합니다, 손님. 갓난애도 몰 수 있을 정도입죠.\"\n\n\"놀라서 날뛰거나 하진 않겠지, 그렇지?\" 픽윅 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"날뛴다고요, 손님?—꼬리가 다 타 버린 원숭이를 한 수레 가득 실은 짐마차와 마주친대도 이놈은 꿈쩍도 안 할 겁니다.\"\n\n이 마지막 보증은 반박의 여지가 없었다. 터프먼 씨와 스노드그래스 씨는 보관함 자리에 올라탔고, 픽윅 씨는 자기 자리로 올라가 그 밑에 그 용도로 설치된 천을 깐 발판 위에 발을 올려놓았다.\n\n\"자, 반들이 빌리엄.\" 마부가 부마부에게 말했다. \"이 신사분께 고삐를 드려라.\"\n\n'반들이 빌리엄'은—아마도 매끈한 머리카락과 기름진 얼굴 때문에 그렇게 불렸을 텐데—고삐를 픽윅 씨의 왼손에 쥐여 주었고, 상급 마부는 채찍을 그의 오른손에 찔러 넣었다.\n\n\"워어!\" 키 큰 네발짐승이 커피실 창문 쪽으로 뒷걸음질 치려는 뚜렷한 기색을 보이자 픽윅 씨가 소리쳤다.\n\n\"워어!\" 보관함 자리에서 터프먼 씨와 스노드그래스 씨가 따라 외쳤다.\n\n\"그저 장난기일 뿐입니다, 손님.\" 우두머리 마부가 격려하듯 말했다. \"그냥 붙잡아라, 빌리엄.\" 부마부가 짐승의 거센 기세를 억눌렀고, 우두머리는 윈클 씨가 말에 오르는 것을 거들려고 달려갔다.\n\n\"반대편입니다, 손님, 죄송하지만요.\"\n\n\"저런, 저 신사분이 엉뚱한 쪽으로 올라타려고 하잖아.\" 히죽거리는 역마차 마부 소년이 이루 말할 수 없이 흡족해하는 종업원에게 속삭였다.\n\n이렇게 안내받은 윈클 씨는 마치 일급 군함의 뱃전을 기어오르듯 거의 그만큼이나 애를 먹으며 안장 위로 기어올랐다.\n\n\"준비됐소?\" 픽윅 씨가 속으로는 모든 게 다 잘못됐다는 예감을 품은 채 물었다.\n\n\"준비됐소.\" 윈클 씨가 힘없이 대답했다.\n\n\"출발하시죠.\" 마부가 외쳤다.—\"단단히 잡으십시오, 손님.\" 그러자 마차와 승용마는 출발했으니, 한쪽 마차석에는 픽윅 씨가, 다른 한쪽 말 등에는 윈클 씨가 탄 채였고, 여관 마당 전체가 기뻐하고 즐거워했다.\n\n\"왜 저놈이 옆으로 가는 거요?\" 보관함 자리의 스노드그래스 씨가 안장 위의 윈클 씨에게 말했다.\n\n\"나도 모르겠소.\" 윈클 씨가 대답했다. 그의 말은 더없이 알 수 없는 모양으로 거리를 따라 옆으로 떠밀려 가고 있었으니—옆구리를 앞세우고, 머리는 길 한쪽을 향하고 꼬리는 다른 쪽을 향한 채였다.\n\n픽윅 씨에게는 이것이며 다른 어떤 것을 살필 여유가 없었으니, 그의 온 정신이 마차에 매인 짐승을 다루는 데 집중되어 있었던 까닭이다. 그 짐승은 갖가지 기묘한 짓을 보였는데, 구경꾼에게는 자못 흥미로웠으나 그 뒤에 앉은 누구에게도 그만큼 즐겁지는 결코 않았다. 매우 불쾌하고 거북한 모양새로 끊임없이 머리를 홱홱 쳐들고, 픽윅 씨가 고삐를 쥐고 있기가 몹시 어려울 만큼 고삐를 잡아당기는 것에 더하여, 이놈은 이따금 느닷없이 길가로 휙 내달았다가는 우뚝 멈춰 서고, 그러고는 도무지 제어할 길 없는 속도로 몇 분간 앞으로 돌진하는 별난 버릇이 있었다.\n\n\"대체 이게 무슨 짓일까?\" 말이 이런 동작을 스무 번째로 해 보였을 때 스노드그래스 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"나도 모르겠소.\" 터프먼 씨가 대답했다. \"놀라서 날뛰는 것처럼 보이긴 하는데, 안 그렇소?\" 스노드그래스 씨가 대답하려는데, 픽윅 씨의 외침에 말이 끊겼다.\n\n\"워!\" 그 신사가 말했다. \"내 채찍을 떨어뜨렸소.\"\n\n\"윈클.\" 스노드그래스 씨가 말했으니, 그때 그 기수는 모자가 귀까지 내려온 채, 격렬한 운동 탓에 온몸이 산산조각 날 듯 사방으로 흔들리며 키 큰 말을 타고 종종걸음으로 다가오고 있었다. \"착한 양반, 채찍 좀 주워 주시오.\" 윈클 씨는 얼굴이 시퍼레지도록 키 큰 말의 고삐를 잡아당겼고, 마침내 말을 세우는 데 성공하자 내려서 채찍을 픽윅 씨에게 건넨 뒤, 고삐를 움켜쥐고 다시 올라탈 채비를 했다.\n\n그런데 그 키 큰 말이 천성의 장난기에서 윈클 씨와 더불어 죄 없는 오락거리를 좀 즐기고 싶었던 것인지, 아니면 기수를 태우든 안 태우든 제 마음에 흡족하게 여정을 마칠 수 있겠다는 생각이 들었던 것인지, 그것은 물론 우리로서는 명확하고 분명한 결론에 이를 수 없는 대목이다. 그 짐승이 어떤 동기로 움직였든 간에, 윈클 씨가 고삐에 손을 대자마자 말이 고삐를 머리 너머로 미끄러뜨려 벗기고는 고삐가 다 풀릴 만큼 뒤로 휙 물러난 것만은 확실하다.\n\n\"가엾은 녀석.\" 윈클 씨가 달래듯 말했다. \"가엾은 녀석—착한 늙은 말아.\" 그 '가엾은 녀석'은 아첨에도 끄떡없었다. 윈클 씨가 가까이 다가가려 할수록 말은 더욱 옆으로 비켜났고, 온갖 어르고 구슬리는 짓에도 불구하고, 윈클 씨와 말은 십 분 동안이나 서로의 주위를 빙빙 맴돌았으니, 그 시간이 끝났을 때 둘은 처음 시작했을 때와 정확히 같은 거리만큼 떨어져 있었다—어떤 상황에서도 만족스러운 일이라 할 수 없겠으나, 도움을 구할 데라곤 없는 외딴길에서는 특히 그러했다.\n\n\"이를 어쩐다?\" 그 술래잡기가 꽤 오래 이어진 끝에 윈클 씨가 소리쳤다. \"이를 어쩌면 좋단 말이오? 도무지 올라탈 수가 없소.\"\n\n\"통행료 징수소에 다다를 때까지 끌고 가는 게 낫겠소.\" 마차에서 픽윅 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"하지만 안 오려고 하는데!\" 윈클 씨가 고함쳤다. \"제발 와서 좀 붙잡아 주시오.\"\n\n픽윅 씨는 친절과 인정의 화신 그 자체였다. 그는 고삐를 말 등에 던져 놓고 자기 자리에서 내려와, 길로 무엇이든 지나갈까 봐 마차를 조심스레 울타리 쪽으로 끌어다 놓고는, 터프먼 씨와 스노드그래스 씨를 마차에 남겨 둔 채 곤경에 빠진 동행을 도우러 되돌아갔다.\n\n말은 픽윅 씨가 마차 채찍을 손에 들고 자기 쪽으로 다가오는 것을 보자마자, 조금 전까지 부리던 회전 운동을 그만두고 어찌나 단호한 후진 운동으로 바꾸었던지, 그 바람에 아직 고삐 끝을 쥐고 있던 윈클 씨는 그들이 막 떠나온 방향으로 빠른 걸음보다 다소 빠른 속도로 단숨에 끌려갔다. 픽윅 씨가 그를 도우러 달려갔으나, 픽윅 씨가 앞으로 빨리 달리면 달릴수록 말은 더 빨리 뒤로 달렸다. 발이 크게 긁히는 소리가 나고 먼지가 일었으며, 마침내 팔이 어깻죽지에서 거의 빠져나갈 지경이 된 윈클 씨는 결국 손을 놓아 버렸다. 말은 멈춰 서서 멍하니 바라보다가 고개를 흔들고는 돌아서서, 윈클 씨와 픽윅 씨가 망연자실한 얼굴로 서로를 바라보게 내버려 둔 채 조용히 종종걸음으로 로체스터를 향해 집으로 돌아가 버렸다. 조금 떨어진 곳에서 덜커덕거리는 소리가 그들의 주의를 끌었다. 그들은 고개를 들었다.\n\n\"이거 야단났군!\" 괴로워하는 픽윅 씨가 외쳤다. \"저기 다른 말이 달아나고 있어!\"\n\n너무도 사실이었다. 그 짐승은 소리에 놀랐고, 고삐는 등 위에 던져져 있었다. 그 결과는 짐작이 갈 것이다. 말은 사륜 경마차를 뒤에 매단 채, 그리고 그 사륜 경마차에 탄 터프먼 씨와 스노드그래스 씨를 매단 채 내달렸다. 경주는 짧았다. 터프먼 씨가 울타리로 몸을 던졌고, 스노드그래스 씨도 그 본을 따랐으며, 말은 사륜 경마차를 나무다리에 들이받아 바퀴를 차체에서 떼어 놓고 보관함 자리를 앞자리에서 갈라놓더니, 마침내 자기가 만들어 낸 그 잔해를 바라보며 꼼짝 않고 우뚝 멈춰 섰다.\n\n엎어지지 않은 두 친구가 가장 먼저 신경 쓴 것은 불운한 동행들을 산울타리 잠자리에서 끌어내는 일이었다—이 과정에서 그들은 동행들이 옷에 난 몇 군데 찢어진 자국과 가시덤불에 긁힌 여러 상처 말고는 아무 부상도 입지 않았음을 발견하고 이루 말할 수 없는 만족을 얻었다. 다음으로 해야 할 일은 말의 마구를 푸는 것이었다. 이 복잡한 작업을 마친 뒤, 일행은 말을 가운데 두고 끌고서 천천히 앞으로 걸어갔으니, 마차는 그 운명에 내맡긴 채였다.\n\n한 시간을 걸어가자 여행자들은 길가의 자그마한 선술집에 다다랐다. 앞에는 느릅나무 두 그루와 말 여물통, 그리고 푯말이 있었고, 뒤에는 모양이 일그러진 건초 더미가 한두 개, 옆에는 채마밭이 있었으며, 사방에는 썩은 헛간과 허물어져 가는 부속 건물들이 기묘하게 뒤죽박죽 뒤엉켜 있었다. 빨간 머리의 사내 하나가 밭에서 일하고 있었는데, 픽윅 씨가 그를 향해 우렁차게 외쳤다. \"여보시오, 거기!\"\n\n빨간 머리 사내는 몸을 일으켜 한 손으로 눈을 가리고는, 픽윅 씨와 그 일행을 오래도록 무덤덤하게 빤히 바라보았다.\n\n\"여보시오, 거기!\" 픽윅 씨가 다시 외쳤다.\n\n\"여보쇼!\" 빨간 머리 사내의 대답이었다.\n\n\"딩글리 델까지 얼마나 멀지요?\"\n\n\"칠 마일은 좋이 되지.\"\n\n\"길은 괜찮소?\"\n\n\"아니, 안 괜찮어.\" 이 짤막한 대답을 내뱉은 빨간 머리 사내는 한 번 더 찬찬히 뜯어보고 나서 흡족해진 모양인지 다시 일을 시작했다. \"우리가 이 말을 여기 좀 맡겼으면 하는데.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"그래도 되겠지요, 그렇지 않소?\"\n\n\"그 말을 여기다 맡기고 싶다, 그거여?\" 빨간 머리 사내가 삽에 몸을 기댄 채 되물었다.\n\n\"물론이오.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했으니, 그는 이때쯤 말을 손에 쥔 채 채마밭 울타리까지 다가와 있었다.\n\n\"여보!\"—빨간 머리 사내가 밭에서 나오며 고함을 지르고는 말을 아주 매섭게 노려보았다—\"여보!\"\n\n키가 크고 뼈만 앙상한—위에서 아래까지 막대기처럼 곧은—여자가, 거친 푸른빛 외투를 입고, 그 허리께가 겨드랑이보다 한두 치 아래에 있는 차림으로 그 부름에 응했다.\n\n\"이 말을 여기 좀 맡길 수 있겠소, 친절한 부인?\" 터프먼 씨가 다가서며 더없이 매혹적인 어조로 말했다. 여자는 일행 전체를 아주 매섭게 노려보았고, 빨간 머리 사내가 그녀의 귀에 무어라 속삭였다.\n\n\"안 돼요.\" 여자가 잠시 생각하더니 대답했다. \"그건 안 될 성싶구먼유.\"\n\n\"안 된다고!\" 픽윅 씨가 외쳤다. \"이 부인이 대체 무엇이 안 된다는 거요?\"\n\n\"지난번에 그놈 땜에 곤욕을 치렀거든유.\" 여자가 집 안으로 들어가며 말했다. \"그놈하곤 상관하고 싶지 않어유.\"\n\n\"내 평생 겪어 본 일 중에 가장 기이한 일이로군.\" 어안이 벙벙해진 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"나, 나는 정말로 이렇게 생각하오.\" 친구들이 자기 주위로 모여들자 윈클 씨가 속삭였다. \"저 사람들은 우리가 이 말을 무슨 부정한 방법으로 손에 넣었다고 여기는 것 같소.\"\n\n\"뭐라고!\" 픽윅 씨가 분노의 폭풍에 휩싸여 외쳤다. 윈클 씨가 조심스레 자기 추측을 되풀이했다.\n\n\"여보시오, 거기 양반.\" 화가 난 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"당신은 우리가 이 말을 훔쳤다고 생각하는 거요?\"\n\n\"훔친 게 분명하지유.\" 빨간 머리 사내가 한쪽 귀에서 다른 쪽 귀까지 얼굴을 온통 흔들어 대는 히죽거림과 함께 대답했다. 그 말을 하고는 집 안으로 들어가 문을 쾅 닫아 버렸다.\n\n\"꼭 꿈만 같군.\" 픽윅 씨가 내뱉었다. \"흉측한 꿈 말이야. 떨쳐 버릴 수도 없는 끔찍한 말 한 마리를 데리고 하루 종일 걸어 다니다니!\" 의기소침해진 픽윅 회원들은 침울하게 발길을 돌렸고, 그들 모두가 더없이 노골적인 혐오를 느끼는 그 키 큰 네발짐승은 천천히 그들 발치를 뒤따라왔다.\n\n네 친구와 그들의 네발 달린 동행이 매너 농장으로 이어지는 샛길로 접어들었을 때는 늦은 오후였다. 그러나 목적지에 그토록 가까이 다다랐을 때조차, 그렇지 않았더라면 누렸을 즐거움은, 그들 행색의 괴상함과 처지의 우스꽝스러움을 떠올리는 통에 적잖이 가셔 버렸다. 찢어진 옷, 긁힌 얼굴, 먼지투성이 신발, 지친 몰골, 그리고 무엇보다도 그 말. 아, 픽윅 씨가 그 말을 얼마나 저주했던가. 그는 이따금 그 늠름한 짐승을 증오와 복수를 드러내는 눈길로 노려보았고, 한 번 이상이나 그놈의 목을 따 버릴 때 드는 비용이 얼마나 될지 어림해 보기도 했으며, 이제는 그놈을 죽여 버리거나 세상에 풀어 내쫓고 싶은 유혹이 열 배의 힘으로 그의 마음에 밀려들었다. 이런 끔찍한 상상에 잠겨 있던 그는 샛길 모퉁이에 두 사람의 모습이 갑자기 나타나는 바람에 정신이 들었다. 워들 씨와 그의 충실한 시종인 뚱보 소년이었다.\n\n\"아니, 다들 어디 있었소?\" 손님 대접을 좋아하는 노신사가 말했다. \"온종일 기다렸다오. 저런, 정말 지쳐 보이는구려. 아니! 긁힌 자국이라니! 다치진 않았겠지요—응? 아, 그 말을 들으니 정말 다행이오—참으로. 그래, 다들 굴러떨어졌다 그거요, 응? 괜찮소. 이 근방에선 흔한 사고라오. 조—이놈이 또 자고 있군!—조, 신사분들한테서 그 말을 받아다가 마구간으로 끌고 가거라.\"\n\n뚱보 소년은 그 짐승을 데리고 그들 뒤에서 무겁게 어슬렁어슬렁 걸었고, 노신사는 손님들이 적당히 전해 줄 만하다고 여긴 그날의 모험에 대해 소박한 말로 위로하면서 부엌으로 앞장서 안내했다.\n\n\"여기서 다들 말끔히 정돈해 드리고,\" 노신사가 말했다. \"그런 다음 응접실 사람들에게 소개해 드리리다. 에마, 체리 브랜디 좀 내오너라. 자, 제인, 여기 바늘과 실을. 메리, 수건과 물을. 자, 얘들아, 어서 부지런히들 움직여라.\"\n\n서너 명의 토실토실한 처녀들이 요구된 갖가지 물건들을 찾으러 잽싸게 흩어졌고, 한편 머리가 크고 얼굴이 둥근 사내 둘이 굴뚝 모퉁이의 자기 자리에서 일어났다(5월 저녁이었건만 장작불에 대한 그들의 애착은 마치 크리스마스라도 된 양 살가워 보였다). 그들은 어떤 컴컴한 구석으로 파고들더니 곧 구두약 한 병과 솔 대여섯 개를 꺼내 왔다.\n\n\"부지런히!\" 노신사가 다시 말했으나 그 당부는 전혀 불필요했으니, 한 처녀는 체리 브랜디를 따랐고 또 한 처녀는 수건을 가져왔으며, 사내 하나는 픽윅 씨가 균형을 잃고 자빠질 뻔할 만큼 위태롭게 그의 다리를 와락 붙들고는 그의 티눈이 시뻘겋게 달아오를 때까지 장화를 솔질해 댔고, 다른 한 사내는 묵직한 옷솔로 윈클 씨를 문질러 대며, 그러는 동안 마부들이 말을 닦아 줄 때 으레 내곤 하는 그 쉭쉭거리는 소리를 즐겨 냈다.\n\n스노드그래스 씨는 몸 씻기를 마치고 불을 등진 채 서서 체리 브랜디를 진심 어린 만족과 함께 홀짝이며 방을 둘러보았다. 그의 묘사에 따르면 그곳은 붉은 벽돌 바닥에 널찍한 굴뚝이 있는 큰 방이었고, 천장은 햄과 베이컨 옆구리 살, 그리고 줄줄이 엮은 양파로 장식되어 있었다. 벽은 사냥용 채찍 여러 개와 두세 개의 굴레, 안장 하나, 그리고 낡고 녹슨 나팔총으로 꾸며져 있었는데, 그 아래에는 그것이 '장전됨'이라고 알리는 글귀가 적혀 있었으니—그 동일한 권위에 따르면 적어도 반세기 동안 줄곧 장전된 상태였다. 엄숙하고 차분한 풍채의 낡은 팔일 시계가 한쪽 구석에서 진중하게 똑딱거렸고, 그에 못지않게 고색창연한 은시계 하나가 찬장을 장식한 여러 갈고리 중 하나에 매달려 흔들거렸다.\n\n\"준비됐소?\" 노신사가 손님들이 씻고, 꿰매고, 솔질하고, 브랜디까지 마시고 나자 물었다.\n\n\"다 됐소.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"그럼 갑시다.\" 그리하여 일행은 어두컴컴한 복도 여럿을 지났고, 에마에게서 입맞춤 한 번을 훔치려고 뒤에 처졌다가 그 대가로 여러 번 떠밀리고 할퀴는 응분의 보상을 받은 터프먼 씨까지 합류하여, 마침내 응접실 문 앞에 다다랐다.\n\n\"어서들 오시오.\" 손님 대접을 좋아하는 주인이 문을 활짝 열고 앞으로 나서서 그들을 알리며 말했다. \"여러분, 매너 농장에 오신 것을 환영하오.\"\n\n낡은 응접실에 모여 있던 손님 여럿이 픽윅 씨와 그의 친구들이 들어서자 자리에서 일어나 그들을 맞이했다. 격식을 모두 갖춘 소개의 의식이 진행되는 동안, 픽윅 씨는 자기를 둘러싼 사람들의 외양을 살피고 그들의 성격과 직업을 헤아려 볼 여유를 얻었으니—이는 다른 많은 위인들과 마찬가지로 그가 즐겨 빠져드는 버릇이었다.\n\n높다란 모자를 쓰고 빛바랜 비단 가운을 입은 아주 늙은 부인—다름 아닌 워들 씨의 어머니였다—이 벽난로 오른쪽 모서리의 상석을 차지하고 있었다. 그리고 그녀가 어려서 마땅히 걸어야 할 길로 자라났고 늙어서도 그 길에서 벗어나지 않았다는 갖가지 증거가 오래된 자수 견본들, 그에 못지않게 오래된 털실 풍경화들, 그리고 좀 더 근래의 진홍빛 비단 찻주전자 받침들의 형태로 벽을 장식하고 있었다. 이모와 두 젊은 아가씨, 그리고 워들 씨는 저마다 노부인께 열렬하고 끊임없는 정성을 바치려 다투며 그녀의 안락의자 주위로 몰려들었으니, 한 사람은 그녀의 보청 나팔을 들고, 다른 사람은 오렌지를, 또 한 사람은 냄새 맡는 병을 들었으며, 네 번째 사람은 그녀를 받쳐 주려고 놓아둔 베개들을 두드리고 매만지느라 분주했다. 맞은편에는 머리가 벗어지고, 사람 좋고 자애로운 얼굴을 한 노신사가 앉아 있었으니—딩글리 델의 목사였다. 그 옆에는 그의 아내가 앉아 있었는데, 통통하고 혈색 좋은 노부인으로, 남들을 크게 만족시킬 만한 집에서 빚은 강장주를 만드는 기술과 비법에 능통할 뿐 아니라, 때때로 자기 자신도 흡족할 만큼 그것을 맛보는 데에도 능한 듯 보였다. 한쪽 구석에서는 머리가 단단하고 립스톤 사과 같은 얼굴을 한 자그마한 사내가 살찐 노신사와 이야기를 나누고 있었다. 그리고 두세 명의 노신사와 두세 명의 노부인이 의자에 꼿꼿이 앉아 꼼짝도 않고, 픽윅 씨와 그의 동행들을 매우 뚫어지게 바라보고 있었다.\n\n\"픽윅 씨예요, 어머니.\" 워들 씨가 목청껏 외쳤다.\n\n\"아!\" 노부인이 고개를 저으며 말했다. \"안 들린다.\"\n\n\"픽윅 씨예요, 할머니!\" 두 젊은 아가씨가 함께 소리쳤다.\n\n\"아!\" 노부인이 탄식했다. \"뭐, 별 상관없다. 나 같은 늙은이한테야 저분이 관심 없겠지, 아무렴.\"\n\n\"부인, 분명히 말씀드리지만,\" 픽윅 씨가 노부인의 손을 꼭 쥐고, 어찌나 큰 소리로 말하던지 그 애씀이 자애로운 그의 얼굴에 진홍빛을 띄울 정도로—\"부인, 분명히 말씀드리지만, 부인 연세에 이토록 훌륭한 가족을 이끄시면서, 이리도 젊고 정정해 보이시는 분을 뵙는 것보다 저를 기쁘게 하는 일은 없습니다.\"\n\n\"아!\" 노부인이 잠깐 뜸을 들였다가 말했다. \"다 좋은 말씀이겠지, 아무렴. 그런데 들리질 않으니.\"\n\n\"할머니가 지금 좀 토라지셨어요.\" 이저벨라 워들 양이 나직한 목소리로 말했다. \"하지만 곧 말씀을 나누실 거예요.\"\n\n픽윅 씨는 노년의 약함을 기꺼이 받아들이겠다는 뜻으로 고개를 끄덕이고는, 그 모임의 다른 사람들과 두루 대화를 나누기 시작했다.\n\n\"여기 참 멋진 곳이로군요.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"멋지군요!\" 스노드그래스 씨, 터프먼 씨, 윈클 씨가 따라 했다.\n\n\"음, 저도 그렇게 생각합니다.\" 워들 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"켄트 전체를 통틀어 이보다 나은 땅뙈기는 없습니다, 선생님.\" 사과 같은 얼굴에 머리가 단단한 사내가 말했다. \"정말로 없지요, 선생님—확실히 없습니다, 선생님.\" 머리가 단단한 사내는 마치 누군가에게 심하게 반박당했다가 마침내 그를 이긴 양, 의기양양하게 주위를 둘러보았다.\n\n\"켄트 전체를 통틀어 이보다 나은 땅뙈기는 없습니다.\" 머리가 단단한 사내가 잠시 뜸을 들였다가 다시 말했다.\n\n\"멀린스네 풀밭만 빼면요.\" 살찐 사내가 엄숙하게 한마디 했다.\n\n\"멀린스네 풀밭이라니!\" 상대가 깊은 경멸을 담아 내뱉었다.\n\n\"아, 멀린스네 풀밭 말입니다.\" 살찐 사내가 되풀이했다.\n\n\"그건 정말 좋은 땅이지요.\" 또 다른 살찐 사내가 끼어들었다.\n\n\"맞아요, 확실히 그렇지요.\" 세 번째 살찐 사내가 말했다.\n\n\"누구나 다 아는 사실이죠.\" 살집 좋은 주인장이 말했다.\n\n머리가 단단한 사내는 미심쩍은 듯 주위를 둘러보았으나, 자기가 소수임을 깨닫고는 가엾어하는 듯한 표정을 짓고 더는 말하지 않았다.\n\n\"저 사람들 무슨 얘기를 하는 게냐?\" 노부인이 손녀 가운데 하나에게 아주 또렷한 목소리로 물었다. 귀먹은 사람들이 흔히 그렇듯, 그녀는 자기가 한 말을 다른 사람들이 들을 수도 있다는 가능성을 결코 헤아리지 않는 듯했다.\n\n\"땅 얘기예요, 할머니.\"\n\n\"땅이 어쨌다고?—무슨 일이라도 났느냐?\"\n\n\"아니에요, 아니에요. 밀러 씨가 우리 땅이 멀린스네 풀밭보다 낫다고 하셨어요.\"\n\n\"그 사람이 그걸 어떻게 안단 말이냐?\" 노부인이 분개하여 물었다. \"밀러는 잘난 척하는 멋쟁이니, 내가 그랬다고 전해도 좋다.\" 그렇게 말하면서, 노부인은 자기가 속삭임보다 큰 소리로 말했다는 것을 전혀 의식하지 못한 채 몸을 곧추세우고는, 머리가 단단한 죄인을 고기 써는 칼날 같은 눈으로 쏘아보았다.\n\n\"자, 자,\" 부산스러운 주인장이 화제를 바꾸고 싶어 하는 자연스러운 조바심으로 말했다. \"픽윅 씨, 휘스트 한 판 어떻습니까?\"\n\n\"무엇보다도 좋지요.\" 그 신사가 대답했다. \"하지만 부디 저 때문에 한 판 차리지는 마십시오.\"\n\n\"오, 분명히 말씀드리지만, 어머니가 휘스트를 무척 좋아하십니다.\" 워들 씨가 말했다. \"그렇지요, 어머니?\"\n\n이 화제에 관해서만은 다른 어떤 일보다 훨씬 덜 귀가 어두운 노부인이 그렇다고 대답했다.\n\n\"조, 조!\" 신사가 말했다. \"조—저놈 망할—오, 여기 있군. 카드 탁자를 차려라.\"\n\n굼뜬 젊은이는 더 깨우지 않아도 어찌어찌 카드 탁자 두 개를 차렸으니, 하나는 포프 존 놀이용이고 다른 하나는 휘스트용이었다. 휘스트를 칠 사람은 픽윅 씨와 노부인, 밀러 씨와 살찐 신사였다. 둘러앉아 하는 놀이에는 나머지 일행이 끼었다.\n\n휘스트 한 판은 '휘스트'라는 이름에 걸맞은 온갖 진중한 몸가짐과 침착한 태도로 진행되었으니—그것은 엄숙한 의식이었으되, 우리가 보기에는 '놀이'라는 명칭이 매우 불경하고 망신스럽게 갖다 붙여진 것이었다. 반면 둘러앉아 하는 놀이의 탁자는 어찌나 떠들썩하게 즐거웠던지 밀러 씨의 사색을 적잖이 방해했으니, 그는 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 몰두하지 못한 탓에 갖가지 중죄와 경범죄를 저지르게 되었고, 이는 살찐 신사의 노여움을 대단히 부추겼으며 그에 비례하여 노부인의 흥을 돋우었다.\n\n\"옳지!\" 죄인 밀러가 한 판이 끝날 무렵 마지막 끗수 패를 거둬들이며 의기양양하게 말했다. \"이보다 더 잘 칠 수는 없었지요, 내 자랑입니다만. 다른 패를 더 따는 건 불가능했어요!\"\n\n\"밀러가 다이아몬드를 으뜸패로 따야 했지요, 그렇지 않습니까, 선생님?\" 노부인이 말했다.\n\n픽윅 씨가 동의하며 고개를 끄덕였다.\n\n\"제가 그랬어야 했나요?\" 그 불운한 사내가 자기 짝꿍에게 미심쩍은 듯 호소하며 말했다.\n\n\"그랬어야지요, 선생.\" 살찐 신사가 무시무시한 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"정말 죄송합니다.\" 풀이 죽은 밀러가 말했다.\n\n\"그래봐야 소용없소.\" 살찐 신사가 으르렁거렸다.\n\n\"으뜸패로 둘—우리가 여덟이 됐습니다.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"또 한 판. 한 판 더 받으시겠소?\" 노부인이 물었다.\n\n\"받겠습니다.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다. \"더블, 싱글, 그리고 마무리 한 판.\"\n\n\"이런 운은 처음이오.\" 밀러 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"이런 패는 처음이오.\" 살찐 신사가 말했다.\n\n엄숙한 침묵. 픽윅 씨는 유쾌했고, 노부인은 진지했으며, 살찐 신사는 까탈스러웠고, 밀러 씨는 겁에 질려 있었다.\n\n\"또 더블이로군요.\" 노부인이 의기양양하게, 그 사실을 적어두는 양 6펜스짜리 하나와 찌그러진 반 페니짜리 하나를 촛대 밑에 놓으며 말했다.\n\n\"더블입니다, 부인.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그 사실은 잘 알고 있소, 선생.\" 살찐 신사가 날카롭게 대꾸했다.\n\n비슷한 결과로 또 한 판이 끝나자, 이번에는 불운한 밀러가 으뜸패 위반을 저질렀다. 이에 살찐 신사는 격한 흥분에 빠져들어 그 판이 끝날 때까지 그 상태를 이어갔고, 끝나자 한쪽 구석으로 물러나 한 시간 이십칠 분 동안 완벽하게 입을 다물고 있었다. 그 시간이 지나자 그는 은둔에서 벗어나, 입은 손해를 기독교인답게 용서하기로 마음을 굳힌 사람의 표정으로 픽윅 씨에게 코담배 한 줌을 권했다. 노부인의 청력은 확연히 좋아졌고, 불운한 밀러는 마치 초소 안에 든 돌고래처럼 자기 처지에 어울리지 않는 기분이었다.\n\n그동안 둘러앉아 하는 놀이는 매우 흥겹게 진행되었다. 이저벨라 워들과 트런들 씨가 '짝꿍'이 되었고, 에밀리 워들과 스노드그래스 씨도 그렇게 했으며, 심지어 터프먼 씨와 노처녀 이모도 칩과 아첨의 합자회사를 차렸다. 워들 영감은 흥겨움이 절정에 달해 있었다. 그가 판을 이끄는 솜씨가 어찌나 우스웠고 노부인들은 어찌나 딴 돈에 약빠르던지, 탁자 전체가 끊임없는 흥겨움과 웃음의 함성으로 들끓었다. 한 노부인은 늘 카드 여섯 장쯤을 물어내야 했는데, 매 판마다 어김없이 모두가 그것을 보고 웃어댔다. 그 노부인이 물어내야 하는 것에 못마땅한 표정을 짓자 사람들은 더욱 큰 소리로 웃었고, 그러자 노부인의 얼굴도 차츰 환해지더니 마침내는 누구보다도 큰 소리로 웃었다. 그러다 노처녀 이모가 '결혼' 패를 얻자 젊은 아가씨들이 다시 웃었고, 노처녀 이모는 토라질 기색이었으나, 터프먼 씨가 탁자 밑에서 자기 손을 꼭 쥐는 것을 느끼고는 그녀도 얼굴이 환해지더니, 마치 결혼이라는 것이 실은 어떤 이들이 생각하는 것만큼 그리 먼 일은 아니라는 듯 제법 아는 체하는 표정을 지었다. 이에 모두가 다시 웃었으니, 특히 워들 영감은 누구 못지않게 농담을 즐겼다. 스노드그래스 씨로 말하자면, 그저 자기 짝꿍의 귀에 시적인 감상을 속삭이기만 했는데, 이 때문에 한 노신사가 카드의 짝꿍과 평생의 짝꿍을 두고 익살스럽게 짓궂은 소리를 했고, 앞서 말한 그 노신사는 그에 대해 갖가지 곁눈질과 낄낄거림을 곁들여 몇 마디 덧붙였으니, 이는 일행을 매우 유쾌하게, 특히 그 노신사의 아내를 더없이 유쾌하게 만들었다. 그리고 윈클 씨는 도시에서는 매우 잘 알려졌으나 시골에서는 다들 알지는 못하는 농담들을 꺼냈다. 모두가 그것들을 매우 진심으로 웃으며 아주 일품이라고 하니, 윈클 씨는 크나큰 영예와 영광의 상태에 놓였다. 그리고 자애로운 목사는 흐뭇하게 바라보았으니, 탁자를 둘러싼 행복한 얼굴들이 그 선량한 노인까지도 행복하게 만들었기 때문이다. 그리고 그 흥겨움이 다소 떠들썩하긴 했어도, 그것은 입에서가 아니라 마음에서 우러난 것이었다. 그리고 결국 이것이야말로 올바른 종류의 흥겨움인 것이다.\n\n이러한 즐거운 놀이 속에 저녁은 빠르게 흘러갔다. 그리고 푸짐하지만 소박한 저녁 식사가 끝나고, 그 작은 일행이 난롯가에 둘러앉아 정다운 원을 이루자, 픽윅 씨는 평생 이토록 행복했던 적이 없으며, 또 어느 때보다도 흘러가는 이 순간을 한껏 즐기고 누리고 싶은 마음이 든다고 생각했다.\n\n\"자, 바로 이거예요.\" 노부인의 안락의자 옆에 그녀의 손을 자기 손에 꼭 쥔 채 위풍당당하게 앉아 있던 인심 좋은 주인장이 말했다. \"바로 이게 제가 좋아하는 거랍니다—제 생애 가장 행복한 순간들은 이 낡은 난롯가에서 보냈지요. 저는 이곳에 어찌나 정이 들었는지, 매일 저녁 정말로 견딜 수 없을 만큼 더워질 때까지 여기 활활 타오르는 불을 피워둔답니다. 아, 여기 제 가엾은 노모께서도 소녀 시절에 저 작은 걸상에 앉아 이 난로 앞에 계시곤 했지요. 그렇지 않습니까, 어머니?\"\n\n옛 시절의 추억과 여러 해 전의 행복이 문득 되살아날 때 부르지 않아도 눈에 솟구치는 그 눈물이, 노부인이 우울한 미소를 띠며 고개를 젓자 그녀의 얼굴을 타고 살며시 흘러내렸다.\n\n\"이 낡은 집에 관해 이야기하는 걸 부디 너그러이 보아주십시오, 픽윅 씨.\" 주인장이 잠깐 뜸을 들였다가 다시 말했다. \"저는 이곳을 끔찍이 사랑하고, 다른 곳은 알지 못하니까요—이 오래된 집과 들판은 제게 살아 있는 벗처럼 느껴진답니다. 담쟁이덩굴이 뒤덮인 우리 작은 교회도 그렇고요. 그러고 보니 여기 우리의 훌륭한 친구께서 처음 우리에게 오셨을 때 그 교회에 관해 노래를 한 곡 지으셨지요. 스노드그래스 씨, 잔에 술이 좀 있습니까?\"\n\n\"넘치도록 있습니다, 고맙습니다.\" 그 신사가 대답했으니, 그의 시적 호기심은 주인장의 마지막 말에 크게 자극받은 터였다. \"실례지만, 담쟁이 노래에 관해 말씀하셨지요.\"\n\n\"그건 저기 맞은편에 계신 우리 친구께 여쭤보셔야 합니다.\" 주인장이 고개를 끄덕여 목사를 가리키며 의미심장하게 말했다.\n\n\"선생님, 그 노래를 한 번 들려주십사 청해도 되겠습니까?\" 스노드그래스 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 사실,\" 목사가 대답했다. \"그건 아주 보잘것없는 것이랍니다. 제가 그런 걸 저지른 데 대해 댈 수 있는 유일한 변명이라면, 그때 제가 젊은 사람이었다는 것뿐이지요. 하지만 그런 대로, 원하신다면 들려드리겠습니다.\"\n\n물론 호기심 어린 웅성거림이 그 답이었다. 그러자 노신사는 아내의 갖가지 거듦을 받아가며 문제의 시를 읊기 시작했다. \"제가 이 시를 부르는 이름은,\" 그가 말했다.\n\n# THE IVY GREEN\n\n푸른 담쟁이\n\n오, 푸른 담쟁이는 어여쁜 풀이라, 오래된 폐허 위를 기어 다니네! 외롭고 차디찬 제 굴속에서, 그가 드는 끼니는 참으로 별미라네, 내 생각엔. 벽은 허물어지고 돌은 삭아야 하니, 그의 별난 입맛을 즐겁게 하려면. 세월이 빚어낸 썩어가는 먼지가, 그에게는 흥겨운 한 끼라네. 생명이라곤 보이지 않는 곳을 기어 다니니, 푸른 담쟁이는 진귀하고 오래된 풀이라네.\n\n날개도 없건만 그는 재빨리 기어들어, 굳건하고 오래된 마음을 지녔네. 얼마나 촘촘히 휘감고, 얼마나 단단히 매달리는가, 그의 벗인 거대한 참나무에게! 그리고 슬며시 땅을 따라 끌리며, 잎사귀를 부드럽게 흔드네, 죽은 자들의 무덤, 그 기름진 흙을 기쁘게 끌어안고 휘감으며. 무시무시한 죽음이 다녀간 곳을 기어 다니니, 푸른 담쟁이는 진귀하고 오래된 풀이라네.\n\n온 세월이 흘러가고 그 자취도 삭았으며, 뭇 나라가 뿔뿔이 흩어졌네. 허나 굳건하고 오래된 담쟁이는 결코 시들지 않으리, 그 정정하고 씩씩한 푸르름에서. 용감하고 오래된 풀은 그 외로운 나날 속에, 지나간 것들을 먹고 살찌리니. 사람이 세울 수 있는 가장 웅장한 건물도, 결국엔 담쟁이의 양식이 되리라. 시간이 다녀간 곳을 기어 다니니, 푸른 담쟁이는 진귀하고 오래된 풀이라네.\n\n노신사가 스노드그래스 씨가 받아 적을 수 있도록 이 시구를 두 번째로 읊는 동안, 픽윅 씨는 큰 흥미가 담긴 표정으로 그의 얼굴 윤곽을 찬찬히 살펴보았다. 노신사가 받아쓰기를 마치고, 스노드그래스 씨가 수첩을 주머니에 도로 넣자, 픽윅 씨가 말했다—\n\n\"선생님, 알게 된 지 얼마 되지도 않아 이런 말씀을 드려 죄송합니다만, 선생님 같은 분이라면 복음의 사역자로 지내오신 그 경험의 과정에서 기록해 둘 만한 장면과 사건들을 많이 보아오셨으리라 생각합니다.\"\n\n\"분명 몇 가지는 목격했지요.\" 노신사가 대답했다. \"하지만 제가 활동하는 영역이 워낙 좁다 보니, 그 사건과 인물들은 소박하고 평범한 것들이었답니다.\"\n\n\"존 에드먼즈에 관해 무언가 적어두신 게 있지 않았습니까?\" 워들 씨가 물었으니, 그는 새로 온 손님들의 교화를 위해 자기 친구의 말문을 트게 하고 싶어 하는 기색이 역력했다.\n\n노신사는 그렇다는 표시로 고개를 살짝 끄덕이고는 화제를 바꾸려 하던 참에, 픽윅 씨가 말했다—\n\n\"실례지만 선생님, 감히 여쭤도 된다면, 존 에드먼즈가 누구입니까?\"\n\n\"바로 제가 여쭤보려던 겁니다.\" 스노드그래스 씨가 열렬히 말했다.\n\"이제 꼼짝없이 걸리셨군요.\" 유쾌한 주인장이 말했다. \"조만간 이 신사분들의 호기심을 풀어주셔야 할 테니, 이 좋은 기회를 이용해 지금 당장 들려주시는 편이 낫겠습니다.\"\n\n노신사는 사람 좋게 미소 지으며 의자를 앞으로 당겼다—나머지 일행도 의자를 더 바싹 끌어당겼으니, 특히 어쩌면 귀가 좀 어두운 듯한 터프먼 씨와 노처녀 이모가 그러했다. 노부인의 보청 나팔이 알맞게 맞춰지고, (시구를 읊는 동안 잠들어 버린) 밀러 씨가 그의 옛 짝꿍인 엄숙한 살찐 사내가 탁자 밑에서 가한 경고의 꼬집음에 잠에서 깨어나자, 노신사는 더 서두 없이 다음 이야기를 시작했으니, 우리는 여기에 외람되이 이런 제목을 붙여 보았다.\n죄수의 귀향\n\n# THE CONVICT’S RETURN\n\n\"제가 처음 이 마을에 자리를 잡았을 때,\" 노신사가 말했다. \"그러니까 지금으로부터 꼭 스물다섯 해 전입니다만, 제 교구민 가운데 가장 악명 높은 인물은 에드먼즈라는 이름의 사내였는데, 이 부근의 작은 농장을 빌려 부치고 있었지요. 그는 음울하고 야만적인 마음을 지닌 못된 사내였습니다. 게으르고 방탕한 습성에, 잔인하고 흉포한 기질이었지요. 들판에서 빈둥거리며 시간을 흘려보내거나 술집에서 진탕 퍼마실 때 어울리던 게으르고 무모한 떠돌이 몇몇을 빼고는, 그에게는 단 한 명의 친구도 아는 이도 없었습니다. 많은 이가 두려워하고 누구나 혐오하는 그 사내에게 아무도 말을 걸려 하지 않았지요—에드먼즈는 모두에게 따돌림을 당했습니다.\n\n이 사내에게는 아내와 외아들이 하나 있었는데, 그 아들은 제가 처음 이곳에 왔을 때 열두 살쯤이었지요. 그 여인이 겪은 고통의 절절함, 그것을 견뎌낸 부드럽고 끈기 있는 태도, 그 아이를 길러내며 품었던 애끓는 근심—그것을 제대로 헤아릴 수 있는 이는 아무도 없을 겁니다. 박정한 추측이라면 하느님께서 용서하시기를 빕니다만, 저는 그 사내가 여러 해에 걸쳐 조직적으로 그녀의 마음을 무너뜨리려 했다고 굳게, 그리고 제 영혼으로 믿습니다. 하지만 그녀는 자식을 위해 그 모든 것을 견뎠고, 많은 이에게는 이상하게 보일지 모르나, 그 아비를 위해서도 견뎠지요. 그가 짐승 같은 자였고 그녀를 잔인하게 대했어도, 그녀는 한때 그를 사랑했으니까요. 그리고 그가 그녀에게 어떤 존재였는지를 떠올리는 마음이, 그녀의 가슴속에 고통을 견디는 인내와 온순함의 정을 일깨웠으니, 그것은 하느님의 모든 피조물 가운데 여인을 제외하고는 누구도 알지 못하는 것이지요.\n\n그들은 가난했습니다—그 사내가 그런 길을 걷는 마당에 가난하지 않을 수 없었지요. 하지만 여인이 아침이나 저녁이나, 아침 점심 저녁으로 쉼 없이 지칠 줄 모르고 애쓴 덕분에 그들은 정작 궁핍에까지 떨어지지는 않았습니다. 이 애씀은 변변히 보답받지 못했지요. 저녁때—때로는 밤이 깊은 시각에—그곳을 지나던 사람들은 고통에 빠진 여인의 신음과 흐느낌, 그리고 매질 소리를 들었다고 전했습니다. 그리고 한두 번이 아니게, 자정이 지났을 때 그 소년이 이웃집 문을 살며시 두드렸으니, 술 취한 비정한 아비의 광포함을 피해 그리로 보내진 것이었지요.\n\n이 모든 세월 동안, 그 가엾은 여인이 차마 다 감추지 못한 학대와 폭력의 흔적을 몸에 지니고 다니던 때에도, 그녀는 우리 작은 교회에 한결같이 나왔습니다. 매주 일요일 아침과 오후에 어김없이 그녀는 옆에 소년을 앉히고 같은 자리에 앉았지요. 그리고 두 사람 다 허름하게—형편이 더 낮은 이웃들보다도 훨씬 더 허름하게 입었어도, 늘 단정하고 깨끗했습니다. 모두가 '가엾은 에드먼즈 부인'에게 다정한 목례와 친절한 말을 건넸지요. 그리고 때로 예배가 끝난 뒤 교회 현관으로 이어지는 작은 느릅나무 줄 사이에서 이웃과 몇 마디 나누려고 걸음을 멈추거나, 건강한 아들이 작은 동무들과 어울려 자기 앞에서 뛰노는 모습을 어머니의 자랑과 애정으로 바라보려 뒤에 남아 머뭇거릴 때면, 근심에 찌든 그녀의 얼굴이 진심 어린 감사의 표정으로 환해지곤 했지요. 그러면 그녀는, 명랑하고 행복해 보이지는 않더라도, 적어도 평온하고 만족스러워 보였습니다.\n\n대여섯 해가 흘러갔습니다. 소년은 다부지고 잘 자란 청년이 되었지요. 아이의 가냘픈 몸을 튼튼하게 하고 약한 팔다리를 어른의 힘으로 다져준 그 세월이, 그 어미의 몸은 굽게 하고 걸음은 쇠하게 했습니다. 하지만 그녀를 부축했어야 할 그 팔은 더 이상 그녀의 팔에 끼워져 있지 않았고, 그녀를 위로했어야 할 그 얼굴은 더 이상 그녀의 얼굴을 바라보지 않았지요. 그녀는 옛 자리를 차지하고 있었으나, 그 곁에는 빈자리가 있었습니다. 성경책은 여느 때처럼 정성스레 간직되었고, 보던 곳을 찾아 예전처럼 접어두었지요. 하지만 그녀와 함께 그것을 읽어 줄 이는 아무도 없었고, 눈물이 그 책 위로 굵게 그리고 빠르게 떨어져 그녀의 눈에서 글자를 지워버렸습니다. 이웃들은 예전처럼 친절했지만, 그녀는 고개를 돌린 채 그들의 인사를 피했지요. 이제는 오래된 느릅나무 사이에서 머뭇거리는 일도 없었고—아직 다가올 행복에 대한 흐뭇한 기대도 없었습니다. 그 쓸쓸한 여인은 보닛을 얼굴 위로 더 깊이 끌어당기고는 황급히 걸어가 버렸지요.\n\n기억과 의식이 미치는 어린 시절의 가장 이른 날들을 돌이켜보며, 그 추억을 바로 그 순간까지 더듬어 보아도, 자기를 위해 어머니가 자진하여 겪은 그 길고 긴 궁핍의 연속, 학대와 모욕과 폭력—그 모두를 자기를 위해 견뎌낸 것과 어떻게든 얽히지 않은 것이라곤 하나도 떠올릴 수 없었던 그 젊은이가, 무너져 가는 어머니의 마음은 무모하게 외면한 채, 그녀가 자기를 위해 행하고 견딘 모든 것을 음울하고 고집스럽게 잊어버리고, 타락하고 막돼먹은 자들과 어울려, 자기에게는 죽음을, 그녀에게는 치욕을 가져올 무모한 행로를 미친 듯이 좇았다고—제가 말씀드려야겠습니까? 아, 슬프도다 인간의 본성이여! 여러분은 이미 오래전에 짐작하셨겠지요.\n\n그 불행한 여인의 비참함과 불운의 잔이 막 채워지려 하고 있었습니다. 그 인근에서 수많은 범죄가 저질러졌지요. 범인들은 잡히지 않았고, 그들의 대담함은 점점 커져 갔습니다. 대담하고 흉악한 성격의 강도 사건이 벌어지자, 그들이 미처 헤아리지 못했던 끈질긴 추적과 철저한 수색이 이루어졌지요. 젊은 에드먼즈가 세 명의 동료와 함께 혐의를 받았습니다. 그는 붙잡혀—기소되고—재판받고—유죄 선고를 받아—사형에 처해지게 되었지요.\n\n엄숙한 판결이 내려졌을 때 법정에 울려 퍼진 한 여인의 목소리, 그 거칠고 가슴을 찌르는 비명이 지금 이 순간에도 제 귀에 울립니다. 그 외침은 죄인의 가슴에 공포를 박아 넣었으니, 그것은 재판도, 유죄 선고도—심지어 죽음 그 자체가 다가오는 것조차도 일깨우지 못한 공포였지요. 내내 완강한 음울함으로 굳게 다물려 있던 그 입술이 저도 모르게 떨리며 벌어졌습니다. 모든 땀구멍에서 식은땀이 솟구치며 얼굴은 잿빛으로 창백해졌지요. 그 중죄인의 다부진 팔다리가 떨렸고, 그는 피고석에서 비틀거렸습니다.\n\n마음의 고통이 처음으로 격하게 터져 나온 가운데, 그 괴로워하는 어머니는 제 발치에 무릎을 꿇고는, 지금껏 모든 고난 속에서 자기를 받쳐 주신 전능하신 분께, 자기를 이 비탄과 비참의 세상에서 풀어 주시고 하나뿐인 자식의 목숨만은 살려 주십사 간절히 빌었습니다. 다시는 목격하지 않기를 바라는 그런 비통의 폭발과 격렬한 몸부림이 뒤따랐지요. 저는 그때부터 그녀의 심장이 부서지고 있음을 알았습니다. 하지만 그녀의 입에서 단 한 번도 불평이나 원망이 새어 나오는 것을 들은 적이 없습니다.\n\n그 여인이 날마다 감옥 마당에서, 애정과 간청으로 완고한 아들의 굳은 마음을 누그러뜨리려 열렬히 그리고 간절히 애쓰는 모습은 가엾기 그지없는 광경이었습니다. 다 헛일이었지요. 그는 침울하고 고집스럽고 꿈쩍도 않은 채로 있었습니다. 형이 뜻밖에 십사 년의 유형으로 감형되었을 때조차도, 그의 음울하고 모진 태도는 한순간도 누그러지지 않았지요.\n\n하지만 그토록 오래 그녀를 지탱해 온 체념과 인내의 정신도, 육신의 쇠약함과 병약함에는 맞설 수가 없었습니다. 그녀는 병들었지요. 비척거리는 다리를 끌고 침대에서 일어나 아들을 한 번 더 보러 가려 했으나, 기력이 다하여 그만 맥없이 땅바닥에 쓰러지고 말았습니다.\n\n이제 그 젊은이가 그토록 뽐내던 냉담함과 무관심이 정말로 시험에 들었습니다. 그리고 그에게 무겁게 내려앉은 응보는 그를 거의 미치게 만들 지경이었지요. 하루가 지나도 어머니는 오지 않았고, 또 하루가 흘러도 그녀는 그의 곁에 오지 않았으며, 사흘째 저녁이 와도 그는 여전히 그녀를 보지 못했습니다—그런데 스물네 시간 뒤면 그는 그녀와, 어쩌면 영영, 갈라서게 될 처지였지요. 오! 오래 잊고 있던 지난날의 생각들이 그의 마음에 얼마나 밀려들었던지, 그는 좁은 마당을 거의 뛰다시피 오르내렸으니—마치 그가 서두르면 소식이 더 빨리 오기라도 할 것처럼 말입니다. 그리고 진실을 듣고 났을 때, 자기가 무력하고 황폐하다는 느낌이 얼마나 쓰라리게 그를 덮쳤던지요! 그가 알았던 단 하나의 부모인 어머니가 병들어 누워 있었으니—어쩌면 죽어 가고 있었으니—그가 서 있는 땅에서 일 마일도 안 되는 곳에서요. 그가 자유로운 몸으로 매여 있지만 않았다면, 단 몇 분이면 그녀 곁에 닿을 수 있었을 겁니다. 그는 문으로 달려들어 절망의 힘으로 쇠창살을 움켜쥐고는, 다시금 쩌렁쩌렁 울리도록 그것을 흔들었고, 마치 돌을 뚫고 길을 내려는 듯 두꺼운 벽에 제 몸을 내던졌습니다. 하지만 그 견고한 건물은 그의 무력한 발버둥을 비웃었고, 그는 두 손을 맞부딪치며 아이처럼 울었지요.\n\n저는 어머니의 용서와 축복을 옥에 갇힌 아들에게 전했습니다. 그리고 회개의 엄숙한 다짐과 용서를 비는 그의 간절한 탄원을 그녀의 병상으로 가져갔지요. 저는 가엾고 측은한 마음으로, 그 회개한 사내가 돌아오면 그녀를 위로하고 부양하겠다며 사소한 계획을 천 가지나 짜는 것을 들었습니다. 하지만 저는 그가 목적지에 닿기 여러 달 전에 그의 어머니가 더 이상 이 세상 사람이 아니리라는 것을 알고 있었지요.\n\n그는 밤중에 이송되었습니다. 몇 주 뒤 그 가엾은 여인의 영혼은 길을 떠났으니, 저는 영원한 행복과 안식의 곳으로 갔으리라 굳게 바라고 또 엄숙히 믿습니다. 저는 그녀의 유해 위에 장례 예배를 올렸지요. 그녀는 우리의 작은 교회 묘지에 누워 있습니다. 그녀의 무덤 머리맡에는 비석이 없습니다. 그녀의 슬픔은 사람들이 알았고, 그녀의 미덕은 하느님께서 아셨지요.\n\n죄수가 떠나기 전에 미리 정해 둔 바가 있었으니, 그가 허락을 얻는 대로 어머니에게 편지를 쓰되, 그 편지는 제 앞으로 부치기로 한 것이었습니다. 아비는 아들이 붙잡힌 순간부터 그를 보기를 단호히 거부했고, 아들이 살든 죽든 그에게는 아무래도 상관없는 일이었지요. 그의 소식 하나 없이 여러 해가 흘러갔습니다. 그리고 유형 기간의 절반이 넘게 지났는데도 제가 편지를 한 통도 받지 못하자, 저는 그가 죽었으리라 결론지었지요. 사실, 저는 그가 차라리 죽었기를 바라기까지 했습니다.\n\n하지만 에드먼즈는 유형지에 도착했을 때 내륙 깊숙이 상당히 먼 곳으로 보내졌습니다. 그리고 어쩌면 이 사정 때문에, 그가 여러 통의 편지를 부쳤음에도 그 가운데 단 한 통도 제 손에 닿지 못한 것일 테지요. 그는 십사 년 내내 같은 곳에 머물렀습니다. 형기가 끝나자, 옛 결심과 어머니에게 한 약속을 한결같이 지키며, 그는 헤아릴 수 없는 어려움을 헤치고 영국으로 돌아오는 길을 찾았고, 걸어서 고향으로 돌아왔지요.\n\n팔월의 어느 화창한 일요일 저녁, 존 에드먼즈는 십칠 년 전 수치와 불명예를 안고 떠났던 그 마을에 발을 들였습니다. 그의 가장 가까운 길은 교회 묘지를 가로지르는 것이었지요. 사내의 가슴은 층계식 울타리를 넘을 때 벅차올랐습니다. 키 큰 오래된 느릅나무들, 그 가지 사이로 기우는 해가 그늘진 자리에 여기저기 풍성한 빛줄기를 드리우니, 그것이 그의 가장 어린 날들의 추억을 일깨웠지요. 그는 그때의 자기 모습을 그려 보았습니다, 어머니의 손에 매달려 평온히 교회로 걸어가던 모습을요. 자기가 어떻게 그녀의 창백한 얼굴을 올려다보곤 했는지, 그녀가 그의 이목구비를 바라볼 때면 어떻게 그 눈에 이따금 눈물이 고이곤 했는지를 떠올렸습니다—그녀가 몸을 굽혀 그에게 입 맞출 때 그 눈물이 그의 이마에 뜨겁게 떨어져 그를 함께 울게 만들었지요. 비록 그때는 그녀의 눈물이 얼마나 쓰라린 것이었는지 거의 알지 못했지만 말입니다. 그는 어린 동무와 함께 그 길을 얼마나 자주 신나게 뛰어 내려가곤 했는지, 그러면서 어머니의 미소를 보거나 다정한 목소리를 들으려고 몇 번이고 뒤돌아보곤 했는지를 떠올렸지요. 그러자 그의 기억에서 베일이 걷히는 듯했고, 보답받지 못한 친절의 말들, 무시당한 경고들, 깨뜨린 약속들이 그의 추억 속으로 밀려들어 마침내 그의 가슴이 무너져 더는 견딜 수 없게 되었습니다.\n\n그는 교회로 들어섰습니다. 저녁 예배는 끝났고 신도들은 흩어졌지만, 아직 문이 닫혀 있지는 않았지요. 그의 발소리가 낮은 건물 안에서 텅 빈 울림으로 메아리쳤고, 그토록 고요하고 적막한지라 그는 혼자 있는 것이 거의 두려울 지경이었습니다. 그는 주위를 둘러보았지요. 아무것도 변하지 않았습니다. 그곳은 예전보다 작아 보였지만, 그가 어린 시절 천 번이나 어린아이의 경외심으로 바라보았던 그 오래된 기념비들이 그대로 있었지요. 빛바랜 방석이 놓인 작은 설교단, 그가 어린 시절 받들었으나 어른이 되어 잊어버린 계명들을 그토록 자주 외던 그 앞의 성찬대도요. 그는 옛 자리로 다가갔습니다. 그 자리는 차갑고 쓸쓸해 보였지요. 방석은 치워졌고 성경책도 거기에 없었습니다. 어쩌면 그의 어머니가 이제는 더 초라한 자리에 앉는지도, 아니면 어쩌면 노쇠하여 혼자서는 교회에 올 수 없게 되었는지도 몰랐지요. 그는 자기가 두려워하는 것을 차마 떠올릴 수 없었습니다. 서늘한 느낌이 그를 엄습했고, 그는 몸을 돌리며 심하게 떨었지요. 그가 현관에 다다른 바로 그때 한 노인이 그곳으로 들어섰습니다. 에드먼즈는 흠칫 물러섰으니, 그를 잘 알았기 때문이지요. 그가 교회 묘지에서 무덤을 파는 것을 여러 번 지켜본 적이 있었습니다. 돌아온 죄수에게 그가 무어라 할까요?\n\n노인은 낯선 이의 얼굴로 눈을 들어 \"안녕하시오\" 인사하고는 천천히 걸어갔습니다. 그를 잊은 것이었지요.\n\n그는 언덕을 내려가 마을을 지났습니다. 날씨가 따뜻하여, 그가 지나갈 때 사람들은 문간에 앉아 있거나 작은 정원을 거닐며, 저녁의 고요함과 일에서 벗어난 휴식을 즐기고 있었지요. 그를 향해 여러 시선이 던져졌고, 그도 누가 자기를 알아보고 피하지나 않는지 살피려 좌우로 미심쩍은 눈길을 여러 번 던졌습니다. 거의 모든 집에 낯선 얼굴들이 있었지요. 어떤 집에서는 옛 학교 동무의 우람한 모습을—그가 마지막으로 보았을 때는 소년이었던—한 무리의 명랑한 아이들에 둘러싸인 채 알아보았습니다. 또 어떤 집에서는, 그저 정정하고 씩씩한 일꾼으로만 기억하던 노인이 오두막 문간의 안락의자에 앉아 있는, 쇠약하고 병약한 모습을 보았지요. 하지만 그들은 모두 그를 잊었고, 그는 알려지지 않은 채 지나갔습니다.\n\n지는 해의 마지막 부드러운 빛이 땅 위에 떨어져, 노란 옥수숫단에 풍성한 빛을 드리우고 과수원 나무들의 그림자를 길게 늘이고 있을 때, 그는 그 오래된 집—그의 어린 시절의 보금자리—앞에 섰으니, 길고 고달픈 유배와 슬픔의 세월 내내 그의 가슴이 형언할 수 없는 격렬한 애정으로 그리워하던 곳이었지요. 울타리는 낮았습니다, 비록 그것이 그에게 높은 담장처럼 보이던 시절을 그는 또렷이 기억했지만요. 그리고 그는 오래된 정원 안을 넘겨다보았습니다. 예전보다 씨앗도 더 많고 꽃도 더 화사했지만, 오래된 나무들은 여전히 그대로 있었지요—햇볕 아래 노는 데 지칠 때면 그가 천 번이나 그 아래 누워, 행복한 소년 시절의 부드럽고 온화한 잠이 살며시 자기에게 스며드는 것을 느끼던 바로 그 나무 말입니다. 집 안에서 목소리들이 들렸습니다. 그는 귀 기울였지만, 그 소리들은 그의 귀에 낯설게 떨어졌지요. 그는 그것들을 알지 못했습니다. 그 소리들은 즐거웠지요. 그리고 그는 자기가 떠나 있는 마당에 가엾은 노모가 명랑할 리 없다는 것을 잘 알았습니다. 문이 열리고, 한 무리의 어린아이들이 소리치고 까불며 뛰어나왔지요. 어린 사내아이를 품에 안은 아버지가 문간에 나타나자, 아이들은 작은 손뼉을 치며 그의 주위로 몰려들어, 즐거운 놀이에 끼우려 그를 끌어냈습니다. 죄수는 자기가 바로 그곳에서 아버지의 눈길을 피해 몇 번이나 움츠러들었는지를 떠올렸지요. 떨리는 머리를 이불 속에 파묻고, 거친 말과 모진 매질, 그리고 어머니의 울부짖음을 듣던 일이 얼마나 잦았는지를 그는 기억했습니다. 그리고 그 사내는 그곳을 떠나며 마음의 고통으로 큰 소리로 흐느꼈지만, 그의 주먹은 꽉 쥐어졌고, 그의 이는 사납고 살기 어린 격정으로 악물려 있었지요.\n\n여러 해의 고달픈 전망을 가로질러 그가 바라보았던, 그토록 많은 고통을 겪으며 견뎌낸 그 귀향이 바로 이러했던 것입니다! 환영하는 얼굴도, 용서의 눈길도, 맞아 줄 집도, 도와줄 손도 없었지요—그것도 옛 마을에서요. 사람이라곤 결코 보이지 않던 거칠고 우거진 숲속에서의 외로움이, 이에 비하면 무엇이었겠습니까!\n\n그는 깨달았지요, 자기가 속박과 오욕의 그 먼 땅에서, 고향을 떠나던 그때 모습 그대로 떠올려 왔지, 돌아왔을 때 어떠할지로는 떠올리지 않았다는 것을요. 그 슬픈 현실이 그의 가슴을 싸늘하게 내리쳤고, 그의 기운은 안으로 가라앉았습니다. 그는 이것저것 물어볼 용기도, 자기를 친절과 측은함으로 맞아 줄 법한 단 하나의 사람 앞에 나설 용기도 없었지요. 그는 천천히 걸었습니다. 그리고 죄지은 사람처럼 길가를 피하여, 잘 기억하는 풀밭으로 접어들었지요. 그러고는 두 손으로 얼굴을 가린 채, 풀밭 위로 몸을 내던졌습니다.\n\n그는 한 사내가 자기 곁 둑에 누워 있는 것을 알아채지 못했습니다. 그 사내가 새로 온 이를 슬쩍 훔쳐보려 몸을 돌리자 그의 옷자락이 바스락거렸지요. 그러자 에드먼즈가 고개를 들었습니다.\n\n그 사내는 앉은 자세로 몸을 옮긴 터였습니다. 그의 몸은 잔뜩 굽었고, 얼굴은 주름지고 누렜지요. 그의 옷차림은 그가 구빈원의 거주자임을 드러냈습니다. 그는 매우 늙어 보였지만, 그것은 세월의 길이보다는 방탕이나 질병의 결과로 보였지요. 그는 낯선 이를 뚫어지게 바라보고 있었는데, 처음에는 그 눈이 광채 없이 흐릿했으나, 잠시 그에게 고정되어 있더니 부자연스럽고 놀란 표정으로 이글거리는 듯하다가, 마침내 눈구멍에서 튀어나올 것만 같았습니다. 에드먼즈는 차츰 무릎을 세워 일어나, 점점 더 진지하게 노인의 얼굴을 바라보았지요. 그들은 말없이 서로를 응시했습니다.\n\n노인은 소름 끼치도록 창백했습니다. 그는 몸서리치며 비틀비틀 일어섰지요. 에드먼즈도 벌떡 일어섰습니다. 노인이 한두 걸음 뒤로 물러섰지요. 에드먼즈가 다가갔습니다.\n\n\"당신 목소리를 들려주시오.\" 죄수가 탁하고 갈라진 목소리로 말했습니다.\n\n\"물러서!\" 노인이 무시무시한 욕설과 함께 소리쳤지요. 죄수가 그에게 더 가까이 다가갔습니다.\n\n\"물러서!\" 노인이 비명을 질렀습니다. 공포에 미쳐 날뛰며, 그는 지팡이를 치켜들어 에드먼즈의 얼굴을 가로질러 호되게 후려쳤지요.\n\n\"아버지—악마 같으니!\" 죄수가 악문 이 사이로 중얼거렸습니다. 그는 미친 듯이 앞으로 달려들어 노인의 목을 움켜쥐었지요—하지만 그는 자기 아버지였습니다. 그러자 그의 팔은 옆구리로 맥없이 떨어졌지요.\n\n노인은 외로운 들판에 악령의 울부짖음처럼 울려 퍼지는 큰 비명을 내질렀습니다. 그의 얼굴은 검게 변했고, 입과 코에서 핏덩이가 쏟아져 나와 풀을 짙고 어두운 붉은빛으로 물들이며, 그는 비틀거리다 쓰러졌지요. 그는 혈관이 터졌고, 아들이 그를 일으키기도 전에 죽은 사람이 되어 있었습니다.\n\n\"그 교회 묘지의 모퉁이에,\" 노신사가 잠시 침묵하더니 말했다. \"제가 앞서 말씀드린 그 교회 묘지의 모퉁이에, 한 사내가 묻혀 있습니다. 그는 이 사건 뒤로 삼 년 동안 제 밑에서 일했으며, 만약 진정으로 뉘우치고 회개하며 겸손해진 사람이 일찍이 있었다면 바로 그런 사람이었지요. 그가 살아 있는 동안 그가 누구인지, 어디서 왔는지를 저 말고는 아무도 알지 못했습니다—그가 바로 존 에드먼즈, 돌아온 죄수였지요.\"\n\n그날의 고단한 모험 탓인지, 아니면 목사의 이야기가 자아낸 졸음의 기운 탓인지, 픽윅 씨의 노곤한 성향이 어찌나 강하게 작용했던지, 그가 아늑한 침실로 안내된 지 채 오 분도 지나지 않아 그는 깊고 꿈 없는 잠에 빠져들었다. 그 잠에서 그를 깨운 것은 오직 아침 해가 책망하듯 환한 빛줄기를 방 안으로 쏘아 보낸 것뿐이었다. 픽윅 씨는 게으름뱅이가 아니었으니, 그는 열렬한 전사가 막사 침상에서 뛰쳐나오듯 벌떡 일어났다.\n\n\"기분 좋은, 기분 좋은 시골이로다.\" 그 열정적인 신사는 격자창을 열며 한숨처럼 내뱉었다. \"한번 이런 풍경의 감화를 느껴 본 사람이라면, 어찌 날이면 날마다 벽돌과 슬레이트나 바라보며 살 수 있겠는가? 굴뚝 통 위에 얹힌 소 모양 말고는 소가 없고, 목신을 떠올리게 하는 것이라곤 평기와뿐이며, 작물이라곤 돌나물밖에 없는 곳에서 어찌 계속 살아갈 수 있겠는가? 이런 곳에서 평생을 질질 끌며 견딜 자가 누가 있겠는가? 도대체 누가 그것을 참아 낼 수 있겠는가?\" 그리하여, 가장 인정받는 선례를 따라 상당히 길게 고독을 심문한 뒤, 픽윅 씨는 격자창 밖으로 고개를 내밀고 주위를 둘러보았다.\n\n건초 더미의 풍성하고 달콤한 냄새가 그의 방 창문까지 피어올랐고, 그 아래 작은 화원에서 풍기는 백 가지 향내가 주위의 공기를 향기롭게 물들였다. 짙푸른 초원은 아침 이슬 속에서 빛났으니, 그 이슬은 부드러운 바람에 떨리는 잎사귀마다 반짝였고, 새들은 마치 반짝이는 물방울 하나하나가 자신들에게 영감의 샘이라도 되는 듯 노래했다. 픽윅 씨는 황홀하고 감미로운 몽상에 빠져들었다.\n\n\"여보시오!\" 하는 소리가 그를 일깨웠다.\n\n그는 오른쪽을 보았으나 아무도 보이지 않았고, 시선이 왼쪽으로 떠돌며 풍경을 꿰뚫었으나 거기에도 없었다. 그는 하늘을 올려다보았지만 거기서 그를 부른 것은 아니었다. 그러고 나서 그는 평범한 사람이라면 진작 했을 일을 했다. 정원을 내려다본 것이다. 그리고 거기서 와들 씨를 보았다.\n\n\"안녕하시오?\" 그 쾌활한 사람이 제 기쁜 기대에 숨이 차서 말했다. \"아름다운 아침 아니오? 이렇게 일찍 일어나신 걸 보니 반갑소이다. 어서 내려와 나오시오. 여기서 기다리리다.\"\n\n픽윅 씨는 두 번 청할 필요가 없었다. 십 분이면 몸단장을 끝내기에 충분했고, 그 시간이 다하자 그는 노신사 곁에 서 있었다.\n\n\"여보시오!\" 이번에는 픽윅 씨가 말했다. 그의 동행이 총으로 무장하고 있었고, 또 한 자루가 풀밭 위에 준비된 채 놓여 있는 것을 보고서였다. \"무슨 일이 벌어지는 게요?\"\n\n\"아, 댁의 친구분과 내가,\" 주인이 대답했다. \"아침 식사 전에 떼까마귀 사냥을 나가려는 참이오. 그분 사격 솜씨가 아주 좋다지요?\"\n\n\"본인이 일류라고 말하는 것은 들었소만,\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다. \"정작 그가 무언가를 겨누는 것은 한 번도 본 적이 없소.\"\n\n\"음,\" 주인이 말했다. \"어서 와 줬으면 좋겠소. 조—조!\"\n\n뚱뚱한 소년이, 아침의 자극적인 기운 속에서도 사 분의 삼하고도 얼마쯤은 여전히 잠들어 있는 듯한 모습으로 집에서 나왔다.\n\n\"올라가서 그분을 불러다가, 나와 픽윅 씨가 떼까마귀 숲에 있다고 전해라. 그분께 그리로 가는 길을 안내해 드려라, 알겠느냐?\"\n\n소년은 분부를 수행하러 떠났고, 주인은 제2의 로빈슨 크루소처럼 총 두 자루를 들고 정원을 나서서 앞장을 섰다.\n\n\"여기가 그곳이오.\" 노신사가 몇 분쯤 걷다가 나무들이 늘어선 가로수 길에서 걸음을 멈추며 말했다. 그 말은 굳이 필요치 않았다. 아무것도 모르는 떼까마귀들이 끊임없이 까악까악 울어 대는 소리가 그들의 소재를 충분히 알려 주었기 때문이다.\n\n노신사는 총 한 자루를 땅에 내려놓고 다른 한 자루에 장전을 했다.\n\n\"저기들 오는군요.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. 그가 말하는 사이, 터프먼 씨와 스노드그래스 씨와 윈클 씨의 모습이 멀리서 나타났다. 뚱뚱한 소년은 어느 신사를 부르라고 지시받았는지 확실치 않았던 터라, 남다른 명민함을 발휘하여, 혹시라도 실수가 생길 여지를 없애려고 셋을 모두 불렀던 것이다.\n\n\"이리 오시오.\" 노신사가 윈클 씨를 향해 소리쳤다. \"댁처럼 솜씨 좋은 분이라면 이런 시시한 일이라도 진작 일어나 나왔어야지요.\"\n\n윈클 씨는 억지웃음으로 응답하며 남아 있던 총을 집어 들었다. 그 얼굴에 떠오른 표정은, 폭력에 의해 곧 죽으리라는 예감에 사로잡힌 형이상학적인 떼까마귀가 지을 법한 표정 같았다. 솜씨 좋음의 표정이었을지도 모르나, 그것은 놀라우리만치 비참함에 가까워 보였다.\n\n노신사가 고개를 끄덕이자, 어린 램버트의 지휘 아래 그 자리에 정렬해 있던 누더기 차림의 소년 둘이 곧바로 나무 두 그루를 타고 오르기 시작했다.\n\n\"저 애들은 무엇 때문이오?\" 픽윅 씨가 불쑥 물었다. 그는 다소 놀랐던 것이다. 익히 여러 번 많은 이야기를 들은 바 있는 농업 부문의 곤궁함이, 혹여나 토지에 매인 어린 소년들로 하여금 서투른 사냥꾼들의 과녁 노릇을 함으로써 위태롭고 불안정한 생계를 잇게 만든 것은 아닐까, 그는 영 확신할 수가 없었기 때문이다.\n\n\"그저 사냥감을 몰아내려는 거지요.\" 와들 씨가 웃으며 대답했다.\n\n\"무엇을 한다고요?\" 픽윅 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"아니, 쉬운 말로 하자면, 떼까마귀를 놀라게 하려는 거외다.\"\n\n\"오, 그게 다요?\"\n\n\"안심이 되시오?\"\n\n\"아주.\"\n\n\"좋소이다. 그럼 내가 시작할까요?\"\n\n\"부디 그래 주시오.\" 윈클 씨가 잠깐의 유예라도 반가운 듯 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 옆으로 비키시오. 자, 갑니다.\"\n\n소년이 소리를 지르며 둥지가 달린 나뭇가지를 흔들었다. 어린 떼까마귀 대여섯 마리가 격렬한 대화를 나누다가 무슨 일인가 알아보려 날아올랐다. 노신사가 답례 삼아 발사했다. 새 한 마리가 떨어지고 나머지는 날아가 버렸다.\n\n\"저것을 주워 오너라, 조.\" 노신사가 말했다.\n\n소년이 다가갈 때 그 얼굴에는 미소가 어려 있었다. 떼까마귀 파이의 어렴풋한 환영이 그의 상상 속을 떠다녔다. 그는 그 새를 들고 물러나며 웃었다. 통통한 놈이었던 것이다.\n\n\"자, 윈클 씨.\" 주인이 제 총에 다시 장전하며 말했다. \"쏘아 보시오.\"\n\n윈클 씨가 앞으로 나서서 총을 겨누었다. 픽윅 씨와 그의 벗들은 자기네 친구의 무시무시한 총신이 틀림없이 떼까마귀를 우수수 떨어뜨리리라 확신하여, 그 묵직한 낙하에서 피해를 면하려고 저도 모르게 몸을 움츠렸다. 엄숙한 정적이 흘렀다—외침이 있었고—날개를 푸드덕거리는 소리가 났고—희미하게 찰칵하는 소리가 났다.\n\n\"여보시오!\" 노신사가 말했다.\n\n\"안 나가오?\" 픽윅 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"불발이오.\" 윈클 씨가 말했다. 그는 몹시 창백했는데—아마도 실망 탓이었으리라.\n\n\"이상한데.\" 노신사가 총을 받아 들며 말했다. \"이놈이 불발 나는 건 여태 본 적이 없소. 아니, 뇌관이 보이질 않는구려.\"\n\n\"어이쿠, 이런!\" 윈클 씨가 말했다. \"글쎄, 내가 뇌관을 깜빡했지 뭐요!\"\n\n사소한 누락은 바로잡혔다. 픽윅 씨가 다시 몸을 웅크렸다. 윈클 씨가 결연하고 단호한 기색으로 앞으로 나섰고, 터프먼 씨는 나무 뒤에서 내다보았다. 소년이 소리를 질렀고, 새 네 마리가 날아올랐다. 윈클 씨가 발사했다. 떼까마귀가 아니라—한 사람이—육체적 고통에 지르는 듯한 비명이 났다. 터프먼 씨가 왼팔에 탄환 일부를 받아 냄으로써, 아무 죄 없는 무수한 새들의 목숨을 구한 것이었다.\n\n뒤이어 벌어진 혼란을 묘사하기란 불가능하리라. 픽윅 씨가 격정의 첫 발작 속에서 윈클 씨를 \"이 못된 자!\"라고 부른 일이며, 터프먼 씨가 땅바닥에 엎어져 누운 일이며, 윈클 씨가 경악하여 그 곁에 무릎 꿇은 일이며, 터프먼 씨가 정신없이 어떤 여성의 세례명을 부르다가, 한쪽 눈을 먼저 뜨고 이어 다른 쪽 눈을 뜬 다음, 다시 뒤로 쓰러지며 두 눈을 모두 감아 버린 일—이 모든 것을 세세히 묘사하기란, 그 불운한 사람이 차차 회복되어 가는 모습과, 손수건으로 그의 팔을 동여매는 모습과, 걱정스러운 벗들의 부축을 받으며 그가 천천히 집으로 옮겨지는 모습을 그려 내기만큼이나 어려운 일일 것이다.\n\n그들은 집 가까이 다가갔다. 숙녀들이 정원 문에서 그들의 도착과 아침 식사를 기다리고 있었다. 노처녀 이모가 나타났다. 그녀는 미소 지으며 더 빨리 걸어오라고 손짓했다. 그녀가 이 재난을 모르고 있음이 분명했다. 가엾기도 하지! 모르는 것이 진정 복인 때가 있는 법이다.\n\n그들은 더 가까이 다가갔다.\n\n\"아니, 저 자그마한 노신사분께 무슨 일이 생긴 거죠?\" 이저벨라 와들이 말했다. 노처녀 이모는 그 말을 귀담아듣지 않았다. 그녀는 그 말이 픽윅 씨를 두고 한 것이라 여겼던 것이다. 그녀의 눈에 트레이시 터프먼은 청년이었으니, 그녀는 그의 나이를 축소경을 통해 바라보았다.\n\n\"놀라지들 마라.\" 노주인이 딸들이 놀랄까 두려워 소리쳤다. 작은 무리가 터프먼 씨 주위로 어찌나 빽빽이 모여들었던지, 그들은 아직 사고의 정체를 또렷이 분간할 수 없었다.\n\n\"놀라지들 마라.\" 주인이 말했다.\n\n\"무슨 일이에요?\" 숙녀들이 비명을 질렀다.\n\n\"터프먼 씨가 작은 사고를 당했단다. 그게 다야.\"\n\n노처녀 이모는 날카로운 비명을 지르더니, 히스테릭한 웃음을 터뜨리며 조카딸들의 품 안으로 뒤로 쓰러졌다.\n\n\"찬물을 좀 끼얹어 줘라.\" 노신사가 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 아니야.\" 노처녀 이모가 중얼거렸다. \"이제 좀 나아졌다. 벨라, 에밀리—의사를! 그분 부상당하셨니?—돌아가셨니?—그분이—하, 하, 하!\" 여기서 노처녀 이모는 비명 사이사이로 히스테릭한 웃음을 터뜨리는 두 번째 발작에 빠져들었다.\n\n\"진정하십시오.\" 터프먼 씨가 제 고통에 대한 이 동정의 표현에 거의 눈물이 날 만큼 감동하여 말했다. \"친애하는, 친애하는 부인, 진정하십시오.\"\n\n\"그분의 목소리야!\" 노처녀 이모가 외쳤고, 곧바로 세 번째 발작의 강한 징후가 나타났다.\n\n\"동요하지 마십시오, 간청하건대, 가장 사랑하는 부인.\" 터프먼 씨가 어르듯 말했다. \"맹세코 저는 거의 다치지 않았습니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 돌아가신 게 아니로군요!\" 히스테리에 빠진 숙녀가 외쳤다. \"오, 돌아가시지 않았다고 말해 주세요!\"\n\n\"바보같이 굴지 마라, 레이철.\" 와들 씨가, 이 장면의 시적인 본질에 어울리는 것보다는 다소 거칠게 끼어들었다. \"그가 죽지 않았다고 말하는 게 도대체 무슨 소용이란 말이냐?\"\n\n\"아니, 아닙니다, 죽지 않았습니다.\" 터프먼 씨가 말했다. \"부인의 도움 말고는 아무것도 필요치 않습니다. 부인의 팔에 기대게 해 주십시오.\" 그는 속삭이듯 덧붙였다. \"오, 레이철 양!\" 동요한 여성이 다가와 팔을 내밀었다. 그들은 아침 식사방으로 들어섰다. 트레이시 터프먼 씨는 그녀의 손을 가만히 입술에 갖다 대고는 소파에 주저앉았다.\n\n\"기절하시겠어요?\" 근심에 찬 레이철이 물었다.\n\n\"아닙니다.\" 터프먼 씨가 말했다. \"아무것도 아닙니다. 곧 나아질 겁니다.\" 그는 눈을 감았다.\n\n\"주무시는구나.\" 노처녀 이모가 중얼거렸다. (그의 시각 기관은 채 이십 초도 감겨 있지 않았다.) \"친애하는—친애하는—터프먼 씨!\"\n\n터프먼 씨가 벌떡 일어났다. \"오, 그 말을 다시 한 번 해 주십시오!\" 그가 외쳤다.\n\n숙녀가 흠칫했다. \"설마 그 말을 들으신 건 아니겠지요!\" 그녀가 부끄러운 듯 말했다.\n\n\"오, 아니요, 들었습니다!\" 터프먼 씨가 대답했다. \"그 말을 다시 해 주십시오. 제가 회복되길 바라신다면, 다시 한 번 해 주십시오.\"\n\n\"쉿!\" 숙녀가 말했다. \"오라버니예요.\" 트레이시 터프먼 씨는 본래 자세로 돌아갔고, 와들 씨가 의사를 데리고 방으로 들어왔다.\n\n팔을 살펴보고, 상처를 처치하고, 아주 가벼운 부상이라는 진단이 내려졌다. 일행의 마음이 이렇게 안심되자, 그들은 다시 쾌활함이 되살아난 표정으로 자신들의 식욕을 채우러 나섰다. 오직 픽윅 씨만이 말이 없고 무뚝뚝했다. 그의 얼굴에는 의심과 불신이 드러나 있었다. 윈클 씨에 대한 그의 신뢰는 그날 아침의 일로 흔들려 버렸던 것이다—크게 흔들려 버렸던 것이다.\n\n\"댁은 크리켓 선수십니까?\" 와들 씨가 그 명사수에게 물었다.\n\n다른 때였다면 윈클 씨는 그렇다고 대답했으리라. 그러나 그는 제 처지의 미묘함을 느끼고 겸손하게 대답했다. \"아니요.\"\n\n\"댁은 어떠시오, 선생?\" 스노드그래스 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"한때는 했었지요.\" 주인이 대답했다. \"그러나 이제는 그만뒀소. 이곳 클럽에 회비는 내지만, 직접 뛰지는 않소이다.\"\n\n\"오늘 큰 시합이 열린다지요.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇소.\" 주인이 대답했다. \"물론 보고 싶으시겠지요.\"\n\n\"저로 말하자면, 선생.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다. \"안전하게 즐길 수 있고, 미숙한 자들의 무력한 행위로 인명이 위태로워지지 않는 운동이라면 무엇이든 기꺼이 구경하렵니다.\" 픽윅 씨는 말을 멈추고 윈클 씨를 빤히 바라보았고, 윈클 씨는 그 단장의 꿰뚫는 시선 아래 움츠러들었다. 그 위인은 몇 분 뒤 눈길을 거두며 덧붙였다. \"부상당한 우리 벗을 숙녀분들께 맡기고 떠나도 무방하겠소?\"\n\n\"이보다 더 좋은 손길에 맡길 수는 없을 겁니다.\" 터프먼 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"전혀 불가능하지요.\" 스노드그래스 씨가 말했다.\n\n이리하여 터프먼 씨는 집에 남아 여성들의 보살핌을 받기로 결정되었고, 나머지 손님들은 와들 씨의 안내에 따라, 머글턴 전체를 무기력에서 흔들어 깨우고 딩글리 델에 흥분의 열병을 옮긴 그 솜씨 겨루기가 열릴 장소로 향하기로 했다.\n\n그들이 걷는 길은 채 이 마일도 되지 않았고, 그늘진 오솔길과 한적한 보행로를 따라 나 있었으며, 그들의 대화는 사방에서 그들을 에워싼 아름다운 풍경으로 흘러갔던 터라, 픽윅 씨는 머글턴 읍의 큰길에 들어서고 보니 자신들이 그토록 서두른 것이 거의 후회스러울 지경이었다.\n\n지형학적 성향의 재능을 지닌 사람이라면 누구나 익히 알고 있듯이, 머글턴은 시장과 자치시민과 자유시민을 거느린 자치 읍이다. 그리고 시장이 자유시민들에게, 혹은 자유시민들이 시장에게, 혹은 양측이 자치체에, 혹은 셋 모두가 의회에 올린 청원서들을 들춰 본 사람이라면 누구나, 진작 알았어야 할 사실을 거기서 깨우치게 될 것이다. 즉 머글턴은 유서 깊고 충성스러운 자치구로서, 기독교 원리에 대한 열렬한 옹호와 상업적 권리에 대한 헌신적인 집착을 한데 섞은 곳이라는 사실이다. 그 증거로, 시장과 자치체와 그 밖의 주민들은 여러 차례에 걸쳐, 외국에서의 흑인 노예제 존속에 반대하는 청원서를 자그마치 천사백스무 통이나 올렸고, 국내 공장 제도에 대한 어떠한 간섭에도 반대하는 청원서 역시 같은 수만큼 올렸으며, 교회 성직록 매매를 찬성하는 청원서 예순여덟 통과, 거리에서의 일요일 장사 폐지를 찬성하는 청원서 여든여섯 통을 올렸던 것이다.\n\n픽윅 씨는 이 저명한 읍의 중심가에 서서, 흥미가 섞이지 않은 것도 아닌 호기심 어린 기색으로 주위의 사물들을 바라보았다. 시장으로 쓰이는 트인 광장이 있었고, 그 한가운데에는 앞에 간판 기둥을 세운 커다란 여관이 있었는데, 그 간판에는 예술에서는 매우 흔하지만 자연에서는 좀처럼 만날 수 없는 형상이 그려져 있었다. 다름 아니라, 휘어진 세 다리를 공중에 쳐들고, 넷째 발의 가운데 발톱 맨 끝점 위에서 균형을 잡고 선 푸른 사자였다. 눈에 들어오는 범위 안에는 경매인 겸 화재 대리점 사무소, 곡물 중개상, 직물상, 마구상, 양조장, 식료품점, 그리고 신발 가게가 있었다. 끝에 언급한 그 창고는 모자, 보닛, 의류, 면 우산, 그리고 유익한 지식을 보급하는 데에도 함께 쓰이고 있었다. 앞에 자그마한 포장된 안마당을 둔 붉은 벽돌집이 있었는데, 누가 보아도 변호사의 집임을 알 만했다. 게다가 베네치아풍 블라인드를 단 또 다른 붉은 벽돌집이 있었으니, 커다란 놋쇠 문패에 아주 또렷하게 외과의의 집이라는 안내가 새겨져 있었다. 소년 몇이 크리켓 경기장으로 향하고 있었고, 가게 문간에 서 있던 두세 명의 상인들도 같은 곳으로 가고 싶어 하는 기색이었는데, 사실 보아하니 그들이 그리 간다 한들 그로 인해 장사를 크게 잃을 것 같지도 않았다. 픽윅 씨는 좀 더 한가한 때에 적어 두려고 이런 관찰을 하느라 걸음을 멈추었다가, 큰길에서 벗어나 이미 전장이 눈에 들어오는 곳까지 가 있던 벗들과 다시 합류하려고 서둘렀다.\n\n위킷이 세워져 있었고, 겨루는 양편의 휴식과 다과를 위한 천막 두 개도 마찬가지로 쳐져 있었다. 시합은 아직 시작되지 않았다. 두세 명의 딩글리 델 사람들과 올머글턴 사람들이 위풍당당한 기색으로 공을 이 손 저 손으로 무심히 던지며 즐기고 있었고, 그들처럼 밀짚모자에 플란넬 재킷, 흰 바지 차림을 한—그 차림새 때문에 영락없이 아마추어 석공처럼 보이는—여러 신사들이 천막 주위에 흩어져 있었다. 와들 씨는 일행을 그 천막 가운데 하나로 이끌었다.\n\n\"안녕하시오\" 하는 소리 수십 마디가 노신사의 도착을 반겼다. 그가 자기 손님들을, 오늘의 경기를 몹시 보고 싶어 하는, 또 틀림없이 크게 즐거워할 런던 출신 신사들이라고 소개하자, 그에 이어 밀짚모자들이 일제히 들어 올려지고 플란넬 재킷들이 앞으로 굽혀졌다.\n\n\"천막 안으로 드시는 게 좋겠습니다, 선생.\" 매우 뚱뚱한 신사 하나가 말했는데, 그 몸통과 다리는 마치 부풀린 베갯잇 두 개 위에 올려놓은 거대한 플란넬 두루마리의 반쪽처럼 보였다.\n\n\"훨씬 쾌적하실 겁니다, 선생.\" 또 다른 뚱뚱한 신사가 권했는데, 그는 앞서 말한 플란넬 두루마리의 나머지 반쪽과 흡사했다.\n\n\"친절도 하시오.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"이쪽입니다.\" 먼저 말한 사람이 말했다. \"여기서 점수를 기록하지요—경기장 전체에서 제일 좋은 자리외다.\" 그러고는 그 크리켓 선수가 앞장서서 헐떡이며 그들을 천막으로 안내했다.\n\n\"훌륭한 경기—신나는 운동—멋진 단련—아주.\" 픽윅 씨가 천막에 들어서자 그의 귀에 떨어진 말들이 이러했다. 그리고 그의 눈에 처음 들어온 것은 로체스터행 마차에서 만난 초록 외투의 친구였으니, 그는 올머글턴의 선택받은 자들로 이루어진 한정된 무리에게 적잖은 기쁨과 교화를 베풀며 열변을 토하고 있었다. 옷차림은 조금 나아졌고 장화를 신고 있었으나, 그를 못 알아볼 수는 없었다.\n\n그 낯선 자는 즉시 자기 벗들을 알아보았고, 앞으로 돌진해 픽윅 씨의 손을 붙잡더니 여느 때처럼 격렬하게 그를 자리로 끌어당겼다. 그러면서 그는 마치 이 모든 준비가 자신의 특별한 후원과 지휘 아래 이루어지기라도 한 듯 줄곧 떠들어 댔다.\n\n\"이쪽—이쪽으로—굉장한 재미—맥주 천지—통째로 들여놨소. 소고기 덩어리—황소 통째로. 겨자—수레로 한가득. 영광스러운 날—앉으시오—편히 계시오—만나서 반갑소—아주.\"\n\n픽윅 씨는 시키는 대로 앉았고, 윈클 씨와 스노드그래스 씨도 그 수수께끼 같은 벗의 지시에 따랐다. 와들 씨는 말없이 의아한 듯 지켜보았다.\n\n\"와들 씨—내 친구요.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"댁의 친구!—이보시오, 안녕하십니까?—내 친구의 친구라—손 좀 주시오, 선생.\" 그러더니 낯선 자는 여러 해 묵은 막역한 친분의 온갖 열정을 담아 와들 씨의 손을 움켜쥐었고, 이내 그의 얼굴과 풍채를 찬찬히 살펴보려는 듯 한두 걸음 뒤로 물러섰다가, 다시 한 번, 가능한 일이라면, 아까보다 더 따뜻하게 그와 악수했다.\n\n\"그래, 어쩌다 여기까지 오셨소?\" 픽윅 씨가, 자비심이 놀라움과 다투는 미소를 띠며 말했다.\n\n\"왔지요.\" 낯선 자가 대답했다. \"크라운에 묵고 있소—머글턴의 크라운에—한 무리를 만났는데—플란넬 재킷에—흰 바지에—멸치 샌드위치에—양념 콩팥구이에—멋진 친구들—영광스럽소.\"\n\n픽윅 씨는 낯선 자의 속기법 체계에 충분히 정통해 있던 터라, 이 빠르고 단편적인 통보에서, 그가 어찌어찌 올머글턴 사람들과 안면을 텄고, 그것을 자기 특유의 과정을 거쳐 일반적인 초대가 손쉽게 성립될 만한 정도의 친분으로 변모시켰음을 추론해 냈다. 이리하여 그의 호기심은 충족되었고, 그는 안경을 쓰고 막 시작되려는 경기를 지켜볼 채비를 했다.\n\n올머글턴이 먼저 공격에 나섰다. 그 가장 저명한 클럽에서도 가장 이름난 회원에 속하는 덤킨스 씨와 포더 씨가 배트를 손에 들고 각자의 위킷으로 걸어 나가자, 흥미는 격렬해졌다. 딩글리 델의 으뜸가는 명예인 러피 씨가 가공할 덤킨스를 상대로 투구를 맡았고, 스트러글스 씨가 여태껏 무패였던 포더에게 똑같은 임무를 수행하도록 선발되었다. 여러 선수들이 경기장 곳곳에 \"수비를 보려고\" 배치되었는데, 저마다 양손을 양 무릎에 짚고, 마치 말타기 놀이를 처음 하는 초보자를 위해 \"등을 내주듯\" 잔뜩 허리를 구부려 적절한 자세를 잡았다. 정규 선수들은 다들 이런 식으로 한다. 사실 다른 자세로는 제대로 수비를 본다는 것이 도무지 불가능하다는 것이 일반적인 생각이다.\n\n심판들은 위킷 뒤에 배치되었고, 기록원들은 득점을 새길 채비를 했으며, 숨 막히는 정적이 흘렀다. 러피 씨는 무심한 포더의 위킷 뒤로 몇 걸음 물러나, 몇 초 동안 공을 자기 오른쪽 눈에 갖다 댔다. 덤킨스는 러피의 동작에 시선을 고정한 채 자신만만하게 공이 날아오기를 기다렸다.\n\n\"플레이!\" 투수가 별안간 외쳤다. 공이 그의 손에서 곧고 빠르게 위킷의 가운데 기둥을 향해 날아갔다. 경계를 늦추지 않던 덤킨스는 만반의 태세였다. 공은 배트 끝에 맞아, 막 공을 머리 위로 넘기려고 몸을 낮추던 수비수들의 머리 위를 멀리 날아 넘어 튀어 갔다.\n\n\"뛰어—뛰어—한 점 더.—자, 이제 던져 올려—던져 올려—거기 서—한 점 더—아니—그래—아니—던져 올려, 던져 올려!\" 그 타격에 뒤이은 외침이 이러했고, 그것이 끝났을 때 올머글턴은 두 점을 기록했다. 포더 역시 자신과 머글턴을 빛낼 영예를 얻는 데 뒤지지 않았다. 그는 미심쩍은 공은 막아 내고, 나쁜 공은 그냥 흘려보내고, 좋은 공은 받아쳐 경기장 사방으로 날려 보냈다. 수비수들은 덥고 지쳐 갔으며, 투수들은 교체되어 팔이 쑤시도록 공을 던졌으나, 덤킨스와 포더는 여전히 무적이었다. 어느 나이 든 신사가 공의 진로를 막아 보려 하면, 공은 그의 다리 사이로 굴러가거나 손가락 사이로 빠져나갔다. 어느 호리호리한 신사가 그것을 잡으려 하면, 공은 그의 코를 때리고 갑절로 거세게 유쾌하게 튕겨 나갔으며, 그러는 사이 호리호리한 신사의 두 눈에는 눈물이 그렁그렁 고이고 그 몸뚱이는 고통에 몸부림쳤다. 위킷을 향해 곧장 던져 올려도, 덤킨스가 공보다 먼저 그곳에 닿아 있었다. 요컨대, 덤킨스가 잡혀 아웃되고 포더가 스텀프 아웃되었을 때, 올머글턴은 쉰넷쯤을 기록했고, 딩글리 델 사람들의 점수는 그들의 표정만큼이나 공백이었다. 그 격차는 너무 커서 만회할 수 없었다. 열성적인 러피와 열광적인 스트러글스가 시합에서 딩글리 델이 잃은 기반을 되찾으려 솜씨와 경험이 시킬 수 있는 온갖 짓을 다해 보았으나 헛수고였다. 이기는 경기가 일찌감치 진행되는 가운데 딩글리 델은 항복하고, 올머글턴의 우월한 기량을 인정했다.\n\n그러는 동안 낯선 자는 쉴 새 없이 먹고 마시고 떠들어 댔다. 좋은 타격이 나올 때마다 그는 더없이 거들먹거리고 생색내는 태도로 그 선수에 대한 만족과 찬사를 표했으니, 그것이 당사자에게 무척 흐뭇하게 받아들여졌으리라는 것은 의심할 여지가 없었다. 한편 공을 잡으려다 서투르게 실패할 때마다, 또 공을 막지 못할 때마다, 그는 그 가엾은 당사자의 머리에다 이런 식의 비난을 퍼부으며 개인적인 불만을 터뜨렸다. \"아아, 아아!—멍청이 같으니\"—\"이런, 손에 버터를 발랐나\"—\"얼간이\"—\"엉터리\"—따위였다. 이런 외침들 덕분에 그는 주위 사람들 모두에게, 저 고귀한 크리켓 경기의 온갖 기예와 비법을 꿰뚫은 더없이 탁월하고 나무랄 데 없는 심판관이라는 인상을 굳히는 듯했다.\n\n\"훌륭한 경기—잘들 했소—몇몇 타격은 감탄할 만했소.\" 경기가 끝나고 양편이 천막 안으로 몰려들 때 낯선 자가 말했다.\n\n\"댁도 해 보셨소, 선생?\" 그의 수다에 무척 재미있어하던 와들 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"해 봤느냐고요! 해 봤다마다요—수천 번도 넘게—여기서는 아니고—서인도제도에서—짜릿한 일이지요—후끈한 일—아주.\" \"그런 기후에서라면 꽤나 더운 일이겠소이다.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"덥다고요!—새빨갛게 달아오르지요—타들어 가는—이글거리는. 한번은 시합을 했지요—단일 위킷으로—친구인 대령과—토머스 블레이조 경 말이오—누가 더 많은 점수를 내는가 겨뤘소.—동전 던지기에서 이겨—먼저 공격—오전 일곱 시에—수비 볼 원주민 여섯 명—타석에 섰소. 계속 버텼지요—더위가 지독해서—원주민들이 모두 졸도—실려 나가고—새로 여섯 명 더 불렀는데—그자들도 졸도—블레이조가 투구—원주민 둘이 부축—날 아웃시키지 못하고—그자도 졸도—대령을 치워 내고—난 굴복하지 않았소—충직한 시중꾼이—콴코 삼바라는 자—마지막 남은 자였소—해가 어찌나 뜨거운지, 배트에 물집이 잡히고, 공은 갈색으로 그을렸지요—오백칠십 점—꽤 지쳤소—콴코가 마지막 남은 힘을 그러모아—날 아웃시켰소—목욕하고 저녁 먹으러 나갔지요.\"\n\n\"그래서, 그 뭐라더라, 그자는 어찌 됐소, 선생?\" 어느 나이 든 신사가 물었다.\n\n\"블레이조 말이오?\"\n\n\"아니—그 다른 분.\"\n\n\"콴코 삼바?\"\n\n\"그렇소, 선생.\"\n\"가엾은 콴코—끝내 회복하지 못했소—나 때문에 던지고 또 던지다가—제 운명도 던져 버린 거지요—죽었소이다, 선생.\" 여기서 낯선 자는 갈색 항아리에 얼굴을 파묻었는데, 그것이 제 감정을 감추려 함인지 그 내용물을 들이켜려 함인지 우리로서는 분명히 단언할 수 없다. 다만 그가 별안간 말을 멈추고, 길고 깊은 숨을 한 번 들이쉬며, 딩글리 델 클럽의 주요 회원 둘이 픽윅 씨에게 다가오는 것을 근심스럽게 지켜보았다는 것만은 우리도 안다. 그 두 사람은 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"저희가 블루 라이언에서 소박한 만찬을 들려고 합니다, 선생. 댁과 댁의 벗들께서도 함께해 주시기를 바랍니다.\"\n\n\"물론이지요.\" 와들 씨가 말했다. \"우리 벗들 가운데는 저—저분도 포함됩니다.\" 그러고는 낯선 자 쪽을 바라보았다.\n\n\"징글이오.\" 그 임기응변에 능한 신사가 즉시 눈치채고 말했다. \"징글—앨프리드 징글, '아무 저택 없음, 아무 데도 없음' 거주.\"\n\n\"저야 정말 기쁘겠지요.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"나도 그렇소이다.\" 앨프리드 징글 씨가, 한쪽 팔을 픽윅 씨의 팔에, 다른 팔을 와들 씨의 팔에 끼며 말했다. 그러면서 그는 픽윅 씨의 귀에 은밀히 속삭였다.\n\n\"끝내주게 좋은 만찬이오—찬 음식이지만 일품이지요—오늘 아침 방을 슬쩍 들여다봤소—닭이며 파이며, 그 비슷한 온갖 것들—유쾌한 친구들이오—예의도 바르고—아주.\"\n\n이제 더 마련할 사전 절차가 없었으므로, 일행은 두셋씩 자그마한 무리를 이루어 어슬렁어슬렁 읍내로 들어갔고, 십오 분도 채 못 되어 모두가 머글턴 블루 라이언 여관의 큰 방에 자리를 잡았다—덤킨스 씨가 좌장을 맡고, 러피 씨가 부좌장 역할을 했다.\n\n엄청난 양의 대화와 함께 나이프와 포크, 접시가 달그락거리는 소리가 났고, 머리통이 묵직한 웨이터 셋이 부산히 뛰어다녔으며, 식탁 위의 푸짐한 음식들이 빠르게 사라져 갔다. 이 모든 혼란의 항목 하나하나에, 익살꾼 징글 씨는 적어도 보통 사람 대여섯 명 몫의 거듦을 보탰다. 모두가 더 이상 못 먹을 만큼 먹고 나자, 식탁보가 치워지고, 병과 잔과 후식이 식탁에 놓였다. 웨이터들은 \"치우러\"—다시 말해, 먹을 것과 마실 것 가운데 손에 넣을 수 있는 찌꺼기란 찌꺼기는 죄다 제 사사로운 소용과 이득으로 챙기러—물러갔다.\n\n이윽고 흥겨움과 대화가 한데 어우러진 떠들썩한 소리 가운데, 부어오른 듯한 얼굴에 \"내게 아무 말도 하지 마시오, 안 그러면 반박하겠소\" 하는 표정을 한 자그마한 사내가 하나 있었는데, 그는 매우 조용히 있었다. 그러다 대화가 뜸해질 때면 이따금 주위를 둘러보았으니, 마치 무언가 무게 있는 말을 끼워 넣을까 궁리하는 듯했고, 또 때때로 형언할 수 없이 위풍당당하게 짧은 기침을 한 번씩 터뜨리곤 했다. 마침내 비교적 조용한 한순간에, 그 자그마한 사내가 매우 크고 엄숙한 목소리로 외쳤다.\n\n\"러피 씨!\"\n\n지명된 그 사람이 대답하자, 모두가 깊은 정적에 잠겨 조용해졌다.\n\n\"예!\"\n\n\"몇 마디 말씀을 드리고 싶습니다, 선생. 부디 신사 여러분께 잔을 채우시도록 청해 주신다면 말입니다.\"\n\n징글 씨가 생색내듯 \"옳소, 옳소\" 하고 외쳤고, 나머지 일행이 그에 호응했다. 잔들이 채워지자, 부좌장은 깊은 주의에 잠긴 현자다운 기색을 취하며 말했다.\n\n\"스테이플 씨.\"\n\n\"선생.\" 자그마한 사내가 일어서며 말했다. \"제가 드릴 말씀을, 우리의 존경하는 좌장이 아니라 선생께 드리고자 합니다. 까닭인즉, 우리의 존경하는 좌장이 어느 정도—아니, 상당한 정도라 해도 좋겠습니다만—제가 드릴 말씀의, 혹은 제가 진술할—진술할—\"\n\n\"진술.\" 징글 씨가 거들었다.\n\n\"그렇소, 진술할 바의 대상이기 때문입니다.\" 자그마한 사내가 말했다. \"그렇게 불러도 좋다면, 저의 존경하는 벗께 그 거듦에 감사드립니다. (네 번의 '옳소', 그리고 분명 그 가운데 하나는 징글 씨에게서 나온 것이었다.) 선생, 저는 델 사람입니다—딩글리 델 사람입니다. (환호.) 저는 머글턴 인구의 한 항목을 이룬다는 영예를 주장할 수 없습니다. 또한, 선생, 솔직히 말씀드리거니와, 저는 그 영예를 탐하지도 않습니다. 그 까닭을 말씀드리지요, 선생. (옳소.) 머글턴이 정당하게 주장할 수 있는 그 모든 영예와 영광을, 저는 기꺼이 머글턴에 양보하겠습니다—그것들은 너무도 많고 너무도 잘 알려져 있어, 제가 거들거나 다시 늘어놓을 필요가 없습니다. 그러나, 선생, 머글턴이 덤킨스와 포더를 낳았음을 우리가 기억하는 동안, 딩글리 델이 러피와 스트러글스를 자랑할 수 있음을 결코 잊지 맙시다. (우레와 같은 환호.) 제가 앞의 두 신사분의 공적을 깎아내리려 한다고 여기지는 말아 주십시오. 선생, 저는 이번 일에서 그분들 자신이 느낄 감정의 호사가 부럽습니다. (환호.) 제 말을 듣고 계신 신사분이라면 누구나, 어느 인물이—흔한 비유로 말하자면—통 속에서 '죽치고' 지내던 그 인물이 알렉산드로스 황제에게 한 대답을 아실 겁니다. '내가 디오게네스가 아니라면,' 그가 말하길, '나는 알렉산드로스가 되고 싶다'고 했지요. 저는 이 신사분들께서 이렇게 말씀하시는 것을 능히 상상할 수 있습니다. '내가 덤킨스가 아니라면 나는 러피가 되고 싶다. 내가 포더가 아니라면 나는 스트러글스가 되고 싶다.' (열광.) 그러나, 머글턴의 신사 여러분, 여러분의 동향인들이 탁월하게 뛰어난 것이 비단 크리켓뿐이겠습니까? 덤킨스와 단호함을 들어 보지 못하셨습니까? 포더와 재산을 결부 짓도록 배우지 못하셨습니까? (큰 박수.) 여러분의 권리와 자유와 특권을 위해 분투하던 중에, 단 한순간이나마 회의와 절망에 빠져든 적이 없으십니까? 그리고 그렇게 의기소침했을 때, 덤킨스라는 이름이 막 꺼져 버린 불을 여러분의 가슴속에 새로이 지펴 주지 않았습니까? 그 사람의 한 마디가, 마치 한 번도 꺼진 적이 없었던 것처럼 그 불을 다시 환하게 밝혀 주지 않았습니까? (큰 환호.) 신사 여러분, 저는 '덤킨스와 포더'라는 두 이름을 한데 묶어 열광적인 환호의 풍성한 후광으로 둘러싸기를 청합니다.\"\n\n여기서 자그마한 사내가 말을 그쳤고, 여기서 일행은 목소리를 높이고 식탁을 쾅쾅 두드리기 시작했으니, 그것은 그날 밤이 다하도록 거의 끊이지 않고 이어졌다. 다른 축배들도 들었다. 러피 씨와 스트러글스 씨, 픽윅 씨와 징글 씨가 저마다 차례로 아낌없는 찬사의 대상이 되었고, 저마다 차례차례 그 영예에 감사를 표했다.\n\n우리가 몸 바친 이 고귀한 대의에 우리 또한 그토록 열성적인 만큼, 이 연설들의 가장 희미한 윤곽이라도 우리의 열렬한 독자들 앞에 펼쳐 보일 수 있었더라면, 우리는 형언할 수 없는 자긍심과, 불멸의 가치를 지닌 무언가를 해냈다는 의식을 느꼈을 것이다—그러나 지금 우리는 그것을 빼앗기고 말았다. 스노드그래스 씨는 여느 때처럼 방대한 양의 기록을 했으니, 그것은 분명 더없이 유용하고 값진 정보를 제공했을 터인데, 다만 그 말들의 불타는 웅변인지 술의 열기 어린 영향인지가 그 신사의 손을 어찌나 심하게 떨리게 했던지, 그의 글씨는 거의 알아볼 수 없게 되었고 그 문체는 아예 알아볼 수 없게 되어 버렸다. 끈기 있는 조사 끝에 우리는 연사들의 이름과 어렴풋이 닮은 글자 몇 개를 알아낼 수 있었다. 그리고 노래 한 곡(징글 씨가 부른 것으로 추정된다)의 기록을 겨우 분간해 낼 수 있었는데, 거기에는 '술잔' '반짝이는' '루비' '환한' '술'이라는 말들이 짧은 간격으로 자주 되풀이되고 있었다. 또한 우리는 그 기록의 맨 끝에서 '구운 뼈'에 대한 어렴풋한 언급 같은 것을 분간해 낼 수 있을 듯하다. 그러고 나서는 '찬' '없이'라는 말들이 나온다. 그러나 우리가 그것들을 근거로 세울 수 있는 어떠한 가설도 필연적으로 한낱 억측에 기댈 수밖에 없는 만큼, 우리는 그것들이 불러일으킬 법한 어떠한 추측에도 빠져들 생각이 없다.\n\n그러므로 우리는 터프먼 씨에게로 돌아가려 하거니와, 다만 이 한 가지만 덧붙이겠다. 그날 밤 열두 시가 되기 몇 분 전, 딩글리 델과 머글턴의 명사들이 모인 그 회합에서 더없이 깊은 감정과 힘을 실어, 아름답고도 애절한 국민 가락을 부르는 소리가 들려왔다는 것이다.\n\n'우린 아침까지 집에 안 가리, 우린 아침까지 집에 안 가리, 우린 아침까지 집에 안 가리, 동이 틀 때까지.'\n\n디ング 리 델의 고요한 은둔, 그토록 많은 여성들의 존재, 그리고 그들이 그를 위해 보여 준 정성과 염려—이 모든 것은 자연이 트레이시 텁먼 씨의 가슴 깊숙이 심어 둔 그 부드러운 감정들이 자라고 피어나는 데 더없이 좋은 토양이었으니, 이제 그 감정은 한 사랑스러운 대상에게 모이도록 운명 지어진 듯 보였다. 젊은 숙녀들은 예뻤고, 태도는 마음을 끌었으며, 성품은 나무랄 데 없었다. 그러나 그 미혼의 고모에게는—그들 나이로는 결코 주장할 수 없는—풍기는 위엄이, 걸음걸이에 깃든 도도한 범접 불가의 기운이, 눈빛에 어린 장엄함이 있었으니, 그것이야말로 텁먼 씨가 일찍이 바라본 어떤 여성과도 그녀를 구별 짓는 것이었다. 두 사람의 본성에 무언가 동류의 것이, 영혼에 무언가 통하는 것이, 가슴속에 무언가 신비롭게 공명하는 것이 있음은 명백했다. 그가 풀밭에 부상당한 채 누워 있을 때 그의 입술에 가장 먼저 떠오른 이름이 그녀의 것이었고, 그가 부축을 받아 집으로 옮겨질 때 그의 귀에 가장 먼저 닿은 소리가 그녀의 히스테릭한 웃음이었다. 그러나 그녀의 동요가 어떤 경우에도 똑같이 억누를 수 없었을 상냥하고 여성스러운 감수성에서 비롯된 것인지, 아니면 살아 있는 모든 사내 가운데 오직 그만이 일깨울 수 있는, 더 뜨겁고 격정적인 감정에서 불려 나온 것인지? 이것이 그가 소파에 길게 누워 있는 동안 그의 머리를 괴롭힌 의혹이었고, 이것이 그가 단번에 영영 풀어 버리겠노라 결심한 의혹이었다.\n\n저녁이었다. 이저벨라와 에밀리는 트런들 씨와 함께 산책을 나갔고, 귀먹은 노부인은 의자에서 잠이 들었으며, 뚱뚱한 소년의 코 고는 소리가 멀리 떨어진 부엌에서 낮고 단조로운 음으로 스며 나왔다. 토실토실한 하녀들은 옆문 곁에서 빈둥거리며 이 시간의 쾌적함과, 농장에 딸린 어떤 둔중한 짐승들과 나누는 가장 원초적인 수준의 시시덕거림이 주는 즐거움을 만끽하고 있었다. 그리고 거기, 누구의 보살핌도 받지 않고 누구도 보살피지 않으며 오직 자기들만을 꿈꾸는 이 흥미로운 한 쌍이 앉아 있었다. 요컨대 그들은 정성껏 접어 둔 한 켤레의 새끼 염소 가죽 장갑처럼—서로에게 꼭 싸여—거기 앉아 있었다.\n\n\"내 꽃들을 깜빡했네요.\" 미혼의 고모가 말했다.\n\n\"지금 물을 주시지요.\" 텁먼 씨가 설득하는 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"저녁 공기에 감기 드시겠어요.\" 미혼의 고모가 다정하게 만류했다.\n\n\"아니요, 아닙니다.\" 텁먼 씨가 일어서며 말했다. \"오히려 제게 좋을 겁니다. 제가 모시고 가게 해 주십시오.\"\n\n부인은 잠시 멈춰 청년의 왼팔을 받친 삼각건을 매만져 주고는, 그의 오른팔을 잡고 정원으로 이끌었다.\n\n정원 저편 끝에는 인동덩굴과 재스민과 덩굴식물들로 둘러싸인 정자가 하나 있었으니—인정 많은 사람들이 거미들의 거처를 마련해 주려 세워 놓는 그런 달콤한 은신처 가운데 하나였다.\n\n미혼의 고모는 한쪽 구석에 놓여 있던 커다란 물뿌리개를 집어 들고 정자를 막 나서려 했다. 텁먼 씨가 그녀를 붙들어 자기 곁의 자리로 끌어당겼다.\n\n\"워들 양!\" 그가 말했다.\n\n미혼의 고모는 몸을 떨었으니, 어쩌다 그 커다란 물뿌리개 속으로 굴러 들어간 조약돌 몇 개가 갓난아기의 딸랑이처럼 달그락거릴 정도였다.\n\n\"워들 양,\" 텁먼 씨가 말했다. \"당신은 천사입니다.\"\n\n\"텁먼 씨!\" 레이철이 물뿌리개만큼이나 새빨갛게 얼굴을 붉히며 외쳤다.\n\n\"아니요,\" 달변의 픽윅 회원이 말했다. \"저는 그것을 너무도 잘 알고 있습니다.\"\n\n\"여자는 다 천사라고들 하지요.\" 부인이 장난스레 속삭였다.\n\n\"그렇다면 당신은 무엇일 수 있겠습니까? 아니, 외람되지 않게 무엇에 당신을 견줄 수 있겠습니까?\" 텁먼 씨가 대답했다. \"당신을 닮은 여인이 일찍이 어디 있었습니까? 이토록 보기 드문 미덕과 미모의 조화를 제가 또 어디서 찾기를 바랄 수 있겠습니까? 또 어디서 제가 구하려—오!\" 여기서 텁먼 씨는 말을 멈추고, 저 행복한 물뿌리개의 손잡이를 쥐고 있는 손을 꼭 눌렀다.\n\n부인은 고개를 돌렸다. \"남자들은 어쩌면 그렇게 속이는지.\" 그녀가 부드럽게 속삭였다.\n\n\"그렇지요, 그렇습니다.\" 텁먼 씨가 토해 내듯 말했다. \"하지만 모든 남자가 그런 건 아닙니다. 적어도 결코 변할 수 없는 한 존재가 살고 있으니—제 온 생애를 당신의 행복에 바치는 것으로 만족할 한 존재가—오직 당신의 눈동자 안에서만 살고—오직 당신의 미소 속에서만 숨 쉬며—오직 당신을 위해서만 인생이라는 무거운 짐 자체를 짊어지는 한 존재가 있습니다.\"\n\n\"그런 사람을 찾을 수 있다면—\" 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 그는 찾을 수 있습니다.\" 열렬한 텁먼 씨가 끼어들며 말했다. \"그는 이미 찾아졌습니다. 그가 여기 있습니다, 워들 양.\" 그리고 부인이 그의 의도를 채 알아차리기도 전에, 텁먼 씨는 그녀의 발치에 무릎을 꿇고 주저앉아 있었다.\n\n\"텁먼 씨, 일어나세요.\" 레이철이 말했다.\n\n\"결코 안 됩니다!\" 용감한 대답이었다. \"오, 레이철!\" 그가 그녀의 가만히 놓인 손을 붙잡자 물뿌리개가 땅에 떨어졌고, 그는 그 손을 입술에 갖다 댔다. \"오, 레이철! 저를 사랑한다고 말해 주세요.\"\n\n\"텁먼 씨,\" 미혼의 고모가 고개를 돌린 채 말했다. \"차마 그 말을 입에 올리기가 힘들지만—하지만—하지만—당신이 제게 영 무관심한 분은 아니랍니다.\"\n\n이 고백을 듣자마자 텁먼 씨는 그의 격앙된 감정이 시키는 대로, 그리고—우리로서는 그런 일에 대해 아는 바가 적으니 단언키 어려우나—그런 처지에 놓인 사람들이 으레 하는 대로 행동에 나섰다. 그는 벌떡 일어나 미혼의 고모의 목에 팔을 두르고는 그녀의 입술에 수많은 입맞춤을 새겼고, 그녀는 응당한 몸부림과 저항의 시늉을 보인 끝에 어찌나 순순히 받아들였던지, 만약 부인이 아주 꾸밈없는 화들짝 놀란 몸짓을 하며 겁먹은 어조로 외치지 않았던들 텁먼 씨가 얼마나 더 많은 입맞춤을 베풀었을지 알 수 없는 노릇이었다—\n\n\"텁먼 씨, 누가 보고 있어요!—들켰어요!\"\n\n텁먼 씨가 둘러보았다. 거기 뚱뚱한 소년이 완전히 미동도 없이 서서, 그 크고 둥근 눈으로 정자 안을 들여다보고 있었으나, 그 얼굴에는 아무리 노련한 관상가라도 놀라움이니 호기심이니, 인간의 가슴을 흔드는 그 밖의 알려진 어떤 격정에 빗댈 만한 표정이 조금도 없었다. 텁먼 씨가 뚱뚱한 소년을 응시하니 뚱뚱한 소년도 그를 빤히 마주 보았다. 그리고 텁먼 씨가 뚱뚱한 소년의 얼굴에 어린 완전한 멍함을 오래 살필수록, 그는 이 소년이 방금 벌어진 일을 전혀 모르거나 아니면 이해하지 못한다고 더욱 확신하게 되었다. 그런 인상에 잠겨 그는 아주 단호하게 말했다—\n\n\"여기서 뭘 원하느냐, 이놈아?\"\n\n\"저녁 식사가 준비됐습니다, 나리.\" 즉각적인 대답이었다.\n\n\"지금 막 왔느냐?\" 텁먼 씨가 꿰뚫는 듯한 눈초리로 물었다.\n\n\"방금요.\" 뚱뚱한 소년이 대답했다.\n\n텁먼 씨가 다시 그를 아주 매섭게 노려보았지만, 그의 눈에는 깜빡임 하나, 그의 얼굴에는 일그러짐 하나 없었다.\n\n텁먼 씨는 미혼의 고모의 팔을 잡고 집을 향해 걸었고, 뚱뚱한 소년은 뒤를 따랐다.\n\n\"저 녀석은 무슨 일이 있었는지 아무것도 모릅니다.\" 그가 속삭였다.\n\n\"아무것도요.\" 미혼의 고모가 말했다.\n\n그들 뒤에서 채 억누르지 못한 킥킥거림 같은 소리가 났다. 텁먼 씨가 홱 돌아보았다. 아니다, 뚱뚱한 소년일 리가 없었다. 그의 얼굴 전체에는 즐거워하는 기색이라곤 한 점도 없고, 오로지 먹을 생각뿐인 표정밖에 없었다.\n\n\"저 녀석은 분명 곤히 잠들어 있었을 겁니다.\" 텁먼 씨가 속삭였다.\n\n\"저도 조금도 의심치 않아요.\" 미혼의 고모가 대답했다.\n\n두 사람은 진심으로 한바탕 웃었다.\n\n텁먼 씨가 틀렸다. 뚱뚱한 소년은 이번만큼은 곤히 잠들어 있지 않았다. 그는 깨어 있었다—말짱히 깨어—방금 벌어진 일에 대해서.\n\n저녁 식사는 이렇다 할 전체적인 대화 시도 없이 지나갔다. 노부인은 잠자리에 들었고, 이저벨라 워들은 오로지 트런들 씨에게만 마음을 쏟았으며, 미혼 고모의 관심은 텁먼 씨에게로 향했고, 에밀리의 생각은 무언가 먼 데 있는 대상에 사로잡힌 듯했으니—아마도 그 생각은 자리에 없는 스노드그래스에게 가 있었으리라.\n\n열한 시—열두 시—한 시를 쳤는데도 신사들은 도착하지 않았다. 얼굴마다 경악이 서렸다. 길에서 매복을 당해 강도라도 만난 걸까? 집으로 돌아왔음직한 길마다 사람과 등불을 보내야 할까? 아니면 그들이—쉿! 저기 왔다. 무엇이 그들을 이토록 늦게 만들었을까? 게다가 낯선 목소리까지! 대체 누구의 것일까? 그들은 무단외출자들이 들어가 있는 부엌으로 우르르 몰려갔고, 단번에 사태의 진상을 어렴풋이 짐작하는 것 이상으로 훤히 알게 되었다.\n\n픽윅 씨는 두 손을 주머니에 찌르고 모자를 왼쪽 눈 위로 완전히 삐딱하게 얹은 채 찬장에 기대어, 고개를 좌우로 흔들면서, 알아챌 만한 이유도 핑계도 전혀 없이 더없이 부드럽고 더없이 자애로운 미소를 끊임없이 연달아 짓고 있었다. 워들 노인은 시뻘겋게 달아오른 얼굴로 낯선 신사의 손을 움켜쥐고 영원한 우정의 다짐을 중얼거리고 있었다. 윈클 씨는 여드레 태엽 시계에 몸을 의지한 채, 자기더러 이만 잠자리에 드는 게 좋겠다고 권하는 가족 누구든 그 머리 위에 파멸이 내리기를 힘없이 빌고 있었다. 그리고 스노드그래스 씨는 의자에 푹 주저앉아, 인간의 정신이 상상할 수 있는 가장 비참하고 가장 절망적인 비탄의 표정을 그 풍부한 표정의 얼굴 구석구석에 그려 내고 있었다.\n\n\"무슨 일 있나요?\" 세 부인이 물었다.\n\n\"아무 일 없어요.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다. \"우리—우린—다 멀쩡합니다.—이봐, 워들, 우리 다 멀쩡하지, 안 그런가?\"\n\n\"그럴 테지.\" 유쾌한 주인이 대답했다. \"얘들아, 여기 내 친구 징글 씨다—픽윅 씨의 친구 징글 씨가, 잠깐—들렀단다.\"\n\n\"스노드그래스 씨에게 무슨 일이라도 있나요?\" 에밀리가 몹시 걱정스럽게 물었다.\n\n\"아무 일 없습니다, 아가씨.\" 낯선 이가 대답했다. \"크리켓 만찬—성대한 자리—멋진 노래들—오래된 포트와인—클라레—좋고—아주 좋은—포도주지요, 아가씨—포도주.\"\n\n\"포도주 때문이 아니었어.\" 스노드그래스 씨가 갈라진 목소리로 중얼거렸다. \"연어 때문이었지.\" (어찌 된 영문인지, 이런 경우에 결코 포도주 탓인 적은 없다.)\n\n\"두 분 다 잠자리에 드시는 게 낫지 않을까요, 마님?\" 에마가 물었다. \"사내아이 둘이서 신사분들을 위층으로 모셔다 드리겠습니다.\"\n\n\"난 잠자리에 안 갈 테야.\" 윈클 씨가 단호하게 말했다.\n\n\"살아 있는 어떤 사내아이도 날 못 옮겨.\" 픽윅 씨가 굳세게 말하고는, 여전히 아까처럼 미소를 지었다.\n\n\"만세!\" 윈클 씨가 힘없이 헐떡였다.\n\n\"만세!\" 픽윅 씨가 따라 외치며 모자를 벗어 바닥에 내동댕이치고는, 정신 나간 사람처럼 안경을 부엌 한복판으로 던져 버렸다. 이 익살스러운 묘기에 그는 큰 소리로 웃음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n\"한—병—더—하자고.\" 윈클 씨가 아주 높은 음으로 시작했다가 아주 가냘픈 음으로 끝맺으며 외쳤다. 그의 머리가 가슴팍으로 떨궈졌다. 그리고 잠자리에 들지 않겠다는 불굴의 결의와, 아침에 \"늙은 텁먼을 해치우지\" 못한 것에 대한 피비린내 나는 후회를 중얼거리면서, 그는 깊이 잠들어 버렸다. 그 상태로 그는 뚱뚱한 소년의 직접적인 감독 아래 젊은 거인 둘에게 들려 자기 방으로 옮겨졌고, 스노드그래스 씨도 곧이어 그 뚱뚱한 소년의 보호에 자기 몸을 맡겼다. 픽윅 씨는 텁먼 씨가 내민 팔을 받아들이고는, 그 어느 때보다 더 환하게 미소 지으며 조용히 사라졌다. 그리고 워들 씨는, 마치 당장 처형 명령이라도 받은 사람처럼 온 가족에게 더없이 다정한 작별을 고한 뒤, 자기를 위층으로 모시는 영예를 트런들 씨에게 맡기고, 자못 근엄하고 위엄 있게 보이려는 지극히 헛된 시도를 하며 물러갔다.\n\n\"이런 끔찍한 광경이라니!\" 미혼의 고모가 말했다.\n\n\"역겨워!\" 두 젊은 숙녀가 동시에 토해 내듯 말했다.\n\n\"끔찍해—끔찍해!\" 징글이 아주 근엄한 표정을 지으며 말했다. 그는 일행 누구보다도 한 병 반쯤은 앞서 마신 처지였다. \"참혹한 꼴이야—정말이지!\"\n\n\"참 멋진 분이네요!\" 미혼의 고모가 텁먼 씨에게 속삭였다.\n\n\"잘생기기도 했고요!\" 에밀리 워들이 속삭였다.\n\n\"아, 그렇고말고.\" 미혼의 고모가 덧붙였다.\n\n텁먼 씨는 로체스터의 그 과부를 떠올렸고, 그의 마음은 어지러웠다. 뒤이은 반 시간의 대화도 그의 동요한 정신을 가라앉힐 만한 것은 아니었다. 새로 온 손님은 무척 말이 많았으니, 그의 일화의 수효는 오직 그의 정중함의 정도에나 따라잡힐 만했다. 텁먼 씨는 징글의 인기가 높아질수록 자기(텁먼)는 점점 더 그늘 속으로 물러난다고 느꼈다. 그의 웃음은 억지였고—그의 흥겨움은 꾸민 것이었다. 그리고 마침내 욱신거리는 관자놀이를 침대 시트 사이에 뉘었을 때, 그는 바로 그 순간 징글의 머리를 깃털 이불과 매트리스 사이에 끼워 두면 얼마나 흐뭇할까 하는 생각에 소름 끼치는 기쁨을 느꼈다.\n\n지칠 줄 모르는 이 낯선 이는 이튿날 아침 일찍 일어났고, 그의 일행은 전날 밤의 흥청거림에 짓눌려 침대에 누워 있었음에도, 그는 아침 식탁의 흥겨움을 돋우려 더없이 성공적으로 애를 썼다. 그 노력이 어찌나 성공적이었던지, 귀먹은 노부인마저 그의 가장 멋진 농담 한두 개를 나팔 보청기에 대고 들려 달라고 졸랐다. 그리고 그 노부인조차 미혼의 고모에게 황송하게도 한마디 평하기를, \"그 사람은\"(징글을 가리키는 것이었다) \"뻔뻔스러운 젊은이로구나\"라고 했으니, 그 자리에 있던 그녀의 모든 친척들이 진심으로 동의하는 견해였다.\n\n화창한 여름 아침이면 노부인은 텁먼 씨가 이미 한 차례 활약했던 그 정자로 가는 습관이 있었는데, 그 형식과 절차는 다음과 같았다. 먼저 뚱뚱한 소년이 노부인 침실 문 뒤의 못에서 빈틈없이 짠 검은 새틴 보닛과 따뜻한 면 숄, 그리고 손잡이가 큼직한 두툼한 지팡이를 가져왔다. 그러면 노부인은 느긋하게 보닛을 쓰고 숄을 두른 다음, 한 손은 지팡이에, 다른 한 손은 뚱뚱한 소년의 어깨에 얹고서 천천히 정자로 걸어갔다. 거기서 뚱뚱한 소년은 그녀를 남겨 두고 반 시간 동안 신선한 공기를 즐기게 했고, 그 시간이 다하면 돌아와 그녀를 다시 집으로 모셨다.\n\n노부인은 아주 깔끔하고 아주 까다로운 사람이었다. 그리고 이 의식은 세 해 여름 동안 정해진 형식에서 조금도 벗어남 없이 지켜져 왔던 터라, 바로 이날 아침 뚱뚱한 소년이 정자를 떠나는 대신 거기서 몇 걸음 걸어 나와 사방을 조심스레 둘러보고는 자못 은밀하게, 더없이 심오한 비밀이라도 품은 듯한 기색으로 자기에게 되돌아오는 것을 보고 그녀는 적잖이 놀랐다.\n\n노부인은 겁이 많았으니—대개의 노부인들이 그렇다—그녀가 받은 첫인상은 이 퉁퉁한 녀석이 자기 잔돈을 차지할 셈으로 무슨 심각한 신체적 위해를 가하려는 게 아닌가 하는 것이었다. 그녀는 도움을 청해 소리치고 싶었지만, 나이와 노쇠함이 오래전에 비명을 지를 힘을 앗아 간 터였다. 그래서 그녀는 극심한 공포의 심정으로 그의 동작을 지켜보았는데, 그가 바짝 다가와 그녀의 귓전에 동요한, 그리고 그녀에게는 위협적으로 들리는 어조로 고함을 질렀을 때 그 공포는 조금도 줄어들지 않았다—\n\n\"마님!\"\n\n그런데 마침 그때 징글 씨가 정자 가까이의 정원을 거닐고 있었다. 그 역시 \"마님!\" 하는 고함을 듣고는 더 들어 보려고 걸음을 멈추었다. 그가 그렇게 한 데에는 세 가지 이유가 있었다. 첫째로 그는 한가했고 호기심이 일었으며, 둘째로 그는 전혀 조심스러운 위인이 아니었고, 셋째이자 마지막으로 그는 꽃이 핀 관목 몇 그루에 가려 보이지 않았다. 그리하여 그는 거기 서서 귀를 기울였다.\n\n\"마님!\" 뚱뚱한 소년이 고함쳤다.\n\n\"왜 그러느냐, 조.\" 떨고 있던 노부인이 말했다. \"나는 분명 너에게 좋은 주인이었다, 조. 너는 한결같이 아주 친절한 대접을 받았어. 일을 너무 많이 한 적도 없고, 늘 먹을 것도 넉넉했지.\"\n\n이 마지막 말은 뚱뚱한 소년의 가장 예민한 감정을 건드리는 호소였다. 그는 감동한 듯, 힘주어 대답했다—\n\n\"알아유, 그건.\"\n\n\"그럼 대체 지금 무엇을 하려는 게냐?\" 노부인이 용기를 내며 말했다.\n\n\"마님 살갗에 소름을 돋게 해 드리고 싶구먼유.\" 소년이 대답했다.\n\n이것은 감사를 표하는 방식치고는 자못 피에 굶주린 소리로 들렸다. 그리고 노부인은 그런 결과가 어떤 과정을 거쳐 얻어질 것인지 정확히 알지 못했으므로, 앞서의 공포가 죄다 되살아났다.\n\n\"제가 어젯밤에 바로 이 정자에서 무얼 봤는지 아세유?\" 소년이 물었다.\n\n\"맙소사! 무얼?\" 노부인이 이 살집 좋은 청년의 엄숙한 태도에 놀라 외쳤다.\n\n\"그 낯선 신사 양반—팔을 다친 그 양반 말이유—입 맞추고 끌어안고—\"\n\n\"누구를 말이냐, 조? 설마 하녀들 중 하나는 아니겠지.\"\n\n\"그보다 더 나쁘구먼유.\" 뚱뚱한 소년이 노부인의 귓전에 대고 우렁차게 말했다.\n\n\"내 손녀들 중 하나는 아니겠지?\"\n\n\"그보다 더 나쁘구먼유.\"\n\n\"그보다 더 나빠, 조!\" 인간이 저지를 수 있는 흉악함의 극한이 그쯤이라고 여겼던 노부인이 말했다. \"그게 누구였느냐, 조? 꼭 알아야겠다.\"\n\n뚱뚱한 소년은 조심스레 사방을 둘러본 뒤, 살핌을 마치고서 노부인의 귓전에 고함쳤다—\n\n\"레이철 아씨유.\"\n\n\"뭐라고!\" 노부인이 날카로운 어조로 말했다. \"더 크게 말해라.\"\n\n\"레이철 아씨유.\" 뚱뚱한 소년이 우렁차게 외쳤다.\n\n\"내 딸이!\"\n\n뚱뚱한 소년이 그렇다는 뜻으로 연거푸 고개를 끄덕이자, 그 통통한 두 뺨이 블랑망제처럼 출렁였다.\n\n\"그런데 그 애가 그걸 받아들였단 말이지!\" 노부인이 외쳤다. 뚱뚱한 소년의 얼굴에 슬며시 히죽거림이 번지더니 그가 말했다—\n\n\"아씨가 그 양반한테 또 입 맞추는 것도 봤구먼유.\"\n\n만약 징글 씨가 그 숨은 자리에서 이 보고를 들은 노부인의 얼굴에 떠오른 표정을 볼 수 있었더라면, 십중팔구 별안간 터져 나온 웃음이 그가 정자 가까이에 있음을 들통나게 했을 것이다. 그는 주의 깊게 귀를 기울였다. \"내 허락도 없이!\"—\"그 나이에\"—\"나 같은 가엾고 늙은 여편네가\"—\"내가 죽을 때까지나 기다리지\" 따위의 노여운 문장 조각들이 그의 귀에 닿았다. 그러고 나서 그는 뚱뚱한 소년이 물러가며 노부인을 홀로 남겨 둘 때 그 부츠 뒤축이 자갈을 자박자박 밟는 소리를 들었다.\n\n어쩌면 놀라운 우연이라 하겠으나, 어쨌든 사실인즉, 징글 씨는 전날 밤 매너 농장에 도착한 지 채 오 분도 지나지 않아 속으로 지체 없이 미혼의 고모의 마음을 함락시키기로 결심했던 것이다. 그는 자기의 격의 없는 태도가 그 공략 대상인 아리따운 여인에게 결코 거슬리지 않는다는 것을 알아챌 만한 관찰력이 있었고, 게다가 그녀가 모든 요건 중에서도 가장 바람직한 것, 곧 약간의 독립 재산을 지녔으리라는 강한 의심마저 품고 있었다. 어떻게든 연적을 몰아내야 한다는 절박한 필요성이 그의 머릿속에 퍼뜩 스쳤고, 그는 즉시 그 목적을 향해 일각의 지체도 없이 일정한 조처를 취하기로 마음먹었다. 필딩은 우리에게 이르기를, 남자는 불이요 여자는 부싯깃이며 암흑의 군주가 거기에 불을 댕긴다고 했다. 징글 씨는 젊은 남자가 미혼의 고모들에게는 화약에 댄 불붙은 가스와 같음을 알았으니, 그는 한시도 허비하지 않고 폭발의 효과를 시험해 보기로 결심했다.\n\n이 중대한 결심에 대한 갖가지 궁리로 가득 찬 채, 그는 숨어 있던 자리에서 살금살금 빠져나와 앞서 말한 관목들의 엄호 아래 집으로 다가갔다. 운명은 그의 계획을 편들기로 작정한 듯했다. 텁먼 씨와 나머지 신사들이 마침 그가 옆문을 시야에 넣는 순간 그 옆문으로 정원을 떠났고, 젊은 숙녀들은 아침 식사 직후에 단둘이 산책을 나갔음을 그는 알고 있었다. 길은 훤히 트여 있었다.\n\n아침 식사 방의 문이 반쯤 열려 있었다. 그가 안을 들여다보았다. 미혼의 고모가 뜨개질을 하고 있었다. 그가 기침을 했다. 그녀가 고개를 들고 미소 지었다. 망설임은 앨프리드 징글 씨의 성격에 결코 없는 부분이었다. 그는 비밀스레 손가락을 입술에 갖다 대고는, 안으로 걸어 들어가 문을 닫았다.\n\n\"워들 양,\" 징글 씨가 짐짓 꾸민 진지함으로 말했다. \"불쑥 찾아온 것 용서를—사귄 지 짧지만—격식 차릴 겨를 없소—전부 들통났소이다.\"\n\n\"네?\" 미혼의 고모가 뜻밖의 출현에 다소 놀라며, 또 징글 씨의 정신 상태를 약간 의심하면서 말했다.\n\n\"쉿!\" 징글 씨가 무대 속삭임으로 말했다. \"덩치 큰 녀석—만두 같은 얼굴—둥근 눈깔—악당!\" 여기서 그가 의미심장하게 고개를 흔들자, 미혼의 고모는 동요로 몸을 떨었다.\n\n\"조지프를 두고 하시는 말씀인 듯한데요?\" 부인이 애써 침착해 보이려 하며 말했다.\n\n\"그렇소, 아가씨—그 조라는 놈, 망할!—배신자 같은 개야, 조는—노부인께 일러바쳤소—노부인 노발대발—펄펄 뛰며—악다구니를—정자—텁먼—입 맞추고 끌어안고—그런 따위 죄다—그렇잖소, 아가씨—그렇지요?\"\n\n\"징글 씨,\" 미혼의 고모가 말했다. \"당신이 여기 저를 모욕하러 오셨다면—\"\n\n\"천만에—결코 아니오.\" 조금도 기죽지 않은 징글 씨가 대답했다. \"그 얘기를 엿들었소—당신의 위험을 경고하러 왔소—제 도움을 바치려—소동을 막으려고. 괜찮소—모욕이라 여기신다면—이 방을 떠나리다.\" 그러고는 그 협박을 실행에 옮기기라도 할 듯 몸을 돌렸다.\n\n\"전 어떡하면 좋아요!\" 가엾은 미혼 여인이 울음을 터뜨리며 말했다. \"오라버니가 노발대발할 거예요.\"\n\n\"물론 그러실 거요.\" 징글 씨가 걸음을 멈추며 말했다. \"괘씸한 일이지요.\"\n\n\"오, 징글 씨, 제가 무슨 말을 할 수 있겠어요!\" 미혼의 고모가 또 한 차례 절망의 눈물을 쏟으며 외쳤다.\n\n\"꿈을 꾼 거라고 하시오.\" 징글 씨가 냉정하게 대답했다.\n\n이 제안에 한 줄기 위안의 빛이 미혼의 고모의 마음을 가로질렀다. 징글 씨가 그것을 알아채고는 자기 우위를 밀고 나갔다.\n\n\"흥, 흥!—그보다 쉬운 게 없소—못된 녀석—사랑스러운 여인—뚱뚱한 녀석을 채찍질하고—당신은 믿었던 거고—그걸로 일은 끝—다 편안해지지요.\"\n\n이 때아닌 발각의 결과에서 벗어날 수 있다는 가능성이 그 미혼 여인의 마음을 기쁘게 한 것인지, 아니면 자기가 \"사랑스러운 여인\"으로 묘사되는 것을 들은 일이 그 비탄의 날을 누그러뜨린 것인지, 우리는 알지 못한다. 그녀는 살짝 얼굴을 붉히며 징글 씨에게 고마운 눈길을 던졌다.\n\n이 알랑거리는 신사는 깊이 한숨을 쉬고는, 미혼의 고모의 얼굴에 한 이 분쯤 시선을 고정했다가, 멜로드라마처럼 흠칫 놀라며 별안간 그 눈길을 거두었다.\n\n\"불행해 보이시는군요, 징글 씨.\" 부인이 애처로운 목소리로 말했다. \"당신의 친절한 개입에 감사하는 뜻으로, 그 까닭을 여쭈어 보아도 될까요? 가능하다면 그것을 없애 드릴 수 있도록 말이에요.\"\n\n\"하!\" 징글 씨가 다시 한 번 흠칫하며 외쳤다. \"없앤다고요! 제 불행을 없애고, 그 축복을 느낄 줄도 모르는 사내에게 당신의 사랑을 베푼다—바로 지금 이 순간에도 그—하지만 안 되오, 그자는 내 친구요, 그자의 악덕을 폭로하지는 않으리다. 워들 양—안녕히!\" 그가 일찍이 입에 올린 가운데 가장 조리 있는 이 연설을 마치자마자, 징글 씨는 앞서 언급한 손수건의 남은 자락을 두 눈에 갖다 대고는 문 쪽으로 돌아섰다.\n\n\"멈추세요, 징글 씨!\" 미혼의 고모가 힘주어 말했다. \"방금 텁먼 씨를 두고 무언가 암시하셨어요—그걸 설명해 주세요.\"\n\n\"결코 안 되오!\" 징글이 직업적인(즉 연극적인) 기색으로 외쳤다. \"결코!\" 그러고는 더 이상 캐물어지고 싶지 않다는 뜻을 보이려는 듯, 미혼의 고모의 의자 가까이로 자기 의자를 끌어다 놓고 앉았다.\n\n\"징글 씨,\" 고모가 말했다. \"간청해요—애원해요, 만약 텁먼 씨와 관련된 무슨 끔찍한 비밀이 있다면, 그것을 밝혀 주세요.\"\n\n\"내가 차마,\" 징글 씨가 고모의 얼굴에 시선을 고정한 채 말했다. \"내가 차마 볼 수 있겠소—사랑스러운 여인이—제단에 제물로 바쳐지는 꼴을—무정한 탐욕에!\" 그는 몇 초 동안 갖가지 상충하는 감정과 씨름하는 듯하더니, 이윽고 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"텁먼은 당신의 돈만 노릴 뿐이오.\"\n\n\"비열한 인간 같으니!\" 미혼 여인이 격렬한 분노로 외쳤다. (징글 씨의 의심이 풀렸다. 그녀에게는 정말로 돈이 있었던 것이다.)\n\n\"그뿐이 아니오.\" 징글이 말했다. \"다른 여자를 사랑하고 있소.\"\n\n\"다른 여자라니!\" 미혼 여인이 토해 내듯 말했다. \"누구를?\"\n\n\"키 작은 아가씨—검은 눈—조카 에밀리.\"\n\n잠시 침묵이 흘렀다.\n\n자, 온 세상에서 미혼의 고모가 죽도록 깊이 뿌리박힌 질투심을 품은 단 한 사람이 있었다면, 그것은 바로 다름 아닌 이 조카였다. 핏기가 그녀의 얼굴과 목으로 확 치솟았고, 그녀는 형언할 수 없는 경멸의 기색으로 말없이 고개를 홱 젖혔다. 마침내 그녀는 얇은 입술을 깨물고 고개를 빳빳이 세우며 말했다—\n\"그럴 리 없어요. 믿지 않겠어요.\"\n\n\"두 사람을 지켜보시오.\" 징글이 말했다.\n\n\"그러겠어요.\" 고모가 말했다.\n\n\"그자의 눈길을 지켜보시오.\"\n\n\"그러겠어요.\"\n\n\"그자의 속삭임을.\"\n\n\"그러겠어요.\"\n\n\"그자는 식탁에서 그 애 옆에 앉을 거요.\"\n\n\"그러라죠.\"\n\n\"그 애에게 아첨할 거요.\"\n\n\"그러라죠.\"\n\n\"그 애에게 온갖 정성을 다할 거요.\"\n\n\"그러라죠.\"\n\n\"그리고 당신은 본 척도 안 할 거요.\"\n\n\"나를 본 척도 안 한다고요!\" 미혼의 고모가 비명을 질렀다. \"그자가 나를 본 척도 안 해, 그렇겠지!\" 그러고는 분노와 실망으로 몸을 떨었다.\n\n\"직접 확인해 보시겠소?\" 징글이 말했다.\n\n\"그러겠어요.\"\n\n\"기개를 보이시겠소?\"\n\n\"그러겠어요.\"\n\n\"그러고 나면 그자를 받아들이지 않으시겠소?\"\n\n\"결코 안 받겠어요.\"\n\"다른 사람을 택하시겠소?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"그러셔야 하오.\"\n\n징글 씨가 무릎을 꿇었고, 그 자세로 그 후 오 분 동안 머물렀다. 그리고 일어섰을 때 그는 미혼의 고모가 받아들인 연인이 되어 있었으니—다만 텁먼 씨의 거짓 맹세가 분명하고 명백하게 드러난다는 조건부였다.\n\n입증의 책임은 앨프리드 징글 씨에게 있었고, 그는 바로 그날 만찬에서 자기 증거를 내놓았다. 미혼의 고모는 자기 눈을 도무지 믿을 수가 없었다. 트레이시 텁먼 씨가 에밀리의 곁에 자리 잡고서, 스노드그래스 씨에 맞서 추파를 던지고 속삭이고 미소 짓고 있었던 것이다. 그는 전날 저녁 자기 마음의 자랑이었던 여인에게 말 한마디, 표정 하나, 눈길 한 번 주지 않았다.\n\n\"저 녀석, 망할!\" 워들 노인이 속으로 생각했다.—그는 어머니에게서 그 이야기를 들은 터였다. \"저 녀석, 망할 놈! 분명 잠들어 있었을 게야. 죄다 상상일 뿐이야.\"\n\n\"배신자!\" 미혼의 고모가 생각했다. \"친애하는 징글 씨가 나를 속인 게 아니었어. 윽! 저 비열한 인간이 어찌나 미운지!\"\n\n다음의 대화는 트레이시 텁먼 씨 편에서 보인 이 겉보기에 설명할 수 없는 태도 변화를 우리 독자들에게 풀이해 주는 데 도움이 될 것이다.\n\n때는 저녁이었고, 무대는 정원이었다. 두 사람의 형체가 한쪽 오솔길을 거닐고 있었으니, 하나는 다소 키가 작고 통통했고, 다른 하나는 키가 크고 호리호리했다. 그들은 텁먼 씨와 징글 씨였다. 통통한 형체가 대화를 시작했다.\n\n\"내가 어떻게 했소?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n\"훌륭했소—기막혔소—나라도 그보다 더 잘 연기하진 못했을 거요—내일도 그 배역을 되풀이하셔야 하오—추후 통지가 있을 때까지 매일 저녁.\"\n\n\"레이철이 아직도 그걸 바라오?\"\n\n\"물론이지요—그녀도 좋아하진 않소—하지만 해야만 하오—의심을 피하려고—오라비를 두려워하니—달리 도리가 없다고 합디다—며칠만 더—늙은이들 눈을 가린 다음—당신 행복에 면류관을 씌우는 거요.\"\n\n\"전할 말이라도?\"\n\n\"사랑—더없는 사랑—더없이 다정한 안부—변치 않는 애정. 당신을 위해 무어라 전해 드릴까요?\"\n\n\"이보게, 친구.\" 의심을 모르는 텁먼 씨가 '친구'의 손을 열렬히 움켜쥐며 대답했다. \"내 더없는 사랑을 전해 주게—이렇게 시치미를 떼는 것이 내게 얼마나 힘든지 말해 주게—무어든 다정한 말을 전해 주되, 오늘 아침 그녀가 자네를 통해 내게 한 그 제안의 필요성을 내가 얼마나 잘 헤아리고 있는지도 덧붙여 주게. 내가 그녀의 지혜에 갈채를 보내고 그 분별을 흠모한다고 말해 주게.\"\n\n\"그러리다. 더 있소?\"\n\n\"없네, 다만 그녀를 내 사람이라 부르고 모든 시치미가 더는 필요 없게 될 그날을 내가 얼마나 애타게 고대하는지만 덧붙여 주게.\"\n\n\"그럼요, 그럼요. 더 있소?\"\n\n\"오, 친구!\" 가엾은 텁먼 씨가 다시 한 번 동행의 손을 움켜쥐며 말했다. \"자네의 사심 없는 친절에 가장 따뜻한 감사를 받아 주게. 그리고 내가 일찍이, 생각으로나마, 자네가 내 앞길을 가로막을 수 있으리라 의심하는 부당한 짓을 저질렀다면 용서해 주게. 친애하는 친구여, 내가 자네에게 이 은혜를 갚을 수 있을까?\"\n\n\"그런 말 마시오.\" 징글 씨가 대답했다. 그는 무언가 문득 떠오른 듯 우뚝 멈춰 서서 말했다. \"참, 그런데—혹 십 파운드만 융통해 줄 수 있겠소?—아주 긴한 데가 있어서—사흘 안에 갚으리다.\"\n\n\"아마 할 수 있을 걸세.\" 텁먼 씨가 충만한 마음으로 대답했다. \"사흘이라 했나?\"\n\n\"사흘뿐이오—그럼 다 끝나오—더는 어려움이 없을 거요.\" 텁먼 씨가 돈을 세어 동행의 손에 쥐여 주자, 그는 집을 향해 걸으면서 그것을 한 닢 한 닢 자기 주머니에 떨어뜨려 넣었다.\n\n\"조심하시오.\" 징글 씨가 말했다. \"눈길 하나도 주지 말고.\"\n\n\"눈 깜빡 하나도.\" 텁먼 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"한마디도.\"\n\n\"속삭임 하나도.\"\n\n\"당신의 온갖 정성은 죄다 조카에게—고모에게는 차라리 무례할 만큼—그것만이 늙은이들을 속이는 길이오.\"\n\n\"명심하겠네.\" 텁먼 씨가 소리 내어 말했다.\n\n\"그리고 나도 명심하겠소.\" 징글 씨가 속으로 말했다. 그리고 두 사람은 집 안으로 들어섰다.\n\n그날 오후의 장면은 그날 저녁에도 되풀이되었고, 이어지는 사흘간의 오후와 저녁마다 거듭되었다. 나흘째 되는 날, 주인은 기분이 매우 좋았으니, 텁먼 씨에 대한 혐의에 아무 근거가 없음을 스스로 납득했기 때문이었다. 텁먼 씨도 기분이 좋았으니, 징글 씨가 그의 일이 곧 결정적 국면에 이르리라 일러 주었기 때문이었다. 픽윅 씨도 기분이 좋았으니, 그가 그렇지 않은 적은 드물었기 때문이었다. 스노드그래스 씨는 기분이 좋지 않았으니, 그가 텁먼 씨를 질투하게 되었기 때문이었다. 노부인은 기분이 좋았으니, 휘스트에서 이기고 있었기 때문이었다. 징글 씨와 워들 양도 기분이 좋았으니, 그 까닭인즉 이 파란만장한 이야기에서 따로 한 장을 할애해 서술할 만큼 충분히 중요한 것이었다.\n\n# CHAPTER IX. A DISCOVERY AND A CHASE\n\n저녁 식사 준비가 다 되어, 의자들이 식탁 둘레로 끌어당겨져 있었고, 술병과 주전자와 잔들이 사이드보드 위에 가지런히 놓여 있었으니, 모든 것이 하루 스물네 시간 가운데 가장 흥겨운 때가 다가오고 있음을 알리고 있었다.\n\n\"레이철은 어디 있지?\" 워들 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"아, 그러고 보니 징글은 어디 있소?\" 픽윅 씨가 덧붙였다.\n\n\"이런,\" 주인이 말했다. \"어째서 진작 그가 없는 걸 몰랐는지 모르겠군. 글쎄, 적어도 두 시간은 그의 목소리를 듣지 못한 것 같아. 에밀리야, 종을 좀 울려라.\"\n\n종이 울렸고, 뚱뚱한 소년이 나타났다.\n\n\"레이철 양은 어디 있느냐?\" 그는 알지 못했다.\n\n\"그럼 징글 씨는 어디 있느냐?\" 그는 모른다고 했다. 모두가 놀란 표정이었다. 밤이 깊었다—열한 시가 지나 있었다. 터프먼 씨는 소매 속에서 혼자 웃었다. 그들은 어디선가 어슬렁거리며 자기 이야기를 하고 있을 것이다. 하, 하! 그거 참 기막힌 생각이야—재미있군.\n\n\"신경 쓸 것 없네,\" 워들이 잠깐 멈췄다가 말했다. \"곧 나타나겠지, 틀림없이. 나는 누구를 기다리느라 저녁을 미루는 법이 없다네.\"\n\n\"그거 훌륭한 원칙이군요,\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"참으로 감탄할 만합니다.\"\n\n\"자, 앉으시지요,\" 주인이 말했다.\n\n\"그러지요,\" 픽윅 씨가 말하니, 모두가 자리에 앉았다.\n\n식탁 위에는 거대한 차가운 소고기 덩어리가 놓여 있었고, 픽윅 씨에게도 푸짐한 몫이 돌아갔다. 그가 포크를 입술로 들어올리고는 막 입을 벌려 소고기 한 점을 받아들이려는 바로 그 순간, 부엌에서 여러 목소리가 웅성거리는 소리가 갑작스레 일었다. 그는 동작을 멈추고 포크를 내려놓았다. 워들 씨도 멈추고는 자기도 모르게 고기 써는 칼을 쥐었던 손을 놓았는데, 칼은 소고기에 꽂힌 채 그대로 남아 있었다. 그는 픽윅 씨를 바라보았다. 픽윅 씨도 그를 바라보았다.\n\n복도에서 묵직한 발소리가 들렸다. 응접실 문이 갑자기 벌컥 열리더니, 픽윅 씨가 처음 도착했을 때 그의 부츠를 닦아 주었던 사내가 방 안으로 뛰어들었고, 뒤이어 뚱뚱한 소년과 하인들이 모두 따라 들어왔다.\n\n\"이게 도대체 무슨 영문이냐?\" 주인이 외쳤다.\n\n\"부엌 굴뚝에 불난 거 아니지, 그렇지, 엠마?\" 노부인이 물었다.\n\n\"어머나, 할머니! 아니에요,\" 두 젊은 아가씨가 동시에 소리쳤다.\n\n\"무슨 일이냐?\" 집주인이 고함쳤다.\n\n사내는 숨을 헐떡이며 가까스로 말을 토해 냈다.\n\n\"그분들이 떠났어유, 나리!—싹 가버렸구먼유, 나리!\" (이 대목에서 터프먼 씨가 나이프와 포크를 내려놓고 얼굴이 몹시 창백해지는 것이 보였다.)\n\n\"누가 갔다는 거냐?\" 워들 씨가 사납게 물었다.\n\n\"징글 씨하구 레이철 양이유, 머글턴 블루 라이언에서 역마차 타구유. 지가 거기 있었는디, 막을 수가 없어서, 그래 알려드릴라구 달려왔구먼유.\"\n\n\"내가 그놈 여비를 댔는데!\" 터프먼 씨가 미친 듯이 벌떡 일어서며 말했다. \"그놈한테 내 돈 십 파운드가 있어!—저놈을 잡아!—저놈이 날 속였어!—난 못 참아!—정의를 바로 세울 테야, 픽윅!—난 그냥 두지 않겠어!\" 그러고는 이와 비슷한 두서없는 외침들을 잇따라 내지르며, 그 불행한 신사는 광란의 발작에 사로잡혀 방 안을 빙글빙글 돌았다.\n\n\"맙소사!\" 픽윅 씨가 친구의 기이한 몸짓을 겁에 질린 놀라움으로 바라보며 외쳤다. \"이 사람이 미쳤구나! 어떻게 하면 좋단 말인가?\"\n\n\"하면 되지!\" 그 문장의 마지막 말만 귀에 담은 건장한 노주인이 말했다. \"이륜마차에 말을 매시오! 나는 라이언에서 사륜마차를 구해 당장 뒤쫓겠소. 어디 있느냐?\"—그가 외쳤으니, 사내가 명령을 수행하러 달려 나간 참이었다—\"그 악당 조는 어디 있느냐?\"\n\n\"여기 있구먼유! 근디 지는 악당이 아니유,\" 한 목소리가 대답했다. 뚱뚱한 소년의 목소리였다.\n\n\"저놈한테 좀 가게 해 주시오, 픽윅,\" 워들이 그 운수 사나운 젊은이에게 달려들며 외쳤다. \"저놈이 그 악당 징글한테 매수당해서, 내 여동생과 자네 친구 터프먼에 관한 터무니없는 거짓말을 늘어놓고는 나를 엉뚱한 데로 따돌린 거요!\" (이 대목에서 터프먼 씨가 의자에 털썩 주저앉았다.) \"저놈한테 가게 해 주시오!\"\n\n\"막아요!\" 여자들이 모두 비명을 질렀는데, 그 외침들 너머로 뚱뚱한 소년이 엉엉 우는 소리가 또렷이 들렸다.\n\n\"날 붙들지 마시오!\" 노인이 소리쳤다. \"윈클 씨, 손을 떼시오. 픽윅 씨, 날 놔주시오, 어서!\"\n\n소란과 혼란의 그 순간에, 픽윅 씨의 얼굴에 떠오른 평온하고 철학적인 표정을 바라보는 것은 참으로 아름다운 광경이었으니, 비록 안간힘을 쓰느라 다소 상기되긴 했지만, 그는 두 팔로 그 뚱뚱한 주인의 널찍한 허리를 단단히 끌어안은 채 그의 격정의 격렬함을 억누르고 있었고, 그러는 동안 뚱뚱한 소년은 그곳에 모인 모든 여자들에게 할퀴이고, 잡아당겨지고, 떠밀려 방에서 쫓겨났다. 그가 손을 놓기 무섭게, 그 사내가 들어와 이륜마차가 준비되었음을 알렸다.\n\n\"저분 혼자 보내지 마요!\" 여자들이 비명을 질렀다. \"누굴 죽이고 말 거예요!\"\n\n\"내가 함께 가겠습니다,\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"자네 참 좋은 사람일세, 픽윅,\" 주인이 그의 손을 꼭 잡으며 말했다. \"엠마, 픽윅 씨가 목에 두를 숄을 갖다 드려라—서둘러. 얘들아, 할머니를 돌봐 드려라. 기절하셨다. 자, 그럼, 준비되었소?\"\n\n픽윅 씨는 입과 턱을 커다란 숄로 황급히 둘둘 감싸고, 모자를 머리에 쓰고, 외투를 팔에 걸치고는 그렇다고 대답했다.\n\n두 사람은 이륜마차에 뛰어올랐다. \"고삐를 늦춰라, 톰!\" 주인이 외쳤다. 그렇게 그들은 좁은 길을 따라 내달렸으니, 수레바퀴 자국을 들락날락 덜컹거리고, 양쪽 산울타리에 쿵쿵 부딪치며, 금방이라도 산산조각이 날 것만 같았다.\n\n\"얼마나 앞섰소?\" 워들이 외쳤으니, 그들은 블루 라이언의 문 앞으로 마차를 몰아 댔는데, 그 둘레에는 늦은 시각인데도 작은 무리가 모여 있었다.\n\n\"기껏해야 사십오 분쯤 앞섰을 겁니다,\" 다들 그렇게 대답했다.\n\n\"당장 사두마차를! 끌어내시오! 이륜마차는 나중에 들여놓고.\"\n\n\"자, 얘들아!\" 여관 주인이 외쳤다. \"사두마차 끌어내라—서둘러—꾸물대지 마라!\"\n\n마부들과 일꾼 소년들이 달려 나갔다. 사람들이 이리저리 뛰어다니자 등불이 깜빡거렸다. 말발굽이 마당의 울퉁불퉁한 포석 위에서 또각거렸다. 마차가 차고에서 끌려나오며 덜커덕거렸다. 온통 소음과 부산함뿐이었다.\n\n\"자, 어떻게 됐나!—그 마차는 오늘 밤 안에 나오는 거냐?\" 워들이 외쳤다.\n\n\"지금 마당으로 내려오고 있습니다, 나리,\" 마부가 대답했다.\n\n마차가 나오고—말들이 매어지고—소년들이 뛰어오르고—여행자들이 올라탔다.\n\n\"명심해라—칠 마일 구간을 삼십 분 안에!\" 워들이 외쳤다.\n\n\"자, 출발!\"\n\n소년들이 채찍과 박차를 가하고, 종업원들이 소리치고, 마부들이 환호하니, 그들은 빠르고 맹렬하게 내달렸다.\n\n\"근사한 처지로군,\" 픽윅 씨는 잠시 생각할 틈이 생기자 속으로 중얼거렸다. \"픽윅 클럽의 회장이라는 사람이 처한 근사한 처지야. 축축한 마차에—낯선 말들에—시속 십오 마일에—게다가 한밤중 열두 시라니!\"\n\n처음 삼사 마일 동안은 두 신사 누구도 한마디도 하지 않았으니, 저마다 제 생각에 너무 깊이 빠져 동행에게 무슨 말을 건넬 겨를이 없었다. 그러나 그만큼 길을 달려, 말들이 완전히 몸이 풀려 정말로 멋들어지게 제 일을 해내기 시작하자, 픽윅 씨는 그 빠른 움직임에 너무도 흥이 올라 더는 입을 다물고 있을 수가 없었다.\n\n\"우리가 꼭 그들을 따라잡을 것 같소,\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"그러길 바라오,\" 동행이 대답했다.\n\n\"좋은 밤이로군요,\" 픽윅 씨가 환하게 빛나는 달을 올려다보며 말했다.\n\n\"그만큼 더 나쁘지요,\" 워들이 받았다. \"저들은 달빛 덕을 톡톡히 보며 우릴 앞질렀을 테고, 우린 그 덕을 못 보게 될 테니. 한 시간만 지나면 달이 져 버릴 거요.\"\n\n\"이런 속도로 어둠 속을 달리려면 꽤 불쾌하지 않겠습니까?\" 픽윅 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"아마 그럴 거요,\" 친구가 무뚝뚝하게 대답했다.\n\n픽윅 씨의 일시적인 흥분은 차츰 가라앉기 시작했으니, 그가 그토록 경솔하게 뛰어든 이 원정의 불편함과 위험을 곰곰 생각하게 된 까닭이었다. 선두 말에 탄 마부 소년이 큰 소리로 외치는 바람에 그는 정신이 번쩍 들었다.\n\n\"요-요-요-요-요-에!\" 첫 번째 소년이 외쳤다.\n\n\"요-요-요-요-에!\" 두 번째 소년이 외쳤다.\n\n\"요-요-요-요-에!\" 워들 노인이 직접 우렁차게 받았으니, 머리와 상반신 절반을 마차 창밖으로 내민 채였다.\n\n\"요-요-요-요-에!\" 픽윅 씨도 그 뜻이나 목적은 전혀 알지 못한 채 그 외침을 따라 외쳤다. 그렇게 넷이서 다 함께 요-요 하고 외치는 가운데, 마차가 멈춰 섰다.\n\n\"무슨 일이오?\" 픽윅 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"여기 통행문이 있소,\" 워들 노인이 대답했다. \"도망자들 소식을 좀 들을 수 있을 거요.\"\n\n쉴 새 없이 두드리고 외치는 데 오 분을 보낸 뒤에야, 셔츠와 바지 차림의 노인이 통행료 징수소에서 나와 문을 열었다.\n\n\"여기를 역마차가 지나간 지 얼마나 됐소?\" 워들 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"얼마나 됐냐고요?\"\n\n\"그래!\"\n\"글쎄, 정확히는 모르겄네유. 오래된 것도 아니구, 그렇다고 얼마 안 된 것도 아니구—아마 그 중간쯤 될라나유.\"\n\n\"마차가 지나가긴 했소?\"\n\n\"아, 예, 마차가 한 대 지나갔쥬.\"\n\n\"얼마나 됐소, 친구,\" 픽윅 씨가 끼어들었다. \"한 시간 됐소?\"\n\n\"아, 그쯤 됐을지두 모르쥬,\" 사내가 대답했다.\n\n\"아니면 두 시간 됐나?\" 뒷말에 탄 마부 소년이 물었다.\n\n\"글쎄유, 그랬다 해두 이상할 거 없쥬,\" 노인이 미심쩍게 받았다.\n\n\"몰아라, 얘들아,\" 성마른 노신사가 외쳤다. \"저 늙은 멍청이하고 더는 시간 낭비하지 마라!\"\n\n\"멍청이라구!\" 노인이 씩 웃으며 외쳤으니, 그는 문을 반쯤 닫은 채 길 한복판에 서서, 점점 멀어지며 빠르게 작아지는 마차를 바라보고 있었다. \"아니지—그렇게 멍청한 것두 아니구먼. 당신네는 여기서 십 분을 까먹고두, 결국 올 때만큼이나 헛똑똑이로 가버렸으니 말이여. 이 길에서 일 기니씩 받는 작자가 죄다 그 절반만큼이라두 제값을 하면, 당신네는 미가엘 축일 전에는 저쪽 마차를 못 잡을 거구먼, 이 땅딸막한 영감탱이야.\" 그러고는 또 한 번 길게 씩 웃고 나서, 노인은 문을 닫고 제 집으로 들어가 뒤로 빗장을 질렀다.\n* 미가엘 축일: 9월 29일. \"그때까지도 못 잡는다\"는 곧 한참 동안 못 잡는다는 비아냥이다.\n\n한편 마차는 속도를 조금도 늦추지 않고 구간의 끝을 향해 나아갔다. 워들이 예언한 대로 달은 빠르게 기울어 가고 있었다. 한동안 차츰 하늘을 뒤덮어 가던 검고 무거운 구름이 겹겹이 쌓여, 이제는 머리 위에 하나의 검은 덩어리를 이루었다. 이따금 마차 창문에 후드득 부딪치는 굵은 빗방울은 폭풍우 치는 밤이 빠르게 다가오고 있음을 여행자들에게 경고하는 듯했다. 바람마저 정면으로 불어와, 좁은 길을 따라 사납게 휘몰아치며, 길가에 늘어선 나무들 사이로 음산하게 울부짖었다. 픽윅 씨는 외투를 몸에 더 단단히 여미고, 마차 구석으로 한층 아늑하게 몸을 웅크리고는 깊은 잠에 빠졌으니, 마차가 멈추는 것과 마부의 종소리, 그리고 \"당장 말을 대령하라!\"는 큰 외침에야 비로소 잠에서 깨어났다.\n\n그러나 여기서 또 한 번 지체가 생겼다. 소년들이 어찌나 신기하리만치 곤히 잠들었던지, 하나씩 깨우는 데 오 분씩이나 걸렸다. 마부는 어찌 된 영문인지 마구간 열쇠를 어디 두었는지 잊어버렸고, 그것을 찾고 나서도 졸음에 겨운 일꾼 둘이 엉뚱한 말에 엉뚱한 마구를 채우는 바람에, 마구 채우는 일을 처음부터 다시 해야만 했다. 픽윅 씨 혼자였다면 이렇게 거듭되는 장애에 추격은 즉시 완전히 끝장나고 말았겠지만, 워들 노인은 그리 쉽게 기죽을 사람이 아니었다. 그는 이 사람을 쥐어박고 저 사람을 떠밀며, 여기서 죔쇠를 채우고 저기서 고리를 조이며, 어찌나 진심 어린 열의로 동분서주했던지, 그 숱한 어려움 속에서도 마차는 응당 예상할 수 있었던 것보다 훨씬 짧은 시간에 채비를 갖추었다.\n\n그들은 다시 길을 떠났다. 그러나 앞에 펼쳐진 전망은 결코 희망적이라 할 수 없었다. 구간은 십오 마일이나 되었고, 밤은 어두웠고, 바람은 거셌으며, 비는 억수같이 퍼붓고 있었다. 이런 장애들이 한데 겹친 상황에서는 좀처럼 길을 크게 나아갈 수가 없었다. 이미 한 시가 다 되어 가고 있었고, 그 구간의 끝에 다다르는 데 거의 두 시간이 걸렸다. 그러나 여기서 한 가지 광경이 눈에 띄어, 그들의 희망에 다시 불을 지피고 가라앉았던 기운을 되살아나게 했다.\n\n\"이 마차는 언제 들어왔소?\" 워들 노인이 자기 마차에서 뛰어내리며 외쳤으니, 마당에 서 있는, 젖은 진흙을 뒤집어쓴 마차 한 대를 가리키고 있었다.\n\n\"십오 분도 채 안 됐습니다, 나리,\" 질문을 받은 마부가 대답했다.\n\n\"숙녀와 신사였소?\" 워들이 조바심에 거의 숨이 넘어갈 듯 물었다.\n\n\"예, 나리.\"\n\n\"키 큰 신사—연미복—긴 다리—마른 몸?\"\n\n\"예, 나리.\"\n\n\"나이 든 숙녀—여윈 얼굴—다소 깡마르고—응?\"\n\n\"예, 나리.\"\n\n\"맙소사, 바로 그 한 쌍일세, 픽윅,\" 노신사가 외쳤다.\n\n\"더 일찍 도착했을 텐데,\" 마부가 말했다. \"끄는 가죽끈이 끊어졌더랬습니다.\"\n\n\"그들이야!\" 워들이 말했다. \"맞아, 정말로! 당장 사두마차를! 다음 구간에 닿기 전에 아직 따라잡을 수 있어. 한 사람당 일 기니다, 얘들아—어서 움직여—부산을 떨어 봐라—착한 녀석들이지.\"\n\n이런 식의 독려를 쏟아내며, 노신사는 마당을 오르내리며 이리저리 부산을 떨었으니, 그 흥분이 픽윅 씨에게도 옮아갔다. 그 영향으로 픽윅 씨도 마구에 복잡하게 뒤엉키고, 더없이 놀라운 방식으로 말들과 마차 바퀴들 사이에 휘말려 들었으니, 그렇게 함으로써 자신이 다시 길을 떠나기 위한 준비를 실질적으로 돕고 있다고 굳게 믿고 있었다.\n\n\"올라타시오—올라타!\" 워들 노인이 마차에 기어오르며, 발판을 끌어올리고, 뒤로 문을 쾅 닫으며 외쳤다. \"어서 갑시다! 서둘러요!\" 픽윅 씨가 정확히 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 미처 깨닫기도 전에, 노신사의 잡아당김 한 번과 마부의 떠밂 한 번에 그는 반대편 문으로 떠밀려 들어가는 자신을 느꼈다. 그렇게 그들은 다시 출발했다.\n\n\"아! 이제 가는군,\" 노신사가 의기양양하게 말했다. 정말로 그러했으니, 픽윅 씨가 마차의 단단한 목재에든 동행의 몸에든 끊임없이 부딪치는 것으로 충분히 입증되었다.\n\n\"버티시오!\" 픽윅 씨가 머리부터 곤두박여 자신의 널찍한 조끼 속으로 파고들자 건장한 워들 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"내 평생 이런 덜컹거림은 겪어 본 적이 없소,\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"신경 쓸 것 없소,\" 동행이 대답했다. \"곧 끝날 거요. 차분히, 차분히.\"\n\n픽윅 씨는 할 수 있는 한 단단히 자기 구석에 몸을 자리 잡았다. 그리고 마차는 그 어느 때보다도 빠르게 내달렸다.\n\n그들이 이런 식으로 약 삼 마일쯤 갔을 때, 이삼 분 동안 창밖을 내다보던 워들 씨가 갑자기 진흙투성이가 된 얼굴을 안으로 들이며, 숨 가쁘게 격앙되어 외쳤다.\n\n\"저기 있다!\"\n\n픽윅 씨가 창밖으로 고개를 내밀었다. 그렇다. 얼마 떨어지지 않은 앞쪽에 사두마차 한 대가 전속력으로 질주하고 있었다.\n\n\"가라, 가!\" 노신사가 거의 비명을 지르듯 외쳤다. \"한 사람당 이 기니다, 얘들아—저들이 우리를 따돌리지 못하게 해라—계속 몰아—계속 몰아.\"\n\n앞선 마차의 말들이 최고 속력으로 내달리기 시작했고, 워들 씨의 말들도 그 뒤를 맹렬히 질주했다.\n\n\"저놈 머리가 보여,\" 성마른 노인이 외쳤다. \"제기랄, 저놈 머리가 보인다.\"\n\n\"나도 보이오,\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"저게 그자요.\"\n\n픽윅 씨는 잘못 본 것이 아니었다. 바퀴가 튀긴 진흙으로 온통 뒤덮인 징글 씨의 얼굴이 그의 마차 창문에 또렷이 보였다. 그리고 마부들을 향해 격렬하게 흔들어 대는 그의 팔짓은, 그가 그들에게 더욱 힘을 내라고 부추기고 있음을 나타냈다.\n\n긴장감은 극에 달했다. 들판과 나무와 산울타리가 회오리바람 같은 속도로 그들 곁을 휙휙 스쳐 지나가는 듯했으니, 그만큼 그들이 내달리는 속도가 빨랐다. 그들은 앞선 마차의 바로 옆에 바짝 따라붙었다. 바퀴 소리의 소란을 뚫고도 소년들을 다그치는 징글의 목소리가 또렷이 들렸다. 워들 노인은 분노와 흥분으로 입에 거품을 물었다. 그는 악당이니 악한이니 하는 욕설을 수십 마디 내질렀고, 주먹을 불끈 쥐어 자기 분노의 대상을 향해 표나게 흔들어 댔다. 그러나 징글 씨는 경멸하는 미소로만 응수했고, 그의 위협에는 승리의 함성으로 답했으니, 그의 말들이 더욱 거세진 채찍과 박차에 응하여 한층 빠른 질주로 박차고 나가, 추격자들을 뒤에 떨궈 버렸다.\n\n픽윅 씨가 막 고개를 안으로 들이고, 외치느라 기진맥진한 워들 씨도 그렇게 한 참이었는데, 엄청난 덜컹거림에 두 사람이 마차 앞쪽으로 내동댕이쳐졌다. 갑작스레 쿵 하는 충격—요란한 와장창 소리—바퀴 하나가 떨어져 굴러가고, 마차가 뒤집어졌다.\n\n당황과 혼란의 아주 짧은 몇 초가 지나는 동안에는 말들이 날뛰는 것과 유리가 깨지는 것밖에 분간할 수 없었는데, 픽윅 씨는 마차의 잔해 속에서 자신이 거칠게 끌려 나오는 것을 느꼈다. 그리고 발을 딛고 일어서, 안경의 쓸모를 적잖이 방해하던 외투 자락에서 머리를 빼내자마자, 이 사태의 전모가 그의 눈앞에 드러났다.\n\n워들 노인이 모자도 없이, 옷은 여러 군데 찢긴 채 그의 곁에 서 있었고, 마차의 파편들이 그들 발치에 흩어져 있었다. 가죽끈을 끊는 데 성공한 마부 소년들은, 진흙으로 일그러지고 험한 질주에 흐트러진 몰골로 말들의 머리맡에 서 있었다. 백 야드쯤 앞에는 다른 마차가 와장창 소리를 듣고 멈춰 서 있었다. 마부들은 저마다 얼굴 가득 활짝 웃음을 띤 채 안장 위에서 상대편을 구경하고 있었고, 징글 씨는 마차 창문에서 그 잔해를 역력히 흡족한 표정으로 바라보고 있었다. 날이 막 밝아 오고 있어, 잿빛 아침 빛에 온 광경이 완연히 드러나 보였다.\n\n\"여보시오!\" 뻔뻔스러운 징글이 외쳤다. \"다친 사람 없소?—나이 지긋한 신사분들—가벼운 몸도 아니고—위험한 일이지—아주.\"\n\n\"이 악당 놈,\" 워들이 고함쳤다.\n\n\"하! 하!\" 징글이 대답했다. 그러고는 알겠다는 듯 한쪽 눈을 찡긋하고 엄지손가락으로 마차 안쪽을 가리키며 덧붙였다. \"이봐요—그 여자분은 아주 잘 있소—안부 전해 달라더군—부디 수고로이 마시라고—터피한테 사랑한다 전해 주오—뒤에 매달려 타지 않겠소?—몰아라, 얘들아.\"\n\n마부들이 다시 제 자세를 갖추니, 마차가 덜커덕거리며 내달렸고, 징글 씨는 마차 창문에서 흰 손수건을 조롱하듯 나풀나풀 흔들어 댔다.\n\n이 모험 전체에서, 마차가 뒤집힌 일조차도, 픽윅 씨의 차분하고 한결같은 성정의 흐름을 흐트러뜨리지는 못했다. 그러나 자기의 충실한 추종자에게서 먼저 돈을 빌려 가고, 그런 다음 그의 이름을 \"터피\"라고 줄여 부르는 그 악랄함만은, 그가 차분히 견딜 수 있는 정도를 넘어섰다. 그는 숨을 거칠게 몰아쉬며 안경테 끝까지 새빨개진 채로, 천천히 그리고 힘주어 말했다.\n\n\"내 다시 저자를 만나는 날에는, 기필코—\"\n\n\"그래요, 그래,\" 워들이 가로막았다. \"다 좋은 말이오. 하지만 우리가 여기 서서 이러쿵저러쿵하는 사이에, 저들은 혼인 허가증을 받아 런던에서 결혼해 버릴 거요.\"\n\n픽윅 씨는 말을 멈추고, 복수심을 병에 담아 마개를 단단히 막았다. \"다음 구간까지 얼마나 되느냐?\" 워들 씨가 소년들 중 하나에게 물었다.\n\n\"육 마일이지, 안 그래, 톰?\"\n\n\"그보다 좀 더 되구먼.\"\n\n\"육 마일보다 좀 더 됩니다, 나리.\"\n\n\"어쩔 수 없군,\" 워들이 말했다. \"걸어가야겠소, 픽윅.\"\n\n\"별 도리가 없지요,\" 그 참으로 위대한 인물이 대답했다.\n\n그리하여 소년 하나를 말에 태워 앞서 보내 새 마차와 말을 구해 오게 하고, 다른 하나는 뒤에 남겨 부서진 마차를 지키게 한 뒤, 픽윅 씨와 워들 씨는 사내답게 걷기 시작했으니, 먼저 숄을 목에 두르고 모자를 푹 눌러써서, 잠시 잦아들었다가 다시 억수같이 퍼붓기 시작한 빗줄기를 되도록 피하려 했다.\n\n런던에는 옛 여관이 여럿 있으니, 한때는 이름난 역마차들의 본거지였던 곳들이다. 그 시절에는 마차가 요즘보다 한결 점잖고 엄숙한 태도로 여행을 수행하였으나, 지금은 시골 짐마차들이 머물고 짐을 부치는 곳 정도로 영락하고 말았다. 개량된 런던 거리에 당당한 정면을 곧추세운 골든 크로스니 불 앤드 마우스니 하는 여관들 가운데서 이런 고풍스러운 객사를 찾으려 한다면 독자는 헛수고만 하게 될 것이다. 이런 옛 장소를 만나려거든 도시의 좀 더 후미진 구역으로 발길을 돌려야 하니, 그곳 한적한 모퉁이에 여러 곳이 아직도 남아, 둘레를 에워싼 근대적 혁신들 한가운데서 일종의 음울한 견실함을 지니고 서 있는 것을 보게 될 것이다.\n\n특히 버러 지역에는 아직도 대여섯 곳의 옛 여관이 남아 있어, 외관을 변함없이 보존한 채 공공 개량의 열풍과 사적 투기의 잠식을 똑같이 면하였다. 그것들은 크고 어수선하고 기묘한 옛 건물들로, 복도와 통로와 계단이 갖추어져 있는데, 그것들이 어찌나 넓고 고색창연한지, 우리가 언젠가 유령 이야기를 백 가지쯤 지어내야 하는 통탄할 처지에 몰린다 하더라도—그리고 세상이 옛 런던 다리와 그 인근 서리 쪽 일대에 얽힌 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 진실한 전설들을 다 써 버릴 만큼 오래 존속한다 하더라도—그 재료가 되기에 충분할 정도였다.\n\n지난 장에서 이야기한 사건들이 있은 다음 날 이른 아침, 이런 여관들 가운데 하나—다름 아닌 그 이름난 화이트 하트—의 마당에서 한 사내가 부지런히 부츠 한 켤레의 흙을 솔질해 떨어내고 있었다. 그는 거친 줄무늬 조끼를 입었는데, 소매는 검은 옥양목이고 단추는 푸른 유리로 되어 있었으며, 황갈색 반바지에 각반을 둘렀다. 새빨간 손수건을 아주 헐겁고 아무렇게나 목에 둘렀고, 낡은 흰 모자를 머리 한쪽에 무심히 걸쳐 놓았다. 그의 앞에는 두 줄로 부츠가 놓여 있었으니, 한 줄은 닦은 것이고 다른 줄은 더러운 것이었으며, 닦은 줄에 한 켤레를 보탤 때마다 그는 일손을 멈추고 그 성과를 흐뭇한 기색으로 바라보았다.\n\n그 마당에는 큰 역마차 여관의 일반적인 특징인 그 부산함과 활기가 조금도 없었다. 큼직한 짐마차 서너 대가 저마다 그 너른 차양 아래 보통 집 이층 창문 높이쯤 되는 짐 더미를 싣고서, 마당 한쪽 끝에 걸쳐진 높다란 지붕 밑에 들이박혀 있었다. 그리고 또 한 대—아마 그날 아침에 길을 떠날 모양이었다—는 빈터로 끌어내져 있었다. 낡고 투박한 난간을 두른 이층짜리 침실 회랑이 흩어진 마당의 두 면을 둘러싸고 있었고, 그에 맞추어 이층으로 달린 종들이 비를 막아 주는 작은 비탈 지붕 아래 걸려, 술집과 커피실로 통하는 문 위에 매달려 있었다. 이륜마차와 경마차 두세 대가 여기저기 작은 헛간과 차양 아래로 밀어 넣어져 있었고, 마당 저쪽 끝에서 이따금 들려오는 짐말의 무거운 발소리며 사슬 절그럭거리는 소리는, 관심 있는 이라면 누구에게나 마구간이 그쪽에 있음을 알려 주었다. 거기에다 작업복을 입은 소년 몇이 무거운 꾸러미며 양모 포대며, 그 밖에 짚 더미 위에 흩어져 놓인 물건들 위에 누워 잠들어 있더라고 덧붙인다면, 바로 그 문제의 아침 버러 하이 스트리트 화이트 하트 여관 마당의 전반적인 모습은 필요한 만큼 충분히 묘사한 셈이 된다.\n\n종 가운데 하나가 요란하게 울리더니, 위층 침실 회랑에 말쑥한 객실 하녀가 나타났다. 그녀는 어느 문을 똑똑 두드리고 안에서 무언가 부탁을 받고는, 난간 너머로 소리쳐 불렀다—\n\n\"샘!\"\n\n\"왜 그래.\" 흰 모자를 쓴 사내가 대답했다.\n\n\"이십이 호 손님이 부츠 달래.\"\n\n\"이십이 호한테 물어봐, 지금 가질 건지 아니면 받을 때까지 기다릴 건지.\" 하는 것이 대답이었다.\n\n\"이봐, 바보같이 굴지 좀 마, 샘.\" 하녀가 어르듯 말했다. \"그 신사분이 부츠를 당장 원하신다니까.\"\n\n\"흥, 댁은 정말이지 음악회에나 어울릴 참한 아가씨로구만.\" 부츠 닦이가 말했다. \"이 부츠들 좀 봐—열한 켤레야. 게다가 나무다리 짚은 육 호 손님 신발 한 짝도 있고. 이 열한 켤레는 여덟 시 반에 가져갈 거고, 신발 한 짝은 아홉 시에 가져갈 거야. 이십이 호가 뭐길래 다른 것들을 다 제쳐 놓으라는 거야? 안 되지, 안 돼. 잭 케치가 사람들 목 매달 때 한 말마따나, 정해진 차례대로 해야지. 기다리시게 해서 죄송합니다, 손님, 곧 댁 차례를 봐 드리겠습니다.\"\n\n이렇게 말하면서 흰 모자를 쓴 사내는 한층 더 정성을 들여 긴 부츠 한 짝에 일을 시작했다.\n\n또 한 번 종이 요란하게 울렸다. 그러자 부산스러운 화이트 하트의 늙은 여주인이 맞은편 회랑에 모습을 드러냈다.\n\n\"샘.\" 여주인이 외쳤다. \"그 게으르고 빈둥거리는—아니, 샘—오, 거기 있구나. 왜 대답을 안 해?\"\n\n\"마님 말씀 다 끝나기도 전에 대답하는 건 점잖지 못한 일이라서요.\" 샘이 퉁명스럽게 대꾸했다.\n\n\"여기, 이 신발 당장 닦아서 십칠 호한테 갖다 줘. 일층 오 호 전용 응접실로 가져가.\"\n\n여주인은 숙녀용 신발 한 켤레를 마당으로 휙 던지고는 부산하게 가 버렸다.\n\n\"오 호라.\" 샘이 신발을 집어 들며 말했다. 그리고 주머니에서 분필 한 토막을 꺼내, 신발 밑창에 행선지를 적어 두었다—\"숙녀용 신발에 전용 응접실이라! 짐마차로 오신 건 아니겠군.\"\n\n\"오늘 아침 일찍 오셨어.\" 아직도 회랑 난간에 기대어 있던 하녀가 외쳤다. \"어떤 신사분하고 삯마차를 타고 오셨는데, 부츠를 원하는 게 바로 그 신사야. 그러니 어서 닦는 게 좋을걸, 그게 전부야.\"\n\n\"진작 그렇게 말하지 그랬어.\" 샘이 크게 분개하며, 앞에 쌓인 더미에서 문제의 부츠를 골라냈다. \"나야 그 사람이 으레 삼 펜스짜리 손님 중 하나인 줄로만 알았지. 전용 객실이라! 게다가 숙녀까지! 그 사람이 정말 신사라면, 심부름값은 빼고도 하루에 일 실링 값어치는 되겠는걸.\"\n\n이렇게 고무적인 생각에 자극받아, 새뮤얼 씨는 어찌나 신명나게 솔질을 해 댔던지, 몇 분 만에 그 부츠와 신발은—화이트 하트에서는 데이 앤드 마틴 구두약을 썼으니, 그 친절한 워런 씨의 영혼에 시샘을 불러일으켰을 만한 광택을 내며—오 호의 문 앞에 당도하였다.\n* 잭 케치: 17세기 영국의 악명 높은 처형 집행인. 이후 사형 집행인의 대명사가 되었다.\n\n\"들어오게.\" 샘이 문을 두드리자 한 남자의 목소리가 대답했다. 샘은 정중하게 절을 하고는, 아침 식사를 하며 앉아 있는 한 숙녀와 신사 앞으로 걸어 들어갔다. 그는 부지런히 신사의 부츠를 그 발치 좌우에 놓고, 숙녀의 신발을 그 발치 좌우에 놓은 뒤, 뒷걸음질로 문 쪽으로 물러섰다.\n\n\"부츠.\" 신사가 말했다.\n\n\"네.\" 샘이 문을 닫고는, 손은 자물쇠 손잡이에 댄 채 대답했다.\n\n\"자네 혹시 아나—거 뭐라더라—닥터스 커먼스 말일세?\"\n\n\"네, 알지요.\"\n\n\"어디 있나?\"\n\n\"폴 교회 뜰입니다. 마차 다니는 쪽으로 나지막한 아치 통로가 있고, 한쪽 모퉁이엔 책방, 다른 쪽엔 호텔, 그 가운데에 허가증 호객꾼 둘이 있지요.\"\n\n\"허가증 호객꾼이라!\" 신사가 말했다.\n\n\"허가증 호객꾼이요.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"흰 앞치마를 두른 사내 둘인데—들어가시면 모자를 들어 인사하면서 '허가증요, 나리, 허가증?' 한답니다. 묘한 작자들이지요, 그 주인들까지도요, 나리—올드 베일리 변호사들 말입니다—틀림없이요.\"\n\n\"그자들이 뭘 하나?\" 신사가 물었다.\n\n\"하다니요! 나리, 댁이요! 그게 제일 나쁜 것도 아니에요. 그자들은 영감님들 머릿속에 꿈에도 생각 못 한 것들을 집어넣지요. 우리 아버지로 말씀드리면, 마부였습니다. 홀아비셨는데, 뭐든 못 할 게 없을 만큼 살이 쪘지요—참말이지 유별나게 뚱뚱했어요. 그 마누라가 죽으면서 사백 파운드를 남겼습니다. 아버지는 변호사를 만나 그 돈을 찾으려고 커먼스로 내려갔지요—아주 멋을 부리고요—긴 부츠를 신고, 단춧구멍엔 꽃다발을 꽂고, 챙 넓은 모자에 초록 숄을 두르고, 영락없는 신사였어요. 아치 통로를 지나며 그 돈을 어떻게 굴릴까 궁리하는데—호객꾼이 다가와 모자를 들고 '허가증요, 나리, 허가증?' 하더랍니다.—'그게 뭔가?' 아버지가 묻지요.—'허가증입니다요, 나리.' 하니까—'무슨 허가증?' 아버지가 묻고—'결혼 허가증이지요.' 호객꾼이 말합니다.—'젠장맞을,' 아버지가 말하길, '그건 생각도 못 했군.'—'제 보기엔 나리께 하나 필요하실 듯한데요.' 호객꾼이 말합니다. 아버지가 걸음을 멈추고 잠깐 생각하더니—'아니야,' 하시며, '제기랄, 난 너무 늙었어. 게다가 몸집이 몇 치수는 너무 크고.' 하셨지요.—'천만에요, 나리.' 호객꾼이 말합니다.—'그렇게 생각하나?' 아버지가 묻고—'틀림없이요.' 호객꾼이 말하길, '지난 월요일엔 나리보다 두 배는 큰 신사분도 결혼시켜 드렸는걸요.'—'정말인가?' 아버지가 말합니다.—'그럼요, 정말입니다.' 호객꾼이 말하길, '나리는 그분에 비하면 애기지요—이쪽입니다, 나리, 이쪽이요!' 하더랍니다. 그래서 정말로 아버지는, 풍금쟁이 뒤를 따르는 길든 원숭이처럼 그 사내 뒤를 따라, 어느 작은 안쪽 사무실로 들어갔지요. 거기엔 서기 하나가 더러운 서류와 양철 상자들 틈에 앉아 바쁜 척하고 있었어요. '제가 진술서를 작성하는 동안 자리에 앉으시지요, 나리.' 변호사가 말합니다.—'고맙소.' 아버지가 말하고는 앉아서, 두 눈을 부릅뜨고 입은 떡 벌린 채 상자들에 적힌 이름들을 멀거니 쳐다봤지요. '성함이 어찌 되십니까, 나리?' 변호사가 묻습니다.—'토니 웰러요.' 아버지가 말하지요.—'교구는요?' 변호사가 묻습니다. '벨 소비지요.' 아버지가 말합니다. 마차를 몰고 와서 거기 묵었던 데다, 교구에 대해선 아무것도 몰랐으니까요.—'그럼 부인 되실 분 성함은요?' 변호사가 묻습니다. 아버지는 그만 어안이 벙벙해졌지요. '내 알 게 뭐요.' 하셨어요.—'모르신다고요!' 변호사가 말합니다.—'댁이나 나나 모르긴 마찬가지지.' 아버지가 말하길, '그건 나중에 적어 넣으면 안 되겠소?'—'안 됩니다!' 변호사가 말합니다.—'그럼 좋소.' 아버지가 잠깐 생각하더니, '클라크 부인이라고 적으시오.'—'어느 클라크요?' 변호사가 펜을 잉크에 적시며 묻습니다.—'도킹의 그랜비 후작 여관, 수전 클라크요.' 아버지가 말하지요. '내가 청하면 날 받아 줄 거요. 아마—한 번도 그 여자한테 말을 꺼낸 적은 없지만, 받아 줄 게 틀림없소.' 허가증이 작성됐고, 정말로 그 여자가 아버지를 받아 줬지요. 그뿐인가요, 지금도 데리고 살아요. 그런데 난 그 사백 파운드 중 한 푼도 못 받았으니, 더 안된 일이지요. 죄송합니다, 나리.\" 샘이 이야기를 끝내고 말했다. \"하지만 이 분한 얘기만 꺼냈다 하면, 저는 바퀴에 기름칠한 새 손수레처럼 줄줄 굴러가 버린답니다.\" 이렇게 말하고는, 더 시킬 일이 있는지 보려고 잠깐 멈추었다가, 샘은 방을 나갔다.\n\n\"아홉 시 반—마침 그 시각이로군—당장 떠나자.\" 신사가 말했으니, 그가 징글 씨임은 굳이 소개할 필요도 없겠다.\n\n\"떠나요—어디로요?\" 노처녀 고모가 교태를 부리며 말했다.\n\n\"허가증 받으러, 천사 중에서도 가장 사랑스러운 이여—교회에 통고하고—내일이면 당신을 내 사람이라 부르는 거요.\" 징글 씨가 말하며 노처녀 고모의 손을 꼭 쥐었다.\n\n\"허가증요!\" 레이철이 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다.\n\n\"허가증.\" 징글 씨가 되뇌었다—\n\n\"허가증 받으러 부랴부랴 서두르고, 부랴부랴 댕그랑댕그랑 돌아오리.\"\n\n\"어쩌면 그렇게 말이 술술 나와요.\" 레이철이 말했다.\n\n\"말이 술술 나온다—우리가 맺어진 뒤의 시간, 날, 주, 달, 해에 비하면 아무것도 아니지—술술 나온다—그것들은 훨훨 날아갈 거요—쏜살같이—슬그머니—증기기관—천 마력—이건 거기 댈 것도 아니야.\"\n\n\"내일 아침 전에—전에 결혼할 순 없나요?\" 레이철이 물었다.\n\n\"불가능하오—안 되오—교회에 통고하고—오늘 허가증을 맡겨 두고—예식은 내일 치르는 거요.\"\n\n\"오빠가 우릴 찾아낼까 봐 너무도 무서워요!\" 레이철이 말했다.\n\n\"찾아내다니—말도 안 되오—마차가 부서지는 통에 너무 놀라서—게다가—극도로 조심해서—역마차도 포기하고—걸어서—삯마차를 잡고—버러로 왔으니—세상에서 그가 마지막으로 찾아볼 곳이지—하하!—기막힌 묘안이오—그렇고말고.\"\n\n\"오래 걸리지 마세요.\" 노처녀가 다정하게 말하자, 징글 씨는 우그러진 모자를 머리에 눌러썼다.\n\n\"당신 곁을 오래 비운다니?—잔인한 매혹의 여인이여.\" 그리고 징글 씨는 장난스럽게 노처녀 고모에게 깡충 다가가, 그 입술에 정숙한 입맞춤을 찍고는, 춤추듯 방을 나갔다.\n\n\"고운 분!\" 그의 뒤로 문이 닫히자 노처녀가 말했다.\n\n\"별난 늙은 여자로군.\" 징글 씨가 복도를 걸어 내려가며 말했다.\n\n우리 인간 종족의 배신을 곱씹는 일은 괴로운 노릇이다. 그러므로 우리는 징글 씨가 닥터스 커먼스로 향하는 길에 했던 명상의 실마리를 좇지는 않으련다. 우리의 목적을 위해서는 다만 이것만 이야기하면 족하리라. 그는 그 마법에 걸린 영역의 입구를 지키는 흰 앞치마 두른 용들의 함정을 피해, 무사히 부주교 사무소에 다다랐고, 캔터베리 대주교가 그의 \"신실하고 사랑스러운 알프레드 징글과 레이철 워들에게 보내는 인사\"라는, 더없이 듣기 좋은 양피지 문서를 손에 넣고는, 그 신비로운 서류를 주머니에 조심스레 넣고서, 의기양양하게 버러로 발길을 되돌렸다.\n\n그가 아직 화이트 하트로 돌아가는 길에 있을 때, 통통한 신사 둘과 마른 신사 하나가 마당으로 들어와, 몇 가지 물어볼 만한 권한 있는 자를 찾아 두리번거렸다. 마침 새뮤얼 웰러 씨는 그때 채색한 긴 부츠 한 켤레를 광내는 일에 매여 있었으니, 그것은 어느 농부의 소유물이었다. 그 농부는 버러 시장에서의 노고를 마친 뒤, 차가운 쇠고기 두세 파운드와 포터 한두 잔의 가벼운 점심으로 원기를 돋우고 있었다. 마른 신사가 곧장 그에게 다가왔다.\n\n\"여보게.\" 마른 신사가 말했다.\n\n'당신은 공짜 충고 패거리 중 하나로군.' 샘은 속으로 생각했다. '안 그러면 갑자기 나한테 이렇게 살갑게 굴 리가 없지.' 하지만 그는 다만—\"네, 무슨 일이신지요.\" 하고 말했다.\n\n\"여보게.\" 마른 신사가 비위를 맞추듯 헛기침을 하며 말했다. \"지금 여기 묵는 사람이 많은가? 꽤 바쁜가? 응?\"\n\n샘은 묻는 이를 슬쩍 훔쳐보았다. 그는 자그마하고 바싹 마른 사내로, 거뭇하고 쪼그라든 얼굴에, 작고 잠시도 가만있지 못하는 검은 눈을 가졌는데, 그 눈은 작은 캐묻는 코의 양옆에서 연신 깜박이고 반짝이며, 마치 그 코하고 끝없이 까꿍 놀이라도 하는 듯했다. 그는 온통 검은 옷차림에, 부츠는 눈만큼이나 번들거렸고, 낮은 흰 넥타이를 매고, 주름 장식 달린 깨끗한 셔츠를 입었다. 회중시계 주머니에서 금 시곗줄과 인장들이 늘어져 있었다. 그는 검은 새끼 염소 가죽 장갑을 손에 끼지 않고 손에 들고 있었으며, 말하면서는, 으레 골치 아픈 난제들을 척척 내놓는 데 익숙한 사람 같은 태도로, 손목을 외투 자락 밑으로 찔러 넣었다.\n\n\"꽤 바쁜가, 응?\" 작은 사내가 말했다.\n\n\"아, 그저 그렇습죠, 나리.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"파산할 일도 없고, 큰돈을 벌 일도 없지요. 케이퍼 없이 삶은 양고기를 먹고, 쇠고기가 있을 땐 서양고추냉이 따위는 거들떠보지도 않습니다요.\"\n\n\"아하.\" 작은 사내가 말했다. \"자네 익살꾼이로군, 그렇지?\"\n\n\"제 맏형이 그 병으로 고생했지요.\" 샘이 말했다. \"옮는 병인가 봐요—제가 형이랑 한방에서 잤거든요.\"\n\n\"여기는 참 별스러운 옛 건물이로군.\" 작은 사내가 둘레를 둘러보며 말했다.\n\n\"오신다고 미리 기별만 주셨어도 손봐 두었을 텐데요.\" 태연자약한 샘이 대꾸했다.\n\n작은 사내는 이 잇따른 퇴짜에 다소 당황한 모양이었다. 그와 통통한 신사 둘 사이에 잠깐 상의가 오갔다. 상의가 끝나자, 작은 사내는 길쭉한 은제 갑에서 코담배를 한 줌 집어 들고는, 막 대화를 다시 시작하려는 참인 듯했다. 그때 통통한 신사 중 하나—자애로운 얼굴에다 안경 한 벌과 검은 각반 한 벌까지 갖춘 이가 끼어들었다—\n\n\"실은 이런 일이오.\" 자애로운 신사가 말했다. \"여기 내 친구가(다른 통통한 신사를 가리키며) 자네에게 반 기니를 주겠다는 거요, 자네가 한두 가지만 대답해 준다면—\"\n\n\"이런, 친애하는 선생—친애하는 선생.\" 작은 사내가 말했다. \"제발, 좀 봐주십시오—친애하는 선생, 이런 경우에 가장 먼저 지켜야 할 원칙은 이것입니다. 일을 전문가의 손에 맡기셨다면, 그 일이 진행되는 데 결코 끼어들어서는 안 됩니다. 그에게 전적인 신뢰를 두셔야 해요. 정말이지, 미스터—\" 그는 다른 통통한 신사를 돌아보며 말했다. \"친구분 성함을 깜빡했군요.\"\n\n\"픽윅이오.\" 워들 씨가 말했으니, 바로 그 유쾌한 인물이 다름 아니었다.\n\n\"아, 픽윅—정말이지 미스터 픽윅, 친애하는 선생, 실례지만—저는 법정의 벗으로서 선생의 어떤 사적인 제안이든 기꺼이 받겠습니다만, 반 기니를 제시하는 따위의 환심 사는 논거를 들어 이 사건에 대한 제 처리에 끼어드시는 것이 부당함은 아셔야 합니다. 정말이지, 친애하는 선생, 정말로요.\" 그러고는 작은 사내는 논쟁이라도 하듯 코담배를 한 줌 들이켜고, 자못 심오한 표정을 지었다.\n* 법정의 벗: 라틴어 amicus curiae. 소송 당사자가 아니면서 법원에 의견을 제출하는 자. 그 뒤의 '환심 사는 논거'는 라틴어 ad captandum으로, 대중의 환심을 사려는 그럴듯한 논법을 뜻한다.\n\n\"제 바람은 다만, 선생,\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"이 매우 불쾌한 일을 되도록 빨리 매듭짓자는 것뿐이었소.\"\n\n\"지당하십니다—지당하시고말고요.\" 작은 사내가 말했다.\n\n\"그런 뜻에서,\" 픽윅 씨가 말을 이었다. \"나는 사람을 겪어 본 내 경험상 어떤 경우에든 가장 성공할 법한 논거를 써 본 것이오.\"\n\n\"예, 예.\" 작은 사내가 말했다. \"아주 좋습니다, 참으로 좋아요. 하지만 그건 제게 먼저 귀띔해 주셨어야지요. 친애하는 선생, 전문가에게 두어야 할 신뢰가 얼마나 큰 것인지를 선생께서 모르실 리 없다고 저는 확신합니다. 그 점에 무슨 전거가 필요하시다면, 친애하는 선생, 그 유명한 사건, 반웰 대—를 참조하시도록 일러 드리지요—\"\n\n\"조지 반웰은 신경 쓰지 마쇼.\" 이 짧은 대화 내내 어리둥절한 청자로 가만히 있던 샘이 끼어들었다. \"그자 사건이 어떤 거였는지야 다들 알지요. 하기야 내 생각으론, 잘 들으슈, 그 젊은 여편네야말로 그자보다 곱절은 더 목매달려 마땅했지만 말이오. 어쨌든, 그건 여기서 할 얘긴 아니고요. 댁들은 나더러 반 기니를 받으라는 거지요. 좋습니다, 받지요. 그보다 더 공정하게 말할 순 없잖소, 안 그렇소, 선생?\"(픽윅 씨가 빙긋 웃었다.) \"그럼 다음 물음은, 유령을 본 사내가 한 말마따나, 대체 나한테 뭘 바라는 거요?\"\n\n\"우리가 알고 싶은 건—\" 워들 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"이런, 친애하는 선생—친애하는 선생.\" 부산스러운 작은 사내가 가로막았다.\n\n워들 씨는 어깨를 으쓱하고는 입을 다물었다.\n\n\"우리가 알고 싶은 건,\" 작은 사내가 엄숙하게 말했다. \"그리고 이걸 자네한테 묻는 건, 안에 있는 이들의 경각심을 일깨우지 않으려는 뜻이니—지금 이 집에 묵고 있는 게 누군지 알고 싶네.\"\n\n\"이 집에 누가 있느냐고요!\" 샘이 말했다. 그의 머릿속에서 투숙객들은 늘 그가 직접 맡아 보는 그 의복의 특정 품목으로 떠올랐다. \"육 호엔 나무다리가 하나 있고, 십삼 호엔 헤시안 부츠 한 켤레가 있고, 상인용 객실엔 단화 두 켤레가 있고, 바 안쪽 아늑한 방엔 이 채색한 긴 부츠가 있고, 커피실엔 긴 부츠 다섯 켤레가 더 있지요.\"\n\n\"그뿐인가?\" 작은 사내가 말했다.\n\n\"잠깐만요.\" 샘이 문득 생각이 났다는 듯 대답했다. \"참, 닳을 대로 닳은 웰링턴 부츠 한 켤레랑 숙녀용 신발 한 켤레가 오 호에 있군요.\"\n\n\"무슨 신발인가?\" 워들이 다급히 물었다. 그는 픽윅 씨와 더불어 그 별난 투숙객 목록에 어리둥절해하고 있던 참이었다.\n\n\"시골 제품입니다.\" 샘이 대답했다.\n\n\"제조자 이름은?\"\n\n\"브라운이요.\"\n\n\"어디 거?\"\n\n\"머글턴이지요.\"\n\"그들이오.\" 워들이 외쳤다. \"맙소사, 찾았어.\"\n\n\"쉿!\" 샘이 말했다. \"그 웰링턴 부츠는 닥터스 커먼스에 갔습니다.\"\n\n\"설마.\" 작은 사내가 말했다.\n\n\"맞아요, 허가증 받으러요.\"\n\n\"늦지 않았어.\" 워들이 외쳤다. \"그 방을 안내해 주게. 한순간도 지체할 수 없네.\"\n\n\"제발, 친애하는 선생—제발.\" 작은 사내가 말했다. \"신중히, 신중히.\" 그는 주머니에서 붉은 비단 지갑을 꺼내고는, 소브린 한 닢을 꺼내며 샘을 뚫어지게 바라보았다.\n\n샘은 의미심장하게 씩 웃었다.\n\n\"우리를 알리지 말고 곧장 그 방으로 안내하게.\" 작은 사내가 말했다. \"그러면 이건 자네 거야.\"\n\n샘은 채색한 긴 부츠를 한쪽 구석으로 던져 놓고는, 앞장서서 어두운 복도를 지나 넓은 계단을 올라갔다. 그는 두 번째 복도 끝에서 걸음을 멈추고 손을 내밀었다.\n\n\"여기 있네.\" 변호사가 안내자의 손에 돈을 얹어 주며 속삭였다.\n\n샘은 몇 걸음 앞으로 나아갔고, 두 친구와 그들의 법률 고문이 뒤를 따랐다. 그가 어느 문 앞에서 멈춰 섰다.\n\n\"이 방인가?\" 작은 신사가 나직이 물었다.\n\n샘이 그렇다는 듯 고개를 끄덕였다.\n\n워들 영감이 문을 열었다. 그리고 셋 모두 방으로 걸어 들어갔으니, 바로 그 순간 막 돌아온 징글 씨가 노처녀 고모에게 허가증을 꺼내 보이던 참이었다.\n\n노처녀가 날카로운 비명을 지르며 의자에 몸을 던지고는 두 손으로 얼굴을 가렸다. 징글 씨는 허가증을 구겨 외투 주머니에 쑤셔 넣었다. 달갑잖은 방문객들이 방 한가운데로 나아갔다.\n\n\"네놈—네놈은 참 멋진 악당이로구나, 안 그러냐?\" 워들이 격분으로 숨을 헐떡이며 외쳤다.\n\n\"친애하는 선생, 친애하는 선생.\" 작은 사내가 모자를 탁자에 내려놓으며 말했다. \"제발, 생각하십시오—제발요. 명예 훼손에다, 손해 배상 소송감입니다. 진정하세요, 친애하는 선생, 제발—\"\n\n\"네 어찌 감히 내 집에서 누이를 끌어냈느냐?\" 노인이 말했다.\n\n\"예—예—아주 좋습니다.\" 작은 신사가 말했다. \"그건 물으셔도 됩니다. 네 어찌 감히 그랬느냐, 이놈?—응, 이놈?\"\n\n\"넌 대체 뭐 하는 놈이냐?\" 징글 씨가 어찌나 험악한 어조로 물었던지, 작은 신사는 저도 모르게 한두 걸음 물러섰다.\n\n\"이분이 누구냐고, 이 악당아.\" 워들이 끼어들었다. \"그레이즈 인의 퍼커 변호사, 내 변호사다. 퍼커, 이놈을 기소하게—고발해—내가—내가—내가 이놈을 파멸시키고 말겠어. 그리고 너는,\" 워들 씨가 누이에게 홱 돌아서며 말을 이었다. \"너, 레이철, 이젠 철이 들 만한 나이에, 떠돌이 놈하고 달아나서, 집안을 욕보이고, 제 신세를 망치다니, 대체 무슨 생각이냐? 보닛을 쓰고 돌아가자. 거기 당장 삯마차를 불러오고, 이 부인 계산서도 가져와, 알겠나—알겠어?\"\n\n\"알겠습니다, 나리.\" 샘이 대답했다. 그는 워들이 사납게 종을 울리자 어찌나 잽싸게 응했던지, 그 회담 내내 그의 눈이 열쇠 구멍 바깥에 붙어 있었음을 모르는 이라면 그 민첩함이 놀랍게 보였을 것이다.\n\n\"보닛을 쓰래도.\" 워들이 되풀이했다.\n\n\"그러지 마시오.\" 징글이 말했다. \"방을 나가시오, 선생—여기서 당신이 상관할 일은 없소—이 숙녀는 제 뜻대로 행동할 자유가 있고—스물한 살이 넘었소.\"\n\n\"스물한 살이 넘었다!\" 워들이 경멸하듯 내뱉었다. \"마흔하나도 넘었어!\"\n\n\"안 넘었어요.\" 노처녀 고모가 말했으니, 분개함이 기절하려던 결심을 이겨 버린 것이다.\n\n\"넘었다.\" 워들이 대꾸했다. \"너는 분명 쉰이야, 한 시간이라도 모자랄 게 없어.\"\n\n이 말에 노처녀 고모는 날카로운 비명을 지르고 정신을 잃었다.\n\n\"물 한 잔.\" 인정 많은 픽윅 씨가 여주인을 부르며 말했다.\n\n\"물 한 잔이라뇨!\" 격분한 워들이 말했다. \"양동이를 가져와 통째로 끼얹으시오. 그게 저 여자한테 약이 될 거요. 그래도 싸지.\"\n\n\"어머, 이 짐승 같은 양반!\" 마음씨 고운 여주인이 내뱉었다. \"가엾어라.\" 그러고는 \"자, 이러세요, 착하지—이걸 좀 드세요—약이 될 거예요—그렇게 정신 놓지 마시고요—착한 분이지\" 따위 갖은 말을 연발하며, 여주인은 객실 하녀의 도움을 받아 노처녀 고모의 이마에 식초를 바르고, 손을 두드리고, 코를 간질이고, 코르셋 끈을 풀고, 또 동정심 많은 부인네들이 히스테리를 부려 일으켜 보려고 안간힘 쓰는 숙녀들에게 으레 베푸는 갖가지 회복 처치를 시작했다.\n\n\"마차 준비됐습니다, 나리.\" 샘이 문가에 나타나며 말했다.\n\n\"가자.\" 워들이 외쳤다. \"내가 아래층으로 데려가겠다.\"\n\n이 제안에, 히스테리는 곱절로 격렬하게 도졌다.\n\n여주인은 이 처사에 대해 매우 격한 항의를 늘어놓으려던 참이었고, 이미 워들 씨가 자기를 만물의 주인쯤으로 여기는 거냐며 분개한 물음을 터뜨린 뒤였다. 그때 징글 씨가 끼어들었다—\n\n\"부츠.\" 그가 말했다. \"경관을 불러오게.\"\n\n\"잠깐, 잠깐만요.\" 작은 퍼커 씨가 말했다. \"생각하십시오, 선생, 생각하셔야지요.\"\n\n\"생각 안 할 거요.\" 징글이 대꾸했다. \"이 여자는 제 몸의 주인이니—본인이 원치 않는 한—누가 감히 이 여자를 데려가는지 어디 봅시다.\"\n\n\"전 끌려가지 않을 거예요.\" 노처녀 고모가 중얼거렸다. \"전 그러길 원치 않아요.\"(이 대목에서 끔찍한 발작이 도졌다.)\n\n\"친애하는 선생.\" 작은 사내가 워들 씨와 픽윅 씨를 따로 데려가 나직한 목소리로 말했다. \"친애하는 선생, 우린 매우 난처한 처지에 놓였습니다. 딱한 사건이에요—참으로요. 저도 이보다 더한 경우는 본 적이 없습니다. 하지만 정말이지, 친애하는 선생, 정말로 우리에겐 이 숙녀의 행동을 통제할 권한이 없습니다. 오기 전에 미리 일러 드렸지요, 친애하는 선생, 타협 말고는 기대할 게 없다고요.\"\n\n잠시 침묵이 흘렀다.\n\n\"어떤 종류의 타협을 권하시오?\" 픽윅 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"글쎄요, 친애하는 선생, 우리 친구분께서 불쾌한 처지에 놓이셨지요—매우 그렇습니다. 우린 얼마간의 금전적 손실을 감수하기로 만족해야 합니다.\"\n\n\"이 망신을 당하며, 저 애가, 바보 같은 애지만, 평생을 비참하게 사느니, 어떤 손실이라도 감수하겠소.\" 워들이 말했다.\n\n\"제 생각엔 해낼 수 있을 듯합니다.\" 부산스러운 작은 사내가 말했다. \"징글 씨, 잠깐 우리와 옆방으로 가 주시겠소?\"\n\n징글 씨가 응했고, 넷은 빈방으로 걸어 들어갔다.\n\n\"자, 선생.\" 작은 사내가 문을 조심스레 닫으며 말했다. \"이 일을 매듭지을 방도가 없겠소—이쪽으로 잠깐, 선생—이 창가로요, 선생, 여기선 단둘이 있을 수 있으니—자, 선생, 자, 제발 앉으시오, 선생. 자, 친애하는 선생, 당신과 나 사이의 얘기지만, 우린 아주 잘 알지요, 친애하는 선생, 당신이 이 숙녀의 돈을 노리고 함께 달아났다는 걸 말이오. 찡그리지 마시오, 선생, 찡그리지 마요. 당신과 나 사이의 얘기라고 했잖소, 우린 그걸 알고 있다고. 우린 둘 다 세상물정에 밝은 사람이고, 우리야 잘 알지, 여기 우리 친구들은 그렇지 못하다는 걸—안 그렇소?\"\n\n징글 씨의 얼굴이 차츰 누그러졌다. 그리고 어렴풋이 윙크 비슷한 것이 그의 왼쪽 눈에서 한순간 떨렸다.\n\n\"좋습니다, 아주 좋아요.\" 자기가 남긴 인상을 알아챈 작은 사내가 말했다. \"자, 사실인즉, 몇백 파운드 말고는, 이 숙녀는 어머니가 죽기 전엔 가진 게 거의, 아니 전혀 없소—훌륭한 노부인이지요, 친애하는 선생.\"\n\n\"늙은이지.\" 징글 씨가 짧지만 힘주어 말했다.\n\n\"뭐, 그렇지요.\" 변호사가 가볍게 헛기침을 하며 말했다. \"옳은 말씀입니다, 친애하는 선생, 제법 연로하시지요. 하지만 유서 깊은 가문 출신입니다, 친애하는 선생. 무슨 뜻으로 보나 오래된 집안이지요. 그 가문의 시조는 율리우스 카이사르가 브리튼을 침공할 때 켄트로 들어왔고, 그 이래로 여든다섯까지 못 산 사람이 단 한 명뿐인데, 그자도 헨리 왕 중 하나에게 목이 잘렸지요. 그 노부인이 지금 일흔셋도 안 됐습니다, 친애하는 선생.\" 작은 사내가 말을 멈추고 코담배를 한 줌 들이켰다.\n\n\"그래서요.\" 징글 씨가 외쳤다.\n\n\"그래서 말입니다, 친애하는 선생—코담배는 안 하시지요!—아! 그게 낫지요—돈 드는 버릇이니—그래서 말이오, 친애하는 선생, 당신은 멋진 젊은이에다, 세상물정에 밝은 사람이라, 밑천만 있다면 한밑천 일굴 수 있는 분이지요, 안 그렇소?\"\n\n\"그래서요.\" 징글 씨가 또 말했다.\n\n\"제 말을 알아들으시겠소?\"\n\n\"썩 잘은 모르겠는데.\"\n\n\"이렇게 생각하지 않으시오—자, 친애하는 선생, 당신에게 묻습니다—오십 파운드와 자유가, 워들 양과 막연한 기대보다 낫다고 생각지 않으시오?\"\n\n\"안 되겠소—턱없이 모자라요!\" 징글 씨가 일어서며 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 아니, 친애하는 선생.\" 작은 변호사가 그의 단추를 붙들며 만류했다. \"두둑한 액수요—당신 같은 사람이라면 눈 깜짝할 새에 세 배로 불릴 수 있지—오십 파운드로 할 수 있는 일이 얼마나 많은데요, 친애하는 선생.\"\n\n\"백오십이면 할 수 있는 일이 더 많지요.\" 징글 씨가 냉랭하게 대꾸했다.\n\n\"자, 친애하는 선생, 자질구레한 흥정으로 시간을 허비하진 맙시다.\" 작은 사내가 다시 말했다. \"그럼—그럼—칠십으로 합시다.\"\n\n\"안 되겠소.\" 징글 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"가지 마시오, 친애하는 선생—제발 서두르지 마요.\" 작은 사내가 말했다. \"팔십이오. 자, 당장 수표를 끊어 드리리다.\"\n\n\"안 되겠소.\" 징글 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"자, 친애하는 선생, 자.\" 작은 사내가 여전히 그를 붙들며 말했다. \"그럼 얼마면 되겠는지 말해 보시오.\"\n\n\"돈 드는 일이지요.\" 징글 씨가 말했다. \"주머니에서 나간 돈만—마차 삯이 구 파운드, 허가증이 삼 파운드—그게 십이 파운드—위자료가 백—백십이 파운드—명예를 저버린 값에다—숙녀를 잃은 값까지—\"\n\n\"예, 친애하는 선생, 예.\" 작은 사내가 알겠다는 표정으로 말했다. \"마지막 두 항목은 신경 쓰지 맙시다. 그럼 백십이 파운드인데—백으로 합시다—자.\"\n\n\"이십을 더해서요.\" 징글 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"자, 자, 수표를 끊어 드리리다.\" 작은 사내가 말하고는, 그러려고 탁자 앞에 앉았다.\n\n\"모레 지급으로 끊겠소.\" 작은 사내가 워들 씨 쪽을 흘끗 보며 말했다. \"그동안 우리는 숙녀를 데려갈 수 있고요.\" 워들 씨가 시무룩하게 고개를 끄덕여 동의했다.\n\n\"백 파운드요.\" 작은 사내가 말했다.\n\n\"이십을 더해서요.\" 징글 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"친애하는 선생.\" 작은 사내가 만류했다.\n\n\"줘 버리게.\" 워들 씨가 끼어들었다. \"그리고 보내 버려.\"\n\n수표는 작은 신사가 작성했고, 징글 씨가 주머니에 챙겼다.\n\n\"자, 당장 이 집에서 나가게!\" 워들이 벌떡 일어서며 말했다.\n\n\"친애하는 선생.\" 작은 사내가 다그쳤다.\n\n\"그리고 명심하게.\" 워들 씨가 말했다. \"이런 타협에 응하게 만든 건—내 집안에 대한 염려조차도—아무것도 없었어. 다만 네놈이 그 주머니에 돈이 들어오는 순간, 돈 없을 때보다, 가능만 하다면, 더 빨리 지옥으로 곤두박질치리라는 걸 알았기에 응한 거다—\"\n\n\"친애하는 선생.\" 작은 사내가 또 다그쳤다.\n\n\"잠자코 있게, 퍼커.\" 워들이 다시 말했다. \"방을 나가, 이놈.\"\n\n\"당장 떠나지요.\" 뻔뻔한 징글이 말했다. \"잘 있게, 픽윅.\"\n\n이 대화의 끝 무렵, 이 작품 제목의 첫머리를 이루는 이름의 그 저명한 인물의 얼굴을, 어느 냉정한 구경꾼이 보았더라면, 그 두 눈에서 번득이는 분노의 불길이 안경알을 녹여 버리지 않은 것이 거의 신기하게 여겨졌으리라—그만큼 그의 노여움은 위풍당당했다. 그 악당에게 이름이 불리는 것을 듣자, 그의 콧구멍은 벌름거렸고 두 주먹은 저절로 불끈 쥐어졌다. 하지만 그는 다시금 자신을 억눌렀다—그자를 가루로 만들어 버리지는 않았다.\n\n\"여기.\" 그 철면피 배신자가 픽윅 씨의 발치에 허가증을 휙 내던지며 말을 이었다. \"이름이나 고치쇼—숙녀를 집에 데려가고—터피한테나 줘 버리쇼.\"\n\n픽윅 씨는 철학자였지만, 철학자라 한들 결국 갑옷 입은 인간일 뿐이다. 그 화살은 그에게 닿아, 그의 철학적 갑옷을 꿰뚫고, 바로 그 심장에까지 박혔다. 격노에 미쳐, 그는 잉크병을 미친 듯이 앞으로 내던지고, 자신도 그 뒤를 따라 돌진했다. 그러나 징글 씨는 사라지고 없었고, 그는 자신이 샘의 두 팔에 붙들려 있음을 깨달았다.\n\n\"이런.\" 그 별난 종업원이 말했다. \"나리 고향에선 가구가 싼가 봅니다요. 그거야말로 저절로 써지는 잉크네요. 벽에다 나리 표시를 적어 놨군요, 영감님. 가만 계세요, 나리. 줄행랑쳐서 이쯤이면 버러 저쪽 끝까지 갔을 사내를 쫓아가 봤자 무슨 소용이래요?\"\n\n픽윅 씨의 마음은, 진정으로 위대한 모든 이의 마음이 그렇듯, 깨달음에 열려 있었다. 그는 빠르고 강력한 추론가였다. 잠깐의 성찰만으로도 자신의 분노가 무력함을 깨닫기에 충분했다. 그것은 솟아오른 만큼이나 빠르게 가라앉았다. 그는 숨을 헐떡이며, 친구들을 너그러운 눈길로 둘러보았다.\n\n신의 없는 징글에게 버림받은 것을 워들 양이 알았을 때 뒤이어진 그 비탄을 우리가 이야기해야 할까? 그 가슴 미어지는 광경에 대한 픽윅 씨의 빼어난 묘사를 우리가 발췌해야 할까? 공감하는 인간애의 눈물로 얼룩진 그의 수첩이 우리 앞에 펼쳐져 있으니, 한마디만 하면 그것은 인쇄공의 손에 넘어간다. 하지만 안 될 말이다! 우리는 단호하리라! 그런 고통을 묘사함으로써 대중의 가슴을 쥐어짜지는 않으리라!\n\n이튿날, 두 친구와 버림받은 숙녀는 무겁고 굼뜬 머글턴행 마차를 타고 느릿느릿 서글피 돌아왔다. 그들이 다시 딩글리 델에 다다라 매너 농장 어귀 안에 들어섰을 때는, 여름밤의 음울한 그림자가 사방에 어슴푸레 어둑하게 드리워 있었다.\n\n딩글리 델의 깊은 정적 속에서 조용히 쉬며 보낸 하룻밤과, 이튿날 아침 신선하고 향기로운 공기를 한 시간 동안 들이마신 것은, 픽윅 씨를 최근의 육체적 피로와 정신적 근심의 여파로부터 완전히 회복시켜 주었다. 이 저명한 인물은 친구들과 추종자들로부터 꼬박 이틀 동안 떨어져 있었다. 그래서 그가 이른 산책에서 돌아오는 길에 윈클 씨와 스노드그래스 씨를 마주쳐 그 신사들을 맞이하러 앞으로 나아갔을 때, 그 기쁨과 환희는 평범한 상상력으로는 도무지 헤아릴 수 없을 정도였다. 기쁨은 서로의 것이었다. 픽윅 씨의 환히 빛나는 얼굴을 바라보고서 그런 감정을 느끼지 않을 자가 어디 있겠는가? 하지만 그래도 그의 동료들 위에는 어떤 구름이 드리워져 있는 듯했으니, 그 위대한 인물도 이를 알아차리지 않을 수 없었으되 그 까닭은 도무지 알 수 없었다. 두 사람에게는 무언가 알 수 없는 분위기가 감돌고 있었는데, 그것은 예사롭지 않은 만큼이나 불안을 자아내는 것이었다.\n\n\"그래,\" 픽윅 씨가 추종자들의 손을 맞잡고 따뜻한 환영의 인사를 나눈 뒤 말했다. \"터프먼은 어떻게 지내나?\"\n\n이 질문이 특별히 향한 대상이었던 윈클 씨는 아무 대답도 하지 않았다. 그는 고개를 돌리고 우울한 상념에 잠긴 듯이 보였다.\n\n\"스노드그래스,\" 픽윅 씨가 간절하게 말했다. \"우리 친구는 어떻게 됐나? 혹시 아픈 건 아니겠지?\"\n\n\"아닙니다.\" 스노드그래스 씨가 대답했다. 그러자 창틀에 맺힌 빗방울처럼 그의 감상적인 눈꺼풀 위에 눈물 한 방울이 떨렸다. \"아닙니다. 아픈 건 아닙니다.\"\n\n픽윅 씨는 걸음을 멈추고 두 친구를 차례로 바라보았다.\n\n\"윈클, 스노드그래스,\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"이게 무슨 뜻인가? 우리 친구는 어디 있나? 무슨 일이 일어난 거야? 말하게—간청하네, 애원하네—아니, 명하노니, 말하게.\"\n\n픽윅 씨의 태도에는 거역할 수 없는 엄숙함이—위엄이—깃들어 있었다.\n\n\"그는 떠났습니다.\" 스노드그래스 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"떠나!\" 픽윅 씨가 소리쳤다. \"떠나다니!\"\n\n\"떠났습니다.\" 스노드그래스 씨가 되풀이했다.\n\n\"어디로!\" 픽윅 씨가 외쳤다.\n\n\"저희도 이 편지를 보고 짐작할 수밖에 없습니다.\" 스노드그래스 씨가 주머니에서 편지 한 통을 꺼내 친구의 손에 쥐여 주며 대답했다. \"어제 아침, 워들 씨로부터 당신이 그의 누이와 함께 밤에 돌아오리라는 편지가 왔을 때, 전날 하루 종일 우리 친구를 덮고 있던 우울함이 더욱 짙어지는 것이 눈에 띄었습니다. 그는 곧 사라졌습니다. 하루 종일 보이지 않았고, 저녁이 되어 머글턴의 크라운 여관 마부가 이 편지를 가져왔습니다. 아침에 그에게 맡기면서, 밤이 되기 전에는 절대로 전하지 말라고 단단히 일러두었다더군요.\"\n\n픽윅 씨는 그 편지를 펼쳤다. 그것은 친구의 필체로 쓰여 있었고, 내용은 다음과 같았다.\n\n\"친애하는 픽윅에게,—자네는, 나의 친애하는 벗이여, 보통 사람들이 극복하지 못하는 수많은 인간적 약점과 결함이 미치지 못하는 저 높은 곳에 놓여 있는 사람일세. 자네는 단 한 번의 타격으로 사랑스럽고 매혹적인 여인에게 버림받는 것이, 그리고 우정의 가면 아래 교활한 미소를 짓고 있던 악당의 술책에 희생되는 것이 어떤 것인지 모를 걸세. 부디 영영 모르기를 바라네.\n\n\"코범, 켄트의 레더 보틀로 내게 보내는 편지는 어디로든 전달될 걸세—내가 아직 살아 있다면 말이지. 나는 내게 가증스러워진 저 세상의 광경으로부터 서둘러 떠나네. 만일 내가 그 세상에서 아주 서둘러 떠나 버린다면, 가엾이 여겨 주게—용서해 주게. 인생은, 친애하는 픽윅이여, 내게 견딜 수 없는 것이 되었네. 우리 안에서 타오르는 정신이란, 세상의 근심과 고난이라는 무거운 짐을 얹어 받치는 짐꾼의 멜빵일세. 그런데 그 정신이 우리를 저버릴 때, 그 짐은 너무 무거워 도저히 질 수 없게 되네. 우리는 그 아래 가라앉고 마는 것이지. 레이철에게 전해 주게—아, 그 이름!—\n\n\"트레이시 터프먼.\"\n\"우리는 즉시 이곳을 떠나야 하네.\" 픽윅 씨가 그 쪽지를 다시 접으며 말했다. \"어떤 사정이 있더라도, 이런 일이 일어난 마당에 우리가 여기 머무는 것은 점잖지 못한 일일 걸세. 그리고 이제 우리는 친구를 찾아 뒤따라 나서야 할 의무가 있네.\" 그렇게 말하며 그는 앞장서서 집으로 향했다.\n\n그의 뜻은 신속히 전해졌다. 머물러 달라는 간청이 빗발쳤지만, 픽윅 씨는 단호했다. 볼일이 있어 당장 가 봐야 한다고 그는 말했다.\n\n늙은 목사도 그 자리에 있었다.\n\n\"정말 가시는 겁니까?\" 그가 픽윅 씨를 한쪽으로 데려가며 말했다.\n\n픽윅 씨는 앞서의 결심을 거듭 밝혔다.\n\n\"그렇다면 여기,\" 노신사가 말했다. \"작은 원고가 하나 있습니다. 본래는 제가 직접 당신께 읽어 드리는 기쁨을 누리고 싶었지요. 제 친구 한 사람이—우리 고을의 정신병원에서 일하던 의사였는데—세상을 떠났을 때, 제가 적당히 처분하거나 보존할 권한이 있던 여러 서류들 가운데서 이것을 발견했습니다. 제 친구의 필체는 분명 아니지만, 이 원고가 진짜인지는 저로서도 도무지 믿기 어렵습니다. 어쨌든 이것이 정말 광인의 손에서 나온 것이든, 아니면 어떤 불행한 자의 헛소리에 바탕을 둔 것이든(저는 후자 쪽이 더 그럴듯하다고 봅니다만), 한번 읽어 보시고 직접 판단해 보십시오.\"\n\n픽윅 씨는 원고를 받아 들고, 호의와 존경의 말을 거듭 건네며 그 인자한 노신사와 작별했다.\n\n매너 농장의 식구들과 헤어지는 것은 더 어려운 일이었으니, 그들에게서 그토록 많은 환대와 친절을 받았기 때문이다. 픽윅 씨는 젊은 아가씨들에게 입을 맞추었다—마치 제 친딸들에게 하듯이, 라고 말하려던 참인데, 다만 그가 그 인사에 다소 더 많은 온기를 불어넣었을 법하니, 그 비유는 그리 적절치 않을 것이다—그리고 노부인을 자식 같은 다정함으로 끌어안았으며, 가장 가부장다운 태도로 하녀들의 발그레한 뺨을 토닥이면서, 그 인정의 보다 실질적인 표시를 저마다의 손에 슬쩍 쥐여 주었다. 그들의 훌륭한 늙은 주인 및 트런들 씨와 나눈 정다운 인사는 한층 더 진심 어렸고 오래 이어졌다. 스노드그래스 씨를 여러 번 불러서야, 그리고 마침내 그가 어두운 복도에서 모습을 드러내고 곧이어 에밀리(그녀의 빛나는 눈은 유난히 흐릿해 보였다)가 뒤따라 나온 다음에야, 비로소 세 친구는 그 다정한 접대자들에게서 몸을 떼어 낼 수 있었다. 천천히 걸어 멀어지면서 그들은 농장을 몇 번이고 돌아보았다. 스노드그래스 씨는 위층 창문 하나에서 흔들리는, 영락없이 숙녀의 손수건처럼 보이는 무언가에 화답하여 공중으로 몇 번이고 입맞춤을 날려 보냈으니, 골목길이 굽이지면서 그 오래된 집이 시야에서 사라질 때까지 그러했다.\n\n머글턴에서 그들은 로체스터로 가는 마차편을 구했다. 그 도시에 다다랐을 무렵에는 슬픔의 격렬함이 어지간히 가라앉아, 아주 훌륭한 이른 식사를 할 만했다. 길에 관한 필요한 정보를 얻은 뒤, 세 친구는 오후에 다시 길을 나서서 코범까지 걸어갔다.\n\n상쾌한 산책이었다. 6월의 화창한 오후였고, 그들의 길은 깊고 그늘진 숲을 지나갔는데, 그 숲은 무성한 잎새들을 부드럽게 스치는 산들바람에 시원했고, 나뭇가지에 앉은 새들의 노래로 활기를 띠었다. 담쟁이와 이끼가 오래된 나무들 위로 빽빽이 뭉쳐 기어오르고, 부드러운 초록 잔디가 비단 깔개처럼 땅을 뒤덮었다. 이윽고 그들은 탁 트인 공원으로 나왔는데, 그곳에는 엘리자베스 시대의 예스럽고 그림 같은 건축 양식을 뽐내는 오래된 저택이 서 있었다. 사방으로 위풍당당한 참나무와 느릅나무가 늘어선 긴 풍경이 펼쳐졌고, 큰 사슴 떼가 싱싱한 풀을 뜯고 있었으며, 이따금 놀란 산토끼 한 마리가, 여름의 한 자락 숨결처럼 햇살 가득한 풍경 위를 스쳐 지나가는 가벼운 구름이 드리우는 그림자만큼이나 빠른 속도로 땅 위를 내달렸다.\n\n\"만일 이곳이,\" 픽윅 씨가 주위를 둘러보며 말했다. \"우리 친구의 병을 앓는 모든 이가 찾아오는 곳이라면, 이 세상에 대한 그들의 옛 애착이 아주 금세 되살아나리라고 보네.\"\n\n\"저도 그렇게 생각합니다.\" 윈클 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그리고 정말이지,\" 픽윅 씨가 반 시간을 걸어 마을에 다다른 뒤 덧붙였다. \"정말이지, 인간 혐오자가 고를 곳치고는, 이곳이야말로 내가 여태껏 만나 본 가장 예쁘고 살 만한 거처 가운데 하나일세.\"\n\n이 의견에도 윈클 씨와 스노드그래스 씨 모두 동의를 표했다. 깨끗하고 널찍한 마을 술집인 레더 보틀로 가는 길을 안내받은 뒤, 세 나그네는 안으로 들어가 곧장 터프먼이라는 이름의 신사를 찾았다.\n\n\"신사분들을 응접실로 모셔라, 톰.\" 안주인이 말했다.\n\n땅딸막한 시골 총각이 복도 끝의 문을 열었고, 세 친구는 길고 천장이 낮은 방으로 들어섰다. 그 방에는 기묘한 모양에 등받이가 높고 가죽 방석을 댄 의자가 잔뜩 놓여 있었고, 다양한 옛 초상화와 다소 오래된, 거칠게 색칠한 판화들로 꾸며져 있었다. 방 안쪽 끝에는 흰 식탁보를 덮은 식탁이 있었는데, 통닭구이며 베이컨이며 에일이며 그 밖의 여러 음식들이 그득히 차려져 있었다. 그리고 식탁에는 터프먼 씨가 앉아 있었으니, 세상에 작별을 고한 사람과는 도무지 닮은 데가 없는 모습이었다.\n\n친구들이 들어서자 그 신사는 나이프와 포크를 내려놓고, 침울한 표정으로 그들을 맞으러 다가왔다.\n\n\"여기서 자네들을 보게 될 줄은 몰랐네.\" 그가 픽윅 씨의 손을 맞잡으며 말했다. \"정말 고맙군.\"\n\n\"아!\" 픽윅 씨가 자리에 앉아 걸어오느라 맺힌 이마의 땀을 닦으며 말했다. \"식사부터 마치고, 나하고 같이 좀 걷세. 자네와 단둘이 이야기하고 싶네.\"\n\n터프먼 씨는 시키는 대로 했다. 픽윅 씨는 에일을 한 모금 듬뿍 들이켜 기운을 차린 뒤, 친구가 여유를 낼 때까지 기다렸다. 식사는 금세 끝났고, 두 사람은 함께 밖으로 나갔다.\n\n반 시간 동안 그들의 모습은 교회 묘지를 이리저리 거니는 것으로 보였을 텐데, 그동안 픽윅 씨는 친구의 결심을 꺾으려 애쓰고 있었다. 그의 논변을 다시 옮긴들 소용없는 일이다. 그 위대한 발설자의 태도가 전하던 그 활력과 힘을, 어떤 언어로 그들에게 전달할 수 있겠는가? 터프먼 씨가 이미 은둔 생활에 싫증이 났던 것인지, 아니면 그에게 던져진 그 웅변적인 호소를 도무지 뿌리칠 수 없었던 것인지는 중요하지 않다. 어쨌든 그는 끝내 그것을 뿌리치지 못했다.\n\n\"자기에게는,\" 그가 말했다. \"비참한 여생을 어디서 질질 끌며 보내든 별 상관이 없네. 그리고 친구가 이 보잘것없는 동행을 그토록 소중히 여겨 주니, 기꺼이 그의 모험을 함께 나누겠네.\"\n\n픽윅 씨는 미소 지었다. 두 사람은 악수했고, 동료들과 다시 합류하러 걸어 돌아갔다.\n\n바로 이 순간에 픽윅 씨는 저 불멸의 발견을 이루어 냈으니, 그것은 그의 친구들에게는 자랑이요 긍지였고, 이 나라뿐 아니라 어느 나라의 모든 골동품 애호가에게도 부러움의 대상이었다. 그들은 묵고 있던 여관의 문을 지나쳐 마을 길을 조금 내려간 뒤에야, 여관이 정확히 어느 자리에 서 있었는지를 떠올렸다. 되돌아가는데, 픽윅 씨의 눈이 어느 오두막 문 앞 땅에 일부가 묻힌 작고 깨진 돌 하나에 가 닿았다. 그는 걸음을 멈췄다.\n\n\"이건 참 이상하군.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"뭐가 이상합니까?\" 터프먼 씨가 자기 가까이의 온갖 물건을 열심히 쳐다보면서—정작 그 돌만 빼고—물었다. \"이런, 무슨 일입니까?\"\n\n이 마지막 말은 억누를 수 없는 경악의 외침이었으니, 픽윅 씨가 발견의 열정에 사로잡혀 그 작은 돌 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 주머니 손수건으로 먼지를 닦아 내기 시작하는 것을 보았기 때문이다.\n\n\"여기 명문(銘文)이 새겨져 있네.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그게 가능합니까?\" 터프먼 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"내가 알아볼 수 있겠는데,\" 픽윅 씨가 온 힘을 다해 문지르며 안경 너머로 뚫어져라 들여다보면서 말을 이었다. \"십자가가 하나 보이고, 13이라는 숫자가 있고, 그다음에 T가 있군. 이건 중요해.\" 픽윅 씨가 벌떡 일어서며 말을 이었다. \"이건 아주 오래된 명문일세. 어쩌면 이 고장의 저 옛 구빈원보다도 훨씬 전부터 있었을 게야. 이걸 잃어서는 안 돼.\"\n\n그는 오두막 문을 두드렸다. 일하던 사내가 문을 열었다.\n\n\"여보게, 이 돌이 어떻게 여기 있게 됐는지 아나?\" 인자한 픽윅 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"아뇨, 모릅니다, 나리.\" 사내가 공손히 대답했다. \"이건 제가 태어나기 한참 전부터, 아니 우리 누구든 태어나기 전부터 여기 있었습니다요.\"\n\n픽윅 씨는 의기양양하게 동행을 흘끗 보았다.\n\n\"자네는—자네는—이 돌에 딱히 애착이 있는 건 아니겠지, 짐작건대.\" 픽윅 씨가 초조함에 떨면서 말했다. \"지금 이걸 파는 거, 괜찮겠나?\"\n\n\"아! 하지만 누가 이런 걸 사겠습니까?\" 사내가, 아마도 무척 교활하게 지으려 했을 표정을 띠며 물었다.\n\n\"내 당장 10실링을 주겠네.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"자네가 나를 위해 이걸 파내 준다면 말일세.\"\n\n(삽으로 한 번 비틀어 그 작은 돌을 들어 올린 뒤) 픽윅 씨가 엄청난 노력을 들여 제 손으로 그것을 여관까지 들고 가, 정성껏 씻은 다음 식탁 위에 내려놓았을 때, 마을 사람들이 얼마나 놀랐을지는 능히 짐작할 수 있다.\n\n픽윅 회원들의 인내와 부지런함, 그 씻고 긁어내는 수고가 성공의 영예로 빛났을 때, 그들의 환희와 기쁨은 끝이 없었다. 돌은 울퉁불퉁하고 깨져 있었으며 글자들은 들쭉날쭉하고 고르지 못했지만, 다음과 같은 명문의 단편을 분명히 판독해 낼 수 있었다.\n✝ B I L S T U M P S H I S. M. 표시\n\n자신이 발견한 보물을 흐뭇이 바라보며 앉아 있는 픽윅 씨의 두 눈은 환희로 반짝였다. 그는 자기 야망의 가장 큰 목표 가운데 하나를 이룬 것이다. 고대의 유물이 많기로 이름난 고을에서, 옛 시절의 기념물이 아직도 더러 남아 있는 마을에서, 그가—그가, 다름 아닌 픽윅 클럽의 회장이—의심할 여지 없는 고대의, 기이하고 진귀한 명문 하나를, 그에 앞선 수많은 학식 있는 인사들의 눈을 죄다 피해 갔던 그것을 발견해 낸 것이다. 그는 자기 감각의 증거를 도무지 믿을 수가 없었다.\n\n\"이것이—이것이,\" 그가 말했다. \"나의 결심을 굳히게 하는군. 우리는 내일 시내로 돌아가네.\"\n\n\"내일이라뇨!\" 그를 흠모하는 추종자들이 외쳤다.\n\n\"내일.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"이 보물은 철저히 조사되고 제대로 이해될 수 있는 곳에 즉시 보관되어야 하네. 이 조치에는 또 다른 이유가 있어. 며칠 뒤에 이턴스윌 자치구에서 선거가 열릴 텐데, 내가 최근에 만난 신사인 퍼커 씨가 거기서 한 후보의 선거 대리인을 맡고 있네. 우리는 모든 영국인에게 그토록 흥미로운 광경을 직접 보고 면밀히 살펴볼 걸세.\"\n\n\"그러겠습니다.\" 세 목소리가 활기차게 외쳤다.\n\n픽윅 씨는 둘레를 둘러보았다. 추종자들의 애착과 열의가 그의 마음속에 열정의 불꽃을 환히 지폈다. 그는 그들의 지도자였고, 그것을 절감했다.\n\n\"이 행복한 만남을 흥겨운 한 잔으로 축하하세.\" 그가 말했다. 이 제안은 앞서의 것과 마찬가지로 만장일치의 갈채로 받아들여졌다. 그는 그 중요한 돌을, 이를 위해 안주인에게서 사들인 작은 전나무 상자에 손수 넣어 두고, 식탁 상석의 안락의자에 자리를 잡았다. 그렇게 저녁은 잔치와 담소에 바쳐졌다.\n\n픽윅 씨가 자기를 위해 마련된 침실로 물러간 것은 열한 시가 지나서였으니—작은 마을 코범으로서는 늦은 시각이었다. 그는 격자창을 활짝 열고, 촛불을 탁자 위에 놓은 다음, 지난 이틀 동안 숨 가쁘게 일어난 일들에 대한 깊은 상념에 빠져들었다.\n\n그 시각과 그 장소는 둘 다 사색에 알맞았다. 픽윅 씨는 교회 시계가 열두 시를 치는 소리에 정신이 들었다. 정각을 알리는 첫 타종 소리가 그의 귀에 엄숙하게 울렸으나, 종이 그치자 정적이 견딜 수 없게 느껴졌다—그는 마치 동무 하나를 잃은 것 같은 기분마저 들었다. 그는 신경이 곤두서고 들떠 있었다. 그래서 서둘러 옷을 벗고 촛불을 벽난로에 놓은 뒤 침대로 들어갔다.\n\n육체적 피로감이 잠을 이루지 못하는 무력함과 헛되이 다투는, 그 언짢은 마음 상태를 누구나 한 번쯤 겪어 보았을 것이다. 이 순간 픽윅 씨의 처지가 바로 그러했다. 그는 먼저 이쪽으로 뒤척이다가 또 저쪽으로 뒤척였고, 잠을 어르듯이 끈질기게 눈을 감았다. 소용없었다. 그가 겪은 평소답지 않은 수고 탓인지, 더위 탓인지, 브랜디 탄 물 탓인지, 낯선 침대 탓인지—그게 무엇이었든, 그의 생각은 아래층의 그 음산한 그림들과, 그것들이 저녁 내내 불러일으킨 옛이야기들로 자꾸만 거북하게 되돌아갔다. 반 시간을 뒤척인 끝에 그는 잠을 청해 봐야 소용없다는 탐탁잖은 결론에 이르렀다. 그래서 일어나 옷을 대강 걸쳤다. 무엇이든, 그는 생각했다, 거기 누워 온갖 무서운 것들을 상상하는 것보다는 나았다. 그는 창밖을 내다보았다—몹시 어두웠다. 그는 방 안을 거닐었다—몹시 적막했다.\n\n문에서 창으로, 창에서 문으로 몇 차례 오가던 차에, 비로소 목사의 원고가 머릿속에 떠올랐다. 좋은 생각이었다. 흥미를 끌지 못한다 해도, 잠이라도 들게 해 줄 터였다. 그는 외투 주머니에서 그것을 꺼내, 작은 탁자를 침대 옆으로 끌어당기고는, 촛불 심지를 다듬고 안경을 쓴 다음 읽으려고 자세를 가다듬었다. 낯선 필체였고, 종이는 몹시 더럽혀지고 얼룩져 있었다. 제목도 그를 흠칫 놀라게 했다. 그는 방 안을 휘 둘러보며 무언가를 갈구하는 듯한 시선을 던지지 않을 수 없었다. 그러나 그런 감정에 굴복하는 것이 어리석다는 생각이 들어, 다시 촛불 심지를 다듬고 다음과 같이 읽어 내려갔다.\n어느 광인의 원고\n\n# A MADMAN’S MANUSCRIPT\n\n\"그렇다!—광인의 것이다! 그 단어가 여러 해 전이었다면 내 가슴을 얼마나 후벼 팠으랴! 그것이 이따금 나를 덮치곤 하던 그 공포를 얼마나 불러일으켰으랴, 피가 쉭쉭 소리를 내며 내 핏줄을 따끔따끔 내달려, 마침내 차가운 두려움의 이슬이 굵은 방울로 내 살갗에 맺히고, 무서움에 두 무릎이 서로 부딪칠 때까지! 하지만 지금은 그 말이 좋다. 멋진 이름이지. 광인의 눈빛처럼 두려움을 산 성난 찌푸림을 가진 군주를 내게 보여 보라—광인의 움켜쥠만큼 절반이라도 확실한 올가미와 도끼를 가진 군주를. 호! 호! 미친다는 건 굉장한 일이지! 쇠창살 너머로 야생 사자처럼 사람들이 엿보고—길고 고요한 밤 내내 묵직한 쇠사슬의 흥겨운 쟁그랑 소리에 맞추어 이를 갈고 울부짖으며—그 용맹한 음악에 도취되어 짚더미 속에서 뒹굴고 몸을 비틀 수 있다니. 정신병원이여, 만세! 오, 정말 멋진 곳이지!\n\n\"내가 미칠까 봐 두려워하던 나날을 나는 기억한다. 잠에서 깜짝 놀라 깨어나 무릎을 꿇고, 우리 핏줄의 저주를 면하게 해 달라고 기도하던 때를. 흥겨움이나 행복의 광경에서 도망쳐, 어느 외딴곳에 숨어, 내 뇌를 좀먹어 갈 그 열병의 진행을 지켜보며 지친 시간을 보내던 때를. 광기가 바로 내 피와 뼛속 골수에 뒤섞여 있다는 것을 나는 알았다! 한 세대는 그 역병이 나타나지 않은 채 지나갔으나, 그것이 되살아날 첫 사람이 바로 나임을 알았다. 반드시 그러하리라는 것을 나는 알았다. 늘 그래 왔고, 또 늘 그러하리라는 것을. 사람들로 붐비는 방의 어느 후미진 구석에 웅크리고 있을 때, 사람들이 수군대고 손가락질하며 내게로 눈길을 돌리는 것을 보면, 나는 그들이 서로에게 저 운명 지어진 광인의 이야기를 하고 있음을 알았다. 그러면 나는 다시 슬그머니 빠져나가 고독 속에 시름겨워했다.\n\n\"나는 여러 해 동안 이렇게 했다. 길고, 긴 세월이었지. 이곳의 밤은 때로 길다—아주 길다. 하지만 그 시절 내가 겪은 잠 못 이루는 밤들과 무시무시한 꿈들에 비하면 아무것도 아니다. 그것들을 떠올리면 몸이 오싹해진다. 음흉하고 비웃는 얼굴을 한 크고 어슴푸레한 형체들이 방구석에 웅크리고 있다가, 밤이면 내 침대 위로 몸을 굽혀 나를 광기로 꾀어냈다. 그들은 낮은 속삭임으로, 내 아버지가 돌아가신 그 오래된 집의 마룻바닥이 그분 자신의 피로 물들어 있다고 내게 일러 주었다—광란의 광기 속에서 그분 자신의 손으로 흘린 피로. 나는 손가락을 귀에 처박았지만, 그들은 방이 쩌렁쩌렁 울리도록 내 머릿속으로 비명을 질러 댔다, 그분보다 한 세대 앞서서는 광기가 잠들어 있었으나, 그분의 할아버지는 스스로를 갈가리 찢지 못하도록 두 손을 땅에 결박당한 채 여러 해를 살았노라고. 나는 그들이 진실을 말하고 있음을 알았다—잘 알았다. 그들이 내게 숨기려 했건만, 나는 여러 해 전에 그것을 알아냈던 것이다. 하! 하! 그들이 나를 광인으로 여겼어도, 나는 그들보다 한 수 위였지.\n\n\"마침내 그것이 내게 닥쳤고, 나는 내가 어떻게 그토록 그것을 두려워할 수 있었는지 의아했다. 이제 나는 세상에 나가, 그들 가운데 누구 못지않게 웃고 떠들 수 있었다. 나는 내가 미쳤음을 알았으나, 그들은 짐작조차 못 했다. 내가 미치지 않았으되 언젠가 그리 될까 두려워하기만 하던 시절, 그들이 손가락질하고 곁눈질하던 그 일에 대해, 이제 내가 그들에게 멋진 장난을 치고 있다고 생각할 때면, 나는 얼마나 기뻐 나 자신을 끌어안았던가! 혼자 있을 때면, 내가 비밀을 얼마나 잘 지키는지, 그리고 진실을 알았더라면 나의 다정한 벗들이 얼마나 빨리 내게서 떨어져 나갔을지를 생각하며, 기쁨에 겨워 얼마나 웃어 댔던가! 어느 호탕하게 떠들어 대는 멋쟁이와 단둘이 식사할 때면, 바로 곁에 앉아 번쩍번쩍 빛나는 칼을 갈고 있는 그 다정한 벗이, 그것을 그의 심장에 꽂을 힘을 온전히 지녔고 그럴 의지도 절반은 지닌 광인임을 그가 알았더라면 얼마나 새파랗게 질리고, 얼마나 잽싸게 달아났을까를 생각하며, 나는 황홀경에 비명이라도 지를 수 있었다. 오, 그것은 흥겨운 삶이었지!\n\n\"부가 내 것이 되었고, 재물이 내게 쏟아져 들어왔으며, 나는 잘 간직한 비밀에 대한 의식 덕분에 천 배로 부풀려진 쾌락 속에서 흥청거렸다. 나는 한 영지를 물려받았다. 법이—저 매서운 눈을 가진 법조차도—속아 넘어가, 분쟁의 대상이던 수천 파운드를 광인의 손에 넘겨주고 만 것이다. 멀쩡한 정신을 가진 그 날카로운 자들의 재간은 어디 갔던가? 허점을 잡아내려 안달하던 변호사들의 기민함은 어디 갔던가? 광인의 교활함이 그들 모두를 한 수 앞질렀던 것이다.\n\n\"내게는 돈이 있었다. 사람들이 나를 얼마나 떠받들었던가! 나는 그것을 흥청망청 썼다. 사람들이 나를 얼마나 칭송했던가! 그 거만하고 오만한 세 형제가 내 앞에서 얼마나 굽실거렸던가! 그 늙고 백발이 성성한 아버지도—그 깍듯한 공경—그 존경—그 헌신적인 우정—그는 나를 떠받들었다! 그 노인에게는 딸이 하나 있었고, 그 젊은이들에게는 누이가 있었다. 그리고 다섯 식구 모두가 가난했다. 나는 부유했다. 그래서 내가 그 처녀와 혼인했을 때, 나는 그 궁핍한 친척들의 얼굴에 의기양양한 미소가 떠도는 것을 보았으니, 그들은 자신들의 잘 짜인 계략과 멋진 노획물을 떠올리고 있었던 것이다. 정작 미소 지어야 할 사람은 나였다. 미소라니! 큰 소리로 웃고, 머리를 쥐어뜯고, 흥에 겨워 비명을 지르며 땅바닥에서 뒹굴어야 할 사람은. 그들은 자기들이 그녀를 광인에게 시집보냈다는 것을 꿈에도 생각지 못했다.\n\n\"잠깐. 그들이 그것을 알았더라면, 그녀를 구했을까? 누이의 행복을 그녀 남편의 황금에 견주다니. 내가 공중에 날려 보내는 가장 가벼운 깃털 한 올을, 내 몸을 장식하는 화려한 사슬에 견주는 격이지!\n\n\"그토록 교활했건만 한 가지에서 나는 속았다. 만일 내가 미치지 않았더라면—우리 광인들이 머리가 제법 날카롭다 해도, 때로는 갈피를 못 잡곤 하니까—나는 알았어야 했다, 그 처녀가 부럽도록 단장한 신부로서 내 부유하고 번쩍이는 집으로 실려 오느니, 차라리 뻣뻣하고 차갑게 칙칙한 납관 속에 누이는 편을 택했으리라는 것을. 나는 알았어야 했다, 그녀의 마음이, 한번은 그녀가 뒤척이는 잠결에 그 이름을 읊조리는 것을 내가 들은 적 있는 그 검은 눈의 소년에게 가 있었다는 것을. 그리고 그녀가 그 늙고 백발인 사내와 거만한 형제들의 가난을 덜어 주고자 내게 제물로 바쳐졌다는 것을.\n\n\"이제는 형체도 얼굴도 기억나지 않지만, 그 처녀가 아름다웠다는 것만은 안다. 그녀가 아름다웠음을 나는 안다. 밝은 달빛 비치는 밤이면, 내가 잠에서 깜짝 놀라 깨어나 사방이 고요할 때, 이 감방의 한 구석에 가냘프고 여윈 형체 하나가 가만히 미동도 없이 서 있는 것이 보이니, 등 뒤로 흘러내린 긴 검은 머리칼은 이 세상의 바람이 아닌 것에 흔들리고, 두 눈은 나를 응시한 채 깜빡이지도 감기지도 않는다. 쉿! 이걸 적자니 심장의 피가 얼어붙는다—그 형체는 그녀의 것이다. 얼굴은 몹시 창백하고 두 눈은 유리처럼 번득이지만, 나는 그것들을 잘 안다. 그 형체는 결코 움직이지 않는다. 이따금 이곳을 채우는 다른 자들처럼 찌푸리거나 입을 놀리지도 않는다. 하지만 그것은 내게, 여러 해 전 나를 꾀어내던 그 망령들보다도 훨씬 더 무섭다—그것은 무덤에서 갓 나온 것이라, 그토록 죽음을 닮아 있다.\n\n\"거의 일 년 동안 나는 그 얼굴이 점점 더 창백해지는 것을 보았다. 거의 일 년 동안 나는 그 슬픈 두 뺨을 타고 눈물이 흘러내리는 것을 보았으되, 그 까닭을 결코 알지 못했다. 하지만 마침내 나는 그것을 알아냈다. 그들은 오래도록 내게 그것을 숨길 수 없었다. 그녀는 한 번도 나를 좋아한 적이 없었다. 나도 그녀가 그러리라고는 생각해 본 적이 없었다. 그녀는 내 재물을 경멸했고, 자신이 살아가는 그 호화로움을 증오했다. 하지만 그것까지는 나도 예상치 못했다. 그녀는 다른 사람을 사랑하고 있었던 것이다. 이것만은 나도 생각해 본 적이 없었다. 기묘한 감정들이 나를 덮쳐 왔고, 어떤 은밀한 힘에 떠밀린 상념들이 내 머릿속을 빙빙 휘돌았다. 나는 그녀를 미워하지 않았다, 그녀가 여전히 눈물 흘리는 그 소년은 미워했어도. 나는 가엾이 여겼다—그렇다, 가엾이 여겼다—그녀의 차갑고 이기적인 친척들이 그녀에게 운명 지운 그 비참한 삶을. 나는 그녀가 오래 살지 못하리라는 것을 알았다. 하지만 죽기 전에 그녀가, 제 자손에게 광기를 물려줄 운명을 지닌 어떤 불운한 존재를 낳을지도 모른다는 생각이 나의 결심을 굳혔다. 나는 그녀를 죽이기로 작정했다.\n\n\"여러 주 동안 나는 독약을 생각했고, 그다음엔 익사를, 그다음엔 불을 생각했다. 멋진 광경이지, 그 으리으리한 집이 불길에 휩싸이고, 광인의 아내가 잿더미로 타들어 가는 것은. 거기다 두둑한 현상금이라는 농지거리며, 결코 저지른 적 없는 짓 때문에 어느 멀쩡한 사내가 바람결에 매달려 흔들리는 꼴을, 그것도 모두 광인의 교활함 때문에 벌어진 일임을 생각해 보라! 나는 이것을 자주 생각했지만, 끝내 단념했다. 오! 날마다 면도칼을 가죽에 갈며, 그 날카로운 날을 만져 보고, 그 얇고 번득이는 날의 단 한 번 그음이 만들어 낼 깊은 상처를 떠올리는 그 즐거움이라니!\n\n\"마침내 전에도 그토록 자주 내 곁에 있던 옛 망령들이 내 귀에 때가 왔다고 속삭이며, 펼쳐진 면도칼을 내 손에 쥐여 주었다. 나는 그것을 단단히 거머쥐고 침대에서 살그머니 일어나, 잠든 아내 위로 몸을 굽혔다. 그녀의 얼굴은 두 손에 파묻혀 있었다. 나는 그 손을 살며시 치웠고, 손은 맥없이 그녀의 가슴 위로 떨어졌다. 그녀는 울었던 모양이다. 눈물 자국이 아직도 뺨에 축축이 남아 있었으니까. 그녀의 얼굴은 차분하고 평온했다. 내가 바로 그것을 바라보고 있는 사이에도, 고요한 미소 하나가 그 창백한 얼굴을 환히 밝혔다. 나는 그녀의 어깨에 살며시 손을 얹었다. 그녀가 흠칫했다—그저 스쳐 가는 꿈이었을 뿐. 나는 다시 몸을 앞으로 숙였다. 그녀가 비명을 지르며 깨어났다.\n\n\"내 손이 한 번만 움직였더라면, 그녀는 다시는 울음소리도 그 어떤 소리도 내지 못했으리라. 그러나 나는 흠칫 놀라 물러섰다. 그녀의 두 눈이 내 눈에 못 박혀 있었다. 어찌 된 영문인지 알 수 없으나, 그 눈이 나를 위압하고 겁먹게 했다. 나는 그 눈 아래 움츠러들었다. 그녀는 여전히 나를 똑바로, 한결같이 응시한 채 침대에서 일어났다. 나는 떨었다. 손에는 면도칼이 들려 있었으나, 나는 움직일 수가 없었다. 그녀는 문 쪽으로 다가갔다. 문에 가까워지자 그녀는 몸을 돌려, 내 얼굴에서 시선을 거두었다. 주문이 풀렸다. 나는 앞으로 뛰쳐나가 그녀의 팔을 움켜쥐었다. 비명에 비명을 거듭 지르며 그녀는 바닥에 쓰러졌다.\n\n\"이제 나는 몸싸움도 없이 그녀를 죽일 수 있었다. 그러나 집안이 발칵 뒤집혔다. 계단을 밟는 발소리가 들려왔다. 나는 면도칼을 늘 두던 서랍에 도로 넣고, 문을 열어젖힌 다음, 큰 소리로 도움을 청했다.\n\n\"그들이 와서 그녀를 일으켜 침대에 눕혔다. 그녀는 여러 시간 동안 의식을 잃고 누워 있었다. 그리고 생기와 눈빛과 말이 되돌아왔을 때, 그녀는 정신이 나가 거칠고 사납게 헛소리를 해 댔다.\n\n\"의사들이 불려 왔다—멋진 말과 화려한 하인을 거느리고 편안한 마차를 타고 내 집 앞에 들이닥치는 거물들이었다. 그들은 여러 주 동안 그녀의 머리맡을 지켰다. 그들은 다른 방에서 거창하게 모여 낮고 엄숙한 목소리로 함께 의논했다. 그중에 가장 명민하고 가장 이름난 한 사람이 나를 한쪽으로 데려가더니, 최악의 사태에 대비하라면서 내게—나, 광인에게!—내 아내가 미쳤다고 일러 주었다. 그는 열린 창가에 내 바로 곁에 서서, 두 눈으로 내 얼굴을 들여다보고 한 손은 내 팔에 얹고 있었다. 단 한 번 힘을 쓰면, 나는 그를 아래 거리로 내던질 수 있었을 것이다. 그리했더라면 진귀한 놀이가 되었으련만, 내 비밀이 걸려 있었기에 나는 그를 놓아주었다. 며칠 뒤 그들은 내게 그녀를 어떤 식으로든 감금해 두어야 한다고, 간수를 한 사람 붙여야 한다고 말했다. 내가! 나는 아무도 들을 수 없는 탁 트인 들판으로 나가, 공기가 내 고함에 쩌렁쩌렁 울릴 때까지 웃어 댔다!\n\n\"그녀는 이튿날 죽었다. 백발의 노인이 그녀를 무덤까지 따라갔고, 거만한 형제들은 그녀가 살아 있을 적엔 그 고통을 쇠심줄 같은 무심함으로 바라보던 주제에, 이제 그 감각 없는 시신 위로 눈물을 떨구었다. 이 모든 것이 내 은밀한 즐거움의 양식이 되었으니, 우리가 마차로 집에 돌아오는 길에, 나는 얼굴에 받쳐 든 흰 손수건 뒤에서 눈물이 나도록 웃어 댔다.\n\n\"하지만 내 목적을 이루어 그녀를 죽이긴 했어도, 나는 안절부절못하고 심란했으며, 머지않아 내 비밀이 드러나고 말리라는 것을 느꼈다. 나는 내 안에서 끓어오르는 그 광포한 환희와 기쁨을 감출 수가 없었으니, 그것은 내가 집에 혼자 있을 때면 나를 벌떡 일어나 두 손을 마주치게 하고, 빙글빙글 돌며 춤추게 하고, 큰 소리로 울부짖게 했다. 밖에 나가 거리를 분주히 오가는 인파를 보거나, 극장에 가서 음악 소리를 듣고 사람들이 춤추는 것을 바라볼 때면, 어찌나 흥겨운지 그들 가운데로 뛰어들어 사지를 갈가리 찢어발기고 황홀경에 울부짖을 수도 있을 것 같았다. 그러나 나는 이를 갈고, 발로 바닥을 구르고, 날카로운 손톱을 손바닥에 처박았다. 나는 그것을 억눌렀고, 아직 아무도 내가 광인임을 알지 못했다.\n\n\"기억난다—그것이 내가 기억할 수 있는 마지막 것들 가운데 하나이긴 하다. 지금은 현실과 꿈이 뒤섞이고, 할 일이 어찌나 많은지 늘 이리로 떠밀려 다니느라, 그 둘이 어떤 기묘한 혼란 속에 뒤엉켜 버려 그것들을 갈라낼 겨를이 없으니 말이다—나는 마침내 그것을 어떻게 발설하고 말았는지를 기억한다. 하! 하! 지금도 그들의 겁에 질린 얼굴이 눈에 선하고, 내가 얼마나 수월하게 그들을 내게서 떨쳐 내고, 움켜쥔 주먹으로 그들의 허연 얼굴을 후려갈겼는지, 그러고는 바람처럼 날아가 그들을 비명 지르고 고함치게 저 뒤에 남겨 두었는지가 느껴진다. 그것을 떠올리면 거인의 힘이 내게 솟구친다. 보라—이 쇠막대가 내 광포한 비틀림 아래 어떻게 휘는지를. 나무 잔가지처럼 부러뜨릴 수도 있으련만, 다만 이곳에는 문이 많이 달린 긴 회랑들이 있어서—내가 그것들을 따라 길을 찾을 수 있을 것 같지가 않다. 설령 찾을 수 있다 해도, 아래쪽에 그들이 잠그고 빗장을 질러 두는 철문들이 있음을 안다. 그들은 내가 얼마나 영리한 광인이었는지를 알기에, 나를 이곳에 두고 구경시키는 것을 자랑스러워한다.\n\n\"가만 보자. 그래, 나는 외출했었다. 집에 다다랐을 때는 밤이 깊었고, 거만한 세 형제 가운데 가장 거만한 자가 나를 기다리고 있는 것을 보았다—긴한 볼일이 있다고 했다. 나는 그것을 잘 기억한다. 나는 그 사내를 광인의 온갖 증오를 다해 미워했다. 몇 번이고 내 손가락은 그를 갈가리 찢고 싶어 안달했다. 그들이 그가 와 있다고 내게 알렸다. 나는 잽싸게 위층으로 뛰어 올라갔다. 그가 내게 할 말이 있다고 했다. 나는 하인들을 물렸다. 늦은 시각이었고, 우리는 단둘이 있었다—처음으로.\n\n\"나는 처음엔 그에게서 조심스레 눈길을 돌렸으니, 그가 거의 짐작도 못 하는 것을—그리고 나는 그 앎에 의기양양했는데—내 눈에서 광기의 빛이 불처럼 번득이고 있음을 알았기 때문이다. 우리는 몇 분 동안 말없이 앉아 있었다. 마침내 그가 입을 열었다. 그의 누이가 죽고 그토록 얼마 되지도 않아 보인 나의 최근의 방탕과 괴이한 언동은 그녀의 추억에 대한 모욕이라고 했다. 처음엔 그의 눈에 띄지 않았던 여러 정황을 한데 꿰맞춰 보니, 내가 그녀를 잘 대해 주지 않았다는 생각이 든다고 했다. 그는 내가 그녀의 추억에 비난을, 그녀의 가문에 모욕을 던지려 한 것이라고 추론하는 것이 옳은지 알고 싶다고 했다. 자신이 입은 군복에 비추어, 이 해명을 요구하는 것이 마땅하다고 했다.\n\n\"이 사내는 군대에서 장교 직위를 가지고 있었다—내 돈으로, 그리고 제 누이의 비참함으로 사들인 직위를! 이자야말로 나를 옭아매어 내 재산을 거머쥐려는 음모에 앞장섰던 사내였다. 이자야말로 제 누이의 마음이 그 칭얼대는 소년에게 가 있음을 뻔히 알면서도, 그녀를 억지로 내게 시집보내는 데 주된 도구가 되었던 사내였다. 군복에 비추어 마땅하다고! 제 타락의 제복에 비추어! 나는 그에게로 눈길을 돌렸다—그러지 않을 수 없었다—그러나 한마디도 입에 담지 않았다.\n\n\"나는 내 응시 아래 그에게 닥친 갑작스러운 변화를 보았다. 그는 담대한 사내였으나, 얼굴에서 핏기가 가시며 의자를 뒤로 물렸다. 나는 내 의자를 그에게 더 가까이 끌어당겼다. 그리고 나는 웃었다—그때 나는 무척 흥겨웠다—그가 몸서리치는 것이 보였다. 나는 광기가 내 안에서 솟구치는 것을 느꼈다. 그는 나를 두려워하고 있었다.\n\n\"'당신은 누이가 살아 있을 적에 그녀를 무척 좋아했지요.' 내가 말했다.—'아주.'\n\n\"그는 불안한 듯 둘레를 둘러보았고, 나는 그의 손이 의자 등받이를 움켜쥐는 것을 보았다. 하지만 그는 아무 말도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"'이 악당아.' 내가 말했다. '나는 너를 간파했다. 나를 노린 네 흉악한 음모를 알아냈다. 네가 그녀를 억지로 내게 시집보내기 전에, 그녀의 마음이 이미 다른 누군가에게 못 박혀 있었음을 안다. 나는 안다—나는 안다.'\n\n\"그는 별안간 의자에서 벌떡 일어나 그것을 높이 휘두르며, 내게 물러서라고 외쳤다—내가 말하는 내내 줄곧 그에게 더 가까이 다가가고 있었으니까.\n\n\"나는 말한다기보다 비명을 질렀으니, 격동하는 격정이 내 핏줄을 따라 소용돌이치는 것을, 그리고 옛 망령들이 그의 심장을 도려내라고 속삭이며 부추기는 것을 느꼈기 때문이다.\n\n\"'빌어먹을 놈.' 나는 벌떡 일어서며, 그에게 달려들며 말했다. '내가 그녀를 죽였다. 나는 광인이다. 쓰러져라. 피, 피다! 내 기어이 그것을 보고야 말겠다!'\n\n\"나는 그가 공포에 질려 내게 내던진 의자를 단번에 후려쳐 옆으로 쳐냈고, 그와 맞붙었다. 그리고 묵직하게 쿵 하는 소리와 함께 우리는 함께 바닥을 뒹굴었다.\n\n\"그것은 멋진 몸싸움이었다. 그는 키 크고 힘센 사내로 제 목숨을 걸고 싸웠으니까. 그리고 나는, 그를 파멸시키기를 갈망하는 힘센 광인이었다. 그 어떤 힘도 내 힘에 맞설 수 없음을 나는 알았고, 내 생각이 옳았다. 광인이건만 또다시 옳았던 것이다! 그의 몸부림이 차츰 약해졌다. 나는 그의 가슴 위에 무릎을 꿇고, 그 억센 목을 두 손으로 단단히 졸랐다. 그의 얼굴이 시퍼레졌다. 두 눈은 머리에서 튀어나올 듯했고, 혀를 쑥 내민 채 나를 비웃는 것만 같았다. 나는 더 세게 졸랐다.\n\n\"문이 요란한 소리와 함께 별안간 벌컥 열리며, 한 무리의 사람들이 서로에게 큰 소리로 저 광인을 붙잡으라고 외치며 앞으로 쏟아져 들어왔다.\n\n\"내 비밀이 드러났다. 이제 나의 유일한 몸부림은 자유를, 해방을 위한 것이었다. 누구의 손이 내게 닿기도 전에 나는 두 발로 일어서서, 나를 공격하는 자들 가운데로 몸을 던졌고, 마치 손에 손도끼라도 든 양 억센 팔로 길을 열며 그들을 앞에서 베어 넘겼다. 나는 문에 다다라, 난간 너머로 뛰어내렸고, 순식간에 거리로 나섰다.\n\n\"나는 곧장 쏜살같이 내달렸고, 아무도 감히 나를 막지 못했다. 등 뒤로 발소리가 들려와, 나는 속도를 곱절로 했다. 그 소리는 멀어지며 점점 희미해지다가, 마침내 아주 잦아들어 버렸다. 그러나 나는 늪과 시내를 가로질러, 울타리와 담을 넘어, 거친 함성을 내지르며 껑충껑충 내달렸으니, 그 함성은 사방에서 내게 떼 지어 몰려든 기괴한 존재들에게 받아들여져, 그 소리가 부풀어 올라 마침내 허공을 꿰뚫었다. 나는 바람을 타고 내달리는 악마들의 팔에 떠받쳐졌으니, 그들은 둑과 산울타리를 앞에서 무너뜨리며, 부스럭거리는 소리와 함께 어찌나 빠르게 나를 빙글빙글 돌렸던지 머리가 어질어질했고, 마침내 그들은 격렬한 충격과 함께 나를 내던졌으며, 나는 땅바닥에 무겁게 떨어졌다. 깨어났을 때 나는 내가 이곳에 있음을 알았다—햇빛은 좀처럼 들지 않고, 달빛이 슬며시 스며드나 그 빛줄기는 그저 내 둘레의 어두운 그림자들과, 제 옛 구석에 선 저 말 없는 형체를 드러내는 데나 쓸모 있는 이 잿빛 감방에. 잠들지 못하고 누워 있노라면, 나는 때때로 이 넓은 곳의 먼 구석들에서 들려오는 기괴한 비명과 절규를 들을 수 있다. 그것들이 무엇인지 나는 모른다. 하지만 그것들은 저 창백한 형체에게서 나오는 것도 아니요, 그 형체가 그것들에 아랑곳하는 것도 아니다. 황혼의 첫 그늘이 질 때부터 아침의 가장 이른 빛이 들 때까지, 그것은 여전히 같은 자리에 미동도 없이 서서, 내 쇠사슬의 음악에 귀 기울이고, 내 짚 침대 위의 까불대는 몸짓을 지켜보고 있으니까.\"\n\n원고 끝에는 다른 필체로 이런 주석이 적혀 있었다.\n\n[위에 헛소리가 기록된 이 불행한 사내는, 젊은 시절 그릇된 방향으로 쓰인 정력과, 그 결과를 결코 돌이킬 수 없게 될 때까지 이어진 무절제가 빚어낸 해로운 결과의 우울한 한 본보기였다. 젊은 날의 분별없는 방탕과 난봉과 음탕은 열병과 섬망을 낳았다. 후자의 첫 영향은 어떤 기이한 망상이었으니, 그것은 어떤 이들은 강력히 주장하고 또 어떤 이들은 그만큼 강력히 반박하는, 잘 알려진 한 의학 이론에 바탕을 둔 것으로, 곧 그의 집안에 유전적 광기가 존재한다는 것이었다. 이것이 고착된 우울을 낳았고, 그것이 차차 병적인 정신착란으로 발전하여, 마침내 광란의 광기로 귀결되었다. 그가 상세히 늘어놓은 사건들은, 그의 병든 상상으로 묘사가 일그러져 있긴 해도, 실제로 일어난 일이라고 믿을 만한 온갖 근거가 있다. 다만 그의 초년 생애의 악덕들을 잘 알던 이들로서는, 이성에 더는 통제되지 않게 된 그의 격정이 그를 한층 더 끔찍한 행위로 몰아가지 않았다는 것만이 놀라울 따름이다.]\n\n픽윅 씨가 늙은 목사의 원고를 다 읽어 갈 무렵, 그의 촛불은 막 받침 구멍에서 꺼져 가고 있었다. 그리고 그 불이, 미리 경고하듯 깜빡이는 일조차 없이 별안간 꺼져 버리자, 그것은 들떠 있던 그의 몸뚱이를 적잖이 흠칫 놀라게 했다. 그는 그 불편한 침대에서 일어났을 때 걸쳐 두었던 옷가지들을 황급히 벗어 던지고, 두려운 눈길을 사방에 던지면서, 다시 한 번 이불 속으로 허둥지둥 기어들었고, 이내 깊이 잠들었다.\n\n그가 깨어났을 때 햇빛이 그의 방 안으로 찬란하게 비쳐 들고 있었고, 아침은 이미 한참 무르익어 있었다. 전날 밤 그를 짓누르던 음울함은, 풍경을 뒤덮었던 어두운 그림자와 함께 사라졌고, 그의 생각과 기분은 아침 그 자체만큼이나 가볍고 명랑했다. 푸짐한 아침 식사를 마친 뒤, 네 신사는 그레이브젠드까지 걸어가려고 힘차게 나섰으니, 전나무 상자에 담긴 그 돌을 든 사내 하나가 뒤따랐다. 그들은 한 시쯤 그 읍에 다다랐고(짐은 로체스터에서 시내로 보내도록 일러 두었다), 다행히 마차 바깥쪽 좌석을 잡을 수 있어서, 바로 그날 오후에 건강하고 활기찬 모습으로 런던에 도착했다.\n\n이어지는 사나흘은 이턴스윌 자치구로 떠나는 여행에 필요한 준비로 채워졌다. 그 더없이 중요한 일에 관한 어떤 언급이든 별도의 장을 요하므로, 우리는 이 장의 끝머리에 남은 몇 줄을, 그 골동품 발견의 내력을 아주 간략히 이야기하는 데 바치기로 하자.\n\n그러므로 클럽의 의사록에 따르면, 픽윅 씨는 그들이 돌아온 이튿날 밤에 소집된 클럽 총회에서 그 발견에 관하여 강연했고, 그 명문의 의미에 대해 갖가지 기발하고 박학한 추론을 펼쳤다. 또한 솜씨 좋은 화공 하나가 그 진귀한 물건을 충실히 묘사한 그림을 그려, 그것을 돌에 새겨서 왕립 골동품 학회와 그 밖의 학술 단체들에 증정한 일도 드러난다. 그 주제를 두고 쓰인 경쟁적 논쟁들로 인해 이루 헤아릴 수 없는 시기와 질투가 일어났으며, 픽윅 씨 자신은 아주 작은 활자로 아흔여섯 면에 걸친, 그 명문에 대한 스물일곱 가지의 서로 다른 판독을 담은 소책자를 써냈다는 것도 드러난다. 노신사 세 사람은 그 단편의 고대성을 감히 의심했다는 이유로 제 맏아들들에게 각기 1실링씩만 떼어 주고 상속에서 내쳤으며, 어느 열성적인 인물 하나는 그 의미를 도무지 헤아릴 수 없다는 절망에 빠져 스스로 제 명을 일찍 끊어 버렸다는 것, 그리고 픽윅 씨는 그 발견을 이루어 낸 공로로 국내외 열일곱 학회의 명예 회원으로 추대되었다는 것도 드러난다. 그 열일곱 학회 가운데 어느 곳도 그것에 대해 무어라 알아내지 못했으나, 열일곱 학회 모두 그것이 참으로 비범한 물건이라는 데는 의견을 같이했다는 것 또한.\n\n사실 블로턴 씨는—그 이름은 신비롭고 숭고한 것을 가꾸는 자들의 영원한 멸시를 받을 운명에 처할 터인데—그 블로턴 씨는, 우리가 말하건대, 천박한 정신에 특유한 의심과 트집을 부려, 격을 떨어뜨리는 만큼이나 우스꽝스러운 견해를 감히 내놓았다. 블로턴 씨는 불멸의 이름 픽윅의 광채를 흐리려는 비열한 욕심에서, 실제로 몸소 코범까지 여행을 떠났고, 돌아와서는 클럽의 한 연설에서 비아냥대며 이렇게 말했다. 자신이 그 돌을 사들였던 그 사내를 만나 보았는데, 그 사내는 돌이 오래된 것이라는 데는 동의하면서도 그 명문의 고대성만큼은 엄숙히 부인했다는 것이다—왜냐하면 그자는 그것을 자신이 한가한 기분에 직접 거칠게 새긴 것이라고 진술했으며, 그 글자들은 다름 아닌 '빌 스텀프스, 그의 표시'라는 단순한 뜻 그 이상도 그 이하도 나타내려 한 것이 아니라고 했다는 것이다. 그리고 스텀프스 씨는 본디 글을 직접 지어 보는 일에 거의 익숙지 않고, 철자법의 엄정한 규칙보다는 말소리에 이끌리는 데 더 익숙한지라, 제 세례명의 끝 'L'을 빠뜨렸다는 것이었다.\n\n픽윅 클럽은 (그토록 계몽된 단체에게서 능히 기대할 법한 대로) 이 진술을 마땅한 멸시로써 받아들였고, 그 주제넘고 성미 고약한 블로턴을 회에서 축출했으며, 신임과 인정의 표시로 픽윅 씨에게 금테 안경 한 벌을 표결로 증정했다. 그 답례로 픽윅 씨는 자신의 초상화를 그리게 하여 클럽 방에 걸어 두도록 했다.\n\n블로턴 씨는 쫓겨나기는 했어도 굴복하지는 않았다. 그 역시 국내외 열일곱 학회 앞으로 소책자 한 권을 써 보냈는데, 거기에는 자신이 이미 했던 진술을 되풀이하고, 더 나아가 그 열일곱 학회가 죄다 한낱 '허풍선이들'이라는 자신의 견해를 절반 이상 내비쳤다. 이에 열일곱 학회의 의로운 분노가 들끓어, 새로운 소책자 여러 권이 나타났다. 국외 학회들은 국내 학회들과 서신을 주고받았고, 국내 학회들은 국외 학회들의 소책자를 영어로 번역했으며, 국외 학회들은 국내 학회들의 소책자를 온갖 언어로 번역했다. 그리하여 모든 사람에게 픽윅 논쟁이라는 이름으로 그토록 잘 알려진, 저 유명한 학술 논쟁이 비롯되었다.\n\n그러나 픽윅 씨를 해치려던 이 비열한 시도는 그 중상모략을 일삼은 장본인 자신의 머리 위로 되돌아왔다. 열일곱 학회는 만장일치로 그 주제넘은 블로턴을 무지한 참견꾼이라 의결하고, 곧장 그 어느 때보다도 더 많은 논문을 써 내는 일에 착수했다. 그리하여 오늘날까지도 그 돌은, 픽윅 씨의 위대함을 보여 주는 판독할 수 없는 기념비로서, 또한 그의 적들의 옹졸함을 영원히 증언하는 전리품으로서 남아 있다.\n\n고스웰 거리에 있는 픽윅 씨의 거처는 규모는 비록 한정되어 있었으나, 더없이 깔끔하고 안락할 뿐 아니라, 그와 같은 천재성과 관찰력을 갖춘 인물이 머물기에 유난히 안성맞춤이었다. 그의 거실은 이 층 앞쪽, 침실은 삼 층 앞쪽이었다. 그리하여 그는 응접실의 책상에 앉아 있을 때나, 침실의 화장 거울 앞에 서 있을 때나, 사람이 그다지 많은 것은 아니나 그만큼 번화한 그 거리에서 인간 본성이 드러내는 수많은 국면을 똑같이 관조할 기회를 누렸다. 그의 하숙집 여주인 바델 부인은—작고한 세관 관리의 미망인이자 유일한 유언 집행인으로—부산스러운 거동에 보기 좋은 용모를 지닌 잘생긴 여인이었으며, 본래 타고난 요리 솜씨가 연구와 오랜 실습을 거쳐 절묘한 재능으로 가다듬어져 있었다. 아이도 없고, 하인도 없고, 닭도 없었다. 집 안에 사는 다른 사람이라곤 덩치 큰 사내 하나와 자그마한 사내아이 하나뿐이었으니, 앞엣것은 하숙인이요, 뒤엣것은 바델 부인의 소산이었다. 덩치 큰 사내는 언제나 밤 열 시 정각에 집에 돌아와, 그 시각이면 어김없이 뒤쪽 응접실에 놓인 난쟁이만 한 프랑스식 침대의 한계 안으로 제 몸을 응축시켰고, 어린 바델 도련님의 유아적 놀이와 체조 운동은 오로지 이웃 보도와 도랑에만 국한되어 있었다. 집 안 곳곳에 청결과 정적이 군림했고, 그 안에서 픽윅 씨의 뜻은 곧 법이었다.\n\n이 집안 살림의 이런 면면을 잘 아는 사람, 그리고 픽윅 씨의 마음이 지닌 감탄스러운 절제와 규율에 정통한 사람이라면, 이턴스윌로 떠나기로 정해 둔 날의 전날 아침에 보인 그의 모습과 거동이야말로 더없이 수수께끼 같고 설명할 길 없는 것으로 비쳤으리라. 그는 황급한 걸음으로 방 안을 이리저리 거닐었고, 한 삼 분 간격으로 창밖에 머리를 쑥 내밀었으며, 끊임없이 시계를 들여다보고, 그 밖에도 평소의 그로서는 자못 드문 초조함의 징후를 여러모로 드러냈다. 무언가 대단히 중대한 일이 궁리되고 있음은 분명했으나, 그 무언가가 무엇인지는 바델 부인조차 알아낼 수 없었다.\n\n\"바델 부인.\" 마침내 픽윅 씨가 입을 열었으니, 그 다정한 여인이 방 안 먼지를 오래도록 털어 내는 일이 막바지에 다다른 무렵이었다.\n\n\"네, 나리.\" 바델 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"댁의 어린 아드님이 나간 지 참 오래되었군요.\"\n\n\"아니, 자치구까지는 꽤 먼 길이지 않습니까, 나리.\" 바델 부인이 항변했다.\n\n\"아.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"참으로 그렇군요. 정말 그래요.\" 픽윅 씨는 다시 침묵에 잠겼고, 바델 부인은 먼지 떨기를 재개했다.\n\n\"바델 부인.\" 몇 분이 지나서 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"네, 나리.\" 바델 부인이 다시 말했다.\n\n\"두 사람을 건사하는 것이 한 사람을 건사하는 것보다 비용이 훨씬 많이 든다고 생각하시오?\"\n\n\"어머나, 픽윅 씨.\" 바델 부인은 모자 가장자리까지 발갛게 물들이며 말했으니, 하숙인의 눈에 어린 일종의 혼인을 암시하는 반짝임을 본 듯한 기분이 든 까닭이었다. \"어머나, 픽윅 씨, 별 질문을 다 하시네요!\"\n\n\"글쎄, 그래도 어떻소?\" 픽윅 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"그야 경우에 따라 다르지요.\" 바델 부인은 먼지떨이를 식탁에 괸 픽윅 씨의 팔꿈치에 바짝 가까이 가져가며 말했다. \"그건 사람에 따라 크게 달라지는 거잖아요, 픽윅 씨. 또 그 사람이 절약하고 알뜰한 사람이냐 아니냐에 따라서요, 나리.\"\n\n\"참으로 옳은 말씀이오.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"하나 내가 점찍어 둔 사람은(여기서 그는 바델 부인을 아주 빤히 바라보았다) 그런 자질을 갖추었다고 생각하오. 게다가 세상 물정에 상당한 식견이 있고 매우 영민하지요, 바델 부인. 그것이 내게 실질적으로 큰 쓸모가 있을 거요.\"\n\n\"어머나, 픽윅 씨.\" 바델 부인은 또다시 모자 가장자리까지 붉은빛이 차오르며 말했다.\n\n\"정말이오.\" 자기가 흥미를 느끼는 화제를 이야기할 때면 으레 그러하듯, 픽윅 씨는 점점 열기를 더해 가며 말했다. \"정말 그렇소. 솔직히 말씀드리면, 바델 부인, 나는 이미 마음을 굳혔소.\"\n\n\"어머, 나리.\" 바델 부인이 탄성을 질렀다.\n\n\"이제 와서 참 이상하다 여기시겠지요.\" 다정한 픽윅 씨는 짝지어 앉은 이를 흐뭇한 눈길로 바라보며 말했다. \"내가 이 일을 두고 부인과 한 번도 상의하지 않았고, 오늘 아침 댁의 어린 아드님을 심부름 보낼 때까지 입 밖에도 내지 않았으니 말이오—그렇지요?\"\n\n바델 부인은 그저 눈빛으로만 대답할 수 있을 따름이었다. 그녀는 오랫동안 멀찍이서 픽윅 씨를 흠모해 왔건만, 이제 별안간 그녀의 가장 터무니없고 분에 넘치는 희망조차 감히 우러러보지 못했던 절정에 들어 올려진 것이었다. 픽윅 씨가 청혼을 하려 하다니—그것도 신중히 짜 둔 계획으로—어린 아들을 방해되지 않게 자치구로 심부름 보내다니—어쩌면 그리도 사려 깊고—어쩌면 그리도 배려심이 깊은가!\n\n\"자.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"어찌 생각하시오?\"\n\n\"오, 픽윅 씨.\" 바델 부인은 격정에 떨며 말했다. \"정말 친절하셔요, 나리.\"\n\n\"부인의 수고를 꽤나 덜어 드리게 될 거요, 그렇지 않겠소?\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"오, 저는 수고 따위는 한 번도 마음에 둔 적이 없어요, 나리.\" 바델 부인이 대답했다. \"그리고 물론 그때부터는 나리를 기쁘게 해 드리려고 여느 때보다 더 애쓸 거예요. 하지만 이렇게 제 외로움을 그토록 헤아려 주시다니, 정말 친절하셔요, 픽윅 씨.\"\n\n\"아, 그렇고말고.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"그건 미처 생각지 못했구려. 내가 시내에 있을 때면 늘 부인 곁에 함께 앉아 있을 사람이 있을 테니. 그렇고말고, 정말 그렇게 되겠군요.\"\n\n\"저는 정말이지 더없이 행복한 여자가 되어 마땅하겠어요.\" 바델 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"그리고 댁의 어린 아드님도—\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"아유, 우리 귀한 것!\" 바델 부인이 모성 어린 흐느낌과 함께 말을 가로챘다.\n\n\"그 아이에게도 동무가 생길 거요.\" 픽윅 씨가 말을 이었다. \"활기찬 동무 말이오. 장담하건대, 그 아이가 일 년 걸려도 못 배울 재주를 일주일 만에 더 많이 가르쳐 줄 거요.\" 그러고서 픽윅 씨는 잔잔하게 미소 지었다.\n\n\"오, 당신은 정말 귀한—\" 바델 부인이 말했다.\n\n픽윅 씨가 흠칫했다.\n\n\"오, 당신은 정말 친절하고 선량하고 다정한 분이셔요.\" 바델 부인은 이렇게 말하고는, 더 망설일 것도 없이 의자에서 일어나 픽윅 씨의 목을 두 팔로 휘감았으니, 폭포수 같은 눈물과 흐느낌의 합창이 곁들여졌다.\n\n\"이런 맙소사.\" 깜짝 놀란 픽윅 씨가 외쳤다. \"바델 부인, 이 선량한 여인이여—어이쿠, 이게 무슨 꼴이오—제발 좀 생각해 보시오.—바델 부인, 이러지 마시오—혹시 누가 들어오기라도 하면—\"\n\n\"오, 누구든 들어오라죠.\" 바델 부인이 정신없이 외쳤다. \"저는 절대 당신을 떠나지 않을 거예요—다정하고, 친절하고, 선량한 분이여.\" 이렇게 말하며 바델 부인은 더욱 단단히 매달렸다.\n\n\"이거 참 큰일이군.\" 픽윅 씨가 격렬히 몸부림치며 말했다. \"누가 계단을 올라오는 소리가 들리오. 이러지, 이러지 마시오, 착한 사람아, 제발 이러지 마시오.\" 그러나 애원이고 항변이고 죄다 소용없었으니, 바델 부인이 픽윅 씨의 품에서 까무러쳐 버린 데다, 그가 그녀를 의자에 내려놓을 짬을 얻기도 전에 바델 도련님이 방으로 들어서며 터프먼 씨, 윈클 씨, 스노드그래스 씨를 안내해 들였기 때문이다.\n\n픽윅 씨는 옴짝달싹 못 하고 말문이 막힌 채 우두커니 서 있었다. 그는 사랑스러운 짐을 두 팔에 안은 채, 알아보거나 해명하려는 시도라곤 손톱만큼도 하지 못하고, 멍하니 친구들의 얼굴만 바라보았다. 그들 또한 그를 빤히 쳐다보았고, 바델 도련님은 또 그 나름으로 사람마다 빤히 쳐다보았다.\n\n픽윅 회원들의 경악은 그토록 넋을 앗아갔고, 픽윅 씨의 당혹은 그토록 극심했던지라, 그 여인의 정지된 의식이 되살아날 때까지 그들은 꼭 그대로의 관계 속에 머물러 있었을지도 모른다. 그녀의 어린 아들 편에서 더없이 아름답고 가슴 뭉클한 효심의 표현이 없었던들 말이다. 무척이나 큼지막한 놋단추가 박힌 꽉 끼는 코듀로이 옷을 입은 그 아이는 처음엔 어쩔 줄 모른 채 어리둥절하여 문가에 서 있었다. 그러나 점차, 어머니가 무슨 신체적 위해를 입은 게 틀림없다는 인상이 채 여물지 않은 그의 머릿속에 퍼져 나갔고, 픽윅 씨를 가해자로 단정한 그는 소름 끼치고 거의 이승의 것 같지 않은 울부짖음을 터뜨리더니, 머리를 앞세워 들이받으며, 그 불멸의 신사의 등과 다리 언저리를 제 팔 힘과 격앙된 흥분이 허락하는 만큼의 주먹질과 꼬집기로 공격하기 시작했다.\n\n\"이 작은 악당을 데려가시오.\" 고통에 찬 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"이놈이 미쳤소.\"\n\n\"대체 무슨 일입니까?\" 혀가 굳어 버린 세 픽윅 회원이 말했다.\n\n\"나도 모르오.\" 픽윅 씨가 짜증스레 대답했다. \"저 아이나 좀 데려가시오.\" (여기서 윈클 씨가 그 흥미로운 아이를, 비명을 지르고 발버둥 치는 채로 방 저쪽 끝으로 데려갔다.) \"자, 이제 나를 좀 도와, 이 부인을 아래층으로 데려다주시오.\"\n\n\"오, 이제 좀 나아졌어요.\" 바델 부인이 가냘프게 말했다.\n\n\"제가 아래층까지 모셔다 드리지요.\" 언제나 정중한 터프먼 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"고마워요, 나리—고마워요.\" 바델 부인이 히스테리에 차 외쳤다. 그리하여 그녀는 그 말마따나 아래층으로 인도되었으니, 애정 깊은 아들이 함께했다.\n\n\"도무지 짐작이 안 가오.\" 친구가 돌아오자 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"저 여인에게 대체 무슨 일이 있었는지 도무지 짐작이 안 가오. 나는 그저 남자 하인을 하나 둘 작정이라고 알렸을 뿐인데, 여러분이 본 그 별난 발작을 일으키지 뭐요. 참으로 별난 일이구려.\"\n\n\"참으로요.\" 세 친구가 말했다.\n\n\"나를 더없이 거북한 처지에 빠뜨려 놓았소.\" 픽윅 씨가 말을 이었다.\n\n\"참으로요.\" 추종자들은 이렇게 대답하며 가볍게 헛기침을 하고는 미심쩍은 눈길로 서로를 바라보았다.\n\n이런 태도를 픽윅 씨가 놓칠 리 없었다. 그는 그들이 믿지 않음을 알아챘다. 그들은 분명 그를 의심하고 있었다.\n\n\"지금 복도에 웬 사내가 와 있소.\" 터프먼 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"내가 여러분께 말한 그 사람이오.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"오늘 아침 자치구로 사람을 보내 불러왔지요. 미안하지만 그를 올라오라 불러 주시오, 스노드그래스.\"\n\n스노드그래스 씨가 시키는 대로 하자, 곧장 새뮤얼 웰러 씨가 모습을 드러냈다.\n\n\"오—나를 기억하겠지요?\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그럼요.\" 샘이 짐짓 너그러운 듯 한쪽 눈을 찡긋하며 대답했다. \"거 참 묘한 사건이었습죠. 헌데 그놈이 나리한테 한 수 위였잖습니까, 안 그래요? 어찌나 약빠른지, 거기다 한두 술 더 떴더란 말입죠—그렇잖아요?\"\n\n\"그 일은 이제 신경 쓰지 마시게.\" 픽윅 씨가 황급히 말했다. \"다른 일을 두고 자네와 얘기를 좀 하고 싶네. 앉게나.\"\n\n\"고맙습니다요, 나리.\" 샘이 말했다. 그러고는 더 권할 것도 없이 자리에 앉았으니, 그에 앞서 낡은 흰 모자를 문밖 층계참에 내려놓았다. \"보기엔 그리 좋은 물건이 아닙죠.\" 샘이 말했다. \"하지만 쓰기엔 기막힌 물건이라, 챙이 나가기 전엔 아주 근사한 모자였습죠. 어쨌든 없으니 머리가 가볍다는 게 하나 좋은 점이고, 구멍마다 바람이 좀 들어온다는 게 또 하나 좋은 점입죠—전 그걸 환기 거미줄이라 부른답니다.\" 이런 소회를 털어놓고서 웰러 씨는 모여 있는 픽윅 회원들을 향해 싹싹하게 미소 지었다.\n\n\"자, 내가 이 신사분들의 동의를 얻어 자네를 부른 그 용건에 관해서인데.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그게 핵심입죠, 나리.\" 샘이 끼어들었다. \"어서 털어놓으십쇼. 동전 한 닢 삼킨 자식한테 아비가 한 말처럼요.\"\n\n\"우선 첫째로 우리가 알고 싶은 것은.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"자네가 지금의 처지에 불만을 가질 만한 까닭이 있느냐 하는 것일세.\"\n\n\"그 질문에 답하기 전에, 신사 여러분.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다. \"저로서는 우선 첫째로 알고 싶은 게 있는데요, 여러분이 저한테 더 나은 자리를 마련해 주실 작정이냐 하는 겁니다요.\"\n\n잔잔한 자애의 햇살이 픽윅 씨의 얼굴에 어리며 그가 말했다. \"내가 직접 자네를 고용하기로 거의 마음을 굳혔네.\"\n\n\"정말입니까요?\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n픽윅 씨가 그렇다는 뜻으로 고개를 끄덕였다.\n\n\"품삯은요?\" 샘이 물었다.\n\n\"일 년에 십이 파운드일세.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"옷은요?\"\n\n\"두 벌.\"\n\n\"일은요?\"\n\n\"나를 시중들고, 나와 여기 이 신사분들과 함께 여기저기 다니는 거지.\"\n\n\"광고지를 내리쇼.\" 샘이 힘주어 말했다. \"저는 독신 신사분께 임대되었고, 조건은 합의됐습니다요.\"\n\n\"그 자리를 받아들이겠나?\" 픽윅 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"여부가 있겠습니까요.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"옷이 자리의 반만큼이라도 제게 맞아떨어진다면야, 그걸로 충분합죠.\"\n\n\"물론 신원 보증서는 받을 수 있겠지?\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그건 화이트 하트의 여주인한테 물어보십쇼, 나리.\" 샘이 대답했다.\n\n\"오늘 저녁에 올 수 있겠나?\"\n\n\"옷만 여기 있다면야 당장 이 자리에서 갈아입겠습니다요.\" 샘이 더없이 기민하게 말했다.\n\n\"오늘 저녁 여덟 시에 들르게.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"조회 결과가 만족스러우면 옷을 마련해 주겠네.\"\n\n보조 가정부도 똑같이 가담했던 어느 다정한 경솔함 하나만을 유일한 예외로 친다면, 웰러 씨의 행실 이력은 그토록 흠잡을 데 없었던지라, 픽윅 씨는 바로 그날 저녁에 고용을 마무리 짓는 것이 충분히 정당하다고 느꼈다. 이 비범한 인물의 공적인 처사뿐 아니라 사적인 행동 일체를 특징짓는 그 신속함과 정력으로, 그는 곧장 새 시종을 데리고, 신사용 새 옷과 헌 옷을 갖추어 놓고 치수 재기라는 성가시고 번거로운 격식을 생략해 주는 그런 편리한 상점 가운데 하나로 향했다. 그리하여 밤이 채 깃들기도 전에, 웰러 씨는 P. C. 단추가 달린 회색 외투, 모표를 단 검은 모자, 분홍 줄무늬 조끼, 밝은색 반바지와 각반, 그 밖에 일일이 늘어놓기엔 너무 많은 갖가지 필수품을 갖추게 되었다.\n\n\"자아.\" 별안간 탈바꿈한 그 인물은 이튿날 아침 이턴스윌행 마차 바깥자리에 올라앉으며 말했다. \"내가 종복이 되라는 건지, 마부가 되라는 건지, 사냥터지기가 되라는 건지, 씨앗 장수가 되라는 건지 모르겠군. 꼭 그 모든 걸 한데 버무린 잡탕 같은 꼴이야. 뭐 상관없지. 공기도 바뀌고, 볼 것도 많고, 할 일은 적고. 이 모든 게 내 형편에 더없이 잘 맞아떨어지거든. 그러니 픽윅 양반들, 만수무강하시라, 내 말이 그거야!\"\n\n제13장. 이턴스윌에 관한 약간의 기록, 그곳 정당들의 상태, 그리고 그 유서 깊고 충성스럽고 애국적인 자치구를 위해 의회에 봉직할 의원을 뽑는 선거에 관하여\n\n우리는 픽윅 클럽의 방대한 문서에 처음 빠져들기 전까지는 이턴스윌이라는 곳을 들어 본 적이 없었음을 솔직히 인정한다. 또한 오늘날 그런 곳이 실제로 존재한다는 증거를 헛되이 찾았음도 같은 솔직함으로 시인한다. 픽윅 씨의 모든 기록과 진술에 깊은 신뢰를 두어야 함을 알기에, 또 그 위대한 인물이 기록으로 남긴 선언에 우리의 기억을 감히 맞세우려 하지 않기에, 우리는 참조할 수 있는 한 이 주제에 관계된 모든 권위 있는 자료를 두루 살폈다. 우리는 명부 갑과 을에 실린 모든 지명을 추적했으나 이턴스윌이라는 이름은 만나지 못했고, 우리의 저명한 출판인들이 사회의 이익을 위해 펴낸 휴대용 군 지도의 구석구석을 면밀히 살폈으나 똑같은 결과만이 우리의 조사를 따라다녔다. 그러므로 우리는 픽윅 씨가, 그 누구에게도 불쾌감을 주지 않으려는 그 애타는 바람과, 그를 잘 아는 모든 이가 그가 유난히 두드러지게 지녔음을 아는 그 섬세한 감정으로, 자신이 관찰을 행한 장소의 실제 이름 대신 일부러 가공의 명칭을 갖다 붙였다고 믿게 되었다. 우리는 그 자체로는 사소하고 하찮아 보이지만 이런 관점에서 고찰하면 주목할 가치가 없지 않은 작은 정황으로 인해 이 믿음을 더욱 굳히게 되었다. 픽윅 씨의 수첩에서 우리는 그와 그의 추종자들의 자리가 노리치 마차에 예약되었다는 사실의 기재를 겨우 알아볼 수 있는데, 이 기재는 나중에 줄이 그어져 지워졌으니, 마치 그 자치구가 위치한 방향마저 감추려는 목적에서인 듯하다. 따라서 우리는 이 주제에 대해 함부로 추측을 시도하지 않고, 그 등장인물들이 우리에게 마련해 준 자료에 만족하며 곧바로 이 역사를 이어 가겠다.\n\n그러니까 이턴스윌 사람들은, 다른 많은 작은 읍의 사람들과 마찬가지로, 자신들을 더없이 막중하고 막강한 존재로 여겼던 모양이고, 이턴스윌의 모든 사람은 자기 본보기에 실린 무게를 의식하여 읍을 갈라놓은 두 거대 정당 가운데 하나, 즉 블루파 아니면 버프파에 마음과 영혼을 다해 합쳐야 한다고 느꼈던 모양이다. 그런데 블루파는 버프파에 반대할 기회를 결코 놓치지 않았고, 버프파는 블루파에 반대할 기회를 결코 놓치지 않았다. 그 결과, 버프파와 블루파가 공개 집회, 읍사무소, 장터, 또는 시장에서 함께 마주칠 때면 언제나 그들 사이에 다툼과 격한 말이 일어났다. 이런 불화로 인해 이턴스윌에서는 모든 일이 당파 문제로 변했다는 것은 거의 말할 필요도 없다. 버프파가 시장에 새 채광창을 달자고 제안하면 블루파는 공개 집회를 열어 그 처사를 규탄했고, 블루파가 큰길에 펌프를 하나 더 세우자고 제안하면 버프파는 한 사람처럼 들고일어나 그 엄청난 일에 아연실색했다. 블루파 가게와 버프파 가게가 있었고, 블루파 여관과 버프파 여관이 있었으며, 바로 그 교회 안에도 블루파 통로와 버프파 통로가 있었다.\n\n물론 이 강력한 두 정당이 저마다 선택한 기관지와 대변자를 두는 것은 본질적이고 불가결하게 필요한 일이었다. 그리하여 읍에는 두 신문이 있었으니, 이턴스윌 가제트와 이턴스윌 인디펜던트였다. 전자는 블루파 원칙을 옹호했고, 후자는 단연 버프파의 입장에서 운영되었다. 훌륭한 신문들이었다. 그 무슨 사설들이며, 그 무슨 패기 넘치는 공격들이던가! \"우리의 가치 없는 동시대 신문 가제트,\" \"저 수치스럽고 비겁한 신문 인디펜던트,\" \"저 거짓되고 상스러운 인쇄물 인디펜던트,\" \"저 비열하고 중상모략을 일삼는 비방꾼 가제트.\" 이런 것들과 그 밖의 정신을 들끓게 하는 규탄들이 매 호마다 양쪽 지면 곳곳에 흠뻑 흩뿌려졌고, 읍 사람들의 가슴속에 더없이 강렬한 기쁨과 분노의 감정을 불러일으켰다.\n\n픽윅 씨는 평소의 선견지명과 명민함으로 이 자치구를 방문하기에 유난히 바람직한 시점을 골랐다. 일찍이 그런 경합은 없었다. 슬럼키 홀의 새뮤얼 슬럼키 각하가 블루파 후보였고, 이턴스윌 인근 피즈킨 로지의 호레이쇼 피즈킨 씨가 친구들의 권유에 못 이겨 버프파의 이익을 위해 나섰다. 가제트는 이턴스윌의 유권자들에게 영국뿐 아니라 온 문명 세계의 눈이 그들에게 쏠려 있다고 일깨웠고, 인디펜던트는 이턴스윌의 유권자들이 줄곧 여겨 온 대로 훌륭한 인물들인지, 아니면 영국인이라는 이름과 자유의 축복 양쪽 모두에 어울리지 않는 비천하고 비굴한 도구들인지 단호히 알기를 요구했다. 일찍이 그런 소동이 읍을 뒤흔든 적은 없었다.\n\n픽윅 씨와 그의 일행이 샘의 도움을 받아 이턴스윌 마차 지붕에서 내린 것은 저녁 늦은 시각이었다. 큰 푸른 비단 깃발들이 타운 암스 여관의 창문들에서 나부끼고 있었고, 모든 창틀에는 새뮤얼 슬럼키 각하의 위원회가 그곳에서 날마다 회의를 연다는 것을 거대한 글자로 알리는 벽보가 붙어 있었다. 한 무리의 한가한 사람들이 길에 모여, 발코니에 선 목쉰 사내를 쳐다보고 있었는데, 그 사내는 슬럼키 씨를 위하여 얼굴이 새빨개지도록 떠들어 대는 듯했다. 그러나 그 논변의 힘과 요지는 피즈킨 씨의 위원회가 길모퉁이에 배치해 둔 큰 북 네 개를 끊임없이 두드려 대는 통에 다소 손상되었다. 하지만 그 옆에는 바삐 움직이는 작은 사내가 있어, 이따금 모자를 벗고 사람들에게 환호하라고 손짓을 했는데, 사람들은 어김없이 더없이 열렬하게 그렇게 했다. 그래서 그 얼굴 붉은 신사가 전보다 더 새빨개지도록 계속 떠들어 대니, 그것은 누가 그의 말을 듣기라도 한 것 못지않게 그의 목적에 잘 부합하는 듯했다.\n\n픽윅 회원들이 마차에서 내리기가 무섭게 그 정직하고 독립적인 사람들의 한 분파가 그들을 에워싸더니 곧바로 귀가 멍멍해지는 세 차례의 환호를 올렸고, 이에 본대가 화답하자(군중이 무엇에 환호하는지 알 필요는 전혀 없으므로) 그것은 어마어마한 승리의 함성으로 부풀어, 발코니의 그 얼굴 붉은 사내마저 멈춰 세웠다.\n\n\"만세!\" 군중이 끝맺으며 외쳤다.\n\n\"한 번 더 환호!\" 발코니의 작은 선창자가 비명을 질렀고, 군중은 폐가 무쇠에 강철 부품이라도 박힌 듯이 다시 함성을 질렀다.\n\n\"슬럼키 만세!\" 그 정직하고 독립적인 사람들이 외쳐 댔다.\n\n\"슬럼키 만세!\" 픽윅 씨가 모자를 벗으며 메아리쳤다.\n\n\"피즈킨은 안 돼!\" 군중이 외쳐 댔다.\n\n\"물론 안 되고말고!\" 픽윅 씨가 외쳤다. \"만세!\" 그러자 마치 코끼리가 식은 고기를 달라고 종을 울렸을 때의 동물원 전체 같은 또 한 번의 함성이 일었다.\n\n\"슬럼키가 누구죠?\" 텁먼 씨가 속삭였다.\n\n\"나도 모르오.\" 픽윅 씨도 같은 어조로 대답했다. \"쉿. 아무것도 묻지 마시오. 이런 자리에서는 군중이 하는 대로 하는 게 언제나 상책이오.\"\n\n\"하지만 군중이 둘이면 어쩌죠?\" 스노드그래스 씨가 의견을 냈다.\n\n\"가장 큰 쪽과 함께 외치면 되오.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다.\n\n여러 권의 책으로도 이보다 더 많은 말을 할 수는 없었으리라.\n\n그들은 여관 안으로 들어갔고, 군중은 좌우로 갈라져 그들이 지나가도록 길을 내주며 떠들썩하게 환호했다. 가장 먼저 고려할 일은 하룻밤 묵을 숙소를 확보하는 것이었다.\n\n\"여기 침대를 얻을 수 있겠소?\" 픽윅 씨가 종업원을 부르며 물었다.\n\n\"모르겠습니다, 선생님.\" 사내가 대답했다. \"다 찼을까 봐 걱정입니다, 선생님. 알아보겠습니다, 선생님.\" 그는 그러려고 가더니 이내 돌아와, 그 신사분들이 \"블루파\"인지 물었다.\n\n픽윅 씨도 그의 일행도 어느 후보의 대의에든 절실한 관심이 없었기에, 그것은 답하기가 다소 어려운 질문이었다. 이 곤경에서 픽윅 씨는 새로 사귄 친구 퍼커 씨를 떠올렸다.\n\n\"퍼커라는 이름의 신사를 아오?\" 픽윅 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"물론입죠, 선생님. 새뮤얼 슬럼키 각하의 대리인이십니다.\"\n\n\"그분은 블루파라고 생각하는데?\"\n\n\"아, 그렇습니다, 선생님.\"\n\"그렇다면 우리도 블루파요.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. 그러나 사내가 이 선뜻한 선언에 다소 미심쩍어하는 눈치를 보이자, 그는 자기 명함을 건네며, 혹시 퍼커 씨가 여관에 계시면 곧바로 전해 달라고 일렀다. 종업원은 물러갔다가 거의 곧바로 다시 나타나, 픽윅 씨가 자기를 따라와 주십사 청하더니, 앞장서서 이층의 큰 방으로 안내했다. 그곳에는 책과 서류로 뒤덮인 긴 탁자 앞에 퍼커 씨가 앉아 있었다.\n\n\"아, 아, 친애하는 선생,\" 작은 사내가 그를 맞으러 다가오며 말했다. \"만나 뵙게 되어 매우 기쁩니다, 친애하는 선생, 매우요. 자, 앉으십시오. 그래, 뜻하신 바를 실행에 옮기셨군요. 선거를 보려고 이리로 내려오신 거지요, 그렇죠?\"\n\n픽윅 씨는 그렇다고 대답했다.\n\n\"기운찬 경합입니다, 친애하는 선생.\" 작은 사내가 말했다.\n\n\"그 말을 들으니 기쁘군요.\" 픽윅 씨가 두 손을 비비며 말했다. \"나는 어느 편에서 발휘되든 굳건한 애국심을 보는 것이 좋소. 그래, 기운찬 경합이란 말이지요?\"\n\n\"아, 그렇고말고요.\" 작은 사내가 말했다. \"정말 무척이나요. 우리는 이곳의 모든 술집을 다 차지하고, 우리 적수에게는 맥줏집만 남겨 주었지요. 그게 절묘한 정략의 한 수였습니다, 친애하는 선생, 그렇죠?\" 작은 사내는 흡족하게 미소 지으며 코담배를 한 줌 크게 들이켰다.\n\n\"그래 경합의 결과에 대한 전망은 어떻소?\" 픽윅 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"글쎄요, 불확실합니다, 친애하는 선생. 아직은 다소 불확실해요.\" 작은 사내가 대답했다. \"피즈킨 쪽 사람들이 화이트 하트의 잠긴 마차 차고에 표 서른셋을 잡아 두고 있거든요.\"\n\n\"마차 차고에라니!\" 픽윅 씨가 이 두 번째 정략의 한 수에 적잖이 놀라 말했다.\n\n\"필요할 때까지 거기에 가둬 두는 거지요.\" 작은 사내가 말을 이었다. \"그 효과는, 아시겠지만, 우리가 그들에게 접근하지 못하게 하는 것입니다. 설령 접근할 수 있다 해도 소용이 없을 게, 일부러 잔뜩 취하게 해 두니까요. 피즈킨의 대리인은 영리한 친구입니다. 정말이지 아주 영리한 친구예요.\"\n\n픽윅 씨는 눈을 휘둥그렇게 떴으나 아무 말도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"그래도 우리는 꽤 자신이 있습니다.\" 퍼커 씨가 목소리를 거의 속삭임으로 낮추며 말했다. \"어젯밤 여기서 작은 다과회를 열었지요. 부인이 마흔다섯 분이었습니다, 친애하는 선생. 그리고 한 분 한 분 돌아갈 때마다 초록색 양산을 하나씩 드렸지요.\"\n\n\"양산을!\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"사실입니다, 친애하는 선생, 사실이에요. 초록 양산 마흔다섯 개를, 하나에 칠 실링 육 펜스씩 주고요. 여자들은 다들 고운 것을 좋아하지요. 그 양산들의 효과가 어찌나 대단하던지. 그 남편들을 죄다 우리 편으로 끌어들였고, 그 형제들의 절반도 잡았습니다. 양말이니 플란넬이니 그런 따위는 댈 것도 못 됩니다. 온전히 제 생각이었지요, 친애하는 선생. 우박이 오나, 비가 오나, 햇볕이 나나, 길을 대여섯 야드도 못 걸어서 초록 양산 대여섯 개를 마주치게 되거든요.\"\n\n여기서 작은 사내는 웃음의 발작에 빠져들었는데, 그것은 제삼자가 들어오면서야 비로소 멎었다.\n\n이는 키가 크고 마른 사내였으니, 머리는 모래빛에 벗어질 듯 성기었고, 얼굴에는 엄숙한 위엄과 헤아릴 수 없는 심오함의 기색이 뒤섞여 있었다. 그는 검은 천 조끼와 황갈색 바지 차림에 긴 갈색 프록코트를 입고 있었다. 조끼에는 겹안경이 달랑거렸고, 머리에는 챙이 넓고 관이 아주 낮은 모자를 쓰고 있었다. 이 새로 온 사람은 픽윅 씨에게 이턴스윌 가제트의 편집인 포트 씨로 소개되었다. 몇 마디 의례적인 인사를 나눈 뒤, 포트 씨는 픽윅 씨를 돌아보며 엄숙하게 말했다.\n\n\"이번 경합이 수도에서 큰 관심을 불러일으키고 있지요, 선생?\"\n\n\"그런 것 같습니다.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"내가 알 만한 이유가 있는 바로는,\" 포트가 확인을 구하듯 퍼커 씨 쪽을 바라보며 말했다. \"내가 알 만한 이유가 있는 바로는, 지난 토요일 내 기사가 거기에 어느 정도 이바지했지요.\"\n\n\"조금도 의심할 여지가 없습니다.\" 작은 사내가 말했다.\n\n\"언론은 막강한 동력 기관입니다, 선생.\" 포트가 말했다.\n\n픽윅 씨는 그 명제에 전적인 동의를 표했다.\n\n\"하지만 나는, 선생,\" 포트가 말했다. \"내가 휘두르는 그 막대한 힘을 결코 남용한 적이 없다고 믿소. 나는, 선생, 내 손에 쥐여진 그 고귀한 도구를 사생활의 신성한 가슴이나 개인의 명예라는 여린 가슴을 겨누어 들이댄 적이 없다고 믿소. 나는, 선생, 내 정력을—그—노력에—비록 보잘것없을지라도, 보잘것없음을 나도 알지만—그—원칙을—그—주입하려는 노력에 바쳐 왔다고 믿소—\"\n\n여기서 이턴스윌 가제트의 편집인이 두서를 잃은 듯 보이자, 픽윅 씨가 그를 구해 주려 말했다.\n\n\"물론이지요.\"\n\n\"그래, 선생,\" 포트가 말했다. \"그래, 선생, 공정한 사람으로서 한 가지 여쭙겠는데, 인디펜던트와의 내 경합에 관하여 런던 여론의 상태가 어떻소?\"\n\n\"틀림없이 크게 들끓고 있겠지요.\" 퍼커 씨가 끼어들었는데, 그 교활한 눈빛은 십중팔구 우연이었으리라.\n\n\"그 경합은,\" 포트가 말했다. \"나에게 건강과 기력이 있고 내가 타고난 그 얼마간의 재능이 있는 한 길이 이어질 것이오. 그 경합으로 사람들의 마음이 흔들리고 감정이 들끓어 평범한 일상의 일을 해낼 수 없게 될지라도, 그 경합에서, 선생, 나는 이턴스윌 인디펜던트를 발뒤꿈치로 짓밟아 버릴 때까지 결코 물러서지 않을 것이오. 나는 런던 사람들과 이 나라 사람들이 알아주기를 바라오, 선생, 그들이 나를 믿어도 된다는 것을—내가 그들을 저버리지 않으리라는 것을, 내가 끝까지 그들 곁을 지키기로 결심했다는 것을, 선생.\"\n\n\"선생의 처신은 더없이 고결합니다, 선생.\" 픽윅 씨가 말하며, 도량 넓은 포트의 손을 꽉 잡았다.\n\n\"선생은, 보아하니, 분별과 재능을 지닌 분이오.\" 포트 씨가 자신의 애국적 선언의 격정에 거의 숨이 차서 말했다. \"그런 분과 사귀게 되어 더없이 기쁩니다, 선생.\"\n\n\"그리고 저는,\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"선생의 그 의견 표명에 깊이 영광을 느낍니다. 선생, 제 길동무들이자, 제가 자랑스럽게 창립한 클럽의 다른 통신 회원들을 소개해 드리도록 허락해 주십시오.\"\n\n\"기꺼이 그러지요.\" 포트 씨가 말했다.\n\n픽윅 씨는 물러갔다가 친구들과 함께 돌아와, 격식에 맞게 그들을 이턴스윌 가제트의 편집인에게 소개했다.\n\n\"자, 친애하는 포트,\" 작은 퍼커 씨가 말했다. \"문제는 여기 우리 친구분들을 어찌해 드리느냐 하는 거요.\"\n\n\"우리는 이 여관에 묵을 수 있겠지요?\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"이 집에는 남는 침대가 하나도 없어요, 친애하는 선생—침대 한 개도요.\"\n\n\"몹시 난처하군요.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"무척이나요.\" 그의 길동무들이 말했다.\n\n\"이 일에 대해 한 가지 생각이 있는데,\" 포트 씨가 말했다. \"아주 성공적으로 채택될 수 있을 것 같소. 피콕에 침대가 둘 있으니, 나는 포트 부인을 대신하여 자신 있게 말할 수 있소, 다른 두 신사분과 그 하인이 피콕에서 형편껏 임시변통하는 데 반대만 안 하신다면, 부인은 픽윅 씨와 그 친구분 한 분을 기꺼이 모실 거라고 말이오.\"\n\n포트 씨가 거듭 권하고 픽윅 씨가 그 상냥한 부인에게 불편이나 폐를 끼칠 수는 없다고 거듭 손사래를 친 끝에, 그것이 이루어질 수 있는 유일한 실행 가능한 방안이라고 결론이 났다. 그리하여 그렇게 정해졌다. 타운 암스에서 함께 저녁을 든 뒤 친구들은 헤어졌으니, 텁먼 씨와 스노드그래스 씨는 피콕으로 향하고, 픽윅 씨와 윈클 씨는 포트 씨의 저택으로 갔다. 그들 모두 아침에 타운 암스에서 다시 모여, 새뮤얼 슬럼키 각하의 행렬을 따라 후보 지명 장소로 가기로 미리 정해 둔 터였다.\n\n포트 씨의 가정 식구는 그 자신과 아내로 한정되어 있었다. 막강한 천재성 덕분에 세상에서 자랑스러운 높은 자리에 오른 모든 사람은 대개 어떤 작은 약점을 지니고 있는데, 그 약점은 그들의 전반적인 성격과 이루는 대조로 인해 한층 더 두드러지게 마련이다. 만약 포트 씨에게 약점이 있었다면, 그것은 아마도 아내의 다소 경멸 어린 통제와 지배에 너무 고분고분하다는 점이었으리라. 그러나 우리는 이 사실을 특별히 강조하는 것이 정당하다고 느끼지 않으니, 이날만큼은 포트 부인의 가장 사랑스러운 매력이 모조리 동원되어 두 신사를 맞이했기 때문이다.\n\n\"여보,\" 포트 씨가 말했다. \"픽윅 씨요—런던의 픽윅 씨.\"\n\n포트 부인은 픽윅 씨가 아버지처럼 손을 꽉 잡는 것을 매혹적인 다정함으로 받아들였다. 그리고 통 소개받지 못한 윈클 씨는 눈에 띄지 않게 으슥한 구석에서 비실비실 다가가 인사를 했다.\n\n\"여보 피,\" 포트 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"왜 그러오, 내 사랑.\" 포트 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"저 다른 신사분을 좀 소개해 주세요.\"\n\n\"천만번 용서를 비오.\" 포트 씨가 말했다. \"포트 부인, 이분은—\"\n\n\"윈클입니다.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"윈클.\" 포트 씨가 되받았고, 이로써 소개의 의식이 완결되었다.\n\n\"이렇게 갑작스레 댁의 가정 살림을 어지럽혀 드려, 여러모로 송구합니다, 부인.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그런 말씀 마세요, 선생.\" 여성다운 포트 부인이 발랄하게 대답했다. \"새로운 얼굴을 뵙는 것은 제게는 더없는 즐거움이랍니다, 정말로요. 저는 이 따분한 곳에서 날마다, 주마다, 아무도 만나지 못한 채 살고 있거든요.\"\n\n\"아무도라니, 여보!\" 포트 씨가 짐짓 능청을 떨며 외쳤다.\n\n\"당신 말고는 아무도요.\" 포트 부인이 쌀쌀맞게 받아쳤다.\n\n\"보십시오, 픽윅 씨,\" 주인이 아내의 한탄을 해명하듯 말했다. \"우리는 어느 정도 여러 즐거움과 기쁨에서 동떨어져 있습니다. 그것들을 누릴 수도 있을 텐데 말이지요. 이턴스윌 가제트 편집인으로서의 제 공적 지위, 그 신문이 이 나라에서 차지하는 위치, 정치의 소용돌이에 끊임없이 몰두하는 일이—\"\n\n\"여보 피—\" 포트 부인이 끼어들었다.\n\n\"내 사랑—\" 편집인이 말했다.\n\n\"여보, 부디 이분들이 좀 합리적으로 관심을 가질 만한 화제를 좀 찾아보려고 애써 주세요.\"\n\n\"하지만, 내 사랑,\" 포트 씨가 자못 겸손하게 말했다. \"픽윅 씨는 거기에 관심을 가지신다오.\"\n\n\"그분이야 그럴 수 있다면 다행이죠.\" 포트 부인이 힘주어 말했다. \"저는 당신의 정치니, 인디펜던트와의 다툼이니, 그 허튼소리에 진절머리가 나서 죽겠어요. 피, 당신이 그렇게 자기 어리석음을 떠벌리시다니 정말 어이가 없네요.\"\n\n\"하지만, 여보—\" 포트 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"아, 허튼소리예요, 제게 말 거시지 마세요.\" 포트 부인이 말했다. \"에카르테 하실 줄 아세요, 선생?\"\n\n\"부인의 가르침을 받아 배운다면 더없이 기쁘겠습니다.\" 윈클 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"그럼, 저 작은 탁자를 이 창가로 끌어다 놓으세요. 그 따분한 정치 얘기가 안 들리는 데로 좀 갑시다.\"\n\n\"제인,\" 포트 씨가 촛불을 들고 들어온 하녀에게 말했다. \"사무실로 내려가서 천팔백이십육 년도 가제트 합본을 가져오너라. 읽어 드리지요.\" 편집인이 픽윅 씨를 돌아보며 덧붙였다. \"그때 여기 통행료 징수소에 새 통행료 징수원을 임명한 버프파의 농간에 관해 내가 쓴 사설을 몇 편 읽어 드리리다. 아마 흥미로워하실 겁니다.\"\n\n\"정말이지 꼭 듣고 싶습니다.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n합본이 올라왔고, 편집인은 픽윅 씨를 곁에 두고 자리에 앉았다.\n\n우리는 이 아름다운 글들의 전반적인 요약을 만나 보려는 바람으로 픽윅 씨의 수첩 갈피를 헛되이 뒤졌다. 우리는 그가 그 문체의 활력과 신선함에 완전히 황홀해했으리라고 믿을 만한 온갖 근거를 가지고 있다. 사실 윈클 씨는 그것들을 낭독하는 내내 픽윅 씨의 눈이, 마치 지나친 기쁨에 겨운 듯, 감겨 있었다는 사실을 기록해 두었다.\n\n밤참이 차려졌다는 알림에 에카르테 놀이도, 이턴스윌 가제트의 아름다움에 대한 요약 낭독도 모두 멎었다. 포트 부인은 더없이 들떠 더없이 유쾌한 기분이었다. 윈클 씨는 이미 그녀의 호감을 상당히 얻어 둔 터라, 그녀는 픽윅 씨가 \"유쾌한 노신사 양반\"이라고 그에게 은밀히 일러 주기를 주저하지 않았다. 이런 표현은 친밀한 표현법을 담고 있으니, 그 거대한 정신의 소유자를 가까이 알던 이들 가운데 감히 그런 말을 함부로 써 본 사람은 거의 없었을 것이다. 그럼에도 우리는 그것을 그대로 보존해 두었으니, 그가 사회의 모든 계층으로부터 받던 존경과, 그가 그들의 마음과 감정 속으로 파고드는 그 수월함을 한꺼번에 보여 주는 감동적이고도 설득력 있는 증거가 되기 때문이다.\n\n밤이 늦은 시각—텁먼 씨와 스노드그래스 씨가 피콕의 가장 깊숙한 구석에서 잠든 지 한참 뒤—에야 두 친구는 잠자리에 들었다. 윈클 씨의 감각에는 이내 잠이 내려앉았으나, 그의 감정은 들떠 있었고 그의 흠모는 깨어나 있었다. 그리하여 잠이 그를 세상사에 무감각하게 만든 뒤로도 여러 시간 동안, 그 사랑스러운 포트 부인의 얼굴과 모습이 그의 떠도는 상상 속에 거듭거듭 나타났다.\n\n아침을 알리는 소음과 부산함은, 세상에 둘도 없는 가장 낭만적인 몽상가의 마음에서라도, 빠르게 다가오는 선거와 직접 관련된 것 말고는 어떤 연상도 떨쳐 버리기에 충분했다. 북 두드리는 소리, 뿔피리와 나팔 부는 소리, 사람들의 외침, 말발굽 소리가 동틀 무렵부터 거리에 메아리치고 또 메아리쳤다. 그리고 양 당파의 경무장 척후병들 사이에서 이따금 벌어지는 싸움질은 그 준비를 활기차게 하는 동시에 그 성격을 흥겹게 다채롭게 했다.\n\n\"그래, 샘,\" 픽윅 씨가 막 몸단장을 끝마칠 무렵 침실 문에 시종이 나타나자 말했다. \"오늘은 다들 활기가 넘치겠지?\"\n\n\"아주 신납니다, 선생님.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다. \"우리 편 사람들이 타운 암스 아래에 모여서, 벌써 목이 쉬도록 고함을 질러 대고 있습죠.\"\n\n\"아,\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"다들 자기 당파에 헌신하는 것 같더냐, 샘?\"\n\n\"제 평생 그런 헌신은 본 적이 없습죠, 선생님.\"\n\n\"기운들이 넘치고, 응?\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"보통이 아닙죠.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"사람들이 그렇게 먹고 마셔 대는 건 여태 못 봤습니다. 배가 터지는 게 안 무서운가 봐요.\"\n\n\"그건 이곳 신사 양반들의 빗나간 친절이지.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그럴 법도 합죠.\" 샘이 짧게 대답했다.\n\n\"다들 멀쩡하고 싱싱하고 기운찬 친구들 같구나.\" 픽윅 씨가 창밖을 흘끗 보며 말했다.\n\n\"아주 싱싱합죠.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"저랑 피콕 종업원 둘이서, 어젯밤 거기서 밤참 먹은 독립 유권자들한테 펌프질을 해 댔거든요.\"\n\n\"독립 유권자들한테 펌프질을 하다니!\" 픽윅 씨가 외쳤다.\n\n\"네.\" 그의 시종이 말했다. \"다들 쓰러진 자리에서 그대로 잤습죠. 오늘 아침에 하나씩 하나씩 끌어내서 펌프 밑에 갖다 놓았더니, 이제 다들 멀쩡하게 좋은 꼴이 됐습니다. 그 일 하라고 위원회가 한 사람당 일 실링씩 줬습죠.\"\n\n\"그런 일이 다 있을 수 있다니!\" 깜짝 놀란 픽윅 씨가 외쳤다.\n\n\"맙소사, 선생님,\" 샘이 말했다. \"도대체 어디서 반쪽짜리 세례라도 받으셨나요? 그건 아무것도 아닙니다요, 그건요.\"\n\n\"아무것도 아니라고?\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"전혀 아무것도 아닙죠, 선생님.\" 그의 시종이 대답했다. \"여기서 지난번 선거 마지막 날 전날 밤에는, 반대편 당파가 타운 암스 술집 아가씨를 매수해서, 그 집에 묵고 있던 투표 안 한 유권자 열넷의 브랜디 워터에 약을 탔습죠.\"\n\n\"브랜디 워터에 '약을 탔다'는 게 무슨 뜻이냐?\" 픽윅 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"아편 팅크를 탄 거죠.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"글쎄 그 아가씨가 선거 끝나고 열두 시간 뒤까지 그자들을 죄다 곯아떨어지게 만들었지 뭡니까. 시험 삼아 한 사람을 짐수레에 실어서 곤히 잠든 채로 투표소까지 데려갔는데, 소용이 없었어요. 투표를 안 받아 주더랍니다. 그래서 도로 데려다가 다시 침대에 눕혔습죠.\"\n\n\"참 별난 짓들이로구나.\" 픽윅 씨가 절반은 혼잣말로, 절반은 샘에게 말하듯 했다.\n\n\"바로 이곳에서, 선거 때, 제 친아버지한테 일어난 기적 같은 사건에 비하면 절반도 별난 게 아닙죠, 선생님.\" 샘이 대답했다.\n\n\"그게 무슨 일이었느냐?\" 픽윅 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"그게요, 아버지가 한번은 이리로 마차를 몰고 왔거든요.\" 샘이 말했다. \"선거철이 닥쳤는데, 한쪽 당파가 아버지를 고용해서 런던에서 유권자들을 실어 오게 했습죠. 마차를 몰고 올라가기로 한 전날 밤, 반대편 위원회가 아버지를 슬그머니 부르더니, 아버지가 심부름꾼을 따라가니까 그자가 안으로 들여보냈답니다. 큰 방에, 신사 양반들이 잔뜩, 서류며 펜이며 잉크며 그런 것들이 산더미였죠. '아, 웰러 씨,' 의장석에 앉은 신사가 말합니다, '만나서 반갑소, 선생, 어떻게 지내시오?'—'아주 잘 지냅니다, 고맙습니다, 선생.' 아버지가 말했죠. '나리도 그럭저럭 잘 지내시길 바랍니다.' 했답니다.—'그럭저럭 잘 지내오, 고맙소, 선생,' 신사가 말합니다. '앉으시오, 웰러 씨—자, 앉으시오, 선생.' 그래서 아버지가 앉으니까, 아버지하고 그 신사하고 서로 아주 빤히 쳐다봤답니다. '나를 기억 못 하시오?' 신사가 말합니다.—'기억난다고는 못 하겠는뎁쇼.' 아버지가 말했죠.—'아, 나는 당신을 아오,' 신사가 말합니다. '당신이 꼬마였을 때 알았지요.' 하더랍니다.—'글쎄, 나는 나리가 기억 안 나는뎁쇼.' 아버지가 말했죠.—'그것참 이상하구려.' 신사가 말합니다.—'이상하죠.' 아버지가 말했죠.—'기억력이 나쁘신가 보오, 웰러 씨.' 신사가 말합니다.—'글쎄, 아주 나쁜 놈입죠.' 아버지가 말했죠.—'그럴 줄 알았소.' 신사가 말합니다. 그러더니 아버지한테 포도주 한 잔을 따라 주고, 마차 모는 솜씨를 추켜세워 가며 아버지를 아주 기분 좋게 만들어 놓더니, 마침내 아버지 손에 이십 파운드짜리 지폐를 슬쩍 찔러 줬습죠. '여기서 런던까지 길이 아주 험하지요.' 신사가 말합니다.—'군데군데 무거운 길이긴 합죠.' 아버지가 말했죠.—'특히 운하 근처가 그렇겠지요.' 신사가 말합니다.—'고약한 구석이죠, 거기가요.' 아버지가 말했죠.—'그런데, 웰러 씨,' 신사가 말합니다. '당신은 아주 솜씨 좋은 마부고, 말을 마음대로 부릴 줄 안다는 걸 우리도 알지요. 우리는 다들 당신을 아주 좋아한다오, 웰러 씨. 그러니 혹시 이 유권자들을 실어 내려올 때 사고가 나서, 그 사람들을 다치게 하지 않고 운하에 처박게 되거든, 이건 당신 몫이오.' 하더랍니다.—'나리, 참 친절하십니다,' 아버지가 말했죠. '포도주 한 잔 더 들면서 나리 건강을 위해 마시겠습니다.' 했답니다. 그래서 그렇게 하고는 돈을 단단히 챙겨 넣고 굽실거리며 나왔습죠. 그런데 믿지 못하실 겁니다, 선생님,\" 샘이 주인을 향해 이루 말할 수 없이 뻔뻔한 표정을 지으며 말을 이었다. \"바로 그 유권자들을 싣고 내려온 바로 그날, 아버지 마차가 바로 그 자리에서 뒤집혀서, 그 사람들이 죄다 운하에 처박혀 버렸지 뭡니까.\"\n\n\"그래서 다시 건져 냈느냐?\" 픽윅 씨가 황급히 물었다.\n\n\"그게요,\" 샘이 아주 느릿느릿 대답했다. \"노신사 한 분이 안 보였던 것 같습니다. 모자는 찾았다는 걸 아는데, 그 안에 머리가 들어 있었는지 어떤지는 잘 모르겠어요. 하지만 제가 주목하는 건, 그 신사가 그런 말을 한 뒤에, 아버지 마차가 바로 그 자리에서, 바로 그날에 뒤집혔다는 그 어마어마하고 놀라운 우연의 일치란 말입죠!\"\n\n\"그건 의심할 바 없이 참으로 비상한 정황이로구나.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"그건 그렇고 내 모자를 좀 솔질해 다오, 샘. 윈클 씨가 아침을 들자고 나를 부르는 소리가 들리는구나.\"\n\n이 말과 함께 픽윅 씨는 응접실로 내려갔는데, 거기에는 아침 식사가 차려져 있고 가족들은 이미 모여 있었다. 식사는 서둘러 끝났다. 신사들의 모자에는 저마다 포트 부인이 손수 고운 손으로 만든 거대한 푸른 휘장이 장식되었다. 그리고 윈클 씨가 그 부인을 후보 지명 연단 바로 근처의 한 지붕 위로 모시기로 맡았으므로, 픽윅 씨와 포트 씨만 함께 타운 암스로 갔다. 그곳 뒤 창문에서는 슬럼키 씨의 위원 한 명이 사내아이 여섯과 계집아이 하나를 향해 연설을 하고 있었는데, 그는 한 문장 걸러 한 번씩 그들에게 \"이턴스윌의 사나이들\"이라는 위풍당당한 칭호를 붙여 주었고, 그러면 앞서 말한 사내아이 여섯이 엄청나게 환호했다.\n\n마구간 마당은 이턴스윌 블루파의 영광과 위세를 명백히 드러내는 징표들을 펼쳐 보였다. 손잡이가 하나인 것도 있고 둘인 것도 있는 푸른 깃발들이 정규군처럼 늘어서서, 높이 사 피트에 그에 걸맞게 굵직한 황금빛 글자로 알맞은 도안들을 드러내고 있었다. 나팔과 바순과 북으로 이루어진 웅장한 악대가 네 줄로 정렬하여, 일찍이 사람이 제값을 한 적이 있다면 바로 그렇게 제 삯값을 하고 있었으니, 특히 북 치는 자들은 근육이 어찌나 우람했던지. 푸른 곤봉을 든 경관 부대들, 푸른 띠를 두른 위원 스무 명, 그리고 푸른 모표를 단 유권자 무리가 있었다. 말을 탄 유권자도 있고 걸어서 온 유권자도 있었다. 새뮤얼 슬럼키 각하를 위한 사두마차 한 대가 있었고, 그의 친구와 지지자들을 위한 이두마차 네 대가 있었다. 깃발은 펄럭이고, 악대는 연주하고, 경관들은 욕설을 퍼붓고, 위원 스무 명은 옥신각신하고, 군중은 외쳐 대고, 말들은 뒷걸음치고, 역마차 마부들은 땀을 흘렸다. 그리고 그때 그곳에 모인 모든 사람과 모든 것이, 연합 왕국 의회 하원에서 이턴스윌 자치구의 대표직을 노리는 후보 가운데 한 사람인, 슬럼키 홀의 새뮤얼 슬럼키 각하를 위한 특별한 쓰임과 이익과 명예와 명성을 위한 것이었다.\n\n아래 군중이 한 창문에서 모래빛 머리의 포트 씨를 알아보았을 때, 환호는 우렁차고 길었으며 \"언론의 자유\"라고 적힌 푸른 깃발 하나의 펄럭임은 우람했다. 그리고 새뮤얼 슬럼키 각하 본인이 통가죽 장화에 푸른 목도리를 두르고 앞으로 나와 앞서 말한 포트의 손을 잡고서, 자신이 이턴스윌 가제트에 진 잊을 수 없는 은혜를 군중에게 과장된 몸짓으로 증언했을 때, 그 열광은 어마어마했다.\n\n\"모든 게 준비되었소?\" 새뮤얼 슬럼키 각하가 퍼커 씨에게 말했다.\n\n\"모두요, 친애하는 선생.\" 작은 사내가 대답했다.\n\n\"빠뜨린 건 없겠지요?\" 새뮤얼 슬럼키 각하가 말했다.\n\n\"빠뜨린 건 하나도 없습니다, 친애하는 선생—단 하나도요. 거리 문 앞에 깨끗이 씻은 사내 스무 명이 선생과 악수하려고 대기하고 있고, 팔에 안긴 아기 여섯이 있으니 머리를 토닥여 주고 나이를 물어봐 주십시오. 아이들에게 신경을 쓰십시오, 친애하는 선생—그런 것이 늘 큰 효과를 내거든요.\"\n\n\"명심하겠소.\" 새뮤얼 슬럼키 각하가 말했다.\n\n\"그리고, 혹시, 친애하는 선생,\" 신중한 작은 사내가 말했다. \"혹시 하실 수 있다면—꼭 필요하다는 말씀은 아닙니다만—그중 하나에게 입을 맞춰 주실 수 있다면, 군중에게 아주 큰 인상을 남길 겁니다.\"\n\n\"제안자나 추천자가 그렇게 해도 똑같이 좋은 효과가 나지 않겠소?\" 새뮤얼 슬럼키 각하가 말했다.\n\n\"글쎄요, 그렇지는 못할 것 같습니다.\" 대리인이 대답했다. \"선생께서 직접 하신다면, 친애하는 선생, 선생의 인기가 아주 높아질 것 같습니다.\"\n\n\"좋소.\" 새뮤얼 슬럼키 각하가 체념한 듯한 기색으로 말했다. \"그럼 해야지요. 그뿐이오.\"\n\n\"행렬을 정돈하라!\" 위원 스무 명이 외쳤다.\n\n모여든 무리의 환호 속에, 악대와 경관들과 위원들과 유권자들과 기마병들과 마차들이 저마다 제자리를 잡았다. 이두마차마다 그 안에 똑바로 설 수 있는 한 많은 신사들이 빽빽이 들어찼고, 퍼커 씨에게 배정된 마차에는 픽윅 씨, 텁먼 씨, 스노드그래스 씨, 그리고 그 밖에 위원 여섯 명가량이 탔다.\n\n행렬이 새뮤얼 슬럼키 각하가 마차에 오르기를 기다리는 동안 끔찍한 긴장의 순간이 있었다. 갑자기 군중이 큰 환호를 올렸다.\n\n\"각하가 나오셨군.\" 작은 퍼커 씨가 크게 흥분하여 말했는데, 그들의 위치에서는 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 볼 수 없었기에 더욱 그러했다.\n\n또 한 번, 훨씬 더 우렁찬 환호.\n\n\"각하가 사람들과 악수하셨군.\" 작은 대리인이 외쳤다.\n\n또 한 번, 한층 더 격렬한 환호.\n\n\"각하가 아기들 머리를 토닥이셨군.\" 퍼커 씨가 불안에 떨며 말했다.\n\n공기를 찢는 갈채의 함성.\n\n\"각하가 그중 하나에게 입을 맞추셨군!\" 기뻐 어쩔 줄 모르는 작은 사내가 외쳤다.\n\n두 번째 함성.\n\n\"각하가 또 하나에게 입을 맞추셨군.\" 흥분한 관리자가 헐떡이며 말했다.\n\n세 번째 함성.\n\n\"각하가 죄다 입을 맞추고 계셔!\" 열광한 작은 신사가 비명을 질렀고, 군중의 귀가 멍멍해지는 외침에 환영받으며 행렬이 움직이기 시작했다.\n\n그것이 어떻게, 또는 무슨 수로 다른 행렬과 뒤엉키게 되었는지, 그리고 그로 인해 빚어진 혼란에서 도대체 어떻게 빠져나왔는지는 우리가 감히 묘사하겠다고 나설 수 있는 바가 아니니, 픽윅 씨의 모자가 일이 시작되자마자 아주 일찌감치 버프파 깃대에 한 번 쿡 찔려 그의 눈과 코와 입 위로 푹 내려앉아 버렸기 때문이다. 그는 그 장면을 어쩌다 한 번씩 흘끗 볼 수 있었을 때, 사방이 성나고 사나운 얼굴들과, 광대한 흙먼지 구름과, 빽빽한 싸움꾼 무리에 에워싸여 있더라고 묘사한다. 그는 자신이 어떤 보이지 않는 힘에 의해 마차에서 떠밀려 나와, 몸소 권투 시합에 휘말렸더라고 진술하는데, 다만 누구와, 어떻게, 또는 왜 그랬는지는 도무지 말할 수가 없다고 한다. 이윽고 그는 뒤에 있는 사람들에게 떠밀려 어떤 나무 계단을 올라가는 자신을 느꼈고, 모자를 벗고 보니 자기 친구들에게 둘러싸인 채 후보 지명 연단 왼편의 맨 앞자리에 있었다. 오른편은 버프파 몫으로, 가운데는 시장과 그의 관리들 몫으로 비워져 있었다. 그 관리들 가운데 하나—이턴스윌의 뚱뚱한 외침꾼—가 정숙을 명하느라 거대한 종을 울리고 있었고, 그동안 호레이쇼 피즈킨 씨와 새뮤얼 슬럼키 각하는 가슴에 손을 얹은 채, 앞쪽 빈터를 가득 메운 머리들의 어수선한 바다를 향해 더없이 상냥하게 절을 하고 있었으니, 그 바다에서는 지진에라도 명예가 될 만한 신음과 외침과 비명과 야유의 폭풍이 일었다.\n\n\"저기 윈클이 있군.\" 텁먼 씨가 친구의 소매를 잡아당기며 말했다.\n\n\"어디?\" 픽윅 씨가, 다행히 그때껏 주머니에 넣어 둔 안경을 쓰며 말했다.\n\n\"저기,\" 텁먼 씨가 말했다. \"저 집 꼭대기에.\" 그러자 과연 거기에, 기와지붕의 납 홈통에 윈클 씨와 포트 부인이 의자 두 개에 편안히 앉아, 알아본다는 표시로 손수건을 흔들고 있었다. 픽윅 씨는 부인에게 손에 입을 맞춰 보이며 그 인사에 화답했다.\n\n연설은 아직 시작되지 않았다. 그런데 일거리 없는 군중은 대체로 익살을 부리기 마련이라, 이 더없이 순진한 동작 하나만으로도 그들의 우스개 기질을 일깨우기에 충분했다.\n\n\"오, 이 고약한 늙은 악당 같으니,\" 한 목소리가 외쳤다. \"처녀들이나 노리고 있는 거요?\"\n\n\"오, 이 점잖빼는 죄인 양반아.\" 다른 목소리가 외쳤다.\n\n\"안경까지 쓰고 유부녀를 쳐다보네!\" 세 번째 목소리가 말했다.\n\n\"저 고약한 늙은 눈으로 부인한테 추파를 던지는 게 보이는구먼.\" 네 번째 목소리가 외쳤다.\n\n\"마누라나 잘 챙기쇼, 포트.\" 다섯 번째 목소리가 고함쳤다. 그러고는 폭소가 터졌다.\n\n이 조롱들에는 픽윅 씨를 늙은 숫양에 빗대는 험한 비유와, 그와 비슷한 종류의 재담 몇 가지가 곁들여졌고, 게다가 그것들은 죄 없는 한 부인의 명예에 대한 비방까지 은근히 내비치는 경향이 있었기에, 픽윅 씨의 분노는 이만저만이 아니었다. 그러나 바로 그 순간 정숙이 선포되었으므로, 그는 그들의 그릇된 정신을 가엾이 여기는 눈빛으로 군중을 쏘아보는 것으로 만족했는데, 그러자 그들은 전보다 더 떠들썩하게 웃어 댔다.\n\n\"정숙!\" 시장의 수행원들이 고함쳤다.\n\n\"휘핀, 정숙을 선포하라.\" 시장이 자신의 드높은 지위에 걸맞은 위풍 어린 태도로 말했다. 이 명령에 따라 외침꾼은 종으로 또 한 차례 협주곡을 연주했고, 그러자 군중 속의 한 신사가 \"머핀!\" 하고 외쳐 또 한 번 웃음을 자아냈다.\n\n\"신사 여러분,\" 시장이 한껏 짜낼 수 있는 가장 큰 목청으로 말했다. \"신사 여러분. 이턴스윌 자치구의 동료 유권자 여러분. 우리는 오늘 우리의 작고하신—의 자리를 대신할 대표를 뽑고자 이 자리에 모였습니다—\"\n\n여기서 시장은 군중 속의 한 목소리에 말이 끊겼다.\n\n\"시장 만세!\" 그 목소리가 외쳤다. \"그리고 그가 돈을 벌게 해 준 못과 냄비 장사를 결코 저버리지 않기를!\"\n\n연설가의 직업적 생업을 이렇게 빗댄 말은 환희의 폭풍으로 받아들여졌고, 그것이 종 반주와 어우러져 그의 연설 나머지를 알아들을 수 없게 만들었으니, 다만 마지막 문장만은 예외였다. 그 문장에서 그는 모임이 처음부터 끝까지 참을성 있게 경청해 준 데 감사를 표했는데, 이 감사의 표현은 약 십오 분간 이어진 또 한 차례의 웃음을 터뜨리게 했다.\n\n다음으로, 아주 빳빳한 흰 목도리를 두른 키 크고 마른 신사가, 군중으로부터 \"베개 밑에 목소리를 두고 오지 않았는지 알아보라고 집에 애를 하나 보내라\"는 말을 거듭 들은 끝에, 의회에서 자신들을 대표할 적격의 인물을 지명하겠다고 청했다. 그리고 그가 그 인물이 이턴스윌 인근 피즈킨 로지의 호레이쇼 피즈킨 씨라고 말하자, 피즈킨파는 갈채를 보내고 슬럼키파는 어찌나 오래, 어찌나 크게 신음을 토했던지, 그와 추천자 둘 다 연설하는 대신 우스개 노래를 불렀더라도 아무도 더 알아채지 못했을 정도였다.\n\n호레이쇼 피즈킨 씨의 친구들이 자기네 차례를 다 치르고 나자, 다소 성마른 분홍빛 얼굴의 작은 사내가 앞으로 나서서, 이턴스윌 유권자들을 의회에서 대표할 또 한 사람의 적격 인물을 추천했다. 그리고 이 분홍빛 얼굴의 신사는, 군중의 장난기를 충분히 알아챌 만큼 마음의 여유를 갖기에는 너무 성마르지만 않았더라면, 아주 순조롭게 일을 풀어 나갔을 것이다. 그러나 비유적인 능변을 몇 마디 늘어놓은 끝에, 분홍빛 얼굴의 신사는 군중 속에서 자신의 말을 가로막는 자들을 규탄하던 데서 나아가, 연단 위 신사들과 도전적인 말을 주고받기에 이르렀다. 그러자 큰 소동이 일어, 그는 어쩔 수 없이 자기 감정을 진지한 무언극으로 표현해야 할 지경이 되었고, 실제로 그렇게 한 다음 무대를 추천자에게 넘겼다. 추천자는 반 시간 길이의 원고 연설을 늘어놓았고, 멈추려 하지 않았으니, 그 원고를 죄다 이턴스윌 가제트에 보냈고 이턴스윌 가제트가 이미 그것을 한 글자도 빠짐없이 인쇄해 둔 까닭이었다.\n\n이윽고 이턴스윌 인근 피즈킨 로지의 호레이쇼 피즈킨 씨가 유권자들에게 연설하고자 모습을 드러냈다. 그가 그러기가 무섭게, 새뮤얼 슬럼키 각하가 고용한 악대가, 그날 아침의 기세는 아무것도 아니었을 만큼의 위력으로 연주를 시작했다. 그 보답으로 버프파 군중은 블루파 군중의 머리와 어깨를 두들겨 댔고, 이에 블루파 군중은 그 더없이 거북한 이웃인 버프파 군중을 떨쳐 내려 애썼다. 그리하여 몸싸움과 밀치기와 주먹다짐의 장면이 이어졌으니, 우리는 그것을 제대로 묘사할 수 없거니와 시장 역시 그러지 못했으니, 비록 그가 경관 열둘에게 주모자들—그 수가 이백오십 명쯤, 아니면 그 언저리쯤 되었을—을 붙잡으라고 단호히 명령하긴 했지만 말이다. 이 모든 충돌에서 피즈킨 로지의 호레이쇼 피즈킨 씨와 그의 친구들은 사납고 격렬해졌다. 마침내 피즈킨 로지의 호레이쇼 피즈킨 씨가 자신의 적수인 슬럼키 홀의 새뮤얼 슬럼키 각하에게, 저 악대가 그의 동의로 연주하는 것인지 여쭙겠다고 청했다. 새뮤얼 슬럼키 각하가 그 질문에 답하기를 거절하자, 피즈킨 로지의 호레이쇼 피즈킨 씨는 슬럼키 홀의 새뮤얼 슬럼키 각하의 면전에 주먹을 흔들어 댔다. 그러자 새뮤얼 슬럼키 각하는 피가 끓어올라, 호레이쇼 피즈킨 씨에게 목숨을 건 결투를 청했다. 알려진 온갖 질서의 규칙과 전례를 이렇게 어기는 데 대해, 시장은 또 한 차례 종으로 환상곡을 연주하라 명하고는, 자신이 피즈킨 로지의 호레이쇼 피즈킨 씨와 슬럼키 홀의 새뮤얼 슬럼키 각하를 함께 자기 앞에 출두시켜, 치안을 지키겠다는 서약을 받겠노라 선언했다. 이 무시무시한 호령에, 두 후보의 지지자들이 끼어들었고, 양 당파의 친구들이 짝을 지어 사십오 분간 다툰 끝에, 호레이쇼 피즈킨 씨가 새뮤얼 슬럼키 각하에게 모자에 손을 대어 인사했고, 새뮤얼 슬럼키 각하도 호레이쇼 피즈킨 씨에게 그렇게 인사했으며, 악대는 멈췄고, 군중은 어느 정도 진정되었으며, 호레이쇼 피즈킨 씨는 연설을 이어 가도록 허락받았다.\n\n두 후보의 연설은, 다른 모든 점에서는 서로 달랐으나, 이턴스윌 유권자들의 미덕과 높은 가치에 대한 아름다운 헌사를 바쳤다는 점에서는 같았다. 둘 다, 자기에게 표를 주겠다고 약속한 사람들보다 더 독립적이고, 더 깨인 의식을 지녔으며, 더 공익에 헌신적이고, 더 고결한 정신을 지녔으며, 더 사심 없는 사람들은 이 땅에 일찍이 존재한 적이 없다는 것이 자신의 의견이라고 밝혔다. 그리고 저마다, 반대편을 지지하는 유권자들은 그들이 수행하라고 부름받은 중대한 임무를 행하기에 부적합하게 만드는, 어떤 돼지 같고 우매한 결함들을 지녔다는 의심을 음험하게 내비쳤다. 피즈킨은 무엇이든 요청받는 일은 다 할 준비가 되어 있다고 밝혔고, 슬럼키는 무엇이든 요청받는 일은 결코 하지 않겠다는 결심을 밝혔다. 둘 다, 이턴스윌의 무역과 제조업과 상업과 번영이 이 세상 그 어떤 대상보다도 자기 가슴에 언제나 더 소중하리라고 말했고, 저마다 자신이야말로 결국 당선될 사람이라고 더없이 자신 있게 진술할 수 있는 처지였다.\n\n거수 표결이 있었고, 시장은 슬럼키 홀의 새뮤얼 슬럼키 각하의 손을 들어 주었다. 피즈킨 로지의 호레이쇼 피즈킨 씨가 투표를 요구하자, 그에 따라 투표일이 정해졌다. 그런 다음 의장석에서의 유능한 처신에 대해 시장에게 감사를 표하는 동의가 제기되었고, 시장은 자신의 유능한 처신을 보여 줄 의자가 있었더라면 좋았을 것을 간절히 바라며(그는 의사 진행 내내 서 있었던 까닭이다) 감사로 화답했다. 행렬은 다시 정돈되었고, 마차들은 군중 사이를 천천히 굴러갔으며, 군중은 저마다의 감정이나 변덕이 시키는 대로 그 뒤를 향해 꽥꽥거리고 고함쳤다.\n\n투표가 진행되는 내내, 읍은 끊임없는 흥분의 열병에 들떠 있었다. 모든 일이 더없이 후하고 즐거운 규모로 진행되었다. 과세 대상 품목들은 모든 술집에서 놀랍도록 쌌다. 그리고 머리에 일시적인 어지럼증을 일으킨 유권자들의 편의를 위해 짐마차들이 거리를 누볐으니, 그 어지럼증은 경합이 벌어지는 동안 유권자들 사이에 더없이 우려스러운 정도로 퍼진 유행병이었고, 그 영향 아래 그들이 완전히 의식을 잃은 채 보도에 누워 있는 모습을 흔히 볼 수 있었다. 한 작은 무리의 유권자들이 바로 마지막 날까지도 투표하지 않은 채로 남아 있었다. 그들은 타산적이고 사려 깊은 사람들로, 양 당파와 자주 회담을 가졌음에도 어느 쪽의 논변에도 아직 설득되지 않은 자들이었다. 투표 마감 한 시간 전, 퍼커 씨는 이 지각 있고, 고결하고, 애국적인 사람들과 사사로이 면담하는 영광을 청했다. 그것은 허락되었다. 그의 논변은 간략했으나 흡족했다. 그들은 떼를 지어 투표소로 갔다. 그리고 그들이 돌아왔을 때, 슬럼키 홀의 새뮤얼 슬럼키 각하 또한 당선되었다.\n\n정치라는 존재의 다툼과 소란을 들여다보다가, 사생활의 평온한 안식으로 눈을 돌리는 것은 유쾌한 일이다. 실은 어느 편의 열렬한 당파도 아니었으나, 픽윅 씨는 포트 씨의 열정에 충분히 불이 붙어, 지난 장에서 그 자신의 비망록을 토대로 엮어 묘사한 그 일련의 사건에 자신의 모든 시간과 주의를 쏟았다. 그가 이렇게 분주한 동안 윈클 씨도 한가로이 지내지는 않았으니, 그의 온 시간은 포트 부인과 함께 즐거운 산책과 짧은 시골 나들이를 다니는 데 바쳐졌다. 부인은 그런 기회가 생길 때마다 자신이 늘 그토록 불평하던 지루한 단조로움에서 벗어날 위안을 구하기를 결코 빠뜨리지 않았다. 이렇게 두 신사가 편집자의 집에 완전히 길들여져 있는 동안, 터프먼 씨와 스노드그래스 씨는 상당 부분 제 나름의 방편에 내맡겨졌다. 공적인 일에는 별 흥미가 없었던 터라, 그들은 주로 공작 여관이 제공하는 오락거리로 시간을 보냈는데, 그 오락이라야 이 층에 있는 바가텔 대와 뒷마당의 한갓진 스키틀 마당으로 한정되어 있었다. 이 두 가지 놀이의 기술과 묘리는 보통 사람들이 짐작하는 것보다 훨씬 심오한데, 그들은 그런 소일거리에 완벽한 지식을 갖춘 웰러 씨에게서 차츰 그 묘법을 전수받았다. 그리하여 픽윅 씨와 함께하는 즐거움과 이점을 상당히 빼앗긴 처지였음에도, 그들은 여전히 시간을 보내며 그것이 손안에서 무겁게 늘어지는 것을 막을 수 있었다.\n\n그러나 공작 여관이 이 두 친구로 하여금 재주는 있되 따분한 포트의 초대마저 뿌리치게 할 만큼 매력을 발하는 때는 바로 저녁이었다. 저녁이 되면 '상인용 객실'은 어떤 사교 모임으로 가득 찼으니, 그 인물됨과 거동을 관찰하는 것이 터프먼 씨의 낙이었고, 그들의 말과 행동을 적어 두는 것이 스노드그래스 씨의 버릇이었다.\n\n상인용 객실이라는 곳이 대개 어떤 곳인지는 대부분 사람들이 안다. 공작 여관의 그것도 그런 부류의 방들이 일반적으로 그러한 것과 본질적인 점에서는 다를 바가 없었다. 다시 말해, 그것은 크고 휑한 방으로, 그 가구들은 새것이었을 때는 분명 더 나았을 터였고, 한가운데에는 널찍한 탁자가, 구석마다 그보다 작은 같은 종류의 탁자들이 놓여 있었다. 갖가지 모양의 의자가 푸짐하게 갖추어져 있었고, 낡은 터키산 양탄자가 깔려 있었는데, 그 양탄자가 방의 크기에 대해 차지하는 비율이란 마치 숙녀의 손수건이 파수막 바닥에 대해 차지하는 비율과 거의 같았다. 벽에는 큼직한 지도가 한두 장 걸려 장식되어 있었고, 한쪽 구석의 긴 못걸이에는 풍상에 시달린 거친 외투 몇 벌이 복잡한 어깨망토를 단 채 늘어져 있었다. 벽난로 선반은 나무로 된 잉크스탠드로 장식되어 있었는데, 그 안에는 펜 한 자루의 몽당이와 봉랍 반쪽이 들어 있었고, 그 밖에 도로 안내서와 인명록 한 권, 표지가 떨어져 나간 주(州) 역사책 한 권, 그리고 유리 관 속에 든 송어의 유해가 놓여 있었다. 공기에는 담배 연기 냄새가 배어 있었으니, 그 연기는 방 전체에, 특히 창문을 가린 먼지투성이의 붉은 커튼에 다소 거무칙칙한 빛깔을 입혀 놓았다. 찬장 위에는 잡다한 물건들이 한데 뒤엉켜 쌓여 있었는데, 그 가운데 가장 눈에 띄는 것은 몹시 뿌예진 생선 소스 병 몇 개, 마부석 방석 두어 개, 채찍 두세 개, 그만큼의 여행용 숄, 나이프와 포크가 담긴 쟁반, 그리고 겨자였다.\n\n선거가 끝난 이튿날 저녁, 터프먼 씨와 스노드그래스 씨가 이 집에 임시로 묵는 다른 몇몇과 함께 담배를 피우고 술을 마시며 앉아 있던 곳이 바로 여기였다.\n\n\"자, 여러분.\" 마흔 살가량의, 외눈인—그러나 그 눈은 더없이 반짝이는 검은 눈으로, 짓궂은 익살과 호탕함이 어려 반짝이는 눈이었다—건장하고 정정한 사내가 말했다. \"우리 고귀한 자신들을 위하여, 여러분. 나는 늘 좌중에 이 건배를 권하고, 메리를 위해서는 혼자 마신다오. 응, 메리!\"\n\n\"저리 가요, 이 못된 양반.\" 하녀가 말했으나, 그 칭찬이 싫지는 않은 기색이 역력했다.\n\n\"가지 마, 메리.\" 검은 눈의 사내가 말했다.\n\n\"좀 내버려 둬요, 뻔뻔하기는.\" 젊은 처녀가 말했다.\n\n\"괜찮아.\" 외눈 사내가 방을 나가는 처녀의 뒤에다 대고 외쳤다. \"조금 있다 내가 나가지, 메리. 기운 내라고, 아가씨.\" 여기서 그는 외눈 하나로 좌중을 향해 윙크하는, 그리 어렵지 않은 동작을 해 보였고, 그것이 더러운 얼굴에 흙으로 빚은 파이프를 문 어느 나이 든 사내를 열광적으로 기쁘게 했다.\n\n\"여자란 별난 족속이야.\" 더러운 얼굴의 사내가 잠시 사이를 두고 말했다.\n\n\"아! 그건 틀림없는 말이지.\" 시가를 문 채 매우 붉은 얼굴의 사내가 말했다.\n\n이 자그마한 철학을 늘어놓은 뒤 또 한 차례 침묵이 흘렀다.\n\n\"그렇지만 말이오, 이 세상엔 여자보다 더 별난 것들도 있다오.\" 검은 눈의 사내가 더없이 넉넉한 통을 가진 큼직한 네덜란드식 파이프에 천천히 담배를 채우며 말했다.\n\n\"결혼은 했소?\" 더러운 얼굴의 사내가 물었다.\n\n\"했다고는 못 하겠소.\"\n\n\"안 한 줄 알았소.\" 여기서 더러운 얼굴의 사내는 자기가 한 응수에 황홀할 만큼 흥겨워 웃음을 터뜨렸고, 늘 모든 사람의 말에 맞장구치기를 원칙으로 삼는, 부드러운 목소리와 온화한 낯빛의 사내가 거기에 합세했다.\n\n\"여자란 결국, 여러분.\" 열정적인 스노드그래스 씨가 말했다. \"우리 존재의 위대한 버팀목이요 위안입니다.\"\n\n\"그렇고말고요.\" 온화한 신사가 말했다.\n\n\"기분이 좋을 때는 말이오.\" 더러운 얼굴의 사내가 끼어들었다.\n\n\"그것도 참으로 옳은 말이오.\" 온화한 사내가 말했다.\n\n\"나는 그 단서를 거부합니다.\" 생각이 어느새 에밀리 와들에게로 빠르게 돌아가고 있던 스노드그래스 씨가 말했다. \"나는 그것을 경멸로—분노로 거부합니다. 여자를, 여자라는 이유로 헐뜯는 자가 있거든 내게 데려와 보시오. 나는 그가 사내가 아니라고 당당히 선언하겠소.\" 그러고는 스노드그래스 씨가 입에서 시가를 빼더니 움켜쥔 주먹으로 탁자를 거세게 내리쳤다.\n\n\"건전하고 훌륭한 논리요.\" 온화한 사내가 말했다.\n\n\"내가 부정하는 명제를 담고 있구먼.\" 더러운 낯빛의 사내가 가로막았다.\n\n\"그리고 선생 말씀에도 분명 대단히 많은 진실이 담겨 있긴 하지요.\" 온화한 신사가 말했다.\n\n\"선생의 건강을 위하여.\" 외눈의 외판원이 스노드그래스 씨에게 동의하듯 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다.\n\n스노드그래스 씨는 그 인사를 받아 답했다.\n\n\"나는 늘 좋은 논쟁을 듣는 걸 즐긴다오.\" 외판원이 말을 이었다. \"이번 같은 날카로운 논쟁 말이오. 매우 유익하지요. 그런데 여자에 관한 이 자그마한 논쟁을 듣다 보니, 내가 옛날에 우리 늙은 삼촌에게서 들은 이야기 하나가 떠오르는구려. 방금 그 이야기가 생각나는 바람에, 더러 여자보다 더 별난 것들과 마주칠 수도 있다고 내가 말한 거라오.\"\n\n\"그 이야기라는 걸 들어 보고 싶군요.\" 시가를 문 붉은 얼굴의 사내가 말했다.\n\n\"그러시오?\" 외판원은 그 한마디만 던지고는 맹렬한 기세로 계속 담배를 피웠다.\n\n\"나도 그렇소.\" 터프먼 씨가 처음으로 입을 열며 말했다. 그는 늘 자신의 경험 보따리를 늘리는 데 열심이었다.\n\n\"당신도 그렇소? 그럼 좋소, 이야기하리다. 아니, 그만두겠소. 당신들이 믿지 않을 게 뻔하니까.\" 짓궂은 눈의 사내가, 그 눈을 어느 때보다 더 짓궂게 보이게 하며 말했다. \"참말이라고 하시면 당연히 믿겠소.\" 터프먼 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 그 약조 아래 이야기하리다.\" 그 나그네가 대답했다. \"빌슨 앤드 슬럼이라는 큰 상사(商社) 이야기를 들어 보신 적 있소? 하긴 들었든 안 들었든 상관없소, 그들은 오래전에 사업에서 손을 뗐으니까. 그 상사의 외판원에게 이 일이 일어난 게 팔십 년 전이오만, 그 사람은 우리 삼촌의 각별한 친구였고, 삼촌이 내게 그 이야기를 들려주었지요. 묘한 제목이오만, 삼촌은 그걸 이렇게 부르곤 했소.\n\n외판원의 이야기\n그리고 대강 이런 식으로 들려주곤 했지요.\n\n'어느 겨울 저녁, 다섯 시쯤, 막 어스름이 깔리기 시작할 무렵, 한 사내가 이륜마차를 몰며 지친 말을 재촉해 말버러 다운스를 가로질러 브리스틀 쪽으로 난 길을 따라가는 모습을 볼 수 있었을지도 모른다오. 볼 수 있었을지도 모른다고 했소만, 장님이 아닌 사람이 그 길을 지나갔다면 틀림없이 보였으리라는 데는 의심의 여지가 없소. 그러나 날씨가 어찌나 사나운지, 또 밤이 어찌나 춥고 축축한지, 바깥에 나도는 거라곤 빗물뿐이었고, 그래서 그 나그네는 길 한복판을 따라 외롭고도 쓸쓸하게 터덜터덜 갔던 거요. 그 시절 어느 외판원이라도 진흙빛 차체에 붉은 바퀴를 단, 죽기 살기로 내달리는 듯한 그 자그마한 이륜마차와, 푸줏간 말과 이펜짜리 우편 조랑말을 섞어 놓은 듯한, 성깔 사납고 심술궂고 빨리 달리는 그 밤색 암말을 한번 보기만 했다면, 이 나그네가 바로 시티의 케이턴 가에 있는 빌슨 앤드 슬럼 대상사의 톰 스마트임을 단박에 알아챘을 거요. 그러나 지켜보는 외판원이 하나도 없었으니, 이 일에 대해 아무도 아무것도 알지 못했고, 그리하여 톰 스마트와 붉은 바퀴를 단 진흙빛 이륜마차와 빨리 달리는 성깔 사나운 암말은 셋이서 비밀을 간직한 채 함께 나아갔고, 아무도 조금도 더 알게 된 바가 없었소.\n\n이 음울한 세상에도 바람이 거셀 때의 말버러 다운스보다 더 유쾌한 곳은 많이 있다오. 게다가 음산한 겨울 저녁과, 진창에 질척이는 길과, 사정없이 퍼붓는 폭우까지 더해서, 시험 삼아 그 효과를 당신 몸으로 직접 겪어 본다면, 이 말이 얼마나 옳은지 절절히 체험하게 될 거요.\n\n바람이 불었소—길을 따라 위로도 아래로도 아니라, 그것도 충분히 고약하긴 하오만, 길을 정통으로 가로질러 불어서, 비를 비스듬히 후려쳐 내려보냈으니, 마치 학교에서 아이들이 글씨를 잘 비스듬히 쓰게 하려고 습자 공책에 그어 놓던 줄 같았소. 한순간 바람이 잦아들면, 나그네는 바람이 앞서의 광포함에 지쳐 조용히 드러누워 쉬고 있는 거라고 스스로를 속여 믿기 시작하는데, 그때 휘이! 멀리서 바람이 으르렁대고 휘파람 부는 소리가 들리는가 싶더니, 그것이 언덕마루를 넘어 휩쓸어 오고 벌판을 쓸며 달려와, 가까워질수록 소리와 기세를 더해, 마침내 묵직한 돌풍이 되어 말과 사람을 후려치며, 날카로운 빗줄기를 그들의 귓속에, 그 차고 축축한 숨결을 그들의 뼛속까지 몰아넣었소. 그러고는 그들을 스쳐 멀리, 저 멀리 귀가 멍멍한 굉음을 내며 내달려 갔으니, 마치 그들의 연약함을 비웃기라도 하듯, 또 제 힘과 위세를 자각하며 의기양양해하듯 말이오.\n\n밤색 암말은 귀를 축 늘어뜨린 채 진흙과 물을 튀기며 첨벙첨벙 나아갔고, 이따금 머리를 내흔드는 것이 마치 이 지극히 무례한 날씨의 거동에 대한 혐오를 드러내는 듯했으나, 그래도 좋은 걸음걸이를 유지하다가, 마침내 그때껏 그들을 덮친 어떤 것보다 더 사나운 돌풍이 불어닥치자 갑자기 멈춰 서서 날아가지 않으려 네 발을 땅에 단단히 버텼소. 암말이 그렇게 한 것은 각별한 자비였소. 만약 정말로 날아가 버렸다면, 성깔 사나운 암말은 어찌나 가벼운지, 이륜마차도 어찌나 가벼운지, 게다가 톰 스마트마저도 그토록 가벼운 몸이라, 세 식구가 영락없이 다 같이 데굴데굴 굴러, 땅끝에 이르거나 바람이 잦아들 때까지 굴렀을 것이고, 어느 쪽이든 그 성깔 사나운 암말도, 붉은 바퀴를 단 진흙빛 이륜마차도, 톰 스마트도, 다시는 쓸모 있게 되지 못했을 게 십중팔구였소.\n\n\"이런, 내 가죽끈과 구레나룻을 걸고 빌어먹을.\" 톰 스마트가 말했소(톰은 가끔 욕을 입에 다는 고약한 버릇이 있었거든). \"내 가죽끈과 구레나룻을 걸고 빌어먹을, 이게 즐겁지 않다면, 바람에 날아가 버려라!\"\n\n톰 스마트가 이미 어지간히 바람에 날릴 만큼 날렸으면서 어째서 같은 일을 또 당하기를 바랐는지, 당신들은 십중팔구 내게 그 까닭을 물을 거요. 나도 모르겠소—내가 아는 거라곤 톰 스마트가 그렇게 말했다는 것뿐이오—적어도 그가 우리 삼촌에게 늘 그렇게 말했다고 했으니, 그게 그거지요.\n\n\"바람에 날아가 버려라.\" 톰 스마트가 말하자, 암말이 자기도 꼭 같은 생각이라는 듯 히힝 울었소.\n\n\"기운 내, 늙은 아가씨.\" 톰이 채찍 끝으로 밤색 암말의 목을 토닥이며 말했소. \"이런 밤에 억지로 밀고 나가 봐야 소용없어. 처음 닿는 집에 묵을 테니, 네가 빨리 갈수록 더 빨리 끝나는 거다. 어허, 늙은 아가씨—살살—살살.\"\n\n성깔 사나운 암말이 톰의 목소리 가락을 알아들을 만큼 익숙해서 그 뜻을 헤아린 건지, 아니면 가만히 서 있는 것이 움직이는 것보다 더 춥다고 느낀 건지, 그야 물론 나로선 알 수 없소. 그러나 내가 말할 수 있는 건, 톰이 말을 마치자마자 암말이 귀를 쫑긋 세우고는 어찌나 빠른 속도로 앞으로 내달았던지, 진흙빛 이륜마차가 어찌나 덜컹거렸던지, 붉은 바큇살이 하나하나 다 말버러 다운스의 잔디 위로 튕겨 날아갈 것만 같았다는 거요. 톰이 아무리 채찍질에 이골이 난 사람이라도 그 걸음을 멈추거나 늦추지 못했고, 마침내 암말이 제풀에 멈춰 선 곳은 다운스 끝에서 반의 사분의 일 마일쯤 떨어진, 길 오른편의 한 길가 여관 앞이었소.\n\n톰은 마부에게 고삐를 던지고 채찍을 마차 통에 꽂으면서 그 집의 위쪽을 흘긋 살펴보았소. 그것은 기묘한 옛 건물로, 일종의 나무 널판으로 지었는데, 마치 들보를 가로질러 박아 넣은 듯했고, 박공 얹은 창들이 보행로 위로 완전히 튀어나와 있었으며, 어두운 현관이 딸린 낮은 문이 있었고, 요즘 식으로 얕은 계단 대여섯이 위로 이어진 게 아니라, 가파른 계단 두어 단이 집 안으로 내려가게 되어 있었소. 그래도 아늑해 보이는 곳이었으니, 술청 창에 강하고 명랑한 불빛이 있어 길 건너로 환한 빛줄기를 뿜어내고 건너편 산울타리까지 비추었으며, 맞은편 창에는 붉게 일렁이는 불빛이 있어, 한순간 어렴풋이 분간될 듯하다가 다음 순간 드리운 커튼 사이로 강하게 빛나, 안에 활활 타오르는 불이 있음을 알려 주었소. 노련한 나그네의 눈으로 이런 자그마한 증거들을 가늠한 톰은, 얼어붙다시피 한 사지가 허락하는 한껏 날렵하게 마차에서 내려 집 안으로 들어갔소.\n\n채 오 분도 안 되어, 톰은 술청 맞은편 방—그가 불이 활활 타고 있으리라 상상했던 바로 그 방—에 자리 잡고, 든든하고 실속 있게 우렁차게 타오르는 불 앞에 앉아 있었소. 그 불은 한 부셸이 채 안 되는 석탄과, 멀쩡한 구스베리 덤불 대여섯 그루는 만들 만한 장작으로 이루어져, 굴뚝 중턱까지 쌓여 우렁우렁 탁탁 타올랐으니, 그 소리만으로도 웬만큼 정상인 사람의 마음은 절로 데워질 만했소. 이것은 아늑했지만, 이게 다가 아니었소. 맵시 있게 차려입은, 반짝이는 눈과 단정한 발목을 지닌 처녀가 탁자에 아주 깨끗한 흰 식탁보를 깔고 있었으니까. 톰이 실내화 신은 발을 난로 받침대에 올리고 열린 문에 등을 돌린 채 앉아 있자니, 벽난로 위 거울에 비친 술청의 매혹적인 풍경이 눈에 들어왔소. 녹색 병들과 금빛 상표가 줄줄이 늘어선 흐뭇한 광경에다, 절임과 잼이 든 단지들, 치즈와 삶은 햄, 둥글게 만 쇠고기가 선반에 더없이 먹음직스럽고 군침 도는 모양새로 진열되어 있었소. 자, 이것도 아늑했지만, 이것조차 다가 아니었으니—술청 안에, 더없이 아담한 조그만 탁자에 앉아, 더없이 환한 조그만 불 앞에 바싹 다가앉아 차를 마시는, 마흔여덟쯤 되었거나 그 언저리쯤 되어 보이는 풍만한 과부가 있었던 거요. 술청만큼이나 푸근한 얼굴을 한 그 여인은, 분명 이 집의 안주인이자 이 모든 흐뭇한 재산을 다스리는 지존이었소. 이 그림 전체의 아름다움에 단 하나 흠이 있었으니, 그것은 키 큰 사내—아주 키 큰 사내—였소. 갈색 외투에 반짝이는 바구니 모양 단추를 달고, 검은 구레나룻에 물결치는 검은 머리를 한 그자가 과부와 마주 앉아 차를 마시고 있었는데, 그자가 과부를 설득해 더는 과부로 지내지 말고 자기에게 그 술청에 눌러앉을 특권을, 그것도 제 타고난 수명이 다하는 그날까지 통째로 누리게 해 달라고 어지간히 잘 구슬리고 있는 중임을 알아채는 데는 대단한 통찰력이 필요치 않았소.\n\n톰 스마트는 결코 성마르거나 시기심 많은 성미가 아니었소만, 어쩐 일인지 갈색 외투에 반짝이는 바구니 단추를 단 그 키 큰 사내가 그의 천성 속에 든 얼마 안 되는 울화를 들쑤셔, 그를 몹시 분개하게 만들었소. 더구나 거울 앞 제자리에서 이따금, 그 키 큰 사내와 과부 사이에 오가는 어떤 자그마한 다정한 친밀함을 엿볼 수 있었기에 더욱 그러했으니, 그것은 그 키 큰 사내가 키가 큰 만큼이나 총애도 두텁다는 걸 충분히 말해 주었소. 톰은 따끈한 펀치를 좋아했소—아주 좋아했다고 감히 말할 수 있겠소—그래서 성깔 사나운 암말이 잘 먹고 잘 깔린 자리에 누운 걸 보고, 과부가 제 손으로 직접 차려 준 맛깔난 더운 저녁을 한 입도 남김없이 먹어 치운 다음, 그저 시험 삼아 펀치 한 잔을 시켰소. 그런데 온갖 가사 기예를 통틀어 그 과부가 무엇보다 더 잘 만드는 게 하나 있다면, 그것이 바로 이 펀치였소. 첫 잔이 어찌나 절묘하게 톰 스마트의 입맛에 딱 맞았던지, 그는 한 치의 지체도 없이 두 잔째를 시켰소. 따끈한 펀치란 유쾌한 것이오, 여러분—어떤 상황에서든 더없이 유쾌한 것이오만—그 아늑한 옛 거실에서, 우렁차게 타는 불 앞에서, 바깥에선 바람이 불어 그 낡은 집의 들보란 들보가 다시금 삐걱대는 가운데, 톰 스마트는 그것이 더없이 흐뭇하게 느껴졌소. 그는 또 한 잔, 그리고 또 한 잔을 시켰고—그다음에도 한 잔을 더 시키지 않았는지 나는 그리 확신할 수 없소만—따끈한 펀치를 마시면 마실수록 키 큰 사내 생각이 더 났소.\n\n\"저 뻔뻔한 작자 같으니!\" 톰이 속으로 말했소. \"저자가 저 아늑한 술청에 무슨 볼일이 있단 말이야? 게다가 저렇게 못생긴 악당이!\" 톰이 말했소. \"과부에게 안목이라는 게 조금이라도 있다면, 저것보단 나은 사내를 분명 골라잡을 수 있을 텐데.\" 여기서 톰의 눈은 벽난로 위 거울에서 탁자 위 술잔으로 옮겨 갔고, 차츰 감상에 젖어 드는 것을 느끼며 그는 펀치 넉 잔째를 비우고 다섯 잔째를 시켰소.\n\n톰 스마트는 말이오, 여러분, 늘 술장사에 무던히도 마음이 끌렸소. 녹색 외투에 무릎까지 오는 끈바지와 장화 차림으로 제 술청에 떡 버티고 서 보는 것이 오래된 그의 야망이었소. 그는 흥겨운 만찬에서 좌장 노릇을 하는 데 거창한 포부가 있었고, 제 방에서 입담으로 좌중을 주재한다면 얼마나 멋들어질지, 또 술 마시는 부문에서 손님들에게 얼마나 본보기가 될지를 자주 떠올리곤 했소. 톰이 우렁찬 불 앞에서 따끈한 펀치를 마시며 앉아 있는 동안 이 모든 것이 그의 머릿속을 빠르게 스쳐 지나갔고, 그는 저 키 큰 사내는 이토록 훌륭한 집을 차지할 어엿한 길목에 서 있는데 자기, 톰 스마트는 그것에서 여전히 멀찍이 떨어져 있다는 사실에 지극히 정당하고 마땅하게 분개했소. 그리하여 마지막 두 잔을 비우며, 저 풍만한 과부의 환심을 용케 사들인 그 키 큰 사내에게 시비를 걸 완벽한 권리가 자기에게 없는 것은 아닌지 곰곰이 따져 본 끝에, 톰 스마트는 마침내 자신이 몹시 부당한 대접을 받고 박해당하는 인간이며, 그러니 자러 가는 편이 낫겠다는 흡족한 결론에 이르렀소.\n\n그 맵시 있는 처녀가 침실용 촛불을 손으로 가린 채 넓고 오래된 계단을 앞장서 톰을 안내했는데, 그렇게 엉성하게 뻗어 나간 낡은 집에서는 바람이 촛불을 끄지 않고도 마음껏 뛰놀 만한 자리가 얼마든지 있었을 텐데도, 바람은 그예 촛불을 꺼 버렸소—그리하여 톰의 원수들에게, 촛불을 끈 것은 바람이 아니라 바로 톰이며, 그가 다시 촛불을 불어 켜는 척하면서 실은 처녀에게 입을 맞추고 있었다고 우길 빌미를 주고 말았소. 어찌 됐든, 다른 불을 마련해, 톰은 미로 같은 방들과 미궁 같은 복도를 지나 그를 위해 준비된 방으로 안내되었고, 거기서 처녀는 그에게 잘 자라는 인사를 하고 그를 혼자 남겨 둔 채 떠났소.\n\n큼직한 벽장들이 딸린 꽤 넓은 방이었고, 침대는 기숙학교 전교생이 다 써도 될 만했으며, 작은 군대 하나의 짐을 다 넣고도 남을 떡갈나무 옷장 두 개는 말할 것도 없었소. 그러나 톰의 마음을 가장 사로잡은 것은 기묘하고도 음산해 보이는, 등받이가 높은 의자였소. 더없이 환상적인 모양새로 조각되어 있었고, 꽃무늬 다마스크 방석이 놓였으며, 다리 끝의 둥근 혹들은 마치 발가락에 통풍이라도 걸린 양 붉은 천으로 정성스레 싸매여 있었소. 다른 어떤 괴상한 의자였다면 톰은 그저 괴상한 의자로구나 하고 말았을 것이고, 그걸로 일은 끝났을 거요. 그러나 이 별난 의자에는 뭔가가 있었으니, 그게 무엇인지 톰 자신도 말할 수 없었소만, 어찌나 야릇하고, 그가 여태 본 어떤 가구와도 어찌나 다르던지, 그를 호리는 것만 같았소. 그는 불 앞에 앉아 반 시간 동안 그 낡은 의자를 빤히 바라보았소—제기랄, 그 의자는 어찌나 기묘한 옛 물건이던지, 그는 거기서 눈을 뗄 수가 없었소.\n\n\"흠.\" 천천히 옷을 벗으면서, 침대맡에 신비로운 자태로 서 있는 그 낡은 의자에서 줄곧 눈을 떼지 못한 채 톰이 말했소. \"내 평생 저런 별난 물건은 처음 보는걸. 참 야릇해.\" 따끈한 펀치 덕에 다소 점잖은 현자가 된 톰이 말했소—\"참 야릇해.\" 톰은 심오한 지혜의 기색으로 고개를 절레절레 흔들고는 다시 의자를 바라보았소. 그래도 도무지 알 수가 없었던지라, 그는 침대로 들어가 몸을 따뜻하게 덮고 잠이 들었소.\n\n반 시간쯤 지나, 톰은 키 큰 사내들과 펀치 잔들이 뒤엉킨 어수선한 꿈에서 화들짝 깨어났소. 그리고 깨어난 그의 머릿속에 가장 먼저 떠오른 것은 그 괴상한 의자였소.\n\n\"다시는 저걸 안 봐야지.\" 톰이 속으로 말하며 두 눈꺼풀을 꼭 맞붙이고 다시 잠들려 한다고 스스로를 타일러 보았소. 소용없었소. 눈앞에서 춤추는 거라곤 괴상한 의자들뿐이었으니, 다리를 차올리고, 서로의 등을 뛰어넘고, 온갖 익살을 부려 댔소.\n\n\"가짜 의자 두세 벌 전체를 보느니, 진짜 의자 하나를 보는 편이 낫겠군.\" 톰이 이불 밑에서 머리를 빼며 말했소. 의자는 거기, 불빛에 또렷이 분간되며, 어느 때보다 더 약 올리는 듯한 모습으로 있었소.\n\n톰이 의자를 가만히 바라보는데, 그가 그것을 보고 있노라니 별안간, 더없이 기이한 변화가 의자에 일어나는 듯했소. 등받이의 조각이 차츰 늙고 쪼글쪼글한 사람 얼굴의 이목구비와 표정을 띠어 갔고, 다마스크 방석은 옛날식의 자락 늘어진 조끼가 되었으며, 둥근 혹들은 붉은 천 슬리퍼에 싸인 두 발로 자라났고, 의자 전체가 마치 지난 세기의, 두 팔을 허리에 짚은 몹시 못생긴 늙은이처럼 보였소. 톰은 침대에 일어나 앉아 그 환영을 떨치려 눈을 비볐소. 천만에. 의자는 한 못생긴 노신사였고, 게다가 그 노신사는 톰 스마트를 향해 윙크를 하고 있었소.\n\n톰은 본래 앞뒤 안 가리고 무모한, 태평한 부류의 사내였고, 게다가 따끈한 펀치를 다섯 잔이나 들이켜기까지 했소. 그래서 처음엔 좀 놀랐으나, 그 노신사가 그토록 뻔뻔한 태도로 윙크를 하고 곁눈질을 하는 걸 보자 다소 분개하기 시작했소. 마침내 그는 더는 못 참겠다고 마음먹었고, 그 늙은 얼굴이 여전히 어느 때 못지않게 빠르게 윙크를 해 대는지라, 톰이 몹시 성난 어조로 말했소—\n\n\"대체 왜 나한테 윙크질이야?\"\n\n\"내가 좋아서 그런다, 톰 스마트.\" 의자가, 아니, 당신들 좋을 대로 부르시오, 노신사가 말했소. 톰이 입을 열자 그는 윙크는 멈췄으나, 늙어 빠진 원숭이처럼 히죽거리기 시작했소.\n\n\"네가 어떻게 내 이름을 알아, 이 호두까기 같은 늙은 얼굴아?\" 톰 스마트가 자못 흔들리면서도, 짐짓 아주 의연한 척하며 물었소.\n\n\"이런, 이런, 톰.\" 노신사가 말했소. \"묵직한 스페인산 마호가니한테 그런 식으로 말하는 게 아니지. 빌어먹을, 내가 무늬목 베니어였대도 그보다 더 무례하게 굴진 못할 게다.\" 노신사가 이 말을 할 때 어찌나 사납게 노려보던지 톰은 겁이 나기 시작했소.\n\n\"무례를 범하려던 건 아니었습니다, 어르신.\" 톰이 처음에 말할 때보다 훨씬 겸손한 어조로 말했소.\n\n\"음, 음.\" 늙은 양반이 말했소. \"그럴 테지—그럴 테지. 톰—\"\n\n\"네, 어르신—\"\n\n\"나는 너에 관해 죄다 안다, 톰. 죄다. 너는 무척 가난하구나, 톰.\"\n\n\"그건 분명 그렇습니다.\" 톰 스마트가 말했소. \"한데 그걸 어떻게 아셨습니까?\"\n\n\"그건 아무래도 좋아.\" 노신사가 말했소. \"너는 펀치를 너무 좋아해, 톰.\"\n\n톰 스마트는 지난 생일 이후로는 한 방울도 입에 댄 적이 없다고 항변하려던 참이었소만, 그의 눈이 노신사의 눈과 마주치자 그 눈이 어찌나 다 안다는 표정이던지, 톰은 얼굴을 붉히고 입을 다물었소.\n\n\"톰.\" 노신사가 말했소. \"그 과부는 참한 여자야—유달리 참한 여자지—안 그러냐, 톰?\" 여기서 늙은 양반이 두 눈을 찡긋 오므리고 비쩍 마른 자그마한 다리 하나를 까딱 치켜들며, 도무지 그 나이에—그 연배에 말이오!—어찌나 보기 흉하게 색을 밝히는 꼴이던지, 톰은 그 경박한 거동에 진절머리가 났소.\n\n\"내가 그 여자의 후견인이다, 톰.\" 노신사가 말했소.\n\n\"그러십니까?\" 톰 스마트가 물었소.\n\n\"나는 그 여자의 어미를 알았지, 톰.\" 늙은 양반이 말했소. \"그리고 그 할미도. 그 할미는 나를 무척 아꼈어—내게 이 조끼를 지어 주었지, 톰.\"\n\n\"그랬습니까?\" 톰 스마트가 말했소.\n\n\"그리고 이 신도.\" 늙은 양반이 붉은 천 덮개 하나를 들어 올리며 말했소. \"하지만 이건 입 밖에 내지 마라, 톰. 그 할미가 나를 그토록 좋아했다는 게 알려지는 건 싫으니까. 집안에 곤란한 일이 생길지도 모르거든.\" 그 늙은 악당이 이 말을 할 때 어찌나 지독히 건방진 꼴이던지, 톰 스마트가 나중에 단언하기를, 그자 위에 가책 없이 털썩 앉아 버릴 수도 있었을 거라 했소.\n\n\"내가 한창때는 여자들 사이에서 대단한 인기였단다, 톰.\" 그 방탕한 늙은 난봉꾼이 말했소. \"참한 여자들 수백이 내 무릎 위에 몇 시간씩 앉아 있곤 했지. 그거 어떠냐, 이 녀석아, 응!\" 노신사가 자기 젊은 날의 다른 무용담을 더 늘어놓으려는데, 어찌나 격렬하게 삐걱대는 발작에 사로잡혔던지 더는 말을 잇지 못했소.\n\n'쌤통이다, 영감.' 톰 스마트는 속으로 생각했소만, 아무 말도 하지 않았소.\n\n\"아!\" 늙은 양반이 말했소. \"요즘은 이게 어지간히 성가셔. 나도 늙어 가는구나, 톰, 못이 거의 다 빠졌어. 수술도 한 번 받았다—등에 작은 조각을 끼워 넣는 수술 말이야—그게 어찌나 혹독한 시련이던지, 톰.\"\n\n\"그러셨을 겁니다, 어르신.\" 톰 스마트가 말했소.\n\n\"하지만.\" 노신사가 말했소. \"그건 요점이 아니지. 톰! 나는 네가 그 과부와 결혼했으면 한다.\"\n\n\"제가요, 어르신!\" 톰이 말했소.\n\n\"네가.\" 노신사가 말했소.\n\n\"고매하신 머리타래에 축복을.\" 톰이 말했소(그에게도 듬성듬성 말총 몇 가닥이 남아 있었거든)—\"고매하신 머리타래에 축복을, 그 여자는 저 같은 걸 받아 주지 않을 겁니다.\" 그러고 톰은 술청을 떠올리며 저도 모르게 한숨을 쉬었소.\n\n\"안 받아 준다고?\" 노신사가 단호하게 말했소.\n\n\"아니요, 아니요.\" 톰이 말했소. \"딴 사람이 점찍혀 있단 말입니다. 키 큰 사내—지독하게 키 큰 사내—검은 구레나룻을 한 자가요.\"\n\n\"톰.\" 노신사가 말했소. \"그 여자는 결코 그자를 받아들이지 않아.\"\n\n\"안 받아들인다고요?\" 톰이 말했소. \"어르신이 그 술청에 서서 보셨다면, 딴소리를 하실 텐데요.\"\n\n\"쳇, 쳇.\" 노신사가 말했소. \"그것에 관해선 내가 죄다 안다.\"\n\n\"무엇에 관해서요?\" 톰이 말했소.\n\n\"문 뒤의 입맞춤이며, 그런 따위 죄다 말이야, 톰.\" 노신사가 말했소. 그러고는 또 한 번 건방진 눈초리를 보냈는데, 그게 톰을 몹시 노엽게 했으니, 여러분도 다들 아시다시피, 마땅히 더 잘 알아야 할 늙은 양반이 그런 일들을 입에 올리는 걸 듣는 건 매우 불쾌한 노릇이오—그보다 더한 노릇도 없지요.\n\n\"나는 그것에 관해 죄다 안다, 톰.\" 노신사가 말했소. \"한창때 나는 그런 짓을 무던히도 자주 봤지, 톰, 네게 거명하고 싶지 않은 숱한 사람들 사이에서 말이야. 하지만 결국 아무 결실도 없었어.\"\n\n\"별별 괴상한 일들을 다 보셨겠군요.\" 톰이 캐묻는 듯한 눈빛으로 말했소.\n\n\"그렇다고 할 수 있지, 톰.\" 늙은 양반이 매우 복잡한 윙크를 하며 대답했소. \"나는 우리 집안의 마지막 자손이란다, 톰.\" 노신사가 우울하게 한숨지으며 말했소.\n\n\"식구가 많았습니까?\" 톰 스마트가 물었소.\n\n\"우리는 열둘이었지, 톰.\" 노신사가 말했소. \"보기 좋게 등이 곧고 잘생긴 녀석들이었어, 누구라도 보고 싶어 할 만큼. 요즘의 그 기형아들 같은 게 아니라—다들 팔걸이가 있고, 내 입으로 말하긴 뭣하다만, 어찌나 윤이 흐르던지, 그걸 보면 네 마음이 흐뭇했을 게다.\"\n\n\"그럼 나머지는 어떻게 됐습니까, 어르신?\" 톰 스마트가 물었소—\n\n노신사는 팔꿈치를 눈가에 갖다 대며 대답했소. \"갔지, 톰, 다 갔어. 우리는 고된 일을 겪었고, 톰, 그 녀석들이 다 나만 한 체질을 타고난 건 아니었지. 다리며 팔에 류머티즘이 와서, 부엌이며 다른 병원들로 들어갔어. 그리고 그중 하나는, 오래 부려지고 험하게 다뤄진 끝에, 정말로 제정신을 잃었지—어찌나 미쳤던지 태워 버릴 수밖에 없었어. 그거 끔찍한 일이었지, 톰.\"\n\n\"무서운 일이군요!\" 톰 스마트가 말했소.\n\n늙은 양반은 잠시 동안, 북받치는 감정과 싸우는 듯, 말을 멈췄다가 이윽고 입을 열었소—\n\n\"하지만, 톰, 내가 요점에서 벗어나고 있구나. 이 키 큰 사내는, 톰, 악당 같은 협잡꾼이야. 그자는 과부와 결혼하는 순간, 가구를 죄다 팔아 치우고 달아날 거다. 그 결과가 어떻게 되겠느냐? 그 여자는 버림받아 파산에 이를 것이고, 나는 어느 고물상 가게에서 감기에 걸려 죽고 말겠지.\"\n\n\"네, 하지만—\"\n\n\"내 말을 끊지 마라.\" 노신사가 말했소. \"너에 대해서는, 톰, 나는 아주 다른 견해를 품고 있다. 네가 일단 어느 술집에 자리를 잡으면, 그 벽 안에 마실 거리가 있는 한 결코 거기를 떠나지 않으리라는 걸 잘 아니까.\"\n\n\"좋게 봐 주셔서 대단히 감사합니다, 어르신.\" 톰 스마트가 말했소.\n\n\"그러니,\" 노신사가 명령조로 말을 이었소. \"그 여자는 네가 차지하고, 그자는 못 차지하게 될 게다.\"\n\n\"무엇으로 그걸 막습니까?\" 톰 스마트가 다급히 말했소.\n\n\"이 폭로지.\" 노신사가 대답했소. \"그자는 이미 결혼한 몸이다.\"\n\n\"그걸 어떻게 증명합니까?\" 톰이 침대에서 반쯤 벌떡 일어나며 말했소.\n\n노신사는 옆구리에서 팔을 빼내, 떡갈나무 옷장 하나를 가리키고는 곧바로 팔을 본디 자리로 되돌려 놓았소.\n\n\"그자는 꿈에도 모르지,\" 노신사가 말했소. \"저 옷장 속에 있는 바지 한 벌의 오른쪽 주머니에, 자기더러 비탄에 잠긴 아내에게 돌아와 달라고 애원하는 편지를 한 통 두고 왔다는 걸 말이야—여섯이나 되는—명심해라, 톰—여섯이나 되는 갓난것들이 있는데, 죄다 어린것들이지.\"\n\n노신사가 이 말을 엄숙하게 내뱉는 동안, 그의 이목구비는 점점 흐릿해지고 그의 형체는 점점 그림자처럼 변해 갔소. 톰 스마트의 눈에 얇은 막이 끼었소. 노인은 차츰 의자 속으로 녹아들고, 다마스크 조끼는 방석으로 풀려나고, 붉은 슬리퍼는 작은 붉은 천 주머니로 쪼그라드는 듯했소. 불빛이 부드럽게 사위어 가고, 톰 스마트는 베개에 도로 쓰러져 잠에 빠져들었소.\n\n아침이 되자 톰은, 노인이 사라진 뒤 빠져들었던 혼곤한 잠에서 깨어났소. 그는 침대에 일어나 앉아, 몇 분 동안 전날 밤의 일들을 떠올리려 헛되이 애썼소. 그러다 별안간 그 일들이 그에게 와락 밀려들었소. 그는 의자를 바라보았소—과연 환상적이고 음산해 보이는 가구이긴 했소만, 그것과 늙은이 사이에서 어떤 닮은 점을 찾아낼 수 있었다면 그건 무던히도 기발하고 발랄한 상상력이었음에 틀림없었소.\n\n\"잘 잤나, 영감?\" 톰이 말했소. 그는 대낮엔 더 대담해졌으니—사내들 대부분이 그렇소.\n\n의자는 꼼짝도 않고, 한마디도 하지 않았소.\n\n\"고약한 아침이군.\" 톰이 말했소. 천만에. 의자는 대화에 끌려들 생각이 없었소.\n\n\"어느 옷장을 가리켰지?—그건 말해 줄 수 있겠지.\" 톰이 말했소. 한마디도, 여러분, 의자는 입을 떼려 들지 않았소.\n\n\"어쨌든 열어 보는 거야 별 수고도 아니지.\" 톰이 자못 신중하게 침대에서 빠져나오며 말했소. 그는 옷장 하나로 걸어갔소. 열쇠가 자물쇠에 꽂혀 있었소. 그는 그것을 돌려 문을 열었소. 거기 바지 한 벌이 있었소. 그는 주머니에 손을 넣어, 노신사가 묘사한 바로 그 편지를 끄집어냈소!\n\n\"이거 참 묘한 일이군.\" 톰 스마트가 처음엔 의자를, 다음엔 옷장을, 다음엔 편지를, 그러고 다시 의자를 바라보며 말했소. \"참 묘해.\" 톰이 말했소. 그러나 어느 쪽에도 그 묘함을 덜어 줄 만한 게 없었던지라, 그는 차라리 옷을 차려입고 키 큰 사내의 일을 당장 처리해 버리는 편이 낫겠다고 생각했소—그자를 비참한 처지에서 벗어나게 해 줄 겸 말이오.\n\n톰은 아래층으로 내려가는 길에 지나치는 방들을, 마치 주인의 매서운 눈으로 찬찬히 살폈소. 머지않아 그 방들과 그 안의 물건들이 제 재산이 되는 게 불가능하지만은 않다고 여기면서 말이오. 키 큰 사내는 아담한 조그만 술청에 뒷짐을 진 채, 아주 제 집인 양 서 있었소. 그는 톰을 향해 멍하니 히죽 웃었소. 무심한 구경꾼이라면 그가 그저 흰 이를 보이려고 그러는 거라 여겼을지 모르오만, 톰 스마트는 그 키 큰 사내에게 정신이라는 게 있었다면 들어앉았을 자리쯤에 승리감이 스쳐 가고 있다고 생각했소. 톰은 그자의 면전에 대고 웃고는 안주인을 불렀소.\n\n\"안녕하십니까, 마님.\" 톰 스마트가 과부가 들어오자 조그만 거실의 문을 닫으며 말했소.\n\n\"안녕하세요, 손님.\" 과부가 말했소. \"아침으로 무얼 드시겠어요?\"\n\n톰은 어떻게 이야기를 꺼낼까 궁리하느라 아무 대답도 하지 않았소.\n\n\"아주 맛 좋은 햄이 있고요.\" 과부가 말했소. \"기름을 잘 넣은 멋진 냉(冷) 닭고기도 있답니다. 들여올까요, 손님?\"\n\n이 말이 톰을 상념에서 일깨웠소. 그녀가 말할수록 그녀에 대한 그의 감탄은 커져 갔소. 사려 깊은 사람이로다! 든든한 살림꾼이로다!\n\n\"술청에 있는 저 양반은 누굽니까, 마님?\" 톰이 물었소.\n\n\"그분 성함은 징킨스예요, 손님.\" 과부가 살짝 얼굴을 붉히며 말했소.\n\n\"키가 큰 양반이군요.\" 톰이 말했소.\n\n\"아주 훤칠한 분이세요, 손님.\" 과부가 대답했소. \"그리고 아주 멋진 신사분이고요.\"\n\n\"아!\" 톰이 말했소.\n\n\"더 필요하신 거라도 있으세요, 손님?\" 톰의 태도에 자못 어리둥절해진 과부가 물었소.\n\n\"음, 그렇습니다.\" 톰이 말했소. \"친애하는 마님, 잠깐만 앉아 주시는 친절을 베풀어 주시겠습니까?\"\n\n과부는 몹시 놀란 기색이었소만, 그래도 자리에 앉았고, 톰도 그녀 바로 곁에 바싹 앉았소. 그게 어떻게 된 일인지 나는 모르오, 여러분—실은 우리 삼촌이 늘 내게 들려주기를, 톰 스마트도 그게 어떻게 된 일인지 자기도 모르겠다고 했다 하오—그런데 어찌어찌하여 톰의 손바닥이 과부의 손등 위에 떨어졌고, 그가 말하는 내내 거기 머물러 있었소.\n\n\"친애하는 마님.\" 톰 스마트가 말했소—그는 늘 상냥하게 구는 데 거창한 포부가 있었거든—\"친애하는 마님, 당신은 아주 훌륭한 남편을 맞을 자격이 있습니다—정말로 그렇습니다.\"\n\n\"어머나, 손님!\" 과부가 말했소—그럴 만도 했지요. 톰이 대화를 시작하는 방식이 자못 별나다 못해 깜짝 놀랄 만했으니, 그가 그 전날 밤 이전엔 그녀에게 눈길 한 번 준 적이 없다는 사실을 감안하면 말이오. \"어머나, 손님!\"\n\n\"나는 아첨 따위는 경멸합니다, 친애하는 마님.\" 톰 스마트가 말했소. \"당신은 아주 훌륭한 남편을 맞을 자격이 있고, 그게 누구든 그는 아주 운 좋은 사내일 겁니다.\" 톰이 이 말을 하면서, 그의 눈은 저도 모르게 과부의 얼굴에서 그를 둘러싼 안락함으로 옮겨 갔소.\n\n과부는 어느 때보다 더 어리둥절한 얼굴로 일어서려 했소. 톰은 그녀를 붙들려는 듯 그녀의 손을 가만히 눌렀고, 그녀는 자리에 그대로 앉아 있었소. 과부란 말이오, 여러분, 대개 겁이 많지 않다고, 우리 삼촌이 늘 말씀하시곤 했소.\n\n\"좋게 봐 주셔서 정말 대단히 감사드려요, 손님.\" 풍만한 안주인이 반쯤 웃으며 말했소. \"그리고 만에 하나 제가 다시 결혼하게 된다면—\"\n\n\"만에 하나라니요.\" 톰 스마트가 왼쪽 눈 오른쪽 귀퉁이로 자못 빈틈없이 흘겨보며 말했소. \"만에 하나라니요—\"\n\n\"그래요.\" 이번엔 과부가 대놓고 웃으며 말했소. \"제가 그리되거든, 손님 말씀처럼 그렇게 좋은 남편을 만났으면 해요.\"\n\n\"이를테면 징킨스 말이군요.\" 톰이 말했소.\n\n\"어머나, 손님!\" 과부가 소리쳤소.\n\n\"아, 내게 말 마십시오.\" 톰이 말했소. \"나는 그자를 압니다.\"\n\n\"그분을 아는 사람치고, 그분에 대해 나쁜 걸 아는 이는 아무도 없을 거예요.\" 톰이 말한 그 알 수 없는 태도에 발끈하며 과부가 말했소.\n\n\"흠!\" 톰 스마트가 말했소.\n\n과부는 이제 슬슬 울 때가 됐다고 여겨, 손수건을 꺼내 들고는, 톰이 자기를 모욕하려는 거냐고, 다른 신사의 인격을 그 등 뒤에서 헐뜯는 게 신사다운 짓이라고 여기느냐고, 아니 할 말이 있으면 사내답게 그 사내에게 직접 말할 일이지 어째서 이런 식으로 가엾고 연약한 여자를 겁주느냐고 따져 물었소, 등등 말이오.\n\n\"그자에게는 얼마든지 직접 말하지요.\" 톰이 말했소. \"다만 당신이 먼저 들어 주었으면 해서요.\"\n\n\"그게 뭔데요?\" 과부가 톰의 얼굴을 뚫어지게 들여다보며 물었소.\n\n\"당신을 깜짝 놀라게 해 드리지요.\" 톰이 주머니에 손을 넣으며 말했소.\n\n\"만약 그게, 그분이 돈을 원한다는 얘기라면,\" 과부가 말했소. \"그건 이미 알고 있으니, 수고하실 것 없어요.\" \"쳇, 당치 않아요, 그런 건 아무것도 아닙니다.\" 톰 스마트가 말했소. \"돈이야 나도 원하는 걸요. 그게 아니라요.\"\n\n\"어머, 저런, 대체 뭘까요?\" 가엾은 과부가 소리쳤소.\n\n\"놀라지 마십시오.\" 톰 스마트가 말했소. 그는 천천히 편지를 끄집어내 펼쳤소. \"비명은 안 지르시겠지요?\" 톰이 미심쩍은 듯 말했소.\n\n\"네, 네.\" 과부가 대답했소. \"어디 보여 주세요.\"\n\n\"기절하거나, 뭐 그런 부질없는 짓은 안 하시겠지요?\" 톰이 말했소.\n\n\"네, 네.\" 과부가 다급히 대답했소.\n\n\"그리고 뛰쳐나가서 그자에게 퍼붓지도 마십시오.\" 톰이 말했소. \"그건 내가 당신을 위해 다 해 드릴 테니까요. 당신은 기력을 빼지 않는 게 좋습니다.\"\n\n\"알았어요, 알았어요.\" 과부가 말했소. \"어디 보여 주세요.\"\n\n\"그러지요.\" 톰 스마트가 대답하고, 이 말과 함께 편지를 과부의 손에 쥐여 주었소.\n\n여러분, 우리 삼촌이 말씀하시기를, 톰 스마트가 그러더랍니다, 그 폭로를 듣고 과부가 터뜨린 통곡은 돌 같은 심장도 꿰뚫었을 거라고요. 톰은 확실히 마음이 무척 여린 사람이었소만, 그 통곡은 그의 심장을 한복판까지 꿰뚫었소. 과부는 몸을 앞뒤로 흔들며 두 손을 비틀어 짰소.\n\n\"오, 그 사람의 기만과 악행이라니!\" 과부가 말했소.\n\n\"끔찍하지요, 친애하는 마님. 하지만 마음을 가라앉히십시오.\" 톰 스마트가 말했소.\n\n\"오, 마음을 가라앉힐 수가 없어요.\" 과부가 비명을 질렀소. \"이만큼 사랑할 수 있는 사람을 다시는 못 찾을 거예요!\"\n\n\"오, 아닙니다, 찾으실 겁니다, 친애하는 분이여.\" 톰 스마트가, 과부의 불행을 가엾이 여겨 더없이 큼직한 눈물을 비 오듯 떨구며 말했소. 톰 스마트는 그 연민의 격정 속에 과부의 허리에 팔을 둘렀고, 과부는 비탄의 격정 속에 톰의 손을 꽉 움켜쥐었소. 그녀는 톰의 얼굴을 올려다보며 눈물 속에 미소 지었소. 톰은 그녀의 얼굴을 내려다보며 눈물 속에 미소 지었소.\n\n나는 도무지 알아낼 수가 없었소, 여러분, 그 특별한 순간에 톰이 과부에게 입을 맞추었는지 안 맞추었는지 말이오. 톰은 우리 삼촌에게 안 맞추었다고 했다는데, 나는 그게 영 미심쩍소. 우리끼리 얘기지만, 여러분, 나는 그가 맞춘 쪽이라고 짐작하오.\n\n여하튼, 톰은 반 시간 뒤 그 아주 키 큰 사내를 현관문 밖으로 걷어차 냈고, 한 달 뒤 과부와 결혼했소. 그리고 그는 붉은 바퀴를 단 진흙빛 이륜마차와 빨리 달리는 성깔 사나운 암말을 몰고 시골을 돌아다니다가, 여러 해 뒤 사업을 접고 아내와 함께 프랑스로 갔소. 그리고 그제야 그 낡은 집은 헐렸소.'\n\n\"한 가지 여쭤도 되겠소이까.\" 캐묻기 좋아하는 노신사가 말했다. \"그 의자는 어떻게 됐소?\"\n\n\"음.\" 외눈의 외판원이 대답했다. \"결혼식 날 그게 몹시 삐걱대더라는 게 사람들 눈에 띄었지요. 그렇지만 톰 스마트는 그게 기뻐서였는지 몸이 성치 않아서였는지는 확실히 말할 수 없었소. 그래도 그는 후자 쪽이라고 짐작했소, 그 뒤로 의자가 다시는 입을 떼지 않았으니까요.\"\n\n\"다들 그 이야기를 믿었겠지요?\" 더러운 얼굴의 사내가 파이프를 다시 채우며 말했다.\n\n\"톰의 원수들만 빼고요.\" 외판원이 대답했다. \"더러는 톰이 그걸 통째로 지어낸 거라 했고, 또 더러는 톰이 취해서 헛것을 본 데다, 잠자리에 들기 전 실수로 엉뚱한 바지를 집어 든 거라 했지요. 하지만 그자들이 하는 말 따위에 신경 쓰는 사람은 아무도 없었소.\"\n\n\"톰 스마트는 그게 다 참말이라고 했고요?\"\n\n\"한마디 한마디 다요.\"\n\n\"그럼 당신 삼촌은요?\"\n\n\"글자 하나하나 다요.\"\n\n\"둘 다 무척 좋은 사람들이었겠구려.\" 더러운 얼굴의 사내가 말했다.\n\n\"그럼요, 그랬지요.\" 외판원이 대답했다. \"정말이지 무척 좋은 사람들이었소!\"\n\n픽윅 씨의 양심은 피코크에 있는 친구들을 요사이 소홀히 한 일로 그를 다소간 책망하고 있었다. 선거가 끝난 지 사흘째 되는 날 아침, 그가 막 친구들을 찾아 나서려던 참에, 충직한 시종이 그의 손에 명함 한 장을 쥐어 주었다. 거기에는 다음과 같은 문구가 새겨져 있었다.—\n\n리오 헌터 부인 디 덴. 이턴스윌.\n\n\"손님이 기다리십니다.\" 샘이 경구처럼 말했다.\n\n\"그 손님이 나를 보러 온 거냐, 샘?\" 픽윅 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"각별히 나리를 찾으십니다. 다른 사람으론 안 된답니다, 악마의 개인 비서가 파우스트 박사를 데리러 왔을 때 한 말마따나요.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"그라니. 신사분이냐?\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"신사가 아니라면, 신사 흉내는 아주 그럴듯하게 내는 양반입죠.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"하지만 이건 부인의 명함인데.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그래도 신사분이 제게 건네주셨습니다요.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"지금 응접실에서 기다리고 계세요—나리를 못 뵈느니 차라리 온종일 기다리겠다고 하시던데요.\"\n\n이 결연한 태도를 듣고 픽윅 씨는 응접실로 내려갔다. 거기에는 근엄한 사내가 앉아 있다가, 그가 들어서자 벌떡 일어나 깊은 경의를 담은 태도로 말했다.—\n\n\"픽윅 씨 되시겠지요?\"\n\n\"그렇습니다.\"\n\n\"선생, 선생의 손을 잡는 영광을 허락해 주십시오. 부디 악수하게 해 주십시오.\" 근엄한 사내가 말했다.\n\n\"물론입니다.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. 낯선 이는 내민 손을 잡아 흔들고는 말을 이었다.—\n\n\"선생의 명성을 익히 들었습니다. 선생의 고고학 토론에 관한 소문이 리오 헌터 부인의 귀에까지 들어갔지요—제 아내입니다, 선생. 저는 리오 헌터입니다.\" 낯선 이는 마치 픽윅 씨가 이 사실에 압도되리라 기대하는 듯 잠시 말을 멈추었으나, 그가 더없이 침착하게 있는 것을 보고는 말을 계속했다.—\n\n\"제 아내, 선생—리오 헌터 부인—은 자신의 작품과 재능으로 명성을 떨친 모든 이들을 지인으로 두는 것을 자랑으로 여깁니다. 선생, 그 명단의 잘 보이는 자리에 픽윅 씨와, 그분의 이름을 딴 클럽의 동료 회원들의 이름을 올리도록 허락해 주십시오.\"\n\n\"그런 부인과 가까이 지내게 된다면 더없이 기쁘겠습니다, 선생.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"꼭 그리 되실 겁니다, 선생.\" 근엄한 사내가 말했다. \"내일 아침에, 선생, 저희가 공개 조찬을—야외 연회를—엽니다. 자신의 작품과 재능으로 명성을 떨친 수많은 이들을 위해서요. 리오 헌터 부인이 디 덴에서 선생을 뵙는 기쁨을 누리게 해 주십시오.\"\n\n\"기꺼이 그러겠습니다.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"리오 헌터 부인은 이런 조찬을 여러 번 엽니다, 선생.\" 새 지인이 말을 이었다. \"'이성의 향연'이지요, 선생, '그리고 영혼의 흐름'이고요. 리오 헌터 부인의 조찬에 부치는 소네트를 쓴 어느 분이 절절하고도 독창적으로 표현한 말입니다.\"\n\n\"그분도 작품과 재능으로 명성을 떨친 분입니까?\" 픽윅 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"그렇습니다, 선생.\" 근엄한 사내가 대답했다. \"리오 헌터 부인의 지인은 모두 그렇습니다. 다른 부류의 지인은 두지 않는 것이 부인의 포부지요, 선생.\"\n\n\"참으로 고결한 포부로군요.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"리오 헌터 부인에게 그 말씀이 선생의 입에서 나왔다고 전하면, 부인은 정말로 자랑스러워할 겁니다, 선생.\" 근엄한 사내가 말했다. \"선생의 일행 중에 아름다운 소품 시들을 지어 낸 신사분이 한 분 계신 걸로 압니다만, 선생.\"\n\n\"제 친구 스노드그래스 씨가 시에 대단한 취향을 지니고 있지요.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"리오 헌터 부인도 그렇습니다, 선생. 부인은 시를 끔찍이 좋아합니다, 선생. 시를 흠모하지요. 부인의 온 영혼과 정신이 시로 칭칭 감겨 얽혀 있다 해도 좋을 정도입니다. 부인 자신도 몇 편의 멋진 작품을 지었습니다, 선생. 부인의 '죽어 가는 개구리에게 부치는 송시'를 접해 보셨을지도 모르겠군요, 선생.\"\n\n\"아마 못 본 것 같습니다.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"놀랍군요, 선생.\" 리오 헌터 씨가 말했다. \"엄청난 반향을 일으켰는데요. 'L'과 별 여덟 개로 서명되어, 처음에는 어느 부인 잡지에 실렸지요. 이렇게 시작합니다.—\n\n\"'배를 깔고 누워 헐떡이는 너를 보며 한숨짓지 않을 수 있으랴. 통나무 위에서 죽어 가는 너를 보며 마음이 흔들리지 않을 수 있으랴, 죽어 가는 개구리여!'\"\n\n\"아름답군요!\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"훌륭하지요.\" 리오 헌터 씨가 말했다. \"참으로 단순하고요.\"\n\n\"매우 그렇습니다.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"다음 연은 한층 더 가슴을 울립니다. 읊어 드릴까요?\"\n\n\"그러시지요.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"이렇게 이어집니다.\" 근엄한 사내가 한층 더 근엄하게 말했다.\n\n\"'말해 다오, 소년의 모습을 한 악귀들이 거친 함성과 잔인한 소음으로, 개 한 마리를 데리고 늪지의 기쁨에서 너를 사냥해 쫓아내었느냐, 죽어 가는 개구리여!'\"\n\n\"표현이 훌륭하군요.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"하나하나가 다 핵심이지요, 선생.\" 리오 헌터 씨가 말했다. \"하지만 리오 헌터 부인이 읊는 것을 들어 보셔야 합니다. 부인은 이 시를 제대로 살려 낼 수 있어요, 선생. 내일 아침에 분장까지 하고 읊을 겁니다, 선생.\"\n\n\"분장까지요!\"\n\n\"미네르바로요. 아, 깜빡했군요—분장 무도 조찬이랍니다.\"\n\n\"이런.\" 픽윅 씨가 자기 몸을 흘끗 내려다보며 말했다. \"저는 도저히—\"\n\n\"못 한다니요, 선생! 못 한다니요!\" 리오 헌터 씨가 외쳤다. \"하이 스트리트의 유대인 솔로몬 루커스가 분장 의상을 수천 벌 갖고 있습니다. 생각해 보십시오, 선생, 선생이 고르실 수 있는 적절한 배역이 얼마나 많은지요. 플라톤, 제논, 에피쿠로스, 피타고라스—모두 클럽의 창시자들이지요.\"\n\n\"그건 압니다.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"하지만 그런 위인들과 저를 견줄 수는 없으니, 감히 그들의 의상을 걸칠 수는 없습니다.\"\n\n근엄한 사내는 몇 초간 깊이 생각하더니 말했다.—\n\n\"다시 생각해 보니, 선생, 손님들이 선생처럼 명성 높은 신사분을 가장 의상이 아니라 본인의 평상복 차림으로 보는 편이 리오 헌터 부인에게 더 큰 기쁨을 줄지도 모르겠습니다. 선생의 경우에는 감히 예외를 약속드릴 수 있겠어요, 선생—그렇습니다, 리오 헌터 부인을 대신해 그리해도 좋다고 확신합니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면,\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"기꺼이 가도록 하겠습니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 제가 선생의 시간을 빼앗고 있군요, 선생.\" 근엄한 사내가 문득 정신을 차린 듯 말했다. \"그 가치를 잘 압니다, 선생. 더는 붙들지 않겠습니다. 그럼 리오 헌터 부인에게, 선생과 선생의 저명한 친구분들이 틀림없이 오시리라 안심하고 기다려도 된다고 전해도 되겠지요? 안녕히 계십시오, 선생. 이렇게 걸출한 인물을 뵙게 되어 영광입니다—나오지 마십시오, 선생. 한마디도 마십시오.\" 그러고는 픽윅 씨가 만류하거나 사양할 틈도 주지 않고, 리오 헌터 씨는 근엄하게 성큼성큼 걸어 나갔다.\n\n픽윅 씨는 모자를 집어 들고 피코크로 갔으나, 윈클 씨가 그보다 앞서 그곳에 분장 무도회 소식을 전한 뒤였다.\n\n\"포트 부인이 가신답니다.\" 그가 자기 지도자에게 건넨 첫마디는 이것이었다.\n\n\"그래?\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"아폴로로 분장하고요.\" 윈클이 대답했다. \"다만 포트 씨가 튜닉은 안 된다고 하셔서요.\"\n\n\"옳은 말이네. 지당한 말이야.\" 픽윅 씨가 힘주어 말했다.\n\n\"네, 그래서 금박 장식이 달린 흰 새틴 가운을 입으신답니다.\"\n\n\"그러면 사람들이 그분이 뭘 표현한 건지 알아보기 힘들지 않을까요?\" 스노드그래스 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"당연히 알아보죠.\" 윈클 씨가 발끈하며 대답했다. \"그분의 리라를 보면 알 거 아닙니까?\"\n\n\"그렇군. 그걸 깜빡했네.\" 스노드그래스 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"저는 산적으로 가겠습니다.\" 터프먼 씨가 끼어들었다.\n\n\"뭐라고요!\" 픽윅 씨가 화들짝 놀라며 말했다.\n\n\"산적으로요.\" 터프먼 씨가 부드럽게 되풀이했다.\n\n\"설마,\" 픽윅 씨가 엄숙하고 준엄한 눈길로 친구를 바라보며 말했다. \"설마, 터프먼 씨, 두 치짜리 꼬리가 달린 초록 벨벳 재킷을 걸치겠다는 건 아니겠지요?\"\n\n\"바로 그게 제 뜻입니다, 선생.\" 터프먼 씨가 발끈하며 대답했다. \"안 될 게 뭐 있습니까, 선생?\"\n\n\"왜냐하면, 선생,\" 픽윅 씨가 상당히 흥분하여 말했다. \"선생은 너무 나이가 많으니까요, 선생.\"\n\n\"너무 나이가 많다니!\" 터프먼 씨가 외쳤다.\n\n\"그리고 반대할 근거가 더 필요하다면,\" 픽윅 씨가 말을 이었다. \"선생은 너무 뚱뚱합니다, 선생.\"\n\n\"선생,\" 터프먼 씨가 얼굴이 시뻘겋게 달아올라 말했다. \"이건 모욕입니다.\"\n\n\"선생,\" 픽윅 씨가 똑같은 어조로 대답했다. \"선생이 두 치짜리 꼬리 달린 초록 벨벳 재킷을 입고 제 앞에 나타나는 것이 제게 주는 모욕에 비하면, 이건 그 절반도 안 되는 모욕입니다.\"\n\n\"선생,\" 터프먼 씨가 말했다. \"당신은 형편없는 작자요.\"\n\n\"선생,\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"당신도 마찬가지요!\"\n\n터프먼 씨가 한두 걸음 다가와 픽윅 씨를 노려보았다. 픽윅 씨도 안경을 통해 초점을 모은 노려보기로 응수하며 당당히 도전의 숨을 내쉬었다. 스노드그래스 씨와 윈클 씨는 그런 두 인물 사이에 벌어진 장면을 보며 돌처럼 굳은 채 지켜보았다.\n\n\"선생,\" 터프먼 씨가 잠시 멈추었다가 낮고 깊은 목소리로 말했다. \"당신은 나를 늙었다고 했소.\"\n\n\"그랬소.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그리고 뚱뚱하다고.\"\n\n\"그 비난을 거듭하겠소.\"\n\n\"그리고 형편없는 작자라고.\"\n\n\"실제로 그러하니까!\"\n\n무시무시한 침묵이 흘렀다.\n\"당신이라는 인물에 대한 제 애착은, 선생,\" 터프먼 씨가 감정으로 떨리는 목소리로 말하며 그러는 사이 소맷부리를 걷어 올렸다. \"크오—매우 크오—하나 그 인물에게 즉결로 복수를 하지 않을 수 없소.\"\n\n\"덤비시오, 선생!\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다. 자극적인 대화의 흥분에 휩싸여, 이 영웅적인 사내는 실제로 중풍 걸린 듯한 자세를 취했는데, 두 구경꾼은 그것이 방어 자세로 의도된 것이라고 자신 있게 짐작했다.\n\n\"뭐라고요!\" 스노드그래스 씨가 외쳤다. 극심한 경악으로 잃었던 말의 능력을 문득 되찾고는, 양쪽에서 관자놀이에 한 방씩 맞을 위험을 무릅쓰며 두 사람 사이로 뛰어들었다. \"뭐라고요! 픽윅 씨, 온 세상의 눈이 당신께 쏠려 있는데요! 터프먼 씨! 우리 모두와 마찬가지로 그의 불멸의 이름에서 광채를 얻는 분께서! 부끄러운 줄 아십시오, 두 분! 부끄러운 줄 아십시오.\"\n\n순간적인 격정이 픽윅 씨의 맑고 트인 이마에 그어 놓았던 낯선 주름들이, 젊은 친구가 말하는 동안 차츰 녹아 사라졌으니, 마치 흑연 연필 자국이 지우개의 부드러운 손길 아래 지워지듯 하였다. 그가 말을 마치기도 전에 그의 얼굴은 평소의 인자한 표정을 되찾았다.\n\n\"내가 성급했네.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"참으로 성급했어. 터프먼, 손을 주게.\"\n\n터프먼 씨가 친구의 손을 따뜻하게 맞잡자, 그의 얼굴에서 어두운 그림자가 걷혔다.\n\n\"저도 성급했습니다.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 아닐세.\" 픽윅 씨가 가로막았다. \"내 잘못이었네. 초록 벨벳 재킷을 입겠지?\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 아니에요.\" 터프먼 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"내 부탁이니 입어 주게나.\" 픽윅 씨가 말을 이었다.\n\n\"좋습니다, 좋아요, 입겠습니다.\" 터프먼 씨가 말했다.\n\n이리하여 터프먼 씨, 윈클 씨, 스노드그래스 씨 모두 분장 의상을 입기로 정해졌다. 이렇게 픽윅 씨는 그 자신의 선량한 감정의 열기 그 자체에 이끌려, 보다 나은 분별이라면 움츠러들었을 일에 동의하고 말았다.—그의 사랑스러운 성품을 이보다 더 인상 깊게 보여 주는 예는, 설령 이 지면에 기록된 사건들이 전부 가상의 것이라 해도 좀처럼 떠올리기 어려울 것이다.\n\n리오 헌터 씨는 솔로몬 루커스 씨의 자원을 과장한 것이 아니었다. 그의 의상 창고는 방대했다—매우 방대했다—어쩌면 엄밀히 고전적이지는 않았고, 아주 새것도 아니었으며, 어느 시대나 시기의 양식을 정확히 따라 지은 옷이라곤 한 벌도 없었으나, 모든 것에 어느 정도씩 금박 장식이 달려 있었다. 그리고 금박만큼 예쁜 것이 또 어디 있겠는가! 그것이 대낮에는 어울리지 않는다고 이의를 제기할 수도 있겠으나, 등불만 있다면 반짝였으리란 것은 누구나 아는 사실이다. 그러니 사람들이 대낮에 분장 무도회를 열어 의상이 밤만큼 돋보이지 않는다면, 그 잘못은 오로지 분장 무도회를 여는 사람들에게 있는 것이지, 결코 금박에 물을 수 있는 일이 아니라는 것보다 더 명백한 이치는 없다. 이것이 솔로몬 루커스 씨의 설득력 있는 논리였다. 그리고 그런 논변에 영향을 받아, 터프먼 씨와 윈클 씨와 스노드그래스 씨는 그의 안목과 경험이 이 행사에 더없이 잘 어울린다고 추천한 의상으로 차려입기로 약속했다.\n\n픽윅 회원들의 편의를 위해 타운 암스에서 마차 한 대를 빌렸고, 같은 곳에서 포트 부부를 리오 헌터 부인의 저택으로 모실 사륜마차도 주문했다. 포트 씨는 초대받은 데 대한 섬세한 답례로, 이미 이턴스윌 가제트에서 그 조찬이 \"다채롭고 황홀한 마법의 광경을—아름다움과 재능의 어지러운 광채를—후하고 아낌없는 환대의 향연을—무엇보다도, 더없이 정교한 안목으로 누그러뜨린 광휘를, 완벽한 조화와 가장 정숙한 절제로 다듬어진 장식을 펼쳐 보일 것이며, 이에 견주면 전설로 전하는 동방 요정 나라의 화려함조차도, 자신의 시샘의 독으로 감히 더럽히려 드는 신경질적이고 사내답지 못한 자의 마음만큼이나 어둡고 칙칙한 빛깔을 두른 것처럼 보이리니, 그 덕성 높고 지극히 걸출한 부인의 신전에 이 보잘것없는 찬미의 공물이 바쳐지는 것\"이라고 자신 있게 예언해 둔 터였다. 이 마지막 대목은 인디펜던트를 겨냥한 신랄한 풍자였으니, 인디펜던트는 아예 초대받지 못한 탓에 네 호에 걸쳐 가장 큰 활자로, 형용사마다 대문자로 박아 가며 이 모든 일을 비웃는 척해 온 터였다.\n\n마침내 아침이 밝았다. 터프먼 씨가 완전한 산적 차림으로 있는 모습은 볼 만한 광경이었으니, 아주 꽉 끼는 재킷이 그의 등과 어깨 위에 바늘방석처럼 얹혀 있었고, 다리 윗부분은 벨벳 반바지에 싸여 있었으며, 아랫부분은 모든 산적이 유독 즐겨 두르는 복잡한 붕대로 칭칭 감겨 있었다. 트인 셔츠 깃 사이로 콧수염을 잘 기르고 그을음으로 칠한 그의 솔직하고 천진한 얼굴이 내다보는 모습이며, 온갖 색의 리본으로 장식한 설탕 덩이 모양의 모자를 보는 것도 즐거운 일이었으니, 그 모자는 그가 무릎 위에 올려 들고 있을 수밖에 없었다. 지붕이 있는 어떤 알려진 탈것도 머리와 천장 사이에 그것을 끼우고 탈 수 있게 해 주지 않았기 때문이다. 마찬가지로 우스꽝스럽고 보기 좋았던 것은 스노드그래스 씨의 모습이었으니, 그는 파란 새틴 짧은 바지와 망토에, 흰 비단 타이츠와 신발을 신고, 그리스식 투구를 썼는데, 그것이 가장 이른 시대부터 음유시인이 지상에서 마침내 자취를 감추던 때까지의, 정식이고 진정한 그들의 일상 복장이었다는 것은 누구나 아는 사실이다(설령 모른다 해도 솔로몬 루커스 씨는 알았다). 이 모든 것이 흐뭇했으나, 마차가 포트 씨의 사륜마차 뒤에 멈춰 섰을 때 군중이 지른 함성에 비하면 아무것도 아니었다. 그 사륜마차는 포트 씨의 문 앞에 멈춰 섰고, 그 문이 열리며 위대한 포트가 모습을 드러냈으니, 그는 러시아 사법 관리 차림으로 손에 어마어마한 채찍을 들고 있었다—이턴스윌 가제트의 준엄하고 막강한 위력과, 그것이 공적 범법자들에게 가하는 무시무시한 매질을 멋들어지게 상징하는 것이었다.\n\n\"브라보!\" 터프먼 씨와 스노드그래스 씨가 그 걸어 다니는 알레고리를 보고 복도에서 외쳤다.\n\n\"브라보!\" 픽윅 씨도 복도에서 외치는 소리가 들렸다.\n\n\"포트 만세!\" 군중이 외쳤다. 이러한 인사 속에서, 포트 씨는 자신의 권력을 느끼고 그것을 행사할 줄 안다는 것을 충분히 드러내는 그런 온화한 위엄의 미소를 띤 채, 사륜마차에 올랐다.\n\n이어 집에서 포트 부인이 나왔는데, 가운만 걸치지 않았더라면 영락없는 아폴로처럼 보였을 것이며, 윈클 씨의 안내를 받고 있었다. 윈클 씨는 연붉은 코트를 입고 있어, 일반 우편배달부와 똑같이 닮아 보이지만 않았더라면 영락없이 사냥꾼으로밖에 보이지 않았을 것이다. 맨 마지막으로 픽윅 씨가 나왔는데, 소년들은 누구 못지않게 크게 그에게 박수를 보냈으니, 아마도 그의 타이츠와 각반을 암흑시대의 잔재쯤으로 여긴 모양이었다. 그러고는 두 대의 탈것이 리오 헌터 부인의 저택을 향해 나아갔다. 웰러 씨는(시중을 거들기로 되어 있었다) 자기 주인이 탄 탈것의 마부석에 자리를 잡았다.\n\n분장 의상을 차려입은 손님들을 보려고 모여든 남자, 여자, 소년, 소녀, 갓난아기 할 것 없이 하나하나가, 픽윅 씨가 한쪽 팔에 산적을, 다른 쪽 팔에 음유시인을 끼고 엄숙하게 입구로 걸어 올라가자 환희와 황홀로 비명을 질렀다. 터프먼 씨가 정원에 멋들어지게 입장하려고 설탕 덩이 모양의 모자를 머리에 고정시키려 애쓰는 모습을 맞이한 함성만큼 대단한 함성은 일찍이 들어 본 적이 없었다.\n\n준비는 더없이 흐뭇한 규모로 갖추어져 있었으니, 동방 요정 나라의 화려함에 관한 예언가 포트의 기대를 완전히 실현하는 것이자, 동시에 파충류 같은 인디펜던트의 악의에 찬 진술을 충분히 반박해 주는 것이었다. 저택은 넓이가 일 에이커 사 분의 일이 넘었고, 사람들로 가득 차 있었다! 그런 미모와 멋과 문학의 향연은 일찍이 없었다. 이턴스윌 가제트의 시 담당을 '맡은' 젊은 숙녀가 술탄 비빈의 차림으로, 서평란을 '맡은' 젊은 신사의 팔에 기대어 있었는데, 그 신사는 적절하게도 야전 원수의 군복을 차려입고 있었다—장화만 빼고 말이다. 이런 천재들이 무리를 이루고 있었으니, 분별 있는 사람이라면 누구라도 그들을 만나는 것만으로도 충분한 영광이라 여겼을 것이다. 그러나 이들 말고도, 런던에서 온 사자가 여섯이나 있었으니—작가들, 진짜 작가들로, 책을 통째로 써내고 그것을 나중에 인쇄까지 한 이들이었다—여기서 당신은 그들이 보통 사람처럼 돌아다니며 웃고 떠드는 것을, 그래, 게다가 꽤나 상당한 헛소리를 떠드는 것을 볼 수 있었으니, 틀림없이 자신들을 둘러싼 보통 사람들이 알아들을 수 있게 하려는 인자한 의도에서였으리라. 게다가 판지 모자를 쓴 악단이 있었고, 자기 나라 의상을 입은 네 명의 무슨무슨 출신 가수들과, 자기네 나라 의상—그것도 아주 더러운 의상—을 입은 고용된 웨이터 열두 명이 있었다. 그리고 무엇보다도, 미네르바로 분장한 리오 헌터 부인이 손님들을 맞이하며, 이토록 저명한 인사들을 한자리에 불러 모았다는 생각에 자부심과 만족으로 넘쳐 있었다.\n\n\"픽윅 씨 오셨습니다, 마님.\" 픽윅 씨가 모자를 손에 들고 산적과 음유시인을 양팔에 낀 채 주재하는 여신에게 다가가자, 한 하인이 말했다.\n\n\"어머! 어디!\" 리오 헌터 부인이 짐짓 꾸민 놀람의 황홀경에 빠져 벌떡 일어나며 외쳤다.\n\n\"여기 있습니다.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"내가 정말로 픽윅 씨 본인을 뵙는 기쁨을 누리는 게 가능한 일인가요!\" 리오 헌터 부인이 부르짖었다.\n\n\"다름 아닌 본인입니다, 마님.\" 픽윅 씨가 아주 깊이 허리를 숙이며 대답했다. \"제 친구들을 소개해 드리지요—터프먼 씨—윈클 씨—스노드그래스 씨를—'죽어 가는 개구리'의 작가께.\"\n\n초록 벨벳 반바지에 꽉 끼는 재킷과 높다란 모자 차림으로, 혹은 파란 새틴 짧은 바지와 흰 비단 차림으로, 혹은 결코 입는 이를 위해 만든 것이 아니어서 그와 옷의 상대적 치수를 전혀 고려하지 않고 그에게 끼워 맞춘 무릎 끈 바지와 장화 차림으로 허리를 숙이는 일이 얼마나 어려운 과정인지는, 직접 해 본 사람이 아니고서는 거의 알지 못한다. 편안하고 우아해 보이려 애쓰는 가운데 터프먼 씨의 몸이 겪은 것 같은 기괴한 뒤틀림은 일찍이 없었으며—그의 분장한 친구들이 보여 준 것 같은 기발한 자세 잡기도 일찍이 없었다.\n\n\"픽윅 씨,\" 리오 헌터 부인이 말했다. \"온종일 제 곁을 떠나지 않겠다고 약속해 주셔야겠어요. 여기 제가 꼭 소개해 드려야 할 분들이 수백 명이나 계시거든요.\"\n\n\"참으로 친절하십니다, 마님.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"우선, 여기 제 어린 딸들이에요. 하마터면 깜빡할 뻔했네요.\" 미네르바가 다 자란 두 젊은 숙녀를 무심히 가리키며 말했는데, 한 명은 스무 살쯤, 다른 한 명은 그보다 한두 살 위였으며, 둘 다 매우 앳된 의상을 입고 있었다.—그것이 그들을 어려 보이게 하려는 것인지, 아니면 그들의 엄마를 더 젊어 보이게 하려는 것인지는, 픽윅 씨가 우리에게 분명히 일러 주지 않는다.\n\n\"참으로 아름다운 따님들입니다.\" 어린 딸들이 소개를 마친 뒤 돌아서자 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"어머니를 꼭 닮았지요, 선생.\" 포트 씨가 위엄 있게 말했다.\n\n\"어머, 짓궂으셔라.\" 리오 헌터 부인이 부채로 편집장의 팔을 장난스레 톡 치며 외쳤다(부채를 든 미네르바라니!).\n\n\"아니 이런, 친애하는 헌터 부인,\" 디 덴의 상임 나팔수인 포트 씨가 말했다. \"부인도 아시다시피 작년에 부인의 초상화가 왕립 미술원 전시회에 걸렸을 때, 모두가 그게 부인을 그린 건지 아니면 부인의 막내따님을 그린 건지 물었지요. 두 분이 어찌나 닮았던지 분간할 수가 없었으니까요.\"\n\n\"그래, 그랬다 한들, 굳이 낯선 분들 앞에서 그 말을 되풀이할 필요가 어디 있어요?\" 리오 헌터 부인이 이턴스윌 가제트의 잠든 사자에게 다시 한 번 톡 치며 말했다.\n\n\"백작님, 백작님.\" 리오 헌터 부인이 마침 지나가던, 외국 군복을 입고 구레나룻이 멋진 인물에게 외쳤다.\n\n\"아! 저를 부르셨소?\" 백작이 돌아서며 말했다.\n\n\"아주 영리한 두 분을 서로 소개해 드리고 싶어서요.\" 리오 헌터 부인이 말했다. \"픽윅 씨, 스모를토크 백작님을 소개해 드리게 되어 더없이 기쁩니다.\" 그러고는 픽윅 씨에게 황급히 속삭이며 덧붙였다.—\"유명한 외국분이세요—영국에 관한 대작에 쓸 자료를 모으고 계시지요—흠!—스모를토크 백작님, 픽윅 씨예요.\"\n\n픽윅 씨는 그토록 위대한 인물에게 마땅한 온갖 경의를 다해 백작에게 인사했고, 백작은 수첩을 한 권 꺼냈다.\n\n\"뭐라고 하셨소, 헌트 부인?\" 만족스러워하는 리오 헌터 부인에게 점잖게 미소 지으며 백작이 물었다. \"피그 비그요, 빅 비그요—뭐라더라—변호사—응? 알겠소—그거로군. 빅 비그.\" 그러고서 백작은 픽윅 씨를, 자기가 속한 직업에서 이름을 따온 법복 신사로 수첩에 적어 넣으려던 참이었는데, 리오 헌터 부인이 끼어들었다.\n\n\"아니에요, 아니에요, 백작님.\" 부인이 말했다. \"픽—윅이에요.\"\n\n\"아, 아, 알겠소.\" 백작이 대답했다. \"피크—세례명이고, 윅스—성이로군. 좋소, 아주 좋아. 피크 윅스. 안녕하시오, 윅스?\"\n\n\"덕분에 아주 잘 지냅니다.\" 픽윅 씨가 여느 때처럼 상냥하게 대답했다. \"영국에 오신 지 오래되셨습니까?\"\n\n\"오래—아주 오래—두 주—그 이상.\"\n\n\"여기 오래 머무십니까?\"\n\n\"한 주요.\"\n\n\"그 시간 안에 필요한 자료를 다 모으시려면,\" 픽윅 씨가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"할 일이 꽤 많으시겠습니다.\"\n\n\"응, 이미 다 모았소.\" 백작이 말했다.\n\n\"정말입니까!\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"여기 다 있소.\" 백작이 자기 이마를 의미심장하게 톡톡 두드리며 덧붙였다. \"집에 큰 책이 있소—메모로 가득—음악, 그림, 과학, 시, 정치, 만사가.\"\n\n\"정치라는 말은, 선생,\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"그 자체로 결코 적지 않은 분량의 어려운 연구를 담고 있지요.\"\n\n\"아!\" 백작이 다시 수첩을 꺼내며 말했다. \"아주 좋소—한 장을 시작하기에 멋진 말이오. 사십칠 장. 정치. 정치라는 말은 그 자체로 놀라움을—\" 그리고 픽윅 씨의 말은 스모를토크 백작의 수첩 속에 적혀 들어갔으니, 백작의 넘쳐 나는 상상이 일러 주거나 그의 불완전한 언어 지식이 빚어낸 갖가지 변주와 추가가 곁들여졌다.\n\n\"백작님.\" 리오 헌터 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"헌트 부인.\" 백작이 대답했다.\n\n\"이분은 스노드그래스 씨인데, 픽윅 씨의 친구분이자 시인이세요.\"\n\n\"잠깐만.\" 백작이 다시 한 번 수첩을 꺼내며 외쳤다. \"항목, 시—장, 문인 친구들—이름, 스노그래스. 아주 좋소. 헌트 부인의 소개로 스노그래스를 알게 됨—위대한 시인, 피크 윅스의 친구—부인은 또 다른 감미로운 시를 썼는데—그 제목이 뭐라더라?—포그—땀 흘리는 포그—아주 좋소—참으로 아주 좋아.\" 그러고서 백작은 수첩을 집어넣고는, 여러 차례 절하고 사례의 인사를 하며 걸어갔으니, 자기 정보 창고에 가장 중요하고 값진 추가를 했노라 철석같이 만족해하고 있었다.\n\n\"놀라운 분이세요, 스모를토크 백작님은.\" 리오 헌터 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"건실한 철학자이지요.\" 포트 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"명석하고 의지가 굳센 분입니다.\" 스노드그래스 씨가 덧붙였다.\n\n곁에 있던 사람들이 일제히 스모를토크 백작을 칭송하는 외침을 받아, 현명하게 고개를 끄덕이며 한목소리로 외쳤다. \"정말로요!\"\n\n스모를토크 백작을 향한 열광이 워낙 드높았던 터라, 만일 무슨무슨 출신의 가수 넷이 그림 같은 모습을 연출하려고 작은 사과나무 앞에 늘어서서, 자기네 민요를 부르기 시작하지만 않았더라면, 그의 찬가는 잔치가 끝날 때까지 이어졌을지도 모른다. 그 민요는 부르기가 결코 어려워 보이지 않았으니, 그 위대한 비결인즉 무슨무슨 출신의 가수 셋이 꿀꿀거리는 동안 넷째가 울부짖는 것이었던 듯하다. 이 흥미로운 공연이 온 좌중의 우레와 같은 갈채 속에 끝나자, 곧이어 한 소년이 의자의 살에 제 몸을 얽고는, 그것을 뛰어넘고, 그 밑으로 기어들고, 그것과 함께 넘어지고, 그 위에 앉는 것만 빼고는 온갖 짓을 다 하더니, 이어 제 다리로 넥타이를 만들어 목에 두르고, 그러고는 인간이 얼마나 쉽게 거대한 두꺼비처럼 보이게 될 수 있는지를 실연해 보였다—이 모든 묘기가 모여든 구경꾼들에게 크나큰 즐거움과 만족을 안겨 주었다. 그러고 나서 포트 부인의 목소리가 희미하게 지저귀듯 흘러나오는 것이 들렸으니, 예의상 노래로 해석된 무언가였는데, 이는 매우 고전적이고 분장에도 엄격히 들어맞는 것이었다. 아폴로 자신이 작곡가였고, 작곡가란 자기 음악도 남의 음악도 좀처럼 노래할 줄 모르는 법이니까. 이어서 리오 헌터 부인이 그 널리 알려진 '죽어 가는 개구리에게 부치는 송시'를 낭송했는데, 한 차례 앙코르를 받았고, 만일 이제 뭔가 먹을 때가 됐다고 여긴 손님들 대다수가 헌터 부인의 호의를 이용해 먹는 것은 정말 부끄러운 일이라고 말하지 않았더라면 두 차례 앙코르를 받았을 것이다. 그리하여 리오 헌터 부인은 송시를 다시 낭송할 뜻이 충분히 있노라 공언했으나, 친절하고 사려 깊은 친구들은 무슨 일이 있어도 들으려 하지 않았다. 그리고 다과실이 활짝 열리자, 전에 한 번이라도 거기 와 본 적이 있는 사람들은 모두 가능한 한 서둘러 우르르 밀려들었다.—리오 헌터 부인의 통상적인 방식인즉, 백 명에게 초대장을 보내고 쉰 명분의 조찬을 차리는 것, 다시 말해 아주 특별한 사자들에게만 먹을 것을 주고 작은 짐승들은 알아서 챙기게 두는 것이었다.\n\n\"포트 씨는 어디 계세요?\" 리오 헌터 부인이 앞서 말한 사자들을 자기 둘레에 앉히며 말했다.\n\n\"여기 있습니다.\" 편집장이 방의 가장 먼 끝에서 말했으니, 안주인이 그를 위해 무언가 해 주지 않는 한 음식이라곤 바랄 가망이 전혀 없는 자리였다.\n\n\"이리 올라오시지 않겠어요?\"\n\n\"아, 부디 저이는 신경 쓰지 마세요.\" 포트 부인이 더없이 상냥한 목소리로 말했다. \"공연히 수고를 끼치고 계세요, 헌터 부인. 저이는 거기서도 잘 지낼 거예요, 그렇죠—여보?\"\n\n\"물론이지—여보.\" 불운한 포트가 음울한 미소를 띠며 대답했다. 아, 그 채찍이여! 공인들에게 그토록 거대한 위력으로 그것을 휘두르던 그 굳센 팔이, 도도한 포트 부인의 눈길 아래 마비되고 말았다.\n\n리오 헌터 부인은 의기양양하게 주위를 둘러보았다. 스모를토크 백작은 접시에 담긴 음식들의 내용을 메모하느라 분주했고, 터프먼 씨는 일찍이 어떤 산적도 보여 준 적 없는 우아함으로 여러 암사자들에게 바닷가재 샐러드를 권하고 있었다. 스노드그래스 씨는 이턴스윌 가제트의 책을 잘게 난도질하던 젊은 신사를 밀어내고는, 시를 담당하던 젊은 숙녀와 열정적인 토론에 빠져 있었으며, 픽윅 씨는 두루 누구에게나 호감을 사고 있었다. 이 가려 뽑은 모임을 완벽하게 만드는 데 모자란 것이라곤 아무것도 없어 보였는데, 그때 리오 헌터 씨가—이런 자리에서 그의 소임이란 문간께 서성이며 덜 중요한 사람들과 이야기를 나누는 것이었다—문득 외쳤다.—\n\n\"여보, 여기 찰스 피츠마셜 씨가 왔소.\"\n\n\"어머나,\" 리오 헌터 부인이 말했다. \"내가 얼마나 애타게 기다렸는지 몰라요. 부디 길을 비켜서 피츠마셜 씨가 지나가게 해 주세요. 여보, 피츠마셜 씨에게 곧장 내게로 올라오라고 일러 주세요, 이렇게 늦게 온 데 대해 야단을 좀 맞으셔야겠으니.\"\n\n\"가요, 친애하는 마님.\" 한 목소리가 외쳤다. \"최대한 빨리 가는 중입니다—사람들이 잔뜩—방이 꽉 차서—힘들군요—매우.\"\n\n픽윅 씨의 나이프와 포크가 손에서 떨어졌다. 그는 식탁 너머로 터프먼 씨를 응시했는데, 터프먼 씨도 나이프와 포크를 떨어뜨린 채, 더는 통보도 없이 그대로 땅속으로 꺼져 들어갈 듯한 표정을 짓고 있었다.\n\n\"아!\" 그 목소리가 외쳤으니, 그 주인이 자기와 식탁 사이에 남아 있는 마지막 스물다섯 명의 터키인과 장교와 기사와 찰스 이세들 틈을 비집고 나오면서였다. \"영락없는 압착기로군—베이커 특허품—이렇게 짓눌렸는데도 코트에 주름 하나 없어—오는 길에 내 속옷에 '풀까지 먹였을' 법한데—하하! 나쁘지 않은 생각이군, 그거—하긴 몸에 걸친 채로 압착당하는 건 묘한 일이지—고된 과정이야—매우.\"\n\n이렇게 두서없는 말과 함께, 해군 장교 차림을 한 한 젊은 남자가 식탁으로 다가와, 경악한 픽윅 회원들 앞에 바로 그 앨프리드 징글 씨의 모습과 이목구비를 그대로 내보였다.\n\n이 죄인은 리오 헌터 부인이 내민 손을 채 잡기도 전에, 그의 눈이 픽윅 씨의 분노로 이글거리는 눈동자와 마주쳤다.\n\n\"이런!\" 징글이 말했다. \"깜빡 잊었군—마부에게 지시를 안 했어—당장 일러 줘야겠소—금방 돌아오리다.\"\n\n\"하인이나 헌터 씨가 곧 처리할 거예요, 피츠마셜 씨.\" 리오 헌터 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 아니오—내가 하겠소—오래 안 걸리오—곧 돌아오리다.\" 징글이 대답했다. 이 말과 함께 그는 군중 속으로 사라졌다.\n\n\"실례지만 여쭤봐도 되겠습니까, 마님,\" 흥분한 픽윅 씨가 자리에서 일어나며 말했다. \"저 젊은이가 누구이며, 어디에 사는지를요?\"\n\n\"재산가이신 신사분이에요, 픽윅 씨.\" 리오 헌터 부인이 말했다. \"제가 꼭 소개해 드리고 싶었던 분이지요. 백작님도 저분을 무척 마음에 들어 하실 거예요.\"\n\n\"네, 네.\" 픽윅 씨가 다급히 말했다. \"그가 사는 곳은—\"\n\n\"지금은 베리의 에인절에 묵고 계세요.\"\n\n\"베리에요?\"\n\n\"베리 세인트 에드먼즈요, 여기서 몇 마일 안 됩니다. 그런데 어머나, 픽윅 씨, 저희를 떠나시려는 건 아니겠지요. 설마 픽윅 씨, 이렇게 일찍 가실 생각은 아니시겠지요?\"\n\n그러나 리오 헌터 부인이 말을 채 마치기도 한참 전에, 픽윅 씨는 인파를 헤치고 뛰어들어 정원에 다다랐으며, 곧이어 친구를 바짝 뒤따라온 터프먼 씨가 그와 합류했다.\n\n\"소용없습니다.\" 터프먼 씨가 말했다. \"가 버렸어요.\"\n\n\"알아.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"그러니 내가 그를 뒤쫓겠네.\"\n\n\"뒤쫓다니! 어디로요?\" 터프먼 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"베리의 에인절로.\" 픽윅 씨가 아주 빠르게 말했다. \"그가 거기서 누구를 속이고 있는지 우리가 어찌 알겠나? 그는 전에도 훌륭한 사람 하나를 속였고, 우리가 그 죄 없는 빌미가 되었네. 내가 막을 수만 있다면 다시는 그러게 두지 않겠어. 그자의 정체를 폭로하겠네! 샘! 내 시종은 어디 있나?\"\n\n\"여기 있습니다, 나리.\" 웰러 씨가 한적한 자리에서 모습을 드러내며 말했으니, 한두 시간 전에 조찬 식탁에서 슬쩍 빼돌린 마데이라 한 병을 처리하느라 거기 있던 참이었다. \"여기 나리의 시종이 있습니다요. 그 칭호가 자랑스럽습죠, 살아 있는 해골이 사람들이 자기를 구경시켜 줄 때 한 말마따나요.\"\n\n\"당장 나를 따라오너라.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"터프먼, 내가 베리에 머물게 되면 편지를 쓸 테니 그때 거기서 합류하게나. 그때까진, 잘 있게!\"\n\n만류해 봐야 소용없었다. 픽윅 씨는 분기탱천해 있었고, 마음을 굳힌 터였다. 터프먼 씨는 일행에게 돌아갔고, 한 시간이 채 못 되어 흥겨운 카드리유 한 곡과 샴페인 한 병에 앨프리드 징글 씨든 찰스 피츠마셜 씨든 당장의 기억을 모조리 묻어 버렸다. 그 무렵, 역마차 바깥자리에 올라탄 픽윅 씨와 샘 웰러는, 매분이 지날 때마다 자신들과 그 유서 깊은 옛 도시 베리 세인트 에드먼즈 사이의 거리를 점점 더 좁혀 가고 있었다.\n\n# CHAPTER XVI. TOO FULL OF ADVENTURE TO BE BRIEFLY DESCRIBED\n\n일 년 열두 달 가운데 자연이 8월보다 더 아름다운 모습을 띠는 달은 없다. 봄에도 여러 아름다움이 있고, 5월은 싱그럽고 꽃이 만발하는 달이지만, 그 시절의 매력은 겨울철과 대비됨으로써 한층 돋보인다. 8월에는 그런 이점이 없다. 8월은 우리가 맑은 하늘과 푸른 들판과 향기로운 꽃 말고는 아무것도 기억하지 못할 때—눈과 얼음과 매서운 바람의 기억이 땅에서 자취를 감춘 만큼이나 우리 마음에서도 말끔히 사라졌을 때—찾아온다. 그런데도 얼마나 즐거운 시절인가! 과수원과 옥수수밭은 노동의 콧노래로 울려 퍼지고, 나무들은 가지를 땅까지 휘게 하는 탐스러운 열매 송이의 무게에 휘어진다. 그리고 곡식은 우아한 단으로 쌓여 있거나, 그 위를 스치는 산들바람마다 마치 낫에게 구애라도 하듯 물결치며 풍경을 황금빛으로 물들인다. 온 대지에 그윽한 부드러움이 감도는 듯하다. 계절의 영향은 잘 베어진 들판을 천천히 가로지르는 마차에까지 미치는 듯한데, 그 느린 움직임은 오직 눈에만 보일 뿐 귀에는 어떤 거친 소리도 와닿지 않는다.\n\n마차가 길가에 늘어선 들판과 과수원을 빠르게 지나칠 때, 체에 과일을 담거나 흩어진 옥수수 이삭을 줍던 아낙네와 아이들의 무리는 잠시 일손을 멈추고, 햇볕에 그을린 얼굴을 그보다 더 까무잡잡한 손으로 가리며 호기심 어린 눈으로 승객들을 바라본다. 그런가 하면 일하기엔 너무 어리지만 집에 두기엔 너무 짓궂은 어떤 통통한 개구쟁이는 안전하게 담겨 있던 바구니 옆으로 기어 올라 발을 차며 기쁨에 겨워 소리를 지른다. 낫질하던 일꾼은 일손을 멈추고 팔짱을 낀 채 휙 지나가는 마차를 바라보고, 투박한 짐말들은 말끔한 역마차의 말 무리에게 졸린 듯한 눈길을 던지는데, 그 눈길은 말의 눈빛이 말할 수 있는 한 또렷이 이렇게 말하는 듯하다. \"보기엔 다 그럴듯하다만, 따지고 보면 먼지 자욱한 길 위에서 저렇게 땀나게 일하느니 무거운 들판을 느릿느릿 가는 편이 낫지.\" 길모퉁이를 돌면서 뒤를 한번 돌아본다. 아낙네와 아이들은 다시 일을 시작했고, 낫질하던 일꾼은 또다시 일감 위로 허리를 굽혔으며, 짐말들은 앞으로 나아갔다. 모두가 다시 움직이고 있다.\n\n이런 광경의 영향이 픽윅 씨의 잘 다스려진 마음에 헛되지 않았다. 그는 사기 행각을 벌이고 있는 곳이 어디든 비열한 징글의 본색을 폭로하겠다는 결심에 골몰한 채, 처음에는 말없이 깊은 생각에 잠겨, 목적을 가장 잘 이룰 수 있는 방법을 곰곰이 궁리하며 앉아 있었다. 차츰 그의 주의는 주변의 사물에 점점 더 끌렸고, 마침내 그는 마치 세상에서 가장 즐거운 이유로 이 길을 나선 것처럼 마차 여행을 한껏 즐기게 되었다.\n\n\"경치가 좋군, 샘.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"굴뚝 항아리보다야 훨씬 낫지요, 나리.\" 웰러 씨가 모자에 손을 대며 대답했다.\n\n\"자네는 평생 굴뚝 항아리와 벽돌과 모르타르 말고는 거의 본 게 없겠지, 샘.\" 픽윅 씨가 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"전 늘 구두닦이였던 건 아니랍니다, 나리.\" 웰러 씨가 고개를 저으며 말했다. \"한때는 짐마차꾼 조수였지요.\"\n\n\"그게 언제였나?\" 픽윅 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"제가 처음으로 세상에 머리부터 내동댕이쳐져, 세상의 온갖 골칫거리와 등넘기 놀이를 하게 됐을 때였죠.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"처음엔 운송꾼 조수였고, 그다음엔 짐마차꾼 조수, 그다음엔 거들이꾼, 그다음엔 구두닦이였습니다. 이제는 신사의 하인이지요. 언젠가는 저 자신이 신사가 될지도 모릅니다요. 입에 파이프를 물고, 뒤뜰에 정자도 하나 두고 말입니다. 누가 알겠어요? 저로선 별로 놀랄 일도 아니지요.\"\n\n\"자네는 아주 철학자로군, 샘.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그건 집안 내력인가 봅니다, 나리.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다. \"제 아버지도 지금 그쪽으로 아주 정통하시죠. 새어머니가 아버지한테 바가지를 긁으면, 아버지는 휘파람을 붑니다. 그러면 어머니는 발끈해서 아버지 파이프를 부러뜨리죠. 아버지는 밖으로 나가 다른 걸 하나 구해 옵니다. 그러면 어머니는 아주 큰 소리로 비명을 지르고 발작을 일으키죠. 아버지는 어머니가 다시 정신 차릴 때까지 아주 편안하게 담배를 피웁니다. 그게 철학이지요, 나리, 안 그렇습니까?\"\n\n\"적어도 철학을 아주 잘 대신하는 것이긴 하군.\" 픽윅 씨가 웃으며 대답했다. \"자네의 떠돌이 인생살이에서 그게 큰 도움이 되었겠어, 샘.\"\n\n\"도움이라뇨, 나리.\" 샘이 외쳤다. \"그렇게 말씀하셔도 좋습니다. 운송꾼한테서 도망친 다음, 짐마차꾼한테 가기 전에, 전 두 주 동안 가구 없는 셋방에 묵었습죠.\"\n\n\"가구 없는 셋방이라고?\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"네—워털루 다리의 마른 아치 밑이었지요. 잠자리로는 그만이었습니다요—온갖 관공서에서 걸어서 십 분 거리고요—다만 흠을 잡자면, 자리가 좀 지나치게 바람이 잘 통한다는 거랄까요. 거기서 별별 희한한 광경을 다 봤습니다.\"\n\n\"아, 그랬겠지.\" 픽윅 씨가 자못 흥미롭다는 기색으로 말했다.\n\n\"광경이라뇨, 나리.\" 웰러 씨가 다시 입을 열었다. \"나리의 자비로운 가슴을 꿰뚫고 반대편으로 빠져나갈 만한 광경들이었죠. 거기서 변변한 부랑자들은 볼 수 없습니다요. 믿으십시오, 그자들은 그보다 더 잘 알거든요. 자기 직업에서 출세하지 못한 젊은 거지들, 남자고 여자고 더러 거기다 잠자리를 틀기도 합죠. 하지만 대개는 그 외롭고 어두운 구석에 몸을 웅크리는 건 닳고 닳아 굶주리고 집 없는 가련한 것들입니다요—이 펜스짜리 밧줄 값도 못 대는 불쌍한 것들 말입니다.\"\n\n\"그런데 말이지, 샘, 이 펜스짜리 밧줄이라는 게 뭔가?\" 픽윅 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"이 펜스짜리 밧줄이라는 건요, 나리.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다. \"그냥 싸구려 여인숙입죠. 거기선 침대가 하룻밤에 이 펜스랍니다.\"\n\n\"왜 침대를 밧줄이라고 부르나?\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"순진하시기도 하셔라, 나리, 그게 아닙니다요.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"그 호텔을 운영하는 양반네 부부가 처음 장사를 시작했을 때는 침대를 바닥에 깔았습죠. 그런데 그게 도무지 수지가 안 맞았던 게, 손님들이 적당히 이 펜스어치 잠만 자는 게 아니라 한낮까지 거기 드러누워 있었거든요. 그래서 이제는 밧줄을 두 줄 매답니다요. 한 육 피트 간격으로, 바닥에서 삼 피트 높이에다 방을 가로질러 쭉 늘이는 거죠. 그리고 거친 자루천 조각을 그 위에 가로질러 펼쳐 침대를 만드는 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그래서?\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그래서요.\" 웰러 씨가 말했다. \"그 방법의 이점이 뻔하지요. 매일 아침 여섯 시면 밧줄의 한쪽 끝을 풀어 버립니다요. 그러면 손님들이 우르르 떨어지죠. 그 결과 완전히 잠이 깬 손님들은 아주 조용히 일어나 걸어 나가는 거죠!\"\n\n\"실례합니다, 나리.\" 샘이 그 수다스러운 이야기를 갑자기 멈추며 말했다. \"여기가 베리 세인트 에드먼즈입니까?\"\n\n\"그렇다네.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다.\n\n마차는 번창하고 깔끔한 모습의 아담하고 멋진 도시의 잘 포장된 거리를 덜컹덜컹 지나, 옛 수도원을 거의 마주 보는 넓고 탁 트인 거리에 자리한 큰 여관 앞에 멈췄다.\n\n\"그리고 이게.\" 픽윅 씨가 올려다보며 말했다. \"에인절 여관이로군! 여기서 내리세, 샘. 하지만 좀 조심해야 하네. 개인실을 하나 주문하고, 내 이름은 입에 올리지 말게. 알겠나.\"\n\n\"빈틈없이 하겠습니다, 나리.\" 웰러 씨가 알겠다는 듯 눈을 찡긋하며 대답했다. 그러고는 이턴스윌에서 마차에 합류할 때 급히 던져 넣었던 뒤쪽 짐칸에서 픽윅 씨의 여행 가방을 끌어낸 뒤, 웰러 씨는 심부름하러 사라졌다. 개인실은 곧 잡혔고, 픽윅 씨는 지체 없이 그리로 안내되었다.\n\n\"자, 샘.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"제일 먼저 해야 할 일은—\"\n\n\"저녁부터 시키는 겁니다, 나리.\" 웰러 씨가 끼어들었다. \"시간이 아주 늦었습니다요, 나리.\"\n\n\"아, 그렇군.\" 픽윅 씨가 시계를 보며 말했다. \"자네 말이 맞네, 샘.\"\n\n\"그리고 제가 한 말씀 올려도 된다면, 나리.\" 웰러 씨가 덧붙였다. \"그다음엔 푹 한잠 주무시고, 이 음흉한 자를 캐묻는 일은 아침까지 미루시는 게 좋겠습니다요. 잠만큼 기운 차리게 하는 건 없으니까요, 나리, 하녀 아이가 달걀잔으로 아편 한 잔을 들이켜기 전에 했던 말처럼 말입니다.\"\n\n\"자네 말이 맞는 것 같네, 샘.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"하지만 먼저 그자가 여기 묵고 있는지, 떠날 낌새는 없는지 확인해야겠어.\"\n\n\"그건 제게 맡기십시오, 나리.\" 샘이 말했다. \"아담한 저녁을 시켜 드리게 해 주시고, 그게 준비되는 동안 제가 아래층에서 알아보겠습니다요. 구두닦이의 가슴속에서 비밀이란 비밀은 죄다 오 분 안에 끄집어낼 수 있습죠, 나리.\"\n\n\"그렇게 하게.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. 그러자 웰러 씨는 즉시 물러갔다.\n\n삼십 분 뒤 픽윅 씨는 아주 흡족한 저녁상 앞에 앉아 있었고, 사십오 분 뒤 웰러 씨는 찰스 피츠마셜 씨가 추후 통지가 있을 때까지 자기 개인실을 비워 두지 말라고 일러 두었다는 소식을 가지고 돌아왔다. 그자는 근처의 어느 개인 저택에서 저녁을 보낼 예정이었고, 구두닦이에게 자기가 돌아올 때까지 자지 말고 기다리라고 일러 두었으며, 하인을 데리고 갔다고 했다.\n\n\"자, 나리.\" 웰러 씨가 보고를 마치고 나서 주장했다. \"제가 아침에 이 하인 녀석하고 얘기를 나눌 수 있다면, 그자가 제 주인의 일이란 일은 죄다 털어놓을 겁니다요.\"\n\n\"그걸 어찌 아나?\" 픽윅 씨가 끼어들었다.\n\n\"세상에, 나리, 하인들은 늘 그러거든요.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"오, 아, 그걸 깜빡했군.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"좋아.\"\n\n\"그러면 나리께서 어떻게 하는 게 최선인지 정하시고, 저희는 거기에 맞춰 움직이면 됩니다요.\"\n\n이것이 세울 수 있는 최선의 방안인 듯했으므로, 결국 그렇게 합의되었다. 웰러 씨는 주인의 허락을 받아 자기 식대로 저녁을 보내러 물러갔고, 곧이어 모여든 일행의 만장일치로 술집 좌장 자리에 추대되었다. 그 영예로운 자리에서 그가 어찌나 단골 신사들을 흡족하게 했던지, 그들의 웃음소리와 갈채가 픽윅 씨의 침실까지 뚫고 들어와 그의 자연스러운 휴식 시간을 적어도 세 시간은 단축시켰다.\n\n이튿날 아침 일찍, 웰러 씨는 반 페니짜리 물세례의 힘을 빌려(마구간 일을 보는 어린 친구에게 그 동전을 주고는, 완전히 정신이 들 때까지 머리와 얼굴에 물을 퍼붓게 했다) 전날 저녁 흥청거림이 남긴 열기를 떨쳐 내고 있었다. 그러던 중 그는 마당 벤치에 앉아 찬송가 책으로 보이는 것을 깊은 상념에 잠긴 듯 읽고 있는, 오디색 제복을 입은 한 젊은 친구의 모습에 눈길이 끌렸다. 그 친구는 그래도 가끔씩 펌프 아래 있는 사람에게 슬쩍슬쩍 눈길을 던졌는데, 마치 그 사람의 행동에 어떤 관심이라도 있는 듯했다.\n\n'너 참 희한하게 생겨 먹었구나, 너 말이야!' 웰러 씨는 오디색 옷을 입은 낯선 자의 눈길과 처음 마주쳤을 때 그렇게 생각했다. 그자는 누렇고 큼지막한 못생긴 얼굴에 움푹 꺼진 눈, 그리고 숱 없는 검은 머리가 늘어진 거대한 머리통을 하고 있었다. '너 참 희한한 놈이로구나!' 웰러 씨는 그렇게 생각하고는, 그렇게 생각하며 계속 몸을 씻었고, 더는 그자에 대해 생각하지 않았다.\n\n그래도 그 사내는 마치 대화를 시작하고 싶기라도 한 듯 찬송가 책에서 샘에게로, 샘에게서 다시 찬송가 책으로 자꾸 눈길을 옮겼다. 그래서 마침내 샘은 기회를 주자는 뜻으로 친근하게 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다.\n\n\"안녕하시오, 양반?\"\n\n\"덕분에 꽤 잘 지낸다고 말씀드릴 수 있어 기쁩니다, 선생.\" 그 사내가 아주 신중하게 말하며 책을 덮었다. \"선생도 마찬가지이시길 바랍니다.\"\n\n\"글쎄, 내가 걸어 다니는 브랜디 병 같은 기분만 좀 덜했어도, 오늘 아침에 이렇게 비틀거리진 않았을 텐데.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"이 집에 묵고 계시오, 양반?\"\n\n오디색 옷의 사내는 그렇다고 대답했다.\n\n\"어쩌다 어젯밤엔 우리 자리에 안 끼셨소?\" 샘이 수건으로 얼굴을 문지르며 물었다. \"흥겨운 부류로 보이는데 말이오—석회 바구니에 든 살아 있는 송어만큼이나 잔치를 즐길 상이구먼.\" 웰러 씨가 나직이 덧붙였다.\n\n\"어젯밤엔 주인 나리와 함께 나가 있었습니다.\" 낯선 자가 대답했다.\n\n\"그분 이름이 뭐요?\" 웰러 씨가 갑작스러운 흥분과 수건 마찰이 겹쳐 얼굴이 시뻘게지며 물었다.\n\n\"피츠마셜입니다.\" 오디색 옷의 사내가 말했다.\n\n\"악수합시다.\" 웰러 씨가 다가서며 말했다. \"당신과 좀 알고 지내고 싶구려. 당신 인상이 마음에 드는걸, 이 친구.\"\n\n\"그거 참 묘하군요.\" 오디색 옷의 사내가 아주 순박한 태도로 말했다. \"저도 당신 인상이 어찌나 마음에 들던지, 펌프 아래 있는 당신을 처음 본 순간부터 말을 걸고 싶었거든요.\"\n\n\"정말이오?\"\n\n\"정말입니다. 자, 이상하지 않습니까?\"\n\n\"아주 희한하구먼.\" 샘은 그 낯선 자의 무름을 속으로 흐뭇해하며 말했다. \"이름이 뭐요, 영감님?\"\n\n\"잡이오.\"\n\n\"아주 좋은 이름이구먼. 별명이 붙지 않은 이름은 그것밖에 모르겠소. 다른 이름은 뭐요?\"\n\n\"트로터요.\" 낯선 자가 말했다. \"당신은 뭐요?\"\n\n샘은 주인의 당부를 명심하고 대답했다.\n\n\"내 이름은 워커요. 우리 주인 이름은 윌킨스고. 오늘 아침에 뭐 한잔 드시겠소, 트로터 씨?\"\n\n트로터 씨는 그 반가운 제안에 응했고, 책을 외투 주머니에 넣은 뒤 웰러 씨를 따라 술집으로 갔다. 거기서 두 사람은 곧 백랍 그릇에다 일정량의 영국산 진과 향긋한 정향 에센스를 섞어 만든, 기운 돋우는 혼합주를 음미하는 일에 빠져들었다.\n\n\"그런데 일자리는 어떻소?\" 샘이 동료의 잔을 두 번째로 채우며 물었다.\n\n\"형편없죠.\" 잡이 입맛을 다시며 말했다. \"아주 형편없습니다.\"\n\n\"설마 그럴 리가?\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n\"정말입니다. 게다가 그보다 더한 게, 우리 주인 나리가 결혼하려 한다는 겁니다.\"\n\n\"설마.\"\n\n\"정말이오. 그리고 그보다 더한 게, 어마어마하게 부유한 상속녀를, 기숙학교에서 빼내 도망치려 한다는 거죠.\"\n\n\"그런 못된 놈이!\" 샘이 동료의 잔을 다시 채우며 말했다. \"이 도시 어딘가에 있는 기숙학교겠구먼, 안 그렇소?\" 그런데 이 질문은 상상할 수 있는 한 가장 무심한 어조로 던져졌건만, 잡 트로터 씨는 새 친구가 거기에 대한 답을 끌어내고 싶어 안달하는 것을 알아챘다는 것을 몸짓으로 분명히 드러냈다. 그는 잔을 비우고, 의미심장하게 동료를 바라보았으며, 작은 두 눈을 하나씩 차례로 찡긋한 뒤, 마침내 팔을 움직여 마치 보이지 않는 펌프 손잡이를 작동시키는 듯한 동작을 했다. 그럼으로써 자기(트로터 씨)가 새뮤얼 웰러 씨에게 펌프질당하는, 즉 정보를 캐내어지는 과정을 겪고 있다고 여긴다는 것을 넌지시 알린 것이었다.\n\n\"아니, 아니죠.\" 트로터 씨가 결론지어 말했다. \"그건 아무한테나 말할 게 못 됩니다. 그건 비밀이거든요—굉장한 비밀이죠, 워커 씨.\" 오디색 옷의 사내는 이렇게 말하며, 동료에게 이제 갈증을 풀 만한 것이 남아 있지 않다는 것을 일깨워 주려는 듯 잔을 거꾸로 뒤집었다. 샘은 그 암시를 알아채고, 그것이 전달된 섬세한 방식을 헤아려, 백랍 그릇을 다시 채우라고 시켰다. 그러자 오디색 옷의 사내의 작은 눈이 반짝였다.\n\n\"그래서 그게 비밀이라고?\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n\"그렇다고 봐야겠죠.\" 오디색 옷의 사내가 흡족한 얼굴로 술을 홀짝이며 말했다.\n\n\"당신 주인이 아주 부자인가 보군?\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n트로터 씨는 미소를 지으며, 왼손에 잔을 든 채 오른손으로 그 오디색 형용할 수 없는 옷의 주머니를 또렷이 네 번 두드렸다. 마치 자기 주인도 그렇게 했더라면 동전 짤랑거리는 소리로 누구를 크게 놀라게 하는 일 없이 그럴 수 있었을 거라고 넌지시 알리려는 듯이.\n\n\"아하.\" 샘이 말했다. \"그게 수작이라 이거지?\"\n\n오디색 옷의 사내가 의미심장하게 고개를 끄덕였다.\n\n\"그래, 이 친구야, 당신은 이런 생각 안 드시오.\" 웰러 씨가 따지듯 말했다. \"당신 주인이 이 아가씨를 채 가게 내버려 둔다면, 당신도 아주 못된 악당이라는 생각 말이오?\"\n\n\"저도 압니다.\" 잡 트로터가 동료에게로 깊이 뉘우치는 얼굴을 돌리고 가볍게 신음하며 말했다. \"저도 압니다. 그게 바로 제 마음을 갉아먹는 거죠. 하지만 제가 뭘 어쩌겠습니까?\"\n\n\"어쩌긴!\" 샘이 말했다. \"마님한테 까발리고, 당신 주인을 버리는 거지.\"\n\n\"누가 제 말을 믿겠습니까?\" 잡 트로터가 대답했다. \"그 아가씨는 순결과 신중함의 표본으로 통하거든요. 아가씨는 부인할 테고, 우리 주인도 부인할 겁니다. 누가 제 말을 믿겠습니까? 전 일자리를 잃을 테고, 음모죄인지 뭔지로 기소되겠죠. 제가 나서서 얻을 거라곤 그게 다입니다.\"\n\n\"거기엔 일리가 있군.\" 샘이 곰곰이 생각하며 말했다. \"거기엔 일리가 있어.\"\n\n\"이 일을 떠맡아 줄 점잖은 신사분을 한 분이라도 안다면.\" 트로터 씨가 말을 이었다. \"그 도망 행각을 막을 가망이 좀 있을지도 모르죠. 하지만 똑같은 어려움이 있단 말입니다, 워커 씨, 꼭 그대로요. 이 낯선 곳에 아는 신사가 한 분도 없으니까요. 설령 안다 한들 그분이 제 이야기를 믿어 줄지는 십중팔구 모를 일이고요.\"\n\n\"이리 와 보시오.\" 샘이 갑자기 벌떡 일어나 오디색 옷의 사내의 팔을 붙잡으며 말했다. \"우리 주인 나리가 바로 당신이 찾는 분이오, 알겠소.\" 그리고 잡 트로터가 약간 저항했으나, 샘은 새로 사귄 친구를 픽윅 씨의 방으로 데려가, 방금 우리가 옮긴 대화의 짤막한 요약과 함께 그를 픽윅 씨에게 소개했다.\n\n\"주인을 배신하게 되어 정말 가슴이 아픕니다, 나리.\" 잡 트로터가 가로세로 육 인치쯤 되는 분홍 체크무늬 손수건을 눈에 갖다 대며 말했다.\n\n\"그 마음씨는 자네에게 큰 명예가 되는 일이네.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다. \"하지만 그래도 그건 자네의 의무야.\"\n\n\"제 의무라는 걸 압니다, 나리.\" 잡이 몹시 격앙되어 대답했다. \"우리는 모두 자기 의무를 다하려고 애써야지요, 나리. 저도 미천하나마 제 의무를 다하려고 애쓰고 있습니다, 나리. 하지만 주인을 배신한다는 건 괴로운 시련입니다, 나리. 그 옷을 입고 그 밥을 얻어먹은 주인을 말입니다. 설령 그가 악당이라 하더라도 말이지요, 나리.\"\n\n\"자네는 아주 착한 친구로군.\" 픽윅 씨가 크게 감동하여 말했다. \"정직한 친구야.\"\n\n\"자, 자.\" 샘이 끼어들었는데, 그는 트로터 씨의 눈물을 자못 못마땅하게 지켜보던 참이었다. \"이 물뿌리개 짓 좀 집어치우쇼. 이런 거 아무 소용 없으니까.\"\n\n\"샘.\" 픽윅 씨가 책망하듯 말했다. \"자네가 이 젊은이의 감정을 그렇게 존중하지 않는다니 유감일세.\"\n\n\"이 친구 감정이야 다 좋습죠, 나리.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다. \"그리고 그게 그렇게 아주 곱고, 잃어버리면 아까운 거라면, 제 생각엔 그걸 뜨거운 물에 증발시키느니 자기 가슴속에 간직하는 게 나을 것 같습니다요. 아무 소용도 없으니 더욱이요. 눈물이 시계를 감아 본 적도, 증기 기관을 돌려 본 적도 없거든요. 다음번에 흡연 파티에 나가거든, 이 친구야, 그 생각을 파이프에 채워 넣으쇼. 그리고 지금은 그 분홍 깅엄 조각이나 주머니에 넣어 두시고. 그게 줄타기 곡예사라도 되는 양 흔들어 댈 만큼 그리 잘난 물건도 아니니까.\"\n\n\"내 하인 말이 옳네.\" 픽윅 씨가 잡에게 말을 걸며 말했다. \"비록 의견을 표현하는 방식이 다소 투박하고, 가끔은 알아들을 수 없긴 하지만 말이야.\"\n\n\"옳습니다, 나리, 아주 옳은 말씀입니다.\" 트로터 씨가 말했다. \"더는 무너지지 않겠습니다.\"\n\n\"좋아.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"자, 그 기숙학교는 어디인가?\"\n\n\"도시 바로 외곽에 있는, 크고 오래된 붉은 벽돌집입니다, 나리.\" 잡 트로터가 대답했다.\n\n\"그러면 언제.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"이 흉악한 계획이 실행에 옮겨지는가—이 도주극은 언제 벌어지는가?\"\n\n\"오늘 밤입니다, 나리.\" 잡이 대답했다.\n\n\"오늘 밤!\" 픽윅 씨가 외쳤다.\n\n\"바로 오늘 밤입니다, 나리.\" 잡 트로터가 대답했다. \"그래서 제가 이토록 마음을 졸이는 겁니다.\"\n\n\"당장 조치를 취해야겠군.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"그 시설을 운영하는 부인을 즉시 만나야겠어.\"\n\n\"죄송합니다만, 나리.\" 잡이 말했다. \"그런 식으로 하시면 절대 안 됩니다.\"\n\n\"왜 안 되나?\" 픽윅 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"우리 주인은, 나리, 아주 교활한 자거든요.\"\n\n\"교활한 줄은 나도 알지.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그리고 그자가 그 노부인의 마음을 어찌나 단단히 휘감아 놓았는지요, 나리.\" 잡이 말을 이었다. \"나리께서 맨무릎을 꿇고 맹세하신다 해도, 그자에게 불리한 말은 부인이 곧이듣지 않을 겁니다. 더구나 나리께는 하인의 말 말고는 증거가 없으니까요. 부인이 알기로는(그리고 우리 주인이 틀림없이 그렇게 말할 테니), 그 하인은 무슨 잘못으로 쫓겨나서 앙심을 품고 이런 짓을 하는 자일 수도 있는 거죠.\"\n\n\"그럼 어떻게 하는 게 낫겠나?\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그자가 도주하는 바로 그 현장을 잡는 것 말고는, 그 노부인을 납득시킬 길이 없습니다, 나리.\" 잡이 대답했다.\n\n\"그런 늙은 고양이들은 죄다 이정표에다 머리를 들이박고 마는 법이죠.\" 웰러 씨가 곁들여 한마디 했다.\n\n\"하지만 도주의 바로 그 현장에서 그자를 잡는다는 건, 해내기가 무척 어려운 일일 것 같은데, 걱정이군.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"잘 모르겠습니다, 나리.\" 트로터 씨가 잠시 생각한 뒤에 말했다. \"제 생각엔 아주 쉽게 해낼 수 있을 것 같습니다.\"\n\n\"어떻게?\" 하는 것이 픽윅 씨의 물음이었다.\n\n\"그게요.\" 트로터 씨가 대답했다. \"우리 주인과 저는, 두 하인의 신임을 얻고 있으니, 열 시에 부엌에 숨어 있을 겁니다. 식구들이 잠자리에 들면, 저희는 부엌에서 나오고, 아가씨는 침실에서 나오는 거죠. 역마차가 대기하고 있을 테고, 그러면 떠나는 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그래서?\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그래서, 나리, 제가 생각해 봤는데요, 만일 나리께서 뒤뜰에서 혼자 기다리고 계신다면—\"\n\n\"혼자라.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"왜 혼자지?\"\n\n\"제 생각엔 그게 아주 자연스러울 것 같아서요.\" 잡이 대답했다. \"노부인으로서는 이런 불쾌한 일이 굳이 더 많은 사람 앞에서 들통나는 걸 바라지 않을 테니까요. 아가씨도요, 나리—그 심정을 헤아려 주십시오.\"\n\n\"자네 말이 아주 옳네.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"그 배려가 자네의 섬세한 감정을 잘 보여 주는군. 계속하게. 자네 말이 아주 옳아.\"\n\n\"그래서요, 나리, 제가 생각해 봤는데, 만일 나리께서 뒤뜰에서 혼자 기다리고 계시면, 제가 복도 끝에서 그 뜰로 통하는 문을 통해, 정확히 열한 시 반에 나리를 들여보내 드리겠습니다. 그러면 나리께서 바로 그 순간에, 불운하게도 제가 옭매인 이 악한의 계략을 좌절시키는 일을 도와주실 수 있을 겁니다.\" 여기서 트로터 씨는 깊이 한숨을 쉬었다.\n\n\"그 일로 자네가 괴로워할 건 없네.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"그자가 비록 자네의 처지는 미천하나 자네에게서 두드러지는 그 섬세한 감정을 단 한 톨이라도 지녔다면, 나는 그자에게도 얼마간 희망을 걸어 보겠네만.\"\n\n잡 트로터는 깊이 허리를 숙였다. 그리고 웰러 씨가 앞서 한 핀잔에도 불구하고, 그의 눈에는 또다시 눈물이 차올랐다.\n\n\"이런 친구는 처음 보는군.\" 샘이 말했다. \"이 친구 머릿속엔 늘 틀어 놓은 수도관이 박혀 있는 게 틀림없어, 내 장담하지.\"\n\n\"샘.\" 픽윅 씨가 자못 엄하게 말했다. \"입 다물게.\"\n\n\"잘 알겠습니다, 나리.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"이 계획이 마음에 들지 않는군.\" 픽윅 씨가 깊이 숙고한 뒤에 말했다. \"왜 내가 그 아가씨의 친지들과 연락을 취할 수는 없는 건가?\"\n\n\"그분들은 여기서 백 마일이나 떨어진 곳에 살거든요, 나리.\" 잡 트로터가 응답했다.\n\n\"그게 결정타로군.\" 웰러 씨가 옆에서 한마디 했다.\n\n\"그럼 그 뜰 말인데.\" 픽윅 씨가 다시 말했다. \"거기엔 어떻게 들어가나?\"\n\n\"담이 아주 낮습니다, 나리. 그리고 나리의 하인이 받쳐 올려 드리면 됩니다.\"\n\n\"내 하인이 나를 받쳐 올린다.\" 픽윅 씨가 기계적으로 되뇌었다. \"자네가 말한 그 문 가까이에 틀림없이 있을 거지?\"\n\n\"틀림없이 알아보실 겁니다, 나리. 뜰로 통하는 문은 그것 하나뿐이거든요. 시계 치는 소리가 들리거든 거기를 두드리십시오. 그러면 제가 즉시 열어 드리겠습니다.\"\n\n\"이 계획은 마음에 들지 않네.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"하지만 달리 방도가 보이지 않고, 또 이 아가씨의 평생의 행복이 걸려 있으니, 받아들이겠네. 틀림없이 그곳에 가 있겠어.\"\n\n이리하여, 두 번째로, 픽윅 씨의 타고난 선량한 마음이 그를, 그가 가장 기꺼이 멀찍이 물러서 있었을 일에 휘말리게 만들었다.\n\n\"그 집 이름이 뭔가?\" 픽윅 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"웨스트게이트 하우스입니다, 나리. 도시 끝에 이르거든 오른쪽으로 조금 도십시오. 큰길에서 조금 떨어진 곳에 외따로 서 있고, 대문에 놋쇠 명패로 이름이 붙어 있습니다.\"\n\n\"알겠네.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"전에 이 도시에 왔을 때 한번 본 적이 있어. 날 믿어도 좋네.\"\n\n트로터 씨가 또 한 번 절을 하고 떠나려고 돌아서자, 픽윅 씨가 그의 손에 기니 한 닢을 쥐여 주었다.\n\n\"자네는 훌륭한 친구야.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"자네의 착한 마음씨가 감탄스럽군. 사례는 됐네. 잊지 말게—열한 시야.\"\n\n\"잊을 염려는 없습니다, 나리.\" 잡 트로터가 대답했다. 이 말과 함께 그는 방을 나섰고, 샘이 그 뒤를 따랐다.\n\n\"이봐.\" 샘이 말했다. \"그 우는 거 말이오, 제법이던데. 나도 그런 좋은 조건이면 소나기 속 빗물받이 홈통처럼 울어 보겠구먼. 어떻게 하는 거요?\"\n\n\"그건 가슴에서 우러나오는 겁니다, 워커 씨.\" 잡이 엄숙하게 대답했다. \"그럼 안녕히 계십시오, 선생.\"\n\n'당신 참 물러 빠진 양반이구먼. 어쨌든 당신한테서 죄다 캐냈으니 됐어.' 잡이 걸어가는 동안 웰러 씨는 그렇게 생각했다.\n\n트로터 씨의 머릿속을 스쳐 간 생각이 정확히 어떤 성질의 것이었는지는 우리도 말할 수 없으니, 그게 무엇이었는지 모르기 때문이다.\n\n하루가 저물고 저녁이 찾아왔다. 그리고 열 시가 조금 못 되어 샘 웰러가, 징글 씨와 잡이 함께 나갔으며, 그들의 짐이 꾸려져 있고, 그들이 역마차를 한 대 주문했다고 보고했다. 트로터 씨가 예고했던 대로, 음모가 분명 실행에 옮겨지고 있었다.\n\n열 시 반이 되었고, 픽윅 씨가 그 미묘한 심부름을 위해 나설 시간이 되었다. 담을 넘을 때 거추장스러운 것이 없도록, 샘이 외투를 내미는 것을 마다하고, 그는 길을 나섰다. 시종이 그 뒤를 따랐다.\n\n환한 달이 떠 있었지만, 구름 뒤에 가려져 있었다. 비가 오지 않는 좋은 밤이었지만, 유난히 캄캄했다. 길도, 산울타리도, 들판도, 집도, 나무도 한 겹 짙은 어둠에 휩싸여 있었다. 대기는 후텁지근하고 무더웠으며, 여름 번개가 지평선 끝에서 희미하게 떨렸는데, 그것이 모든 것을 감싼 그 침침한 어둠에 변화를 주는 유일한 광경이었다. 소리라곤 없었다. 멀리서 들려오는 어느 안절부절못하는 집 개의 짖는 소리 말고는.\n\n그들은 그 집을 찾아냈고, 놋쇠 명패를 읽었으며, 담을 빙 돌아, 뜰 안쪽과 그들을 갈라놓는 담의 한 지점에 멈춰 섰다.\n\n\"내가 넘어가는 것을 도와준 다음에는 여관으로 돌아가게, 샘.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"잘 알겠습니다, 나리.\"\n\n\"그리고 내가 돌아올 때까지 자지 말고 기다리게.\"\n\n\"여부가 있겠습니까, 나리.\"\n\n\"내 다리를 붙잡게. 그리고 내가 '넘어간다'고 하면, 살살 들어 올리게.\"\n\n\"알겠습니다, 나리.\"\n\n이 사전 절차를 마무리하고, 픽윅 씨는 담 꼭대기를 붙잡고 \"넘어간다\"는 말을 내렸다. 그 말은 글자 그대로 이행되었다. 그의 몸이 그의 정신이 지닌 탄력성을 어느 정도 닮았던 것인지, 아니면 살살 밀어 올린다는 웰러 씨의 관념이 픽윅 씨의 것보다 다소 거칠었던 것인지는 모르겠으나, 그 도움의 즉각적인 효과는 그 불멸의 신사를 담 너머로 완전히 휙 넘겨, 아래쪽 화단에 떨어뜨린 것이었다. 거기서 그는 구스베리 덤불 셋과 장미나무 한 그루를 으스러뜨린 뒤, 마침내 큰대자로 착지했다.\n\n\"다치진 않으셨겠지요, 나리?\" 샘이, 주인이 신비롭게 사라진 데서 온 놀라움에서 정신을 차리자마자, 큰 소리로 속삭이며 말했다.\n\n\"나는 분명 안 다쳤네, 샘.\" 픽윅 씨가 담 저편에서 대답했다. \"하지만 아무래도 자네가 나를 다치게 한 것 같군.\"\n\n\"안 그러셨길 바랍니다, 나리.\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n\"괜찮네.\" 픽윅 씨가 일어서며 말했다. \"긁힌 데 몇 군데뿐이야. 어서 가게, 안 그러면 우리 소리가 들킬 테니.\"\n\n\"안녕히 계십시오, 나리.\"\n\n\"잘 가게.\"\n\n샘 웰러는 살금살금 걸음으로 떠나며, 픽윅 씨를 뜰에 홀로 남겨 두었다.\n\n집 안의 여러 창문에 이따금 불빛이 나타나거나 계단에서 새어 나왔는데, 마치 식구들이 잠자리에 드는 듯했다. 정해진 시각이 되기까지는 문에 너무 가까이 가지 않으려고, 픽윅 씨는 담 한 모퉁이에 몸을 웅크리고는 그 시각이 오기를 기다렸다.\n\n그것은 능히 많은 사람의 기를 꺾어 놓을 만한 처지였다. 그러나 픽윅 씨는 의기소침함도 불안함도 느끼지 않았다. 그는 자기 목적이 근본적으로 선한 것임을 알았고, 고매한 잡을 전적으로 믿었다. 따분한 건 분명했다. 음울하다고까지는 않더라도. 하지만 사색을 즐기는 사람은 언제든 명상으로 시간을 보낼 수 있는 법이다. 픽윅 씨가 명상에 잠겨 어느새 꾸벅꾸벅 졸고 있을 때, 이웃 교회의 종소리가 시각을 알리며 울려 퍼져 그를 깨웠다—열한 시 반이었다.\n\n'시간이 됐군.' 픽윅 씨는 그렇게 생각하며 조심스레 일어섰다. 그는 집을 올려다보았다. 불빛은 사라졌고 덧문은 닫혀 있었다—다들 잠자리에 든 게 틀림없었다. 그는 발끝으로 문까지 걸어가 살며시 두드렸다. 이삼 분이 지나도록 아무 대답이 없자, 그는 좀 더 크게 한 번 더 두드렸고, 그다음엔 그보다 좀 더 크게 또 두드렸다.\n\n마침내 계단 위에서 발소리가 들리고, 이어 촛불 빛이 문의 열쇠 구멍을 통해 비쳤다. 한참 동안 사슬을 풀고 빗장을 빼는 소리가 나더니, 문이 천천히 열렸다.\n\n그런데 문은 바깥쪽으로 열렸다. 그리고 문이 점점 더 넓게 열림에 따라, 픽윅 씨는 점점 더 그 뒤로 물러섰다. 조심하느라 살짝 내다보았을 때, 문을 연 사람이—잡 트로터가 아니라, 손에 촛불을 든 하녀였음을 보았으니, 그의 놀라움이 어떠했겠는가! 픽윅 씨는, 저 훌륭한 멜로드라마 배우 펀치가 양철 음악 상자를 든 머리 납작한 광대를 노리며 매복할 때 보이는 그 날쌤으로, 다시 머리를 쑥 집어넣었다.\n\n\"고양이였나 봐, 세라.\" 하녀가 집 안의 누군가에게 말을 걸며 말했다. \"야옹아, 야옹아, 야옹아—나비야, 나비야, 나비야.\"\n\n하지만 이런 어르는 소리에 꾀여 오는 짐승이 한 마리도 없자, 하녀는 천천히 문을 닫고 다시 걸어 잠갔다. 픽윅 씨는 담에 바짝 붙어 곧추선 채 남겨졌다.\n\n'이거 참 묘하군.' 픽윅 씨는 생각했다. '평소 시간보다 늦게까지 안 자고 있나 보군. 하필 다른 날 다 두고 오늘 밤을 그런 일에 골랐다니, 지극히 불운한 일이야—지극히 말이지.' 그리고 이런 생각을 하며, 픽윅 씨는 조심스레, 전에 몸을 숨겼던 담 모퉁이로 물러났다. 다시 신호를 보내도 안전하리라 여겨질 때까지 기다리려는 것이었다.\n\n거기 있은 지 채 오 분도 안 되어, 강렬한 번갯불이 번쩍이더니 요란한 천둥이 뒤따라, 무시무시한 소리를 내며 콰르릉 멀리 굴러갔다. 그러더니 또 한 번, 먼젓번보다 더 밝은 번갯불이 번쩍이고, 처음 것보다 더 큰 두 번째 천둥이 울렸다. 그러더니 모든 것을 휩쓸어 버리는 듯한 기세와 맹렬함으로 비가 쏟아져 내렸다.\n\n픽윅 씨는 뇌우 속에서 나무가 무척 위험한 이웃이라는 것을 익히 알고 있었다. 그의 오른쪽에 나무가 한 그루, 왼쪽에 한 그루, 앞에 세 번째, 뒤에 네 번째가 있었다. 있던 자리에 그대로 있으면 사고의 희생자가 될지도 모르고, 뜰 한복판에 모습을 드러내면 순경 손에 넘겨질지도 몰랐다. 그는 한두 번 담을 넘으려 해 보았지만, 이번엔 자연이 그에게 준 다리 말고는 다른 다리가 없는지라, 그 안간힘의 유일한 효과라곤 무릎과 정강이에 무척 불쾌한 갖가지 생채기를 내고, 자신을 비 오듯 땀에 젖은 상태로 만든 것뿐이었다.\n\n\"끔찍한 처지로군.\" 픽윅 씨가 이 운동을 한 뒤에 이마를 닦으려고 멈춰 서서 말했다. 그는 집을 올려다보았다—온통 캄캄했다. 이제는 다들 잠자리에 들었을 것이다. 다시 신호를 해 보아야겠다.\n\n그는 축축한 자갈 위를 발끝으로 걸어가 문을 두드렸다. 그는 숨을 죽이고 열쇠 구멍에 귀를 기울였다. 대답이 없었다. 무척 이상했다. 또 한 번 두드렸다. 그는 다시 귀를 기울였다. 안에서 낮게 속삭이는 소리가 나더니, 이윽고 한 목소리가 외쳤다.\n\n\"누구세요?\"\n\n'잡이 아니군.' 픽윅 씨는 다시 황급히 담에 바짝 붙어 곧추서며 생각했다. '여자야.'\n\n그가 이 결론을 채 내릴 새도 없이, 위층 창문 하나가 벌컥 열리더니, 서너 명의 여자 목소리가 그 물음을 되풀이했다—\"누구세요?\"\n\n픽윅 씨는 손도 발도 감히 움직이지 못했다. 시설 전체가 깨어났다는 것이 분명했다. 그는 소동이 가라앉을 때까지 있던 자리에 그대로 있다가, 그런 다음 초인적인 노력으로 담을 넘거나 그러지 못하면 그 시도 중에 죽기로 마음을 굳혔다.\n\n픽윅 씨의 모든 결심이 그렇듯, 이것도 그 상황에서 세울 수 있는 최선의 결심이었다. 그러나 불행히도 그것은 그들이 감히 문을 다시 열지는 않으리라는 가정에 근거를 둔 것이었다. 사슬과 빗장이 풀리는 소리를 듣고, 문이 점점 더 넓게 천천히 열리는 것을 보았을 때, 그의 낭패감이 어떠했겠는가! 그는 한 걸음 한 걸음 모퉁이로 물러섰지만, 아무리 애를 써도 자기 몸이 가로막은 탓에 문이 최대한 넓게 열리지는 못했다.\n\n\"누구세요?\" 안쪽 계단에서 여럿이 합창하듯 새된 목소리가 비명을 질렀다. 그 합창단은 그 시설의 노처녀 원장과 교사 셋, 하녀 다섯, 그리고 기숙생 서른 명으로 이루어져 있었는데, 모두 옷을 반쯤 걸친 채 컬페이퍼를 산더미처럼 말고 있었다.\n\n물론 픽윅 씨는 누구라고 말하지 않았다. 그러자 합창의 후렴구가 바뀌었다—\"어머나! 무서워 죽겠어.\"\n\n\"요리사.\" 맨 윗계단에, 그러니까 무리의 맨 끄트머리에 서 있도록 단단히 신경 쓴 원장이 말했다. \"요리사, 자네가 뜰로 조금만 들어가 보지 그래?\"\n\n\"제발요, 마님, 싫습니다.\" 요리사가 응답했다.\n\n\"어머, 저 요리사 정말 멍청하기도 해라!\" 기숙생 서른 명이 말했다.\n\n\"요리사.\" 원장이 자못 위엄 있게 말했다. \"내게 말대꾸하지 말게나. 당장 뜰을 들여다보라고 명하네.\"\n\n이에 요리사가 울기 시작하자, 하녀가 \"너무하셔!\"라고 했고, 그렇게 편을 든 죄로 그녀는 그 자리에서 한 달 후 해고 통보를 받았다.\n\n\"안 들리나, 요리사?\" 원장이 짜증스레 발을 구르며 말했다.\n\n\"마님 말씀이 안 들려, 요리사?\" 교사 셋이 말했다.\n\n\"저 요리사 정말 뻔뻔하기도 해라!\" 기숙생 서른 명이 말했다.\n\n이렇게 강하게 떠밀린 불운한 요리사는 한두 걸음 앞으로 나아가, 촛불을 하필 자기가 아무것도 못 보게 가리는 자리에 들고서, 거기엔 아무것도 없으며 바람이었던 게 틀림없다고 단언했다. 그래서 문을 막 닫으려던 참인데, 경첩 틈으로 엿보고 있던 호기심 많은 기숙생 하나가 무시무시한 비명을 질러 댔고, 그 소리에 요리사와 하녀, 그리고 더 대담한 이들 모두가 순식간에 도로 불려 왔다.\n\n\"스미더스 양이 왜 저러나?\" 원장이 말했다. 앞서 말한 스미더스 양이 젊은 아가씨 넷 분량의 히스테리 발작을 일으키기 시작했던 것이다.\n\n\"어머, 스미더스 양, 얘.\" 나머지 기숙생 스물아홉 명이 말했다.\n\n\"아, 남자—남자가—문 뒤에!\" 스미더스 양이 비명을 질렀다.\n\n원장은 이 소름 끼치는 외침을 듣자마자 자기 침실로 물러나, 문을 이중으로 잠그고는 편안하게 기절해 버렸다. 기숙생들과 교사들과 하인들은 계단으로, 그리고 서로의 몸 위로 뒷걸음질 쳐 나동그라졌다. 그토록 비명을 지르고 기절하고 발버둥 치는 광경은 일찍이 본 적이 없었다. 그 소동의 한복판에서 픽윅 씨가 숨어 있던 곳에서 모습을 드러내, 그들 가운데로 나섰다.\n\n\"숙녀 여러분—사랑스러운 숙녀 여러분.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"어머, 우릴 사랑스럽대.\" 가장 나이 많고 가장 못생긴 교사가 소리쳤다. \"어머, 저 흉악한 작자!\"\n\n\"숙녀 여러분.\" 자기 처지의 위태로움에 절박해진 픽윅 씨가 고함쳤다. \"내 말을 들어 보시오. 나는 강도가 아니오. 나는 이 댁의 안주인을 만나러 왔소.\"\n\n\"어머, 정말 흉포한 괴물 같으니!\" 다른 교사가 비명을 질렀다. \"톰킨스 양을 노리는 거야.\"\n\n이에 일제히 비명이 터져 나왔다.\n\n\"누가 경보 종을 울려요!\" 열 몇 개의 목소리가 외쳤다.\n\n\"안 돼요—안 됩니다.\" 픽윅 씨가 소리쳤다. \"나를 보시오. 내가 강도처럼 생겼소! 사랑스러운 숙녀 여러분—원하신다면 내 손발을 묶으셔도, 벽장에 가두셔도 좋소. 다만 내가 할 말만 들어 주시오—내 말만 들어 주시오.\"\n\n\"우리 뜰엔 어떻게 들어오셨어요?\" 하녀가 더듬거리며 말했다.\n\n\"이 댁 안주인을 불러 주시오. 그러면 그분께 모든 걸 말씀드리겠소.\" 픽윅 씨가 폐활량을 최대한 끌어올려 말했다. \"그분을 불러 주시오—다만 조용히 하시고, 그분을 불러 주시면, 모든 걸 듣게 되실 거요.\"\n\n픽윅 씨의 외모 때문이었는지, 그의 태도 때문이었는지, 아니면 지금 신비에 싸인 무언가를 듣게 된다는—여성의 마음으로서는 거부할 수 없는—유혹 때문이었는지는 모르겠으나, 그 시설에서 그나마 분별 있는 이들(네댓 명쯤)이 비교적 잠잠한 상태로 진정되었다. 이들은 픽윅 씨의 진실성을 시험하는 방편으로, 그가 즉시 신체의 구속에 따를 것을 제안했다. 그리고 그 신사가, 통학생들이 보닛과 샌드위치 가방을 걸어 두는 벽장 안에서 톰킨스 양과 회담을 갖는 데 동의하자, 그는 즉시 제 발로 그 안에 들어갔고, 단단히 갇혔다. 이로써 나머지 사람들도 기운을 차렸다. 그리고 톰킨스 양이 정신을 차리고 또 아래층으로 내려오자, 회담이 시작되었다.\n\n\"내 뜰에서 무얼 했어요, 이 사람아?\" 톰킨스 양이 가냘픈 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"댁의 아가씨들 가운데 한 분이 오늘 밤 도망치려 한다는 것을 경고하러 왔습니다.\" 픽윅 씨가 벽장 안에서 대답했다.\n\n\"도망이라뇨!\" 톰킨스 양과 교사 셋, 기숙생 서른 명, 그리고 하인 다섯이 외쳤다. \"누구하고요?\"\n\n\"댁의 친구인 찰스 피츠마셜 씨하고요.\"\n\n\"내 친구라뇨! 난 그런 사람 모르는데.\"\n\n\"그럼, 징글 씨하고요.\"\n\n\"그런 이름은 평생 들어 본 적도 없어요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 나는 속고 기만당한 것이군.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"나는 음모의 희생양이 된 거요—더럽고 비열한 음모의. 내 말이 못 믿기시거든, 부인, 에인절 여관으로 사람을 보내 보십시오. 부탁드립니다, 부인, 에인절 여관으로 사람을 보내 픽윅 씨의 하인을 불러 주십시오.\"\n\n\"점잖은 분인 게 분명해—하인을 두셨다니.\" 톰킨스 양이 글쓰기와 셈하기를 가르치는 가정교사에게 말했다.\n\n\"제 생각엔요, 톰킨스 양.\" 글쓰기와 셈하기 가정교사가 말했다. \"그 하인이 저 사람을 두고 있는 것 같아요. 저는 저 사람이 미치광이고, 다른 사람이 그 보호자인 것 같습니다, 톰킨스 양.\"\n\n\"그웬 양 말이 아주 옳은 것 같네요.\" 톰킨스 양이 응답했다. \"하인 둘을 에인절 여관으로 보내고, 나머지는 여기 남아 우릴 지켜요.\"\n\n그리하여 하인 둘이 새뮤얼 웰러 씨를 찾아 에인절 여관으로 파견되었고, 나머지 셋은 톰킨스 양과 교사 셋, 기숙생 서른 명을 지키려고 뒤에 남았다. 그리고 픽윅 씨는 샌드위치 가방이 늘어선 숲 아래 벽장 안에 앉아, 끌어모을 수 있는 온갖 철학과 꿋꿋함으로 심부름꾼들이 돌아오기를 기다렸다.\n\n그들이 돌아오기까지 한 시간 반이 흘렀다. 그리고 그들이 마침내 왔을 때, 픽윅 씨는 새뮤얼 웰러 씨의 목소리에 더해, 그의 귀에 익숙하게 와닿는 다른 두 목소리를 알아챘다. 하지만 그것이 누구의 목소리인지는 아무리 애를 써도 도무지 떠올릴 수가 없었다.\n\n아주 짤막한 대화가 오갔다. 문이 열렸다. 픽윅 씨는 벽장에서 걸어 나와, 웨스트게이트 하우스의 시설 전원과 새뮤얼 웰러 씨, 그리고—워들 영감과 그의 장차 사위가 될 트런들 씨 앞에 서 있는 자신을 발견했다!\n\n\"이보게, 친구.\" 픽윅 씨가 앞으로 달려나가 워들의 손을 붙잡으며 말했다. \"이보게, 친구, 제발, 하늘에 맹세코, 내가 처한 이 불운하고 끔찍한 처지를 이 부인께 설명해 주게. 내 하인한테서 들었을 테지. 아무튼, 이 사람아, 내가 강도도 미치광이도 아니라고 말해 주게.\"\n\n\"이미 그렇게 말했네, 친구. 벌써 그렇게 말했어.\" 워들 씨가 친구의 오른손을 흔들며 대답했다. 트런들 씨는 그의 왼손을 흔들었다.\n\n\"그리고 이분이 강도라거나 미치광이라고 말하는 자나, 말했던 자가 있다면.\" 웰러 씨가 앞으로 나서며 끼어들었다. \"그건 사실이 아닌 걸 말하는 거고, 사실은커녕 정반대로 완전히 거꾸로지요. 그리고 이 건물 안에 그렇게 말한 자들이 몇이 됐든, 저는 이 아주 점잖으신 숙녀분들께서 물러가시고 한 번에 한 놈씩 올려 보내 주시는 친절을 베푸신다면, 바로 이 방에서 그놈들 모두에게 자기들이 틀렸다는 아주 확실한 증거를 제공해 드리는 영광을 누리겠습니다요.\" 이 도전장을 무척 거침없이 쏟아낸 뒤, 웰러 씨는 펼친 손바닥을 꽉 쥔 주먹으로 힘차게 내리치고는, 톰킨스 양에게 유쾌하게 눈을 찡긋했다. 그가 웨스트게이트 하우스 영애 양성소의 건물 안에 남자가 있을 가능성의 범위 안에 있다고 상정한 데 대해 톰킨스 양이 느낀 그 격렬한 공포는 이루 다 묘사할 수가 없다.\n\n픽윅 씨의 설명은 이미 부분적으로 이루어졌던지라 곧 마무리되었다. 그러나 친구들과 함께 집으로 걸어가는 동안에도, 그 뒤 그토록 절실했던 야식을 위해 활활 타오르는 난롯불 앞에 앉았을 때에도, 그에게서는 단 한마디 말도 끌어낼 수 없었다. 그는 어리둥절하고 어안이 벙벙해 보였다. 단 한 번, 오직 한 번, 그는 워들 씨에게로 돌아서서 말했다.\n\n\"자네는 어쩌다 여기 온 건가?\"\n\n\"트런들과 내가 초하룻날 사냥 좀 제대로 해 보려고 여기 내려온 걸세.\" 워들이 대답했다. \"오늘 밤에 도착했는데, 자네 하인한테서 자네도 여기 와 있다는 말을 듣고 깜짝 놀랐지. 하지만 자네가 와 있어서 반갑네.\" 노인이 그의 등을 탁 치며 말했다. \"와 있어서 반가워. 초하룻날 흥겨운 잔치를 벌이세. 그리고 윙클에게 한 번 더 기회를 주자고—안 그런가, 친구?\"\n\n픽윅 씨는 아무 대답도 하지 않았다. 그는 딩글리 델에 있는 친구들의 안부조차 묻지 않았으며, 얼마 안 있어 잠자리에 들기 위해 물러갔다. 그러면서 종을 울리면 샘에게 촛불을 가져오라고 일러 두었다.\n\n종은 적당한 때에 울렸고, 웰러 씨가 모습을 드러냈다.\n\n\"샘.\" 픽윅 씨가 이불 밑에서 내다보며 말했다.\n\n\"네, 나리.\" 웰러 씨가 말했다.\n\n픽윅 씨가 잠시 말을 멈추자, 웰러 씨는 촛불 심지를 잘랐다.\n\n\"샘.\" 픽윅 씨가 마치 필사적인 노력을 기울이듯 다시 말했다.\n\n\"네, 나리.\" 웰러 씨가 또 한 번 말했다.\n\n\"그 트로터는 어디 있나?\"\n\n\"잡 말씀입니까, 나리?\"\n\n\"그래.\"\n\n\"갔습니다, 나리.\"\n\"제 주인하고 함께겠지?\"\n\n\"친구든 주인이든 뭐든 간에, 그자와 함께 갔습니다요, 나리.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다. \"그 둘은 한 짝패지요, 나리.\"\n\n\"징글이 내 계략을 눈치채고, 그 녀석을 시켜 자네한테 이 이야기를 흘리게 한 거겠지, 그렇지?\" 픽윅 씨가 거의 숨이 막힌 채 말했다.\n\n\"바로 그렇습니다, 나리.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"물론 죄다 거짓이었고?\"\n\n\"죄다 거짓이었지요, 나리.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다. \"완전히 한 방 먹인 거죠, 나리. 교활한 술책입니다요.\"\n\n\"다음번엔 그자가 우리한테서 그리 쉽게 빠져나가진 못할 걸세, 샘!\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그러지 못할 겁니다, 나리.\"\n\n\"그 징글을 다시 만나는 날엔, 그게 어디든 간에.\" 픽윅 씨가 침대에서 몸을 일으키며 베개를 엄청난 일격으로 푹 꺼지게 내리치고 말했다. \"그자가 그토록 톡톡히 받아 마땅한 폭로에 더해, 직접 손수 응징을 가하고 말겠어. 반드시 그러겠어. 안 그러면 내 이름이 픽윅이 아니지.\"\n\n\"그리고 그 검은 머리의 우중충한 녀석을 제가 붙잡는 날엔.\" 샘이 말했다. \"이번 한 번만큼은 그 눈에서 진짜 물이 나오게 해 주지 못한다면, 제 이름은 웰러가 아닙죠. 안녕히 주무십시오, 나리!\"\n\n픽윅 씨의 체질은 상당한 정도의 노고와 피로를 견뎌 낼 수 있었으나, 앞 장에 기록된 그 잊지 못할 밤에 그가 겪은 것과 같은 여러 공격이 한꺼번에 닥치는 데에는 끄떡없지 못했다. 밤공기에 씻기고 벽장 안에서 거칠게 말려지는 과정이란 기이한 만큼이나 위험한 것이다. 픽윅 씨는 류머티즘 발작으로 자리에 눕고 말았다.\n\n그러나 이 위대한 인물의 육신의 힘이 그렇듯 손상되었음에도, 그의 정신적 활력은 본래의 기운을 그대로 간직하고 있었다. 그의 기분은 탄력이 있었고, 그의 쾌활함도 되살아났다. 최근의 모험에서 비롯된 언짢음마저도 그의 마음에서 사라졌으니, 워들 씨가 그 일을 들먹일 때마다 터뜨리는 호탕한 웃음에 그도 노여움 없이, 거북함 없이 함께 어울릴 수 있었다. 아니, 그뿐이 아니었다. 픽윅 씨가 자리에 묶여 있던 이틀 동안 샘은 한결같이 그의 곁을 지켰다. 첫째 날, 샘은 일화와 이야기로 주인을 즐겁게 해 주려 애썼고, 둘째 날, 픽윅 씨는 책상과 펜과 잉크를 청하더니 온종일 무언가에 깊이 몰두했다. 셋째 날, 침실에서 일어나 앉을 수 있게 되자 그는 시종을 보내 워들 씨와 트런들 씨에게 전갈을 전했으니, 그날 저녁 그곳에서 함께 술을 들어 준다면 큰 기쁨이겠다는 내용이었다. 초대는 더없이 흔쾌히 받아들여졌고, 두 사람이 술잔을 앞에 두고 자리에 앉자, 픽윅 씨는 이런저런 낯을 붉히며 다음과 같은 짤막한 이야기를 내놓았다. 그것은 그가 최근의 병중에 웰러 씨의 꾸밈없는 구술 기록을 손수 \"편집한\" 것이었다.\n교구 서기—참된 사랑의 이야기\n\n# THE PARISH CLERK A TALE OF TRUE LOVE\n\n\"옛날 옛적, 런던에서 꽤 멀리 떨어진 아주 작은 시골 마을에 너새니얼 핍킨이라는 작은 사내가 살았으니, 그는 그 작은 마을의 교구 서기였고, 작은 교회에서 걸어서 십 분도 안 되는 작은 한길가의 작은 집에 살았으며, 날마다 아홉 시부터 네 시까지 작은 사내아이들에게 작은 학문을 가르치는 일로 만날 수 있는 사람이었다. 너새니얼 핍킨은 해롭지 않고 거슬리지 않으며 마음씨 고운 사람으로, 위로 들린 코에 안쪽으로 휜 다리, 사팔뜨기 눈에 절뚝거리는 걸음걸이를 지녔다. 그는 교회와 학교 사이로 시간을 나누어 보내며, 이 땅 위에 부목사만큼 똑똑한 사람도, 제의실만큼 위풍당당한 방도, 자기 학교만큼 잘 정돈된 학당도 없다고 진실로 믿었다. 평생에 단 한 번, 오직 한 번 너새니얼 핍킨은 주교를 본 적이 있었다—두 팔을 고운 모시 소매에 넣고 머리에 가발을 쓴, 진짜 주교 말이다. 그는 견진성사 자리에서 그 주교가 걷는 것을 보고 말하는 것을 들었는데, 그 중대한 자리에서 앞서 말한 주교가 그의 머리에 손을 얹자 너새니얼 핍킨은 경외와 두려움에 그만 압도되어 깨끗이 기절해 버렸고, 교회지기의 품에 안겨 교회 밖으로 실려 나왔다.\n\n\"이것이 너새니얼 핍킨의 인생에서 한 큰 사건이요, 어마어마한 시대였으며, 그의 조용한 생애의 잔잔한 물결을 흐트러뜨린 유일한 일이었다. 그러던 어느 화창한 오후, 그가 멍하니 정신을 놓고 있다가, 잘못을 저지른 한 개구쟁이에게 풀게 하려고 복합 덧셈의 어마어마한 문제를 궁리하던 석판에서 눈을 들었을 때, 그의 시선이 문득 길 건너편 큰 마구상 노인 롭스의 외동딸인 마리아 롭스의 꽃다운 얼굴에 가서 멎었다. 그런데 핍킨 씨의 눈은 그전에도 교회에서나 다른 곳에서나 마리아 롭스의 어여쁜 얼굴에 여러 번 멎은 적이 있었지만, 이날만큼 마리아 롭스의 눈이 그토록 빛나 보인 적도, 마리아 롭스의 두 뺨이 그토록 발그레해 보인 적도 없었다. 그러니 너새니얼 핍킨이 롭스 양의 얼굴에서 눈을 떼지 못한 것도 이상할 게 없고, 롭스 양이 한 젊은이가 자기를 빤히 쳐다보는 것을 알아채고는 내다보던 창문에서 고개를 거두고 여닫이창을 닫더니 블라인드를 내린 것도 이상할 게 없으며, 그 직후에 너새니얼 핍킨이 앞서 잘못을 저지른 어린 개구쟁이에게 달려들어 마음껏 두들기고 쥐어박은 것도 이상할 게 없다. 이 모든 것이 지극히 자연스러운 일이라 조금도 이상할 것이 없다.\n\n\"그러나 정작 이상한 일은, 너새니얼 핍킨 씨처럼 내성적인 성품에 신경질적인 기질에다 무엇보다도 쥐꼬리만 한 수입을 가진 사람이, 이날 이후로 그 불같은 노인 롭스의 외동딸의 손과 마음을 감히 넘본 것이다—롭스, 그 큰 마구상 노인은 펜을 한 번 놀려 온 마을을 통째로 사들이고도 그 지출을 아랑곳하지 않을 만한 사람이었고—가장 가까운 장터 읍내의 은행에 산더미 같은 돈을 넣어 두었다고 익히 알려진 노인이었으며—뒷거실 벽난로 위에 놓인, 큼지막한 열쇠 구멍이 달린 작은 쇠금고 안에 헤아릴 수 없고 마르지 않는 보물을 쟁여 두었다고 소문난 노인이었고—잔치가 있을 때면 식탁을 진짜 은제 찻주전자와 크림 단지와 설탕 그릇으로 장식하던 노인이었으니, 그는 자랑스러운 마음에 딸이 마음에 드는 사내를 만나는 날엔 그것들이 모두 딸의 것이 되리라 으스대곤 했다. 거듭 말하거니와, 너새니얼 핍킨이 감히 그 방향으로 눈을 던졌다는 것은 깊은 경악과 강렬한 의아함을 자아내는 일이다. 그러나 사랑은 눈이 멀게 마련이고, 너새니얼은 사팔뜨기 눈을 가졌으니, 어쩌면 이 두 사정이 합쳐져 그가 사태를 제대로 보지 못하게 했는지도 모른다.\n\n\"한데 만일 노인 롭스가 너새니얼 핍킨의 애정 상태를 조금이라도, 아주 어렴풋이라도 알아챘다면, 그는 학교 건물을 송두리째 땅바닥에 헐어 버리거나, 그 선생을 지상에서 박멸하거나, 그에 못지않게 사납고 난폭한 어떤 폭거와 만행을 저질렀을 것이다. 자존심이 상하거나 피가 끓어오를 때면 롭스란 노인은 끔찍한 사내였으니까. 욕설이라니! 다리가 가느다란 깡마른 도제의 게으름을 호되게 꾸짖을 때면, 때때로 길 건너편까지 욕설의 행렬이 우르르 천둥처럼 굴러와, 너새니얼 핍킨은 공포에 신발 속에서 발을 부들부들 떨었고, 학생들의 머리카락은 겁에 질려 곤두서곤 했다.\n\n\"자! 날이면 날마다 수업이 끝나고 학생들이 돌아가고 나면, 너새니얼 핍킨은 앞창가에 자리를 잡고 앉아, 책을 읽는 척하면서 마리아 롭스의 빛나는 눈을 찾아 길 건너편으로 곁눈질을 던지곤 했다. 그렇게 앉은 지 며칠이 채 못 되어, 위층 창문에 그 빛나는 눈이 나타났는데, 보아하니 그쪽도 독서에 깊이 빠져 있는 모양이었다. 이것은 너새니얼 핍킨의 마음에 더없이 흐뭇하고 기쁜 일이었다. 그 어여쁜 얼굴의 눈이 내리깔려 있을 때라도 거기 몇 시간이고 앉아 바라보는 것만으로도 대단한 일이었지만, 마리아 롭스가 책에서 눈을 들어 그 눈빛을 너새니얼 핍킨 쪽으로 쏘기 시작하면 그의 기쁨과 흠모는 그야말로 끝이 없었다. 마침내 어느 날, 노인 롭스가 외출한 것을 안 너새니얼 핍킨은 대담하게도 마리아 롭스에게 자기 손에 입을 맞춰 보였고, 마리아 롭스는 창문을 닫고 블라인드를 내리는 대신, 그에게 제 손에 입을 맞춰 보이며 미소를 지었다. 이에 너새니얼 핍킨은 무슨 일이 있어도 더 미루지 않고 제 마음의 상태를 펼쳐 보이리라 결심했다.\n\n\"이보다 더 어여쁜 발, 더 명랑한 마음, 더 보조개진 얼굴, 더 맵시 있는 몸매가 자기가 빛내 주는 땅 위를 그토록 가볍게 뛰노닌 적은 없었으니, 늙은 마구상의 딸 마리아 롭스가 바로 그러했다. 그 반짝이는 눈에는 짓궂은 빛이 어른거려, 너새니얼 핍킨보다 훨씬 둔감한 가슴이라도 능히 파고들었을 것이고, 그 흥겨운 웃음소리에는 어찌나 즐거운 가락이 있던지 아무리 무뚝뚝한 염세가라도 그것을 들으면 미소 짓지 않을 수 없었을 것이다. 노인 롭스 자신마저도, 그 사나움이 한창 치솟을 때조차 어여쁜 딸의 어리광에는 당해 낼 수 없었다. 그래서 그녀와 그녀의 사촌 케이트—짓궂고 뻔뻔스러워 보이는, 사람을 호리는 작은 아가씨—가 둘이서 함께 노인을 작정하고 졸라 댈 때면, 사실 그들은 곧잘 그러곤 했는데, 노인은 그들에게 아무것도 거절하지 못했을 것이니, 설령 그들이 빛을 보지 못한 채 쇠금고 안에 숨겨진 그 헤아릴 수 없고 마르지 않는 보물의 일부를 달라고 했더라도 그러했을 것이다.\n\n\"어느 여름 저녁, 너새니얼 핍킨이 이 매혹적인 작은 두 사람을 백 야드쯤 앞에서 보았을 때 그의 가슴은 높이 뛰었다. 그곳은 바로 그가 여러 번 밤이 깊도록 거닐며 마리아 롭스의 아름다움을 곱씹던 바로 그 들판이었다. 그러나 그때마다 그는, 그녀를 만나기만 하면 얼마나 씩씩하게 마리아 롭스에게 다가가 제 연정을 고백하겠노라 곧잘 생각했건만, 막상 그녀가 뜻밖에 눈앞에 나타나자 온몸의 피가 얼굴로 죄다 솟구치는 것을 느꼈고, 그 탓에 평소의 몫을 빼앗긴 두 다리가 그 아래에서 후들거리니, 이는 분명 다리에 큰 손해였다. 그들이 울타리 꽃을 꺾으려, 혹은 새소리를 들으려 멈춰 서면 너새니얼 핍킨도 멈춰 서서 사색에 잠긴 척했는데, 사실 그는 정말로 사색에 잠겨 있었다. 머잖아 그들이 반드시 그래야 할 테고, 그래서 그들이 돌아서서 얼굴을 마주 보게 되면 도대체 어찌해야 할지를 궁리하고 있었으니 말이다. 그러나 그는 그들에게 다가가기는 두려워하면서도 그들을 시야에서 놓치는 것은 견딜 수 없었다. 그래서 그들이 빨리 걸으면 그도 빨리 걸었고, 그들이 머뭇거리면 그도 머뭇거렸으며, 그들이 멈추면 그도 멈췄다. 그렇게 어둠이 그들을 가로막을 때까지 계속 갔을지도 모르나, 케이트가 슬쩍 뒤를 돌아보며 너새니얼에게 어서 오라고 부추기듯 손짓하지 않았던들 그러했을 것이다. 케이트의 태도에는 거역할 수 없는 무언가가 있었던지라 너새니얼 핍킨은 그 청에 응했다. 그러고는 그가 한바탕 낯을 붉히고, 그 짓궂은 작은 사촌이 한바탕 분에 넘치게 웃어 댄 끝에, 너새니얼 핍킨은 이슬 젖은 풀밭에 무릎을 꿇고, 마리아 롭스의 받아들여진 연인으로 일어서도록 허락받지 않는 한 영원히 그 자리에 머물겠다는 결심을 선언했다. 이에 롭스 양의 흥겨운 웃음소리가 고요한 저녁 공기를 가르며 울려 퍼졌으나—그러면서도 그 공기를 어지럽히지는 않는 듯했으니, 그 소리가 그토록 듣기 좋았던 까닭이다—짓궂은 작은 사촌은 아까보다 더 분에 넘치게 웃어 댔고, 너새니얼 핍킨은 어느 때보다 더 깊이 낯을 붉혔다. 마침내 마리아 롭스는 사랑에 애가 닳은 작은 사내가 더욱 간곡히 졸라 대자 고개를 돌려 사촌에게 대신 말해 달라고 속삭였고, 어쨌거나 케이트가 이렇게 말했다. 마리아는 핍킨 씨의 구애를 무척 영광스럽게 여기며, 자신의 손과 마음은 아버지의 뜻에 달려 있으나, 핍킨 씨의 장점에 무심할 사람은 아무도 없을 것이라고. 이 모든 말이 무척이나 진지하게 전해졌고, 너새니얼 핍킨은 마리아 롭스와 함께 집으로 걸어가며 헤어질 적에 입맞춤을 얻으려 애쓴 끝에, 행복한 사내가 되어 잠자리에 들었고, 밤새도록 노인 롭스를 누그러뜨리고, 금고를 열고, 마리아와 결혼하는 꿈을 꾸었다.\n\n\"이튿날, 너새니얼 핍킨은 노인 롭스가 그 늙은 잿빛 조랑말을 타고 외출하는 것을 보았고, 짓궂은 작은 사촌이 창문에서 여러 차례 신호를 보냈으나 그 뜻과 의미를 도무지 알아챌 수 없던 차에, 다리가 가느다란 깡마른 도제가 건너와서, 주인이 밤새 집에 돌아오지 않을 것이며, 아가씨들이 핍킨 씨가 정확히 여섯 시에 차를 들러 오기를 기다린다고 전했다. 그날 수업이 어떻게 끝났는지는 너새니얼 핍킨도 그의 학생들도 여러분만큼이나 모를 일이지만, 어쨌든 어떻게든 끝나기는 했고, 아이들이 돌아간 뒤 너새니얼 핍킨은 만족스럽게 차려입느라 여섯 시가 꽉 차도록 시간을 들였다. 입을 옷을 고르는 데 오래 걸려서가 아니었다. 그 문제에서는 달리 선택의 여지가 없었으니까. 그러나 그 옷을 가장 돋보이게 차려입고, 미리 손질을 해 두는 일은 결코 가볍지 않은 어려움과 중요성을 지닌 과업이었다.\n\n\"그곳에는 마리아 롭스와 그녀의 사촌 케이트, 그리고 서너 명의 떠들썩하고 명랑하며 볼이 발그레한 처녀들로 이루어진, 아주 아늑한 작은 모임이 있었다. 너새니얼 핍킨은 노인 롭스의 보물에 관한 소문이 과장이 아니었다는 사실을 두 눈으로 똑똑히 확인했다. 식탁에는 진짜 묵직한 은제 찻주전자와 크림 단지와 설탕 그릇이 놓여 있었고, 차를 저을 진짜 은수저와, 차를 따라 마실 진짜 도자기 잔과, 케이크와 토스트를 담을 같은 도자기 접시들이 있었다. 그 모든 곳에서 단 하나 눈엣가시는 마리아 롭스의 또 다른 사촌이자 케이트의 오라비였으니, 마리아 롭스는 그를 \"헨리\"라 불렀고, 그는 식탁 한쪽 구석에서 마리아 롭스를 온통 제 차지로 붙들어 두는 것 같았다. 가족 간의 정을 보는 것은 흐뭇한 일이지만, 그것도 좀 지나치면 곤란한 법인데, 너새니얼 핍킨은 마리아 롭스가 모든 친척에게 이 한 사람의 사촌에게 쏟는 만큼의 관심을 쏟는다면, 그녀는 친척을 유난히도 끔찍이 아끼는 사람임에 틀림없다고 생각하지 않을 수 없었다. 게다가 차를 마신 뒤, 짓궂은 작은 사촌이 술래잡기 놀이를 제안하자, 어찌 된 영문인지 너새니얼 핍킨은 거의 늘 눈을 가린 술래가 되었고, 그가 그 사내 사촌에게 손을 댈 때마다 어김없이 마리아 롭스가 멀지 않은 곳에 있음을 알게 되었다. 그리고 짓궂은 작은 사촌과 다른 처녀들이 그를 꼬집고, 머리카락을 잡아당기고, 그의 앞길에 의자를 밀어 놓는 따위 온갖 짓을 하는데도, 마리아 롭스는 도무지 그의 곁에 오는 법이 없는 것 같았다. 그리고 한 번—단 한 번—너새니얼 핍킨은 분명 입맞춤 소리를 들었고, 뒤이어 마리아 롭스의 희미한 항의와 그녀의 여자 친구들의 반쯤 억눌린 웃음소리를 들었노라 맹세할 수도 있었다. 이 모든 것이 야릇했다—참으로 야릇했다—그리고 만일 그의 생각이 갑자기 새로운 물길로 돌려지지 않았던들, 그 결과 너새니얼 핍킨이 무슨 짓을 했을지 안 했을지는 알 수 없는 노릇이다.\n\n\"그의 생각을 새로운 물길로 돌린 사정인즉, 거리 쪽 문을 쾅쾅 두드리는 요란한 소리였으니, 그렇게 거리 쪽 문을 요란하게 두드린 사람은 다름 아닌 노인 롭스 자신이었다. 그는 뜻밖에 돌아와 관 짜는 목수처럼 쾅쾅 두들겨 대고 있었으니, 저녁밥을 먹고 싶었던 것이다. 다리가 가느다란 깡마른 도제가 이 놀라운 소식을 전하기가 무섭게, 처녀들은 마리아 롭스의 침실로 종종걸음으로 올라갔고, 사내 사촌과 너새니얼 핍킨은 더 나은 은신처가 없던 터라 거실에 있는 벽장 두 개에 떠밀려 들어갔다. 마리아 롭스와 짓궂은 작은 사촌이 그들을 안에 숨기고 방을 말끔히 정돈한 뒤, 처음 두드리기 시작한 이래 한 번도 멈추지 않은 노인 롭스를 향해 거리 쪽 문을 열어 주었다.\n\n\"그런데 공교롭게도 노인 롭스는 몹시 시장한 탓에 무지막지하게 심술이 나 있었다. 너새니얼 핍킨은 그가 목이 쉰 늙은 마스티프처럼 으르렁대는 소리를 들을 수 있었고, 다리가 가느다란 그 가엾은 도제가 방에 들어올 때마다 노인 롭스는 어김없이 더없이 사라센적이고 사나운 투로 그에게 욕을 퍼붓기 시작했는데, 보아하니 군더더기 욕설 몇 마디를 토해 내어 제 가슴을 후련하게 하는 것 말고는 다른 목적이나 의도가 없는 듯했다. 마침내 데워 두었던 저녁밥이 식탁에 차려졌고, 노인 롭스는 격식대로 먹기 시작하더니, 눈 깜짝할 새에 말끔히 해치우고는 딸에게 입을 맞추고 파이프를 가져오라고 했다.\n\n\"자연은 너새니얼 핍킨의 두 무릎을 아주 바싹 붙여 놓았는데, 노인 롭스가 제 파이프를 가져오라고 하는 소리를 듣자 그 두 무릎이 서로 가루로 만들어 버리기라도 할 듯이 맞부딪쳤다. 왜냐하면 그가 서 있는 바로 그 벽장 안에, 갈고리 두 개에 매달려, 갈색 대롱에 은제 통이 달린 커다란 파이프가 걸려 있었으니, 바로 지난 오 년 동안 그가 매일 오후와 저녁마다 어김없이 노인 롭스의 입에 물려 있는 것을 봐 온 그 파이프였기 때문이다. 두 처녀는 파이프를 찾으러 아래층으로 내려가고 위층으로 올라가고, 정작 파이프가 있는 줄 아는 곳만 빼고 사방을 다 뒤졌으며, 그동안 노인 롭스는 더없이 놀라운 기세로 길길이 날뛰었다. 마침내 그는 벽장을 떠올리고 그쪽으로 걸어갔다. 노인 롭스 같은 덩치 크고 힘센 사내가 문을 바깥쪽으로 잡아당기는데, 너새니얼 핍킨 같은 작은 사내가 문을 안쪽으로 잡아당겨 봐야 아무 소용이 없었다. 노인 롭스가 한 번 홱 잡아당기자 문이 벌컥 열리며, 안에 빳빳이 곧추서서 머리끝부터 발끝까지 두려움에 떨고 있는 너새니얼 핍킨이 드러났다. 어이쿠! 노인 롭스가 그의 멱살을 잡아 끌어내어 팔을 쭉 뻗어 붙들고는 그에게 던진 그 무시무시한 눈초리란!\n\n\"《그래, 도대체 여기서 뭘 하자는 거냐?》 노인 롭스가 무시무시한 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"너새니얼 핍킨은 아무 대답도 못 했고, 그러자 노인 롭스는 그의 생각을 정리해 주려는 셈으로 그를 두세 분 동안 앞뒤로 뒤흔들었다.\n\n\"《여기서 뭘 하자는 거냐?》 롭스가 고함쳤다. 《내 딸을 노리고 온 게로구나, 그렇지!》\n\n\"노인 롭스가 이렇게 말한 것은 그저 비웃으려는 것이었으니, 인간의 주제넘음이 너새니얼 핍킨을 그토록 멀리까지 끌고 갔으리라고는 믿지 않았던 것이다. 그러니 그 가엾은 사내가 이렇게 대답했을 때 그의 분노가 어떠했겠는가—\n\n\"《예, 그렇습니다, 롭스 씨, 저는 따님을 노리고 왔습니다. 저는 따님을 사랑합니다, 롭스 씨.》\n\n\"《이런, 이 훌쩍거리는 비뚜름한 낯짝의 하찮은 악당아.》 노인 롭스는 그 끔찍한 고백에 얼이 빠진 채 헐떡였다. 《그게 무슨 소리냐? 내 얼굴에다 대고 이런 말을 해? 빌어먹을, 네 멱을 비틀어 놓겠다!》\n\n\"노인 롭스가 격노한 나머지 그 위협을 정말로 실행에 옮겼을 법도 했으나, 아주 뜻밖의 출현에 그의 팔이 멈춰 섰으니, 곧 그 사내 사촌이었다. 그가 제 벽장에서 걸어 나와 노인 롭스에게 다가가더니 이렇게 말했다—\n\n\"《어르신, 어떤 처녀들의 장난질에 불려 와서, 제가 저질렀고 또 기꺼이 인정하려는 잘못(만일 그것이 잘못이라면)을 그토록 갸륵하게 제 탓으로 떠안으려는 이 해롭지 않은 사람을, 저는 그대로 두고 볼 수 없습니다. 저는 따님을 사랑합니다, 어르신. 그리고 저는 그녀를 만나려고 여기 왔습니다.》\n\n\"노인 롭스는 이 말에 눈을 휘둥그레 떴으나, 너새니얼 핍킨이 휘둥그레 뜬 것만큼은 아니었다.\n\n\"《네가?》 마침내 말할 숨을 되찾은 롭스가 말했다.\n\n\"《제가요.》\n\n\"《내가 오래전에 이 집에 발도 들이지 말라고 했거늘.》\n\n\"《그러셨지요. 그러지 않으셨다면 오늘 밤 제가 몰래 여기 와 있지도 않았겠지요.》\n\n\"노인 롭스에 관해 이렇게 기록하기는 가슴 아프지만, 만일 그 어여쁜 딸이 눈물 어린 빛나는 눈으로 그의 팔에 매달리지 않았던들, 그는 그 사촌을 후려쳤으리라고 나는 생각한다.\n\n\"《아버지를 말리지 마시오, 마리아.》 젊은이가 말했다. 《어르신이 나를 칠 마음이라면, 치게 두시오. 나는 세상의 온갖 재물을 준다 해도 그분의 흰 머리카락 한 올 다치게 하지 않을 것이오.》\n\n\"노인은 이 꾸짖음에 눈을 내리깔았고, 그 눈이 딸의 눈과 마주쳤다. 앞서 한두 번 넌지시 일렀듯이 그것은 무척 빛나는 눈이었고, 비록 지금은 눈물에 젖어 있었으나 그 위력은 결코 줄어들지 않았다. 노인 롭스는 그 눈에 설득당하지 않으려는 듯 고개를 옆으로 돌렸는데, 운명의 장난인지 그만 짓궂은 작은 사촌의 얼굴과 마주쳤다. 그녀는 제 오라비가 반쯤 걱정스럽고 너새니얼 핍킨이 반쯤 우스워서, 늙은이든 젊은이든 어느 사내가 보아도 좋을 만큼, 거기에 약간의 능청까지 깃든 더없이 사람을 호리는 표정을 짓고 있었다. 그녀가 노인의 팔에 제 팔을 어리광 부리듯 끼고는 그의 귀에 무언가 속삭이자, 노인 롭스는 아무리 애써도 빙그레 미소를 터뜨리지 않을 수 없었고, 그와 동시에 한 줄기 눈물이 그의 뺨을 타고 흘러내렸다.\n\n\"이로부터 오 분 뒤, 처녀들이 한바탕 키득거림과 수줍음과 함께 침실에서 아래로 내려왔고, 젊은이들이 더없이 행복해하는 동안 노인 롭스는 파이프를 내려 피웠는데, 그 한 대의 담배에 관해 한 가지 주목할 만한 점은, 그것이 그가 일찍이 피워 본 것 가운데 가장 마음을 누그러뜨리고 가장 흐뭇한 한 대였다는 것이다.\n\n\"너새니얼 핍킨은 제 속을 잠자코 간직하는 것이 상책이라 여겼고, 그렇게 함으로써 차츰 노인 롭스의 두터운 총애를 받게 되었으니, 노인은 머잖아 그에게 담배 피우는 법까지 가르쳐 주었다. 그래서 두 사람은 그 뒤로 여러 해 동안 날씨 좋은 저녁이면 정원에 나앉아 거하게 담배를 피우고 술을 들곤 했다. 그는 곧 제 연정의 후유증을 떨쳐 냈으니, 교구 등록부에 마리아 롭스와 그녀의 사촌의 결혼 증인으로 그의 이름이 올라 있는 것이 보이는 까닭이다. 또한 다른 문서들을 참조해 보면, 결혼식 날 밤 그는 만취한 상태로 거리에서 이런저런 난동을 부린 죄로 마을 유치장에 갇혔다고 나오는데, 그 모든 난동에서 그는 다리가 가느다란 깡마른 도제의 거들고 부추김을 받았다고 한다.\"\n\n헌터 부인 댁의 조찬이 있은 뒤 이틀 동안, 픽윅 회원들은 이턴스윌에 머무르며 존경하는 지도자로부터 무슨 소식이 도착하기를 애타게 기다렸다. 터프먼 씨와 스노드그래스 씨는 다시 한 번 저마다 알아서 소일거리를 찾아야 하는 처지가 되었으니, 윈클 씨는 더없이 간곡한 초대에 응하여 여전히 포트 씨 댁에 머무르며 그의 상냥한 부인과 어울리는 데 시간을 바치고 있었기 때문이다. 게다가 이따금 포트 씨 자신과의 교제까지 곁들여져 그들의 행복은 완전해졌다. 공공의 복리와 인디펜던트 신문의 파멸을 위한 깊은 궁리에 골몰해 있던 이 위대한 인물은, 자신의 정신적 정점에서 평범한 머리들의 비천한 수준으로 내려오는 법이 좀처럼 없었다. 그러나 이번에는, 마치 픽윅 씨를 따르는 이라면 누구든 각별히 예우하려는 듯, 그는 마음을 풀고 긴장을 늦추며 받침대에서 내려와 땅 위를 걸었고, 무리들이 알아들을 수 있게 자비롭게 자신의 말을 맞추어, 정신은 아닐지언정 겉모습으로는 그들 가운데 하나처럼 보였다.\n\n이 저명한 공인이 윈클 씨를 대하는 태도가 이러했으니, 그가 조찬실에 혼자 앉아 있을 때 문이 황급히 열렸다가 포트 씨가 들어서자마자 또 황급히 닫히고, 그 신사가 위풍당당하게 그를 향해 성큼성큼 다가와 내미는 손을 뿌리치며, 이제 막 내뱉으려는 말에 한층 날을 세우기라도 하듯 이를 갈고는, 톱질하는 듯한 목소리로 외쳤을 때, 후자 신사의 얼굴에 상당한 놀라움이 떠올랐으리라는 것은 쉽사리 짐작이 갈 것이다.\n\n\"이 뱀 같은 자!\"\n\n\"이보시오!\" 윈클 씨가 의자에서 벌떡 일어서며 외쳤다.\n\n\"뱀 같은 자라고 했소.\" 포트 씨가 목소리를 높였다가 별안간 낮추며 되풀이했다. \"내가 뱀 같은 자라고 했소—마음껏 새겨들으시오.\"\n\n새벽 두 시에 더없이 두터운 우정을 나누며 헤어진 사람이 아홉 시 반에 다시 만나 뱀이라고 인사를 건넨다면, 그사이에 뭔가 불쾌한 일이 일어났으리라 결론짓는 것이 무리는 아닐 것이다. 윈클 씨도 그렇게 생각했다. 그는 포트 씨의 돌 같은 시선을 마주 받았고, 그 신사의 요청에 따라 \"뱀\"이라는 말을 할 수 있는 한 마음껏 새겨 보았다. 그러나 마음껏 새긴 결과란 아무것도 아니었다. 그래서 몇 분간의 깊은 침묵 끝에 그가 말했다—\n\n\"뱀이라뇨, 이보시오! 뱀이라니, 포트 씨! 대체 무슨 뜻입니까?—농담이겠지요.\"\n\n\"농담이라!\" 포트가 외치며, 영국제 백랍 찻주전자를 방문객의 머리에 내던지고 싶은 강한 욕구를 드러내는 손짓을 했다. \"농담이라니!—하지만—아니, 침착하자, 침착해야지.\" 자신의 침착함을 입증하려는 듯, 포트 씨는 의자에 몸을 내던지고는 입에 거품을 물었다.\n\n\"이보시오, 친애하는 분.\" 윈클 씨가 끼어들었다.\n\n\"친애하는 분이라!\" 포트가 대꾸했다. \"감히 나를 친애하는 분이라 부르다니, 이 사람아? 감히 내 얼굴을 똑바로 보면서 그렇게 부른단 말이오?\"\n\n\"글쎄요, 그렇게 나오신다면,\" 윈클 씨가 받아쳤다. \"당신이야말로 어떻게 감히 내 얼굴을 똑바로 보면서 나를 뱀이라 부른단 말이오?\"\n\n\"네가 뱀이니까 그러지.\" 포트 씨가 대꾸했다.\n\n\"증명해 보시오.\" 윈클 씨가 발끈하여 말했다. \"증명해 보시라고요.\"\n\n편집장의 음흉한 얼굴 위로 악의에 찬 찡그림이 스쳐 갔다. 그는 주머니에서 그날 아침 자 인디펜던트를 꺼내, 특정 문단에 손가락을 짚고는, 그 신문을 탁자 너머 윈클 씨에게로 던졌다.\n\n윈클 씨는 그것을 집어 들고 다음과 같이 읽었다—\n\n\"우리의 보잘것없고 더러운 동업지는, 이 자치구에서 최근 치러진 선거에 대한 역겨운 논평 가운데, 감히 사생활의 신성한 영역을 침범하고, 오해의 여지가 없는 방식으로 우리의 지난번 후보—그렇다, 그의 비열한 패배에도 불구하고, 덧붙이건대 우리의 장차 의원이 되실 분, 피즈킨 씨의 사적인 일을 들먹였다. 비겁한 우리 동업지는 대체 무슨 속셈인가? 만일 우리가 저자처럼 사회적 교제의 예의를 헌신짝처럼 내던지고, 다행히 그자의 사생활을 세상의 조롱은 물론 세상의 저주로부터 가려 주고 있는 장막을 걷어 올린다면, 그 불한당은 무어라 하겠는가? 만일 우리가, 두더지 눈을 한 동업지를 빼고는 누구나 다 아는, 세상에 널리 알려진 사실과 정황을 들추어내어 논평한다면 어떻겠는가—만일 우리가 이 글의 첫머리를 쓰고 있을 때 재능 있는 한 동향인이자 기고가로부터 받은 다음의 일필을 인쇄한다면 어떻겠는가?\n\"'놋 단지에게 부치는 시구\n\n\"'오 포트여! 그대가 결혼의 종소리 딸랑이는 것을 들었을 때, 그녀가 얼마나 거짓될지 알았더라면, 그대는 그때 했으리라, 내 맹세코, 이제는 어쩔 수 없는 그 일을—'\"\n\n\"'딸랑'에\" 포트 씨가 엄숙하게 말했다—\"'딸랑'에 운이 맞는 말이 무엇이냐, 이 악당아?\"\n\n\"'딸랑'에 운이 맞는 말이라뇨?\" 마침 그 순간 들어선 포트 부인이 대답을 가로채며 말했다. \"'딸랑'에 운이 맞는 말이라고요? 어머, '윙클'이겠지요.\" 이렇게 말하며 포트 부인은 당혹스러워하는 픽윅 회원에게 다정하게 미소 짓고 손을 내밀었다. 마음이 흔들린 젊은이는 어찌할 바를 모른 채 그 손을 받았을 터인데, 포트가 분개하여 끼어들지 않았더라면 그러했을 것이다.\n\n\"물러서시오, 부인—물러서!\" 편집장이 말했다. \"내 코앞에서 저자의 손을 잡다니!\"\n\n\"여보!\" 깜짝 놀란 그의 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"한심한 여자 같으니, 여기를 보시오.\" 남편이 외쳤다. \"여기를 보란 말이오, 부인—'놋 단지에게 부치는 시구.' '놋 단지', 그게 바로 나요, 부인. '그녀가 얼마나 거짓될지', 그게 바로 당신이오, 부인—당신 말이오.\" 이렇게 분통을 터뜨리는데, 아내의 얼굴 표정을 보고는 어딘가 떨림 같은 것도 섞여 있었으니, 포트 씨는 이번 호 이턴스윌 인디펜던트를 그녀의 발치에 내동댕이쳤다.\n\n\"기가 막혀서, 여보.\" 깜짝 놀란 포트 부인이 신문을 집으려 몸을 굽히며 말했다. \"기가 막혀서, 정말!\"\n\n포트 씨는 아내의 경멸 어린 시선 아래 움찔했다. 그는 용기를 끌어올리려 필사적으로 안간힘을 썼으나, 그 용기는 다시 빠르게 풀려 나가고 있었다.\n\n이 짧은 한마디 \"기가 막혀서, 여보\"라는 말은, 글로만 읽으면 그리 대단해 보이지 않는다. 그러나 그 말이 전달된 목소리의 어조와 거기에 곁들여진 표정은, 둘 다 장차 포트의 머리 위에 내려질 어떤 복수를 가리키는 듯하여, 그에게 효력을 발휘했다. 아무리 서툰 관찰자라도 그의 어지러운 얼굴에서, 지금 이 순간 자신의 웰링턴 부츠를 신고 대신 서 주겠다고 할 만한 유능한 대역이 있다면 기꺼이 그 자리를 넘겨주고픈 마음을 읽어 낼 수 있었으리라.\n\n포트 부인은 그 문단을 읽고는 요란한 비명을 지르며 난로 앞 깔개 위에 길게 몸을 던지더니, 비명을 지르며 구두 뒤축으로 깔개를 두드려 댔는데, 그 모양새는 이 일에 대한 그녀의 감정이 지극히 정당하다는 것을 의심할 여지가 없게 만들었다.\n\n\"여보,\" 겁에 질린 포트가 말했다. \"내가 그걸 믿는다고는 안 했소—나는—\" 그러나 그 가엾은 사내의 목소리는 아내의 비명에 묻혀 버렸다.\n\n\"포트 부인, 부탁드립니다, 친애하는 부인, 진정하십시오.\" 윈클 씨가 말했다. 그러나 비명과 두드림은 그 어느 때보다 더 크고 더 잦아질 뿐이었다.\n\n\"여보,\" 포트 씨가 말했다. \"정말 미안하오. 당신 건강은 생각 안 하더라도, 나를 좀 생각해 주오, 여보. 집 주위에 사람들이 몰려들 거요.\" 그러나 포트 씨가 간절히 빌면 빌수록 비명은 더욱 격렬하게 쏟아져 나왔다.\n\n그런데 아주 다행스럽게도, 포트 부인에게는 한 사람으로 이루어진 호위병이 딸려 있었으니, 표면상 임무는 그녀의 몸단장을 맡아보는 것이었으나, 갖가지 방식으로 제 쓸모를 발휘하던 젊은 처자였다. 그중에서도 가장 두드러진 분야는, 불행한 포트의 바람에 어긋나는 안주인의 온갖 소망과 성향을 끊임없이 거들고 부추기는 일이었다. 비명 소리는 때를 맞추어 이 젊은 처자의 귀에 닿았고, 그녀를 어찌나 빠른 속도로 방 안으로 불러들였던지, 더없이 정교하게 매만진 모자와 곱슬머리가 크게 흐트러질 지경이었다.\n\n\"오, 친애하는, 친애하는 마님!\" 호위병이 엎드린 포트 부인 곁에 미친 듯이 무릎 꿇으며 외쳤다. \"오, 친애하는 마님, 무슨 일이세요?\"\n\n\"네 주인—저 잔인한 주인이.\" 환자가 중얼거렸다.\n\n포트는 누가 봐도 무너져 가고 있었다.\n\n\"너무하셨어요.\" 호위병이 나무라듯 말했다. \"저분이 마님을 잡고 말 거예요, 정말. 가엾은 마님 같으니!\"\n\n그는 더욱 무너졌다. 상대편은 공격을 밀어붙였다.\n\n\"오, 날 떠나지 마라—날 떠나지 마, 굿윈.\" 포트 부인이 히스테릭하게 움찔하며 굿윈의 손목을 움켜쥐고 중얼거렸다. \"내게 잘해 주는 사람은 너밖에 없어, 굿윈.\"\n\n이 가슴 뭉클한 호소에, 굿윈은 자기 나름의 자그마한 가정 비극을 한바탕 꾸며 내어 눈물을 펑펑 쏟았다.\n\n\"절대로요, 마님—절대로.\" 굿윈이 말했다. \"오, 나리, 조심하셔야 해요—정말로요. 마님께 얼마나 해를 끼치는지 모르시는 거예요. 언젠가 후회하실 거예요, 정말—제가 늘 그렇게 말씀드렸잖아요.\"\n\n운 없는 포트는 겁먹은 눈으로 바라보기만 할 뿐 아무 말도 하지 못했다.\n\n\"굿윈.\" 포트 부인이 부드러운 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"네, 마님.\" 굿윈이 말했다.\n\n\"내가 저 사람을 얼마나 사랑했는지 네가 알기만 한다면—\"\n\n\"그걸 떠올리며 마음 아파하지 마세요, 마님.\" 호위병이 말했다.\n\n포트는 몹시 겁에 질린 표정이었다. 그를 끝장낼 때가 되었다.\n\n\"그런데 이제,\" 포트 부인이 흐느꼈다. \"이제, 결국, 이런 식으로 취급당하다니. 제삼자가 보는 앞에서, 그것도 거의 낯선 이가 보는 앞에서 비난받고 모욕당하다니. 하지만 난 이런 일을 참지 않겠어! 굿윈,\" 포트 부인이 시중드는 처자의 품에서 몸을 일으키며 말을 이었다. \"내 오빠, 그 중위가 나설 거야. 난 별거하겠어, 굿윈!\"\n\n\"그래야 저분이 마땅한 벌을 받지요, 마님.\" 굿윈이 말했다.\n\n별거하겠다는 위협이 포트 씨의 마음속에 어떤 생각을 불러일으켰든, 그는 그것을 입 밖에 내기를 삼가고, 지극히 겸손하게 이렇게 말하는 것으로 만족했다—\n\n\"여보, 내 말 좀 들어 주겠소?\"\n\n새로운 흐느낌의 행렬만이 유일한 대답이었으니, 포트 부인은 더욱 히스테릭해져서 자신이 대체 왜 태어났는지 알려 달라고 청하고, 그와 비슷한 갖가지 정보들을 요구했다.\n\n\"여보,\" 포트 씨가 타일렀다. \"그런 예민한 감정에 빠지지 마오. 난 그 문단에 무슨 근거가 있다고는 결코 믿지 않았소, 여보—그럴 리가 없지. 난 그저 화가 났을 뿐이오, 여보—격분했다고 해도 좋소—감히 그걸 실은 인디펜던트 사람들한테 말이오. 그뿐이오.\" 포트 씨는 이 사달의 무고한 원인을 향해 애원하는 눈길을 던졌으니, 마치 그 뱀 운운한 일에 대해서는 아무 말도 말아 달라고 간청하는 듯했다.\n\n\"그렇다면 어떤 조치를 취하셔서 명예를 회복하실 작정입니까?\" 포트가 용기를 잃는 것을 보고 도리어 용기를 얻은 윈클 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"오, 굿윈,\" 포트 부인이 말했다. \"저 사람이 인디펜던트 편집장을 채찍질하겠다는 거니—그렇다는 거니, 굿윈?\"\n\n\"쉿, 쉿, 마님. 부디 조용히 하세요.\" 호위병이 대답했다. \"마님이 바라신다면 분명 그러실 거예요, 마님.\"\n\n\"물론이지.\" 아내가 다시 발작을 일으킬 뚜렷한 징후를 보이자 포트가 말했다. \"당연히 그래야지.\"\n\n\"언제, 굿윈—언제?\" 발작을 일으킬지 말지 여전히 결정짓지 못한 채 포트 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"물론 당장이오.\" 포트 씨가 말했다. \"오늘이 가기 전에.\"\n\n\"오, 굿윈,\" 포트 부인이 다시 입을 열었다. \"그게 그 중상에 맞서고 세상 앞에서 내 결백을 밝히는 유일한 길이야.\"\n\n\"물론이죠, 마님.\" 굿윈이 대답했다. \"사내대장부라면 누구도 그걸 마다할 수 없지요, 마님.\"\n\n그리하여 히스테리가 여전히 주위를 맴돌고 있었으므로, 포트 씨는 다시 한 번 그렇게 하겠노라 말했다. 그러나 포트 부인은 단 한 번이라도 의심받았다는 생각만으로도 어찌나 압도되었던지, 예닐곱 번이나 재발 직전에까지 이르렀고, 부지런한 굿윈의 지칠 줄 모르는 노력과 굴복한 포트의 거듭된 용서 간청이 없었더라면 영락없이 발작을 일으켰을 것이다. 마침내 그 가엾은 사내가 겁먹고 핀잔을 들으며 제자리로 눌려 내려간 뒤에야 포트 부인은 회복되었고, 그들은 조찬을 들러 갔다.\n\n\"이 비열한 신문 중상 때문에 이곳 체류를 줄이지는 않으실 거지요, 윈클 씨?\" 눈물 자국 사이로 미소 지으며 포트 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"그러지 않으시길 바라오.\" 포트 씨가 말했는데, 말하는 그 순간 그의 마음은, 방문객이 마침 입가로 들어 올리던 마른 토스트 조각에 목이 막혀 그렇게 체류를 확실히 끝내 버렸으면 하는 바람에 사로잡혀 있었다.\n\"그러지 않으시길 바라오.\"\n\n\"친절하시군요.\" 윈클 씨가 말했다. \"하지만 픽윅 씨로부터 편지가 한 통 왔습니다—오늘 아침 침실 문 앞으로 전해진 터프먼 씨의 쪽지로 알게 된 일인데—그분이 오늘 베리에서 합류하자고 청하셨더군요. 정오에 마차로 떠나야 합니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 돌아오시겠지요?\" 포트 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"오, 물론이지요.\" 윈클 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"정말 확실하지요?\" 포트 부인이 방문객에게 다정한 눈길을 슬쩍 던지며 말했다.\n\n\"확실합니다.\" 윈클 씨가 대답했다.\n\n조찬은 침묵 속에 지나갔으니, 일행 저마다 자신의 개인적인 불만을 곱씹고 있었기 때문이다. 포트 부인은 멋진 사내를 잃는 것을 애석해했고, 포트 씨는 인디펜던트를 채찍질하겠다는 경솔한 맹세를, 윈클 씨는 자신이 무고하게 이토록 거북한 처지에 놓인 것을 한탄했다. 정오가 다가왔고, 숱한 작별 인사와 돌아오겠다는 약속을 남긴 뒤, 그는 가까스로 몸을 떼어 떠났다.\n\n'저자가 다시 돌아오기만 하면, 독을 먹여 버리겠어.' 포트 씨는 자신의 벼락을 벼리는 자그마한 뒷사무실로 들어서며 생각했다.\n\n'내가 다시 돌아와서 이 사람들과 또 얽히게 된다면,' 윈클 씨는 피콕 여관으로 발걸음을 옮기며 생각했다. '나야말로 채찍질당해 마땅하지—그뿐이야.'\n\n그의 친구들은 채비를 마쳤고, 마차도 거의 채비가 되어 있었으며, 반 시간 뒤 그들은 길을 따라 여정에 올랐으니, 그 길은 픽윅 씨와 샘이 얼마 전에 지나온 바로 그 길이었다. 그 길에 대해서는 이미 어느 정도 이야기한 바 있으므로, 스노드그래스 씨의 시적이고 아름다운 묘사를 굳이 옮겨야 한다고는 느끼지 않는다.\n\n웰러 씨가 엔젤 여관 문간에 서서 그들을 맞을 채비를 하고 있었고, 그 신사의 안내로 그들은 픽윅 씨의 방으로 들어갔는데, 거기서 윈클 씨와 스노드그래스 씨는 적잖이 놀랐고 터프먼 씨는 적잖이 당황했으니, 늙은 와들과 트런들이 와 있었던 것이다.\n\n\"잘들 지냈는가?\" 노인이 터프먼 씨의 손을 잡으며 말했다. \"뒤로 빼지도 말고, 그 일로 감상에 젖지도 말게. 어쩔 수 없는 일이었네, 이 사람아. 그녀를 위해서는 자네가 그녀를 얻었더라면 싶지만, 자네 자신을 위해서는 그러지 않은 게 천만다행일세. 자네 같은 젊은이는 머잖아 더 나은 사람을 만날 게야, 안 그런가?\" 이렇게 말을 맺으며 와들은 터프먼 씨의 등을 철썩 치고 호탕하게 웃었다.\n\n\"그래, 자네들은 어떤가, 멋진 친구들?\" 노신사가 윈클 씨와 스노드그래스 씨와 동시에 악수를 나누며 말했다. \"방금 픽윅한테 말하던 참인데, 크리스마스에는 자네들 모두 우리 집에 내려와야 하네. 결혼식이 있을 거야—이번에는 진짜 결혼식이지.\"\n\n\"결혼식이라뇨!\" 스노드그래스 씨가 새파랗게 질려 외쳤다.\n\n\"그래, 결혼식. 하지만 놀라지 말게.\" 마음씨 좋은 노인이 말했다. \"저기 저 트런들과 벨라뿐이니까.\"\n\n\"오, 그게 다입니까?\" 스노드그래스 씨가, 그의 가슴을 무겁게 짓누르던 괴로운 의심에서 벗어나 말했다. \"축하드립니다. 조는 어떻습니까?\"\n\n\"아주 잘 지내네.\" 노신사가 대답했다. \"여전히 잠만 자지.\"\n\n\"어머님과 목사님, 그리고 다들 어떠십니까?\"\n\n\"다들 잘 지내네.\"\n\n\"그,\" 터프먼 씨가 안간힘을 쓰며 말했다—\"그분은 어디 계십니까, 어르신?\" 그러고는 고개를 돌리며 손으로 두 눈을 가렸다.\n\n\"그분이라!\" 노신사가 무언가 알겠다는 듯 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 말했다. \"내 결혼 안 한 친척을 말하는 건가—응?\"\n\n터프먼 씨는 고갯짓으로, 자신의 물음이 실연당한 레이철에게 향한 것임을 알렸다.\n\n\"오, 그 사람은 떠났네.\" 노신사가 말했다. \"멀찍이 떨어진 친척 집에서 살고 있지. 처녀들 보는 걸 견디지 못하길래 내가 보내 줬네. 하지만 자, 저녁 식사가 나왔구먼. 마차를 타고 왔으니 다들 시장할 게야. 나는 마차를 타지 않고도 시장하니, 어서들 들세나.\"\n\n식사는 충분히 대접받았고, 식사가 치워진 뒤 그들이 탁자에 둘러앉자, 픽윅 씨는 추종자들이 격렬한 공포와 분노에 휩싸이는 가운데, 자신이 겪은 모험과 그 악마 같은 징글의 비열한 술책이 거둔 성공을 이야기했다.\n\n\"그리고 그 정원에서 걸린 류머티즘 발작으로,\" 픽윅 씨가 끝맺으며 말했다. \"지금 이 순간에도 다리를 절고 있다네.\"\n\n\"저도 모험이라 할 만한 일을 좀 겪었습니다.\" 윈클 씨가 미소 지으며 말했다. 그러고는 픽윅 씨의 청에 따라, 이턴스윌 인디펜던트의 악의에 찬 명예 훼손과 그로 인한 그들의 친구, 그 편집장의 흥분을 자세히 들려주었다.\n\n이야기가 진행되는 동안 픽윅 씨의 이마가 어두워졌다. 그의 친구들도 그것을 알아채고, 윈클 씨가 이야기를 마치자 깊은 침묵을 지켰다. 픽윅 씨는 꽉 쥔 주먹으로 탁자를 힘주어 내리치고는 다음과 같이 말했다—\n\n\"이 어찌 놀라운 노릇이 아닌가.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"우리는 어느 집에 들든 그 주인을 얼마간 곤경에 끌어들이지 않고는 못 배기는 운명을 타고난 듯하니 말일세. 묻건대, 내 추종자들이 어떤 지붕 아래 머무르든 어느 순진한 여인의 마음의 평화와 행복을 어지럽히고 마는 것은, 분별없음을, 아니 그보다 더 나쁘게는—내가 이런 말을 하다니!—마음의 시커멈을 드러내는 것이 아닌가? 이것이야말로—\"\n\n픽윅 씨는 십중팔구 한참을 더 늘어놓았을 터인데, 샘이 편지 한 통을 들고 들어서는 바람에 그 웅변적인 일장 연설을 중단하게 되었다. 그는 손수건으로 이마를 닦고, 안경을 벗어 닦은 뒤 다시 쓰고는, 여느 때의 부드러운 어조를 되찾은 목소리로 말했다—\n\n\"거기 든 게 무엇인가, 샘?\"\n\n\"방금 우체국에 들렀더니 이 편지가 이틀이나 놓여 있더군요.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다. \"봉랍 대신 풀로 봉해져 있고, 둥근 글씨로 수신인이 적혀 있습니다.\"\n\n\"이 글씨는 모르겠는데.\" 픽윅 씨가 편지를 열며 말했다. \"맙소사! 이게 무슨 일인가? 농담이렷다. 이건—이건—사실일 리가 없어.\"\n\n\"무슨 일입니까?\" 모두가 물었다.\n\n\"누가 죽기라도 한 건 아니지요?\" 픽윅 씨의 얼굴에 떠오른 공포에 놀란 와들이 말했다.\n\n픽윅 씨는 아무 대답도 하지 않고, 탁자 너머로 편지를 밀어 놓으며 터프먼 씨에게 큰 소리로 읽어 달라고 청하고는, 보기에도 섬뜩할 만큼 멍하니 놀란 표정으로 의자에 털썩 기댔다.\n\n터프먼 씨가 떨리는 목소리로 편지를 읽었으니, 그 사본은 다음과 같다—\n\n콘힐, 프리먼스 코트, 1827년 8월 28일.\n\n바델 대 픽윅 건.\n귀하께,\n\n저희는 마사 바델 부인으로부터 혼인 약속 위반에 대하여 귀하를 상대로 소송을 제기하라는 위임을 받았으며, 원고는 그 손해액을 천오백 파운드로 산정하고 있습니다. 이에 본 소송에 관하여 민사 법원에서 귀하를 상대로 영장이 발부되었음을 알려 드리며, 그 송달을 접수할 런던 측 귀하의 대리인 이름을 회신 편으로 알려 주시기를 청하는 바입니다.\n\n귀하의 충실한 종복, 도드슨 앤드 포그 올림.\n\n새뮤얼 픽윅 귀하.\n\n저마다 옆 사람을 바라보고, 또 모두가 픽윅 씨를 바라보던 그 말없는 경악에는 어딘가 깊은 인상을 주는 데가 있어, 다들 입을 떼기를 두려워하는 듯했다. 마침내 그 침묵을 깬 것은 터프먼 씨였다.\n\n\"도드슨 앤드 포그.\" 그가 기계적으로 되뇌었다.\n\n\"바델 대 픽윅.\" 스노드그래스 씨가 골똘히 생각하며 말했다.\n\n\"순진한 여인의 마음의 평화와 행복.\" 윈클 씨가 넋이 나간 듯 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"음모일세.\" 마침내 말할 기운을 되찾은 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"탐욕스러운 저 두 변호사, 도드슨과 포그가 꾸민 비열한 음모야. 바델 부인은 결코 이런 짓을 할 리가 없네—그럴 마음도 없고—그럴 만한 사정도 없어. 우습군—우스워.\"\n\n\"그녀의 마음에 대해서야,\" 와들이 미소 지으며 말했다. \"분명 자네가 가장 잘 아는 판관이겠지. 자네 기를 꺾고 싶지는 않네만, 그녀의 사정에 대해서라면, 도드슨과 포그가 우리 누구보다도 훨씬 더 잘 아는 판관이라고 분명히 말할 수 있겠네.\"\n\n\"돈을 뜯어내려는 비열한 수작일세.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그러길 바라네.\" 와들이 짧고 메마른 기침을 하며 말했다.\n\n\"하숙인이 집주인 여자에게 건넬 법한 말 말고, 내가 그녀에게 다른 식으로 말하는 걸 들은 사람이 누가 있단 말인가?\" 픽윅 씨가 무서운 기세로 말을 이었다. \"내가 그녀와 함께 있는 걸 본 사람이 누가 있나? 여기 내 친구들조차도—\"\n\n\"한 번 빼고요.\" 터프먼 씨가 말했다.\n\n픽윅 씨의 얼굴빛이 변했다.\n\n\"아하,\" 와들 씨가 말했다. \"음, 그건 중요하군. 그때 의심스러운 점은 없었겠지?\"\n\n터프먼 씨가 겁먹은 눈으로 자신의 지도자를 슬쩍 보았다. \"글쎄요,\" 그가 말했다. \"의심스러운 점은 없었습니다. 하지만—어쩌다 그렇게 됐는지는 모르겠는데, 아셔야 할 게—그분이 분명 그녀를 품에 안고 있었습니다.\"\n\n\"맙소사!\" 픽윅 씨가 문제의 그 장면이 또렷이 떠오르자 부르짖었다. \"정황의 위력이 빚어낸 이 무서운 본보기라니! 그래, 그랬지—그랬어.\"\n\n\"그리고 우리의 친구께서는 그녀의 고뇌를 달래 주고 계셨고요.\" 윈클 씨가 다소 짓궂게 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 그랬네.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"부인하지 않겠네. 그래, 그랬어.\"\n\n\"이런!\" 와들이 말했다. \"의심스러운 점이라곤 없는 사건치고는, 이거 영 수상쩍어 보이는데—안 그런가, 픽윅? 아하, 능구렁이 같으니—능구렁이!\" 그러고는 식기대 위의 잔들이 다시 울리도록 한바탕 웃어 댔다.\n\n\"이 무슨 끔찍한 정황의 결합이란 말인가!\" 픽윅 씨가 두 손에 턱을 괴며 외쳤다. \"윈클—터프먼—방금 내가 한 말은 사과하겠네. 우리는 모두 정황의 희생양이고, 그중에서도 내가 가장 큰 희생양일세.\" 이렇게 사과하고 픽윅 씨는 두 손에 얼굴을 묻고 깊은 생각에 잠겼으며, 와들은 좌중의 다른 회원들을 향해 고갯짓과 윙크를 한 바퀴 빙 돌려 보냈다.\n\n\"하지만 기어이 해명을 듣고야 말겠네.\" 픽윅 씨가 고개를 들고 탁자를 두드리며 말했다. \"이 도드슨인지 포그인지를 만나 보겠어! 내일 런던으로 가겠네.\"\n\n\"내일은 안 되네.\" 와들이 말했다. \"다리를 너무 절잖나.\"\n\n\"그럼, 그다음 날.\"\n\n\"그다음 날은 9월 1일이고, 자네는 우리와 함께 적어도 제프리 매닝 경의 영지까지는 나가기로 약속했네. 사냥에 나서지 않더라도 점심때 우리와 만나기로 했고.\"\n\n\"그럼, 그 다음다음 날.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"목요일에.—샘!\"\n\n\"네, 어르신.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"목요일 아침에 런던행 마차 바깥 자리 두 개를 잡아 주게, 자네와 나 몫으로.\"\n\n\"잘 알겠습니다, 어르신.\"\n\n웰러 씨는 방을 나가, 두 손을 주머니에 찌르고 두 눈을 땅에 박은 채, 느릿느릿 심부름을 하러 떠났다.\n\n\"별난 양반이라니까, 우리 황제 폐하께서는.\" 웰러 씨가 거리를 천천히 걸어 올라가며 말했다. \"저 바델 부인한테 수작을 걸다니—게다가 어린 사내애까지 딸린 여자한테 말이야! 하지만 늘 이런 식이지, 보기엔 점잖고 진중한 이런 늙은 양반들이 말이야. 그래도 그분이 그런 짓을 했을 줄은 몰랐는데—정말 그랬을 줄은 몰랐어!\" 이런 투로 도덕적 사색을 펼치며, 새뮤얼 웰러 씨는 마차 예약소를 향해 발걸음을 옮겼다.\n\n# CHAPTER XIX. A PLEASANT DAY WITH AN UNPLEASANT TERMINATION\n\n새들은 — 다행히도 제 마음의 평화와 몸의 안락을 위하여 — 자기들을 놀라게 하려고 마련된 채비를 까맣게 모르는 복된 무지 속에서, 9월 첫날을 분명 그 계절에 보아 온 가장 상쾌한 아침의 하나로 반겼을 것이다. 그루터기 사이를 젊음의 온갖 까다로운 멋부림으로 우쭐대며 거닐던 어린 자고새도 많았고, 그 경박함을 지혜와 경험을 갖춘 새다운 경멸의 태도로 작고 동그란 눈으로 지켜보던 나이 든 자고새도 많았으나, 다 같이 다가오는 운명을 모른 채 발랄하고 명랑한 기분으로 싱그러운 아침 공기 속에 몸을 쬐다가, 몇 시간 뒤에는 땅바닥에 쓰러져 버렸다. 그러나 우리가 너무 감상에 젖고 있다. 이야기를 계속하자.\n\n그렇다면 평범하고 꾸밈없는 사실대로 말하건대, 화창한 아침이었다 — 어찌나 화창한지 영국 여름의 그 몇 달이 벌써 지나갔다고는 도저히 믿기 어려울 정도였다. 산울타리며 들판이며 나무며, 언덕과 황야가, 짙고 그윽한 초록의 끊임없이 변하는 색조를 눈앞에 펼쳐 보였다. 떨어진 잎은 거의 없었고, 여름의 빛깔에 섞여 가을이 시작되었음을 알려 줄 노란 기운도 거의 흩뿌려져 있지 않았다. 하늘은 구름 한 점 없었고, 해는 밝고 따뜻하게 빛났다. 새들의 노래와 무수한 여름 벌레들의 윙윙거림이 공기를 가득 채웠고, 온갖 그윽하고 아름다운 빛깔의 꽃들로 빼곡한 오두막 정원들은 짙은 이슬 속에서 반짝이는 보석을 깔아 놓은 화단처럼 반짝였다. 모든 것이 여름의 도장을 찍고 있었고, 그 아름다운 빛깔 가운데 어느 하나도 아직 그 색판에서 바래지 않았다.\n\n이런 아침에, 픽윅 회원 셋(스노드그래스 씨는 집에 남기를 택했다)과 워들 씨와 트런들 씨가 탄 무개 마차가, 마부 옆 좌석에 샘 웰러를 앉히고서, 길가의 문 옆에 멈춰 섰다. 그 문 앞에는 키 크고 뼈만 앙상한 사냥터지기 한 명과, 반장화에 가죽 각반을 두른 소년 하나가 서 있었는데, 둘 다 넉넉한 크기의 자루를 하나씩 메고 포인터 두 마리를 데리고 있었다.\n\n\"이봐요,\" 사내가 발판을 내리자 윈클 씨가 워들에게 속삭였다. \"설마 우리가 저 자루를 채울 만큼 사냥감을 잡으리라고 생각하는 건 아니겠죠?\"\n\n\"채운다고!\" 워들 영감이 외쳤다. \"원, 그럼 채우고말고! 자네가 하나 채우고, 내가 다른 하나를 채울 걸세. 그러고도 다 채우고 나면 우리 사냥 재킷 주머니에도 그만큼 더 들어갈 테고.\"\n\n윈클 씨는 이 말에 아무 대꾸도 하지 않고 마차에서 내렸으나, 속으로는 만일 일행이 자기가 자루 하나를 채울 때까지 바깥 공기 속에 머물러 있어야 한다면 머리에 고뿔이 들 가능성이 상당하리라고 생각했다.\n\n\"이리 와, 주노, 아가야 — 이리 와, 늙은 것아. 엎드려, 다프, 엎드려.\" 워들이 개들을 어루만지며 말했다. \"제프리 경께서는 아직 스코틀랜드에 계시겠지, 마틴?\"\n\n키 큰 사냥터지기는 그렇다고 대답하면서, 마치 코트 주머니가 방아쇠 당기는 수고를 덜어 주기를 바라기라도 하는 듯 총을 쥐고 있는 윈클 씨로부터, 마치 총을 무서워하는 듯이 — 정말로 그랬다는 데 의심할 여지가 조금도 없거니와 — 총을 쥐고 있는 터프먼 씨에게로, 다소 놀란 듯 눈길을 옮겼다.\n\n\"내 친구들은 아직 이런 일에 익숙지가 않다네, 마틴.\" 워들이 그 눈길을 알아채고 말했다. \"살아가며 배우는 거지, 안 그런가. 머지않아 명사수가 될 걸세. 그래도 내 친구 윈클에게는 미안하군. 이 사람은 연습을 좀 했거든.\"\n\n윈클 씨는 그 칭찬을 받아들인다는 뜻으로 파란 목도리 너머로 힘없이 미소 지었는데, 겸손한 당혹감에 어찌나 알 수 없이 총과 뒤엉켜 버렸던지, 만일 총에 탄알이 장전되어 있었더라면 그 자리에서 자기 몸을 쏘아 즉사하고 말았을 것임에 틀림없었다.\n\n\"총알을 재고 나면 총을 그딴 식으로 다루시면 안 됩니다요, 나리.\" 키 큰 사냥터지기가 퉁명스레 말했다. \"안 그러면 영락없이 우리 중 누구 하나를 싸늘한 고깃덩이로 만들어 놓고 마실 게요.\"\n\n이렇게 주의를 받은 윈클 씨는 황급히 자세를 바꾸었는데, 그러는 와중에 총신을 웰러 씨의 머리에 제법 세게 부딪치고 말았다.\n\n\"이런!\" 샘이 떨어진 모자를 주워 들고 관자놀이를 문지르며 말했다. \"이런, 나리! 이런 식으로 가시다간 한 발에 저 자루 중 하나는 채우고도 남겠습니다요.\"\n\n이 말에 가죽 각반을 두른 소년이 아주 진심으로 웃어 댔다가는, 마치 웃은 게 딴 사람인 양 시치미를 떼려 애썼고, 그 바람에 윈클 씨가 위엄 있게 눈살을 찌푸렸다.\n\n\"그 소년더러 어디로 새참을 가지고 우리한테 오라고 일렀나, 마틴?\" 워들이 물었다.\n\n\"외나무 언덕 기슭으로, 열두 시에 오라 했습니다요, 나리.\"\n\n\"거긴 제프리 경의 땅은 아니지, 그렇지?\"\n\n\"네, 나리. 하지만 바로 옆이지요. 볼드위그 대위의 땅인데, 거기선 우릴 방해할 사람도 없고, 잔디밭도 썩 좋습니다요.\"\n\n\"좋아.\" 워들 영감이 말했다. \"자, 이제 빨리 떠날수록 좋겠군. 그럼 열두 시에 우리한테 합류하겠나, 픽윅?\"\n\n픽윅 씨는 그 사냥을 구경하고 싶은 마음이 각별했는데, 특히나 윈클 씨의 목숨과 팔다리가 다소 걱정스러웠기 때문이다. 게다가 이토록 마음을 끄는 아침에 돌아서서 친구들이 즐기도록 내버려 두는 것은 무척이나 애가 타는 일이었다. 그래서 그는 몹시 서글픈 표정으로 대답했다.\n\n\"글쎄, 그래야겠지요.\"\n\n\"저 신사분은 사냥꾼이 아니십니까요, 나리?\" 키 큰 사냥터지기가 물었다.\n\n\"아닐세.\" 워들이 대답했다. \"게다가 다리도 절고.\"\n\n\"나도 정말 가고 싶은데요.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"정말이지.\"\n\n잠시 동정 어린 침묵이 흘렀다.\n\n\"산울타리 저편에 외바퀴 손수레가 하나 있습니다요.\" 소년이 말했다. \"저 신사분의 하인이 길을 따라 그걸 밀고 오면, 우리 가까이 따라올 수 있고요, 층계 디딤대 같은 데선 우리가 들어 넘겨 드리면 됩지요.\"\n\n\"바로 그거다.\" 웰러 씨가 말했는데, 그도 사냥을 보고 싶은 마음이 간절했던 터라 이해관계가 걸린 당사자였다. \"바로 그거야. 잘 말했다, 볼따구. 내 금방 끌어내 오마.\"\n\n그러나 여기서 난관이 생겼다. 키 큰 사냥터지기가, 외바퀴 손수레에 신사를 태워 사냥 행렬에 끼워 넣는 것은 확립된 온갖 규칙과 선례를 노골적으로 위반하는 짓이라며 단호히 반대하고 나선 것이다.\n\n큰 반대였으나, 넘지 못할 것은 아니었다. 사냥터지기를 어르고 돈도 좀 쥐여 주고, 게다가 그 기계를 쓰자고 맨 처음 제안한 발명심 강한 젊은 녀석의 머리통을 \"쥐어박아\" 속을 좀 푼 뒤에, 픽윅 씨는 그 수레에 앉혀졌고, 일행은 출발했다. 워들과 키 큰 사냥터지기가 앞장서고, 손수레에 탄 픽윅 씨를 샘이 밀며 후미를 맡았다.\n\n\"멈춰, 샘.\" 첫 번째 들판을 절반쯤 가로질렀을 때 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"이번엔 또 뭐요?\" 워들이 말했다.\n\n\"이 손수레가 한 발짝도 더 움직이는 걸 두고 보지 않겠습니다.\" 픽윅 씨가 단호히 말했다. \"윈클이 저 총을 다른 식으로 들지 않는 한은요.\"\n\n\"그럼 대체 어떻게 들라는 거요?\" 가엾은 윈클이 말했다.\n\n\"총구를 땅으로 향하게 들어요.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"그건 영 사냥꾼답지 못한데요.\" 윈클이 따졌다.\n\n\"사냥꾼답건 말건 난 상관 안 합니다.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다. \"남들 보기 좋자고, 누구 비위 맞추자고 외바퀴 수레에서 총 맞아 죽을 생각은 없으니까요.\"\n\n\"저 신사분이 끝장 보기 전에 누구한테든 그 총알을 박아 넣고 말 게라니까요.\" 키 큰 사내가 으르렁거리듯 말했다.\n\n\"알았어요, 알았어 — 난 상관없어요.\" 가엾은 윈클이 개머리판을 위로 가게 돌려 들며 말했다. \"자, 됐죠.\"\n\n\"조용히 살자면 뭔들 못 하겠습니까요.\" 웰러 씨가 말했다. 그리고 그들은 다시 나아갔다.\n\n\"멈춰!\" 몇 야드 더 갔을 때 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"이번엔 또 뭐요?\" 워들이 말했다.\n\n\"터프먼의 저 총은 안전하지가 않아요. 분명 안전하지 않다니까요.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"네? 뭐라고요! 안전하지 않다고요?\" 터프먼 씨가 몹시 놀란 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"당신이 들고 있는 그 꼴로는요.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"또 이의를 제기해서 정말 미안하지만, 윈클이 자기 총을 드는 식으로 당신도 들지 않는 한, 더 가는 데 동의할 수 없어요.\"\n\n\"그러시는 게 좋겠는뎁쇼, 나리.\" 키 큰 사냥터지기가 말했다. \"안 그러면 무엇보다도 당신 자신한테 그 총알을 박을 공산이 큽니다요.\"\n\n터프먼 씨는 더없이 친절하고 급하게 자기 총을 요구받은 자세로 옮겼고, 일행은 다시 나아갔다. 두 아마추어는, 왕실 장례식의 사병 두 명처럼 총을 거꾸로 멘 채 행진했다.\n\n개들이 갑자기 딱 멈춰 섰고, 일행도 살금살금 한 발짝 나아갔다가 역시 멈춰 섰다.\n\n\"저 개들 다리가 왜 저래요?\" 윈클 씨가 속삭였다. \"서 있는 꼴이 어찌 저리 이상하담.\"\n\n\"쉿, 좀 가만히 못 있겠나?\" 워들이 나직이 대답했다. \"안 보이나, 저것들이 포인트를 잡고 있는 거잖나?\"\n\n\"포인트를 잡는다고요!\" 윈클 씨가, 마치 저 영리한 짐승들이 특별히 주목을 끌고 있는 무슨 빼어난 풍경이라도 발견하리라 기대하는 양 사방을 두리번거리며 말했다. \"포인트를 잡는다니! 뭘 가리키고 있다는 거예요?\"\n\n\"눈 똑바로 떠.\" 워들이 그 순간의 흥분으로 질문에는 아랑곳하지 않고 말했다. \"자, 간다.\"\n\n날카롭게 푸드덕거리는 소리가 나, 윈클 씨가 마치 자기가 총에 맞기라도 한 양 흠칫 물러섰다. 탕, 탕, 총 두 자루가 울렸다 — 연기가 들판 위로 재빨리 휩쓸려 가더니 공중으로 말려 올랐다.\n\n\"어디 있어요!\" 윈클 씨가 잔뜩 흥분한 상태로, 사방으로 빙글빙글 돌며 말했다. \"어디 있냐고요? 언제 쏘면 되는지 말해 줘요. 어디 있어요 — 어디 있냐고요?\"\n\n\"어디 있냐고!\" 워들이 개들이 제 발치에 물어다 놓은 새 두 마리를 집어 들며 말했다. \"원, 여기 있잖나.\"\n\n\"아니, 아니요. 난 다른 것들 말이에요.\" 어리둥절해진 윈클이 말했다.\n\n\"지금쯤이면 멀찌감치 갔겠지.\" 워들이 태연히 총을 다시 장전하며 대답했다.\n\n\"오 분쯤 뒤면 십중팔구 또 한 무리를 만나게 될 겁니다요.\" 키 큰 사냥터지기가 말했다. \"저 신사분이 지금부터 쏘기 시작하시면, 새들이 날아오를 때쯤에는 총구에서 막 탄알이 나올 겝니다요.\"\n\n\"하! 하! 하!\" 웰러 씨가 폭소를 터뜨렸다.\n\n\"샘.\" 픽윅 씨가 자기 하인의 혼란과 당혹을 가엾이 여기며 말했다.\n\"네, 나리.\"\n\n\"웃지 마라.\"\n\n\"여부가 있겠습니까요, 나리.\" 그래서 웰러 씨는 그 보상으로, 외바퀴 손수레 뒤에 숨어 오로지 각반을 두른 소년만 즐기라고 얼굴을 일그러뜨려 보였고, 소년은 이에 요란하게 웃음을 터뜨렸다가, 제 즐거움을 감추려고 돌아설 핑계가 필요했던 키 큰 사냥터지기에게 즉결로 한 대 쥐어박혔다.\n\n\"잘했네, 이 친구!\" 워들이 터프먼 씨에게 말했다. \"어쨌든 이번엔 발사는 했군그래.\"\n\n\"오, 그럼요.\" 터프먼 씨가 흐뭇한 자부심으로 대답했다. \"쏘아 버렸지요.\"\n\n\"잘했어. 다음번엔 눈 똑바로 뜨면 뭐라도 맞힐 걸세. 아주 쉽지, 안 그런가?\"\n\n\"네, 아주 쉽군요.\" 터프먼 씨가 말했다. \"그런데 어깨가 어찌 이리 아픈지. 하마터면 뒤로 나자빠질 뻔했어요. 이런 작은 총이 이렇게 반동이 셀 줄은 몰랐네요.\"\n\n\"아.\" 영감이 미소 지으며 말했다. \"차차 익숙해질 걸세. 자, 그럼 — 다들 준비됐나 — 거기 손수레는 괜찮나?\"\n\n\"괜찮습니다요, 나리.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"그럼 가세.\"\n\n\"꽉 잡으십쇼, 나리.\" 샘이 손수레를 들어 올리며 말했다.\n\n\"아무렴, 아무렴.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다. 그리고 그들은 더없이 활기차게 나아갔다.\n\n\"이제 그 손수레는 뒤에 세워 두게.\" 손수레가 층계 디딤대를 넘어 또 다른 들판으로 들어 올려지고 픽윅 씨가 다시 그 안에 앉혀진 뒤, 워들이 외쳤다.\n\n\"알겠습니다요, 나리.\" 웰러 씨가 멈춰 서며 대답했다.\n\n\"자, 윈클.\" 영감이 말했다. \"조용히 날 따라오게. 그리고 이번엔 너무 늦지 말고.\"\n\n\"걱정 마세요.\" 윈클 씨가 말했다. \"포인트를 잡고 있나요?\"\n\n\"아니, 아니. 지금은 아닐세. 자, 조용히, 조용히.\" 그들은 살금살금 기어 나아갔고, 정말 아주 조용히 전진했을 텐데, 다만 윈클 씨가 자기 총으로 몹시 복잡한 동작을 하던 중, 가장 결정적인 순간에 어쩌다 그만 소년의 머리 위로 — 만일 그 키 큰 사내가 거기 대신 있었다면 바로 그의 골이 있었을 자리로 — 총을 쏘아 버리고 말았다.\n\n\"아니, 대체 왜 그런 짓을 한 건가?\" 새들이 다친 데 없이 날아가 버리자 워들 영감이 말했다.\n\n\"내 평생 이런 총은 처음 봅니다.\" 가엾은 윈클 씨가, 마치 그래 봐야 무슨 소용이라도 있는 양 격발 장치를 들여다보며 대답했다. \"제멋대로 발사돼 버려요. 자꾸 그런다니까요.\"\n\n\"자꾸 그런다고!\" 워들이 다소 짜증 섞인 투로 되받았다. \"그놈이 제멋대로 뭐라도 좀 죽여 주면 좋겠구먼.\"\n\n\"머지않아 그럴 겝니다요, 나리.\" 키 큰 사내가 나직이 예언하는 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"그 말이 무슨 뜻이오, 응?\" 윈클 씨가 화를 내며 물었다.\n\n\"신경 쓰지 마십쇼, 나리, 신경 쓰지 마세요.\" 키 큰 사냥터지기가 대답했다. \"전 제 식솔도 없는 사람입니다요, 나리. 그리고 이 소년의 어미야, 만일 이 녀석이 제프리 경의 땅에서 죽으면 경한테서 후한 보상을 받을 테고요. 다시 장전하십쇼, 나리, 다시 장전하시라고요.\"\n\n\"저 사람 총을 치워 버려요.\" 키 큰 사내의 음산한 암시에 질겁한 픽윅 씨가 손수레에서 외쳤다. \"저 사람 총을 치우란 말이오, 안 들려요, 누구든?\"\n\n그러나 아무도 그 명령에 자청해서 따르지 않았고, 윈클 씨는 픽윅 씨에게 반항하는 눈길을 한 번 쏘아 던진 뒤, 자기 총을 다시 장전하고는 나머지 일행과 함께 앞으로 나아갔다.\n\n우리는 픽윅 씨의 권위에 의거하여, 터프먼 씨의 처신 방식이 윈클 씨가 취한 것보다 훨씬 더 신중하고 사려 깊었음을 밝히지 않을 수 없다. 그렇다고 이것이 사냥에 관련된 모든 일에 있어 후자 신사가 지닌 대단한 권위를 결코 깎아내리는 것은 아니다. 왜냐하면, 픽윅 씨가 멋지게 지적하듯, 까마득한 옛날부터 어쩐 일인지, 이론상으로는 학문의 완전한 등불이라 할 더없이 훌륭하고 유능한 철학자들 가운데 많은 이들이 그것을 실천으로 옮기는 데는 전혀 무력했던 일이 곧잘 있어 왔기 때문이다.\n\n터프먼 씨의 방식은, 우리의 가장 숭고한 발견들 가운데 많은 것이 그러하듯, 지극히 단순했다. 천재다운 기민함과 통찰력으로, 그는 도달해야 할 두 가지 큰 요점을 단번에 간파했던 것이다 — 첫째는 자기 자신을 다치지 않게 하면서 총을 발사하는 것이요, 둘째는 그러면서도 곁에 선 이들에게 위험을 끼치지 않는 것이었다. 분명히, 총을 쏜다는 난관 자체를 넘어선 다음에 할 수 있는 최선책은, 두 눈을 꼭 감고 공중을 향해 쏘는 것이었다.\n\n한번은 이 묘기를 펼친 뒤 터프먼 씨가 눈을 떠 보니, 통통한 자고새 한 마리가 부상을 입고 땅으로 떨어지는 참이었다. 그는 워들 씨에게 그 변함없는 성공을 축하해 주려던 참이었는데, 바로 그 신사가 그에게로 다가와 그의 손을 따뜻하게 움켜쥐는 것이었다.\n\n\"터프먼.\" 영감이 말했다. \"자네가 바로 저 새를 골라서 쐈지?\"\n\n\"아니에요.\" 터프먼 씨가 말했다. \"아닙니다.\"\n\n\"맞아.\" 워들이 말했다. \"내가 자네가 그러는 걸 봤어 — 자네가 그놈을 골라내는 걸 보았다고 — 자네가 겨누려고 총을 들어 올릴 때 내가 눈여겨봤거든. 그리고 내 한마디 하건대, 세상에 둘도 없는 명사수라도 그보다 더 멋들어지게는 못 했을 걸세. 자넨 내가 생각했던 것보다 이 방면에 노련한 솜씨꾼이군그래, 터프먼. 전에도 나가 본 적이 있었어.\"\n\n터프먼 씨가 결코 그런 적 없노라고 겸양의 미소를 띠며 항변해 봐야 헛일이었다. 바로 그 미소가 그 반대의 증거로 받아들여졌고, 그때부터 그의 명성은 확립되었다. 그렇게 쉽사리 얻어진 명성이 이것뿐만도 아니거니와, 그런 운수 좋은 정황이 자고새 사냥에만 국한된 것도 아니다.\n\n그러는 사이에 윈클 씨는 번쩍이고 불을 뿜고 연기를 피워 댔으나, 적어 둘 만한 이렇다 할 성과는 하나도 내지 못했다. 어떤 때는 탄알을 허공에 흩뿌렸고, 또 어떤 때는 땅 표면에 바싹 붙여 스치듯 날려서, 두 마리 개의 목숨을 자못 불확실하고 위태로운 처지에 놓이게 했다. 묘기 사격의 시범으로 치자면 그것은 지극히 다채롭고 진기했으나, 어떤 특정한 표적을 겨눈 사격의 시연으로 치자면, 대체로 보아 실패라 할 만했다. \"총알마다 제 묵을 자리가 있다\"는 것은 확립된 격언이다. 만일 그것이 산탄에도 똑같이 적용된다면, 윈클 씨의 산탄들은 타고난 권리를 빼앗긴 채 세상에 내던져져 어디에도 묵을 자리를 얻지 못한 불운한 미아들이었다.\n\n\"이거.\" 워들이 손수레 옆으로 걸어와, 그 유쾌하고 불그레한 얼굴에서 줄줄 흐르는 땀을 닦으며 말했다. \"찌는 날이로군, 안 그런가?\"\n\n\"정말 그렇군요.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다. \"햇볕이 이 나한테도 어마어마하게 뜨겁군요. 당신은 얼마나 덥게 느껴질지 모르겠어요.\"\n\n\"글쎄.\" 영감이 말했다. \"꽤나 덥지. 하지만 벌써 열두 시가 지났네. 저기 저 푸른 언덕 보이나?\"\n\n\"보이지요.\"\n\n\"저기가 우리가 점심을 들 곳일세. 그리고 어럽쇼, 저기 그 소년이 바구니를 들고 있군, 시계처럼 정확하게 말이야!\"\n\n\"정말 그렇군요.\" 픽윅 씨가 환해지며 말했다. \"착한 소년이로군. 이따 내 그 애한테 1실링 줘야겠어. 자, 그럼, 샘, 밀고 가게.\"\n\n\"꽉 잡으십쇼, 나리.\" 먹을거리에 대한 기대로 기운이 솟은 웰러 씨가 말했다. \"비켜라, 가죽 각반 꼬마야. 너 만약에 내 귀하신 목숨을 소중히 여긴다면 날 엎지 말거라, 마치 타이번으로 끌려가던 그 신사 양반이 마부한테 한 말처럼 말이다.\" 그러고는 걸음을 빠른 달음질로 재촉하며, 웰러 씨는 주인을 잽싸게 푸른 언덕으로 밀고 가, 바로 바구니 옆에다 솜씨 좋게 쏟아 내려놓고는, 더없이 부랴부랴 바구니를 풀기 시작했다.\n* 타이번: 옛 런던의 처형장.\n\n\"송아지 고기 파이로군.\" 웰러 씨가 풀밭에 먹을거리를 늘어놓으며 혼잣말로 중얼거렸다. \"송아지 고기 파이야말로 아주 좋은 거지, 그걸 만든 부인을 알고 그게 새끼고양이가 아니라는 걸 확실히 알 때는 말이야. 하긴 그게 어쨌든, 송아지 고기랑 어찌나 똑같은지 파이 장수 자신들도 그 차이를 모를 지경인데 무슨 상관이람?\"\n\n\"모른단 말이냐, 샘?\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"모르고말굽쇼, 나리.\" 웰러 씨가 모자에 손을 갖다 대며 대답했다. \"한번은 어떤 파이 장수랑 같은 집에서 하숙한 적이 있는데요, 나리, 아주 좋은 사람이었지요 — 게다가 영락없는 솜씨꾼이라, 뭐로든 파이를 만들어 낼 줄 알았답니다. '브룩스 씨, 고양이를 어찌 이리 많이 기르슈?' 하고 그 사람이랑 친해졌을 때 제가 물었지요. '아,' 하고 그가 말합디다, '많이 기르지요,' 하고요. '고양이를 어지간히 좋아하시나 보네,' 하고 제가 그랬더니, '남들도 그렇지요,' 하면서 저한테 눈을 찡긋합디다. '그래도 그놈들은 겨울이 돼야 제철이라오,' 그래요. '제철이 아니라뇨!' 제가 그랬죠. '그렇소,' 그가 말합디다, '과일이 들면 고양이는 나가는 거요.' '아니, 그게 무슨 말이오?' 제가 그랬더니, '말이냐고요!' 그래요. '내 결단코 고깃값을 떠받치려는 푸줏간들 작당에는 끼지 않으리란 거지요,' 하고요. '웰러 씨,' 하고 그가 제 손을 아주 꽉 쥐고 제 귀에 대고 속삭이는 거예요 — '이건 다시는 입 밖에 내지 마쇼 — 하지만 결국 양념이 다 하는 거라오. 죄다 저 고귀한 짐승들로 만드는 거지요,' 하고 아주 예쁘장한 작은 얼룩 새끼고양이를 가리키면서 말합디다, '난 수요에 따라 그놈들을 비프스테이크용으로, 송아지 고기용으로, 콩팥용으로 양념을 하지요. 그뿐인 줄 아쇼,' 그래요, '난 시장이 변하고 입맛이 바뀌는 대로, 눈 깜짝할 새에 송아지 고기를 비프스테이크로, 비프스테이크를 콩팥으로, 그것들 중 뭐든 양고기로 만들 수도 있다오!' 합디다.\"\n\n\"아주 재간 있는 젊은이였나 보구나, 그자가, 샘.\" 픽윅 씨가 살짝 몸서리치며 말했다.\n\n\"그렇고말굽쇼, 나리.\" 웰러 씨가 계속해서 바구니를 비우며 대답했다. \"게다가 파이는 끝내줬답니다. 우설 — 음, 그것도 여자 혀만 아니면 아주 좋은 거지요. 빵 — 햄 정강이, 그림 같습니다 — 차가운 소고기 저민 것, 아주 좋고요. 저 돌단지엔 뭐가 들었느냐, 위험천만한 꼬마야?\"\n\n\"이건 맥주고요.\" 소년이 어깨에서, 가죽끈으로 함께 묶인 큼직한 돌병 두 개를 내리며 대답했다. \"저건 차가운 펀치예요.\"\n\n\"이만하면 점심으로 아주 그럴듯한 생각이로군, 죄다 합쳐 놓고 보니.\" 웰러 씨가 자기가 차려 놓은 식사를 크게 만족스러워하며 둘러보고 말했다. \"자, 신사 여러분, '돌격하시지요,' 마치 영국군이 총검을 꽂았을 때 프랑스군한테 한 말처럼요.\"\n\n일행더러 그 식사에 한껏 정의를 베풀라고 두 번 청할 필요는 없었고, 웰러 씨와 키 큰 사냥터지기와 두 소년더러 조금 떨어진 풀밭에 자리 잡고 음식의 상당한 몫을 거뜬히 해치우라고 권하는 데도 별 강권이 필요 없었다. 늙은 떡갈나무 한 그루가 이 무리에게 기분 좋은 그늘을 드리워 주었고, 무성한 산울타리로 갈리고 숲으로 그윽하게 단장된 경작지와 목초지의 풍요로운 전경이 그들 앞에 펼쳐져 있었다.\n\n\"이거 참 즐겁군요 — 더할 나위 없이 즐거워요!\" 픽윅 씨가 말했는데, 그의 표정 풍부한 얼굴 가죽은 햇볕에 노출되어 빠르게 벗겨지고 있었다.\n\n\"그렇지 — 그렇고말고, 이 친구.\" 워들이 대답했다. \"자, 펀치 한 잔!\"\n\n\"기꺼이요.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했고, 그것을 마신 뒤 그의 얼굴에 떠오른 만족감이 그 대답의 진심을 증언해 주었다.\n\n\"좋군요.\" 픽윅 씨가 입맛을 다시며 말했다. \"아주 좋아요. 한 잔 더 들겠어요. 시원하군요, 아주 시원해요. 자, 신사 여러분.\" 픽윅 씨가 여전히 단지를 손에서 놓지 않고 말을 이었다. \"건배합시다. 딩글리 델의 우리 친구들을 위하여.\"\n\n건배는 우렁찬 환호 속에 이루어졌다.\n\n\"내가 사격 솜씨를 다시 키우려면 어떻게 할지 말씀드리지요.\" 주머니칼로 빵과 햄을 먹고 있던 윈클 씨가 말했다. \"기둥 꼭대기에 박제 자고새를 하나 올려놓고, 가까운 거리에서 시작해서 차츰 거리를 늘려 가며 연습할 겁니다. 그게 아주 훌륭한 연습이라더군요.\"\n\n\"제가 어떤 신사분을 아는데요, 나리.\" 웰러 씨가 말했다. \"그 양반이 그걸 했지요, 2야드에서 시작해서요. 하지만 다시는 해 보지 않았습니다. 첫 발에 그 새를 깨끗이 날려 버려서, 그 뒤로 깃털 하나 본 사람이 없었거든요.\"\n\n\"샘.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"네, 나리.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"네 일화는 청할 때까지 좀 아껴 두는 친절을 베풀어 다오.\"\n\n\"여부가 있겠습니까요, 나리.\"\n\n이때 웰러 씨가, 입술로 가져가던 맥주 깡통에 가려지지 않은 한쪽 눈을 어찌나 절묘하게 익살스럽게 찡긋했던지, 두 소년이 절로 자지러졌고, 심지어 키 큰 사내까지 황송하게도 미소를 지었다.\n\n\"음, 이건 정말이지 더없이 훌륭한 차가운 펀치로군요.\" 픽윅 씨가 돌병을 골똘히 바라보며 말했다. \"게다가 날은 몹시 덥고, 또 — 터프먼, 이보게, 펀치 한 잔?\"\n\n\"더없이 기쁘게요.\" 터프먼 씨가 대답했다. 그리고 그 잔을 비우자 픽윅 씨는, 펀치에 오렌지 껍질이 들었는지 그저 알아보려고 — 오렌지 껍질은 늘 그에게 받지 않았던 까닭에 — 또 한 잔을 들었고, 들어 있지 않음을 확인하자 픽윅 씨는 자리에 없는 친구의 건강을 위해 또 한 잔을 들었으며, 그러고 나니 누군지 모를 펀치 제조자를 기려 또 한 잔을 권해야 한다는 절박한 의무감이 들었다.\n\n이렇게 잔이 끊임없이 이어지자 픽윅 씨에게는 상당한 효과가 나타났다. 그의 얼굴은 더없이 화창한 미소로 빛났고, 입가에는 웃음이 어렸으며, 눈에는 사람 좋은 즐거움이 반짝였다. 차츰차츰, 그 자극적인 음료의 영향에 — 더위로 한층 더해진 그 영향에 — 굴복한 픽윅 씨는, 어릴 적에 들었던 노래 한 곡을 떠올리고 싶다는 강한 바람을 내비쳤고, 그 시도가 헛수고로 돌아가자 펀치를 더 들어 기억을 자극하려 했는데, 그것은 정반대의 효과를 내는 듯했다. 노랫말을 잊어버리던 그가, 이젠 아무 말이나마 또박또박 발음하는 법조차 잊기 시작했고, 마침내는 좌중을 향해 유창한 연설을 하겠다고 두 다리로 일어섰다가 손수레 안으로 쓰러짐과 동시에 곤히 잠들어 버렸다.\n\n바구니를 다시 챙기고 나서도 픽윅 씨를 그 혼곤한 잠에서 깨우기란 도무지 불가능하다는 것이 드러나자, 웰러 씨가 주인을 다시 밀고 돌아가는 편이 나을지, 아니면 다들 돌아갈 채비가 될 때까지 그를 있는 자리에 두는 편이 나을지를 두고 약간의 논의가 벌어졌다. 결국 후자 쪽으로 결정이 났다. 이후의 사냥 행차가 한 시간을 넘기지 않을 터인 데다, 웰러 씨가 일행에 끼게 해 달라고 몹시 간곡히 청했으므로, 픽윅 씨는 손수레 안에서 잠들게 둔 채 돌아오는 길에 데리러 오기로 정해졌다. 그리하여 그들은, 그늘에서 더없이 편안하게 코를 고는 픽윅 씨를 남겨 두고 떠났다.\n\n픽윅 씨가 친구들이 돌아올 때까지, 혹은 그들이 돌아오지 않을 경우 저녁 어스름이 풍경에 깔릴 때까지 그늘에서 계속 코를 골았으리라는 데에는 — 그가 평온하게 거기 머물도록 내버려졌으리라고 가정한다면 — 의심할 만한 합당한 이유가 전혀 없어 보인다. 그러나 그는 평온하게 거기 머물도록 내버려지지 않았다. 그리고 그것을 가로막은 것은 다음과 같은 일이었다.\n\n볼드위그 대위는 빳빳한 검은 목도리와 푸른 프록코트 차림의 자그마하고 사나운 사내였는데, 황송하게도 자기 영지를 거닐 때면 놋쇠 물미를 댄 굵은 등나무 지팡이와 함께, 그리고 온순한 얼굴을 한 정원사와 보조 정원사를 거느리고 다녔으며, 볼드위그 대위는 그들에게(지팡이가 아니라 정원사들에게) 온갖 위엄과 사나움을 갖추어 명령을 내렸다. 볼드위그 대위 부인의 언니가 후작과 결혼했고, 대위의 집은 별장이며, 그의 땅은 \"장원\"이고, 그러니 그것이 다 대단하고 위세 등등하고 굉장한 것이었기 때문이다.\n\n픽윅 씨가 잠든 지 반 시간도 채 안 되었을 때, 자그마한 볼드위그 대위가 두 정원사를 거느리고, 자기 덩치와 위신이 허락하는 한 빠른 걸음으로 성큼성큼 걸어왔다. 그리고 떡갈나무 가까이에 이르자 볼드위그 대위는 걸음을 멈추고 길게 숨을 들이쉬며, 마치 이 경치가 자기 같은 사람의 주목을 받게 된 것을 더없이 흡족해해야 마땅하다고 여기는 양 경치를 바라보았다. 그러고는 지팡이로 땅을 힘주어 내리치고 우두머리 정원사를 불렀다.\n\n\"헌트.\" 볼드위그 대위가 말했다.\n\n\"네, 나리.\" 정원사가 말했다.\n\n\"내일 아침 이곳을 골라 다지게 — 알겠나, 헌트?\"\n\n\"네, 나리.\"\n\n\"그리고 이곳을 잘 관리하도록 신경 써 — 알겠나, 헌트?\"\n\n\"네, 나리.\"\n\n\"그리고 무단출입과 자동 발사 총 따위에 관한 푯말을 세우라고 일러 주게, 천한 것들이 못 들어오게 말이야. 알겠나, 헌트, 알겠어?\"\n\n\"잊지 않겠습니다, 나리.\"\n\n\"실례합니다, 나리.\" 다른 사내가 모자에 손을 갖다 대며 앞으로 나서서 말했다.\n\n\"무슨 일인가, 윌킨스, 뭐가 잘못됐나?\" 볼드위그 대위가 말했다.\n\n\"실례합니다만, 나리 — 오늘 여기 무단출입자들이 있었던 것 같습니다.\"\n\n\"하!\" 대위가 주위를 노려보며 말했다.\n\n\"네, 나리 — 여기서 식사를 한 모양입니다, 나리.\"\n\n\"이런, 저놈들의 뻔뻔함을 봐라, 정말 그랬군.\" 풀밭에 흩어진 빵 부스러기와 음식 찌꺼기가 그의 눈에 들어오자 볼드위그 대위가 말했다. \"실제로 여기서 음식을 먹어 치웠어. 그 부랑자 놈들을 여기서 잡았더라면!\" 대위가 굵은 지팡이를 움켜쥐며 말했다.\n\n\"그 부랑자 놈들을 잡았더라면.\" 대위가 분노에 차 말했다.\n\n\"실례합니다, 나리.\" 윌킨스가 말했다. \"그런데—\"\n\n\"그런데 뭐? 응?\" 대위가 고함쳤다. 그리고 윌킨스의 겁먹은 시선을 좇아가던 그의 눈이 외바퀴 손수레와 픽윅 씨와 마주쳤다.\n\n\"넌 누구냐, 이 악당아?\" 대위가 굵은 지팡이로 픽윅 씨의 몸을 몇 차례 쿡쿡 찌르며 말했다. \"네 이름이 뭐야?\"\n\n\"차가운 펀치.\" 픽윅 씨가 다시 잠 속으로 가라앉으며 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"뭐라고?\" 볼드위그 대위가 다그쳤다.\n\n대답이 없었다.\n\n\"제 이름이 뭐라고 하던가?\" 대위가 물었다.\n\n\"펀치라고 한 것 같습니다, 나리.\" 윌킨스가 대답했다.\n\n\"저게 저놈의 무례함이야 — 저게 저 빌어먹을 무례함이라고.\" 볼드위그 대위가 말했다. \"지금 그저 잠든 척하는 거야.\" 대위가 격분하여 말했다. \"취했어, 술 취한 천민이야. 밀어 내, 윌킨스, 당장 밀어 내라고.\"\n\n\"어디로 밀고 갈까요, 나리?\" 윌킨스가 몹시 겁먹은 채 물었다.\n\n\"악마한테로 밀고 가.\" 볼드위그 대위가 대답했다.\n\n\"알겠습니다, 나리.\" 윌킨스가 말했다.\n\n\"잠깐.\" 대위가 말했다.\n\n윌킨스가 그에 따라 멈춰 섰다.\n\n\"이놈을 밀어다가.\" 대위가 말했다. \"유치장으로 밀어다 넣어. 그리고 제정신이 들었을 때도 제 이름을 펀치라고 하는지 어디 두고 보자고. 감히 날 윽박지르려 들다니 — 어림없지, 날 윽박질러? 밀어 내.\"\n\n이 위압적인 명령에 따라 픽윅 씨는 밀려갔고, 위대한 볼드위그 대위는 분개로 부풀어 오른 채 산책을 계속했다.\n\n저 작은 일행이 돌아와서 픽윅 씨가 외바퀴 손수레를 끌고 사라져 버린 것을 알았을 때의 경악이란 이루 말로 다 할 수 없었다. 그것은 일찍이 들어 본 적 없는 더없이 불가사의하고 설명할 길 없는 일이었다. 다리를 저는 사람이 아무런 예고도 없이 두 다리로 일어나 걸어 가 버렸다는 것만 해도 더없이 놀라운 일이었을 텐데, 게다가 무거운 손수레를 앞세워 밀고 가며 그것을 소일거리로 삼았다는 데 이르러서는, 그야말로 기적이라 할 만했다. 그들은 주위의 구석구석을 함께 또 따로 뒤졌다. 소리치고 휘파람 불고 웃고 부르고 했으나 — 모두 한결같은 결과였다. 픽윅 씨는 찾을 수가 없었다. 몇 시간을 헛되이 찾아 헤맨 끝에, 그들은 그를 두고 집에 돌아가야 한다는 달갑지 않은 결론에 이르렀다.\n\n그러는 사이 픽윅 씨는 유치장으로 밀려가, 외바퀴 손수레 안에 곤히 잠든 채 그 안에 무사히 안치되었는데, 이는 마을의 모든 소년들뿐 아니라 그가 깨어나기를 기대하며 둘러 모여든 전체 주민의 4분의 3에게도 더없는 기쁨과 만족을 안겨 주었다. 그가 밀려 들어오는 것을 보고 그토록 강렬한 즐거움을 맛본 그들이었으니, 몇 마디 알아들을 수 없는 \"샘!\" 소리를 내뱉고 나서 그가 손수레 안에서 일어나 앉아 눈앞의 얼굴들을 형언할 수 없는 경악으로 바라보았을 때, 그들의 기쁨이 몇백 곱절로 커졌으랴.\n\n물론 그가 깨어난 것을 알리는 신호는 일제히 터진 함성이었고, 그의 무심코 던진 \"무슨 일이오?\"라는 물음은, 가능하다면 첫 번째보다 더 큰 또 한 차례의 함성을 불러일으켰다.\n\n\"구경거리 났다!\" 군중이 떠들썩하게 외쳤다.\n\n\"내가 어디 있는 거요?\" 픽윅 씨가 외쳤다.\n\n\"유치장이오.\" 군중이 대답했다.\n\n\"내가 어쩌다 여기 왔지? 내가 뭘 하고 있었나? 어디서 끌려온 거야?\"\n\n\"볼드위그! 볼드위그 대위!\"가 유일한 대답이었다.\n\n\"내보내 주시오.\" 픽윅 씨가 외쳤다. \"내 하인은 어디 있소? 내 친구들은 어디 있냐고?\"\n\n\"당신한텐 친구 따위 없어. 와아!\" 그러더니 무 한 개가 날아오고, 이어 감자 한 개, 그다음엔 달걀 한 개가 날아왔으며, 그 밖에도 머리 여럿 달린 군중의 장난기 어린 성품을 보여 주는 자잘한 표시들이 몇 가지 더 날아왔다.\n\n이 광경이 얼마나 오래 이어졌을지, 또 픽윅 씨가 얼마나 더 고초를 겪었을지는, 마침 빠르게 지나가던 마차 한 대가 갑자기 멈춰 서지 않았더라면 아무도 알 수 없었으리라. 그 마차에서 워들 영감과 샘 웰러가 내렸는데, 전자는 — 읽는 데는 몰라도 적는 데 걸리는 시간보다 훨씬 짧은 새에 — 픽윅 씨 곁으로 길을 헤치고 나아가 그를 마차에 태웠다. 바로 그때 픽윅 씨는 마을 교구 관리와의 일대일 격투에서 세 번째이자 마지막 라운드를 막 끝낸 참이었다.\n\n\"치안판사한테 달려가!\" 열댓 명의 목소리가 외쳤다.\n\n\"아, 달려가렴.\" 웰러 씨가 마부석으로 뛰어오르며 말했다. \"내 인사 전해라 — 웰러 씨의 인사를 — 치안판사한테 전하고, 내가 그 양반 교구 관리를 망가뜨려 놨다고, 그리고 만약에 그가 새 놈을 하나 선서시켜 앉히면 내일 다시 와서 그놈도 망가뜨려 주겠다고 일러라. 몰아라, 이 영감님아.\"\n\n\"런던에 도착하는 즉시 이 볼드위그 대위를 상대로 불법 감금 소송을 시작하라고 지시하겠습니다.\" 마차가 마을을 벗어나자마자 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"우리가 무단출입을 한 모양이던데.\" 워들이 말했다.\n\n\"상관없어요.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"소송을 걸겠습니다.\"\n\n\"아니, 안 그럴 걸세.\" 워들이 말했다.\n\n\"걸 겁니다, 맹세코—\" 그러나 워들의 얼굴에 익살스러운 표정이 어려 있는 것을 보고 픽윅 씨는 말을 멈추고 물었다. \"왜 안 된다는 거지요?\"\n\n\"왜냐하면,\" 워들 영감이 웃음을 터뜨릴 듯 말했다. \"왜냐하면 그쪽에서 도리어 우리 중 몇몇한테 화살을 돌려, 우리가 차가운 펀치를 너무 많이 마셨다고 할지도 모르니까.\"\n\n아무리 안 그러려 해도 픽윅 씨의 얼굴에는 미소가 번지고 말았다. 그 미소는 웃음으로 번지고, 웃음은 폭소로 번졌으며, 폭소는 모두에게로 번졌다. 그래서 그들은 그 좋은 기분을 이어 가려고, 처음 만난 길가의 선술집에 들러 모두에게 브랜디 워터 한 잔씩을, 그리고 새뮤얼 웰러 씨에게는 특별히 독한 큰 병 하나를 주문했다.\n\n콘힐의 프리먼스 코트 맨 끝, 어느 우중충한 집의 일층 앞방에는 도드슨 앤드 포그 사무소의 사무원 네 사람이 앉아 있었으니, 이들은 웨스트민스터의 국왕좌부 법원과 민사 법원에 소속된 폐하의 변호사 두 분이자 고등 형평법원의 사무 변호사들이었다—앞서 말한 사무원들로 말하자면, 매일같이 일하는 동안 하늘의 빛과 하늘의 해를 어쩌다 엿보는 정도가, 적당히 깊은 우물 바닥에 놓인 사람이 바랄 법한 만큼에 지나지 않았으며, 더구나 그 깊숙한 자리라면 한낮에도 별을 볼 기회가 있으련만 그런 기회조차 없었다.\n\n도드슨 앤드 포그 사무소의 사무원실은 어둡고 곰팡내 나는, 흙냄새 풍기는 방으로, 사무원들을 속된 시선으로부터 가려 주는 높은 판벽 칸막이와 낡은 나무 의자 두 개, 몹시 시끄럽게 똑딱거리는 시계 하나, 달력 한 부, 우산꽂이 하나, 모자걸이 한 줄, 그리고 선반 몇 개가 있었는데, 그 선반에는 꼬리표를 단 더러운 서류 묶음 여러 개와 종이 라벨이 붙은 낡은 전나무 상자 몇 개, 그리고 갖가지 모양과 크기의 삭아 빠진 돌 잉크병 여럿이 놓여 있었다. 그 안뜰로 들어가는 입구를 이루는 복도로 통하는 유리문이 하나 있었는데, 지난 장에서 충실히 서술한 사건이 있고 난 뒤 금요일 아침, 픽윅 씨가 샘 웰러를 바짝 뒤에 거느린 채 그 유리문 바깥쪽에 모습을 드러냈다.\n\n\"들어와요, 못 들어와!\" 픽윅 씨가 문을 가볍게 두드리자 칸막이 뒤에서 한 목소리가 외쳤다. 그리하여 픽윅 씨와 샘은 안으로 들어갔다.\n\n\"도드슨 씨나 포그 씨 계십니까?\" 픽윅 씨가 모자를 손에 든 채 칸막이 쪽으로 다가가며 점잖게 물었다.\n\n\"도드슨 씨는 안 계시고, 포그 씨는 특별히 바쁘십니다.\" 그 목소리가 대답했다. 그와 동시에 그 목소리의 주인인 머리통이, 귀 뒤에 펜을 꽂은 채 칸막이 너머로 픽윅 씨를 내다보았다.\n\n그것은 텁수룩한 머리통이었는데, 모래빛 머리카락은 한쪽으로 정성스레 가르마를 타 포마드로 납작하게 눌러 붙였고, 작은 두 눈으로 장식되고 몹시 더러운 셔츠 깃과 녹슨 듯한 검정 목도리로 마무리된 납작한 얼굴 둘레로 작은 반원형 꼬랑지처럼 비틀려 말려 있었다.\n\n\"도드슨 씨는 안 계시고, 포그 씨는 특별히 바쁘십니다.\" 그 머리통의 주인이 말했다.\n\n\"도드슨 씨는 언제쯤 돌아오십니까?\" 픽윅 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"몰라요.\"\n\n\"포그 씨가 한가해지려면 오래 걸리겠습니까?\"\n\n\"모르죠.\"\n\n여기서 그 사내는 자기 펜을 아주 느긋하게 고치기 시작했고, 그동안 책상 뚜껑에 가려진 채 자이들리츠 가루약을 타고 있던 또 다른 사무원이 동의하듯 웃었다.\n\n\"기다려야겠군요.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. 아무 대답도 없었으므로, 픽윅 씨는 권하지도 않았는데 자리에 앉아 시계의 요란한 똑딱거림과 사무원들의 소곤대는 대화에 귀를 기울였다.\n\n\"한판 제대로였지, 안 그래?\" 갈색 외투에 놋쇠 단추를 달고, 잉크 얼룩이 진 누런 바지에 단화를 신은 신사들 가운데 하나가, 들리지 않는 전날 저녁 모험담을 마무리 지으면서 말했다.\n\n\"끝내줬지—끝내줬어.\" 자이들리츠 가루약을 타던 사내가 말했다.\n\n\"톰 커민스가 의장석에 앉았었어.\" 갈색 외투의 사내가 말했다. \"내가 서머스 타운에 닿았을 때가 네 시 반이었는데, 그때쯤엔 어찌나 곤드레만드레였던지 열쇠 구멍 자리도 못 찾아서 결국 노친네를 깨워야 했지 뭐야. 그나저나 영감 포그가 이 일을 알면 뭐라 할지 궁금하군. 잘리겠지, 아마—응?\"\n\n이 우스운 생각에 사무원들이 일제히 웃음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n\"오늘 아침에 여기서 포그 영감이랑 한바탕 제대로 있었지.\" 갈색 외투의 사내가 말했다. \"잭이 위층에서 서류 정리를 하고, 너희 둘은 인지 사무소에 가 있던 동안 말이야. 포그가 여기 내려와서 편지를 뜯고 있는데, 왜 우리가 캠버웰에서 영장 발부한 그 친구 있잖아, 그 사람이 들어온 거야—이름이 뭐였더라?\"\n\n\"램지.\" 픽윅 씨에게 말을 건넸던 사무원이 말했다.\n\n\"아, 램지—꽤나 추레하게 생긴 손님이지. '자, 선생,' 포그 영감이 그를 아주 사납게 노려보며 말하는 거야—그 양반 버릇 알잖아—'자, 선생, 갚으러 오셨소?' '예, 그렇습니다, 선생.' 램지가 주머니에 손을 넣어 돈을 꺼내며 말했지. '빚이 2파운드 10실링이고, 비용이 3파운드 5실링, 여기 있습니다, 선생.' 그러면서 압지 조각에 싸인 돈을 끌어내는데, 벽돌 무너지듯 한숨을 푹푹 쉬더라고. 포그 영감이 먼저 돈을 보고, 다음엔 그 사람을 보고, 그러더니 그 묘한 식으로 기침을 하는 거야. 그래서 나는 뭔가 일이 벌어지겠구나 했지. '소장이 접수되어 비용이 상당히 불어난 걸 모르나 보군.' 포그가 말하니까, '그럴 리가요, 선생.' 램지가 흠칫 물러서며 말하더군. '기한이 어젯밤에야 끝났는데요, 선생.' '내 말이 맞소.' 포그가 말했지. '내 사무원이 방금 접수하러 갔거든. 위크스 군, 잭슨 군이 불먼 대 램지 건 소장을 접수하러 가지 않았나?' 물론 나는 그렇다고 했고, 그러자 포그가 다시 기침을 하고 램지를 보았어. '맙소사!' 램지가 말하더군. '이 돈 긁어모으느라 미칠 지경이었는데, 죄다 헛수고였단 말이오.' '그렇소, 죄다 헛수고지.' 포그가 냉정하게 말했어. '그러니 돌아가서 더 긁어모아서 제때 가져오는 게 좋겠소.' '도저히 못 구하겠소, 맙소사!' 램지가 주먹으로 책상을 치며 말했지. '나한테 윽박지르지 마시오, 선생.' 포그가 일부러 발끈하면서 말했어. '윽박지르는 게 아닙니다, 선생.' 램지가 말했지. '윽박지르고 있잖소.' 포그가 말했어. '나가시오, 선생. 이 사무실에서 나가시오, 선생. 그리고 처신을 어떻게 해야 하는지 알게 되거든 다시 오시오, 선생.' 자, 램지가 뭐라 말하려 했지만 포그가 도무지 틈을 안 주니, 결국 돈을 도로 주머니에 넣고 슬그머니 나갔지. 문이 채 닫히기도 전에 포그 영감이 얼굴 가득 달콤한 미소를 띠고 나를 돌아보더니, 외투 주머니에서 소장을 꺼내는 거야. '자, 위크스,' 포그가 말하길, '마차 잡아타고 되도록 빨리 템플로 내려가서 이걸 접수하게. 비용은 떼일 염려 없어. 그 사람은 식솔이 많고 주급 25실링 받는 착실한 사람이거든. 결국엔 어쩔 수 없이 위임장을 써 줄 텐데, 그러면 고용주들이 분명히 갚도록 해 줄 거야. 그러니 우리는 받아낼 수 있는 건 죄다 받아내는 게 좋단 말일세, 위크스 군. 이건 기독교인다운 처사야, 위크스 군. 식솔은 많고 벌이는 적으니, 빚지지 말라는 따끔한 교훈을 받는 편이 그 사람한테도 외려 이로울 테니까—안 그런가, 위크스 군, 안 그래?'—그러고는 어찌나 사람 좋게 웃으며 나가던지, 보기만 해도 흐뭇하더라니까. 정말이지 일에 밝은 분이라니까.\" 위크스가 더없이 깊은 감탄이 어린 어조로 말했다. \"기막힌 분 아냐, 응?\"\n\n나머지 세 사람도 이 의견에 진심으로 동조했고, 그 일화는 더없이 한량없는 만족을 안겨 주었다.\n\n\"여기 사람들 멋지죠, 나리.\" 웰러 씨가 주인에게 속삭였다. \"놀음에 대해선 아주 멋진 생각을 가졌네요, 나리.\"\n\n픽윅 씨는 고개를 끄덕여 동의를 표하고는, 칸막이 뒤 젊은 신사들의 주의를 끌려고 기침을 했는데, 이제 자기들끼리 잠시 잡담을 나누며 머리를 식힌 그들은 그 낯선 사람에게 어느 정도 관심을 두는 것을 너그러이 허락했다.\n\n\"포그가 이제 한가한지 모르겠군.\" 잭슨이 말했다.\n\n\"내가 보고 오지.\" 위크스가 의자에서 느릿느릿 내려오며 말했다. \"포그 씨한테 성함을 뭐라고 전할까요?\"\n\n\"픽윅이오.\" 이 회고록의 저명한 주인공이 대답했다.\n\n잭슨 씨가 그 심부름으로 위층에 올라갔다가 곧장 돌아와, 포그 씨가 오 분 뒤에 픽윅 씨를 만나겠다는 전갈을 전하고는, 그 말을 전한 뒤 다시 자기 책상으로 돌아갔다.\n\n\"이름이 뭐랬더라?\" 위크스가 속삭였다.\n\n\"픽윅.\" 잭슨이 대답했다. \"바델 대 픽윅 건의 피고야.\"\n\n칸막이 뒤에서 발을 끄는 소리가 별안간 나더니, 억눌린 웃음소리와 뒤섞여 들려왔다.\n\n\"저 사람들이 나리를 훑어보고 있어요, 나리.\" 웰러 씨가 속삭였다.\n\n\"날 훑어봐, 샘!\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다. \"날 훑어본다는 게 무슨 말이냐?\"\n\n웰러 씨는 어깨너머로 엄지손가락을 들어 가리키는 것으로 대답을 대신했고, 픽윅 씨가 고개를 들어 보니, 사무원 넷 모두가 더없이 즐거워하는 표정으로 나무 칸막이 너머로 머리를 쑥 내민 채, 여성의 마음을 농락하고 여성의 행복을 어지럽혔다고 여겨지는 자의 풍채와 전반적 외양을 샅샅이 뜯어보고 있다는 흐뭇한 사실을 깨달았다. 그가 고개를 들자 그 한 줄로 늘어선 머리통들이 별안간 사라지고, 곧이어 펜이 종이 위를 맹렬한 속도로 내달리는 소리가 뒤따랐다.\n\n사무실에 매달린 종이 별안간 울리자 잭슨 씨가 포그의 방으로 불려 갔고, 그는 거기서 돌아와 그분(포그)이 위층으로 올라오시면 픽윅 씨를 만날 준비가 되었다고 전했다.\n\n이에 픽윅 씨는 샘 웰러를 아래에 남겨 둔 채 위층으로 올라갔다. 한 층 위 뒤편 방문에는 알아보기 쉬운 글자로 '포그 씨'라는 위풍당당한 글귀가 적혀 있었다. 그 문을 두드리고 들어오라는 말을 듣자, 잭슨은 픽윅 씨를 그 앞으로 안내했다.\n\n\"도드슨 씨 계신가?\" 포그 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"방금 들어오셨습니다, 선생님.\" 잭슨이 대답했다.\n\n\"이리 좀 오시라 하게.\"\n\n\"예, 선생님.\" 잭슨 퇴장.\n\n\"앉으시지요, 선생.\" 포그가 말했다. \"신문이 여기 있습니다, 선생. 제 동업자가 곧 올 테니, 이 일에 관해 이야기를 나눌 수 있을 겁니다, 선생.\"\n\n픽윅 씨는 자리에 앉아 신문을 집어 들었으나, 그것을 읽는 대신 신문 위로 흘끔 넘겨다보며 그 일에 밝은 사내를 살폈다. 그는 여드름투성이 얼굴에 채식이나 할 법한 나이 든 사내로, 검정 외투에 어두운 혼방 바지를 입고 작은 검정 각반을 차고 있었으니, 그가 글을 쓰고 있는 책상의 없어서는 안 될 일부인 듯, 그 책상만큼이나 생각도 감정도 없어 보이는 부류의 인간이었다.\n\n몇 분간의 침묵 끝에, 포동포동하고 풍채 좋으며 근엄한 얼굴에 목소리가 우렁찬 도드슨 씨가 나타났고, 대화가 시작되었다.\n\n\"이분이 픽윅 씨요.\" 포그가 말했다.\n\n\"아! 당신이 바델 대 픽윅 건의 피고로군요, 선생?\" 도드슨이 말했다.\n\n\"그렇습니다, 선생.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"자, 선생,\" 도드슨이 말했다. \"그래, 어떻게 하시겠다는 거요?\"\n\n\"아!\" 포그가 두 손을 바지 주머니에 찔러 넣고 의자에 몸을 젖히며 말했다. \"어떻게 하시겠다는 겁니까, 픽윅 씨?\"\n\n\"쉿, 포그.\" 도드슨이 말했다. \"픽윅 씨가 할 말을 들어 봅시다.\"\n\n\"내가 여기 온 것은, 여러분,\" 픽윅 씨가 두 동업자를 차분히 바라보며 말했다. \"내가 여기 온 것은, 여러분, 며칠 전 보내신 편지를 받고 느낀 놀라움을 표하고, 또 당신들이 나를 상대로 무슨 소송 근거를 가질 수 있는지 묻기 위해서요.\"\n\n\"소송 근거라니—\" 포그가 여기까지 내뱉었을 때 도드슨이 그를 막았다.\n\n\"포그 군,\" 도드슨이 말했다. \"내가 말하겠네.\"\n\n\"실례했소, 도드슨 씨.\" 포그가 말했다.\n\n\"소송 근거로 말할 것 같으면, 선생,\" 도드슨이 도덕적으로 고매한 기색을 띠며 말을 이었다. \"당신 자신의 양심과 당신 자신의 감정에 물어보시오. 우리로 말하면, 선생, 우리는 전적으로 의뢰인의 진술에 따를 뿐이오. 그 진술은, 선생, 참일 수도 거짓일 수도 있고, 믿을 만할 수도 믿지 못할 수도 있소. 허나 그것이 참이고 또 믿을 만한 것이라면, 나는 서슴없이 말하겠소, 선생, 우리의 소송 근거는, 선생, 탄탄하여 흔들리지 않는다고 말이오. 당신은 불운한 사람일 수도, 음흉한 사람일 수도 있소. 허나 내가 선서한 배심원으로서, 선생, 당신 행실에 대한 의견을 표하라는 요청을 받는다면, 선생, 나는 서슴없이 단언하건대 그에 대해 단 하나의 의견밖에 갖지 않을 거요.\" 여기서 도드슨은 모욕당한 미덕의 기색으로 몸을 곧추세우고 포그를 바라보았는데, 포그는 두 손을 주머니에 더 깊이 찔러 넣고 슬기로운 듯 고개를 끄덕이며 더없이 동조하는 어조로 말했다. \"두말할 나위 없지요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면, 선생,\" 픽윅 씨가 얼굴에 상당한 고통을 드러내며 말했다. \"당신들에게 한 가지 분명히 말해 두건대, 적어도 이 사건에 관한 한 나는 더없이 불운한 사람이오.\"\n\n\"그러시기를 바라오, 선생.\" 도드슨이 대답했다. \"그러시기를 진정 바라오, 선생. 당신이 받은 혐의에 대해 정말로 결백하다면, 당신은 내가 사람으로서 가능하리라 믿었던 것보다 더 불운한 사람이오. 어떻게 생각하나, 포그 군?\"\n\n\"나는 당신이 말한 그대로 생각하오.\" 포그가 못 믿겠다는 미소를 띠며 대답했다.\n\n\"소송을 개시하는 영장은, 선생,\" 도드슨이 말을 이었다. \"정식으로 발부되었소. 포그 군, 그 소환 명령부가 어디 있나?\"\n\n\"여기 있소.\" 포그가 양피지 표지를 댄 네모난 장부를 건네며 말했다.\n\n\"여기 기재가 있소.\" 도드슨이 다시 말했다. \"'미들섹스, 신병 구인. 과부 마사 바델, 새뮤얼 픽윅을 상대로. 손해 배상 1500파운드. 원고 대리인 도드슨 앤드 포그, 1827년 8월 28일.' 모두 정식이오, 선생. 완벽하게.\" 도드슨이 기침을 하고 포그를 바라보자, 포그 역시 \"완벽하게요.\" 하고 말했다. 그러고 나서 두 사람 모두 픽윅 씨를 바라보았다.\n\n\"그렇다면,\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"정말로 이 소송을 진행할 의사라는 것으로 이해하면 되겠소?\"\n\n\"이해라니, 선생—그야 분명히 그렇게 이해하셔도 좋소.\" 도드슨이 자신의 위엄이 허락하는 한 미소에 가장 가까운 무엇을 지으며 대답했다.\n\n\"그리고 손해 배상액이 실제로 천오백 파운드로 책정되었다는 거요?\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그 이해에 한 가지 더 보태드리자면, 우리가 의뢰인을 설득할 수만 있었다면 그 액수의 세 배로 책정되었을 거라는 점을 장담하오, 선생.\" 도드슨이 대답했다.\n\n\"하지만 바델 부인이 특별히 한 말씀이 있었지요.\" 포그가 도드슨을 흘끗 보며 말했다. \"단 한 푼이라도 적게는 절대 합의하지 않겠다고요.\"\n\n\"두말할 나위 없소.\" 도드슨이 근엄하게 대답했다. 소송은 이제 막 시작되었을 뿐이었고, 설령 픽윅 씨가 그럴 마음이 있었다 한들 지금 그가 합의하도록 두는 것은 될 일이 아니었던 것이다.\n\n\"당신이 아무 조건도 제시하지 않으니, 선생,\" 도드슨이 오른손에 든 양피지 한 장을 펼쳐 보이며, 왼손으로는 그 종이 사본을 픽윅 씨에게 다정하게 들이밀면서 말했다. \"이 영장의 사본을 송달하는 편이 낫겠소, 선생. 여기 원본이오, 선생.\"\n\n\"좋소, 여러분, 아주 좋소.\" 픽윅 씨가 몸과 함께 노기까지 일으키며 말했다. \"내 사무 변호사로부터 연락이 갈 거요, 여러분.\"\n\n\"기꺼이 받겠소이다.\" 포그가 두 손을 비비며 말했다.\n\n\"기꺼이.\" 도드슨이 문을 열며 말했다.\n\n\"가기 전에 한마디 하겠소, 여러분,\" 흥분한 픽윅 씨가 층계참에서 돌아서며 말했다. \"온갖 수치스럽고 악랄한 처사 중에서도—\"\n\n\"잠깐, 선생, 잠깐만.\" 도드슨이 더없이 정중하게 끼어들었다. \"잭슨 군! 위크스 군!\"\n\n\"예, 선생님.\" 두 사무원이 계단 아래에 나타나 말했다.\n\n\"이 신사분이 하는 말을 그저 자네들이 들어 두었으면 해서 그러네.\" 도드슨이 대답했다. \"어서 계속하시오, 선생—수치스럽고 악랄한 처사라 하셨던 것 같은데?\"\n\n\"그렇소.\" 픽윅 씨가 단단히 격분하여 말했다. \"내가 말했소, 선생, 일찍이 꾀해진 온갖 수치스럽고 악랄한 처사 중에서도 이것이 가장 심하다고 말이오. 다시 한번 말하오, 선생.\"\n\n\"들었지, 위크스 군.\" 도드슨이 말했다.\n\n\"이 표현들을 잊지 않겠지, 잭슨 군?\" 포그가 말했다.\n\n\"우리를 사기꾼이라 부르고 싶으실 테지요, 선생.\" 도드슨이 말했다. \"어서 그러시오, 선생, 그러고 싶으시거든. 자, 어서 그러시오, 선생.\"\n\n\"그러겠소.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"당신들은 사기꾼이오.\"\n\n\"아주 좋습니다.\" 도드슨이 말했다. \"거기서 잘 들리겠지, 위크스 군?\"\n\n\"오, 예, 선생님.\" 위크스가 말했다.\n\n\"잘 안 들리거든 한두 계단 더 올라오는 게 좋을 거야.\" 포그 씨가 덧붙였다. \"계속하시오, 선생, 어서 계속하시오. 우리를 도둑놈이라 부르시는 게 좋겠소, 선생. 아니면 우리 둘 중 하나를 한 대 치고 싶으실 테지. 어서 그러시오, 선생, 그러고 싶으시거든. 우리는 조금도 저항하지 않겠소. 어서 그러시오, 선생.\"\n\n포그가 픽윅 씨의 움켜쥔 주먹 사정거리 안으로 아주 솔깃하게 제 몸을 들이밀었으니, 그 신사가 그 간곡한 청을 따랐으리라는 것은 거의 의심할 여지가 없었으나, 다툼 소리를 듣고 사무실에서 나와 계단을 올라온 샘이 주인의 팔을 붙든 덕분에 그 일은 일어나지 않았다.\n\n\"그냥 가시지요.\" 웰러 씨가 말했다. \"배틀도어 앤드 셔틀콕도 좋은 놀이긴 합니다만, 나리가 셔틀콕이고 변호사 둘이 배틀도어 채일 때는 얘기가 다르죠. 그런 경우엔 너무 흥분돼서 도무지 즐겁질 않거든요. 가시지요, 나리. 누굴 한바탕 깨부숴서 속을 풀고 싶으시거든, 안뜰로 나가서 저를 깨부수십쇼. 하지만 여기서 그걸 계속하기엔 비용이 너무 많이 들거든요.\"\n\n그리고 웰러 씨는 조금의 예의도 차리지 않고 주인을 계단 아래로, 또 안뜰 밖으로 끌어내고는, 콘힐에 무사히 내려놓고 나서, 어디로 이끌든 따라갈 채비를 하고 뒤로 물러섰다.\n\n픽윅 씨는 멍하니 걸어 맨션 하우스 맞은편을 가로질러 칩사이드 쪽으로 발길을 옮겼다. 샘이 대체 어디로 가는 건가 궁금해하기 시작할 무렵, 주인이 돌아서며 말했다—\n\n\"샘, 곧장 퍼커 씨에게 가야겠다.\"\n\n\"그게 바로 어젯밤 진작에 가셨어야 할 곳입니다, 나리.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"그런 것 같구나, 샘.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"틀림없습니다, 나리.\" 웰러 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 그래, 샘.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다. \"당장 거기로 가자. 하지만 그전에, 내가 좀 심란해졌으니 따끈한 브랜디 물 한 잔을 마셨으면 싶구나, 샘. 어디서 마실 수 있겠느냐, 샘?\"\n\n런던에 대한 웰러 씨의 지식은 폭넓고도 독특했다. 그는 조금의 망설임도 없이 대답했다—\n\n\"오른편 두 번째 안뜰로 들어가서—같은 쪽 끝에서 두 번째 집입니다요—첫 번째 벽난로 옆에 있는 자리를 잡으십쇼. 거긴 다른 탁자들엔 다 있는 가운데 다리가 없어서, 그게 아주 불편하거든요.\"\n\n픽윅 씨는 시종의 지시를 고스란히 따랐고, 샘에게 따라오라 이르고는 그가 가리킨 선술집으로 들어갔는데, 그곳에서는 따끈한 브랜디 물이 곧장 그의 앞에 놓였다. 한편 웰러 씨는 주인과 같은 탁자에 앉되 공손하게 거리를 둔 자리에 앉아 포터 한 파인트를 대접받았다.\n\n그 방은 매우 소박한 부류였으며, 보아하니 역마차 마부들의 각별한 단골집인 듯했다. 그 학식 있는 직업에 속한 듯한 풍채의 신사 여럿이 여러 자리에서 술을 마시고 담배를 피우고 있었기 때문이다. 그 무리 중에서도 특히 맞은편 자리에 앉은, 풍채 좋고 얼굴이 붉으며 나이 든 사내 하나가 픽윅 씨의 주의를 끌었다. 그 풍채 좋은 사내는 몹시 격렬하게 담배를 피웠는데, 여섯 모금쯤 빨 때마다 한 번씩 입에서 파이프를 떼고는 먼저 웰러 씨를, 그다음엔 픽윅 씨를 바라보았다. 그러고는 1쿼트들이 술 단지가 받아들일 수 있는 만큼 얼굴을 그 안에 파묻었다가, 다시 샘과 픽윅 씨를 한 번 바라보았다. 그러고는 깊은 사색에 잠긴 기색으로 또 여섯 모금쯤 빨고는 그들을 다시 바라보았다. 마침내 그 풍채 좋은 사내는 두 다리를 자리에 올리고 등을 벽에 기대더니, 조금도 멈추지 않고 파이프를 빨아대며, 마치 이들을 최대한 자세히 봐 두기로 작정한 듯 연기 너머로 새로 온 사람들을 빤히 응시하기 시작했다.\n\n처음에는 그 풍채 좋은 사내의 동작이 웰러 씨의 눈에 띄지 않았으나, 차츰 주인 픽윅 씨의 눈이 이따금 그쪽으로 향하는 것을 보고는, 자기도 같은 방향을 바라보기 시작하면서 동시에 손으로 눈에 그늘을 만들었으니, 마치 앞에 있는 대상을 어렴풋이 알아보고 그 정체를 확실히 가려내려는 듯했다. 그러나 그의 의심은 곧 풀렸다. 풍채 좋은 사내가 파이프에서 짙은 연기 구름을 내뿜더니, 목과 가슴을 휘감은 넉넉한 숄 아래에서 무슨 기이한 복화술의 안간힘 같은 쉰 목소리가 새어 나와, 이런 소리를 느릿느릿 내뱉었기 때문이다—\"아니, 새미!\"\n\n\"저 사람이 누구냐, 샘?\" 픽윅 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"이거 참, 믿기지가 않는군요, 나리.\" 웰러 씨가 놀란 눈으로 대답했다. \"우리 영감이네요.\"\n\n\"영감이라니,\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"무슨 영감 말이냐?\"\n\n\"제 아버지요, 나리.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다. \"어떻게 지내셨어요, 노친네?\" 그리고 이 아름다운 효심의 분출과 함께 웰러 씨는 자기 옆자리를 비워 주었고, 풍채 좋은 사내는 파이프를 입에 물고 술 단지를 손에 든 채 그를 맞으러 다가왔다.\n\n\"아니, 새미,\" 아버지가 말했다. \"이 년 하고도 더 너를 못 봤구나.\"\n\n\"그러게요, 어쩌다 보니 그랬네요, 노친네.\" 아들이 대답했다. \"새어머니는 어때요?\"\n\n\"이봐, 새미, 내 말 좀 들어 봐라.\" 웰러 영감이 자못 엄숙한 태도로 말했다. \"과부로 말할 것 같으면 그 두 번째 마누라보다 더 괜찮은 여자는 없었지—참 사랑스러운 사람이었어, 새미. 지금 그 여자에 대해 내가 할 수 있는 말이라곤, 그토록 보기 드물게 상냥한 과부였으니 신세를 바꾼 게 참으로 애석하다는 것뿐이야. 마누라 노릇은 영 못 하거든, 새미.\"\n\n\"정말요?\" 웰러 군이 물었다.\n\n웰러 영감은 고개를 절레절레 저으며 한숨과 함께 대답했다. \"내가 한 번 더 저질러 버린 거야, 새미. 한 번 더 저지른 거지. 네 아비를 본보기 삼아라, 얘야. 그리고 평생 과부를 아주 조심하렷다. 특히 술집을 해 본 과부라면 더더욱이지, 새미.\" 이 아비다운 충고를 자못 비장하게 전한 뒤, 웰러 영감은 주머니에 지니고 다니던 양철통에서 파이프를 다시 채우고는, 묵은 파이프의 재로 새 파이프에 불을 붙여 맹렬한 속도로 담배를 피우기 시작했다.\n\n\"실례합니다, 선생.\" 그는 한참 뜸을 들인 뒤 그 화제를 다시 꺼내며 픽윅 씨에게 말을 건넸다. \"별다른 뜻은 없습니다요, 선생. 선생께선 과부를 두고 계시진 않겠지요, 선생?\"\n\n\"아니요.\" 픽윅 씨가 웃으며 대답했다. 픽윅 씨가 웃는 동안, 샘 웰러는 아버지에게 자기가 그 신사와 어떤 관계인지를 속삭여 일러 주었다.\n\n\"실례했습니다, 선생.\" 웰러 영감이 모자를 벗으며 말했다. \"새미한테 흠잡으실 일은 없겠지요, 선생?\"\n\n\"전혀 없소이다.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그 말씀 들으니 참 기쁩니다요, 선생.\" 노인이 대답했다. \"제가 저 녀석 교육에 꽤나 공을 들였거든요, 선생. 아주 어렸을 때부터 거리를 쏘다니며 제 앞가림은 제가 하게 내버려뒀지요. 그게 사내아이를 영리하게 만드는 유일한 방법이거든요, 선생.\"\n\n\"꽤나 위험한 방법인 듯싶군요.\" 픽윅 씨가 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"그렇다고 아주 확실한 방법도 아니죠.\" 웰러 씨가 덧붙였다. \"얼마 전에 저도 제대로 한 방 먹었거든요.\"\n\n\"설마!\" 그의 아버지가 말했다.\n\n\"먹었다니까요.\" 아들이 말했다. 그러고는 자기가 어떻게 잡 트로터의 계략에 호락호락 넘어갔는지를 되도록 간략한 말로 이야기하기 시작했다.\n\n웰러 영감은 더없이 깊은 주의를 기울여 그 이야기를 듣고는, 끝나자 이렇게 말했다—\n\n\"그 패거리 중 하나가 호리호리하고 키 크고, 머리는 길고, 입담이 아주 청산유수 같지 않더냐?\"\n\n픽윅 씨는 마지막 묘사 항목을 썩 알아듣지는 못했으나, 첫 부분은 이해했으므로 짐작으로 \"그렇소.\" 하고 대답했다.\n\n\"다른 하나는 머리 검은 녀석으로 오디빛 제복을 입고, 머리통이 아주 크지요?\"\n\n\"그렇소, 그렇소, 맞소.\" 픽윅 씨와 샘이 자못 진지하게 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 그놈들이 어디 있는지 내가 알지, 그게 다일세.\" 웰러 씨가 말했다. \"그 둘은 입스위치에 있어, 틀림없이.\"\n\n\"설마!\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"사실입니다요.\" 웰러 씨가 말했다. \"어떻게 아는지 말씀드리죠. 제가 가끔 친구 대신 입스위치 마차를 몹니다. 선생께서 류머티즘에 걸리신 그 밤 바로 다음 날 마차를 몰고 내려갔는데, 첼름스퍼드의 블랙 보이 여관에서—바로 그놈들이 와 있던 곳이죠—그 둘을 태워서 입스위치까지 곧장 데려다줬거든요. 거기서 그 하인 녀석이—오디빛 옷 입은 놈 말입니다—한동안 묵을 거라고 하더군요.\"\n\n\"그놈을 쫓아가겠다.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"입스위치도 다른 데 못지않게 가 볼 만하지. 그놈을 쫓아가겠다.\"\n\n\"그놈들이 틀림없었어요, 영감님?\" 웰러 군이 물었다.\n\n\"틀림없어, 새미, 틀림없고말고.\" 그의 아버지가 대답했다. \"생김새가 아주 별나거든. 게다가 그 신사 양반이 하인이랑 어찌나 허물없이 구는지 의아하기도 했고, 더구나 그놈들이 마부석 바로 뒤 앞자리에 앉아서 늙은 폭죽을 어떻게 골탕 먹였느니 하며 웃고 떠드는 걸 들었으니까.\"\n\n\"늙은 누구라고요?\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"늙은 폭죽요, 선생. 그게 분명 선생을 두고 한 말일 겁니다요, 선생.\"\n\n'늙은 폭죽'이라는 호칭에 딱히 흉악하거나 잔악한 데는 없으나, 그렇다 해도 결코 존경스럽거나 듣기 좋은 명칭은 아니었다. 웰러 씨가 말을 꺼내는 순간, 징글의 손에 당했던 온갖 부당한 일들의 기억이 픽윅 씨의 마음속에 밀려들었으니, 저울추를 기울이는 데는 깃털 하나면 족했는데, '늙은 폭죽'이 바로 그 깃털 노릇을 했다.\n\n\"그놈을 쫓아가겠다.\" 픽윅 씨가 탁자를 힘껏 내려치며 말했다.\n\n\"제가 모레 입스위치까지 마차를 몰고 내려갑니다요, 선생.\" 웰러 영감이 말했다. \"화이트채플의 불 여관에서요. 정말로 가실 생각이시면 저와 함께 가시는 게 좋겠습니다.\"\n\n\"그러는 게 좋겠군.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"지당한 말이오. 베리에 편지를 써서 입스위치로 나를 만나러 오라 이르면 되겠소. 당신과 함께 가겠소. 하지만 서둘러 가지 마시오, 웰러 씨. 뭐 좀 들지 않겠소?\"\n\n\"참 친절하십니다요, 선생.\" 웰러 씨가 우뚝 멈춰 서며 대답했다.—\"브랜디 작은 잔으로 한 잔, 선생의 건강과 새미의 성공을 빌며 마시는 것쯤이야 나쁠 것 없겠지요, 선생.\"\n\n\"물론 나쁠 것 없소이다.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"여기 브랜디 한 잔!\" 브랜디가 나왔고, 웰러 씨는 픽윅 씨에게 머리카락을 잡아당겨 인사하고 샘에게 고개를 끄덕인 뒤, 마치 골무 한 모금밖에 안 되는 양 그것을 자기 널찍한 목구멍으로 꿀꺽 털어 넣었다.\n\n\"잘하셨어요, 아버지.\" 샘이 말했다. \"조심하세요, 노친네. 안 그러면 옛날 지병인 통풍이 도질 테니까요.\"\n\n\"그건 내가 특효약을 찾아냈지, 새미.\" 웰러 씨가 잔을 내려놓으며 말했다.\n\n\"통풍 특효약이라고요?\" 픽윅 씨가 황급히 수첩을 꺼내며 말했다.—\"그게 뭐요?\"\n\n\"통풍 말입니다요, 선생.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다. \"통풍이란 건 너무 편하고 안락해서 생기는 병이거든요. 혹여 통풍에 걸리시거든, 선생, 목소리 크고 그걸 쓸 줄도 제법 아는 과부랑 그저 결혼하십쇼. 그럼 다시는 통풍에 안 걸리실 겁니다. 기막힌 처방이죠, 선생. 저는 그걸 꼬박꼬박 복용하는데, 너무 흥청댄 탓에 생긴 병이라면 무엇이든 싹 쫓아낸다고 보증할 수 있습니다요.\" 이 값진 비방을 전한 뒤, 웰러 씨는 잔을 한 번 더 비우고는 힘겹게 한쪽 눈을 찡긋해 보이고, 깊은 한숨을 내쉬며 천천히 물러갔다.\n\n\"자, 네 아버지 말씀을 어떻게 생각하느냐, 샘?\" 픽윅 씨가 미소 지으며 물었다.\n\n\"어떻게 생각하느냐고요, 나리!\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다. \"글쎄요, 푸른 수염의 전속 목사가 그를 묻으면서 가엾어 눈물 한 방울 흘리며 말했듯이, 아버지는 부부 생활의 희생양이로구나 싶습니다요.\"\n\n이 더없이 적절한 결론에는 달리 대꾸할 말이 없었으므로, 픽윅 씨는 셈을 치른 뒤 그레이즈 인을 향한 발걸음을 다시 옮겼다. 그러나 그곳의 외진 숲에 다다랐을 무렵엔 이미 여덟 시를 알리는 종이 울렸고, 진흙투성이 반장화에 더러워진 흰 모자를 쓰고 낡은 옷차림을 한 신사들이 여러 출구의 길목으로 끊임없이 쏟아져 나오는 행렬은, 그날 대부분의 사무실이 문을 닫았음을 그에게 일러 주었다.\n\n가파르고 더러운 계단을 두 층 오른 뒤, 그는 자기 예상이 들어맞았음을 알았다. 퍼커 씨의 '바깥문'은 닫혀 있었고, 웰러 씨가 거기를 거듭 발로 찼으나 뒤따른 죽은 듯한 정적은 직원들이 그날 밤 일을 접고 물러갔음을 알려 주었다.\n\n\"이거 난감하구나, 샘.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"그를 만나는 데 한 시간도 허비하고 싶지 않은데. 이 일을 전문가에게 맡겼다는 생각으로 마음을 놓지 못한다면, 오늘 밤 나는 한숨도 못 잘 게 뻔하다.\"\n\n\"노파 하나가 계단을 올라오는데요, 나리.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다. \"어쩌면 누굴 찾을 수 있는지 알지도 모르죠. 여보쇼, 할멈, 퍼커 씨 사무소 사람들은 어디 있소?\"\n\n\"퍼커 씨 사무소 사람들이라.\" 비쩍 마르고 초라해 보이는 노파가 계단을 오른 뒤 숨을 고르려 멈춰 서며 말했다.—\"퍼커 씨 사무소 사람들은 다 갔고, 나는 이제 사무실 청소를 하려는 참이오.\"\n\n\"당신이 퍼커 씨의 하인이오?\" 픽윅 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"나는 퍼커 씨의 세탁부요.\" 여자가 대답했다.\n\n\"아,\" 픽윅 씨가 샘에게 반쯤 돌아서서 말했다. \"참 묘한 일이로구나, 샘. 이런 법학원에서는 노파들을 세탁부라 부르니 말이다. 대체 왜 그러는지 모르겠구나.\"\n\n\"뭐든 빠는 걸 죽도록 싫어해서 그런 것 아니겠습니까, 나리.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"그럴 만도 하구나.\" 픽윅 씨가 그 노파를 바라보며 말했는데, 그녀의 행색은 물론이거니와 이제 막 그녀가 연 사무실의 상태로 보아도 비누와 물을 들이대는 일에 뿌리 깊은 반감을 품고 있음이 드러났다. \"퍼커 씨를 어디서 찾을 수 있는지 아시오, 할멈?\"\n\n\"아니, 몰라요.\" 노파가 퉁명스레 대답했다. \"지금 시외에 나가 계시니까.\"\n\n\"그것참 안됐군.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"그의 사무원은 어디 있소? 아시오?\"\n\n\"예, 그 사람이 어디 있는지는 알지만, 알려 줘도 그쪽이 나한테 고마워하진 않을 거요.\" 세탁부가 대답했다.\n\n\"그와 아주 긴한 볼일이 있소.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"아침에 보면 안 되겠소?\" 여자가 말했다.\n\n\"그러기엔 마땅찮소.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"그럼,\" 노파가 말했다. \"아주 긴한 일이거든 그 사람 있는 데를 일러 주라고 했으니, 알려 줘도 별 탈은 없겠지. 매그파이 앤드 스텀프에 가서 바에서 로튼 씨를 찾으면, 그 사람한테 안내해 줄 거요. 그 사람이 퍼커 씨의 사무원이오.\"\n\n이런 안내를 받고, 또 그 술집이 클레어 시장 근처에 있으면서 동시에 뉴 인 뒤편과 바짝 맞닿아 있다는 이중의 이점을 누리는 어느 안뜰에 자리 잡고 있다는 추가 정보를 듣고서, 픽윅 씨와 샘은 삐걱대는 계단을 무사히 내려와 매그파이 앤드 스텀프를 찾아 나섰다.\n\n로튼 씨와 그의 패거리가 저녁마다 벌이는 잔치에 바쳐진 이 특별한 선술집은, 보통 사람들이라면 그저 술집이라 부를 만한 곳이었다. 주인이 돈벌이에 밝은 사람이라는 것은, 술청 창문 아래의 작은 칸막이 공간이—크기나 모양이 가마와 별반 다르지 않은—구두 수선공에게 세놓아져 있다는 사실로 충분히 입증되었다. 또 그가 박애심을 지닌 사람이라는 것은, 바로 그 문간 계단에서 방해받을 염려 없이 별미를 파는 파이 장수에게 그가 베풀어 준 비호로 분명히 드러났다. 사프란빛 커튼으로 꾸며진 아래층 창문에는 데번셔 사과주와 단치히 가문비주를 가리키는 인쇄된 카드 두세 장이 매달려 있었고, 한편 흰 글씨로 깨인 대중에게 이 가게의 지하 저장고에 이중 흑맥주 50만 통이 있노라 알리는 커다란 흑판은, 이 거대한 동굴이 땅속 어느 방향으로 뻗어 있으리라 짐작해야 할지에 대해 마음을 그리 불쾌하지만은 않은 의혹과 불확실 속에 남겨 두었다. 풍상에 시달린 간판이 한 마리 까치가 갈색 페인트로 그은 구부정한 줄을—이웃들이 어릴 적부터 '그루터기'로 여기도록 배워 온 그것을—골똘히 노려보는 반쯤 지워진 형상을 담고 있었다고 덧붙이면, 이 건물의 외관에 관해 굳이 더 할 말은 다 한 셈이다.\n\n픽윅 씨가 바에 나타나자, 그 안의 가림막 뒤에서 나이 든 여자 하나가 나와 그의 앞에 모습을 드러냈다.\n\n\"로튼 씨가 여기 계십니까, 부인?\" 픽윅 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"예, 계세요, 선생님.\" 여주인이 대답했다. \"얘, 찰리, 이 신사분을 로튼 씨한테로 모셔라.\"\n\n\"이 신사분은 지금은 들어가실 수 없어요.\" 빨강 머리의 어기적거리는 술집 사환이 말했다. \"로튼 씨가 익살스러운 노래를 부르고 계셔서요. 들어가면 흥을 깰 거예요. 곧 끝나실 거예요, 선생님.\"\n\n빨강 머리 사환이 미처 말을 끝맺기도 전에, 더없이 일사불란하게 탁자를 두드리고 잔을 쨍그랑대는 소리가 그 노래가 방금 끝났음을 알렸다. 그리하여 픽윅 씨는 샘에게 술청에서 한잔 즐기라 이른 뒤, 로튼 씨 앞으로 안내받아 들어갔다.\n\n\"신사 한 분이 말씀 나누자 하십니다, 선생님.\" 하는 통보에, 탁자 상석에 앉아 있던 부어오른 얼굴의 젊은이가 그 목소리가 난 쪽으로 다소 놀란 듯 바라보았다. 그리고 그 놀라움은 그의 눈길이 일찍이 본 적 없는 한 인물에 가닿았을 때 결코 줄어드는 것 같지 않았다.\n\n\"실례합니다, 선생.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"다른 신사분들까지 방해하게 되어 매우 죄송합니다만, 아주 긴한 볼일로 왔습니다. 이 방 이쪽 끝에서 오 분만 시간을 내주신다면 대단히 감사하겠습니다.\"\n\n부어오른 얼굴의 젊은이는 일어나 방 한쪽 어두운 구석에서 픽윅 씨 곁으로 의자를 바짝 끌어다 놓고, 그의 딱한 사연에 귀를 기울였다.\n\n\"아,\" 픽윅 씨가 이야기를 끝맺자 그가 말했다. \"도드슨 앤드 포그라—약삭빠른 수완이지요, 그들은—일에 밝은 사람들입니다, 도드슨 앤드 포그는, 선생.\"\n\n픽윅 씨는 도드슨 앤드 포그의 약삭빠른 수완을 인정했고, 로튼은 말을 이었다.\n\n\"퍼커는 시내에 안 계시고, 다음 주말 전엔 돌아오지도 않으실 겁니다. 하지만 소송을 방어하실 생각이고 그 사본을 저한테 맡겨 두신다면, 그분이 돌아오실 때까지 필요한 일은 제가 다 해 둘 수 있습니다.\"\n\n\"바로 그 일로 여기 온 겁니다.\" 픽윅 씨가 그 서류를 건네며 말했다. \"긴한 일이 생기면 입스위치 우체국으로 편지하시면 됩니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 됐습니다.\" 퍼커 씨의 사무원이 대답했다. 그러고는 픽윅 씨의 눈이 호기심 어린 듯 탁자 쪽으로 향하는 것을 보고 덧붙였다. \"삼십 분쯤 저희와 어울리지 않으시렵니까? 오늘 밤 여기 모인 사람들이 아주 좋은 친구들이거든요. 샘킨 앤드 그린의 사무장도 있고, 스미더스 앤드 프라이스의 형평법원 담당도 있고, 핌킨 앤드 토머스의 외근 사무원도—그 사람 노래 기막히게 잘 부르죠—거기다 잭 뱀버에, 그 밖에도 아주 많습니다. 시골에서 올라오신 것 같은데. 함께 어울리시겠어요?\"\n\n픽윅 씨는 인간 본성을 연구할 이토록 솔깃한 기회를 뿌리칠 수 없었다. 그는 탁자로 이끌려 가, 정식으로 좌중에 소개된 뒤 의장 곁의 자리를 권받았고, 자기가 좋아하는 음료 한 잔을 청했다.\n\n픽윅 씨의 예상과는 사뭇 다르게, 깊은 침묵이 뒤따랐다.\n\n\"이런 분위기가 거북하진 않으시겠지요, 선생?\" 그의 오른편 이웃이 말했는데, 체크무늬 셔츠에 모자이크 장식 단추를 달고 입에 시가를 문 신사였다.\n\n\"조금도 그렇지 않소.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다. \"나는 담배를 피우진 않습니다만, 이런 자리를 아주 좋아합니다.\"\n\n\"나는 안 피운다고는 말도 못 하겠소.\" 탁자 맞은편의 또 다른 신사가 끼어들었다. \"담배는 내게 숙식이나 마찬가지거든.\"\n\n픽윅 씨는 그 말한 이를 흘끔 보며, 만약 빨래까지 그렇다면 더 좋으련만 하고 생각했다.\n\n여기서 또 한 번 침묵이 흘렀다. 픽윅 씨는 낯선 사람이었고, 그의 등장은 분명 좌중에 찬물을 끼얹은 셈이었다.\n\n\"그런디 씨가 여러분께 노래 한 곡 들려주실 겁니다.\" 의장이 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 안 부를 거요.\" 그런디 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"왜 안 부르시오?\" 의장이 말했다.\n\n\"못 부르니까요.\" 그런디 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"안 부르겠다고 하는 게 낫겠소.\" 의장이 대꾸했다.\n\n\"그럼, 안 부르겠소.\" 그런디 씨가 받아쳤다. 좌중을 즐겁게 해 주기를 한사코 거부하는 그런디 씨 탓에 또다시 침묵이 찾아왔다.\n\n\"아무도 우릴 좀 흥겹게 해 주지 않으려오?\" 의장이 풀죽은 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"의장 양반이 직접 흥을 돋우시지 그래요?\" 탁자 끝자리에서 구레나룻에 사팔뜨기 눈, (지저분한) 풀어 헤친 셔츠 깃을 한 젊은이가 말했다.\n\n\"옳소! 옳소!\" 모자이크 장신구를 단, 담배 피우는 신사가 말했다.\n\n\"나는 노래 한 곡밖에 모르고, 그것도 이미 불렀거든. 그리고 한 곳에서 같은 노래를 한 밤에 두 번 부르면 '한 바퀴 술 돌리기' 벌금이 있단 말이오.\" 의장이 대답했다.\n\n이것은 반박할 수 없는 대답이었고, 다시금 침묵이 자리를 지배했다.\n\n\"여러분, 저는 오늘 밤,\" 픽윅 씨가 좌중 모두가 함께 논할 만한 화제를 꺼내 보려는 마음으로 말했다. \"저는 오늘 밤, 여러분 모두가 분명 잘 아시겠지만 저로서는 몇 해 동안 가 본 적이 없어 거의 알지 못하는 곳에 다녀왔습니다. 그레이즈 인 말입니다, 여러분. 런던같이 큰 곳에 자리한 이런 오래된 법학원들은 참으로 묘한 작은 구석들이지요.\"\n\n\"이런!\" 의장이 탁자 너머로 픽윅 씨에게 속삭였다. \"당신이 우리 중 적어도 한 사람은 끝없이 떠들어 댈 화제를 건드리셨군요. 잭 뱀버 영감을 끌어내실 거예요. 저 양반은 법학원 말고 다른 얘기를 하는 걸 도무지 들어 본 적이 없죠. 거기서 혼자 살다 보니 반쯤 정신이 나가 버린 양반이거든요.\"\n\n로튼이 가리킨 인물은 키 작고 누르스름하며 어깨가 올라간 사내로, 말이 없을 때면 앞으로 구부정하게 숙이는 버릇 탓에 픽윅 씨가 미처 그 얼굴을 못 알아본 터였다. 그러나 막상 그 노인이 쪼글쪼글한 얼굴을 들어 회색 눈을 픽윅 씨에게 박으며 날카롭게 캐묻는 눈초리를 보냈을 때, 픽윅 씨는 그토록 두드러진 이목구비가 어떻게 한순간이라도 자기 눈을 비껴갔을까 의아했다. 그의 얼굴에는 굳어 버린 음산한 미소가 끊임없이 어려 있었고, 그는 유난히 길게 자란 손톱이 달린 길고 앙상한 손에 턱을 괴고 있었다. 그가 고개를 한쪽으로 갸웃하고 텁수룩한 회색 눈썹 아래로 날카롭게 내다보았을 때, 그 곁눈질에는 보기에 자못 역겨운, 기이하고 사나운 교활함이 어려 있었다.\n\n이제 앞으로 불쑥 나선 것이 바로 이 인물이었고, 그는 활기 넘치는 말의 격류를 터뜨렸다. 그러나 이 장이 길어졌고, 또 그 노인이 주목할 만한 인물인 만큼, 그를 위해서도 더 정중하고 우리에게도 더 편하도록, 그가 새로운 장에서 스스로 이야기하게 두기로 한다.\n\n\"아하!\" 앞 장 끝머리에 그 태도와 외양이 간략히 묘사된 그 노인이 말했다. \"아하! 누가 그 여관들 이야기를 하고 있었던가?\"\n\n\"제가 했습니다.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다. \"그곳들이 참으로 별난 옛 장소라고 말하던 참이었지요.\"\n\n\"당신이!\" 노인이 경멸하듯 말했다. \"젊은이들이 그 적막한 방에 틀어박혀 읽고 또 읽으며, 한 시간 또 한 시간, 밤이 가고 또 밤이 와도, 마침내 그 한밤의 학업 아래 이성이 헤매기 시작하고, 정신의 힘이 다 닳아 없어지며, 아침의 빛이 와도 그들에게 아무런 생기도 건강도 가져다주지 못한 채, 젊은 기력을 메마른 옛 책들에 부자연스레 바친 끝에 스러져 가던 그 시절을 당신이 무엇을 안단 말이오? 좀 더 뒤의 시대, 아주 다른 시절로 내려와서, 이 똑같은 방에서 사람들이 겪어 온 폐병의 점진적 침몰이나 열병의 빠른 소진—'인생'과 방탕의 거창한 결말—에 대해 당신이 무엇을 안단 말이오? 자비를 헛되이 빌던 자들 가운데 얼마나 많은 이가 변호사 사무실에서 가슴이 무너진 채 돌아서서, 템스강에서 안식처를, 아니면 감옥에서 피난처를 찾았는지 짐작이나 하시오? 그곳들은 예사로운 집이 아니오. 그 낡은 벽판자에 박힌 판자 한 장이라도, 만약 말하고 기억하는 힘을 부여받는다면, 벽에서 튀어나와 제 끔찍한 이야기를 들려줄 수 있을 것이오—인생의 로맨스 말이오, 선생, 인생의 로맨스! 지금은 아무리 평범해 보일지언정, 말하건대 그곳들은 기이한 옛 장소요. 나는 무시무시한 이름이 붙은 전설을 숱하게 듣느니, 차라리 어느 낡은 방 한 채의 참된 역사를 듣는 편이 낫겠소.\"\n\n노인의 갑작스러운 격정과, 그것을 불러일으킨 화제에는 어딘가 너무도 기묘한 데가 있어서, 픽윅 씨는 대꾸할 말을 미처 준비하지 못했다. 그러자 노인은 그 격렬함을 누그러뜨리고, 앞서의 흥분 동안 사라졌던 곁눈질을 다시 되살리며 말했다.\n\n\"또 다른 측면에서 그곳들을 보시오—가장 평범하고 가장 로맨스가 없는 면을. 얼마나 훌륭한 더딘 고문의 장소들인지! 가진 것을 다 써 버리고, 스스로 거지가 되고, 친구들의 것까지 졸라내어 이 직업에 발을 들였으나, 끝내 빵 한 조각도 안겨 주지 않을 운명인 그 궁핍한 사내를 생각해 보시오. 그 기다림—희망—실망—두려움—비참—가난—꺾여 버린 그의 희망과 끝장난 그의 경력—어쩌면 자살, 아니면 추레하고 칠칠치 못한 술꾼이 되는 것. 내 말이 그르오?\" 그러더니 노인은 두 손을 비비며, 자기가 좋아하는 화제를 놓아둘 또 다른 시각을 찾아낸 것이 기쁜 듯 곁눈질했다.\n\n픽윅 씨는 큰 호기심으로 노인을 바라보았고, 나머지 일행은 미소를 띤 채 말없이 지켜보았다.\n\n\"당신네 독일 대학들 이야기를 하지만.\" 자그마한 노인이 말했다. \"쳇, 쳇! 반 마일도 가지 않고 바로 우리 안방에 로맨스가 넘쳐 나는데, 다만 사람들이 도무지 그걸 생각지 않을 뿐이오.\"\n\n\"확실히 저는 이 특별한 화제의 로맨스를 전에는 생각해 본 적이 없군요.\" 픽윅 씨가 웃으며 말했다.\n\n\"물론 안 했겠지요.\" 자그마한 노인이 말했다. \"당연하지. 내 친구 하나가 늘 내게 하던 말이 있소. '대체 방이라는 게 뭐 특별할 게 있나?' '기이한 옛 장소지.' 내가 말했소. '천만에.' 그가 말했소. '쓸쓸하지.' 내가 말했소. '조금도.' 그가 말했소. 그 친구는 어느 날 아침 바깥문을 열려다가 졸중으로 죽었소. 머리를 제 편지함에 처박은 채 쓰러져서는, 거기 열여덟 달을 누워 있었지. 다들 그가 마을을 떠난 줄 알았소.\"\n\n\"그래서 결국 어떻게 발견됐습니까?\" 픽윅 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"이 년 동안 집세를 한 푼도 내지 않으니, 법학원 간부들이 그의 문을 부수기로 했지. 그래서 그렇게 했소. 자물쇠를 비틀어 부수니, 푸른 외투에 검은 무릎바지, 비단 양말을 신은 먼지투성이 해골 하나가 문을 연 짐꾼의 팔 안으로 앞으로 고꾸라졌소. 기이하지, 그게. 좀 그렇지 않소, 그렇지 않소, 응?\" 자그마한 노인은 고개를 더욱 한쪽으로 기울이고, 이루 말할 수 없는 희열에 젖어 두 손을 비볐다.\n\n\"또 다른 사례를 알고 있소.\" 그의 낄낄거림이 어느 정도 가라앉자 자그마한 노인이 말했다. \"클리퍼드 법학원에서 일어난 일이오. 맨 위층 방의 세입자였는데—평판이 나쁜 자였소—제 침실 벽장에 틀어박혀 비소를 한 모금 들이켰소. 관리인은 그가 달아난 줄 알고, 문을 열고 세놓는다는 안내문을 붙였지. 또 다른 사내가 와서 그 방을 빌리고, 가구를 들이고, 거기 들어가 살았소. 그런데 웬일인지 그는 잠을 잘 수가 없었지—늘 안절부절못하고 불편했소. '이상하군.' 그가 말했소. '다른 방을 침실로 하고, 이 방을 거실로 삼아야겠어.' 그렇게 바꾸고 나니 밤엔 아주 잘 잤소만, 문득 어찌 된 일인지 저녁에 책을 읽을 수 없게 됐음을 알아챘소. 신경이 곤두서고 불편해져서, 늘 촛불 심지를 다듬으며 주위를 두리번거리곤 했지. '도무지 영문을 모르겠군.' 어느 날 밤 연극을 보고 돌아와 차가운 그로그 한 잔을 마시며—누가 등 뒤에 있다는 상상을 못 하도록 벽에 등을 대고 앉아—그가 말했소. '도무지 영문을 모르겠어.' 바로 그때 그의 눈이 늘 잠겨 있던 그 작은 벽장에 멎었고, 머리끝부터 발끝까지 온몸에 전율이 흘렀소. '전에도 이 이상한 느낌을 받았어.' 그가 말했소. '저 벽장에 뭔가 잘못된 게 있다는 생각을 떨칠 수가 없군.' 그는 안간힘을 다해 용기를 끌어모아, 부지깽이로 한두 번 내리쳐 자물쇠를 박살내고 문을 열었소. 그랬더니 과연, 구석에 꼿꼿이 서 있는 것은 다름 아닌 지난 세입자였는데, 작은 병을 손에 꽉 움켜쥐고, 그 얼굴은—이런!\" 자그마한 노인은 이야기를 맺으며, 놀라움에 귀 기울이는 청중의 얼굴들을 둘러보며 음산한 희열의 미소를 지었다.\n\n\"참으로 이상한 이야기들을 들려주시는군요, 선생.\" 픽윅 씨가 안경의 힘을 빌려 노인의 안색을 세세히 살피며 말했다.\n\n\"이상하다고!\" 자그마한 노인이 말했다. \"허튼소리. 당신이 그것들을 이상하게 여기는 건 그에 대해 아무것도 모르기 때문이오. 그건 우스운 일이지, 드문 일이 아니라오.\"\n\n\"우스운 일이라고요!\" 픽윅 씨가 저도 모르게 외쳤다.\n\n\"그렇소, 우습지 않소?\" 자그마한 노인이 악마 같은 곁눈질로 대답하더니, 대답을 기다리지도 않고 말을 이었다.\n\n\"또 다른 사내를 알았소—가만있자—이젠 사십 년 전 일이군—그 사내가 가장 오래된 여관들 가운데 한 곳에서, 여러 해 동안 닫힌 채 텅 비어 있던 낡고 축축하고 썩어 가는 방 한 채를 빌렸소. 그곳에 대해서는 노파들의 이야기가 잔뜩 돌았고, 결코 유쾌한 곳이라고는 할 수 없었지. 하지만 그는 가난했고 방은 쌌으니, 설령 실제보다 열 배 더 형편없었던들 그에겐 그것으로 충분한 이유가 됐을 거요. 그는 그곳에 있던 곰팡내 나는 비품 몇 가지를 떠맡아야 했는데, 그 가운데 큼지막한 유리문이 달리고 안쪽에 초록색 휘장이 쳐진, 육중하고 거추장스러운 서류용 나무 진열장이 있었소. 그에겐 거의 쓸모없는 물건이었지. 거기 넣을 서류가 없었으니까. 옷으로 말하자면 그는 그것을 몸에 지니고 다녔고, 그것도 그리 힘든 일은 아니었소. 자, 그는 가구를 모조리 들여놓았는데—짐수레 하나에도 차지 않았지—의자 넷이 되도록 열두 개처럼 보이게끔 방 안에 흩어 놓고는, 밤에 난롯불 앞에 앉아, 외상으로 주문한 위스키 두 갤런 가운데 첫 잔을 마시며, 이게 과연 값이 치러질는지, 치러진다면 몇 년 뒤에나 그리될지를 궁리하고 있었소. 그때 그의 눈이 나무 진열장의 유리문에 맞닥뜨렸소. '아.' 그가 말했소. '저 추한 물건을 늙은 중개인이 매긴 값에 떠맡지만 않았던들, 그 돈으로 뭔가 안락한 걸 장만했을 텐데. 이것 봐, 친구.' 달리 말 붙일 데가 없어 진열장을 향해 소리 내어 말했소. '네 늙은 몸뚱이를 쪼개는 데 드는 비용이, 그 뒤에 그게 가질 값어치보다 더 들지만 않는다면야, 당장에라도 너로 불을 피울 텐데.' 그 말을 채 끝내기도 전에, 그 궤짝 속에서 희미한 신음 같은 소리가 새어 나오는 듯했소. 처음엔 깜짝 놀랐지만, 잠시 곰곰 생각해 보니 옆방에서 밖에 나가 식사를 하고 온 어느 젊은이일 거라 여기고는, 두 발을 난로 가리개 위에 올리고 불을 쑤시려 부지깽이를 들어 올렸소. 그 순간 그 소리가 다시 울렸고, 유리문 하나가 천천히 열리더니, 더럽고 해진 옷차림에 창백하고 수척한 형체가 진열장 안에 꼿꼿이 서 있는 것이 드러났소. 그 형체는 키가 크고 야위었으며, 얼굴에는 근심과 불안이 어려 있었지. 하지만 그 살갗의 빛깔과, 온몸의 수척하고 이승의 것 같지 않은 모습에는, 이 세상 어떤 존재도 띤 적 없는 무엇인가가 있었소. '당신은 누구요?' 새 세입자가 새파랗게 질리면서도, 부지깽이를 손에 겨누어 형체의 얼굴을 제법 정확히 조준하며 말했소. '당신은 누구요?' '그 부지깽이를 내게 던지지 마시오.' 형체가 대답했소. '제아무리 정확히 겨누어 던진들, 그것은 아무 저항 없이 나를 꿰뚫고 지나가 뒤의 나무에다 그 힘을 다 쏟을 뿐이오. 나는 영혼이오.' '그래, 대체 여기서 무엇을 바라는 거요?' 세입자가 더듬거렸소. '이 방에서.' 유령이 대답했소. '나의 세속적 파멸이 이루어졌고, 나와 내 자식들이 거지가 되었소. 이 진열장에는 여러 해 동안 쌓여 온, 길고도 긴 소송의 서류들이 보관되어 있었소. 이 방에서, 내가 슬픔과 오래 미루어진 희망 끝에 죽고 나자, 교활한 독수리 두 마리가, 내가 비참한 일생 동안 다투어 온 그 재산을 나누어 가졌고, 끝내 내 불행한 후손들에겐 단 한 푼도 남지 않았소. 나는 그자들을 이곳에서 겁주어 쫓아냈고, 그날 이후로 밤이면—내가 이 땅을 다시 찾을 수 있는 유일한 때라오—오래 끌어온 내 비참의 무대를 어슬렁거려 왔소. 이 거처는 내 것이오. 내게 비워 주시오.' '굳이 여기 나타나야 하겠다면야.' 유령의 그 지루한 진술 동안 마음을 추스를 틈을 얻은 세입자가 말했소. '더없이 기꺼이 점유권을 내드리리다. 다만 허락하신다면 한 가지 물어보고 싶소.' '말하시오.' 유령이 엄하게 말했소. '저.' 세입자가 말했소. '이 말을 당신께 개인적으로 적용하는 건 아니오. 내가 들어 본 대부분의 유령에게 똑같이 해당하는 말이니까. 하지만 내가 보기엔 좀 앞뒤가 안 맞는 듯하오. 당신은 이 땅의 가장 아름다운 곳들을 찾아갈 기회가 있을 텐데—당신에게 공간이란 아무것도 아닐 테니 말이오—어째서 늘 하필 가장 비참했던 바로 그 장소들로만 정확히 돌아오는 거요?' '아이고, 그건 참으로 옳은 말이오. 전에는 그런 생각을 미처 못 했군.' 유령이 말했소. '보시다시피, 선생.' 세입자가 말을 이었소. '여긴 아주 불편한 방이오. 저 진열장 꼴을 보아하니 빈대가 전혀 없지도 않을 것 같소. 그러니 정말이지 훨씬 더 안락한 거처를 찾으실 수 있을 거요. 게다가 런던의 기후는 지극히 불쾌하기 짝이 없으니 말할 것도 없고요.' '당신 말이 참으로 옳소, 선생.' 유령이 정중하게 말했소. '지금껏 그런 생각이 떠오른 적이 없었구려. 당장 공기를 바꿔 보리다.'—그리고 실은, 그가 말하는 동안 이미 사라지기 시작하더니, 다리는 아예 완전히 자취를 감추었소. '그리고, 선생.' 세입자가 그를 향해 외쳤소. '낡고 텅 빈 집들을 출몰하는 일에 지금 골몰해 계신 다른 신사 숙녀분들께, 다른 데 가면 훨씬 더 편안하리라고 일러 주실 친절을 베푸신다면, 사회에 참으로 큰 은덕을 베푸시는 거요.' '그러리다.' 유령이 대답했소. '우리는 어지간히 둔한 작자들임이 틀림없소—참으로 둔한 작자들 말이오. 어떻게 우리가 그토록 멍청할 수 있었는지 도무지 모르겠구려.' 이 말과 함께 영혼은 사라졌소. 그리고 자못 주목할 만한 것은.\" 노인이 식탁을 빈틈없이 둘러보며 덧붙였다. \"그가 두 번 다시 돌아오지 않았다는 거요.\"\n\n\"사실이라면 그리 나쁘지 않군요.\" 모자이크 장식 단추를 단 사내가 새 시가에 불을 붙이며 말했다.\n\n\"사실이라면이라고!\" 노인이 지극한 경멸의 표정으로 외쳤다. \"내 짐작에는.\" 그가 로튼을 돌아보며 덧붙였다. \"이 친구는 다음엔, 내가 변호사 사무실에 있을 적에 우리에게 찾아온 그 기이한 의뢰인 이야기도 사실이 아니라고 할 게요—그래도 난 놀라지 않겠지만.\"\n\n\"저야 그 이야기를 들어 본 적이 없으니, 그것에 대해선 아무 말도 감히 못 하지요.\" 모자이크 장식의 소유자가 말했다.\n\n\"그 이야기를 한번 들려주시지요, 선생.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"아, 그래요.\" 로튼이 말했다. \"그건 저 말고는 아무도 못 들었는데, 저마저 거의 잊어버렸으니까요.\"\n\n노인은 식탁을 둘러보았고, 모든 얼굴에 그려진 주목에 의기양양한 듯, 어느 때보다 더 소름 끼치게 곁눈질했다. 그러고는 손으로 턱을 문지르고, 그 정황들을 기억에서 불러내려는 듯 천장을 올려다보며, 다음과 같이 시작했다.\n기이한 의뢰인에 관한 노인의 이야기\n\n# THE OLD MAN’S TALE ABOUT THE QUEER CLIENT\n\n\"내가 이 짤막한 사연을 어디서, 어떻게 주워듣게 됐는지는 별로 중요치 않소.\" 노인이 말했다. \"이것이 내게 전해진 순서대로 이야기하려 든다면, 중간에서 시작해 결말에 이르렀다가 다시 처음을 찾아 거슬러 가야 할 거요. 그저 그 정황의 일부는 내 눈앞에서 지나간 일이라는 것, 나머지는 실제로 일어난 일임을 내가 안다는 것, 그리고 그것을 너무도 잘 기억할 사람들이 아직 더러 살아 있다는 것만 말해 두면 되겠소.\n\n자치구 큰길, 세인트 조지 교회 가까이, 그 길의 같은 편에, 대다수 사람들이 알다시피, 우리네 채무자 감옥 가운데 가장 작은 마셜시가 서 있소. 후대에 와서는 한때의 그 오물과 더러움의 소굴과는 사뭇 다른 곳이 되었지만, 개선된 지금의 상태조차도 낭비하는 자에게 별다른 유혹이 되지 못하고, 앞날을 헤아리지 못하는 자에게 별다른 위안이 되지 못하오. 사형을 선고받은 중죄인이 뉴게이트에서 누리는 바람 쐬고 운동할 마당이, 마셜시 감옥의 지급 불능 채무자가 누리는 것만큼이나 좋다오. [더 낫지. 하지만 이는 지난 일, 더 나은 시대에 지나간 일이고, 그 감옥은 이제 존재하지 않소.]\n\n내 공상일지도 모르고, 아니면 그곳을 그에 얽힌 옛 기억들로부터 떼어 낼 수 없어서일지도 모르나, 나는 런던의 이 일대를 견딜 수가 없소. 거리는 넓고 가게들은 널찍하며, 지나가는 마차 소리와 끊임없이 흐르는 인파의 발소리—교통의 온갖 분주한 소리들이 아침부터 한밤중까지 거기서 울리지만, 그 둘레의 골목들은 누추하고 답답하오. 가난과 방탕이 붐비는 골목에 곪아 들고, 궁핍과 불운이 좁은 감옥에 갇혀 있소. 적어도 내 눈에는, 음울하고 황량한 기운이 그 풍경에 감돌면서, 거기에 더럽고 병약한 빛깔을 입히는 것만 같소.\n\n그 옛 마셜시 감옥의 문으로 처음 들어설 때, 지금은 무덤 속에 닫힌 지 오래된 숱한 눈들이 그 풍경을 제법 가볍게 둘러보았을 거요. 절망이란 불운의 첫 호된 충격과 함께 찾아오는 일이 드무니까. 사람은 시험해 보지 않은 친구들에 대한 믿음을 품고, 자신이 도움을 바라지 않을 때 술친구들이 그토록 선뜻 베풀던 숱한 봉사의 제안을 떠올리며, 희망을—행복한 무경험의 희망을—품소. 첫 충격에 아무리 무너진다 한들, 그것은 가슴속에서 다시 솟아올라 잠시 동안 거기서 피어나다가, 마침내 실망과 외면의 시들음 아래 고개를 떨구지. 채무자들이 풀려날 가망도, 자유의 전망도 없이 감옥에서 썩어 간다는 말이 결코 비유가 아니던 시절, 그 똑같은 눈들이 머리 깊숙이 꺼져 들어, 굶주림에 야위고 감금으로 누렇게 뜬 얼굴에서 이글거리기까지는 얼마나 빨랐던가! 그 잔혹함이 온전한 정도로는 더 이상 존재하지 않으나, 가슴을 피 흘리게 하는 일들을 일으키기에 족할 만큼은 아직 남아 있소.\n\n이십 년 전, 그 보도는 한 어머니와 아이의 발소리에 닳아 있었소. 그들은 날마다, 아침이 오면 어김없이 감옥 문 앞에 모습을 드러냈지. 안식 없는 비참과 근심 어린 생각으로 지새운 밤 끝에, 꼬박 한 시간이나 일찍 거기 와 있는 일도 잦았소. 그러면 젊은 어머니는 온순히 돌아서서 아이를 옛 다리로 데려가, 두 팔로 안아 올려 반짝이는 강물을—아침 햇살로 물들고, 그 이른 시각 강이 펼쳐 보이는 사업과 즐거움의 온갖 분주한 채비로 일렁이는 그 물을—보여 주며, 눈앞의 것들에 아이의 마음을 끌어 보려 애썼소. 하지만 그녀는 곧 아이를 내려놓고, 숄에 얼굴을 묻은 채, 눈을 가리는 눈물을 쏟아 내곤 했소. 아이의 여위고 병약한 얼굴에는 흥미나 즐거움의 빛이 도무지 어리지 않았으니까. 그 아이의 기억은 몇 개 되지 않았으나, 죄다 한 가지뿐이었소—모두가 제 부모의 가난과 비참에 얽힌 것이었지. 그 아이는 한 시간 또 한 시간 어머니 무릎에 앉아, 어린아이다운 연민으로 어머니 얼굴을 타고 흐르는 눈물을 지켜보다가, 어느 어두운 구석으로 조용히 기어가 흐느끼다 잠들곤 했소. 세상의 모진 현실은, 그 가장 혹독한 결핍의 여러 가지—굶주림과 목마름, 추위와 궁핍—와 더불어, 이성이 처음 동트던 때부터 그 아이에게 죄다 다가와 있었소. 그리고 어린아이의 형상은 거기 있었으나, 그 가벼운 마음도, 그 명랑한 웃음도, 반짝이는 눈동자도 없었소.\n\n아버지와 어머니는 이것을 바라보았고, 또 서로를 바라보았소. 차마 말로 내뱉지 못할 고뇌의 생각을 품은 채로. 활발히 일하는 데서 오는 거의 어떤 피로도 견딜 수 있었을 건강하고 다부진 사내는, 붐비는 감옥의 갑갑한 감금과 건강에 해로운 공기 아래 쇠약해져 갔소. 가냘프고 연약한 여인은 육체와 정신의 병이 한데 미친 영향 아래 가라앉아 갔소. 아이의 어린 가슴은 무너지고 있었소.\n\n겨울이 왔고, 그와 함께 몇 주에 걸친 춥고 거센 비가 왔소. 그 가엾은 여인은 남편이 갇힌 곳 바로 가까이의 누추한 거처로 옮겨 갔소. 그 이사는 날로 깊어 가는 가난 탓에 어쩔 수 없이 하게 된 것이었으나, 그녀는 이제 남편에게 더 가까웠으니 더 행복했소. 두 달 동안 그녀와 그 어린 동행은 여느 때처럼 문 열리는 것을 지켜보았소. 어느 날 그녀는 처음으로 오지 못했소. 또 다른 아침이 왔고, 그녀는 혼자 왔소. 아이가 죽은 것이었소.\n\n가난한 자의 사별을, 떠난 이에게는 고통에서 벗어나는 행복한 해방이요 남은 이에게는 비용에서 벗어나는 자비로운 안도라고 차갑게 입에 올리는 자들은, 그 사별의 고뇌가 어떤 것인지를 거의 모르오—거의 모른다고 나는 말하오. 다른 모든 눈이 차갑게 돌아설 때 보내오는 애정과 존중의 말 없는 눈길, 다른 모든 이가 우리를 버렸을 때 한 존재의 연민과 애정을 우리가 지니고 있다는 그 의식—그것은 가장 깊은 고난 속에서, 어떤 재물로도 살 수 없고 어떤 권력으로도 베풀 수 없는 의지처요, 버팀목이요, 위안이라오. 그 아이는 부모의 발치에 몇 시간이고 함께 앉아, 작은 두 손을 참을성 있게 맞잡고, 여위고 핼쑥한 얼굴을 그들을 향해 쳐든 채 있곤 했소. 그들은 그 아이가 날이면 날마다 시들어 가는 것을 지켜보았소. 그 짧은 생애가 기쁨이라곤 없는 것이었고, 이제는 어린아이였음에도 이 세상에서 결코 알지 못했던 그 평화와 안식으로 옮겨 갔다 한들, 그들은 그 아이의 부모였고, 그 상실은 그들 영혼 깊숙이 가라앉았소.\n\n어머니의 변해 버린 얼굴을 바라본 이들에게는, 죽음이 머지않아 그녀의 역경과 시련의 무대를 닫으리라는 것이 분명했소. 남편의 동료 죄수들은 그의 슬픔과 비참을 방해하기를 꺼려, 그가 전에 두 동료와 함께 쓰던 작은 방을 그 혼자에게 내주었소. 그녀는 그와 그 방을 함께 썼고, 고통 없이, 그러나 희망도 없이 명을 이어 가다가, 그 생명이 천천히 스러져 갔소.\n\n어느 저녁 그녀는 남편의 품에서 정신을 잃었고, 그는 그녀를 되살리려 바람을 쐬게 하려고 열린 창가로 그녀를 안고 갔소. 그때 달빛이 그녀의 얼굴에 가득 떨어지며, 그에게 그녀의 이목구비에 어린 변화를 보여 주었으니, 그는 무력한 갓난아이처럼 그녀의 무게 아래 비틀거렸소.\n\n'나를 내려 줘요, 조지.' 그녀가 힘없이 말했소. 그가 그렇게 하고는, 그녀 곁에 앉아 두 손으로 얼굴을 가린 채 울음을 터뜨렸소.\n\n'당신을 두고 가기가 너무도 힘드네요, 조지.' 그녀가 말했소. '하지만 이건 하느님의 뜻이니, 나를 위해서라도 견뎌야 해요. 오! 우리 아이를 데려가 주신 그분께 얼마나 감사한지! 그 애는 이제 행복하게, 천국에 있어요. 여기서 엄마 없이 그 애가 어찌 살았겠어요!'\n\n'당신은 죽지 않을 거야, 메리, 죽지 않아.' 남편이 벌떡 일어서며 말했소. 그는 주먹 쥔 손으로 제 머리를 치며 황급히 이리저리 거닐다가, 다시 그녀 곁에 앉아 그녀를 두 팔로 받치며 한결 차분히 덧붙였소. '정신 차려요, 여보. 제발, 제발 그래 줘요. 당신은 아직 회복할 거야.'\n\n'다신 안 돼요, 조지. 다신.' 죽어 가는 여인이 말했소. '이제 나를 우리 가엾은 아이 곁에 뉘여 줘요. 다만 약속해 줘요, 언젠가 당신이 이 끔찍한 곳을 떠나 부유해지거든, 우리를 어딘가 조용한 시골 교회 묘지로, 아주 멀리, 멀리—여기서 아주 먼 곳으로 옮겨 주겠다고. 우리가 평화로이 쉴 수 있는 곳으로. 사랑하는 조지, 그러겠다고 약속해 줘요.'\n\n'약속해, 약속하지.' 사내가 그녀 앞에 격정에 차 무릎을 던지며 말했소. '내게 말해 줘요, 메리, 한마디만 더. 눈길 한 번만—단 한 번만!'\n\n그는 말을 멈췄소. 그의 목을 감았던 팔이 뻣뻣하고 무거워졌기 때문이오. 그의 앞에 있는 쇠잔한 형체에서 깊은 한숨이 새어 나왔고, 입술이 움직이며 얼굴에 미소가 어렸소. 하지만 그 입술은 핏기 없이 창백했고, 그 미소는 굳어 버린 섬뜩한 응시로 잦아들었소. 그는 이 세상에 홀로 남았소.\n\n그날 밤, 그 비참한 방의 정적과 적막 속에서, 그 가련한 사내는 아내의 시신 곁에 무릎을 꿇고, 하느님을 증인으로 삼아 무서운 맹세를 외쳤소. 그 시각부터 그는 아내와 아이의 죽음을 복수하는 데 자신을 바치겠노라고, 그날 이후 제 생의 마지막 순간까지 온 기력을 이 한 가지 목표에 쏟겠노라고, 그 복수는 길게 끌며 무시무시하리라고, 그 증오는 죽지도 꺼지지도 않은 채 세상 끝까지 그 대상을 쫓으리라고.\n\n그 단 하룻밤에, 더없이 깊은 절망과 인간의 것이라곤 거의 할 수 없는 격정이 그의 얼굴과 몸에 어찌나 사납게 자국을 남겼던지, 그가 지나갈 때면 불운을 함께한 동료들이 겁에 질려 그에게서 움츠러들었소. 그의 눈은 핏발이 서고 무거웠으며, 얼굴은 죽은 듯이 희었고, 몸은 노쇠한 듯 굽어 있었소. 정신적 고통의 격렬함 속에서 그는 아랫입술을 거의 깨물어 뚫어 놓았고, 그 상처에서 흐른 피가 턱을 타고 흘러내려 셔츠와 목수건을 물들였소. 눈물 한 방울, 한탄의 소리 한 마디 새어 나오지 않았으나, 그가 마당을 오르내리는 동요된 눈빛과 흐트러진 조급함은 그 안에서 타오르는 열병을 드러내고 있었소.\n\n아내의 시신을 지체 없이 감옥에서 내가야 했소. 그는 그 통보를 더없이 차분하게 받아들였고, 그것이 마땅함을 수긍했소. 감옥의 거의 모든 수감자가 그 운구를 지켜보려 모여들었는데, 홀아비가 나타나자 그들은 양옆으로 물러섰소. 그는 황급히 앞으로 걸어 나가, 수위실 문 가까이 작게 난간으로 둘린 구역에 홀로 자리를 잡았소. 군중은 본능적인 배려의 정으로 거기서 물러나 있었지. 거친 관이 사람들의 어깨에 얹혀 천천히 앞으로 옮겨졌소. 무리 사이에 죽은 듯한 정적이 감돌았고, 그것을 깨뜨리는 것이라곤 여인들의 또렷한 통곡과, 돌바닥을 끄는 운구꾼들의 발소리뿐이었소. 그들은 사별한 남편이 서 있는 곳에 이르러—멈춰 섰소. 그는 관에 손을 얹고, 그 위에 덮인 관보를 기계적으로 가다듬더니, 그들에게 계속 가라고 손짓했소. 그것이 지나갈 때 감옥 현관의 간수들은 모자를 벗었고, 다음 순간 육중한 문이 그 뒤로 닫혔소. 그는 멍하니 군중을 바라보더니, 땅바닥에 무겁게 쓰러졌소.\n\n이 일이 있고 여러 주 동안, 그는 더없이 거친 열병의 헛소리 속에서 밤낮으로 지켜졌으나, 자신의 상실에 대한 의식도, 자신이 한 맹세에 대한 기억도, 한순간도 그를 떠나지 않았소. 그의 눈앞에서 장면들이 바뀌고, 장소가 장소를 잇고, 사건이 사건을 뒤따르며 섬망의 온갖 분주함이 펼쳐졌으나, 그것들은 모두 어떤 식으로든 그의 마음의 그 거대한 목표와 이어져 있었소. 그는 가없는 바다 위를 항해하고 있었고, 위로는 핏빛 붉은 하늘이, 아래로는 광란에 채찍질당한 성난 물결이 사방에서 끓어오르며 소용돌이쳤소. 앞에는 또 다른 배 한 척이 울부짖는 폭풍 속에서 안간힘 쓰며 고생하고 있었소. 그 돛은 돛대에서 누더기처럼 펄럭였고, 갑판에는 양옆에 몸을 묶은 형체들이 빽빽이 들어차 있었는데, 그 위로 시시각각 거대한 파도가 부서지며 가엾은 몇몇을 거품 이는 바다로 휩쓸어 갔소. 무엇으로도 막을 수 없는 속도와 힘으로, 그들은 포효하는 물 더미 한가운데를 헤치고 앞으로 나아가, 맨 앞 배의 이물을 들이받아 제 용골 아래 그것을 으스러뜨렸소. 가라앉는 난파선이 일으킨 거대한 소용돌이로부터 비명이 솟았는데—익사하는 백 명의 단말마가 하나의 사나운 절규로 한데 뒤섞인 그것은—어찌나 크고 날카롭던지, 자연의 전투 함성 위로 까마득히 울려 퍼지며 메아리치고 또 메아리쳐, 공기와 하늘과 대양을 꿰뚫는 듯했소. 한데 저것은 무엇인가—물 위로 솟아올라, 고뇌의 표정과 도와 달라는 비명으로 파도와 싸우는 저 늙은 잿빛 머리는! 한 번 보더니, 그는 뱃전에서 뛰어내려, 힘찬 팔놀림으로 그것을 향해 헤엄쳐 갔소. 그것에 다다랐고, 바싹 다가갔소. 그것은 바로 그자의 얼굴이었소. 노인은 그가 다가오는 것을 보고, 그 손아귀를 벗어나려 헛되이 발버둥쳤소. 하지만 그는 노인을 꽉 붙잡아 물 밑으로 끌고 내려갔소. 아래로, 아래로, 그와 함께 쉰 길 아래로. 노인의 발버둥은 점점 희미해지다가 마침내 완전히 멎었소. 노인은 죽었소. 그는 노인을 죽였고, 제 맹세를 지켰소.\n\n그는 맨발에 홀로, 어느 광대한 사막의 타는 듯한 모래를 가로질러 나아가고 있었소. 모래가 그를 숨 막히게 하고 눈멀게 했으며, 그 곱고 가는 알갱이들이 살갗의 모공 속까지 파고들어 그를 거의 미칠 지경으로 들볶았소. 바람에 실려 앞으로 떠밀려 가고, 타는 듯한 태양에 꿰뚫린 똑같은 물질의 거대한 덩어리들이 멀리서 살아 있는 불기둥처럼 우뚝우뚝 걸어 다녔소. 그 황량한 불모지에서 스러진 사람들의 뼈가 그의 발치에 흩어져 있었고, 무시무시한 빛이 주위 만물에 떨어졌소. 눈이 닿는 데까지, 두려움과 공포의 대상밖에는 아무것도 나타나지 않았소. 혀가 입천장에 들러붙은 채 공포의 비명을 지르려 헛되이 애쓰며, 그는 미친 듯이 앞으로 내달렸소. 초자연적인 힘으로 무장한 채, 그는 모래를 헤치고 나아가다가, 마침내 피로와 갈증에 지쳐 정신을 잃고 땅바닥에 쓰러졌소. 무슨 향기로운 서늘함이 그를 되살렸는가. 저 콸콸 솟는 소리는 무엇이었던가. 물이었소! 그것은 과연 우물이었고, 맑고 시원한 물줄기가 그의 발치에 흐르고 있었소. 그는 그것을 깊이 들이켜고, 욱신거리는 사지를 둑에 던지더니, 황홀한 혼몽 속으로 가라앉았소. 다가오는 발소리가 그를 깨웠소. 잿빛 머리의 노인 하나가 타는 듯한 갈증을 축이려 비틀비틀 다가왔소. 또 그자였소! 그는 두 팔로 노인의 몸을 휘감아 끌어당겨 막았소. 노인은 발버둥치며 물을 달라고 비명을 질렀소—제 목숨을 구할 단 한 방울의 물을! 하지만 그는 노인을 단단히 붙잡고, 게걸스러운 눈으로 그 고통을 지켜보았소. 그리고 노인의 생기 없는 머리가 그의 가슴 위로 앞으로 떨어지자, 그는 발로 그 시신을 제게서 굴려 냈소.\n\n열병이 그를 떠나고 의식이 돌아왔을 때, 그는 깨어나 자신이 부유하고 자유로운 몸이 되었음을, 그리고 자신을 감옥에서 죽게 내버려 두려 했던—기어이!—제 목숨보다 훨씬 더 소중했던 이들을 궁핍과, 약으로도 못 고칠 마음의 병으로 죽게 내버려 둔 그 어버이가, 제 깃털 침대에서 죽은 채로 발견되었다는 소식을 들었소. 그자는 제 아들을 거지로 남길 만한 모진 마음을 충분히 품고 있었으나, 제 건강과 기력을 자랑스러워한 나머지 그 일을 미루다가, 마침내 때를 놓쳤고, 이제 저세상에서 제 태만이 그에게 남겨 준 재산을 생각하며 이를 갈지도 모를 노릇이었소. 그는 이 사실에 눈떴고, 그 이상의 것에도 눈떴소. 자신이 무엇을 위해 살아가는지 그 목적을 다시 떠올렸고, 자신의 원수가 다름 아닌 제 아내의 친아버지—자신을 감옥에 처넣은 자요, 제 딸과 그 손주가 발치에서 자비를 빌었을 때 문전에서 그들을 박차 버린 자—임을 기억해 냈소. 오, 그가 일어나 제 복수의 계획에 부지런히 나서지 못하게 막은 그 쇠약함을 그는 얼마나 저주했던가!\n\n그는 자신을 그 상실과 비참의 무대에서 옮겨, 해안가의 조용한 거처로 데려가게 했소. 마음의 평화나 행복을 되찾으려는 바람에서가 아니었으니, 그 둘은 다 영영 달아나 버렸기 때문이오. 다만 무너진 기력을 되살리고, 제 간절한 목표를 골똘히 궁리하기 위해서였소. 그리고 여기서, 어떤 악령이 그가 첫 번째이자 가장 끔찍한 복수를 행할 기회를 그의 앞에 던져 놓았소.\n\n여름철이었소. 그는 음울한 생각에 잠긴 채, 저녁 일찍 외딴 거처에서 나와, 절벽 아래 좁은 길을 따라, 떠돌다 마음에 든 거칠고 호젓한 곳까지 거닐어 가서, 떨어져 나온 바위 조각에 앉아 두 손에 얼굴을 묻은 채 몇 시간이고 거기 머물곤 했소—때로는 밤이 완전히 깔리고, 머리 위 우뚝 솟은 절벽의 긴 그림자가 그 가까이 만물 위로 짙고 검은 어둠을 드리울 때까지.\n\n어느 고요한 저녁, 그는 옛 자세 그대로 여기 앉아, 이따금 고개를 들어 갈매기의 비상을 바라보거나, 대양 한복판에서 시작해 해가 지는 바로 그 끝자락까지 이어지는 듯한 그 찬란한 진홍빛 길을 따라 눈길을 옮기고 있었소. 그때 그곳의 깊은 정적을 깨뜨리며 도와 달라는 큰 외침이 들려왔소. 그는 제가 제대로 들었는지 미심쩍어하며 귀를 기울였는데, 그 외침이 전보다 한층 더 격렬하게 되풀이되자, 그는 벌떡 일어나 그 소리가 나는 쪽으로 서둘러 갔소.\n\n그 사연은 한눈에 절로 드러났소. 흩어진 옷가지 몇 점이 모래밭에 놓여 있었고, 사람의 머리 하나가 해안에서 조금 떨어진 파도 위로 간신히 보였으며, 한 노인이 고뇌에 차 두 손을 비틀며 이리저리 뛰어다니면서 도움을 구해 비명을 지르고 있었소. 이제 기력이 어지간히 회복된 그 병약자는 외투를 벗어 던지고, 물속에 뛰어들어 익사하는 자를 해안으로 끌어내려는 작정으로 바다를 향해 내달렸소.\n\n'이리로 서둘러 오시오, 선생, 하느님의 이름으로. 도와주시오, 도와주시오, 선생, 하늘의 사랑으로. 저 애는 제 아들이오, 선생, 제 외아들이오!' 노인이 그를 맞으러 다가오며 미친 듯이 말했소. '제 외아들이오, 선생, 그런데 제 아비의 눈앞에서 죽어 가고 있소!'\n\n노인이 입을 떼어 첫 마디를 내뱉는 순간, 그 낯선 사내는 내닫던 걸음을 멈추고, 두 팔을 포갠 채 미동도 없이 우뚝 섰소.\n\n'아아 하느님!' 노인이 뒷걸음치며 외쳤소. '헤일링!'\n\n낯선 사내는 미소 지을 뿐 말이 없었소.\n\n'헤일링!' 노인이 사납게 말했소. '내 아들, 헤일링, 내 사랑하는 아들, 저기, 저기 좀 봐!' 숨을 헐떡이며, 그 비참한 아비는 젊은이가 목숨을 부지하려 발버둥치는 곳을 가리켰소.\n\n'들어 봐!' 노인이 말했소. '저 애가 또 한 번 외쳐. 아직 살아 있어. 헤일링, 저 애를 구해 줘, 구해 줘!'\n\n낯선 사내는 다시 미소 지었고, 조각상처럼 꼼짝도 하지 않았소.\n\n'내가 자네에게 잘못했네.' 노인이 무릎을 꿇고 두 손을 맞잡으며 비명을 질렀소. '복수하게. 내 모든 것을, 내 목숨을 가져가게. 자네 발치의 물속에 나를 던져 넣게. 그리고 인간의 본성이 발버둥을 억누를 수 있다면, 나는 손발 하나 까딱 않고 죽겠네. 그리하게, 헤일링, 그리하게. 다만 내 아들을 구해 줘. 저 애는 너무 어려, 헤일링, 죽기엔 너무 어려!'\n\n'들어 보시오.' 낯선 사내가 노인의 손목을 사납게 움켜쥐며 말했소. '나는 목숨에는 목숨을 받겠소. 그리고 여기 하나가 있소. 내 자식은 제 아비의 눈앞에서, 제 누이의 가치를 헐뜯던 저 젊은 비방자가 내가 말하는 지금 맞이하고 있는 것보다 훨씬 더 고통스럽고 괴로운 죽음을 맞아 죽었소. 그때 당신은 웃었소—이미 죽음이 손을 얹은 당신 딸의 얼굴에 대고, 우리의 고통을 비웃으며 웃었소. 이제 그 고통을 어찌 생각하오! 저기를 보시오, 저기를!'\n\n낯선 사내가 말하며 바다를 가리켰소. 희미한 외침 하나가 그 수면 위에서 잦아들었고, 죽어 가는 사내의 마지막 안간힘이 잔물결을 몇 초간 흔들었소. 그리고 그가 이른 무덤으로 가라앉은 그 자리는 둘레의 물과 분간할 수 없게 되었소.\n\n삼 년이 흘렀을 무렵, 한 신사가 자가용 마차에서 내려, 당시 직업적 거래에서 그리 깐깐하지 않은 사람으로 잘 알려져 있던 런던의 한 변호사 집 문 앞에 서서, 중요한 용무로 사적인 면담을 청했소. 분명 인생의 한창때를 넘기지 않았는데도, 그의 얼굴은 창백하고 초췌하며 풀이 죽어 있었소. 질병이나 고통이 그의 평생을 두 배로 합친 세월 동안 한낱 시간의 손이 이룰 수 있었을 것보다 더 큰 변화를 그의 외양에 빚어냈음을 한눈에 알아보는 데는, 사업가의 예리한 통찰력까지는 필요치 않았소.\n\n'나를 위해 어떤 법률 사무를 맡아 주셨으면 하오.' 낯선 사내가 말했소.\n\n변호사는 굽실거리며 절하고, 그 신사가 손에 든 큼지막한 꾸러미를 흘끗 보았소. 손님은 그 눈길을 알아채고 말을 이었소.\n\n'예사로운 일이 아니오.' 그가 말했소. '또한 이 서류들이 오랜 수고와 막대한 비용 없이 내 손에 들어온 것도 아니오.'\n\n변호사는 그 꾸러미를 더욱 갈망에 찬 눈길로 던졌소. 그리고 손님은 그것을 묶은 끈을 풀어, 한 무더기의 약속어음과, 증서 사본들과 그 밖의 문서들을 드러냈소.\n\n'이 서류들로.' 의뢰인이 말했소. '여기 이름이 적힌 사내가, 보시다시피 지난 여러 해 동안 거액의 돈을 빌렸소. 그와, 이 어음들이 원래 넘어갔던 자들—나는 그들로부터 명목 가치의 세 배, 네 배를 주고 차츰차츰 전부를 사들였소—사이에는 암묵적인 양해가 있었소. 정해진 기한이 지날 때까지 이 차용을 그때그때 갱신해 주기로 한 거요. 그런 양해는 어디에도 명시되어 있지 않소. 그는 근래 많은 손실을 입었소. 그러니 이 채무들이 한꺼번에 그에게 쌓이면, 그를 땅바닥에 짓뭉개 버릴 거요.'\n\n'총액이 수천 파운드에 달하는군요.' 변호사가 서류를 훑어보며 말했소.\n\n'그렇소.' 의뢰인이 말했소.\n\n'우리가 어찌하면 되겠습니까?' 사업가가 물었소.\n\n'어찌하다니!' 의뢰인이 갑작스러운 격정으로 대답했소. '법의 온갖 장치를 다 동원하고, 교묘한 머리로 짜낼 수 있고 악랄함으로 실행할 수 있는 온갖 술수를 다 쓰시오. 정당한 수단이든 부정한 수단이든. 법의 노골적인 압제에다, 그 가장 교활한 종사자들의 온갖 술책을 더하시오. 나는 그자가 시달리며 더디게 끌려가는 죽음을 맞기를 바라오. 그를 파멸시키고, 그의 토지와 재물을 압류해 팔아 치우고, 그를 집과 가정에서 내쫓고, 늘그막에 거지로 끌어내어 흔한 감옥에서 죽게 하시오.'\n\n'하지만 비용이, 선생, 이 모든 일의 비용이 문제입니다.' 변호사가 순간의 놀라움에서 헤어나자 따져 말했소. '피고가 빈털터리라면, 그 비용은 누가 치릅니까, 선생?'\n\n'액수를 말해 보시오.' 낯선 사내가 말했는데, 그 손이 흥분으로 어찌나 격하게 떨리던지, 말하면서 움켜쥔 펜을 거의 쥐고 있을 수가 없었소—'얼마든, 그것은 당신 것이오. 겁내지 말고 액수를 말하시오, 이 사람아. 내 목표를 이루어 준다면, 나는 그것을 비싸다 여기지 않으리다.'\n\n변호사는 손실의 가능성에 대비해 자신을 지키기 위해 요구할 선금이라며 큰 액수를 되는대로 말했소. 하지만 그것은 그가 그 요구에 응하리라는 생각에서라기보다는, 자신의 의뢰인이 실제로 어디까지 갈 작정인지를 알아보려는 뜻에서였소. 낯선 사내는 그 전액에 대해 제 은행 앞으로 수표를 끊어 주고 떠났소.\n\n그 어음은 어김없이 결제되었고, 변호사는 자신의 기이한 의뢰인이 믿고 의지해도 안전함을 알고는 본격적으로 일에 착수했소. 그 뒤로 이 년 넘게, 헤일링 씨는 며칠씩 내리 사무실에 앉아, 쌓여 가는 서류들을 들여다보고, 또 소송이 줄을 잇고 절차가 절차를 잇따라 시작될 때마다 쏟아져 들어오는 항의의 편지들, 잠시만 미뤄 달라는 간청들, 상대편이 틀림없이 빠져들 파멸에 대한 호소들을, 기쁨에 눈을 번득이며 읽고 또 읽었소. 잠깐의 관용을 구하는 온갖 청원에 돌아오는 대답은 단 하나뿐이었소—돈을 갚아야 한다. 토지, 집, 가구가, 발부된 숱한 강제 집행 영장 가운데 하나하나에 따라 차례차례 압류되었소. 그리고 그 노인 자신도, 관리들의 감시를 피해 달아나지 않았더라면 감옥에 갇혔을 거요.\n\n헤일링의 가차 없는 적의는, 박해의 성공으로 누그러지기는커녕, 그가 끼친 파멸과 더불어 백 배로 커졌소. 노인이 달아났다는 소식을 듣자, 그의 분노는 끝이 없었소. 그는 격분하여 이를 갈고, 제 머리털을 쥐어뜯었으며, 영장을 맡았던 자들에게 끔찍한 저주를 퍼부었소. 도망자를 반드시 찾아내겠다는 거듭된 다짐을 받고서야 그는 겨우 어느 정도 진정되었소. 사방으로 그를 찾는 대리인들이 보내졌고, 그의 은신처를 알아낼 목적으로 궁리해 낼 수 있는 온갖 책략이 동원되었으나, 모두 헛수고였소. 반년이 지나도록 그는 여전히 발견되지 않았소.\n\n마침내 어느 늦은 밤, 그 전 여러 주 동안 도무지 모습을 보이지 않던 헤일링이 변호사의 사저에 나타나, 한 신사가 즉시 만나기를 청한다고 위로 전갈을 올렸소. 위층에서 그 목소리를 알아챈 변호사가 하인에게 그를 들이라 명하기도 전에, 그는 이미 계단을 뛰어 올라와, 창백하고 숨 가쁜 모습으로 응접실에 들어섰소. 엿들리지 않도록 문을 닫고, 그는 의자에 털썩 주저앉아 낮은 목소리로 말했소.\n\n'쉿! 마침내 그자를 찾아냈소.'\n\n'설마!' 변호사가 말했소. '잘하셨소, 선생, 참 잘하셨소.'\n\n'그자는 캠던 타운의 누추한 거처에 숨어 있소.' 헤일링이 말했소. '어쩌면 우리가 그자를 시야에서 놓친 게 차라리 잘된 일인지도 모르오. 그동안 내내 그자는 거기서 더없이 비참하게 홀로 살아왔고, 가난하니까—몹시도 가난하니까.'\n\n'아주 좋군요.' 변호사가 말했소. '물론 내일 체포 영장을 집행하시겠지요?'\n\n'그렇소.' 헤일링이 대답했소. '잠깐! 아니오! 모레로 합시다. 내가 그것을 미루려는 게 당신은 의아하겠지.' 그가 섬뜩한 미소를 지으며 덧붙였소. '하지만 깜빡했군. 모레는 그자의 생애에서 어떤 기념일이오. 그날 하도록 합시다.'\n\n'아주 좋군요.' 변호사가 말했소. '관리에게 줄 지시 사항을 적어 주시겠습니까?'\n\n'아니오. 그자를 여기로, 저녁 여덟 시에 나와 만나게 하시오. 내가 직접 그자와 동행하리다.'\n\n그들은 약속한 밤에 만났고, 삯마차를 빌려, 옛 팽크러스 가의 모퉁이—교구 구빈원이 서 있는 그 자리에 세우라고 마부에게 지시했소. 그들이 거기서 내렸을 무렵엔 이미 사방이 캄캄했소. 그리고 수의(獸醫) 병원 앞의 막다른 담을 따라 나아가, 그들은 리틀 칼리지 가라고 불리는—혹은 그 당시에 그렇게 불리던—작은 골목길로 들어섰는데, 그곳은 지금이야 어떨지 몰라도 그 시절엔 들판과 도랑 말고는 거의 아무것도 없이 둘러싸인, 제법 황량한 곳이었소.\n\n쓰고 있던 여행 모자를 얼굴 절반쯤까지 내려쓰고 망토로 몸을 감싼 채, 헤일링은 그 거리에서 가장 초라해 보이는 집 앞에 멈춰 서서 문을 가만히 두드렸소. 곧 한 여인이 문을 열고 알아본다는 듯 무릎을 굽혀 인사했고, 헤일링은 관리에게 아래에 있으라고 속삭이고는, 조용히 위층으로 살금살금 올라가, 앞방 문을 열고 곧장 들어섰소.\n\n그의 추적과 가차 없는 적의의 대상은, 이제 노쇠한 늙은이가 되어, 보잘것없는 양초 하나가 놓인 휑한 전나무 탁자에 앉아 있었소. 그는 낯선 사내가 들어서자 흠칫하더니, 힘없이 일어섰소.\n\n'무슨 일이오, 무슨 일이오?' 노인이 말했소. '이번엔 또 무슨 새로운 고통이오? 여기서 무엇을 바라는 거요?'\n\n'당신과 할 말이 있소.' 헤일링이 대답했소. 그러고는 탁자 반대편 끝에 앉아, 망토와 모자를 벗어 던져 제 얼굴을 드러냈소.\n\n노인은 순간 말문이 막힌 듯했소. 그는 의자에 뒤로 넘어지며, 두 손을 맞잡고, 혐오와 두려움이 뒤섞인 눈으로 그 유령 같은 자를 응시했소.\n\n'육 년 전 오늘.' 헤일링이 말했소. '나는 내 자식의 목숨 값으로 당신이 내게 진 목숨을 요구했소. 당신 딸의 생기 없는 시신 곁에서, 늙은이여, 나는 복수의 삶을 살겠다고 맹세했소. 나는 단 한순간도 그 목적에서 벗어난 적이 없소. 설령 벗어났던들, 그녀가 시들어 갈 때의 그 불평 없이 고통받던 눈길을, 또 우리 죄 없는 아이의 굶주린 얼굴을 한 번만 떠올렸다면, 그것이 나를 다시 내 과업으로 다잡아 세웠을 거요. 내 첫 번째 보복의 행위를 당신은 잘 기억할 거요. 이것이 내 마지막 행위요.'\n\n노인은 부르르 떨었고, 두 손이 옆구리에 맥없이 떨어졌소.\n\n'나는 내일 잉글랜드를 떠나오.' 헤일링이 잠시 멈췄다가 말했소. '오늘 밤, 나는 당신이 그녀를 몰아넣었던 그 산 채로의 죽음으로—희망 없는 감옥으로 당신을 넘기겠소—'\n\n그는 노인의 얼굴로 눈을 들어 올리다가, 말을 멈췄소. 그는 양초를 그 얼굴로 들어 올렸다가, 가만히 내려놓고는, 그 방을 떠났소.\n\n'저 노인을 좀 살펴보는 게 좋겠소.' 그가 문을 열며 여인에게 말하고는, 관리에게 자기를 따라 거리로 나오라고 손짓했소. '아무래도 아픈 것 같소.' 여인은 문을 닫고 황급히 위층으로 뛰어 올라가, 그가 숨이 끊긴 것을 발견했소.\n\n켄트의 가장 평화롭고 외진 교회 묘지 가운데 한 곳, 들꽃이 풀과 어우러지고, 둘레의 부드러운 풍경이 잉글랜드라는 정원에서 가장 아름다운 자리를 이루는 그곳, 소박한 묘석 아래 그 젊은 어머니와 그 가녀린 아이의 뼈가 누워 있소. 하지만 아버지의 유해는 그들의 것과 섞이지 않았소. 또한 그날 밤 이후로, 변호사는 자신의 기이한 의뢰인의 그 이후 행적에 대해 단 한 가닥의 실마리도 끝내 얻지 못했소.\"\n\n노인은 이야기를 맺고는, 한쪽 구석의 못으로 다가가 거기 걸린 모자와 외투를 내려, 더없이 느긋하게 그것들을 걸쳐 입었소. 그러고는 한마디 말도 없이 천천히 걸어 나갔다. 모자이크 장식 단추를 단 신사는 잠이 들어 버렸고, 일행의 대부분은 그의 브랜디 탄 물에 녹은 양초 기름을 떨어뜨리는 우스꽝스러운 작업에 깊이 빠져 있었으므로, 픽윅 씨는 아무에게도 들키지 않고 자리를 떴다. 그리고 자신의 계산과 웰러 씨의 계산을 치른 뒤, 그 신사와 함께 매그파이 앤드 스텀프의 정문 아래로 걸어 나왔다.\n\n「그게 자네 주인 짐인가, 새미?」 웰러 씨가 화이트채플의 불 여관 마당으로 여행 가방 하나와 작은 트렁크 하나를 들고 들어오는 사랑하는 아들에게 물었다.\n\n「그보다 더 형편없는 짐작도 할 수 있었을 텐데, 영감님.」 아들 웰러 씨가 짐을 마당에 내려놓고 그 위에 털썩 주저앉으며 대답했다. 「주인 양반은 곧 여기로 내려오실 거예요.」\n\n「마차 타고 오시는 모양이지?」 아버지가 물었다.\n\n「예, 8펜스에 2마일짜리 위험을 즐기고 계시죠.」 아들이 대답했다. 「새어머니는 오늘 아침 어떠세요?」\n\n「영 시원찮아, 새미, 시원찮아.」 아버지 웰러 씨가 자못 엄숙한 어조로 대답했다. 「요즘 들어 점점 메소디스트풍으로 돌아서고 있어, 새미. 게다가 어찌나 신앙심이 깊은지 말도 못 한다. 나한테는 과분한 사람이야, 새미. 내가 그 사람을 누릴 자격이 없다는 생각이 들어.」\n\n「아.」 새뮤얼 씨가 말했다. 「그거 참 대단한 극기네요.」\n\n「대단하지.」 그의 아버지가 한숨을 쉬며 대답했다. 「다 자란 어른이 다시 태어난다는 무슨 발명품에 빠졌어, 새미. 그 사람들이 거듭남이라고 부르는 거지, 아마. 그 방식이 실제로 돌아가는 걸 한번 보고 싶구나, 새미. 자네 새어머니가 다시 태어나는 걸 정말 보고 싶어. 그러면 내가 그 사람을 유모한테 갖다 맡길 텐데!」\n\n「엊그제 그 여편네들이 무슨 짓을 했는지 알겠나.」 웰러 씨가 잠깐 말을 멈추고는 집게손가락으로 콧등을 대여섯 번 의미심장하게 두드린 뒤 말을 이었다. 「엊그제 그 여편네들이 무슨 짓을 했는지 알겠나, 새미?」\n\n「몰라요.」 샘이 대답했다. 「뭔데요?」\n\n「자기네가 목자라고 부르는 작자를 위해서 대단한 다과회를 열었지 뭐냐.」 웰러 씨가 말했다. 「내가 우리 동네 그림 가게 앞에 서서 안을 멀거니 들여다보고 있는데, 그것에 관한 작은 광고지가 눈에 띄더라고. ‘입장권 반 크라운. 모든 신청은 위원회로. 간사, 웰러 부인.’ 그러고 집에 가 보니 위원회가 우리 집 뒤채 응접실에 떡하니 앉아 있더라. 여편네가 열넷이야. 자네도 그 소리를 들었어야 하는 건데, 새미. 거기 앉아서 결의안을 통과시키고, 자금을 표결하고, 별의별 놀음을 다 하더라고. 그런데 자네 새어머니는 가자고 나를 들들 볶지, 또 가면 무슨 희한한 구경거리가 생길까 싶어 나도 기대가 되지, 그래서 입장권에 이름을 올렸지. 금요일 저녁 여섯 시에 아주 말쑥하게 차려입고는 마누라랑 같이 나서서, 이층으로 올라가니까 거기 서른 명분 찻잔이 차려져 있고, 여편네들이 떼로 모여서 서로 귓속말을 주고받으면서, 쉰여덟 먹은 좀 뚱뚱한 신사를 난생처음 본다는 듯이 나를 쳐다보더라고. 이윽고 아래층에서 한바탕 소란이 일더니, 빨간 코에 흰 목도리를 두른 비쩍 마른 작자가 후다닥 올라와서 외치는 거야. ‘목자께서 충실한 양 떼를 찾아오십니다.’ 그러고는 검은 옷을 입은 뚱뚱한 작자가, 큼지막한 흰 얼굴로, 태엽 인형처럼 싱글싱글 웃으면서 들어오더라고. 그 꼴이라니, 새미! ‘평화의 입맞춤이오.’ 목자가 말하더니 여편네들한테 한 사람씩 빙 돌아가며 입을 맞추는 거야. 그게 끝나니까 빨간 코 작자가 시작하더라고. 나도 시작하는 게 낫지 않을까 막 생각하던 참이었어—특히 내 옆에 아주 참한 부인이 앉아 있었거든—그런데 그때 차가 들어왔어, 자네 새어머니가 아래층에서 주전자를 끓이고 있었지. 그러더니 다들 죽기 살기로 달려들더라. 차가 우러나는 동안 어찌나 요란하게 찬송가를 부르던지, 새미. 그 감사 기도하며, 그 먹고 마시는 꼴하며! 자네도 그 목자가 햄이랑 머핀을 해치우는 걸 봤어야 하는데. 그렇게 먹고 마시는 작자는 생전 처음 봤다—정말로. 그 빨간 코 작자도 도급으로 먹여 살리고 싶은 부류는 결코 아니었지만, 목자에 비하면 아무것도 아니었어. 그래, 차가 끝나니까 찬송가를 한 곡 더 부르고, 목자가 설교를 시작했지. 그 머핀이 가슴에 얼마나 묵직하게 얹혔을지 생각하면 제법 잘하더라고. 한참 하다가 갑자기 딱 멈추더니 고래고래 소리치는 거야. ‘죄인은 어디 있는가, 비참한 죄인은 어디 있는가?’ 그러자 여편네들이 죄다 나를 쳐다보면서 죽어 가는 사람들처럼 신음을 내기 시작하더라. 좀 묘하다 싶었지만, 어쨌든 나는 아무 말도 안 했어. 그런데 한참 있다가 또 멈추더니 나를 빤히 노려보면서 말하는 거야. ‘죄인은 어디 있는가, 비참한 죄인은 어디 있는가?’ 그러자 여편네들이 아까보다 열 배는 더 크게 또 신음을 내더라. 이쯤 되니 나도 슬슬 사나워져서, 한두 발짝 앞으로 나서서 말했지. ‘여보쇼.’ 내가 말했어. ‘그 말씀, 나한테 한 거요?’ 그런데 점잖은 신사라면 으레 그랬을 것처럼 사과를 하기는커녕, 더 험한 욕을 퍼붓더라고—나더러 그릇이라더라, 새미—진노의 그릇이라며—별의별 욕을 다 하더라고. 그래서 나도 피가 제대로 거꾸로 솟아서, 우선 그 작자한테 두세 대 안기고, 그다음에 빨간 코 작자한테 전하라고 두세 대 더 안기고는 걸어 나왔지. 자네도 그 여편네들이 어떻게 비명을 질렀는지 들었어야 하는데, 새미, 식탁 밑에서 그 목자를 끌어냈을 때 말이야—이런! 저기 주인 양반이 오시는구나, 실물 크기로 말이야.」\n\n웰러 씨가 이렇게 말하는 동안 픽윅 씨가 마차에서 내려 마당으로 들어섰다.\n\n「좋은 아침입니다, 나리.」 아버지 웰러 씨가 말했다.\n\n「참으로 아름다운 아침이군.」 픽윅 씨가 대답했다.\n\n「참으로 아름다운 아침입니다.」 캐묻기 좋아할 것 같은 코에 초록색 안경을 쓴 붉은 머리 사내가 따라 말했는데, 그는 픽윅 씨와 같은 순간에 마차에서 몸을 빼낸 참이었다. 「입스위치로 가십니까, 선생?」\n\n「그렇소.」 픽윅 씨가 대답했다.\n\n「기막힌 우연이로군요. 저도 그렇습니다.」\n\n픽윅 씨는 고개를 숙여 인사했다.\n\n「바깥자리에 타십니까?」 붉은 머리 사내가 물었다.\n\n픽윅 씨는 다시 고개를 숙였다.\n\n「이런 세상에, 참으로 놀랍군요—저도 바깥자리에 탑니다.」 붉은 머리 사내가 말했다. 「우리는 영락없이 동행하게 되는군요.」 그 붉은 머리 사내는 거드름을 피우는 듯한 인상에 코가 뾰족하고 말투가 알쏭달쏭한 인물이었는데, 무슨 말을 할 때마다 새처럼 고개를 홱홱 젖히는 버릇이 있었고, 인간의 지혜에 일찍이 떨어진 적 없는 가장 기이한 발견이라도 해낸 듯이 빙긋 웃었다.\n\n「선생과 동행하게 되어 기쁩니다.」 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n「아.」 새로 온 사내가 말했다. 「우리 둘 다한테 좋은 일이지요, 안 그렇습니까? 동행이라는 건, 보십시오—동행이라는 건—혼자인 것과는—참 다른 거지요, 안 그래요?」\n\n「그건 부인할 수 없는 노릇이죠.」 웰러 씨가 싹싹한 미소를 지으며 대화에 끼어들었다. 「그게 바로 제가 말하는 자명한 명제라는 겁니다요, 개고기 장수가 하녀더러 자기는 신사가 아니라는 말을 들었을 때 한 말처럼요.」\n\n「아.」 붉은 머리 사내가 멸시하는 눈초리로 웰러 씨를 머리끝부터 발끝까지 훑어보며 말했다. 「선생의 친구분이오?」\n\n「친구라고 하기는 좀 그렇소.」 픽윅 씨가 낮은 목소리로 대답했다. 「사실은 내 하인인데, 내가 꽤 많은 무례를 허용해 주고 있지요. 우리끼리 얘기지만, 이 친구가 보기 드문 인물이라고 자부하고 있고, 또 이 친구가 제법 자랑스럽기도 하거든요.」\n\n「아.」 붉은 머리 사내가 말했다. 「그건, 보십시오, 취향 문제지요. 나는 보기 드문 것이라면 뭐든 좋아하지 않습니다. 마음에 안 들어요. 그게 왜 필요한지 모르겠소. 성함이 어떻게 되십니까, 선생?」\n\n「여기 제 명함이 있습니다.」 픽윅 씨가 질문의 느닷없음과 낯선 이의 별난 태도에 적잖이 흥겨워하며 대답했다.\n\n「아.」 붉은 머리 사내가 명함을 수첩에 끼워 넣으며 말했다. 「픽윅이라. 아주 좋군요. 나는 사람 이름을 알아 두는 걸 좋아합니다, 수고를 많이 덜어 주거든요. 그게 제 명함입니다, 선생. 매그너스, 보시다시피요—매그너스가 제 이름입니다. 제법 좋은 이름이라고 생각해요, 선생.」\n\n「참으로 좋은 이름이군요.」 픽윅 씨가 도저히 미소를 억누르지 못한 채 말했다.\n\n「예, 저도 그렇게 생각합니다.」 매그너스 씨가 말을 이었다. 「그 앞에 붙은 이름도 좋지요, 보십시오. 잠깐만요, 선생—명함을 이렇게 약간 비스듬히 들면, 올려 쓴 획에 빛이 걸립니다. 자—피터 매그너스—듣기 좋지요, 그렇지 않습니까, 선생?」\n\n「아주 그렇소.」 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n「그 머리글자에 묘한 점이 있습니다, 선생.」 매그너스 씨가 말했다. 「보십시오—P.M.—오후라는 뜻이지요. 친한 사이에 급하게 쪽지를 쓸 때면 가끔 ‘오후’라고 서명하곤 합니다. 친구들이 아주 재미있어해요, 픽윅 씨.」\n\n「그들에게 더없는 흡족함을 안겨 주게끔 짜인 것이겠지요.」 픽윅 씨가, 매그너스 씨의 친구들이 그토록 손쉽게 즐거워한다는 것에 다소 부러움을 느끼며 말했다.\n\n「자, 신사분들.」 마부가 말했다. 「준비되시면, 마차가 떠납니다.」\n\n「제 짐은 다 실렸습니까?」 매그너스 씨가 물었다.\n\n「다 됐습니다, 선생.」\n\n「빨간 가방은 실렸나요?」\n\n「됐습니다, 선생.」\n\n「줄무늬 가방은요?」\n\n「앞쪽 짐칸에요, 선생.」\n\n「갈색 종이 꾸러미는요?」\n\n「좌석 밑에요, 선생.」\n\n「가죽 모자 상자는요?」\n\n「전부 다 실렸습니다, 선생.」\n\n「자, 이제 올라타시겠습니까?」 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n「실례합니다.」 매그너스가 바퀴 위에 올라선 채 대답했다. 「실례합니다, 픽윅 씨. 이런 불확실한 상태로는 올라탈 수가 없군요. 저 사람 태도로 보아 가죽 모자 상자가 실리지 않았다고 확신이 듭니다.」\n\n마부의 엄숙한 항변도 아무 소용이 없어, 결국 가죽 모자 상자를 짐칸 맨 밑바닥에서 끄집어내어 그것이 무사히 실렸음을 그에게 납득시켜야 했다. 그 점에 대해 확신을 얻고 나자, 그는 이번엔 엄숙한 예감이 들어, 처음엔 빨간 가방이 잘못 놓였고, 다음엔 줄무늬 가방을 도둑맞았으며, 그다음엔 갈색 종이 꾸러미가 ‘풀려 버렸다’고 했다. 마침내 이 의심들 하나하나가 모두 근거 없는 것임을 눈으로 직접 확인하고 나서야, 그는 마차 지붕에 올라가기로 했고, 이제 마음의 짐을 다 덜었으니 아주 편안하고 행복하다고 말했다.\n\n「선생은 신경이 예민한 편이시지요, 안 그렇습니까, 선생?」 아버지 웰러 씨가, 낯선 이가 제자리에 오르는 동안 곁눈질로 그를 보며 물었다.\n\n「예, 이런 자잘한 일에는 늘 좀 그렇습니다.」 낯선 이가 말했다. 「하지만 이제 괜찮아요—아주 괜찮습니다.」\n\n「그거 참 다행이군요.」 웰러 씨가 말했다. 「새미, 주인 어른께서 마부석에 오르시는 걸 도와드려라. 그쪽 다리요, 선생, 그렇지. 손을 주십시오, 선생. 자, 올라가시죠. 어릴 적엔 훨씬 가벼우셨을 텐데요, 선생.」\n\n「딱 맞는 말이군, 웰러.」 숨을 헐떡이는 픽윅 씨가 그의 옆 마부석에 앉으며 흔쾌히 말했다.\n\n「앞쪽에 올라타라, 새미.」 웰러 씨가 말했다. 「자, 빌럼, 말들을 몰아라. 아치 길 조심하시고요, 신사분들. ‘머리 조심’, 파이 장수 말마따나요. 됐다, 빌럼. 내버려 두어라.」 그러자 마차는 화이트채플 거리를 따라 달려 나갔고, 꽤나 인구가 빽빽한 그 동네 주민 전체의 감탄을 자아냈다.\n\n「그리 좋은 동네는 아니군요, 선생.」 샘이 주인과 대화를 시작하기에 앞서 늘 그러듯 모자에 손을 살짝 대며 말했다.\n\n「정말 그렇구나, 샘.」 픽윅 씨가, 자기들이 지나고 있는 붐비고 더러운 거리를 둘러보며 대답했다.\n\n「참 묘한 일입니다요, 선생.」 샘이 말했다. 「가난과 굴은 늘 짝을 지어 다니는 것 같단 말이죠.」\n\n「무슨 소린지 모르겠구나, 샘.」 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n「제 말은요, 선생.」 샘이 말했다. 「동네가 가난하면 할수록 굴을 찾는 소리가 더 커지는 것 같다는 겁니다요. 보십시오, 선생. 집 여섯 채마다 굴 좌판이 하나씩 있어요. 거리에 쭉 늘어서 있다고요. 사람이 아주 가난해지면 하숙방에서 뛰쳐나와 영락없이 자포자기 심정으로 굴을 먹어 치우는 거라고, 저는 그렇게 생각합니다요.」\n\n「당연히 그렇지.」 아버지 웰러 씨가 말했다. 「절인 연어도 꼭 마찬가지란다!」\n\n「그건 두 가지 다 참으로 주목할 만한 사실이군, 전에는 미처 생각해 본 적이 없었어.」 픽윅 씨가 말했다. 「우리가 처음 멈추는 곳에서 그것들을 적어 두어야겠다.」\n\n이즈음 그들은 마일 엔드의 통행세 징수소에 다다랐다. 그 뒤로 두세 마일을 더 갈 때까지 깊은 침묵이 감돌았는데, 그때 아버지 웰러 씨가 갑자기 픽윅 씨를 돌아보며 말했다.\n\n「참 묘한 인생이지요, 통행세지기란 게 말입니다, 선생.」\n\n「뭐라고?」 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n「통행세지기 말입니다.」\n\n「통행세지기라는 게 무슨 뜻이오?」 피터 매그너스 씨가 물었다.\n\n「이 영감님은 통행세 징수소를 지키는 사람을 말하는 겁니다, 신사분들.」 새뮤얼 웰러 씨가 설명조로 거들었다.\n\n「아.」 픽윅 씨가 말했다. 「알겠네. 그래, 참 묘한 인생이지. 무척 불편하기도 하고.」\n\n「그자들은 죄다 인생에서 무슨 좌절을 겪은 사람들이지요.」 아버지 웰러 씨가 말했다.\n\n「그렇지, 그렇지.」 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n「예. 그래서 세상을 등지고 징수소에 틀어박히는 거지요. 한편으로는 혼자 있고 싶어서고, 또 한편으로는 통행세를 거두어서 인류에게 복수하려는 거지요.」\n\n「저런.」 픽윅 씨가 말했다. 「그건 전에 미처 몰랐군.」\n\n「사실입니다, 선생.」 웰러 씨가 말했다. 「그자들이 신사였다면 인간 혐오자라고 불렀겠지만, 사정이 사정인지라 그저 통행세지기 노릇을 하는 거지요.」\n\n이렇듯 재미와 가르침을 함께 버무리는 더없는 매력을 지닌 대화로, 웰러 씨는 하루의 대부분에 걸쳐 여행의 지루함을 달래 주었다. 화제는 결코 동나는 법이 없었으니, 웰러 씨의 수다가 잠시 멎을 때면, 동행들의 신상 내력을 죄다 알고 싶어 하는 매그너스 씨의 욕구가, 또 정거장마다 두 가방과 가죽 모자 상자와 갈색 종이 꾸러미의 안전과 무사함에 대해 큰 소리로 표하는 그의 근심이 그 빈자리를 넉넉히 채워 주었기 때문이다.\n\n입스위치의 큰길에서, 길의 왼편으로, 시청 앞 광장을 지나고 나서 조금 더 가면, 멀리까지 그레이트 화이트 호스라는 이름으로 알려진 여관이 서 있다. 그 여관은 큰 문 위에 높이 솟은, 갈기와 꼬리를 휘날리는 어떤 사납기 그지없는 짐승의 돌 조각상 덕분에 한층 눈에 띄는데, 그 조각상은 멀리서 보면 미쳐 버린 짐마차 말을 어렴풋이 닮았다. 그레이트 화이트 호스는 그 일대에서 유명한데, 상을 탄 황소나 지방 신문에 실린 무 또는 거대한 돼지가 유명한 것과 똑같은 까닭—곧 어마어마한 크기 때문이다. 카펫도 깔리지 않은 복도의 미로며, 곰팡이 슬고 어두컴컴한 방들의 무리며, 먹거나 잠자기 위한 작은 굴 같은 방들의 엄청난 수가, 입스위치의 그레이트 화이트 호스의 네 벽 안에 한데 모여 있는 것만큼 하나의 지붕 아래 모여 있는 곳은 일찍이 없었다.\n\n런던발 마차가 저녁마다 같은 시각에 멈추어 서는 곳이 바로 이 터무니없이 커진 여관의 문 앞이었다. 그리고 우리 이야기의 이 장이 가리키는 바로 그 저녁에, 픽윅 씨와 샘 웰러와 피터 매그너스 씨가 내린 곳도 바로 이 런던발 마차였다.\n\n「여기 묵으십니까, 선생?」 피터 매그너스 씨가, 줄무늬 가방과 빨간 가방과 갈색 종이 꾸러미와 가죽 모자 상자가 모두 복도에 내려놓아지자 물었다. 「여기 묵으십니까, 선생?」\n\n「그렇소.」 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n「이런 세상에.」 매그너스 씨가 말했다. 「이렇게 기막힌 우연은 처음 봅니다. 아니, 저도 여기 묵거든요. 함께 저녁을 드실 수 있겠지요?」\n\n「기꺼이 그러지요.」 픽윅 씨가 대답했다. 「다만 여기 제 친구들이 와 있는지 어떤지 확실치가 않군요. 여기 텁먼이라는 이름의 신사가 와 있소, 종업원?」\n\n뚱뚱한 사내 하나가, 보름은 묵었을 행주를 겨드랑이에 끼고, 그와 동년배쯤 되어 보이는 양말을 신은 채, 픽윅 씨가 이 질문을 던지자 거리를 멀거니 내려다보던 일을 천천히 멈추었다. 그러고는 모자 꼭대기부터 각반의 맨 아래 단추까지 그 신사의 행색을 세세히 뜯어본 뒤, 단호하게 대답했다.\n「없습니다!」\n\n「스노드그래스라는 이름의 신사도 없소?」 픽윅 씨가 물었다.\n「없습니다!」\n\n「윈클도?」\n\n「없습니다!」\n\n「내 친구들이 오늘 도착하지 않았군요, 선생.」 픽윅 씨가 말했다. 「그러면 우리끼리 저녁을 듭시다. 별실로 안내해 주게, 종업원.」\n\n이 요청이 들어가자, 뚱뚱한 사내는 황송하게도 구두닦이에게 신사분들의 짐을 들여오라고 명한 뒤, 그들을 앞장세워 길고 어두운 복도를 따라 내려가, 더러운 화로가 놓인 크고 가구가 변변찮은 방으로 안내했다. 그 화로에서는 작은 불이 즐거워 보이려고 가련하게 애쓰고 있었으나, 그 장소의 의기소침한 기운에 짓눌려 빠르게 사그라지고 있었다. 한 시간이 지난 뒤 여행객들에게 생선 한 토막과 스테이크가 차려졌고, 저녁상이 치워지자 픽윅 씨와 피터 매그너스 씨는 의자를 불가로 끌어당겼다. 그러고는 여관을 위하는 셈치고 가능한 한 형편없는 포트와인 한 병을 가능한 한 비싼 값에 주문한 뒤, 자기들이 마실 것으로는 브랜디에 물을 탄 것을 마셨다.\n\n피터 매그너스 씨는 천성이 매우 말이 많은 사람이었는데, 브랜디에 물을 탄 것이 놀라운 효력을 발휘하여 그의 가슴 깊이 숨겨 둔 비밀들에까지 생기를 불어넣어 데워 놓았다. 자기 자신과 가족과 인척과 친구들과 농담과 사업과 형제들(말이 많은 사람들은 대개 자기 형제들에 관해 할 말이 아주 많다)에 관해 이런저런 이야기를 늘어놓은 뒤, 피터 매그너스 씨는 색안경 너머로 몇 분 동안 픽윅 씨를 바라보더니, 겸손한 기색을 띠고 말했다.\n\n「그런데 어떻게 생각하십니까—대체 어떻게 생각하시느냐고요, 픽윅 씨—제가 여기에 무엇 때문에 내려왔다고요?」\n\n「솔직히 말해서.」 픽윅 씨가 말했다. 「도무지 짐작할 수가 없군요. 사업 때문이려나요.」\n\n「반은 맞고요, 선생.」 피터 매그너스 씨가 대답했다. 「하지만 동시에 반은 틀렸습니다. 다시 한번 맞혀 보시지요, 픽윅 씨.」\n\n「정말이지.」 픽윅 씨가 말했다. 「말씀하시든 말든 좋으실 대로 하시도록 선생의 자비에 맡겨야겠군요. 밤새 맞혀 본다 한들 도저히 알아낼 수가 없겠으니 말입니다.」\n\n「그럼, 그러면, 헤헤헤!」 피터 매그너스 씨가 수줍게 킥킥거리며 말했다. 「픽윅 씨, 제가 여기에 청혼을 하러 내려왔다면 어떻게 생각하시겠습니까, 선생, 예? 헤, 헤, 헤!」\n\n「생각이라! 성공하실 가능성이 매우 높다고 보지요.」 픽윅 씨가 그 특유의 환한 미소를 지으며 대답했다.\n\n「아!」 매그너스 씨가 말했다. 「하지만 정말 그렇게 생각하십니까, 픽윅 씨? 정말요?」\n\n「물론이지요.」 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n「아니, 농담하시는 거죠.」\n\n「정말이지 농담이 아닙니다.」\n\n「그럼, 그렇다면.」 매그너스 씨가 말했다. 「작은 비밀을 하나 털어놓자면, 저도 그렇게 생각합니다. 픽윅 씨, 제가 천성이 끔찍하게 질투가 많은 사람이긴 합니다만—지독하지요—말씀드려도 상관없겠지요, 그 부인이 이 여관에 있습니다.」 여기서 매그너스 씨는 윙크를 하려고 일부러 안경을 벗었다가 다시 썼다.\n\n「그래서 저녁 식사 전에 그렇게 자주 방을 들락날락하셨던 거로군요.」 픽윅 씨가 짓궂게 말했다.\n\n「쉿! 예, 맞습니다, 바로 그거였어요. 그래도 그 부인을 만날 만큼 어리석지는 않지요.」\n\n「안 만나신다고요!」\n「예. 그래서야 안 되지요, 아시다시피, 막 여행에서 내린 참인데요. 내일까지 기다려야지요, 선생. 그러면 가능성이 두 배는 되니까요. 픽윅 씨, 선생, 저 가방 안에 옷 한 벌이 있고 저 상자 안에 모자가 하나 있는데, 그것들이 자아낼 효과로 따지면 제게 더없이 귀중한 것이 되리라 기대합니다, 선생.」\n\n「그렇습니까!」 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n「예. 오늘 그것들에 대한 제 염려를 분명 보셨겠지요. 그만한 옷 한 벌과 그만한 모자는 돈을 주고도 살 수 없으리라고 봅니다, 픽윅 씨.」\n\n픽윅 씨는 그 거부할 수 없는 의복을 손에 넣은 행운의 주인에게 축하를 건넸고, 피터 매그너스 씨는 잠시 무언가에 골똘히 잠겨 있는 듯했다.\n\n「그분은 참한 사람입니다.」 매그너스 씨가 말했다.\n\n「그렇습니까?」 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n「아주요.」 매그너스 씨가 말했다. 「아주 그렇지요. 여기서 한 이십 마일 떨어진 곳에 삽니다, 픽윅 씨. 오늘 밤과 내일 오전 내내 여기 와 있을 거라는 소식을 듣고, 그 기회를 잡으려고 내려온 거지요. 미혼 여성에게 청혼하기에는 여관이 좋은 곳이라고 봅니다, 픽윅 씨. 집에 있을 때보다 여행 중일 때 자기 처지의 외로움을 더 느낄 법하잖습니까. 어떻게 생각하십니까, 픽윅 씨?」\n\n「충분히 그럴 법하다고 봅니다.」 그 신사가 대답했다.\n\n「실례합니다만, 픽윅 씨.」 피터 매그너스 씨가 말했다. 「제가 천성이 좀 호기심이 많아서요. 선생께서는 무엇 때문에 여기 내려오셨는지요?」\n\n「훨씬 덜 유쾌한 용무 때문이지요, 선생.」 픽윅 씨가, 그 기억에 얼굴이 붉어지며 대답했다. 「저는 여기에, 선생, 제가 그 진실함과 명예를 전적으로 믿었던 한 인간의 배신과 거짓을 폭로하러 내려온 것입니다.」\n\n「이런.」 피터 매그너스 씨가 말했다. 「그것 참 불쾌한 일이군요. 여자분이겠지요, 아마? 예? 아하! 능청스러우시군요, 픽윅 씨, 능청맞으세요. 자, 픽윅 씨, 선생, 무슨 일이 있어도 선생의 심정을 캐묻지는 않겠습니다. 고통스러운 화제지요, 선생, 무척 고통스러운. 저는 신경 쓰지 마시고, 픽윅 씨, 심정을 토로하고 싶으시면 그렇게 하십시오. 저도 차인다는 게 어떤 건지 압니다, 선생. 그런 일을 서너 번은 견뎌 봤거든요.」\n\n「제 우울한 처지라고 짐작하시는 일에 대한 위로의 말씀, 대단히 고맙습니다.」 픽윅 씨가 회중시계의 태엽을 감아 탁자 위에 내려놓으며 말했다. 「하지만—」\n\n「아니, 아닙니다.」 피터 매그너스 씨가 말했다. 「더는 한마디도 마세요. 고통스러운 화제니까요. 압니다, 알아요. 지금 몇 시입니까, 픽윅 씨?」\n\n「열두 시가 넘었군요.」\n\n「이런, 잠자리에 들 시간이군요. 이렇게 앉아 있어서는 안 되겠어요. 내일 얼굴이 창백해질 겁니다, 픽윅 씨.」\n\n그런 참사를 떠올리기만 해도 견딜 수 없었던지, 피터 매그너스 씨는 객실 담당 하녀를 부르려고 종을 울렸다. 줄무늬 가방과 빨간 가방과 가죽 모자 상자와 갈색 종이 꾸러미가 그의 침실로 옮겨지고 나자, 그는 옻칠한 촛대 하나를 벗 삼아 여관의 한쪽으로 물러갔고, 한편 픽윅 씨는 또 다른 옻칠한 촛대 하나와 함께, 구불구불 꼬인 수많은 길을 거쳐 또 다른 쪽으로 안내되었다.\n\n「여기가 손님 방입니다, 선생.」 객실 담당 하녀가 말했다.\n\n「좋군.」 픽윅 씨가 주위를 둘러보며 대답했다. 그 방은 침대가 둘 놓인 제법 큰 방으로 불이 피워져 있었으니, 전체적으로 보아 픽윅 씨가 그레이트 화이트 호스의 숙박 설비를 짧게나마 겪어 본 끝에 기대했던 것보다 한결 안락해 보이는 방이었다.\n\n「물론 다른 침대에는 아무도 자지 않겠지.」 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n「예, 아무도 없습니다, 선생.」\n「아주 좋아. 내 하인에게 아침 여덟 시 반에 더운물을 좀 가져오라고, 그리고 오늘 밤에는 더 부를 일이 없다고 전해 주게.」\n\n「예, 선생.」 그러고는 객실 담당 하녀가 픽윅 씨에게 안녕히 주무시라고 인사한 뒤 물러가, 그를 혼자 남겨 두었다.\n\n픽윅 씨는 불 앞 의자에 앉아 두서없는 명상의 흐름에 빠져들었다. 처음에는 친구들 생각을 하며 그들이 언제 합류할지 궁금해했다. 그다음 그의 마음은 마사 바델 부인에게로 돌아갔고, 그 부인에게서 자연스러운 흐름을 따라 도드슨 앤드 포그의 칙칙한 사무실로 흘러갔다. 도드슨 앤드 포그에서 그것은 옆으로 휙 튀어 그 괴상한 의뢰인 이야기의 한복판으로 갔다가, 이윽고 다시 입스위치의 그레이트 화이트 호스로 돌아왔는데, 그 또렷함이 충분하여 픽윅 씨는 자기가 잠들고 있음을 깨달을 수 있었다. 그래서 그는 정신을 차리고 옷을 벗기 시작하다가, 회중시계를 아래층 탁자에 두고 왔다는 사실이 떠올랐다.\n\n그런데 이 회중시계는 픽윅 씨가 각별히 아끼는 물건이었으니, 지금 이 자리에서 굳이 밝힐 필요가 있다고 여겨지지 않을 만큼 오랜 세월 동안 그의 조끼 그늘 아래 지니고 다닌 것이었다. 그것이 베개 밑에서나 머리맡 시계 주머니에서 조용히 째깍거리지 않는다면 잠들 수 있으리라는 생각은 픽윅 씨의 머릿속에 한 번도 떠오른 적이 없었다. 그래서 지금은 꽤 늦은 시각인 데다 이런 야밤에 종을 울려 사람을 부르고 싶지도 않았으므로, 그는 방금 벗어 둔 외투를 다시 걸치고, 옻칠한 촛대를 손에 든 채 조용히 아래층으로 내려갔다.\n\n픽윅 씨가 계단을 내려가면 내려갈수록 내려가야 할 계단이 더 많아지는 것만 같았고, 픽윅 씨가 어느 좁은 복도에 들어서서 일층에 다다랐다고 스스로를 축하하려 할 때마다, 또 한 줄기 계단이 그의 놀란 눈앞에 거듭거듭 나타나곤 했다. 마침내 그는 이 여관에 들어설 때 본 기억이 있는 돌 깔린 현관에 이르렀다. 복도란 복도는 죄다 뒤졌고, 방이란 방은 죄다 들여다보았다. 마침내 절망에 빠져 수색을 포기하려던 바로 그 순간, 그는 자기가 저녁을 보냈던 바로 그 방의 문을 열었고, 탁자 위에서 잃어버린 자기 물건을 발견했다.\n\n픽윅 씨는 의기양양하게 시계를 움켜쥐고는 침실로 발걸음을 되짚어 가기 시작했다. 내려올 때의 여정이 어려움과 불확실함을 동반한 것이었다면, 돌아가는 길은 그보다 한없이 더 헷갈렸다. 온갖 모양과 제작 방식과 크기의 구두들로 장식된 문들이 줄지어, 생각할 수 있는 온갖 방향으로 갈라져 나갔다. 그는 열두 번이나 자기 방과 비슷한 어느 침실 문의 손잡이를 살며시 돌렸는데, 그때마다 안에서 「대체 누구야?」라거나 「여기서 뭘 하는 거야?」라는 퉁명스러운 외침이 들려와, 그는 참으로 경이로울 만큼 날쌔게 발끝으로 살금살금 달아나곤 했다. 그가 절망의 벼랑 끝까지 내몰렸을 때, 열린 문 하나가 그의 눈길을 끌었다. 그는 안을 들여다보았다. 드디어 맞았다! 그 위치를 똑똑히 기억하는 두 침대가 거기 있었고, 불도 여전히 타고 있었다. 처음 받았을 때도 그리 길지 않았던 그의 촛불은, 그가 지나온 외풍 속에서 깜박이며 사그라들다가, 그가 등 뒤로 문을 닫는 순간 촛대 구멍 속으로 폭 꺼지고 말았다. 「상관없어.」 픽윅 씨가 말했다. 「불빛만으로도 옷 정도는 거뜬히 벗을 수 있지.」\n\n침대들은 문 양쪽에 하나씩 놓여 있었고, 각 침대의 안쪽으로는 골풀 방석을 깐 의자에서 끝나는 작은 통로가 나 있었는데, 그 통로는 본인이 원한다면 그쪽으로 침대에 들거나 나올 수 있을 만큼만 폭이 넓었다. 픽윅 씨는 침대의 바깥쪽 휘장을 조심스럽게 친 뒤, 골풀 방석 의자에 앉아 느긋하게 구두와 각반을 벗었다. 그러고는 외투와 조끼와 목도리를 벗어 개켜 놓고, 술 달린 잠잘 때 쓰는 모자를 천천히 쓴 뒤, 그 의복에 늘 달아 두던 끈을 턱 아래로 매어 머리에 단단히 고정시켰다. 바로 이 순간, 조금 전 자기가 겪은 갈팡질팡함이 얼마나 우스꽝스러운 것이었는지가 그의 마음에 와닿았다. 픽윅 씨는 골풀 방석 의자에 몸을 뒤로 젖히고는 어찌나 진심으로 혼자 웃었던지, 잠잘 때 쓰는 모자 아래로 환히 빛나며 그 선량한 얼굴을 활짝 펴는 미소를 지켜본다면, 제대로 된 마음을 지닌 사람이라면 누구라도 무척 흐뭇했을 것이다.\n\n「이건 정말 기막힌 일이로군.」 픽윅 씨가 잠잘 때 쓰는 모자 끈이 끊어질 만큼 미소를 지으며 혼잣말을 했다. 「이런 데서 길을 잃고 이 계단들을 헤매고 다니다니, 이건 내가 들어 본 것 중에서도 가장 기막힌 일이야. 우습군, 우스워, 정말 우스워.」 여기서 픽윅 씨는 아까보다 더 활짝 다시 미소를 짓고는, 더없이 유쾌한 기분으로 옷 벗는 일을 마저 계속하려던 참이었는데, 더없이 뜻밖의 방해—곧 어떤 사람이 촛불을 들고 방으로 들어와, 문을 잠근 뒤 화장대로 다가가 그 위에 불빛을 내려놓는 일—로 인해 갑작스레 멈추고 말았다.\n\n픽윅 씨의 얼굴에 어리던 미소는 끝없이 놀라고 경악한 표정 속으로 순식간에 사라져 버렸다. 그 사람은, 누구인지는 몰라도, 어찌나 갑작스럽게, 어찌나 소리 없이 들어왔던지, 픽윅 씨는 소리를 지를 새도, 그 입장을 막을 새도 없었다. 누구일까? 강도일까? 어쩌면 자기가 멋진 회중시계를 손에 들고 위층으로 올라오는 것을 본 어떤 악한일지도 몰랐다. 어떻게 해야 한단 말인가?\n\n픽윅 씨가 자신이 들킬 위험을 가장 적게 무릅쓰면서 그 수수께끼 같은 방문객을 슬쩍 엿볼 수 있는 유일한 방법은, 침대 위로 기어 올라가 반대쪽 휘장 사이로 내다보는 것이었다. 그리하여 그는 이 책략에 의지했다. 얼굴과 잠잘 때 쓰는 모자 말고는 자기 모습이 더 보이지 않도록 휘장을 손으로 조심스럽게 닫아 쥔 채, 안경을 끼고는 용기를 그러모아 밖을 내다보았다.\n\n픽윅 씨는 공포와 경악으로 거의 까무러칠 뻔했다. 화장대 거울 앞에 노란 컬페이퍼를 만 중년 부인 하나가 서서, 부인들이 ‘뒷머리’라고 부르는 것을 부지런히 빗질하고 있었다. 그 의식하지 못하는 중년 부인이 어떻게 이 방에 들어왔든 간에, 그녀가 이곳에서 하룻밤을 보낼 작정이라는 것만은 분명했으니, 골풀 양초와 갓을 가지고 들어와서는, 화재에 대한 칭찬할 만한 조심성을 발휘하여 그것을 바닥의 대야 안에 세워 두었기 때문이다. 거기서 그것은 유난히 작은 물웅덩이 속에 선 거대한 등대처럼 가물거리고 있었다.\n\n「맙소사!」 픽윅 씨가 생각했다. 「이거 큰일이로군!」\n\n「에헴!」 부인이 말했다. 그러자 픽윅 씨의 머리가 자동인형 같은 속도로 쑥 들어갔다.\n\n「이렇게 끔찍한 일은 난생처음 겪는군.」 가엾은 픽윅 씨는 잠잘 때 쓰는 모자 위로 식은땀이 방울방울 솟는 것을 느끼며 생각했다. 「난생처음이야. 이거 무서운 일이로군.」\n\n지금 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 보고 싶은 절박한 욕구를 도저히 억누를 수 없었다. 그래서 픽윅 씨의 머리가 다시 쑥 나왔다. 광경은 아까보다 더 나빴다. 중년 부인은 머리 손질을 마치고, 작은 주름 가두리가 달린 모슬린 잠잘 때 쓰는 모자로 그것을 조심스럽게 감싼 뒤, 시름에 잠긴 듯 불을 바라보고 있었다.\n\n「이 일이 점점 심상찮게 되어 가는군.」 픽윅 씨가 혼자 따져 보았다. 「일이 이런 식으로 흘러가게 둘 수는 없어. 저 부인이 저토록 침착한 걸 보니, 내가 방을 잘못 들어온 것이 분명해. 내가 소리를 지르면 부인이 온 집 안을 놀라게 할 테고, 그렇다고 여기 그대로 있으면 그 결과는 더더욱 끔찍할 거야.」\n\n굳이 말할 것도 없이, 픽윅 씨는 인간들 가운데 가장 정숙하고 섬세한 마음을 지닌 사람 중 하나였다. 한 부인에게 자기 잠잘 때 쓰는 모자를 내보인다는 생각만으로도 그는 견딜 수가 없었으나, 그 망할 끈을 매듭으로 묶어 둔 탓에, 아무리 애를 써도 벗을 수가 없었다. 정체를 밝혀야만 했다. 그렇게 하는 다른 방법은 단 하나뿐이었다. 그는 휘장 뒤로 몸을 움츠리고는 아주 큰 소리로 외쳤다.\n\n「하-험!」\n\n이 뜻밖의 소리에 부인이 흠칫 놀란 것은 그녀가 골풀 양초 갓에 부딪혀 비틀거린 것으로 보아 분명했다. 또 그것이 상상의 장난임에 틀림없다고 그녀가 스스로를 납득시켰다는 것도 똑같이 분명했으니, 그녀가 공포로 새파랗게 질려 까무러쳤으리라 생각한 픽윅 씨가 다시 한번 밖을 내다보았을 때, 그녀는 아까처럼 시름에 잠긴 듯 불을 바라보고 있었기 때문이다.\n\n「참으로 별난 여자로군.」 픽윅 씨가 다시 머리를 쑥 집어넣으며 생각했다. 「하-험!」\n\n이 마지막 소리는, 전설이 우리에게 일러 주는 바, 사나운 거인 블런더보어가 이제 식탁보를 깔 때가 되었다는 자기 견해를 표할 때 즐겨 내던 소리와 너무도 비슷한 것이어서, 또렷이 들렸으므로 다시 공상의 장난으로 오해받기에는 너무도 분명했다.\n\n「세상에!」 중년 부인이 말했다. 「저게 뭐지?」\n\n「그건—그건—그저 신사일 뿐입니다, 부인.」 픽윅 씨가 휘장 뒤에서 말했다.\n\n「신사라니!」 부인이 무시무시한 비명을 지르며 말했다.\n\n「이제 다 끝났구나!」 픽윅 씨가 생각했다.\n\n「낯선 남자야!」 부인이 새된 소리로 외쳤다. 한순간만 더 지나면 온 집 안이 발칵 뒤집힐 판이었다. 그녀가 문 쪽으로 달려가자 그녀의 옷자락이 바스락거렸다.\n\n「부인.」 픽윅 씨가 절박함의 극에 달하여 머리를 쑥 내밀며 말했다. 「부인!」\n\n그런데 픽윅 씨가 머리를 내민 데에 어떤 분명한 목적이 있었던 것은 아니지만, 그것은 즉각 좋은 효과를 낳았다. 앞서 말했듯이 부인은 문 가까이에 있었다. 계단으로 가려면 그 문을 지나야 했는데, 픽윅 씨의 잠잘 때 쓰는 모자가 별안간 나타난 통에 그녀가 방의 가장 먼 구석으로 떠밀려 가지만 않았더라면, 그녀는 분명 이때쯤 이미 그렇게 했을 것이다. 그곳에서 그녀는 픽윅 씨를 사납게 노려보며 서 있었고, 픽윅 씨 또한 그녀를 사납게 노려보았다.\n\n「파렴치한.」 부인이 두 손으로 눈을 가리며 말했다. 「여기서 뭘 하려는 거예요?」\n\n「아무것도, 부인. 아무것도 하려는 게 아닙니다, 부인.」 픽윅 씨가 간절하게 말했다.\n\n「아무것도라니!」 부인이 고개를 들며 말했다.\n\n「아무것도 없습니다, 부인, 제 명예를 걸고서요.」 픽윅 씨가 어찌나 힘차게 고개를 끄덕였던지 잠잘 때 쓰는 모자의 술이 다시 춤을 추었다. 「부인, 저는 잠잘 때 쓰는 모자를 쓴 채 부인께 말을 건네는 이 당혹스러움에 거의 쓰러질 지경입니다(여기서 부인이 황급히 자기 모자를 벗어 던졌다). 하지만 이걸 벗을 수가 없군요, 부인(여기서 픽윅 씨는 그 말이 사실임을 입증하려는 듯 모자를 어마어마하게 잡아당겼다). 부인, 이제 제게 분명해진 것은, 제가 이 침실을 제 방으로 착각했다는 겁니다. 부인, 여기 들어온 지 오 분도 채 안 되었는데, 부인께서 별안간 들어오셨던 거지요.」\n\n「이 있을 법하지 않은 이야기가 정말 사실이라면, 선생.」 부인이 격렬하게 흐느끼며 말했다. 「당장 이 방에서 나가실 테죠.」\n\n「나가지요, 부인, 더없이 기꺼이요.」 픽윅 씨가 대답했다.\n\n「당장이요, 선생.」 부인이 말했다.\n\n「물론입니다, 부인.」 픽윅 씨가 아주 재빨리 끼어들었다. 「물론이지요, 부인. 저는—저는—참으로 죄송합니다, 부인.」 픽윅 씨가 침대 발치에 모습을 드러내며 말했다. 「이 놀람과 동요의 죄 없는 빌미가 된 것이 말입니다. 깊이 죄송합니다, 부인.」\n\n부인이 문을 가리켰다. 더없이 곤혹스러운 상황에서, 바로 이 순간 픽윅 씨 성품의 한 가지 빼어난 자질이 아름답게 드러났다. 그가 옛 야경꾼처럼 잠잘 때 쓰는 모자 위에 황급히 모자를 덮어쓰긴 했어도, 구두와 각반을 손에 들고 외투와 조끼를 팔에 걸치고 있긴 해도, 그 무엇도 그의 타고난 예의 바름을 누를 수는 없었다.\n\n「대단히 죄송합니다, 부인.」 픽윅 씨가 아주 깊이 고개를 숙이며 말했다.\n\n「죄송하시다면, 선생, 당장 이 방에서 나가실 테죠.」 부인이 말했다.\n\n「즉시요, 부인. 이 순간 당장요, 부인.」 픽윅 씨가 문을 열며 말했는데, 그러는 사이 구두 두 짝을 와장창 떨어뜨리고 말았다.\n\n「바라옵건대, 부인.」 픽윅 씨가 구두를 주워 들고 다시 고개를 숙이려 돌아서며 말을 이었다. 「바라옵건대, 부인, 저의 티끌 하나 없는 인품과, 제가 부인 같은 여성분들에 대해 품은 헌신적인 존경심이, 이 일에 대한 약간의 변명거리나마 되어 주기를—」 그러나 픽윅 씨가 그 문장을 마치기도 전에, 부인은 그를 복도로 떠밀어 내고는 등 뒤로 문을 잠그고 빗장을 질러 버렸다.\n\n조금 전의 난처한 처지에서 그토록 조용히 빠져나온 데 대해 픽윅 씨가 아무리 스스로를 축하할 근거가 있다 한들, 지금의 그의 형편은 결코 부러워할 만한 것이 아니었다. 그는 한밤중에, 낯선 집의, 트인 복도에, 옷도 반쯤만 걸친 채 홀로 있었다. 불빛을 들고서도 도무지 찾지 못했던 방을 칠흑 같은 어둠 속에서 찾아갈 수 있으리라고는 도무지 기대할 수 없었고, 그렇게 헛되이 더듬는 중에 조금이라도 소리를 낸다면, 잠 못 든 어떤 여행객에게 총을 맞고, 어쩌면 죽임을 당할 위험이 다분했다. 날이 밝을 때까지 있던 자리에 머무는 것 말고는 달리 방도가 없었다. 그리하여 복도를 따라 몇 걸음 더듬어 가다가, 그러는 와중에 구두 여러 켤레에 걸려 넘어져 한없이 놀란 픽윅 씨는, 벽의 작은 우묵한 곳으로 몸을 웅크려 들어가, 되도록 의연하게 아침을 기다렸다.\n\n그러나 그는 이 인내의 추가 시련을 겪을 운명은 아니었으니, 그가 지금의 은신처에 몸을 숨긴 지 얼마 되지 않아, 말로 다 할 수 없는 공포 속에서, 불빛을 든 한 사내가 복도 끝에 나타났기 때문이다. 그러나 그가 자기 충실한 시종의 모습을 알아보자, 그 공포는 별안간 기쁨으로 바뀌었다. 그는 과연 새뮤얼 웰러 씨였으니, 우편 마차를 기다리느라 자지 않고 있던 구두닦이와 이렇게 늦은 시각까지 이야기를 나누다가, 이제 막 잠자리에 들려던 참이었다.\n\n「샘.」 픽윅 씨가 그의 앞에 불쑥 나타나며 말했다. 「내 침실이 어디냐?」\n\n웰러 씨는 더없이 분명한 놀라움을 담아 주인을 빤히 쳐다보았다. 그러고는 그 질문을 세 차례나 거듭한 뒤에야 비로소 몸을 돌려, 그토록 오래 찾아 헤매던 방으로 앞장서 안내했다.\n\n「샘.」 침대에 들면서 픽윅 씨가 말했다. 「오늘 밤 나는 일찍이 들어 본 적도 없는 가장 기막힌 실수를 하나 저질렀구나.」\n\n「충분히 그러실 만하지요, 선생.」 웰러 씨가 무뚝뚝하게 대답했다.\n\n「하지만 이것만은 단단히 마음먹었다, 샘.」 픽윅 씨가 말했다. 「이 집에서 여섯 달을 묵는다 해도, 다시는 이 집 안을 혼자서 돌아다니는 일에 나 자신을 맡기지 않으리라는 것을.」\n\n「그게 선생께서 내리실 수 있는 가장 신중한 결심입지요, 선생.」 웰러 씨가 대답했다. 「선생께서는 판단력이 잠깐 마실이라도 나갈 때면, 누가 곁에서 좀 보살펴 드릴 사람이 꽤나 필요하시거든요, 선생.」\n\n「그게 무슨 소리냐, 샘?」 픽윅 씨가 말했다. 그는 침대에서 몸을 일으켜 손을 뻗으며, 무언가 더 말하려는 듯하더니, 별안간 스스로를 다잡고는 몸을 돌려, 시종에게 「잘 자게」라고 일렀다.\n\n「안녕히 주무십시오, 선생.」 웰러 씨가 대답했다. 그는 문밖으로 나가서 걸음을 멈추더니—고개를 절레절레 흔들고—다시 걸어가다가—멈춰 서서—촛불 심지를 다듬고—또다시 고개를 절레절레 흔들고는—마침내 더없이 깊은 명상에 잠긴 듯, 천천히 자기 방으로 걸어갔다.\n\n픽윅 씨가 노란 컬페이퍼를 만 중년 부인과 모험을 겪던 그 아침이 밝아 올 무렵, 마구간 마당 가까이에 있는 작은 방 안에서, 웰러 영감은 런던으로 떠날 채비를 차리며 앉아 있었다. 그는 초상화를 그리기에 더없이 좋은 자세로 앉아 있었으니, 여기 그 모습이 있다.\n\n웰러 씨의 옆얼굴은 그의 인생 어느 이른 시기에는 어쩌면 굳세고 단호한 윤곽을 드러냈을지도 모른다. 그러나 그의 얼굴은 잘 먹고 사는 생활과, 체념이라는 점에서 유난스러운 기질의 영향 아래 부풀어 올랐고, 그 굳세고 살집 좋은 곡선들이 본래 정해졌던 한계를 한참 넘어 뻗어 나간 탓에, 정면에서 그 얼굴 전체를 똑바로 바라보지 않는 한 그저 아주 불그스레한 코의 맨 끄트머리만 겨우 가려낼 수 있을 정도였다. 그의 턱은 같은 이유로 그 인상적인 부위 앞에 흔히 '이중'이라는 말을 붙여 묘사하곤 하는 그 묵직하고 위풍당당한 형태를 갖추게 되었으며, 그의 안색은 그의 직업에 종사하는 신사들과 덜 익힌 로스트비프에서나 볼 수 있는, 그 독특하게 얼룩덜룩한 색의 조합을 드러내고 있었다. 목에는 진홍색 여행용 숄을 두르고 있었는데, 그것이 어찌나 알아차릴 수 없을 만큼 점진적으로 턱과 뒤섞여 들어가던지, 어느 쪽 주름이 숄의 주름이고 어느 쪽이 턱의 주름인지 분간하기 어려울 지경이었다. 그 위로 그는 넓은 분홍 줄무늬 무늬의 긴 조끼를 걸쳤고, 다시 그 위로는 커다란 놋쇠 단추들로 장식한 자락 넓은 초록색 코트를 걸쳤는데, 그 가운데 허리께를 장식한 두 단추는 어찌나 멀리 떨어져 있던지 그 둘을 동시에 본 사람은 일찍이 아무도 없었다. 그의 머리카락은 짧고 매끄럽고 검었으며, 챙이 넓고 우듬지가 낮은 갈색 모자의 너른 챙 아래로 겨우 보일락 말락 했다. 그의 다리는 무릎까지 오는 골덴 반바지와 칠한 톱부츠로 감싸여 있었고, 구리 시곗줄 하나가 인장 하나와 같은 재질의 열쇠 하나로 끝나며 그의 너른 허리띠에서 헐겁게 늘어져 달랑거리고 있었다.\n\n우리는 웰러 씨가 런던으로 떠날 채비에 여념이 없었다고 말했거니와—실은 그가 음식을 섭취하는 중이었다. 그의 앞 탁자 위에는 에일 한 단지와 차가운 소고기 한 덩이, 그리고 매우 점잖아 보이는 빵 한 덩어리가 놓여 있었는데, 그는 더없이 엄격한 공평함으로 그 각각에 차례로 자신의 은혜를 베풀고 있었다. 그가 막 그 빵에서 큼직한 한 조각을 잘라 냈을 때, 누군가 방으로 들어오는 발소리에 그는 고개를 들었고, 자기 아들의 모습을 보았다.\n\n\"안녕, 새미!\" 아버지가 말했다.\n\n아들은 에일 단지로 걸어가더니, 부모에게 의미심장하게 고개를 끄덕여 보이고는, 대답 삼아 한 모금 길게 들이켰다.\n\n\"제법 빨아들이는 힘이 좋구나, 새미.\" 웰러 영감이, 맏아들이 단지를 반쯤 비워 내려놓자 단지 속을 들여다보며 말했다. \"새미, 네가 만약 그런 처지로 태어났더라면, 보기 드물게 훌륭한 굴이 됐을 게다.\"\n\n\"예, 그렇겠지요. 점잖게 입에 풀칠은 했을 겁니다.\" 샘은 상당한 기세로 차가운 소고기에 달려들며 대답했다.\n\n\"새미, 난 정말 유감스럽다.\" 웰러 영감이, 마시기 전에 단지로 작은 원을 그리며 에일을 흔들어 섞으면서 말했다. \"새미, 그 오디색 옷 입은 작자한테 네가 속아 넘어갔다는 말을 네 입으로 듣다니, 정말 유감스럽다. 사흘 전까지만 해도 난 늘 웰러라는 이름하고 속아 넘어간다는 게 한데 어울릴 일은 결코 없으리라 생각했다, 새미, 결코.\"\n\n\"물론 과부 경우는 늘 예외로 치고 말이죠.\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n\"과부들 말이냐, 새미.\" 웰러 씨가 살짝 안색을 바꾸며 대답했다. \"과부들이야 어떤 규칙에든 예외지. 사람 홀려 넘기는 점에서 과부 하나가 보통 여자 몇 명에 맞먹는지 들은 적이 있다. 스물다섯이었던 것 같은데, 그보다 더는 아닌지는 똑똑히 모르겠구나.\"\n\n\"흠, 그 정도면 제법이네요.\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n\"게다가,\" 웰러 씨가 끼어든 말은 못 들은 체하고 이어 갔다. \"그건 아주 다른 얘기다. 새미, 술만 거나해지면 부지깽이로 마누라를 패던 신사를 변호한 변호사가 뭐라고 했는지 너도 알지. '재판장님, 결국 따지고 보면,' 그가 말했지, '그건 사랑스러운 약점입니다.' 그러니 새미, 과부들에 대해선 나도 그렇게 말하는 거고, 너도 내 나이쯤 되면 그렇게 말하게 될 게다.\"\n\n\"제가 좀 더 분별이 있었어야 했지요, 압니다.\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n\"분별이 있었어야 했다고!\" 웰러 씨가 주먹으로 탁자를 치며 되풀이했다. \"분별이 있었어야 했다고! 아니, 난 네 교육의 절반은커녕 사분의 일도 못 받은 어린놈을 아는데—시장 바닥에서 떠돌며 잠자 본 적도 없고, 그래, 여섯 달도 안 되는 놈이지만—그런 식으로 속아 넘어가는 건 경멸했을 게다. 경멸했을 거야, 새미.\" 이 가슴 아픈 회상이 불러일으킨 감정의 격앙 속에서, 웰러 씨는 종을 울려 에일 한 파인트를 더 시켰다.\n\n\"뭐, 지금 와서 그 얘길 해 봐야 소용없죠.\" 샘이 말했다. \"다 끝난 일이고, 어쩔 수도 없고, 그게 그나마 위안이에요. 터키에서 엉뚱한 사람 목을 베고 나서 늘 하는 말처럼 말이죠. 이제 제 차례예요, 아버지. 이 트로터란 작자만 붙잡으면 한판 제대로 할 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그러길 바란다, 새미. 그러길 바라.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다. \"네 건강을 위하여, 새미, 그리고 네가 집안 이름에 끼친 그 망신을 어서 씻어 내기를.\" 이 건배를 기리며 웰러 씨는 새로 도착한 파인트의 적어도 삼분의 이는 단숨에 들이켜고는, 나머지를 처리하라고 아들에게 건넸으며, 아들은 그것을 즉각 해치웠다.\n\n\"자 이제, 새미.\" 웰러 씨가 구리 줄 끝에 매달린 커다란 양면짜리 은시계를 들여다보며 말했다. \"이제 사무소에 올라가 운행표를 받고 마차에 짐 싣는 걸 봐야 할 시간이구나. 새미, 마차란 건 총하고 같아서 말이다—발사되기 전에 아주 조심스럽게 장전해야 하거든.\"\n\n이 아버지답고 직업적인 농담에 웰러 2세는 효성스러운 미소를 지었다. 그의 존경하는 아버지는 엄숙한 어조로 말을 이어 갔다.\n\n\"난 이제 널 떠난다, 새뮤얼아, 내 아들아. 그리고 언제 또 널 보게 될지는 알 수 없구나. 네 새어미가 날 너무 들들 볶았을 수도 있고, 네가 그 유명한 벨 새비지의 웰러 씨 소식을 다음번에 듣게 될 즈음엔 천 가지 일이 일어나 있을지도 모르지. 집안 이름은 네게 크게 달려 있다, 새뮤얼아, 부디 그 이름에 옳은 일을 해 주길 바란다. 자잘한 예절 같은 거야 내 자신만큼이나 너를 믿을 수 있다는 걸 안다. 그러니 딱 한 가지 작은 충고만 해 두마. 혹시라도 네가 쉰 살이 넘어서 누구든—누구든 상관없다—결혼할 마음이 들거든, 방이 하나 있으면 네 방에 틀어박혀서, 당장 독약을 먹고 죽어라. 목매다는 건 천박하니 그런 건 얼씬도 못 하게 해라. 독약을 먹어라, 새뮤얼아, 내 아들아, 독약을 먹어. 나중에 잘했다 싶을 게다.\" 이 감동적인 말과 함께, 웰러 씨는 아들을 물끄러미 바라보더니, 천천히 발꿈치를 돌려 그의 시야에서 사라졌다.\n\n이 말들이 불러일으킨 사색에 잠긴 기분으로, 새뮤얼 웰러 씨는 아버지가 떠난 뒤 그레이트 화이트 호스를 나섰다. 그는 세인트 클레먼트 교회 쪽으로 걸음을 옮기며, 그 유서 깊은 경내를 거니는 것으로 우울함을 떨쳐 보려 애썼다. 한동안 어슬렁거리던 그는 어느 외진 곳에 이르렀는데—고풍스러운 모습의 안마당 같은 곳이었다—그곳에는 자신이 들어온 모퉁이 말고는 다른 출구가 없다는 것을 알게 되었다. 그가 막 발길을 되돌리려는 참에, 별안간 나타난 어떤 광경에 그 자리에 못 박힌 듯 멈춰 섰다. 이 출현의 양상과 방식을 이제부터 이야기하기로 한다.\n\n새뮤얼 웰러 씨는 깊은 멍한 생각에 잠겨 이따금 그 오래된 벽돌집들을 올려다보며, 건강해 보이는 어느 하녀가 블라인드를 올리거나 침실 창문을 활짝 열 때마다 그녀에게 한 번씩 윙크를 던지고 있었는데, 그때 마당 안쪽에 있는 어느 정원의 초록색 문이 열리더니, 한 남자가 거기서 나와 등 뒤로 그 초록색 문을 아주 조심스럽게 닫고는, 웰러 씨가 서 있는 바로 그 자리를 향해 빠른 걸음으로 걸어왔다.\n\n자, 이것을 다른 어떤 정황도 곁들이지 않은 하나의 동떨어진 사실로만 본다면, 거기엔 그다지 별날 것이 없었다. 세상 여러 곳에서 사람들은 정원에서 나와 등 뒤로 초록색 문을 닫고, 심지어 빠른 걸음으로 걸어가기까지 하지만, 대중의 각별한 주목을 끄는 일이란 전혀 없으니 말이다. 그러므로 그 남자에게든, 그의 거동에든, 아니면 그 둘 다에든, 웰러 씨의 특별한 주의를 끌 만한 무언가가 분명 있었음에 틀림없다. 그것이 있었는지 없었는지는, 우리가 문제의 그 인물의 행동을 충실히 기록한 다음, 독자의 판단에 맡기기로 하자.\n\n그 남자는 등 뒤로 초록색 문을 닫고서, 이미 두 번이나 말했듯이, 빠른 걸음으로 안마당을 가로질러 걸어왔다. 그러나 웰러 씨를 보자마자 그는 주춤거리며 멈춰 섰는데, 마치 그 순간 어떤 길을 택해야 할지 잠시 망설이는 듯했다. 그러나 초록색 문이 등 뒤로 닫혀 있고 앞쪽 하나 말고는 다른 출구가 없었으므로, 그는 빠져나가려면 새뮤얼 웰러 씨를 지나쳐야 한다는 것을 알아차리는 데 오래 걸리지 않았다. 그리하여 그는 다시 빠른 걸음을 되찾아, 똑바로 앞만 응시하며 다가왔다. 그 남자에게서 가장 별난 점은, 일찍이 본 적 없는 더없이 무시무시하고 놀라운 찡그림으로 얼굴을 일그러뜨리고 있다는 것이었다. 그 남자가 한순간에 자기 얼굴 위에 덧씌운 것만큼 그토록 기상천외한 인공의 조각으로 자연의 솜씨를 위장한 적은 일찍이 없었다.\n\n\"이런!\" 웰러 씨는 남자가 다가오자 속으로 중얼거렸다. \"이거 참 묘하군. 그 작자라고 맹세라도 할 수 있었는데.\"\n\n남자가 다가왔고, 가까이 올수록 그의 얼굴은 그 어느 때보다 더 무섭게 일그러졌다.\n\n\"저 검은 머리하고 오디색 옷에는 맹세를 걸 수 있겠는데.\" 웰러 씨가 말했다. \"다만 저런 얼굴은 여태 본 적이 없단 말이야.\"\n\n웰러 씨가 이렇게 말하는 사이, 남자의 얼굴이 완전히 흉측한, 이 세상 것 같지 않은 경련으로 뒤틀렸다. 그러나 그는 샘의 아주 가까이를 지나칠 수밖에 없었고, 그 신사의 꼼꼼히 살피는 눈길은 그 소름 끼치는 온갖 표정의 뒤틀림 아래서, 잡 트로터 씨의 그 작은 눈과 너무도 닮아 쉽게 착각할 수 없는 무언가를 알아차릴 수 있게 해 주었다.\n\n\"어이, 거기 양반!\" 샘이 사납게 외쳤다.\n\n낯선 자는 멈춰 섰다.\n\n\"어이!\" 샘이 한층 더 퉁명스럽게 되풀이했다.\n\n그 끔찍한 얼굴의 남자는 더없이 놀란 기색으로 마당 위쪽을 보고, 마당 아래쪽을 보고, 집들의 창문 안을 들여다보고—샘 웰러만 빼고 사방을 두리번거리더니—한 발짝 더 앞으로 내디뎠는데, 또 한 번의 고함에 다시 멈춰 섰다.\n\n\"어이, 거기 양반!\" 샘이 세 번째로 말했다.\n\n이제는 그 목소리가 어디서 나오는지 모르는 척할 도리가 없었으므로, 낯선 자는 달리 방도가 없어 마침내 샘 웰러의 얼굴을 똑바로 바라보았다.\n\n\"안 통해, 잡 트로터.\" 샘이 말했다. \"어서! 그런 수작은 그만둬. 넌 그렇게 잘생긴 얼굴도 아니라서 그 멀쩡한 인상을 함부로 내던질 처지가 못 돼. 그 눈깔 제자리로 되돌려 놓으라고, 안 그러면 머리통에서 아예 빼 버릴 테니까. 알아들어?\"\n\n웰러 씨가 이 말의 취지대로 행동할 작정이 완연해 보이자, 트로터 씨는 차츰 얼굴이 본래의 표정을 되찾게 내버려 두더니, 기쁨에 찬 듯 화들짝 놀라며 외쳤다. \"이게 누구신가요? 워커 씨!\"\n\n\"아.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"날 만나서 무척 반갑단 말이지, 안 그래?\"\n\n\"반갑다마다요!\" 잡 트로터가 외쳤다. \"오, 워커 씨, 제가 이 만남을 얼마나 고대했는지 아셨더라면! 너무 벅찹니다, 워커 씨. 견딜 수가 없어요, 정말 견딜 수가 없습니다.\" 그러고는 이 말과 함께, 트로터 씨는 그야말로 눈물의 홍수를 터뜨리며, 두 팔을 웰러 씨의 팔에 휘감고는 기쁨의 황홀경에 젖어 그를 꼭 껴안았다.\n\n\"떨어져!\" 샘이 이 과정에 분개하여 외치며, 열렬한 지인의 포옹에서 벗어나려 헛되이 애를 썼다. \"떨어지라니까. 왜 나한테 매달려서 우는 거야, 이 들고 다니는 펌프 같은 놈아?\"\n\n\"당신을 만나서 너무도 기쁘기 때문이지요.\" 잡 트로터가, 웰러 씨의 호전적인 기색이 처음으로 누그러지자 차츰 그를 놓아주며 대답했다. \"오, 워커 씨, 너무 벅찹니다.\"\n\n\"벅차다고!\" 샘이 받아 말했다. \"내가 보기엔 너무 벅찬 것 같군—그것도 좀! 자, 나한테 할 말이 뭐야, 응?\"\n\n트로터 씨는 아무 대답도 하지 않았다. 그 작은 분홍색 손수건이 한창 위력을 발휘하고 있었기 때문이다.\n\n\"내가 네 머리통을 박살 내기 전에, 나한테 할 말이 뭐냐고?\" 웰러 씨가 위협적인 태도로 되풀이했다.\n\n\"네?\" 트로터 씨가 더없이 고결하게 놀란 표정으로 말했다.\n\n\"나한테 할 말이 뭐냐고?\"\n\n\"제가요, 워커 씨!\"\n\n\"날 워커라고 부르지 마. 내 이름은 웰러야. 너도 잘 알면서. 나한테 할 말이 뭐냐고?\"\n\n\"이런, 워커 씨—웰러 씨 말입니다—할 말이 아주 많지요, 어디 편하게 얘기할 만한 데로 가신다면요. 제가 당신을 얼마나 찾아 헤맸는지 아신다면, 웰러 씨—\"\n\n\"무척 애써서 찾았겠지, 안 그래?\" 샘이 무뚝뚝하게 말했다.\n\n\"무척, 무척요, 나리.\" 트로터 씨가 얼굴 근육 하나 까딱하지 않고 대답했다. \"하지만 악수를 청하지요, 웰러 씨.\"\n\n샘은 몇 초간 동행을 빤히 바라보더니, 갑작스러운 충동에 이끌린 듯 그의 청에 응했다.\n\n\"그래.\" 잡 트로터가 걸음을 옮기며 말했다. \"당신의 그 친절하고 훌륭하신 주인 어른은 어떻게 지내십니까? 오, 정말 훌륭한 신사분이시지요, 웰러 씨! 그 끔찍하던 밤에 감기는 안 드셨길 바랍니다, 나리.\"\n\n잡 트로터가 이 말을 할 때 그의 눈에 깊은 능청스러움이 순간적으로 스쳤고, 그것은 웰러 씨의 움켜쥔 주먹에 짜릿한 떨림을 일으켜, 그는 그자의 갈비뼈에 한 방 시연을 해 보이고 싶은 욕망으로 달아올랐다. 그러나 샘은 자신을 억누르고는, 주인 어른은 더없이 평안하시다고 대답했다.\n\n\"오, 정말 다행입니다.\" 트로터 씨가 대답했다. \"여기 계신가요?\"\n\n\"네 주인은?\" 샘이 대답 삼아 물었다.\n\n\"오, 네, 여기 계시지요. 그런데 말씀드리기 가슴 아프지만, 웰러 씨, 그분은 그 어느 때보다도 더 못된 짓을 하고 계십니다.\"\n\n\"아하, 아하!\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n\"오, 충격적이에요—끔찍합니다!\"\n\n\"기숙 여학교에서?\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n\"아뇨, 기숙 여학교는 아닙니다.\" 잡 트로터가, 샘이 앞서 알아챘던 그 능청스러운 표정으로 대답했다. \"기숙 여학교는 아니에요.\"\n\n\"초록색 문 달린 그 집에서?\" 샘이 동행을 빤히 살피며 말했다.\n\n\"아뇨, 아뇨—오, 거기가 아니에요.\" 잡이 그답지 않게 재빠른 어조로 대답했다. \"거기가 아닙니다.\"\n\n\"넌 거기서 뭘 하고 있었는데?\" 샘이 날카로운 눈길로 물었다. \"어쩌다 우연히 그 문 안으로 들어갔나 보지?\"\n\n\"이런, 웰러 씨.\" 잡이 대답했다. \"당신한테는 제 자잘한 비밀을 털어놓아도 상관없지요. 왜냐면, 아시다시피, 우린 처음 만났을 때 서로한테 그렇게 정이 들었으니까요. 그날 아침 우리가 얼마나 즐거웠는지 기억하시지요?\"\n\n\"오, 그래.\" 샘이 짜증스럽게 말했다. \"기억하지. 그래서?\"\n\n\"그래서요.\" 잡이 대단히 정확한 말투로, 중대한 비밀을 전하는 사람의 나직한 어조로 말했다. \"그 초록색 문 달린 집에는요, 웰러 씨, 하인을 꽤 많이 두고 있답니다.\"\n\n\"그 집 꼴을 보아하니 그럴 것 같더군.\" 샘이 끼어들었다.\n\n\"그렇지요.\" 트로터 씨가 이어 갔다. \"그리고 그중 하나가 요리사인데, 돈을 조금 모아 두었답니다, 웰러 씨. 그래서 살림을 꾸릴 수 있게 되면, 잡화점이라도 작게 하나 열고 싶어 하지요.\"\n\"그렇군.\"\n\n\"네, 웰러 씨. 그런데 나리, 제가 다니는 예배당에서 그 여자를 만났습니다. 이 고장에 있는 아주 아담하고 작은 예배당이지요, 웰러 씨. 거기선 찬송가 제4집을 부르는데, 저는 그걸 늘 작은 책자에 담아 가지고 다닌답니다. 어쩌면 제 손에 들린 걸 보셨을지도 모르지요—그래서 그 여자랑 좀 가까워졌고, 거기서부터 우리 사이에 친분이 싹텄지요. 그래서 감히 말씀드리자면, 웰러 씨, 그 잡화상은 제가 되게 생겼습니다.\"\n\n\"아, 그것 참 사랑스러운 잡화상이 되겠군.\" 샘이 강렬한 혐오의 곁눈질로 잡을 바라보며 대답했다.\n\n\"이 일의 가장 큰 이점은요, 웰러 씨.\" 잡이 말하면서 눈에 눈물을 글썽이며 이어 갔다. \"지금 그 못된 자 밑에서 하고 있는 이 치욕스러운 시중살이를 그만두고, 더 낫고 더 덕스러운 삶에 저를 바칠 수 있게 된다는 거지요. 제가 자라 온 방식에 더 가까운 그런 삶 말입니다, 웰러 씨.\"\n\n\"넌 무척 곱게 자랐나 보군.\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n\"오, 무척요, 웰러 씨, 무척이요.\" 잡이 대답했다. 자기 어린 시절의 순결함을 떠올리며 트로터 씨는 분홍색 손수건을 꺼내 들고 펑펑 울었다.\n\n\"넌 함께 학교 다니기에 보기 드물게 좋은 아이였겠어.\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n\"그랬지요, 나리.\" 잡이 깊은 한숨을 내쉬며 대답했다. \"전 그곳의 우상이었답니다.\"\n\n\"아.\" 샘이 말했다. \"그럴 만도 하지. 네 복된 어머니한테 넌 얼마나 큰 위안이었겠어.\"\n\n이 말에 잡 트로터 씨는 분홍색 손수건 끝을 양쪽 눈가에 차례로 하나씩 갖다 대고는 펑펑 울기 시작했다.\n\n\"이 작자 왜 이래.\" 샘이 분개하여 말했다. \"첼시 수도장이 너한테는 명함도 못 내밀겠군. 이번엔 뭣 때문에 녹아내리는 거야? 악행을 저질렀다는 양심 때문에?\"\n\n\"제 감정을 억누를 수가 없습니다, 웰러 씨.\" 잡이 잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤 말했다. \"제 주인이, 제가 당신 주인과 나눈 대화를 의심해서, 저를 역마차에 태워 끌고 가 버리고, 그 사랑스러운 젊은 아가씨를 설득해 자기는 아무것도 모른다고 말하게 하고, 여교장에게 뇌물을 먹여 똑같이 하게 해 놓고는, 더 짭짤한 투기 거리를 찾아 그 아가씨를 버렸다고 생각하면! 오! 웰러 씨, 몸서리가 쳐집니다.\"\n\n\"오, 그게 사정이었구먼, 그래?\" 웰러 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"두말할 것 없이 그렇지요.\" 잡이 대답했다.\n\n\"그럼.\" 샘이, 이제 호텔 가까이에 다다랐으므로 말했다. \"너랑 좀 얘기를 나누고 싶은데, 잡. 그러니 특별히 바쁜 일이 없으면, 오늘 밤 여덟 시쯤 그레이트 화이트 호스에서 너를 보고 싶다.\"\n\n\"꼭 오겠습니다.\" 잡이 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 그러는 게 좋을 거야.\" 샘이 자못 의미심장한 표정으로 대답했다. \"안 그러면 어쩌면 내가 저 초록색 문 너머에서 너를 찾게 될지도 모르거든. 그러면 내가 너를 가로채 버릴 수도 있고 말이지, 알겠어?\"\n\n\"꼭 함께하겠습니다, 나리.\" 트로터 씨가 말하고는, 더없이 열렬하게 샘의 손을 꼭 쥔 뒤 걸어갔다.\n\n\"조심해라, 잡 트로터, 조심해.\" 샘이 그의 뒷모습을 바라보며 말했다. \"안 그러면 이번엔 내가 너한테 한 수 위가 될 테니까. 정말로 그럴 거야.\" 이 혼잣말을 내뱉고, 잡이 더는 보이지 않을 때까지 그 뒷모습을 바라본 뒤, 웰러 씨는 부지런히 주인의 침실로 향했다.\n\n\"전부 준비 중입니다, 나리.\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n\"뭐가 준비 중이라는 거냐, 샘?\" 픽윅 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"그자들을 찾아냈습니다, 나리.\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n\"누굴 찾아냈다고?\"\n\n\"그 수상쩍은 작자하고, 검은 머리의 그 우울한 녀석 말입니다.\"\n\n\"그럴 리가, 샘!\" 픽윅 씨가 더없는 기세로 말했다. \"그들이 어디 있느냐, 샘. 어디 있어?\"\n\n\"쉿, 쉿!\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다. 그러고는 픽윅 씨가 옷 입는 것을 거들면서, 자신이 착수하고자 하는 행동 계획을 자세히 일러 주었다.\n\n\"하지만 이건 언제 하게 되는 거냐, 샘?\" 픽윅 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"다 때가 되면요, 나리.\" 샘이 대답했다.\n\n그것이 제때에 이루어졌는지 아닌지는 이후에 밝혀질 것이다.\n\n피터 매그너스 씨가 그와 전날 저녁을 함께 보냈던 방으로 픽윅 씨가 내려가 보니, 그 신사는 두 개의 가방과 가죽 모자 상자와 갈색 종이 꾸러미에 들어 있던 물건들의 대부분을 자기 몸에 가능한 한 돋보이게 걸치고서, 더없이 흥분하고 동요한 상태로 방 안을 이리저리 거닐고 있었다.\n\n\"좋은 아침입니다, 선생.\" 피터 매그너스 씨가 말했다. \"이거 어떻게 보십니까, 선생?\"\n\n\"정말 아주 효과적이군요.\" 픽윅 씨가 사람 좋은 미소를 띠고 피터 매그너스 씨의 의복을 살펴보며 대답했다.\n\n\"네, 이만하면 될 것 같습니다.\" 매그너스 씨가 말했다. \"픽윅 씨, 선생, 제 명함을 올려 보냈습니다.\"\n\n\"그러셨습니까?\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그랬더니 종업원이 답을 가지고 돌아왔는데, 열한 시에 저를 만나 주시겠다는 겁니다—열한 시에요, 선생. 지금 십오 분밖에 안 남았습니다.\"\n\n\"시간이 다 됐군요.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"네, 꽤 가까워졌지요.\" 매그너스 씨가 대답했다. \"마음 편하기엔 좀 지나치게 가까워졌어요—그렇지 않습니까! 픽윅 씨, 선생?\"\n\n\"이런 경우엔 자신감이 큰 힘이 됩니다.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그런 것 같습니다, 선생.\" 피터 매그너스 씨가 말했다. \"저는 아주 자신이 있습니다, 선생. 정말이지, 픽윅 씨, 이런 경우에 사내가 무슨 두려움을 느낄 까닭이 있는지 저는 모르겠어요, 선생. 무엇이 문제입니까, 선생? 부끄러워할 것이라곤 없지요. 서로의 편의를 위한 일일 뿐, 그 이상도 그 이하도 아닙니다. 한쪽에는 남편, 다른 쪽에는 아내. 그게 이 문제에 대한 제 견해입니다, 픽윅 씨.\"\n\n\"아주 철학적인 견해로군요.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다. \"하지만 아침 식사가 기다리고 있습니다, 매그너스 씨. 갑시다.\"\n\n두 사람은 아침 식사를 하러 앉았다. 그러나 피터 매그너스 씨의 큰소리에도 불구하고, 그가 상당한 정도의 긴장에 시달리고 있다는 것이 역력했으니, 식욕의 상실, 찻잔을 엎으려는 경향, 익살을 부려 보려는 유령 같은 시도, 그리고 매 순간마다 시계를 들여다보려는 억누를 수 없는 충동 따위가 그 주요 증상 가운데 들어 있었다.\n\n\"헤헤헤.\" 매그너스 씨가 명랑한 척하면서, 동요로 숨을 헐떡이며 킥킥거렸다. \"이제 이 분밖에 안 남았습니다, 픽윅 씨. 저 창백해 보입니까, 선생?\"\n\n\"그리 심하진 않습니다.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다.\n\n잠시 짤막한 침묵이 흘렀다.\n\n\"실례합니다만, 픽윅 씨, 혹시 살아오시면서 이런 일을 해 보신 적이 있으십니까?\" 매그너스 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"청혼 말씀이십니까?\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"전혀 없습니다.\" 픽윅 씨가 대단한 기세로 말했다. \"전혀요.\"\n\n\"그러면 어떻게 시작하는 것이 가장 좋은지 짐작도 못 하시겠군요?\" 매그너스 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"글쎄요.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"그 문제에 대해 어떤 생각을 떠올려 본 적은 있을지 모르나, 한 번도 경험의 시험에 부쳐 본 적이 없으니, 만약 당신이 그것에 따라 일을 진행하시게 된다면 제가 송구스러울 것입니다.\"\n\n\"무슨 조언이든 주시면 대단히 감사하겠습니다, 선생.\" 매그너스 씨가 말하며 다시 시계를 한번 보았는데, 바늘은 막 오 분이 지나려는 참이었다.\n\n\"그러면, 선생.\" 픽윅 씨가, 그 위대한 인물이 마음만 먹으면 자신의 말을 그토록 깊이 인상적으로 만들 수 있는 그 심오한 엄숙함으로 말했다. \"저라면, 선생, 먼저 그 숙녀의 아름다움과 훌륭한 자질에 대한 찬사로 시작하겠습니다. 그러고 나서, 선생, 거기서 저 자신의 부족함으로 화제를 옮기겠습니다.\"\n\n\"아주 좋습니다.\" 매그너스 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"오직 그분에게만 부족하다는 것이지요, 명심하십시오, 선생.\" 픽윅 씨가 이었다. \"제가 전적으로 부족한 인물은 아님을 보이기 위해, 선생, 저는 제 지난 삶과 현재의 처지를 간략히 되돌아보겠습니다. 유추를 통해, 다른 누구에게라면 제가 아주 바람직한 상대일 것이라고 주장하겠습니다. 그러고 나서 제 사랑의 뜨거움과 헌신의 깊이를 펼쳐 보이겠습니다. 어쩌면 그쯤에서 그분의 손을 잡고 싶은 유혹을 느낄지도 모르지요.\"\n\n\"네, 알겠습니다.\" 매그너스 씨가 말했다. \"그게 아주 중요한 대목이겠군요.\"\n\n\"그러고 나서, 선생.\" 픽윅 씨가, 화제가 더욱 찬란한 빛깔로 눈앞에 떠오를수록 점점 열을 올리며 계속했다. \"그러고 나서, 선생, 저는 솔직하고 단순한 질문에 이르겠습니다. '저를 받아 주시겠습니까?' 이쯤에서 그분이 고개를 돌리리라고 가정해도 무방하리라 봅니다.\"\n\n\"그게 당연한 거라고 보십니까?\" 매그너스 씨가 말했다. \"왜냐하면 만약 그분이 적절한 대목에서 그렇게 하지 않으면 난처해질 테니까요.\"\n\n\"그렇게 하리라 봅니다.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"그러면, 선생, 저는 그분의 손을 꼭 쥐겠습니다. 그리고 제 생각엔—제 생각엔, 매그너스 씨—그렇게 한 다음, 거절이 없다고 가정하면, 그 순간 그분이 눈가에 갖다 대고 있으리라고—제 얕은 인간 본성 지식이 그렇게 짐작하게 합니다만—그 손수건을 살며시 치우고, 정중하게 살짝 입을 맞추겠습니다. 제 생각엔 그분에게 입을 맞출 것 같습니다, 매그너스 씨. 그리고 바로 이 대목에서, 만약 그분이 저를 받아들일 마음이 조금이라도 있다면, 수줍은 승낙을 제 귀에 속삭일 것이라고 저는 단연코 확신합니다.\"\n\n매그너스 씨는 흠칫 놀랐다. 그러고는 잠시 말없이 픽윅 씨의 영민한 얼굴을 바라보다가, (시계 바늘이 십 분 지난 지점을 가리키자) 그의 손을 따뜻하게 흔들고는 필사적으로 방에서 뛰쳐나갔다.\n\n픽윅 씨가 몇 걸음 이리저리 거닐고 있는 동안, 시계의 작은 바늘도 그가 보인 마지막 본보기를 따라, 삼십 분을 가리키는 숫자에 다다랐을 무렵, 갑자기 문이 열렸다. 그는 피터 매그너스 씨를 맞으려고 돌아섰으나, 그 대신 마주친 것은 터프먼 씨의 즐거운 얼굴, 윈클 씨의 평온한 표정, 그리고 스노드그래스 씨의 지적인 이목구비였다.\n\n픽윅 씨가 그들에게 인사하고 있을 때, 피터 매그너스 씨가 종종걸음으로 방에 들어왔다.\n\n\"여러분, 제가 말씀드렸던 그 신사분—매그너스 씨입니다.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"안녕하십니까, 여러분.\" 매그너스 씨가, 분명히 한껏 흥분한 상태로 말했다. \"픽윅 씨, 잠시만 말씀 좀 나누겠습니다, 선생.\"\n\n이렇게 말하면서 매그너스 씨는 자기 집게손가락을 픽윅 씨의 단춧구멍에 걸고는, 그를 창가 구석으로 끌고 가서 말했다—\n\n\"축하해 주십시오, 픽윅 씨. 저는 당신의 조언을 글자 그대로 따랐습니다.\"\n\n\"그래서 다 잘되었습니까?\" 픽윅 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"그렇습니다, 선생. 그보다 더 잘될 수는 없었을 겁니다.\" 매그너스 씨가 대답했다. \"픽윅 씨, 그분은 제 사람이 되었습니다.\"\n\n\"진심으로 축하드립니다.\" 픽윅 씨가 새 친구의 손을 따뜻하게 흔들며 대답했다.\n\n\"그분을 꼭 만나 보셔야 합니다, 선생.\" 매그너스 씨가 말했다. \"이쪽입니다, 괜찮으시다면. 잠깐만 실례하겠습니다, 여러분.\" 이렇게 서두르며 피터 매그너스 씨는 픽윅 씨를 방에서 끌고 나갔다. 그는 복도의 다음 문 앞에서 멈추더니, 그 문을 가볍게 두드렸다.\n\n\"들어오세요.\" 여인의 목소리가 말했다. 그래서 두 사람은 안으로 들어갔다.\n\n\"위더필드 양.\" 매그너스 씨가 말했다. \"제 아주 각별한 친구 픽윅 씨를 소개해 드리겠습니다. 픽윅 씨, 위더필드 양을 소개해 드리겠습니다.\"\n\n그 숙녀는 방의 안쪽 끝에 있었다. 픽윅 씨가 고개를 숙이면서 조끼 주머니에서 안경을 꺼내 썼는데, 그 과정을 마치자마자 픽윅 씨는 놀라움의 탄성을 내지르며 몇 걸음 뒤로 물러섰고, 그 숙녀는 반쯤 억누른 비명과 함께 두 손에 얼굴을 묻고 의자에 풀썩 주저앉았다. 그 광경에 피터 매그너스 씨는 그 자리에 못 박힌 듯 꼼짝 못 하고 서서, 공포와 놀라움의 극치를 드러내는 표정으로 두 사람을 번갈아 바라보았다.\n\n이것은 분명, 겉보기에는, 매우 알 수 없는 행동이었다. 그러나 사실인즉, 픽윅 씨는 안경을 쓰자마자 곧바로 미래의 매그너스 부인에게서 전날 밤 그토록 무엄하게 침입했던 그 방의 숙녀를 알아보았고, 그 안경이 픽윅 씨의 코에 걸리자마자 그 숙녀는 잠자리 모자의 온갖 끔찍함에 둘러싸여 있던 그 얼굴을 곧바로 알아본 것이었다. 그래서 숙녀는 비명을 질렀고, 픽윅 씨는 흠칫 놀랐던 것이다.\n\n\"픽윅 씨!\" 매그너스 씨가 경악에 빠져 외쳤다. \"이게 무슨 뜻입니까, 선생? 이게 무슨 뜻이냐고요, 선생?\" 매그너스 씨가 위협적이고 더 큰 목소리로 덧붙였다.\n\n\"선생.\" 픽윅 씨가, 피터 매그너스 씨가 그토록 갑작스럽게 자신을 명령조로 활용한 데에 다소 분개하여 말했다. \"저는 그 질문에 답하기를 거절합니다.\"\n\n\"거절하신다고요, 선생?\" 매그너스 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇습니다, 선생.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다. \"저 숙녀분의 입장을 곤란하게 하거나, 그분의 가슴에 불쾌한 기억을 일깨울 만한 어떤 말도, 그분의 동의와 허락 없이는 하지 않겠습니다.\"\n\n\"위더필드 양.\" 피터 매그너스 씨가 말했다. \"이 사람을 아십니까?\"\n\n\"이 사람을 아느냐고요!\" 중년 숙녀가 머뭇거리며 되뇌었다.\n\n\"네, 아느냐고요, 부인. 안다고 했습니다.\" 매그너스 씨가 사납게 대답했다.\n\n\"본 적은 있어요.\" 중년 숙녀가 대답했다.\n\n\"어디서요?\" 매그너스 씨가 물었다. \"어디서 말입니까?\"\n\n\"그건,\" 중년 숙녀가 자리에서 일어서며 고개를 돌리고 말했다. \"무슨 일이 있어도 밝히지 않겠어요.\"\n\n\"이해합니다, 부인.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"그리고 그 조심스러움을 존중합니다. 제 입으로는 결코 밝히지 않을 테니, 안심하십시오.\"\n\n\"이거 참, 부인.\" 매그너스 씨가 말했다. \"제가 당신과 관련해 처해 있는 처지를 생각하면, 부인께서 이 일을 꽤나 태연하게 넘기시는군요—꽤나 태연하게요, 부인.\"\n\n\"잔인하신 매그너스 씨!\" 중년 숙녀가 말했다. 여기서 그녀는 정말로 펑펑 울었다.\n\n\"하실 말씀은 저에게 하십시오, 선생.\" 픽윅 씨가 끼어들었다. \"누가 탓을 받아야 한다면, 그건 오직 저뿐입니다.\"\n\n\"오! 오직 당신만 탓을 받아야 한다고요, 선생?\" 매그너스 씨가 말했다. \"저—저는—이게 다 보입니다, 선생. 이제 와서 당신의 결심을 후회하시는 거군요, 그렇죠?\"\n\n\"제 결심이라니요!\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"당신의 결심 말입니다, 선생. 오! 저를 빤히 노려보지 마십시오, 선생.\" 매그너스 씨가 말했다. \"어젯밤 당신이 한 말을 기억합니다, 선생. 당신은 이리로 내려오신 거지요, 선생, 당신이 그 진실과 명예를 전적으로 신뢰했던 어떤 인물의 배신과 거짓을 폭로하러—그렇죠?\" 여기서 피터 매그너스 씨는 길게 비웃음을 흘리고는, 아마도 질투의 발작 속에서 거추장스럽게 느껴졌을 초록빛 안경을 벗어 들고, 보기에도 끔찍한 모양으로 작은 두 눈을 이리저리 굴렸다.\n\n\"그렇죠?\" 매그너스 씨가 말했다. 그러고는 한층 효과를 더해 다시 비웃었다. \"하지만 그 대가를 치르셔야 할 겁니다, 선생.\"\n\n\"무슨 대가 말입니까?\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"신경 쓰지 마십시오, 선생.\" 매그너스 씨가 방 안을 성큼성큼 오가며 대답했다. \"신경 쓰지 마십시오.\"\n\n이 '신경 쓰지 마십시오'라는 말에는 무언가 대단히 포괄적인 데가 있는 것이 틀림없으니, 우리는 거리에서든, 극장에서든, 공공장소에서든, 그 어디에서든 다툼을 목격할 때마다 이 말이 모든 호전적인 질문에 대한 표준적인 대답이 아니었던 적을 본 기억이 없다. \"당신, 자기가 신사라고 생각하시오, 선생?\"—\"신경 쓰지 마시오, 선생.\"\n\n\"내가 그 젊은 여인에게 무슨 말이라도 건넸소, 선생?\"—\"신경 쓰지 마시오, 선생.\"\n\n\"당신, 저 벽에다 머리를 처박히고 싶소, 선생?\"—\"신경 쓰지 마시오, 선생.\" 또한 주목할 만한 것은, 이 어디서나 통하는 '신경 쓰지 마시오' 속에는 무언가 숨겨진 조롱이 들어 있는 듯하여, 아무리 입에 담을 수 있는 욕설을 마구 퍼붓는다 한들 그것이 일깨울 수 있는 것보다도 더 큰 분개를 그 말을 듣는 사람의 가슴속에 불러일으킨다는 점이다.\n\n우리는 이 짧은 말이 자신에게 쓰였을 때 픽윅 씨의 영혼에, 천박한 가슴이라면 틀림없이 불러일으켰을 바로 그런 분개를 정확히 일으켰다고 주장하려는 것은 아니다. 우리는 다만 픽윅 씨가 방문을 열고 불쑥, \"터프먼, 이리 오게!\" 하고 외쳤다는 사실을 기록할 뿐이다.\n\n터프먼 씨가 자못 놀란 표정으로 곧장 나타났다.\n\n\"터프먼.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"저 숙녀분과 관련된, 다소 민감한 비밀 하나가 방금 이 신사분과 나 사이에 일어난 의견 차이의 원인일세. 내가 자네 앞에서, 이것이 저 신사분과는 아무 관련이 없으며 그의 일과는 어떤 식으로도 얽혀 있지 않다고 단언하는 마당에, 만약 저분이 계속 그것을 의심한다면, 그것은 내 진실성을 의심하는 것이니, 나는 그것을 극히 모욕적으로 여기리라는 점을 자네가 유념해 주기를 굳이 청할 필요도 없겠지.\" 픽윅 씨는 이렇게 말하면서 백과사전만큼이나 의미심장한 눈길로 피터 매그너스 씨를 바라보았다.\n\n픽윅 씨의 곧고 명예로운 태도에, 그를 그토록 두드러지게 돋보이게 하는 그 힘차고 정력적인 화술이 더해졌으니, 이성적인 사람이라면 누구든 납득시켰을 것이다. 그러나 불행히도 바로 그 순간 피터 매그너스 씨의 정신은 이성적인 상태와는 거리가 멀었다. 따라서 그는 마땅히 받아들였어야 할 픽윅 씨의 해명을 받아들이는 대신, 곧장 시뻘겋게 달아오른, 타는 듯한, 모든 것을 삼킬 듯한 격정 속으로 자신을 몰아넣으며, 자기 감정에 대한 도리가 어떻고 하는 그런 따위의 이야기를 늘어놓기 시작했다. 그러면서 이리저리 성큼성큼 거닐고 제 머리카락을 쥐어뜯는 것으로 그 열변에 힘을 보탰으니—이런 놀이는 이따금씩 픽윅 씨의 박애로운 얼굴 앞에 주먹을 흔들어 대는 것으로 변화를 주었다.\n\n이번에는 픽윅 씨가, 제 결백과 올바름을 자각하고 있으면서도, 불운하게 중년 숙녀를 이런 불쾌한 일에 끌어들이고 만 데에 화가 나서, 평소만큼 차분하게 굴지 못했다. 그 결과 말이 거칠어지고 목소리는 더욱 높아졌으며, 마침내 매그너스 씨는 픽윅 씨에게 곧 자신의 연락을 받게 될 것이라고 말했고, 이에 픽윅 씨는 칭찬할 만한 정중함으로, 그 연락이 빠를수록 좋다고 대답했다. 그러자 중년 숙녀는 겁에 질려 방에서 뛰쳐나갔고, 터프먼 씨는 거기서 픽윅 씨를 끌어내어, 피터 매그너스 씨를 홀로 명상에 잠기게 내버려 두었다.\n\n만약 그 중년 숙녀가 분주한 세상과 많이 어울려 보았거나, 법을 만들고 유행을 정하는 자들의 풍습과 관습에서 조금이라도 득을 본 적이 있었다면, 이런 종류의 사나움이야말로 세상에서 가장 무해한 것임을 알았을 것이다. 그러나 그녀는 거의 평생을 시골에서 살았고 의회 토론을 읽은 적도 없었기에, 문명 생활의 이런 특정한 세련됨에는 거의 정통하지 못했다. 따라서 그녀가 제 침실로 들어가 안에서 빗장을 지르고, 방금 목격한 장면을 곰곰이 생각하기 시작하자, 살육과 파멸의 더없이 무시무시한 그림들이 그녀의 상상 속에 떠올랐으니, 그 가운데 피터 매그너스 씨가 네 사내에게 떠메여 집으로 옮겨지고, 그의 왼쪽 옆구리에 총알 한 통이 통째로 박힌 장식이 더해진 전신 초상화 따위는 가장 약과에 속하는 것이었다. 중년 숙녀는 생각하면 할수록 더욱 겁에 질렸고, 마침내 그 고을의 으뜸 치안판사 댁으로 가서, 픽윅 씨와 터프먼 씨의 신병을 지체 없이 확보해 달라고 청하기로 결심했다.\n\n중년 숙녀가 이런 결심에 이르게 된 데에는 여러 가지 고려가 작용했는데, 그중 으뜸은 그것이 피터 매그너스 씨에 대한 자신의 헌신과 그의 안전에 대한 염려를 반박할 수 없게 증명해 주리라는 점이었다. 그녀는 그의 질투심 많은 기질을 너무도 잘 알고 있었기에, 픽윅 씨를 보고 동요한 진짜 원인을 조금이라도 내비칠 엄두를 내지 못했다. 그래서 그녀는 픽윅 씨가 치워지고 새로운 다툼이 일어날 일이 없어진다면, 자신이 그 작은 사내에게 미치는 영향력과 설득력으로 그의 떠들썩한 질투를 가라앉힐 수 있으리라 믿었다. 이런 생각들로 가득 찬 중년 숙녀는 보닛과 숄을 차려입고는, 곧장 시장의 거처로 향했다.\n\n그런데 앞서 말한 그 으뜸 치안판사인 조지 너프킨스 님은, 가장 발 빠른 보행자라도 유월 이십일일 해 뜰 녘부터 해 질 녘 사이에 찾아낼 수 있을 만큼 대단한 인물이었으니—그날은 책력에 따르면 일 년 중 가장 긴 날이라, 자연히 그를 찾는 데 가장 긴 시간을 허락해 줄 터였다. 바로 그날 아침, 너프킨스 씨는 더없이 흥분하고 격앙된 상태였으니, 고을에 반란이 일어났던 까닭이다. 가장 큰 주간 학교의 통학생들이 죄다 공모하여 눈엣가시 같은 사과 장수의 창문을 깨뜨렸고, 교구 관리에게 야유를 퍼붓고 경찰관에게 돌팔매질을 했으니—그 경찰관이란 장화를 신은 노신사로, 소동을 진압하라고 불려 나온 자였으며, 적어도 반세기 동안 어릴 때부터 줄곧 치안 관리로 지내 온 사람이었다. 그래서 너프킨스 씨는 안락의자에 앉아 위엄 있게 인상을 찌푸리며 분노로 끓어오르고 있었는데, 그때 한 숙녀가 긴급하고 사사로우며 특별한 용무로 왔다는 전갈이 전해졌다. 너프킨스 씨는 침착하면서도 무시무시한 표정을 짓고는 그 숙녀를 들이라고 명했다. 그 명령은, 황제와 치안판사와 그 밖에 이 땅의 위대한 권력자들의 모든 분부가 그러하듯, 즉각 받들어졌고, 흥미롭게 동요한 위더필드 양이 그에 따라 안으로 안내되었다.\n\n\"머즐!\" 치안판사가 말했다.\n\n머즐은 몸통은 길고 다리는 짧은, 키 작은 하인이었다.\n\n\"머즐!\"\n\n\"네, 나리.\"\n\n\"의자를 놓고 방에서 나가게.\"\n\n\"네, 나리.\"\n\n\"자, 부인, 용건을 말씀해 보시겠습니까?\" 치안판사가 말했다.\n\n\"매우 괴로운 종류의 일입니다, 나리.\" 위더필드 양이 말했다.\n\n\"그럴 법도 하지요, 부인.\" 치안판사가 말했다. \"마음을 가라앉히십시오, 부인.\" 여기서 너프킨스 씨는 인자한 표정을 지었다. \"그러고 나서 어떤 법적인 용무로 여기 오셨는지 말씀해 주시지요, 부인.\" 여기서 치안판사는 인간적인 면을 누르고 직무자로서 의기양양해져, 다시 근엄한 표정을 지었다.\n\n\"이 정보를 알려 드리는 것이 저로서는 매우 괴롭습니다, 나리.\" 위더필드 양이 말했다. \"하지만 여기서 결투가 벌어질 것 같아 두렵습니다.\"\n\n\"여기서요, 부인?\" 치안판사가 말했다. \"어디서 말입니까, 부인?\"\n\n\"입스위치에서요.\"\n\n\"입스위치에서라니요, 부인! 입스위치에서 결투라니!\" 치안판사가 그 생각에 완전히 아연실색하여 말했다. \"있을 수 없는 일입니다, 부인. 이 고을에서는 그런 일이 도모될 수 없다고 저는 확신합니다. 맙소사, 부인, 우리 지방 치안 당국의 활약상을 알고 계십니까? 혹시 들으신 적이 있습니까, 부인, 제가 지난 오월 사일에, 단 예순 명의 특별 순경만을 거느리고 권투 시합장으로 뛰어들어, 격분한 군중의 성난 격정에 희생될 위험을 무릅쓰고, 미들섹스 덤플링과 서퍽 밴텀 사이의 권투 시합을 금지시켰다는 것을요? 입스위치에서 결투라니요, 부인? 저는 생각지 않습니다—저는 도무지 생각할 수 없습니다.\" 치안판사가 혼자 따져 보며 말했다. \"이 고을에서 어떤 두 사람이 그런 치안 문란을 꾸밀 만큼 대담했으리라고는요.\"\n\n\"제 정보는, 안타깝게도, 지나치리만큼 정확합니다.\" 중년 숙녀가 말했다. \"제가 그 다툼 자리에 있었습니다.\"\n\n\"참으로 기이한 일이로군요.\" 깜짝 놀란 치안판사가 말했다. \"머즐!\"\n\n\"네, 나리.\"\n\n\"징크스 씨를 당장 이리로 보내게! 즉시.\"\n\n\"네, 나리.\"\n\n머즐이 물러갔고, 창백하고 코가 뾰족하며 영양 상태가 부실하고 초라한 옷차림을 한 중년의 서기가 방으로 들어왔다.\n\n\"징크스 씨.\" 치안판사가 말했다. \"징크스 씨.\"\n\n\"네, 나리.\" 징크스 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"이 부인께서, 징크스 씨, 이 고을에서 계획된 결투에 관한 정보를 알리러 오셨네.\"\n\n징크스 씨는 정확히 어떻게 해야 할지 몰라, 아랫사람다운 미소를 지었다.\n\n\"무엇이 우습소, 징크스 씨?\" 치안판사가 말했다.\n\n징크스 씨는 즉시 진지한 표정을 지었다.\n\n\"징크스 씨.\" 치안판사가 말했다. \"자네는 바보일세.\"\n\n징크스 씨는 그 위대한 인물을 겸손하게 바라보며, 펜 끝을 깨물었다.\n\n\"이 정보에서 자네는 무언가 아주 우스운 점을 볼지도 모르지, 선생—하지만 내가 말해 두건대, 징크스 씨, 자네에게는 웃을 거리가 거의 없네.\" 치안판사가 말했다.\n\n굶주린 듯한 징크스는, 자신에게 즐거워할 거리가 정말이지 거의 없다는 사실을 잘 알고 있다는 듯 한숨을 내쉬었다. 그러고는 숙녀의 정보를 받아 적으라는 명을 받자, 비척비척 자리로 가서 그것을 받아 적기 시작했다.\n\n\"제가 이해하기로는, 이 픽윅이라는 자가 주범이지요?\" 진술이 끝나자 치안판사가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇습니다.\" 중년 숙녀가 말했다.\n\n\"그리고 다른 폭도는—그자 이름이 뭐였소, 징크스 씨?\"\n\n\"터프먼입니다, 나리.\"\n\n\"터프먼이 입회인인가?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"또 다른 주범은, 말씀하시기를, 달아났다고요, 부인?\"\n\n\"네.\" 위더필드 양이 짧게 헛기침을 하며 대답했다.\n\n\"좋습니다.\" 치안판사가 말했다. \"이자들은 런던에서 온 두 명의 살인배로, 수도에서 이만큼 떨어진 곳이라면 법의 팔이 약하고 마비되어 있으리라 여기고, 폐하의 백성을 해치러 이리로 내려온 것이오. 본보기로 삼아 주겠소. 영장을 작성하게, 징크스 씨. 머즐!\"\n\n\"네, 나리.\"\n\n\"그러머는 아래층에 있는가?\"\n\n\"네, 나리.\"\n\n\"그를 올려 보내게.\"\n\n굽실거리는 머즐이 물러갔다가, 이내 돌아오며 장화를 신은 그 노신사를 안내해 들였는데, 그자는 무엇보다도 술병 모양의 코, 쉰 목소리, 코담배 빛깔의 외투, 그리고 두리번거리는 눈으로 두드러지는 인물이었다.\n\n\"그러머.\" 치안판사가 말했다.\n\n\"넵, 나리.\"\n\n\"고을은 이제 잠잠해졌는가?\"\n\n\"꽤 잠잠합니다요, 나리.\" 그러머가 대답했다. \"민중의 감정이 어느 정도 가라앉았는데요, 사내애들이 크리켓 하러 흩어진 덕분입죠.\"\n\n\"이런 시대엔 단호한 조치만이 통하네, 그러머.\" 치안판사가 단호한 태도로 말했다. \"국왕의 관리들의 권위가 무시당한다면, 우리는 폭동 진압령을 낭독해야 하네. 만약 민간 권력이 이 창문들을 보호하지 못한다면, 그러머, 군대가 민간 권력을 보호해야 하고, 창문들도 보호해야 하네. 그것이 헌법의 금언이라고 보는데, 징크스 씨?\"\n\n\"물론입니다, 나리.\" 징크스가 말했다.\n\n\"아주 좋아.\" 치안판사가 영장에 서명하며 말했다. \"그러머, 오늘 오후에 이자들을 내 앞으로 데려오게. 그레이트 화이트 호스에 가면 그들을 찾을 수 있을 걸세. 미들섹스 덤플링과 서퍽 밴텀 사건을 기억하지, 그러머?\"\n\n그러머 씨는 회상하듯 고개를 가로저어, 그 일을 결코 잊지 못하리라는 뜻을 내비쳤으니—사실 그 일이 날마다 거론되는 한, 그가 잊을 리도 없었다.\n\n\"이번 일은 그보다 한층 더 헌법에 어긋나는 일이네.\" 치안판사가 말했다. \"이건 한층 더 큰 치안 문란이고, 폐하의 대권을 한층 더 심하게 침해하는 일일세. 결투란 폐하의 가장 의심할 바 없는 대권 가운데 하나라고 보는데, 징크스 씨?\"\n\n\"마그나 카르타에 명시적으로 규정되어 있습니다, 나리.\" 징크스 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"영국 왕관의 가장 빛나는 보석 가운데 하나로, 귀족들이 폐하로부터 쟁취해 낸 것이라고 보는데, 징크스 씨?\" 치안판사가 말했다.\n\n\"바로 그렇습니다, 나리.\" 징크스 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"아주 좋아.\" 치안판사가 자랑스럽게 몸을 곧추세우며 말했다. \"그것이 짐의 영토 이 부분에서 침해당하는 일은 없을 것이다. 그러머, 인력을 구해서, 되도록 지체 없이 이 영장들을 집행하게. 머즐!\"\n\n\"네, 나리.\"\n\n\"숙녀분을 배웅해 드리게.\"\n\n위더필드 양은 치안판사의 학식과 박식함에 깊은 감명을 받고 물러갔다. 너프킨스 씨는 점심을 들러 물러갔다. 징크스 씨는 제 안으로 물러갔으니—낮 동안에는 집주인의 식구들이 차지하는 작은 응접실의 소파 겸 침대를 제외하면, 그것이 그가 가진 유일한 안식처였다. 그리고 그러머 씨는, 그날 아침 자신과 폐하의 또 다른 대리인—교구 관리—에게 가해진 모욕을, 이번 임무를 수행하는 방식으로 씻어 내고자 물러갔다.\n\n국왕의 치안 보전을 위한 이 결연하고 단호한 준비가 진행되는 동안, 픽윅 씨와 그의 친구들은, 진행 중인 이 엄청난 사건들을 전혀 알지 못한 채, 조용히 만찬 자리에 앉아 있었다. 그리고 그들은 모두 매우 이야기가 많고 사이좋게 어울렸다. 픽윅 씨가 마침 전날 밤의 모험담을 들려주어 추종자들, 특히 터프먼 씨를 크게 즐겁게 해 주고 있는데, 그때 문이 열리며 다소 험상궂은 얼굴 하나가 방 안을 들여다보았다. 그 험상궂은 얼굴의 두 눈은 몇 초 동안 픽윅 씨를 매우 진지하게 바라보았고, 겉보기에는 그 조사에 만족한 듯했다. 그 험상궂은 얼굴이 달려 있는 몸뚱이가 천천히 방 안으로 들어와, 장화를 신은 한 노인의 형상을 드러냈기 때문이다—독자를 더 이상 마음 졸이게 하지 않자면, 요컨대 그 두 눈은 그러머 씨의 두리번거리는 눈이었고, 그 몸뚱이는 바로 그 신사의 몸뚱이였다.\n\n그러머 씨의 일 처리 방식은 직업적이었으나, 별난 데가 있었다. 그의 첫 번째 행동은 안에서 문에 빗장을 지르는 것이었고, 두 번째는 면 손수건으로 자신의 머리와 얼굴을 매우 정성스레 닦는 것이었으며, 세 번째는 모자를 그 안에 면 손수건을 넣은 채 가장 가까운 의자에 놓는 것이었고, 네 번째는 외투 가슴 주머니에서 놋쇠 왕관이 얹힌 짧은 곤봉을 꺼내, 그것으로 엄숙하고 유령 같은 태도로 픽윅 씨에게 까딱 손짓하는 것이었다.\n\n놀란 침묵을 처음 깬 것은 스노드그래스 씨였다. 그는 잠깐 동안 그러머 씨를 빤히 바라보다가, 힘주어 말했다. \"여기는 사적인 방입니다, 선생. 사적인 방이라고요.\"\n\n그러머 씨는 고개를 가로저으며 대답했다. \"일단 길가 문턱을 넘고 나면 폐하에게는 사적인 방이란 없소. 그게 법이오. 어떤 사람들은 영국인의 집은 그의 성채라고 우기지. 그건 헛소리요.\"\n\n픽윅 회원들은 놀란 눈으로 서로를 바라보았다.\n\n\"누가 터프먼 씨요?\" 그러머 씨가 물었다. 그는 픽윅 씨에 대해서는 직관적인 감이 있어, 단번에 그를 알아보았다.\n\n\"내가 터프먼이오.\" 그 신사가 말했다.\n\n\"내 이름은 법이오.\" 그러머 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"뭐라고요?\" 터프먼 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"법이오.\" 그러머 씨가 대답했다. \"법, 민간 권력, 그리고 집행권. 그게 내 직함이오. 여기 내 권한이 있고. 백지 터프먼, 백지 픽윅—우리의 수난하시는 군주 폐하의 평안을 거스른 죄로—해당 사건에 대해 제정되고 규정된 법령에 따라—모두 정식이오. 픽윅, 그대를 체포하오! 터프먼—앞서 말한 자도.\"\n\n\"이 무례함은 대체 무슨 뜻이오?\" 터프먼 씨가 벌떡 일어서며 말했다. \"방에서 나가시오!\"\n\n\"여보게.\" 그러머 씨가 아주 잽싸게 문 쪽으로 물러나 문을 한두 치쯤 열며 말했다. \"더블리.\"\n\n\"네.\" 복도에서 굵은 목소리가 들려왔다.\n\n\"앞으로 나오게, 더블리.\"\n\n그 명령 한마디에, 키가 육 척이 조금 넘고 그에 걸맞게 몸집이 우람한, 얼굴이 더러운 사내가 반쯤 열린 문 사이로 몸을 비집고 들어와(그 과정에서 얼굴이 시뻘게진 채), 방으로 들어섰다.\n\n\"나머지 특별 순경들은 밖에 있는가, 더블리?\" 그러머 씨가 물었다.\n\n말수가 적은 사내인 더블리 씨는 고개를 끄덕여 그렇다고 했다.\n\n\"자네 휘하의 분대를 들여보내게, 더블리.\" 그러머 씨가 말했다.\n\n더블리 씨가 시키는 대로 했고, 저마다 짧은 곤봉과 놋쇠 왕관을 든 사내 여섯 명이 우르르 방으로 몰려 들어왔다. 그러머 씨는 제 곤봉을 주머니에 넣고 더블리 씨를 바라보았다. 더블리 씨는 제 곤봉을 주머니에 넣고 분대를 바라보았다. 분대는 저마다 곤봉을 주머니에 넣고 터프먼 씨와 픽윅 씨를 바라보았다.\n\n픽윅 씨와 그의 추종자들은 한 사람처럼 일제히 일어섰다.\n\n\"내 사생활에 대한 이 흉악한 침입은 무슨 뜻이오?\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"누가 감히 나를 체포한단 말이오?\" 터프먼 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"여기서 무엇을 원하는 거요, 이 악당들아?\" 스노드그래스 씨가 말했다.\n\n윈클 씨는 아무 말도 하지 않았으나, 두 눈을 그러머에게 고정시키고는 그에게 한 눈길을 던졌으니, 만약 그자에게 조금이라도 감각이 있었다면 그 눈길은 틀림없이 그의 뇌를 꿰뚫었을 것이다. 그러나 실상은, 그것이 그자에게 눈에 띄는 영향이라곤 전혀 미치지 못했다.\n\n집행관들은 픽윅 씨와 그의 친구들이 법의 권위에 저항할 기색임을 알아채자, 매우 의미심장하게 외투 소맷자락을 걷어 올렸으니, 마치 우선 그들을 때려눕히고 나중에 거두어들이는 일이란, 그저 떠올리기만 하면 당연히 해치울 수 있는 단순한 직업적 행위라는 듯했다. 이 시위는 픽윅 씨에게 헛되지 않았다. 그는 잠시 터프먼 씨와 따로 의논한 뒤, 시장의 거처로 가겠다는 뜻을 밝혔는데, 다만 그 자리에 모인 자들에게, 자유의 몸이 되는 순간 영국인으로서의 자기 특권에 대한 이 괴이한 침해를 응징하리라는 것이 자신의 확고한 의도임을 유념해 두라고 청할 뿐이었다. 그러자 그 자리에 모인 자들은, 치안판사의 신성한 권리에 가해진 어떤 사소한 흠집도 용납할 수 없는 일종의 신성모독으로 여기는 듯한 그러머 씨 한 사람을 빼고는, 모두 매우 신나게 웃어 댔다.\n\n그러나 픽윅 씨가 제 나라의 법에 복종하겠다는 뜻을 밝히고, 그가 위협하던 고집에서 즐거운 소동을 기대했던 종업원들과 마부들과 객실 하녀들과 우편 마차 소년들이 막 실망하고 못마땅해하며 돌아서기 시작하던 바로 그때, 미처 예견하지 못했던 난관이 생겼다. 픽윅 씨는 구성된 당국에 대한 온갖 경의를 품으면서도, 흔한 범죄자처럼 사법 관리들에게 둘러싸여 호위받으며 공공 거리에 모습을 드러내는 것에 대해서는 단호히 항의했다. 그러머 씨는, 당시 동요된 민심 상태로 보아(반공일이라 사내애들이 아직 집에 가지 않은 까닭이었다), 길 건너편으로 걷거나 픽윅 씨가 곧장 치안판사에게 가겠다는 서약을 받는 것에 대해 마찬가지로 단호히 항의했다. 그리고 픽윅 씨와 터프먼 씨는 둘 다, 구할 수 있는 유일한 점잖은 탈것인 역마차의 비용에 대해 한사코 반대했다. 논쟁은 격해지고 진퇴양난은 오래 끌었다. 그리고 집행관들이 픽윅 씨를 그냥 떠메어 치안판사에게 데려가는 진부한 방편으로 그의 도보 항의를 막 꺾으려던 참에, 여관 마당에 낡은 가마 하나가 서 있다는 것이 떠올랐으니, 원래 공채를 가진 통풍 환자 신사를 위해 지어진 것이라, 적어도 요즘의 역마차만큼은 편안하게 픽윅 씨와 터프먼 씨를 태울 수 있을 터였다. 가마를 빌려 현관 홀로 들여왔다. 픽윅 씨와 터프먼 씨가 그 안으로 몸을 비집고 들어가 가리개를 내렸다. 가마꾼 두 명이 곧 구해졌고, 행렬은 당당한 격식을 갖추어 출발했다. 특별 순경들은 가마의 몸체를 에워쌌고, 그러머 씨와 더블리 씨는 의기양양하게 앞장서 행진했으며, 스노드그래스 씨와 윈클 씨는 팔짱을 끼고 뒤따랐고, 입스위치의 씻지 않은 무리가 후미를 맡았다.\n\n고을의 상인들은, 그 죄목의 성격에 대해서는 매우 막연한 관념밖에 없었으나, 이 광경에 크게 교화되고 흡족해하지 않을 수 없었다. 여기 법의 강력한 팔이, 다름 아닌 수도에서 온 두 죄인 위로 금박공 스무 명의 힘으로 내려치고 있었다. 그 강력한 기계는 그들 자신의 치안판사가 지휘하고 그들 자신의 관리들이 작동시키는 것이었으며, 두 죄인은 그 합심한 노력 덕에 가마 한 채의 비좁은 공간 속에 단단히 갇혀 있었다. 그러머 씨가 곤봉을 손에 들고 행렬을 이끌자, 그를 맞이하는 찬동과 감탄의 표현이 숱하게 쏟아졌다. 씻지 않은 무리가 지르는 함성은 크고도 길었다. 그리고 이렇게 하나로 모인 대중의 갈채 속에서, 행렬은 천천히 그리고 위풍당당하게 나아갔다.\n\n웰러 씨는, 검은 옥양목 소매가 달린 아침나절 웃옷을 입고서, 초록색 대문이 달린 그 수수께끼 같은 집을 살피러 갔다가 허탕을 치고 다소 풀이 죽은 채로 돌아오던 길에, 눈을 들어 보니 군중이 거리로 쏟아져 내려오며 어떤 물체를 에워싸고 있는데, 그것이 영락없이 가마처럼 보였다. 자신의 일이 실패한 데서 생각을 돌리고 싶었던 그는, 군중이 지나가는 것을 보려고 옆으로 비켜섰다. 그러다 그들이 저희끼리 매우 흡족해하며 환호성을 지르고 있는 것을 보자, (기운을 북돋울 셈으로) 곧장 그도 있는 힘껏 환호하기 시작했다.\n\n그러머 씨가 지나가고, 더블리 씨가 지나가고, 가마가 지나가고, 특별 순경 호위대가 지나가는 동안에도, 샘은 여전히 군중의 열광적인 환호에 화답하며, (물론 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지는 전혀 짐작도 못 한 채) 더없이 광적인 기쁨의 마지막 극한에 이른 것처럼 모자를 휘둘러 대고 있었는데, 그때 윈클 씨와 스노드그래스 씨가 뜻밖에 나타나는 바람에 그는 갑자기 멈춰 섰다.\n\n\"무슨 소동입니까, 나리들?\" 샘이 외쳤다. \"상복 입은 이 파수막 같은 데에 누구를 집어넣은 겁니까?\"\n\n두 신사가 동시에 대답했으나, 그들의 말은 소란 속에 묻혀 버렸다.\n\n\"누굽니까?\" 샘이 다시 외쳤다.\n\n또 한 번 두 사람이 함께 대답을 했는데, 비록 말소리는 들리지 않았으나, 샘은 두 쌍의 입술이 움직이는 모양을 보고 그들이 마법의 단어 '픽윅'을 내뱉었음을 알아챘다.\n\n그것으로 충분했다. 다음 순간 웰러 씨는 군중을 헤치고 나아가 가마꾼들을 멈춰 세우고, 뚱뚱한 그러머와 정면으로 맞섰다.\n\n\"이보쇼, 영감님!\" 샘이 말했다. \"이 탈것에 누굴 태운 거요?\"\n\n\"물러서라.\" 그러머 씨가 말했는데, 그의 위엄은 다른 숱한 사내들의 위엄이 그러하듯, 약간의 인기로 놀랍도록 부풀어 있었다.\n\n\"안 물러서면 때려눕혀.\" 더블리 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"내 편의를 챙겨 주셔서 대단히 고맙소, 영감님.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"그리고 거인의 순회 마차에서 막 도망쳐 나온 것 같은 저 양반한테는, 그 아주 멋들어진 제안에 더더욱 고맙고. 하지만 댁만 괜찮으시다면, 내 질문에 대답이나 좀 해 주시는 게 좋겠소.—안녕하십니까, 선생?\" 이 마지막 말은 앞쪽 창으로 내다보고 있던 픽윅 씨에게 너그럽게 거드름 피우는 투로 건넨 것이었다.\n\n분개하여 완전히 말문이 막힌 그러머 씨는, 놋쇠 왕관이 달린 곤봉을 그 특별한 주머니에서 끌어내어 샘의 눈앞에 휘둘러 보였다.\n\n\"아.\" 샘이 말했다. \"거 참 예쁘구려, 특히 그 왕관이 말이오. 진짜하고 똑 닮은 게 보통 아니구려.\"\n\n\"물러서라!\" 격분한 그러머 씨가 말했다. 그 명령에 힘을 더할 셈으로, 그는 한 손으로 그 왕권의 놋쇠 표상을 샘의 목도리에 들이밀고, 다른 손으로 샘의 멱살을 움켜쥐었다—이에 웰러 씨는 그를 즉석에서 때려눕히는 것으로 그 인사에 답했는데, 그에 앞서 더없이 세심하게도, 그가 깔고 누울 가마꾼 하나를 먼저 때려눕혀 두었다.\n\n윈클 씨가, 모욕감에서 비롯되는 그런 종류의 일시적인 광기 발작에 사로잡힌 것인지, 아니면 웰러 씨의 이 용맹스러운 시범에 고무된 것인지는 확실치 않다. 그러나 확실한 것은, 그가 그러머 씨가 쓰러지는 것을 보자마자 곁에 서 있던 한 어린 소년에게 무시무시하게 달려들었다는 것이다. 이에 스노드그래스 씨는, 참으로 그리스도교적인 정신으로, 또 누구도 불시에 덮치지 않으려고, 매우 큰 소리로 자신이 이제 시작하겠노라고 알린 다음, 더없이 신중하게 외투를 벗기 시작했다. 그는 즉시 둘러싸여 제압당했다. 그와 윈클 씨 모두를 위해 공정하게 말해 두자면, 그들은 자신들이든 웰러 씨든 구하려고 조금도 시도하지 않았다. 그 웰러 씨는, 더없이 격렬하게 저항한 끝에, 수에 눌려 제압당하고 포로가 되었다. 그러고 나서 행렬은 다시 정렬했다. 가마꾼들은 제자리로 돌아갔고, 행진이 다시 시작되었다.\n\n이 모든 과정 동안 픽윅 씨의 분개는 그 끝을 알 수 없을 지경이었다. 그는 샘이 특별 순경들을 자빠뜨리며 사방으로 날뛰는 것을 겨우 볼 수 있었는데, 그것이 그가 볼 수 있는 전부였으니, 가마 문은 열리지 않고 가리개는 올라가지 않았기 때문이다. 마침내 그는 터프먼 씨의 도움을 받아 지붕을 밀어 열어젖히는 데 성공했다. 그러고는 좌석 위에 올라서서, 그 신사의 어깨에 한 손을 짚고 되도록 몸을 가눈 채, 픽윅 씨는 군중을 향해 연설하기 시작했다. 자신이 당한 부당하기 그지없는 대우를 역설하고, 자신의 하인이 먼저 폭행당했다는 점을 유념해 달라고 그들에게 호소했다. 이런 대형으로 그들은 치안판사의 집에 다다랐으니, 가마꾼들은 종종걸음을 치고, 죄수들은 뒤따르고, 픽윅 씨는 열변을 토하고, 군중은 함성을 질렀다.\n\n웰러 씨는 끌려가는 내내 격렬하게 분개했다. 그루머 씨와 그 일행의 생김새와 태도에 대한 빈정거림이 줄을 이었고, 여기 모인 신사들 가운데 여섯쯤은 한꺼번에 덤벼도 좋다는 용맹한 도전으로 그는 자신의 불만을 토해 냈다. 스노드그래스 씨와 윈클 씨는, 그들의 지도자가 가마 안에서 쏟아 내는 웅변의 격류에 침울한 경의를 표하며 귀를 기울였는데, 그 거침없는 흐름은 가마 뚜껑을 닫게 해 달라는 터프먼 씨의 간곡한 애원으로도 한순간조차 막을 수 없었다. 그러나 행렬이, 그가 달아나던 잡 트로터와 마주쳤던 바로 그 안마당으로 꺾어 들자, 웰러 씨의 분노는 이내 호기심에 자리를 내주었다. 그리고 그 무엇보다도 중요한 그루머 씨가 가마꾼들에게 멈추라 명한 뒤, 위엄 있고 거창한 걸음걸이로, 잡 트로터가 빠져나왔던 바로 그 초록 대문 앞까지 나아가, 그 옆에 매달린 종 손잡이를 힘껏 잡아당겼을 때, 호기심은 더없이 흥겨운 경악의 감정으로 바뀌었다. 종소리에 응답하여 나온 것은 아주 맵시 있고 예쁜 얼굴의 하녀였는데, 그 여자는 죄인들의 반항적인 행색과 픽윅 씨의 격앙된 언사에 놀라 두 손을 치켜올린 뒤, 머즐 씨를 불렀다. 머즐 씨는 마차 대문의 한쪽을 열어 가마와 붙잡힌 자들과 특별 순경들을 들이고는, 곧장 그것을 군중의 면전에서 쾅 닫아 버렸다. 따돌림을 당해 분개한 데다 그다음에 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 보고 싶어 안달이 난 군중은, 그 뒤로 한두 시간 동안 대문을 발로 차고 종을 울려 대며 분풀이를 했다. 이 놀이에는 모두가 돌아가며 참여했는데, 다만 서너 명의 운 좋은 자들만은 예외였으니, 그들은 대문에서 아무것도 보이지 않는 쪽을 향한 쇠살창을 발견하고는, 길에서 개수레에 치인 술 취한 사내가 어느 약방 뒷방에서 외과 진찰을 받고 있을 때 사람들이 그 약방 앞 유리창에 코를 납작하게 들이대며 보이는 그런 지칠 줄 모르는 끈기로, 그 살창 사이로 빤히 들여다보았다.\n\n집 현관문으로 이어지는 계단 발치에서 가마가 멈추었는데, 그 문은 양옆으로 초록 통에 심긴 미국산 알로에가 지키고 있었다. 픽윅 씨와 그 친구들은 현관 홀로 안내되었고, 거기서 머즐이 미리 알린 뒤 너프킨스 씨의 지시에 따라, 그 공공심에 불타는 관리의 존엄한 면전으로 인도되었다.\n\n그 광경은 인상적이었으니, 죄인들의 가슴에 공포를 심고 법의 준엄한 위엄에 관한 적절한 관념을 새겨 넣기에 알맞게 꾸며진 것이었다. 큼직한 책장 앞, 큼직한 의자에, 큼직한 탁자 뒤, 큼직한 책을 앞에 두고 너프킨스 씨가 앉아 있었는데, 그 모두가 큼직했음에도 그는 그 가운데 어느 것보다도 한 치수쯤 더 커 보였다. 탁자에는 서류 더미가 장식처럼 쌓여 있었고, 그 저편 끝 너머로 징크스 씨의 머리와 어깨가 나타났는데, 그는 될 수 있는 대로 바빠 보이려 애쓰느라 분주했다. 일행이 모두 들어서자 머즐은 조심스레 문을 닫고, 주인의 의자 뒤에 자리를 잡고 분부를 기다렸다. 너프킨스 씨는 오싹할 만큼 엄숙하게 몸을 뒤로 젖히고는, 마지못해 끌려온 방문객들의 얼굴을 찬찬히 뜯어보았다.\n\n\"자, 그루머, 저 사람은 누구냐?\" 너프킨스 씨가 픽윅 씨를 가리키며 말했다. 픽윅 씨는 친구들의 대변자로서 모자를 손에 들고 더없이 공손하고 정중하게 허리를 굽히고 서 있었다.\n\n\"이 사람이 픽윅입니다요, 나리.\" 그루머가 말했다.\n\n\"자, 그런 소리 마쇼, 이 늙은 부싯돌 양반.\" 웰러 씨가 팔꿈치로 비집고 맨 앞줄로 나서며 끼어들었다. \"실례합니다, 나리. 그렇지만 등황색 장화를 신은 이 나리의 부하 양반은 어디 가서도 의전 담당으로는 변변한 밥벌이를 못 할 겁니다. 자, 이분이 말입니다, 나리.\" 웰러 씨는 그루머를 옆으로 밀치고, 유쾌한 허물없음으로 치안판사를 향해 말을 이었다. \"이분이 바로 S. 픽윅 님이시고, 이분이 터프먼 씨, 저분이 스노드그래스 씨, 그리고 더 가서 그 반대쪽 옆이 윈클 씨—모두 아주 훌륭한 신사분들이라, 나리께서도 알게 되시면 무척 기쁘실 겁니다. 그러니 나리께서 이 부하 양반들을 한두 달쯤 디딜방아 형틀로 빨리 보내시면 보내실수록, 우리가 유쾌하게 서로를 이해하기 시작하는 것도 그만큼 빨라질 겁니다. 일이 먼저, 즐거움은 나중이라고, 리처드 3세께서 갓난애들 숨통을 막기 전에 탑에서 다른 임금을 찔러 죽이며 하신 말씀처럼 말입죠.\"\n\n이 연설을 끝맺으며 웰러 씨는 오른쪽 팔꿈치로 모자를 쓱 닦고는, 처음부터 끝까지 형언할 수 없는 경외감에 차서 그의 말을 들었던 징크스에게 인자하게 고개를 끄덕여 보였다.\n\n\"이 사람은 누구냐, 그루머?\" 치안판사가 말했다.\n\n\"아주 위험천만한 자입니다요, 나리.\" 그루머가 대답했다. \"죄인들을 빼내려고 시도하고 관리들을 폭행했습니다요. 그래서 저희가 신병을 확보하여 여기로 데려왔습니다요.\"\n\n\"잘했다.\" 치안판사가 대답했다. \"딱 보아도 위험한 불한당이로군.\"\n\n\"이 사람은 제 하인입니다, 나리.\" 픽윅 씨가 화가 나서 말했다.\n\n\"오! 이자가 당신 하인이라, 그렇소?\" 너프킨스 씨가 말했다. \"정의의 목적을 좌절시키고 그 관리들을 살해하려는 음모로군. 픽윅의 하인이라. 그것을 적어 두시오, 징크스 씨.\"\n\n징크스 씨가 그대로 적었다.\n\n\"이름이 뭐냐, 이놈아?\" 너프킨스 씨가 우레같이 호통쳤다.\n\n\"웰러요.\" 샘이 대답했다.\n\n\"뉴게이트 죄수 명부에 딱 어울리는 이름이로군.\" 너프킨스 씨가 말했다.\n\n이것은 농담이었다. 그래서 징크스, 그루머, 더블리, 모든 특별 순경들, 그리고 머즐이 오 분 동안이나 폭소를 터뜨렸다.\n\n\"이름을 적으시오, 징크스 씨.\" 치안판사가 말했다.\n\n\"L이 두 개요, 영감.\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n여기서 운 나쁜 특별 순경 하나가 또 웃자, 치안판사가 당장 그를 구류시키겠노라 으름장을 놓았다. 이런 경우에는 엉뚱한 사람을 보고 웃는 것이 위험한 일이다.\n\n\"어디에 사느냐?\" 치안판사가 말했다.\n\n\"살 수 있는 데면 어디든요.\" 샘이 대답했다.\n\n\"그것을 적으시오, 징크스 씨.\" 점점 분노가 치미는 치안판사가 말했다.\n\n\"아래에 죽 그어 두쇼.\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n\"이자는 부랑자요, 징크스 씨.\" 치안판사가 말했다. \"제 입으로 한 진술로 보아도 부랑자란 말이오—그렇지 않소, 징크스 씨?\"\n\n\"분명히 그렇습니다, 나리.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 이자를 구류시키겠다—그런 자로서 구류시키겠다.\" 너프킨스 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"여긴 정의에 관한 한 아주 공평한 나라로구먼.\" 샘이 말했다. \"남을 구류시키는 만큼 제 몸도 두 배로 망신시키지 않는 치안판사가 없으니 말이야.\"\n\n이 익살에 또 다른 특별 순경이 웃었다가, 무섭도록 엄숙한 표정을 지으려 애썼는데, 그 바람에 치안판사가 곧바로 그를 적발해 냈다.\n\n\"그루머.\" 너프킨스 씨가 분노로 얼굴이 벌게져서 말했다. \"어찌 감히 저런 무능하고 평판 나쁜 자를 특별 순경으로 골라 뽑았느냐? 어찌 감히 그런 짓을 했느냐, 이놈아?\"\n\n\"대단히 죄송합니다요, 나리.\" 그루머가 더듬거렸다.\n\n\"대단히 죄송하다고!\" 격분한 치안판사가 말했다. \"이 직무 태만을 후회하게 될 것이다, 그루머 씨. 본보기로 삼겠다. 저자의 봉을 빼앗아라. 저자는 취했다. 너 취했구나, 이놈아.\"\n\n\"저는 취하지 않았습니다요, 나리.\" 그 사내가 말했다.\n\n\"너는 취했다.\" 치안판사가 받아쳤다. \"내가 취했다는데 어찌 감히 취하지 않았다고 말하느냐, 이놈아? 저자한테 술 냄새가 나지 않느냐, 그루머?\"\n\n\"지독하게 납니다요, 나리.\" 어딘가에서 럼주 냄새가 난다는 어렴풋한 인상을 받은 그루머가 대답했다.\n\n\"그럴 줄 알았다.\" 너프킨스 씨가 말했다. \"저자가 방에 처음 들어왔을 때 흥분한 눈을 보고 취한 줄 알았지. 저자의 흥분한 눈을 보았소, 징크스 씨?\"\n\n\"분명히 보았습니다, 나리.\"\n\n\"저는 오늘 아침 술이라곤 한 방울도 입에 안 댔습니다요.\" 더할 나위 없이 말짱하게 정신이 든 그 사내가 말했다.\n\n\"어찌 감히 내게 거짓말을 하느냐?\" 너프킨스 씨가 말했다. \"이자가 지금 이 순간 취해 있지 않소, 징크스 씨?\"\n\n\"분명히 그렇습니다, 나리.\" 징크스가 대답했다.\n\n\"징크스 씨.\" 치안판사가 말했다. \"저자를 모욕죄로 구류시키겠소. 구류장을 작성하시오, 징크스 씨.\"\n\n그래서 그 특별 순경은 구류될 뻔했으나, 다만 치안판사의 자문역인 징크스가(시골 변호사 사무소에서 삼 년간 법률 교육을 받은 적이 있었다) 그래서는 안 될 것 같다고 치안판사에게 속삭인 덕에 모면했다. 그리하여 치안판사는 일장 연설을 늘어놓으며, 그 특별 순경의 가족을 헤아려 그저 견책하고 방면하겠노라 말했다. 따라서 그 특별 순경은 십오 분 동안 격렬하게 욕을 먹고는 제 일을 보러 쫓겨났고, 그루머, 더블리, 머즐, 그리고 다른 모든 특별 순경들은 너프킨스 씨의 도량에 감탄하여 웅성거렸다.\n\n\"자, 징크스 씨.\" 치안판사가 말했다. \"그루머에게 선서를 시키시오.\"\n\n그루머는 즉시 선서를 했다. 그러나 그루머가 이야기를 종잡지 못하고 헤매는 데다 너프킨스 씨의 만찬이 거의 준비된 터라, 너프킨스 씨는 그루머에게 유도 질문을 던져 일을 빨리 매듭지었고, 그루머는 할 수 있는 한 긍정에 가깝게 대답했다. 그리하여 심문은 아주 매끄럽고 편안하게 진행되어, 웰러 씨에게는 두 건의 폭행이, 윈클 씨에게는 협박이, 스노드그래스 씨에게는 밀친 행위가 입증되었다. 이 모든 일이 치안판사가 만족할 만큼 마무리되자, 치안판사와 징크스 씨는 속삭이며 의논했다.\n\n그 의논이 십 분쯤 이어진 뒤, 징크스 씨는 탁자 끝 자기 자리로 물러났다. 그리고 치안판사가 예비 삼아 헛기침을 하고 의자에 몸을 곧추세우며 일장 연설을 시작하려는 참에, 픽윅 씨가 끼어들었다.\n\n\"말씀을 끊어 죄송합니다, 나리.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"그렇지만 여기서 이루어진 진술들에 대해 나리께서 형성하신 어떤 의견을 표명하고 그에 따라 처분하시기 전에, 저는 제 개인에 관련된 한 발언할 권리가 있음을 주장하지 않을 수 없습니다.\"\n\n\"입 다무시오.\" 치안판사가 단호하게 말했다.\n\n\"제가 나리께 말씀드려야 할 것은—\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"입 다무시오.\" 치안판사가 끼어들었다. \"안 그러면 관리를 시켜 당신을 끌어내게 하겠소.\"\n\n\"나리께서 좋으실 대로 관리들에게 무슨 일이든 명하셔도 좋습니다, 나리.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"그리고 그들 사이에 유지되는 복종의 표본을 제가 이미 보았으니, 나리께서 무엇을 명하시든 그들이 실행하리라는 것은 의심치 않습니다. 그러나 저는 외람되나마, 나리, 힘으로 끌려 나가기 전까지는 발언할 권리를 주장하겠습니다.\"\n\n\"픽윅과 원칙이다!\" 웰러 씨가 아주 또렷한 목소리로 외쳤다.\n\n\"샘, 조용히 해라.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"구멍 뚫린 북처럼 벙어리가 되겠습니다, 나리.\" 샘이 대답했다.\n\n너프킨스 씨는 픽윅 씨가 이토록 유례없는 대담함을 드러내는 데 강렬한 경악의 눈빛으로 그를 바라보았다. 그리고 막 몹시 화난 대답을 돌려주려는 듯했는데, 그때 징크스 씨가 그의 소매를 잡아당기며 귀에 무언가를 속삭였다. 이에 치안판사는 반쯤 들릴락 말락 한 대답을 했고, 그러자 속삭임이 다시 이어졌다. 징크스가 항변하고 있는 것이 분명했다.\n\n마침내 치안판사는 더 듣고 싶지 않은 마음을 아주 마지못한 태도로 꿀꺽 삼키고는, 픽윅 씨를 향해 날카롭게 말했다. \"무슨 말이 하고 싶소?\"\n\n\"먼저,\" 픽윅 씨가 안경 너머로 한 줄기 시선을 보내며 말했는데, 그 앞에서는 너프킨스마저 움찔했다. \"먼저, 저와 제 친구가 무슨 이유로 여기 끌려왔는지 알고 싶습니다.\"\n\n\"이자에게 말해 주어야 하오?\" 치안판사가 징크스에게 속삭였다.\n\n\"말씀하시는 편이 나을 듯합니다, 나리.\" 징크스가 치안판사에게 속삭였다.\n\n\"내 앞에서 선서된 고발이 있었소.\" 치안판사가 말했다. \"당신이 결투를 하려 한다는 혐의가 있고, 다른 사람, 터프먼이 그 일에서 당신의 조력자이자 교사자라는 것이오. 따라서—응, 징크스 씨?\"\n\n\"분명히 그렇습니다, 나리.\"\n\n\"따라서 나는 당신네 두 사람에게 명하노니—그게 절차이지요, 징크스 씨?\"\n\n\"분명히 그렇습니다, 나리.\"\n\n\"명하노니—명하노니—무엇이오, 징크스 씨?\" 치안판사가 짜증스럽게 말했다.\n\n\"보증인을 세우라는 것입니다, 나리.\"\n\n\"그렇지. 따라서 나는 당신네 두 사람에게 명하노니—내 서기가 끼어들기 전에 막 말하려던 대로—보증인을 세우시오.\"\n\n\"건실한 보증인입니다.\" 징크스 씨가 속삭였다.\n\n\"건실한 보증인을 요구하겠소.\" 치안판사가 말했다.\n\n\"이 고장 사람이어야 합니다.\" 징크스가 속삭였다.\n\n\"이 고장 사람이어야 하오.\" 치안판사가 말했다.\n\n\"각각 오십 파운드씩입니다.\" 징크스가 속삭였다. \"물론 가구주여야 하고요.\"\n\n\"각각 오십 파운드의 보증인 두 사람을 요구하겠소.\" 치안판사가 큰 위엄을 담아 소리 내어 말했다. \"그리고 물론 가구주여야 하오.\"\n\n\"하지만 맙소사, 나리.\" 터프먼 씨와 더불어 온통 경악과 분개에 휩싸인 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"우리는 이 고장에서 완전히 낯선 사람들입니다. 여기 어느 가구주에 대해서도 아는 바가 없는 것이, 누구와 결투할 생각이 없는 것과 똑같습니다.\"\n\n\"그렇겠지요.\" 치안판사가 대답했다. \"그렇겠지요—그렇지 않소, 징크스 씨?\"\n\n\"분명히 그렇습니다, 나리.\"\n\n\"더 할 말이 있소?\" 치안판사가 물었다.\n\n픽윅 씨는 할 말이 훨씬 더 많았고, 의심할 바 없이 그 말을 했을 것이며, 그것은 자기 자신에게 거의 득이 되지 않았을뿐더러 치안판사를 만족시키지도 못했을 것이다. 다만 그가 말을 멈춘 순간 웰러 씨가 그의 소매를 잡아당겼고, 그가 곧장 너무도 진지한 대화에 빠져든 나머지 치안판사의 질문을 전혀 알아채지 못한 채 흘려보냈기에 그렇지 않았다. 너프킨스 씨는 그런 종류의 질문을 두 번 되풀이할 사람이 아니었다. 그래서 그는 또 한 번 예비 삼아 헛기침을 하고는, 순경들의 경건하고 감탄에 찬 침묵 가운데서 자신의 판결을 선고하기 시작했다.\n\n그는 웰러에게 첫 번째 폭행에 대해 이 파운드, 두 번째 폭행에 대해 삼 파운드의 벌금을 부과하겠다고 했다. 윈클에게는 이 파운드, 스노드그래스에게는 일 파운드의 벌금을 부과하고, 아울러 그들에게 폐하의 모든 백성, 특히 그의 충실한 종 대니얼 그루머에 대해 평화를 지키겠다는 서약서를 작성하도록 요구하겠다고 했다. 픽윅과 터프먼은 이미 보증 처분에 부쳤다.\n\n치안판사가 말을 마치자마자, 픽윅 씨는 다시 온화해진 얼굴에 미소를 가득 띠고 앞으로 나서서 말했다.\n\n\"치안판사님께 송구하오나, 나리 자신에게 대단히 중요한 일에 관하여 잠시 단둘이 이야기를 나눌 수 있도록 청해도 되겠습니까?\"\n\n\"뭐라고?\" 치안판사가 말했다. 픽윅 씨가 청을 되풀이했다.\n\n\"이것은 참으로 별난 요청이로군.\" 치안판사가 말했다. \"단둘이 면담하자고?\"\n\n\"단둘이 면담하자는 것입니다.\" 픽윅 씨가 단호히 대답했다. \"다만 제가 전하려는 정보의 일부가 제 하인에게서 나온 것이라, 그가 함께 있었으면 합니다.\"\n\n치안판사는 징크스 씨를 보았고, 징크스 씨는 치안판사를 보았으며, 관리들은 어리둥절하여 서로를 마주 보았다. 너프킨스 씨가 갑자기 창백해졌다. 저 웰러라는 자가 후회의 순간에, 자신을 암살하려는 어떤 은밀한 음모를 폭로하려는 것일까? 그것은 무시무시한 생각이었다. 그는 공인이었다. 그리고 율리우스 카이사르와 퍼시벌 씨를 떠올리며 그는 더욱 창백해졌다.\n\n치안판사는 다시 픽윅 씨를 보고는 징크스 씨를 손짓해 불렀다.\n\n\"이 요청을 어떻게 생각하오, 징크스 씨?\" 너프킨스 씨가 중얼거렸다.\n\n이를 정확히 어떻게 생각해야 할지 알 수 없었고 행여 비위를 거스를까 두려웠던 징크스 씨는, 어정쩡한 투로 힘없이 미소를 짓고는 입꼬리를 비틀며 천천히 고개를 좌우로 저었다.\n\n\"징크스 씨.\" 치안판사가 근엄하게 말했다. \"당신은 멍청이요.\"\n\n이 짤막한 견해 표명에 징크스 씨는 다시 미소를 지었는데—아까보다도 한층 더 힘없이—그러고는 조금씩 슬금슬금 제 구석 자리로 물러났다.\n\n너프킨스 씨는 몇 초 동안 속으로 그 문제를 따져 본 뒤, 의자에서 일어나 픽윅 씨와 샘에게 따라오라 청하고는, 재판정 응접실로 통하는 작은 방으로 앞장서 갔다. 픽윅 씨에게 그 작은 방의 안쪽 끝으로 가라 이르고, 조금이라도 적대적인 기색이 드러나는 경우 즉시 도망칠 수 있도록 반쯤 닫힌 문에 손을 얹은 채, 너프킨스 씨는 그 전갈이 무엇이든 들을 준비가 되어 있노라 밝혔다.\n\n\"단도직입으로 말씀드리겠습니다, 나리.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"이것은 나리 자신과 나리의 신용에 중대하게 관련된 일입니다. 저로서는 나리께서 댁에 어느 지독한 사기꾼을 숨겨 두고 계시다고 믿을 만한 온갖 이유가 있습니다, 나리!\"\n\n\"둘입니다.\" 샘이 끼어들었다. \"눈물과 악행으로 치면 천하무적인 멀버리 양반이죠!\"\n\n\"샘.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"내가 이 신사분께 내 말을 알아듣게 하려면, 네가 감정을 좀 다스려 주어야겠다.\"\n\n\"대단히 죄송합니다, 나리.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다. \"하지만 저 잡이라는 놈을 생각하면, 밸브를 한두 치 열지 않고는 못 배기겠습니다.\"\n\n\"한마디로 말씀드리겠습니다, 나리.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"어떤 피츠마셜 대위라는 자가 이곳에 드나드는 버릇이 있다고 의심하는 제 하인의 말이 옳습니까? 왜냐하면,\" 픽윅 씨는 너프킨스 씨가 막 몹시 분개하여 끼어들려는 것을 보고 덧붙였다. \"왜냐하면 그렇다면, 저는 그 사람이—\"\n\n\"쉿, 쉿.\" 너프킨스 씨가 문을 닫으며 말했다. \"그 사람이 무엇임을 안다는 거요?\"\n\n\"파렴치한 협잡꾼—명예라곤 없는 자—사회를 등쳐 먹으며 잘 속아 넘어가는 사람들을 제 봉으로 삼는 자입니다, 나리. 그 어이없고, 어리석고, 가련한 봉들 말입니다, 나리.\" 흥분한 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"이런 세상에.\" 너프킨스 씨가 얼굴이 새빨개지며 태도를 단번에 바꾸어 말했다. \"이런 세상에, 저—\"\n\n\"픽윅입니다.\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n\"픽윅 씨.\" 치안판사가 말했다. \"이런 세상에, 픽윅 씨—부디 앉으시오—설마 그럴 리가? 피츠마셜 대위가!\"\n\n\"대위라고 부르지 마쇼.\" 샘이 말했다. \"피츠마셜도 아니고요. 그자는 이것도 저것도 아닙니다. 그자는 떠돌이 배우고, 이름은 징글입니다. 그리고 멀버리 양복을 입은 늑대가 세상에 있었다면, 그 잡 트로터라는 놈이 바로 그놈입니다.\"\n\n\"정말입니다, 나리.\" 픽윅 씨가 치안판사의 경악한 표정에 응하여 말했다. \"제가 이 고장에 온 유일한 용무는, 지금 우리가 말하는 그 인물의 정체를 폭로하는 것입니다.\"\n\n픽윅 씨는 너프킨스 씨의 경악한 귀에다 징글 씨의 온갖 악행을 추려 들려주기 시작했다. 처음에 그를 어떻게 만났는지, 그가 워들 양과 어떻게 사랑의 도피를 했는지, 금전적 대가를 받고 어떻게 흔쾌히 그 숙녀를 포기했는지, 한밤중에 어느 숙녀 기숙학교로 어떻게 자신을 끌어들였는지, 그리고 그(픽윅 씨)가 이제 그자가 지금의 이름과 신분을 사칭하는 것을 폭로하는 일을 자신의 의무로 여긴다는 것을 그는 이야기했다.\n\n이야기가 진행됨에 따라, 너프킨스 씨의 몸속에 흐르는 뜨거운 피가 죄다 귓불 끝까지 화끈하게 솟아올랐다. 그는 이웃 경마장에서 그 대위를 주워 온 터였다. 그의 장황한 귀족 지인 명단과 광범위한 여행 경력, 세련된 태도에 매료되어, 너프킨스 부인과 너프킨스 양은 피츠마셜 대위를 전시하고 다녔고, 피츠마셜 대위를 입에 올렸으며, 자기네가 엄선한 지인들의 충실한 머리 위로 피츠마셜 대위를 내던지고 다녀, 결국 그들의 절친한 벗인 포컨엄 부인과 포컨엄 자매, 그리고 시드니 포컨엄 씨가 질투와 절망으로 터질 지경에 이르렀다. 그런데 이제 와서, 결국 그자가 빈털터리 협잡꾼이요, 떠돌이 배우요, 사기꾼이 아니라 해도 그와 너무도 비슷해서 차이를 가려내기 어려운 자라는 말을 듣다니! 맙소사! 포컨엄 일가가 뭐라고 할까! 자기 청혼이 그런 연적 때문에 외면당했음을 알게 된다면 시드니 포컨엄 씨가 얼마나 의기양양해할까! 그, 너프킨스가 다음 분기 순회재판에서 늙은 포컨엄의 눈을 어떻게 마주한단 말인가! 그리고 이 이야기가 새어 나가면 반대편 치안판사 일파에게 얼마나 좋은 빌미가 될 것인가!\n\n\"하지만 결국에는,\" 한참 말이 없다가 잠시 얼굴이 환해지며 너프킨스 씨가 말했다. \"결국에는, 이것은 한낱 진술에 불과하지 않소. 피츠마셜 대위는 사람을 끄는 매력이 아주 많은 사람이고, 짐작컨대 적도 많을 게요. 이 주장들이 사실이라는 무슨 증거가 있소?\"\n\n\"저를 그자와 대면시키십시오.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"그것이 제가 청하는 전부이자 요구하는 전부입니다. 그자를 저와 여기 제 친구들과 대면시키십시오. 그러면 더 이상의 증거는 필요 없을 것입니다.\"\n\n\"음,\" 너프킨스 씨가 말했다. \"그거야 아주 쉽게 할 수 있겠소. 그자가 오늘 밤 여기 올 테니 말이오. 그렇게 하면 이 일을 굳이 세상에 알릴 필요가 없겠지, 그저—그저—그 젊은이 자신을 위해서라도 말이오, 아시겠소. 다만 나는—나는—먼저 그 조치가 타당한지 너프킨스 부인과 상의하고 싶소. 어쨌든, 픽윅 씨, 다른 무엇을 하기에 앞서 이 법적인 일부터 처리해야 하오. 부디 옆방으로 다시 들어가 주시오.\"\n\n그들은 옆방으로 들어갔다.\n\n\"그루머.\" 치안판사가 무시무시한 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"예, 나리.\" 그루머가 총애받는 자의 미소를 띠며 대답했다.\n\n\"이봐, 이봐.\" 치안판사가 준엄하게 말했다. \"여기서 그런 경박한 짓일랑 보이지 말게. 그건 아주 꼴사납고, 단언컨대 자네가 웃을 일이라곤 거의 없네. 자네가 방금 내게 해 준 진술이 한 치도 틀림없이 사실이었나? 자, 조심하게, 이 사람아!\"\n\n\"나리.\" 그루머가 더듬거렸다. \"저는—\"\n\n\"오, 자네 당황하고 있군, 그렇지?\" 치안판사가 말했다. \"징크스 씨, 이 당황한 모습이 보이오?\"\n\n\"분명히 보입니다, 나리.\" 징크스가 대답했다.\n\n\"자.\" 치안판사가 말했다. \"진술을 되풀이해 보게, 그루머. 다시 한번 조심하라고 경고하네. 징크스 씨, 그자의 말을 받아 적으시오.\"\n\n불운한 그루머는 자기 고발을 다시 진술하기 시작했으나, 징크스 씨가 그의 말을 받아 적는 데다 치안판사가 그것을 꼬집어 따지고, 게다가 천성적으로 횡설수설하는 버릇과 극도의 당황이 겹쳐, 그는 채 삼 분도 안 되어 그토록 엄청난 뒤엉킴과 모순의 덩어리에 휘말린 나머지, 너프킨스 씨는 당장 그를 믿지 못하겠노라 선언해 버렸다. 그리하여 벌금은 면제되었고, 징크스 씨는 순식간에 보증인 두 사람을 구해 냈다. 그리고 이 모든 엄숙한 절차가 흡족하게 마무리되자, 그루머 씨는 치욕스럽게 밖으로 쫓겨났으니—인간의 위대함이 얼마나 덧없는지, 그리고 위대한 자들의 총애가 얼마나 불안정한 것인지를 보여 주는 무서운 본보기였다.\n\n너프킨스 부인은 분홍 사 터번을 쓰고 연갈색 가발을 얹은 위엄 있는 여인이었다. 너프킨스 양은 터번을 빼고 어머니의 도도함을 죄다 물려받았고, 가발을 빼고 어머니의 심술을 죄다 물려받았다. 그리고 이 두 가지 사랑스러운 자질을 발휘하다가 모녀가 어떤 불쾌한 곤경에 빠질 때면—적잖이 그런 일이 있었다—그들은 한결같이 너프킨스 씨의 어깨에 그 책임을 지우는 데 의기투합했다. 따라서 너프킨스 씨가 너프킨스 부인을 찾아가 픽윅 씨가 전한 이야기를 소상히 늘어놓자, 너프킨스 부인은 자기가 늘 그런 일이 있으리라 예상했었다는 것, 늘 그렇게 될 거라고 말했었다는 것, 자기 충고는 결코 받아들여지지 않는다는 것, 너프킨스 씨가 도대체 자기를 무엇으로 여기는지 정말 모르겠다는 것 따위를 문득 떠올렸다.\n\n\"기가 막혀!\" 너프킨스 양이 양쪽 눈꼬리에 아주 인색한 분량의 눈물을 억지로 짜내며 말했다. \"내가 이런 바보 취급을 당하다니, 기가 막혀!\"\n\n\"아! 그건 네 아빠한테 고마워해야지, 얘야.\" 너프킨스 부인이 말했다. \"내가 저 양반더러 대위의 가문 내력을 알아보라고 얼마나 사정하고 애원했는지. 무슨 결단을 내리라고 얼마나 다그치고 간청했는지! 아무도 안 믿을 거야, 정말—정말로.\"\n\n\"하지만, 여보.\" 너프킨스 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"내게 말 걸지 말아요, 이 속 터지게 하는 양반아, 말 걸지 마요!\" 너프킨스 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"여보.\" 너프킨스 씨가 말했다. \"당신이 피츠마셜 대위를 무척 좋아한다고 공언했잖소. 당신이 줄곧 그자를 여기로 초대했고, 다른 데서도 그자를 소개할 기회를 한 번도 놓치지 않았잖소, 여보.\"\n\n\"내가 그렇게 말했지, 헨리에타?\" 너프킨스 부인이 몹시 억울한 여인의 태도로 딸에게 호소하며 외쳤다. \"네 아빠가 돌변해서 이 모든 걸 내 탓으로 돌릴 거라고 내가 말했지? 내가 그렇게 말했지?\" 여기서 너프킨스 부인은 흐느꼈다.\n\n\"오, 아빠!\" 너프킨스 양이 항변했다. 그리고 여기서 그녀도 흐느꼈다.\n\n\"이 모든 망신과 웃음거리를 우리에게 안겨 놓고도, 그게 내 탓이라고 빈정거리다니, 너무하지 않니?\" 너프킨스 부인이 외쳤다.\n\n\"우리가 어떻게 사교계에 얼굴을 내밀어요!\" 너프킨스 양이 말했다.\n\n\"포컨엄 일가를 어떻게 봐!\" 너프킨스 부인이 외쳤다.\n\n\"아니면 그릭스 일가는요!\" 너프킨스 양이 외쳤다.\n\n\"아니면 슬러민토우큰 일가는!\" 너프킨스 부인이 외쳤다. \"하지만 네 아빠가 무슨 상관이람! 저 양반한테 그게 무슨 대수겠어!\" 이 끔찍한 생각에 너프킨스 부인은 마음의 고통으로 울었고, 너프킨스 양도 같은 편에서 뒤따라 울었다.\n\n너프킨스 부인의 눈물은 그 문제를 곰곰이 생각할 짬을 좀 벌 때까지 엄청난 기세로 계속 솟구쳤다. 그러다 그녀는 속으로 결론을 내렸으니, 가장 좋은 방도는 픽윅 씨와 그 친구들에게 대위가 도착할 때까지 머물러 달라고 청한 뒤, 픽윅 씨에게 그가 바라는 기회를 주는 것이라고. 만일 그가 사실을 말한 것으로 드러나면, 일을 세상에 떠벌리지 않고도 대위를 집에서 내쫓을 수 있고, 그자가 가문의 궁정 연줄을 통해 시에라리온이나 사우거 곶이나, 혹은 유럽인들을 어찌나 홀리는지 한번 발을 들이면 좀처럼 돌아올 마음을 못 내게 만드는 그런 다른 어느 쾌적한 풍토의 총독으로 임명되었다고 말함으로써, 그가 사라진 것을 포컨엄 일가에게 쉽게 둘러댈 수 있을 터였다.\n\n너프킨스 부인이 눈물을 거두자 너프킨스 양도 눈물을 거두었고, 너프킨스 씨는 너프킨스 부인이 제안한 대로 일을 매듭짓게 되어 무척 기뻤다. 그리하여 픽윅 씨와 그 친구들은 조금 전 다툼의 흔적을 죄다 씻어 낸 뒤 부인들에게 소개되었고, 이내 만찬 자리로 안내되었다. 그리고 치안판사가 특유의 통찰력으로 반 시간 만에 세상에 둘도 없는 멋진 사내라고 알아본 웰러 씨는, 머즐 씨의 보살핌과 보호에 맡겨졌으니, 머즐 씨는 특별히 그를 아래층으로 데려가 융숭히 대접하라는 분부를 받았다.\n\n\"안녕하시오, 선생?\" 머즐 씨가 웰러 씨를 부엌 계단 아래로 안내하며 말했다.\n\n\"뭐, 조금 전에 응접실에서 당신네 영감 의자 뒤에 떡하니 버티고 있던 걸 본 뒤로 내 몸 상태에 별다른 변화는 없소이다.\" 샘이 대답했다.\n\n\"그때 당신을 더 살갑게 못 챙긴 건 양해해 주시오.\" 머즐 씨가 말했다. \"보다시피 그땐 주인 어른이 우릴 소개해 주지 않았으니 말이오. 아이고, 주인 어른이 당신을 어찌나 좋아하시는지, 웰러 씨, 정말이지!\"\n\n\"아!\" 샘이 말했다. \"참 유쾌한 양반이지!\"\n\n\"안 그렇소?\" 머즐 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"익살도 어찌나 풍부한지.\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n\"게다가 말솜씨는 또 어떻고.\" 머즐 씨가 말했다. \"생각이 어찌나 술술 흘러나오는지, 안 그렇소?\"\n\n\"기가 막히지.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"콸콸 쏟아져 나오면서 서로 머리를 어찌나 빨리 부딪치는지, 저희끼리 기절을 시키는 것 같다니까. 당최 무슨 말을 하려는 건지 알 수가 없지, 안 그렇소?\"\n\n\"그게 바로 그분 말씨의 큰 장점이지.\" 머즐 씨가 받았다. \"마지막 계단을 조심하시오, 웰러 씨. 부인들과 합석하기 전에 손이나 좀 씻으시겠소, 선생? 여기 물 받아 둔 개수통이 있고, 문 뒤에 깨끗한 수건이 걸려 있소이다.\"\n\n\"아! 한번 헹구는 것도 좋겠구먼.\" 웰러 씨가 노란 비누를 수건에 듬뿍 묻혀 얼굴이 다시 번들번들 빛날 때까지 문질러 대며 대답했다. \"부인이 몇이나 되오?\"\n\n\"우리 부엌엔 둘뿐이오.\" 머즐 씨가 말했다. \"요리사와 가정부 말이오. 궂은일 할 사내아이 하나와 계집아이도 하나 더 두긴 했지만, 그것들은 빨래방에서 밥을 먹소.\"\n\n\"오, 그것들은 빨래방에서 먹는다, 그렇소?\" 웰러 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇소.\" 머즐 씨가 대답했다. \"처음 왔을 때는 우리 식탁에서 먹여 봤는데, 도무지 두고 볼 수가 없더이다. 계집아이는 행실이 끔찍하게 천하고, 사내아이는 먹는 동안 숨을 어찌나 거칠게 몰아쉬는지, 같은 식탁에 앉아 있기가 도저히 불가능하더란 말이오.\"\n\n\"새끼 고래 같은 놈이로구먼!\" 웰러 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"오, 끔찍하지.\" 머즐 씨가 받았다. \"하지만 그게 시골 하인살이의 가장 못된 점이라오, 웰러 씨. 어린것들은 늘 그렇게 야만스럽거든. 이쪽이오, 선생, 괜찮다면 이쪽으로.\"\n\n머즐 씨는 더없이 정중하게 웰러 씨를 앞장세워 부엌으로 안내했다.\n\n\"메리.\" 머즐 씨가 그 예쁜 하녀에게 말했다. \"이분은 웰러 씨인데, 주인 어른이 될 수 있는 대로 편히 모시라고 아래로 보내신 분이오.\"\n\n\"그리고 당신네 주인 어른은 사정에 밝은 양반이라, 나를 딱 옳은 곳으로 보내셨군요.\" 웰러 씨가 메리에게 감탄의 눈길을 던지며 말했다. \"내가 만약 이 집 주인이라면, 편안함의 재료는 늘 메리 양이 있는 곳에서 찾을 거요.\"\n\n\"어머, 웰러 씨도!\" 메리가 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다.\n\n\"아유, 별꼴이야!\" 요리사가 소리쳤다.\n\n\"이런, 요리사 양반, 깜박했군.\" 머즐 씨가 말했다. \"웰러 씨, 소개해 드리리다.\"\n\n\"안녕하시오, 부인?\" 웰러 씨가 말했다. \"참으로 반갑소이다. 그리고 우리 사귐이 오래가길 바라오, 5파운드짜리 지폐한테 그 신사가 말했듯이 말이지.\"\n\n이 소개 의식이 다 끝나자, 요리사와 메리는 뒷부엌으로 물러가 십 분 동안 킥킥거리다가, 온통 깔깔거리며 얼굴을 붉힌 채 돌아와 만찬에 둘러앉았다.\n\n웰러 씨의 편안한 태도와 입담은 새로 사귄 벗들에게 어찌나 거부할 수 없는 영향을 미쳤던지, 만찬이 반도 채 끝나기 전에 그들은 완전히 허물없는 사이가 되었고, 잡 트로터의 비행에 관한 자초지종을 죄다 알게 되었다.\n\n\"난 그 잡이라는 작자를 도무지 못 참겠더라고요.\" 메리가 말했다.\n\n\"그야 절대 참아선 안 되지, 아가씨.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"왜요?\" 메리가 물었다.\n\n\"왜냐면 추함과 사기 행각이 우아함과 미덕과 친해서는 절대 안 되니까.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다. \"안 그렇소, 머즐 씨?\"\n\n\"결코 안 되지.\" 그 신사가 대답했다.\n\n여기서 메리가 웃으며 요리사 때문에 웃었다고 말하자, 요리사는 웃으며 자기는 아니라고 했다.\n\n\"전 잔이 없어요.\" 메리가 말했다.\n\n\"나하고 같이 마십시다, 아가씨.\" 웰러 씨가 말했다. \"이 잔에 입술을 대요, 그럼 내가 대리로 키스할 수 있으니.\"\n\n\"창피하게, 웰러 씨도!\" 메리가 말했다.\n\n\"뭐가 창피하단 거요, 아가씨?\"\n\n\"그런 식으로 말하는 거요.\"\n\n\"실없는 소리. 해로울 거 하나 없소. 자연스러운 거지, 안 그렇소, 요리사 양반?\"\n\n\"내게 묻지 말아요, 뻔뻔한 양반.\" 요리사가 한껏 기뻐하며 대답했다. 그러자 요리사와 메리가 또 웃어 댔는데, 맥주에다 차가운 고기에다 웃음까지 겹쳐, 메리 양은 그만 숨이 막힐 지경에 이르렀다—샘 웰러 씨가 더없이 섬세하게 등을 몇 차례 토닥이고 그 밖의 필요한 시중을 든 덕에 가까스로 회복된, 아찔한 위기였다.\n\n이 모든 흥겨움과 화기애애함 한가운데서, 정원 문에서 요란한 종소리가 들렸고, 빨래방에서 끼니를 때우는 그 젊은 양반이 즉시 응답하러 갔다. 웰러 씨는 예쁜 가정부에게 한창 정성을 쏟고 있었고, 머즐 씨는 식탁에서 주인 노릇 하기에 바빴으며, 요리사는 마침 커다란 음식 조각을 입으로 가져가던 바로 그 순간에 웃느라 멈춰 있었다—바로 그때 부엌문이 열리고 잡 트로터 씨가 걸어 들어왔다.\n\n잡 트로터 씨가 걸어 들어왔다고 우리는 말했으나, 이 진술은 우리가 늘 사실에 꼼꼼히 충실한 것과는 사뭇 다르다. 문이 열리고 트로터 씨가 나타났다. 그는 걸어 들어오려 했고, 실로 막 그렇게 하려던 참이었으나, 웰러 씨를 발견하는 순간 자기도 모르게 한두 걸음 움찔 물러서더니, 경악과 공포로 완전히 꼼짝 못 한 채, 눈앞에 펼쳐진 뜻밖의 광경을 응시하며 서 있었다.\n\n\"여기 왔네!\" 샘이 더없이 신이 나서 일어나며 말했다. \"아니, 우리가 바로 그 순간에 자네 얘길 하고 있었거든. 잘 지냈나? 어디 갔다 왔어? 어서 들어와.\"\n\n웰러 씨는 저항 없는 잡의 멀버리 옷깃에 손을 얹고 그를 부엌 안으로 끌고 들어왔다. 그러고는 문을 잠그고 열쇠를 머즐 씨에게 건네자, 머즐 씨는 아주 태연하게 그것을 옆 호주머니에 넣고 단추를 채웠다.\n\n\"자, 이거 한판 벌어졌구먼!\" 샘이 외쳤다. \"글쎄 우리 주인 어른이 위층에서 자네 주인 만나는 기쁨을 누리시고, 나는 여기 아래서 자네 만나는 즐거움을 누리다니 말이야. 어떻게 지내나? 그 양초 가게 장사는 잘될 것 같은가? 아, 자네를 보니 어찌나 반가운지. 어찌나 행복해 보이는지. 자네를 보는 게 그야말로 큰 즐거움이야, 안 그렇소, 머즐 씨?\"\n\n\"그렇소.\" 머즐 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"어찌나 명랑한지!\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n\"기분이 어찌나 좋아 보이는지!\" 머즐이 말했다.\n\n\"게다가 우릴 보고 어찌나 반가워하는지—그러니 한결 더 흐뭇하단 말이야.\" 샘이 말했다. \"앉아, 앉으라고.\"\n\n트로터 씨는 떠밀려 난롯가 의자에 앉혀지도록 자신을 내맡겼다. 그는 그 자그마한 눈을 먼저 웰러 씨에게, 그다음 머즐 씨에게 던졌으나, 아무 말도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"자, 그럼.\" 샘이 말했다. \"여기 이 부인들 앞에서, 그저 호기심 삼아 자네한테 좀 묻고 싶은데 말이야, 자네는 분홍 체크 손수건과 4번 헌금 바구니를 써 본 그 어떤 젊은 양반 못지않게 자네 스스로가 곱고 예의 바른 젊은 양반이라고 여기지 않나?\"\n\n\"게다가 요리사하고 결혼까지 하려던 양반 아니랍니까.\" 그 부인이 분개하여 말했다. \"그 악당이!\"\n\n\"그러고는 못된 짓을 청산하고 나중에 양초 장사를 차리겠다던 양반이고요.\" 가정부가 말했다.\n\n\"자, 내 말 들어 봐, 젊은이.\" 머즐 씨가 방금 한 두 마디 언급에 격분하여 근엄하게 말했다. \"여기 이 부인이(요리사를 가리키며) 나와 사귀고 있단 말이야. 그런데 자네가, 선생, 감히 이 사람과 양초 가게를 차리겠다는 둥 떠벌리면, 그건 한 사내가 다른 사내를 해칠 수 있는 가장 미묘한 대목에서 나를 해치는 거란 말이야. 알아듣겠나, 선생?\"\n\n여기서 자기 웅변에 대단한 자부심을 품고 그 점에서 주인을 흉내 내던 머즐 씨가, 대답을 기다리며 잠시 말을 멈추었다.\n\n그러나 트로터 씨는 아무 대답도 하지 않았다. 그래서 머즐 씨는 근엄한 태도로 말을 이었다.\n\n\"십중팔구, 선생, 자네는 몇 분 동안은 위층에서 부르지 않을 걸세, 선생. 왜냐하면 우리 주인 어른이 지금 이 순간 자네 주인의 죽을 끓여 매듭짓느라 각별히 바쁘시거든, 선생. 그러니 자네한텐, 선생, 나하고 잠시 단둘이 얘기할 짬이 있을 걸세, 선생. 알아듣겠나, 선생?\"\n\n머즐 씨가 다시 대답을 기다리며 멈췄으나, 트로터 씨는 또다시 그를 실망시켰다.\n\n\"자, 그럼.\" 머즐 씨가 말했다. \"부인들 앞에서 내 뜻을 분명히 밝혀야 하는 게 참 유감이지만, 사정이 급하니 그것으로 양해를 구하겠네. 뒷부엌이 비어 있어, 선생. 자네가 그리로 들어가 준다면, 선생, 웰러 씨가 공정하게 지켜볼 테고, 우리는 종이 울릴 때까지 서로 만족스럽게 결판을 낼 수 있을 거야. 날 따라오게, 선생!\"\n\n머즐 씨는 이 말을 하면서 문 쪽으로 한두 걸음 내딛었고, 시간을 아끼느라 걸어가며 윗도리를 벗기 시작했다.\n\n자, 요리사는 이 무시무시한 도전의 끝맺는 말을 듣고 머즐 씨가 막 그것을 실행에 옮기려는 것을 보자마자, 크고 날카로운 비명을 질렀다. 그러고는 즉각 의자에서 일어선 잡 트로터 씨에게 달려들어, 흥분한 여성 특유의 기세로 그의 크고 넓적한 얼굴을 할퀴고 쥐어박았으며, 두 손을 그의 긴 검은 머리칼에 휘감아 가장 큰 치수의 추모 반지를 대여섯 다스는 만들 만큼의 머리털을 잡아 뽑았다. 머즐 씨를 향한 헌신적인 사랑에서 우러난 온갖 열정으로 이 위업을 이뤄 낸 그녀는 비틀거리며 물러섰다. 그리고 몹시 흥분 잘하고 섬세한 감정의 숙녀인지라, 그녀는 즉각 찬장 밑으로 쓰러져 기절해 버렸다.\n\n이 순간, 종이 울렸다.\n\n\"자넬 부르는 거야, 잡 트로터.\" 샘이 말했다. 그리고 트로터 씨가 항변이나 대답을 할 새도 없이—기절한 숙녀가 입힌 상처를 지혈할 틈조차 없이—샘이 한쪽 팔을, 머즐 씨가 다른 쪽 팔을 붙잡고, 하나는 앞에서 끌고 하나는 뒤에서 밀며, 그를 위층 응접실로 데려갔다.\n\n인상적인 활인화였다. 앨프리드 징글 님, 일명 피츠마셜 대위는 모자를 손에 들고 얼굴에 미소를 띤 채, 자신의 매우 곤란한 처지에는 조금도 동요하지 않고 문 가까이에 서 있었다. 그와 마주하여 픽윅 씨가 서 있었는데, 그는 분명 어떤 고매한 도덕적 교훈을 가르치던 참이었으니, 왼손은 윗도리 자락 밑에, 오른손은 허공으로 뻗고 있었던바, 인상적인 연설을 토해 낼 때면 늘 그러던 자세였다. 조금 떨어진 곳에는 터프먼 씨가 분개한 얼굴로, 두 젊은 친구에게 조심스레 제지당한 채 서 있었다. 그리고 방 저편 끝에는 너프킨스 씨와 너프킨스 부인, 너프킨스 양이 침울하게 도도하고 사납게 속상해하며 서 있었다.\n\n\"무엇이 나를 막는가.\" 잡이 끌려 들어오자 너프킨스 씨가 치안판사다운 위엄으로 말했다. \"무엇이 나를 막아, 이자들을 악당이요 사기꾼으로 구류하지 못하게 하는가? 어리석은 자비로다. 무엇이 나를 막는가?\"\n\n\"자존심이지, 영감, 자존심.\" 징글이 아주 느긋하게 대답했다. \"안 될걸—틀렸어—대위를 하나 낚았겠다, 응?—하! 하! 아주 좋아—딸 사윗감으로 말이지—물려던 자가 물렸군—세상에 알린다—천만에—멍청해 보일걸—아주!\"\n\n\"비열한 놈.\" 너프킨스 씨가 말했다. \"우리는 네놈의 천박한 암시 따위 경멸한다.\"\n\n\"전 처음부터 저 사람이 싫었어요.\" 헨리에타가 덧붙였다.\n\n\"오, 그렇겠지.\" 징글이 말했다. \"키 큰 젊은이—옛 연인—시드니 포컨엄—부자—훌륭한 친구—그래도 대위만큼 부자는 아니지, 응?—그자를 내쳐라—꺼지게 해—대위를 위해서라면 뭐든지—대위만 한 게 어디 없으니—계집애들 죄다—미쳐 날뛰지—응, 잡, 응?\"\n\n여기서 징글 씨는 아주 진심으로 껄껄 웃었고, 잡은 기뻐서 두 손을 비비며, 집에 들어온 뒤로 처음으로 소리를 냈으니—낮고 소리 없는 킬킬거림이었는데, 그것은 자기 웃음을 너무도 만끽한 나머지 그 어느 한 조각도 소리로 새어 나가게 하지 않으려는 듯했다.\n\n\"너프킨스 씨.\" 나이 든 부인이 말했다. \"이건 하인들이 엿들을 만한 대화가 아니에요. 저 비열한 것들을 내보내요.\"\n\n\"물론이오, 여보.\" 너프킨스 씨가 말했다. \"머즐!\"\n\n\"예, 나리.\"\n\n\"현관문을 열어라.\"\n\n\"예, 나리.\"\n\n\"이 집에서 나가라!\" 너프킨스 씨가 단호하게 손을 내저으며 말했다.\n\n징글이 미소를 짓고는 문 쪽으로 움직였다.\n\n\"멈추시오!\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. 징글이 멈췄다.\n\n\"나는,\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"당신과 저기 저 위선적인 당신 친구의 손에 겪은 처사에 대해 훨씬 더 큰 복수를 할 수도 있었소.\"\n\n잡 트로터가 더없이 정중하게 절을 하며 가슴에 손을 얹었다.\n\n\"내 말은,\" 픽윅 씨가 차츰 화가 치밀어 말했다. \"내가 더 큰 복수를 할 수도 있었지만, 당신의 정체를 폭로하는 것으로 만족한다는 것이오. 나는 그것을 사회에 진 의무로 여기오. 이것은 관용이오, 선생. 부디 그것을 기억해 두기 바라오.\"\n\n픽윅 씨가 이 대목에 이르자, 잡 트로터는 익살스러운 엄숙함으로 손을 귀에 갖다 댔으니, 그가 내뱉는 한 음절도 놓치지 않으려는 듯했다.\n\n\"그리고 한마디만 덧붙이겠소, 선생.\" 이제 완전히 화가 난 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"나는 당신을 악당이요, 또—또—불한당이요—또—또 멀버리 제복을 입은 저 경건하고 신성한 척하는 부랑자를 빼고는, 내가 보았거나 들었던 그 누구보다도 더 못된 자로 여기는 바이오.\"\n\n\"하! 하!\" 징글이 말했다. \"좋은 친구야, 픽윅—마음씨 곱고—다부진 영감이지—하지만 격해지면 안 돼—나쁜 거야, 아주—잘 있어, 잘 있어—언젠가 또 봅시다—기운 내고—자, 잡—종종걸음!\"\n\n이 말과 함께 징글 씨는 예의 그 방식대로 모자를 척 눌러쓰고 성큼성큼 방을 나섰다. 잡 트로터는 잠시 멈춰 둘러보고는 미소를 짓더니, 픽윅 씨에게는 가짜 엄숙함을 담은 절을, 웰러 씨에게는 그 뻔뻔한 능청스러움이 도무지 형언할 길 없는 윙크를 보내고, 제 알량한 주인의 발자취를 뒤따라갔다.\n\n\"샘.\" 웰러 씨가 따라 나서려 하자 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\"예, 나리.\"\n\n\"여기 있거라.\"\n\n웰러 씨는 머뭇거리는 듯했다.\n\n\"여기 있거라.\" 픽윅 씨가 되풀이했다.\n\n\"저 잡이라는 놈을 앞뜰에서 한번 손봐 줘도 안 되겠습니까, 나리?\" 웰러 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"절대 안 된다.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"문밖으로 걷어차 내쫓는 것도 안 됩니까, 나리?\" 웰러 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"무슨 일이 있어도 안 된다.\" 그의 주인이 대답했다.\n\n고용된 이래 처음으로, 웰러 씨는 한순간 불만스럽고 언짢은 표정을 지었다. 그러나 그의 얼굴은 곧장 환해졌으니, 약삭빠른 머즐 씨가 현관문 뒤에 몸을 숨겼다가 바로 알맞은 순간에 사납게 뛰쳐나와, 더없이 솜씨 좋게 징글 씨와 그 종자를 둘 다 계단 아래로, 그 밑에 놓인 미국산 알로에 통 속으로 굴러떨어뜨리는 데 성공했기 때문이다.\n\n\"제 의무를 다했으니, 나리.\" 픽윅 씨가 너프킨스 씨에게 말했다. \"저는 제 친구들과 더불어 작별을 고하겠습니다. 저희가 받은 환대에 감사드리면서도, 저희 모두의 이름으로 단언하건대, 저희가 강한 의무감에 떠밀리지 않았던들, 저희는 그 환대를 받아들이지도, 앞서의 곤경에서 이런 식으로 벗어나는 데 동의하지도 않았을 것입니다. 저희는 내일 런던으로 돌아갑니다. 나리의 비밀은 저희가 안전하게 지켜 드리겠습니다.\"\n\n이렇듯 그날 아침의 처사에 대한 항의를 표한 뒤, 픽윅 씨는 부인들에게 깊이 절하고는, 가족의 만류에도 불구하고 친구들과 함께 방을 나섰다.\n\n\"네 모자를 가져오너라, 샘.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"아래층에 있습니다, 나리.\" 샘이 말하고는 그것을 가지러 뛰어 내려갔다.\n\n자, 부엌에는 그 예쁜 가정부 말고는 아무도 없었다. 그리고 샘의 모자가 어디 놓였는지 알 수 없었던 터라 그것을 찾아야 했고, 예쁜 가정부가 불을 비춰 주었다. 그들은 온 사방을 뒤져 모자를 찾아야 했다. 예쁜 가정부는 그것을 찾으려는 조바심에 무릎을 꿇고 앉아, 문 옆 작은 구석에 쌓여 있는 온갖 물건을 들춰 보았다. 거북한 구석이었다. 먼저 문을 닫지 않고는 거기에 손이 닿지 않았다.\n\n\"여기 있네요.\" 예쁜 가정부가 말했다. \"이거죠, 그렇죠?\"\n\n\"어디 봅시다.\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n예쁜 가정부는 촛불을 바닥에 세워 두었는데, 불빛이 아주 흐릿했던 탓에 샘은 그것이 정말 자기 모자인지 아닌지 보려면 무릎을 꿇어야 했다. 유난히 좁은 구석이었고, 그래서—집을 지은 사람 말고는 누구의 잘못도 아니었으나—샘과 예쁜 가정부는 어쩔 수 없이 아주 바싹 붙어 있게 되었다.\n\n\"맞소, 이거요.\" 샘이 말했다. \"잘 있어요!\"\n\n\"잘 가요!\" 예쁜 가정부가 말했다.\n\n\"잘 있어요!\" 샘이 말했다. 그리고 그렇게 말하면서, 그토록 애써 찾아낸 모자를 떨어뜨렸다.\n\n\"어쩜 그리 칠칠치 못해요.\" 예쁜 가정부가 말했다. \"조심하지 않으면 또 잃어버릴 거예요.\"\n\n그래서 그가 또 잃어버리지 않도록, 그녀가 직접 그것을 그에게 씌워 주었다.\n\n예쁜 가정부의 얼굴이 샘의 얼굴을 향해 들어 올려졌을 때 한층 더 예뻐 보였기 때문인지, 아니면 둘이 그토록 가까이 있던 데서 비롯된 우연한 결과였는지는 오늘날까지도 알 수 없는 일이다. 그러나 샘은 그녀에게 입을 맞추었다.\n\n\"설마 일부러 그런 건 아니겠죠.\" 예쁜 가정부가 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 그땐 일부러는 아니었소.\" 샘이 말했다. \"하지만 이번엔 일부러 하겠소.\"\n\n그러고는 다시 그녀에게 입을 맞추었다.\n\n\"샘!\" 픽윅 씨가 난간 너머로 부르며 말했다.\n\n\"갑니다, 나리.\" 샘이 위층으로 뛰어 올라가며 대답했다.\n\n\"어찌 그리 오래 걸렸느냐!\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\"문 뒤에 뭔가가 있어서, 한참 동안 도무지 열 수가 없었습니다, 나리.\" 샘이 대답했다.\n\n그리고 이것이 웰러 씨의 첫사랑의 첫 대목이었다.\n\n징글의 정체를 폭로함으로써 여행의 주된 목적과 목표를 이룬 픽윅 씨는, 그동안 도드슨과 포그 두 사람이 자신을 상대로 취해 온 소송 절차를 알아보기 위해 즉시 런던으로 돌아가기로 결심했다. 자신의 성격다운 온갖 정력과 결단력으로 이 결심을 실행에 옮긴 그는, 앞의 두 장에서 자세히 서술한 잊지 못할 사건들이 있은 다음 날 아침, 입스위치를 떠나는 첫 마차의 뒷좌석에 올라탔다. 그리고 세 친구와 새뮤얼 웰러 씨를 대동하고 그날 저녁 더없이 건강하고 무사하게 수도에 도착했다.\n\n여기서 친구들은 잠시 동안 헤어졌다. 터프먼 씨, 윈클 씨, 스노드그래스 씨는 곧 있을 딩글리 델 방문에 필요한 준비를 하기 위해 각자의 집으로 향했고, 픽윅 씨와 샘은 매우 훌륭하고 고풍스러우며 안락한 숙소, 즉 롬바드 가 조지 야드에 있는 조지 앤드 벌처 여관 겸 호텔에 당분간 거처를 정했다.\n\n픽윅 씨는 저녁 식사를 마치고 특제 포트와인 두 번째 잔까지 비운 뒤, 비단 손수건을 머리 위에 끌어 덮고 발을 난로 받침대에 올린 채 안락의자에 몸을 깊숙이 기대고 있었는데, 그때 웰러 씨가 융단 가방을 들고 들어오는 바람에 고요한 명상에서 깨어났다.\n\n\"샘.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"네, 어르신.\" 웰러 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"방금 생각해 보았는데 말이다, 샘.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"고스웰 가에 있는 바델 부인 댁에 물건을 꽤 많이 두고 왔으니, 다시 시내를 떠나기 전에 그것들을 가져올 채비를 해 두어야겠다.\"\n\n\"아주 좋습니다, 어르신.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"당장은 그것들을 터프먼 씨 댁으로 보내 두면 되겠다, 샘.\" 픽윅 씨가 말을 이었다. \"하지만 가져오기 전에, 물건들을 찾아내어 한데 모아 두어야 한다. 네가 고스웰 가에 좀 가서 그 일을 처리해 주었으면 한다, 샘.\"\n\n\"지금 당장 말씀입니까, 어르신?\" 웰러 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"당장 가거라.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다. \"그리고 잠깐, 샘.\" 픽윅 씨가 지갑을 꺼내며 덧붙였다. \"치를 집세가 좀 있다. 분기 집세는 크리스마스까지는 만기가 아니다만, 미리 치러서 끝내 두어도 좋다. 한 달 전 통지로 내 세입이 끝나게 된다. 여기 그 통지서를 적어 두었다. 이것을 건네고, 바델 부인에게 언제든 원할 때 세놓는다는 광고를 붙여도 된다고 전하거라.\"\n\n\"아주 좋습니다, 어르신.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다. \"그 밖에 더 하실 말씀은요, 어르신?\"\n\n\"더 없다, 샘.\"\n\n웰러 씨는 무언가 더 있기를 기대하기라도 하듯 천천히 문 쪽으로 걸어가, 천천히 문을 열고, 천천히 밖으로 나가서는, 천천히 문을 두어 인치쯤 남기고 닫았는데, 그때 픽윅 씨가 불러 세웠다.\n\n\"샘.\"\n\n\"네, 어르신.\" 웰러 씨가 얼른 되돌아와 등 뒤로 문을 닫으며 말했다.\n\n\"샘, 나는 네가 바델 부인 본인이 나를 어떻게 여기는 듯한지, 그리고 이 비열하고 근거 없는 소송이 정말로 끝까지 갈 가능성이 있는지를 알아보려 애쓰는 데 반대하지 않는다. 네가 원한다면 그렇게 하는 데 반대하지 않는다는 말이다, 샘.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n샘은 알아들었다는 듯 짧게 고개를 끄덕이고 방을 나섰다. 픽윅 씨는 다시 한번 비단 손수건을 머리 위로 끌어 덮고 한숨 잘 채비를 했다. 웰러 씨는 곧장 자신의 임무를 수행하러 걸어 나갔다.\n\n그가 고스웰 가에 다다랐을 때는 거의 아홉 시였다. 자그마한 앞쪽 거실에는 촛불 두 자루가 타고 있었고, 창문 블라인드에는 모자 두 개가 비쳐 있었다. 바델 부인에게 손님이 와 있었던 것이다.\n\n웰러 씨가 문을 두드리자, 꽤 긴 시간이 흐른 뒤에야—그동안 밖에 있는 쪽에서는 가락을 휘파람으로 불고, 안에 있는 쪽에서는 말을 안 듣는 평평한 양초가 불 켜지기를 받아들이도록 구슬리느라 시간이 걸렸는데—자그마한 장화 한 켤레가 바닥 깔개 위를 타닥타닥 걸어왔고, 바델 도련님이 모습을 드러냈다.\n\n\"어이, 꼬마 동네 양반.\" 샘이 말했다. \"어머니는 좀 어떠시냐?\"\n\n\"잘 지내세요.\" 바델 도련님이 대답했다. \"저도 그렇고요.\"\n\n\"그것참 다행이로구나.\" 샘이 말했다. \"어머니께 내가 좀 뵙고 싶다고 전해 주겠니, 우리 천재 신동 아기씨?\"\n\n이렇게 부탁을 받은 바델 도련님은 말 안 듣는 평평한 양초를 맨 아래 계단에 내려놓고, 전갈을 들고 앞쪽 거실로 사라졌다.\n\n창문 블라인드에 비쳤던 두 모자는, 바델 부인의 가장 각별한 지인 두 사람이 각각 쓴 머리 장식이었으니, 그들은 조용히 차나 한잔 마시고, 돼지 족발 두 벌과 구운 치즈로 따끈한 야식이나 좀 들려고 막 들른 참이었다. 치즈는 난로 앞 작은 더치 오븐 속에서 더없이 먹음직스럽게 보글보글 끓으며 노릇노릇 익어 가고 있었고, 족발은 화덕 선반 위 작은 양철 냄비 속에서 맛있게 익어 가고 있었으며, 바델 부인과 두 친구 또한 자기들의 각별한 친구들과 지인들에 관한 조용한 잡담을 나누며 매우 사이좋게 지내고 있던 차에, 바델 도련님이 문에 응대하고 돌아와 새뮤얼 웰러 씨가 맡긴 전갈을 전했다.\n\n\"픽윅 씨의 하인이라고!\" 바델 부인이 새파랗게 질리며 말했다.\n\n\"어머나 세상에!\" 클러핀스 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"글쎄, 내가 마침 여기 있지 않았더라면 정말이지 믿지 못했을 거예요!\" 샌더스 부인이 말했다.\n\n클러핀스 부인은 작고 활발하며 분주해 보이는 여자였고, 샌더스 부인은 크고 뚱뚱하며 우둔한 얼굴을 한 인물이었으니, 이 둘이 손님이었다.\n\n바델 부인은 동요하는 것이 마땅하다고 느꼈다. 그리고 세 사람 중 누구도 지금 상황에서 도드슨과 포그를 거치지 않고 픽윅 씨의 하인과 어떤 식으로든 이야기를 나누어도 되는 것인지 정확히 알지 못했으므로, 모두가 다소 당황했다. 이런 우유부단한 상태에서, 가장 먼저 해야 할 일은 분명, 웰러 씨를 문간에서 맞아들인 죄로 아이를 한 대 쥐어박는 것이었다. 그래서 어머니가 아이를 쥐어박았고, 아이는 가락을 맞춰 울어 댔다.\n\n\"시끄럽게 좀 굴지 마라—제발—이 못된 녀석아!\" 바델 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 가엾은 어머니를 속 썩이지 말거라.\" 샌더스 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"안 그래도 어머니는 너 말고도 속 썩일 일이 충분하단다, 토미.\" 클러핀스 부인이 동정 어린 체념의 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"아! 더 안된 일이지, 가엾은 어린양!\" 샌더스 부인이 말했다.\n\n이 모든 교훈적인 훈계를 들을 때마다, 바델 도련님은 더욱 크게 울부짖었다.\n\n\"자, 이제 어쩌면 좋죠?\" 바델 부인이 클러핀스 부인에게 물었다.\n\n\"내 생각엔 그자를 만나 봐야 할 것 같아요.\" 클러핀스 부인이 대답했다. \"하지만 절대로 증인 없이는 안 돼요.\"\n\n\"증인은 둘이 있어야 더 법에 맞을 것 같은데요.\" 다른 친구와 마찬가지로 호기심으로 터질 듯한 샌더스 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"이리로 들어오라고 하는 게 낫겠어요.\" 바델 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"물론이지요.\" 클러핀스 부인이 그 생각에 얼른 매달리며 대답했다. \"들어와요, 젊은이. 그리고 먼저 현관문부터 좀 닫고요.\"\n\n웰러 씨는 즉시 그 뜻을 알아차렸다. 그리고 거실에 모습을 드러내고는, 자신의 용건을 바델 부인에게 이렇게 설명했다.\n\n\"개인적으로 폐를 끼치게 되어 대단히 죄송합니다, 부인. 강도가 노부인을 불 위에 올려놓으면서 한 말마따나 말입니다. 하지만 저와 제 주인 어른이 막 시내에 온 데다 또 막 떠나려는 참이라, 보시다시피 어쩔 수가 없습니다요.\"\n\n\"물론, 젊은이가 주인의 잘못을 어쩔 수는 없지요.\" 웰러 씨의 외모와 말솜씨에 자못 감명받은 클러핀스 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"그럼요, 그렇고말고요.\" 샌더스 부인이 맞장구를 쳤는데, 그녀는 작은 양철 냄비를 향해 무언가 아쉬운 눈길을 던지는 것으로 보아, 샘이 야식을 들고 가라는 권유를 받을 경우 족발이 어느 정도까지 줄어들지를 마음속으로 계산하고 있는 듯했다.\n\n\"그래서 제가 온 용건은 그저 이런 것입니다.\" 샘이 그 끼어드는 말은 아랑곳 않고 말했다. \"첫째, 제 주인 어른의 통지를 전하는 것—여기 있습니다. 둘째, 집세를 치르는 것—여기 있습니다. 셋째, 주인 어른의 물건을 모두 한데 모아 두었다가 우리가 사람을 보내면 누구에게든 내어 달라는 것. 넷째, 부인께서 언제든 원하시는 대로 이 집을 세놓으셔도 된다는 것—이상입니다.\"\n\n\"무슨 일이 있었든 간에,\" 바델 부인이 말했다. \"저는 늘 말해 왔고 앞으로도 늘 말하겠지만, 딱 한 가지만 빼면 모든 면에서 픽윅 씨는 언제나 완벽한 신사답게 처신하셨어요. 그분 돈은 언제나 은행만큼이나 확실했지요—언제나요.\"\n\n바델 부인은 이렇게 말하며 손수건을 눈가에 갖다 대고는, 영수증을 가지러 방을 나갔다.\n\n샘은 자기가 그저 가만히 있기만 하면 여자들이 틀림없이 떠들어 댈 것임을 잘 알고 있었다. 그래서 그는 양철 냄비, 구운 치즈, 벽, 천장을 번갈아 바라보며 깊은 침묵을 지켰다.\n\n\"가엾은 양반!\" 클러핀스 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"아, 가엾은 사람!\" 샌더스 부인이 응수했다.\n\n샘은 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 그들이 본론으로 다가오고 있음을 알아챘던 것이다.\n\n\"그런 위증을 생각하면 정말이지 견딜 수가 없어요.\" 클러핀스 부인이 말했다. \"젊은이를 불편하게 할 말은 하고 싶지 않지만, 자네 주인은 늙은 짐승이고, 그 작자가 여기 있다면 면전에 대고 그렇게 말해 주고 싶군요.\"\n\n\"여기 계셨더라면 좋았겠네요.\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n\"그 부인이 얼마나 끔찍하게 시름에 잠겨 있는지, 멍하니 돌아다니며 아무것에도 즐거움을 못 느끼고, 오직 친구들이 자비심으로 찾아와 곁에 앉아 마음을 달래 줄 때나 좀 나아지는지를 본다면—\" 클러핀스 부인이 양철 냄비와 더치 오븐을 흘끔거리며 말을 이었다. \"정말 충격적이라니까요!\"\n\n\"야만적이지요.\" 샌더스 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"게다가 자네 주인 말이에요, 젊은이! 돈 많은 신사라서 아내 두는 비용쯤은 아무것도 아닌 듯 전혀 부담도 안 될 양반이.\" 클러핀스 부인이 매우 거침없이 말을 이었다. \"그자의 행동에는 변명할 구석이 털끝만큼도 없어요! 어째서 그 부인과 결혼하지 않는 거죠?\"\n\n\"아.\" 샘이 말했다. \"물론이죠. 바로 그게 문제지요.\"\n\n\"문제고말고요.\" 클러핀스 부인이 받아쳤다. \"내가 가진 기개가 그 부인에게 있었다면, 그 작자를 문초했을 거예요. 하지만 어쨌든, 우리 여자들에게도 법이라는 게 있지요. 그자들은 할 수만 있다면 우리를 비참한 처지로 만들려 들겠지만요. 그리고 그건 자네 주인이, 젊은이, 반년이 채 지나기 전에 호된 대가를 치르고서 알게 될 거예요.\"\n\n이 위안 어린 훈계를 하면서, 클러핀스 부인은 거드름을 피우며 샌더스 부인에게 미소를 지었고, 샌더스 부인도 마주 미소로 답했다.\n\n'소송은 진행 중이로군, 틀림없어.' 바델 부인이 영수증을 들고 다시 들어올 때 샘은 그렇게 생각했다.\n\n\"여기 영수증이에요, 웰러 씨.\" 바델 부인이 말했다. \"그리고 여기 거스름돈이고요. 그리고 추위도 막을 겸 뭐라도 한 모금 드셨으면 좋겠어요, 옛정을 봐서라도요, 웰러 씨.\"\n\n샘은 자기가 얻게 될 이점을 알아채고 즉시 응했다. 그러자 바델 부인은 작은 벽장에서 검은 병 하나와 포도주 잔 하나를 꺼냈다. 그런데 깊은 심적 고통으로 인한 그녀의 정신없음이 어찌나 심했던지, 웰러 씨의 잔을 채운 뒤에 포도주 잔 세 개를 더 꺼내어 거기에도 가득 따랐다.\n\n\"어머나, 바델 부인.\" 클러핀스 부인이 말했다. \"당신이 무슨 짓을 했는지 좀 봐요!\"\n\n\"어머, 이거 정말 우습네!\" 샌더스 부인이 탄성을 질렀다.\n\n\"아, 내 가엾은 정신머리!\" 바델 부인이 희미한 미소를 띠며 말했다.\n\n샘은 물론 이 모든 것을 알아챘으므로, 곧바로 자기는 숙녀 분이 함께 마시지 않는 한 결코 야식 전에는 술을 못 마신다고 말했다. 한바탕 웃음이 터졌고, 샌더스 부인이 그의 비위를 맞춰 주겠다고 자청하여 자기 잔에서 살짝 한 모금 마셨다. 그러자 샘은 모두 돌아가며 마셔야 한다고 했고, 그래서 다들 살짝 한 모금씩 마셨다. 그러고 나서 작은 클러핀스 부인이 \"바델 대 픽윅 소송의 승리를 위하여\"라는 건배사를 제안했고, 그러자 숙녀들은 그 취지를 기려 잔을 비웠으며, 곧장 매우 수다스러워졌다.\n\n\"무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 들으셨겠죠, 웰러 씨?\" 바델 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"그것에 대해 좀 듣긴 했습니다.\" 샘이 대답했다.\n\n\"그런 식으로 세상 사람들 앞에 끌려 나가는 건 끔찍한 일이에요, 웰러 씨.\" 바델 부인이 말했다. \"하지만 이제 알겠어요, 이게 내가 해야 할 단 하나의 길이라는 걸요. 그리고 제 변호사인 도드슨 씨와 포그 씨가 그러는데, 우리가 부를 증거를 가지고서라면 틀림없이 이긴다고 해요. 만약 못 이긴다면, 웰러 씨, 저는 어찌해야 할지 모르겠어요.\"\n\n바델 부인이 소송에서 진다는 생각만으로도 샌더스 부인은 어찌나 깊이 충격을 받았던지, 즉시 자기 잔을 다시 채워 다시 비울 수밖에 없었다. 나중에 그녀가 말하기를, 그렇게 할 마음의 침착함이 없었더라면 자기는 그 자리에서 쓰러지고 말았으리라는 것이었다.\n\n\"언제쯤 재판이 열릴 것 같습니까?\" 샘이 물었다.\n\n\"2월 아니면 3월쯤일 거예요.\" 바델 부인이 대답했다.\n\n\"증인이 얼마나 많이 나올까요, 안 그래요?\" 클러핀스 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"아! 그렇고말고요!\" 샌더스 부인이 응수했다.\n\n\"그리고 만약 원고가 이기지 못하면 도드슨 씨와 포그 씨가 얼마나 길길이 날뛸까요.\" 클러핀스 부인이 덧붙였다. \"이 일을 죄다 성공 보수로만 맡아 했으니 말이에요!\"\n\n\"아! 그렇고말고요!\" 샌더스 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 원고가 틀림없이 이길 거예요.\" 클러핀스 부인이 말을 이었다.\n\n\"그러길 바라요.\" 바델 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"오, 그건 의심할 여지가 없어요.\" 샌더스 부인이 거들었다.\n\n\"그럼.\" 샘이 일어서서 잔을 내려놓으며 말했다. \"제가 드릴 말씀은 그저, 부인께서 부디 이기시기를 바란다는 것뿐입니다.\"\n\n\"고마워요, 웰러 씨.\" 바델 부인이 간절히 말했다.\n\n\"그리고 이런 일을 성공 보수로 맡아 하는 그 도드슨이니 포그니 하는 작자들이며,\" 웰러 씨가 말을 이었다. \"또 그와 같은 직업에 종사하면서 친절하고 너그러운 척하며, 공짜로 거저 사람들을 서로 싸움 붙이고, 자기 서기들을 시켜 이웃이나 지인들 사이에 소송으로 해결해야 할 자그마한 분쟁거리를 찾아내게 하는 그 종류의 사람들 말입니다만—제가 그들에 대해 드릴 수 있는 말은 그저, 제가 그들에게 주고 싶은 보상을 그들이 받았으면 좋겠다는 것뿐입니다.\"\n\n\"아, 친절하고 너그러운 마음을 가진 사람이라면 누구나 그들에게 베풀고 싶어 할 그런 보상을 그들이 받았으면 좋겠어요!\" 흡족해진 바델 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"그 말씀에 아멘입니다.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"그러면 그자들은 거기서 살지고 행복한 생계를 얻게 될 테니까요! 그럼 안녕히 주무십시오, 숙녀 여러분.\"\n\n샌더스 부인으로서는 무척 다행스럽게도, 샘은 안주인 쪽에서 족발이나 구운 치즈에 관해 아무런 언급도 없이 떠나도록 허락받았다. 그리고 그 음식들에는, 바델 도련님이 거들 수 있는 어린아이다운 도움까지 더해, 숙녀들이 곧이어 더없이 충분한 정당한 대접을 해 주었다—실로 그것들은 그들의 맹렬한 분투 앞에 깡그리 사라져 버렸다.\n\n웰러 씨는 조지 앤드 벌처로 발걸음을 되돌려, 바델 부인 댁을 방문하는 동안 용케 알아낸 도드슨과 포그의 교활한 술책의 정황들을 주인에게 충실히 전했다. 이튿날 퍼커 씨와의 면담은 웰러 씨의 진술을 충분히 확증하고도 남았다. 그리하여 픽윅 씨는, 두어 석 달 뒤에는 혼인 약속 위반으로 인해 입은 손해에 대한 배상 청구 소송이 민사 법원에서 공개적으로 재판에 부쳐지리라는 유쾌한 예상을 안고, 딩글리 델에서의 크리스마스 방문을 준비할 수밖에 없었다. 원고는 정황의 위력에서 비롯되는 온갖 이점뿐 아니라, 거기에 더해 도드슨과 포그의 교활한 술책에서 비롯되는 이점까지 모조리 누리고 있었다.\n\n픽윅 회원들이 딩글리 델로 출발하기로 약속한 날까지는 아직 이틀의 여유가 남아 있었으므로, 웰러 씨는 이른 점심을 먹은 뒤 조지 앤드 벌처의 뒷방에 자리를 잡고 앉아 자신의 시간을 가장 잘 보낼 방도를 곰곰이 궁리하였다. 유난히 화창한 날이었다. 그런데 그 문제를 마음속으로 십 분도 채 굴려 보기 전에, 그는 갑자기 효심과 애정에 사로잡혔다. 마땅히 내려가서 아버지를 뵙고 새어머니께 도리를 다해야 한다는 생각이 어찌나 강하게 떠오르던지, 이제껏 이런 도덕적 의무를 한 번도 떠올려 보지 못한 자신의 게으름에 그는 그만 망연자실하고 말았다. 한 시간이라도 더 미루지 않고 지난날의 소홀함을 메우려는 마음이 간절하여, 그는 곧장 위층 픽윅 씨에게로 걸어 올라가 이 갸륵한 목적을 위한 휴가를 청하였다.\n\n\"물론이지, 샘, 물론이고말고,\" 픽윅 씨는 자기 시중꾼에게서 이런 효심의 발로가 나타난 것에 기뻐서 두 눈을 반짝이며 말하였다. \"물론이지, 샘.\"\n\n웰러 씨는 고마워하며 고개를 숙여 인사하였다.\n\n\"네가 아들로서의 도리를 그토록 높이 여기는 것을 보니 나는 무척 기쁘구나, 샘,\" 픽윅 씨가 말하였다.\n\n\"늘 그래 왔습니다요, 나리,\" 웰러 씨가 대답하였다.\n\n\"그것 참 흐뭇한 생각이로구나, 샘,\" 픽윅 씨가 흡족해하며 말하였다.\n\n\"그렇습죠, 나리,\" 웰러 씨가 대답하였다. \"저는 아버지한테 뭐가 필요하면 늘 아주 공손하고 싹싹하게 청을 드렸습니다요. 그래도 안 주시면, 그걸 못 가져서 무슨 나쁜 짓이라도 저지를까 봐 제가 그냥 가져왔습죠. 이런 식으로 저는 아버지의 골칫거리를 엄청나게 덜어 드렸습니다요, 나리.\"\n\n\"내가 말한 건 꼭 그런 뜻이 아니었다, 샘,\" 픽윅 씨가 가벼운 미소를 띠며 고개를 저었다.\n\n\"다 좋은 마음에서 한 거죠, 나리—더할 나위 없이 좋은 뜻이었습니다요, 자기 곁에서 마누라가 불행해 보인다고 마누라를 버리고 도망친 신사 양반이 한 말처럼요,\" 웰러 씨가 대답하였다.\n\n\"가도 좋다, 샘,\" 픽윅 씨가 말하였다.\n\n\"고맙습니다요, 나리,\" 웰러 씨가 대답하였다. 그리고 정중하게 절을 올리고 가장 좋은 옷을 차려입은 뒤, 샘은 어런들 마차 지붕 위에 자리를 잡고 도킹을 향해 길을 떠났다.\n\n마퀴스 오브 그랜비는 웰러 부인이 운영하던 시절에 더 나은 부류의 길가 선술집의 본보기라 할 만한 곳이었다—편리할 만큼은 충분히 크고, 아늑할 만큼은 충분히 작았다. 길 건너편에는 높은 기둥에 큼직한 간판이 걸려 있었는데, 거기에는 짙은 파란 깃을 단 붉은 외투 차림에, 중풍 든 듯한 얼굴을 한 신사의 머리와 어깨가 그려져 있었고, 삼각모 위로도 같은 파란빛을 살짝 칠해 하늘을 표현해 놓았다. 그 위로는 다시 깃발 한 쌍이 있었고, 외투의 맨 아랫단추 밑에는 대포 두 문이 자리했으니, 그 전체가 영광스러운 기억 속의 마퀴스 오브 그랜비를 표현한, 의심할 바 없이 생생한 초상이었다. 술집 창문에는 제라늄 화분이 정성껏 골라 진열되어 있었고, 먼지 하나 없이 닦인 양주병들이 한 줄로 늘어서 있었다. 활짝 열린 덧문에는 좋은 침대와 산뜻한 포도주를 칭송하는 금빛 글귀가 갖가지로 적혀 있었으며, 마구간 문과 말 물통 주위를 어슬렁거리는 시골 사람들과 마부들의 그럴듯한 무리는 그 안에서 파는 에일과 양주가 일품이라는 사실을 그럴싸하게 증언하고 있었다. 샘 웰러는 마차에서 내리며 잠시 멈춰 서서, 노련한 여행자의 눈으로 장사가 잘되고 있음을 보여 주는 이 자잘한 징표들을 모두 살폈다. 그러고 나서 자기가 본 모든 것에 흡족해하며 곧장 안으로 들어섰다.\n\n\"자, 이봐요!\" 샘이 문 안으로 머리를 들이미는 순간, 날카로운 여자 목소리가 들려왔다. \"뭘 원하는 거예요, 젊은이?\"\n\n샘은 목소리가 나는 쪽으로 둘러보았다. 그것은 편안해 보이는 인상의 다소 통통한 부인에게서 나온 것이었는데, 부인은 술집 벽난로 곁에 앉아 차 끓일 주전자 물을 데우려고 불을 불고 있었다. 부인은 혼자가 아니었다. 벽난로 반대편 등받이 높은 의자에 꼿꼿이 앉아 있는 사내가 있었으니, 닳아빠진 검은 옷을 입은 그자는 등이 의자 등받이만큼이나 길고 뻣뻣해서, 단번에 샘의 각별하고도 특별한 주목을 끌었다.\n\n그는 새침한 얼굴에 코가 붉고, 길고 여윈 인상에다 반쯤 방울뱀 같은 눈—다소 날카로우면서도 분명히 음흉한 눈—을 지닌 사내였다. 그는 아주 짧은 바지에 검은 무명 양말을 신고 있었는데, 그 양말은 나머지 옷가지와 마찬가지로 유난히 낡아빠져 있었다. 그의 표정은 빳빳하게 풀먹인 듯했으나, 흰 목도리만은 그렇지 못해, 그 길고 축 처진 끝자락이 단단히 단추 채운 조끼 위로 아주 보기 흉하고 멋없게 늘어져 있었다. 낡고 닳은 비버 가죽 장갑 한 켤레와 챙 넓은 모자, 그리고 빛바랜 초록색 우산—맨 위에 손잡이가 없는 것을 메우기라도 하려는 듯 바닥으로는 고래수염이 잔뜩 삐져나온 우산—이 그 곁의 의자 위에 놓여 있었다. 그것들이 아주 단정하고 조심스럽게 정돈되어 있는 것을 보니, 이 붉은 코 사내가 누구든 간에 서둘러 떠날 생각은 없는 듯하였다.\n\n붉은 코 사내를 공정하게 평하자면, 그가 그런 생각을 품었다면 그건 결코 현명한 일이 못 되었을 것이다. 무릇 모든 정황으로 미루어 보건대, 그가 다른 어디에서든 이보다 더 편안할 거라 기대할 수 있었다면, 그는 더없이 바람직한 교우 관계를 누리고 있었음에 틀림없으니 말이다. 풀무질 덕분에 불은 환하게 타오르고 있었고, 그 둘의 영향으로 주전자는 흥겹게 노래하고 있었다. 작은 쟁반에 찻그릇이 차려져 있었고, 뜨겁게 버터 바른 토스트 한 접시가 불 앞에서 부드럽게 지글거리고 있었다. 그리고 붉은 코 사내는 긴 놋쇠 토스트 포크를 매개 삼아, 두툼한 빵 한 조각을 바로 그 먹음직스러운 음식으로 바꾸는 일에 분주하였다. 그의 곁에는 김이 무럭무럭 나는 뜨거운 파인애플 럼주 물 한 잔이 레몬 한 조각과 함께 놓여 있었는데, 붉은 코 사내는 토스트가 어떻게 구워지는지 살피려고 빵 한 바퀴를 눈앞으로 가져올 때마다, 그 뜨거운 파인애플 럼주 물을 한두 모금씩 들이켜고는, 불을 불고 있는 다소 통통한 부인을 향해 미소를 지었다.\n\n샘은 이 안락한 광경을 들여다보는 데 어찌나 빠져 있었던지, 다소 통통한 부인의 첫 질문을 흘려듣고 말았다. 그 질문이 두 번이나 되풀이되고, 그때마다 더 날카로운 목소리로 거듭되고 나서야, 그는 비로소 자기 행동이 무례했음을 깨달았다.\n\n\"주인장 계십니까?\" 샘이 그 물음에 대답 삼아 물었다.\n\n\"아니요, 안 계세요,\" 웰러 부인이 대답하였다. 그 다소 통통한 부인이란 다름 아닌, 세상을 떠난 클라크 씨의 옛 미망인이자 유일한 유언집행인이었다. \"아니요, 안 계시고, 오시리라 기대하지도 않아요.\"\n\n\"오늘 마차 몰고 올라오시지 않습니까요?\" 샘이 말하였다.\n\n\"올지도 모르고, 안 올지도 모르죠,\" 웰러 부인이 붉은 코 사내가 막 구워 낸 토스트에 버터를 바르며 대답하였다. \"나는 모르고, 게다가 알 바도 아니에요.—축복 기도를 올려 주세요, 스티긴스 씨.\"\n\n붉은 코 사내는 청을 받은 대로 하더니, 곧장 무서운 식탐으로 토스트를 먹기 시작하였다.\n\n붉은 코 사내의 생김새는 샘으로 하여금 첫눈에, 그자가 자신의 존경하는 아버지가 말한 적 있는 그 부목자(副牧者)가 아닐까 하는 의심을 절반 넘게 품게 하였다. 그자가 먹는 모습을 보는 순간, 그 점에 관한 모든 의심이 사라지고, 샘은 곧 만약 자기가 지금 있는 이곳에 잠시 머물 작정이라면 지체 없이 자기 자리를 단단히 굳혀 두어야 함을 깨달았다. 그래서 그는 술집 반쪽 문 너머로 팔을 뻗어 태연하게 빗장을 풀고는 느긋하게 안으로 걸어 들어가는 것으로 일을 시작하였다.\n\n\"새어머니,\" 샘이 말하였다. \"안녕하셨습니까?\"\n\n\"어머, 이 사람 정말 웰러 집안 사람이네!\" 웰러 부인이 그다지 반갑지 않은 표정으로 샘의 얼굴을 올려다보며 말하였다.\n\n\"저도 그런 것 같습니다요,\" 태연한 샘이 말하였다. \"그리고 이 점잖으신 목사 양반께서 부디 너그러이 봐주시기 바랍니다만, 저는 차라리 제가 어머니를 차지한 바로 그 웰러였으면 좋겠습니다요, 새어머니.\"\n\n이것은 일거양득의 찬사였다. 웰러 부인이 더없이 매력적인 여인이라는 뜻이면서, 동시에 스티긴스 씨가 성직자다운 풍모를 지녔다는 뜻이기도 했다. 그것은 즉시 눈에 띄는 효과를 냈다. 그리고 샘은 새어머니에게 입을 맞추는 것으로 그 우세를 한층 밀어붙였다.\n\n\"저리 비켜요!\" 웰러 부인이 그를 밀어내며 말하였다.\n\n\"부끄러운 줄 아시오, 젊은이!\" 붉은 코 신사가 말하였다.\n\n\"기분 상하시게 할 뜻은 없었습니다요, 나리, 정말입니다요,\" 샘이 대답하였다. \"그렇지만 나리 말씀이 지당하십니다요. 새어머니가 젊고 인물이 고우실 때는 그게 마땅한 일이 아니지요, 안 그렇습니까요, 나리?\"\n\n\"모든 것이 헛되도다,\" 스티긴스 씨가 말하였다.\n\n\"아, 그렇고말고요,\" 웰러 부인이 모자를 바로잡으며 말하였다.\n\n샘도 그렇다고 생각했지만, 입을 다물었다.\n\n부목자는 샘이 온 것이 도무지 달갑지 않은 눈치였다. 그리고 찬사의 첫 거품이 가라앉고 나자, 웰러 부인조차 샘이 없었더라면 조금도 불편할 게 없었으리라는 듯한 표정이었다. 그러나 어쨌든 그는 거기 있었고, 점잖게 내쫓을 수도 없는 노릇이라, 셋은 모두 자리에 앉아 차를 마셨다.\n\n\"그래, 아버지는 어떻습니까요?\" 샘이 말하였다.\n\n이 물음에, 웰러 부인은 마치 그 화제가 입에 올리기에 너무도 고통스럽다는 듯, 두 손을 들어 올리고 눈을 치떴다.\n\n스티긴스 씨가 신음 소리를 냈다.\n\n\"저 양반 무슨 일이랍니까요?\" 샘이 물었다.\n\n\"네 아버지가 처신하는 꼴에 충격을 받으신 거란다,\" 웰러 부인이 대답하였다.\n\n\"아, 그렇습니까요?\" 샘이 말하였다.\n\n\"게다가 그럴 만한 이유가 너무도 충분하지,\" 웰러 부인이 엄숙하게 덧붙였다.\n\n스티긴스 씨는 새 토스트 한 조각을 집어 들고 묵직하게 신음하였다.\n\n\"그 사람은 끔찍한 난봉꾼이야,\" 웰러 부인이 말하였다.\n\n\"진노의 사람이라!\" 스티긴스 씨가 외쳤다. 그는 토스트를 반원 모양으로 큼직하게 한 입 베어 물고는 다시 신음하였다.\n\n샘은 목사 스티긴스 씨에게 신음할 거리를 한번 만들어 주고 싶은 마음이 굴뚝같았으나, 그 충동을 억누르고 그저 이렇게만 물었다. \"영감님이 지금 무슨 짓을 하고 계십니까요?\"\n\n\"무슨 짓이라니, 원!\" 웰러 부인이 말하였다. \"오, 그이는 마음이 모질어. 밤이면 밤마다 이 훌륭한 분이—얼굴 찌푸리지 마세요, 스티긴스 씨. 당신은 정말 훌륭한 분이라고 나는 말할 거예요—여기 와서 몇 시간씩 앉아 계신데도, 그이한테는 눈곱만큼도 효과가 없으니.\"\n\n\"거참, 그것 이상하네요,\" 샘이 말하였다. \"제가 그 양반 처지였더라면 저한테는 아주 상당한 효과가 있었을 텐데 말입니다요. 그건 제가 압니다요.\"\n\n\"실은 말이오, 젊은 친구,\" 스티긴스 씨가 엄숙하게 말하였다. \"그분은 완고한 가슴을 지녔소이다. 오, 젊은 친구여, 그분이 아니라면 그 누가, 우리의 가장 어여쁜 자매 열여섯 분의 간청을 뿌리치고, 서인도제도의 검둥이 아기들에게 플란넬 조끼와 도덕적 손수건을 마련해 주려는 우리의 고귀한 협회에 기부하라는 그분들의 권유를 끝까지 거부할 수 있었겠소?\"\n\n\"도덕적 손수건이라는 게 뭡니까요?\" 샘이 말하였다. \"저는 그런 가구 같은 물건은 본 적이 없는데요.\"\n\n\"오락과 교훈을 한데 아우른 것이라오, 젊은 친구,\" 스티긴스 씨가 대답하였다. \"엄선한 이야기에 목판화를 곁들인 것이지요.\"\n\n\"아, 알겠습니다요,\" 샘이 말하였다. \"포목점에다 걸어 놓는, 거지들 청원서 같은 게 잔뜩 박혀 있는 그런 거 말입니까요?\"\n\n스티긴스 씨는 세 번째 토스트를 먹기 시작하며 그렇다고 고개를 끄덕였다.\n\n\"그래서 그 부인들 말에도 넘어가지 않으셨다는 겁니까요?\" 샘이 말하였다.\n\n\"앉아서 파이프 담배만 피우면서, 검둥이 아기들이란—그이가 검둥이 아기들이 뭐라고 했더라?\" 웰러 부인이 말하였다.\n\n\"하찮은 사기꾼들이라 했지요,\" 스티긴스 씨가 깊이 마음 상한 표정으로 대답하였다.\n\n\"검둥이 아기들이 하찮은 사기꾼들이라 했다지,\" 웰러 부인이 되풀이하였다. 그리고 두 사람은 늙은 웰러 씨의 흉악한 행실에 함께 신음하였다.\n\n이와 비슷한 죄상들이 훨씬 더 많이 폭로될 수도 있었으나, 토스트가 다 떨어지고 차는 아주 싱거워진 데다, 샘이 갈 기색이라곤 조금도 내비치지 않자, 스티긴스 씨는 문득 자기가 목자와 더없이 긴급한 약속이 있음을 떠올리고 그에 따라 자리를 떠났다.\n\n찻그릇이 채 치워지고 난로 바닥이 쓸리기도 전에, 런던행 마차가 늙은 웰러 씨를 문간에 부려 놓았다. 그의 두 다리는 그를 술집 안에 부려 놓았고, 그의 두 눈은 그에게 아들을 보여 주었다.\n\n\"아니, 새미!\" 아버지가 외쳤다.\n\n\"아니, 영감님!\" 아들이 소리쳤다. 그리고 둘은 정답게 악수를 나누었다.\n\n\"너를 보니 참 반갑구나, 새미,\" 늙은 웰러 씨가 말하였다. \"그나저나 네가 새어머니를 어떻게 구워삶았는지는 나한테는 수수께끼야. 그 비법을 좀 적어 줬으면 좋겠다, 그뿐이야.\"\n\n\"쉿!\" 샘이 말하였다. \"어머니 집에 계세요, 영감님.\"\n\n\"들릴 데 있는 게 아니야,\" 웰러 씨가 대답하였다. \"네 어머니는 차 마신 뒤에 늘 아래층에 내려가서 두어 시간씩 화풀이를 하거든. 그러니 우리 한잔 축이자꾸나, 새미.\"\n\n이렇게 말하면서 웰러 씨는 양주에 물을 탄 술을 두 잔 만들고, 파이프 두 개를 꺼냈다. 아버지와 아들은 마주 보고 앉았으니, 샘은 불 한쪽 편의 등받이 높은 의자에 앉고, 늙은 웰러 씨는 반대편의 안락의자에 앉아, 둘은 마땅한 진중함을 갖추고 한껏 즐기기 시작하였다.\n\n\"누구 왔다 갔니, 새미?\" 늙은 웰러 씨가 한참 침묵하다가 무심하게 물었다.\n\n샘은 의미심장하게 고개를 끄덕여 그렇다고 답하였다.\n\n\"코 빨간 녀석이지?\" 웰러 씨가 물었다.\n\n샘은 다시 고개를 끄덕였다.\n\n\"붙임성 좋은 사람이지, 그 녀석,\" 웰러 씨가 거세게 담배를 피우며 말하였다.\n\n\"그런 것 같습디다요,\" 샘이 말하였다.\n\n\"셈에 밝은 친구야,\" 웰러 씨가 말하였다. \"그렇습니까요?\" 샘이 말하였다.\n\n\"월요일에 십팔 펜스를 꿔 가고는, 화요일에 와서 일 실링을 더해 반 크라운을 채우고, 수요일에 다시 와서 반 크라운을 더 받아 오 실링을 만들고, 그렇게 자꾸 곱절로 늘려 가다 보면 눈 깜짝할 새에 오 파운드짜리 지폐까지 채운다니까, 새미. 산수책에 나오는 그 말발굽 못 셈하는 문제처럼 말이야.\"\n\n샘은 아버지가 말하는 그 문제를 기억한다는 뜻으로 고개를 끄덕였다.\n\n\"그래서 플란넬 조끼에는 기부를 안 하셨군요?\" 한참 더 담배를 피우다가 샘이 말하였다.\n\n\"당연히 안 했지,\" 웰러 씨가 대답하였다. \"저 멀리 있는 어린 검둥이들한테 플란넬 조끼가 무슨 소용이람? 그렇지만 내가 무슨 말을 할까, 새미,\" 웰러 씨가 목소리를 낮추고 벽난로 너머로 몸을 기울이며 말하였다. \"집 안에 있는 어떤 사람들한테 구속복을 입히는 일이라면 내 아주 후하게 내놓겠다.\"\n\n* 구속복: 원문 strait veskits(straitwaistcoats), 광인을 묶어 두는 옷. 플란넬 조끼(flannel veskits)와 말장난.\n\n웰러 씨는 이렇게 말하며 천천히 본래 자세로 돌아오더니, 자기 맏아들에게 의미심장하게 한쪽 눈을 찡긋해 보였다.\n\n\"손수건 쓸 줄도 모르는 사람들한테 손수건을 보낸다는 건 확실히 이상한 짓 같습니다요,\" 샘이 말하였다.\n\"그자들은 늘 그런 식으로 무슨 수작을 부린다니까, 새미,\" 그의 아버지가 대답하였다. \"지난 일요일에 내가 한길을 걸어 올라가는데, 글쎄 누가 예배당 문간에 파란 수프 접시를 손에 들고 서 있나 했더니, 바로 네 새어머니였단 말이야! 그 접시 안에는 그때 일 파운드짜리 둘은 족히 거슬러 줄 만큼 잔돈이 들어 있었을걸, 새미, 죄다 반 페니짜리로 말이야. 그리고 사람들이 나오면서 그 안에 페니를 떨그럭떨그럭 던져 넣는데, 이제껏 구워 낸 그 어떤 접시도 그 닳고 부대끼는 걸 못 견딜 거라 싶을 정도였어. 그게 다 뭣 때문이었는지 알겠니?\"\n\n\"또 차 모임 하려는 거였겠죠,\" 샘이 말하였다.\n\n\"천만에,\" 아버지가 대답하였다. \"목자의 수도세 때문이었어, 새미.\"\n\n\"목자의 수도세요!\" 샘이 말하였다.\n\n\"그래,\" 웰러 씨가 대답하였다. \"세 분기치가 밀려 있었는데, 목자란 작자는 한 푼도 안 냈거든, 정말 안 냈어—어쩌면 물이 그 작자한테는 별 쓸모가 없어서 그랬는지도 모르지, 그 양반은 그 수도꼭지 물은 아주 조금밖에 안 마시니까, 새미, 아주 조금. 그 양반은 그보다 여섯 배는 나은 수작을 알고 있거든, 암. 어쨌든 돈을 안 내니까, 그래서 물을 끊어 버렸지. 목자가 예배당으로 내려가서는 자기가 박해받는 성도라고 떠벌리고, 물 끊은 수도 검침원의 마음이 누그러져서 올바른 길로 돌아서기를 바란다고 하면서도, 그자는 아무래도 불편한 데로 가도록 예약된 것 같다고 말하지. 그러자 여자들이 모임을 열어 찬송가를 부르고, 네 새어머니를 의장으로 뽑고, 다음 일요일에 헌금을 걷자고 자청해서는, 그걸 죄다 목자한테 넘겨준 거야. 그래서 그 작자가 그 여자들한테서 평생 수도회사에 거저 다닐 만큼 충분히 뜯어내지 못했다면,\" 웰러 씨가 결론지어 말하였다. \"내가 네덜란드 놈이고 너도 네덜란드 놈인 거다, 그뿐이야.\"\n* 네덜란드 놈: I'm a Dutchman, 영어 관용표현으로 \"그렇지 않다면 내 손에 장을 지진다\" 정도의 강한 부정.\n\n웰러 씨는 몇 분 동안 말없이 담배를 피우다가, 다시 입을 열었다—\n\n\"이 동네 목자들의 가장 못된 점은, 얘야, 그자들이 이 근방 젊은 아가씨들의 머리를 죄다 돌게 만든다는 거야. 가엾게도 그 어린것들은 다 옳은 일인 줄 알고 더 나은 걸 모르지. 하지만 그 애들은 수작의 희생자야, 새뮤얼, 수작의 희생자라고.\"\n\n\"그런 것 같습니다요,\" 샘이 말하였다.\n\n\"바로 그거지,\" 웰러 씨가 무겁게 고개를 저으며 말하였다. \"그리고 나를 약 오르게 하는 건, 새뮤얼, 그자들이 제 시간과 노력을 다 허비해 가며 필요로 하지도 않는 구릿빛 사람들한테 줄 옷을 만들면서, 정작 옷이 필요한 살빛 기독교인들은 거들떠보지도 않는 꼴을 보는 거야. 내 마음대로 할 수만 있다면, 새뮤얼, 이 게으른 목자 놈들을 무거운 외바퀴 손수레 뒤에 매달아 놓고, 폭 십사 인치짜리 널빤지 위를 하루 종일 오르락내리락 굴려 댈 텐데. 그러면 그 헛소리가 쏙 빠질 거야, 뭐라도 빠진다면 말이지.\"\n\n웰러 씨는 이 점잖은 처방을, 강한 어조에다 갖가지 고갯짓과 눈을 찡그리는 표정까지 곁들여 늘어놓고는, 한입에 잔을 비우고, 타고난 위엄을 갖추어 파이프의 재를 털어 냈다.\n\n그가 이 일을 하고 있을 때, 복도에서 날카로운 목소리가 들려왔다.\n\n\"네 사랑하는 친척분이 오신다, 새미,\" 웰러 씨가 말하자, 웰러 부인이 황급히 방으로 들어왔다.\n\n\"오, 돌아오셨군요, 그래요!\" 웰러 부인이 말하였다.\n\n\"그래, 여보,\" 웰러 씨가 새 파이프에 담배를 채우며 대답하였다.\n\n\"스티긴스 씨는 다시 다녀가셨나요?\" 웰러 부인이 말하였다.\n\n\"아니, 여보, 안 왔어,\" 웰러 씨가 옆 난로에서 시뻘겋게 단 석탄 한 조각을 부젓가락 사이에 끼워 파이프 통에 갖다 대는 기발한 방법으로 불을 붙이며 대답하였다. \"게다가 말이지, 여보, 그자가 영영 안 돌아온다 해도 나는 그럭저럭 견디며 살아남을 거요.\"\n\n\"으, 이 망할 인간!\" 웰러 부인이 말하였다.\n\n\"고맙구려, 내 사랑,\" 웰러 씨가 말하였다.\n\n\"자, 자, 아버지,\" 샘이 말하였다. \"남들 앞에서 그런 알콩달콩한 짓 그만하세요. 저기 목사 양반이 지금 들어오십니다요.\"\n\n이 알림에, 웰러 부인은 막 짜내기 시작한 눈물을 황급히 닦아 냈다. 그리고 웰러 씨는 부루퉁하게 자기 의자를 굴뚝 모퉁이로 끌어당겼다.\n\n스티긴스 씨는 뜨거운 파인애플 럼주 물을 한 잔 더, 또 두 잔째, 세 잔째 들도록 쉽게 꾀임에 넘어갔고, 그런 다음 다시 시작하기에 앞서 가벼운 저녁식사로 원기를 돋우었다. 그는 늙은 웰러 씨와 같은 편에 앉았다. 그리고 아내 눈에 띄지 않게 그렇게 할 수 있을 때마다, 웰러 씨는 부목자의 머리 위로 주먹을 흔들어 자기 가슴속에 숨은 감정을 아들에게 내비쳤다. 이 동작은 아들에게 더없이 순수한 기쁨과 만족을 안겨 주었으니, 특히 스티긴스 씨가 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 전혀 모른 채 조용히 뜨거운 파인애플 럼주 물을 들이켜고 있었기에 더욱 그러하였다.\n\n대화의 대부분은 웰러 부인과 목사 스티긴스 씨에게 국한되었다. 그리고 주로 늘어놓은 화제란 목자의 덕성, 그 양 떼의 갸륵함, 그리고 그 밖의 모든 사람들의 중대한 죄악과 비행이었으니—이런 일장연설을, 늙은 웰러 씨는 워커라는 이름의 신사에 대한 반쯤 억눌린 언급이나 그와 비슷한 갖가지 곁말로 이따금 끼어들어 가로막았다.\n* 워커(Walker): 당대 영어 속어로, 믿기 힘든 헛소리에 대한 야유성 추임새.\n\n마침내 스티긴스 씨는 자기가 편안히 감당할 수 있는 만큼의 파인애플 럼주 물을 이미 몸에 채웠다는, 의심할 여지 없는 여러 징후를 보이며 모자를 집어 들고 작별을 고하였다. 그리고 그 직후 샘은 아버지의 안내를 받아 잠자리로 갔다. 점잖은 노신사는 아들의 손을 뜨겁게 부여잡고, 무언가 한마디 일러 주려는 기색이었으나, 웰러 부인이 그를 향해 다가오자 그 뜻을 접은 듯, 불쑥 잘 자라는 인사를 건넸다.\n\n이튿날 샘은 일찌감치 일어나, 서둘러 아침을 먹고 런던으로 돌아갈 채비를 하였다. 그가 미처 집 밖으로 발을 내딛기도 전에, 아버지가 그의 앞에 서 있었다.\n\n\"가는 거냐, 새미?\" 웰러 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"곧장 떠납니다요,\" 샘이 대답하였다.\n\n\"저 스티긴스란 놈을 자루에 처넣어서 너랑 같이 데려갔으면 좋겠다,\" 웰러 씨가 말하였다.\n\n\"부끄러운 줄 아세요!\" 샘이 나무라듯 말하였다. \"대체 왜 그 작자가 마퀴스 오브 그랜비에 그 빨간 코를 들이밀도록 내버려 두시는 겁니까요?\"\n\n늙은 웰러 씨는 아들에게 진지한 눈길을 던지고 대답하였다. \"내가 결혼한 몸이니까 그러는 거야, 새뮤얼, 내가 결혼한 몸이니까. 너도 결혼한 몸이 되면, 새뮤얼, 지금은 모르는 많은 것들을 알게 될 거야. 하지만 그렇게 많은 걸 겪어 가면서 그토록 적은 걸 배우는 게 과연 그만한 값어치가 있는지는, 자선학교 아이가 알파벳 끝까지 다 떼고 나서 한 말마따나, 취향 나름인 게지. 내 생각엔 아무래도 값어치가 없는 것 같다만.\"\n\n\"자,\" 샘이 말하였다. \"안녕히 계세요.\"\n\n\"잘 가라, 잘 가, 새미,\" 그의 아버지가 대답하였다.\n\n\"저는 이 말씀만 드리겠습니다요,\" 샘이 우뚝 멈춰 서며 말하였다. \"제가 만약 마퀴스 오브 그랜비의 주인이고, 그 스티긴스란 놈이 와서 제 술집에서 토스트나 굽고 있다면, 저라면—\"\n\n\"뭘?\" 웰러 씨가 잔뜩 조바심을 내며 끼어들었다. \"뭘 어쩐다고?\"\n\n\"그놈 럼주 물에 독을 타겠습니다요,\" 샘이 말하였다.\n\n\"안 돼!\" 웰러 씨가 아들의 손을 와락 흔들며 말하였다. \"정말로 그러겠니, 새미—정말 그럴 테냐?\"\n\n\"그러겠습니다요,\" 샘이 말하였다. \"그래도 처음부터 너무 모질게 굴진 않겠습니다요. 일단 물통에 빠뜨리고 뚜껑을 덮어 두죠. 그래서 그놈이 친절에도 꿈쩍 않는다 싶으면, 그때 다른 방법으로 설득해 보겠습니다요.\"\n\n늙은 웰러 씨는 아들에게 깊고도 형언할 수 없는 감탄의 눈길을 보내고는, 다시 한번 그 손을 부여잡은 뒤, 자기 충고가 불러일으킨 갖가지 상념을 마음속으로 곱씹으며 천천히 걸어갔다.\n\n샘은 아버지가 한길 모퉁이를 돌아 사라질 때까지 그 뒷모습을 바라보았다. 그러고 나서 런던으로 걷는 길에 나섰다. 그는 처음에는 자기 충고가 가져올 법한 결과와, 아버지가 그것을 받아들일 가능성에 대해 곰곰이 생각하였다. 그러나 그는 오직 시간만이 알려 주리라는 위안 어린 생각으로 그 문제를 머릿속에서 떨쳐 냈다. 그리고 이것이야말로 우리가 독자에게 새겨 두고 싶은 생각이기도 하다.\n\n제28장. 결혼식에 관한 기록과 그 밖의 몇 가지 놀이를 담은 유쾌한 크리스마스 장이니, 그 놀이들은 그 나름으로 결혼 자체만큼이나 좋은 풍습이지만, 이 타락한 시대에는 그만큼 경건하게 지켜지지는 않는다\n\n요정처럼 가뿐하지는 않았더라도 꿀벌만큼이나 활기차게, 네 명의 픽윅 회원들은 12월 22일 아침에 모여들었다. 충실히 기록된 그들의 모험이 시작되고 이루어진 바로 그 은총의 해였다. 크리스마스가 그 무뚝뚝하고도 정 깊은 정직함을 온통 띤 채 코앞에 다가와 있었다. 그것은 환대와 즐거움과 활짝 열린 마음의 계절이었다. 묵은해는 마치 옛 철학자처럼 벗들을 둘레에 불러 모으고, 잔치와 환락의 소리 가운데서 부드럽고 고요하게 스러질 채비를 하고 있었다. 흥겹고 즐거운 때였으니, 그 도래로 기뻐진 수많은 가슴들 가운데 적어도 네 가슴은 참으로 흥겹고 즐거웠다.\n\n그리고 실로 크리스마스가 짧은 행복과 기쁨의 한 철을 가져다주는 가슴들은 수없이 많다. 삶의 쉼 없는 분투 속에서 식구들이 멀리멀리 흩어지고 떨어졌던 그 많은 가정들이 이때 다시 모여, 그토록 순수하고 티 없는 기쁨의 원천이 되는, 그리고 세상의 근심과 슬픔과는 도무지 어울리지 않는 그 행복한 동무됨과 서로를 향한 선의의 상태 속에서 다시 한 번 만난다. 그 기쁨은 세상의 근심과 슬픔과 너무도 맞지 않기에, 가장 문명화된 나라들의 종교적 신앙도, 가장 거친 야만인들의 투박한 전승도, 한결같이 그것을 복되고 행복한 자들을 위해 마련된 내세의 첫 기쁨들 가운데 하나로 꼽는다. 크리스마스철은 얼마나 많은 옛 추억을, 또 얼마나 많은 잠든 정을 일깨우는가!\n\n우리는 지금 이 글을 쓰고 있는바, 해마다 그날이면 우리가 흥겹고 즐거운 무리를 이루어 만나던 그 자리로부터 수십 리 떨어진 곳에 있다. 그때 그토록 흥겹게 두근거리던 많은 가슴들이 이제는 멎었고, 그때 그토록 환히 빛나던 많은 눈길들이 이제는 광채를 잃었으며, 우리가 맞잡았던 손들은 차갑게 식었고, 우리가 찾던 눈들은 무덤 속에 그 광채를 감추었다. 그러나 그 옛집, 그 방, 흥겨운 목소리들과 미소 짓던 얼굴들, 농담과 웃음, 그 행복한 만남에 얽힌 더없이 사소하고 자질구레한 정황들이, 그 계절이 돌아올 적마다 마치 마지막 모임이 어제 일이었던 것처럼 우리 마음에 밀려든다! 행복하고 행복한 크리스마스여, 우리를 어린 시절의 헛된 꿈으로 되돌려 줄 수 있고, 노인에게 젊은 날의 즐거움을 되살려 줄 수 있으며, 수천 리 떨어진 뱃사람과 나그네를 제 화롯가와 고요한 집으로 실어다 줄 수 있는 크리스마스여!\n\n그러나 우리는 이 성자 크리스마스의 미덕에 너무도 사로잡히고 빠져들어, 픽윅 씨와 그 친구들을 머글턴행 마차 바깥 추위 속에 세워 둔 채 기다리게 하고 있다. 그들은 외투며 숄이며 목도리로 단단히 싸맨 채 방금 그 마차에 막 올라탄 참이다. 여행 가방과 융단 가방은 다 실어 두었고, 웰러 씨와 차장은 앞칸에 엄청나게 큰 대구 한 마리를 밀어 넣으려 애쓰고 있다. 그 대구는 앞칸에 비해 몇 치수나 더 컸는데, 긴 갈색 바구니 속에 짚을 한 켜 덮어 아늑하게 담겨 있었고, 맨 마지막에 싣도록 남겨 두었으니, 픽윅 씨의 소유인 진짜 자연산 굴 여섯 통이 그 칸 바닥에 가지런히 정돈되어 있는 그 위에 안전하게 누여 두기 위함이었다. 웰러 씨와 차장이 대구를 앞칸에 밀어 넣으려고, 머리부터 넣어 보고, 다음엔 꼬리부터, 다음엔 위가 위로 가게, 다음엔 아래가 위로 가게, 다음엔 옆으로, 다음엔 길게 눕혀 가며 온갖 수를 다 쓰는 동안, 픽윅 씨의 낯빛에 떠오른 관심은 더없이 강렬했다. 그 굽힘 없는 대구는 그 모든 술책에 완강히 버텼으나, 마침내 차장이 우연히 바구니 한복판을 들이치자 대구는 별안간 앞칸 속으로 사라졌고, 그와 함께 차장 자신의 머리와 어깨도 같이 사라졌다. 대구의 소극적 저항이 그토록 갑작스레 멎으리라곤 미처 헤아리지 못했던 그는 자못 뜻밖의 충격을 겪었으니, 짐꾼들과 구경꾼들 모두는 도저히 참을 수 없는 환희에 빠졌다. 이에 픽윅 씨는 자못 너그러운 마음으로 미소 지으며 조끼 주머니에서 1실링을 꺼내, 앞칸에서 기어 나오는 차장에게 뜨거운 브랜디 물 한 잔으로 제 건강을 위해 마셔 달라고 청했다. 그 말에 차장도 미소 짓고, 스노드그래스 씨와 윈클 씨와 텁먼 씨도 다 같이 미소 지었다. 차장과 웰러 씨는 오 분쯤 사라졌는데, 십중팔구 뜨거운 브랜디 물을 마시러 간 것이었다. 돌아왔을 때 그 냄새가 자못 진동했기 때문이다. 마부가 마부석에 올라타고, 웰러 씨가 뒤로 뛰어오르고, 픽윅 회원들이 외투를 다리에 두르고 숄을 코까지 끌어 올리고, 일꾼들이 말 덮개를 벗기고, 마부가 기운차게 \"출발!\" 하고 외치자 그들은 떠나갔다.\n\n그들은 거리를 덜컹덜컹 지나고 돌길 위를 흔들흔들 넘어, 마침내 드넓고 탁 트인 시골에 다다랐다. 바퀴들이 단단하고 서리 내린 땅 위를 미끄러져 갔고, 채찍이 날카롭게 한 번 울리자 말들은 구보로 내달아, 마치 그 뒤에 실린 짐 ― 마차며 승객이며 대구며 굴통이며 죄다 ― 이 제 발꿈치에 매달린 깃털 한 올에 지나지 않는다는 듯 길을 따라 성큼성큼 나아갔다. 그들은 완만한 비탈을 내려가 단단한 대리석 덩이처럼 다부지고 메마른, 이 마일이나 뻗은 평지로 들어섰다. 채찍이 또 한 번 울리자 그들은 날쌘 질주로 내달았고, 말들은 그 빠른 움직임에 신이 난 듯 머리를 흔들고 마구를 절그럭거렸다. 한편 마부는 채찍과 고삐를 한 손에 쥔 채 다른 손으로 모자를 벗어 무릎에 얹고는 손수건을 꺼내 이마를 닦았으니, 한편으로는 그것이 버릇이기 때문이요, 한편으로는 자기가 얼마나 태평한지, 그리고 자기만큼 숙달이 되면 사두마차를 모는 게 얼마나 쉬운 일인지를 승객들에게 보여 주는 편이 좋기 때문이었다. 이 일을 자못 느긋하게 마치고 나서(안 그러면 그 효과가 크게 줄어들 테니), 그는 손수건을 도로 넣고, 모자를 쓰고, 장갑을 매만지고, 팔꿈치를 떡 벌리고, 다시 채찍을 울리니, 그들은 전보다 더 흥겹게 내달았다.\n\n길 양편에 드문드문 흩어진 자그마한 집 몇 채가 어느 읍이나 마을의 어귀임을 알렸다. 차장의 건반 나팔에서 흘러나오는 경쾌한 가락이 맑고 차가운 공기 속에 울려 안에 탄 노신사를 깨웠으니, 그는 창틀을 조심스레 절반쯤 내리고 공기를 향해 보초라도 서듯 잠깐 밖을 내다보고는, 다시 조심스레 그것을 올리며 같이 안에 탄 다른 이에게 곧 말을 바꿔 맬 거라고 일러 주었다. 그 말에 다른 이는 잠을 깨고는 다음 낮잠은 정차 후로 미루기로 작정했다. 또다시 나팔이 우렁차게 울려 오두막집 아낙과 아이들을 일깨우니, 그들은 집 문가로 빼꼼 내다보며 마차가 모퉁이를 돌 때까지 지켜보다가, 다시금 활활 타는 불가에 둘러앉아 아버지가 집에 올 것에 대비해 장작 한 개비를 더 던져 넣었다. 한편 아버지 자신은 족히 일 마일 떨어진 곳에서 막 마부와 다정하게 고개를 끄덕여 인사를 나누고는, 휭 하니 내달아 가는 마차를 오래도록 물끄러미 바라보려고 몸을 돌린 참이었다.\n\n이제 마차가 어느 시골 읍의 험하게 깔린 거리를 덜컹덜컹 지나는 동안 나팔이 경쾌한 가락을 연주했다. 마부는 고삐들을 한데 묶어 두는 버클을 풀어, 멈추는 순간 곧바로 고삐를 벗어 던질 채비를 했다. 픽윅 씨는 외투 깃에서 얼굴을 내밀고 자못 호기심 어린 눈으로 주위를 둘러보았다. 그것을 알아챈 마부가 픽윅 씨에게 그 읍의 이름을 일러 주고 어제가 장날이었다고 말해 주니, 픽윅 씨는 그 두 가지 정보를 동행한 승객들에게 그대로 전했다. 그러자 그들도 외투 깃에서 얼굴을 내밀고 역시 주위를 둘러보았다. 한쪽 다리를 허공에 늘어뜨린 채 맨 가장자리에 앉아 있던 윈클 씨는, 마차가 치즈 가게 옆 날카로운 모퉁이를 휙 돌아 장터로 접어들 때 하마터면 거리로 곤두박질칠 뻔했다. 그의 옆에 앉은 스노드그래스 씨가 놀란 가슴을 채 진정하기도 전에, 마차는 새 말들이 덮개를 두른 채 이미 기다리고 있는 여관 마당에 멈춰 섰다. 마부가 고삐를 내던지고 제가 먼저 내려서고, 다른 바깥쪽 승객들도 따라 내렸다. 다만 다시 올라탈 자신이 없는 이들은 빼고였으니, 그들은 제자리에 남아 발을 마차에 굴러 몸을 녹였고 ― 갈망 어린 눈과 빨개진 코로, 여관 술청의 환한 불과 창을 장식한 빨간 열매 달린 호랑가시나무 가지를 바라보았다.\n\n그러나 차장은 가죽끈으로 어깨에 걸친 자그마한 주머니에서 꺼낸 갈색 종이 꾸러미를 곡물상에게 건네주었고, 말들을 조심스레 매는 것을 지켜보았으며, 런던에서 마차 지붕에 실어 온 안장을 보도에 내던졌고, 지난 화요일에 오른쪽 앞다리를 다친 잿빛 암말에 관해 마부와 마부 조수가 나누는 의논을 거들었다. 이제 그와 웰러 씨는 뒤쪽에서 만반의 준비가 되었고, 마부는 앞쪽에서 만반의 준비가 되었고, 그동안 내내 창을 꼬박 두 치나 내려 두고 있던 안쪽 노신사는 그것을 다시 올렸고, 덮개도 벗겨졌으니, \"건장한 두 신사\"만 빼고는 모두 출발 준비가 끝났다. 마부는 그 두 사람을 다소 조바심을 내며 찾았다. 이에 마부와 차장과 샘 웰러와 윈클 씨와 스노드그래스 씨와 마부 조수들 전부와, 다 합친 수보다도 많은 빈둥거리는 구경꾼들 하나하나가 사라진 신사들을 목청껏 불러 댔다. 마당 쪽에서 멀리 응답이 들려오더니, 픽윅 씨와 텁먼 씨가 숨을 헐떡이며 그리로 달려 내려왔다. 둘이 각각 에일 한 잔씩을 들이켠 데다, 픽윅 씨의 손가락이 어찌나 시렸던지 그 값을 치를 6펜스를 찾는 데만 꼬박 오 분이 걸린 탓이었다. 마부가 타이르듯 \"자 그럼, 신사 양반들\" 하고 외치자 차장이 그것을 따라 외쳤다. 안쪽 노신사는 시간이 없는 줄 빤히 알면서도 사람들이 굳이 내리려 든다는 게 참으로 별난 일이라고 생각했다. 픽윅 씨가 한쪽으로 끙끙대며 올라타고, 텁먼 씨가 다른 쪽으로 올라타고, 윈클 씨가 \"출발!\" 하고 외치자 그들은 떠났다. 숄을 끌어 올리고, 외투 깃을 다시 여미고, 보도가 끝나고, 집들이 사라지고, 그들은 다시 한 번 탁 트인 길을 따라 내달았으니, 상쾌하고 맑은 공기가 얼굴에 불어와 그들 가슴속 깊은 곳까지 기쁘게 했다.\n\n머글턴 텔레그래프 마차로 딩글리 델로 향하는 픽윅 씨와 그 친구들의 여정은 이러했다. 그날 오후 세 시에 그들은 모두 블루 라이언 여관 계단 위에 높직하고 뽀송하게, 무사하고 멀쩡하게, 정정하고 기운차게 서 있었으니, 길에서 에일과 브랜디를 넉넉히 들이켠 덕에 대지를 쇠사슬로 옭아매고 나무며 산울타리에 아름다운 그물을 짜 놓는 서리에 능히 맞설 만했다. 픽윅 씨가 굴통을 세고 대구를 파내는 일을 감독하느라 분주하던 차에, 누군가 외투 자락을 살그머니 잡아당기는 것을 느꼈다. 돌아보니 이런 식으로 그의 주의를 끌려 한 그 사람은 다름 아닌 워들 씨의 총애받는 시동, 이 꾸밈없는 역사의 독자들에게 뚱보 소년이라는 별난 호칭으로 더 잘 알려진 바로 그 아이였다.\n\n\"아하!\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"아하!\" 뚱보 소년이 말했다.\n\n그렇게 말하면서 그는 대구에서 굴통으로 눈길을 옮기며 흐뭇하게 빙긋거렸다. 그는 전보다 더 뚱뚱해져 있었다.\n\n\"이런, 자네 혈색이 아주 좋아 보이는구먼, 젊은 친구.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"술청 불 바로 앞에서 자고 있었거든요.\" 뚱보 소년이 대답했다. 그는 한 시간 낮잠을 자는 동안 새 굴뚝 단지 빛깔로 달아올라 있었다. \"주인 어른이 짐을 댁으로 실어 가라고 저를 마차에 태워 보내셨어요. 안장말도 몇 마리 보내려고 하셨는데, 추운 날이니 걸으시는 편이 낫겠다고 생각하셨어요.\"\n\n\"그래, 그래.\" 픽윅 씨가 서둘러 말했으니, 지난번에 거의 같은 길을 어떻게 지났던가가 떠올랐던 것이다. \"그래, 우리는 걷는 편이 낫겠네. 이보게, 샘!\"\n\n\"예, 나리.\" 웰러 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"워들 씨네 하인이 짐을 마차에 싣는 걸 거들고, 그런 다음 같이 타고 오게. 우리는 곧장 앞서 걸어가겠네.\"\n\n이렇게 일러두고 마부와 셈을 치른 뒤, 픽윅 씨와 세 친구는 들판을 가로지르는 오솔길로 접어들어 활기차게 걸어 떠났다. 웰러 씨와 뚱보 소년만 처음으로 단둘이 마주 보게 남겨 둔 채였다. 셈은 한마디 말도 없이 자못 놀란 눈으로 뚱보 소년을 바라보더니, 짐을 후딱후딱 마차에 챙겨 싣기 시작했다. 그동안 뚱보 소년은 곁에 가만히 서서, 웰러 씨가 혼자 일하는 꼴을 구경하는 게 자못 흥미로운 일이라도 되는 양 여기는 듯했다.\n\n\"자.\" 마지막 융단 가방을 던져 넣으며 셈이 말했다. \"다 됐다!\"\n\n\"그래요.\" 뚱보 소년이 자못 흡족한 투로 말했다. \"다 됐네요.\"\n\n\"이봐, 스무 관짜리 젊은 양반.\" 셈이 말했다. \"넌 정말이지 우량아 표본으로 딱이로구나!\"\n\n\"고맙수다.\" 뚱보 소년이 말했다.\n\n\"마음에 걸려서 끙끙대게 만드는 일 같은 건 없겠지, 응?\" 셈이 물었다.\n\n\"제가 알기론 없는데요.\" 뚱보 소년이 대답했다.\n\n\"널 보고 있자니, 난 차라리 네가 어느 처녀한테 짝사랑이라도 앓고 있는 줄로 알았다.\" 셈이 말했다.\n\n뚱보 소년이 고개를 가로저었다.\n\n\"이런.\" 셈이 말했다. \"그 말 들으니 다행이군. 넌 뭘 좀 마시기는 하냐?\"\n\n\"전 먹는 게 더 좋아요.\" 소년이 대답했다.\n\n\"아.\" 셈이 말했다. \"그럴 줄 알았다. 한데 내 말은, 몸을 좀 녹여 줄 만한 걸 한 방울 마시고 싶지 않냐는 거다. 하긴 너야 그 탄력 좋은 살집을 다 갖췄으니 추운 적이 없었겠지, 안 그러냐?\"\n\n\"가끔은요.\" 소년이 대답했다. \"그리고 좋은 거라면 한 방울쯤 마시는 거 좋아해요.\"\n\n\"오, 그러냐, 그래?\" 셈이 말했다. \"그럼 이리 와라!\"\n\n블루 라이언 술청에 곧 다다랐고, 뚱보 소년은 눈 하나 깜빡 않고 술 한 잔을 들이켰다. 그 솜씨에 웰러 씨의 호감이 적잖이 올라갔다. 웰러 씨도 제 몫으로 같은 일을 처리한 뒤, 둘은 마차에 올라탔다.\n\n\"몰 줄 알아요?\" 뚱보 소년이 말했다.\n\n\"그렇다고 봐야지.\" 셈이 대답했다.\n\n\"그럼 자요.\" 뚱보 소년이 셈의 손에 고삐를 쥐여 주고 골목 위쪽을 가리키며 말했다. \"곧장 가기만 하면 돼요. 절대 못 놓쳐요.\"\n\n이 말과 함께 뚱보 소년은 대구 옆에 다정스레 드러눕더니, 굴통 하나를 베개 삼아 머리 밑에 받치고는 그 자리에서 곧바로 잠들어 버렸다.\n\n\"이거 참.\" 셈이 말했다. \"내 평생 본 태평스런 녀석 중에서도 이 어린 양반이 제일 태평하네. 자, 일어나, 이 물오른 녀석아!\"\n\n그러나 물오른 녀석은 다시 살아날 기색을 도무지 보이지 않으니, 셈 웰러는 마차 앞쪽에 자리를 잡고 앉아 고삐를 한 번 홱 당겨 늙은 말을 출발시키고는, 매너 농장을 향해 꾸준히 덜컹덜컹 나아갔다.\n\n그러는 동안 픽윅 씨와 그 친구들은 걸어서 피를 활발히 돌게 한 뒤 즐겁게 나아갔다. 길은 단단했고, 풀은 빳빳하게 서리를 머금었으며, 공기는 곱고 메마른, 정신을 번쩍 들게 하는 찬 기운을 띠고 있었다. 그리고 잿빛 황혼(서리 내린 날씨엔 슬레이트 빛이라는 말이 더 낫겠다)이 빠르게 다가오니, 그들은 환대 잘하는 주인의 집에서 기다리고 있을 안락함을 흐뭇한 기대로 고대하게 되었다. 그것은 외딴 들판에서 노신사 둘이 순전히 마음의 가벼움과 흥겨움에 겨워 외투를 벗어 던지고 등 짚고 뛰어넘기 놀이라도 하게 만들 법한 그런 오후였다. 우리는 만약 그 순간 텁먼 씨가 \"등을 대 주마\" 하고 나섰더라면 픽윅 씨가 더없이 간절하게 그 제의를 받아들였으리라고 굳게 믿는다.\n\n그러나 텁먼 씨는 그런 편의를 자청하지 않았으니, 친구들은 흥겹게 담소하며 계속 걸었다. 그들이 건너야 할 어느 골목으로 접어들 때, 여러 목소리가 그들의 귀에 와락 밀려들었다. 그것이 누구의 목소리인지 짐작해 볼 겨를도 없이, 그들은 도착을 기다리고 있던 무리의 한복판으로 곧장 걸어 들어갔다. 그 사실은 그들이 시야에 들어선 순간 워들 영감의 입에서 터져 나온 우렁찬 \"만세!\" 소리로 픽윅 회원들에게 처음 알려졌다.\n\n먼저 워들 자신이 있었으니, 가능한 일이라면 전보다 더 명랑해 보였다. 다음엔 벨라와 그녀의 충실한 트런들이 있었고, 마지막으로 에밀리와 여덟이나 열쯤 되는 젊은 숙녀들이 있었다. 이들은 모두 이튿날 치러질 결혼식을 보러 내려와 있었고, 그런 중대한 행사 때 젊은 숙녀들이 으레 그렇듯 행복하고 자못 거드름 피우는 상태였다. 그들은 너 나 할 것 없이 멀고 넓게 들판과 골목을 그들의 장난과 웃음으로 뒤흔들고 있었다.\n\n그런 상황에서 소개의 격식은 아주 금세 치러졌으니, 차라리 아무런 격식도 없이 소개가 금세 끝나 버렸다고 말하는 편이 낫겠다. 그로부터 이 분 뒤, 픽윅 씨는 자기가 보고 있는 동안엔 디딤대를 넘어오려 하지 않는 ― 혹은 예쁜 발과 나무랄 데 없는 발목을 가진지라 맨 윗단에 오 분쯤 서 있는 편을 택하며 너무 무서워 꼼짝 못하겠다고 우기는 ― 젊은 숙녀들과, 마치 평생을 알고 지낸 사이인 양 거리낌이나 어색함 없이 편안하게 농을 주고받고 있었다. 또한 주목할 만한 일은, 스노드그래스 씨가 에밀리에게, 그 디딤대의 정녕 무서운 정도가(비록 그것이 족히 석 자 높이에 디딤돌이라곤 두어 개뿐이었지만) 요구할 법한 것보다 훨씬 더 많은 도움을 베풀었다는 것이다. 한편 위쪽에 모피가 둘린 자못 멋진 작은 부츠를 신은 어느 검은 눈의 젊은 숙녀는, 윈클 씨가 넘는 것을 도와주겠다고 나서자 자못 크게 비명을 지른 것이 목격되었다.\n\n이 모든 것이 자못 아늑하고 즐거웠다. 마침내 디딤대의 난관이 극복되고 그들이 다시 한 번 탁 트인 들판으로 들어섰을 때, 워들 영감은 픽윅 씨에게, 크리스마스 휴가가 지난 뒤 그 젊은 부부가 살게 될 집의 가구와 비품을 다 같이 떼 지어 내려가 둘러보고 왔노라고 일러 주었다. 그 말에 벨라와 트런들은 둘 다 술청 불 앞에 있던 뚱보 소년처럼 새빨갛게 낯을 붉혔다. 또 검은 눈에 부츠에 모피를 두른 젊은 숙녀가 에밀리의 귀에 뭐라고 속삭이고는 짓궂게 스노드그래스 씨를 흘끔 보니, 에밀리는 그녀더러 바보 같은 애라고 응수했지만 그럼에도 자못 빨개졌다. 그리고 위대한 천재들이 으레 그렇듯 겸손한 스노드그래스 씨는 정수리까지 붉은 기운이 차오르는 것을 느끼며, 제 마음속 깊디깊은 곳에서, 앞서 말한 그 젊은 숙녀가 검은 눈도 짓궂음도 위쪽에 모피 둘린 부츠도 죄다, 아예 이웃 고을에 편안히 가 있었으면 좋겠다고 간절히 바랐다.\n\n집 밖에서도 그토록 정겹고 행복했다면, 농장에 다다랐을 때의 환영은 또 얼마나 따뜻하고 정겨웠겠는가! 하인들조차 픽윅 씨를 보고 기뻐서 활짝 웃었다. 그리고 엠마는 텁먼 씨에게 반은 새침하고 반은 발칙하며 죄다 사랑스러운 알은체의 눈길을 보냈으니, 복도에 선 보나파르트 석상이라도 팔짱을 풀고 그녀를 끌어안게 만들 만한 것이었다.\n\n노부인은 앞 객실에 여느 때처럼 위엄 있게 앉아 있었으나, 다소 심사가 뒤틀려 있었고, 그 탓에 유난히도 귀가 먹어 있었다. 그녀 자신은 결코 바깥출입을 하지 않았으니, 같은 부류의 다른 노부인들 다수가 그렇듯, 누구든 자기가 할 수 없는 일을 제멋대로 하면 그것을 집안에 대한 반역 행위쯤으로 여기기 쉬웠다. 그러니 그 늙은 영혼에게 복이 있기를, 그녀는 큰 의자에 될 수 있는 한 꼿꼿이 앉아 더없이 사납게 보이려 했으나 ― 그래 봤자 결국은 인자해 보였다.\n\n\"어머니.\" 워들이 말했다. \"픽윅 씨예요. 기억나시죠?\"\n\n\"신경 쓰지 마라.\" 노부인이 자못 위엄 있게 대답했다. \"나 같은 늙은이 일로 픽윅 씨를 성가시게 하지 마. 이제 아무도 날 챙기지 않으니, 그게 당연한 일이지.\" 여기서 노부인은 고개를 휙 젖히고는 떨리는 손으로 라벤더 빛 비단 드레스를 매만져 내렸다.\n\n\"이런, 이런, 부인.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"이런 식으로 옛 친구를 모른 체하게 둘 수는 없지요. 저는 부인과 오래도록 이야기도 나누고 카드도 한판 더 하려고 일부러 내려온 겁니다. 그리고 우리 둘이서 이 사내애들과 계집애들에게, 마흔여덟 시간이 더 지나기 전에 미뉴에트 추는 법을 보여 줍시다.\"\n\n노부인은 빠르게 마음이 누그러지고 있었으나, 한꺼번에 그러기는 싫었던지라, 그저 \"아! 안 들려요!\" 하고만 말했다.\n\n\"무슨 말씀이세요, 어머니.\" 워들이 말했다. \"자, 자, 토라지지 마세요, 착한 분이시잖아요. 벨라를 생각하세요. 자, 저 가엾은 애의 기운을 북돋워 주셔야지요.\"\n\n선량한 노부인은 이 말을 들었으니, 아들이 그 말을 하자 입술이 떨렸기 때문이다. 그러나 나이에는 사소한 심술의 약점이 따르는 법이라, 그녀는 아직 완전히 풀리지는 않았다. 그래서 라벤더 빛 드레스를 다시 한 번 매만져 내리고는 픽윅 씨를 향해 돌아앉아 말했다. \"아, 픽윅 씨, 내가 처녀였을 적엔 젊은이들이 아주 달랐다오.\"\n\n\"의심할 바 없지요, 부인.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"그러니 옛 혈통의 자취를 조금이라도 지닌 몇 안 되는 이들을 제가 그토록 귀히 여기는 것이지요.\" 이렇게 말하며 픽윅 씨는 벨라를 가만히 제 쪽으로 끌어당겨 이마에 입을 맞추고는, 할머니 발치의 작은 걸상에 앉으라고 일렀다. 노부인의 얼굴을 향해 들어 올려진 그녀의 표정이 옛 시절의 생각을 불러일으켰던 것인지, 아니면 노부인이 픽윅 씨의 다정한 선량함에 감동했던 것인지, 그 까닭이 무엇이었든 간에 그녀는 끝내 마음이 녹아내렸다. 그래서 손녀의 목을 끌어안았고, 그 모든 자그마한 심술은 소리 없는 눈물의 봇물 속에 다 씻겨 사라졌다.\n\n그날 밤 그들은 행복한 무리였다. 픽윅 씨와 노부인이 함께한 스무 판 남짓의 카드놀이는 점잖고 엄숙했으며, 둥근 식탁의 흥겨움은 떠들썩했다. 숙녀들이 물러간 한참 뒤까지도, 브랜디와 향료를 넉넉히 탄 뜨거운 엘더베리 술이 돌고, 또 돌고, 거듭 돌았다. 그 뒤에 이어진 잠은 깊었고 꿈은 즐거웠다. 주목할 만한 사실은, 스노드그래스 씨의 꿈은 끊임없이 에밀리 워들을 향했다는 것이요, 윈클 씨의 환상 속 주역은 검은 눈에 짓궂은 미소를 띠고 위쪽에 모피가 둘린 자못 멋진 부츠 한 켤레를 신은 젊은 숙녀였다는 것이다.\n\n픽윅 씨는 이른 아침에, 여러 목소리의 웅성거림과 발소리의 자박거림에 잠이 깼으니, 그것은 뚱보 소년조차 그 깊은 잠에서 깨워 낼 만한 것이었다. 그는 침대에 일어나 앉아 귀를 기울였다. 하녀들과 여자 손님들이 쉴 새 없이 이리저리 뛰어다녔다. 그리고 뜨거운 물을 찾는 외침이 어찌나 빗발치고, 바늘과 실을 찾는 부르짖음이 어찌나 거듭되며, \"오, 좀 와서 매 줘, 제발!\" 하는 반쯤 죽인 애원이 어찌나 많았던지, 순진한 픽윅 씨는 무슨 끔찍한 일이라도 벌어진 게 틀림없다고 상상하기 시작했다 ― 그러다 정신이 더 들면서 결혼식이 생각났다. 중대한 행사인지라 그는 유난히 공들여 옷을 차려입고는 아침 식사방으로 내려갔다.\n\n거기엔 하녀들이 죄다 분홍 모슬린 가운에 모자엔 흰 리본을 단 산뜻한 새 제복 차림으로, 도저히 형용할 수 없는 흥분과 동요의 상태로 집 안을 뛰어다니고 있었다. 노부인은 이십 년 동안 빛을 보지 못한 양단 가운을 차려입었으니, 다만 그동안 내내 그것을 넣어 둔 상자 틈새로 슬며시 새어 든 길 잃은 빛줄기 몇 가닥만이 예외였다. 트런들 씨는 자못 의기양양하고 기운이 넘쳤으나, 그러면서도 다소 긴장해 있었다. 정 많은 늙은 집주인은 자못 명랑하고 태연한 척하려 했으나, 그 시도에 보기 좋게 실패하고 있었다. 처녀들은 위층에서 신부와 들러리들을 따로 구경하는 영예를 누리고 있던 특별히 선택된 두엇을 빼고는, 죄다 눈물에 젖은 채 흰 모슬린을 입고 있었다. 픽윅 회원들은 모두 더없이 화사한 차림이었다. 그리고 집 앞 잔디밭에서는 무시무시한 함성이 울려 퍼졌으니, 농장에 딸린 사내들과 소년들과 어중간한 풋내기들이 죄다 단춧구멍에 흰 리본을 달고 있던 차에, 하나같이 있는 힘껏 환호성을 질러 댄 탓이었다. 그들을 그렇게 부추기고 북돋운 것은 새뮤얼 웰러 씨의 가르침과 본보기였으니, 그는 벌써 대단히 인기를 얻는 데 성공하여, 마치 이 땅에서 나고 자란 사람처럼 너무도 편안해 보였다.\n\n결혼식은 농담거리로 삼아도 좋다고 허락된 주제이지만, 사실 따지고 보면 그리 큰 농담거리는 못 된다. 우리는 그저 그 의식을 두고 하는 말이며, 결혼 생활에 대해 무슨 숨은 빈정거림을 늘어놓는 것이 결코 아님을 분명히 이해해 주기를 청한다. 그 행사의 기쁨과 즐거움에 뒤섞여 있는 것은, 집을 떠나는 데 따르는 숱한 아쉬움이요, 부모와 자식이 헤어지며 흘리는 눈물이요, 인생에서 가장 행복한 한 시절의 더없이 사랑스럽고 다정한 벗들을 떠나, 아직 시험해 보지 못하고 거의 알지도 못하는 다른 이들과 더불어 그 근심과 시련을 맞으러 간다는 자각이다 ― 그것들은 자연스러운 감정이거니와, 우리는 그것을 묘사하여 이 장을 구슬프게 만들고 싶지 않으며, 그것을 비웃는다고 여겨지는 것은 더더욱 원치 않는다.\n\n그러니 간략히 말하자면, 그 의식은 딩글리 델의 교구 교회에서 그 늙은 목사에 의해 치러졌고, 픽윅 씨의 이름이 그 교회 제의실에 아직도 보존되어 있는 혼인 명부에 적혀 있으며, 검은 눈의 젊은 숙녀는 자못 떨리고 불안정한 필체로 제 이름을 서명했고, 또 다른 들러리인 에밀리의 서명은 거의 알아볼 수 없을 지경이며, 모든 것이 자못 훌륭하게 치러졌고, 젊은 숙녀들은 대체로 그것이 예상했던 것보다 훨씬 덜 충격적이라고 여겼으며, 또 검은 눈과 짓궂은 미소의 임자가 워들 씨에게 자기는 결코 그런 끔찍한 일은 견뎌 내지 못할 것이 분명하다고 일러두었으나, 우리는 그녀가 잘못 알았다고 생각할 더없이 확실한 근거를 가지고 있다. 이 모든 것에 덧붙여 말할 수 있는 것은, 픽윅 씨가 신부에게 맨 처음으로 입맞춤을 한 사람이라는 것, 그리고 그러면서 그녀의 목에 값진 금시계와 금줄을 걸어 주었는데, 그것은 보석상 말고는 어떤 인간의 눈도 일찍이 본 적이 없는 물건이었다는 것이다. 그러고 나서 늙은 교회 종이 더없이 흥겹게 울렸고, 그들은 모두 아침을 먹으러 돌아왔다.\n\n\"민스파이는 어디 놓냐, 이 어린 아편쟁이야?\" 웰러 씨가 전날 밤에 제대로 차려 두지 못한 먹을거리들을 늘어놓는 것을 거들며 뚱보 소년에게 말했다.\n\n뚱보 소년은 파이를 놓을 자리를 가리켰다.\n\n\"아주 좋아.\" 셈이 말했다. \"거기다 크리스마스 장식을 한 조각 꽂아라. 다른 접시는 맞은편에. 옳지, 이제 우리가 보기에 아담하고 편안하구먼, 사팔뜨기 고치겠다고 어린 아들 머리를 댕강 잘라 버린 아버지 말마따나 말이야.\"\n\n웰러 씨는 이 비유를 하면서, 그 효과를 한껏 살리려고 한두 걸음 뒤로 물러서서는 더없이 흡족한 눈으로 차려 둔 것을 둘러보았다.\n\n\"워들.\" 다들 자리에 앉기가 무섭게 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"이 기쁜 행사를 기려 술 한 잔!\"\n\n\"기꺼이 들지, 이 사람아.\" 워들이 말했다. \"조 ― 빌어먹을 녀석, 또 잠들었구먼.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 나리.\" 뚱보 소년이 외딴 구석에서 벌떡 일어나며 대답했다. 거기서 그는 뚱보 소년들의 수호성인 ― 불멸의 호너 ― 처럼 크리스마스 파이를 게걸스레 먹어 치우고 있었으니, 다만 그 젊은 신사의 행적을 특징짓는 그 침착함과 신중함은 없었다.\n\n\"픽윅 씨 잔을 채워라.\"\n\n\"예, 나리.\"\n\n뚱보 소년은 픽윅 씨의 잔을 채우고는 주인 의자 뒤로 물러나, 거기서 나이프와 포크의 놀림이며, 접시에서 일행의 입으로 옮겨 가는 맛난 음식들의 행로를, 더없이 인상적인 어둡고 음울한 기쁨으로 지켜보았다.\n\n\"신의 가호가 함께하길, 이 친구야!\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"자네도 그러하길, 이 사람아.\" 워들이 대답했다. 그리고 그들은 진심으로 서로를 위해 축배를 들었다.\n\n\"워들 부인.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"우리 같은 늙은이들은 이 기쁜 경사를 기려 함께 술 한 잔 들어야지요.\"\n\n노부인은 그때 자못 위엄을 갖춘 상태였으니, 양단 가운을 입고 식탁 상석에 앉아, 한쪽엔 갓 결혼한 손녀를, 다른 쪽엔 고기를 썰어 줄 픽윅 씨를 거느리고 있었던 까닭이다. 픽윅 씨는 자못 큰 소리로 말한 것이 아니었으나 그녀는 단번에 알아듣고는, 그의 만수무강과 행복을 비는 술을 한 잔 가득 들이켰다. 그러고 나서 그 갸륵한 노인은 자신의 결혼식에 관한 시시콜콜하고 자세한 이야기를 늘어놓기 시작했으니, 굽 높은 신을 신는 유행에 관한 일장 논설과, 작고한 아름다운 톨림글로어 부인의 생애와 모험에 관한 몇 가지 자세한 사연을 곁들였다. 그 모든 것에 노부인 자신은 실로 자못 흥겹게 웃어 댔고, 젊은 숙녀들도 따라 웃었으니, 그들은 자기들끼리 도대체 할머니가 무슨 이야기를 하고 있는 건지 궁금해하던 참이었다. 그들이 웃자 노부인은 열 곱절은 더 흥겹게 웃으며, 이건 늘 일품 이야기로 쳐 주던 것이라고 말했고, 그 말에 그들은 다 같이 또 웃어, 노부인을 더없이 좋은 기분으로 만들어 주었다. 그러고 나서 케이크를 잘라 반지 사이로 통과시켰다. 젊은 숙녀들은 미래의 남편을 꿈에서 보려고 베개 밑에 둘 조각들을 챙겼고, 그리하여 자못 많은 낯붉힘과 웃음판이 벌어졌다.\n\n\"밀러 씨.\" 픽윅 씨가 옛 지인인 그 고집스러운 신사에게 말했다. \"술 한 잔?\"\n\n\"더없는 만족과 함께 들지요, 픽윅 씨.\" 고집스러운 신사가 엄숙하게 대답했다.\n\n\"저도 끼워 주시겠소?\" 인자한 늙은 목사가 말했다.\n\n\"저도요.\" 그의 아내가 끼어들었다.\n\n\"저도, 저도요.\" 식탁 끝에 앉은 가난한 친척 두 사람이 말했다. 그들은 자못 진탕 먹고 마시고 무엇에든 다 웃어 대던 이들이었다.\n\n픽윅 씨는 그렇게 더해지는 제안마다 진심 어린 기쁨을 드러냈고, 그의 눈은 흥겨움과 명랑함으로 빛났다.\n\n\"신사 숙녀 여러분.\" 픽윅 씨가 별안간 일어서며 말했다.\n\n\"옳소, 옳소! 옳소, 옳소! 옳소, 옳소!\" 웰러 씨가 북받치는 감정에 못 이겨 외쳤다.\n\n\"하인들을 죄다 불러들이게.\" 워들 영감이 외쳤으니, 안 그랬다면 웰러 씨가 그 주인에게서 틀림없이 받았을 공개적인 꾸지람을 막으려고 끼어든 것이었다. \"저마다 술 한 잔씩 들려 이 축배를 같이 들게 하게. 자, 픽윅.\"\n\n일행의 침묵과 하녀들의 소곤거림과 사내들의 어색한 쭈뼛거림 가운데서, 픽윅 씨는 말을 이어 갔다.\n\n\"신사 숙녀 여러분 ― 아니, 신사 숙녀라고는 하지 않겠습니다. 숙녀분들께서 제가 그토록 큰 무례를 범하도록 허락해 주신다면, 여러분을 제 친구들, 제 소중한 친구들이라고 부르겠습니다 ―\"\n\n여기서 픽윅 씨는 숙녀들의 엄청난 박수에 말이 끊겼고, 신사들이 그것을 받아 따라 했으니, 그 와중에 그 눈의 임자가 저 사랑스러운 픽윅 씨에게 입을 맞출 수도 있겠노라고 말하는 것이 또렷이 들렸다. 이에 윈클 씨가 정중하게, 그 일을 대리인이 대신 할 수는 없겠느냐고 물으니, 검은 눈의 젊은 숙녀는 \"저리 가세요\" 하고 대답하면서, 그 청에다 \"할 수 있으면 해 보든가\" 하는 말을 눈빛으로 할 수 있는 한 분명하게 곁들였다.\n\n\"제 소중한 친구들이여.\" 픽윅 씨가 다시 말을 이었다. \"저는 신랑 신부의 건강을 위해 축배를 제의하려 합니다 ― 신의 가호가 그들에게 있기를(환호와 눈물). 제 젊은 친구 트런들은 제가 믿건대 자못 훌륭하고 사내다운 친구입니다. 그리고 그의 아내는 제가 알건대 자못 상냥하고 사랑스러운 처녀이니, 이십 년 동안 아버지의 집에서 둘레에 베풀어 온 그 행복을 또 다른 활동의 영역으로 옮길 자격이 충분합니다. (여기서 뚱보 소년이 우렁차게 엉엉 울음을 터뜨려, 웰러 씨가 그의 외투 깃을 잡아 밖으로 끌어냈다.) 저는 바랍니다.\" 픽윅 씨가 덧붙였다. \"저는 그녀의 동생의 남편이 될 만큼 젊었으면 좋겠습니다(환호). 그러나 그러지 못하니, 그녀의 아버지가 될 만큼 늙은 것을 기쁘게 여깁니다. 그러한즉, 제가 두 사람 다 흠모하고 존경하며 사랑한다고 말할 때(환호와 흐느낌), 무슨 숨은 속셈이 있다고 의심받지는 않을 테니까요. 신부의 아버지, 저기 계신 우리의 선량한 친구는 고결한 분이며, 저는 그분을 아는 것이 자랑스럽습니다(큰 소란). 그분은 친절하고, 훌륭하고, 독립된 기개를 지녔으며, 마음이 고운, 환대 잘하고, 너그러운 분입니다(그 모든 형용사마다, 특히 마지막 둘에 가난한 친척들의 열광적인 함성이 터졌다). 그분의 따님이, 그분 자신이 바랄 수 있는 모든 행복을 누리기를, 그리고 그분이 따님의 행복을 바라보는 데서 그토록 받아 마땅한 마음의 온갖 흡족함과 평안을 얻으시기를 비는 것이, 저는 확신하건대, 우리 모두의 한결같은 소원입니다. 그러니 그들의 건강을 위해 마시고, 그들의 장수와 온갖 축복을 빕시다!\"\n\n픽윅 씨는 박수갈채의 회오리 가운데서 말을 맺었다. 그리고 다시 한 번 웰러 씨의 지휘 아래, 임시 동원된 사람들의 허파가 활발하고도 능률적으로 작동에 들어갔다. 워들 씨가 픽윅 씨를 위해 축배를 제의했고, 픽윅 씨가 노부인을 위해 제의했다. 스노드그래스 씨가 워들 씨를 위해 제의했고, 워들 씨가 스노드그래스 씨를 위해 제의했다. 가난한 친척 하나가 텁먼 씨를 위해 제의했고, 다른 가난한 친척이 윈클 씨를 위해 제의했다. 모든 것이 행복과 잔치판이었으니, 마침내 두 가난한 친척이 둘 다 식탁 밑으로 알 수 없이 사라져 버린 것이, 이제 파할 때가 되었음을 일행에게 일러 주었다.\n\n저녁 식사 때 그들은 다시 만났으니, 그 사이 사내들이 아침 술기운을 떨쳐 내려고 워들의 권유로 이십오 마일 도보 행군에 나섰던 것이다. 가난한 친척들은 같은 행복한 결말에 이르고자 종일 침대에 누워 있었으나, 성공하지 못한 까닭에 거기 그대로 머물러 있었다. 웰러 씨는 하인들을 끊임없는 흥겨움의 상태에 빠뜨려 놓았고, 뚱보 소년은 먹기와 잠자기를 자그맣게 번갈아 나눠 가며 시간을 보냈다.\n\n저녁 식사는 아침 식사만큼이나 푸짐한 자리였고, 눈물만 빠졌을 뿐 꼭 그만큼 떠들썩했다. 그다음엔 후식과 몇 차례 더 이어진 축배가 있었다. 그다음엔 차와 커피가, 그리고 그다음엔 무도회가 있었다.\n\n매너 농장의 가장 좋은 거실은, 높직한 벽난로 선반과 널찍한 굴뚝을 갖춘, 길고 어두운 판벽 두른 좋은 방이었다. 그 굴뚝은 새로 나온 특허 마차를 바퀴까지 통째로 몰고 올라갈 수 있을 만큼 컸다. 방 위쪽 끝에는 호랑가시나무와 상록수로 그늘진 정자처럼 꾸민 자리에, 머글턴 전체에서 으뜸가는 바이올린 주자 둘과 단 하나뿐인 하프가 앉아 있었다. 온갖 벽감과 온갖 받침 위에는, 저마다 네 갈래로 갈라진 묵직한 옛 은촛대가 서 있었다. 양탄자는 걷어 두었고, 촛불은 환히 타올랐고, 화롯불은 벽난로에서 활활 타며 탁탁거렸고, 흥겨운 목소리와 명랑한 웃음이 방 안에 울려 퍼졌다. 만약 옛 잉글랜드 자영농 중 누군가가 죽어서 요정이 되었다면, 바로 이런 곳이 그들이 잔치를 벌일 만한 자리였을 것이다.\n\n이 유쾌한 정경의 흥취에 무언가를 더할 수 있는 것이 있었다면, 그것은 픽윅 씨가, 그의 가장 오랜 친구들의 기억으로도 처음으로, 각반 없이 나타났다는 주목할 만한 사실이었으리라.\n\n\"춤추실 작정이오?\" 워들이 말했다.\n\n\"물론이지.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다. \"보다시피 내가 그러려고 차려입지 않았소?\" 픽윅 씨는 자기가 신은 얼룩무늬 비단 양말과 맵시 있게 끈을 묶은 펌프스 구두에 눈길을 모았다.\n\n\"당신이 비단 양말을!\" 텁먼 씨가 농조로 외쳤다.\n\n\"왜 안 되겠소, 이 사람아 ― 왜 안 돼?\" 픽윅 씨가 발끈하며 그를 향해 돌아서며 말했다.\n\n\"오, 물론 당신이 그걸 신으면 안 될 이유야 없지요.\" 텁먼 씨가 응수했다.\n\n\"나도 없다고 보오, 이 사람아 ― 없다고 보오.\" 픽윅 씨가 자못 단호한 어조로 말했다.\n\n텁먼 씨는 웃어 볼까 했으나, 이게 진지한 문제임을 알아챘다. 그래서 정색하고는, 무늬가 예쁘다고 말했다.\n\n\"그러길 바라오.\" 픽윅 씨가 친구에게 눈을 박으며 말했다. \"양말을 양말로서 볼 때, 거기에 별난 데라곤 없을 줄로 믿소만, 그렇지 않소?\"\n\n\"물론이지요. 오, 물론 없고말고요.\" 텁먼 씨가 대답했다. 그는 자리를 떴고, 픽윅 씨의 낯빛은 여느 때의 인자한 표정으로 돌아갔다.\n\n\"우리 다 준비된 것 같소이다.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했으니, 그는 노부인과 함께 춤의 맨 앞자리에 서 있었고, 시작하고 싶은 지나친 조바심에 벌써 네 번이나 헛출발을 한 참이었다.\n\n\"그럼 당장 시작하시오.\" 워들이 말했다. \"자!\"\n\n바이올린 둘과 하프 하나가 울려 퍼지자, 픽윅 씨가 손을 엇잡는 동작으로 막 나아가려는데, 사방에서 일제히 손뼉을 치며 \"멈춰요, 멈춰!\" 하는 외침이 일었다.\n\n\"무슨 일이오?\" 픽윅 씨가 말했으니, 그는 바이올린과 하프가 멎고서야 겨우 정신이 들었지, 집에 불이 났다 한들 이 세상 어떤 힘으로도 그를 멈출 수 없었을 것이다.\n\n\"아라벨라 앨런은 어딨소?\" 열댓 목소리가 외쳤다.\n\n\"그리고 윈클은?\" 텁먼 씨가 덧붙였다.\n\n\"여기 있소!\" 그 신사가 그의 어여쁜 동행과 함께 구석에서 나오며 외쳤다. 그렇게 나올 때, 그와 검은 눈의 젊은 숙녀 중 누구의 얼굴이 더 빨갰는지 가려내기란 어려운 일이었으리라.\n\n\"참 별난 일이로군, 윈클.\" 픽윅 씨가 다소 부루퉁하게 말했다. \"진작 제자리에 와 있을 수가 없었다니 말이오.\"\n\n\"전혀 별난 일이 아닙니다.\" 윈클 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"글쎄.\" 픽윅 씨가, 아라벨라에게 눈길을 멈춘 채 자못 의미심장한 미소를 띠며 말했다. \"글쎄, 따지고 보면 그게 그리 별난 일이었는지도 잘 모르겠군그래.\"\n\n그러나 그 일을 더 생각할 겨를은 없었으니, 바이올린과 하프가 본격적으로 시작한 까닭이다. 픽윅 씨가 내달았다 ― 손을 엇잡고 ― 한가운데를 따라 방 끝까지, 그리고 굴뚝 절반쯤까지 올라갔다가, 다시 문 쪽으로 돌아오고 ― 도처에서 푸세트를 추고 ― 바닥을 쾅 구르고 ― 다음 짝을 맞을 채비를 하고 ― 다시 내달았다 ― 그 도형 전체를 한 번 더 거치고 ― 박자를 맞추려 또 한 번 쾅 구르고 ― 다음 짝, 또 다음 짝, 또 그다음 짝 ― 일찍이 그런 신명은 없었다. 마침내 그들이 춤의 맨 끝에 다다르고, 노부인이 기진하여 물러나고 그 자리에 목사 부인이 대신 들어선 뒤로도 족히 열네 짝이 지난 다음에도, 그 신사는 정작 제 수고가 전혀 필요 없는 마당에까지, 음악에 박자를 맞추느라 제자리에서 쉼 없이 춤을 추며, 그 내내 짝을 향해 도무지 형용할 수 없는 부드러운 태도로 미소 지었다.\n\n픽윅 씨가 춤에 싫증을 내기 훨씬 전에, 갓 결혼한 부부는 그 자리에서 물러갔다. 그래도 아래층에는 성대한 야식이 있었고, 그 뒤에는 한참 오래도록 앉아 있는 자리가 이어졌다. 그리고 이튿날 아침 늦게 픽윅 씨가 잠에서 깼을 때, 그는 어렴풋한 기억으로, 어딘가에서 한 사람 한 사람 따로따로 은밀하게, 마흔다섯 명쯤 되는 사람들을, 그들이 런던에 오는 바로 그 첫날 조지 앤드 벌처 여관에서 자기와 함께 식사하자고 초대했다는 것을 떠올렸다. 픽윅 씨는 이것을, 그가 전날 밤에 운동 말고도 무언가를 더 들었다는 자못 확실한 징표로 옳게 여겼다.\n\n\"그래, 자네 댁에서는 오늘 밤 부엌에서 놀이를 한다지, 응, 그렇지?\" 셈이 엠마에게 물었다.\n\n\"네, 웰러 씨.\" 엠마가 대답했다. \"우린 크리스마스이브엔 늘 그래요. 주인 어른은 무슨 일이 있어도 그걸 거르지 않으세요.\"\n\n\"자네 주인 어른은 뭐든 챙기는 데 아주 멋진 생각을 갖고 계신 분이야, 아가씨.\" 웰러 씨가 말했다. \"내 그분만큼 분별 있는 사람도, 그분만큼 진짜배기 신사도 본 적이 없네.\"\n\n\"오, 그렇고말고요!\" 뚱보 소년이 대화에 끼어들며 말했다. \"그분이 키우는 돼지고기는 정말 좋다니까요!\" 뚱보 젊은이는 구운 다리와 육즙을 떠올리며 웰러 씨를 향해 반쯤 식인종 같은 곁눈질을 보냈다.\n\n\"오, 드디어 깼냐, 응?\" 셈이 말했다.\n\n뚱보 소년이 고개를 끄덕였다.\n\n\"내 한마디 해 두마, 이 어린 보아뱀아.\" 웰러 씨가 자못 인상 깊게 말했다. \"네가 잠을 좀 덜 자고 운동을 좀 더 하지 않으면, 어른이 됐을 때 변발 늘어뜨린 그 노신사 양반이 당한 거랑 똑같은 신변의 불편을 당하기 십상이야.\"\n\n\"그 사람한테 무슨 짓을 했는데요?\" 뚱보 소년이 떨리는 목소리로 물었다.\n\n\"이제 말해 주마.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다. \"그분은 여태껏 빚어낸 것 중에 가장 큰 표본 중 하나였지 ― 진짜배기 뚱뚱한 양반이라, 마흔다섯 해 동안 제 발끝을 한 번도 본 적이 없었어.\"\n\n\"어머나!\" 엠마가 외쳤다.\n\n\"그래, 한 번도 못 봤지, 아가씨.\" 웰러 씨가 말했다. \"그리고 그분 자기 다리를 똑같이 본떠 만들어 눈앞 식탁에 올려놨대도, 자기 거라곤 알아보지 못했을 거야. 자, 그분은 늘 한 자 반쯤 늘어뜨린 아주 근사한 금시곗줄에다, 안주머니엔 값나가는 금시계를 차고 사무실로 걸어갔는데 ― 얼마짜리라고 말하기도 겁이 나지만, 시계로서 값나갈 수 있는 만큼은 나가는 ― 크고 묵직하고 둥근 물건이라, 시계로 치면 그분이 사람으로 치는 만큼이나 굵직했고, 거기 걸맞게 큼직한 문자판이 달려 있었지. '그 시계 가지고 다니지 않는 게 좋을걸,' 그 노신사의 친구들이 말했어. '도둑맞을 거야,' 하고 말이야. '내가?' 그분이 말했지. '그래, 자네 그럴 거야,' 친구들이 말했어. '글쎄,' 그분이 말했지. '이 시계를 빼낼 수 있는 도둑이 있다면 어디 한번 보고 싶군. 나도 도무지 못 빼낼 만큼 어찌나 꽉 끼는지, 시간이 알고 싶을 때마다 빵집 진열창을 들여다봐야 할 판이거든,' 하고 말이야. 그러고는 마치 산산조각이라도 날 듯이 너털웃음을 웃고는, 분 바른 머리에 변발을 늘어뜨리고 다시 걸어 나가, 시곗줄을 그 어느 때보다 더 길게 늘어뜨린 채, 그 크고 둥근 시계가 잿빛 커지 바지를 거의 뚫고 나올 듯한 모양새로 스트랜드 거리를 어슬렁어슬렁 내려갔지. 런던 천지 어느 소매치기 하나 그 줄을 잡아당겨 보지 않은 자가 없었지만, 줄은 절대 끊기지 않고 시계는 절대 빠져나오지 않으니, 그들은 그 묵직한 노신사를 보도 위로 끌고 다니는 데 곧 진력이 났고, 그분은 집에 가서 변발이 네덜란드 시계 추처럼 부르르 떨릴 만큼 웃어 댔지. 그러던 어느 날, 그 노신사가 어슬렁거리며 가다가, 낯이 익은 어떤 소매치기 하나가 머리가 아주 큰 어린애와 팔짱을 끼고 오는 걸 봤어. '재미있게 됐군,' 노신사가 속으로 말했지. '또 한번 해 보려는 모양인데, 어림없지!' 그러고는 자못 흥겹게 낄낄대기 시작했는데, 별안간 그 어린애가 소매치기 팔을 놓더니 머리부터 곧장 노신사 배때기로 들이받아, 한순간 그 아픔에 그분을 그대로 허리가 꺾이게 만들었어. '사람 살려!' 노신사가 외쳤지. '됐습니다, 나리,' 소매치기가 그분 귀에 대고 속삭였어. 그리고 그분이 다시 몸을 곧추세웠을 땐, 시계도 줄도 사라지고 없었고, 그보다 더 나쁜 건, 그 뒤로 노신사의 소화가 평생 마지막 날까지 죄다 엉망이 돼 버렸다는 거야. 그러니 너도 정신 똑바로 차리고, 이 젊은 친구야, 너무 뚱뚱해지지 않게 조심해라.\"\n\n웰러 씨가 이 교훈적인 이야기를 매듭짓자 ― 뚱보 소년은 자못 감명받은 듯했다 ― 셋은 다 함께 큰 부엌으로 갔다. 거기엔 이때쯤 식구들이 다 모여 있었으니, 워들 영감의 조상들이 아득한 옛날부터 지켜 온, 크리스마스이브의 해마다의 관습을 따른 것이었다.\n\n이 부엌의 천장 한복판에, 워들 영감은 방금 제 손으로 커다란 겨우살이 가지 하나를 매달아 놓은 참이었고, 바로 이 겨우살이 가지가 즉각 온통 더없이 유쾌한 다툼과 소란의 한 장면을 빚어냈다. 그 와중에 픽윅 씨는, 톨림글로어 부인 자신의 후손에게도 손색없을 정중함으로, 노부인의 손을 잡아 그 신비로운 가지 아래로 인도하고는, 더없는 예의와 격식을 갖추어 그녀에게 입을 맞추었다. 노부인은 그토록 중대하고 엄숙한 의례에 걸맞은 온갖 위엄을 갖추어 이 실천적 예법의 행위에 응했으나, 그 관습에 대한 미신적 숭배에 그만큼 푹 젖어 있지 않았거나, 아니면 입맞춤의 가치란 그것을 얻는 데 약간의 수고가 든다면 한껏 높아진다고 여긴 젊은 숙녀들은, 비명을 지르고 발버둥치고 구석으로 달아나고 으름장을 놓고 항의를 하며, 방을 나가는 것만 빼고는 별짓을 다 하다가, 덜 모험적인 신사 몇몇이 막 단념하려는 참에, 다들 별안간 더 이상 버텨 봐야 소용없음을 깨닫고는 흔쾌히 입맞춤을 받아들였다. 윈클 씨는 검은 눈의 젊은 숙녀에게 입을 맞췄고, 스노드그래스 씨는 에밀리에게 입을 맞췄다. 그리고 겨우살이 아래에 있어야 한다는 형식에 그리 까다롭지 않은 웰러 씨는, 엠마와 다른 하녀들을 붙잡는 족족 입을 맞췄다. 가난한 친척들로 말하자면, 그들은 누구에게나 다 입을 맞췄으니, 젊은 숙녀 손님들 중 다소 못생긴 축들조차 예외가 아니었다. 그 숙녀들은 겨우살이가 매달리기가 무섭게 너무도 허둥대다가 저도 모르게 그 바로 아래로 달려 들어가 버렸던 것이다! 워들은 불에 등을 댄 채 서서, 더없는 흡족함으로 그 모든 정경을 둘러보았다. 그리고 뚱보 소년은 그 틈을 타, 누군가 다른 사람을 위해 고이 챙겨 두었던 유난히 좋은 민스파이 하나를 제 것으로 가로채어 단숨에 먹어 치웠다.\n\n이제 비명은 가라앉았고, 얼굴들은 발그레 달아올랐고, 머리채는 헝클어졌다. 픽윅 씨는 앞서 말한 대로 노부인에게 입을 맞춘 뒤 겨우살이 아래에 서서, 자못 흐뭇한 낯빛으로 둘레에서 벌어지는 모든 일을 바라보고 있었다. 그때 검은 눈의 젊은 숙녀가 다른 젊은 숙녀들과 잠깐 속닥거리더니, 별안간 앞으로 휙 달려 나와, 픽윅 씨의 목에 팔을 두르고는 그의 왼뺨에 다정하게 입을 맞추었다. 그리고 픽윅 씨가 무슨 영문인지 채 또렷이 알기도 전에, 그는 그 무리 전체에 둘러싸여, 그들 하나하나에게서 입맞춤을 받았다.\n\n무리 한복판에 선 픽윅 씨가, 이리 끌리고 저리 끌리며, 턱에 입맞춤을 받고, 다음엔 코에, 다음엔 안경에 입맞춤을 받는 꼴을 보고, 사방에서 터져 나오는 웃음의 폭소를 듣는 것은 유쾌한 일이었다. 그러나 그보다 더 유쾌한 것은, 잠시 뒤 비단 손수건으로 눈을 가린 픽윅 씨가 벽에 가서 부딪히고, 구석으로 기어들고, 그 놀이에 더없는 흥을 붙여 술래잡기의 온갖 신비를 다 거치다가, 마침내 가난한 친척 하나를 붙잡고는 이번엔 술래 자신을 따돌려야 하게 되자, 모든 구경꾼의 감탄과 갈채를 자아낼 만한 날쌤과 민첩함으로 그것을 해내는 꼴을 보는 것이었다. 가난한 친척들은 좋아할 성싶은 사람들을 붙잡았고, 놀이가 시들해지면 제가 붙잡혀 주었다. 그들이 모두 술래잡기에 싫증이 났을 때, 스냅드래건이라는 큰 놀이판이 벌어졌고, 거기서 충분히 많은 손가락을 데고 건포도가 죄다 동나자, 그들은 활활 타오르는 장작불의 거대한 불가에 둘러앉아 푸짐한 야식과, 여느 세탁장 구리솥보다 조금 작을 만한 거대한 와사일 사발을 들었다. 그 사발 속에서는 뜨거운 사과들이 쉭쉭거리고 보글거리며, 도무지 뿌리칠 수 없는 먹음직스러운 빛깔과 흥겨운 소리를 내고 있었다.\n\n\"이것이.\" 픽윅 씨가 둘레를 둘러보며 말했다. \"이것이 진정한 안락이로구려.\" \"우리 한결같은 관습이지요.\" 워들 씨가 대답했다. \"크리스마스이브엔 누구나 다, 지금 자네가 보는 대로 우리와 함께 앉습니다 ― 하인들까지 죄다 말이오. 그리고 우리는 여기서 시계가 열두 시를 칠 때까지 기다리며 크리스마스를 맞아들이고, 벌금 놀이와 옛이야기로 시간을 보내지요. 트런들, 이 사람아, 불을 좀 돋워 주게.\"\n\n장작을 휘젓자 환한 불똥이 무수히 솟아올랐다. 짙붉은 불꽃이 풍성한 빛을 내뿜어 방의 가장 먼 구석까지 스며들고, 그 명랑한 빛깔을 모든 얼굴에 드리웠다.\n\n\"자.\" 워들이 말했다. \"노래 ― 크리스마스 노래! 더 나은 게 없으니, 내가 한 곡 들려주리다.\"\n\n\"좋소!\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"잔을 채우게.\" 워들이 외쳤다. \"그 와사일의 짙고 풍성한 빛깔 너머로 사발 바닥을 보게 되기까지는 족히 두 시간은 걸릴 거요. 다들 잔을 채우고, 자 이제 노래요.\"\n\n이렇게 말하며, 그 흥겨운 노신사는 좋고 우렁차고 다부진 목소리로 더 지체 없이 시작했다.\n크리스마스 캐럴\n\n# A CHRISTMAS CAROL\n\n\"봄 따위 나는 아랑곳없네. 그 변덕스런 날개에 / 꽃송이와 봉오리일랑 실려 가라지. 그는 음흉한 비로 그것들을 한껏 꾀어내고는 / 동트기도 전에 흩뿌려 버린다네. 변덕스런 요정이라, 제 자신도 / 한 시간이면 바뀌는 제 마음도 알지 못하니, 네 얼굴을 향해 미소 짓다가도, 찡그린 상으로 / 네 가장 어린 꽃을 시들게 하리라.\n\n\"여름 해일랑 그 밝은 집으로 내달려 가라지, / 나는 결코 그를 찾지 않으리. 구름에 그가 흐려질 때 나는 큰 소리로 웃을 수 있으니 / 그가 아무리 시무룩한들 무슨 상관이랴! 그의 귀염둥이 자식이란 사나운 열병의 행렬에 / 날뛰는 거친 광기인 것을. 그리고 사랑이 너무 강하면, 오래가지 못하는 법이니, / 많은 이가 제 아픔으로 알아낸 바라네.\n\n\"고요한 추수의 밤, 정숙하고 부드러운 / 달의 잔잔한 빛 아래라면, 내 생각에 그것은 넓고 부끄럼 없는 한낮보다 / 내게 훨씬 더 고운 광채를 띤다네. 허나 잎새마다 내 슬픔을 일깨우니, / 그것이 나무 아래 누워 있는 까닭이라. 그러니 가을 공기 아무리 고운들, / 그것은 결코 내겐 맞지 않다네.\n\n\"허나 내 노래를 소리 높여 부르노라, 든든한 크리스마스를 위해, / 정 깊고, 참되고, 담대한 그를 위해. 가득 찬 잔을 비우고, 있는 힘껏 / 이 늙은 크리스마스를 위해 세 번 환호하리! 우리는 흥겨운 떠들썩함으로 그를 맞아들여 / 그 기쁜 가슴을 즐겁게 하고, 한 입, 한 모금 남아 있는 동안 그를 받들다가, / 좋은 벗됨 속에 작별하리라. 그 곱고 정직한 긍지로, 그는 모진 비바람의 흉터를 / 한 점도 감추기를 마다하니, 그건 부끄러움이 아니라네, 우리의 가장 용감한 뱃사람들의 / 뺨에도 꼭 같은 자취가 숱하니. 그러니 다시 한 번 노래하리, 지붕이 울리고 / 벽에서 벽으로 메아리칠 때까지 ― 그 든든한 옛 호걸에게, 오늘 밤 어서 오시라고, / 모든 계절의 임금님이시여!\"\n\n이 노래는 떠들썩한 갈채를 받았으니 ― 벗들과 식솔들이야말로 일품 청중인 까닭이다 ― 특히 가난한 친척들은 완전한 환희의 황홀경에 빠져 있었다. 다시 불을 돋우었고, 다시 와사일이 한 바퀴 돌았다.\n\n\"눈이 어찌나 내리는지!\" 사내들 중 하나가 낮은 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"눈이 내려, 그래?\" 워들이 말했다.\n\n\"모질고 추운 밤입니다, 나리.\" 그 사내가 대답했다. \"게다가 바람이 일어, 눈을 들판 너머로 짙은 흰 구름처럼 몰아가고 있어요.\"\n\n\"젬이 뭐라는 거냐?\" 노부인이 물었다. \"무슨 일이 있는 건 아니지, 응?\"\n\n\"아니에요, 아니에요, 어머니.\" 워들이 대답했다. \"눈보라가 치고, 바람이 살을 에듯 차다는군요. 굴뚝에서 우르릉거리는 소리를 들으니 저도 알겠어요.\"\n\n\"아!\" 노부인이 말했다. \"꼭 이런 바람, 꼭 이런 눈이 내린 적이 있었지, 여러 해 전에, 내 기억나는구나 ― 네 가엾은 아버지가 돌아가시기 꼭 다섯 해 전에. 그날도 크리스마스이브였지. 그리고 기억하기로, 바로 그날 밤에 그이가 도깨비들이 늙은 게이브리얼 그럽을 데려가 버린 이야기를 우리에게 들려줬단다.\"\n\n\"무엇에 관한 이야기라고요?\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"오, 아무것도, 아무것도 아니오.\" 워들이 대답했다. \"이 고장 선량한 사람들이 도깨비들에게 끌려갔다고 여기는 어느 늙은 교회지기에 관한 이야기지요.\"\n\n\"여긴다고!\" 노부인이 내뱉었다. \"그걸 안 믿을 만큼 간 큰 사람이 어디 있단 말이냐? 여긴다니! 너도 어렸을 적부터, 그이가 도깨비들에게 끌려갔다는 걸 줄곧 들어 오지 않았느냐, 그리고 그게 사실인 줄 너도 알지 않느냐?\"\n\n\"알았어요, 어머니, 그러셨다 치죠, 어머니가 그러길 바라신다면.\" 워들이 웃으며 말했다. \"그분은 도깨비들에게 끌려갔습니다, 픽윅. 자, 그걸로 그 일은 끝입니다.\"\n\n\"아니, 아니지요.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"끝이 아닙니다, 정말이지. 어떻게, 왜, 그리고 그에 관한 모든 것을 저는 꼭 들어야겠소.\"\n\n워들은 모두가 들으려고 고개를 앞으로 숙이자 빙긋 웃고는, 인색하지 않은 손길로 와사일을 따라 채우고, 픽윅 씨에게 고개를 끄덕여 건강을 빌고는, 다음과 같이 이야기를 시작했다.\n\n그러나 우리의 편집자 된 가슴에 복이 있기를, 우리가 얼마나 긴 장에 그만 빠져들고 말았는가! 우리는 장이라느니 하는 그런 자질구레한 제약 따위는 깡그리 잊고 있었노라고, 우리는 엄숙히 고백한다. 그러니 자 이렇게, 도깨비에게 새 장에서 정정당당한 출발을 안겨 주기로 하자. 도깨비들을 위해 무대를 비워 두고 봐주기란 없기를, 신사 숙녀 여러분, 부디 그리해 주시기를.\n\n# CHAPTER XXIX. THE STORY OF THE GOBLINS WHO STOLE A SEXTON\n\n\"아주 오래전, 이 고장 어느 옛 수도원 도시에서—얼마나 오래전인고 하니, 우리 증조부들이 그 이야기를 곧이곧대로 믿었을 만큼이나 오래전이라, 그러니 그 이야기는 틀림없이 사실일 게요—개브리얼 그럽이라는 이가 교회 묘지의 교회지기 겸 무덤 파는 일꾼으로 일하고 있었더랍니다. 사람이 교회지기라 해서, 또 늘 죽음의 표상들에 둘러싸여 산다 해서, 반드시 침울하고 우울한 사람이 되어야 한다는 법은 결코 없지요. 장의사들로 말하자면 세상에서 가장 유쾌한 친구들이거든요. 나도 한때 어느 상여꾼과 가까이 지내는 영광을 누린 적이 있는데, 그자는 사생활에서, 그러니까 일을 쉴 때면, 기억에 막힘 한 번 없이 '될 대로 되라'는 노래를 신나게 뽑아내거나, 숨 한 번 돌리지 않고 독한 술 한 잔을 쭉 들이켜는, 그런 익살스럽고 우스꽝스러운 작달막한 친구였답니다. 하지만 이런 반대되는 선례들에도 불구하고, 개브리얼 그럽은 심사가 고약하고 비뚤어지고 무뚝뚝한 자였고—침울하고 외로운 사람이라, 제 자신과, 그리고 크고 깊은 조끼 주머니에 꼭 들어맞는 낡은 고리버들 술병 말고는 아무와도 어울리지 않았으며—제 곁을 스쳐 지나가는 유쾌한 얼굴들마다 어찌나 깊은 악의와 심술의 눈초리로 쏘아보던지, 그 시선을 마주하고서 무언가 언짢은 기분이 들지 않기란 좀처럼 어려운 일이었지요.\n\n어느 크리스마스이브, 땅거미가 지기 조금 전에, 개브리얼은 삽을 둘러메고 등불을 켜고는 옛 교회 묘지로 향했답니다. 이튿날 아침까지 무덤 하나를 마저 파 놓아야 했고, 기분이 몹시 가라앉아 있던 터라, 어쩌면 당장 일을 시작하면 기운이 좀 날까 싶었던 게지요. 그가 오래된 거리를 따라 올라가는 길에, 활활 타오르는 난롯불의 정겨운 빛이 낡은 여닫이창 너머로 새어 나오는 것을 보았고, 그 둘레에 모여 앉은 이들의 큰 웃음소리와 흥겨운 환성을 들었답니다. 이튿날 잔치를 위해 분주히 채비하는 모습을 눈여겨보았고, 거기서 비롯되어 부엌 창문으로 구름처럼 무럭무럭 피어오르는 갖가지 군침 도는 냄새를 맡았지요. 이 모든 것이 개브리얼 그럽의 마음에는 쓸개즙이요 쓴 쑥이었습니다. 그리고 아이들 무리가 집에서 우르르 뛰쳐나와 길을 가로질러 종종거리며 건너가, 미처 맞은편 문을 두드리기도 전에, 곱슬머리 꼬마 녀석 대여섯이 우르르 몰려나와 그들을 맞이하고는 다 함께 위층으로 몰려 올라가 크리스마스 놀이로 저녁을 보내려 할 때, 개브리얼은 홍역, 성홍열, 아구창, 백일해, 그리고 그 밖에도 위안이 될 만한 여러 가지를 떠올리며 음산하게 미소 짓고는 삽자루를 한층 단단히 움켜쥐었답니다.\n\n이렇게 흐뭇한 심정으로 개브리얼은 성큼성큼 걸으며, 이따금 곁을 지나가는 이웃들이 상냥하게 건네는 인사에 짤막하고 시무룩한 으르렁거림으로 응수하다가, 마침내 교회 묘지로 이어지는 어두운 골목으로 접어들었습니다. 그런데 개브리얼은 그 어두운 골목에 다다르기를 손꼽아 기다리고 있었으니, 대체로 그곳은 사람들이 환한 대낮, 해가 쨍쨍할 때가 아니고서는 별로 들어가기를 꺼리는, 음침하고 으스스하고 처량한 좋은 장소였기 때문이지요. 그러니, 옛 수도원 시절부터, 머리를 빡빡 깎은 수도승들의 시대로부터 줄곧 관(棺) 골목이라 불려 온 바로 이 성역에서, 어린 개구쟁이 녀석 하나가 즐거운 크리스마스를 노래하는 흥겨운 가락을 목청껏 외쳐 대는 것을 듣고는 적잖이 분개했답니다. 개브리얼이 계속 걸어가고 그 목소리가 점점 가까워지자, 그것이 어느 작은 사내아이에게서 나오는 것임을 알게 되었지요. 그 아이는 옛 거리의 어느 모임에 끼려고 부랴부랴 가던 길이었는데, 한편으로는 제 자신에게 동무가 되어 주려고, 또 한편으로는 그 자리를 위해 미리 채비하려고, 노래를 폐가 터져라 목청껏 외쳐 대고 있었던 겁니다. 그래서 개브리얼은 아이가 다가오기를 기다렸다가, 그 녀석을 한 모퉁이로 몰아넣고는 목소리 좀 가다듬는 법을 가르쳐 주려고 등불로 그 머리통을 대여섯 번 후려쳤답니다. 그러자 아이는 머리에 손을 얹고 사뭇 다른 곡조를 부르며 부랴부랴 달아났고, 개브리얼 그럽은 속으로 아주 흐뭇하게 낄낄거리며 교회 묘지로 들어가 등 뒤로 문을 잠갔지요.\n\n그는 외투를 벗고 등불을 내려놓은 다음, 아직 마저 파지 못한 무덤 속으로 들어가 한 시간 남짓 신명나게 일했답니다. 그러나 땅은 서리에 꽁꽁 얼어붙어, 그것을 깨뜨려 퍼내기란 그리 쉬운 일이 아니었지요. 게다가 달이 떠 있긴 했으나 갓 뜬 초승달이라, 교회 그늘에 묻힌 무덤에는 빛을 거의 비추지 못했습니다. 다른 때 같았으면 이런 장애들 탓에 개브리얼 그럽은 몹시 침울하고 비참한 기분에 빠졌겠지만, 그 어린 사내아이의 노래를 그치게 한 일이 어찌나 흡족하던지, 작업이 더디게 진척된 것 따위는 별로 개의치 않았답니다. 그날 밤 일을 마치고 나서, 그는 음산한 만족감을 품고 무덤 속을 내려다보며, 연장을 주섬주섬 챙기면서 이렇게 흥얼거렸지요.\n\n한 사람을 위한 멋진 거처, 한 사람을 위한 멋진 거처, 목숨이 다하면 얻는 차디찬 흙 몇 자(尺); 머리맡에 돌 하나, 발치에 돌 하나, 구더기들이 먹어 치울 기름지고 통통한 한 끼 식사; 머리 위엔 무성한 잡초, 둘레엔 축축한 진흙, 거룩한 땅에 마련된 한 사람을 위한 멋진 거처, 이거로구나!\n\n'호! 호!' 개브리얼 그럽은 자기가 즐겨 쉬던 자리인 납작한 묘비 위에 걸터앉아 고리버들 술병을 꺼내 들며 웃었습니다. '크리스마스에 관(棺)이라니! 크리스마스 선물이로다! 호! 호! 호!'\n\n'호! 호! 호!' 바로 등 뒤에서 어떤 목소리가 그 말을 따라 했습니다.\n\n개브리얼은 고리버들 술병을 입술로 가져가던 손길을 멈추고, 다소 놀라 주위를 둘러보았지요. 곁에 있는 가장 오래된 무덤의 밑바닥조차도, 창백한 달빛 아래 펼쳐진 그 교회 묘지보다 더 고요하고 적막하지는 않았습니다. 차디찬 흰 서리가 묘비들 위에서 반짝였고, 옛 교회의 돌 조각들 사이에서 보석을 줄지어 박은 듯 빛났지요. 눈은 땅 위에 단단하고 바삭하게 깔려 있었고, 빽빽이 흩어진 흙더미들 위로 어찌나 희고 매끈한 덮개를 펼쳐 놓았던지, 마치 시신들이 그 아래 누워, 오직 수의(壽衣)에만 가려진 채 거기 묻혀 있는 듯했습니다. 그 엄숙한 정경의 깊디깊은 적막을 깨뜨리는 것은 아주 희미한 바스락 소리 하나조차 없었지요. 모든 것이 어찌나 차갑고 고요하던지, 소리 그 자체가 얼어붙어 버린 것만 같았답니다.\n\n'메아리였구나.' 개브리얼 그럽은 다시 술병을 입술로 가져가며 말했습니다.\n\n'아니거든.' 어떤 굵직한 목소리가 말했지요.\n\n개브리얼은 벌떡 일어나, 놀라움과 공포에 그 자리에 못 박힌 듯 서 버렸습니다. 그의 시선이 머문 형상이 그의 피를 얼어붙게 했기 때문이지요.\n\n곧추선 묘비 하나 위에, 그의 바로 곁에, 기이하고 이 세상 것 같지 않은 형상 하나가 앉아 있었는데, 개브리얼은 그것이 이 세상의 존재가 아님을 단번에 느꼈답니다. 땅에 닿았을 법한 길고 괴상한 다리를 위로 추켜올려 묘하고 기괴한 모양새로 꼬고 있었고, 힘줄이 불거진 두 팔은 맨살을 드러냈으며, 두 손은 무릎 위에 얹혀 있었지요. 짧고 둥근 몸통에는 자잘하게 칼집을 낸 장식이 달린 몸에 꼭 맞는 옷을 걸쳤고, 등 뒤로는 짧은 망토가 늘어져 있었습니다. 옷깃은 기묘한 뾰족 모양으로 잘려 있어, 도깨비에게 주름 깃이나 목도리 노릇을 대신해 주었지요. 신발은 발끝이 길게 위로 말려 올라가 뾰족했답니다. 머리에는 깃털 하나를 꽂아 장식한, 챙이 넓은 원뿔형 모자를 쓰고 있었습니다. 그 모자는 흰 서리로 뒤덮여 있어, 도깨비는 마치 그 똑같은 묘비 위에 이삼백 년 동안 아주 편안히 앉아 있었던 것처럼 보였지요. 그는 꼼짝 않고 완벽히 가만히 앉아 있었습니다. 혀를 쑥 내밀어 마치 비웃기라도 하는 듯했고, 오직 도깨비만이 지을 수 있는 그런 히죽거리는 웃음으로 개브리얼 그럽을 향해 싱글거리고 있었답니다.\n\n'메아리가 아니라니까.' 도깨비가 말했습니다.\n\n개브리얼 그럽은 온몸이 마비되어 아무런 대꾸도 할 수 없었지요.\n\n'크리스마스이브에 여기서 무얼 하는 게냐?' 도깨비가 준엄하게 말했습니다.\n\n'무덤을 파러 왔습니다요, 나리.' 개브리얼 그럽이 말을 더듬었지요.\n\n'이런 밤에 무덤과 교회 묘지를 헤매고 다니는 자는 어떤 인간이냐?' 도깨비가 외쳤습니다.\n\n'개브리얼 그럽! 개브리얼 그럽!' 교회 묘지를 가득 메우는 듯한 사나운 목소리들이 한꺼번에 비명처럼 외쳤지요. 개브리얼은 겁에 질려 주위를 둘러보았으나—아무것도 보이지 않았습니다.\n\n'그 병 속에 든 게 무엇이냐?' 도깨비가 말했습니다.\n\n'네덜란드 진(酒)입니다요, 나리.' 교회지기는 어느 때보다 더 떨면서 대답했지요. 그것을 밀수꾼들에게서 산 터라, 어쩌면 자기에게 묻는 이가 도깨비들의 세무서 소속일지도 모른다는 생각이 들었기 때문입니다.\n\n'이런 밤에 교회 묘지에서 혼자 네덜란드 진을 마시는 자는 누구냐?' 도깨비가 말했습니다.\n\n'개브리얼 그럽! 개브리얼 그럽!' 사나운 목소리들이 다시 외쳤지요.\n\n도깨비는 겁에 질린 교회지기를 심술궂게 곁눈질하더니, 목소리를 높여 이렇게 외쳤습니다.\n\n'그렇다면, 우리의 정당하고 마땅한 노획물은 누구냐?'\n\n이 물음에, 보이지 않는 합창이 응답했는데, 그것은 옛 교회 오르간의 우렁찬 울림에 맞추어 수많은 성가대원들이 노래하는 목소리처럼 들렸지요—사나운 바람을 타고 교회지기의 귓전에 실려 오다가, 스쳐 지나가며 잦아드는 듯한 가락이었습니다. 그러나 그 응답의 후렴은 여전히 똑같았으니, '개브리얼 그럽! 개브리얼 그럽!'이었지요.\n\n도깨비는 전보다 더 활짝 히죽거리며 말했습니다. '자, 개브리얼, 이것에 대해 뭐라고 할 테냐?'\n\n교회지기는 숨이 막혀 헐떡였지요.\n\n'이것을 어찌 생각하느냐, 개브리얼?' 도깨비가 묘비 양옆으로 두 발을 허공에 차올리며, 그 위로 말려 올라간 신발 끝을, 마치 본드 가(街)의 가장 유행하는 웰링턴 부츠 한 켤레라도 흐뭇이 바라보듯 흡족하게 들여다보며 말했습니다.\n\n'그게—그게—참 묘하군요, 나리.' 교회지기가 겁에 질려 반쯤 죽은 채로 대답했지요. '참 묘하고, 참 예쁩니다요. 하지만 나리만 괜찮으시다면 저는 돌아가서 제 일을 마저 끝내야 할 것 같습니다요, 나리.'\n\n'일이라!' 도깨비가 말했습니다. '무슨 일?'\n\n'무덤 말입니다요, 나리. 무덤을 파는 일이요.' 교회지기가 더듬거렸지요.\n\n'아, 무덤이라, 응?' 도깨비가 말했습니다. '다른 사람들이 다 흥겨워하는 때에 무덤을 파면서 그걸 즐기는 자는 누구냐?'\n\n다시 그 알 수 없는 목소리들이 응답했지요. '개브리얼 그럽! 개브리얼 그럽!'\n\n'아무래도 내 친구들이 너를 원하는 모양이다, 개브리얼.' 도깨비가 어느 때보다 더 깊숙이 혀를 볼 안쪽으로 밀어 넣으며 말했습니다—참으로 기막힌 혀였지요—'아무래도 내 친구들이 너를 원하는 모양이야, 개브리얼.' 도깨비가 말했습니다.\n\n'황송하오나, 나리,' 공포에 사로잡힌 교회지기가 대답했지요. '그럴 리가 없습니다요, 나리. 그분들은 저를 모릅니다요, 나리. 그분들이 저를 본 적이 있을 리 없다고 생각합니다요, 나리.'\n\n'아니, 본 적이 있고말고.' 도깨비가 대답했습니다. '오늘 밤 거리를 내려오며 아이들에게 사악한 눈초리를 던지고 무덤 파는 삽을 한층 단단히 움켜쥐던, 그 시무룩한 낯짝에 음산한 눈초리를 한 사내를 우리는 알지. 아이는 흥겨울 수 있는데 자기는 그러지 못하는 까닭에, 시샘하는 마음의 악의로 그 아이를 후려친 사내를 우리는 알아. 우리는 그를 알아, 우리는 그를 안다고.'\n\n여기서 도깨비는 크고 날카로운 웃음을 터뜨렸고, 메아리가 그것을 스무 배로 되돌려 주었지요. 그러고는 두 다리를 허공으로 차올려 머리로, 아니 정확히는 그 원뿔형 모자의 바로 그 뾰족한 끝으로, 묘비의 좁은 가장자리 위에 물구나무를 섰답니다. 거기서 놀라운 날렵함으로 공중제비를 넘어, 곧장 교회지기의 발치에 내려서더니, 양복장이들이 흔히 작업대 위에 앉는 자세로 척 자리를 잡았지요.\n\n'저—저는 아무래도 나리 곁을 떠나야 할 것 같습니다요, 나리.' 교회지기가 움직여 보려 애쓰며 말했습니다.\n\n'우리를 떠나겠다고!' 도깨비가 말했지요. '개브리얼 그럽이 우리를 떠나겠다는군. 호! 호! 호!'\n\n도깨비가 웃는 순간, 교회지기는 한 찰나, 교회 창문 안쪽에 마치 건물 전체가 환히 밝혀진 듯 눈부신 불빛이 비치는 것을 보았답니다. 그 빛은 사라졌고, 오르간이 경쾌한 가락을 울려 퍼뜨렸으며, 첫 번째 도깨비와 똑 닮은 도깨비 떼거리가 교회 묘지로 쏟아져 들어와 묘비들을 놓고 등 짚고 뛰어넘기 놀이를 시작했지요. 한순간도 숨 돌리려 멈추는 법 없이, 그 가운데 가장 높은 묘비들을 차례차례, 더없이 놀라운 솜씨로 훌쩍훌쩍 뛰어넘었습니다. 첫 번째 도깨비는 더없이 놀라운 도약 솜씨를 지녀, 다른 누구도 그를 따를 수 없었지요. 극도의 공포에 사로잡힌 와중에도 교회지기는, 다른 친구들은 보통 크기의 묘비들을 뛰어넘는 것으로 만족하는 데 반해, 첫 번째 도깨비는 가족 납골당을, 쇠 울타리까지 통째로, 마치 그것들이 한낱 길거리 기둥이나 되는 양 거뜬히 뛰어넘는 것을 눈여겨보지 않을 수 없었답니다.\n\n마침내 그 놀이가 더없이 흥겨운 절정에 다다랐지요. 오르간은 점점 더 빠르게 연주되었고, 도깨비들은 점점 더 빠르게 뛰어, 몸을 둥글게 말고, 땅바닥에서 곤두박질치며 데굴데굴 구르고, 묘비들을 축구공처럼 펄쩍펄쩍 뛰어넘었습니다. 교회지기의 머릿속은 그가 바라보는 그 빠른 움직임에 어지러이 빙빙 돌았고, 그 정령들이 눈앞에서 날아다니는 통에 다리가 후들거렸지요. 그때 도깨비 왕이 별안간 그를 향해 쏜살같이 달려들더니, 그의 옷깃에 손을 얹고는 함께 땅속으로 가라앉았답니다.\n\n개브리얼 그럽이, 너무 빠른 추락에 한순간 잃었던 숨을 겨우 돌릴 만한 틈을 얻고 보니, 자기가 커다란 동굴인 듯한 곳에 와 있고, 사방이 추하고 음산한 도깨비 떼거리로 둘러싸여 있는 것을 알게 되었지요. 그 방 한가운데, 높직한 자리 위에는 교회 묘지에서 만났던 그 친구가 떡하니 앉아 있었고, 그 바로 뒤에는 개브리얼 그럽 자신이 꼼짝달싹 못 한 채 서 있었습니다.\n\n'오늘 밤은 춥군.' 도깨비 왕이 말했습니다. '아주 추워. 여기 따뜻한 걸로 한 잔!'\n\n이 명령이 떨어지자, 얼굴에 영원토록 미소를 띤 도깨비 대여섯이—개브리얼 그럽은 그래서 그들이 신하들이려니 짐작했지요—부랴부랴 사라지더니, 이내 액체로 된 불이 담긴 잔을 들고 돌아와 왕에게 바쳤답니다.\n\n'아!' 뺨과 목구멍이 훤히 비치는 도깨비가 그 불꽃을 쭉 들이켜고는 외쳤지요. '이거야말로 정말 몸을 덥혀 주는구나! 같은 걸로 그럽 씨에게도 한 잔 가득 갖다주어라.'\n\n불운한 교회지기가 밤에는 따뜻한 걸 마시는 버릇이 없노라고 항변해 봐야 헛일이었지요. 도깨비 하나가 그를 붙들고 다른 하나가 그 활활 타는 액체를 목구멍에 부어 넣었고, 그가 콜록거리며 캑캑대고, 그 타는 듯한 음료를 삼킨 뒤 두 눈에서 펑펑 솟구쳐 나오는 눈물을 닦아 내자, 그 자리에 모인 무리 전체가 깔깔거리며 웃어 댔습니다.\n\n'자 이제,' 왕이 원뿔형 모자의 뾰족한 끝을 짓궂게 교회지기의 눈에 쿡 찔러 넣어 더없이 지독한 통증을 안기며 말했지요. '자 이제, 이 비참하고 음울한 사내에게 우리 자신의 저 위대한 보고(寶庫)에서 그림 몇 점을 보여 주어라!'\n\n도깨비가 이 말을 하자, 동굴의 저 안쪽 끝을 가리고 있던 짙은 구름이 차츰 걷히더니, 보아하니 아주 먼 곳에, 작고 가구도 변변찮지만 정갈하고 깨끗한 방 하나가 드러났습니다. 한 무리의 어린아이들이 환한 난롯불 둘레에 모여, 어머니의 치맛자락에 매달리고, 그 의자 둘레에서 깡충깡충 뛰놀고 있었지요. 어머니는 이따금 일어나 창문 커튼을 한쪽으로 젖히며, 마치 기다리던 무언가를 살피는 듯했습니다. 검소한 한 끼 식사가 식탁 위에 차려져 있었고, 난롯가에는 팔걸이의자 하나가 놓여 있었지요. 문 두드리는 소리가 들리자, 어머니가 문을 열었고, 아버지가 들어서자 아이들이 그 둘레에 우르르 몰려들며 기뻐서 손뼉을 쳤답니다. 그는 젖고 지쳐 있었고, 옷에서 눈을 털어 냈지요. 아이들이 그 둘레에 몰려들어, 그의 망토, 모자, 지팡이, 장갑을 분주한 열성으로 받아 들고는 그것들을 들고 방에서 우르르 달려 나갔습니다. 그러고는 그가 난롯가에 앉아 식사를 들 때, 아이들이 그의 무릎 위로 기어오르고, 어머니가 그 곁에 앉으니, 모든 것이 행복하고 안락해 보였지요.\n\n그러나 그 광경에 변화가 일었는데, 거의 알아채지 못할 만큼 슬그머니였습니다. 장면은 작은 침실로 바뀌었고, 거기서 가장 곱고 가장 어린 아이가 죽어 가고 있었지요. 그 뺨에서는 장밋빛이 가셨고, 두 눈에서는 빛이 사라졌습니다. 교회지기가 전에는 한 번도 느껴 보지도 알지도 못했던 관심을 품고 그 아이를 바라보는 바로 그 순간에, 아이는 숨을 거두었지요. 어린 남동생들과 여동생들이 그 작은 침대 둘레에 몰려들어, 그토록 차갑고 무거운 그 조그만 손을 붙잡았으나, 그 감촉에 움찔 뒤로 물러나며 그 아기 같은 얼굴을 경외에 차서 바라보았답니다. 그 얼굴이 그토록 차분하고 평온하며, 그 아름다운 아이가 안식과 평화 속에 잠들어 있는 듯 보였지만, 아이들은 그가 죽었음을 보았고, 또 그가 환하고 행복한 천국에서 자기들을 내려다보며 축복해 주는 천사가 되었음을 알았기 때문이지요.\n\n다시 엷은 구름이 그림 위를 스쳐 지나가더니, 다시 장면이 바뀌었습니다. 이제 아버지와 어머니는 늙고 무력해졌고, 그들 둘레에 있는 이들의 수는 절반도 더 줄어들었지요. 그러나 그들이 난롯가에 모여 옛 지난날의 이야기를 들려주고 또 귀 기울이는 동안, 만족과 흐뭇함이 모든 얼굴에 깃들고 모든 눈에서 빛났답니다. 천천히 그리고 평화롭게, 아버지는 무덤 속으로 가라앉았고, 머지않아 그의 온갖 근심과 고생을 함께 나누던 이가 안식의 자리로 그를 뒤따랐지요. 아직 살아남은 몇 안 되는 이들은 그 무덤 곁에 무릎 꿇고, 그것을 덮은 푸른 잔디를 눈물로 적셨습니다. 그러고는 일어나, 슬프고 애달프게 발길을 돌렸으나, 쓰라린 울부짖음이나 절망에 찬 통곡과는 거리가 멀었지요. 언젠가 다시 만나리라는 것을 알았기 때문입니다. 그리하여 그들은 다시 한번 분주한 세상 속에 섞여 들었고, 그들의 만족과 흐뭇함은 되살아났지요. 구름이 그림 위에 내려앉아, 그것을 교회지기의 시야에서 가려 버렸답니다.\n\n'저것을 어찌 생각하느냐?' 도깨비가 그 커다란 얼굴을 개브리얼 그럽 쪽으로 돌리며 말했습니다.\n\n개브리얼은 참 예쁘다는 둥 무어라 우물우물 중얼거렸고, 도깨비가 그 불타는 눈을 자기에게 쏘아붙이자 다소 부끄러운 기색이었지요.\n\n'네가 비참한 사람이라고!' 도깨비가 지독한 경멸의 어조로 말했습니다. '네가!' 더 무어라 덧붙이려는 듯했으나, 분개에 목이 메어 말이 막히자, 그 무척이나 나긋나긋한 다리 하나를 들어 올려, 겨냥을 확실히 하려고 머리 위로 잠시 휘두르더니, 개브리얼 그럽에게 보기 좋게 한 방 걷어찼지요. 그러자 곧바로 시중들던 도깨비들이 죄다 그 가엾은 교회지기 둘레에 몰려들어, 인정사정없이 그를 걷어찼답니다. 왕이 걷어차는 자를 함께 걷어차고, 왕이 껴안는 자를 함께 껴안는, 지상 신하들의 확립되고 변함없는 관습을 따라서 말이지요.\n\n'더 보여 주어라!' 도깨비 왕이 말했습니다.\n\n이 말에 구름이 흩어지더니, 풍요롭고 아름다운 풍경이 시야에 드러났지요—바로 그런 풍경이 오늘날까지도 그 옛 수도원 도시에서 반 마일 이내에 펼쳐져 있답니다. 해가 맑고 푸른 하늘에서 빛났고, 그 빛살 아래 물이 반짝였으며, 그 정겨운 기운 아래 나무들은 한층 푸르러 보이고 꽃들은 한층 화사해 보였지요. 물은 기분 좋은 소리를 내며 졸졸 흘렀고, 나무들은 그 잎새 사이로 속삭이는 산들바람에 살랑거렸으며, 새들은 나뭇가지 위에서 노래했고, 종달새는 높은 하늘에서 아침을 반기는 노래를 지저귀었습니다. 그래요, 아침이었지요. 여름의 환하고 향긋한 아침이었습니다. 가장 자그마한 잎새 하나, 가장 작은 풀잎 하나에도 생명이 가득했지요. 개미는 그날의 노역을 위해 기어 나왔고, 나비는 따스한 햇살 속에 팔랑거리며 몸을 데웠으며, 무수한 곤충들이 투명한 날개를 펼치고 짧지만 행복한 삶을 만끽했답니다. 사람은 그 정경에 마음이 들떠 걸어 나왔고, 모든 것이 환하고 찬란했지요.\n\n'네가 비참한 사람이라고!' 도깨비 왕이 전보다 더 경멸에 찬 어조로 말했습니다. 그리고 다시 도깨비 왕이 다리를 휘둘렀고, 다시 그것이 교회지기의 어깨 위로 내리쳤으며, 다시 시중드는 도깨비들이 우두머리의 본을 따라 했지요.\n\n구름은 몇 번이고 갔다가 다시 왔고, 그때마다 개브리얼 그럽에게 숱한 가르침을 일깨워 주었답니다. 그는 비록 도깨비들이 거기다 거듭 발길질을 해 대는 통에 어깨가 아파 쑤셨지만, 무엇으로도 줄일 수 없는 관심을 품고 바라보았지요. 그는 보았습니다. 고되게 일하며 노역의 나날로 변변찮은 빵을 벌어먹는 사람들이 흐뭇하고 행복하다는 것을. 또 가장 무지한 이에게조차 자연의 정겨운 얼굴이 끊임없이 솟아나는 흐뭇함과 기쁨의 원천이라는 것을. 그는 보았지요. 곱게 길러지고 다정히 자란 이들이, 더 거친 천성의 많은 이들을 짓눌렀을 궁핍 속에서도 흐뭇하고, 고통을 넘어서 있는 것을. 그들이 제 가슴속에 행복과 만족과 평화의 재료를 품고 있었기 때문이지요. 그는 보았습니다. 하느님의 모든 피조물 가운데 가장 다정하고 가장 연약한 여인들이, 슬픔과 역경과 고난을 가장 자주 넘어서 있다는 것을. 또 그것은 그들이 제 가슴속에 마르지 않는 애정과 헌신의 샘을 품고 있었기 때문임을 보았지요. 무엇보다도 그는 보았답니다. 자기처럼 남들의 흥겨움과 즐거움에 으르렁대는 자들이야말로 이 아름다운 대지의 표면에 돋은 가장 더러운 잡초라는 것을. 그리고 세상의 온갖 선(善)을 악(惡)에 견주어 저울질해 본 끝에, 그는 결국 이 세상이 제법 괜찮고 훌륭한 곳이라는 결론에 이르렀지요. 그가 그 생각을 채 굳히기가 무섭게, 마지막 그림 위로 닫혀 든 그 구름이 그의 감각 위에 내려앉아 그를 잠으로 달래는 듯했습니다. 하나둘씩 도깨비들이 그의 시야에서 흐려져 갔고, 마지막 하나가 사라지자 그는 깊은 잠 속으로 가라앉았지요.\n\n개브리얼 그럽이 깨어났을 때는 날이 밝아 있었고, 자기가 교회 묘지의 그 납작한 묘비 위에 큰대자로 누워 있는 것을 알게 되었답니다. 곁에는 텅 빈 고리버들 술병이 나뒹굴었고, 외투와 삽과 등불은 죄다 간밤의 서리에 허옇게 덮인 채 땅바닥에 흩어져 있었지요. 그가 처음 도깨비가 앉아 있는 것을 보았던 그 돌은 그의 앞에 빳빳이 곧추서 있었고, 간밤에 그가 파던 무덤도 멀지 않은 곳에 있었습니다. 처음에 그는 자기가 겪은 모험이 정말이었는지 의심하기 시작했으나, 일어서려 하자 어깨가 쑤시는 그 날카로운 통증이, 도깨비들의 발길질이 결코 헛것이 아니었음을 일러 주었지요. 도깨비들이 묘비를 놓고 등 짚고 뛰어넘기 놀이를 하던 그 눈밭에 발자국 하나 보이지 않는 것을 보고 다시 한번 어리둥절했으나, 그들이 정령인지라 눈에 보이는 자취 따위는 남기지 않으리라는 데 생각이 미치자 이내 그 일을 납득했답니다. 그리하여 개브리얼 그럽은 등이 아픈 와중에도 그럭저럭 몸을 일으켜, 외투에 묻은 서리를 털어 내고는 그것을 걸치고, 도시 쪽으로 얼굴을 돌렸지요.\n\n그러나 그는 달라진 사람이었고, 자기의 뉘우침이 비웃음을 사고 자기의 개심(改心)이 믿어지지 않을 그런 곳으로 돌아간다는 생각을 견딜 수 없었답니다. 그는 잠시 망설였지요. 그러고는 발길을 돌려, 어디로든 정처 없이 떠돌며 다른 곳에서 밥벌이를 구하기로 했습니다.\n\n그날, 등불과 삽과 고리버들 술병이 교회 묘지에서 발견되었지요. 처음에는 교회지기의 운명을 두고 별별 추측이 다 있었으나, 이내 그가 도깨비들에게 끌려갔다는 것으로 결론이 났답니다. 게다가 그가 한쪽 눈이 먼 밤색 말 등에 실려 공중을 휙 가로질러 가는 것을 똑똑히 보았다는, 자못 믿을 만한 목격자들도 없지 않았지요. 그 말은 사자의 뒷다리에 곰의 꼬리를 달고 있었다더군요. 마침내 이 모든 이야기가 독실하게 믿어졌고, 새 교회지기는 호기심 많은 이들에게 약간의 돈을 받고, 앞서 말한 그 말이 공중을 날다가 우연히 걷어차 떨어뜨린 교회 풍향계의 제법 큼직한 조각 하나를 보여 주곤 했답니다. 그 조각은 일이 년 뒤에 그 자신이 교회 묘지에서 주워 온 것이라고 했지요.\n\n공교롭게도, 이 이야기들은 한 십 년쯤 뒤에 개브리얼 그럽 본인이 뜻밖에 다시 나타나는 바람에 다소 어수선해졌답니다. 그는 누덕누덕한 차림에, 만족스러워 보이는, 류머티즘을 앓는 노인이 되어 있었지요. 그는 자기 이야기를 목사에게, 또 시장에게도 들려주었고, 세월이 흐르면서 그것은 하나의 역사적 사실로 받아들여지기 시작했으며, 바로 오늘날까지도 그런 형태로 전해져 내려오고 있답니다. 풍향계 이야기를 믿던 이들은 한 번 자기들 믿음을 엉뚱한 데 두었던 터라, 그것을 좀처럼 다시 놓으려 하지 않았지요. 그래서 그들은 짐짓 아는 체하는 표정을 지으며 어깨를 으쓱하고, 이마를 손가락으로 톡톡 두드리고는, 개브리얼 그럽이 네덜란드 진을 죄다 마셔 버리고 그 납작한 묘비 위에서 곯아떨어진 것이라고 무어라 중얼거렸답니다. 그리고 그가 도깨비 동굴에서 보았다고 여기는 것들을, 그가 세상 구경을 하고 더 지혜로워진 것이라고 설명하는 척했지요. 그러나 어느 때고 결코 널리 받아들여진 적 없던 이 견해는 차츰 사그라들고 말았습니다. 그리고 사정이야 어떻든 간에, 개브리얼 그럽이 평생토록 류머티즘에 시달렸으니, 이 이야기는 더 나은 교훈을 가르치지는 못할지언정 적어도 하나의 교훈은 담고 있는 셈이지요. 그것인즉, 사람이 크리스마스철에 시무룩해져서 혼자 술을 마신다면, 그것으로 조금도 나아질 게 없으리라 각오하는 편이 좋다는 것입니다. 그 술이 제아무리 좋은 것이든, 또 개브리얼 그럽이 도깨비 동굴에서 본 그 술처럼 표준 도수를 제아무리 여러 배나 웃도는 것이든 말이지요.'\n\n「자, 샘.」 크리스마스 아침, 그 총애받는 하인이 따뜻한 물을 들고 침실로 들어오자 픽윅 씨가 말했다. 「아직도 서리가 내렸나?」\n\n「세면대 물이 얼음 가면을 뒤집어썼습니다요, 나리.」 샘이 대답했다.\n\n「혹독한 날씨로군, 샘.」 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n「몸을 단단히 싸맨 자들한테는 좋은 시절이죠, 북극곰이 스케이트를 연습하면서 혼잣말로 그랬듯이 말입니다.」 웰러 씨가 대답했다.\n\n「십오 분 뒤에 내려가겠네, 샘.」 픽윅 씨가 잠잘 때 쓰는 모자를 풀며 말했다.\n\n「아주 좋습니다요, 나리.」 샘이 대답했다. 「아래층에 톱쟁이가 두 명 와 있습니다.」\n\n「두 명의 뭐라고!」 픽윅 씨가 침대에서 벌떡 일어나 앉으며 외쳤다.\n\n「톱쟁이 두 명요.」 샘이 말했다.\n\n「톱쟁이가 뭔가?」 픽윅 씨가 물었다. 그것이 살아 있는 동물인지, 먹는 것인지 도무지 확신이 서지 않았다.\n\n「아니! 톱쟁이가 뭔지 모르십니까요, 나리?」 웰러 씨가 물었다. 「톱쟁이가 외과 의사라는 건 누구나 다 아는 줄 알았는데요.」\n\n「오, 외과 의사라, 응?」 픽윅 씨가 미소를 띠며 말했다.\n\n「바로 그겁니다요, 나리.」 샘이 대답했다. 「하지만 저 아래 있는 것들은 정식 순종 톱쟁이는 아니고, 그냥 훈련 중인 놈들입니다.」\n\n「다시 말해 의대생들이라는 거지, 그런가?」 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n샘 웰러는 그렇다는 듯 고개를 끄덕였다.\n\n「반가운 일이군.」 픽윅 씨가 잠잘 때 쓰는 모자를 침대보 위에 힘차게 내던지며 말했다. 「훌륭한 친구들이지—아주 훌륭한 친구들이야. 관찰과 성찰로 판단력이 무르익고, 독서와 학문으로 취향이 세련된 자들이지. 정말 반가운 일이야.」\n\n「부엌 난롯가에서 시가를 피우고 있습니다요.」 샘이 말했다.\n\n「아!」 픽윅 씨가 두 손을 비비며 말했다. 「다정한 정과 활기가 넘쳐흐르는 게로군. 내가 보고 싶어 하던 바로 그 모습이야.」\n\n「그리고 한 놈은요,」 샘이 주인의 말 끊김에는 아랑곳하지 않고 말했다. 「한 놈은 다리를 탁자에 올려놓고 브랜디를 물도 안 타고 들이켜고 있고, 다른 놈은—안경 낀 놈 말입니다—무릎 사이에 굴 한 통을 끼고서 김 나듯이 후딱후딱 까고 있는데, 까먹는 족족 굴 껍데기를 굴뚝 모퉁이에 앉아서 곯아떨어진 어린 수종 환자 놈한테 겨눠서 던지고 있습니다요.」\n\n「천재의 기벽이지, 샘.」 픽윅 씨가 말했다. 「물러가도 좋네.」\n\n샘은 그에 따라 물러갔다. 십오 분이 지나자 픽윅 씨는 아침을 먹으러 아래층으로 내려갔다.\n\n「드디어 오셨군!」 늙은 워들 씨가 말했다. 「픽윅, 이쪽은 앨런 양의 오빠인 벤저민 앨런 씨라네. 우린 그를 벤이라 부르는데, 자네도 원한다면 그렇게 불러도 좋네. 이 신사분은 그의 아주 절친한 친구인—」\n\n「밥 소여 씨입니다.」 벤저민 앨런 씨가 끼어들었고, 그러자 밥 소여 씨와 벤저민 앨런 씨가 한목소리로 웃음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n픽윅 씨가 밥 소여에게 인사하자, 밥 소여도 픽윅 씨에게 인사했다. 밥과 그의 아주 절친한 친구는 그러고 나서 눈앞에 놓인 먹을 것에 무척이나 부지런히 달려들었고, 픽윅 씨는 두 사람을 찬찬히 살펴볼 기회를 얻었다.\n\n벤저민 앨런 씨는 거칠고 통통하며 다부진 체격의 청년으로, 검은 머리는 다소 짧게 깎았고 흰 얼굴은 다소 길었다. 안경으로 멋을 부렸고 흰 목도리를 둘렀다. 턱까지 단추를 채운 외줄 단추의 검은 프록코트 아래로는 으레 그렇듯 후추소금색 다리 한 쌍이 드러났고, 그 끝에는 어설프게 닦은 부츠 한 켤레가 달려 있었다. 코트는 소매가 짧았지만 셔츠 소맷부리의 흔적은 조금도 비치지 않았고, 얼굴은 셔츠 깃이 침범할 만한 여유가 충분했음에도 그런 부속물의 그림자조차 드리워지지 않았다. 그는 전체적으로 다소 곰팡내 나는 인상을 풍겼으며, 향이 풍부한 쿠바 시가의 그윽한 냄새를 발산하고 있었다.\n\n밥 소여 씨는 거친 푸른색 코트를 입고 있었는데, 그것은 외투도 프록코트도 아니면서 둘의 성질과 특성을 동시에 지닌 옷이었다. 그에게는 낮에 거리에서 담배를 피우고 밤에 같은 거리에서 고함치고 소리 지르며 웨이터를 세례명으로 부르고 그 밖에도 비슷한 정도의 우스꽝스러운 짓거리를 두루 일삼는 젊은 신사 특유의, 그 헝클어진 멋스러움과 으스대는 걸음걸이가 있었다. 그는 격자무늬 바지를 입고 크고 거친 더블 단추의 조끼를 걸쳤다. 밖에 나갈 때는 머리가 굵직한 두툼한 지팡이를 들고 다녔다. 장갑은 멀리했고, 전체적으로 어딘가 방탕한 로빈슨 크루소 같아 보였다.\n\n이것이 크리스마스 아침 아침 식탁에 자리를 잡은 픽윅 씨에게 소개된 두 인물이었다.\n\n「멋진 아침이군요, 신사분들.」 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n밥 소여 씨는 그 말에 가볍게 고개를 끄덕여 동의를 표하고는 벤저민 앨런 씨에게 겨자를 좀 건네 달라고 했다.\n\n「오늘 아침 멀리서들 오셨습니까, 신사분들?」 픽윅 씨가 물었다.\n\n「머글턴의 블루 라이언에서요.」 앨런 씨가 짧게 대답했다.\n\n「어젯밤에 우리와 합류하셨어야지요.」 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n「그랬어야 했죠.」 밥 소여가 대답했다. 「하지만 브랜디가 너무 좋아서 서둘러 자리를 뜰 수가 없었거든요. 안 그런가, 벤?」\n\n「그렇고말고.」 벤저민 앨런 씨가 말했다. 「시가도 나쁘지 않았고, 돼지고기 갈비도 그랬지. 안 그런가, 밥?」\n\n「두말할 것도 없지.」 밥이 말했다. 절친한 두 친구는 마치 어젯밤 저녁 식사의 추억이 이 식사에 새로운 풍미를 더해 주기라도 한 듯, 전보다 한층 거리낌 없이 아침 식사 공략을 재개했다.\n\n「쉬지 말고 해치워, 밥.」 앨런 씨가 동무를 격려하듯 말했다.\n\n「하고 있어.」 밥 소여가 대답했다. 그리고 정말이지, 그의 명예를 위해 말하건대, 그는 그렇게 하고 있었다.\n\n「식욕을 돋우는 데는 해부만 한 게 없지.」 밥 소여 씨가 식탁을 둘러보며 말했다.\n\n픽윅 씨는 가볍게 몸서리쳤다.\n\n「그건 그렇고, 밥.」 앨런 씨가 말했다. 「그 다리는 다 끝냈나?」\n\n「거의.」 소여가 그렇게 말하면서 닭 반 마리를 자기 접시에 덜었다. 「어린애 것치고는 근육이 아주 많은 다리야.」\n\n「그런가?」 앨런 씨가 무심하게 물었다.\n\n「아주.」 밥 소여가 입 가득 음식을 문 채 말했다.\n\n「난 우리 데서 팔 하나에 이름을 올려뒀어.」 앨런 씨가 말했다. 「시신 하나를 다 같이 추렴하는 중인데, 명단이 거의 다 찼지. 다만 머리를 원하는 친구를 도무지 못 구하고 있어. 자네가 좀 맡아 줬으면 하는데.」\n\n「싫어.」 밥 소여가 대답했다. 「값비싼 사치는 감당이 안 돼.」\n\n「말도 안 되는 소리!」 앨런이 말했다.\n\n「정말이지 못 해.」 밥 소여가 받았다. 「뇌라면야 마다하지 않겠지만, 머리통 전체는 못 견뎌.」\n\n「쉿, 쉿, 신사분들, 제발.」 픽윅 씨가 말했다. 「숙녀분들 오시는 소리가 들립니다.」\n\n픽윅 씨가 말하는 사이, 숙녀들이 스노드그래스, 윈클, 터프먼 씨의 정중한 호위를 받으며 이른 아침 산책에서 돌아왔다.\n\n「어머, 벤!」 아라벨라가 오빠를 본 반가움보다는 놀라움이 더 짙게 묻어나는 어조로 말했다.\n\n「내일 너를 집으로 데려가려고 왔다.」 벤저민이 대답했다.\n\n윈클 씨의 얼굴이 창백해졌다.\n\n「밥 소여가 안 보이니, 아라벨라?」 벤저민 앨런 씨가 다소 나무라는 듯 물었다. 아라벨라는 밥 소여가 있음을 알은체하며 우아하게 손을 내밀었다. 밥 소여가 내민 그 손을 눈에 띄게 꽉 쥐자, 증오의 전율이 윈클 씨의 가슴을 찔렀다.\n\n「벤 오빠!」 아라벨라가 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다. 「윈클 씨와—인사—인사는 나눴어?」\n\n「아직 못 나눴지만, 그렇게 된다면 더없이 기쁠 게다, 아라벨라.」 오빠가 근엄하게 대답했다. 이때 앨런 씨가 윈클 씨에게 떨떠름하게 인사했고, 윈클 씨와 밥 소여 씨는 곁눈질로 서로에 대한 불신을 주고받았다.\n\n새로 온 두 손님의 도착과, 그로 인해 윈클 씨와 부츠에 모피를 두른 젊은 숙녀에게 가해진 제약은, 만약 픽윅 씨의 쾌활함과 주인장의 너그러운 기분이 공동의 안녕을 위해 한껏 발휘되지 않았더라면 십중팔구 일행의 즐거움에 무척 불쾌한 훼방이 되었을 것이다. 윈클 씨는 차츰 벤저민 앨런 씨의 호감 속으로 슬며시 파고들었고, 심지어 밥 소여 씨와 다정한 대화까지 나누기에 이르렀다. 밥 소여는 브랜디와 아침 식사와 잡담으로 기운이 돋아 점차 극도로 익살스러운 상태로 무르익었고, 어떤 신사의 머리에 난 종양을 제거한 일에 관한 유쾌한 일화를 무척 신이 나서 들려주었는데, 그것을 굴 까는 칼과 4분의 1짜리 빵 반 덩이를 동원해 설명하니 모여 있던 일행에게는 큰 교화가 되었다. 그러고 나서 일행 전체가 교회에 갔는데, 거기서 벤저민 앨런 씨는 깊이 곯아떨어졌고, 밥 소여 씨는 신도석 자리에 4인치 길이의 통통한 글자로 제 이름을 새기는 기발한 방식으로 세속의 일에서 마음을 떼어 놓았다.\n\n「자.」 진한 맥주와 체리 브랜디라는 흐뭇한 곁들이가 딸린 푸짐한 점심을 충분히 즐긴 뒤 워들이 말했다. 「얼음 위에서 한 시간 보내는 건 어떻소? 시간은 넉넉하니.」\n\n「근사하군요!」 벤저민 앨런 씨가 말했다.\n\n「최고예요!」 밥 소여 씨가 내뱉었다.\n\n「당연히 스케이트는 타시겠지요, 윈클?」 워들이 말했다.\n\n「네—네, 아, 네.」 윈클 씨가 대답했다. 「제가—제가—연습이 좀 부족하긴 합니다.」\n\n「오, 꼭 타세요, 윈클 씨.」 아라벨라가 말했다. 「전 그걸 보는 게 무척 좋거든요.」\n\n「오, 정말 우아하죠.」 다른 젊은 숙녀가 말했다.\n\n세 번째 젊은 숙녀는 그것이 고상하다고 했고, 네 번째 숙녀는 그것이 ‘백조 같다’는 의견을 표했다.\n\n「정말이지 무척 기쁠 겁니다.」 윈클 씨가 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다. 「하지만 제겐 스케이트가 없습니다.」\n\n이 반론은 즉각 기각되었다. 트런들에게 두 켤레가 있었고, 뚱뚱한 소년이 아래층에 여섯 켤레는 더 있다고 알렸다. 그러자 윈클 씨는 더할 나위 없는 기쁨을 표하면서도, 더할 나위 없이 불편한 표정을 지었다.\n\n늙은 워들이 앞장서서 꽤 넓은 얼음판으로 일행을 이끌었다. 뚱뚱한 소년과 웰러 씨가 밤사이 그 위에 내려앉은 눈을 삽으로 퍼내고 쓸어낸 뒤, 밥 소여 씨는 윈클 씨가 보기에 완전히 경이로운 솜씨로 스케이트를 맞춰 신고는, 왼쪽 다리로 원을 그리고 8자를 새기며, 한 번도 숨을 돌리려 멈추는 일 없이 얼음 위에 그 밖에도 유쾌하고 놀라운 무늬를 잔뜩 그려 보였다. 픽윅 씨와 터프먼 씨와 숙녀들은 더없이 흡족해했고, 늙은 워들과 벤저민 앨런이 앞서 말한 밥 소여의 도움을 받아 그들이 ‘릴’이라 부르는 어떤 신비로운 회전 동작을 선보이자 그 만족은 그야말로 열광의 절정에 이르렀다.\n\n그러는 내내 윈클 씨는 얼굴과 손이 추위로 시퍼레진 채, 자기 발바닥에 송곳을 비집어 넣으며 스케이트를 신느라 애쓰고 있었는데, 날 끝을 뒤로 돌려 신질 않나, 스케이트라면 힌두인보다도 잘 모르는 스노드그래스 씨의 도움을 받아 끈을 아주 복잡하고 뒤엉킨 상태로 만들어 놓질 않나, 야단이었다. 그러다 마침내 웰러 씨의 도움으로 그 불운한 스케이트가 단단히 조여지고 채워졌고, 윈클 씨는 일으켜 세워졌다.\n\n「자, 이제, 나리.」 샘이 격려하는 어조로 말했다. 「출발하셔서 저 사람들한테 어떻게 타는 건지 보여 주십쇼.」\n\n「멈춰, 샘, 멈춰!」 윈클 씨가 격렬하게 떨면서, 물에 빠진 사람처럼 샘의 팔을 움켜쥐며 말했다. 「어찌나 미끄러운지, 샘!」\n\n「얼음 위에서야 드문 일은 아닙죠, 나리.」 웰러 씨가 대답했다. 「똑바로 서십쇼, 나리!」\n\n웰러 씨의 이 마지막 말은, 바로 그 순간 윈클 씨가 두 발을 허공으로 내던지고 뒤통수를 얼음에 처박으려는 듯한 미친 욕망을 드러내 보인 데 대한 것이었다.\n\n「이—이건—참 다루기 거북한 스케이트로군. 안 그런가, 샘?」 윈클 씨가 비틀거리며 물었다.\n\n「아무래도 그 안에 거북한 신사 양반이 들어 있는 것 같습니다요, 나리.」 샘이 대답했다.\n\n「자, 윈클.」 무슨 문제가 있는지 전혀 모른 채 픽윅 씨가 외쳤다. 「자, 오게. 숙녀분들이 모두 애타게 기다리고 있네.」\n\n「예, 예.」 윈클 씨가 무시무시한 미소를 지으며 대답했다. 「갑니다.」\n\n「막 시작하려는 참입니다요.」 샘이 몸을 빼내려 애쓰며 말했다. 「자, 나리, 출발하십쇼!」\n\n「잠깐만, 샘.」 윈클 씨가 더없이 다정하게 웰러 씨에게 매달리며 헐떡였다. 「집에 보니 내가 안 쓰는 코트가 두 벌 있더군, 샘. 자네가 가져도 좋네, 샘.」\n\n「감사합니다요, 나리.」 웰러 씨가 대답했다.\n\n「모자에 손 올려 인사할 것 없네, 샘.」 윈클 씨가 황급히 말했다. 「그러느라 손을 뗄 필요는 없어. 오늘 아침 크리스마스 선물로 자네한테 5실링을 주려고 했었네, 샘. 오후에 주지, 샘.」\n\n「참 너그러우십니다요, 나리.」 웰러 씨가 대답했다.\n\n「처음엔 그냥 날 좀 잡아 주게, 샘, 그래 주겠나?」 윈클 씨가 말했다. 「거기—그래, 됐어. 곧 요령을 익힐 거야, 샘. 너무 빠르지 않게, 샘. 너무 빠르지 않게.」\n\n윈클 씨가 몸을 반쯤 구부리고 앞으로 숙인 채, 도무지 백조 같지 않은 아주 기묘한 모양새로 웰러 씨에게 의지해 얼음 위를 건너가고 있을 때, 픽윅 씨가 더없이 천진하게 맞은편 둔덕에서 소리쳤다—\n「샘!」\n\n「예, 나리?」\n\n「이리 와. 자네가 필요해.」\n\n「놓으십쇼, 나리.」 샘이 말했다. 「주인 나리가 부르시는 거 안 들립니까? 놓으십쇼, 나리.」\n\n웰러 씨는 격렬하게 힘을 주어 괴로워하는 픽윅 회원의 손아귀에서 몸을 빼냈는데, 그러면서 불운한 윈클 씨에게 상당한 추진력을 가하고 말았다. 어떤 솜씨나 연습으로도 결코 보장할 수 없을 만큼의 정확함으로, 그 불운한 신사는 밥 소여 씨가 비할 데 없이 아름다운 묘기를 부리고 있던 바로 그 순간에 릴의 한가운데로 쏜살같이 돌진했다. 윈클 씨가 그에게 사납게 부딪치자, 두 사람은 요란한 소리와 함께 묵직하게 나동그라졌다. 픽윅 씨가 그 자리로 달려갔다. 밥 소여는 일어섰지만, 윈클 씨는 스케이트를 신은 채로 그런 짓을 할 만큼 어리석지 않았다. 그는 얼음 위에 앉은 채 발작하듯 미소를 지어 보이려 애썼지만, 그의 얼굴 윤곽 하나하나에는 고통이 새겨져 있었다.\n\n「다치셨소?」 벤저민 앨런 씨가 몹시 걱정하며 물었다.\n\n「많이는 아닙니다.」 윈클 씨가 등을 아주 세게 문지르며 말했다.\n\n「제가 피를 좀 뽑아 드렸으면 합니다만.」 벤저민 씨가 무척 열렬하게 말했다.\n\n「아뇨, 괜찮습니다.」 윈클 씨가 황급히 대답했다.\n\n「정말이지 그러시는 편이 좋을 것 같은데요.」 앨런이 말했다.\n\n「고맙습니다만,」 윈클 씨가 대답했다. 「사양하겠습니다.」\n\n「픽윅 씨, 당신 생각은 어떻습니까?」 밥 소여가 물었다.\n\n픽윅 씨는 흥분했고 분개해 있었다. 그는 웰러 씨에게 손짓하고는 엄한 목소리로 말했다. 「저 사람 스케이트를 벗기게.」\n\n「아니, 하지만 정말이지 전 이제 막 시작했을 뿐인데요.」 윈클 씨가 항의했다.\n\n「저 사람 스케이트를 벗기게.」 픽윅 씨가 단호하게 되풀이했다.\n\n그 명령에는 거역할 수가 없었다. 윈클 씨는 묵묵히 샘이 그 명을 따르도록 내버려 두었다.\n\n「일으켜 세우게.」 픽윅 씨가 말했다. 샘이 그를 일으키도록 거들었다.\n\n픽윅 씨는 구경꾼들로부터 몇 걸음 떨어진 곳으로 물러나, 친구에게 다가오라고 손짓한 뒤, 그를 꿰뚫어 보듯 응시하면서 낮지만 또렷하고 힘 있는 어조로 이 주목할 만한 말을 내뱉었다—\n\n「자네는 사기꾼일세.」\n\n「뭐라고요?」 윈클 씨가 흠칫하며 말했다.\n\n「사기꾼이라고. 원한다면 더 분명하게 말해 주지. 협잡꾼이야, 자네는.」\n\n그 말과 함께 픽윅 씨는 천천히 발꿈치를 돌려 친구들에게로 되돌아갔다.\n\n픽윅 씨가 방금 적은 그 소회를 토로하고 있는 동안, 웰러 씨와 뚱뚱한 소년은 힘을 합쳐 미끄럼 길을 닦아 놓고는 그 위에서 아주 능숙하고 멋들어진 솜씨로 몸을 놀리고 있었다. 특히 샘 웰러는 흔히 ‘구둣방 문 두드리기’라 불리는 그 아름다운 묘기 미끄럼을 선보이고 있었는데, 그것은 한 발로 얼음 위를 미끄러져 가다가 이따금 다른 발로 우편배달부처럼 얼음을 똑똑 두드리는 식으로 해내는 것이었다. 꽤 길고 멋진 미끄럼 길이었고, 가만히 서 있느라 무척 추웠던 픽윅 씨로서는 그 움직임에 어딘가 부러움을 느끼지 않을 수 없는 데가 있었다.\n\n「저거 보기 좋고 몸도 데워지는 운동 같지 않소?」 픽윅 씨가 워들에게 물었다. 워들이 두 다리를 컴퍼스 한 쌍으로 만들어 얼음 위에 복잡한 도형을 지칠 줄 모르고 그려 댄 통에 완전히 숨이 차 있을 때였다.\n\n「아, 정말 그렇소.」 워들이 대답했다. 「미끄럼 탈 줄 아시오?」\n\n「어릴 적엔 도랑에서 타곤 했지요.」 픽윅 씨가 대답했다.\n\n「지금 한번 해 보시오.」 워들이 말했다.\n\n「오, 제발, 픽윅 씨, 그러세요!」 숙녀들이 모두 외쳤다.\n\n「여러분께 즐거움을 드릴 수 있다면 무척 기쁘겠소만,」 픽윅 씨가 대답했다. 「그런 걸 안 해 본 지가 삼십 년이오.」\n\n「쳇! 쳇! 말도 안 되는 소리!」 워들이, 그의 모든 행동을 특징짓는 그 성급함으로 스케이트를 휙 벗어 던지며 말했다. 「자, 내가 함께해 드리리다. 갑시다!」 그러더니 마음씨 좋은 늙은이는 미끄럼 길을 따라 내달렸는데, 그 속도가 웰러 씨에 거의 육박하고 뚱뚱한 소년을 완전히 압도할 정도였다.\n\n픽윅 씨는 잠시 멈춰 곰곰이 생각하더니, 장갑을 벗어 모자 속에 넣고는, 두세 번 짧게 달려 보았다가 그만큼 자주 멈칫거렸고, 마침내 다시 한번 달려나가, 두 발을 1야드 4분의 1쯤 벌린 채 천천히 그리고 진지하게 미끄럼 길을 내려갔으니, 그 사이 구경꾼 모두가 흡족해하며 환호성을 질렀다.\n\n「불 꺼뜨리지 마십쇼, 나리!」 샘이 말했다. 그러자 워들이 다시 내려갔고, 그다음엔 픽윅 씨가, 그다음엔 샘이, 그다음엔 윈클 씨가, 그다음엔 밥 소여 씨가, 그다음엔 뚱뚱한 소년이, 그다음엔 스노드그래스 씨가, 서로의 발꿈치를 바짝 뒤따르며, 마치 앞으로의 인생 전망이 그 빠르기에 달려 있기라도 한 듯 열심히 서로를 뒤쫓아 달렸다.\n\n픽윅 씨가 이 의식에서 제 몫을 해내는 모습을 지켜보는 것은 더없이 흥미로운 일이었다. 뒤에 오는 사람이 자칫 그를 넘어뜨릴 위험을 무릅쓰며 점점 따라붙는 것을 바라보는 그 애타는 고뇌, 처음에 잔뜩 준 그 안간힘을 차츰 다 써 버리고는 출발한 지점을 향해 미끄럼 길 위에서 천천히 몸을 돌리는 모습, 그 거리를 다 해냈을 때 얼굴에 어리는 장난스러운 미소, 그러고 나서 몸을 돌려 앞사람을 뒤쫓아 달릴 때의 그 열성, 검은 각반이 눈 위로 경쾌하게 종종거리고 안경 너머로 두 눈이 명랑함과 기쁨을 발하던 그 모습을 음미하는 것 말이다. 그리고 그가 넘어졌을 때면(평균 세 바퀴마다 한 번씩 일어난 일이었다), 발그레 상기된 얼굴로 모자와 장갑과 손수건을 주워 모아, 그 무엇으로도 가라앉힐 수 없는 열의와 열정으로 다시 대열에 제자리를 잡는 그를 바라보는 것은 상상할 수 있는 가장 활기를 북돋우는 광경이었다.\n\n놀이가 한창 무르익고, 미끄럼이 가장 빨라지고, 웃음소리가 가장 커졌을 때, 날카롭고 짤막하게 쩍 갈라지는 소리가 들렸다. 둔덕을 향해 황급히 내달리는 무리, 숙녀들의 거친 비명, 그리고 터프먼 씨의 고함이 일었다. 커다란 얼음덩이가 사라지고, 물이 그 위로 부글부글 솟구쳐 올랐다. 픽윅 씨의 모자와 장갑과 손수건이 수면 위에 떠 있었으니, 누구든 볼 수 있는 픽윅 씨의 흔적이라고는 그것이 전부였다.\n\n낙담과 고뇌가 모든 얼굴에 새겨졌다. 남자들은 창백해지고 여자들은 기절했다. 스노드그래스 씨와 윈클 씨는 서로의 손을 맞잡고, 그들의 지도자가 빠져든 그 자리를 미친 듯한 간절함으로 응시했다. 한편 터프먼 씨는 가장 신속한 도움을 베푸는 동시에, 소리가 닿는 곳에 있을지 모를 누구에게나 이 참사를 더없이 분명하게 알리고자, 온 힘을 다해 「불이야!」를 외쳐 대며 들판을 가로질러 죽을힘을 다해 내달렸다.\n\n늙은 워들과 샘 웰러가 조심스러운 걸음으로 그 구멍에 다가가고, 벤저민 앨런 씨가 직업적 실습을 좀 향상시킬 작은 기회로서 일행 전원의 피를 뽑는 것이 좋을지를 두고 밥 소여 씨와 황급히 의논하던 바로 그 순간이었다—바로 그 순간에 얼굴과 머리와 어깨가 물속에서 솟아올라 픽윅 씨의 이목구비와 안경을 드러냈다.\n\n「잠깐만 몸을 띄우고 계세요—단 한순간만요!」 스노드그래스 씨가 고래고래 외쳤다.\n\n「그래요, 그러세요. 제발 부탁입니다—저를 위해서라도요!」 윈클 씨가 깊이 감동한 채 울부짖었다. 그 간청은 다소 불필요한 것이었으니, 십중팔구 픽윅 씨가 다른 누군가를 위해서 몸을 띄우기를 거부했다 한들, 적어도 제 자신을 위해서라면 그렇게 해 두는 편이 낫겠다는 생각쯤은 들었을 것이기 때문이다.\n\n「거기 바닥에 발이 닿소, 친구?」 워들이 말했다.\n\n「응, 물론이오.」 픽윅 씨가 머리와 얼굴에서 물을 짜내며 숨을 헐떡이며 대답했다. 「등으로 떨어졌소. 처음엔 도무지 일어설 수가 없었다오.」\n\n픽윅 씨의 코트에서 아직 드러나 있는 부분에 묻은 진흙이 이 말의 정확함을 증언해 주었다. 그리고 뚱뚱한 소년이 그곳 물이 어디든 5피트보다 깊지 않다는 것을 불현듯 떠올린 덕에 구경꾼들의 두려움이 한층 더 가신 가운데, 그를 끌어내기 위한 용맹의 위업이 펼쳐졌다. 첨벙거리고 우지끈거리고 버둥거리기를 한참 거듭한 끝에, 픽윅 씨는 마침내 그 불쾌한 처지에서 무사히 빠져나와 다시 한번 마른 땅 위에 섰다.\n\n「오, 감기로 돌아가시겠어요.」 에밀리가 말했다.\n\n「가엾어라!」 아라벨라가 말했다. 「이 숄을 둘러 드릴게요, 픽윅 씨.」\n\n「아, 그게 가장 좋은 방법이오.」 워들이 말했다. 「그걸 두르거든 다리야 날 살려라 하고 집으로 내달려서 곧장 침대로 뛰어드시오.」\n\n순식간에 숄 열두 장이 내밀어졌다. 그중 가장 두꺼운 서너 장이 골라지고, 픽윅 씨는 그것에 둘둘 싸여 웰러 씨의 안내를 받아 출발했으니, 흠뻑 젖은 데다 모자도 없고 두 팔은 옆구리에 묶인 채, 뚜렷한 목적도 없이 시속 6 잉글랜드 마일은 족히 되는 속도로 땅 위를 미끄러져 가는 노신사라는 진기한 광경을 선사했다.\n\n그러나 픽윅 씨는 이런 극단적인 상황에서 남의 눈 따위는 개의치 않았고, 샘 웰러의 재촉을 받으며 매너 농장 문 앞에 다다를 때까지 최고 속력을 유지했다. 그곳에는 터프먼 씨가 한 5분 먼저 도착해 있었는데, 부엌 굴뚝에 불이 났다는 흔들림 없는 확신을 노부인의 머릿속에 심어 놓아 그녀를 심장이 두근거리도록 겁먹게 만든 참이었다—그것은 주위의 누군가가 조금이라도 동요를 내비치기만 하면 늘 노부인의 마음속에 선명한 빛깔로 떠오르는 재앙이었다.\n\n픽윅 씨는 아늑하게 침대에 누울 때까지 한순간도 멈추지 않았다. 샘 웰러가 방에 활활 타는 불을 지피고 그의 저녁을 날라 왔다. 이어 펀치 한 그릇이 올라왔고, 그의 무사함을 기리는 거창한 술잔치가 벌어졌다. 늙은 워들은 그가 일어나려는 것을 도무지 들으려 하지 않았으므로, 그들은 침대를 의자 삼았고 픽윅 씨가 좌장을 맡았다. 두 번째, 세 번째 그릇이 주문되어 들어왔다. 그리고 픽윅 씨가 이튿날 아침 잠에서 깼을 때 그에게는 류머티즘의 기미라곤 한 점도 없었으니, 이는 밥 소여 씨가 아주 지당하게 일렀듯, 이런 경우엔 뜨거운 펀치만 한 것이 없음을, 그리고 혹여 뜨거운 펀치가 예방제로서 효험을 보이지 못한 적이 있었다면 그것은 단지 환자가 그것을 충분히 마시지 않는다는 저속한 오류에 빠졌기 때문임을 입증하는 것이다.\n\n흥겨운 일행은 이튿날 아침 해산했다. 헤어짐이란 학창 시절에는 더없이 멋진 일이지만, 그 후의 삶에서는 꽤나 고통스러운 법이다. 죽음과 사리사욕과 운명의 변천은 날마다 수많은 행복한 무리를 흩어 놓고 사방으로 멀리 갈라놓으며, 그 소년 소녀들은 결코 다시 돌아오지 않는다. 우리는 이 특정한 경우가 정확히 그러했다고 말하려는 것은 아니다. 다만 독자에게 알리고 싶은 것은, 일행의 여러 구성원이 각자의 집으로 흩어졌다는 것, 픽윅 씨와 그 친구들이 다시 한번 머글턴행 마차 지붕 위에 자리를 잡았다는 것, 그리고 아라벨라 앨런이 그녀의 오빠 벤저민과 그의 더없이 친밀하고 절친한 친구인 밥 소여 씨의 보살핌과 보호 아래, 그곳이 어디였든 간에 그녀의 목적지로 향했다는 것이다—윈클 씨는 알고 있었으리라 짐작되지만, 우리는 솔직히 모른다.\n\n그러나 헤어지기 전에, 그 신사와 벤저민 앨런 씨는 어딘가 은밀한 기색으로 픽윅 씨를 한쪽으로 끌고 갔다. 그리고 밥 소여 씨는 픽윅 씨의 갈비뼈 두 대 사이로 집게손가락을 쑤셔 넣어, 타고난 익살과 인체 해부에 관한 지식을 동시에 내보이면서 물었다—\n\n「이봐요, 영감님, 어디 묵고 계시오?」 픽윅 씨는 현재 조지 앤드 벌처에 매달려 있다고 대답했다.\n\n「저를 한번 보러 와 주셨으면 합니다.」 밥 소여가 말했다.\n\n「그보다 더 큰 기쁨은 없을 거요.」 픽윅 씨가 대답했다.\n\n「제 하숙집은 여깁니다.」 밥 소여 씨가 명함 한 장을 꺼내며 말했다. 「버러의 랜트 가입니다. 가이 병원 근처라서 저한테는 편하지요. 세인트 조지 교회를 지나서 조금만 가면 됩니다—큰길에서 오른쪽으로 빠지는 길입죠.」\n\n「찾아가지요.」 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n「두 주 뒤 목요일에 오시고, 다른 친구분들도 데려오세요.」 밥 소여 씨가 말했다. 「그날 밤엔 의학 하는 친구들 몇을 부를 참이거든요.」\n\n픽윅 씨는 그 의학 하는 친구들을 만나는 것이 자신에게 얼마나 큰 기쁨이 될지를 표했다. 그리고 밥 소여 씨가 아주 아늑하게 차릴 작정이며 친구 벤도 일행 중 하나가 될 것이라고 알린 뒤, 그들은 악수를 나누고 헤어졌다.\n\n여기서 우리는, 이 짧은 대화가 오가는 동안 윈클 씨가 아라벨라 앨런에게 속삭이고 있었는지, 그렇다면 무어라 말했는지, 더 나아가 스노드그래스 씨가 에밀리 워들과 따로 떨어져 이야기를 나누고 있었는지, 그렇다면 그가 무어라 말했는지 하는 물음에 스스로를 내맡기게 됨을 느낀다. 이에 대해 우리는 이렇게 답하노라—그들이 숙녀들에게 무어라 말했든 간에, 픽윅 씨나 터프먼 씨에게는 28마일을 가는 내내 한마디도 하지 않았으며, 그들은 무척 자주 한숨을 내쉬고 에일과 브랜디를 마다하며 침울한 표정을 지었다고. 만약 우리의 관찰력 깊은 여성 독자들이 이 사실들로부터 어떤 흡족한 추론을 이끌어 낼 수 있다면, 부디 마음껏 그리하시기를 청하는 바이다.\n\n템플 곳곳의 갖가지 구멍과 모퉁이에 어둡고 지저분한 사무실들이 흩어져 있는데, 그 안팎으로 휴정기에는 오전 내내, 개정기에는 저녁때까지도 절반은, 팔 밑에 서류 뭉치를 끼고 주머니에서도 서류가 삐죽이 비어져 나온 변호사 사무원들이 거의 끊임없이 줄지어 분주히 오가는 모습을 볼 수 있다. 변호사 사무원에도 여러 등급이 있다. 우선 수습 사무원이 있으니, 그는 권리금을 치렀고 장차 변호사가 될 사람으로, 양복점에 외상을 달아 놓고 파티 초대를 받으며, 가워 가에 한 집, 태비스톡 광장에 또 한 집 아는 가족을 두고 있고, 긴 휴정기마다 시골로 가서 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 살아 있는 말을 기르는 제 아버지를 만나 뵙는, 한마디로 사무원들 가운데 단연 귀족이다. 다음으로 봉급 사무원이 있으니, 경우에 따라 외근이거나 내근이거나 하는데, 주급 삼십 실링의 대부분을 제 향락과 치장에 쏟아붓고, 적어도 일주일에 세 번은 반값으로 아델파이 극장에 드나들며, 그 뒤에는 사과주 지하술집에서 위엄 있게 흥청거리고, 반년 전에 사라진 유행을 흉내 낸 지저분한 풍자화 같은 자다. 또 처자식 많은 중년의 필사 사무원이 있으니, 그는 늘 행색이 초라하고 자주 술에 취해 있다. 그리고 처음으로 외투를 걸친 사무실 심부름꾼 소년들이 있으니, 그들은 주간 학교에 다니는 아이들을 그럴싸하게 깔보고, 밤에 집으로 돌아갈 때면 돈을 추렴해 소시지와 흑맥주를 사 먹으며, '인생'만 한 것이 없다고 여긴다. 이 종류에는 일일이 다 헤아릴 수 없을 만큼 변종이 많지만, 아무리 많다 해도 그들은 모두 정해진 업무 시간이면 방금 말한 그 장소들을 오가며 분주히 움직이는 모습으로 볼 수 있다.\n\n이 외딴 구석들이야말로 법조계의 공공 사무소이니, 영장이 발부되고 판결이 서명되며 소장이 접수되고, 그 밖에도 폐하의 충성스러운 신민을 괴롭히고 고통스럽게 하는 한편 법조인에게는 편안함과 보수를 안겨 주는 갖가지 교묘한 기계 장치가 작동되는 곳이다. 그곳들은 대개 천장이 낮고 곰팡내 나는 방으로, 지난 한 세기 동안 남몰래 땀을 흘려 온 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 양피지 두루마리가 그윽한 냄새를 풍기는데, 그 냄새는 낮에는 마른 부패 냄새와 뒤섞이고, 밤에는 축축한 외투와 곪아 가는 우산, 그리고 가장 조악한 수지 양초에서 피어오르는 갖가지 냄새와 뒤섞인다.\n\n픽윅 씨와 그의 친구들이 런던으로 돌아온 지 열흘 내지 보름쯤 지난 어느 날 저녁 일곱 시 반쯤, 이런 사무소 가운데 한 곳으로 한 사내가 분주히 들어왔으니, 갈색 외투에 놋쇠 단추를 단 그자는 긴 머리카락을 보풀 다 빠진 모자챙에 정성스레 둘둘 감아 두었고, 때 묻은 황갈색 바지를 블뤼허 부츠 위로 어찌나 팽팽하게 졸라매었던지 무릎이 금방이라도 가려진 데서 튀어나올 것만 같았다. 그는 외투 주머니에서 길고 좁은 양피지 한 장을 꺼냈고, 주재하던 직원이 거기에 알아볼 수 없는 검은 도장을 찍었다. 그러자 그는 같은 크기의 종잇조각 넉 장을 꺼냈는데, 저마다 그 양피지의 내용을 인쇄해 베껴 놓고 이름 적을 빈칸을 둔 것이었다. 그는 빈칸을 채운 뒤 다섯 장의 서류를 모두 주머니에 넣고 부랴부랴 사라졌다.\n\n주머니에 그 비밀스러운 서류를 넣은 갈색 외투의 사내는 다름 아닌 우리의 옛 친구, 콘힐의 프리먼 광장에 있는 도드슨 앤드 포그 사무소의 잭슨 씨였다. 그러나 그는 자기가 나온 사무소로 돌아가지 않고 곧장 선 광장 쪽으로 발걸음을 옮겼고, 곧장 조지 앤드 벌처 여관으로 걸어 들어가 픽윅이라는 분이 안에 계신지 알고 싶다고 청했다.\n\n\"톰, 픽윅 씨 하인을 불러와요.\" 조지 앤드 벌처의 술집 아가씨가 말했다.\n\n\"수고하실 것 없습니다.\" 잭슨 씨가 말했다. \"일이 있어 왔으니까요. 픽윅 씨 방을 알려 주시면 제가 직접 올라가지요.\"\n\n\"성함이 어떻게 되시는지요?\" 종업원이 물었다.\n\n\"잭슨이오.\" 사무원이 대답했다.\n\n종업원이 잭슨 씨를 알리려고 위층으로 올라갔으나, 잭슨 씨는 그의 발꿈치를 바짝 따라가 그가 미처 한마디도 내뱉기 전에 방 안으로 걸어 들어감으로써 그 수고를 덜어 주었다.\n\n픽윅 씨는 그날 세 친구를 만찬에 초대해 두었는데, 잭슨 씨가 앞서 말한 대로 모습을 드러냈을 때 그들은 모두 난롯가에 둘러앉아 포도주를 마시고 있었다.\n\n\"안녕하십니까, 선생?\" 잭슨 씨가 픽윅 씨에게 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다.\n\n픽윅 씨는 인사를 하면서도 다소 놀란 듯한 표정을 지었으니, 잭슨 씨의 인상은 그의 기억 속에 남아 있지 않았기 때문이다.\n\n\"도드슨 앤드 포그 사무소에서 왔습니다.\" 잭슨 씨가 설명하는 투로 말했다.\n\n픽윅 씨는 그 이름에 정신이 번쩍 들었다. \"내 변호사에게 가시오, 선생. 그레이즈 인의 퍼커 씨 말이오.\" 그가 말했다. \"종업원, 이분을 모셔 내가시오.\"\n\n\"실례합니다만, 픽윅 씨.\" 잭슨이 모자를 차분히 바닥에 내려놓고 주머니에서 양피지를 꺼내며 말했다. \"이런 경우에는 사무원이나 대리인이 직접 송달을 해야 하거든요, 픽윅 씨. 아시다시피, 모든 법적 절차에서는 신중함만 한 것이 없지요, 안 그렇습니까?\"\n\n여기서 잭슨 씨는 양피지에 눈길을 던지더니, 두 손을 탁자에 짚고 사람을 끌어당기는 듯 설득력 있는 미소를 띠며 주위를 둘러보고 말했다. \"자, 어서요. 이렇게 사소한 일을 가지고 이러쿵저러쿵하지 맙시다. 여러분 가운데 스노드그래스라는 분이 어느 분이십니까?\"\n\n이 물음에 스노드그래스 씨가 어찌나 노골적이고 뚜렷하게 움찔하던지, 더 이상 대답할 필요도 없었다.\n\n\"아! 그러실 줄 알았습니다.\" 잭슨 씨가 아까보다 더 상냥하게 말했다. \"선생께 폐를 좀 끼칠 일이 하나 있습니다.\"\n\n\"나한테!\" 스노드그래스 씨가 외쳤다.\n\n\"바델 대 픽윅 소송에서 원고 측을 위한 소환장일 뿐입니다.\" 잭슨이 종잇조각 가운데 하나를 골라내고 조끼 주머니에서 실링 한 닢을 꺼내며 대답했다. \"개정기가 끝난 뒤 회기 중에 열릴 겁니다. 우리 예상으로는 이월 십사일이지요. 특별 배심 사건으로 표시해 두었고, 명단에서 열 번째밖에 안 됩니다. 이게 선생 것입니다, 스노드그래스 씨.\" 잭슨은 이렇게 말하면서 양피지를 스노드그래스 씨의 눈앞에 들이밀고는 종이와 실링을 그의 손에 슬쩍 쥐여 주었다.\n\n터프먼 씨가 말없이 놀라며 이 과정을 지켜보고 있는데, 잭슨이 그에게 홱 돌아서며 말했다.\n\n\"선생 성함이 터프먼이라고 해도 틀린 건 아니겠지요, 그렇죠?\"\n\n터프먼 씨는 픽윅 씨를 바라보았으나, 그 신사의 휘둥그레진 눈에서 제 이름을 부인하라는 기색을 찾지 못하자 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"그렇소, 내 이름은 터프먼이오, 선생.\"\n\n\"그리고 저 다른 신사분은 윈클 씨이신 것 같은데요?\" 잭슨이 말했다. 윈클 씨가 더듬더듬 그렇다고 대답하자, 솜씨 좋은 잭슨 씨는 곧바로 두 신사에게 각각 종잇조각 한 장과 실링 한 닢씩을 안겨 주었다.\n\n\"자,\" 잭슨이 말했다. \"제가 좀 성가시게 군다고 여기실까 봐 걱정입니다만, 불편하지 않으시다면 한 분 더 뵈어야겠습니다. 여기 새뮤얼 웰러라는 이름이 적혀 있습니다, 픽윅 씨.\"\n\n\"종업원, 내 하인을 이리로 보내 주시오.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. 종업원은 적잖이 놀란 채 물러갔고, 픽윅 씨는 잭슨에게 자리에 앉으라고 손짓했다.\n\n괴로운 침묵이 흘렀고, 마침내 그 침묵을 깨뜨린 것은 결백한 피고였다.\n\n\"내 짐작에는, 선생,\" 픽윅 씨가 말하면서 점점 분노가 치밀어 올랐다. \"내 짐작에는, 선생, 당신네 고용주들의 의도가 내 친구들의 증언을 이용해 나를 죄인으로 몰려는 것 같소만?\"\n\n잭슨 씨는 자기가 감옥의 비밀을 누설하러 온 사람이 아님을 내비치려는 듯 집게손가락으로 코 왼쪽을 몇 번 두드리더니 짓궂게 받아쳤다.\n\n\"모르는 일이니 말씀드릴 수 없지요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 다른 무슨 이유로,\" 픽윅 씨가 계속했다. \"이런 소환장들이 그들에게 송달되겠소, 그게 아니라면 말이오?\"\n\n\"수가 아주 좋습니다, 픽윅 씨.\" 잭슨이 천천히 고개를 가로저으며 대답했다. \"하지만 안 통합니다. 시도하는 거야 나쁠 것 없지만, 저한테서는 캐낼 게 별로 없을 겁니다.\"\n\n여기서 잭슨 씨는 일행을 향해 다시 한번 미소를 짓더니, 왼손 엄지를 코끝에 갖다 대고 오른손으로 허공의 커피 분쇄기를 돌리는 시늉을 하며 더없이 우아한 무언극(그때는 크게 유행했으나 지금은 안타깝게도 거의 자취를 감춘) 한 토막을 선보였으니, 그것은 흔히 '맷돌질하기'라고 불리던 것이었다.\n\n\"아닙니다, 아니에요, 픽윅 씨.\" 잭슨이 끝맺으며 말했다. \"퍼커네 사람들이 우리가 왜 이 소환장들을 송달했는지 알아맞혀야겠지요. 못 알아맞히면 소송이 시작될 때까지 기다렸다가 그때 가서 알게 될 겁니다.\"\n\n픽윅 씨는 반갑잖은 방문객에게 극도의 혐오가 담긴 눈길을 던졌고, 마침 샘이 들어와 가로막지 않았더라면 도드슨 앤드 포그 두 분의 머리 위에 어떤 무시무시한 저주를 퍼부었을지도 모른다.\n\n\"새뮤얼 웰러?\" 잭슨 씨가 묻듯이 말했다.\n\n\"여러 해 만에 처음으로 맞는 말씀을 하나 하셨네요.\" 샘이 더없이 침착한 태도로 대답했다.\n\n\"여기 소환장이 있소, 웰러 씨.\" 잭슨이 말했다.\n\n\"그게 사람 말로 하면 뭐요?\" 샘이 물었다.\n\n\"여기 원본이 있소.\" 잭슨은 요구받은 설명을 거절하며 말했다.\n\n\"어느 거요?\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n\"이거요.\" 잭슨이 양피지를 흔들며 대답했다.\n\n\"오, 그게 원본입니까?\" 샘이 말했다. \"음, 원본을 보게 되어 아주 기쁘네요. 흐뭇한 일이고, 마음이 한결 놓이거든요.\"\n\n\"그리고 여기 실링도 있소.\" 잭슨이 말했다. \"도드슨 앤드 포그가 주는 거요.\"\n\n\"저를 그렇게나 모르시는 도드슨 앤드 포그가 선물을 다 내려 주시다니 참으로 후하시네요.\" 샘이 말했다. \"아주 높은 칭찬으로 여기겠습니다, 선생. 어디서든 공로를 만나면 그걸 보상할 줄 아시니, 그분들로서는 참 명예로운 일이지요. 게다가 사람 마음을 다 뭉클하게 합니다그려.\"\n\n웰러 씨는 이렇게 말하면서 가정극의 비장한 장면에 임한 배우들이 으레 쓰는 가장 그럴듯한 방식으로 외투 소매로 오른쪽 눈꺼풀을 살짝 비볐다.\n\n잭슨 씨는 샘의 행동에 다소 어리둥절한 듯했으나, 소환장을 다 송달했고 더 할 말도 없는지라, 체면을 차리려고 늘 손에 들고 다니던 장갑 한 짝을 끼는 시늉을 하고는 진행 상황을 보고하러 사무소로 돌아갔다.\n\n픽윅 씨는 그날 밤 잠을 거의 이루지 못했으니, 바델 부인의 소송 건에 대한 그의 기억이 매우 불쾌하게 되살아났기 때문이다. 그는 이튿날 아침 일찍 식사를 마치고 샘에게 따라오라 이른 뒤 그레이즈 인 광장 쪽으로 길을 나섰다.\n\n\"샘!\" 칩사이드 끝에 이르렀을 때 픽윅 씨가 주위를 둘러보며 말했다.\n\n\"네, 나리?\" 샘이 주인에게 다가서며 말했다.\n\n\"어느 쪽이냐?\"\n\n\"뉴게이트 가로 올라가십니다.\"\n\n픽윅 씨는 곧바로 돌아서지 않고 잠시 멍하니 샘의 얼굴을 바라보다가 깊은 한숨을 내쉬었다.\n\n\"무슨 일이십니까, 나리?\" 샘이 물었다.\n\n\"이 소송 말이다, 샘.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"다음 달 십사일에 열린다는구나.\"\n\n\"그것참 묘한 우연이네요, 나리.\" 샘이 대답했다.\n\n\"왜 묘하다는 거냐, 샘?\" 픽윅 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"밸런타인 날이거든요, 나리.\" 샘이 응했다. \"혼인 약속 위반 재판에는 더없이 잘 어울리는 날이지요.\"\n\n웰러 씨의 미소도 주인의 얼굴에는 한 줄기 기쁨의 빛조차 불러일으키지 못했다. 픽윅 씨는 휙 돌아서서 말없이 앞장섰다.\n\n그들은 얼마간 걸어갔는데, 픽윅 씨는 깊은 사색에 잠긴 채 앞서 종종걸음을 쳤고, 샘은 무엇이든 누구든 가장 부럽고도 태평하게 무시하는 표정으로 뒤를 따랐다. 그러다 샘은—그는 늘 자기만 아는 정보를 주인에게 일러 주고 싶어 안달이었으므로—걸음을 빨리해 픽윅 씨의 발꿈치 바로 뒤까지 따라붙더니, 지나치던 어느 집을 가리키며 말했다.\n\n\"저거 아주 근사한 돼지고기 가게입니다요, 나리.\"\n\n\"그래, 그런 것 같구나.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"이름난 소시지 공장이지요.\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n\"그러냐?\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그러냐고요!\" 샘이 다소 분개하며 되받았다. \"그렇고말고요. 아니, 나리, 그 순진한 눈썹에 복이나 받으십시오, 사 년 전에 어느 점잖은 상인이 수상쩍게 사라진 데가 바로 저깁니다요.\"\n\n\"설마 그 사람이 살해당해 시신을 팔아넘겨졌다는 건 아니겠지, 샘?\" 픽윅 씨가 황급히 주위를 둘러보며 말했다.\n\n\"아니요, 정말 그런 건 아닙니다, 나리.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다. \"차라리 그랬으면 좋겠습니다. 그보다 훨씬 끔찍하지요. 그 사람은 저 가게 주인이었는데요, 나리, 특허 받은 '결코-멈추지-않는' 소시지 증기 기계를 발명한 사람이었지요. 그 기계가 어찌나 대단한지, 포석을 너무 가까이 갖다 대면 통째로 삼켜서는 연한 갓난아기를 다루듯 손쉽게 소시지로 갈아 버렸답니다. 그 사람은 그 기계를 무척 자랑스러워했지요, 그럴 만도 했고요. 기계가 한창 돌아갈 때면 지하실에 내려가 서서 그걸 바라보다가 기쁨에 겨워 아주 우울해질 지경이었답니다. 그 기계 하고, 거기다 사랑스러운 어린것이 둘이나 더 있었으니, 나리, 그 사람은 그야말로 행복한 사람이었을 겁니다. 그 아내만 아니었더라면요. 그 여편네가 아주 발칙한 표독한 여자였거든요. 늘 남편을 졸졸 따라다니며 귀에다 잔소리를 퍼부어 댔으니, 마침내 그 사람도 더는 못 견디게 됐지요. '여보, 내 분명히 말해 두겠는데,' 어느 날 그 사람이 그럽니다. '당신이 이런 식의 재미를 계속 부린다면, 내가 미국으로 떠나 버려도 난 모르겠소. 그게 다요.' '당신은 게으른 망나니야,' 여편네가 그러죠. '미국 사람들이 당신 같은 걸 차지하게 됐으니 좋아라 하겠네.' 그러고는 반 시간 동안 그 사람을 욕해 대다가 가게 뒤편 작은 거실로 뛰어 들어가 비명을 질러 대며, 당신이 날 죽이려 든다고 하더니, 발작을 일으켜 꼬박 세 시간이나 가는 겁니다. 온통 비명에 발길질뿐인 그런 발작 중 하나였지요. 자, 그런데 이튿날 아침, 남편이 사라진 거예요. 금고에서 한 푼도 가져가지 않았고, 외투조차 걸치지 않았으니, 미국으로 가지 않은 건 분명했지요. 다음 날도 안 돌아오고, 다음 주에도 안 돌아오자, 마누라가 전단을 인쇄해서, 돌아오기만 하면 다 용서하겠다고 했답니다(아주 너그러운 일이었지요, 그 사람이 아무 잘못도 하지 않은 걸 생각하면요). 운하란 운하는 죄다 훑었고, 그 뒤로 두 달 동안은 시신이 떠오를 때마다 으레 곧장 소시지 가게로 실어 갔답니다. 하지만 그 가운데 들어맞는 건 하나도 없었지요. 그래서 사람들은 그가 달아났다고 결론짓고, 마누라는 계속 장사를 했어요. 그런데 어느 토요일 밤, 작고 비쩍 마른 늙은 신사 양반이 잔뜩 화가 난 채 가게로 들어와서 그럽니다. '당신이 이 가게 주인 마누라요?' '네, 그런데요.' 여편네가 그래요. '음, 부인,' 그 양반이 그러죠. '그럼 잠깐 들러 한마디 하리다. 나와 내 가족은 까닭 없이 목이 막혀 죽지는 않겠소이다. 게다가 부인,' 그 양반이 그래요. '한마디 덧붙이자면, 소시지를 만드는 데 고기에서 제일 좋은 부위를 안 쓰는 모양인데, 그렇다면 소고기가 단추만큼이나 싸게 먹힐 것 같소이다.' '단추라니요, 선생!' 여편네가 그래요. '단추 말이오, 부인.' 작고 늙은 신사 양반이 종잇조각을 펼쳐 스무 개에서 서른 개쯤 되는 반 토막 난 단추들을 보여 주며 그럽니다. '바지 단추란 게 소시지 양념으로는 아주 그만이로군요, 부인.' '그거 우리 남편 단추예요!' 과부가 기절하기 시작하며 그래요. '뭐라고요!' 작고 늙은 신사 양반이 새파랗게 질려 소리칩니다. '이제야 다 알겠어.' 과부가 그래요. '일시적인 정신착란을 일으켜서 무모하게도 제 몸을 소시지로 만들어 버린 거야!' 정말 그랬던 겁니다, 나리.\" 웰러 씨가 픽윅 씨의 공포에 질린 얼굴을 빤히 들여다보며 말했다. \"아니면 기계에 빨려 들어갔거나요. 어느 쪽이었든 간에, 평생 소시지를 끔찍이 좋아하던 그 작고 늙은 신사 양반은 미친 듯이 가게를 뛰쳐나가서는 그 뒤로 다시는 소식이 없었답니다!\"\n\n사생활의 이 가슴 아픈 사건을 늘어놓는 동안 주인과 하인은 퍼커 씨의 사무실에 다다랐다. 라우튼이 문을 반쯤 연 채로, 발가락 없는 부츠에 손가락 없는 장갑을 낀, 녹슨 듯 초라한 차림의 비참한 행색을 한 사내와 이야기를 나누고 있었다. 야위고 근심에 찌든 그의 얼굴에는 궁핍과 고통의—거의 절망에 가까운—흔적이 서려 있었다. 그는 제 가난을 의식하는지, 픽윅 씨가 다가가자 계단의 어두운 쪽으로 몸을 움츠렸다.\n\n\"참으로 딱하게 됐군요.\" 그 낯선 사내가 한숨을 쉬며 말했다.\n\n\"딱하지요.\" 라우튼이 펜으로 문기둥에 제 이름을 끄적이고는 깃촉으로 다시 지우며 말했다. \"그분께 전할 말이라도 남기시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"언제쯤 돌아오실 것 같소?\" 낯선 사내가 물었다.\n\n\"전혀 알 수 없습니다.\" 라우튼은 낯선 사내가 눈길을 바닥으로 떨구는 사이 픽윅 씨에게 눈을 찡긋하며 대답했다.\n\n\"내가 기다린다 한들 아무 소용 없을 것 같소?\" 낯선 사내가 간절한 눈빛으로 사무실 안을 들여다보며 말했다.\n\n\"아, 그럼요, 소용없을 게 분명합니다.\" 사무원이 문간 한가운데로 조금 더 옮겨 서며 대답했다. \"이번 주에는 틀림없이 안 돌아오실 거고, 다음 주에 돌아오실지도 장담 못 합니다. 퍼커 씨는 한번 시내를 벗어나면 좀처럼 서둘러 돌아오시는 법이 없거든요.\"\n\n\"시내를 벗어났다고요!\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"이런, 참 딱하게 됐군요!\"\n\n\"가지 마십시오, 픽윅 씨.\" 라우튼이 말했다. \"선생께 드릴 편지가 있습니다.\" 낯선 사내는 망설이는 듯 다시 한번 바닥 쪽으로 눈길을 던졌고, 사무원은 무슨 절묘한 익살이 벌어지고 있다는 듯 픽윅 씨에게 슬쩍 눈을 찡긋했다. 그러나 그게 무엇인지 픽윅 씨로서는 도무지 짐작할 수 없었다.\n\n\"들어오십시오, 픽윅 씨.\" 라우튼이 말했다. \"자, 전할 말을 남기시겠습니까, 와티 씨, 아니면 다시 들르시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"제발 부탁이니 제 일이 어떻게 됐는지 한 말씀 남겨 주십사 그분께 청해 주시오.\" 사내가 말했다. \"제발 부탁이니 잊지 말아 주시오, 라우튼 씨.\"\n\n\"네, 네, 잊지 않겠습니다.\" 사무원이 대답했다. \"들어오십시오, 픽윅 씨. 안녕히 가십시오, 와티 씨. 산책하기 좋은 날씨네요, 그렇지 않습니까?\" 낯선 사내가 여전히 머뭇거리는 것을 보고, 사무원은 샘 웰러에게 주인을 따라 들어오라고 손짓하고는 그의 면전에서 문을 닫았다.\n\n\"세상이 생긴 이래로 저렇게 사람 귀찮게 구는 파산자는 또 없을 겁니다, 정말이지!\" 라우튼이 모욕당한 사람의 표정으로 펜을 내던지며 말했다. \"저 사람 사건이 형평법원에 걸린 지 아직 사 년도 안 됐는데, 일주일에 두 번씩 여기 와서 들볶지 뭡니까, 제기랄. 이쪽으로 오시지요, 픽윅 씨. 퍼커 씨는 안에 계십니다. 선생을 만나 주실 겁니다, 제가 압니다. 지독하게 춥군요.\" 그가 짜증스레 덧붙였다. \"저런 꾀죄죄한 부랑자랑 시간이나 허비하며 저 문간에 서 있자니 말입니다!\" 사무원은 유달리 작은 부지깽이로 유달리 큰 불을 사납게 휘젓고는 자기 상관의 개인 방으로 앞장서 가서 픽윅 씨가 왔음을 알렸다.\n\n\"아, 친애하는 선생.\" 작은 체구의 퍼커 씨가 의자에서 부산스레 일어나며 말했다. \"자, 친애하는 선생, 선생 일은 좀 어찌 돼 갑니까, 응? 프리먼 광장의 우리 친구들에 관해 더 들으신 거라도 있습니까? 그들이 손 놓고 있지는 않았을 겁니다, 그건 제가 압니다. 아, 아주 영민한 친구들이지요. 정말이지 아주 영민해요.\"\n\n작은 사내는 말을 맺으며 도드슨과 포그 두 분의 영민함에 바치는 헌사인 양 코담배를 힘주어 한 줌 들이마셨다.\n\n\"그들은 대단한 악당들이오.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"아, 그렇지요.\" 작은 사내가 말했다. \"그거야 보기 나름이지요. 그러니 표현을 두고 다투지는 맙시다. 선생께서야 당연히 이런 일들을 전문가의 눈으로 보실 수는 없을 테니까요. 자, 필요한 건 다 해 두었습니다. 스너빈 변호인을 선임했지요.\"\n\n\"실력 있는 사람이오?\" 픽윅 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"실력 있는 사람이냐고요!\" 퍼커가 대답했다. \"이런, 친애하는 선생, 마음과 영혼에 복이나 받으십시오, 스너빈 변호인은 그 직업의 정점에 있는 분입니다. 법정의 그 누구보다도 세 배나 많은 사건을 맡고, 어느 사건에나 관여하지요. 밖에 나가 말씀하실 건 없습니다만, 우리끼리는—우리 같은 동업자들끼리는—스너빈 변호인이 법정을 제멋대로 쥐락펴락한다고들 합니다.\"\n\n작은 사내는 이 말을 전하며 코담배를 또 한 줌 들이마시고는 픽윅 씨에게 의미심장하게 고개를 끄덕였다.\n\n\"그들이 내 친구 셋에게 소환장을 보냈소.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"아! 당연히 그러겠지요.\" 퍼커가 대답했다. \"중요한 증인들이니까요. 선생이 미묘한 상황에 처한 걸 본 사람들 아닙니까.\"\n\n\"하지만 그 여자가 제멋대로 기절한 거요.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"내 품으로 제가 알아서 뛰어든 거란 말이오.\"\n\n\"충분히 그럴 법한 일이지요, 친애하는 선생.\" 퍼커가 대답했다. \"충분히 그럴 법하고 아주 자연스러운 일입니다. 그보다 더 자연스러운 일도 없지요, 친애하는 선생, 없고말고요. 하지만 그걸 누가 입증해 주겠습니까?\"\n\n\"그들은 내 하인에게도 소환장을 보냈소.\" 픽윅 씨가 그 점은 접어 두고 말했으니, 거기서 퍼커 씨의 질문이 그를 다소 당황하게 했기 때문이다.\n\n\"샘 말입니까?\" 퍼커가 말했다.\n\n픽윅 씨가 그렇다고 대답했다.\n\n\"당연하지요, 친애하는 선생, 당연합니다. 그럴 줄 알았어요. 한 달 전에도 말씀드릴 수 있었습니다. 아시다시피, 친애하는 선생, 일을 변호사에게 맡겨 놓고서도 그 처리를 굳이 손수 떠맡으신다면, 그 결과 또한 떠안으셔야 하는 법이지요.\" 여기서 퍼커 씨는 자못 위엄을 차리며 몸을 곧추세우고는 셔츠 주름 장식에 떨어진 코담배 가루 몇 알을 털어 냈다.\n\n\"그래서 그들이 그에게서 입증하려는 게 뭐요?\" 이삼 분 침묵한 뒤 픽윅 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"선생께서 그를 원고에게 보내 무슨 화해 제안을 하게 했다는 것이겠지요, 아마도요.\" 퍼커가 대답했다. \"하지만 그건 별로 대수롭지 않습니다. 그에게서 많은 걸 캐낼 수 있는 변호사는 그리 많지 않을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"나도 그렇게 생각하오.\" 픽윅 씨가 샘이 증인으로 나설 모습을 떠올리며, 짜증 속에서도 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"그럼 우리는 어떤 방침을 취하오?\"\n\n\"우리가 취할 길은 단 하나뿐입니다, 친애하는 선생.\" 퍼커가 대답했다. \"증인들을 반대신문하고, 스너빈의 웅변에 기대고, 판사의 눈에 먼지를 뿌리고, 배심원들에게 우리를 맡기는 것이지요.\"\n\n\"그래서 평결이 내게 불리하게 나오면?\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n퍼커 씨는 미소를 짓고 코담배를 아주 길게 한 줌 들이마시더니, 불을 휘젓고 어깨를 으쓱하고는 의미심장하게 침묵을 지켰다.\n\n\"그 경우엔 내가 손해 배상금을 물어야 한다는 뜻이오?\" 이 무언의 답을 자못 엄숙하게 지켜보던 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n퍼커는 또다시 전혀 쓸데없이 불을 한 번 쑤시고는 말했다. \"유감스럽게도 그렇습니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 분명히 알려 드리리다. 나는 손해 배상금을 단 한 푼도 물지 않기로 단단히 마음먹었소.\" 픽윅 씨가 더없이 단호하게 말했다. \"한 푼도요, 퍼커. 내 돈은 단 일 파운드도, 단 일 페니도 도드슨과 포그의 주머니로 들어가는 일은 없을 것이오. 이것이 신중히 내린, 결코 번복할 수 없는 결심이오.\" 픽윅 씨는 제 결심이 번복될 수 없음을 확인하듯 앞에 놓인 탁자를 세게 내리쳤다.\n\n\"좋습니다, 친애하는 선생, 좋아요.\" 퍼커가 말했다. \"물론 선생이 제일 잘 아시겠지요.\"\n\n\"물론이오.\" 픽윅 씨가 황급히 대답했다. \"스너빈 변호인은 어디 사오?\"\n\n\"링컨스 인 올드 광장에 삽니다.\" 퍼커가 대답했다.\n\n\"그를 만나고 싶소.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"스너빈 변호인을 만나시겠다고요, 친애하는 선생!\" 퍼커가 어처구니없다는 듯 되받았다. \"쯧쯧, 친애하는 선생, 그건 불가능합니다. 스너빈 변호인을 만나다니요! 이런, 친애하는 선생, 그런 일은 들어 본 적도 없습니다. 상담료를 미리 치르고 상담 일정을 잡지 않고서는요. 안 될 일입니다, 친애하는 선생. 안 될 일이에요.\"\n\n그러나 픽윅 씨는 그 일이 될 수 있을 뿐만 아니라 반드시 되어야 한다고 마음을 굳혔다. 그리하여 그 일이 불가능하다는 다짐을 들은 지 십 분도 채 안 되어, 그는 자기 변호사의 안내를 받아 다름 아닌 위대한 스너빈 변호인의 바깥 사무실로 들어서게 되었다.\n\n그곳은 카펫이 깔리지 않은 제법 넓은 방으로, 난로 가까이에 커다란 필기용 탁자가 놓여 있었는데, 그 탁자에 씌운 모직 천은 본래의 초록빛을 주장할 자격을 잃은 지 오래여서, 잉크 얼룩으로 본래 색깔의 흔적마저 지워진 자리를 빼고는 먼지와 세월에 차츰 잿빛으로 바래 있었다. 탁자 위에는 붉은 끈으로 묶인 작은 서류 뭉치들이 잔뜩 널려 있었고, 그 뒤에는 한 늙은 사무원이 앉아 있었으니, 그의 매끈한 행색과 묵직한 금시계 줄은 스너빈 변호인의 폭넓고 짭짤한 일거리를 위풍당당하게 드러내고 있었다.\n\n\"변호인께서는 방에 계십니까, 맬러드 씨?\" 퍼커가 더없이 정중하게 코담배갑을 내밀며 물었다.\n\n\"네, 계십니다.\" 대답이 돌아왔다. \"하지만 무척 바쁘십니다. 보십시오, 이 사건들 가운데 어느 하나에도 아직 의견서를 안 내셨는데, 다들 신속 처리비까지 치렀거든요.\" 사무원은 이렇게 말하며 미소를 짓고는, 코담배를 좋아하는 마음과 수수료를 즐기는 마음이 한데 어우러진 듯한 흐뭇함으로 코담배를 한 줌 들이마셨다.\n\n\"제법 일이 많으신 모양이군요.\" 퍼커가 말했다.\n\n\"네.\" 변호인의 사무원이 제 코담배갑을 꺼내 더없이 다정하게 내밀며 말했다. \"그리고 무엇보다 좋은 건, 저 말고는 살아 있는 어느 누구도 변호인의 글씨를 못 읽으니, 변호인이 의견서를 내 주셔도 제가 그걸 베껴 쓸 때까지 다들 꼼짝없이 기다려야 한다는 거지요, 하하하!\"\n\n\"그러면 변호인 말고도 우리가 다 아는 누군가한테 득이 되고, 의뢰인들한테서 조금 더 우려낼 수 있겠군요, 응?\" 퍼커가 말했다. \"하, 하, 하!\" 이 말에 변호인의 사무원이 다시 웃었으니, 떠들썩하게 요란한 웃음이 아니라 소리 없이 속으로 킬킬대는 웃음이었는데, 픽윅 씨는 그 소리를 듣기 싫어했다. 사람이 속으로 피를 흘리는 것은 제 몸에 위험한 일이지만, 속으로 웃는 것은 남들에게 좋은 조짐이 아니다.\n\n\"제가 갚아야 할 수수료 목록은 아직 안 만들어 주셨지요?\" 퍼커가 말했다.\n\n\"네, 아직 안 만들었습니다.\" 사무원이 대답했다.\n\n\"좀 만들어 주셨으면 합니다.\" 퍼커가 말했다. \"그걸 주시면 수표를 보내 드리지요. 하지만 현찰 챙기느라 너무 바빠서 빚진 사람들 생각할 겨를이 없으신 모양이군요, 응? 하, 하, 하!\" 이 농담이 사무원을 굉장히 즐겁게 한 듯, 그는 또 한 번 혼자 조용히 웃음을 즐겼다.\n\n\"한데 맬러드 씨, 친애하는 벗이여.\" 퍼커가 갑자기 진지한 표정을 되찾고는 그 위대한 분의 위대한 분을 외투 깃을 잡아 한쪽 구석으로 끌고 가며 말했다. \"변호인께서 저와 여기 제 의뢰인을 만나 주시도록 설득해 주셔야겠습니다.\"\n\n\"이런, 이런.\" 사무원이 말했다. \"그것도 제법 괜찮은 말씀이군요. 변호인을 만난다니요! 원, 너무 터무니없습니다.\" 그러나 그 제안이 터무니없음에도 불구하고, 사무원은 픽윅 씨의 귀에 들리지 않는 곳까지 슬슬 끌려가도록 내버려 두었고, 속닥속닥 짧게 이야기를 나눈 뒤 어두운 작은 복도를 살금살금 내려가더니 그 법조계 명사의 성소 안으로 사라졌다가, 이내 발끝으로 살그머니 돌아와 퍼커 씨와 픽윅 씨에게, 변호인이 모든 확립된 규칙과 관례를 어기고 두 사람을 당장 들이도록 설득되었다고 알렸다.\n\n스너빈 변호인은 얼굴이 등잔처럼 길쭉하고 안색이 누르께한 사내로, 마흔다섯쯤 되었거나—소설에서 으레 말하듯—쉰쯤 되었을 수도 있었다. 그는 여러 해 동안 고되고 힘겨운 학업에 매달려 온 사람들의 머리에서 흔히 볼 수 있는, 흐리멍덩하고 삶은 듯한 눈을 가지고 있었으니, 그것만으로도—목에 두른 넓은 검은 리본에 매달린 안경이 없더라도—낯선 이에게 그가 몹시 근시임을 알리기에 충분했다. 그의 머리카락은 가늘고 힘이 없었는데, 이는 한편으로는 그가 머리 손질에 시간을 들인 적이 없기 때문이요, 한편으로는 이십오 년 동안 옆에 놓인 받침대에 걸려 있는 법정용 가발을 써 온 탓이었다. 외투 깃에 묻은 가발 분 자국과, 목에 두른 잘 빨지도 못하고 더 엉성하게 맨 흰 목도리를 보면, 그가 법정을 나선 뒤 옷차림에 손볼 겨를조차 없었음을 알 수 있었다. 한편 나머지 차림새가 칠칠치 못한 것으로 미루어, 설령 손을 봤다 한들 그의 외양이 크게 나아지지는 않았으리라 짐작할 만했다. 실무 서적과 서류 더미, 뜯어 놓은 편지들이 정돈하거나 배열하려는 어떤 시도도 없이 탁자 위에 어지러이 흩어져 있었고, 방의 가구는 낡고 삐걱거렸으며, 책장 문은 경첩에서 썩어 가고 있었다. 걸음을 옮길 때마다 카펫에서 먼지가 작은 구름처럼 풀썩풀썩 피어올랐고, 차양은 세월과 때로 누렇게 바래 있었다. 방 안 모든 것의 상태가, 의심할 여지없이 분명하게, 스너빈 변호인이 제 직업적 일에 너무도 몰두한 나머지 제 개인적 안락 따위에는 그다지 마음 쓰거나 신경 쓸 겨를이 없음을 보여 주고 있었다.\n\n변호인은 의뢰인들이 들어왔을 때 글을 쓰고 있었다. 그는 픽윅 씨가 그의 변호사에게 소개되자 건성으로 고개를 숙였고, 이어 두 사람에게 자리에 앉으라 손짓하고는 펜을 조심스레 잉크병에 꽂은 뒤, 왼쪽 다리를 끌어안고 누가 말을 걸어 주기를 기다렸다.\n\n\"픽윅 씨가 바델 대 픽윅 소송의 피고입니다, 스너빈 변호인.\" 퍼커가 말했다.\n\n\"내가 그 사건을 맡았던가요?\" 변호인이 말했다.\n\n\"그렇습니다, 선생.\" 퍼커가 대답했다.\n\n변호인은 고개를 끄덕이고는 또 다른 무언가를 기다렸다.\n\n\"픽윅 씨가 변호인을 꼭 찾아뵙고 싶어 하셨습니다, 스너빈 변호인.\" 퍼커가 말했다. \"변호인께서 사건에 착수하시기 전에 말씀드리고 싶으셨던 거지요. 자기를 상대로 한 이 소송에는 어떤 근거나 구실도 전혀 없음을 부인하신다고요. 그리고 자신이 깨끗한 손으로, 또 원고의 요구에 맞서는 것이 옳다는 더없이 양심적인 확신을 품고 법정에 서는 것이 아니라면, 애초에 법정에 나가지도 않겠다고요. 제가 선생의 뜻을 정확히 전하고 있는 거지요? 그렇지 않습니까, 친애하는 선생?\" 작은 사내가 픽윅 씨를 돌아보며 말했다.\n\n\"바로 그렇소.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다.\n\n스너빈 변호인은 안경을 펴서 눈에 갖다 대고는, 잠시 큰 호기심을 품고 픽윅 씨를 바라보더니, 퍼커 씨에게 돌아서며 말하는 내내 가볍게 미소를 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"픽윅 씨는 사건이 유리합니까?\"\n\n변호사는 어깨를 으쓱했다.\n\n\"증인을 부를 작정이오?\"\n\"아닙니다.\"\n\n변호인의 얼굴에 떠오른 미소가 한층 또렷해졌다. 그는 더욱 사납게 다리를 흔들더니, 안락의자에 몸을 뒤로 젖히고는 미심쩍은 듯 기침을 했다.\n\n이 문제에 대한 변호인의 예감을 드러내는 이런 징표들은, 비록 사소하기는 했으나, 픽윅 씨에게 그냥 지나쳐지지 않았다. 그는 변호인이 스스로 드러내도록 허락한 만큼의 감정 표시를 주의 깊게 살피던 안경을 코 위에 더 단단히 고쳐 쓰고는, 퍼커 씨가 보내는 온갖 경고의 눈짓과 찌푸림을 전혀 아랑곳하지 않은 채 자못 힘차게 말했다.\n\n\"제가 이런 목적으로 선생을 찾아뵙고자 한 것이, 이런 일들을 으레 그토록 많이 보아 오셨을 신사분께는, 의심할 바 없이 매우 유별난 일로 비치리라 봅니다.\"\n\n변호인은 진지하게 불을 바라보려 했으나, 미소가 다시 떠올랐다.\n\n\"선생 같은 직업을 가진 분들은,\" 픽윅 씨가 계속했다. \"인간 본성의 가장 추악한 면을 보십니다. 인간의 온갖 분쟁, 온갖 악의와 반목이 선생 앞에 펼쳐지지요. 선생은 배심원에 대한 경험으로(선생이나 배심원들을 깎아내리려는 뜻은 결코 아닙니다) 효과라는 것이 얼마나 큰 비중을 차지하는지 아십니다. 그리고 선생은, 순수한 정직과 명예로운 목적으로, 의뢰인을 위해 최선을 다하려는 갸륵한 마음으로, 또 늘 손수 다루어 오신 까닭에 그 본성과 가치를 너무도 잘 알고 계신 바로 그 수단들을, 남들도 속임수와 사리사욕을 위해 쓰려 든다고 여기기 쉽습니다. 정말이지 저는, 선생들이 한 집단으로서 의심 많고 불신에 차 있으며 지나치게 조심스럽다는 저속하지만 매우 일반적인 통념이 바로 이 정황에서 비롯되었다고 믿습니다. 이런 상황에서 선생께 이런 선언을 하는 것이 불리하다는 걸 잘 알면서도, 저는 여기 왔습니다. 제 벗 퍼커 씨가 말씀드렸듯이, 제게 씌워진 거짓 혐의에 저는 결백하다는 것을 선생께서 분명히 이해해 주시기를 바라기 때문입니다. 또한 선생의 도움이 더없이 값진 것임을 잘 알면서도, 한 말씀 덧붙이지 않을 수 없습니다. 선생께서 이를 진심으로 믿어 주시지 않는다면, 저는 차라리 선생의 재능이 주는 이점을 누리느니 그 도움을 받지 못하는 편을 택하겠습니다.\"\n\n우리로서는 픽윅 씨치고는 매우 장황한 연설이었다고 말하지 않을 수 없는 이 일장 연설이 채 끝나기도 훨씬 전에, 변호인은 이미 멍한 상태로 빠져들어 있었다. 그러나 몇 분 뒤, 그사이 다시 펜을 집어 들었던 그는 의뢰인들이 곁에 있음을 다시금 깨달은 듯, 종이에서 고개를 들고 다소 퉁명스럽게 말했다.\n\n\"이 사건에 나와 함께하는 사람이 누구지요?\"\n\n\"펑키 씨입니다, 스너빈 변호인.\" 변호사가 대답했다.\n\n\"펑키, 펑키.\" 변호인이 말했다. \"그런 이름은 전에 들어 본 적이 없는데. 아주 젊은 사람이겠군요.\"\n\n\"네, 아주 젊은 사람입니다.\" 변호사가 대답했다. \"얼마 전에야 변호사 자격을 얻었지요. 어디 보자, 법정에 선 지 아직 팔 년도 안 됐습니다.\"\n\n\"아, 그러리라 생각했소.\" 변호인이, 보통 사람들이 아주 가엾고 무력한 어린아이를 두고 말할 법한 그런 측은해하는 투로 말했다. \"맬러드 씨, 사람을 보내 미스터—미스터—\"\n\n\"펑키 씨네에요. 그레이즈 인의 홀번 광장입니다.\" 퍼커가 끼어들었다. (그런데 홀번 광장은 지금은 사우스 광장이다.) \"펑키 씨에게, 잠깐 이리로 와 주면 고맙겠다고 전해 주십시오.\"\n\n맬러드 씨는 그 분부를 수행하러 떠났고, 스너빈 변호인은 펑키 씨가 직접 안내되어 들어올 때까지 다시 멍한 상태에 빠져들었다.\n\n비록 풋내기 변호사였으나, 그는 다 자란 성인이었다. 그는 몹시 신경질적인 태도에다 말을 할 때면 괴로울 만큼 더듬거렸는데, 그것은 타고난 결함이라기보다는, 형편이든 인맥이든 연줄이든 뻔뻔함이든, 그 무엇이 부족하여 '눌려 지낸다'는 의식에서 비롯된 소심함의 결과인 듯했다. 그는 변호인 앞에서는 주눅이 들었고, 변호사에게는 더없이 깍듯했다.\n\n\"전에 뵌 적이 없었던 것 같군요, 펑키 씨.\" 스너빈 변호인이 거만하게 생색을 내며 말했다.\n\n펑키 씨는 고개를 숙였다. 그는 팔 년 하고도 사분기 동안, 가난한 사람 특유의 온갖 부러움을 품고서, 변호인을 뵙고 또 그를 부러워하는 기쁨을 누려 왔던 것이다.\n\n\"이 사건에 나와 함께하신다고 알고 있는데, 맞소?\" 변호인이 말했다.\n\n펑키 씨가 부유한 사람이었다면, 즉시 사무원을 불러 일깨워 달라 했을 것이다. 현명한 사람이었다면, 집게손가락을 이마에 갖다 대고 자신의 그 숱한 일거리 가운데 이 사건을 맡았는지 안 맡았는지 떠올리려 애썼을 것이다. 그러나 그는 (적어도 이런 면에서는) 부유하지도 현명하지도 못했으므로, 얼굴이 빨개진 채 고개를 숙였다.\n\n\"서류는 읽어 보셨소, 펑키 씨?\" 변호인이 물었다.\n\n여기서도 펑키 씨는 이 사건의 시시비비에 대해 까맣게 잊었노라 둘러댔어야 했다. 그러나 그는 소송이 진행되는 동안 자기 앞에 놓인 서류라는 서류는 죄다 읽었고, 스너빈 변호인의 후배 변호사로 선임된 두 달 내내 깨어 있을 때나 잠잘 때나 그 생각밖에 하지 않았던 터라, 더욱 붉게 얼굴을 물들이며 다시 고개를 숙였다.\n\n\"이분이 픽윅 씨요.\" 변호인이 그 신사가 서 있는 쪽으로 펜을 흔들며 말했다.\n\n펑키 씨는 첫 의뢰인이라면 으레 불러일으키기 마련인 그런 경의를 담아 픽윅 씨에게 고개를 숙였고, 다시 자기 선임자 쪽으로 머리를 기울였다.\n\n\"펑키 씨가 픽윅 씨를 모시고 나가서,\" 변호인이 말했다. \"그리고—그리고—그리고—픽윅 씨가 전하고 싶어 하시는 것은 무엇이든 들어 주시는 게 좋겠소. 물론 우리는 상담을 하게 될 테고요.\" 충분히 오래 방해받았다는 그 암시와 함께, 점점 더 멍한 상태로 빠져들고 있던 스너빈 변호인은 안경을 잠시 눈에 갖다 대고는 주위를 향해 가볍게 고개를 숙인 뒤, 다시금 눈앞의 사건에 깊이 몰두했으니, 그 사건이란 한 세기쯤 전에 죽은 어느 인물의 행위에서 비롯된 끝없는 소송에서 생겨난 것으로, 그 인물은 아무도 오지 않는 어딘가에서 아무도 가지 않는 또 다른 어딘가로 이어지는 오솔길을 막아 버렸던 것이다.\n\n펑키 씨는 픽윅 씨와 그의 변호사가 먼저 지나갈 때까지는 어떤 문도 결코 먼저 지나가려 하지 않았으므로, 그들이 광장으로 나오기까지는 한참이 걸렸다. 그리고 마침내 광장에 다다르자, 그들은 이리저리 거닐며 긴 회담을 나누었는데, 그 결론인즉, 평결이 어떻게 날지 말하기는 매우 어려운 일이라는 것, 누구도 소송의 결과를 감히 예측할 수 없다는 것, 상대편이 스너빈 변호인을 선임하지 못하게 막은 것은 매우 운 좋은 일이라는 것, 그 밖에 이런 처지에서 흔히 나누는 갖가지 의구심과 위안의 화제들이었다.\n\n그러고 나서 웰러 씨는 한 시간에 걸친 달콤한 잠에서 주인에게 깨워졌고, 라우튼에게 작별을 고한 뒤 그들은 도심으로 돌아왔다.\n\n버러의 랜트 거리에는 어떤 고요함이 깃들어 있어 영혼에 잔잔한 우수를 드리운다. 이 거리에는 언제나 세놓을 집이 꽤 많다. 게다가 뒷골목이어서 그 적막함이 마음을 달래 준다. 랜트 거리의 집이란, 말의 엄밀한 뜻을 따지자면 일류 주거지에 드는 부류는 결코 아니지만, 그래도 더없이 바람직한 곳이다. 만일 어떤 사람이 세상으로부터 자기를 떼어 놓고 싶다면—유혹의 손길이 닿지 않는 곳으로 몸을 옮기고 싶다면—창밖을 내다보고 싶어질 어떤 유인조차 생길 수 없는 곳에 자기를 두고 싶다면, 우리는 무슨 일이 있어도 랜트 거리로 가라고 권하겠다.\n\n이 행복한 은신처에는 풀 먹여 다림질하는 여인 몇과, 제본 직공 몇 사람, 파산법원의 감옥 대리인 한둘, 부두에서 일하는 자잘한 살림꾼 여럿, 망토 짓는 여인 한 줌, 그리고 임시 양복쟁이 약간이 자리 잡고 산다. 주민의 대다수는 가구 딸린 셋방을 놓는 일에 정력을 쏟거나, 빨래를 짜고 다리는 건강하고 활기찬 생업에 몸을 바친다. 이 거리의 정물(靜物) 중 으뜸가는 특징은 초록색 덧문, 셋방 안내문, 놋쇠 문패, 그리고 초인종 손잡이이고, 살아 움직이는 자연의 주요 표본은 술집 사환과 머핀 파는 소년과 군고구마 장수다. 인구는 떠돌이라 대개 분기 결산일* 어름이면 사라지는데, 흔히 밤을 틈탄다. 폐하의 세입은 이 행복한 골짜기에서는 좀처럼 거두어지지 않는다. 집세는 미덥지 못하고, 수도 공급은 매우 자주 끊긴다.\n* 분기 결산일: 집세 따위를 치르는 분기별 정해진 날.\n\n밥 소여 씨는 픽윅 씨를 초대한 그날 저녁 일찌감치, 자기 이층 앞방의 난롯가 한쪽을 차지하고 앉았고, 벤 앨런 씨가 다른 한쪽을 차지했다. 손님 맞을 채비는 다 끝난 듯했다. 복도의 우산들은 뒷거실 문밖 자그마한 구석에 쌓아 두었고, 안주인 하녀의 보닛과 숄은 난간에서 치워졌으며, 현관 매트 위에는 덧신이 두 켤레밖에 남아 있지 않았고, 심지가 아주 길게 자란 부엌 양초 하나가 층계 창턱에서 명랑하게 타고 있었다. 밥 소여 씨는 직접 하이 거리의 술 창고에서 독한 술을 사 와서, 엉뚱한 집에 배달될 가능성을 미연에 막으려고 그것을 나르는 사람보다 앞장서서 집으로 돌아왔다. 펀치는 침실의 빨간 냄비에 미리 만들어 두었고, 카드놀이를 할 작은 탁자 하나는 초록색 베이즈 천을 씌워 거실에서 빌려 왔으며, 이 집의 술잔들은 이번 일을 위해 술집에서 빌려 온 잔들과 함께 모두 쟁반에 가지런히 올려 문밖 층계참에 놓아 두었다.\n\n이 모든 준비가 더없이 흡족하게 갖추어졌건만, 난롯가에 앉은 밥 소여 씨의 얼굴에는 먹구름이 끼어 있었다. 벤 앨런 씨의 얼굴에도 동정 어린 표정이 어려 있어, 그는 석탄불을 골똘히 바라보다가 오랜 침묵 끝에 우수 어린 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"음, 하필 이런 날 그 여자가 심사가 뒤틀릴 생각을 하다니 운도 없군. 적어도 내일까지는 기다려 줄 수도 있었을 텐데.\"\n\n\"그게 그 여자 심술이지—그게 다 그 여자 심술이라고.\" 밥 소여 씨가 격하게 받았다. \"그 여자가 그러는 거야. 내가 파티를 열 형편이 되면 자기 그 망할 '소액 청구서'쯤은 갚을 수 있어야 하는 거 아니냐고 말이야.\"\n\n\"그게 얼마나 밀린 건데?\" 벤 앨런 씨가 물었다. 그건 그렇고, 청구서란 인간의 천재성이 일찍이 만들어 낸 가장 비상한 기관차다. 그것은 한 사람의 가장 긴 일생 내내 굴러가면서도 제 발로 한 번도 멈추는 법이 없다.\n\n\"한 분기에 한 달쯤 더 됐을까.\" 밥 소여 씨가 대답했다.\n\n벤 앨런은 가망 없다는 듯 기침을 하고, 난로의 위쪽 살 두 개 사이를 샅샅이 살피는 눈길을 보냈다.\n\n\"저 친구들이 와 있을 때 그 여자가 떠들어 댈 생각을 하면 정말 망할 만큼 불쾌한 일일 텐데, 안 그래?\" 마침내 벤 앨런 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"끔찍하지.\" 밥 소여가 대답했다. \"끔찍하고말고.\"\n\n방문을 가볍게 두드리는 소리가 들렸다. 밥 소여 씨는 친구를 의미심장하게 바라보고는 두드린 사람더러 들어오라고 했다. 그러자 검은 무명 양말을 신은, 꾀죄죄하고 단정치 못한 처녀 하나가—몹시 영락한 처지에 놓인 늙은 청소부의 돌보지 못한 딸쯤으로 보일 만한 모습이었다—고개를 디밀고 말했다.\n\n\"저, 소여 선생님, 래들 마님이 선생님하고 할 말이 있으시대요.\"\n\n밥 소여 씨가 미처 대꾸하기도 전에, 처녀는 마치 누가 뒤에서 거칠게 잡아챈 듯 홱 하고 갑자기 사라졌다. 이 알 수 없는 퇴장이 이루어지자마자 문에서 또 한 번 두드리는 소리가 났다—날카롭고 또렷한 노크였는데, 마치 \"나 여기 있고, 이제 들어간다.\"라고 말하는 듯했다.\n\n밥 소여 씨는 비굴한 두려움이 어린 눈으로 친구를 흘끗 보고는 다시 한 번 외쳤다. \"들어오시오.\"\n\n그 허락은 전혀 필요치 않았다. 밥 소여 씨가 그 말을 채 내뱉기도 전에, 자그마하고 사나운 여자 하나가 방 안으로 뛰어들었기 때문이다. 격분으로 온몸을 떨고, 분노로 새파랗게 질린 채였다.\n\n\"자, 소여 선생,\" 그 자그맣고 사나운 여자가 무척 침착해 보이려 애쓰며 말했다. \"내 그 소액 청구서를 친절히 갚아 주신다면 고맙겠어요. 오늘 오후에 집세를 내야 하는데, 집주인이 지금 아래층에서 기다리고 있거든요.\" 여기서 그 자그마한 여자는 두 손을 비비며 밥 소여 씨의 머리 너머 뒤쪽 벽을 뚫어지게 바라보았다.\n\n\"불편을 끼쳐 드려서 정말 죄송합니다, 래들 부인.\" 밥 소여가 공손하게 말했다. \"하지만—\"\n\n\"오, 불편이랄 것도 없어요.\" 그 자그마한 여자가 새된 소리로 키득거리며 받았다. \"오늘 전까지는 딱히 급할 것도 없었으니까요. 적어도, 어차피 바로 집주인한테 넘어갈 돈이니, 내가 쥐고 있으나 선생이 쥐고 있으나 매한가지였죠. 선생이 오늘 오후에 약속하지 않았어요, 소여 선생. 여기 살다 간 신사분들은 누구든 다 약속을 지켰답니다, 선생. 물론 스스로를 신사라 부르는 사람이라면 누구나 그러는 법이지요.\" 래들 부인은 고개를 홱 젖히고, 입술을 깨물고, 두 손을 더 세차게 비비며, 그 어느 때보다 더 뚫어지게 벽을 바라보았다. 밥 소여 씨가 훗날 어느 자리에서 동방식 우화풍으로 평했듯, 그 여자가 \"증기를 끌어올리고\" 있다는 것은 누가 봐도 뻔했다.\n\n\"정말 죄송합니다, 래들 부인.\" 밥 소여가 상상할 수 있는 한껏 겸손하게 말했다. \"하지만 실은, 오늘 시내에서 일이 어긋났지 뭡니까.\"—참으로 별난 곳이다, 그 시내라는 데는. 거기서는 놀랄 만큼 많은 사람들이 늘 일이 어긋난다.\n\n\"그래서요, 소여 선생,\" 래들 부인이 키더민스터 카펫의 보라색 콜리플라워 무늬 위에 두 발을 단단히 디디고 서서 말했다. \"그게 나하고 무슨 상관이죠, 선생?\"\n\n\"저는—저는—틀림없습니다, 래들 부인.\" 밥 소여가 이 마지막 질문을 슬쩍 피하며 말했다. \"다음 주 중반 전에는 우리가 셈을 말끔히 맞출 수 있을 거고, 그 뒤로는 더 나은 방식으로 해 나갈 수 있을 겁니다.\"\n\n이것이야말로 래들 부인이 원하던 전부였다. 부인은 운수 사나운 밥 소여의 방으로 부산하게 올라오면서 한바탕 성을 낼 작정을 단단히 굳히고 있었던 터라, 십중팔구 돈을 받았다면 오히려 김이 빠졌을 정도였다. 부인은 앞쪽 부엌에서 래들 씨와 막 서두 인사 격으로 몇 마디 입씨름을 나눈 참이라, 이런 종류의 자그마한 여흥을 즐기기에는 더없이 좋은 상태였다.\n\n\"소여 선생, 당신 생각엔 말이에요,\" 래들 부인이 이웃들 들으라고 목청을 높이며 말했다. \"내가 날이면 날마다, 집세 낼 생각은커녕 아침 식사로 산 신선한 버터와 각설탕 값, 현관에서 받아 들이는 우유 값조차 낼 생각이 없는 작자한테 내 셋방을 내주고 있을 거라고 생각해요? 이 거리에서 이십 년을(건너편에서 십 년, 바로 이 집에서 구 년 하고 사분의 삼 년) 살아온, 부지런하고 열심히 일하는 여자가, 청구서 갚는 데 보탬이 될 일이라면 무슨 일이든 기꺼이 손을 대야 마땅할 마당에 늘 담배나 피우고 술이나 마시고 빈둥거리기만 하는 게으르고 나태한 작자들 뒤치다꺼리하느라 죽도록 일하는 것 말고는 달리 할 일이 없을 거라고 생각해요? 당신 생각엔—\"\n\n\"이보세요, 부인.\" 벤저민 앨런 씨가 달래듯 끼어들었다.\n\n\"선생, 제발 부탁이니 그 소견은 혼자 간직하시지요.\" 래들 부인이 거침없이 쏟아 내던 말의 물줄기를 별안간 멈추고는, 위압적인 느릿함과 엄숙함으로 제삼자를 향해 말했다. \"선생, 나는 당신이 내게 말을 걸 권리가 있는 줄은 미처 몰랐는데요. 내가 이 방을 당신한테 세준 것 같지는 않은데, 선생.\"\n\n\"네, 분명 그러지 않으셨지요.\" 벤저민 앨런 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"아주 좋아요, 선생.\" 래들 부인이 도도하고 정중하게 응수했다. \"그렇다면 선생, 당신은 병원에 있는 가엾은 사람들 팔다리나 부러뜨리는 일에나 매달리고, 자기 일은 자기나 챙기시지요, 선생. 안 그러면 여기엔 당신을 그렇게 만들어 줄 사람들이 있을지도 모르니까요, 선생.\"\n\n\"하지만 부인은 너무도 막무가내시군요.\" 벤저민 앨런 씨가 항변했다.\n\n\"실례지만, 젊은이.\" 래들 부인이 분노로 식은땀을 흘리며 말했다. \"방금 그 말, 한 번만 더 친절히 해 주시겠어요, 선생?\"\n\n\"무슨 악의가 있어서 그 말을 쓴 건 아닙니다, 부인.\" 벤저민 앨런 씨가 자기 처지도 좀 거북해지면서 대답했다.\n\n\"실례지만, 젊은이.\" 래들 부인이 한층 크고 명령조인 어조로 다그쳤다. \"방금 누구더러 막무가내라는 거죠? 그 말을 나한테 한 거예요, 선생?\"\n\n\"원, 세상에!\" 벤저민 앨런 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그 호칭을 나한테 갖다 붙인 거냐고 묻잖아요, 선생?\" 래들 부인이 사납기 그지없는 기세로 가로막으며 문을 활짝 열어젖혔다.\n\n\"그야, 물론 그랬습니다.\" 벤저민 앨런 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"그래요, 물론 그랬겠지요.\" 래들 부인이 슬금슬금 문 쪽으로 물러서며, 부엌에 있는 래들 씨더러 특별히 들으라고 목청을 한껏 높여 말했다. \"그래요, 물론 그랬겠지! 누구나 알다시피, 내 남편이 아래층에서 자빠져 자면서 내가 길거리의 개라도 되는 양 거들떠보지도 않는 동안에는, 다들 내 집에서 마음 놓고 나를 모욕해도 되는 거지. 저 사람은 부끄러운 줄 알아야 해요(여기서 래들 부인이 흐느꼈다). 셋방 망신이나 시키는, 살아 있는 사람 몸뚱이나 째고 자르는 풋내기 패거리한테 제 아내가 이런 꼴을 당하도록 내버려 두다니(또 한 번 흐느꼈다), 온갖 행패에 고스란히 노출되도록 내버려 두다니. 천하고, 비겁하고, 겁 많은 작자, 위층에 올라와서 저 불한당 같은 것들 앞에 나설 엄두도 못 내는—엄두도—엄두도 못 내는!\" 래들 부인은 이 빈정거림을 되풀이한 것이 그 반쪽을 분기시켰는지 어떤지 귀 기울여 들으려 잠깐 멈추었다가, 효험이 없자 헤아릴 수 없이 흐느끼며 층계를 내려가기 시작했다. 그때 현관문에서 쾅쾅, 큰 노크 소리가 두 번 났다. 그러자 부인은 처량한 신음과 함께 발작적인 통곡을 터뜨렸고, 그것은 노크가 여섯 번 되풀이될 때까지 이어지다가, 마침내 걷잡을 수 없는 정신적 고뇌가 폭발한 끝에 우산을 죄다 내동댕이치고 뒷거실로 사라지면서 무시무시한 굉음과 함께 문을 쾅 닫았다.\n\n\"여기가 소여 씨 사는 집이오?\" 문이 열리자 픽윅 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"네.\" 처녀가 말했다. \"이층이에요. 층계 꼭대기에 올라가면 바로 앞에 보이는 문이에요.\" 이렇게 일러 준 뒤, 서더크의 토박이들 틈에서 자란 그 하녀는 양초를 손에 든 채 부엌 층계 아래로 사라졌다. 이런 상황에서 자기에게 요구될 만한 일은 모두 다 했다는 더없는 만족감에 젖어서였다.\n\n맨 나중에 들어온 스노드그래스 씨는 몇 번 헛수고를 한 끝에 사슬을 걸어 현관문을 잠그는 데 성공했다. 그러고 나서 친구들은 비틀거리며 층계를 올라갔는데, 거기서 밥 소여 씨의 영접을 받았다. 그는 래들 부인에게 길목에서 붙잡힐까 봐 아래층으로 내려갈 엄두를 못 냈던 것이다.\n\n\"안녕들 하십니까?\" 풀이 죽은 의학도가 말했다. \"만나서 반갑소—잔들 조심하시고요.\" 이 주의는 모자를 쟁반에 올려놓은 픽윅 씨를 향한 것이었다.\n\n\"이런.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"미안하오.\"\n\n\"별말씀을, 별말씀을요.\" 밥 소여가 말했다. \"여기 방이 좀 비좁긴 해도, 미혼 청년을 보러 오신 거니 그 정도는 참아 주셔야지요. 어서 들어오십시오. 이 신사분과는 전에 뵌 적이 있으시죠?\" 픽윅 씨는 벤저민 앨런 씨와 악수했고, 친구들도 그를 따라 했다. 그들이 자리에 채 앉기도 전에 또 한 번 쾅쾅, 노크 소리가 두 번 났다.\n\n\"잭 홉킨스이길 바라는데!\" 밥 소여 씨가 말했다. \"쉿. 그래, 맞아. 올라와, 잭. 올라오라고.\"\n\n층계에서 묵직한 발소리가 들리더니 잭 홉킨스가 모습을 드러냈다. 그는 번개무늬 단추가 달린 검은 벨벳 조끼에, 흰 가짜 깃을 단 푸른 줄무늬 셔츠를 입고 있었다.\n\n\"늦었군, 잭?\" 벤저민 앨런 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"바살러뮤 병원에 붙들려 있었어.\" 홉킨스가 대답했다.\n\n\"뭐 새로운 거라도?\"\n\n\"아니, 별다른 건 없고. 응급 병동에 꽤 괜찮은 사고가 하나 실려 왔지.\"\n\n\"그게 뭐였습니까, 선생?\" 픽윅 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"그저 사 층 창문에서 떨어진 사내일 뿐인데, 정말이지 아주 좋은 사례예요.\"\n\n\"환자가 회복될 가망이 좋다는 말씀입니까?\" 픽윅 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"아니요.\" 홉킨스 씨가 무심하게 대답했다. \"아니요, 오히려 가망이 없다고 해야겠지요. 그래도 내일 굉장한 수술이 있을 거예요—슬래셔가 집도하면 장관이거든요.\"\n\n\"슬래셔 씨를 솜씨 좋은 집도의로 보시나 보군요?\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"살아 있는 사람 중에 으뜸이죠.\" 홉킨스가 대답했다. \"지난주에는 어떤 소년의 다리를 관절에서 떼어 냈는데—그 애가 사과를 다섯 개에 생강 케이크를 하나 먹고는—수술이 다 끝나고 딱 이 분 뒤에, 자기를 놀림감으로 만들려고 여기 누워 있는 게 아니라며, 당장 시작하지 않으면 엄마한테 이르겠다고 했다니까요.\"\n\n\"이런!\" 픽윅 씨가 놀라며 말했다.\n\n\"흥! 그 정도야 아무것도 아니죠.\" 잭 홉킨스가 말했다. \"안 그래, 밥?\"\n\n\"아무것도 아니지.\" 밥 소여 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"그러고 보니, 밥,\" 홉킨스가 픽윅 씨의 주의 깊은 얼굴을 거의 알아챌 수 없을 만큼 슬쩍 흘끔거리며 말했다. \"어젯밤에 묘한 사고가 하나 있었어. 목걸이를 삼킨 아이가 하나 실려 왔지.\"\n\n\"뭘 삼켰다고요, 선생?\" 픽윅 씨가 가로막았다.\n\n\"목걸이요.\" 잭 홉킨스가 대답했다. \"한꺼번에는 아니고요, 아시잖습니까. 그건 무리지요—그 애가 삼켰다 해도 선생님은 못 삼키실 겁니다—안 그래요, 픽윅 씨? 하하!\" 홉킨스 씨는 제 농담에 무척 흡족해하며 말을 이었다. \"아니요, 사정은 이렇습니다. 그 애 부모는 어느 골목에 사는 가난한 사람들이었어요. 그 애의 맏누이가 목걸이를 하나 샀는데—큼지막한 검은 나무 구슬로 만든 흔한 목걸이였죠. 그 애가 장난감을 좋아해서 그 목걸이를 슬쩍 훔쳐다 감추고 가지고 놀다가, 끈을 끊고 구슬을 하나 삼켜 버렸지요. 그 애는 그게 기막힌 놀이라고 여기고는, 이튿날 또 가서 구슬을 하나 더 삼켰어요.\"\n\n\"세상에.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"그 무슨 끔찍한 일이오! 미안하오, 선생. 계속하시오.\"\n\n\"그다음 날엔 구슬을 두 개 삼켰고, 그 이튿날엔 자기한테 세 개를 대접했고, 그렇게 계속하다가 일주일 만에 목걸이 하나를 다 해치웠어요—구슬이 죄다 합쳐서 스물다섯 개였지요. 그 누이는 부지런한 처녀라 변변한 장신구 하나 사 본 적이 거의 없었던 터라, 목걸이를 잃고는 눈이 빠지도록 울었어요. 위아래로 샅샅이 찾아봤지만, 말할 것도 없이 못 찾았지요. 며칠 뒤에 식구들이 저녁을 먹는데—양 어깨살 구이에, 그 밑에 감자를 깔았더랬죠—배가 안 고팠던 그 애가 방을 이리저리 돌아다니고 있는데, 별안간 작은 우박 폭풍 같은 요란한 소리가 들리는 거예요. '그러지 마라, 얘야.' 아버지가 말했죠. '난 아무것도 안 했는데요.' 아이가 말했어요. '그래, 다시는 그러지 마라.' 아버지가 말했죠. 잠깐 조용하더니, 그러고는 그 소리가 전보다 더 심하게 또 나기 시작했어요. '내가 한 말 안 들으면, 얘야,' 아버지가 말했죠. '돼지 멱따는 소리 한 번 낼 새도 없이 침대에 처박힐 줄 알아라.' 아버지가 말을 잘 듣게 하려고 아이를 흔들어 댔더니, 일찍이 아무도 들어 본 적 없는 덜그럭 소리가 나는 거예요. '아니, 빌어먹을, 이거 아이 뱃속에 들었잖아!' 아버지가 말했죠. '이놈이 엉뚱한 데에 후두염*이 걸렸구나!' '아니에요, 아버지.' 아이가 울먹이며 말했어요. '그건 목걸이예요. 제가 삼켰어요, 아버지.'—아버지는 아이를 번쩍 안아 들고 병원으로 달려갔는데, 가는 내내 덜컹거릴 때마다 그 애 뱃속 구슬들이 달가닥거렸고, 사람들은 그 유별난 소리가 어디서 나는지 보려고 하늘을 올려다보고 지하실을 내려다보고 했지요. 그 애는 지금 병원에 있어요.\" 잭 홉킨스가 말했다. \"그런데 돌아다닐 때마다 어찌나 빌어먹게 요란한 소리를 내는지, 환자들이 깰까 봐 야경꾼 외투로 그 애를 둘둘 감싸 두어야 할 지경이랍니다.\"\n* 후두염: 원문에서 아버지는 '뱃속에서 나는 덜그럭 소리'를 후두에 생긴 병으로 착각한다.\n\n\"내 평생 들어 본 것 중에 가장 비상한 사례로구려.\" 픽윅 씨가 탁자를 힘주어 한 번 내리치며 말했다.\n\n\"오, 그 정도야 아무것도 아니죠.\" 잭 홉킨스가 말했다. \"안 그래, 밥?\"\n\n\"물론 아니지.\" 밥 소여가 대답했다.\n\n\"우리 직업에는 정말 별난 일들이 일어난답니다, 선생, 장담하지요.\" 홉킨스가 말했다.\n\n\"그럴 거라고 짐작이 가는군요.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다.\n\n문에서 또 한 번 노크가 나더니, 검은 가발을 쓴 머리 큰 청년 하나가 들어왔는데, 긴 목도리를 두른 괴혈병 기색의 젊은이를 데리고 왔다. 그다음에 온 사람은 분홍 닻 무늬로 장식한 셔츠를 입은 신사였고, 그 뒤를 도금한 시곗줄을 단 창백한 젊은이가 바짝 따라왔다. 깨끗한 아마포 옷에 천 장화를 신은 새침한 인물이 도착하자 모임이 완성되었다. 초록색 베이즈를 씌운 작은 탁자를 끌어내고, 펀치 첫 분량을 흰 주전자에 담아 들여왔다. 그러고 나서 이어진 세 시간은 한 다스에 육 펜스를 거는 뱅테욍\\에 바쳐졌는데, 도중에 단 한 번, 괴혈병 기색의 젊은이와 분홍 닻 무늬 신사 사이에 사소한 다툼이 있어 중단되었다. 그 와중에 괴혈병 기색의 젊은이는 희망의 표상을 단 신사의 코를 비틀어 주고 싶다는 불타는 욕망을 내비쳤고, 이에 대해 그 인물은, 괴혈병 기색의 성마른 젊은 신사한테서든 머리 달린 다른 어떤 사람한테서든, 거저 주는 '건방진 소리'\\*는 결단코 받아들일 뜻이 없음을 표명했다.\n* 뱅테욍: 카드놀이의 일종(블랙잭). \\\\ '건방진 소리': 'sauce'에는 '소스'와 '건방진 말대꾸'라는 두 뜻이 있다.\n\n마지막 '내추럴'*이 선언되고, 생선 패와 육 펜스의 손익 계산이 모두의 만족스럽게 정산되자, 밥 소여 씨는 종을 울려 야식을 청했고, 손님들은 그것이 준비되는 동안 구석으로 비집고 들어갔다.\n* 내추럴: 카드놀이에서 처음 두 장으로 21을 만드는 패.\n\n야식은 더러 생각하는 것만큼 쉽게 준비되지는 않았다. 우선, 부엌 탁자에 얼굴을 묻고 잠들어 버린 처녀를 깨워야 했다. 여기에 시간이 좀 걸렸고, 마침내 종소리에 응답한 뒤에도 그 애에게 어렴풋하고 아득한 분별의 빛 한 가닥이나마 일깨우려는 헛된 노력에 또 십오 분이 허비되었다. 굴을 가져오라고 시킨 사람에게는 그것을 까 두라는 말을 미처 하지 않은 터였다. 흐물거리는 칼과 두 갈래 포크로 굴을 까는 일이란 여간 어려운 게 아니어서, 이쪽 일은 거의 진척이 없었다. 쇠고기도 거의 익지 않았고, 햄(이것도 모퉁이의 독일식 소시지 가게에서 사 온 것이었다) 역시 비슷한 처지였다. 그래도 양철통에 든 흑맥주는 넉넉했고, 치즈는 워낙 독해서 멀리까지 갔다. 그러니 전체적으로 보면, 야식은 그런 자리에서 흔히 보는 정도로는 그럭저럭 괜찮았다고 할 만했다.\n\n야식이 끝나고, 펀치를 담은 주전자가 하나 더, 시가 한 봉지, 그리고 독한 술 두 병과 함께 탁자에 올랐다. 그러고 나서 무시무시한 침묵이 찾아왔는데, 이 무시무시한 침묵은 이런 곳에서 흔히 있는 일이 빚어낸 것이었으나, 그럼에도 무척 난처한 것이었다.\n\n실은, 처녀가 술잔들을 씻고 있었다. 이 집이 자랑하는 잔은 넉 개였다. 우리는 이 사정을 결코 래들 부인의 흠으로 적는 것이 아니다. 잔이 모자라지 않은 셋방집이란 일찍이 없었으니까. 안주인의 잔은 작고 얇은, 입으로 불어 만든 텀블러였고, 술집에서 빌려 온 잔은 크고 부석부석하게 부푼 물건으로, 저마다 통풍에 걸린 듯 굵직한 다리 하나로 받쳐져 있었다. 이것만으로도 모임의 사람들에게 사정의 진상을 알아채게 하기에 충분했을 테지만, 만능 잡역부 처녀는 어느 신사든 이 문제로 행여 오해를 품지 못하도록 미연에 막아 버렸다. 그가 맥주를 다 마시기도 훨씬 전에 사내들 각자의 잔을 억지로 끌어가면서, 밥 소여 씨가 눈짓을 하고 말을 가로막는데도 아랑곳없이, 잔을 아래층으로 가져가 당장 씻어야 한다고 또렷이 말해 댔던 것이다.\n\n누구에게도 득이 되지 않는 바람은 정말 고약한 바람이다.* 천 장화를 신은 새침한 사내는 그 돌림판\\\\ 놀이가 이어지는 내내 농담 한마디 던져 보려다 번번이 실패했는데, 이제 기회를 잡고는 그것을 놓치지 않았다. 잔들이 사라지는 순간, 그는 이름이 기억나지 않는 어느 위대한 공인이, 자기로서는 끝내 누구인지 알아낼 수 없었던 또 다른 저명하고 이름난 인물에게 유달리 멋들어진 응수를 한 이야기를 길게 풀어 놓기 시작했다. 그는 지금 다루는 일화와 멀찍이 얽힌 여러 부수적 정황을 꽤 길게, 무척 세세하게 늘어놓았으나, 죽었다 깨도 그 순간 정작 그 일화가 무엇이었는지는 도무지 기억해 낼 수 없었다. 지난 십 년간 그 이야기를 큰 갈채를 받으며 들려주는 것이 버릇이었는데도 말이다.\n* 누구에게도 득이 되지 않는 바람: \"어느 바람이든 누군가에게는 득이 된다\"는 영어 속담을 뒤집은 표현. \\\\ 돌림판 놀이: 여럿이 돌아가며 하는 카드놀이.\n\n\"이런.\" 천 장화를 신은 새침한 사내가 말했다. \"참으로 비상한 일이로군요.\"\n\n\"잊으셨다니 안타깝습니다.\" 밥 소여 씨가, 잔들이 짤랑거리는 소리를 들은 듯해서 문 쪽을 안달하며 흘끔거리면서 말했다. \"참 안타깝네요.\"\n\n\"저도 그렇습니다.\" 새침한 사내가 응수했다. \"정말 큰 즐거움을 드렸을 이야기라는 걸 아니까요. 괜찮습니다. 한 삼십 분쯤 지나면 어떻게든 기억해 낼 수 있을 듯하군요.\"\n\n새침한 사내가 이 대목에 이르렀을 때 마침 잔들이 돌아왔는데, 그 내내 정신을 빼앗긴 채 귀 기울이던 밥 소여 씨는, 자기가 들은 한에서 그것이 단연코 여태 들어 본 가장 훌륭한 이야기였으니, 그 결말을 꼭 듣고 싶다고 말했다.\n\n텀블러들이 눈에 들어오자 밥 소여는 안주인과의 면담 이래 줄곧 잃었던 평정을 어느 정도 되찾았다. 그의 얼굴이 환해지고, 그는 자못 흥겨워지기 시작했다.\n\n\"자, 베치.\" 밥 소여 씨가 더없이 상냥하게 말하면서, 동시에 처녀가 탁자 한가운데에 그러모아 놓은 잔들의 어수선한 작은 무리를 흩어 놓았다. \"자, 베치, 따뜻한 물 좀. 빨리, 착하지.\"\n\n\"따뜻한 물은 없는데요.\" 베치가 대답했다.\n\n\"따뜻한 물이 없다고!\" 밥 소여 씨가 소리쳤다.\n\n\"네.\" 처녀가 고개를 절레절레 저으며 말했는데, 그 몸짓은 아무리 풍부한 말로도 전할 수 없을 만큼 단호한 부정을 나타냈다. \"래들 마님이 선생님한테 물은 한 방울도 안 된다고 하셨어요.\"\n\n손님들의 얼굴에 떠오른 놀란 기색이 주인에게 새로운 용기를 불어넣었다.\n\n\"당장 따뜻한 물을 가져와—당장!\" 밥 소여 씨가 필사적으로 엄하게 말했다.\n\n\"아뇨. 못 해요.\" 처녀가 대답했다. \"래들 마님이 주무시기 전에 부엌 불을 다 긁어내 버리시고 주전자도 잠가 두셨거든요.\"\n\n\"오, 괜찮네, 괜찮아. 그런 사소한 일로 마음 쓰지 마시오.\" 밥 소여의 격정이 얼굴에 드러나며 부딪치는 것을 본 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"찬물이면 충분하오.\"\n\n\"오, 더할 나위 없지요.\" 벤저민 앨런 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"우리 안주인이 가벼운 정신 착란 발작을 좀 일으키곤 해서요.\" 밥 소여가 섬뜩한 미소를 띠며 말했다. \"아무래도 통고를 해야겠습니다.\"\n\n\"아니, 그러지 마.\" 벤 앨런이 말했다.\n\n\"아무래도 그래야겠어.\" 밥이 영웅적인 단호함으로 말했다. \"빚진 걸 갚고, 내일 아침에 나가라고 통고할 거야.\" 가엾은 친구! 그게 정말 가능하기를 그가 얼마나 간절히 바랐던가!\n\n이 마지막 타격을 받고도 기운을 차려 보려는 밥 소여 씨의 마음 시린 안간힘은 좌중에 의기소침한 기운을 옮겨 놓았다. 그 대다수는 흥을 돋워 보겠다고 찬 브랜디 물에 한층 정답게 매달렸는데, 그 첫 번째 알아챌 만한 효과는 괴혈병 기색의 젊은이와 셔츠 입은 신사 사이에 적대 행위가 재개된 것으로 나타났다. 두 교전자는 한동안 갖가지 찡그림과 콧방귀로 서로에 대한 경멸의 감정을 토해 내다가, 마침내 괴혈병 기색의 젊은이가 이 문제를 좀 더 명확히 매듭지을 필요를 느꼈다. 그리하여 다음과 같은 명료한 합의가 이루어졌다.\n\n\"소여.\" 괴혈병 기색의 젊은이가 큰 소리로 말했다.\n\n\"왜 그래, 노디.\" 밥 소여 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"소여, 나는 어느 친구의 식탁에서든 불쾌한 일을 일으키는 건 정말 유감스럽게 여기고, 더구나 자네 식탁에서야 말할 것도 없네, 소여—정말로 그래. 하지만 나는 이 기회를 빌려 건터 씨에게, 당신은 신사가 아니라고 알려 줘야겠어.\"\n\n\"그리고 소여, 나도 자네가 사는 거리에서 소동을 일으키는 건 정말 유감스럽게 여기네만,\" 건터 씨가 말했다. \"방금 말한 자를 창밖으로 내던져 이웃들을 놀라게 할 수밖에 없을 듯하군.\"\n\n\"그게 무슨 소리요, 선생?\" 노디 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"말 그대로요, 선생.\" 건터 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"어디 한번 해 보시지, 선생.\" 노디 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"삼십 초 안에 내가 하는 걸 몸소 느끼게 될 거요, 선생.\" 건터 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"당신 명함을 좀 주시지요, 선생.\" 노디 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그런 짓은 절대 안 하겠소, 선생.\" 건터 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"왜 안 되는데요, 선생?\" 노디 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"그걸 벽난로 선반 위에 꽂아 두고, 신사가 자기를 찾아온 적이 있다는 헛된 믿음으로 손님들을 속일 테니까요, 선생.\" 건터 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"선생, 내일 아침에 내 친구가 당신을 찾아갈 거요.\" 노디 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"선생, 미리 일러 주셔서 정말 고맙소. 하인한테 숟가락을 잘 챙겨 두라고 특별히 단단히 일러 두겠소.\" 건터 씨가 대답했다.\n\n이 대목에서 나머지 손님들이 끼어들어, 두 사람 모두에게 그 행동거지의 온당치 못함을 들어 타일렀다. 그러자 노디 씨는 자기 아버지도 건터 씨 아버지 못지않게 훌륭한 분이라고 밝혔고, 이에 건터 씨는 자기 아버지도 노디 씨 아버지에 조금도 못지않게 훌륭한 분이며, 그 아버지의 아들은 노디 씨에 비해 어느 날을 잡아도 손색없는 사내라고 응수했다. 이 선언이 다툼을 다시 시작하는 서막처럼 보였으므로 좌중이 또 한 번 끼어들었고, 엄청난 양의 말과 떠들썩한 소동이 이어졌다. 그 와중에 노디 씨는 차츰 감정에 북받쳐, 자기는 건터 씨에게 늘 헌신적인 개인적 애정을 품어 왔노라고 고백했다. 이에 건터 씨는, 전체적으로 보아 자기는 친형제보다 차라리 노디 씨가 더 좋다고 응수했다. 이 고백을 듣자 노디 씨는 너그럽게 자리에서 일어나 건터 씨에게 손을 내밀었다. 건터 씨는 가슴 뭉클한 열정으로 그 손을 맞잡았고, 모두가 이 다툼 전체가 두 당사자 모두에게 더없이 명예로운 방식으로 처리되었다고 입을 모았다.\n\n\"자,\" 잭 홉킨스가 말했다. \"분위기를 다시 띄울 겸, 밥, 내가 노래 한 곡 부르는 것도 괜찮겠지.\" 그러고는 우레 같은 갈채에 부추겨진 홉킨스가 단숨에 '국왕 폐하, 신의 가호를'에 뛰어들어, '비스케이 만'과 '개구리 한 마리가'를 섞어 만든 새로운 곡조에 맞춰 목청껏 불러 댔다. 노래의 정수는 후렴이었는데, 신사들 저마다 자기가 가장 잘 아는 가락으로 그것을 부르는 통에 그 효과는 참으로 충격적이었다.\n\n첫째 절 후렴이 끝날 무렵, 픽윅 씨가 귀를 기울이는 자세로 손을 들어 올리고는, 조용해지자마자 말했다.\n\n\"쉿! 미안합니다. 위층에서 누가 부르는 소리를 들은 것 같아서요.\"\n\n곧바로 깊은 침묵이 뒤따랐고, 밥 소여 씨의 얼굴이 새파랗게 질리는 것이 눈에 띄었다.\n\n\"지금 또 들리는 것 같소.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"문 좀 열어 주시겠소.\"\n\n문이 열리기가 무섭게 그 문제에 관한 의심은 모두 가셨다.\n\n\"소여 씨! 소여 씨!\" 삼 층 층계참에서 어떤 목소리가 비명을 질렀다.\n\n\"우리 안주인이오.\" 밥 소여가 몹시 당황하여 둘러보며 말했다. \"네, 래들 부인.\"\n\n\"이게 대체 무슨 짓이에요, 소여 씨?\" 그 목소리가 무척 새되고 빠른 말투로 응수했다. \"집세를 떼이고, 게다가 내 돈까지 빌려주고, 사내라고 감히 자처하는 당신 친구들한테 욕을 먹고 모욕을 당하는 것도 모자라서, 새벽 두 시에 집을 창밖으로 뒤집어엎고 소방차라도 불러올 만큼 시끄럽게 굴어야겠어요?—저 못된 것들을 내쫓아요.\"\n\n\"당신들 부끄러운 줄 알아야 하오.\" 래들 씨의 목소리가 말했는데, 어디 먼 이불 속에서 새어 나오는 듯했다.\n\n\"부끄러운 줄 알라고!\" 래들 부인이 말했다. \"당신은 어째서 내려가서 저것들을 하나하나 층계 아래로 처박지 않아요? 당신이 사내라면 그렇게 했을 거 아니에요.\"\n\n\"내가 열두 사람이라면 그랬을 거요, 여보.\" 래들 씨가 누그러뜨리려는 듯 대답했다. \"하지만 머릿수에서 저쪽이 나보다 한 수 위라오, 여보.\"\n\n\"흥, 이 겁쟁이!\" 래들 부인이 더없는 경멸로 응수했다. \"저 못된 것들을 내쫓을 거예요, 말 거예요, 소여 씨?\"\n\n\"가는 중입니다, 래들 부인, 가는 중이에요.\" 가엾은 밥이 말했다. \"아무래도 자네들 가는 게 좋겠어.\" 밥 소여 씨가 친구들에게 말했다. \"내 생각에도 우리가 너무 시끄럽게 굴었어.\"\n\n\"이거 참 불운한 일이군요.\" 새침한 사내가 말했다. \"마침 이렇게 편안해지던 참인데!\" 새침한 사내는 막 그 잊어버렸던 이야기가 어렴풋이 떠오르기 시작한 참이었다.\n\n\"이건 도저히 견딜 수 없는 일이오.\" 새침한 사내가 둘러보며 말했다. \"도저히 견딜 수 없죠, 안 그렇습니까?\"\n\n\"참을 수 없지요.\" 잭 홉킨스가 대답했다. \"나머지 절을 부르세, 밥. 자, 간다!\"\n\n\"안 돼, 안 돼, 잭, 그러지 마.\" 밥 소여가 끼어들었다. \"기막힌 노래이긴 한데, 아무래도 나머지 절은 부르지 않는 게 좋겠어. 이 집 사람들이 아주 사나운 사람들이거든.\"\n\n\"내가 위층에 올라가서 그 집주인 양반을 한 대 갈겨 줄까?\" 홉킨스가 물었다. \"아니면 종을 계속 울려 댈까, 아니면 층계에 가서 신음 소리라도 낼까? 자네가 시키는 대로 하지, 밥.\"\n\n\"자네의 우정과 호의에 정말 큰 신세를 지는군, 홉킨스.\" 비참해진 밥 소여 씨가 말했다. \"하지만 더 이상의 다툼을 피하는 데는 우리가 당장 자리를 파하는 게 상책일 것 같네.\"\n\n\"자, 소여 씨.\" 래들 부인의 새된 목소리가 비명을 질렀다. \"저 짐승 같은 것들이 가고 있어요?\"\n\n\"모자를 찾고 있을 뿐입니다, 래들 부인.\" 밥이 말했다. \"곧 갈 겁니다.\"\n\n\"가긴!\" 래들 부인이, 픽윅 씨가 터프먼 씨를 뒤에 거느리고 거실에서 막 나오는 바로 그 순간에 난간 너머로 나이트캡을 쑥 내밀며 말했다. \"가긴! 대체 무엇 하러 온 거야?\"\n\n\"이보세요, 부인.\" 픽윅 씨가 고개를 들고 항변했다.\n\n\"썩 꺼져요, 이 늙은 망나니!\" 래들 부인이 황급히 나이트캡을 도로 거두며 응수했다. \"저 사람 할아버지뻘이나 되는 게, 이 능구렁이 같으니! 당신이 저것들 중에 제일 고약해.\"\n\n픽윅 씨는 자기 결백을 항변해 봐야 소용없음을 깨닫고 서둘러 층계를 내려가 거리로 나갔다. 그 뒤를 터프먼 씨와 윈클 씨와 스노드그래스 씨가 바짝 따랐다. 술기운과 격앙으로 처량하게 풀이 죽은 벤 앨런 씨는 런던 다리까지 그들과 동행했는데, 걷는 도중에 윈클 씨에게—비밀을 털어놓기에 유달리 적임인 인물이라 여겨—자기 누이 아라벨라의 애정을 넘본다면 밥 소여 씨를 제외한 어떤 신사든 그 목을 따고 말리라 결심했노라고 속내를 털어놓았다. 오라비로서 이 괴로운 의무를 마땅한 단호함으로 수행하겠다는 결의를 밝히고 나서, 그는 와락 눈물을 쏟더니 모자를 눈가까지 눌러쓰고, 되는대로 길을 되짚어 가서는, 버러 시장 사무소 문을 쾅쾅 두드렸다가 계단에서 잠깐씩 졸기를 번갈아 하며 동틀 녘까지 그러고 있었다. 자기가 거기 사는데 열쇠를 잊어버렸다고 굳게 믿으면서 말이다.\n\n손님들이 래들 부인의 자못 절박한 요청에 따라 모두 떠나 버리자, 운수 사나운 밥 소여 씨만 홀로 남아, 내일 벌어질 법한 일들과 그날 저녁의 즐거움을 곰곰이 곱씹게 되었다.\n\n이 진실한 이야기의 독자들이라면 우리만큼이나 잘 알고 있겠지만, 2월 13일 아침은 바델 부인의 소송 재판이 열리기로 정해진 날의 바로 전날이었으니, 그날은 새뮤얼 웰러 씨에게 몹시 바쁜 시간이었다. 그는 아침 아홉 시부터 오후 두 시까지, 양 끝 시각을 모두 포함하여, 조지 앤드 벌처 여관에서 퍼커 씨의 사무실로, 다시 그 반대로 끊임없이 오가는 일에 매달려 있었다. 사실 무슨 할 일이 있어서가 아니었다. 협의는 이미 끝났고, 채택할 소송 진행 방향도 최종적으로 결정된 터였다. 다만 픽윅 씨가 극도의 흥분 상태에 빠져, 자기 변호사에게 끊임없이 짧은 쪽지를 보내기를 고집했던 것인데, 그 쪽지란 그저 \"퍼커 씨에게. 모든 일이 잘되어 가고 있소?\" 하는 물음만을 담은 것이었고, 거기에 퍼커 씨는 한결같이 \"픽윅 씨에게. 더할 나위 없이 잘되고 있습니다\" 하는 답을 보내 왔다. 사실은 우리가 이미 넌지시 비친 대로, 이튿날 아침 법정이 열리기 전까지는 잘되든 못되든 진행될 일이라곤 전혀 없었던 것이다.\n\n그러나 자진해서든 강제로든 난생처음 법정에 가는 사람이라면 한동안 신경과민과 불안에 시달리는 것쯤은 너그러이 봐줄 수 있는 법이다. 그리고 샘은 인간 본성의 약점에 대한 마땅한 헤아림으로, 그 누구도 흔들 수 없는 차분한 쾌활함과 한결같은 평정심으로 주인의 모든 분부를 따랐으니, 이는 그의 가장 두드러지고 사랑스러운 특성 가운데 하나였다.\n\n샘은 더없이 흡족한 점심으로 자신을 위로한 뒤, 픽윅 씨가 아침 산책의 피로를 씻어 내라고 권한 따끈한 혼합주 한 잔을 바에서 기다리고 있었다. 그때 키가 삼 피트쯤 되어 보이는 어린 사내아이 하나가 털모자를 쓰고 퍼스티언 작업복을 걸치고 조지 앤드 벌처 여관의 복도로 들어왔는데, 그 차림새는 머지않아 마구간지기의 지위에 오르겠다는 가상한 포부를 드러내고 있었다. 아이는 먼저 계단 위를 올려다보고, 다음으로 복도를 따라 살펴보고, 또 바 안을 들여다보았으니, 마치 무슨 심부름을 전할 사람을 찾는 듯한 모양새였다. 그러자 바의 아가씨는 그 심부름이라는 것이 가게의 찻숟가락이나 식탁용 숟가락을 노리는 것일지도 모른다고 여겨, 아이에게 말을 걸었다—\n\n\"자, 젊은이, 무슨 일이지?\"\n\n\"여기에 샘이라는 사람 있어요?\" 그 아이가 새된 고음으로 큰 소리를 내며 물었다.\n\n\"성은 뭐냐?\" 샘 웰러가 돌아보며 말했다.\n\n\"그걸 내가 어떻게 알아요?\" 털모자 아래의 젊은 신사가 잽싸게 대꾸했다.\n\n\"너 참 똘똘한 녀석이로구나.\" 웰러 씨가 말했다. \"다만 내가 너라면 그 아주 잘 선 날을 너무 드러내지는 않겠어. 누가 그걸 갈아 없애 버릴까 봐 말이지. 호-텔에 들어와서 야생 인디언만큼이나 공손하게 샘을 찾는 건 또 무슨 뜻이냐?\"\n\n\"어떤 늙은 신사분이 그러라고 시켰거든요.\" 아이가 대답했다.\n\n\"어떤 늙은 신사 말이냐?\" 샘이 깊은 경멸을 담아 물었다.\n\n\"입스위치 마차를 모는 분이요, 우리 가게 응접실을 쓰는 분이요.\" 아이가 받아 말했다. \"어제 아침에 저더러 오늘 오후에 조지 앤드 벌처로 가서 샘을 찾으라고 하셨어요.\"\n\n\"우리 아버지로구나, 아가씨.\" 웰러 씨가 바의 젊은 아가씨를 향해 설명하는 투로 돌아서며 말했다. \"내 성이 뭔지도 거의 모르시는 것 같네, 분명히. 자, 어린 브로콜리 새싹아, 그래서 어쩌라는 거냐?\"\n\n\"그러니까요,\" 아이가 말했다. \"아저씨가 여섯 시에 우리 가게로 그분한테 오래요, 그분이 아저씨를 보고 싶어 하니까요—리든홀 시장의 블루 보어요. 온다고 전할까요?\"\n\n\"그렇게 전해도 좋다, 도련님.\" 샘이 대답했다. 이렇게 권한을 받은 젊은 신사는 걸어가면서, 유난히 풍부하고 우렁찬 가락으로 가축몰이꾼의 휘파람을 몇 차례 더없이 정확하고 깔끔하게 흉내 내어, 조지 야드의 메아리란 메아리를 모조리 깨워 놓고 떠났다.\n\n웰러 씨는 픽윅 씨에게서 외출 허락을 얻었는데, 픽윅 씨는 그때의 흥분과 근심 상태에서 혼자 남겨지는 것을 결코 언짢아하지 않았다. 그는 정해진 시각보다 한참 일찍 나섰고, 시간이 넉넉했으므로 맨션 하우스까지 어슬렁어슬렁 걸어 내려가, 그 유명한 집결지 근처에 모여 이 나라 노부인 무리에게 큰 공포와 혼란을 안기는 수많은 마차 호객꾼과 단거리 마차 마부들을 더없이 차분하고 철학적인 표정으로 바라보았다. 한 삼십 분쯤 여기서 어정거린 뒤, 웰러 씨는 발길을 돌려 여러 골목과 안마당을 지나 리든홀 시장 쪽으로 걸어가기 시작했다. 그는 남는 시간을 어슬렁거리며 흘려보내고 있었고 눈에 띄는 거의 모든 것 앞에 멈춰 서서 구경했으므로, 웰러 씨가 어느 작은 문구점 겸 판화 가게 진열창 앞에서 걸음을 멈춘 것은 조금도 놀랄 일이 아니었다. 그러나 더 이상의 설명 없이 보면, 거기에 팔려고 내놓은 어떤 그림들에 그의 눈길이 닿자마자, 그가 갑자기 흠칫 놀라 오른쪽 다리를 세차게 치며 기운차게 외친 것은 자못 놀라워 보인다. \"이게 아니었으면 너무 늦을 때까지 까맣게 잊고 있을 뻔했네!\"\n\n샘 웰러가 이렇게 말할 때 그의 눈이 못 박힌 그 그림은, 화살에 꼬치처럼 꿰인 두 개의 사람 심장이 활활 타오르는 불 위에서 구워지고 있는 것을 화려한 색채로 그린 것이었다. 한편 현대식 차림을 한 남녀 식인종이—신사는 파란 코트에 흰 바지를 입고 숙녀는 진홍색 펠리스에 같은 빛깔의 양산을 들고—거기로 이어지는 구불구불한 자갈길을 따라 굶주린 눈빛으로 그 식사에 다가가고 있었다. 날개 한 쌍만 달고 그 밖에는 아무것도 걸치지 않은, 누가 봐도 점잖지 못한 젊은 신사가 그 요리를 감독하는 모습으로 그려져 있었고, 멀리 런던 랭엄 플레이스 교회의 첨탑이 보였다. 그리고 그 전체가 하나의 \"밸런타인\"을 이루고 있었는데, 진열창에 적힌 안내문이 증언하듯, 가게 안에는 이런 것이 잔뜩 갖추어져 있어, 가게 주인은 동포 여러분께 한 장당 일 실링 육 펜스의 할인가로 처분할 것을 다짐하고 있었다.\n\n\"잊고 있을 뻔했네, 정말 까맣게 잊고 있을 뻔했어!\" 샘이 말했다. 이렇게 말하면서 그는 곧장 문구점 안으로 들어가, 최상품 금테 편지지 한 장과 결코 잉크가 튀지 않는다고 보증할 수 있는 단단한 펜촉 하나를 달라고 했다. 이 물건들이 즉시 마련되자, 그는 최근의 느릿느릿한 걸음과는 사뭇 다른, 빠르고 활기찬 걸음으로 곧장 리든홀 시장을 향해 걸어갔다. 주위를 둘러보다가 그는 거기서 간판 하나를 보았는데, 화가의 솜씨로 코끼리 비슷한 무엇이 그려져 있었으되, 코 대신 매부리코가 달린 하늘빛 코끼리 같은 것이었다. 이것이 바로 블루 보어임에 틀림없다고 옳게 짐작하고, 그는 가게 안으로 들어가 자기 아버지의 소식을 물었다.\n\n\"그분은 앞으로 사십오 분이나 그 이상은 더 있어야 오실 텐데요.\" 블루 보어의 집안일을 맡아보는 젊은 아가씨가 말했다.\n\n\"아주 좋아요, 아가씨.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"미지근한 브랜디 워터 구 펜스어치하고 잉크병 좀 갖다주겠어요, 아가씨?\"\n\n미지근한 브랜디 워터와 잉크병이 작은 응접실로 들어왔고, 젊은 아가씨가 석탄이 활활 타오르지 않도록 조심스레 눌러 다지고, 블루 보어의 충분한 양해와 동의를 먼저 얻지 않고서는 불을 휘저을 가능성을 아예 막으려고 부지깽이를 치워 버린 뒤, 샘 웰러는 난로 가까운 칸막이 자리에 앉아 금테 편지지와 단단한 펜촉을 꺼냈다. 그러고는 펜에 털이 끼지 않았는지 꼼꼼히 살피고, 종이 밑에 빵 부스러기가 없도록 식탁을 털어 낸 다음, 샘은 코트 소맷부리를 걷어 올리고 팔꿈치를 떡 벌리고는, 마음을 가다듬고 쓰기 시작했다.\n\n서법(書法)이라는 학문에 실제로 몸 바쳐 본 적이 없는 신사 숙녀에게 편지를 쓴다는 것은 결코 쉬운 일이 아니다. 그런 경우에는 으레 쓰는 사람이 왼팔에 머리를 기대어 두 눈을 가능한 한 종이와 같은 높이에 두고, 자기가 만들고 있는 글자들을 곁눈질하면서, 거기에 맞춰 혀로 가상의 글자들을 빚어내는 것이 필요하다고 여겨지는 법이다. 이런 동작들은 의심할 바 없이 독창적인 글짓기에 더없이 큰 도움이 되긴 하지만, 어느 정도 쓰는 이의 진척을 더디게 한다. 그리하여 샘은 자기도 모르게 꼬박 한 시간 반 동안이나, 작은 글씨로 단어들을 쓰고, 잘못 쓴 글자를 새끼손가락으로 문질러 지우고, 새 글자를 써 넣되 옛 얼룩 사이로 드러나 보이게 하려고 몇 번씩 덧칠해야 했다. 그러던 차에 문이 열리고 자기 아버지가 들어오는 바람에 정신이 번쩍 들었다.\n\n\"잘 있었냐, 새미.\" 아버지가 말했다.\n\n\"안녕하셨어요, 우리 프러시안 블루.\" 아들이 펜을 내려놓으며 대꾸했다. \"새어머니에 관한 최신 소식은 어떻게 됐어요?\"\n\n\"웰러 부인께서는 밤은 아주 잘 보내셨으나, 오늘 아침엔 유난히 심술궂고 까칠하시다. 토니 웰러 경(卿), 선서하고 서명함. 그게 가장 최근에 나온 거다, 새미.\" 웰러 씨가 목도리를 풀며 대답했다.\n\n\"아직도 나아진 게 없어요?\" 샘이 물었다.\n\n\"증세가 죄다 더 악화됐다.\" 웰러 씨가 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 대답했다. \"그런데 너 그거 뭘 하고 있는 거냐? 역경 속에서 학문을 추구하는 거냐, 새미?\"\n\n\"이제 다 했어요.\" 샘이 약간 멋쩍어하며 말했다. \"편지를 쓰고 있었어요.\"\n\n\"그래 보이는구나.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다. \"설마 어떤 젊은 아가씨한테 쓴 건 아니겠지, 새미?\"\n\n\"아니라고 해 봐야 소용없죠.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"밸런타인이에요.\"\n\n\"뭐라고!\" 웰러 씨가 그 말에 질겁한 듯 외쳤다.\n\n\"밸런타인이라고요.\" 샘이 대답했다.\n\n\"새미블, 새미블아.\" 웰러 씨가 나무라는 어조로 말했다. \"네가 이럴 줄은 몰랐다. 네 애비의 못된 성벽(性癖)에 관해 그토록 경고를 받았으면서, 내가 바로 이 문제에 대해 너한테 그토록 일러 줬으면서, 게다가 네 새어머니를 직접 보고 함께 지내고도 말이지. 그건 어떤 사내도 죽는 날까지 결코 잊을 수 없는 도덕적 교훈이라고 생각했건만! 네가 이럴 줄은 몰랐다, 새미, 네가 이럴 줄은 정말 몰랐어!\" 이런 생각들은 마음씨 착한 노인에게 너무 벅찼다. 그는 샘의 잔을 입에 들어 올려 그 안의 것을 단숨에 들이켰다.\n\n\"이제 또 왜 그러세요?\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n\"신경 쓸 거 없다, 새미.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다. \"이 나이에 나한텐 참으로 고통스러운 시련이 되겠지만, 그래도 내가 꽤 질기다는 게 한 가지 위안이긴 하다. 농부가 런던 시장에 내다 팔려면 어쩔 수 없이 너를 잡아야겠다고 하니까, 아주 늙은 칠면조가 그러더라지. '난 꽤 질겨서요' 하고 말이야.\"\n\n\"뭐가 시련이 된다는 거예요?\" 샘이 물었다.\n\n\"네가 결혼하는 걸 보는 거 말이다, 새미—네가 속아 넘어간 희생자가 돼서, 순진하게도 그게 다 아주 멋진 일이라고 여기는 꼴을 보는 거 말이야.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다. \"그게 애비 심정엔 끔찍한 시련이라고, 새미—\"\n\n\"말도 안 돼요.\" 샘이 말했다. \"전 결혼 안 할 거니까 그런 거 가지고 애태우지 마세요. 아버지가 이런 일엔 도가 텄다는 거 잘 알아요. 파이프나 시키세요. 편지를 읽어 드릴 테니까. 자!\"\n\n웰러 씨의 마음을 가라앉히고 그의 슬픔을 잦아들게 한 것이, 파이프를 피우게 된다는 기대였는지, 아니면 결혼하고야 마는 치명적인 기질이 집안 내력이라 어쩔 도리가 없다는 위안의 생각이었는지는 우리도 또렷이 말할 수 없다. 차라리 이 두 가지 위안의 원천을 합친 데서 그 결과가 나왔다고 보는 편이 옳을 듯하다. 그가 두 번째 것을 낮은 목소리로 아주 자주 되뇌면서, 그러는 사이 종을 울려 첫 번째 것을 시켰으니 말이다. 이윽고 그는 겉옷을 벗고, 파이프에 불을 붙인 다음, 불기운을 흠뻑 느끼는 동시에 벽난로 선반에 기댈 수 있도록 불을 등지고 그 앞에 자리를 잡았다. 그러고는 샘 쪽으로 돌아서서, 담배의 누그러뜨리는 영향으로 한결 부드러워진 얼굴로, 그에게 \"어디 한번 쏴 봐라\" 하고 청했다.\n\n샘은 어떤 수정에도 대비하려고 펜을 잉크에 적시고는, 자못 연극적인 태도로 시작했다—\n\n\"'사랑스러운—'\"\n\n\"잠깐.\" 웰러 씨가 종을 울리며 말했다. \"늘 마시던 그거 두 잔 분량으로다가, 아가씨.\"\n\n\"알겠습니다, 손님.\" 그 아가씨가 대답했다. 그녀는 무척 날쌔게 나타났다가 사라지고, 돌아왔다가 다시 자취를 감췄다.\n\n\"여기선 아버지 습관을 잘 아는 것 같네요.\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n\"그렇지.\" 그의 아버지가 대답했다. \"나도 한창때 여기 와 본 적이 있으니까. 계속해라, 새미.\"\n\n\"'사랑스러운 그대여,'\" 샘이 되풀이했다.\n\n\"그거 시는 아니겠지, 응?\" 그의 아버지가 끼어들었다.\n\n\"아니에요, 아니에요.\" 샘이 대답했다.\n\n\"그거 들으니 아주 다행이다.\" 웰러 씨가 말했다. \"시란 건 부자연스러운 거야. 권투의 날 교회 머슴이나, 워런 구두약이나, 롤런드 기름이나, 그런 천박한 부류 말고는 시로 말하는 놈을 본 적이 없다. 절대로 시 따위로 말하면서 네 격을 떨어뜨리지 마라, 얘야. 다시 시작해라, 새미.\"\n\n웰러 씨는 비평가다운 엄숙함으로 다시 파이프를 물었고, 샘은 또 한 번 시작하여 다음과 같이 읽었다—\n\n\"'사랑스러운 그대여 나는 빌어먹을—'\"\n\n\"그건 적절치 않다.\" 웰러 씨가 입에서 파이프를 떼며 말했다.\n\n\"아니, '빌어먹을'이 아니에요.\" 샘이 편지를 불빛에 비춰 보며 말했다. \"'부끄러운'이에요, 거기 얼룩이 있어요—'나는 부끄러움을 느낍니다.'\"\n\n\"아주 좋다.\" 웰러 씨가 말했다. \"계속해라.\"\n\n\"'부끄러움을 느끼며, 완전히 제—' 이 단어가 뭔지 까먹었네.\" 샘이 펜으로 머리를 긁으며 기억해 내려고 헛되이 애쓰며 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 들여다보면 되잖냐?\" 웰러 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"그러니까 들여다보고 있는 거잖아요.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"근데 또 얼룩이 있어요. 여기 'ㅈ'하고 'ㅔ'하고 'ㅎ'이 있는데.\"\n\n\"'제압당했다'겠지, 아마.\" 웰러 씨가 넌지시 일러 줬다.\n\n\"아니, 그게 아니에요.\" 샘이 말했다. \"'제한되어'예요, 그거예요.\"\n\n\"그건 '제압당했다'만큼 좋은 단어는 아니다, 새미.\" 웰러 씨가 진지하게 말했다.\n\n\"그렇게 생각하세요?\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n\"비교도 안 되지.\" 그의 아버지가 대답했다.\n\n\"하지만 이게 뜻이 더 깊다고 생각 안 하세요?\" 샘이 물었다.\n\n\"흠, 어쩌면 더 부드러운 단어일지도 모르겠다.\" 웰러 씨가 잠시 생각한 뒤에 말했다. \"계속해라, 새미.\"\n\n\"'당신께 말을 거는 데 있어 부끄러움을 느끼며 완전히 제한되어 있습니다, 당신은 멋진 아가씨고 그 외엔 아무것도 아니니까요.'\"\n\n\"그것 참 아주 예쁜 정서로구나.\" 늙은 웰러 씨가 그 말을 하려고 파이프를 입에서 떼며 말했다.\n\n\"네, 저도 제법 괜찮다고 생각해요.\" 샘이 한껏 우쭐해하며 말했다.\n\n\"그런 식의 글쓰기에서 내가 마음에 드는 건 말이다,\" 늙은 웰러 씨가 말했다. \"거기엔 별명 부르기가 없다는 거다—비너스니 뭐니 그런 게 없잖냐. 젊은 아가씨를 비너스니 천사니 하고 부르는 게 무슨 소용이냐, 새미?\"\n\n\"아! 정말, 무슨 소용이겠어요?\" 샘이 대답했다.\n\n\"그럴 바엔 차라리 그 여자를 그리핀이라든가, 유니콘이라든가, 아니면 아예 왕가의 문장(紋章)이라고 부르는 편이 낫지. 그건 다들 잘 알다시피 허구의 동물들을 모아 놓은 거니까 말이다.\" 웰러 씨가 덧붙였다.\n\n\"그러는 편이 낫겠죠.\" 샘이 대답했다.\n\n\"몰아붙여라, 새미.\" 웰러 씨가 말했다.\n\n샘은 그 청에 따라 다음과 같이 이어 갔고, 그의 아버지는 지혜와 흐뭇함이 뒤섞인, 자못 교화적인 표정으로 계속 담배를 피웠다.\n\n\"'당신을 보기 전에는 여자들이 다 똑같은 줄 알았습니다.'\"\n\n\"맞는 말이지.\" 늙은 웰러 씨가 곁다리로 한마디 했다.\n\n\"'하지만 이제,'\" 샘이 이어 갔다. \"'이제는 제가 얼마나 멍청하고 잘 속아 넘어가는 순무 같은 놈이었는지 알게 됐습니다. 당신 같은 사람은 아무도 없으니까요. 그래도 저는 아무것도 없는 것보다는 당신을 더 좋아합니다.' 그 부분은 좀 강하게 하는 게 좋겠다 싶었어요.\" 샘이 고개를 들며 말했다.\n\n웰러 씨가 흡족하게 고개를 끄덕이자, 샘이 다시 이어 갔다.\n\n\"'그래서 저는 이 날의 특권을 누리기로 합니다, 메리, 내 사랑—곤경에 처한 그 신사가 일요일에 걸어 나갔을 때 그랬던 것처럼 말이죠—당신께 말씀드리려고요. 당신을 처음이자 단 한 번 보았던 그때, 당신의 모습이 제 가슴에 새겨졌는데, 그 어떤 옆얼굴 그리는 기계가 새긴 것보다(메리, 내 사랑, 당신도 그 기계 얘긴 들어 봤을지 모르겠네요) 훨씬 빠른 시간에 훨씬 밝은 색으로 새겨졌습니다. 비록 그 기계가 초상화를 완성해서 액자와 유리까지 다 끼우고, 걸 수 있게 끝에 고리까지 달아 주는 걸 단 이 분 십오 초 만에 다 해내긴 하지만요.'\"\n\n\"그건 시적인 데로 흐르는 것 같아 걱정이구나, 새미.\" 웰러 씨가 미심쩍어하며 말했다.\n\n\"아니에요, 안 그래요.\" 샘이 그 점을 두고 다투기 싫어 아주 빠르게 읽어 나가며 대답했다—\n\n\"'메리, 내 사랑, 저를 당신의 밸런타인으로 받아 주시고 제가 한 말을 곰곰이 생각해 주세요.—내 사랑 메리, 이제 이만 줄이겠습니다.' 그게 다예요.\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n\"그건 좀 갑작스럽게 멈추는 것 아니냐, 새미?\" 웰러 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"전혀 아니에요.\" 샘이 말했다. \"그 여자는 더 있었으면 하고 바랄 거예요. 그게 바로 편지 쓰기의 위대한 기술이라고요.\"\n\n\"흠,\" 웰러 씨가 말했다. \"거기엔 일리가 있구나. 네 새어머니도 그 똑같은 우아한 원칙으로 대화를 좀 이끌어 갔으면 좋겠다만. 서명은 안 할 거냐?\"\n\n\"그게 문제예요.\" 샘이 말했다. \"뭐라고 서명해야 할지 모르겠어요.\"\n\n\"'웰러'라고 서명해라.\" 그 성을 가진 현존하는 가장 오래된 소유자가 말했다.\n\n\"안 돼요.\" 샘이 말했다. \"밸런타인에는 절대 자기 본명으로 서명하지 않는 법이에요.\"\n\n\"그럼 '픽윅'이라고 서명해라.\" 웰러 씨가 말했다. \"아주 좋은 이름이고, 철자 쓰기도 쉽잖냐.\"\n\n\"바로 그거예요.\" 샘이 말했다. \"시 한 구절로 끝맺을 수도 있는데, 어떻게 생각하세요?\"\n\n\"난 별로다, 샘.\" 웰러 씨가 받아 말했다. \"시 쓰는 점잖은 마부는 단 하나밖에 못 봤다. 그것도 노상강도 죄로 교수형 당하기 전날 밤에 가슴 저미는 시 한 편을 지은 놈인데, 그자도 고작 캠버웰 출신이었으니, 그것조차 통례라곤 할 수 없지.\"\n\n그러나 샘은 자기에게 떠오른 시적인 발상을 단념할 마음이 없었으므로, 편지에 이렇게 서명했다—\n\n\"상사병에 걸린 당신의 픽윅.\"\n\n그러고는 아주 복잡한 방식으로 편지를 접어, 한쪽 귀퉁이에 비뚤배뚤 내리막으로 수신처를 쑤셔 넣었다. \"서퍽주 입스위치, 시장 너프킨스 씨 댁, 가정부 메리에게.\" 그러고는 봉인용 풀로 붙여 일반 우편으로 보낼 채비를 갖추어 주머니에 넣었다. 이 중대한 일을 처리하고 나자, 늙은 웰러 씨는 자기가 아들을 부른 그 용건을 꺼내기 시작했다.\n\n\"첫째 용건은 네 주인 나리에 관한 거다, 새미.\" 웰러 씨가 말했다. \"내일 재판을 받는다지, 그렇지?\"\n\n\"재판이 다가오고 있죠.\" 샘이 대답했다.\n\n\"음,\" 웰러 씨가 말했다. \"그럼 이제 나리께서 인품을 증언해 줄 증인을 좀 부르고 싶어 하실 테고, 아니면 알리바이를 입증하려 하실지도 모르지. 내가 이 일을 마음속으로 곰곰이 굴려 봤는데, 나리께서 마음 푹 놓으셔도 된다, 새미. 둘 중 어느 쪽이든 나리를 위해 해 줄 친구들이 나한테 있으니까. 하지만 내 충고는 이거다—인품 따위는 신경 쓰지 말고 알리바이로 밀어붙이라는 거야. 알리바이만 한 게 없다, 새미, 그만한 게 없어.\" 웰러 씨는 이 법적 견해를 펼치면서 자못 심오한 표정을 지었고, 잔에 코를 처박더니 그 너머로 어리둥절해하는 아들을 향해 한쪽 눈을 찡긋했다.\n\n\"아니, 무슨 말씀이세요?\" 샘이 말했다. \"설마 나리가 올드 베일리에서 재판받는다고 생각하시는 건 아니죠?\"\n\n\"그건 지금 고려할 사항이 아니다, 새미.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다. \"나리가 어디서 재판받든, 얘야, 그분을 빼내는 데는 알리바이가 제일이다. 우리가 톰 와일드스파크를 그 과실치사에서 알리바이로 빼냈잖냐, 그 거물 양반들이 하나같이 그놈을 구할 길이 없다고 했는데도 말이다. 그래서 내 생각엔, 새미, 네 주인 나리가 알리바이를 입증하지 못하면, 이탈리아 사람들 말로 아주 옴짝달싹 못 하게 될 거라는 거지, 그게 다야.\"\n\n늙은 웰러 씨는 올드 베일리가 이 나라의 최고 사법 법원이며, 그 규칙과 소송 절차가 다른 모든 사법 법원의 관행을 규제하고 통제한다는 굳고 흔들림 없는 확신을 품고 있었으므로, 알리바이는 받아들여질 수 없다는 것을 보여 주려는 아들의 장담과 논거를 완전히 무시하고, 픽윅 씨가 \"희생양이 되고 있다\"라고 격렬히 항변했다. 이 문제를 더 논해 봐야 소용없다는 것을 알고, 샘은 화제를 바꾸어, 자기의 존경하는 부친이 자기와 상의하고 싶어 한 두 번째 화제가 무엇인지 물었다.\n\n\"그건 집안 정책에 관한 문제다, 새미.\" 웰러 씨가 말했다. \"이 스티긴스라는 자가—\"\n\n\"빨간 코의 사내요?\" 샘이 물었다.\n\n\"바로 그자다.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다. \"이 빨간 코의 사내가 말이다, 새미, 네 새어머니를 찾아오는데, 그 친절함과 한결같음이란 게 내가 일찍이 견줄 만한 걸 본 적이 없을 정도야. 어찌나 우리 집안의 벗인지, 새미, 우리한테서 떨어져 있으면 우리를 기억할 만한 뭔가를 갖지 않고선 도통 편안해하질 못한단 말이지.\"\n\n\"제가 아버지라면 그자의 기억력을 앞으로 한 십 년쯤은 테레빈유와 밀랍으로 잘 닦아 줄 만한 걸 한 방 먹여 주겠어요.\" 샘이 끼어들었다.\n\n\"잠깐만.\" 웰러 씨가 말했다. \"내가 하려던 말은, 그자가 이제 항상 한 파인트 반쯤 들어가는 납작한 병을 가져와서, 떠나기 전에 거기다 파인애플 럼을 채운다는 거다.\"\n\n\"그러고는 돌아오기 전에 다 비우겠죠, 아마?\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n\"싹 비우지!\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다. \"그 안에 코르크 마개랑 냄새 말곤 아무것도 안 남긴다, 그 점은 그자한테 맡겨 둬도 된다, 새미. 자, 그런데 이 작자들이 말이다, 얘야, 오늘 밤에 통합 대(大) 연합 에버니저 금주 협회 브릭 레인 지부의 월례 모임을 연다는 거다. 네 새어머니가 가려고 했는데, 새미, 류머티즘에 걸려서 못 가게 됐어. 그래서 내가, 새미—그이한테 보내온 표 두 장을 내가 손에 넣었다.\" 웰러 씨는 이 비밀을 잔뜩 신이 나서 전했고, 그러고 나서 어찌나 지칠 줄 모르고 눈을 찡긋대던지, 샘은 아버지가 오른쪽 눈꺼풀에 안면 신경통이라도 걸린 게 아닌가 생각하기 시작했다.\n\n\"그래서요?\" 그 젊은 신사가 말했다.\n\n\"그래서,\" 그의 아버지가 무척 조심스레 주위를 둘러보며 이어 갔다. \"너랑 나랑 시간 딱 맞춰서 가는 거다. 부목사 양반은 못 갈 거야, 새미. 부목사 양반은 못 가.\" 여기서 웰러 씨는 발작적인 킬킬거림에 사로잡혔고, 그것은 차츰차츰 노신사가 안전하게 견뎌 낼 수 있는 한도에서 거의 질식에 가까운 지경에 이르렀다.\n\n\"아니, 평생 살면서 이런 늙은 도깨비는 처음 보네요.\" 샘이 노신사의 등을 어찌나 세게 문질렀던지 마찰로 불이라도 날 지경이었다. \"뭐가 그렇게 우스워요, 뚱보 영감님?\"\n\n\"쉿! 새미.\" 웰러 씨가 한층 조심스레 주위를 둘러보며 속삭이는 목소리로 말했다. \"옥스퍼드 로드에서 일하는 데다 온갖 수작에 도가 튼 내 친구 두 명이, 그 부목사 양반을 단단히 끌고 다니고 있다, 새미. 그래서 그자가 에버니저 정거장에 정말로 오게 되면(틀림없이 올 거다. 그 친구들이 그자를 문 앞까지 데려다 주고, 필요하면 떠밀어 넣을 거니까), 그자는 도킹의 마키스 오브 그랜비에서 그랬던 만큼이나 럼 워터에 흠뻑 취해 있을 거다. 그것도 결코 적게 말하는 게 아니지.\" 그러고 나서 웰러 씨는 또 한 번 걷잡을 수 없이 웃어 댔고, 그 결과 또 한 번 반쯤 질식한 상태에 빠져들었다.\n\n빨간 코의 사내가 지닌 진짜 성벽과 본바탕을 까발리려는 이 계획만큼 샘 웰러의 마음에 쏙 드는 것도 없었다. 모임이 약속된 시각에 무척 임박했으므로, 부자는 곧장 브릭 레인 쪽으로 길을 잡았고, 걸어가는 동안 샘은 자기 편지를 일반 우체국에 떨어뜨리는 것을 잊지 않았다.\n\n통합 대 연합 에버니저 금주 협회 브릭 레인 지부의 월례 모임은, 안전하고 널찍한 사다리 꼭대기에 쾌적하고 바람 잘 통하게 자리 잡은 큰 방에서 열렸다. 의장은 곧은 걸음걸이의 앤서니 험 씨였는데, 회심한 소방관 출신으로 지금은 학교 교사이자 이따금 순회 설교자였다. 그리고 서기는 양초 가게 주인인 조나스 머지 씨였는데, 열성적이고 사심 없는 그릇으로, 회원들에게 차를 파는 사람이었다. 일이 시작되기에 앞서, 부인들은 긴 의자에 앉아, 이제 그만두는 게 좋겠다 싶을 때까지 차를 마셨다. 그리고 큰 나무 돈 상자 하나가 사무용 탁자의 초록색 베이즈 천 위에 눈에 잘 띄게 놓여 있었으니, 서기는 그 뒤에 서서, 그 안에 숨겨진 풍부한 구리 광맥에 새로 더해지는 한 닢 한 닢마다 자애로운 미소로 답례했다.\n\n이 특별한 자리에서 여자들은 더없이 놀라운 정도로 차를 마셔 댔으니, 늙은 웰러 씨로서는 여간 질겁할 일이 아니었다. 그는 샘이 아무리 옆구리를 쿡쿡 찔러 가며 주의를 줘도 도무지 아랑곳없이, 더없이 노골적인 경악의 표정으로 사방을 두리번거렸다.\n\n\"새미,\" 웰러 씨가 속삭였다. \"이 사람들 중 몇몇은 내일 아침에 마개 따 줘야 할 판 아니냐, 안 그럼 내가 네 애비가 아니다, 그게 사실이라니까. 아니, 내 옆에 이 노부인은 차에 빠져 죽고 있구나.\"\n\n\"좀 가만히 계실 수 없어요?\" 샘이 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"샘,\" 웰러 씨가 잠시 후 깊이 동요한 어조로 속삭였다. \"내 말 명심해라, 얘야. 저기 저 서기 녀석이 단 오 분만 더 저러고 있으면, 토스트 워터로 제 몸을 터뜨려 버릴 거다.\"\n\n\"그럼 그러게 두세요, 그자가 그러고 싶다면요.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"아버지가 상관할 바 아니잖아요.\"\n\n\"이게 좀 더 오래가면, 새미,\" 웰러 씨가 똑같이 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"나는 인간으로서 일어나 의장한테 한마디 하는 게 내 의무라고 느낄 것 같다. 두 줄 건너 긴 의자에 젊은 아가씨가 아침 찻잔으로 아홉 잔 반을 마셨는데, 내 바로 눈앞에서 눈에 띄게 부풀어 오르고 있단 말이지.\"\n\n찻잔과 받침을 치우면서 난 큰 소리가, 다행스럽게도 차 마시기가 끝났음을 알려 주지 않았더라면, 웰러 씨가 그 자비로운 의도를 당장 실행에 옮겼으리라는 것은 의심할 여지가 거의 없다. 그릇이 치워지자, 초록색 베이즈 천을 덮은 탁자가 방 한가운데로 옮겨졌고, 그날 저녁의 일은, 대머리에 황갈색 반바지를 입은 자그마하고 힘 있는 사내가 시작했는데, 그는 황갈색 반바지에 싸인 가느다란 두 다리가 부러질 만큼 위태롭게 갑작스레 사다리를 뛰어 올라와 이렇게 말했다—\n\n\"신사 숙녀 여러분, 저는 우리의 훌륭하신 형제, 앤서니 험 씨를 의장으로 추대합니다.\"\n\n부인들은 이 제안에 엄선한 손수건들을 흔들어 댔다. 그리고 그 성마른 자그마한 사내는, 험 씨의 어깨를 붙잡아 한때 의장석이라는 가구를 이루었던 마호가니 틀 속으로 밀어 넣음으로써, 말 그대로 험 씨를 의장석으로 옮겨 놓았다. 손수건 흔들기가 다시 이어졌다. 그리고 매끄럽고 핼쑥한 얼굴에 늘 땀을 흘리는 험 씨는 온순하게 절을 했으니, 여자들의 큰 감탄을 자아냈고, 정식으로 자리에 앉았다. 그러자 황갈색 반바지의 자그마한 사내가 정숙을 선포했고, 험 씨가 일어나 말했다—그곳에 모인 브릭 레인 지부의 형제자매들이 허락한다면, 서기가 브릭 레인 지부 위원회의 보고서를 낭독하겠다는 것이었다. 이 제안은 또 한 번 손수건의 시위로 환영받았다.\n\n서기가 자못 인상적인 방식으로 재채기를 하고, 무슨 특별한 일이 벌어지려 할 때면 으레 회중을 사로잡는 그 헛기침이 마땅히 행해진 뒤에, 다음 문서가 낭독되었다—\n\n\"통합 대 연합 에버니저 금주 협회 브릭 레인 지부 위원회 보고서\n\n\"귀 위원회는 지난 한 달 동안 그 고마운 수고를 계속해 왔으며, 다음과 같이 금주로 전향한 추가 사례들을 보고하게 되어 이루 말할 수 없는 기쁨을 느끼는 바입니다.\n\n\"재단사 H. 워커, 아내, 그리고 두 자녀. 형편이 더 나았을 때, 끊임없이 에일과 맥주를 마시는 습관이 있었음을 인정함. 이십 년간 일주일에 두 번 '도그스 노즈'를 맛보지 않았는지 확신할 수 없다고 함. 귀 위원회가 조사한바, 그것은 따뜻한 포터, 축축한 설탕, 진, 육두구를 섞어 만든 것이라 함(어느 나이 든 여성에게서 신음 소리와 '바로 그래요!' 하는 외침). 지금은 실직하고 무일푼임. 그게 포터 탓이 틀림없다고 생각함(환호). 아니면 오른손을 못 쓰게 된 탓이라고 함. 어느 쪽인지 확실치는 않으나, 평생 물밖에 마시지 않았더라면 동료 일꾼이 녹슨 바늘로 자기를 찔러 그 사고를 일으키는 일은 결코 없었으리라고 생각함(엄청난 환호). 마실 것은 찬물밖에 없으며, 결코 목마름을 느끼지 않음(큰 박수).\n\n\"과부 벳시 마틴, 자녀 하나, 외눈 하나. 날품으로 청소와 빨래일을 나감. 눈은 처음부터 하나밖에 없었으나, 어머니가 병맥주 스타우트를 마셨다는 것을 알고 있으며, 그게 원인이래도 놀랍지 않다고 함(엄청난 환호). 늘 독한 술을 멀리했더라면 지금쯤 두 눈을 가졌을 수도 있다는 게 불가능한 일은 아니라고 생각함(어마어마한 박수). 일하러 가는 곳마다 하루 일 실링 육 펜스, 포터 한 파인트, 독주 한 잔을 받곤 했으나, 브릭 레인 지부 회원이 된 뒤로는 늘 삼 실링 육 펜스를 요구해 왔다고 함(이 더없이 흥미로운 사실의 발표는 귀를 멍하게 하는 열광으로 환영받음).\n\n\"헨리 벨러는 여러 해 동안 갖가지 시(市) 당국 만찬에서 건배 진행을 맡았으며, 그동안 외국산 포도주를 엄청나게 마셨음. 때로는 한두 병을 집에 가져갔을 수도 있으나, 그 점은 확실치 않다고 함. 그러나 만약 그랬다면 그 내용물을 다 마셨으리라는 것만은 확실하다고 함. 몹시 처지고 우울하며, 열이 심하고, 끊임없이 목마름에 시달림. 그게 예전에 마시던 포도주 탓이 틀림없다고 생각함(환호). 지금은 실직 상태이며, 어떤 일이 있어도 외국산 포도주는 한 방울도 입에 대지 않음(엄청난 갈채).\n\n\"토머스 버턴은 시장님과 주(州) 장관들, 그리고 시 참사회 의원 여러 명에게 고양이 먹이를 납품하는 사람임(이 신사의 이름 발표는 숨죽인 관심으로 환영받음). 나무 의족이 하나 있음. 돌길을 다니다 보니 나무 의족 값이 많이 든다는 것을 알게 됨. 예전엔 중고 나무 의족을 차고, 매일 밤 규칙적으로 뜨거운 진 워터 한 잔씩을—때로는 두 잔씩 마시곤 했음(깊은 한숨). 중고 나무 의족이 아주 빨리 갈라지고 썩는 것을 발견함. 그 의족들의 체질이 진 워터에 의해 약해진 것이라고 굳게 믿음(길게 이어진 환호). 지금은 새 나무 의족을 사며, 물과 묽은 차밖에 마시지 않음. 새 의족은 예전 것보다 두 배나 오래가는데, 이것이 오로지 자신의 절제하는 습관 덕분이라고 봄(의기양양한 환호).\"\n\n앤서니 험은 이제 회중이 노래 한 곡으로 즐기자고 발의했다. 회중의 이성적이고 도덕적인 즐거움을 위하여, 모들린 형제가 '유쾌한 젊은 뱃사공을 모르는 이 누구랴?'라는 아름다운 노랫말을 '구(舊) 백 편'의 가락에 맞춰 편곡했으니, 자기와 함께 그것을 부르는 데 동참해 주기를 청한다는 것이었다(큰 박수). 그는 이 기회를 빌려, 작고하신 디브딘 씨가 자신의 지난 삶의 잘못을 깨닫고, 절제의 이로움을 보여 주려고 그 노래를 지었다는 자신의 굳은 확신을 표명하고자 했다. 그것은 금주 노래였다(회오리바람 같은 환호). 젊은이의 단정한 옷차림, 노 젓는 솜씨, 그리고 시인의 아름다운 말마따나 그로 하여금\n\n\"아무 생각 없이 노 저어 가게\"\n\n한 그 부러운 마음 상태—이 모든 것이 한데 모여, 그가 물 마시는 사람임에 틀림없다는 것을 입증했다(환호). 오, 이 얼마나 덕스럽고 유쾌한 상태인가!(황홀한 환호). 그리고 그 젊은이가 받은 보상은 무엇이었던가? 여기 계신 모든 젊은이는 이것을 새겨들으라—\n\n\"처녀들이 모두 그의 배로 그토록 선뜻 모여들었네.\"\n\n(우렁찬 환호, 거기에 부인들도 가담함.) 이 얼마나 빛나는 본보기인가! 자매들이, 처녀들이, 젊은 뱃사공 주위로 모여들어 그를 의무와 절제의 물줄기를 따라 떠밀어 주었던 것이다. 그러나 그를 어루만지고 위로하고 떠받쳐 준 것이 비천한 신분의 처녀들뿐이었던가? 아니다!\n\n\"그는 우아한 도시 숙녀들과도 늘 으뜸 노잡이였네.\"\n\n(엄청난 환호.) 그 연약한 성(性)이 하나같이—그는 사죄하건대, 여성이 하나같이—젊은 뱃사공 주위로 결집했고, 독주 마시는 자에게서는 진저리치며 등을 돌렸던 것이다(환호). 브릭 레인 지부 형제들은 뱃사공이었다(환호와 웃음). 이 방은 그들의 배요, 이 청중은 그 처녀들이요, 그리고 그(앤서니 험 씨)는, 비록 변변치 못하나마, '으뜸 노잡이'였다(끝없는 박수).\n\n\"연약한 성이라는 게 무슨 뜻이냐, 새미?\" 웰러 씨가 속삭이며 물었다.\n\n\"여자들요.\" 샘이 같은 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"거기선 그자가 영 틀린 말은 아니구나, 새미.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다. \"여자들이 분명 연약한 성이긴 하지—아주 연약한 성이고말고—저런 작자들한테 속아 넘어가게 제 몸을 내맡길 정도라면 말이야.\"\n\n격분한 노신사의 더 이상의 논평은, 노래가 발표되면서 끊겨 버렸으니, 앤서니 험 씨는 그 노래의 사연을 모르는 청중을 위해 한 번에 두 줄씩 일러 주었다. 노래가 불리는 동안, 황갈색 반바지의 자그마한 사내가 사라졌다가, 노래가 끝나자마자 곧장 돌아와, 더없이 중대한 표정으로 앤서니 험 씨에게 귀엣말을 했다.\n\n\"여러분,\" 험 씨가, 아직 한두 줄 뒤처진 뚱뚱한 노부인들의 정숙을 청하려고 만류하는 몸짓으로 손을 들어 올리며 말했다. \"여러분, 우리 협회 도킹 지부의 대표인 스티긴스 형제가 아래에 와 계십니다.\"\n\n다시 손수건들이 나왔으니, 그 어느 때보다 기세 좋았다. 스티긴스 씨는 브릭 레인의 여성 회원들 사이에서 대단히 인기가 높았던 것이다.\n\n\"올라오셔도 될 것 같습니다.\" 험 씨가 살진 미소를 띠고 주위를 둘러보며 말했다. \"태저 형제, 그분을 모셔 올라와 우리에게 인사시켜 주세요.\"\n\n태저 형제라는 이름에 답한 황갈색 반바지의 자그마한 사내는 무척 빠르게 사다리를 부산히 내려갔고, 곧이어 스티긴스 목사와 함께 비틀비틀 올라오는 소리가 들렸다.\n\n\"올라온다, 새미.\" 웰러 씨가 억누른 웃음으로 얼굴이 시뻘게져 속삭였다.\n\n\"저한테 아무 말도 하지 마세요.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"도저히 못 참겠으니까요. 그자가 문 바로 앞에 와 있네요. 지금 욋가지하고 회반죽 벽에 머리를 쿵쿵 박는 소리가 들려요.\"\n\n샘 웰러가 말하는 사이, 작은 문이 활짝 열리며 태저 형제가 나타났고, 바로 뒤에 스티긴스 목사가 따라왔다. 그가 들어서기가 무섭게 요란한 박수와 발 구르는 소리와 손수건 흔들기가 터져 나왔다. 그러나 이 모든 환희의 표시에 스티긴스 형제는, 탁자 위 촛불 심지의 맨 꼭대기를 광기 어린 눈과 굳은 미소로 응시하며, 그러는 동안 자기 몸을 무척 불안하고 종잡을 수 없는 모양새로 앞뒤로 흔드는 것 말고는 아무런 답례도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"몸이 편찮으십니까, 스티긴스 형제?\" 앤서니 험 씨가 속삭였다.\n\n\"멀쩡합니다, 선생.\" 스티긴스 씨가, 사나움과 극도로 굳은 발음이 뒤섞인 어조로 대답했다. \"멀쩡하다고요, 선생.\"\n\n\"오, 그렇군요.\" 앤서니 험 씨가 몇 걸음 물러서며 받아 말했다.\n\n\"여기 있는 누구도 감히 내가 멀쩡하지 않다고는 못 했을 텐데요, 선생?\" 스티긴스 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"오, 물론입니다, 아무도요.\" 험 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"안 그러는 게 좋을 거요, 선생. 안 그러는 게 좋을 거라고 충고하겠소, 선생.\" 스티긴스 씨가 말했다.\n\n이쯤 되자 청중은 완전히 조용해졌고, 다소 불안한 마음으로 일이 재개되기를 기다렸다.\n\n\"모임을 향해 한 말씀 하시겠습니까, 형제?\" 험 씨가 권유하는 미소를 띠고 말했다.\n\n\"아니오, 선생.\" 스티긴스 씨가 받아 말했다. \"아니오, 선생. 안 하겠소, 선생.\"\n\n모임에 참석한 이들은 눈꺼풀을 치켜뜨고 서로를 바라보았고, 경악의 웅성거림이 방 안을 휩쓸었다.\n\n\"내 의견은 말이오, 선생,\" 스티긴스 씨가 코트 단추를 풀며 무척 큰 소리로 말했다. \"내 의견은, 선생, 이 모임이 취해 있다는 거요, 선생. 태저 형제, 선생!\" 스티긴스 씨가 갑자기 사나움을 더하며 황갈색 반바지의 자그마한 사내에게 홱 돌아서서 말했다. \"당신이 취했소, 선생!\" 이렇게 말하면서 스티긴스 씨는, 모임의 금주를 도모하고 거기서 모든 부적절한 인물을 배제하려는 가상한 욕심을 품고, 태저 형제의 콧등 꼭대기를 어찌나 정확히 겨냥하여 후려쳤던지, 황갈색 반바지가 번갯불처럼 사라져 버렸다. 태저 형제가 머리부터 사다리 아래로 처박힌 것이다.\n\n이에 여자들은 요란하고 처량한 비명을 질러 댔고, 작은 무리를 지어 자기들이 좋아하는 형제들 앞으로 달려가, 위험에서 그들을 지키려고 그들에게 두 팔을 둘러 감았다. 그러한 애정의 한 사례는 험에게 거의 치명적일 뻔했으니, 그는 워낙 인기가 많아, 그의 목에 매달려 애무를 퍼붓는 여성 신도들의 무리에 거의 질식할 지경이었다. 등불의 대부분이 금세 꺼졌고, 사방에 시끄러운 소리와 혼란만이 울려 퍼졌다.\n\n\"자, 새미,\" 웰러 씨가 무척 신중하게 외투를 벗으며 말했다. \"너 어서 나가서, 야경꾼 한 명 데려와라.\"\n\n\"그러는 동안 아버진 뭘 하시려고요?\" 샘이 물었다.\n\n\"넌 신경 쓸 거 없다, 새미.\" 노신사가 대답했다. \"난 저기 저 스티긴스랑 자그마한 결판을 내느라 바쁠 테니까.\" 샘이 끼어들어 그것을 막을 새도 없이, 그의 영웅적인 부친은 방의 외진 구석으로 파고들어, 스티긴스 목사에게 능숙한 주먹질로 달려들었다.\n\n\"그만두세요!\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n\"덤벼라!\" 웰러 씨가 외쳤다. 그러고는 더 청할 것도 없이 스티긴스 목사의 머리를 한 대 선제로 톡 치고는, 그 주위를 통통 튀듯, 코르크처럼 가볍게 춤추며 돌기 시작했으니, 그 연배의 신사에게서 그것은 보기에 그야말로 경이로운 광경이었다.\n\n아무리 타일러도 소용없다는 것을 알고, 샘은 모자를 단단히 눌러쓰고, 아버지의 코트를 팔에 걸친 다음, 노인의 허리를 붙잡아 힘껏 사다리 아래로, 그리고 거리로 끌어냈다. 모퉁이에 이를 때까지 결코 붙든 손을 놓지도, 아버지를 멈춰 세우게 하지도 않았다. 모퉁이에 다다랐을 때, 그들은 스티긴스 목사가 그날 밤 묵을 튼튼한 숙소로 끌려가는 광경을 지켜보는 군중의 외침 소리를 들을 수 있었고, 또한 통합 대 연합 에버니저 금주 협회 브릭 레인 지부 회원들이 사방으로 흩어지면서 일으키는 소란도 들을 수 있었다.\n\n이번 2월 14일의 중대한 아침, 대화를 이어 가려는 듯 스노드그래스 씨가 입을 열었다. \"배심원장이—누가 됐든 간에—아침으로 뭘 먹었을지 궁금하군요.\"\n\n\"아!\" 퍼커가 말했다. \"잘 챙겨 먹었기를 바라야지요.\"\n\n\"어째서요?\" 픽윅 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"매우 중요한 일입니다—아주 중요한 일이지요, 친애하는 선생.\" 퍼커가 대답했다. \"잘 먹어 만족한 배심원을 만나는 것보다 더 좋은 일은 없습니다. 불만에 차 있거나 굶주린 배심원은, 친애하는 선생, 언제나 원고에게 유리한 평결을 내리거든요.\"\n\n\"이런 세상에.\" 픽윅 씨가 몹시 멍한 표정을 지으며 말했다. \"어째서 그런답니까?\"\n\n\"글쎄요, 저도 잘 모르겠습니다.\" 작은 사내는 태연히 대답했다. \"시간을 아끼려고 그러는 것이겠지요. 저녁 식사 시간이 가까우면, 배심원들이 물러난 뒤 배심원장이 시계를 꺼내 보고는 이러는 겁니다. '이런, 여러분, 벌써 다섯 시 십 분 전이로군요! 나는 다섯 시에 저녁을 든답니다, 여러분.' 그러면 다들 '나도 그렇소'라고 하지요. 다만 세 시에 저녁을 들었어야 할 두 사람만은 그 때문에 끝까지 버틸 기미를 절반 넘게 보입니다. 배심원장은 빙긋 웃으며 시계를 도로 넣고 말하지요. '자, 여러분, 우리가 어떻게 정할까요, 원고요 피고요, 여러분? 내 생각으로는, 어디까지나 나로서는 말입니다, 여러분—말하건대, 내 생각엔—하지만 그게 여러분에게 영향을 미치게 하진 마십시오—내 생각엔 원고 쪽이 옳은 것 같소.' 그러면 두세 사람이 분명 자기들도 그렇게 생각한다고 말하지요—물론 그러고말고요. 그렇게 해서 아주 만장일치로, 아주 편안하게 일이 흘러가는 겁니다. 아홉 시 십 분이로군요!\" 작은 사내가 시계를 보며 말했다. \"떠날 시간입니다, 친애하는 선생. 약혼 불이행 재판이 열리는 법정은 이런 사건엔 으레 만원이지요. 마차를 부르시는 게 좋겠습니다, 친애하는 선생. 안 그러면 좀 늦겠어요.\"\n\n픽윅 씨가 즉시 종을 울렸고, 마차가 마련되자 네 명의 픽윅 회원과 퍼커 씨가 그 안에 자리를 잡고 길드홀로 달려갔다. 샘 웰러와 라우튼 씨, 그리고 파란 가방은 삯마차를 타고 뒤따랐다.\n\n\"라우튼,\" 법정 바깥 홀에 이르렀을 때 퍼커가 말했다. \"픽윅 씨의 친구분들은 방청석에 모시고, 픽윅 씨는 내 옆에 앉으시는 게 좋겠네. 이쪽입니다, 친애하는 선생, 이쪽으로.\" 작은 사내는 픽윅 씨의 외투 소매를 잡고, 칙선 변호사들의 책상 바로 아래에 있는 낮은 자리로 그를 이끌었다. 그곳은 사무 변호사들의 편의를 위해 마련된 자리로, 거기서는 재판이 진행되는 동안 필요한 지시 사항을 사건 주임 변호사의 귀에 속삭여 줄 수 있었다. 이 자리에 앉은 사람들은 대다수 방청객의 눈에는 보이지 않았으니, 변호사들이나 청중보다 훨씬 낮은 곳에 앉아 있기 때문이었다. 변호사석이나 방청석은 바닥보다 높이 솟아 있었다. 물론 그들은 양쪽에 등을 돌리고 판사를 마주 보고 앉아 있었다.\n\n\"저게 증인석이겠지요?\" 픽윅 씨가 왼편에 있는, 놋쇠 난간이 달린 설교단 비슷한 것을 가리키며 말했다.\n\n\"저게 증인석입니다, 친애하는 선생.\" 라우튼이 막 발치에 내려놓은 파란 가방에서 잔뜩 서류를 파내며 퍼커가 대답했다.\n\n\"그러면 저건,\" 픽윅 씨가 오른편의 막힌 자리 둘을 가리키며 말했다. \"배심원들이 앉는 곳이지요, 그렇지 않습니까?\"\n\n\"바로 그곳입니다, 친애하는 선생.\" 퍼커가 코담배갑 뚜껑을 톡톡 두드리며 대답했다.\n\n픽윅 씨는 몹시 불안한 상태로 일어서서 법정을 한 차례 둘러보았다. 방청석에는 이미 꽤 많은 구경꾼들이 흩어져 있었고, 변호사석에는 가발을 쓴 신사들이 수두룩하게 모여 있었으니, 그들은 한 무리로서 영국 법조계가 그토록 정당하게 명성을 떨치는, 그 유쾌하고도 다양한 갖가지 코와 구레나룻을 죄다 갖추고 있었다. 변론서를 들고 갈 일이 있는 신사들은 되도록 눈에 띄게 그것을 들고 다녔고, 이따금 그것으로 코를 긁어 그 사실을 더 강하게 구경꾼들의 눈에 새겨 넣었다. 보여 줄 변론서가 없는 다른 신사들은 뒤쪽에 빨간 딱지가 붙고, 설익은 파이 껍질 빛깔의 표지를 댄—전문 용어로 '법률용 송아지 가죽'이라 불리는—멋진 8절판 책들을 옆구리에 끼고 있었다. 변론서도 책도 없는 다른 이들은 두 손을 주머니에 찔러 넣고서 되도록 현명해 보이는 표정을 지었다. 또 다른 이들은 안절부절못하며 자못 진지한 태도로 여기저기 오가면서, 그것으로써 사정을 모르는 낯선 이들의 감탄과 놀라움을 자아내는 데 만족했다. 픽윅 씨가 크게 놀란 것은, 그 전부가 작은 무리들로 나뉘어, 마치 재판 따위는 닥쳐오지도 않는다는 듯, 더없이 무심한 태도로 그날의 소식을 떠들고 논하고 있다는 점이었다.\n\n펑키 씨가 들어와 칙선 변호사들에게 배정된 줄 뒤에 자리를 잡으면서 인사를 보냈고, 그것이 픽윅 씨의 주의를 끌었다. 픽윅 씨가 미처 답례를 다 하기도 전에 스너빈 변호사가 나타났다. 그 뒤를 맬러드 씨가 따랐는데, 그가 커다란 진홍색 가방을 책상 위에 내려놓는 바람에 변호사의 모습이 반쯤 가려졌다. 맬러드 씨는 퍼커와 악수를 나눈 뒤 물러갔다. 이어서 두세 명의 변호사가 더 들어왔는데, 그 가운데 몸집이 뚱뚱하고 얼굴이 불그레한 자가 스너빈 변호사에게 다정하게 고갯짓하며 좋은 아침이라고 인사했다.\n\n\"좋은 아침이라며 우리 변호사에게 고갯짓한 저 얼굴 붉은 사람은 누굽니까?\" 픽윅 씨가 속삭였다.\n\n\"버즈퍼즈 변호사입니다.\" 퍼커가 대답했다. \"우리 쪽 반대편이지요. 상대측 주임 변호사입니다. 그 뒤에 있는 신사는 그의 부변호사인 스킴핀 씨입니다.\"\n\n픽윅 씨는 그 사내의 냉혈한 같은 악랄함에 큰 혐오를 느끼며, 상대측 변호사인 버즈퍼즈 변호사가 어떻게 감히 자기 측 변호사인 스너빈 변호사에게 좋은 아침이라고 말할 수 있는지 막 따져 물으려던 참이었다. 그때 변호사들이 일제히 일어서고 법정 관리들이 \"정숙!\" 하고 큰 소리로 외치는 바람에 말이 끊겼다. 둘러보니 그것은 판사가 입정하기 때문이었다.\n\n스테어리 판사는(수석 판사가 몸이 편치 않아 자리를 비운 탓에 대신 앉은 것이었다) 유독 키가 작은 사내였고, 어찌나 뚱뚱한지 온통 얼굴과 조끼뿐인 것 같았다. 그는 깎아 만든 듯한 작은 두 다리로 굴러들어 와, 법조인들에게 엄숙히 고개를 까딱했고—그들도 그에게 엄숙히 고개를 까딱했다—작은 다리를 책상 밑에 넣고 작은 삼각모를 책상 위에 올려놓았다. 스테어리 판사가 이렇게 하고 나니, 그에게서 볼 수 있는 것이라곤 괴상한 작은 두 눈과 널찍한 분홍빛 얼굴 하나, 그리고 크고 매우 우스꽝스러워 보이는 가발의 절반쯤뿐이었다.\n\n판사가 자리에 앉기 무섭게 법정 바닥에 있던 관리가 명령조로 \"정숙!\"이라 외쳤고, 그러자 방청석에 있던 또 다른 관리가 성난 투로 \"정숙!\"이라 외쳤으며, 이어서 서너 명의 정리들이 분개해 항의하는 목소리로 \"정숙!\"이라 고함쳤다. 이 일이 끝나자, 판사 아래에 앉아 있던 검은 옷의 신사가 배심원들의 이름을 부르기 시작했다. 한참을 외쳐 댄 끝에, 특별 배심원이 열 명밖에 출석하지 않은 사실이 드러났다. 이에 버즈퍼즈 변호사가 보충 배심을 청했고, 검은 옷의 신사는 일반 배심원 가운데 둘을 특별 배심에 밀어 넣었으니, 청과상 한 명과 약제사 한 명이 곧장 붙잡혔다.\n\n\"선서하실 수 있도록, 호명에 답하십시오, 여러분.\" 검은 옷의 신사가 말했다. \"리처드 업위치.\"\n\n\"여기 있습니다.\" 청과상이 말했다.\n\n\"토머스 그로핀.\"\n\n\"여기 있습니다.\" 약제사가 말했다.\n\n\"성경을 받으십시오, 여러분. 두 분은 성실하고 진실하게 심리하실 것이며—\"\n\n\"법정에 죄송합니다만,\" 키가 크고 마른, 누런 낯빛의 사내인 약제사가 말했다. \"법정께서 제 출석을 면해 주시기를 바랍니다.\"\n\n\"무슨 근거로 그러시오, 선생?\" 스테어리 판사가 말했다.\n\n\"저는 조수가 없습니다, 판사님.\" 약제사가 말했다.\n\n\"그건 내가 어쩔 수 없는 일이오, 선생.\" 스테어리 판사가 대답했다. \"한 사람 고용하시오.\"\n\n\"그럴 형편이 못 됩니다, 판사님.\" 약제사가 받아쳤다.\n\n\"그러면 그럴 형편이 되어야 마땅하오, 선생.\" 판사가 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다. 스테어리 판사의 성미는 짜증에 가까웠고, 반박을 참지 못했다.\n\n\"제가 받아 마땅한 만큼 잘되었다면 마땅히 그래야겠지요. 하지만 그렇질 못합니다, 판사님.\" 약제사가 대답했다.\n\n\"저 신사에게 선서를 시키시오.\" 판사가 단호하게 말했다.\n\n관리가 \"두 분은 성실하고 진실하게 심리하실 것이며—\"라는 데까지 갔을 때, 또다시 약제사가 끼어들었다.\n\n\"저더러 선서를 하라는 거지요, 판사님? 그렇습니까?\" 약제사가 말했다.\n\n\"물론이오, 선생.\" 짜증 난 작은 판사가 대답했다.\n\n\"좋습니다, 판사님.\" 약제사가 체념한 듯 대답했다. \"그러면 이 재판이 끝나기 전에 살인이 날 겁니다. 그뿐입니다. 그럼 선서를 시키십시오, 부디.\" 판사가 입을 열 말을 미처 찾기도 전에 약제사는 선서를 마쳤다.\n\n\"제가 그저 말씀드리고 싶었던 건, 판사님,\" 약제사가 아주 신중하게 자리에 앉으며 말했다. \"제 가게에 심부름꾼 소년 하나밖에 남겨 두지 않았다는 겁니다. 아주 착한 아이지요, 판사님. 하지만 약에 대해선 잘 모릅니다. 게다가 그 아이 머릿속에는 엡솜소금이 옥살산이고, 센나 시럽이 아편팅크라는 생각이 박혀 있는 게 분명합니다. 그뿐입니다, 판사님.\" 이렇게 말하고 키 큰 약제사는 편안한 자세를 취하더니, 유쾌한 표정을 지으며 최악의 사태에 대비한 듯이 보였다.\n\n픽윅 씨가 더없이 깊은 공포의 심정으로 약제사를 바라보고 있을 때, 법정 안에 가벼운 동요가 일었다. 그러더니 곧 바델 부인이 클러핀스 부인의 부축을 받으며 끌려 들어와, 픽윅 씨가 앉은 자리의 반대쪽 끝에 풀 죽은 모습으로 앉혀졌다. 이어서 도드슨 씨가 특대 크기의 우산을 건넸고, 포그 씨가 나막신 한 켤레를 건넸으니, 두 사람 모두 그 자리를 위해 더없이 동정 어린 우울한 표정을 지어 두었다. 그다음에 샌더스 부인이 어린 바델을 데리고 나타났다. 제 아이를 보자 바델 부인은 흠칫 놀랐다. 갑자기 정신을 차린 그녀는 미친 듯이 아이에게 입을 맞췄다. 그러더니 다시 히스테릭한 얼빠진 상태로 빠져들어, 이 선량한 부인은 자기가 어디에 있느냐고 물었다. 이에 클러핀스 부인과 샌더스 부인은 고개를 돌리고 흐느꼈고, 도드슨 씨와 포그 씨는 원고에게 마음을 가라앉히라고 간청했다. 버즈퍼즈 변호사는 큼지막한 흰 손수건으로 눈을 몹시 비비며 배심원들을 향해 호소하는 눈길을 던졌고, 판사는 눈에 띄게 감동한 듯했으며, 여러 방청객이 기침으로 감정을 눌러 보려 애썼다.\n\n\"참으로 기막힌 착상이로군요.\" 퍼커가 픽윅 씨에게 속삭였다. \"대단한 자들입니다, 저 도드슨과 포그는. 효과를 내는 데 탁월한 발상을 지녔지요, 친애하는 선생. 탁월합니다.\"\n\n퍼커가 말하는 동안, 바델 부인은 천천히 조금씩 정신을 차리기 시작했고, 클러핀스 부인은 어린 바델의 단추들과 그 각각이 끼워질 단춧구멍을 꼼꼼히 살핀 뒤, 아이를 어머니 앞쪽 법정 바닥에 세워 놓았다—거기서라면 판사와 배심원 모두의 충분한 연민과 동정을 자아내지 않을 수 없는, 그야말로 좌중을 압도하는 위치였다. 이 일은 적잖은 반발과, 어린 신사 본인의 숱한 눈물 없이는 이루어지지 못했으니, 아이는 자기를 판사의 눈길이 환히 쏟아지는 곳에 세워 놓는 것이, 곧 즉결 처형이나, 적어도 평생토록 바다 너머로 유배되도록 명령받기 직전의 형식적인 서막에 불과하다는 어떤 내심의 불안을 품고 있었던 것이다.\n\n\"바델 대 픽윅.\" 검은 옷의 신사가, 명단 첫머리에 오른 사건을 호명하며 외쳤다.\n\n\"원고 측을 맡고 있습니다, 판사님.\" 버즈퍼즈 변호사가 말했다.\n\n\"누가 함께하시오, 버즈퍼즈 형제?\" 판사가 말했다. 스킴핀 씨가 자기라는 뜻으로 고개를 숙였다.\n\n\"피고 측을 맡고 있습니다, 판사님.\" 스너빈 변호사가 말했다.\n\n\"누가 함께하시오, 스너빈 형제?\" 법정이 물었다.\n\n\"펑키 씨입니다, 판사님.\" 스너빈 변호사가 대답했다.\n\n\"원고 측에 버즈퍼즈 변호사와 스킴핀 씨.\" 판사가 수첩에 이름을 적으면서, 쓰는 대로 읽으며 말했다. \"피고 측에 스너빈 변호사와 멍키 씨.\"\n\n\"송구하오나 판사님, 펑키입니다.\"\n\n\"오, 좋소.\" 판사가 말했다. \"전에는 그 신사의 이름을 들어 본 적이 없었구려.\" 이에 펑키 씨가 고개를 숙이며 미소를 지었고, 판사도 고개를 숙이며 미소를 지었으며, 그러고 나서 펑키 씨는 눈 흰자위까지 새빨개지면서, 모두가 자기를 쳐다보고 있다는 사실을 모르는 척하려 애썼다—이는 일찍이 그 누구도 성공한 적이 없었고, 합리적인 개연성으로 보아 앞으로도 결코 성공하지 못할 일이었다.\n\n\"진행하시오.\" 판사가 말했다.\n\n정리들이 다시 정숙을 외쳤고, 스킴핀 씨가 '사건 모두진술'에 들어갔다. 그런데 그가 모두진술을 마치고 보니 그 안에는 알맹이가 거의 없는 듯했으니, 그는 자기가 아는 자세한 내용을 전부 혼자만 간직한 채, 삼 분쯤 지나서 자리에 앉아 버렸고, 배심원들은 이전과 정확히 똑같은 정도의 지혜에 머문 채 남겨졌다. 그러자 버즈퍼즈 변호사가, 이 절차의 엄숙한 성격이 요구하는 온갖 위엄과 품위를 갖추어 일어섰다. 그는 도드슨에게 속삭이고 포그와 잠시 의논한 뒤, 가운을 어깨 위로 끌어당기고 가발을 바로잡고서 배심원들에게 변론을 시작했다.\n\n버즈퍼즈 변호사는 먼저, 자신의 직업 생활을 통틀어—법을 공부하고 실무에 임하기 시작한 바로 그 첫 순간 이래로—이토록 깊은 감회를 품고, 혹은 자신에게 지워진 책임에 대해 이토록 무거운 느낌을 안고 사건에 임한 적이 결코 없었다고 말했다. 그 책임이란, 만일 한 가지 확신—실로 적극적인 확실성에 이를 만큼 강한 확신, 곧 진실과 정의의 대의, 다시 말해 더없이 부당하게 상처 입고 더없이 억압받은 자신의 의뢰인의 대의가, 지금 자기 앞 저 배심석에서 보고 있는 고결하고 지성 있는 열두 명의 사람들 앞에서 반드시 승리하리라는 확신—에 의해 떠받쳐지고 지탱되지 않았더라면, 결코 감당할 수 없었을 책임이라고 그는 말했다.\n\n변호사들이 으레 이런 식으로 시작하는 까닭은, 그것이 배심원들을 더없이 흐뭇한 기분에 젖게 하여, 자기들이 얼마나 명민한 사람들인가 생각하게 만들기 때문이다. 그 효과는 즉시 눈에 띄게 나타났으니, 몇몇 배심원은 더없이 열심히 장황한 기록을 적기 시작했다.\n\n\"여러분은 저의 학식 있는 동료에게서 들으셨습니다.\" 버즈퍼즈 변호사가 말을 이었다. 사실 방금 언급한 그 학식 있는 동료에게서 배심원 여러분이 들은 것이라곤 아무것도 없다는 사실을 그는 익히 알고 있었다. \"여러분은 저의 학식 있는 동료에게서, 이것이 약혼 불이행에 관한 소송이며, 그 손해 배상액이 1,500파운드로 산정되어 있다는 것을 들으셨습니다. 그러나 여러분은 저의 학식 있는 동료에게서, 이 사건의 사실과 정황이 무엇인지는 듣지 못하셨으니, 그것을 말씀드리는 것은 저의 학식 있는 동료의 소관이 아니었기 때문입니다. 그 사실과 정황을, 여러분, 제가 상세히 진술해 드리고, 또 제가 저 증인석에 세울 흠잡을 데 없는 부인을 통해 입증해 보이겠습니다.\"\n\n여기서 버즈퍼즈 변호사는 '증인석'이라는 말에 엄청난 강세를 주며 책상을 힘껏 내리쳐 우렁찬 소리를 냈고, 도드슨과 포그를 흘끗 보았다. 두 사람은 변호사에 대한 감탄과 피고에 대한 분개 어린 적의를 담아 고개를 끄덕였다.\n\n\"원고는, 여러분,\" 버즈퍼즈 변호사가 부드럽고 우울한 목소리로 말을 이었다. \"원고는 미망인입니다. 그렇습니다, 여러분, 미망인이지요. 고인이 된 바델 씨는 여러 해 동안 국왕의 세입을 지키는 수호자의 한 사람으로서 군주의 존경과 신임을 누리다가, 거의 알아차릴 수 없을 만큼 조용히 이 세상을 빠져나가, 세관 따위는 결코 줄 수 없는 그 안식과 평화를 다른 곳에서 구하고자 떠났습니다.\"\n\n선술집 지하 저장고에서 일 쿼트들이 술병에 머리를 얻어맞아 죽은 바델 씨의 이 비통한 사망 묘사 대목에서, 학식 있는 변호사의 목소리는 떨렸고, 그는 북받치는 감정으로 말을 이었다—\n\n\"그는 죽기 얼마 전, 한 어린 사내아이에게 자신의 모습을 새겨 놓았습니다. 떠난 세무 관리가 남긴 유일한 정표인 이 어린 사내아이와 함께, 바델 부인은 세상으로부터 움츠러들어 가스웰 거리의 은거와 평온을 찾았습니다. 그리고 그녀는 이곳 앞쪽 거실 창문에 다음과 같은 문구가 적힌 벽보를 붙였습니다—'독신 신사를 위한 가구 딸린 셋방 있음. 안에서 문의 바람.'\" 여기서 버즈퍼즈 변호사는 잠시 멈추었고, 그동안 몇몇 배심원이 그 문서를 받아 적었다.\n\n\"거기엔 날짜가 없지요, 그렇지요?\" 한 배심원이 물었다.\n\n\"날짜는 없습니다, 여러분.\" 버즈퍼즈 변호사가 대답했다. \"그러나 제가 지시받은 바로는, 그것이 원고의 거실 창문에 붙은 것은 바로 삼 년 전 이맘때였습니다. 저는 배심원 여러분께서 이 문서의 문구에 주목해 주시기를 간청합니다—'독신 신사를 위한 가구 딸린 셋방 있음'! 바델 부인이 이성에 대해 가졌던 견해는, 여러분, 잃어버린 남편의 더없이 값진 품성을 오래 음미한 데서 비롯된 것입니다. 그녀에게는 두려움도, 불신도, 의심도 없었습니다. 모든 것이 신뢰요 의지였지요. '바델 씨는,' 미망인은 말했습니다—'바델 씨는 명예로운 사람이었고, 바델 씨는 약속을 지키는 사람이었으며, 바델 씨는 남을 속이는 사람이 아니었고, 바델 씨도 한때는 독신 신사였다. 나는 독신 신사들에게서 보호와 도움과 위안과 위로를 구하리라. 독신 신사들에게서 나는, 바델 씨가 나의 젊고 순진한 애정을 처음 얻었을 때 어떠했는지를 떠올리게 하는 무언가를 끊임없이 보게 되리라. 그러니 내 셋방은 독신 신사에게 세놓으리라.' 이 아름답고 가슴 뭉클한 충동에 이끌려(우리 불완전한 본성 가운데 가장 선한 충동의 하나이지요, 여러분), 외롭고 쓸쓸한 미망인은 눈물을 닦고, 이 층을 꾸미고, 죄 없는 아이를 모성의 가슴에 끌어안고서, 거실 창문에 그 벽보를 붙였습니다. 그것이 오래 거기 붙어 있었을까요? 아닙니다. 뱀이 노리고 있었고, 화약 도화선이 깔렸으며, 지뢰가 준비되고 있었고, 공병과 굴착병이 일에 착수해 있었습니다. 그 벽보가 거실 창문에 붙은 지 사흘도 채 되지 않아—사흘입니다, 여러분—두 다리로 곧추선, 괴물이 아니라 사람의 온갖 겉모습을 갖춘 한 존재가 바델 부인 집의 문을 두드렸습니다. 그는 안에서 문의했고—그 셋방을 빌렸습니다. 그리고 바로 그 이튿날 그곳을 차지하게 되었지요. 이 사내가 바로 픽윅—피고 픽윅이었습니다.\"\n\n버즈퍼즈 변호사는 어찌나 청산유수로 진행했던지 얼굴이 완전히 진홍빛이 되어, 여기서 숨을 돌리려 멈추었다. 그 정적이 스테어리 판사를 깨웠고, 판사는 즉시 잉크도 묻히지 않은 펜으로 뭔가를 적으며, 자기는 늘 눈을 감을 때 가장 깊이 생각한다는 믿음을 배심원들에게 심어 주려고 유난히 심오한 표정을 지었다. 버즈퍼즈 변호사가 말을 이었다—\n\n\"이 사내 픽윅에 관해서는 별로 말하지 않겠습니다. 이 주제에는 끌릴 만한 점이 거의 없으니까요. 그리고 저는, 여러분, 그리고 여러분도, 여러분, 혐오스러운 무정함과 체계적인 악행을 음미하며 즐길 그런 사람들이 아닙니다.\"\n\n여기서, 한동안 말없이 몸을 비비 꼬고 있던 픽윅 씨가 격렬하게 흠칫했으니, 마치 정의와 법의 위엄 어린 면전에서 버즈퍼즈 변호사에게 폭행을 가하겠다는 어떤 막연한 생각이 머릿속에 떠오른 듯했다. 퍼커의 만류하는 몸짓이 그를 제지했고, 그는 학식 있는 신사가 말을 잇는 것을, 클러핀스 부인과 샌더스 부인의 감탄 어린 얼굴과는 극명하게 대비되는 분개의 표정으로 듣고 있었다.\n\n\"체계적인 악행이라고 저는 말합니다, 여러분.\" 버즈퍼즈 변호사가 픽윅 씨를 꿰뚫어 보듯 하면서, 그를 향해 쏘아붙이며 말했다. \"그리고 체계적인 악행이라 말하는 김에, 피고 픽윅에게—제가 들은 바로는 그가 법정에 있다 하니—이 말을 해 두겠습니다. 그가 차라리 나오지 않았더라면 더 점잖고, 더 합당하고, 더 분별 있고, 더 품위 있는 처사였을 거라고요. 그리고 여러분께도 말씀드리건대, 그가 이 법정에서 부리는 그 어떤 부정이나 못마땅함의 몸짓도 여러분에게 통하지 않을 것이며, 여러분은 그것을 어떻게 평가하고 어떻게 헤아려야 하는지 아실 겁니다. 또한 그에게 더 말해 두겠습니다—판사님께서도 여러분께 말씀하시겠지만—변호사란, 의뢰인에 대한 자기 직무를 수행함에 있어, 위협받지도, 윽박질리지도, 짓눌리지도 않으며, 그 어느 하나라도, 또는 그중 처음 것이든 마지막 것이든 시도하려는 자에게는, 그가 원고이든 피고이든, 그 이름이 픽윅이든 노크스든 스토크스든 스타일스든 브라운이든 톰슨이든, 그 시도가 시도한 자의 머리 위로 되돌아오리라는 것을요.\"\n\n이렇게 본론에서 잠시 벗어난 것은 물론 의도한 대로 모든 눈을 픽윅 씨에게 돌리는 효과를 냈다. 버즈퍼즈 변호사는 스스로 채찍질해 몰아넣었던 도덕적 격앙 상태에서 어느 정도 회복하고 나서 다시 말을 이었다—\n\n\"여러분께 보여 드리겠습니다. 두 해 동안 픽윅이 끊임없이, 중단이나 단절 없이, 바델 부인의 집에 계속 거주했다는 것을요. 또한 그 기간 내내 바델 부인이 그의 시중을 들고, 그의 안락을 살피고, 그의 식사를 짓고, 빨랫감이 밖으로 나갈 때면 세탁부에게 보낼 그의 속옷을 챙겨 주고, 그것이 돌아오면 깁고 말려 입을 수 있도록 손질해 주었으며, 한마디로 그의 더없는 신뢰와 믿음을 누렸다는 것도 보여 드리겠습니다. 또한 그가 여러 차례 그녀의 어린 아들에게 반 페니씩을, 어떤 때는 심지어 육 펜스씩을 주었다는 것을 보여 드리겠습니다. 그리고 저의 학식 있는 동료가 도저히 약화시키거나 반박할 수 없는 증언을 지닌 한 증인을 통해, 그가 한번은 그 아이의 머리를 쓰다듬으며 요즘 '앨리 토어'나 '커머니'(둘 다 이 고장 청소년들이 무척 귀히 여기는 구슬의 특정 종류라고 들었습니다)를 좀 땄느냐고 묻고는, 다음과 같은 주목할 만한 표현을 썼다는 것을 입증해 보이겠습니다. '또 다른 아버지가 생기면 어떻겠니?' 또한 여러분께 입증해 보이겠습니다. 약 일 년 전, 픽윅이 마치 점차 제 의뢰인에게서 손을 끊으려는 의도로, 오랫동안 집을 비우기 시작했다는 것을요. 그러나 동시에 보여 드리겠습니다. 그 결심이 그때만 해도 충분히 강하지 못했거나, 혹은—그에게 그런 게 있다면—더 선한 감정이 이겼거나, 혹은 제 의뢰인의 매력과 재능이 그의 사내답지 못한 의도를 이겨 냈다는 것을, 다음 사실을 입증함으로써 보여 드리겠습니다. 한번은 그가 시골에서 돌아왔을 때, 그가 그녀에게 분명하게, 말로써 청혼했다는 것을요. 다만 그에 앞서, 그들의 엄숙한 약속에 증인이 없도록 각별히 신경을 썼습니다. 그리고 저는 그 자신의 친구 셋의 증언으로—더없이 마지못해 나선 증인들이지요, 여러분—더없이 마지못해 나선 증인들이지만—그날 아침 그가 원고를 두 팔에 안고서 애무와 다정한 말로 그녀의 동요를 달래고 있는 것을 그들이 발견했다는 것을 입증할 위치에 있습니다.\"\n\n학식 있는 변호사의 변론 가운데 이 대목에서 청중에게 눈에 띄는 인상이 새겨졌다. 그는 아주 작은 종잇조각 둘을 꺼내 들며 말을 이었다—\n\n\"자, 여러분, 이제 한마디만 더 하겠습니다. 이 두 사람 사이에 편지 두 통이 오갔으니, 피고의 필적임이 인정된 편지들로서, 실로 많은 것을 말해 줍니다. 더욱이 이 편지들은 그 사내의 성품을 드러냅니다. 이것들은 오로지 애정 어린 정의 언어만을 토로하는, 솔직하고 열렬하고 유려한 서한이 아닙니다. 은밀하고, 교활하고, 음흉한 통신이지요. 그러나 다행히도, 더없이 불타는 듯한 언어와 더없이 시적인 표현으로 적혀 있을 때보다 훨씬 더 결정적입니다—조심스럽고 의심 어린 눈으로 살펴야 할 편지들이요—픽윅이 당시에, 그것이 손에 들어갈 수 있는 제삼자라면 누구든 오도하고 현혹하려고 명백히 의도한 편지들입니다. 첫 번째 것을 읽어 보겠습니다. '개러웨이즈, 열두 시. 친애하는 비 부인—양갈비와 토마토소스. 그대의, 픽윅.' 여러분, 이게 무슨 뜻이겠습니까? 양갈비와 토마토소스. 그대의, 픽윅! 양갈비라니! 맙소사! 게다가 토마토소스라니! 여러분, 예민하고 사람을 믿는 한 여인의 행복이 이런 얄팍한 술책으로 농락당해도 좋단 말입니까? 다음 것에는 날짜가 전혀 없으니, 그 자체로 의심스럽습니다. '친애하는 비 부인, 내일까지는 집에 들어가지 못하겠소. 느림보 마차.' 그러고는 이 매우 주목할 만한 표현이 뒤따릅니다. '보온팬은 걱정하지 마시오.' 보온팬이라니! 아니, 여러분, 누가 보온팬 따위를 걱정한단 말입니까? 보온팬으로 인해 남자든 여자든 마음의 평화가 깨지거나 어지럽혀진 적이 언제 있었답니까? 보온팬은 그 자체로 무해하고 유용하며, 덧붙이자면, 여러분, 위안을 주는 가재도구가 아닙니까? 어째서 바델 부인은 이 보온팬에 대해 마음을 졸이지 말라고 그토록 간절히 당부받는 것일까요. 그것이(틀림없이 그러하듯) 숨겨진 불길을 가리는 한낱 위장에 불과한 것이 아니라면—픽윅이 작정한 배신을 염두에 두고 교묘히 꾸며 낸, 미리 짜인 통신 방식에 따라, 어떤 다정한 말이나 약속을 대신하는 한낱 대용물이 아니라면 말입니다. 그리고 그것이 무엇인지는 제가 설명할 처지가 못 됩니다. 그렇다면 이 '느림보 마차' 운운하는 것은 또 무슨 뜻이겠습니까? 제가 아는 한, 그것은 픽윅 자신을 가리키는 것일 수 있습니다. 그는 이 사건 전체를 통틀어 두말할 나위 없이 죄스럽도록 느려 터진 마차였으나, 이제 그 속도가 매우 뜻밖에도 빨라질 것이며, 그의 수레바퀴는, 여러분, 그가 호되게 깨닫게 되겠지만, 머지않아 여러분에 의해 기름칠을 받게 될 것입니다!\"\n\n버즈퍼즈 변호사는 여기서 잠시 멈춰, 배심원들이 자기 농담에 미소 짓는지 살펴보았다. 그러나 그것을 알아들은 자라곤 청과상뿐이었으니—그가 이 문제에 예민했던 것은 십중팔구 바로 그날 아침에 자기 손수레에 바로 그 작업을 했던 탓이리라—학식 있는 변호사는 변론을 마무리하기 전에 잠시 다시 침울한 기분에 빠져드는 것이 상책이라 여겼다.\n\n\"하지만 이 이야기는 이만하면 됐습니다, 여러분.\" 버즈퍼즈 변호사가 말했다. \"아픈 마음으로 미소 짓기란 어려운 일이지요. 우리의 가장 깊은 연민이 일깨워질 때 농담하기란 온당치 못한 일입니다. 제 의뢰인의 희망과 앞날은 망가졌으니, 그녀의 생업이 실로 사라졌다고 해도 그것은 결코 비유적인 표현이 아닙니다. 벽보는 내려졌으나—세입자는 없습니다. 적격의 독신 신사들이 오가지만—안에서든 밖에서든 문의하라는 권유는 없습니다. 집 안은 온통 음울함과 침묵뿐이고, 아이의 목소리마저 잠잠합니다. 어머니가 우는데 그 어린것의 놀이가 무슨 소용이겠습니까. 그의 '앨리 토어'도 '커머니'도 한결같이 내버려져 있습니다. 그는 오래 친숙했던 '엄지로 받쳐!'라는 외침마저 잊었고, 막대 치기 놀이나 홀짝 놀이에서도 솜씨가 무뎌졌습니다. 그러나 픽윅은, 여러분, 픽윅은, 가스웰 거리라는 사막의 이 가정이라는 오아시스를 무자비하게 파괴한 자—우물을 막아 버리고, 잔디밭에 재를 끼얹은 픽윅—그 무정한 토마토소스와 보온팬을 들고 오늘 여러분 앞에 나선 픽윅—그런 픽윅이 여전히 낯빛 하나 바꾸지 않는 뻔뻔함으로 고개를 쳐들고서, 자기가 저질러 놓은 폐허를 한숨 한 번 없이 바라보고 있습니다. 손해 배상이, 여러분—무거운 손해 배상이야말로 여러분이 그에게 내릴 수 있는 유일한 벌이요, 제 의뢰인에게 베풀 수 있는 유일한 보상입니다. 그리고 그 손해 배상을 위해 그녀는 이제, 계몽되고, 고결하고, 올바른 정서를 지니고, 양심적이고, 냉정하고, 동정심 있고, 사려 깊은, 문명화된 동포로 이루어진 배심원 여러분께 호소하는 바입니다.\" 이 아름다운 결론과 함께 버즈퍼즈 변호사가 자리에 앉았고, 스테어리 판사는 잠에서 깼다.\n\n\"엘리자베스 클러핀스를 부르시오.\" 버즈퍼즈 변호사가 잠시 후, 새로운 기운을 차려 일어서며 말했다.\n\n가장 가까이 있던 정리가 엘리자베스 터핀스를 불렀고, 조금 떨어진 또 다른 정리가 엘리자베스 줍킨스를 찾았으며, 세 번째 정리는 숨이 차도록 킹 거리로 뛰쳐나가 목이 쉬도록 엘리자베스 머핀스를 외쳐 댔다.\n\n그러는 사이 클러핀스 부인은 바델 부인, 샌더스 부인, 도드슨 씨, 포그 씨의 합심한 도움으로 증인석에 올려졌다. 그녀가 맨 윗단에 무사히 자리를 잡자, 바델 부인은 맨 아랫단에 서서, 한 손에는 손수건과 나막신을, 다른 손에는 사 분의 일 파인트쯤 들어갈 각성제 병을 들고 만일의 사태에 대비했다. 판사의 얼굴에 눈을 골똘히 고정한 샌더스 부인은 커다란 우산을 들고 바짝 곁에 자리 잡았으니, 오른손 엄지로 우산 용수철을 누른 채 진지한 표정을 짓고 있어, 마치 한순간이라도 즉시 우산을 펼칠 만반의 준비가 된 듯했다.\n\n\"클러핀스 부인,\" 버즈퍼즈 변호사가 말했다. \"부디 마음을 가라앉히십시오, 부인.\"\n물론 클러핀스 부인은 마음을 가라앉히라는 청을 받자마자 더욱 격하게 흐느꼈고, 곧 기절할 듯한—혹은 그녀가 나중에 표현한 대로, 감정이 북받쳐 주체할 수 없는—여러 가지 놀라운 징후를 보였다.\n\n\"기억하십니까, 클러핀스 부인?\" 버즈퍼즈 변호사가 몇 가지 사소한 질문을 던진 뒤에 말했다. \"지난 7월의 어느 날 아침, 바델 부인이 픽윅 씨의 방을 청소하고 있을 때, 부인께서 바델 부인 댁 뒤편 한 층 위 방에 계셨던 것을 기억하십니까?\"\n\n\"예, 판사님과 배심원 여러분, 기억합니다.\" 클러핀스 부인이 대답했다.\n\n\"픽윅 씨의 거실은 이 층 앞쪽이었지요, 제가 알기로는?\"\n\n\"예, 그랬지요, 선생님.\" 클러핀스 부인이 대답했다.\n\n\"부인은 뒷방에서 무엇을 하고 계셨소?\" 작은 판사가 물었다.\n\n\"판사님과 배심원 여러분,\" 클러핀스 부인이 흥미로운 동요를 보이며 말했다. \"여러분을 속이지 않겠습니다.\"\n\n\"그러지 않는 게 좋소, 부인.\" 작은 판사가 말했다.\n\n\"저는 거기 있었습니다,\" 클러핀스 부인이 말을 이었다. \"바델 부인 모르게요. 저는 작은 바구니를 들고 빨강강낭콩 감자 세 파운드를 사러 나갔었는데요, 여러분, 그게 세 파운드에 이 펜스 반이었지요. 그러다 바델 부인 댁 대문이 빠끔히 열려 있는 걸 봤지요.\"\n\n\"무엇 위에 열려 있다고요?\" 작은 판사가 외쳤다.\n\n\"조금 열려 있었다는 겁니다, 판사님.\" 스너빈 변호사가 말했다.\n\n\"부인은 빠끔히라고 했소만.\" 작은 판사가 영악한 표정으로 말했다.\n\n\"매한가지입니다, 판사님.\" 스너빈 변호사가 말했다. 작은 판사는 미심쩍은 표정을 짓더니, 그것을 기록해 두겠다고 말했다. 그러자 클러핀스 부인이 다시 말을 이었다—\n\n\"저는 그저 안녕하시냐고 인사나 하려고 들어갔지요, 여러분. 그러고는 두서없이 위층으로, 뒷방으로 올라갔습니다. 여러분, 앞방에서 말소리가 들렸는데요, 그러니까—\"\n\n\"그래서 부인은 엿들으셨겠지요, 그렇지요, 클러핀스 부인?\" 버즈퍼즈 변호사가 말했다.\n\n\"외람되지만, 선생님,\" 클러핀스 부인이 위엄 있는 태도로 대답했다. \"저는 그런 짓을 경멸합니다. 말소리가 아주 컸지요, 선생님. 제 귀에 절로 들이닥쳤어요.\"\n\n\"좋습니다, 클러핀스 부인. 부인은 엿들으신 게 아니라, 말소리를 들으신 거지요. 그 목소리 가운데 하나가 픽윅의 것이었습니까?\"\n\n\"예, 그랬지요, 선생님.\" 그리고 클러핀스 부인은 픽윅 씨가 바델 부인에게 말을 건넸다고 분명히 밝힌 뒤, 우리 독자들이 이미 알고 있는 그 대화를 천천히, 숱한 질문을 거쳐 가며 되풀이했다.\n\n배심원들은 미심쩍어하는 표정을 지었고, 버즈퍼즈 변호사는 자리에 앉으며 미소를 지었다. 스너빈 변호사가 이 증인을 반대 신문하지 않겠다는 뜻을 밝히자 배심원들의 표정은 그야말로 험악해졌으니, 픽윅 씨가 그녀의 진술이 본질적으로 정확하다고 말해 주는 것이 그녀에 대한 도리라는 점을 분명히 밝혀 두기를 원했기 때문이다.\n\n일단 말문을 튼 클러핀스 부인은 이때야말로 자기 집안 사정에 관한 짤막한 논설을 펼칠 절호의 기회라 여겼다. 그래서 그녀는 곧장 법정을 향해, 자기가 지금 말하는 이 순간에 여덟 아이의 어머니이며, 앞으로 여섯 달쯤 뒤 그 무렵에는 클러핀스 씨에게 아홉 번째 아이를 안겨 드릴 것을 확실히 기대하고 있노라고 알리기 시작했다. 바로 이 흥미로운 대목에서 작은 판사가 더없이 짜증스럽게 끼어들었고, 그 끼어듦의 결과로, 이 훌륭한 부인과 샌더스 부인 둘 다 더 이상의 실랑이 없이 잭슨 씨의 호위 아래 정중히 법정 밖으로 데려가졌다.\n\n\"너새니얼 윈클!\" 스킴핀 씨가 외쳤다.\n\n\"여기 있습니다!\" 가냘픈 목소리가 대답했다. 윈클 씨가 증인석에 들어서서 정식으로 선서를 마친 뒤, 자못 공손하게 판사에게 고개를 숙였다.\n\n\"날 보지 마시오, 선생.\" 판사가 그 인사에 대한 응답으로 날카롭게 말했다. \"배심원을 보시오.\"\n\n윈클 씨는 명령에 따라, 배심원이 있을 법한 자리라고 생각되는 곳을 바라보았다. 그가 처한 그 지적 혼란 상태로는 무엇이든 분간하는 것이 전혀 불가능했기 때문이다.\n\n이어서 윈클 씨는 스킴핀 씨의 신문을 받았다. 스킴핀 씨는 마흔두세 살가량의 장래가 촉망되는 젊은이로서, 물론 상대편에 유리하게 기울어 있기로 소문난 증인을 되도록 혼란에 빠뜨리고 싶어 안달이었다.\n\n\"자, 선생,\" 스킴핀 씨가 말했다. \"친절히 판사님께 선생의 이름이 무엇인지 알려 주시겠소?\" 그러고는 스킴핀 씨가 답을 매우 날카롭게 들으려는 듯 고개를 한쪽으로 기울였고, 그러는 동안 배심원들을 흘끗 보았으니, 마치 윈클 씨의 타고난 위증 취향이 그로 하여금 자기 것이 아닌 어떤 이름을 대게 하리라고 은근히 예상한다는 듯한 눈치였다.\n\n\"윈클입니다.\" 증인이 대답했다.\n\n\"세례명은 무엇이오, 선생?\" 작은 판사가 성을 내며 물었다.\n\n\"너새니얼입니다, 선생.\"\n\n\"대니얼—다른 이름은 또 없소?\"\n\n\"너새니얼입니다, 선생—아니, 판사님이라는 뜻입니다.\"\n\n\"너새니얼 대니얼이오, 아니면 대니얼 너새니얼이오?\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 판사님. 그냥 너새니얼입니다—대니얼은 전혀 아닙니다.\"\n\n\"그러면 어째서 나한테 대니얼이라고 했소, 선생?\" 판사가 물었다.\n\n\"그러지 않았습니다, 판사님.\" 윈클 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"했소, 선생.\" 판사가 매섭게 눈살을 찌푸리며 대답했다. \"선생이 그렇게 말하지 않았다면, 내가 어떻게 메모에 대니얼이라고 적었겠소, 선생?\"\n\n이 논리에는 물론 대꾸할 도리가 없었다.\n\n\"윈클 씨는 기억력이 좀 짧은 모양입니다, 판사님.\" 스킴핀 씨가 다시 한번 배심원들을 흘끗 보며 끼어들었다. \"이 증인을 마저 다루기 전에 그 기억을 되살릴 방도를 찾게 되겠지요, 아마도요.\"\n\n\"조심하는 게 좋을 거요, 선생.\" 작은 판사가 증인을 음흉하게 노려보며 말했다.\n\n가엾은 윈클 씨는 고개를 숙이며 태연한 척하려 애썼으나, 그가 처한 그 혼란 상태에서는 오히려 당황한 소매치기 같은 인상을 풍겼다.\n\n\"자, 윈클 씨,\" 스킴핀 씨가 말했다. \"부디 제 말에 주의를 기울이시오, 선생. 그리고 선생 자신을 위해, 조심하라는 판사님의 당부를 명심하시기를 권하는 바이오. 선생은 피고 픽윅 씨의 각별한 친구로 아는데, 그렇지 않소?\"\n\n\"제가 픽윅 씨를 알아 온 것은, 지금 이 순간 기억하기로는, 거의—\"\n\n\"제발, 윈클 씨, 질문을 회피하지 마시오. 선생은 피고의 각별한 친구요, 아니오?\"\n\n\"제가 막 말씀드리려던 건—\"\n\n\"제 질문에 답하시겠소, 안 하시겠소, 선생?\"\n\n\"질문에 답하지 않으면, 구금될 거요, 선생.\" 작은 판사가 자기 수첩 너머로 들여다보며 끼어들었다.\n\n\"자, 선생,\" 스킴핀 씨가 말했다. \"예 또는 아니오로, 부디.\"\n\n\"예, 그렇습니다.\" 윈클 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"예, 그렇다. 그러면 어째서 그 말을 진작 못 했소, 선생? 어쩌면 원고도 아시겠구려? 그렇지요, 윈클 씨?\"\n\n\"그분을 알지는 못합니다. 본 적은 있지요.\"\n\n\"오, 알지는 못하지만 본 적은 있다? 자, 친절히 배심원 여러분께 그게 무슨 뜻인지 말씀해 주시오, 윈클 씨.\"\n\n\"제 말은, 그분과 친밀한 사이는 아니지만, 가스웰 거리로 픽윅 씨를 찾아갔을 때 본 적이 있다는 겁니다.\"\n\n\"몇 번이나 보셨소, 선생?\"\n\n\"몇 번이냐고요?\"\n\n\"그렇소, 윈클 씨, 몇 번이오? 원하신다면 질문을 열두 번이라도 되풀이해 드리리다, 선생.\" 그러고는 학식 있는 신사가 단호하고 흔들림 없이 눈살을 찌푸린 채, 두 손을 허리에 얹고서 배심원들을 향해 미심쩍게 미소 지었다.\n\n이 질문을 두고, 이런 대목에서 으레 벌어지는 그 유익한 윽박지름이 시작되었다. 우선 윈클 씨는 바델 부인을 몇 번 보았는지 도저히 말할 수 없다고 했다. 그러자 스무 번은 보았느냐는 질문을 받았고, 이에 그는 \"물론이지요—그보다는 많습니다\"라고 답했다. 이어서 백 번은 보지 않았느냐—쉰 번 넘게 보았다고 맹세할 수 없느냐—적어도 일흔다섯 번은 보았다는 걸 알고 있지 않느냐는 등의 질문이 이어졌다. 마침내 도달한 흡족한 결론이란, 그가 몸조심을 하고 자기 처신에 유의하는 게 좋겠다는 것이었다. 이런 수단으로 증인이 필요한 만큼 신경이 곤두선 당혹 상태에 이르자, 신문은 다음과 같이 이어졌다—\n\n\"제발, 윈클 씨, 지난 7월의 어느 날 아침, 가스웰 거리에 있는 원고의 집 그 셋방으로 피고 픽윅을 찾아갔던 것을 기억하시오?\"\n\n\"예, 기억합니다.\"\n\n\"그때 텁먼이라는 이름의 친구와 스노드그래스라는 이름의 또 다른 친구가 동행했소?\"\n\n\"예, 그랬습니다.\"\n\n\"그들이 여기 있소?\"\n\n\"예, 있습니다.\" 윈클 씨가 자기 친구들이 자리한 곳을 자못 간절히 바라보며 대답했다.\n\n\"제발 제 말에 주의하시고, 친구분들은 신경 쓰지 마시오, 윈클 씨.\" 스킴핀 씨가 다시금 배심원들을 향해 의미심장한 눈길을 보내며 말했다. \"그들은, 아직 그런 일이 없었다면, 선생과 사전에 일절 상의하지 않고 자기 이야기를 해야 하니까요(다시 배심원들을 향한 눈길). 자, 선생, 배심원 여러분께 이 특정한 아침에 피고의 방에 들어섰을 때 무엇을 보았는지 말씀하시오. 자, 어서 털어놓으시오, 선생. 우린 늦든 빠르든 그것을 들어야 하오.\"\n\n\"피고 픽윅 씨가 원고를 두 팔로 안고서, 두 손으로 그녀의 허리를 감싸고 있었습니다.\" 윈클 씨가 자연스러운 망설임을 보이며 대답했다. \"그리고 원고는 기절한 듯 보였습니다.\"\n\n\"피고가 무슨 말을 하는 것을 들었소?\"\n\n\"그가 바델 부인을 착한 사람이라 부르는 것을 들었고, 누가 들어오기라도 하면 이게 무슨 꼴이냐며, 마음을 진정하라고 그녀에게 청하는 것을, 혹은 그 비슷한 말을 들었습니다.\"\n\n\"자, 윈클 씨, 이제 한 가지만 더 묻겠소. 부디 판사님의 주의를 명심하시오. 선생은 문제의 그때 피고 픽윅이—'친애하는 바델 부인, 당신은 착한 사람이오. 이 상황에 마음을 맞추시오, 당신은 이 상황에 이르게 될 테니'라거나, 그 비슷한 말을 하지 않았다고 맹세할 수 있겠소?\"\n\n\"저는—저는 분명히 그렇게 이해하진 않았습니다.\" 윈클 씨가, 자기가 들은 몇 마디 말을 이렇게 교묘하게 짜 맞춘 데에 어안이 벙벙해져서 말했다. \"저는 계단에 있어서 똑똑히 들을 수 없었습니다. 제 머릿속에 남은 인상으로는—\"\n\n\"배심원 여러분은 선생의 머릿속에 남은 인상 따위는 원치 않으십니다, 윈클 씨. 그런 건 정직하고 솔직한 사람들에게 별 도움이 안 될 것 같으니까요.\" 스킴핀 씨가 끼어들었다. \"선생은 계단에 있어서 똑똑히 듣지 못했다, 하지만 선생은 픽윅이 제가 인용한 그 표현을 쓰지 않았다고 맹세하지는 못하겠다? 제가 그렇게 이해하면 되겠소?\"\n\n\"아니요, 못 하겠습니다.\" 윈클 씨가 대답했다. 그러자 스킴핀 씨가 의기양양한 표정으로 자리에 앉았다.\n\n픽윅 씨의 사건은 여기까지 그다지 순조롭게 흘러오지 못한지라, 추가로 의심을 뒤집어쓸 여유 따위는 없었다. 그러나 가능하다면 좀 더 나은 쪽으로 비춰 볼 여지는 있었으므로, 펑키 씨가 반대 신문에서 윈클 씨에게 무언가 중요한 것을 끌어낼 요량으로 일어섰다. 그가 과연 무언가 중요한 것을 끌어냈는지는 곧 드러날 것이다.\n\n\"제가 알기로, 윈클 씨,\" 펑키 씨가 말했다. \"픽윅 씨는 젊은이가 아니지요?\"\n\n\"오, 아닙니다.\" 윈클 씨가 대답했다. \"제 아버지뻘은 되십니다.\"\n\n\"선생은 저의 학식 있는 동료에게, 픽윅 씨를 오래 알아 왔다고 말씀하셨소. 그가 결혼할 참이라고 추측하거나 믿을 만한 어떤 이유라도 있었던 적이 있소?\"\n\n\"오, 아닙니다. 결코 없었지요.\" 윈클 씨가 어찌나 열을 올려 대답했던지, 펑키 씨로서는 되도록 서둘러 그를 증인석에서 내보냈어야 마땅했다. 변호사들은 유독 나쁜 증인에 두 종류가 있다고 본다—마지못해 말하는 증인과 지나치게 잘 협조하는 증인이다. 그런데 윈클 씨는 그 두 가지 역할을 다 떠맡는 것이 운명이었다.\n\n\"이보다 더 나아가 보겠습니다, 윈클 씨.\" 펑키 씨가 더없이 부드럽고 흡족한 태도로 말을 이었다. \"선생은 픽윅 씨가 이성을 대하는 태도와 행동에서, 그가 근년 들어 어떤 경우에든 결혼을 염두에 두었다고 믿게 할 만한 무언가를 본 적이 있소?\"\n\n\"오, 아닙니다. 결코 없습니다.\" 윈클 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"여성이 관련된 자리에서 그의 행동은 늘, 인생의 꽤 지긋한 시기에 이르러 자기 일과 즐거움에 만족하며, 여성을 오직 아버지가 딸을 대하듯 대하는 사람의 그것이었소?\"\n\n\"조금도 의심할 여지가 없습니다.\" 윈클 씨가 진심을 다해 대답했다. \"그러니까—예—오, 예—물론입니다.\"\n\n\"선생은 그가 바델 부인이나 그 어떤 다른 여성을 대하는 행동에서, 조금이라도 의심스러운 점을 알아챈 적이 결코 없소?\" 펑키 씨가 자리에 앉으려 채비하며 말했다. 스너빈 변호사가 그에게 눈짓을 보내고 있었던 것이다.\n\n\"어—없—없습니다.\" 윈클 씨가 대답했다. \"다만 한 가지 사소한 경우가 있긴 한데요, 그건 틀림없이 쉽게 해명될 수 있을 겁니다.\"\n\n자, 만일 불운한 펑키 씨가 스너빈 변호사가 눈짓을 보냈을 때 자리에 앉았더라면, 혹은 버즈퍼즈 변호사가 이 변칙적인 반대 신문을 처음부터 막았더라면(그러나 그는 그러지 않을 만큼 영악했으니, 윈클 씨의 안달하는 모습을 보고서 그것이 십중팔구 자기에게 쓸모 있는 무언가로 이어지리라는 것을 익히 알았던 것이다), 이 불운한 시인은 끌려 나오지 않았을 것이다. 윈클 씨의 입에서 그 말이 떨어지는 순간, 펑키 씨는 자리에 앉았고, 스너빈 변호사는 다소 황급히 그에게 증인석에서 내려가도 좋다고 일렀다. 윈클 씨가 더없이 선뜻 그러려는 참에, 버즈퍼즈 변호사가 그를 멈춰 세웠다.\n\n\"잠깐, 윈클 씨, 잠깐!\" 버즈퍼즈 변호사가 말했다. \"판사님, 친절을 베푸시어, 자기 아버지뻘은 되는 이 신사가 여성을 대하는 의심스러운 행동의 그 한 가지 사례가 무엇이었는지 그에게 물어봐 주시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"학식 있는 변호사가 하는 말을 들으셨소, 선생.\" 판사가 비참하고 괴로워하는 윈클 씨를 돌아보며 말했다. \"선생이 말한 그 경우를 설명하시오.\"\n\n\"판사님,\" 윈클 씨가 불안에 떨며 말했다. \"저—저는 말하지 않는 편이 좋겠습니다.\"\n\n\"그렇겠지요.\" 작은 판사가 말했다. \"하지만 말해야 하오.\"\n\n법정 전체의 깊은 정적 속에서, 윈클 씨는 더듬더듬 털어놓았다. 그 사소한 의심의 정황이란, 픽윅 씨가 한밤중에 어느 부인의 침실에서 발견된 일이었으며, 그 일은 그가 알기로 문제의 그 부인이 예정했던 결혼이 깨지는 것으로 끝났고, 또한 그가 아는 바로는 일행 전체가 입스위치 자치구의 치안 판사이자 치안관인 조지 너프킨스 님 앞으로 강제로 끌려가는 결과로 이어졌다는 것이었다!\n\n\"증인석에서 내려가도 좋소, 선생.\" 스너빈 변호사가 말했다. 윈클 씨는 정말로 증인석에서 내려와, 정신없이 허둥지둥 조지 앤드 벌처로 달려갔으며, 몇 시간 뒤 웨이터에게 발견되었으니, 머리를 소파 쿠션 밑에 처박은 채 공허하고 음울한 소리로 끙끙대고 있었다.\n\n이어서 트레이시 텁먼과 오거스터스 스노드그래스가 차례로 증인석에 불려 나왔다. 둘 다 불운한 친구의 증언을 뒷받침했으며, 각자 지나친 윽박지름에 시달려 절망의 벼랑 끝으로 내몰렸다.\n\n다음으로 수재나 샌더스가 불려 나와, 버즈퍼즈 변호사의 신문을 받고 스너빈 변호사의 반대 신문을 받았다. 그녀는 픽윅이 바델 부인과 결혼하리라고 늘 말하고 또 믿어 왔다. 7월의 그 기절 사건 이후, 바델 부인이 픽윅과 약혼했다는 것이 동네에 떠도는 이야깃거리였음을 알고 있었다. 그것은 직접 다림질 일을 하는 머드베리 부인과 풀 먹이는 일을 하는 벙킨 부인에게서 들은 것이지만, 머드베리 부인도 벙킨 부인도 법정에는 보이지 않았다. 픽윅이 어린 아들에게 또 다른 아버지가 생기면 어떻겠느냐고 묻는 것을 들었다. 당시 바델 부인이 빵집 주인과 사귀고 있었는지는 몰랐으나, 그 빵집 주인이 당시에는 독신이었고 지금은 결혼했다는 것은 안다. 바델 부인이 그 빵집 주인을 무척 좋아하지 않았다고 단언할 수는 없으나, 빵집 주인은 바델 부인을 그리 좋아하지 않았으리라 생각하니, 그렇지 않았다면 다른 사람과 결혼하지 않았을 것이기 때문이다. 바델 부인이 7월의 그날 아침 기절한 것은 픽윅이 그녀에게 날을 잡으라고 청했기 때문이라 생각한다. 그녀(증인)도 샌더스 씨가 날을 잡으라고 청했을 때 죽은 듯이 기절했음을 알고 있으며, 자기를 숙녀라 일컫는 사람이라면 누구든 비슷한 상황에서 똑같이 할 것이라 믿는다. 픽윅이 아이에게 구슬에 관한 질문을 하는 것을 들었으나, 맹세코 '앨리 토어'와 '커머니'의 차이는 모른다.\n\n법정의 질문에 대하여—샌더스 씨와 사귀던 동안 다른 숙녀들처럼 연애편지를 받았다. 편지를 주고받는 동안 샌더스 씨는 그녀를 종종 '오리'라 불렀으나, '양갈비'라 부른 적은 없었고 '토마토소스'라 부른 적도 없었다. 그는 오리를 유난히 좋아했다. 어쩌면 그가 양갈비와 토마토소스를 그만큼 좋아했더라면, 애정의 표현으로 그녀를 그렇게 불렀을지도 모른다.\n\n이제 버즈퍼즈 변호사가, 가능하다면, 지금껏 보인 것보다 더 거드름을 피우며 일어서서 고함쳤다. \"새뮤얼 웰러를 부르시오.\"\n\n새뮤얼 웰러를 부를 필요는 전혀 없었다. 새뮤얼 웰러는 자기 이름이 불리는 순간 잽싸게 증인석에 들어섰으니, 모자를 바닥에 놓고 두 팔을 난간에 얹은 채, 유난히 쾌활하고 발랄한 표정으로 법조계를 한눈에 내려다보고 재판석을 두루 둘러보았던 것이다.\n\n\"이름이 무엇이오, 선생?\" 판사가 물었다.\n\n\"샘 웰러입니다, 판사님.\" 그 신사가 대답했다.\n\n\"철자를 'V'로 쓰오, 아니면 'W'로 쓰오?\" 판사가 물었다.\n\n\"그건 쓰는 사람의 취향과 기분에 달렸지요, 판사님.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"평생 한두 번밖에 그걸 써 볼 일이 없었습니다만, 저는 'V'로 씁니다.\"\n\n이때 방청석에서 한 목소리가 크게 외쳤다. \"옳소, 새미빌, 옳고말고. 'we'로 적으시오, 판사님, 'we'로 적으시오.\"\n\n\"누구냐, 감히 법정에 말을 거는 자가?\" 작은 판사가 고개를 들며 말했다. \"정리.\"\n\n\"예, 판사님.\"\n\n\"저 사람을 당장 이리 데려오너라.\"\n\n\"예, 판사님.\"\n\n그러나 정리가 그 사람을 찾지 못했으니, 데려오지도 못했다. 한바탕 소동이 인 끝에, 그 죄인을 찾으려고 일어섰던 사람들이 모두 도로 자리에 앉았다. 작은 판사는 분이 가라앉아 말을 할 수 있게 되자마자 증인을 돌아보며 말했다—\n\n\"저게 누구인지 아오, 선생?\"\n\n\"제 아버지가 아닌가 싶습니다만, 판사님.\" 샘이 대답했다.\n\n\"지금 여기 보이오?\" 판사가 말했다.\n\n\"아니요, 안 보입니다, 판사님.\" 샘이 법정 지붕의 채광창을 똑바로 올려다보며 대답했다.\n\n\"그를 가리킬 수 있었다면, 당장 구금했을 것이오.\" 판사가 말했다. 샘은 감사의 뜻으로 고개를 숙이고는, 변함없이 쾌활한 표정으로 버즈퍼즈 변호사를 향해 돌아섰다.\n\n\"자, 웰러 씨.\" 버즈퍼즈 변호사가 말했다.\n\n\"자, 선생.\" 샘이 대답했다.\n\n\"내가 알기로 선생은 이 사건의 피고 픽윅 씨를 모시고 있지요? 크게 말씀하시오, 부디, 웰러 씨.\"\n\n\"크게 말할 작정입니다, 선생.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"저는 그 양반을 모시고 있고요, 아주 좋은 일자리지요.\"\n\n\"할 일은 적고 받는 건 많겠구려?\" 버즈퍼즈 변호사가 농담조로 말했다.\n\n\"오, 받는 건 넉넉합니다요, 선생. 그 병사가 채찍 삼백쉰 대를 맞으라는 명령을 받았을 때 한 말마따나요.\" 샘이 대답했다.\n\n\"그 병사가, 혹은 다른 누가 무슨 말을 했는지 우리한테 말해선 안 되오, 선생.\" 판사가 끼어들었다. \"그건 증거가 아니오.\"\n\n\"잘 알겠습니다, 판사님.\" 샘이 대답했다.\n\n\"선생이 피고에게 처음 고용된 날 아침에 무언가 특별한 일이 있었던 걸 기억하오, 응, 웰러 씨?\" 버즈퍼즈 변호사가 말했다.\n\n\"예, 기억합니다, 선생.\" 샘이 대답했다.\n\n\"친절히 그게 무엇이었는지 배심원에게 말씀하시오.\"\n\n\"그날 아침 제가 옷을 한 벌 새로 쫙 빼입었지요, 배심원 여러분.\" 샘이 말했다. \"그건 그 시절 저한테 아주 특별하고 흔치 않은 일이었습니다요.\"\n\n이에 좌중에 한바탕 웃음이 일었고, 작은 판사가 성난 표정으로 책상 너머를 노려보며 말했다. \"조심하는 게 좋을 거요, 선생.\"\n\n\"그때 픽윅 씨도 그렇게 말씀하셨습니다요, 판사님.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"그래서 저는 그 옷을 아주 조심해서 다뤘지요. 정말 아주 조심했습니다요, 판사님.\"\n\n판사는 족히 이 분 동안 샘을 엄하게 노려보았으나, 샘의 표정이 어찌나 완벽하게 차분하고 평온했던지, 판사는 아무 말도 못 하고 버즈퍼즈 변호사에게 진행하라고 손짓했다.\n\n\"내게 말하려는 거요, 웰러 씨?\" 버즈퍼즈 변호사가 두 팔을 단호하게 팔짱 끼고 반쯤 배심원 쪽으로 돌아서며 말했으니, 마치 자기가 이 증인을 기어이 곤란하게 만들겠다고 말없이 다짐하는 듯했다. \"내게 말하려는 거요, 웰러 씨, 선생이 증인들의 입을 통해 묘사된 것을 들은 그 일, 곧 원고가 피고의 품에서 기절한 그 일을 선생은 전혀 보지 못했단 말이오?\"\n\n\"못 봤고말고요.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"저는 불려 올라갈 때까지 복도에 있었고, 그러고 나니 그 노부인은 거기 안 계셨지요.\"\n\n\"자, 주목하시오, 웰러 씨.\" 버즈퍼즈 변호사가 샘의 답변을 받아 적는 시늉을 해 그를 겁주려고, 앞에 놓인 잉크병에 큼지막한 펜을 적시며 말했다. \"선생은 복도에 있었으면서도 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 전혀 보지 못했다. 선생, 눈은 두 개 달려 있소, 웰러 씨?\"\n\n\"예, 눈은 두 개 달려 있습죠.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"바로 그게 문제지요. 그게 만일 특별히 성능 좋은, 특허받은 이백만 배 확대 가스 현미경 한 쌍이었다면야, 어쩌면 계단 한 층과 두꺼운 널문도 꿰뚫어 볼 수 있었을지도 모릅니다요. 하지만 그저 눈일 뿐이니, 아시다시피 제 시야엔 한계가 있는 거지요.\"\n\n조금도 짜증 내는 기색 없이, 더없이 천연덕스럽고 평온한 태도로 던진 이 답변에, 방청객들은 킥킥거렸고, 작은 판사는 미소를 지었으며, 버즈퍼즈 변호사는 유난히 멍청해 보였다. 도드슨 및 포그와 잠시 의논한 뒤, 학식 있는 변호사는 다시 샘을 향해 돌아서서, 분함을 감추려 애쓰는 괴로운 표정으로 말했다. \"자, 웰러 씨, 괜찮다면 다른 점에 관해 한 가지 묻겠소.\"\n\n\"좋습니다요, 선생.\" 샘이 더없이 좋은 기분으로 받았다.\n\n\"지난 11월의 어느 밤, 바델 부인 댁에 올라갔던 것을 기억하오?\"\n\n\"오, 예, 아주 잘 기억합니다.\"\n\n\"오, 그건 기억하는구려, 웰러 씨.\" 버즈퍼즈 변호사가 기운을 차리며 말했다. \"마침내 뭔가 건질 게 있겠다 싶었소.\"\n\n\"저도 그럴 줄 알았습니다요, 선생.\" 샘이 대답했다. 이에 방청객들이 또 킥킥거렸다.\n\n\"자, 내 생각에 선생은 이 재판에 관해 잠깐 이야기를 나누려고 올라간 거겠지, 응, 웰러 씨?\" 버즈퍼즈 변호사가 배심원들을 의미심장하게 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"집세를 내러 올라갔지요. 하지만 재판에 관해 얘기를 나누긴 했습니다요.\" 샘이 대답했다.\n\n\"오, 재판에 관해 이야기를 나눴구려.\" 버즈퍼즈 변호사가 무언가 중대한 발견을 기대하며 기색이 밝아져 말했다. \"자, 재판에 관해 무슨 말이 오갔소? 친절히 우리에게 말씀해 주시겠소, 웰러 씨?\"\n\n\"기꺼이 그러지요, 선생.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"오늘 여기서 신문받은 그 정숙한 두 부인이 대수롭잖은 얘기를 몇 마디 한 뒤에, 그 부인들이 도드슨 씨하고 포그 씨의—지금 선생 곁에 앉아 계신 저 두 양반의—훌륭한 처사에 아주 크게 감탄하는 상태에 빠졌습죠.\" 이 말은 물론 좌중의 시선을 도드슨 및 포그에게로 끌어모았고, 두 사람은 더없이 고결해 보이는 표정을 지었다.\n\n\"원고 측 사무 변호사들 말이오.\" 버즈퍼즈 변호사가 말했다. \"좋소! 그들이 원고 측 사무 변호사인 도드슨 및 포그 씨의 훌륭한 처사를 높이 칭찬했다는 거지요?\"\n\n\"예.\" 샘이 말했다. \"그 두 양반이 이 사건을 성공 보수로만 맡고, 픽윅 씨한테서 받아 내지 못하면 소송 비용을 한 푼도 안 받기로 한 게 참 너그러운 일이라고들 했습죠.\"\n\n이 더없이 뜻밖의 대답에 방청객들이 또 킥킥거렸고, 도드슨과 포그는 얼굴이 새빨개져서 버즈퍼즈 변호사에게 몸을 기울이고는 황급히 그의 귀에 무언가를 속삭였다.\n\n\"지당한 말씀이오.\" 버즈퍼즈 변호사가 짐짓 침착한 척하며 큰 소리로 말했다. \"판사님, 이 증인의 뚫을 수 없는 우둔함을 통해 무슨 증거를 끌어내려 애쓰는 것은 완전히 헛수고입니다. 더는 그에게 질문을 하여 법정을 번거롭게 하지 않겠습니다. 내려가시오, 선생.\"\n\n\"또 다른 어느 양반이 저한테 뭔가 묻고 싶으십니까?\" 샘이 모자를 집어 들며 더없이 느긋하게 둘러보며 물었다.\n\n\"난 됐소, 웰러 씨, 고맙소.\" 스너빈 변호사가 웃으며 말했다.\n\n\"내려가도 좋소, 선생.\" 버즈퍼즈 변호사가 짜증스레 손을 내저으며 말했다. 샘은 그에 따라 내려갔으니, 도드슨 및 포그 씨의 소송에 되도록 해를 끼치고, 픽윅 씨에 관해서는 되도록 말을 아낀 뒤였다. 그것이야말로 처음부터 그가 노린 바로 그 목적이었다.\n\n\"인정하는 데 이의가 없습니다, 판사님.\" 스너빈 변호사가 말했다. \"또 다른 증인의 신문을 덜 수 있다면 말입니다만, 픽윅 씨가 사업에서 은퇴했으며, 상당한 독립적 자산을 지닌 신사라는 점 말입니다.\"\n\n\"좋소.\" 버즈퍼즈 변호사가 낭독될 두 통의 편지를 제출하며 말했다. \"그러면 이것으로 저의 입증을 마칩니다, 판사님.\"\n\n이어서 스너빈 변호사가 피고를 위해 배심원들에게 변론했으니, 더없이 길고 더없이 힘주어 변론을 펼치며, 그 안에서 픽윅 씨의 행실과 인품에 더없이 높은 찬사를 바쳤다. 그러나 우리 독자들은 그 신사의 장점과 됨됨이를 스너빈 변호사가 도저히 할 수 있는 것보다 훨씬 더 정확히 가늠할 수 있는 처지이므로, 우리는 그 학식 있는 신사의 변론을 길게 늘어놓을 필요를 느끼지 못한다. 그는 제출된 편지들이 한낱 픽윅 씨의 식사에 관한 것이거나, 그가 어느 시골 나들이에서 돌아올 때 그의 방에서 맞이할 준비에 관한 것에 불과하다는 것을 보여 주려 애썼다. 대략적으로 덧붙이자면, 그는 픽윅 씨를 위해 할 수 있는 최선을 다했다고 하는 것으로 충분하다. 그리고 그 최선이란, 누구나 알다시피 그 옛 격언의 틀림없는 권위에 따르면, 그 이상은 할 수 없는 법이다.\n\n스테어리 판사는 오랜 관례에 따른, 더없이 인정받는 방식으로 사건을 정리했다. 그는 그토록 짧은 시간에 해독할 수 있는 만큼의 자기 메모를 배심원들에게 읽어 주었고, 읽어 나가면서 증거에 즉석 논평을 곁들였다. 만일 바델 부인이 옳다면 픽윅 씨가 그르다는 것이 더없이 분명하며, 만일 그들이 클러핀스 부인의 증언을 믿을 만하다고 여긴다면 그것을 믿을 것이요, 그렇지 않다면야 믿지 않을 것이다. 만일 그들이 약혼 불이행이 저질러졌다고 납득한다면, 그들은 적절하다고 여기는 손해 배상과 함께 원고에게 유리한 평결을 내릴 것이요, 반면에 결혼 약속이 애초에 전혀 주어진 적이 없다고 그들에게 여겨진다면, 손해 배상은 일절 없이 피고에게 유리한 평결을 내릴 것이다. 그런 다음 배심원들은 그 문제를 의논하려고 자기들의 밀실로 물러갔고, 판사는 양갈비 한 점과 셰리주 한 잔으로 원기를 돋우려고 자기 밀실로 물러갔다.\n\n애가 타는 십오 분이 흘렀다. 배심원들이 돌아왔고, 판사가 불려 들어왔다. 픽윅 씨는 안경을 쓰고, 동요하는 표정과 빠르게 뛰는 가슴으로 배심원장을 바라보았다.\n\n\"여러분,\" 검은 옷의 인물이 말했다. \"평결에 모두 합의하셨습니까?\"\n\n\"합의했습니다.\" 배심원장이 대답했다.\n\n\"원고에게 유리한 평결입니까, 여러분, 아니면 피고에게 유리한 평결입니까?\"\n\n\"원고에게 유리한 평결입니다.\"\n\n\"손해 배상액은 얼마입니까, 여러분?\"\n\n\"칠백오십 파운드입니다.\"\n\n픽윅 씨는 안경을 벗어, 알을 정성껏 닦고, 그것을 안경집에 접어 넣어 주머니에 넣었다. 그러고는,\n\n장갑을 대단히 정성스럽게 끼고서 그동안 내내 배심장을 노려보던 그는, 기계적으로 퍼커 씨와 푸른 가방을 따라 법정을 나섰다.\n\n퍼커가 법정 수수료를 치르는 동안 그들은 곁방에 들렀고, 여기서 픽윅 씨는 친구들과 합류했다. 그리고 여기서 그는 도드슨과 포그 두 사람과도 마주쳤는데, 그들은 겉으로 드러나는 온갖 만족의 기색을 띠며 두 손을 비비고 있었다.\n\n\"그래, 여러분.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그래요, 선생.\" 도드슨이 자신과 동업자를 대표해서 말했다.\n\n\"당신들 소송 비용을 받아 낼 거라고 생각하나 본데, 그렇소, 여러분?\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n포그는 그렇게 될 가능성이 꽤 높다고 생각한다고 말했다. 도드슨은 미소를 지으며 한번 해 보겠다고 말했다.\n\n\"해 보고, 또 해 보고, 또다시 해 보시오, 도드슨, 포그 두 분.\" 픽윅 씨가 격렬하게 말했다. \"하지만 내가 남은 평생을 채무자 감옥에서 보낸다 할지라도, 당신들은 나한테서 비용이든 손해배상이든 단 한 푼도 받아 내지 못할 거요.\"\n\n\"하! 하!\" 도드슨이 웃었다. \"다음 개정기 전에 생각이 달라지실 겁니다, 픽윅 씨.\"\n\n\"헤, 헤, 헤! 곧 알게 되겠지요, 픽윅 씨.\" 포그가 이를 드러내며 히죽거렸다.\n\n분노로 말문이 막힌 픽윅 씨는 변호사와 친구들에게 이끌려 문간까지 갔고, 거기서 늘 빈틈없이 살피던 샘 웰러가 미리 불러다 놓은 삯마차에 오르는 것을 부축받았다.\n\n샘은 발판을 올려놓고 마부석으로 뛰어오를 채비를 하던 차에, 누군가 어깨를 살며시 건드리는 것을 느꼈다. 돌아보니 그의 아버지가 앞에 서 있었다. 노신사의 얼굴에는 애처로운 표정이 어려 있었고, 그는 엄숙하게 고개를 가로저으며 타이르는 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"이런 식으로 일을 처리하면 이렇게 될 줄 내 진작 알았다. 아이고, 새미야, 새미야, 어째서 알리바이가 없었더란 말이냐!\"\n\n\"하지만 분명히, 선생님,\" 재판 다음 날 아침 픽윅 씨의 방에 서 있던 작은 퍼커가 말했다. \"분명히 정말로 그러시는 건 아니겠지요—이제 진심으로, 진지하게, 흥분은 빼고 말입니다만—이 소송 비용과 손해배상금을 안 내시겠다는 말씀은 정말 아니시겠지요?\"\n\n\"반 페니도 못 낸다네.\" 픽윅 씨가 단호히 말했다. \"반 페니도 못 내.\"\n\n\"원칙을 위하여 만세, 라고 어떤 고리대금업자가 어음을 갱신해 주지 않을 때 말했다죠.\" 아침 식기를 치우던 웰러 씨가 한마디 했다.\n\n\"샘,\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"수고스럽지만 아래층으로 좀 내려가 주게.\"\n\n\"알겠습니다요, 나리.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다. 그리고 픽윅 씨의 부드러운 암시를 받아들여 샘은 물러갔다.\n\n\"안 되네, 퍼커.\" 픽윅 씨가 매우 진지한 태도로 말했다. \"여기 내 친구들이 이 결심을 단념시키려 애썼지만 헛수고였네. 나는 상대편이 나에게 법적 집행 절차를 발부할 권한을 가질 때까지 평소대로 지내겠네. 그리고 만약 그들이 그 권한을 이용해서 내 몸을 잡아 가둘 만큼 비열하다면, 나는 더없이 명랑하고 흡족한 마음으로 순순히 응하겠네. 그자들이 언제 그렇게 할 수 있나?\"\n\n\"손해배상금과 산정된 소송 비용에 대해서는 다음 개정기에 집행을 발부할 수 있습니다, 선생님.\" 퍼커가 대답했다. \"지금부터 꼭 두 달 뒤지요, 선생님.\"\n\n\"아주 좋네.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"그때까지는, 이 사람아, 이 문제에 대해서 더는 듣고 싶지 않네. 자 그럼,\" 픽윅 씨는 어떤 안경으로도 흐리거나 가릴 수 없는 눈빛의 반짝임과 더불어 유쾌한 미소를 띠고 친구들을 둘러보며 말을 이었다. \"이제 유일한 문제는, 다음엔 어디로 갈까 하는 거네.\"\n\n터프먼 씨와 스노드그래스 씨는 친구의 영웅적 태도에 너무 감동한 나머지 아무 대답도 내놓지 못했다. 윈클 씨는 재판에서 자기가 한 증언의 기억에서 아직 충분히 회복되지 못해 어떤 주제에 대해서도 한마디 할 처지가 못 되었다. 그래서 픽윅 씨는 헛되이 잠시 기다렸다.\n\n\"자,\" 그 신사가 말했다. \"행선지를 정하는 일을 나에게 맡긴다면, 나는 배스라고 말하겠네. 우리 중 아무도 거기 가 본 적이 없는 것 같으니 말일세.\"\n\n아무도 없었다. 그리고 그 제안은 퍼커가 따뜻이 거들었으니, 그는 픽윅 씨가 약간의 변화와 흥겨움을 맛본다면 자기 결심에 대해서는 다시 생각해 보고 채무자 감옥에 대해서는 더 나쁘게 여기게 될 가능성이 매우 크다고 보았던 것이다. 그리하여 만장일치로 가결되었고, 샘은 즉시 화이트 호스 셀러로 보내져 이튿날 아침 일곱 시 반 마차의 다섯 자리를 예약하게 되었다.\n\n마침 안쪽에 두 자리, 바깥쪽에 세 자리가 있었다. 그래서 샘 웰러는 그것을 모두 예약하고, '거스름돈'의 일부랍시고 건네받은 백랍으로 된 가짜 반 크라운 동전을 두고 예약 사무소 직원과 몇 마디 인사치레를 주고받은 뒤, 조지 앤드 벌처로 걸어 돌아왔다. 거기서 그는 잠자리에 들 때까지 꽤 분주히 일했으니, 옷가지와 속옷을 가능한 한 작게 줄여 넣고, 자물쇠도 경첩도 없는 상자의 뚜껑을 닫아 두기 위한 갖가지 기발한 장치를 고안하는 데 그의 기계적 천재성을 발휘했다.\n\n이튿날 아침은 여행하기에 매우 좋지 못한 날씨였다—후텁지근하고, 축축하고, 가랑비가 내렸다. 도시를 지나 나가는 역마차의 말들은 어찌나 김을 뿜어 대는지 바깥쪽 승객들이 보이지 않을 정도였다. 신문팔이들은 눅눅해 보였고 곰팡내가 났다. 오렌지 장수들이 마차 창문으로 머리를 들이밀 때 그들의 모자에서 빗물이 흘러내려 상쾌하게도 안쪽 승객들을 적셨다. 칼날이 쉰 개나 달린 주머니칼을 파는 유대인들은 절망 속에 그것을 접어 넣었고, 수첩을 파는 사내들은 자기네 자신이 수첩 꼴이 되었다.* 시곗줄과 토스트용 포크는 똑같이 헐값이었고, 연필집과 스펀지는 시장에서 거저나 다름없었다.\n\n* 수첩 꼴: 비에 흠뻑 젖어 후줄근해진 모습을 빗댄 말장난.\n마차가 멈추는 순간 거칠게 달려들어 짐에 매달리는 일고여덟 명의 짐꾼들로부터 짐을 구해 내는 일은 샘 웰러에게 맡기고, 자기들이 한 이십 분쯤 너무 일찍 왔다는 것을 안 픽윅 씨와 친구들은 비를 피하러 여행객 대기실로 들어갔다—인간의 낙심이 마지막으로 기댈 곳이었다.\n\n화이트 호스 셀러의 여행객 대기실은 물론 불편하다. 불편하지 않다면 여행객 대기실이랄 수도 없을 것이다. 그곳은 오른편 응접실인데, 야심만만한 부엌 벽난로가 반항적인 부지깽이며 부젓가락이며 부삽을 거느리고 거기로 걸어 들어온 듯한 모양새였다. 그 방은 여행객들의 독방 감금을 위해 칸막이로 나뉘어 있었고, 시계 하나와 거울 하나, 그리고 살아 있는 웨이터 하나가 비치되어 있었다. 마지막 물건은 방 한구석의 유리잔 닦는 작은 개집 같은 데에 보관되어 있었다.\n\n이 칸막이 자리들 가운데 하나를 이날따라 마흔다섯 살가량 된, 눈매가 험상궂은 사내가 차지하고 있었다. 그는 대머리에 반들거리는 이마를 가졌으며, 머리 양옆과 뒤쪽에는 검은 머리카락이 꽤 많고, 크고 검은 구레나룻을 길렀다. 그는 갈색 외투를 턱까지 단추로 채워 입었고, 옆자리에는 큼직한 물범 가죽 여행 모자와 두꺼운 외투와 망토가 놓여 있었다. 그는 픽윅 씨가 들어서자 아침 식사에서 눈을 들어, 매우 위엄 있어 보이는 사납고 단호한 태도로 쳐다보았다. 그리고 그 신사와 그 일행을 흡족할 때까지 샅샅이 뜯어본 다음, 누군가 자기를 이용해 먹으려는 게 아닐까 의심스럽지만 그렇게는 안 될 거라고 말하는 듯한 투로 콧노래를 흥얼거렸다.\n\n\"웨이터.\" 구레나룻 신사가 말했다.\n\n\"예, 손님?\" 더러운 안색에다 같은 색깔의 수건을 든 사내가 앞서 말한 개집에서 나오며 대답했다.\n\n\"토스트 좀 더.\"\n\n\"예, 손님.\"\n\n\"버터 바른 토스트로, 알겠나.\" 신사가 사납게 말했다.\n\n\"곧 가져오겠습니다, 손님.\" 웨이터가 대답했다.\n\n구레나룻 신사는 아까와 똑같은 식으로 콧노래를 흥얼거리고는, 토스트가 오기를 기다리는 동안 난롯불 앞으로 나아가 외투 자락을 팔 밑에 끼고서 자기 부츠를 내려다보며 생각에 잠겼다.\n\n\"이 마차가 배스 어디쯤에 서는지 궁금하군.\" 픽윅 씨가 윈클 씨에게 부드럽게 말을 건넸다.\n\n\"흠—어—그게 무슨 말이오?\" 낯선 사내가 말했다.\n\n\"제 친구에게 한마디 했을 뿐입니다, 선생님.\" 언제나 대화에 끼어들 준비가 되어 있는 픽윅 씨가 대답했다. \"배스행 마차가 어느 여관에 서는지 궁금해서요. 혹시 알려 주실 수 있을까 하고요.\"\n\n\"배스에 가시오?\" 낯선 사내가 말했다.\n\n\"갑니다, 선생님.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"저 다른 신사분들도?\"\n\n\"그들도 갑니다.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"안쪽은 아니겠지—당신들이 안쪽에 탄다면 내 목을 걸겠소.\" 낯선 사내가 말했다.\n\n\"우리 모두가 타는 건 아닙니다.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇지, 당신들 모두는 아니지.\" 낯선 사내가 힘주어 말했다. \"내가 두 자리를 잡아 놨소. 만약 네 명밖에 안 들어가는 그 빌어먹을 칸에 여섯을 욱여넣으려 든다면, 나는 역마차를 따로 빌리고 소송을 걸겠소. 나는 내 차비를 다 냈소. 그렇게는 안 되지. 자리를 잡을 때 직원에게 그렇게는 안 된다고 말했소. 이런 일이 벌어진다는 걸 나도 아오. 매일같이 벌어진다는 걸 알지. 하지만 나는 한 번도 당한 적이 없고, 앞으로도 당하지 않을 거요. 나를 가장 잘 아는 자들이 그걸 가장 잘 알지. 빌어먹을!\" 여기서 그 사나운 신사는 종을 몹시 거칠게 울려 대고는, 웨이터에게 오 초 안에 토스트를 가져오는 게 좋을 거라고, 안 그러면 그 이유를 알게 될 거라고 말했다.\n\n\"이보세요, 선생님.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"한 말씀 드리는 걸 허락해 주신다면, 이건 아주 불필요한 흥분의 표출입니다. 저는 안쪽에 두 자리만 잡았으니까요.\"\n\n\"그 말을 들으니 반갑소.\" 사나운 사내가 말했다. \"내 말을 거두겠소. 사과를 드리지요. 여기 내 명함이오. 나와 친분을 맺어 주시오.\"\n\n\"기꺼이 그러지요, 선생님.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다. \"우리는 같이 여행하게 될 처지이니, 서로의 동행이 피차 즐거운 것이 되기를 바랍니다.\"\n\n\"그러기를 바라오.\" 사나운 신사가 말했다. \"그렇게 될 줄 아오. 당신 인상이 마음에 드오. 흡족하오. 신사 여러분, 손과 이름을 주시오. 나를 알아 두시오.\"\n\n물론 이 정중한 말에 뒤이어 우호적인 인사가 오갔다. 그리고 그 사나운 신사는 곧바로, 똑같이 짧고 퉁명스럽고 끊어지는 듯한 문장으로, 자기 이름은 다울러이며, 즐기러 배스에 가는 길이고, 예전에는 군에 있었으며, 지금은 신사로서 사업을 차렸고, 그 이익으로 먹고살며, 두 번째 자리를 잡은 그 인물은 다름 아닌 자기 아내 다울러 부인이라는 저명한 분이라고 친구들에게 알렸다.\n\n\"훌륭한 여자요.\" 다울러 씨가 말했다. \"나는 그녀가 자랑스럽소. 그럴 만한 이유가 있지.\"\n\n\"제가 직접 판단하는 기쁨을 누리게 되기를 바랍니다.\" 픽윅 씨가 미소를 띠고 말했다.\n\n\"그러실 거요.\" 다울러가 대답했다. \"그녀가 당신을 알게 될 거요. 당신을 존중하게 될 거요. 나는 별난 상황에서 그녀에게 구애했소. 무모한 맹세로 그녀를 얻었지. 이렇게 말이오. 나는 그녀를 보았소. 나는 그녀를 사랑했소. 나는 청혼했소. 그녀는 나를 거절했소.—'다른 사람을 사랑하시오?'—'제 얼굴 붉히는 일을 면하게 해 주세요.'—'그자를 내가 아오.'—'아시는군요.'—'아주 좋소. 그자가 여기 남는다면, 내가 그자의 가죽을 벗겨 버리겠소.'\"\n\n\"맙소사!\" 픽윅 씨가 저도 모르게 외쳤다.\n\n\"그 신사 양반의 가죽을 벗기셨습니까, 선생님?\" 윈클 씨가 몹시 창백한 얼굴로 물었다.\n\n\"그자에게 쪽지를 썼소. 괴로운 일이라고 했지. 정말 그랬소.\"\n\n\"그렇고말고요.\" 윈클 씨가 끼어들었다.\n\n\"나는 신사로서 그자의 가죽을 벗기겠다고 내 말로 맹세했다고 했소. 내 명예가 걸린 일이었지. 달리 방법이 없었소. 국왕 폐하의 군에 복무하는 장교로서, 나는 그자의 가죽을 벗길 의무가 있었소. 그 불가피함이 유감스러웠지만, 해야만 하는 일이었소. 그자는 설득에 귀를 기울였소. 군의 규율이 절대적이라는 것을 알아본 거지. 그자는 달아났소. 나는 그녀와 결혼했고. 마차가 왔군. 저게 그녀의 머리요.\"\n\n다울러 씨는 말을 마치며, 마침 막 들어온 역마차를 가리켰다. 그 열린 창문으로 밝은 파란색 보닛을 쓴 제법 예쁜 얼굴이 보도 위 군중 사이를 살펴보고 있었는데, 십중팔구 그 무모한 사내, 곧 자기를 찾는 것이리라. 다울러 씨는 계산을 치르고 여행 모자와 외투와 망토를 챙겨 서둘러 나갔다. 그리고 픽윅 씨와 친구들도 자리를 잡으러 뒤따라 나갔다.\n\n터프먼 씨와 스노드그래스 씨는 마차의 뒤쪽에 자리를 잡았고, 윈클 씨는 안쪽에 올라탔으며, 픽윅 씨도 그를 뒤따르려는 참이었다. 그때 샘 웰러가 주인에게 다가와 귀에 대고 속삭이며, 더없이 은밀한 태도로 잠깐 이야기를 나누고 싶다고 청했다.\n\n\"그래, 샘.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"이번엔 또 무슨 일인가?\"\n\n\"이거 좀 묘한 일입니다요, 나리.\" 샘이 대답했다.\n\n\"뭔데?\" 픽윅 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"이거 말입니다요, 나리.\" 샘이 대꾸했다. \"제 생각엔 말입니다요, 이 마차 주인이 우리한테 무슨 건방진 수작을 부리는 게 아닌가 아주 걱정이 됩니다요.\"\n\n\"어째서 그러는가, 샘?\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"운행표에 이름이 적혀 있지 않던가?\"\n\n\"이름이 운행표에 적혀 있을 뿐만 아니라요, 나리,\" 샘이 대답했다. \"그중 하나를 마차 문짝에다 칠해 놨지 뭡니까요.\" 샘은 말하면서, 보통 마차 주인의 이름이 적히는 마차 문짝 부분을 가리켰다. 그러자 거기에는 정말로, 큼지막한 금빛 글자로 마법 같은 이름 픽윅이 적혀 있었다!\n\n\"이런.\" 픽윅 씨가 그 우연의 일치에 어지간히 놀라 외쳤다. \"참으로 별난 일이로군!\"\n\n\"네, 그런데 그게 다가 아닙니다요.\" 샘이 다시 주인의 주의를 마차 문짝으로 돌리며 말했다. \"픽윅이라고 써 놓는 걸로 만족하지 못하고, 그 앞에 모지스라고 붙여 놨지 뭡니까요. 이건 다친 데 모욕을 더하는 격이라고 저는 말하겠습니다요. 그 앵무새가 한 말마따나요, 사람들이 그놈을 제 고향에서 데려온 것도 모자라 그 뒤에 영어까지 지껄이게 만들었다는 거 말입니다요.\"\n\n\"확실히 충분히 이상한 일이구나, 샘.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"하지만 우리가 여기 서서 떠들고 있다가는 자리를 놓치겠다.\"\n\n\"아니, 그 결과로 아무것도 안 하실 겁니까요, 나리?\" 픽윅 씨가 안쪽에 자리 잡으려고 태연하게 준비하는 그 침착함에 완전히 기겁한 샘이 외쳤다.\n\n\"한다고!\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"뭘 한단 말이냐?\"\n\n\"이런 무례를 저질렀는데 누구 한 놈 두들겨 패 줘야 하는 거 아닙니까요, 나리?\" 웰러 씨가 말했다. 그는 적어도 자기가 그 자리에서 차장과 마부에게 주먹다짐을 신청하는 임무라도 맡으리라 기대했던 것이다.\n\n\"절대 아니다.\" 픽윅 씨가 간곡히 대답했다. \"무슨 일이 있어도 안 된다. 당장 네 자리로 올라가거라.\"\n\n\"제 생각엔 말이야,\" 샘은 돌아서며 혼잣말로 중얼거렸다. \"나리한테 뭔가 묘한 게 씌었어. 안 그러면 이걸 이렇게 조용히 넘기실 리가 없지. 그 빌어먹을 재판이 나리 기개를 꺾어 놓지 않았어야 할 텐데, 영 안 좋아 보여, 아주 안 좋아.\" 웰러 씨는 심각하게 고개를 저었다. 그리고 그가 이 일을 얼마나 가슴에 새겼는지를 보여 주는 예로서 기록해 둘 만한 것은, 마차가 켄싱턴 통행 요금소에 이를 때까지 그가 한마디도 더 하지 않았다는 점이다. 그가 그토록 오래 입을 다물고 있었다는 것은, 가히 전례 없는 일이라 할 만했다.\n\n여행 중에 특별히 언급할 만한 일은 일어나지 않았다. 다울러 씨는 갖가지 일화를 늘어놓았는데, 하나같이 자기 자신의 용맹과 결사의 기개를 보여 주는 것들이었고, 그 입증을 위해 다울러 부인에게 동의를 구했다. 그러면 다울러 부인은 어김없이 부록 형식으로, 다울러 씨가 잊었거나 혹은 어쩌면 겸손해서 빠뜨린 어떤 놀라운 사실이나 정황을 끼워 넣었다. 그 추가 내용은 매번 다울러 씨가 자기가 스스로 내세우는 것보다도 한층 더 놀라운 사내임을 드러내는 것이었기 때문이다. 픽윅 씨와 윈클 씨는 크게 감탄하며 귀를 기울였고, 틈틈이 다울러 부인과 이야기를 나누었으니, 그녀는 매우 상냥하고 매력적인 사람이었다. 그리하여 다울러 씨의 이야기와 다울러 부인의 매력과 픽윅 씨의 유쾌한 기분과 윈클 씨의 훌륭한 경청 덕분에, 안쪽 승객들은 가는 내내 그럭저럭 아주 화기애애하게 지냈다.\n\n바깥쪽 승객들은 바깥쪽 승객들이 언제나 그러하듯 굴었다. 그들은 매 구간 시작에는 아주 명랑하고 수다스러웠고, 중간에는 아주 침울하고 졸렸으며, 끝 무렵에는 다시 아주 활기차고 정신이 말짱했다. 인도 고무 망토를 걸친 한 젊은 신사는 온종일 시가를 피웠고, 외투랍시고 우스꽝스럽게 흉내 낸 것을 걸친 또 다른 젊은 신사는 시가를 여러 개 붙였다가 두 모금 빨고 나서는 분명 속이 거북한 듯, 아무도 자기를 안 본다 싶을 때 던져 버렸다. 마부석에는 가축에 정통하고 싶어 하는 세 번째 젊은이가 있었고, 뒤쪽에는 농사일에 밝은 노인이 있었다. 작업복과 흰 외투를 걸친 채 세례명으로 불리는 자들이 끊임없이 줄을 이었는데, 그들은 차장에게서 '태워 주겠다'는 권유를 받았고, 길 위에서나 길 밖에서나 말이며 마부며 모르는 게 없었다. 그리고 만약 어지간한 수의 입들이 그 시간 안에 먹어 치울 수만 있었다면 한 입에 반 크라운이라도 싸다 할 만찬이 있었다. 그리하여 저녁 일곱 시에 픽윅 씨와 친구들, 그리고 다울러 씨와 그의 아내는 각각, 배스의 대온천장 맞은편에 있는 화이트 하트 호텔의 자기네 전용 거실로 물러났다. 그곳 웨이터들은 차림새로 보아 웨스트민스터 학교 학생들로 오인받을 만했으나, 다만 훨씬 더 점잖게 처신함으로써 그 착각을 깨뜨렸다.\n\n이튿날 아침 식사가 채 치워지기도 전에, 웨이터 하나가 다울러 씨의 명함을 들여왔는데, 친구 한 사람을 소개하도록 허락해 달라는 청이 적혀 있었다. 다울러 씨는 명함을 들여보낸 데 곧바로 뒤이어, 자기 자신과 그 친구를 함께 데리고 들어왔다.\n\n그 친구는 쉰을 그리 넘기지 않은 매력적인 젊은이로, 번쩍이는 단추가 달린 매우 산뜻한 파란색 외투에 검은 바지를 입고, 한껏 광을 낸 더없이 얇은 부츠를 신고 있었다. 짧고 넓은 검은 리본으로 목에 매단 금테 안경이 늘어뜨려져 있었고, 금 코담배갑이 왼손에 가볍게 쥐여 있었으며, 손가락마다 셀 수 없이 많은 금반지가 반짝였고, 셔츠의 주름 장식에는 금에 박은 커다란 다이아몬드 핀이 빛났다. 그는 금시계와, 큼직한 금 도장이 달린 금 사슬을 지녔으며, 금 손잡이가 달린 낭창낭창한 흑단 지팡이를 들고 다녔다. 그의 속옷은 더없이 희고, 곱고, 빳빳했으며, 그의 가발은 더없이 반들거리고, 검고, 곱슬곱슬했다. 그의 코담배는 프린스 믹스처였고, 향수는 부케 뒤 루아였다. 그의 얼굴은 영원한 미소로 오므라져 있었고, 그의 이는 어찌나 가지런한지 조금만 떨어져서는 진짜와 가짜를 분간하기 어려울 정도였다.\n\n\"픽윅 씨.\" 다울러 씨가 말했다. \"제 친구, 안젤로 사이러스 밴텀 님, 의전관이올시다. 밴텀, 픽윅 씨라네. 서로 인사하시오.\"\n\n\"배스에 오신 것을 환영합니다, 선생님. 이건 참으로 큰 소득입니다. 배스에 더없이 환영합니다, 선생님. 오래되었군요—참 오래되었습니다, 픽윅 씨, 선생께서 이 온천물을 마신 지가요. 한 세월은 된 것 같습니다, 픽윅 씨. 놀라운 일입니다!\"\n\n안젤로 사이러스 밴텀 의전관 님이 픽윅 씨의 손을 잡으며 한 말들이 그러했다. 그동안 그는 그 손을 자기 손에 쥔 채로, 마치 그 손을 다시 놓는 시련을 정말이지 도무지 결단할 수 없다는 듯, 연거푸 절을 하며 어깨를 으쓱거렸다.\n\n\"제가 이 온천물을 마신 지가 아주 오래된 것은 확실합니다.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다. \"왜냐하면 제가 아는 한, 저는 여기 와 본 적이 한 번도 없으니까요.\"\n\n\"배스에 와 보신 적이 없다니요, 픽윅 씨!\" 그 위대한 의전관이 놀라 손을 떨어뜨리며 외쳤다. \"배스에 와 보신 적이 없다니! 헤! 헤! 픽윅 씨, 농담도 잘하십니다. 나쁘지 않군요, 나쁘지 않아요. 좋습니다, 좋아요. 헤! 헤! 헤! 놀랍습니다!\"\n\n\"부끄러운 일입니다만, 저는 완전히 진지하다는 말씀을 드려야겠습니다.\" 픽윅 씨가 대꾸했다. \"저는 정말로 여기 와 본 적이 한 번도 없습니다.\"\n\n\"오, 알겠습니다.\" 그 위대한 의전관이 몹시 흡족해하는 표정으로 외쳤다. \"그래요, 그래—좋습니다, 좋아요—점점 더 좋습니다. 선생이 바로 저희가 소문으로 듣던 그분이시군요. 그래요, 저희는 선생을 압니다, 픽윅 씨. 저희는 선생을 알지요.\"\n\n'그 빌어먹을 신문들에 난 재판 기사로군.' 픽윅 씨는 생각했다. '나에 관한 걸 죄다 들었구나.'\n\n\"선생은 클래펌 그린에 사시는 그 신사분이지요.\" 밴텀이 다시 말을 이었다. \"포트와인을 드신 뒤 경솔하게 감기에 걸려 사지를 못 쓰게 되셨고, 그 결과 극심한 고통으로 몸을 움직일 수 없게 되어, 킹스 배스의 온천물을 화씨 103도로 병에 담게 하여 짐마차로 도시의 침실까지 보내게 한 다음, 거기서 목욕을 하고 재채기를 하고는 그날로 회복하셨다지요. 참으로 놀라운 일입니다!\"\n\n픽윅 씨는 그 추측이 함축한 칭찬을 받아들이긴 했으나, 그럼에도 그것을 부인하는 극기심을 발휘했다. 그리고 의전관이 잠시 침묵하는 틈을 타, 자기 친구들인 터프먼 씨, 윈클 씨, 스노드그래스 씨를 소개하기를 청했다. 그 소개는 의전관을 기쁨과 영광으로 압도했다.\n\n\"밴텀,\" 다울러 씨가 말했다. \"픽윅 씨와 그 친구들은 이곳이 처음이라네. 이름을 등록해야 하네. 그 명부는 어디 있나?\"\n\n\"배스를 찾은 귀빈 명부는 오늘 아침 두 시에 대온천장에 비치될 겁니다.\" 의전관이 대답했다. \"우리 친구분들을 그 훌륭한 건물로 안내해 주셔서, 제가 그분들의 서명을 받을 수 있도록 해 주시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"그러지.\" 다울러가 대꾸했다. \"방문이 길어졌군. 갈 시간일세. 한 시간 뒤에 다시 오겠소. 가세.\"\n\n\"오늘은 무도회가 있는 밤입니다.\" 의전관이 일어서며 다시 픽윅 씨의 손을 잡고 말했다. \"배스의 무도회 밤은 낙원에서 떼어 온 순간들이지요. 음악과 미모와 우아함과 유행과 예절로, 그리고—그리고—무엇보다도 상인들이 없음으로 인해 황홀해지는 순간들입니다. 상인들이란 낙원과는 도무지 어울리지 않는 자들이고, 그자들은 격주로 길드홀에서 자기네끼리 한데 뭉치는데, 그건 좋게 말해도 놀라운 일이지요. 안녕히 계십시오, 안녕히!\" 그리하여 계단을 내려가는 내내 자기는 더없이 만족스럽고, 더없이 기쁘고, 더없이 압도되었으며, 더없이 영광스럽다고 거듭 말하면서, 안젤로 사이러스 밴텀 의전관 님은 문 앞에서 기다리던 매우 우아한 사륜마차에 올라타 덜커덩거리며 떠나갔다.\n\n정해진 시각에 픽윅 씨와 친구들은 다울러의 안내를 받아 집회장으로 가서 명부에 자기네 이름을 적었다—이 황송한 처사에 안젤로 밴텀은 전보다도 더욱 압도되었다. 그날 저녁 모임의 입장권은 일행 전부를 위해 미리 준비되었어야 했으나, 아직 마련되지 않았기에, 픽윅 씨는 안젤로 밴텀이 한사코 만류하는데도 불구하고, 오후 네 시에 퀸 광장에 있는 의전관의 집으로 샘을 보내 입장권을 받아 오게 하기로 했다. 시내를 잠시 거닐고 나서, 파크 거리가 꿈에서 보는 가파른 거리—아무리 애를 써도 도무지 올라갈 수 없는 그런 거리—와 매우 닮았다는 만장일치의 결론에 이른 뒤, 그들은 화이트 하트로 돌아왔고, 주인이 그에게 약속한 그 심부름을 위해 샘을 보냈다.\n\n샘 웰러는 아주 편안하고 우아한 태도로 모자를 쓰고, 두 손을 조끼 주머니에 찔러 넣은 채 매우 느긋하게 퀸 광장으로 걸어갔다. 그러면서 그는 당대 가장 인기 있는 곡조 여러 개를, 입으로 부는 것이든 손잡이를 돌리는 것이든 저 고귀한 악기인 오르간을 위해 완전히 새로운 악장으로 편곡한 양 휘파람으로 불며 갔다. 안내받은 퀸 광장의 그 번지에 이르자, 그는 휘파람을 멈추고 명랑하게 문을 두드렸고, 즉각 화려한 제복을 입은, 균형 잡힌 체격에 머리에 분을 바른 하인이 응답했다.\n\n\"여기가 밴텀 씨 댁이오, 영감?\" 샘 웰러가 화려한 제복을 입고 머리에 분을 바른 하인이라는 형상으로 눈앞에 펼쳐진 그 휘황한 광채에 조금도 주눅 들지 않고 물었다.\n\n\"무슨 일이오, 젊은이?\" 머리에 분을 바른 하인이 거만하게 물었다.\n\n\"여기가 거기라면, 이 명함을 들고 그분께 들어가서, 웰러 씨가 기다린다고 전해 주시겠소?\" 샘이 말했다. 그렇게 말하면서 그는 아주 태연하게 현관으로 걸어 들어가 앉았다.\n\n머리에 분을 바른 하인은 문을 몹시 세게 쾅 닫고, 매우 거창하게 인상을 찌푸렸다. 그러나 그 쾅 소리도 찌푸린 인상도 샘에게는 헛수고였으니, 그는 마호가니 우산꽂이를 비평적 찬탄의 온갖 외적 표시를 띠고 살펴보고 있었다.\n\n명함에 대한 주인의 응대가 머리에 분을 바른 하인에게 샘에 대한 호의를 심어 준 듯했다. 명함을 전하고 돌아온 그가 다정한 태도로 미소를 지으며, 답장이 곧 준비될 거라고 말했기 때문이다.\n\n\"아주 좋소.\" 샘이 말했다. \"그 영감님더러 진땀 빼지 마시라고 전하시오. 서두를 거 없소, 키다리 양반. 나는 점심을 먹었으니.\"\n\n\"점심을 일찍 드시는군요, 손님.\" 머리에 분을 바른 하인이 말했다.\n\n\"그래야 저녁이 더 잘 넘어가더라고요.\" 샘이 대답했다.\n\n\"배스에 오래 계셨습니까, 손님?\" 머리에 분을 바른 하인이 물었다. \"전에 손님에 대한 소문을 들어 본 기쁨이 없어서요.\"\n\n\"아직 여기서 무슨 대단한 화제를 일으키진 못했소.\" 샘이 대꾸했다. \"나하고 다른 멋쟁이분들은 어젯밤에야 막 왔으니 말이오.\"\n\n\"좋은 곳입니다, 손님.\" 머리에 분을 바른 하인이 말했다.\n\n\"그런 것 같소.\" 샘이 한마디 했다.\n\n\"기분 좋은 사교계지요, 손님.\" 머리에 분을 바른 하인이 말했다. \"아주 상냥한 하인들이 있고요, 손님.\"\n\n\"그럴 거라고 생각했소.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"싹싹하고, 꾸밈없고, 아무한테도-아무 말도-안 하는 그런 부류들 말이지.\"\n\n\"오, 정말 그렇습니다, 손님.\" 샘의 말을 더없는 칭찬으로 받아들인 머리에 분을 바른 하인이 말했다. \"정말 그렇고말고요. 손님께서는 이쪽으로 뭐 좀 하십니까?\" 키다리 하인이 위에 여우 머리가 달린 작은 코담배갑을 꺼내며 물었다.\n\n\"재채기 없이는 못 하오.\" 샘이 대답했다.\n\n\"음, 그게 어렵긴 합니다, 손님, 인정하지요.\" 키다리 하인이 말했다. \"차츰차츰 하면 됩니다, 손님. 커피가 제일 좋은 연습이지요. 저는 한동안 커피를 가지고 다녔습니다, 손님. 그게 라피 담배랑 아주 비슷해 보이거든요.\"\n\n이때 종이 날카롭게 울려 대는 바람에, 머리에 분을 바른 하인은 여우 머리를 주머니에 넣고 겸손한 낯빛으로 밴텀 씨의 '서재'로 서둘러 가야 하는 굴욕스러운 처지가 되었다. 그건 그렇고, 글을 읽지도 쓰지도 않으면서도 굳이 '서재'라고 부르는 작은 뒷방 하나쯤은 가지고 있지 않은 사람을 본 적이 있는가!\n\n\"답장이 여기 있습니다, 손님.\" 머리에 분을 바른 하인이 말했다. \"불편하실 만큼 크진 않을까 걱정입니다만.\"\n\n\"그런 말씀 마시오.\" 샘이 작은 동봉물이 든 편지를 받으며 말했다. \"기진맥진한 자연이 그럭저럭 이걸 견뎌 낼 수 있을지도 모르지요.\"\n\n\"또 뵙기를 바랍니다, 손님.\" 머리에 분을 바른 하인이 두 손을 비비며 샘을 따라 문 앞 계단까지 나오며 말했다.\n\n\"참 친절하시오, 선생.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"자, 기운을 넘어설 만큼 너무 무리하지는 마시오. 참으로 사랑스러운 분이군. 당신이 사회에 진 빚을 생각하시고, 너무 많은 일로 몸 상하게 하지 마시오. 동포들을 위해서, 되도록 조용히 지내시오. 당신을 잃는다면 그 손실이 얼마나 클지 생각해 보시오!\" 이 가슴 절절한 말과 함께, 샘 웰러는 떠나갔다.\n\n\"참 별난 젊은이로군.\" 머리에 분을 바른 하인이 웰러 씨의 뒷모습을 바라보며 말했는데, 그 표정은 도무지 그를 이해할 수 없다는 것을 분명히 드러냈다.\n\n샘은 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 그는 한쪽 눈을 찡긋하고, 고개를 흔들고, 미소 짓고, 다시 한쪽 눈을 찡긋했다. 그러고는 뭔가에 몹시 즐거워하는 듯한 표정을 띠고서 명랑하게 걸어가 버렸다.\n\n바로 그날 밤 여덟 시 이십 분 전, 의전관인 안젤로 사이러스 밴텀 님은, 똑같은 가발, 똑같은 이, 똑같은 안경, 똑같은 시계와 도장, 똑같은 반지들, 똑같은 셔츠 핀, 똑같은 지팡이 차림으로, 집회장 문 앞에서 자기 사륜마차에서 내려섰다. 그의 차림새에서 눈에 띄게 달라진 점이라곤, 흰 비단 안감을 댄 한층 더 산뜻한 파란색 외투에 검은 짧은 바지와 검은 비단 양말과 펌프스를 신고, 흰 조끼를 입었으며, 가능하다면, 향수를 딱 한 점쯤 더 뿌렸다는 것뿐이었다.\n\n이렇게 차려입고서, 의전관은 그 막중하기 그지없는 직책의 중요한 임무를 엄정히 수행하고자, 손님들을 맞이하러 홀에 자리를 잡았다.\n\n배스가 만원이었던 터라, 손님들과 차 값 6펜스 동전이 떼를 지어 쏟아져 들어왔다. 무도회장과 기다란 카드실, 팔각형 카드실, 계단, 복도에서 수많은 목소리의 웅성거림과 수많은 발소리가 정신을 아득하게 했다. 드레스가 바스락거리고, 깃털이 나부끼고, 불빛이 빛나고, 보석이 반짝였다. 음악이 있었으니—사각무 악단의 음악은 아니었으니 아직 시작되지 않은 까닭이요—부드럽고 자그마한 발소리의 음악이었고, 이따금 맑고 즐거운 웃음소리가 섞였으니, 그 웃음은 낮고 부드러웠으나 여성의 목소리로 듣기에는 매우 듣기 좋았으니, 배스에서든 다른 곳에서든 마찬가지였다. 즐거운 기대로 빛나는 영롱한 눈동자들이 사방에서 반짝였고, 어디를 둘러보든 어떤 우아한 자태가 인파 사이를 매끄럽게 미끄러져 가다가, 사라지기가 무섭게 그에 못지않게 앙증맞고 매혹적인 또 다른 자태로 대체되곤 했다.\n\n다실 안과 카드 탁자 주위를 맴도는 곳에는, 그날의 온갖 잡담과 추문을 논하는 별난 노부인들과 노쇠한 노신사들이 어마어마하게 많았는데, 그 음미하는 맛과 흥취가 그들이 그 소일거리에서 얻는 즐거움이 얼마나 강렬한지를 충분히 말해 주었다. 이 무리들 사이에 섞여 있는 것은 서너 명의 혼사 주선에 열중한 어머니들이었으니, 그들은 자기가 끼어든 대화에 온통 몰두한 듯이 보였으나, 때때로 딸들에게 근심 어린 곁눈질을 던지는 것을 잊지 않았다. 그 딸들은 젊음을 한껏 활용하라는 어머니의 당부를 기억하여, 벌써 풋풋한 추파 던지기에 들어가 있었으니, 스카프를 흘리고, 장갑을 끼고, 찻잔을 내려놓고 하는 따위였다—겉보기엔 사소한 일들이나, 능숙한 솜씨꾼들의 손에서는 놀랍도록 큰 효과를 낼 수 있는 것들이었다.\n\n문 가까이며 외진 구석에서 어슬렁거리는 것은 어리석은 젊은이들의 여러 무리였으니, 갖가지 종류의 멋부림과 우둔함을 뽐내며, 자기네 곁에 있는 분별 있는 모든 이들을 그 어리석음과 자만으로 즐겁게 해 주었고, 다행히도 자기네가 만인의 찬탄의 대상이라고 여기고 있었으니—이는 어떤 선량한 사람도 시비 걸지 않을 현명하고 자비로운 섭리였다.\n\n그리고 마지막으로, 저녁 내내 이미 자리를 잡은 뒤쪽 의자 몇 군데에 앉아 있는 것은, 한창때를 넘긴 미혼의 여러 부인들이었으니, 그들은 상대가 없어 춤을 추지 않았고, 돌이킬 수 없이 노처녀로 낙인찍힐까 두려워 카드도 하지 않았기에, 자기 자신은 돌아보지 않고서 누구든 헐뜯을 수 있는 유리한 처지에 있었다. 요컨대, 그들은 누구든 헐뜯을 수 있었으니, 모두가 거기 있었던 까닭이다. 그것은 화려함과 광채와 볼거리의 광경이었고, 호화롭게 차려입은 사람들과 멋진 거울들과 백묵 가루를 뿌린 마룻바닥과 가지촛대와 밀랍 양초의 광경이었다. 그리고 그 광경 곳곳에서, 소리 없이 부드럽게 이곳저곳을 미끄러져 다니며, 이쪽 무리에게는 굽실굽실 절하고, 저쪽에게는 허물없이 고갯짓하고, 모두에게 흡족하게 미소 짓는 것은, 말끔하게 차려입은 의전관 안젤로 사이러스 밴텀 님의 모습이었다.\n\n\"다실에서 멈추시오. 6펜스어치를 받으시오. 그런 다음 뜨거운 물을 부어 그걸 차라 부르시오. 그걸 드시오.\" 다울러 씨가 큰 소리로, 다울러 부인을 팔에 끼고 작은 일행의 선두에 서서 나아가는 픽윅 씨에게 일러 주며 말했다. 픽윅 씨는 다실로 들어섰고, 그를 발견한 밴텀 씨는 인파를 코르크 마개 따듯 비집고 나아와 황홀하게 그를 맞이했다.\n\n\"선생님, 더없는 영광입니다. 배스가 은총을 입었군요. 다울러 부인, 부인께서 이 방들을 빛내 주십니다. 그 깃털을 축하드립니다. 놀랍습니다!\"\n\n\"여기 누구 와 있나?\" 다울러가 미심쩍은 듯 물었다.\n\n\"누구라니요! 배스의 최상류층이지요. 픽윅 씨, 저 사 모자를 쓴 노부인이 보이십니까?\"\n\n\"저 뚱뚱한 노부인 말입니까?\" 픽윅 씨가 순진하게 물었다.\n\n\"쉿, 선생님—배스에서는 누구도 뚱뚱하거나 늙지 않았답니다. 저분이 스누퍼누프 미망인 마님이십니다.\"\n\n\"정말입니까?\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"바로 그분이올시다, 장담합니다.\" 의전관이 말했다. \"쉿. 좀 더 가까이 오십시오, 픽윅 씨. 이쪽으로 오고 있는 저 호화롭게 차려입은 젊은이가 보이십니까?\"\n\n\"머리가 길고 이마가 유난히 좁은 저 사람 말입니까?\" 픽윅 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"바로 그자입니다. 지금 이 순간 배스에서 가장 부유한 젊은이지요. 젊은 머탄헤드 경이올시다.\"\n\n\"설마요?\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇습니다. 곧 그분 목소리를 듣게 되실 겁니다, 픽윅 씨. 그분이 제게 말을 걸 테니까요. 그분과 함께 있는, 붉은 속조끼에 검은 콧수염을 기른 저 다른 신사분은 크러시턴 님이신데, 그분의 절친한 벗이지요. 안녕하십니까, 경?\"\n\n\"무척 덥구먼, 밴텀.\" 경께서 말했다.\n\n\"정말 무척 따뜻합니다, 경.\" 의전관이 대답했다.\n\n\"지독하지.\" 크러시턴 님이 동의했다.\n\n\"경의 우편 마차를 보셨습니까, 밴텀?\" 크러시턴 님이 잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤 물었는데, 그동안 젊은 머탄헤드 경은 픽윅 씨를 빤히 쳐다보아 무안하게 만들려 애쓰고 있었고, 크러시턴 씨는 경께서 어떤 화제를 가장 잘 떠들 수 있을지 궁리하고 있었다.\n\n\"이런, 못 봤습니다.\" 의전관이 대답했다. \"우편 마차라니! 참으로 훌륭한 발상이십니다. 놀랍습니다!\"\n\n\"맙소사!\" 경께서 말했다. \"다들 그 새 우편 마차를 본 줄 알았는데. 바퀴로 굴러간 것 중에 그렇게 산뜻하고 예쁘고 우아한 건 없지. 빨간색으로 칠하고, 크림빛 얼룩말을 매달았다네.\"\n\n\"편지를 넣는 진짜 함까지 달려서, 완전히 다 갖춰져 있지요.\" 크러시턴 님이 말했다.\n\n\"그리고 앞쪽엔 마부를 위한 작은 자리가, 쇠 난간을 둘러서 있다네.\" 경께서 덧붙였다. \"요전 날 아침엔 진홍색 외투를 입고 그걸 몰아 브리스틀까지 갔는데, 하인 둘이 사 분의 일 마일쯤 뒤에서 따라왔지. 그런데 빌어먹을, 사람들이 오두막에서 뛰쳐나와 내 갈 길을 막고서, 내가 우편배달부가 아니냐고 묻지 뭔가. 영광이었지—영광이었어!\"\n\n이 일화에 경께서 무척 호탕하게 웃었고, 물론 듣던 이들도 그랬다. 그러고는 굽실거리는 크러시턴 씨의 팔에 자기 팔을 끼고, 머탄헤드 경은 걸어가 버렸다.\n\n\"참 유쾌한 젊은이지요, 경께서는.\" 의전관이 말했다.\n\n\"그런 것 같군요.\" 픽윅 씨가 무뚝뚝하게 대꾸했다.\n\n춤이 시작되고, 필요한 소개가 다 이루어지고, 모든 사전 준비가 마련되자, 안젤로 밴텀은 픽윅 씨에게 다시 합류하여 그를 카드실로 데려갔다.\n\n그들이 들어선 바로 그 순간, 스누퍼누프 미망인 마님과, 고색창연하고 휘스트깨나 칠 듯한 풍모의 다른 두 부인이 비어 있는 카드 탁자 위를 맴돌고 있었다. 그들은 안젤로 밴텀의 호위를 받는 픽윅 씨에게 눈길이 닿기가 무섭게, 그가 바로 자기네가 러버 판을 채우는 데 필요한 바로 그 사람임을 알아보고서 서로 눈짓을 주고받았다.\n\n\"밴텀, 이 친구.\" 스누퍼누프 미망인 마님이 어르듯 말했다. \"이 탁자를 채울 멋진 분을 좀 찾아 주시오. 착한 양반이니.\" 마침 그 순간 픽윅 씨가 다른 쪽을 보고 있었던 터라, 마님께서는 그를 향해 고갯짓을 하고 의미심장하게 눈살을 찌푸렸다.\n\n\"제 친구 픽윅 씨가, 마님, 더없이 기뻐할 겁니다, 분명히, 대단히 기뻐할 거예요.\" 그 암시를 알아챈 의전관이 말했다. \"픽윅 씨, 스누퍼누프 마님—웍스비 대령 부인—볼로 양이올시다.\"\n\n픽윅 씨는 부인들에게 일일이 절을 하고, 빠져나갈 수 없음을 알고서 패를 갈랐다. 픽윅 씨와 볼로 양이 스누퍼누프 마님과 웍스비 대령 부인을 상대하게 되었다.\n\n두 번째 패를 돌리기 시작할 때 으뜸패가 뒤집히는 순간, 두 젊은 숙녀가 서둘러 방으로 들어와 웍스비 대령 부인의 의자 양옆에 자리를 잡고, 그 판이 끝날 때까지 참을성 있게 기다렸다.\n\n\"자, 제인.\" 웍스비 대령 부인이 두 처녀 중 하나에게 돌아서며 말했다. \"무슨 일이냐?\"\n\n\"엄마, 막내 크롤리 씨와 춤을 춰도 되는지 여쭤보러 왔어요.\" 둘 중 더 예쁘고 어린 쪽이 속삭였다.\n\n\"맙소사, 제인, 어떻게 그런 생각을 할 수 있니?\" 어머니가 분개하여 대답했다. \"그 사람 아버지가 연 팔백 파운드 수입이 있는데 그게 본인 대에서 끊긴다는 말을 누누이 듣지 않았니? 네가 부끄럽구나. 무슨 일이 있어도 안 된다.\"\n\n\"엄마,\" 다른 쪽이 속삭였는데, 그녀는 동생보다 훨씬 나이가 많고 매우 맥없고 꾸민 티가 났다. \"머탄헤드 경께서 제게 소개되었어요. 제 생각엔 약속이 없는 것 같다고 말씀드렸어요, 엄마.\"\n\n\"넌 참 사랑스러운 내 보배다, 얘야.\" 웍스비 대령 부인이 부채로 딸의 뺨을 톡톡 두드리며 대답했다. \"언제나 믿음직스럽지. 그분은 어마어마하게 부자란다, 얘야. 복 받으렴!\" 이 말과 함께 웍스비 대령 부인은 큰딸에게 더없이 다정하게 입을 맞추고, 다른 딸에게는 경고하듯 눈살을 찌푸리고서, 자기 패를 추렸다.\n\n가엾은 픽윅 씨! 그는 노련하기 짝이 없는 세 여성 카드꾼과 함께 쳐 본 적이 한 번도 없었다. 그들은 어찌나 지독하게 날카로운지 그는 아주 겁이 났다. 그가 잘못된 패를 내면 볼로 양은 단검 한 무더기 같은 눈빛을 보냈고, 그가 멈춰 서서 어느 패가 옳은지 곰곰이 생각하면 스누퍼누프 마님은 의자에 몸을 젖히고 웍스비 대령 부인에게 짜증과 연민이 뒤섞인 눈길로 미소 지었으며, 그러면 웍스비 대령 부인은 어깨를 으쓱하고 기침을 했으니, 마치 저 양반이 도대체 시작이나 할는지 모르겠다고 말하는 듯했다. 그러고는 매 판이 끝날 때마다 볼로 양은 침울한 낯빛과 책망 어린 한숨으로, 픽윅 씨가 왜 그 다이아몬드를 받아 내지 않았는지, 혹은 클럽을 내지 않았는지, 혹은 스페이드를 끊지 않았는지, 혹은 하트로 수를 부리지 않았는지, 혹은 으뜸패를 거쳐 내지 않았는지, 혹은 에이스를 내지 않았는지, 혹은 킹에 맞춰 내지 않았는지, 그 비슷한 무언가를 묻곤 했다. 그리고 이 모든 엄중한 추궁에 대해, 픽윅 씨는 이쯤 되자 게임에 관한 것을 죄다 잊어버린 터라, 무슨 변명도 전혀 내놓을 수가 없었다. 게다가 사람들이 와서 구경까지 하는 바람에 픽윅 씨는 긴장이 됐다. 이 모든 것에 더하여, 탁자 가까이서는 안젤로 밴텀과 두 마틴터 양 사이에 정신 사나운 대화가 잔뜩 오갔으니, 그들은 미혼인 데다 별나기까지 한지라, 이따금 짝 없는 상대라도 하나 얻을까 하는 희망에서 의전관에게 한껏 비위를 맞추고 있었다. 끊임없는 출입의 소음과 방해가 더해진 이 모든 것이 픽윅 씨가 패를 다소 서투르게 치게 만들었다. 패도 그에게 불리했다. 그리고 그들이 열한 시 십 분에 판을 끝냈을 때, 볼로 양은 상당히 격앙되어 탁자에서 일어나, 눈물을 펑펑 쏟으며 가마를 타고 곧장 집으로 가 버렸다.\n\n친구들이 합류했는데, 하나같이 이보다 더 유쾌한 저녁을 보낸 적이 거의 없다고 입을 모으는 가운데, 픽윅 씨는 그들과 함께 화이트 하트로 갔고, 뜨거운 무언가로 마음을 달랜 다음, 잠자리에 들면서 거의 동시에 잠이 들었다.\n\n픽윅 씨는 적어도 두 달은 배스에 머물 생각이었으므로, 그동안 자신과 친구들이 쓸 개인 숙소를 빌려 두는 편이 좋겠다고 판단했다. 마침 로열 크레센트에 있는 어느 집의 위층을 적당한 조건으로 확보할 좋은 기회가 생겼는데, 그 공간은 필요 이상으로 넓었으므로, 다울러 부부가 침실 하나와 거실 하나를 맡아 부담을 덜어 주겠다고 제안했다. 이 제안은 곧바로 받아들여졌고, 사흘 만에 그들은 모두 새 거처에 자리를 잡았으며, 그때부터 픽윅 씨는 더없는 부지런함으로 광천수를 마시기 시작했다. 픽윅 씨는 체계적으로 그것을 마셨다. 아침 식사 전에 4분의 1파인트를 마시고 언덕을 올라갔다가, 아침 식사 후에 또 4분의 1파인트를 마시고 언덕을 내려왔다. 그리고 새로 4분의 1파인트를 마실 때마다, 픽윅 씨는 더없이 엄숙하고 단호한 어조로 자신이 훨씬 나아진 것 같다고 선언했고, 그러면 친구들은 무척 기뻐했다—비록 그들은 그 전까지 그에게 어디 아픈 데가 있는 줄도 몰랐지만.\n\n대(大)펌프 룸은 코린트식 기둥과 음악 회랑, 톰피온 시계, 내시의 조각상, 그리고 모든 물 마시는 이들이 주목해야 할 금빛 명문(銘文)으로 장식된 널찍한 살롱인데, 그 명문은 마땅히 도울 만한 자선 사업을 위해 그들에게 호소하는 글이다. 그곳에는 대리석 항아리가 놓인 커다란 바가 있어 물 따르는 사람이 거기서 물을 길어 내고, 노르스름한 텀블러가 여럿 있어 손님들이 거기에 받아 마시는데, 그들이 얼마나 끈기 있고 진지하게 그것을 삼키는지를 바라보는 것은 더없이 교훈적이고 만족스러운 광경이다. 가까운 곳에는 목욕탕이 있어 손님 중 일부가 몸을 씻고, 그러고 나면 악단이 연주하여 그들이 그 일을 해낸 것을 나머지 사람들과 함께 축하해 준다. 또 다른 펌프 룸도 있는데, 거기에는 병약한 신사 숙녀들이 어찌나 놀랍도록 다양한 의자와 가마에 실려 들어오는지, 정상적인 수의 발가락을 갖추고 들어간 모험적인 인물은 그것들을 잃은 채 나올 위험이 코앞에 닥칠 정도이다. 그리고 세 번째 펌프 룸도 있어, 조용한 사람들이 그리로 가는데, 그곳이 다른 두 곳보다 덜 시끄럽기 때문이다. 목발을 짚거나 짚지 않거나, 지팡이를 들거나 들지 않거나 하며 거니는 사람들이 어마어마하게 많고, 대화와 활기와 익살도 무척 풍성하다.\n\n매일 아침, 정규로 물을 마시는 이들은—픽윅 씨도 그 가운데 하나였다—펌프 룸에서 서로 만나, 4분의 1파인트씩 마시고는 건강을 위해 산책했다. 오후의 산책 때에는 무탄헤드 경과 명예로운 크러시턴 씨, 미망인 스누퍼누프 부인, 워그스비 대령 부인, 그 밖의 온갖 지체 높은 사람들과 아침에 물을 마신 모든 이들이 성대한 회합을 이루며 만났다. 그러고 나면 그들은 걸어서 나가거나, 마차를 타고 나가거나, 목욕 의자에 밀려 나가서는 다시 서로 만났다. 그러고 나면 신사들은 열람실로 가서 무리의 일부와 만났다. 그러고 나면 집으로 돌아갔다. 연극이 있는 밤이면 어쩌면 극장에서 만나고, 무도회가 있는 밤이면 무도장에서 만났으며, 둘 다 아니면 이튿날 만났다. 어쩌면 약간의 단조로운 기색이 감돌긴 했지만, 무척 즐거운 일과였다.\n\n이런 식으로 하루를 보낸 어느 날, 친구들은 모두 잠자리에 들고 픽윅 씨 혼자 늦게까지 앉아 일기에 기록을 적고 있었는데, 방문을 가볍게 두드리는 소리에 정신이 들었다.\n\n\"실례합니다, 선생님.\" 집주인 크래덕 부인이 안을 들여다보며 말했다. \"혹시 더 필요하신 것이라도 있으신지요, 선생님?\"\n\n\"더 필요한 건 없습니다, 부인.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"제 어린 하녀는 잠자리에 들었고요, 선생님.\" 크래덕 부인이 말했다. \"다울러 씨께서 친절하게도 다울러 부인을 기다려 늦게까지 앉아 있겠다고 하셨어요. 파티가 늦게야 끝날 것 같으니까요. 그래서 저는 픽윅 선생님께서 더 필요하신 게 없으시다면 저도 자러 갈까 생각하고 있었답니다.\"\n\n\"얼마든지 그러십시오, 부인.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"안녕히 주무세요, 선생님.\" 크래덕 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"안녕히 주무십시오, 부인.\" 픽윅 씨가 화답했다.\n\n크래덕 부인이 문을 닫았고, 픽윅 씨는 다시 글쓰기를 이어 갔다.\n\n반 시간쯤 지나 기록이 마무리되었다. 픽윅 씨는 마지막 장(張)을 압지로 정성껏 문지르고, 책을 덮은 뒤, 펜을 자기 코트 자락 안쪽 밑단에 닦고는, 그것을 잘 넣어 두려고 잉크스탠드 서랍을 열었다. 잉크스탠드 서랍 안에는 꽤 빽빽이 글이 적힌 편지지 두어 장이 들어 있었는데, 제법 둥글둥글한 좋은 필체로 쓴 제목이 그에게 온전히 드러나도록 접혀 있었다. 그것을 보고 사적인 문서가 아님을 알았고, 배스에 관한 것인 듯하며 길이도 무척 짧았으므로, 픽윅 씨는 그것을 펼치고, 다 읽을 즈음 잘 타오르도록 침실 양초에 불을 붙인 뒤, 의자를 난로 가까이 끌어당기고 다음과 같이 읽어 나갔다—\n\n# THE TRUE LEGEND OF PRINCE BLADUD\n\n블래더드 왕자의 참된 전설\n\"이백 년이 채 되지 않은 옛날, 이 도시의 공중목욕탕 중 하나에는 그 위대한 창건자, 이름 높은 블래더드 왕자를 기리는 명문이 새겨져 있었다. 그 명문은 이제 지워졌다.\n\n\"그보다 수백 년 전부터, 한 시대에서 다음 시대로 옛 전설 하나가 전해 내려왔으니, 그 명망 높은 왕자가 아테네에서 풍성한 지식의 수확을 거두고 돌아오던 중 나병에 걸려, 부왕(父王)의 궁정을 피하고 침울하게 농부와 돼지들과 어울려 지냈다는 이야기였다. 그 무리 가운데에는(전설은 이렇게 말한다) 진지하고 엄숙한 표정을 한 돼지 한 마리가 있었으니, 왕자는 그 돼지에게 동질감을 느꼈다—그 또한 현명했기 때문이다—사려 깊고 과묵한 태도를 지닌 돼지였고, 동료들보다 뛰어난 짐승이었으며, 그 꿀꿀거리는 소리는 무시무시했고 무는 힘은 날카로웠다. 젊은 왕자는 그 위풍당당한 돼지의 얼굴을 바라보며 깊이 한숨을 쉬었다. 부왕을 떠올리자 그의 두 눈은 눈물로 젖어 들었다.\n\n\"이 영리한 돼지는 기름지고 축축한 진흙 속에서 목욕하기를 좋아했다. 요즘 흔한 돼지들이 그러하고 또 그 먼 옛날에도 그러했던 것처럼(이는 비록 미약하게나마 문명의 빛이 이미 동트기 시작했다는 증거다) 여름철에 몸을 식히기 위해서가 아니라, 춥고 매서운 겨울날에 그리했다. 그 돼지의 털은 늘 어찌나 매끄럽고 그 살빛은 어찌나 맑았던지, 왕자는 자기 친구가 즐겨 찾는 바로 그 물의 정화하는 효능을 시험해 보기로 결심했다. 그는 시험해 보았다. 그 검은 진흙 아래에서 배스의 온천이 부글부글 솟아오르고 있었다. 그는 몸을 씻었고, 병이 나았다. 부왕의 궁정으로 서둘러 가 최고의 경의를 표한 뒤, 재빨리 이곳으로 돌아와 이 도시와 그 유명한 목욕탕을 세웠다.\n\n\"그는 옛 우정의 온갖 열정을 다해 그 돼지를 찾았으나—아아! 그 물이 돼지의 죽음이 되고 말았다. 돼지는 경솔하게도 너무 높은 온도의 목욕을 했고, 그 자연철학자는 더 이상 이 세상 존재가 아니었다! 그의 뒤를 이은 것은 플리니우스였으니, 그 또한 지식을 향한 갈증의 희생양이 되었다.\n\n\"이상이 그 전설이었다. 이제 참된 이야기를 들어 보라.\n\n\"아주 여러 세기 전, 영국의 왕 그 유명하고 이름난 러드 휴디브래스가 크나큰 위세를 떨치며 번영했다. 그는 강대한 군주였다. 그가 걸으면 대지가 흔들렸으니—그가 너무도 뚱뚱했기 때문이다. 그의 백성은 그의 얼굴빛 속에서 햇볕을 쬐었으니—그 얼굴이 너무도 붉고 이글거렸기 때문이다. 그는 참으로 어느 모로 보나 왕이었다. 게다가 그에게는 '모(吋)'가 꽤 여럿 있었으니, 키가 그리 크지는 않았어도 둘레로는 놀랄 만한 크기였고, 키에서 모자란 모는 둘레로 채웠던 것이다. 현대의 타락한 군주 가운데 누구라도 그에게 견줄 만한 자가 있다면, 나는 저 유서 깊은 콜 왕이야말로 그 이름난 권력자라고 말하겠다.\n\n\"이 선량한 왕에게는 왕비가 있었으니, 그 왕비는 십팔 년 전에 아들을 하나 낳았고 그 이름을 블래더드라 했다. 그는 열 살이 될 때까지 부왕의 영토에 있는 예비 학교로 보내졌다가, 그 후 믿을 만한 사자(使者)의 손에 맡겨져 아테네의 마무리 학교로 파견되었다. 그리고 방학 동안 머물러도 추가 비용이 들지 않았고, 학생을 데려갈 때 사전 통지도 필요치 않았으므로, 그는 그곳에 여덟 해 동안이나 머물렀으며, 그 기간이 끝나자 부왕은 시종장(侍從長)을 보내 청구서를 정산하고 그를 집으로 데려오게 했다. 시종장이 그 일을 해내자, 그는 환호 속에 영접받고 즉시 연금을 받았다.\n\n\"러드 왕은 아들인 왕자를 보고 그가 그토록 훌륭한 청년으로 자란 것을 알자, 지체 없이 그를 결혼시키는 것이 얼마나 멋진 일일지를 깨달았으니, 그리하면 그 자손들이 러드의 영광스러운 혈통을 세상 가장 후세에 이르도록 영구히 이어 갈 방편이 될 터였다. 이런 생각으로 그는, 특별히 할 일이 없으며 돈벌이 되는 일자리를 원하는 위대한 귀족들로 구성된 특별 사절단을 이웃 나라 왕에게 보내어, 자기 아들의 아내로 그 아리따운 딸을 달라고 요구했다. 그러면서 동시에 자신은 형제이자 벗인 그와 더없이 다정한 사이로 지내고 싶지만, 만일 이 혼사를 주선하는 데 뜻이 맞지 않는다면 부득이 그의 왕국을 침공하여 그의 두 눈을 후벼 파야 하는 달갑잖은 처지에 놓일 것이라고 밝혔다. 이에 대해 다른 왕은(둘 중 더 약한 쪽이었다) 자신의 벗이자 형제인 그의 온갖 호의와 도량에 깊이 감사하며, 블래더드 왕자가 언제든 와서 딸을 데려가고자 하면 자기 딸은 결혼할 준비가 충분히 되어 있다고 답했다.\n\n\"이 답이 영국에 닿기가 무섭게 온 나라가 기쁨에 휩싸였다. 사방에서 들리는 것이라곤 잔치와 흥청거림의 소리뿐이었다—다만 그 행복한 의식의 비용을 치르고자 백성들이 왕실 재고 징수관에게 바치는 돈이 짤랑이는 소리만은 예외였다. 바로 이 자리에서 러드 왕은 어전 회의 중 옥좌 꼭대기에 앉았다가 벅찬 감정에 못 이겨 일어나, 대법관에게 가장 값진 포도주와 궁정 악사들을 들이라 명했으니—이 너그러운 처사는 전승 사가(史家)들의 무지로 인해 콜 왕에게 돌려졌던바, 그 폐하가 다음과 같이 묘사되는 저 유명한 시구가 그것이다.\n\n파이프를 청하고, 술단지를 청하며, 세 명의 바이올린 연주자를 청하셨네.\n\n이는 러드 왕의 기억에 대한 명백한 부당함이요, 콜 왕의 미덕을 부정직하게 떠받드는 일이다.\n\n\"그러나 이 모든 잔치와 환희 한가운데, 반짝이는 포도주가 따라질 때 입에 대지 않고, 악사들이 연주할 때 춤추지 않는 인물이 하나 있었다. 그는 다름 아닌 블래더드 왕자 본인이었으니, 바로 그 순간 온 백성이 그의 행복을 기리며 목청과 지갑 끈을 한꺼번에 쥐어짜고 있던 그 장본인이었다. 사실인즉, 왕자는 외무대신이 자기를 대신해 사랑에 빠질 의심할 바 없는 권리를 가졌다는 것을 잊고서, 정치와 외교의 온갖 선례에 어긋나게 이미 자기 멋대로 사랑에 빠져, 어느 고귀한 아테네인의 아리따운 딸과 남몰래 혼약을 맺어 둔 것이었다.\n\n\"여기서 우리는 문명과 세련됨이 지닌 수많은 이점 가운데 하나의 두드러진 본보기를 본다. 만일 왕자가 후세에 살았더라면, 그는 아버지가 택한 상대와 곧장 결혼한 뒤, 자기를 무겁게 짓누르는 그 짐을 덜고자 본격적으로 일에 착수할 수 있었으리라. 체계적인 모욕과 무관심의 과정을 통해 그녀의 가슴을 찢어 놓으려 애쓸 수도 있었을 테고, 혹은 여성 특유의 기개와 자신이 당한 숱한 부당함에 대한 도도한 자각이 이런 학대 속에서도 그녀를 지탱해 주었다면, 그녀의 목숨을 빼앗아 깨끗이 그녀를 치워 버리려 들었을지도 모른다. 그러나 이 두 가지 해소 방법 어느 것도 블래더드 왕자에게는 떠오르지 않았다. 그래서 그는 단독 알현을 청하여 부왕에게 사실을 털어놓았다.\n\n\"제 격정을 빼고는 무엇이든 다스리는 것이 왕의 오랜 특권이다. 러드 왕은 무시무시한 격노에 사로잡혀 왕관을 천장까지 던졌다가 다시 받았으니—그 시절에는 왕들이 왕관을 탑(塔)이 아니라 머리에 두고 있었기 때문이다—땅을 쾅쾅 구르고, 제 이마를 두드리며, 어찌하여 제 살붙이가 자신에게 반역하는지 의아해하다가, 마침내 호위병들을 불러들여 왕자를 즉각 높다란 망루에 가두라 명했으니, 이는 옛 왕들이 자기 아들들의 혼인 의향이 자신과 같은 쪽을 향하지 않을 때 흔히 취하던 처사였다.\n\n\"블래더드 왕자가 한 해의 거의 대부분을 그 높다란 망루에 갇혀, 육신의 눈앞에는 돌벽보다 나은 전망이 없고, 마음의 눈앞에는 기약 없는 감금보다 나은 전망이 없게 되자, 그는 자연히 탈출 계획을 궁리하기 시작했고, 여러 달의 준비 끝에 그것을 용케 실행해 냈다. 그러면서 사려 깊게도 자신의 만찬용 칼을 간수의 심장에 꽂아 두었으니, 행여 그 가엾은 사람이(처자식이 있었다) 그의 도주에 가담한 것으로 여겨져 격분한 왕에게 합당한 벌을 받지 않게 하려는 배려였다.\n\n\"군주는 아들을 잃고 미칠 듯이 날뛰었다. 누구에게 자신의 비통과 분노를 쏟아 내야 할지 알 수 없던 차에, 다행히 왕자를 집으로 데려왔던 시종장이 생각나, 그자의 연금과 머리를 한꺼번에 잘라 버렸다.\n\n\"한편, 감쪽같이 변장한 젊은 왕자는 부왕의 영토를 걸어서 떠돌았으니, 자신의 고단한 시련의 죄 없는 빌미가 된 그 아테네 처녀에 대한 달콤한 생각이 온갖 고생 속에서도 그를 북돋우고 떠받쳐 주었다. 어느 날 그는 한 시골 마을에서 쉬려고 걸음을 멈췄는데, 풀밭에서 흥겨운 춤이 벌어지고 명랑한 얼굴들이 오가는 것을 보고는, 가까이 서 있던 한 흥겨운 사람에게 이 환희의 까닭을 감히 물어보았다.\n\n\"'아니, 그대는 모르시오, 오 나그네여,' 하는 대답이 돌아왔다. '우리 자비로우신 왕께서 최근에 내리신 포고를 말이오?'\n\n\"'포고라니! 모르오. 무슨 포고 말이오?' 왕자가 되물었다—그가 한적하고 인적 드문 샛길로만 다녀, 그런 큰길에서 무슨 일이 일어났는지 전혀 몰랐기 때문이다.\n\n\"'아니 글쎄,' 농부가 대답했다. '우리 왕자께서 혼인하려 하시던 외국 아가씨가 제 나라의 외국 귀족과 결혼해 버려서, 왕께서 그 사실을 포고하시고 게다가 큰 공공 축제까지 여신 거요. 이제 당연히 블래더드 왕자께서 돌아오셔서 부왕께서 택하신 아가씨와 혼인하실 테니까요. 그 아가씨가 한낮의 태양처럼 아름답다고들 합디다. 자, 당신의 건강을 위하여, 신께서 왕을 지켜 주시기를!'\n\n\"왕자는 더 듣고 있지 않았다. 그는 그 자리에서 달아나, 이웃한 숲의 가장 깊은 후미진 곳으로 뛰어들었다. 그는 밤낮으로 계속 떠돌았다. 이글거리는 태양 아래, 차고 창백한 달 아래, 한낮의 메마른 더위를 뚫고, 밤의 축축한 추위를 뚫고, 아침의 잿빛 어스름 속에서, 저녁의 붉은 노을 속에서. 그는 시간이나 목적지에 어찌나 무심했던지, 아테네를 향해 가던 것이 길을 벗어나 배스까지나 헤매어 왔다.\n\n\"그때는 배스가 서 있는 자리에 도시라곤 없었다. 그 이름으로 불릴 만한 인간 거주지의 흔적도, 사람들이 모여드는 기색도 없었다. 그러나 거기에는 똑같은 고귀한 들판이 있었고, 언덕과 골짜기가 똑같이 너르게 펼쳐져 있었으며, 똑같이 아름다운 물줄기가 저 멀리로 슬며시 흘러갔고, 인생의 시련이 멀리서 바라보고 그 아침의 환한 안개에 부분적으로 가려졌을 때 그 험준함과 거침을 잃고 온통 편안하고 부드러워 보이듯, 똑같이 우뚝 솟은 산들이 있었다. 그 경치의 그윽한 아름다움에 마음이 움직여, 왕자는 푸른 잔디 위에 주저앉아 부르튼 두 발을 자신의 눈물로 적셨다.\n\n\"'오!' 불행한 블래더드가 두 손을 맞잡고 슬프게 두 눈을 하늘로 들어 올리며 말했다. '내 방랑이 여기서 끝날 수 있다면! 그릇된 희망과 멸시받은 사랑을 이제 애도하는 이 고마운 눈물이 영원히 평화로이 흐를 수 있다면!'\n\n\"그 소원은 들어졌다. 그때는 이교의 신들이 다스리던 시절이었으니, 그들은 이따금 사람들의 말을 곧이곧대로 받아들이곤 했고, 어떤 경우에는 그 신속함이 지극히 곤란하기도 했다. 왕자의 발밑에서 땅이 갈라졌고, 그는 그 틈으로 가라앉았으며, 그 순간 그것이 영영 그의 머리 위로 닫혔으니, 다만 그의 뜨거운 눈물이 땅을 뚫고 솟아오른 곳만은 예외였고, 그곳에서 그 눈물은 그 이래로 줄곧 솟구쳐 나오고 있다.\n\n\"눈여겨볼 만한 것은, 오늘날까지도 짝을 구하지 못해 실망한 수많은 노년의 신사 숙녀들과, 짝을 얻고자 애태우는 그에 못지않게 많은 젊은이들이, 해마다 배스로 와서 그 물을 마시고 거기서 큰 힘과 위안을 얻는다는 사실이다. 이는 블래더드 왕자의 눈물이 지닌 효험에 더없이 찬사를 보내는 일이요, 이 전설의 진실성을 강력히 뒷받침해 주는 일이다.\"\n\n픽윅 씨는 이 짤막한 원고의 끝에 이르렀을 때 여러 차례 하품을 하고는, 그것을 조심스레 다시 접어 잉크스탠드 서랍에 도로 넣은 뒤, 더없는 피로가 어린 얼굴로 침실 양초에 불을 붙이고 잠자리에 들려고 위층으로 올라갔다.\n\n그는 늘 하던 대로 다울러 씨의 방문 앞에 멈춰, 잘 자라고 인사하려 노크했다.\n\n\"아!\" 다울러가 말했다. \"주무시러 가시오? 나도 그랬으면 좋겠소. 음울한 밤이군. 바람이 부오. 안 그렇소?\"\n\n\"몹시 그렇군요.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"안녕히 주무십시오.\"\n\n\"안녕히 주무시오.\"\n픽윅 씨는 침실로 갔고, 다울러 씨는 아내가 집에 돌아올 때까지 깨어 있겠다는 경솔한 약속을 지키느라 다시 난로 앞 자리에 앉았다.\n\n누군가를 기다리며 깨어 앉아 있는 것보다 더 애를 태우는 일은 흔치 않으니, 그 누군가가 파티에 가 있다면 더욱 그러하다. 자기에게는 그토록 더디게 흘러가는 시간이 그들에게는 얼마나 빨리 지나가고 있을까 하는 생각을 떨칠 수가 없고, 이 생각을 하면 할수록 그들이 곧 도착하리라는 희망은 점점 줄어든다. 게다가 혼자 깨어 앉아 있으면 시계가 어찌나 크게 째깍거리는지, 마치 거미줄로 짠 속옷을 걸친 것만 같다. 처음에는 무언가가 오른쪽 무릎을 간질이고, 그다음에는 똑같은 감각이 왼쪽 무릎을 괴롭힌다. 자세를 바꾸기가 무섭게 그 느낌이 이번에는 두 팔에 찾아온다. 사지를 온갖 괴상한 모양으로 비비 꼬고 나면, 느닷없이 코가 다시 도지는데, 그것을 비벼 떼어 내기라도 할 듯이 문질러 댄다—실제로 할 수만 있다면 그렇게 하리라는 것은 의심할 바 없다. 두 눈 또한 한낱 개인적인 불편거리에 지나지 않고, 한쪽 촛불의 심지를 다듬는 동안 다른 쪽 촛불 심지는 1.5인치나 길게 자라 버린다. 이런저런 자잘한 신경질적 성가심들이, 다른 사람들이 모두 잠자리에 든 뒤 오래도록 깨어 앉아 있는 일을 결코 유쾌한 소일거리라 할 수 없게 만든다.\n\n이것이 바로, 난로 앞에 앉아 자기를 잠 못 이루게 붙잡아 두고 있는 그 파티의 비정한 사람들 모두에게 진심으로 분개하던 다울러 씨의 심정이었다. 게다가 저녁 일찍 자신이 거기 어딘가 아픈 데가 있다는 생각이 들어 그래서 집에 남았다는 사실을 곱씹어 보아도 그의 기분은 조금도 나아지지 않았다. 마침내, 여러 번 깜빡 졸다가 난로 살 쪽으로 앞으로 고꾸라지고, 얼굴에 낙인이 찍히기 직전에 가까스로 뒤로 몸을 젖히기를 거듭한 끝에, 다울러 씨는 안쪽 방의 침대에 몸을 던지고 생각이나 하기로—물론 잠은 자지 않고—마음먹었다.\n\n\"난 잠귀가 어두워.\" 다울러 씨가 침대에 몸을 던지며 말했다. \"깨어 있어야 해. 여기 노크 소리는 들리겠지. 그래. 그럴 줄 알았어. 야경꾼 소리가 들리는군. 저기 가는군. 그래도 이젠 좀 희미해졌어. 조금 더 희미해졌고. 모퉁이를 돌고 있군. 아!\" 다울러 씨가 이 지점에 이르렀을 때, 그는 오래도록 망설이고 있던 그 모퉁이를 돌아 깊이 잠들어 버렸다.\n\n마침 시계가 세 시를 칠 무렵, 크레센트 안으로 가마 하나가 바람에 떠밀리듯 들어왔으니, 안에는 다울러 부인이 타고 있었고, 한 명은 키 작고 뚱뚱하며 다른 한 명은 키 크고 비쩍 마른 두 가마꾼이 그것을 메고 있었는데, 두 사람은 가마는 말할 것도 없고 제 몸뚱이를 똑바로 가누는 데에도 여간 애를 먹지 않았다. 그러나 그 높은 지대, 게다가 바람이 마치 포석을 죄다 뜯어 올리려는 듯 빙빙 휘몰아치는 그 크레센트에서는, 바람의 기세가 어마어마했다. 그들은 가마를 내려놓고 현관문을 두 번씩 우렁차게 쾅쾅 두드릴 수 있게 되자 무척 기뻐했다.\n\n그들은 한참을 기다렸으나, 아무도 나오지 않았다.\n\n\"하인들이 잠의 신 모르페우스 품에 안겨 있나 보군.\" 키 작은 가마꾼이 곁에 선 횃불 든 소년의 횃불에 두 손을 녹이며 말했다.\n\n\"그자가 놈들을 한번 꽉 쥐어서 깨워 줬으면 좋겠구먼.\" 키 큰 가마꾼이 한마디 했다.\n\n\"미안하지만, 한 번 더 두드려 주겠어요?\" 다울러 부인이 가마 안에서 외쳤다. \"미안하지만 두세 번 두드려 줘요.\"\n\n키 작은 사내는 되도록 빨리 이 일을 끝내고 싶은 마음이 굴뚝같았으므로, 계단에 올라서서 한 번에 여덟 번이고 열 번이고 두드리는 식으로 더없이 깜짝 놀랄 만한 노크를 네댓 차례나 해 댔고, 그동안 키 큰 사내는 길로 나가 불빛이 켜지는지 창문을 올려다보았다.\n\n아무도 나오지 않았다. 모든 것이 여전히 고요하고 어두웠다.\n\n\"이런!\" 다울러 부인이 말했다. \"미안하지만 한 번 더 두드려 줘야겠어요.\"\n\n\"초인종은 없습니까, 부인?\" 키 작은 가마꾼이 말했다.\n\n\"아뇨, 있어요.\" 횃불 든 소년이 끼어들었다. \"제가 아까부터 한참이나 그걸 당겨 댔는걸요.\"\n\n\"그건 그냥 손잡이일 뿐이에요.\" 다울러 부인이 말했다. \"줄이 끊어졌거든요.\"\n\n\"하인 놈들 머리통이 끊어졌으면 좋겠구먼.\" 키 큰 사내가 으르렁거렸다.\n\n\"미안하지만 한 번 더 두드려 주는 수고를 끼쳐야겠어요.\" 다울러 부인이 더없이 정중하게 말했다.\n\n키 작은 사내는 과연 여러 차례 다시 두드렸으나, 아무런 효과도 거두지 못했다. 그러자 몹시 조바심이 난 키 큰 사내가 그와 교대하여, 미친 우체부처럼 두 번씩 우렁차게 쾅쾅 노크를 끊임없이 해 댔다.\n\n마침내 윈클 씨는 자기가 어느 클럽에 와 있는데, 회원들이 어찌나 말을 안 듣는지 의장이 질서를 잡으려고 탁자를 어지간히 두드려야 한다는 꿈을 꾸기 시작했다. 그러다가 입찰자가 하나도 없어 경매인이 모든 것을 떠안아 사들이는 경매장에 와 있는 듯한 어렴풋한 생각이 들었고, 종내에는 누군가 현관문을 두드리고 있을지도 모른다는 것이 그럭저럭 가능성의 범위 안에 든다고 여기기 시작했다. 그러나 확실히 하려고 그는 십 분쯤 침대에 가만히 누운 채 귀를 기울였고, 노크 소리를 서른두세 번쯤 헤아리고 나자 완전히 납득했으며, 자신이 이토록 잠귀가 밝은 것을 두고 스스로를 대단히 대견스러워했다.\n\n\"쾅 쾅—쾅 쾅—쾅 쾅—쿵, 쿵, 쿵, 쿵, 쿵, 쾅!\" 노커가 울려 댔다.\n\n윈클 씨는 도대체 무슨 일일까 몹시 의아해하며 침대에서 벌떡 뛰어내려, 부랴부랴 양말과 슬리퍼를 신고, 실내복을 몸에 둘둘 두른 뒤, 벽난로에서 타고 있던 골풀 등불로 납작한 양초에 불을 붙이고는 서둘러 아래층으로 내려갔다.\n\n\"드디어 누가 나오나 봐요, 부인.\" 키 작은 가마꾼이 말했다.\n\n\"내가 송곳을 들고 저놈 뒤에 있었으면 좋겠구먼.\" 키 큰 사내가 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"누구시오?\" 윈클 씨가 사슬을 풀며 외쳤다.\n\n\"질문 따위 하느라 멈추지 말고, 이 무쇠 대가리야.\" 키 큰 사내가 묻는 자를 으레 하인으로 단정하고는 잔뜩 못마땅한 투로 대꾸했다. \"문이나 열어.\"\n\n\"자, 어서 서둘러라, 나무 눈꺼풀아.\" 다른 사내가 부추기듯 덧붙였다.\n\n윈클 씨는 반쯤 잠든 상태였으므로 기계적으로 그 명령에 따라, 문을 조금 열고 밖을 내다보았다. 그가 맨 처음 본 것은 횃불 든 소년의 횃불이 내뿜는 붉은 불빛이었다. 집에 불이 난 것은 아닐까 하는 느닷없는 두려움에 깜짝 놀라, 그는 황급히 문을 활짝 열어젖히고, 양초를 머리 위로 치켜든 채 앞을 뚫어지게 노려보았으니, 자기가 본 것이 가마인지 소방차인지 영 확신이 서지 않았다. 바로 그 순간 거센 돌풍이 휘몰아쳐 불이 꺼졌고, 윈클 씨는 거역할 수 없는 힘에 떠밀려 계단 위로 떠밀려 나갔으며, 문은 요란한 소리를 내며 쾅 닫혀 버렸다.\n\n\"이런, 젊은 양반, 이제 일을 제대로 저질러 놨구먼!\" 키 작은 가마꾼이 말했다.\n\n윈클 씨는 가마 창문에 비친 한 여인의 얼굴을 언뜻 보고는 황급히 돌아서서, 있는 힘껏 노커를 두드려 대며 가마꾼들에게 가마를 도로 치워 달라고 미친 듯이 외쳤다.\n\n\"치워 줘요, 치워 줘요.\" 윈클 씨가 외쳤다. \"저기 다른 집에서 누가 나오고 있어요. 나를 가마 안에 넣어 줘요. 나를 숨겨 줘요! 어떻게 좀 해 줘요!\"\n\n이러는 내내 그는 추위에 덜덜 떨고 있었고, 노커에 손을 들어 올릴 때마다 바람이 더없이 고약하게 실내복을 들춰 댔다.\n\n\"이젠 사람들이 크레센트를 내려오고 있어요. 부인들도 함께 있고요. 뭐라도 나를 좀 덮어 줘요. 내 앞을 막아서 줘요!\" 윈클 씨가 고함쳤다. 그러나 가마꾼들은 너무 웃느라 기진맥진하여 그에게 손톱만큼의 도움도 줄 수 없었고, 부인들은 시시각각 점점 더 가까이 다가오고 있었다.\n\n윈클 씨가 마지막으로 절망적인 노크를 했다. 부인들은 불과 몇 집 떨어진 곳까지 와 있었다. 그는 이러는 내내 머리 위로 치켜들고 있던 꺼진 양초를 내던지고, 다울러 부인이 타고 있는 가마 속으로 곧장 뛰어들었다.\n\n한편 크래덕 부인은 마침내 노크 소리와 말소리를 듣고, 잠자리 모자보다 좀 더 그럴듯한 것을 머리에 쓰느라 잠깐 멈춘 것 말고는 곧장 앞쪽 응접실로 달려 내려와, 정말로 기다리던 사람들인지 확인하려 했다. 윈클 씨가 막 가마 안으로 뛰어들고 있을 때 창틀을 밀어 올리던 그녀는, 아래에서 벌어지고 있는 일을 보자마자 격렬하고 처참한 비명을 지르며, 부인이 다른 신사와 함께 달아나고 있으니 당장 일어나라고 다울러 씨에게 애원했다.\n\n이 말에 다울러 씨는 고무공처럼 별안간 침대에서 튀어 올라, 앞방으로 달려가 한쪽 창문에 다다랐는데, 마침 픽윅 씨가 다른 쪽 창문을 밀어 올린 순간이었으니, 두 사람의 눈에 맨 처음 들어온 것은 가마 안으로 뛰어드는 윈클 씨였다.\n\n\"야경꾼!\" 다울러가 노발대발하며 고함쳤다. \"저놈을 멈춰—붙잡아—꽉 붙들어—내가 내려갈 때까지 가둬 둬. 내가 저놈 목을 따 버릴 테다—칼을 가져와—이쪽 귀에서 저쪽 귀까지, 크래덕 부인—내 그렇게 하고 말겠소!\" 그리고 비명을 질러 대는 집주인 여자와 픽윅 씨에게서 몸을 뿌리치고는, 분개한 남편은 작은 만찬용 칼 하나를 움켜쥐고 거리로 뛰쳐나갔다.\n\n그러나 윈클 씨는 그를 기다리지 않았다. 그 용맹한 다울러의 끔찍한 위협을 듣자마자, 그는 뛰어들었던 것만큼이나 잽싸게 가마에서 튀어나와, 슬리퍼를 길바닥에 벗어 던지고는 부리나케 줄행랑을 쳐 크레센트를 빙 돌아 내달렸으니, 다울러와 야경꾼이 맹렬히 그 뒤를 쫓았다. 그는 계속 앞서 달렸다. 두 번째로 돌아왔을 때 문이 열려 있어, 그는 안으로 뛰어들어 다울러의 코앞에서 문을 쾅 닫고, 침실로 올라가 문을 잠그고는, 세면대와 서랍장과 탁자를 그 앞에 쌓아 올린 뒤, 동이 트는 첫 햇살과 함께 달아날 채비로 몇 가지 필수품을 챙겨 꾸렸다.\n\n다울러는 문 바깥까지 올라와, 열쇠 구멍을 통해 이튿날 윈클 씨의 목을 따고 말겠다는 확고한 결심을 천명했다. 그러고는 응접실에서 한바탕 어수선하게 목소리들이 뒤엉킨 끝에—그 가운데 화해를 붙이려 애쓰는 픽윅 씨의 목소리가 또렷이 들렸다—거주자들은 저마다의 침실로 흩어졌고, 다시 한번 모든 것이 고요해졌다.\n\n이 모든 일이 벌어지는 동안 웰러 씨는 어디 있었는지 묻고 싶어질 법도 하다. 그가 어디 있었는지는 다음 장에서 밝히겠다.\n\n「웰러 씨」 하고 크래덕 부인이 바로 이 다사다난한 날 아침에 말했다. \"당신한테 편지가 한 통 왔어요.\"\n\n\"그것참 묘한 일이군요.\" 샘이 말했다. \"뭔가 일이 잘못된 게 아닌가 걱정되는데요. 제 아는 사람들 가운데 편지 한 장이라도 쓸 줄 아는 신사 양반은 떠오르질 않아서 말입니다.\"\n\n\"뭔가 흔치 않은 일이 일어났는지도 모르죠.\" 크래덕 부인이 한마디 했다.\n\n\"제 친구들 가운데 누군가한테서 편지가 나올 정도라면, 그건 정말로 어지간히 흔치 않은 일이어야 할 겁니다.\" 샘이 미심쩍게 고개를 저으며 대답했다. \"발작을 일으킨 그 젊은 신사 양반이 말한 대로, 천재지변쯤은 돼야 한다는 거죠. 영감님한테서 온 건 아닐 테고요.\" 샘이 수신인 주소를 들여다보며 말했다. \"영감님은 늘 활자체로 쓰시거든요, 글씨를 매표소의 큼직한 광고판에서 배우셨으니까요. 지금으로선 이 편지가 대체 어디서 왔는지 알 수가 없으니, 거참 이상한 노릇이네요.\"\n\n샘은 이렇게 말하면서, 쪽지를 쓴 사람이 누구인지 확신이 서지 않을 때 수많은 사람들이 하는 짓을 했다—봉인을 보고, 그다음에 앞면을 보고, 그다음에 뒷면을 보고, 그다음에 옆면들을 보고, 그다음에 겉봉의 글씨를 보았다. 그러다가 마지막 수단으로, 차라리 안쪽을 들여다보고 거기서 알아내는 편이 낫겠다고 생각했다.\n\n\"금테 두른 종이에 썼군.\" 샘이 편지를 펼치며 말했다. \"그리고 청동색 밀랍에다 문 열쇠 대가리로 봉인을 했고. 자, 이제 읽어 보자.\" 그러고는 웰러 씨가 자못 근엄한 얼굴로 천천히 다음과 같이 읽어 내려갔다—\n\n\"배스의 정선된 하인 일동은 웰러 씨께 인사를 전하며, 오늘 저녁 친목 만찬회에 왕림하시는 기쁨을 청하나이다. 만찬은 양다리 삶은 요리에 으레 곁들이는 것들로 이루어져 있나이다. 만찬은 정각 아홉 시 반에 식탁에 오를 것이옵니다.\"\n\n이것은 또 다른 쪽지 안에 동봉되어 있었는데, 그 내용은 이러했다—\n\n\"존 스모커 씨는, 며칠 전 두 사람의 공통된 지인이신 밴텀 씨 댁에서 웰러 씨를 만나 뵙는 기쁨을 누렸던 신사인바, 이에 웰러 씨께 초대장을 동봉하여 보내드리는 바입니다. 만약 웰러 씨께서 아홉 시에 존 스모커 씨를 찾아주신다면, 존 스모커 씨는 웰러 씨를 소개해 드리는 기쁨을 누리게 될 것입니다.\n\n(서명) 존 스모커.\"\n\n봉투에는 픽윅 씨 댁의 모씨 웰러 귀하라고 적혀 있었고, 왼쪽 귀퉁이에는 괄호 안에 \"지하 출입구 초인종\"이라는 말이 심부름꾼에게 주는 안내로 적혀 있었다.\n\n\"그것참.\" 샘이 말했다. \"이건 좀 거창하게 나오는걸. 양다리 삶은 걸 만찬회라고 부르는 건 여태 들어 본 적이 없어. 그럼 구운 다리는 뭐라고 부를는지 궁금하군.\"\n\n그러나 그 점을 두고 따져 볼 겨를도 없이, 샘은 곧장 픽윅 씨 앞으로 나아가 그날 저녁의 외출 허락을 청했고, 허락은 선뜻 내려졌다. 이 허락과 길거리로 통하는 대문 열쇠를 받아 든 채, 샘 웰러는 약속 시간보다 조금 일찍 나섰고, 퀸 광장 쪽으로 느긋하게 거닐어 갔다. 광장에 다다르자마자 그는 존 스모커 씨가 조금 떨어진 곳의 가로등 기둥에 분 바른 머리를 기댄 채 호박색 물부리로 시가를 피우고 있는 모습을 보는 만족을 누렸다.\n\n\"안녕하시오, 웰러 씨?\" 존 스모커 씨가 한 손으로는 우아하게 모자를 들어 올리고 다른 한 손으로는 점잖게 거들먹거리며 부드럽게 흔들면서 말했다. \"어떻게 지내시오, 선생?\"\n\n\"뭐, 그럭저럭 회복 중입니다.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"댁은 어떻게 지내시나요, 친애하는 양반?\"\n\n\"그저 그렇소.\" 존 스모커 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"아, 너무 무리하게 일을 하셨군요.\" 샘이 한마디 했다. \"그러실까 봐 걱정했습니다. 그러시면 안 되지요, 아시잖아요. 그 타협을 모르는 기질에 너무 자신을 내맡기시면 안 됩니다.\"\n\n\"그런 것보다는요, 웰러 씨,\" 존 스모커 씨가 대답했다. \"나쁜 포도주 탓입니다. 아무래도 제가 방탕하게 지낸 모양이에요.\"\n\n\"오! 그게 원인이군요, 그렇죠?\" 샘이 말했다. \"그거참 아주 고약한 병이올시다.\"\n\n\"하지만 그 유혹이라는 게 말이오, 웰러 씨.\" 존 스모커 씨가 한마디 했다.\n\n\"아, 그렇고말고요.\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n\"사교계의 한복판 소용돌이 속에 빠져 있으니 말이오, 웰러 씨.\" 존 스모커 씨가 한숨을 쉬며 말했다.\n\n\"정말이지 끔찍한 노릇이지요!\" 샘이 맞장구쳤다.\n\n\"하지만 늘 그런 법이라오.\" 존 스모커 씨가 말했다. \"운명이 당신을 공적인 생활과 공적인 지위로 이끈다면, 다른 사람들은 면제받는 유혹에 시달릴 각오를 해야 하는 거지요, 웰러 씨.\"\n\n\"우리 삼촌이 술집 일에 발을 들였을 때 한 말이 바로 그거였어요.\" 샘이 한마디 했다. \"그리고 그 영감님 말씀이 아주 옳았지요. 석 달도 채 안 돼서 스스로 술을 퍼마시다 죽었으니까요.\"\n\n존 스모커 씨는 자신과 그 작고한 신사 양반 사이에 어떤 견줌이 그어지는 것에 깊은 분개를 내비쳤다. 하지만 샘의 얼굴이 도무지 흔들림 없는 평온의 상태에 있었으므로, 그는 마음을 고쳐먹고 다시 상냥한 표정을 지었다.\n\n\"슬슬 걸어가는 편이 낫겠소.\" 스모커 씨가 깊은 시계 주머니 바닥에 들어앉아 있다가 검은 끈—그 끝에는 구리 열쇠가 달려 있었다—의 도움으로 수면 위로 끌어올려진 구리 시계를 들여다보며 말했다.\n\n\"그러는 편이 낫겠네요.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"안 그러면 만찬이 너무 익어서 못 쓰게 될 테니까요.\"\n\n\"광천수는 마셔 보셨소, 웰러 씨?\" 그들이 하이 스트리트 쪽으로 걸어가는 동안 동행이 물었다.\n\n\"한 번요.\" 샘이 대답했다.\n\n\"어떻게 생각하셨소, 선생?\"\n\n\"유난히 역겹다고 생각했지요.\" 샘이 대답했다.\n\n\"아,\" 존 스모커 씨가 말했다. \"그 철분 맛이 마음에 안 드셨던 모양이군요?\"\n\n\"그게 뭔지는 잘 모르겠습니다만.\" 샘이 말했다. \"따뜻한 다리미 냄새가 아주 진하게 난다고 생각했지요.\"\n\n\"그게 바로 철분이라는 거요, 웰러 씨.\" 존 스모커 씨가 깔보듯 한마디 했다.\n\n\"뭐, 그렇다면 그건 영 뜻을 알 수 없는 단어로군요, 그뿐입니다.\" 샘이 말했다. \"그럴지도 모르지만, 저야 화학 쪽으로는 별로 아는 게 없으니 뭐라 할 수 없지요.\" 그러더니 여기서, 존 스모커 씨가 몹시 질겁하게도, 샘 웰러는 휘파람을 불기 시작했다.\n\n\"죄송합니다만, 웰러 씨.\" 존 스모커 씨가 그 지극히 천박한 소리에 괴로워하며 말했다. \"제 팔을 잡으시겠소?\"\n\n\"고맙습니다만, 친절은 고마우나 그 팔을 뺏지는 않겠습니다.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"저는 손을 주머니에 찔러 넣는 버릇이 좀 있어서요, 댁만 괜찮으시다면 말입니다.\" 샘은 이렇게 말하면서 말에 행동을 맞추었고, 아까보다 훨씬 더 크게 휘파람을 불었다.\n\n\"이쪽이오.\" 그의 새 친구가, 그들이 골목길로 접어들 때 눈에 띄게 안도하며 말했다. \"곧 도착할 거요.\"\n\n\"그래요?\" 배스의 정선된 하인들 가까이에 와 있다는 알림에도 전혀 동요하지 않은 채 샘이 말했다.\n\n\"그렇소.\" 존 스모커 씨가 말했다. \"겁먹지 마시오, 웰러 씨.\"\n\n\"오, 아닙니다.\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n\"아주 멋진 제복들을 보게 될 거요, 웰러 씨.\" 존 스모커 씨가 말을 이었다. \"그리고 처음에는 신사분들 몇몇이 좀 콧대를 세울지도 모릅니다만, 아시잖소, 금세 누그러질 거요.\"\n\n\"그것참 친절한 분들이군요.\" 샘이 대답했다.\n\n\"그리고 말이오,\" 존 스모커 씨가 한껏 고상하게 비호하는 태도로 다시 말을 이었다. \"아시다시피 당신은 처음 온 사람이니, 어쩌면 처음에는 좀 호되게 굴지도 몰라요.\"\n\n\"그래도 너무 잔인하게 굴진 않겠지요, 안 그래요?\" 샘이 물었다.\n\n\"아니, 아니오.\" 존 스모커 씨가 여우 머리 장식 코담뱃갑을 꺼내 신사답게 한 줌 집으며 대답했다. \"우리 가운데 우스꽝스러운 친구들이 몇 있어서, 아시다시피, 그들 나름의 농을 칠 거요. 하지만 마음에 두면 안 됩니다, 마음에 두면 안 돼요.\"\n\n\"그런 어지간한 재기의 한 방쯤이야 견뎌 보도록 애써 보겠습니다.\" 샘이 대답했다.\n\n\"그게 옳소.\" 존 스모커 씨가 여우 머리 코담뱃갑을 내밀고 제 머리를 치켜들며 말했다. \"내가 당신 편을 들어 주리다.\"\n\n이즈음 그들은 자그마한 청과물 가게에 다다랐는데, 존 스모커 씨가 그 안으로 들어섰고 샘이 그 뒤를 따랐다. 샘은 그의 등 뒤로 가는 순간, 더할 나위 없이 큼지막하고 거리낌 없는 싱글거림을 잇따라 터뜨리며, 속으로 더없이 부러울 만큼 즐거운 상태에 있음을 드러내는 갖가지 표시를 내보였다.\n\n청과물 가게를 가로질러, 그 뒤쪽 작은 통로의 계단 위에 모자를 놓아두고서, 그들은 작은 응접실로 걸어 들어갔다. 그리고 여기서 그 광경의 온전한 화려함이 웰러 씨의 눈앞에 와락 펼쳐졌다.\n\n응접실 한가운데에는 탁자 두 개가 맞붙여져 있었는데, 그 위에는 빨아 온 햇수와 시기가 제각각인 천 서너 장이 덮여 있었고, 사정이 허락하는 한 한 장처럼 보이도록 배치되어 있었다. 그 위에는 예닐곱 명 몫의 나이프와 포크가 놓여 있었다. 나이프 손잡이는 더러는 초록, 더러는 빨강, 몇 개는 노랑이었다. 그리고 포크가 죄다 검정이었으므로 색깔의 어우러짐이 자못 눈길을 끌었다. 그에 걸맞은 수의 손님 몫 접시들이 난로 가리개 뒤에서 데워지고 있었고, 손님들 자신은 그 앞에서 몸을 녹이고 있었다. 그 가운데 우두머리이자 가장 중요해 보이는 이는, 긴 자락이 달린 새빨간 코트에 선명한 붉은 반바지를 입고 삼각모를 쓴 다소 뚱뚱한 신사였다. 그는 불을 등지고 서 있었는데, 보아하니 막 들어온 듯했다. 머리에 삼각모를 그대로 쓰고 있을 뿐 아니라, 손에는 그의 직업에 속하는 신사들이 으레 마차 지붕 위로 비스듬히 치켜드는 긴 막대기를 쥐고 있었기 때문이다.\n\n\"스모커, 이 친구, 자네 손 좀 주게.\" 삼각모를 쓴 신사가 말했다.\n\n스모커 씨는 자기 오른손 새끼손가락 맨 윗마디를 삼각모를 쓴 신사의 그것에 맞물려 끼우고는, 그가 그렇게 좋아 보이니 반갑다고 말했다.\n\n\"뭐, 내가 제법 혈색이 좋아 보인다고들 하더군.\" 삼각모를 쓴 사내가 말했다. \"그것도 신통한 일이지. 지난 보름 동안 하루에 두 시간씩 우리 마님을 졸졸 따라다녔거든. 그 망할 라벤더색 헌 가운을 등 뒤에서 갈고리단추로 채우는 꼴을 줄곧 바라보고 있노라면, 그것만으로도 누구든 평생 의기소침의 구렁텅이에 빠뜨리기에 충분하지 않다면, 내 석 달 치 봉급을 떼 가도 좋아.\"\n\n이 말에 모인 정선된 인사들이 아주 호탕하게 웃었다. 그리고 마부 장식 테두리가 둘러진 노란 조끼를 입은 신사 하나가, 초록 금박을 입힌 반바지 차림의 옆 사람에게, 오늘 밤 터클이 기분이 좋군, 하고 속삭였다.\n\n\"그건 그렇고,\" 터클 씨가 말했다. \"스모커, 이 사람, 자네는—\" 그 문장의 나머지는 속삭임으로 존 스모커 씨의 귀에 전해졌다.\n\n\"오, 이런, 깜빡 잊고 있었군요.\" 존 스모커 씨가 말했다. \"여러분, 제 친구 웰러 씨입니다.\"\n\n\"불을 가려서 미안하오, 웰러.\" 터클 씨가 허물없이 고개를 까딱이며 말했다. \"춥지는 않기를 바라오, 웰러.\"\n\n\"천만에요, 불꽃 양반.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"댁 맞은편에 서 있으면서 추위를 타는 작자가 있다면 그거야말로 어지간히 냉랭한 인물일 테죠. 관청 대기실 난로 가리개 뒤에 댁을 갖다 놓으면 석탄값을 아끼겠습니다그려.\"\n\n이 응수가 터클 씨의 진홍색 제복을 두고 다소 인신공격적인 빗댐을 담은 듯 보였으므로, 그 신사는 몇 초 동안 위엄을 부렸으나, 차츰 불에서 옆으로 비켜서며 억지 미소를 짓고는 나쁘지 않다고 말했다.\n\n\"좋게 봐 주셔서 대단히 고맙습니다, 선생.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"차츰차츰 친해지겠지요, 아마. 이따가 더 나은 농담을 한번 해 보지요.\"\n\n이 대목에서 대화는 오렌지빛 플러시 천을 입은 신사 하나가 도착하는 바람에 끊겼는데, 그는 보랏빛 천을 두르고 양말을 넓게 드러낸 또 다른 정선된 인사를 데리고 왔다. 새로 온 이들이 먼저 와 있던 이들의 환영을 받자, 터클 씨가 저녁 식사를 들여오도록 하자는 안건을 내놓았고, 그것은 만장일치로 가결되었다.\n\n그러자 청과물 가게 주인과 그의 아내가 양다리 삶은 요리를 뜨끈하게, 케이퍼 소스와 순무와 감자를 곁들여 식탁에 차렸다. 터클 씨가 상석에 앉았고, 식탁 반대쪽 끝에서는 오렌지빛 플러시 천을 입은 신사가 받쳐 주었다. 청과물 가게 주인은 접시를 건네주려고 무두질한 가죽 장갑 한 켤레를 끼고 터클 씨 의자 뒤에 자리를 잡았다.\n\n\"해리스.\" 터클 씨가 명령조로 말했다.\n\n\"예, 나리.\" 청과물 가게 주인이 말했다.\n\n\"장갑은 꼈나?\"\n\n\"예, 나리.\"\n\n\"그럼 뚜껑을 벗기게.\"\n\n\"예, 나리.\"\n\n청과물 가게 주인은 자못 공손한 태도로 시키는 대로 했고, 굽실거리며 터클 씨에게 고기 써는 칼을 건넸다. 그렇게 하다가 그는 그만 무심코 하품을 했다.\n\n\"그게 무슨 짓인가, 이 사람아?\" 터클 씨가 자못 매섭게 말했다.\n\n\"죄송합니다, 나리.\" 풀 죽은 청과물 가게 주인이 대답했다. \"일부러 그런 게 아닙니다, 나리. 어젯밤에 아주 늦게까지 깨어 있었거든요, 나리.\"\n\n\"내가 자네를 어떻게 보는지 말해 주지, 해리스.\" 터클 씨가 더없이 위압적인 태도로 말했다. \"자네는 천박한 짐승이야.\"\n\n\"부디 여러분,\" 해리스가 말했다. \"저한테 너무 엄하게 굴지 말아 주십쇼, 여러분. 여러분의 후원에, 또 시중드는 일손이 더 필요할 때마다 해 주시는 추천에, 저는 정말이지 대단히 감사드리고 있습니다, 여러분. 부디 제가 만족스럽게 해 드리고 있기를 바랍니다, 여러분.\"\n\n\"아니, 그렇지 않네, 이 사람아.\" 터클 씨가 말했다. \"그러기는커녕 어림도 없지, 이 사람아.\"\n\n\"우리는 자네를 부주의한 불한당으로 여기네.\" 오렌지빛 플러시 천을 입은 신사가 말했다.\n\n\"게다가 저열한 도둑놈이지.\" 초록 금박 반바지를 입은 신사가 덧붙였다.\n\n\"또 구제 불능의 악당이고.\" 보랏빛 옷을 입은 신사가 덧붙였다.\n\n가엾은 청과물 가게 주인은 가장 옹졸한 폭정의 참된 정신으로 이런 자그마한 욕설들이 그에게 퍼부어지는 동안 아주 공손하게 머리를 조아렸다. 그리고 모두가 저마다 자신의 우월함을 내보일 만한 말을 한마디씩 하고 나자, 터클 씨가 양다리를 썰어 일동에게 나누어 주기 시작했다.\n\n저녁의 이 중대한 일이 막 시작되었나 싶을 때, 문이 활기차게 벌컥 열리며, 또 다른 신사 하나가 연푸른 양복에 납빛 단추를 달고 나타났다.\n\n\"규칙 위반이야.\" 터클 씨가 말했다. \"너무 늦었어, 너무 늦었다고.\"\n\n\"아니, 아니. 정말이지 어쩔 수 없었다네.\" 푸른 옷을 입은 신사가 말했다. \"여러분께 호소하겠네. 글쎄 연애 사건이라, 극장에서 만나기로 약속한 게 있었거든.\"\n\n\"오, 그거라면야.\" 오렌지빛 플러시 천을 입은 신사가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇다네. 정말이지, 명예를 걸고 하는 말이야.\" 푸른 옷을 입은 사내가 말했다. \"우리 집 막내딸을 열 시 반에 데리러 가겠다고 약속을 했거든. 그런데 그 아가씨가 어찌나 보기 드물게 고운 처자인지, 정말이지 차마 그 애를 실망시킬 마음이 안 나더라고. 여기 계신 분들께 무례하려는 건 아니네만, 선생, 치마폭이란—치마폭이란, 선생, 돌이킬 수 없는 거란 말이지.\"\n\n\"그쪽에 뭔가 있는 게 아닌가 슬슬 의심이 드는걸.\" 새로 온 이가 샘 옆에 자리를 잡자 터클이 말했다. \"한두 번 눈여겨봤는데, 그 아가씨가 마차에서 타고 내릴 때 자네 어깨에 아주 무겁게 기대더란 말이야.\"\n\n\"오, 정말, 정말이지, 터클, 그러면 못쓰네.\" 푸른 옷을 입은 사내가 말했다. \"그건 공정하지 못해. 내가 한두 친구한테 그 처자가 아주 신성한 여인이며 별 뚜렷한 까닭도 없이 한두 번 청혼을 거절했다고 말했을지는 몰라도, 그래도—아니, 아니, 정말 아니야, 터클—더구나 모르는 사람들 앞에서—그건 옳지 않아—그러면 못써. 신중함이야, 친애하는 친구, 신중함이라고!\" 그러고는 푸른 옷을 입은 사내가 목도리를 끌어올리고 소맷부리를 매만지면서, 마치 마음만 먹으면 더 할 말이 있지만 명예를 걸고 입을 다물고 있다는 듯 고개를 끄덕이며 눈살을 찌푸렸다.\n\n푸른 옷을 입은 사내는 머리칼이 옅고 목이 뻣뻣하며 거리낌 없고 무람없는 부류의 하인으로, 으스대는 태도에 건방진 얼굴을 하고 있어서 처음부터 웰러 씨의 각별한 주목을 끌었다. 그런데 그가 이런 식으로 본색을 드러내기 시작하자, 샘은 그 어느 때보다도 더 그와 사귀어 두고 싶은 마음이 일었다. 그래서 그는 특유의 거침없음으로 단숨에 대화에 뛰어들었다.\n\n\"건강을 위하여, 선생.\" 샘이 말했다. \"댁의 이야기가 아주 마음에 듭니다. 정말 근사하다고 생각해요.\"\n\n이 말에 푸른 옷을 입은 사내는, 흔히 들어 익숙한 칭찬이라는 듯 미소 지었다. 그러나 동시에 샘을 흐뭇하게 바라보면서, 더 잘 알고 지내게 되기를 바란다고 말했다. 빈말이 아니라 그에게는 아주 괜찮은 친구가 될 만한 자질이 엿보이고, 꼭 제 마음에 드는 사람인 듯하다는 것이었다.\n\n\"친절하시네요, 선생.\" 샘이 말했다. \"댁은 참 운 좋은 양반이올시다!\"\n\n\"그게 무슨 뜻이오?\" 푸른 옷을 입은 신사가 물었다.\n\n\"그 아가씨 말입니다.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"그 처자는 뭘 좀 아는 양반이거든요, 그렇고말고요. 아! 알겠어요.\" 웰러 씨가 한쪽 눈을 감고 머리를 좌우로 흔들었는데, 그 모양이 푸른 옷을 입은 신사의 개인적 허영심을 자못 흡족하게 했다.\n\n\"댁은 아무래도 능구렁이 같은 양반이군요, 웰러 씨.\" 그 인물이 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 아닙니다.\" 샘이 말했다. \"그런 건 죄다 댁한테 맡기렵니다. 그건 저보다 댁한테 훨씬 어울리는 일이지요. 정원 담장 옳은 쪽에 있던 신사 양반이, 미친 황소가 골목길로 올라오고 있을 때, 담장 그른 쪽에 있던 사람한테 한 말마따나요.\"\n\n\"뭐, 뭐, 웰러 씨.\" 푸른 옷을 입은 신사가 말했다. \"내 생각엔 그 아가씨가 내 분위기와 몸가짐을 눈여겨본 것 같소, 웰러 씨.\"\n\n\"그걸 못 본 척하기란 어지간히 어려웠을 겁니다.\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n\"댁도 그런 자그마한 일이 손안에 하나쯤 있소, 선생?\" 호의를 입은 푸른 옷의 신사가 조끼 주머니에서 이쑤시개를 꺼내며 물었다.\n\n\"꼭 그렇지는 않습니다.\" 샘이 말했다. \"제가 있는 데는 딸들이 없거든요. 아니면 물론 그중 하나한테 수작을 걸어 봤겠지요. 사정이 이러하니, 저는 여성 후작쯤은 돼야 만족할 수 있을 것 같습니다. 작위는 없어도 재산이 많은 젊은 여인이 저한테 아주 열렬히 사랑을 퍼부어 준다면 그럭저럭 어울려 볼 수는 있겠지만요. 그게 아니면 안 돼요.\"\n\n\"물론이지요, 웰러 씨.\" 푸른 옷을 입은 신사가 말했다. \"성가신 일에 매일 수야 없지요, 아시잖소. 그리고 우리는 압니다, 웰러 씨—세상 물정에 밝은 우리 같은 사람들은요—멋진 제복은 머지않아 여인들 사이에서 제 몫을 해낸다는 것을 말이오. 사실 우리끼리 얘기지만, 그게 이 일에 발을 들일 만한 가치가 있게 만드는 유일한 거지요.\"\n\n\"바로 그렇습니다.\" 샘이 말했다. \"그게 다지요, 물론입니다.\"\n\n이 은밀한 대화가 여기까지 이르렀을 때, 잔이 돌려졌고, 신사들은 저마다 술집 문 닫기 전에 제일 좋아하는 것을 시켰다. 일행 가운데 으뜸가는 멋쟁이인 푸른 옷의 신사와 오렌지빛 옷의 사내는 \"찬 과실주에 물을 탄 것\"을 시켰으나, 나머지 사람들 사이에서는 달콤한 진토닉이 가장 인기 있는 음료인 듯했다. 샘은 청과물 가게 주인을 \"지독한 악당\"이라고 부르며 큼지막한 펀치 한 사발을 시켰는데—이 두 가지 일이 정선된 인사들의 평가에서 그의 주가를 상당히 끌어올린 듯했다.\n\n\"여러분,\" 푸른 옷을 입은 사내가 더없이 완벽한 멋쟁이의 태도로 말했다. \"숙녀분들을 위하여 건배하겠소. 자.\"\n\n\"옳소, 옳소!\" 샘이 말했다. \"젊은 마님들을 위하여.\"\n\n이때 \"질서!\" 하고 외치는 소리가 크게 일었고, 웰러 씨를 그 자리에 소개한 신사로서 존 스모커 씨가, 그가 방금 쓴 그 말은 의회 규칙에 어긋난다고 알려 주었다.\n\n\"그게 어떤 말이었나요, 선생?\" 샘이 물었다.\n\n\"마님들이라는 말이오, 선생.\" 존 스모커 씨가 섬뜩하게 눈살을 찌푸리며 대답했다. \"여기서는 그런 구별을 인정하지 않소.\"\n\n\"오, 아주 좋습니다.\" 샘이 말했다. \"그럼 그 말을 고쳐서 그분들을 사랑스러운 여인들이라고 부르지요, 불꽃 양반이 허락해 주신다면 말입니다.\"\n\n초록 금박 반바지를 입은 신사의 마음속에는, 의장을 \"불꽃 양반\"이라 부르며 호소하는 것이 적법한지 어떤지 다소 의문이 이는 듯했다. 하지만 일동이 그의 권리보다는 자신들의 권리를 내세우는 쪽으로 더 기운 듯했으므로, 그 문제는 제기되지 않았다. 삼각모를 쓴 사내는 숨을 가쁘게 몰아쉬며 샘을 오래도록 노려보았으나, 자칫 봉변을 당할까 봐 아무 말도 안 하는 편이 낫겠다고 여긴 듯했다. 잠시 침묵이 흐른 뒤, 발뒤꿈치까지 내려오는 수놓은 코트와, 다리의 절반을 따뜻하게 덮어 주는 똑같은 천의 조끼를 입은 신사 하나가, 진토닉을 힘차게 휘젓더니, 별안간 안간힘을 써서 벌떡 일어서서는, 일동에게 몇 마디 의견을 내고 싶다고 말했다. 그러자 삼각모를 쓴 인물은, 긴 코트를 입은 사내가 내놓고자 하는 어떤 의견이든 일동이 기꺼이 듣고 싶어 할 것이 분명하다고 했다.\n\n\"여러분, 저는 나서기가 몹시 조심스럽습니다.\" 긴 코트를 입은 사내가 말했다. \"불행히도 마부 신세인 데다, 이 유쾌한 만찬회에 명예 회원으로만 받아들여진 처지인지라 말입니다. 하지만 여러분, 저는 제 자신이—이런 표현을 써도 된다면, 구석에 몰린 듯한 심정으로—제가 알게 된 어떤 통탄할 사정을 알려야 할 의무를 느낍니다. 말하자면 제 일상의 사색이 미치는 비누 거리 안에서 일어난 일이지요. 여러분, 우리 친구 위퍼스 씨가(모두가 오렌지빛 옷을 입은 인물을 바라보았다), 우리 친구 위퍼스 씨가 사직했습니다.\"\n\n만장의 경악이 듣는 이들을 덮쳤다. 신사들은 저마다 옆 사람의 얼굴을 보고는, 이어 그 시선을 벌떡 일어선 마부에게로 옮겼다.\n\n\"여러분, 놀라시는 것도 무리가 아닙니다.\" 마부가 말했다. \"저는 이 직업계에 돌이킬 수 없는 손실이 된 이번 일의 이유를 감히 말씀드리지는 못하겠습니다만, 위퍼스 씨더러 직접 그 이유를 말씀해 주십사 청하겠습니다. 그를 흠모하는 벗들의 발전과 본보기를 위해서 말이지요.\"\n\n이 제안이 큰 소리로 찬동을 얻자, 위퍼스 씨가 사정을 설명했다. 그는 분명 자기가 방금 사직한 그 자리를 계속 지키고 싶었노라고 말했다. 제복은 더없이 호화롭고 값진 것이었으며, 그 집안의 여인들은 더없이 상냥했고, 그 자리의 직무도, 정말이지 너무 고되지는 않았다고 했다. 그에게 요구된 주된 일이라고는, 역시 사직한 또 다른 신사와 함께, 되도록 자주 현관 창밖을 내다보는 것이었으니까. 그는 자신이 이제부터 늘어놓으려는 그 고통스럽고 역겨운 자초지종으로부터 일동을 면해 주고 싶었으나, 해명을 요구받은 이상, 대담하고도 또렷이 밝히는 것 말고는 달리 도리가 없다고 했다—그는 식은 고기를 먹으라는 요구를 받았던 것이다.\n\n이 고백이 듣는 이들의 가슴속에 불러일으킨 혐오감은 이루 헤아릴 수 없다. \"수치다!\" 하는 큰 외침이 신음과 야유에 뒤섞여 십오 분 동안이나 이어졌다.\n\n이어 위퍼스 씨는, 이 만행의 일부는 자기 자신의 참고 맞춰 주는 성품에서 비롯되었을지도 모른다고 덧붙였다. 그는 한때 짠 버터를 먹는 데 동의했던 일을 또렷이 기억하고 있으며, 게다가 집안에 갑작스레 환자가 생겼던 어느 때에는, 분수도 잊고서 석탄통을 이 층까지 들고 올라간 일까지 있었다는 것이다. 그는 자신의 잘못에 대한 이 솔직한 고백으로 인해 벗들이 자기를 보는 좋은 평판이 깎이지 않았기를 바라며, 또 만약 깎였다 하더라도, 방금 언급한, 자신의 감정에 가해진 그 마지막 비열한 만행에 자기가 보인 단호한 분노로 인해 다시금 좋은 평판을 되찾기를 바란다고 했다.\n\n위퍼스 씨의 연설에는 감탄의 함성으로 화답이 일었고, 그 흥미로운 순교자의 건강을 위해 더없이 열렬하게 건배가 이루어졌다. 이에 순교자는 사례하고서, 그들의 손님 웰러 씨를 위해 건배하자고 제안했다—친밀하게 알고 지내는 기쁨은 누리지 못한 신사이나, 존 스모커 씨의 친구이니, 그것이야말로 어떤 신사들의 모임이든, 그것이 어디에 있든 충분한 추천장이 되는 것이었다. 이러한 까닭에, 그는 벗들이 포도주를 마시고 있었다면 모든 예우를 갖추어 웰러 씨의 건강을 위해 건배하고 싶었을 것이나, 기분 전환 삼아 다들 독한 술을 들고 있는 데다, 건배할 때마다 잔을 비우는 것이 거북할 수도 있으니, 예우는 마음으로 갖춘 것으로 여기자고 제안했다.\n\n이 연설이 끝나자, 모두가 샘을 위하여 한 모금씩 들이켰다. 그리고 샘은 자신을 위하여 펀치 두 잔을 가득 떠서 들이켜고는, 말끔한 연설로 사례했다.\n\n\"대단히 고맙습니다, 오랜 친구분들.\" 샘이 더없이 거리낌 없는 태도로 펀치를 연신 퍼 담으며 말했다. \"이렇게 분에 넘치는 찬사를 보내 주셔서 말입니다. 이런 데서 나오는 칭찬이고 보니 그저 황송할 따름이지요. 저는 여러분에 대해 한 집단으로서 익히 들어 왔습니다만, 이렇게 막상 뵙고 보니 여러분이 이토록 보기 드물게 좋은 분들일 줄은 미처 생각도 못 했다고 말씀드리지 않을 수 없습니다. 다만 부디 몸 잘 챙기시고, 그 위엄을 조금도 손상시키지 마시기를 바랄 뿐입니다. 그 위엄이라는 게, 길을 거닐다 보면 정말이지 보기에 매력적인 것이어서, 제가 저기 계신 더없이 존경스러운 제 친구 불꽃 양반의 놋쇠 머리 막대기의 절반쯤밖에 안 되던 꼬맹이 시절부터 늘 그걸 바라보는 것이 저를 아주 행복하게 해 주었지요. 유황빛 양복을 입은 저 핍박의 희생자 양반으로 말하자면, 제가 그분께 드릴 수 있는 말은, 부디 그분이 마땅히 받을 만한 좋은 자리를 얻으시기를 바란다는 것뿐입니다. 그렇게만 된다면, 그분이 다시는 식은 만찬 따위로 시달릴 일은 거의 없을 테니까요.\"\n\n여기서 샘은 유쾌한 미소를 지으며 자리에 앉았고, 그의 연설이 떠나갈 듯한 갈채를 받자, 일동은 자리를 파했다.\n\n\"아니, 설마 가시려는 건 아니겠죠, 오랜 친구 양반?\" 샘 웰러가 그의 친구 존 스모커 씨에게 말했다.\n\n\"정말 가야 하오.\" 스모커 씨가 말했다. \"밴텀과 약속을 했거든요.\"\n\n\"오, 그렇군요.\" 샘이 말했다. \"그건 또 다른 문제지요. 댁이 바람을 맞히면 그분이 사직이라도 하실지 모르니까요. 불꽃 양반은 안 가시겠죠?\"\n\n\"아니, 나도 가야겠소.\" 삼각모를 쓴 사내가 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 펀치를 사발 가득 사 분의 삼이나 남겨 두고요!\" 샘이 말했다. \"말도 안 됩니다, 도로 앉으세요.\"\n\n터클 씨는 이 권유를 당해 내지 못했다. 그는 방금 집어 든 삼각모와 막대기를 다시 내려놓고는, 좋은 우정을 위해 한 잔 들겠노라고 말했다.\n\n푸른 옷을 입은 신사는 터클 씨와 가는 길이 같았으므로, 그 역시 못 이기는 척 머물렀다. 펀치가 절반쯤 비었을 때, 샘이 청과물 가게에서 굴을 좀 들여오게 했는데, 그 두 가지의 효과가 어찌나 흥을 돋우었던지, 터클 씨는 삼각모와 막대기로 차려입은 채 식탁 위 굴 껍데기 사이에서 개구리 혼파이프 춤을 추었고, 그동안 푸른 옷을 입은 신사는 곱슬머리 마는 종이에 머리빗을 댄, 기발한 악기로 반주를 넣었다. 마침내 펀치가 죄다 비고 밤도 거의 다 지나갔을 때, 그들은 서로 바래다주려고 우르르 밖으로 나섰다. 터클 씨는 바깥 공기를 쐬자마자 별안간 길가 연석 위에 드러눕고 싶은 욕구에 사로잡혔다. 샘은 그를 거스르는 것이 딱하다 싶어 그가 하고 싶은 대로 내버려 두었다. 삼각모를 거기 두면 못 쓰게 될 터였으므로, 샘은 자못 사려 깊게 그것을 푸른 옷을 입은 신사의 머리에 납작하게 눌러 씌우고, 큼지막한 막대기를 그의 손에 쥐여 준 다음, 그를 그의 집 대문에 기대 세워 두고 초인종을 울리고는, 조용히 집으로 걸어갔다.\n\n이튿날 아침, 픽윅 씨는 여느 때 일어나는 시각보다 훨씬 이른 시각에 옷을 말끔히 차려입고 아래층으로 내려와 초인종을 울렸다.\n\n\"샘,\" 부름에 응해 웰러 씨가 나타나자 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"문을 닫게.\"\n\n웰러 씨는 그렇게 했다.\n\n\"간밤에 여기서 불행한 일이 하나 있었네, 샘.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"그 일로 윈클 씨가 다울러 씨한테서 봉변을 당할까 봐 다소 우려할 만한 까닭이 생겼지.\"\n\n\"그 얘기라면 아래층 노부인한테서 들었습니다, 선생님.\" 샘이 대답했다.\n\n\"그런데 안타깝게도 말일세, 샘,\" 픽윅 씨가 더없이 난처한 얼굴로 말을 이었다. \"이 봉변이 두려운 나머지 윈클 씨가 떠나 버렸다네.\"\n\n\"떠나 버렸다고요!\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n\"오늘 아침 일찌감치, 나한테 미리 한마디 귀띔도 없이 집을 나갔다네.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다. \"그리고 어디로 갔는지 나는 알 수가 없어.\"\n\n\"그냥 남아서 끝장을 봤어야지요, 선생님.\" 샘이 깔보듯 대답했다. \"그 다울러라는 작자쯤 처치하는 데는 별것 안 들 텐데요, 선생님.\"\n\n\"글쎄, 샘,\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"나도 그의 대단한 용기와 결단력에 대해서는 의심이 들지도 모르겠네. 하지만 어찌 됐든, 윈클 씨는 가 버렸어. 그를 찾아내야 하네, 샘. 찾아내서 나한테 데려와야 해.\"\n\n\"그런데 그분이 돌아오지 않겠다고 하면요, 선생님?\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n\"오게 만들어야지, 샘.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"누가 그 일을 합니까, 선생님?\" 샘이 미소 지으며 물었다.\n\n\"자네지.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"아주 좋습니다, 선생님.\"\n\n이 말과 함께 웰러 씨는 방을 나갔고, 곧이어 그가 대문을 닫는 소리가 들렸다. 두 시간쯤 지나서 그는, 더없이 평범한 심부름이라도 다녀온 양 어찌나 태연한지 모를 모습으로 돌아와서는, 모든 점에서 윈클 씨의 인상착의에 들어맞는 한 인물이 그날 아침 로열 호텔에서 출발하는 지선 마차를 타고 브리스틀로 건너갔다는 정보를 가져왔다.\n\n\"샘,\" 픽윅 씨가 그의 손을 부여잡으며 말했다. \"자네는 훌륭한 친구야. 더할 나위 없이 소중한 친구일세. 자네가 그를 뒤쫓아야 하네, 샘.\"\n\n\"물론이지요, 선생님.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"그를 찾아내는 즉시 나한테 곧바로 편지를 쓰게, 샘.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"그가 자네한테서 달아나려고 하거든, 때려눕히든지 가둬 버리게. 자네한테 전권을 주는 걸세, 샘.\"\n\n\"아주 조심하겠습니다, 선생님.\" 샘이 응했다.\n\n\"그에게 전하게,\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"그가 적절하다고 여겨 취한 이 지극히 유별난 처사에, 내가 몹시 격앙되고, 몹시 언짢고, 당연히 분개하고 있다고 말일세.\"\n\n\"그러겠습니다, 선생님.\" 샘이 대답했다.\n\n\"그에게 전하게,\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"만약 그가 자네와 함께 바로 이 집으로 돌아오지 않는다면, 나와 함께 돌아오게 될 거라고. 내가 직접 가서 데려올 테니 말일세.\"\n\n\"그 말씀을 전하겠습니다, 선생님.\" 샘이 응했다.\n\n\"자네는 그를 찾아낼 수 있겠나, 샘?\" 픽윅 씨가 그의 얼굴을 진지하게 들여다보며 말했다.\n\n\"오, 그분이 어디 있기만 하다면 찾아내고말고요.\" 샘이 자못 자신만만하게 응했다.\n\n\"아주 좋아.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"그럼 빨리 갈수록 좋겠지.\"\n\n이런 지시와 함께, 픽윅 씨는 충직한 하인의 손에 얼마간의 돈을 쥐여 주고는, 그에게 즉시 브리스틀로 떠나 달아난 자를 뒤쫓으라고 일렀다.\n\n샘은 여행용 가방에 필요한 물건 몇 가지를 챙겨 넣고 떠날 채비를 마쳤다. 그런데 통로 끝까지 갔다가 걸음을 멈추고, 조용히 되돌아와서는 응접실 문 안으로 머리를 들이밀었다.\n\n\"선생님.\" 샘이 속삭였다.\n\n\"왜 그러나, 샘.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"제 지시를 제가 다 잘 알아들었지요, 선생님?\" 샘이 물었다.\n\n\"그러기를 바라네.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그 때려눕히는 일에 대해선 확실히 정해진 거지요, 선생님?\" 샘이 물었다.\n\n\"완벽하게 정해졌네.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다. \"철저하게 말이야. 필요하다고 생각하는 대로 하게. 자네한테 명령을 내렸으니.\"\n\n샘은 알아들었다는 뜻으로 고개를 끄덕이고는, 문에서 머리를 거두며, 가벼운 마음으로 자신의 순례 길에 올랐다.\n\n이미 묘사한 방식과 형태로 로열 크레슨트의 주민들을 놀라게 했던 그 예사롭지 않은 소동과 소란의 불운한 장본인이었던 별 박복한 신사는, 큰 혼란과 불안 속에 하룻밤을 보낸 뒤, 친구들이 아직 잠들어 있는 지붕 아래를 떠나, 어디로 가는지도 모른 채 길을 나섰다. 윈클 씨로 하여금 이런 조치를 취하게 만든 그 훌륭하고도 사려 깊은 마음은 아무리 높이 평가하고 아무리 따뜻하게 칭송해도 지나치지 않을 것이다. \"만일,\" 하고 윈클 씨는 혼자 추론했다. \"만일 이 다울러가 (틀림없이 그러리라 의심치 않는바) 나에게 신체적 폭력을 가하겠다는 위협을 실행에 옮기려 든다면, 나로서는 그에게 결투를 신청하지 않을 수 없게 된다. 그에게는 아내가 있다. 그 아내는 그를 사랑하고 그에게 의지하고 있다. 맙소사! 만일 내가 분노에 눈이 멀어 그를 죽이기라도 한다면, 그 후로 내 심정이 어떠하겠는가!\" 이 고통스러운 생각이 이 인정 많은 청년의 감정에 어찌나 강하게 작용했던지, 그의 두 무릎은 서로 부딪쳤고, 그의 얼굴은 내면의 격정을 드러내는 놀라운 징후들을 나타냈다. 그러한 상념에 떠밀려, 그는 자신의 융단 가방을 움켜쥐고는, 살금살금 아래층으로 내려가, 그 가증스러운 거리 쪽 문을 되도록 소리 안 나게 닫고 걸어 나갔다. 로열 호텔 쪽으로 발걸음을 옮기다가, 그는 마침 브리스틀로 출발하려는 마차를 발견했고, 브리스틀이 자기 목적에 다른 어느 곳 못지않게 적당하다고 여겨, 마부석에 올라탔다. 그리하여 하루에 두 번 이상 그 구간을 왕복하는 두 마리 말이 도착하리라고 합리적으로 기대할 만한 시간 안에 목적지에 다다랐다.\n\n그는 부시 여관에 거처를 정했고, 다울러 씨의 분노가 어느 정도 가라앉았으리라 짐작될 때까지 픽윅 씨에게 편지로 알리는 일을 미루기로 작정한 뒤, 도시를 구경하러 나섰는데, 그 도시는 그가 여태껏 본 어느 곳보다도 한층 더 지저분하다는 인상을 주었다. 부두와 선박들을 살피고 대성당을 둘러본 다음, 그는 클리프턴으로 가는 길을 물었고, 그쪽으로 안내받아 가르쳐 준 노선을 따라갔다. 그러나 브리스틀의 보도가 지상에서 가장 넓거나 깨끗한 편이 못 되는 것처럼, 그 거리들 또한 도통 가장 곧거나 가장 덜 복잡한 편이 못 되었다. 그래서 윈클 씨는 그 갖가지 굽이와 비틀림에 크게 어리둥절해져, 다시 한번 길 안내와 조언을 청할 만한 변변한 가게가 없는지 주위를 둘러보았다.\n\n그의 눈길은 새로 페인트칠을 한 한 건물에 떨어졌는데, 그것은 최근에 가게와 개인 주택의 중간쯤 되는 무언가로 개조된 것이었다. 거리 쪽 문 채광창 위로 튀어나온 붉은 등 하나만으로도 그곳이 의사의 거처임을 충분히 알려 주었을 터인데, 설령 지난날에는 응접실이었던 곳의 창문 위쪽 징두리널 바탕에 금빛 글자로 '수술'이라는 단어가 새겨져 있지 않았더라도 그러했을 것이다. 여기가 자기 질문을 던지기에 적합한 곳이라 여기고, 윈클 씨는 금빛 표딱지가 붙은 서랍들과 병들이 놓인 작은 가게 안으로 들어섰다. 그러나 거기에는 아무도 없었으므로, 그는 안쪽 응접실에 혹 있을지 모를 누군가의 주의를 끌려고 반 크라운짜리 동전으로 계산대를 두드렸다. 그 응접실이 이 가게의 가장 깊숙하고도 특별한 성소이리라고 짐작한 것은, 문에 다시금 '수술'이라는 단어가—이번에는 단조로움을 덜고자 흰 글자로—적혀 있었기 때문이다.\n\n첫 노크 소리에, 그때까지 또렷이 들려오던, 부지깽이로 펜싱이라도 하는 듯한 소리가 갑자기 멎었다. 두 번째 노크 소리에, 초록색 안경을 쓰고 매우 커다란 책 한 권을 손에 든, 학구적으로 보이는 한 젊은 신사가 조용히 가게 안으로 미끄러져 들어와, 계산대 뒤로 돌아가 손님의 용건을 알고 싶다고 청했다.\n\n\"폐를 끼쳐 죄송합니다만, 선생님,\" 윈클 씨가 말했다. \"괜찮으시다면 제게 길을 좀 가르쳐 주시겠습니까—\"\n\n\"하! 하! 하!\" 그 학구적인 젊은 신사가 큰 소리로 웃으며, 커다란 책을 공중으로 던져 올렸다가, 그것이 계산대 위의 모든 병을 산산조각 내려는 바로 그 순간에 대단한 솜씨로 낚아챘다. \"이거 한판 놀랄 일인데!\"\n\n과연 놀랄 일이었다. 윈클 씨는 그 의사 양반의 비상한 행동에 어찌나 깜짝 놀랐던지, 저도 모르게 문 쪽으로 물러섰고, 자신을 맞이하는 그 괴이한 태도에 몹시 당혹스러운 표정을 지었다.\n\n\"아니, 나를 모른단 말이오?\" 의사 양반이 말했다.\n\n윈클 씨는 그런 영광은 누리지 못했노라고 중얼거리며 대답했다.\n\n\"그렇다면,\" 의사 양반이 말했다. \"내게도 아직 희망이 있군. 운만 어지간히 따라 주면 브리스틀의 늙은 여편네 절반은 진료할 수 있겠어. 꺼져라, 이 곰팡내 나는 늙은 악당아, 꺼져!\" 커다란 책을 향해 던진 이 호통과 함께, 의사 양반은 놀라운 민첩함으로 그 책을 발로 차서 가게 저 끝으로 날려 보냈고, 초록색 안경을 벗어 던지더니, 일찍이 자치구의 가이 병원에 적을 두었으며 랜트 거리에 개인 거처를 두었던 로버트 소여 씨 바로 그 사람의 그 웃음을 씩 지어 보였다.\n\n\"나를 알아보지 못했다는 말은 아니겠죠?\" 보브 소여 씨가 다정한 온기로 윈클 씨의 손을 흔들며 말했다.\n\n\"정말이지 못 알아봤습니다.\" 윈클 씨가 그의 악수에 응하며 대답했다.\n\n\"이름을 못 봤다니 이상하군요.\" 보브 소여가 바깥쪽 문으로 친구의 주의를 끌며 말했는데, 그 문에는 똑같은 흰 페인트로 '소여, 전 녹케모프'라는 글자가 새겨져 있었다.\n\n\"그건 제 눈에 안 띄었어요.\" 윈클 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"이런, 당신인 줄 알았더라면, 뛰쳐나가 두 팔로 끌어안았을 텐데,\" 보브 소여가 말했다. \"그런데 정말이지, 나는 당신이 국세청 사람인 줄로만 알았다니까요.\"\n\n\"설마요!\" 윈클 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"정말 그랬다니까요.\" 보브 소여가 응수했다. \"마침 집에 없다고 말하려던 참이었어요. 그래도 전할 말씀이 있으면 내가 꼭 나 자신에게 전해 주겠노라고 말이오. 그자는 나를 모르거든요. 가로등·포장 관리국도 마찬가지고. 교회세 쪽은 내가 누군지 어렴풋이 짐작하는 것 같고, 수도국 쪽은 분명히 알지요. 왜냐하면 내가 여기 처음 내려왔을 때 그 사람 이를 하나 뽑아 줬으니까. 어쨌든 들어와요, 들어와!\" 이런 식으로 재잘대며, 보브 소여 씨는 윈클 씨를 안쪽 방으로 밀어 넣었는데, 거기에는 다름 아닌 벤저민 앨런 씨가, 시뻘겋게 달군 부지깽이로 벽난로 선반에 자그마한 둥근 동굴들을 뚫으며 혼자 즐기고 앉아 있었다.\n\n\"이야!\" 윈클 씨가 말했다. \"이거 정말 뜻밖의 기쁨이군요. 참으로 멋진 곳을 가지셨네요!\"\n\n\"그저 그렇소, 그저 그래요.\" 보브 소여가 대답했다. \"그 진귀한 파티가 있고 나서 얼마 안 되어 시험에 통과했고, 친구들이 이 사업에 필요한 돈을 대 줘서 말이오. 그래서 검은 양복 한 벌에 안경 한 쌍을 걸치고 여기 내려와, 되도록 근엄해 보이려고 하는 거랍니다.\"\n\n\"그리고 분명 아주 아늑하고 자그마한 사업을 하고 계시겠죠?\" 윈클 씨가 아는 체하며 말했다.\n\n\"아주 그렇소.\" 보브 소여가 대답했다. \"어찌나 아늑한지, 몇 해가 지나고 나면 그동안 번 이문을 전부 포도주잔 하나에 담아 까치밥나무 잎사귀 한 장으로 덮어 둘 수 있을 정도라오.\"\n\n\"설마 진심으로 하는 말은 아니겠죠?\" 윈클 씨가 말했다. \"재고만 해도—\"\n\n\"눈속임이라오, 이 친구야.\" 보브 소여가 말했다. \"서랍의 절반은 안에 아무것도 없고, 나머지 절반은 아예 열리지도 않소.\"\n\n\"말도 안 돼요!\" 윈클 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"사실이오—명예를 걸고!\" 보브 소여가 응수하며, 가게로 나가서 가짜 서랍에 달린 자그마한 금빛 손잡이들을 몇 번이고 세게 잡아당겨 그 단언이 참임을 입증해 보였다. \"이 가게에 진짜라곤 거머리밖에 없는데, 그것도 중고품이라오.\"\n\n\"전혀 그런 줄 몰랐는데요!\" 윈클 씨가 몹시 놀라 외쳤다.\n\n\"몰랐어야지요.\" 보브 소여가 대답했다. \"안 그러면 겉모양을 꾸민 보람이 어디 있겠소, 안 그래요? 한데 뭘 좀 드시겠소? 우리 하는 대로 하시겠소? 그게 좋지요. 벤, 이 멋진 친구야, 찬장에 손 넣어서 특허 소화제 좀 꺼내 주게.\"\n\n벤저민 앨런 씨는 기꺼이 그러겠다는 듯 미소 지으며, 팔꿈치 옆 벽장에서 브랜디가 반쯤 든 검은 병 하나를 꺼냈다.\n\n\"물은 안 타시겠죠, 물론?\" 보브 소여가 말했다.\n\n\"고맙습니다만,\" 윈클 씨가 대답했다. \"시간이 좀 이르니까요. 괜찮으시다면 좀 묽게 타고 싶습니다.\"\n\n\"양심에 거리낌만 없다면야 조금도 상관없소.\" 보브 소여가, 말하는 동안에도 그 술 한 잔을 아주 맛나게 쭉 들이켜며 대답했다. \"벤, 그 작은 냄비!\"\n\n벤저민 앨런 씨는 같은 은닉처에서 자그마한 놋쇠 냄비 하나를 꺼냈는데, 보브 소여는 그것이 특히나 사업장다워 보인다는 이유로 자랑스럽게 여긴다고 말했다. 보브 소여 씨가 '소다수'라고 표딱지를 붙인, 열리게 되어 있는 창가 의자에서 꺼낸 자그마한 석탄 몇 삽으로 그 직업용 냄비 속 물을 한참 끓여 내자, 윈클 씨는 자기 브랜디에 그것을 섞었다. 그리고 대화가 두루 무르익어 가던 차에, 수수한 회색 제복에 금줄 두른 모자를 쓰고, 뚜껑 덮인 자그마한 바구니를 옆구리에 낀 한 소년이 가게로 들어서면서 대화가 끊겼다. 보브 소여 씨가 그 소년을 즉시 불러 세웠다. \"톰, 이 부랑아 녀석, 이리 와.\"\n\n소년이 그에 맞춰 앞으로 나섰다.\n\n\"브리스틀의 기둥이란 기둥은 죄다 멈춰 서서 '뛰어넘기'를 하고 다녔지, 이 게으른 망나니 녀석!\" 보브 소여 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"아닙니다, 선생님, 안 그랬어요.\" 소년이 대답했다.\n\n\"안 그러는 게 좋을 거다!\" 보브 소여 씨가 위협하는 기색으로 말했다. \"심부름하는 녀석이 도랑에서 구슬치기를 하거나 한길에서 가터놀이를 하는 꼴을 보고서, 그 어느 누가 의사 양반에게 일을 맡기겠느냐? 너는 네 직업에 대한 자긍심도 없느냐, 이 비굴한 녀석아? 약은 다 전했냐?\"\n\n\"네, 선생님.\"\n\n\"새 식구 들어온 큰 저택에 보내는 아이 가루약하고, 통풍 든 다리에 성질 고약한 노신사한테 하루 네 번 먹으라는 알약 말이다?\"\n\n\"네, 선생님.\"\n\n\"그럼 문 닫고 가게나 지키고 있어라.\"\n\n\"이거 보세요,\" 소년이 물러가자 윈클 씨가 말했다. \"내게 믿게 하려던 만큼 형편이 그리 나쁜 것도 아니잖아요. 그래도 내보낼 약이 좀 있지 않습니까.\"\n\n보브 소여 씨는 엿듣고 있는 낯선 이가 없는지 가게 안을 슬쩍 살피고는, 윈클 씨 쪽으로 몸을 기울이며 나직한 목소리로 말했다—\n\n\"그 녀석은 그걸 죄다 엉뚱한 집에 두고 온다오.\"\n\n윈클 씨가 어리둥절한 표정을 짓자, 보브 소여와 그의 친구가 웃었다.\n\n\"모르겠소?\" 보브가 말했다. \"그 녀석이 어느 집에 가서 지하실 초인종을 울리고, 받는 사람 이름도 안 적힌 약 꾸러미를 하인 손에 쥐여 주고는 가 버린단 말이오. 하인은 그걸 식당으로 가져가지. 주인이 그걸 뜯어 표딱지를 읽어요. '취침 시 복용할 물약—알약은 종전대로—물약은 평소대로—가루약. 소여, 전 녹케모프 약방에서. 의사 처방전 정성껏 조제함', 그 밖에 어쩌고저쩌고 다 적혀 있지. 그걸 아내한테 보여 주면, 아내가 표딱지를 읽고, 그게 하인들한테까지 내려가서, 그들도 표딱지를 읽소. 이튿날 그 녀석이 와서 이러지. '대단히 죄송합니다—저의 실수입니다—워낙 일이 많아서—배달할 꾸러미가 무척 많거든요—소여 씨가 안부 전하랍니다—전 녹케모프 약방요.' 그렇게 이름이 알려지는 거요. 그게 바로 의술 쪽에서 통하는 비결이지, 이 친구야. 정말이지, 이 사람아, 그게 세상 어떤 광고보다도 낫소. 4온스짜리 병 하나는 브리스틀 집들의 절반은 다녀왔는데도 아직도 끝이 안 났다오.\"\n\n\"이런, 알겠어요.\" 윈클 씨가 말했다. \"정말 기막힌 방법이군요!\"\n\n\"오, 벤하고 나는 그런 수법을 열두 가지나 생각해 냈다오.\" 보브 소여가 신이 나서 대답했다. \"가로등지기한테는 일주일에 1실링 6펜스를 주고, 순찰 돌 때마다 야간 초인종을 십 분씩 잡아당기게 하지. 그리고 우리 심부름 녀석은 늘 찬송가 부르기 직전에 교회로 뛰어 들어가는데, 사람들이 마침 두리번거리는 것 말고는 할 일이 없을 그때, 얼굴에 공포와 낭패를 잔뜩 그린 채로 나를 불러내는 거요. 그러면 다들 이러지. '아이고 저런, 누가 갑자기 병이 났나 보군! 소여, 전 녹케모프 약방을 부르러 왔구먼. 저 젊은 양반은 일이 어찌나 많은지!'\"\n\n이렇게 의술의 비밀 몇 가지를 폭로한 끝에, 보브 소여 씨와 그의 친구 벤 앨런은 저마다 의자에 등을 기대고 떠들썩하게 웃어 댔다. 마음껏 그 농담을 즐기고 나자, 화제는 윈클 씨가 한결 직접적으로 관심을 가진 주제로 옮겨 갔다.\n\n벤저민 앨런 씨가 브랜디를 마신 뒤에는 감상적이 되는 버릇이 있다는 점은, 우리가 다른 데서 이미 넌지시 일러 둔 듯하다. 그런 경우는 별스러운 일이 아니니, 우리 자신도 몇 차례 그와 비슷한 증세에 시달리는 환자들을 다뤄 본 적이 있어 증언할 수 있다. 그의 생애에서 바로 이 무렵, 벤저민 앨런 씨는 일찍이 알았던 것보다도 어쩌면 더 심하게 감상에 빠지기 쉬운 상태였는데, 그 병의 원인은 간단히 이러했다. 그는 보브 소여 씨와 거의 삼 주 가까이 함께 지내고 있었다. 보브 소여 씨는 절제로 이름난 사람이 아니었고, 벤저민 앨런 씨 역시 머리가 썩 튼튼한 편이 못 되었다. 그 결과, 방금 말한 그 기간 내내 벤저민 앨런 씨는 절반쯤 취한 상태와 완전히 취한 상태 사이를 오락가락하고 있었던 것이다.\n\n\"이보게, 친구,\" 하고 벤 앨런 씨가, 앞서 말한 그 중고 거머리를 좀 내주려고 계산대 뒤로 잠시 자리를 비운 보브 소여 씨의 부재를 틈타 말했다. \"이보게, 친구, 나는 무척 비참하다네.\"\n\n윈클 씨는 그 말을 듣고 진심으로 안됐다는 뜻을 표하며, 고통받는 이 학도의 슬픔을 덜어 줄 무슨 일이라도 자기가 할 수 있는지 알고 싶다고 청했다.\n\n\"아무것도 없네, 이 사람아, 아무것도.\" 벤이 말했다. \"아라벨라 기억하나, 윈클? 내 누이동생 아라벨라 말일세—자그마한 처녀였지, 윈클, 검은 눈을 한—우리가 와들네 집에 내려가 있었을 때 말이야. 자네가 그 애를 눈여겨봤는지는 모르겠네만—참 어여쁜 처녀였지, 윈클. 어쩌면 내 얼굴 생김새가 그 애 얼굴을 자네 기억에 떠올려 줄지도 모르겠군?\"\n\n윈클 씨는 그 사랑스러운 아라벨라를 마음속에 떠올리는 데 아무것도 필요치 않았다. 그게 도리어 다행이었으니, 그녀의 오라비 벤저민의 얼굴 생김새는 분명 그의 기억을 되살려 주는 데 영 신통찮은 자극제였을 것이기 때문이다. 그는 애써 침착함을 가장하여, 말씀하신 그 젊은 숙녀를 똑똑히 기억하며, 그녀가 부디 건강하기를 진심으로 바란다고 대답했다.\n\n\"우리 친구 보브는 유쾌한 친구지, 윈클.\" 벤 앨런 씨의 대답이라곤 이것뿐이었다.\n\n\"아주 그렇죠.\" 윈클 씨가, 두 이름이 이렇게 가깝게 엮이는 것이 그리 달갑지 않은 채로 말했다.\n\n\"나는 그 둘을 짝지어 줄 작정이었네. 그 둘은 서로를 위해 만들어졌고, 서로를 위해 세상에 보내졌고, 서로를 위해 태어난 사이라고, 윈클.\" 벤 앨런 씨가 잔을 힘주어 내려놓으며 말했다. \"이건 특별한 운명이 깃든 일이라네, 이 사람아. 둘은 나이 차가 다섯 살밖에 안 나고, 둘 다 생일이 팔월이거든.\"\n\n윈클 씨는 그다음에 무슨 말이 이어질지 너무도 듣고 싶은 나머지, 그 비범한 우연이 아무리 놀랍다 한들 별로 놀라움을 드러내지 못했다. 그래서 벤 앨런 씨는 눈물 한두 방울을 흘린 뒤, 자기가 친구에게 품은 그 모든 존경과 경의와 흠모에도 불구하고, 아라벨라가 알 수 없게도, 또 도리에 어긋나게도, 그의 인품에 더없이 단호한 반감을 드러냈노라고 말을 이었다.\n\n\"그래서 내 생각엔,\" 벤 앨런 씨가 결론짓듯 말했다. \"내 생각엔 이미 마음 둔 사람이 따로 있는 것 같네.\"\n\n\"그 상대가 누구일지 짐작 가는 데가 있습니까?\" 윈클 씨가 몹시 떨면서 물었다.\n\n벤 앨런 씨는 부지깽이를 거머쥐고는, 그것을 머리 위로 호전적으로 휘두르며, 상상 속 두개골에 사나운 일격을 가하더니, 매우 의미심장한 태도로 마무리하기를, 그저 그게 누군지 짐작이라도 갈 수 있으면 좋으련만, 그뿐이라고 했다.\n\n\"내가 그놈을 어떻게 생각하는지 똑똑히 보여 줄 텐데.\" 벤 앨런 씨가 말했다. 그러면서 부지깽이가 다시 한번, 아까보다도 더 사납게 휘둘러졌다.\n\n이 모든 것이 물론 윈클 씨의 심정에는 무척이나 위안이 되었으니, 그는 몇 분 동안 잠자코 있었다. 그러나 마침내 결심을 다잡고, 앨런 양이 켄트에 있느냐고 물었다.\n\n\"아니, 아닐세.\" 벤 앨런 씨가 부지깽이를 옆으로 치워 놓고 자못 교활한 표정을 지으며 말했다. \"고집 센 처녀에겐 와들네 집이 꼭 알맞은 데라곤 생각지 않았네. 그래서, 부모님이 돌아가신 마당에 내가 그 애의 마땅한 보호자요 후견인인지라, 그 애를 이 지방으로 데려와서, 어느 늙은 고모님 댁의, 아주 따분하고 답답한 곳에서 몇 달 지내게 하고 있다네. 그러면 그 애가 나으리라 보네, 이 사람아. 그래도 안 되면, 잠시 외국에 데려가서 그게 어떤지 두고 볼 참이야.\"\n\n\"오, 그 고모님 댁이 브리스틀에 있는 거군요?\" 윈클 씨가 더듬거렸다.\n\n\"아니, 아니, 브리스틀은 아닐세.\" 벤 앨런 씨가 오른쪽 어깨 너머로 엄지손가락을 홱 젖히며 대답했다. \"저쪽이지—저 아래 말이야. 그런데 쉿, 보브가 오네. 한마디도 말게, 이 사람아, 한마디도.\"\n\n이 대화는 짧았지만, 윈클 씨의 마음속에 더없이 높은 정도의 흥분과 불안을 불러일으켰다. 이미 마음 둔 사람이 있으리라는 그 의심이 그의 가슴속에 사무쳤다. 혹 그 상대가 자기일 수 있을까? 어여쁜 아라벨라가 발랄한 보브 소여를 멸시하듯 바라본 것이 혹 자기 때문이었을까, 아니면 자기에게 성공한 연적이 따로 있는 것일까? 그는 어떤 대가를 치르든 그녀를 만나 보기로 결심했다. 그러나 여기서 넘을 수 없는 난관이 하나 나타났으니, 벤 앨런 씨가 설명조로 한 그 '저쪽'이며 '저 아래'라는 말이 삼 마일을 뜻하는지, 삼십 마일을 뜻하는지, 아니면 삼백 마일을 뜻하는지 그로서는 도무지 짐작할 길이 없었던 것이다.\n\n하지만 당장은 자기 연정을 곰곰이 곱씹을 겨를이 없었으니, 보브 소여가 돌아온 직후 곧바로 빵집에서 고기 파이가 도착했고, 그 양반이 함께 남아 들자고 우긴 까닭이다. 보브 소여 씨의 가정부 노릇을 하는 한 임시 잡역부 여자가 식탁보를 깔았고, 회색 제복을 입은 그 소년의 어머니한테서 세 번째 칼과 포크를 빌려 온 뒤(소여 씨의 살림살이는 아직 옹색한 규모로 꾸려지고 있었으므로), 그들은 자리에 앉아 식사를 했다. 맥주는, 소여 씨의 말마따나 '제 본래의 백랍 잔에 담겨' 나왔다.\n\n식사 후, 보브 소여 씨는 가게에서 제일 큰 약절구를 들여오게 하더니, 그 안에서 김이 모락모락 나는 럼 펀치 한 사발을 빚기 시작했는데, 약공이 같은 솜씨로 자못 그럴듯하게 재료를 휘저어 섞어 냈다. 소여 씨는 독신이라 집에 텀블러가 하나밖에 없었는데, 그것은 손님에 대한 예우로 윈클 씨에게 배정되었고, 벤 앨런 씨에게는 좁은 끝에 코르크를 박은 깔때기가 주어졌으며, 보브 소여는 약제사들이 처방을 조제할 때 액체 약을 따라 재는 데 흔히 쓰는, 입구가 넓고 갖가지 알 수 없는 부호들이 새겨진 그 수정 그릇 가운데 하나로 만족했다. 이런 준비가 갖추어지자, 펀치를 맛보아 훌륭하다는 평을 내렸다. 그리고 보브 소여와 벤 앨런은 윈클 씨가 한 잔 채울 동안 두 잔씩 채워도 무방하다고 정해 두고서, 다 같이 큰 만족과 우애 속에 공평하게 시작했다.\n\n노래는 없었으니, 보브 소여 씨가 그건 의사다워 보이지 않는다고 했기 때문이다. 그러나 그 박탈을 벌충하느라 떠들고 웃는 소리가 어찌나 컸던지, 거리 끝까지 들렸을 만했고, 십중팔구 실제로도 들렸을 것이다. 그 대화는 보브 소여 씨네 심부름 소년의 시간을 한결 가볍게 해 주었고 그의 머리도 깨우쳐 주었으니, 그 소년은 평소 하던 대로 계산대에 제 이름을 썼다 다시 지우는 일로 저녁을 보내는 대신, 유리문 너머로 엿보며 듣는 동시에 구경까지 했던 것이다.\n\n보브 소여 씨의 흥취가 빠르게 광적으로 무르익어 가고, 벤 앨런 씨는 다시금 감상에 빠져들며, 펀치는 거의 다 동나 갈 무렵, 심부름 소년이 황급히 뛰어 들어와 알리기를, 한 젊은 여자가 방금 건너와서 두 블록 떨어진 곳에 소여, 전 녹케모프 약방을 당장 찾는다고 전했다는 것이었다. 이로써 모임이 파했다. 보브 소여 씨는 그 전갈을 스무 번쯤 되풀이해 듣고서야 알아듣고는, 정신을 차리려고 젖은 헝겊을 머리에 동여맸고, 어느 정도 효험을 본 뒤 초록색 안경을 쓰고 나섰다. 돌아올 때까지 기다리라는 온갖 간청을 뿌리치고, 또 벤 앨런 씨를 붙들고 자기 마음에 가장 사무친 화제로든 아니 실은 그 어떤 화제로든 알아들을 만한 대화를 나누기란 도무지 불가능함을 알아챈 윈클 씨는, 자리를 떠나 부시 여관으로 돌아왔다.\n\n마음속 불안과 아라벨라가 불러일으킨 숱한 상념 탓에, 그가 마신 그 약절구 펀치는 다른 상황에서라면 미쳤을 효과를 그에게 미치지 못했다. 그래서 그는 바에서 소다수에 브랜디를 탄 술 한 잔을 마신 뒤, 그날 저녁의 일들로 고양되기는커녕 도리어 풀이 죽은 채 커피실로 들어갔다.\n\n벽난로 앞에, 그에게 등을 돌리고 앉아 있는 것은, 두꺼운 외투를 걸친 키가 큰 편인 한 신사였으니, 그 방에 있는 다른 사람은 그뿐이었다. 그해 그 계절치고는 꽤 쌀쌀한 저녁이라, 그 신사는 새로 들어온 사람에게 불을 쬘 수 있도록 의자를 옆으로 비켜 놓았다. 그런데 그렇게 하면서 그 복수심에 불타고 피에 굶주린 다울러의 얼굴과 모습이 드러났을 때, 윈클 씨의 심정이 어떠했겠는가!\n\n윈클 씨의 첫 충동은 가장 가까운 초인종 손잡이를 힘껏 잡아당기는 것이었으나, 공교롭게도 그것은 마침 다울러 씨의 머리 바로 뒤에 있었다. 그는 그쪽으로 한 발짝 내디뎠다가 스스로를 제지했다. 그가 그러는 사이, 다울러 씨가 몹시 황급히 뒤로 물러섰다.\n\n\"윈클 씨. 진정하시오. 나를 치지 마시오. 나는 참지 않겠소. 주먹질이라니! 절대 안 되오!\" 다울러 씨가, 그 사나운 성격의 신사에게서 윈클 씨가 예상했던 것보다 한결 온순한 얼굴로 말했다.\n\n\"주먹질이라뇨, 선생님?\" 윈클 씨가 더듬거렸다.\n\n\"주먹질 말이오, 선생.\" 다울러가 대답했다. \"마음을 가라앉히시오. 앉으시오. 내 말을 들어 보시오.\"\n\n\"선생님,\" 윈클 씨가 머리끝부터 발끝까지 떨면서 말했다. \"급사도 없는 자리에서 당신 곁에, 혹은 당신 맞은편에 앉기로 응하기 전에, 나는 무언가 더 분명한 다짐으로 안심을 얻어야겠소. 당신은 어젯밤 나에게 위협을 가했소, 선생, 끔찍한 위협을 말이오.\" 여기서 윈클 씨는 정말이지 새파랗게 질려 말을 뚝 끊었다.\n\n\"그랬소.\" 다울러가, 윈클 씨 못지않게 새하얀 얼굴로 말했다. \"정황이 수상했소. 그게 해명되었소. 나는 당신의 용기를 존경하오. 당신의 마음은 올곧소. 떳떳한 결백 말이오. 자, 내 손이오. 잡으시오.\"\n\n\"실은, 선생님,\" 윈클 씨가 손을 내밀어야 할지 말아야 할지 망설이며, 혹 자기가 허를 찔리게 하려고 손을 청하는 게 아닐까 거의 두려워하며 말했다. \"실은, 선생님, 저는—\"\n\n\"무슨 말인지 알겠소.\" 다울러가 가로막았다. \"억울하게 느끼는 거지. 지극히 당연하오. 나라도 그랬을 거요. 내가 잘못했소. 사과하오. 우리 친구가 됩시다. 나를 용서하시오.\" 이 말과 함께, 다울러는 거의 강제로 자기 손을 윈클 씨의 손에 쥐여 주고는, 더없이 격렬하게 그것을 흔들어 대며, 윈클 씨야말로 더없이 기개 있는 사람이며 자기는 어느 때보다도 그를 높이 평가하게 되었노라고 선언했다.\n\n\"자,\" 다울러가 말했다. \"앉으시오. 다 이야기해 보시오. 어떻게 나를 찾았소? 언제부터 뒤를 따라왔소? 솔직히 말해 보시오. 내게 들려주시오.\"\n\n\"순전히 우연입니다.\" 윈클 씨가, 이 만남의 야릇하고도 뜻밖인 성격에 몹시 당혹스러워하며 대답했다. \"순전히요.\"\n\n\"다행이오.\" 다울러가 말했다. \"오늘 아침 잠에서 깼더니, 내가 한 위협을 까맣게 잊고 있었소. 그 일을 떠올리며 웃었지. 친근한 마음이 들었소. 그렇게 말했다오.\"\n\n\"누구한테요?\" 윈클 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"다울러 부인한테. '당신, 맹세를 했잖아요' 하고 그녀가 말합디다. '그랬지' 하고 내가 말했소. '그건 경솔한 맹세였어요' 하고 그녀가 말했소. '그랬지' 하고 내가 말했소. '내가 사과하리다. 그 사람 어디 있소?'\"\n\n\"누구 말이오?\" 윈클 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"당신 말이오.\" 다울러가 대답했다. \"나는 아래층으로 내려갔소. 당신은 어디서도 찾을 수 없었지. 픽윅은 침울한 표정이었소. 고개를 절레절레 흔들면서, 무슨 폭력 사태가 벌어지지 않기를 바란다고 합디다. 나는 전부 알아챘소. 당신은 모욕을 당했다고 느꼈던 거요. 친구를 데리러, 어쩌면 갔겠지. 어쩌면 권총을 구하러 갔는지도. '대단한 기개야' 하고 내가 말했소. '나는 그를 존경하오' 하고.\"\n\n윈클 씨는 헛기침을 하더니, 사정이 어떻게 돌아가는지 알아채기 시작하며, 거드름 피우는 표정을 지었다.\n\n\"나는 당신 앞으로 쪽지를 남겼소.\" 다울러가 말을 이었다. \"미안하다고 적었지. 정말 그랬소. 급한 용무가 있어 나는 이리로 왔던 거요. 당신은 흡족하지 않았던 거고. 그래서 뒤따라왔지. 직접 말로 듣는 해명을 원했던 거요. 그건 옳았소. 이제 다 끝났소. 내 용무는 마무리됐소. 나는 내일 돌아가오. 같이 갑시다.\"\n\n다울러가 해명을 이어 갈수록, 윈클 씨의 표정은 점점 더 위엄을 띠어 갔다. 두 사람 대화가 처음에 어찌하여 그토록 알쏭달쏭하게 시작되었는지가 이제 밝혀졌다. 다울러 씨도 그 자신 못지않게 결투를 질색했던 것이다. 요컨대 이 허세 가득하고 무시무시한 인물은 세상에 둘도 없는 지독한 겁쟁이 가운데 하나여서, 윈클 씨의 부재를 제 두려움이라는 잣대로 해석한 나머지, 자기와 똑같은 조치를 취해, 감정의 흥분이 죄다 가라앉을 때까지 신중하게 몸을 피해 있었던 것이다.\n\n사태의 진상이 윈클 씨의 머릿속에 차츰 밝아 오자, 그는 자못 무시무시한 표정을 지으며 자기는 더없이 흡족하다고 말했다. 그러나 동시에, 그렇게 말하는 그의 태도는, 만일 자기가 흡족하지 않았더라면 무언가 더없이 끔찍하고 파괴적인 일이 틀림없이 벌어졌으리라고 다울러 씨가 미루어 짐작하지 않을 수 없게 하는 것이었다. 다울러 씨는 윈클 씨의 도량과 너그러움에 걸맞은 감명을 받은 듯했다. 그리하여 두 교전자는 영원한 우정을 거듭 다짐하며 그날 밤 헤어졌다.\n\n열두 시 반쯤, 윈클 씨가 첫잠의 더없는 호사에 이십 분쯤 흠뻑 빠져 있을 때, 그는 자기 방문을 쾅쾅 두드리는 요란한 소리에 별안간 깨어났다. 그 소리가 한층 더 격렬하게 되풀이되자, 그는 침대에서 벌떡 일어나 거기 누구냐고, 무슨 일이냐고 물었다.\n\n\"저, 선생님, 여기 젊은 사람이 와서 당장 선생님을 뵈어야 한다는데요.\" 객실 하녀의 목소리가 대답했다.\n\n\"젊은 사람이라고!\" 윈클 씨가 외쳤다.\n\n\"그 점에 대해선 추호도 틀림없습니다, 나리.\" 또 다른 목소리가 열쇠 구멍을 통해 대답했다. \"그리고 만약 바로 그 흥미로운 젊은 양반을 지체 없이 들이지 않으시면, 그 양반 얼굴보다 다리가 먼저 들어올지도 모르는 일이고말굽쇼.\" 젊은 사람은 이 암시를 입 밖에 내고 나서, 그 말에 무게와 요점을 더하기라도 하려는 듯, 문 아래쪽 널빤지 하나를 슬쩍 걷어찼다.\n\n\"자네인가, 샘?\" 윈클 씨가 침대에서 펄쩍 뛰어 일어나며 물었다.\n\n\"어떤 신사분이든 직접 쳐다보지 않고서야 마음에 흡족할 만큼 알아본다는 건 도무지 불가능한 노릇입죠, 나리.\" 그 목소리가 단정적으로 대답했다.\n\n그 젊은 사람이 누군지 의심할 여지가 별로 없었던 윈클 씨는 문을 열었다. 그가 문을 열기가 무섭게 새뮤얼 웰러 씨가 몹시 황급히 들어와서는, 안쪽에서 도로 정성껏 문을 잠그더니, 천연덕스럽게 열쇠를 조끼 주머니에 넣었다. 그러고는 윈클 씨를 머리끝부터 발끝까지 훑어본 뒤 말했다—\n\n\"나리는 참 익살맞은 젊은 신사분이십니다그려, 그렇고말굽쇼, 나리!\"\n\n\"이게 무슨 행동인가, 샘?\" 윈클 씨가 분개하여 물었다. \"당장 나가게, 이 사람아. 무슨 뜻인가, 이 사람아?\"\n\n\"무슨 뜻이냐굽쇼,\" 샘이 응수했다. \"이거 보십쇼, 나리, 이건 좀 너무 기름진뎁쇼, 어느 젊은 아가씨가 빵집 주인한테 따지면서 한 말마따나요, 그 양반이 속에 비곗덩어리밖에 안 든 돼지고기 파이를 팔아먹고 나서 말입죠. 무슨 뜻이냐고요! 거 참, 그것도 제법 그럴듯한 농담입니다요, 그렇고말굽쇼.\"\n\n\"그 문을 열고 당장 이 방에서 나가게, 이 사람아.\" 윈클 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"제가 이 방을 나가는 건요, 나리, 딱 나리께서 나가시는 바로 그 순간에 나갈 겁니다요.\" 샘이 단호한 어조로 말하며, 더없이 진지하게 자리에 앉았다. \"만약 나리를 들쳐 업고 모셔 가야 할 형편이 되면야, 물론 나리보다 아주 조금 먼저 나가게 되겠습죠. 하지만 부디 저를 극단으로 몰아가지 않으시길 바랍니다요. 이렇게 말씀드리는 건, 그 귀족 양반이 성질 고약한 경단고둥한테 한 말을 그냥 인용한 것뿐입니다요. 핀으로 쑤셔도 껍데기에서 안 나오니까, 결국 응접실 문짝에 끼워 깨뜨려야 하나 보다 걱정하기 시작했을 때 말입죠.\" 그로서는 유난히 긴 이 연설을 마치고서, 웰러 씨는 두 손을 무릎에 짚고, 자기가 만만하게 놀아날 생각이 추호도 없음을 똑똑히 드러내는 표정으로 윈클 씨의 얼굴을 정면으로 마주 보았다.\n\n\"나리는 참으로 상냥한 마음씨를 지닌 젊은 분이올시다, 그것도 못 믿겠습니다만,\" 웰러 씨가 도덕적인 책망조로 말을 이었다. \"우리 귀하신 주인 어른이 신념을 위해 무슨 일이든 다 감당하기로 마음을 굳히셨는데, 그분을 온갖 골칫거리에 끌어들이시다니요. 나리는 도드슨보다도 한참 더 못된 분이십니다요, 나리. 그리고 포그로 말할 것 같으면, 저는 그자가 나리에 비하면 타고난 천사라고 봅니다요!\" 웰러 씨는 이 마지막 소회에 양 무릎을 철썩 내려치며 힘을 실은 뒤, 몹시 역겨운 듯한 표정으로 팔짱을 끼고는, 죄인의 변론을 기다리기라도 하듯 의자에 등을 기댔다.\n\n\"여보게, 친구,\" 하고 윈클 씨가 손을 내밀며 말했다—웰러 씨의 일장 훈계 내내 그는 잠옷 차림으로 서 있었던 터라, 말하는 동안 줄곧 이가 딱딱 부딪쳤다—\"여보게, 친구, 나는 자네가 내 훌륭한 친구에게 품은 충정을 존경하네. 그리고 그분의 근심거리를 하나 더 보탠 것을 정말이지 깊이 뉘우치네. 자, 샘, 자!\"\n\n\"음,\" 샘이 다소 뚱한 채로, 그러나 동시에 내민 손을 공손히 흔들며 말했다. \"음, 그러셔야 마땅합죠. 그리고 정말로 그러신다니 저도 무척 기쁩니다요. 왜냐하면, 제가 막을 수만 있다면, 누구도 그분을 함부로 못 하게 할 작정이거든요. 뭐, 그게 다입니다요.\"\n\n\"물론 그래야지, 샘.\" 윈클 씨가 말했다. \"자! 이제 자러 가게, 샘. 이 일은 아침에 더 이야기하세.\"\n\n\"정말 죄송합니다만,\" 샘이 말했다. \"저는 자러 갈 수가 없습니다요.\"\n\n\"자러 갈 수 없다고!\" 윈클 씨가 되뇌었다.\n\n\"네.\" 샘이 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 말했다. \"그건 안 될 일입니다요.\"\n\n\"설마 오늘 밤에 돌아간다는 말은 아니겠지, 샘?\" 윈클 씨가 몹시 놀라 다그쳤다.\n\n\"나리께서 특별히 그러길 바라지 않으시면 안 갑니다요.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"하지만 저는 이 방을 떠나선 안 됩니다요. 주인 어른의 명령이 지엄하셨거든요.\"\n\n\"말도 안 되네, 샘.\" 윈클 씨가 말했다. \"나는 여기서 이삼 일은 머물러야 하네. 그뿐만 아니라, 샘, 자네도 여기 머물러서, 내가 어느 젊은 숙녀와 만나도록 도와줘야 하네—앨런 양 말일세, 샘. 자네도 그분을 기억하겠지—나는 브리스틀을 떠나기 전에 반드시 그분을 만나야 하고, 또 만나고야 말 것이네.\"\n\n그러나 이 주장 하나하나에 대해, 샘은 자못 단호하게 고개를 흔들며 힘주어 대답했다. \"그건 안 될 일입니다요.\"\n\n하지만 윈클 씨가 한참을 따지고 설득하고, 또 다울러와의 만남에서 있었던 일을 죄다 털어놓고 나자, 샘이 흔들리기 시작했다. 그리하여 마침내 하나의 타협이 이루어졌는데, 그 주된 골자는 다음과 같았다.\n\n샘은 물러가서 윈클 씨가 방을 거리낌 없이 차지하도록 내버려 두되, 그 대신 바깥에서 문을 잠그고 열쇠를 가져갈 권한을 갖는다. 단, 화재 경보가 울리거나 그 밖에 위험한 사태가 벌어질 경우에는 즉시 문을 연다. 이튿날 아침 일찍 픽윅 씨 앞으로 편지 한 통을 써서 다울러 편에 부쳐, 앞서 정한 목적과 취지로 샘과 윈클 씨가 브리스틀에 머무는 데 대한 동의를 청하고, 다음 마차 편으로 답신을 구한다. 만일 허락이 떨어지면 앞서 말한 당사자들은 그대로 머물고, 그렇지 않으면 답신을 받는 즉시 배스로 돌아간다. 그리고 마지막으로, 윈클 씨는 그동안 창문이나 벽난로, 혹은 그 밖의 은밀한 도주 수단에 의지하지 않겠다고 분명히 서약한 것으로 한다. 이 조항들이 매듭지어지자, 샘은 문을 잠그고 떠나갔다.\n\n그는 거의 아래층까지 다 내려왔다가, 걸음을 멈추고 주머니에서 열쇠를 꺼냈다.\n\n\"때려눕히는 일을 깜빡했네.\" 샘이 반쯤 돌아서며 말했다. \"주인 어른께서 분명히 그렇게 하라고 하셨는데. 거 참, 어지간히 멍청하구먼, 내가! 뭐 상관없지.\" 샘이 표정이 밝아지며 말했다. \"어쨌거나 그건 내일 하면 쉬운 일이니까.\"\n\n이 생각에 적잖이 위안을 얻은 듯, 웰러 씨는 다시 한번 열쇠를 주머니에 집어넣고, 더는 양심의 가책을 받지 않고 남은 계단을 마저 내려가, 이내 그 집의 다른 식구들과 마찬가지로 깊은 잠 속에 파묻혔다.\n\n이튿날 내내 샘은 윈클 씨를 한시도 놓치지 않으려고 단단히 마음먹은 채 그를 줄곧 지켜보았으니, 우두머리로부터 분명한 지시를 받을 때까지는 단 한순간도 그에게서 눈을 떼지 않겠다고 작정한 터였다. 샘의 바짝 붙은 감시와 대단한 경계가 윈클 씨에게는 아무리 불쾌했다 하더라도, 그는 격렬히 저항하는 어떤 행동을 했다가 힘에 의해 끌려가는 위험을 무릅쓰느니 차라리 그것을 참는 편이 낫다고 생각했다. 웰러 씨가 한 번 이상 강하게 내비친 바로는, 그것이야말로 엄격한 의무감이 그에게 따르라고 재촉하는 행동 방침이었던 것이다. 다우러가 전하기로 맡았던 쪽지에 픽윅 씨가 즉각 주의를 기울이지 않았더라면, 샘이 윈클 씨를 손발을 꽁꽁 묶어 배스로 도로 떠메고 가서 자신의 거리낌을 아주 신속히 가라앉혔으리라는 것은 의심할 여지가 거의 없다. 요컨대 저녁 여덟 시에 픽윅 씨가 직접 부시 여관의 커피룸으로 걸어 들어와, 샘에게 미소를 지으며 그가 아주 잘했고 더 이상 보초를 설 필요가 없다고 말해 주어, 샘은 크게 안도했다.\n\n\"내가 직접 오는 편이 낫겠다고 생각했네.\" 샘이 외투와 여행용 숄을 벗겨 주는 동안 픽윅 씨가 윈클 씨를 향해 말했다. \"이 일에 샘을 부리는 것에 동의하기에 앞서, 자네가 그 젊은 숙녀와 관련해 정말로 진지하고 진심인지 확인하려고 말일세.\"\n\n\"진심입니다, 가슴 깊이에서—제 영혼에서요!\" 윈클 씨가 대단한 기세로 대답했다.\n\n\"기억하게.\" 픽윅 씨가 환히 빛나는 눈으로 말했다. \"우리는 그 훌륭하고 환대 깊은 친구의 집에서 그녀를 만났네, 윈클. 충분한 숙고 없이 가볍게 이 젊은 숙녀의 애정을 가지고 장난친다면 그것은 못된 보답이 될 걸세. 나는 그것을 용납하지 않겠네, 자네. 용납하지 않겠어.\"\n\n\"정말 저는 그런 의도가 전혀 없습니다.\" 윈클 씨가 열렬히 외쳤다. \"저는 오랫동안 이 문제를 잘 숙고했고, 제 행복이 그녀에게 달려 있음을 느낍니다.\"\n\n\"그게 바로 우리가 말하는 행복을 조그만 꾸러미에 동여매는 일이라는 거죠, 나리.\" 웰러 씨가 유쾌한 미소를 지으며 끼어들었다.\n\n이 끼어듦에 윈클 씨가 다소 엄한 표정을 짓자, 픽윅 씨가 화를 내며 자기 하인에게 인간 본성의 가장 좋은 감정 가운데 하나를 가지고 농담하지 말라고 요청했다. 이에 샘은 \"그게 좋은 감정인 줄 알았더라면 그러지 않았을 텐데, 그런 감정이 하도 많아서 누가 그것들을 입에 올리는 걸 들을 때 어느 게 제일 좋은 건지 도통 분간을 못 하겠다.\"라고 대꾸했다.\n\n이어 윈클 씨는 아라벨라와 관련해 자신과 벤 앨런 씨 사이에 오간 일을 자세히 이야기했고, 자신의 목적은 그 젊은 숙녀와 만나 자신의 연정을 정식으로 털어놓는 것이라고 밝혔으며, 앞서 말한 벤이 흘린 어떤 모호한 암시와 중얼거림에 근거하여, 그녀가 지금 어디에 갇혀 있든 그곳은 다운스 근처 어딘가라는 확신을 표명했다. 그리고 이것이 그가 이 문제에 관해 알거나 짐작하고 있는 전부였다.\n\n이처럼 지극히 빈약한 단서를 길잡이로 삼아, 웰러 씨가 이튿날 아침 탐색 원정에 나서기로 결정되었다. 또한 자신들의 능력에 자신이 덜한 픽윅 씨와 윈클 씨는 그동안 시내를 돌아다니다가 그날 중에 우연인 척 보브 소여 씨를 들러, 그 젊은 숙녀의 행방에 관해 무언가 보거나 듣게 되기를 기대하기로 정해졌다.\n\n그리하여 이튿날 아침, 샘 웰러는 앞에 놓인 무척이나 맥 빠지는 전망에도 조금도 기죽지 않고 탐색에 나섰다. 그는 한 거리를 올라갔다가 다른 거리를 내려가며 걸었으니—한 언덕을 올라갔다 다른 언덕을 내려갔다고 말하려 했으나, 클리프턴에서는 죄다 오르막이라 그럴 수가 없다—다루는 일에 가장 희미한 빛이라도 던져 줄 만한 무엇이나 누구와도 마주치지 못했다. 샘이 길에서 말을 산책시키는 마부들이며 골목에서 아이들을 바람 쐬게 하는 보모들과 나눈 대화는 수없이 많았으나, 앞에 말한 부류든 뒤에 말한 부류든 어느 쪽에게서도 그가 교묘하게 진행하는 탐문의 대상과 조금이라도 관련된 것은 아무것도 캐낼 수 없었다. 숱한 집에 숱한 젊은 숙녀들이 있었고, 그 대부분은 남녀 하인들에게서 누군가에게 깊이 마음을 두고 있거나, 기회만 닿으면 기꺼이 그리되리라는 예리한 의심을 사고 있었다. 그러나 그 젊은 숙녀들 가운데 아라벨라 앨런 양은 없었으므로, 그 정보는 샘을 이전에 서 있던 바로 그 지혜의 지점에 그대로 남겨 두었다.\n\n샘은 제법 거센 바람을 거슬러 다운스를 가로질러 헤쳐 나가며, 이 고장에서는 늘 두 손으로 모자를 붙들고 있어야 하는 건지 궁금해하다가, 그늘진 외진 곳에 이르렀는데, 그 주위로는 조용하고 한적해 보이는 작은 별장 몇 채가 흩어져 있었다. 막다른 긴 뒷골목 끝에 있는 마구간 문밖에서, 작업복 차림의 마부 하나가 빈둥거리며, 삽 한 자루와 외바퀴 손수레로 무언가 하고 있노라고 스스로 믿게 만드는 듯했다. 여기서 한마디 해 두자면, 우리는 마구간 근처에서 한가한 시간을 보내는 마부치고 정도의 차이는 있을지언정 이 묘한 망상의 희생양이 아닌 자를 거의 본 적이 없다.\n\n샘은 어차피 다른 누구에게 말을 거나 이 마부에게 말을 거나 매한가지라고 생각했는데, 더욱이 그는 걸어 다니느라 몹시 지쳐 있었고, 외바퀴 손수레 바로 맞은편에 제법 큼직한 돌 하나가 있었던 것이다. 그래서 그는 골목을 어슬렁어슬렁 내려가, 그 돌에 앉아, 그 특유의 편안함과 거리낌 없음으로 대화를 텄다.\n\n\"안녕하슈, 친구 양반.\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n\"오후라고 해야지.\" 마부가 샘에게 퉁명스러운 눈길을 던지며 대꾸했다.\n\n\"맞는 말이오, 친구 양반.\" 샘이 말했다. \"내 말은 오후 안녕이오. 어떻게 지내시오?\"\n\n\"글쎄, 댁을 본다고 내 기분이 그닥 나아질 것 같진 않은데.\" 성질 고약한 마부가 대꾸했다.\n\n\"그것참 이상하구먼.\" 샘이 말했다. \"댁은 어찌나 보기 드물게 명랑해 보이고 통 활기차 보이는지, 댁을 보고 있으면 사람 마음이 다 흐뭇해지는데 말이오.\"\n\n이 말에 퉁명스러운 마부는 한층 더 퉁명스러운 표정을 지었으나, 샘에게 무슨 영향을 줄 만큼은 못 되었고, 샘은 곧장 대단히 근심스러운 얼굴로, 댁 주인장 성함이 워커가 아니냐고 물었다.\n\n\"아니, 아닌데.\" 마부가 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 브라운도 아니겠구먼?\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 아니야.\"\n\n\"윌슨도 아니고?\"\n\n\"아니, 그것도 아니야.\" 마부가 말했다.\n\n\"흠.\" 샘이 대꾸했다. \"그럼 내가 잘못 안 거고, 그 양반이 나랑 알고 지내는 영광은 없는 거구먼. 그런 줄로 알았는데 말이오. 나한테 예의 차리느라 여기 서 있을 거 없소.\" 마부가 손수레를 밀고 들어가며 문을 닫을 채비를 하자 샘이 말했다. \"격식보다 편한 게 낫지, 이 양반아. 내 봐줄 테니.\"\n\n\"반 크라운만 주면 댁 대가리를 날려 버리겠는데.\" 퉁명스러운 마부가 문짝 한쪽에 빗장을 지르며 말했다.\n\n\"그런 조건으로 그 짓을 시킬 여유는 없소.\" 샘이 대꾸했다. \"그건 적어도 댁한테 평생 치 식비 수당만큼 값나가는 일일 텐데, 그 값에 한다면 거저나 다름없는 거지. 안에다 내 안부 전해 주쇼. 나 기다려 가며 저녁 차리지 말라고 하고, 따로 챙겨 둘 것도 없다고 해 주쇼. 내가 들어갈 즈음엔 다 식어 있을 테니까.\"\n\n이에 대한 대답으로 마부는 잔뜩 노발대발하며 누군가의 신체에 손상을 입히고 싶다는 뜻을 중얼거렸으나, 그것을 실행에 옮기지는 않은 채 사라졌으니, 화가 나서 문을 쾅 닫고는, 가기 전에 머리카락 한 줌만 남겨 두고 가 달라는 샘의 다정한 부탁을 깡그리 무시했다.\n\n샘은 큼직한 돌에 계속 앉아, 어떻게 하는 게 최선일지 곰곰이 궁리하며, 브리스틀 반경 5마일 안의 모든 집 문을 하루에 백오십 채니 이백 채니 하는 식으로 차례차례 두드려, 그 방편으로 아라벨라 양을 찾아내 보려는 계획을 머릿속에서 굴리고 있었는데, 그때 우연이 느닷없이 그의 길에 던져 준 것이라곤, 그것 없이는 일 년을 앉아 있어도 찾아내지 못했을 바로 그것이었다.\n\n그가 앉아 있는 골목으로는 서너 채의 집에 딸린 정원 문 서너 개가 나 있었는데, 그 집들은 서로 떨어져 있었으나 오로지 정원으로만 갈라져 있었다. 그 정원들이 넓고 길었으며 나무가 빽빽이 심겨 있었던지라, 집들은 어느 정도 떨어져 있을 뿐 아니라 대부분이 거의 시야에서 가려져 있었다. 샘은 마부가 사라진 문 바로 옆 문밖에 쌓인 쓰레기 더미에 눈길을 고정한 채, 지금 떠맡은 일의 어려움을 깊이 곱씹고 있었는데, 그때 그 문이 열리며 하녀 하나가 침대맡 양탄자 몇 장을 털러 골목으로 나왔다.\n\n샘은 자기 생각에 어찌나 깊이 빠져 있었던지, 그 여자에 대해서도 그저 고개를 들어 그녀의 몸매가 무척 단정하고 예쁘다고 여기는 것 이상의 관심은 두지 않았을 것이다. 다만 그녀를 도와줄 사람이 아무도 없고 양탄자가 그녀 혼자 힘으로는 너무 무거워 보인다는 것을 알아챈 순간, 그의 기사도 정신이 가장 강하게 동했다. 웰러 씨는 그 나름으로 기사도가 대단한 신사였던지라, 이 정황을 알아채자마자 큼직한 돌에서 황급히 일어나 그녀에게 다가갔다.\n\n\"아가씨.\" 샘이 대단히 정중한 태도로 미끄러지듯 다가가며 말했다. \"그 양탄자를 혼자 털다간 그 아주 예쁜 몸매를 영 어울리잖게 망쳐 놓을 거요. 내가 도와드리리다.\"\n\n신사가 그토록 가까이 있는 줄 모르는 척 새침을 떨고 있던 젊은 숙녀는, 샘이 말하자 돌아섰으니—틀림없이 (사실 그녀는 나중에 그렇게 말했다) 생판 모르는 남자의 이 제안을 거절하려던 것이었으나—말을 꺼내는 대신, 그녀는 흠칫 뒤로 물러서며 반쯤 억눌린 비명을 질렀다. 샘도 못지않게 휘청했으니, 맵시 있는 하녀의 얼굴에서 다름 아닌 자신의 연인, 너프킨스 씨 댁의 예쁜 하녀의 바로 그 이목구비를 본 것이다.\n\n\"아니, 메리, 내 사랑!\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n\"어머나, 웰러 씨.\" 메리가 말했다. \"사람을 어쩜 그렇게 놀래키세요!\"\n\n샘은 이 불평에 말로 대답하지 않았고, 정확히 그가 무슨 대답을 했는지는 우리도 말할 수 없다. 우리가 아는 것이라곤, 잠시 뒤 메리가 \"어머, 그만 좀 하세요, 웰러 씨!\" 하고 말했다는 것, 그리고 그의 모자가 몇 순간 전에 벗겨져 떨어졌다는 것뿐이다—이 두 가지 정황으로 미루어, 두 사람 사이에 입맞춤이 한 번, 아니 그 이상 오갔으리라고 짐작할 만하다.\n\n\"근데, 여기 어떻게 오셨어요?\" 끼어들었던 일로 끊겼던 대화가 다시 이어졌을 때 메리가 말했다.\n\n\"당연히 당신을 찾으러 왔지, 내 사랑.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했으니, 이번만큼은 자신의 정열이 진실함을 이기도록 내버려 둔 것이었다.\n\n\"근데 제가 여기 있는 줄은 어떻게 아셨어요?\" 메리가 물었다. \"제가 입스위치에서 다른 집으로 옮겼고, 그러고 나서 그 집이 여기까지 멀리 이사 왔다는 걸 누가 말해 줄 수 있었겠어요? 그걸 누가 말해 줄 수 있었겠어요, 웰러 씨?\"\n\n\"아, 그렇지.\" 샘이 능청스러운 표정으로 말했다. \"그게 요점이지. 누가 나한테 말해 줄 수 있었을까?\"\n\n\"머즐 씨는 아니죠, 그렇죠?\" 메리가 물었다.\n\n\"오, 아니지.\" 샘이 엄숙하게 고개를 저으며 대답했다. \"그 사람은 아니야.\"\n\n\"그럼 요리사가 틀림없어요.\" 메리가 말했다.\n\n\"당연히 그렇겠지.\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n\"어머, 그런 얘긴 난생처음 들어 봐요!\" 메리가 외쳤다.\n\n\"나도 마찬가지야.\" 샘이 말했다. \"근데 메리, 내 사랑\"—여기서 샘의 태도가 무척 다정해졌다—\"메리, 내 사랑, 나한테 또 한 가지 아주 급한 일이 있어. 우리 주인 양반 친구분 중 하나가—윈클 씨라고, 기억하지?\"\n\n\"녹색 코트 입은 그분요?\" 메리가 말했다. \"아, 네, 기억해요.\"\n\n\"그게 말이지.\" 샘이 말했다. \"그분이 끔찍하게 사랑에 빠졌거든. 아주 정신이 혼미해져서, 완전히 맥을 못 추고 있어.\"\n\n\"어머나!\" 메리가 끼어들었다.\n\n\"그래.\" 샘이 말했다. \"근데 그 젊은 아가씨를 찾을 수만 있으면 그쯤은 아무것도 아니지.\" 그러고 나서 샘은 메리의 미모와, 그녀를 마지막으로 본 이후 자신이 겪은 이루 말할 수 없는 고통에 관해 숱하게 곁가지를 치면서, 윈클 씨가 지금 처한 곤경을 충실히 들려주었다.\n\n\"어머, 세상에!\" 메리가 말했다.\n\n\"당연히 처음 듣겠지.\" 샘이 말했다. \"그런 일은 아무도 들어 본 적 없고, 앞으로도 없을 테니까. 그래서 내가 방랑하는 유대인처럼 돌아다니고 있는 거야—아마 들어 봤을 텐데 메리, 늘 시간과 경주를 벌이면서 잠 한숨 안 자고 다닌다는 그 유명한 작자 말이야—이 아라벨라 앨런 양인지 하는 사람을 찾으면서.\"\n\n\"누구 양이라고요?\" 메리가 몹시 놀라며 말했다.\n\n\"아라벨라 앨런 양.\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n\"어머나 세상에!\" 메리가 그 뚱한 마부가 잠가 둔 정원 문을 가리키며 말했다. \"아니, 바로 저 집이에요. 그분이 거기서 산 지 육 주나 됐어요. 어느 날 아침, 그 집 식구들이 아직 잠자리에서 일어나기도 전에, 거기 수석 하녀가, 시녀 일도 같이 하는 사람인데, 세탁장 울타리 너머로 저한테 그 얘기를 다 해 줬거든요.\"\n\n\"뭐라고, 너희 집 바로 옆집이라고?\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n\"바로 옆집요.\" 메리가 대답했다.\n\n이 소식을 듣고 웰러 씨는 어찌나 깊이 마음이 북받쳤던지, 어쩔 수 없이 자신의 어여쁜 정보 제공자에게 매달려 몸을 지탱해야 했다. 그가 다시 본론으로 돌아갈 만큼 정신을 추스르기까지, 두 사람 사이에는 자잘한 사랑의 실랑이가 몇 차례 오갔다.\n\n\"흠.\" 마침내 샘이 말했다. \"이거야말로 닭싸움보다 더 기막힌 일인데, 이걸 능가할 게 없겠어—만찬 뒤에 국무 장관이 자기 마님 건강을 위해 건배하자고 했을 때 시장 나리가 한 말마따나 말이야. 바로 그 옆집이라니! 아니, 내가 그 아가씨한테 전할 전갈이 있어서 온종일 전하려고 애썼는데 말이야.\"\n\n\"아.\" 메리가 말했다. \"하지만 지금은 전할 수 없어요. 그분은 저녁에만 정원을 거니는 데다, 그것도 아주 잠깐뿐이거든요. 늙은 마님 없이는 절대 밖에 안 나가요.\"\n\n샘은 잠시 곰곰이 생각하다가, 마침내 다음과 같은 작전 계획을 떠올렸다. 즉, 그가 해 질 녘—아라벨라가 으레 산책하는 시각—에 맞춰 돌아와, 메리가 일하는 집 정원으로 그녀의 안내를 받아 들어간 다음, 커다란 배나무의 늘어진 가지들 아래로 담장을 기어올라 가는데, 그 가지들이 그를 남의 눈에서 확실히 가려 줄 터였다. 거기서 그가 전갈을 전하고, 가능하다면 윈클 씨를 위해 이튿날 저녁 같은 시각에 만남을 주선하기로 한 것이다. 이 계획을 아주 잽싸게 마련한 뒤, 그는 메리가 한참 미뤄 두었던 양탄자 터는 일을 거들었다.\n\n조그만 양탄자 조각들을 터는 그 일이란, 보이는 것처럼 그렇게 순진한 일이 결코 아니다—적어도 터는 데에는 별 해될 것이 없을지 모르나, 개키는 일이야말로 무척 음흉한 과정이다. 터는 일이 계속되어 두 사람이 양탄자 길이만큼 떨어져 있는 동안은, 그야말로 더없이 순진한 놀이라 할 만하다. 그러나 개키기가 시작되어 둘 사이의 거리가 본래 길이의 절반에서 차츰 사분의 일로, 다시 팔분의 일로, 다시 십육분의 일로, 다시 양탄자가 충분히 길다면 삼십이분의 일로 줄어들면, 그것은 위험해진다. 이 경우에 정확히 몇 조각의 양탄자가 개켜졌는지 우리는 콕 집어 알지 못하나, 그 조각의 수가 몇이었든 그만큼 샘이 그 예쁜 하녀에게 입을 맞추었으리라고는 감히 단언할 수 있다.\n\n웰러 씨는 거의 해 질 녘이 될 때까지 가장 가까운 술집에서 절도 있게 한잔 즐긴 뒤, 막다른 골목으로 돌아왔다. 메리의 안내로 정원에 들어가, 그 숙녀에게서 자기 팔다리와 목의 안전에 관한 갖가지 당부를 들은 다음, 샘은 아라벨라가 시야에 나타날 때까지 기다리려고 배나무 위로 올라갔다.\n\n그토록 기다려 마지않던 일이 일어나지 않은 채 어찌나 오래 기다렸던지, 그는 그 일이 영 일어나지 않을 모양이라고 생각하기 시작했는데, 그때 자갈 위로 가벼운 발소리가 들리더니, 곧이어 아라벨라가 정원을 따라 시름에 잠긴 채 걸어 내려오는 것이 보였다. 그녀가 나무 거의 바로 아래까지 다가오자, 샘은 자기가 있음을 넌지시 알릴 셈으로, 갓난아기 적부터 인후염과 후두염과 백일해를 한꺼번에 앓아 온 중년 남자에게나 어울릴 법한 갖가지 끔찍한 소리를 내기 시작했다.\n\n이에 그 젊은 숙녀는 그 무시무시한 소리가 나는 쪽으로 황급히 눈길을 던졌다. 나뭇가지 사이에서 남자 하나를 보았을 때 그녀의 앞선 놀라움이 조금도 누그러지지 않았으니, 그녀는 틀림없이 줄행랑을 쳐서 온 집 안을 깨워 놓았을 것이다. 다행히 공포가 그녀에게서 움직일 힘을 앗아 가, 마침 운 좋게도 가까이에 있던 정원 의자에 주저앉게 만들지 않았더라면 말이다.\n\n\"기절하시는구먼.\" 샘이 몹시 난처해하며 혼잣말을 했다. \"거참 이상한 일이지, 이 젊은 처자들은 꼭 기절하면 안 될 때 기절을 한단 말이야. 이봐요, 아가씨, 톱장이 아가씨, 윈클 부인, 그러지 마쇼!\"\n\n아라벨라를 되살린 것이 윈클 씨라는 이름의 마력이었는지, 바깥 공기의 서늘함이었는지, 아니면 웰러 씨의 목소리에 대한 어떤 기억이었는지는 중요하지 않다. 그녀는 고개를 들고 나른하게 물었다. \"거기 누구예요, 뭘 원하는 거예요?\"\n\n\"쉿.\" 샘이 담장 위로 몸을 휙 올려, 될 수 있는 대로 작게 몸을 웅크리며 말했다. \"저예요, 아가씨, 저뿐이라고요.\"\n\n\"픽윅 씨네 하인이군요!\" 아라벨라가 진지하게 말했다.\n\n\"바로 그렇습니다, 아가씨.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"윈클 씨가 절망에 빠져 아주 단단히 꿰매어진 꼴이 됐답니다, 아가씨.\"\n\n\"아!\" 아라벨라가 담장 가까이 다가서며 말했다.\n\n\"아, 정말입니다.\" 샘이 말했다. \"저희가 어젯밤엔 그분한테 구속복을 입혀야 하나 싶었다니까요. 온종일 헛소리를 해 대시거든요. 내일 밤이 다 가기 전에 아가씨를 못 보면, 물에 빠져 죽지 않으면 무슨 험한 꼴이라도 당하고 싶다고 그러십니다.\"\n\n\"오, 안 돼요, 안 돼, 웰러 씨!\" 아라벨라가 두 손을 맞잡으며 말했다.\n\n\"그분이 그렇게 말씀하셨어요, 아가씨.\" 샘이 차분히 대답했다. \"약속을 지키는 분이고, 제 생각엔 정말로 그러실 겁니다, 아가씨. 그분은 안경 쓴 톱장이한테서 아가씨 얘기를 다 들으셨거든요.\"\n\n\"오라버니한테서요!\" 아라벨라가 샘의 묘사에서 어렴풋이 누군지 알아채며 말했다.\n\n\"어느 분이 아가씨 오라버니인지는 정확히 모르겠습니다, 아가씨.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"둘 중에 더 꾀죄죄한 쪽인가요?\"\n\n\"네, 네, 웰러 씨.\" 아라벨라가 대꾸했다. \"계속하세요. 제발 어서요.\"\n\n\"그러니까요, 아가씨.\" 샘이 말했다. \"그분이 그한테서 자초지종을 다 들으셨고요. 주인 양반 생각으로는, 아가씨가 그분을 아주 빨리 안 만나시면, 우리가 얘기하던 그 톱장이 머리통에 납이 좀 넉넉히 더 박혀서, 나중에 알코올에 담가 보존이라도 하려 들 때 장기들 상태에 적잖이 해가 될 거랍니다.\"\n\n\"오, 이 끔찍한 다툼을 막으려면 제가 뭘 할 수 있을까요!\" 아라벨라가 외쳤다.\n\n\"이게 다 옛정 운운하는 의심 때문에 벌어진 일입니다.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"그분을 만나 보시는 게 좋겠어요, 아가씨.\"\n\n\"하지만 어떻게요?—어디서요?\" 아라벨라가 외쳤다. \"저는 혼자서는 감히 집 밖에 못 나가요. 오라버니가 어찌나 매정하고, 어찌나 막무가내인지! 제가 이렇게 당신께 말하는 게 얼마나 이상해 보일지 알아요, 웰러 씨. 하지만 저는 정말, 정말 불행해요—\" 그러고는 가엾은 아라벨라가 어찌나 서럽게 울던지 샘은 기사도가 솟구쳤다.\n\n\"아가씨가 이런 일들을 저한테 말씀하시는 게 무척 이상해 보일지 모릅니다, 아가씨.\" 샘이 대단한 열의를 담아 말했다. \"하지만 제가 드릴 수 있는 말은, 저는 일이 잘 풀리게 할 거라면 무엇이든 할 준비가 됐을 뿐 아니라 기꺼이 하겠다는 겁니다. 그리고 그 톱장이들 중 누구든 창밖으로 내던지는 게 도움이 된다면, 제가 바로 그 일을 할 사람입니다.\" 샘 웰러는 이렇게 말하면서, 당장이라도 일에 착수할 채비가 됐음을 내비치려고, 그러다 담장에서 떨어질 위험을 무릅쓰면서까지 소맷부리를 걷어붙였다.\n\n이러한 선의의 다짐이 아무리 흐뭇한 것이었어도, 아라벨라는 (샘이 보기엔 도무지 영문 모를 일이었지만) 단호히 그것을 받아들이기를 사양했다. 한동안 그녀는 샘이 그토록 애처롭게 청한 윈클 씨와의 만남을 허락하기를 한사코 거절했다. 그러나 마침내, 반갑지 않은 제삼자의 출현으로 대화가 끊길 위기에 처하자, 그녀는 거듭 고마움을 표하면서, 이튿날 저녁 한 시간쯤 늦게라면 어쩌면 정원에 나와 있을 수도 있겠다고 황급히 일러 주었다. 샘은 이를 완벽히 알아들었다. 아라벨라는 그에게 더없이 사랑스러운 미소를 하나 지어 보이고는, 우아하게 종종걸음으로 사라져, 웰러 씨를 그녀의 매력에 대한—외모와 마음씨 양쪽 다에 대한—깊은 감탄에 젖게 한 채 남겨 두었다.\n\n담장에서 무사히 내려온 뒤, 같은 부문에서 자신만의 사사로운 용무에 몇 순간을 바치는 것도 잊지 않은 채, 웰러 씨는 부시 여관으로 부지런히 돌아갔으니, 그곳에서는 그의 길어진 부재가 갖은 추측과 약간의 불안을 자아내고 있었다.\n\n\"우리는 조심해야 하네.\" 픽윅 씨가 샘의 이야기를 주의 깊게 들은 뒤 말했다. \"우리 자신을 위해서가 아니라 그 젊은 숙녀를 위해서 말일세. 우리는 아주 신중해야 해.\"\n\n\"우리라고요!\" 윈클 씨가 두드러지게 힘주어 말했다.\n\n이 말의 어조에 픽윅 씨의 얼굴에 잠깐 떠올랐던 분개의 빛은, 이내 그의 특유의 자비로운 표정으로 가라앉았고, 그가 대답했다—\n\n\"우리 말일세, 자네! 내가 자네와 동행하겠네.\"\n\n\"선생님이요!\" 윈클 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"내가.\" 픽윅 씨가 온화하게 대답했다. \"이 만남을 자네에게 허락함으로써, 그 젊은 숙녀는 어쩌면 자연스러운, 그러나 그래도 무척 경솔한 발걸음을 내디딘 걸세. 내가 그 만남 자리에 함께 있다면—두 사람 모두의 아버지뻘은 됨 직한 공동의 친구로서 말일세—이후로 그녀를 두고 중상의 목소리가 결코 일지 못할 걸세.\"\n\n이렇게 말하면서 픽윅 씨의 두 눈은 자신의 선견지명에 대한 정직한 기쁨으로 빛났다. 윈클 씨는 친구의 젊은 피보호자에 대한 이 섬세한 존중의 작은 표시에 감동하여, 숭배에 가까운 경애의 마음으로 그의 손을 잡았다.\n\n\"꼭 가셔야 합니다.\" 윈클 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"가겠네.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"샘, 내 외투와 숄을 챙겨 두고, 내일 저녁 문 앞에 탈것을 대기시키되, 우리가 넉넉히 시간을 맞출 수 있게 꼭 필요한 시각보다 다소 일찍 오도록 일러 두게.\"\n\n웰러 씨는 복종의 표시로 모자에 손을 갖다 댄 뒤, 원정에 필요한 모든 준비를 하러 물러갔다.\n\n마차는 정한 시각에 어김없이 도착했다. 웰러 씨는 픽윅 씨와 윈클 씨를 안에 제대로 앉힌 다음, 마부 옆 마부석에 자리를 잡았다. 그들은 약속한 대로 만남의 장소에서 사 분의 일 마일쯤 떨어진 곳에서 내려, 마부에게 자신들이 돌아올 때까지 기다리라고 이른 뒤, 나머지 거리는 걸어서 나아갔다.\n\n일이 이 단계에 이르렀을 때 픽윅 씨는, 숱한 미소와 더불어 대단한 자기만족을 드러내는 갖가지 표시와 함께, 외투 주머니 하나에서 이 일을 위해 특별히 마련해 둔 어둠 등을 꺼내, 그 대단한 기계적 정교함을 길을 가면서 윈클 씨에게 설명하기 시작했으니, 그들이 마주친 몇 안 되는 행인들이 적잖이 놀란 것은 말할 것도 없었다.\n\n* 어둠 등: 빛을 한 방향으로만 비추고 가릴 수 있게 만든 휴대용 등.\n\"지난번 밤중에 정원 원정을 갔을 때 이런 게 있었으면 좋았을 텐데 말이야. 안 그런가, 샘?\" 픽윅 씨가 뒤에서 터덜터덜 따라오는 하인을 기분 좋게 돌아보며 말했다.\n\n\"제대로만 다루면 아주 좋은 물건이죠, 나리.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다. \"하지만 남 눈에 안 띄고 싶을 땐, 제 생각엔 불이 켜져 있을 때보다 촛불이 꺼진 다음에 더 쓸모가 있을 것 같은데요.\"\n\n픽윅 씨는 샘의 말에 적잖이 마음이 찔린 듯, 등을 도로 주머니에 집어넣었고, 두 사람은 말없이 걸어갔다.\n\n\"이쪽으로 내려가시죠, 나리.\" 샘이 말했다. \"제가 앞장서겠습니다. 이게 그 골목입니다, 나리.\"\n\n그들은 골목을 내려갔는데, 골목은 충분히 어두웠다. 픽윅 씨가 더듬더듬 길을 헤쳐 가며 한두 차례 등을 꺼내, 지름이 한 자쯤 되는 아주 환한 빛의 작은 터널을 앞쪽으로 비추었다. 보기에는 무척 예뻤으나, 주위의 사물들을 도리어 전보다 더 어둡게 만드는 효과가 있는 듯했다.\n\n마침내 그들은 큼직한 돌에 이르렀다. 여기서 샘은 주인과 윈클 씨에게 앉아 있으라고 권하고, 자신은 정찰을 나가 메리가 벌써 기다리고 있는지 확인했다.\n\n오 분인지 십 분인지 자리를 비운 뒤, 샘은 돌아와 문이 열렸고 모든 게 조용하다고 알렸다. 그를 따라 살금살금 발을 옮겨, 픽윅 씨와 윈클 씨는 이내 정원 안에 들어서 있었다. 여기서 모두가 \"쉿!\" 하고 여러 차례 말했고, 그러고 나자 다음에 무엇을 해야 할지에 대해서는 아무도 그다지 또렷한 생각이 없는 듯했다.\n\n\"앨런 양이 벌써 정원에 나와 있나, 메리?\" 윈클 씨가 몹시 안절부절못하며 물었다.\n\n\"모르겠어요, 나리.\" 예쁜 하녀가 대답했다. \"제일 좋은 방법은요, 나리, 웰러 씨가 나리를 나무 위로 밀어 올려 드리고, 픽윅 씨께서는 친절하게 골목으로 아무도 안 오는지 봐 주시고, 그동안 제가 정원 저쪽 끝에서 망을 보는 거예요. 어머나 세상에, 저게 뭐예요?\"\n\n\"저 망할 놈의 등 때문에 우리 다 죽게 생겼네.\" 샘이 짜증스럽게 외쳤다. \"뭘 하시는 건지 조심하세요, 나리. 뒷방 창문으로 곧장 한바탕 빛을 쏘아 보내고 계시잖아요.\"\n\n\"이런!\" 픽윅 씨가 황급히 옆으로 돌아서며 말했다. \"그럴 생각은 아니었는데.\"\n\n\"이번엔 옆집으로 가는데요, 나리.\" 샘이 항의했다.\n\n\"맙소사!\" 픽윅 씨가 다시 빙 돌아서며 외쳤다.\n\n\"이번엔 마구간이에요, 불난 줄 알겠어요.\" 샘이 말했다. \"그거 좀 닫으시라니까요, 나리, 못 닫으세요?\"\n\n\"내 평생 만나 본 중에 가장 별난 등이로구먼!\" 픽윅 씨가, 자신이 그토록 본의 아니게 만들어 낸 효과에 크게 어리둥절해하며 외쳤다. \"이렇게 강력한 반사경은 본 적이 없네.\"\n\n\"그렇게 계속 빛을 쏘아 대시다간 우리한테 너무 과하게 강력한 물건이 되겠는데요, 나리.\" 픽윅 씨가 갖은 헛수고 끝에 가까스로 덮개를 닫는 동안 샘이 대답했다. \"아가씨 발소리가 나네요. 자, 윈클 씨, 나리, 올라가시죠.\"\n\n\"잠깐, 잠깐!\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"내가 먼저 그녀와 이야기를 해야겠네. 좀 밀어 올려 주게, 샘.\"\n\n\"살살요, 나리.\" 샘이 자기 머리를 담장에 대고 등으로 발판을 만들며 말했다. \"저기 저 화분 위에 발을 디디세요, 나리. 자, 그럼 올라가시죠.\"\n\n\"내가 자네를 다치게 할까 봐 걱정이네, 샘.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"저는 신경 쓰지 마세요, 나리.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"손 좀 빌려주세요, 윈클 씨, 나리. 가만히, 나리, 가만히! 그렇지, 바로 그겁니다!\"\n\n샘이 말하는 동안, 픽윅 씨는 그 나이와 몸무게의 신사로서는 거의 초인적인 분투 끝에 가까스로 샘의 등에 올라탔다. 샘이 가만히 몸을 일으키고, 픽윅 씨가 담장 꼭대기를 단단히 붙잡고, 그동안 윈클 씨가 그의 두 다리를 꽉 끌어안아, 이런 방법으로 그들은 그의 안경을 담장 갓돌 높이 바로 위까지 올려놓는 데 성공했다.\n\n\"아가씨.\" 픽윅 씨가 담장 너머를 넘겨다보다가 반대편에 있는 아라벨라를 발견하고 말했다. \"겁내지 마세요, 아가씨, 저뿐입니다.\" \"오, 제발 가세요, 픽윅 씨.\" 아라벨라가 말했다. \"다들 가라고 하세요. 저는 너무너무 무서워요. 친애하는, 친애하는 픽윅 씨, 거기 계시지 마세요. 떨어져서 목숨을 잃으실 거예요, 분명히 그러실 거예요.\"\n\n\"자, 제발 놀라지 마세요, 아가씨.\" 픽윅 씨가 달래듯 말했다. \"조금도 두려워할 까닭이 없습니다, 정말입니다. 단단히 버텨 주게, 샘.\" 픽윅 씨가 아래를 내려다보며 말했다.\n\n\"괜찮습니다, 나리.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다. \"편하게 봐주실 수 있는 것보다 더 오래 걸리진 마세요, 나리. 좀 무거우시거든요.\"\n\n\"잠깐이면 되네, 샘.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"그저 아가씨가 알아 두셨으면 해서요. 아가씨가 처한 처지가 제 젊은 친구에게 다른 선택의 여지를 남겨 두지 않았더라면, 저는 그가 이렇게 은밀한 방식으로 아가씨를 만나도록 결코 허락하지 않았을 겁니다. 그리고 이 발걸음의 온당치 못함이 아가씨에게 어떤 불편함을 끼치지 않도록, 내 사랑, 제가 함께 있다는 것을 아시는 게 위안이 될 듯합니다. 그게 전부입니다, 아가씨.\"\n\n\"정말이지, 픽윅 씨, 친절과 배려에 무척 감사드려요.\" 아라벨라가 손수건으로 눈물을 닦으며 대답했다. 그녀는 아마 훨씬 더 많은 말을 했을 테지만, 샘의 어깨를 잘못 디딘 탓에 픽윅 씨가 갑자기 땅바닥으로 떨어지면서 그의 머리가 대단히 잽싸게 사라져 버렸다. 그러나 그는 순식간에 다시 일어났다. 그러고는 윈클 씨에게 어서 서둘러 만남을 끝내라 이르고는, 젊은이다운 온갖 용기와 열의로 골목으로 달려 나가 망을 보았다. 윈클 씨 본인은 이 상황에 고무되어 눈 깜짝할 새 담장 위에 올라가, 다만 샘에게 주인을 잘 보살피라고 부탁하느라 잠시 멈추었을 뿐이었다.\n\n\"제가 잘 모실 테니까요, 나리.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"저한테 맡기세요.\"\n\n\"그분은 어디 계셔? 뭘 하고 계시지, 샘?\" 윈클 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"그 양반 낡은 각반에 복이 깃들기를.\" 샘이 정원 문밖을 내다보며 대꾸했다. \"저 어둠 등을 들고 골목에서 망을 보고 계시네요, 무슨 상냥한 가이 포크스처럼 말이죠! 내 평생 저렇게 훌륭한 양반은 본 적이 없어요. 분명코 저분 심장은 적어도 몸뚱이보다 스물다섯 해는 늦게 태어났을 거라니까!\"\n\n* 가이 포크스: 1605년 영국 의회 폭파를 꾀한 화약 음모 사건의 인물로, 등불을 들고 지하실에 숨어 있던 모습으로 흔히 그려진다.\n\n윈클 씨는 친구에 대한 그 찬사를 끝까지 듣고 있지 않았다. 그는 이미 담장에서 뛰어내려, 아라벨라의 발치에 몸을 던졌고, 이즈음에는 픽윅 씨 본인에게도 손색없을 만한 웅변으로 자신의 정열이 진실함을 호소하고 있었다.\n이런 일들이 야외에서 벌어지고 있는 동안, 두세 집 떨어진 곳에서는 과학적 소양을 갖춘 한 노신사가 서재에 앉아 철학 논문을 쓰면서, 이따금 곁에 놓인 고풍스러워 보이는 병에서 클라레 한 잔을 따라 자기 흙덩이며 노고를 적시고 있었다. 글쓰기의 고통 속에서 노신사는 때로는 양탄자를, 때로는 천장을, 때로는 벽을 바라보았고, 양탄자도 천장도 벽도 필요한 만큼의 영감을 주지 못하면, 창밖을 내다보았다.\n\n이렇게 착상이 멎은 어느 한순간, 그 과학적 신사가 바깥의 짙은 어둠을 멍하니 응시하고 있었는데, 지면에서 조금 떨어진 높이로 더없이 환한 빛 하나가 공중을 미끄러져 지나가더니 거의 순식간에 사라지는 것을 보고 그는 몹시 놀랐다. 잠시 뒤 그 현상이 되풀이되었는데, 한두 번이 아니라 여러 번이었다. 마침내 그 과학적 신사는 펜을 내려놓고, 이 출현들을 어떤 자연적 원인에 돌려야 할지 궁리하기 시작했다.\n\n그것들은 유성이 아니었다. 너무 낮았다. 반딧불이도 아니었다. 너무 높았다. 도깨비불도 아니었고, 개똥벌레도 아니었으며, 불꽃놀이도 아니었다. 대체 무엇일 수 있단 말인가? 어떤 철학자도 일찍이 본 적 없는, 자연의 어떤 비범하고 경이로운 현상, 오직 그 혼자만이 발견하도록 남겨져 있었던 무엇이며, 후세의 이익을 위해 그것을 기록함으로써 자신의 이름을 불멸케 할 무엇이었다. 이 생각에 사로잡혀, 그 과학적 신사는 다시 펜을 집어 들고, 이 유례없는 출현들에 관한 갖가지 기록을, 그것들이 보인 날짜와 요일, 시각, 분, 그리고 정확한 초까지 곁들여 종이에 적어 두었다. 이 모든 것이, 문명 세계 어느 구석에서든 일찍이 숨 쉬어 본 적 있는 모든 대기학 풋내기 학자들을 깜짝 놀라게 할, 방대한 연구와 깊은 학식이 담긴 논문의 자료가 될 터였다.\n\n그는 안락의자에 등을 기댄 채 장차 자신이 누릴 위대함에 대한 상념에 잠겼다. 그 신비로운 빛은 전보다 더 찬란하게 나타나, 보아하니 골목을 오르내리며 춤추듯, 이쪽저쪽을 가로지르며, 혜성 그 자체만큼이나 변덕스러운 궤도를 그리며 움직였다.\n\n그 과학적 신사는 독신이었다. 불러들여 놀라게 해 줄 아내가 없었던지라, 그는 종을 울려 하인을 불렀다.\n\n\"프러플.\" 과학적 신사가 말했다. \"오늘 밤 공중에 아주 비범한 무언가가 있네. 자네도 저걸 봤나?\" 빛이 다시 보이게 되자 그가 창밖을 가리키며 말했다.\n\n\"네, 봤습니다, 나리.\"\n\n\"저게 뭐라고 생각하나, 프러플?\"\n\n\"저걸 뭐라고 생각하느냐고요, 나리?\"\n\n\"그래. 자네는 이 고장에서 나고 자랐지 않은가. 저 빛들의 원인이 무엇이라고 말하겠나, 어디?\"\n\n그 과학적 신사는, 자기로서는 그 원인을 도무지 댈 수 없노라는 프러플의 대답을 미소 지으며 미리 예상하고 있었다. 프러플은 곰곰이 생각했다.\n\n\"제 생각엔 도둑들 같습니다, 나리.\" 마침내 프러플이 말했다.\n\n\"자네는 바보일세, 내려가도 좋네.\" 과학적 신사가 말했다.\n\n\"고맙습니다, 나리.\" 프러플이 말했다. 그러고는 내려갔다.\n\n그러나 그 과학적 신사는, 자신이 구상한 그 기발한 논문이 세상에서 사라져 버릴지 모른다는 생각에 가만히 있을 수가 없었으니, 기발한 프러플 씨의 추측이 그 싹부터 짓눌리지 않는다면 그런 일은 반드시 벌어지고 말 터였다. 그는 모자를 쓰고 정원으로 빠르게 걸어 내려가, 이 문제를 끝까지 파헤쳐 보기로 작정했다.\n\n그런데 그 과학적 신사가 정원으로 나오기 얼마 전, 픽윅 씨는 누군가 그쪽으로 오고 있다는 거짓 경보를 전하려고 골목을 있는 힘껏 달려 내려왔으니, 도랑에 빠지지 않으려고 이따금 어둠 등의 덮개를 도로 열었던 것이다. 경보가 떨어지기가 무섭게 윈클 씨는 담장을 기어 넘어 돌아왔고 아라벨라는 집 안으로 달려 들어갔다. 정원 문이 닫혔고, 세 모험가가 골목을 부지런히 내려가던 차에, 그 과학적 신사가 자기 정원 문을 따는 바람에 그들은 흠칫 놀랐다.\n\n\"멈추세요.\" 물론 일행 중 맨 앞에 있던 샘이 속삭였다. \"딱 일 초만 불을 비춰 주세요, 나리.\"\n\n픽윅 씨가 시키는 대로 하자, 샘은 자기 머리에서 반 야드도 안 되는 곳에서 한 남자의 머리가 무척 조심스럽게 빼꼼 내미는 것을 보고는, 그것을 꽉 쥔 주먹으로 가볍게 한 대 쳤으니, 그 머리는 텅 빈 소리를 내며 문에 부딪쳤다. 이 묘기를 대단히 갑작스럽고 능숙하게 해치운 뒤, 웰러 씨는 픽윅 씨를 들쳐 업고, 짊어진 짐을 생각하면 그야말로 놀라운 속도로 윈클 씨를 뒤따라 골목을 내려갔다.\n\n\"숨은 다시 돌아오셨나요, 나리?\" 골목 끝에 다다랐을 때 샘이 물었다.\n\n\"아주, 아주 좋아졌네, 이제.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"그럼 가시죠, 나리.\" 샘이 주인을 다시 발로 서게 하며 말했다. \"우리 사이로 들어오세요, 나리. 달릴 거리는 반 마일도 안 됩니다. 우승컵을 따는 중이라고 생각하세요, 나리. 자, 갑니다.\"\n\n이렇게 격려받아, 픽윅 씨는 자기 두 다리를 더없이 잘 놀렸다. 그 기억할 만한 날 픽윅 씨의 검정 각반보다 더 멋진 모양새로 땅을 가로질러 본 검정 각반은 일찍이 없었노라고 자신 있게 말할 수 있다.\n\n마차가 기다리고 있었고, 말들은 기운이 넘쳤으며, 길은 좋았고, 마부는 기꺼이 나섰다. 일행 전원은 픽윅 씨가 숨을 채 고르기도 전에 무사히 부시 여관에 도착했다.\n\n\"당장 안으로 들어가시죠, 나리.\" 샘이 주인을 부축해 내리며 말했다. \"그렇게 운동을 하신 뒤에 길거리에서 일 초도 멈춰 계시면 안 됩니다. 실례합니다, 나리.\" 윈클 씨가 내리자 샘이 모자에 손을 갖다 대며 말을 이었다. \"옛정 운운하는 거리낌 같은 건 없었기를 바랍니다, 나리?\"\n\n윈클 씨는 자신의 미천한 친구의 손을 꽉 잡고는 그의 귀에 대고 속삭였다. \"다 잘됐네, 샘. 아주 잘됐어.\" 이에 웰러 씨는 알았다는 표시로 자기 코를 또렷하게 세 번 두드리고, 미소 짓고, 한쪽 눈을 찡긋한 다음, 활기찬 만족이 어린 얼굴로 발판을 올리기 시작했다.\n\n그 과학적 신사로 말하자면, 그는 한 편의 명저에서 이 경이로운 빛들이 전기의 작용이었음을 증명해 보였고, 자신이 문밖으로 머리를 내밀었을 때 한 줄기 불꽃이 눈앞에서 춤추던 일이며, 그 뒤로 십오 분 동안 자신을 멍하게 만든 충격을 받은 일을 상세히 서술함으로써 그것을 명백히 입증했다. 이 논증은 모든 과학 협회를 더없이 기쁘게 했고, 이후로 그를 과학의 등불로 여겨지게 만들었다.\n\n픽윅 씨가 배스에 머물기로 정해 두었던 기간의 나머지는 별다른 일 없이 지나갔다. 삼위일체 개정기가 시작되었다. 그 첫 주가 끝나자 픽윅 씨와 그의 친구들은 런던으로 돌아왔고, 픽윅 씨는 물론 샘을 거느리고 곧장 조지 앤드 벌처에 있는 그의 옛 숙소로 향했다.\n\n* 삼위일체 개정기: 영국 법원의 개정 기간 중 하나로, 삼위일체 주일 이후에 열렸다.\n그들이 도착한 지 사흘째 되는 아침, 시내의 모든 시계가 저마다 아홉 시를 치고, 다 합치면 어림잡아 구백구십아홉 번쯤 울리고 있을 때, 샘이 조지 야드에서 바람을 쐬고 있는데, 갓 칠한 듯한 묘한 종류의 마차 한 대가 다가오더니, 그 안에서 무척 날렵하게 한 사람이 뛰어내리며 옆자리에 앉아 있던 건장한 사내에게 고삐를 던졌으니, 그는 마차를 위해 만들어진 듯하고 마차는 그를 위해 만들어진 듯한, 묘한 종류의 신사였다.\n\n그 마차는 정확히 말해 이륜마차도 아니었고, 스탠호프도 아니었다. 흔히 말하는 도그카트도 아니었고, 과세 짐마차도 아니었으며, 짐수레도 아니었고, 위쪽이 잘려 나간 이인승 마차도 아니었다. 그러면서도 이 모든 기계 하나하나의 특징을 조금씩 지니고 있었다. 그것은 밝은 노란색으로 칠해져 있었고, 끌채와 바퀴는 검은색으로 도드라지게 그려져 있었다. 마부는 정통 스포츠 양식으로, 난간보다 두 자쯤 위로 쌓아 올린 방석 위에 앉아 있었다. 말은 적갈색으로, 제법 잘생긴 짐승이었으나, 그럼에도 어딘가 번지르르하고 투견판 분위기 같은 것이 풍겼으니, 그것은 마차와 그 주인 양쪽에 다 어울렸다.\n\n주인 자신은 마흔쯤 된 사내로, 검은 머리에 정성껏 빗어 넘긴 구레나룻을 기르고 있었다. 그는 유난히 화려한 차림이었고, 몸에는 보석붙이를 잔뜩 달고 있었는데—모두 신사들이 보통 차고 다니는 것보다 세 치수쯤 큰 것들이었다—그 모든 것 위에 거친 외투를 걸쳐 마무리를 지었다. 그는 마차에서 내리자마자 이 외투의 한쪽 주머니에 왼손을 찔러 넣고, 다른 주머니에서 오른손으로 무척 밝고 번쩍이는 비단 손수건을 꺼내, 그것으로 자기 장화에서 먼지 한두 점을 털어 내더니, 그것을 손안에 구겨 쥐고는 으스대며 안마당으로 걸어 올라갔다.\n\n이 사람이 마차에서 내릴 때, 앞서 길 건너편에서 어슬렁거리며 단추가 여럿 떨어져 나간 갈색 외투를 입은 초라한 행색의 사내 하나가 길을 건너와 바로 곁에 멈춰 선 것이 샘의 눈을 벗어나지는 않았다. 그 신사가 찾아온 목적에 대해 의심을 넘어선 무엇을 품고 있던 샘은 그보다 앞서 조지 앤드 벌처로 들어가, 홱 돌아서서 문간 한가운데에 떡 버티고 섰다.\n\n\"이봐, 젊은이!\" 거친 외투를 입은 사내가 위압적인 어조로 말하면서, 동시에 밀치고 지나가려 했다.\n\n\"이봐요, 선생, 무슨 일이슈?\" 샘이 그 떠밂을 이자까지 쳐서 되갚으며 대꾸했다.\n\n\"이봐, 그만두게, 이 사람아. 나한테는 그게 안 통해.\" 거친 외투의 임자가 목소리를 높이며 새파랗게 질려 말했다. \"여기, 스마우치!\"\n\n\"왜, 무슨 일이오?\" 갈색 외투를 입은 사내가 으르렁거리며 말했으니, 그는 이 짧은 대화가 오가는 동안 슬금슬금 안마당으로 다가와 있었다.\n\n\"이 젊은 녀석이 좀 무례하게 굴어서 그래.\" 우두머리가 샘을 한 번 더 밀치며 말했다.\n\n\"자, 그딴 수작 부리지 마.\" 스마우치가 으르렁거리며 그를 또 한 번, 그것도 더 세게 밀쳤다.\n\n이 마지막 떠밂은 노련한 스마우치 씨가 의도한 대로의 효과를 냈다. 샘이 그 신사에게 인사를 되갚고 싶어 그자의 몸을 문기둥에 짓이기고 있는 사이, 우두머리가 슬그머니 지나쳐 술청으로 길을 냈으니, 샘은 스마우치 씨와 몇 마디 욕설을 주고받은 뒤 곧장 그 뒤를 따랐다.\n\n\"안녕하시오, 아가씨.\" 우두머리가 보터니 만의 느긋함과 뉴사우스웨일스의 점잖음으로 술청에 있는 젊은 여자에게 말을 건넸다. \"픽윅 씨 방이 어디요, 아가씨?\"\n\n* 보터니 만, 뉴사우스웨일스: 오스트레일리아의 죄수 유형지로, 화자의 출신을 빈정대는 표현이다.\n\n\"안내해 드려요.\" 술청 아가씨가 그 멋쟁이의 물음에 답하면서, 그를 두 번 다시 쳐다볼 것도 없다는 듯 웨이터에게 말했다.\n웨이터가 일러 준 대로 앞장서서 위층으로 길을 안내했고, 거친 외투를 입은 사내가 그 뒤를 따랐으며, 샘이 그 뒤를 따랐으니, 샘은 계단을 올라가는 동안 더할 나위 없는 경멸과 도전의 갖가지 몸짓을 마음껏 보여, 하인들과 구경하던 다른 이들을 이루 말할 수 없이 흐뭇하게 했다. 쉰 기침으로 고생하던 스마우치 씨는 아래에 남아 통로에다 가래를 뱉었다.\n\n이른 방문객이 샘을 뒤따라 방에 들어섰을 때 픽윅 씨는 침대에서 곤히 잠들어 있었다. 그들이 그러면서 낸 소리에 그는 잠을 깼다.\n\n\"면도할 물, 샘.\" 픽윅 씨가 휘장 안에서 말했다.\n\n\"당장 면도해 드리지요, 픽윅 씨.\" 방문객이 침대 머리맡의 휘장 하나를 젖히며 말했다. \"당신을 상대로 한 집행 영장을 가지고 왔소, 바델의 소송 건으로요.—여기 영장이오.—민사 법원이오.—여기 내 명함이오. 우리 집으로 가 주셔야겠소이다.\" 픽윅 씨의 어깨를 다정하게 한 번 툭 치고는, 그 집행관은(그가 바로 그런 사람이었다) 명함을 침대보 위에 던지고 조끼 주머니에서 금이쑤시개를 꺼냈다.\n\n\"이름은 냄비요.\" 픽윅 씨가 베개 밑에서 안경을 꺼내 쓰고 명함을 읽는 동안, 그 집달리가 말했다. \"콜먼 가, 벨 골목, 냄비요.\"\n\n이 대목에서, 그때까지 냄비 씨의 번쩍이는 비버 모자에 눈을 박고 있던 샘 웰러가 끼어들었다.\n\n\"당신, 퀘이커요?\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n* 퀘이커: 모자를 벗지 않는 관습으로 알려진 종파로, 모자를 쓴 채 있는 냄비를 빈정대는 말이다.\n\"내가 너랑 끝장을 보기 전에 내가 퀘이커인지 아닌지 알려 주마.\" 분노한 집행관이 대꾸했다. \"조만간 어느 좋은 아침에 너한테 예의범절을 가르쳐 주마, 이 녀석아.\"\n\n\"고맙수.\" 샘이 말했다. \"나도 똑같이 해 드리지. 모자 벗으시오.\" 이 말과 함께 웰러 씨는 더없이 능란한 솜씨로 냄비 씨의 모자를 방 저쪽으로 후려쳐 날렸으니, 그 기세가 어찌나 거셌던지 하마터면 그가 금이쑤시개까지 덤으로 삼켜 버릴 뻔했다.\n\n\"이걸 똑똑히 봐 두시오, 픽윅 씨.\" 당황한 집행관이 숨을 헐떡이며 말했다. \"내가 당신 방에서 당신 하인에게 직무 집행 중에 폭행을 당했소. 신변에 위협을 느끼오. 이걸 증인으로 서 주시오.\"\n\n\"아무것도 증언하지 마쇼, 선생.\" 샘이 끼어들었다. \"눈을 꼭 감으쇼, 선생. 창밖으로 내던져 버리고 싶지만, 바깥 납지붕 때문에 충분히 멀리 떨어지지도 못할 테니 관두지.\"\n\n\"샘.\" 픽윅 씨가 그의 시종이 적대적인 갖가지 시위를 벌이자 성난 목소리로 말했다. \"네가 한마디만 더 하거나, 이 사람에게 조금이라도 간섭하려 들면, 그 즉시 너를 내보내겠다.\"\n\n\"하지만, 어르신!\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n\"입 다물어라.\" 픽윅 씨가 끼어들었다. \"저 모자 도로 주워 드려라.\"\n\n그러나 샘은 이를 딱 잘라 단호히 거부했고, 주인에게 호되게 꾸지람을 들은 뒤, 집행관은 바쁜 처지라 황송하게도 손수 모자를 주워 들면서, 그러는 내내 샘에게 온갖 위협을 늘어놓았으니, 그 신사는 그것을 완벽한 침착함으로 받아넘기며, 다만 냄비 씨가 모자를 다시 쓰는 친절을 베풀어만 주신다면 그것을 다음 주 끝물까지 날려 버리겠노라고 한마디 했을 따름이다. 냄비 씨는 그런 절차가 자신에게 불편을 초래할지도 모른다고 생각했는지, 그 유혹을 내밀기를 사양했고, 곧이어 스마우치를 불러올렸다. 체포가 이루어졌으며 죄수가 옷을 다 입을 때까지 기다리고 있으라고 그에게 일러 준 뒤, 냄비는 으스대며 나가 마차를 몰고 떠나 버렸다. 스마우치는 픽윅 씨에게 무뚝뚝한 태도로 \"지금이 바쁜 때이니 되도록 빨리 서두르시오\"라고 청하고는, 문 옆에 의자를 끌어다 놓고, 그가 옷을 다 입을 때까지 거기 앉아 있었다. 그러고 나서 샘은 삯마차를 부르러 보내졌고, 세 사람은 그것을 타고 콜먼 가로 향했다. 거리가 짧았던 것이 다행이었으니, 스마우치 씨는 그다지 매혹적인 대화 능력을 지니지 못한 데다, 우리가 다른 데서 언급한 바 있는 신체적 약점 탓에 좁은 공간 안에서는 결정적으로 불쾌한 동행이 되었기 때문이다.\n\n마차는 무척 좁고 어두운 거리로 접어들더니, 창마다 쇠창살이 둘린 집 앞에 멈춰 섰다. 그 집 문기둥은 \"냄비, 런던 주 장관 직속 집행관\"이라는 이름과 직함으로 장식되어 있었다. 안쪽 문은 스마우치 씨의 방치된 쌍둥이 형제라 해도 통할 만한 신사가 열어 주었는데, 그는 그 목적을 위해 큼직한 열쇠 하나를 지니고 있었으며, 픽윅 씨는 \"커피실\"로 안내되었다.\n\n이 커피실은 앞쪽 응접실로, 그 주된 특징이라면 갓 깐 모래와 케케묵은 담배 연기였다. 픽윅 씨는 들어설 때 거기 앉아 있던 세 사람에게 인사를 건넸고, 샘을 퍼커에게 보내고 나서 으슥한 구석으로 물러나, 거기서 다소 호기심 어린 눈으로 자신의 새 동료들을 바라보았다.\n\n이들 가운데 하나는 열아홉이나 스무 살밖에 안 된 한낱 소년이었는데, 아직 열 시도 채 안 되었건만 진토닉을 마시고 시가를 피우고 있었으니—그 벌겋게 달아오른 얼굴로 미루어 보건대, 지난 한두 해 동안 줄곧 이 오락에 꽤나 골몰해 온 모양이었다. 그 맞은편에서는, 오른쪽 장화 끝으로 불을 쑤석거리고 있는, 누르께한 얼굴에 거친 목소리를 가진 서른쯤 된 상스럽고 천박한 젊은이가 있었으니, 술집 응접실과 싸구려 당구대에서 얻을 수 있는 그런 세상 물정과 사람을 홀리는 거리낌 없는 태도를 분명히 지니고 있었다. 그 방의 세 번째 거주자는 무척 낡은 검은 양복을 입은 중년 사내로, 창백하고 초췌한 얼굴로 쉴 새 없이 방 안을 오락가락하다가, 이따금 멈춰 서서 누군가를 기다리기라도 하듯 무척 초조하게 창밖을 내다보고는, 다시 걸음을 이어 갔다.\n\n\"오늘 아침에는 제 면도칼을 빌려 쓰시는 게 좋겠수다, 에어슬리 씨.\" 불을 쑤석거리던 사내가 그의 친구인 소년에게 눈을 찡긋해 보이며 말했다.\n\n\"고맙소만, 됐소. 필요 없을 거요. 한 시간 남짓이면 나갈 것 같으니까.\" 상대가 다급한 태도로 대꾸했다. 그러고는 다시 창가로 걸어가, 또 한 번 실망한 채 돌아오더니, 깊이 한숨을 내쉬고 방을 나가 버렸다. 그러자 나머지 두 사람은 와락 큰 소리로 웃음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n\"이거 원, 저런 꼴은 난생처음 보네.\" 면도칼을 권했던 신사가 말했으니, 그의 이름은 프라이스인 듯했다. \"정말이지!\" 프라이스 씨는 욕설을 한마디 보태어 그 단언을 다지고는 다시 웃었으며, 물론 그러자 소년도(자기 동료를 세상에서 가장 멋진 친구 가운데 하나로 여기던) 따라 웃었다.\n\n\"당신은 잘 짐작도 못 하실 거요, 안 그렇소?\" 프라이스가 픽윅 씨 쪽으로 돌아서며 말했다. \"저 친구가 어제로 여기 들어온 지 일주일째인데, 아직 한 번도 면도를 안 했다는 거 말이오. 삼십 분이면 나갈 거라고 어찌나 굳게 믿는지, 집에 가서 할 때까지 미뤄 두는 편이 낫겠다 싶은 거지요?\"\n\n\"가엾은 사람이군요!\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"정말로 그가 곤경에서 벗어날 가능성이 그렇게나 크답니까?\"\n\n\"가능성이라니, 제기랄.\" 프라이스가 대꾸했다. \"그런 건 손톱만큼도 없소. 지금부터 십 년 뒤 그가 거리를 활보할 가능성에 나라면 이것만큼도 안 걸겠소.\" 이 말과 함께 프라이스 씨는 경멸하듯 손가락을 딱 튕기고는 종을 울렸다.\n\n\"종이 한 장 주게, 크루키.\" 프라이스 씨가 시중꾼에게 말했으니, 그자는 차림새며 전반적인 행색이 파산한 유리장이와 지급 불능에 빠진 가축 장수 사이쯤 되어 보였다. \"그리고 브랜디토닉 한 잔도, 크루키, 알겠나? 우리 아버지한테 편지를 쓸 참인데, 자극제가 좀 있어야겠어. 안 그러면 그 영감을 충분히 세게 몰아붙일 수가 없거든.\" 이 익살스러운 말에, 그 어린 소년이 그야말로 자지러졌으리라는 것은 굳이 말할 것도 없다.\n\n\"그래야지.\" 프라이스 씨가 말했다. \"결코 죽는소리 하지 말 것. 다 장난질이지, 안 그래?\"\n\n\"끝내주죠!\" 그 젊은 신사가 말했다.\n\n\"자넨 기개가 좀 있어, 그래.\" 프라이스가 말했다. \"세상을 좀 본 게야.\"\n\n\"그런 것 같습니다!\" 소년이 대꾸했다. 그는 술집 문의 더러운 유리창 너머로 세상을 본 것이었다.\n\n이 대화에, 그리고 그것을 주고받은 두 인간의 태도와 분위기에 적잖이 넌더리가 난 픽윅 씨는, 따로 쓸 수 있는 응접실은 없겠느냐고 물어보려던 참인데, 점잖은 행색의 낯선 사람 두셋이 들어왔으니, 그들을 보자 소년은 시가를 불 속에 던져 버리고, 프라이스 씨에게 저들이 자기 일을 \"다 해결해\" 주러 왔다고 속삭이고는, 방 저편 끝의 탁자에 앉은 그들과 합류했다.\n\n그러나 일은 그 젊은 신사가 기대한 만큼 그렇게 빨리 다 해결될 것 같지는 않아 보였다. 무척 긴 대화가 이어졌고, 픽윅 씨는 방탕한 행실과 거듭된 용서에 관한 성난 단편들을 더러 듣지 않을 수 없었기 때문이다. 마침내 일행 중 가장 나이 든 신사가 화이트크로스 가의 어떤 곳을 무척 분명하게 들먹였으니, 그러자 그 젊은 신사는 자신의 끝내주는 기개에도, 그 패기에도, 게다가 세상 물정에 대한 식견에도 아랑곳없이, 머리를 탁자에 처박고 처량하게 울부짖었다.\n\n* 화이트크로스 가: 채무자 감옥이 있던 곳으로, 그곳에 수감되리라는 위협을 가리킨다.\n이렇게 젊은이의 기세가 별안간 꺾이고, 그 거드름이 효과적으로 꺾인 것에 무척 흡족해진 픽윅 씨는, 종을 울려 자신의 요청대로 양탄자와 탁자, 의자, 찬장, 소파가 갖추어지고, 거울과 갖가지 오래된 판화로 장식된 개인실로 안내되었다. 여기서 그는 아침 식사가 차려지는 동안 위층에서 냄비 부인이 사각 피아노를 연주하는 것을 들을 호사를 누렸다. 식사가 나왔을 때 퍼커 씨도 함께 왔다.\n\n\"아하, 친애하는 선생.\" 그 작은 사내가 말했다. \"마침내 붙들리셨군요, 응? 자, 자, 저도 그게 섭섭하지만은 않습니다. 이제야 선생께서 이 행동이 얼마나 어처구니없는지 아실 테니까요. 이번 신병 구속 영장이 발부된 명목인 과세 비용과 손해 배상액을 제가 적어 두었으니, 지체 없이 당장 정산하는 편이 낫겠습니다. 지금쯤이면 냄비도 집에 돌아왔겠지요, 아마. 어떻게 하시겠습니까, 친애하는 선생? 제가 수표를 발행할까요, 아니면 선생께서 하시겠습니까?\" 그 작은 사내는 이렇게 말하며 짐짓 명랑한 척 두 손을 비볐으나, 픽윅 씨의 표정을 흘끔 보고는 동시에 샘 웰러 쪽으로 낙담한 눈길을 던지지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n\"퍼커.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"이 이야기는 더 이상 듣지 않게 해 주게, 부탁이네. 여기 머물러 봐야 아무 득도 없어 보이니, 오늘 밤 감옥으로 가겠네.\"\n\n\"화이트크로스 가로 가셔서는 안 됩니다, 친애하는 선생.\" 퍼커가 말했다. \"안 됩니다! 한 방에 침대가 예순 개나 되고, 빗장은 하루 스물네 시간 가운데 열여섯 시간이나 걸려 있습니다.\"\n\n\"가능하다면 다른 어떤 구금 장소로 가는 편이 좋겠네.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"그럴 수 없다면 그곳에서나마 최선을 다해 보아야겠지.\"\n\n\"기어이 어딘가로 가시겠다면, 플리트로 가실 수 있습니다, 친애하는 선생.\" 퍼커가 말했다.\n\n\"그거면 됐네.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"아침 식사를 마치는 대로 곧장 그리로 가겠네.\"\n\n\"잠깐, 잠깐만요, 친애하는 선생. 다른 대부분의 사람들이 그토록 빠져나오려고 안달하는 곳에 그렇게 맹렬히 서둘러 들어가실 까닭은 조금도 없습니다.\" 마음씨 좋은 그 작은 변호사가 말했다. \"인신 보호 영장을 받아야 합니다. 오늘 오후 네 시 전에는 판사실에 판사가 없을 겁니다. 그때까지 기다리셔야 합니다.\"\n\n\"아주 좋네.\" 픽윅 씨가 흔들림 없는 인내심으로 말했다. \"그러면 두 시에 여기서 갈비를 좀 먹세. 그 일을 좀 챙겨라, 샘. 그리고 시간을 어기지 말라고 일러 두어라.\"\n\n퍼커의 온갖 항의와 설득에도 불구하고 픽윅 씨가 단호한 태도를 굽히지 않자, 갈비는 제때에 나왔다가 사라졌다. 그러고 나서 그는 또 다른 삯마차에 태워져 챈서리 레인으로 끌려갔으니, 그 전에 냄비 씨를 삼십 분쯤 기다려야 했는데, 그는 엄선한 만찬 모임을 벌이고 있던 터라 그 전에는 무슨 일이 있어도 방해받을 수 없었기 때문이다.\n\n서전트 인에는 판사 두 사람이 출석해 있었으니—하나는 왕좌 법원, 하나는 민사 법원이었다—그들 앞에서는 적잖은 일이 처리되고 있는 듯했으니, 서류 뭉치를 들고 부산히 드나드는 법률 사무원들의 수가 무슨 잣대라도 된다면 말이다. 그들이 그 법학원 입구를 이루는 나지막한 아치문에 이르렀을 때, 퍼커는 마부와 삯과 거스름돈을 두고 흥정하느라 잠시 붙들려 있었다. 그래서 픽윅 씨는 드나드는 사람들의 물결을 피하려고 한쪽으로 비켜서서, 다소 호기심 어린 눈으로 주위를 둘러보았다.\n\n그의 주의를 가장 끈 사람들은 초라하지만 점잖은 행색의 사내 서넛이었는데, 지나가는 변호사들 여럿에게 모자를 살짝 들어 인사하는 것이 거기서 무슨 볼일이 있는 듯했으나, 그 일의 성격을 픽윅 씨로서는 짐작할 수 없었다. 그들은 묘하게 생긴 친구들이었다. 하나는 낡아 빛바랜 검정 옷에 흰 목수건을 두른, 호리호리하고 다리를 좀 저는 사내였다. 또 하나는 똑같은 옷차림에 목둘레에 불그스레한 검정 천을 두른, 떡 벌어지고 우람한 자였다. 세 번째는 여드름투성이 얼굴에, 술에 찌든 듯한, 자그맣고 비쩍 마른 자였다. 그들은 뒷짐을 진 채 어슬렁거리다가, 이따금 초조한 낯빛으로, 서류를 든 신사들이 바삐 지나갈 때 그 귀에 대고 무언가를 속삭였다. 픽윅 씨는 자신이 그 앞을 지나다닐 때 그들이 아치문 아래에서 빈둥거리는 것을 무척 자주 본 적이 있음을 떠올렸다. 그래서 이 추레해 보이는 빈둥거리는 자들이 도대체 그 직업의 어느 갈래에 속할 수 있는지 알고 싶은 호기심이 잔뜩 일었다.\n\n그는 새끼손가락에 낀 큼직한 금반지를 빨면서 그의 곁에 바짝 붙어 있던 냄비에게 그 물음을 던지려던 참인데, 퍼커가 부산히 다가와 지체할 시간이 없다고 하면서 앞장서서 법학원 안으로 길을 안내했다. 픽윅 씨가 뒤를 따르는데, 다리를 저는 사내가 그에게 다가와 공손히 모자를 살짝 들고는 글씨가 적힌 명함 한 장을 내밀었으니, 픽윅 씨는 거절해서 그 사내의 기분을 상하게 하고 싶지 않아 정중히 그것을 받아 조끼 주머니에 넣었다.\n\n\"자.\" 퍼커가 사무실 하나로 들어가기 전에 돌아서서, 동행들이 바로 뒤에 따라붙었는지 확인하며 말했다. \"이리로 드시지요, 친애하는 선생. 아니, 자넨 뭘 원하나?\"\n\n이 마지막 물음은 다리를 저는 사내에게 던진 것이었으니, 그는 픽윅 씨도 모르는 새에 일행에 끼어 있었다. 그에 대한 대답으로, 다리를 저는 사내는 상상할 수 있는 한 더없이 공손하게 다시 모자를 살짝 들고는 픽윅 씨 쪽을 가리켰다.\n\n\"아니, 아니야.\" 퍼커가 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"우린 자네가 필요 없네, 이보게, 자네가 필요 없어.\"\n\n\"실례합니다, 나리.\" 다리를 저는 사내가 말했다. \"이 신사분께서 제 명함을 받으셨습니다. 저를 써 주시길 바랍니다, 나리. 이 신사분께서 제게 고개를 끄덕이셨어요. 이 신사분 본인의 판단에 맡기겠습니다. 제게 고개를 끄덕이셨지요, 나리?\"\n\n\"쳇, 쳇, 말도 안 되는 소리. 자넨 아무에게도 고개를 안 끄덕였지, 픽윅? 착각이야, 착각.\" 퍼커가 말했다.\n\n\"이 신사분이 내게 자기 명함을 건넸네.\" 픽윅 씨가 조끼 주머니에서 그것을 꺼내며 대꾸했다. \"이 신사분이 그러길 바라는 듯하기에 받았네.—사실 짬이 날 때 한번 들여다보고 싶은 호기심이 좀 있었거든. 나는—\"\n\n그 작은 변호사가 와락 큰 소리로 웃음을 터뜨리며 명함을 다리를 저는 사내에게 돌려주고, 그것이 다 착각이라고 일러 주고는, 사내가 분이 나서 돌아서는 사이 픽윅 씨에게 그자가 한낱 보석 보증인일 뿐이라고 속삭였다.\n\n\"한낱 뭐라고!\" 픽윅 씨가 소리쳤다.\n\n\"보석 보증인이지요.\" 퍼커가 대꾸했다.\n\n\"보석 보증인이라니!\"\n\"그렇습니다, 친애하는 선생—여기 그런 자들이 대여섯은 됩니다. 액수가 얼마든 선생을 위해 보증을 서 주고, 고작 반 크라운만 받지요. 묘한 장사지요, 그렇잖습니까?\" 퍼커가 코담배를 한 줌 즐기며 말했다.\n\n\"뭐라고요! 그러니까 이 사람들이 여기서 어슬렁거리며 한 건당 반 크라운씩 받고 이 나라의 판사들 앞에서 위증을 하는 것으로 생계를 꾸린다고 이해하면 되는 겁니까?\" 픽윅 씨가 그 폭로에 그만 아연실색하여 소리쳤다.\n\n\"글쎄요, 위증이라고까지는 잘 모르겠습니다만, 친애하는 선생.\" 그 작은 신사가 대꾸했다. \"험한 말씀입니다, 친애하는 선생, 참으로 험한 말씀이에요. 그건 법률상의 의제일 뿐, 그 이상 아무것도 아닙니다, 친애하는 선생.\" 이렇게 말하면서 변호사는 어깨를 으쓱하고는 미소를 짓고, 코담배를 한 줌 더 들이마시고는, 앞장서서 판사 서기의 사무실로 길을 안내했다.\n\n이곳은 유난히 더러운 행색의 방으로, 천장이 무척 낮고 벽은 오래된 판자로 덧대어져 있었으며, 어찌나 조명이 형편없던지 바깥은 대낮인데도 책상 위에는 굵직한 수지 양초들이 타오르고 있었다. 한쪽 끝에는 판사의 개인실로 통하는 문이 있었고, 그 둘레에는 변호사와 사무 주임들이 한 무리 모여 있었으니, 그들은 각자의 약속이 명부에 오른 순서대로 불려 들어갔다. 한 무리를 내보내려고 이 문이 열릴 때마다, 다음 무리가 들어가려고 맹렬히 돌진했다. 거기다 판사를 만나려고 기다리는 신사들끼리 오가는 무수한 대화에 더해, 그를 만나고 나온 사람들 대부분 사이에서 갖가지 개인적인 말다툼까지 벌어졌으니, 그렇게 비좁은 방 안에서 일으킬 수 있는 한 가장 요란한 소음이 일었다.\n\n이 신사들의 대화만이 귀를 때리는 유일한 소리는 아니었다. 방의 또 다른 끝에서, 나무 난간 뒤 상자 위에 올라선 안경 쓴 서기 하나가 \"선서 진술서를 받고\" 있었으니, 그것은 한 무더기씩 이따금 또 다른 서기에 의해 판사의 서명을 받으러 개인실로 옮겨졌다. 선서를 해야 할 변호사 사무원들이 워낙 많았고, 그들 모두에게 한꺼번에 선서를 시키기란 도덕적으로 불가능한 일이었던지라, 그 안경 쓴 서기에게 다가가려는 이 신사들의 몸부림은, 자비로우신 폐하께서 왕림하시는 영광을 베푸실 때 극장의 일반석 입구로 비집고 들어가려는 군중의 그것과 같았다. 또 다른 직원 하나는 판사의 서명을 받은 뒤 선서 진술서를 돌려주려고, 이따금 선서를 마친 자들의 이름을 부르느라 폐를 혹사했으니, 이는 또 몇 차례의 실랑이를 불러일으켰다. 그리고 이 모든 일이 동시에 벌어지면서, 더없이 활동적이고 흥분 잘하는 사람이라도 흡족하게 구경할 만큼의 소란이 일었다. 또 다른 부류의 사람들도 있었으니—자기 고용주가 신청한 소환에 출석하려고 기다리는 자들로, 그 소환에는 상대편 변호사가 출석하든 말든 자유였으며—그들이 할 일은 이따금 상대편 변호사의 이름을 외쳐, 그가 자기들 모르는 새에 출석해 있지는 않은지 확인하는 것이었다.\n\n이를테면 이런 식이었다. 픽윅 씨가 자리 잡은 좌석 바로 옆 벽에 기대어, 테너 목소리를 가진 열네 살짜리 사무소 사환이 있었다. 그 근처에는 베이스 목소리를 가진 보통법 사무원이 있었다.\n\n한 사무원이 서류 뭉치를 들고 황급히 들어와 두리번거렸다.\n\n\"스니글 앤드 블링크.\" 테너가 외쳤다.\n\n\"포킨 앤드 스놉.\" 베이스가 으르렁거렸다.\n\n\"스텀피 앤드 디컨.\" 새로 온 자가 말했다.\n\n아무도 대답하지 않았다. 다음에 들어온 사람은 셋 모두에게 호명되었다. 그러자 그도 자기 차례에 또 다른 회사 이름을 외쳤고, 그러자 누군가 또 다른 회사 이름을 큰 소리로 외쳐 댔으며, 이런 식이었다.\n\n이 내내, 안경 쓴 사내는 사무원들에게 선서를 시키느라 분주히 일하고 있었으니, 그 선서는 어김없이 구두점이라고는 찍을 생각도 없이, 대개 다음과 같은 식으로 집행되었다.—\n\n\"오른손에 성서를 잡으시오 이게 당신 이름이고 필적이오 당신은 이 선서 진술서의 내용이 사실임을 맹세하니 그러므로 하느님이 도우시리라 일 실링이오 잔돈은 알아서 마련하시오 나한테는 없으니.\"\n\n\"이보게, 샘.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"인신 보호 영장인지 뭔지를 준비하고 있는 모양이지?\"\n\n\"네.\" 샘이 말했다. \"어서 그 시체-있나 보자 영장인지 뭔지를 가져왔으면 좋겠네요. 우릴 여기 이렇게 기다리게 하다니 참 고약하지 뭡니까. 저 같으면 이맘때쯤이면 시체-있나 보자 영장을 대여섯 장은 다 준비해서, 포장까지 싹 해 놓았을 텐데요.\"\n\n* 샘은 'habeas-corpus'(인신 보호 영장)를 'have-his-carcase'(그자의 시체를 가져와라)로 잘못 알아들어 부린 말장난이다.\n\n샘 웰러가 인신 보호 영장을 어떤 종류의 다루기 거추장스럽고 거북한 기계로 상상했는지는 드러나지 않는다. 바로 그 순간 퍼커가 다가와 픽윅 씨를 데려갔기 때문이다.\n통상의 절차가 다 끝나자, 새뮤얼 픽윅의 신병은 곧이어 집달관에게 인도되어, 그에 의해 플리트 감옥의 간수장에게 넘겨졌으니, 바델 대 픽윅 소송의 손해 배상액과 비용 전액이 완납되어 변제될 때까지 그곳에 구금될 것이었다.\n\n\"그러자면,\" 픽윅 씨가 웃으며 말했다. \"아주 오랜 시간이 걸리겠지. 샘, 삯마차를 또 한 대 불러라. 퍼커, 친애하는 친구여, 잘 있게.\"\n\n\"제가 함께 가서 무사히 도착하시는 걸 보겠습니다.\" 퍼커가 말했다.\n\n\"실은,\" 픽윅 씨가 대꾸했다. \"샘 말고는 다른 누구의 시중도 없이 가는 편이 좋겠네. 자리를 잡는 대로 편지를 써서 알려 줄 테니, 그러면 즉시 와 주게나. 그때까지, 잘 있게.\"\n\n픽윅 씨는 이렇게 말하면서 그새 도착해 있던 마차에 올랐고, 집달관이 뒤를 따랐다. 샘이 마부석에 자리 잡자, 마차는 굴러 떠나갔다.\n\n\"참으로 별난 양반이로군!\" 퍼커가 장갑을 끼느라 멈춰 서서 말했다.\n\n\"파산자로 치면 어땠겠습니까, 선생님.\" 가까이 서 있던 로턴 씨가 한마디 했다. \"파산 위원들을 얼마나 곤란하게 했을까요! 그들이 그를 수감하겠다고 말이라도 꺼냈다간, 보란 듯이 들이받았을 겁니다, 선생님.\"\n\n변호사는 픽윅 씨의 인품에 대한 자기 사무원의 그런 직업적 평가가 그다지 마음에 들지 않는 듯, 아무 대꾸도 황송히 베풀지 않은 채 걸어가 버렸다.\n\n삯마차는 삯마차가 흔히 그러하듯 덜커덩거리며 플리트 가를 따라 나아갔다. 마부의 말로는 앞에 무언가 있을 때 말들이 \"더 잘 갔다\"는데(아무것도 없을 때는 어지간히 무시무시한 속도로 달렸을 게 틀림없다), 그래서 마차는 짐수레 한 대 뒤를 따라갔다. 짐수레가 멈추면 마차도 멈췄고, 짐수레가 다시 가면 마차도 그리했다. 픽윅 씨는 집달관 맞은편에 앉아 있었고, 집달관은 무릎 사이에 모자를 끼운 채 가락을 휘파람으로 불며 마차 창밖을 내다보고 앉아 있었다.\n\n세월은 기적을 일으킨다. 그 막강한 노신사의 도움으로, 삯마차조차 반 마일의 거리를 헤쳐 나간다. 마침내 그들은 멈춰 섰고, 픽윅 씨는 플리트 정문 앞에서 내렸다.\n\n집달관은 자기가 맡은 자가 바로 발뒤꿈치에 따라오는지 어깨너머로 흘끔 살피고는, 픽윅 씨보다 앞서 감옥 안으로 들어갔다. 들어선 뒤 왼쪽으로 돌아, 열린 문을 지나 로비로 들어섰는데, 그곳에서는 그들이 들어온 문의 맞은편에 있는, 열쇠를 손에 든 건장한 간수가 지키고 선 묵직한 문이 곧장 감옥 내부로 통했다.\n\n여기서 그들은 멈춰 섰고, 그동안 집달관은 자기 서류를 인계했다. 그리고 여기서 픽윅 씨는, 그 방면에 정통한 자들에게 \"초상화 모델로 앉기\"라고 알려진 의식을 치를 때까지 머물러 있어야 한다는 통보를 받았다.\n\n\"초상화 모델로 앉는다고요?\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"당신 얼굴 모습을 떠 두는 겁니다, 나리.\" 건장한 간수가 대꾸했다.\n\n\"우린 여기서 얼굴 떠내는 데 솜씨가 일품이지요. 눈 깜짝할 새에 떠내고, 언제나 정확합니다. 들어오세요, 나리, 편히 앉으시고요.\"\n\n픽윅 씨는 그 권유에 따라 자리에 앉았다. 그러자 의자 등받이 뒤에 자리 잡은 웰러 씨가, 모델로 앉는 것이란 여러 간수들에게 검사를 받는 것을 달리 일컫는 말일 뿐이며, 그래야 그들이 죄수와 방문객을 구분할 수 있다고 속삭였다.\n\n\"음, 샘.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"그럼 화가들이 어서 왔으면 좋겠구나. 여기는 사람이 좀 많은 곳이로다.\"\n\n\"오래 안 걸릴 겁니다, 어르신, 아마요.\" 샘이 대꾸했다. \"저기 네덜란드제 시계가 있네요, 어르신.\"\n\n\"그래, 보이는구나.\" 픽윅 씨가 한마디 했다.\n\n\"그리고 새장도 있어요, 어르신.\" 샘이 말했다. \"바퀴 속의 바퀴랄까, 감옥 속의 감옥이지요. 안 그렇습니까, 어르신?\"\n\n웰러 씨가 이 철학적인 한마디를 하는 동안, 픽윅 씨는 자기 모델 앉기가 시작되었음을 알아챘다. 자물쇠 당번에서 풀려난 건장한 간수가 자리에 앉아, 이따금 무심하게 그를 쳐다보았고, 그를 교대한 키 크고 마른 사내는 두 손을 외투 자락 밑에 찔러 넣고 맞은편에 떡 버티고 서서 그를 한참 동안 찬찬히 살펴보았다. 다소 무뚝뚝해 보이는 세 번째 신사는, 들어올 때 빵 껍질에 버터 바른 것의 마지막 조각을 처치하고 있었던 것으로 보아 차를 마시다 방해받은 모양인데, 픽윅 씨 바로 곁에 자리 잡고는 두 손을 허리에 짚은 채 그를 면밀히 검사했다. 한편 다른 두 사람도 그 무리에 끼어, 더없이 골똘하고 신중한 낯빛으로 그의 이목구비를 연구했다. 픽윅 씨는 그 작업 내내 꽤나 움찔거렸고, 의자에 무척 불편하게 앉아 있는 듯했으나, 그것이 진행되는 동안 누구에게도, 심지어 샘에게조차 아무 말도 하지 않았으니, 샘은 의자 등받이에 기댄 채, 한편으로는 주인의 처지를 곰곰이 생각하고, 한편으로는 거기 모인 모든 간수들을 하나씩 차례로 사납게 들이받을 수 있다면—만약 그렇게 하는 것이 합법적이고 평화로운 일이기만 하다면—얼마나 큰 만족을 안겨 줄까를 곱씹고 있었다.\n\n마침내 얼굴 모습 떠내기가 완료되었고, 픽윅 씨는 이제 감옥 안으로 들어가도 좋다는 통보를 받았다.\n\n\"오늘 밤은 어디서 자야 하오?\" 픽윅 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"글쎄요, 오늘 밤은 잘 모르겠습니다.\" 건장한 간수가 대꾸했다. \"내일이면 누군가와 한방을 쓰게 배정될 테니, 그러면 아늑하고 편안하게 지내실 겁니다. 첫날 밤은 으레 좀 어수선하지만, 내일이면 다 자리가 잡힐 겁니다.\"\n\n이런저런 의논 끝에, 간수들 중 하나에게 세놓을 침대가 하나 있어, 픽윅 씨가 그날 밤 그것을 쓸 수 있음이 드러났다. 그는 기꺼이 그것을 빌리기로 했다.\n\n\"저랑 같이 가시면 당장 보여 드리지요.\" 그 사내가 말했다. \"큰 건 아니지만, 자는 데는 끝내주는 자립니다. 이쪽입니다, 나리.\"\n\n그들은 안쪽 문을 지나 짧은 계단을 내려갔다. 그들 뒤에서 열쇠가 돌아갔다. 그리하여 픽윅 씨는 난생처음으로 채무자 감옥의 담장 안에 있는 자신을 발견했다.\n\n픽윅 씨를 따라 감옥으로 들어온 신사인 톰 로커 씨는, 그 짧은 층계의 맨 아래에 이르자 오른쪽으로 홱 돌아서서, 열려 있는 철문을 지나 또 다른 짧은 층계를 올라가, 더럽고 천장이 낮으며 돌로 포장된, 그리고 양쪽 먼 끝에 난 창 하나로만 매우 흐릿하게 밝혀진 길고 좁은 복도로 앞장서 안내했다.\n\n\"여기가,\" 신사는 두 손을 주머니에 찔러 넣고 어깨너머로 픽윅 씨를 무심히 돌아보며 말했다. \"여기가 바로 일층 복도지요.\"\n\n\"오,\" 픽윅 씨는 어둡고 더러운 계단을 내려다보며 대답했는데, 그 계단은 땅 밑으로 죽 이어진 축축하고 음침한 돌 지하실로 통하는 것처럼 보였다. \"그리고 저것들은, 짐작컨대, 죄수들이 자기네 얼마 안 되는 석탄을 보관하는 작은 광들이겠군요. 내려가야 할 일이 생기면 영 불쾌한 곳이지만, 어쨌든 아주 편리하겠습니다.\"\n\n\"네, 편리하다고 해도 놀랄 일은 아니죠,\" 신사가 대답했다. \"거기엔 사람 몇이 살거든요, 꽤나 아늑하게 말입니다. 저게 박람횟간(폐어)이지요, 저게요.\"\n\n\"이보시오,\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"정말로 저 비참한 지하 감방에 사람들이 산다는 말을 하는 건 아니겠지요?\"\n\n\"그게 아니라뇨?\" 로커 씨가 분개하여 놀라며 대답했다. \"안 될 게 뭐 있습니까?\"\n\n\"산다고요!—저 아래에서 산다고요!\" 픽윅 씨가 외쳤다.\n\n\"저 아래에서 살죠! 네, 게다가 저 아래에서 죽기도 합니다, 아주 흔히요!\" 로커 씨가 대답했다. \"그게 어쨌다는 겁니까? 누가 그걸 두고 뭐라 할 수 있단 말입니까? 저 아래에서 살죠! 네, 살기에 아주 좋은 곳이라니까요, 안 그래요?\"\n\n로커가 이 말을 하면서 픽윅 씨를 향해 다소 사납게 돌아섰고, 더욱이 자기 눈과 사지와 혈액에 관한 어떤 불쾌한 저주들을 흥분한 어조로 중얼거렸으므로, 픽윅 씨는 이 이야기를 더 이어가지 않는 편이 현명하겠다고 판단했다. 로커 씨는 그러고 나서 또 다른 계단을 오르기 시작했는데, 방금 화제가 된 그 장소로 통하던 계단만큼이나 더러웠다. 그가 그 계단을 오르는 동안 픽윅 씨와 샘이 바짝 뒤를 따랐다.\n\n\"자,\" 아래층 복도와 똑같은 크기의 또 다른 복도에 이르자 로커 씨가 숨을 돌리려 멈춰 서며 말했다. \"여기가 커피실 복도고, 그 위층이 삼층, 그 위가 꼭대기층입니다. 그리고 오늘 밤 당신이 잘 방은 간수실인데, 이쪽입니다—오시죠.\" 이 모든 말을 단숨에 내뱉고서, 로커 씨는 또 한 차례 계단을 올랐고, 픽윅 씨와 샘 웰러가 그 뒤를 바짝 따랐다.\n\n이 계단들은 바닥에서 조금 위에 자리한 여러 창을 통해 빛을 받아들였는데, 그 창들은 높은 벽돌담으로 둘러싸인 자갈 마당을 내다보고 있었고, 담 꼭대기에는 쇠 방어책(슈보드프리즈)이 박혀 있었다. 이 마당은, 로커 씨의 말에 따르면, 라켓 경기장이었다. 그리고 같은 신사의 증언에 따르면, 감옥의 패링던 거리에 가장 가까운 부분에는 더 작은 마당이 있었는데, '채색 마당'이라 불렸으니, 그 담벼락이 한때 돛을 활짝 편 갖가지 군함의 형상과, 옛 시절 어느 수감된 도안공이 한가한 시간에 그려 놓은 그 밖의 예술적 솜씨들을 드러냈던 까닭에서였다.\n\n이런 정보를 일러 준 것은, 픽윅 씨를 깨우치려는 어떤 구체적인 의도에서라기보다는, 분명 중요한 사실 하나를 가슴에서 털어 버리려는 목적에서였던 듯한데, 안내인은 마침내 또 다른 복도에 이르자 맨 끝의 작은 통로로 앞장서 들어가 문 하나를 열었고, 결코 마음 끌리는 모습이라고는 할 수 없는, 여덟아홉 개의 쇠 침대틀이 놓인 방을 드러냈다.\n\n\"자,\" 로커 씨가 문을 잡고서 의기양양하게 픽윅 씨를 돌아보며 말했다. \"방이올시다!\"\n\n그러나 픽윅 씨의 얼굴은 자기 숙소의 꼴에 대해 지극히 하찮은 정도의 만족만을 드러냈으므로, 로커 씨는 감정의 공감을 얻으려고 새뮤얼 웰러의 얼굴을 들여다보았는데, 웰러는 그때까지 위엄 있는 침묵을 지키고 있었다.\n\n\"방이 있구먼, 젊은이,\" 로커 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"보입니다,\" 샘은 평온하게 고개를 끄덕이며 대답했다.\n\n\"패링던 호텔에서 이런 방을 찾으리라곤 생각 못 했을걸, 안 그래?\" 로커 씨가 흡족한 미소를 지으며 말했다.\n\n이에 웰러 씨는 한쪽 눈을 편안하고 꾸밈없이 감는 것으로 대답했는데, 그것은 그가 그렇게 생각했으리라는 뜻으로도, 그렇게 생각 못 했으리라는 뜻으로도, 또는 그 일에 대해 애초에 아무 생각도 해본 적 없다는 뜻으로도 받아들일 수 있었으니, 보는 이의 상상이 일러주는 대로였다. 이 묘기를 해내고 다시 눈을 뜬 뒤, 웰러 씨는 로커 씨가 그토록 아첨하듯 잠자기에 더없이 그만이라고 묘사한 그 침대틀이 어느 것이냐고 물었다.\n\n\"저것일세,\" 로커 씨가 한구석에 놓인 매우 녹슨 침대를 가리키며 대답했다. \"저 침대틀은 누구라도 잠들게 만들지, 본인이 원하든 원치 않든 말이야.\"\n\n\"제 생각엔,\" 샘이 문제의 그 가구를 극도의 혐오가 담긴 눈길로 바라보며 말했다. \"양귀비도 저것에 비하면 아무것도 아닐 것 같습니다요.\"\n\n\"아무것도 아니지,\" 로커 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그리고 짐작컨대,\" 샘은 방금 오간 이야기로 주인의 결심이 흔들리는 기미가 있는지 살피려는 듯 주인 쪽을 곁눈질하며 말했다. \"여기서 자는 다른 신사분들도 신사분들이겠지요.\"\n\n\"바로 그렇지,\" 로커 씨가 말했다. \"그중 하나는 하루에 에일 열두 파인트를 마시고, 식사 때조차 담배를 손에서 놓지 않는다네.\"\n\n\"대단한 일급 인물이겠습니다요,\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n\"최고급이지,\" 로커 씨가 대답했다.\n\n이런 소식에도 전혀 기죽지 않고, 픽윅 씨는 그날 밤 그 마취제 같은 침대틀의 위력을 시험해 보겠노라고 미소 지으며 알렸다. 그리고 로커 씨는, 별다른 통지나 격식 없이 아무 때나 마음 내키는 시각에 잠자리에 들어도 된다고 일러 준 뒤, 픽윅 씨를 샘과 함께 복도에 세워 둔 채 떠나갔다.\n\n날이 저물고 있었다. 다시 말해, 결코 밝은 적 없는 이곳에도 바깥에 깃든 저녁에 대한 예의로 가스등 몇 개가 켜졌다. 날씨가 다소 따뜻했으므로, 양쪽으로 복도와 통하는 수많은 작은 방들의 거주자 가운데 몇몇은 문을 반쯤 열어 두고 있었다. 픽윅 씨는 지나가면서 큰 호기심과 흥미를 가지고 그 방들을 들여다보았다. 여기서는, 담배 연기 구름 사이로 간신히 보이는 덩치 큰 사내 네댓 명이 절반쯤 비운 맥주 단지를 놓고 시끄럽고 떠들썩한 대화를 나누거나, 몹시 때 묻은 카드 한 벌로 올포어즈 놀이를 하고 있었다. 그 옆방에서는, 어떤 외로운 거주자가 희미한 수지 양초 불빛에 의지해, 먼지로 누렇게 바래고 세월에 낡아 부스러져 가는 더럽고 너덜너덜한 서류 뭉치를 들여다보며, 백 번째로 자기 고충을 길게 늘어놓는 진술서를 쓰고 있는 모습이 보였으니, 그것은 결코 그 눈에 닿지 않을, 혹은 그 가슴을 결코 움직이지 못할 어느 높으신 분이 읽어 주기를 바라며 쓰는 것이었다. 세 번째 방에서는, 한 사내가 아내와 한 무리의 아이들과 함께, 어린것들이 밤을 보내도록 바닥이나 의자 몇 개 위에 빈약한 잠자리를 마련하고 있었다. 그리고 네 번째, 다섯 번째, 여섯 번째, 일곱 번째 방에서는, 소음과 맥주와 담배 연기와 카드가 모두 전보다 더 큰 기세로 되풀이되고 있었다.\n\n복도 자체에도, 특히 계단에는 수많은 사람이 어슬렁거리고 있었으니, 어떤 이는 자기 방이 비어 적막해서, 어떤 이는 자기 방이 꽉 차서 더워서 나와 있었다. 그러나 대부분은 안절부절못하고 불편한 데다, 자기 자신을 정확히 어떻게 다루어야 할지 그 비결을 알지 못해서였다. 여기에는 여러 부류의 사람들이 있었으니, 면포 재킷을 입은 노동자에서부터, 더없이 어울리게도 팔꿈치가 해진 숄 가운을 걸친 영락한 낭비가에 이르기까지 다양했다. 그러나 그들 모두에게는 똑같은 분위기가 감돌았으니—일종의 나른하고, 옥살이에 찌든, 무심한 거들먹거림, 떠돌이 같은 \"그래서 어쩔 건데\" 하는 태도였다. 그것은 말로는 도저히 표현할 수 없는 것이지만, 누구든 원한다면 가장 가까운 채무자 감옥에 발을 들여, 픽윅 씨가 그러했던 것과 똑같은 흥미를 가지고 거기서 처음 눈에 띄는 사람 무리를 바라보기만 하면, 단번에 이해할 수 있는 것이었다.\n\n\"내 생각엔 말일세, 샘,\" 픽윅 씨가 계단참의 쇠 난간에 기대며 말했다. \"내 생각엔, 샘, 빚 때문에 갇히는 일은 도무지 형벌이라고 할 게 못 되는 것 같네.\"\n\n\"그렇게 생각 안 하십니까요, 나리?\" 웰러 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"이 친구들이 어떻게 마시고, 담배 피우고, 떠들어 대는지 보게나,\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다. \"이들이 이걸 크게 괴로워한다는 건 도무지 있을 수 없는 일이야.\"\n\n\"아, 바로 그게 문제입죠, 나리,\" 샘이 받아 말했다. \"저들은 괴로워하지 않습니다요. 저들한텐 아주 휴일이나 다름없지요—온통 흑맥주에 구주희(스키틀) 놀이죠. 이런 식의 일로 망가지는 건 딴 사람들이올시다. 맥주를 들이켜지도 못하고 구주희를 치지도 못하는, 그 풀 죽은 친구들 말입죠. 돈을 낼 수 있다면 낼 텐데, 갇혀 있는 바람에 풀이 죽어 버린 사람들요. 제가 말씀드리자면 이렇습니다, 나리. 늘 술집에서 빈둥거리는 자들한테는 이게 조금도 해가 안 되고, 할 수 있을 때면 늘 일하는 자들한테는 지나치게 해가 됩지요. '이건 공평치가 않아,' 제 아버지가 그로그가 반반으로 안 섞였을 때 늘 하시던 말씀이올시다. '이건 공평치가 않아, 그게 흠이지.'\"\n\n\"자네 말이 맞는 것 같네, 샘,\" 잠시 생각에 잠겼다가 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"아주 옳은 말이야.\"\n\n\"어쩌면, 이따금, 이걸 좋아하는 정직한 사람도 더러 있긴 합죠,\" 웰러 씨가 곰곰이 생각하는 어조로 말했다. \"하지만 제가 떠올릴 수 있는 사람으로는, 갈색 외투를 입은 얼굴 작고 더러운 사내 말고는 한 번도 들어 본 적이 없습니다요. 게다가 그것도 습관의 힘이었습죠.\"\n\n\"그래, 그가 누구였나?\" 픽윅 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"아, 바로 그게 아무도 한 번도 알아내지 못한 점입죠,\" 샘이 대답했다.\n\n\"그런데 그가 뭘 했단 말인가?\"\n\n\"아, 그자는 그자보다 훨씬 더 이름난 많은 사람들이 한때 했던 일을 했습죠, 나리,\" 샘이 대답했다. \"집행관과 시합을 벌여서, 이겼지요.\"\n\n\"바꿔 말하면, 짐작컨대,\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"빚을 졌다는 거군.\"\n\n\"바로 그렇습니다요, 나리,\" 샘이 대답했다. \"그러다 때가 되니 그 결과로 여기 들어온 거죠. 큰 빚도 아니었습니다—집행이 구 파운드에 한 푼도 안 되는 거였고, 거기에 비용으로 다섯 배가 곱해졌습죠. 그래도 어쨌든 그자는 여기서 십칠 년을 머물렀습니다요. 얼굴에 주름이 잡혔다 해도 때로 메워졌을 겁니다, 그 더러운 얼굴이나 갈색 외투나 그 세월이 끝날 무렵에도 처음과 똑같았으니까요. 그자는 아주 온순하고 남한테 해 끼치지 않는 작은 사람이었고, 늘 누군가를 위해 부산을 떨거나 라켓을 치면서도 한 번도 이기는 법이 없었습죠. 그러다 마침내 간수들이 그자를 아주 좋아하게 됐고, 그자는 밤마다 수위실에 와서 그들과 잡담을 나누고 이야기를 들려주고 그러곤 했답니다. 어느 날 밤 그자가 여느 때처럼 거기 들어가, 자물쇠를 맡고 있던 아주 오랜 친구 하나와 같이 있다가, 갑자기 이러는 겁니다, '난 십칠 년 동안 바깥 시장을 못 봤어, 빌,' 하고요 (그때 거기엔 플리트 시장이 있었거든요)—'난 십칠 년 동안 바깥 시장을 못 봤어, 빌,' 하고요. '안 봤겠지,' 간수가 파이프를 피우며 말합니다. '잠깐만이라도 그걸 보고 싶어, 빌,' 그자가 말합니다. '그럴 만도 하지,' 간수가 파이프를 아주 사납게 빨며, 작은 사내가 뭘 원하는지 모르는 척하며 말합니다. '빌,' 작은 사내가 아까보다 더 퉁명스럽게 말합니다. '머릿속에 그 생각이 들었어. 죽기 전에 한 번만 더 거리를 보게 해 줘. 졸중이라도 일어나지 않는 한, 시계로 오 분 안에 돌아올게.' '그럼 네가 졸중이라도 일어나면 내 꼴은 어떻게 되고?' 간수가 말합니다. '아니,' 작은 사내가 말합니다. '누가 날 발견하든 집으로 데려다줄 거야, 내 주머니에 명함이 있으니까, 빌,' 그자가 말합니다. '커피실 복도 20호'라고 말이죠. 그런데 그건 정말 사실이었습죠, 새로 온 사람과 사귀고 싶을 때면 그자는 그 글자만 적히고 다른 건 아무것도 없는 작고 흐물흐물한 명함을 꺼내 들곤 했으니까요. 그래서 그자는 늘 20호라고 불렸답니다. 간수가 그자를 빤히 바라보다가 마침내 엄숙한 태도로 말합니다, '20호,' 그가 말합니다. '내 자넬 믿겠네. 자넨 오랜 친구를 곤경에 빠뜨리진 않겠지.' '암, 그래야지, 친구. 내 가슴속엔 그보다 나은 게 있다고 믿네,' 작은 사내가 말합니다. 그러고는 그 말을 하면서 자기 작은 조끼를 세게 두드렸고, 그러자 두 눈에서 눈물이 한 방울씩 솟아났는데, 그건 아주 비상한 일이었습죠, 물이 그자 얼굴에 닿은 적이 없다고들 여겼으니까요. 그자는 간수와 악수를 했고, 밖으로 나갔습니다—\"\n\n\"그러곤 다시는 돌아오지 않았겠지,\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"한 번은 틀리셨습니다요, 나리,\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다. \"그자는 약속한 시간보다 이 분 일찍 돌아왔거든요, 분통이 끓어올라서요. 자기가 익숙지 않은 삯마차에 치일 뻔했다며, 시장(市長) 어른께 편지를 쓰지 않으면 자기가 사람도 아니라고 했답니다. 마침내 사람들이 그자를 진정시켰고, 그 후 오 년 동안 그자는 수위실 문밖을 한 번도 내다보지조차 않았습죠.\"\n\n\"그 기간이 끝나고 죽었겠군, 짐작컨대,\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"아닙니다, 안 죽었습니다요, 나리,\" 샘이 대답했다. \"그자는 길 건너 새로 생긴 술집에 가서 맥주 맛을 보고 싶다는 호기심이 일었는데, 그 응접실이 어찌나 좋던지 밤마다 거기 가기로 마음먹었고, 오랫동안 그렇게 했답니다. 늘 문 닫기 십오 분쯤 전에 규칙적으로 돌아오곤 했으니, 모든 게 아주 아늑하고 편안했죠. 그러다 마침내 그자는 너무도 흥겨워진 나머지, 시간이 어떻게 가는지 잊거나 그에 대해 전혀 신경 쓰지 않게 됐고, 점점 더 늦어지다가, 어느 날 밤 그자의 오랜 친구가 막 문을 닫으려던 참에—사실은 열쇠를 돌려 버린 참에—그자가 올라온 겁니다. '잠깐만, 빌,' 그자가 말합니다. '뭐야, 아직 안 들어왔었어, 20호?' 간수가 말합니다. '난 자네가 진작 들어온 줄 알았는데.' '아니, 안 들어왔어,' 작은 사내가 미소 지으며 말합니다. '그럼, 이보게 친구, 내 말하겠네,' 간수가 문을 아주 천천히 시무룩하게 열며 말합니다. '내 생각엔 자네가 요즘 나쁜 패거리와 어울리게 된 것 같아, 그걸 보니 아주 안타깝군. 자, 난 모진 짓은 하고 싶지 않네,' 그가 말합니다. '하지만 자네가 점잖은 모임으로 처신을 한정하고 규칙적인 시간에 돌아올 수 없다면, 자네가 거기 서 있는 게 분명한 것만큼이나 분명하게, 내 자넬 아주 내쫓아 버리겠네!' 작은 사내는 심한 떨림 발작에 사로잡혔고, 그 후로는 다시는 감옥 담장 밖으로 나가지 않았답니다!\"\n\n샘이 이야기를 마치자, 픽윅 씨는 천천히 다시 계단을 내려갔다. 이제 어두워져서 거의 인적이 끊긴 채색 마당을 생각에 잠겨 몇 바퀴 돈 뒤, 그는 웰러 씨에게 이제 밤을 위해 물러갈 때가 된 것 같다고 알리며, 인근 술집에서 잠자리를 구하고, 아침 일찍 돌아와 주인의 옷가지를 조지 앤드 벌처에서 옮겨 올 채비를 하라고 일렀다. 새뮤얼 웰러 씨는 이 부탁을 가능한 한 좋은 낯빛으로 따르려 했으나, 그럼에도 상당히 마지못한 기색을 드러냈다. 그는 심지어 그날 밤 자갈밭에 몸을 뉘는 편이 좋겠다는 갖가지 부질없는 암시를 슬쩍 던져 보기까지 했으나, 픽윅 씨가 그런 제안에는 한사코 귀를 막고 있는 것을 알고는, 마침내 물러갔다.\n\n픽윅 씨가 몹시 의기소침하고 불편함을 느꼈다는 사실은 감출 수 없다—사람들과 어울릴 데가 없어서는 아니었으니, 감옥은 사람들로 매우 붐볐고, 포도주 한 병이면 더 이상의 격식 차린 소개 절차 없이도 당장에 몇몇 별난 부류의 더없는 친교를 살 수 있었을 것이다. 그러나 그는 거칠고 천박한 군중 속에서 혼자였고, 풀려날 가망도 없이 갇히고 우리에 넣어졌다는 생각에서 자연히 따라오는, 그 의기소침과 가슴이 가라앉는 느낌을 맛보고 있었다. 도드슨과 포그의 모진 요구를 들어줌으로써 스스로 풀려나겠다는 생각은, 단 한순간도 그의 머릿속에 떠오르지 않았다.\n\n이런 심정으로 그는 다시 커피실 복도로 들어서서 천천히 이리저리 거닐었다. 그곳은 견딜 수 없을 만큼 더러웠고, 담배 연기 냄새는 완전히 숨이 막힐 지경이었다. 사람들이 드나들면서 끊임없이 문을 쾅쾅 여닫았고, 그들의 목소리와 발소리가 통로를 따라 쉴 새 없이 메아리치고 또 메아리쳤다. 한 젊은 여인이, 야위고 비참한 탓에 거의 기지도 못할 듯한 아이를 품에 안고서, 통로를 오르내리며 남편과 이야기를 나누고 있었으니, 남편으로서는 그녀를 만날 다른 곳이 없었던 것이다. 그들이 픽윅 씨 곁을 지날 때, 그는 그 여인이 비통하게 흐느끼는 소리를 들을 수 있었다. 그리고 한번은 그녀가 너무도 격렬한 슬픔에 북받쳐, 몸을 지탱하려고 벽에 기대지 않을 수 없었고, 그동안 남편은 아이를 팔에 안고 그녀를 달래려 애썼다.\n\n픽윅 씨의 가슴은 정말이지 그것을 견디기에는 너무나 벅찼고, 그는 잠자리에 들려고 위층으로 올라갔다.\n\n자, 비록 간수실은 매우 불편한 방이었지만(장식과 편의의 모든 점에서 어느 주(州) 감옥의 일반 병동보다도 몇백 도는 못한 방이었지만), 지금으로서는 픽윅 씨 자신을 제외하고는 완전히 비어 있다는 장점이 있었다. 그래서 그는 자신의 작은 쇠 침대틀 발치에 앉아, 간수가 이 더러운 방으로 한 해에 얼마나 버는지 궁금해하기 시작했다. 수학적 계산으로, 이 방의 연간 가치가 런던 변두리 작은 거리 하나의 자유 보유권과 대략 맞먹는다는 결론에 만족한 뒤, 그는 자기 바지 위를 기어 다니는 거무튀튀한 파리 한 마리가, 그토록 통풍 좋은 곳을 골라 갈 수 있었으면서 도대체 무슨 유혹에 이끌려 이 답답한 감옥에 들어왔을까 궁금해하기 시작했다—이 명상의 흐름은 결국 그 곤충이 미쳤다는 거부할 수 없는 결론으로 그를 이끌었다. 이 점을 매듭짓고 나자, 그는 자신이 졸음에 겨워지는 것을 의식하기 시작했다. 그래서 그는 아침에 신중하게 챙겨 넣어 둔 주머니에서 잠자리 모자를 꺼냈고, 느긋하게 옷을 벗고 침대에 들어가 잠이 들었다.\n\n\"브라보! 뒤꿈치를 돌리고—발끝을 차고 끌어라—한껏 해 봐라, 제피로스! 오페라 극장이 네 본고장이 아니라면 내가 숨이 막혀 죽지. 계속해라! 만세!\" 더없이 떠들썩한 어조로 내뱉어지고 요란한 웃음소리가 곁들여진 이 외침은, 실제로는 반 시간쯤 지속되었으나 잠든 이에게는 삼 주나 한 달은 이어진 듯이 느껴지는 그런 깊은 잠 가운데 하나로부터 픽윅 씨를 깨웠다.\n\n그 목소리가 멎자마자 방은 어찌나 격렬하게 흔들렸던지 창문이 창틀에서 덜컹거렸고 침대틀들이 거듭 떨렸다. 픽윅 씨는 벌떡 일어나, 눈앞의 광경에 말없이 놀란 채 몇 분 동안 꼼짝 못 하고 있었다.\n\n방바닥에서, 자락 넓은 초록색 외투에 코르덴 무릎 반바지와 회색 무명 양말을 신은 한 사내가, 호른파이프의 가장 인기 있는 스텝들을, 우아함과 경쾌함을 속되고 익살스럽게 흉내 내며 추고 있었는데, 그의 의상이 지닌 더없이 어울리는 성격과 어우러져 형언할 수 없을 만큼 우스꽝스러웠다. 또 다른 사내는, 분명 몹시 취해서 아마도 동료들에게 침대로 떠밀려 들어간 듯한데, 시트 사이에 일어나 앉아, 자기가 기억해 낼 수 있는 만큼의 희극 노래를 더없이 감상에 젖은 감정과 표정으로 흥얼거리고 있었다. 한편 세 번째 사내는, 침대틀 하나에 앉아, 깊은 감식가의 태도로 두 연기자에게 박수를 보내고, 이미 픽윅 씨를 잠에서 깨운 그런 감정의 발산으로 그들을 부추기고 있었다.\n\n이 마지막 사내는, 그런 장소에서가 아니고서는 도저히 완전한 형태로 볼 수 없는 부류의 신사를 보여 주는 훌륭한 표본이었다—이들은 이따금 마구간 마당이나 술집 근처에서 불완전한 상태로 마주칠 수는 있으나, 마치 입법부가 오로지 이들을 길러 내려는 목적으로 사려 깊게 마련해 둔 듯한 이런 온상에서가 아니고서는 결코 만개에 이르지 못한다.\n\n그는 키 큰 사내로, 올리브빛 안색에, 길고 검은 머리카락과, 턱 밑에서 만나는 매우 무성하고 텁수룩한 구레나룻을 지니고 있었다. 그는 종일 라켓을 친 탓에 목수건을 두르지 않았고, 풀어헤친 셔츠 깃은 그 구레나룻의 무성함을 한껏 드러내고 있었다. 머리에는 흔한 십팔 펜스짜리 프랑스식 챙 없는 모자를 쓰고 있었는데, 거기 매달린 화려한 술이 흔한 면포 외투와 더없이 잘 어울렸다. 그의 다리는, 길긴 했으나 허약함에 시달려, 그 사지의 균형을 온전히 드러내도록 지은 옥스퍼드 혼방 바지로 멋을 부리고 있었다. 그러나 다소 아무렇게나 멜빵을 매고, 게다가 단추도 제대로 채우지 않아서, 바지는 결코 가장 우아하달 수 없는 여러 주름을 이루며, 뒤축이 충분히 닳아 매우 더러운 흰 양말 한 켤레가 드러나 보이는 구두 위로 흘러내려 있었다. 사내 전체에는 방탕하고 떠돌이 같은 멋과, 일종의 허세 부리는 악당의 기질이 감돌았으니, 그것은 금광 하나만큼의 값어치가 있는 것이었다.\n\n이 인물이 픽윅 씨가 지켜보고 있다는 것을 가장 먼저 알아챘다. 그러자 그는 제피로스에게 눈짓하며, 짐짓 엄숙한 척하면서 저 신사를 깨우지 말라고 간청했다.\n\n\"아니, 저 신사분의 정직한 가슴과 영혼에 축복을!\" 제피로스가 돌아서서 극도로 놀란 척하며 말했다. \"신사분이 깨셨네. 어흠, 셰익스피어 양반! 안녕하십니까, 나리? 메리와 새러는 안녕하신지요, 나리? 그리고 댁의 사랑스러운 노부인께서는요, 나리? 그쪽으로 보내실 첫 번째 작은 꾸러미에 제 안부를 넣어 주시는 친절을 베풀어 주시겠습니까, 나리? 진작 보냈을 텐데 마차에서 깨질까 봐 겁이 났다고 전해 주시고요, 나리?\"\n\n\"신사분이 마실 것을 간절히 바라는 게 보이는데도 흔해 빠진 인사로 신사분을 질리게 하지 말게나,\" 구레나룻 난 신사가 익살스러운 태도로 말했다. \"신사분께 뭘 드시겠느냐고 여쭤보지 그러나?\"\n\n\"이런, 까맣게 잊었네그려,\" 다른 사내가 대답했다. \"뭘 드시겠습니까, 나리? 포트와인을 드시겠습니까, 나리, 아니면 셰리주를 드시겠습니까, 나리? 에일을 권해 드릴 수 있습니다, 나리. 아니면 흑맥주를 맛보고 싶으신지요, 나리? 나리의 잠자리 모자를 걸어 드리는 영광을 누리게 해 주십시오, 나리.\"\n\n이 말과 함께, 말하던 자는 그 의복을 픽윅 씨의 머리에서 낚아채, 눈 깜짝할 사이에 술 취한 사내의 머리에 씌웠는데, 그 사내는 자기가 수많은 청중을 즐겁게 하고 있다고 굳게 믿으며, 상상할 수 있는 가장 구슬픈 가락으로 희극 노래를 계속 두들겨 대고 있었다.\n\n사람의 잠자리 모자를 그 이마에서 난폭한 수단으로 벗겨내, 더러운 행색의 낯선 신사 머리에 씌우는 것은, 그 자체로 아무리 기발한 익살이라 한들, 의문의 여지 없이 짓궂은 장난이라는 명칭에 드는 것 가운데 하나다. 이 일을 바로 그런 관점에서 본 픽윅 씨는, 자신의 의도를 조금도 내비치지 않은 채, 침대에서 힘차게 뛰쳐나와, 제피로스의 가슴에 어찌나 매서운 일격을 가했던지 그가 때때로 자기 이름으로 불리는 그 물건의 상당 부분을 빼앗겼고, 그러고는 자신의 잠자리 모자를 되찾아, 대담하게 방어 자세를 취했다.\n\n\"자,\" 픽윅 씨가 흥분 못지않게 그토록 많은 기운을 쏟은 탓에 숨을 헐떡이며 말했다. \"덤벼라—둘 다—둘 다 말이다!\" 이 호기로운 초대와 함께 이 훌륭한 신사는 꽉 쥔 주먹에 빙글빙글 도는 동작을 부여했으니, 적들을 그 기술의 과시로 질겁하게 하려는 것이었다.\n\n픽윅 씨의 더없이 뜻밖의 용감함 때문이었는지, 아니면 그가 침대에서 빠져나와 호른파이프 춘 사내 위로 한 덩어리가 되어 쓰러진 그 복잡한 방식 때문이었는지, 어쨌든 그의 적들은 마음이 움직였다. 마음이 움직인 것은 사실이었으니, 픽윅 씨가 틀림없이 그러리라 굳게 믿었던 것처럼 그 자리에서 살인을 저지르려 시도하는 대신, 그들은 멈춰 서서, 잠시 서로를 빤히 쳐다보다가, 마침내 큰 소리로 웃어 버렸기 때문이다.\n\n\"이거 참, 당신은 호걸이오. 그래서 더 마음에 드는구려,\" 제피로스가 말했다. \"자, 다시 침대로 뛰어드시오, 안 그러면 류머티즘에 걸릴 거요. 악의는 없으시겠지요?\" 사내가 손을 내밀며 말했는데, 그 손은 때때로 장갑 가게 문 위에 매달려 흔들리는 누런 손가락 뭉치만 한 크기였다.\n\n\"물론 없소,\" 픽윅 씨가 아주 선뜻 말했다. 이제 흥분이 가라앉고 나니 다리가 다소 서늘해지는 것을 느끼기 시작했기 때문이다.\n\n\"제게 여 — 영광을 주십시오,\" 구레나룻 난 신사가 오른손을 내밀며, h 음을 거세게 발음하여 말했다.\n\n\"매우 기쁘오, 선생,\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. 그리고 아주 길고 엄숙하게 악수를 나눈 뒤, 다시 침대에 들었다.\n\n\"제 이름은 스맹글입니다, 선생,\" 구레나룻 난 사내가 말했다.\n\n\"오,\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"제 것은 미빈스올시다,\" 양말 신은 사내가 말했다.\n\n\"반갑게 듣소이다, 선생,\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"어흠,\" 스맹글 씨가 헛기침을 했다.\n\n\"무슨 말씀 하셨소, 선생?\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"아니요, 안 했습니다, 선생,\" 스맹글 씨가 말했다.\n\n이 모든 것이 매우 점잖고 유쾌했다. 그리고 일을 한층 더 화기애애하게 만들려고, 스맹글 씨는 자기가 신사의 감정에 대해 매우 높은 경의를 품고 있노라고 픽윅 씨에게 여러 번 거듭 장담했는데, 이 정서는 실로 그에게 무한한 명예가 되는 것이었으니, 그가 그 감정을 도무지 이해할 리 없다고 여겨졌기 때문이다.\n\n\"법정을 거치실 겁니까, 선생?\" 스맹글 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"무엇을 거친다고요?\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"법정 말입니다—포르투갈 거리—구제를 위한 법정—아시잖습니까.\"\n\n\"오, 아니오,\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다. \"아니, 그렇지 않소.\"\n\n\"그럼, 어쩌면 나가실 참인가요?\" 미빈스 씨가 넌지시 물었다.\n\n\"그렇지도 않을 것 같소,\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다. \"내가 어떤 손해 배상금을 내기를 거부하여, 그 결과 여기 있는 것이오.\"\n\n\"아,\" 스맹글 씨가 말했다. \"종이가 제 파멸이었지요.\"\n\n\"문구상이셨던 모양이군요, 선생?\" 픽윅 씨가 순진하게 말했다.\n\n\"문구상! 아니, 아닙니다. 빌어먹을, 저주받을! 그렇게까지 천하진 않습니다. 장사는 안 했어요. 제가 종이라고 할 때는, 어음을 말하는 겁니다.\"\n\n\"오, 그런 뜻으로 그 말을 쓰는군요. 알겠소,\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"제기랄! 신사라면 역경을 각오해야지요,\" 스맹글이 말했다. \"그게 어쨌단 말입니까? 여기 제가 플리트 감옥에 있습니다. 좋아요, 좋아. 그래서 어떻단 말입니까? 그렇다고 제가 조금이라도 못해진 건 아니지 않습니까, 안 그래요?\"\n\n\"조금도 그렇지 않소,\" 미빈스 씨가 대답했다. 그의 말은 아주 옳았으니, 스맹글 씨가 그 때문에 조금이라도 못해지기는커녕, 오히려 다소 나아진 셈이었기 때문이다. 이 자리에 들어올 자격을 갖추려고, 그는 어떤 장신구 몇 점을 거저 손에 넣었는데, 그것들은 그보다 훨씬 전에 이미 전당포로 흘러 들어가 있었던 것이다.\n\n\"자, 그건 그렇고,\" 스맹글 씨가 말했다. \"이거 목마른 일이올시다. 구운 셰리주 한 모금으로 입가심이나 합시다. 마지막에 온 분이 한턱내고, 미빈스가 가져오고, 나는 마시는 걸 거들지요. 어쨌거나 그게 공정하고 신사다운 분업 아니겠습니까. 제기랄!\"\n\n또 한 차례의 다툼을 무릅쓰고 싶지 않았던 픽윅 씨는 그 제안에 기꺼이 동의하고, 미빈스 씨에게 돈을 맡겼는데, 미빈스는 거의 열한 시가 다 된지라 지체 없이 그 심부름을 하러 커피실로 향했다.\n\n\"이보시오,\" 친구가 방을 나서자마자 스맹글이 속삭였다. \"저자에게 얼마를 주셨습니까?\"\n\n\"반 소버린이오,\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"저자는 지독히 유쾌하고 신사다운 친구지요,\" 스맹글 씨가 말했다. \"지독하게 유쾌해요. 저보다 더한 사람은 모릅니다. 하지만—\" 여기서 스맹글 씨는 말을 뚝 끊고, 미심쩍은 듯 고개를 저었다.\n\n\"그 돈을 자기 용도로 가로챌 가능성이 있다고 생각하는 건 아니겠지요?\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"오, 아니요! 명심하십시오, 그런 말은 안 합니다. 저자가 지독히 신사다운 친구라고 분명히 말씀드리는 겁니다,\" 스맹글 씨가 말했다. \"하지만 제 생각엔, 어쩌면, 누가 내려가서, 저자가 어쩌다 단지에 부리를 담그지나 않는지, 또는 위층으로 올라오다 빌어먹을 실수로 돈을 잃어버리지나 않는지 살펴보는 편이 좋을 듯합니다. 이보게, 거기 자네, 잠깐 아래층으로 달려 내려가서 저 신사분을 좀 살피고 와 주겠나?\"\n\n이 부탁은 행색이 몹시 가난해 보이는, 작고 소심하고 신경질적인 사내에게 한 것이었는데, 그는 그동안 내내 자기 침대틀에 웅크리고 앉아, 분명 자신의 처지가 새로운 데 어리둥절해 있는 듯했다.\n\n\"커피실이 어딘지 알지,\" 스맹글이 말했다. \"냉큼 내려가서, 저 신사분께 단지 드는 걸 거들러 왔다고 말하게. 아니—잠깐—내가 일러 주지—우리가 저자를 어떻게 골탕 먹일지 일러 주겠습니다,\" 스맹글이 교활한 표정으로 말했다.\n\n\"어떻게요?\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"거스름돈은 시가에 쓰라고 전갈을 보내는 거지요. 기막힌 생각입니다. 가서 저자에게 그렇게 전하게, 알겠나? 시가를 헛되이 쓰진 않을 겁니다,\" 스맹글이 픽윅 씨를 돌아보며 말을 이었다. \"내가 그걸 피울 테니까요.\"\n\n이 술책이 어찌나 지극히 기발하고, 게다가 어찌나 미동도 없는 침착함과 냉정함으로 행해졌던지, 픽윅 씨는 설령 그럴 힘이 있었다 해도 그것을 방해하고 싶은 마음이 들지 않았을 것이다. 얼마 안 가 미빈스 씨가 셰리주를 들고 돌아왔고, 스맹글 씨가 그것을 금이 간 작은 머그잔 두 개에 따랐다. 그는 자기 자신에 관해서는, 이런 상황에서 신사라면 까다롭게 굴어서는 안 되며, 자기로 말하자면 단지째 마시는 것을 그리 자존심 상해하지 않는다고 사려 깊게 말했다. 그러고는 자신의 진심을 보이려고, 곧바로 단지를 반쯤 비우는 들이켬으로 일동을 위해 축배를 들었다.\n\n이런 수단으로 더없이 훌륭한 화목이 조성되자, 스맹글 씨는 자기가 때때로 겪었던 갖가지 낭만적인 모험에 관한 이야기로 청중을 즐겁게 하기 시작했는데, 거기에는 순종 말 한 마리와 화려한 유대인 여인에 관한 여러 흥미로운 일화가 얽혀 있었으니, 둘 다 빼어나게 아름다워 이 왕국의 귀족과 신사들이 몹시 탐냈다는 것이었다.\n\n한 신사의 전기에서 발췌한 이 우아한 이야기들이 채 끝나기 한참 전에, 미빈스 씨는 이미 잠자리에 들어 밤새 코를 골기 시작했고, 소심한 낯선 이와 픽윅 씨만이 스맹글 씨의 경험담의 온전한 혜택을 누리도록 남겨졌다.\n\n게다가 마지막에 언급된 이 두 신사도, 그 감동적인 이야기 대목들로 마땅히 받을 만큼 교화되지는 못했다. 픽윅 씨는 한동안 졸음 속에 빠져 있었는데, 그때 술 취한 사내가 다시금 희극 노래를 터뜨리는 것과, 청중이 음악적 기분이 아니라는 점을 스맹글 씨가 물 단지를 매개로 부드럽게 일러 주는 것을 어렴풋이 알아챘다. 그러고 나서 픽윅 씨는 다시 한 번 잠에 빠져들었는데, 스맹글 씨가 여전히 긴 이야기를 늘어놓고 있다는 어렴풋한 의식이 남아 있었으니, 그 이야기의 요점은, 특별히 명시되고 자세히 진술된 어떤 경우에, 그가 어음 한 장과 신사 한 사람을 동시에 '해치웠다'는 것인 듯했다.\n\n픽윅 씨가 이튿날 아침 눈을 떴을 때, 그의 눈에 가장 먼저 들어온 것은 작은 검정 여행 가방 위에 걸터앉아, 겉보기에 깊은 상념에 잠긴 채로, 화려한 스맹글 씨의 당당한 모습을 골똘히 바라보고 있는 새뮤얼 웰러였다. 한편 스맹글 씨 본인은 이미 어느 정도 옷을 갖춰 입은 채 자기 침대에 앉아, 웰러 씨를 노려보아 무안하게 만들려는, 절망적일 만큼 가망 없는 시도에 골몰하고 있었다. 절망적일 만큼 가망 없다고 말하는 까닭은, 샘이 스맹글 씨의 모자며 발이며 머리며 얼굴이며 다리며 구레나룻을 한꺼번에 다 담아내는 포괄적인 눈길로, 흡족한 기색을 한껏 드러내며 꾸준히 바라보면서도, 그 일에 관한 스맹글 씨 개인의 감정에 대해서는, 나무로 깎은 조각상이나 짚을 속에 채운 가이 포크스 인형을 살펴볼 때만큼의 관심밖에 보이지 않았기 때문이다.\n\n\"그래, 다음에 또 날 알아보겠소?\" 스맹글 씨가 인상을 찌푸리며 말했다.\n\n\"어디서든 당신이라고 맹세할 수 있겠습니다, 선생님.\" 샘이 명랑하게 대답했다.\n\n\"신사한테 무례하게 굴지 마시오, 이 사람아.\" 스맹글 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"천만의 말씀입니다.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"그분이 언제 깨시는지 알려 주시면, 제가 더할 나위 없이 극상으로 예의 바르게 굴겠습니다요!\" 이 말은 스맹글 씨가 신사가 아니라는 뜻을 은근히 내비치는 구석이 있어서, 그의 화를 돋우었다.\n\n\"마이빈스!\" 스맹글 씨가 발끈한 기색으로 말했다.\n\n\"무슨 일이오?\" 그 신사가 자기 잠자리에서 대답했다.\n\n\"대체 이 작자는 누구요?\"\n\n\"맙소사,\" 마이빈스 씨가 이불 밑에서 게으르게 내다보며 말했다. \"그건 내가 당신한테 물어야 할 말이지. 여기 무슨 볼일이 있다는 거요?\"\n\n\"없소.\" 스맹글 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"그럼 계단 아래로 차 버리고, 내가 가서 발길질해 줄 때까지 함부로 일어나지 말라고 일러 두시오.\" 마이빈스 씨가 응수했다. 이 신속한 충고를 던진 다음, 그 훌륭한 신사는 다시 잠 속으로 빠져들었다.\n\n이 대화가 인신공격으로 치닫는 명백한 조짐을 보이자, 픽윅 씨는 끼어들기에 알맞은 대목이라고 판단했다.\n\n\"샘.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"예, 선생님.\" 그가 응답했다.\n\n\"간밤 이후로 무슨 새로운 일이라도 있었나?\"\n\n\"별일 없습니다요, 선생님.\" 샘이 스맹글 씨의 구레나룻을 흘끗 보며 대답했다. \"근래에 답답하고 갑갑한 공기가 만연한 탓에, 놀랍고도 살벌한 성질의 잡초들이 자라기에는 다소 알맞은 형편이었습니다만, 그 예외만 빼면 모든 게 꽤 잠잠합니다요.\"\n\n\"일어나야겠다.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"깨끗한 옷가지를 좀 가져다주게.\"\n\n스맹글 씨가 어떤 적대적 의도를 품고 있었든 간에, 여행 가방을 푸는 광경에 그의 생각은 금세 다른 데로 쏠렸다. 가방 속의 물건들은 단번에 그에게 픽윅 씨뿐 아니라 샘에 대해서도 더없이 호의적인 인상을 심어 준 모양이었다. 그는 그 별난 인물이 엿들을 수 있을 만큼 충분히 큰 목소리로, 샘이야말로 순종 중의 순종이요, 따라서 자기 마음에 꼭 드는 사람이라고 일찌감치 선언할 기회를 잡았다. 픽윅 씨로 말하자면, 그가 픽윅 씨에게 품은 애정에는 한계가 없었다.\n\n\"자, 제가 뭐 도와드릴 일이라도 있을까요, 선생님?\" 스맹글이 말했다.\n\n\"내가 아는 한은 없소만, 고맙소.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"세탁부에게 보낼 빨랫감은 없으십니까? 바깥에 아주 마음에 드는 세탁부를 한 사람 아는데, 일주일에 두 번씩 제 빨랫감을 가지러 옵니다. 그런데, 이런!—이렇게 운이 좋을 데가!—바로 오늘이 그 여자가 오는 날입니다. 그 자잘한 것들 중에 몇 개를 제 것과 함께 부쳐 드릴까요? 수고로움 따위는 말씀도 마십시오. 빌어먹을! 곤경에 처한 신사 하나가 같은 처지의 또 다른 신사를 돕기 위해 약간의 수고도 마다한다면, 그게 무슨 인간의 본성이겠습니까?\"\n\n스맹글 씨는 이렇게 말하면서, 그동안 슬금슬금 여행 가방에 되도록 가까이 다가가, 더없이 열렬하고 사심 없는 우정의 눈빛을 환히 발산하고 있었다.\n\n\"이보게, 사람을 시켜 솔질해 둘 만한 것은 없는가, 응?\" 스맹글이 말을 이었다.\n\n\"전혀 없습니다요, 멋쟁이 양반.\" 샘이 그 대답을 자기 입으로 가로채며 응수했다. \"사람을 번거롭게 할 것 없이 우리 중 누가 솔질을 한다면, 모두에게 더 좋을 겁니다. 집사한테 매를 맞기 싫다고 도련님이 투정하니까 선생님이 한 말처럼 말입니다요.\"\n\n\"그럼 제 작은 상자에 넣어 세탁부에게 보낼 만한 건 없습니까?\" 스맹글이 다소 무안해진 기색으로 샘에게서 픽윅 씨에게로 돌아서며 말했다.\n\n\"전혀 없습니다요, 선생님.\" 샘이 받아쳤다. \"그 작은 상자는 안 그래도 선생님 것만으로 꽉 차 있을 것 같아 걱정인뎁쇼.\"\n\n이 말과 더불어 샘이 스맹글 씨의 옷차림 가운데서도 바로 그 부분—세탁부가 신사의 빨랫감을 손질하는 솜씨가 대개 그것으로 가늠되는 부분—을 향해 어찌나 의미심장한 눈길을 보냈는지, 스맹글은 발길을 돌릴 수밖에 없었고, 적어도 당분간은 픽윅 씨의 지갑과 옷장에 대한 모든 계획을 단념할 수밖에 없었다. 그리하여 그는 골이 나서 운동장으로 물러갔는데, 거기서 전날 밤에 사 둔 시가 두 개로 가볍고 속 편한 아침 식사를 때웠다.\n\n담배를 피우지 않는 데다, 잡화점에 진 자잘한 외상 또한 석판 맨 아래까지 적혀 내려가 결국 반대편으로 '넘어가' 버린 마이빈스 씨는 침대에 그대로 누운 채, 그 자신의 표현을 빌리자면 '잠으로 때웠다'.\n\n커피실에 딸린 작은 골방—'스너거리'라는 거창한 이름이 붙어 있고, 거기 잠시 머무는 이는 약간의 추가 요금을 내는 대가로 앞서 말한 커피실의 모든 대화를 엿들을 수 있다는 말할 수 없는 이점을 누리는 곳—에서 아침을 든 뒤, 그리고 웰러 씨를 몇 가지 필요한 심부름에 내보낸 뒤, 픽윅 씨는 앞으로 묵을 거처에 관해 의논하려고 관리실로 향했다.\n\n\"거처라, 응?\" 그 신사가 큼직한 장부를 들여다보며 말했다. \"그거야 얼마든지 있지요, 픽윅 씨. 당신 동숙 배정표는 삼층 이십칠 호로 나올 겁니다.\"\n\n\"오,\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"내 뭐라고 하셨소?\"\n\n\"당신 동숙 배정표 말입니다.\" 로커 씨가 대답했다. \"그게 무슨 뜻인지 아시지요?\"\n\n\"잘은 모르겠소만.\" 픽윅 씨가 미소 지으며 대답했다.\n\n\"이런,\" 로커 씨가 말했다. \"솔즈베리 평원처럼 빤한 얘기 아닙니까. 당신은 삼층 이십칠 호로 동숙 배정표를 받게 되고, 그 방에 있는 자들이 당신의 동숙인이 되는 거지요.\"\n\n\"그 사람들이 많소?\" 픽윅 씨가 미심쩍어하며 물었다.\n\n\"셋입니다.\" 로커 씨가 대답했다.\n\n픽윅 씨는 기침을 했다.\n\n\"그중 하나는 목사고,\" 로커 씨가 말하면서 작은 종이쪽지를 채워 넣었다. \"또 하나는 푸주한이지요.\"\n\n\"네?\" 픽윅 씨가 외쳤다.\n\n\"푸주한 말입니다.\" 로커 씨가 되풀이하며, 글씨가 잘 안 나오는 것을 고쳐 보려고 펜촉을 책상에 톡톡 두드렸다. \"그 친구, 정말이지 못 말릴 한량이었지요! 자네 톰 마틴 기억하나, 네디?\" 로커가 관리실의 다른 사내에게 물었는데, 그 사내는 스물다섯 개의 날이 달린 주머니칼로 신발에서 진흙을 깎아 내고 있었다.\n\n\"기억하다마다.\" 그 말을 들은 자가 인칭대명사에 잔뜩 힘을 주어 대답했다.\n\n\"이런, 세상에!\" 로커 씨가 고개를 천천히 좌우로 저으며, 마치 젊은 시절의 어느 평화로운 정경을 애틋하게 떠올리기라도 하듯 앞에 놓인 쇠창살 창밖을 멍하니 바라보며 말했다. \"그 친구가 저기 부두 옆 폭스언더더힐에서 석탄 부리는 인부를 두들겨 패던 게 꼭 어제 일 같은데. 지금도 눈에 선하구먼. 두들겨 맞은 덕에 술이 좀 깬 채로, 거리 단속꾼 둘 사이에 끼여, 오른쪽 눈꺼풀에 식초 적신 갈색 종이를 붙이고, 나중에 그 어린애를 물어뜯은 그 사랑스러운 불도그를 발치에 달고서, 스트랜드 거리를 올라오던 모습이 말이야. 세월이란 거 참 묘한 거 아닌가, 응, 네디?\"\n\n이 말을 들은 신사는 과묵하고 사색적인 기질로 보였는데, 그저 그 물음을 따라 되뇔 뿐이었다. 로커 씨는 자신이 잠시 빠져들었던 시적이고 음울한 상념의 흐름을 떨쳐 내고는, 다시 일상사로 내려와 펜을 들었다.\n\n\"세 번째 신사는 어떤 사람인지 아시오?\" 픽윅 씨가 앞으로 함께 지낼 동료들에 관한 이 설명에 그다지 흡족하지 못한 채 물었다.\n\n\"그 심슨이란 작자는 뭐 하던 사람이지, 네디?\" 로커 씨가 동료에게 돌아서며 말했다.\n\n\"어느 심슨 말이오?\" 네디가 말했다.\n\n\"왜, 삼층 이십칠 호에 있는 자, 이 신사분이 동숙하게 될 그 자 말이야.\"\n\n\"오, 그 작자!\" 네디가 대답했다. \"이렇다 할 게 없는 자요. 전에는 말 사기꾼이었는데, 지금은 노름꾼이지요.\"\n\n\"아, 그럴 줄 알았어.\" 로커 씨가 장부를 덮고 작은 종이쪽지를 픽윅 씨의 손에 쥐여 주며 응수했다. \"이게 배정표입니다, 선생님.\"\n\n이 인물에 대한 이런 즉결 처리에 몹시 어리둥절해진 픽윅 씨는, 어찌하는 게 좋을지 마음속으로 궁리하며 감옥 안으로 되돌아 걸어갔다. 그러나 다른 어떤 조치를 취하기에 앞서, 함께 묵게 될 세 신사를 직접 만나 이야기를 나눠 보는 것이 현명하리라 판단하고, 서둘러 삼층으로 올라갔다.\n\n복도에서 한동안 더듬거리며, 흐릿한 빛 속에서 여러 문에 적힌 번호를 알아보려 애쓰던 끝에, 그는 마침 아침 일과인 백랍 그릇 거두는 일에 나선 술집 사환에게 물어보았다.\n\n\"여보게, 이십칠 호가 어디인가?\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"다섯 칸 더 가시면 됩니다.\" 사환이 대답했다. \"교수형 당하면서 그 와중에 담배를 피우는 사내 모습이 문밖에 분필로 그려져 있습니다요.\"\n\n이 안내에 따라 픽윅 씨는 복도를 따라 천천히 나아가, 앞서 말한 '신사의 초상'을 마주칠 때까지 걸었다. 그러고는 그 얼굴 부분을 집게손가락 마디로 두드렸는데—처음에는 가만히, 그다음에는 소리가 나게 두드렸다. 이 과정을 여러 번 되풀이해도 아무 반응이 없자, 그는 큰맘 먹고 문을 열어 안을 들여다보았다.\n\n방 안에는 단 한 사람뿐이었는데, 그는 몸이 넘어가지 않을 만큼 최대한 창밖으로 몸을 내밀고, 아래 운동장에 있는 어느 친구의 모자 꼭대기에 침을 뱉으려고 대단한 끈기로 애쓰고 있었다. 말을 걸어도, 기침을 해도, 재채기를 해도, 문을 두드려도, 그 밖에 주의를 끄는 다른 어떤 흔한 방법도 이 사람에게 방문객이 와 있음을 알리지 못하자, 픽윅 씨는 한참 머뭇거린 끝에 창가로 다가가 그의 외투 자락을 가만히 잡아당겼다. 그 사내는 머리와 어깨를 매우 잽싸게 안으로 들이밀고는, 픽윅 씨를 머리끝부터 발끝까지 훑어보더니, 무뚝뚝한 어조로 대문자 H로 시작하는—무슨 일로 왔느냐고 물었다.\n\n\"제가 보기엔,\" 픽윅 씨가 배정표를 들여다보며 말했다. \"여기가 삼층 이십칠 호인 것 같은데요?\"\n\n\"그래서요?\" 그 신사가 대답했다.\n\n\"이 종이쪽지를 받고 여기로 온 겁니다.\" 픽윅 씨가 응수했다.\n\n\"이리 줘 보시오.\" 그 신사가 말했다.\n\n픽윅 씨는 그대로 따랐다.\n\n\"로커가 당신을 다른 데로 보냈으면 좋았을 텐데.\" 심슨 씨가(바로 그 노름꾼이었다) 몹시 못마땅한 듯 잠시 뜸을 들인 후에 말했다.\n\n픽윅 씨도 그렇게 생각했다. 그러나 모든 정황을 고려할 때, 잠자코 있는 편이 현명한 처사라고 여겼다.\n\n심슨 씨는 그러고 나서 잠시 골똘히 생각하더니, 창밖으로 고개를 내밀고 날카롭게 휘파람을 불며 무슨 말인가를 큰 소리로 여러 번 외쳤다. 그 말이 무엇인지 픽윅 씨는 알아들을 수 없었으나, 아래 운동장의 수많은 신사들이 곧바로 \"푸주한!\" 하고 외치기 시작하는 것으로 미루어—그 유익한 부류가 매일같이 지하실 난간에서 자기들이 왔음을 알릴 때 쓰는 어조를 흉내 내며—그것이 마틴 씨를 가리키는 무슨 별명이리라고 짐작했다.\n\n뒤이은 일들로 픽윅 씨의 짐작이 정확했음이 확인되었다. 몇 초 뒤, 나이에 비해 너무 일찍 떡 벌어진 신사 하나가, 직업용 푸른 진 작업복에 코가 둥근 장화 차림으로 거의 숨이 차서 방에 들어왔고, 그 뒤를 몹시 초라한 검은 옷에 물개 가죽 모자를 쓴 또 다른 신사가 바짝 뒤따라 들어왔다. 뒤의 신사는 핀과 단추를 번갈아 써서 외투를 턱밑까지 단단히 여미고 있었는데, 매우 거친 붉은 얼굴이 술 취한 목사처럼 보였고, 실제로도 그러했다.\n\n이 두 신사가 번갈아 픽윅 씨의 쪽지를 찬찬히 읽고 나서, 한 사람은 그것이 '장난질'이라는 의견을 내놓았고, 다른 한 사람은 그것이 '제대로 된 일'이라는 확신을 내비쳤다. 이렇게 매우 알아듣기 쉬운 말로 자기들 감정을 기록한 뒤, 그들은 픽윅 씨와 서로를 어색한 침묵 속에 바라보았다.\n\n\"이거 골치 아프게 됐는걸, 이제 막 잠자리를 아늑하게 꾸며 놨는데.\" 목사가, 각각 담요에 둘둘 말린 세 개의 더러운 매트리스를 바라보며 말했다. 그 매트리스들은 낮 동안 방 한구석을 차지하고서 일종의 받침대 노릇을 했는데, 그 위에는 파란 꽃무늬가 그려진 흔한 노란 질그릇으로 된 금 간 낡은 대야와 물주전자와 비누 접시가 놓여 있었다. \"정말 골치 아프게 됐어.\"\n\n마틴 씨는 다소 더 거센 말로 같은 의견을 표했다. 심슨 씨는 명사 한 마디도 붙이지 않은 채 갖가지 욕설 형용사를 세상에 마구 풀어 놓은 다음, 소매를 걷어붙이고 저녁거리로 쓸 채소를 씻기 시작했다.\n\n이러는 동안 픽윅 씨는 방 안을 둘러보고 있었는데, 방은 더럽기 짝이 없었고 참을 수 없을 만큼 답답한 냄새가 났다. 양탄자도, 커튼도, 차양도 흔적조차 없었다. 벽장 하나도 없었다. 설령 벽장이 있었던들 치워 넣을 물건이라곤 거의 없었으리라는 건 의심할 여지가 없었다. 그러나 수가 아무리 적고 하나하나가 아무리 보잘것없다 한들, 빵 부스러기며 치즈 조각이며 축축한 수건이며 고기 자투리며 입던 옷가지며 깨진 그릇이며 주둥이 떨어진 풀무며 살이 빠진 토스트 포크가, 세 한량의 공동 거실 겸 침실인 좁은 방바닥에 여기저기 흩어져 있는 모습은, 그래도 어딘가 불편한 광경을 자아내기 마련이었다.\n\n\"이 일은 어떻게든 처리할 수 있을 것 같소만.\" 푸주한이 꽤 오랜 침묵 끝에 말했다. \"나가시는 데 얼마면 되겠소?\"\n\n\"미안하지만,\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다. \"뭐라고 하셨소? 무슨 말인지 잘 모르겠소만.\"\n\n\"빚을 치르고 나가시는 데 얼마면 되겠냐는 말이오.\" 푸주한이 말했다. \"정해진 동숙료는 이 실링 육 펜스요. 삼 실링이면 되겠소?\"\n\n\"한턱 내는 것도 곁들여서.\" 목사 양반이 덧붙였다.\n\n\"음, 그건 나도 상관없소. 한 사람당 이 펜스 더 얹는 것뿐이니.\" 마틴 씨가 말했다. \"자, 어떠시오? 일주일에 삼 실링 육 펜스에 당신을 빼 드리겠소. 자!\"\n\n\"맥주 한 갤런도 내놓으시고.\" 심슨 씨가 거들었다. \"그렇지!\"\n\n\"그리고 그 자리에서 마셔 버리고.\" 목사가 말했다. \"자, 어서!\"\n\n\"내가 이곳 규칙에 워낙 깜깜이라,\" 픽윅 씨가 응수했다. \"아직 무슨 말씀인지 모르겠소. 내가 다른 데서 지낼 수도 있단 말이오? 그럴 수 없는 줄 알았는데.\"\n\n이 물음에 마틴 씨는 몹시 놀란 표정으로 두 친구를 바라보았고, 그러자 세 신사는 저마다 오른손 엄지손가락으로 왼쪽 어깨 너머를 가리켰다. '뒷전으로'라는 매우 빈약한 말로밖에 글로는 어설프게 설명되지 않는 이 동작은, 한데 어울려 행동하는 데 익숙한 여러 신사 숙녀가 행할 때면 매우 우아하고 경쾌한 효과를 내는데, 그 의미는 가볍고 장난스러운 빈정거림이다.\n\n\"할 수 있느냐고요!\" 마틴 씨가 가엾다는 듯 미소를 지으며 되풀이했다.\n\n\"음, 내가 그렇게 세상 물정을 모른다면, 내 모자를 먹어 치우고 버클까지 통째로 삼키겠소.\" 목사 양반이 말했다.\n\n\"나도 그러겠소.\" 그 노름꾼 신사가 엄숙하게 덧붙였다.\n\n이런 서두를 늘어놓은 다음, 세 동숙인은 한목소리로 픽윅 씨에게 일러 주기를, 플리트 안에서도 돈은 바깥에서나 다름없는 것이라, 원하는 것은 거의 무엇이든 당장 마련해 줄 수 있으며, 만약 그에게 돈이 있고 또 그것을 쓰는 데 거리낌이 없다면, 혼자 쓸 방을 갖고 싶다는 뜻만 비치면 삼십 분 안에 가구까지 갖춰져 딸린 방 하나를 차지할 수 있다고 했다.\n\n이로써 양측은 서로 크게 만족하며 헤어졌다. 픽윅 씨는 다시 한 번 관리실로 발길을 돌렸고, 세 동료는 커피실로 자리를 옮겨, 목사 양반이 감탄스러운 신중함과 선견지명으로 바로 그 목적을 위해 픽윅 씨에게서 빌린 오 실링을 거기서 써 버리기로 했다.\n\n\"내 그럴 줄 알았지!\" 픽윅 씨가 되돌아온 목적을 밝히자 로커 씨가 빙긋이 웃으며 말했다. \"내가 그렇다고 안 했나, 네디?\"\n\n만능 주머니칼의 철학적 주인은 끙 하고 동의의 소리를 냈다.\n\n\"당신이 혼자 쓸 방을 원할 줄 알았다니까요, 정말이지!\" 로커 씨가 말했다. \"어디 봅시다. 가구가 좀 필요하시겠지요. 그건 나한테서 빌리실 거고요? 그게 통상적인 방식이거든요.\"\n\n\"기꺼이 그러지요.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"커피실 쪽 계단 위에 아주 훌륭한 방이 하나 있는데, 형평법원 죄수 거랍니다.\" 로커 씨가 말했다. \"일주일에 일 파운드 들 겁니다. 그쯤은 괜찮으시겠지요?\"\n\n\"전혀 상관없소.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"나랑 같이 그리로 가 보십시다.\" 로커가 몹시 잽싸게 모자를 집어 들며 말했다. \"일은 오 분이면 결판납니다. 원, 처음부터 통 크게 내겠다고 말씀하시지 그러셨소!\"\n\n간수가 미리 말한 대로 일은 금세 마무리되었다. 그 형평법원 죄수는 친구도, 재산도, 집도, 행복도 다 잃을 만큼, 그리고 혼자 쓸 방을 가질 권리를 얻을 만큼 오래 그곳에 있어 온 터였다. 그러나 그는 빵 한 조각이 아쉬울 때가 잦은 불편을 겪고 있었던지라, 방을 세놓으라는 픽윅 씨의 제안에 귀가 솔깃했고, 매주 이십 실링을 받는 대가로 그 방을 오로지 픽윅 씨 혼자 방해받지 않고 차지하도록 넘겨주기로 선뜻 계약하고 합의했다. 더 나아가 그는 그 돈으로, 그 방에 동숙하게 될지도 모르는 어떤 사람이든 빚을 치르고 내보내 주기로도 약정했다.\n\n흥정을 매듭짓는 동안, 픽윅 씨는 가슴 아픈 심정으로 그를 살펴보았다. 그는 키가 크고 수척하며 송장 같은 사내로, 낡은 외투에 슬리퍼 차림이었고, 두 볼은 움푹 꺼지고 눈은 안절부절 갈망에 차 있었다. 입술에는 핏기가 없었고 뼈는 날카롭고 앙상했다. 가엾어라! 감금과 결핍의 무쇠 이빨이 이십 년 세월에 걸쳐 그를 서서히 갈아 깎아 온 것이었다.\n\n\"그동안 선생은 어디서 지내시려오?\" 픽윅 씨가 흔들거리는 탁자 위에 첫 주 방세를 선불로 내려놓으며 말했다.\n\n그 사내는 떨리는 손으로 돈을 그러모으며, 아직 모르겠다고, 침대를 어디로 옮길 수 있을지 가서 알아봐야겠다고 대답했다.\n\n\"내 염려가 됩니다, 선생.\" 픽윅 씨가 그의 팔에 가만히 측은한 손길을 얹으며 말했다. \"선생이 어딘가 시끄럽고 북적이는 데서 지내셔야 할까 봐 말이오. 그러니 부디, 조용히 있고 싶으실 때나 친구분들이 찾아오실 때면 이 방을 선생 방으로 여기십시오.\"\n\n\"친구!\" 그 사내가, 목구멍에서 그르렁거리는 목소리로 끼어들었다. \"내가 이 세상에서 가장 깊은 광산 바닥에 죽어 누워, 관 속에 단단히 못 박히고 납땜되어, 이 감옥 토대 밑으로 진창을 끌고 흐르는 어둡고 더러운 도랑 속에서 썩어 간다 한들, 지금 여기 있는 것보다 더 잊히거나 외면당할 수는 없을 거요. 나는 죽은 사람이오. 사회에 대해 죽은 사람이지요. 영혼이 심판대로 넘어간 자들에게 베푸는 그 동정조차 받지 못하는 처지요. 친구가 날 찾아온다니! 맙소사! 나는 한창나이에서 노년에 이르기까지 이곳에서 가라앉아 왔건만, 내가 침대에 죽어 누우면 내 머리맡에 손을 들어 '그가 떠났으니 다행이다!' 하고 말해 줄 이 하나 없소이다.\"\n\n말하는 동안 그 사내의 얼굴에 예사롭지 않은 빛을 드리웠던 흥분은, 말을 마치자 가라앉았다. 그는 시들어 빠진 두 손을 황급히 흐트러진 모양새로 맞잡고는, 발을 질질 끌며 방을 나갔다.\n\n\"좀 까칠하게 구는구먼요.\" 로커 씨가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"아! 저런 자들은 코끼리 같지요. 이따금 그게 느껴지면, 그러면 사나워지는 겁니다!\"\n\n이렇게 깊은 동정을 담은 말을 한 다음, 로커 씨는 어찌나 척척 일을 처리했는지, 얼마 안 가 그 방은 양탄자, 의자 여섯, 탁자, 소파 겸 침대, 찻주전자, 그리고 갖가지 자잘한 물건들로 갖춰졌는데, 일주일에 이십칠 실링 육 펜스라는 매우 합리적인 세로 빌린 것이었다.\n\n\"자, 또 뭐 해 드릴 일이 있을까요?\" 로커 씨가 매우 흡족한 듯 방을 둘러보며, 그리고 첫 주 임대료를 움켜쥔 주먹 안에서 흥겹게 절렁거리며 물었다.\n\n\"음, 그렇소.\" 한동안 깊이 생각에 잠겨 있던 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"여기 심부름 따위를 다니는 사람이 있소?\"\n\n\"바깥 심부름 말씀입니까?\" 로커 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"그렇소. 바깥에 나갈 수 있는 사람 말이오. 죄수가 아닌 사람.\"\n\n\"예, 있습니다.\" 로커가 말했다. \"빈민 구역에 친구가 한 사람 있는, 불쌍한 작자가 하나 있는데, 그런 일이라면 뭐든 기꺼이 합니다. 지난 두 달 동안 잡일이며 그런 걸 해 오고 있지요. 그 사람을 보내 드릴까요?\"\n\n\"그래 주시오.\" 픽윅 씨가 응수했다. \"가만—아니오. 빈민 구역이라 하셨소? 그곳을 한번 보고 싶구려. 내가 직접 그 사람한테 가 보겠소.\"\n\n채무자 감옥의 빈민 구역이란, 그 이름이 뜻하는 대로, 가장 비참하고 가장 비천한 부류의 채무자들이 갇혀 있는 곳이다. 빈민 구역에 등록한 죄수는 방세도 동숙료도 내지 않는다. 감옥에 들어오고 나갈 때 무는 비용도 그만큼 줄어들고, 약간의 식량을 나눠 받을 자격이 생기는데, 그 식량을 마련하기 위해 자선심 깊은 몇몇 사람들이 때때로 유언으로 얼마 안 되는 유산을 남겨 두었다. 우리 독자 대부분은 기억하겠지만, 불과 몇 해 전까지만 해도 플리트 감옥 담장에는 일종의 쇠우리가 있었고, 그 안에는 굶주린 표정의 사내가 한 사람 자리하고 있어, 이따금 돈통을 절렁절렁 흔들며 구슬픈 목소리로 외치곤 했다. \"제발, 가엾은 채무자들을 생각해 주십시오. 제발 가엾은 채무자들을 생각해 주십시오.\" 이 돈통에 들어온 돈은, 들어온 게 있을 때면, 가난한 죄수들에게 나눠 주었고, 빈민 구역의 사내들은 이 비참한 임무를 서로 번갈아 맡았다.\n\n이 관습은 폐지되었고 우리는 이제 그 쇠우리에 판자를 둘러막았지만, 이 가련한 사람들의 비참하고 궁핍한 처지는 여전히 그대로다. 우리는 이제 그들이 감옥 문에서 지나가는 행인들의 자비와 연민에 호소하도록 내버려 두지 않는다. 그러나 우리는 여전히, 후세의 존경과 찬탄을 받도록, 법전의 책장에 한 점 얼룩도 없이 남겨 두고 있으니, 그 정의롭고 유익한 법이란 곧, 억센 중죄인은 먹이고 입혀야 하나, 한 푼 없는 채무자는 굶주림과 헐벗음 속에 죽도록 내버려 두어야 한다고 선언하는 법이다. 이것은 결코 지어낸 이야기가 아니다. 채무자를 가두는 우리의 감옥마다, 동료 죄수들이 구제해 주지 않는다면, 이런 사람들 가운데 누군가가 결핍의 느린 고통 속에서 숨을 거두지 않고 지나가는 한 주가 없다.\n\n로커가 픽윅 씨를 남겨 두고 떠난 그 좁은 계단을 올라가며 이런 것들을 마음속으로 곱씹다가, 픽윅 씨는 차츰 끓어 넘치는 지경에 이르렀다. 이 문제에 관한 상념에 어찌나 격앙되었던지, 그는 자신이 어디에 있는지, 무슨 일로 찾아왔는지조차 똑똑히 떠올리기도 전에, 안내받은 그 방으로 불쑥 뛰어들고 말았다.\n\n방의 전반적인 모습이 그를 단번에 정신 들게 했다. 그러나 먼지투성이 난롯불 앞에서 깊은 시름에 잠겨 있는 한 사내의 모습에 눈길이 닿기가 무섭게, 그는 모자를 바닥에 떨어뜨린 채, 놀라움에 그대로 못 박힌 듯 꼼짝도 하지 못하고 서 있었다.\n\n그렇다. 누덕누덕 해진 옷에 외투도 걸치지 못한 채, 흔한 옥양목 셔츠는 누렇게 바래 너덜너덜하고, 머리카락은 얼굴 위로 늘어지고, 이목구비는 고통으로 일그러지고 굶주림으로 핼쑥해진—거기 앨프리드 징글 씨가 앉아 있었다. 그는 두 손에 머리를 받치고 눈은 불을 응시한 채, 온몸으로 비참함과 낙담을 드러내고 있었다!\n\n그의 곁에는, 벽에 맥없이 기대선 채, 건장한 시골 사내 하나가, 닳아 빠진 사냥용 채찍으로 오른발을 장식한 장화를 툭툭 치고 있었다. 그의 왼발은 낡은 슬리퍼에 꿰어져 있었다. 말과 개와 술이 그를 한꺼번에 이리로 몰아넣었던 것이다. 그 외짝 장화에는 녹슨 박차가 달려 있었는데, 그는 이따금 그것을 허공으로 휙 차올리는 동시에 장화를 세게 두드리며, 사냥꾼이 말을 다그칠 때 내는 소리를 몇 마디 중얼거렸다. 그는 그 순간, 상상 속에서 어떤 무모한 장애물 경마를 달리고 있었던 것이다. 가엾은 자여! 그가 값비싼 마구간의 가장 빠른 짐승에 올라 시합을 치를 때도, 플리트로 끝나 버린 그 코스를 내달리던 속도의 절반에도 미치지 못했으리라.\n\n방 맞은편에는 노인 하나가 작은 나무 상자 위에 앉아, 눈을 바닥에 못 박은 채, 얼굴은 더없이 깊고 가망 없는 절망의 표정으로 굳어 있었다. 어린 소녀 하나가—그의 어린 손녀가—그에게 매달려, 온갖 어린애다운 갖은 수를 써 가며 그의 주의를 끌어 보려 애쓰고 있었지만, 노인은 그 아이를 보지도 듣지도 못했다. 한때 그에게 음악이었던 목소리도, 한때 그에게 빛이었던 눈동자도, 이제 그의 감각에 차갑게 떨어질 뿐이었다. 그의 사지는 병으로 떨리고 있었고, 마비가 그의 정신마저 사로잡고 있었다.\n\n방에는 두세 사람이 더 있어, 작은 무리를 이루어 모여 자기들끼리 소리 없이 이야기를 나누고 있었다. 야위고 초췌한 여인도 하나 있었는데—어느 죄수의 아내였다—그녀는 바싹 마르고 시들어 버린 식물의 가련한 그루터기에 정성스레 물을 주고 있었다. 그 식물은 한눈에 보아도 다시는 푸른 잎을 틔우지 못할 것이 분명했으니—어쩌면 그녀가 이곳에 와서 떠맡은 그 소임을 너무도 잘 나타내는 표상이었으리라.\n\n픽윅 씨가 놀라움 속에 주위를 둘러보았을 때 그의 눈앞에 나타난 것들은 그러했다. 누군가가 허둥지둥 방으로 비틀거리며 들어오는 소리에 그는 정신이 들었다. 문 쪽으로 눈을 돌리자 새로 들어온 자와 시선이 마주쳤는데, 그 누더기와 때 너머로 그는 낯익은 잡 트로터 씨의 이목구비를 알아보았다.\n\n\"픽윅 씨!\" 잡이 큰 소리로 외쳤다.\n\n\"네?\" 징글이 자리에서 벌떡 일어나며 말했다.\n\n\"픽—! 정말 그렇군—묘한 곳—이상한 일—꼴좋다—참으로.\" 징글 씨는 바지 주머니가 있던 자리에 두 손을 찔러 넣고, 턱을 가슴께로 떨군 채 도로 의자에 주저앉았다.\n\n픽윅 씨는 마음이 동했다. 두 사람의 몰골이 너무도 비참해 보였기 때문이다. 잡이 들고 들어온 작은 양 허릿살 한 조각을 향해 징글이 자기도 모르게 던진 날카로운 눈길은, 두 시간에 걸친 설명보다도 그들의 영락한 처지를 더 많이 말해 주었다. 픽윅 씨는 부드럽게 징글을 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"긴히 할 얘기가 있소. 잠깐 나와 주겠소?\"\n\n\"물론이지요.\" 징글이 황급히 일어서며 말했다. \"멀리는 못 가지만—여기선 너무 걸어 다닐 염려는 없으니—철창 정원—풍경 좋고—낭만적이나 넓지는 않고—일반 공개—주인은 늘 도시에 나가 있고—가정부는 끔찍이도 꼼꼼하고—참으로.\"\n\n\"외투를 두고 왔구려.\" 그들이 계단으로 걸어 나와 등 뒤로 문을 닫을 때 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"네?\" 징글이 말했다. \"전당포에—소중한 친척—톰 삼촌—어쩔 수 없었지요—먹어야 하니까요. 본능의 요구—뭐 그런 거지요.\"\n\n\"무슨 말이오?\"\n\n\"가 버렸지요, 선생님—마지막 외투—어쩔 수 없었습니다. 장화 한 켤레로 꼬박 두 주를 버텼지요. 비단 우산—상아 손잡이—한 주—사실입니다—명예를 걸고—잡한테 물어보세요—알고 있으니까.\"\n\n\"장화 한 켤레와 상아 손잡이가 달린 비단 우산으로 세 주를 버텼다고!\" 픽윅 씨가 외쳤다. 그는 그런 일을 난파선 이야기에서나 들었거나 콘스터블 잡지에서나 읽어 보았을 뿐이었다.\n\n\"사실입니다.\" 징글이 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다. \"전당포—전당표가 여기 있지요—푼돈—아무것도 아닌 액수—죄다 악당들이라.\"\n\n\"오,\" 픽윅 씨가 이 설명에 크게 안도하며 말했다. \"알겠소. 옷가지를 전당 잡혔다는 말이군.\"\n\n\"전부 다—잡 것도—셔츠도 다 가 버렸지요—상관없습니다—빨래 수고를 더는 셈이니. 곧 아무것도 안 남겠지요—침대에 누워—굶고—죽고—검시—작은 영안실—가엾은 죄수—최소한의 필수품—쉬쉬하고—배심원 여러분—교도소장 단골 상인들—조용히 덮고—자연사—검시관 명령—구빈원 장례—꼴좋다—다 끝나고—막을 내리고.\"\n\n징글은 자기 인생의 앞날에 관한 이 기이한 요약을, 늘 그렇듯 거침없이 늘어놓으며, 미소를 꾸며 내려고 얼굴을 갖가지로 씰룩거렸다. 픽윅 씨는 그의 무모함이 가장된 것임을 어렵잖게 알아챘고, 그의 얼굴을 정면으로, 그러나 모질지 않게 들여다보다가, 그의 눈이 눈물로 젖어 있는 것을 보았다.\n\n\"좋은 분이지요.\" 징글이 그의 손을 꼭 쥐고 고개를 돌리며 말했다. \"배은망덕한 놈—사내답잖게 울다니—어쩔 수가 없네요—고약한 열병—몸이 약해져—병들고—굶주려서. 죄다 자업자득—허나 어지간히 고생했지요—참으로.\" 더는 도무지 겉치레를 유지할 수 없었던 데다, 어쩌면 그 안간힘 때문에 한층 더 무너져 버린 이 낙담한 떠돌이 배우는, 계단에 주저앉아 두 손에 얼굴을 묻고 어린아이처럼 흐느꼈다.\n\n\"자, 자,\" 픽윅 씨가 적잖이 감정에 북받쳐 말했다. \"내가 사정을 다 알게 되면, 무엇을 할 수 있을지 알아봅시다. 이보게, 잡, 그 친구는 어디 있나?\"\n\n\"여기 있습니다, 선생님.\" 잡이 계단에 모습을 드러내며 대답했다. 그런데 앞서 말했듯, 우리는 그를 가장 형편이 좋을 때조차 눈이 움푹 꺼진 자로 묘사한 바 있다. 지금의 궁핍하고 곤궁한 처지에서는, 마치 그 두 눈이 아예 도시 밖으로 떠나 버린 것처럼 보였다.\n\n\"여기 있습니다, 선생님.\" 잡이 외쳤다.\n\n\"이리 오게.\" 픽윅 씨가, 조끼 위로 굵은 눈물 네 줄기를 흘리면서, 짐짓 엄한 표정을 지으려 애쓰며 말했다. \"이걸 받게.\"\n\n무엇을 받으라는 것인가? 이런 말이 흔히 쓰이는 뜻대로라면, 그것은 한 대 갈기는 일이어야 했다. 세상 돌아가는 이치로 보자면, 그것은 묵직하고 호된 따귀여야 마땅했다. 픽윅 씨는 지금 완전히 자기 손아귀에 들어온 이 궁핍한 떠돌이에게 속고, 기만당하고, 부당한 일을 당해 왔기 때문이다. 사실대로 말해야 할까? 그것은 픽윅 씨의 조끼 주머니에서 나온 무엇이었으니, 잡의 손에 건네질 때 절렁 소리를 냈다. 그리고 그것을 건네는 일은, 어찌 된 영문인지, 우리의 훌륭한 옛 친구가 황급히 자리를 뜰 때 그 눈에는 반짝임을, 그 가슴에는 벅참을 안겨 주었다.\n\n픽윅 씨가 자기 방에 이르렀을 때 샘은 이미 돌아와 있었고, 주인의 편의를 위해 마련된 것들을 일종의 시무룩한 만족감을 띤 채 살피고 있었는데, 그 모습이 보기에 무척 즐거웠다. 주인이 애초에 이런 곳에 있는 것 자체에 단호히 반대하던 웰러 씨는, 행해지거나 말해지거나 제안되거나 권해진 그 무엇에도 너무 기뻐하는 기색을 보이지 않는 것이 자신의 높은 도덕적 의무라고 여기는 모양이었다.\n\n\"그래, 샘.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"예, 선생님.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"이만하면 꽤 편안하지, 응, 샘?\"\n\n\"꽤 괜찮습니다요, 선생님.\" 샘이 깔보는 듯한 태도로 주위를 둘러보며 응답했다.\n\n\"터프먼 씨와 다른 친구들은 만났나?\"\n\n\"예, 만나 뵈었습니다, 선생님. 내일 오시겠답니다. 오늘은 못 오신다는 말을 듣고 다들 무척 놀라시던뎁쇼.\" 샘이 대답했다.\n\n\"내가 부탁한 물건들은 가져왔고?\"\n\n웰러 씨는 대답 대신, 자기가 되도록 가지런히 방 한구석에 정돈해 둔 여러 꾸러미를 가리켰다.\n\n\"좋아, 샘.\" 픽윅 씨가 잠시 머뭇거린 뒤에 말했다. \"내가 하려는 말을 잘 들어 보게, 샘.\"\n\n\"물론입죠, 선생님.\" 웰러 씨가 응수했다. \"어서 말씀하십시오, 선생님.\"\n\n\"나는 처음부터 느껴 왔네, 샘.\" 픽윅 씨가 자못 엄숙하게 말했다. \"여기가 젊은이를 데려올 곳이 못 된다고 말일세.\"\n\n\"늙은이를 데려올 곳도 못 됩니다요, 선생님.\" 웰러 씨가 한마디 했다.\n\n\"네 말이 맞다, 샘.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"하지만 늙은이야 제 부주의와 방심 탓에 여기 올 수 있다손 쳐도, 젊은이는 제가 섬기는 자들의 이기심 때문에 여기 끌려오는 것일세. 어느 모로 보나, 그런 젊은이들은 여기 머물지 않는 편이 낫네. 내 말 알아듣겠나, 샘?\"\n\n\"아뇨, 선생님, 모르겠습니다요.\" 웰러 씨가 고집스럽게 대답했다.\n\n\"잘 생각해 보게, 샘.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"음, 선생님.\" 샘이 잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤 응수했다. \"선생님 속뜻을 알 것도 같습니다요. 그리고 제가 그 속뜻을 제대로 헤아린 거라면, 제 생각엔 선생님이 너무 심하게 밀어붙이시는 겁니다요. 우편 마차 마부가 눈보라에 따라잡혔을 때 그 눈보라더러 한 말처럼 말입죠.\"\n\n\"내 말을 알아들었구나, 샘.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"네가 앞으로 여러 해 동안 이런 곳에서 빈둥거리지 않았으면 하는 내 바람은 차치하더라도, 플리트의 채무자가 제 하인의 시중을 받는다는 것은 터무니없이 어이없는 일이라고 느낀다네. 샘,\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"한동안 너는 나를 떠나 있어야겠다.\"\n\n\"오, 한동안이라고요, 선생님?\" 웰러 씨가 다소 빈정거리듯 응수했다.\n\n\"그래, 내가 여기 있는 동안만일세.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"네 품삯은 계속 줄 것이야. 내 세 친구 가운데 누구라도, 나를 봐서라도 기꺼이 너를 거둘 게다. 그리고 내가 만에 하나 이곳을 벗어나게 된다면, 샘,\" 픽윅 씨가 짐짓 명랑한 척하며 덧붙였다. \"그렇게 되면, 너는 즉시 내게 돌아오게 되리라고 내 약속하마.\"\n\n\"그럼 제가 말씀드리겠습니다, 선생님.\" 웰러 씨가 진지하고 엄숙한 목소리로 말했다. \"이런 식은 전혀 안 될 일입니다요. 그러니 다시는 그런 말씀 듣지 맙시다요.\"\n\n\"나는 진심이고, 또 굳게 마음먹었다, 샘.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그러시단 말이지요, 선생님?\" 웰러 씨가 단호히 물었다. \"아주 좋습니다요, 선생님. 그럼 저도 그렇습니다요.\"\n\n이렇게 말하고 나서 웰러 씨는 매우 정확하게 모자를 머리에 눌러쓰더니, 불쑥 방을 나가 버렸다.\n\n\"샘!\" 픽윅 씨가 그를 뒤따라 부르며 외쳤다. \"샘! 이리 오너라!\"\n\n그러나 긴 복도는 더 이상 발소리를 되울리지 않았다. 샘 웰러는 가 버린 것이다.\n\n# CHAPTER XLIII. SHOWING HOW MR. SAMUEL WELLER GOT INTO DIFFICULTIES\n\n링컨스 인 필즈의 포르투갈 거리에 자리한, 높직하고 조명은 어두우며 환기는 그보다 더 나쁜 어느 방에는, 거의 한 해 내내 가발을 쓴 신사 한 명, 두 명, 세 명, 또는 네 명이—그때그때 형편에 따라—앉아 있는데, 그들 앞에는 이 나라 판사들이 쓰는 것을 본떠 만든 작은 필기용 책상이 놓여 있다. 다만 프랑스식 광택칠만은 빠져 있다. 그들의 오른편에는 법정 변호사들이 들어앉은 칸막이가 있고, 왼편에는 파산 채무자들이 가둬진 울이 있으며, 정면에는 유독 지저분한 얼굴들이 비탈을 이루며 늘어서 있다. 이 신사들이 바로 파산법원의 위원들이고, 그들이 앉아 있는 곳이 바로 파산법원 그 자체다.\n\n이 법원에는 기억할 수 없을 만큼 먼 옛날부터 한 가지 기묘한 운명이 따라붙어 있으니, 어찌 된 영문인지 런던의 가난하고 행색은 초라하나 신사연하는 모든 자들의 공통된 집합소이자 매일의 피난처로, 그들 모두의 묵계에 따라 통하고 또 그렇게 받아들여진다는 것이다. 이곳은 늘 만원이다. 맥주와 독주의 김이 끊임없이 천장으로 올라가고, 열기에 응결되어 비처럼 벽을 타고 흘러내린다. 한 해 동안 하운즈디치 전체에서 팔리려고 내놓일 헌 옷보다도 더 많은 헌 옷가지가 이곳에는 한꺼번에 들어차 있고, 해 뜰 녘부터 해 질 녘까지 타이번과 화이트채플 사이의 모든 펌프와 이발소를 동원해도 말끔히 단장할 수 없을 만큼 많은 씻지 않은 살갗과 희끗한 수염이 이곳에 모여 있다.\n\n이 사람들 가운데 누구라도 그토록 부지런히 드나드는 그곳에 일말의 볼일이 있거나 조금이라도 관계가 있으리라고 생각해서는 안 된다. 만일 그런 게 있었다면 놀랄 일도 아니고, 그 기이함도 사라졌을 것이다. 그들 중 어떤 이는 개정 시간 대부분을 잠으로 보내고, 또 어떤 이는 손수건에 싼 작은 도시락을 들고 다니거나 다 해진 주머니에 삐죽 꽂은 채로, 오물오물 먹으면서 똑같은 흥미로 귀를 기울인다. 그러나 그들 가운데 누구도 일찍이 상정된 어떤 사건에 조금이라도 개인적 이해관계가 있었던 적은 없다. 무엇을 하든 그들은 처음 순간부터 마지막 순간까지 그곳에 앉아 있다. 날이 궂고 비가 내리면 그들은 모두 흠뻑 젖은 채로 들어오는데, 그럴 때면 법정 안의 수증기는 버섯 재배 구덩이의 그것과 다름없다.\n\n우연히 들른 방문객이라면 이곳이 초라함의 수호신에게 바쳐진 신전이라 여길 법하다. 이곳에 딸린 전령이나 송달리 가운데 자기 몸에 맞게 지은 외투를 걸친 자는 하나도 없고, 그나마 봐줄 만큼 생기 있거나 건강해 보이는 사람이라곤 이 건물 전체를 통틀어 머리가 희고 얼굴이 사과 같은 작은 법정 정리 하나뿐이다. 그러나 그조차도 브랜디에 절인 상태 나쁜 버찌처럼, 본래 그에게 주장할 권리가 없는 보존 상태에 이르도록 인위적으로 바싹 말라 쪼그라든 듯 보인다. 법정 변호사들의 가발마저 분이 형편없이 발려 있고, 그 곱슬머리에는 빳빳함이 없다.\n\n그러나 위원들 아래쪽의 크고 휑한 탁자에 앉은 사무 변호사들이야말로 결국 가장 큰 구경거리다. 이 신사들 중에서도 더 넉넉한 자의 직업적 살림살이라야 푸른 가방 하나와 사환 아이 하나로 이루어지는데, 그 아이는 대개 유대교를 믿는 소년이다. 그들에게는 고정된 사무실이 없으니, 법률 업무는 술집의 응접실이나 감옥의 마당에서 처리되며, 그들은 떼를 지어 그리로 몰려가 합승마차의 호객꾼처럼 손님을 끌어모은다. 그들은 기름지고 곰팡내 나는 행색을 하고 있는데, 그들에게 무슨 악덕이라 할 만한 것이 있다면 아마 술 마시는 일과 속이는 일이 그 가운데 가장 두드러질 것이다. 그들의 거처는 대개 '거주 허가 구역' 변두리에 있으며, 주로 세인트 조지스 필즈의 오벨리스크에서 반경 1마일 안에 자리 잡고 있다. 그들의 인상은 호감을 주지 못하고, 그 거동은 별나다.\n\n이 유식한 무리의 한 사람인 솔로몬 펠 씨는 살집이 무르고 축 늘어진 창백한 사내로, 한순간은 초록빛으로 보이다가 다음 순간엔 갈색으로 보이는 외투를 걸쳤는데, 그 벨벳 깃 역시 똑같은 카멜레온 색조를 띠고 있었다. 그의 이마는 좁고 얼굴은 넓으며 머리는 컸고, 코는 온통 한쪽으로 쏠려 있어, 마치 자연이 그가 태어날 때 그에게서 본 성벽에 격분하여 코를 화김에 한번 비틀어 놓았는데 그것이 영영 회복되지 못한 것 같았다. 그러나 목이 짧고 천식기가 있던 터라 그는 주로 이 기관으로 숨을 쉬었으니, 어쩌면 그 코가 꾸밈에서 모자란 것을 쓸모로 메운 셈인지도 모른다.\n\n\"제가 틀림없이 그분을 무사히 통과시켜 드리지요.\" 펠 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"정말 그렇겠소?\" 그 장담을 받은 사람이 대꾸했다.\n\n\"확실히 틀림없습니다.\" 펠이 대답했다. \"하지만 말이죠, 그분이 무자격 변호사한테 갔더라면, 그 결과는 제가 책임 못 졌을 겝니다.\"\n\n\"아!\" 상대가 입을 벌린 채 말했다.\n\n\"그럼요, 못 책임지고말고요.\" 펠 씨가 말했다. 그러고는 입술을 오므리고 눈살을 찌푸리며 알 수 없다는 듯 고개를 저었다.\n\n그런데 이 대화가 오간 곳은 바로 파산법원 맞은편에 있는 술집이었고, 대화 상대는 다름 아닌 웰러 영감이었다. 그는 법령에 따라 면책 처분을 받게 해 달라는 청원이 그날 심리될 예정인 친구를 위로하고 달래러 그곳에 왔으며, 마침 그 친구의 사무 변호사와 상의하고 있던 참이었다.\n\n\"그래, 조지는 어디 있소?\" 노신사가 물었다.\n\n펠 씨가 뒤편 응접실 쪽으로 고갯짓을 했고, 웰러 씨가 곧장 그리로 가자, 직업적 형제 대여섯 명이 그의 도착을 반긴다는 표시로 더없이 따뜻하고 살가운 태도로 그를 맞았다. 장거리 역마차에 투기적이지만 경솔한 열정을 품었다가 지금의 곤경에 빠진 그 파산 신사는 안색이 무척 좋았고, 새우와 흑맥주로 들뜬 마음을 달래고 있었다.\n\n웰러 씨와 그 벗들 사이에 오간 인사는 그 업종 종사자들만의 비밀스러운 의식에 엄격히 국한된 것으로, 오른 손목을 홱 돌리는 동작과 동시에 새끼손가락을 공중으로 튕겨 올리는 것으로 이루어졌다. 우리는 일찍이 유명한 마부 둘을 알았는데(가엾게도 지금은 둘 다 세상을 떠났다) 그들은 쌍둥이로, 서로 간에 꾸밈없고 헌신적인 정이 오갔다. 그들은 이십사 년 동안 매일같이 도버 가도에서 서로를 스쳐 지나갔으되 이 인사 말고는 어떤 다른 인사도 나누지 않았다. 그런데도 한 사람이 죽자 다른 한 사람은 시름시름 야위어 가더니 머지않아 그의 뒤를 따랐다!\n\n\"그래, 조지,\" 웰러 영감이 겉옷을 벗으며 늘 그렇듯 점잖게 자리에 앉으며 말했다. \"어떻게 됐나? 뒤는 다 괜찮고, 안은 꽉 찼나?\"\n\n\"다 괜찮네, 이 친구야.\" 곤경에 빠진 신사가 대답했다.\n\n\"잿빛 암말은 누구한테 넘겼고?\" 웰러 씨가 근심스레 물었다.\n\n조지가 그렇다고 고개를 끄덕였다.\n\n\"그래, 그럼 됐네.\" 웰러 씨가 말했다. \"마차도 잘 처리했고?\"\n\n\"안전한 데다 위탁했네.\" 조지가 새우 대여섯 마리의 머리를 비틀어 떼고는 더 군말 없이 삼키며 대답했다.\n\n\"아주 좋아, 아주 좋아.\" 웰러 씨가 말했다. \"내리막길 갈 땐 늘 제동장치를 살피게. 운송장은 다 깔끔하고 떳떳하고?\"\n\n\"진술 명세서 말씀이군요.\" 펠 씨가 웰러 씨의 뜻을 짐작하고 말했다. \"그 명세서는 펜과 잉크로 만들 수 있는 한 더없이 명료하고 흠잡을 데 없습니다.\"\n\n웰러 씨는 이 처리에 속으로 흡족해한다는 듯 고개를 끄덕이고는, 펠 씨를 향해 돌아서서 친구 조지를 가리키며 말했다.\n\n\"이 친구 옷은 언제 벗기시오?\"\n\n\"음,\" 펠 씨가 대답했다. \"그분은 이의 신청자 명단에서 세 번째에 있으니, 한 삼십 분쯤 지나면 차례가 올 듯합니다. 제 사무원더러 차례가 올 만하면 건너와서 알려 달라고 일러 뒀습니다.\"\n\n웰러 씨가 그 사무 변호사를 머리끝부터 발끝까지 크게 감탄하며 훑어보고는 힘주어 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 한잔 뭘로 드시겠소, 선생?\"\n\n\"아니, 정말이지,\" 펠 씨가 대답했다. \"참으로 친절하시군요—. 맹세코 저는 평소에 그런 걸—. 아침나절이 워낙 일러서 사실 저는 거의—. 음, 럼주 삼 펜스어치만 가져다주오, 아가씨.\"\n\n주문이 떨어지기도 전에 미리 짐작했던 시중드는 처녀가 펠 앞에 독주 잔을 놓고는 물러갔다.\n\n\"여러분,\" 펠 씨가 좌중을 둘러보며 말했다. \"여러분 친구의 성공을 위하여! 자랑하기는 싫습니다, 여러분. 그건 제 방식이 아니지요. 하지만 한마디 안 할 수가 없군요. 만일 여러분 친구가 운 좋게도 다음과 같은 손에 떨어지지 않았더라면—아니, 하려던 말은 안 하겠습니다. 여러분, 여러분께 인사 올립니다.\" 눈 깜짝할 사이에 잔을 비운 펠 씨는 입맛을 다시며 둘러앉은 마부들을 흐뭇하게 둘러보았는데, 그들은 분명 그를 일종의 신적 존재로 여기고 있었다.\n\n\"가만있자,\" 그 법률 권위자가 말했다. \"내가 무슨 말을 하고 있었더라, 여러분?\"\n\n\"제 생각엔 똑같은 걸로 한 잔 더 들어도 사양 안 하시겠다고 말씀하시던 참이었지요, 선생.\" 웰러 씨가 점잖은 익살을 부리며 말했다.\n\n\"하하!\" 펠 씨가 웃었다. \"나쁘지 않군, 나쁘지 않아. 게다가 전문직 종사자가 말이오! 이 아침나절에 그건 좀 지나치게 좋은—음, 글쎄, 아가씨—원한다면 다시 한 잔 가져다줘도 좋겠소. 험!\"\n\n이 마지막 소리는 엄숙하고 위엄 있는 헛기침이었으니, 펠 씨는 청중 가운데 몇몇에게서 점잖지 못하게 웃으려는 기미를 알아채고는, 스스로의 체면을 위해 그렇게 해 두는 것이 마땅하다고 여긴 것이었다.\n\n\"고인이 되신 대법관께서는 여러분, 저를 무척 아끼셨지요.\" 펠 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그것도 그분으로선 무척 갸륵한 일이었구려.\" 웰러 씨가 끼어들었다.\n\n\"옳소, 옳소.\" 펠 씨의 의뢰인이 맞장구쳤다. \"안 그럴 까닭이 없지요?\"\n\n\"아! 그렇고말고요!\" 그때껏 한마디도 않던, 앞으로도 더 말할 것 같지 않아 보이는 얼굴이 무척 붉은 사내가 말했다. \"안 그럴 까닭이 어디 있겠소?\"\n\n좌중에 동의의 웅성거림이 번졌다.\n\n\"기억합니다, 여러분,\" 펠 씨가 말했다. \"한번은 그분과 만찬을 함께했지요. 우리 둘뿐이었는데도 마치 스무 명이 올 것처럼 모든 게 호화로웠습니다—그분 오른편의 식기 받침대 위에는 옥새가 놓여 있고, 가발에 갑옷을 차려입은 자가 칼을 빼 들고 비단 양말을 신은 채 권표를 지키고 서 있었지요—여러분, 그건 밤낮없이 늘 그렇게 한답니다. 그때 그분이 말씀하시길, '펠,' 하시더니, '괜한 겸양은 부리지 말게, 펠. 자넨 재능 있는 사람이야. 자넨 누구든 파산법원을 통과시킬 수 있어, 펠. 자네 나라가 자네를 자랑스러워해야 마땅하네.' 바로 그 말씀이셨습니다. '대법관님,' 제가 말했지요, '과찬이십니다.'—'펠,' 그분이 말씀하셨습니다, '내가 과찬한다면 내 목을 내놓겠네.'\"\n\n\"그분이 그렇게 말씀하셨소?\" 웰러 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"그러셨지요.\" 펠이 대답했다.\n\n\"그렇다면,\" 웰러 씨가 말했다. \"내 생각엔 의회가 그 일을 따졌어야 했소. 그분이 가난뱅이였다면 그렇게 했을 거요.\"\n\n\"하지만 여보게,\" 펠 씨가 항변했다. \"그건 비밀히 하신 말씀이었소.\"\n\n\"뭐라고요?\" 웰러 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"비밀히 하신 말씀이라고.\"\n\n\"오! 그럼 됐소이다.\" 웰러 씨가 잠시 곰곰 생각하더니 대꾸했다. \"비밀히 자기 목을 걸겠다 하셨다면야 그건 또 다른 얘기지.\"\n\n\"물론 그렇지요.\" 펠 씨가 말했다. \"그 차이는 명백하니, 보시면 아실 겝니다.\"\n\n\"사정이 완전히 달라지는구려.\" 웰러 씨가 말했다. \"계속하시오, 선생.\"\n\n\"아니요, 더는 계속하지 않겠습니다, 선생.\" 펠 씨가 낮고 진지한 어조로 말했다. \"선생께서는 이 대화가 사사로운 것이었음을—사사롭고 은밀한 것이었음을 일깨워 주셨습니다, 여러분. 여러분, 저는 전문직 종사자입니다. 어쩌면 제가 제 직업에서 상당히 존경받는 사람일 수도—아닐 수도 있겠지요. 대개는 다들 압니다. 저는 아무 말 않겠습니다. 이 방에서 이미 제 고귀한 벗의 명성을 해치는 발언들이 나왔습니다. 양해해 주시지요, 여러분. 제가 경솔했습니다. 그분의 동의 없이 이 문제를 입에 올릴 권리가 제게 없음을 느낍니다. 고맙소, 선생. 고맙소이다.\" 이렇게 일장 연설을 마친 펠 씨는 두 손을 주머니에 찔러 넣고 험상궂게 좌중을 노려보며, 무시무시한 결의로 반 페니짜리 동전 세 닢을 짤랑거렸다.\n\n이 갸륵한 결심이 채 다잡아지기도 전에, 떼려야 뗄 수 없는 동행인 사환 아이와 푸른 가방이 방 안으로 우당탕 뛰어들더니, 사건이 곧 시작된다고 알렸다(적어도 아이가 그랬다는 말이지, 푸른 가방은 그 통보에 한몫하지 않았으니까). 이 소식이 전해지자마자 일행 전체가 길을 건너 서둘러 가서 법정 안으로 비집고 들어가려 다투기 시작했는데, 이 예비 의식은 보통의 경우 이십오 분에서 삼십 분쯤 걸리는 것으로 따져진다.\n\n웰러 씨는 몸집이 튼실한지라 단번에 군중 속으로 몸을 던졌으니, 결국엔 자기에게 알맞은 어느 자리쯤에 떠오르리라는 필사적인 기대를 품고서였다. 그러나 그 성공은 그의 기대에 그리 미치지 못했으니, 모자 벗는 것을 잊은 탓에, 그가 적잖은 힘으로 발을 디뎌 밟아 버린 보이지 않는 어떤 자가 그의 모자를 눈 위까지 푹 내려쳐 버린 것이었다. 그자는 곧바로 자기 성급함을 후회했던 모양인지, 알아들을 수 없는 놀람의 외마디를 중얼거리며 노인을 복도로 끌어내더니, 한바탕 격렬한 씨름 끝에 그의 머리와 얼굴을 자유롭게 풀어 주었다.\n\n\"새뮤얼!\" 웰러 씨가 이렇게 하여 자기 구원자를 보게 되자 외쳤다.\n샘이 고개를 끄덕였다.\n\n\"넌 효성스럽고 다정한 꼬맹이로구나, 안 그러냐,\" 웰러 씨가 말했다. \"늘그막에 든 아비 모자를 푹 눌러 씌우러 오다니 말이다.\"\n\n\"내가 누군지 어떻게 알았겠소?\" 아들이 대꾸했다. \"발 무게로 아버지를 알아보길 바랐소?\"\n\n\"음, 그건 참 맞는 말이다, 새미.\" 웰러 씨가 단번에 누그러져 대답했다. \"한데 여긴 무슨 일로 왔느냐? 네 주인 양반은 여기서 아무 득 볼 게 없다, 새미. 저들은 그 평결을 통과시키지 않을 게야, 안 시킬 게다, 새미.\" 그러고는 웰러 씨가 법정 같은 엄숙함으로 고개를 저었다.\n\n\"참 별난 영감탱이네!\" 샘이 소리쳤다. \"맨날 평결이니 알리바이니 그딴 소리만 늘어놓고. 누가 평결 얘기를 했수?\"\n\n웰러 씨는 아무 대꾸도 않고 다시 한번 더없이 유식한 척 고개를 저었다.\n\n\"그 골통일랑 짤랑대는 거 그만두쇼, 통째로 용수철에서 빠지길 바라지 않거든.\" 샘이 참다못해 말했다. \"그리고 사리에 맞게 처신 좀 하쇼. 어젯밤 아버지 찾으러 마퀴스 오브 그랜비까지 내처 갔다 왔수.\"\n\n\"마쇼니스 오브 그랜비는 만나 봤느냐, 새미?\" 웰러 씨가 한숨을 쉬며 물었다.\n\n\"네, 만나 봤수.\" 샘이 대답했다.\n\n\"그 가여운 사람은 어떤 낯빛이더냐?\"\n\n\"아주 이상합디다.\" 샘이 말했다. \"내 생각엔 그 파인애플 럼주랑 비슷한 독한 약들을 너무 들이켜서 차츰 제 몸을 해치고 있는 것 같습디다.\"\n\n\"설마 그런 건 아니겠지, 새미?\" 영감이 진지하게 말했다.\n\n\"정말이라니까요.\" 아들이 대답했다.\n\n웰러 씨가 아들의 손을 잡고 꽉 쥐었다가 떨어뜨렸다. 그러는 그의 얼굴에는 어떤 표정이 어렸으니—낙담이나 우려가 아니라, 오히려 희망의 달콤하고 부드러운 빛깔에 더 가까운 것이었다. 체념의, 아니 심지어 명랑함의 어렴풋한 빛까지도 그의 얼굴을 스쳐 지나갔으니, 그가 천천히 입을 떼며 말하길, \"확실친 않다, 새미. 나중에 행여 실망할까 싶어 아주 단정한다고는 말 못 하겠다만, 그래도 내 생각엔, 얘야, 내 생각엔, 그 목자 양반이 간에 병이 든 것 같구나!\"\n\n\"안색이 나쁘던가요?\" 샘이 물었다.\n\n\"유난히 핼쑥하더라.\" 아비가 대답했다. \"코빼기만 빼고 말이다, 그건 그 어느 때보다 더 붉었지. 입맛은 그저 그렇다만, 들이켜는 건 기막히게 잘하더라.\"\n\n이렇게 말하면서 럼주 생각이 웰러 씨의 마음에 끼어든 모양이었다. 그가 침울하고 골똘한 표정을 지었으니 말이다. 그러나 그는 이내 본정신을 되찾았으니, 유달리 흡족할 때만 부리곤 하는 온갖 종류의 눈짓을 완벽한 알파벳처럼 늘어놓은 것이 그 증거였다.\n\n\"자, 그럼,\" 샘이 말했다. \"내 일 얘기로 갑시다. 그 귀나 좀 활짝 열고, 내 말 끝날 때까지 아무 말 마쇼.\" 이런 서두를 떼고서 샘은 픽윅 씨와 마지막으로 나눈 기억할 만한 대화를 되도록 간추려 들려주었다.\n\n\"혼자 거기 남으시겠다니, 가여운 양반!\" 웰러 영감이 외쳤다. \"편들어 줄 사람 하나 없이! 그건 안 될 일이다, 새뮤얼, 안 될 일이야.\"\n\n\"당연히 안 되지요.\" 샘이 단언했다. \"오기 전부터 그건 알고 있었수.\"\n\n\"이런, 저들이 그 양반을 산 채로 잡아먹을 게다, 새미.\" 웰러 씨가 외쳤다.\n\n샘은 그 의견에 동의한다는 듯 고개를 끄덕였다.\n\n\"그 양반은 좀 설익은 채로 들어가시는 거다, 새미,\" 웰러 씨가 비유적으로 말했다. \"그리고 나올 땐 어찌나 바싹 익어 나오시는지, 가장 친한 벗들도 못 알아볼 게다. 비둘기 구이쯤은 그에 비하면 아무것도 아니지, 새미.\"\n\n샘 웰러가 다시 고개를 끄덕였다.\n\n\"그래선 안 된다, 새뮤얼.\" 웰러 씨가 엄숙하게 말했다.\n\n\"그래서야 안 되지요.\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n\"암, 안 되고말고.\" 웰러 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"자, 그럼,\" 샘이 말했다. \"아버진 마치 육 펜스짜리 책에 그림으로 나오는 그 얼굴 붉은 닉슨처럼 그럴듯하게 예언만 늘어놓고 있잖수.\"\n\n\"그게 누구냐, 새미?\" 웰러 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"누군진 신경 쓰지 마쇼.\" 샘이 받아쳤다. \"마부는 아니었으니, 그거면 아버진 충분하지.\"\n\n\"그 이름 가진 마구간지기는 하나 알았었다만.\" 웰러 씨가 곰곰 생각하며 말했다.\n\n\"그 사람 아니우.\" 샘이 말했다. \"이 양반은 예언자였수.\"\n\n\"예언자가 뭐냐?\" 웰러 씨가 아들을 매섭게 바라보며 물었다.\n\n\"앞으로 무슨 일이 일어날지 말해 주는 사람이지요.\" 샘이 대답했다.\n\n\"그 사람을 알았더라면 좋았을걸, 새미.\" 웰러 씨가 말했다. \"아까 우리가 얘기하던 그 간 병에 대해 작게나마 빛을 비춰 줬을지도 모르잖느냐. 하기야, 그 사람이 죽었고 그 장사를 아무한테도 안 물려줬다면야, 그걸로 끝이지. 계속해라, 새미.\" 웰러 씨가 한숨을 쉬며 말했다.\n\n\"좋수,\" 샘이 말했다. \"아버진 주인 양반이 혼자 남으면 무슨 일이 닥칠지 그럴듯하게 예언만 늘어놨잖수. 그분을 돌볼 무슨 방도가 안 보이우?\"\n\n\"안 보인다, 새미.\" 웰러 씨가 골똘한 낯으로 말했다.\n\n\"전혀 방도가 없수?\" 샘이 물었다.\n\n\"방도가 없어.\" 웰러 씨가 말했다. \"다만—\" 그러더니 슬기의 빛이 그의 얼굴을 환히 밝혔으니, 그는 목소리를 속삭임으로 낮추고 입을 제 자식의 귀에 갖다 대며—\"다만, 간수들 몰래 접이침대에 넣어서 그 양반을 빼내거나, 초록 너울 두른 노파처럼 변장시키는 거라면 또 모르지, 새미.\"\n\n샘 웰러는 이 두 제안을 다 뜻밖일 만큼 경멸하며 받아들이고는, 다시 한번 자기 질문을 던졌다.\n\n\"아니다.\" 노신사가 말했다. \"그 양반이 널 거기 남게 하지 않는다면, 도무지 방도가 안 보인다. 막다른 길이야, 새미, 막다른 길.\"\n\n\"그럼, 좋수, 내가 말하리다.\" 샘이 말했다. \"스물다섯 파운드만 빌려 주쇼.\"\n\n\"그게 무슨 소용이냐?\" 웰러 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"신경 쓰지 마쇼.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"오 분쯤 지나서 아버지가 그 돈 내놓으라 할지도 모르고, 어쩌면 내가 안 갚겠다며 험하게 굴지도 모르지. 설마 돈 때문에 친아들을 잡아다 플리트로 보낼 생각이야 안 하시겠죠, 이 비정한 늙은 떠돌이 양반?\"\n\n샘의 이 대답에 아비와 아들은 완벽한 전신 신호 같은 끄덕임과 손짓을 주고받았고, 그러고 나서 웰러 영감은 돌계단에 주저앉아 얼굴이 시뻘게지도록 웃어 댔다.\n\n\"참 별난 우상이네!\" 샘이 시간을 허비하는 데 화가 나서 외쳤다. \"할 일이 산더민데 거기 주저앉아 얼굴을 대문 두드리개로 만들고 뭐 하는 거유. 돈은 어디 있수?\"\n\n\"장화 속에 있다, 새미, 장화 속에.\" 웰러 씨가 얼굴을 추스르며 대답했다. \"내 모자 좀 들어라, 새미.\"\n\n이 거추장스러운 물건을 벗어 던진 웰러 씨는 몸을 한쪽으로 홱 비틀더니, 능숙한 뒤틀림으로 오른손을 더없이 넉넉한 주머니에 집어넣었고, 한바탕 헐떡이며 용을 쓴 끝에 거대한 가죽끈으로 채운 큼지막한 팔절판 크기의 수첩을 끄집어냈다. 그는 이 장부에서 채찍 끈 두 가닥, 버클 서너 개, 곡식 견본이 든 작은 자루 하나를 차례로 꺼내고, 마지막으로 아주 더러운 지폐 한 뭉치를 꺼내, 거기서 필요한 액수를 골라 샘에게 건넸다.\n\n\"자, 새미,\" 채찍 끈과 버클과 견본들을 도로 다 넣고 수첩을 다시 그 주머니 밑바닥에 넣고서 노신사가 말했다. \"자, 새미, 여기 내가 아는 신사 양반이 하나 있는데, 나머지 일은 그분이 단숨에 처리해 줄 게다—법률 나부랭이지, 새미, 개구리처럼 온몸에 뇌수가 흩어져 손가락 끄트머리까지 닿아 있는 양반이야. 대법관님의 친구인데, 새미, 자기가 뭘 원하는지 그분한테 말만 하면, 그게 다라면 널 평생 가둬 둘 수도 있는 양반이다.\"\n\n\"이봐요,\" 샘이 말했다. \"그런 짓은 안 돼요.\"\n\n\"무슨 짓 말이냐?\" 웰러 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"왜, 그런 위헌적인 방식으로 하는 거 말이우.\" 샘이 받아쳤다. \"인신 보호 영장이란 건 영구 운동 다음으로 여태 만들어진 것 중에 가장 복된 물건이오. 그 얘긴 신문에서 아주 자주 읽었수.\"\n\n\"그래, 그게 이 일이랑 무슨 상관이냐?\" 웰러 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"바로 이거지요.\" 샘이 말했다. \"내가 그 발명품을 애용해서, 그 방식으로 들어가겠다는 거요. 대법관님께 귓속말 따위는 안 해요—그런 생각은 마음에 안 드오. 도로 나오는 거에 관해선, 그게 영 안전하지 않을지도 모르니까.\"\n\n이 점에서 아들의 뜻을 따라, 웰러 씨는 곧장 박식한 솔로몬 펠을 찾아가, 스물다섯 파운드의 금액과 소송 비용에 대해 당장 영장을 발부하고 싶다는 바람을 알렸으니, 그것은 새뮤얼 웰러라는 자의 신병에 대해 지체 없이 집행되어야 하며, 그로 인해 발생하는 비용은 솔로몬 펠에게 선금으로 지불하겠다고 했다.\n\n그 사무 변호사는 더없이 기뻐했으니, 곤경에 빠진 그 역마차 임대업자에게 즉시 면책 처분이 내려졌기 때문이었다. 그는 샘이 주인에게 보이는 애착을 크게 칭찬하며, 그것이 자기가 벗인 대법관에게 품었던 헌신의 감정을 절절히 떠올리게 한다고 단언하고는, 곧장 웰러 영감을 템플로 데려가 채무 진술서에 선서하게 했으니, 그 진술서는 사환 아이가 푸른 가방의 도움을 받아 그 자리에서 작성한 것이었다.\n\n그동안 샘은 벨 소비지의 웰러 씨의 자제분이라며 그 면책된 신사와 그 벗들에게 정식으로 소개되어 각별한 대접을 받았고, 이 일을 기념해 함께 한잔하자는 권유를 받았으니—샘은 결코 그 권유를 마다하지 않았다.\n\n이런 부류 신사들의 유흥은 으레 진중하고 조용한 성격을 띠지만, 이번 경우는 유달리 흥겨운 자리였던지라 그들도 그에 걸맞게 마음을 풀어놓았다. 그날 그토록 탁월한 솜씨를 발휘한 수석 위원과 솔로몬 펠 씨를 위해 다소 떠들썩하게 건배를 든 뒤, 푸른 숄을 두른 얼룩덜룩한 얼굴의 신사가 누군가 노래 한 곡 불러야 한다고 제안했다. 노래가 그토록 듣고 싶다면 그 얼룩덜룩한 얼굴의 신사가 직접 부르는 것이 당연한 제안이었으나, 그 얼룩덜룩한 얼굴의 신사는 완강히, 그것도 다소 무례하게 그러기를 거절했다. 그러자 이런 경우에 흔히 그렇듯 다소 험악한 입씨름이 벌어졌다.\n\n\"여러분,\" 그 역마차 임대업자가 말했다. \"이 즐거운 자리의 화기를 깨느니, 차라리 새뮤얼 웰러 씨가 우리 좌중을 위해 한 곡 들려주시면 어떻겠소.\"\n\n\"참말로요, 여러분,\" 샘이 말했다. \"저는 악기 없이 노래하는 데는 그리 익숙지 않습니다만, 조용히 살 수만 있다면 뭐든 하지요, 등대지기 자리를 맡으면서 그 사내가 한 말마따나 말입니다.\"\n\n이런 서곡과 함께 새뮤얼 웰러 씨가 곧장 다음과 같은 거칠고도 아름다운 전설을 우렁차게 뽑아냈으니, 그것이 널리 알려지지 않았으리라는 생각에 우리는 외람되이 그것을 옮겨 적는 바이다. 둘째 행과 넷째 행 끝의 단음절에 특히 주목해 주시기를 청하노니, 그것은 부르는 이가 그 지점에서 숨을 고르게 해 줄 뿐 아니라 운율에도 크게 보탬이 되는 까닭이다.\n로맨스\n\n# I\n\n1\n대담한 터핀이 한번은 하운즐로 황야에서, 그의 대담한 암말 베스에 올라타—니. 거기서 주교의 마차가 길을 따라 오는 걸 봤다—니. 그래서 그는 말다리 곁으로 바싹 달려가, 제 머리를 안으로 쑥 들이미니. 주교가 말하길, \"달걀이 달걀인 게 분명하듯, 이자가 바로 그 대담한 터핀이로다!\"\n\n후렴\n그리고 주교가 말하길, \"달걀이 달걀인 게 분명하듯, 이자가 바로 그 대담한 터핀이로다!\"\n\n# II\n\n2\n터핀이 말하길, \"네놈 한 말 도로 처먹게 해 주마, 납 총알 양념을 곁들여—서.\" 그러고 권총을 그자 입에 들이대고, 그자 목구멍에 대고 쏘아 댔—네. 마부는 그 일이 못마땅하여, 냅다 줄행랑을 놓았다만, 딕이 그자 골통에 총알 두 방을 박아 넣고, 그자를 멈추도록 설득해 냈—네.\n\n후렴 (비꼬듯이)\n\n하지만 딕이 그자 골통에 총알 두 방을 박아 넣고, 그자를 멈추도록 설득해 냈네.\n\n\"내 주장컨대 그 노래는 성직자를 빗댄 거요.\" 얼룩덜룩한 얼굴의 신사가 여기서 노래를 가로막으며 말했다. \"그 마부 이름을 대시오.\"\n\n\"아무도 몰랐다오.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"주머니에 명함을 안 넣고 있었거든요.\"\n\n\"나는 정치 끌어들이는 데 반대요.\" 얼룩덜룩한 얼굴의 신사가 말했다. \"내 단언컨대, 지금 이 자리에서 그 노래는 정치적이오. 그리고 그와 매한가지로, 사실도 아니오. 내 말하건대 그 마부는 도망치지 않았소. 그자는 꿩처럼 용맹하게—용맹하게 죽었소. 그 반대로 말하는 건 한마디도 못 듣겠소.\"\n\n얼룩덜룩한 얼굴의 신사가 워낙 기운차고 단호하게 말하는 데다 좌중의 의견이 그 문제를 두고 갈린 듯하여, 새 입씨름이 벌어질 판이었는데, 그때 웰러 씨와 펠 씨가 더없이 때맞춰 도착했다.\n\n\"다 됐다, 새미.\" 웰러 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"집행관이 네 시에 올 겁니다.\" 펠 씨가 말했다. \"설마 그때까지 도망치진 않겠지요, 응? 하! 하!\"\n\n\"어쩌면 그 전에 우리 무정한 아버지가 마음을 누그러뜨릴지도 모르죠.\" 샘이 활짝 웃으며 대답했다.\n\"천만에.\" 웰러 영감이 말했다.\n\n\"좀 그래 줘 봐요.\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n\"무슨 일이 있어도 안 된다.\" 그 가차 없는 채권자가 대답했다.\n\n\"한 달에 육 펜스씩 어음으로 갚으리다.\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n\"그딴 건 안 받는다.\" 웰러 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"하, 하, 하! 좋습니다, 좋아요.\" 자기 비용 청구서를 작성하던 솔로몬 펠 씨가 말했다. \"참으로 재미난 일화로군요! 벤저민, 이거 베껴 두게.\" 그러고는 펠 씨가 다시 미소를 지으며 웰러 씨에게 그 액수를 일러 주었다.\n\n\"고맙습니다, 고맙습니다.\" 웰러 씨가 수첩에서 꺼내 주는 기름진 지폐를 또 한 장 받아 들며 그 전문직 신사가 말했다. \"삼 파운드 십에 일 파운드 십이면 오 파운드로군요. 대단히 고맙습니다, 웰러 씨. 자제분은 더없이 갸륵한 청년이십니다, 정말 그렇습니다, 선생. 젊은이의 성품 가운데 참으로 보기 좋은 자질이지요, 정말 그렇습니다.\" 펠 씨가 돈을 단추 채워 넣으며 빙긋 좌중을 둘러보며 덧붙였다.\n\n\"이거 참 별난 노릇 아닌가!\" 웰러 영감이 빙긋 웃으며 말했다. \"영락없는 천재(天才) 아들이로구먼!\"\n\n\"탕자(蕩子)—탕자 아들이겠지요, 선생.\" 펠 씨가 부드럽게 일러 주었다.\n\n\"신경 쓰지 마쇼, 선생.\" 웰러 씨가 점잖게 말했다. \"내 사정은 내가 다 압니다, 선생. 모를 때면 내가 선생한테 물어보지요, 선생.\"\n\n집행관이 도착할 무렵 샘은 어찌나 인기를 끌었던지, 모여든 신사들이 다 함께 그를 감옥까지 바래다주기로 작정했다. 그리하여 그들은 출발했으니, 원고와 피고가 팔짱을 끼고 걷고, 집행관이 앞장서고, 건장한 마부 여덟이 뒤를 따랐다. 서전트 인 커피점에서 일행 전체가 멈춰 기운을 차렸고, 법적 절차가 마무리되자 행렬은 다시 움직였다.\n\n플리트 거리에서는 옆구리에 선 여덟 신사가 끝까지 네 명씩 나란히 걷겠다고 고집을 부리는 익살 탓에 자그마한 소동이 빚어졌다. 또 얼룩덜룩한 얼굴의 신사는 짐꾼과 한판 붙겠다고 해서 뒤에 남겨 둘 수밖에 없었으니, 그의 벗들이 돌아오는 길에 그를 데려가기로 합의했다. 가는 길에 일어난 일이라곤 이런 자질구레한 사건들뿐이었다. 그들이 플리트 정문에 다다르자, 그 기마행렬은 원고를 따라 박자를 맞춰 피고를 위해 우렁찬 만세 삼창을 외쳤고, 모두 두루 악수를 나눈 뒤 그를 두고 떠났다.\n\n샘은 정식으로 간수의 보호 아래 넘겨졌으니, 로커는 몹시 놀랐고, 무뚝뚝한 네디조차 눈에 띄게 감동한 가운데, 곧장 감옥 안으로 들어가 주인의 방으로 곧장 걸어가 문을 두드렸다.\n\n\"들어오게.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n샘이 모습을 드러내며 모자를 벗고 미소를 지었다.\n\n\"아, 샘, 이 착한 친구야!\" 보잘것없는 벗을 다시 보게 되어 분명 기뻐하며 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"어제 내가 한 말로 자네 마음을 상하게 할 생각은 없었네, 충직한 친구. 모자를 내려놓게, 샘. 내 뜻을 좀 더 자세히 설명해 주지.\"\n\n\"이따 하면 안 되겠습니까요, 나리?\" 샘이 물었다.\n\n\"되고말고.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"한데 왜 지금은 안 되나?\"\n\n\"지금은 좀 안 하는 게 좋겠습니다요, 나리.\" 샘이 응수했다.\n\n\"어째서?\" 픽윅 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"그게—\" 샘이 머뭇거리며 말했다.\n\n\"무엇 때문에 그러나?\" 부하의 태도에 놀란 픽윅 씨가 물었다. \"터놓고 말해 보게, 샘.\"\n\n\"그게요,\" 샘이 응수했다. \"처리하고 싶은 자그마한 볼일이 좀 있어서요.\"\n\n\"무슨 볼일인가?\" 샘의 당황한 태도에 의아해하며 픽윅 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"별 대단한 건 아닙니다요, 나리.\" 샘이 대답했다.\n\n\"오, 별 대단한 게 아니라면,\" 픽윅 씨가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"먼저 나하고 얘기해도 되겠군.\"\n\n\"제 생각엔 그 일을 곧장 살펴보는 게 낫겠습니다요.\" 여전히 머뭇거리며 샘이 말했다.\n\n픽윅 씨는 어이없다는 표정을 지었으나 아무 말도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"실은—\" 샘이 말하다 뚝 멈췄다.\n\n\"이런!\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"터놓고 말해 보게, 샘.\"\n\n\"그게, 실은요,\" 샘이 필사적으로 용을 쓰며 말했다. \"다른 일을 하기 전에 제 잠자리부터 먼저 살펴보는 게 낫겠습니다요.\"\n\n\"자네 잠자리라니!\" 픽윅 씨가 깜짝 놀라 외쳤다.\n\n\"네, 제 잠자리요, 나리.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"저는 죄수입니다요. 바로 오늘 오후에 빚 때문에 잡혀 왔습죠.\"\n\n\"자네가 빚 때문에 잡혀 왔다고!\" 픽윅 씨가 의자에 털썩 주저앉으며 외쳤다.\n\n\"네, 빚 때문에요, 나리.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"그리고 저를 집어넣은 사람은 나리께서 직접 나가시기 전엔 절대 저를 안 내보내 줄 겁니다요.\"\n\n\"맙소사, 어찌 이런 일이!\" 픽윅 씨가 부르짖었다. \"그게 무슨 소린가?\"\n\n\"제가 말씀드린 그대롭니다요, 나리.\" 샘이 응수했다. \"앞으로 사십 년이 걸린대도 저는 죄수로 남을 겁니다요. 그리고 저는 그게 무척 기쁩니다요. 설령 그게 뉴게이트였대도 매한가지였을 겁니다요. 자, 이제 비밀은 다 드러났고, 제기랄, 그걸로 끝입죠!\"\n\n이 말들을 무척 힘주어, 격렬하게 거듭 내뱉으며, 샘 웰러는 좀처럼 보기 드물게 흥분한 상태로 모자를 땅바닥에 내동댕이쳤다. 그러고는 팔짱을 끼고 주인의 얼굴을 굳세고도 흔들림 없이 똑바로 바라보았다.\n\n픽윅 씨는 샘이 보여 준 애정의 따뜻함에 너무도 깊이 감동한 나머지, 빚쟁이 감옥에 무기한으로 제 발로 들어가 버린 그 성급한 처사에 대해 노여움이나 불쾌함을 조금도 드러낼 수가 없었다. 그가 끝까지 설명을 요구한 단 한 가지는 샘을 붙잡아 둔 채권자의 이름이었으나, 웰러 씨는 그만큼이나 끈질기게 그것을 밝히지 않았다.\n\n\"그래 봐야 아무 소용 없습니다, 나리.\" 샘은 거듭거듭 말했다. \"그 작자는 악의에 차고, 심보 고약하고, 세속적이고, 앙심 많고, 보복심 강한 놈이라, 마음이 어찌나 모진지 도무지 누그러지질 않습니다. 그 덕망 있는 목사 양반이 수종증 걸린 노신사를 두고 한 말마따나지요. 그 양반은 이렇게 말했더랬습니다. 곰곰이 생각해 보니 그 재산으로 예배당을 짓느니 차라리 마누라한테 물려주는 편이 낫겠다고 말입니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 생각해 보게, 샘.\" 픽윅 씨가 타일렀다. \"그 액수가 워낙 적어서 아주 쉽게 갚을 수 있어. 게다가 자네가 나와 함께 있어야 한다고 내가 마음을 정한 이상, 자네가 담장 바깥으로 나갈 수 있다면 훨씬 더 쓸모가 있으리라는 걸 자네도 떠올려야 하지 않겠나.\"\n\n\"대단히 감사합니다요, 나리.\" 웰러 씨가 진지하게 대답했다. \"하지만 전 그러지 않는 편이 낫겠습니다.\"\n\n\"무얼 그러지 않는 편이 낫다는 건가, 샘?\"\n\n\"아, 저 무자비한 원수 놈한테 부탁이나 하려고 제 자신을 낮추는 짓은 안 하는 게 낫겠다는 겁니다요.\"\n\n\"하지만 그자더러 자기 돈을 받아 가라고 하는 건 부탁이 아니잖나, 샘.\" 픽윅 씨가 논리를 폈다.\n\n\"죄송합니다만, 나리.\" 샘이 받아쳤다. \"그걸 갚아 준다는 게 그자한테는 엄청나게 큰 호의가 될 텐데, 그자는 그런 호의를 받을 자격이 없습니다. 바로 그게 문제입니다, 나리.\"\n\n이 대목에서 픽윅 씨가 다소 짜증스러운 기색으로 코를 문지르자, 웰러 씨는 화제를 바꾸는 편이 현명하겠다고 생각했다.\n\n\"전 원칙에 따라 제 결심을 세운 겁니다, 나리.\" 샘이 말했다. \"나리께서도 같은 근거에서 결심을 세우신 거고요. 이걸 보니 원칙에 따라 자살한 사내가 생각나는군요. 물론 나리께서도 들어 보셨겠지요.\" 웰러 씨는 이 대목에 이르자 말을 멈추고, 눈꼬리로 주인을 슬쩍 익살스러운 눈길을 던졌다.\n\n\"그 일에는 '물론'이랄 게 없네, 샘.\" 샘의 고집이 안겨 준 불편함에도 불구하고 픽윅 씨는 차츰 미소를 띠며 말했다. \"그 양반의 명성은 내 귀에 닿은 적이 한 번도 없으니까.\"\n\n\"아니, 그럴 리가요, 나리!\" 웰러 씨가 외쳤다. \"정말 놀랍습니다, 나리. 그 양반은 관청 서기였는데요, 나리.\"\n\n\"그랬나?\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"네, 그랬습니다, 나리.\" 웰러 씨가 받았다. \"게다가 아주 유쾌한 양반이었지요. 깔끔하고 단정한 부류라, 비 오는 날이면 발을 조그만 고무 양동이에 집어넣고, 토끼 가죽 말고는 가슴에 품는 친구라곤 없는 사람이었습니다. 원칙에 따라 돈을 모았고, 원칙에 따라 매일 깨끗한 셔츠를 입었고, 원칙에 따라 친척이라곤 누구하고도 말을 섞지 않았습니다. 혹시라도 자기한테 돈을 꾸려 들까 봐서요. 한마디로 보기 드물게 사람 좋은 위인이었지요. 원칙에 따라 보름에 한 번 머리를 깎았고, 절약 원칙에 따라 옷을 계약해서 맞췄습니다. 일 년에 양복 세 벌, 헌 것은 돌려보내는 식으로요. 워낙 규칙적인 양반이라 매일 같은 데서 식사를 했는데, 거기서는 고기를 잘라 주는 데 일 실링 구 펜스를 받았습니다. 그런데 그 양반이 잘라 먹는 양이 일 실링 구 펜스 값을 톡톡히 했지요. 주인장이 얼굴에 눈물을 줄줄 흘리며 자주 하던 말이었습니다. 겨울철에 불을 쑤석거리는 버릇은 또 어떻고요. 그건 하루에 사 펜스 반의 순손실이었습니다. 그 짓을 지켜보는 짜증스러움은 말할 것도 없고요. 게다가 어찌나 거드름을 피우던지! '다음 손님 차례 끝나면 이리 주게.' 하고 들어올 때마다 외쳐 댔지요. '타임스 좀 챙겨 주게, 토머스. 모닝 헤럴드는 손에서 놓이면 내가 보겠네. 크로니클 예약하는 거 잊지 말고, 타이저도 가져다주게나.' 그러고는 시계에 눈을 못 박은 채 앉아 있다가, 시간 십오 초 전이면 후닥닥 뛰쳐나가서 석간을 들고 들어오는 소년을 길목에서 가로챘습니다. 그러고는 어찌나 열중해서, 또 어찌나 끈질기게 읽어 대던지 다른 손님들은 절망과 광기의 막바지로 내몰릴 지경이었지요. 특히 성마른 노신사 하나가 그랬는데, 종업원은 그럴 때마다 그 양반을 눈여겨봐야 했습니다. 혹시라도 고기 써는 칼로 무슨 무모한 짓을 저지를까 봐서요. 어쨌든, 나리, 그 양반은 거기 죽치고 앉아 제일 좋은 자리를 세 시간씩 차지하고는, 식사 후엔 잠 말고 아무것도 더 들지 않았습니다. 그러고 나서 몇 블록 떨어진 커피점으로 가서 커피 작은 주전자 하나에 크럼펫 네 개를 먹고는, 켄싱턴까지 걸어가 잠자리에 들었지요. 그러던 어느 밤 그 양반이 몹시 앓아누웠습니다. 의사를 불렀더니 의사가 초록색 마차를 타고 왔는데, 로빈슨 크루소 식 발판이 달린 마차였습니다. 내릴 때 펼쳐 놓고 탈 때 다시 접어 올리는 식이라, 마부가 내려올 필요가 없었지요. 그래야 마부가 걸친 게 제복 윗도리뿐이고 거기 어울리는 바지는 없다는 걸 들켜서 대중이 속는 일이 없을 테니까요. '뭐가 문젭니까?' 하고 의사가 물었습니다. '몹시 아픕니다.' 하고 환자가 답했지요. '뭘 드셨습니까?' 하고 의사가 물었습니다. '구운 송아지 고기요.' 하고 환자가 답했지요. '마지막으로 삼킨 게 뭡니까?' 하고 의사가 물었습니다. '크럼펫이요.' 하고 환자가 답했지요. '바로 그겁니다!' 하고 의사가 말했습니다. '당장 알약 한 상자를 보내 드릴 테니, 다시는 그걸 들지 마십시오.' 하고 말했지요. '뭘 더 안 든다고요?' 하고 환자가 물었습니다. '알약을요?' '아니요, 크럼펫 말입니다.' 하고 의사가 답했지요. '왜요?' 하고 환자가 침대에서 벌떡 일어나며 물었습니다. '난 십오 년 동안 매일 밤 원칙에 따라 크럼펫을 네 개씩 먹었단 말입니다.' '그렇다면, 원칙에 따라 끊으시는 게 낫겠군요.' 하고 의사가 말했습니다. '크럼펫은 몸에 좋지 않습니다, 선생.' 하고 의사가 아주 사납게 말했지요. '하지만 워낙 싸잖습니까.' 하고 환자가 조금 누그러지며 말했습니다. '그 값에 그렇게 든든하기도 하고요.' '그건 어떤 값에도 비싼 겁니다. 거저 먹으래도 비싸지요.' 하고 의사가 말했습니다. '하룻밤에 크럼펫 네 개면,' 하고 그가 말했습니다. '여섯 달이면 당신 끝장입니다!' 환자는 의사의 얼굴을 정면으로 바라보며 한참 동안 머릿속으로 곱씹어 보더니, 마침내 이렇게 말했습니다. '그게 정말 확실합니까, 선생?' '내 직업적 명예를 걸지요.' 하고 의사가 답했습니다. '한자리에서 크럼펫을 몇 개나 먹으면 단번에 죽을 것 같습니까?' 하고 환자가 물었습니다. '모르겠습니다.' 하고 의사가 답했지요. '반 크라운어치면 되겠습니까?' 하고 환자가 물었습니다. '될 것 같군요.' 하고 의사가 답했습니다. '삼 실링어치면 확실히 되겠지요?' 하고 환자가 물었습니다. '물론이지요.' 하고 의사가 답했습니다. '아주 좋습니다.' 하고 환자가 말했지요. '안녕히 가십시오.' 이튿날 아침 그 양반은 일어나서 불을 지피게 하고, 삼 실링어치 크럼펫을 들이라고 시켜서는, 죄다 구워, 죄다 먹어 치우고, 제 머리통을 날려 버렸습니다.\"\n\n\"그자는 왜 그런 짓을 한 건가?\" 픽윅 씨가 불쑥 물었다. 이야기의 비극적 결말에 적잖이 놀란 탓이었다.\n\n\"왜 그랬냐고요, 나리?\" 샘이 되받았다. \"아, 크럼펫이 몸에 좋다는 자기 위대한 원칙을 떠받치려고, 그리고 자기는 누구 때문에도 제 방식을 굽히지 않는다는 걸 보여 주려고 그런 겁니다요!\"\n\n이런 식으로 화제를 요리조리 돌리고 바꾸어 가며, 웰러 씨는 플리트에 거처를 잡은 그날 밤 주인의 추궁을 받아넘겼다. 온건한 타이름이 죄다 소용없음을 깨달은 픽윅 씨는 마침내 마지못해 동의하여, 샘이 대머리 구두장이에게서 주 단위로 셋방을 빌리는 것을 허락했다. 그 구두장이는 위층 회랑 가운데 한 곳에 딸린 비좁은 골방을 세 들어 살고 있었다. 이 초라한 방으로 웰러 씨는 로커 씨에게서 빌린 매트리스와 침구를 옮겨 왔고, 밤이 되어 그 위에 누울 무렵에는 마치 감옥에서 자라나 온 집안이 삼대에 걸쳐 그곳에서 풀처럼 자라난 듯이 편안한 기분이 되었다.\n\n\"영감님은 잠자리에 든 다음에도 늘 담배를 피웁니까, 영감?\" 둘 다 밤을 맞아 자리에 든 뒤, 웰러 씨가 집주인에게 물었다.\n\n\"그렇다네, 젊은 친구.\" 구두장이가 답했다.\n\n\"저 송판 식탁 밑에다 잠자리를 까는 이유를 좀 여쭤봐도 되겠습니까?\" 샘이 물었다.\n\n\"여기 오기 전엔 늘 사주식 침대를 썼는데, 식탁 다리가 꼭 그만큼 구실을 해 주거든.\" 구두장이가 답했다.\n\n\"영감님은 별난 인물(character)이시군요.\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n\"그런 건 내게 딸려 있질 않아.\" 구두장이가 고개를 저으며 받았다. \"좋은 추천장(character)을 만나고 싶거든, 이 직업소개소에서 자네 마음에 맞는 걸 찾긴 좀 어려울 거야.\"\n* character: '인물'과 '신원 보증서·추천장' 두 뜻을 함께 쓴 말장난.\n\n위의 짧은 대화는 웰러 씨가 방 한쪽 끝에 깔린 매트리스에 길게 뻗고, 구두장이는 반대편 끝의 제 자리에 누운 채로 오간 것이었다. 방은 골풀 양초의 불빛과, 식탁 아래에서 시뻘건 숯덩이처럼 이글거리는 구두장이의 담뱃대로 밝혀져 있었다. 짧긴 했어도 그 대화는 웰러 씨로 하여금 집주인에게 강하게 호감을 품게 했고, 그는 팔꿈치를 짚고 몸을 일으켜, 여태까지 시간도 마음도 없어 못 했던 만큼 좀 더 찬찬히 그의 모습을 살펴보았다.\n\n그는 누르께한 얼굴을 한 사내였다. 구두장이란 다 그렇다. 그리고 빳빳한 수염이 더부룩했다. 구두장이란 다 그렇다. 그의 얼굴은 묘하고 사람 좋고 이목구비가 비뚜름한 작품이었는데, 한때는 무척 즐거운 표정을 띠었음에 틀림없는 두 눈으로 장식되어 있었다. 그 눈은 아직도 반짝였기 때문이다. 사내는 햇수로 예순이었고, 수감 햇수로는 하늘만이 알 만큼 늙어 있었으니, 그가 즐거움이나 만족에 가까운 표정을 조금이라도 띠고 있다는 건 어지간히 별난 일이었다. 그는 키가 작은 사내였고, 침대에 누워 몸을 반으로 접고 있으니 다리를 뺀 키만큼이나 짧아 보였다. 입에는 커다란 빨간 담뱃대를 물고서, 부러울 만큼 평온한 상태로 담배를 피우며 골풀 양초를 바라보고 있었다.\n\n\"여기 오래 계셨습니까?\" 한동안 이어진 침묵을 깨고 샘이 물었다.\n\n\"십이 년일세.\" 구두장이가 담뱃대 끝을 깨물며 답했다.\n\n\"법정모독으로요?\" 샘이 물었다.\n\n구두장이가 고개를 끄덕였다.\n\n\"그럼 말입니다.\" 샘이 다소 엄한 어조로 말했다. \"대체 뭐 하러 고집을 부려서, 이 부풀려 놓은 가축우리에서 귀한 인생을 허비하고 계십니까? 왜 항복하고 대법관 양반한테 '법정을 모독해서 정말 죄송하고, 다신 안 그러겠습니다.' 하고 말하지 않으시는 겁니까?\"\n\n구두장이는 담뱃대를 입꼬리로 옮겨 물고 빙긋 웃더니, 다시 본래 자리로 가져왔다. 그러나 아무 말도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"왜 안 그러십니까?\" 샘이 끈질기게 다그쳐 물었다.\n\n\"아.\" 구두장이가 말했다. \"자네는 이런 일들을 잘 모르는군. 대체 뭐가 날 망쳤다고 생각하나?\"\n\n\"글쎄요.\" 샘이 골풀 양초의 심지를 다듬으며 말했다. \"처음엔 빚을 진 데서 비롯됐겠지요, 안 그렇습니까?\"\n\n\"한 푼도 빚진 적 없네.\" 구두장이가 말했다. \"다시 맞혀 보게.\"\n\n\"그럼, 아마.\" 샘이 말했다. \"집을 사셨겠지요. 그건 미쳐 간다는 말의 점잖은 영어 표현이지요. 아니면 집 짓는 일에 빠지셨거나요. 그건 회복 불능이라는 뜻의 의학 용어고요.\"\n\n구두장이는 고개를 저으며 말했다. \"다시 맞혀 보게.\"\n\n\"설마 소송을 거신 건 아니겠지요?\" 샘이 미심쩍게 물었다.\n\n\"평생 한 번도.\" 구두장이가 답했다. \"사실인즉, 난 유산을 물려받는 바람에 망했네.\"\n\n\"이런, 이런.\" 샘이 말했다. \"그건 안 통하는 소립니다. 부자 원수 놈이 있어서 저를 그런 식으로 망쳐 주겠다면, 저는 기꺼이 망해 드리겠습니다요.\"\n\n\"오, 자네가 안 믿는 모양이군.\" 구두장이가 조용히 담뱃대를 빨며 말했다. \"나라도 안 믿겠지. 하지만 그래도 사실이라네.\"\n\n\"어떻게 된 겁니까?\" 구두장이가 던진 눈빛에 벌써 절반쯤 그 말을 믿게 된 샘이 물었다.\n\n\"바로 이렇다네.\" 구두장이가 답했다. \"내가 시골에서 일해 주던 노신사가 있었는데, 그 양반의 미천한 친척과 내가 결혼했지. 그 사람은 죽었네. 하느님이 보살피시기를, 또 그렇게 해 주신 데 감사하기를! 어쨌든 그 노신사가 발작을 일으켜 가 버렸네.\"\n\n\"어디로요?\" 그날 하루의 숱한 일들로 졸음이 밀려오던 샘이 물었다.\n\n\"그 양반이 어디로 갔는지 낸들 어찌 아나?\" 담뱃대를 한껏 음미하며 코맹맹이 소리로 구두장이가 말했다. \"죽어서 가 버렸다는 말일세.\"\n\n\"아, 그렇군요.\" 샘이 말했다. \"그래서요?\"\n\n\"그래서.\" 구두장이가 말했다. \"그 양반이 오천 파운드를 남겼네.\"\n\n\"그렇게 하시다니 어지간히 점잖은 양반이군요.\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n\"그중 천 파운드를.\" 구두장이가 이었다. \"내게 남겼네. 내가 자기 친척과 결혼했으니까 말이야.\"\n\n\"아주 좋습니다.\" 샘이 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"그런데 그 양반한테는 조카딸이며 조카아들이 잔뜩 있어서, 늘 재산을 두고 저희끼리 다투고 싸워 댔거든. 그래서 그 양반은 날 유언 집행인으로 삼고, 나머지를 신탁으로 내게 맡겨, 유언에 정해진 대로 그자들 사이에 나눠 주게 했지.\"\n\n\"신탁으로 남긴다는 게 무슨 말입니까?\" 샘이 살짝 정신이 들며 물었다. \"현찰이 아니면, 그게 무슨 소용입니까?\"\n\n\"법률 용어일 뿐일세.\" 구두장이가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇게는 생각 안 됩니다.\" 샘이 고개를 저으며 말했다. \"그 가게엔 믿을 만한 게 별로 없거든요. 어쨌든 계속하시지요.\"\n\n* 신탁(trust): 영어 trust에는 '신탁'과 '믿음'의 뜻이 함께 있어, 샘이 말장난을 한 것.\n\"그래서.\" 구두장이가 말했다. \"내가 유언 검인을 받으려 하니까, 그 돈을 전부 차지하지 못해 잔뜩 실망한 조카딸이며 조카아들이 거기에 이의신청을 걸었네.\"\n\n\"그게 뭡니까?\" 샘이 물었다.\n\n\"법적 절차인데, 한마디로 안 된다는 소리지.\" 구두장이가 답했다.\n\n\"알겠습니다.\" 샘이 말했다. \"인신보호영장의 처남쯤 되는 거군요. 계속하시지요.\"\n\n\"그런데.\" 구두장이가 이었다. \"저희끼리도 의견이 안 맞아서 결국 유언에 맞설 소송을 꾸리지 못하자, 그자들은 이의신청을 거둬들였고, 난 유산을 죄다 나눠 줬네. 그걸 막 끝내자마자 조카아들 하나가 유언을 무효로 돌리려고 소송을 걸었지. 몇 달 뒤에 그 사건이 폴 성당 묘지 근처 어디 뒷방에서 귀먹은 노신사 앞에 올라갔네. 그러고는 변호사 넷이 하루씩 차례로 그 양반을 정성껏 괴롭히고 나니, 그 양반이 일이 주를 들여 곰곰이 따져 보고 여섯 권짜리 증거를 읽어 본 다음, 판결을 내렸지. 유언자가 머리가 온전치 못했으니, 내가 돈을 죄다 도로 물어내고 비용도 다 물어야 한다고 말이야. 난 항소했네. 사건은 아주 졸린 신사 서넛 앞에 올라갔는데, 그자들은 다른 법정에서 그걸 이미 다 들은 자들이었지. 거긴 일거리 없는 변호사들 자리거든. 유일한 차이라면 거기선 그자들을 박사라고 부르고 다른 데선 위원이라고 부른다는 것뿐이야. 무슨 말인지 알겠나. 어쨌든 그자들은 아주 충실하게 아래 노신사의 판결을 확정했네. 그런 다음 우린 형평법원으로 넘어갔고, 지금도 거기 있고, 난 영영 거기 있을 걸세. 변호사들은 내 천 파운드를 벌써 오래전에 다 가져갔지. 게다가 그자들이 재산이라고 부르는 것과 비용을 다 합쳐서, 난 만 파운드 빚으로 여기 들어와 있고, 죽을 때까지 여기서 구두나 꿰매고 있을 걸세. 어떤 신사 양반들이 이 일을 의회에 올리겠다고 말한 적도 있고, 정말 그랬을지도 몰라. 다만 그자들은 내게 올 시간이 없었고, 난 그자들한테 갈 힘이 없었고, 그자들은 내 긴 편지에 진절머리가 나서 그 일을 그만뒀지. 이게 하느님께 맹세코 사실이라네. 한마디도 숨기거나 부풀린 데 없이 말이야. 이곳 안팎의 쉰 명이 다 잘 알고 있는 일이지.\"\n\n구두장이는 제 이야기가 샘에게 어떤 영향을 주었는지 확인하려고 말을 멈췄으나, 그가 잠들어 버린 것을 알자 담뱃대의 재를 털어 내고, 한숨을 쉬고, 담뱃대를 내려놓고, 이불을 머리까지 끌어 올리고는, 자기도 잠이 들었다.\n\n이튿날 아침 픽윅 씨는 혼자 식탁에 앉아 아침을 먹고 있었는데(샘은 구두장이의 방에서 주인의 구두를 닦고 검은 각반을 솔질하느라 분주했다), 그때 문을 두드리는 소리가 났다. 픽윅 씨가 \"들어오시오!\" 하고 외치기도 전에, 그 뒤를 이어 한 무더기의 머리털과 무명 벨벳 모자가 나타났는데, 그 두 가지 복장은 어렵잖게 스맹글 씨의 개인 소유물임을 알아볼 수 있었다.\n\n\"안녕하시오?\" 그 훌륭한 양반이 스무 번 남짓 고개를 끄덕이며 이렇게 물었다. \"이봐요, 오늘 아침에 누구 올 사람이라도 있소? 사내 셋이, 지독히도 신사다운 작자들이, 아래층에서 당신을 찾으며 복도 층 문이란 문은 죄다 두드려 댔다오. 그 바람에 문을 열어 주느라 고생한 동료 수감자들한테 엄청나게 욕을 먹었지요.\"\n\n\"이런! 그 친구들도 어지간히 어리석군요.\" 픽윅 씨가 일어서며 말했다. \"그래요, 틀림없이 어제 좀 만날까 했던 친구들일 겁니다.\"\n\n\"당신 친구들이라고요!\" 스맹글이 픽윅 씨의 손을 덥석 잡으며 외쳤다. \"더 말할 것 없소. 빌어먹을, 이 순간부터 그들은 내 친구이기도 하고, 마이빈스의 친구이기도 하오. 마이빈스란 작자, 지독히도 유쾌하고 신사다운 녀석이지요, 안 그렇소?\" 스맹글이 자못 감격에 차서 말했다.\n\n\"제가 그분에 대해 아는 게 너무 적어서요.\" 픽윅 씨가 머뭇거리며 말했다. \"그러니 저는—\"\n\n\"알지요, 잘 알고말고요.\" 스맹글이 픽윅 씨의 어깨를 부여잡으며 말을 가로챘다. \"차차 더 잘 알게 될 거요. 그 친구한테 푹 빠질 거외다. 그 사람으로 말하자면, 선생.\" 스맹글이 엄숙한 얼굴로 말했다. \"드루리 레인 극장에서도 빛을 발할 만한 희극적 재능을 지녔지요.\"\n\n\"정말 그렇습니까?\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"아, 맹세코 그렇소!\" 스맹글이 답했다. \"외바퀴 손수레에 탄 고양이 네 마리 흉내를 내는 걸 한번 들어 보시오. 또렷이 구별되는 고양이 네 마리를, 명예를 걸고 장담하리다. 자, 그게 지독히도 영리한 솜씨 아니겠소! 빌어먹을, 그런 면모를 보면 그 친구를 좋아하지 않을 수가 없다오. 한 가지 흠이라면, 아까 내가 당신한테 말한 그 사소한 결점뿐이지요.\"\n\n이 대목에서 스맹글 씨가 은밀하고도 동정 어린 태도로 고개를 가로젓자, 픽윅 씨는 자기가 무어라 한마디 해야 할 처지임을 느끼고 \"아!\" 하고는 안절부절 문 쪽을 바라보았다.\n\n\"아!\" 스맹글 씨도 길게 한숨을 내쉬며 똑같이 받았다. \"그 사람으로 말하자면 더없이 유쾌한 동무라오, 선생. 어디서도 그보다 나은 동무는 못 봤소. 다만 그 한 가지 결점이 있을 뿐이지요. 만약 지금 이 순간 그의 할아버지 유령이 눈앞에 솟아오른다면, 그자는 팔 펜스짜리 인지를 붙인 어음에 배서나 좀 해 달라고 부탁할 위인이외다.\"\n\n\"이런!\" 픽윅 씨가 외쳤다.\n\n\"그렇소.\" 스맹글 씨가 덧붙였다. \"그리고 그 유령을 다시 불러낼 힘이 있다면, 지금부터 두 달 사흘 뒤에 어음을 갱신하려고 또 불러낼 자란 말이오!\"\n\n\"그것 참 놀라운 면모로군요.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"하지만 우리가 여기서 이야기하는 동안 제 친구들이 절 못 찾아 몹시 난처해하고 있을까 봐 걱정입니다.\"\n\n\"내가 길을 안내하리다.\" 스맹글이 문 쪽으로 향하며 말했다. \"그럼 또 봅시다. 그들이 여기 있는 동안은 방해하지 않으리다, 알겠소. 아 참, 그런데—\"\n\n스맹글은 이 마지막 세 마디를 내뱉다 말고 문득 멈춰 서더니, 방금 연 문을 다시 닫고는, 사뿐사뿐 픽윅 씨에게 되돌아와 발끝으로 바싹 다가서서 아주 부드러운 속삭임으로 말했다.\n\n\"다음 주 후반까지 반 크라운만 좀 융통해 주실 형편이 안 되겠소?\"\n\n픽윅 씨는 웃음을 참기 힘들었으나, 가까스로 진지한 표정을 지키며 동전을 꺼내어 스맹글 씨의 손바닥에 놓아 주었다. 그러자 그 신사는 깊은 비밀을 암시하는 양 연신 고개를 끄덕이고 눈을 찡긋거리며, 세 낯선 이를 찾으러 사라졌다가 이내 그들을 데리고 돌아왔다. 그리고 갚는 걸 잊지 않겠노라는 픽윅 씨에 대한 다짐으로 세 번 기침하고 세 번 고개를 끄덕인 뒤, 붙임성 있는 태도로 두루 악수를 나누고는 마침내 자리를 떴다.\n\"여보게들.\" 픽윅 씨가 터프먼 씨, 윈클 씨, 스노드그래스 씨와 차례로 악수하며 말했다. 문제의 세 방문객은 바로 그들이었다. \"이렇게 보니 정말 기쁘네.\"\n\n세 사람은 깊이 감격했다. 터프먼 씨는 애통하게 고개를 가로저었고, 스노드그래스 씨는 감정을 감추지 않고 손수건을 꺼냈으며, 윈클 씨는 창가로 물러나 소리 내어 코를 훌쩍였다.\n\n\"안녕하십니까, 신사 여러분.\" 마침 그때 구두와 각반을 들고 들어온 샘이 말했다. \"우울일랑 떨쳐 버리십시다. 학교 여선생이 죽었을 때 꼬마가 한 말마따나 말입니다. 학교(college)에 오신 걸 환영합니다, 신사 여러분.\"\n\n* college: 당시 채무자 감옥을 '칼리지(college)'라 익살스럽게 부른 데서 온 말.\n\n\"이 어리석은 친구가 말일세.\" 픽윅 씨가, 주인의 각반 단추를 채우려고 무릎을 꿇은 샘의 머리를 톡톡 두드리며 말했다. \"이 어리석은 친구가 나와 가까이 있겠다고 일부러 붙잡혀 들어왔다네.\"\n\n\"뭐라고요!\" 세 친구가 외쳤다.\n\n\"그렇습니다, 신사 여러분.\" 샘이 말했다. \"저는, 가만히 좀 계세요, 나리, 부탁드립니다, 저는 죄수입니다, 신사 여러분. 갇힌 몸이지요. 그 부인이 말한 것처럼요.\"\n\n\"죄수라고!\" 윈클 씨가 영문 모를 격렬함으로 외쳤다.\n\n\"이런, 나리!\" 샘이 고개를 들며 받았다. \"무슨 일이십니까, 나리?\"\n\n\"난 자네가, 샘, 그러기를 바랐는데, 아니, 아무것도 아닐세, 아무것도.\" 윈클 씨가 황급히 말했다.\n\n윈클 씨의 태도에 어딘가 너무도 불쑥스럽고 불안정한 데가 있어서, 픽윅 씨는 저도 모르게 설명을 구하듯 다른 두 친구를 바라보았다.\n\n\"저희도 모릅니다.\" 이 무언의 호소에 터프먼 씨가 소리 내어 답했다. \"그는 지난 이틀 동안 몹시 들떠 있었고, 전체적으로 평소와는 영 딴판입니다. 무슨 일이 있는 게 분명하다고 걱정했지만, 본인은 한사코 아니라고만 합니다.\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 아니에요.\" 픽윅 씨의 시선 아래 얼굴을 붉히며 윈클 씨가 말했다. \"정말 아무 일도 없습니다. 단언컨대 아무 일도 없어요, 선생님. 다만 제가 개인적인 용무로 잠시 런던을 떠나야 할 일이 있어서, 선생님께서 샘이 저와 동행하도록 허락해 주십사 부탁드리려 했던 겁니다.\"\n\n픽윅 씨는 아까보다 더 놀란 표정을 지었다.\n\n\"제 생각엔.\" 윈클 씨가 더듬거렸다. \"샘도 그러는 데 반대하지 않았을 듯합니다만, 물론 그가 여기 죄수로 잡혀 있으니 그건 불가능하지요. 그러니 저 혼자 가야겠습니다.\"\n\n윈클 씨가 이 말을 하는 동안, 픽윅 씨는 다소 놀라며 느꼈는데, 각반을 만지던 샘의 손가락이, 마치 적잖이 놀라거나 흠칫한 듯 떨리고 있었다. 샘도 윈클 씨가 말을 마치자 그를 올려다보았다. 그리고 두 사람이 주고받은 눈길은 순식간이었으나, 서로 뜻을 알아챈 듯했다.\n\n\"자네, 이 일에 대해 무얼 좀 아나, 샘?\" 픽윅 씨가 날카롭게 물었다.\n\n\"아니요, 모릅니다, 나리.\" 웰러 씨가 유난스러운 부지런함으로 단추를 채우기 시작하며 답했다.\n\n\"확실한가, 샘?\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"글쎄요, 나리.\" 웰러 씨가 받았다. \"지금 이 순간까지 그 일에 대해 한마디도 들은 적 없다는 것만큼은 확실합니다. 혹 제가 짐작하는 바가 있다 해도.\" 샘이 윈클 씨를 바라보며 덧붙였다. \"그게 뭔지 함부로 말할 권리는 없습니다. 틀린 짐작일까 봐서요.\"\n\n\"아무리 가까운 친구라 한들, 친구의 사사로운 일을 더 캐물을 권리는 내게 없네.\" 짧은 침묵 끝에 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"지금은 다만 이 말만 해 두겠네. 나로선 이게 도무지 이해가 안 가는군. 자, 이 이야기는 충분히 했으니 그만하지.\"\n\n이렇게 말하고 나서 픽윅 씨는 화제를 다른 데로 돌렸고, 윈클 씨도 차츰 마음이 편해 보였으나, 여전히 완전히 편하기에는 멀었다. 다들 나눌 이야기가 어찌나 많던지 오전이 순식간에 지나갔다. 그리고 세 시에 웰러 씨가 작은 식탁 위에 양고기 구운 다리와 거대한 고기 파이, 거기에 갖가지 채소 접시와 흑맥주 단지 몇 개를 차려 놓자—단지들은 의자며 소파 겸용 침대며 놓일 만한 데마다 놓였다—고기는 바로 옆 감옥 조리장에서 사다 손질한 것이고 파이도 거기서 만들어 구운 것이었음에도, 다들 식사를 제대로 즐길 마음이 생겼다.\n\n여기에 더해 아주 좋은 포도주 한두 병이 곁들여졌는데, 그것을 가지러 픽윅 씨는 닥터스 코먼스에 있는 혼 커피점으로 심부름꾼을 보냈다. 사실 한두 병이라기보다는 한 댓 병이라 하는 편이 더 정확하겠다. 그 술을 다 비우고 차까지 마실 무렵에는, 면회객들더러 물러가라는 종이 울리기 시작했으니까.\n\n그러나 윈클 씨의 행동이 아침에도 영문 모를 것이었다면, 자기 감정과 한 댓 병 가운데 제 몫의 영향 아래서 친구와 작별하려 할 즈음에는 완전히 섬뜩하고 비장해졌다. 그는 터프먼 씨와 스노드그래스 씨가 사라질 때까지 뒤에 남았다가, 픽윅 씨의 손을 열렬히 움켜쥐었는데, 그 얼굴 표정에는 깊고 거창한 결의가 더없이 응축된 우수의 정수와 무섭게 뒤섞여 있었다.\n\n\"안녕히 주무십시오, 선생님!\" 윈클 씨가 이를 악문 채 말했다.\n\n\"잘 가게, 여보게!\" 따뜻한 마음의 픽윅 씨가 젊은 친구의 손을 마주 쥐며 답했다.\n\n\"어이, 이제 그만!\" 회랑에서 터프먼 씨가 외쳤다.\n\n\"그래, 그래, 곧 가네.\" 윈클 씨가 답했다. \"안녕히 주무십시오!\"\n\n\"잘 가게.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n또 한 번의 작별 인사가 오갔고, 또 한 번, 그러고도 대여섯 번이 더 이어졌지만, 윈클 씨는 여전히 친구의 손을 단단히 붙잡은 채, 한결같은 그 묘한 표정으로 그의 얼굴을 들여다보고 있었다.\n\n\"무슨 일이라도 있나?\" 마침내 픽윅 씨가, 흔들어 댄 통에 팔이 아주 욱신거리게 되자 물었다.\n\n\"아무 일 없습니다.\" 윈클 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 잘 가게.\" 픽윅 씨가 손을 빼려 애쓰며 말했다.\n\n\"제 벗이시여, 제 은인이시여, 제가 존경하는 동무시여.\" 윈클 씨가 그의 손목을 붙들며 중얼거렸다. \"저를 모질게 판단하지 말아 주십시오. 가망 없는 장애에 밀려 막다른 데까지 내몰린 제가, 그러니까 제가 그러했다는 걸 들으시더라도—\"\n\n\"어이, 이제 그만.\" 터프먼 씨가 다시 문간에 나타나며 말했다. \"올 텐가, 아니면 우리가 갇혀 버려야 하나?\"\n\n\"그래, 그래, 갈 준비됐네.\" 윈클 씨가 답했다. 그러고는 격렬하게 안간힘을 쓰며 몸을 뿌리쳤다.\n\n픽윅 씨가 말없이 놀란 채 복도 너머로 그들의 뒷모습을 바라보고 있을 때, 샘 웰러가 계단 위쪽에 나타나 한순간 윈클 씨의 귀에 대고 무언가를 속삭였다.\n\n\"아, 물론이지, 나만 믿게.\" 그 신사가 소리 내어 말했다.\n\n\"고맙습니다, 나리. 잊지 않으실 거지요, 나리?\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n\"물론 잊지 않네.\" 윈클 씨가 답했다.\n\n\"행운을 빕니다, 나리.\" 샘이 모자에 손을 갖다 대며 말했다. \"저도 정말이지 따라가고 싶었습니다만, 나리, 물론 주인 어른이 먼저지요.\"\n\n\"자네가 여기 남는다는 건 자네에게 큰 명예일세.\" 윈클 씨가 말했다. 이 말과 함께 그들은 계단 아래로 사라졌다.\n\n\"참으로 별일이군.\" 픽윅 씨가 방으로 돌아와 생각에 잠긴 자세로 식탁에 앉으며 말했다. \"저 젊은 친구가 대체 무얼 하려는 걸까?\"\n\n그가 한동안 그 일을 곱씹으며 앉아 있는데, 간수 로커의 목소리가 들어가도 되겠느냐고 물었다.\n\n\"얼마든지요.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"더 부드러운 베개를 가져왔습니다, 나리.\" 로커 씨가 말했다. \"어젯밤에 쓰신 임시 베개 대신에요.\"\n\n\"고맙소.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"포도주 한 잔 하겠소?\"\n\n\"친절도 하십니다, 나리.\" 내미는 잔을 받으며 로커 씨가 답했다. \"나리를 위하여.\"\n\n\"고맙소.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"안된 말이지만, 오늘 밤 나리 집주인이 아주 위독합니다, 나리.\" 로커가 잔을 내려놓고, 다시 쓰려고 모자 안쪽 안감을 살펴보며 말했다.\n\n\"뭐라고요! 그 형평법원 죄수 말이오!\" 픽윅 씨가 외쳤다.\n\n\"그 양반은 형평법원 죄수로 그리 오래 있진 못할 겁니다, 나리.\" 로커가 모자를 빙 돌려, 들여다보면서 제조자 이름이 똑바로 위로 가도록 맞추며 답했다.\n\n\"당신 말에 등골이 서늘해지는군요.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"무슨 뜻이오?\"\n\n\"그 양반은 오래전부터 폐병을 앓아 왔는데요.\" 로커 씨가 말했다. \"오늘 밤 숨쉬기가 아주 나빠졌습니다. 의사가 여섯 달 전에 말하기를, 공기를 바꾸는 것 말고는 그 양반을 살릴 길이 없다고 했지요.\"\n\n\"이런 맙소사!\" 픽윅 씨가 외쳤다. \"이 사람이 여섯 달 동안 법에 의해 서서히 살해당해 왔단 말이오?\"\n\n\"그건 잘 모르겠습니다.\" 로커가 모자챙을 두 손으로 잡고 무게를 가늠하며 답했다. \"그 양반은 어디 있었어도 똑같이 됐을 겁니다. 오늘 아침 의무실로 옮겨졌습니다. 의사 말로는 되도록 기력을 지켜 주어야 한다더군요. 그래서 교도소장이 자기 집에서 포도주랑 죽이랑 그런 걸 보내 줬습니다. 이건 교도소장 탓이 아닙니다, 아시겠지요, 나리.\"\n\n\"물론 아니지요.\" 픽윅 씨가 황급히 답했다.\n\n\"하지만 안됐지만.\" 로커가 고개를 가로저으며 말했다. \"그 양반은 이제 끝장난 것 같습니다. 방금 네디한테 한쪽에 육 펜스어치 둘을 걸고 내기를 걸었더니, 받질 않더군요. 잘한 일이지요. 고맙습니다, 나리. 안녕히 주무십시오, 나리.\"\n\n\"잠깐.\" 픽윅 씨가 간곡하게 말했다. \"그 의무실이 어디요?\"\n\n\"나리께서 주무신 바로 위입니다, 나리.\" 로커가 답했다. \"오시려거든 안내해 드리지요.\" 픽윅 씨는 아무 말 없이 모자를 집어 들고 곧장 뒤따랐다.\n\n간수는 말없이 앞장섰다. 그러고는 방문 빗장을 가만히 들어 올리며, 픽윅 씨에게 들어가라고 손짓했다. 그곳은 넓고 휑하고 황량한 방으로, 철제 막침대가 여러 개 놓여 있었고, 그중 하나에 한 사내의 그림자가 길게 뻗어 누워 있었다. 핏기 없고, 창백하고, 송장 같았다. 그의 숨결은 거칠고 탁했으며, 숨이 들고 날 때마다 고통스럽게 신음했다. 침대 머리맡에는 구두장이 앞치마를 두른 키 작은 노인이 앉아, 뿔테 안경의 도움을 받아 성경을 소리 내어 읽고 있었다. 그 행운의 유산 상속인이었다.\n\n병든 사내가 손을 들어 곁을 지키는 이의 팔에 얹고는, 그만 읽으라고 손짓했다. 그는 책을 덮어 침대 위에 내려놓았다.\n\n\"창문을 열어 주게.\" 병든 사내가 말했다.\n\n노인이 그렇게 했다. 마차와 짐수레의 소음, 바퀴 덜컹거리는 소리, 사내들과 소년들의 외침, 삶과 일로 약동하는 거대한 군중의 온갖 분주한 소리가 한데 어우러져 깊은 웅성거림이 되어 방 안으로 떠밀려 들어왔다. 그 쉰 듯한 큰 웅성거림 위로 이따금 떠들썩한 웃음소리가 솟아올랐고, 들뜬 군중 가운데 누군가가 외쳐 부르는 어떤 흥얼대는 노랫가락 한 토막이 한순간 귀를 스쳤다가, 목소리들의 포효와 발걸음 소리 속에 묻혀 사라지곤 했다. 바깥에서 묵직하게 굽이쳐 가는, 쉴 줄 모르는 삶의 바다, 그 물결이 부서지는 소리였다. 이런 것들은 언제 들어도 조용한 듣는 이에게 우울한 소리이건만, 죽음의 침상을 지키는 자에게는 그 얼마나 더 우울한가!\n\n\"여긴 공기가 없네.\" 사내가 가냘프게 말했다. \"이곳이 공기를 더럽혀. 몇 해 전 저 바깥을 거닐 때만 해도 사방이 신선했지. 그런데 이 담장을 지나오면서 뜨겁고 답답해진다네. 도저히 숨을 쉴 수가 없어.\"\n\n\"우리 함께 오래도록 그걸 들이마셔 오지 않았나.\" 노인이 말했다. \"자, 자, 마음을 가라앉히게.\"\n\n잠시 침묵이 흘렀고, 그 사이에 두 구경꾼이 침대로 다가갔다. 병든 사내는 늙은 옥살이 동무의 손 하나를 제 쪽으로 끌어당겨, 두 손 사이에 다정히 감싸 쥐고는, 그대로 놓지 않았다.\n\n\"바라건대.\" 그가 잠시 후 헐떡이며 말했는데, 어찌나 가냘픈지 두 사람은 그 창백한 입술이 토해 내는 어렴풋한 소리를 받아들으려고 침대 위로 귀를 바싹 숙였다. \"바라건대 자비로우신 심판자께서 이 땅에서 내가 받은 무거운 형벌을 헤아려 주시기를. 이십 년일세, 친구, 이 끔찍한 무덤에서 이십 년이야! 내 아이가 죽었을 때 내 가슴은 무너졌고, 그 조그만 관 속의 아이에게 입맞춤조차 할 수 없었네. 그 후로 이 온갖 소음과 소동 속에서 느낀 외로움은 참으로 끔찍했지. 하느님, 저를 용서하소서! 그분은 외롭게, 더디게 죽어 가는 내 모습을 지켜보셨네.\"\n\n그는 두 손을 모으고, 그들이 알아들을 수 없는 무언가를 더 중얼거리더니, 잠에 빠져들었다. 처음엔 그저 잠일 뿐이었으니, 그가 미소 짓는 것을 그들이 보았기 때문이다.\n\n그들은 잠시 함께 소곤거렸고, 간수가 베개 위로 몸을 숙였다가 황급히 물러섰다. \"하느님 맙소사, 이 양반 석방됐소!\" 사내가 말했다.\n\n그는 정말 그러했다. 그러나 살아 있으면서도 너무나 죽음과 닮아 있었던 터라, 그들은 그가 언제 죽었는지조차 알지 못했다.\n\n새뮤얼 웰러 씨가 수감된 지 며칠 지난 어느 날 아침, 그는 주인의 방을 할 수 있는 한 정성껏 정돈하고, 주인이 책과 서류를 앞에 두고 편안히 자리 잡은 것을 확인한 다음, 앞으로 한두 시간을 제 나름대로 보내려고 물러났다. 화창한 아침이었고, 샘은 한 잔의 포터를 야외에서 마시면 어떤 자잘한 오락 못지않게 다음 십오 분쯤을 가볍게 만들어 주리라는 생각이 들었다.\n\n이런 결론에 다다른 그는 술집 쪽으로 발걸음을 옮겼다. 맥주를 사고, 게다가 그저께 신문까지 한 부 손에 넣은 그는 스키틀 놀이터로 가서 벤치에 앉아 매우 차분하고 체계적인 방식으로 즐기기 시작했다.\n\n우선 그는 시원하게 맥주를 한 모금 들이켰고, 그런 다음 창문을 올려다보며 거기서 감자 껍질을 벗기고 있는 젊은 아가씨에게 플라토닉한 윙크를 던졌다. 그러고는 신문을 펼쳐 경찰 보도가 바깥쪽으로 오도록 접었는데, 바람이 조금이라도 부는 날에는 이것이 짜증나고 어려운 일인지라, 그 일을 해내고 나서는 맥주를 또 한 모금 들이켰다. 그런 다음 신문을 두 줄 읽다가, 라켓 경기를 마무리하던 두 사내를 보려고 멈춰 섰는데, 경기가 끝나자 그는 흡족한 태도로 \"아주 좋군\" 하고 외치고는, 구경꾼들의 감정이 자신의 것과 일치하는지 확인하려고 둘러보았다. 이것은 또 창문을 올려다보는 일도 수반했고, 젊은 아가씨가 아직 거기 있었으므로, 다시 윙크를 하고 또 한 모금의 맥주로 무언극처럼 그녀의 건강을 위해 건배하는 것은 흔한 예의의 행위였으니, 샘은 그렇게 했다. 그리고 이 마지막 거동을 눈을 동그랗게 뜨고 지켜본 어린 소년에게 무섭게 인상을 찌푸린 뒤, 그는 한쪽 다리를 다른 쪽 위로 척 올리고는, 양손으로 신문을 든 채 진지하게 읽기 시작했다.\n\n그가 필요한 만큼의 몰입 상태에 채 가라앉기도 전에, 어딘가 먼 통로에서 자기 이름이 외쳐지는 것을 들은 듯했다. 잘못 들은 것도 아니었으니, 그 소리는 곧 입에서 입으로 옮아가더니 몇 초 만에 \"웰러!\" 하는 외침이 공기를 가득 메웠다.\n\n\"여기!\" 샘이 우렁찬 목소리로 고함쳤다. \"무슨 일이오? 누가 그를 찾소? 시골 별장에 불이 났다는 급보라도 왔소?\"\n\n\"홀에서 누가 자네를 찾고 있네.\" 곁에 서 있던 한 사내가 말했다.\n\n\"여보게, 저 신문하고 술잔 좀 봐 주겠나?\" 샘이 말했다. \"나 가는 중일세. 제기랄, 나를 법정으로 불러내는 거라 해도 이보다 더 야단법석을 떨진 못하겠군!\"\n\n이 말과 더불어, 앞서 언급한 젊은 신사 — 정작 찾는 그 인물이 바로 곁에 있는 줄도 모르고 온 힘을 다해 \"웰러!\"를 외쳐 대던 — 의 머리를 가볍게 툭 쳐 주고는, 샘은 마당을 가로질러 서둘러 가서 계단을 뛰어 올라 홀로 들어섰다. 거기서 그의 눈에 처음 들어온 것은, 사랑하는 아버지가 맨 아래 계단에 모자를 손에 든 채 앉아서 삼십 초 간격으로 가장 큰 목소리로 \"웰러!\"를 외쳐 대고 있는 광경이었다.\n\n\"뭘 그렇게 고래고래 외쳐 대는 거요?\" 노신사가 또 한 차례 고함을 토해 냈을 때 샘이 발끈하여 말했다. \"그렇게 잔뜩 달아올라서는 꼭 성난 유리 부는 직공처럼 보이오. 무슨 일이오?\"\n\n\"아하!\" 노신사가 대답했다. \"네가 리젠시 공원을 한 바퀴 산책하러 간 게 아닌가 걱정되기 시작하던 참이다, 새미.\"\n\n\"이봐요,\" 샘이 말했다. \"탐욕의 희생자한테 그런 비아냥은 그만두고, 그 계단에서 좀 내려오시오. 거기 왜 앉아 계신 거요? 내가 거기 사는 것도 아닌데.\"\n\n\"너한테 보여 줄 재미난 게 있단다, 새미.\" 웰러 영감이 일어서며 말했다.\n\n\"잠깐만,\" 샘이 말했다. \"등이 온통 허옇소.\"\n\n\"그래, 됐다, 새미, 털어 내거라.\" 아들이 먼지를 털어 주자 웰러 씨가 말했다. \"옷에 회반죽을 묻히고 돌아다니면 여기선 좀 사적인 의미로 비칠지도 모르니까 말이다, 안 그러냐, 새미?\"\n\n웰러 씨가 이 대목에서 곧 킥킥대는 발작이 닥쳐올 명백한 징후를 드러내자, 샘이 끼어들어 그것을 막았다.\n\n\"가만히 좀 계시오, 정말.\" 샘이 말했다. \"이런 늙은 그림딱지 같은 양반은 세상에 또 없을 거요. 지금 또 뭐 때문에 터지려는 거요?\"\n\n\"새미,\" 웰러 씨가 이마를 닦으며 말했다. \"이러다 언젠가는 내가 웃다가 중풍에 걸릴까 걱정이다, 얘야.\"\n\n\"그럼, 도대체 왜 그러시오?\" 샘이 말했다. \"자, 그래서, 무슨 할 말이 있는 거요?\"\n\n\"누가 나하고 같이 여기 왔는지 알겠느냐, 새미벨?\" 웰러 씨가 한두 걸음 물러나 입을 오므리고 눈썹을 치켜올리며 말했다.\n\n\"펠이오?\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n웰러 씨는 고개를 저었고, 그의 붉은 뺨은 터져 나갈 구멍을 찾고 있던 웃음으로 부풀어 올랐다.\n\n\"얼룩진 얼굴의 사내인가?\" 샘이 물었다.\n\n웰러 씨는 다시 고개를 저었다.\n\n\"그럼 누구요?\" 샘이 물었다.\n\n\"네 새어머니다.\" 웰러 씨가 말했다. 그가 그 말을 한 것은 다행이었으니, 그러지 않았더라면 더없이 부자연스럽게 부푼 그의 뺨이 영락없이 터지고 말았을 것이다.\n\n\"네 새어머니다, 새미,\" 웰러 씨가 말했다. \"그리고 빨간 코의 사내다, 얘야. 빨간 코의 사내 말이다. 호! 호! 호!\"\n\n이 말과 함께 웰러 씨는 발작적인 웃음 속으로 빠져들었고, 샘은 차츰 온 얼굴에 함박웃음이 번져 가는 채로 그를 바라보았다.\n\n\"그들이 너하고 진지한 이야기를 좀 하러 온 거다, 새미벨.\" 웰러 씨가 눈물을 닦으며 말했다. \"그 못된 채권자에 대해선 한마디도 흘리지 마라, 새미.\"\n\n\"뭐요, 그게 누군지 모른단 말이오?\" 샘이 물었다.\n\n\"눈곱만큼도 모르지.\" 아버지가 대답했다.\n\n\"그들이 어디 있소?\" 샘이 노신사의 온갖 함박웃음을 똑같이 되받으며 말했다.\n\n\"아늑한 방에.\" 웰러 씨가 대꾸했다. \"빨간 코 사내가 술 있는 데 말고 어디 다른 데 가 있을 리 있겠느냐. 천만에, 새미벨, 그럴 리 없지. 오늘 아침 마키스에서 여기까지 길을 따라 꽤나 유쾌하게 달려왔단다, 새미.\" 웰러 씨는 또박또박 말할 수 있을 만큼 추슬렀다고 느끼자 말했다. \"네 새어머니의 첫 남편 거였던 그 자그마한 이륜마차에 늙은 얼룩말을 매고 몰았는데, 거기에다 목자님을 위해 안락의자를 하나 들어 올려 실었지. 그런데 정말이지,\" 웰러 씨가 깊은 경멸의 표정을 지으며 말했다. \"정말이지, 그자가 올라타기 좋으라고 우리 집 문 앞 길거리까지 휴대용 계단을 들고 나오는 게 아니냐 말이다.\"\n\n\"설마요?\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n\"정말 그랬다니까, 새미.\" 아버지가 대답했다. \"그자가 막상 올라탈 때, 마치 꼬박 육 피트 아래로 곤두박질쳐서 백만 조각으로 박살날까 봐 두려운 듯이, 양옆을 얼마나 꽉 붙잡던지 네가 봤어야 하는데. 그래도 마침내 굴러떨어지듯 올라탔고, 우리는 출발했지. 그리고 내 생각엔 — 내 말은, 새미벨, 내 생각엔 — 모퉁이를 돌 때마다 그자가 좀 덜컹거렸을 게다.\"\n\n\"뭐, 기둥 한두 개에 부딪치도록 모셨다는 거요?\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n\"그게 걱정이다,\" 웰러 씨가 황홀하게 윙크를 연발하며 대답했다. \"한두 개쯤 들이받은 것 같아서 말이다, 새미. 그자는 내내 안락의자에서 튕겨 나가고 있었거든.\"\n\n여기서 노신사는 고개를 좌우로 흔들더니, 얼굴이 격렬하게 부풀고 이목구비가 죄다 별안간 넓어지는 것을 동반한 채, 목구멍 깊은 데서 쉰 우르릉 소리에 사로잡혔는데, 그 징후에 아들은 적잖이 놀랐다.\n\n\"놀라지 마라, 새미, 놀라지 마.\" 노신사가 한참을 버둥거리고 땅을 발작적으로 몇 번 쾅쾅 구른 끝에 목소리를 되찾고서 말했다. \"그저 조용한 웃음이란 걸 한번 해 보려는 것뿐이다, 새미.\"\n\n\"음, 그런 거라면,\" 샘이 말했다. \"다시는 해 보지 않는 게 좋겠소. 꽤나 위험한 발명품이라는 걸 알게 될 거요.\"\n\n\"마음에 안 드냐, 새미?\" 노신사가 물었다.\n\n\"전혀요.\" 샘이 대답했다.\n\n\"음,\" 웰러 씨가 여전히 뺨에 눈물을 줄줄 흘리며 말했다. \"내가 그걸 할 수만 있었더라면 나한테 아주 큰 도움이 됐을 거고, 가끔 네 새어머니하고 나 사이에 오갈 말도 꽤나 아낄 수 있었을 텐데. 하지만 네 말이 맞는 것 같다, 새미, 그건 너무 중풍 쪽에 가깝구나 — 너무 지나치게 그쪽이야, 새미벨.\"\n\n이 대화로 그들은 아늑한 방 문 앞에 다다랐고, 샘은 — 어깨너머로 돌아보며 여태 뒤에서 킥킥대고 있는 존경하는 아버지에게 짓궂은 곁눈질을 한 번 던지려고 잠시 멈춘 뒤 — 곧장 앞장서 들어갔다.\n\n\"새어머니,\" 샘이 부인에게 정중히 인사하며 말했다. \"이렇게 와 주셔서 정말 고맙습니다. — 목자님, 안녕하시오?\"\n\n\"오, 새뮤얼!\" 웰러 부인이 말했다. \"끔찍하구나.\"\n\n\"전혀 아닙니다, 부인.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"안 그렇소, 목자님?\"\n\n스티긴스 씨는 두 손을 들어 올리고 눈을 치켜떠, 흰자위 — 아니 차라리 누런자위 — 만 보일 지경이었으나, 말로는 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"이 신사분께선 무슨 괴로운 병이라도 앓고 계십니까?\" 샘이 설명을 구하듯 새어머니를 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"이 선량한 분은 네가 여기 있는 걸 보고 비통해하시는 거다, 새뮤얼.\" 웰러 부인이 대답했다.\n\n\"오, 그게 그거였구나, 그렇소?\" 샘이 말했다. \"저분 태도를 보고는, 마지막으로 드신 그 오이에 후추 뿌리는 걸 깜빡하신 게 아닌가 했지요. 앉으시오, 선생. 앉는다고 추가 요금은 안 받습니다, 왕이 신하들을 닦아세우며 말한 것처럼요.\"\n\n\"젊은이,\" 스티긴스 씨가 거드름을 피우며 말했다. \"투옥으로도 자네가 누그러지지 않은 것 같아 걱정이군.\"\n\n\"실례합니다, 선생,\" 샘이 대답했다. \"황송하게도 무어라 분부하셨소?\"\n\n\"내가 보기에 젊은이, 자네 성정이 이 징벌로도 조금도 부드러워지지 않은 것 같네.\" 스티긴스 씨가 큰 소리로 말했다.\n\n\"선생,\" 샘이 대답했다. \"그렇게 말씀해 주시니 참 친절하시오. 내 성정이 부드러운 게 아니길 바랍니다, 선생. 좋게 봐 주셔서 정말 고맙소, 선생.\"\n\n대화가 이 대목에 이르렀을 때, 웃음에 위태로울 만큼 가까운 소리가 웰러 영감이 앉아 있던 의자에서 새어 나오는 것이 들렸다. 그러자 웰러 부인은 사정의 모든 정황을 황급히 헤아린 끝에, 차츰 히스테리에 빠지는 것이 자기의 마땅한 의무라 여겼다.\n\n\"웰러,\" 웰러 부인이 말했다(노신사는 구석에 앉아 있었다). \"웰러! 나오세요.\"\n\n\"정말 고맙구려, 여보,\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다. \"하지만 난 여기가 아주 편안하다오.\"\n\n이에 웰러 부인은 울음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n\"뭐가 잘못된 거요, 부인?\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n\"오, 새뮤얼!\" 웰러 부인이 대답했다. \"네 아버지가 나를 비참하게 만드는구나. 저이한테는 무엇으로도 도움이 안 될까?\"\n\n\"이 말 들었소?\" 샘이 말했다. \"부인께서 무엇으로도 당신한테 도움이 안 될지 알고 싶어 하시오.\"\n\n\"웰러 부인의 정중한 물음에 참으로 신세를 졌구나, 새미.\" 노신사가 대답했다. \"내 생각엔 담배 한 대가 나한테 꽤나 도움이 될 것 같다. 그걸 좀 누릴 수 있겠느냐, 새미?\"\n\n여기서 웰러 부인은 또 몇 방울 눈물을 떨어뜨렸고, 스티긴스 씨는 신음했다.\n\n\"이런! 이 가엾은 신사분이 또 편찮으신가 보군.\" 샘이 둘러보며 말했다. \"이제 어디가 아프시오, 선생?\"\n\n\"같은 자리라네, 젊은이,\" 스티긴스 씨가 대꾸했다. \"같은 자리야.\"\n\n\"그게 어디일까요, 선생?\" 샘이 겉으로는 더없이 순진하게 물었다.\n\n\"가슴속이라네, 젊은이.\" 스티긴스 씨가 우산을 조끼에 갖다 대며 대답했다.\n\n이 가슴 저미는 대답에 웰러 부인은 도무지 감정을 억누를 수 없어 큰 소리로 흐느꼈고, 빨간 코의 사내가 성인(聖人)이라고 확신한다고 말했다. 그러자 웰러 영감은 나직한 소리로, 저자는 바깥 성 시몬 교구와 안쪽 성 워커 교구를 합친 교구의 대표가 틀림없다고 넌지시 끼어들었다.\n\n\"걱정이군요, 부인,\" 샘이 말했다. \"얼굴이 일그러진 이 신사분께서 눈앞의 우울한 광경 탓에 좀 목이 마르신 모양입니다. 그렇습니까, 부인?\"\n\n그 갸륵한 부인은 대답을 구하려고 스티긴스 씨를 쳐다보았다. 그 신사는 눈알을 여러 번 굴리며 오른손으로 목을 움켜쥐고 무언가를 삼키는 시늉을 하여, 자기가 목마르다는 것을 넌지시 알렸다.\n\n\"안타깝게도, 새뮤얼, 정말로 감정 탓에 그리되신 것 같구나.\" 웰러 부인이 애처롭게 말했다.\n\n\"평소에 즐기시는 술은 무엇이오, 선생?\" 샘이 대답했다.\n\n\"오, 내 친애하는 젊은 벗이여,\" 스티긴스 씨가 대답했다. \"온갖 술이란 다 헛된 것이라네!\"\n\n\"참으로 옳아요, 참으로 옳아요.\" 웰러 부인이 신음을 중얼거리며 고개를 끄덕여 동의하며 말했다.\n\n\"음,\" 샘이 말했다. \"그럴지도 모르겠습니다, 선생. 하지만 어느 게 선생께서 특히 좋아하시는 헛된 것이오? 어느 헛된 것의 풍미를 제일 좋아하시오, 선생?\"\n\n\"오, 내 친애하는 젊은 벗이여,\" 스티긴스 씨가 대답했다. \"나는 그 모두를 경멸한다네. 만약,\" 스티긴스 씨가 말했다. \"만약 그중에 다른 것보다 덜 역겨운 게 하나 있다면, 그건 럼이라 부르는 술이지. 따뜻하게, 친애하는 젊은 벗이여, 잔마다 설탕 세 덩이를 넣어서 말일세.\"\n\n\"이런 말씀 드리기 정말 안타깝지만, 선생,\" 샘이 말했다. \"이 시설에서는 그 특정한 헛된 것을 파는 걸 허락하지 않습니다.\"\n\n\"오, 이 고질적인 자들의 모진 마음이여!\" 스티긴스 씨가 외쳤다. \"오, 이 비인간적인 박해자들의 저주받을 잔혹함이여!\"\n\n이 말과 함께 스티긴스 씨는 다시 눈을 치떴고, 우산으로 가슴을 두들겼다. 그리고 이 목사 양반에게 공정을 기하자면, 그의 분개는 참으로 진실하고 꾸밈없어 보였다고 말해 둘 만하다.\n\n웰러 부인과 빨간 코 신사가 이 비인간적인 처우에 대해 자못 강력한 어조로 논평하고, 그 장본인들을 향해 갖가지 경건하고 거룩한 저주를 퍼붓고 난 뒤, 후자는 포도주 한 병에 물과 향신료와 설탕을 조금 넣어 데운 것을 권했으니, 위에 좋고 여느 혼합주보다 헛됨의 맛이 덜하다는 것이었다. 그리하여 그것을 준비하라는 분부가 내려졌고, 그것이 마련되는 동안 빨간 코의 사내와 웰러 부인은 웰러 영감을 바라보며 신음했다.\n\n\"음, 새미,\" 노신사가 말했다. \"이번의 이 유쾌한 방문으로 네 기분이 좀 나아졌으면 좋겠구나. 아주 명랑하고 유익한 대화 아니냐, 새미?\"\n\n\"못된 인간이오.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"그리고 그런 꼴사나운 말을 나한테 더는 하지 마시오.\"\n\n이 지극히 온당한 대꾸에 교화되기는커녕, 웰러 영감은 곧장 다시 함박웃음에 빠져들었다. 그리고 이 막무가내의 행동이 부인과 스티긴스 씨로 하여금 눈을 감고 의자에서 괴로운 듯 앞뒤로 몸을 흔들게 하자, 그는 한술 더 떠 앞서 말한 스티긴스의 코를 후려치고 비틀어 주고 싶다는 뜻을 담은 몇 가지 무언극을 연출했는데, 그 연기를 펼치는 것이 그에게 큰 정신적 위안을 주는 듯했다. 노신사는 한번은 하마터면 들킬 뻔했으니, 마침 니거스 술이 도착하자 스티긴스 씨가 흠칫 놀라는 바람에, 웰러 씨가 몇 분 동안 그의 귀에서 두 인치도 안 되는 거리에서 허공에 상상의 불꽃놀이를 그리던 그 움켜쥔 주먹에 머리를 정통으로 부딪쳤기 때문이다.\n\n\"뭘 그렇게 야만스럽게 잔을 향해 손을 뻗는 거요?\" 샘이 잽싸게 말했다. \"신사분을 친 게 안 보이오?\"\n\n\"일부러 그런 게 아니다, 새미.\" 웰러 씨가 그 뜻밖의 사건이 벌어진 데에 다소 무안해하며 말했다.\n\n\"내복용으로 한번 드셔 보시오, 선생.\" 빨간 코 신사가 처량한 얼굴로 머리를 문지르자 샘이 말했다. \"이건 어떻소, 따뜻한 헛된 것 한 잔으로 말이오, 선생?\"\n\n스티긴스 씨는 말로는 아무 대답도 하지 않았으나, 그 태도가 웅변적이었다. 그는 샘이 손에 쥐여 준 잔의 내용물을 맛보고, 우산을 바닥에 내려놓고는 다시 맛보았으며, 두세 번 손으로 배를 차분히 쓰다듬었다. 그러고는 단숨에 전부 들이켜고 입맛을 다신 다음, 더 달라고 잔을 내밀었다.\n\n웰러 부인 또한 그 혼합주에 마땅한 예우를 다하는 데 뒤지지 않았다. 그 갸륵한 부인은 한 방울도 입에 댈 수 없다고 항변하는 것으로 시작하더니 — 그러고는 조금 한 모금 — 다음엔 큰 한 모금 — 그다음엔 아주 여러 모금을 들었다. 그리고 그녀의 감정이란 독한 술이 닿으면 강하게 영향을 받는 그런 물질의 성질을 띤지라, 니거스 한 모금마다 눈물 한 방울을 떨구었고, 그렇게 감정을 녹여 내려가다가 마침내 자못 애처롭고 점잖은 비탄의 경지에 이르렀다.\n\n웰러 영감은 이런 조짐과 징후를 갖은 혐오의 표시와 함께 지켜보았고, 같은 술을 두 잔째 들이켠 스티긴스 씨가 처량한 모양새로 한숨짓기 시작하자, 그는 갖가지 두서없는 횡설수설로 이 모든 거동에 대한 못마땅함을 분명히 드러냈는데, 그중에서 귀에 알아들을 수 있는 것이라고는 \"허튼소리\"라는 말을 성난 어조로 자주 되뇌는 것뿐이었다.\n\n\"내가 무슨 말 하려는지 알겠느냐, 새미벨, 얘야.\" 노신사가 자기 부인과 스티긴스 씨를 오래도록 한결같이 바라본 끝에 아들의 귀에 대고 속삭였다. \"내 생각엔 네 새어머니의 뱃속에도, 저 빨간 코 사내의 뱃속에도 뭔가 잘못된 게 있는 게 틀림없다.\"\n\n\"무슨 말이오?\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n\"내 말은 이거다, 새미,\" 노신사가 대답했다. \"저들이 마시는 게 아무 자양분도 안 되는 것 같단 말이다. 죄다 미지근한 물로 변해서 눈에서 줄줄 쏟아져 나오니 말이야. 틀림없다, 새미, 이건 체질적인 결함이야.\"\n\n웰러 씨는 이 과학적인 견해를 갖가지 확신에 찬 찌푸림과 끄덕임으로 전했는데, 웰러 부인이 그것을 알아채고는 그것이 자기든 스티긴스 씨든, 아니면 둘 다든 무언가 깎아내리는 의미를 담고 있다고 단정하여, 한없이 더 나빠지려던 참이었다. 바로 그때 스티긴스 씨가 간신히 다리로 일어서더니, 좌중을 위해, 특히 새뮤얼 씨를 위해 교화적인 설교를 펼치기 시작했다. 그는 새뮤얼에게 감동적인 어조로, 그가 던져진 이 죄악의 소굴에서 경계를 게을리하지 말고, 온갖 위선과 마음의 교만을 멀리하며, 만사에 자기(스티긴스)를 정확한 본보기와 모범으로 삼으라고 간곡히 권했으니, 그리하면 자기처럼 그도 머잖아 자기가 더없이 존경할 만하고 흠 없는 인물이며 자기의 모든 지인과 벗들은 가망 없이 타락하고 방탕한 자들이라는 흐뭇한 결론에 이르리라 기대할 수 있다는 것이었다. 그 생각은, 그의 말로는, 자기에게 더없이 생생한 만족을 안겨 주지 않을 수 없다는 것이었다.\n\n그는 더 나아가, 무엇보다도 만취의 악덕을 피하라고 그에게 간청했으니, 그것을 돼지의 더러운 습성에, 그리고 입에 씹으면 기억을 슬쩍 앗아 간다는 그 독하고 해로운 약물에 빗대었다. 설교의 이 대목에 이르러 빨간 코의 목사 양반은 유난히 두서가 없어졌고, 자기 웅변의 흥분에 휩싸여 이리저리 비틀거리다가, 수직을 유지하려고 의자 등받이를 부득이 붙잡아야 했다.\n\n스티긴스 씨는, 종교의 첫 교리를 풀이할 분별도 그 첫 원리를 느낄 마음도 없으면서, 여느 범죄자보다 사회에 더 위험한 자들인 그 거짓 예언자와 종교를 조롱하는 가련한 자들 — 가장 약하고 가장 무지한 이들을 으레 기만하고, 가장 신성하게 여겨야 할 것에 멸시와 경멸을 퍼부으며, 훌륭한 여러 종파와 신조에 속한 덕망 높고 행실 바른 수많은 이들을 부분적인 오명에 빠뜨리는 자들 — 에 대해 청중에게 경계하라고는 하지 않았다. 다만 그가 한참 동안 의자 등받이에 기대어 한쪽 눈을 감고 다른 쪽 눈으로 한참 윙크를 해 댔으므로, 짐작건대 그는 이 모든 것을 생각하면서도 혼자 마음에만 담아 두었던 듯하다.\n\n연설이 펼쳐지는 동안 웰러 부인은 문단이 끝날 때마다 흐느끼며 울었다. 한편 샘은 의자에 책상다리를 하고 앉아 등받이 맨 윗단에 팔을 얹은 채, 더없이 부드럽고 온화한 태도로 연설자를 바라보며, 이따금 노신사에게 알아본다는 눈길을 보내곤 했는데, 그 노신사는 처음엔 좋아하다가 중간쯤에 잠이 들어 버렸다.\n\n\"브라보, 아주 멋지오!\" 빨간 코의 사내가 설교를 마치고는 닳아빠진 장갑을 끼면서, 그 바람에 터진 손가락 끝으로 손가락을 밀어 넣어 마디까지 드러내자 샘이 말했다. \"아주 멋지오.\"\n\n\"네게 도움이 되기를 바란다, 새뮤얼.\" 웰러 부인이 엄숙하게 말했다.\n\n\"도움이 될 것 같습니다, 부인.\" 샘이 대답했다.\n\n\"네 아버지한테도 도움이 되리라 바랄 수 있으면 좋으련만.\" 웰러 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"고맙구려, 여보.\" 웰러 영감이 말했다. \"그걸 듣고 나니 당신 기분은 좀 어떻소, 내 사랑?\"\n\n\"조롱꾼!\" 웰러 부인이 외쳤다.\n\n\"미망에 빠진 자여!\" 스티긴스 목사가 말했다.\n\n\"내가 당신의 그 달빛 같은 헛소리보다 더 나은 빛을 얻지 못한다면, 갸륵한 양반,\" 웰러 영감이 말했다. \"나는 영영 길에서 쫓겨날 때까지 밤마차 신세를 면치 못할 것 같구려. 자, 웰러 부인, 저 얼룩말이 마구간에 더 오래 서 있으면, 돌아갈 때 한 발짝도 안 움직이려 할 거고, 그러면 저 안락의자가 목자님을 태운 채 어느 산울타리엔가 처박힐지도 모르겠소.\"\n\n이 추측에 스티긴스 목사는 역력히 당황하여 모자와 우산을 챙겨 들고 즉시 떠나자고 제안했고, 웰러 부인도 이에 동의했다. 샘은 정문 수위실까지 그들을 배웅하며 공손하게 작별했다.\n\n\"안녕, 새미벨.\" 노신사가 말했다.\n\n\"'안녕'이 뭐요?\" 새미가 물었다.\n\n\"음, 그럼, 잘 있거라.\" 노신사가 말했다.\n\n\"오, 그게 하려던 말이오?\" 샘이 말했다. \"안녕히 가시오!\"\n\n\"새미,\" 웰러 씨가 조심스레 둘러보며 속삭였다. \"네 주인 나리께 안부 전하고, 만약 이번 일을 다시 생각해 보실 마음이 있으시면 나한테 연락하시라고 해라. 나하고 가구장이 하나가 나리를 빼낼 묘안을 짜냈단다. 피아노다, 새미벨 — 피아노!\" 웰러 씨가 손등으로 아들의 가슴을 치고는 한두 걸음 물러서며 말했다.\n\n\"무슨 말이오?\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n\"피아노포르테 말이다, 새미벨,\" 웰러 씨가 한층 더 비밀스러운 태도로 대꾸했다. \"빌려 올 수 있는 거 말이다. 소리는 안 나는 걸로, 새미.\"\n\n\"그게 무슨 소용이 있소?\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n\"내 친구 가구장이한테 그걸 도로 가져가라고 사람을 보내시라는 거다, 새미.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다. \"이제 알아듣겠느냐?\"\n\n\"아니오.\" 샘이 대꾸했다.\n\n\"그 안에 아무 장치도 없는 거야.\" 아버지가 속삭였다. \"나리가 모자랑 신발을 신은 채로도 넉넉히 들어가실 거고, 다리 구멍으로 숨을 쉬면 돼. 그게 텅 비어 있거든. 아메리카로 가는 배편을 미리 잡아 두고. 아메리카 정부는 나리한테 쓸 돈이 있다는 걸 알기만 하면 결코 넘겨주지 않을 거다, 새미. 바델 부인이 죽거나 도드슨과 포그 씨가 교수형에 처해질 때까지(내 생각엔 이 마지막 일이 먼저 일어날 가능성이 제일 크다만, 새미) 나리를 거기 머물게 하고, 그런 다음 돌아와서 아메리카 사람들에 관한 책을 한 권 쓰시면, 그들을 실컷 헐뜯기만 하면 경비를 다 대고도 남을 게다.\"\n\n웰러 씨는 이 서두른 음모의 개요를 자못 격렬한 속삭임으로 전했고, 그런 다음 이 어마어마한 통보의 효과를 더 이상의 대화로 약화시킬까 두려운 듯, 마부식 경례를 붙이고는 사라졌다.\n\n존경하는 친척의 비밀스러운 통보에 적잖이 흐트러졌던 평소의 얼굴빛을 샘이 채 되찾기도 전에, 픽윅 씨가 그에게 말을 걸었다.\n\n\"샘.\" 그 신사가 말했다.\n\n\"네, 나리.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"감옥을 한 바퀴 산책하려는데, 네가 따라와 주었으면 한다. 우리가 아는 죄수 하나가 이쪽으로 오는 게 보이는구나, 샘.\" 픽윅 씨가 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"어느 분 말입니까, 나리?\" 웰러 씨가 물었다. \"머리숱 많은 그 신사분이오, 아니면 양말 차림의 흥미로운 그 포로 말입니까?\"\n\n\"둘 다 아니다.\" 픽윅 씨가 대꾸했다. \"네 더 오랜 친구다, 샘.\"\n\n\"제 친구요, 나리?\" 웰러 씨가 외쳤다.\n\n\"분명히 그 신사를 아주 잘 기억할 게다, 샘,\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다. \"그렇지 않다면 내가 생각하는 것보다 네가 옛 지인에 더 무심한 거겠지. 쉿! 한마디도, 샘. 한 음절도 하지 마라. 저기 오는구나.\"\n\n픽윅 씨가 말하는 사이, 징글이 다가왔다. 그는 픽윅 씨의 도움으로 전당포에서 되찾은, 절반쯤 닳은 옷 한 벌을 입고 있어, 전보다는 덜 비참해 보였다. 깨끗한 속옷도 입고 머리도 깎았다. 그러나 몹시 창백하고 야위었으며, 지팡이에 기대어 천천히 기어오듯 다가오는 것을 보면, 그가 병과 궁핍으로 혹독하게 고생했고 아직도 매우 쇠약하다는 것을 쉬이 알 수 있었다. 그는 픽윅 씨가 인사하자 모자를 벗었고, 샘 웰러를 보고는 몹시 풀이 죽고 무안해하는 듯했다.\n\n그의 바로 뒤를 바짝 따라 잡 트로터 씨가 왔는데, 그의 악덕 목록에 동료에 대한 신의와 헌신의 결여만큼은 도무지 자리할 곳이 없었다. 그는 여전히 누더기를 걸치고 꾀죄죄했으나, 며칠 전 픽윅 씨와 처음 만났을 때만큼 얼굴이 그리 홀쭉하지는 않았다. 그는 우리의 인자한 옛 친구에게 모자를 벗어 들며 띄엄띄엄 감사의 말을 중얼거렸고, 굶어 죽을 뻔한 것을 구원받았다는 둥 무언가를 웅얼거렸다.\n\n\"됐네, 됐어.\" 픽윅 씨가 그의 말을 조급하게 가로막으며 말했다. \"자네는 샘하고 따라오게. 나는 자네와 이야기를 좀 하고 싶네, 징글 군. 그의 팔에 기대지 않고 걸을 수 있겠나?\"\n\n\"물론입죠, 선생 — 다 준비됐고 — 너무 빠르지만 않게 — 다리가 후들거리고 — 머리가 이상하고 — 빙글빙글 — 지진이 난 듯한 느낌이 — 아주.\"\n\n\"자, 내 팔을 잡게.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"아뇨, 아뇨.\" 징글이 대답했다. \"정말 안 됩니다 — 차라리 사양하겠습니다.\"\n\n\"쓸데없는 소리.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"내게 기대게, 그러길 바라네, 선생.\"\n\n그가 당황하고 동요하여 어쩔 줄 몰라 하는 것을 보고, 픽윅 씨는 그 병약한 떠돌이의 팔을 자기 팔에 끼워 더는 그 일에 대해 한마디도 하지 않고 데리고 감으로써 문제를 단숨에 매듭지었다.\n\n이 모든 시간 내내 새뮤얼 웰러 씨의 얼굴에는 상상으로 그려 낼 수 있는 가장 압도적이고 넋을 빼앗는 경악의 표정이 떠올라 있었다. 깊은 침묵 속에 잡에게서 징글로, 징글에게서 잡으로 눈길을 옮긴 끝에, 그는 나직이 \"이런, 기가 막혀 죽겠네!\"라는 말을 내뱉었는데, 그 말을 적어도 스무 번은 되뇌었다. 그러고 나서는 완전히 말문이 막힌 듯, 다시 한쪽에서 다른 쪽으로 눈길을 던지며 말없이 어리둥절해하고 어쩔 줄 몰라 했다.\n\n\"자, 샘!\" 픽윅 씨가 뒤돌아보며 말했다.\n\n\"가는 중입니다, 나리.\" 웰러 씨가 기계적으로 주인을 따르며 대답했다. 그러면서도 여전히 자기 곁에서 말없이 걷고 있는 잡 트로터 씨에게서 눈을 떼지 못했다.\n\n잡은 한동안 눈을 땅에 박고 있었다. 잡의 얼굴에 눈을 붙박은 샘은, 주위를 거니는 사람들과 부딪치고, 어린아이들에게 발이 걸려 넘어지고, 계단과 난간에 비틀거리면서도 도무지 그것을 의식하지 못하는 듯하더니, 마침내 잡이 슬그머니 눈을 들어 말했다 —\n\n\"안녕하시오, 웰러 씨?\"\n\n\"그자가 맞군!\" 샘이 외쳤다. 그리고 잡의 신원을 추호의 의심도 없이 확인하고서, 그는 제 다리를 철썩 치며 길고 날카로운 휘파람으로 감정을 토해 냈다.\n\n\"내 처지가 달라졌소, 선생.\" 잡이 말했다.\n\n\"그런 것 같군.\" 웰러 씨가 동료의 누더기를 노골적인 경탄으로 훑어보며 외쳤다. \"이거 꽤나 나쁜 쪽으로 바뀐 거구려, 트로터 씨, 멀쩡한 반 크라운을 주고 미심쩍은 실링 두 닢에 육 펜스어치 가짜 동전을 받은 신사가 말한 것처럼 말이오.\"\n\n\"정말 그렇소.\" 잡이 고개를 저으며 대답했다. \"이젠 속임수가 없소, 웰러 씨. 눈물이란,\" 잡이 잠깐 교활한 기색을 띠며 말했다. \"눈물이란 고통의 유일한 증거도, 최선의 증거도 아니지요.\"\n\n\"그렇소, 아니고말고.\" 샘이 의미심장하게 대답했다.\n\n\"눈물은 꾸며 낼 수도 있소, 웰러 씨.\" 잡이 말했다.\n\n\"그럴 수 있다는 거 나도 알지요.\" 샘이 말했다. \"실은 어떤 자들은 늘 눈물을 준비해 두었다가, 마음 내킬 때마다 마개를 뽑아낼 수 있더군요.\"\n\n\"그렇소.\" 잡이 대답했다. \"하지만 이런 것들은 그리 쉽게 꾸며 낼 수 없고, 그걸 만들어 내려면 더 고통스러운 과정을 거쳐야 하지요.\" 그렇게 말하며 그는 자기의 누렇고 움푹 꺼진 뺨을 가리키고는, 외투 소매를 걷어 올려 한쪽 팔을 드러냈는데, 그 팔은 얇은 살가죽 아래로 어찌나 앙상하고 부서질 듯해 보이던지, 손만 대도 뼈가 부러질 것 같았다.\n\n\"대체 자네 몸에 무슨 짓을 한 거요?\" 샘이 흠칫 물러서며 말했다.\n\n\"아무것도 안 했소.\" 잡이 대답했다.\n\n\"아무것도!\" 샘이 따라 외쳤다.\n\n\"여러 주째 아무것도 안 했소.\" 잡이 말했다. \"먹고 마시는 것도 거의 그만큼 안 했고요.\"\n\n샘은 트로터 씨의 야윈 얼굴과 비참한 행색을 한 번에 죽 훑어보더니, 그의 팔을 붙잡고는 자못 거칠게 끌고 가기 시작했다.\n\n\"어디로 가는 거요, 웰러 씨?\" 잡이 옛 적수의 억센 손아귀에서 부질없이 발버둥치며 말했다.\n\n\"따라오시오,\" 샘이 말했다. \"따라와요!\" 그는 술집에 다다를 때까지 더 이상의 설명을 해 주지 않았고, 그러고는 포터 한 잔을 청하니 곧 나왔다.\n\n\"자,\" 샘이 말했다. \"그걸 다 마시시오, 한 방울도 남기지 말고. 그런 다음 잔을 거꾸로 뒤집어, 약을 다 먹었는지 내게 보여 주시오.\"\n\n\"하지만, 친애하는 웰러 씨.\" 잡이 항변했다.\n\n\"쭉 들이켜요!\" 샘이 단호하게 말했다.\n\n이렇게 다그침을 받자 트로터 씨는 잔을 입술로 가져가, 부드럽고 거의 알아챌 수 없는 정도로 차츰 허공으로 기울였다. 그는 단 한 번, 오직 한 번 긴 숨을 들이쉬려고 멈추었으나, 잔에서 얼굴을 떼지는 않았고, 그러고 나서 잠시 뒤 잔을 팔 길이만큼 쭉 뻗어 바닥이 위로 가도록 들어 보였다. 바닥에 떨어진 것이라곤, 천천히 잔 가장자리에서 떨어져 나와 게으르게 흘러내리는 거품 몇 알갱이뿐이었다.\n\n\"잘했소!\" 샘이 말했다. \"그러고 나니 기분이 좀 어떻소?\"\n\n\"낫소, 선생. 나아진 것 같소.\" 잡이 대답했다.\n\n\"당연히 그렇겠지요.\" 샘이 따지듯 말했다. \"기구에 가스를 넣는 거랑 같소. 그 작업으로 당신이 더 통통해지는 걸 맨눈으로도 알겠소. 같은 크기로 한 잔 더 어떻소?\"\n\n\"사양하겠소, 정말 고맙소, 선생.\" 잡이 대답했다. \"정말이지 사양하겠소.\"\n\n\"그럼, 먹을 거라도 어떻소?\" 샘이 물었다.\n\n\"당신의 갸륵한 주인 나리 덕분에, 선생,\" 트로터 씨가 말했다. \"우리는 양 다리 반 짝을 구워서 세 시 십오 분 전에 먹는데, 삶는 수고를 덜려고 그 밑에 감자를 깔아 두지요.\"\n\n\"뭐! 그분이 당신들을 먹여 살리고 있었단 말이오?\" 샘이 힘주어 물었다.\n\n\"그렇소, 선생.\" 잡이 대답했다. \"그뿐 아니라, 웰러 씨, 내 주인이 몹시 편찮으셔서, 그분이 우리에게 방을 하나 마련해 주셨소 — 전엔 개집 같은 데 있었는데 말이오 — 그 방값을 치러 주시고, 아무도 모를 밤에 우리를 보러 오셨소. 웰러 씨,\" 잡이 처음으로 진짜 눈물을 눈에 글썽이며 말했다. \"나는 그 신사분 발치에 쓰러져 죽을 때까지라도 그분을 섬길 수 있소.\"\n\n\"이봐요!\" 샘이 말했다. \"미안하지만, 이 친구야! 그런 소리 마시오!\"\n\n잡 트로터는 어리둥절한 표정을 지었다.\n\n\"그런 소리 말라니까, 젊은 친구.\" 샘이 단호히 되풀이했다. \"나 말고는 아무도 그분을 섬기지 못하오. 그리고 이왕 말이 나온 김에, 그것 말고 또 다른 비밀 하나를 알려 주리다.\" 샘이 맥줏값을 치르며 말했다. \"내 분명히 말하건대, 나는 타이츠와 각반을 차림한 천사 따위는 들어 본 적도, 이야기책에서 읽은 적도, 그림에서 본 적도 없소 — 내 기억으론 안경을 낀 천사조차 없었지만, 혹 어딘가에 그런 게 있었을지도 모르니 단정은 못 하겠소 — 그렇지만 내 말 잘 들으시오, 잡 트로터, 그분은 그럼에도 불구하고 어엿한 순종 천사요. 그분보다 더 나은 천사를 안다고 감히 내게 말할 자가 있거든 어디 한번 데려와 보시오.\" 이 도전과 함께 웰러 씨는 거스름돈을 옆주머니에 챙겨 넣고는, 길을 가면서 갖가지 확신에 찬 끄덕임과 몸짓을 곁들이며, 화제의 주인공을 찾아 나섰다.\n\n그들은 픽윅 씨가 징글과 함께 자못 진지하게 이야기를 나누며, 라켓 놀이터에 모여 있는 무리들에게는 눈길 한 번 주지 않고 있는 것을 발견했다. 그 무리들 또한 매우 잡다하여, 그저 한가한 호기심에서라도 볼 만한 가치가 있었다.\n\n\"음,\" 샘과 그의 동료가 가까이 다가오자 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"자네 건강이 어떻게 되는지 지켜보고, 그동안 그 일에 대해 생각해 보게. 자네가 할 만하다고 느낄 때 내게 진술서를 작성해 주면, 나도 그것을 숙고한 뒤에 그 문제를 자네와 논의하겠네. 자, 이제 방으로 가게. 자네는 지쳤고, 오래 밖에 있을 만큼 튼튼하지 못하니.\"\n\n앨프리드 징글 씨는, 옛 활기라곤 한 점도 없이 — 픽윅 씨가 처음 그의 비참한 처지에 맞닥뜨렸을 때 그가 꾸며 보였던 그 음울한 명랑함조차 없이 — 말없이 깊이 허리를 숙이고는, 잡에게 아직 따라오지 말라고 손짓하며, 천천히 기어가듯 물러갔다.\n\n\"묘한 광경이지, 그렇지 않으냐, 샘?\" 픽윅 씨가 흐뭇한 듯 둘러보며 말했다.\n\n\"아주 그렇습니다, 나리.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"놀라운 일은 끝이 없군.\" 샘이 혼잣말로 덧붙였다. \"저 징글이 물뿌리개 수레질 비슷한 짓을 하고 있었던 게 아니라면 내가 단단히 잘못 본 거지!\"\n\n* 물뿌리개 수레질: 우는 척하며 눈물을 흘리는 것을 빗댄 샘의 농.\n픽윅 씨가 서 있던 플리트 감옥의 그 구역에서 담장으로 둘러싸인 공간은 마침 좋은 라켓 코트가 될 만큼 널찍했다. 한쪽은 물론 담장 자체로 이루어졌고, 다른 한쪽은 세인트폴 대성당 쪽을 바라보는(아니, 차라리 담장만 없었더라면 바라보았을) 감옥의 그 부분으로 이루어져 있었다. 무기력한 권태의 온갖 자세로 어슬렁거리거나 앉아 있는 채무자들이 수두룩했는데, 그 대부분은 파산 법정에 '출두할' 날이 오기를 감옥에서 기다리는 자들이었고, 나머지는 여러 기간 동안 재구류되어 그 시간을 제 나름대로 빈둥거리며 흘려보내고 있었다. 어떤 이는 초라했고 어떤 이는 멀끔했으며, 많은 이가 더럽고 몇몇은 깨끗했다. 그러나 그들은 모두 거기서, 짐승 우리 속의 짐승들만큼이나 기운도 목적도 없이, 빈둥대고 어슬렁대고 슬그머니 돌아다니고 있었다.\n\n이 산책로가 내려다보이는 창문에서 몸을 내밀고 있는 이들도 여럿이었으니, 어떤 이는 아래쪽 지인들과 시끌벅적 이야기를 나누고, 어떤 이는 바깥에서 과감하게 공을 던지는 자들과 공놀이를 하고, 어떤 이는 라켓 치는 자들을 구경하거나 점수를 외치는 소년들을 지켜보고 있었다. 더럽고 추레한 차림의 여인들이 마당 한구석의 취사장으로 오가며 지나치고 또 지나쳤다. 또 다른 구석에서는 아이들이 비명을 지르고, 싸우고, 함께 뛰놀았다. 스키틀이 쓰러지는 소리와 노는 자들의 고함이 이것들, 그리고 백 가지 다른 소리들과 끊임없이 뒤섞였다. 모든 것이 소음과 소란이었다 — 다만 몇 야드 떨어진 어느 초라한 작은 헛간만은 예외였으니, 거기에는 전날 밤에 죽은 형평법원 죄수의 시신이 온통 고요하고 섬뜩하게 누운 채, 검시라는 조롱극을 기다리고 있었다. 시신! 그것은, 살아 있는 사람을 이루는 그 쉴 새 없이 소용돌이치는 근심과 불안, 애정과 희망과 슬픔의 덩어리를 가리키는 법률가의 용어다. 법은 그의 시신을 차지했고, 그것은 거기, 수의를 입은 채 누워, 그 법의 자비로운 인정에 대한 끔찍한 증인이 되어 있었다.\n\n\"휘파람 가게를 한번 보시겠습니까, 나리?\" 잡 트로터가 물었다.\n\n\"그게 무슨 말인가?\" 픽윅 씨가 되물었다.\n\n\"휘파람 가게입니다, 나리.\" 웰러 씨가 끼어들었다.\n\n\"그게 뭐냐, 샘? 새 장수냐?\" 픽윅 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"천만에요, 나리.\" 잡이 대답했다. \"휘파람 가게란, 나리, 술을 파는 곳입죠.\" 여기서 잡 트로터 씨는, 모든 사람이 무거운 벌칙 아래 채무자 감옥으로 술을 들여오는 것이 금지되어 있고, 그런 물건이 갇혀 있는 신사 숙녀들에게 매우 귀히 여겨지는지라, 어느 약삭빠른 간수가 일정한 짭짤한 대가를 받고, 죄수 두세 명이 자기 이득과 잇속을 위해 인기 품목인 진을 소매로 파는 것을 눈감아 주기에 이르렀다고 간략히 설명했다.\n\n\"이 방식이, 보시다시피, 나리, 채무자 감옥 전체에 차츰 도입되었습죠.\" 트로터 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"게다가 이건 아주 큰 이점이 있지요.\" 샘이 말했다. \"간수들이, 그 못된 짓을 시도하는 자들 가운데 자기들한테 돈 바치는 자만 빼고 모조리 잡아들이는 데 아주 정성을 쏟는다는 거요. 그래서 그게 신문에 나면 그자들은 경계심이 투철하다고 칭송을 받지요. 그러니 일거양득이오 — 다른 자들이 그 장사를 못 하게 겁주고, 제 평판은 높이는 거지요.\"\n\n\"바로 그렇소, 웰러 씨.\" 잡이 말했다.\n\n\"한데, 이 방들은 술이 숨겨져 있는지 알아보려고 수색당하는 일이 결코 없는가?\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"물론 수색당하지요, 나리.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"하지만 간수들이 미리 알고 휘파람장이들한테 귀띔을 해 주니, 막상 찾으러 가면 헛물만 켜게 되지요.\"\n\n이쯤에서 잡이 어느 문을 두드리자, 헝클어진 머리의 신사가 문을 열었고, 그들이 안으로 걸어 들어가자 뒤에서 빗장을 지르고는 씩 웃었다. 그러자 잡도 씩 웃었고, 샘도 마찬가지였다. 이에 픽윅 씨도, 자기에게도 그런 것이 기대될지 모른다는 생각에, 면담이 끝날 때까지 줄곧 미소를 짓고 있었다.\n\n헝클어진 머리의 신사는 그들의 용건을 알리는 이 무언의 통고에 꽤나 만족한 듯, 침대틀 밑에서 두 쿼트쯤 들어갈 만한 납작한 돌병을 꺼내더니 진 세 잔을 따랐고, 잡 트로터와 샘은 그것을 더없이 능숙한 솜씨로 해치웠다.\n\n\"더 드릴까요?\" 휘파람장이 신사가 말했다.\n\n\"그만하면 됐소.\" 잡 트로터가 대답했다.\n\n픽윅 씨가 값을 치르자 문의 빗장이 풀렸고, 그들은 밖으로 나왔다. 헝클어진 머리의 신사는 마침 그때 지나가던 로커 씨에게 다정하게 고개를 끄덕여 보였다.\n\n이 자리에서 픽윅 씨는 모든 복도를 따라 거닐었고, 모든 계단을 오르내렸으며, 다시 한 번 마당 전체를 한 바퀴 돌았다. 감옥 인구의 대다수는 미빈스와 스맹글, 그리고 목사와 푸주한과 사기꾼이, 몇 번이고 거듭거듭 되풀이되는 듯했다. 어느 구석에서나, 가장 좋은 곳에서나 가장 나쁜 곳에서나 똑같이, 같은 추레함, 같은 소란과 소음, 같은 일반적인 특징들이 있었다. 그곳 전체가 쉴 새 없고 어수선해 보였고, 사람들은 불안한 꿈속의 그림자들처럼 이리저리 북적이며 어른거렸다.\n\n\"이만하면 충분히 보았다.\" 픽윅 씨가 자기의 작은 방에서 의자에 몸을 던지며 말했다. \"이런 광경들로 머리가 아프고, 가슴도 아프구나. 이제부터 나는 내 방 안의 죄수가 되겠다.\"\n\n그리고 픽윅 씨는 이 결심을 굳건히 지켰다. 석 달이라는 긴 세월 동안 그는 하루 종일 틀어박혀 지냈고, 다만 동료 죄수들 대부분이 잠자리에 들거나 제 방에서 흥청대고 있을 밤에만 공기를 쐬러 몰래 빠져나왔다. 갇힌 채 지내는 답답함에 그의 건강이 상하기 시작했으나, 퍼커와 그의 친구들이 거듭거듭 간청해도, 또 새뮤얼 웰러 씨가 그보다 더 자주 거듭한 경고와 충고도, 그의 굽힐 줄 모르는 결심을 털끝만큼도 바꾸게 하지는 못했다.\n\n7월이 다 저물기 한 주 전쯤, 번호는 기록되지 않은 어느 삯마차 한 대가 고스웰 거리를 빠른 속도로 올라가는 것이 보였다. 마차에는 마부 외에도 세 사람이 끼여 타고 있었는데, 마부는 옆쪽에 따로 마련된 자기만의 작은 마부석에 앉아 있었다. 가리개 위로는 두 장의 숄이 걸쳐져 있었고, 그것은 가리개 아래에 앉은 사납게 생긴 두 자그마한 여인의 것이었다. 그 두 여인 사이에는 무겁고 풀죽은 태도의 한 신사가 아주 비좁은 공간에 꾸겨넣어져 있었는데, 그는 어쩌다 한마디 입을 떼어 보려 할 때마다 앞서 말한 사나운 여인들 가운데 하나에게 단번에 말허리를 잘렸다. 마지막으로, 그 사나운 두 여인과 무거운 신사는 마부에게 서로 모순되는 지시들을 내리고 있었으나, 결국 모두 한 가지로 귀결되었으니, 바델 부인의 집 문 앞에 세우라는 것이었다. 다만 무거운 신사는 사나운 여인들과 정면으로 맞서며, 그것이 노란 문이 아니라 초록 문이라고 우겼다.\n\n\"마부 양반, 초록 문 있는 집에 세워 주시오.\" 무거운 신사가 말했다.\n\n\"오! 이 못된 양반아!\" 사나운 여인들 가운데 하나가 외쳤다. \"노란 문 있는 집으로 몰아요, 마부 양반.\"\n\n이 말에 마부는, 초록 문 있는 집 앞에 급히 세우려고 한껏 고삐를 잡아챈 나머지 말을 거의 마차 안으로 자빠뜨릴 뻔했다가, 다시 말의 앞다리를 땅바닥에 내려놓고는 멈춰 섰다.\n\n\"그래, 어디다 세우란 말이오?\" 마부가 물었다. \"자기들끼리 정하시구려. 내가 묻는 건 그저, 어디냐 그거요?\"\n\n여기서 다툼은 한층 더 격렬하게 다시 불붙었고, 말은 코에 앉은 파리 때문에 성가셔하니, 마부는 인정 깊게도 그 짬을 이용해, 자극을 다른 데로 돌린다는 원리에 따라 말의 머리를 채찍으로 후려갈겼다.\n\n\"표가 많은 쪽이 이기는 거지!\" 마침내 사나운 여인들 가운데 하나가 말했다. \"노란 문 있는 집이에요, 마부 양반.\"\n\n그러나 삯마차가 노란 문 있는 집 앞으로, 사나운 여인들 가운데 하나가 의기양양하게 말한 대로 \"마치 자기 마차를 타고 온 것보다도 더 요란한 소리를 내며\" 멋들어지게 달려가 멈춘 뒤, 마부가 여인들이 내리는 것을 거들려고 마부석에서 내려온 다음에야, 몇 집 떨어진 빨간 문 있는 집의 이 층 창밖으로 토머스 바델 도련님의 작고 둥근 머리가 쑥 내밀어졌다.\n\n\"기막힐 노릇이네!\" 마지막으로 언급된 사나운 여인이 무거운 신사에게 쏘아붙이는 눈길을 던지며 말했다.\n\n\"여보, 내 잘못이 아니잖소.\" 신사가 말했다.\n\n\"나한테 말 걸지 마요, 이 양반아, 걸지 말라고요.\" 여인이 되받았다. \"노란 문이 아니라 빨간 문 있는 집이에요, 마부 양반. 오! 세상에 어떤 여자가 이런 망나니 같은 양반 때문에 속을 썩었을까, 틈만 나면 낯선 사람들 앞에서 제 마누라 망신 주는 걸 자랑이자 낙으로 아는 양반이라니, 그게 바로 나라고요!\"\n\n\"부끄러운 줄 알아야지, 래들 양반.\" 다른 자그마한 여인이 말했는데, 그는 다름 아닌 클러핀스 부인이었다.\n\n\"내가 뭘 했다고 그러오?\" 래들 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"나한테 말 걸지 마요, 걸지 말라니까, 이 짐승 같은 양반아, 내가 성질을 못 이겨 여자 체면도 잊고 당신을 치기라도 할까 봐 그래요!\" 래들 부인이 말했다.\n\n이 대화가 오가는 동안, 마부는 더없이 창피스러운 모양새로 말굴레를 잡아끌며 빨간 문 있는 집으로 데려갔는데, 그 문은 이미 바델 도련님이 열어 놓은 터였다. 친구 집에 도착하는 데 이 얼마나 초라하고 격 떨어지는 방식이란 말인가! 말의 온갖 기세와 사나움을 안고 멋지게 달려와 멈추는 일도 없고, 마부가 펄쩍 뛰어내리는 일도 없고, 요란하게 문을 두드리는 일도 없고, 행여 여인들이 외풍을 맞을세라 맨 마지막 순간에 가리개를 와락 젖히는 일도 없고, 그런 다음 마부가 마치 전속 마부라도 되는 양 숄을 건네주는 일도 없었다! 멋이라는 멋은 죄다 깎여 나갔으니 — 차라리 걸어오느니만도 못한 밋밋한 도착이었다.\n\n\"자, 토미야,\" 클러핀스 부인이 말했다. \"네 가엾은 엄마는 어떠시니?\"\n\n\"아, 아주 잘 계세요.\" 바델 도련님이 대답했다. \"앞쪽 응접실에 다 차려 놓고 계세요. 나도 준비됐어요, 나도요.\" 여기서 바델 도련님은 두 손을 주머니에 찔러 넣고 문간 맨 아랫계단을 폴짝폴짝 오르내렸다.\n\n\"또 누가 가니, 토미야?\" 클러핀스 부인이 어깨걸이를 매만지며 말했다.\n\n\"샌더스 부인이 가요, 그분이요.\" 토미가 대답했다. \"나도 가요, 나도요.\"\n\n\"이 녀석 좀 봐.\" 자그마한 클러핀스 부인이 말했다. \"제 생각밖에 안 하네. 이리 와 보렴, 토미야.\"\n\n\"왜요?\" 바델 도련님이 말했다.\n\n\"또 누가 가니, 얘야?\" 클러핀스 부인이 살살 구슬리는 투로 물었다.\n\n\"아! 로저스 부인도 가요.\" 바델 도련님이 그 소식을 전하며 눈을 휘둥그레 뜨고 대답했다.\n\n\"뭐? 세든 그 부인 말이냐!\" 클러핀스 부인이 소리쳤다.\n\n바델 도련님은 두 손을 주머니에 더 깊숙이 찔러 넣고는, 그게 바로 세 들어 사는 그 부인이지 다른 사람이 아니라는 뜻으로, 정확히 서른다섯 번 고개를 끄덕였다.\n\n\"어머나!\" 클러핀스 부인이 말했다. \"이거 아주 잔치로구나!\"\n\n\"아, 찬장 안에 뭐가 있는지 아주머니가 안다면 그렇게 말할걸요.\" 바델 도련님이 대답했다.\n\n\"뭐가 있는데, 토미야?\" 클러핀스 부인이 어르듯이 말했다. \"나한테는 말해 주겠지, 토미야, 그렇지?\"\n\n\"아니요, 안 할래요.\" 바델 도련님이 고개를 저으며 대답하고는 다시 아랫계단으로 매달렸다.\n\n\"이 녀석 좀 봐!\" 클러핀스 부인이 중얼거렸다. \"어찌 이리 약 올리는 망나니람! 자, 토미야, 너의 다정한 클러피 아주머니한테 말해 주렴.\"\n\n\"엄마가 말하지 말랬어요.\" 바델 도련님이 받아쳤다. \"나도 좀 먹을 거예요, 나도요.\" 그런 전망에 신이 난 그 조숙한 소년은 한층 더 힘차게 제 어린애다운 발판 오르내리기에 몰두했다.\n\n이렇듯 어린아이를 상대로 캐묻는 일이 벌어지는 동안, 래들 씨 부부와 마부는 삯을 두고 옥신각신하고 있었는데, 그 다툼이 마부 쪽에 유리하게 마무리되자 래들 부인이 비틀거리며 다가왔다.\n\n\"어머, 메리 앤! 무슨 일이야?\" 클러핀스 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"온몸이 다 이렇게 떨려, 벳시.\" 래들 부인이 대답했다. \"래들은 사내답지가 못해. 뭐든지 다 나한테 떠넘긴다니까.\"\n\n이는 불운한 래들 씨에게 도무지 공정한 처사가 못 되었으니, 그는 다툼이 시작되자마자 그 착하신 부인에게 옆으로 떠밀려, 입 닥치고 있으라는 단호한 명을 받았던 터였다. 그러나 그는 변명할 기회조차 얻지 못했으니, 래들 부인이 영락없이 기절할 조짐을 보였기 때문이다. 그 광경을 응접실 창에서 본 바델 부인과 샌더스 부인, 세 든 부인과 그 부인의 하녀가 부리나케 뛰쳐나와, 마치 그녀가 이 세상에서 가장 고통받는 사람이기라도 한 양 저마다 동시에 떠들어 대며 갖은 동정과 위로의 말을 쏟아 내면서 그녀를 집 안으로 옮겼다. 앞쪽 응접실로 옮겨진 그녀는 거기 소파에 뉘어졌고, 이 층에 사는 부인이 이 층으로 달려 올라가더니 각성제 한 병을 들고 돌아와, 래들 부인의 목을 단단히 끌어안은 채 더없이 여성스러운 친절과 연민으로 그것을 그녀의 코에 갖다 댔으니, 마침내 그 부인은 몇 번이나 몸을 들썩이고 버둥거린 끝에 자기가 확실히 좀 나아졌다고 인정하지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n\"아, 가엾어라!\" 로저스 부인이 말했다. \"저분 심정이 어떨지 나도 너무 잘 알아요.\"\n\n\"아, 가엾어라! 나도 알지요.\" 샌더스 부인이 말했다. 그러자 모든 부인이 한목소리로 신음하며, 그게 어떤 심정인지 자기들도 안다고, 진심으로 그녀를 가엾게 여긴다고, 정말로 그렇다고 말했다. 열세 살에 키가 석 자밖에 안 되는 세 든 부인의 어린 하녀조차 동정의 말을 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"그런데 대체 무슨 일이 있었던 거예요?\" 바델 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"아, 부인, 뭐가 부인을 이리 흐트러뜨려 놓았나요?\" 로저스 부인이 물었다.\n\n\"제가 몹시 마음이 산란했답니다.\" 래들 부인이 나무라는 투로 대답했다. 그러자 부인들은 래들 씨에게 분개한 눈길을 던졌다.\n\n\"실은 말이죠,\" 그 불행한 신사가 앞으로 나서며 말했다. \"이 집 앞에서 우리가 내릴 때, 그 삯마차 모는 양반하고 다툼이 일어나서—\" 이 말이 나오자마자 그의 아내가 큰 소리로 비명을 지르는 바람에 그 뒤의 설명은 도무지 들리지 않게 되었다.\n\n\"래들 양반, 우리한테 맡기고 자리를 비워 주는 게 좋겠어요.\" 클러핀스 부인이 말했다. \"당신이 여기 있는 한 절대 나아지질 않을 테니까요.\"\n\n부인들 모두 이 의견에 동의했고, 그리하여 래들 씨는 방에서 떠밀려 나가 뒷마당에서 바람이나 쐬고 오라는 요청을 받았다. 그는 십오 분쯤 그렇게 했는데, 그 무렵 바델 부인이 엄숙한 얼굴로, 이제 들어와도 되지만 아내를 대할 때는 행동을 아주 조심해야 한다고 일러 주었다. 그가 불친절하게 굴려는 게 아닌 줄은 안다, 하지만 메리 앤은 결코 튼튼한 사람이 못 되니, 조심하지 않으면 전혀 뜻하지 않은 때에 그녀를 잃을 수도 있고, 그러면 두고두고 끔찍한 회한으로 남을 거라는 둥 그런 말이었다. 이 모든 말을 래들 씨는 더없이 고분고분하게 듣고는, 곧 더없이 양처럼 순한 태도로 응접실로 돌아왔다.\n\n\"어머, 로저스 부인,\" 바델 부인이 말했다. \"정말이지 아직 인사도 못 나누셨네요! 이쪽은 래들 씨예요, 부인. 이쪽은 클러핀스 부인이고요. 이쪽은 래들 부인이에요, 부인.\"\n\n\"클러핀스 부인의 언니 되시는 분이지요.\" 샌더스 부인이 덧붙였다.\n\n\"아, 그러시군요!\" 로저스 부인이 점잖게 말했다. 그도 그럴 것이 그녀는 세 든 부인이었고 그녀의 하녀까지 대기하고 있었으니, 자기 처지에 걸맞게 친근하기보다는 점잖은 편이었다. \"아, 그러시군요!\"\n\n래들 부인은 상냥하게 미소 지었고, 래들 씨는 고개를 숙여 인사했으며, 클러핀스 부인은 \"로저스 부인처럼 그토록 좋은 평판을 많이 들어 온 분과 알고 지낼 기회를 얻게 되어 참으로 기쁘다\"고 말했다. 마지막에 언급된 그 부인은 우아하게 겸양을 베풀며 그 인사치레를 받아들였다.\n\n\"자, 래들 양반,\" 바델 부인이 말했다. \"당신과 토미가 이렇게 많은 부인들을 햄프스테드의 스패니어즈까지 죽 모셔 가는 유일한 신사분들이니, 정말 무척 영광스럽게 여기셔야 할 거예요. 그렇지 않나요, 로저스 부인?\"\n\n\"아, 그럼요, 부인.\" 로저스 부인이 대답했고, 그 뒤를 따라 다른 부인들도 모두 \"아, 그럼요\" 하고 화답했다.\n\n\"물론 영광으로 여기고말고요, 부인.\" 래들 씨가 두 손을 비비며, 조금 기운이 살아나려는 기색을 보이며 말했다. \"사실 솔직히 말씀드리자면, 그 삯마차를 타고 오면서 제가 그랬지요—\"\n\n그토록 많은 괴로운 기억을 불러일으키는 그 말이 또다시 되풀이되자, 래들 부인은 다시 손수건을 눈가에 갖다 대며 반쯤 억누른 비명을 내질렀다. 그러자 바델 부인은 래들 씨에게 더는 아무 말도 하지 않는 게 좋겠다는 뜻으로 눈살을 찌푸려 보이고는, 짐짓 점잔을 빼며 로저스 부인의 하녀에게 \"포도주를 내오라\"고 일렀다.\n\n이는 찬장에 감추어 둔 보물들을 펼쳐 놓으라는 신호였으니, 거기에는 오렌지와 비스킷 몇 접시, 그리고 한 병에 일 실링 구 펜스짜리 묵은 포트와인 한 병과, 십사 펜스짜리 그 유명한 동인도산 셰리주 한 병이 들어 있었는데, 이 모두가 세 든 부인을 기려 내어진 것이었고 모두에게 한없는 만족을 안겨 주었다. 토미가, 지금 다들 마시고 있는 그 찬장에 관해 자기가 어떻게 캐물음을 당했는지 떠벌리려는 바람에 클러핀스 부인의 마음에 큰 동요가 일었으나(다행히도 토미가 묵은 포트와인 반 잔을 \"엉뚱한 데로\" 넘기는 바람에 몇 초간 목숨이 위태로워진 덕에 그 일은 싹부터 잘려 나갔다), 일행은 햄프스테드행 합승마차를 찾아 나섰다. 마차는 곧 찾아냈고, 두어 시간 만에 모두 무사히 스패니어즈 다원에 도착했다. 그런데 불운한 래들 씨가 거기서 맨 처음 한 행동이 하마터면 그 착하신 부인을 다시 도지게 할 뻔했으니, 그것은 다름 아니라 일곱 사람 몫의 차를 주문한 일이었다. 부인들이 너 나 할 것 없이 지적한 대로, 종업원이 안 볼 때 토미가 아무 잔에서나—그것뿐이라면 모든 잔에서—마시면 그만 아닌가, 그러면 차 한 사람 몫이 절약되고 차 맛도 똑같았을 텐데 말이다!\n\n그러나 어쩔 도리가 없었고, 차 쟁반이 일곱 개의 잔과 받침과, 거기에 맞춘 양의 버터 바른 빵과 함께 나왔다. 바델 부인이 만장일치로 좌장으로 추대되었고, 로저스 부인이 그녀의 오른편에, 래들 부인이 왼편에 자리를 잡으니, 차 모임은 큰 흥겨움과 성공 속에서 진행되었다.\n\n\"시골은 정말이지 어찌나 상쾌한지!\" 로저스 부인이 한숨지었다. \"차라리 늘 시골에서 살았으면 싶을 정도예요.\"\n\n\"오, 그건 마음에 안 드실 거예요, 부인.\" 바델 부인이 다소 황급히 대답했으니, 세입자 문제를 생각하면 그런 생각을 부추기는 일은 결코 바람직하지 않았던 것이다. \"마음에 안 드실 거예요, 부인.\"\n\n\"오! 부인은 너무 발랄하시고 찾는 분도 많으셔서, 시골에 만족하실 분이 아닌 것 같아요, 부인.\" 자그마한 클러핀스 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"그럴지도 모르죠, 부인. 그럴지도 모르죠.\" 이 층에 든 부인이 한숨지었다.\n\n\"자기를 아껴 줄 사람도, 보살펴 줄 사람도 없는 외로운 사람들이라든가, 마음에 상처를 입은 사람들이라든가, 그런 부류한테는요,\" 래들 씨가 조금 기운을 차리고 좌중을 둘러보며 말을 꺼냈다. \"시골이 아주 그만이지요. 시골은 상처받은 영혼을 위한 곳이라고들 하니까요.\"\n\n그런데 그 불운한 사내가 할 수 있는 세상의 온갖 말 가운데, 차라리 다른 어떤 말이라도 이보다는 나았을 것이다. 당연히 바델 부인은 울음을 터뜨리며 당장 식탁에서 데려가 달라고 청했고, 그러자 그 애틋한 아이도 더없이 처량하게 따라 울기 시작했다.\n\n\"세상에 누가 믿겠어요, 부인,\" 래들 부인이 이 층에 든 부인을 향해 사납게 돌아서며 외쳤다. \"여자가 이렇게 사내답지 못한 양반하고 결혼했다는 걸요, 하루도 빠짐없이 시도 때도 없이 여자의 마음을 함부로 들쑤셔 대는 양반하고 말예요, 부인?\"\n\n\"여보,\" 래들 씨가 항변했다. \"난 아무 뜻도 없었소, 여보.\"\n\n\"뜻이 없었다고요!\" 래들 부인이 잔뜩 비웃고 멸시하며 되받았다. \"저리 가요. 당신 꼴은 보기도 싫으니까, 이 짐승 같은 양반아.\"\n\n\"마음을 산란하게 하면 안 돼요, 메리 앤.\" 클러핀스 부인이 끼어들었다. \"정말이지 네 몸을 좀 생각해야지, 얘야, 넌 도무지 안 하니까. 자, 저리 가요, 래들 양반, 착한 양반이라면 말예요, 안 그러면 부인 속만 더 뒤집어 놓을 테니까.\"\n\n\"정말이지 차는 따로 드시는 게 좋겠어요, 선생님.\" 로저스 부인이 다시 각성제 병을 갖다 대며 말했다.\n\n평소 버릇대로 버터 바른 빵에 여념이 없던 샌더스 부인도 같은 의견을 내놓았고, 래들 씨는 조용히 물러갔다.\n\n이 일이 있은 뒤, 끌어안기에는 덩치가 제법 큰 편인 바델 도련님을 그 어머니의 품으로 끌어올리는 큰 작업이 벌어졌는데, 그 와중에 도련님의 장화가 차 쟁반에 닿는 바람에 잔과 받침 사이에 약간의 소동이 일었다. 그러나 부인들 사이에 전염되곤 하는 그런 종류의 기절 발작은 좀처럼 오래가지 않는 법, 그리하여 도련님이 실컷 입맞춤을 받고 그 위로 눈물도 조금 뿌려진 다음, 바델 부인은 정신을 차리고 그를 도로 내려놓으며 자기가 어쩌면 그리 어리석을 수 있었나 의아해하더니, 차를 좀 더 따랐다.\n\n바로 이 순간, 다가오는 바퀴 소리가 들려왔고, 부인들이 고개를 들어 보니 삯마차 한 대가 다원 정문 앞에 멈춰 서 있었다.\n\n\"손님이 또 오네!\" 샌더스 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"신사분이에요.\" 래들 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"어머, 저거 도드슨 앤드 포그 사무소의 젊은이, 잭슨 씨 아니에요!\" 바델 부인이 외쳤다. \"어머나, 세상에! 설마 픽윅 씨가 손해 배상금을 치른 건 아니겠죠.\"\n\n\"아니면 결혼 신청을 했다든가!\" 클러핀스 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"어쩌면, 저 신사분은 어찌 저리 굼뜰까.\" 로저스 부인이 외쳤다. \"왜 서두르질 않는 거람!\"\n\n부인이 이 말을 하는 동안, 잭슨 씨는 마차에서 몸을 돌렸으니, 그는 거기서 검은 각반을 두른 허름한 사내에게 몇 마디 건네던 참이었는데, 그 사내는 굵은 물푸레나무 지팡이를 손에 들고 막 마차에서 내린 터였다. 잭슨 씨는 부인들이 앉아 있는 곳으로 걸어오며, 오는 내내 모자챙에 머리카락을 빙빙 감았다.\n\n\"무슨 일이라도 있나요? 무슨 일이 생긴 거예요, 잭슨 씨?\" 바델 부인이 다급히 말했다.\n\n\"아무 일도 없습니다, 부인.\" 잭슨 씨가 대답했다. \"안녕하십니까, 부인들? 불쑥 끼어들어 송구합니다, 부인들—하지만 법이 그래서요, 부인들—법 때문에 말입니다.\" 이렇게 양해를 구하며 잭슨 씨는 미소 짓고, 좌중을 두루 향해 인사를 건네고는, 다시 한 번 머리카락을 빙 감았다. 로저스 부인은 래들 부인에게 그가 정말 우아한 청년이라고 귓속말을 했다.\n\n\"고스웰 거리에 들렀다가,\" 잭슨 씨가 말을 이었다. \"하녀한테서 부인이 여기 계시다는 말을 듣고, 마차를 잡아 곧장 달려왔습니다. 저희 사무소 분들이 부인께서 당장 시내로 와 주셨으면 하십니다, 바델 부인.\"\n\n\"어머나!\" 그 부인이 그 통보의 갑작스러움에 흠칫 놀라며 소리쳤다.\n\n\"네.\" 잭슨 씨가 입술을 깨물며 말했다. \"아주 중대하고 급한 일이라, 무슨 일이 있어도 미룰 수 없는 거랍니다. 정말이지 도드슨 씨가 저한테 분명히 그렇게 말씀하셨고, 포그 씨도 그러셨고요. 부인이 타고 돌아가시라고 일부러 마차를 잡아 두었습니다.\"\n\n\"참 이상도 하네요!\" 바델 부인이 외쳤다.\n\n부인들은 정말이지 이상한 일이라는 데 동의했으나, 한목소리로, 분명 아주 중대한 일일 거라고, 안 그러면 도드슨 앤드 포그가 사람을 보냈을 리 없다고 의견을 모았다. 더 나아가, 그 일이 급하니 부인은 지체 없이 도드슨 앤드 포그 사무소로 가야 한다고 했다.\n\n자기 변호사들이 그토록 어마어마하게 다급히 자기를 찾는다는 데에는 어느 정도 으쓱하고 대단해지는 느낌이 있어, 바델 부인에게는 결코 싫지 않은 일이었으니, 더구나 그것은 이 층에 든 부인의 눈에 자기 위상을 한층 높여 주리라고 충분히 짐작할 만했다. 그녀는 살짝 새침을 떨며, 짐짓 몹시 난처하고 망설이는 척하다가, 마침내 아무래도 가야겠다는 결론에 이르렀다.\n\n\"하지만 걸어오셨는데 뭐라도 좀 드시지 않겠어요, 잭슨 씨?\" 바델 부인이 권하듯이 말했다.\n\n\"실은 시간이 그리 많지 않습니다.\" 잭슨이 대답했다. \"게다가 일행이 한 사람 있어서요.\" 그는 물푸레나무 지팡이를 든 사내 쪽을 바라보며 말을 이었다.\n\n\"오, 일행분도 이리 오시라고 하세요, 선생님.\" 바델 부인이 말했다. \"부디 일행분도 이리로 청하세요, 선생님.\"\n\n\"아니요, 고맙습니다만, 그러지 않는 게 낫겠습니다.\" 잭슨 씨가 다소 거북한 태도로 말했다. \"저 친구가 부인들과 어울리는 데 그리 익숙지 않아서, 그러면 부끄러워한답니다. 종업원더러 독한 걸로 뭐든 갖다주라고 시키시면, 그 친구가 그걸 단번에 들이켜진 않을걸요, 안 그러겠어요!—한번 시험해 보세요!\" 잭슨 씨는 이 대목에서 손가락을 코 언저리로 장난스레 놀려, 자기가 빈정대는 말을 하고 있음을 듣는 이들에게 일러 주었다.\n\n종업원이 곧장 그 수줍은 신사에게 보내졌고, 수줍은 신사는 무언가를 받아 마셨다. 잭슨 씨도 무언가를 마셨고, 부인들도 접대하는 뜻에서 무언가를 마셨다. 그러자 잭슨 씨가 이제 갈 시간인 것 같다고 말했고, 그 말에 샌더스 부인과 클러핀스 부인과 토미가(이들은 바델 부인을 따라가고 나머지 사람들은 래들 씨의 보호에 맡기기로 정해진 터였다) 마차에 올랐다.\n\n\"아이작,\" 바델 부인이 막 마차에 오르려 할 때, 잭슨이 마부석에 앉아 시가를 피우던 물푸레나무 지팡이의 사내를 올려다보며 말했다.\n\n\"왜?\"\n\n\"이분이 바델 부인이오.\"\n\n\"아, 그건 진작에 알았소.\" 사내가 말했다.\n\n바델 부인이 올라타고, 그 뒤를 이어 잭슨 씨가 올라타니, 마차는 떠났다. 바델 부인은 잭슨 씨의 일행이 한 말을 곱씹어 보지 않을 수 없었다. 약빠른 사람들이지, 그 변호사들이란. 어머나, 어쩜 그리도 사람을 잘 알아본담!\n\n\"우리 사무소 분들 그 소송 비용 문제가 딱하게 됐어요, 안 그래요.\" 클러핀스 부인과 샌더스 부인이 잠들자 잭슨이 말했다. \"부인의 소송 비용 청구서 말입니다.\"\n\n\"그분들이 그걸 못 받게 돼서 정말 안됐어요.\" 바델 부인이 대답했다. \"하지만 변호사 양반들이 이런 일을 투기 삼아 하는 거라면, 이따금 손해도 봐야 하는 거지요, 아시다시피요.\"\n\n\"재판이 끝난 뒤에 부인이 소송 비용 액수만큼 채무 인정서를 그분들한테 써 주셨다고 들었는데요!\" 잭슨이 말했다.\n\n\"네. 그저 형식상으로요.\" 바델 부인이 대답했다.\n\n\"그럼요.\" 잭슨이 무덤덤하게 대답했다. \"순전히 형식이지요. 순전히요.\"\n\n* 채무 인정서: 원어는 cognovit. 피고가 원고의 청구를 인정하여 법적 집행이 가능하도록 작성하는 문서.\n마차는 계속 달렸고, 바델 부인은 잠이 들었다. 얼마쯤 지난 뒤, 그녀는 마차가 멈추는 바람에 잠에서 깨어났다.\n\n\"어머나!\" 부인이 말했다. \"여기가 프리먼스 코트인가요?\"\n\n\"거기까지 가는 건 아닙니다.\" 잭슨이 대답했다. \"어서 내리시지요.\"\n\n아직 잠이 채 깨지 않은 바델 부인은 시키는 대로 했다. 묘한 곳이었다. 커다란 담벼락 한가운데에 문이 하나 나 있고, 그 안쪽에 가스등이 타고 있었다.\n\n\"자, 부인들,\" 물푸레나무 지팡이의 사내가 마차 안을 들여다보며, 샌더스 부인을 흔들어 깨우면서 외쳤다. \"어서요!\" 친구를 흔들어 깨운 샌더스 부인이 내렸다. 바델 부인은 잭슨의 팔에 기대고 토미의 손을 잡은 채, 이미 현관에 들어선 뒤였다. 부인들이 그 뒤를 따랐다.\n\n그들이 들어선 방은 현관보다도 더 괴상한 모양새였다. 그렇게나 많은 사내들이 서성거리고 있다니! 게다가 어찌나 빤히 쳐다보던지!\n\n\"여기가 무슨 곳이죠?\" 바델 부인이 멈춰 서며 물었다.\n\n\"그저 저희 관청 가운데 하나일 뿐입니다.\" 잭슨이 대답하며 그녀를 어느 문으로 황급히 밀어넣고는, 다른 부인들이 따라오는지 둘러보았다. \"서둘러, 아이작!\"\n\n\"무사히 다 데려왔소.\" 물푸레나무 지팡이의 사내가 대답했다. 문이 그들 뒤로 묵직하게 닫혔고, 그들은 짤막한 계단을 내려갔다.\n\n\"마침내 다 왔습니다. 만사형통, 바델 부인!\" 잭슨이 의기양양하게 둘러보며 말했다.\n\n\"무슨 말이에요?\" 바델 부인이 가슴이 두근거리며 말했다.\n\n\"바로 이런 겁니다.\" 잭슨이 그녀를 한쪽으로 살짝 끌어당기며 대답했다. \"겁먹지 마세요, 바델 부인. 도드슨 씨보다 더 섬세한 분도, 포그 씨보다 더 인정 많은 분도 세상에 없답니다, 부인. 그분들로서는 업무상, 그 소송 비용 때문에 부인을 잡아들이는 게 의무였지요. 하지만 그분들은 되도록이면 부인의 심정을 헤아려 드리려고 무척 마음을 쓰셨답니다. 일이 어떻게 처리됐는지 생각하면 부인께 얼마나 위안이 될지요! 여기는 플리트입니다, 부인. 안녕히 주무세요, 바델 부인. 잘 자라, 토미!\"\n\n잭슨이 물푸레나무 지팡이의 사내와 함께 황급히 자리를 뜨는 사이, 곁에서 지켜보던 또 다른 사내가, 손에 열쇠를 들고서, 어리둥절해하는 그 여인을 어느 문간으로 이어지는 두 번째 짤막한 계단으로 이끌었다. 바델 부인은 격렬하게 비명을 질렀고, 토미는 울부짖었고, 클러핀스 부인은 잔뜩 움츠러들었으며, 샌더스 부인은 더 따질 것도 없이 줄행랑을 쳤다. 거기에는 억울하게 당한 픽윅 씨가 밤마다의 산책으로 바람을 쐬며 서 있었고, 그 곁에는 새뮤얼 웰러가 기대어 있었던 것이다. 새뮤얼은 바델 부인을 보자 짐짓 공손한 척 모자를 벗었고, 한편 그의 주인은 분개하여 홱 발길을 돌렸다.\n\n\"저 여자분을 괴롭히지 마시오.\" 간수가 웰러에게 말했다. \"방금 들어온 사람이오.\"\n\n\"죄수라고요!\" 샘이 얼른 다시 모자를 쓰며 말했다. \"원고가 누구요? 무슨 죄목으로요? 어서 말해 보쇼, 영감님.\"\n\n\"도드슨 앤드 포그요.\" 사내가 대답했다. \"소송 비용에 대한 채무 인정서에 따른 집행이오.\"\n\n\"이봐, 잡, 잡!\" 샘이 복도로 뛰어들며 소리쳤다. \"퍼커 씨한테 달려가, 잡. 그분이 당장 필요해. 이거 뭔가 잘 풀릴 것 같단 말이야. 한바탕 볼만하겠는걸. 만세! 주인 어른은 어디 계시오?\"\n\n그러나 이 물음들에는 아무 대답이 없었으니, 잡은 임무를 받자마자 부리나케 달려 나간 뒤였고, 바델 부인은 정말로 영락없이 기절해 버린 뒤였다.\n\n잡 트로터는 조금도 속도를 늦추지 않고 홀번 거리를 내달렸는데, 길의 각 구역마다 남자와 여자, 아이들, 그리고 마차들이 붐비는 정도에 따라 빠져나갈 틈이 달라졌으므로, 때로는 길 한복판으로, 때로는 인도 위로, 때로는 도랑 속으로 뛰었고, 온갖 장애물에 아랑곳하지 않은 채 그레이즈 인의 문에 다다를 때까지 한순간도 멈추지 않았다. 그러나 그토록 서둘렀음에도 그가 도착했을 때 문은 이미 족히 삼십 분 전에 닫혀 있었고, 게다가 퍼커 씨의 세탁부를 찾아냈을 무렵에는—그 세탁부는 어느 출퇴근하는 웨이터에게 손을 내준 시집간 딸과 함께 살았고, 그 웨이터는 그레이즈 인 레인 뒤편 어딘가 어느 양조장에 바싹 붙은 어느 거리 어느 번지의 이층 단칸방을 차지하고 있었다—밤을 위해 감옥이 닫히기까지 십오 분도 채 남지 않은 시각이었다. 게다가 매그파이 앤드 스텀프의 뒷방에서 라우튼 씨를 또 끄집어내야 했는데, 잡이 겨우 이 일을 해내고 샘 웰러의 전갈을 전했을 때 시계가 열 시를 쳤다.\n\n\"이런,\" 라우튼이 말했다. \"이젠 너무 늦었어. 오늘 밤엔 못 들어가. 자넨 거리 열쇠를 받은 셈이군, 친구.\"\n\n\"저는 신경 쓰지 마십시오,\" 잡이 대답했다. \"저야 어디서든 잘 수 있습니다. 하지만 오늘 밤에 퍼커 씨를 뵙는 편이 낫지 않겠습니까? 그래야 아침에 제일 먼저 거기 가 있을 수 있을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"음,\" 라우튼이 잠시 생각한 끝에 대답했다. \"다른 사람 일이라면 퍼커 씨는 내가 자기 집까지 찾아오는 걸 그리 달가워하지 않을 거야. 하지만 픽윅 씨 일이니, 마차를 한 대 잡아서 사무소 앞으로 청구해도 괜찮겠지.\" 이렇게 행동 방침을 정한 라우튼 씨는 모자를 집어 들고, 모여 있던 사람들에게 자신이 잠시 자리를 비운 동안 부의장을 한 명 뽑으라고 청한 뒤, 가장 가까운 마차 정류장으로 앞장섰다. 가장 그럴듯해 보이는 마차를 불러 세운 그는 마부에게 러셀 광장 몬터규 플레이스로 가라고 일렀다.\n\n퍼커 씨는 그날 만찬 모임을 열고 있었는데, 응접실 창에 불빛이 보이는 것이며, 거기서 흘러나오는 어느 고급 그랜드 피아노 소리와 더 갈고닦을 여지가 있는 어느 실내악풍 목소리, 그리고 계단과 현관에 자욱이 퍼진 다소 압도적인 고기 냄새가 이를 증언하고 있었다. 사실인즉슨, 매우 좋은 지방 대리점 두 곳이 마침 동시에 상경하는 바람에, 그들을 맞이하기 위해 유쾌하고 자그마한 모임이 꾸려졌던 것이다. 그 자리에는 생명보험사무소 비서인 스닉스 씨, 저명한 법정 변호사 프로지 씨, 사무 변호사 세 사람, 파산 관재인 한 명, 템플 소속의 특별 변호사 한 명, 그리고 그의 제자인 눈이 작고 단호한 젊은 신사가 있었는데, 이 제자는 부동산 양도법에 관해 방대한 분량의 방주와 참조를 곁들인 발랄한 책을 쓴 자였다. 그 밖에도 저명하고 명망 높은 여러 인사들이 있었다. 이 모임에서 작달막한 퍼커 씨는 자기 서기가 왔다는 전갈을 귓속말로 듣고 슬그머니 빠져나와, 식당으로 가서 라우튼 씨와 잡 트로터가 부엌 양초 불빛 아래 매우 흐릿하고 그림자처럼 서 있는 것을 발견했다. 그 양초는, 분기마다 받는 봉급을 위해 플러시 반바지와 무명 양말 차림으로 황송하게 등장하시는 신사 양반이 서기를 비롯해 '사무소'에 관련된 모든 것에 대한 합당한 경멸을 담아 탁자 위에 놓아둔 것이었다.\n\n\"자, 라우튼,\" 작달막한 퍼커 씨가 문을 닫으며 말했다. \"무슨 일인가? 소포에 중요한 편지라도 온 건 아니겠지?\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 나리,\" 라우튼이 대답했다. \"이 사람은 픽윅 씨가 보낸 전령입니다, 나리.\"\n\n\"픽윅한테서, 응?\" 작달막한 사내가 재빨리 잡 쪽으로 돌아서며 말했다. \"그래, 무슨 일인가?\"\n\n\"도드슨과 포그가 소송 비용 때문에 바델 부인을 구금했습니다, 나리,\" 잡이 말했다.\n\n\"설마!\" 퍼커가 두 손을 주머니에 찔러 넣고 찬장에 기대며 소리쳤다.\n\n\"사실입니다,\" 잡이 말했다. \"재판이 끝나자마자 그 비용 액수에 대한 채무 인낙서를 부인한테서 받아냈던 모양입니다.\"\n\n\"세상에!\" 퍼커가 두 손을 주머니에서 빼내며, 오른손 마디를 왼손 손바닥에 힘주어 탁 치며 말했다. \"내가 여태 상대해 본 자들 중에 가장 영악한 악당들이로군!\"\n\n\"제가 알기로 가장 약삭빠른 자들입니다, 나리,\" 라우튼이 한마디 보탰다.\n\n\"약삭빠르다!\" 퍼커가 되받았다. \"어디서 그들의 꼬리를 잡아야 할지 도무지 알 수가 없다니까.\"\n\n\"맞는 말씀입니다, 나리, 정말 그렇습니다,\" 라우튼이 대답했다. 그러고는 주인과 하인이 둘 다 몇 초 동안 생기 도는 얼굴로 골똘히 생각에 잠겼는데, 마치 인간의 지성이 일찍이 이뤄 낸 가장 아름답고 기발한 발견 가운데 하나를 음미하고 있는 듯했다. 어느 정도 그 감탄의 황홀경에서 깨어났을 때, 잡 트로터는 나머지 임무를 마저 전했다. 퍼커는 생각에 잠긴 듯 고개를 끄덕이더니 회중시계를 꺼냈다.\n\n\"정각 열 시에 거기로 가겠네,\" 작달막한 사내가 말했다. \"샘이 아주 옳아. 그렇게 전해. 라우튼, 포도주 한 잔 들겠나?\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 고맙습니다, 나리.\"\n\n\"내 생각엔 그렇다는 뜻인 것 같은데,\" 작달막한 사내가 술병과 잔을 가지러 찬장 쪽으로 돌아서며 말했다.\n\n라우튼이 정말로 그렇다는 뜻이었으므로, 그는 그 일에 대해 더 말하지 않고, 들릴 만한 귓속말로 잡에게, 벽난로 맞은편에 걸린 퍼커 씨의 초상화가 놀랄 만큼 닮지 않았느냐고 물었고, 잡은 물론 그렇다고 대답했다. 이때쯤 포도주가 따라졌고, 라우튼은 퍼커 부인과 아이들을 위해, 잡은 퍼커를 위해 건배했다. 플러시 반바지와 무명 양말 차림의 신사 양반은 사무소에서 온 사람들을 배웅하는 것이 자기 임무의 일부가 아니라고 여겨 한결같이 초인종에 응하기를 거부했고, 그래서 그들은 알아서 나갔다. 변호사는 응접실로, 서기는 매그파이 앤드 스텀프로, 그리고 잡은 코번트 가든 시장으로 가서 채소 바구니 속에서 밤을 보냈다.\n\n이튿날 아침, 약속한 시각 정각에 그 쾌활하고 작달막한 변호사가 픽윅 씨의 방문을 두드렸고, 샘 웰러가 매우 민첩하게 그 문을 열었다.\n\n\"퍼커 씨가 오셨습니다, 나리,\" 샘이 말하며, 생각에 잠긴 자세로 창가에 앉아 있던 픽윅 씨에게 손님을 알렸다. \"우연히 들러 주셔서 정말 기쁩니다, 나리. 제 짐작에 어르신께서 나리와 한두 마디 나누고 싶어 하시는 것 같습니다, 나리.\"\n\n퍼커는 샘에게 알겠다는 눈짓을 보냈는데, 자기가 부름을 받고 왔다는 말을 해서는 안 된다는 걸 알아들었다는 뜻이었다. 그러고는 샘에게 가까이 오라고 손짓하더니 그의 귀에 짤막하게 속삭였다.\n\n\"설마 정말 그러신 건 아니죠, 나리?\" 샘이 너무 놀란 나머지 뒤로 펄쩍 물러서며 말했다.\n\n퍼커는 고개를 끄덕이며 미소를 지었다.\n\n새뮤얼 웰러 씨는 작달막한 변호사를 보다가, 다음엔 픽윅 씨를, 다음엔 천장을, 다음엔 다시 퍼커를 보더니, 씩 웃다가 큰 소리로 웃음을 터뜨렸고, 마침내 더 이상의 설명도 없이 양탄자에서 모자를 집어 들고는 사라져 버렸다.\n\n\"이게 무슨 뜻이오?\" 픽윅 씨가 놀라서 퍼커를 바라보며 물었다. \"무엇이 샘을 저런 별난 상태로 만든 거요?\"\n\n\"오, 아무것도 아닙니다, 아무것도,\" 퍼커가 대답했다. \"자, 친애하는 선생, 의자를 탁자 쪽으로 당기시지요. 드릴 말씀이 꽤 많습니다.\"\n\n\"그 서류들은 뭐요?\" 픽윅 씨가 물었다. 작달막한 사내가 빨간 끈으로 묶인 작은 서류 뭉치를 탁자 위에 내려놓던 참이었다.\n\n\"바델 대 픽윅 사건의 서류입니다,\" 퍼커가 이로 매듭을 풀며 대답했다.\n\n픽윅 씨는 의자 다리를 바닥에 박박 긁으며 끌었고, 의자에 털썩 몸을 던지고 두 손을 맞잡은 채 자신의 법률가 친구를 엄하게—픽윅 씨가 엄하게 볼 수 있는 사람이라면—노려보았다.\n\n\"그 사건 이름을 듣는 게 싫으신 게로군요?\" 작달막한 사내가 여전히 매듭과 씨름하며 말했다.\n\n\"그렇소, 정말이지 싫소,\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"그것 참 안됐습니다,\" 퍼커가 말을 이었다. \"바로 그게 우리 대화의 주제가 될 테니까요.\"\n\n\"그 주제는 우리 사이에 결코 입에 올리지 않았으면 하오, 퍼커,\" 픽윅 씨가 황급히 끼어들었다.\n\n\"쯧쯧, 친애하는 선생,\" 작달막한 사내가 뭉치를 풀며, 눈 가장자리로 픽윅 씨를 간절히 곁눈질하며 말했다. \"이 얘긴 꼭 해야 합니다. 일부러 이러려고 온걸요. 자, 제 말을 들으실 준비가 되셨습니까, 친애하는 선생? 급할 거 없습니다. 준비가 안 되셨다면 기다릴 수 있어요. 여기 오늘 아침 신문이 있으니까요. 선생 시간에 맞추겠습니다. 자, 보십시오!\" 그러면서 작달막한 사내는 한쪽 다리를 다른 쪽 다리 위에 포개고, 자못 침착하고 열성적으로 신문을 읽기 시작하는 시늉을 했다.\n\n\"알았소, 알았어,\" 픽윅 씨가 한숨을 쉬면서도 동시에 미소로 누그러지며 말했다. \"할 말이 있으면 하시오. 늘 하던 얘기겠지, 아마?\"\n\n\"조금 다릅니다, 친애하는 선생. 조금 달라요,\" 퍼커가 신문을 천천히 접어 다시 주머니에 넣으며 대꾸했다. \"이 소송의 원고인 바델 부인이 지금 이 담장 안에 있습니다, 선생.\"\n\n\"알고 있소,\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"좋습니다,\" 퍼커가 받아쳤다. \"그럼 그 부인이 어떻게 여기 오게 됐는지도 아시겠지요? 무슨 사유로, 누구의 소송으로 왔는지 말입니다.\"\n\n\"그렇소. 적어도 샘이 그 일을 설명하는 걸 들었소,\" 픽윅 씨가 짐짓 무심한 척하며 말했다.\n\n\"샘의 설명은,\" 퍼커가 대답했다. \"감히 말씀드리건대 완벽하게 정확한 것입니다. 자, 친애하는 선생, 제가 여쭤야 할 첫 번째 질문은, 이 여자가 여기 그대로 머물러야 하느냐는 것입니다.\"\n\n\"여기 그대로 머문다고!\" 픽윅 씨가 되뇌었다.\n\n\"여기 그대로 머문다는 것입니다, 친애하는 선생,\" 퍼커가 의자에 등을 기대고 자기 의뢰인을 가만히 바라보며 대꾸했다.\n\n\"어찌 내게 그걸 묻소?\" 그 신사가 말했다. \"그건 도드슨과 포그에게 달린 일이오. 당신도 그건 잘 알지 않소.\"\n\n\"전 그런 건 전혀 모르겠습니다,\" 퍼커가 단호히 받아쳤다. \"그건 도드슨과 포그에게 달린 일이 아닙니다. 친애하는 선생, 선생도 그자들을 저만큼 잘 아시잖습니까. 그건 오로지, 전적으로, 온전히 선생께 달린 일입니다.\"\n\n\"내게!\" 픽윅 씨가 신경질적으로 의자에서 벌떡 일어났다가 곧바로 다시 앉으며 내뱉었다.\n\n작달막한 사내는 코담뱃갑 뚜껑을 두 번 똑똑 두드리고는 갑을 열어 코담배를 한 줌 듬뿍 집은 뒤 다시 닫고, \"선생께\"라는 말을 되풀이했다.\n\n\"제 말씀은, 친애하는 선생,\" 코담배에서 자신감을 얻은 듯한 작달막한 사내가 말을 이었다. \"제 말씀은, 그 여자가 곧 풀려나느냐 영영 갇혀 있느냐가 선생께, 오직 선생께만 달려 있다는 것입니다. 부디 제 말을 끝까지 들어 주십시오, 친애하는 선생. 그리고 그렇게 흥분하지 마십시오. 땀만 나고 아무 도움도 안 됩니다. 제 말씀은,\" 퍼커가 한 가지 논점을 꺼낼 때마다 손가락을 하나씩 꼽으며 말을 이었다. \"이 비참함의 소굴에서 그 여자를 구해 낼 수 있는 사람은 선생밖에 없다는 것입니다. 그리고 선생께서 그렇게 하실 수 있는 유일한 방법은, 이 소송 비용—원고 측과 피고 측 모두의 비용—을 저 프리먼 코트의 상어 같은 자들 손에 치르는 것뿐입니다. 자, 부디 진정하십시오, 친애하는 선생.\"\n\n이 말이 이어지는 동안 픽윅 씨의 얼굴은 놀라우리만치 다양하게 변해 갔고, 분명 격한 분노가 터질락 말락 한 지경이었으나, 그는 있는 힘껏 노여움을 가라앉혔다. 퍼커는 코담배를 한 줌 더 집어 논변의 힘을 보강한 뒤 말을 이었다.\n\n\"오늘 아침에 제가 그 여자를 만났습니다. 비용을 치르면, 손해 배상에 대한 완전한 면제와 청산을 얻을 수 있습니다. 게다가—이건 분명 선생께서 훨씬 더 중하게 여기실 바이지만—그 여자가 친필 서명을 한 자발적 진술서, 그러니까 제게 보내는 편지 형식의 진술서를 받을 수 있습니다. 그 내용인즉, 이 사건은 애초부터 도드슨과 포그라는 자들에 의해 부추겨지고 조장되고 꾸며진 것이라는 점, 그 여자가 선생을 괴롭히고 해를 끼치는 도구가 되었던 것을 깊이 뉘우친다는 점, 그리고 제게 선생을 중재해 달라, 선생의 용서를 구해 달라 간청한다는 점입니다.\"\n\n\"내가 그 여자의 비용을 대신 치러 준다면 말이지,\" 픽윅 씨가 분개해 말했다. \"참으로 값진 문서로군!\"\n\n\"이 경우엔 '~한다면'이란 게 없습니다, 친애하는 선생,\" 퍼커가 의기양양하게 말했다. \"여기 바로 제가 말씀드린 그 편지가 있습니다. 오늘 아침 아홉 시에, 제가 이 자리에 발을 들이거나 바델 부인과 어떤 연락도 주고받기 전에, 다른 여자가 제 사무소로 가져온 것입니다. 명예를 걸고 맹세합니다.\" 작달막한 변호사는 뭉치에서 그 편지를 골라내 픽윅 씨의 팔꿈치 옆에 놓고는, 눈도 깜빡이지 않은 채 꼬박 이 분 동안 코담배를 들이마셨다.\n\n\"내게 할 말이라는 게 이게 다요?\" 픽윅 씨가 부드럽게 물었다.\n\n\"꼭 그렇진 않습니다,\" 퍼커가 대답했다. \"이 순간에 제가 장담할 수는 없습니다만, 채무 인낙서의 문구, 표면상의 약인의 성격, 그리고 이 소송의 전 과정에 관해 우리가 그러모을 수 있는 증거가, 공모 혐의로 기소하기에 충분할지 어떨지는 모르겠습니다. 안 될까 걱정입니다, 친애하는 선생. 그러기엔 그자들이 너무 영악하니까요, 아무래도. 하지만 이것만은 말씀드리겠습니다. 모든 사실을 종합해 보면, 분별 있는 모든 사람의 마음속에서 선생을 정당화하기에 충분하다는 것을요. 자, 친애하는 선생, 이제 솔직히 여쭙겠습니다. 이 백오십 파운드, 혹은 얼마가 됐든—어림수로 잡아서요—그건 선생께 아무것도 아닙니다. 배심원단이 선생께 불리한 평결을 내렸지요. 그래요, 그들의 평결은 잘못됐습니다만, 그래도 그들은 옳다고 생각한 대로 결정한 것이고, 그건 어쨌든 선생께 불리합니다. 지금 선생께는 손쉬운 조건으로, 여기 그냥 머무는 것으로는 결코 이를 수 없는 훨씬 더 높은 위치에 자신을 세울 기회가 있습니다. 여기 머무는 것은, 선생을 모르는 사람들 눈에는 그저 완고하고 고집스럽고 무지막지한 외곬으로밖에 비치지 않을 겁니다. 다른 그 무엇도 아니에요, 친애하는 선생, 제 말을 믿으십시오. 이 기회를 잡으시는 데 망설일 까닭이 있겠습니까? 그것은 선생을 친구들에게, 옛 일들에게, 건강과 즐거움에게 되돌려 줍니다. 또 그것은 선생의 충실하고 헌신적인 하인을—그렇지 않으면 선생께서 평생 옥살이를 선고하시는 셈인 그 하인을—풀어 줍니다. 그리고 무엇보다도, 그것은 선생께 더없이 도량 넓은 복수를—제가 알기로, 친애하는 선생, 이건 바로 선생 마음에 꼭 드는 종류의 것입니다만—할 수 있게 해 줍니다. 곧, 이 여자를 비참함과 타락의 현장에서 풀어 주는 일 말입니다. 제 뜻대로라면 그 어떤 남자도 그런 곳에 보내져선 안 되거니와, 하물며 여자에게 그것을 가하는 일은 한층 더 끔찍하고 야만적이지요. 자, 친애하는 선생, 이제 여쭙겠습니다. 선생의 법률 고문으로서뿐 아니라 진정한 벗으로서 여쭙는 것입니다만, 이 모든 목적을 이루고 이 모든 선을 행할 이 기회를, 고작 몇 파운드가 두 악당의 주머니로 흘러 들어가는 것을 막겠다는 그 하찮은 이유로 흘려보내시겠습니까? 그 몇 파운드란 그자들에겐 아무런 차이도 만들지 못합니다. 그저 더 많이 챙길수록 더 많이 탐낼 테고, 그러다 그만큼 더 빨리 어떤 악행에 빠져들어 결국 파국으로 끝날 뿐이지요. 친애하는 선생, 제가 이 여러 사정을 매우 서툴고 불완전하게 말씀드렸습니다만, 부디 곰곰이 생각해 보시기를 청합니다. 마음속에서 얼마든지 오래 굴려 보십시오. 저는 여기서 더없이 참을성 있게 선생의 대답을 기다리겠습니다.\"\n\n픽윅 씨가 미처 대답하기도 전에, 그리고 퍼커 씨가 이토록 유난히 긴 연설 뒤에 반드시 뒤따라야 할 코담배의 이십분의 일도 채 들이마시기 전에, 밖에서 나직한 웅성거림이 일더니 머뭇거리는 노크 소리가 문에서 났다.\n\n\"어허, 이런,\" 친구의 호소에 분명히 마음이 움직였던 픽윅 씨가 소리쳤다. \"저 문이란 게 어찌 이리 성가신지! 거 누구요?\"\n\n\"접니다, 나리,\" 샘 웰러가 머리를 들이밀며 대답했다.\n\n\"지금은 자네와 얘기할 수 없네, 샘,\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"지금 바쁜 참일세, 샘.\"\n\n\"죄송합니다, 나리,\" 웰러 씨가 대꾸했다. \"하지만 여기 어떤 부인이 와 계신데요, 나리, 아주 특별히 털어놓을 게 있다고 하십니다.\"\n\n\"어떤 부인도 만날 수 없네,\" 머릿속이 바델 부인의 환영으로 가득한 픽윅 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"그건 너무 장담 마십시오, 나리,\" 웰러 씨가 고개를 저으며 다그쳤다. \"누가 가까이 와 있는지 아신다면, 나리, 제 생각엔 곡조를 바꾸실 것 같습니다요. 매가 모퉁이 돌아서 울새가 지저귀는 소리를 듣고는 흐뭇하게 웃으며 혼잣말하던 것처럼 말입니다.\"\n\n\"그게 누군가?\" 픽윅 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"만나 보시겠습니까, 나리?\" 웰러 씨가 마치 문 저쪽에 무슨 신기한 산짐승이라도 있는 양 문을 손으로 잡은 채 물었다.\n\n\"만나야겠지, 아무래도,\" 픽윅 씨가 퍼커를 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"그럼, 다들 들어와서 시작합시다!\" 샘이 외쳤다. \"징을 울리고, 막을 걷고, 두 공모자께서 등장하시오.\"\n\n샘 웰러가 이렇게 말하면서 문을 활짝 열어젖히자, 너새니얼 윈클 씨가 와락 방 안으로 뛰어들어 왔는데, 그 뒤로 어느 젊은 숙녀의 손을 잡아 이끌고 있었다. 바로 딩글리 델에서 윗부분에 털을 두른 부츠를 신었던 그 숙녀로, 이제는 발그레한 홍조와 수줍음, 연보랏빛 비단과 멋진 보닛, 그리고 값진 레이스 베일이 어우러진 더없이 사랑스러운 모습으로, 어느 때보다도 예뻐 보였다.\n\n\"아라벨라 앨런 양!\" 픽윅 씨가 의자에서 일어서며 소리쳤다.\n\n\"아닙니다,\" 윈클 씨가 무릎을 꿇으며 대답했다. \"윈클 부인입니다. 용서하십시오, 친애하는 친구여, 용서해 주십시오!\"\n\n픽윅 씨는 자기 감각이 일러 주는 바를 좀처럼 믿을 수가 없었고, 퍼커의 미소 짓는 얼굴이 보태 주는 뒷받침의 증언과, 뒤편에 실제로 와 있는 샘과 예쁜 하녀의 모습이 아니었다면 아마 믿지 않았을 것이다. 샘과 하녀는 이 일이 벌어지는 것을 더없이 흐뭇하게 지켜보고 있는 듯했다.\n\n\"오, 픽윅 씨!\" 아라벨라가 그 침묵에 겁이라도 난 듯 나직한 목소리로 말했다. \"제 경솔함을 용서해 주실 수 있나요?\"\n\n픽윅 씨는 이 호소에 말로는 아무런 대답도 하지 않았으나, 황급히 안경을 벗고는 젊은 숙녀의 두 손을 자기 손에 잡아 여러 번 입을 맞추었는데—어쩌면 꼭 필요한 것보다 더 여러 번이었을 것이다—그러고는 여전히 그녀의 한 손을 쥔 채, 윈클 씨더러 대담한 강아지 같은 놈이라며 어서 일어나라고 일렀다. 몇 초 동안 참회하는 태도로 모자 챙으로 코를 긁어 대던 윈클 씨가 일어서자, 픽윅 씨는 그의 등을 여러 번 철썩 두드린 뒤 퍼커와 진심으로 악수를 나누었다. 퍼커도 이런 경사의 인사에 뒤지지 않으려고, 신부와 예쁜 하녀에게 한껏 호의를 담아 인사를 건넸으며, 윈클 씨의 손을 더없이 정답게 꽉 쥔 뒤, 평범하게 생긴 코를 가진 사내 대여섯쯤은 평생 재채기하게 만들 만큼의 코담배를 들이마시는 것으로 기쁨의 표현을 마무리했다.\n\n\"이런, 얘야,\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"이게 다 어찌 된 일이냐? 자! 앉아라, 그리고 다 들려다오. 정말 좋아 보이지 않소, 퍼커?\" 픽윅 씨가 마치 아라벨라가 제 딸이라도 되는 양 자랑스럽고 의기양양한 눈빛으로 그녀의 얼굴을 살피며 덧붙였다.\n\n\"근사합니다, 친애하는 선생,\" 작달막한 사내가 대답했다. \"저도 유부남만 아니라면, 자네를 시샘하고 싶을 지경이야, 이 능청맞은 친구.\" 이렇게 말하면서 작달막한 변호사는 윈클 씨의 가슴을 쿡 찔렀고, 윈클 씨도 그대로 되갚았다. 그러고는 둘 다 매우 큰 소리로 웃었으나, 마침 찬장 문 뒤에서 예쁜 하녀에게 입을 맞춰 자기 감정을 막 풀어낸 새뮤얼 웰러 씨만큼 크지는 않았다.\n\n\"제가 아무리 감사해도 모자랄 거예요, 샘, 정말이에요,\" 아라벨라가 더없이 다정한 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"클리프턴 정원에서 애써 주신 일을 잊지 않을게요.\"\n\n\"그런 건 아무 말씀 마십시오, 마님,\" 샘이 대답했다. \"전 그저 자연을 거들어 드렸을 뿐인걸요, 마님. 의사가 사내아이를 피 빼다가 죽여 놓고 그 어미한테 한 말처럼요.\"\n\n\"메리, 얘야, 앉으렴,\" 픽윅 씨가 이 인사치레들을 끊으며 말했다. \"자, 그래. 결혼한 지는 얼마나 됐느냐, 응?\"\n\n아라벨라가 수줍게 자기 주인이자 남편을 바라보자, 그가 대답했다. \"고작 사흘입니다.\"\n\n\"고작 사흘이라고, 응?\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"이런, 그럼 지난 석 달 동안엔 대체 뭘 한 게냐?\"\n\n\"아, 그러게나 말일세!\" 퍼커가 끼어들었다. \"어디, 그 빈둥거린 까닭을 좀 대 보게나. 보다시피 픽윅 씨가 놀라는 건 다름 아니라, 그게 벌써 몇 달 전에 다 끝나지 않았느냐는 것이거든.\"\n\n\"실은 이렇습니다,\" 윈클 씨가 발그레해진 어린 아내를 바라보며 대답했다. \"벨라를 함께 달아나자고 설득하는 데 오랜 시간이 걸렸습니다. 그리고 겨우 설득한 뒤에도, 기회를 잡기까지 또 한참이 걸렸지요. 메리도 옆집 일자리를 그만두려면 한 달 전에 미리 알려야 했는데, 그녀의 도움 없이는 도저히 해낼 수가 없었거든요.\"\n\n\"이런, 참,\" 픽윅 씨가 소리쳤다. 이때쯤 그는 다시 안경을 쓰고서, 따뜻한 마음과 친절한 정이 인간의 얼굴에 전할 수 있는 온갖 기쁨을 표정 가득 담은 채, 아라벨라에서 윈클로, 윈클에서 다시 아라벨라로 번갈아 보고 있었다. \"이런, 참! 자네들 꽤 빈틈없이 일을 꾸민 모양이로군. 그래, 얘야, 네 오라버니는 이걸 다 알고 있느냐?\"\n\n\"오, 아니에요, 아니에요,\" 아라벨라가 안색을 바꾸며 대답했다. \"친애하는 픽윅 씨, 오라버니는 오직 선생님을 통해서만—오직 선생님 입을 통해서만 알아야 해요. 오라버니는 워낙 불같고, 워낙 편견이 심한 데다, 자기 친구 소여 씨를 위해서 어찌나—어찌나 마음을 써 왔는지,\" 아라벨라가 고개를 떨구며 덧붙였다. \"그래서 그 결과가 정말 끔찍하게 두려워요.\"\n\n\"아, 그렇고말고,\" 퍼커가 진지하게 말했다. \"이 일은 선생께서 저 사람들을 위해 떠맡으셔야 합니다, 친애하는 선생. 저 젊은이들은 다른 누구의 말도 듣지 않을 때조차 선생은 존중할 겁니다. 화를 미연에 막으셔야 합니다, 친애하는 선생. 뜨거운 피, 뜨거운 피니까요.\" 그러면서 작달막한 사내는 경계의 코담배를 한 줌 집어 들이마시고는 미심쩍은 듯 고개를 저었다.\n\n\"잊으셨군요, 얘야,\" 픽윅 씨가 부드럽게 말했다. \"내가 죄수 신세라는 걸 잊은 게로구나.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 정말이지 잊지 않았어요, 친애하는 선생님,\" 아라벨라가 대답했다. \"한 번도 잊은 적 없어요. 이 끔찍한 곳에서 선생님께서 얼마나 큰 고초를 겪으셨을지 늘 생각해 왔는걸요. 다만, 선생님 자신을 위해서는 결코 하지 않으실 일을, 저희의 행복을 헤아리는 마음에서라면 해 주실까 바랐던 거예요. 만약 제 오라버니가 이 일을 선생님께 먼저 듣게 된다면, 저희가 화해하리라고 확신해요. 오라버니는 이 세상에서 제 유일한 혈육이에요, 픽윅 씨. 선생님께서 저를 위해 변호해 주시지 않는다면, 저는 그 오라버니마저 잃은 게 아닐까 두려워요. 제가 잘못했어요, 정말, 정말 잘못한 거 알아요.\" 이쯤에서 가엾은 아라벨라는 손수건에 얼굴을 묻고 서럽게 울었다.\n\n픽윅 씨의 천성은 바로 이 눈물에 적잖이 마음이 흔들렸다. 그러나 윈클 부인이 눈물을 닦고는, 더없이 고운 목소리의 가장 달콤한 어조로 어르고 간청하기 시작하자, 그는 유난히 안절부절못하며, 어찌해야 할지 갈피를 못 잡는 기색이 역력했는데, 안경알이며 코, 타이츠, 머리, 각반을 초조하게 자꾸 문지르는 모습이 이를 잘 드러냈다.\n\n이 결단을 못 내리는 기색을 틈타, 퍼커 씨는—그날 아침 이 젊은 부부가 곧장 그에게로 마차를 몰고 왔던 모양이다—법률가다운 정연함과 빈틈없음으로 다음과 같이 다그쳤다. 곧, 아버지 윈클 씨는 아들이 인생의 계단에서 밟아 올라간 그 중대한 진전을 아직 모르고 있다는 것, 위에 말한 아들의 장래 기대는 전적으로 위에 말한 아버지 윈클 씨가 그를 줄어듦 없는 애정과 친애의 정으로 계속 대해 주느냐에 달려 있는데, 이 큰 일이 오래도록 비밀에 부쳐진다면 그가 그러기는 매우 어려우리라는 것, 픽윅 씨가 앨런 씨를 찾아 브리스틀로 가실 수 있다면, 같은 이치로 아버지 윈클 씨를 찾아 버밍엄으로도 가실 수 있으리라는 것, 끝으로, 아버지 윈클 씨는 픽윅 씨를 어느 정도 자기 아들의 후견인이자 조언자로 여길 만한 충분한 권리와 자격이 있으며, 따라서 그 신사가 위에 말한 아버지 윈클 씨를 직접, 입으로, 이 사건의 모든 정황과 자신이 이 일에 맡았던 몫에 관해 알려 드리는 것이 마땅하고, 실로 그 인품에 비추어 당연한 도리라는 것이었다.\n\n이 변론이 이쯤 진행되었을 때, 더없이 알맞게도 터프먼 씨와 스노드그래스 씨가 도착했고, 그들에게 그동안 일어난 모든 일과 더불어 찬반 양쪽의 갖가지 이유를 설명해야 했으므로 논의가 처음부터 다시 한 차례 되풀이되었으며, 그 뒤로는 저마다 자기 방식대로, 자기가 원하는 만큼 길게 온갖 논거를 다그쳤다. 그리고 마침내 픽윅 씨는 자신의 모든 결심을 깨끗이 논파당하고 따져 물림당한 끝에, 이러다 제정신마저 논파당해 잃을 위기에 임박하자, 아라벨라를 품에 끌어안으며, 그녀가 참으로 사랑스러운 사람이라는 것, 어찌 된 영문인지 자신도 모르겠으나 처음부터 늘 그녀를 무척 아껴 왔다는 것을 밝히고는, 젊은이들의 행복을 가로막을 마음은 도저히 들지 않는다며, 자네들 마음대로 나를 부려도 좋다고 말했다.\n\n이 양보를 듣고 웰러 씨가 맨 먼저 한 일은, 잡 트로터를 그 명망 높은 펠 씨에게로 급파하여, 이 편지를 지닌 자에게 정식 석방 서류를 내어 주라는 위임장을 전한 것이었다. 그 석방 서류란, 그의 신중한 부친이 만일 어느 때고 비상시에 필요할 경우에 대비해 선견지명으로 그 박식한 신사의 손에 미리 맡겨 둔 것이었다. 그가 다음으로 한 일은, 가진 현금을 몽땅 털어 순한 흑맥주 스물다섯 갤런을 사들인 뒤, 라켓 운동장에서 마시고자 하는 모든 이에게 손수 따라 돌린 것이었다. 이를 마치고 나서 그는 건물 여기저기를 돌며 목이 쉴 때까지 만세를 부르고는, 조용히 평소의 침착하고 사색적인 상태로 되돌아갔다.\n\n그날 오후 세 시에 픽윅 씨는 자신의 자그마한 방을 마지막으로 둘러본 뒤, 앞다투어 밀려들어 그와 악수하려는 채무자들 무리를 헤치며 가까스로 길을 내어 마침내 수위실 계단에 이르렀다. 그는 거기서 걸음을 멈추고 주위를 둘러보았고, 그렇게 둘러보는 동안 그의 눈이 환해졌다. 창백하고 야윈 얼굴들로 가득한 그 군중 속에서, 그의 동정과 자선 덕분에 더 행복해지지 않은 얼굴은 단 하나도 보이지 않았던 것이다.\n\n\"퍼커,\" 픽윅 씨가 한 젊은이를 손짓해 부르며 말했다. \"이 사람이 내가 당신에게 말했던 그 징글이오.\"\n\n\"좋습니다, 친애하는 선생,\" 퍼커가 징글을 빤히 쳐다보며 대답했다. \"젊은이, 내일 나를 다시 보게 될 걸세. 내가 자네에게 전할 말을, 부디 오래 살아 깊이 새기고 절감하기를 바라네.\"\n\n징글은 공손히 고개를 숙였고, 픽윅 씨가 내민 손을 잡을 때 몹시 떨더니, 물러갔다.\n\n\"잡은 자네도 아는 사이지, 아마?\" 픽윅 씨가 그 신사를 소개하며 말했다.\n\n\"그 악당이라면 알지요,\" 퍼커가 너그럽게 대답했다. \"자네 친구를 잘 돌보고, 내일 한 시에 나타나도록 하게. 알겠나? 자, 그 밖에 더 있습니까?\"\n\n\"없소,\" 픽윅 씨가 대꾸했다. \"샘, 자네 옛 주인 양반에게 전하라고 내가 준 그 작은 꾸러미는 전했나?\"\n\n\"전했습니다, 나리,\" 샘이 대답했다. \"그분이 울음을 터뜨리시더니, 나리가 아주 후하고 사려 깊은 분이라며, 다만 자기를 급성 폐병에 걸리게 좀 해 주셨으면 좋겠다고 하셨습니다요. 이리 오래 같이 살던 옛 친구가 죽고 나니, 새 친구를 어디서 찾아야 할지 도무지 막막하다면서요.\"\n\n\"가엾은 사람, 가엾은 사람!\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"다들 신의 가호가 있기를, 친구들!\"\n\n픽윅 씨가 이 작별 인사를 입에 올리자, 군중은 우렁찬 함성을 질렀다. 그들 가운데 많은 이가 다시 그와 악수하려고 앞으로 밀려들고 있었으나, 그는 자기 팔을 퍼커의 팔에 끼고는 감옥을 서둘러 빠져나왔는데, 그 순간만큼은 처음 그곳에 들어왔을 때보다 훨씬 더 슬프고 우울했다. 아아, 그가 뒤에 남겨 두고 떠난 슬프고 불행한 사람들이 얼마나 많았던가!\n\n조지 앤드 벌처에서 적어도 한 무리에게는 그날 저녁이 행복한 저녁이었다. 그리고 이튿날 아침 그 정겨운 문을 나선 두 사람의 마음은 가볍고 명랑했다. 그 주인공은 픽윅 씨와 샘 웰러였는데, 앞 사람은 이내 안락한 역마차 안에 자리 잡았고, 뒤쪽에는 작은 마부석이 달려 있어 뒷사람이 그 위에 날렵하게 올라탔다.\n\n\"나리,\" 웰러 씨가 주인을 향해 소리쳤다.\n\n\"왜 그러나, 샘,\" 픽윅 씨가 창밖으로 머리를 내밀며 대답했다.\n\n\"저 말들이 플리트에서 석 달 넘게 갇혀 있었더라면 좋았겠습니다요, 나리.\"\n\n\"어째서, 샘?\" 픽윅 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"아, 나리,\" 웰러 씨가 두 손을 비비며 외쳤다. \"그랬다면 저놈들이 얼마나 잘 내달리겠습니까요!\"\n\n벤 앨런 씨와 보브 소여 씨는 가게 뒤편의 작은 진료실에 함께 앉아 다진 송아지 고기와 앞으로의 전망을 두고 이야기를 나누고 있었는데, 화제는 자연스럽게도 앞서 말한 보브가 일궈 놓은 진료와, 그가 몸 바친 그 명예로운 직업에서 넉넉한 자립을 끌어낼 그의 현재 가능성으로 옮겨 갔다.\n\n\"내 생각에는 말이지,\" 보브 소여 씨가 화제의 실마리를 이어 가며 말했다. \"내 생각에는, 벤, 그게 좀 미심쩍단 말이야.\"\n\n\"뭐가 좀 미심쩍다는 거야?\" 벤 앨런 씨가 맥주 한 모금으로 자기 지력을 가다듬으며 물었다. \"뭐가 미심쩍다는 거지?\"\n\n\"아니, 그 가능성 말이야.\" 보브 소여 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"잊어버렸군,\" 벤 앨런 씨가 말했다. \"맥주를 보니 내가 잊고 있었던 게 떠오르는걸, 보브—그래, 그것들은 미심쩍지.\"\n\n\"가난한 사람들이 나를 얼마나 끔찍이도 찾아 주는지 놀라울 정도야,\" 보브 소여 씨가 생각에 잠긴 듯 말했다. \"한밤중 아무 때나 나를 두들겨 깨우지, 약은 내가 도저히 불가능하다 여겼을 만큼 잔뜩 먹어 대고, 발포고와 거머리를 더 나은 일에나 어울릴 끈기로 붙여 대고, 또 식구는 정말 무시무시한 방식으로 불려 댄단 말이야. 마지막에 말한 그 자그마한 약속어음이 여섯 장이나 되는데, 죄다 같은 날짜에 만기가 돌아오고, 죄다 나한테 맡겨졌단 말이야, 벤!\"\n\n\"참 흐뭇한 일 아닌가?\" 벤 앨런 씨가 다진 송아지 고기를 좀 더 달라고 접시를 내밀며 말했다.\n\n\"오, 아주 그렇지,\" 보브가 대답했다. \"다만 한두 실링쯤 여윳돈이 있는 환자들의 신뢰만큼 흐뭇하진 않을 뿐이야. 이 사업은 광고에서 기막히게 잘 묘사돼 있었지, 벤. 진료, 그것도 아주 광범위한 진료야—그게 다라네.\"\n\n\"보브,\" 벤 앨런 씨가 나이프와 포크를 내려놓고 친구의 얼굴에 눈길을 박으며 말했다. \"보브, 내가 어떻게 된 건지 말해 주지.\"\n\n\"어떻게 된 건데?\" 보브 소여 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"자네는 되도록 지체 없이 어래벨러의 천 파운드를 손에 넣어야 해.\"\n\n\"잉글랜드 은행 총재 회사의 장부, 또는 장부들에 지금 그녀 이름으로 올라 있는 3퍼센트 통합 은행 연금 말이지,\" 보브 소여가 법률 용어로 덧붙였다.\n\n\"바로 그거야,\" 벤이 말했다. \"성년이 되거나 결혼하면 그 돈이 그녀 차지가 돼. 성년까지는 한 해가 모자라지만, 자네가 기운만 좀 내면 결혼까지는 한 달도 모자랄 게 없어.\"\n\n\"그녀는 아주 매력적이고 즐거운 사람이지,\" 로버트 소여 씨가 대답했다. \"그리고 내가 아는 한 흠이라곤 하나뿐이야, 벤. 불행히도 그 하나뿐인 결점이 안목이 없다는 거지. 그녀가 나를 좋아하질 않거든.\"\n\n\"그 애는 자기가 뭘 좋아하는지도 모른다는 게 내 생각이야,\" 벤 앨런 씨가 경멸하듯 말했다.\n\n\"그럴지도 모르지,\" 보브 소여 씨가 말했다. \"하지만 그녀가 뭘 안 좋아하는지는 안다는 게 내 생각이고, 그게 더 중요한 문제거든.\"\n\n\"내 소원은 말이야,\" 벤 앨런 씨가 이를 악물고, 나이프와 포크로 다진 송아지 고기를 먹는 점잖은 청년이라기보다 손가락으로 잘라 낸 날 늑대 고기를 뜯어 먹는 야만 전사처럼 말했다. \"어떤 악당이라도 정말로 그 애를 꾀어내려 들고, 그 애 마음을 사로잡으려 했는지 알아내는 거야. 그런 놈이라면 내가 암살해 버려야겠지, 보브.\"\n\n\"내가 그놈을 찾아내면 총알을 한 방 박아 넣겠어,\" 소여 씨가 맥주를 길게 들이켜다 말고 멈추어, 흑맥주 잔 너머로 사납게 노려보며 말했다. \"그래도 끝장이 안 나면, 그 뒤에 총알을 도로 꺼내서 그 방법으로 죽여 버리지.\"\n\n벤저민 앨런 씨는 몇 분 동안 말없이 멍하니 친구를 바라보다가 이윽고 입을 열었다—\n\n\"자네 한 번도 그 애한테 단도직입으로 청혼한 적이 없지, 보브?\"\n\n\"없어. 소용없을 게 뻔히 보였으니까,\" 로버트 소여 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"자네는 스물네 시간이 더 지나기 전에 그렇게 해야 해,\" 벤이 필사적인 침착함으로 받아쳤다. \"그 애는 자네 사람이 되든지, 아니면 내가 그 까닭을 알아내고 말 거야. 내 권위를 발휘하겠어.\"\n\n\"음,\" 보브 소여 씨가 말했다. \"두고 보자고.\"\n\n\"두고 보세, 이 사람아,\" 벤 앨런 씨가 사납게 대답했다. 그는 몇 초간 말을 멈추었다가, 감정에 북받쳐 갈라진 목소리로 덧붙였다. \"자네는 어릴 때부터 그 애를 사랑했지, 이 사람아. 우리가 함께 학교에 다니던 소년 시절에도 자네는 그 애를 사랑했어. 그런데 그때조차 그 애는 변덕스럽게도 자네의 어린 마음을 무시했지. 기억나나, 어린아이의 사랑이 지닌 온갖 열성으로, 어느 날 작은 캐러웨이 씨 비스킷 두 개와 달콤한 사과 한 개를 공책 잎으로 동그랗게 곱게 싸서 그 애더러 받으라고 들이밀었던 일이?\"\n\n\"기억나지,\" 보브 소여가 대답했다.\n\n\"그 애가 그걸 무시했지, 아마?\" 벤 앨런이 말했다.\n\n\"그랬지,\" 보브가 받았다. \"내가 그 꾸러미를 코듀로이 바지 주머니에 너무 오래 넣고 다녀서 사과가 불쾌하게 미지근해졌다고 그러더군.\"\n\n\"기억나,\" 앨런 씨가 침울하게 말했다. \"그래서 우리 둘이 번갈아 한 입씩 베어 먹어 치웠지.\"\n\n보브 소여는 방금 언급된 그 정황을 기억한다는 뜻을 우울한 찡그림으로 내비쳤고, 두 친구는 한동안 각자 자기 상념에 잠겨 있었다.\n\n보브 소여 씨와 벤저민 앨런 씨 사이에 이런 말이 오가는 동안, 그리고 회색 제복을 입은 소년이 평소와 달리 식사가 길어지는 것을 의아해하며 이따금 유리문 쪽으로 불안한 눈길을 던지면서, 결국 자기 몫의 갈망에 돌아올 다진 송아지 고기의 양을 두고 마음속 불안에 시달리고 있는 동안, 브리스틀 거리를 따라 점잖게 굴러가는 것이 있었으니, 그것은 음울한 초록빛으로 칠한 개인용 마차였다. 통통한 갈색 말이 끌고, 다리는 마부 종자의 다리처럼 차려입었으되 몸통에는 마부의 외투를 걸친 무뚝뚝한 인상의 남자가 몰고 있었다. 이런 행색은 검소한 살림을 하는 노부인들이 소유하고 건사하는 많은 탈것에서 흔한 것이었고, 이 탈것에는 그 주인이자 소유주인 노부인이 타고 있었다.\n\n\"마틴!\" 노부인이 앞쪽 창밖으로 무뚝뚝한 남자를 부르며 말했다.\n\n\"예?\" 무뚝뚝한 남자가 노부인에게 모자에 손을 대며 말했다.\n\n\"소여 씨 댁으로.\" 노부인이 말했다.\n\n\"가던 참입니다.\" 무뚝뚝한 남자가 말했다.\n\n노부인은 무뚝뚝한 남자의 이 선견지명이 그녀의 마음에 안겨 준 만족을 끄덕임으로 표했고, 무뚝뚝한 남자가 통통한 말에게 채찍을 매섭게 한 번 휘두르자 그들 모두는 함께 보브 소여 씨 댁으로 향했다.\n\n\"마틴!\" 마차가 옛 노케모프 가게의 로버트 소여 씨 댁 문 앞에 멈추자 노부인이 말했다.\n\n\"예?\" 마틴이 말했다.\n\n\"저 아이더러 나와서 말을 보라고 해.\"\n\n\"제가 직접 말을 보겠습니다,\" 마틴이 채찍을 마차 지붕 위에 올려놓으며 말했다.\n\n\"그건 무슨 일이 있어도 허락할 수 없네,\" 노부인이 말했다. \"자네 증언이 아주 중요할 테니 자네를 데리고 안으로 들어가야 해. 면담이 진행되는 내내 내 곁을 떠나서는 안 돼. 알아듣겠나?\"\n\n\"알아듣습니다,\" 마틴이 대답했다.\n\n\"그래, 뭘 꾸물거리는 게야?\"\n\n\"아무것도 아닙니다,\" 마틴이 대답했다. 그렇게 말하면서 무뚝뚝한 남자는 오른발 발끝으로 균형을 잡고 올라타 있던 바퀴에서 느긋이 내려와, 회색 제복의 소년을 불러 놓고 마차 문을 열고 발판을 내린 다음, 짙은 색 섀미 가죽 장갑을 낀 손을 들이밀어, 마치 그녀가 무슨 모자 상자라도 되는 양 태연한 태도로 노부인을 끌어냈다.\n\n\"어머나!\" 노부인이 외쳤다. \"이제 막상 여기 도착하고 나니, 마틴, 마음이 어찌나 어수선한지 온몸이 떨리는구나.\"\n\n마틴 씨는 짙은 색 섀미 가죽 장갑 뒤에서 기침을 했을 뿐 아무 동정도 내비치지 않았으므로, 노부인은 마음을 추슬러 보브 소여 씨 댁 계단을 종종걸음으로 올라갔고 마틴 씨가 뒤따랐다. 노부인이 가게에 들어서기가 무섭게, 술과 물을 안 보이는 데로 치우고 담배 연기 냄새를 없애려 역겨운 약품을 엎질러 두던 벤저민 앨런 씨와 보브 소여 씨가 기쁨과 애정에 들떠 황급히 나왔다.\n\n\"사랑하는 이모님,\" 벤 앨런 씨가 외쳤다. \"저희를 들여다봐 주시다니 어찌나 친절하신지요! 소여 씨입니다, 이모님. 제가 이모님께 말씀드렸던 제 친구 보브 소여 씨인데, 바로—아시잖아요, 이모님.\" 그러고는 마침 그리 멀쩡한 정신이 아니었던 벤 앨런 씨가 \"어래벨러\"라는 말을 속삭인다고 한 것이, 실은 누구라도 마음만 먹으면 안 들을 도리가 없을 만큼 유난히 또렷하고 분명한 어조로 덧붙였다.\n\n\"사랑하는 벤저민,\" 노부인이 심한 숨 가쁨과 씨름하고 머리끝부터 발끝까지 떨면서 말했다. \"놀라지 마라, 얘야, 하지만 내 생각에는 잠깐 소여 씨와 단둘이 이야기하는 게 좋을 것 같구나. 잠깐이면 돼.\"\n\n\"보브,\" 앨런 씨가 말했다. \"이모님을 진료실로 모셔다드리겠나?\"\n\n\"물론이지,\" 보브가 더없이 직업적인 목소리로 대답했다. \"이쪽으로 오시지요, 사모님. 겁내지 마세요, 사모님. 아주 짧은 시간 안에 멀쩡하게 해 드릴 수 있을 겁니다, 틀림없이요, 사모님. 자, 이쪽입니다, 사모님. 그럼!\" 이렇게 말하며 보브 소여 씨는 노부인을 의자에 앉히고 문을 닫은 다음, 다른 의자를 그녀 가까이 끌어다 놓고, 앞으로 길게 이어질 이득과 혜택의 행렬이 내다보이는 어떤 질환의 증상이 상세히 설명되기를 기다렸다.\n\n노부인이 맨 처음 한 일은 고개를 여러 번 가로젓는 것이었고, 그러더니 울기 시작했다.\n\n\"신경성이군요,\" 보브 소여가 흐뭇하게 말했다. \"장뇌 물약과 물을 하루 세 번, 그리고 밤에는 진정제를 드시지요.\"\n\n\"어떻게 말을 꺼내야 할지 모르겠네요, 소여 씨,\" 노부인이 말했다. \"너무나 괴롭고 가슴 아픈 일이라서요.\"\n\n\"말씀을 꺼내실 필요 없습니다, 사모님,\" 보브 소여 씨가 받았다. \"하실 말씀을 제가 다 짐작할 수 있으니까요. 머리에 탈이 난 거지요.\"\n\n\"심장이 아니길 정말 바라고 있어요,\" 노부인이 가벼운 신음과 함께 말했다.\n\n\"그럴 위험은 조금도 없습니다, 사모님,\" 보브 소여가 대답했다. \"위장이 일차적 원인이지요.\"\n\n\"소여 씨!\" 노부인이 흠칫하며 외쳤다.\n\n\"조금도 의심할 여지가 없습니다, 사모님,\" 보브가 놀랍도록 현명한 표정을 지으며 받았다. \"제때에 약을 드셨더라면, 사모님, 이 모든 걸 막을 수 있었을 텐데요.\"\n\n\"소여 씨,\" 노부인이 아까보다 더 어수선해진 채 말했다. \"이런 태도는 나 같은 처지의 사람에게 큰 무례이거나, 아니면 선생이 내 방문의 목적을 이해하지 못한 데서 비롯된 거예요. 약의 힘으로든, 내가 쓸 수 있었던 어떤 선견지명으로든 벌어진 일을 막을 수 있었다면, 나는 분명히 그렇게 했을 거예요. 차라리 곧장 조카를 만나 보는 게 낫겠어요,\" 노부인이 분개하여 손지갑을 빙빙 돌리며 말하더니, 말하면서 일어섰다.\n\n\"잠깐만요, 사모님,\" 보브 소여가 말했다. \"제가 사모님 말씀을 이해하지 못한 듯합니다. 무슨 일이신가요, 사모님?\"\n\n\"제 조카딸 말이에요, 소여 씨,\" 노부인이 말했다. \"선생 친구의 누이동생요.\"\n\n\"예, 사모님,\" 보브가 잔뜩 안달이 나서 말했다. 노부인이, 몹시 동요한 와중에도 노부인들이 흔히 그러듯 사람 애간장을 태울 만큼 느릿느릿 말했기 때문이다. \"예, 사모님.\"\n\n\"사흘 전에, 소여 씨, 제 집을 떠났어요. 제 여동생, 그 애한테는 또 다른 이모인데, 세 번째 이정표 바로 너머에서 큰 기숙학교를 하는, 아주 커다란 금사슬나무 한 그루와 떡갈나무 대문이 있는 그곳을 찾아간다는 핑계로 말이지요,\" 노부인이 여기서 말을 멈추고 눈물을 닦았다.\n\n\"오, 금사슬나무 따위 악마한테나 가라죠, 사모님!\" 보브가 안달이 난 나머지 직업적 위엄을 까맣게 잊고 말했다. \"좀 빨리 진행하시지요. 증기를 좀 더 올려 주세요, 사모님, 제발.\"\n\n\"오늘 아침에,\" 노부인이 느릿느릿 말했다. \"오늘 아침에 그 애가—\"\n\n\"돌아왔겠지요, 사모님, 아마?\" 보브가 잔뜩 활기를 띠며 말했다. \"돌아왔나요?\"\n\n\"아니요, 안 돌아왔어요. 편지를 보냈답니다,\" 노부인이 대답했다.\n\n\"뭐라고 썼던가요?\" 보브가 다급하게 물었다.\n\n\"이렇게 썼더군요, 소여 씨,\" 노부인이 대답했다. \"—그리고 이것이야말로 제가 벤저민의 마음을 부드럽게, 차근차근 준비시키고 싶은 바인데요. 그 애가 쓰기를 자기가—편지는 주머니에 들어 있어요, 소여 씨, 하지만 안경이 마차에 있어서, 안경 없이 그 대목을 짚어 드리려 한다면 선생 시간만 허비하게 될 거예요. 한마디로, 소여 씨, 그 애가 결혼했다고 썼답니다.\"\n\n\"뭐라고요!\" 보브 소여 씨가 말했다기보다는 고함을 질렀다.\n\n\"결혼했다고요,\" 노부인이 되풀이했다.\n\n보브 소여 씨는 더 들으려 멈추지 않고, 진료실에서 바깥 가게로 뛰쳐나가 우렁찬 목소리로 외쳤다. \"벤, 이 친구야, 그 애가 달아났어!\"\n\n머리를 무릎보다 한 반 자쯤 아래로 떨군 채 계산대 뒤에서 졸고 있던 벤 앨런 씨는 이 소름 끼치는 전갈을 듣자마자 마틴 씨에게 곧장 돌진하여, 그 과묵한 종자의 목수건에 손을 비틀어 넣고는, 그 자리에서 그를 목 졸라 버리겠다는 친절한 의향을 표명했다. 이 의향을, 흔히 절망에서 비롯되는 그 신속함으로 그는 곧장, 대단한 기운과 외과적 솜씨로 실행에 옮기기 시작했다.\n\n말수가 적고 웅변이나 설득의 힘이라고는 거의 없는 사람이었던 마틴 씨는, 몇 초 동안은 아주 침착하고 흡족한 표정으로 이 수술을 받아들였다. 그러나 그것이 머지않아 자신이 앞으로 영영 어떤 품삯이나, 식비든 무엇이든 청구할 수 없게 만들 결과로 이어질 조짐이 보이자, 그는 알아들을 수 없는 항의를 중얼거리며 벤저민 앨런 씨를 바닥에 메다꽂았다. 그 신사는 손이 그의 목도리에 엉켜 있었으므로 그를 따라 바닥에 함께 쓰러지는 수밖에 없었다. 둘이 거기 누워 뒤엉켜 다투고 있는데 가게 문이 열리더니, 더없이 뜻밖의 방문객 둘, 곧 픽윅 씨와 새뮤얼 웰러 씨의 도착으로 그 무리가 불어났다.\n\n자기 눈으로 본 광경이 웰러 씨의 마음에 곧바로 안긴 인상은, 마틴 씨가 옛 노케모프의 소여 진료소에 고용되어 독한 약을 먹거나, 발작을 일으켜 실험 대상이 되거나, 어떤 새 해독제의 효능을 시험하려고 이따금 독을 삼키거나, 의학이라는 위대한 학문을 증진하고 그곳 두 젊은 시술자의 가슴에 타오르는 열렬한 탐구 정신을 만족시키기 위해 이런저런 일을 하는 처지라는 것이었다. 그래서 샘은 감히 끼어들 생각도 없이, 마치 지금 진행 중인 실험의 결과에 몹시 관심이 있다는 듯 완벽하게 가만히 서서 지켜보고만 있었다. 픽윅 씨는 그렇지 않았다. 그는 곧장, 평소의 기운으로 어리둥절해하는 두 격투가에게 몸을 던지며, 구경꾼들을 향해 큰 소리로 끼어들어 말려 달라고 외쳤다.\n\n이것이 그때까지 동료의 광기에 완전히 마비되어 있던 보브 소여 씨를 깨어나게 했다. 그 신사의 도움으로 픽윅 씨는 벤 앨런을 일으켜 세웠다. 마틴 씨는 바닥에 혼자 남은 것을 알고는 일어나 주위를 둘러보았다.\n\n\"앨런 씨,\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"무슨 일이오, 선생?\"\n\n\"신경 쓰지 마시오, 선생!\" 앨런 씨가 오만하게 대들 듯 대답했다.\n\n\"무슨 일이오?\" 픽윅 씨가 보브 소여를 바라보며 물었다. \"이분이 편찮으신가?\"\n\n보브가 대답하기도 전에 벤 앨런 씨가 픽윅 씨의 손을 붙잡고 슬픔에 찬 어조로 중얼거렸다. \"제 누이동생입니다, 선생님. 제 누이동생요.\"\n\n\"오, 그게 다요!\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"그 문제라면 쉽게 정리할 수 있으리라 보오. 누이동생분은 무사하고 잘 있소. 그래서 내가 여기 온 것이오, 선생, 바로—\"\n\n\"임금이 의회를 해산할 때 한 말마따나, 그렇게 아주 기분 좋은 진행을 방해하게 돼서 죄송합니다만,\" 유리문 너머로 들여다보고 있던 웰러 씨가 끼어들었다. \"여기 또 다른 실험이 있습니다요, 나리. 여기 점잖은 노부인 한 분이 양탄자 위에 누워서 해부나, 전기 자극이나, 그 밖에 무슨 사람 되살리는 과학적 발명을 기다리고 계신뎁쇼.\"\n\n\"아차, 잊었군,\" 벤 앨런 씨가 외쳤다. \"제 이모님입니다.\"\n\n\"저런!\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"가엾은 부인! 살살, 샘, 살살.\"\n\n\"집안 한 분이 처하기엔 묘한 처지군입쇼,\" 샘 웰러가 이모를 의자에 들어 올리며 말했다. \"자, 부원장 돌팔이 선생님, 휘발성 약 좀 가져와 보슈!\"\n\n뒤의 말은 회색 옷의 소년을 두고 한 것이었는데, 그 소년은 마차를 거리 관리인에게 맡겨 두고 무슨 소란인가 보려고 돌아온 참이었다. 회색 옷의 소년과 보브 소여 씨, 그리고 (이모를 놀라게 해 기절시켜 놓고는 그 회복을 애정 어린 마음으로 안달하던) 벤저민 앨런 씨, 이들 사이에서 노부인은 마침내 의식을 되찾았다. 그러자 벤 앨런 씨가 어리둥절한 얼굴로 픽윅 씨를 돌아보며, 그렇게 놀랍게 말이 끊겼을 때 무슨 말을 하려던 참이었는지 물었다.\n\n\"여기 있는 사람들은 다 한편이라고 봐도 되겠지요?\" 픽윅 씨가 목청을 가다듬고, 통통한 말이 끄는 마차를 몬, 무뚝뚝한 얼굴의 말수 적은 사내 쪽을 바라보며 말했다.\n\n이 말에 보브 소여 씨는 회색 옷의 소년이 눈을 휘둥그레 뜨고 귀를 탐욕스럽게 세운 채 구경하고 있다는 것을 떠올렸다. 그 풋내기 약제사가 외투 깃이 잡혀 들려서 문밖으로 내던져진 뒤에야, 보브 소여는 픽윅 씨에게 거리낌 없이 말씀하셔도 된다고 일러 주었다.\n\n\"누이동생분은, 선생,\" 픽윅 씨가 벤저민 앨런을 향해 돌아서며 말했다. \"런던에 있소. 잘 있고 행복하오.\"\n\n\"그 애의 행복 따위 제게는 아무 상관 없습니다, 선생님,\" 벤저민 앨런이 손을 휘저으며 말했다.\n\n\"그 애 남편은 나한테 상관이 있소, 선생,\" 보브 소여가 말했다. \"그놈은 나한테 상관있는 표적이 될 거요, 선생, 열두 보 거리에서 말이오. 내가 그놈을 아주 볼만한 표적으로 만들어 주지요, 선생—그 비열한 악당 놈을!\" 이 정도라면 그 자체로 아주 그럴듯한 규탄이었고 더구나 통이 컸지만, 보브 소여 씨는 머리통을 후려치고 눈깔을 뽑아 버리겠다는, 그에 비하면 진부하기 짝이 없는 일반적 소견으로 끝맺어 그 효과를 다소 약화시켰다.\n\n\"가만, 선생,\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"문제의 그 신사에게 그런 별칭을 갖다 붙이기 전에, 그의 잘못의 정도를 냉정하게 헤아려 보시오. 그리고 무엇보다도 그가 내 친구라는 점을 기억하시오.\"\n\n\"뭐라고요!\" 보브 소여 씨가 말했다. \"그 이름!\" 벤 앨런이 외쳤다. \"그자의 이름을!\"\n\n\"너새니얼 윈클 씨요,\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n벤저민 앨런 씨는 신중하게 자기 안경을 장화 뒤축으로 짓밟아 부순 다음, 그 조각을 주워 세 군데 주머니에 나누어 넣고는, 팔짱을 끼고 입술을 깨물며 픽윅 씨의 온화한 이목구비를 위협적인 태도로 노려보았다.\n\n\"그렇다면 이 혼사를 부추기고 성사시킨 게 바로 당신이군요, 선생, 그렇소?\" 벤저민 앨런 씨가 마침내 물었다.\n\n\"그리고 제 집 주변을 어슬렁거리며 제 하인들을 꾀어 안주인을 거역하는 음모를 꾸미게 하려던 게 이 신사분의 하인이로군요, 아마.—마틴!\" 노부인이 끼어들었다.\n\n\"예?\" 무뚝뚝한 남자가 앞으로 나서며 말했다.\n\n\"오늘 아침에 자네가 골목길에서 봤다고 내게 말한 그 젊은이가 바로 저자인가?\"\n\n이미 드러났듯 말수가 적은 사람이었던 마틴 씨는 샘 웰러를 보고 고개를 끄덕이며 으르렁대듯 내뱉었다. \"저 사람 맞습니다.\" 도무지 잘난 척이라곤 모르던 웰러 씨는 무뚝뚝한 마부와 눈이 마주치자 다정한 알은체의 미소를 짓고는, 그를 \"전에 알던 사이\"라고 정중한 말로 인정했다.\n\n\"그러니까 이 사람이 바로, 제가 하마터면 질식시킬 뻔한 그 충직한 사람이로군요!\" 벤 앨런 씨가 외쳤다. \"픽윅 씨, 어찌 감히 댁의 하인을 내 누이동생을 유괴하는 데 쓰도록 내버려 두셨소? 이 일을 해명하실 것을 요구합니다, 선생.\"\n\n\"해명하시오, 선생!\" 보브 소여가 사납게 외쳤다.\n\n\"음모야,\" 벤 앨런이 말했다.\n\n\"제대로 짠 함정이지,\" 보브 소여 씨가 덧붙였다.\n\n\"수치스러운 사기예요,\" 노부인이 한마디 했다.\n\n\"순전히 속임수일 뿐이오,\" 마틴이 말했다.\n\n\"부디 내 말을 들어 보시오,\" 픽윅 씨가 다그쳤다. 그동안 벤 앨런 씨는 환자들의 피를 뽑던 의자에 털썩 주저앉아 손수건에 얼굴을 묻고 흐느꼈다. \"나는 이 일에서 어떤 도움도 준 적이 없소, 다만 내가 막을 수 없었던 그 두 젊은이의 면담 한 차례에 함께 있었던 것뿐이오. 그리고 내가 자리에 있으면 그 면담에 자칫 깃들 수 있는 부적절함의 미묘한 기색을 없앨 수 있으리라 여겼던 것이오. 이것이 이 일에서 내가 맡은 몫의 전부이며, 나는 당장의 결혼이 계획되고 있으리라고는 짐작조차 못 했소. 다만 말이오,\" 픽윅 씨가 황급히 자제하며 덧붙였다. \"다만, 그것이 계획돼 있는 줄 알았다 한들 내가 그것을 막았으리라고는 말하지 않겠소.\"\n\n\"다들 들었지요, 여러분, 다들 들으셨소?\" 벤저민 앨런 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"부디 들었길 바라오,\" 픽윅 씨가 주위를 둘러보며 온화하게 말했다. \"그리고,\" 그 신사가 말하면서 얼굴빛이 점점 달아오르며 덧붙였다. \"이것도 부디 들으시길 바라오, 선생. 내게 진술된 바로 미루어, 나는 단언하건대 당신이 그렇게 누이동생의 마음을 강제하려 든 것은 결코 정당하지 못했으며, 오히려 당신은 친절과 인내로써, 그 애가 어릴 때부터 한 번도 알지 못했던 더 가까운 다른 친척들의 자리를 대신해 주려고 애썼어야 마땅했소. 내 젊은 친구로 말하자면, 한마디 덧붙이지 않을 수 없는데, 세속적 이점이라는 모든 점에서 그는 적어도 당신과 대등하며, 훨씬 더 나은 처지에 있다고 해도 좋소. 그러니 이 문제를 합당한 절제와 온건함으로 논하는 것을 듣지 못한다면, 나는 이 주제에 관해 더 이상 듣기를 거절하겠소.\"\n\n\"방금 말씀을 마치신 명예로운 신사분께서 내놓으신 바에 더해 저도 몇 마디 아주 약간만 보태고 싶은뎁쇼,\" 웰러 씨가 앞으로 나서며 말했다. \"다름이 아니라 이겁니다요. 이 자리에 계신 어느 분이 절 보고 '놈'이라고 하셨거든요.\"\n\n\"그건 이 문제와 아무 상관도 없는 일이다, 샘,\" 픽윅 씨가 끼어들었다. \"제발 입 좀 다물어라.\"\n\n\"그 점에 대해선 아무 말도 안 하렵니다요, 나리,\" 샘이 대답했다. \"그저 이것만 말씀드리지요. 어쩌면 저 신사분께선 무슨 옛정이 있었던 게 아닌가 생각하실지 모릅니다만, 그런 건 전혀 없었습니다요. 왜냐면 그 아가씨가 사귀기 시작한 바로 그 처음부터 그분을 도무지 못 견디겠다고 하셨거든요. 아무도 그분을 밀어낸 게 아니고, 설사 그 아가씨가 윈클 씨를 한 번도 못 봤더라도 그분한텐 사정이 똑같았을 겁니다요. 그게 제가 말씀드리고 싶었던 거고요, 나리, 이제 저 신사분 마음이 편해졌기를 바랍니다요.\"\n\n웰러 씨의 이 위로하는 말 뒤에 잠깐 침묵이 흘렀다. 이윽고 벤 앨런 씨가 의자에서 일어서며 다시는 어래벨러의 얼굴을 보지 않겠다고 단언했고, 한편 보브 소여 씨는 샘의 듣기 좋은 장담에도 아랑곳없이 그 행복한 신랑에게 무시무시한 복수를 하겠다고 맹세했다.\n\n그러나 사태가 절정에 이르러 그대로 머물 조짐을 보이던 바로 그때, 픽윅 씨는 노부인이라는 강력한 조력자를 얻었다. 노부인은 그가 자기 조카딸의 처지를 옹호한 방식에 분명 크게 감동한 듯, 위로가 될 만한 몇 마디 소회를 들고 벤저민 앨런 씨에게 다가가 보았는데, 그 요지인즉, 어쨌거나 그래도 이만하기 다행인지 모른다, 말을 적게 할수록 빨리 아물 것이다, 정말이지 따지고 보면 그렇게 나쁜 일도 아닌 것 같다, 지나간 일은 다시 시작할 수 없고 고칠 수 없는 일은 견뎌야 하는 법이다, 라는 것이었고, 그 밖에도 그처럼 참신하고 기운을 북돋우는 갖가지 장담을 곁들였다. 이 모든 말에 벤저민 앨런 씨는, 이모님이나 여기 계신 누구에게도 무례를 끼칠 뜻은 없으나, 다들 상관없다면, 그리고 자기 뜻대로 하도록 허락해 준다면, 자기는 차라리 누이동생을 죽을 때까지, 아니 죽은 뒤에도 미워하는 즐거움을 누리고 싶다고 대답했다.\n\n마침내 이 결심을 쉰 번쯤 되풀이해 선언하고 났을 때, 노부인이 별안간 발끈하며 자못 위엄 있는 모습으로, 도대체 내가 무슨 짓을 했길래 내 나이나 지위에 아무 존경도 표하지 않는 거냐, 그래서 내가 이런 식으로 내 친조카에게 빌고 사정해야 하는 거냐, 나는 이 애가 태어나기 한 스물다섯 해 전부터 기억하고, 입에 이 하나 안 났을 때부터 직접 알고 지냈으며, 이 애가 처음 머리를 깎던 자리에도 있어 주었고, 갓난쟁이 시절 그 밖에 수많은 때와 의식에서 거들어 주어 그 애정과 순종과 동정을 영원토록 요구할 만한 권리를 충분히 세워 둔 사람인데, 라고 알고 싶어 했다.\n\n그 선량한 부인이 벤 앨런 씨에게 이런 질책을 퍼붓고 있는 동안, 보브 소여와 픽윅 씨는 은밀한 대화를 나누며 안쪽 방으로 물러나 있었는데, 거기서 소여 씨는 검은 병 주둥이에 여러 차례 입을 갖다 대는 것이 목격되었고, 그 영향으로 그의 표정은 차츰 명랑하다 못해 흥겨운 빛마저 띠게 되었다. 그러고는 마침내 병을 손에 든 채 방에서 나와, 자기가 바보짓을 했다고 말하게 되어 몹시 유감이라면서, 윈클 부부의 건강과 행복을 위해 건배할 것을 청했다. 그들의 행복을 시기하기는커녕 자기야말로 맨 먼저 그들을 축하하겠노라는 것이었다. 이 말을 듣자 벤 앨런 씨가 별안간 의자에서 일어나 검은 병을 움켜쥐고 어찌나 진심으로 건배를 들이켰던지, 술이 독한 탓에 그의 얼굴은 거의 병만큼이나 시커메지고 말았다. 마침내 검은 병은 텅 빌 때까지 돌고 돌았고, 어찌나 악수를 나누고 인사치레를 주고받았던지, 쇠붙이 같은 낯짝의 마틴 씨조차 황송하게도 미소를 지을 정도였다.\n\n\"자 이제,\" 보브 소여가 두 손을 비비며 말했다. \"유쾌한 밤을 보냅시다.\"\n\n\"미안하지만,\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"나는 여관으로 돌아가야 하오. 요즘 피곤한 일에 익숙지 않은 데다, 이번 여정이 나를 몹시 지치게 했소.\"\n\n\"차라도 좀 드시고 가시지요, 픽윅 씨?\" 노부인이 거부할 수 없는 다정함으로 말했다.\n\n\"고맙소만, 안 드는 게 낫겠소,\" 그 신사가 대답했다. 사실인즉, 노부인의 흠모가 눈에 띄게 짙어 가는 것이야말로 픽윅 씨가 자리를 뜨려는 주된 이유였다. 그는 바델 부인을 떠올렸고, 노부인의 눈길이 한 번 던져질 때마다 식은땀이 흘렀다.\n\n픽윅 씨를 도무지 머물게 할 수 없었으므로, 그 자신의 제안에 따라 곧장 정해진 바, 벤저민 앨런 씨가 윈클 노인을 향한 픽윅 씨의 여정에 동행하기로 하고, 마차는 이튿날 아침 아홉 시에 문 앞에 대기로 했다. 그러고 나서 그는 작별을 고하고, 새뮤얼 웰러를 거느린 채 부시 여관으로 향했다. 한 가지 적어 둘 만한 것은, 마틴 씨가 헤어지면서 샘과 악수할 때 그의 얼굴이 끔찍하게 일그러지더니, 동시에 미소와 욕설을 한꺼번에 터뜨렸다는 점이다. 이러한 징표로 미루어, 그 신사의 별난 기질을 가장 잘 아는 이들은 그가 웰러 씨와 어울린 것이 무척 흡족했으며 앞으로도 더 사귀는 영광을 누리고 싶다는 뜻을 표한 것이라고 추측했다.\n\n\"독방을 잡을까요, 나리?\" 부시 여관에 이르자 샘이 물었다.\n\n\"아니다, 샘,\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다. \"식사는 커피실에서 했고 곧 잠자리에 들 테니, 굳이 그럴 것까지 없겠다. 여행자실에 누가 있는지 보고 오너라, 샘.\"\n\n웰러 씨가 그 심부름을 하러 갔다가 곧 돌아와, 외눈박이 신사 한 분뿐인데 그와 여관 주인이 함께 비숍을 한 사발 마시고 있다고 전했다.\n\n\"내가 합석하지,\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"별난 손님입니다요, 그 외눈박이 양반은요, 나리,\" 웰러 씨가 앞장서며 말했다. \"여관 주인을 어찌나 구워삶고 있는지요, 나리, 주인 양반이 지금 자기가 장화 밑창을 딛고 서 있는지 모자 꼭대기를 딛고 서 있는지도 제대로 모를 지경입니다요.\"\n\n이 말이 가리킨 사람은 픽윅 씨가 들어섰을 때 방 위쪽 끝에 앉아, 커다란 네덜란드식 파이프를 피우면서 여관 주인의 둥근 얼굴에 외눈을 골똘히 박고 있었다. 명랑해 보이는 노인인 주인에게 그가 방금 어떤 기이한 이야기를 들려준 참이라는 것은, \"글쎄, 도무지 못 믿겠는걸! 내 평생 들어 본 중에 가장 이상한 이야기야! 그게 가능하리라곤 상상도 못 했네!\" 따위의 여러 토막 난 감탄사와, 외눈박이 사내의 응시에 마주하며 그의 입에서 절로 터져 나온 그 밖의 갖가지 놀라움의 표현으로 입증되고 있었다.\n\n\"안녕하시오, 선생,\" 외눈박이 사내가 픽윅 씨에게 말했다. \"좋은 밤이올시다, 선생.\"\n\n\"정말이지 그렇구려,\" 픽윅 씨가 대답하는 동안 종업원이 그의 앞에 작은 브랜디 병과 뜨거운 물을 좀 놓아 주었다.\n\n픽윅 씨가 브랜디에 물을 타는 동안, 외눈박이 사내가 이따금 그를 진지하게 둘러보더니 마침내 입을 열었다—\n\n\"전에 당신을 본 적이 있는 것 같소만.\"\n\n\"나는 당신이 기억나지 않는구려,\" 픽윅 씨가 받았다.\n\n\"그렇겠지요,\" 외눈박이 사내가 말했다. \"당신은 나를 몰랐지만, 나는 선거 때 이턴스윌의 피콕 여관에 묵고 있던 당신 친구 두 분을 알았소.\"\n\n\"오, 그래요!\" 픽윅 씨가 외쳤다.\n\n\"그렇소,\" 외눈박이 사내가 받았다. \"내가 그들에게 톰 스마트라는 이름의 내 친구에 관한 사소한 이야기 하나를 들려주었지요. 아마 그들이 그 이야기를 하는 걸 들어 보셨을 거요.\"\n\n\"자주 들었소,\" 픽윅 씨가 미소 지으며 받았다. \"그분이 당신 삼촌이었지요, 아마?\"\n\n\"아니, 아니오. 그저 제 삼촌의 친구였을 뿐이지요,\" 외눈박이 사내가 대답했다.\n\n\"하지만 그 삼촌 되시는 분은 대단한 분이었지요,\" 여관 주인이 고개를 가로저으며 한마디 했다.\n\n\"음, 그랬다고 봅니다. 그랬다고 말해도 좋겠지요,\" 외눈박이 사내가 대답했다. \"그 똑같은 삼촌에 관해 한 가지 이야기를 들려드릴 수 있는데, 여러분, 꽤 놀라실 거외다.\"\n\n\"그래요?\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"부디 들어 봅시다.\"\n\n외눈박이 외판원은 사발에서 니거스 한 잔을 국자로 떠서 마시고, 네덜란드식 파이프를 길게 한 모금 빨더니, 문 가까이 서성거리던 샘 웰러에게, 이 이야기는 비밀이 아니니 가고 싶지 않거든 굳이 가지 않아도 된다고 일러 준 다음, 여관 주인의 얼굴에 외눈을 박은 채 다음 장에 적힌 말로 이야기를 시작했다.\n\n# 제17장. 류머티즘의 발작이 어떤 경우에는 창의적 천재성을 촉진하는 작용을 한다는 것을 보여 주는 장\n\n신사 여러분, 제 삼촌으로 말씀드리자면,\" 외판원이 말했다, \"이제껏 살았던 사람들 가운데 가장 유쾌하고, 가장 상냥하고, 가장 영리한 분들 중 한 분이었습니다. 여러분이 그분을 아셨더라면 좋았을 텐데요, 신사 여러분. 다시 생각해 보니, 신사 여러분, 여러분이 그분을 아셨더라면 하고 바라지 않겠습니다. 만약 아셨더라면 여러분은 지금쯤 자연의 순리에 따라, 죽지는 않았더라도 적어도 그에 가까워져서, 집에만 틀어박혀 사람들과의 교제를 끊으셨을 테고, 그러면 저는 이 순간 여러분께 말씀드리는 값으로 따질 수 없는 기쁨을 빼앗기고 말았을 테니까요. 신사 여러분, 차라리 여러분의 아버님과 어머님께서 제 삼촌을 아셨더라면 좋았을 겁니다. 그분을 무척이나 좋아하셨을 테지요, 특히 여러분의 점잖으신 어머님들께서요. 틀림없이 그러셨을 겁니다. 그분의 인품을 꾸미던 수많은 미덕 가운데서도 어느 둘이 다른 많은 것을 압도했다고 한다면, 저는 그것이 그분이 만든 혼합 펀치와 저녁 식사 후의 노래였다고 말씀드리겠습니다. 떠나간 분의 미덕에 관한 이런 우울한 회상에 제가 잠겨 있는 것을 용서해 주십시오. 제 삼촌 같은 분은 일주일 내내 매일같이 만날 수 있는 사람이 아니니까요.\n\n\"저는 늘 제 삼촌의 인품에서 큰 자랑거리로 여겨 온 것이 있습니다, 신사 여러분. 그분이 시티의 케이트아턴 거리에 있는 대상회 빌슨 앤드 슬럼의 톰 스마트와 막역한 벗이자 동무였다는 점입니다. 제 삼촌은 티긴 앤드 웰프스를 위해 수금을 다니셨지만, 오랫동안 톰과 거의 같은 노선을 도셨습니다. 두 분이 만난 바로 첫날 밤, 제 삼촌은 톰이 마음에 들었고, 톰은 제 삼촌이 마음에 들었지요. 두 분은 서로 알게 된 지 반 시간도 채 되지 않아 새 모자를 걸고 내기를 했습니다. 누가 더 좋은 펀치 한 쿼트를 만들고, 그것을 누가 더 빨리 마시느냐 하는 것이었지요. 만드는 쪽은 제 삼촌이 이긴 것으로 판정이 났지만, 마시는 쪽에서는 톰 스마트가 소금 숟가락 반 술쯤 차이로 그분을 이겼습니다. 두 분은 서로의 건강을 빌며 또 한 쿼트씩을 마셨고, 그 후로는 변함없는 벗이 되었지요. 이런 일들에는 운명이라는 것이 있는 법입니다, 신사 여러분. 어쩔 수 없는 일이지요.\n\n\"외양으로 말하자면, 제 삼촌은 중키보다 약간 작은 편이었습니다. 보통 사람들보다 조금 더 통통했고, 어쩌면 얼굴빛도 한층 더 붉었을지 모르겠습니다. 여러분이 이제껏 보신 중에 가장 쾌활한 얼굴을 하고 계셨지요, 신사 여러분. 펀치 인형 비슷한데, 잘생긴 코와 턱이 있었습니다. 두 눈은 늘 호의로 반짝거리며 빛났고, 미소가—저 무의미하고 나무토막 같은 히죽거림이 아니라, 진짜로 즐겁고 진심 어리고 너그러운 미소가—그분 얼굴에 항상 어려 있었지요. 한번은 마차에서 튕겨 나가 머리부터 이정표에 부딪치신 적이 있습니다. 그분은 기절한 채 거기 누워 계셨는데, 옆에 쌓여 있던 자갈에 얼굴이 어찌나 긁혔던지, 제 삼촌 자신의 강렬한 표현을 빌리자면, 그분 어머니가 다시 이 세상에 오셨더라도 그분을 알아보지 못하셨을 거랍니다. 사실 이 문제를 곰곰이 따져 보면, 신사 여러분, 저는 어머니가 정말로 못 알아보셨을 거라고 거의 확신합니다. 어머니는 제 삼촌이 두 살 일곱 달 되던 때에 돌아가셨으니까요. 그리고 제 생각엔 자갈이 없었더라도 그분의 통가죽 장화만으로도 그 선량한 부인을 적잖이 어리둥절하게 했을 것 같습니다. 그분의 쾌활하게 붉은 얼굴은 말할 것도 없고요. 아무튼 그분은 거기 누워 계셨는데, 저는 제 삼촌이 여러 번 하시는 말씀을 들었습니다. 그분을 일으켜 세운 사람 말로는, 그분이 마치 한턱 얻어먹으려고 굴러떨어진 것처럼 즐겁게 미소 짓고 있었다는군요. 그리고 사람들이 그분의 피를 뽑고 나자, 의식이 돌아오는 첫 희미한 기미라는 것이 침대에서 벌떡 일어나 큰 소리로 웃음을 터뜨리고, 대야를 들고 있던 젊은 여자에게 입을 맞추고는 양고기 갈비와 호두 절임을 달라고 한 것이었답니다. 그분은 호두 절임을 무척 좋아하셨지요, 신사 여러분. 식초를 치지 않고 먹으면 맥주 맛이 한층 살아난다는 걸 늘 알게 되었다고 하셨습니다.\n\n\"제 삼촌의 큰 여정은 낙엽이 지는 가을철에 있었습니다. 그때 그분은 북쪽에서 빚을 거두고 주문을 받으셨지요. 런던에서 에든버러로, 에든버러에서 글래스고로, 글래스고에서 다시 에든버러로, 거기서 다시 소형 범선을 타고 런던으로 돌아오셨습니다. 두 번째로 에든버러를 찾으신 것은 그분 자신의 즐거움을 위해서였다는 점을 알아 두셔야 합니다. 그분은 옛 친구들을 만나려고 일주일쯤 다시 그곳에 가시곤 했지요. 그런데 이 친구와 아침을 먹고, 저 친구와 점심을 먹고, 세 번째 친구와 저녁을 먹고, 또 다른 친구와 야식을 먹다 보면, 꽤나 빡빡한 일주일을 보내곤 하셨습니다. 신사 여러분 가운데 누구라도 진짜 푸짐하고 후한 스코틀랜드식 아침 식사를 드신 다음, 다시 굴 한 부셸과 병맥주 열두어 병에다 마무리로 위스키 한두 잔을 곁들이는 가벼운 점심을 드시러 나가 본 적이 있는지 모르겠습니다. 만약 한 번이라도 그러신 적이 있다면, 그러고 나서 다시 저녁과 야식을 먹으러 나가려면 꽤나 강한 머리가 필요하다는 데 제 의견에 동의하실 겁니다.\n\n\"하지만, 가슴과 눈썹에 복이 있기를! 이런 종류의 일은 제 삼촌에게는 아무것도 아니었습니다! 그분은 워낙 단련이 잘 되어 있어서 그저 애들 장난에 불과했지요. 저는 그분이 던디 사람들과 어느 날이든 끝까지 술자리를 함께한 다음 비틀거리지 않고 걸어서 집에 돌아올 수 있다고 말씀하시는 걸 들었습니다. 그런데 던디 사람들로 말하자면, 신사 여러분, 여러분이 지구의 양극 사이 어디에서 만나게 될 만한 이들 중에 머리도 가장 강하고 펀치도 가장 독한 자들입니다. 저는 글래스고 사람 하나와 던디 사람 하나가 한자리에 앉아 열다섯 시간 동안 서로 술 대결을 벌였다는 이야기를 들은 적이 있습니다. 확인할 수 있는 한 두 사람은 거의 같은 순간에 질식했다고 하는데, 그 사소한 예외만 빼면, 신사 여러분, 두 사람은 그 일로 조금도 더 나빠지지 않았답니다.\n\n\"어느 날 밤, 그분이 런던행 배를 타기로 정한 시각을 스물네 시간 앞두고, 제 삼촌은 아주 오랜 친구의 집에서 야식을 드셨습니다. 그 친구는 매크 무엇이라는, 그 뒤에 음절 네 개가 붙는 이름의 시의원이었는데, 에든버러의 구시가지에 살고 있었지요. 그곳에는 시의원의 아내와, 시의원의 세 딸과, 다 큰 시의원의 아들과, 그리고 제 삼촌을 위해 시의원이 한자리에 불러 모아 흥을 돋우는 데 한몫하게 한, 건장하고 눈썹이 덥수룩하며 신중한 스코틀랜드 노인 서넛이 있었습니다. 그것은 굉장한 야식이었지요. 훈제 연어가 있었고, 피넌 대구가 있었고, 양 머리 요리가 있었고, 해기스가—이건 유명한 스코틀랜드 요리인데, 신사 여러분, 제 삼촌은 그것이 식탁에 오르면 늘 큐피드의 위장처럼 보인다고 하셨지요—그 밖에도 제가 이름은 잊어버렸지만 그래도 아주 훌륭한 음식들이 많이 있었습니다. 처녀들은 예쁘고 상냥했고, 시의원의 아내는 이제껏 살았던 사람들 중 가장 좋은 분이었으며, 제 삼촌은 더할 나위 없이 흥이 올라 있었습니다. 그 결과, 젊은 아가씨들은 키득키득 웃었고, 노부인은 큰 소리로 웃어 댔으며, 시의원과 다른 노인들은 그 내내 얼굴이 벌게지도록 껄껄대고 웃었지요. 야식 후에 각자가 위스키 토디를 몇 잔이나 마셨는지는 정확히 기억나지 않습니다만, 이것만은 압니다. 새벽 한 시쯤 시의원의 다 큰 아들이 「윌리가 보리 한 펙으로 술을 빚었네」의 첫 소절을 부르려다가 정신을 잃었고, 그가 그 반 시간 전부터 마호가니 식탁 위로 모습을 드러낸 유일한 다른 남자였던지라, 제 삼촌은 슬슬 갈 때를 생각해야겠다는 생각이 드셨습니다. 더구나 술자리가 일곱 시에 시작되었으니, 점잖은 시각에 집에 돌아가려면 말이지요. 하지만 바로 그때 떠나는 것이 그다지 예의에 맞지 않을지 모른다고 생각하신 제 삼촌은, 스스로를 좌장으로 추대하고, 한 잔을 더 섞은 다음, 일어서서 자신의 건강을 위한 건배를 제의하고, 단정하고 정중한 연설로 자기 자신에게 인사를 건넨 뒤, 큰 열의를 담아 그 건배를 들이켰습니다. 그래도 아무도 깨어나지 않았지요. 그래서 제 삼촌은 한 방울 더—이번에는 토디가 속에 거슬리지 않도록 그냥 위스키만—드신 다음, 모자를 거칠게 움켜잡고 거리로 나섰습니다.\n\n\"제 삼촌이 시의원의 집 문을 닫고, 바람에 모자가 날아가지 않도록 머리에 단단히 눌러쓰고, 두 손을 주머니에 찔러 넣은 채 위를 올려다보며 날씨 상태를 잠깐 살폈을 때, 그것은 거칠고 돌풍이 부는 밤이었습니다. 구름은 더없이 어지러운 속도로 달을 가로질러 흘러갔습니다. 어느 때는 달을 완전히 가렸다가, 또 어느 때는 달이 온전한 광채로 터져 나와 주위의 온갖 것에 빛을 뿌리도록 내버려 두었다가, 곧 다시 한층 빨라진 속도로 달 위를 내달려 모든 것을 어둠 속에 감추었지요. '정말이지, 이건 안 되겠는걸.' 제 삼촌은 마치 개인적으로 모욕이라도 당한 듯 날씨를 향해 말했습니다. '내 항해에는 도무지 어울리지 않는 날씨야. 어떤 값을 치르더라도 안 되겠어.' 하고 제 삼촌은 아주 단호하게 말했지요. 이 말을 여러 번 되풀이하신 다음, 그분은 다소 어렵사리 균형을 되찾았습니다—하늘을 너무 오래 올려다본 탓에 좀 어지러웠던 거지요—그러고는 즐겁게 걸어 나아갔습니다.\n\n\"시의원의 집은 캐넌게이트에 있었고, 제 삼촌은 리스 워크의 반대편 끝으로 가는 길이었습니다. 1마일이 조금 넘는 여정이었지요. 그분 양옆으로는 검은 하늘을 향해 우뚝 솟은, 키 크고 앙상하며 제멋대로 뻗은 집들이 솟아 있었습니다. 세월에 얼룩진 정면과, 사람의 눈이 겪는 운명을 함께 나눈 듯 나이 들어 흐려지고 움푹 꺼진 창문을 단 집들이었지요. 그 집들은 여섯, 일곱, 여덟 층 높이였습니다. 아이들이 카드로 쌓아 올리듯 층 위에 층이 포개져 있어, 거칠게 포장된 길 위로 어두운 그림자를 드리우며 어두운 밤을 더욱 어둡게 만들었지요. 기름 등불 몇 개가 멀찍멀찍 떨어진 채 흩어져 있었지만, 그것들은 그저 어느 좁은 골목길의 더러운 입구를 표시하거나, 가파르고 복잡하게 굽이진 계단이 위쪽의 여러 층과 통하는 곳을 보여 줄 뿐이었습니다. 이 모든 것을, 너무 자주 보아 와서 이제는 별로 주목할 가치가 없다고 여기는 사람의 태도로 슬쩍 훑어보며, 제 삼촌은 양쪽 조끼 주머니에 엄지손가락을 하나씩 찔러 넣고 거리 한복판을 걸어 올라갔습니다. 이따금 노래의 이런저런 토막을 흥얼거렸는데, 어찌나 신명과 기운을 담아 불러 댔던지, 조용하고 정직한 사람들이 첫잠에서 깜짝 놀라 깨어나 그 소리가 멀리서 잦아들 때까지 침대에서 떨며 누워 있다가, 그저 어느 술 취한 건달이 제 집을 찾아가는 소리일 뿐이라고 스스로를 안심시키고는, 다시 이불을 따뜻하게 뒤집어쓰고 잠들곤 했지요.\n\n\"제가 제 삼촌이 조끼 주머니에 엄지손가락을 찔러 넣고 거리 한복판을 걸어 올라갔다는 것을 이렇게 자세히 묘사하는 데는 까닭이 있습니다, 신사 여러분. 그분이 자주 하시던 말씀처럼(그리고 그건 충분히 일리 있는 말씀이지요), 이 이야기에는 조금도 비범한 구석이 없거든요. 다만 그분이 결코 기이하거나 낭만적인 기질의 인물이 아니었다는 것을 처음부터 분명히 이해하셔야 합니다.\n\n\"신사 여러분, 제 삼촌은 조끼 주머니에 엄지손가락을 찔러 넣고 거리 한복판을 독차지한 채 걸었습니다. 이번에는 연가의 한 소절을, 다음에는 권주가의 한 소절을 부르다가, 둘 다 부르기 지치면 가락에 맞춰 멋들어지게 휘파람을 불며 걸어갔지요. 그렇게 노스 브리지에 이르렀는데, 이 다리는 이 지점에서 에든버러의 구시가지와 신시가지를 잇고 있습니다. 여기서 그분은 잠시 멈춰 서서, 차곡차곡 포개진 채 멀리서 그토록 높이 반짝이는, 기이하고 불규칙하게 무리 지은 불빛들을 바라보았습니다. 그 불빛들은 마치 별처럼 보였지요. 한쪽으로는 성벽에서, 다른 쪽으로는 캘턴 힐에서 반짝이며, 마치 진짜로 공중에 떠 있는 성들을 환히 밝히는 듯했습니다. 그동안 그림처럼 아름다운 옛 시가지는 그 아래 음울함과 어둠 속에서 무겁게 잠들어 있었습니다. 그 왕궁과 홀리루드 예배당은, 제 삼촌의 한 친구가 늘 하던 말마따나, 밤낮으로 늙은 아서즈 시트가 지키고 있었지요. 그 늙은 봉우리는 오래도록 지켜봐 온 그 고대 도시 위로, 무뚝뚝하고 시커멓게 솟아올라, 마치 무뚝뚝한 정령처럼 우뚝 서 있었습니다. 말씀드리자면, 신사 여러분, 제 삼촌은 여기서 잠시 멈춰 주위를 둘러보았고, 그러고는 비록 달이 기울고 있었지만 조금 개어진 날씨에 한마디 찬사를 보낸 뒤, 다시 전처럼 당당하게 걸어 나아갔습니다. 큰 위엄을 갖추고 길 한복판을 지키며, 마치 누구든 그 자리의 소유권을 두고 다투려는 자가 나타나기를 몹시 바라는 듯한 표정으로요. 그런데 마침 그 점을 다투려는 자는 아무도 없었지요. 그래서 그분은 조끼 주머니에 엄지손가락을 찔러 넣은 채, 어린 양처럼 걸어갔습니다.\n\n\"제 삼촌이 리스 워크의 끝에 이르렀을 때, 그분은 꽤 넓은 빈터를 가로질러야 했습니다. 그 빈터는 그분이 곧장 숙소로 가려면 꺾어 들어가야 하는 짧은 거리와 그분 사이를 갈라놓고 있었지요. 그런데 이 빈터에는 그 무렵 어느 수레바퀴 목수의 울타리 친 마당이 하나 있었습니다. 그 목수는 낡고 닳아 빠진 우편 마차를 사들이는 계약을 우체국과 맺고 있었지요. 그리고 마차라면 낡았든 새것이든 중년이든 무척이나 좋아하셨던 제 삼촌은, 갑자기 이 우편 마차들을 울타리 틈으로 들여다보겠다는 일념으로 가던 길에서 벗어나야겠다는 생각이 들었습니다. 그분은 그 안에 열두어 대쯤의 마차가 아주 처량하게 해체된 상태로 빽빽이 모여 있는 것을 본 기억이 있었거든요. 제 삼촌은 아주 열정적이고 적극적인 부류의 사람이었습니다, 신사 여러분. 그래서 울타리 틈으로는 제대로 들여다볼 수가 없다는 것을 알게 되자, 그분은 울타리를 넘어가 낡은 차축 위에 조용히 앉아서는, 그 우편 마차들을 자못 진지하게 바라보기 시작했습니다.\n\n\"마차는 열두 대쯤 되었을 수도 있고, 더 많았을 수도 있습니다—제 삼촌은 이 점에 대해서는 결코 확신하지 못했고, 숫자에 관해서는 무척 양심적으로 정확을 기하는 분이었던지라 함부로 말씀하기를 꺼리셨지요—아무튼 마차들은 거기, 상상할 수 있는 가장 황량한 꼴로 한데 옹기종기 모여 서 있었습니다. 문짝은 경첩에서 뜯겨 나가 치워져 있었고, 안감은 벗겨져 여기저기 녹슨 못 하나에 조각만 너덜너덜 매달려 있었습니다. 등불은 사라졌고, 끌채는 진작에 없어졌으며, 쇠붙이는 녹슬었고, 칠은 다 벗겨졌습니다. 바람은 헐벗은 목재의 틈새로 휘파람 소리를 냈고, 지붕에 고였던 빗물은 텅 빈 듯 우울한 소리를 내며 한 방울 한 방울 마차 안으로 떨어졌습니다. 그것들은 떠나간 우편 마차들의 썩어 가는 해골이었고, 그 외딴곳에서, 그 밤 시각에, 그것들은 으스스하고 음산해 보였습니다.\n\n\"제 삼촌은 두 손에 머리를 괴고, 여러 해 전 그 낡은 마차들 속에서 덜컹거리며 분주히 오가던 바쁜 사람들을, 그리고 이제는 그 마차들처럼 말없이 변해 버린 그들을 생각했습니다. 그분은 이 낡아 가고 곰팡이 슨 마차들 가운데 한 대가 여러 해 동안 밤마다, 온갖 날씨를 뚫고, 수많은 사람들에게 애타게 기다리던 소식을, 간절히 바라던 송금을, 약속된 건강과 안전에 대한 보증을, 또 갑작스러운 병과 죽음의 통지를 실어 날랐던 것을 생각했습니다. 상인, 연인, 아내, 과부, 어머니, 학생, 그리고 우편배달부의 노크 소리에 비틀비틀 문가로 걸어 나오던 바로 그 어린아이까지—그들 모두가 얼마나 그 낡은 마차의 도착을 손꼽아 기다렸던가요. 그런데 그들은 지금 모두 어디에 있을까요?\n\n\"신사 여러분, 제 삼촌은 자기가 그때 이 모든 것을 생각했다고 말씀하시곤 했지만, 저는 그분이 나중에 어떤 책에서 그것을 배운 게 아닌가 하는 의심이 좀 듭니다. 왜냐하면 그분은 낡은 차축 위에 앉아 썩어 가는 우편 마차를 바라보다가 일종의 선잠에 빠져들었고, 어느 깊은 교회 종이 두 시를 치는 소리에 불현듯 잠에서 깨어났다고 분명히 말씀하셨거든요. 그런데 제 삼촌은 결코 생각이 빠른 분이 아니었습니다. 만약 그분이 이 모든 것을 정말로 생각하셨다면, 저는 적어도 두 시 반은 꽉 채워 걸렸으리라 확신합니다. 그러므로 저는, 신사 여러분, 제 삼촌이 아무것도 생각하지 않은 채 일종의 선잠에 빠져들었다는 쪽으로 단연 의견이 기웁니다.\n\n\"어찌 되었든, 교회 종이 두 시를 쳤습니다. 제 삼촌은 잠에서 깨어, 눈을 비비고, 깜짝 놀라 벌떡 일어났습니다.\n\n\"시계가 두 시를 친 직후 그 순간, 이 버려지고 고요하던 곳 전체가 더없이 비상한 생기와 활기의 무대로 변해 있었습니다. 우편 마차의 문은 경첩에 달려 있었고, 안감은 새로 갈려 있었으며, 쇠붙이는 새것이나 다름없었고, 칠은 새로 입혀졌으며, 등불은 켜져 있었습니다. 마부석마다 방석과 두꺼운 외투가 놓여 있었고, 짐꾼들은 짐칸마다 소포를 밀어 넣고 있었으며, 차장들은 우편 행낭을 챙겨 넣고 있었고, 마부 조수들은 새로 단장한 바퀴에 물통의 물을 끼얹고 있었습니다. 수많은 사람이 분주히 오가며 마차마다 끌채를 끼워 맞추고 있었고, 승객들이 도착했으며, 여행 가방들이 위로 건네지고, 말들이 매여졌습니다. 요컨대, 거기 있는 모든 우편 마차가 곧 떠날 참이라는 것이 더없이 분명했습니다. 신사 여러분, 제 삼촌은 이 모든 것에 어찌나 눈을 크게 떴던지, 죽는 날까지도 어떻게 그가 그 눈을 다시 감을 수 있었는지 의아해하시곤 했습니다.\n\n\"'자, 그럼!' 제 삼촌이 어깨에 손이 닿는 것을 느꼈을 때 한 목소리가 말했습니다. '당신은 안쪽 자리 하나에 예약되어 있소. 어서 타는 게 좋겠소.'\n\n\"'내가 예약을 했다고!' 제 삼촌이 돌아서며 말했습니다.\n\n\"'그렇소, 틀림없이.'\n\n\"신사 여러분, 제 삼촌은 너무나 놀란 나머지 아무 말도 할 수 없었습니다. 그 가운데서도 가장 기이한 것은, 그토록 많은 사람이 북적이고 매 순간 새로운 얼굴들이 밀려들었는데도, 도대체 그들이 어디서 왔는지 알 수가 없다는 점이었습니다. 그들은 어떤 기이한 방식으로 땅에서, 혹은 공기에서 불쑥 솟아났다가, 같은 방식으로 사라지는 듯했습니다. 짐꾼 하나가 짐을 마차에 실어 주고 삯을 받고 나면, 그는 돌아서서 사라져 버렸지요. 그리고 제 삼촌이 미처 그가 어디로 갔는지 의아해하기도 전에, 여섯 명쯤의 새로운 짐꾼이 불쑥 솟아나, 그들을 짓눌러 버릴 만큼 커다란 소포의 무게에 휘청거리며 걸어왔습니다. 승객들도 모두 어찌나 괴상하게 차려입었던지요! 큼지막한 소맷부리에 옷깃은 없고, 자락이 넓게 퍼진 레이스 달린 큰 코트를 입었고, 가발을—신사 여러분, 뒤에 끈을 묶은 크고 격식 차린 가발을 쓰고 있었습니다. 제 삼촌은 도무지 영문을 알 수가 없었지요.\n\n\"'자, 탈 거요, 안 탈 거요?' 앞서 제 삼촌에게 말을 걸었던 사람이 말했습니다. 그는 우편 마차 차장 차림이었는데, 머리에 가발을 쓰고 코트에는 엄청나게 큰 소맷부리를 달았으며, 한 손에는 등불을, 다른 손에는 거대한 나팔총을 들고 있었습니다. 그는 그 총을 자기 작은 팔걸이 상자에 챙겨 넣으려던 참이었지요. '탈 거요, 안 탈 거요, 잭 마틴?' 차장이 제 삼촌의 얼굴에 등불을 들이대며 말했습니다.\n\n\"'이봐!' 제 삼촌이 한두 걸음 물러서며 말했습니다. '말버릇이 너무 무람없군!'\n\n\"'운송 명부에 그렇게 적혀 있소.' 차장이 말했습니다.\n\n\"'그 앞에 '미스터'가 안 붙어 있나?' 제 삼촌이 말했습니다. 신사 여러분, 그분은 일면식도 없는 차장이 자기를 잭 마틴이라고 부르는 것은, 우체국이 그 사실을 알았더라면 결코 허용하지 않았을 무례라고 느끼셨던 거지요.\n\n\"'아니, 안 붙어 있소.' 차장이 냉랭하게 대꾸했습니다.\n\n\"'삯은 치러졌나?' 제 삼촌이 물었습니다.\n\n\"'물론 치러졌소.' 차장이 대꾸했습니다.\n\n\"'치러졌다, 이 말이지?' 제 삼촌이 말했습니다. '그럼 탑시다! 어느 마차요?'\n\n\"'이거요.' 차장이 발판이 내려지고 문이 열린 구식 에든버러-런던 우편 마차를 가리키며 말했습니다. '잠깐! 다른 승객들이 왔소. 저들이 먼저 타게 하시오.'\n\n\"차장이 말하는 사이, 제 삼촌 바로 앞에 별안간 한 젊은 신사가 나타났습니다. 분을 바른 가발을 쓰고, 은빛 장식을 두른 하늘색 코트를 입었는데, 자락은 아주 풍성하고 넓게 만들어졌고 안쪽에는 빳빳한 심을 댄 천을 대었습니다. 티긴 앤드 웰프스는 무늬 옥양목과 조끼 옷감을 취급하는 곳이었지요, 신사 여러분. 그래서 제 삼촌은 그 옷감들을 한눈에 다 알아보았습니다. 그는 무릎까지 오는 반바지를 입었고, 비단 양말 위로 일종의 각반을 둘둘 말아 올렸으며, 버클이 달린 구두를 신고 있었습니다. 손목에는 주름 장식을 달았고, 머리에는 삼각모를 썼으며, 옆구리에는 길고 가느다란 검을 차고 있었습니다. 조끼 자락은 허벅지 중간까지 내려왔고, 크라바트 끝은 허리까지 닿았지요. 그는 점잔을 빼며 마차 문 쪽으로 성큼성큼 걸어가, 모자를 벗어 팔을 쭉 뻗어 머리 위로 들어 올렸는데, 그와 동시에 새끼손가락을 공중에 까딱 세웠습니다. 어떤 점잔 빼는 사람들이 차를 한 잔 들 때 그러듯이요. 그러고는 두 발을 모으고, 낮고 정중하게 절을 한 다음, 왼손을 내밀었습니다. 제 삼촌은 막 앞으로 나서서 그 손을 힘차게 잡으려던 참이었는데, 그 정중한 예가 자기가 아니라 바로 그때 발판 아래에 나타난 한 젊은 숙녀를 향한 것임을 알아차렸습니다. 그 숙녀는 허리가 길고 가슴 장식을 댄 구식 초록색 벨벳 드레스를 입고 있었습니다. 신사 여러분, 그녀는 머리에 보닛을 쓰지 않았고 대신 검은 비단 두건으로 머리를 감쌌는데, 마차에 오르려고 채비하면서 잠깐 주위를 둘러보았습니다. 그때 드러난 그토록 아름다운 얼굴을 제 삼촌은 한 번도 본 적이 없었지요—심지어 그림에서조차도요. 그녀는 한 손으로 드레스 자락을 추켜올린 채 마차에 올랐습니다. 그런데 제 삼촌이 이 이야기를 하실 때마다 큼지막한 욕을 곁들여 늘 하시던 말씀이지만, 그분은 자기 두 눈으로 직접 보지 않았더라면 다리와 발이 그토록 완벽한 경지에 이를 수 있다는 것을 도무지 믿지 못했을 거랍니다.\n\n\"하지만 그 아름다운 얼굴을 한 번 흘끗 보는 사이에, 제 삼촌은 그 젊은 숙녀가 자기에게 애원하는 눈길을 던지는 것을, 그리고 그녀가 겁에 질려 괴로워하는 듯이 보이는 것을 알아차렸습니다. 또한 그분은, 분을 바른 가발을 쓴 젊은 사내가, 겉으로는 매우 멋지고 거창한 정중함을 내보이면서도, 그녀가 탈 때 그녀의 손목을 꽉 움켜잡고는, 곧바로 자기도 뒤따라 올라타는 것을 눈여겨보았습니다. 일행 중에는 또 한 사람, 유난히 험상궂게 생긴 사내가 있었는데, 바싹 붙는 갈색 가발을 쓰고 자줏빛 옷을 입었으며, 아주 큰 검을 차고 엉덩이까지 올라오는 장화를 신고 있었습니다. 그가 젊은 숙녀 옆에 앉자—그녀는 그가 다가오자 구석으로 움츠러들었지요—제 삼촌은 무언가 어둡고 수상쩍은 일이 벌어지고 있다는, 혹은 그분 자신이 늘 하시던 말마따나 '어딘가 나사가 하나 빠져 있다'는 처음의 인상을 굳히게 되었습니다. 그분이 만약 그 숙녀가 도움을 필요로 한다면 어떤 위험을 무릅쓰고라도 그녀를 돕겠다고 얼마나 빨리 마음을 정했는지는 정말이지 놀라울 정도입니다.\n\n\"'죽음과 번개로다!' 제 삼촌이 마차에 오를 때 젊은 신사가 검에 손을 얹으며 외쳤습니다.\n\n\"'피와 천둥이다!' 다른 신사가 으르렁거렸습니다. 그러고는 검을 휙 뽑아 들더니, 더 이상 격식도 차리지 않고 제 삼촌을 향해 찔러 들어왔습니다. 제 삼촌은 몸에 무기 하나 지니고 있지 않았지만, 굉장한 솜씨로 그 험상궂은 신사의 삼각모를 머리에서 낚아채, 검 끝이 모자 꼭대기를 곧장 꿰뚫고 들어오자, 양쪽을 한데 쥐어짜 단단히 붙들었습니다.\n\n\"'뒤에서 찔러 버려!' 험상궂은 신사가 검을 되찾으려 안간힘을 쓰며 동료에게 소리쳤습니다.\n\n\"'안 하는 게 좋을걸.' 제 삼촌이 위협하듯 한쪽 구두 뒤축을 들어 보이며 외쳤습니다. '머리통에 골이 있거든 그 골을 걷어차 버리겠다—없거든 두개골을 박살 내 주지.' 바로 이 순간, 제 삼촌은 온 힘을 다해 그 험상궂은 사내의 검을 그의 손아귀에서 비틀어 빼앗아, 마차 창밖으로 멀찍이 내던졌습니다. 그러자 젊은 신사가 다시 '죽음과 번개로다!' 하고 고래고래 외치며, 아주 사납게 자기 검 자루에 손을 얹었지만, 검을 뽑지는 않았습니다. 어쩌면, 신사 여러분, 제 삼촌이 미소를 지으며 늘 하시던 말씀처럼, 어쩌면 그는 숙녀를 놀라게 할까 봐 두려웠던 것이겠지요.\n\n\"'자, 신사 여러분.' 제 삼촌이 침착하게 자리에 앉으며 말했습니다. '나는 숙녀가 계신 자리에서 죽음이든 뭐든, 번개가 따르든 안 따르든, 그런 걸 벌이고 싶지 않소. 그리고 우리는 이번 여정 하나치고는 피와 천둥을 충분히 겪었소. 그러니 괜찮다면 우리 모두 얌전한 안쪽 승객답게 제자리에 앉읍시다. 이봐요, 차장, 저 신사의 식칼이나 좀 주워 주시오.'\n\n\"제 삼촌이 그 말을 하기가 무섭게, 차장이 그 신사의 검을 손에 들고 마차 창가에 나타났습니다. 그는 검을 안으로 건네면서 등불을 치켜들고 제 삼촌의 얼굴을 진지하게 들여다보았는데, 그 불빛으로 제 삼촌은 크게 놀라며 보았습니다. 어마어마한 수의 우편 마차 차장들이 창문 둘레에 떼 지어 몰려 있었고, 그들 하나하나가 역시 진지한 눈으로 자기를 응시하고 있었던 것이지요. 그분은 태어나서 그토록 많은 하얀 얼굴과 붉은 몸뚱이와 진지한 눈동자의 바다를 본 적이 없었습니다.\n\n\"'이건 내가 이제껏 관여해 본 것 중에 가장 기이한 일이로군.' 제 삼촌은 속으로 생각했습니다. '모자를 돌려 드리지요, 선생.'\n\n\"험상궂은 신사는 말없이 자기 삼각모를 받아, 한가운데 뚫린 구멍을 미심쩍은 표정으로 바라보다가, 마침내 자못 엄숙하게 그것을 자기 가발 꼭대기에 눌러썼습니다. 그런데 그 순간 그가 격렬하게 재채기를 하며 모자를 다시 홱 떨어뜨리는 바람에, 그 엄숙함의 효과가 약간 손상되고 말았지요.\n\n\"'다 됐소!' 등불을 든 차장이 뒤쪽 자기 작은 자리에 올라타며 외쳤습니다. 그들은 출발했습니다. 제 삼촌은 마당을 빠져나오면서 마차 창밖을 내다보았는데, 다른 우편 마차들이 마부와 차장과 말과 승객까지 완비한 채, 시속 5마일쯤의 느린 속보로 원을 그리며 빙글빙글 돌고 있는 것을 보았습니다. 제 삼촌은 분노로 들끓었지요, 신사 여러분. 상업에 종사하는 사람으로서 그분은 우편 행낭을 가지고 장난쳐서는 안 된다고 느꼈고, 런던에 도착하는 바로 그 순간 이 문제에 관해 우체국에 진정서를 내기로 결심했습니다.\n\n\"하지만 지금 당장은, 그분의 생각이 마차의 가장 먼 구석에 두건으로 얼굴을 바싹 감싸고 앉아 있는 젊은 숙녀에게 쏠려 있었습니다. 하늘색 코트를 입은 신사는 그녀 맞은편에 앉아 있었고, 자줏빛 옷을 입은 다른 사내는 그녀 옆에 앉아 있었으며, 둘 다 그녀를 뚫어지게 지켜보고 있었습니다. 그녀가 두건 자락을 조금이라도 바스락거리면, 험상궂은 사내가 검에 손을 탁 얹는 소리가 들렸고, 다른 사내의 숨소리로 보아(너무 어두워서 그 얼굴은 볼 수 없었지요) 그가 마치 그녀를 한입에 집어삼킬 듯이 험악한 표정을 짓고 있다는 것을 알 수 있었습니다. 이것이 제 삼촌을 점점 더 격앙시켰고, 그분은 무슨 일이 있어도 이 일의 결말을 보고야 말겠다고 결심했습니다. 그분은 빛나는 눈과 어여쁜 얼굴과 예쁜 다리와 발을 무척 흠모했습니다. 요컨대 그분은 그 성(性) 전체를 좋아하셨지요. 이건 우리 집안 내력입니다, 신사 여러분—저도 그러니까요.\n\n\"제 삼촌은 그 숙녀의 주의를 끌거나, 적어도 그 수상쩍은 신사들을 대화에 끌어들이려고 갖가지 수단을 부려 보았습니다. 모두 헛수고였지요. 신사들은 말을 하려 들지 않았고, 숙녀는 감히 말을 하지 못했습니다. 그분은 이따금 마차 창밖으로 머리를 내밀고, 왜 더 빨리 가지 않느냐고 고래고래 소리쳤습니다. 하지만 목이 쉬도록 불러 댔어도, 아무도 그분에게 조금도 주의를 기울이지 않았습니다. 그분은 마차에 등을 기대고 그 아름다운 얼굴과 발과 다리를 생각했습니다. 이쪽이 더 나았지요. 그것은 시간을 때워 주었고, 자기가 어디로 가는 건지, 어쩌다 이런 괴상한 처지에 놓이게 되었는지 의아해하지 않게 해 주었습니다. 하기야 그런 것이 어차피 그분을 그리 괴롭히지도 않았겠지만요—제 삼촌은 더없이 자유롭고 느긋하며, 떠돌기 좋아하고, 될 대로 되라는 식의 사람이었으니까요, 신사 여러분.\n\n\"별안간 마차가 멈춰 섰습니다. '이봐!' 제 삼촌이 말했습니다. '이번엔 또 무슨 바람이 분 거요?'\n\n\"'여기서 내리시오.' 차장이 발판을 내리며 말했습니다.\n\n\"'여기서!' 제 삼촌이 외쳤습니다.\n\n\"'여기서요.' 차장이 대꾸했습니다.\n\n\"'난 그런 짓은 하지 않겠소.' 제 삼촌이 말했습니다.\n\n\"'좋소, 그럼 그 자리에 그대로 있으시오.' 차장이 말했습니다.\n\n\"'그러겠소.' 제 삼촌이 말했습니다.\n\n\"'그러시구려.' 차장이 말했습니다.\n\n\"승객들은 이 대화를 매우 주의 깊게 지켜보고 있었는데, 제 삼촌이 내리지 않기로 작정했다는 것을 알자, 젊은 사내가 그를 비집고 지나가 숙녀를 부축해 내렸습니다. 그때 험상궂은 사내는 자기 삼각모 꼭대기에 뚫린 구멍을 살피고 있었지요. 젊은 숙녀가 스쳐 지나가면서, 제 삼촌의 손에 자기 장갑 한 짝을 떨어뜨리고는, 그 입술을 그의 얼굴에 어찌나 가까이 댔던지 그가 자기 코에 그녀의 따뜻한 숨결을 느낄 만큼 가까이서, '도와주세요!'라는 단 한마디를 가만히 속삭였습니다. 신사 여러분, 제 삼촌은 당장 마차에서 펄쩍 뛰어내렸는데, 어찌나 힘차게 뛰어내렸던지 마차가 다시 용수철 위에서 흔들거렸습니다.\n\n\"'오! 마음을 고쳐먹으셨구려, 응?' 제 삼촌이 땅 위에 서 있는 것을 본 차장이 말했습니다.\n\n\"제 삼촌은 몇 초 동안 차장을 바라보며, 차라리 그에게서 나팔총을 비틀어 빼앗아, 큰 검을 든 사내의 얼굴에 그것을 쏘아 버리고, 개머리판으로 나머지 일행의 머리를 후려친 다음, 젊은 숙녀를 낚아채 연기 속으로 달아나 버리는 게 낫지 않을까 잠시 망설였습니다. 그러나 다시 생각해 보니, 그것은 실행에 옮기기에는 약간 지나치게 멜로드라마 같다는 생각이 들어 그 계획을 단념하고, 두 수상쩍은 사내를 뒤따랐습니다. 그들은 숙녀를 가운데 둔 채, 마차가 멈춰 선 그 앞의 어느 낡은 집으로 들어서고 있었지요. 그들이 통로로 꺾어 들자, 제 삼촌도 뒤따랐습니다.\n\n\"제 삼촌이 이제껏 본 그 모든 폐허가 되고 황량한 곳들 가운데, 이곳이 가장 심했습니다. 그것은 한때 큰 유흥 시설이었던 것처럼 보였지만, 지붕은 여러 군데가 내려앉았고, 계단은 가파르고 울퉁불퉁하며 부서져 있었습니다. 그들이 걸어 들어간 방에는 거대한 벽난로가 있었고, 굴뚝은 그을음으로 시커멨습니다. 하지만 이제는 어떤 따뜻한 불꽃도 그것을 밝혀 주지 않았지요. 타다 남은 나무의 하얀 깃털 같은 재가 아직 화덕 위에 흩어져 있었지만, 난로는 차가웠고, 모든 것이 어둡고 음울했습니다.\n\n\"'허허,' 제 삼촌이 주위를 둘러보며 말했습니다. '시속 6마일 반으로 달리는 우편 마차가 이런 누추한 굴에서 기약 없이 멈춰 서다니, 이건 꽤나 규칙에 어긋나는 처사로군. 이 일은 알려져야겠어. 내가 신문에다 투고하겠소.'\n\n\"제 삼촌은 가능하다면 두 낯선 자를 대화에 끌어들일 속셈으로, 꽤 큰 목소리로, 거리낌 없이 탁 트인 태도로 이 말을 했습니다. 하지만 그들 중 누구도 그분에게 그 이상의 주의를 기울이지 않았고, 그저 서로 속닥거리며 그러는 동안 그분을 노려보기만 했습니다. 숙녀는 방의 가장 먼 끝에 있었는데, 한번은 마치 제 삼촌의 도움을 간청하듯 손을 흔들어 보이는 모험을 감행했습니다.\n\n\"마침내 두 낯선 자가 조금 다가왔고, 대화가 본격적으로 시작되었습니다.\n\n\"'여기가 사사로운 방이라는 걸 모르는 모양이군, 이 작자야?' 하늘색 옷을 입은 신사가 말했습니다.\n\n\"'모르오, 이 작자야.' 제 삼촌이 대꾸했습니다. '다만, 이게 이번 일을 위해 특별히 마련한 사사로운 방이라면, 공용 방이라는 데는 어지간히 안락한 곳이겠다는 생각이 드는군.' 그러고 나서 제 삼촌은 등받이가 높은 의자에 앉아, 두 눈으로 그 신사의 치수를 어찌나 정확히 재었던지, 티긴 앤드 웰프스는 그 어림짐작 하나만으로도 그에게 한 치도 많거나 적지 않게 옷 한 벌 분량의 무늬 옥양목을 댈 수 있었을 겁니다.\n\n\"'이 방에서 나가시지.' 두 사내가 검을 움켜쥐며 동시에 말했습니다.\n\n\"'엥?' 제 삼촌이 그들의 말뜻을 도무지 못 알아듣는 시늉을 하며 말했습니다.\n\n\"'이 방에서 나가, 안 그러면 넌 죽은 목숨이다.' 큰 검을 든 험상궂은 사내가, 동시에 검을 뽑아 공중에 휘두르며 말했습니다.\n\n\"'저놈을 쓰러뜨려라!' 하늘색 옷을 입은 신사가 역시 검을 뽑아 들고 두세 야드 뒤로 물러서며 외쳤습니다. '저놈을 쓰러뜨려!' 숙녀가 크게 비명을 질렀습니다.\n\n\"그런데 제 삼촌은 늘 대단한 대담함과 대단한 침착함으로 유명한 분이었습니다. 그분이 무슨 일이 벌어지든 그토록 무심한 척하던 내내, 사실은 방어용으로 쓸 무슨 던질 만한 것이나 무기가 없나 슬그머니 둘러보고 있었던 것이지요. 그리고 검이 뽑힌 바로 그 순간, 그분은 굴뚝 모퉁이에 녹슨 칼집에 든 낡은 바구니 손잡이 레이피어 한 자루가 세워져 있는 것을 발견했습니다. 제 삼촌은 단번에 그것을 손에 움켜쥐고, 칼을 뽑아, 늠름하게 머리 위로 휘두르고는, 숙녀에게 비키라고 큰 소리로 외친 다음, 의자를 하늘색 옷의 사내에게, 칼집을 자줏빛 옷의 사내에게 내던지고, 그 혼란을 틈타 두 사내 모두에게 마구잡이로 덤벼들었습니다.\n* 레이피어: 가늘고 긴 양날 찌르기용 검.\n\n\"신사 여러분, 한 멋진 아일랜드 청년에 관한 옛이야기가 하나 있습니다—사실이라고 해서 못할 것도 없는 이야기지요. 누군가 그에게 바이올린을 켤 줄 아느냐고 묻자, 그는 분명 켤 수 있을 거라고는 생각하지만, 한 번도 켜 본 적이 없어서 확실히는 말 못 하겠다고 대답했답니다. 이 이야기는 제 삼촌과 그분의 검술에 들어맞지 않는 바가 아닙니다. 그분은 그때까지 한 번 말고는 손에 검을 쥐어 본 적이 없었거든요. 그 한 번이란 어느 사설 극장에서 리처드 3세를 연기했을 때였는데, 그때는 그분이 전혀 싸우는 시늉도 없이 뒤에서 찔리는 것으로 리치먼드와 미리 짜 놓았던 터였지요. 그런데 지금 그분은 두 노련한 검객을 상대로 베고 후려치고, 찌르고 막고, 쑤시고 가르며, 가능한 한 가장 사내답고 능란한 솜씨로 자기 몫을 해내고 있었습니다. 그때까지 그분은 자기에게 그 기술에 대한 소양이 조금이라도 있다는 것을 전혀 알지 못했는데도 말이지요. 이것은 사람이란 한번 해 보기 전에는 자기가 무엇을 할 수 있는지 결코 모른다는 옛말이 얼마나 참된 것인지를 보여 줄 뿐입니다, 신사 여러분.\n\n\"싸움의 소음은 무시무시했습니다. 세 명의 싸움꾼은 저마다 기병처럼 욕설을 퍼부었고, 그들의 검은 마치 뉴포트 시장의 모든 칼과 강철이 동시에 한데 부딪쳐 덜그럭거리는 것만큼이나 큰 소리를 내며 맞부딪쳤습니다. 그것이 한창 절정에 이르렀을 때, 숙녀는(아마도 제 삼촌을 격려하기 위해서였겠지요) 두건을 얼굴에서 완전히 걷어 냈고, 그토록 눈부시게 아름다운 용모를 드러냈으니, 그분은 그 얼굴에서 미소 하나를 얻을 수만 있다면 쉰 명의 사내를 상대로라도 싸우다 죽었을 겁니다. 그분은 이미 놀라운 일을 해내고 있었지만, 이제는 미쳐 날뛰는 거인처럼 마구 휘둘러 대기 시작했습니다.\n\n\"바로 이 순간, 하늘색 옷을 입은 신사가 돌아서서 젊은 숙녀가 얼굴을 드러낸 것을 보고는, 분노와 질투의 외침을 토해 내며, 자기 무기를 그녀의 아름다운 가슴을 향해 돌려, 그녀의 심장을 겨누어 찔러 들어갔습니다. 그 바람에 제 삼촌은 건물이 울리도록 우려의 비명을 내질렀지요. 숙녀는 가볍게 옆으로 비켜서서, 그가 균형을 되찾기 전에 젊은 사내의 손에서 검을 낚아채, 그를 벽 쪽으로 몰아붙이고는, 칼을 그의 몸과 벽 판자를 자루 끝까지 꿰뚫도록 찔러 넣어, 그를 거기에 단단히 못 박아 버렸습니다. 그것은 훌륭한 본보기였습니다. 제 삼촌은 승리의 함성을 크게 내지르며, 거역할 수 없는 힘으로 자기 상대를 같은 방향으로 후퇴시켜, 그 낡은 레이피어를 그자 조끼 무늬에 있는 커다란 붉은 꽃의 한복판에 푹 찔러 넣어, 그를 그 친구 옆에 못 박아 버렸습니다. 거기서 두 사내는, 신사 여러분, 노끈 한 가닥으로 움직이는 장난감 가게의 인형들처럼, 고통 속에 팔다리를 버둥거리며 서 있었지요. 제 삼촌은 나중에 늘 말씀하시기를, 이것이 그분이 아는 적을 처치하는 가장 확실한 방법 가운데 하나라고 하셨습니다. 다만 비용 면에서 한 가지 흠이 있었으니, 무력화시키는 사람 하나당 검 한 자루를 잃는 셈이었기 때문이지요.\n\n\"'마차예요, 마차!' 숙녀가 제 삼촌에게 달려와 그 아름다운 두 팔로 그의 목을 끌어안으며 외쳤습니다. '우리 아직 달아날 수 있어요.'\n\n\"'있다고요!' 제 삼촌이 외쳤습니다. '아니, 그런데 여보, 이제 더 죽일 사람이 아무도 없잖소, 그렇지 않소?' 제 삼촌은 다소 실망하셨지요, 신사 여러분. 그분은 학살이 끝난 뒤에는 화제라도 좀 바꿀 겸, 조용한 사랑 놀음이 좀 있으면 좋겠다고 생각하셨거든요.\n\n\"'여기서 한순간도 지체할 수 없어요.' 젊은 숙녀가 말했습니다. '저 사람은(하늘색 옷을 입은 젊은 신사를 가리키며) 막강한 필레토빌 후작의 외아들이에요.'\n\n\"'그렇다면, 여보, 그자는 영영 작위를 잇지 못할까 봐 걱정이구려.' 제 삼촌이, 제가 앞서 묘사한 그 풍뎅이 같은 모양새로 벽에 박힌 채 서 있는 젊은 신사를 냉랭하게 바라보며 말했습니다. '당신이 한정 상속을 끊어 버렸으니까, 내 사랑.'\n\n\"'저는 저 악당들에게 제 집과 친구들에게서 끌려왔어요.' 젊은 숙녀가, 분노로 이목구비를 붉히며 말했습니다. '저 비열한 자가 한 시간만 더 있으면 폭력으로 저와 결혼하려 했을 거예요.'\n\n\"'저런 뻔뻔한 놈을 봤나!' 제 삼촌이 죽어 가는 필레토빌의 상속자에게 자못 경멸 어린 눈길을 던지며 말했습니다.\n\n\"'보신 것으로 짐작하시겠지만,' 젊은 숙녀가 말했습니다. '저 일당은 제가 누구에게든 도움을 청하면 저를 죽일 작정이었어요. 저들의 공범들이 우리를 여기서 발견하면, 우린 끝장이에요. 지금부터 이 분 후면 너무 늦을지도 몰라요. 마차로!' 이 말과 함께, 그녀는 북받치는 감정과 젊은 필레토빌 후작을 찌르느라 들인 힘에 압도되어, 제 삼촌의 두 팔 안으로 쓰러졌습니다. 제 삼촌은 그녀를 받아 안아 집 문 쪽으로 데려갔습니다. 거기에는 마차가, 꼬리가 길고 갈기가 휘날리는 네 마리의 검은 말이 이미 마구를 갖춘 채, 서 있었습니다. 하지만 마부도 없고, 차장도 없고, 말머리 곁에는 마부 조수조차 없었습니다.\n\n\"신사 여러분, 제 삼촌이 비록 독신이긴 했지만 이때 이전에도 몇몇 숙녀를 품에 안아 본 적이 있다는 제 의견을 밝히더라도, 그분의 명예에 누를 끼치는 것은 아니리라 생각합니다. 실은 그분이 술집 아가씨들에게 입맞추는 버릇이 꽤 있었다고 저는 믿습니다. 그리고 한두 차례는 그분이 어느 여관 안주인을 아주 눈에 띄게 끌어안는 것을 믿을 만한 증인들이 보았다는 것도 압니다. 제가 이런 정황을 언급하는 까닭은, 이 아름다운 젊은 숙녀가 제 삼촌을 그토록 사로잡았으니 얼마나 보기 드문 부류의 사람이었음이 틀림없는지를 보여 드리기 위해서입니다. 그분이 늘 하시던 말씀이, 그녀의 길고 검은 머리카락이 자기 팔 위로 늘어지고, 그녀가 정신을 차렸을 때 그 아름답고 검은 두 눈이 자기 얼굴에 못 박히자, 자기는 어찌나 이상하고 떨렸던지 다리가 후들거렸다는 겁니다. 하지만 누가 그 달콤하고 부드러운 검은 두 눈을 들여다보면서 기분이 묘해지지 않을 수 있겠습니까? 저는 못 합니다, 신사 여러분. 저는 제가 아는 어떤 눈들은 차마 들여다보기가 두렵습니다. 그게 사실입니다.\n\n\"'당신은 절대 저를 떠나지 않으실 거죠.' 젊은 숙녀가 속삭였습니다.\n\n\"'절대로.' 제 삼촌이 말했습니다. 그리고 그분은 진심으로 그럴 작정이었지요.\n\n\"'제 소중한 구원자여!' 젊은 숙녀가 외쳤습니다. '제 소중하고, 친절하고, 용감한 구원자여!'\n\n\"'그러지 마시오.' 제 삼촌이 그녀의 말을 가로막으며 말했습니다.\n\n\"'왜요?' 젊은 숙녀가 물었습니다.\n\n\"'당신이 말할 때면 그 입이 너무도 아름다워 보여서 말이오.' 제 삼촌이 대꾸했습니다. '그만 내가 무례하게도 거기에 입을 맞춰 버릴까 봐 두렵소.'\n\n\"젊은 숙녀는 제 삼촌에게 그러지 말라고 주의를 주기라도 하듯 손을 들어 올리고는—아니, 그녀는 아무 말도 하지 않았습니다—미소 지었습니다. 세상에서 가장 감미로운 한 쌍의 입술을 바라보고 있을 때, 그 입술이 부드럽게 장난기 어린 미소로 살며시 벌어지는 것을 본다면—그것도 아주 가까이 있고 곁에 아무도 없다면—그 아름다운 모양과 빛깔에 대한 흠모를 곧장 입맞춤하는 것보다 더 잘 증명할 길은 없는 법이지요. 제 삼촌은 그렇게 했고, 저는 그 일로 그분을 떳떳이 여깁니다.\n\n\"'쉿!' 젊은 숙녀가 흠칫 놀라며 외쳤습니다. '바퀴 소리예요, 그리고 말들이!'\n\n\"'정말 그렇군.' 제 삼촌이 귀를 기울이며 말했습니다. 그분은 바퀴 소리와 말발굽 소리에 밝은 귀를 지니고 있었지요. 하지만 멀리서 어찌나 많은 말과 마차가 그들을 향해 덜컹거리며 다가오는 것 같던지, 그 수를 짐작하기란 불가능했습니다. 그 소리는 마치 짐마차 쉰 대가, 각각에 혈통 좋은 소 여섯 마리씩을 매단 것 같았지요.\n\n\"'우린 쫓기고 있어요!' 젊은 숙녀가 두 손을 맞잡으며 외쳤습니다. '우린 쫓기고 있어요. 제겐 당신밖에는 희망이 없어요!'\n\n\"그녀의 아름다운 얼굴에는 그토록 공포에 질린 표정이 어려 있어서, 제 삼촌은 단번에 마음을 정했습니다. 그분은 그녀를 마차 안으로 들어 올리고, 겁먹지 말라고 말한 뒤, 다시 한번 그녀의 입술에 자기 입술을 댄 다음, 찬 공기가 들어오지 않도록 창문을 올리라고 일러 주고는, 마부석으로 올라갔습니다.\n\n\"'잠깐만요, 여보.' 젊은 숙녀가 외쳤습니다.\n\n\"'무슨 일이오?' 제 삼촌이 마부석에서 말했습니다.\n\n\"'당신에게 할 말이 있어요.' 젊은 숙녀가 말했습니다. '딱 한마디만요. 단 한마디만요, 가장 사랑하는 분.'\n\n\"'내가 내려가야 하오?' 제 삼촌이 물었습니다. 숙녀는 아무 대답도 하지 않았지만, 다시 미소 지었습니다. 그 미소라니요, 신사 여러분! 앞서의 그것을 완전히 무색하게 만들 정도였지요. 제 삼촌은 눈 깜짝할 사이에 자기 자리에서 내려왔습니다.\n\n\"'무슨 일이오, 여보?' 제 삼촌이 마차 창 안을 들여다보며 말했습니다. 숙녀가 마침 그와 동시에 몸을 앞으로 숙였는데, 제 삼촌은 그녀가 지금껏보다 한층 더 아름다워 보인다고 생각했습니다. 바로 그때 그분은 그녀에게 아주 가까이 있었지요, 신사 여러분. 그러니 그분이야말로 정말로 잘 알았을 겁니다.\n\n\"'무슨 일이오, 여보?' 제 삼촌이 말했습니다.\n\n\"'당신은 저 말고 다른 누구도 절대 사랑하지 않으시겠죠—저 말고 다른 누구와도 절대 결혼하지 않으시겠죠?' 젊은 숙녀가 말했습니다.\n\n\"제 삼촌은 자기는 절대 다른 누구와도 결혼하지 않겠다고 큼지막한 맹세를 했고, 젊은 숙녀는 머리를 안으로 들이고 창문을 올렸습니다. 그분은 마부석으로 펄쩍 뛰어올라, 양 팔꿈치를 가지런히 펴고, 고삐를 매만지고, 지붕에 놓여 있던 채찍을 움켜쥐고는, 바깥쪽 선두마에게 한 번 휙 채찍질을 했습니다. 그러자 꼬리가 길고 갈기가 휘날리는 네 마리 검은 말이, 그 뒤로 낡은 우편 마차를 매단 채, 시속 15마일이라는 어엿한 영국식 속도로 내달렸습니다. 휘익! 그것들이 어떻게 질주했던지요!\n\n\"뒤쪽의 소음이 점점 커졌습니다. 낡은 우편 마차가 빨리 갈수록, 추격자들도 그만큼 빨리 따라왔습니다—사람과 말과 개가 추격에 한 패가 되었지요. 그 소음은 무시무시했지만, 무엇보다 젊은 숙녀의 목소리가 그 위로 높이 솟아올라, 제 삼촌을 다그치며 '더 빨리! 더 빨리!' 하고 비명을 질렀습니다.\n\n\"그들은 마치 깃털이 허리케인 앞에 휩쓸리듯 어두운 나무들을 휙휙 지나쳤습니다. 집과 대문과 교회와 건초더미와 온갖 종류의 사물들을, 그들은 갑자기 풀려난 으르렁대는 물살 같은 속도와 소음으로 쏜살같이 지나쳤습니다. 하지만 그래도 추격의 소음은 점점 커져만 갔고, 그래도 제 삼촌은 젊은 숙녀가 미친 듯이 '더 빨리! 더 빨리!' 하고 비명 지르는 것을 들을 수 있었습니다.\n\n\"제 삼촌은 채찍과 고삐를 부지런히 놀렸고, 말들은 거품으로 하얘질 때까지 앞으로 날아갔습니다. 그래도 뒤쪽의 소음은 더해 갔고, 그래도 젊은 숙녀는 '더 빨리! 더 빨리!' 하고 외쳤습니다. 제 삼촌은 그 순간의 격정에 차서 마차 짐칸을 쾅 하고 세게 굴렀는데—그러자 어느덧 잿빛 아침이었고, 그분은 수레바퀴 목수의 마당에서, 낡은 에든버러 우편 마차의 마부석에 앉아, 추위와 습기에 덜덜 떨며 발을 따뜻하게 하려고 동동 구르고 있는 것이었습니다! 그분은 내려가서, 그 아름다운 젊은 숙녀를 찾으려고 안쪽을 열심히 들여다보았습니다. 아아! 마차에는 문도 좌석도 없었습니다. 그것은 그저 텅 빈 껍데기였지요.\n\n\"물론 제 삼촌은 이 일에 무언가 신비로운 것이 있다는 것을, 그리고 모든 일이 그분이 늘 이야기하시던 그대로 정확히 일어났다는 것을 아주 잘 알고 계셨습니다. 그분은 그 아름다운 젊은 숙녀에게 맹세한 그 큼지막한 맹세에 변함없이 충실하여, 그녀를 생각해 마땅한 여관 안주인 여럿을 마다하고, 끝내 독신으로 돌아가셨습니다. 그분은 늘 말씀하시기를, 그저 울타리를 기어 넘는 것 같은 순전한 우연으로, 우편 마차와 말과 차장과 마부와 승객의 유령들이 밤마다 규칙적으로 여행을 다니는 버릇이 있다는 것을 알아냈으니 참으로 묘한 일이라고 하셨습니다. 그분은 덧붙여 말씀하시기를, 자기야말로 이런 야간 행차들 가운데 한 번에 승객으로 태워진 유일한 산 사람이리라 믿는다고 하셨지요. 그리고 저는 그분이 옳았다고 생각합니다, 신사 여러분—적어도 저는 그런 사람이 또 있다는 이야기는 들어 본 적이 없으니까요.\"\n\n\"그 우편 마차 유령들은 행낭 속에 무엇을 싣고 다니는지 궁금하군요.\" 그 이야기 전체를 깊은 주의를 기울여 듣고 있던 여관 주인이 말했다.\n\"물론 사신(死信)들이지요.\" 외판원이 말했다.\n\n\"오, 아! 그렇고말고.\" 여관 주인이 대꾸했다. \"그건 미처 생각 못 했군요.\"\n\n* 사신(死信): 수취인 불명 등으로 배달되지 못한 우편물.\n\n이튿날 아침, 정확히 여덟 시 사십오 분에 말이 매여졌고, 픽윅 씨와 샘 웰러가 각각 자리를 잡되 한 사람은 안에, 다른 한 사람은 바깥에 앉자, 마부에게는 우선 보브 소여 씨의 집으로 가서 벤저민 앨런 씨를 태우라는 지시가 정식으로 내려졌다.\n\n마차가 붉은 등이 달린 문 앞에, 그리고 ‘소여, 전 노케모프’라는 매우 또렷한 글귀가 적힌 그 문 앞에 멈춰 섰을 때, 픽윅 씨가 마차 창밖으로 머리를 쑥 내밀고 회색 제복을 입은 소년이 분주하게 덧문을 닫는 모습을 본 것은 적잖은 놀라움이었다. 아침 이 시각에 그런 일은 흔치 않고 또 영업답지 않은 처사인지라, 그것은 곧 그의 마음속에 두 가지 추론을 불러일으켰다. 하나는 보브 소여 씨의 어떤 좋은 친구이자 환자가 죽었으리라는 것이고, 다른 하나는 보브 소여 씨 자신이 파산했으리라는 것이었다.\n\n“무슨 일이냐?” 픽윅 씨가 소년에게 물었다.\n\n“아무 일도 없어요, 선생님.” 소년이 얼굴 전체 너비만큼 입을 벌리며 대답했다.\n\n“좋아, 좋아!” 보브 소여가 한 손에는 축 늘어지고 더러운 작은 가죽 배낭을, 다른 팔에는 거친 외투와 숄을 걸친 채 갑자기 문간에 나타나며 외쳤다. “나도 가네, 친구.”\n\n“자네가!” 픽윅 씨가 소리쳤다.\n\n“그렇다네.” 보브 소여가 대답했다. “아주 제대로 된 원정으로 만들어 보세나. 여기, 샘! 받게!” 이렇게 짧게 웰러 씨의 주의를 청한 보브 소여 씨는 가죽 배낭을 마부석 뒤 좌석으로 휙 던졌고, 샘은 그 일을 크게 감탄하며 지켜보다가 곧장 좌석 밑에 챙겨 넣었다. 이 일이 끝나자 보브 소여 씨는 소년의 도움을 받아 몇 치수 작은 거친 외투 속으로 힘껏 몸을 밀어 넣고는, 마차 창으로 다가가 머리를 들이밀며 요란하게 웃었다.\n\n“이것 참 깜짝 출발 아닌가, 안 그래?” 보브가 거친 외투 소맷부리 한쪽으로 눈물을 닦으며 외쳤다.\n\n“여보게,” 픽윅 씨가 다소 당황하여 말했다. “자네가 우리와 동행하리라곤 생각도 못 했네.”\n\n“그렇지, 바로 그게 핵심이라네.” 보브가 픽윅 씨의 외투 깃을 움켜쥐며 대꾸했다. “그게 농담이지.”\n\n“오, 그게 농담이란 말이지?” 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n“물론이지.” 보브가 대답했다. “그게 전부 핵심이라네—그것, 그리고 영업을 제 알아서 돌아가게 내버려 두는 것 말일세. 영업이 나를 돌볼 마음이 없는 모양이니 말이야.” 덧문 현상에 대한 이런 설명과 함께, 보브 소여 씨는 가게를 가리키더니 다시 환희에 찬 웃음으로 빠져들었다.\n\n“이런, 자네 설마 아무도 돌볼 사람 없이 환자들을 버려두려는 미친 생각을 하는 건 아니겠지!” 픽윅 씨가 매우 진지한 어조로 항의했다.\n\n“왜 안 되나?” 보브가 되물었다. “그렇게 하면 절약이 된다네, 알다시피. 그자들 누구도 돈을 안 내거든. 게다가,” 보브가 은밀한 속삭임으로 목소리를 낮추며 말했다. “그렇게 하는 편이 그들에게도 훨씬 나아. 약이 거의 다 떨어졌고 지금 당장은 외상을 늘릴 수도 없으니, 결국 모두에게 감홍을 처방해야 했을 텐데, 그러면 그중 몇몇은 분명 탈이 났을 테니까. 그러니 모든 게 잘된 일이지.”\n\n이 대답에는 픽윅 씨가 미처 예상치 못한 철학과 추론의 힘이 깃들어 있었다. 그는 잠시 말을 멈추었다가, 아까보다 덜 단호하게 덧붙였다—\n\n“하지만 이 마차는, 여보게 젊은 친구, 두 사람밖에 못 태우네. 게다가 나는 앨런 씨에게 약속을 했네.”\n\n“저는 조금도 신경 쓰지 마십시오.” 보브가 대답했다. “다 마련해 두었습니다. 샘과 제가 마부석을 나눠 앉으면 됩니다. 보십시오. 이 작은 안내문은 가게 문에 붙일 겁니다. ‘소여, 전 노케모프. 건너편 크립스 부인에게 문의하시오.’ 크립스 부인은 제 소년의 어머니입니다. ‘소여 씨가 몹시 죄송해하십니다.’ 크립스 부인이 말하겠지요. ‘어쩔 수 없는 일이었답니다—오늘 아침 일찍이 이 나라 최고 외과 의사들의 진료 회의에 불려 가셨답니다—그분 없이는 안 된다고—어떤 값을 치르더라도 모셔 가겠다고—엄청난 수술이랍니다.’ 사실은,” 보브가 결론지으며 말했다. “이게 오히려 제게 득이 될 거라고 봅니다. 지방 신문 어딘가에 실리기라도 하면, 그게 제 출세를 만들어 줄 테죠. 자, 벤이 왔습니다. 어서, 타게!”\n\n이렇게 서두른 말과 함께 보브 소여 씨는 마부 소년을 한쪽으로 밀치고, 친구를 마차 안으로 홱 밀어 넣고, 문을 쾅 닫고, 발판을 올리고, 거리 쪽 문에 안내문을 붙이고, 문을 잠그고, 열쇠를 주머니에 넣고, 마부석으로 뛰어올라 출발 신호를 내렸으니, 그 모든 일을 어찌나 비상한 신속함으로 해치웠던지, 픽윅 씨가 보브 소여 씨가 가야 할지 말아야 할지를 제대로 따져 보기도 전에, 일행은 보브 소여 씨를 마차의 떼려야 뗄 수 없는 일부로 완전히 자리잡힌 채로 굴러가고 있었다.\n\n브리스틀 거리를 벗어나지 않는 동안에는, 익살꾼 보브도 직업용 녹색 안경을 쓴 채 점잖고 진중한 태도로 처신했고, 그저 새뮤얼 웰러 씨만을 위한 갖가지 말장난을 늘어놓을 뿐이었다. 그러나 탁 트인 길로 나서자, 그는 녹색 안경과 진중함을 한꺼번에 벗어 던지고는, 행인들의 이목을 끌고 마차와 그 안의 사람들을 보통 이상의 호기심거리로 만들기에 딱 알맞은 갖가지 짓궂은 장난을 펼쳤다. 그 묘기들 가운데 가장 덜 눈에 띄는 것이라야, 매우 큰 소리로 키뷰글을 흉내 내는 것과, 지팡이에 매단 진홍빛 비단 손수건을 과시하듯 내보이는 것으로, 그 손수건은 이따금 우월함과 도전을 나타내는 갖가지 몸짓과 함께 공중에 휘둘러졌다.\n\n“궁금하군.” 윈클 씨와 그 누이의 수많은 미덕에 관한 매우 차분한 이야기를 벤 앨런과 나누던 중에 말을 멈추며 픽윅 씨가 말했다. “우리가 지나치는 사람들이 우리에게서 대체 무얼 보길래 저렇게들 빤히 쳐다보는지 궁금하군.”\n\n“멋진 차림새 아닙니까.” 벤 앨런이 어딘지 자랑스러운 어조로 대답했다. “저들은 이런 걸 매일 보는 데 익숙지 않을 테죠, 아마.”\n\n“그럴 수도 있지.” 픽윅 씨가 대답했다. “그럴지도 모르지. 아마 그렇겠지.”\n\n픽윅 씨는 정말로 그러하다는 믿음에 스스로를 설득시켰을지도 모른다. 다만 바로 그때 마차 창밖을 내다보다가, 행인들의 표정이 공손한 놀라움과는 거리가 멀다는 것을, 그리고 그들과 마차 바깥의 어떤 사람들 사이에 갖가지 신호 같은 교신이 오가는 듯하다는 것을 알아채지 않았더라면 말이다. 그제야 그는 이 소동이 어느 정도 멀게나마 로버트 소여 씨의 익살스러운 처신에서 비롯된 것일지도 모른다는 데에 생각이 미쳤다.\n\n“부디 우리의 들뜬 친구가 저 뒤 마부석에서 무슨 어처구니없는 짓을 벌이고 있지 않기를 바라네.” 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n“오, 천만에요, 절대로요.” 벤 앨런이 대답했다. “취했을 때만 빼면, 보브는 세상에서 가장 조용한 녀석입니다.”\n\n이때 길게 끄는 키뷰글 흉내 소리가 귀를 때렸고, 그 뒤를 환호와 비명이 이었으니, 그 모든 것은 분명 세상에서 가장 조용한 녀석, 더 쉽게 말하자면 보브 소여 씨 본인의 목구멍과 폐에서 터져 나온 것이었다.\n\n픽윅 씨와 벤 앨런 씨는 의미심장하게 서로를 마주 보았고, 픽윅 씨는 모자를 벗고 조끼가 거의 다 밖으로 나올 만큼 마차 창밖으로 몸을 내밀어, 마침내 그 익살스러운 친구를 한눈에 볼 수 있었다.\n\n보브 소여 씨는 마부석이 아니라 마차 지붕 위에 앉아 있었으니, 두 다리를 편하게 벌릴 수 있는 데까지 한껏 벌리고, 새뮤얼 웰러 씨의 모자를 머리 한쪽으로 비스듬히 쓰고, 한 손에는 어마어마하게 큰 샌드위치를, 다른 손에는 제법 큼직한 휴대용 술병을 받쳐 든 채, 둘 다에 강렬한 흥취로 매달려 있었다. 그 단조로움은 이따금 내지르는 고함이나, 지나가는 낯선 이와 주고받는 활기찬 농담으로 변주되었다. 진홍빛 깃발은 마부석 난간에 똑바로 선 채 정성스레 매여 있었고, 보브 소여의 모자로 치장한 새뮤얼 웰러 씨는 그 한가운데 앉아 쌍둥이 같은 샌드위치를 먹어 치우며, 이 모든 조처를 온전히, 완벽하게 승인한다는 뜻을 드러내는 생기 넘치는 표정을 짓고 있었다.\n\n이것만으로도 픽윅 씨처럼 예의범절을 중시하는 신사를 화나게 하기에 충분했지만, 그것이 화의 전부는 아니었다. 마침 그 순간 안팎으로 손님이 가득 찬 역마차 한 대가 그들과 마주쳤고, 그 승객들의 놀라움은 매우 또렷이 드러났던 것이다. 게다가 마차를 따라오며 내내 구걸하던 아일랜드인 가족의 환호도 자못 떠들썩한 것이었으니, 특히 그 집안의 남자 가장은 이 광경을 무슨 정치적인, 혹은 그 밖의 개선 행렬의 일부쯤으로 여기는 듯했다.\n\n“소여 씨!” 픽윅 씨가 몹시 흥분한 상태로 외쳤다. “소여 씨, 이보게!”\n\n“여보세요!” 그 신사는 세상 더없이 태연하게 마차 옆으로 몸을 내밀며 대꾸했다.\n\n“자네 미쳤나?” 픽윅 씨가 따져 물었다.\n\n“조금도 아니올시다.” 보브가 대답했다. “그저 유쾌할 뿐이죠.”\n\n“유쾌하다고!” 픽윅 씨가 소리쳤다. “그 망측한 붉은 손수건을 내리게, 제발. 명령일세. 샘, 그걸 내리게.”\n\n샘이 끼어들 새도 없이, 보브 소여 씨는 우아하게 그의 깃발을 내려 주머니에 넣고는, 픽윅 씨에게 정중하게 고개를 끄덕이고, 술병 주둥이를 닦아 제 입에 갖다 댐으로써, 군말 없이, 그 한 모금을 픽윅 씨의 온갖 행복과 번영을 비는 데 바친다는 뜻을 전했다. 이렇게 하고 나서 보브는 마개를 매우 조심스럽게 도로 막고, 픽윅 씨를 인자하게 내려다보며 샌드위치를 큼직하게 한 입 베어 물고 미소 지었다.\n\n“자,” 순간의 노여움이 보브의 흔들림 없는 침착함을 당해 내지 못한 픽윅 씨가 말했다. “부디 이런 어처구니없는 짓은 더 이상 하지 말게.”\n\n“네, 네.” 보브가 다시 한 번 웰러 씨와 모자를 바꿔 쓰며 대답했다. “그러려던 건 아니었습니다. 다만 마차 타는 게 어찌나 신이 나던지 어쩔 수 없었던 거죠.”\n\n“이게 어떻게 보일지 생각해 보게.” 픽윅 씨가 타일렀다. “체면을 좀 생각하란 말일세.”\n\n“오, 그럼요.” 보브가 말했다. “전혀 그럴 만한 일이 아니죠. 다 끝났습니다, 단장님.”\n\n이 다짐에 만족한 픽윅 씨는 다시 한 번 마차 안으로 머리를 들이고 유리창을 올렸다. 그러나 보브 소여 씨가 중단시켰던 이야기를 채 다시 시작하기도 전에, 창밖에 나타난 길쭉한 모양의 작고 검은 물체에 다소 흠칫 놀라고 말았으니, 그것은 마치 들여보내 달라고 안달이라도 난 듯 창문을 톡톡 두드려 댔다.\n\n“이게 뭔가?” 픽윅 씨가 소리쳤다.\n\n“휴대용 술병 같은데요.” 벤 앨런이 안경 너머로 자못 흥미롭게 그 물체를 살피며 말했다. “아무래도 보브의 것 같습니다.”\n\n그 짐작은 완벽하게 정확했다. 보브 소여 씨가 휴대용 술병을 지팡이 끝에 매달아, 안에 탄 친구들이 우애와 화목 속에서 그 내용물을 함께 들기를 바라는 표시로 창문을 두드려 대고 있었던 것이다.\n\n“어찌하면 좋을까?” 픽윅 씨가 술병을 바라보며 말했다. “이 짓은 아까 것보다 더 어처구니없군.”\n\n“받아들이는 게 제일 좋을 것 같습니다.” 벤 앨런 씨가 대답했다. “받아서 그냥 가지고 있으면 그자에게 딱 좋은 응징이 되지 않겠습니까?”\n\n“그렇겠군.” 픽윅 씨가 말했다. “그럴까?”\n\n“우리가 취할 수 있는 가장 적절한 방책이라고 생각합니다.” 벤이 대답했다.\n\n이 조언이 자신의 생각과 완전히 일치하는지라, 픽윅 씨는 가만히 창문을 내리고 지팡이에서 술병을 떼어 냈다. 그러자 지팡이는 위로 끌려 올라갔고, 보브 소여 씨가 유쾌하게 웃는 소리가 들려왔다.\n\n“참으로 명랑한 친구로군!” 픽윅 씨가 손에 술병을 든 채 동행을 돌아보며 말했다.\n\n“그렇습죠.” 앨런 씨가 말했다.\n\n“도저히 저 친구에게 화를 낼 수가 없군.” 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n“화를 낸다는 건 어림도 없는 일이지요.” 벤저민 앨런이 말했다.\n\n이렇게 짧게 소회를 주고받는 동안, 픽윅 씨는 무심결에 술병 마개를 따고 있었다.\n\n“그게 뭡니까?” 벤 앨런이 무심히 물었다.\n\n“모르겠네.” 픽윅 씨도 똑같이 무심하게 대답했다. “냄새로 보아선, 밀크펀치 같군.”\n\n“오, 그렇습니까?” 벤이 말했다.\n\n“그런 것 같네.” 픽윅 씨가 거짓을 말할 가능성을 매우 적절하게 경계하며 덧붙였다. “명심하게, 맛을 보지 않고서야 확실히 말할 수는 없네.”\n\n“맛을 보시는 편이 낫겠습니다.” 벤이 말했다. “그게 뭔지 알아 두는 것도 나쁘지 않죠.”\n\n“그렇게 생각하나?” 픽윅 씨가 대답했다. “좋아, 자네가 그리 알고 싶다면 나야 반대할 까닭이 없지.”\n\n언제나 자신의 감정을 친구의 바람에 기꺼이 희생하는 픽윅 씨는, 곧장 제법 길게 한 모금을 맛보았다.\n\n“그게 뭡니까?” 벤 앨런이 다소 안달하며 그를 가로막고 물었다.\n\n“묘하군.” 픽윅 씨가 입맛을 다시며 말했다. “지금도 잘 모르겠네. 오, 그렇지!” 두 번째로 맛본 뒤에 픽윅 씨가 말했다. “이건 펀치일세, 정말로.”\n\n벤 앨런 씨가 픽윅 씨를 바라보았고, 픽윅 씨가 벤 앨런 씨를 바라보았다. 벤 앨런 씨는 미소 지었지만, 픽윅 씨는 그러지 않았다.\n\n“그자에게 딱 좋은 응징이 될 걸세.” 끝에 말한 그 신사가 다소 엄하게 말했다. “한 방울도 남김없이 마셔 버리는 게 그자에게 딱 좋은 응징이겠지.”\n\n“저도 바로 그 생각을 했습니다.” 벤 앨런이 말했다.\n\n“정말 그런가?” 픽윅 씨가 대꾸했다. “그렇다면 그자의 건강을 위하여!” 이 말과 함께 그 훌륭한 인물은 병을 매우 힘차게 들이켜고는 벤 앨런에게 건넸으니, 벤 또한 그 본보기를 따르는 데 굼뜨지 않았다. 미소는 서로에게 번졌고, 밀크펀치는 차츰차츰 유쾌하게 비워져 갔다.\n\n“어쨌든 말이야,” 마지막 한 방울을 비우며 픽윅 씨가 말했다. “저 친구의 장난은 정말이지 무척 재미있어. 정말로 흥겹단 말일세.”\n\n“그렇게 말씀하셔도 됩니다.” 벤 앨런 씨가 대꾸했다. 보브 소여가 살아 있는 사람 중 가장 우스운 녀석이라는 증거로, 그는 픽윅 씨에게 그 신사가 한번은 술을 마시다가 열병이 나서 머리를 박박 밀었던 일을 길고 자세하게 들려주기 시작했다. 이 유쾌하고 흐뭇한 이야기는 마차가 버클리 히스의 벨 여관에 말을 갈아매려고 멈춰 서면서야 비로소 중단되었다.\n\n“이보세요! 여기서 저녁을 들 거죠, 그렇죠?” 보브가 창 안을 들여다보며 말했다.\n\n“저녁이라니!” 픽윅 씨가 말했다. “겨우 십구 마일밖에 안 왔고, 아직 팔십칠 마일하고 반이나 더 가야 하는데.”\n\n“그러니까 더더욱 이 피로를 견뎌 낼 무언가를 들어 둬야지요.” 보브 소여 씨가 항변했다.\n\n“오, 낮 열한 시 반에 저녁을 든다는 건 도무지 말도 안 되네.” 픽윅 씨가 자기 시계를 보며 대답했다.\n\n“그렇긴 하군요.” 보브가 대꾸했다. “점심이 딱이겠네요. 여보시오, 거기! 셋이 먹을 점심을 당장. 그리고 말은 십오 분만 묶어 두시오. 차게 둔 건 죄다 식탁에 올리고, 병맥주도 좀 내오고, 댁의 최고급 마데이라도 맛보게 해 주시오.” 어마어마한 거드름과 소동을 떨며 이런 주문을 내린 보브 소여 씨는, 곧장 안으로 서둘러 들어가 준비를 감독했고, 오 분도 채 안 되어 돌아와서는 모든 게 훌륭하다고 단언했다.\n\n점심의 질은 보브가 늘어놓은 찬사를 충분히 입증했고, 그 신사뿐 아니라 벤 앨런 씨와 픽윅 씨도 점심에 한껏 정당한 대접을 베풀었다. 셋의 후원 아래 병맥주와 마데이라는 즉각 비워졌고, (말이 다시 매여) 그들이, 그토록 촉박한 통고로 구할 수 있는 최고의 밀크펀치 대용품으로 가득 채운 휴대용 술병을 들고 다시 자리에 앉자, 키뷰글이 울리고 붉은 깃발이 휘날렸으되, 픽윅 씨 쪽에서는 조금의 반대도 없었다.\n\n튜크스베리의 홉 폴 여관에서 그들은 저녁을 들려고 멈췄다. 이때에는 병맥주가 더 나왔고, 마데이라도 더, 그리고 포트와인까지 곁들였다. 그리고 여기서 휴대용 술병은 네 번째로 다시 채워졌다. 이 합쳐진 자극제들의 영향 아래, 픽윅 씨와 벤 앨런 씨는 삼십 마일 동안 곤히 잠들었고, 그동안 보브와 웰러 씨는 마부석에서 이중창을 불렀다.\n\n픽윅 씨가 정신을 충분히 차려 창밖을 내다보았을 때는 사방이 완전히 어두워져 있었다. 길가에 띄엄띄엄 흩어진 오두막들, 눈에 보이는 모든 사물의 거무튀튀한 빛깔, 음울한 대기, 석탄재와 벽돌 가루가 깔린 길, 저 멀리 용광로 불의 짙붉은 빛, 우뚝 솟은 높다란 굴뚝에서 묵직하게 뿜어 나와 주위 온 사물을 검게 물들이고 흐릿하게 가리는 자욱한 연기 무더기, 멀리서 번득이는 불빛들, 부딪치며 덜그럭대는 쇠막대를 싣거나 무거운 화물을 산더미처럼 쌓고 길을 따라 힘겹게 나아가는 육중한 짐마차들—이 모든 것이 그들이 거대한 공업 도시 버밍엄에 빠르게 다가가고 있음을 알려 주었다.\n\n소동의 심장부로 이어지는 좁은 길들을 덜컹거리며 지나갈 때, 진지한 노동의 광경과 소리가 더욱 강렬하게 감각을 두드렸다. 거리는 일하는 사람들로 북적였다. 노동의 윙윙거림이 집집마다 울려 퍼졌고, 다락층의 길쭉한 여닫이창에서 불빛이 새어 나왔으며, 바퀴 도는 소리와 기계 돌아가는 소음이 떨리는 벽을 뒤흔들었다. 그 붉고 음침한 빛이 몇 마일 밖에서도 보이던 그 불들은, 이 도시의 거대한 공장과 작업장에서 사납게 타올랐다. 망치 두드리는 소리, 증기 뿜는 소리, 그리고 기관의 둔하고 묵직한 쩔그렁 소리가 사방에서 일어나는 거친 음악이었다.\n\n마부 소년이 탁 트인 거리를 지나, 도시 변두리와 올드 로열 호텔 사이에 늘어선 멋지고 환하게 불 밝힌 가게들을 지나 활기차게 마차를 몰고 가는 동안에도, 픽윅 씨는 자신을 그곳까지 이끌어 온 임무가 얼마나 어렵고 미묘한 성질의 것인지를 미처 헤아리기 시작하지 못하고 있었다.\n\n이 임무의 미묘한 성질과, 그것을 만족스럽게 수행하기 어려운 점은, 보브 소여 씨의 자발적인 동행으로 인해 결코 줄어들지 않았다. 사실대로 말하자면, 픽윅 씨는 이런 자리에서 그가 함께 있는 것이, 아무리 사려 깊고 흐뭇한 일이라 해도, 결코 자신이 기꺼이 구했을 만한 영예가 아니라고 느꼈다. 실은, 보브 소여 씨를 지체 없이 적어도 오십 마일은 떨어진 어딘가로 치워 버릴 수만 있다면, 그는 기꺼이 상당한 액수의 돈이라도 내놓았을 것이다.\n\n픽윅 씨는 노 윈클 씨와 한 번도 직접 만나 본 적이 없었으나, 한두 번 편지로 서신을 주고받으며 그 아들의 도덕적 품성과 행실에 관한 문의에 만족스러운 답을 보낸 적은 있었다. 그가 처음 그를 찾아뵙는 자리에, 약간씩 거나하게 취한 보브 소여와 벤 앨런을 거느리고 가는 것이, 자신에게 호의를 품게 만들기에 결코 가장 기발하거나 가망 있는 방편이 아니라는 사실이 그는 신경 쓰여 견딜 수 없었다.\n\n“그렇지만,” 픽윅 씨가 스스로를 다잡으려 애쓰며 말했다. “나는 할 수 있는 한 최선을 다해야 한다. 오늘 밤 그를 만나야 해, 그렇게 하겠다고 굳게 약속했으니까. 저들이 굳이 따라오겠다면, 면담을 되도록 짧게 끝내고, 저들도 제 체면을 위해서 추태를 부리지는 않으리라는 데 만족할 수밖에.”\n\n이런 생각으로 마음을 달래고 있을 때, 마차가 올드 로열 호텔 문 앞에 멈췄다. 엄청난 잠에서 어느 정도 깨어난 벤 앨런이 새뮤얼 웰러 씨에게 멱살을 잡혀 끌려 나오자, 픽윅 씨도 비로소 내릴 수 있었다. 그들은 아늑한 방으로 안내되었고, 픽윅 씨는 즉시 급사에게 윈클 씨의 거처가 어디인지를 물었다.\n\n“바로 근처입니다, 선생님.” 급사가 말했다. “오백 야드도 채 안 됩니다, 선생님. 윈클 씨는 운하의 부두 관리인이십니다, 선생님. 사저는 아니—오, 천만에요, 선생님, 오백 야드도 안 됩니다, 선생님.” 여기서 급사는 촛불 하나를 끄고는, 픽윅 씨가 그럴 마음이 있다면 더 물어볼 기회를 주려고, 다시 불을 붙이는 시늉을 했다.\n\n“지금 무얼 좀 드시겠습니까, 선생님?” 픽윅 씨의 침묵에 절박해진 급사가 촛불을 켜며 말했다. “차나 커피요, 선생님? 저녁 식사요, 선생님?”\n“지금은 아무것도 안 하겠네.”\n\n“알겠습니다, 선생님. 야식을 주문하시겠습니까, 선생님?”\n\n“지금은 아닐세.”\n\n“알겠습니다, 선생님.” 여기서 그는 천천히 문 쪽으로 걸어가다가, 문득 멈춰 서서 돌아보며 매우 상냥하게 말했다—\n\n“객실 하녀를 보내 드릴까요, 신사분들?”\n\n“원한다면 그러게나.” 픽윅 씨가 대답했다.\n\n“선생님께서 원하신다면요.”\n\n“그리고 소다수를 좀 가져오게.” 보브 소여가 말했다.\n\n“소다수요, 선생님! 네, 선생님.” 마침내 무엇이든 주문을 하나 받았다는 사실에 짓누르던 무거운 짐을 덜어 낸 듯한 마음으로, 급사는 어느새 소리 없이 녹아 사라졌다. 급사들은 결코 걷거나 뛰는 법이 없다. 그들에게는 다른 인간에게는 없는, 방에서 스르르 미끄러져 나가는 독특하고 신비로운 능력이 있다.\n\n소다수 덕분에 벤 앨런 씨에게 약간의 생기가 되살아나자, 그는 얼굴과 손을 씻고 샘에게 옷을 솔질받는 일에 마지못해 응했다. 픽윅 씨와 보브 소여도 여행으로 흐트러진 옷차림을 추스른 뒤, 셋은 팔짱을 끼고 윈클 씨 댁으로 출발했다. 보브 소여는 걸어가는 내내 담배 연기로 대기를 물들였다.\n\n사 분의 일 마일쯤 떨어진, 조용하고 견실해 보이는 거리에, 문 앞에 세 단의 계단이 있고 그 위에 ‘윈클 씨’라는 글자가 두툼한 로마자 대문자로 새겨진 놋쇠 명패가 붙은 낡은 붉은 벽돌집이 서 있었다. 계단은 무척 희고, 벽돌은 무척 붉고, 집은 무척 깨끗했다. 시계가 열 시를 칠 때, 픽윅 씨와 벤저민 앨런 씨와 보브 소여 씨가 바로 여기에 와 섰다.\n\n말쑥한 하녀가 노크에 응답하더니, 낯선 사람 셋을 보고 흠칫 놀랐다.\n\n“윈클 씨 계신가, 아가씨?” 픽윅 씨가 물었다.\n\n“막 저녁 식사를 하려던 참이세요, 선생님.” 하녀가 대답했다.\n\n“이 명함을 그분께 전해 주겠나.” 픽윅 씨가 대꾸했다. “이렇게 늦은 시각에 폐를 끼쳐 죄송하다고 말씀드려 주게. 다만 오늘 밤 꼭 뵙고 싶고, 방금 막 도착했다고 말일세.”\n\n하녀는 갖가지 놀라운 우스꽝스러운 표정으로 자신의 미모에 대한 감탄을 표현하고 있는 보브 소여 씨를 겁먹은 듯 쳐다보았다. 그러고는 복도에 걸린 모자와 외투들에 눈길을 던지더니, 자기가 위층에 올라가는 동안 문을 지키라고 다른 하녀를 불렀다. 그 보초는 곧 임무에서 풀려났으니, 하녀가 즉시 돌아와 신사분들을 거리에 세워 둔 일을 사과하고는, 그들을 바닥에 천을 깐 안쪽 응접실로 안내했던 것이다. 그곳은 절반은 사무실, 절반은 화장실 격으로, 쓸모 있고 보기 좋은 가구라야 책상 하나, 세면대와 면도용 거울, 부츠걸이와 부츠 빼는 기구, 높은 걸상 하나, 의자 넷, 탁자 하나, 그리고 여드레 태엽을 감는 낡은 괘종시계 하나가 주된 것이었다. 벽난로 선반 위에는 철제 금고의 움푹 들어간 문짝들이 있었고, 책을 얹는 매달린 선반 한 쌍, 달력 하나, 그리고 먼지 쌓인 서류 묶음 몇 개가 벽을 장식하고 있었다.\n\n“문 앞에 세워 두어서 정말 죄송합니다, 선생님.” 하녀가 등불을 켜며 매혹적인 미소로 픽윅 씨에게 말했다. “하지만 선생님들이 제겐 전혀 낯선 분들이었던 데다, 그저 손댈 만한 게 뭐 없나 보러 오는 떠돌이가 워낙 많아서, 정말이지—”\n\n“조금도 사과할 필요 없다네, 아가씨.” 픽윅 씨가 너그러이 말했다.\n\n“추호도 그럴 필요 없소, 내 사랑.” 보브 소여가 마치 아가씨가 방을 나가지 못하게 막으려는 듯, 장난스레 두 팔을 쭉 뻗고 이쪽저쪽으로 깡충거리며 말했다.\n\n이 유혹에 아가씨는 조금도 누그러지지 않았다. 그도 그럴 것이 그녀는 곧장 보브 소여 씨가 ‘망측한 인간’이라는 자기 의견을 밝혔고, 그가 좀 더 들이대며 수작을 걸자, 자기 고운 손가락을 그의 얼굴에 찍어 누르고는, 혐오와 경멸의 표현을 잔뜩 쏟아 내며 방에서 튕겨 나가 버렸다.\n\n아가씨와의 교제를 빼앗긴 보브 소여 씨는, 책상 안을 들여다보고, 탁자 서랍을 죄다 뒤지고, 철제 금고의 자물쇠를 따는 시늉을 하고, 달력을 벽 쪽으로 돌려놓고, 노 윈클 씨의 부츠를 자기 부츠 위에 신어 보고, 그 밖에도 가구를 가지고 여러 익살스러운 실험을 하며 시간을 보냈으니, 이 모든 것이 픽윅 씨에게는 이루 말할 수 없는 공포와 고통을 안겨 주었고, 보브 소여 씨에게는 그에 걸맞은 기쁨을 안겨 주었다.\n\n마침내 문이 열리고, 코담배 빛깔의 옷을 입은 자그마한 노신사가 들어왔으니, 그 머리와 얼굴은 다소 대머리인 점만 빼면 젊은 윈클 씨의 것과 정확히 똑같았다. 그는 한 손에 픽윅 씨의 명함을, 다른 손에 은촛대를 들고 종종걸음으로 방에 들어왔다.\n\n“픽윅 씨, 안녕하십니까?” 노 윈클이 촛대를 내려놓고 손을 내밀며 말했다. “건강하신 모습이길 바랍니다, 선생. 뵙게 되어 반갑습니다. 앉으시지요, 픽윅 씨, 부디 앉으십시오. 이 신사분은—”\n\n“제 친구 소여 씨입니다.” 픽윅 씨가 끼어들었다. “댁의 아드님 친구이지요.”\n\n“오,” 노 윈클 씨가 다소 험상궂게 보브를 바라보며 말했다. “건강하시길 바랍니다, 선생.”\n\n“삼발이처럼 멀쩡합니다, 선생.” 보브 소여가 대답했다.\n\n“이쪽 다른 신사분은,” 픽윅 씨가 말했다. “제가 맡아 온 편지를 읽어 보시면 아시겠지만, 댁의 아드님과 매우 가까운 친척, 아니 차라리 매우 각별한 친구라 해야겠지요. 성함은 앨런입니다.”\n\n“저 신사분 말이오?” 윈클 씨가 명함으로 벤 앨런을 가리키며 물었다. 벤 앨런은 등뼈와 외투 깃 말고는 아무것도 보이지 않는 자세로 잠들어 있었다.\n\n픽윅 씨가 막 그 물음에 답하며 벤저민 앨런 씨의 이름과 명예로운 칭호들을 빠짐없이 늘어놓으려던 참에, 활기찬 보브 소여 씨가 친구에게 제 처지를 일깨워 줄 요량으로 그의 팔 살집을 깜짝 놀랄 만큼 세게 꼬집었고, 그 바람에 벤은 비명을 지르며 펄쩍 뛰어올랐다. 자신이 낯선 사람 앞에 있다는 것을 문득 깨달은 벤 앨런 씨는 앞으로 나서서, 윈클 씨의 두 손을 약 오 분 동안 더없이 다정하게 흔들며, 반쯤만 알아들을 수 있는 토막 문장들로, 그를 뵙게 되어 크게 기쁘다는 말과, 산책 뒤에 무얼 좀 들 마음이 있으신지, 아니면 ‘저녁 식사 때까지’ 기다리시는 편이 좋으신지를 묻는 환대의 말을 중얼거렸다. 그러고는 자리에 앉아 자기가 대체 어디 있는지 전혀 모르겠다는 듯 굳어 버린 눈으로 주위를 두리번거렸으니, 실제로 그는 정말 몰랐던 것이다.\n\n이 모든 것이 픽윅 씨에게는 더없이 거북한 일이었으니, 더더욱 노 윈클 씨가 두 동행의 별난—아니, 기이하다 할 만한—행동에 또렷이 놀라움을 드러냈기 때문이었다. 이 문제를 단번에 매듭짓고자, 그는 주머니에서 편지를 꺼내 노 윈클 씨에게 건네며 말했다—\n\n“이 편지는, 선생, 댁의 아드님이 보낸 것입니다. 그 내용을 보시면 아시겠지만, 그에 대한 선생의 호의적이고 자애로운 헤아림에 그 아이의 앞날의 행복과 안녕이 달려 있습니다. 부디 그것을 더없이 차분하고 냉정하게 읽어 주시고, 그 뒤에 마땅히 그래야 할 어조와 정신으로 저와 그 문제를 의논해 주시겠습니까? 선생의 결정이 아드님에게 얼마나 중요한지, 그리고 그 아이가 이 문제로 얼마나 애를 태우고 있는지는, 제가 사전에 아무 기별도 없이 이렇게 늦은 시각에 선생을 찾아뵙는 것만 보아도 짐작하실 수 있을 것입니다. 그리고,” 픽윅 씨가 두 동행을 슬쩍 곁눈질하며 덧붙였다. “이렇게 불리한 상황에서 말입니다.”\n\n이런 서두와 함께, 픽윅 씨는 극상의 고급 직조지에 빽빽이 써 내려간 참회의 글 넉 장을 깜짝 놀란 노 윈클 씨의 손에 쥐여 주었다. 그러고는 다시 의자에 앉아 그의 표정과 태도를 살폈으니, 과연 마음 졸이기는 했으나, 변명하거나 얼버무릴 어떤 짓도 하지 않았다고 느끼는 신사의 떳떳한 낯빛으로였다.\n\n늙은 부두 관리인은 편지를 이리저리 뒤집어, 앞면과 뒷면과 옆면을 살펴보고, 봉인에 찍힌 통통한 어린 소년을 현미경이라도 들이대듯 들여다본 다음, 눈을 들어 픽윅 씨의 얼굴을 바라보았다. 그러고는 높은 걸상에 앉아 등불을 자기 쪽으로 더 가까이 끌어당기고, 봉랍을 뜯어 편지를 펼치고는, 그것을 불빛에 비추어 읽을 채비를 했다.\n\n바로 이 순간, 몇 분간 잠잠하던 보브 소여 씨의 익살기가 발동하여, 그는 두 손을 무릎에 얹고는 고인이 된 그리말디 씨가 어릿광대로 분한 초상화를 본떠 우스꽝스러운 표정을 지었다. 그런데 공교롭게도 노 윈클 씨는 보브 소여 씨가 생각한 것처럼 편지 읽기에 깊이 빠져 있던 것이 아니라, 마침 편지 위쪽 너머로 다름 아닌 바로 그 보브 소여 씨 본인을 바라보고 있던 참이었다. 앞서 말한 그 표정이 자기 자신을 조롱하고 비웃으려 지어진 것임을 정확히 짐작한 그는, 어찌나 의미심장하게 엄한 눈길을 보브에게 박았던지, 고인이 된 그리말디 씨의 이목구비는 차츰차츰 매우 그럴듯한 겸손과 당황의 표정으로 풀려 갔다.\n\n“무어라 하셨소, 선생?” 끔찍한 침묵 끝에 노 윈클 씨가 물었다.\n\n“아닙니다, 선생.” 보브가 대답했다. 그에게서 어릿광대의 흔적이라곤, 두 뺨이 유난히 붉어진 것 말고는 남아 있지 않았다.\n\n“정말 아무 말 안 하신 게 분명하오, 선생?” 노 윈클 씨가 말했다.\n\n“오, 그럼요, 선생, 전혀 안 했습니다.” 보브가 대답했다.\n\n“하신 줄 알았소, 선생.” 노신사가 분개한 어조로 힘주어 대꾸했다. “혹 나를 빤히 쳐다보신 거요, 선생?”\n\n“오, 아닙니다! 선생, 천만에요.” 보브가 더없이 정중하게 대답했다.\n\n“그 말을 들으니 매우 기쁘오, 선생.” 노 윈클 씨가 말했다. 무안해진 보브에게 자못 위풍당당하게 눈살을 찌푸린 노신사는, 다시 편지를 불빛에 가져가 진지하게 읽기 시작했다.\n\n픽윅 씨는 그가 첫 장 맨 아랫줄에서 둘째 장 맨 윗줄로, 둘째 장 맨 아래에서 셋째 장 맨 위로, 셋째 장 맨 아래에서 넷째 장 맨 위로 옮겨 가는 동안 그를 뚫어지게 지켜보았다. 그러나 아들의 결혼 소식을 받아 든 그 심정을 알아챌 단서가 될 만한 표정 변화는 조금도 없었으니, 픽윅 씨는 그 소식이 맨 첫머리 대여섯 줄 안에 들어 있음을 알고 있었다.\n\n그는 편지를 마지막 한 마디까지 다 읽고는, 사업가다운 온갖 꼼꼼함과 정확함으로 다시 접었다. 그러고는 픽윅 씨가 무슨 큰 감정의 폭발을 예상하던 바로 그때, 잉크병에 펜을 적시며, 더없이 평범한 회계실 화제라도 말하듯 차분하게 말했다—\n\n“너새니얼의 주소가 어찌 되오, 픽윅 씨?”\n\n“현재로선 조지 앤드 벌처입니다.” 픽윅 씨가 대답했다.\n\n“조지 앤드 벌처. 그게 어디요?”\n\n“롬바드 가, 조지 야드입니다.”\n“시내요?”\n\n“그렇습니다.”\n\n노신사는 그 주소를 편지 뒷면에 차근차근 적어 넣고는, 그것을 책상에 넣고 잠근 다음, 걸상에서 내려와 열쇠 꾸러미를 주머니에 넣으며 말했다—\n\n“이제 우리를 더 붙들어 둘 다른 일은 없으리라 생각하오, 픽윅 씨?”\n\n“다른 일이 없다니요, 선생!” 그 다정한 인물이 분개한 경악 속에 말했다. “다른 일이 없다니요! 우리 젊은 친구의 인생에서 이토록 중대한 사건을 두고 표명하실 의견이 하나도 없단 말입니까? 저를 통해 그 아이에게 전할, 변함없는 애정과 보호의 다짐도 하나 없단 말입니까? 그 아이와, 또 위로와 지지를 구하며 그를 바라보고 있는 그 애타는 처녀를 격려하고 떠받쳐 줄 한마디 말도 없단 말입니까? 여보시오, 선생, 생각해 보십시오.”\n\n“생각해 보겠소.” 노신사가 대답했다. “지금 당장은 할 말이 없소. 나는 사업가요, 픽윅 씨. 나는 어떤 일에서도 결코 성급하게 자신을 내맡기지 않으며, 내가 본 바로는 이 일의 모양새가 도무지 마음에 들지 않소. 천 파운드는 큰돈이 아니오, 픽윅 씨.”\n\n“지당하신 말씀입니다, 선생.” 자기 천 파운드를 아무 어려움 없이 다 써 버렸다는 것쯤은 알아챌 만큼 막 깨어난 벤 앨런이 끼어들었다. “선생은 지혜로운 분이십니다. 보브, 이분은 아주 사리에 밝은 양반이야.”\n\n“그 점을 인정해 주는 공정함을 베푸시니 매우 기쁘구려, 선생.” 노 윈클 씨가, 심오하게 고개를 끄덕이고 있는 벤 앨런을 경멸하듯 바라보며 말했다. “사실인즉, 픽윅 씨, 내가 아들에게 한 해 남짓 떠돌 면허를 주어 세상 사람과 풍속을 좀 보게 한 것은 (그 아이가 선생의 후원 아래 그리해 온 것이지요), 그 아이가 누구에게나 속아 넘어가는 한낱 기숙학교 물러 터진 풋내기로 인생에 들어서지 않게 하려던 것이지, 이런 일을 흥정한 것은 결코 아니었소. 그 아이도 그것을 잘 알고 있으니, 이 일로 내가 그에게서 후원을 거두어들인다 한들 놀랄 까닭이 없소. 그 아이는 내 소식을 듣게 될 것이오, 픽윅 씨. 안녕히 가시오, 선생.—마거릿, 문을 열어라.”\n\n이 내내 보브 소여는 벤 앨런 씨를 쿡쿡 찔러 옳은 편에서 무어라도 말하게 부추기고 있었다. 그리하여 벤은 이제 아무런 사전 예고도 없이, 짧지만 격정적인 웅변 한 토막을 터뜨렸다.\n\n“선생,” 벤 앨런 씨가 자못 흐리멍덩하고 나른한 두 눈으로 노신사를 노려보며, 오른팔을 사납게 위아래로 휘저으며 말했다. “당신—당신은 스스로 부끄러운 줄 알아야 하오.”\n\n“그 숙녀의 오라비이시니, 물론 이 문제에 대해서는 탁월한 심판관이시겠지요.” 노 윈클 씨가 받아쳤다. “자, 그만하면 됐소. 부디 더는 말씀 마시오, 픽윅 씨. 안녕히들 가시오, 신사분들!”\n\n이 말과 함께 노신사는 촛대를 집어 들고 방문을 열어, 정중하게 복도 쪽을 가리켰다.\n\n“후회하실 겁니다, 선생.” 픽윅 씨가 치미는 노여움을 억누르려 이를 악물며 말했으니, 그 결과가 젊은 친구에게 얼마나 중대한 것일지 절감했기 때문이었다.\n\n“나는 현재로선 생각이 다르오.” 노 윈클 씨가 차분하게 대답했다. “다시 한 번, 신사분들, 안녕히 가시기를 비오.”\n\n픽윅 씨는 성난 큰 걸음으로 거리로 걸어 나갔다. 노신사의 단호한 태도에 완전히 기가 꺾인 보브 소여 씨도 같은 길을 따랐다. 곧이어 벤 앨런 씨의 모자가 계단 아래로 굴러떨어졌고, 그 뒤를 곧장 벤 앨런 씨의 몸뚱이가 따랐다. 일행은 모두 말없이, 저녁도 거른 채 잠자리에 들었다. 그리고 픽윅 씨는 잠들기 직전, 노 윈클 씨가 그토록 철저한 사업가인 줄 미리 알았더라면, 그런 용건으로 그를 찾아뵙는 일은 결코 없었으리라는 것이 십중팔구라고 생각했다.\n\n픽윅 씨의 눈앞에 여덟 시에 밝아 온 아침은 그의 기분을 북돋우거나, 사절의 임무가 뜻밖의 결과로 끝난 데서 비롯된 침울함을 덜어 줄 만한 것이 전혀 못 되었다. 하늘은 어둡고 음울했고, 공기는 축축하고 쌀쌀했으며, 거리는 젖어 질척거렸다. 연기는 솟아오를 용기조차 없는 듯 굴뚝 꼭대기 위에 굼뜨게 매달려 있었고, 비는 쏟아질 기운마저 없는 듯 느릿느릿하고 끈질기게 내렸다. 마구간 마당의 싸움닭은 평소의 활기를 한 톨도 남김없이 빼앗긴 채 구석에서 처량하게 한 다리로 몸을 가누고 있었고, 헛간의 좁은 지붕 아래에서 고개를 떨군 채 멍하니 서 있는 당나귀는 그 사색에 잠긴 비참한 표정으로 보아 자살을 궁리하고 있는 것만 같았다. 거리에서 보이는 것이라고는 우산뿐이었고, 들리는 소리라고는 나막신 굽이 또각거리는 소리와 빗방울이 튀는 소리뿐이었다.\n\n아침 식사는 대화가 거의 없는 가운데 진행되었다. 보브 소여 씨조차 날씨와 전날의 흥분이 미친 영향을 느꼈다. 그 자신의 표현력 넘치는 말로 하자면, 그는 \"뻗어 버린\" 상태였다. 벤 앨런 씨도 마찬가지였다. 픽윅 씨도 마찬가지였다.\n\n날씨가 갤까 하는 기대를 길게 끌면서, 런던에서 온 지난 저녁 신문을 극도의 궁핍한 처지에서나 알 법한 강렬한 관심을 가지고 읽고 또 읽었다. 카펫의 한 치 한 치를 그와 비슷한 끈기로 밟고 다녔으며, 창문에는 추가 세금을 매길 만한 정당한 구실이 될 만큼 자주 내다보았다. 온갖 종류의 대화 화제가 시작되었다가 실패했다. 마침내 정오가 되어도 나아질 기미가 없자, 픽윅 씨는 단호히 종을 울려 마차를 대령시키라고 명했다.\n\n길은 진창이었고, 보슬보슬 내리던 비는 지금까지보다 더 거세게 쏟아졌으며, 진흙과 빗물이 열린 마차 창문으로 어찌나 많이 튀어 들어오는지 안에 탄 두 사람의 불편함이 밖에 탄 두 사람의 그것에 거의 맞먹을 지경이었다. 그럼에도 그 움직임, 그리고 자리를 떨치고 일어나 무언가를 하고 있다는 느낌에는 무언가가 있어서, 칙칙한 방에 갇혀 칙칙한 비가 칙칙한 거리로 떨어지는 것을 바라보는 것보다 한없이 나았다. 그래서 일행은 출발하면서 이 변화가 대단한 개선이라는 데 모두 동의했고, 어째서 여태껏 이렇게 오래 미룰 수 있었던 것인지 의아해했다.\n\n코번트리에서 말을 바꾸려고 멈췄을 때, 말들에서 김이 어찌나 구름처럼 피어오르는지 마구간지기의 모습이 온통 가려져 보이지 않았다. 다만 안개 속에서 그의 목소리가 들려왔는데, 자기는 다음번 인도주의협회 포상 때 첫 금메달을 기대한다는 것이었다. 우편마차 마부의 모자를 벗겨 주었기 때문이라고 했다. 그 보이지 않는 신사가 단언하기를, 모자챙에서 흘러내리던 물 때문에 마부는 (그 마부 말이다) 익사할 뻔했으나, 자기가 침착하게 그 모자를 재빨리 머리에서 잡아채어, 헐떡거리는 그 사내의 얼굴을 짚단 한 줌으로 닦아 준 덕분에 살았다는 것이었다.\n\n\"이거 기분 좋군.\" 보브 소여가 외투 깃을 세우고, 방금 삼킨 브랜디 한 잔의 김을 모으려고 숄을 입까지 끌어올리며 말했다.\n\n\"그렇구먼유.\" 샘이 차분하게 대답했다.\n\n\"자넨 별로 신경 쓰지 않는 모양이군.\" 보브가 말했다.\n\n\"아, 그게유, 제가 신경 쓴다고 무슨 좋은 일이 생길 것 같지가 않아서유, 나리.\" 샘이 대답했다.\n\n\"하여간 그건 반박할 수 없는 이유로군.\" 보브가 말했다.\n\n\"맞습니다, 나리.\" 웰러 씨가 받았다. \"있는 그대로가 옳은 거지유, 어느 젊은 귀족 양반이 다정하게 말했듯이 말입니다. 그 양반 어머니의 삼촌의 부인의 할아버지가 옛날에 휴대용 부싯깃통으로 임금님 담뱃대에 불을 붙여 드린 적이 있다는 이유로 연금 명단에 그 양반을 올려 줬을 때 말이지유.\"\n\n\"그거 나쁘지 않은 생각이군, 샘.\" 보브 소여 씨가 흡족하게 말했다.\n\n\"그 젊은 귀족 양반이 그 후로 평생 분기 결산일마다 했던 말이 바로 그거였지유.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"나리는 말입니다.\" 잠시 침묵한 뒤 샘이 마부를 흘끗 보고는, 목소리를 신비스러운 속삭임으로 낮추며 물었다. \"나리가 의사 양반 밑에서 견습 시절에, 우편마차 마부 진찰하러 불려 간 적 있으셨어유?\"\n\n\"그런 적은 없었던 것 같은데.\" 보브 소여가 대답했다.\n\n\"그 병원에서 (유령에 대해 말할 때처럼) 어슬렁대는 우편마차 마부를 본 적도 없으시지유?\" 샘이 물었다.\n\n\"없어.\" 보브 소여가 대답했다. \"그런 적 없는 것 같아.\"\n\n\"우편마차 마부의 묘비가 있는 묘지를 아신 적도 없고, 죽은 마부를 보신 적도 없으시지유?\" 샘이 문답을 이어 가며 물었다.\n\n\"없어.\" 보브가 받았다. \"그런 적 없어.\"\n\n\"없지유!\" 샘이 의기양양하게 받았다. \"앞으로도 없으실 겁니다. 그리고 아무도 본 적 없는 게 또 하나 있는데, 그게 바로 죽은 당나귀입니다. 죽은 당나귀를 본 사람은 아무도 없어유, 검은 비단 반바지를 입고 염소 치는 젊은 아가씨를 알던 그 신사 양반 빼고는유. 그것도 프랑스 당나귀였으니, 십중팔구 정통 혈통은 아니었을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그래, 그게 우편마차 마부랑 무슨 상관인데?\" 보브 소여가 물었다.\n\n\"이런 겁니다.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"아주 분별 있는 사람들이 그러는 것처럼 우편마차 마부랑 당나귀가 둘 다 불사신이라고까지 주장하지는 않겠지만, 제가 말하고 싶은 건 이겁니다. 그것들은 자기가 뻣뻣해지고 일에서 한물갔다 싶으면 둘이 함께 떠나 버린다, 이겁니다. 늘 그렇듯이 한 쌍에 마부 하나꼴로 말이지유. 그것들이 어떻게 되는지는 아무도 모르지만, 십중팔구 다른 세상에서 즐기려고 떠나는 거지유. 왜냐하면 이 세상에서 당나귀든 우편마차 마부든 즐기는 꼴을 본 사람은 살아 있는 한 아무도 없으니까유!\"\n\n이 박식하고 주목할 만한 이론을 길게 늘어놓으며, 그것을 뒷받침할 진기한 통계며 여러 사실을 인용하면서, 샘 웰러는 던처치에 닿을 때까지 시간을 잘도 보냈다. 그곳에서 마른 마부와 싱싱한 말들을 구했다. 다음 역참은 데번트리였고, 그다음은 토스터였다. 그리고 각 역참 끝에 다다를 때마다 비는 처음 출발할 때보다 더 거세게 내렸다.\n\n\"이봐요.\" 토스터의 새러슨스 헤드 여관 문 앞에 마차를 세웠을 때, 보브 소여가 마차 창문 안을 들여다보며 항의했다. \"이래선 안 되겠어요, 아시겠어요.\"\n\n\"이런!\" 막 선잠에서 깨어난 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"젖으신 모양이군요.\"\n\n\"아, 그러시군요, 그렇죠?\" 보브가 받았다. \"예, 좀 그렇긴 합니다. 어쩌면 불편할 만큼 축축할지도요.\"\n\n보브는 정말 축축해 보였으니, 목이며 팔꿈치며 소맷부리며 옷자락이며 무릎에서 비가 줄줄 흘러내렸고, 옷차림 전체가 젖어 어찌나 번들거리는지 무두질한 방수포 한 벌로 오해받을 만했다.\n\n\"제가 좀 젖긴 했네요.\" 보브가 몸을 한 번 부르르 떨어, 막 물에서 나온 뉴펀들랜드 개처럼 자그마한 물줄기를 사방으로 흩뿌리며 말했다.\n\n\"오늘 밤에 더 가는 건 도저히 불가능하다고 봐요.\" 벤이 끼어들었다.\n\n\"말도 안 되지유, 나리.\" 샘 웰러가 의논에 거들려고 다가오며 한마디 했다. \"짐승들한테 그걸 시키는 건 학대입니다요, 나리. 여기 침대도 있고유, 나리,\" 주인을 향해 샘이 말했다. \"다 깨끗하고 편안합니다. 아주 괜찮은 자그마한 저녁 식사를 삼십 분이면 준비해 준다는데유, 나리. 닭 한 쌍에 송아지 커틀릿, 강낭콩에 감자, 타르트에 깔끔함까지지유. 제가 권해도 된다면, 나리, 그냥 여기 머무시는 게 좋겠습니다. 의사 양반 말마따나 충고를 받아들이시지유, 나리.\"\n\n마침 이 순간 새러슨스 헤드의 주인이 때맞춰 나타나, 이 여관의 숙박 시설에 관한 웰러 씨의 말을 확인해 주었다. 그리고 길 사정에 관한 갖가지 음울한 추측, 다음 역참에서 싱싱한 말을 구할 수 있을지 의심스럽다는 것, 밤새 비가 올 게 확실하다는 것, 아침이면 갤 게 그만큼 확실하다는 것, 그 밖에 여관 주인들이 흔히 쓰는 유인책을 동원하여 그의 간청을 거들었다.\n\n\"좋소.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"하지만 무슨 수를 써서든 런던으로 편지를 한 통 보내야겠소. 내일 아침 제일 먼저 배달되도록 말이오. 그게 아니면 무슨 위험을 무릅쓰고서라도 앞으로 가야 하오.\"\n\n여관 주인은 기쁨에 차 미소 지었다. 그 신사분이 편지를 갈색 종이로 싸서, 버밍엄에서 오는 우편마차나 야간 마차 편에 부치는 것보다 더 쉬운 일은 없다는 것이었다. 신사분이 되도록 빨리 전달되기를 특별히 원하신다면, 겉면에 \"즉시 배달 요망\"이라고 쓰면 되는데, 그러면 틀림없이 처리될 것이고, 또는 \"즉시 배달 시 배달부에게 반 크라운 추가 지급\"이라고 쓰면 더더욱 확실하다고 했다.\n\n\"아주 좋소.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"그럼 여기서 머물겠소.\"\n\n\"선룸에 불 켜라, 존. 난로 지피고. 신사분들 젖으셨다!\" 여관 주인이 외쳤다. \"이쪽으로 오시지요, 신사분들. 마부는 지금 신경 쓰지 마십시오, 나리. 부르시면 보내 드리겠습니다, 나리. 자, 존, 촛불 가져와.\"\n\n촛불을 가져오고, 난로를 헤집어 새 장작 한 토막을 던져 넣었다. 십 분쯤 지나자 종업원 하나가 저녁 식탁보를 깔고, 커튼을 치고, 난로가 활활 타올라, 모든 것이 (점잖은 영국 여관이라면 어디서나 늘 그렇듯이) 마치 여러 날 전부터 여행객들을 기다리며 그들의 편의를 준비해 둔 것처럼 보였다.\n\n픽윅 씨는 한쪽 곁상에 앉아 윈클 씨에게 보내는 쪽지를 서둘러 적었는데, 단지 자기가 날씨 탓에 발이 묶였으나 내일은 틀림없이 런던에 도착할 것이라고만 알렸을 뿐, 그때까지는 자신이 한 일에 관한 어떤 보고도 미뤄 두었다. 이 쪽지는 부랴부랴 꾸러미로 만들어져, 새뮤얼 웰러 씨를 통해 카운터로 보내졌다.\n\n샘은 그것을 여주인에게 맡기고, 부엌 난롯불에 몸을 말린 뒤 주인의 장화를 벗겨 드리려고 돌아가던 참이었다. 그때 반쯤 열린 문 안을 무심코 흘끗 보다가, 모래빛 머리를 한 어떤 신사의 모습에 발길을 멈추었다. 그 신사는 앞에 놓인 탁자 위에 신문 한 무더기를 쌓아 두고, 그중 한 신문의 사설을 읽고 있었는데, 코며 다른 모든 이목구비를 한껏 치켜올려 오만한 경멸이 깃든 위풍당당한 표정을 짓는, 고정된 비웃음을 띠고 있었다.\n\n\"아니!\" 샘이 말했다. \"저 머리랑 저 얼굴 생김새는 내가 알 만한데. 저 외알 안경이랑, 챙 넓은 모자까지! 이턴스윌이라 이거지, 아니면 내가 로마 사람이게.\"\n\n샘은 곧장 성가신 기침병이 든 척했으니, 그 신사의 주의를 끌기 위함이었다. 그 소리에 놀란 신사는 고개와 외알 안경을 들어 올렸고, 이턴스윌 가제트의 깊고 사색적인 얼굴, 곧 포트 씨의 얼굴을 드러내 보였다.\n\n\"실례합니다요, 나리.\" 샘이 절을 하며 다가가 말했다. \"제 주인이 여기 와 계십니다, 포트 씨.\"\n\n\"쉿! 쉿!\" 포트가 신비스러운 공포와 불안의 표정을 지으며 샘을 방 안으로 끌어들이고는 문을 닫으며 외쳤다.\n\n\"무슨 일이십니까요, 나리?\" 샘이 멍하니 주위를 둘러보며 물었다.\n\n\"내 이름은 한 마디도 속삭이지 말게.\" 포트가 대답했다. \"여긴 버프파 동네야. 흥분 잘하고 성질 급한 군중이 내가 여기 있는 걸 알면, 난 갈가리 찢기고 말 걸세.\"\n\n\"그래유! 그러시겠어유, 나리?\" 샘이 물었다.\n\n\"난 그들의 분노의 희생양이 될 거야.\" 포트가 대답했다. \"자, 젊은이, 자네 주인은 무슨 일인가?\"\n\n\"두 분 친구와 함께 도시로 가시는 길에 오늘 밤 여기 묵으십니다요.\" 샘이 대답했다.\n\n\"윈클 씨도 그중 한 사람인가?\" 포트가 살짝 인상을 찌푸리며 물었다.\n\n\"아닙니다요, 나리. 윈클 씨는 이제 집에 계세유.\" 샘이 받았다. \"결혼하셨거든유.\"\n\n\"결혼!\" 포트가 무시무시한 격정으로 외쳤다. 그는 말을 멈추고 음침하게 미소 지으며, 낮고 앙심에 찬 어조로 덧붙였다. \"그 친구한테는 그게 딱 어울리는 꼴이지!\"\n\n쓰러진 적에 대한 이 잔인한 악의의 분출과 냉혹한 승리감을 토해 낸 뒤, 포트 씨는 픽윅 씨의 친구들이 \"블루파\"인지 물었다. 그 문제에 대해 포트 자신만큼이나 잘 아는 샘에게서 더없이 만족스러운 긍정의 대답을 듣고, 그는 샘을 따라 픽윅 씨의 방으로 가는 데 동의했다. 그곳에서는 진심 어린 환영이 그를 기다리고 있었고, 저녁 식사를 함께 하기로 하는 합의가 즉석에서 이루어지고 확정되었다.\n\n\"그래, 이턴스윌은 사정이 어떻게 돌아가고 있소?\" 포트가 난로 가까이 자리를 잡고, 일행 모두가 젖은 장화를 벗고 마른 슬리퍼를 신은 뒤, 픽윅 씨가 물었다. \"인디펜던트는 아직도 명맥을 잇고 있소?\"\n\n\"인디펜던트 말입니까, 나리.\" 포트가 대답했다. \"그건 여전히 비참하고 질질 끄는 명줄을 끌고 있습니다. 그 비참하고 수치스러운 존재를 아는 몇 안 되는 자들에게마저 혐오와 멸시를 받고, 제가 그토록 흥청망청 뿌려 대는 그 오물에 질식하고, 제 진창에서 나는 독기에 눈멀고 귀먹어, 그 외설스러운 신문은 다행히도 제 비천한 처지를 의식하지 못한 채, 그 음흉한 진흙 밑으로 빠르게 가라앉고 있지요. 그 진흙은 사회의 천하고 타락한 계층 사이에서 그것에게 굳건한 발판을 주는 것처럼 보이지만, 그럼에도 그 가증스러운 머리 위로 차오르고 있어 머지않아 그것을 영영 삼켜 버릴 것입니다.\"\n\n(그가 지난주 사설의 일부였던) 이 선언문을 격렬한 어조로 토해 낸 뒤, 편집장은 숨을 고르려고 잠시 멈추고는 위풍당당하게 보브 소여를 바라보았다.\n\n\"자네는 젊은이로군.\" 포트가 말했다.\n\n보브 소여 씨가 고개를 끄덕였다.\n\n\"자네도 마찬가지고.\" 벤 앨런 씨를 향해 포트가 말했다.\n\n벤이 그 부드러운 지적을 인정했다.\n\n\"그리고 두 사람 다, 내가 살아 있는 한 이 왕국의 국민들에게 지지하고 지켜 나가겠노라 맹세한 그 블루파의 신조에 깊이 물들어 있겠지?\" 포트가 넌지시 물었다.\n\n\"글쎄요, 그건 정확히 잘 모르겠는데요.\" 보브 소여가 대답했다. \"저는—\"\n\n\"버프파는 아니지요, 픽윅 씨.\" 포트가 의자를 뒤로 빼며 끼어들었다. \"당신 친구가 버프파는 아니겠지요, 나리?\"\n\n\"아니, 아닙니다.\" 보브가 받았다. \"전 지금 일종의 격자무늬랄까요, 온갖 색이 뒤섞인 거랄까요.\"\n\n\"줏대 없는 사람이로군.\" 포트가 엄숙하게 말했다. \"줏대 없는 사람. 자네에게 이턴스윌 가제트에 실린 여덟 편의 연재 기사를 보여 주고 싶군, 나리. 감히 말하건대, 자네는 머지않아 자네 견해를 굳건하고 견실한 블루파의 토대 위에 세우게 될 거라고 보네, 나리.\"\n\n\"그 끝까지 다 읽기도 훨씬 전에 제가 새파랗게 질려 버릴 것 같은데요.\" 보브가 응수했다.\n\n* 블루(blue)에는 '블루파'라는 뜻과 '우울하다·새파랗다'는 뜻이 함께 있어 말장난이 된다.\n\n포트 씨는 몇 초 동안 보브 소여를 미심쩍게 바라보다가, 픽윅 씨에게 고개를 돌리고 말했다.\n\"지난 석 달간 이턴스윌 가제트에 간간이 실려, 그토록 널리—아니, 보편적이라 해도 좋겠습니다만—주목과 찬사를 불러일으킨 그 문예 기사들을 보셨겠지요?\"\n\n\"글쎄요.\" 그 질문에 약간 당황하여 픽윅 씨가 대답했다. \"실은, 제가 다른 일들에 워낙 매여 있었던 탓에, 정말이지 그것을 읽어 볼 기회가 없었습니다.\"\n\n\"꼭 읽으셔야 합니다, 나리.\" 포트가 근엄한 얼굴로 말했다.\n\n\"읽겠습니다.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그것은 중국 형이상학을 다룬 어떤 저작에 대한 방대한 서평의 형식으로 실렸지요, 나리.\" 포트가 말했다.\n\n\"아.\" 픽윅 씨가 한마디 했다. \"당신 펜에서 나온 것이길 바랍니다만?\"\n\n\"제 평론가의 펜에서 나온 것입니다, 나리.\" 포트가 위엄 있게 받았다.\n\n\"꽤 난해한 주제일 텐데요.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"아주 그렇지요, 나리.\" 포트가 한껏 현명한 표정을 지으며 응답했다. \"그는, 전문적이지만 적절한 표현을 쓰자면, 그걸 위해 '벼락공부'를 했습니다. 제 청에 따라 '브리태니커 백과사전'에서 그 주제를 미리 읽어 두었지요.\"\n\n\"그렇군요!\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"그 귀중한 저작에 중국 형이상학에 관한 정보가 들어 있는 줄은 몰랐습니다.\"\n\n\"그는 말입니다, 나리.\" 포트가 픽윅 씨의 무릎에 손을 얹고는 지적 우월감이 깃든 미소를 띠며 둘러보고 받았다. \"그는 형이상학(Metaphysics)은 M 항목에서, 중국(China)은 C 항목에서 읽고, 그 정보를 합친 겁니다, 나리!\"\n\n문제의 그 박식한 글에 발휘된 역량과 연구의 기억에 포트 씨의 얼굴이 어찌나 한층 위풍당당해졌는지, 픽윅 씨가 대화를 다시 이어 갈 엄두를 내기까지 몇 분이 흘렀다. 마침내 편집장의 얼굴이 차츰 평소의 도덕적 우월감 어린 표정으로 풀어지자, 픽윅 씨는 이렇게 물으며 용기를 내어 대화를 재개했다.\n\n\"이렇게 멀리까지 집을 떠나오시게 한 위대한 목적이 무엇인지 여쭤도 되겠습니까?\"\n\n\"제 모든 거대한 노고에서 저를 움직이고 분발케 하는 그 목적이지요, 나리.\" 포트가 차분한 미소를 띠며 대답했다. \"나라의 안녕 말입니다.\"\n\n\"무슨 공적 사명인가 보다고 짐작했습니다.\" 픽윅 씨가 한마디 했다.\n\n\"그렇습니다, 나리.\" 포트가 다시 말을 이었다. \"바로 그렇지요.\" 여기서 그는 픽윅 씨 쪽으로 몸을 숙여, 깊고 공허한 목소리로 속삭였다. \"내일 저녁 버밍엄에서 버프파 무도회가 열립니다, 나리.\"\n\n\"맙소사!\" 픽윅 씨가 외쳤다.\n\n\"그렇습니다, 나리, 게다가 만찬까지 곁들여서요.\" 포트가 덧붙였다.\n\n\"설마요!\" 픽윅 씨가 내뱉었다.\n\n포트가 불길하게 고개를 끄덕였다.\n그런데 픽윅 씨는 이 폭로에 경악한 척했지만, 사실 지역 정치에 워낙 어두웠던 터라 그것이 가리키는 끔찍한 음모의 중대성을 제대로 헤아릴 수가 없었다. 이를 알아챈 포트 씨는 이턴스윌 가제트 최근 호를 꺼내 그것을 참조하며 다음 문단을 읊었다.\n\n# HOLE-AND-CORNER BUFFERY.\n\n은밀한 구석에서 벌이는 버프파의 책동.\n\"파충류 같은 어느 동업지가 최근 시커먼 독기를 뿜어내며, 우리의 걸출하고 훌륭한 대표이신 존경하는 슬럼키 씨—그분이 지금의 고귀하고 드높은 지위를 얻기 훨씬 전부터 우리가, 언젠가는 지금처럼 나라의 가장 빛나는 영예이자 가장 자랑스러운 자긍심이 되리라고, 그 대담한 수호자이자 정직한 긍지가 되리라고 예언했던 바로 그 슬럼키 씨—의 깨끗한 이름을 더럽히려는 헛되고 가망 없는 시도를 했다. 우리가 말하는 이 파충류 같은 동업지는, 그 영광스러운 인물에게 황홀해진 유권자들이 헌정한, 더없이 훌륭하게 양각 무늬를 넣은 도금 석탄통을 두고 비아냥거렸다. 그 이름 없는 비열한은, 존경하는 슬럼키 씨 본인이 자기 집사의 한 막역한 친구를 통해, 모금된 전체 금액의 사분의 삼이 넘는 돈을 그 석탄통 구입에 보탰다고 넌지시 흘렸다. 아니, 그 기어 다니는 족속은 보지 못하는가, 설령 그것이 사실이라 한들, 존경하는 슬럼키 씨는 (그게 가능하다면) 전보다 한층 더 자애롭고 빛나는 모습으로 보일 따름이라는 것을? 그 둔감한 머리로도 알아채지 못하는가, 유권자단의 바람을 받들고자 하는 이 자애롭고 가슴 뭉클한 열망이야말로, 돼지만도 못하지 않은—다시 말해 우리 동업지 자신만큼 타락하지 않은—그의 동향인들의 마음과 영혼에 그를 영원히 사랑받게 만들 것임을? 그러나 은밀한 구석에서 벌이는 버프파의 책동이란 이토록 비참한 속임수다! 이것이 그들의 유일한 술책도 아니다. 반역이 횡행하고 있다. 우리는 폭로하라는 자극을 받은 지금, 대담하게 진술하노니, 그리고 보호를 구하여 이 나라와 그 경관들에게 우리 자신을 내맡기노니—우리는 대담하게 진술하노라, 바로 이 순간 버프파 무도회를 위한 은밀한 준비가 진행 중이라는 것을. 그것은 버프파의 도시에서, 버프파 인구의 한복판에서 열릴 것이며, 버프파 무도회 진행자가 주관하고, 네 명의 극단적 버프파 의원이 참석하며, 입장은 버프파 입장권으로만 가능하다! 우리의 악마 같은 동업지여, 움찔하는가? 우리가 이 글을 쓰는 동안 무력한 악의에 차서 몸부림치게 하라, 우리는 그곳에 갈 것이다.\"\n\n\"보십시오, 나리.\" 포트가 완전히 진이 빠져 신문을 접으며 말했다. \"이게 사태의 전모입니다!\"\n\n마침 이때 여관 주인과 종업원이 저녁 식사를 들고 들어오는 바람에, 포트 씨는 자기 목숨이 픽윅 씨의 손에 달려 있고 그의 비밀 엄수에 의존하고 있다는 표시로 손가락을 입술에 갖다 댔다. 이턴스윌 가제트 인용문을 읽는 동안과 그에 뒤이은 토론 동안 불경스럽게도 잠들어 있던 보브 소여 씨와 벤저민 앨런 씨는, 귀에 대고 속삭인 마법의 단어 \"저녁 식사\"라는 한마디에 깨어났다. 그리고 그들은 식욕에 따르는 좋은 소화력을 갖추고, 둘 다에 따르는 건강을 갖추고, 셋 모두에 따르는 종업원을 갖추고서 저녁 식탁으로 나아갔다.\n\n저녁 식사와 그에 이은 자리가 이어지는 동안, 포트 씨는 잠시 가정사로 화제를 낮추어, 이턴스윌의 공기가 자기 부인에게 맞지 않아, 부인이 잃었던 건강과 기운을 되찾고자 여러 유행하는 온천지를 두루 여행하는 중이라고 픽윅 씨에게 알렸다. 이는 다음과 같은 사실을 우아하게 가린 것이었다. 포트 부인은 그동안 거듭 위협해 온 별거를, 그녀의 오빠인 중위가 협상하고 포트 씨가 매듭지은 합의에 따라, 충직한 호위병을 거느린 채, 이턴스윌 가제트의 편집과 판매에서 나오는 연간 수입과 이익의 절반에 해당하는 몫을 받기로 하고 영구히 떠나 버렸던 것이다.\n\n위대한 포트 씨가 이런저런 일들을 늘어놓으며, 이따금 자신의 노작에서 뽑은 갖가지 발췌문으로 대화에 활기를 더하고 있는 동안, 짐 꾸러미를 부려 놓으려고 여관 앞에 멈춰 선, 외곽으로 향하는 역마차 창문에서 어떤 근엄한 낯선 사람이 부르며, 만일 자기가 여정을 중도에 끊고 여기서 하룻밤 묵는다면 침대와 침대틀이라는 꼭 필요한 숙박 편의를 제공받을 수 있겠느냐고 물었다.\n\n\"물론입니다, 나리.\" 여관 주인이 대답했다.\n\n\"그럴 수 있다, 그 말이지?\" 외모며 태도가 습관적으로 의심에 차 보이는 그 낯선 사람이 물었다.\n\n\"의심의 여지가 없습니다, 나리.\" 여관 주인이 대답했다.\n\n\"좋소.\" 낯선 사람이 말했다. \"마부, 나 여기서 내리겠소. 차장, 내 융단 가방!\"\n\n다른 승객들에게 다소 퉁명스러운 투로 잘 자라는 인사를 건네고, 낯선 사람은 내렸다. 그는 약간 키가 작은 신사로, 호저나 구두솔 양식으로 깎은 아주 뻣뻣한 검은 머리카락이 머리 전체에 곧추서 빳빳하게 솟아 있었다. 그 인상은 거만하고 위협적이었으며, 태도는 단호했고, 두 눈은 날카롭고 잠시도 가만있질 못했다. 그리고 그 거동 전체가 자기 자신에 대한 대단한 자신감과, 다른 모든 사람들에 대한 헤아릴 수 없는 우월감의 의식을 드러내 보였다.\n\n이 신사는 애초에 애국자 포트 씨에게 배정되었던 방으로 안내되었다. 그런데 종업원은, 그 기묘한 우연의 일치에 말없이 놀라며, 그가 촛불을 켜자마자 신사가 모자 속으로 손을 집어넣어 신문 한 부를 꺼내더니, 한 시간 전 포트의 위풍당당한 얼굴에 떠올라 그의 기운을 마비시켰던 것과 똑같은 분노 어린 경멸의 표정을 지으며 그것을 읽기 시작했다고 말했다. 종업원은 또 이런 점도 알아챘으니, 포트 씨의 경멸은 이턴스윌 인디펜던트라는 제호의 신문에 의해 촉발된 반면, 이 신사의 신랄한 경멸은 이턴스윌 가제트라는 제목의 신문에 의해 일깨워졌다는 것이었다.\n\n\"여관 주인을 보내게.\" 낯선 사람이 말했다.\n\n\"예, 나리.\" 종업원이 받았다.\n\n여관 주인이 불려 왔다.\n\n\"당신이 여관 주인이오?\" 신사가 물었다.\n\n\"그렇습니다, 나리.\" 여관 주인이 대답했다.\n\n\"날 아시오?\" 신사가 따져 물었다.\n\n\"그런 영광을 누린 적은 없습니다, 나리.\" 여관 주인이 받았다.\n\n\"내 이름은 슬러크요.\" 신사가 말했다.\n\n여관 주인은 살짝 고개를 숙였다.\n\n\"슬러크요, 나리.\" 신사가 거만하게 되풀이했다. \"이제 날 알겠소, 이 사람아?\"\n\n여관 주인은 머리를 긁적이며 천장을 보고, 낯선 사람을 보고, 힘없이 미소 지었다.\n\n\"날 알겠소, 이 사람아?\" 낯선 사람이 성을 내며 물었다.\n\n여관 주인은 안간힘을 쓰다가 마침내 대답했다. \"글쎄요, 나리, 저는 당신을 모르겠습니다.\"\n\n\"맙소사!\" 낯선 사람이 꽉 쥔 주먹을 탁자에 내리치며 말했다. \"이것이 바로 인기로구나!\"\n\n여관 주인이 문 쪽으로 한두 걸음 옮기자, 낯선 사람은 그에게 눈길을 박고 다시 말을 이었다.\n\n\"이것이,\" 낯선 사람이 말했다. \"이것이 대중을 위해 수년간 바친 노고와 연구에 대한 보답이로구나. 나는 젖고 지친 몸으로 내렸건만, 자기들의 투사를 맞으려 앞다투어 몰려드는 열광적인 군중은 없고, 교회 종소리도 잠잠하며, 그 이름조차 그들의 무딘 가슴에 아무런 응답의 감정을 불러일으키지 못하는구나. 이거야말로,\" 격앙된 슬러크 씨가 이리저리 거닐며 말했다. \"사람의 펜 속 잉크를 응고시키고, 그들의 대의를 영영 저버리게 만들 만한 일이로다.\"\n\n\"브랜디에 물을 타 드릴까요, 나리?\" 여관 주인이 슬쩍 운을 떼며 말했다.\n\n\"럼이오.\" 슬러크 씨가 사납게 돌아서며 말했다. \"어디 불 피워 놓은 데 있소?\"\n\n\"곧바로 하나 지필 수 있습니다, 나리.\" 여관 주인이 말했다.\n\n\"그건 잠자리에 들 시간이나 돼야 열기를 내겠지.\" 슬러크 씨가 가로막았다. \"부엌엔 누구 있소?\"\n\n아무도 없었다. 부엌에는 아름다운 불이 타고 있었다. 다들 가 버렸고, 여관 문도 밤이라 닫혀 있었다.\n\n\"내 럼앤드워터는,\" 슬러크 씨가 말했다. \"부엌 난롯가에서 마시겠소.\" 그리하여 모자와 신문을 챙겨 들고, 그는 여관 주인을 뒤따라 그 초라한 방으로 엄숙하게 성큼성큼 걸어가, 난롯가의 긴 의자에 몸을 던지고는 다시 경멸의 표정을 지으며 묵묵히 위엄을 갖추고 읽고 마시기 시작했다.\n\n그런데 그때 불화의 악마 하나가 새러슨스 헤드 위를 날아가다가, 그저 한가한 호기심에 눈을 아래로 던졌는데, 마침 슬러크가 부엌 난롯가에 편안히 자리 잡고 있고, 포트가 다른 방에서 술기운에 살짝 들떠 있는 것을 보게 되었다. 그러자 그 심술궂은 악마는 상상도 못 할 속도로 후자의 방으로 쏜살같이 내려와, 곧바로 보브 소여 씨의 머릿속으로 들어가, 자신의(그 악마의) 사악한 목적을 위해 그가 다음과 같이 말하도록 부추겼다.\n\n\"이봐요, 우리 불이 다 꺼졌어요. 비 온 뒤라 유난히 춥군요, 안 그래요?\"\n\n\"정말 그렇습니다.\" 픽윅 씨가 몸을 떨며 대답했다.\n\n\"부엌 난롯가에서 시가나 한 대 피우는 것도 나쁘지 않겠는데요, 안 그래요?\" 보브 소여가, 여전히 앞서 말한 악마에게 부추김을 받아 말했다.\n\n\"그거 아주 편안할 것 같군요.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다. \"포트 씨, 어떻게 생각하시오?\"\n\n포트 씨가 선뜻 동의했다. 그리하여 네 여행객은 저마다 손에 잔을 들고 곧장 부엌으로 향했고, 샘 웰러가 길을 안내하며 행렬의 선두에 섰다.\n\n낯선 사람은 여전히 읽고 있다가, 고개를 들고 흠칫했다. 포트 씨도 흠칫했다.\n\n\"무슨 일이오?\" 픽윅 씨가 속삭였다.\n\n\"저 파충류!\" 포트가 대답했다.\n\n\"무슨 파충류요?\" 픽윅 씨가, 혹시 웃자란 검은 딱정벌레나 수종에 걸린 거미를 밟을까 두려워 주위를 둘러보며 말했다.\n\n\"저 파충류 말입니다.\" 포트가 픽윅 씨의 팔을 붙잡고 낯선 사람 쪽을 가리키며 속삭였다. \"인디펜던트의 저 파충류 슬러크!\"\n\n\"우리가 물러나는 게 낫지 않을까요.\" 픽윅 씨가 속삭였다.\n\n\"절대 안 됩니다, 나리.\" 포트가 두 가지 의미로 술기운에 용감해져서 받았다. \"절대로.\" 이 말과 함께 포트 씨는 맞은편 긴 의자에 자리를 잡고, 자그마한 신문 뭉치에서 하나를 골라 자기 적수에 맞서 읽기 시작했다.\n\n* 술기운에 용감해진다는 표현은 영어의 'pot-valiant'를 옮긴 것으로, 포트(Pott)의 이름과도 겹쳐 말장난이 된다.\n포트 씨는 물론 인디펜던트를 읽었고, 슬러크 씨는 물론 가제트를 읽었다. 그리고 두 신사는 각각 상대방의 글에 대한 경멸을 신랄한 웃음과 비아냥거리는 코웃음으로 또렷이 드러냈다. 거기서 그들은 한층 더 노골적인 의견 표명으로 나아갔으니, 이를테면 \"터무니없군\", \"한심하군\", \"잔학무도해\", \"허튼소리야\", \"협잡질\", \"오물\", \"쓰레기\", \"진창\", \"도랑물\" 따위, 그 밖에 그와 비슷한 성격의 평론적 언급들이었다.\n\n보브 소여 씨와 벤 앨런 씨는 이 경쟁과 증오의 징후를 어찌나 흐뭇하게 지켜보았는지, 그 덕에 그들이 한껏 힘차게 뻐끔거리던 시가에 큰 풍미가 더해졌다. 그 기세가 수그러들기 시작하는 순간, 짓궂은 보브 소여 씨가 슬러크에게 아주 정중하게 말을 건넸다.\n\n\"나리, 그 신문 다 보시거든 제가 좀 봐도 되겠습니까?\"\n\n\"이 하찮은 물건에서는 수고한 보람을 거둘 만한 게 거의 없을 겁니다, 나리.\" 슬러크가 포트에게 악마 같은 인상을 쓰며 대답했다.\n\n\"이건 곧 보시게 해 드리지요.\" 포트가 같은 이유로 분노에 새파래진 채, 떨리는 목소리로 고개를 들며 말했다. \"하! 하! 이 작자의 뻔뻔함에는 재미있어하시게 될 겁니다.\"\n\n\"물건\"과 \"작자\"에는 무시무시한 강조가 실렸고, 두 편집장의 얼굴은 도전으로 이글거리기 시작했다.\n\n\"이 비참한 자의 상스러운 욕설은 경멸스러울 만큼 역겹군요.\" 포트가 보브 소여에게 말을 거는 척하면서 슬러크를 노려보며 말했다.\n\n여기서 슬러크 씨가 아주 호탕하게 웃으며, 새 단을 편하게 읽을 수 있도록 신문을 접고는, 그 멍청이가 정말이지 자기를 즐겁게 한다고 말했다.\n\n\"이 작자, 어찌나 뻔뻔한 얼간이인지.\" 포트가 분홍빛에서 진홍빛으로 변하며 말했다.\n\n\"이 사람이 쓴 어리석은 글을 읽어 본 적 있으십니까, 나리?\" 슬러크가 보브 소여에게 물었다.\n\n\"전혀요.\" 보브가 대답했다. \"그렇게 형편없습니까?\"\n\n\"오, 끔찍하지요! 끔찍해요!\" 슬러크가 받았다.\n\n\"정말이지! 이런, 이건 너무 잔학무도하군!\" 바로 이 대목에서 포트가, 여전히 자기 신문에 몰두한 척하며 외쳤다.\n\n\"악의와 비열함과 거짓과 위증과 배신과 위선으로 가득한 몇 문장을 헤쳐 나갈 수 있으시다면,\" 슬러크가 보브에게 신문을 건네며 말했다. \"이 문법도 모르는 허튼소리꾼의 문체를 비웃는 것으로 다소간 보답을 받으실지도 모르겠군요.\"\n\n\"방금 뭐라고 하셨소, 나리?\" 포트 씨가 격분으로 온몸을 부들부들 떨며 고개를 들고 물었다.\n\n\"당신과 무슨 상관이오, 나리?\" 슬러크가 대답했다.\n\n\"문법도 모르는 허튼소리꾼이라고 했소, 나리?\" 포트가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇소, 나리, 그랬소.\" 슬러크가 대답했다. \"그게 더 마음에 드신다면, '블루파 멧돼지'라고 하지요, 나리. 하! 하!\"\n\n* '블루 보어(blue bore)'는 '블루파의 따분한 자'라는 뜻과 '파란 멧돼지'라는 뜻을 함께 노린 조롱이다.\n포트 씨는 이 익살스러운 모욕에 한마디도 응수하지 않고, 천천히 자기 인디펜던트 신문을 접어, 조심스럽게 납작하게 펴고, 장화 밑에 짓밟고, 거창한 격식을 차려 침을 뱉고는, 그것을 불 속에 던져 버렸다.\n\n\"보시오, 나리.\" 포트가 난로에서 물러서며 말했다. \"다행히 그자에게는 다행스럽게도 내가 우리나라 법에 매여 있지 않다면, 그것을 만들어 내는 그 독사를 바로 이렇게 처리할 거요.\"\n\n\"그자를 그렇게 처리하시오, 나리!\" 슬러크가 벌떡 일어서며 외쳤다. \"이런 경우라면 그자는 결코 그 법에 호소하지 않을 거요, 나리. 그자를 그렇게 처리하시오, 나리!\"\n\n\"옳소! 옳소!\" 보브 소여가 말했다.\n\n\"더없이 공정한 말씀이오.\" 벤 앨런 씨가 한마디 했다.\n\n\"그자를 그렇게 처리하시오, 나리!\" 슬러크가 큰 소리로 거듭 외쳤다.\n\n포트 씨가 경멸의 눈길을 쏘아붙였는데, 그것은 닻이라도 시들게 할 만했다.\n\n\"그자를 그렇게 처리하시오, 나리!\" 슬러크가 아까보다 더 큰 소리로 거듭 외쳤다.\n\n\"그러지 않겠소, 나리.\" 포트가 받았다.\n\n\"오, 그러지 않겠다, 그러지 않겠다 이거지요, 나리?\" 슬러크 씨가 비아냥거리는 투로 말했다. \"여러분, 이 말 들으셨지요! 이 사람은 안 하겠다는군요. 겁이 나서가 아니라—오, 천만에! 안 하겠다는 거예요. 하! 하!\"\n\n\"나는 당신을, 나리,\" 이 빈정거림에 발끈한 포트 씨가 말했다. \"나는 당신을 독사로 봅니다. 나는 당신을, 나리, 더없이 뻔뻔하고 수치스럽고 가증스러운 공적 행위로 스스로를 사회의 울타리 밖에 내던진 인간으로 봅니다. 나는 당신을, 나리, 사적으로나 정치적으로나 오로지 더없이 유례없고 에누리 없는 독사로밖에 보지 않습니다.\"\n\n분개한 인디펜던트는 이 인신공격성 규탄의 끝을 들으려고 기다리지 않았다. 옮길 만한 물건들로 가득 채운 융단 가방을 집어 들어, 포트가 돌아서는 순간 공중에서 휘둘러, 그것을 빙 돌리는 동작으로 그의 머리 위에 떨어뜨렸기 때문이다. 마침 두툼한 머리솔이 들어 있던 바로 그 가방 모서리가 떨어지면서 부엌 전체에 날카로운 충돌음이 울려 퍼졌고, 포트는 단번에 바닥에 나뒹굴었다.\n\n\"여러분.\" 포트가 벌떡 일어나 부삽을 움켜쥐자 픽윅 씨가 외쳤다. \"여러분! 제발 좀 생각해 보시오—도와줘—샘—이리 와—제발, 여러분—누가 좀 말려 주시오.\"\n\n이런 두서없는 외침을 내뱉으며, 픽윅 씨는 격분한 두 싸움꾼 사이로 돌진했는데, 마침 융단 가방을 한쪽 옆구리에, 부삽을 다른 쪽 옆구리에 받기에 딱 알맞은 때였다. 이턴스윌 여론을 대표하는 이 두 사람이 적개심에 눈이 멀었던 것인지, 아니면 (둘 다 예리한 논리가였던 만큼) 모든 타격을 대신 받아 줄 제삼자를 사이에 두는 이점을 알아챈 것인지는 모르겠으나, 분명한 것은 그들이 픽윅 씨에게는 조금도 주의를 기울이지 않은 채, 대단한 기세로 서로에게 맞서며 융단 가방과 부삽을 더없이 겁 없이 휘둘러 댔다는 사실이다. 주인의 외침에 이끌려 웰러 씨가 그 순간 뛰어 들어와 곡식 자루를 낚아채, 힘센 포트의 머리와 어깨 위로 그것을 뒤집어씌워 어깨를 단단히 끌어안고서 효과적으로 싸움을 멈추지 않았더라면, 픽윅 씨는 그 인도적인 개입의 대가로 틀림없이 심하게 다쳤을 것이다.\n\n\"저 다른 미치광이한테서 저 가방을 뺏어요.\" 샘이 벤 앨런과 보브 소여에게 말했다. 그 둘은 거북 등딱지 란셋을 저마다 손에 들고, 처음 기절하는 사람의 피를 뽑을 채비를 한 채 무리 주위를 빙빙 돌며 피하기만 하고 있었던 것이다. \"내놔, 이 가엾고 쪼끄만 작자야, 안 그러면 그 안에다 처박아 질식시켜 버릴 테니까.\"\n\n이 위협에 겁먹고, 또 완전히 숨이 차서, 인디펜던트는 순순히 무장 해제당했다. 그리고 웰러 씨는 포트에게서 그 덮개를 벗겨 내고, 한마디 주의를 주며 그를 풀어 주었다.\n\n\"두 분은 조용히 자러 들어가세유.\" 샘이 말했다. \"안 그러면 두 분 다 거기 처넣고, 입을 묶은 채로 결판을 내게 할 테니까유. 이런 짓거리를 한다면 이런 작자 열둘이라도 그렇게 해 버릴 겁니다. 그리고 나리, 괜찮으시다면 이쪽으로 와 주시겠어유.\"\n\n이렇게 주인에게 말을 건네며, 샘은 그의 팔을 잡고 데리고 나갔다. 그동안 경쟁 관계의 두 편집장은 보브 소여 씨와 벤저민 앨런 씨의 감독하에 여관 주인에 의해 각각 침대로 옮겨졌는데, 가면서도 피비린내 나는 위협을 잔뜩 내뱉고, 이튿날 목숨을 건 결투를 하자며 막연한 약속을 잡았다. 그러나 곰곰이 생각해 보고 나니, 인쇄물로 하면 훨씬 더 잘할 수 있겠다는 데 생각이 미쳤고, 그래서 그들은 지체 없이 죽기 살기 식 적대를 재개했다. 그리하여 이턴스윌 전역이 그들의 대담함으로—지면상에서—떠들썩하게 울려 퍼졌다.\n\n그들은 다른 여행객들이 채 움직이기도 전, 이튿날 아침 일찍 각자 다른 마차를 타고 떠나 버렸다. 그리고 이제 날씨가 개었으므로, 마차에 함께 탄 일행은 다시 한번 런던으로 얼굴을 향했다.\n\n젊은 부부가 두 사람을 맞을 마음의 준비가 충분히 될 때까지 보브 소여나 벤 앨런을 그들에게 소개하지 않는 것이 도리에 맞는 일이라 여기고, 또 가능한 한 아라벨라의 감정을 배려하고자 한 픽윅 씨는, 자기와 샘은 조지 앤드 벌처 근처에서 내리고 두 청년은 당분간 다른 곳에 묵는 것이 좋겠다고 제안했다. 두 사람은 이에 흔쾌히 동의하여 제안은 그대로 실행되었으니, 벤 앨런 씨와 보브 소여 씨는 자치구의 가장 외진 변두리에 있는 어느 한적한 술집으로 물러갔다. 그 술집 카운터 문 뒤에는 지난날 그들의 이름이 흰 분필로 길고 복잡하게 적힌 외상 계산서의 머리에 무던히도 자주 올라 있곤 했었다.\n\n\"어머나, 웰러 씨.\" 문간에서 샘을 맞으며 예쁜 하녀가 말했다.\n\n\"어머나라, 정말 그랬으면 좋겠구려, 내 사랑.\" 샘은 주인이 들리지 않을 만큼 멀어지도록 뒤로 처지면서 대꾸했다. \"당신은 어쩌면 그렇게 사랑스럽게 생겼소, 메리!\"\n\n\"어머, 웰러 씨, 무슨 그런 실없는 말씀을 하세요!\" 메리가 말했다. \"어머! 그러지 마세요, 웰러 씨.\"\n\n\"뭘 그러지 말라는 거요, 내 사랑?\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 그거 말이에요.\" 예쁜 하녀가 대꾸했다. \"어머, 저리 좀 비키세요.\" 이렇게 그를 나무라면서 예쁜 하녀는 샘을 벽 쪽으로 밀치며, 그가 자기 모자를 헝클어뜨리고 머리의 곱슬을 다 풀어 놓았다고 항변했다.\n\n\"게다가 제가 하려던 말까지 못 하게 했잖아요.\" 메리가 덧붙였다. \"여기 나흘이나 당신을 기다리던 편지가 있어요. 당신이 떠난 지 반 시간도 안 돼서 왔지 뭐예요. 게다가 겉봉에 '급함'이라고 적혀 있다고요.\"\n\n\"어디 있소, 내 사랑?\" 샘이 물었다.\n\n\"제가 잘 챙겨 뒀어요. 안 그랬으면 진작에 없어졌을 거예요.\" 메리가 대꾸했다. \"자, 받으세요. 당신한테는 과분하지만요.\"\n\n이렇게 말하면서, 혹시 잃어버린 건 아닐까 하는 갖가지 귀엽고 새침한 의심과 걱정과 바람을 늘어놓은 끝에, 메리는 더없이 곱고 자그마한 모슬린 가슴 장식 뒤에서 편지를 꺼내 샘에게 건넸고, 샘은 그에 무척이나 정중하고 헌신적인 태도로 그 편지에 입을 맞추었다.\n\n\"어머, 세상에!\" 가슴 장식을 매만지며 짐짓 모르는 척하면서 메리가 말했다. \"갑자기 그 편지를 무척이나 아끼시게 됐나 봐요.\"\n\n이에 웰러 씨는 그저 윙크 하나로 답했을 뿐인데, 그 깊디깊은 뜻이라는 것은 어떤 묘사로도 그 희미한 기색조차 전할 수 없는 것이었다. 그러고는 창가 자리에 메리 곁에 앉아 편지를 펴서 그 내용을 훑어보았다.\n\n\"아니!\" 샘이 외쳤다. \"이게 다 뭐람?\"\n\n\"무슨 일 생긴 건 아니겠죠?\" 메리가 어깨 너머로 들여다보며 말했다.\n\n\"그 눈 좀 보게나!\" 샘이 고개를 들며 말했다.\n\n\"제 눈은 신경 쓰지 마시고, 편지나 읽으시는 게 훨씬 낫겠어요.\" 예쁜 하녀가 말했다. 그러면서 어찌나 교활하고 어여쁘게 눈을 반짝거리는지, 도무지 견딜 수가 없을 정도였다.\n샘은 입맞춤으로 기운을 차린 뒤, 다음과 같이 읽어 내려갔다.\n\n\"그랜비 후작 여관, 도킹 인근, 수요일.\n사랑하는 새믈에게,\n\n너의 새어머이가 빗속에 축축한 풀밭에 너무 오래 분별없이 앉아 어떤 목자의 설교를 듣다가 감기가 든 까닭에 나쁜 소식을 전하는 곰이 되는 기쁨을 누리게 되어 무척 안타깝구나 그 목자란 작자가 브랜디와 물로 제 몸에 태엽을 감아 놓는 바람에 밤늦도록 그칠 줄을 몰랏고 어느 정도 술이 깰 때까지 제 입을 멈출 수가 업서서 그러느라 여러 시간이 걸렷단다 의사 말이 만일 너의 새어머이가 그 전이 아니라 그 후에 따뜻한 브랜디 물을 들이켯더면 더 나뻐지지 안앗을 거라더구나 즉시 바퀴에 기름을 치고 다시 굴러가게 하느라 생각해낼 수 잇는 온갖 일을 다 햇단다 너의 아버이는 늘 하던 대로 회복하기를 바랏지마는 모퉁이를 돌려는 찰나에 얘야 그만 길을 잘못 들어 너는 본 적도 업는 속도로 비탈을 내려가 버렷고 의사 양반이 곧바로 제동을 걸엇건마는 아무 소용이 업서서 어제 저녁 여섯시 이십분 전에 마지막 요금소를 지나고 말앗단다 정해진 시간보다 훨씬 일찍 여정을 마첫스니 이는 아마도 도중에 짐을 아주 조금밖에 싣지 안앗던 까닭이리라 너의 아버이가 말하기를 새미야 네가 와서 나를 본다면 무척 큰 호의로 여기겟다더구나 나는 무척 외롭단다 새미블 추신 아버이가 굳이 그 철자로 쓰라 하는데 나는 그게 옳지 안타고 말한다 그리고 정리할 일이 하도 만흐니 네 주인 양반도 분명 반대하지 안을 거란다 물론 반대하지 안켓지 새미 나는 그분을 더 잘 아니까 그러니 그분이 안부를 전하시고 나도 거기 더하여 새미블 지긋지긋하게 너를 사랑하는\n\n토니 웰러로부터.\"\n\"참 알아먹기 힘든 편지로군.\" 샘이 말했다. \"이 '그가' '내가' 하는 통에 누가 무슨 뜻인지 알겠어! 이 인쇄체로 박은 서명만 빼고는 아버지 글씨가 아냐. 이건 아버지가 쓴 게 맞고.\"\n\n\"누구한테 대신 써 달라고 하시고는 서명만 직접 하셨나 봐요.\" 예쁜 하녀가 말했다.\n\n\"잠깐만.\" 샘은 다시 편지를 훑어보며 이따금 멈춰 곰곰이 생각하면서 대꾸했다. \"당신이 맞혔어. 이걸 쓴 양반은 그 불행한 일을 제대로 차근차근 다 적어 가던 참인데, 그때 아버지가 들여다보면서 끼어들어 가지고 일을 죄다 뒤죽박죽으로 만든 거야. 딱 아버지가 할 만한 짓이지. 당신 말이 맞아, 메리 내 사랑.\"\n\n이 점에 대해 확신을 얻은 샘은 편지를 다시 한 번 처음부터 끝까지 읽었고, 비로소 그 내용을 또렷이 파악한 듯 편지를 접으며 생각에 잠긴 채 내뱉었다.\n\n\"그래서 가엾은 양반이 죽었단 말이지! 안됐구먼. 그 목자들만 가만 내버려 뒀더라면 나쁜 마음씨를 가진 여자는 아니었는데. 참 안됐어.\"\n\n웰러 씨가 이 말을 어찌나 진지하게 내뱉는지, 예쁜 하녀는 눈을 내리깔고 무척 엄숙한 표정을 지었다.\n\n\"하지만,\" 샘은 가벼운 한숨과 함께 편지를 주머니에 넣으며 말했다. \"이렇게 될 일이었고—그렇게 됐지, 하인이랑 결혼하고 나서 노부인이 한 말처럼 말이야. 이제 와서 어쩔 수 없는 일이지, 안 그래, 메리?\"\n\n메리는 고개를 가로젓고 자기도 한숨을 쉬었다.\n\n\"황제 폐하께 휴가를 청해야겠어.\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n메리가 다시 한숨을 쉬었다—편지가 어찌나 가슴 아픈지.\n\n\"잘 있어!\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n\"잘 가요.\" 예쁜 하녀가 고개를 돌리며 받아 말했다.\n\n\"이봐, 악수는 하고 가야지, 안 그래?\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n예쁜 하녀가 손을 내밀었는데, 하녀의 손이라고는 하나 무척 작고 고운 손이었다. 그러고는 일어나서 가려 했다.\n\n\"오래 떠나 있진 않을 거야.\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n\"당신은 늘 떠나 있잖아요.\" 메리가 고개를 살짝 까딱 치켜들며 말했다. \"웰러 씨, 당신은 오기가 무섭게 또 떠나 버린다고요.\"\n\n웰러 씨는 이 집안의 미인을 자기 쪽으로 더 가까이 끌어당기고는 속닥속닥 대화를 시작했는데, 얼마 진행되지도 않아 그녀는 얼굴을 돌려 그를 너그러이 다시 바라보았다. 두 사람이 헤어질 무렵, 어찌 된 영문인지 그녀는 안주인 앞에 나설 생각을 하기에 앞서 반드시 자기 방에 가서 모자와 곱슬머리를 다시 매만져야만 했다. 그 준비 의식을 치르러 가면서 그녀는 계단을 종종걸음으로 올라가며 난간 너머로 샘에게 수없이 고갯짓과 미소를 보냈다.\n\n\"길어야 하루나 이틀이면 됩니다요, 나리.\" 아버지가 상을 당했다는 소식을 픽윅 씨에게 전한 뒤 샘이 말했다.\n\n\"필요한 만큼 얼마든지 있어도 좋네, 샘.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다. \"내가 전적으로 허락할 테니 머물러 있게.\"\n\n샘이 고개를 숙여 인사했다.\n\"자네 아버지께 전하게, 샘. 지금 같은 처지에 내가 무엇이든 도울 일이 있다면, 내 힘이 닿는 한 어떤 도움이든 기꺼이 흔쾌히 베풀겠노라고 말일세.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"고맙습니다요, 나리.\" 샘이 받아 말했다. \"그렇게 전하겠습니다요, 나리.\"\n\n그리고 서로의 호의와 관심을 담은 몇 마디를 나눈 뒤, 주인과 하인은 헤어졌다.\n\n새뮤얼 웰러가 도킹을 지나는 역마차의 마부석에서 내려 그랜비 후작 여관에서 몇백 야드도 안 되는 곳에 섰을 때는 막 일곱 시였다. 춥고 음울한 저녁이었다. 작은 거리는 황량하고 쓸쓸해 보였고, 그 고귀하고 늠름한 후작의 마호가니 얼굴은 바람에 구슬프게 삐걱대며 이리저리 흔들리는 동안 평소보다 한층 더 슬프고 우울한 표정을 띤 듯했다. 차양은 내려져 있었고 덧창은 반쯤 닫혀 있었다. 평소 문간에 모여 있던 한가한 무리는 한 사람도 보이지 않았다. 그곳은 적막하고 황량했다.\n\n미리 무언가 물어볼 만한 사람이 아무도 보이지 않자, 샘은 살그머니 안으로 걸어 들어가 주위를 둘러보다가 이내 저만치에서 아버지를 알아보았다.\n\n홀아비가 된 그는 카운터 뒤편의 작은 방에서 둥근 탁자 앞에 앉아 파이프를 피우며 두 눈을 불에 골똘히 박고 있었다. 장례식은 분명 그날 치러진 모양이었으니, 그가 여태 머리에 쓰고 있는 모자에는 한 야드 반쯤 되는 길이의 상장(喪章) 띠가 달려 의자 등받이 위로 넘겨져 무심하게 늘어져 있었다. 웰러 씨는 무척 넋이 나간 듯 사색에 잠긴 기분이었다. 샘이 몇 번이나 이름을 불렀건만 그는 여전히 그 한결같이 고요한 표정으로 담배만 피워 댔고, 마침내 아들이 그의 어깨에 손바닥을 얹고 나서야 비로소 정신을 차렸다.\n\n\"새미야,\" 웰러 씨가 말했다. \"어서 오너라.\"\n\n\"여섯 번이나 불렀는데,\" 샘이 못걸이에 모자를 걸며 말했다. \"못 들으셨어요.\"\n\n\"못 들었다, 새미야.\" 웰러 씨가 다시 생각에 잠긴 채 불을 바라보며 대답했다. \"내가 영중(靈中)에 빠져 있었지, 새미야.\"\n\n\"무슨 영중요?\" 샘이 의자를 불 쪽으로 당기며 물었다.\n\n\"영중이지, 새미야.\" 웰러 영감이 대답했다. \"그 사람 생각하느라, 새미블.\" 여기서 웰러 씨는 그 말이 고인이 된 웰러 부인을 가리키는 것임을 무언으로 설명하듯 도킹 교회 묘지 쪽으로 고개를 까딱했다.\n\n\"내가 생각하고 있었단다, 새미야.\" 웰러 씨가 파이프 너머로 아들을 무척 진지하게 바라보며 말했는데, 마치 이 말이 아무리 별나고 믿기 어렵게 들릴지라도 차분하고 신중하게 한 말임을 일러 주려는 듯했다. \"내가 생각하고 있었어, 새미야, 대체로 그 사람이 떠난 게 무척 안타깝다고 말이다.\"\n\n\"그야, 안타까워하셔야죠.\" 샘이 대답했다.\n\n웰러 씨는 그 말에 동의하듯 고개를 끄덕이고는, 다시 두 눈을 불에 박고 연기 구름 속에 몸을 감추며 깊은 상념에 잠겼다.\n\n\"그 사람이 한 말이 무척 사리에 닿는 소리였지, 새미야.\" 한참 침묵을 지키던 웰러 씨가 손으로 연기를 휘저으며 말했다.\n\n\"무슨 말요?\" 샘이 물었다.\n\n\"그 사람이 병들고 나서 한 말 말이다.\" 노인이 대답했다.\n\n\"무슨 말이었는데요?\"\n\n\"대충 이런 뜻이었지. '여보,' 그 사람이 말하더구나, '내가 당신한테 마땅히 했어야 할 만큼 제대로 못 해 준 것 같아 마음이 쓰여요. 당신은 무척 마음씨 고운 사람인데, 내가 우리 집을 더 편안하게 만들 수도 있었을 텐데. 이제야 알겠어요,' 그 사람이 말하길, '너무 늦어 버렸지만, 결혼한 여자가 신앙심을 가지려면 우선 집안에서 제 본분을 다하고 곁에 있는 사람들을 즐겁고 행복하게 해 주는 데서부터 시작해야 하고, 적당한 때에 교회든 예배당이든 어디든 가더라도 이런 일을 게으름이나 제 욕심의 핑계로 둔갑시키지 않도록 무척 조심해야 한다는 걸요. 나는 그렇게 해 버렸어요,' 그 사람이 말하길, '나보다 그런 짓을 더 한 작자들한테 시간이며 재물을 다 허비해 버렸지요. 하지만 여보, 내가 떠나고 나면 그 사람들을 알기 전의 나를, 본디 천성 그대로의 나를 떠올려 주기를 바라요.' '수전,' 내가 말했다—나는 이 말에 그만 목이 콱 메었다, 새미블. 그건 부인하지 않으마, 얘야—'수전,' 내가 말했지, '당신은 통틀어서 나한테 무척 좋은 아내였소. 그런 말은 일절 하지 마오. 마음 단단히 먹어요, 여보. 그러면 당신은 살아서 내가 저 스티긴스란 작자 머리통을 후려치는 꼴을 보게 될 거요.' 그 사람이 이 말에 웃더구나, 새미블.\" 노인이 파이프로 한숨을 짓누르며 말했다. \"그래도 결국 죽고 말았지!\"\n\n\"뭐,\" 노인이 천천히 고개를 좌우로 가로젓고 엄숙하게 담배를 피우며 보낸 서너 분이 흐른 뒤, 샘이 소박한 위로를 한마디 건네 보며 말했다. \"뭐, 아버지, 우리도 다 언젠가는 그리 되는 거잖아요.\"\n\n\"그렇고말고, 새미야.\" 웰러 영감이 말했다.\n\n\"그게 다 섭리지요.\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n\"물론 그렇지.\" 그의 아버지가 진지하게 동의하듯 고개를 끄덕이며 대답했다. \"그게 없으면 장의사들은 뭐가 되겠냐, 새미야?\"\n\n이 생각이 열어 놓은 무한한 추측의 들판에서 길을 잃은 웰러 영감은 파이프를 탁자에 내려놓고 사색에 잠긴 얼굴로 불을 쑤셨다.\n\n노인이 이러고 있는 사이, 상복을 차려입은 무척 풍만하게 생긴 요리사 여인이, 그때껏 카운터에서 부산을 떨고 있다가, 슬며시 방 안으로 미끄러져 들어와 샘에게 알은체하는 웃음을 잔뜩 흘리고는 조용히 그 아버지의 의자 뒤에 자리를 잡고서 가벼운 기침으로 자기가 왔음을 알렸다. 그 기침이 무시당하자 더 큰 기침이 뒤따랐다.\n\n\"이런!\" 웰러 영감이 둘러보다가 부지깽이를 떨어뜨리고 황급히 의자를 빼며 말했다. \"또 무슨 일이오?\"\n\n\"차 한잔 드세요, 그래야 착하시죠.\" 풍만한 여인이 어르듯 대답했다.\n\n\"안 마시오.\" 웰러 씨가 다소 거칠게 대답했다. \"그쪽 얼굴이나—\" 웰러 씨는 황급히 말을 멈추고 낮은 소리로 덧붙였다. \"다신 안 볼 테요.\"\n\n\"어머나, 저런, 저런! 역경이란 게 사람을 어찌나 변하게 하는지!\" 여인이 위를 올려다보며 말했다.\n\n\"내 처지를 바꿔 놓을 거라곤 여기서 의사한테까지 가는 일밖에 없소.\" 웰러 씨가 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"이렇게 심술궂은 사람은 정말이지 처음 봐요.\" 풍만한 여인이 말했다.\n\n\"상관없소. 다 내 좋자고 하는 거니까. 매를 맞을 때 뉘우치는 학동이 제 심정을 달랜다는 게 바로 이런 생각이지.\" 노인이 받아 말했다.\n\n풍만한 여인은 가엾고 측은해하는 기색으로 고개를 가로젓고는, 샘에게 호소하며 그의 아버지가 정말이지 기운을 차리려고 애써야 하지 않겠느냐고, 저렇게 의기소침에 빠져들면 안 되는 것 아니냐고 물었다.\n\n\"있잖아요, 새뮤얼 씨,\" 풍만한 여인이 말했다. \"제가 어제 그분께 말씀드렸듯이, 외로우신 거야 당연하지요, 어쩔 수 없는 일이고요, 하지만 마음을 굳게 먹으셔야지요. 어머나, 우리 모두 그분이 상을 당한 걸 안타까워하고 무엇이든 해 드리려는 마음인걸요. 그리고 새뮤얼 씨, 세상에 아무리 나쁜 처지라도 나아질 수 없는 처지란 없는 법이에요. 그건 제 남편이 죽었을 때 어느 무척 훌륭한 분이 제게 해 주신 말씀이지요.\" 여기서 여인은 손을 입에 갖다 대고 다시 기침을 하고는 웰러 영감을 다정하게 바라보았다.\n\n\"지금 당장 당신 얘기 따위는 필요 없으니, 그만 물러나 주시는 친절을 베풀어 주시겠소, 부인?\" 웰러 씨가 진중하고 차분한 목소리로 물었다.\n\n\"어머나, 웰러 씨,\" 풍만한 여인이 말했다. \"저는 그저 친절한 마음에서 말씀드린 거예요.\"\n\n\"그렇겠지요, 부인.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다. \"새미블, 이 부인을 모셔다 드리고 나가시거든 문을 닫아라.\"\n\n이 암시가 풍만한 여인에게 헛되이 들리지는 않았으니, 그녀는 즉시 방을 나가며 등 뒤로 문을 쾅 닫았다. 그러자 웰러 영감은 진땀을 줄줄 흘리며 의자에 털썩 기대어 말했다.\n\n\"새미야, 내가 여기 혼자 일주일만—단 일주일만, 얘야—머물러 있다간, 저 여편네가 그 안에 힘으로 우격다짐으로 나랑 결혼해 버릴 게다.\"\n\n\"아니! 그렇게나 아버지를 좋아한답니까?\" 샘이 물었다.\n\n\"좋아하다마다!\" 그의 아버지가 대답했다. \"도무지 떼어 놓을 수가 없어. 내가 특허 자물쇠 달린 내화 금고 속에 갇혀 있어도, 저 여자는 기어이 나한테 다가올 방도를 찾아낼 게다, 새미야.\"\n\n\"그렇게나 사람 좋아함을 받는다는 게 어떤 일인지!\" 샘이 웃으며 한마디 했다.\n\n\"난 거기서 자랑거리라곤 하나도 못 느낀다, 새미야.\" 웰러 씨가 사납게 불을 쑤시며 대답했다. \"끔찍한 처지야. 그것 때문에 내가 멀쩡한 내 집에서 쫓겨나다시피 했지. 네 가엾은 새어머이 몸에서 숨이 채 빠져나가기도 전에, 어떤 노파는 잼 한 단지를 보내오고, 또 다른 노파는 젤리 한 단지를 보내오고, 또 다른 여편네는 캐모마일 차를 큰 주전자로 한가득 끓여서 제 손으로 직접 들고 오더구나.\" 웰러 씨는 진절머리 나는 기색으로 말을 멈추고는 주위를 둘러보며 속삭여 덧붙였다. \"다들 과부였다, 새미야, 죄다 과부였어. 캐모마일 차 끓여 온 여편네만 빼고. 그건 쉰셋 먹은 미혼 처녀였지.\"\n\n샘이 익살스러운 표정으로 답하자, 노인은 마지막에 언급한 과부들 중 하나의 머리통이라도 되는 양 진지함과 악의가 가득 어린 얼굴로 고집스러운 석탄 덩이를 깨뜨리고는 말했다.\n\n\"한마디로, 새미야, 난 마부석 위 말고는 어디서도 안전하지가 않다는 걸 느낀다.\"\n\n\"거기가 다른 데보다 어째서 더 안전한데요?\" 샘이 끼어들었다.\n\n\"마부란 특권을 누리는 사람이니까.\" 웰러 씨가 아들을 빤히 바라보며 대답했다. \"마부는 다른 사내들이 의심받을 일을 의심 안 받고 할 수 있으니까. 마부는 팔십 마일에 걸친 여자들과 아주 다정한 사이로 지내도, 그중 누구랑 결혼할 마음이라고 생각하는 사람이 아무도 없으니까. 다른 어떤 사내가 그런 말을 할 수 있겠냐, 새미야?\"\n\n\"그야, 일리가 있네요.\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n\"네 주인 양반이 마부였더라면,\" 웰러 씨가 따져 말했다. \"설령 그 일이 그 지경까지 갈 수 있었다 쳐도, 그 배심원들이 과연 유죄 판결을 내렸겠냐? 감히 그러지 못했을 게다.\"\n\n\"왜 못 해요?\" 샘이 다소 깔보듯 말했다.\n\n\"왜 못 하냐고!\" 웰러 씨가 받아 말했다. \"그건 양심에 거슬리는 일이 됐을 테니까. 제대로 된 마부란 독신과 결혼 사이를 잇는 일종의 연결 고리거든. 분별 있는 사람이라면 누구나 그걸 안다.\"\n\n\"그러니까! 마부들이 다들 인기를 누리고, 아무도 그들을 함부로 이용해 먹지 않는다, 뭐 그런 뜻이에요?\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n그의 아버지가 고개를 끄덕였다.\n\n\"어쩌다가 그 지경에까지 이르렀는지는,\" 웰러 영감이 말을 이었다. \"나도 말 못 한다. 어째서 장거리 마부들이 그런 매력을 지녀서, 거쳐 가는 마을마다 젊은 여자란 여자는 죄다 그들을 우러러보는지—숭배한다고까지 할 수 있지—나도 모른다. 그저 그렇다는 것만 안다. 그건 천성의 규율이지—네 가엾은 새어머이 말마따나 제약(製藥)이라고나 할까.\"\n\n\"섭리(攝理)요.\" 샘이 노인의 말을 바로잡았다.\n\n\"좋아, 새미블, 네 마음에 더 들면 섭리라고 하자꾸나.\" 웰러 씨가 받아 말했다. \"난 제약이라고 부른다. 네 빈 병에 약을 공짜로 담아 주는 곳마다 늘 그렇게 써 붙여 놨거든. 그뿐이다.\"\n\n이렇게 말한 뒤 웰러 씨는 파이프에 다시 담배를 채워 불을 붙이고, 또 한 번 사색에 잠긴 표정을 불러일으키며 다음과 같이 말을 이었다.\n\n\"그러니까, 얘야, 내가 결혼할 마음이 있건 없건 여기 머물러 결혼이나 당하고 있는 게 현명한 일이라고는 보이지 않고, 그렇다고 동시에 그 흥미로운 사회 구성원들과 영영 인연을 끊고 싶지도 않으니, 나는 세이프티 마차를 몰고 다시 한 번 벨 새비지 여관에 묵기로 결심했단다. 그게 내 타고난 천성에 맞는 자리거든, 새미야.\"\n\n\"그럼 이 가게는 어떻게 되는 거예요?\" 샘이 물었다.\n\n\"가게는, 새미블,\" 노인이 대답했다. \"영업권이며 물건이며 비품이며 죄다 개인 간 매매로 팔 게다. 그래서 들어온 돈 중에서 이백 파운드는, 네 새어머이가 죽기 조금 전에 나한테 부탁한 대로, 네 이름으로—그 뭐라더라, 그게?\"\n\n\"그게 뭐요?\" 샘이 물었다.\n\n\"시내에서 늘 오르락내리락하는 그것 말이다.\"\n\n\"합승마차요?\" 샘이 넘겨짚었다.\n\n\"무슨 헛소리냐.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다. \"늘 등락을 거듭하고, 어찌어찌해서 국채니 재무 어음이니 하는 것들에 얽혀 들어가는 그것 말이다.\"\n\n\"아! 공채(公債)요.\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n\"옳지!\" 웰러 씨가 받아 말했다. \"공채지. 그 돈 중에서 이백 파운드를 네 이름으로 공채에 넣을 거다, 새미블. 사 푼 오 리짜리 감리 공채에 말이다, 새미야.\"\n\n\"저를 생각해 주시다니 새어머이도 참 인정 많은 분이셨네요.\" 샘이 말했다. \"정말 감사하지요.\"\n\n\"나머지는 내 이름으로 넣어 둘 거다.\" 웰러 영감이 말을 이었다. \"내가 마차 일에서 손을 떼면 그게 너한테로 갈 테니, 한꺼번에 다 써 버리지 않게 조심하고, 어떤 과부도 네 재산 낌새를 채지 못하게 명심해라, 안 그러면 넌 끝장이다.\"\n\n이 경고를 마치고 웰러 씨는 한결 평온해진 얼굴로 다시 파이프를 물었다. 이런 일들을 털어놓고 나니 마음이 한결 가벼워진 모양이었다.\n\n\"누가 문을 두드리는데요.\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n\"두드리라고 해라.\" 그의 아버지가 점잖게 대답했다.\n\n샘은 그 지시대로 했다. 또 한 번 두드리는 소리가 났고, 또 한 번, 그러고는 길게 줄지어 두드리는 소리가 났다. 그러자 샘이 어째서 두드리는 사람을 들이지 않느냐고 물었다.\n\n\"쉿,\" 웰러 씨가 겁먹은 표정으로 속삭였다. \"신경 쓰지 마라, 새미야. 아마 과부들 중 하나일 게다.\"\n\n두드리는 소리에 아무런 반응이 없자, 보이지 않던 방문객은 잠시 뒤 큰맘 먹고 문을 열어 안을 들여다보았다. 반쯤 열린 문틈으로 쑥 들이민 것은 여자의 머리가 아니라, 길고 검은 머리타래에 붉은 얼굴을 한 스티긴스 씨였다. 웰러 씨의 손에서 파이프가 떨어졌다.\n\n목사 양반은 자기 빼빼 마른 몸뚱이가 겨우 빠져나갈 만큼 틈이 벌어질 때까지 거의 알아챌 수 없을 만큼 조금씩 문을 열더니, 방 안으로 슬며시 미끄러져 들어와 무척이나 조심스럽고 부드럽게 등 뒤로 문을 닫았다. 샘 쪽으로 돌아서서, 이 집안에 닥친 재앙을 바라보는 말할 수 없는 슬픔의 표시로 두 손과 두 눈을 치켜든 그는, 등받이 높은 의자를 불가의 자기 옛 구석 자리로 옮겨다 놓고 그 맨 끝에 걸터앉아, 갈색 손수건을 꺼내 자기 두 눈에 갖다 댔다.\n\n이러는 동안 웰러 영감은 두 눈을 활짝 뜨고 두 손을 무릎에 짚은 채 의자에 등을 기대고 앉아 있었는데, 그 얼굴 전체에는 사로잡힐 듯 압도적인 경악이 어려 있었다. 샘은 그 맞은편에 완전한 침묵 속에 앉아, 이 장면이 어떻게 끝날지 호기심에 가득 차 기다렸다.\n\n스티긴스 씨는 몇 분 동안 갈색 손수건을 두 눈에 댄 채 그동안 점잖게 신음 소리를 내더니, 이윽고 안간힘을 써 감정을 다스리고는 손수건을 주머니에 넣고 단추를 채웠다. 그런 다음 그는 불을 쑤셨고, 그 다음에는 두 손을 비비며 샘을 바라보았다.\n\n\"오, 나의 젊은 친구여.\" 스티긴스 씨가 무척 낮은 목소리로 침묵을 깨며 말했다. \"이 무슨 슬픈 고난이란 말이오!\"\n\n샘이 아주 살짝 고개를 끄덕였다.\n\n\"게다가 저 분노의 사내에게도 말이오!\" 스티긴스 씨가 덧붙였다. \"그릇의 마음이 피를 흘릴 일이로다!\"\n\n웰러 씨가 그릇의 코에서 피가 나게 해 주겠다는 식의 무언가를 중얼거리는 것을 아들이 엿들었으나, 스티긴스 씨는 듣지 못했다.\n\n\"젊은이, 혹시 아시오?\" 스티긴스 씨가 의자를 샘 쪽으로 더 가까이 끌어당기며 속삭였다. \"그분이 에마누엘에게 무언가 남기셨는지?\"\n\n\"그게 누군데요?\" 샘이 물었다.\n\n\"예배당 말이오.\" 스티긴스 씨가 대답했다. \"우리 예배당, 우리 양 우리 말이오, 새뮤얼 씨.\"\n\n\"우리에도 아무것도 안 남겼고, 목자에게도 아무것도 안 남겼고, 짐승들에게도 아무것도 안 남겼어요.\" 샘이 단호하게 말했다. \"개들한테도요.\"\n\n스티긴스 씨는 샘을 슬쩍 곁눈질하고는, 잠든 듯 눈을 감고 앉아 있는 노인을 흘긋 보더니, 의자를 한층 더 가까이 끌어당기며 말했다.\n\n\"나한테는 아무것도 없소, 새뮤얼 씨?\"\n\n샘이 고개를 가로저었다.\n\n\"무언가 있을 텐데.\" 스티긴스가 핼쑥해질 수 있는 만큼 핼쑥해지며 말했다. \"잘 생각해 보시오, 새뮤얼 씨. 작은 정표 하나도 없소?\"\n\n\"그쪽 그 낡은 우산 값어치만큼도 없어요.\" 샘이 대답했다.\n\n\"어쩌면,\" 스티긴스 씨가 잠시 깊이 생각하더니 머뭇거리며 말했다. \"어쩌면 그분이 나를 저 분노의 사내가 돌봐 주기를 부탁하셨을지도 모르오, 새뮤얼 씨?\"\n\n\"그분이 한 말로 보아 그럴 가능성이 꽤 높아 보이네요.\" 샘이 받아 말했다. \"방금 그쪽 얘기를 하고 계셨거든요.\"\n\n\"정말이오?\" 스티긴스가 환해지며 외쳤다. \"아! 그분이 변하셨나 보오, 틀림없이. 이제 우리 둘이 아주 편안하게 함께 지낼 수도 있겠소, 새뮤얼 씨, 응? 당신이 떠나 있는 동안 내가 그분의 재산을 돌봐 드릴 수 있을 테고—잘 돌봐 드리는 거지, 알겠소?\"\n\n길게 끄는 한숨을 내쉬며 스티긴스 씨는 대답을 기다리며 말을 멈추었다. 샘이 고개를 끄덕이자, 웰러 영감이 기이한 소리를 내뱉었는데, 그것은 신음도 아니고, 끙 하는 소리도 아니고, 헐떡임도 아니고, 으르렁거림도 아니면서, 어느 정도는 그 네 가지 성질을 다 띤 듯한 소리였다.\n\n이 소리에 용기를 얻은 스티긴스 씨는, 그것이 회한이나 뉘우침을 나타내는 것이라 여기고는 주위를 둘러보고, 두 손을 비비고, 울고, 웃고, 다시 울더니, 살그머니 방을 가로질러 한구석의 익히 기억하던 선반으로 가서 텀블러 하나를 내려다가 무척 신중하게 그 안에 각설탕 네 덩이를 넣었다. 여기까지 한 다음 그는 다시 한 번 주위를 둘러보고 비통하게 한숨을 쉬었다. 그러고는 살며시 카운터로 걸어 들어갔다가 이내 텀블러에 파인애플 럼을 반쯤 채워 돌아와, 화로 선반에서 흥겹게 노래하던 주전자 쪽으로 다가가 자기 그로그주를 타고, 휘젓고, 한 모금 맛보고, 자리에 앉아, 럼과 물을 길고도 흡족하게 들이켠 뒤, 숨을 돌리려 멈추었다.\n\n여전히 잠든 척하려고 갖가지 기묘하고 어설픈 시도를 거듭하던 웰러 영감은 이 모든 과정 동안 단 한 마디도 하지 않았다. 그러나 스티긴스가 숨을 돌리려 멈추자, 그는 그에게 달려들어 손에서 텀블러를 낚아채 남은 럼과 물을 그의 얼굴에 끼얹고 잔 자체는 난로 속으로 내던졌다. 그러고는 목사 양반의 멱살을 단단히 움켜쥐고 별안간 무지막지하게 발길질을 퍼붓기 시작했으니, 그의 장화가 스티긴스 씨의 몸에 한 번 닿을 때마다 그의 사지며 두 눈이며 몸뚱이에 대고 갖은 사납고 두서없는 저주를 곁들였다.\n\n\"새미야,\" 웰러 씨가 말했다. \"내 모자 좀 단단히 씌워 다오.\"\n\n샘은 효성스럽게 긴 상장 띠가 달린 모자를 아버지의 머리에 한층 단단히 고쳐 씌웠고, 노인은 전보다 한층 민첩하게 발길질을 다시 시작하며 스티긴스 씨와 함께 카운터를 지나, 복도를 지나, 정문 밖으로, 그렇게 거리로 굴러나갔다—발길질은 그 내내 이어졌고, 장화를 들어 올릴 때마다 줄어들기는커녕 오히려 그 기세가 더해 갔다.\n\n발길질이 빠르게 잇따르는 가운데 빨간 코의 사내가 웰러 씨의 손아귀에서 몸부림치며 온몸을 고통에 떠는 광경은 아름답고도 흥겨운 구경거리였다. 한바탕 격렬한 몸싸움 끝에 웰러 씨가 스티긴스 씨의 머리를 물이 가득한 말구유에 처박고 그가 반쯤 질식할 때까지 거기 붙들고 있는 광경은 한층 더 짜릿한 장관이었다.\n\n\"옜다!\" 웰러 씨가 마침내 스티긴스 씨에게 구유에서 머리를 빼게 허락하며 가장 복잡한 발길질 한 방에 온 힘을 쏟아부으며 말했다. \"저 게으른 목자란 작자들 아무나 여기 보내 봐라, 먼저 곤죽이 되도록 두들겨 패고 나서 물에 처박아 죽여 버릴 테니! 새미야, 나 좀 부축해서 들여보내 주고, 브랜디 한 잔 작은 걸로 따라 다오. 숨이 차는구나, 얘야.\"\n\n아라벨라는 픽윅 씨가 부드러운 운을 떼고 풀 죽을 일이 조금도 없다고 거듭 다독인 끝에 그의 버밍엄 방문이 만족스럽지 못한 결과로 끝났다는 것을 마침내 알게 되자, 왈칵 눈물을 쏟으며 소리 내어 흐느꼈고, 자기가 아버지와 아들 사이를 갈라놓은 불행한 원인이 되었다며 가슴 아픈 말로 한탄했다.\n\n\"아가야,\" 픽윅 씨가 다정하게 말했다. \"그건 네 잘못이 아니다. 그 노신사가 아들의 결혼에 그토록 강하게 반대하고 있을 줄 누가 미리 알 수 있었겠느냐, 안 그러냐. 분명히 말하건대,\" 픽윅 씨는 그녀의 어여쁜 얼굴을 바라보며 덧붙였다. \"그는 자기가 스스로에게서 빼앗고 있는 기쁨이 어떤 것인지 거의 짐작도 못 하고 있을 게다.\"\n\n\"오, 픽윅 선생님,\" 아라벨라가 말했다. \"그분이 계속 저희에게 화를 내신다면 저희는 어떻게 해야 할까요?\"\n\n\"이런, 그분이 마음을 고쳐먹을 때까지 참고 기다리는 거지, 아가야.\" 픽윅 씨가 쾌활하게 대답했다.\n\n\"하지만 픽윅 선생님, 시아버님께서 도움을 거두신다면 너새니얼은 어떻게 되는 거죠?\" 아라벨라가 채근했다.\n\n\"그렇게 된다면, 얘야,\" 픽윅 씨가 받아 말했다. \"감히 예언하건대, 그를 도와 세상에 첫발을 내딛게 하는 데 주저하지 않을 다른 친구를 찾게 될 게다.\"\n\n이 대답에 담긴 뜻을 픽윅 씨가 그리 잘 감추지는 못한 터라 아라벨라는 그것을 알아차렸다. 그래서 그녀는 두 팔로 그의 목을 끌어안고 다정하게 입을 맞추며 전보다 더 크게 흐느꼈다.\n\n\"자, 자,\" 픽윅 씨가 그녀의 손을 잡으며 말했다. \"여기서 며칠만 더 기다려 보자꾸나. 그분이 편지를 쓰는지, 아니면 네 남편이 보낸 소식에 어떤 다른 응답이라도 하는지 두고 보자. 그렇지 않으면, 너를 당장 행복하게 해 줄 방법을 여섯 가지쯤 생각해 두었으니 그중 어느 것이든 쓰면 된다. 자, 아가야, 자!\"\n\n이렇게 말하며 픽윅 씨는 아라벨라의 손을 가만히 쥐었고, 눈물을 닦고 남편을 괴롭히지 말라고 일렀다. 그러자 세상에서 가장 사랑스러운 작은 사람 중 하나인 아라벨라는 손수건을 손가방에 넣었고, 윈클 씨가 그들에게 합류했을 무렵에는 애초에 그를 사로잡았던 바로 그 환한 미소와 반짝이는 눈빛을 온전한 광채로 드러내 보였다.\n\n\"이 젊은이들에게는 참으로 난처한 곤경이로구나.\" 이튿날 아침 옷을 차려입으며 픽윅 씨는 생각했다. \"퍼커한테 가서 이 일을 상의해 봐야겠다.\"\n\n게다가 픽윅 씨는 그 마음씨 고운 작은 변호사와 더 미루지 않고 금전 문제를 정산하고 싶다는 조바심에 더욱 그레이즈 인 광장으로 발길을 재촉하게 되어, 아침을 서둘러 먹고는 그 작정을 어찌나 빨리 실행에 옮겼던지, 그레이즈 인에 도착했을 때는 아직 열 시도 치지 않았다.\n\n퍼커의 사무실이 있는 계단을 다 올라갔을 때만 해도 아직 정각까지 십 분이 남아 있었다. 사무원들은 아직 도착하지 않았고, 그는 계단 창밖을 내다보며 시간을 보냈다.\n\n화창한 시월 아침의 건강한 햇살은 칙칙하고 낡은 집들조차 조금은 환하게 밝혀 주었다. 먼지 낀 창문 몇몇은 햇빛이 그 위에서 반짝일 때 정말이지 거의 명랑해 보일 정도였다. 사무원들이 잇따라 이쪽저쪽 입구로 광장에 황급히 들어서서는 회관 시계를 올려다보며, 명목상 자기 근무 시간이 시작되는 때에 맞추어 걸음을 빨리하기도 하고 늦추기도 했다. 아홉 시 반인 사람들은 갑자기 무척 부산해졌고, 열 시인 신사들은 더없이 귀족적인 느릿함의 보조로 접어들었다. 시계가 열 시를 쳤고, 사무원들은 그 어느 때보다 빠르게 밀려들었는데, 저마다 앞사람보다 더 땀을 뻘뻘 흘리고 있었다. 문을 따고 여는 소리가 사방에서 메아리치고 또 메아리쳤다. 마치 마법이라도 부린 듯 창마다 사람 머리가 나타났다. 수위들은 그날의 자기 자리를 잡았고, 슬리퍼를 질질 끄는 세탁부들은 부랴부랴 떠났으며, 우편배달부는 이 집 저 집 뛰어다녔다. 법조계라는 벌집 전체가 분주했다.\n\n\"일찍 오셨군요, 픽윅 선생님.\" 그의 등 뒤에서 한 목소리가 말했다.\n\n\"아, 로튼 씨.\" 그 신사는 돌아보며 옛 지인을 알아보고 대답했다.\n\n\"걷기에 무지하게 더운 날씨죠, 안 그래요?\" 로튼은 먼지가 들어가지 않게 작은 마개를 끼운 브라마 열쇠를 주머니에서 꺼내며 말했다.\n\n\"그렇게 느끼시는 모양이군요.\" 픽윅 씨는 말 그대로 벌겋게 달아오른 사무원을 보고 미소 지으며 받아 말했다.\n\n\"꽤나 빨리 걸어왔거든요, 정말이지.\" 로튼이 대답했다. \"폴리곤을 지나올 때 반 시를 쳤어요. 그래도 제가 그분보다 먼저 와 있으니 상관없죠.\"\n\n이런 생각으로 스스로를 위안하며 로튼 씨는 문 열쇠에서 마개를 빼냈다. 문을 열고 나서 브라마 열쇠에 다시 마개를 끼워 주머니에 넣은 다음, 우편배달부가 우편함 구멍으로 떨어뜨린 편지들을 집어 들고는 픽윅 씨를 사무실로 안내했다. 여기서 그는 눈 깜짝할 사이에 외투를 벗고, 책상에서 꺼낸 올이 다 드러난 옷을 걸치고, 모자를 걸고, 두꺼운 종이와 압지를 번갈아 겹친 몇 장을 끄집어내고는, 펜 한 자루를 귀 뒤에 꽂은 채 매우 흡족한 기색으로 두 손을 비볐다.\n\n\"자, 보세요, 픽윅 선생님.\" 그가 말했다. \"이제 만반의 준비가 됐습니다. 사무실 외투도 걸쳤고, 받침판도 꺼내 놨으니, 그분이 오고 싶을 때 오시면 되죠. 혹시 코담배 한 줌 가지고 계시진 않죠?\"\n\n\"아니, 없는데요.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"그것 참 아쉽군요.\" 로튼이 말했다. \"괜찮습니다. 이따 잠깐 나가서 소다수나 한 병 사 오죠. 저, 제 눈 좀 이상해 보이지 않나요, 픽윅 선생님?\"\n\n질문을 받은 당사자는 멀찍이서 로튼 씨의 두 눈을 살펴보고는, 그 이목구비에 별다른 이상은 보이지 않는다는 의견을 밝혔다.\n\n\"그것 다행이네요.\" 로튼이 말했다. \"어젯밤 스텀프에서 꽤나 흥청거렸거든요. 그래서 오늘 아침엔 컨디션이 영 별로예요. 그건 그렇고, 퍼커 씨가 선생님 그 일을 봐 주셨답니다.\"\n\n\"무슨 일이요?\" 픽윅 씨가 물었다. \"바델 부인의 소송 비용 말입니까?\"\n\n\"아니요, 그게 아니라.\" 로튼 씨가 대답했다. \"선생님 부탁으로 어음 할인업자한테 파운드당 십 실링을 쳐서 빚을 갚아 준 그 손님 말입니다. 그를 플리트에서 빼내 주는 일이요, 아시죠. 그를 데메라라로 보내는 그 일 말입니다.\"\n\n\"아, 징글 씨요.\" 픽윅 씨가 다급히 말했다. \"그래요. 그래서요?\"\n\n\"그래서, 다 정리됐습니다.\" 로튼이 펜촉을 다듬으며 말했다. \"리버풀 대리인이 말하길, 선생님이 사업하실 때 여러 번 신세를 졌으니 선생님 추천이라면 기꺼이 그를 받아들이겠답니다.\"\n\n\"그것 잘됐군요.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"그 소식을 들으니 기쁩니다.\"\n\n\"근데 말이죠,\" 로튼이 펜촉을 새로 가르려고 뒷면을 긁으며 말을 이었다. \"그 다른 친구는 정말 물러 터진 작자더군요!\"\n\n\"어느 다른 친구요?\"\n\n\"아니, 그 하인인지 친구인지 뭔지 하는 사람 말입니다. 아시잖아요, 트로터요.\"\n\n\"아!\" 픽윅 씨가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"나는 늘 그를 그 반대라고 생각했는데요.\"\n\n\"글쎄요, 저도 잠깐 본 바로는 그랬는데요.\" 로튼이 대답했다. \"사람이 얼마나 속을 수 있는지를 보여 줄 뿐이죠. 글쎄, 그 사람도 데메라라로 간다는 거 어떻게 생각하세요?\"\n\n\"뭐라고요! 여기서 제안받은 자리를 마다하고 말입니까!\" 픽윅 씨가 외쳤다.\n\n\"퍼커 씨가 주당 십팔 실링에, 처신만 잘하면 올려 주겠다고 한 제안을 똥처럼 취급했지 뭡니까.\" 로튼이 대답했다. \"그가 말하길 그 다른 사람과 꼭 함께 가야겠다는 겁니다. 그래서 그들이 퍼커 씨를 졸라 다시 편지를 쓰게 했고, 같은 농장에 그 사람 일자리를 하나 마련해 주긴 했는데, 퍼커 씨 말로는 죄수가 새 옷을 차려입고 재판에 나타나서 뉴사우스웨일스에서 얻는 자리만도 훨씬 못한 거랍니다.\"\n\n\"어리석은 친구로군.\" 픽윅 씨가 눈을 반짝이며 말했다. \"어리석은 친구야.\"\n\n\"오, 어리석은 정도가 아니죠. 그건 정말 비굴한 짓이라니까요, 아시죠.\" 로튼이 경멸 어린 표정으로 펜촉을 다듬으며 대답했다. \"그 사람이 자기한테 있었던 유일한 친구라느니, 정이 들었다느니 어쩌고 하더군요. 우정이란 게 그 나름으로는 아주 좋은 거죠. 이를테면 우리도 스텀프에서 그로그주를 앞에 놓고 다들 우애 좋고 편안하게 지냅니다. 거기선 다들 제 몫은 제가 내지만 말이죠. 하지만 빌어먹을, 남을 위해 자기를 해치다니, 원! 사람이라면 애착은 두 가지 이상 두면 안 되는 겁니다. 첫째는 제일인자, 곧 자기 자신한테, 둘째는 숙녀분들한테. 제 말이 그겁니다. 하하!\" 로튼 씨는 반은 익살, 반은 조롱이 섞인 큰 웃음으로 말을 맺었는데, 그 웃음은 계단을 오르는 퍼커의 발소리에 미처 끝나기도 전에 뚝 끊겼다. 그 소리가 처음 다가오자마자 그는 놀랄 만큼 날렵하게 의자 위로 훌쩍 올라앉아 맹렬히 글을 쓰기 시작했다.\n\n픽윅 씨와 그의 법률 고문 사이의 인사는 따뜻하고 정겨웠다. 그러나 의뢰인이 변호사의 안락의자에 채 자리 잡기도 전에 문을 두드리는 소리가 들리더니, 퍼커 씨가 안에 계시냐고 묻는 목소리가 났다.\n\n\"들어 보세요!\" 퍼커가 말했다. \"우리의 그 떠돌이 친구로군요. 바로 징글입니다, 선생님. 만나 보시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"어떻게 생각하세요?\" 픽윅 씨가 머뭇거리며 물었다.\n\n\"네, 만나 보시는 게 좋겠어요. 거기, 선생, 이름이 뭐든 간에, 들어오시겠소?\"\n\n이 격식 없는 초대에 따라 징글과 잡이 방으로 걸어 들어왔으나, 픽윅 씨를 보고는 다소 당황하여 우뚝 멈춰 섰다.\n\n\"이런,\" 퍼커가 말했다. \"저 신사분을 모르시오?\"\n\n\"잘 알다마다요.\" 징글이 앞으로 나서며 대답했다. \"픽윅 씨, 더없는 은혜를, 생명의 은인을, 저를 사람으로 만들어 주신 분을, 절대 후회하실 일 없게 하겠습니다, 선생님.\"\n\n\"그렇게 말해 주니 기쁘군요.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"한결 좋아 보입니다.\"\n\n\"선생님 덕분이지요. 엄청난 변화입니다. 폐하의 플리트, 건강에 안 좋은 곳, 아주.\" 징글이 고개를 저으며 말했다. 그는 단정하고 깔끔하게 차려입고 있었고, 그의 뒤에 꼿꼿이 서서 쇠처럼 굳은 얼굴로 픽윅 씨를 응시하고 있는 잡도 마찬가지였다.\n\n\"저들이 리버풀로 떠나는 게 언제죠?\" 픽윅 씨가 퍼커에게 반쯤 곁눈질하며 물었다.\n\n\"오늘 저녁입니다, 선생님. 일곱 시에요.\" 잡이 한 걸음 앞으로 나서며 말했다. \"시내에서 출발하는 짐마차 편으로요, 선생님.\"\n\n\"좌석은 예약했고?\"\n\n\"네, 했습니다, 선생님.\" 잡이 대답했다.\n\n\"떠나기로 완전히 마음을 굳혔나?\"\n\n\"네, 그렇습니다, 선생님.\" 잡이 답했다.\n\n\"징글에게 꼭 필요한 채비 말씀인데요,\" 퍼커가 픽윅 씨에게 큰 소리로 말했다. \"제가 나서서 그의 분기별 봉급에서 소액을 공제하도록 조처를 해 두었습니다. 단 일 년만 떼고 꼬박꼬박 송금하면 그 비용을 충당할 수 있습니다. 그의 노력과 선행에 달려 있지 않은 일은 그를 위해 무엇 하나라도 하시는 것을 저는 전적으로 반대합니다, 선생님.\"\n\n\"물론입니다.\" 징글이 아주 단호하게 끼어들었다. \"명석한 머리, 세상 물정에 밝은 분, 지당하신 말씀, 완벽합니다.\"\n\n\"채권자와 화해하고, 전당포에서 그의 옷을 되찾고, 감옥에서 그를 구해 내고, 뱃삯까지 치르느라,\" 퍼커가 징글의 말에 아랑곳없이 계속했다. \"선생님은 이미 오십 파운드 넘게 잃으셨습니다.\"\n\n\"잃은 게 아니죠.\" 징글이 다급히 말했다. \"전부 갚습니다, 사업에 매진해서, 현금으로 갚습니다, 한 푼도 빠짐없이. 황열병에 걸릴지도 모르지만, 그건 어쩔 수 없죠, 만약 안 걸리면.\" 여기서 징글 씨는 말을 멈추고, 모자 꼭대기를 세게 후려치고는, 손으로 두 눈을 훔치고 자리에 앉았다.\n\n\"저 사람 말은요,\" 잡이 몇 걸음 다가서며 말했다. \"열병으로 죽지만 않으면 그 돈을 도로 갚겠다는 겁니다. 살아만 있으면 갚을 겁니다, 픽윅 씨. 제가 반드시 그렇게 되도록 지켜보겠습니다. 저는 그가 갚으리란 걸 압니다, 선생님.\" 잡이 힘주어 말했다. \"맹세코 장담할 수 있습니다.\"\n\n\"좋네, 좋아.\" 픽윅 씨는 그동안 베푼 은혜를 일일이 요약하는 것을 그만두라고 퍼커에게 스무 번도 넘게 눈살을 찌푸려 보였으나 그 작은 변호사가 고집스레 무시한 터라 이렇게 말했다. \"이제 다시는 그런 무모한 크리켓 시합을 하거나 토머스 블레이조 경과 다시 사귀는 일은 조심해야 하네, 징글 씨. 그러면 건강을 지키는 데 별 어려움은 없으리라 보네.\"\n\n징글 씨는 이 농담에 미소를 지었으나, 그래도 다소 멋쩍은 표정이었다. 그래서 픽윅 씨는 화제를 바꾸어 말했다.\n\n\"혹시 자네의 또 다른 친구, 좀 더 처지가 딱한 친구 말인데, 내가 로체스터에서 본 그 사람이 어떻게 되었는지 알고 있나?\"\n\n\"음울한 제미 말입니까?\" 징글이 물었다.\n\n\"그래.\"\n\n징글이 고개를 저었다.\n\n\"영리한 악당, 별난 녀석, 사람 골리는 천재, 잡의 형입니다.\"\n\n\"잡의 형!\" 픽윅 씨가 외쳤다. \"허, 이제 자세히 보니 정말 닮은 데가 있군.\"\n\n\"저희는 늘 서로 닮았다는 말을 들었습니다, 선생님.\" 잡이 눈가에 살짝 교활한 기색을 드리운 채 말했다. \"다만 저는 정말로 진지한 천성이었고 그는 한 번도 그런 적이 없었지요. 그는 여기서 너무 많이 쫓겨 다녀 편할 수가 없게 된 통에 미국으로 이민을 갔고, 그 뒤로는 소식이 끊겼습니다, 선생님.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 그가 로체스터 다리에서 자살을 궁리하는 듯 보이던 어느 아침에 내게 약속했던 '실제 인생이라는 소설의 한 페이지'를 내가 끝내 받지 못한 까닭이 설명되는군.\" 픽윅 씨가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"그의 음울한 행동이 천성이었는지 꾸민 것이었는지 굳이 물어볼 필요는 없겠어.\"\n\n\"그는 무엇이든 꾸며 낼 수 있었습니다, 선생님.\" 잡이 말했다. \"그를 그토록 쉽게 피하신 것을 큰 행운으로 여기셔도 됩니다. 가까이 지냈더라면 그는 저보다도 더 위험한 지인이 되었을 겁니다.\" 잡은 징글을 바라보며 머뭇거리다가, 마침내 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"저보다도, 저보다도 말입니다.\"\n\n\"가망 넘치는 집안이로군요, 트로터 씨.\" 퍼커가 막 다 쓴 편지를 봉하며 말했다.\n\n\"네, 그렇습니다, 선생님.\" 잡이 대답했다. \"매우 그렇지요.\"\n\n\"흠,\" 작은 남자가 웃으며 말했다. \"당신이 그 집안의 명예를 더럽혀 주기를 바라겠소. 리버풀에 도착하면 이 편지를 대리인에게 전하시오. 그리고 두 분에게 충고하건대, 서인도제도에서 너무 약게 굴지 마시오. 이 기회를 내던진다면 두 분 다 교수형감이 마땅하니, 진심으로 그리되기를 바라오. 자, 이제 픽윅 씨와 나만 있게 해 주는 게 좋겠소. 우린 의논할 다른 일이 있고 시간이 귀하니 말이오.\" 퍼커는 이렇게 말하며, 작별을 되도록 짧게 끝내고 싶다는 기색이 역력한 채 문 쪽을 바라보았다.\n\n징글 씨 쪽에서는 작별이 충분히 짧았다. 그는 도움을 베풀어 준 친절과 신속함에 대해 몇 마디 다급한 말로 작은 변호사에게 감사하고는, 자기 은인 쪽으로 돌아서서, 무슨 말을 해야 할지 어떻게 행동해야 할지 결정하지 못한 듯 잠시 서 있었다. 잡 트로터가 그의 난처함을 덜어 주었으니, 픽윅 씨에게 겸손하고 감사에 찬 절을 올리고는 친구의 팔을 부드럽게 잡아 데리고 나갔다.\n\n\"훌륭한 한 쌍이로군요!\" 그들 뒤로 문이 닫히자 퍼커가 말했다.\n\n\"그들이 그리되기를 바랍니다.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다. \"어떻게 생각하세요? 저들이 영영 개과천선할 가망이 있을까요?\"\n\n퍼커는 미심쩍은 듯 어깨를 으쓱했으나, 픽윅 씨의 근심스럽고 실망한 표정을 보고는 이렇게 받아 말했다.\n\n\"물론 가망은 있지요. 좋은 쪽으로 판명되기를 바랍니다. 저들은 지금 틀림없이 뉘우치고 있습니다. 하지만 아시다시피, 저들에게는 아주 최근에 겪은 고난의 기억이 생생하게 남아 있으니까요. 그 기억이 희미해질 때 저들이 어떻게 될지는 선생님도 저도 풀 수 없는 문제입니다. 하지만 선생님,\" 퍼커가 픽윅 씨의 어깨에 손을 얹으며 덧붙였다. \"결과야 어떻든 선생님의 의도는 한결같이 훌륭합니다. 너무도 신중하고 멀리 내다보는 나머지, 베푸는 이가 속아 넘어가 자존심에 상처를 입을까 봐 좀처럼 실행되지 않는 그런 종류의 자선이 참된 자비인지 아니면 세속적인 가짜인지는, 저보다 더 현명한 사람들의 판단에 맡기겠습니다. 하지만 저 두 친구가 내일 강도질을 저지른다 해도, 이 행위에 대한 제 평가는 변함없이 높을 것입니다.\"\n\n법조계 신사들에게서 흔히 볼 수 있는 것보다 훨씬 더 활기차고 진지한 태도로 전해진 이런 말과 함께, 퍼커는 의자를 책상으로 끌어당기고는 늙은 윈클 씨의 고집에 관한 픽윅 씨의 이야기에 귀를 기울였다.\n\n\"일주일 말미를 주세요.\" 퍼커가 예언하듯 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다.\n\n\"그가 마음을 돌릴 거라고 생각하세요?\" 픽윅 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"그러리라 봅니다.\" 퍼커가 받아 말했다. \"그렇지 않으면 그 젊은 숙녀의 설득을 시도해야지요. 선생님이 아닌 다른 사람이라면 누구든 처음부터 그렇게 했을 일입니다.\"\n\n퍼커 씨가 젊은 숙녀들에게 딸린 설득력을 찬미하느라 갖가지 기괴하게 얼굴을 찡그려 가며 코담배를 한 줌 들이마시고 있을 때, 바깥 사무실에서 묻고 답하는 웅성거림이 들리더니 로튼이 문을 두드렸다.\n\n\"들어오게!\" 작은 남자가 외쳤다.\n\n사무원이 들어와 무척 비밀스러운 태도로 등 뒤의 문을 닫았다.\n\n\"무슨 일인가?\" 퍼커가 물었다.\n\n\"손님이 찾으십니다, 선생님.\"\n\n\"누가 날 찾는데?\"\n\n로튼이 픽윅 씨를 흘끗 보고는 기침을 했다.\n\n\"누가 날 찾느냐니까? 말을 못 하겠나, 로튼 씨?\"\n\n\"저, 선생님,\" 로튼이 대답했다. \"도드슨입니다. 포그도 함께 와 있고요.\"\n\n\"이런 맙소사!\" 작은 남자가 회중시계를 보며 말했다. \"그자들한테 열한 시 반에 여기로 오라고 약속해 두었지. 자네 그 일을 매듭짓기로 말일세, 픽윅. 내가 각서를 한 장 써 주니 그자들이 자네 석방 서류를 내려보냈거든. 참 거북하게 됐군요, 선생님. 어떻게 하시겠습니까? 옆방으로 가 계시겠어요?\"\n\n그 옆방이라는 게 바로 도드슨 씨와 포그 씨가 있는 그 방인지라, 픽윅 씨는 자기가 있던 자리에 그대로 있겠다고 대답했다. 더구나 자기가 그들을 보기 부끄러워할 게 아니라, 도드슨 씨와 포그 씨야말로 자기 얼굴을 똑바로 보기 부끄러워해야 마땅하다는 것이었다. 픽윅 씨는 이 후자의 사정을 잘 새겨 두라고, 벌겋게 달아오른 얼굴로 분개의 기색을 잔뜩 드러내며 퍼커 씨에게 당부했다.\n\n\"좋습니다, 선생님, 아주 좋아요.\" 퍼커가 대답했다. \"다만 한 가지 말씀드릴 수 있는 건, 도드슨이든 포그든 선생님이나 그 누구의 얼굴을 마주하면서 부끄러움이나 당혹스러움의 기미라도 보이리라 기대하신다면, 제가 만나 본 사람 중 가장 낙천적인 분이시라는 겁니다. 모셔 들이게, 로튼 씨.\"\n\n로튼 씨는 씩 웃으며 사라졌다가, 곧 격식에 따른 순서대로 그 회사를 안내해 들어왔으니, 도드슨이 먼저요 포그가 그 뒤였다.\n\n\"픽윅 씨를 만나 보셨지요?\" 퍼커가 그 신사가 앉아 있는 방향으로 펜을 기울이며 도드슨에게 말했다.\n\n\"안녕하십니까, 픽윅 씨?\" 도드슨이 큰 소리로 말했다.\n\n\"이런, 세상에,\" 포그가 외쳤다. \"안녕하십니까, 픽윅 씨? 평안하시기를 바랍니다, 선생님. 어쩐지 낯이 익다 했지요.\" 포그는 의자를 끌어당기고 미소를 지으며 주위를 둘러보며 말했다.\n\n픽윅 씨는 이 인사에 답하여 고개를 아주 살짝 숙였을 뿐, 포그가 외투 주머니에서 서류 뭉치를 꺼내는 것을 보고는 일어나 창가로 걸어갔다.\n\n\"픽윅 씨께서 자리를 옮기실 필요는 없습니다, 퍼커 씨.\" 포그가 그 작은 뭉치를 두른 빨간 끈을 풀고 전보다 더 달콤하게 다시 미소 지으며 말했다. \"픽윅 씨께서는 이 절차를 꽤 잘 아십니다. 우리 사이엔 비밀이랄 게 없잖습니까. 헤! 헤! 헤!\"\n\n\"별로 없지요.\" 도드슨이 말했다. \"하하하!\" 그러고 나서 두 동업자는 함께 웃었으니, 돈을 받으러 가는 사람들이 흔히 그러하듯 유쾌하고 명랑하게 웃었다.\n\n\"엿본 값은 픽윅 씨께서 치르시도록 해야겠군요.\" 포그가 서류를 펼치며 제법 타고난 익살을 부려 말했다. \"사정된 소송 비용 총액은 백삼십삼 파운드 육 실링 사 펜스입니다, 퍼커 씨.\"\n\n이 손익 명세가 나온 뒤로 포그와 퍼커는 서류를 대조하고 책장을 넘기느라 한참을 보냈다. 그러는 동안 도드슨이 픽윅 씨에게 상냥한 투로 말을 걸었다.\n\n\"지난번 뵙는 기쁨을 누렸을 때만큼 건장해 보이시진 않는 것 같군요, 픽윅 씨.\"\n\n\"아마 그렇겠죠, 선생님.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다. 그는 그 두 약삭빠른 모사꾼 어느 쪽에도 손톱만큼의 효과조차 내지 못한 채 사나운 분개의 눈길을 번뜩이고 있던 참이었다. \"그렇지 못할 겁니다, 선생님. 요즘 들어 악당들에게 시달리고 시끄럽게 부대껴 왔으니까요, 선생님.\"\n\n퍼커가 심하게 기침을 하고는 픽윅 씨에게 조간신문이라도 보시지 않겠냐고 물었다. 그 질문에 픽윅 씨는 더없이 단호하게 싫다고 답했다.\n\n\"맞습니다,\" 도드슨이 말했다. \"플리트에서 시달리셨겠지요. 거긴 별난 작자들이 좀 있으니까요. 선생님 방은 어디쯤이었습니까, 픽윅 씨?\"\n\n\"내 방은,\" 그 크게 상처 입은 신사가 대답했다. \"휴게실 계단 쪽에 있었소.\"\n\n\"오, 그래요!\" 도드슨이 말했다. \"거긴 그 시설에서 아주 쾌적한 구역이라던데요.\"\n\n\"매우 그렇지요.\" 픽윅 씨가 무뚝뚝하게 대답했다.\n\n이 모든 일에는 일종의 냉랭함이 감돌았으니, 흥분하기 쉬운 기질의 신사에게는 이런 상황에서 다분히 화를 돋우는 데가 있었다. 픽윅 씨는 어마어마한 노력으로 분노를 억눌렀다. 그러나 퍼커가 전액에 해당하는 수표를 써 주고, 포그가 여드름투성이 얼굴에 의기양양한 미소를 띤 채—그 미소는 도드슨의 근엄한 얼굴에도 옮아갔다—그것을 작은 수첩에 챙겨 넣자, 그는 두 뺨의 피가 분노로 따끔거리는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n\"자, 도드슨 씨,\" 포그가 수첩을 집어넣고 장갑을 끼며 말했다. \"준비됐습니다.\"\n\n\"좋아요,\" 도드슨이 일어서며 말했다. \"나도 다 됐습니다.\"\n\n\"참으로 기쁩니다,\" 수표로 마음이 누그러진 포그가 말했다. \"픽윅 씨와 알게 되는 기쁨을 누리게 되어서요. 처음 뵙는 기쁨을 누렸을 때만큼 저희를 나쁘게 여기시진 않으시기를 바랍니다, 픽윅 씨.\"\n\n\"그러시지 않기를 바랍니다.\" 도드슨이 중상모략당한 미덕의 고상한 어조로 말했다. \"픽윅 씨께서 이제 저희를 더 잘 아시게 되었으리라 믿습니다. 저희 직업에 종사하는 사람들을 어떻게 여기시든, 제 동업자가 언급한 그 일이 있던 날 콘힐 프리먼스 코트의 저희 사무실에서 선생님께서 표하시기에 합당하다 여기신 그 견해에 대해, 저는 선생님께 아무런 악의나 앙심도 품고 있지 않음을 분명히 말씀드립니다.\"\n\n\"오, 그럼요, 그럼요. 저도 마찬가지입니다.\" 포그가 더없이 너그러운 투로 말했다.\n\n\"저희의 행실은, 선생님,\" 도드슨이 말했다. \"어떤 경우에든 스스로 말해 주고 스스로를 정당화하리라 믿습니다. 저희는 이 직업에 몇 해를 몸담아 왔습니다, 픽윅 씨. 그리고 수많은 훌륭한 의뢰인들의 신임을 받는 영예를 누려 왔지요. 그럼 안녕히 계십시오, 선생님.\"\n\n\"안녕히 계십시오, 픽윅 씨.\" 포그가 말했다. 이렇게 말하며 그는 우산을 겨드랑이에 끼고, 오른쪽 장갑을 벗어, 그 더없이 분개한 신사에게 화해의 손을 내밀었다. 그러자 그 신사는 두 손을 외투 자락 밑으로 쑤셔 넣고는 경멸 어린 경악의 눈초리로 그 변호사를 노려보았다.\n\n\"로튼!\" 바로 이때 퍼커가 외쳤다. \"문 열어 드리게.\"\n\n\"잠깐만 기다리세요.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"퍼커, 나는 한마디 해야겠소.\"\n\n\"선생님, 부디 그냥 이쯤에서 덮어 두십시오.\" 면담 내내 안절부절못하던 작은 변호사가 말했다. \"픽윅 씨, 제발—\"\n\n\"나는 입 다물고 있지 않겠소, 선생.\" 픽윅 씨가 급히 대꾸했다. \"도드슨 씨, 당신은 내게 몇 마디 말을 건넸소.\"\n\n도드슨이 돌아서서 온순하게 고개를 숙이고는 미소 지었다.\n\n\"내게 몇 마디 말을 건넸소.\" 픽윅 씨가 거의 숨이 차도록 되풀이했다. \"그리고 당신 동업자는 내게 손을 내밀었고, 두 사람 다 용서와 고결함의 어조를 취했으니, 그것은 당신네한테서조차 미처 예상치 못한 정도의 뻔뻔함이오.\"\n\n\"뭐라고요, 선생!\" 도드슨이 외쳤다.\n\n\"뭐라고요, 선생!\" 포그가 따라 외쳤다.\n\n\"내가 당신네 음모와 계략의 희생자였다는 걸 알기나 하오?\" 픽윅 씨가 계속했다. \"내가 바로 당신네가 가두고 등쳐 먹은 그 사람이라는 걸 아오? 당신네가 바델 대 픽윅 사건에서 원고 측 변호인이었다는 걸 아오?\"\n\n\"네, 선생, 알고 있습니다.\" 도드슨이 대답했다.\n\n\"물론 알지요, 선생.\" 포그가 받아 말하며 자기 주머니를 툭 쳤으니, 어쩌면 무심결이었으리라.\n\n\"당신네가 그걸 흡족하게 기억하고 있다는 게 보이는군.\" 픽윅 씨가 평생 처음으로 비웃음을 지어 보려 애썼으나 더없이 명백하게 실패하며 말했다. \"당신네에 대한 내 생각이 어떤지를 단도직입적으로 말해 주고 싶은 마음이 오래전부터 굴뚝같았으나, 친구 퍼커의 뜻을 존중하여 이 기회마저도 그냥 지나쳤을 거요. 당신네가 취한 그 가당찮은 어조와 그 무례한 허물없음만 아니었다면 말이오. 무례한 허물없음이라고 했소, 선생.\" 픽윅 씨가 어찌나 사납게 몸짓하며 포그 쪽으로 돌아섰던지, 그자는 황급히 문 쪽으로 물러났다.\n\n\"조심하시오, 선생.\" 도드슨이 말했다. 그는 일행 중 가장 덩치가 컸으나 슬그머니 포그 뒤로 몸을 사리고는, 새파랗게 질린 얼굴로 그의 머리 너머로 말하고 있었다. \"그가 당신을 폭행하게 두시오, 포그 씨. 무슨 일이 있어도 되받아치지는 마시오.\"\n\n\"네, 네, 되받아치지 않겠습니다.\" 포그가 말하면서 조금 더 뒤로 물러났으니, 그 덕에 차츰 바깥 사무실로 빠져나가게 된 동업자는 안도하는 기색이 역력했다.\n\n\"당신네는,\" 픽윅 씨가 다시 말의 실마리를 이어 갔다. \"당신네는 천하고 악랄하며 비열한 짓을 일삼는 강도, 잘 어울리는 한 쌍이오.\"\n\n\"자,\" 퍼커가 끼어들었다. \"그게 전부입니까?\"\n\n\"그 한마디에 다 들어 있소.\" 픽윅 씨가 받아 말했다. \"저들은 천하고 악랄하며 비열한 짓을 일삼는 강도들이오.\"\n\n\"자, 됐습니다!\" 퍼커가 더없이 달래는 투로 말했다. \"선생님들, 이분이 하실 말씀은 다 하셨습니다. 이제 어서 가시지요. 로튼, 그 문 열려 있나?\"\n\n로튼 씨가 멀찍이서 킬킬거리며 그렇다고 대답했다.\n\n\"자, 자, 안녕히 가십시오, 안녕히, 자 부디, 선생님들, 로튼 씨, 문!\" 작은 남자가 마다하지 않는 도드슨과 포그를 사무실 밖으로 떠밀며 외쳤다. \"이쪽입니다, 선생님들, 부디 이 일을 더 길게 끌지 마시고, 이런 참, 로튼 씨, 문 말이오, 선생, 왜 거기 신경을 안 쓰나?\"\n\n\"영국에 법이라는 게 있다면, 선생,\" 도드슨이 모자를 쓰며 픽윅 씨 쪽을 바라보고 말했다. \"당신은 이 일로 호되게 대가를 치를 거요.\"\n\n\"당신네는 천하고—\"\n\n\"기억하시오, 선생, 이 값을 비싸게 치르게 될 거요.\" 포그가 말했다.\n\n\"악랄하며 비열한 짓을 일삼는 강도들이오!\" 픽윅 씨가 자기에게 던져진 위협에는 눈곱만큼도 아랑곳하지 않고 계속했다.\n\n\"강도들!\" 픽윅 씨가 두 변호사가 내려가는 동안 계단 꼭대기로 달려가며 외쳤다.\n\n\"강도들!\" 픽윅 씨가 로튼과 퍼커를 뿌리치고 계단 창밖으로 머리를 내밀며 고함쳤다.\n\n픽윅 씨가 다시 머리를 들여놓았을 때, 그의 얼굴은 미소 띤 평온한 표정이었다. 그리고 조용히 사무실로 걸어 들어와, 이제 마음에서 큰 짐을 덜어 냈으며, 더없이 후련하고 행복하다고 선언했다.\n\n퍼커는 코담배갑을 다 비우고 로튼을 내보내 채워 오게 할 때까지 아무 말도 하지 않다가, 별안간 웃음보가 터져 오 분 동안이나 웃어 댔다. 그 시간이 다하자 그는 자기가 무척 화를 내야 마땅하겠지만 아직은 그 일을 진지하게 생각할 수가 없다고, 진지하게 생각할 수 있게 되면 그러겠노라고 말했다.\n\n\"자, 그럼,\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"당신과 정산을 좀 합시다.\"\n\n\"방금 그것과 같은 종류로요?\" 퍼커가 또 한 번 웃으며 물었다.\n\n\"그건 아니고요.\" 픽윅 씨가 수첩을 꺼내고 작은 남자의 손을 진심으로 정답게 흔들며 받아 말했다. \"내 말은 그저 금전상의 정산을 뜻한 거요. 당신은 내가 결코 갚을 수 없는, 그리고 갚고 싶지도 않은 친절을 여러 번 베풀어 주었소. 나는 그 은혜를 계속 진 채로 있는 편이 더 좋으니 말이오.\"\n\n이런 머리말과 함께, 두 친구는 무척 복잡한 계산서와 영수증 더미 속으로 파고들었다. 퍼커가 그것들을 차근차근 펼쳐 보이며 일일이 검토한 뒤, 픽윅 씨는 존경과 우정의 마음을 거듭 표하며 그 자리에서 모두 결제했다.\n\n그들이 이 단계에 막 다다랐을 때, 더없이 거세고 깜짝 놀랄 만한 문 두드리는 소리가 들렸다. 그것은 흔히 들리는 똑똑 두 번 두드리는 소리가 아니라, 마치 노커에 영구 운동의 힘이 깃들었거나 밖에 선 사람이 멈출 줄을 잊어버린 듯, 더없이 요란하게 한 번씩 끊임없이 줄기차게 두드려 대는 소리였다.\n\n\"이런 세상에, 저게 뭡니까?\" 퍼커가 깜짝 놀라며 외쳤다.\n\n\"문을 두드리는 소리 같은데요.\" 픽윅 씨가, 마치 그 사실에 조금이라도 의심의 여지가 있다는 듯 말했다.\n\n노커는 말보다 더 힘찬 대답을 해 보였으니, 한순간도 멈추지 않고 놀라운 힘과 소음으로 계속 쾅쾅 두들겨 댔던 것이다.\n\n\"이런 참!\" 퍼커가 종을 울리며 말했다. \"온 여관을 놀라게 하겠군. 로튼 씨, 노크 소리 안 들리나?\"\n\n\"곧 문 열어 드리겠습니다, 선생님.\" 사무원이 대답했다.\n\n노커는 그 대답을 듣기라도 한 듯, 그렇게 오래 기다리기란 도무지 불가능하다고 우기는 것 같았다. 그것은 어마어마한 소동을 일으켰다.\n\n\"정말 끔찍하군요.\" 픽윅 씨가 두 귀를 틀어막으며 말했다.\n\n\"서두르게, 로튼 씨.\" 퍼커가 큰 소리로 불렀다. \"문짝이 부서지겠어.\"\n\n어두운 골방에서 손을 씻고 있던 로튼 씨가 부랴부랴 문으로 가서 손잡이를 돌리자, 다음 장에 묘사될 그 광경이 눈앞에 나타났다.\n\n깜짝 놀란 사무원의 눈앞에 나타난 것은 한 소년이었다—놀랄 만큼 뚱뚱한 소년이었다—종복 차림을 하고서, 문 앞 매트 위에 똑바로 서서, 마치 잠든 듯 눈을 감고 있었다. 여행하는 유랑 마차 안에서든 밖에서든 그렇게 뚱뚱한 소년은 본 적이 없었다. 게다가 그 침착하고 평온한 겉모습은, 그런 노크를 해 댄 자에게서 으레 기대할 법한 것과는 너무도 달라서, 그는 경이감에 사로잡혔다.\n\n\"무슨 일이냐?\" 사무원이 물었다.\n\n그 기이한 소년은 한마디도 대답하지 않았다. 다만 한 번 고개를 끄덕였고, 사무원의 상상에는, 희미하게 코를 고는 듯했다.\n\n\"어디서 왔느냐?\" 사무원이 물었다.\n\n소년은 아무런 기척이 없었다. 숨을 무겁게 쉬었지만, 다른 모든 점에서는 미동도 없었다.\n\n사무원은 세 번이나 같은 질문을 되풀이했으나 답이 없자, 문을 닫으려고 했다. 그러자 소년이 갑자기 눈을 뜨더니, 몇 번 눈을 깜박이고, 한 번 재채기를 하고, 다시 노크를 하려는 듯 손을 들어 올렸다. 문이 열려 있는 것을 발견한 소년은 놀란 표정으로 주위를 두리번거리다가, 마침내 로튼 씨의 얼굴에 시선을 멈췄다.\n\n\"대체 왜 그렇게 노크를 해 대는 거냐?\" 사무원이 화를 내며 물었다.\n\n\"어떻게요?\" 소년이 느리고 졸린 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 마흔 명의 삯마차꾼처럼 말이다.\" 사무원이 대답했다.\n\n\"주인 나리가 그러셨거든요, 문 열 때까지 노크를 멈추지 말라고요, 안 그러면 제가 잠들까 봐서요.\" 소년이 말했다.\n\n\"그래,\" 사무원이 말했다. \"무슨 전갈을 가져왔느냐?\"\n\n\"아래층에 계셔요.\" 소년이 대답했다.\n\n\"누가?\"\n\n\"주인 나리요. 댁에 계신지 알고 싶어 하셔요.\"\n\n이 대목에서 로튼 씨는 창밖을 내다볼 생각이 났다. 뚜껑 없는 마차 안에 건장한 노신사가 타고서 몹시 초조하게 위를 올려다보고 있는 것을 보고, 그는 큰맘 먹고 손짓을 했다. 그러자 노신사가 곧장 마차에서 뛰어내렸다.\n\n\"마차에 탄 저분이 네 주인이겠지?\" 로튼이 말했다.\n\n소년이 고개를 끄덕였다.\n\n더 이상의 질문은 와들 영감의 등장으로 가로막혔다. 그는 계단을 뛰어 올라와 로튼을 겨우 알아보고는 곧장 퍼커 씨의 방으로 들어갔다.\n\n\"픽윅!\" 노신사가 말했다. \"손 좀 줘 보게, 이 사람아! 어째서 자네가 감옥에 갇혀 지냈다는 걸 그저께서야 듣게 된 건가? 그리고 자넨 어째서 저 사람이 그러도록 내버려 둔 건가, 퍼커?\"\n\n\"어쩔 수가 없었습니다, 선생님.\" 퍼커가 미소를 지으며 코담배 한 자밤을 집으며 대답했다. \"이분이 얼마나 고집이 센지 아시잖습니까?\"\n\n\"물론 알지, 물론 알고말고.\" 노신사가 대답했다. \"그래도 이 사람을 보니 진심으로 반갑네. 다시는 이 사람을 함부로 놓치지 않을 걸세.\"\n\n이 말과 함께 와들은 다시 한 번 픽윅 씨의 손을 잡고 흔들었고, 퍼커에게도 똑같이 한 뒤, 안락의자에 몸을 던졌다. 그의 명랑한 붉은 얼굴이 미소와 건강함으로 다시 빛나고 있었다.\n\n\"자!\" 와들이 말했다. \"참 볼만한 일들이 벌어지고 있군—코담배 한 자밤 주게, 퍼커—이런 시절은 일찍이 없었어, 안 그런가?\"\n\n\"무슨 말씀이십니까?\" 픽윅 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"무슨 말이냐고!\" 와들이 대답했다. \"아니, 내 생각엔 처녀들이 죄다 미쳐 돌아가는 것 같단 말이야. 그게 새로울 것 없는 소리라고 하겠지? 하긴 그럴지도 모르지. 하지만 그래도 그게 사실이라네.\"\n\n\"세상 어느 곳을 다 두고서 그 말씀을 하시러 런던까지 올라오신 건 아니겠지요, 선생님?\" 퍼커가 물었다.\n\n\"아니, 꼭 그것만은 아니지.\" 와들이 대답했다. \"그래도 그게 내가 올라온 주된 까닭이긴 해. 아라벨라는 어떤가?\"\n\n\"아주 잘 지냅니다.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다. \"선생님을 뵈면 분명 무척 기뻐할 겁니다.\"\n\n\"검은 눈동자의 앙큼한 것 같으니!\" 와들이 대답했다. \"나도 언젠가 그 애와 결혼해 볼까 하는 생각을 꽤나 했었지. 하지만 이렇게 된 것도 기뻐, 아주 기쁘다네.\"\n\n\"그 소식이 어떻게 선생님께 닿았습니까?\" 픽윅 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"오, 물론 우리 집 딸들한테 왔지.\" 와들이 대답했다. \"아라벨라가 그저께 편지를 보냈더군, 남편 아버지의 동의 없이 몰래 결혼을 했고, 그래서 자네가 그 동의를 받아 내러 내려갔다고 말이야, 그자가 거절해도 결혼을 막을 수 없게 된 마당에 말일세, 그 밖의 사연들도 전부. 나는 우리 딸들에게 뭔가 진지한 말을 해 줄 아주 좋은 기회라고 생각했지. 그래서 자식이 부모 동의 없이 결혼하는 게 얼마나 끔찍한 일인지 어쩌고저쩌고 말했다네. 그런데 이런, 그 애들에겐 손톱만큼도 감명을 줄 수가 없었어. 그 애들은 들러리도 없이 결혼식이 치러졌다는 게 훨씬 더 끔찍한 일이라고 여기더군. 차라리 조 녀석한테 설교하는 편이 나았을 거야.\"\n\n여기서 노신사는 웃느라 잠시 말을 멈췄다. 실컷 웃고 난 뒤 곧 다시 말을 이었다—\n\n\"하지만 이게 다가 아닌 모양이야. 이건 그동안 벌어진 연애와 음모의 절반에 지나지 않거든. 우린 지난 여섯 달 동안 지뢰밭을 걷고 있었던 거고, 그게 마침내 터져 버린 게지.\"\n\n\"무슨 말씀이십니까?\" 픽윅 씨가 창백해지며 소리쳤다. \"또 다른 비밀 결혼은 아니겠지요, 제발?\"\n\n\"아니, 아니야.\" 와들 영감이 대답했다. \"그렇게까지 나쁘진 않아. 아니야.\"\n\n\"그럼 뭡니까?\" 픽윅 씨가 물었다. \"제가 거기 얽혀 있습니까?\"\n\n\"그 질문에 대답해도 되겠나, 퍼커?\" 와들이 말했다.\n\n\"그렇게 하셔도 책잡힐 일이 없으시다면야, 선생님.\"\n\n\"그럼 좋아, 자넨 얽혀 있다네.\" 와들이 말했다.\n\n\"어떻게요?\" 픽윅 씨가 초조하게 물었다. \"어떤 식으로 말입니까?\"\n\n\"솔직히,\" 와들이 대답했다. \"자넨 워낙 불같은 젊은이라서 말하기가 거의 두려울 지경이야. 하지만 어쨌든 퍼커가 우리 사이에 앉아 사고를 막아 준다면, 한번 해 보지.\"\n\n방문을 닫고 퍼커의 코담배갑에 다시 한 번 손을 대어 기운을 차린 노신사는, 이런 말로 그 중대한 폭로를 시작했다—\n\n\"실은 말이야, 내 딸 벨라가—벨라, 그 왜 트런들 청년과 결혼한 그 애 말일세.\"\n\n\"예, 예, 압니다.\" 픽윅 씨가 조바심을 내며 말했다.\n\n\"시작부터 나를 놀라게 하지 말게나. 내 딸 벨라가—에밀리는 내게 아라벨라의 편지를 읽어 준 뒤 두통이 나서 잠자리에 들었고—요전 저녁에 내 곁에 앉더니 이 결혼 이야기를 꺼내기 시작했어. '저기, 아빠,' 그 애가 말하길, '어떻게 생각하세요?' '글쎄, 얘야,' 내가 말했지, '다 괜찮은 일이겠지. 잘된 일이길 바란다.' 내가 이렇게 대답한 건, 그때 마침 난롯가에 앉아 꽤 골똘히 생각하며 그로그를 마시고 있던 참이라, 이따금 이렇게 어정쩡한 말을 던져 주면 그 애가 이야기를 계속하리라는 걸 알았기 때문이지. 우리 집 두 딸은 다 제 어미를 빼다 박았어. 나도 나이가 들수록 그 애들만 곁에 두고 앉아 있는 게 좋아졌다네. 그 애들의 목소리와 모습이 나를 내 인생에서 가장 행복했던 시절로 데려다주고, 그 순간만큼은 나를 그 시절처럼 젊게 만들어 주거든, 그만큼 마음이 가볍진 않더라도 말일세. '정말 애정으로 맺어진 결혼이에요, 아빠.' 잠시 침묵하더니 벨라가 말했어. '그래, 얘야,' 내가 말했지, '하지만 그런 결혼이 늘 가장 행복한 결말을 맺는 건 아니란다.'\"\n\n\"그건 저로선 의문입니다, 명심하세요!\" 픽윅 씨가 열을 내며 끼어들었다.\n\n\"좋아, 좋아.\" 와들이 응수했다. \"자네 차례가 되면 뭐든 의문을 제기하게, 다만 내 말을 끊진 말게.\"\n\n\"죄송합니다.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"용서하지.\" 와들이 대답했다. \"'애정으로 맺어진 결혼에 반대하는 의견을 말씀하시니 마음이 아파요, 아빠.' 벨라가 약간 얼굴을 붉히며 말했어. '내가 틀렸다. 그런 말을 하지 말았어야 했어, 얘야.' 내가 말했지, 나 같은 거친 늙은이가 할 수 있는 한 다정하게 그 애의 뺨을 토닥이면서 말이야, '네 어미도 그런 결혼이었고, 너도 그랬으니까.' '제가 말하려는 건 그게 아니에요, 아빠.' 벨라가 말했어. '실은요, 아빠, 에밀리에 관해서 말씀드리고 싶었어요.'\"\n\n픽윅 씨가 흠칫했다.\n\n\"이번엔 또 무슨 일인가?\" 와들이 이야기를 멈추며 물었다.\n\n\"아무것도 아닙니다.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다. \"어서 계속하십시오.\"\n\n\"난 도무지 이야기를 길게 끌 줄을 몰라.\" 와들이 퉁명스럽게 말했다. \"어차피 조만간 나올 일이니, 한꺼번에 나오는 편이 우리 모두 시간을 크게 아끼는 거지. 그러니 요컨대 이런 거야. 벨라가 마침내 용기를 내어 내게 말하길, 에밀리가 몹시 불행해한다는 거야. 지난 크리스마스 이후로 그 애와 자네의 젊은 친구 스노드그래스가 끊임없이 편지를 주고받고 소식을 나누어 왔다는 거지. 그 애가 제 오랜 친구이자 학교 동무를 본받아 그자와 함께 도망칠 마음을 아주 효녀답게 먹었다는 거야. 다만 그 일에 양심의 가책이 좀 있었다는데, 내가 그 둘 모두에게 늘 제법 호의를 품어 왔던 터라, 그래서 우선은 나한테 예의를 갖춰 그 둘이 흔한 평범한 방식으로 결혼하는 데 내가 반대할 마음이 있는지 물어보는 편이 낫겠다고 둘이서 생각했다는 거지. 자, 픽윅, 자네가 그 눈을 다시 본래 크기로 줄여 주고, 우리가 어떻게 해야 한다고 생각하는지 좀 들려준다면, 내 자네한테 꽤나 고마워하겠네!\"\n\n이 건장한 노신사가 마지막 문장을 내뱉을 때의 그 짜증스러운 태도는 전혀 까닭 없는 것은 아니었다. 픽윅 씨의 얼굴이 멍한 경악과 당혹의 표정으로 굳어져, 보기에 자못 신기할 지경이었으니 말이다.\n\n\"스노드그래스가!—지난 크리스마스 이후로!\" 어안이 벙벙해진 신사의 입에서 나온 첫 토막말이 그것이었다.\n\n\"지난 크리스마스 이후로지.\" 와들이 대답했다. \"그건 아주 분명해. 진작 알아채지 못했으니 우리가 어지간히 형편없는 안경을 끼고 있었던 게 틀림없어.\"\n\n\"이해가 안 됩니다.\" 픽윅 씨가 곰곰이 생각하며 말했다. \"정말이지 이해할 수가 없군요.\"\n\n\"이해하기야 충분히 쉽지.\" 성마른 노신사가 대답했다. \"자네가 더 젊은 사람이었다면, 진작에 그 비밀을 알았을 거야. 게다가,\" 와들이 잠시 머뭇거리다 덧붙였다. \"사실은 말이야, 내가 이 일을 전혀 모르고서, 지난 너덧 달 동안 에밀리에게 우리 동네 어느 젊은 신사의 구애를 호의적으로 받아 주라고 (받아 줄 수 있다면 말일세. 난 결코 처녀의 마음을 억지로 강요할 생각은 없으니까) 제법 채근했거든. 의심할 바 없이, 그 애가 처녀답게, 제 몸값을 높이고 스노드그래스 씨의 열정을 부추기려고, 이 일을 아주 화려한 색깔로 부풀려 이야기했을 거고, 그래서 둘이 함께 자기들이 끔찍하게 박해받는 가련한 한 쌍이라는 결론에 이르렀을 게야, 비밀 결혼 아니면 숯불 말고는 달리 방도가 없다는 식으로 말이지. 자, 이제 문제는, 어떻게 해야 하느냐는 걸세.\"\n\n\"선생님은 어떻게 하셨습니까?\" 픽윅 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"나 말인가!\"\n\"제 말은, 결혼한 따님이 이 이야기를 했을 때 선생님은 어떻게 하셨냐는 겁니다.\"\n\n\"오, 물론 바보짓을 했지.\" 와들이 응수했다.\n\n\"바로 그렇습니다.\" 퍼커가 끼어들었다. 그는 이 대화 내내 회중시계 줄을 만지작거리고, 분풀이하듯 코를 문지르고, 그 밖의 초조한 기색을 보여 왔던 터였다. \"지극히 자연스러운 일이지요. 한데 어떻게 하셨습니까?\"\n\n\"내가 잔뜩 성을 내는 바람에 내 어머니가 놀라 발작을 일으키셨지.\" 와들이 말했다.\n\n\"현명하셨군요.\" 퍼커가 한마디 했다. \"그리고 또요?\"\n\n\"이튿날 온종일 끙끙 앓고 씩씩대면서 한바탕 큰 소란을 피웠지.\" 노신사가 응수했다. \"마침내 나도 나 자신을 불쾌하게 만들고 모두를 비참하게 만드는 데 지쳐 버렸어. 그래서 머글턴에서 마차를 빌리고, 거기에 내 말들을 매어, 에밀리를 데리고 아라벨라를 보러 간다는 핑계로 도시로 올라온 거야.\"\n\n\"그럼 와들 양도 함께 오셨군요?\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇고말고.\" 와들이 대답했다. \"지금 이 순간 애덜파이의 오즈번 호텔에 있어, 자네의 그 진취적인 친구가 내가 오늘 아침 나온 사이에 데리고 도망치지 않았다면 말일세.\"\n\n\"그럼 화해는 하셨습니까?\" 퍼커가 말했다.\n\n\"전혀 아니야.\" 와들이 답했다. \"그 애는 그 뒤로 줄곧 울고 시무룩하게 지냈어. 어젯밤 차와 저녁 식사 사이에, 내가 짐짓 못 본 척했던 편지 한 통을 거창하게 써 댈 때만 빼고는 말이야.\"\n\n\"이 일에 제 조언을 바라시는 거겠지요?\" 퍼커가, 픽윅 씨의 골똘한 얼굴과 와들의 간절한 표정을 번갈아 보고서, 자기가 좋아하는 그 자극제를 연거푸 몇 자밤 들이켜며 말했다.\n\n\"그런 셈이네.\" 와들이 픽윅 씨를 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"물론입니다.\" 그 신사가 대답했다.\n\n\"그럼 좋습니다.\" 퍼커가 일어나 의자를 뒤로 밀며 말했다. \"제 조언은, 두 분이 함께 걸어 나가시든, 마차를 타고 나가시든, 어떤 수단으로든 자리를 뜨시라는 겁니다. 저는 두 분께 지쳤으니까요. 그리고 이 일을 두 분끼리 의논해 보십시오. 다음번에 제가 두 분을 뵐 때까지 매듭을 짓지 못하시면, 그때 제가 어떻게 해야 할지 말씀드리겠습니다.\"\n\n\"이거 만족스럽군요.\" 와들이, 웃어야 할지 기분이 상해야 할지 도통 모르겠다는 투로 말했다.\n\n\"천만에요, 선생님.\" 퍼커가 응수했다. \"전 두 분 자신보다 두 분을 훨씬 잘 압니다. 두 분은 사실상 이미 매듭을 다 지으신 겁니다.\"\n\n이렇게 말하면서 자그마한 신사는 코담배갑을 먼저 픽윅 씨의 가슴팍에, 다음엔 와들 씨의 조끼에 쿡 찔러 댔고, 그 바람에 셋 모두 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 특히 뒤에 언급된 두 신사가 더했으니, 그들은 별다른 뚜렷한 이유도 없이 곧장 다시 손을 맞잡았다.\n\n\"오늘 나와 저녁을 함께 드세.\" 와들이 그들을 배웅하며 퍼커에게 말했다.\n\n\"약속은 못 드립니다, 선생님, 약속은 못 드려요.\" 퍼커가 대답했다. \"어쨌든 저녁에 들러 보겠습니다.\"\n\n\"다섯 시에 기다리겠네.\" 와들이 말했다. \"자, 조!\" 그리고 조가 마침내 깨워지자, 두 친구는 와들 씨의 마차에 올라 떠났다. 그 마차에는 인정상 뚱뚱한 소년을 위해 뒤쪽에 좌석이 달려 있었는데, 만약 거기에 발판만 달려 있었더라면 소년은 첫 낮잠을 자다가 굴러떨어져 죽고 말았을 것이다.\n\n조지 앤드 벌처로 마차를 몰고 가 보니, 아라벨라와 그 하녀는 에밀리가 도시에 도착했음을 알리는 짧은 쪽지를 받자마자 삯마차를 불러서 곧장 애덜파이로 떠났다는 것이었다. 와들은 시내에서 볼일이 있었으므로, 마차와 뚱뚱한 소년을 그의 호텔로 보내며, 그와 픽윅 씨가 다섯 시에 함께 저녁을 먹으러 돌아올 것이라고 전하게 했다.\n\n이 전갈을 맡은 뚱뚱한 소년은, 마치 용수철 위에 깔린 깃털 침대라도 되는 양, 돌길 위를 평온하게 뒷좌석에서 졸며 돌아왔다. 무슨 기이한 기적인지, 마차가 멈추자 소년은 제풀에 잠에서 깨어났고, 정신을 차리려고 몸을 한 번 호되게 흔든 뒤 임무를 수행하러 위층으로 올라갔다.\n\n그런데 그 흔들기가 뚱뚱한 소년의 정신을 제대로 정돈해 주기는커녕 죄다 뒤죽박죽으로 섞어 놓았는지, 아니면 그 안에 새로운 생각을 잔뜩 일깨워 그가 평소의 격식과 예절을 까맣게 잊게 만들었는지, 아니면 (이 또한 있을 법한 일인데) 계단을 오르는 동안 그가 잠드는 것을 막는 데 실패했는지는 모르겠으나, 한 가지 분명한 사실은 그가 먼저 문을 두드리지도 않고 거실로 걸어 들어갔다는 것이다. 그리하여 그는 한 신사가 두 팔로 자기의 젊은 여주인의 허리를 감싸 안고 소파에서 그녀 곁에 아주 다정하게 앉아 있는 광경을 목격했다. 한편 아라벨라와 그 어여쁜 하녀는 방 저쪽 끝 창밖을 내다보는 데 골몰한 척하고 있었다. 이 진풍경을 보고서, 뚱뚱한 소년은 감탄사를, 숙녀들은 비명을, 그리고 신사는 욕설을, 거의 동시에 내뱉었다.\n\n\"한심한 녀석 같으니, 여기 무슨 볼일이냐?\" 신사가 말했는데, 그가 스노드그래스 씨임은 굳이 말할 필요도 없겠다.\n\n이에 대해 뚱뚱한 소년은 잔뜩 겁을 먹은 채로 짧게 대답했다. \"마님이요.\"\n\n\"무슨 일로 나를 찾는 거냐,\" 에밀리가 고개를 옆으로 돌리며 물었다. \"이 멍청한 녀석아?\"\n\n\"주인 나리하고 픽윅 씨가 다섯 시에 여기서 저녁을 드시러 오신답니다.\" 뚱뚱한 소년이 대답했다.\n\n\"방에서 나가!\" 스노드그래스 씨가 어쩔 줄 모르는 소년을 노려보며 말했다.\n\n\"안 돼요, 안 돼요, 안 돼.\" 에밀리가 황급히 덧붙였다. \"벨라, 얘, 어떻게 하면 좋을지 좀 알려 줘.\"\n\n이에 에밀리와 스노드그래스 씨, 그리고 아라벨라와 메리가 한쪽 구석에 모여, 몇 분 동안 속삭이며 진지하게 의논했다. 그동안 뚱뚱한 소년은 꾸벅꾸벅 졸았다.\n\n\"조.\" 마침내 아라벨라가 더없이 매혹적인 미소를 띠며 돌아보고 말했다. \"잘 지냈니, 조?\"\n\n\"조.\" 에밀리가 말했다. \"넌 참 착한 아이야. 널 잊지 않으마, 조.\"\n\n\"조.\" 스노드그래스 씨가 어리둥절해하는 소년에게 다가가 그 손을 붙잡으며 말했다. \"내가 전엔 널 몰라봤구나. 자, 5실링이다, 조!\"\n\n\"난 5실링을 빚지마, 조.\" 아라벨라가 말했다. \"오랜 정을 봐서 말이야, 알겠지?\" 그러고는 또 한 번 더없이 사람을 사로잡는 미소를 그 살집 좋은 침입자에게 베풀었다.\n\n뚱뚱한 소년은 머리 회전이 느려서, 처음엔 자기에게 갑자기 쏟아지는 이 호의를 어떻게 받아들여야 할지 몰라 어리둥절한 표정을 짓고는, 자못 섬뜩한 태도로 주위를 두리번거렸다. 마침내 그 넓적한 얼굴이 그에 걸맞게 넓적한 헤벌쭉한 웃음의 기미를 보이기 시작했다. 그러더니 양쪽 주머니에 반 크라운씩을 쑤셔 넣고, 그 뒤로 손과 손목까지 따라 집어넣고는, 말처럼 큰 소리로 껄껄 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 그것은 그의 생애에서 처음이자 유일한 일이었다.\n\n\"이 애가 우리 뜻을 알아들었나 봐요.\" 아라벨라가 말했다.\n\n\"당장 뭘 좀 먹이는 게 좋겠어요.\" 에밀리가 한마디 했다.\n\n뚱뚱한 소년은 이 제안을 듣고 또다시 웃을 뻔했다. 메리는 조금 더 속삭이고 나서 무리에서 종종걸음으로 나오며 말했다—\n\n\"오늘 너랑 같이 저녁을 먹을 거란다, 괜찮다면 말이야.\"\n\n\"이쪽이에요.\" 뚱뚱한 소년이 신이 나서 말했다. \"정말 끝내주는 고기 파이가 있거든요!\"\n\n이 말과 함께 뚱뚱한 소년이 앞장서서 아래층으로 내려갔다. 그의 어여쁜 동행은 식당으로 그를 따라가면서 종업원들을 죄다 사로잡고 객실 하녀들을 죄다 화나게 했다.\n\n소년이 그토록 감개무량하게 말하던 그 고기 파이가 거기 있었고, 게다가 스테이크 한 접시와 감자 요리 한 접시, 그리고 포터 한 병이 있었다.\n\n\"앉아요.\" 뚱뚱한 소년이 말했다. \"와, 이거 죽이네! 난 정말 배고파요.\"\n\n황홀경에 빠진 양 다섯 번인가 여섯 번 \"와\" 하고 감탄사를 내뱉고 나서, 소년은 작은 식탁의 상석에 앉았고, 메리는 말석에 자리를 잡았다.\n\n\"이거 좀 들래요?\" 뚱뚱한 소년이 나이프와 포크의 자루 끝까지 푹 파이 속에 박아 넣으며 말했다.\n\n\"조금만, 괜찮다면.\" 메리가 대답했다.\n\n뚱뚱한 소년은 메리에게는 조금, 자기에게는 잔뜩 덜어 주고서, 막 먹기 시작하려다가, 갑자기 나이프와 포크를 내려놓고 의자에서 몸을 앞으로 숙이더니, 나이프와 포크를 쥔 두 손을 무릎 위에 떨구고는, 아주 느릿느릿 말했다—\n\n\"있잖아요! 정말 예쁘네요!\"\n\n이 말은 감탄하는 투로 한 것이었으니, 거기까지는 듣기 좋은 소리였다. 그러나 이 젊은 신사의 눈에는 식인종 같은 기색이 여전히 충분히 남아 있어서, 그 찬사를 이중의 의미로 만들고 말았다.\n\n\"어머나, 조지프.\" 메리가 얼굴을 붉히는 시늉을 하며 말했다. \"그게 무슨 소리야?\"\n\n뚱뚱한 소년은 차츰 본래 자세를 되찾으며, 무거운 한숨으로 답하고는, 몇 순간 생각에 잠겨 있다가, 포터를 길게 한 모금 들이켰다. 이 위업을 이루고 난 뒤 다시 한숨을 쉬고는, 부지런히 파이에 매달렸다.\n\n\"에밀리 아가씨는 참 고운 분이셔!\" 메리가 한참 침묵하다가 말했다.\n\n이때쯤 뚱뚱한 소년은 파이를 다 먹어 치운 뒤였다. 그는 메리에게 시선을 고정하고 대답했다—\n\n\"난 더 고운 사람도 알아요.\"\n\n\"어머, 정말!\" 메리가 말했다.\n\n\"그럼요, 정말이고말고요!\" 뚱뚱한 소년이 평소답지 않은 활기를 띠며 대답했다.\n\n\"그 사람 이름이 뭔데?\" 메리가 물었다.\n\n\"누나 이름은 뭐예요?\"\n\n\"메리야.\"\n\n\"그 사람도 그래요.\" 뚱뚱한 소년이 말했다. \"누나가 바로 그 사람이에요.\" 소년은 그 찬사에 쐐기를 박으려고 히죽 웃으며, 두 눈을 사팔뜨기와 곁눈질의 중간쯤으로 만들었는데, 그것은 추파를 던지려던 것이라 짐작할 만한 이유가 있다.\n\n\"그런 식으로 나한테 말하면 안 돼.\" 메리가 말했다. \"진심도 아니면서.\"\n\n\"진심이 아니라고요?\" 뚱뚱한 소년이 대답했다. \"있잖아요?\"\n\n\"왜?\"\n\n\"여기 계속 오실 거예요?\"\n\n\"아니.\" 메리가 고개를 저으며 응수했다. \"오늘 밤 다시 떠날 거야. 왜?\"\n\n\"오.\" 뚱뚱한 소년이 격한 감정을 담아 말했다. \"누나가 계셨더라면 식사 때마다 우리 얼마나 즐거웠을까요!\"\n\n\"가끔은 여기 와서 너를 볼 수도 있겠지, 어쩌면.\" 메리가 짐짓 수줍은 척하며 식탁보를 접으며 말했다. \"네가 내 부탁 하나만 들어준다면 말이야.\"\n\n뚱뚱한 소년은 부탁이라는 것이 으레 먹을 것과 어떤 식으로든 관계가 있다고 여기는 듯 파이 접시에서 스테이크로 시선을 옮겼다. 그러더니 반 크라운 하나를 꺼내 초조하게 그것을 흘끔 보았다.\n\n\"내 말 못 알아듣겠어?\" 메리가 그의 뚱뚱한 얼굴을 짓궂게 들여다보며 말했다.\n\n소년은 또다시 반 크라운을 바라보고는 힘없이 말했다. \"네.\"\n\n\"숙녀분들이 네가 그 젊은 신사분이 위층에 와 계셨던 걸 영감님께 아무 말도 하지 않기를 바라셔. 나도 그러길 바라고.\"\n\n\"그게 다예요?\" 뚱뚱한 소년이 반 크라운을 다시 주머니에 넣으며, 분명 크게 안심한 표정으로 말했다. \"당연히 말 안 할 거예요.\"\n\n\"있잖아,\" 메리가 말했다. \"스노드그래스 씨는 에밀리 아가씨를 무척 좋아하시고, 에밀리 아가씨도 그분을 무척 좋아하셔. 만약 네가 그 얘기를 해 버리면, 영감님이 너희를 모두 시골 멀리 데려가서, 거기선 아무도 못 보게 될 거야.\"\n\n\"아니, 아니, 말 안 할게요.\" 뚱뚱한 소년이 단호하게 말했다.\n\n\"착하기도 해라.\" 메리가 말했다. \"이제 위층에 올라가서 아가씨 저녁 식사 채비를 해 드릴 시간이네.\"\n\n\"아직 가지 마요.\" 뚱뚱한 소년이 졸랐다.\n\n\"가야 해.\" 메리가 대답했다. \"그럼 또 봐, 이만.\"\n\n뚱뚱한 소년은 코끼리 같은 장난기로 두 팔을 뻗어 입맞춤을 빼앗으려 했다. 그러나 그를 피하는 데에는 그리 대단한 날렵함이 필요치 않았으므로, 그가 다시 두 팔을 오므리기도 전에 그 어여쁜 매혹자는 사라져 버리고 없었다. 그러자 무덤덤한 소년은 감상적인 표정으로 스테이크를 한 파운드쯤 먹고서, 깊이 곯아떨어졌다.\n\n위층에서는 할 말이 어찌나 많은지, 그리고 와들 영감이 계속 모질게 군다면 도망치고 결혼하기 위해 짜야 할 계획이 어찌나 많은지, 스노드그래스 씨가 마지막 작별을 고했을 때는 저녁 식사까지 겨우 반 시간밖에 남지 않았다. 숙녀들은 옷을 갈아입으러 에밀리의 침실로 달려갔고, 연인은 모자를 집어 들고 방을 나섰다. 그가 문밖으로 채 나서기도 전에, 와들이 큰 소리로 떠드는 목소리가 들려왔다. 난간 너머로 내려다보니, 다른 몇몇 신사를 거느린 그가 곧장 위층으로 올라오고 있는 것이 보였다. 이 집을 전혀 모르는 스노드그래스 씨는 당황한 나머지 황급히 방금 나온 방으로 도로 들어갔고, 거기서 다시 안쪽 방(와들 씨의 침실)으로 들어가, 자기가 얼핏 본 사람들이 거실로 들어서는 바로 그 순간 살그머니 문을 닫았다. 그들은 와들 씨, 픽윅 씨, 너새니얼 윈클 씨, 그리고 벤저민 앨런 씨였는데, 그는 그들의 목소리로 어렵지 않게 그들을 알아볼 수 있었다.\n\n'그들을 피할 침착함을 발휘했으니 천만다행이군.' 스노드그래스 씨는 미소를 지으며 생각하고는, 침대 옆 또 다른 문으로 발끝을 세워 걸어갔다. '이 문은 같은 복도로 통하니, 조용히 편안하게 빠져나갈 수 있겠어.'\n\n그가 조용하고 편안하게 빠져나가는 데에는 단 하나의 장애물이 있었으니, 문이 잠겨 있고 열쇠가 없어졌다는 것이었다.\n\n\"오늘은 자네네 가장 좋은 포도주를 좀 내오게, 종업원.\" 와들 영감이 두 손을 비비며 말했다.\n\n\"아주 최상품으로 내드리겠습니다, 손님.\" 종업원이 대답했다.\n\n\"숙녀분들께 우리가 들어왔다고 알려 주게.\"\n\n\"예, 손님.\"\n\n스노드그래스 씨는 숙녀들이 자기가 들어와 있음을 알아 줄 수 있기를 경건하고도 간절하게 바랐다. 그는 한 번은 큰맘 먹고 열쇠 구멍에 대고 \"종업원!\" 하고 속삭여 보았지만, 엉뚱한 종업원이 자기를 구하러 올지도 모른다는 가능성이 머릿속에 번뜩 떠올랐다. 동시에 자기 처지가, 최근 이웃 호텔에서 또 다른 신사가 발견되었던 상황(그 불운에 관한 기사가 그날 아침 신문의 '경찰' 면에 실린 바 있었다)과 너무도 흡사하다는 생각이 들자, 그는 여행 가방 위에 걸터앉아 심하게 떨었다.\n\n\"퍼커는 잠시도 기다리지 말지.\" 와들이 시계를 보며 말했다. \"그 친구는 늘 정확하거든. 올 생각이면 시간 맞춰 올 테고, 안 올 거면 기다려 봐야 소용없지. 아! 아라벨라!\"\n\n\"내 동생!\" 벤저민 앨런 씨가 더없이 낭만적인 포옹으로 그녀를 끌어안으며 외쳤다.\n\n\"어머, 벤 오빠, 담배 냄새가 어쩜 이렇게 진동해.\" 아라벨라가 이 애정 표시에 다소 질려서 말했다.\n\n\"내가?\" 벤저민 앨런 씨가 말했다. \"내가 그래, 벨라? 음, 그럴지도 모르지.\"\n\n그럴지도 몰랐다. 그는 방금 큼직한 난로가 있는 작은 뒷방에서 의대생 열두 명이 모인 정겨운 자그마한 흡연 모임을 막 빠져나온 참이었으니 말이다.\n\n\"하지만 널 보니 정말 기쁘다.\" 벤 앨런 씨가 말했다. \"잘 지냈지, 벨라!\"\n\n\"자, 됐어요.\" 아라벨라가 오빠에게 입을 맞추려 몸을 앞으로 기울이며 말했다. \"다시 날 붙잡지 마, 벤 오빠, 자꾸 날 헝클어뜨리니까.\"\n\n화해의 이 대목에서, 벤 앨런 씨는 감정과 시가와 포터에 압도되어, 안경에 김이 서린 채로 둘러선 사람들을 바라보았다.\n\n\"내겐 할 말이 없는 거냐?\" 와들이 두 팔을 벌리며 소리쳤다.\n\n\"아주 많죠.\" 아라벨라가, 노신사의 다정한 포옹과 축하를 받으며 속삭였다. \"당신은 매정하고 무정하고 잔인한 괴물이에요.\"\n\n\"넌 자그마한 반역자로구나.\" 와들이 같은 투로 대꾸했다. \"아무래도 너를 이 집에 못 들어오게 해야겠어. 너처럼 모두의 반대를 무릅쓰고 결혼하는 사람들은 세상에 풀어놓아선 안 되는 법이야. 하지만 자!\" 노신사가 큰 소리로 덧붙였다. \"여기 저녁 식사가 왔구나. 넌 내 곁에 앉거라. 조. 아니, 이 녀석 좀 보게, 깨어 있잖아!\"\n\n주인을 크게 당혹스럽게 한 일이지만, 뚱뚱한 소년은 정말로 눈에 띄게 또렷한 상태였다. 두 눈을 활짝 뜨고서, 계속 그렇게 뜨고 있을 작정인 듯이 보였다. 게다가 그의 태도에는 역시 영문 모를 민첩함이 있었다. 그의 눈이 에밀리나 아라벨라의 눈과 마주칠 때마다, 그는 능글맞게 웃고 헤벌쭉거렸다. 한 번은, 와들이 단언컨대, 그가 윙크하는 것을 본 듯도 했다.\n\n뚱뚱한 소년의 이 달라진 거동은, 자신의 중요성에 대한 한층 높아진 자각과, 젊은 숙녀들의 비밀을 함께 나누게 됨으로써 얻은 위엄에서 비롯된 것이었다. 그 능글맞은 웃음과 헤벌쭉거림과 윙크는, 그들이 자기의 충성심을 믿어도 좋다는 갖가지 생색내는 듯한 확언이었다. 그러나 이런 표시들은 의심을 가라앉히기보다는 오히려 불러일으키기 십상이었고, 게다가 다소 난처하기도 했으므로, 이따금 아라벨라가 눈살을 찌푸리거나 고개를 흔드는 것으로 응답했다. 뚱뚱한 소년은 이를 조심하라는 암시로 여겨, 그것을 완벽히 알아들었다는 뜻을 곱절로 부지런히 능글맞게 웃고 헤벌쭉거리고 윙크하는 것으로 표현했다.\n\n\"조.\" 와들 씨가 주머니란 주머니를 다 뒤지고도 허탕을 친 뒤에 말했다. \"내 코담배갑이 소파 위에 있느냐?\"\n\n\"없는데요, 나리.\" 뚱뚱한 소년이 대답했다.\n\n\"아, 생각났다. 오늘 아침 화장대 위에 두고 왔지.\" 와들이 말했다. \"옆방에 가서 가져오너라.\"\n\n뚱뚱한 소년은 옆방으로 들어갔다. 그리고 한 일 분쯤 자리를 비웠다가, 코담배갑과 함께, 뚱뚱한 소년으로서 일찍이 지어 본 적 없는 더없이 창백한 얼굴을 하고 돌아왔다.\n\n\"이 녀석한테 무슨 일이 있는 거냐?\" 와들이 소리쳤다.\n\n\"아무 일도 없는데요.\" 조가 초조하게 대답했다.\n\n\"귀신이라도 본 게냐?\" 노신사가 물었다.\n\n\"아니면 술이라도 마신 게냐?\" 벤 앨런이 덧붙였다.\n\n\"자네 말이 맞는 것 같네.\" 와들이 식탁 너머로 속삭였다. \"이 녀석 취했어, 틀림없어.\"\n\n벤 앨런은 자기도 그런 것 같다고 대답했다. 그리고 그 신사가 바로 그 병을 숱하게 보아 온 터라, 와들은 반 시간 전부터 머릿속을 맴돌던 인상에 확신을 얻어, 곧장 뚱뚱한 소년이 취했다는 결론에 이르렀다.\n\n\"몇 분만 저 녀석을 잘 지켜보게.\" 와들이 중얼거렸다. \"취했는지 아닌지 곧 알게 될 테니.\"\n\n이 불운한 소년은 스노드그래스 씨와 고작 열 마디쯤 주고받았을 뿐이었다. 그 신사가 소년에게 어느 친구에게든 은밀히 부탁하여 자기를 풀어 달라고 간청한 다음, 자리를 오래 비우면 발각될까 봐 코담배갑을 들려 그를 밀어낸 것이었다. 소년은 더없이 심란한 표정으로 잠깐 골똘히 생각하다가, 메리를 찾으러 방을 나섰다.\n\n그러나 메리는 여주인의 단장을 마친 뒤 집으로 돌아간 뒤였고, 뚱뚱한 소년은 전보다 더 심란해져서 다시 돌아왔다.\n\n와들과 벤 앨런 씨가 서로 눈길을 주고받았다.\n\n\"조!\" 와들이 말했다.\n\n\"예, 나리.\"\n\n\"왜 나갔던 거냐?\"\n\n뚱뚱한 소년은 식탁에 둘러앉은 모두의 얼굴을 막막한 심정으로 바라보다가, 자기도 모르겠다고 더듬거렸다.\n\n\"오.\" 와들이 말했다. \"모르겠다, 그거냐? 이 치즈를 픽윅 씨께 갖다 드려라.\"\n\n그런데 픽윅 씨는 더없이 좋은 건강과 기분 상태여서, 저녁 식사 내내 자신을 완벽하게 유쾌한 사람으로 만들고 있었다. 마침 이 순간에는 에밀리, 윈클 씨와 더불어 활기찬 대화에 열중하던 참이었다. 자기 이야기에 힘을 줄 때면 정중하게 고개를 숙이고, 자신의 견해에 무게를 싣고자 왼손을 부드럽게 흔들며, 온통 잔잔한 미소로 빛나고 있었다. 그는 접시에서 치즈 한 조각을 집어 들고, 막 몸을 돌려 대화를 다시 이어 가려던 참이었다. 그때 뚱뚱한 소년이 자기 머리가 픽윅 씨의 머리와 같은 높이가 되도록 몸을 숙이고는, 엄지손가락으로 자기 어깨 너머를 가리키며, 크리스마스 무언극에서도 본 적 없는 가장 끔찍하고 흉측한 표정을 지어 보였다.\n\n\"아니 이런!\" 픽윅 씨가 흠칫하며 말했다. \"이 무슨 참으로—어?\" 그는 말을 멈췄다. 뚱뚱한 소년이 몸을 똑바로 세우고는, 깊이 잠들었거나 잠든 척하고 있었기 때문이다.\n\n\"무슨 일입니까?\" 와들이 물었다.\n\n\"이 아이는 정말이지 지극히 별난 녀석이군요!\" 픽윅 씨가 불안하게 소년을 바라보며 대답했다. \"이런 말을 하기는 좀 이상하지만, 정말이지 저는 이 아이가 때때로 약간 정신이 온전치 않은 게 아닌가 걱정됩니다.\"\n\n\"어머! 픽윅 씨, 제발 그런 말씀 마세요.\" 에밀리와 아라벨라가 동시에 소리쳤다.\n\n\"물론 확실치는 않습니다만,\" 픽윅 씨가 깊은 침묵과 모두의 당혹스러운 표정 가운데서 말했다. \"방금 저를 대하는 이 아이의 태도가 정말 무척 섬뜩했습니다. 아얏!\" 픽윅 씨가 짧은 비명과 함께 갑자기 벌떡 일어서며 외쳤다. \"용서하십시오, 숙녀분들. 하지만 방금 이 아이가 무슨 뾰족한 도구로 제 다리를 찔렀습니다. 정말이지 이 아이는 위험합니다.\"\n\n\"이 녀석 취했어.\" 와들 영감이 격하게 고함쳤다. \"종을 울려라! 종업원들을 불러! 이 녀석 취했다고.\"\n\n\"안 취했어요.\" 뚱뚱한 소년이, 주인이 멱살을 잡자 무릎을 꿇으며 말했다. \"전 안 취했어요.\"\n\n\"그럼 미친 게로구나. 그게 더 나쁘지. 종업원들을 불러라.\" 노신사가 말했다.\n\n\"전 안 미쳤어요. 제정신이라고요.\" 뚱뚱한 소년이 울먹이기 시작하며 응수했다.\n\n\"그럼 도대체 왜 픽윅 씨 다리에 뾰족한 도구를 찔러 댔단 말이냐?\" 와들이 화를 내며 물었다.\n\n\"저분이 절 안 봐 주셔서요.\" 소년이 대답했다. \"저분께 말씀드리고 싶었거든요.\"\n\n\"무슨 말을 하고 싶었던 거냐?\" 대여섯 사람의 목소리가 한꺼번에 물었다.\n\n뚱뚱한 소년은 숨을 헐떡이며 침실 문을 바라보고, 다시 헐떡이고는, 양 검지의 손가락 마디로 눈물 두 방울을 닦았다.\n\n\"무슨 말을 하고 싶었던 거냐?\" 와들이 그를 흔들며 다그쳤다.\n\n\"잠깐!\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"제게 맡기십시오. 내게 무슨 이야기를 전하고 싶었니, 가엾은 아이야?\"\n\n\"속삭여서 말씀드리고 싶어요.\" 뚱뚱한 소년이 대답했다.\n\n\"이분 귀를 물어뜯고 싶다는 거겠지.\" 와들이 말했다. \"저 녀석 가까이 가지 마십시오. 사나운 녀석이니까. 종을 울려서 아래층으로 끌고 가게 합시다.\"\n\n윈클 씨가 막 종 줄을 손에 쥐려던 바로 그 순간, 그 손길이 일제히 터진 경악의 외침에 멈춰졌다. 갇혀 있던 연인이, 당혹감으로 얼굴이 벌겋게 달아오른 채로, 침실에서 불쑥 걸어 나와 일동에게 두루 절을 한 것이다.\n\n\"이런!\" 와들이 뚱뚱한 소년의 멱살을 놓고 비틀거리며 뒤로 물러나며 외쳤다. \"이게 무슨 일인가?\"\n\n\"선생님이 돌아오신 뒤로 옆방에 숨어 있었습니다.\" 스노드그래스 씨가 해명했다.\n\n\"에밀리, 얘야.\" 와들이 나무라듯 말했다. \"난 비열함과 기만을 몹시 싫어한다. 이건 도무지 변명의 여지가 없고, 더없이 점잖지 못한 짓이야. 난 네게서 이런 일을 당할 사람이 아니야, 에밀리, 정말이지!\"\n\n\"아빠.\" 에밀리가 말했다. \"아라벨라도 알아요—여기 있는 모두가 알아요—조도 알고요—이 숨긴 일에 제가 가담하지 않았다는 걸요. 오거스터스, 제발 좀 설명해 줘요!\"\n\n그저 발언할 기회만 기다리고 있던 스노드그래스 씨는, 곧장 자신이 어떻게 그 곤혹스러운 처지에 놓이게 되었는지를 죽 이야기했다. 집안의 불화를 일으킬까 하는 두려움 하나 때문에 와들 씨가 들어올 때 그를 피했던 것이며, 그저 다른 문으로 나가려 했는데 그 문이 잠겨 있는 바람에 본의 아니게 머물 수밖에 없었던 것이라고. 처하기에 괴로운 상황이긴 했으나, 지금은 그것을 그리 후회하지 않는다고 했다. 왜냐하면 그것이 그에게, 서로의 친구들 앞에서, 자신이 와들 씨의 따님을 깊고 진실하게 사랑한다는 것을 고백할 기회를 주었기 때문이라고. 그 마음이 서로 통하는 것임을 자랑스럽게 밝히는 바이며, 설령 수천 마일이 두 사람 사이에 가로놓이거나 대양이 그 물결을 굽이치게 한다 해도, 처음으로—기타 등등, 기타 등등 했던 그 행복한 나날들을 한순간도 결코 잊을 수 없으리라고.\n\n이런 취지로 자기 심정을 토로하고 나서, 스노드그래스 씨는 다시 절을 하고, 모자의 꼭대기 속을 들여다보고는, 문 쪽으로 걸음을 옮겼다.\n\n\"멈추게!\" 와들이 외쳤다. \"아니, 도대체 무엇의 이름으로—\"\n\n\"불같은 것의.\" 픽윅 씨가, 무언가 더 험한 말이 나올까 봐 부드럽게 거들어 주었다.\n\n\"음—불같은 것의 이름으로 말일세.\" 와들이 그 대용어를 받아들이며 말했다. \"이 모든 걸 애초에 나한테 말할 수는 없었나?\"\n\n\"아니면 제게 털어놓든지요?\" 픽윅 씨가 덧붙였다.\n\n\"어머, 어머.\" 아라벨라가 변호에 나서며 말했다. \"지금 와서 그런 걸 다 따져 무슨 소용이에요, 더구나 당신의 그 욕심 많은 늙은 마음이 더 부유한 사위에게 가 있었던 걸 알면서, 게다가 어찌나 거칠고 사나운지 저 빼고는 모두가 무서워하는 분이면서요? 이분과 악수하고, 저녁이라도 좀 시켜 주세요, 제발 좀요, 이분 반쯤 굶주린 얼굴이잖아요. 그리고 포도주도 어서 올려 오라고 하세요, 적어도 두 병은 드셔야 봐 줄 만해지실 테니까요.\"\n\n이 갸륵한 노신사는 아라벨라의 귀를 잡아당기고, 조금의 거리낌도 없이 그녀에게 입을 맞추고, 제 딸에게도 더없는 애정으로 입을 맞춘 뒤, 스노드그래스 씨의 손을 따뜻하게 잡고 흔들었다.\n\n\"적어도 한 가지 점에서는 저 애 말이 옳아.\" 노신사가 유쾌하게 말했다. \"포도주를 가져오라고 종을 울려라!\"\n\n포도주가 왔고, 바로 그 순간 퍼커가 위층으로 올라왔다. 스노드그래스 씨는 곁상에서 저녁을 들었고, 다 먹고 나자 노신사 쪽의 조금의 반대도 없이 의자를 에밀리 곁으로 끌어다 놓았다.\n\n저녁 시간은 더없이 훌륭했다. 자그마한 퍼커 씨는 놀랍도록 흥이 올라, 갖가지 우스운 이야기를 들려주고, 그 일화들 못지않게 우스운 진지한 노래 한 곡을 불렀다. 아라벨라는 무척 사랑스러웠고, 와들 씨는 무척 유쾌했고, 픽윅 씨는 무척 조화로웠고, 벤 앨런 씨는 무척 떠들썩했고, 연인들은 무척 말이 없었고, 윈클 씨는 무척 수다스러웠으며, 그들 모두는 무척 행복했다.\n\n\"새뮤얼,\" 장례식 다음 날 아침, 웰러 씨가 아들에게 말을 건넸다. \"그걸 찾았다, 새미. 거기 있을 줄 알았어.\"\n\n\"뭐가 거기 있을 줄 알았다고요?\" 샘이 물었다.\n\n\"네 새어머니의 유언장 말이다, 새미,\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다. \"그게 있어야 어젯밤에 내가 너한테 말한 그 자금 처리 일을 손볼 수 있는 거 아니냐.\"\n\n\"아니, 그게 어디 있는지 새어머니가 말 안 해 줬어요?\" 샘이 물었다.\n\n\"한마디도 없었다, 새미,\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다. \"우리는 우리끼리 작은 다툼을 풀고 있었고, 나는 그 사람 기운을 북돋아 주고 받쳐 주느라 그것에 대해 물어볼 생각조차 못 했지. 사실 기억했더라도 물어봤을지는 모르겠다만,\" 웰러 씨가 덧붙였다. \"새미, 누군가 병들어 도와주는 마당에 그 재산을 탐내고 돌아다니는 건 영 고약한 짓이거든. 마차에서 떨어진 바깥 승객을 일으켜 세워 주는 척하면서 한숨 쉬며 '괜찮으시오?' 하고 묻는 동안 그자 주머니에 손을 집어넣는 거나 마찬가지지, 새미.\"\n\n이렇게 자기 뜻을 비유적으로 설명한 뒤, 웰러 씨는 수첩을 펼쳐 더러운 편지지 한 장을 끄집어냈는데, 거기에는 갖가지 글자가 놀랄 만큼 어지럽게 뒤엉켜 빽빽하게 적혀 있었다.\n\n\"이게 바로 그 문서다, 새미,\" 웰러 씨가 말했다. \"술집 벽장 맨 위 선반에 있던 작은 검은 찻주전자 속에서 찾았다. 결혼하기 전엔 거기다 지폐를 넣어 두곤 했지, 새뮤얼. 계산하려고 뚜껑 여는 걸 내가 여러 번, 여러 번 봤다. 가엾은 사람, 집 안의 찻주전자란 찻주전자를 죄다 유언장으로 채워 놨어도 조금도 불편하지 않았을 게다. 요즘 들어선 그쪽 방면으로 뭐 그리 많이 마시지도 않았으니까, 금주의 밤만 빼고 말이지. 그날만은 차로 기초를 깔고 그 위에 독한 술을 부어 올렸거든!\"\n\n\"뭐라고 적혀 있는데요?\" 샘이 물었다.\n\n\"내가 너한테 말한 그대로다, 얘야,\" 그의 아버지가 대답했다. \"'감액 공채 이백 파운드어치를 내 사위 새뮤얼에게, 그 밖의 온갖 종류와 명목의 내 모든 재산은 내 남편 토니 웰러 씨에게 남기며, 그를 나의 유일한 유언 집행인으로 임명한다.'\"\n\n\"그게 전부예요?\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n\"그게 전부다,\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다. \"그래서 말인데, 이해 당사자가 너하고 나뿐이고 다 옳고 만족스러우니, 이 종잇조각은 불 속에 던져 버려도 무방할 게다.\"\n\n\"미친 영감님, 지금 뭐 하시는 거예요?\" 샘이 종이를 낚아채며 말했다. 그의 아버지가 천연덕스럽게 말한 대로 행하려고 불을 휘젓고 있던 참이었다. \"유언 집행인 한번 훌륭하시네요, 정말.\"\n\n\"왜 안 되는데?\" 웰러 씨가 부지깽이를 손에 든 채 엄하게 둘러보며 물었다.\n\n\"왜 안 되냐고요?\" 샘이 외쳤다. \"검인을 받고, 증명하고, 선서하고, 온갖 격식을 다 거쳐야 하니까요.\"\n\n\"그게 정말이냐?\" 웰러 씨가 부지깽이를 내려놓으며 말했다.\n\n샘은 유언장을 옆주머니에 조심스레 단추까지 채워 넣었다. 그러면서 눈빛으로, 자기 말이 진심이며 그것도 아주 진지한 진심임을 내비쳤다.\n\n\"그렇다면 내 말해 두마,\" 잠시 생각에 잠겼다가 웰러 씨가 말했다. \"이건 그 대법관님과 친한 양반한테 맡길 일이다. 펠이 이걸 살펴봐야 해, 새미. 그 사람이 까다로운 법률 문제엔 적임자지. 이걸 곧장 파산 법원에 올려야겠다, 새뮤얼.\"\n\n\"이렇게 머리가 멍청한 영감은 또 처음 보네!\" 샘이 짜증을 내며 외쳤다. \"올드 베일리니, 파산 법원이니, 현장 부재 증명이니, 온갖 헛소리가 늘 머릿속을 휘젓고 다닌다니까. 자기가 아무것도 모르는 일을 가지고 거기 서서 설교하느니, 외출복이나 챙겨 입고 이 일로 시내에 가는 게 낫겠어요.\"\n\n\"아주 좋다, 새미,\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다. \"일을 신속하게 처리하는 일이라면 나는 뭐든 찬성이다, 새미. 하지만 이거 하난 명심해라, 얘야. 펠 말고는 아무도 안 돼. 법률 자문은 펠 말고는 누구도 안 돼.\"\n\n\"저도 다른 사람은 필요 없어요,\" 샘이 대답했다. \"자, 이제 가시겠어요?\"\n\n\"잠깐 기다려라, 새미,\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다. 창가에 걸린 작은 거울의 도움을 받아 목도리를 매고 난 그는, 이제 놀라울 만큼 안간힘을 쓰며 윗옷 속으로 몸을 비집어 넣느라 씨름하고 있었다. \"잠깐만 기다려라, 새미. 네가 네 아비만큼 나이가 들면, 지금처럼 조끼에 쉽게 들어가지지가 않는단다, 얘야.\"\n\n\"그렇게 들어가는 것보다 더 쉽게 안 들어간다면, 차라리 조끼 따위 아예 안 입고 말겠어요,\" 그의 아들이 대꾸했다.\n\n\"지금이야 그렇게 생각하겠지,\" 웰러 씨가 노인다운 진중함으로 말했다. \"허나 몸이 넓어질수록 지혜도 넓어진다는 걸 알게 될 게다. 넓이와 지혜는 말이다, 새미, 늘 함께 자라는 법이야.\"\n\n웰러 씨가 여러 해에 걸친 몸소의 경험과 관찰의 결실인 이 틀림없는 격언을 늘어놓는 동안, 그는 몸을 능숙하게 비틀어 마침내 외투의 맨 아래 단추가 제 구실을 하도록 만들었다. 몇 초간 숨을 고른 뒤, 그는 팔꿈치로 모자를 쓱쓱 닦고는 준비가 됐다고 선언했다.\n\n\"머리 넷이 둘보다 나으니까, 새미,\" 그들이 이륜마차를 타고 런던 가도를 달리는 동안 웰러 씨가 말했다. \"또 이 재산이란 게 법률 양반한테는 엄청난 유혹이니까, 내 친구 두엇을 데려가자꾸나. 그자가 조금이라도 수상한 짓을 하면 곧장 덮칠 친구들이지. 그날 너를 플리트까지 바래다준 그 두 사람 말이다. 그 친구들이야말로 최고의 감정가들이거든,\" 웰러 씨가 반쯤 속삭이며 덧붙였다. \"네가 아는 한 가장 뛰어난 말 감정가들이지.\"\n\n\"변호사 감정도요?\" 샘이 물었다.\n\n\"짐승을 정확하게 판단할 줄 아는 사람은 뭐든 정확하게 판단할 줄 아는 법이다,\" 그의 아버지가 어찌나 단정적으로 대답하던지, 샘은 그 주장을 반박하려 들지 않았다.\n\n이 훌륭한 결심을 좇아, 얼룩진 얼굴의 신사와 다른 살집 좋은 마부 둘—아마도 웰러 씨가 그들의 넓이와, 따라서 거기서 비롯되는 지혜를 보고 골랐을 것이다—의 협력을 요청했다. 이 지원을 확보한 일행은 포르투갈 가의 선술집으로 향했고, 거기서 길 건너 파산 법원으로 심부름꾼을 보내 솔로몬 펠 씨가 즉시 와 줄 것을 청했다.\n\n심부름꾼은 다행히도 펠 씨가 법정 안에 있는 것을 찾아냈다. 일감이 다소 한산한 터라, 그는 애버네시 비스킷과 소시지로 된 간단한 찬 음식으로 요기를 하고 있었다. 그 전갈이 귓속에 속삭여지자마자 그는 그것들을 갖가지 직무용 서류 사이에 끼워 주머니에 쑤셔 넣고는, 어찌나 날쌔게 길을 건너왔는지 심부름꾼이 법정에서 채 빠져나오기도 전에 응접실에 닿았다.\n\n\"여러분,\" 펠 씨가 모자에 손을 대며 말했다. \"모두에게 인사 올립니다. 아첨하려고 하는 말은 아닙니다만, 여러분, 오늘 제가 저 법정에서 나올 만한 사람은 세상에 다섯 명도 안 됩니다.\"\n\n\"그렇게 바쁘셨소, 응?\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n\"바쁘냐고요!\" 펠 씨가 대답했다. \"완전히 녹초가 되었지요. 고인이 되신 제 친구 대법관님께서 상원에서 항소심을 듣고 나오실 때마다 저한테 여러 번 하시던 말씀입니다, 여러분. 가엾은 양반, 피로에 무척 약하셨지요. 그 항소심들을 유독 힘들어하셨습니다. 저는 솔직히 한두 번 이상, 그분이 그 일에 짓눌려 쓰러지실 거라고 생각했습니다. 정말이지요.\"\n\n여기서 펠 씨가 고개를 젓고 말을 멈추자, 웰러 영감은 이웃한 친구를 슬쩍 찔러 그 변호사의 높은 인맥을 눈여겨보라는 듯이 하고는, 그 직무가 그분 고귀한 친구의 체질에 어떤 영구적인 해를 끼쳤는지 물었다.\n\n\"그분이 거기서 완전히 회복하신 적은 없는 것 같습니다,\" 펠 씨가 대답했다. \"아니, 결코 회복하지 못하셨다고 확신합니다. '펠,' 그분이 저한테 여러 번 말씀하셨지요. '자네가 그 골치 아픈 머리 일을 어떻게 견뎌 내는지 나로선 도무지 알 수가 없네.' '글쎄요,' 제가 대답하곤 했습니다. '제가 어떻게 해내는지 저도 잘 모르겠습니다, 정말로.' '펠,' 그분은 한숨을 쉬며, 약간의 부러움—친근한 부러움이지요, 여러분, 그저 친근한 부러움 말입니다. 저는 한 번도 개의치 않았습니다만—그런 눈으로 저를 보시며 덧붙이셨지요. '펠, 자네는 경이야. 경이로운 사람이야.' 아! 여러분이 그분을 아셨더라면 무척 좋아하셨을 겁니다, 여러분. 럼주 삼 펜스어치만 가져다주게, 아가씨.\"\n\n이 마지막 말을 웨이트리스에게 가라앉은 슬픔의 어조로 건넨 뒤, 펠 씨는 한숨을 내쉬며 자기 구두와 천장을 바라보았다. 그러는 사이 럼주가 도착하자 그것을 들이켰다.\n\n\"하지만,\" 펠 씨가 의자를 탁자 쪽으로 끌어당기며 말했다. \"법조인이란 법률적 도움이 필요한 마당에 사사로운 친분을 생각해서는 안 되는 법이지요. 그건 그렇고, 여러분, 지난번 여기서 뵌 뒤로 우리는 참으로 애통한 일을 두고 눈물을 흘려야 했지요.\"\n\n펠 씨는 '눈물'이라는 말에 이르자 손수건을 꺼냈으나, 그것을 윗입술에 묻은 약간의 럼주 자국을 닦는 데밖에 더 쓰지는 않았다.\n\n\"광고지에서 봤습니다, 웰러 씨,\" 펠 씨가 말을 이었다. \"맙소사, 겨우 쉰둘이라니! 저런—생각 좀 해 보십시오.\"\n\n이 사색에 잠긴 듯한 표현은 얼룩진 얼굴의 사내를 향한 것이었는데, 펠 씨가 우연히 그의 눈을 마주친 까닭이었다. 그러자 매사에 대한 이해가 안개처럼 흐릿한 그 얼룩진 얼굴의 사내는 자리에서 거북하게 몸을 움직이더니, 정말이지 그 점에 관한 한 일이 어떻게 되는 건지는 누구도 알 수 없는 노릇이라는 의견을 내놓았다. 이 발언은 논쟁에서 맞서기 어려운 그 미묘한 명제들 가운데 하나를 담고 있던 터라, 아무도 반박하지 않았다.\n\n\"고인이 무척 미인이셨다는 이야기를 들었습니다, 웰러 씨,\" 펠 씨가 동정 어린 태도로 말했다.\n\n\"그렇소, 그랬지요,\" 웰러 영감이 대답했다. 이런 식으로 그 화제를 논하는 것이 그리 달갑지는 않았으나, 그래도 그 변호사가 고인이 된 대법관과 오래 친분을 쌓은 사람이니 점잖은 예법에 관한 모든 일에서 가장 잘 알 거라고 여겼다. \"내가 처음 알았을 적엔 무척 고운 여자였소. 그땐 과부였지요, 선생.\"\n\n\"그것참 묘하군요,\" 펠 씨가 서글픈 미소를 지으며 둘러보았다. \"펠 부인도 과부였지요.\"\n\n\"그것참 신기한 일이네요,\" 얼룩진 얼굴의 사내가 말했다.\n\n\"그래요, 묘한 우연이지요,\" 펠 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"전혀 안 그렇소,\" 웰러 영감이 무뚝뚝하게 한마디 했다. \"처녀보다 과부가 더 많이 시집가니까.\"\n\n\"좋습니다, 좋습니다,\" 펠 씨가 말했다. \"지당하신 말씀입니다, 웰러 씨. 펠 부인은 무척 우아하고 교양 있는 여자였지요. 그 사람의 몸가짐은 우리 동네에서 누구나 칭송하던 화제였습니다. 저는 그 사람이 춤추는 걸 보는 게 자랑스러웠지요. 그 동작에는 어딘가 그토록 단단하고 기품 있으면서도 자연스러운 데가 있었습니다. 그 사람의 스텝은 말이지요, 여러분, 단순함 그 자체였습니다. 아! 그래요, 그래요! 이런 걸 여쭤서 죄송합니다만, 새뮤얼 씨,\" 변호사가 목소리를 낮추어 말을 이었다. \"댁의 새어머니는 키가 크셨습니까?\"\n\n\"별로 안 컸소,\" 샘이 대답했다.\n\n\"펠 부인은 키가 컸지요,\" 펠 씨가 말했다. \"고귀한 몸매에, 명령하고 위엄을 떨치도록 빚어진 코를 가진, 멋진 여자였습니다, 여러분. 그 사람은 저를 무척 따랐지요—무척이나—집안도 훌륭했고요. 그 사람 어머니의 오라비는 말이지요, 여러분, 법률 문구상을 하다가 팔백 파운드 빚을 지고 망했답니다.\"\n\n\"자,\" 이 논의가 이어지는 동안 다소 안절부절못하던 웰러 씨가 말했다. \"이제 일 얘기로 갑시다.\"\n\n그 말은 펠 씨의 귀에 음악과도 같았다. 그는 정말로 처리할 일거리가 있는 건지, 아니면 그저 브랜디에 물 탄 술 한 잔이나 펀치 한 사발, 혹은 그와 비슷한 직무상의 호의를 함께하자고 초대받은 것뿐인지 머릿속으로 곱씹던 참이었는데, 이제 그 의문이, 그가 굳이 답을 갈망하는 기색을 전혀 내비치지 않고도 풀린 것이었다. 그는 모자를 탁자에 내려놓으며, 눈을 반짝이며 말했다.\n\n\"무슨 일로—흠? 이분들 가운데 누가 법원을 거쳐야 할 일이 있으신지요? 우리에겐 체포가 필요합니다. 형식적인 체포면 됩니다, 아시지요. 여기 다들 친구들이지 않습니까, 그렇지요?\"\n\n\"그 문서를 이리 다오, 새미,\" 웰러 씨가 아들에게서 유언장을 받아 들며 말했다. 아들은 이 면담을 무척이나 즐기는 듯 보였다. \"우리가 필요한 건 말이오, 선생, 이것의 시험이오.\"\n\n\"검인입니다, 선생, 검인이지요,\" 펠 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"글쎄, 선생,\" 웰러 씨가 날카롭게 대꾸했다. \"시험이든 검인이든 거기서 거기지. 내가 무슨 말 하는지 못 알아들으시겠다면, 알아듣는 사람을 내 찾아낼 수 있을 거요.\"\n\n\"기분 상하게 할 생각은 없었습니다, 웰러 씨,\" 펠 씨가 온순하게 말했다. \"보아하니 당신이 유언 집행인이시군요,\" 그가 종이를 훑어보며 덧붙였다.\n\n\"그렇소, 선생,\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"여기 다른 분들은, 제 짐작에, 유산 수령인이신가요?\" 펠 씨가 축하하는 듯한 미소를 띠며 물었다.\n\n\"새미가 다리 편한 수령인이오,\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다. \"여기 다른 양반들은 내 친구들인데, 그저 공정하게 진행되나 보러 온 거요. 일종의 심판이라고나 할까.\"\n\n\"오!\" 펠 씨가 말했다. \"아주 좋습니다. 저야 물론 이의 없습니다. 시작하기 전에 댁에게서 오 파운드쯤 받아야겠군요, 하! 하! 하!\"\n\n위원회에서 그 오 파운드를 선불해도 되겠다고 결정하자, 웰러 씨가 그 금액을 내놓았다. 그러고 나서 별것도 아닌 일을 두고 긴 협의가 이어졌는데, 그 과정에서 펠 씨는, 이 일의 처리가 자신에게 맡겨지지 않았더라면 무슨 까닭에선지—명확히 밝혀지지는 않았으나 분명 충분한 까닭에서—죄다 어긋나 버렸을 것임을, 공정한 진행을 지켜보던 신사들이 완전히 만족할 만큼 입증해 보였다. 이 중대한 점이 처리되자, 펠 씨는 유산의 비용으로 돼지갈비 세 점과 맥아주와 독주를 곁들여 원기를 회복했다. 그런 뒤 일행은 모두 닥터스 코먼스로 떠났다.\n\n이튿날 닥터스 코먼스를 또 한 차례 방문했고, 증인으로 나선 마부 때문에 한바탕 소동이 벌어졌다. 그자는 취한 상태라 불경한 욕설 외에는 어떤 선서도 하지 않으려 들어, 대리인과 대리 판사를 크게 곤혹스럽게 만들었다. 그다음 주에는 닥터스 코먼스를 더 여러 번 방문했고, 그 밖에 유산세 사무소에도 한 차례 들렀으며, 임차권과 영업권 처분을 위한 계약이 체결되고 그것이 비준되었으며, 재산 목록이 작성되어야 했고, 점심을 들고, 저녁을 먹고, 이득이 될 만한 갖가지 일들을 처리해야 했으며, 또 어찌나 많은 서류가 쌓였는지, 솔로몬 펠 씨와 사환과 거기에 푸른 가방까지 죄다 어찌나 살이 올랐던지, 며칠 전 포르투갈 가를 어슬렁거리던 그 사람, 그 소년, 그 가방인 줄을 누구도 거의 알아보지 못할 지경이었다.\n\n마침내 이 모든 막중한 일들이 정리되자, 공채를 매각하고 자산을 이전하는 날이 정해졌고, 그 일을 위해 윌킨스 플래셔라는 신사를 찾아가기로 했다. 그는 은행 근처 어딘가에 있는 증권 중개인으로, 솔로몬 펠 씨가 그 일에 추천한 사람이었다.\n\n그것은 일종의 축제 같은 행사였고, 일행은 그에 걸맞게 차려입었다. 웰러 씨의 장화는 새로 닦였고, 옷차림은 각별히 신경 써서 갖추어졌다. 얼룩진 얼굴의 신사는 단춧구멍에 잎이 여러 장 달린 큼지막한 달리아 한 송이를 꽂았고, 그의 두 친구의 외투에는 월계수와 그 밖의 상록수 꽃다발이 장식되어 있었다. 세 사람 모두 엄격한 명절 차림을 하고 있었으니, 다시 말해 턱까지 칭칭 싸매고 옷이란 옷은 되도록 많이 껴입었는데, 이는 역마차가 발명된 이래로 마부들이 정장이라고 여겨 온, 또 여전히 여기는 그것이었다.\n\n펠 씨는 정해진 시각에 늘 만나던 장소에서 기다리고 있었다. 그조차도 장갑을 끼고 깨끗한 셔츠를 입고 있었는데, 그 셔츠는 잦은 세탁으로 깃과 소맷동이 몹시 닳아 있었다.\n\n\"두 시 십오 분 전이군요,\" 펠 씨가 응접실 시계를 보며 말했다. \"십오 분에 플래셔 씨 댁에 닿으면 마침 딱 좋은 때를 맞추는 셈입니다.\"\n\n\"여러분, 맥주 한잔 어떻겠소?\" 얼룩진 얼굴의 사내가 제안했다.\n\n\"찬 쇠고기도 조금 곁들이고,\" 두 번째 마부가 말했다.\n\n\"아니면 굴이라도,\" 세 번째 사내가 덧붙였다. 그는 목소리가 쉰 신사로, 몹시 둥근 다리로 몸을 받치고 있었다.\n\n\"옳소, 옳소!\" 펠 씨가 말했다. \"웰러 씨가 재산을 손에 넣으신 걸 축하하는 뜻으로, 응? 하! 하!\"\n\n\"나야 얼마든지 찬성이오, 여러분,\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다. \"새미, 종을 울려라.\"\n\n새미가 그대로 따랐고, 흑맥주와 찬 쇠고기와 굴이 즉시 차려지자 점심은 충분히 대접받았다. 모두가 그토록 적극적으로 가담한 마당에 우열을 가리는 것은 거의 부당한 일이겠으나, 굳이 누가 남보다 더 큰 역량을 보였다고 한다면, 그것은 목소리가 쉰 마부였으니, 그는 굴에 식초를 한 임페리얼 파인트나 곁들이면서도 조금의 동요도 내비치지 않았다.\n\n\"펠 씨,\" 굴 껍데기가 치워지자 신사마다 앞에 놓인 브랜디에 물 탄 술 한 잔을 휘저으며 웰러 영감이 말했다. \"펠 씨, 이 자리에서 자금 얘기를 꺼내려 했소만, 새뮤얼이 나한테 귀띔하기를—\"\n\n여기서, 잔잔한 미소를 지으며 잠자코 굴을 먹고 있던 새뮤얼 웰러 씨가 아주 우렁찬 목소리로 \"옳소!\" 하고 외쳤다.\n\n\"—나한테 귀띔하기를,\" 그의 아버지가 말을 이었다. \"이 술은 차라리 당신의 성공과 번영을 비는 데, 그리고 이 일을 이렇게 잘 처리해 준 것에 감사하는 데 바치는 게 낫겠다더군. 당신의 건강을 위하여, 선생.\"\n\n\"잠깐 거기 멈추시오,\" 얼룩진 얼굴의 신사가 별안간 기운차게 끼어들었다. \"여러분, 나를 보시오!\"\n\n이렇게 말하며 얼룩진 얼굴의 신사가 일어서자, 다른 신사들도 일어섰다. 얼룩진 얼굴의 신사는 좌중을 둘러보고는 천천히 손을 들어 올렸고, 그러자 모든 사람이(얼룩진 얼굴의 그자까지 포함하여) 길게 숨을 들이쉬며 잔을 입술로 가져갔다. 순식간에 얼룩진 얼굴의 신사가 손을 다시 내리자, 잔이란 잔은 모두 빈 채로 내려놓였다. 이 인상적인 의식이 자아낸 짜릿한 효과는 이루 말로 다 할 수 없다. 동시에 위엄 있고, 엄숙하고, 감명 깊은 그것은 장엄함의 온갖 요소를 두루 갖추고 있었다.\n\n\"자, 여러분,\" 펠 씨가 말했다. \"제가 드릴 수 있는 말씀은, 이런 신임의 표시는 법조인에게 무척이나 흐뭇한 일이라는 것뿐입니다. 자만하는 것처럼 들릴 소리는 하고 싶지 않습니다, 여러분, 하지만 여러분 자신을 위해서라도 저를 찾아오신 게 정말 다행입니다. 그게 다입니다. 만일 이 직종의 하찮은 자에게 가셨더라면, 이건 제 굳은 확신이고 또 사실로서 장담드립니다만, 여러분은 지금쯤 곤경에 빠지셨을 겁니다. 저는 제 고귀한 친구분이 살아 계셔서 제가 이 사건을 처리하는 솜씨를 보셨더라면 좋았을 거라고 생각합니다. 자랑삼아 하는 말은 아닙니다만, 제 생각엔—하지만 여러분, 그 얘기로 폐를 끼치지는 않겠습니다. 저는 대개 여기서 만나실 수 있습니다, 여러분, 그래도 여기나 길 건너에 없거든, 그게 제 주소입니다. 제 보수는 무척 싸고 합당하다는 걸 아시게 될 겁니다. 또 저만큼 의뢰인에게 마음을 쓰는 사람도 없고요. 그 밖에도 제 직무에 대해서는 제법 알고 있다고 자부합니다. 혹 여러분의 벗들 가운데 누구에게라도 저를 추천해 주실 기회가 있다면, 여러분, 저는 무척 감사하겠고, 그분들도 저를 알게 되면 마찬가지로 고마워할 겁니다. 여러분의 건강을 위하여.\"\n\n이렇게 자기 심정을 토로한 뒤, 솔로몬 펠 씨는 글씨가 적힌 작은 명함 석 장을 웰러 씨의 친구들 앞에 내려놓고는, 다시 시계를 보더니 그만 걸음을 옮길 때가 된 듯하다고 했다. 이 암시에 따라 웰러 씨가 셈을 치렀고, 밖으로 나선 유언 집행인과 유산 수령인과 변호사와 심판들은 시내 쪽으로 발걸음을 향했다.\n\n증권 거래소의 윌킨스 플래셔 신사의 사무소는 잉글랜드 은행 뒤편 골목 안 어느 건물 이 층에 있었다. 윌킨스 플래셔 신사의 집은 서리 주 브릭스턴에 있었다. 윌킨스 플래셔 신사의 말과 이륜마차는 가까운 대여 마구간에 있었다. 윌킨스 플래셔 신사의 마부는 사냥감 고기를 배달하러 웨스트 엔드로 가는 길이었다. 윌킨스 플래셔 신사의 사무원은 점심을 먹으러 갔다. 그래서 펠 씨와 그 일행이 사무소 문을 두드렸을 때, 윌킨스 플래셔 신사 본인이 \"들어오시오\"라고 외쳤다.\n\n\"안녕하십니까, 선생,\" 펠 씨가 굽실거리며 인사했다. \"괜찮으시다면 자산 이전을 좀 하고 싶습니다.\"\n\n\"오, 어서 들어오시지요?\" 플래셔 씨가 말했다. \"잠깐 앉으세요. 곧 봐 드리겠습니다.\"\n\n\"고맙습니다, 선생,\" 펠 씨가 말했다. \"급할 것 없습니다. 의자에 앉으시지요, 웰러 씨.\"\n\n웰러 씨는 의자에 앉았고, 샘은 상자에 걸터앉았으며, 심판들은 앉을 만한 데를 골라 앉아서는, 벽에 풀로 붙여 놓은 달력과 종이 한두 장을 마치 옛 거장들의 더없이 훌륭한 역작이라도 되는 양 눈을 휘둥그레 뜨고 경건하게 바라보았다.\n\n\"좋아, 클라레 여섯 병 걸겠어. 어때!\" 윌킨스 플래셔 신사가 말했다. 펠 씨의 등장으로 잠시 끊겼던 대화를 다시 이어 가는 참이었다.\n\n이 말은 무척 말쑥한 젊은 신사에게 건넨 것이었는데, 그자는 모자를 오른쪽 구레나룻에 얹은 채 책상에 늘어져서 자로 파리를 때려잡고 있었다. 윌킨스 플래셔 신사는 사무용 걸상의 두 다리로 몸을 가누며, 작은 칼로 풀 상자를 콕콕 찌르고 있었는데, 이따금 그 칼을 떨어뜨려 바깥에 붙은 작은 빨간 봉인지의 한가운데에 정확히 꽂아 넣는 솜씨가 여간 능숙하지 않았다. 두 신사 모두 조끼를 무척 헤쳐 입었고, 깃을 잔뜩 늘어뜨렸으며, 무척 작은 부츠에, 무척 큼지막한 반지에, 무척 자그마한 시계에, 무척 큼직한 시곗줄에, 균형 잡힌 바지에, 향수 뿌린 손수건까지 갖추고 있었다.\n\n\"난 여섯 병으로는 안 걸어!\" 다른 신사가 말했다. \"열두 병으로 받지.\"\n\n\"좋아, 시머리, 좋아!\" 윌킨스 플래셔 신사가 말했다.\n\n\"잊지 마, 무를 수 없는 거야,\" 상대가 일렀다.\n\n\"물론이지,\" 윌킨스 플래셔 신사가 대답했다. 윌킨스 플래셔 신사는 금 연필갑을 꺼내 작은 수첩에 그것을 적었고, 다른 신사도 또 다른 금 연필갑을 꺼내 또 다른 작은 수첩에 그것을 적었다.\n\n\"오늘 아침에 보퍼 건으로 공고가 났던데,\" 시머리 씨가 말했다. \"불쌍한 녀석, 거래소에서 제명됐어!\"\n\n\"열 기니 대 다섯 기니로 걸지. 그자가 제 목을 그을 거라고,\" 윌킨스 플래셔 신사가 말했다.\n\n\"좋아,\" 시머리 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"잠깐! 그건 안 돼,\" 윌킨스 플래셔 신사가 곰곰이 말했다. \"어쩌면 목을 맬지도 모르잖아.\"\n\n\"아주 좋아,\" 시머리 씨가 다시 금 연필갑을 꺼내며 대꾸했다. \"그쪽으로 받아도 난 이의 없어. 그럼 '스스로 목숨을 끊는다'로 하지.\"\n\n\"요컨대 자살한다는 거지,\" 윌킨스 플래셔 신사가 말했다.\n\n\"바로 그거야,\" 시머리 씨가 그것을 적으며 대답했다. \"'플래셔—열 기니 대 다섯 기니, 보퍼가 자살한다.' 기한은 얼마로 할까?\"\n\n\"이 주일?\" 윌킨스 플래셔 신사가 제안했다.\n\n\"제기랄, 안 돼,\" 시머리 씨가 잠시 멈추고 자로 파리를 때려잡으며 대꾸했다. \"일주일로 하지.\"\n\n\"중간으로 하자,\" 윌킨스 플래셔 신사가 말했다. \"열흘로 해.\"\n\n\"좋아, 열흘이야,\" 시머리 씨가 대꾸했다.\n\n그리하여 작은 수첩들에 이렇게 적혔다. 보퍼가 열흘 안에 자살하든가, 아니면 윌킨스 플래셔 신사가 프랭크 시머리 신사에게 열 기니를 건네야 한다고. 그리고 보퍼가 그 기한 안에 정말로 자살하면, 그 대신 프랭크 시머리 신사가 윌킨스 플래셔 신사에게 다섯 기니를 치러야 한다고.\n\n\"그자가 망한 건 참 안됐어,\" 윌킨스 플래셔 신사가 말했다. \"기막힌 만찬을 베풀었었는데.\"\n\n\"포트와인도 훌륭한 게 있었지,\" 시머리 씨가 한마디 했다. \"우리 집은 내일 그 집사 사람을 경매에 보내서 그 육사년산을 좀 사 올 참이야.\"\n\n\"설마 그래?\" 윌킨스 플래셔 신사가 말했다. \"우리 사람도 갈 거야. 우리 사람이 자네 사람보다 높이 부르는 데 다섯 기니 걸지.\"\n\n\"좋아.\"\n\n작은 수첩들에 금 연필갑으로 또 한 줄이 적혔다. 그러는 사이 파리를 죄다 잡고 내기도 죄다 건 시머리 씨는, 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있나 보러 증권 거래소로 어슬렁어슬렁 떠났다.\n\n이제 윌킨스 플래셔 신사는 솔로몬 펠 씨의 지시를 받는 데로 너그러이 마음을 돌려, 인쇄된 양식 몇 장을 작성하고는 일행더러 자기를 따라 은행으로 가자고 청했고, 일행은 그대로 따랐다. 웰러 씨와 그의 세 친구는 눈에 보이는 모든 것을 끝없는 경탄에 차서 멀거니 바라보았고, 샘은 무엇에도 흔들리지 않는 침착함으로 매사에 맞섰다.\n\n온통 시끄럽고 부산한 안뜰을 가로지르고, 한쪽 구석으로 밀어 둔 빨간 소방차에 맞춰 차려입은 듯한 짐꾼 두 사람을 지나, 그들은 업무를 처리할 사무실로 들어섰다. 거기서 펠 씨와 플래셔 씨는 그들을 잠시 세워 둔 채, 위층 유언 사무소로 올라갔다.\n\n\"여긴 무슨 곳이오?\" 얼룩진 얼굴의 신사가 웰러 영감에게 속삭였다.\n\n\"공채 사무소요,\" 유언 집행인이 속삭여 대답했다.\n\n\"저 계산대 뒤에 앉아 있는 양반들은 뭐요?\" 목소리 쉰 마부가 물었다.\n\n\"감액 공채들인가 보오,\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다. \"저게 그 감액 공채들 아니냐, 새뮤얼?\"\n\n\"아니, 설마 감액 공채가 살아 있는 거라고 생각하는 건 아니죠?\" 샘이 다소 경멸하듯 물었다.\n\n\"내가 어찌 알겠냐?\" 웰러 씨가 받아쳤다. \"꼭 그래 보이길래 그랬지. 그럼 저게 뭐냐?\"\n\n\"사무원들이에요,\" 샘이 대답했다.\n\n\"근데 다들 왜 햄 샌드위치를 먹고 있는 거냐?\" 그의 아버지가 물었다.\n\n\"그게 직무라서겠죠,\" 샘이 대답했다. \"제도의 일부예요. 여기선 늘 그러고 있다니까요, 온종일!\"\n\n웰러 씨와 그의 친구들이 이 나라의 화폐 제도와 연관된 이 기묘한 규정을 미처 곱씹어 볼 겨를도 없이, 펠 씨와 윌킨스 플래셔 신사가 다시 합류하여 그들을 계산대의 한쪽으로 데려갔는데, 그 위쪽에는 큼지막한 'W' 자가 적힌 둥근 흑판이 걸려 있었다.\n\n\"그건 뭐 하는 거요, 선생?\" 웰러 씨가 펠 씨의 주의를 그 표적으로 돌리며 물었다.\n\n\"고인의 이름 첫 글자입니다,\" 펠 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"이보시오,\" 웰러 씨가 심판들을 돌아보며 말했다. \"여기 뭔가 잘못됐소. 우린 우리 글자라야지—이건 안 돼.\"\n\n심판들은 곧장, W 자 아래에서는 이 일을 합법적으로 진행할 수 없다는 것이 자신들의 단호한 의견이라고 내놓았고, 그러니 십중팔구 그 일은 적어도 하루는 미루어졌을 것이다. 샘의 신속한—언뜻 보기엔 불효막심한—행동만 아니었더라면 말이다. 샘은 아버지의 외투 자락을 움켜쥐고 계산대로 끌고 가, 두 가지 서류에 서명을 마칠 때까지 거기에 붙박아 두었다. 웰러 씨가 글자를 인쇄하듯 한 자 한 자 또박또박 쓰는 버릇이 있는 탓에 그 일이 어찌나 품과 시간이 드는 작업이었던지, 그것이 행해지는 동안 담당 사무원이 립스턴 피핀 사과 세 개를 까먹을 정도였다.\n\n웰러 영감이 자기 몫을 즉시 매각하겠다고 고집을 부린 터라, 그들은 은행에서 증권 거래소 정문으로 옮겨 갔고, 윌킨스 플래셔 신사가 잠시 자리를 비웠다가 스미스, 페인 앤드 스미스 앞으로 발행된 오백삼십 파운드짜리 수표를 들고 돌아왔다. 그것은 둘째 웰러 부인의 공채 저축 잔액에 대한 대가로, 그날의 시장 가격에 따라 웰러 씨가 받을 권리가 있는 돈이었다. 샘의 이백 파운드는 그의 명의로 이전되었고, 윌킨스 플래셔 신사는 수수료를 지급받자 그 돈을 무심히 외투 주머니에 떨어뜨려 넣고는 어슬렁어슬렁 자기 사무소로 돌아갔다.\n\n웰러 씨는 처음에는 그 수표를 소버린 금화로만 바꾸겠다고 완강하게 고집했으나, 그렇게 하면 그것을 집까지 나르려고 작은 자루를 사야 하는 비용이 든다는 점을 심판들이 일러 주자, 오 파운드짜리 지폐로 그 금액을 받는 데 동의했다.\n\n\"내 아들,\" 그들이 은행 건물에서 나오며 웰러 씨가 말했다. \"내 아들과 내가 오늘 오후에 아주 각별한 약속이 있는데, 이 일은 후딱 마무리 짓고 싶구려. 그러니 어디 곧장 가서 계산을 따져 볼 만한 데로 갑시다.\"\n\n곧 조용한 방을 찾아내, 장부를 꺼내 검토했다. 펠 씨의 청구서는 샘이 따져 깎았고, 몇몇 비용은 심판들이 인정하지 않았다. 그러나 펠 씨가 정말이지 자기에게 너무 가혹하다고 여러 번 엄숙히 단언하며 항변했음에도, 그것은 그가 여태껏 맡은 일 가운데 단연 가장 짭짤한 일거리였고, 그 덕에 그는 이후로 여섯 달이나 먹고, 자고, 빨래까지 해결했다.\n\n심판들은 독주를 한 잔씩 들이켜고는 악수를 나눈 뒤 떠났으니, 그날 밤 안으로 시 외곽으로 마차를 몰아 나가야 했기 때문이다. 솔로몬 펠 씨는 더는 먹거나 마실 일이 벌어지지 않으리란 걸 알아채고는 다정하게 작별을 고했고, 샘과 그의 아버지만 남게 되었다.\n\n\"자!\" 웰러 씨가 수첩을 옆주머니에 쑤셔 넣으며 말했다. \"임차권 대금이랑 뭐랑 합쳐서 여기 천백팔십 파운드가 있다. 자, 새뮤얼, 얘야, 말 머리를 조지 앤드 벌처로 돌려라!\"\n\n픽윅 씨는 홀로 앉아 여러 가지 일을 곰곰이 생각하고 있었는데, 다른 여러 고려사항 가운데서도, 지금 자리잡지 못한 처지가 그에게 끊임없는 안타까움과 근심의 씨앗이 되고 있는 그 젊은 부부를 어떻게 하면 가장 잘 챙겨 줄 수 있을까 궁리하던 참이었다. 그때 메리가 가벼운 걸음으로 방에 들어와 탁자 쪽으로 다가오며 다소 다급하게 말했다—\n\n\"아, 죄송하지만 나으리, 새뮤얼이 아래층에 와 있는데요, 자기 아버지가 나으리를 좀 뵈어도 되겠느냐고 묻습니다.\"\n\n\"물론이지.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"고맙습니다, 나으리.\" 메리가 다시 종종걸음으로 문 쪽으로 향하며 말했다.\n\n\"새뮤얼이 온 지 오래되지는 않았지?\" 픽윅 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"아, 아니에요, 나으리.\" 메리가 열심히 대답했다. \"방금 막 돌아왔어요. 더는 휴가를 청하지 않을 거라고 하던데요, 나으리.\"\n\n메리는 이 마지막 소식을 실제로 필요해 보이는 것보다 더 따뜻하게 전했다는 것을 스스로 의식하고 있었는지도 모른다. 아니면 말을 마쳤을 때 픽윅 씨가 자기를 바라보던 그 다정한 미소를 알아챘는지도 모른다. 어쨌든 그녀는 분명히 고개를 숙이고서, 굳이 그럴 까닭이 있어 보이지도 않는데도 아주 깔끔한 작은 앞치마의 한 귀퉁이를 필요 이상으로 꼼꼼히 들여다보았다.\n\n\"어서 올라오라고, 꼭 그렇게 전해 주게.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n메리는 짐짓 크게 안도한 듯, 그 전갈을 들고 서둘러 나갔다.\n\n픽윅 씨는 방 안을 두세 번 오르내렸다. 그러면서 왼손으로 턱을 문지르며 생각에 잠긴 듯했다.\n\n\"음, 음.\" 픽윅 씨가 마침내 다정하나 다소 우울한 어조로 말했다. \"그의 충직함과 헌신에 보답할 수 있는 가장 좋은 길이지. 하느님의 이름으로, 그렇게 되게 하자. 곁에 있는 사람들이 새롭고 다른 인연을 맺고 자기를 떠나는 것, 그것이 외로운 늙은이의 운명이지. 나라고 달리되리라 기대할 권리는 없어. 아니, 아니지.\" 픽윅 씨가 좀 더 쾌활하게 덧붙였다. \"그건 이기적이고 배은망덕한 일일 거야. 그를 이토록 잘 자리잡게 해 줄 기회를 얻었으니 기뻐해야 마땅하지. 나는 기쁘다. 물론 기쁘고말고.\"\n\n픽윅 씨는 이런 생각에 너무도 깊이 빠져 있던 터라, 문 두드리는 소리가 서너 번 거듭되고서야 비로소 들었다. 황급히 자리에 앉아 평소의 유쾌한 표정을 불러 모은 다음, 들어오라는 허락을 내렸다. 그러자 샘 웰러가 들어왔고, 그 뒤를 아버지가 따라 들어왔다.\n\n\"돌아온 걸 보니 반갑네, 샘.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"안녕하시오, 웰러 씨?\"\n\n\"아주 정정합니다요, 고맙습니다, 나으리.\" 홀아비가 대답했다. \"나으리도 평안하시지요?\"\n\n\"아주 좋소, 고맙소.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"나으리, 좀 말씀을 나누고 싶은 게 있어서요.\" 웰러 씨가 말했다. \"오 분쯤만 시간을 내주실 수 있다면 말입니다요, 나으리.\"\n\n\"물론이오.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다. \"샘, 아버지께 의자를 갖다 드리게.\"\n\n\"고맙다, 새뮤얼, 여기 의자 하나 챙겼다.\" 웰러 씨가 말하면서 의자 하나를 앞으로 끌어왔다. \"참으로 날씨가 좋은 날이었습니다, 나으리.\" 노신사가 자리에 앉으며 모자를 바닥에 내려놓고 덧붙였다.\n\n\"정말이지 놀랍도록 좋았소.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다. \"아주 철에 맞는 날씨였소.\"\n\n\"이제껏 본 중에 가장 철에 맞는 날씨였습죠, 나으리.\" 웰러 씨가 맞장구쳤다. 여기서 노신사는 격렬한 기침 발작에 사로잡혔는데, 그것이 끝나자 고개를 끄덕이고 눈을 찡긋하며 아들에게 애원하는 듯도 하고 으르는 듯도 한 몸짓을 여러 번 해 보였다. 그러나 샘 웰러는 그 모든 것을 한사코 못 본 척하였다.\n\n픽윅 씨는 노신사 쪽에 어떤 거북함이 있다는 것을 알아채고, 곁에 놓인 책의 책장을 가르는 일에 골몰한 척하면서, 웰러 씨가 방문의 용건에 다다를 때까지 끈기 있게 기다렸다.\n\n\"너처럼 사람 속을 뒤집는 녀석은 처음 본다, 새뮤얼.\" 웰러 씨가 아들을 분하게 노려보며 말했다. \"내 평생 처음이야.\"\n\n\"이 사람이 뭘 어쨌소, 웰러 씨?\" 픽윅 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"이놈이 운을 안 떼지 뭡니까, 나으리.\" 웰러 씨가 받아 말했다. \"뭔가 특별히 처리할 일이 있을 때 내가 말주변이 없다는 걸 뻔히 알면서도, 나으리의 귀중한 시간을 잡아먹으며 내가 여기 이렇게 앉아 꼴사납게 구경거리가 되도록 그냥 서서 보고만 있단 말입니다. 한마디라도 거들어 줄 생각은 안 하고요. 그건 자식 된 도리가 아닙니다, 새뮤얼.\" 웰러 씨가 이마를 닦으며 말했다. \"도리하고는 한참 멀어.\"\n\n\"아버지가 직접 말하겠다 했잖아요.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"운도 떼기 전에 이미 막힐 줄을 내가 어떻게 알아요?\"\n\n\"내가 출발도 못 하는 걸 봤으면 알았어야지.\" 아버지가 응수했다. \"길 한복판도 아니고 엉뚱한 쪽에 들어서서 울타리에 들이박고 온갖 험한 꼴을 다 당하고 있는데도, 손 하나 내밀어 거들어 줄 생각을 안 해. 너 때문에 내가 부끄럽다, 새뮤얼.\"\n\n\"사실인즉, 나으리.\" 샘이 가볍게 고개를 숙이며 말했다. \"아버지가 자기 돈을 인출하셨거든요.\"\n\n\"좋다, 새뮤얼, 좋아.\" 웰러 씨가 흡족한 기색으로 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다. \"너한테 모질게 말하려던 건 아니다, 새미. 좋아. 그렇게 시작하는 거다. 곧장 본론으로 들어가야지. 아주 좋다, 새뮤얼.\"\n\n웰러 씨는 만족이 넘쳐 유난히 여러 번 고개를 끄덕이고는, 샘이 진술을 이어 가기를 귀 기울이는 자세로 기다렸다.\n\n\"샘, 자네는 앉아도 좋네.\" 이 면담이 예상보다 다소 길어질 듯하다는 것을 알아챈 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n샘은 다시 고개를 숙이고 앉았다. 아버지가 둘레를 살피자, 그가 말을 이었다—\n\n\"아버지가요, 나으리, 오백삼십 파운드를 인출하셨어요.\"\n\n\"감채 공채.\" 웰러 영감이 나직한 목소리로 끼어들었다.\n\n\"감채 공채든 뭐든 그건 별로 중요하지 않아요.\" 샘이 말했다. \"오백삼십 파운드, 그게 금액 맞죠?\"\n\n\"맞다, 새뮤얼.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"거기에 집과 사업을 합쳐서—\"\n\n\"임차권, 영업권, 재고, 비품.\" 웰러 씨가 끼어들었다.\n\n\"다 합치면,\" 샘이 말을 이었다. \"통틀어 천백팔십 파운드가 됩니다.\"\n\n\"그래요!\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"그 말을 들으니 기쁘오. 웰러 씨, 그토록 잘되셨다니 축하하오.\"\n\n\"잠깐만요, 나으리.\" 웰러 씨가 만류하는 몸짓으로 손을 들며 말했다. \"계속해라, 새뮤얼.\"\n\n\"이 돈을요,\" 샘이 약간 머뭇거리며 말했다. \"아버지는 안전한 곳에다 두고 싶어 안달이세요. 저도 무척 그러길 바라고요. 아버지가 그냥 가지고 계시면, 누군가한테 빌려주든가, 말에다 재산을 처박든가, 지하 통풍구에 지갑을 떨어뜨리든가, 아니면 어떻게든 자기 자신을 이집트 미라로 만들어 버리실 게 뻔하니까요.\"\n\n\"아주 좋다, 새뮤얼.\" 웰러 씨가, 마치 샘이 자기의 신중함과 선견지명에 최고의 찬사를 바치기라도 한 듯이 흐뭇한 태도로 한마디 했다. \"아주 좋아.\"\n\n\"그런 까닭에,\" 샘이 모자 챙을 초조하게 잡아 뜯으며 말을 이었다. \"그런 까닭에 아버지가 오늘 이걸 인출하셨고, 저와 함께 이리로 와서 말씀드리려는 거예요, 적어도 청하시려는, 그러니까 다시 말하면—\"\n\n\"그러니까 이걸 말하려는 거다.\" 웰러 영감이 참다못해 말했다. \"나한테는 이게 아무 쓸모가 없습니다. 나는 마차를 정기로 몰러 갈 참인데, 이걸 둘 데가 아무 데도 없어요. 차장한테 돈을 줘서 맡기든가, 아니면 마차 주머니에 넣어 두든가 해야 할 텐데, 그러면 안에 탄 손님들한테 유혹거리가 되지 않겠습니까. 나으리가 저를 위해 이걸 맡아 주신다면, 정말 크게 감사하겠습니다. 어쩌면,\" 웰러 씨가 픽윅 씨에게 다가가 귀에 대고 속삭였다. \"어쩌면 그 유죄판결 비용에 조금이나마 보탬이 될지도 모르고요. 제 말은 그저, 제가 다시 달라고 할 때까지 맡아만 주십사 하는 겁니다.\" 이 말과 함께 웰러 씨는 지갑을 픽윅 씨의 손에 쥐여 주고, 모자를 집어 들더니, 저렇게 뚱뚱한 사람한테서는 좀처럼 기대하기 어려운 날렵함으로 방을 뛰쳐나갔다.\n\n\"붙들게, 샘!\" 픽윅 씨가 간절히 외쳤다. \"따라가게. 당장 데려와! 웰러 씨—여기—돌아오시오!\"\n\n샘은 주인의 분부를 거역할 수 없다는 것을 알았다. 그래서 계단을 내려가던 아버지의 팔을 붙들어, 완력으로 끌고 도로 올라왔다.\n\n\"여보시오, 친구.\" 픽윅 씨가 노인의 손을 잡으며 말했다. \"당신의 그 정직한 신뢰가 나를 압도하는구려.\"\n\n\"그런 일에는 아무 까닭도 없지 않습니까, 나으리.\" 웰러 씨가 고집스럽게 대답했다.\n\n\"정말이지, 친구, 나는 평생 다 못 쓸 만큼 돈이 많소. 내 나이의 사람이 살아 있는 동안 도저히 다 쓸 수 없을 만큼이오.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"써 보기 전엔 자기가 얼마나 쓸 수 있는지 아무도 모릅니다.\" 웰러 씨가 한마디 했다.\n\n\"그럴지도 모르지.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다. \"하지만 나는 그런 실험을 해 볼 생각이 전혀 없으니, 궁핍에 빠질 일도 없을 거요. 이걸 도로 가져가시오, 웰러 씨. 부디 부탁이오.\"\n\n\"좋습니다요.\" 웰러 씨가 못마땅한 표정으로 말했다. \"내 말 잘 들어라, 새미. 이 재산으로 뭔가 큰일을 저지르고 말 테다. 큰일을!\"\n\n\"그러지 않는 게 좋을 텐데요.\" 샘이 대답했다.\n\n웰러 씨는 잠시 생각에 잠겼다가, 단단히 결심한 듯 외투 단추를 채우며 말했다—\n\n\"통행료 징수소를 차리겠다.\"\n\n\"뭐라고요!\" 샘이 외쳤다.\n\n\"통행료 징수소!\" 웰러 씨가 앙다문 잇새로 받아 말했다. \"통행료 징수소를 차리겠다. 아버지한테 작별 인사나 해라, 새뮤얼. 남은 평생을 통행료 징수소에 바치겠다.\"\n\n이 협박이 너무도 끔찍한 것인 데다, 웰러 씨가 정말로 그것을 실행에 옮기리라 단단히 작정한 듯 보였고, 게다가 픽윅 씨의 거절에 몹시 마음이 상한 듯 보였기에, 픽윅 씨는 잠시 생각한 끝에 이렇게 말했다—\n\n\"음, 음, 웰러 씨, 그럼 당신의 돈을 맡아 두겠소. 어쩌면 당신보다 내가 이것으로 더 좋은 일을 할 수 있을지도 모르겠고.\"\n\n\"바로 그겁니다, 암요.\" 웰러 씨가 환해지며 말했다. \"당연히 그러실 수 있고말굽쇼, 나으리.\"\n\n\"그 얘긴 더는 하지 맙시다.\" 픽윅 씨가 지갑을 책상에 넣고 잠그며 말했다. \"진심으로 고맙소, 친구. 자, 이제 다시 앉으시오. 당신의 조언을 좀 구하고 싶소.\"\n\n방문이 의기양양한 성공으로 끝난 데서 비롯된 속웃음이, 지갑을 잠그는 동안 웰러 씨의 얼굴뿐 아니라 팔과 다리와 온몸까지 들썩이게 했는데, 이 말을 듣는 순간 그것은 더없이 위엄 있는 근엄함으로 돌변하였다.\n\n\"샘, 잠깐만 밖에 나가 있어 주겠나?\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n샘은 즉시 물러갔다.\n\n픽윅 씨가 이렇게 말문을 열자, 웰러 씨는 유난히 영리해 보이면서도 몹시 놀란 표정을 지었다—\n\n\"웰러 씨, 당신은 결혼을 찬성하는 편은 아니지 싶은데, 그렇소?\"\n\n웰러 씨는 고개를 가로저었다. 그는 도무지 말을 할 수가 없었다. 어떤 사악한 과부가 픽윅 씨에게 흉계를 꾸며 성공을 거두었다는 막연한 생각이 그의 목구멍을 틀어막았다.\n\n\"방금 아들과 함께 들어올 때, 혹시 아래층에서 젊은 처녀를 보지 못했소?\" 픽윅 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"봤습니다. 젊은 처자를 봤습죠.\" 웰러 씨가 짧게 대답했다.\n\n\"그래, 그녀를 어떻게 보았소? 솔직하게, 웰러 씨, 그녀를 어떻게 생각하오?\"\n\n\"통통하고 몸매가 잘 빠졌다 싶었습니다.\" 웰러 씨가 품평하는 투로 말했다.\n\n\"바로 그렇소.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"정말 그렇소. 당신이 본 바로는, 그녀의 태도는 어떻게 생각했소?\"\n\n\"아주 상냥하더군요.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다. \"아주 상냥하고 만족스러웠습죠.\"\n\n웰러 씨가 이 마지막 형용사에 정확히 어떤 뜻을 담았는지는 드러나지 않았으나, 그가 그 말을 쓴 어조로 보아 그것이 호의적인 표현임이 분명했으므로, 픽윅 씨는 마치 그 점에 관해 속속들이 깨우침을 얻은 것처럼 흡족해하였다.\n\n\"나는 그녀에게 큰 관심을 두고 있소, 웰러 씨.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n웰러 씨가 헛기침을 했다.\n\n\"내 말은, 그녀가 잘되기를 바라는 관심이오.\" 픽윅 씨가 말을 이었다. \"그녀가 편안하고 넉넉하게 살았으면 하는 바람이지. 알겠소?\"\n\n\"아주 분명히 알겠습니다.\" 아직 아무것도 알아듣지 못한 웰러 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"그 젊은 처녀가,\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"당신의 아들을 마음에 두고 있소.\"\n\n\"새뮤얼 웰러를요!\" 그 아비가 외쳤다.\n\n\"그렇소.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"자연스러운 일이지요.\" 웰러 씨가 잠시 생각하더니 말했다. \"자연스럽긴 하나, 좀 불안한 일입니다. 새미가 조심해야겠어요.\"\n\n\"무슨 뜻이오?\" 픽윅 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"그 처자한테 아무 말도 하지 않도록 아주 조심해야 한다는 거죠.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다. \"순진한 순간에 끌려가서, 혼인 약속 파기 소송으로 유죄판결을 받게 할 만한 말을 입 밖에 내지 않도록 아주 조심해야 합니다. 그것들이 일단 누구한테 흉계를 품으면, 절대 안심할 수가 없거든요, 픽윅 씨. 어디서 어떻게 잡아야 할지 알 수가 없어요. 이쪽에서 이리저리 궁리하는 동안에, 그것들이 벌써 이쪽을 낚아채 버린단 말입니다. 저도 처음에 바로 그런 식으로 장가를 들었습죠, 나으리. 새미가 바로 그 술수의 결과물이고요.\"\n\n\"내가 하려는 말의 결론을 내리는 데 그리 큰 격려가 되지는 않는구려.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"그래도 차라리 단번에 말하는 게 낫겠소. 이 젊은 처녀가 당신 아들을 마음에 둔 것만이 아니라, 당신 아들 또한 그녀를 마음에 두고 있소, 웰러 씨.\"\n\n\"허허,\" 웰러 씨가 말했다. \"이거 아비 귀에 들어오기엔 참으로 묘한 종류의 일이로군요, 이거!\"\n\n\"나는 여러 차례 그 둘을 눈여겨보았소.\" 픽윅 씨가 웰러 씨의 마지막 말에는 아무런 토를 달지 않고 말했다. \"그래서 그 점에 대해 조금도 의심하지 않소. 만약 내가 그 둘을 부부로 맺어 주고, 어떤 작은 사업이나 자리에서 편안히 자리잡게 하여, 그들이 그럭저럭 괜찮은 살림을 꾸려 갈 희망을 가지게 해 주고 싶다면, 당신은 그것을 어떻게 생각하겠소, 웰러 씨?\"\n\n처음에는 웰러 씨가, 자기가 관심을 두는 사람이 누구든 그 결혼이 걸린 제안에는 떨떠름한 표정으로 응했다. 그러나 픽윅 씨가 그 점을 두고 그를 설득하면서, 메리가 과부가 아니라는 사실을 크게 강조하자, 그는 차츰 다루기 쉬워졌다. 픽윅 씨는 그에게 큰 영향력을 가지고 있었고, 그 자신도 메리의 자태에 적잖이 마음이 끌렸던 터였다. 사실 그는 이미 그녀에게 도무지 아비답지 못한 눈짓을 여러 번 던진 바 있었다. 마침내 그는, 픽윅 씨의 뜻을 거스를 처지가 못 되며, 그의 조언에 기꺼이 따르겠다고 말했다. 이에 픽윅 씨는 기쁘게 그 말을 받아들이고, 샘을 다시 방으로 불러들였다.\n\n\"샘,\" 픽윅 씨가 헛기침을 하며 말했다. \"자네 아버지와 내가 자네에 관해 이야기를 좀 나누었네.\"\n\n\"네 얘기를 말이다, 새뮤얼.\" 웰러 씨가 거들먹거리는, 짐짓 무게 잡힌 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"샘, 나는 자네가 윈클 부인의 하녀에게 단순한 친근감 이상의 무언가를 품고 있다는 걸 진작부터 알아차리지 못할 만큼 눈이 어둡지는 않다네.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"들었지, 새뮤얼?\" 웰러 씨가 아까와 똑같은 재판관 같은 말투로 말했다.\n\n\"바라건대, 나리,\" 샘이 주인을 향해 말했다. \"젊은 남자가 누가 봐도 예쁘고 행실 바른 젊은 처자에게 마음을 둔다고 해서 거기에 잘못이 있을 거라고는 생각지 않습니다.\"\n\n\"그야 당연하지.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"천만에, 절대 아니지.\" 웰러 씨가 상냥하면서도 위엄 있게 동의했다.\n\n\"그처럼 자연스러운 행동에 무언가 잘못이 있다고 생각하기는커녕,\" 픽윅 씨가 다시 말을 이었다. \"나는 이 점에서 자네의 바람을 돕고 북돋아 주고 싶다네. 그런 뜻에서 자네 아버지와 잠깐 이야기를 나누었고, 그분도 나와 같은 생각이라는 걸 알게 되었으니—\"\n\n\"그 처자가 과부가 아니라는 점에서 말입니다.\" 웰러 씨가 설명을 덧붙이며 끼어들었다.\n\n\"그 처자가 과부가 아니라는 점에서.\" 픽윅 씨가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"나는 자네가 지금 처지 때문에 받고 있는 속박에서 벗어나게 해 주고 싶고, 또 자네의 충실함과 여러 훌륭한 자질에 대한 내 마음을 표하고 싶다네. 그러기 위해 자네가 당장 이 처자와 결혼하여 자네 자신과 가족을 위한 독립된 생계를 꾸릴 수 있도록 해 주려는 것이지. 샘,\" 지금까지 조금 떨리던 픽윅 씨의 목소리가 이제 다시 평소의 어조를 되찾았다. \"자네 앞날의 인생 설계를 내가 고맙고도 각별한 마음으로 보살피는 것이 나로서는 자랑스럽고도 행복한 일일세.\"\n\n잠깐 깊은 침묵이 흘렀고, 이윽고 샘이 낮고 쉰 듯하지만 단호한 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"베풀어 주시는 친절에 정말 감사드립니다, 나리. 나리다운 일이지요. 하지만 그건 안 될 일입니다.\"\n\n\"안 될 일이라니!\" 픽윅 씨가 놀라서 외쳤다.\n\n\"새뮤얼!\" 웰러 씨가 위엄을 갖추어 말했다.\n\n\"안 될 일이라고 했습니다.\" 샘이 좀 더 큰 소리로 되풀이했다. \"나리는 어떻게 되시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"여보게,\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다. \"내 친구들 사이에 최근에 일어난 변화들로 해서 앞으로 내 생활 방식은 완전히 달라질 걸세. 게다가 나도 나이를 먹어 가니 휴식과 안정이 필요하다네. 샘, 내 떠돌이 시절은 끝났어.\"\n\n\"그걸 제가 어떻게 압니까, 나리?\" 샘이 따지듯 말했다. \"지금은 그렇게 생각하시지요! 하지만 마음이 바뀌실 수도 있지 않습니까. 그럴 법한 일이지요. 나리께는 아직도 스물다섯 살짜리 기운이 남아 있으니까요. 그럴 때 제가 곁에 없으면 어떻게 되시겠습니까? 안 될 일입니다, 나리. 안 될 일입니다.\"\n\n\"옳거니, 새뮤얼, 그 말에 일리가 있다.\" 웰러 씨가 부추기듯 말했다.\n\n\"샘, 나는 오래 숙고한 끝에 말하는 것이고, 내 말을 반드시 지키리라는 확신을 가지고 말하는 걸세.\" 픽윅 씨가 고개를 저으며 말했다. \"이제 새로운 국면들이 내 앞에 펼쳐졌고, 내 떠돌이 시절은 막을 내렸다네.\"\n\n\"좋습니다.\" 샘이 대꾸했다. \"그렇다면 그것이야말로 나리를 이해하고, 떠받쳐 드리고, 편안하게 해 드릴 누군가를 늘 곁에 두셔야 할 가장 확실한 이유입니다. 더 세련된 친구가 필요하시면 그것도 좋습니다, 그런 사람을 두십시오. 하지만 봉급이 있건 없건, 통고가 있건 없건, 식사가 나오건 안 나오건, 잠자리가 있건 없건, 나리께서 자치구의 그 낡은 여관에서 데려오신 샘 웰러는 무슨 일이 닥쳐도 나리 곁을 지킬 겁니다. 모든 일과 모든 사람이 제아무리 사납게 막아선들, 그 무엇도 결코 그걸 막지 못할 겁니다!\"\n\n샘이 큰 감정을 담아 이렇게 선언을 마치자, 아버지 웰러 씨가 의자에서 벌떡 일어나, 때와 장소와 예의에 대한 일체의 고려를 잊은 채 모자를 머리 위로 휘두르며 세 번 우렁차게 환호했다.\n\n\"여보게,\" 자신의 열의에 다소 머쓱해진 웰러 씨가 다시 자리에 앉았을 때 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"자네는 그 처자도 생각해야 하지 않겠나.\"\n\n\"그 처자도 생각하고 있습니다, 나리.\" 샘이 말했다. \"그 처자를 생각해 보았지요. 그 애와 이야기도 나누었습니다. 제 처지가 어떤지 다 말해 주었고요. 그 애는 제가 준비될 때까지 기다리겠다고 합니다. 그렇게 할 거라고 저는 믿습니다. 만약 그러지 않는다면 그건 제가 생각하던 그 처자가 아닌 거고, 그땐 미련 없이 단념하겠습니다. 나리는 예전부터 저를 잘 아시지 않습니까. 제 마음은 정해졌고, 그 무엇도 결코 바꾸지 못합니다.\"\n\n이 결심을 누가 꺾을 수 있겠는가? 픽윅 씨는 아니었다. 그는 그 순간, 세상에서 가장 위대한 사람들이 만 번을 다짐한다 한들 그의 마음에 불러일으키지 못했을 만큼의 긍지와 더없이 흐뭇한 감정을, 이 소박한 벗들의 사심 없는 애정에서 길어 올렸다.\n\n이런 대화가 픽윅 씨의 방에서 오가는 동안, 코담배 빛깔의 옷을 차려입은 자그마한 노신사가 작은 여행 가방을 든 짐꾼을 뒤에 거느리고 아래층에 나타났다. 그는 하룻밤 묵을 침대를 잡고 나서 웨이터에게 윈클 부인이라는 분이 여기 묵고 있느냐고 물었고, 이 물음에 웨이터는 물론 그렇다고 대답했다.\n\n\"혼자 있소?\" 노신사가 물었다.\n\n\"그런 줄로 압니다, 손님.\" 웨이터가 대답했다. \"원하시면 부인의 하녀를 불러 드릴—\"\n\n\"아니, 그 사람은 필요 없소.\" 노신사가 얼른 말했다. \"내가 왔다는 것을 알리지 말고 그 방으로 안내하시오.\"\n\n\"네, 손님?\" 웨이터가 말했다.\n\n\"귀가 먹었소?\" 자그마한 노신사가 물었다.\n\n\"아닙니다, 손님.\"\n\n\"그럼 잘 들으시오. 이제 내 말 들리오?\"\n\n\"네, 손님.\"\n\n\"좋소. 내가 왔다는 것을 알리지 말고 윈클 부인의 방으로 안내하시오.\"\n\n자그마한 노신사는 이렇게 명령하면서 웨이터의 손에 슬며시 오 실링을 쥐여 주고는 그를 빤히 쳐다보았다.\n\n\"하지만 손님,\" 웨이터가 말했다. \"잘 모르겠습니다, 손님, 그게—\"\n\n\"아! 자네가 해 줄 걸세, 보아하니.\" 자그마한 노신사가 말했다. \"지금 당장 하는 게 좋아. 시간을 아끼게 될 테니.\"\n\n신사의 태도에 어찌나 침착하고 흔들림 없는 데가 있던지, 웨이터는 오 실링을 호주머니에 넣고 더는 한마디도 없이 그를 위층으로 안내했다.\n\n\"이 방이지, 그렇지?\" 신사가 말했다. \"이제 가도 좋네.\"\n\n웨이터는 이 신사가 도대체 누구이며 무엇을 원하는지 몹시 궁금해하면서 시키는 대로 했다. 자그마한 노신사는 그가 시야에서 사라질 때까지 기다렸다가 문을 두드렸다.\n\n\"들어오세요.\" 아라벨라가 말했다.\n\n\"음, 어쨌든 목소리는 곱군.\" 자그마한 노신사가 중얼거렸다. \"하지만 그건 아무것도 아니지.\" 이렇게 말하면서 그는 문을 열고 안으로 들어갔다. 바느질을 하며 앉아 있던 아라벨라는 낯선 사람을 보고 일어섰다—조금 당황한 기색이었으나 결코 볼품없이 그러지는 않았다.\n\n\"부디 일어나지 마시오, 부인.\" 낯선 사람이 안으로 들어와 뒤로 문을 닫으며 말했다. \"윈클 부인이시지요?\"\n\n아라벨라가 고개를 숙여 인사했다.\n\n\"버밍엄의 노인의 아들과 결혼한 너새니얼 윈클 부인 맞소?\" 낯선 사람이 눈에 띄는 호기심으로 아라벨라를 살피며 말했다.\n\n아라벨라가 다시 고개를 숙여 인사하고는, 도움을 청해야 할지 어쩔지 몰라 하는 듯 불안하게 주위를 둘러보았다.\n\n\"내가 부인을 놀라게 한 모양이군.\" 노신사가 말했다.\n\n\"솔직히, 조금은요.\" 점점 더 의아해하며 아라벨라가 대답했다.\n\n\"실례지만 의자에 좀 앉겠소, 부인.\" 낯선 사람이 말했다.\n\n그는 의자 하나를 끌어다 앉고는, 호주머니에서 안경집을 꺼내 천천히 안경을 끄집어내 코에 걸쳤다.\n\n\"나를 모르겠소, 부인?\" 그가 아라벨라를 어찌나 빤히 바라보던지 그녀가 겁이 나기 시작할 정도로 말했다.\n\n\"네, 손님.\" 그녀가 머뭇거리며 대답했다.\n\n\"그렇지.\" 노신사가 왼쪽 다리를 어루만지며 말했다. \"부인이 나를 알 리가 없지. 하지만 부인은 내 이름은 알 거요.\"\n\n\"제가요?\" 아라벨라가, 왜인지도 잘 모른 채 떨면서 말했다. \"여쭤봐도 될까요, 어떤 이름이신지?\"\n\n\"곧 알게 되오, 부인, 곧.\" 낯선 사람이 그녀의 얼굴에서 아직 눈을 떼지 않은 채 말했다. \"최근에 결혼하셨소, 부인?\"\n\n\"네.\" 아라벨라가 들릴락 말락 한 목소리로 대답하며 바느질감을 내려놓았다. 앞서 한 번 떠올랐던 생각이 더 강하게 마음을 치고 들어오자 그녀는 몹시 동요했다.\n\n\"부인의 남편이 의지하고 있는 그의 아버지와 먼저 상의하는 것이 옳다는 점을, 부인은 남편에게 깨우쳐 주지도 않은 채 말이오?\" 낯선 사람이 말했다.\n\n아라벨라가 손수건을 눈가에 갖다 댔다.\n\n\"그 노인이 당연히 큰 관심을 가질 만한 일에 관해 그가 어떤 생각을 품고 있는지, 어떤 간접적인 방법으로 슬쩍 떠보아 알아내려는 시도조차 없이 말이오?\" 낯선 사람이 말했다.\n\n\"부인할 수 없습니다, 손님.\" 아라벨라가 말했다.\n\n\"또 남편이 아버지의 뜻에 맞게 결혼했더라면 얻었을 세속적 이득과 맞바꿀 만큼, 그에게 지속적인 도움을 줄 변변한 재산도 부인 자신에게 없으면서 말이오?\" 노신사가 말했다. \"이런 걸 두고 사내아이들과 계집아이들은 사심 없는 애정이라고 부르지. 그러다가 제 자식들을 두게 되면, 그때 가서는 훨씬 거칠고 사뭇 다른 빛깔로 그것을 보게 되는 법이오!\"\n\n아라벨라는 눈물을 줄줄 흘리며 변명 삼아 호소하기를, 자신은 어리고 세상 물정에 어두웠으며, 오로지 그 애정 하나 때문에 자신이 택한 그런 행동을 하게 되었고, 거의 어릴 적부터 부모의 조언과 인도를 받지 못한 채 자랐다고 했다.\n\n\"잘못한 일이오.\" 노신사가 한결 누그러진 어조로 말했다. \"아주 잘못한 일이지. 낭만적이고, 분별없고, 어리석은 일이었소.\"\n\n\"제 잘못입니다. 모두 제 잘못이에요, 손님.\" 가엾은 아라벨라가 흐느끼며 대답했다.\n\n\"무슨 소리.\" 노신사가 말했다. \"그가 부인에게 반한 게 부인 잘못은 아니지 않소? 아니, 따지고 보면 그것도 그렇구먼.\" 노신사가 아라벨라를 다소 짓궂게 바라보며 말했다. \"그것도 부인 잘못이지. 그 녀석으로서는 어쩔 수가 없었을 테니까.\"\n\n이 자그마한 칭찬이, 혹은 자그마한 신사가 그것을 건네는 별난 방식이, 혹은 처음과는 사뭇 다르게 한층 다정해진 그의 태도가, 아니면 이 셋이 다 합쳐져서, 아라벨라는 눈물 가운데서도 그만 미소를 짓고 말았다.\n\n\"부인의 남편은 어디 있소?\" 노신사가, 막 제 얼굴에 번지려던 미소를 거두며 불쑥 물었다.\n\n\"이제 곧 돌아올 거예요, 손님.\" 아라벨라가 말했다. \"제가 권해서 오늘 아침 산책을 나갔어요. 아버지에게서 아무 소식이 없어 그이는 몹시 기가 죽고 비참해하고 있답니다.\"\n\n\"기가 죽었다고?\" 노신사가 말했다. \"고소하군!\"\n\n\"제 탓이라고 여겨 그러는 것 같아요.\" 아라벨라가 말했다. \"그리고 정말이지, 손님, 저도 그이 때문에 마음이 너무나 아픕니다. 그이를 지금 이 지경으로 몰고 온 것이 오로지 저 하나 때문이니까요.\"\n\n\"그 녀석 때문이라면 마음 쓰지 마시오, 부인.\" 노신사가 말했다. \"고소한 일이지. 나는 그게 기쁘오—그 녀석에 관한 한, 정말로 기쁘다오.\"\n\n이 말이 노신사의 입에서 채 떨어지기도 전에 계단을 올라오는 발소리가 들렸는데, 그와 아라벨라는 같은 순간에 그 소리를 알아챈 듯했다. 자그마한 신사는 얼굴이 창백해졌고, 침착해 보이려고 애써 안간힘을 쓰며 자리에서 일어섰다. 그때 윈클 씨가 방으로 들어왔다.\n\n\"아버지!\" 윈클 씨가 놀라서 움찔 뒤로 물러서며 외쳤다.\n\n\"그래, 이놈아.\" 자그마한 노신사가 대답했다. \"자, 이놈아, 나한테 할 말이 뭐냐?\"\n\n윈클 씨는 잠자코 있었다.\n\n\"부끄러운 줄 알겠지, 응?\" 노신사가 말했다.\n\n여전히 윈클 씨는 아무 말도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"부끄러운 줄 아느냐, 모르느냐?\" 노신사가 물었다.\n\n\"아닙니다, 아버지.\" 윈클 씨가 아라벨라의 팔을 자기 팔에 끼며 대답했다. \"저는 제 자신이 부끄럽지도 않고, 제 아내가 부끄럽지도 않습니다.\"\n\n\"허, 그것참!\" 노신사가 비꼬듯 외쳤다.\n\n\"아버지를 향한 정을 깎아내릴 일을 한 것은 정말로 죄송합니다, 아버지.\" 윈클 씨가 말했다. \"하지만 동시에 이 말씀은 드려야겠습니다. 저는 이 사람을 아내로 맞은 것을 부끄러워할 이유가 없으며, 아버지께서도 이 사람을 며느리로 두신 것을 부끄러워하실 이유가 없습니다.\"\n\n\"네 손을 다오, 냇.\" 노신사가 달라진 목소리로 말했다. \"내게 입맞춤을 해 다오, 얘야. 따지고 보니 너는 아주 사랑스러운 며느리로구나!\"\n\n몇 분 뒤 윈클 씨가 픽윅 씨를 찾으러 나갔다가 그 신사와 함께 돌아와 자기 아버지에게 인사시켰고, 그리하여 그들은 오 분 동안이나 쉼 없이 악수를 나누었다.\n\n\"픽윅 씨, 내 아들에게 베풀어 주신 온갖 친절에 진심으로 감사드립니다.\" 윈클 노인이 무뚝뚝하면서도 솔직한 투로 말했다. \"나는 성미가 급한 사람이라, 지난번에 당신을 보았을 때는 화가 난 데다 갑작스러운 일에 당황했었지요. 이제 내 눈으로 직접 판단했고, 더할 나위 없이 만족스럽습니다. 사과를 더 드려야겠습니까, 픽윅 씨?\"\n\n\"한마디도 필요 없습니다.\" 그 신사가 대답했다. \"당신은 내 행복을 완성하는 데 빠져 있던 단 한 가지 일을 해 주셨으니까요.\"\n\n이리하여 다시 오 분 동안 더 악수가 이어졌고, 거기에 더해 수많은 칭찬의 말이 오갔는데, 이 말들은 칭찬일 뿐 아니라 진심에서 우러났다는, 매우 새삼스러운 미덕까지 갖추고 있었다.\n\n샘은 도리를 다해 아버지를 벨 소바주까지 바래다주었는데, 돌아오는 길에 안마당에서 그 뚱뚱한 소년과 마주쳤다. 그 아이는 에밀리 워들이 보낸 쪽지를 전하는 심부름을 맡고 있었다.\n\n\"있잖아요,\" 평소답지 않게 수다스러워진 조가 말했다. \"메리는 참 예쁜 처자지요, 안 그래요? 나는 그 애가 어찌나 좋은지 몰라요, 정말로요!\"\n\n웰러 씨는 말로 대꾸하지 않았다. 다만 잠시 그 뚱뚱한 소년을 노려보며 그 주제넘음에 그만 어이가 없어 멍해졌다가, 멱살을 잡아 구석으로 끌고 가서는 해롭지는 않으나 격식을 차린 발길질 한 방으로 그를 내쫓았다. 그러고 나서 그는 휘파람을 불며 집으로 걸어갔다.\n\n윈클 씨가 버밍엄에서 무사히 돌아온 뒤로 꼬박 한 주 동안, 픽윅 씨와 샘 웰러는 온종일 집을 비웠다가 저녁 식사 시간에 딱 맞춰 돌아올 뿐이었고, 그럴 때면 그들의 천성과는 영 어울리지 않는 비밀스럽고 거창한 분위기를 풍겼다. 무언가 매우 중대하고 파란만장한 일이 벌어지고 있음은 분명했으나, 그 일의 정확한 성격에 관해서는 갖가지 추측이 떠돌았다. 어떤 이들(그 가운데 터프먼 씨가 있었다)은 픽윅 씨가 혼인 동맹을 꾀하고 있다고 생각하는 쪽이었으나, 이 견해는 부인들이 가장 강력히 부인했다. 다른 이들은 그가 어떤 먼 여행을 계획하고 지금 그 사전 준비를 진행하고 있다는 쪽으로 기울었으나, 이 또한 샘 자신이 강하게 부정했으니, 메리가 캐물었을 때 그는 새로운 여행 따위는 결코 떠나지 않는다고 분명히 잘라 말했던 것이다. 마침내 온 일행의 머리가 엿새라는 긴 날 동안 부질없는 억측으로 쥐어짜인 끝에, 픽윅 씨에게 그의 행동을 해명할 것을 요구하고, 어찌하여 그를 흠모하는 친구들의 모임에서 이렇게 자취를 감추었는지 분명히 밝히도록 하자는 데에 만장일치로 의견이 모아졌다.\n\n이런 뜻에서 워들 씨는 일행 전원을 애덜파이로 초대해 만찬을 베풀었고, 술병이 세 차례 돌고 나자 본론을 꺼냈다.\n\n\"우리는 모두 알고 싶소.\" 노신사가 말했다. \"우리가 대체 무슨 잘못을 저질러 당신을 노하게 하고, 우리를 저버린 채 이런 외로운 산책에만 몰두하게 만들었는지 말이오.\"\n\n\"그렇소?\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"참으로 묘하게도, 나는 바로 오늘 자청해서 전부 해명하려던 참이었소. 그러니 포도주를 한 잔 더 주신다면, 여러분의 궁금증을 풀어 드리리다.\"\n\n술병이 여느 때와 달리 활기차게 이 손에서 저 손으로 건너갔고, 픽윅 씨는 친구들의 얼굴을 둘러보며 흐뭇한 미소를 띤 채 말을 이었다.\n\n\"우리 사이에 일어난 모든 변화는,\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"이미 이루어진 혼인과, 앞으로 이루어질 혼인을 두고 하는 말이오만, 그에 따르는 변화들과 더불어, 나로 하여금 내 앞날의 계획을 차분하게, 그리고 곧장 생각하지 않을 수 없게 만들었소. 나는 런던 근교의 조용하고 아담한 동네로 물러나기로 마음먹었소. 내 마음에 꼭 드는 집을 보았기에, 그것을 사들이고 가구도 들여놓았소. 이제 나를 맞이할 채비가 다 되었으니, 곧장 그리로 들어갈 작정이오. 평화로운 은거 속에서 조용한 세월을 여러 해 더 보낼 수 있기를, 살아생전엔 친구들과의 교제로 위로받고, 죽은 뒤엔 그들의 다정한 추억으로 기림을 받기를 바라면서 말이오.\"\n\n여기서 픽윅 씨가 말을 멈추자, 식탁을 따라 나직한 웅성거림이 돌았다.\n\n\"내가 마련한 집은,\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"덜위치에 있소. 널찍한 정원이 딸려 있고, 런던 근방에서 가장 쾌적한 곳 가운데 하나에 자리 잡고 있소. 실질적인 안락함을 빠짐없이 갖추도록 손을 보았고, 어쩌면 거기에 약간의 우아함까지 더했을지도 모르오. 허나 그것은 여러분이 직접 판단하시오. 샘이 거기까지 나를 따라가오. 퍼커의 추천을 받아 가정부를 한 사람 들였는데, 아주 나이 든 사람이오. 그리고 그녀가 내게 필요하리라고 여기는 그 밖의 하인들도 들였소. 나는 이 작은 안식처를, 내가 큰 관심을 두고 있는 어떤 의식을 그곳에서 치름으로써 봉헌하고자 하오. 내 친구 워들이 반대하지 않는다면, 그의 따님이 내가 집에 들어가는 그날 내 새집에서 혼례를 올렸으면 하오. 젊은이들의 행복이란,\" 픽윅 씨가 다소 감정에 북받쳐 말했다. \"언제나 내 인생의 가장 큰 기쁨이었소. 내게 가장 소중한 친구들의 행복을 내 지붕 아래서 지켜보는 일은 내 마음을 따뜻하게 데워 줄 것이오.\"\n\n픽윅 씨가 다시 말을 멈추었다. 에밀리와 아라벨라는 소리 내어 흐느꼈다.\n\n\"나는 직접 만나서도, 또 편지로도 클럽에 알렸소.\" 픽윅 씨가 말을 이었다. \"내 뜻을 전했지요. 우리가 오래 자리를 비운 동안, 클럽은 내부의 불화로 큰 어려움을 겪었소. 거기에 내 이름이 빠지게 된 일까지 더해지고, 또 다른 사정들과 겹치면서 클럽은 해산하게 되었소. 픽윅 클럽은 이제 더 이상 존재하지 않소.\n\n\"나는 결코 후회하지 않을 것이오.\" 픽윅 씨가 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"비록 새로운 것을 좇는 내 행각이 많은 이들에게 부질없어 보였을지언정, 2년의 대부분을 인간 성품의 갖가지 부류와 갖가지 결을 두루 겪는 데 바친 것을 나는 결코 후회하지 않을 것이오. 내 이전 삶의 거의 전부가 사업과 부의 추구에 바쳐졌던 터라, 일찍이 짐작조차 못 했던 수많은 장면들이 내 앞에 펼쳐졌소. 바라건대 그것이 내 마음을 넓히고 내 식견을 길러 주었기를 바라오. 내가 한 선행이 보잘것없다 할지라도, 끼친 해악은 그보다 더 적기를, 그리고 내 모험들 가운데 어느 것도 인생의 황혼기에 내게 즐겁고 유쾌한 추억의 샘이 아닌 것이 없기를 바라오. 여러분 모두에게 신의 축복이 함께하기를!\"\n\n이 말과 함께 픽윅 씨는 떨리는 손으로 잔을 가득 채워 단숨에 비웠고, 친구들이 한마음으로 자리에서 일어나 진심으로 그를 위해 축배를 들자 그의 두 눈이 촉촉이 젖었다.\n\n스노드그래스 씨의 혼인을 위해 사전에 해 둘 준비는 별로 없었다. 그에게는 아버지도 어머니도 없었고, 미성년 시절엔 픽윅 씨의 피후견인이었던 터라, 그 신사는 그의 재산과 앞날의 전망을 속속들이 잘 알고 있었다. 두 가지에 대한 그의 설명은 워들을 흡족하게 했으니 — 사실 거의 어떤 설명이라도 그러했을 터인데, 그 선량한 노신사는 유쾌함과 친절로 넘쳐흐르고 있었기 때문이다 — 에밀리에게 두둑한 지참금이 주어지자, 혼례는 그날로부터 나흘째 되는 날에 치르기로 정해졌다. 이렇듯 갑작스러운 준비 탓에 양재사 셋과 재단사 하나가 거의 실성하기 직전까지 내몰렸다.\n\n이튿날, 워들 영감은 마차에 역마를 매어 어머니를 모시고 런던으로 돌아오려고 길을 나섰다. 그가 특유의 성급함으로 노부인에게 그 소식을 전하자, 그녀는 당장 까무러쳤다. 허나 곧바로 정신을 차리고는 비단 능직 가운을 즉시 꾸리라 분부했고, 이어서 고인이 된 톨림글라워 부인의 맏딸 혼사에 따랐던 비슷한 종류의 사정들을 늘어놓기 시작했는데, 이를 이야기하는 데 세 시간이 걸렸으면서도 끝내 절반도 마치지 못했다.\n\n런던에서 이루어지고 있는 그 대단한 준비들을 트런들 부인에게도 알려야 했는데, 그녀가 몸 상태가 미묘한 처지였던 터라 그 소식이 그녀에게 너무 벅찰까 염려하여 트런들 씨를 통해 전했다. 허나 그녀에게 그 소식은 결코 벅차지 않았으니, 그녀는 즉시 머글턴으로 편지를 띄워 새 모자와 검은 공단 가운을 주문했을 뿐만 아니라, 그 의식에 참석하겠다는 결심까지 밝혔던 것이다. 이에 트런들 씨가 의사를 불러왔고, 의사는 트런들 부인이 자기 몸 상태가 어떤지 가장 잘 알 터라고 말했다. 그러자 트런들 부인은 자신이 충분히 감당할 만하며 가기로 마음을 굳혔다고 답했다. 이에 현명하고 신중한 의사인지라, 다른 사람들에게 무엇이 좋은지뿐 아니라 자기 자신에게 무엇이 좋은지도 잘 아는 그 의사는, 어쩌면 트런들 부인이 집에 머무르면 가는 것보다 애를 태워 몸을 더 상하게 할지도 모르니, 차라리 가는 편이 나을 것 같다고 말했다. 그리하여 그녀는 갔다. 의사는 세심한 배려로 길에서 마시도록 약 여섯 병을 보내 주었다.\n\n이러한 분주한 일들에 더하여, 워들은 신부 들러리를 맡기로 한 두 명의 어린 아가씨에게 보낼 짤막한 편지 두 통을 맡았다. 그 편지를 받자 두 어린 아가씨는, 그토록 중대한 행사에 갖춰 입을 \"옷가지\"가 하나도 준비되어 있지 않고 만들 시간도 없다는 사실에 절망에 빠졌다. 한데 이 사정은 두 어린 아가씨의 두 훌륭한 아버지에게는 오히려 불만보다는 흡족함을 안겨 주는 듯했다. 어쨌든 낡은 드레스에 장식을 달고 새 보닛을 만들었으며, 두 아가씨는 누가 보아도 기대할 수 있는 만큼 곱게 차려입었다. 그리고 뒤이은 의식에서 적절한 대목마다 울고, 알맞은 순간마다 떨었기에, 그들은 보는 이 모두의 감탄을 자아낼 만큼 제 몫을 훌륭히 해냈다.\n\n그 두 가난한 친척이 대체 어떻게 런던에 다다랐는지 — 걸어왔는지, 마차 뒤꽁무니에 매달려 왔는지, 짐마차에 얻어 탔는지, 아니면 번갈아 서로 업고 왔는지 — 는 알 수 없다. 허나 어쨌든 그들은 워들보다 먼저 와 있었으며, 혼례날 아침 픽윅 씨 집의 문을 맨 처음 두드린 이가 바로 그 두 가난한 친척이었으니, 온통 웃음과 빳빳한 셔츠 깃 차림이었다.\n\n그래도 그들은 진심으로 환대받았으니, 부유함이나 가난함 따위는 픽윅 씨에게 아무 영향도 끼치지 못했기 때문이다. 새 하인들은 모두 민첩하고 빈틈없었고, 샘은 더할 나위 없이 들뜨고 신이 난 상태였으며, 메리는 아름다움과 산뜻한 리본으로 환히 빛나고 있었다.\n\n신랑은 이삼 일 전부터 그 집에 머물고 있었는데, 픽윅 씨와 벤 앨런, 보브 소여, 터프먼 씨를 거느리고 신부를 맞으러 덜위치 교회로 늠름하게 나섰다. 샘 웰러는 마차 밖에 자리 잡았는데, 단춧구멍에는 연인이 선물한 흰 장식을 꽂고, 이 행사를 위해 새로 고안한 화려한 제복을 차려입은 모습이었다. 그들은 워들 일가와 윈클 일가, 신부와 들러리들, 그리고 트런들 일가와 만났다. 의식이 끝나자 마차들은 덜컹거리며 픽윅 씨 집으로 되돌아가 조찬을 들었는데, 그곳에는 자그마한 퍼커 씨가 이미 그들을 기다리고 있었다.\n\n여기서, 그 엄숙한 절차의 부분에 드리웠던 옅은 구름이 모두 걷혔다. 모든 얼굴이 즐겁게 빛났고, 들리는 것이라고는 축하와 칭찬의 말뿐이었다. 모든 것이 어찌나 아름답던지! 앞쪽의 잔디밭, 뒤쪽의 정원, 자그마한 온실, 식당, 응접실, 침실들, 흡연실, 그리고 무엇보다도 서재 — 그림들과 안락의자들, 진기한 장식장들, 별난 탁자들, 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 책들이 있고, 쾌적한 잔디밭으로 열려 나무에 거의 가려진 작은 집들이 여기저기 점점이 박힌 아름다운 풍경을 굽어보는 크고 밝은 창문이 있는 서재. 그리고 또 커튼들, 양탄자들, 의자들, 소파들이라니! 모든 것이 어찌나 아름답고, 어찌나 알차고, 어찌나 정갈하며, 또 어찌나 빼어난 안목으로 꾸며졌던지, 무엇을 가장 감탄해야 할지 정말이지 도무지 정할 수 없다고 모두가 입을 모았다.\n\n그리고 이 모든 것의 한가운데에 픽윅 씨가 서 있었으니, 그의 얼굴은 미소로 환히 빛나 어떤 남자도, 여자도, 아이도 그 미소를 거역할 수 없을 정도였다. 그 자신이 일행 가운데 가장 행복한 사람이었다. 같은 사람들과 몇 번이고 거듭 악수를 나누었고, 그 두 손이 악수에 쓰이지 않을 때면 즐거움에 겨워 두 손을 비벼 댔다. 기쁨이나 호기심이 새로이 표현될 때마다 그쪽으로 몸을 돌렸으며, 환희와 기쁨이 어린 그 표정으로 모두에게 흥을 불어넣었다.\n\n조찬이 차려졌음을 알린다. 픽윅 씨는 노부인(톨림글라워 부인을 주제로 자못 열변을 토하던)을 긴 식탁의 상석으로 안내한다. 워들은 말석에 앉는다. 친구들은 양쪽으로 자리를 잡는다. 샘은 주인의 의자 뒤에 자리를 잡는다. 웃음소리와 이야기 소리가 멎는다. 픽윅 씨는 감사 기도를 드리고 나서 잠시 멈추어 주위를 둘러본다. 그러는 동안, 벅차오르는 기쁨에 그의 두 뺨 위로 눈물이 흘러내린다.\n\n이렇듯 한 점 티 없는 행복의 순간에 우리의 옛 친구를 남겨 두기로 하자. 그런 순간이란, 우리가 찾으려 들기만 하면 이 덧없는 삶을 위로해 줄 것이 언제나 얼마쯤은 있는 법이다. 이 땅 위에는 어두운 그림자들이 드리워 있지만, 그 대비 덕분에 빛은 한층 더 강렬해진다. 어떤 이들은 박쥐나 올빼미처럼 빛보다 어둠을 더 잘 보는 눈을 지녔다. 그런 시력을 지니지 못한 우리는, 세상의 짧은 햇살이 그들 위에 환히 타오를 때, 여러 외로운 시간을 함께해 온 환상 속 벗들에게 마지막 작별의 눈길을 보내는 편이 더 기껍다.\n\n세상과 어울려 인생의 한창때에 이르기까지 한 사람은, 많은 진실한 벗을 사귀었다가 자연의 흐름 속에서 그들을 잃는 것이 대부분의 운명이다. 모든 작가나 연대기 기록자는 상상 속의 벗들을 지어냈다가 예술의 흐름 속에서 그들을 잃는 것이 그 운명이다. 그러나 이것이 그들 불운의 전부는 아니니, 그들은 그 벗들에 관한 결말까지 마련해야 하는 요구를 받기 때문이다.\n\n이 관습에 따라 — 의심할 바 없이 좋지 못한 관습이지만 — 우리는 픽윅 씨 집에 모였던 일행에 관하여 몇 마디 전기적인 글을 덧붙이고자 한다.\n\n윈클 씨 부부는 노신사에게서 완전히 호감을 되찾아, 머지않아 픽윅 씨 집에서 반 마일도 채 떨어지지 않은 새로 지은 집에 자리를 잡았다. 윈클 씨는 도시에서 아버지의 대리인 겸 시내 통신원으로 일하게 되었고, 옛 복장을 영국인들의 평범한 옷차림으로 바꾸었으며, 그 후로 줄곧 교양 있는 기독교인의 외양을 빠짐없이 갖추었다.\n\n스노드그래스 씨 부부는 딩글리 델에 정착하여 작은 농장을 사들여 일구었는데, 이는 수익보다는 소일거리를 위한 것이었다. 스노드그래스 씨는 이따금 넋을 놓고 우울에 잠기곤 하는 까닭에, 오늘날까지도 친구와 지인들 사이에서 대단한 시인으로 평판이 자자하다. 비록 그가 그러한 믿음을 뒷받침할 만한 글을 단 한 줄이라도 쓴 적이 있는지는 우리로서는 알 수 없지만 말이다. 문학이며 철학이며 그 밖의 분야에서 이와 비슷한 근거로 높은 명성을 누리는 저명한 인물들은 수두룩하다.\n\n터프먼 씨는 친구들이 혼인하고 픽윅 씨가 정착하자, 리치먼드에 거처를 마련하여 그 후로 줄곧 그곳에서 살았다. 그는 여름철이면 늘 산책로를 거니는데, 젊은이다운 활기차고 경쾌한 분위기를 풍기는 탓에, 인근에 사는 수많은 독신의 나이 든 부인들로부터 흠모를 받는다. 그는 다시는 청혼하지 않았다.\n\n보브 소여 씨는 앞서 파산 공보에 이름을 올린 뒤 벵골로 건너갔는데, 벤저민 앨런 씨가 동행했다. 두 신사 모두 동인도 회사로부터 외과의 직책을 받았던 것이다. 그들은 각각 황열병을 열네 번씩 앓고 나서, 약간의 절제를 시도해 보기로 마음먹었으며, 그 후로는 잘 지내고 있다. 바델 부인은 말동무가 될 만한 여러 독신 신사들에게 하숙을 놓아 큰 이득을 보았으나, 다시는 혼인 약속 위반으로 소송을 걸지 않았다. 그녀의 변호인인 도드슨 앤드 포그 양반들은 여전히 영업을 이어 가고 있으며, 그로써 막대한 수입을 거두어들이고 있고, 또한 약삭빠른 자들 가운데서도 가장 약삭빠른 축으로 누구에게나 여겨지고 있다.\n\n샘 웰러는 약속을 지켜 2년 동안 미혼으로 지냈다. 그 기간이 끝날 무렵 나이 든 가정부가 세상을 떠나자, 픽윅 씨는 메리를 그 자리에 앉히되 즉시 웰러 씨와 혼인할 것을 조건으로 삼았고, 그녀는 군소리 없이 그리했다. 뒤뜰 정원의 문간에서 튼실한 사내아이 둘이 거듭 눈에 띄었다는 사정으로 미루어 보아, 샘에게 식구가 좀 생긴 것이라고 짐작할 만한 근거가 있다.\n\n웰러 영감은 열두 달 동안 마차를 몰았으나, 통풍에 시달린 탓에 일을 그만둘 수밖에 없었다. 허나 픽윅 씨가 그를 위해 그 지갑의 내용물을 어찌나 잘 투자해 두었던지, 그는 두둑한 노후 자금으로 은퇴할 수 있었다. 그는 그 돈으로 슈터스 힐 근처의 훌륭한 선술집에서 여전히 살고 있는데, 그곳에서는 마치 신탁을 내리는 현자처럼 받들어지고 있다. 그는 픽윅 씨와의 친분을 무던히도 자랑하며, 과부들에 대해서는 도무지 누를 길 없는 혐오감을 지금껏 간직하고 있다.\n\n픽윅 씨 자신은 새집에서 계속 살면서, 한가한 시간이면 훗날 그 한때 이름났던 클럽의 간사에게 넘긴 비망록들을 정리하거나, 샘 웰러가 소리 내어 책 읽는 것을 들으며 떠오르는 대로 이런저런 논평을 곁들이곤 했는데, 그것은 어김없이 픽윅 씨에게 크나큰 즐거움을 안겨 주었다. 그는 처음에는 스노드그래스 씨와 윈클 씨, 트런들 씨가 저마다 제 아이의 대부가 되어 달라며 거듭 청해 오는 통에 무척 시달렸으나, 이제는 거기에 익숙해져 으레 그러려니 하며 그 역할을 맡고 있다. 그는 징글 씨에게 베푼 너그러움을 후회할 일은 한 번도 없었으니, 징글이라는 사람과 잡 트로터 둘 다 때가 지나면서 사회의 떳떳한 일원이 되었기 때문이다. 다만 그들은 옛 소굴과 유혹의 무대로 되돌아가는 일만큼은 한결같이 단호히 거부해 왔다. 픽윅 씨는 이제 몸이 다소 쇠약해졌지만, 예전의 그 젊은이다운 기상은 여전히 고스란히 간직하고 있어서, 날씨 좋은 날이면 덜위치 미술관의 그림들을 감상하거나 쾌적한 인근을 거니는 모습이 지금도 자주 눈에 띈다. 그는 인근의 가난한 사람들 모두에게 알려져 있으며, 그들은 그가 지나갈 때면 어김없이 깊은 존경을 담아 모자를 벗어 보인다. 아이들은 그를 우상처럼 떠받들며, 실로 온 동네가 그러하다. 해마다 그는 워들 씨 댁에서 열리는 대가족의 흥겨운 잔치에 찾아가는데, 이때에도, 그리고 다른 모든 경우에도, 그는 변함없이 충직한 샘을 거느리니, 주인과 그 사이에는 오직 죽음만이 끝맺을 수 있는 한결같고 서로를 향한 애정이 깃들어 있다.";
if(typeof BODIES_SENT!=="undefined") BODIES_SENT["gb-580"]=[[["1.", "1."], ["The Pickwickians 2.", "픽윅 회원들 2."], ["The first Day’s Journey, and the first Evening’s Adventures; with their Consequences 3.", "첫째 날의 여정과 첫째 날 저녁의 모험, 그리고 그 결과 3."], ["A new Acquaintance--The Stroller’s Tale--A disagreeable Interruption, and an unpleasant Encounter 4.", "새로운 친구—떠돌이 배우의 이야기—불쾌한 방해와 언짢은 만남 4."], ["A Field Day and Bivouac--More new Friends--An Invitation to the Country 5. A short one--Showing, among other Matters, how Mr.", "야외 훈련일과 야영—더 많은 새 친구들—시골로의 초대 5."], ["Pickwick undertook to drive, and Mr. Winkle to ride, and how they both did it 6.", "짧은 장—여러 일들 가운데서도 픽윅 씨가 마차 모는 일을 맡고 윈클 씨가 말 타는 일을 맡은 사연과, 두 사람이 그것을 어떻게 해냈는지를 보여 주는 장 6."], ["An old-fashioned Card-party--The Clergyman’s verses--The Story of the Convict’s Return 7. How Mr.", "구식 카드놀이 모임—목사의 시구—귀환한 죄수의 이야기 7."], ["Winkle, instead of shooting at the Pigeon and killing the Crow, shot at the Crow and wounded the Pigeon; how the Dingley Dell Cricket Club played All-Muggleton, and how All- Muggleton dined at the Dingley Dell Expense; with other interesting and instructive Matters 8. Strongly illustrative of the Position, that the Course of True Love is not a Railway 9.", "윈클 씨가 비둘기를 쏘아 까마귀를 죽이는 대신 까마귀를 쏘아 비둘기를 다치게 한 사연, 딩글리 델 크리켓 클럽이 올머글턴과 시합한 사연, 그리고 올머글턴이 딩글리 델의 비용으로 만찬을 든 사연, 그 밖의 흥미롭고 유익한 일들 8."], ["A Discovery and a Chase 10. Clearing up all Doubts (if any existed) of the Disinterestedness of Mr.", "참된 사랑의 길이 결코 철길처럼 곧지 않다는 명제를 강력히 예증하는 장 9."], ["A. Jingle’s Character 11.", "발견과 추격 10."], ["Involving another Journey, and an Antiquarian Discovery; Recording Mr. Pickwick’s Determination to be present at an Election; and containing a Manuscript of the old Clergyman’s 12.", "에이 징글 씨의 인격이 사심 없음에 관한 모든 의혹을(있었다 하더라도) 말끔히 풀어 주는 장 11."], ["Descriptive of a very important Proceeding on the Part of Mr. Pickwick; no less an Epoch in his Life, than in this History 13.", "또 다른 여정과 골동품 발견을 다루고, 픽윅 씨가 선거에 참석하기로 결심한 일을 기록하며, 늙은 목사의 원고를 담은 장 12."], ["Some Account of Eatanswill; of the State of Parties therein; and of the Election of a Member to serve in Parliament for that ancient, loyal, and patriotic Borough 14.", "픽윅 씨 편에서 있었던 매우 중요한 처사를 묘사하는 장이니, 그의 생애에서뿐 아니라 이 역사에서도 한 시대를 긋는 일이다 13."], ["Comprising a brief Description of the Company at the Peacock assembled; and a Tale told by a Bagman 15. In which is given a faithful Portraiture of two distinguished Persons; and an accurate Description of a public Breakfast in their House and Grounds: which public Breakfast leads to the Recognition of an old Acquaintance, and the Commencement of anothe r Chapter 16.", "이턴스윌에 관한 약간의 기록, 그곳 정당들의 상태, 그리고 그 유서 깊고 충성스럽고 애국적인 자치구를 위해 의회에 봉직할 의원을 뽑는 선거에 관하여 14."], ["Too full of Adventure to be briefly described 17. Showing that an Attack of Rheumatism, in some Cases, acts as a Quickener to inventive Genius 18.", "공작 여관에 모인 일행에 대한 간략한 묘사와 어느 외판원이 들려준 이야기를 담은 장 15."], ["Briefly illustrative of two Points; first, the Power of Hysterics, and, secondly, the Force of Circumstances 19. A pleasant Day with an unpleasant Termination 20.", "두 명사에 대한 충실한 초상과, 그들의 저택과 정원에서 열린 공개 조찬에 대한 정확한 묘사가 주어지는 장이니, 그 공개 조찬은 옛 친구를 알아보는 일과 또 다른 장의 시작으로 이어진다 16."], ["Showing how Dodson and Fogg were Men of Business, and their Clerks Men of pleasure; and how an affecting Interview took place between Mr. Weller and his long-lost Parent; showing also what Choice Spirits assembled at the Magpie and Stump, and what a C apital Chapter the next one will be 21.", "간략히 묘사하기에는 모험이 너무도 가득한 장 17."], ["In which the old Man launches forth into his favourite Theme, and relates a Story about a queer Client 22. Mr.", "류머티즘의 발작이 어떤 경우에는 창의적 천재성을 촉진하는 작용을 한다는 것을 보여 주는 장 18."], ["Pickwick journeys to Ipswich and meets with a romantic Adventure with a middle-aged Lady in yellow Curl-papers 23. In which Mr.", "두 가지 점을 간략히 예증하는 장이니, 첫째는 히스테리의 힘이고, 둘째는 정황의 위력이다 19."], ["Samuel Weller begins to devote his Energies to the Return Match between himself and Mr. Trotter 24.", "유쾌한 하루와 그 불쾌한 결말 20."], ["Wherein Mr. Peter Magnus grows jealous, and the middle-aged Lady apprehensive, which brings the Pickwickians within the Grasp of the Law 25.", "도드슨과 포그가 어떻게 일에 밝은 자들이고 그들의 사무원들은 어떻게 노는 데 밝은 자들인지, 그리고 웰러 씨와 오래 잃었던 그의 아버지 사이에 어떻게 감동적인 상봉이 이루어졌는지를 보여 주며, 또한 매그파이 앤드 스텀프에 어떤 별난 무리가 모였는지, 다음 장이 얼마나 훌륭한 장이 될 것인지를 보여 주는 장 21."], ["Showing, among a Variety of pleasant Matters, how majestic and impartial Mr. Nupkins was; and how Mr.", "늙은이가 그가 좋아하는 화제에 빠져들어 어느 괴상한 의뢰인에 관한 이야기를 들려주는 장 22."], ["Weller returned Mr. Job Trotter’s Shuttlecock as heavily as it came- -With another Matter, which will be found in its Place 26.", "픽윅 씨가 입스위치로 여행하여 노란 컬페이퍼를 만 중년 부인과 낭만적인 모험을 겪는 장 23."], ["Which contains a brief Account of the Progress of the Action of Bardell against Pickwick 27.", "새뮤얼 웰러 씨가 자신과 트로터 씨 사이의 설욕전에 정력을 쏟기 시작하는 장 24."], ["Samuel Weller makes a Pilgrimage to Dorking, and beholds his Mother-in-law 28.", "피터 매그너스 씨가 질투하고 중년 부인이 불안해하여 픽윅 회원들이 법의 손아귀에 들어가게 되는 장 25."], ["A good-humoured Christmas Chapter, containing an Account of a Wedding, and some other Sports beside: which although in their Way even as good Customs as Marriage itself, are not quite so religiously kept up, in these degenerate Times 29. The Story of the Goblins who stole a Sexton 30.", "여러 가지 유쾌한 일들 가운데서도 너프킨스 씨가 얼마나 위엄 있고 공정했는지, 그리고 웰러 씨가 잡 트로터 씨의 셔틀콕을 날아온 만큼이나 묵직하게 되받아쳤는지를 보여 주는 장—그리고 제자리에서 밝혀질 또 다른 일을 함께 담은 장 26."], ["How the Pickwickians made and cultivated the Acquaintance of a Couple of nice young Men belonging to one of the liberal Professions; how they disported themselves on the Ice; and how their Visit came to a Conclusion 31.", "바델 대 픽윅 소송의 진행에 대한 간략한 기록을 담은 장 27. 새뮤얼 웰러가 도킹으로 순례를 떠나 그의 새어머니를 만나는 장 28."], ["Which is all about the Law, and sundry Great Authorities learned therein 32. Describes, far more fully than the Court Newsman ever did, a Bachelor’s Party, given by Mr.", "결혼식에 관한 기록과 그 밖의 몇 가지 놀이를 담은 유쾌한 크리스마스 장이니, 그 놀이들은 그 나름으로 결혼 자체만큼이나 좋은 풍습이지만, 이 타락한 시대에는 그만큼 경건하게 지켜지지는 않는다 29."], ["Bob Sawyer at his Lodgings in the Borough 33. Mr.", "교회지기를 훔쳐 간 도깨비들의 이야기 30."], ["Weller the elder delivers some Critical Sentiments respecting Literary Composition; and, assisted by his Son Samuel, pays a small Instalment of Retaliation to the Account of the Reverend Gentleman with the Red Nose 34. Is wholly devoted to a full and faithful Report of the memorable Trial of Bardell against Pickwick 35.", "픽윅 회원들이 어느 자유직에 속한 멋진 두 청년과 어떻게 알게 되어 사귀었는지, 어떻게 얼음 위에서 즐겼는지, 그리고 그들의 방문이 어떻게 끝났는지를 다룬 장 31."], ["In which Mr. Pickwick thinks he had better go to Bath; and goes accordingly 36.", "온통 법과 그에 정통한 여러 위대한 권위자들에 관한 장 32."], ["The chief Features of which will be found to be an authentic Version of the Legend of Prince Bladud, and a most extraordinary Calamity that befell Mr. Winkle 37.", "보브 소여 씨가 자치구의 자기 하숙집에서 베푼 총각 파티를, 궁정 기자가 일찍이 했던 것보다 훨씬 더 충실하게 묘사하는 장 33."], ["Honourably accounts for Mr. Weller’s Absence, by describing a Soiree to which he was invited and went; also relates how he was intrusted by Mr.", "웰러 영감이 문학 작품에 관한 몇 가지 비평적 견해를 펼치고, 아들 새뮤얼의 도움을 받아 빨간 코의 목사 양반의 계정에 약간의 보복 할부금을 치르는 장 34."], ["Pickwick with a Private Mission of Delicacy and Importance 38. How Mr.", "바델 대 픽윅의 기억할 만한 재판을 온전하고 충실하게 보고하는 데 전적으로 바친 장 35."], ["Winkle, when he stepped out of the Frying-pan, walked gently and comfortably into the Fire 39.", "픽윅 씨가 배스로 가는 편이 낫겠다고 생각하여 그에 따라 가는 장 36."], ["Mr. Samuel Weller, being intrusted with a Mission of Love, proceeds to execute it; with what Success will hereinafter appear 40.", "그 주요 내용이 블래더드 왕자 전설의 믿을 만한 판본과, 윈클 씨에게 닥친 지극히 기이한 재난임이 밝혀질 장 37."], ["Introduces Mr. Pickwick to a new and not uninteresting Scene in the great Drama of Life 41.", "웰러 씨가 초대받아 참석한 야회를 묘사함으로써 그의 부재를 명예롭게 해명하고, 또한 그가 픽윅 씨로부터 미묘하고 중요한 비밀 임무를 어떻게 위임받았는지를 이야기하는 장 38."], ["What befell Mr. Pickwick when he got into the Fleet; what Prisoners he saw there; and how he passed the Night 42.", "윈클 씨가 프라이팬에서 빠져나오자마자 어떻게 부드럽고 편안하게 불 속으로 걸어 들어갔는지를 다룬 장 39."], ["Illustrative, like the preceding one, of the old Proverb, that Adversity brings a Man acquainted with strange Bedfellows--Likewise containing Mr.", "새뮤얼 웰러 씨가 사랑의 임무를 위임받아 그것을 수행하러 나서는 장이니, 그 성과가 어떠했는지는 이후에 밝혀질 것이다 40."], ["Pickwick’s extraordinary and startling Announcement to Mr. Samuel Weller 43.", "픽윅 씨를 인생이라는 위대한 연극의 새롭고도 결코 흥미롭지 않다 할 수 없는 장면으로 안내하는 장 41."], ["Showing how Mr. Samuel Weller got into Difficulties 44.", "픽윅 씨가 플리트에 들어갔을 때 어떤 일이 닥쳤는지, 그곳에서 어떤 죄수들을 보았는지, 그리고 그날 밤을 어떻게 보냈는지를 다룬 장 42."], ["Treats of divers little Matters which occurred in the Fleet, and of Mr. Winkle’s mysterious Behaviour; and shows how the poor Chancery Prisoner obtained his Release at last 45.", "앞 장과 마찬가지로, 역경이 사람을 낯선 잠자리 동무들과 사귀게 한다는 옛 속담을 예증하는 장—또한 픽윅 씨가 새뮤얼 웰러 씨에게 한 놀랍고도 충격적인 선언을 담은 장 43."], ["Descriptive of an affecting Interview between Mr. Samuel Weller and a Family Party.", "새뮤얼 웰러 씨가 어떻게 곤경에 빠졌는지를 보여 주는 장 44."], ["Mr. Pickwick makes a Tour of the diminutive World he inhabits, and resolves to mix with it, in Future, as little as possible 46.", "플리트에서 일어난 여러 가지 소소한 일들과 윈클 씨의 알 수 없는 행동을 다루고, 가엾은 형평법원 죄수가 어떻게 마침내 석방되었는지를 보여 주는 장 45."], ["Records a touching Act of delicate Feeling not unmixed with Pleasantry, achieved and performed by Messrs. Dodson and Fogg 47.", "새뮤얼 웰러 씨와 한 가족 일행 사이의 감동적인 상봉을 묘사하는 장."], ["Is chiefly devoted to Matters of Business, and the temporal Advantage of Dodson and Fogg--Mr. Winkle reappears under extraordinary Circumstances--Mr.", "픽윅 씨가 자신이 사는 이 작디작은 세계를 두루 돌아보고, 앞으로는 그것과 되도록 적게 어울리리라 결심하는 장 46."], ["Pickwick’s Benevolence proves stronger than his Obstinacy 48. Relates how Mr.", "도드슨과 포그 두 분이 이룩하고 행한, 익살과 뒤섞이지 않은 것은 아닌, 섬세한 감정의 갸륵한 행위를 기록하는 장 47."], ["Pickwick, with the Assistance of Samuel Weller, essayed to soften the Heart of Mr. Benjamin Allen, and to mollify the Wrath of Mr.", "주로 사무적인 일들과 도드슨과 포그의 현세적 이득에 바친 장—윈클 씨가 기이한 정황 아래 다시 나타나고—픽윅 씨의 자비심이 그의 고집보다 더 강함이 드러나는 장 48."], ["Robert Sawyer 49. Containing the Story of the Bagman’s Uncle 50.", "픽윅 씨가 새뮤얼 웰러의 도움을 받아 어떻게 벤저민 앨런 씨의 마음을 누그러뜨리고 로버트 소여 씨의 분노를 가라앉히려 애썼는지를 이야기하는 장 49."], ["How Mr. Pickwick sped upon his Mission, and how he was reinforced in the Outset by a most unexpected Auxiliary 51.", "외판원의 삼촌 이야기를 담은 장 50."], ["In which Mr. Pickwick encounters an old Acquaintance--To which fortunate Circumstance the Reader is mainly indebted for Matter of thrilling Interest herein set down, concerning two great Public Men of Might and Power 52.", "픽윅 씨가 그의 임무를 어떻게 서둘러 수행했는지, 그리고 그가 출발하자마자 어떻게 더없이 뜻밖의 조력자에 의해 힘을 얻게 되었는지를 다룬 장 51."], ["Involving a serious Change in the Weller Family, and the untimely Downfall of Mr. Stiggins 53.", "픽윅 씨가 옛 친구와 마주치는 장—독자는 바로 그 다행스러운 정황 덕분에, 여기에 적힌 두 위대한 권력자에 관한 짜릿하게 흥미로운 이야기를 주로 얻게 된다 52."], ["Comprising the final Exit of Mr. Jingle and Job Trotter, with a great Morning of business in Gray’s Inn Square--Concluding with a Double Knock at Mr.", "웰러 가에 일어난 심각한 변화와 스티긴스 씨의 때 이른 몰락을 다룬 장 53."], ["Perker’s Door 54. Containing some Particulars relative to the Double Knock, and other Matters: among which certain interesting Disclosures relative to Mr.", "징글 씨와 잡 트로터의 마지막 퇴장, 그레이즈 인 광장에서 벌어진 분주한 위대한 아침나절을 포함하는 장—퍼커 씨의 문을 두 번 두드리는 소리로 끝맺는 장 54."], ["Snodgrass and a Young Lady are by no Means irrelevant to this History 55. Mr.", "그 두 번의 노크에 관련된 몇 가지 자세한 사정과 그 밖의 일들을 담은 장이니, 그 가운데 스노드그래스 씨와 한 젊은 숙녀에 관한 흥미로운 폭로들은 결코 이 역사와 무관하지 않다 55."], ["Solomon Pell, assisted by a Select Committee of Coachmen, arranges the affairs of the elder Mr. Weller 56.", "솔로몬 펠 씨가 마부들로 구성된 특별 위원회의 도움을 받아 웰러 영감의 일을 처리하는 장 56."], ["An important Conference takes place between Mr. Pickwick and Samuel Weller, at which his Parent assists--An old Gentleman in a snuff-coloured Suit arrives unexpectedly 57.", "픽윅 씨와 새뮤얼 웰러 사이에 중요한 회담이 벌어지고 그의 아버지가 거드는 장—코담배 빛깔의 옷을 입은 노신사가 뜻밖에 도착하는 장 57."], ["In which the Pickwick Club is finally dissolved, and everything concluded to the Satisfaction of Everybody", "픽윅 클럽이 마침내 해산되고 모든 일이 모두를 만족시키는 가운데 마무리되는 장\n픽윅 클럽 유고집"]], [["# CHAPTER I. THE PICKWICKIANS", "# CHAPTER I. THE PICKWICKIANS"]], [["The first ray of light which illumines the gloom, and converts into a dazzling brilliancy that obscurity in which the earlier history of the public career of the immortal Pickwick would appear to be involved, is derived from the perusal of the following entry in the Transactions of the Pickwick Club, which the editor of these papers feels the highest pleasure in laying before his readers, as a proof of the careful attention, indefatigable assiduity, and nice discrimination, with which his search among the multifarious documents confided to him has been conducted.", "불멸의 픽윅이 걸어온 공적 생애의 초기 역사를 뒤덮은 듯한 그 어둠을 밝히고, 그 모호함을 눈부신 광채로 바꾸어 놓는 첫 줄기 빛은, 픽윅 클럽 의사록에 실린 다음 기록을 정독함으로써 얻어진다. 이 기록을 독자 앞에 내놓으면서 본 문서의 편집자는 더없는 기쁨을 느끼는바, 그에게 맡겨진 갖가지 문서들 사이에서 행해진 그의 탐색이 얼마나 세심한 주의와 지칠 줄 모르는 근면, 그리고 정밀한 분별로 이루어졌는지를 입증하는 증거로서 그러하다."]], [["‘May 12, 1827. Joseph Smiggers, Esq., P.V.P.M.P.C.", "'1827년 5월 12일."], ["[Perpetual Vice- President--Member Pickwick Club], presiding.", "조지프 스미거스 귀하, 픽윅 클럽 종신 부회장 겸 회원(P.V.P.M.P.C."], ["The following resolutions unanimously agreed to:--", "— 종신 부회장, 픽윅 클럽 회원) 사회. 다음 결의안들이 만장일치로 가결되었음."]], [["‘That this Association has heard read, with feelings of unmingled satisfaction, and unqualified approval, the paper communicated by Samuel Pickwick, Esq., G.C.M.P.C.", "'본 협회는, 새뮤얼 픽윅 귀하, 픽윅 클럽 총의장 겸 회원(G.C.M.P.C."], ["[General Chairman--Member Pickwick Club], entitled “Speculations on the Source of the Hampstead Ponds, with some Observations on the Theory of Tittlebats;”", "— 총의장, 픽윅 클럽 회원)께서 전달하신 「햄프스테드 연못들의 수원(水源)에 관한 고찰, 그리고 가시고기 이론에 관한 약간의 소견」이라는 제목의 논문이 낭독되는 것을, 섞임 없는 만족과 무조건적인 찬동의 마음으로 청취하였음."], ["and that this Association does hereby return its warmest thanks to the said Samuel Pickwick, Esq., G.C.M.P.C., for the same.", "또한 본 협회는 이에 대하여 상기 새뮤얼 픽윅 귀하, 총의장 겸 회원께 가장 따뜻한 감사의 뜻을 표하는 바임."]], [["‘That while this Association is deeply sensible of the advantages which must accrue to the cause of science, from the production to which they have just adverted--no less than from the unwearied researches of Samuel Pickwick, Esq., G.C.M.P.C., in Hornsey, Highgate, Brixton, and Camberwell--they cannot but entertain a lively sense of the inestimable benefits which must inevitably result from carrying the speculations of that learned man into a wider field, from extending his travels, and, consequently, enlarging his sphere of observation, to the advancement of knowledge, and the diffusion of learning.", "'본 협회는, 방금 언급한 그 저작으로부터 — 또한 새뮤얼 픽윅 귀하, 총의장 겸 회원께서 혼지, 하이게이트, 브릭스턴, 캠버웰에서 행하신 지칠 줄 모르는 연구로부터 — 학문의 대의에 반드시 돌아올 이익을 깊이 인식하는 동시에, 그 박식한 분의 고찰을 더 넓은 영역으로 확대하고, 그분의 여행을 넓혀, 따라서 그 관찰의 범위를 확장함으로써, 지식의 진보와 학문의 보급에 반드시 그리고 불가피하게 따라올 헤아릴 수 없는 이익에 대하여 생생한 감각을 품지 않을 수 없음."]], [["‘That, with the view just mentioned, this Association has taken into its serious consideration a proposal, emanating from the aforesaid, Samuel Pickwick, Esq., G.C.M.P.C., and three other Pickwickians hereinafter named, for forming a new branch of United Pickwickians, under the title of The Corresponding Society of the Pickwick Club.", "'방금 말한 견지에서, 본 협회는 상기 새뮤얼 픽윅 귀하, 총의장 겸 회원과 이후에 명시될 세 명의 다른 픽윅 회원으로부터 나온 제안, 곧 픽윅 클럽 통신협회라는 명칭 아래 연합 픽윅 회원의 새 지부를 결성하자는 제안을 진지하게 고려하였음."]], [["‘That the said proposal has received the sanction and approval of this Association.", "'상기 제안은 본 협회의 인가와 승인을 받았음."]], [["‘That the Corresponding Society of the Pickwick Club is therefore hereby constituted; and that Samuel Pickwick, Esq., G.C.M.P.C., Tracy Tupman, Esq., M.P.C., Augustus Snodgrass, Esq., M.P.C., and Nathaniel Winkle, Esq., M.P.C., are hereby nominated and appointed members of the same; and that they be requested to forward, from time to time, authenticated accounts of their journeys and investigations, of their observations of character and manners, and of the whole of their adventures, together with all tales and papers to which local scenery or associations may give rise, to the Pickwick Club, stationed in London.", "'따라서 이에 픽윅 클럽 통신협회를 구성하는 바이며, 새뮤얼 픽윅 귀하, 총의장 겸 회원, 트레이시 텁먼 귀하, 회원, 오거스터스 스노드그래스 귀하, 회원, 그리고 너새니얼 윈클 귀하, 회원을 이에 그 협회의 회원으로 지명하고 임명하는 바임. 또한 이들에게 요청하건대, 그들의 여행과 조사, 인물과 풍습에 대한 관찰, 그리고 그들의 모든 모험에 관한 확증된 기록을, 그 지역의 풍경이나 연고에서 비롯될 수 있는 모든 이야기와 문서와 더불어, 런던에 자리한 픽윅 클럽으로 수시로 보내 주기를 바라는 바임."]], [["‘That this Association cordially recognises the principle of every member of the Corresponding Society defraying his own travelling expenses; and that it sees no objection whatever to the members of the said society pursuing their inquiries for any length of time they please, upon the same terms.", "'본 협회는 통신협회의 모든 회원이 자신의 여비를 스스로 부담한다는 원칙을 진심으로 인정하며, 상기 협회의 회원들이 동일한 조건으로 원하는 어떠한 기간 동안에도 그들의 탐구를 계속하는 데 대해 어떠한 이의도 없음."]], [["‘That the members of the aforesaid Corresponding Society be, and are hereby informed, that their proposal to pay the postage of their letters, and the carriage of their parcels, has been deliberated upon by this Association: that this Association considers such proposal worthy of the great minds from which it emanated, and that it hereby signifies its perfect acquiescence therein.’", "'상기 통신협회의 회원들에게 다음과 같이 알리며 또한 이에 통지하는 바임. 곧 그들의 편지 우편료와 소포 운송료를 스스로 지불하겠다는 제안은 본 협회에서 심의되었음. 본 협회는 그러한 제안이 그것을 내놓은 위대한 정신에 걸맞은 것이라 여기며, 이에 그에 대한 완전한 동의를 표명하는 바임.'"]], [["A casual observer, adds the secretary, to whose notes we are indebted for the following account--a casual observer might possibly have remarked nothing extraordinary in the bald head, and circular spectacles, which were intently turned towards his (the secretary’s) face, during the reading of the above resolutions: to those who knew that the gigantic brain of Pickwick was working beneath that forehead, and that the beaming eyes of Pickwick were twinkling behind those glasses, the sight was indeed an interesting one.", "비망록 작성자는 덧붙이기를 — 다음에 이어지는 기록은 그의 메모에 힘입은 것이다 — 무심한 관찰자라면, 위 결의안들이 낭독되는 동안 그(비망록 작성자)의 얼굴을 향해 골똘히 돌려져 있던 그 대머리와 둥근 안경에서 어쩌면 아무런 비범함도 알아채지 못했을 것이다. 그러나 픽윅의 거대한 두뇌가 그 이마 아래에서 움직이고 있으며, 픽윅의 빛나는 두 눈이 그 안경 너머에서 반짝이고 있음을 아는 이들에게, 그 광경은 참으로 흥미로운 것이었다."], ["There sat the man who had traced to their source the mighty ponds of Hampstead, and agitated the scientific world with his Theory of Tittlebats, as calm and unmoved as the deep waters of the one on a frosty day, or as a solitary specimen of the other in the inmost recesses of an earthen jar.", "햄프스테드의 그 웅대한 연못들을 그 수원까지 추적하고, 가시고기 이론으로 학계를 들썩이게 한 바로 그 사람이 거기 앉아 있었으니, 서리 내린 날 그 연못 가운데 하나의 깊은 물처럼, 혹은 흙으로 빚은 항아리의 가장 깊숙한 곳에 들어 있는 그 가시고기 한 마리의 표본처럼 고요하고 미동도 없었다. 그리고 그 광경은 얼마나 더 흥미로워졌던가 — 추종자들로부터 일제히 \"픽윅\"을 부르는 소리가 터져 나오자, 그 저명한 인물이 온전한 생기와 활력으로 불쑥 살아나, 앞서 앉아 있던 윈저식 의자 위로 천천히 올라서서, 그 자신이 창립한 클럽을 향해 연설하기 시작했을 때 말이다."], ["And how much more interesting did the spectacle become, when, starting into full life and animation, as a simultaneous call for ‘Pickwick’ burst from his followers, that illustrious man slowly mounted into the Windsor chair, on which he had been previously seated, and addressed the club himself had founded.", "그 짜릿한 장면은 화가에게 얼마나 좋은 연구 거리가 되었으랴! 웅변하는 픽윅, 한 손은 우아하게 연미복 자락 뒤로 감추고, 다른 한 손은 그의 열렬한 웅변을 거들고자 허공에 흔들면서."], ["What a study for an artist did that exciting scene present!", "그 높아진 자리는 저 타이츠와 각반을 드러냈는데, 그것들이 평범한 사람의 몸에 걸쳐졌더라면 아무 주목도 받지 못하고 지나쳤을지 모르나, 픽윅이 그것들을 — 이런 표현을 써도 된다면 — 입혔을 때는 저절로 솟는 경외와 존경을 불러일으켰다."], ["The eloquent Pickwick, with one hand gracefully concealed behind his coat tails, and the other waving in air to assist his glowing declamation; his elevated position revealing those tights and gaiters, which, had they clothed an ordinary man, might have passed without observation, but which, when Pickwick clothed them--if we may use the expression--inspired involuntary awe and respect; surrounded by the men who had volunteered to share the perils of his travels, and who were destined to participate in the glories of his discoveries.", "그의 둘레에는 그의 여행이 안고 있는 위험을 기꺼이 함께 나누겠다고 자원한, 그리고 그의 발견의 영광에 동참할 운명을 지닌 사람들이 둘러서 있었다. 그의 오른편에는 트레이시 텁먼 씨가 앉아 있었다 — 너무도 마음 약한 텁먼, 무르익은 나이의 지혜와 경험에 더하여, 인간의 가장 흥미롭고도 용서받을 만한 약점인 사랑에 있어서는 소년의 열정과 정열까지 겸비한 사람이었다."], ["On his right sat Mr. Tracy Tupman--the too susceptible Tupman, who to the wisdom and experience of maturer years superadded the enthusiasm and ardour of a boy in the most interesting and pardonable of human weaknesses--love.", "세월과 포식은 한때 낭만적이던 그 몸매를 불려 놓았다."], ["Time and feeding had expanded that once romantic form; the black silk waistcoat had become more and more developed; inch by inch had the gold watch-chain beneath it disappeared from within the range of Tupman’s vision; and gradually had the capacious chin encroached upon the borders of the white cravat: but the soul of Tupman had known no change-- admiration of the fair sex was still its ruling passion.", "검은 비단 조끼는 점점 더 불룩해졌고, 그 아래 금시곗줄은 한 치 한 치 텁먼의 시야 밖으로 사라져 갔으며, 그 넉넉한 턱은 차츰 흰 크라바트의 경계를 잠식해 들어갔다. 그러나 텁먼의 영혼만은 아무 변화도 모른 채였으니 — 여성에 대한 흠모가 여전히 그를 지배하는 정념이었다."], ["On the left of his great leader sat the poetic Snodgrass, and near him again the sporting Winkle; the former poetically enveloped in a mysterious blue cloak with a canine-skin collar, and the latter communicating additional lustre to a new green shooting-coat, plaid neckerchief, and closely- fitted drabs.", "그의 위대한 지도자 왼편에는 시인 기질의 스노드그래스가 앉아 있었고, 그 곁에는 또 사냥을 즐기는 윈클이 있었다. 전자는 개가죽 깃을 단 신비로운 푸른 망토에 시적으로 감싸여 있었고, 후자는 새로 산 초록빛 사냥 코트와 격자무늬 목도리, 그리고 몸에 꼭 맞는 황갈색 바지에 한층 더한 광채를 더하고 있었다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick’s oration upon this occasion, together with the debate thereon, is entered on the Transactions of the Club.", "이 자리에서 픽윅 씨가 행한 연설과 그에 뒤이은 토론은 클럽 의사록에 기록되어 있다."], ["Both bear a strong affinity to the discussions of other celebrated bodies; and, as it is always interesting to trace a resemblance between the proceedings of great men, we transfer the entry to these pages.", "둘 다 다른 저명한 단체들의 토의와 강한 유사성을 띠는데, 위대한 인물들의 의사 진행 사이에서 닮은 점을 더듬어 보는 일은 언제나 흥미로운 법이므로, 우리는 그 기록을 이 지면에 옮겨 싣는다."]], [["‘Mr. Pickwick observed (says the secretary) that fame was dear to the heart of every man.", "'픽윅 씨는 관찰하기를(이라고 비망록 작성자는 적는다) 명성이란 모든 사람의 마음에 소중한 것이라 하였다."], ["Poetic fame was dear to the heart of his friend Snodgrass; the fame of conquest was equally dear to his friend Tupman; and the desire of earning fame in the sports of the field, the air, and the water was uppermost in the breast of his friend Winkle. He (Mr.", "시적 명성은 그의 벗 스노드그래스의 마음에 소중하고, 정복의 명성은 그의 벗 텁먼에게 똑같이 소중하며, 들과 하늘과 물에서 벌이는 스포츠로 명성을 얻고자 하는 욕망은 그의 벗 윈클의 가슴속에 가장 높이 자리하고 있다 하였다."], ["Pickwick) would not deny that he was influenced by human passions and human feelings (cheers)--possibly by human weaknesses (loud cries of “No”); but this he would say, that if ever the fire of self-importance broke out in his bosom, the desire to benefit the human race in preference effectually quenched it. The praise of mankind was his swing; philanthropy was his insurance office.", "그(픽윅 씨) 자신도 인간적인 정념과 인간적인 감정에 좌우된다는 것을(환호) — 어쩌면 인간적인 약점에까지 좌우된다는 것을(요란하게 \"아니오\"를 외치는 소리) 부인하지는 않겠으나, 다만 이것만은 말하겠노라 하였다."], ["(Vehement cheering.) He had felt some pride--he acknowledged it freely, and let his enemies make the most of it--he had felt some pride when he presented his Tittlebatian Theory to the world; it might be celebrated or it might not. (A cry of “It is,”", "곧 만일 자만이라는 불길이 그의 가슴속에 일찍이 타오른 적이 있다면, 무엇보다 인류에게 이바지하고자 하는 욕망이 그것을 어김없이 꺼 버렸노라고."], ["and great cheering.) He would take the assertion of that honourable Pickwickian whose voice he had just heard--it was celebrated; but if the fame of that treatise were to extend to the farthest confines of the known world, the pride with which he should reflect on the authorship of that production would be as nothing compared with the pride with which he looked around him, on this, the proudest moment of his existence. (Cheers.) He was a humble individual.", "인류의 칭송이 그가 매달리는 그네요, 박애가 그의 보험사무소라 하였다.(맹렬한 환호) 그는 얼마간 자부심을 느낀 적이 있었다 — 그는 이를 거리낌 없이 인정하며, 적들이 그것으로 마음껏 떠들도록 내버려 두겠노라 하였다 — 그는 자신의 가시고기 이론을 세상에 내놓았을 때 얼마간 자부심을 느꼈다 하였으니, 그것이 유명할 수도 있고 아닐 수도 있다 하였다.(\"유명합니다\"라는 외침과 큰 환호) 방금 그 목소리를 들은 그 명예로운 픽윅 회원의 주장을 받아들이겠노라 — 그것은 유명하다 — 그러나 설령 그 논저의 명성이 알려진 세계의 가장 먼 끝까지 뻗어 나간다 하더라도, 그 저작의 저술에 대해 그가 느낄 자부심은, 그의 생애에서 가장 자랑스러운 이 순간에 자기 둘레를 둘러보며 느끼는 자부심에 비하면 아무것도 아니라 하였다.(환호) 그는 보잘것없는 한 개인이다.(\"아니오, 아니오\") 그럼에도 그는 그들이 큰 영예와 더불어 얼마간의 위험이 따르는 임무를 위해 자신을 뽑아 주었음을 느끼지 않을 수 없다 하였다."], ["(“No, no.”) Still he could not but feel that they had selected him for a service of great honour, and of some danger. Travelling was in a troubled state, and the minds of coachmen were unsettled.", "여행은 어지러운 상태에 놓여 있고, 마부들의 마음은 안정을 잃었다 하였다."], ["Let them look abroad and contemplate the scenes which were enacting around them. Stage-coaches were upsetting in all directions, horses were bolting, boats were overturning, and boilers were bursting.", "사방을 둘러보아 자기들 주위에서 벌어지고 있는 광경들을 헤아려 보라 하였다."], ["(Cheers--a voice “No.”) No! (Cheers.) Let that honourable Pickwickian who cried “No”", "역마차들이 사방에서 뒤집히고, 말들이 날뛰며, 배들이 전복되고, 보일러들이 터지고 있다 하였다.(환호 — \"아니오\"라는 목소리) 아니라고요!"], ["so loudly come forward and deny it, if he could. (Cheers.) Who was it that cried “No”?", "(환호) 그토록 큰 소리로 \"아니오\"를 외친 그 명예로운 픽윅 회원더러 앞으로 나와, 할 수 있거든 그것을 부인해 보라 하였다.(환호) \"아니오\"를 외친 자가 누구인가?"], ["(Enthusiastic cheering.) Was it some vain and disappointed man--he would not say haberdasher (loud cheers)--who, jealous of the praise which had been--perhaps undeservedly--bestowed on his (Mr. Pickwick’s) researches, and smarting under the censure which had been heaped upon his own feeble attempts at rivalry, now took this vile and calumnious mode of--", "(열광적인 환호) 그것은 어떤 헛되고 좌절한 사내 — 그는 잡화상이라고는 말하지 않겠다 하였다(요란한 환호) — 그(픽윅 씨)의 연구에 어쩌면 분에 넘치게 주어진 칭송을 시샘하고, 자신의 변변찮은 경쟁 시도에 쏟아진 비난에 따끔하게 쓰라림을 느낀 나머지, 이제 이런 비열하고 중상모략적인 방식으로 —"]], [["‘MR. BLOTTON (of Aldgate) rose to order. Did the honourable Pickwickian allude to him? (Cries of “Order,” “Chair,” “Yes,” “No,” “Go on,” “Leave off,” etc.)", "'블로턴 씨(올드게이트 출신)가 의사 진행 발언을 위해 일어섰다. 그 명예로운 픽윅 회원은 자기를 두고 말한 것인가? (\"질서를 지키시오\", \"의장\", \"그렇소\", \"아니오\", \"계속하시오\", \"그만두시오\" 등의 외침)"]], [["‘MR. PICKWICK would not put up to be put down by clamour.", "'픽윅 씨는 떠들썩한 소리에 짓눌려 주저앉을 사람이 아니라 하였다."], ["He had alluded to the honourable gentleman. (Great excitement.)", "그는 그 명예로운 신사를 두고 말한 것이라 하였다.(크게 술렁임)"]], [["‘MR. BLOTTON would only say then, that he repelled the hon. gent.’s false and scurrilous accusation, with profound contempt. (Great cheering.) The hon. gent. was a humbug. (Immense confusion, and loud cries of “Chair,” and “Order.”)", "'블로턴 씨는 그렇다면 다만 이것만 말하겠노라 하였으니, 곧 그 명예로운 신사의 거짓되고 천박한 비난을 깊은 경멸로 물리치노라 하였다.(큰 환호) 그 명예로운 신사는 사기꾼이라 하였다.(극심한 혼란과 \"의장\", \"질서\"를 외치는 요란한 소리)"]], [["‘Mr. A. SNODGRASS rose to order. He threw himself upon the chair. (Hear.) He wished to know whether this disgraceful contest between two members of that club should be allowed to continue. (Hear, hear.)", "'에이 스노드그래스 씨가 의사 진행 발언을 위해 일어섰다. 그는 의장에게 호소하였다.(경청) 그는 그 클럽의 두 회원 사이의 이 수치스러운 다툼이 계속되도록 허용되어야 하는지 알고 싶다 하였다.(옳소, 옳소)"]], [["‘The CHAIRMAN was quite sure the hon. Pickwickian would withdraw the expression he had just made use of.", "'의장은 그 명예로운 픽윅 회원이 방금 사용한 표현을 틀림없이 철회하리라 확신하였다."]], [["‘MR. BLOTTON, with all possible respect for the chair, was quite sure he would not.", "'블로턴 씨는 의장에 대해 더없는 경의를 표하면서도, 자신은 결코 그러지 않으리라 확신한다 하였다."]], [["‘The CHAIRMAN felt it his imperative duty to demand of the honourable gentleman, whether he had used the expression which had just escaped him in a common sense.", "'의장은 그 명예로운 신사에게, 방금 그의 입에서 새어 나온 표현을 통상적인 의미로 사용한 것인지 묻는 것이 자신의 절대적인 의무라 여겼다."]], [["‘MR. BLOTTON had no hesitation in saying that he had not--he had used the word in its Pickwickian sense. (Hear, hear.) He was bound to acknowledge that, personally, he entertained the highest regard and esteem for the honourable gentleman; he had merely considered him a humbug in a Pickwickian point of view. (Hear, hear.)", "'블로턴 씨는 주저 없이 말하기를, 그렇지 않다 — 자신은 그 말을 픽윅적인 의미로 사용한 것이라 하였다.(옳소, 옳소) 그는 개인적으로는 그 명예로운 신사에 대해 더없이 높은 존경과 경의를 품고 있음을 마땅히 인정하노라 하였으며, 다만 픽윅적인 관점에서 그를 사기꾼으로 여겼을 뿐이라 하였다.(옳소, 옳소)"]], [["‘MR. PICKWICK felt much gratified by the fair, candid, and full explanation of his honourable friend.", "'픽윅 씨는 그 명예로운 벗의 공정하고 솔직하며 충분한 해명에 크게 흡족함을 느꼈다 하였다."], ["He begged it to be at once understood, that his own observations had been merely intended to bear a Pickwickian construction. (Cheers.)’", "그는 자신의 발언 또한 단지 픽윅적인 해석을 띠도록 의도된 것임을 즉시 양해해 주기를 바란다 하였다.(환호)'"]], [["Here the entry terminates, as we have no doubt the debate did also, after arriving at such a highly satisfactory and intelligible point.", "여기서 기록은 끝나는데, 그토록 더없이 만족스럽고 명료한 지점에 이른 뒤였으니, 토론 또한 그러했으리라는 데 우리는 의심을 두지 않는다."], ["We have no official statement of the facts which the reader will find recorded in the next chapter, but they have been carefully collated from letters and other MS. authorities, so unquestionably genuine as to justify their narration in a connected form.", "독자가 다음 장에서 보게 될 사실들에 관해서는 어떠한 공식 진술도 우리에게 없으나, 그것들은 편지와 그 밖의 필사 전거들로부터 세심하게 대조된 것이며, 그 진위가 너무도 확실하여 그것들을 하나로 이어진 형태로 서술하는 일을 정당하게 해 준다."]], [["CHAPTER II. THE FIRST DAY’S JOURNEY, AND THE FIRST EVENING’S ADVENTURES; WITH THEIR CONSEQUENCES\nThat punctual servant of all work, the sun, had just risen, and begun to strike a light on the morning of the thirteenth of May, one thousand eight hundred and twenty-seven, when Mr.", "만물을 부지런히 거드는 시종, 곧 태양이 막 떠올라 1827년 5월 13일 아침에 빛을 비추기 시작했을 때, 새뮤얼 픽윅 씨는 또 하나의 태양처럼 잠에서 솟구쳐 침실 창문을 활짝 열어젖히고 아래 세상을 내려다보았다."], ["Samuel Pickwick burst like another sun from his slumbers, threw open his chamber window, and looked out upon the world beneath.", "고스웰 거리가 그의 발밑에 있었고, 고스웰 거리가 그의 오른편에 있었으며—눈이 닿는 데까지 고스웰 거리가 그의 왼편으로 뻗어 있었고, 고스웰 거리의 맞은편은 길 건너에 있었다."], ["Goswell Street was at his feet, Goswell Street was on his right hand--as far as the eye could reach, Goswell Street extended on his left; and the opposite side of Goswell Street was over the way.", "'눈앞에 놓인 것들을 살피는 데 만족하고 그 너머에 감추어진 진리는 들여다보지 않는 철학자들의 시야란,' 픽윅 씨는 생각했다, '이처럼 좁은 것이다."], ["‘Such,’ thought Mr. Pickwick, ‘are the narrow views of those philosophers who, content with examining the things that lie before them, look not to the truths which are hidden beyond.", "사방에서 고스웰 거리를 둘러싸고 있는 저 감추어진 나라들을 꿰뚫어 보려는 노력 한 번 없이, 영원토록 고스웰 거리만 바라보고 만족할 수도 있으리라.'"], ["As well might I be content to gaze on Goswell Street for ever, without one effort to penetrate to the hidden countries which on every side surround it.’ And having given vent to this beautiful reflection, Mr.", "이 아름다운 사색을 토로한 뒤, 픽윅 씨는 옷을 몸에 꿰고, 또 옷가지들을 여행 가방에 꿰어 넣기 시작했다."], ["Pickwick proceeded to put himself into his clothes, and his clothes into his portmanteau.", "위인은 의복을 정돈하는 데 좀처럼 지나치게 까다롭지 않은 법이다."], ["Great men are seldom over scrupulous in the arrangement of their attire; the operation of shaving, dressing, and coffee-imbibing was soon performed; and, in another hour, Mr.", "면도하고, 옷을 입고, 커피를 들이켜는 일이 곧 끝났다."], ["Pickwick, with his portmanteau in his hand, his telescope in his greatcoat pocket, and his note-book in his waistcoat, ready for the reception of any discoveries worthy of being noted down, had arrived at the coach-stand in St. Martin’s-le-Grand.", "그리고 다시 한 시간 뒤, 픽윅 씨는 한 손에 여행 가방을 들고, 외투 주머니에 망원경을 넣고, 적어 둘 만한 어떤 발견이든 기록할 채비로 조끼에 수첩을 꽂은 채, 세인트 마틴스르그랜드의 마차 정거장에 다다라 있었다."]], [["‘Cab!’ said Mr.", "\"마차!\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Here you are, sir,’ shouted a strange specimen of the human race, in a sackcloth coat, and apron of the same, who, with a brass label and number round his neck, looked as if he were catalogued in some collection of rarities.", "\"여기 있습니다, 손님.\" 자루천으로 만든 외투와 같은 천의 앞치마를 두른, 인류 가운데 기묘한 표본 하나가 외쳤다."], ["This was the waterman. ‘Here you are, sir.", "그는 목에 놋쇠 표찰과 번호를 걸고 있어, 마치 무슨 진기품 수집 목록에라도 올라 있는 것처럼 보였다."], ["Now, then, fust cab!’ And the first cab having been fetched from the public- house, where he had been smoking his first pipe, Mr.", "이 사람은 물지기였다. \"여기 있습니다, 손님."], ["Pickwick and his portmanteau were thrown into the vehicle.", "자, 그럼, 첫 번째 마차!\" 그리고 첫 번째 마차의 마부가 첫 파이프를 피우고 있던 선술집에서 불려 나오자, 픽윅 씨와 그의 여행 가방은 마차 안으로 던져 넣어졌다."]], [["‘Golden Cross,’ said Mr.", "\"골든 크로스로.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Only a bob’s vorth, Tommy,’ cried the driver sulkily, for the information of his friend the waterman, as the cab drove off.", "\"겨우 1실링짜리 손님이로구먼, 토미.\" 마차가 출발하자 마부가 친구인 물지기에게 알려 주려는 듯 시무룩하게 외쳤다."]], [["‘How old is that horse, my friend?’ inquired Mr.", "\"이 말은 나이가 몇이오, 친구?\""], ["Pickwick, rubbing his nose with the shilling he had reserved for the fare.", "픽윅 씨가 차비로 떼어 둔 1실링으로 코를 문지르며 물었다."]], [["‘Forty-two,’ replied the driver, eyeing him askant.", "\"마흔둘이오.\" 마부가 그를 곁눈질하며 대답했다."]], [["‘What!’ ejaculated Mr.", "\"뭐라고요!\""], ["Pickwick, laying his hand upon his note-book.", "픽윅 씨가 수첩에 손을 얹으며 소리쳤다."], ["The driver reiterated his former statement. Mr.", "마부는 앞서 한 말을 다시 한 번 되풀이했다."], ["Pickwick looked very hard at the man’s face, but his features were immovable, so he noted down the fact forthwith.", "픽윅 씨가 그 사내의 얼굴을 뚫어지게 바라보았으나, 그 표정은 미동도 없었으므로, 그는 곧바로 그 사실을 적어 두었다."]], [["‘And how long do you keep him out at a time?’ inquired Mr.", "\"그러면 한 번 끌고 나오면 얼마나 오래 부리오?\""], ["Pickwick, searching for further information.", "픽윅 씨가 더 많은 정보를 캐며 물었다."]], [["‘Two or three veeks,’ replied the man.", "\"두세 주일이오.\" 사내가 대답했다."]], [["‘Weeks!’ said Mr.", "\"주일이라고요!\""], ["Pickwick in astonishment, and out came the note-book again.", "픽윅 씨가 놀라 말했고, 다시 수첩이 나왔다."]], [["‘He lives at Pentonwil when he’s at home,’ observed the driver coolly, ‘but we seldom takes him home, on account of his weakness.’", "\"집에 있을 때는 펜턴빌에서 지내지요.\" 마부가 태연하게 말했다, \"하지만 우리는 좀처럼 이 녀석을 집에 데려가지 않소, 약해 빠진 탓에.\""]], [["‘On account of his weakness!’ reiterated the perplexed Mr.", "\"약해 빠진 탓이라고요!\""], ["Pickwick.", "어리둥절해진 픽윅 씨가 되뇌었다."]], [["‘He always falls down when he’s took out o’ the cab,’ continued the driver, ‘but when he’s in it, we bears him up werry tight, and takes him in werry short, so as he can’t werry well fall down; and we’ve got a pair o’ precious large wheels on, so ven he does move, they run after him, and he must go on--he can’t help it.’", "\"이 녀석은 마차에서 풀려나기만 하면 늘 쓰러져 버린답니다.\" 마부가 말을 이었다, \"하지만 마차에 매여 있을 때는 우리가 아주 바짝 떠받쳐 주고, 또 고삐를 아주 짧게 잡아 주니까, 좀처럼 쓰러질 수가 없지요. 게다가 우리한테는 아주 큼지막한 바퀴 한 쌍이 달려 있으니, 녀석이 일단 움직이기만 하면 그 바퀴가 뒤따라 굴러오는 통에, 녀석은 나아가는 수밖에 없소—어쩔 도리가 없지요.\""]], [["Mr. Pickwick entered every word of this statement in his note-book, with the view of communicating it to the club, as a singular instance of the tenacity of life in horses under trying circumstances. The entry was scarcely completed when they reached the Golden Cross. Down jumped the driver, and out got Mr. Pickwick. Mr. Tupman, Mr. Snodgrass, and Mr. Winkle, who had been anxiously waiting the arrival of their illustrious leader, crowded to welcome him.", "픽윅 씨는 이 진술을 한 마디도 빠짐없이 수첩에 적어 넣었으니, 고된 처지에 놓인 말의 끈질긴 생명력을 보여 주는 진기한 사례로 클럽에 전할 작정이었다. 그 기록을 채 마치기도 전에 그들은 골든 크로스에 다다랐다. 마부가 뛰어내렸고, 픽윅 씨가 내렸다. 자기네 저명한 지도자가 도착하기를 애타게 기다리고 있던 터프먼 씨, 스노드그래스 씨, 그리고 윈클 씨가 그를 맞이하려 몰려들었다."]], [["‘Here’s your fare,’ said Mr.", "\"여기 차비요.\""], ["Pickwick, holding out the shilling to the driver.", "픽윅 씨가 마부에게 1실링을 내밀며 말했다."]], [["What was the learned man’s astonishment, when that unaccountable person flung the money on the pavement, and requested in figurative terms to be allowed the pleasure of fighting him (Mr. Pickwick) for the amount!", "그 까닭 모를 인간이 돈을 길바닥에 내동댕이치더니, 그(픽윅 씨)와 그 액수를 걸고 한바탕 붙어 볼 즐거움을 허락해 달라고 비유적인 말투로 청했을 때, 그 학식 높은 분의 놀라움이란 어떠했겠는가!"]], [["‘You are mad,’ said Mr.", "\"미쳤군.\""], ["Snodgrass.", "스노드그래스 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Or drunk,’ said Mr.", "\"아니면 취했거나.\""], ["Winkle.", "윈클 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Or both,’ said Mr.", "\"아니면 둘 다거나.\""], ["Tupman.", "터프먼 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Come on!’ said the cab-driver, sparring away like clockwork.", "\"덤벼!\" 마부가 태엽 인형처럼 주먹을 휘두르며 말했다."], ["‘Come on-- all four on you.’", "\"덤벼—넷 다 덤비라고.\""]], [["‘Here’s a lark!’ shouted half a dozen hackney coachmen.", "\"여기 구경거리 났다!\" 삯마차 마부 대여섯이 외쳤다."], ["‘Go to vork, Sam!--and they crowded with great glee round the party.", "\"한판 붙어, 샘!\" 그러고는 신이 나서 일행 주위로 우르르 몰려들었다."]], [["‘What’s the row, Sam?’ inquired one gentleman in black calico sleeves.", "\"무슨 소동이야, 샘?\" 검은 옥양목 소매를 단 신사 한 사람이 물었다."]], [["‘Row!’ replied the cabman, ‘what did he want my number for?’", "\"소동!\" 마부가 대답했다. \"이자가 내 번호는 왜 적어 가는 거냐고?\""]], [["‘I didn’t want your number,’ said the astonished Mr.", "\"난 당신 번호를 적은 적이 없소.\""], ["Pickwick.", "깜짝 놀란 픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘What did you take it for, then?’ inquired the cabman.", "\"그럼 왜 적어 갔소?\" 마부가 물었다."]], [["‘I didn’t take it,’ said Mr.", "\"난 적은 적 없다니까.\""], ["Pickwick indignantly.", "픽윅 씨가 분개하여 말했다."]], [["‘Would anybody believe,’ continued the cab-driver, appealing to the crowd, ‘would anybody believe as an informer’ud go about in a man’s cab, not only takin’ down his number, but ev’ry word he says into the bargain’ (a light flashed upon Mr.", "\"누가 믿겠소,\" 마부가 군중에게 호소하며 말을 이었다, \"어느 밀고꾼이 남의 마차에 올라타서는 번호를 적어 가는 것도 모자라, 덤으로 사람이 하는 말까지 한 마디도 빠짐없이 받아 적었다는 걸 누가 믿겠냐고요.\""], ["Pickwick--it was the note-book).", "(그제야 픽윅 씨의 머리에 번쩍 떠오르는 것이 있었으니—바로 수첩이었다.)"]], [["‘Did he though?’ inquired another cabman.", "\"정말 그랬어?\" 다른 마부가 물었다."]], [["‘Yes, did he,’ replied the first; ‘and then arter aggerawatin’ me to assault him, gets three witnesses here to prove it. But I’ll give it him, if I’ve six months for it.", "\"그래, 그랬다니까.\""], ["Come on!’ and the cabman dashed his hat upon the ground, with a reckless disregard of his own private property, and knocked Mr.", "처음 마부가 대답했다. \"게다가 날 약 올려서 자기를 치게 만들어 놓고는, 여기 증인을 셋이나 세워 증명하려 들잖아."], ["Pickwick’s spectacles off, and followed up the attack with a blow on Mr.", "하지만 여섯 달 콩밥을 먹는 한이 있어도 한 방 먹여 주겠어."], ["Pickwick’s nose, and another on Mr. Pickwick’s chest, and a third in Mr.", "덤벼!\""], ["Snodgrass’s eye, and a fourth, by way of variety, in Mr. Tupman’s waistcoat, and then danced into the road, and then back again to the pavement, and finally dashed the whole temporary supply of breath out of Mr.", "그러고는 마부가 제 사유 재산은 안중에도 없다는 듯이 모자를 땅바닥에 내동댕이치더니, 픽윅 씨의 안경을 후려쳐 떨어뜨리고, 이어 픽윅 씨의 코에 한 방, 픽윅 씨의 가슴에 또 한 방, 스노드그래스 씨의 눈에 세 번째 방, 그리고 변화를 주느라 터프먼 씨의 조끼에 네 번째 방을 날린 다음, 길 한복판으로 춤추듯 뛰어나갔다가 다시 보도로 돌아와, 마침내 윈클 씨의 몸에 잠시 채워져 있던 숨을 통째로 후려쳐 빼 버렸다."], ["Winkle’s body; and all in half a dozen seconds.", "그 모든 것이 겨우 대여섯 초 만의 일이었다."]], [["‘Where’s an officer?’ said Mr.", "\"순경은 어디 있어?\""], ["Snodgrass.", "스노드그래스 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Put ‘em under the pump,’ suggested a hot-pieman.", "\"펌프 밑에다 처박아 버려.\" 뜨거운 파이를 파는 사람이 제안했다."]], [["‘You shall smart for this,’ gasped Mr.", "\"이 일로 톡톡히 대가를 치를 거요.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 헐떡이며 말했다."]], [["‘Informers!’ shouted the crowd.", "\"밀고꾼이다!\" 군중이 외쳤다."]], [["‘Come on,’ cried the cabman, who had been sparring without cessation the whole time.", "\"덤벼.\" 그동안 쉴 새 없이 주먹을 휘두르던 마부가 외쳤다."]], [["The mob hitherto had been passive spectators of the scene, but as the intelligence of the Pickwickians being informers was spread among them, they began to canvass with considerable vivacity the propriety of enforcing the heated pastry-vendor’s proposition: and there is no saying what acts of personal aggression they might have committed, had not the affray been unexpectedly terminated by the interposition of a new-comer.", "여태껏 무리는 그 장면을 가만히 구경만 하고 있었으나, 픽윅 회원들이 밀고꾼이라는 소식이 그들 사이에 퍼지자, 그 흥분한 파이 장수의 제안을 실행에 옮기는 것이 옳은지 어떤지를 자못 활기차게 의논하기 시작했다. 그리고 만일 새로 나타난 한 인물의 끼어듦으로 이 난투극이 뜻밖에 끝나지 않았더라면, 그들이 어떤 폭행을 저질렀을지는 알 수 없는 노릇이었다."]], [["‘What’s the fun?’ said a rather tall, thin, young man, in a green coat, emerging suddenly from the coach-yard.", "\"무슨 재미난 일이오?\" 초록색 외투를 입은, 다소 키가 크고 마른 청년 하나가 마차 마당에서 불쑥 나타나며 말했다."]], [["‘Informers!’ shouted the crowd again.", "\"밀고꾼이오!\" 군중이 다시 외쳤다."]], [["‘We are not,’ roared Mr.", "\"우리는 그렇지 않소.\""], ["Pickwick, in a tone which, to any dispassionate listener, carried conviction with it.", "픽윅 씨가 사심 없는 청자라면 누구든 믿을 만한 어조로 우렁차게 말했다."]], [["‘Ain’t you, though--ain’t you?’ said the young man, appealing to Mr.", "\"아니라고요, 정말?—정말 아니에요?\""], ["Pickwick, and making his way through the crowd by the infallible process of elbowing the countenances of its component members.", "청년이 픽윅 씨에게 호소하듯 말하며, 무리를 이룬 자들의 얼굴을 팔꿈치로 헤집는다는 어김없는 방법으로 군중을 뚫고 나아갔다."]], [["That learned man in a few hurried words explained the real state of the case.", "그 학식 높은 분은 몇 마디 다급한 말로 일의 실상을 설명했다."]], [["‘Come along, then,’ said he of the green coat, lugging Mr.", "\"그럼, 이리 오시오.\""], ["Pickwick after him by main force, and talking the whole way. Here, No.", "초록 외투의 사내가 픽윅 씨를 억지로 끌어당기며, 가는 내내 떠들어 댔다."], ["924, take your fare, and take yourself off--respectable gentleman--know him well-- none of your nonsense--this way, sir--where’s your friends?--all a mistake, I see--never mind--accidents will happen--best regulated families--never say die--down upon your luck--Pull him up--Put that in his pipe--like the flavour--damned rascals.’ And with a lengthened string of similar broken sentences, delivered with extraordinary volubility, the stranger led the way to the traveller’s waiting-room, whither he was closely followed by Mr.", "\"이봐, 924호, 차비 받고 썩 꺼져—훌륭한 신사분이야—내가 잘 아는 분이지—허튼소리 집어치워—이쪽이오, 손님—친구분들은 어디 계시오?—다 오해였소, 알겠소—신경 쓰지 마시오—사고란 일어나기 마련이지—아무리 점잖은 집안에서도—기죽지 마시오—운이 나빴을 뿐—저놈을 혼내 주시오—저놈 콧대를 꺾어 놔야지—본때를 보여 줘야 해—빌어먹을 악당 같으니.\""], ["Pickwick and his disciples.", "이런 식의 끊어진 문장들을 길게 늘어놓으며, 그것도 놀라운 속도로 쏟아 내며, 낯선 사내는 여행객 대기실로 앞장서 갔고, 픽윅 씨와 그의 제자들이 바짝 뒤따랐다."]], [["‘Here, waiter!’ shouted the stranger, ringing the bell with tremendous violence, ‘glasses round--brandy-and-water, hot and strong, and sweet, and plenty,--eye damaged, Sir?", "\"여기, 종업원!\" 낯선 사내가 종을 무섭게 흔들어 대며 외쳤다."], ["Waiter!", "\"잔을 죽 돌려—브랜디에 물 타서, 뜨겁고 진하게, 달게, 넉넉히—눈을 다치셨소, 손님? 종업원!"], ["raw beef-steak for the gentleman’s eye--nothing like raw beef-steak for a bruise, sir; cold lamp-post very good, but lamp-post inconvenient--damned odd standing in the open street half an hour, with your eye against a lamp-post--eh,-- very good--ha!", "이 신사분 눈에 댈 날고기 비프스테이크 가져와—멍에는 날고기 비프스테이크만 한 게 없지요, 손님. 차가운 가로등 기둥도 아주 좋지만, 가로등 기둥은 불편하거든—거리 한복판에 반 시간이나 눈을 가로등에 댄 채 서 있는 건 빌어먹게 우스운 꼴이지—어, 그렇잖소—하!"], ["ha!’ And the stranger, without stopping to take breath, swallowed at a draught full half a pint of the reeking brandy-and-water, and flung himself into a chair with as much ease as if nothing uncommon had occurred.", "하!\" 그러고는 낯선 사내가 숨 돌릴 새도 없이 김이 무럭무럭 나는 브랜디 물을 단숨에 반 파인트나 들이켜고는, 무슨 별일도 없었다는 듯 태연히 의자에 몸을 던졌다."]], [["While his three companions were busily engaged in proffering their thanks to their new acquaintance, Mr. Pickwick had leisure to examine his costume and appearance.", "그의 세 동료가 새로 사귄 이에게 고마움을 표하느라 분주한 동안, 픽윅 씨는 그의 옷차림과 풍모를 살펴볼 여유가 있었다."]], [["He was about the middle height, but the thinness of his body, and the length of his legs, gave him the appearance of being much taller.", "그는 중간쯤 되는 키였으나, 몸이 마른 데다 다리가 길어 훨씬 더 커 보였다."], ["The green coat had been a smart dress garment in the days of swallow-tails, but had evidently in those times adorned a much shorter man than the stranger, for the soiled and faded sleeves scarcely reached to his wrists.", "초록 외투는 연미복이 유행하던 시절에는 멋들어진 정장이었겠으나, 그 시절에는 분명 이 낯선 사내보다 훨씬 키가 작은 사람을 꾸며 주었을 것이니, 더럽고 빛바랜 소매가 손목에도 채 닿지 않았기 때문이다."], ["It was buttoned closely up to his chin, at the imminent hazard of splitting the back; and an old stock, without a vestige of shirt collar, ornamented his neck.", "외투는 등판이 쩍 갈라질 위험을 무릅쓰고 턱밑까지 단단히 단추를 채워 놓았고, 셔츠 깃의 자취라곤 없는 낡은 목띠가 그의 목을 장식하고 있었다."], ["His scanty black trousers displayed here and there those shiny patches which bespeak long service, and were strapped very tightly over a pair of patched and mended shoes, as if to conceal the dirty white stockings, which were nevertheless distinctly visible.", "그의 변변찮은 검은 바지에는 군데군데 오랜 봉사를 말해 주는 반들거리는 자국이 드러나 있었고, 그것이 기우고 또 기운 한 켤레 구두 위로 아주 팽팽하게 끈으로 졸라매여 있었으니, 마치 더러운 흰 양말을 감추려는 듯했으나, 그래도 그 양말은 또렷이 드러나 보였다."], ["His long, black hair escaped in negligent waves from beneath each side of his old pinched-up hat; and glimpses of his bare wrists might be observed between the tops of his gloves and the cuffs of his coat sleeves.", "그의 길고 검은 머리카락은 낡고 찌그러진 모자 양옆 아래로 아무렇게나 물결치며 삐져나와 있었고, 장갑 끝과 외투 소맷부리 사이로 그의 맨손목이 언뜻언뜻 보였다."], ["His face was thin and haggard; but an indescribable air of jaunty impudence and perfect self-possession pervaded the whole man.", "그의 얼굴은 야위고 초췌했으나, 형언할 수 없는 멋들어진 뻔뻔함과 완벽한 침착함의 분위기가 그 사람 전체에 배어 있었다."]], [["Such was the individual on whom Mr. Pickwick gazed through his spectacles (which he had fortunately recovered), and to whom he proceeded, when his friends had exhausted themselves, to return in chosen terms his warmest thanks for his recent assistance.", "픽윅 씨가 (다행히도 되찾은) 안경 너머로 바라본 인물이 바로 이러했으며, 친구들이 인사치레를 다 마치자 픽윅 씨는 곧 골라 뽑은 말로 그가 방금 베푼 도움에 더없이 따뜻한 감사를 표하기 시작했다."]], [["‘Never mind,’ said the stranger, cutting the address very short, ‘said enough--no more; smart chap that cabman--handled his fives well; but if I’d been your friend in the green jemmy--damn me--punch his head,--‘cod I would,--pig’s whisper--pieman too,--no gammon.’", "\"신경 쓰지 마시오.\" 낯선 사내가 인사말을 짤막하게 잘라 버리며 말했다. \"충분히 하셨소—그만하면 됐어. 그 마부 녀석 제법 야무지더군—주먹을 곧잘 썼어. 하지만 내가 만일 댁의 초록 외투를 걸친 친구였다면—빌어먹을—그놈 대갈통을 한 방 갈겨 줬을 거요—암, 그랬고말고—한순간에 끝내 버렸지—파이 장수 녀석도—농담이 아니오.\""]], [["This coherent speech was interrupted by the entrance of the Rochester coachman, to announce that ‘the Commodore’ was on the point of starting.", "이 조리 있는 연설은 '코모도어호'가 막 출발하려 한다고 알리러 들어온 로체스터행 마차의 마부 때문에 중단되었다."]], [["‘Commodore!’ said the stranger, starting up, ‘my coach--place booked,-- one outside--leave you to pay for the brandy-and-water,--want change for a five,--bad silver--Brummagem buttons--won’t do--no go--eh?’ and he shook his head most knowingly.", "\"코모도어!\" 낯선 사내가 벌떡 일어서며 말했다. \"내가 탈 마차요—자리는 예약해 뒀지—바깥자리 하나—브랜디 물값은 댁이 치르도록 맡기겠소—5파운드 거슬러 받아야 하는데—불량 은화라—버밍엄제 단추 같은 가짜—안 통해—어림없지—어떻소?\" 그러고는 다 안다는 듯 고개를 절레절레 흔들었다."]], [["Now it so happened that Mr.", "그런데 마침 픽윅 씨와 그의 세 동료도 로체스터를 첫 기착지로 삼기로 정한 터였다."], ["Pickwick and his three companions had resolved to make Rochester their first halting-place too; and having intimated to their new-found acquaintance that they were journeying to the same city, they agreed to occupy the seat at the back of the coach, where they could all sit together.", "그래서 새로 사귄 이에게 자기들도 같은 도시로 가는 길이라고 알린 뒤, 그들은 다 함께 앉을 수 있는 마차 뒤쪽 자리를 차지하기로 합의했다."]], [["‘Up with you,’ said the stranger, assisting Mr.", "\"자, 올라가시오.\""], ["Pickwick on to the roof with so much precipitation as to impair the gravity of that gentleman’s deportment very materially.", "낯선 사내가 픽윅 씨를 지붕 위로 부축해 올리면서, 어찌나 급하게 서둘렀던지 그 신사의 위엄 있는 거동을 적잖이 해치고 말았다."]], [["‘Any luggage, Sir?’ inquired the coachman.", "\"짐이 있으십니까, 손님?\" 마부가 물었다."]], [["‘Who--I?", "\"누구—나 말이오?"], ["Brown paper parcel here, that’s all--other luggage gone by water--packing-cases, nailed up--big as houses--heavy, heavy, damned heavy,’ replied the stranger, as he forced into his pocket as much as he could of the brown paper parcel, which presented most suspicious indications of containing one shirt and a handkerchief.", "여기 갈색 종이 꾸러미 하나, 그게 전부요—나머지 짐은 배편으로 보냈지—못질해 봉한 포장 상자들—집채만 한 것들이오—무겁고, 무겁고, 빌어먹게 무겁지.\" 낯선 사내가 대답하며, 갈색 종이 꾸러미를 되도록 많이 주머니에 우겨 넣었는데, 그 꾸러미는 셔츠 한 장과 손수건 한 장이 들어 있을 뿐임을 자못 수상쩍게 드러내고 있었다."]], [["‘Heads, heads--take care of your heads!’ cried the loquacious stranger, as they came out under the low archway, which in those days formed the entrance to the coach-yard.", "\"머리, 머리—머리 조심하시오!\" 그 시절 마차 마당의 입구를 이루고 있던 낮은 아치 아래로 나오면서 수다스러운 낯선 사내가 외쳤다."], ["‘Terrible place--dangerous work--other day-- five children--mother--tall lady, eating sandwiches--forgot the arch-- crash--knock--children look round--mother’s head off--sandwich in her hand--no mouth to put it in--head of a family off--shocking, shocking!", "\"끔찍한 곳이지—위험한 일이오—얼마 전에도—아이 다섯에—어머니가—키 큰 부인이, 샌드위치를 먹으면서—아치를 깜박했지—와장창—쾅—아이들이 돌아보니—어머니 머리가 떨어져 나갔어—손에는 샌드위치를 쥔 채로—넣을 입이 없어진 거지—한 집안의 가장이 머리가 날아간 거요—끔찍해, 끔찍해!"], ["Looking at Whitehall, sir?--fine place--little window--somebody else’s head off there, eh, sir?--he didn’t keep a sharp look-out enough either- -eh, Sir, eh?’", "화이트홀을 보고 계시오, 손님?—훌륭한 곳이지—조그만 창문 하나—거기서도 어떤 분 머리가 날아갔지, 안 그렇소, 손님?—그분도 충분히 정신을 바짝 차리지 못했던 거요—어떻소, 손님, 어떻소?\" * 화이트홀: 찰스 1세가 처형된 곳을 가리킨다."]], [["‘I am ruminating,’ said Mr.", "\"인간사의 기이한 무상함을 곰곰 생각하던 참이오.\""], ["Pickwick, ‘on the strange mutability of human affairs.’", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Ah!", "\"아!"], ["I see--in at the palace door one day, out at the window the next.", "알겠소—오늘은 궁전 문으로 들어가고, 내일은 창문으로 나가는 거지."], ["Philosopher, Sir?’", "철학자시오, 손님?\""]], [["‘An observer of human nature, Sir,’ said Mr.", "\"인간 본성을 관찰하는 사람이오.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Ah, so am I.", "\"아, 나도 그렇소."], ["Most people are when they’ve little to do and less to get.", "할 일은 적고 벌이는 더 적을 때면 사람들 대개가 그렇게 되지요."], ["Poet, Sir?’", "시인이시오, 손님?\""]], [["‘My friend Mr. Snodgrass has a strong poetic turn,’ said Mr.", "\"내 친구 스노드그래스 씨가 시적인 기질이 다분하지요.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘So have I,’ said the stranger.", "\"나도 그렇소.\" 낯선 사내가 말했다."], ["‘Epic poem--ten thousand lines-- revolution of July--composed it on the spot--Mars by day, Apollo by night--bang the field-piece, twang the lyre.’", "\"서사시—만 행짜리—칠월 혁명을—현장에서 지었지—낮에는 마르스요 밤에는 아폴로—대포를 쾅 쏘고, 수금을 뚱땅 켜고.\" * 이것은 징글 씨의 상상력이 지닌 예언적 힘을 보여 주는 놀라운 사례이니, 이 대화는 1827년에 일어난 일이고 혁명은 1830년에 일어났다."]], [["‘You were present at that glorious scene, sir?’ said Mr.", "\"그 영광스러운 장면에 직접 계셨소, 손님?\""], ["Snodgrass.", "스노드그래스 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Present!", "\"있었냐고!"], ["think I was;* fired a musket--fired with an idea--rushed into wine shop--wrote it down--back again--whiz, bang--another idea--wine shop again--pen and ink--back again--cut and slash--noble time, Sir.", "있고말고. 머스킷 총을 쏘았지—한 가지 착상에 불이 붙어서 말이오—포도주 가게로 뛰어들어가—적어 두고—다시 뛰쳐나와—휙, 쾅—또 한 가지 착상—다시 포도주 가게로—펜과 잉크—다시 뛰쳐나와—베고 또 찌르고—굉장한 시절이었소, 손님."], ["Sportsman, sir?’ abruptly turning to Mr. Winkle.", "사냥꾼이시오, 손님?\""], ["* A remarkable instance of the prophetic force of Mr. Jingle’s imagination; this dialogue occurring in the year 1827, and the Revolution in 1830.", "그가 불쑥 윈클 씨에게 돌아서며 물었다."]], [["‘A little, Sir,’ replied that gentleman.", "\"조금 합니다, 손님.\" 그 신사가 대답했다."]], [["‘Fine pursuit, sir--fine pursuit.--Dogs, Sir?’", "\"훌륭한 소일거리지요, 손님—훌륭한 소일거리고말고요—개는 기르시오, 손님?\""]], [["‘Not just now,’ said Mr.", "\"지금은 없습니다.\""], ["Winkle.", "윈클 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Ah!", "\"아!"], ["you should keep dogs--fine animals--sagacious creatures--dog of my own once--pointer--surprising instinct--out shooting one day--entering inclosure--whistled--dog stopped--whistled again--Ponto--no go; stock still--called him--Ponto, Ponto--wouldn’t move--dog transfixed--staring at a board--looked up, saw an inscription--“Gamekeeper has orders to shoot all dogs found in this inclosure”--wouldn’t pass it--wonderful dog--valuable dog that--very.’", "개를 길러야 하오—멋진 짐승들이지—영리한 녀석들이오—나도 한때 개를 한 마리 길렀는데—포인터였소—놀라운 본능을 지녔지—어느 날 사냥을 나갔는데—울타리 친 땅에 들어서서—휘파람을 불었지—개가 멈춰 섰소—다시 휘파람을 불었는데—'폰토'—꿈쩍도 않더군. 돌처럼 굳어서—불렀지—'폰토, 폰토'—움직이지를 않는 거요—개가 못 박힌 듯이—어떤 푯말을 노려보고 있길래—올려다보니, 거기 이런 글귀가 적혀 있더군—'사냥터지기에게 이 울타리 안에서 발견되는 개는 모두 쏘아 죽이라는 명령이 내려져 있음'—그 앞을 지나가려 하질 않았소—기특한 개였지—값진 개였소, 그게—참으로 그랬소.\""]], [["‘Singular circumstance that,’ said Mr.", "\"그것 참 진기한 일이군요.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick. ‘Will you allow me to make a note of it?’", "\"제가 그걸 적어 두어도 되겠습니까?\""]], [["‘Certainly, Sir, certainly--hundred more anecdotes of the same animal.-- Fine girl, Sir’ (to Mr.", "\"물론이오, 손님, 물론이지요—같은 짐승에 관한 일화가 백 가지는 더 있소—고운 아가씨군요, 손님\""], ["Tracy Tupman, who had been bestowing sundry anti-Pickwickian glances on a young lady by the roadside).", "(그는 트레이시 터프먼 씨에게 말했는데, 터프먼 씨는 길가의 한 젊은 숙녀에게 여러 차례 픽윅답지 않은 추파를 던지고 있던 참이었다)."]], [["‘Very!’ said Mr.", "\"참으로 곱군요!\""], ["Tupman.", "터프먼 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘English girls not so fine as Spanish--noble creatures--jet hair--black eyes--lovely forms--sweet creatures--beautiful.’", "\"영국 아가씨들은 스페인 아가씨들만큼 곱지는 못하오—참으로 고귀한 여인들이지—칠흑 같은 머리에—검은 눈동자에—어여쁜 자태—사랑스러운 여인들—아름답소.\""]], [["‘You have been in Spain, sir?’ said Mr.", "\"스페인에 가 보셨소, 손님?\""], ["Tracy Tupman.", "트레이시 터프먼 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Lived there--ages.’", "\"거기서 살았소—오랜 세월을.\""]], [["‘Many conquests, sir?’ inquired Mr.", "\"많은 여인을 정복하셨소, 손님?\""], ["Tupman.", "터프먼 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘Conquests!", "\"정복이라!"], ["Thousands.", "수천이오."], ["Don Bolaro Fizzgig--grandee--only daughter--Donna Christina--splendid creature--loved me to distraction--jealous father-- high-souled daughter--handsome Englishman--Donna Christina in despair-- prussic acid--stomach pump in my portmanteau--operation performed--old Bolaro in ecstasies--consent to our union--join hands and floods of tears--romantic story--very.’", "돈 볼라로 피즈긱—대귀족이지—외동딸이 있었는데—도냐 크리스티나—눈부신 여인이었소—나를 미치도록 사랑했지—질투심 많은 아버지—기개 높은 딸—잘생긴 영국인—절망에 빠진 도냐 크리스티나—청산가리를—내 여행 가방에 위세척기가 있어서—시술을 했지—늙은 볼라로가 황홀경에 빠져—우리 결합을 승낙했소—두 손을 맞잡고 눈물을 펑펑 쏟았지—낭만적인 이야기요—참으로.\""]], [["‘Is the lady in England now, sir?’ inquired Mr.", "\"그 부인은 지금 영국에 있소, 손님?\""], ["Tupman, on whom the description of her charms had produced a powerful impression.", "그녀의 매력을 묘사한 말이 강렬한 인상을 남긴 터프먼 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘Dead, sir--dead,’ said the stranger, applying to his right eye the brief remnant of a very old cambric handkerchief.", "\"죽었소, 손님—죽었지.\" 낯선 사내가 아주 낡은 삼베 손수건의 얼마 남지 않은 자투리를 오른쪽 눈에 갖다 대며 말했다."], ["‘Never recovered the stomach pump--undermined constitution--fell a victim.’", "\"위세척기에서 끝내 회복하지 못했소—체질이 망가져서—희생되고 말았지.\""]], [["‘And her father?’ inquired the poetic Snodgrass.", "\"그러면 그녀의 아버지는요?\" 시적인 스노드그래스가 물었다."]], [["‘Remorse and misery,’ replied the stranger.", "\"회한과 비참뿐이었소.\" 낯선 사내가 대답했다."], ["‘Sudden disappearance--talk of the whole city--search made everywhere without success--public fountain in the great square suddenly ceased playing--weeks elapsed-- still a stoppage--workmen employed to clean it--water drawn off--father- in-law discovered sticking head first in the main pipe, with a full confession in his right boot--took him out, and the fountain played away again, as well as ever.’", "\"갑작스러운 실종—온 도시가 떠들썩했지—샅샅이 찾았지만 허사였소—큰 광장의 공공 분수가 별안간 물을 뿜기를 멈추더니—몇 주가 지나도—여전히 막힌 채였소—인부들을 불러 청소를 시켰지—물을 다 빼냈더니—장인 어른이 본관 파이프에 머리부터 처박힌 채 발견되었소, 오른쪽 장화 속에는 전부를 자백한 글을 넣어 둔 채로—끌어내자, 분수가 예전처럼 멀쩡히 다시 물을 뿜더군.\""]], [["‘Will you allow me to note that little romance down, Sir?’ said Mr.", "\"그 짤막한 사연을 적어 두어도 되겠습니까, 손님?\""], ["Snodgrass, deeply affected.", "깊이 감동한 스노드그래스 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Certainly, Sir, certainly--fifty more if you like to hear ‘em--strange life mine--rather curious history--not extraordinary, but singular.’", "\"물론이오, 손님, 물론이지요—듣고 싶으시면 쉰 가지는 더 있소—내 삶이 참 기이하지—꽤나 별난 내력이오—대단치는 않아도, 진기하지요.\""]], [["In this strain, with an occasional glass of ale, by way of parenthesis, when the coach changed horses, did the stranger proceed, until they reached Rochester bridge, by which time the note-books, both of Mr. Pickwick and Mr. Snodgrass, were completely filled with selections from his adventures.", "이런 식으로, 마차가 말을 바꿀 때면 그 사이사이 에일 한 잔씩을 곁들여 가며, 낯선 사내는 이야기를 계속해 나갔으니, 그들이 로체스터 다리에 다다랐을 무렵에는 픽윅 씨와 스노드그래스 씨의 수첩이 둘 다 그의 모험담에서 골라 뽑은 내용으로 빼곡히 채워져 있었다."]], [["‘Magnificent ruin!’ said Mr.", "\"장엄한 폐허로다!\""], ["Augustus Snodgrass, with all the poetic fervour that distinguished him, when they came in sight of the fine old castle.", "그 멋진 옛 성이 시야에 들어오자, 오거스터스 스노드그래스 씨가 그를 돋보이게 하는 온갖 시적 열정을 담아 말했다."]], [["‘What a study for an antiquarian!’ were the very words which fell from Mr.", "\"호고가에게 얼마나 좋은 연구 거리인가!\""], ["Pickwick’s mouth, as he applied his telescope to his eye.", "픽윅 씨가 망원경을 눈에 갖다 대며 입에서 떨어뜨린 말이 바로 이것이었다."]], [["‘Ah! fine place,’ said the stranger, ‘glorious pile--frowning walls-- tottering arches--dark nooks--crumbling staircases--old cathedral too-- earthy smell--pilgrims’ feet wore away the old steps--little Saxon doors--confessionals like money-takers’ boxes at theatres--queer customers those monks--popes, and lord treasurers, and all sorts of old fellows, with great red faces, and broken noses, turning up every day-- buff jerkins too--match-locks--sarcophagus--fine place--old legends too- -strange stories: capital;’ and the stranger continued to soliloquise until they reached the Bull Inn, in the High Street, where the coach stopped.", "\"아! 훌륭한 곳이오.\" 낯선 사내가 말했다. \"장엄한 건축물이지—험상궂은 성벽—허물어져 가는 아치들—어두운 구석들—부서져 내리는 계단들—오래된 대성당도 있고—흙냄새가 나지—순례자들의 발길이 옛 계단을 닳게 했소—작은 색슨식 문들—극장 매표소 상자 같은 고해실들—그 수도사들 참 별난 손님들이었지—교황이며, 재무 대신이며, 온갖 부류의 늙은이들이, 시뻘건 큰 얼굴에 부러진 코를 하고서, 날마다 나타났지—무두질한 가죽 조끼며—화승총이며—석관이며—훌륭한 곳이오—오래된 전설들도 있지—기이한 이야기들이오. 일품이지.\" 그러고는 낯선 사내가 마차가 멈춰 선 하이 가의 불 여관에 다다를 때까지 혼잣말을 이어 갔다."]], [["‘Do you remain here, Sir?’ inquired Mr.", "\"여기 묵으시오, 손님?\""], ["Nathaniel Winkle.", "너새니얼 윈클 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘Here--not I--but you’d better--good house--nice beds--Wright’s next house, dear--very dear--half-a-crown in the bill if you look at the waiter--charge you more if you dine at a friend’s than they would if you dined in the coffee-room--rum fellows--very.’", "\"여기—나는 아니오—하지만 댁들은 묵는 게 좋겠소—좋은 집이오—침대도 훌륭하고—라이트 여관은 바로 옆집인데, 비싸오—아주 비싸지—종업원을 쳐다보기만 해도 계산서에 반 크라운이 붙소—친구 집에서 식사를 하면 커피실에서 먹는 것보다 더 받지—괴상한 작자들이오—참으로.\""]], [["Mr. Winkle turned to Mr. Pickwick, and murmured a few words; a whisper passed from Mr. Pickwick to Mr. Snodgrass, from Mr. Snodgrass to Mr. Tupman, and nods of assent were exchanged. Mr. Pickwick addressed the stranger.", "윈클 씨가 픽윅 씨에게 돌아서서 몇 마디 중얼거렸다. 픽윅 씨에게서 스노드그래스 씨에게로, 스노드그래스 씨에게서 터프먼 씨에게로 속삭임이 오갔고, 동의하는 끄덕임이 오갔다. 픽윅 씨가 낯선 사내에게 말을 건넸다."]], [["‘You rendered us a very important service this morning, sir,’ said he, ‘will you allow us to offer a slight mark of our gratitude by begging the favour of your company at dinner?’", "\"오늘 아침 당신은 우리에게 아주 중요한 도움을 베풀어 주셨소, 손님.\" 그가 말했다, \"우리의 작은 감사의 표시로, 만찬에 함께해 주시는 영광을 청해도 되겠습니까?\""]], [["‘Great pleasure--not presume to dictate, but broiled fowl and mushrooms- -capital thing!", "\"더없는 기쁨이오—이래라저래라 할 처지는 아니지만, 구운 닭에 버섯이면—일품이지!"], ["What time?’", "몇 시요?\""]], [["‘Let me see,’ replied Mr.", "\"어디 봅시다.\""], ["Pickwick, referring to his watch, ‘it is now nearly three.", "픽윅 씨가 회중시계를 보며 대답했다. \"지금이 거의 세 시군요."], ["Shall we say five?’", "다섯 시로 할까요?\""]], [["‘Suit me excellently,’ said the stranger, ‘five precisely--till then-- care of yourselves;’ and lifting the pinched-up hat a few inches from his head, and carelessly replacing it very much on one side, the stranger, with half the brown paper parcel sticking out of his pocket, walked briskly up the yard, and turned into the High Street.", "\"내게 더없이 좋소.\" 낯선 사내가 말했다. \"정각 다섯 시에—그때까지—몸조심들 하시오.\" 그러고는 찌그러진 모자를 머리에서 몇 인치 들어 올렸다가 아무렇게나 한쪽으로 잔뜩 기울여 다시 얹은 다음, 낯선 사내는 갈색 종이 꾸러미를 주머니에서 반쯤 삐죽 내민 채, 마당을 활기차게 걸어 올라가 하이 가로 접어들었다."]], [["‘Evidently a traveller in many countries, and a close observer of men and things,’ said Mr.", "\"여러 나라를 두루 다닌 여행객이자, 사람과 사물을 면밀히 관찰하는 사람임이 분명해.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘I should like to see his poem,’ said Mr.", "\"그분의 시를 보고 싶군요.\""], ["Snodgrass.", "스노드그래스 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘I should like to have seen that dog,’ said Mr.", "\"그 개를 봤더라면 좋았을 텐데요.\""], ["Winkle.", "윈클 씨가 말했다."]], [["Mr.", "터프먼 씨는 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["Tupman said nothing; but he thought of Donna Christina, the stomach pump, and the fountain; and his eyes filled with tears.", "그러나 그는 도냐 크리스티나와, 위세척기와, 분수를 생각했다. 그리고 그의 눈에는 눈물이 그렁그렁 고였다."]], [["A private sitting-room having been engaged, bedrooms inspected, and dinner ordered, the party walked out to view the city and adjoining neighbourhood.", "전용 응접실을 잡고, 침실들을 살펴보고, 만찬을 주문한 뒤, 일행은 도시와 인근 지역을 구경하러 걸어 나갔다."]], [["We do not find, from a careful perusal of Mr. Pickwick’s notes of the four towns, Stroud, Rochester, Chatham, and Brompton, that his impressions of their appearance differ in any material point from those of other travellers who have gone over the same ground.", "스트라우드, 로체스터, 채텀, 브롬프턴, 이 네 도시에 관한 픽윅 씨의 기록을 꼼꼼히 통독해 보아도, 그곳들의 외관에 대한 그의 인상이 같은 곳을 두루 다닌 다른 여행객들의 인상과 어떤 중요한 점에서도 다르다는 사실은 찾아볼 수 없다."], ["His general description is easily abridged.", "그의 전반적인 묘사는 쉽게 간추릴 수 있다."]], [["‘The principal productions of these towns,’ says Mr.", "\"이 도시들의 주요 산물은,\""], ["Pickwick, ‘appear to be soldiers, sailors, Jews, chalk, shrimps, officers, and dockyard men.", "픽윅 씨는 말한다, \"군인, 선원, 유대인, 백악, 새우, 장교, 그리고 조선소 인부인 듯하다."], ["The commodities chiefly exposed for sale in the public streets are marine stores, hard-bake, apples, flat-fish, and oysters.", "큰길가에 주로 내놓고 파는 상품은 선박 용품, 캐러멜 과자, 사과, 넙치류, 그리고 굴이다."], ["The streets present a lively and animated appearance, occasioned chiefly by the conviviality of the military.", "거리는 활기차고 생기 넘치는 모습을 띠는데, 이는 주로 군인들의 흥겨움에서 비롯된다."], ["It is truly delightful to a philanthropic mind to see these gallant men staggering along under the influence of an overflow both of animal and ardent spirits; more especially when we remember that the following them about, and jesting with them, affords a cheap and innocent amusement for the boy population.", "박애의 정신을 지닌 사람에게는, 이 늠름한 사내들이 동물적 혈기와 독한 술이 넘쳐흐른 까닭에 비틀거리며 걸어가는 모습을 보는 것이 참으로 즐거운 일이다. 더욱이 그들을 졸졸 따라다니며 놀려 대는 것이 소년 무리에게 값싸고 순진한 오락거리가 됨을 떠올린다면 더욱 그러하다."], ["Nothing,’ adds Mr.", "그들의 더없는 호인다움은,\""], ["Pickwick, ‘can exceed their good-humour.", "픽윅 씨는 덧붙인다, \"무엇과도 견줄 수 없다."], ["It was but the day before my arrival that one of them had been most grossly insulted in the house of a publican.", "내가 도착하기 바로 전날에도, 그들 가운데 한 사람이 어느 선술집에서 더없이 무례한 모욕을 당한 일이 있었다."], ["The barmaid had positively refused to draw him any more liquor; in return for which he had (merely in playfulness) drawn his bayonet, and wounded the girl in the shoulder.", "술집 아가씨가 그에게 술을 더 따라 주기를 딱 잘라 거절했던 것이다. 그 보답으로 그는 (그저 장난삼아) 총검을 뽑아 그 아가씨의 어깨에 상처를 입혔다."], ["And yet this fine fellow was the very first to go down to the house next morning and express his readiness to overlook the matter, and forget what had occurred!", "그런데도 이 훌륭한 사내는 이튿날 아침 그 집에 제일 먼저 내려가, 그 일을 너그러이 눈감아 주고 일어난 일을 잊을 용의가 있노라고 기꺼이 표명했던 것이다!"]], [["‘The consumption of tobacco in these towns,’ continues Mr.", "\"이 도시들의 담배 소비량은,\""], ["Pickwick, ‘must be very great, and the smell which pervades the streets must be exceedingly delicious to those who are extremely fond of smoking.", "픽윅 씨는 말을 잇는다, \"어마어마함이 틀림없으며, 거리에 배어 있는 그 냄새는 흡연을 극히 좋아하는 사람들에게는 더없이 향기로울 것이 분명하다."], ["A superficial traveller might object to the dirt, which is their leading characteristic; but to those who view it as an indication of traffic and commercial prosperity, it is truly gratifying.’", "피상적인 여행객이라면 이곳의 두드러진 특징인 불결함에 대해 반감을 품을지도 모르나, 그것을 교역과 상업적 번영의 징표로 보는 사람들에게는 참으로 흐뭇한 일이다.\""]], [["Punctual to five o’clock came the stranger, and shortly afterwards the dinner.", "다섯 시 정각에 낯선 사내가 왔고, 곧이어 만찬이 나왔다."], ["He had divested himself of his brown paper parcel, but had made no alteration in his attire, and was, if possible, more loquacious than ever.", "그는 갈색 종이 꾸러미는 벗어 놓았으나, 옷차림에는 아무런 변화가 없었으며, 가능하다면 전보다 한층 더 수다스러웠다."]], [["‘What’s that?’ he inquired, as the waiter removed one of the covers.", "\"이건 뭐요?\" 종업원이 덮개 하나를 치우자 그가 물었다."]], [["‘Soles, Sir.’", "\"가자미입니다, 손님.\""]], [["‘Soles--ah!--capital fish--all come from London-stage-coach proprietors get up political dinners--carriage of soles--dozens of baskets--cunning fellows.", "\"가자미라—아!—일품 생선이지—다 런던에서 오는 거요—역마차 업자들이 정치 만찬을 여느라—가자미 운송이지—바구니가 수십 개씩—교활한 작자들이오."], ["Glass of wine, Sir.’", "포도주 한잔하시지요, 손님.\""]], [["‘With pleasure,’ said Mr.", "\"기꺼이 하지요.\""], ["Pickwick; and the stranger took wine, first with him, and then with Mr. Snodgrass, and then with Mr.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Tupman, and then with Mr. Winkle, and then with the whole party together, almost as rapidly as he talked.", "그러자 낯선 사내는 먼저 그와 한 잔, 그다음 스노드그래스 씨와 한 잔, 그다음 터프먼 씨와 한 잔, 그다음 윈클 씨와 한 잔, 그다음 일행 전체와 다 함께 한 잔을, 그가 떠들어 대는 만큼이나 거의 빠른 속도로 들이켰다."]], [["‘Devil of a mess on the staircase, waiter,’ said the stranger.", "\"계단이 지독하게 어수선하군, 종업원.\" 낯선 사내가 말했다."], ["‘Forms going up--carpenters coming down--lamps, glasses, harps.", "\"긴 의자들은 올라가고—목수들은 내려오고—등이며, 유리잔이며, 하프며."], ["What’s going forward?’", "무슨 일이 벌어지는 거요?\""]], [["‘Ball, Sir,’ said the waiter.", "\"무도회입니다, 손님.\" 종업원이 말했다."]], [["‘Assembly, eh?’", "\"친목회 말이오?\""]], [["‘No, Sir, not assembly, Sir.", "\"아니요, 손님, 친목회가 아닙니다, 손님."], ["Ball for the benefit of a charity, Sir.’", "자선 기금을 마련하는 무도회입니다, 손님.\""]], [["‘Many fine women in this town, do you know, Sir?’ inquired Mr.", "\"이 도시에 고운 여인들이 많소, 혹시 아시오, 손님?\""], ["Tupman, with great interest.", "터프먼 씨가 큰 관심을 보이며 물었다."]], [["‘Splendid--capital.", "\"눈부시지요—일품이오."], ["Kent, sir--everybody knows Kent--apples, cherries, hops, and women.", "켄트요, 손님—켄트는 누구나 알지요—사과며, 버찌며, 홉이며, 그리고 여인들."], ["Glass of wine, Sir!’", "포도주 한잔하시지요, 손님!\""]], [["‘With great pleasure,’ replied Mr.", "\"기꺼이 하지요.\" 터프먼 씨가 대답했다."], ["Tupman. The stranger filled, and emptied.", "낯선 사내가 잔을 채우고, 또 비웠다."]], [["‘I should very much like to go,’ said Mr.", "\"저도 꼭 가 보고 싶군요.\""], ["Tupman, resuming the subject of the ball, ‘very much.’", "터프먼 씨가 무도회 이야기를 다시 꺼내며 말했다, \"꼭이요.\""]], [["‘Tickets at the bar, Sir,’ interposed the waiter; ‘half-a-guinea each, Sir.’", "\"입장권은 카운터에서 파십니다, 손님.\" 종업원이 끼어들었다. \"한 장에 반 기니입니다, 손님.\""]], [["Mr. Tupman again expressed an earnest wish to be present at the festivity; but meeting with no response in the darkened eye of Mr. Snodgrass, or the abstracted gaze of Mr. Pickwick, he applied himself with great interest to the port wine and dessert, which had just been placed on the table. The waiter withdrew, and the party were left to enjoy the cosy couple of hours succeeding dinner.", "터프먼 씨가 그 흥겨운 자리에 꼭 참석하고 싶다는 간절한 뜻을 다시 한 번 내비쳤으나, 스노드그래스 씨의 흐릿해진 눈에서도, 픽윅 씨의 멍한 시선에서도 아무런 호응을 얻지 못하자, 막 식탁에 차려진 포트와인과 후식에 큰 관심을 쏟았다. 종업원이 물러갔고, 일행은 만찬 뒤에 이어지는 아늑한 두어 시간을 즐기도록 남겨졌다."]], [["‘Beg your pardon, sir,’ said the stranger, ‘bottle stands--pass it round--way of the sun--through the button-hole--no heeltaps,’ and he emptied his glass, which he had filled about two minutes before, and poured out another, with the air of a man who was used to it.", "\"실례하오, 손님.\" 낯선 사내가 말했다, \"병이 멈춰 있구려—죽 돌리시오—해가 도는 방향으로—단춧구멍을 거쳐서—한 방울도 남기지 말고.\" 그러고는 두어 분 전에 채워 두었던 잔을 비우고는, 이런 일에 익숙한 사람다운 태도로 또 한 잔을 따랐다."]], [["The wine was passed, and a fresh supply ordered. The visitor talked, the Pickwickians listened.", "포도주가 돌았고, 새 병이 주문되었다."], ["Mr. Tupman felt every moment more disposed for the ball.", "손님은 떠들어 댔고, 픽윅 회원들은 귀를 기울였다."], ["Mr. Pickwick’s countenance glowed with an expression of universal philanthropy, and Mr.", "터프먼 씨는 시간이 갈수록 점점 더 무도회에 마음이 기울었다."], ["Winkle and Mr. Snodgrass fell fast asleep.", "픽윅 씨의 얼굴은 만인을 향한 박애의 표정으로 빛났고, 윈클 씨와 스노드그래스 씨는 곤히 잠들어 버렸다."]], [["‘They’re beginning upstairs,’ said the stranger--‘hear the company-- fiddles tuning--now the harp--there they go.’ The various sounds which found their way downstairs announced the commencement of the first quadrille.", "\"위층에서 시작하는군.\" 낯선 사내가 말했다. \"사람들 소리가 들리오—바이올린을 조율하는데—이제 하프요—자, 시작이오.\" 아래층까지 흘러내려 온 갖가지 소리가 첫 카드리유의 시작을 알리고 있었다."]], [["‘How I should like to go,’ said Mr.", "\"아, 정말 가 보고 싶군요.\""], ["Tupman again.", "터프먼 씨가 다시 말했다."]], [["‘So should I,’ said the stranger--‘confounded luggage,--heavy smacks-- nothing to go in--odd, ain’t it?’", "\"나도 그렇소.\" 낯선 사내가 말했다, \"빌어먹을 짐짝이—무거운 화물선에—입고 갈 게 없으니—고약하지 않소?\""]], [["Now general benevolence was one of the leading features of the Pickwickian theory, and no one was more remarkable for the zealous manner in which he observed so noble a principle than Mr. Tracy Tupman.", "그런데 만인을 향한 자비심이야말로 픽윅 사상의 으뜸가는 특징 가운데 하나였고, 그 고귀한 원칙을 그토록 열성적으로 받드는 데 트레이시 터프먼 씨보다 더 두드러진 사람도 없었다."], ["The number of instances recorded on the Transactions of the Society, in which that excellent man referred objects of charity to the houses of other members for left-off garments or pecuniary relief is almost incredible.", "그 훌륭한 분이 자선의 대상들에게 입던 옷가지나 금전적 도움을 얻도록 다른 회원들의 집으로 안내해 준 사례가 클럽 회보에 기록된 것만 해도 거의 믿기 어려울 만큼 많았다."]], [["‘I should be very happy to lend you a change of apparel for the purpose,’ said Mr.", "\"그 일에 쓰시도록 옷 한 벌 기꺼이 빌려 드리고 싶습니다만,\""], ["Tracy Tupman, ‘but you are rather slim, and I am--’", "트레이시 터프먼 씨가 말했다, \"당신은 다소 호리호리하시고, 저는—\""]], [["‘Rather fat--grown-up Bacchus--cut the leaves--dismounted from the tub, and adopted kersey, eh?--not double distilled, but double milled--ha!", "\"다소 뚱뚱하시지—다 자란 바쿠스—포도 잎은 벗어 던지고—술통에서 내려와 거친 모직을 걸치셨군, 어떻소?—두 번 증류한 게 아니라 두 번 압착한 거지—하!"], ["ha!", "하!"], ["pass the wine.’", "포도주 좀 돌리시오.\""]], [["Whether Mr. Tupman was somewhat indignant at the peremptory tone in which he was desired to pass the wine which the stranger passed so quickly away, or whether he felt very properly scandalised at an influential member of the Pickwick Club being ignominiously compared to a dismounted Bacchus, is a fact not yet completely ascertained.", "터프먼 씨가, 낯선 사내가 그토록 재빨리 비워 버리는 포도주를 어서 돌리라고 명령조로 요구받은 데에 다소 분개했는지, 아니면 픽윅 클럽의 영향력 있는 회원이 술통에서 내려온 바쿠스에 망신스럽게 견주어진 데에 지당하게도 발끈했는지는 아직 완전히 밝혀지지 않은 사실이다."], ["He passed the wine, coughed twice, and looked at the stranger for several seconds with a stern intensity; as that individual, however, appeared perfectly collected, and quite calm under his searching glance, he gradually relaxed, and reverted to the subject of the ball.", "그는 포도주를 돌리고, 두 번 헛기침을 하고는, 몇 초 동안 낯선 사내를 매서운 강렬함으로 노려보았다. 그러나 그 인물이 그의 살피는 시선 아래서도 더없이 침착하고 아주 태연해 보였으므로, 그는 차츰 누그러져 다시 무도회 이야기로 돌아갔다."]], [["‘I was about to observe, Sir,’ he said, ‘that though my apparel would be too large, a suit of my friend Mr.", "\"제가 말씀드리려던 것은요, 손님,\""], ["Winkle’s would, perhaps, fit you better.’", "그가 말했다, \"제 옷은 너무 클 테지만, 제 친구 윈클 씨의 옷 한 벌이라면 어쩌면 더 잘 맞으실 것 같다는 겁니다.\""]], [["The stranger took Mr. Winkle’s measure with his eye, and that feature glistened with satisfaction as he said, ‘Just the thing.’", "낯선 사내가 눈으로 윈클 씨의 치수를 가늠하더니, 그 눈이 만족스러움으로 반짝이며 말했다, \"안성맞춤이오.\""]], [["Mr. Tupman looked round him.", "터프먼 씨가 주위를 둘러보았다."], ["The wine, which had exerted its somniferous influence over Mr. Snodgrass and Mr.", "스노드그래스 씨와 윈클 씨에게 잠을 부르는 위력을 떨쳤던 포도주가, 어느새 픽윅 씨의 의식 속으로도 스며들어 있었다."], ["Winkle, had stolen upon the senses of Mr. Pickwick.", "그 신사는 만찬과 그 여파로 빚어지는 혼곤함에 앞서 거치는 여러 단계를 차츰 지나온 터였다."], ["That gentleman had gradually passed through the various stages which precede the lethargy produced by dinner, and its consequences.", "그는 흥겨움의 절정에서 비참함의 나락으로, 그리고 비참함의 나락에서 다시 흥겨움의 절정으로 옮아가는 흔한 변천을 겪었다."], ["He had undergone the ordinary transitions from the height of conviviality to the depth of misery, and from the depth of misery to the height of conviviality. Like a gas-lamp in the street, with the wind in the pipe, he had exhibited for a moment an unnatural brilliancy, then sank so low as to be scarcely discernible; after a short interval, he had burst out again, to enlighten for a moment; then flickered with an uncertain, staggering sort of light, and then gone out altogether.", "마치 파이프에 바람이 든 거리의 가스등처럼, 그는 한순간 부자연스러운 광채를 내뿜었다가, 이내 거의 알아볼 수 없을 만큼 사그라들었고, 잠시 뒤 다시 확 타올라 한순간 주위를 밝혔다가, 흔들리며 비틀거리는 듯한 불안정한 불빛으로 가물거리다가, 마침내 아예 꺼져 버렸다."], ["His head was sunk upon his bosom, and perpetual snoring, with a partial choke occasionally, were the only audible indications of the great man’s presence.", "그의 머리는 가슴 위로 푹 떨어졌고, 이따금 부분적으로 컥컥 막히는 소리를 곁들인 끊임없는 코골이만이 이 위인이 거기 있음을 알려 주는 유일한 청각적 표시였다."]], [["The temptation to be present at the ball, and to form his first impressions of the beauty of the Kentish ladies, was strong upon Mr.", "무도회에 참석하여 켄트 숙녀들의 아름다움에 대한 첫인상을 얻어 보고 싶은 유혹이 터프먼 씨를 강하게 사로잡았다."], ["Tupman. The temptation to take the stranger with him was equally great.", "낯선 사내를 함께 데려가고 싶은 유혹도 그에 못지않게 컸다."], ["He was wholly unacquainted with the place and its inhabitants, and the stranger seemed to possess as great a knowledge of both as if he had lived there from his infancy. Mr.", "그는 이곳과 이곳 사람들을 전혀 알지 못했는데, 낯선 사내는 어릴 때부터 여기서 살아온 사람처럼 그 둘 모두를 훤히 꿰고 있는 듯했다."], ["Winkle was asleep, and Mr. Tupman had had sufficient experience in such matters to know that the moment he awoke he would, in the ordinary course of nature, roll heavily to bed.", "윈클 씨는 잠들어 있었고, 터프먼 씨는 이런 일에 충분한 경험이 있어, 그가 깨어나는 순간 자연의 흔한 순리대로 묵직하게 침대로 굴러갈 것임을 알고 있었다."], ["He was undecided.", "그는 마음을 정하지 못했다."], ["‘Fill your glass, and pass the wine,’ said the indefatigable visitor.", "\"잔을 채워 죽 돌리시오.\" 지칠 줄 모르는 손님이 말했다."]], [["Mr.", "터프먼 씨는 요청받은 대로 했다."], ["Tupman did as he was requested; and the additional stimulus of the last glass settled his determination.", "그리고 마지막 잔이 더해 준 자극이 그의 결심을 굳혀 주었다."]], [["‘Winkle’s bedroom is inside mine,’ said Mr.", "\"윈클의 침실이 제 방 안쪽에 붙어 있습니다.\" 터프먼 씨가 말했다."], ["Tupman; ‘I couldn’t make him understand what I wanted, if I woke him now, but I know he has a dress- suit in a carpet bag; and supposing you wore it to the ball, and took it off when we returned, I could replace it without troubling him at all about the matter.’", "\"지금 그를 깨운들 제가 원하는 바를 이해시킬 수가 없겠지만, 그가 융단 가방 속에 정장 한 벌을 넣어 둔 건 압니다. 그러니 당신이 그걸 무도회에 입고 갔다가 돌아와서 벗어 놓으면, 제가 그를 조금도 성가시게 하지 않고 도로 갖다 둘 수 있을 겁니다.\""]], [["‘Capital,’ said the stranger, ‘famous plan--damned odd situation-- fourteen coats in the packing-cases, and obliged to wear another man’s-- very good notion, that--very.’", "\"일품이오.\" 낯선 사내가 말했다, \"기막힌 계획이지—빌어먹게 묘한 처지군—포장 상자 속에 외투가 열네 벌이나 있는데, 남의 옷을 빌려 입어야 하다니—아주 좋은 생각이오, 그게—참으로.\""]], [["‘We must purchase our tickets,’ said Mr.", "\"입장권을 사야 합니다.\""], ["Tupman.", "터프먼 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Not worth while splitting a guinea,’ said the stranger, ‘toss who shall pay for both--I call; you spin--first time--woman--woman--bewitching woman,’ and down came the sovereign with the dragon (called by courtesy a woman) uppermost.", "\"1기니를 쪼갤 것까지야.\" 낯선 사내가 말했다, \"누가 둘 값을 다 낼지 동전을 던집시다—내가 부르겠소. 댁이 던지시오—단번에—여자—여자—매혹적인 여자라.\" 그러자 용(예의상 여자라 부르는 그것)이 위로 오게 동전이 떨어졌다."]], [["Mr. Tupman rang the bell, purchased the tickets, and ordered chamber candlesticks.", "* 당시 소버린 금화 뒷면에는 용을 무찌르는 성 게오르기우스가 새겨져 있었는데, 이를 농으로 '여자(앞면의 여왕상)'라 부른 것이다."], ["In another quarter of an hour the stranger was completely arrayed in a full suit of Mr.", "터프먼 씨가 종을 울려 입장권을 사고, 침실용 촛대를 주문했다."], ["Nathaniel Winkle’s.", "다시 십오 분 뒤, 낯선 사내는 너새니얼 윈클 씨의 정장 한 벌을 완벽하게 차려입고 있었다."]], [["‘It’s a new coat,’ said Mr.", "\"새 외투입니다.\""], ["Tupman, as the stranger surveyed himself with great complacency in a cheval glass; ‘the first that’s been made with our club button,’ and he called his companions’ attention to the large gilt button which displayed a bust of Mr.", "낯선 사내가 체경 앞에서 자못 흡족하게 제 모습을 살피는 동안 터프먼 씨가 말했다. \"우리 클럽 단추를 달아 처음 지은 것이지요.\""], ["Pickwick in the centre, and the letters ‘P.", "그러면서 그는 동료들의 주의를, 한가운데 픽윅 씨의 흉상이 새겨지고 양옆에 'P."], ["C.’ on either side.", "C.'라는 글자가 박힌 커다란 금박 단추로 이끌었다."]], [["‘“P.", "\"'P."], ["C.”’ said the stranger--‘queer set out--old fellow’s likeness, and “P.", "C.'라.\""], ["C.”--What does “P. C.”", "낯선 사내가 말했다."], ["stand for--Peculiar Coat, eh?’ Mr.", "\"괴상한 차림이군—노친네 초상에다—'P. C.'까지—'P."], ["Tupman, with rising indignation and great importance, explained the mystic device.", "C.'가 무슨 뜻이오—'괴상한 외투(Peculiar Coat)'요, 어떻소?\" 터프먼 씨가 치미는 분노와 큰 자부심을 안고 그 신비로운 표장을 설명해 주었다."]], [["‘Rather short in the waist, ain’t it?’ said the stranger, screwing himself round to catch a glimpse in the glass of the waist buttons, which were half-way up his back.", "\"허리가 좀 짧지 않소?\" 낯선 사내가 등 한가운데까지 올라와 붙은 허리 단추를 거울로 흘끗 보려고 몸을 비틀며 말했다."], ["‘Like a general postman’s coat--queer coats those--made by contract--no measuring--mysterious dispensations of Providence--all the short men get long coats--all the long men short ones.’ Running on in this way, Mr. Tupman’s new companion adjusted his dress, or rather the dress of Mr.", "\"관리 우편배달부 외투 같군—그런 외투들 참 괴상하지—도급으로 짓거든—치수도 안 재고—신의 알 수 없는 섭리지—키 작은 사람은 죄다 긴 외투를 받고—키 큰 사람은 죄다 짧은 걸 받으니.\""], ["Winkle; and, accompanied by Mr. Tupman, ascended the staircase leading to the ballroom.", "이런 식으로 떠들어 대면서, 터프먼 씨의 새 동료는 자기 옷을, 아니 차라리 윈클 씨의 옷을 매만진 뒤, 터프먼 씨를 대동하고 무도회장으로 통하는 계단을 올라갔다."]], [["‘What names, sir?’ said the man at the door.", "\"성함이 어찌 되십니까, 손님?\" 문가에 선 사내가 말했다."], ["Mr. Tracy Tupman was stepping forward to announce his own titles, when the stranger prevented him.", "트레이시 터프먼 씨가 자기 직함을 알리려 앞으로 나서려는데, 낯선 사내가 그를 막았다."]], [["‘No names at all;’ and then he whispered Mr.", "\"이름은 아예 대지 마시오.\""], ["Tupman, ‘names won’t do-- not known--very good names in their way, but not great ones--capital names for a small party, but won’t make an impression in public assemblies--incog. the thing--gentlemen from London--distinguished foreigners--anything.’ The door was thrown open, and Mr.", "그러고는 터프먼 씨에게 속삭였다, \"이름은 안 통하오—알려지지 않았으니—그런대로 괜찮은 이름이긴 하지만, 대단한 이름은 아니지—작은 모임에서는 일품인 이름이지만, 공개 집회에서는 인상을 못 남기지—익명이 상책이오—런던에서 온 신사들—저명한 외국 인사들—뭐든 좋소.\""], ["Tracy Tupman and the stranger entered the ballroom.", "문이 활짝 열렸고, 트레이시 터프먼 씨와 낯선 사내가 무도회장으로 들어섰다."]], [["It was a long room, with crimson-covered benches, and wax candles in glass chandeliers.", "그곳은 진홍색 천을 씌운 긴 의자들과, 유리 샹들리에에 꽂힌 밀랍 양초들이 있는 기다란 방이었다."], ["The musicians were securely confined in an elevated den, and quadrilles were being systematically got through by two or three sets of dancers.", "악사들은 높직한 우리 안에 단단히 갇혀 있었고, 두세 무리의 무용수들이 카드리유를 차례차례 추어 나가고 있었다."], ["Two card-tables were made up in the adjoining card-room, and two pair of old ladies, and a corresponding number of stout gentlemen, were executing whist therein.", "옆에 딸린 카드방에는 카드 탁자 둘이 차려져, 늙은 부인 두 쌍과 그에 걸맞은 수의 살집 좋은 신사들이 휘스트를 치고 있었다."]], [["The finale concluded, the dancers promenaded the room, and Mr. Tupman and his companion stationed themselves in a corner to observe the company.", "피날레가 끝나자 무용수들이 방 안을 거닐었고, 터프먼 씨와 그의 동료는 한쪽 구석에 자리를 잡고 사람들을 살펴보았다."]], [["‘Charming women,’ said Mr.", "\"매혹적인 여인들이군요.\""], ["Tupman.", "터프먼 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Wait a minute,’ said the stranger, ‘fun presently--nobs not come yet-- queer place--dockyard people of upper rank don’t know dockyard people of lower rank--dockyard people of lower rank don’t know small gentry--small gentry don’t know tradespeople--commissioner don’t know anybody.’", "\"잠깐 기다려 보시오.\" 낯선 사내가 말했다, \"곧 재미난 구경거리가 생길 거요—거물들이 아직 안 왔거든—괴상한 곳이지—조선소의 상류층 사람들은 조선소의 하류층 사람들을 모른 척하고—조선소의 하류층 사람들은 하급 귀족을 모른 척하고—하급 귀족은 상인들을 모른 척하고—감독관은 아무도 모른 척하지.\""]], [["‘Who’s that little boy with the light hair and pink eyes, in a fancy dress?’ inquired Mr.", "\"저기 머리칼이 옅고 눈이 분홍빛인, 별난 옷을 차려입은 어린 소년은 누구요?\""], ["Tupman.", "터프먼 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘Hush, pray--pink eyes--fancy dress--little boy--nonsense--ensign 97th-- Honourable Wilmot Snipe--great family--Snipes--very.’", "\"쉿, 제발—분홍빛 눈에—별난 옷에—어린 소년이라니—당치도 않소—제97연대 소위요—존경하올 윌멋 스나이프—대단한 집안이지—스나이프 가문—참으로.\""]], [["‘Sir Thomas Clubber, Lady Clubber, and the Misses Clubber!’ shouted the man at the door in a stentorian voice.", "\"토머스 클러버 경, 클러버 부인, 그리고 클러버 양들께서 입장하십니다!\" 문가의 사내가 우렁찬 목소리로 외쳤다."], ["A great sensation was created throughout the room by the entrance of a tall gentleman in a blue coat and bright buttons, a large lady in blue satin, and two young ladies, on a similar scale, in fashionably-made dresses of the same hue.", "푸른 외투에 번쩍이는 단추를 단 키 큰 신사 하나와, 푸른 새틴을 걸친 덩치 큰 부인 하나와, 같은 빛깔의 유행하는 옷을 차려입은 비슷한 체격의 젊은 숙녀 둘이 입장하자, 온 방 안에 일대 소동이 일었다."]], [["‘Commissioner--head of the yard--great man--remarkably great man,’ whispered the stranger in Mr.", "\"감독관이오—조선소장이지—거물이오—놀랄 만한 거물이지.\""], ["Tupman’s ear, as the charitable committee ushered Sir Thomas Clubber and family to the top of the room.", "자선 위원회가 토머스 클러버 경과 그 가족을 방 윗자리로 안내하는 동안, 낯선 사내가 터프먼 씨의 귀에 대고 속삭였다."], ["The Honourable Wilmot Snipe, and other distinguished gentlemen crowded to render homage to the Misses Clubber; and Sir Thomas Clubber stood bolt upright, and looked majestically over his black kerchief at the assembled company.", "존경하올 윌멋 스나이프와 그 밖의 저명한 신사들이 클러버 양들에게 경의를 표하려 몰려들었고, 토머스 클러버 경은 꼿꼿이 똑바로 서서, 검은 목수건 너머로 모인 사람들을 위엄 있게 굽어보았다."]], [["‘Mr. Smithie, Mrs.", "\"스미시 씨, 스미시 부인, 그리고 스미시 양들.\""], ["Smithie, and the Misses Smithie,’ was the next announcement.", "다음 입장 소개였다."]], [["‘What’s Mr. Smithie?’ inquired Mr.", "\"스미시 씨는 뭐 하는 분이오?\""], ["Tracy Tupman.", "트레이시 터프먼 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘Something in the yard,’ replied the stranger.", "\"조선소에서 뭔가 하는 사람이오.\""], ["Mr. Smithie bowed deferentially to Sir Thomas Clubber; and Sir Thomas Clubber acknowledged the salute with conscious condescension.", "낯선 사내가 대답했다."], ["Lady Clubber took a telescopic view of Mrs. Smithie and family through her eye-glass and Mrs.", "스미시 씨가 토머스 클러버 경에게 공손히 절했고, 토머스 클러버 경은 의식적인 겸양으로 그 인사에 답했다."], ["Smithie stared in her turn at Mrs. Somebody-else, whose husband was not in the dockyard at all.", "클러버 부인이 안경 너머로 스미시 부인과 그 가족을 망원경 들여다보듯 훑어보았고, 스미시 부인은 또 그 나름으로, 남편이 조선소와는 아무 상관도 없는 어느 딴 부인을 빤히 쳐다보았다."]], [["‘Colonel Bulder, Mrs.", "\"불더 대령, 불더 대령 부인, 그리고 불더 양.\""], ["Colonel Bulder, and Miss Bulder,’ were the next arrivals.", "다음 도착이었다."]], [["‘Head of the garrison,’ said the stranger, in reply to Mr.", "\"수비대장이오.\""], ["Tupman’s inquiring look.", "터프먼 씨의 묻는 듯한 눈길에 답하여 낯선 사내가 말했다."]], [["Miss Bulder was warmly welcomed by the Misses Clubber; the greeting between Mrs.", "불더 양은 클러버 양들의 따뜻한 환영을 받았고, 불더 대령 부인과 클러버 부인 사이의 인사는 더없이 다정한 것이었으며, 불더 대령과 토머스 클러버 경은 코담뱃갑을 주고받으며, 마치 한 쌍의 알렉산더 셀커크처럼—'굽어보는 모든 것의 군주'처럼—보였다."], ["Colonel Bulder and Lady Clubber was of the most affectionate description; Colonel Bulder and Sir Thomas Clubber exchanged snuff-boxes, and looked very much like a pair of Alexander Selkirks--‘Monarchs of all they surveyed.’", "* 알렉산더 셀커크: 로빈슨 크루소의 모델이 된 무인도 표류자로, 카우퍼의 시 한 구절에서 인용한 것이다."]], [["While the aristocracy of the place--the Bulders, and Clubbers, and Snipes--were thus preserving their dignity at the upper end of the room, the other classes of society were imitating their example in other parts of it.", "그곳의 귀족층—불더 가, 클러버 가, 스나이프 가—이 방 윗자리에서 이렇듯 위엄을 지키는 동안, 다른 계층의 사람들도 방 다른 곳에서 그들을 본받고 있었다."], ["The less aristocratic officers of the 97th devoted themselves to the families of the less important functionaries from the dockyard.", "제97연대의 덜 귀족적인 장교들은 조선소의 덜 중요한 관리들의 가족에게 헌신했다."], ["The solicitors’ wives, and the wine-merchant’s wife, headed another grade (the brewer’s wife visited the Bulders); and Mrs. Tomlinson, the post- office keeper, seemed by mutual consent to have been chosen the leader of the trade party.", "변호사들의 부인들과 포도주 상인의 부인이 또 다른 등급을 이끌었고 (양조업자의 부인은 불더 가를 방문했다), 우체국장인 톰린슨 부인은 만장일치로 상인 패거리의 우두머리로 추대된 듯했다."]], [["One of the most popular personages, in his own circle, present, was a little fat man, with a ring of upright black hair round his head, and an extensive bald plain on the top of it--Doctor Slammer, surgeon to the 97th.", "그 자리에서, 제 패거리 안에서 가장 인기 있는 인물 가운데 하나는, 머리 둘레에 뻣뻣한 검은 머리칼이 고리처럼 둘려 있고 정수리에는 널찍한 대머리 벌판이 펼쳐진 자그마한 뚱뚱보였으니—제97연대 군의관 슬래머 박사였다."], ["The doctor took snuff with everybody, chatted with everybody, laughed, danced, made jokes, played whist, did everything, and was everywhere.", "박사는 누구하고나 코담배를 나누고, 누구하고나 잡담을 나누고, 웃고, 춤추고, 농담을 하고, 휘스트를 치고, 무엇이든 다 하고, 어디에나 있었다."], ["To these pursuits, multifarious as they were, the little doctor added a more important one than any--he was indefatigable in paying the most unremitting and devoted attention to a little old widow, whose rich dress and profusion of ornament bespoke her a most desirable addition to a limited income.", "이토록 잡다한 활동에 더하여, 그 자그마한 박사는 그 무엇보다 중요한 일을 하나 더 보태고 있었으니—그는 자그마한 늙은 과부 하나에게 더없이 끊임없고 헌신적인 정성을 쏟는 데 지칠 줄을 몰랐던 것이다. 그 과부의 값진 옷차림과 넘쳐 나는 장신구는 그녀가 빠듯한 수입에 더없이 탐나는 보탬이 될 사람임을 말해 주고 있었다."]], [["Upon the doctor, and the widow, the eyes of both Mr. Tupman and his companion had been fixed for some time, when the stranger broke silence.", "터프먼 씨와 그의 동료의 눈은 한동안 그 박사와 과부에게 못 박혀 있었는데, 그때 낯선 사내가 침묵을 깼다."]], [["‘Lots of money--old girl--pompous doctor--not a bad idea--good fun,’ were the intelligible sentences which issued from his lips.", "\"돈이 많군—늙은 아가씨라—거드름 피우는 박사라—나쁘지 않은 생각이지—재미있겠어.\" 그의 입에서 흘러나온 알아들을 만한 문장이 이것이었다."], ["Mr. Tupman looked inquisitively in his face.", "터프먼 씨가 캐묻듯 그의 얼굴을 들여다보았다."]], [["‘I’ll dance with the widow,’ said the stranger.", "\"내가 저 과부와 춤을 추겠소.\" 낯선 사내가 말했다."]], [["‘Who is she?’ inquired Mr.", "\"저 여자가 누구요?\""], ["Tupman.", "터프먼 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘Don’t know--never saw her in all my life--cut out the doctor--here goes.’ And the stranger forthwith crossed the room; and, leaning against a mantel-piece, commenced gazing with an air of respectful and melancholy admiration on the fat countenance of the little old lady.", "\"모르오—생전 처음 보는 여자요—저 박사 놈을 따돌리고—자, 갑니다.\" 그러더니 낯선 사내는 곧장 방을 가로질러 가, 벽난로 선반에 기대선 채, 그 자그마한 늙은 부인의 통통한 얼굴을 공손하고도 우수에 잠긴 흠모의 태도로 바라보기 시작했다."], ["Mr. Tupman looked on, in mute astonishment.", "터프먼 씨는 말없이 놀라며 지켜보았다."], ["The stranger progressed rapidly; the little doctor danced with another lady; the widow dropped her fan; the stranger picked it up, and presented it--a smile--a bow--a curtsey-- a few words of conversation.", "낯선 사내는 빠르게 일을 진척시켰다. 자그마한 박사가 다른 부인과 춤을 추는 사이, 과부가 부채를 떨어뜨렸고, 낯선 사내가 그것을 주워 건넸다—미소 한 번—절 한 번—답례의 인사 한 번—몇 마디 대화."], ["The stranger walked boldly up to, and returned with, the master of the ceremonies; a little introductory pantomime; and the stranger and Mrs.", "낯선 사내가 대담하게 무도회 진행자에게로 걸어가, 그를 데리고 돌아왔다."], ["Budger took their places in a quadrille.", "짤막한 소개의 무언극이 있은 뒤, 낯선 사내와 버저 부인이 카드리유 대형 속에 자리를 잡았다."]], [["The surprise of Mr. Tupman at this summary proceeding, great as it was, was immeasurably exceeded by the astonishment of the doctor.", "이 일사천리의 진행에 터프먼 씨가 느낀 놀라움도 컸지만, 박사의 경악은 그보다 이루 헤아릴 수 없이 더 컸다."], ["The stranger was young, and the widow was flattered.", "낯선 사내는 젊었고, 과부는 우쭐해 있었다."], ["The doctor’s attentions were unheeded by the widow; and the doctor’s indignation was wholly lost on his imperturbable rival.", "박사의 정성은 과부에게 안중에도 없었고, 박사의 분노는 그 태연한 연적에게 아무 효과도 없이 사그라졌다."], ["Doctor Slammer was paralysed.", "슬래머 박사는 망연자실했다."], ["He, Doctor Slammer, of the 97th, to be extinguished in a moment, by a man whom nobody had ever seen before, and whom nobody knew even now!", "자기, 곧 제97연대의 슬래머 박사가, 여태껏 아무도 본 적이 없고 지금조차 아무도 모르는 사내에게, 한순간에 꺼져 버리다니!"], ["Doctor Slammer--Doctor Slammer of the 97th rejected!", "슬래머 박사가—제97연대의 슬래머 박사가 퇴짜를 맞다니!"], ["Impossible! It could not be!", "있을 수 없는 일이었다!"], ["Yes, it was; there they were.", "그럴 리가 없었다! 그런데 사실이었다."], ["What! introducing his friend!", "저기 두 사람이 있지 않은가."], ["Could he believe his eyes!", "뭐라고! 자기 친구를 소개하다니!"], ["He looked again, and was under the painful necessity of admitting the veracity of his optics; Mrs.", "제 눈을 믿을 수 있단 말인가!"], ["Budger was dancing with Mr. Tracy Tupman; there was no mistaking the fact.", "그는 다시 보았고, 제 눈의 정확함을 인정해야 하는 고통스러운 처지에 놓였다."], ["There was the widow before him, bouncing bodily here and there, with unwonted vigour; and Mr.", "버저 부인이 트레이시 터프먼 씨와 춤을 추고 있었으니, 그 사실에는 오해의 여지가 없었다."], ["Tracy Tupman hopping about, with a face expressive of the most intense solemnity, dancing (as a good many people do) as if a quadrille were not a thing to be laughed at, but a severe trial to the feelings, which it requires inflexible resolution to encounter.", "과부가 바로 그의 눈앞에서 평소답지 않은 기운으로 온몸을 이리저리 들썩이고 있었고, 트레이시 터프먼 씨가 더없이 진지한 표정을 한 얼굴로 (적잖은 사람들이 그러하듯이) 마치 카드리유가 웃어넘길 거리가 아니라 흔들림 없는 결의로 맞서야 할 가혹한 시련이라도 되는 듯이 춤을 추며 깡충깡충 뛰어다니고 있었다."]], [["Silently and patiently did the doctor bear all this, and all the handings of negus, and watching for glasses, and darting for biscuits, and coquetting, that ensued; but, a few seconds after the stranger had disappeared to lead Mrs.", "박사는 이 모든 것을, 그리고 뒤이어 벌어진 온갖 니거스 술 건네기며, 잔 챙기기며, 비스킷 가져다 바치기며, 시시덕거림을, 말없이 참을성 있게 견뎌 냈다."], ["Budger to her carriage, he darted swiftly from the room with every particle of his hitherto-bottled-up indignation effervescing, from all parts of his countenance, in a perspiration of passion.", "그러나 낯선 사내가 버저 부인을 마차까지 바래다주려고 사라진 지 몇 초 뒤, 그는 여태껏 병 속에 가둬 두었던 분노가 한 톨도 빠짐없이 부글부글 끓어올라, 격정의 진땀이 되어 얼굴 구석구석에서 배어 나오는 채로, 쏜살같이 방을 빠져나갔다. * 니거스: 포도주에 더운물, 설탕, 향료를 탄 음료."]], [["The stranger was returning, and Mr. Tupman was beside him.", "낯선 사내가 돌아오고 있었고, 터프먼 씨가 그 곁에 있었다."], ["He spoke in a low tone, and laughed.", "그는 낮은 목소리로 무어라 말하며 웃었다."], ["The little doctor thirsted for his life.", "자그마한 박사는 그의 목숨에 굶주려 있었다."], ["He was exulting.", "그는 의기양양했다."], ["He had triumphed.", "그는 승리한 것이었다."]], [["‘Sir!’ said the doctor, in an awful voice, producing a card, and retiring into an angle of the passage, ‘my name is Slammer, Doctor Slammer, sir--97th Regiment--Chatham Barracks--my card, Sir, my card.’ He would have added more, but his indignation choked him.", "\"이보시오!\" 박사가 무시무시한 목소리로 말하며 명함 한 장을 꺼내 들고, 복도 한쪽 구석으로 물러서며 말했다, \"내 이름은 슬래머요, 슬래머 박사요, 손님—제97연대요—채텀 병영이오—내 명함이오, 손님, 내 명함.\" 그는 더 보태고 싶었으나, 분노가 그의 목을 틀어막았다."]], [["‘Ah!’ replied the stranger coolly, ‘Slammer--much obliged--polite attention--not ill now, Slammer--but when I am--knock you up.’", "\"아!\" 낯선 사내가 태연히 대답했다, \"슬래머라—고맙소—정중한 호의로구려—지금은 아프지 않소, 슬래머—하지만 아프게 되면—댁을 부르리다.\""]], [["‘You--you’re a shuffler, sir,’ gasped the furious doctor, ‘a poltroon--a coward--a liar--a--a--will nothing induce you to give me your card, sir!’", "\"댁은—댁은 발뺌하는 자요, 손님.\" 격분한 박사가 헐떡이며 말했다, \"비겁자에—겁쟁이에—거짓말쟁이에—그—그—무엇으로도 댁의 명함을 내놓게 할 수 없단 말이오, 손님!\""]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오! 알겠소.\""], ["I see,’ said the stranger, half aside, ‘negus too strong here-- liberal landlord--very foolish--very--lemonade much better--hot rooms-- elderly gentlemen--suffer for it in the morning--cruel--cruel;’ and he moved on a step or two.", "낯선 사내가 반쯤 혼잣말로 말했다, \"여기 니거스가 너무 독하군—인심 좋은 주인장이지—참 어리석은 일이오—참으로—레모네이드가 훨씬 낫지—더운 방에—나이 지긋한 양반들이라—아침이면 그 대가를 치르지—가혹해—가혹하구려.\" 그러고는 한두 걸음 옮겨 갔다."]], [["‘You are stopping in this house, Sir,’ said the indignant little man; ‘you are intoxicated now, Sir; you shall hear from me in the morning, sir. I shall find you out, sir; I shall find you out.’", "\"댁은 이 집에 묵고 있소, 손님.\" 분개한 자그마한 사내가 말했다. \"지금 댁은 취해 있소, 손님. 아침에 내 소식을 듣게 될 거요, 손님. 댁을 기어이 찾아내고 말겠소, 손님. 기어이 찾아내고 말겠소.\""]], [["‘Rather you found me out than found me at home,’ replied the unmoved stranger.", "\"댁이 날 집에서 찾아내느니 차라리 알아채는 편이 낫겠구려.\" 꿈쩍도 않는 낯선 사내가 대답했다."]], [["Doctor Slammer looked unutterable ferocity, as he fixed his hat on his head with an indignant knock; and the stranger and Mr.", "* 'find me out'에는 '찾아내다'와 '정체를 간파하다'라는 두 뜻이 있어, 낯선 사내가 말장난으로 받아친 것이다. 슬래머 박사는 분개하여 모자를 탁 내리쳐 머리에 눌러쓰며 말할 수 없는 흉포함을 드러냈다."], ["Tupman ascended to the bedroom of the latter to restore the borrowed plumage to the unconscious Winkle.", "그리고 낯선 사내와 터프먼 씨는, 아무것도 모르는 윈클에게 빌린 깃털을 도로 돌려주려고, 후자의 침실로 올라갔다."]], [["That gentleman was fast asleep; the restoration was soon made.", "그 신사는 곤히 잠들어 있었다."], ["The stranger was extremely jocose; and Mr.", "옷은 곧 제자리에 돌려놓았다."], ["Tracy Tupman, being quite bewildered with wine, negus, lights, and ladies, thought the whole affair was an exquisite joke.", "낯선 사내는 더없이 익살스러웠고, 트레이시 터프먼 씨는 포도주와 니거스, 불빛, 그리고 숙녀들에 완전히 얼이 빠져, 이 모든 일을 더없이 절묘한 농담쯤으로 여겼다. 그의 새 친구가 떠났다."], ["His new friend departed; and, after experiencing some slight difficulty in finding the orifice in his nightcap, originally intended for the reception of his head, and finally overturning his candlestick in his struggles to put it on, Mr. Tracy Tupman managed to get into bed by a series of complicated evolutions, and shortly afterwards sank into repose.", "그리고 트레이시 터프먼 씨는, 본래 제 머리를 넣게 되어 있는 잠자리 모자의 구멍을 찾는 데 약간의 어려움을 겪은 끝에, 마침내 그것을 쓰려고 안간힘을 쓰다가 촛대를 넘어뜨린 다음, 갖가지 복잡한 곡예를 거쳐 가까스로 침대로 기어들어, 곧 잠 속으로 가라앉았다."]], [["Seven o’clock had hardly ceased striking on the following morning, when Mr. Pickwick’s comprehensive mind was aroused from the state of unconsciousness, in which slumber had plunged it, by a loud knocking at his chamber door.", "이튿날 아침 일곱 시를 알리는 종소리가 채 멎기도 전에, 픽윅 씨의 포괄적인 정신은 침실 문을 요란하게 두드리는 소리에, 잠이 빠뜨려 놓았던 무의식 상태에서 깨어났다."]], [["‘Who’s there?’ said Mr.", "\"누구요?\""], ["Pickwick, starting up in bed.", "픽윅 씨가 침대에서 벌떡 일어나며 말했다."]], [["‘Boots, sir.’", "\"구두닦이입니다, 손님.\""]], [["‘What do you want?’", "\"무슨 일이오?\""]], [["‘Please, sir, can you tell me which gentleman of your party wears a bright blue dress-coat, with a gilt button with “P.", "\"황송하지만, 손님, 일행 중에 어느 신사분이 'P."], ["C.” on it?’", "C.'라는 금박 단추가 달린 밝은 푸른색 정장 외투를 입으시는지 말씀해 주시겠습니까?\""]], [["‘It’s been given out to brush,’ thought Mr.", "'손질하라고 내놓았는데,'"], ["Pickwick, ‘and the man has forgotten whom it belongs to.’", "픽윅 씨는 생각했다, '저 사람이 그게 누구 것인지 잊었나 보군.'"]], [["Mr.", "\"윈클 씨요.\""], ["Winkle,’ he called out, ‘next room but two, on the right hand.’", "그가 큰 소리로 외쳤다, \"오른편으로 두 칸 건너 다음 방이오.\""]], [["‘Thank’ee, sir,’ said the Boots, and away he went.", "\"고맙습니다, 손님.\" 구두닦이가 말하고는 가 버렸다."]], [["‘What’s the matter?’ cried Mr.", "\"무슨 일이야?\""], ["Tupman, as a loud knocking at his door roused him from his oblivious repose.", "문을 요란하게 두드리는 소리가 망각의 잠에서 그를 깨우자, 터프먼 씨가 외쳤다."]], [["‘Can I speak to Mr.", "\"윈클 씨와 말씀 좀 나눌 수 있겠습니까, 손님?\""], ["Winkle, sir?’ replied Boots from the outside.", "구두닦이가 밖에서 대답했다."]], [["‘Winkle--Winkle!’ shouted Mr.", "\"윈클—윈클!\""], ["Tupman, calling into the inner room.", "터프먼 씨가 안쪽 방을 향해 외쳤다."]], [["‘Hollo!’ replied a faint voice from within the bed-clothes.", "\"이봐요!\" 이불 속에서 가냘픈 목소리가 대답했다."]], [["‘You’re wanted--some one at the door;’ and, having exerted himself to articulate thus much, Mr.", "\"누가 찾아—문 앞에 누가 왔어.\""], ["Tracy Tupman turned round and fell fast asleep again.", "그러고는 이만큼 분명히 말하느라 안간힘을 쓴 트레이시 터프먼 씨는 돌아누워 다시 곤히 잠들어 버렸다."]], [["‘Wanted!’ said Mr.", "\"찾는다고!\""], ["Winkle, hastily jumping out of bed, and putting on a few articles of clothing; ‘wanted!", "윈클 씨가 황급히 침대에서 뛰어나와 옷가지 몇 개를 걸치며 말했다. \"찾는다고!"], ["at this distance from town--who on earth can want me?’", "도시에서 이렇게 멀리 떨어진 데서—대체 누가 날 찾는단 말인가?\""]], [["‘Gentleman in the coffee-room, sir,’ replied the Boots, as Mr.", "\"커피실에 어떤 신사분이 계십니다, 손님.\""], ["Winkle opened the door and confronted him; ‘gentleman says he’ll not detain you a moment, Sir, but he can take no denial.’", "윈클 씨가 문을 열고 마주서자 구두닦이가 대답했다. \"그 신사분 말씀이, 잠시도 붙들지 않겠지만 거절은 절대 받아들일 수 없다십니다.\""]], [["‘Very odd!’ said Mr.", "\"참 이상하군!\""], ["Winkle; ‘I’ll be down directly.’", "윈클 씨가 말했다. \"곧 내려가겠소.\""]], [["He hurriedly wrapped himself in a travelling-shawl and dressing-gown, and proceeded downstairs.", "그는 여행용 숄과 실내복을 황급히 두르고 아래층으로 내려갔다."], ["An old woman and a couple of waiters were cleaning the coffee-room, and an officer in undress uniform was looking out of the window.", "노파 한 사람과 종업원 두 사람이 커피실을 치우고 있었고, 평상복 차림의 장교 하나가 창밖을 내다보고 있었다."], ["He turned round as Mr. Winkle entered, and made a stiff inclination of the head.", "그는 윈클 씨가 들어서자 돌아서서 뻣뻣하게 고개를 까딱했다."], ["Having ordered the attendants to retire, and closed the door very carefully, he said, ‘Mr. Winkle, I presume?’", "그러고는 시중드는 이들에게 물러가라 이르고 문을 아주 조심스레 닫은 뒤 말했다, \"윈클 씨가 맞으십니까?\""]], [["‘My name is Winkle, sir.’", "\"제 이름이 윈클입니다, 손님.\""]], [["‘You will not be surprised, sir, when I inform you that I have called here this morning on behalf of my friend, Doctor Slammer, of the 97th.’", "\"제가 오늘 아침 제 친구인 제97연대의 슬래머 박사를 대신하여 이곳에 찾아왔다고 말씀드려도, 손님께서는 놀라지 않으시겠지요.\""]], [["‘Doctor Slammer!’ said Mr.", "\"슬래머 박사라고요!\""], ["Winkle.", "윈클 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Doctor Slammer.", "\"슬래머 박사입니다."], ["He begged me to express his opinion that your conduct of last evening was of a description which no gentleman could endure; and’ (he added) ‘which no one gentleman would pursue towards another.’", "그가 제게 청하기를, 어젯밤 손님의 행동은 어떤 신사도 견딜 수 없는, 그리고\" (그가 덧붙였다) \"어떤 신사도 다른 신사에게 결코 하지 않을 종류의 것이었다는 그의 견해를 전해 달라고 하더군요.\""]], [["Mr.", "윈클 씨의 놀라움은 너무도 진실하고 너무도 분명하여 슬래머 박사의 친구의 눈을 피할 수가 없었다."], ["Winkle’s astonishment was too real, and too evident, to escape the observation of Doctor Slammer’s friend; he therefore proceeded--", "그래서 그는 말을 이었다—"]], [["‘My friend, Doctor Slammer, requested me to add, that he was firmly persuaded you were intoxicated during a portion of the evening, and possibly unconscious of the extent of the insult you were guilty of.", "\"제 친구 슬래머 박사가 덧붙여 달라 청하기를, 그는 손님께서 어젯밤 한동안 취해 계셨고, 어쩌면 자신이 저지른 모욕의 정도를 의식하지 못하셨으리라고 굳게 믿는다고 합니다."], ["He commissioned me to say, that should this be pleaded as an excuse for your behaviour, he will consent to accept a written apology, to be penned by you, from my dictation.’", "만일 이것을 손님의 행동에 대한 변명으로 내세우신다면, 제가 불러 드리는 대로 손님께서 손수 쓰신 서면 사과를 받아들이는 데 동의하겠노라고 그가 제게 말해 달라 하더군요.\""]], [["‘A written apology!’ repeated Mr.", "\"서면 사과라고요!\""], ["Winkle, in the most emphatic tone of amazement possible.", "윈클 씨가 더없이 강한 경악의 어조로 되뇌었다."]], [["‘Of course you know the alternative,’ replied the visitor coolly.", "\"물론 그 대안이 무엇인지는 아시겠지요.\" 방문객이 태연하게 대답했다."]], [["‘Were you intrusted with this message to me by name?’ inquired Mr.", "\"이 전갈을 제 이름을 지목하여 받으셨소?\""], ["Winkle, whose intellects were hopelessly confused by this extraordinary conversation.", "이 기이한 대화에 머리가 절망적으로 뒤죽박죽이 된 윈클 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘I was not present myself,’ replied the visitor, ‘and in consequence of your firm refusal to give your card to Doctor Slammer, I was desired by that gentleman to identify the wearer of a very uncommon coat--a bright blue dress-coat, with a gilt button displaying a bust, and the letters “P.", "\"저는 그 자리에 직접 있지 않았습니다.\" 방문객이 대답했다."], ["C.”’", "\"그리고 손님께서 슬래머 박사에게 명함을 내놓기를 한사코 거절하신 까닭에, 저는 그 신사로부터 아주 보기 드문 외투를 입은 사람을 식별해 달라는 부탁을 받았습니다—흉상과 'P. C.'라는 글자가 박힌 금박 단추가 달린, 밝은 푸른색 정장 외투를 말입니다.\""]], [["Mr. Winkle actually staggered with astonishment as he heard his own costume thus minutely described.", "윈클 씨는 자신의 옷차림이 이토록 세세히 묘사되는 것을 듣고는 정말로 놀라 비틀거렸다."], ["Doctor Slammer’s friend proceeded:-- ‘From the inquiries I made at the bar, just now, I was convinced that the owner of the coat in question arrived here, with three gentlemen, yesterday afternoon.", "슬래머 박사의 친구가 말을 이었다—\n\"방금 카운터에서 알아본 바로, 문제의 그 외투 주인이 어제 오후 세 신사와 함께 이곳에 도착했음을 확신하게 되었습니다."], ["I immediately sent up to the gentleman who was described as appearing the head of the party, and he at once referred me to you.’", "저는 즉시 일행의 우두머리로 보인다는 신사에게 사람을 올려보냈고, 그 신사는 곧바로 저를 손님께로 안내해 주었습니다.\""]], [["If the principal tower of Rochester Castle had suddenly walked from its foundation, and stationed itself opposite the coffee-room window, Mr. Winkle’s surprise would have been as nothing compared with the profound astonishment with which he had heard this address.", "만일 로체스터 성의 본루가 별안간 그 토대에서 걸어 나와 커피실 창문 맞은편에 떡 버티고 섰다 한들, 윈클 씨의 놀라움은 그가 이 말을 들으며 느낀 깊디깊은 경악에 비하면 아무것도 아니었을 것이다."], ["His first impression was that his coat had been stolen.", "그의 첫 느낌은 자신의 외투를 도둑맞았다는 것이었다."], ["‘Will you allow me to detain you one moment?’ said he.", "\"잠시만 기다려 주시겠소?\" 그가 말했다."]], [["‘Certainly,’ replied the unwelcome visitor.", "\"물론이지요.\" 달갑잖은 방문객이 대답했다."]], [["Mr. Winkle ran hastily upstairs, and with a trembling hand opened the bag.", "윈클 씨는 황급히 위층으로 달려가, 떨리는 손으로 가방을 열었다."], ["There was the coat in its usual place, but exhibiting, on a close inspection, evident tokens of having been worn on the preceding night.", "외투는 늘 두던 자리에 있었으나, 가까이 들여다보니 지난밤 입었던 명백한 흔적을 드러내고 있었다."], ["‘It must be so,’ said Mr.", "\"그렇구나.\""], ["Winkle, letting the coat fall from his hands.", "윈클 씨가 외투를 손에서 떨어뜨리며 말했다."], ["‘I took too much wine after dinner, and have a very vague recollection of walking about the streets, and smoking a cigar afterwards.", "\"내가 만찬 뒤에 술을 너무 많이 마셔서, 거리를 쏘다니다 그 뒤에 시가를 피운 기억이 아주 어렴풋이 나는군."], ["The fact is, I was very drunk;--I must have changed my coat--gone somewhere--and insulted somebody--I have no doubt of it; and this message is the terrible consequence.’ Saying which, Mr.", "사실, 나는 잔뜩 취해 있었어—외투를 바꿔 입은 게 틀림없고—어딘가에 가서—누군가를 모욕한 거야—틀림없어. 그리고 이 전갈이 그 끔찍한 결과인 거지.\""], ["Winkle retraced his steps in the direction of the coffee-room, with the gloomy and dreadful resolve of accepting the challenge of the warlike Doctor Slammer, and abiding by the worst consequences that might ensue.", "이렇게 말하며 윈클 씨는, 호전적인 슬래머 박사의 결투 신청을 받아들이고 뒤따를지 모를 최악의 결과를 감수하리라는 음울하고 무시무시한 결심을 안고, 커피실 쪽으로 발길을 되돌렸다."]], [["To this determination Mr. Winkle was urged by a variety of considerations, the first of which was his reputation with the club.", "윈클 씨가 이 결심에 이르게 된 데에는 여러 가지 고려가 작용했는데, 그 첫째는 클럽에서의 그의 명성이었다."], ["He had always been looked up to as a high authority on all matters of amusement and dexterity, whether offensive, defensive, or inoffensive; and if, on this very first occasion of being put to the test, he shrunk back from the trial, beneath his leader’s eye, his name and standing were lost for ever.", "그는 공격이든 방어든 무해한 것이든, 온갖 오락과 기예에 관한 한 늘 높은 권위자로 추앙받아 온 터였다. 그러니 만일 시험대에 오르는 바로 이 첫 기회에, 그것도 지도자가 지켜보는 가운데 그가 시련 앞에서 움츠러든다면, 그의 이름과 지위는 영영 잃고 마는 것이었다."], ["Besides, he remembered to have heard it frequently surmised by the uninitiated in such matters that by an understood arrangement between the seconds, the pistols were seldom loaded with ball; and, furthermore, he reflected that if he applied to Mr.", "게다가 그는, 이런 일에 어두운 사람들이 흔히 추측하는 말, 곧 입회인들 사이의 묵계로 권총에 실탄을 재는 일은 좀처럼 없다는 말을 자주 들은 것을 떠올렸다."], ["Snodgrass to act as his second, and depicted the danger in glowing terms, that gentleman might possibly communicate the intelligence to Mr. Pickwick, who would certainly lose no time in transmitting it to the local authorities, and thus prevent the killing or maiming of his follower.", "더욱이 그는, 만일 스노드그래스 씨에게 입회인이 되어 달라 청하면서 그 위험을 생생한 말로 그려 보인다면, 그 신사가 어쩌면 그 소식을 픽윅 씨에게 알릴 것이고, 픽윅 씨는 분명 지체 없이 그것을 지방 당국에 전하여, 자기 추종자가 살해되거나 불구가 되는 것을 막아 줄지도 모른다고 생각했다."], ["Such were his thoughts when he returned to the coffee-room, and intimated his intention of accepting the doctor’s challenge.", "이런 것들이 그가 커피실로 돌아와 박사의 결투 신청을 받아들이겠다는 뜻을 내비쳤을 때 품고 있던 생각이었다."]], [["‘Will you refer me to a friend, to arrange the time and place of meeting?’ said the officer.", "\"만날 시간과 장소를 정할 입회인을 제게 소개해 주시겠소?\" 장교가 말했다."]], [["‘Quite unnecessary,’ replied Mr.", "\"전혀 그럴 필요 없소.\""], ["Winkle; ‘name them to me, and I can procure the attendance of a friend afterwards.’", "윈클 씨가 대답했다. \"그것들을 제게 일러 주시면, 입회인의 참석은 제가 나중에 마련하겠소.\""]], [["‘Shall we say--sunset this evening?’ inquired the officer, in a careless tone.", "\"그럼—오늘 저녁 해 질 녘으로 할까요?\" 장교가 무심한 어조로 물었다."]], [["‘Very good,’ replied Mr.", "\"좋소.\""], ["Winkle, thinking in his heart it was very bad.", "윈클 씨가 속으로는 아주 나쁘다고 여기면서 대답했다."]], [["‘You know Fort Pitt?’", "\"피트 요새는 아시오?\""]], [["‘Yes; I saw it yesterday.’", "\"예, 어제 봤소.\""]], [["‘If you will take the trouble to turn into the field which borders the trench, take the foot-path to the left when you arrive at an angle of the fortification, and keep straight on, till you see me, I will precede you to a secluded place, where the affair can be conducted without fear of interruption.’", "\"수고스럽겠지만, 참호와 맞닿은 들판으로 들어서서, 요새의 한 모퉁이에 이르거든 왼편 오솔길을 잡아, 저를 보실 때까지 곧장 나아오시면, 제가 앞장서서 방해받을 염려 없이 일을 치를 수 있는 외진 곳으로 안내하겠습니다.\""]], [["‘Fear of interruption!’ thought Mr.", "'방해받을 염려라!'"], ["Winkle.", "윈클 씨는 생각했다."]], [["‘Nothing more to arrange, I think,’ said the officer.", "\"더 정할 것은 없는 듯하군요.\" 장교가 말했다."]], [["‘I am not aware of anything more,’ replied Mr.", "\"더 생각나는 것은 없소.\" 윈클 씨가 대답했다."], ["Winkle. ‘Good-morning.’", "\"그럼, 안녕히.\""]], [["‘Good-morning;’ and the officer whistled a lively air as he strode away.", "\"안녕히 가시오.\" 그러고는 장교가 경쾌한 곡조를 휘파람으로 불며 성큼성큼 걸어갔다."]], [["That morning’s breakfast passed heavily off. Mr. Tupman was not in a condition to rise, after the unwonted dissipation of the previous night; Mr. Snodgrass appeared to labour under a poetical depression of spirits; and even Mr. Pickwick evinced an unusual attachment to silence and soda- water. Mr. Winkle eagerly watched his opportunity: it was not long wanting. Mr. Snodgrass proposed a visit to the castle, and as Mr. Winkle was the only other member of the party disposed to walk, they went out together.", "그날 아침 식사는 무겁게 흘러갔다. 터프먼 씨는 지난밤의 평소답지 않은 방탕 끝에 일어날 형편이 못 되었고, 스노드그래스 씨는 시적인 의기소침에 시달리는 듯했으며, 픽윅 씨조차 평소답지 않게 침묵과 소다수에 애착을 보였다. 윈클 씨는 열심히 기회를 엿보았는데, 그것은 오래 기다릴 것도 없었다. 스노드그래스 씨가 성을 구경하러 가자고 제안했고, 일행 가운데 걸을 마음이 있는 사람은 윈클 씨뿐이었으므로, 둘은 함께 나갔다."]], [["‘Snodgrass,’ said Mr.", "\"스노드그래스.\" 큰길을 벗어나자 윈클 씨가 말했다."], ["Winkle, when they had turned out of the public street.’Snodgrass, my dear fellow, can I rely upon your secrecy?’ As he said this, he most devoutly and earnestly hoped he could not.", "\"스노드그래스, 이보게, 자네의 입이 무거우리라 믿어도 되겠나?\" 이렇게 말하면서, 그는 더없이 간절하고 진심으로 그러지 못하기를 바랐다."]], [["‘You can,’ replied Mr.", "\"믿어도 되네.\" 스노드그래스 씨가 대답했다."], ["Snodgrass. ‘Hear me swear--’", "\"내 맹세를 들어 보게—\""]], [["‘No, no,’ interrupted Winkle, terrified at the idea of his companion’s unconsciously pledging himself not to give information; ‘don’t swear, don’t swear; it’s quite unnecessary.’", "\"아니, 아니.\" 윈클이, 제 동료가 무심코 발설하지 않겠노라 스스로 다짐하지나 않을까 겁이 나서 가로막았다. \"맹세는 말게, 맹세는 말아—그럴 필요 전혀 없네.\""]], [["Mr. Snodgrass dropped the hand which he had, in the spirit of poesy, raised towards the clouds as he made the above appeal, and assumed an attitude of attention.", "스노드그래스 씨는 위와 같이 호소하면서 시정에 겨워 구름을 향해 치켜들었던 손을 내리고, 경청하는 자세를 취했다."]], [["‘I want your assistance, my dear fellow, in an affair of honour,’ said Mr.", "\"내게 자네 도움이 필요하네, 이보게, 명예에 관한 일에서.\""], ["Winkle.", "윈클 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘You shall have it,’ replied Mr.", "\"도와주고말고.\""], ["Snodgrass, clasping his friend’s hand.", "스노드그래스 씨가 친구의 손을 꽉 잡으며 대답했다."]], [["‘With a doctor--Doctor Slammer, of the 97th,’ said Mr.", "\"어느 박사와의 일이야—제97연대의 슬래머 박사 말일세.\" 윈클 씨가 일을 되도록 엄숙하게 보이려 애쓰며 말했다."], ["Winkle, wishing to make the matter appear as solemn as possible; ‘an affair with an officer, seconded by another officer, at sunset this evening, in a lonely field beyond Fort Pitt.’", "\"한 장교와의 일이고, 또 다른 장교가 그의 입회인이지. 오늘 저녁 해 질 녘에, 피트 요새 너머 외딴 들판에서 말이야.\""]], [["‘I will attend you,’ said Mr.", "\"내 자네와 함께하겠네.\""], ["Snodgrass.", "스노드그래스 씨가 말했다."]], [["He was astonished, but by no means dismayed.", "그는 놀라기는 했으나 결코 겁먹지 않았다."], ["It is extraordinary how cool any party but the principal can be in such cases.", "이런 경우에 당사자가 아닌 쪽은 어찌 그리 태연할 수 있는지 참으로 놀라운 노릇이다."], ["Mr. Winkle had forgotten this.", "윈클 씨는 이것을 깜박했던 것이다."], ["He had judged of his friend’s feelings by his own.", "그는 제 친구의 심정을 제 심정에 빗대어 헤아렸던 것이다."]], [["‘The consequences may be dreadful,’ said Mr.", "\"그 결과가 끔찍할지도 모르네.\""], ["Winkle.", "윈클 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘I hope not,’ said Mr.", "\"그렇지 않기를 바라네.\""], ["Snodgrass.", "스노드그래스 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘The doctor, I believe, is a very good shot,’ said Mr.", "\"그 박사는 명사수라던데.\""], ["Winkle.", "윈클 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Most of these military men are,’ observed Mr.", "\"그런 군인들이야 대개 그렇지.\""], ["Snodgrass calmly; ‘but so are you, ain’t you?’", "스노드그래스 씨가 차분히 말했다. \"하지만 자네도 그렇지 않은가?\""]], [["Mr.", "윈클 씨가 그렇다고 대답했다."], ["Winkle replied in the affirmative; and perceiving that he had not alarmed his companion sufficiently, changed his ground.", "그리고 제 동료를 충분히 겁먹게 하지 못했음을 알아채고는, 방향을 바꾸었다."]], [["‘Snodgrass,’ he said, in a voice tremulous with emotion, ‘if I fall, you will find in a packet which I shall place in your hands a note for my-- for my father.’", "\"스노드그래스.\" 그가 감정에 떨리는 목소리로 말했다. \"만일 내가 쓰러지거든, 내가 자네 손에 맡길 꾸러미 속에서, 내—내 아버지께 드리는 편지를 발견하게 될 걸세.\""]], [["This attack was a failure also.", "이 공략 또한 실패였다."], ["Mr. Snodgrass was affected, but he undertook the delivery of the note as readily as if he had been a twopenny postman.", "스노드그래스 씨는 마음이 움직이기는 했으나, 마치 2펜스짜리 우편배달부라도 되는 양 선뜻 그 편지를 전해 주겠노라 맡고 나섰다."]], [["‘If I fall,’ said Mr.", "\"만일 내가 쓰러지거나,\""], ["Winkle, ‘or if the doctor falls, you, my dear friend, will be tried as an accessory before the fact.", "윈클 씨가 말했다, \"혹은 박사가 쓰러지면, 이보게 친구, 자네는 사전 종범으로 재판을 받게 될 걸세."], ["Shall I involve my friend in transportation--possibly for life!’", "내가 친구를 유형에—어쩌면 종신 유형에 끌어들여야 한단 말인가!\""]], [["Mr. Snodgrass winced a little at this, but his heroism was invincible.", "스노드그래스 씨가 이 말에 조금 움찔했으나, 그의 영웅심은 천하무적이었다."], ["‘In the cause of friendship,’ he fervently exclaimed, ‘I would brave all dangers.’", "\"우정을 위해서라면,\" 그가 열렬히 외쳤다, \"내 어떤 위험도 무릅쓰겠네.\""]], [["How Mr. Winkle cursed his companion’s devoted friendship internally, as they walked silently along, side by side, for some minutes, each immersed in his own meditations!", "그들이 한동안 나란히 말없이 걸으며 저마다 제 사색에 잠겨 있는 동안, 윈클 씨는 제 동료의 헌신적인 우정을 속으로 얼마나 저주했던가!"], ["The morning was wearing away; he grew desperate.", "아침은 자꾸 지나가고 있었다. 그는 절박해졌다."]], [["‘Snodgrass,’ he said, stopping suddenly, ‘do not let me be balked in this matter--do not give information to the local authorities--do not obtain the assistance of several peace officers, to take either me or Doctor Slammer, of the 97th Regiment, at present quartered in Chatham Barracks, into custody, and thus prevent this duel!--I say, do not.’", "\"스노드그래스.\" 그가 우뚝 멈춰 서며 말했다. \"이 일에서 내가 좌절되게 하지 말아 주게—지방 당국에 알리지 말고—여러 치안관의 도움을 얻어, 나든 현재 채텀 병영에 주둔 중인 제97연대의 슬래머 박사든 붙잡아 가두게 하여, 이 결투를 막는 짓일랑 하지 말아 주게!—제발, 그러지 말게.\""]], [["Mr. Snodgrass seized his friend’s hand warmly, as he enthusiastically replied, ‘Not for worlds!’", "스노드그래스 씨는 친구의 손을 따뜻하게 움켜쥐고는 열정적으로 대답했다, \"천하를 준대도 그러지 않겠네!\""]], [["A thrill passed over Mr. Winkle’s frame as the conviction that he had nothing to hope from his friend’s fears, and that he was destined to become an animated target, rushed forcibly upon him.", "제 친구의 두려움에서 바랄 것이 아무것도 없으며, 자신은 살아 있는 과녁이 될 운명임을 깨닫는 확신이 거세게 밀려들자, 윈클 씨의 온몸에 전율이 스쳐 지나갔다."]], [["The state of the case having been formally explained to Mr. Snodgrass, and a case of satisfactory pistols, with the satisfactory accompaniments of powder, ball, and caps, having been hired from a manufacturer in Rochester, the two friends returned to their inn; Mr.", "스노드그래스 씨에게 사정이 정식으로 설명되고, 화약과 탄알과 뇌관이라는 흡족한 부속품을 갖춘 흡족한 권총 한 벌이 로체스터의 어느 제조업자에게서 빌려진 뒤, 두 친구는 여관으로 돌아왔다."], ["Winkle to ruminate on the approaching struggle, and Mr. Snodgrass to arrange the weapons of war, and put them into proper order for immediate use.", "윈클 씨는 다가올 싸움을 곰곰 생각하기 위해, 스노드그래스 씨는 전쟁 무기를 손질하여 당장이라도 쓸 수 있도록 제대로 갖추어 두기 위해서였다."]], [["It was a dull and heavy evening when they again sallied forth on their awkward errand. Mr.", "그들이 다시 그 거북한 임무를 띠고 나선 것은 흐리고 무거운 저녁나절이었다."], ["Winkle was muffled up in a huge cloak to escape observation, and Mr. Snodgrass bore under his the instruments of destruction.", "윈클 씨는 남의 눈을 피하려 커다란 망토로 몸을 칭칭 감쌌고, 스노드그래스 씨는 제 망토 밑에 파멸의 도구를 품고 있었다."]], [["‘Have you got everything?’ said Mr.", "\"다 챙겼나?\""], ["Winkle, in an agitated tone.", "윈클 씨가 동요한 목소리로 말했다."]], [["‘Everything,’ replied Mr.", "\"다 챙겼네.\""], ["Snodgrass; ‘plenty of ammunition, in case the shots don’t take effect.", "스노드그래스 씨가 대답했다. \"탄약도 넉넉히 챙겼지, 사격이 빗나갈 경우를 대비해서 말이야."], ["There’s a quarter of a pound of powder in the case, and I have got two newspapers in my pocket for the loadings.’", "통에 화약이 4분의 1파운드 들어 있고, 장전할 때 쓰려고 신문지도 두 장 주머니에 넣어 왔네.\""]], [["These were instances of friendship for which any man might reasonably feel most grateful.", "이것들은 어느 누구라도 마땅히 더없이 고마워할 만한 우정의 사례였다."], ["The presumption is, that the gratitude of Mr. Winkle was too powerful for utterance, as he said nothing, but continued to walk on--rather slowly.", "윈클 씨의 감사가 너무도 벅차 차마 말로 표현할 수 없었으리라 짐작되는바, 그는 아무 말도 없이 그저 걸음을 계속할 뿐이었다—그것도 다소 느릿느릿."]], [["‘We are in excellent time,’ said Mr.", "\"우리 시간이 아주 넉넉하군.\""], ["Snodgrass, as they climbed the fence of the first field; ‘the sun is just going down.’ Mr.", "첫 번째 들판의 울타리를 넘으며 스노드그래스 씨가 말했다. \"해가 막 지려는 참이야.\""], ["Winkle looked up at the declining orb and painfully thought of the probability of his ‘going down’ himself, before long.", "윈클 씨가 기울어 가는 해를 올려다보며, 머지않아 자신도 '기울어' 버릴지 모른다는 가능성을 고통스레 떠올렸다."]], [["‘There’s the officer,’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"저기 장교가 있군.\""], ["Winkle, after a few minutes walking.", "몇 분쯤 걷고 나서 윈클 씨가 외쳤다."]], [["‘Where?’ said Mr.", "\"어디?\""], ["Snodgrass.", "스노드그래스 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘There--the gentleman in the blue cloak.’ Mr.", "\"저기—저 푸른 망토를 두른 신사 말이야.\""], ["Snodgrass looked in the direction indicated by the forefinger of his friend, and observed a figure, muffled up, as he had described.", "스노드그래스 씨가 친구의 집게손가락이 가리키는 쪽을 바라보니, 그가 묘사한 대로 몸을 칭칭 감싼 사람 하나가 눈에 들어왔다."], ["The officer evinced his consciousness of their presence by slightly beckoning with his hand; and the two friends followed him at a little distance, as he walked away.", "장교는 손짓을 살짝 함으로써 그들의 존재를 알아챘음을 내비쳤고, 두 친구는 그가 걸어가자 조금 떨어져 그를 뒤따랐다."]], [["The evening grew more dull every moment, and a melancholy wind sounded through the deserted fields, like a distant giant whistling for his house-dog.", "저녁은 시시각각 더욱 흐려졌고, 인적 없는 들판으로 구슬픈 바람이 윙윙 울려 퍼졌으니, 마치 멀리 있는 거인이 제 집 지키는 개를 부르려고 휘파람을 부는 듯했다."], ["The sadness of the scene imparted a sombre tinge to the feelings of Mr.", "그 풍경의 서글픔이 윈클 씨의 심정에 침울한 빛깔을 드리웠다."], ["Winkle. He started as they passed the angle of the trench--it looked like a colossal grave.", "그는 참호의 모퉁이를 지날 때 흠칫했으니—그것이 거대한 무덤처럼 보였던 것이다."]], [["The officer turned suddenly from the path, and after climbing a paling, and scaling a hedge, entered a secluded field.", "장교가 오솔길에서 별안간 벗어나, 말뚝 울타리를 넘고 산울타리를 기어 넘은 끝에, 외진 들판으로 들어섰다."], ["Two gentlemen were waiting in it; one was a little, fat man, with black hair; and the other--a portly personage in a braided surtout--was sitting with perfect equanimity on a camp-stool.", "그곳에는 두 신사가 기다리고 있었으니, 하나는 검은 머리의 자그마한 뚱뚱보였고, 다른 하나는—장식 끈을 두른 프록코트를 입은 풍채 좋은 인물로—야영용 걸상에 더없이 평온하게 앉아 있었다."]], [["‘The other party, and a surgeon, I suppose,’ said Mr.", "\"상대편이로군, 그리고 저쪽은 외과의인가 보네.\""], ["Snodgrass; ‘take a drop of brandy.’ Mr.", "스노드그래스 씨가 말했다."], ["Winkle seized the wicker bottle which his friend proffered, and took a lengthened pull at the exhilarating liquid.", "\"브랜디 한 모금 들게나.\" 윈클 씨가 친구가 내민 고리버들 술병을 움켜쥐고, 그 기운을 돋우는 액체를 길게 한 모금 들이켰다."]], [["‘My friend, Sir, Mr. Snodgrass,’ said Mr.", "\"제 친구입니다, 손님, 스노드그래스 씨예요.\""], ["Winkle, as the officer approached.", "장교가 다가오자 윈클 씨가 말했다."], ["Doctor Slammer’s friend bowed, and produced a case similar to that which Mr. Snodgrass carried.", "슬래머 박사의 친구가 절하고는, 스노드그래스 씨가 들고 있는 것과 비슷한 상자를 꺼내 보였다."]], [["‘We have nothing further to say, Sir, I think,’ he coldly remarked, as he opened the case; ‘an apology has been resolutely declined.’", "\"더 드릴 말씀은 없는 것 같군요, 손님.\" 그가 상자를 열며 차갑게 말했다. \"사과는 단호히 거절되었으니까요.\""]], [["‘Nothing, Sir,’ said Mr.", "\"없습니다, 손님.\""], ["Snodgrass, who began to feel rather uncomfortable himself.", "슬슬 거북해지기 시작한 스노드그래스 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Will you step forward?’ said the officer.", "\"앞으로 나와 주시겠습니까?\" 장교가 말했다."]], [["‘Certainly,’ replied Mr. Snodgrass.", "\"물론이지요.\""], ["The ground was measured, and preliminaries arranged.", "스노드그래스 씨가 대답했다. 거리를 재고, 사전 절차가 준비되었다."]], [["‘You will find these better than your own,’ said the opposite second, producing his pistols.", "\"이것이 그쪽 것보다 나을 겁니다.\" 상대편 입회인이 제 권총을 꺼내 보이며 말했다."], ["‘You saw me load them.", "\"제가 장전하는 걸 보셨지요."], ["Do you object to use them?’", "이것을 쓰는 데 이의 없으십니까?\""]], [["‘Certainly not,’ replied Mr.", "\"전혀 없습니다.\" 스노드그래스 씨가 대답했다."], ["Snodgrass. The offer relieved him from considerable embarrassment, for his previous notions of loading a pistol were rather vague and undefined.", "이 제안 덕분에 그는 적잖은 곤혹에서 벗어났으니, 권총을 장전하는 일에 대한 그의 이전 지식이란 자못 흐릿하고 막연했기 때문이다."]], [["‘We may place our men, then, I think,’ observed the officer, with as much indifference as if the principals were chess-men, and the seconds players.", "\"그럼 우리 두 사람을 세워도 될 듯하군요.\" 장교가, 마치 당사자들은 장기 말이고 입회인들은 두는 사람이기라도 한 듯 무심한 태도로 말했다."]], [["‘I think we may,’ replied Mr.", "\"그래도 될 것 같습니다.\""], ["Snodgrass; who would have assented to any proposition, because he knew nothing about the matter.", "스노드그래스 씨가 대답했다."], ["The officer crossed to Doctor Slammer, and Mr.", "그는 이 일에 관해 아무것도 몰랐으므로 어떤 제안에든 동의했을 것이다."], ["Snodgrass went up to Mr. Winkle.", "장교는 슬래머 박사에게로 건너갔고, 스노드그래스 씨는 윈클 씨에게로 다가갔다."]], [["‘It’s all ready,’ said he, offering the pistol.", "\"다 준비됐네.\" 그가 권총을 내밀며 말했다."], ["‘Give me your cloak.’", "\"망토를 이리 주게.\""]], [["‘You have got the packet, my dear fellow,’ said poor Winkle.", "\"꾸러미는 자네가 가졌지, 이보게.\" 가엾은 윈클이 말했다."]], [["‘All right,’ said Mr.", "\"걱정 말게.\" 스노드그래스 씨가 말했다."], ["Snodgrass. ‘Be steady, and wing him.’", "\"침착하게, 한 방 먹여 주게.\""]], [["It occurred to Mr. Winkle that this advice was very like that which bystanders invariably give to the smallest boy in a street fight, namely, ‘Go in, and win’--an admirable thing to recommend, if you only know how to do it.", "윈클 씨는 이 조언이, 길거리 싸움에서 구경꾼들이 가장 작은 소년에게 한결같이 던지는 말, 곧 '덤벼서 이겨라'"], ["He took off his cloak, however, in silence--it always took a long time to undo that cloak--and accepted the pistol.", "하는 말과 꼭 닮았다는 생각이 들었다—할 줄만 안다면야 권할 만한 훌륭한 말이렷다. 그러나 그는 말없이 망토를 벗었으니—그 망토를 벗는 데는 늘 한참이 걸렸다—그러고는 권총을 받아 들었다."], ["The seconds retired, the gentleman on the camp-stool did the same, and the belligerents approached each other.", "입회인들이 물러갔고, 야영용 걸상에 앉아 있던 신사도 똑같이 물러갔으며, 두 교전자가 서로에게 다가섰다."]], [["Mr. Winkle was always remarkable for extreme humanity.", "윈클 씨는 언제나 지극한 인정으로 유명한 사람이었다."], ["It is conjectured that his unwillingness to hurt a fellow-creature intentionally was the cause of his shutting his eyes when he arrived at the fatal spot; and that the circumstance of his eyes being closed, prevented his observing the very extraordinary and unaccountable demeanour of Doctor Slammer.", "그가 동족을 고의로 다치게 하기를 꺼린 까닭에 운명의 자리에 다다랐을 때 두 눈을 감아 버렸으리라는 것, 그리고 그렇게 눈을 감고 있던 사정 때문에 슬래머 박사의 매우 기이하고 영문 모를 거동을 보지 못했으리라는 것이 추측된다."], ["That gentleman started, stared, retreated, rubbed his eyes, stared again, and, finally, shouted, ‘Stop, stop!’", "그 신사는 흠칫 놀라 빤히 바라보더니 뒷걸음질을 치고 눈을 비비고는 다시 빤히 바라보다가, 마침내 이렇게 외쳤다. \"멈춰요, 멈춰!\""]], [["‘What’s all this?’ said Doctor Slammer, as his friend and Mr.", "\"이게 다 무슨 일이오?\" 슬래머 박사가 자기 친구와 스노드그래스 씨가 달려오자 말했다."], ["Snodgrass came running up; ‘that’s not the man.’", "\"저 사람이 아니오.\""]], [["‘Not the man!’ said Doctor Slammer’s second.", "\"저 사람이 아니라니!\" 슬래머 박사의 입회인이 말했다."]], [["‘Not the man!’ said Mr.", "\"저 사람이 아니라니!\""], ["Snodgrass.", "스노드그래스 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Not the man!’ said the gentleman with the camp-stool in his hand.", "\"저 사람이 아니라니!\" 손에 야영 의자를 든 신사가 말했다."]], [["‘Certainly not,’ replied the little doctor.", "\"분명히 아니오.\" 작은 박사가 대답했다."], ["‘That’s not the person who insulted me last night.’", "\"저 사람은 어젯밤 나를 모욕한 사람이 아니오.\""]], [["‘Very extraordinary!’ exclaimed the officer.", "\"참으로 기이하군!\" 장교가 소리쳤다."]], [["‘Very,’ said the gentleman with the camp-stool.", "\"참으로.\" 야영 의자를 든 신사가 말했다."], ["‘The only question is, whether the gentleman, being on the ground, must not be considered, as a matter of form, to be the individual who insulted our friend, Doctor Slammer, yesterday evening, whether he is really that individual or not;’ and having delivered this suggestion, with a very sage and mysterious air, the man with the camp-stool took a large pinch of snuff, and looked profoundly round, with the air of an authority in such matters.", "\"다만 문제는, 저 신사가 결투장에 나와 있는 이상, 그가 실제로 그 사람이든 아니든, 형식상으로 어제저녁 우리 친구 슬래머 박사를 모욕한 당사자로 간주되어야 하지 않겠는가 하는 것이오.\" 그리고 이 제안을 매우 현명하고 그윽한 태도로 내놓은 뒤, 야영 의자를 든 사내는 코담배를 한 줌 크게 집어 들이켜고는, 이런 문제에 정통한 권위자다운 태도로 주위를 깊이 둘러보았다."]], [["Now Mr.", "이제 윈클 씨는 상대편이 적대 행위를 중지하라고 외치는 소리를 들었을 때 두 눈을, 그리고 두 귀까지도 활짝 열었다."], ["Winkle had opened his eyes, and his ears too, when he heard his adversary call out for a cessation of hostilities; and perceiving by what he had afterwards said that there was, beyond all question, some mistake in the matter, he at once foresaw the increase of reputation he should inevitably acquire by concealing the real motive of his coming out; he therefore stepped boldly forward, and said--", "그리고 그 뒤에 오간 말로 미루어 이 일에 무언가 착오가 있다는 것을 의심할 여지 없이 알아차리자, 그는 자기가 나온 진짜 동기를 숨김으로써 반드시 얻게 될 명성의 증대를 즉시 내다보았다. 그리하여 그는 당당히 앞으로 나서서 이렇게 말했다."]], [["‘I am not the person.", "\"제가 그 사람이 아닙니다."], ["I know it.’", "저도 압니다.\""]], [["‘Then, that,’ said the man with the camp-stool, ‘is an affront to Doctor Slammer, and a sufficient reason for proceeding immediately.’", "\"그렇다면, 그것은,\" 야영 의자를 든 사내가 말했다. \"슬래머 박사에 대한 모욕이며, 즉각 진행할 충분한 이유가 되오.\""]], [["‘Pray be quiet, Payne,’ said the doctor’s second.", "\"제발 조용히 하시오, 페인.\" 박사의 입회인이 말했다."], ["‘Why did you not communicate this fact to me this morning, Sir?’", "\"선생, 어째서 오늘 아침에 이 사실을 나에게 알리지 않으셨소?\""]], [["‘To be sure--to be sure,’ said the man with the camp-stool indignantly.", "\"옳거니, 옳거니.\" 야영 의자를 든 사내가 분개하여 말했다."]], [["‘I entreat you to be quiet, Payne,’ said the other.", "\"제발 좀 조용히 해 주시오, 페인.\" 다른 이가 말했다."], ["‘May I repeat my question, Sir?’", "\"선생, 내 질문을 다시 해도 되겠소?\""]], [["‘Because, Sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"그것은, 선생,\""], ["Winkle, who had had time to deliberate upon his answer, ‘because, Sir, you described an intoxicated and ungentlemanly person as wearing a coat which I have the honour, not only to wear but to have invented--the proposed uniform, Sir, of the Pickwick Club in London.", "그동안 자기 대답을 곰곰이 헤아릴 시간이 있었던 윈클 씨가 대답했다. \"그것은, 선생, 선생께서 술 취하고 신사답지 못한 사람이 어떤 외투를 입고 있더라고 묘사하셨는데, 그 외투는 제가 입는 영광을 가졌을 뿐 아니라 고안해 낸 영광까지 가진 것이기 때문입니다."], ["The honour of that uniform I feel bound to maintain, and I therefore, without inquiry, accepted the challenge which you offered me.’", "곧 런던 픽윅 클럽의 제정 예정 제복이지요, 선생. 저는 그 제복의 명예를 지켜야 한다고 느꼈고, 그래서 더 묻지도 않고 선생께서 내미신 결투를 받아들인 것입니다.\""]], [["‘My dear Sir,’ said the good-humoured little doctor advancing with extended hand, ‘I honour your gallantry.", "\"이보시오, 선생,\" 사람 좋은 작은 박사가 손을 내밀며 다가와 말했다."], ["Permit me to say, Sir, that I highly admire your conduct, and extremely regret having caused you the inconvenience of this meeting, to no purpose.’", "\"나는 선생의 의기를 존경하오. 한 말씀 드려도 된다면, 선생, 나는 선생의 처신을 크게 흠모하며, 아무 보람도 없이 이 만남의 불편을 끼쳐 드린 것을 몹시 유감스럽게 여기오.\""]], [["‘I beg you won’t mention it, Sir,’ said Mr.", "\"부디 그런 말씀 마십시오, 선생.\""], ["Winkle.", "윈클 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘I shall feel proud of your acquaintance, Sir,’ said the little doctor.", "\"선생과 알게 된 것을 자랑스럽게 여기겠소, 선생.\" 작은 박사가 말했다."]], [["‘It will afford me the greatest pleasure to know you, sir,’ replied Mr. Winkle. Thereupon the doctor and Mr. Winkle shook hands, and then Mr. Winkle and Lieutenant Tappleton (the doctor’s second), and then Mr. Winkle and the man with the camp-stool, and, finally, Mr. Winkle and Mr. Snodgrass--the last-named gentleman in an excess of admiration at the noble conduct of his heroic friend.", "\"선생을 알게 되어 더없는 기쁨이옵니다, 선생.\" 윈클 씨가 대답했다. 그리하여 박사와 윈클 씨가 악수를 나누고, 그다음에는 윈클 씨와 태플턴 중위(박사의 입회인)가, 그다음에는 윈클 씨와 야영 의자를 든 사내가, 마지막으로 윈클 씨와 스노드그래스 씨가 악수를 나누었으니, 마지막에 든 그 신사는 영웅적인 친구의 고귀한 처신에 감탄해 마지않는 모습이었다."]], [["‘I think we may adjourn,’ said Lieutenant Tappleton.", "\"이제 그만 자리를 파해도 되겠소이다.\" 태플턴 중위가 말했다."]], [["‘Certainly,’ added the doctor.", "\"물론이오.\" 박사가 덧붙였다."]], [["‘Unless,’ interposed the man with the camp-stool, ‘unless Mr.", "\"다만,\" 야영 의자를 든 사내가 끼어들었다."], ["Winkle feels himself aggrieved by the challenge; in which case, I submit, he has a right to satisfaction.’", "\"다만 윈클 씨가 이 결투 신청으로 모욕을 당했다고 느끼지 않는다면 말이오. 그렇게 느낀다면, 내 생각에 그분은 만족을 요구할 권리가 있소.\""]], [["Mr. Winkle, with great self-denial, expressed himself quite satisfied already.", "윈클 씨는 대단한 극기심을 발휘하여 이미 충분히 만족하고 있노라고 밝혔다."]], [["‘Or possibly,’ said the man with the camp-stool, ‘the gentleman’s second may feel himself affronted with some observations which fell from me at an early period of this meeting; if so, I shall be happy to give him satisfaction immediately.’", "\"혹은 어쩌면,\" 야영 의자를 든 사내가 말했다. \"이 만남의 초입에서 내 입에서 나온 어떤 말 때문에 저 신사의 입회인이 모욕을 느꼈을지도 모르오. 만일 그렇다면, 나는 기꺼이 그분에게 즉시 만족을 드리리다.\""]], [["Mr. Snodgrass hastily professed himself very much obliged with the handsome offer of the gentleman who had spoken last, which he was only induced to decline by his entire contentment with the whole proceedings.", "스노드그래스 씨는 방금 말한 신사의 너그러운 제안에 매우 감사한다고 황급히 밝히면서, 다만 모든 진행에 전적으로 만족하기에 사양할 수밖에 없노라고 했다."], ["The two seconds adjusted the cases, and the whole party left the ground in a much more lively manner than they had proceeded to it.", "두 입회인은 권총집을 챙겼고, 일행은 올 때보다 훨씬 활기찬 모습으로 결투장을 떠났다."]], [["‘Do you remain long here?’ inquired Doctor Slammer of Mr.", "\"여기 오래 머무르시오?\""], ["Winkle, as they walked on most amicably together.", "슬래머 박사가 윈클 씨와 더없이 다정하게 나란히 걸으며 물었다."]], [["‘I think we shall leave here the day after to-morrow,’ was the reply.", "\"모레쯤 이곳을 떠날 듯합니다.\" 하는 대답이었다."]], [["‘I trust I shall have the pleasure of seeing you and your friend at my rooms, and of spending a pleasant evening with you, after this awkward mistake,’ said the little doctor; ‘are you disengaged this evening?’", "\"이 어색한 착오를 겪은 마당이니, 선생과 선생의 친구분을 내 거처에서 뵙고 즐거운 저녁을 함께 보내는 기쁨을 누렸으면 하오.\" 작은 박사가 말했다. \"오늘 저녁은 한가하시오?\""]], [["‘We have some friends here,’ replied Mr.", "\"여기 친구들이 몇 있어서,\" 윈클 씨가 대답했다."], ["Winkle, ‘and I should not like to leave them to-night.", "\"오늘 밤은 그들을 두고 자리를 비우고 싶지 않습니다."], ["Perhaps you and your friend will join us at the Bull.’", "차라리 선생과 선생의 친구분이 불 여관으로 와서 저희와 함께하시지요.\""]], [["‘With great pleasure,’ said the little doctor; ‘will ten o’clock be too late to look in for half an hour?’", "\"기꺼이 그러리다.\" 작은 박사가 말했다. \"열 시에 들러 반 시간쯤 머무는 것이 너무 늦겠소?\""]], [["‘Oh dear, no,’ said Mr.", "\"아, 천만에요.\""], ["Winkle. ‘I shall be most happy to introduce you to my friends, Mr.", "윈클 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick and Mr. Tupman.’", "\"제 친구들인 픽윅 씨와 텁먼 씨에게 선생을 소개하게 되어 더없이 기쁘겠습니다.\""]], [["‘It will give me great pleasure, I am sure,’ replied Doctor Slammer, little suspecting who Mr.", "\"틀림없이 큰 기쁨이 되겠소이다.\""], ["Tupman was.", "슬래머 박사가 대답했으니, 텁먼 씨가 누구인지는 조금도 짐작하지 못한 채였다."]], [["‘You will be sure to come?’ said Mr.", "\"꼭 오시는 거죠?\""], ["Snodgrass.", "스노드그래스 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Oh, certainly.’", "\"아, 물론이오.\""]], [["By this time they had reached the road.", "이때쯤 그들은 큰길에 다다랐다."], ["Cordial farewells were exchanged, and the party separated. Doctor Slammer and his friends repaired to the barracks, and Mr.", "다정한 작별 인사가 오갔고, 일행은 헤어졌다."], ["Winkle, accompanied by Mr. Snodgrass, returned to their inn.", "슬래머 박사와 그의 친구들은 병영으로 돌아갔고, 윈클 씨는 스노드그래스 씨와 함께 그들의 여관으로 돌아갔다."]], [["CHAPTER III. A NEW ACQUAINTANCE--THE STROLLER’S TALE--A DISAGREEABLE INTERRUPTION, AND AN UNPLEASANT ENCOUNTER\nMr.", "픽윅 씨는 두 친구가 평소와 달리 자리를 비운 탓에 적잖이 불안한 마음을 품고 있었는데, 아침 내내 이어진 그들의 알 수 없는 행동은 그 불안을 누그러뜨리는 데 조금도 도움이 되지 못했다."], ["Pickwick had felt some apprehensions in consequence of the unusual absence of his two friends, which their mysterious behaviour during the whole morning had by no means tended to diminish.", "그러므로 그들이 다시 들어왔을 때 그가 일어나 맞이한 것은 여느 때보다 더한 기쁨에서였고, 무슨 일이 있어 자기와의 자리를 이토록 늦추었는지 물은 것도 여느 때보다 더한 관심에서였다."], ["It was, therefore, with more than ordinary pleasure that he rose to greet them when they again entered; and with more than ordinary interest that he inquired what had occurred to detain them from his society. In reply to his questions on this point, Mr.", "이 점에 관한 그의 질문에 답하여, 스노드그래스 씨는 방금 자세히 이야기된 정황을 역사적으로 설명하려 했으나, 좌중에 터프먼 씨와 전날의 역마차 동행뿐 아니라 그에 못지않게 기이한 풍모를 한 또 다른 낯선 이가 있음을 알아채고는 불현듯 말을 멈추었다."], ["Snodgrass was about to offer an historical account of the circumstances just now detailed, when he was suddenly checked by observing that there were present, not only Mr. Tupman and their stage-coach companion of the preceding day, but another stranger of equally singular appearance.", "근심에 찌든 듯한 사내였는데, 누런 얼굴과 깊이 꺼진 두 눈은 곧고 검은 머리카락이 헝클어진 채 얼굴 절반까지 늘어진 탓에 자연이 만들어 놓은 것보다 한층 더 두드러져 보였다."], ["It was a careworn-looking man, whose sallow face, and deeply-sunken eyes, were rendered still more striking than Nature had made them, by the straight black hair which hung in matted disorder half-way down his face. His eyes were almost unnaturally bright and piercing; his cheek-bones were high and prominent; and his jaws were so long and lank, that an observer would have supposed that he was drawing the flesh of his face in, for a moment, by some contraction of the muscles, if his half-opened mouth and immovable expression had not announced that it was his ordinary appearance.", "그의 눈은 거의 부자연스러울 만큼 밝고 날카로웠고, 광대뼈는 높이 솟아 도드라졌으며, 턱은 어찌나 길고 야위었던지, 만일 반쯤 벌어진 입과 미동 없는 표정이 그것이 그의 본래 모습임을 알려 주지 않았더라면, 보는 이는 그가 잠시 근육을 수축시켜 얼굴 살을 안으로 끌어당기고 있는 줄로 여겼을 것이다."], ["Round his neck he wore a green shawl, with the large ends straggling over his chest, and making their appearance occasionally beneath the worn button-holes of his old waistcoat.", "목에는 초록빛 숄을 둘렀는데, 그 큰 양 끝이 가슴께로 어수선하게 흘러내려 낡은 조끼의 해진 단춧구멍 아래로 이따금 모습을 드러냈다."], ["His upper garment was a long black surtout; and below it he wore wide drab trousers, and large boots, running rapidly to seed.", "겉옷은 검은빛의 긴 프록코트였고, 그 아래로는 헐렁한 칙칙한 빛깔의 바지와 빠르게 망가져 가는 큼직한 부츠를 신고 있었다."]], [["It was on this uncouth-looking person that Mr. Winkle’s eye rested, and it was towards him that Mr.", "윈클 씨의 시선이 머문 것은 바로 이 거칠어 보이는 인물이었고, 픽윅 씨가 손을 내민 것도 바로 그를 향해서였다."], ["Pickwick extended his hand when he said, ‘A friend of our friend’s here.", "픽윅 씨는 말했다. \"여기 우리 친구의 친구분일세."], ["We discovered this morning that our friend was connected with the theatre in this place, though he is not desirous to have it generally known, and this gentleman is a member of the same profession.", "오늘 아침에 우리는 이 친구가 이곳 극장과 연이 있다는 걸 알게 되었네. 본인은 널리 알려지길 바라지 않지만 말이야."], ["He was about to favour us with a little anecdote connected with it, when you entered.’", "그리고 이 신사분도 같은 직종에 몸담은 분일세. 자네가 들어왔을 때 마침 그와 관련된 짤막한 일화를 들려주려던 참이었네.\""]], [["‘Lots of anecdote,’ said the green-coated stranger of the day before, advancing to Mr.", "\"일화라면 산더미죠.\" 전날의 초록 코트를 입은 낯선 이가 윈클 씨에게 다가와 나직하고 은밀한 어조로 말했다."], ["Winkle and speaking in a low and confidential tone. ‘Rum fellow--does the heavy business--no actor--strange man--all sorts of miseries--Dismal Jemmy, we call him on the circuit.’ Mr.", "\"괴짜라오—비극 같은 무게 있는 역을 맡지요—배우는 못 되고—이상한 사람이오—온갖 비참을 다 겪었지요—우리 순회 패거리에선 '음울한 제미'라고 부른다오.\""], ["Winkle and Mr. Snodgrass politely welcomed the gentleman, elegantly designated as ‘Dismal Jemmy’; and calling for brandy-and-water, in imitation of the remainder of the company, seated themselves at the table.", "윈클 씨와 스노드그래스 씨는 '음울한 제미'라고 우아하게 지칭된 신사를 정중히 맞이했고, 좌중의 나머지를 본떠 브랜디 워터를 청한 뒤 탁자에 자리를 잡았다."]], [["‘Now sir,’ said Mr.", "\"자, 선생.\""], ["Pickwick, ‘will you oblige us by proceeding with what you were going to relate?’", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"들려주시려던 이야기를 마저 해 주시는 호의를 베풀어 주시겠소?\""]], [["The dismal individual took a dirty roll of paper from his pocket, and turning to Mr.", "음울한 사내는 주머니에서 지저분한 종이 두루마리를 꺼내고는, 마침 수첩을 꺼내 든 스노드그래스 씨를 돌아보며, 그의 겉모습과 완벽히 어울리는 공허한 목소리로 말했다."], ["Snodgrass, who had just taken out his note-book, said in a hollow voice, perfectly in keeping with his outward man--‘Are you the poet?’", "\"당신이 시인이오?\""]], [["‘I--I do a little in that way,’ replied Mr.", "\"저—저는 그 방면에 약간 손을 대지요.\""], ["Snodgrass, rather taken aback by the abruptness of the question.", "스노드그래스 씨가 질문의 느닷없음에 다소 당황하여 대답했다."]], [["‘Ah!", "\"아!"], ["poetry makes life what light and music do the stage--strip the one of the false embellishments, and the other of its illusions, and what is there real in either to live or care for?’", "시가 인생에 해 주는 것은 조명과 음악이 무대에 해 주는 것과 같소—한쪽에서 거짓 장식을 벗기고, 다른 쪽에서 환영을 벗겨 내면, 그 어느 쪽에 살아 있고 마음 쓸 만한 무엇이 남겠소?\""]], [["‘Very true, Sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"참으로 옳은 말씀이오, 선생.\""], ["Snodgrass.", "스노드그래스 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘To be before the footlights,’ continued the dismal man, ‘is like sitting at a grand court show, and admiring the silken dresses of the gaudy throng; to be behind them is to be the people who make that finery, uncared for and unknown, and left to sink or swim, to starve or live, as fortune wills it.’", "\"각광 앞에 선다는 건,\" 음울한 사내가 말을 이었다. \"성대한 궁정 행사 자리에 앉아 화려한 무리의 비단옷을 감탄하며 바라보는 것과 같소. 그 뒤에 선다는 건, 아무도 돌보지 않고 알지도 못하는 채로 그 화려함을 만들어 내는 사람들이 되는 것이지요. 운명이 정하는 대로 가라앉든 헤엄치든, 굶든 살든 내버려진 사람들 말이오.\""]], [["‘Certainly,’ said Mr.", "\"그렇고말고요.\""], ["Snodgrass: for the sunken eye of the dismal man rested on him, and he felt it necessary to say something.", "스노드그래스 씨가 말했다. 음울한 사내의 꺼진 눈이 그에게 머물러 있어, 무어라도 말해야 할 것 같았기 때문이다."]], [["‘Go on, Jemmy,’ said the Spanish traveller, ‘like black-eyed Susan--all in the Downs--no croaking--speak out--look lively.’", "\"계속하게, 제미.\" 스페인 여행자가 말했다. \"'검은 눈의 수전'처럼 말이야—다운스 한복판에서—꿍얼대지 말고—툭 터놓고—활기차게.\""]], [["‘Will you make another glass before you begin, Sir?’ said Mr.", "\"시작하기 전에 한 잔 더 만드시겠소, 선생?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["The dismal man took the hint, and having mixed a glass of brandy-and- water, and slowly swallowed half of it, opened the roll of paper and proceeded, partly to read, and partly to relate, the following incident, which we find recorded on the Transactions of the Club as ‘The Stroller’s Tale.’", "음울한 사내는 그 말귀를 알아듣고, 브랜디 워터를 한 잔 섞어 천천히 절반쯤 들이켠 뒤, 종이 두루마리를 펼쳐 다음의 사건을 반쯤은 읽고 반쯤은 이야기하는 식으로 들려주었으니, 이는 클럽 회의록에 '떠돌이 배우의 이야기'라는 제목으로 기록되어 있는 그대로다. 떠돌이 배우의 이야기"]], [["# THE STROLLER’S TALE", "# THE STROLLER’S TALE"]], [["‘There is nothing of the marvellous in what I am going to relate,’ said the dismal man; ‘there is nothing even uncommon in it.", "\"제가 이제 들려드릴 이야기에는 경이로운 구석이라곤 없습니다.\" 음울한 사내가 말했다."], ["Want and sickness are too common in many stations of life to deserve more notice than is usually bestowed on the most ordinary vicissitudes of human nature.", "\"심지어 별스러운 데도 없지요. 궁핍과 병고는 여러 처지의 삶에서 너무도 흔한 것이어서, 인간 본성의 가장 평범한 부침에 으레 베풀어지는 정도 이상의 주목을 받을 만하지 않습니다."], ["I have thrown these few notes together, because the subject of them was well known to me for many years.", "제가 이 몇 가지 기록을 한데 엮은 까닭은, 그 이야기의 당사자를 여러 해 동안 잘 알고 지냈기 때문입니다."], ["I traced his progress downwards, step by step, until at last he reached that excess of destitution from which he never rose again.", "저는 그가 한 걸음 한 걸음 추락해 가는 과정을 좇았고, 마침내 그는 다시는 헤어나지 못할 극도의 궁핍에 이르고 말았습니다."]], [["‘The man of whom I speak was a low pantomime actor; and, like many people of his class, an habitual drunkard.", "\"제가 말하는 사내는 천한 무언극 배우였습니다. 그리고 그 부류의 많은 이들이 그렇듯, 상습적인 술꾼이었지요."], ["In his better days, before he had become enfeebled by dissipation and emaciated by disease, he had been in the receipt of a good salary, which, if he had been careful and prudent, he might have continued to receive for some years--not many; because these men either die early, or by unnaturally taxing their bodily energies, lose, prematurely, those physical powers on which alone they can depend for subsistence.", "형편이 나았던 시절, 방탕으로 쇠약해지고 병으로 야위기 전에는 제법 두둑한 보수를 받았습니다. 신중하고 분별 있게 굴었더라면 몇 해는—그리 오래는 아니더라도—계속 받았을 것입니다."], ["His besetting sin gained so fast upon him, however, that it was found impossible to employ him in the situations in which he really was useful to the theatre.", "이런 사내들은 일찍 죽거나, 아니면 제 몸의 기력을 부자연스럽게 혹사하여 호구지책으로 의지할 유일한 밑천인 체력을 너무 일찍 잃어버리기 때문이지요. 그러나 그의 고질적인 악습이 너무도 빠르게 그를 사로잡은 탓에, 그가 정말로 극장에 쓸모 있던 자리에조차 그를 부리기가 불가능해지고 말았습니다."], ["The public- house had a fascination for him which he could not resist.", "선술집은 그가 도무지 뿌리칠 수 없는 매혹을 지니고 있었지요."], ["Neglected disease and hopeless poverty were as certain to be his portion as death itself, if he persevered in the same course; yet he did persevere, and the result may be guessed.", "같은 길을 고집한다면, 방치된 병과 가망 없는 가난이 죽음 그 자체만큼이나 확실히 그의 몫이 될 터였습니다. 그런데도 그는 끝내 고집했고, 그 결과야 짐작이 가실 겁니다."], ["He could obtain no engagement, and he wanted bread.", "그는 아무 일자리도 얻지 못했고, 빵에 굶주렸습니다."]], [["‘Everybody who is at all acquainted with theatrical matters knows what a host of shabby, poverty-stricken men hang about the stage of a large establishment--not regularly engaged actors, but ballet people, procession men, tumblers, and so forth, who are taken on during the run of a pantomime, or an Easter piece, and are then discharged, until the production of some heavy spectacle occasions a new demand for their services. To this mode of life the man was compelled to resort; and taking the chair every night, at some low theatrical house, at once put him in possession of a few more shillings weekly, and enabled him to gratify his old propensity. Even this resource shortly failed him; his irregularities were too great to admit of his earning the wretched pittance he might thus have procured, and he was actually reduced to a state bordering on starvation, only procuring a trifle occasionally by borrowing it of some old companion, or by obtaining an appearance at one or other of the commonest of the minor theatres; and when he did earn anything it was spent in the old way.", "\"극장 사정을 조금이라도 아는 사람이라면, 큰 극장의 무대 언저리에 얼마나 많은 초라하고 가난에 찌든 사내들이 어슬렁거리는지 알 것입니다. 정식으로 고용된 배우가 아니라, 발레 인원이며 행렬 인원이며 곡예꾼 따위 말이지요. 그들은 무언극이나 부활절 연극이 상연되는 동안에만 고용되었다가 곧 해고되고, 이윽고 어떤 대형 볼거리가 제작되어 그들의 손이 새로 필요해질 때까지 기다리는 신세입니다. 그 사내도 이런 생활 방식에 매달릴 수밖에 없었습니다. 밤마다 어느 천한 극장 패거리에서 사회를 보는 일은 그에게 주당 몇 실링을 더 쥐여 주는 동시에, 그가 오랜 버릇을 채울 수 있게 해 주었지요. 이 밑천마저도 곧 그를 저버렸습니다. 그의 무절제가 너무 심해, 그렇게 마련할 수 있었을 그 보잘것없는 푼돈조차 벌 수 없었던 것입니다. 그리하여 그는 실제로 굶주림 직전의 처지로 전락했고, 어쩌다 옛 동료에게서 푼돈을 빌리거나, 가장 천한 군소 극장 가운데 어느 한 곳에 출연 자리를 얻어 가까스로 약간을 손에 넣을 뿐이었습니다. 그리고 무얼 좀 벌더라도, 그것은 늘 하던 식으로 탕진되었지요."]], [["‘About this time, and when he had been existing for upwards of a year no one knew how, I had a short engagement at one of the theatres on the Surrey side of the water, and here I saw this man, whom I had lost sight of for some time; for I had been travelling in the provinces, and he had been skulking in the lanes and alleys of London.", "\"이 무렵, 그가 어떻게 일 년 넘게 연명해 왔는지 아무도 모르던 그때에, 저는 강 건너 서리 쪽의 어느 극장에서 짧게 일하게 되었고, 거기서 한동안 소식을 모르던 이 사내를 보았습니다. 저는 지방을 돌며 순회 공연을 다녔고, 그는 런던의 골목과 뒷길에 숨어 지내고 있었던 것이지요."], ["I was dressed to leave the house, and was crossing the stage on my way out, when he tapped me on the shoulder.", "저는 극장을 나서려고 옷을 갈아입은 차림으로 나가는 길에 무대를 가로지르고 있었는데, 그가 제 어깨를 톡톡 두드렸습니다."], ["Never shall I forget the repulsive sight that met my eye when I turned round.", "제가 돌아섰을 때 눈에 들어온 그 역겨운 광경을 저는 결코 잊지 못할 것입니다."], ["He was dressed for the pantomimes in all the absurdity of a clown’s costume.", "그는 광대 의상의 온갖 우스꽝스러움을 다 갖춰 무언극용으로 분장하고 있었지요."], ["The spectral figures in the Dance of Death, the most frightful shapes that the ablest painter ever portrayed on canvas, never presented an appearance half so ghastly.", "'죽음의 춤'에 등장하는 유령 같은 형상들이며, 가장 유능한 화가가 일찍이 화폭에 그려 낸 가장 무시무시한 형체들도, 그 절반만큼도 소름 끼치는 모습을 보여 준 적이 없었습니다."], ["His bloated body and shrunken legs--their deformity enhanced a hundredfold by the fantastic dress--the glassy eyes, contrasting fearfully with the thick white paint with which the face was besmeared; the grotesquely- ornamented head, trembling with paralysis, and the long skinny hands, rubbed with white chalk--all gave him a hideous and unnatural appearance, of which no description could convey an adequate idea, and which, to this day, I shudder to think of.", "부어오른 몸뚱이와 쪼그라든 다리—그 기괴한 의상이 그 흉함을 백 배는 부풀려 놓았지요—얼굴에 두껍게 처바른 흰 분과 무섭게 대조를 이루는 흐리멍덩한 두 눈, 마비로 떨리는 우스꽝스럽게 치장된 머리, 그리고 흰 분필을 문질러 댄 길고 앙상한 두 손—이 모든 것이 그에게 끔찍하고 부자연스러운 모습을 부여했으니, 어떤 묘사로도 그 적확한 인상을 전할 수 없을 것이며, 지금까지도 떠올리면 몸서리가 쳐집니다."], ["His voice was hollow and tremulous as he took me aside, and in broken words recounted a long catalogue of sickness and privations, terminating as usual with an urgent request for the loan of a trifling sum of money.", "그가 저를 한쪽으로 데려가며 띄엄띄엄한 말로 병고와 궁핍의 긴 목록을 늘어놓을 때, 그의 목소리는 공허하고 떨렸습니다. 그러더니 늘 그렇듯 약간의 돈을 빌려 달라는 간절한 청으로 끝맺었지요."], ["I put a few shillings in his hand, and as I turned away I heard the roar of laughter which followed his first tumble on the stage.", "저는 그의 손에 몇 실링을 쥐여 주었고, 돌아서는 순간 그가 무대에서 처음 곤두박질치자 뒤따라 터지는 웃음의 함성을 들었습니다."]], [["‘A few nights afterwards, a boy put a dirty scrap of paper in my hand, on which were scrawled a few words in pencil, intimating that the man was dangerously ill, and begging me, after the performance, to see him at his lodgings in some street--I forget the name of it now--at no great distance from the theatre.", "\"며칠 밤이 지난 뒤, 한 소년이 제 손에 지저분한 종잇조각을 쥐여 주었는데, 거기엔 연필로 몇 마디가 휘갈겨져 있었습니다. 그 사내가 위독하다는 것, 그리고 공연이 끝나거든 극장에서 그리 멀지 않은 어느 거리—이제 그 이름은 잊었습니다—에 있는 그의 하숙으로 와서 그를 봐 달라고 간청하는 내용이었지요."], ["I promised to comply, as soon as I could get away; and after the curtain fell, sallied forth on my melancholy errand.", "저는 빠져나오는 대로 가겠노라 약속했고, 막이 내린 뒤 그 우울한 심부름 길에 나섰습니다."]], [["‘It was late, for I had been playing in the last piece; and, as it was a benefit night, the performances had been protracted to an unusual length.", "\"마지막 연극에 출연했던 터라 시각이 늦었고, 게다가 자선 공연의 밤이어서 공연이 평소보다 길게 늘어졌던 것입니다."], ["It was a dark, cold night, with a chill, damp wind, which blew the rain heavily against the windows and house-fronts.", "어둡고 추운 밤이었으며, 으슬으슬 축축한 바람이 빗줄기를 창문과 집 정면에 사납게 들이쳤지요."], ["Pools of water had collected in the narrow and little-frequented streets, and as many of the thinly-scattered oil-lamps had been blown out by the violence of the wind, the walk was not only a comfortless, but most uncertain one.", "좁고 인적 드문 거리에는 빗물 웅덩이가 고여 있었고, 띄엄띄엄 흩어진 기름 등불도 거센 바람에 많이 꺼져 버려, 그 길은 음산할 뿐 아니라 갈피를 잡기조차 어려웠습니다."], ["I had fortunately taken the right course, however, and succeeded, after a little difficulty, in finding the house to which I had been directed--a coal-shed, with one storey above it, in the back room of which lay the object of my search.", "그래도 다행히 저는 옳은 방향을 잡았고, 약간의 고생 끝에 일러 준 집을 찾아내는 데 성공했습니다. 그 집은 석탄 창고였고 그 위로 한 층이 있었는데, 그 위층 뒷방에 제가 찾던 이가 누워 있었지요."]], [["‘A wretched-looking woman, the man’s wife, met me on the stairs, and, telling me that he had just fallen into a kind of doze, led me softly in, and placed a chair for me at the bedside.", "\"그 사내의 아내인, 비참한 몰골의 여인이 계단에서 저를 맞이하며, 그가 방금 일종의 선잠에 빠졌다고 일러 주고는 저를 조용히 안으로 이끌어 침대맡에 의자를 놓아 주었습니다."], ["The sick man was lying with his face turned towards the wall; and as he took no heed of my presence, I had leisure to observe the place in which I found myself.", "병든 사내는 얼굴을 벽 쪽으로 돌린 채 누워 있었고, 제가 와 있다는 데 아무 신경을 쓰지 않았기에, 저는 제가 들어선 그곳을 찬찬히 살펴볼 겨를이 있었습니다."]], [["‘He was lying on an old bedstead, which turned up during the day.", "\"그는 낮이면 접어 올려 두는 낡은 침대 틀 위에 누워 있었습니다."], ["The tattered remains of a checked curtain were drawn round the bed’s head, to exclude the wind, which, however, made its way into the comfortless room through the numerous chinks in the door, and blew it to and fro every instant.", "격자무늬 휘장의 누덕누덕 해진 잔해가 바람을 막으려 침대 머리맡에 둘러쳐져 있었으나, 바람은 문에 난 무수한 틈으로 그 안락이라곤 없는 방으로 비집고 들어와 그 휘장을 순간순간 이리저리 나부끼게 했습니다."], ["There was a low cinder fire in a rusty, unfixed grate; and an old three-cornered stained table, with some medicine bottles, a broken glass, and a few other domestic articles, was drawn out before it.", "녹슨, 붙박이지 않은 화격자에는 낮은 잿불이 타고 있었고, 그 앞으로는 얼룩진 낡은 삼각 탁자가 끌려 나와 있었는데, 그 위에는 약병 몇 개와 깨진 유리잔, 그리고 몇 가지 살림 물건이 놓여 있었지요."], ["A little child was sleeping on a temporary bed which had been made for it on the floor, and the woman sat on a chair by its side.", "어린아이 하나가 바닥에 임시로 마련된 잠자리에서 자고 있었고, 여인은 그 곁의 의자에 앉아 있었습니다."], ["There were a couple of shelves, with a few plates and cups and saucers; and a pair of stage shoes and a couple of foils hung beneath them.", "선반 두 개에는 접시 몇 개와 찻잔과 받침이 놓여 있었으며, 그 아래에는 무대용 신발 한 켤레와 펜싱용 검 두 자루가 걸려 있었습니다."], ["With the exception of little heaps of rags and bundles which had been carelessly thrown into the corners of the room, these were the only things in the apartment.", "방 구석구석에 아무렇게나 던져 놓은 누더기와 보따리 무더기를 빼고는, 이것이 그 방에 있는 전부였습니다."]], [["‘I had had time to note these little particulars, and to mark the heavy breathing and feverish startings of the sick man, before he was aware of my presence.", "\"저는 이 자잘한 것들을 눈여겨보고, 그 병든 사내의 거친 숨소리와 열에 들떠 흠칫거리는 모습을 살필 만큼의 시간이 있었습니다. 그가 제가 와 있음을 알아채기 전까지요."], ["In the restless attempts to procure some easy resting-place for his head, he tossed his hand out of the bed, and it fell on mine.", "머리를 편히 둘 자리를 찾으려고 안절부절 애쓰던 그는 손을 침대 밖으로 내던졌고, 그 손이 제 손 위에 떨어졌습니다."], ["He started up, and stared eagerly in my face.", "그는 화들짝 일어나 제 얼굴을 골똘히 들여다보았습니다."]], [["‘“Mr. Hutley, John,”", "\"'허틀리 씨예요, 존.'"], ["said his wife; “Mr.", "그의 아내가 말했습니다."], ["Hutley, that you sent for to- night, you know.”", "'오늘 밤에 당신이 부르러 보냈던 허틀리 씨 말이에요, 알잖아요.'"]], [["‘“Ah!”", "\"'아!'"], ["said the invalid, passing his hand across his forehead; “Hutley-- Hutley--let me see.”", "환자가 이마를 손으로 쓸며 말했습니다. '허틀리—허틀리—어디 보자.'"], ["He seemed endeavouring to collect his thoughts for a few seconds, and then grasping me tightly by the wrist said, “Don’t leave me--don’t leave me, old fellow.", "그는 몇 초간 생각을 추스르려 애쓰는 듯하더니, 제 손목을 꽉 움켜쥐며 말했습니다. '날 떠나지 마—날 떠나지 마요, 친구."], ["She’ll murder me; I know she will.”", "저 여자가 날 죽일 거요. 죽일 게 분명해.'"]], [["‘“Has he been long so?”", "\"'이런 지 오래되었소?'"], ["said I, addressing his weeping wife.", "제가 흐느끼는 그의 아내에게 물었습니다."]], [["‘“Since yesterday night,”", "\"'어젯밤부터요.'"], ["she replied.", "그녀가 대답했습니다."], ["“John, John, don’t you know me?”", "'존, 존, 나 몰라보겠어요?'"]], [["‘“Don’t let her come near me,”", "\"'저 여자를 내게 가까이 오지 못하게 해요.'"], ["said the man, with a shudder, as she stooped over him.", "사내가 그녀가 자기 위로 몸을 굽히자 몸서리치며 말했습니다. '저 여자를 쫓아내요."], ["“Drive her away; I can’t bear her near me.”", "곁에 두는 걸 견딜 수가 없어.' 그는 죽음 같은 두려움의 눈빛으로 그녀를 사납게 노려보더니, 제 귀에 대고 속삭였습니다."], ["He stared wildly at her, with a look of deadly apprehension, and then whispered in my ear, “I beat her, Jem; I beat her yesterday, and many times before.", "'내가 저 여자를 때렸어, 젬. 어제도 때렸고, 그 전에도 여러 번 때렸지."], ["I have starved her and the boy too; and now I am weak and helpless, Jem, she’ll murder me for it; I know she will.", "저 여자도 아이도 굶겼고. 그런데 이제 내가 약해지고 무력해졌으니, 젬, 저 여자가 그 앙갚음으로 날 죽일 거야."], ["If you’d seen her cry, as I have, you’d know it too.", "분명히 그럴 거야. 자네가 나처럼 저 여자가 우는 걸 봤더라면, 자네도 알 거야."], ["Keep her off.”", "저 여자를 떼어 놔 줘.'"], ["He relaxed his grasp, and sank back exhausted on the pillow.", "그는 움켜쥔 손을 늦추고는 기진하여 베개에 다시 쓰러졌습니다."]], [["‘I knew but too well what all this meant.", "\"저는 이 모든 것이 무엇을 뜻하는지 너무도 잘 알았습니다."], ["If I could have entertained any doubt of it, for an instant, one glance at the woman’s pale face and wasted form would have sufficiently explained the real state of the case.", "설령 잠시라도 의심을 품었다 한들, 그 여인의 핼쑥한 얼굴과 야윈 몸을 한 번 보는 것만으로도 그 실상을 충분히 알 수 있었을 겁니다. '비켜서 계시는 게 낫겠습니다.'"], ["“You had better stand aside,”", "제가 그 가엾은 여인에게 말했습니다."], ["said I to the poor creature. “You can do him no good.", "'당신은 그에게 도움이 되지 못합니다."], ["Perhaps he will be calmer, if he does not see you.”", "당신을 보지 않으면 좀 진정될지도 모르지요.'"], ["She retired out of the man’s sight.", "그녀는 사내의 시야 밖으로 물러났습니다."], ["He opened his eyes after a few seconds, and looked anxiously round.", "그는 몇 초 뒤 눈을 뜨고 불안하게 사방을 둘러보았습니다."]], [["‘“Is she gone?”", "\"'저 여자 갔소?'"], ["he eagerly inquired.", "그가 다급히 물었습니다."]], [["‘“Yes--yes,”", "\"'네—네.'"], ["said I; “she shall not hurt you.”", "제가 말했습니다. '저분이 당신을 해치지 못할 겁니다.'"]], [["‘“I’ll tell you what, Jem,”", "\"'내 말 좀 들어 봐, 젬.'"], ["said the man, in a low voice, “she does hurt me.", "사내가 나직한 목소리로 말했습니다. '저 여자가 정말로 날 해친단 말이야."], ["There’s something in her eyes wakes such a dreadful fear in my heart, that it drives me mad.", "저 여자 눈에는 뭔가가 있어서, 내 가슴에 그토록 끔찍한 두려움을 깨우니, 그게 날 미치게 만들어."], ["All last night, her large, staring eyes and pale face were close to mine; wherever I turned, they turned; and whenever I started up from my sleep, she was at the bedside looking at me.”", "어젯밤 내내, 저 크고 부릅뜬 눈과 핼쑥한 얼굴이 내 얼굴 바로 곁에 붙어 있었어. 내가 어디로 돌아눕든 그것들도 돌아눕고, 잠결에 화들짝 깰 때마다 저 여자가 침대맡에서 날 들여다보고 있었지.'"], ["He drew me closer to him, as he said in a deep alarmed whisper, “Jem, she must be an evil spirit--a devil!", "그는 저를 더 가까이 끌어당기며, 깊고 겁에 질린 속삭임으로 말했습니다. '젬, 저 여자는 악령이 틀림없어—악마야!"], ["Hush!", "쉿!"], ["I know she is.", "분명히 그래."], ["If she had been a woman she would have died long ago.", "저 여자가 사람이었다면 벌써 옛날에 죽었을 거야."], ["No woman could have borne what she has.”", "어떤 여자도 저 여자가 견뎌 낸 걸 견디지 못했을 테니까.'"]], [["‘I sickened at the thought of the long course of cruelty and neglect which must have occurred to produce such an impression on such a man.", "\"그런 사내에게 그런 인상을 남기기까지 얼마나 길고 잔혹한 학대와 방치가 있었을지를 생각하니, 저는 속이 메스꺼워졌습니다."], ["I could say nothing in reply; for who could offer hope, or consolation, to the abject being before me?", "저는 아무 대꾸도 할 수 없었습니다. 제 앞에 놓인 이 비참한 존재에게 누가 희망이나 위안을 건넬 수 있겠습니까?"]], [["‘I sat there for upwards of two hours, during which time he tossed about, murmuring exclamations of pain or impatience, restlessly throwing his arms here and there, and turning constantly from side to side.", "\"저는 거기 두 시간 넘게 앉아 있었는데, 그동안 그는 고통이나 초조의 신음을 중얼거리며 몸을 뒤척였고, 안절부절 두 팔을 이리저리 내던지며 끊임없이 옆으로 돌아누웠습니다."], ["At length he fell into that state of partial unconsciousness, in which the mind wanders uneasily from scene to scene, and from place to place, without the control of reason, but still without being able to divest itself of an indescribable sense of present suffering.", "마침내 그는 부분적인 무의식 상태에 빠졌는데, 그 상태에서는 정신이 이성의 통제 없이 이 장면에서 저 장면으로, 이곳에서 저곳으로 불안하게 떠돌면서도, 여전히 형언할 수 없는 현재의 고통 감각만은 떨쳐 내지 못합니다."], ["Finding from his incoherent wanderings that this was the case, and knowing that in all probability the fever would not grow immediately worse, I left him, promising his miserable wife that I would repeat my visit next evening, and, if necessary, sit up with the patient during the night.", "그의 두서없는 헛소리에서 사정이 그러함을 알아챈 데다, 십중팔구 열이 당장 더 악화되지는 않으리라는 것을 알았기에, 저는 그를 떠나며 그 비참한 아내에게 이튿날 저녁 다시 와서, 필요하다면 밤새 환자 곁을 지키겠노라 약속했습니다."]], [["‘I kept my promise.", "\"저는 약속을 지켰습니다."], ["The last four-and-twenty hours had produced a frightful alteration.", "지난 스물네 시간이 무서운 변화를 빚어 놓았더군요."], ["The eyes, though deeply sunk and heavy, shone with a lustre frightful to behold.", "두 눈은 깊이 꺼지고 무거워졌으나, 보기에 섬뜩한 광채로 빛나고 있었습니다."], ["The lips were parched, and cracked in many places; the hard, dry skin glowed with a burning heat; and there was an almost unearthly air of wild anxiety in the man’s face, indicating even more strongly the ravages of the disease.", "입술은 바싹 마르고 여러 군데 갈라졌으며, 딱딱하고 메마른 살갗은 타는 듯한 열기로 달아올라 있었고, 사내의 얼굴에는 거의 이 세상 것 같지 않은 격렬한 불안의 기색이 어려 있어, 병이 갉아먹은 자취를 한층 더 강하게 드러냈습니다."], ["The fever was at its height.", "열은 절정에 달해 있었지요."]], [["‘I took the seat I had occupied the night before, and there I sat for hours, listening to sounds which must strike deep to the heart of the most callous among human beings--the awful ravings of a dying man.", "\"저는 전날 밤 앉았던 자리에 앉아, 몇 시간이고 거기 앉아, 인간 가운데 가장 무딘 자의 가슴까지도 깊이 찔러 들 소리를—죽어 가는 사내의 끔찍한 헛소리를 들었습니다."], ["From what I had heard of the medical attendant’s opinion, I knew there was no hope for him: I was sitting by his death-bed.", "진료한 의사의 소견을 전해 들은 바로, 저는 그에게 아무 가망이 없음을 알았습니다. 저는 그의 임종 침상 곁에 앉아 있었던 것이지요."], ["I saw the wasted limbs-- which a few hours before had been distorted for the amusement of a boisterous gallery, writhing under the tortures of a burning fever--I heard the clown’s shrill laugh, blending with the low murmurings of the dying man.", "저는 그 여윈 사지를 보았습니다—불과 몇 시간 전만 해도 소란스러운 객석을 즐겁게 하려고 일그러뜨려졌던 그 사지가, 이제 타는 듯한 열의 고문 아래 뒤틀리고 있었지요—저는 죽어 가는 사내의 나직한 웅얼거림에 뒤섞이는, 광대의 날카로운 웃음소리를 들었습니다."]], [["‘It is a touching thing to hear the mind reverting to the ordinary occupations and pursuits of health, when the body lies before you weak and helpless; but when those occupations are of a character the most strongly opposed to anything we associate with grave and solemn ideas, the impression produced is infinitely more powerful.", "\"몸이 약하고 무력하게 눈앞에 누워 있는데 정신은 건강하던 시절의 평범한 일거리와 본분으로 되돌아가는 것을 듣는 일은 가슴을 저미는 일입니다. 그러나 그 일거리들이 진지하고 엄숙한 관념과 우리가 연결 짓는 모든 것과 가장 강하게 어긋나는 성질의 것일 때, 거기서 빚어지는 인상은 한없이 더 강렬합니다."], ["The theatre and the public-house were the chief themes of the wretched man’s wanderings.", "극장과 선술집이 그 비참한 사내의 헛소리에서 주된 주제였습니다."], ["It was evening, he fancied; he had a part to play that night; it was late, and he must leave home instantly.", "저녁이라고 그는 상상했지요. 그날 밤에 맡은 배역이 있고, 시간이 늦었으니 당장 집을 나서야 한다고요."], ["Why did they hold him, and prevent his going?--he should lose the money--he must go.", "어째서 사람들이 그를 붙잡아 못 가게 하는가?—돈을 못 받게 될 텐데—가야만 하는데. 안 돼!"], ["No! they would not let him.", "그들이 그를 놓아주지 않아."], ["He hid his face in his burning hands, and feebly bemoaned his own weakness, and the cruelty of his persecutors.", "그는 타는 듯한 두 손에 얼굴을 묻고, 제 약함과 자기를 핍박하는 자들의 잔인함을 가냘프게 한탄했습니다."], ["A short pause, and he shouted out a few doggerel rhymes--the last he had ever learned.", "잠시 멈추는가 싶더니, 그는 졸렬한 운율의 시구 몇 줄을—그가 마지막으로 익혔던 시구를—외쳐 댔습니다."], ["He rose in bed, drew up his withered limbs, and rolled about in uncouth positions; he was acting--he was at the theatre.", "그는 침대에서 일어나 시든 사지를 끌어모으고는 거칠고 우스꽝스러운 자세로 뒹굴었습니다. 연기를 하고 있었던 것이지요—극장에 있었던 것입니다."], ["A minute’s silence, and he murmured the burden of some roaring song.", "일 분간의 침묵이 흐르고, 그는 어떤 떠들썩한 노래의 후렴을 흥얼거렸습니다."], ["He had reached the old house at last--how hot the room was.", "마침내 그는 그 오래된 선술집에 닿았던 것입니다—방 안이 어찌 이리 더운가."], ["He had been ill, very ill, but he was well now, and happy.", "그는 아팠습니다, 몹시 아팠지요, 하지만 이제는 멀쩡하고 행복합니다."], ["Fill up his glass.", "그의 잔을 채워라."], ["Who was that, that dashed it from his lips?", "그의 입술에서 잔을 쳐서 떨어뜨린 게 누구인가?"], ["It was the same persecutor that had followed him before.", "전에도 그를 따라다니던 바로 그 핍박자였습니다."], ["He fell back upon his pillow and moaned aloud.", "그는 베개에 다시 쓰러져 큰 소리로 신음했습니다."], ["A short period of oblivion, and he was wandering through a tedious maze of low-arched rooms--so low, sometimes, that he must creep upon his hands and knees to make his way along; it was close and dark, and every way he turned, some obstacle impeded his progress.", "잠깐의 망각이 흐르고, 그는 천장이 낮은 방들이 이어진 지긋지긋한 미로를 헤매고 있었습니다—어떤 데는 어찌나 낮은지 손과 무릎으로 기어야만 길을 나아갈 수 있었지요. 답답하고 어두웠으며, 어디로 돌아서든 무언가 장애물이 그의 앞길을 막았습니다."], ["There were insects, too, hideous crawling things, with eyes that stared upon him, and filled the very air around, glistening horribly amidst the thick darkness of the place.", "벌레들도 있었지요, 그를 노려보는 눈을 가진 흉측하게 기어 다니는 것들이, 사방의 공기를 가득 채우며 그곳의 짙은 어둠 속에서 끔찍하게 번득였습니다."], ["The walls and ceiling were alive with reptiles--the vault expanded to an enormous size--frightful figures flitted to and fro--and the faces of men he knew, rendered hideous by gibing and mouthing, peered out from among them; they were searing him with heated irons, and binding his head with cords till the blood started; and he struggled madly for life.", "벽과 천장이 파충류로 우글거렸고—그 토굴은 거대한 크기로 부풀었으며—무시무시한 형상들이 이리저리 휙휙 날아다녔고—그가 아는 사람들의 얼굴이, 비웃고 입을 일그러뜨려 흉측해진 채 그것들 사이로 내다보았습니다. 그들은 달군 쇠로 그를 지지고, 피가 솟구칠 때까지 그의 머리를 밧줄로 동여맸으며—그는 살려고 미친 듯이 몸부림쳤습니다."]], [["‘At the close of one of these paroxysms, when I had with great difficulty held him down in his bed, he sank into what appeared to be a slumber.", "\"이런 발작 가운데 하나가 끝날 무렵, 제가 무척 애를 써서 그를 침대에 눌러 뉘었을 때, 그는 잠인 듯한 상태로 빠져들었습니다."], ["Overpowered with watching and exertion, I had closed my eyes for a few minutes, when I felt a violent clutch on my shoulder.", "지켜보느라, 또 힘쓰느라 진이 빠진 저는 몇 분간 눈을 감았는데, 그때 누군가 제 어깨를 사납게 움켜쥐는 것을 느꼈습니다."], ["I awoke instantly.", "저는 곧바로 깨어났지요."], ["He had raised himself up, so as to seat himself in bed--a dreadful change had come over his face, but consciousness had returned, for he evidently knew me.", "그는 몸을 일으켜 침대에 앉은 자세였고—그의 얼굴에는 무서운 변화가 닥쳐 있었으나, 의식이 돌아와 있었으니, 분명 저를 알아보았기 때문입니다."], ["The child, who had been long since disturbed by his ravings, rose from its little bed, and ran towards its father, screaming with fright--the mother hastily caught it in her arms, lest he should injure it in the violence of his insanity; but, terrified by the alteration of his features, stood transfixed by the bedside.", "그의 헛소리에 진작부터 잠을 설치던 아이가 제 작은 잠자리에서 일어나, 겁에 질려 비명을 지르며 아버지에게 달려갔습니다—어머니는 광기의 발작 속에서 그가 아이를 해칠까 봐 황급히 아이를 품에 끌어안았으나, 그의 일그러진 얼굴에 겁을 먹어 침대맡에 못 박힌 듯 서 있었습니다."], ["He grasped my shoulder convulsively, and, striking his breast with the other hand, made a desperate attempt to articulate.", "그는 제 어깨를 발작적으로 움켜쥐고, 다른 손으로 제 가슴을 치며, 필사적으로 무어라 말하려 했습니다."], ["It was unavailing; he extended his arm towards them, and made another violent effort.", "헛일이었지요. 그는 그들을 향해 팔을 뻗고 또 한 번 사납게 안간힘을 썼습니다."], ["There was a rattling noise in the throat--a glare of the eye--a short stifled groan- -and he fell back--dead!’", "목구멍에서 그렁거리는 소리가 났고—두 눈이 번쩍였으며—짧게 막힌 신음이 새어 나오더니—그는 뒤로 쓰러졌습니다—숨이 끊긴 채로!\""]], [["It would afford us the highest gratification to be enabled to record Mr. Pickwick’s opinion of the foregoing anecdote.", "앞의 일화에 대한 픽윅 씨의 견해를 기록할 수 있다면 우리로서는 더없는 만족일 것이다."], ["We have little doubt that we should have been enabled to present it to our readers, but for a most unfortunate occurrence.", "더없이 불행한 사건만 아니었더라면 그것을 독자들께 제시할 수 있었으리라는 데 우리는 별 의심이 없다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick had replaced on the table the glass which, during the last few sentences of the tale, he had retained in his hand; and had just made up his mind to speak--indeed, we have the authority of Mr. Snodgrass’s note-book for stating, that he had actually opened his mouth--when the waiter entered the room, and said--", "픽윅 씨는 이야기의 마지막 몇 문장 동안 손에 쥐고 있던 잔을 탁자에 도로 내려놓고는, 막 입을 떼기로 마음먹은 참이었다—실은, 그가 실제로 입을 벌렸다고 단언할 근거를 우리는 스노드그래스 씨의 수첩에서 얻고 있다—그때 급사가 방으로 들어와 말했다."]], [["‘Some gentlemen, Sir.’", "\"손님들이 오셨습니다, 선생님.\""]], [["It has been conjectured that Mr.", "이렇게 방해받았을 때 픽윅 씨가 막 어떤 발언을 내놓으려던 참이었으리라는 추측이 있는데, 그 발언은 템스강까지는 아니더라도 세상을 밝혀 주었을 것이다."], ["Pickwick was on the point of delivering some remarks which would have enlightened the world, if not the Thames, when he was thus interrupted; for he gazed sternly on the waiter’s countenance, and then looked round on the company generally, as if seeking for information relative to the new-comers.", "그는 급사의 얼굴을 준엄하게 쏘아본 뒤, 새로 온 이들에 관한 정보를 구하기라도 하듯 좌중을 두루 둘러보았기 때문이다."]], [["‘Oh!’ said Mr.", "\"오!\""], ["Winkle, rising, ‘some friends of mine--show them in.", "윈클 씨가 일어서며 말했다. \"내 친구들일세—들여보내게."], ["Very pleasant fellows,’ added Mr.", "아주 유쾌한 친구들이지요.\""], ["Winkle, after the waiter had retired-- ‘officers of the 97th, whose acquaintance I made rather oddly this morning.", "급사가 물러간 뒤 윈클 씨가 덧붙였다. \"제97연대 장교들인데, 오늘 아침 제법 묘하게 알게 되었답니다."], ["You will like them very much.’", "다들 무척 마음에 드실 겁니다.\""]], [["Mr. Pickwick’s equanimity was at once restored.", "픽윅 씨의 평정심은 곧바로 회복되었다."], ["The waiter returned, and ushered three gentlemen into the room.", "급사가 돌아와 세 신사를 방으로 안내해 들였다."]], [["‘Lieutenant Tappleton,’ said Mr. Winkle, ‘Lieutenant Tappleton, Mr. Pickwick--Doctor Payne, Mr. Pickwick--Mr. Snodgrass you have seen before, my friend Mr. Tupman, Doctor Payne--Doctor Slammer, Mr. Pickwick--Mr. Tupman, Doctor Slam--’", "\"태플턴 중위입니다.\" 윈클 씨가 말했다. \"태플턴 중위, 이쪽은 픽윅 씨—페인 군의관, 이쪽은 픽윅 씨—스노드그래스 씨는 전에 만나셨고, 제 친구 터프먼 씨, 이쪽은 페인 군의관—슬래머 군의관, 이쪽은 픽윅 씨—터프먼 씨, 이쪽은 슬래머 군의—\""]], [["Here Mr. Winkle suddenly paused; for strong emotion was visible on the countenance both of Mr.", "여기서 윈클 씨는 불현듯 말을 멈추었다."], ["Tupman and the doctor.", "터프먼 씨와 군의관 양쪽의 얼굴에 강한 감정이 역력했기 때문이다."]], [["‘I have met this gentleman before,’ said the Doctor, with marked emphasis.", "\"나는 이 신사분을 전에 만난 적이 있소.\" 군의관이 또렷이 힘을 주어 말했다."]], [["‘Indeed!’ said Mr.", "\"그렇소이까!\""], ["Winkle.", "윈클 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘And--and that person, too, if I am not mistaken,’ said the doctor, bestowing a scrutinising glance on the green-coated stranger.", "\"그리고—그리고 저자도, 내가 잘못 본 게 아니라면 말이오.\" 군의관이 초록 코트를 입은 낯선 이에게 살피는 듯한 눈길을 던지며 말했다."], ["‘I think I gave that person a very pressing invitation last night, which he thought proper to decline.’ Saying which the doctor scowled magnanimously on the stranger, and whispered his friend Lieutenant Tappleton.", "\"내 생각엔 어젯밤 저자에게 아주 절박한 초대를 한 것 같은데, 저자는 그걸 마다하는 게 옳다고 여겼지요.\" 그렇게 말하면서 군의관은 그 낯선 이를 도량 있게 노려보고는, 친구인 태플턴 중위에게 무언가를 속삭였다."]], [["‘You don’t say so,’ said that gentleman, at the conclusion of the whisper.", "\"설마 그럴 리가.\" 그 신사가 속삭임이 끝나자 말했다."]], [["‘I do, indeed,’ replied Doctor Slammer.", "\"정말이라니까.\" 슬래머 군의관이 대답했다."]], [["‘You are bound to kick him on the spot,’ murmured the owner of the camp- stool, with great importance.", "\"당장 저자를 걷어차 줘야 하네.\" 야영 의자의 주인이 자못 거드름을 피우며 중얼거렸다."]], [["‘Do be quiet, Payne,’ interposed the lieutenant.", "\"좀 조용히 하게, 페인.\" 중위가 끼어들었다."], ["‘Will you allow me to ask you, sir,’ he said, addressing Mr.", "\"선생, 한 가지 여쭤도 되겠습니까.\""], ["Pickwick, who was considerably mystified by this very unpolite by-play--‘will you allow me to ask you, Sir, whether that person belongs to your party?’", "그는 이 무척이나 무례한 곁다리 연극에 적잖이 어리둥절해진 픽윅 씨를 향해 말했다. \"선생, 저 사람이 댁의 일행에 속하는지 여쭤도 되겠습니까?\""]], [["‘No, Sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"아니오, 선생.\""], ["Pickwick, ‘he is a guest of ours.’", "픽윅 씨가 대답했다. \"저분은 우리의 손님이오.\""]], [["‘He is a member of your club, or I am mistaken?’ said the lieutenant inquiringly.", "\"그럼 댁네 클럽 회원이로군요, 내가 잘못 안 게 아니라면?\" 중위가 캐묻듯 말했다."]], [["‘Certainly not,’ responded Mr.", "\"전혀 아니오.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 응했다."]], [["‘And never wears your club-button?’ said the lieutenant.", "\"그리고 댁네 클럽 단추도 한 번도 단 적이 없고요?\" 중위가 말했다."]], [["‘No--never!’ replied the astonished Mr.", "\"없소—한 번도!\""], ["Pickwick.", "어리둥절해진 픽윅 씨가 대답했다."]], [["Lieutenant Tappleton turned round to his friend Doctor Slammer, with a scarcely perceptible shrug of the shoulder, as if implying some doubt of the accuracy of his recollection.", "태플턴 중위는 자기 기억의 정확성에 대한 의심을 내비치기라도 하듯, 거의 알아채기 힘들 만큼 어깨를 으쓱하며 친구인 슬래머 군의관 쪽으로 돌아섰다."], ["The little doctor looked wrathful, but confounded; and Mr. Payne gazed with a ferocious aspect on the beaming countenance of the unconscious Pickwick.", "키 작은 군의관은 노기를 띠었으나 당혹스러운 표정이었고, 페인 씨는 아무것도 모르는 픽윅의 환한 얼굴을 사나운 기색으로 노려보았다."]], [["‘Sir,’ said the doctor, suddenly addressing Mr.", "\"선생.\""], ["Tupman, in a tone which made that gentleman start as perceptibly as if a pin had been cunningly inserted in the calf of his leg, ‘you were at the ball here last night!’", "군의관이 불쑥 터프먼 씨에게 말을 건넸는데, 그 어조는 마치 종아리에 핀을 교묘히 찔러 넣기라도 한 듯 그 신사를 눈에 띄게 움찔하게 했다. \"댁은 어젯밤 여기 무도회에 있었지요!\""]], [["Mr. Tupman gasped a faint affirmative, looking very hard at Mr. Pickwick all the while.", "터프먼 씨는 내내 픽윅 씨를 몹시 뚫어지게 바라보며, 가냘프게 그렇다고 헐떡이듯 답했다."]], [["‘That person was your companion,’ said the doctor, pointing to the still unmoved stranger.", "\"저 사람이 댁의 동행이었소.\" 군의관이 여전히 미동도 없는 낯선 이를 가리키며 말했다."]], [["Mr. Tupman admitted the fact.", "터프먼 씨는 그 사실을 인정했다."]], [["‘Now, sir,’ said the doctor to the stranger, ‘I ask you once again, in the presence of these gentlemen, whether you choose to give me your card, and to receive the treatment of a gentleman; or whether you impose upon me the necessity of personally chastising you on the spot?’", "\"자, 선생.\" 군의관이 낯선 이에게 말했다. \"이 신사분들 앞에서 다시 한번 묻겠소. 댁은 내게 명함을 건네고 신사로서의 대우를 받겠소, 아니면 내가 이 자리에서 직접 댁을 응징할 수밖에 없게 만들겠소?\""]], [["‘Stay, sir,’ said Mr.", "\"잠깐, 선생.\""], ["Pickwick, ‘I really cannot allow this matter to go any further without some explanation.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"나는 정말이지 이 일이 약간의 해명 없이 더 진행되도록 둘 수가 없소."], ["Tupman, recount the circumstances.’", "터프먼, 사정을 이야기해 보게.\""]], [["Mr.", "이처럼 엄숙히 종용받은 터프먼 씨는 몇 마디로 그 사정을 진술했다."], ["Tupman, thus solemnly adjured, stated the case in a few words; touched slightly on the borrowing of the coat; expatiated largely on its having been done ‘after dinner’; wound up with a little penitence on his own account; and left the stranger to clear himself as best he could.", "코트를 빌린 일은 슬쩍 건드리고, 그것이 '저녁 식사 후'에 일어났다는 점은 길게 부연했으며, 자기 책임에 대해 약간의 뉘우침으로 마무리한 뒤, 낯선 이가 알아서 변명하도록 내버려 두었다."]], [["He was apparently about to proceed to do so, when Lieutenant Tappleton, who had been eyeing him with great curiosity, said with considerable scorn, ‘Haven’t I seen you at the theatre, Sir?’", "낯선 이가 막 그렇게 하려는 참이었는데, 그를 큰 호기심으로 훑어보던 태플턴 중위가 자못 경멸조로 말했다. \"내가 댁을 극장에서 본 적이 있지 않소, 선생?\""]], [["‘Certainly,’ replied the unabashed stranger.", "\"그렇소.\" 낯선 이가 주눅 들지 않고 대답했다."]], [["‘He is a strolling actor!’ said the lieutenant contemptuously, turning to Doctor Slammer.--‘He acts in the piece that the officers of the 52nd get up at the Rochester Theatre to-morrow night.", "\"이자는 떠돌이 배우라네!\" 중위가 슬래머 군의관을 돌아보며 경멸조로 말했다."], ["You cannot proceed in this affair, Slammer--impossible!’", "\"내일 밤 제52연대 장교들이 로체스터 극장에서 올리는 연극에 출연하는 자야. 슬래머, 이 일을 더 진행할 수는 없네—불가능해!\""]], [["‘Quite!’ said the dignified Payne.", "\"전혀!\" 위엄을 차린 페인이 말했다."]], [["‘Sorry to have placed you in this disagreeable situation,’ said Lieutenant Tappleton, addressing Mr.", "\"이런 불쾌한 처지에 빠뜨려 드려 유감입니다.\" 태플턴 중위가 픽윅 씨에게 말했다."], ["Pickwick; ‘allow me to suggest, that the best way of avoiding a recurrence of such scenes in future will be to be more select in the choice of your companions.", "\"앞으로 이런 장면이 되풀이되는 것을 피하는 최선의 길은, 동행을 고르는 데 좀 더 신중을 기하시는 것임을 일러 드리지요."], ["Good-evening, Sir!’ and the lieutenant bounced out of the room.", "안녕히 계시오, 선생!\" 그러고는 중위는 방에서 펄쩍 뛰쳐나갔다."]], [["‘And allow me to say, Sir,’ said the irascible Doctor Payne, ‘that if I had been Tappleton, or if I had been Slammer, I would have pulled your nose, Sir, and the nose of every man in this company.", "\"그리고 한마디 하겠소, 선생.\" 성마른 페인 군의관이 말했다."], ["I would, sir-- every man.", "\"만일 내가 태플턴이었거나, 내가 슬래머였더라면, 댁의 코를, 그리고 이 자리에 있는 모든 사람의 코를 비틀어 놓았을 거요."], ["Payne is my name, sir--Doctor Payne of the 43rd.", "그랬을 거요, 선생—한 사람도 빠짐없이. 내 이름은 페인이오, 선생—제43연대 페인 군의관이지."], ["Good- evening, Sir.’ Having concluded this speech, and uttered the last three words in a loud key, he stalked majestically after his friend, closely followed by Doctor Slammer, who said nothing, but contented himself by withering the company with a look.", "안녕히 계시오, 선생.\" 이 연설을 마치고 마지막 세 마디를 큰 소리로 내뱉은 뒤, 그는 친구의 뒤를 위풍당당하게 성큼성큼 따라 나갔고, 슬래머 군의관이 바짝 뒤따랐는데, 그는 아무 말도 하지 않고 그저 한 차례 눈길로 좌중을 짓눌러 버리는 것으로 만족했다."]], [["Rising rage and extreme bewilderment had swelled the noble breast of Mr. Pickwick, almost to the bursting of his waistcoat, during the delivery of the above defiance.", "위의 도전적 언사가 쏟아지는 동안, 치미는 분노와 극도의 당혹감이 픽윅 씨의 고결한 가슴을 부풀려, 조끼가 터질 지경에 이르게 했다."], ["He stood transfixed to the spot, gazing on vacancy.", "그는 그 자리에 못 박힌 듯 서서 허공을 응시했다."], ["The closing of the door recalled him to himself.", "문이 닫히는 소리가 그를 제정신으로 돌려놓았다."], ["He rushed forward with fury in his looks, and fire in his eye.", "그는 얼굴에 격분을, 눈에 불을 담고 앞으로 달려 나갔다. 그의 손이 문고리에 닿았다."], ["His hand was upon the lock of the door; in another instant it would have been on the throat of Doctor Payne of the 43rd, had not Mr. Snodgrass seized his revered leader by the coat tail, and dragged him backwards.", "한순간만 더 있었더라면 그것은 제43연대 페인 군의관의 멱살에 닿았을 터인데, 스노드그래스 씨가 자기 존경하는 지도자의 코트 자락을 붙잡아 뒤로 끌어당기지 않았더라면 말이다."]], [["‘Restrain him,’ cried Mr.", "\"저분을 말리게.\""], ["Snodgrass; ‘Winkle, Tupman--he must not peril his distinguished life in such a cause as this.’", "스노드그래스 씨가 외쳤다. \"윈클, 터프먼—이런 명분 따위에 저분의 고귀한 목숨을 걸게 해서는 안 되네.\""]], [["‘Let me go,’ said Mr.", "\"놓아 주게.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Hold him tight,’ shouted Mr.", "\"단단히 붙잡게.\" 스노드그래스 씨가 소리쳤다."], ["Snodgrass; and by the united efforts of the whole company, Mr. Pickwick was forced into an arm-chair.", "그리하여 좌중 전원의 합심한 노력으로 픽윅 씨는 안락의자에 떠밀려 앉혀졌다."]], [["‘Leave him alone,’ said the green-coated stranger; ‘brandy-and-water-- jolly old gentleman--lots of pluck--swallow this--ah!--capital stuff.’ Having previously tested the virtues of a bumper, which had been mixed by the dismal man, the stranger applied the glass to Mr.", "\"내버려 두시오.\" 초록 코트를 입은 낯선 이가 말했다."], ["Pickwick’s mouth; and the remainder of its contents rapidly disappeared.", "\"브랜디 워터—유쾌한 노신사—배짱도 두둑하시고—이걸 들이켜시오—아!—기막힌 물건이지.\" 음울한 사내가 섞어 놓은 가득 찬 잔의 효능을 미리 시험해 본 뒤, 낯선 이는 그 잔을 픽윅 씨의 입에 갖다 댔고, 잔에 남은 것은 순식간에 사라졌다."]], [["There was a short pause; the brandy-and-water had done its work; the amiable countenance of Mr.", "잠깐의 멈춤이 흘렀다."], ["Pickwick was fast recovering its customary expression.", "브랜디 워터가 제 몫을 해냈고, 픽윅 씨의 온화한 얼굴은 빠르게 평소의 표정을 되찾아 갔다."]], [["‘They are not worth your notice,’ said the dismal man.", "\"저들은 당신이 마음 쓸 가치도 없는 자들입니다.\" 음울한 사내가 말했다."]], [["‘You are right, sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"옳은 말씀이오, 선생.\""], ["Pickwick, ‘they are not.", "픽윅 씨가 대답했다."], ["I am ashamed to have been betrayed into this warmth of feeling.", "\"가치도 없지요. 이런 격앙된 감정에 휩쓸린 것이 부끄럽소."], ["Draw your chair up to the table, Sir.’", "의자를 탁자 쪽으로 끌어당기시오, 선생.\""]], [["The dismal man readily complied; a circle was again formed round the table, and harmony once more prevailed.", "음울한 사내는 선뜻 응했다. 다시 탁자를 둘러싸고 둥글게 자리가 잡혔고, 화기애애함이 또 한 번 자리를 지배했다."], ["Some lingering irritability appeared to find a resting-place in Mr. Winkle’s bosom, occasioned possibly by the temporary abstraction of his coat--though it is scarcely reasonable to suppose that so slight a circumstance can have excited even a passing feeling of anger in a Pickwickian’s breast.", "어딘가 가시지 않은 짜증이 윈클 씨의 가슴속에 머물 자리를 찾은 듯했는데, 아마도 그의 코트가 잠시 빌려진 일 탓이리라—하긴 그토록 사소한 일이 픽윅 회원의 가슴에 스치는 분노의 감정이나마 불러일으켰으리라고 보기는 좀처럼 합당치 않지만 말이다."], ["With this exception, their good-humour was completely restored; and the evening concluded with the conviviality with which it had begun.", "이 한 가지를 빼고는, 그들의 좋은 기분은 온전히 회복되었고, 그날 저녁은 시작될 때와 같은 흥겨움으로 마무리되었다."]], [["CHAPTER IV. A FIELD DAY AND BIVOUAC--MORE NEW FRIENDS--AN INVITATION TO THE COUNTRY\nMany authors entertain, not only a foolish, but a really dishonest objection to acknowledge the sources whence they derive much valuable information.", "많은 저자들은 그들이 귀중한 정보를 길어 올린 출처를 밝히는 일에 어리석을 뿐만 아니라 실로 정직하지 못한 거부감을 품는다."], ["We have no such feeling.", "우리는 그런 감정이 없다."], ["We are merely endeavouring to discharge, in an upright manner, the responsible duties of our editorial functions; and whatever ambition we might have felt under other circumstances to lay claim to the authorship of these adventures, a regard for truth forbids us to do more than claim the merit of their judicious arrangement and impartial narration.", "우리는 다만 편집자로서의 책임 있는 직무를 올곧게 수행하려 애쓸 따름이다. 다른 정황이었더라면 이 모험담들의 저작권을 주장하고 싶은 야심을 어느 정도 느꼈을지 모르나, 진실에 대한 존중이 우리로 하여금 그것들을 분별 있게 배열하고 공정하게 서술한 공로를 주장하는 것 이상은 하지 못하게 막는다."], ["The Pickwick papers are our New River Head; and we may be compared to the New River Company.", "픽윅 페이퍼스는 우리의 뉴 리버 헤드이며, 우리는 뉴 리버 회사에 견줄 만하다."], ["The labours of others have raised for us an immense reservoir of important facts.", "다른 이들의 노고가 우리를 위해 중요한 사실들의 거대한 저수지를 쌓아 올렸다."], ["We merely lay them on, and communicate them, in a clear and gentle stream, through the medium of these pages, to a world thirsting for Pickwickian knowledge.", "우리는 그저 그것들을 끌어다가, 맑고 부드러운 물줄기로, 이 지면이라는 매개를 통해, 픽윅적 지식에 목말라하는 세상에 흘려보낼 뿐이다."]], [["Acting in this spirit, and resolutely proceeding on our determination to avow our obligations to the authorities we have consulted, we frankly say, that to the note-book of Mr.", "이러한 정신에 따라, 우리가 참고한 권위들에 진 빚을 밝히겠다는 결심을 단호히 밀고 나가면서, 우리는 솔직히 말한다."], ["Snodgrass are we indebted for the particulars recorded in this and the succeeding chapter--particulars which, now that we have disburdened our consciences, we shall proceed to detail without further comment.", "이 장과 다음 장에 기록된 세부 사실들은 스노드그래스 씨의 수첩에 빚진 것이라고. 이제 양심의 짐을 벗었으니, 우리는 더 이상의 부연 없이 그 세부 사실들을 상세히 풀어 나가기로 한다."]], [["The whole population of Rochester and the adjoining towns rose from their beds at an early hour of the following morning, in a state of the utmost bustle and excitement.", "로체스터와 그 인근 마을의 주민 전체가 이튿날 아침 이른 시각에 더없는 부산함과 흥분 속에서 자리를 박차고 일어났다."], ["A grand review was to take place upon the lines.", "진지에서 성대한 열병식이 열릴 예정이었다."], ["The manoeuvres of half a dozen regiments were to be inspected by the eagle eye of the commander-in-chief; temporary fortifications had been erected, the citadel was to be attacked and taken, and a mine was to be sprung.", "여섯 개 연대의 기동훈련을 총사령관의 매서운 눈이 사열할 것이었으며, 임시 요새가 세워졌고, 성채가 공격받아 함락될 것이었으며, 지뢰가 터질 것이었다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick was, as our readers may have gathered from the slight extract we gave from his description of Chatham, an enthusiastic admirer of the army.", "픽윅 씨는, 우리가 그의 채텀 묘사에서 인용한 짤막한 발췌를 통해 독자들이 짐작했을지 모르나, 군대를 열렬히 흠모하는 사람이었다."], ["Nothing could have been more delightful to him--nothing could have harmonised so well with the peculiar feeling of each of his companions--as this sight.", "이 광경만큼 그를 기쁘게 할 수 있는 것은 없었으며, 그의 동행들 각자가 지닌 독특한 감정과 이토록 잘 어우러질 수 있는 것도 없었다."], ["Accordingly they were soon afoot, and walking in the direction of the scene of action, towards which crowds of people were already pouring from a variety of quarters.", "그리하여 그들은 곧 길을 나서, 작전이 벌어질 무대를 향해 걸어갔는데, 그쪽으로는 이미 사방에서 사람들이 무리 지어 쏟아져 들어오고 있었다."]], [["The appearance of everything on the lines denoted that the approaching ceremony was one of the utmost grandeur and importance.", "진지에서 모든 것의 모습은 다가오는 의식이 더없이 웅장하고 중대한 것임을 말해 주고 있었다."], ["There were sentries posted to keep the ground for the troops, and servants on the batteries keeping places for the ladies, and sergeants running to and fro, with vellum-covered books under their arms, and Colonel Bulder, in full military uniform, on horseback, galloping first to one place and then to another, and backing his horse among the people, and prancing, and curvetting, and shouting in a most alarming manner, and making himself very hoarse in the voice, and very red in the face, without any assignable cause or reason whatever.", "군대를 위해 자리를 지키려고 보초들이 배치되어 있었고, 포대 위에는 부인들의 자리를 맡아 두는 하인들이 있었으며, 양피지로 표지를 씌운 책을 옆구리에 낀 하사관들이 이리저리 뛰어다녔고, 정장 군복을 차려입은 불더 대령은 말 위에 올라 처음에는 이쪽으로, 다음에는 저쪽으로 질주하며, 사람들 사이로 말을 뒤로 몰고, 앞발을 들게 하고, 껑충껑충 뛰게 하며, 더없이 놀라운 태도로 고함을 질러 대 목소리가 몹시 쉬고 얼굴이 몹시 벌게졌으나, 거기에는 어떤 짐작할 만한 까닭이나 이유도 전혀 없었다."], ["Officers were running backwards and forwards, first communicating with Colonel Bulder, and then ordering the sergeants, and then running away altogether; and even the very privates themselves looked from behind their glazed stocks with an air of mysterious solemnity, which sufficiently bespoke the special nature of the occasion.", "장교들은 앞뒤로 뛰어다니며, 처음에는 불더 대령과 의논하고, 다음에는 하사관들에게 명령하고, 그다음에는 죄다 달아나 버렸으며, 심지어 사병들조차 광택 나는 옷깃 너머로 신비로운 엄숙함을 풍기며 바라보고 있었으니, 그것은 이 행사의 특별한 성격을 충분히 드러내 주었다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick and his three companions stationed themselves in the front of the crowd, and patiently awaited the commencement of the proceedings.", "픽윅 씨와 그의 세 동행은 군중의 맨 앞에 자리를 잡고, 행사가 시작되기를 참을성 있게 기다렸다."], ["The throng was increasing every moment; and the efforts they were compelled to make, to retain the position they had gained, sufficiently occupied their attention during the two hours that ensued.", "군중은 시시각각 불어났으며, 그들이 차지한 자리를 지키려고 어쩔 수 없이 들여야 했던 노력은 그 뒤로 이어진 두 시간 동안 그들의 주의를 충분히 사로잡았다."], ["At one time there was a sudden pressure from behind, and then Mr. Pickwick was jerked forward for several yards, with a degree of speed and elasticity highly inconsistent with the general gravity of his demeanour; at another moment there was a request to ‘keep back’ from the front, and then the butt-end of a musket was either dropped upon Mr.", "한번은 뒤에서 갑자기 밀어붙이는 힘이 가해져, 픽윅 씨가 그의 전반적인 점잖은 태도와는 도무지 어울리지 않는 속도와 탄력으로 몇 야드 앞으로 홱 떠밀려 나갔다."], ["Pickwick’s toe, to remind him of the demand, or thrust into his chest, to insure its being complied with. Then some facetious gentlemen on the left, after pressing sideways in a body, and squeezing Mr.", "또 한번은 앞쪽에서 \"물러서라\"는 요구가 있었는데, 그러면 머스킷 총의 개머리판이 픽윅 씨의 발가락 위로 떨어져 그 요구를 일깨워 주거나, 아니면 그의 가슴팍에 들이밀려 그 요구가 반드시 지켜지도록 만들었다."], ["Snodgrass into the very last extreme of human torture, would request to know ‘vere he vos a shovin’ to’; and when Mr.", "그러더니 왼편의 어떤 익살스러운 신사들이 한 무리로 옆에서 밀어붙여 스노드그래스 씨를 인간이 견딜 수 있는 고문의 극한까지 짓눌러 놓고는, \"워디로 떠미는 거유?\"라고 알고 싶어 했다."], ["Winkle had done expressing his excessive indignation at witnessing this unprovoked assault, some person behind would knock his hat over his eyes, and beg the favour of his putting his head in his pocket.", "그리고 윈클 씨가 이 까닭 없는 폭행을 목격한 데 대한 극심한 분노를 다 표하고 나면, 뒤의 누군가가 그의 모자를 눈 위까지 푹 눌러 씌우고는 머리를 호주머니에 넣어 주십사 청하는 것이었다."], ["These, and other practical witticisms, coupled with the unaccountable absence of Mr. Tupman (who had suddenly disappeared, and was nowhere to be found), rendered their situation upon the whole rather more uncomfortable than pleasing or desirable.", "이런 것들과 그 밖의 짓궂은 익살들이, 터프먼 씨가 영문 모르게 사라져 버린 일과 (그는 갑자기 자취를 감추어 어디서도 찾을 수 없었다) 맞물려, 그들의 처지를 전체적으로 보아 즐겁거나 바람직하다기보다는 오히려 더 불편한 것으로 만들었다."]], [["At length that low roar of many voices ran through the crowd which usually announces the arrival of whatever they have been waiting for.", "마침내 무언가 기다리던 것이 도착했음을 알리는, 여러 목소리의 그 낮은 함성이 군중 사이로 퍼져 나갔다."], ["All eyes were turned in the direction of the sally-port.", "모든 눈이 출격문 쪽으로 향했다."], ["A few moments of eager expectation, and colours were seen fluttering gaily in the air, arms glistened brightly in the sun, column after column poured on to the plain.", "간절한 기대의 몇 순간이 지나자, 군기들이 공중에서 흥겹게 펄럭이는 것이 보였고, 무기들이 햇빛 속에서 환하게 번쩍였으며, 종대가 잇따라 평원으로 쏟아져 나왔다."], ["The troops halted and formed; the word of command rang through the line; there was a general clash of muskets as arms were presented; and the commander-in-chief, attended by Colonel Bulder and numerous officers, cantered to the front.", "군대가 멈춰 서서 대형을 갖추었고, 명령이 대열을 따라 울려 퍼졌으며, 받들어총을 하느라 머스킷 총들이 일제히 철컥하는 소리를 냈다. 그리고 불더 대령과 수많은 장교를 거느린 총사령관이 가벼운 구보로 앞으로 나왔다."], ["The military bands struck up altogether; the horses stood upon two legs each, cantered backwards, and whisked their tails about in all directions; the dogs barked, the mob screamed, the troops recovered, and nothing was to be seen on either side, as far as the eye could reach, but a long perspective of red coats and white trousers, fixed and motionless.", "군악대들이 일제히 연주를 시작했고, 말들은 저마다 두 다리로 서서 뒷걸음치며 사방으로 꼬리를 휘둘러 댔으며, 개들은 짖고, 군중은 비명을 지르고, 군대는 자세를 가다듬었으며, 눈길이 닿는 한 어느 쪽을 보아도 보이는 것은 붉은 군복과 흰 바지가 고정된 채 미동도 없이 길게 늘어선 광경뿐이었다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick had been so fully occupied in falling about, and disentangling himself, miraculously, from between the legs of horses, that he had not enjoyed sufficient leisure to observe the scene before him, until it assumed the appearance we have just described.", "픽윅 씨는 여기저기 넘어지며, 그리고 말들의 다리 사이에서 기적적으로 몸을 빼내느라 너무도 분주했던 터라, 눈앞의 광경이 우리가 방금 묘사한 모습을 갖추게 될 때까지는 그것을 관찰할 여유를 충분히 누리지 못했다."], ["When he was at last enabled to stand firmly on his legs, his gratification and delight were unbounded.", "마침내 두 다리로 굳건히 설 수 있게 되었을 때, 그의 만족과 기쁨은 끝이 없었다."]], [["‘Can anything be finer or more delightful?’ he inquired of Mr.", "\"이보다 더 멋지고 더 즐거운 것이 어디 있겠소?\""], ["Winkle.", "그가 윈클 씨에게 물었다."]], [["‘Nothing,’ replied that gentleman, who had had a short man standing on each of his feet for the quarter of an hour immediately preceding.", "\"없지요.\" 그 신사가 대답했는데, 그는 바로 앞선 십오 분 동안 두 발을 각각 키 작은 남자에게 하나씩 밟히고 있던 터였다."]], [["‘It is indeed a noble and a brilliant sight,’ said Mr. Snodgrass, in whose bosom a blaze of poetry was rapidly bursting forth, ‘to see the gallant defenders of their country drawn up in brilliant array before its peaceful citizens; their faces beaming--not with warlike ferocity, but with civilised gentleness; their eyes flashing--not with the rude fire of rapine or revenge, but with the soft light of humanity and intelligence.’", "\"참으로 고결하고 찬란한 광경입니다.\" 스노드그래스 씨가 말했는데, 그의 가슴속에서는 시의 불길이 빠르게 타오르고 있었다. \"조국의 용맹한 수호자들이 평화로운 시민들 앞에 찬란하게 정렬한 모습을 보다니요. 그들의 얼굴은 빛나고 있습니다—호전적인 사나움이 아니라 문명화된 온화함으로 말입니다. 그들의 눈은 번뜩이고 있습니다—약탈이나 복수의 거친 불꽃이 아니라 인간애와 지성의 부드러운 빛으로 말입니다.\""]], [["Mr.", "픽윅 씨는 이 찬사의 정신에 충분히 공감했으나, 그 표현을 그대로 되받아 외칠 수는 없었다."], ["Pickwick fully entered into the spirit of this eulogium, but he could not exactly re-echo its terms; for the soft light of intelligence burned rather feebly in the eyes of the warriors, inasmuch as the command ‘eyes front’ had been given, and all the spectator saw before him was several thousand pair of optics, staring straight forward, wholly divested of any expression whatever.", "왜냐하면 지성의 부드러운 빛은 그 전사들의 눈 속에서 다소 희미하게 타오르고 있었기 때문이다. \"앞을 보라\"는 명령이 떨어진 터라, 구경꾼의 눈앞에 보이는 것이라곤 정면을 똑바로 응시하는, 어떤 표정도 전혀 깃들지 않은 수천 쌍의 눈동자뿐이었던 것이다."]], [["‘We are in a capital situation now,’ said Mr.", "\"우리는 이제 아주 좋은 자리에 있군요.\""], ["Pickwick, looking round him.", "픽윅 씨가 주위를 둘러보며 말했다."], ["The crowd had gradually dispersed in their immediate vicinity, and they were nearly alone.", "그들 바로 가까이에 있던 군중이 차츰 흩어져, 그들은 거의 그들끼리만 남아 있었다."]], [["‘Capital!’ echoed both Mr.", "\"아주 좋군요!\""], ["Snodgrass and Mr. Winkle.", "스노드그래스 씨와 윈클 씨가 입을 모아 따라 했다."]], [["‘What are they doing now?’ inquired Mr.", "\"저들이 지금 뭘 하는 거지요?\""], ["Pickwick, adjusting his spectacles.", "픽윅 씨가 안경을 고쳐 쓰며 물었다."]], [["‘I--I--rather think,’ said Mr.", "\"제—제 생각엔 말이죠.\""], ["Winkle, changing colour--‘I rather think they’re going to fire.’", "윈클 씨가 안색이 변하며 말했다. \"제 생각엔 발포할 것 같습니다.\""]], [["‘Nonsense,’ said Mr.", "\"말도 안 되는 소리.\""], ["Pickwick hastily.", "픽윅 씨가 다급히 말했다."]], [["‘I--I--really think they are,’ urged Mr.", "\"저—저는 정말로 그럴 것 같습니다.\""], ["Snodgrass, somewhat alarmed.", "스노드그래스 씨가 다소 겁먹은 채 거들었다."]], [["‘Impossible,’ replied Mr. Pickwick.", "\"그럴 리가 없소.\""], ["He had hardly uttered the word, when the whole half-dozen regiments levelled their muskets as if they had but one common object, and that object the Pickwickians, and burst forth with the most awful and tremendous discharge that ever shook the earth to its centres, or an elderly gentleman off his.", "픽윅 씨가 대답했다. 그가 그 말을 채 내뱉기도 전에, 여섯 개 연대 전체가 마치 단 하나의 공통된 목표를 가진 듯 머스킷 총을 겨누었으니, 그 목표란 바로 픽윅 회원들이었고, 일찍이 대지를 그 중심까지 뒤흔든 적도, 또는 노신사를 그 중심에서 떨어뜨린 적도 없을 만큼 더없이 무시무시하고 어마어마한 일제 사격을 터뜨렸다."]], [["It was in this trying situation, exposed to a galling fire of blank cartridges, and harassed by the operations of the military, a fresh body of whom had begun to fall in on the opposite side, that Mr. Pickwick displayed that perfect coolness and self-possession, which are the indispensable accompaniments of a great mind. He seized Mr. Winkle by the arm, and placing himself between that gentleman and Mr. Snodgrass, earnestly besought them to remember that beyond the possibility of being rendered deaf by the noise, there was no immediate danger to be apprehended from the firing.", "바로 이 시련의 상황에서, 공포탄의 따가운 사격에 노출되고, 반대편에서 새로운 한 무리가 정렬하기 시작한 군대의 작전에 시달리는 가운데, 픽윅 씨는 위대한 정신에 없어서는 안 될 동반자인 그 완벽한 침착함과 자제력을 보여 주었다. 그는 윈클 씨의 팔을 붙잡고, 그 신사와 스노드그래스 씨 사이에 자신을 세운 뒤, 소음 때문에 귀가 먹먹해질 가능성을 넘어서는, 발포로 인한 어떤 임박한 위험도 염려할 것이 없음을 기억하라고 간곡히 당부했다."]], [["‘But--but--suppose some of the men should happen to have ball cartridges by mistake,’ remonstrated Mr.", "\"하지만—하지만—혹시 누군가 실수로 실탄을 가지고 있다면 어쩌지요.\""], ["Winkle, pallid at the supposition he was himself conjuring up.", "윈클 씨가 스스로 떠올린 그 상상에 새파랗게 질려 항변했다."], ["‘I heard something whistle through the air now--so sharp; close to my ear.’", "\"방금 무언가 공중을 가르고 휙 지나가는 소리를 들었거든요—아주 날카롭게—제 귀 바로 옆을요.\""]], [["‘We had better throw ourselves on our faces, hadn’t we?’ said Mr.", "\"우리 얼굴을 땅에 대고 엎드리는 게 낫지 않을까요?\""], ["Snodgrass.", "스노드그래스 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘No, no--it’s over now,’ said Mr.", "\"아니, 아니오—이제 끝났소.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick. His lip might quiver, and his cheek might blanch, but no expression of fear or concern escaped the lips of that immortal man.", "그의 입술은 떨렸을지 모르고 그의 뺨은 창백해졌을지 모르나, 그 불멸의 인물의 입술에서는 두려움이나 근심의 기색이라곤 한마디도 새어 나오지 않았다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick was right--the firing ceased; but he had scarcely time to congratulate himself on the accuracy of his opinion, when a quick movement was visible in the line; the hoarse shout of the word of command ran along it, and before either of the party could form a guess at the meaning of this new manoeuvre, the whole of the half-dozen regiments, with fixed bayonets, charged at double-quick time down upon the very spot on which Mr.", "픽윅 씨가 옳았다—발포가 그쳤다."], ["Pickwick and his friends were stationed.", "그러나 그가 자신의 견해가 정확했음을 자축할 겨를도 거의 갖기 전에, 대열 안에서 재빠른 움직임이 보였다. 명령의 쉰 외침이 대열을 따라 퍼져 나갔고, 일행 중 누구도 이 새로운 기동의 의미를 미처 짐작하기도 전에, 여섯 개 연대 전체가 총검을 꽂은 채 속보로 픽윅 씨와 그의 친구들이 서 있는 바로 그 지점을 향해 돌격해 내려왔다."]], [["Man is but mortal; and there is a point beyond which human courage cannot extend. Mr.", "인간은 한낱 죽을 운명일 뿐이며, 인간의 용기가 미칠 수 없는 한계점이라는 것이 있다."], ["Pickwick gazed through his spectacles for an instant on the advancing mass, and then fairly turned his back and--we will not say fled; firstly, because it is an ignoble term, and, secondly, because Mr.", "픽윅 씨는 안경 너머로 한순간 밀려드는 무리를 응시하더니, 그러고는 완전히 등을 돌리고는—달아났다고는 하지 않겠다."], ["Pickwick’s figure was by no means adapted for that mode of retreat-- he trotted away, at as quick a rate as his legs would convey him; so quickly, indeed, that he did not perceive the awkwardness of his situation, to the full extent, until too late.", "첫째로 그것은 비천한 표현이기 때문이요, 둘째로 픽윅 씨의 몸매는 결코 그런 식의 후퇴에 적합하지 않았기 때문이다—그는 다리가 옮겨 주는 한 빠른 속도로 종종걸음을 쳤으니, 실로 어찌나 빨랐던지 자신이 처한 상황의 난처함을 그 온전한 크기로 깨달았을 때는 이미 너무 늦은 뒤였다."]], [["The opposite troops, whose falling-in had perplexed Mr. Pickwick a few seconds before, were drawn up to repel the mimic attack of the sham besiegers of the citadel; and the consequence was that Mr.", "몇 초 전 그 정렬로 픽윅 씨를 어리둥절하게 했던 반대편 군대는, 성채를 거짓으로 포위한 가짜 공격자들의 모의 공격을 격퇴하려고 대형을 갖춘 것이었다."], ["Pickwick and his two companions found themselves suddenly inclosed between two lines of great length, the one advancing at a rapid pace, and the other firmly waiting the collision in hostile array.", "그리하여 픽윅 씨와 그의 두 동행은 매우 길게 늘어선 두 대열 사이에 별안간 갇혀 버린 자신들을 발견하게 되었으니, 한쪽은 빠른 속도로 진격해 오고 있었고, 다른 한쪽은 적대적인 대형을 이룬 채 충돌을 굳건히 기다리고 있었다."]], [["‘Hoi!’ shouted the officers of the advancing line.", "\"어이!\" 진격해 오는 대열의 장교들이 고함쳤다."]], [["‘Get out of the way!’ cried the officers of the stationary one.", "\"비켜라!\" 멈춰 서 있는 대열의 장교들이 외쳤다."]], [["‘Where are we to go to?’ screamed the agitated Pickwickians.", "\"우리더러 어디로 가란 말이오?\" 당황한 픽윅 회원들이 비명을 질렀다."]], [["‘Hoi--hoi--hoi!’ was the only reply.", "\"어이—어이—어이!\""], ["There was a moment of intense bewilderment, a heavy tramp of footsteps, a violent concussion, a smothered laugh; the half-dozen regiments were half a thousand yards off, and the soles of Mr.", "그것이 유일한 대답이었다. 한순간 극심한 당혹이 있었고, 묵직한 발소리, 격렬한 충돌, 숨죽인 웃음소리가 있었다."], ["Pickwick’s boots were elevated in air.", "여섯 개 연대는 반 마일 저편에 있었고, 픽윅 씨의 장화 밑창은 공중에 치켜올려져 있었다."]], [["Mr. Snodgrass and Mr. Winkle had each performed a compulsory somerset with remarkable agility, when the first object that met the eyes of the latter as he sat on the ground, staunching with a yellow silk handkerchief the stream of life which issued from his nose, was his venerated leader at some distance off, running after his own hat, which was gambolling playfully away in perspective.", "스노드그래스 씨와 윈클 씨는 저마다 놀라운 민첩함으로 강제적인 공중제비를 한 차례씩 넘었는데, 후자가 땅바닥에 주저앉아 노란 비단 손수건으로 코에서 흘러나오는 생명의 물줄기를 막으며 눈에 처음 들어온 광경은, 저만치 떨어진 곳에서 자신의 모자를 쫓아 달려가는 그들의 존경하는 지도자의 모습이었으니, 그 모자는 저 멀리로 장난스럽게 깡충거리며 달아나고 있었다."]], [["There are very few moments in a man’s existence when he experiences so much ludicrous distress, or meets with so little charitable commiseration, as when he is in pursuit of his own hat.", "사람의 일생에서 자신의 모자를 쫓아갈 때만큼 우스꽝스러운 곤경을 그토록 많이 겪거나, 그토록 적은 자비로운 동정을 받는 순간도 드물다."], ["A vast deal of coolness, and a peculiar degree of judgment, are requisite in catching a hat.", "모자를 붙잡는 데는 엄청난 침착함과 남다른 정도의 판단력이 필요하다."], ["A man must not be precipitate, or he runs over it; he must not rush into the opposite extreme, or he loses it altogether.", "사람은 성급해서는 안 되니, 그러면 모자를 밟고 넘어가 버린다. 또 그 반대의 극단으로 달려들어서도 안 되니, 그러면 모자를 아주 놓쳐 버린다."], ["The best way is to keep gently up with the object of pursuit, to be wary and cautious, to watch your opportunity well, get gradually before it, then make a rapid dive, seize it by the crown, and stick it firmly on your head; smiling pleasantly all the time, as if you thought it as good a joke as anybody else.", "가장 좋은 방법은 쫓는 대상과 부드럽게 보조를 맞추며, 빈틈없이 신중하게 기회를 잘 엿보아, 차츰 그 앞으로 나아간 다음, 재빨리 몸을 날려 모자의 우묵한 꼭대기를 붙잡아 머리에 단단히 눌러쓰는 것이다. 그러면서도 줄곧 즐겁게 미소를 지어, 마치 그것이 누구에게나 그렇듯 자신에게도 썩 좋은 농담이라고 여기는 듯 보이는 것이다."]], [["There was a fine gentle wind, and Mr. Pickwick’s hat rolled sportively before it.", "부드럽고 산뜻한 바람이 불었고, 픽윅 씨의 모자는 그 앞에서 장난스럽게 굴러갔다."], ["The wind puffed, and Mr. Pickwick puffed, and the hat rolled over and over as merrily as a lively porpoise in a strong tide: and on it might have rolled, far beyond Mr.", "바람이 푹푹 불었고, 픽윅 씨도 헉헉거렸으며, 모자는 거센 물살 속의 활기찬 돌고래처럼 흥겹게 데굴데굴 굴러갔다."], ["Pickwick’s reach, had not its course been providentially stopped, just as that gentleman was on the point of resigning it to its fate.", "그리고 그 신사가 모자를 운명에 내맡기려던 바로 그 찰나에 섭리의 손길로 그 진로가 막히지 않았더라면, 모자는 픽윅 씨의 손이 닿지 않는 저 멀리까지 계속 굴러갔을 것이다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick, we say, was completely exhausted, and about to give up the chase, when the hat was blown with some violence against the wheel of a carriage, which was drawn up in a line with half a dozen other vehicles on the spot to which his steps had been directed.", "우리가 말하건대, 픽윅 씨는 완전히 기진맥진하여 추격을 막 포기하려던 참이었는데, 그때 모자가 어느 정도 세차게 날려 가 어느 마차의 바퀴에 부딪혔으니, 그 마차는 그의 발걸음이 향하던 바로 그 자리에 다른 여섯 대의 탈것과 한 줄로 세워져 있었다."], ["Mr. Pickwick, perceiving his advantage, darted briskly forward, secured his property, planted it on his head, and paused to take breath.", "픽윅 씨는 자신에게 유리해진 형국을 알아채고는 잽싸게 앞으로 달려 나가, 자기 소유물을 확보하여 머리에 얹고는, 숨을 돌리려고 멈춰 섰다."], ["He had not been stationary half a minute, when he heard his own name eagerly pronounced by a voice, which he at once recognised as Mr.", "그가 가만히 멈춰 선 지 채 삼십 초도 되지 않았을 때, 어떤 목소리가 그의 이름을 간절히 부르는 것을 들었는데, 그는 그것이 터프먼 씨의 목소리임을 즉시 알아차렸다."], ["Tupman’s, and, looking upwards, he beheld a sight which filled him with surprise and pleasure.", "위를 올려다보니 그는 놀라움과 기쁨으로 가득 찬 광경을 보게 되었다."]], [["In an open barouche, the horses of which had been taken out, the better to accommodate it to the crowded place, stood a stout old gentleman, in a blue coat and bright buttons, corduroy breeches and top-boots, two young ladies in scarfs and feathers, a young gentleman apparently enamoured of one of the young ladies in scarfs and feathers, a lady of doubtful age, probably the aunt of the aforesaid, and Mr. Tupman, as easy and unconcerned as if he had belonged to the family from the first moments of his infancy.", "지붕 없는 사륜마차 한 대—붐비는 장소에 더 잘 맞추려고 말들을 떼어 낸 마차—안에 서 있는 것은, 푸른 외투에 번쩍이는 단추를 달고 코듀로이 반바지에 목 긴 장화를 신은 뚱뚱한 노신사와, 숄을 두르고 깃털을 단 두 젊은 숙녀와, 숄을 두르고 깃털을 단 그 젊은 숙녀들 중 한 명에게 반한 듯한 한 젊은 신사와, 나이를 가늠하기 어려운, 아마도 앞서 말한 숙녀들의 고모인 듯한 부인과, 그리고 터프먼 씨였는데, 터프먼 씨는 마치 갓난아기 시절부터 그 가족의 일원이었던 것처럼 편안하고 태평스러웠다."], ["Fastened up behind the barouche was a hamper of spacious dimensions--one of those hampers which always awakens in a contemplative mind associations connected with cold fowls, tongues, and bottles of wine--and on the box sat a fat and red-faced boy, in a state of somnolency, whom no speculative observer could have regarded for an instant without setting down as the official dispenser of the contents of the before-mentioned hamper, when the proper time for their consumption should arrive.", "사륜마차 뒤에 묶여 있는 것은 넉넉한 크기의 바구니였으니—사색에 잠긴 마음에 차가운 닭고기와 혓바닥 고기와 포도주병에 얽힌 연상을 언제나 불러일으키는 그런 바구니들 가운데 하나였다—그리고 마부석에는 뚱뚱하고 얼굴이 벌건 소년이 졸음에 겨운 상태로 앉아 있었으니, 사색을 즐기는 관찰자라면 누구든 그 소년을, 앞서 말한 바구니 속 내용물을 소비할 적절한 때가 오면 그것을 나누어 줄 공식 분배자라고 단번에 단정 짓지 않고서는 한순간도 바라볼 수 없었을 것이다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick had bestowed a hasty glance on these interesting objects, when he was again greeted by his faithful disciple.", "픽윅 씨가 이 흥미로운 대상들에 황급히 한 번 눈길을 던졌을 때, 그의 충실한 제자가 다시 그를 향해 인사를 건넸다."]], [["‘Pickwick--Pickwick,’ said Mr.", "\"픽윅—픽윅.\" 터프먼 씨가 말했다."], ["Tupman; ‘come up here.", "\"이리 올라오시오."], ["Make haste.’", "어서요.\""]], [["‘Come along, Sir.", "\"이리 오시지요, 선생. 어서 올라오십시오.\""], ["Pray, come up,’ said the stout gentleman.", "뚱뚱한 신사가 말했다. \"조!—저 녀석 망할, 또 잠들어 버렸군.—조, 발판 좀 내려라.\""], ["‘Joe!--damn that boy, he’s gone to sleep again.--Joe, let down the steps.’ The fat boy rolled slowly off the box, let down the steps, and held the carriage door invitingly open. Mr.", "뚱뚱한 소년이 마부석에서 느릿느릿 굴러 내려와 발판을 내리고는 마차 문을 손짓하듯 활짝 열어 놓았다."], ["Snodgrass and Mr. Winkle came up at the moment.", "바로 그 순간 스노드그래스 씨와 윈클 씨가 다가왔다."]], [["‘Room for you all, gentlemen,’ said the stout man.", "\"여러분 모두 탈 자리가 있소이다.\""], ["‘Two inside, and one out.", "뚱뚱한 사내가 말했다."], ["Joe, make room for one of these gentlemen on the box.", "\"둘은 안에, 하나는 밖에 타시오."], ["Now, Sir, come along;’ and the stout gentleman extended his arm, and pulled first Mr.", "조, 이분들 중 한 분을 위해 마부석에 자리를 만들어라. 자, 선생, 이리 오시오.\""], ["Pickwick, and then Mr. Snodgrass, into the barouche by main force.", "그러고는 뚱뚱한 신사가 팔을 뻗어, 먼저 픽윅 씨를, 다음에 스노드그래스 씨를 힘으로 끌어당겨 사륜마차 안으로 들였다."], ["Mr. Winkle mounted to the box, the fat boy waddled to the same perch, and fell fast asleep instantly.", "윈클 씨는 마부석에 올라탔고, 뚱뚱한 소년은 똑같은 자리로 뒤뚱뒤뚱 올라가더니 곧바로 깊이 잠들어 버렸다."]], [["‘Well, gentlemen,’ said the stout man, ‘very glad to see you.", "\"자, 여러분.\" 뚱뚱한 사내가 말했다."], ["Know you very well, gentlemen, though you mayn’t remember me.", "\"만나 뵙게 되어 무척 반갑소이다. 여러분이 나를 기억하지 못하실지 몰라도, 나는 여러분을 아주 잘 알지요."], ["I spent some ev’nin’s at your club last winter--picked up my friend Mr. Tupman here this morning, and very glad I was to see him.", "지난겨울에 여러분의 클럽에서 몇 차례 저녁을 보냈소이다—오늘 아침에 여기 내 친구 터프먼 씨를 만났는데, 그를 보게 되어 어찌나 반가웠는지 모른다오."], ["Well, Sir, and how are you?", "그래, 선생, 어떻게 지내시오?"], ["You do look uncommon well, to be sure.’", "참으로 보기 드물게 좋아 보이시는구려, 정말로.\""]], [["Mr. Pickwick acknowledged the compliment, and cordially shook hands with the stout gentleman in the top-boots.", "픽윅 씨는 그 찬사를 받아들이고, 목 긴 장화를 신은 뚱뚱한 신사와 다정하게 악수를 나누었다."]], [["‘Well, and how are you, sir?’ said the stout gentleman, addressing Mr.", "\"자, 그래 댁은 어떻소, 선생?\" 뚱뚱한 신사가 아버지 같은 근심을 담아 스노드그래스 씨에게 말을 건넸다."], ["Snodgrass with paternal anxiety.", "\"아주 좋으시다고? 그래요, 그거 잘됐소—잘됐어."], ["‘Charming, eh? Well, that’s right-- that’s right.", "그리고 댁은 어떻소, 선생 (윈클 씨에게)?"], ["And how are you, sir (to Mr. Winkle)?", "거참, 좋다고 하시니 반갑소이다."], ["Well, I am glad to hear you say you are well; very glad I am, to be sure.", "정말로 무척 반가워요, 암. 여러분, 내 딸들이오—이 애들이 내 딸내미들이지요."], ["My daughters, gentlemen--my gals these are; and that’s my sister, Miss Rachael Wardle.", "그리고 저쪽은 내 누이, 레이철 와들 양이오. 이 사람은 미스인데—그러면서도 미스가 아니지요—그렇잖소, 선생, 안 그렇소?\""], ["She’s a Miss, she is; and yet she ain’t a Miss--eh, Sir, eh?’ And the stout gentleman playfully inserted his elbow between the ribs of Mr. Pickwick, and laughed very heartily.", "그러고는 뚱뚱한 신사가 장난스럽게 팔꿈치를 픽윅 씨의 갈비뼈 사이로 찔러 넣으며 아주 호탕하게 웃었다."]], [["‘Lor, brother!’ said Miss Wardle, with a deprecating smile.", "\"어머, 오라버니도!\" 와들 양이 못마땅한 듯 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["‘True, true,’ said the stout gentleman; ‘no one can deny it.", "\"맞지, 맞아.\" 뚱뚱한 신사가 말했다."], ["Gentlemen, I beg your pardon; this is my friend Mr.", "\"아무도 그걸 부정할 순 없지."], ["Trundle.", "여러분, 실례했소이다."], ["And now you all know each other, let’s be comfortable and happy, and see what’s going forward; that’s what I say.’ So the stout gentleman put on his spectacles, and Mr.", "이쪽은 내 친구 트런들 씨요. 자, 이제 다들 서로 알게 되었으니, 편안하고 즐겁게 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 구경합시다."], ["Pickwick pulled out his glass, and everybody stood up in the carriage, and looked over somebody else’s shoulder at the evolutions of the military.", "내 말이 그 말이오.\" 그러고는 뚱뚱한 신사가 안경을 쓰자, 픽윅 씨도 자기 안경을 꺼냈고, 모두가 마차 안에서 일어서서 서로의 어깨 너머로 군대의 기동을 바라보았다."]], [["Astounding evolutions they were, one rank firing over the heads of another rank, and then running away; and then the other rank firing over the heads of another rank, and running away in their turn; and then forming squares, with officers in the centre; and then descending the trench on one side with scaling-ladders, and ascending it on the other again by the same means; and knocking down barricades of baskets, and behaving in the most gallant manner possible.", "그것은 놀라운 기동이었으니, 한 열이 다른 열의 머리 너머로 발포하고는 달아나고, 그러면 다른 열이 또 다른 열의 머리 너머로 발포하고는 그 차례로 달아나고, 그러고는 장교들을 가운데 두고 방진을 이루고, 그러고는 한쪽에서 공성 사다리를 타고 참호로 내려갔다가 같은 수단으로 다시 다른 쪽에서 올라오고, 바구니로 만든 방책을 쓰러뜨리고, 더없이 용맹스럽게 처신하는 것이었다."], ["Then there was such a ramming down of the contents of enormous guns on the battery, with instruments like magnified mops; such a preparation before they were let off, and such an awful noise when they did go, that the air resounded with the screams of ladies. The young Misses Wardle were so frightened, that Mr.", "그러더니 포대 위의 거대한 대포들의 내용물을 거대하게 확대한 대걸레 같은 도구로 쑤셔 넣는 일이 있었고, 발사하기 전에 어찌나 준비가 요란했던지, 또 막상 발사할 때는 어찌나 무시무시한 소리가 났던지, 공중이 부인들의 비명으로 울려 퍼졌다."], ["Trundle was actually obliged to hold one of them up in the carriage, while Mr. Snodgrass supported the other; and Mr.", "어린 와들 양들은 어찌나 겁에 질렸던지, 트런들 씨가 실제로 마차 안에서 한 명을 부축해 주어야 했고, 그동안 스노드그래스 씨가 다른 한 명을 떠받쳤다."], ["Wardle’s sister suffered under such a dreadful state of nervous alarm, that Mr. Tupman found it indispensably necessary to put his arm round her waist, to keep her up at all.", "그리고 와들 씨의 누이는 어찌나 끔찍한 신경성 경악에 시달렸던지, 터프먼 씨는 그녀가 도무지 쓰러지지 않게 하려면 그녀의 허리에 팔을 두르는 것이 절대로 필요하다는 것을 깨달았다."], ["Everybody was excited, except the fat boy, and he slept as soundly as if the roaring of cannon were his ordinary lullaby.", "모두가 흥분했으나, 뚱뚱한 소년만은 예외였으니, 그는 대포의 포효가 마치 평소의 자장가라도 되는 듯 곤히 잠들어 있었다."]], [["‘Joe, Joe!’ said the stout gentleman, when the citadel was taken, and the besiegers and besieged sat down to dinner.", "\"조, 조!\" 성채가 함락되고 포위한 자들과 포위당한 자들이 식사를 하려고 자리에 앉았을 때 뚱뚱한 신사가 말했다."], ["‘Damn that boy, he’s gone to sleep again.", "\"저 녀석 망할, 또 잠들어 버렸군."], ["Be good enough to pinch him, sir--in the leg, if you please; nothing else wakes him--thank you.", "선생, 미안하지만 저 녀석 좀 꼬집어 주시겠소—다리를요, 괜찮으시다면. 다른 건 저 녀석을 깨우질 못한다오—고맙소이다."], ["Undo the hamper, Joe.’", "조, 바구니를 풀어라.\""]], [["The fat boy, who had been effectually roused by the compression of a portion of his leg between the finger and thumb of Mr. Winkle, rolled off the box once again, and proceeded to unpack the hamper with more expedition than could have been expected from his previous inactivity.", "윈클 씨의 엄지와 검지 사이에 다리의 한 부분이 눌리는 바람에 효과적으로 깨어난 뚱뚱한 소년은 다시 한번 마부석에서 굴러 내려와, 앞서의 굼뜬 모습으로는 예상할 수 없었을 만큼 빠르게 바구니 푸는 일에 착수했다."]], [["‘Now we must sit close,’ said the stout gentleman.", "\"이제 바싹 붙어 앉아야겠소.\" 뚱뚱한 신사가 말했다."], ["After a great many jokes about squeezing the ladies’ sleeves, and a vast quantity of blushing at sundry jocose proposals, that the ladies should sit in the gentlemen’s laps, the whole party were stowed down in the barouche; and the stout gentleman proceeded to hand the things from the fat boy (who had mounted up behind for the purpose) into the carriage.", "숙녀들의 소매를 짓누른다는 둥 갖가지 농담이 오가고, 숙녀들이 신사들 무릎에 앉아야 한다는 여러 익살스러운 제안에 잔뜩 얼굴이 붉어진 끝에, 일행 전체가 사륜마차 안에 차곡차곡 자리 잡았다. 그러고 나서 뚱뚱한 신사는 뚱뚱한 소년에게서 (이를 위해 소년은 뒤쪽에 올라타 있었다) 물건들을 받아 마차 안으로 건네주기 시작했다."]], [["‘Now, Joe, knives and forks.’ The knives and forks were handed in, and the ladies and gentlemen inside, and Mr.", "\"자, 조, 칼하고 포크.\""], ["Winkle on the box, were each furnished with those useful instruments.", "칼과 포크가 건네져, 안에 있는 숙녀와 신사들, 그리고 마부석의 윈클 씨가 저마다 그 유용한 도구들을 받았다."]], [["‘Plates, Joe, plates.’ A similar process employed in the distribution of the crockery.", "\"접시다, 조, 접시.\" 그릇을 나누어 주는 데도 비슷한 절차가 쓰였다."]], [["‘Now, Joe, the fowls.", "\"자, 조, 닭고기."], ["Damn that boy; he’s gone to sleep again.", "저 녀석 망할, 또 잠들어 버렸군."], ["Joe!", "조!"], ["Joe!’ (Sundry taps on the head with a stick, and the fat boy, with some difficulty, roused from his lethargy.) ‘Come, hand in the eatables.’", "조!\" (막대기로 머리를 몇 차례 톡톡 두드리자, 뚱뚱한 소년이 다소 힘겹게 무기력에서 깨어났다.) \"자, 먹을거리를 건네라.\""]], [["There was something in the sound of the last word which roused the unctuous boy.", "마지막 단어의 울림에는 그 기름진 소년을 일깨우는 무언가가 있었다."], ["He jumped up, and the leaden eyes which twinkled behind his mountainous cheeks leered horribly upon the food as he unpacked it from the basket.", "그는 벌떡 일어났고, 산더미 같은 뺨 뒤에서 반짝이는 납빛 눈동자가 그가 바구니에서 음식을 풀어낼 때 그것을 향해 끔찍하게 곁눈질을 했다."]], [["‘Now make haste,’ said Mr.", "\"자, 어서 서둘러라.\""], ["Wardle; for the fat boy was hanging fondly over a capon, which he seemed wholly unable to part with.", "와들 씨가 말했다. 뚱뚱한 소년이 거세한 수탉 한 마리 위로 애틋하게 몸을 숙인 채 도무지 그것과 헤어지지 못하는 듯했기 때문이다."], ["The boy sighed deeply, and, bestowing an ardent gaze upon its plumpness, unwillingly consigned it to his master.", "소년은 깊이 한숨을 쉬고는, 그 통통한 살집에 뜨거운 시선을 던지더니, 마지못해 그것을 주인에게 넘겨주었다."]], [["‘That’s right--look sharp.", "\"옳지—눈치껏 빨리 해라."], ["Now the tongue--now the pigeon pie.", "자 이제 혓바닥 고기—이제 비둘기 파이."], ["Take care of that veal and ham--mind the lobsters--take the salad out of the cloth--give me the dressing.’ Such were the hurried orders which issued from the lips of Mr.", "그 송아지 고기랑 햄 조심하고—바닷가재 조심하고—샐러드는 보자기에서 꺼내고—드레싱은 나한테 다오.\""], ["Wardle, as he handed in the different articles described, and placed dishes in everybody’s hands, and on everybody’s knees, in endless number.", "와들 씨가 묘사된 여러 가지 음식을 건네주며, 그리고 끝도 없이 많은 접시를 모두의 손에, 또 모두의 무릎에 올려놓으며 입에서 쏟아 낸 다급한 명령들이 이러했다."]], [["‘Now ain’t this capital?’ inquired that jolly personage, when the work of destruction had commenced.", "\"자, 이거 아주 근사하지 않소?\" 파괴의 작업이 시작되자 그 유쾌한 인물이 물었다."]], [["‘Capital!’ said Mr.", "\"근사하군요!\""], ["Winkle, who was carving a fowl on the box.", "마부석에서 닭고기를 썰고 있던 윈클 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Glass of wine?’", "\"포도주 한잔 하시겠소?\""]], [["‘With the greatest pleasure.’", "\"더없이 기쁘게 마시지요.\""]], [["‘You’d better have a bottle to yourself up there, hadn’t you?’", "\"거기서는 아예 한 병 통째로 가지시는 게 낫지 않겠소?\""]], [["‘You’re very good.’", "\"참으로 친절하시군요.\""]], [["‘Joe!’", "\"조!\""]], [["‘Yes, Sir.’ (He wasn’t asleep this time, having just succeeded in abstracting a veal patty.)", "\"예, 선생.\" (이번에는 잠들어 있지 않았으니, 마침 송아지 고기 파이 한 조각을 슬쩍 빼내는 데 성공한 참이었기 때문이다.)"]], [["‘Bottle of wine to the gentleman on the box.", "\"마부석의 신사분께 포도주 한 병."], ["Glad to see you, Sir.’", "만나서 반갑소이다, 선생.\""]], [["‘Thank’ee.’ Mr.", "\"고맙습니다.\""], ["Winkle emptied his glass, and placed the bottle on the coach-box, by his side.", "윈클 씨는 잔을 비우고 병을 자기 옆 마부석 위에 놓았다."]], [["‘Will you permit me to have the pleasure, Sir?’ said Mr.", "\"제게 그 영광을 허락해 주시겠습니까, 선생?\""], ["Trundle to Mr. Winkle.", "트런들 씨가 윈클 씨에게 말했다."]], [["‘With great pleasure,’ replied Mr.", "\"기꺼이 그러지요.\""], ["Winkle to Mr. Trundle, and then the two gentlemen took wine, after which they took a glass of wine round, ladies and all.", "윈클 씨가 트런들 씨에게 대답했고, 그러고 나서 두 신사가 함께 술을 들었으며, 그 뒤에는 숙녀들까지 모두 돌아가며 한 잔씩 들었다."]], [["‘How dear Emily is flirting with the strange gentleman,’ whispered the spinster aunt, with true spinster-aunt-like envy, to her brother, Mr.", "\"가여운 에밀리가 낯선 신사하고 어찌나 시시덕거리는지.\""], ["Wardle.", "노처녀 고모가 진정한 노처녀 고모다운 시샘을 담아 오라버니 와들 씨에게 속삭였다."]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["I don’t know,’ said the jolly old gentleman; ‘all very natural, I dare say--nothing unusual.", "글쎄다.\" 유쾌한 노신사가 말했다."], ["Mr. Pickwick, some wine, Sir?’ Mr.", "\"지극히 자연스러운 일이지 싶다—별난 일도 아니고."], ["Pickwick, who had been deeply investigating the interior of the pigeon-pie, readily assented.", "픽윅 씨, 포도주 좀 드시겠소, 선생?\" 비둘기 파이 속을 깊이 탐구하고 있던 픽윅 씨가 선뜻 응했다."]], [["‘Emily, my dear,’ said the spinster aunt, with a patronising air, ‘don’t talk so loud, love.’", "\"에밀리, 얘야.\" 노처녀 고모가 거들먹거리는 태도로 말했다. \"그렇게 큰 소리로 떠들지 말렴, 아가.\""]], [["‘Lor, aunt!’", "\"어머, 고모도!\""]], [["‘Aunt and the little old gentleman want to have it all to themselves, I think,’ whispered Miss Isabella Wardle to her sister Emily.", "\"고모하고 저 자그마한 노신사는 자기들끼리만 다 차지하고 싶은 모양이야, 내 보기엔.\" 이저벨라 와들 양이 동생 에밀리에게 속삭였다."], ["The young ladies laughed very heartily, and the old one tried to look amiable, but couldn’t manage it.", "젊은 숙녀들은 아주 호탕하게 웃었고, 나이 든 쪽은 상냥하게 보이려 애썼으나 그러지 못했다."]], [["‘Young girls have such spirits,’ said Miss Wardle to Mr.", "\"젊은 처녀들은 원기가 어찌나 넘치는지.\""], ["Tupman, with an air of gentle commiseration, as if animal spirits were contraband, and their possession without a permit a high crime and misdemeanour.", "와들 양이 부드러운 동정의 기색을 띠고 터프먼 씨에게 말했으니, 마치 혈기란 금지된 밀수품이고 허가 없이 그것을 지니는 일은 중대한 범죄이자 비행이라도 되는 듯했다."]], [["‘Oh, they have,’ replied Mr.", "\"오, 그렇지요.\""], ["Tupman, not exactly making the sort of reply that was expected from him.", "터프먼 씨가 자기에게 기대된 종류의 대답을 정확히 하지는 못한 채 응수했다."], ["‘It’s quite delightful.’", "\"참으로 즐거운 일이지요.\""]], [["‘Hem!’ said Miss Wardle, rather dubiously.", "\"흠!\" 와들 양이 다소 미심쩍은 듯 말했다."]], [["‘Will you permit me?’ said Mr.", "\"제가 좀 따라 드려도 될까요?\""], ["Tupman, in his blandest manner, touching the enchanting Rachael’s wrist with one hand, and gently elevating the bottle with the other.", "터프먼 씨가 더없이 부드러운 태도로, 한 손으로 매혹적인 레이철의 손목을 건드리고 다른 손으로는 살며시 병을 들어 올리며 말했다."], ["‘Will you permit me?’", "\"허락해 주시겠습니까?\""]], [["‘Oh, sir!’ Mr.", "\"어머나, 선생!\""], ["Tupman looked most impressive; and Rachael expressed her fear that more guns were going off, in which case, of course, she should have required support again.", "터프먼 씨는 더없이 인상적인 표정을 지었고, 레이철은 혹시 대포가 더 발사되는 게 아닐까 두렵다는 마음을 내비쳤으니, 그렇게 되면 당연히 그녀는 다시 부축을 받아야 할 터였다."]], [["‘Do you think my dear nieces pretty?’ whispered their affectionate aunt to Mr.", "\"내 사랑스러운 조카들이 예쁘다고 생각하세요?\""], ["Tupman.", "그 다정한 고모가 터프먼 씨에게 속삭였다."]], [["‘I should, if their aunt wasn’t here,’ replied the ready Pickwickian, with a passionate glance.", "\"그렇게 생각하겠지요, 그 애들의 고모님께서 여기 안 계신다면 말입니다.\" 발 빠른 픽윅 회원이 정열적인 눈길을 던지며 대답했다."]], [["‘Oh, you naughty man--but really, if their complexions were a little better, don’t you think they would be nice-looking girls--by candlelight?’", "\"어머, 짓궂으셔라—하지만 정말이지, 저 애들 안색이 조금만 더 좋다면, 촛불 아래서는—제법 예쁜 처녀들이라고 생각하지 않으세요?\""]], [["‘Yes; I think they would,’ said Mr.", "\"그렇지요. 그러리라 생각합니다.\""], ["Tupman, with an air of indifference.", "터프먼 씨가 무심한 태도로 말했다."]], [["‘Oh, you quiz--I know what you were going to say.’", "\"어머, 짓궂은 양반—무슨 말씀을 하려던 건지 다 알아요.\""]], [["‘What?’ inquired Mr.", "\"무엇을요?\""], ["Tupman, who had not precisely made up his mind to say anything at all.", "터프먼 씨가 물었는데, 그는 사실 무슨 말이든 할 작정을 정확히 정해 둔 것은 아니었다."]], [["‘You were going to say that Isabel stoops--I know you were--you men are such observers.", "\"이저벨이 구부정하다고 말씀하시려던 거죠—다 알아요—당신네 남자들은 어찌나 관찰력이 좋은지. 그래요, 그 애가 그렇긴 해요."], ["Well, so she does; it can’t be denied; and, certainly, if there is one thing more than another that makes a girl look ugly it is stooping.", "부정할 수 없죠. 그리고 확실히, 처녀를 못나 보이게 하는 게 무엇보다 하나 있다면 그건 바로 구부정한 자세예요."], ["I often tell her that when she gets a little older she’ll be quite frightful.", "저는 그 애한테 자주 말하지요, 좀 더 나이가 들면 아주 흉해 보일 거라고요."], ["Well, you are a quiz!’", "어머, 정말 짓궂은 양반이셔!\""]], [["Mr.", "터프먼 씨는 그토록 싼값에 그런 평판을 얻는 데 이의가 없었다."], ["Tupman had no objection to earning the reputation at so cheap a rate: so he looked very knowing, and smiled mysteriously.", "그래서 그는 무척이나 사정을 다 안다는 듯한 표정을 짓고는 알 수 없게 미소 지었다."]], [["‘What a sarcastic smile,’ said the admiring Rachael; ‘I declare I’m quite afraid of you.’", "\"어쩌면 그렇게 비꼬는 미소를 짓는지.\" 흠모에 찬 레이철이 말했다. \"정말이지 당신이 두려울 지경이에요.\""]], [["‘Afraid of me!’", "\"제가 두렵다고요!\""]], [["‘Oh, you can’t disguise anything from me--I know what that smile means very well.’", "\"오, 당신은 제게 아무것도 감추지 못해요—저 미소가 무슨 뜻인지 아주 잘 알거든요.\""]], [["‘What?’ said Mr.", "\"무슨 뜻인데요?\""], ["Tupman, who had not the slightest notion himself.", "터프먼 씨가 말했는데, 정작 그 자신은 조금도 짐작이 가지 않았다."]], [["‘You mean,’ said the amiable aunt, sinking her voice still lower--‘you mean, that you don’t think Isabella’s stooping is as bad as Emily’s boldness.", "\"당신 뜻은요.\" 상냥한 고모가 목소리를 한층 더 낮추며 말했다."], ["Well, she is bold!", "\"이저벨라의 구부정함이 에밀리의 그 대담함만큼 나쁘진 않다는 거죠. 글쎄, 그 애는 정말 대담하다니까요!"], ["You cannot think how wretched it makes me sometimes--I’m sure I cry about it for hours together--my dear brother is so good, and so unsuspicious, that he never sees it; if he did, I’m quite certain it would break his heart.", "그게 가끔 저를 얼마나 비참하게 만드는지 당신은 모르실 거예요—정말 몇 시간이고 그 일로 울곤 한답니다—내 사랑하는 오라버니는 너무도 선량하고 의심할 줄을 몰라서, 그걸 도무지 보지 못하시지요. 만약 보셨더라면, 분명코 가슴이 찢어지셨을 거예요."], ["I wish I could think it was only manner--I hope it may be--’ (Here the affectionate relative heaved a deep sigh, and shook her head despondingly).", "그게 그저 태도일 뿐이라고 생각할 수 있다면 좋으련만—제발 그렇기를 바라요—\" (여기서 그 다정한 친척은 깊은 한숨을 내쉬며 낙담한 듯 고개를 가로저었다.)"]], [["‘I’m sure aunt’s talking about us,’ whispered Miss Emily Wardle to her sister--‘I’m quite certain of it--she looks so malicious.’", "\"고모가 분명 우리 얘기를 하고 있어.\" 에밀리 와들 양이 언니에게 속삭였다. \"틀림없어—저렇게 심술궂은 표정을 짓는 걸 보면.\""]], [["‘Is she?’ replied Isabella.--‘Hem!", "\"그럴까?\" 이저벨라가 대답했다."], ["aunt, dear!’", "\"흠! 고모, 고모도!\""]], [["‘Yes, my dear love!’", "\"왜 그러니, 내 사랑?\""]], [["‘I’m so afraid you’ll catch cold, aunt--have a silk handkerchief to tie round your dear old head--you really should take care of yourself-- consider your age!’", "\"고모가 감기 드실까 봐 너무 걱정돼요—비단 손수건을 그 사랑스러운 늙은 머리에 둘러매세요—정말로 몸조심하셔야 해요—나이를 생각하셔야죠!\""]], [["However well deserved this piece of retaliation might have been, it was as vindictive a one as could well have been resorted to.", "이 보복이 아무리 받아 마땅한 것이었다 해도, 그것은 동원할 수 있는 가장 앙심에 찬 보복이었다."], ["There is no guessing in what form of reply the aunt’s indignation would have vented itself, had not Mr. Wardle unconsciously changed the subject, by calling emphatically for Joe.", "와들 씨가 무심코 조를 힘주어 부르는 바람에 화제를 바꾸지 않았더라면, 고모의 분노가 어떤 형태의 대꾸로 터져 나왔을지는 짐작도 할 수 없는 일이었다."]], [["‘Damn that boy,’ said the old gentleman, ‘he’s gone to sleep again.’", "\"저 녀석 망할.\" 노신사가 말했다. \"또 잠들어 버렸군.\""]], [["‘Very extraordinary boy, that,’ said Mr.", "\"참 별난 소년이로군요.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick; ‘does he always sleep in this way?’", "\"저 애는 늘 저렇게 자나요?\""]], [["‘Sleep!’ said the old gentleman, ‘he’s always asleep.", "\"잔다고요!\" 노신사가 말했다."], ["Goes on errands fast asleep, and snores as he waits at table.’", "\"저 녀석은 늘 잠들어 있지요. 심부름도 곤히 잠든 채 다니고, 식탁에서 시중들면서 코를 곤다니까요.\""]], [["‘How very odd!’ said Mr.", "\"참으로 이상하군요!\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Ah!", "\"아!"], ["odd indeed,’ returned the old gentleman; ‘I’m proud of that boy-- wouldn’t part with him on any account--he’s a natural curiosity!", "이상하다마다요.\" 노신사가 응수했다."], ["Here, Joe--Joe--take these things away, and open another bottle--d’ye hear?’", "\"나는 저 녀석이 자랑스럽소—무슨 일이 있어도 저 녀석을 내놓지 않을 거요—저건 자연이 만든 진귀한 물건이지요! 자, 조—조—이것들 좀 치우고, 또 한 병 따라—알겠느냐?\""]], [["The fat boy rose, opened his eyes, swallowed the huge piece of pie he had been in the act of masticating when he last fell asleep, and slowly obeyed his master’s orders--gloating languidly over the remains of the feast, as he removed the plates, and deposited them in the hamper.", "뚱뚱한 소년은 일어나서 눈을 뜨고, 지난번 잠들었을 때 마침 씹고 있던 거대한 파이 조각을 꿀꺽 삼키고는, 천천히 주인의 명령에 따랐다—접시들을 치워 바구니에 담으면서, 잔치의 남은 음식 위로 나른하게 게걸스러운 눈길을 흘리면서."], ["The fresh bottle was produced, and speedily emptied: the hamper was made fast in its old place--the fat boy once more mounted the box--the spectacles and pocket-glass were again adjusted--and the evolutions of the military recommenced.", "새 병이 나와 순식간에 비워졌고, 바구니는 원래 자리에 단단히 묶였으며, 뚱뚱한 소년은 다시 한번 마부석에 올랐고, 안경과 휴대용 안경이 다시 고쳐 쓰였으며, 군대의 기동이 다시 시작되었다."], ["There was a great fizzing and banging of guns, and starting of ladies--and then a mine was sprung, to the gratification of everybody--and when the mine had gone off, the military and the company followed its example, and went off too.", "대포가 요란하게 쉬익거리며 쾅쾅 터졌고, 부인들이 화들짝 놀랐다—그러고 나서 모두를 흡족하게 하며 지뢰가 터졌고—지뢰가 터지고 나자 군대와 일행도 그 본을 따라, 자리를 떴다."]], [["‘Now, mind,’ said the old gentleman, as he shook hands with Mr.", "\"자, 잊지 마시오.\""], ["Pickwick at the conclusion of a conversation which had been carried on at intervals, during the conclusion of the proceedings, ‘we shall see you all to-morrow.’", "노신사가, 행사가 끝나 가는 동안 틈틈이 이어 온 대화의 마무리에서 픽윅 씨와 악수를 나누며 말했다. \"내일 여러분 모두를 뵙기로 한 거요.\""]], [["‘Most certainly,’ replied Mr.", "\"그야 물론이지요.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘You have got the address?’", "\"주소는 받아 두셨소?\""]], [["‘Manor Farm, Dingley Dell,’ said Mr.", "\"매너 농장, 딩글리 델.\""], ["Pickwick, consulting his pocket- book.", "픽윅 씨가 자기 수첩을 들여다보며 말했다."]], [["‘That’s it,’ said the old gentleman.", "\"바로 그거요.\" 노신사가 말했다."], ["‘I don’t let you off, mind, under a week; and undertake that you shall see everything worth seeing.", "\"잊지 마시오, 일주일은 안 채우면 못 보내드리오. 그리고 볼 만한 것은 죄다 보여 드리겠다고 약속하지요."], ["If you’ve come down for a country life, come to me, and I’ll give you plenty of it.", "시골 생활을 맛보러 내려오신 거라면, 내게로 오시오, 내 그걸 실컷 맛보게 해 드리리다."], ["Joe--damn that boy, he’s gone to sleep again--Joe, help Tom put in the horses.’", "조—저 녀석 망할, 또 잠들어 버렸군—조, 톰이 말 매는 것 좀 도와라.\""]], [["The horses were put in--the driver mounted--the fat boy clambered up by his side--farewells were exchanged--and the carriage rattled off.", "말들이 매여졌고—마부가 올라탔고—뚱뚱한 소년이 그 옆으로 기어 올라갔고—작별 인사가 오갔고—마차가 덜커덩거리며 떠나갔다."], ["As the Pickwickians turned round to take a last glimpse of it, the setting sun cast a rich glow on the faces of their entertainers, and fell upon the form of the fat boy.", "픽윅 회원들이 마차를 마지막으로 한 번 더 보려고 돌아섰을 때, 지는 해가 그들을 환대해 준 이들의 얼굴에 풍요로운 노을빛을 드리웠고, 그 빛은 뚱뚱한 소년의 모습 위로도 떨어졌다."], ["His head was sunk upon his bosom; and he slumbered again.", "그의 머리는 가슴팍에 푹 파묻혀 있었으니, 그는 또다시 잠들어 있었다."]], [["CHAPTER V. A SHORT ONE--SHOWING, AMONG OTHER MATTERS, HOW Mr.", "하늘은 밝고 쾌청했으며, 공기는 향긋했고, 주위의 모든 사물은 아름다운 모습을 띠고 있었다."], ["PICKWICK UNDERTOOK TO DRIVE, AND MR. WINKLE TO RIDE, AND HOW THEY BOTH DID IT\nBright and pleasant was the sky, balmy the air, and beautiful the appearance of every object around, as Mr.", "픽윅 씨는 로체스터 다리의 난간에 몸을 기댄 채 자연을 바라보며 아침 식사를 기다리고 있었다."], ["Pickwick leaned over the balustrades of Rochester Bridge, contemplating nature, and waiting for breakfast. The scene was indeed one which might well have charmed a far less reflective mind, than that to which it was presented.", "그 광경은 실로 그것을 바라보는 이보다 훨씬 사색에 덜 잠긴 마음이라도 능히 매료시킬 만한 것이었다."]], [["On the left of the spectator lay the ruined wall, broken in many places, and in some, overhanging the narrow beach below in rude and heavy masses.", "바라보는 이의 왼편에는 여러 군데가 무너진 폐허의 성벽이 놓여 있었고, 어떤 곳에서는 거칠고 육중한 덩어리째 아래쪽의 좁은 모래톱 위로 비스듬히 드리워져 있었다."], ["Huge knots of seaweed hung upon the jagged and pointed stones, trembling in every breath of wind; and the green ivy clung mournfully round the dark and ruined battlements.", "들쭉날쭉 뾰족한 돌들 위에는 거대한 해초 무더기가 매달려 바람이 불 때마다 떨었고, 푸른 담쟁이덩굴은 어둡고 황폐한 흉벽 둘레를 구슬프게 휘감고 있었다."], ["Behind it rose the ancient castle, its towers roofless, and its massive walls crumbling away, but telling us proudly of its old might and strength, as when, seven hundred years ago, it rang with the clash of arms, or resounded with the noise of feasting and revelry.", "그 뒤로는 고색창연한 성이 솟아 있었으니, 탑들은 지붕이 사라졌고 육중한 벽은 허물어져 가고 있었으나, 칠백 년 전 무기 부딪치는 소리로 울리고 잔치와 흥청거림의 소음으로 메아리치던 그 옛 위세와 힘을 자랑스레 우리에게 들려주고 있었다."], ["On either side, the banks of the Medway, covered with cornfields and pastures, with here and there a windmill, or a distant church, stretched away as far as the eye could see, presenting a rich and varied landscape, rendered more beautiful by the changing shadows which passed swiftly across it as the thin and half-formed clouds skimmed away in the light of the morning sun.", "양편으로는 옥수수밭과 목초지로 뒤덮인 메드웨이 강의 강둑이 군데군데 풍차나 멀리 교회를 곁들인 채 눈길이 닿는 데까지 뻗어 있었고, 풍요롭고 변화무쌍한 풍경을 펼쳐 보였으니, 그것은 아침 햇살 속에 얇고 채 여물지 않은 구름들이 스쳐 가며 그 위로 재빠르게 지나가는 변화하는 그림자들로 한층 더 아름다워졌다."], ["The river, reflecting the clear blue of the sky, glistened and sparkled as it flowed noiselessly on; and the oars of the fishermen dipped into the water with a clear and liquid sound, as their heavy but picturesque boats glided slowly down the stream.", "강물은 맑고 푸른 하늘빛을 비추며 소리 없이 흐르는 동안 반짝이고 빛났고, 어부들의 노는 맑고 투명한 소리를 내며 물속에 잠겼으니, 그들의 묵직하면서도 그림 같은 배들은 천천히 강을 따라 미끄러져 내려가고 있었다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick was roused from the agreeable reverie into which he had been led by the objects before him, by a deep sigh, and a touch on his shoulder.", "픽윅 씨는 눈앞의 사물들이 이끌어 낸 그 흐뭇한 몽상에서, 깊은 한숨 소리와 어깨를 두드리는 손길에 정신이 들었다."], ["He turned round: and the dismal man was at his side.", "그는 돌아보았다. 그 침울한 사내가 그의 곁에 서 있었다."]], [["‘Contemplating the scene?’ inquired the dismal man.", "\"경치를 감상하시는 중이오?\" 침울한 사내가 물었다."]], [["‘I was,’ said Mr.", "\"그렇소.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘And congratulating yourself on being up so soon?’", "\"그리고 이렇게 일찍 일어난 걸 스스로 축하하고 계셨겠지요?\""]], [["Mr. Pickwick nodded assent.", "픽윅 씨는 그렇다는 듯 고개를 끄덕였다."]], [["‘Ah!", "\"아!"], ["people need to rise early, to see the sun in all his splendour, for his brightness seldom lasts the day through.", "사람들은 해가 온전한 광휘를 떨치는 모습을 보려면 일찍 일어나야 하지요. 그 밝음은 하루 종일 가는 일이 드무니까요."], ["The morning of day and the morning of life are but too much alike.’", "하루의 아침과 인생의 아침은 너무도 닮았소이다.\""]], [["‘You speak truly, sir,’ said Mr.", "\"옳은 말씀이오.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘How common the saying,’ continued the dismal man, ‘“The morning’s too fine to last.”", "\"얼마나 흔한 말이오.\" 침울한 사내가 이어 말했다."], ["How well might it be applied to our everyday existence.", "\"'아침이 너무 화창해서 오래갈 리 없다'는 말 말이오. 그것을 우리네 나날의 삶에 얼마나 잘 들어맞게 쓸 수 있겠소."], ["God!", "하느님!"], ["what would I forfeit to have the days of my childhood restored, or to be able to forget them for ever!’", "어린 시절을 되돌릴 수만 있다면, 아니 그 시절을 영영 잊을 수만 있다면 나는 무엇인들 내놓지 못하겠소!\""]], [["‘You have seen much trouble, sir,’ said Mr.", "\"많은 고초를 겪으셨군요, 선생.\""], ["Pickwick compassionately.", "픽윅 씨가 측은한 마음으로 말했다."]], [["‘I have,’ said the dismal man hurriedly; ‘I have. More than those who see me now would believe possible.’ He paused for an instant, and then said abruptly--", "\"그렇소.\" 침울한 사내가 황급히 말했다. \"그렇소. 지금 나를 보는 이들이 가능하다고 믿을 수 있는 것보다 훨씬 더 많이.\" 그는 잠시 말을 멈추었다가, 이윽고 불쑥 내뱉었다."]], [["‘Did it ever strike you, on such a morning as this, that drowning would be happiness and peace?’", "\"이런 아침에, 물에 빠져 죽는 것이 곧 행복이요 평화이리라는 생각이 든 적이 있소?\""]], [["‘God bless me, no!’ replied Mr.", "\"맙소사, 천만에!\""], ["Pickwick, edging a little from the balustrade, as the possibility of the dismal man’s tipping him over, by way of experiment, occurred to him rather forcibly.", "픽윅 씨가 대답하며 난간에서 조금 비켜섰으니, 침울한 사내가 실험 삼아 자기를 떠밀어 떨어뜨릴 수도 있겠다는 가능성이 자못 강하게 떠올랐던 것이다."]], [["‘I have thought so, often,’ said the dismal man, without noticing the action. ‘The calm, cool water seems to me to murmur an invitation to repose and rest. A bound, a splash, a brief struggle; there is an eddy for an instant, it gradually subsides into a gentle ripple; the waters have closed above your head, and the world has closed upon your miseries and misfortunes for ever.’ The sunken eye of the dismal man flashed brightly as he spoke, but the momentary excitement quickly subsided; and he turned calmly away, as he said--", "\"나는 종종 그런 생각을 했소.\" 침울한 사내가 그 동작을 알아채지 못한 채 말했다. \"고요하고 차가운 물이 내게는 안식과 휴식으로의 초대를 속삭이는 것만 같았소. 한번 뛰어들고, 첨벙 소리가 나고, 잠깐 발버둥치면, 한순간 소용돌이가 일다가 차츰 잔잔한 물결로 가라앉지요. 물은 당신의 머리 위로 닫히고, 세상은 당신의 고통과 불운 위로 영영 닫히는 것이오.\" 침울한 사내의 움푹 꺼진 눈은 그가 말하는 동안 환히 번득였으나, 그 순간의 흥분은 이내 가라앉았다. 그러더니 그는 차분하게 몸을 돌리며 말했다."]], [["‘There--enough of that.", "\"자, 그 이야기는 그만하면 됐소."], ["I wish to see you on another subject.", "다른 일로 당신을 보고 싶었던 거요."], ["You invited me to read that paper, the night before last, and listened attentively while I did so.’", "당신은 그제 밤에 내게 그 원고를 읽어 보라고 청했고, 내가 읽는 동안 주의 깊게 들어 주었소.\""]], [["‘I did,’ replied Mr.", "\"그랬지요.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다."], ["Pickwick; ‘and I certainly thought--’", "\"그리고 분명히 이런 생각이 들었는데—\""]], [["‘I asked for no opinion,’ said the dismal man, interrupting him, ‘and I want none.", "\"나는 의견을 청하지 않았소.\" 침울한 사내가 그의 말을 가로막으며 말했다."], ["You are travelling for amusement and instruction.", "\"그리고 의견 따위는 바라지도 않소."], ["Suppose I forward you a curious manuscript--observe, not curious because wild or improbable, but curious as a leaf from the romance of real life--would you communicate it to the club, of which you have spoken so frequently?’", "당신은 즐거움과 배움을 위해 여행하고 있소. 만일 내가 당신에게 어떤 진기한 원고를 보낸다고 칩시다—잘 들으시오, 황당하거나 있을 법하지 않아서 진기한 게 아니라, 실제 삶의 로맨스에서 떼어 낸 한 장이기에 진기한 것이오—그것을 당신이 그토록 자주 입에 올린 그 클럽에 전해 주겠소?\""]], [["‘Certainly,’ replied Mr.", "\"물론이오.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다."], ["Pickwick, ‘if you wished it; and it would be entered on their transactions.’", "\"당신이 원하신다면 말이오. 그리고 그것은 클럽의 회보에 기록될 것이오.\""]], [["‘You shall have it,’ replied the dismal man.", "\"그것을 받게 될 거요.\" 침울한 사내가 대답했다."], ["‘Your address;’ and, Mr.", "\"당신 주소를 말해 주시오.\""], ["Pickwick having communicated their probable route, the dismal man carefully noted it down in a greasy pocket-book, and, resisting Mr. Pickwick’s pressing invitation to breakfast, left that gentleman at his inn, and walked slowly away.", "그리하여 픽윅 씨가 그들의 예정된 노정을 일러 주자, 침울한 사내는 기름때 묻은 수첩에 그것을 꼼꼼히 적어 두고는, 함께 아침 식사를 하자는 픽윅 씨의 간곡한 권유를 뿌리치고 그를 여관에 남겨 둔 채 천천히 걸어가 버렸다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick found that his three companions had risen, and were waiting his arrival to commence breakfast, which was ready laid in tempting display.", "픽윅 씨는 세 동행이 이미 일어나 그가 오기를 기다리며 아침 식사를 시작하려는 참임을 알았으니, 식사는 먹음직스럽게 차려져 있었다."], ["They sat down to the meal; and broiled ham, eggs, tea, coffee and sundries, began to disappear with a rapidity which at once bore testimony to the excellence of the fare, and the appetites of its consumers.", "그들은 식탁에 둘러앉았고, 구운 햄과 달걀, 차, 커피, 그 밖의 여러 가지가 빠른 속도로 사라지기 시작했으니, 그것은 음식의 훌륭함과 먹는 이들의 식욕을 동시에 증언하는 것이었다."]], [["‘Now, about Manor Farm,’ said Mr.", "\"자, 매너 농장 말인데.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick. ‘How shall we go?’", "\"어떻게 가는 게 좋겠소?\""]], [["‘We had better consult the waiter, perhaps,’ said Mr.", "\"아마 종업원에게 물어보는 게 낫겠소.\""], ["Tupman; and the waiter was summoned accordingly.", "터프먼 씨가 말했다. 그래서 종업원이 불려 왔다."]], [["‘Dingley Dell, gentlemen--fifteen miles, gentlemen--cross road--post- chaise, sir?’", "\"딩글리 델 말씀이군요, 여러분—십오 마일입니다, 여러분—샛길이지요—역마차로 하시겠습니까, 손님?\""]], [["‘Post-chaise won’t hold more than two,’ said Mr.", "\"역마차는 둘밖에 못 타지 않소.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘True, sir--beg your pardon, sir.--Very nice four-wheel chaise, sir-- seat for two behind--one in front for the gentleman that drives--oh!", "\"맞습니다, 손님—실례했습니다, 손님.—아주 멋진 사륜 경마차가 있습니다, 손님—뒤에 두 분 자리가 있고요—앞에 모는 분 자리가 하나 있습죠—아!"], ["beg your pardon, sir--that’ll only hold three.’", "죄송합니다, 손님—그건 세 분밖에 못 탑니다.\""]], [["‘What’s to be done?’ said Mr.", "\"그럼 어쩐다?\""], ["Snodgrass.", "스노드그래스 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Perhaps one of the gentlemen would like to ride, sir?’ suggested the waiter, looking towards Mr.", "\"혹시 한 분이 말을 타고 싶으실지요, 손님?\" 종업원이 윈클 씨를 바라보며 제안했다."], ["Winkle; ‘very good saddle-horses, sir--any of Mr. Wardle’s men coming to Rochester, bring ‘em back, Sir.’", "\"아주 좋은 승용마가 있습니다, 손님—워들 씨 댁 일꾼 누구든 로체스터에 오는 길에 데려가시면 되돌려 보내 주실 겁니다, 손님.\""]], [["‘The very thing,’ said Mr.", "\"바로 그거요.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick. ‘Winkle, will you go on horseback?’", "\"윈클, 말을 타고 가시겠소?\""]], [["Now Mr.", "그런데 윈클 씨는 자기 마음 가장 깊은 구석에서 자신의 승마 솜씨에 관해 상당한 불안을 품고 있었다."], ["Winkle did entertain considerable misgivings in the very lowest recesses of his own heart, relative to his equestrian skill; but, as he would not have them even suspected, on any account, he at once replied with great hardihood, ‘Certainly.", "그러나 그것을 무슨 일이 있어도 남이 눈치채게 할 수는 없었기에, 그는 곧장 대단히 대담하게 대답했다. \"물론이오."], ["I should enjoy it of all things.’", "무엇보다도 그게 좋겠소.\""]], [["Mr.", "윈클 씨는 자기 운명을 향해 돌진하고 있었다."], ["Winkle had rushed upon his fate; there was no resource.", "달리 길이 없었다."]], [["‘Let them be at the door by eleven,’ said Mr.", "\"열한 시까지 문 앞에 대 주시오.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Very well, sir,’ replied the waiter.", "\"알겠습니다, 손님.\" 종업원이 대답했다."]], [["The waiter retired; the breakfast concluded; and the travellers ascended to their respective bedrooms, to prepare a change of clothing, to take with them on their approaching expedition.", "종업원은 물러갔고, 아침 식사는 끝났다. 여행자들은 다가오는 원정에 가지고 갈 갈아입을 옷을 챙기러 각자의 침실로 올라갔다."]], [["Mr.", "픽윅 씨는 사전 준비를 마치고 커피실의 차양 너머로 거리의 행인들을 내다보고 있었는데, 그때 종업원이 들어와 마차가 준비되었다고 알렸다."], ["Pickwick had made his preliminary arrangements, and was looking over the coffee-room blinds at the passengers in the street, when the waiter entered, and announced that the chaise was ready--an announcement which the vehicle itself confirmed, by forthwith appearing before the coffee- room blinds aforesaid.", "이 알림은 곧 앞서 말한 커피실 차양 앞에 마차 자체가 나타남으로써 확인되었다."]], [["It was a curious little green box on four wheels, with a low place like a wine-bin for two behind, and an elevated perch for one in front, drawn by an immense brown horse, displaying great symmetry of bone.", "그것은 네 바퀴 달린 진기한 작은 초록색 상자 같은 것이었으니, 뒤에는 두 사람이 탈 술통 보관함 같은 낮은 자리가 있고, 앞에는 한 사람이 앉을 높이 솟은 자리가 있었으며, 뼈대가 매우 균형 잡힌 거대한 갈색 말이 끌고 있었다."], ["An hostler stood near, holding by the bridle another immense horse--apparently a near relative of the animal in the chaise--ready saddled for Mr. Winkle.", "마부 하나가 곁에 서서 또 한 마리의 거대한 말—마차에 매인 짐승과 가까운 친척인 듯한—의 고삐를 쥐고 있었는데, 그 말은 윈클 씨를 위해 안장을 얹은 채 준비되어 있었다."]], [["‘Bless my soul!’ said Mr.", "\"이거 야단났군!\""], ["Pickwick, as they stood upon the pavement while the coats were being put in.", "외투들을 싣는 동안 보도에 서 있던 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"야단났어!"], ["‘Bless my soul!", "누가 마차를 몬담?"], ["who’s to drive? I never thought of that.’", "그건 미처 생각도 못 했는데.\""]], [["‘Oh!", "\"아!"], ["you, of course,’ said Mr.", "당연히 당신이지요.\""], ["Tupman.", "터프먼 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Of course,’ said Mr.", "\"당연하지요.\""], ["Snodgrass.", "스노드그래스 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘I!’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"내가!\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 소리쳤다."]], [["‘Not the slightest fear, Sir,’ interposed the hostler.", "\"전혀 걱정 마십시오, 손님.\" 마부가 끼어들었다."], ["‘Warrant him quiet, Sir; a hinfant in arms might drive him.’", "\"이놈은 얌전하기로 보장합니다, 손님. 갓난애도 몰 수 있을 정도입죠.\""]], [["‘He don’t shy, does he?’ inquired Mr.", "\"놀라서 날뛰거나 하진 않겠지, 그렇지?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘Shy, sir?-he wouldn’t shy if he was to meet a vagin-load of monkeys with their tails burned off.’", "\"날뛴다고요, 손님?—꼬리가 다 타 버린 원숭이를 한 수레 가득 실은 짐마차와 마주친대도 이놈은 꿈쩍도 안 할 겁니다.\""]], [["The last recommendation was indisputable. Mr. Tupman and Mr. Snodgrass got into the bin; Mr. Pickwick ascended to his perch, and deposited his feet on a floor-clothed shelf, erected beneath it for that purpose.", "이 마지막 보증은 반박의 여지가 없었다. 터프먼 씨와 스노드그래스 씨는 보관함 자리에 올라탔고, 픽윅 씨는 자기 자리로 올라가 그 밑에 그 용도로 설치된 천을 깐 발판 위에 발을 올려놓았다."]], [["‘Now, shiny Villiam,’ said the hostler to the deputy hostler, ‘give the gen’lm’n the ribbons.’", "\"자, 반들이 빌리엄.\" 마부가 부마부에게 말했다. \"이 신사분께 고삐를 드려라.\""]], [["Shiny Villiam’--so called, probably, from his sleek hair and oily countenance--placed the reins in Mr. Pickwick’s left hand; and the upper hostler thrust a whip into his right.", "'반들이 빌리엄'은—아마도 매끈한 머리카락과 기름진 얼굴 때문에 그렇게 불렸을 텐데—고삐를 픽윅 씨의 왼손에 쥐여 주었고, 상급 마부는 채찍을 그의 오른손에 찔러 넣었다."]], [["‘Wo-o!’ cried Mr.", "\"워어!\""], ["Pickwick, as the tall quadruped evinced a decided inclination to back into the coffee-room window.", "키 큰 네발짐승이 커피실 창문 쪽으로 뒷걸음질 치려는 뚜렷한 기색을 보이자 픽윅 씨가 소리쳤다."]], [["‘Wo-o!’ echoed Mr.", "\"워어!\""], ["Tupman and Mr. Snodgrass, from the bin.", "보관함 자리에서 터프먼 씨와 스노드그래스 씨가 따라 외쳤다."]], [["‘Only his playfulness, gen’lm’n,’ said the head hostler encouragingly; ‘jist kitch hold on him, Villiam.’ The deputy restrained the animal’s impetuosity, and the principal ran to assist Mr.", "\"그저 장난기일 뿐입니다, 손님.\" 우두머리 마부가 격려하듯 말했다."], ["Winkle in mounting.", "\"그냥 붙잡아라, 빌리엄.\" 부마부가 짐승의 거센 기세를 억눌렀고, 우두머리는 윈클 씨가 말에 오르는 것을 거들려고 달려갔다."]], [["‘T’other side, sir, if you please.’", "\"반대편입니다, 손님, 죄송하지만요.\""]], [["‘Blowed if the gen’lm’n worn’t a-gettin’ up on the wrong side,’ whispered a grinning post-boy to the inexpressibly gratified waiter.", "\"저런, 저 신사분이 엉뚱한 쪽으로 올라타려고 하잖아.\" 히죽거리는 역마차 마부 소년이 이루 말할 수 없이 흡족해하는 종업원에게 속삭였다."]], [["Mr. Winkle, thus instructed, climbed into his saddle, with about as much difficulty as he would have experienced in getting up the side of a first-rate man-of-war.", "이렇게 안내받은 윈클 씨는 마치 일급 군함의 뱃전을 기어오르듯 거의 그만큼이나 애를 먹으며 안장 위로 기어올랐다."]], [["‘All right?’ inquired Mr.", "\"준비됐소?\""], ["Pickwick, with an inward presentiment that it was all wrong.", "픽윅 씨가 속으로는 모든 게 다 잘못됐다는 예감을 품은 채 물었다."]], [["‘All right,’ replied Mr.", "\"준비됐소.\""], ["Winkle faintly.", "윈클 씨가 힘없이 대답했다."]], [["‘Let ‘em go,’ cried the hostler.--‘Hold him in, sir;’ and away went the chaise, and the saddle-horse, with Mr.", "\"출발하시죠.\" 마부가 외쳤다.—\"단단히 잡으십시오, 손님.\""], ["Pickwick on the box of the one, and Mr. Winkle on the back of the other, to the delight and gratification of the whole inn-yard.", "그러자 마차와 승용마는 출발했으니, 한쪽 마차석에는 픽윅 씨가, 다른 한쪽 말 등에는 윈클 씨가 탄 채였고, 여관 마당 전체가 기뻐하고 즐거워했다."]], [["‘What makes him go sideways?’ said Mr.", "\"왜 저놈이 옆으로 가는 거요?\""], ["Snodgrass in the bin, to Mr. Winkle in the saddle.", "보관함 자리의 스노드그래스 씨가 안장 위의 윈클 씨에게 말했다."]], [["‘I can’t imagine,’ replied Mr.", "\"나도 모르겠소.\" 윈클 씨가 대답했다."], ["Winkle. His horse was drifting up the street in the most mysterious manner--side first, with his head towards one side of the way, and his tail towards the other.", "그의 말은 더없이 알 수 없는 모양으로 거리를 따라 옆으로 떠밀려 가고 있었으니—옆구리를 앞세우고, 머리는 길 한쪽을 향하고 꼬리는 다른 쪽을 향한 채였다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick had no leisure to observe either this or any other particular, the whole of his faculties being concentrated in the management of the animal attached to the chaise, who displayed various peculiarities, highly interesting to a bystander, but by no means equally amusing to any one seated behind him.", "픽윅 씨에게는 이것이며 다른 어떤 것을 살필 여유가 없었으니, 그의 온 정신이 마차에 매인 짐승을 다루는 데 집중되어 있었던 까닭이다."], ["Besides constantly jerking his head up, in a very unpleasant and uncomfortable manner, and tugging at the reins to an extent which rendered it a matter of great difficulty for Mr.", "그 짐승은 갖가지 기묘한 짓을 보였는데, 구경꾼에게는 자못 흥미로웠으나 그 뒤에 앉은 누구에게도 그만큼 즐겁지는 결코 않았다."], ["Pickwick to hold them, he had a singular propensity for darting suddenly every now and then to the side of the road, then stopping short, and then rushing forward for some minutes, at a speed which it was wholly impossible to control.", "매우 불쾌하고 거북한 모양새로 끊임없이 머리를 홱홱 쳐들고, 픽윅 씨가 고삐를 쥐고 있기가 몹시 어려울 만큼 고삐를 잡아당기는 것에 더하여, 이놈은 이따금 느닷없이 길가로 휙 내달았다가는 우뚝 멈춰 서고, 그러고는 도무지 제어할 길 없는 속도로 몇 분간 앞으로 돌진하는 별난 버릇이 있었다."]], [["‘What can he mean by this?’ said Mr.", "\"대체 이게 무슨 짓일까?\""], ["Snodgrass, when the horse had executed this manoeuvre for the twentieth time.", "말이 이런 동작을 스무 번째로 해 보였을 때 스노드그래스 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘I don’t know,’ replied Mr.", "\"나도 모르겠소.\" 터프먼 씨가 대답했다."], ["Tupman; ‘it looks very like shying, don’t it?’ Mr.", "\"놀라서 날뛰는 것처럼 보이긴 하는데, 안 그렇소?\""], ["Snodgrass was about to reply, when he was interrupted by a shout from Mr. Pickwick.", "스노드그래스 씨가 대답하려는데, 픽윅 씨의 외침에 말이 끊겼다."]], [["‘Woo!’ said that gentleman; ‘I have dropped my whip.’", "\"워!\" 그 신사가 말했다. \"내 채찍을 떨어뜨렸소.\""]], [["‘Winkle,’ said Mr.", "\"윈클.\""], ["Snodgrass, as the equestrian came trotting up on the tall horse, with his hat over his ears, and shaking all over, as if he would shake to pieces, with the violence of the exercise, ‘pick up the whip, there’s a good fellow.’ Mr.", "스노드그래스 씨가 말했으니, 그때 그 기수는 모자가 귀까지 내려온 채, 격렬한 운동 탓에 온몸이 산산조각 날 듯 사방으로 흔들리며 키 큰 말을 타고 종종걸음으로 다가오고 있었다. \"착한 양반, 채찍 좀 주워 주시오.\""], ["Winkle pulled at the bridle of the tall horse till he was black in the face; and having at length succeeded in stopping him, dismounted, handed the whip to Mr. Pickwick, and grasping the reins, prepared to remount.", "윈클 씨는 얼굴이 시퍼레지도록 키 큰 말의 고삐를 잡아당겼고, 마침내 말을 세우는 데 성공하자 내려서 채찍을 픽윅 씨에게 건넨 뒤, 고삐를 움켜쥐고 다시 올라탈 채비를 했다."]], [["Now whether the tall horse, in the natural playfulness of his disposition, was desirous of having a little innocent recreation with Mr. Winkle, or whether it occurred to him that he could perform the journey as much to his own satisfaction without a rider as with one, are points upon which, of course, we can arrive at no definite and distinct conclusion.", "그런데 그 키 큰 말이 천성의 장난기에서 윈클 씨와 더불어 죄 없는 오락거리를 좀 즐기고 싶었던 것인지, 아니면 기수를 태우든 안 태우든 제 마음에 흡족하게 여정을 마칠 수 있겠다는 생각이 들었던 것인지, 그것은 물론 우리로서는 명확하고 분명한 결론에 이를 수 없는 대목이다."], ["By whatever motives the animal was actuated, certain it is that Mr. Winkle had no sooner touched the reins, than he slipped them over his head, and darted backwards to their full length.", "그 짐승이 어떤 동기로 움직였든 간에, 윈클 씨가 고삐에 손을 대자마자 말이 고삐를 머리 너머로 미끄러뜨려 벗기고는 고삐가 다 풀릴 만큼 뒤로 휙 물러난 것만은 확실하다."]], [["‘Poor fellow,’ said Mr.", "\"가엾은 녀석.\""], ["Winkle soothingly--‘poor fellow--good old horse.’ The ‘poor fellow’ was proof against flattery; the more Mr.", "윈클 씨가 달래듯 말했다. \"가엾은 녀석—착한 늙은 말아.\""], ["Winkle tried to get nearer him, the more he sidled away; and, notwithstanding all kinds of coaxing and wheedling, there were Mr.", "그 '가엾은 녀석'은 아첨에도 끄떡없었다."], ["Winkle and the horse going round and round each other for ten minutes, at the end of which time each was at precisely the same distance from the other as when they first commenced--an unsatisfactory sort of thing under any circumstances, but particularly so in a lonely road, where no assistance can be procured.", "윈클 씨가 가까이 다가가려 할수록 말은 더욱 옆으로 비켜났고, 온갖 어르고 구슬리는 짓에도 불구하고, 윈클 씨와 말은 십 분 동안이나 서로의 주위를 빙빙 맴돌았으니, 그 시간이 끝났을 때 둘은 처음 시작했을 때와 정확히 같은 거리만큼 떨어져 있었다—어떤 상황에서도 만족스러운 일이라 할 수 없겠으나, 도움을 구할 데라곤 없는 외딴길에서는 특히 그러했다."]], [["‘What am I to do?’ shouted Mr.", "\"이를 어쩐다?\""], ["Winkle, after the dodging had been prolonged for a considerable time.", "그 술래잡기가 꽤 오래 이어진 끝에 윈클 씨가 소리쳤다. \"이를 어쩌면 좋단 말이오?"], ["‘What am I to do? I can’t get on him.’", "도무지 올라탈 수가 없소.\""]], [["‘You had better lead him till we come to a turnpike,’ replied Mr.", "\"통행료 징수소에 다다를 때까지 끌고 가는 게 낫겠소.\""], ["Pickwick from the chaise.", "마차에서 픽윅 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘But he won’t come!’ roared Mr.", "\"하지만 안 오려고 하는데!\" 윈클 씨가 고함쳤다."], ["Winkle. ‘Do come and hold him.’", "\"제발 와서 좀 붙잡아 주시오.\""]], [["Mr. Pickwick was the very personation of kindness and humanity: he threw the reins on the horse’s back, and having descended from his seat, carefully drew the chaise into the hedge, lest anything should come along the road, and stepped back to the assistance of his distressed companion, leaving Mr.", "픽윅 씨는 친절과 인정의 화신 그 자체였다."], ["Tupman and Mr. Snodgrass in the vehicle.", "그는 고삐를 말 등에 던져 놓고 자기 자리에서 내려와, 길로 무엇이든 지나갈까 봐 마차를 조심스레 울타리 쪽으로 끌어다 놓고는, 터프먼 씨와 스노드그래스 씨를 마차에 남겨 둔 채 곤경에 빠진 동행을 도우러 되돌아갔다."]], [["The horse no sooner beheld Mr. Pickwick advancing towards him with the chaise whip in his hand, than he exchanged the rotary motion in which he had previously indulged, for a retrograde movement of so very determined a character, that it at once drew Mr. Winkle, who was still at the end of the bridle, at a rather quicker rate than fast walking, in the direction from which they had just come. Mr. Pickwick ran to his assistance, but the faster Mr. Pickwick ran forward, the faster the horse ran backward. There was a great scraping of feet, and kicking up of the dust; and at last Mr. Winkle, his arms being nearly pulled out of their sockets, fairly let go his hold. The horse paused, stared, shook his head, turned round, and quietly trotted home to Rochester, leaving Mr. Winkle and Mr. Pickwick gazing on each other with countenances of blank dismay. A rattling noise at a little distance attracted their attention. They looked up.", "말은 픽윅 씨가 마차 채찍을 손에 들고 자기 쪽으로 다가오는 것을 보자마자, 조금 전까지 부리던 회전 운동을 그만두고 어찌나 단호한 후진 운동으로 바꾸었던지, 그 바람에 아직 고삐 끝을 쥐고 있던 윈클 씨는 그들이 막 떠나온 방향으로 빠른 걸음보다 다소 빠른 속도로 단숨에 끌려갔다. 픽윅 씨가 그를 도우러 달려갔으나, 픽윅 씨가 앞으로 빨리 달리면 달릴수록 말은 더 빨리 뒤로 달렸다. 발이 크게 긁히는 소리가 나고 먼지가 일었으며, 마침내 팔이 어깻죽지에서 거의 빠져나갈 지경이 된 윈클 씨는 결국 손을 놓아 버렸다. 말은 멈춰 서서 멍하니 바라보다가 고개를 흔들고는 돌아서서, 윈클 씨와 픽윅 씨가 망연자실한 얼굴로 서로를 바라보게 내버려 둔 채 조용히 종종걸음으로 로체스터를 향해 집으로 돌아가 버렸다. 조금 떨어진 곳에서 덜커덕거리는 소리가 그들의 주의를 끌었다. 그들은 고개를 들었다."]], [["‘Bless my soul!’ exclaimed the agonised Mr.", "\"이거 야단났군!\" 괴로워하는 픽윅 씨가 외쳤다."], ["Pickwick; ‘there’s the other horse running away!’", "\"저기 다른 말이 달아나고 있어!\""]], [["It was but too true.", "너무도 사실이었다."], ["The animal was startled by the noise, and the reins were on his back.", "그 짐승은 소리에 놀랐고, 고삐는 등 위에 던져져 있었다."], ["The results may be guessed. He tore off with the four- wheeled chaise behind him, and Mr.", "그 결과는 짐작이 갈 것이다."], ["Tupman and Mr. Snodgrass in the four- wheeled chaise.", "말은 사륜 경마차를 뒤에 매단 채, 그리고 그 사륜 경마차에 탄 터프먼 씨와 스노드그래스 씨를 매단 채 내달렸다."], ["The heat was a short one. Mr.", "경주는 짧았다."], ["Tupman threw himself into the hedge, Mr. Snodgrass followed his example, the horse dashed the four--wheeled chaise against a wooden bridge, separated the wheels from the body, and the bin from the perch; and finally stood stock still to gaze upon the ruin he had made.", "터프먼 씨가 울타리로 몸을 던졌고, 스노드그래스 씨도 그 본을 따랐으며, 말은 사륜 경마차를 나무다리에 들이받아 바퀴를 차체에서 떼어 놓고 보관함 자리를 앞자리에서 갈라놓더니, 마침내 자기가 만들어 낸 그 잔해를 바라보며 꼼짝 않고 우뚝 멈춰 섰다."]], [["The first care of the two unspilt friends was to extricate their unfortunate companions from their bed of quickset--a process which gave them the unspeakable satisfaction of discovering that they had sustained no injury, beyond sundry rents in their garments, and various lacerations from the brambles.", "엎어지지 않은 두 친구가 가장 먼저 신경 쓴 것은 불운한 동행들을 산울타리 잠자리에서 끌어내는 일이었다—이 과정에서 그들은 동행들이 옷에 난 몇 군데 찢어진 자국과 가시덤불에 긁힌 여러 상처 말고는 아무 부상도 입지 않았음을 발견하고 이루 말할 수 없는 만족을 얻었다."], ["The next thing to be done was to unharness the horse.", "다음으로 해야 할 일은 말의 마구를 푸는 것이었다."], ["This complicated process having been effected, the party walked slowly forward, leading the horse among them, and abandoning the chaise to its fate.", "이 복잡한 작업을 마친 뒤, 일행은 말을 가운데 두고 끌고서 천천히 앞으로 걸어갔으니, 마차는 그 운명에 내맡긴 채였다."]], [["An hour’s walk brought the travellers to a little road-side public- house, with two elm-trees, a horse trough, and a signpost, in front; one or two deformed hay-ricks behind, a kitchen garden at the side, and rotten sheds and mouldering outhouses jumbled in strange confusion all about it.", "한 시간을 걸어가자 여행자들은 길가의 자그마한 선술집에 다다랐다. 앞에는 느릅나무 두 그루와 말 여물통, 그리고 푯말이 있었고, 뒤에는 모양이 일그러진 건초 더미가 한두 개, 옆에는 채마밭이 있었으며, 사방에는 썩은 헛간과 허물어져 가는 부속 건물들이 기묘하게 뒤죽박죽 뒤엉켜 있었다."], ["A red-headed man was working in the garden; and to him Mr.", "빨간 머리의 사내 하나가 밭에서 일하고 있었는데, 픽윅 씨가 그를 향해 우렁차게 외쳤다."], ["Pickwick called lustily, ‘Hollo there!’", "\"여보시오, 거기!\""]], [["The red-headed man raised his body, shaded his eyes with his hand, and stared, long and coolly, at Mr. Pickwick and his companions.", "빨간 머리 사내는 몸을 일으켜 한 손으로 눈을 가리고는, 픽윅 씨와 그 일행을 오래도록 무덤덤하게 빤히 바라보았다."]], [["‘Hollo there!’ repeated Mr.", "\"여보시오, 거기!\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 다시 외쳤다."]], [["‘Hollo!’ was the red-headed man’s reply.", "\"여보쇼!\" 빨간 머리 사내의 대답이었다."]], [["‘How far is it to Dingley Dell?’", "\"딩글리 델까지 얼마나 멀지요?\""]], [["‘Better er seven mile.’", "\"칠 마일은 좋이 되지.\""]], [["‘Is it a good road?’", "\"길은 괜찮소?\""]], [["‘No, ‘tain’t.’ Having uttered this brief reply, and apparently satisfied himself with another scrutiny, the red-headed man resumed his work.", "\"아니, 안 괜찮어.\" 이 짤막한 대답을 내뱉은 빨간 머리 사내는 한 번 더 찬찬히 뜯어보고 나서 흡족해진 모양인지 다시 일을 시작했다."], ["‘We want to put this horse up here,’ said Mr.", "\"우리가 이 말을 여기 좀 맡겼으면 하는데.\""], ["Pickwick; ‘I suppose we can, can’t we?’", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"그래도 되겠지요, 그렇지 않소?\""]], [["Want to put that ere horse up, do ee?’ repeated the red-headed man, leaning on his spade.", "\"그 말을 여기다 맡기고 싶다, 그거여?\" 빨간 머리 사내가 삽에 몸을 기댄 채 되물었다."]], [["‘Of course,’ replied Mr.", "\"물론이오.\""], ["Pickwick, who had by this time advanced, horse in hand, to the garden rails.", "픽윅 씨가 대답했으니, 그는 이때쯤 말을 손에 쥔 채 채마밭 울타리까지 다가와 있었다."]], [["‘Missus’--roared the man with the red head, emerging from the garden, and looking very hard at the horse--‘missus!’", "\"여보!\"—빨간 머리 사내가 밭에서 나오며 고함을 지르고는 말을 아주 매섭게 노려보았다—\"여보!\""]], [["A tall, bony woman--straight all the way down--in a coarse, blue pelisse, with the waist an inch or two below her arm-pits, responded to the call.", "키가 크고 뼈만 앙상한—위에서 아래까지 막대기처럼 곧은—여자가, 거친 푸른빛 외투를 입고, 그 허리께가 겨드랑이보다 한두 치 아래에 있는 차림으로 그 부름에 응했다."]], [["‘Can we put this horse up here, my good woman?’ said Mr.", "\"이 말을 여기 좀 맡길 수 있겠소, 친절한 부인?\""], ["Tupman, advancing, and speaking in his most seductive tones.", "터프먼 씨가 다가서며 더없이 매혹적인 어조로 말했다."], ["The woman looked very hard at the whole party; and the red-headed man whispered something in her ear.", "여자는 일행 전체를 아주 매섭게 노려보았고, 빨간 머리 사내가 그녀의 귀에 무어라 속삭였다."]], [["‘No,’ replied the woman, after a little consideration, ‘I’m afeerd on it.’", "\"안 돼요.\" 여자가 잠시 생각하더니 대답했다. \"그건 안 될 성싶구먼유.\""]], [["‘Afraid!’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"안 된다고!\" 픽윅 씨가 외쳤다."], ["Pickwick, ‘what’s the woman afraid of?’", "\"이 부인이 대체 무엇이 안 된다는 거요?\""]], [["‘It got us in trouble last time,’ said the woman, turning into the house; ‘I woan’t have nothin’ to say to ‘un.’", "\"지난번에 그놈 땜에 곤욕을 치렀거든유.\" 여자가 집 안으로 들어가며 말했다. \"그놈하곤 상관하고 싶지 않어유.\""]], [["‘Most extraordinary thing I have ever met with in my life,’ said the astonished Mr.", "\"내 평생 겪어 본 일 중에 가장 기이한 일이로군.\""], ["Pickwick.", "어안이 벙벙해진 픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘I--I--really believe,’ whispered Mr.", "\"나, 나는 정말로 이렇게 생각하오.\""], ["Winkle, as his friends gathered round him, ‘that they think we have come by this horse in some dishonest manner.’", "친구들이 자기 주위로 모여들자 윈클 씨가 속삭였다. \"저 사람들은 우리가 이 말을 무슨 부정한 방법으로 손에 넣었다고 여기는 것 같소.\""]], [["‘What!’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"뭐라고!\""], ["Pickwick, in a storm of indignation. Mr.", "픽윅 씨가 분노의 폭풍에 휩싸여 외쳤다."], ["Winkle modestly repeated his suggestion.", "윈클 씨가 조심스레 자기 추측을 되풀이했다."]], [["‘Hollo, you fellow,’ said the angry Mr.", "\"여보시오, 거기 양반.\" 화가 난 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick, ‘do you think we stole the horse?’", "\"당신은 우리가 이 말을 훔쳤다고 생각하는 거요?\""]], [["‘I’m sure ye did,’ replied the red-headed man, with a grin which agitated his countenance from one auricular organ to the other.", "\"훔친 게 분명하지유.\" 빨간 머리 사내가 한쪽 귀에서 다른 쪽 귀까지 얼굴을 온통 흔들어 대는 히죽거림과 함께 대답했다."], ["Saying which he turned into the house and banged the door after him.", "그 말을 하고는 집 안으로 들어가 문을 쾅 닫아 버렸다."]], [["‘It’s like a dream,’ ejaculated Mr.", "\"꼭 꿈만 같군.\" 픽윅 씨가 내뱉었다."], ["Pickwick, ‘a hideous dream.", "\"흉측한 꿈 말이야."], ["The idea of a man’s walking about all day with a dreadful horse that he can’t get rid of!’ The depressed Pickwickians turned moodily away, with the tall quadruped, for which they all felt the most unmitigated disgust, following slowly at their heels.", "떨쳐 버릴 수도 없는 끔찍한 말 한 마리를 데리고 하루 종일 걸어 다니다니!\" 의기소침해진 픽윅 회원들은 침울하게 발길을 돌렸고, 그들 모두가 더없이 노골적인 혐오를 느끼는 그 키 큰 네발짐승은 천천히 그들 발치를 뒤따라왔다."]], [["It was late in the afternoon when the four friends and their four-footed companion turned into the lane leading to Manor Farm; and even when they were so near their place of destination, the pleasure they would otherwise have experienced was materially damped as they reflected on the singularity of their appearance, and the absurdity of their situation.", "네 친구와 그들의 네발 달린 동행이 매너 농장으로 이어지는 샛길로 접어들었을 때는 늦은 오후였다. 그러나 목적지에 그토록 가까이 다다랐을 때조차, 그렇지 않았더라면 누렸을 즐거움은, 그들 행색의 괴상함과 처지의 우스꽝스러움을 떠올리는 통에 적잖이 가셔 버렸다."], ["Torn clothes, lacerated faces, dusty shoes, exhausted looks, and, above all, the horse. Oh, how Mr.", "찢어진 옷, 긁힌 얼굴, 먼지투성이 신발, 지친 몰골, 그리고 무엇보다도 그 말."], ["Pickwick cursed that horse: he had eyed the noble animal from time to time with looks expressive of hatred and revenge; more than once he had calculated the probable amount of the expense he would incur by cutting his throat; and now the temptation to destroy him, or to cast him loose upon the world, rushed upon his mind with tenfold force.", "아, 픽윅 씨가 그 말을 얼마나 저주했던가. 그는 이따금 그 늠름한 짐승을 증오와 복수를 드러내는 눈길로 노려보았고, 한 번 이상이나 그놈의 목을 따 버릴 때 드는 비용이 얼마나 될지 어림해 보기도 했으며, 이제는 그놈을 죽여 버리거나 세상에 풀어 내쫓고 싶은 유혹이 열 배의 힘으로 그의 마음에 밀려들었다."], ["He was roused from a meditation on these dire imaginings by the sudden appearance of two figures at a turn of the lane.", "이런 끔찍한 상상에 잠겨 있던 그는 샛길 모퉁이에 두 사람의 모습이 갑자기 나타나는 바람에 정신이 들었다."], ["It was Mr. Wardle, and his faithful attendant, the fat boy.", "워들 씨와 그의 충실한 시종인 뚱보 소년이었다."]], [["‘Why, where have you been?’ said the hospitable old gentleman; ‘I’ve been waiting for you all day.", "\"아니, 다들 어디 있었소?\" 손님 대접을 좋아하는 노신사가 말했다."], ["Well, you do look tired.", "\"온종일 기다렸다오. 저런, 정말 지쳐 보이는구려."], ["What!", "아니!"], ["Scratches!", "긁힌 자국이라니!"], ["Not hurt, I hope--eh?", "다치진 않았겠지요—응?"], ["Well, I am glad to hear that--very.", "아, 그 말을 들으니 정말 다행이오—참으로."], ["So you’ve been spilt, eh? Never mind.", "그래, 다들 굴러떨어졌다 그거요, 응?"], ["Common accident in these parts.", "괜찮소. 이 근방에선 흔한 사고라오."], ["Joe--he’s asleep again!--Joe, take that horse from the gentlemen, and lead it into the stable.’", "조—이놈이 또 자고 있군!—조, 신사분들한테서 그 말을 받아다가 마구간으로 끌고 가거라.\""]], [["The fat boy sauntered heavily behind them with the animal; and the old gentleman, condoling with his guests in homely phrase on so much of the day’s adventures as they thought proper to communicate, led the way to the kitchen.", "뚱보 소년은 그 짐승을 데리고 그들 뒤에서 무겁게 어슬렁어슬렁 걸었고, 노신사는 손님들이 적당히 전해 줄 만하다고 여긴 그날의 모험에 대해 소박한 말로 위로하면서 부엌으로 앞장서 안내했다."]], [["‘We’ll have you put to rights here,’ said the old gentleman, ‘and then I’ll introduce you to the people in the parlour. Emma, bring out the cherry brandy; now, Jane, a needle and thread here; towels and water, Mary. Come, girls, bustle about.’", "\"여기서 다들 말끔히 정돈해 드리고,\" 노신사가 말했다. \"그런 다음 응접실 사람들에게 소개해 드리리다. 에마, 체리 브랜디 좀 내오너라. 자, 제인, 여기 바늘과 실을. 메리, 수건과 물을. 자, 얘들아, 어서 부지런히들 움직여라.\""]], [["Three or four buxom girls speedily dispersed in search of the different articles in requisition, while a couple of large-headed, circular- visaged males rose from their seats in the chimney-corner (for although it was a May evening their attachment to the wood fire appeared as cordial as if it were Christmas), and dived into some obscure recesses, from which they speedily produced a bottle of blacking, and some half- dozen brushes.", "서너 명의 토실토실한 처녀들이 요구된 갖가지 물건들을 찾으러 잽싸게 흩어졌고, 한편 머리가 크고 얼굴이 둥근 사내 둘이 굴뚝 모퉁이의 자기 자리에서 일어났다(5월 저녁이었건만 장작불에 대한 그들의 애착은 마치 크리스마스라도 된 양 살가워 보였다). 그들은 어떤 컴컴한 구석으로 파고들더니 곧 구두약 한 병과 솔 대여섯 개를 꺼내 왔다."]], [["‘Bustle!’ said the old gentleman again, but the admonition was quite unnecessary, for one of the girls poured out the cherry brandy, and another brought in the towels, and one of the men suddenly seizing Mr.", "\"부지런히!\""], ["Pickwick by the leg, at imminent hazard of throwing him off his balance, brushed away at his boot till his corns were red-hot; while the other shampooed Mr. Winkle with a heavy clothes-brush, indulging, during the operation, in that hissing sound which hostlers are wont to produce when engaged in rubbing down a horse.", "노신사가 다시 말했으나 그 당부는 전혀 불필요했으니, 한 처녀는 체리 브랜디를 따랐고 또 한 처녀는 수건을 가져왔으며, 사내 하나는 픽윅 씨가 균형을 잃고 자빠질 뻔할 만큼 위태롭게 그의 다리를 와락 붙들고는 그의 티눈이 시뻘겋게 달아오를 때까지 장화를 솔질해 댔고, 다른 한 사내는 묵직한 옷솔로 윈클 씨를 문질러 대며, 그러는 동안 마부들이 말을 닦아 줄 때 으레 내곤 하는 그 쉭쉭거리는 소리를 즐겨 냈다."]], [["Mr. Snodgrass, having concluded his ablutions, took a survey of the room, while standing with his back to the fire, sipping his cherry brandy with heartfelt satisfaction.", "스노드그래스 씨는 몸 씻기를 마치고 불을 등진 채 서서 체리 브랜디를 진심 어린 만족과 함께 홀짝이며 방을 둘러보았다."], ["He describes it as a large apartment, with a red brick floor and a capacious chimney; the ceiling garnished with hams, sides of bacon, and ropes of onions.", "그의 묘사에 따르면 그곳은 붉은 벽돌 바닥에 널찍한 굴뚝이 있는 큰 방이었고, 천장은 햄과 베이컨 옆구리 살, 그리고 줄줄이 엮은 양파로 장식되어 있었다."], ["The walls were decorated with several hunting-whips, two or three bridles, a saddle, and an old rusty blunderbuss, with an inscription below it, intimating that it was ‘Loaded’--as it had been, on the same authority, for half a century at least.", "벽은 사냥용 채찍 여러 개와 두세 개의 굴레, 안장 하나, 그리고 낡고 녹슨 나팔총으로 꾸며져 있었는데, 그 아래에는 그것이 '장전됨'이라고 알리는 글귀가 적혀 있었으니—그 동일한 권위에 따르면 적어도 반세기 동안 줄곧 장전된 상태였다."], ["An old eight-day clock, of solemn and sedate demeanour, ticked gravely in one corner; and a silver watch, of equal antiquity, dangled from one of the many hooks which ornamented the dresser.", "엄숙하고 차분한 풍채의 낡은 팔일 시계가 한쪽 구석에서 진중하게 똑딱거렸고, 그에 못지않게 고색창연한 은시계 하나가 찬장을 장식한 여러 갈고리 중 하나에 매달려 흔들거렸다."]], [["‘Ready?’ said the old gentleman inquiringly, when his guests had been washed, mended, brushed, and brandied.", "\"준비됐소?\" 노신사가 손님들이 씻고, 꿰매고, 솔질하고, 브랜디까지 마시고 나자 물었다."]], [["‘Quite,’ replied Mr.", "\"다 됐소.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘Come along, then;’ and the party having traversed several dark passages, and being joined by Mr.", "\"그럼 갑시다.\""], ["Tupman, who had lingered behind to snatch a kiss from Emma, for which he had been duly rewarded with sundry pushings and scratchings, arrived at the parlour door.", "그리하여 일행은 어두컴컴한 복도 여럿을 지났고, 에마에게서 입맞춤 한 번을 훔치려고 뒤에 처졌다가 그 대가로 여러 번 떠밀리고 할퀴는 응분의 보상을 받은 터프먼 씨까지 합류하여, 마침내 응접실 문 앞에 다다랐다."]], [["‘Welcome,’ said their hospitable host, throwing it open and stepping forward to announce them, ‘welcome, gentlemen, to Manor Farm.’", "\"어서들 오시오.\" 손님 대접을 좋아하는 주인이 문을 활짝 열고 앞으로 나서서 그들을 알리며 말했다. \"여러분, 매너 농장에 오신 것을 환영하오.\""]], [["CHAPTER VI. AN OLD-FASHIONED CARD-PARTY--THE CLERGYMAN’S VERSES--THE STORY OF THE CONVICT’S RETURN\nSeveral guests who were assembled in the old parlour rose to greet Mr.", "낡은 응접실에 모여 있던 손님 여럿이 픽윅 씨와 그의 친구들이 들어서자 자리에서 일어나 그들을 맞이했다."], ["Pickwick and his friends upon their entrance; and during the performance of the ceremony of introduction, with all due formalities, Mr. Pickwick had leisure to observe the appearance, and speculate upon the characters and pursuits, of the persons by whom he was surrounded--a habit in which he, in common with many other great men, delighted to indulge.", "격식을 모두 갖춘 소개의 의식이 진행되는 동안, 픽윅 씨는 자기를 둘러싼 사람들의 외양을 살피고 그들의 성격과 직업을 헤아려 볼 여유를 얻었으니—이는 다른 많은 위인들과 마찬가지로 그가 즐겨 빠져드는 버릇이었다."]], [["A very old lady, in a lofty cap and faded silk gown--no less a personage than Mr.", "높다란 모자를 쓰고 빛바랜 비단 가운을 입은 아주 늙은 부인—다름 아닌 워들 씨의 어머니였다—이 벽난로 오른쪽 모서리의 상석을 차지하고 있었다."], ["Wardle’s mother--occupied the post of honour on the right-hand corner of the chimney-piece; and various certificates of her having been brought up in the way she should go when young, and of her not having departed from it when old, ornamented the walls, in the form of samplers of ancient date, worsted landscapes of equal antiquity, and crimson silk tea-kettle holders of a more modern period. The aunt, the two young ladies, and Mr.", "그리고 그녀가 어려서 마땅히 걸어야 할 길로 자라났고 늙어서도 그 길에서 벗어나지 않았다는 갖가지 증거가 오래된 자수 견본들, 그에 못지않게 오래된 털실 풍경화들, 그리고 좀 더 근래의 진홍빛 비단 찻주전자 받침들의 형태로 벽을 장식하고 있었다."], ["Wardle, each vying with the other in paying zealous and unremitting attentions to the old lady, crowded round her easy-chair, one holding her ear-trumpet, another an orange, and a third a smelling- bottle, while a fourth was busily engaged in patting and punching the pillows which were arranged for her support.", "이모와 두 젊은 아가씨, 그리고 워들 씨는 저마다 노부인께 열렬하고 끊임없는 정성을 바치려 다투며 그녀의 안락의자 주위로 몰려들었으니, 한 사람은 그녀의 보청 나팔을 들고, 다른 사람은 오렌지를, 또 한 사람은 냄새 맡는 병을 들었으며, 네 번째 사람은 그녀를 받쳐 주려고 놓아둔 베개들을 두드리고 매만지느라 분주했다. 맞은편에는 머리가 벗어지고, 사람 좋고 자애로운 얼굴을 한 노신사가 앉아 있었으니—딩글리 델의 목사였다."], ["On the opposite side sat a bald-headed old gentleman, with a good-humoured, benevolent face--the clergyman of Dingley Dell; and next him sat his wife, a stout, blooming old lady, who looked as if she were well skilled, not only in the art and mystery of manufacturing home-made cordials greatly to other people’s satisfaction, but of tasting them occasionally very much to her own.", "그 옆에는 그의 아내가 앉아 있었는데, 통통하고 혈색 좋은 노부인으로, 남들을 크게 만족시킬 만한 집에서 빚은 강장주를 만드는 기술과 비법에 능통할 뿐 아니라, 때때로 자기 자신도 흡족할 만큼 그것을 맛보는 데에도 능한 듯 보였다. 한쪽 구석에서는 머리가 단단하고 립스톤 사과 같은 얼굴을 한 자그마한 사내가 살찐 노신사와 이야기를 나누고 있었다."], ["A little hard-headed, Ripstone pippin-faced man, was conversing with a fat old gentleman in one corner; and two or three more old gentlemen, and two or three more old ladies, sat bolt upright and motionless on their chairs, staring very hard at Mr. Pickwick and his fellow-voyagers.", "그리고 두세 명의 노신사와 두세 명의 노부인이 의자에 꼿꼿이 앉아 꼼짝도 않고, 픽윅 씨와 그의 동행들을 매우 뚫어지게 바라보고 있었다."]], [["‘Mr. Pickwick, mother,’ said Mr.", "\"픽윅 씨예요, 어머니.\""], ["Wardle, at the very top of his voice.", "워들 씨가 목청껏 외쳤다."]], [["‘Ah!’ said the old lady, shaking her head; ‘I can’t hear you.’", "\"아!\" 노부인이 고개를 저으며 말했다. \"안 들린다.\""]], [["‘Mr.", "\"픽윅 씨예요, 할머니!\""], ["Pickwick, grandma!’ screamed both the young ladies together.", "두 젊은 아가씨가 함께 소리쳤다."]], [["‘Ah!’ exclaimed the old lady.", "\"아!\" 노부인이 탄식했다."], ["‘Well, it don’t much matter.", "\"뭐, 별 상관없다."], ["He don’t care for an old ‘ooman like me, I dare say.’", "나 같은 늙은이한테야 저분이 관심 없겠지, 아무렴.\""]], [["‘I assure you, ma’am,’ said Mr.", "\"부인, 분명히 말씀드리지만,\""], ["Pickwick, grasping the old lady’s hand, and speaking so loud that the exertion imparted a crimson hue to his benevolent countenance--‘I assure you, ma’am, that nothing delights me more than to see a lady of your time of life heading so fine a family, and looking so young and well.’", "픽윅 씨가 노부인의 손을 꼭 쥐고, 어찌나 큰 소리로 말하던지 그 애씀이 자애로운 그의 얼굴에 진홍빛을 띄울 정도로—\"부인, 분명히 말씀드리지만, 부인 연세에 이토록 훌륭한 가족을 이끄시면서, 이리도 젊고 정정해 보이시는 분을 뵙는 것보다 저를 기쁘게 하는 일은 없습니다.\""]], [["‘Ah!’ said the old lady, after a short pause: ‘it’s all very fine, I dare say; but I can’t hear him.’", "\"아!\" 노부인이 잠깐 뜸을 들였다가 말했다. \"다 좋은 말씀이겠지, 아무렴. 그런데 들리질 않으니.\""]], [["‘Grandma’s rather put out now,’ said Miss Isabella Wardle, in a low tone; ‘but she’ll talk to you presently.’", "\"할머니가 지금 좀 토라지셨어요.\" 이저벨라 워들 양이 나직한 목소리로 말했다. \"하지만 곧 말씀을 나누실 거예요.\""]], [["Mr. Pickwick nodded his readiness to humour the infirmities of age, and entered into a general conversation with the other members of the circle.", "픽윅 씨는 노년의 약함을 기꺼이 받아들이겠다는 뜻으로 고개를 끄덕이고는, 그 모임의 다른 사람들과 두루 대화를 나누기 시작했다."]], [["‘Delightful situation this,’ said Mr.", "\"여기 참 멋진 곳이로군요.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Delightful!’ echoed Messrs.", "\"멋지군요!\""], ["Snodgrass, Tupman, and Winkle.", "스노드그래스 씨, 터프먼 씨, 윈클 씨가 따라 했다."]], [["‘Well, I think it is,’ said Mr.", "\"음, 저도 그렇게 생각합니다.\""], ["Wardle.", "워들 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘There ain’t a better spot o’ ground in all Kent, sir,’ said the hard- headed man with the pippin--face; ‘there ain’t indeed, sir--I’m sure there ain’t, Sir.’ The hard-headed man looked triumphantly round, as if he had been very much contradicted by somebody, but had got the better of him at last.", "\"켄트 전체를 통틀어 이보다 나은 땅뙈기는 없습니다, 선생님.\" 사과 같은 얼굴에 머리가 단단한 사내가 말했다. \"정말로 없지요, 선생님—확실히 없습니다, 선생님.\" 머리가 단단한 사내는 마치 누군가에게 심하게 반박당했다가 마침내 그를 이긴 양, 의기양양하게 주위를 둘러보았다."]], [["‘There ain’t a better spot o’ ground in all Kent,’ said the hard-headed man again, after a pause.", "\"켄트 전체를 통틀어 이보다 나은 땅뙈기는 없습니다.\" 머리가 단단한 사내가 잠시 뜸을 들였다가 다시 말했다."]], [["‘’Cept Mullins’s Meadows,’ observed the fat man solemnly.", "\"멀린스네 풀밭만 빼면요.\" 살찐 사내가 엄숙하게 한마디 했다."]], [["‘Mullins’s Meadows!’ ejaculated the other, with profound contempt.", "\"멀린스네 풀밭이라니!\" 상대가 깊은 경멸을 담아 내뱉었다."]], [["‘Ah, Mullins’s Meadows,’ repeated the fat man.", "\"아, 멀린스네 풀밭 말입니다.\" 살찐 사내가 되풀이했다."]], [["‘Reg’lar good land that,’ interposed another fat man.", "\"그건 정말 좋은 땅이지요.\" 또 다른 살찐 사내가 끼어들었다."]], [["‘And so it is, sure-ly,’ said a third fat man.", "\"맞아요, 확실히 그렇지요.\" 세 번째 살찐 사내가 말했다."]], [["‘Everybody knows that,’ said the corpulent host.", "\"누구나 다 아는 사실이죠.\" 살집 좋은 주인장이 말했다."]], [["The hard-headed man looked dubiously round, but finding himself in a minority, assumed a compassionate air and said no more.", "머리가 단단한 사내는 미심쩍은 듯 주위를 둘러보았으나, 자기가 소수임을 깨닫고는 가엾어하는 듯한 표정을 짓고 더는 말하지 않았다."]], [["‘What are they talking about?’ inquired the old lady of one of her granddaughters, in a very audible voice; for, like many deaf people, she never seemed to calculate on the possibility of other persons hearing what she said herself.", "\"저 사람들 무슨 얘기를 하는 게냐?\" 노부인이 손녀 가운데 하나에게 아주 또렷한 목소리로 물었다. 귀먹은 사람들이 흔히 그렇듯, 그녀는 자기가 한 말을 다른 사람들이 들을 수도 있다는 가능성을 결코 헤아리지 않는 듯했다."]], [["‘About the land, grandma.’", "\"땅 얘기예요, 할머니.\""]], [["‘What about the land?--Nothing the matter, is there?’", "\"땅이 어쨌다고?—무슨 일이라도 났느냐?\""]], [["‘No, no. Mr.", "\"아니에요, 아니에요."], ["Miller was saying our land was better than Mullins’s Meadows.’", "밀러 씨가 우리 땅이 멀린스네 풀밭보다 낫다고 하셨어요.\""]], [["‘How should he know anything about it?’ inquired the old lady indignantly.", "\"그 사람이 그걸 어떻게 안단 말이냐?\" 노부인이 분개하여 물었다."], ["‘Miller’s a conceited coxcomb, and you may tell him I said so.’ Saying which, the old lady, quite unconscious that she had spoken above a whisper, drew herself up, and looked carving-knives at the hard- headed delinquent.", "\"밀러는 잘난 척하는 멋쟁이니, 내가 그랬다고 전해도 좋다.\" 그렇게 말하면서, 노부인은 자기가 속삭임보다 큰 소리로 말했다는 것을 전혀 의식하지 못한 채 몸을 곧추세우고는, 머리가 단단한 죄인을 고기 써는 칼날 같은 눈으로 쏘아보았다."]], [["‘Come, come,’ said the bustling host, with a natural anxiety to change the conversation, ‘what say you to a rubber, Mr.", "\"자, 자,\" 부산스러운 주인장이 화제를 바꾸고 싶어 하는 자연스러운 조바심으로 말했다."], ["Pickwick?’", "\"픽윅 씨, 휘스트 한 판 어떻습니까?\""]], [["‘I should like it of all things,’ replied that gentleman; ‘but pray don’t make up one on my account.’", "\"무엇보다도 좋지요.\" 그 신사가 대답했다. \"하지만 부디 저 때문에 한 판 차리지는 마십시오.\""]], [["‘Oh, I assure you, mother’s very fond of a rubber,’ said Mr.", "\"오, 분명히 말씀드리지만, 어머니가 휘스트를 무척 좋아하십니다.\""], ["Wardle; ‘ain’t you, mother?’", "워들 씨가 말했다. \"그렇지요, 어머니?\""]], [["The old lady, who was much less deaf on this subject than on any other, replied in the affirmative.", "이 화제에 관해서만은 다른 어떤 일보다 훨씬 덜 귀가 어두운 노부인이 그렇다고 대답했다."]], [["‘Joe, Joe!’ said the gentleman; ‘Joe--damn that--oh, here he is; put out the card-tables.’", "\"조, 조!\" 신사가 말했다. \"조—저놈 망할—오, 여기 있군. 카드 탁자를 차려라.\""]], [["The lethargic youth contrived without any additional rousing to set out two card-tables; the one for Pope Joan, and the other for whist. The whist-players were Mr.", "굼뜬 젊은이는 더 깨우지 않아도 어찌어찌 카드 탁자 두 개를 차렸으니, 하나는 포프 존 놀이용이고 다른 하나는 휘스트용이었다."], ["Pickwick and the old lady, Mr. Miller and the fat gentleman.", "휘스트를 칠 사람은 픽윅 씨와 노부인, 밀러 씨와 살찐 신사였다."], ["The round game comprised the rest of the company.", "둘러앉아 하는 놀이에는 나머지 일행이 끼었다."]], [["The rubber was conducted with all that gravity of deportment and sedateness of demeanour which befit the pursuit entitled ‘whist’--a solemn observance, to which, as it appears to us, the title of ‘game’ has been very irreverently and ignominiously applied.", "휘스트 한 판은 '휘스트'라는 이름에 걸맞은 온갖 진중한 몸가짐과 침착한 태도로 진행되었으니—그것은 엄숙한 의식이었으되, 우리가 보기에는 '놀이'라는 명칭이 매우 불경하고 망신스럽게 갖다 붙여진 것이었다."], ["The round-game table, on the other hand, was so boisterously merry as materially to interrupt the contemplations of Mr. Miller, who, not being quite so much absorbed as he ought to have been, contrived to commit various high crimes and misdemeanours, which excited the wrath of the fat gentleman to a very great extent, and called forth the good-humour of the old lady in a proportionate degree.", "반면 둘러앉아 하는 놀이의 탁자는 어찌나 떠들썩하게 즐거웠던지 밀러 씨의 사색을 적잖이 방해했으니, 그는 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 몰두하지 못한 탓에 갖가지 중죄와 경범죄를 저지르게 되었고, 이는 살찐 신사의 노여움을 대단히 부추겼으며 그에 비례하여 노부인의 흥을 돋우었다."]], [["‘There!’ said the criminal Miller triumphantly, as he took up the odd trick at the conclusion of a hand; ‘that could not have been played better, I flatter myself; impossible to have made another trick!’", "\"옳지!\" 죄인 밀러가 한 판이 끝날 무렵 마지막 끗수 패를 거둬들이며 의기양양하게 말했다. \"이보다 더 잘 칠 수는 없었지요, 내 자랑입니다만. 다른 패를 더 따는 건 불가능했어요!\""]], [["‘Miller ought to have trumped the diamond, oughtn’t he, Sir?’ said the old lady.", "\"밀러가 다이아몬드를 으뜸패로 따야 했지요, 그렇지 않습니까, 선생님?\" 노부인이 말했다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick nodded assent.", "픽윅 씨가 동의하며 고개를 끄덕였다."]], [["‘Ought I, though?’ said the unfortunate, with a doubtful appeal to his partner.", "\"제가 그랬어야 했나요?\" 그 불운한 사내가 자기 짝꿍에게 미심쩍은 듯 호소하며 말했다."]], [["‘You ought, Sir,’ said the fat gentleman, in an awful voice.", "\"그랬어야지요, 선생.\" 살찐 신사가 무시무시한 목소리로 말했다."]], [["‘Very sorry,’ said the crestfallen Miller.", "\"정말 죄송합니다.\" 풀이 죽은 밀러가 말했다."]], [["‘Much use that,’ growled the fat gentleman.", "\"그래봐야 소용없소.\" 살찐 신사가 으르렁거렸다."]], [["‘Two by honours--makes us eight,’ said Mr.", "\"으뜸패로 둘—우리가 여덟이 됐습니다.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Another hand.", "\"또 한 판."], ["‘Can you one?’ inquired the old lady.", "한 판 더 받으시겠소?\" 노부인이 물었다."]], [["‘I can,’ replied Mr.", "\"받겠습니다.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다."], ["Pickwick. ‘Double, single, and the rub.’", "\"더블, 싱글, 그리고 마무리 한 판.\""]], [["‘Never was such luck,’ said Mr.", "\"이런 운은 처음이오.\""], ["Miller.", "밀러 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Never was such cards,’ said the fat gentleman.", "\"이런 패는 처음이오.\" 살찐 신사가 말했다."]], [["A solemn silence; Mr.", "엄숙한 침묵."], ["Pickwick humorous, the old lady serious, the fat gentleman captious, and Mr. Miller timorous.", "픽윅 씨는 유쾌했고, 노부인은 진지했으며, 살찐 신사는 까탈스러웠고, 밀러 씨는 겁에 질려 있었다."]], [["‘Another double,’ said the old lady, triumphantly making a memorandum of the circumstance, by placing one sixpence and a battered halfpenny under the candlestick.", "\"또 더블이로군요.\" 노부인이 의기양양하게, 그 사실을 적어두는 양 6펜스짜리 하나와 찌그러진 반 페니짜리 하나를 촛대 밑에 놓으며 말했다."]], [["‘A double, sir,’ said Mr.", "\"더블입니다, 부인.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Quite aware of the fact, Sir,’ replied the fat gentleman sharply.", "\"그 사실은 잘 알고 있소, 선생.\" 살찐 신사가 날카롭게 대꾸했다."]], [["Another game, with a similar result, was followed by a revoke from the unlucky Miller; on which the fat gentleman burst into a state of high personal excitement which lasted until the conclusion of the game, when he retired into a corner, and remained perfectly mute for one hour and twenty-seven minutes; at the end of which time he emerged from his retirement, and offered Mr.", "비슷한 결과로 또 한 판이 끝나자, 이번에는 불운한 밀러가 으뜸패 위반을 저질렀다. 이에 살찐 신사는 격한 흥분에 빠져들어 그 판이 끝날 때까지 그 상태를 이어갔고, 끝나자 한쪽 구석으로 물러나 한 시간 이십칠 분 동안 완벽하게 입을 다물고 있었다."], ["Pickwick a pinch of snuff with the air of a man who had made up his mind to a Christian forgiveness of injuries sustained.", "그 시간이 지나자 그는 은둔에서 벗어나, 입은 손해를 기독교인답게 용서하기로 마음을 굳힌 사람의 표정으로 픽윅 씨에게 코담배 한 줌을 권했다."], ["The old lady’s hearing decidedly improved and the unlucky Miller felt as much out of his element as a dolphin in a sentry-box.", "노부인의 청력은 확연히 좋아졌고, 불운한 밀러는 마치 초소 안에 든 돌고래처럼 자기 처지에 어울리지 않는 기분이었다."]], [["Meanwhile the round game proceeded right merrily. Isabella Wardle and Mr.", "그동안 둘러앉아 하는 놀이는 매우 흥겹게 진행되었다."], ["Trundle ‘went partners,’ and Emily Wardle and Mr. Snodgrass did the same; and even Mr.", "이저벨라 워들과 트런들 씨가 '짝꿍'이 되었고, 에밀리 워들과 스노드그래스 씨도 그렇게 했으며, 심지어 터프먼 씨와 노처녀 이모도 칩과 아첨의 합자회사를 차렸다."], ["Tupman and the spinster aunt established a joint- stock company of fish and flattery. Old Mr.", "워들 영감은 흥겨움이 절정에 달해 있었다."], ["Wardle was in the very height of his jollity; and he was so funny in his management of the board, and the old ladies were so sharp after their winnings, that the whole table was in a perpetual roar of merriment and laughter.", "그가 판을 이끄는 솜씨가 어찌나 우스웠고 노부인들은 어찌나 딴 돈에 약빠르던지, 탁자 전체가 끊임없는 흥겨움과 웃음의 함성으로 들끓었다. 한 노부인은 늘 카드 여섯 장쯤을 물어내야 했는데, 매 판마다 어김없이 모두가 그것을 보고 웃어댔다."], ["There was one old lady who always had about half a dozen cards to pay for, at which everybody laughed, regularly every round; and when the old lady looked cross at having to pay, they laughed louder than ever; on which the old lady’s face gradually brightened up, till at last she laughed louder than any of them, Then, when the spinster aunt got ‘matrimony,’ the young ladies laughed afresh, and the Spinster aunt seemed disposed to be pettish; till, feeling Mr.", "그 노부인이 물어내야 하는 것에 못마땅한 표정을 짓자 사람들은 더욱 큰 소리로 웃었고, 그러자 노부인의 얼굴도 차츰 환해지더니 마침내는 누구보다도 큰 소리로 웃었다. 그러다 노처녀 이모가 '결혼'"], ["Tupman squeezing her hand under the table, she brightened up too, and looked rather knowing, as if matrimony in reality were not quite so far off as some people thought for; whereupon everybody laughed again, and especially old Mr.", "패를 얻자 젊은 아가씨들이 다시 웃었고, 노처녀 이모는 토라질 기색이었으나, 터프먼 씨가 탁자 밑에서 자기 손을 꼭 쥐는 것을 느끼고는 그녀도 얼굴이 환해지더니, 마치 결혼이라는 것이 실은 어떤 이들이 생각하는 것만큼 그리 먼 일은 아니라는 듯 제법 아는 체하는 표정을 지었다."], ["Wardle, who enjoyed a joke as much as the youngest. As to Mr.", "이에 모두가 다시 웃었으니, 특히 워들 영감은 누구 못지않게 농담을 즐겼다."], ["Snodgrass, he did nothing but whisper poetical sentiments into his partner’s ear, which made one old gentleman facetiously sly, about partnerships at cards and partnerships for life, and caused the aforesaid old gentleman to make some remarks thereupon, accompanied with divers winks and chuckles, which made the company very merry and the old gentleman’s wife especially so. And Mr.", "스노드그래스 씨로 말하자면, 그저 자기 짝꿍의 귀에 시적인 감상을 속삭이기만 했는데, 이 때문에 한 노신사가 카드의 짝꿍과 평생의 짝꿍을 두고 익살스럽게 짓궂은 소리를 했고, 앞서 말한 그 노신사는 그에 대해 갖가지 곁눈질과 낄낄거림을 곁들여 몇 마디 덧붙였으니, 이는 일행을 매우 유쾌하게, 특히 그 노신사의 아내를 더없이 유쾌하게 만들었다."], ["Winkle came out with jokes which are very well known in town, but are not all known in the country; and as everybody laughed at them very heartily, and said they were very capital, Mr.", "그리고 윈클 씨는 도시에서는 매우 잘 알려졌으나 시골에서는 다들 알지는 못하는 농담들을 꺼냈다. 모두가 그것들을 매우 진심으로 웃으며 아주 일품이라고 하니, 윈클 씨는 크나큰 영예와 영광의 상태에 놓였다."], ["Winkle was in a state of great honour and glory.", "그리고 자애로운 목사는 흐뭇하게 바라보았으니, 탁자를 둘러싼 행복한 얼굴들이 그 선량한 노인까지도 행복하게 만들었기 때문이다."], ["And the benevolent clergyman looked pleasantly on; for the happy faces which surrounded the table made the good old man feel happy too; and though the merriment was rather boisterous, still it came from the heart and not from the lips; and this is the right sort of merriment, after all.", "그리고 그 흥겨움이 다소 떠들썩하긴 했어도, 그것은 입에서가 아니라 마음에서 우러난 것이었다. 그리고 결국 이것이야말로 올바른 종류의 흥겨움인 것이다."]], [["The evening glided swiftly away, in these cheerful recreations; and when the substantial though homely supper had been despatched, and the little party formed a social circle round the fire, Mr.", "이러한 즐거운 놀이 속에 저녁은 빠르게 흘러갔다."], ["Pickwick thought he had never felt so happy in his life, and at no time so much disposed to enjoy, and make the most of, the passing moment.", "그리고 푸짐하지만 소박한 저녁 식사가 끝나고, 그 작은 일행이 난롯가에 둘러앉아 정다운 원을 이루자, 픽윅 씨는 평생 이토록 행복했던 적이 없으며, 또 어느 때보다도 흘러가는 이 순간을 한껏 즐기고 누리고 싶은 마음이 든다고 생각했다."]], [["‘Now this,’ said the hospitable host, who was sitting in great state next the old lady’s arm-chair, with her hand fast clasped in his--‘this is just what I like--the happiest moments of my life have been passed at this old fireside; and I am so attached to it, that I keep up a blazing fire here every evening, until it actually grows too hot to bear it. Why, my poor old mother, here, used to sit before this fireplace upon that little stool when she was a girl; didn’t you, mother?’", "\"자, 바로 이거예요.\" 노부인의 안락의자 옆에 그녀의 손을 자기 손에 꼭 쥔 채 위풍당당하게 앉아 있던 인심 좋은 주인장이 말했다. \"바로 이게 제가 좋아하는 거랍니다—제 생애 가장 행복한 순간들은 이 낡은 난롯가에서 보냈지요. 저는 이곳에 어찌나 정이 들었는지, 매일 저녁 정말로 견딜 수 없을 만큼 더워질 때까지 여기 활활 타오르는 불을 피워둔답니다. 아, 여기 제 가엾은 노모께서도 소녀 시절에 저 작은 걸상에 앉아 이 난로 앞에 계시곤 했지요. 그렇지 않습니까, 어머니?\""]], [["The tear which starts unbidden to the eye when the recollection of old times and the happiness of many years ago is suddenly recalled, stole down the old lady’s face as she shook her head with a melancholy smile.", "옛 시절의 추억과 여러 해 전의 행복이 문득 되살아날 때 부르지 않아도 눈에 솟구치는 그 눈물이, 노부인이 우울한 미소를 띠며 고개를 젓자 그녀의 얼굴을 타고 살며시 흘러내렸다."]], [["‘You must excuse my talking about this old place, Mr.", "\"이 낡은 집에 관해 이야기하는 걸 부디 너그러이 보아주십시오, 픽윅 씨.\""], ["Pickwick,’ resumed the host, after a short pause, ‘for I love it dearly, and know no other- -the old houses and fields seem like living friends to me; and so does our little church with the ivy, about which, by the bye, our excellent friend there made a song when he first came amongst us.", "주인장이 잠깐 뜸을 들였다가 다시 말했다. \"저는 이곳을 끔찍이 사랑하고, 다른 곳은 알지 못하니까요—이 오래된 집과 들판은 제게 살아 있는 벗처럼 느껴진답니다."], ["Mr.", "담쟁이덩굴이 뒤덮인 우리 작은 교회도 그렇고요."], ["Snodgrass, have you anything in your glass?’", "그러고 보니 여기 우리의 훌륭한 친구께서 처음 우리에게 오셨을 때 그 교회에 관해 노래를 한 곡 지으셨지요. 스노드그래스 씨, 잔에 술이 좀 있습니까?\""]], [["‘Plenty, thank you,’ replied that gentleman, whose poetic curiosity had been greatly excited by the last observation of his entertainer.", "\"넘치도록 있습니다, 고맙습니다.\" 그 신사가 대답했으니, 그의 시적 호기심은 주인장의 마지막 말에 크게 자극받은 터였다."], ["‘I beg your pardon, but you were talking about the song of the Ivy.’", "\"실례지만, 담쟁이 노래에 관해 말씀하셨지요.\""]], [["‘You must ask our friend opposite about that,’ said the host knowingly, indicating the clergyman by a nod of his head.", "\"그건 저기 맞은편에 계신 우리 친구께 여쭤보셔야 합니다.\" 주인장이 고개를 끄덕여 목사를 가리키며 의미심장하게 말했다."]], [["‘May I say that I should like to hear you repeat it, sir?’ said Mr.", "\"선생님, 그 노래를 한 번 들려주십사 청해도 되겠습니까?\""], ["Snodgrass.", "스노드그래스 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Why, really,’ replied the clergyman, ‘it’s a very slight affair; and the only excuse I have for having ever perpetrated it is, that I was a young man at the time. Such as it is, however, you shall hear it, if you wish.’", "\"아니, 사실,\" 목사가 대답했다. \"그건 아주 보잘것없는 것이랍니다. 제가 그런 걸 저지른 데 대해 댈 수 있는 유일한 변명이라면, 그때 제가 젊은 사람이었다는 것뿐이지요. 하지만 그런 대로, 원하신다면 들려드리겠습니다.\""]], [["A murmur of curiosity was of course the reply; and the old gentleman proceeded to recite, with the aid of sundry promptings from his wife, the lines in question.", "물론 호기심 어린 웅성거림이 그 답이었다. 그러자 노신사는 아내의 갖가지 거듦을 받아가며 문제의 시를 읊기 시작했다."], ["‘I call them,’ said he,", "\"제가 이 시를 부르는 이름은,\" 그가 말했다."]], [["# THE IVY GREEN", "# THE IVY GREEN"]], [["Oh, a dainty plant is the Ivy green, That creepeth o’er ruins old! Of right choice food are his meals, I ween, In his cell so lone and cold. The wall must be crumbled, the stone decayed, To pleasure his dainty whim; And the mouldering dust that years have made, Is a merry meal for him. Creeping where no life is seen, A rare old plant is the Ivy green.", "푸른 담쟁이"]], [["Fast he stealeth on, though he wears no wings, And a staunch old heart has he.", "오, 푸른 담쟁이는 어여쁜 풀이라, 오래된 폐허 위를 기어 다니네!"], ["How closely he twineth, how tight he clings To his friend the huge Oak Tree!", "외롭고 차디찬 제 굴속에서, 그가 드는 끼니는 참으로 별미라네, 내 생각엔."], ["And slily he traileth along the ground, And his leaves he gently waves, As he joyously hugs and crawleth round The rich mould of dead men’s graves.", "벽은 허물어지고 돌은 삭아야 하니, 그의 별난 입맛을 즐겁게 하려면. 세월이 빚어낸 썩어가는 먼지가, 그에게는 흥겨운 한 끼라네."], ["Creeping where grim death has been, A rare old plant is the Ivy green.", "생명이라곤 보이지 않는 곳을 기어 다니니, 푸른 담쟁이는 진귀하고 오래된 풀이라네."]], [["Whole ages have fled and their works decayed, And nations have scattered been; But the stout old Ivy shall never fade, From its hale and hearty green.", "날개도 없건만 그는 재빨리 기어들어, 굳건하고 오래된 마음을 지녔네. 얼마나 촘촘히 휘감고, 얼마나 단단히 매달리는가, 그의 벗인 거대한 참나무에게!"], ["The brave old plant in its lonely days, Shall fatten upon the past; For the stateliest building man can raise, Is the Ivy’s food at last.", "그리고 슬며시 땅을 따라 끌리며, 잎사귀를 부드럽게 흔드네, 죽은 자들의 무덤, 그 기름진 흙을 기쁘게 끌어안고 휘감으며."], ["Creeping on where time has been, A rare old plant is the Ivy green.", "무시무시한 죽음이 다녀간 곳을 기어 다니니, 푸른 담쟁이는 진귀하고 오래된 풀이라네."]], [["While the old gentleman repeated these lines a second time, to enable Mr.", "온 세월이 흘러가고 그 자취도 삭았으며, 뭇 나라가 뿔뿔이 흩어졌네."], ["Snodgrass to note them down, Mr.", "허나 굳건하고 오래된 담쟁이는 결코 시들지 않으리, 그 정정하고 씩씩한 푸르름에서."], ["Pickwick perused the lineaments of his face with an expression of great interest.", "용감하고 오래된 풀은 그 외로운 나날 속에, 지나간 것들을 먹고 살찌리니."], ["The old gentleman having concluded his dictation, and Mr.", "사람이 세울 수 있는 가장 웅장한 건물도, 결국엔 담쟁이의 양식이 되리라."], ["Snodgrass having returned his note-book to his pocket, Mr. Pickwick said--", "시간이 다녀간 곳을 기어 다니니, 푸른 담쟁이는 진귀하고 오래된 풀이라네."]], [["‘Excuse me, sir, for making the remark on so short an acquaintance; but a gentleman like yourself cannot fail, I should think, to have observed many scenes and incidents worth recording, in the course of your experience as a minister of the Gospel.’", "노신사가 스노드그래스 씨가 받아 적을 수 있도록 이 시구를 두 번째로 읊는 동안, 픽윅 씨는 큰 흥미가 담긴 표정으로 그의 얼굴 윤곽을 찬찬히 살펴보았다. 노신사가 받아쓰기를 마치고, 스노드그래스 씨가 수첩을 주머니에 도로 넣자, 픽윅 씨가 말했다—"]], [["‘I have witnessed some certainly,’ replied the old gentleman, ‘but the incidents and characters have been of a homely and ordinary nature, my sphere of action being so very limited.’", "\"선생님, 알게 된 지 얼마 되지도 않아 이런 말씀을 드려 죄송합니다만, 선생님 같은 분이라면 복음의 사역자로 지내오신 그 경험의 과정에서 기록해 둘 만한 장면과 사건들을 많이 보아오셨으리라 생각합니다.\""]], [["‘You did make some notes, I think, about John Edmunds, did you not?’ inquired Mr.", "\"분명 몇 가지는 목격했지요.\" 노신사가 대답했다."], ["Wardle, who appeared very desirous to draw his friend out, for the edification of his new visitors.", "\"하지만 제가 활동하는 영역이 워낙 좁다 보니, 그 사건과 인물들은 소박하고 평범한 것들이었답니다.\""]], [["The old gentleman slightly nodded his head in token of assent, and was proceeding to change the subject, when Mr.", "\"존 에드먼즈에 관해 무언가 적어두신 게 있지 않았습니까?\""], ["Pickwick said--", "워들 씨가 물었으니, 그는 새로 온 손님들의 교화를 위해 자기 친구의 말문을 트게 하고 싶어 하는 기색이 역력했다."]], [["‘I beg your pardon, sir, but pray, if I may venture to inquire, who was John Edmunds?’", "노신사는 그렇다는 표시로 고개를 살짝 끄덕이고는 화제를 바꾸려 하던 참에, 픽윅 씨가 말했다—"]], [["‘The very thing I was about to ask,’ said Mr. Snodgrass eagerly.", "\"실례지만 선생님, 감히 여쭤도 된다면, 존 에드먼즈가 누구입니까?\""]], [["‘You are fairly in for it,’ said the jolly host. ‘You must satisfy the curiosity of these gentlemen, sooner or later; so you had better take advantage of this favourable opportunity, and do so at once.’", "\"바로 제가 여쭤보려던 겁니다.\" 스노드그래스 씨가 열렬히 말했다. \"이제 꼼짝없이 걸리셨군요.\" 유쾌한 주인장이 말했다. \"조만간 이 신사분들의 호기심을 풀어주셔야 할 테니, 이 좋은 기회를 이용해 지금 당장 들려주시는 편이 낫겠습니다.\""]], [["The old gentleman smiled good-humouredly as he drew his chair forward-- the remainder of the party drew their chairs closer together, especially Mr. Tupman and the spinster aunt, who were possibly rather hard of hearing; and the old lady’s ear-trumpet having been duly adjusted, and Mr.", "노신사는 사람 좋게 미소 지으며 의자를 앞으로 당겼다—나머지 일행도 의자를 더 바싹 끌어당겼으니, 특히 어쩌면 귀가 좀 어두운 듯한 터프먼 씨와 노처녀 이모가 그러했다."], ["Miller (who had fallen asleep during the recital of the verses) roused from his slumbers by an admonitory pinch, administered beneath the table by his ex-partner the solemn fat man, the old gentleman, without further preface, commenced the following tale, to which we have taken the liberty of prefixing the title of", "노부인의 보청 나팔이 알맞게 맞춰지고, (시구를 읊는 동안 잠들어 버린) 밀러 씨가 그의 옛 짝꿍인 엄숙한 살찐 사내가 탁자 밑에서 가한 경고의 꼬집음에 잠에서 깨어나자, 노신사는 더 서두 없이 다음 이야기를 시작했으니, 우리는 여기에 외람되이 이런 제목을 붙여 보았다. 죄수의 귀향"]], [["# THE CONVICT’S RETURN", "# THE CONVICT’S RETURN"]], [["‘When I first settled in this village,’ said the old gentleman, ‘which is now just five-and-twenty years ago, the most notorious person among my parishioners was a man of the name of Edmunds, who leased a small farm near this spot. He was a morose, savage-hearted, bad man; idle and dissolute in his habits; cruel and ferocious in his disposition. Beyond the few lazy and reckless vagabonds with whom he sauntered away his time in the fields, or sotted in the ale-house, he had not a single friend or acquaintance; no one cared to speak to the man whom many feared, and every one detested--and Edmunds was shunned by all.", "\"제가 처음 이 마을에 자리를 잡았을 때,\" 노신사가 말했다. \"그러니까 지금으로부터 꼭 스물다섯 해 전입니다만, 제 교구민 가운데 가장 악명 높은 인물은 에드먼즈라는 이름의 사내였는데, 이 부근의 작은 농장을 빌려 부치고 있었지요. 그는 음울하고 야만적인 마음을 지닌 못된 사내였습니다. 게으르고 방탕한 습성에, 잔인하고 흉포한 기질이었지요. 들판에서 빈둥거리며 시간을 흘려보내거나 술집에서 진탕 퍼마실 때 어울리던 게으르고 무모한 떠돌이 몇몇을 빼고는, 그에게는 단 한 명의 친구도 아는 이도 없었습니다. 많은 이가 두려워하고 누구나 혐오하는 그 사내에게 아무도 말을 걸려 하지 않았지요—에드먼즈는 모두에게 따돌림을 당했습니다."]], [["‘This man had a wife and one son, who, when I first came here, was about twelve years old.", "이 사내에게는 아내와 외아들이 하나 있었는데, 그 아들은 제가 처음 이곳에 왔을 때 열두 살쯤이었지요. 그 여인이 겪은 고통의 절절함, 그것을 견뎌낸 부드럽고 끈기 있는 태도, 그 아이를 길러내며 품었던 애끓는 근심—그것을 제대로 헤아릴 수 있는 이는 아무도 없을 겁니다."], ["Of the acuteness of that woman’s sufferings, of the gentle and enduring manner in which she bore them, of the agony of solicitude with which she reared that boy, no one can form an adequate conception.", "박정한 추측이라면 하느님께서 용서하시기를 빕니다만, 저는 그 사내가 여러 해에 걸쳐 조직적으로 그녀의 마음을 무너뜨리려 했다고 굳게, 그리고 제 영혼으로 믿습니다. 하지만 그녀는 자식을 위해 그 모든 것을 견뎠고, 많은 이에게는 이상하게 보일지 모르나, 그 아비를 위해서도 견뎠지요."], ["Heaven forgive me the supposition, if it be an uncharitable one, but I do firmly and in my soul believe, that the man systematically tried for many years to break her heart; but she bore it all for her child’s sake, and, however strange it may seem to many, for his father’s too; for brute as he was, and cruelly as he had treated her, she had loved him once; and the recollection of what he had been to her, awakened feelings of forbearance and meekness under suffering in her bosom, to which all God’s creatures, but women, are strangers.", "그가 짐승 같은 자였고 그녀를 잔인하게 대했어도, 그녀는 한때 그를 사랑했으니까요. 그리고 그가 그녀에게 어떤 존재였는지를 떠올리는 마음이, 그녀의 가슴속에 고통을 견디는 인내와 온순함의 정을 일깨웠으니, 그것은 하느님의 모든 피조물 가운데 여인을 제외하고는 누구도 알지 못하는 것이지요."]], [["‘They were poor--they could not be otherwise when the man pursued such courses; but the woman’s unceasing and unwearied exertions, early and late, morning, noon, and night, kept them above actual want.", "그들은 가난했습니다—그 사내가 그런 길을 걷는 마당에 가난하지 않을 수 없었지요. 하지만 여인이 아침이나 저녁이나, 아침 점심 저녁으로 쉼 없이 지칠 줄 모르고 애쓴 덕분에 그들은 정작 궁핍에까지 떨어지지는 않았습니다."], ["These exertions were but ill repaid.", "이 애씀은 변변히 보답받지 못했지요."], ["People who passed the spot in the evening--sometimes at a late hour of the night--reported that they had heard the moans and sobs of a woman in distress, and the sound of blows; and more than once, when it was past midnight, the boy knocked softly at the door of a neighbour’s house, whither he had been sent, to escape the drunken fury of his unnatural father.", "저녁때—때로는 밤이 깊은 시각에—그곳을 지나던 사람들은 고통에 빠진 여인의 신음과 흐느낌, 그리고 매질 소리를 들었다고 전했습니다. 그리고 한두 번이 아니게, 자정이 지났을 때 그 소년이 이웃집 문을 살며시 두드렸으니, 술 취한 비정한 아비의 광포함을 피해 그리로 보내진 것이었지요."]], [["‘During the whole of this time, and when the poor creature often bore about her marks of ill-usage and violence which she could not wholly conceal, she was a constant attendant at our little church.", "이 모든 세월 동안, 그 가엾은 여인이 차마 다 감추지 못한 학대와 폭력의 흔적을 몸에 지니고 다니던 때에도, 그녀는 우리 작은 교회에 한결같이 나왔습니다."], ["Regularly every Sunday, morning and afternoon, she occupied the same seat with the boy at her side; and though they were both poorly dressed--much more so than many of their neighbours who were in a lower station--they were always neat and clean.", "매주 일요일 아침과 오후에 어김없이 그녀는 옆에 소년을 앉히고 같은 자리에 앉았지요. 그리고 두 사람 다 허름하게—형편이 더 낮은 이웃들보다도 훨씬 더 허름하게 입었어도, 늘 단정하고 깨끗했습니다."], ["Every one had a friendly nod and a kind word for “poor Mrs.", "모두가 '가엾은 에드먼즈 부인'에게 다정한 목례와 친절한 말을 건넸지요."], ["Edmunds”; and sometimes, when she stopped to exchange a few words with a neighbour at the conclusion of the service in the little row of elm-trees which leads to the church porch, or lingered behind to gaze with a mother’s pride and fondness upon her healthy boy, as he sported before her with some little companions, her careworn face would lighten up with an expression of heartfelt gratitude; and she would look, if not cheerful and happy, at least tranquil and contented.", "그리고 때로 예배가 끝난 뒤 교회 현관으로 이어지는 작은 느릅나무 줄 사이에서 이웃과 몇 마디 나누려고 걸음을 멈추거나, 건강한 아들이 작은 동무들과 어울려 자기 앞에서 뛰노는 모습을 어머니의 자랑과 애정으로 바라보려 뒤에 남아 머뭇거릴 때면, 근심에 찌든 그녀의 얼굴이 진심 어린 감사의 표정으로 환해지곤 했지요. 그러면 그녀는, 명랑하고 행복해 보이지는 않더라도, 적어도 평온하고 만족스러워 보였습니다."]], [["‘Five or six years passed away; the boy had become a robust and well- grown youth.", "대여섯 해가 흘러갔습니다. 소년은 다부지고 잘 자란 청년이 되었지요."], ["The time that had strengthened the child’s slight frame and knit his weak limbs into the strength of manhood had bowed his mother’s form, and enfeebled her steps; but the arm that should have supported her was no longer locked in hers; the face that should have cheered her, no more looked upon her own.", "아이의 가냘픈 몸을 튼튼하게 하고 약한 팔다리를 어른의 힘으로 다져준 그 세월이, 그 어미의 몸은 굽게 하고 걸음은 쇠하게 했습니다. 하지만 그녀를 부축했어야 할 그 팔은 더 이상 그녀의 팔에 끼워져 있지 않았고, 그녀를 위로했어야 할 그 얼굴은 더 이상 그녀의 얼굴을 바라보지 않았지요."], ["She occupied her old seat, but there was a vacant one beside her.", "그녀는 옛 자리를 차지하고 있었으나, 그 곁에는 빈자리가 있었습니다."], ["The Bible was kept as carefully as ever, the places were found and folded down as they used to be: but there was no one to read it with her; and the tears fell thick and fast upon the book, and blotted the words from her eyes.", "성경책은 여느 때처럼 정성스레 간직되었고, 보던 곳을 찾아 예전처럼 접어두었지요. 하지만 그녀와 함께 그것을 읽어 줄 이는 아무도 없었고, 눈물이 그 책 위로 굵게 그리고 빠르게 떨어져 그녀의 눈에서 글자를 지워버렸습니다."], ["Neighbours were as kind as they were wont to be of old, but she shunned their greetings with averted head.", "이웃들은 예전처럼 친절했지만, 그녀는 고개를 돌린 채 그들의 인사를 피했지요."], ["There was no lingering among the old elm-trees now--no cheering anticipations of happiness yet in store.", "이제는 오래된 느릅나무 사이에서 머뭇거리는 일도 없었고—아직 다가올 행복에 대한 흐뭇한 기대도 없었습니다."], ["The desolate woman drew her bonnet closer over her face, and walked hurriedly away.", "그 쓸쓸한 여인은 보닛을 얼굴 위로 더 깊이 끌어당기고는 황급히 걸어가 버렸지요."]], [["‘Shall I tell you that the young man, who, looking back to the earliest of his childhood’s days to which memory and consciousness extended, and carrying his recollection down to that moment, could remember nothing which was not in some way connected with a long series of voluntary privations suffered by his mother for his sake, with ill-usage, and insult, and violence, and all endured for him--shall I tell you, that he, with a reckless disregard for her breaking heart, and a sullen, wilful forgetfulness of all she had done and borne for him, had linked himself with depraved and abandoned men, and was madly pursuing a headlong career, which must bring death to him, and shame to her?", "기억과 의식이 미치는 어린 시절의 가장 이른 날들을 돌이켜보며, 그 추억을 바로 그 순간까지 더듬어 보아도, 자기를 위해 어머니가 자진하여 겪은 그 길고 긴 궁핍의 연속, 학대와 모욕과 폭력—그 모두를 자기를 위해 견뎌낸 것과 어떻게든 얽히지 않은 것이라곤 하나도 떠올릴 수 없었던 그 젊은이가, 무너져 가는 어머니의 마음은 무모하게 외면한 채, 그녀가 자기를 위해 행하고 견딘 모든 것을 음울하고 고집스럽게 잊어버리고, 타락하고 막돼먹은 자들과 어울려, 자기에게는 죽음을, 그녀에게는 치욕을 가져올 무모한 행로를 미친 듯이 좇았다고—제가 말씀드려야겠습니까? 아, 슬프도다 인간의 본성이여!"], ["Alas for human nature! You have anticipated it long since.", "여러분은 이미 오래전에 짐작하셨겠지요."]], [["‘The measure of the unhappy woman’s misery and misfortune was about to be completed.", "그 불행한 여인의 비참함과 불운의 잔이 막 채워지려 하고 있었습니다."], ["Numerous offences had been committed in the neighbourhood; the perpetrators remained undiscovered, and their boldness increased.", "그 인근에서 수많은 범죄가 저질러졌지요. 범인들은 잡히지 않았고, 그들의 대담함은 점점 커져 갔습니다."], ["A robbery of a daring and aggravated nature occasioned a vigilance of pursuit, and a strictness of search, they had not calculated on.", "대담하고 흉악한 성격의 강도 사건이 벌어지자, 그들이 미처 헤아리지 못했던 끈질긴 추적과 철저한 수색이 이루어졌지요."], ["Young Edmunds was suspected, with three companions.", "젊은 에드먼즈가 세 명의 동료와 함께 혐의를 받았습니다."], ["He was apprehended-- committed--tried--condemned--to die.", "그는 붙잡혀—기소되고—재판받고—유죄 선고를 받아—사형에 처해지게 되었지요."]], [["‘The wild and piercing shriek from a woman’s voice, which resounded through the court when the solemn sentence was pronounced, rings in my ears at this moment.", "엄숙한 판결이 내려졌을 때 법정에 울려 퍼진 한 여인의 목소리, 그 거칠고 가슴을 찌르는 비명이 지금 이 순간에도 제 귀에 울립니다."], ["That cry struck a terror to the culprit’s heart, which trial, condemnation--the approach of death itself, had failed to awaken.", "그 외침은 죄인의 가슴에 공포를 박아 넣었으니, 그것은 재판도, 유죄 선고도—심지어 죽음 그 자체가 다가오는 것조차도 일깨우지 못한 공포였지요. 내내 완강한 음울함으로 굳게 다물려 있던 그 입술이 저도 모르게 떨리며 벌어졌습니다."], ["The lips which had been compressed in dogged sullenness throughout, quivered and parted involuntarily; the face turned ashy pale as the cold perspiration broke forth from every pore; the sturdy limbs of the felon trembled, and he staggered in the dock.", "모든 땀구멍에서 식은땀이 솟구치며 얼굴은 잿빛으로 창백해졌지요. 그 중죄인의 다부진 팔다리가 떨렸고, 그는 피고석에서 비틀거렸습니다."]], [["‘In the first transports of her mental anguish, the suffering mother threw herself on her knees at my feet, and fervently sought the Almighty Being who had hitherto supported her in all her troubles to release her from a world of woe and misery, and to spare the life of her only child.", "마음의 고통이 처음으로 격하게 터져 나온 가운데, 그 괴로워하는 어머니는 제 발치에 무릎을 꿇고는, 지금껏 모든 고난 속에서 자기를 받쳐 주신 전능하신 분께, 자기를 이 비탄과 비참의 세상에서 풀어 주시고 하나뿐인 자식의 목숨만은 살려 주십사 간절히 빌었습니다."], ["A burst of grief, and a violent struggle, such as I hope I may never have to witness again, succeeded.", "다시는 목격하지 않기를 바라는 그런 비통의 폭발과 격렬한 몸부림이 뒤따랐지요. 저는 그때부터 그녀의 심장이 부서지고 있음을 알았습니다."], ["I knew that her heart was breaking from that hour; but I never once heard complaint or murmur escape her lips.", "하지만 그녀의 입에서 단 한 번도 불평이나 원망이 새어 나오는 것을 들은 적이 없습니다."]], [["‘It was a piteous spectacle to see that woman in the prison-yard from day to day, eagerly and fervently attempting, by affection and entreaty, to soften the hard heart of her obdurate son.", "그 여인이 날마다 감옥 마당에서, 애정과 간청으로 완고한 아들의 굳은 마음을 누그러뜨리려 열렬히 그리고 간절히 애쓰는 모습은 가엾기 그지없는 광경이었습니다. 다 헛일이었지요."], ["It was in vain. He remained moody, obstinate, and unmoved.", "그는 침울하고 고집스럽고 꿈쩍도 않은 채로 있었습니다."], ["Not even the unlooked-for commutation of his sentence to transportation for fourteen years, softened for an instant the sullen hardihood of his demeanour.", "형이 뜻밖에 십사 년의 유형으로 감형되었을 때조차도, 그의 음울하고 모진 태도는 한순간도 누그러지지 않았지요."]], [["‘But the spirit of resignation and endurance that had so long upheld her, was unable to contend against bodily weakness and infirmity.", "하지만 그토록 오래 그녀를 지탱해 온 체념과 인내의 정신도, 육신의 쇠약함과 병약함에는 맞설 수가 없었습니다."], ["She fell sick.", "그녀는 병들었지요."], ["She dragged her tottering limbs from the bed to visit her son once more, but her strength failed her, and she sank powerless on the ground.", "비척거리는 다리를 끌고 침대에서 일어나 아들을 한 번 더 보러 가려 했으나, 기력이 다하여 그만 맥없이 땅바닥에 쓰러지고 말았습니다."]], [["‘And now the boasted coldness and indifference of the young man were tested indeed; and the retribution that fell heavily upon him nearly drove him mad.", "이제 그 젊은이가 그토록 뽐내던 냉담함과 무관심이 정말로 시험에 들었습니다. 그리고 그에게 무겁게 내려앉은 응보는 그를 거의 미치게 만들 지경이었지요."], ["A day passed away and his mother was not there; another flew by, and she came not near him; a third evening arrived, and yet he had not seen her--, and in four-and-twenty hours he was to be separated from her, perhaps for ever.", "하루가 지나도 어머니는 오지 않았고, 또 하루가 흘러도 그녀는 그의 곁에 오지 않았으며, 사흘째 저녁이 와도 그는 여전히 그녀를 보지 못했습니다—그런데 스물네 시간 뒤면 그는 그녀와, 어쩌면 영영, 갈라서게 될 처지였지요."], ["Oh!", "오!"], ["how the long-forgotten thoughts of former days rushed upon his mind, as he almost ran up and down the narrow yard--as if intelligence would arrive the sooner for his hurrying--and how bitterly a sense of his helplessness and desolation rushed upon him, when he heard the truth!", "오래 잊고 있던 지난날의 생각들이 그의 마음에 얼마나 밀려들었던지, 그는 좁은 마당을 거의 뛰다시피 오르내렸으니—마치 그가 서두르면 소식이 더 빨리 오기라도 할 것처럼 말입니다. 그리고 진실을 듣고 났을 때, 자기가 무력하고 황폐하다는 느낌이 얼마나 쓰라리게 그를 덮쳤던지요!"], ["His mother, the only parent he had ever known, lay ill--it might be, dying--within one mile of the ground he stood on; were he free and unfettered, a few minutes would place him by her side.", "그가 알았던 단 하나의 부모인 어머니가 병들어 누워 있었으니—어쩌면 죽어 가고 있었으니—그가 서 있는 땅에서 일 마일도 안 되는 곳에서요. 그가 자유로운 몸으로 매여 있지만 않았다면, 단 몇 분이면 그녀 곁에 닿을 수 있었을 겁니다."], ["He rushed to the gate, and grasping the iron rails with the energy of desperation, shook it till it rang again, and threw himself against the thick wall as if to force a passage through the stone; but the strong building mocked his feeble efforts, and he beat his hands together and wept like a child.", "그는 문으로 달려들어 절망의 힘으로 쇠창살을 움켜쥐고는, 다시금 쩌렁쩌렁 울리도록 그것을 흔들었고, 마치 돌을 뚫고 길을 내려는 듯 두꺼운 벽에 제 몸을 내던졌습니다. 하지만 그 견고한 건물은 그의 무력한 발버둥을 비웃었고, 그는 두 손을 맞부딪치며 아이처럼 울었지요."]], [["‘I bore the mother’s forgiveness and blessing to her son in prison; and I carried the solemn assurance of repentance, and his fervent supplication for pardon, to her sick-bed.", "저는 어머니의 용서와 축복을 옥에 갇힌 아들에게 전했습니다. 그리고 회개의 엄숙한 다짐과 용서를 비는 그의 간절한 탄원을 그녀의 병상으로 가져갔지요."], ["I heard, with pity and compassion, the repentant man devise a thousand little plans for her comfort and support when he returned; but I knew that many months before he could reach his place of destination, his mother would be no longer of this world.", "저는 가엾고 측은한 마음으로, 그 회개한 사내가 돌아오면 그녀를 위로하고 부양하겠다며 사소한 계획을 천 가지나 짜는 것을 들었습니다. 하지만 저는 그가 목적지에 닿기 여러 달 전에 그의 어머니가 더 이상 이 세상 사람이 아니리라는 것을 알고 있었지요."]], [["‘He was removed by night.", "그는 밤중에 이송되었습니다."], ["A few weeks afterwards the poor woman’s soul took its flight, I confidently hope, and solemnly believe, to a place of eternal happiness and rest.", "몇 주 뒤 그 가엾은 여인의 영혼은 길을 떠났으니, 저는 영원한 행복과 안식의 곳으로 갔으리라 굳게 바라고 또 엄숙히 믿습니다."], ["I performed the burial service over her remains.", "저는 그녀의 유해 위에 장례 예배를 올렸지요."], ["She lies in our little churchyard.", "그녀는 우리의 작은 교회 묘지에 누워 있습니다."], ["There is no stone at her grave’s head.", "그녀의 무덤 머리맡에는 비석이 없습니다."], ["Her sorrows were known to man; her virtues to God.", "그녀의 슬픔은 사람들이 알았고, 그녀의 미덕은 하느님께서 아셨지요."]], [["‘It had been arranged previously to the convict’s departure, that he should write to his mother as soon as he could obtain permission, and that the letter should be addressed to me.", "죄수가 떠나기 전에 미리 정해 둔 바가 있었으니, 그가 허락을 얻는 대로 어머니에게 편지를 쓰되, 그 편지는 제 앞으로 부치기로 한 것이었습니다."], ["The father had positively refused to see his son from the moment of his apprehension; and it was a matter of indifference to him whether he lived or died.", "아비는 아들이 붙잡힌 순간부터 그를 보기를 단호히 거부했고, 아들이 살든 죽든 그에게는 아무래도 상관없는 일이었지요. 그의 소식 하나 없이 여러 해가 흘러갔습니다."], ["Many years passed over without any intelligence of him; and when more than half his term of transportation had expired, and I had received no letter, I concluded him to be dead, as, indeed, I almost hoped he might be.", "그리고 유형 기간의 절반이 넘게 지났는데도 제가 편지를 한 통도 받지 못하자, 저는 그가 죽었으리라 결론지었지요. 사실, 저는 그가 차라리 죽었기를 바라기까지 했습니다."]], [["‘Edmunds, however, had been sent a considerable distance up the country on his arrival at the settlement; and to this circumstance, perhaps, may be attributed the fact, that though several letters were despatched, none of them ever reached my hands.", "하지만 에드먼즈는 유형지에 도착했을 때 내륙 깊숙이 상당히 먼 곳으로 보내졌습니다. 그리고 어쩌면 이 사정 때문에, 그가 여러 통의 편지를 부쳤음에도 그 가운데 단 한 통도 제 손에 닿지 못한 것일 테지요."], ["He remained in the same place during the whole fourteen years.", "그는 십사 년 내내 같은 곳에 머물렀습니다."], ["At the expiration of the term, steadily adhering to his old resolution and the pledge he gave his mother, he made his way back to England amidst innumerable difficulties, and returned, on foot, to his native place.", "형기가 끝나자, 옛 결심과 어머니에게 한 약속을 한결같이 지키며, 그는 헤아릴 수 없는 어려움을 헤치고 영국으로 돌아오는 길을 찾았고, 걸어서 고향으로 돌아왔지요."]], [["‘On a fine Sunday evening, in the month of August, John Edmunds set foot in the village he had left with shame and disgrace seventeen years before.", "팔월의 어느 화창한 일요일 저녁, 존 에드먼즈는 십칠 년 전 수치와 불명예를 안고 떠났던 그 마을에 발을 들였습니다."], ["His nearest way lay through the churchyard.", "그의 가장 가까운 길은 교회 묘지를 가로지르는 것이었지요."], ["The man’s heart swelled as he crossed the stile.", "사내의 가슴은 층계식 울타리를 넘을 때 벅차올랐습니다."], ["The tall old elms, through whose branches the declining sun cast here and there a rich ray of light upon the shady part, awakened the associations of his earliest days.", "키 큰 오래된 느릅나무들, 그 가지 사이로 기우는 해가 그늘진 자리에 여기저기 풍성한 빛줄기를 드리우니, 그것이 그의 가장 어린 날들의 추억을 일깨웠지요."], ["He pictured himself as he was then, clinging to his mother’s hand, and walking peacefully to church.", "그는 그때의 자기 모습을 그려 보았습니다, 어머니의 손에 매달려 평온히 교회로 걸어가던 모습을요."], ["He remembered how he used to look up into her pale face; and how her eyes would sometimes fill with tears as she gazed upon his features--tears which fell hot upon his forehead as she stooped to kiss him, and made him weep too, although he little knew then what bitter tears hers were.", "자기가 어떻게 그녀의 창백한 얼굴을 올려다보곤 했는지, 그녀가 그의 이목구비를 바라볼 때면 어떻게 그 눈에 이따금 눈물이 고이곤 했는지를 떠올렸습니다—그녀가 몸을 굽혀 그에게 입 맞출 때 그 눈물이 그의 이마에 뜨겁게 떨어져 그를 함께 울게 만들었지요. 비록 그때는 그녀의 눈물이 얼마나 쓰라린 것이었는지 거의 알지 못했지만 말입니다."], ["He thought how often he had run merrily down that path with some childish playfellow, looking back, ever and again, to catch his mother’s smile, or hear her gentle voice; and then a veil seemed lifted from his memory, and words of kindness unrequited, and warnings despised, and promises broken, thronged upon his recollection till his heart failed him, and he could bear it no longer.", "그는 어린 동무와 함께 그 길을 얼마나 자주 신나게 뛰어 내려가곤 했는지, 그러면서 어머니의 미소를 보거나 다정한 목소리를 들으려고 몇 번이고 뒤돌아보곤 했는지를 떠올렸지요. 그러자 그의 기억에서 베일이 걷히는 듯했고, 보답받지 못한 친절의 말들, 무시당한 경고들, 깨뜨린 약속들이 그의 추억 속으로 밀려들어 마침내 그의 가슴이 무너져 더는 견딜 수 없게 되었습니다."]], [["‘He entered the church.", "그는 교회로 들어섰습니다."], ["The evening service was concluded and the congregation had dispersed, but it was not yet closed.", "저녁 예배는 끝났고 신도들은 흩어졌지만, 아직 문이 닫혀 있지는 않았지요."], ["His steps echoed through the low building with a hollow sound, and he almost feared to be alone, it was so still and quiet.", "그의 발소리가 낮은 건물 안에서 텅 빈 울림으로 메아리쳤고, 그토록 고요하고 적막한지라 그는 혼자 있는 것이 거의 두려울 지경이었습니다."], ["He looked round him.", "그는 주위를 둘러보았지요."], ["Nothing was changed.", "아무것도 변하지 않았습니다."], ["The place seemed smaller than it used to be; but there were the old monuments on which he had gazed with childish awe a thousand times; the little pulpit with its faded cushion; the Communion table before which he had so often repeated the Commandments he had reverenced as a child, and forgotten as a man.", "그곳은 예전보다 작아 보였지만, 그가 어린 시절 천 번이나 어린아이의 경외심으로 바라보았던 그 오래된 기념비들이 그대로 있었지요. 빛바랜 방석이 놓인 작은 설교단, 그가 어린 시절 받들었으나 어른이 되어 잊어버린 계명들을 그토록 자주 외던 그 앞의 성찬대도요."], ["He approached the old seat; it looked cold and desolate.", "그는 옛 자리로 다가갔습니다. 그 자리는 차갑고 쓸쓸해 보였지요."], ["The cushion had been removed, and the Bible was not there.", "방석은 치워졌고 성경책도 거기에 없었습니다."], ["Perhaps his mother now occupied a poorer seat, or possibly she had grown infirm and could not reach the church alone.", "어쩌면 그의 어머니가 이제는 더 초라한 자리에 앉는지도, 아니면 어쩌면 노쇠하여 혼자서는 교회에 올 수 없게 되었는지도 몰랐지요."], ["He dared not think of what he feared.", "그는 자기가 두려워하는 것을 차마 떠올릴 수 없었습니다."], ["A cold feeling crept over him, and he trembled violently as he turned away.", "서늘한 느낌이 그를 엄습했고, 그는 몸을 돌리며 심하게 떨었지요."], ["‘An old man entered the porch just as he reached it.", "그가 현관에 다다른 바로 그때 한 노인이 그곳으로 들어섰습니다."], ["Edmunds started back, for he knew him well; many a time he had watched him digging graves in the churchyard.", "에드먼즈는 흠칫 물러섰으니, 그를 잘 알았기 때문이지요. 그가 교회 묘지에서 무덤을 파는 것을 여러 번 지켜본 적이 있었습니다."], ["What would he say to the returned convict?", "돌아온 죄수에게 그가 무어라 할까요?"]], [["‘The old man raised his eyes to the stranger’s face, bade him “good- evening,”", "노인은 낯선 이의 얼굴로 눈을 들어 \"안녕하시오\" 인사하고는 천천히 걸어갔습니다."], ["and walked slowly on. He had forgotten him.", "그를 잊은 것이었지요."]], [["‘He walked down the hill, and through the village.", "그는 언덕을 내려가 마을을 지났습니다."], ["The weather was warm, and the people were sitting at their doors, or strolling in their little gardens as he passed, enjoying the serenity of the evening, and their rest from labour.", "날씨가 따뜻하여, 그가 지나갈 때 사람들은 문간에 앉아 있거나 작은 정원을 거닐며, 저녁의 고요함과 일에서 벗어난 휴식을 즐기고 있었지요. 그를 향해 여러 시선이 던져졌고, 그도 누가 자기를 알아보고 피하지나 않는지 살피려 좌우로 미심쩍은 눈길을 여러 번 던졌습니다."], ["Many a look was turned towards him, and many a doubtful glance he cast on either side to see whether any knew and shunned him.", "거의 모든 집에 낯선 얼굴들이 있었지요. 어떤 집에서는 옛 학교 동무의 우람한 모습을—그가 마지막으로 보았을 때는 소년이었던—한 무리의 명랑한 아이들에 둘러싸인 채 알아보았습니다."], ["There were strange faces in almost every house; in some he recognised the burly form of some old schoolfellow--a boy when he last saw him--surrounded by a troop of merry children; in others he saw, seated in an easy-chair at a cottage door, a feeble and infirm old man, whom he only remembered as a hale and hearty labourer; but they had all forgotten him, and he passed on unknown.", "또 어떤 집에서는, 그저 정정하고 씩씩한 일꾼으로만 기억하던 노인이 오두막 문간의 안락의자에 앉아 있는, 쇠약하고 병약한 모습을 보았지요. 하지만 그들은 모두 그를 잊었고, 그는 알려지지 않은 채 지나갔습니다."]], [["‘The last soft light of the setting sun had fallen on the earth, casting a rich glow on the yellow corn sheaves, and lengthening the shadows of the orchard trees, as he stood before the old house--the home of his infancy--to which his heart had yearned with an intensity of affection not to be described, through long and weary years of captivity and sorrow.", "지는 해의 마지막 부드러운 빛이 땅 위에 떨어져, 노란 옥수숫단에 풍성한 빛을 드리우고 과수원 나무들의 그림자를 길게 늘이고 있을 때, 그는 그 오래된 집—그의 어린 시절의 보금자리—앞에 섰으니, 길고 고달픈 유배와 슬픔의 세월 내내 그의 가슴이 형언할 수 없는 격렬한 애정으로 그리워하던 곳이었지요."], ["The paling was low, though he well remembered the time that it had seemed a high wall to him; and he looked over into the old garden.", "울타리는 낮았습니다, 비록 그것이 그에게 높은 담장처럼 보이던 시절을 그는 또렷이 기억했지만요. 그리고 그는 오래된 정원 안을 넘겨다보았습니다."], ["There were more seeds and gayer flowers than there used to be, but there were the old trees still--the very tree under which he had lain a thousand times when tired of playing in the sun, and felt the soft, mild sleep of happy boyhood steal gently upon him.", "예전보다 씨앗도 더 많고 꽃도 더 화사했지만, 오래된 나무들은 여전히 그대로 있었지요—햇볕 아래 노는 데 지칠 때면 그가 천 번이나 그 아래 누워, 행복한 소년 시절의 부드럽고 온화한 잠이 살며시 자기에게 스며드는 것을 느끼던 바로 그 나무 말입니다."], ["There were voices within the house.", "집 안에서 목소리들이 들렸습니다."], ["He listened, but they fell strangely upon his ear; he knew them not.", "그는 귀 기울였지만, 그 소리들은 그의 귀에 낯설게 떨어졌지요."], ["They were merry too; and he well knew that his poor old mother could not be cheerful, and he away.", "그는 그것들을 알지 못했습니다. 그 소리들은 즐거웠지요."], ["The door opened, and a group of little children bounded out, shouting and romping.", "그리고 그는 자기가 떠나 있는 마당에 가엾은 노모가 명랑할 리 없다는 것을 잘 알았습니다."], ["The father, with a little boy in his arms, appeared at the door, and they crowded round him, clapping their tiny hands, and dragging him out, to join their joyous sports.", "문이 열리고, 한 무리의 어린아이들이 소리치고 까불며 뛰어나왔지요. 어린 사내아이를 품에 안은 아버지가 문간에 나타나자, 아이들은 작은 손뼉을 치며 그의 주위로 몰려들어, 즐거운 놀이에 끼우려 그를 끌어냈습니다."], ["The convict thought on the many times he had shrunk from his father’s sight in that very place.", "죄수는 자기가 바로 그곳에서 아버지의 눈길을 피해 몇 번이나 움츠러들었는지를 떠올렸지요."], ["He remembered how often he had buried his trembling head beneath the bedclothes, and heard the harsh word, and the hard stripe, and his mother’s wailing; and though the man sobbed aloud with agony of mind as he left the spot, his fist was clenched, and his teeth were set, in a fierce and deadly passion.", "떨리는 머리를 이불 속에 파묻고, 거친 말과 모진 매질, 그리고 어머니의 울부짖음을 듣던 일이 얼마나 잦았는지를 그는 기억했습니다. 그리고 그 사내는 그곳을 떠나며 마음의 고통으로 큰 소리로 흐느꼈지만, 그의 주먹은 꽉 쥐어졌고, 그의 이는 사납고 살기 어린 격정으로 악물려 있었지요."]], [["‘And such was the return to which he had looked through the weary perspective of many years, and for which he had undergone so much suffering!", "여러 해의 고달픈 전망을 가로질러 그가 바라보았던, 그토록 많은 고통을 겪으며 견뎌낸 그 귀향이 바로 이러했던 것입니다!"], ["No face of welcome, no look of forgiveness, no house to receive, no hand to help him--and this too in the old village.", "환영하는 얼굴도, 용서의 눈길도, 맞아 줄 집도, 도와줄 손도 없었지요—그것도 옛 마을에서요."], ["What was his loneliness in the wild, thick woods, where man was never seen, to this!", "사람이라곤 결코 보이지 않던 거칠고 우거진 숲속에서의 외로움이, 이에 비하면 무엇이었겠습니까!"]], [["‘He felt that in the distant land of his bondage and infamy, he had thought of his native place as it was when he left it; and not as it would be when he returned.", "그는 깨달았지요, 자기가 속박과 오욕의 그 먼 땅에서, 고향을 떠나던 그때 모습 그대로 떠올려 왔지, 돌아왔을 때 어떠할지로는 떠올리지 않았다는 것을요."], ["The sad reality struck coldly at his heart, and his spirit sank within him.", "그 슬픈 현실이 그의 가슴을 싸늘하게 내리쳤고, 그의 기운은 안으로 가라앉았습니다."], ["He had not courage to make inquiries, or to present himself to the only person who was likely to receive him with kindness and compassion.", "그는 이것저것 물어볼 용기도, 자기를 친절과 측은함으로 맞아 줄 법한 단 하나의 사람 앞에 나설 용기도 없었지요. 그는 천천히 걸었습니다."], ["He walked slowly on; and shunning the roadside like a guilty man, turned into a meadow he well remembered; and covering his face with his hands, threw himself upon the grass.", "그리고 죄지은 사람처럼 길가를 피하여, 잘 기억하는 풀밭으로 접어들었지요. 그러고는 두 손으로 얼굴을 가린 채, 풀밭 위로 몸을 내던졌습니다."]], [["‘He had not observed that a man was lying on the bank beside him; his garments rustled as he turned round to steal a look at the new-comer; and Edmunds raised his head.", "그는 한 사내가 자기 곁 둑에 누워 있는 것을 알아채지 못했습니다. 그 사내가 새로 온 이를 슬쩍 훔쳐보려 몸을 돌리자 그의 옷자락이 바스락거렸지요. 그러자 에드먼즈가 고개를 들었습니다."]], [["‘The man had moved into a sitting posture.", "그 사내는 앉은 자세로 몸을 옮긴 터였습니다."], ["His body was much bent, and his face was wrinkled and yellow.", "그의 몸은 잔뜩 굽었고, 얼굴은 주름지고 누렜지요."], ["His dress denoted him an inmate of the workhouse: he had the appearance of being very old, but it looked more the effect of dissipation or disease, than the length of years.", "그의 옷차림은 그가 구빈원의 거주자임을 드러냈습니다. 그는 매우 늙어 보였지만, 그것은 세월의 길이보다는 방탕이나 질병의 결과로 보였지요."], ["He was staring hard at the stranger, and though his eyes were lustreless and heavy at first, they appeared to glow with an unnatural and alarmed expression after they had been fixed upon him for a short time, until they seemed to be starting from their sockets.", "그는 낯선 이를 뚫어지게 바라보고 있었는데, 처음에는 그 눈이 광채 없이 흐릿했으나, 잠시 그에게 고정되어 있더니 부자연스럽고 놀란 표정으로 이글거리는 듯하다가, 마침내 눈구멍에서 튀어나올 것만 같았습니다."], ["Edmunds gradually raised himself to his knees, and looked more and more earnestly on the old man’s face.", "에드먼즈는 차츰 무릎을 세워 일어나, 점점 더 진지하게 노인의 얼굴을 바라보았지요."], ["They gazed upon each other in silence.", "그들은 말없이 서로를 응시했습니다."]], [["‘The old man was ghastly pale.", "노인은 소름 끼치도록 창백했습니다."], ["He shuddered and tottered to his feet.", "그는 몸서리치며 비틀비틀 일어섰지요."], ["Edmunds sprang to his.", "에드먼즈도 벌떡 일어섰습니다."], ["He stepped back a pace or two.", "노인이 한두 걸음 뒤로 물러섰지요."], ["Edmunds advanced.", "에드먼즈가 다가갔습니다."]], [["‘“Let me hear you speak,”", "\"당신 목소리를 들려주시오.\""], ["said the convict, in a thick, broken voice.", "죄수가 탁하고 갈라진 목소리로 말했습니다."]], [["‘“Stand off!”", "\"물러서!\""], ["cried the old man, with a dreadful oath.", "노인이 무시무시한 욕설과 함께 소리쳤지요."], ["The convict drew closer to him.", "죄수가 그에게 더 가까이 다가갔습니다."]], [["‘“Stand off!”", "\"물러서!\""], ["shrieked the old man.", "노인이 비명을 질렀습니다."], ["Furious with terror, he raised his stick, and struck Edmunds a heavy blow across the face.", "공포에 미쳐 날뛰며, 그는 지팡이를 치켜들어 에드먼즈의 얼굴을 가로질러 호되게 후려쳤지요."]], [["‘“Father--devil!”", "\"아버지—악마 같으니!\""], ["murmured the convict between his set teeth.", "죄수가 악문 이 사이로 중얼거렸습니다."], ["He rushed wildly forward, and clenched the old man by the throat--but he was his father; and his arm fell powerless by his side.", "그는 미친 듯이 앞으로 달려들어 노인의 목을 움켜쥐었지요—하지만 그는 자기 아버지였습니다. 그러자 그의 팔은 옆구리로 맥없이 떨어졌지요."]], [["‘The old man uttered a loud yell which rang through the lonely fields like the howl of an evil spirit.", "노인은 외로운 들판에 악령의 울부짖음처럼 울려 퍼지는 큰 비명을 내질렀습니다."], ["His face turned black, the gore rushed from his mouth and nose, and dyed the grass a deep, dark red, as he staggered and fell.", "그의 얼굴은 검게 변했고, 입과 코에서 핏덩이가 쏟아져 나와 풀을 짙고 어두운 붉은빛으로 물들이며, 그는 비틀거리다 쓰러졌지요."], ["He had ruptured a blood-vessel, and he was a dead man before his son could raise him.", "그는 혈관이 터졌고, 아들이 그를 일으키기도 전에 죽은 사람이 되어 있었습니다."]], [["‘In that corner of the churchyard,’ said the old gentleman, after a silence of a few moments, ‘in that corner of the churchyard of which I have before spoken, there lies buried a man who was in my employment for three years after this event, and who was truly contrite, penitent, and humbled, if ever man was. No one save myself knew in that man’s lifetime who he was, or whence he came--it was John Edmunds, the returned convict.’", "\"그 교회 묘지의 모퉁이에,\" 노신사가 잠시 침묵하더니 말했다. \"제가 앞서 말씀드린 그 교회 묘지의 모퉁이에, 한 사내가 묻혀 있습니다. 그는 이 사건 뒤로 삼 년 동안 제 밑에서 일했으며, 만약 진정으로 뉘우치고 회개하며 겸손해진 사람이 일찍이 있었다면 바로 그런 사람이었지요. 그가 살아 있는 동안 그가 누구인지, 어디서 왔는지를 저 말고는 아무도 알지 못했습니다—그가 바로 존 에드먼즈, 돌아온 죄수였지요.\""]], [["CHAPTER VII. HOW MR.", "그날의 고단한 모험 탓인지, 아니면 목사의 이야기가 자아낸 졸음의 기운 탓인지, 픽윅 씨의 노곤한 성향이 어찌나 강하게 작용했던지, 그가 아늑한 침실로 안내된 지 채 오 분도 지나지 않아 그는 깊고 꿈 없는 잠에 빠져들었다."], ["WINKLE, INSTEAD OF SHOOTING AT THE PIGEON AND KILLING THE CROW, SHOT AT THE CROW AND WOUNDED THE PIGEON; HOW THE DINGLEY DELL CRICKET CLUB PLAYED ALL-MUGGLETON, AND HOW ALL-MUGGLETON DINED AT THE DINGLEY DELL EXPENSE; WITH OTHER INTERESTING AND INSTRUCTIVE MATTERS\nThe fatiguing adventures of the day or the somniferous influence of the clergyman’s tale operated so strongly on the drowsy tendencies of Mr. Pickwick, that in less than five minutes after he had been shown to his comfortable bedroom he fell into a sound and dreamless sleep, from which he was only awakened by the morning sun darting his bright beams reproachfully into the apartment.", "그 잠에서 그를 깨운 것은 오직 아침 해가 책망하듯 환한 빛줄기를 방 안으로 쏘아 보낸 것뿐이었다."], ["Mr. Pickwick was no sluggard, and he sprang like an ardent warrior from his tent-bedstead.", "픽윅 씨는 게으름뱅이가 아니었으니, 그는 열렬한 전사가 막사 침상에서 뛰쳐나오듯 벌떡 일어났다."]], [["‘Pleasant, pleasant country,’ sighed the enthusiastic gentleman, as he opened his lattice window.", "\"기분 좋은, 기분 좋은 시골이로다.\" 그 열정적인 신사는 격자창을 열며 한숨처럼 내뱉었다."], ["‘Who could live to gaze from day to day on bricks and slates who had once felt the influence of a scene like this?", "\"한번 이런 풍경의 감화를 느껴 본 사람이라면, 어찌 날이면 날마다 벽돌과 슬레이트나 바라보며 살 수 있겠는가?"], ["Who could continue to exist where there are no cows but the cows on the chimney-pots; nothing redolent of Pan but pan-tiles; no crop but stone crop?", "굴뚝 통 위에 얹힌 소 모양 말고는 소가 없고, 목신을 떠올리게 하는 것이라곤 평기와뿐이며, 작물이라곤 돌나물밖에 없는 곳에서 어찌 계속 살아갈 수 있겠는가? 이런 곳에서 평생을 질질 끌며 견딜 자가 누가 있겠는가?"], ["Who could bear to drag out a life in such a spot?", "도대체 누가 그것을 참아 낼 수 있겠는가?\""], ["Who, I ask, could endure it?’ and, having cross-examined solitude after the most approved precedents, at considerable length, Mr. Pickwick thrust his head out of the lattice and looked around him.", "그리하여, 가장 인정받는 선례를 따라 상당히 길게 고독을 심문한 뒤, 픽윅 씨는 격자창 밖으로 고개를 내밀고 주위를 둘러보았다."]], [["The rich, sweet smell of the hay-ricks rose to his chamber window; the hundred perfumes of the little flower-garden beneath scented the air around; the deep-green meadows shone in the morning dew that glistened on every leaf as it trembled in the gentle air; and the birds sang as if every sparkling drop were to them a fountain of inspiration.", "건초 더미의 풍성하고 달콤한 냄새가 그의 방 창문까지 피어올랐고, 그 아래 작은 화원에서 풍기는 백 가지 향내가 주위의 공기를 향기롭게 물들였다. 짙푸른 초원은 아침 이슬 속에서 빛났으니, 그 이슬은 부드러운 바람에 떨리는 잎사귀마다 반짝였고, 새들은 마치 반짝이는 물방울 하나하나가 자신들에게 영감의 샘이라도 되는 듯 노래했다."], ["Mr. Pickwick fell into an enchanting and delicious reverie.", "픽윅 씨는 황홀하고 감미로운 몽상에 빠져들었다."]], [["‘Hollo!’ was the sound that roused him.", "\"여보시오!\" 하는 소리가 그를 일깨웠다."]], [["He looked to the right, but he saw nobody; his eyes wandered to the left, and pierced the prospect; he stared into the sky, but he wasn’t wanted there; and then he did what a common mind would have done at once--looked into the garden, and there saw Mr. Wardle.", "그는 오른쪽을 보았으나 아무도 보이지 않았고, 시선이 왼쪽으로 떠돌며 풍경을 꿰뚫었으나 거기에도 없었다. 그는 하늘을 올려다보았지만 거기서 그를 부른 것은 아니었다. 그러고 나서 그는 평범한 사람이라면 진작 했을 일을 했다. 정원을 내려다본 것이다. 그리고 거기서 와들 씨를 보았다."]], [["‘How are you?’ said the good-humoured individual, out of breath with his own anticipations of pleasure.’Beautiful morning, ain’t it?", "\"안녕하시오?\" 그 쾌활한 사람이 제 기쁜 기대에 숨이 차서 말했다."], ["Glad to see you up so early.", "\"아름다운 아침 아니오?"], ["Make haste down, and come out.", "이렇게 일찍 일어나신 걸 보니 반갑소이다. 어서 내려와 나오시오."], ["I’ll wait for you here.’", "여기서 기다리리다.\""]], [["Mr. Pickwick needed no second invitation.", "픽윅 씨는 두 번 청할 필요가 없었다."], ["Ten minutes sufficed for the completion of his toilet, and at the expiration of that time he was by the old gentleman’s side.", "십 분이면 몸단장을 끝내기에 충분했고, 그 시간이 다하자 그는 노신사 곁에 서 있었다."]], [["‘Hollo!’ said Mr.", "\"여보시오!\" 이번에는 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick in his turn, seeing that his companion was armed with a gun, and that another lay ready on the grass; ‘what’s going forward?’", "그의 동행이 총으로 무장하고 있었고, 또 한 자루가 풀밭 위에 준비된 채 놓여 있는 것을 보고서였다. \"무슨 일이 벌어지는 게요?\""]], [["‘Why, your friend and I,’ replied the host, ‘are going out rook-shooting before breakfast.", "\"아, 댁의 친구분과 내가,\" 주인이 대답했다."], ["He’s a very good shot, ain’t he?’", "\"아침 식사 전에 떼까마귀 사냥을 나가려는 참이오. 그분 사격 솜씨가 아주 좋다지요?\""]], [["‘I’ve heard him say he’s a capital one,’ replied Mr.", "\"본인이 일류라고 말하는 것은 들었소만,\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다."], ["Pickwick, ‘but I never saw him aim at anything.’", "\"정작 그가 무언가를 겨누는 것은 한 번도 본 적이 없소.\""]], [["‘Well,’ said the host, ‘I wish he’d come.", "\"음,\" 주인이 말했다."], ["Joe--Joe!’", "\"어서 와 줬으면 좋겠소. 조—조!\""]], [["The fat boy, who under the exciting influence of the morning did not appear to be more than three parts and a fraction asleep, emerged from the house.", "뚱뚱한 소년이, 아침의 자극적인 기운 속에서도 사 분의 삼하고도 얼마쯤은 여전히 잠들어 있는 듯한 모습으로 집에서 나왔다."]], [["‘Go up, and call the gentleman, and tell him he’ll find me and Mr. Pickwick in the rookery.", "\"올라가서 그분을 불러다가, 나와 픽윅 씨가 떼까마귀 숲에 있다고 전해라."], ["Show the gentleman the way there; d’ye hear?’", "그분께 그리로 가는 길을 안내해 드려라, 알겠느냐?\""]], [["The boy departed to execute his commission; and the host, carrying both guns like a second Robinson Crusoe, led the way from the garden.", "소년은 분부를 수행하러 떠났고, 주인은 제2의 로빈슨 크루소처럼 총 두 자루를 들고 정원을 나서서 앞장을 섰다."]], [["‘This is the place,’ said the old gentleman, pausing after a few minutes walking, in an avenue of trees.", "\"여기가 그곳이오.\" 노신사가 몇 분쯤 걷다가 나무들이 늘어선 가로수 길에서 걸음을 멈추며 말했다."], ["The information was unnecessary; for the incessant cawing of the unconscious rooks sufficiently indicated their whereabouts.", "그 말은 굳이 필요치 않았다. 아무것도 모르는 떼까마귀들이 끊임없이 까악까악 울어 대는 소리가 그들의 소재를 충분히 알려 주었기 때문이다."]], [["The old gentleman laid one gun on the ground, and loaded the other.", "노신사는 총 한 자루를 땅에 내려놓고 다른 한 자루에 장전을 했다."]], [["‘Here they are,’ said Mr.", "\"저기들 오는군요.\""], ["Pickwick; and, as he spoke, the forms of Mr. Tupman, Mr.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Snodgrass, and Mr. Winkle appeared in the distance.", "그가 말하는 사이, 터프먼 씨와 스노드그래스 씨와 윈클 씨의 모습이 멀리서 나타났다."], ["The fat boy, not being quite certain which gentleman he was directed to call, had with peculiar sagacity, and to prevent the possibility of any mistake, called them all.", "뚱뚱한 소년은 어느 신사를 부르라고 지시받았는지 확실치 않았던 터라, 남다른 명민함을 발휘하여, 혹시라도 실수가 생길 여지를 없애려고 셋을 모두 불렀던 것이다."]], [["‘Come along,’ shouted the old gentleman, addressing Mr.", "\"이리 오시오.\" 노신사가 윈클 씨를 향해 소리쳤다."], ["Winkle; ‘a keen hand like you ought to have been up long ago, even to such poor work as this.’", "\"댁처럼 솜씨 좋은 분이라면 이런 시시한 일이라도 진작 일어나 나왔어야지요.\""]], [["Mr. Winkle responded with a forced smile, and took up the spare gun with an expression of countenance which a metaphysical rook, impressed with a foreboding of his approaching death by violence, may be supposed to assume.", "윈클 씨는 억지웃음으로 응답하며 남아 있던 총을 집어 들었다."], ["It might have been keenness, but it looked remarkably like misery.", "그 얼굴에 떠오른 표정은, 폭력에 의해 곧 죽으리라는 예감에 사로잡힌 형이상학적인 떼까마귀가 지을 법한 표정 같았다. 솜씨 좋음의 표정이었을지도 모르나, 그것은 놀라우리만치 비참함에 가까워 보였다."]], [["The old gentleman nodded; and two ragged boys who had been marshalled to the spot under the direction of the infant Lambert, forthwith commenced climbing up two of the trees.", "노신사가 고개를 끄덕이자, 어린 램버트의 지휘 아래 그 자리에 정렬해 있던 누더기 차림의 소년 둘이 곧바로 나무 두 그루를 타고 오르기 시작했다."]], [["‘What are these lads for?’ inquired Mr.", "\"저 애들은 무엇 때문이오?\""], ["Pickwick abruptly.", "픽윅 씨가 불쑥 물었다."], ["He was rather alarmed; for he was not quite certain but that the distress of the agricultural interest, about which he had often heard a great deal, might have compelled the small boys attached to the soil to earn a precarious and hazardous subsistence by making marks of themselves for inexperienced sportsmen.", "그는 다소 놀랐던 것이다. 익히 여러 번 많은 이야기를 들은 바 있는 농업 부문의 곤궁함이, 혹여나 토지에 매인 어린 소년들로 하여금 서투른 사냥꾼들의 과녁 노릇을 함으로써 위태롭고 불안정한 생계를 잇게 만든 것은 아닐까, 그는 영 확신할 수가 없었기 때문이다."]], [["‘Only to start the game,’ replied Mr.", "\"그저 사냥감을 몰아내려는 거지요.\""], ["Wardle, laughing.", "와들 씨가 웃으며 대답했다."]], [["‘To what?’ inquired Mr.", "\"무엇을 한다고요?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘Why, in plain English, to frighten the rooks.’", "\"아니, 쉬운 말로 하자면, 떼까마귀를 놀라게 하려는 거외다.\""]], [["‘Oh, is that all?’", "\"오, 그게 다요?\""]], [["‘You are satisfied?’", "\"안심이 되시오?\""]], [["‘Quite.’", "\"아주.\""]], [["‘Very well.", "\"좋소이다."], ["Shall I begin?’", "그럼 내가 시작할까요?\""]], [["‘If you please,’ said Mr.", "\"부디 그래 주시오.\""], ["Winkle, glad of any respite.", "윈클 씨가 잠깐의 유예라도 반가운 듯 말했다."]], [["‘Stand aside, then.", "\"그럼 옆으로 비키시오."], ["Now for it.’", "자, 갑니다.\""]], [["The boy shouted, and shook a branch with a nest on it.", "소년이 소리를 지르며 둥지가 달린 나뭇가지를 흔들었다."], ["Half a dozen young rooks in violent conversation, flew out to ask what the matter was.", "어린 떼까마귀 대여섯 마리가 격렬한 대화를 나누다가 무슨 일인가 알아보려 날아올랐다."], ["The old gentleman fired by way of reply.", "노신사가 답례 삼아 발사했다."], ["Down fell one bird, and off flew the others.", "새 한 마리가 떨어지고 나머지는 날아가 버렸다."]], [["‘Take him up, Joe,’ said the old gentleman.", "\"저것을 주워 오너라, 조.\" 노신사가 말했다."]], [["There was a smile upon the youth’s face as he advanced.", "소년이 다가갈 때 그 얼굴에는 미소가 어려 있었다."], ["Indistinct visions of rook-pie floated through his imagination.", "떼까마귀 파이의 어렴풋한 환영이 그의 상상 속을 떠다녔다."], ["He laughed as he retired with the bird--it was a plump one.", "그는 그 새를 들고 물러나며 웃었다. 통통한 놈이었던 것이다."]], [["‘Now, Mr.", "\"자, 윈클 씨.\""], ["Winkle,’ said the host, reloading his own gun.", "주인이 제 총에 다시 장전하며 말했다."], ["‘Fire away.’", "\"쏘아 보시오.\""]], [["Mr. Winkle advanced, and levelled his gun.", "윈클 씨가 앞으로 나서서 총을 겨누었다."], ["Mr. Pickwick and his friends cowered involuntarily to escape damage from the heavy fall of rooks, which they felt quite certain would be occasioned by the devastating barrel of their friend.", "픽윅 씨와 그의 벗들은 자기네 친구의 무시무시한 총신이 틀림없이 떼까마귀를 우수수 떨어뜨리리라 확신하여, 그 묵직한 낙하에서 피해를 면하려고 저도 모르게 몸을 움츠렸다."], ["There was a solemn pause--a shout--a flapping of wings--a faint click.", "엄숙한 정적이 흘렀다—외침이 있었고—날개를 푸드덕거리는 소리가 났고—희미하게 찰칵하는 소리가 났다."]], [["‘Hollo!’ said the old gentleman.", "\"여보시오!\" 노신사가 말했다."]], [["‘Won’t it go?’ inquired Mr.", "\"안 나가오?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘Missed fire,’ said Mr.", "\"불발이오.\" 윈클 씨가 말했다."], ["Winkle, who was very pale--probably from disappointment.", "그는 몹시 창백했는데—아마도 실망 탓이었으리라."]], [["‘Odd,’ said the old gentleman, taking the gun.", "\"이상한데.\" 노신사가 총을 받아 들며 말했다."], ["‘Never knew one of them miss fire before.", "\"이놈이 불발 나는 건 여태 본 적이 없소."], ["Why, I don’t see anything of the cap.’", "아니, 뇌관이 보이질 않는구려.\""]], [["Bless my soul!’ said Mr.", "\"어이쿠, 이런!\" 윈클 씨가 말했다."], ["Winkle, ‘I declare I forgot the cap!’", "\"글쎄, 내가 뇌관을 깜빡했지 뭐요!\""]], [["The slight omission was rectified.", "사소한 누락은 바로잡혔다."], ["Mr. Pickwick crouched again.", "픽윅 씨가 다시 몸을 웅크렸다."], ["Mr. Winkle stepped forward with an air of determination and resolution; and Mr.", "윈클 씨가 결연하고 단호한 기색으로 앞으로 나섰고, 터프먼 씨는 나무 뒤에서 내다보았다."], ["Tupman looked out from behind a tree. The boy shouted; four birds flew out.", "소년이 소리를 질렀고, 새 네 마리가 날아올랐다."], ["Mr. Winkle fired.", "윈클 씨가 발사했다."], ["There was a scream as of an individual--not a rook--in corporal anguish. Mr.", "떼까마귀가 아니라—한 사람이—육체적 고통에 지르는 듯한 비명이 났다."], ["Tupman had saved the lives of innumerable unoffending birds by receiving a portion of the charge in his left arm.", "터프먼 씨가 왼팔에 탄환 일부를 받아 냄으로써, 아무 죄 없는 무수한 새들의 목숨을 구한 것이었다."]], [["To describe the confusion that ensued would be impossible. To tell how Mr. Pickwick in the first transports of emotion called Mr. Winkle ‘Wretch!’ how Mr. Tupman lay prostrate on the ground; and how Mr. Winkle knelt horror-stricken beside him; how Mr. Tupman called distractedly upon some feminine Christian name, and then opened first one eye, and then the other, and then fell back and shut them both--all this would be as difficult to describe in detail, as it would be to depict the gradual recovering of the unfortunate individual, the binding up of his arm with pocket-handkerchiefs, and the conveying him back by slow degrees supported by the arms of his anxious friends.", "뒤이어 벌어진 혼란을 묘사하기란 불가능하리라. 픽윅 씨가 격정의 첫 발작 속에서 윈클 씨를 \"이 못된 자!\"라고 부른 일이며, 터프먼 씨가 땅바닥에 엎어져 누운 일이며, 윈클 씨가 경악하여 그 곁에 무릎 꿇은 일이며, 터프먼 씨가 정신없이 어떤 여성의 세례명을 부르다가, 한쪽 눈을 먼저 뜨고 이어 다른 쪽 눈을 뜬 다음, 다시 뒤로 쓰러지며 두 눈을 모두 감아 버린 일—이 모든 것을 세세히 묘사하기란, 그 불운한 사람이 차차 회복되어 가는 모습과, 손수건으로 그의 팔을 동여매는 모습과, 걱정스러운 벗들의 부축을 받으며 그가 천천히 집으로 옮겨지는 모습을 그려 내기만큼이나 어려운 일일 것이다."]], [["They drew near the house.", "그들은 집 가까이 다가갔다."], ["The ladies were at the garden gate, waiting for their arrival and their breakfast.", "숙녀들이 정원 문에서 그들의 도착과 아침 식사를 기다리고 있었다."], ["The spinster aunt appeared; she smiled, and beckoned them to walk quicker.", "노처녀 이모가 나타났다. 그녀는 미소 지으며 더 빨리 걸어오라고 손짓했다."], ["‘Twas evident she knew not of the disaster.", "그녀가 이 재난을 모르고 있음이 분명했다."], ["Poor thing!", "가엾기도 하지!"], ["there are times when ignorance is bliss indeed.", "모르는 것이 진정 복인 때가 있는 법이다."]], [["They approached nearer.", "그들은 더 가까이 다가갔다."]], [["‘Why, what is the matter with the little old gentleman?’ said Isabella Wardle.", "\"아니, 저 자그마한 노신사분께 무슨 일이 생긴 거죠?\" 이저벨라 와들이 말했다."], ["The spinster aunt heeded not the remark; she thought it applied to Mr.", "노처녀 이모는 그 말을 귀담아듣지 않았다. 그녀는 그 말이 픽윅 씨를 두고 한 것이라 여겼던 것이다."], ["Pickwick. In her eyes Tracy Tupman was a youth; she viewed his years through a diminishing glass.", "그녀의 눈에 트레이시 터프먼은 청년이었으니, 그녀는 그의 나이를 축소경을 통해 바라보았다."]], [["‘Don’t be frightened,’ called out the old host, fearful of alarming his daughters.", "\"놀라지들 마라.\" 노주인이 딸들이 놀랄까 두려워 소리쳤다."], ["The little party had crowded so completely round Mr. Tupman, that they could not yet clearly discern the nature of the accident.", "작은 무리가 터프먼 씨 주위로 어찌나 빽빽이 모여들었던지, 그들은 아직 사고의 정체를 또렷이 분간할 수 없었다."]], [["‘Don’t be frightened,’ said the host.", "\"놀라지들 마라.\" 주인이 말했다."]], [["‘What’s the matter?’ screamed the ladies.", "\"무슨 일이에요?\" 숙녀들이 비명을 질렀다."]], [["‘Mr.", "\"터프먼 씨가 작은 사고를 당했단다."], ["Tupman has met with a little accident; that’s all.’", "그게 다야.\""]], [["The spinster aunt uttered a piercing scream, burst into an hysteric laugh, and fell backwards in the arms of her nieces.", "노처녀 이모는 날카로운 비명을 지르더니, 히스테릭한 웃음을 터뜨리며 조카딸들의 품 안으로 뒤로 쓰러졌다."]], [["‘Throw some cold water over her,’ said the old gentleman.", "\"찬물을 좀 끼얹어 줘라.\" 노신사가 말했다."]], [["‘No, no,’ murmured the spinster aunt; ‘I am better now.", "\"아니, 아니야.\" 노처녀 이모가 중얼거렸다."], ["Bella, Emily--a surgeon!", "\"이제 좀 나아졌다. 벨라, 에밀리—의사를!"], ["Is he wounded?--Is he dead?--Is he--Ha, ha, ha!’ Here the spinster aunt burst into fit number two, of hysteric laughter interspersed with screams.", "그분 부상당하셨니?—돌아가셨니?—그분이—하, 하, 하!\" 여기서 노처녀 이모는 비명 사이사이로 히스테릭한 웃음을 터뜨리는 두 번째 발작에 빠져들었다."]], [["‘Calm yourself,’ said Mr.", "\"진정하십시오.\""], ["Tupman, affected almost to tears by this expression of sympathy with his sufferings.", "터프먼 씨가 제 고통에 대한 이 동정의 표현에 거의 눈물이 날 만큼 감동하여 말했다."], ["‘Dear, dear madam, calm yourself.’", "\"친애하는, 친애하는 부인, 진정하십시오.\""]], [["‘It is his voice!’ exclaimed the spinster aunt; and strong symptoms of fit number three developed themselves forthwith.", "\"그분의 목소리야!\" 노처녀 이모가 외쳤고, 곧바로 세 번째 발작의 강한 징후가 나타났다."]], [["‘Do not agitate yourself, I entreat you, dearest madam,’ said Mr.", "\"동요하지 마십시오, 간청하건대, 가장 사랑하는 부인.\""], ["Tupman soothingly.", "터프먼 씨가 어르듯 말했다."], ["‘I am very little hurt, I assure you.’", "\"맹세코 저는 거의 다치지 않았습니다.\""]], [["‘Then you are not dead!’ ejaculated the hysterical lady.", "\"그럼 돌아가신 게 아니로군요!\" 히스테리에 빠진 숙녀가 외쳤다."], ["‘Oh, say you are not dead!’", "\"오, 돌아가시지 않았다고 말해 주세요!\""]], [["‘Don’t be a fool, Rachael,’ interposed Mr.", "\"바보같이 굴지 마라, 레이철.\""], ["Wardle, rather more roughly than was consistent with the poetic nature of the scene.", "와들 씨가, 이 장면의 시적인 본질에 어울리는 것보다는 다소 거칠게 끼어들었다."], ["‘What the devil’s the use of his saying he isn’t dead?’", "\"그가 죽지 않았다고 말하는 게 도대체 무슨 소용이란 말이냐?\""]], [["‘No, no, I am not,’ said Mr.", "\"아니, 아닙니다, 죽지 않았습니다.\""], ["Tupman.", "터프먼 씨가 말했다."], ["‘I require no assistance but yours.", "\"부인의 도움 말고는 아무것도 필요치 않습니다. 부인의 팔에 기대게 해 주십시오.\""], ["Let me lean on your arm.’ He added, in a whisper, ‘Oh, Miss Rachael!’ The agitated female advanced, and offered her arm.", "그는 속삭이듯 덧붙였다. \"오, 레이철 양!\""], ["They turned into the breakfast parlour. Mr.", "동요한 여성이 다가와 팔을 내밀었다."], ["Tracy Tupman gently pressed her hand to his lips, and sank upon the sofa.", "그들은 아침 식사방으로 들어섰다. 트레이시 터프먼 씨는 그녀의 손을 가만히 입술에 갖다 대고는 소파에 주저앉았다."]], [["‘Are you faint?’ inquired the anxious Rachael.", "\"기절하시겠어요?\" 근심에 찬 레이철이 물었다."]], [["‘No,’ said Mr.", "\"아닙니다.\" 터프먼 씨가 말했다."], ["Tupman. ‘It is nothing.", "\"아무것도 아닙니다."], ["I shall be better presently.’ He closed his eyes.", "곧 나아질 겁니다.\" 그는 눈을 감았다."]], [["‘He sleeps,’ murmured the spinster aunt.", "\"주무시는구나.\" 노처녀 이모가 중얼거렸다."], ["(His organs of vision had been closed nearly twenty seconds.) ‘Dear--dear--Mr. Tupman!’", "(그의 시각 기관은 채 이십 초도 감겨 있지 않았다.) \"친애하는—친애하는—터프먼 씨!\""]], [["Mr.", "터프먼 씨가 벌떡 일어났다."], ["Tupman jumped up--‘Oh, say those words again!’ he exclaimed.", "\"오, 그 말을 다시 한 번 해 주십시오!\" 그가 외쳤다."]], [["The lady started.", "숙녀가 흠칫했다."], ["‘Surely you did not hear them!’ she said bashfully.", "\"설마 그 말을 들으신 건 아니겠지요!\" 그녀가 부끄러운 듯 말했다."]], [["‘Oh, yes, I did!’ replied Mr.", "\"오, 아니요, 들었습니다!\" 터프먼 씨가 대답했다."], ["Tupman; ‘repeat them.", "\"그 말을 다시 해 주십시오."], ["If you would have me recover, repeat them.’", "제가 회복되길 바라신다면, 다시 한 번 해 주십시오.\""]], [["Hush!’ said the lady.", "\"쉿!\" 숙녀가 말했다."], ["‘My brother.’ Mr.", "\"오라버니예요.\""], ["Tracy Tupman resumed his former position; and Mr. Wardle, accompanied by a surgeon, entered the room.", "트레이시 터프먼 씨는 본래 자세로 돌아갔고, 와들 씨가 의사를 데리고 방으로 들어왔다."]], [["The arm was examined, the wound dressed, and pronounced to be a very slight one; and the minds of the company having been thus satisfied, they proceeded to satisfy their appetites with countenances to which an expression of cheerfulness was again restored.", "팔을 살펴보고, 상처를 처치하고, 아주 가벼운 부상이라는 진단이 내려졌다. 일행의 마음이 이렇게 안심되자, 그들은 다시 쾌활함이 되살아난 표정으로 자신들의 식욕을 채우러 나섰다."], ["Mr. Pickwick alone was silent and reserved.", "오직 픽윅 씨만이 말이 없고 무뚝뚝했다."], ["Doubt and distrust were exhibited in his countenance.", "그의 얼굴에는 의심과 불신이 드러나 있었다."], ["His confidence in Mr. Winkle had been shaken--greatly shaken--by the proceedings of the morning.", "윈클 씨에 대한 그의 신뢰는 그날 아침의 일로 흔들려 버렸던 것이다—크게 흔들려 버렸던 것이다."]], [["‘Are you a cricketer?’ inquired Mr.", "\"댁은 크리켓 선수십니까?\""], ["Wardle of the marksman.", "와들 씨가 그 명사수에게 물었다."]], [["At any other time, Mr. Winkle would have replied in the affirmative.", "다른 때였다면 윈클 씨는 그렇다고 대답했으리라."], ["He felt the delicacy of his situation, and modestly replied, ‘No.’", "그러나 그는 제 처지의 미묘함을 느끼고 겸손하게 대답했다. \"아니요.\""]], [["‘Are you, sir?’ inquired Mr.", "\"댁은 어떠시오, 선생?\""], ["Snodgrass.", "스노드그래스 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘I was once upon a time,’ replied the host; ‘but I have given it up now.", "\"한때는 했었지요.\" 주인이 대답했다."], ["I subscribe to the club here, but I don’t play.’", "\"그러나 이제는 그만뒀소. 이곳 클럽에 회비는 내지만, 직접 뛰지는 않소이다.\""]], [["‘The grand match is played to-day, I believe,’ said Mr.", "\"오늘 큰 시합이 열린다지요.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘It is,’ replied the host.", "\"그렇소.\" 주인이 대답했다."], ["‘Of course you would like to see it.’", "\"물론 보고 싶으시겠지요.\""]], [["‘I, sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"저로 말하자면, 선생.\""], ["Pickwick, ‘am delighted to view any sports which may be safely indulged in, and in which the impotent effects of unskilful people do not endanger human life.’ Mr.", "픽윅 씨가 대답했다. \"안전하게 즐길 수 있고, 미숙한 자들의 무력한 행위로 인명이 위태로워지지 않는 운동이라면 무엇이든 기꺼이 구경하렵니다.\""], ["Pickwick paused, and looked steadily on Mr. Winkle, who quailed beneath his leader’s searching glance.", "픽윅 씨는 말을 멈추고 윈클 씨를 빤히 바라보았고, 윈클 씨는 그 단장의 꿰뚫는 시선 아래 움츠러들었다."], ["The great man withdrew his eyes after a few minutes, and added: ‘Shall we be justified in leaving our wounded friend to the care of the ladies?’", "그 위인은 몇 분 뒤 눈길을 거두며 덧붙였다. \"부상당한 우리 벗을 숙녀분들께 맡기고 떠나도 무방하겠소?\""]], [["‘You cannot leave me in better hands,’ said Mr.", "\"이보다 더 좋은 손길에 맡길 수는 없을 겁니다.\""], ["Tupman.", "터프먼 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Quite impossible,’ said Mr.", "\"전혀 불가능하지요.\""], ["Snodgrass.", "스노드그래스 씨가 말했다."]], [["It was therefore settled that Mr. Tupman should be left at home in charge of the females; and that the remainder of the guests, under the guidance of Mr. Wardle, should proceed to the spot where was to be held that trial of skill, which had roused all Muggleton from its torpor, and inoculated Dingley Dell with a fever of excitement.", "이리하여 터프먼 씨는 집에 남아 여성들의 보살핌을 받기로 결정되었고, 나머지 손님들은 와들 씨의 안내에 따라, 머글턴 전체를 무기력에서 흔들어 깨우고 딩글리 델에 흥분의 열병을 옮긴 그 솜씨 겨루기가 열릴 장소로 향하기로 했다."]], [["As their walk, which was not above two miles long, lay through shady lanes and sequestered footpaths, and as their conversation turned upon the delightful scenery by which they were on every side surrounded, Mr. Pickwick was almost inclined to regret the expedition they had used, when he found himself in the main street of the town of Muggleton.", "그들이 걷는 길은 채 이 마일도 되지 않았고, 그늘진 오솔길과 한적한 보행로를 따라 나 있었으며, 그들의 대화는 사방에서 그들을 에워싼 아름다운 풍경으로 흘러갔던 터라, 픽윅 씨는 머글턴 읍의 큰길에 들어서고 보니 자신들이 그토록 서두른 것이 거의 후회스러울 지경이었다."]], [["Everybody whose genius has a topographical bent knows perfectly well that Muggleton is a corporate town, with a mayor, burgesses, and freemen; and anybody who has consulted the addresses of the mayor to the freemen, or the freemen to the mayor, or both to the corporation, or all three to Parliament, will learn from thence what they ought to have known before, that Muggleton is an ancient and loyal borough, mingling a zealous advocacy of Christian principles with a devoted attachment to commercial rights; in demonstration whereof, the mayor, corporation, and other inhabitants, have presented at divers times, no fewer than one thousand four hundred and twenty petitions against the continuance of negro slavery abroad, and an equal number against any interference with the factory system at home; sixty-eight in favour of the sale of livings in the Church, and eighty-six for abolishing Sunday trading in the street.", "지형학적 성향의 재능을 지닌 사람이라면 누구나 익히 알고 있듯이, 머글턴은 시장과 자치시민과 자유시민을 거느린 자치 읍이다. 그리고 시장이 자유시민들에게, 혹은 자유시민들이 시장에게, 혹은 양측이 자치체에, 혹은 셋 모두가 의회에 올린 청원서들을 들춰 본 사람이라면 누구나, 진작 알았어야 할 사실을 거기서 깨우치게 될 것이다. 즉 머글턴은 유서 깊고 충성스러운 자치구로서, 기독교 원리에 대한 열렬한 옹호와 상업적 권리에 대한 헌신적인 집착을 한데 섞은 곳이라는 사실이다. 그 증거로, 시장과 자치체와 그 밖의 주민들은 여러 차례에 걸쳐, 외국에서의 흑인 노예제 존속에 반대하는 청원서를 자그마치 천사백스무 통이나 올렸고, 국내 공장 제도에 대한 어떠한 간섭에도 반대하는 청원서 역시 같은 수만큼 올렸으며, 교회 성직록 매매를 찬성하는 청원서 예순여덟 통과, 거리에서의 일요일 장사 폐지를 찬성하는 청원서 여든여섯 통을 올렸던 것이다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick stood in the principal street of this illustrious town, and gazed with an air of curiosity, not unmixed with interest, on the objects around him.", "픽윅 씨는 이 저명한 읍의 중심가에 서서, 흥미가 섞이지 않은 것도 아닌 호기심 어린 기색으로 주위의 사물들을 바라보았다."], ["There was an open square for the market-place; and in the centre of it, a large inn with a sign-post in front, displaying an object very common in art, but rarely met with in nature--to wit, a blue lion, with three bow legs in the air, balancing himself on the extreme point of the centre claw of his fourth foot.", "시장으로 쓰이는 트인 광장이 있었고, 그 한가운데에는 앞에 간판 기둥을 세운 커다란 여관이 있었는데, 그 간판에는 예술에서는 매우 흔하지만 자연에서는 좀처럼 만날 수 없는 형상이 그려져 있었다. 다름 아니라, 휘어진 세 다리를 공중에 쳐들고, 넷째 발의 가운데 발톱 맨 끝점 위에서 균형을 잡고 선 푸른 사자였다."], ["There were, within sight, an auctioneer’s and fire-agency office, a corn-factor’s, a linen- draper’s, a saddler’s, a distiller’s, a grocer’s, and a shoe-shop--the last-mentioned warehouse being also appropriated to the diffusion of hats, bonnets, wearing apparel, cotton umbrellas, and useful knowledge.", "눈에 들어오는 범위 안에는 경매인 겸 화재 대리점 사무소, 곡물 중개상, 직물상, 마구상, 양조장, 식료품점, 그리고 신발 가게가 있었다. 끝에 언급한 그 창고는 모자, 보닛, 의류, 면 우산, 그리고 유익한 지식을 보급하는 데에도 함께 쓰이고 있었다."], ["There was a red brick house with a small paved courtyard in front, which anybody might have known belonged to the attorney; and there was, moreover, another red brick house with Venetian blinds, and a large brass door-plate with a very legible announcement that it belonged to the surgeon.", "앞에 자그마한 포장된 안마당을 둔 붉은 벽돌집이 있었는데, 누가 보아도 변호사의 집임을 알 만했다. 게다가 베네치아풍 블라인드를 단 또 다른 붉은 벽돌집이 있었으니, 커다란 놋쇠 문패에 아주 또렷하게 외과의의 집이라는 안내가 새겨져 있었다."], ["A few boys were making their way to the cricket-field; and two or three shopkeepers who were standing at their doors looked as if they should like to be making their way to the same spot, as indeed to all appearance they might have done, without losing any great amount of custom thereby. Mr.", "소년 몇이 크리켓 경기장으로 향하고 있었고, 가게 문간에 서 있던 두세 명의 상인들도 같은 곳으로 가고 싶어 하는 기색이었는데, 사실 보아하니 그들이 그리 간다 한들 그로 인해 장사를 크게 잃을 것 같지도 않았다."], ["Pickwick having paused to make these observations, to be noted down at a more convenient period, hastened to rejoin his friends, who had turned out of the main street, and were already within sight of the field of battle.", "픽윅 씨는 좀 더 한가한 때에 적어 두려고 이런 관찰을 하느라 걸음을 멈추었다가, 큰길에서 벗어나 이미 전장이 눈에 들어오는 곳까지 가 있던 벗들과 다시 합류하려고 서둘렀다."]], [["The wickets were pitched, and so were a couple of marquees for the rest and refreshment of the contending parties.", "위킷이 세워져 있었고, 겨루는 양편의 휴식과 다과를 위한 천막 두 개도 마찬가지로 쳐져 있었다."], ["The game had not yet commenced.", "시합은 아직 시작되지 않았다."], ["Two or three Dingley Dellers, and All-Muggletonians, were amusing themselves with a majestic air by throwing the ball carelessly from hand to hand; and several other gentlemen dressed like them, in straw hats, flannel jackets, and white trousers--a costume in which they looked very much like amateur stone-masons--were sprinkled about the tents, towards one of which Mr.", "두세 명의 딩글리 델 사람들과 올머글턴 사람들이 위풍당당한 기색으로 공을 이 손 저 손으로 무심히 던지며 즐기고 있었고, 그들처럼 밀짚모자에 플란넬 재킷, 흰 바지 차림을 한—그 차림새 때문에 영락없이 아마추어 석공처럼 보이는—여러 신사들이 천막 주위에 흩어져 있었다."], ["Wardle conducted the party.", "와들 씨는 일행을 그 천막 가운데 하나로 이끌었다."]], [["Several dozen of ‘How-are-you’s?’ hailed the old gentleman’s arrival; and a general raising of the straw hats, and bending forward of the flannel jackets, followed his introduction of his guests as gentlemen from London, who were extremely anxious to witness the proceedings of the day, with which, he had no doubt, they would be greatly delighted.", "\"안녕하시오\" 하는 소리 수십 마디가 노신사의 도착을 반겼다. 그가 자기 손님들을, 오늘의 경기를 몹시 보고 싶어 하는, 또 틀림없이 크게 즐거워할 런던 출신 신사들이라고 소개하자, 그에 이어 밀짚모자들이 일제히 들어 올려지고 플란넬 재킷들이 앞으로 굽혀졌다."]], [["‘You had better step into the marquee, I think, Sir,’ said one very stout gentleman, whose body and legs looked like half a gigantic roll of flannel, elevated on a couple of inflated pillow-cases.", "\"천막 안으로 드시는 게 좋겠습니다, 선생.\" 매우 뚱뚱한 신사 하나가 말했는데, 그 몸통과 다리는 마치 부풀린 베갯잇 두 개 위에 올려놓은 거대한 플란넬 두루마리의 반쪽처럼 보였다."]], [["‘You’ll find it much pleasanter, Sir,’ urged another stout gentleman, who strongly resembled the other half of the roll of flannel aforesaid.", "\"훨씬 쾌적하실 겁니다, 선생.\" 또 다른 뚱뚱한 신사가 권했는데, 그는 앞서 말한 플란넬 두루마리의 나머지 반쪽과 흡사했다."]], [["‘You’re very good,’ said Mr.", "\"친절도 하시오.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘This way,’ said the first speaker; ‘they notch in here--it’s the best place in the whole field;’ and the cricketer, panting on before, preceded them to the tent.", "\"이쪽입니다.\" 먼저 말한 사람이 말했다. \"여기서 점수를 기록하지요—경기장 전체에서 제일 좋은 자리외다.\" 그러고는 그 크리켓 선수가 앞장서서 헐떡이며 그들을 천막으로 안내했다."]], [["‘Capital game--smart sport--fine exercise--very,’ were the words which fell upon Mr.", "\"훌륭한 경기—신나는 운동—멋진 단련—아주.\" 픽윅 씨가 천막에 들어서자 그의 귀에 떨어진 말들이 이러했다."], ["Pickwick’s ear as he entered the tent; and the first object that met his eyes was his green-coated friend of the Rochester coach, holding forth, to the no small delight and edification of a select circle of the chosen of All-Muggleton.", "그리고 그의 눈에 처음 들어온 것은 로체스터행 마차에서 만난 초록 외투의 친구였으니, 그는 올머글턴의 선택받은 자들로 이루어진 한정된 무리에게 적잖은 기쁨과 교화를 베풀며 열변을 토하고 있었다."], ["His dress was slightly improved, and he wore boots; but there was no mistaking him.", "옷차림은 조금 나아졌고 장화를 신고 있었으나, 그를 못 알아볼 수는 없었다."]], [["The stranger recognised his friends immediately; and, darting forward and seizing Mr.", "그 낯선 자는 즉시 자기 벗들을 알아보았고, 앞으로 돌진해 픽윅 씨의 손을 붙잡더니 여느 때처럼 격렬하게 그를 자리로 끌어당겼다."], ["Pickwick by the hand, dragged him to a seat with his usual impetuosity, talking all the while as if the whole of the arrangements were under his especial patronage and direction.", "그러면서 그는 마치 이 모든 준비가 자신의 특별한 후원과 지휘 아래 이루어지기라도 한 듯 줄곧 떠들어 댔다."]], [["‘This way--this way--capital fun--lots of beer--hogsheads; rounds of beef--bullocks; mustard--cart-loads; glorious day--down with you--make yourself at home--glad to see you--very.’", "\"이쪽—이쪽으로—굉장한 재미—맥주 천지—통째로 들여놨소. 소고기 덩어리—황소 통째로. 겨자—수레로 한가득. 영광스러운 날—앉으시오—편히 계시오—만나서 반갑소—아주.\""]], [["Mr. Pickwick sat down as he was bid, and Mr. Winkle and Mr. Snodgrass also complied with the directions of their mysterious friend. Mr. Wardle looked on in silent wonder.", "픽윅 씨는 시키는 대로 앉았고, 윈클 씨와 스노드그래스 씨도 그 수수께끼 같은 벗의 지시에 따랐다. 와들 씨는 말없이 의아한 듯 지켜보았다."]], [["‘Mr. Wardle--a friend of mine,’ said Mr.", "\"와들 씨—내 친구요.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Friend of yours!--My dear sir, how are you?--Friend of my friend’s-- give me your hand, sir’--and the stranger grasped Mr.", "\"댁의 친구!—이보시오, 안녕하십니까?—내 친구의 친구라—손 좀 주시오, 선생.\""], ["Wardle’s hand with all the fervour of a close intimacy of many years, and then stepped back a pace or two as if to take a full survey of his face and figure, and then shook hands with him again, if possible, more warmly than before.", "그러더니 낯선 자는 여러 해 묵은 막역한 친분의 온갖 열정을 담아 와들 씨의 손을 움켜쥐었고, 이내 그의 얼굴과 풍채를 찬찬히 살펴보려는 듯 한두 걸음 뒤로 물러섰다가, 다시 한 번, 가능한 일이라면, 아까보다 더 따뜻하게 그와 악수했다."]], [["‘Well; and how came you here?’ said Mr.", "\"그래, 어쩌다 여기까지 오셨소?\""], ["Pickwick, with a smile in which benevolence struggled with surprise.", "픽윅 씨가, 자비심이 놀라움과 다투는 미소를 띠며 말했다."]], [["‘Come,’ replied the stranger--‘stopping at Crown--Crown at Muggleton-- met a party--flannel jackets--white trousers--anchovy sandwiches-- devilled kidney--splendid fellows--glorious.’", "\"왔지요.\" 낯선 자가 대답했다. \"크라운에 묵고 있소—머글턴의 크라운에—한 무리를 만났는데—플란넬 재킷에—흰 바지에—멸치 샌드위치에—양념 콩팥구이에—멋진 친구들—영광스럽소.\""]], [["Mr. Pickwick was sufficiently versed in the stranger’s system of stenography to infer from this rapid and disjointed communication that he had, somehow or other, contracted an acquaintance with the All- Muggletons, which he had converted, by a process peculiar to himself, into that extent of good-fellowship on which a general invitation may be easily founded.", "픽윅 씨는 낯선 자의 속기법 체계에 충분히 정통해 있던 터라, 이 빠르고 단편적인 통보에서, 그가 어찌어찌 올머글턴 사람들과 안면을 텄고, 그것을 자기 특유의 과정을 거쳐 일반적인 초대가 손쉽게 성립될 만한 정도의 친분으로 변모시켰음을 추론해 냈다."], ["His curiosity was therefore satisfied, and putting on his spectacles he prepared himself to watch the play which was just commencing.", "이리하여 그의 호기심은 충족되었고, 그는 안경을 쓰고 막 시작되려는 경기를 지켜볼 채비를 했다."]], [["All-Muggleton had the first innings; and the interest became intense when Mr.", "올머글턴이 먼저 공격에 나섰다."], ["Dumkins and Mr. Podder, two of the most renowned members of that most distinguished club, walked, bat in hand, to their respective wickets.", "그 가장 저명한 클럽에서도 가장 이름난 회원에 속하는 덤킨스 씨와 포더 씨가 배트를 손에 들고 각자의 위킷으로 걸어 나가자, 흥미는 격렬해졌다."], ["Mr. Luffey, the highest ornament of Dingley Dell, was pitched to bowl against the redoubtable Dumkins, and Mr.", "딩글리 델의 으뜸가는 명예인 러피 씨가 가공할 덤킨스를 상대로 투구를 맡았고, 스트러글스 씨가 여태껏 무패였던 포더에게 똑같은 임무를 수행하도록 선발되었다."], ["Struggles was selected to do the same kind office for the hitherto unconquered Podder.", "여러 선수들이 경기장 곳곳에 \"수비를 보려고\""], ["Several players were stationed, to ‘look out,’ in different parts of the field, and each fixed himself into the proper attitude by placing one hand on each knee, and stooping very much as if he were ‘making a back’ for some beginner at leap-frog.", "배치되었는데, 저마다 양손을 양 무릎에 짚고, 마치 말타기 놀이를 처음 하는 초보자를 위해 \"등을 내주듯\" 잔뜩 허리를 구부려 적절한 자세를 잡았다."], ["All the regular players do this sort of thing;-- indeed it is generally supposed that it is quite impossible to look out properly in any other position.", "정규 선수들은 다들 이런 식으로 한다. 사실 다른 자세로는 제대로 수비를 본다는 것이 도무지 불가능하다는 것이 일반적인 생각이다."]], [["The umpires were stationed behind the wickets; the scorers were prepared to notch the runs; a breathless silence ensued.", "심판들은 위킷 뒤에 배치되었고, 기록원들은 득점을 새길 채비를 했으며, 숨 막히는 정적이 흘렀다."], ["Mr. Luffey retired a few paces behind the wicket of the passive Podder, and applied the ball to his right eye for several seconds.", "러피 씨는 무심한 포더의 위킷 뒤로 몇 걸음 물러나, 몇 초 동안 공을 자기 오른쪽 눈에 갖다 댔다."], ["Dumkins confidently awaited its coming with his eyes fixed on the motions of Luffey.", "덤킨스는 러피의 동작에 시선을 고정한 채 자신만만하게 공이 날아오기를 기다렸다."]], [["‘Play!’ suddenly cried the bowler.", "\"플레이!\" 투수가 별안간 외쳤다."], ["The ball flew from his hand straight and swift towards the centre stump of the wicket.", "공이 그의 손에서 곧고 빠르게 위킷의 가운데 기둥을 향해 날아갔다."], ["The wary Dumkins was on the alert: it fell upon the tip of the bat, and bounded far away over the heads of the scouts, who had just stooped low enough to let it fly over them.", "경계를 늦추지 않던 덤킨스는 만반의 태세였다. 공은 배트 끝에 맞아, 막 공을 머리 위로 넘기려고 몸을 낮추던 수비수들의 머리 위를 멀리 날아 넘어 튀어 갔다."]], [["‘Run--run--another.--Now, then throw her up--up with her--stop there-- another--no--yes--no--throw her up, throw her up!’--Such were the shouts which followed the stroke; and at the conclusion of which All-Muggleton had scored two.", "\"뛰어—뛰어—한 점 더.—자, 이제 던져 올려—던져 올려—거기 서—한 점 더—아니—그래—아니—던져 올려, 던져 올려!\" 그 타격에 뒤이은 외침이 이러했고, 그것이 끝났을 때 올머글턴은 두 점을 기록했다."], ["Nor was Podder behindhand in earning laurels wherewith to garnish himself and Muggleton.", "포더 역시 자신과 머글턴을 빛낼 영예를 얻는 데 뒤지지 않았다."], ["He blocked the doubtful balls, missed the bad ones, took the good ones, and sent them flying to all parts of the field.", "그는 미심쩍은 공은 막아 내고, 나쁜 공은 그냥 흘려보내고, 좋은 공은 받아쳐 경기장 사방으로 날려 보냈다."], ["The scouts were hot and tired; the bowlers were changed and bowled till their arms ached; but Dumkins and Podder remained unconquered.", "수비수들은 덥고 지쳐 갔으며, 투수들은 교체되어 팔이 쑤시도록 공을 던졌으나, 덤킨스와 포더는 여전히 무적이었다."], ["Did an elderly gentleman essay to stop the progress of the ball, it rolled between his legs or slipped between his fingers.", "어느 나이 든 신사가 공의 진로를 막아 보려 하면, 공은 그의 다리 사이로 굴러가거나 손가락 사이로 빠져나갔다."], ["Did a slim gentleman try to catch it, it struck him on the nose, and bounded pleasantly off with redoubled violence, while the slim gentleman’s eyes filled with water, and his form writhed with anguish.", "어느 호리호리한 신사가 그것을 잡으려 하면, 공은 그의 코를 때리고 갑절로 거세게 유쾌하게 튕겨 나갔으며, 그러는 사이 호리호리한 신사의 두 눈에는 눈물이 그렁그렁 고이고 그 몸뚱이는 고통에 몸부림쳤다."], ["Was it thrown straight up to the wicket, Dumkins had reached it before the ball.", "위킷을 향해 곧장 던져 올려도, 덤킨스가 공보다 먼저 그곳에 닿아 있었다."], ["In short, when Dumkins was caught out, and Podder stumped out, All- Muggleton had notched some fifty-four, while the score of the Dingley Dellers was as blank as their faces.", "요컨대, 덤킨스가 잡혀 아웃되고 포더가 스텀프 아웃되었을 때, 올머글턴은 쉰넷쯤을 기록했고, 딩글리 델 사람들의 점수는 그들의 표정만큼이나 공백이었다."], ["The advantage was too great to be recovered.", "그 격차는 너무 커서 만회할 수 없었다."], ["In vain did the eager Luffey, and the enthusiastic Struggles, do all that skill and experience could suggest, to regain the ground Dingley Dell had lost in the contest--it was of no avail; and in an early period of the winning game Dingley Dell gave in, and allowed the superior prowess of All-Muggleton.", "열성적인 러피와 열광적인 스트러글스가 시합에서 딩글리 델이 잃은 기반을 되찾으려 솜씨와 경험이 시킬 수 있는 온갖 짓을 다해 보았으나 헛수고였다. 이기는 경기가 일찌감치 진행되는 가운데 딩글리 델은 항복하고, 올머글턴의 우월한 기량을 인정했다."]], [["The stranger, meanwhile, had been eating, drinking, and talking, without cessation. At every good stroke he expressed his satisfaction and approval of the player in a most condescending and patronising manner, which could not fail to have been highly gratifying to the party concerned; while at every bad attempt at a catch, and every failure to stop the ball, he launched his personal displeasure at the head of the devoted individual in such denunciations as--‘Ah, ah!--stupid’--‘Now, butter-fingers’--‘Muff’--‘Humbug’--and so forth--ejaculations which seemed to establish him in the opinion of all around, as a most excellent and undeniable judge of the whole art and mystery of the noble game of cricket.", "그러는 동안 낯선 자는 쉴 새 없이 먹고 마시고 떠들어 댔다. 좋은 타격이 나올 때마다 그는 더없이 거들먹거리고 생색내는 태도로 그 선수에 대한 만족과 찬사를 표했으니, 그것이 당사자에게 무척 흐뭇하게 받아들여졌으리라는 것은 의심할 여지가 없었다. 한편 공을 잡으려다 서투르게 실패할 때마다, 또 공을 막지 못할 때마다, 그는 그 가엾은 당사자의 머리에다 이런 식의 비난을 퍼부으며 개인적인 불만을 터뜨렸다. \"아아, 아아!—멍청이 같으니\"—\"이런, 손에 버터를 발랐나\"—\"얼간이\"—\"엉터리\"—따위였다. 이런 외침들 덕분에 그는 주위 사람들 모두에게, 저 고귀한 크리켓 경기의 온갖 기예와 비법을 꿰뚫은 더없이 탁월하고 나무랄 데 없는 심판관이라는 인상을 굳히는 듯했다."]], [["‘Capital game--well played--some strokes admirable,’ said the stranger, as both sides crowded into the tent, at the conclusion of the game.", "\"훌륭한 경기—잘들 했소—몇몇 타격은 감탄할 만했소.\" 경기가 끝나고 양편이 천막 안으로 몰려들 때 낯선 자가 말했다."]], [["‘You have played it, sir?’ inquired Mr.", "\"댁도 해 보셨소, 선생?\""], ["Wardle, who had been much amused by his loquacity.", "그의 수다에 무척 재미있어하던 와들 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘Played it!", "\"해 봤느냐고요!"], ["Think I have--thousands of times--not here--West Indies-- exciting thing--hot work--very.’ ‘It must be rather a warm pursuit in such a climate,’ observed Mr.", "해 봤다마다요—수천 번도 넘게—여기서는 아니고—서인도제도에서—짜릿한 일이지요—후끈한 일—아주.\" \"그런 기후에서라면 꽤나 더운 일이겠소이다.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Warm!--red hot--scorching--glowing.", "\"덥다고요!—새빨갛게 달아오르지요—타들어 가는—이글거리는."], ["Played a match once--single wicket- -friend the colonel--Sir Thomas Blazo--who should get the greatest number of runs.--Won the toss--first innings--seven o’clock A.M.--six natives to look out--went in; kept in--heat intense--natives all fainted--taken away--fresh half-dozen ordered--fainted also--Blazo bowling--supported by two natives--couldn’t bowl me out--fainted too-- cleared away the colonel--wouldn’t give in--faithful attendant--Quanko Samba--last man left--sun so hot, bat in blisters, ball scorched brown-- five hundred and seventy runs--rather exhausted--Quanko mustered up last remaining strength--bowled me out--had a bath, and went out to dinner.’", "한번은 시합을 했지요—단일 위킷으로—친구인 대령과—토머스 블레이조 경 말이오—누가 더 많은 점수를 내는가 겨뤘소.—동전 던지기에서 이겨—먼저 공격—오전 일곱 시에—수비 볼 원주민 여섯 명—타석에 섰소. 계속 버텼지요—더위가 지독해서—원주민들이 모두 졸도—실려 나가고—새로 여섯 명 더 불렀는데—그자들도 졸도—블레이조가 투구—원주민 둘이 부축—날 아웃시키지 못하고—그자도 졸도—대령을 치워 내고—난 굴복하지 않았소—충직한 시중꾼이—콴코 삼바라는 자—마지막 남은 자였소—해가 어찌나 뜨거운지, 배트에 물집이 잡히고, 공은 갈색으로 그을렸지요—오백칠십 점—꽤 지쳤소—콴코가 마지막 남은 힘을 그러모아—날 아웃시켰소—목욕하고 저녁 먹으러 나갔지요.\""]], [["‘And what became of what’s-his-name, Sir?’ inquired an old gentleman.", "\"그래서, 그 뭐라더라, 그자는 어찌 됐소, 선생?\""], ["‘Blazo?’", "어느 나이 든 신사가 물었다."]], [["‘No--the other gentleman.’", "\"블레이조 말이오?\""]], [["Quanko Samba?’", "\"아니—그 다른 분.\""]], [["‘Yes, sir.’", "\"콴코 삼바?\""]], [["‘Poor Quanko--never recovered it--bowled on, on my account--bowled off, on his own--died, sir.’ Here the stranger buried his countenance in a brown jug, but whether to hide his emotion or imbibe its contents, we cannot distinctly affirm.", "\"그렇소, 선생.\" \"가엾은 콴코—끝내 회복하지 못했소—나 때문에 던지고 또 던지다가—제 운명도 던져 버린 거지요—죽었소이다, 선생.\""], ["We only know that he paused suddenly, drew a long and deep breath, and looked anxiously on, as two of the principal members of the Dingley Dell club approached Mr.", "여기서 낯선 자는 갈색 항아리에 얼굴을 파묻었는데, 그것이 제 감정을 감추려 함인지 그 내용물을 들이켜려 함인지 우리로서는 분명히 단언할 수 없다. 다만 그가 별안간 말을 멈추고, 길고 깊은 숨을 한 번 들이쉬며, 딩글리 델 클럽의 주요 회원 둘이 픽윅 씨에게 다가오는 것을 근심스럽게 지켜보았다는 것만은 우리도 안다."], ["Pickwick, and said--", "그 두 사람은 이렇게 말했다."]], [["‘We are about to partake of a plain dinner at the Blue Lion, Sir; we hope you and your friends will join us.’", "\"저희가 블루 라이언에서 소박한 만찬을 들려고 합니다, 선생. 댁과 댁의 벗들께서도 함께해 주시기를 바랍니다.\""]], [["Of course,’ said Mr.", "\"물론이지요.\" 와들 씨가 말했다."], ["Wardle, ‘among our friends we include Mr.--;’ and he looked towards the stranger.", "\"우리 벗들 가운데는 저—저분도 포함됩니다.\" 그러고는 낯선 자 쪽을 바라보았다."]], [["‘Jingle,’ said that versatile gentleman, taking the hint at once.", "\"징글이오.\" 그 임기응변에 능한 신사가 즉시 눈치채고 말했다."], ["‘Jingle--Alfred Jingle, Esq., of No Hall, Nowhere.’", "\"징글—앨프리드 징글, '아무 저택 없음, 아무 데도 없음' 거주.\""]], [["‘I shall be very happy, I am sure,’ said Mr.", "\"저야 정말 기쁘겠지요.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘So shall I,’ said Mr.", "\"나도 그렇소이다.\""], ["Alfred Jingle, drawing one arm through Mr. Pickwick’s, and another through Mr.", "앨프리드 징글 씨가, 한쪽 팔을 픽윅 씨의 팔에, 다른 팔을 와들 씨의 팔에 끼며 말했다."], ["Wardle’s, as he whispered confidentially in the ear of the former gentleman:--", "그러면서 그는 픽윅 씨의 귀에 은밀히 속삭였다."]], [["‘Devilish good dinner--cold, but capital--peeped into the room this morning--fowls and pies, and all that sort of thing--pleasant fellows these--well behaved, too--very.’", "\"끝내주게 좋은 만찬이오—찬 음식이지만 일품이지요—오늘 아침 방을 슬쩍 들여다봤소—닭이며 파이며, 그 비슷한 온갖 것들—유쾌한 친구들이오—예의도 바르고—아주.\""]], [["There being no further preliminaries to arrange, the company straggled into the town in little knots of twos and threes; and within a quarter of an hour were all seated in the great room of the Blue Lion Inn, Muggleton--Mr. Dumkins acting as chairman, and Mr. Luffey officiating as vice.", "이제 더 마련할 사전 절차가 없었으므로, 일행은 두셋씩 자그마한 무리를 이루어 어슬렁어슬렁 읍내로 들어갔고, 십오 분도 채 못 되어 모두가 머글턴 블루 라이언 여관의 큰 방에 자리를 잡았다—덤킨스 씨가 좌장을 맡고, 러피 씨가 부좌장 역할을 했다."]], [["There was a vast deal of talking and rattling of knives and forks, and plates; a great running about of three ponderous-headed waiters, and a rapid disappearance of the substantial viands on the table; to each and every of which item of confusion, the facetious Mr.", "엄청난 양의 대화와 함께 나이프와 포크, 접시가 달그락거리는 소리가 났고, 머리통이 묵직한 웨이터 셋이 부산히 뛰어다녔으며, 식탁 위의 푸짐한 음식들이 빠르게 사라져 갔다."], ["Jingle lent the aid of half-a-dozen ordinary men at least.", "이 모든 혼란의 항목 하나하나에, 익살꾼 징글 씨는 적어도 보통 사람 대여섯 명 몫의 거듦을 보탰다."], ["When everybody had eaten as much as possible, the cloth was removed, bottles, glasses, and dessert were placed on the table; and the waiters withdrew to ‘clear away,’ or in other words, to appropriate to their own private use and emolument whatever remnants of the eatables and drinkables they could contrive to lay their hands on.", "모두가 더 이상 못 먹을 만큼 먹고 나자, 식탁보가 치워지고, 병과 잔과 후식이 식탁에 놓였다. 웨이터들은 \"치우러\"—다시 말해, 먹을 것과 마실 것 가운데 손에 넣을 수 있는 찌꺼기란 찌꺼기는 죄다 제 사사로운 소용과 이득으로 챙기러—물러갔다."]], [["Amidst the general hum of mirth and conversation that ensued, there was a little man with a puffy Say-nothing-to-me,-or-I’ll-contradict-you sort of countenance, who remained very quiet; occasionally looking round him when the conversation slackened, as if he contemplated putting in something very weighty; and now and then bursting into a short cough of inexpressible grandeur.", "이윽고 흥겨움과 대화가 한데 어우러진 떠들썩한 소리 가운데, 부어오른 듯한 얼굴에 \"내게 아무 말도 하지 마시오, 안 그러면 반박하겠소\" 하는 표정을 한 자그마한 사내가 하나 있었는데, 그는 매우 조용히 있었다."], ["At length, during a moment of comparative silence, the little man called out in a very loud, solemn voice,--", "그러다 대화가 뜸해질 때면 이따금 주위를 둘러보았으니, 마치 무언가 무게 있는 말을 끼워 넣을까 궁리하는 듯했고, 또 때때로 형언할 수 없이 위풍당당하게 짧은 기침을 한 번씩 터뜨리곤 했다. 마침내 비교적 조용한 한순간에, 그 자그마한 사내가 매우 크고 엄숙한 목소리로 외쳤다."]], [["‘Mr. Luffey!’", "\"러피 씨!\""]], [["Everybody was hushed into a profound stillness as the individual addressed, replied--", "지명된 그 사람이 대답하자, 모두가 깊은 정적에 잠겨 조용해졌다."]], [["‘Sir!’", "\"예!\""]], [["‘I wish to address a few words to you, Sir, if you will entreat the gentlemen to fill their glasses.’", "\"몇 마디 말씀을 드리고 싶습니다, 선생. 부디 신사 여러분께 잔을 채우시도록 청해 주신다면 말입니다.\""]], [["Mr.", "징글 씨가 생색내듯 \"옳소, 옳소\""], ["Jingle uttered a patronising ‘Hear, hear,’ which was responded to by the remainder of the company; and the glasses having been filled, the vice-president assumed an air of wisdom in a state of profound attention; and said--", "하고 외쳤고, 나머지 일행이 그에 호응했다. 잔들이 채워지자, 부좌장은 깊은 주의에 잠긴 현자다운 기색을 취하며 말했다."]], [["‘Mr. Staple.’", "\"스테이플 씨.\""]], [["‘Sir,’ said the little man, rising, ‘I wish to address what I have to say to you and not to our worthy chairman, because our worthy chairman is in some measure--I may say in a great degree--the subject of what I have to say, or I may say to--to--’", "\"선생.\" 자그마한 사내가 일어서며 말했다. \"제가 드릴 말씀을, 우리의 존경하는 좌장이 아니라 선생께 드리고자 합니다. 까닭인즉, 우리의 존경하는 좌장이 어느 정도—아니, 상당한 정도라 해도 좋겠습니다만—제가 드릴 말씀의, 혹은 제가 진술할—진술할—\""]], [["‘State,’ suggested Mr.", "\"진술.\""], ["Jingle.", "징글 씨가 거들었다."]], [["‘Yes, to state,’ said the little man, ‘I thank my honourable friend, if he will allow me to call him so (four hears and one certainly from Mr.", "\"그렇소, 진술할 바의 대상이기 때문입니다.\" 자그마한 사내가 말했다."], ["Jingle), for the suggestion. Sir, I am a Deller--a Dingley Deller (cheers).", "\"그렇게 불러도 좋다면, 저의 존경하는 벗께 그 거듦에 감사드립니다."], ["I cannot lay claim to the honour of forming an item in the population of Muggleton; nor, Sir, I will frankly admit, do I covet that honour: and I will tell you why, Sir (hear); to Muggleton I will readily concede all these honours and distinctions to which it can fairly lay claim--they are too numerous and too well known to require aid or recapitulation from me.", "(네 번의 '옳소', 그리고 분명 그 가운데 하나는 징글 씨에게서 나온 것이었다.) 선생, 저는 델 사람입니다—딩글리 델 사람입니다. (환호.) 저는 머글턴 인구의 한 항목을 이룬다는 영예를 주장할 수 없습니다."], ["But, sir, while we remember that Muggleton has given birth to a Dumkins and a Podder, let us never forget that Dingley Dell can boast a Luffey and a Struggles.", "또한, 선생, 솔직히 말씀드리거니와, 저는 그 영예를 탐하지도 않습니다. 그 까닭을 말씀드리지요, 선생."], ["(Vociferous cheering.) Let me not be considered as wishing to detract from the merits of the former gentlemen.", "(옳소.) 머글턴이 정당하게 주장할 수 있는 그 모든 영예와 영광을, 저는 기꺼이 머글턴에 양보하겠습니다—그것들은 너무도 많고 너무도 잘 알려져 있어, 제가 거들거나 다시 늘어놓을 필요가 없습니다. 그러나, 선생, 머글턴이 덤킨스와 포더를 낳았음을 우리가 기억하는 동안, 딩글리 델이 러피와 스트러글스를 자랑할 수 있음을 결코 잊지 맙시다."], ["Sir, I envy them the luxury of their own feelings on this occasion.", "(우레와 같은 환호.) 제가 앞의 두 신사분의 공적을 깎아내리려 한다고 여기지는 말아 주십시오."], ["(Cheers.) Every gentleman who hears me, is probably acquainted with the reply made by an individual, who--to use an ordinary figure of speech--“hung out”", "선생, 저는 이번 일에서 그분들 자신이 느낄 감정의 호사가 부럽습니다. (환호.) 제 말을 듣고 계신 신사분이라면 누구나, 어느 인물이—흔한 비유로 말하자면—통 속에서 '죽치고'"], ["in a tub, to the emperor Alexander:--“if I were not Diogenes,”", "지내던 그 인물이 알렉산드로스 황제에게 한 대답을 아실 겁니다."], ["said he, “I would be Alexander.”", "'내가 디오게네스가 아니라면,'"], ["I can well imagine these gentlemen to say, “If I were not Dumkins I would be Luffey; if I were not Podder I would be Struggles.”", "그가 말하길, '나는 알렉산드로스가 되고 싶다'고 했지요. 저는 이 신사분들께서 이렇게 말씀하시는 것을 능히 상상할 수 있습니다."], ["(Enthusiasm.) But, gentlemen of Muggleton, is it in cricket alone that your fellow-townsmen stand pre- eminent?", "'내가 덤킨스가 아니라면 나는 러피가 되고 싶다. 내가 포더가 아니라면 나는 스트러글스가 되고 싶다.'"], ["Have you never heard of Dumkins and determination?", "(열광.) 그러나, 머글턴의 신사 여러분, 여러분의 동향인들이 탁월하게 뛰어난 것이 비단 크리켓뿐이겠습니까?"], ["Have you never been taught to associate Podder with property?", "덤킨스와 단호함을 들어 보지 못하셨습니까?"], ["(Great applause.) Have you never, when struggling for your rights, your liberties, and your privileges, been reduced, if only for an instant, to misgiving and despair?", "포더와 재산을 결부 짓도록 배우지 못하셨습니까? (큰 박수.) 여러분의 권리와 자유와 특권을 위해 분투하던 중에, 단 한순간이나마 회의와 절망에 빠져든 적이 없으십니까?"], ["And when you have been thus depressed, has not the name of Dumkins laid afresh within your breast the fire which had just gone out; and has not a word from that man lighted it again as brightly as if it had never expired?", "그리고 그렇게 의기소침했을 때, 덤킨스라는 이름이 막 꺼져 버린 불을 여러분의 가슴속에 새로이 지펴 주지 않았습니까? 그 사람의 한 마디가, 마치 한 번도 꺼진 적이 없었던 것처럼 그 불을 다시 환하게 밝혀 주지 않았습니까?"], ["(Great cheering.) Gentlemen, I beg to surround with a rich halo of enthusiastic cheering the united names of “Dumkins and Podder.”’", "(큰 환호.) 신사 여러분, 저는 '덤킨스와 포더'라는 두 이름을 한데 묶어 열광적인 환호의 풍성한 후광으로 둘러싸기를 청합니다.\""]], [["Here the little man ceased, and here the company commenced a raising of voices, and thumping of tables, which lasted with little intermission during the remainder of the evening. Other toasts were drunk. Mr. Luffey and Mr. Struggles, Mr. Pickwick and Mr. Jingle, were, each in his turn, the subject of unqualified eulogium; and each in due course returned thanks for the honour.", "여기서 자그마한 사내가 말을 그쳤고, 여기서 일행은 목소리를 높이고 식탁을 쾅쾅 두드리기 시작했으니, 그것은 그날 밤이 다하도록 거의 끊이지 않고 이어졌다. 다른 축배들도 들었다. 러피 씨와 스트러글스 씨, 픽윅 씨와 징글 씨가 저마다 차례로 아낌없는 찬사의 대상이 되었고, 저마다 차례차례 그 영예에 감사를 표했다."]], [["Enthusiastic as we are in the noble cause to which we have devoted ourselves, we should have felt a sensation of pride which we cannot express, and a consciousness of having done something to merit immortality of which we are now deprived, could we have laid the faintest outline on these addresses before our ardent readers.", "우리가 몸 바친 이 고귀한 대의에 우리 또한 그토록 열성적인 만큼, 이 연설들의 가장 희미한 윤곽이라도 우리의 열렬한 독자들 앞에 펼쳐 보일 수 있었더라면, 우리는 형언할 수 없는 자긍심과, 불멸의 가치를 지닌 무언가를 해냈다는 의식을 느꼈을 것이다—그러나 지금 우리는 그것을 빼앗기고 말았다. 스노드그래스 씨는 여느 때처럼 방대한 양의 기록을 했으니, 그것은 분명 더없이 유용하고 값진 정보를 제공했을 터인데, 다만 그 말들의 불타는 웅변인지 술의 열기 어린 영향인지가 그 신사의 손을 어찌나 심하게 떨리게 했던지, 그의 글씨는 거의 알아볼 수 없게 되었고 그 문체는 아예 알아볼 수 없게 되어 버렸다."], ["Mr.", "끈기 있는 조사 끝에 우리는 연사들의 이름과 어렴풋이 닮은 글자 몇 개를 알아낼 수 있었다. 그리고 노래 한 곡(징글 씨가 부른 것으로 추정된다)의 기록을 겨우 분간해 낼 수 있었는데, 거기에는 '술잔'"], ["Snodgrass, as usual, took a great mass of notes, which would no doubt have afforded most useful and valuable information, had not the burning eloquence of the words or the feverish influence of the wine made that gentleman’s hand so extremely unsteady, as to render his writing nearly unintelligible, and his style wholly so.", "'반짝이는' '루비'"], ["By dint of patient investigation, we have been enabled to trace some characters bearing a faint resemblance to the names of the speakers; and we can only discern an entry of a song (supposed to have been sung by Mr.", "'환한' '술'이라는 말들이 짧은 간격으로 자주 되풀이되고 있었다."], ["Jingle), in which the words ‘bowl’ ‘sparkling’ ‘ruby’ ‘bright’ and ‘wine’ are frequently repeated at short intervals.", "또한 우리는 그 기록의 맨 끝에서 '구운 뼈'에 대한 어렴풋한 언급 같은 것을 분간해 낼 수 있을 듯하다. 그러고 나서는 '찬'"], ["We fancy, too, that we can discern at the very end of the notes, some indistinct reference to ‘broiled bones’; and then the words ‘cold’ ‘without’ occur: but as any hypothesis we could found upon them must necessarily rest upon mere conjecture, we are not disposed to indulge in any of the speculations to which they may give rise.", "'없이'라는 말들이 나온다. 그러나 우리가 그것들을 근거로 세울 수 있는 어떠한 가설도 필연적으로 한낱 억측에 기댈 수밖에 없는 만큼, 우리는 그것들이 불러일으킬 법한 어떠한 추측에도 빠져들 생각이 없다."]], [["We will therefore return to Mr.", "그러므로 우리는 터프먼 씨에게로 돌아가려 하거니와, 다만 이 한 가지만 덧붙이겠다."], ["Tupman; merely adding that within some few minutes before twelve o’clock that night, the convocation of worthies of Dingley Dell and Muggleton were heard to sing, with great feeling and emphasis, the beautiful and pathetic national air of", "그날 밤 열두 시가 되기 몇 분 전, 딩글리 델과 머글턴의 명사들이 모인 그 회합에서 더없이 깊은 감정과 힘을 실어, 아름답고도 애절한 국민 가락을 부르는 소리가 들려왔다는 것이다."]], [["‘We won’t go home till morning, We won’t go home till morning, We won’t go home till morning, Till daylight doth appear.’", "'우린 아침까지 집에 안 가리, 우린 아침까지 집에 안 가리, 우린 아침까지 집에 안 가리, 동이 틀 때까지.'"]], [["CHAPTER VIII. STRONGLY ILLUSTRATIVE OF THE POSITION, THAT THE COURSE OF TRUE LOVE IS NOT A RAILWAY\nThe quiet seclusion of Dingley Dell, the presence of so many of the gentler sex, and the solicitude and anxiety they evinced in his behalf, were all favourable to the growth and development of those softer feelings which nature had implanted deep in the bosom of Mr.", "디ング 리 델의 고요한 은둔, 그토록 많은 여성들의 존재, 그리고 그들이 그를 위해 보여 준 정성과 염려—이 모든 것은 자연이 트레이시 텁먼 씨의 가슴 깊숙이 심어 둔 그 부드러운 감정들이 자라고 피어나는 데 더없이 좋은 토양이었으니, 이제 그 감정은 한 사랑스러운 대상에게 모이도록 운명 지어진 듯 보였다."], ["Tracy Tupman, and which now appeared destined to centre in one lovely object.", "젊은 숙녀들은 예뻤고, 태도는 마음을 끌었으며, 성품은 나무랄 데 없었다."], ["The young ladies were pretty, their manners winning, their dispositions unexceptionable; but there was a dignity in the air, a touch-me-not- ishness in the walk, a majesty in the eye, of the spinster aunt, to which, at their time of life, they could lay no claim, which distinguished her from any female on whom Mr. Tupman had ever gazed.", "그러나 그 미혼의 고모에게는—그들 나이로는 결코 주장할 수 없는—풍기는 위엄이, 걸음걸이에 깃든 도도한 범접 불가의 기운이, 눈빛에 어린 장엄함이 있었으니, 그것이야말로 텁먼 씨가 일찍이 바라본 어떤 여성과도 그녀를 구별 짓는 것이었다."], ["That there was something kindred in their nature, something congenial in their souls, something mysteriously sympathetic in their bosoms, was evident.", "두 사람의 본성에 무언가 동류의 것이, 영혼에 무언가 통하는 것이, 가슴속에 무언가 신비롭게 공명하는 것이 있음은 명백했다."], ["Her name was the first that rose to Mr. Tupman’s lips as he lay wounded on the grass; and her hysteric laughter was the first sound that fell upon his ear when he was supported to the house.", "그가 풀밭에 부상당한 채 누워 있을 때 그의 입술에 가장 먼저 떠오른 이름이 그녀의 것이었고, 그가 부축을 받아 집으로 옮겨질 때 그의 귀에 가장 먼저 닿은 소리가 그녀의 히스테릭한 웃음이었다."], ["But had her agitation arisen from an amiable and feminine sensibility which would have been equally irrepressible in any case; or had it been called forth by a more ardent and passionate feeling, which he, of all men living, could alone awaken?", "그러나 그녀의 동요가 어떤 경우에도 똑같이 억누를 수 없었을 상냥하고 여성스러운 감수성에서 비롯된 것인지, 아니면 살아 있는 모든 사내 가운데 오직 그만이 일깨울 수 있는, 더 뜨겁고 격정적인 감정에서 불려 나온 것인지?"], ["These were the doubts which racked his brain as he lay extended on the sofa; these were the doubts which he determined should be at once and for ever resolved.", "이것이 그가 소파에 길게 누워 있는 동안 그의 머리를 괴롭힌 의혹이었고, 이것이 그가 단번에 영영 풀어 버리겠노라 결심한 의혹이었다."]], [["It was evening.", "저녁이었다."], ["Isabella and Emily had strolled out with Mr.", "이저벨라와 에밀리는 트런들 씨와 함께 산책을 나갔고, 귀먹은 노부인은 의자에서 잠이 들었으며, 뚱뚱한 소년의 코 고는 소리가 멀리 떨어진 부엌에서 낮고 단조로운 음으로 스며 나왔다. 토실토실한 하녀들은 옆문 곁에서 빈둥거리며 이 시간의 쾌적함과, 농장에 딸린 어떤 둔중한 짐승들과 나누는 가장 원초적인 수준의 시시덕거림이 주는 즐거움을 만끽하고 있었다."], ["Trundle; the deaf old lady had fallen asleep in her chair; the snoring of the fat boy, penetrated in a low and monotonous sound from the distant kitchen; the buxom servants were lounging at the side door, enjoying the pleasantness of the hour, and the delights of a flirtation, on first principles, with certain unwieldy animals attached to the farm; and there sat the interesting pair, uncared for by all, caring for none, and dreaming only of themselves; there they sat, in short, like a pair of carefully-folded kid gloves--bound up in each other.", "그리고 거기, 누구의 보살핌도 받지 않고 누구도 보살피지 않으며 오직 자기들만을 꿈꾸는 이 흥미로운 한 쌍이 앉아 있었다. 요컨대 그들은 정성껏 접어 둔 한 켤레의 새끼 염소 가죽 장갑처럼—서로에게 꼭 싸여—거기 앉아 있었다."]], [["‘I have forgotten my flowers,’ said the spinster aunt.", "\"내 꽃들을 깜빡했네요.\" 미혼의 고모가 말했다."]], [["‘Water them now,’ said Mr.", "\"지금 물을 주시지요.\""], ["Tupman, in accents of persuasion.", "텁먼 씨가 설득하는 어조로 말했다."]], [["‘You will take cold in the evening air,’ urged the spinster aunt affectionately.", "\"저녁 공기에 감기 드시겠어요.\" 미혼의 고모가 다정하게 만류했다."]], [["‘No, no,’ said Mr.", "\"아니요, 아닙니다.\""], ["Tupman, rising; ‘it will do me good.", "텁먼 씨가 일어서며 말했다. \"오히려 제게 좋을 겁니다."], ["Let me accompany you.’", "제가 모시고 가게 해 주십시오.\""]], [["The lady paused to adjust the sling in which the left arm of the youth was placed, and taking his right arm led him to the garden.", "부인은 잠시 멈춰 청년의 왼팔을 받친 삼각건을 매만져 주고는, 그의 오른팔을 잡고 정원으로 이끌었다."]], [["There was a bower at the farther end, with honeysuckle, jessamine, and creeping plants--one of those sweet retreats which humane men erect for the accommodation of spiders.", "정원 저편 끝에는 인동덩굴과 재스민과 덩굴식물들로 둘러싸인 정자가 하나 있었으니—인정 많은 사람들이 거미들의 거처를 마련해 주려 세워 놓는 그런 달콤한 은신처 가운데 하나였다."]], [["The spinster aunt took up a large watering-pot which lay in one corner, and was about to leave the arbour.", "미혼의 고모는 한쪽 구석에 놓여 있던 커다란 물뿌리개를 집어 들고 정자를 막 나서려 했다."], ["Mr. Tupman detained her, and drew her to a seat beside him.", "텁먼 씨가 그녀를 붙들어 자기 곁의 자리로 끌어당겼다."]], [["‘Miss Wardle!’ said he.", "\"워들 양!\" 그가 말했다."]], [["The spinster aunt trembled, till some pebbles which had accidentally found their way into the large watering-pot shook like an infant’s rattle.", "미혼의 고모는 몸을 떨었으니, 어쩌다 그 커다란 물뿌리개 속으로 굴러 들어간 조약돌 몇 개가 갓난아기의 딸랑이처럼 달그락거릴 정도였다."]], [["‘Miss Wardle,’ said Mr.", "\"워들 양,\" 텁먼 씨가 말했다."], ["Tupman, ‘you are an angel.’", "\"당신은 천사입니다.\""]], [["‘Mr.", "\"텁먼 씨!\""], ["Tupman!’ exclaimed Rachael, blushing as red as the watering-pot itself.", "레이철이 물뿌리개만큼이나 새빨갛게 얼굴을 붉히며 외쳤다."]], [["‘Nay,’ said the eloquent Pickwickian--‘I know it but too well.’", "\"아니요,\" 달변의 픽윅 회원이 말했다. \"저는 그것을 너무도 잘 알고 있습니다.\""]], [["‘All women are angels, they say,’ murmured the lady playfully.", "\"여자는 다 천사라고들 하지요.\" 부인이 장난스레 속삭였다."]], [["‘Then what can you be; or to what, without presumption, can I compare you?’ replied Mr.", "\"그렇다면 당신은 무엇일 수 있겠습니까? 아니, 외람되지 않게 무엇에 당신을 견줄 수 있겠습니까?\""], ["Tupman. ‘Where was the woman ever seen who resembled you?", "텁먼 씨가 대답했다."], ["Where else could I hope to find so rare a combination of excellence and beauty?", "\"당신을 닮은 여인이 일찍이 어디 있었습니까? 이토록 보기 드문 미덕과 미모의 조화를 제가 또 어디서 찾기를 바랄 수 있겠습니까?"], ["Where else could I seek to--Oh!’ Here Mr.", "또 어디서 제가 구하려—오!\""], ["Tupman paused, and pressed the hand which clasped the handle of the happy watering-pot.", "여기서 텁먼 씨는 말을 멈추고, 저 행복한 물뿌리개의 손잡이를 쥐고 있는 손을 꼭 눌렀다."]], [["The lady turned aside her head.", "부인은 고개를 돌렸다."], ["‘Men are such deceivers,’ she softly whispered.", "\"남자들은 어쩌면 그렇게 속이는지.\" 그녀가 부드럽게 속삭였다."]], [["‘They are, they are,’ ejaculated Mr.", "\"그렇지요, 그렇습니다.\""], ["Tupman; ‘but not all men.", "텁먼 씨가 토해 내듯 말했다."], ["There lives at least one being who can never change--one being who would be content to devote his whole existence to your happiness--who lives but in your eyes--who breathes but in your smiles--who bears the heavy burden of life itself only for you.’", "\"하지만 모든 남자가 그런 건 아닙니다. 적어도 결코 변할 수 없는 한 존재가 살고 있으니—제 온 생애를 당신의 행복에 바치는 것으로 만족할 한 존재가—오직 당신의 눈동자 안에서만 살고—오직 당신의 미소 속에서만 숨 쉬며—오직 당신을 위해서만 인생이라는 무거운 짐 자체를 짊어지는 한 존재가 있습니다.\""]], [["‘Could such an individual be found--’ said the lady.", "\"그런 사람을 찾을 수 있다면—\" 부인이 말했다."]], [["‘But he can be found,’ said the ardent Mr.", "\"하지만 그는 찾을 수 있습니다.\" 열렬한 텁먼 씨가 끼어들며 말했다."], ["Tupman, interposing.", "\"그는 이미 찾아졌습니다."], ["‘He is found.", "그가 여기 있습니다, 워들 양.\""], ["He is here, Miss Wardle.’ And ere the lady was aware of his intention, Mr. Tupman had sunk upon his knees at her feet.", "그리고 부인이 그의 의도를 채 알아차리기도 전에, 텁먼 씨는 그녀의 발치에 무릎을 꿇고 주저앉아 있었다."]], [["‘Mr.", "\"텁먼 씨, 일어나세요.\""], ["Tupman, rise,’ said Rachael.", "레이철이 말했다."]], [["‘Never!’ was the valorous reply.", "\"결코 안 됩니다!\" 용감한 대답이었다."], ["‘Oh, Rachael!’ He seized her passive hand, and the watering-pot fell to the ground as he pressed it to his lips.--‘Oh, Rachael!", "\"오, 레이철!\" 그가 그녀의 가만히 놓인 손을 붙잡자 물뿌리개가 땅에 떨어졌고, 그는 그 손을 입술에 갖다 댔다."], ["say you love me.’", "\"오, 레이철! 저를 사랑한다고 말해 주세요.\""]], [["‘Mr.", "\"텁먼 씨,\""], ["Tupman,’ said the spinster aunt, with averted head, ‘I can hardly speak the words; but--but--you are not wholly indifferent to me.’", "미혼의 고모가 고개를 돌린 채 말했다. \"차마 그 말을 입에 올리기가 힘들지만—하지만—하지만—당신이 제게 영 무관심한 분은 아니랍니다.\""]], [["Mr. Tupman no sooner heard this avowal, than he proceeded to do what his enthusiastic emotions prompted, and what, for aught we know (for we are but little acquainted with such matters), people so circumstanced always do.", "이 고백을 듣자마자 텁먼 씨는 그의 격앙된 감정이 시키는 대로, 그리고—우리로서는 그런 일에 대해 아는 바가 적으니 단언키 어려우나—그런 처지에 놓인 사람들이 으레 하는 대로 행동에 나섰다."], ["He jumped up, and, throwing his arm round the neck of the spinster aunt, imprinted upon her lips numerous kisses, which after a due show of struggling and resistance, she received so passively, that there is no telling how many more Mr. Tupman might have bestowed, if the lady had not given a very unaffected start, and exclaimed in an affrighted tone--", "그는 벌떡 일어나 미혼의 고모의 목에 팔을 두르고는 그녀의 입술에 수많은 입맞춤을 새겼고, 그녀는 응당한 몸부림과 저항의 시늉을 보인 끝에 어찌나 순순히 받아들였던지, 만약 부인이 아주 꾸밈없는 화들짝 놀란 몸짓을 하며 겁먹은 어조로 외치지 않았던들 텁먼 씨가 얼마나 더 많은 입맞춤을 베풀었을지 알 수 없는 노릇이었다—"]], [["‘Mr. Tupman, we are observed!--we are discovered!’", "\"텁먼 씨, 누가 보고 있어요!—들켰어요!\""]], [["Mr. Tupman looked round.", "텁먼 씨가 둘러보았다."], ["There was the fat boy, perfectly motionless, with his large circular eyes staring into the arbour, but without the slightest expression on his face that the most expert physiognomist could have referred to astonishment, curiosity, or any other known passion that agitates the human breast.", "거기 뚱뚱한 소년이 완전히 미동도 없이 서서, 그 크고 둥근 눈으로 정자 안을 들여다보고 있었으나, 그 얼굴에는 아무리 노련한 관상가라도 놀라움이니 호기심이니, 인간의 가슴을 흔드는 그 밖의 알려진 어떤 격정에 빗댈 만한 표정이 조금도 없었다."], ["Mr. Tupman gazed on the fat boy, and the fat boy stared at him; and the longer Mr.", "텁먼 씨가 뚱뚱한 소년을 응시하니 뚱뚱한 소년도 그를 빤히 마주 보았다."], ["Tupman observed the utter vacancy of the fat boy’s countenance, the more convinced he became that he either did not know, or did not understand, anything that had been going forward.", "그리고 텁먼 씨가 뚱뚱한 소년의 얼굴에 어린 완전한 멍함을 오래 살필수록, 그는 이 소년이 방금 벌어진 일을 전혀 모르거나 아니면 이해하지 못한다고 더욱 확신하게 되었다."], ["Under this impression, he said with great firmness--", "그런 인상에 잠겨 그는 아주 단호하게 말했다—"]], [["‘What do you want here, Sir?’", "\"여기서 뭘 원하느냐, 이놈아?\""]], [["‘Supper’s ready, sir,’ was the prompt reply.", "\"저녁 식사가 준비됐습니다, 나리.\" 즉각적인 대답이었다."]], [["‘Have you just come here, sir?’ inquired Mr.", "\"지금 막 왔느냐?\""], ["Tupman, with a piercing look.", "텁먼 씨가 꿰뚫는 듯한 눈초리로 물었다."]], [["‘Just,’ replied the fat boy.", "\"방금요.\" 뚱뚱한 소년이 대답했다."]], [["Mr. Tupman looked at him very hard again; but there was not a wink in his eye, or a curve in his face.", "텁먼 씨가 다시 그를 아주 매섭게 노려보았지만, 그의 눈에는 깜빡임 하나, 그의 얼굴에는 일그러짐 하나 없었다."]], [["Mr. Tupman took the arm of the spinster aunt, and walked towards the house; the fat boy followed behind.", "텁먼 씨는 미혼의 고모의 팔을 잡고 집을 향해 걸었고, 뚱뚱한 소년은 뒤를 따랐다."]], [["‘He knows nothing of what has happened,’ he whispered.", "\"저 녀석은 무슨 일이 있었는지 아무것도 모릅니다.\" 그가 속삭였다."]], [["‘Nothing,’ said the spinster aunt.", "\"아무것도요.\" 미혼의 고모가 말했다."]], [["There was a sound behind them, as of an imperfectly suppressed chuckle. Mr.", "그들 뒤에서 채 억누르지 못한 킥킥거림 같은 소리가 났다."], ["Tupman turned sharply round.", "텁먼 씨가 홱 돌아보았다."], ["No; it could not have been the fat boy; there was not a gleam of mirth, or anything but feeding in his whole visage.", "아니다, 뚱뚱한 소년일 리가 없었다. 그의 얼굴 전체에는 즐거워하는 기색이라곤 한 점도 없고, 오로지 먹을 생각뿐인 표정밖에 없었다."]], [["‘He must have been fast asleep,’ whispered Mr.", "\"저 녀석은 분명 곤히 잠들어 있었을 겁니다.\""], ["Tupman.", "텁먼 씨가 속삭였다."]], [["‘I have not the least doubt of it,’ replied the spinster aunt.", "\"저도 조금도 의심치 않아요.\" 미혼의 고모가 대답했다."]], [["They both laughed heartily.", "두 사람은 진심으로 한바탕 웃었다."]], [["Mr. Tupman was wrong.", "텁먼 씨가 틀렸다."], ["The fat boy, for once, had not been fast asleep.", "뚱뚱한 소년은 이번만큼은 곤히 잠들어 있지 않았다."], ["He was awake--wide awake--to what had been going forward.", "그는 깨어 있었다—말짱히 깨어—방금 벌어진 일에 대해서."]], [["The supper passed off without any attempt at a general conversation. The old lady had gone to bed; Isabella Wardle devoted herself exclusively to Mr.", "저녁 식사는 이렇다 할 전체적인 대화 시도 없이 지나갔다."], ["Trundle; the spinster’s attentions were reserved for Mr. Tupman; and Emily’s thoughts appeared to be engrossed by some distant object-- possibly they were with the absent Snodgrass.", "노부인은 잠자리에 들었고, 이저벨라 워들은 오로지 트런들 씨에게만 마음을 쏟았으며, 미혼 고모의 관심은 텁먼 씨에게로 향했고, 에밀리의 생각은 무언가 먼 데 있는 대상에 사로잡힌 듯했으니—아마도 그 생각은 자리에 없는 스노드그래스에게 가 있었으리라."]], [["Eleven--twelve--one o’clock had struck, and the gentlemen had not arrived.", "열한 시—열두 시—한 시를 쳤는데도 신사들은 도착하지 않았다."], ["Consternation sat on every face.", "얼굴마다 경악이 서렸다."], ["Could they have been waylaid and robbed?", "길에서 매복을 당해 강도라도 만난 걸까?"], ["Should they send men and lanterns in every direction by which they could be supposed likely to have travelled home?", "집으로 돌아왔음직한 길마다 사람과 등불을 보내야 할까?"], ["or should they--Hark!", "아니면 그들이—쉿!"], ["there they were.", "저기 왔다."], ["What could have made them so late?", "무엇이 그들을 이토록 늦게 만들었을까?"], ["A strange voice, too!", "게다가 낯선 목소리까지!"], ["To whom could it belong?", "대체 누구의 것일까?"], ["They rushed into the kitchen, whither the truants had repaired, and at once obtained rather more than a glimmering of the real state of the case.", "그들은 무단외출자들이 들어가 있는 부엌으로 우르르 몰려갔고, 단번에 사태의 진상을 어렴풋이 짐작하는 것 이상으로 훤히 알게 되었다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick, with his hands in his pockets and his hat cocked completely over his left eye, was leaning against the dresser, shaking his head from side to side, and producing a constant succession of the blandest and most benevolent smiles without being moved thereunto by any discernible cause or pretence whatsoever; old Mr.", "픽윅 씨는 두 손을 주머니에 찌르고 모자를 왼쪽 눈 위로 완전히 삐딱하게 얹은 채 찬장에 기대어, 고개를 좌우로 흔들면서, 알아챌 만한 이유도 핑계도 전혀 없이 더없이 부드럽고 더없이 자애로운 미소를 끊임없이 연달아 짓고 있었다."], ["Wardle, with a highly- inflamed countenance, was grasping the hand of a strange gentleman muttering protestations of eternal friendship; Mr.", "워들 노인은 시뻘겋게 달아오른 얼굴로 낯선 신사의 손을 움켜쥐고 영원한 우정의 다짐을 중얼거리고 있었다."], ["Winkle, supporting himself by the eight-day clock, was feebly invoking destruction upon the head of any member of the family who should suggest the propriety of his retiring for the night; and Mr.", "윈클 씨는 여드레 태엽 시계에 몸을 의지한 채, 자기더러 이만 잠자리에 드는 게 좋겠다고 권하는 가족 누구든 그 머리 위에 파멸이 내리기를 힘없이 빌고 있었다."], ["Snodgrass had sunk into a chair, with an expression of the most abject and hopeless misery that the human mind can imagine, portrayed in every lineament of his expressive face.", "그리고 스노드그래스 씨는 의자에 푹 주저앉아, 인간의 정신이 상상할 수 있는 가장 비참하고 가장 절망적인 비탄의 표정을 그 풍부한 표정의 얼굴 구석구석에 그려 내고 있었다."]], [["‘Is anything the matter?’ inquired the three ladies.", "\"무슨 일 있나요?\" 세 부인이 물었다."]], [["‘Nothing the matter,’ replied Mr.", "\"아무 일 없어요.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다."], ["Pickwick. ‘We--we’re--all right.--I say, Wardle, we’re all right, ain’t we?’", "\"우리—우린—다 멀쩡합니다.—이봐, 워들, 우리 다 멀쩡하지, 안 그런가?\""]], [["‘I should think so,’ replied the jolly host.--‘My dears, here’s my friend Mr.", "\"그럴 테지.\""], ["Jingle--Mr. Pickwick’s friend, Mr.", "유쾌한 주인이 대답했다."], ["Jingle, come ‘pon--little visit.’", "\"얘들아, 여기 내 친구 징글 씨다—픽윅 씨의 친구 징글 씨가, 잠깐—들렀단다.\""]], [["‘Is anything the matter with Mr.", "\"스노드그래스 씨에게 무슨 일이라도 있나요?\""], ["Snodgrass, Sir?’ inquired Emily, with great anxiety.", "에밀리가 몹시 걱정스럽게 물었다."]], [["‘Nothing the matter, ma’am,’ replied the stranger.", "\"아무 일 없습니다, 아가씨.\" 낯선 이가 대답했다."], ["‘Cricket dinner-- glorious party--capital songs--old port--claret--good--very good--wine, ma’am--wine.’", "\"크리켓 만찬—성대한 자리—멋진 노래들—오래된 포트와인—클라레—좋고—아주 좋은—포도주지요, 아가씨—포도주.\""]], [["‘It wasn’t the wine,’ murmured Mr.", "\"포도주 때문이 아니었어.\""], ["Snodgrass, in a broken voice.", "스노드그래스 씨가 갈라진 목소리로 중얼거렸다."], ["‘It was the salmon.’ (Somehow or other, it never is the wine, in these cases.)", "\"연어 때문이었지.\" (어찌 된 영문인지, 이런 경우에 결코 포도주 탓인 적은 없다.)"]], [["‘Hadn’t they better go to bed, ma’am?’ inquired Emma.", "\"두 분 다 잠자리에 드시는 게 낫지 않을까요, 마님?\" 에마가 물었다."], ["‘Two of the boys will carry the gentlemen upstairs.’", "\"사내아이 둘이서 신사분들을 위층으로 모셔다 드리겠습니다.\""]], [["‘I won’t go to bed,’ said Mr.", "\"난 잠자리에 안 갈 테야.\""], ["Winkle firmly.", "윈클 씨가 단호하게 말했다."]], [["‘No living boy shall carry me,’ said Mr.", "\"살아 있는 어떤 사내아이도 날 못 옮겨.\""], ["Pickwick stoutly; and he went on smiling as before.", "픽윅 씨가 굳세게 말하고는, 여전히 아까처럼 미소를 지었다."]], [["‘Hurrah!’ gasped Mr.", "\"만세!\""], ["Winkle faintly.", "윈클 씨가 힘없이 헐떡였다."]], [["‘Hurrah!’ echoed Mr.", "\"만세!\""], ["Pickwick, taking off his hat and dashing it on the floor, and insanely casting his spectacles into the middle of the kitchen.", "픽윅 씨가 따라 외치며 모자를 벗어 바닥에 내동댕이치고는, 정신 나간 사람처럼 안경을 부엌 한복판으로 던져 버렸다."], ["At this humorous feat he laughed outright.", "이 익살스러운 묘기에 그는 큰 소리로 웃음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["‘Let’s--have--‘nother--bottle,’ cried Mr.", "\"한—병—더—하자고.\""], ["Winkle, commencing in a very loud key, and ending in a very faint one.", "윈클 씨가 아주 높은 음으로 시작했다가 아주 가냘픈 음으로 끝맺으며 외쳤다."], ["His head dropped upon his breast; and, muttering his invincible determination not to go to his bed, and a sanguinary regret that he had not ‘done for old Tupman’ in the morning, he fell fast asleep; in which condition he was borne to his apartment by two young giants under the personal superintendence of the fat boy, to whose protecting care Mr.", "그의 머리가 가슴팍으로 떨궈졌다. 그리고 잠자리에 들지 않겠다는 불굴의 결의와, 아침에 \"늙은 텁먼을 해치우지\""], ["Snodgrass shortly afterwards confided his own person, Mr.", "못한 것에 대한 피비린내 나는 후회를 중얼거리면서, 그는 깊이 잠들어 버렸다."], ["Pickwick accepted the proffered arm of Mr. Tupman and quietly disappeared, smiling more than ever; and Mr.", "그 상태로 그는 뚱뚱한 소년의 직접적인 감독 아래 젊은 거인 둘에게 들려 자기 방으로 옮겨졌고, 스노드그래스 씨도 곧이어 그 뚱뚱한 소년의 보호에 자기 몸을 맡겼다."], ["Wardle, after taking as affectionate a leave of the whole family as if he were ordered for immediate execution, consigned to Mr.", "픽윅 씨는 텁먼 씨가 내민 팔을 받아들이고는, 그 어느 때보다 더 환하게 미소 지으며 조용히 사라졌다."], ["Trundle the honour of conveying him upstairs, and retired, with a very futile attempt to look impressively solemn and dignified.", "그리고 워들 씨는, 마치 당장 처형 명령이라도 받은 사람처럼 온 가족에게 더없이 다정한 작별을 고한 뒤, 자기를 위층으로 모시는 영예를 트런들 씨에게 맡기고, 자못 근엄하고 위엄 있게 보이려는 지극히 헛된 시도를 하며 물러갔다."]], [["‘What a shocking scene!’ said the spinster aunt.", "\"이런 끔찍한 광경이라니!\" 미혼의 고모가 말했다."]], [["‘Dis-gusting!’ ejaculated both the young ladies.", "\"역겨워!\" 두 젊은 숙녀가 동시에 토해 내듯 말했다."]], [["‘Dreadful--dreadful!’ said Jingle, looking very grave: he was about a bottle and a half ahead of any of his companions.", "\"끔찍해—끔찍해!\" 징글이 아주 근엄한 표정을 지으며 말했다."], ["‘Horrid spectacle-- very!’", "그는 일행 누구보다도 한 병 반쯤은 앞서 마신 처지였다. \"참혹한 꼴이야—정말이지!\""]], [["‘What a nice man!’ whispered the spinster aunt to Mr.", "\"참 멋진 분이네요!\""], ["Tupman.", "미혼의 고모가 텁먼 씨에게 속삭였다."]], [["‘Good-looking, too!’ whispered Emily Wardle.", "\"잘생기기도 했고요!\" 에밀리 워들이 속삭였다."]], [["‘Oh, decidedly,’ observed the spinster aunt.", "\"아, 그렇고말고.\" 미혼의 고모가 덧붙였다."]], [["Mr. Tupman thought of the widow at Rochester, and his mind was troubled.", "텁먼 씨는 로체스터의 그 과부를 떠올렸고, 그의 마음은 어지러웠다."], ["The succeeding half-hour’s conversation was not of a nature to calm his perturbed spirit.", "뒤이은 반 시간의 대화도 그의 동요한 정신을 가라앉힐 만한 것은 아니었다."], ["The new visitor was very talkative, and the number of his anecdotes was only to be exceeded by the extent of his politeness.", "새로 온 손님은 무척 말이 많았으니, 그의 일화의 수효는 오직 그의 정중함의 정도에나 따라잡힐 만했다."], ["Mr. Tupman felt that as Jingle’s popularity increased, he (Tupman) retired further into the shade.", "텁먼 씨는 징글의 인기가 높아질수록 자기(텁먼)는 점점 더 그늘 속으로 물러난다고 느꼈다."], ["His laughter was forced--his merriment feigned; and when at last he laid his aching temples between the sheets, he thought, with horrid delight, on the satisfaction it would afford him to have Jingle’s head at that moment between the feather bed and the mattress.", "그의 웃음은 억지였고—그의 흥겨움은 꾸민 것이었다. 그리고 마침내 욱신거리는 관자놀이를 침대 시트 사이에 뉘었을 때, 그는 바로 그 순간 징글의 머리를 깃털 이불과 매트리스 사이에 끼워 두면 얼마나 흐뭇할까 하는 생각에 소름 끼치는 기쁨을 느꼈다."]], [["The indefatigable stranger rose betimes next morning, and, although his companions remained in bed overpowered with the dissipation of the previous night, exerted himself most successfully to promote the hilarity of the breakfast-table.", "지칠 줄 모르는 이 낯선 이는 이튿날 아침 일찍 일어났고, 그의 일행은 전날 밤의 흥청거림에 짓눌려 침대에 누워 있었음에도, 그는 아침 식탁의 흥겨움을 돋우려 더없이 성공적으로 애를 썼다."], ["So successful were his efforts, that even the deaf old lady insisted on having one or two of his best jokes retailed through the trumpet; and even she condescended to observe to the spinster aunt, that ‘He’ (meaning Jingle) ‘was an impudent young fellow:’ a sentiment in which all her relations then and there present thoroughly coincided.", "그 노력이 어찌나 성공적이었던지, 귀먹은 노부인마저 그의 가장 멋진 농담 한두 개를 나팔 보청기에 대고 들려 달라고 졸랐다. 그리고 그 노부인조차 미혼의 고모에게 황송하게도 한마디 평하기를, \"그 사람은\"(징글을 가리키는 것이었다) \"뻔뻔스러운 젊은이로구나\"라고 했으니, 그 자리에 있던 그녀의 모든 친척들이 진심으로 동의하는 견해였다."]], [["It was the old lady’s habit on the fine summer mornings to repair to the arbour in which Mr.", "화창한 여름 아침이면 노부인은 텁먼 씨가 이미 한 차례 활약했던 그 정자로 가는 습관이 있었는데, 그 형식과 절차는 다음과 같았다. 먼저 뚱뚱한 소년이 노부인 침실 문 뒤의 못에서 빈틈없이 짠 검은 새틴 보닛과 따뜻한 면 숄, 그리고 손잡이가 큼직한 두툼한 지팡이를 가져왔다."], ["Tupman had already signalised himself, in form and manner following: first, the fat boy fetched from a peg behind the old lady’s bedroom door, a close black satin bonnet, a warm cotton shawl, and a thick stick with a capacious handle; and the old lady, having put on the bonnet and shawl at her leisure, would lean one hand on the stick and the other on the fat boy’s shoulder, and walk leisurely to the arbour, where the fat boy would leave her to enjoy the fresh air for the space of half an hour; at the expiration of which time he would return and reconduct her to the house.", "그러면 노부인은 느긋하게 보닛을 쓰고 숄을 두른 다음, 한 손은 지팡이에, 다른 한 손은 뚱뚱한 소년의 어깨에 얹고서 천천히 정자로 걸어갔다. 거기서 뚱뚱한 소년은 그녀를 남겨 두고 반 시간 동안 신선한 공기를 즐기게 했고, 그 시간이 다하면 돌아와 그녀를 다시 집으로 모셨다."]], [["The old lady was very precise and very particular; and as this ceremony had been observed for three successive summers without the slightest deviation from the accustomed form, she was not a little surprised on this particular morning to see the fat boy, instead of leaving the arbour, walk a few paces out of it, look carefully round him in every direction, and return towards her with great stealth and an air of the most profound mystery.", "노부인은 아주 깔끔하고 아주 까다로운 사람이었다. 그리고 이 의식은 세 해 여름 동안 정해진 형식에서 조금도 벗어남 없이 지켜져 왔던 터라, 바로 이날 아침 뚱뚱한 소년이 정자를 떠나는 대신 거기서 몇 걸음 걸어 나와 사방을 조심스레 둘러보고는 자못 은밀하게, 더없이 심오한 비밀이라도 품은 듯한 기색으로 자기에게 되돌아오는 것을 보고 그녀는 적잖이 놀랐다."]], [["The old lady was timorous--most old ladies are--and her first impression was that the bloated lad was about to do her some grievous bodily harm with the view of possessing himself of her loose coin.", "노부인은 겁이 많았으니—대개의 노부인들이 그렇다—그녀가 받은 첫인상은 이 퉁퉁한 녀석이 자기 잔돈을 차지할 셈으로 무슨 심각한 신체적 위해를 가하려는 게 아닌가 하는 것이었다."], ["She would have cried for assistance, but age and infirmity had long ago deprived her of the power of screaming; she, therefore, watched his motions with feelings of intense horror which were in no degree diminished by his coming close up to her, and shouting in her ear in an agitated, and as it seemed to her, a threatening tone--", "그녀는 도움을 청해 소리치고 싶었지만, 나이와 노쇠함이 오래전에 비명을 지를 힘을 앗아 간 터였다. 그래서 그녀는 극심한 공포의 심정으로 그의 동작을 지켜보았는데, 그가 바짝 다가와 그녀의 귓전에 동요한, 그리고 그녀에게는 위협적으로 들리는 어조로 고함을 질렀을 때 그 공포는 조금도 줄어들지 않았다—"]], [["‘Missus!’", "\"마님!\""]], [["Now it so happened that Mr. Jingle was walking in the garden close to the arbour at that moment.", "그런데 마침 그때 징글 씨가 정자 가까이의 정원을 거닐고 있었다."], ["He too heard the shouts of ‘Missus,’ and stopped to hear more.", "그 역시 \"마님!\" 하는 고함을 듣고는 더 들어 보려고 걸음을 멈추었다."], ["There were three reasons for his doing so.", "그가 그렇게 한 데에는 세 가지 이유가 있었다."], ["In the first place, he was idle and curious; secondly, he was by no means scrupulous; thirdly, and lastly, he was concealed from view by some flowering shrubs.", "첫째로 그는 한가했고 호기심이 일었으며, 둘째로 그는 전혀 조심스러운 위인이 아니었고, 셋째이자 마지막으로 그는 꽃이 핀 관목 몇 그루에 가려 보이지 않았다."], ["So there he stood, and there he listened.", "그리하여 그는 거기 서서 귀를 기울였다."]], [["‘Missus!’ shouted the fat boy.", "\"마님!\" 뚱뚱한 소년이 고함쳤다."]], [["‘Well, Joe,’ said the trembling old lady.", "\"왜 그러느냐, 조.\" 떨고 있던 노부인이 말했다."], ["‘I’m sure I have been a good mistress to you, Joe.", "\"나는 분명 너에게 좋은 주인이었다, 조."], ["You have invariably been treated very kindly.", "너는 한결같이 아주 친절한 대접을 받았어."], ["You have never had too much to do; and you have always had enough to eat.’", "일을 너무 많이 한 적도 없고, 늘 먹을 것도 넉넉했지.\""]], [["This last was an appeal to the fat boy’s most sensitive feelings.", "이 마지막 말은 뚱뚱한 소년의 가장 예민한 감정을 건드리는 호소였다."], ["He seemed touched, as he replied emphatically--", "그는 감동한 듯, 힘주어 대답했다—"]], [["‘I knows I has.’", "\"알아유, 그건.\""]], [["‘Then what can you want to do now?’ said the old lady, gaining courage.", "\"그럼 대체 지금 무엇을 하려는 게냐?\" 노부인이 용기를 내며 말했다."]], [["‘I wants to make your flesh creep,’ replied the boy.", "\"마님 살갗에 소름을 돋게 해 드리고 싶구먼유.\" 소년이 대답했다."]], [["This sounded like a very bloodthirsty mode of showing one’s gratitude; and as the old lady did not precisely understand the process by which such a result was to be attained, all her former horrors returned.", "이것은 감사를 표하는 방식치고는 자못 피에 굶주린 소리로 들렸다. 그리고 노부인은 그런 결과가 어떤 과정을 거쳐 얻어질 것인지 정확히 알지 못했으므로, 앞서의 공포가 죄다 되살아났다."]], [["‘What do you think I see in this very arbour last night?’ inquired the boy.", "\"제가 어젯밤에 바로 이 정자에서 무얼 봤는지 아세유?\" 소년이 물었다."]], [["‘Bless us!", "\"맙소사!"], ["What?’ exclaimed the old lady, alarmed at the solemn manner of the corpulent youth.", "무얼?\" 노부인이 이 살집 좋은 청년의 엄숙한 태도에 놀라 외쳤다."]], [["‘The strange gentleman--him as had his arm hurt--a-kissin’ and huggin’-- ‘", "\"그 낯선 신사 양반—팔을 다친 그 양반 말이유—입 맞추고 끌어안고—\""]], [["‘Who, Joe?", "\"누구를 말이냐, 조?"], ["None of the servants, I hope.’", "설마 하녀들 중 하나는 아니겠지.\""]], [["Worser than that,’ roared the fat boy, in the old lady’s ear.", "\"그보다 더 나쁘구먼유.\" 뚱뚱한 소년이 노부인의 귓전에 대고 우렁차게 말했다."]], [["‘Not one of my grandda’aters?’", "\"내 손녀들 중 하나는 아니겠지?\""]], [["‘Worser than that.’", "\"그보다 더 나쁘구먼유.\""]], [["‘Worse than that, Joe!’ said the old lady, who had thought this the extreme limit of human atrocity.", "\"그보다 더 나빠, 조!\" 인간이 저지를 수 있는 흉악함의 극한이 그쯤이라고 여겼던 노부인이 말했다."], ["‘Who was it, Joe?", "\"그게 누구였느냐, 조?"], ["I insist upon knowing.’", "꼭 알아야겠다.\""]], [["The fat boy looked cautiously round, and having concluded his survey, shouted in the old lady’s ear--", "뚱뚱한 소년은 조심스레 사방을 둘러본 뒤, 살핌을 마치고서 노부인의 귓전에 고함쳤다—"]], [["‘Miss Rachael.’", "\"레이철 아씨유.\""]], [["‘What!’ said the old lady, in a shrill tone.", "\"뭐라고!\" 노부인이 날카로운 어조로 말했다."], ["‘Speak louder.’", "\"더 크게 말해라.\""]], [["‘Miss Rachael,’ roared the fat boy.", "\"레이철 아씨유.\" 뚱뚱한 소년이 우렁차게 외쳤다."]], [["‘My da’ater!’", "\"내 딸이!\""]], [["The train of nods which the fat boy gave by way of assent, communicated a blanc-mange like motion to his fat cheeks.", "뚱뚱한 소년이 그렇다는 뜻으로 연거푸 고개를 끄덕이자, 그 통통한 두 뺨이 블랑망제처럼 출렁였다."]], [["‘And she suffered him!’ exclaimed the old lady.", "\"그런데 그 애가 그걸 받아들였단 말이지!\" 노부인이 외쳤다."], ["A grin stole over the fat boy’s features as he said--", "뚱뚱한 소년의 얼굴에 슬며시 히죽거림이 번지더니 그가 말했다—"]], [["‘I see her a-kissin’ of him agin.’", "\"아씨가 그 양반한테 또 입 맞추는 것도 봤구먼유.\""]], [["If Mr. Jingle, from his place of concealment, could have beheld the expression which the old lady’s face assumed at this communication, the probability is that a sudden burst of laughter would have betrayed his close vicinity to the summer-house.", "만약 징글 씨가 그 숨은 자리에서 이 보고를 들은 노부인의 얼굴에 떠오른 표정을 볼 수 있었더라면, 십중팔구 별안간 터져 나온 웃음이 그가 정자 가까이에 있음을 들통나게 했을 것이다."], ["He listened attentively.", "그는 주의 깊게 귀를 기울였다. \"내 허락도 없이!\"—\"그 나이에\"—\"나 같은 가엾고 늙은 여편네가\"—\"내가 죽을 때까지나 기다리지\""], ["Fragments of angry sentences such as, ‘Without my permission!’--‘At her time of life’--‘Miserable old ‘ooman like me’--‘Might have waited till I was dead,’ and so forth, reached his ears; and then he heard the heels of the fat boy’s boots crunching the gravel, as he retired and left the old lady alone.", "따위의 노여운 문장 조각들이 그의 귀에 닿았다. 그러고 나서 그는 뚱뚱한 소년이 물러가며 노부인을 홀로 남겨 둘 때 그 부츠 뒤축이 자갈을 자박자박 밟는 소리를 들었다."]], [["It was a remarkable coincidence perhaps, but it was nevertheless a fact, that Mr. Jingle within five minutes of his arrival at Manor Farm on the preceding night, had inwardly resolved to lay siege to the heart of the spinster aunt, without delay.", "어쩌면 놀라운 우연이라 하겠으나, 어쨌든 사실인즉, 징글 씨는 전날 밤 매너 농장에 도착한 지 채 오 분도 지나지 않아 속으로 지체 없이 미혼의 고모의 마음을 함락시키기로 결심했던 것이다."], ["He had observation enough to see, that his off-hand manner was by no means disagreeable to the fair object of his attack; and he had more than a strong suspicion that she possessed that most desirable of all requisites, a small independence.", "그는 자기의 격의 없는 태도가 그 공략 대상인 아리따운 여인에게 결코 거슬리지 않는다는 것을 알아챌 만한 관찰력이 있었고, 게다가 그녀가 모든 요건 중에서도 가장 바람직한 것, 곧 약간의 독립 재산을 지녔으리라는 강한 의심마저 품고 있었다."], ["The imperative necessity of ousting his rival by some means or other, flashed quickly upon him, and he immediately resolved to adopt certain proceedings tending to that end and object, without a moment’s delay.", "어떻게든 연적을 몰아내야 한다는 절박한 필요성이 그의 머릿속에 퍼뜩 스쳤고, 그는 즉시 그 목적을 향해 일각의 지체도 없이 일정한 조처를 취하기로 마음먹었다."], ["Fielding tells us that man is fire, and woman tow, and the Prince of Darkness sets a light to ‘em. Mr.", "필딩은 우리에게 이르기를, 남자는 불이요 여자는 부싯깃이며 암흑의 군주가 거기에 불을 댕긴다고 했다."], ["Jingle knew that young men, to spinster aunts, are as lighted gas to gunpowder, and he determined to essay the effect of an explosion without loss of time.", "징글 씨는 젊은 남자가 미혼의 고모들에게는 화약에 댄 불붙은 가스와 같음을 알았으니, 그는 한시도 허비하지 않고 폭발의 효과를 시험해 보기로 결심했다."]], [["Full of reflections upon this important decision, he crept from his place of concealment, and, under cover of the shrubs before mentioned, approached the house.", "이 중대한 결심에 대한 갖가지 궁리로 가득 찬 채, 그는 숨어 있던 자리에서 살금살금 빠져나와 앞서 말한 관목들의 엄호 아래 집으로 다가갔다."], ["Fortune seemed determined to favour his design.", "운명은 그의 계획을 편들기로 작정한 듯했다."], ["Mr. Tupman and the rest of the gentlemen left the garden by the side gate just as he obtained a view of it; and the young ladies, he knew, had walked out alone, soon after breakfast.", "텁먼 씨와 나머지 신사들이 마침 그가 옆문을 시야에 넣는 순간 그 옆문으로 정원을 떠났고, 젊은 숙녀들은 아침 식사 직후에 단둘이 산책을 나갔음을 그는 알고 있었다."], ["The coast was clear.", "길은 훤히 트여 있었다."]], [["The breakfast-parlour door was partially open.", "아침 식사 방의 문이 반쯤 열려 있었다."], ["He peeped in.", "그가 안을 들여다보았다."], ["The spinster aunt was knitting.", "미혼의 고모가 뜨개질을 하고 있었다."], ["He coughed; she looked up and smiled.", "그가 기침을 했다. 그녀가 고개를 들고 미소 지었다."], ["Hesitation formed no part of Mr. Alfred Jingle’s character.", "망설임은 앨프리드 징글 씨의 성격에 결코 없는 부분이었다."], ["He laid his finger on his lips mysteriously, walked in, and closed the door.", "그는 비밀스레 손가락을 입술에 갖다 대고는, 안으로 걸어 들어가 문을 닫았다."]], [["‘Miss Wardle,’ said Mr.", "\"워들 양,\""], ["Jingle, with affected earnestness, ‘forgive intrusion--short acquaintance--no time for ceremony--all discovered.’", "징글 씨가 짐짓 꾸민 진지함으로 말했다. \"불쑥 찾아온 것 용서를—사귄 지 짧지만—격식 차릴 겨를 없소—전부 들통났소이다.\""]], [["‘Sir!’ said the spinster aunt, rather astonished by the unexpected apparition and somewhat doubtful of Mr.", "\"네?\""], ["Jingle’s sanity.", "미혼의 고모가 뜻밖의 출현에 다소 놀라며, 또 징글 씨의 정신 상태를 약간 의심하면서 말했다."]], [["‘Hush!’ said Mr.", "\"쉿!\" 징글 씨가 무대 속삭임으로 말했다."], ["Jingle, in a stage-whisper--‘Large boy--dumpling face-- round eyes--rascal!’ Here he shook his head expressively, and the spinster aunt trembled with agitation.", "\"덩치 큰 녀석—만두 같은 얼굴—둥근 눈깔—악당!\" 여기서 그가 의미심장하게 고개를 흔들자, 미혼의 고모는 동요로 몸을 떨었다."]], [["‘I presume you allude to Joseph, Sir?’ said the lady, making an effort to appear composed.", "\"조지프를 두고 하시는 말씀인 듯한데요?\" 부인이 애써 침착해 보이려 하며 말했다."]], [["‘Yes, ma’am--damn that Joe!--treacherous dog, Joe--told the old lady-- old lady furious--wild--raving--arbour--Tupman--kissing and hugging--all that sort of thing--eh, ma’am--eh?’", "\"그렇소, 아가씨—그 조라는 놈, 망할!—배신자 같은 개야, 조는—노부인께 일러바쳤소—노부인 노발대발—펄펄 뛰며—악다구니를—정자—텁먼—입 맞추고 끌어안고—그런 따위 죄다—그렇잖소, 아가씨—그렇지요?\""]], [["‘Mr.", "\"징글 씨,\""], ["Jingle,’ said the spinster aunt, ‘if you come here, Sir, to insult me--’", "미혼의 고모가 말했다. \"당신이 여기 저를 모욕하러 오셨다면—\""]], [["‘Not at all--by no means,’ replied the unabashed Mr.", "\"천만에—결코 아니오.\" 조금도 기죽지 않은 징글 씨가 대답했다."], ["Jingle--‘overheard the tale--came to warn you of your danger--tender my services--prevent the hubbub.", "\"그 얘기를 엿들었소—당신의 위험을 경고하러 왔소—제 도움을 바치려—소동을 막으려고."], ["Never mind--think it an insult--leave the room’--and he turned, as if to carry the threat into execution.", "괜찮소—모욕이라 여기신다면—이 방을 떠나리다.\" 그러고는 그 협박을 실행에 옮기기라도 할 듯 몸을 돌렸다."]], [["‘What shall I do!’ said the poor spinster, bursting into tears.", "\"전 어떡하면 좋아요!\" 가엾은 미혼 여인이 울음을 터뜨리며 말했다."], ["‘My brother will be furious.’", "\"오라버니가 노발대발할 거예요.\""]], [["‘Of course he will,’ said Mr.", "\"물론 그러실 거요.\""], ["Jingle pausing--‘outrageous.’", "징글 씨가 걸음을 멈추며 말했다. \"괘씸한 일이지요.\""]], [["Oh, Mr.", "\"오, 징글 씨, 제가 무슨 말을 할 수 있겠어요!\""], ["Jingle, what can I say!’ exclaimed the spinster aunt, in another flood of despair.", "미혼의 고모가 또 한 차례 절망의 눈물을 쏟으며 외쳤다."]], [["‘Say he dreamt it,’ replied Mr.", "\"꿈을 꾼 거라고 하시오.\""], ["Jingle coolly.", "징글 씨가 냉정하게 대답했다."]], [["A ray of comfort darted across the mind of the spinster aunt at this suggestion.", "이 제안에 한 줄기 위안의 빛이 미혼의 고모의 마음을 가로질렀다."], ["Mr. Jingle perceived it, and followed up his advantage.", "징글 씨가 그것을 알아채고는 자기 우위를 밀고 나갔다."]], [["‘Pooh, pooh!--nothing more easy--blackguard boy--lovely woman--fat boy horsewhipped--you believed--end of the matter--all comfortable.’", "\"흥, 흥!—그보다 쉬운 게 없소—못된 녀석—사랑스러운 여인—뚱뚱한 녀석을 채찍질하고—당신은 믿었던 거고—그걸로 일은 끝—다 편안해지지요.\""]], [["Whether the probability of escaping from the consequences of this ill- timed discovery was delightful to the spinster’s feelings, or whether the hearing herself described as a ‘lovely woman’ softened the asperity of her grief, we know not.", "이 때아닌 발각의 결과에서 벗어날 수 있다는 가능성이 그 미혼 여인의 마음을 기쁘게 한 것인지, 아니면 자기가 \"사랑스러운 여인\"으로 묘사되는 것을 들은 일이 그 비탄의 날을 누그러뜨린 것인지, 우리는 알지 못한다."], ["She blushed slightly, and cast a grateful look on Mr. Jingle.", "그녀는 살짝 얼굴을 붉히며 징글 씨에게 고마운 눈길을 던졌다."]], [["That insinuating gentleman sighed deeply, fixed his eyes on the spinster aunt’s face for a couple of minutes, started melodramatically, and suddenly withdrew them.", "이 알랑거리는 신사는 깊이 한숨을 쉬고는, 미혼의 고모의 얼굴에 한 이 분쯤 시선을 고정했다가, 멜로드라마처럼 흠칫 놀라며 별안간 그 눈길을 거두었다."]], [["‘You seem unhappy, Mr.", "\"불행해 보이시는군요, 징글 씨.\""], ["Jingle,’ said the lady, in a plaintive voice.", "부인이 애처로운 목소리로 말했다."], ["‘May I show my gratitude for your kind interference, by inquiring into the cause, with a view, if possible, to its removal?’", "\"당신의 친절한 개입에 감사하는 뜻으로, 그 까닭을 여쭈어 보아도 될까요? 가능하다면 그것을 없애 드릴 수 있도록 말이에요.\""]], [["‘Ha!’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"하!\""], ["Jingle, with another start--‘removal!", "징글 씨가 다시 한 번 흠칫하며 외쳤다. \"없앤다고요!"], ["remove my unhappiness, and your love bestowed upon a man who is insensible to the blessing--who even now contemplates a design upon the affections of the niece of the creature who--but no; he is my friend; I will not expose his vices.", "제 불행을 없애고, 그 축복을 느낄 줄도 모르는 사내에게 당신의 사랑을 베푼다—바로 지금 이 순간에도 그—하지만 안 되오, 그자는 내 친구요, 그자의 악덕을 폭로하지는 않으리다. 워들 양—안녕히!\""], ["Miss Wardle--farewell!’ At the conclusion of this address, the most consecutive he was ever known to utter, Mr. Jingle applied to his eyes the remnant of a handkerchief before noticed, and turned towards the door.", "그가 일찍이 입에 올린 가운데 가장 조리 있는 이 연설을 마치자마자, 징글 씨는 앞서 언급한 손수건의 남은 자락을 두 눈에 갖다 대고는 문 쪽으로 돌아섰다."]], [["‘Stay, Mr.", "\"멈추세요, 징글 씨!\""], ["Jingle!’ said the spinster aunt emphatically.", "미혼의 고모가 힘주어 말했다."], ["‘You have made an allusion to Mr. Tupman--explain it.’", "\"방금 텁먼 씨를 두고 무언가 암시하셨어요—그걸 설명해 주세요.\""]], [["‘Never!’ exclaimed Jingle, with a professional (i.e., theatrical) air.", "\"결코 안 되오!\" 징글이 직업적인(즉 연극적인) 기색으로 외쳤다."], ["‘Never!’ and, by way of showing that he had no desire to be questioned further, he drew a chair close to that of the spinster aunt and sat down.", "\"결코!\" 그러고는 더 이상 캐물어지고 싶지 않다는 뜻을 보이려는 듯, 미혼의 고모의 의자 가까이로 자기 의자를 끌어다 놓고 앉았다."]], [["‘Mr. Jingle,’ said the aunt, ‘I entreat--I implore you, if there is any dreadful mystery connected with Mr.", "\"징글 씨,\""], ["Tupman, reveal it.’", "고모가 말했다. \"간청해요—애원해요, 만약 텁먼 씨와 관련된 무슨 끔찍한 비밀이 있다면, 그것을 밝혀 주세요.\""]], [["‘Can I,’ said Mr. Jingle, fixing his eyes on the aunt’s face--‘can I see--lovely creature--sacrificed at the shrine--heartless avarice!’ He appeared to be struggling with various conflicting emotions for a few seconds, and then said in a low voice-- ‘Tupman only wants your money.’", "\"내가 차마,\" 징글 씨가 고모의 얼굴에 시선을 고정한 채 말했다. \"내가 차마 볼 수 있겠소—사랑스러운 여인이—제단에 제물로 바쳐지는 꼴을—무정한 탐욕에!\" 그는 몇 초 동안 갖가지 상충하는 감정과 씨름하는 듯하더니, 이윽고 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"텁먼은 당신의 돈만 노릴 뿐이오.\""]], [["‘The wretch!’ exclaimed the spinster, with energetic indignation.", "\"비열한 인간 같으니!\" 미혼 여인이 격렬한 분노로 외쳤다."], ["(Mr. Jingle’s doubts were resolved.", "(징글 씨의 의심이 풀렸다."], ["She had money.)", "그녀에게는 정말로 돈이 있었던 것이다.)"]], [["‘More than that,’ said Jingle--‘loves another.’", "\"그뿐이 아니오.\" 징글이 말했다. \"다른 여자를 사랑하고 있소.\""]], [["‘Another!’ ejaculated the spinster.", "\"다른 여자라니!\" 미혼 여인이 토해 내듯 말했다."], ["‘Who?’", "\"누구를?\""]], [["Short girl--black eyes--niece Emily.’", "\"키 작은 아가씨—검은 눈—조카 에밀리.\""]], [["There was a pause.", "잠시 침묵이 흘렀다."]], [["Now, if there was one individual in the whole world, of whom the spinster aunt entertained a mortal and deep-rooted jealousy, it was this identical niece.", "자, 온 세상에서 미혼의 고모가 죽도록 깊이 뿌리박힌 질투심을 품은 단 한 사람이 있었다면, 그것은 바로 다름 아닌 이 조카였다."], ["The colour rushed over her face and neck, and she tossed her head in silence with an air of ineffable contempt.", "핏기가 그녀의 얼굴과 목으로 확 치솟았고, 그녀는 형언할 수 없는 경멸의 기색으로 말없이 고개를 홱 젖혔다."], ["At last, biting her thin lips, and bridling up, she said--", "마침내 그녀는 얇은 입술을 깨물고 고개를 빳빳이 세우며 말했다—\n\"그럴 리 없어요. 믿지 않겠어요.\""]], [["‘It can’t be.", "\"두 사람을 지켜보시오.\""], ["I won’t believe it.’", "징글이 말했다."]], [["‘Watch ‘em,’ said Jingle.", "\"그러겠어요.\" 고모가 말했다."]], [["‘I will,’ said the aunt.", "\"그자의 눈길을 지켜보시오.\""]], [["‘Watch his looks.’", "\"그러겠어요.\""]], [["‘I will.’\n‘His whispers.’", "\"그자의 속삭임을.\""]], [["‘I will.’", "\"그러겠어요.\""]], [["‘He’ll sit next her at table.’", "\"그자는 식탁에서 그 애 옆에 앉을 거요.\""]], [["‘Let him.’", "\"그러라죠.\""]], [["‘He’ll flatter her.’", "\"그 애에게 아첨할 거요.\""]], [["‘Let him.’", "\"그러라죠.\""]], [["‘He’ll pay her every possible attention.’", "\"그 애에게 온갖 정성을 다할 거요.\""]], [["‘Let him.’", "\"그러라죠.\""]], [["‘And he’ll cut you.’", "\"그리고 당신은 본 척도 안 할 거요.\""]], [["‘Cut me!’ screamed the spinster aunt.", "\"나를 본 척도 안 한다고요!\" 미혼의 고모가 비명을 질렀다."], ["‘he cut me; will he!’ and she trembled with rage and disappointment.", "\"그자가 나를 본 척도 안 해, 그렇겠지!\" 그러고는 분노와 실망으로 몸을 떨었다."]], [["‘You will convince yourself?’ said Jingle.", "\"직접 확인해 보시겠소?\" 징글이 말했다."]], [["‘I will.’", "\"그러겠어요.\""]], [["‘You’ll show your spirit?’", "\"기개를 보이시겠소?\""]], [["‘I will.’", "\"그러겠어요.\""]], [["You’ll not have him afterwards?’\n‘Never.’", "\"그러고 나면 그자를 받아들이지 않으시겠소?\""]], [["‘You’ll take somebody else?’", "\"결코 안 받겠어요.\" \"다른 사람을 택하시겠소?\""]], [["Yes.’", "\"네.\""]], [["‘You shall.’", "\"그러셔야 하오.\""]], [["Mr. Jingle fell on his knees, remained thereupon for five minutes thereafter; and rose the accepted lover of the spinster aunt-- conditionally upon Mr.", "징글 씨가 무릎을 꿇었고, 그 자세로 그 후 오 분 동안 머물렀다."], ["Tupman’s perjury being made clear and manifest.", "그리고 일어섰을 때 그는 미혼의 고모가 받아들인 연인이 되어 있었으니—다만 텁먼 씨의 거짓 맹세가 분명하고 명백하게 드러난다는 조건부였다."]], [["The burden of proof lay with Mr. Alfred Jingle; and he produced his evidence that very day at dinner.", "입증의 책임은 앨프리드 징글 씨에게 있었고, 그는 바로 그날 만찬에서 자기 증거를 내놓았다."], ["The spinster aunt could hardly believe her eyes. Mr.", "미혼의 고모는 자기 눈을 도무지 믿을 수가 없었다."], ["Tracy Tupman was established at Emily’s side, ogling, whispering, and smiling, in opposition to Mr. Snodgrass.", "트레이시 텁먼 씨가 에밀리의 곁에 자리 잡고서, 스노드그래스 씨에 맞서 추파를 던지고 속삭이고 미소 짓고 있었던 것이다."], ["Not a word, not a look, not a glance, did he bestow upon his heart’s pride of the evening before.", "그는 전날 저녁 자기 마음의 자랑이었던 여인에게 말 한마디, 표정 하나, 눈길 한 번 주지 않았다."]], [["‘Damn that boy!’ thought old Mr.", "\"저 녀석, 망할!\""], ["Wardle to himself.--He had heard the story from his mother.", "워들 노인이 속으로 생각했다.—그는 어머니에게서 그 이야기를 들은 터였다."], ["‘Damn that boy!", "\"저 녀석, 망할 놈!"], ["He must have been asleep.", "분명 잠들어 있었을 게야."], ["It’s all imagination.’", "죄다 상상일 뿐이야.\""]], [["‘Traitor!’ thought the spinster aunt.", "\"배신자!\" 미혼의 고모가 생각했다."], ["‘Dear Mr. Jingle was not deceiving me.", "\"친애하는 징글 씨가 나를 속인 게 아니었어."], ["Ugh!", "윽!"], ["how I hate the wretch!’", "저 비열한 인간이 어찌나 미운지!\""]], [["The following conversation may serve to explain to our readers this apparently unaccountable alteration of deportment on the part of Mr. Tracy Tupman.", "다음의 대화는 트레이시 텁먼 씨 편에서 보인 이 겉보기에 설명할 수 없는 태도 변화를 우리 독자들에게 풀이해 주는 데 도움이 될 것이다."]], [["The time was evening; the scene the garden.", "때는 저녁이었고, 무대는 정원이었다."], ["There were two figures walking in a side path; one was rather short and stout; the other tall and slim.", "두 사람의 형체가 한쪽 오솔길을 거닐고 있었으니, 하나는 다소 키가 작고 통통했고, 다른 하나는 키가 크고 호리호리했다."], ["They were Mr. Tupman and Mr.", "그들은 텁먼 씨와 징글 씨였다."], ["Jingle. The stout figure commenced the dialogue.", "통통한 형체가 대화를 시작했다."]], [["‘How did I do it?’ he inquired.", "\"내가 어떻게 했소?\" 그가 물었다."]], [["‘Splendid--capital--couldn’t act better myself--you must repeat the part to-morrow--every evening till further notice.’", "\"훌륭했소—기막혔소—나라도 그보다 더 잘 연기하진 못했을 거요—내일도 그 배역을 되풀이하셔야 하오—추후 통지가 있을 때까지 매일 저녁.\""]], [["‘Does Rachael still wish it?’", "\"레이철이 아직도 그걸 바라오?\""]], [["‘Of course--she don’t like it--but must be done--avert suspicion--afraid of her brother--says there’s no help for it--only a few days more--when old folks blinded--crown your happiness.’", "\"물론이지요—그녀도 좋아하진 않소—하지만 해야만 하오—의심을 피하려고—오라비를 두려워하니—달리 도리가 없다고 합디다—며칠만 더—늙은이들 눈을 가린 다음—당신 행복에 면류관을 씌우는 거요.\""]], [["‘Any message?’", "\"전할 말이라도?\""]], [["‘Love--best love--kindest regards--unalterable affection.", "\"사랑—더없는 사랑—더없이 다정한 안부—변치 않는 애정."], ["Can I say anything for you?’", "당신을 위해 무어라 전해 드릴까요?\""]], [["‘My dear fellow,’ replied the unsuspicious Mr.", "\"이보게, 친구.\""], ["Tupman, fervently grasping his ‘friend’s’ hand--‘carry my best love--say how hard I find it to dissemble--say anything that’s kind: but add how sensible I am of the necessity of the suggestion she made to me, through you, this morning.", "의심을 모르는 텁먼 씨가 '친구'의 손을 열렬히 움켜쥐며 대답했다. \"내 더없는 사랑을 전해 주게—이렇게 시치미를 떼는 것이 내게 얼마나 힘든지 말해 주게—무어든 다정한 말을 전해 주되, 오늘 아침 그녀가 자네를 통해 내게 한 그 제안의 필요성을 내가 얼마나 잘 헤아리고 있는지도 덧붙여 주게."], ["Say I applaud her wisdom and admire her discretion.’", "내가 그녀의 지혜에 갈채를 보내고 그 분별을 흠모한다고 말해 주게.\""]], [["I will.", "\"그러리다."], ["Anything more?’", "더 있소?\""]], [["‘Nothing, only add how ardently I long for the time when I may call her mine, and all dissimulation may be unnecessary.’", "\"없네, 다만 그녀를 내 사람이라 부르고 모든 시치미가 더는 필요 없게 될 그날을 내가 얼마나 애타게 고대하는지만 덧붙여 주게.\""]], [["‘Certainly, certainly.", "\"그럼요, 그럼요."], ["Anything more?’", "더 있소?\""]], [["‘Oh, my friend!’ said poor Mr.", "\"오, 친구!\" 가엾은 텁먼 씨가 다시 한 번 동행의 손을 움켜쥐며 말했다."], ["Tupman, again grasping the hand of his companion, ‘receive my warmest thanks for your disinterested kindness; and forgive me if I have ever, even in thought, done you the injustice of supposing that you could stand in my way.", "\"자네의 사심 없는 친절에 가장 따뜻한 감사를 받아 주게. 그리고 내가 일찍이, 생각으로나마, 자네가 내 앞길을 가로막을 수 있으리라 의심하는 부당한 짓을 저질렀다면 용서해 주게."], ["My dear friend, can I ever repay you?’", "친애하는 친구여, 내가 자네에게 이 은혜를 갚을 수 있을까?\""]], [["‘Don’t talk of it,’ replied Mr.", "\"그런 말 마시오.\""], ["Jingle.", "징글 씨가 대답했다."], ["He stopped short, as if suddenly recollecting something, and said--‘By the bye--can’t spare ten pounds, can you?--very particular purpose--pay you in three days.’", "그는 무언가 문득 떠오른 듯 우뚝 멈춰 서서 말했다. \"참, 그런데—혹 십 파운드만 융통해 줄 수 있겠소?—아주 긴한 데가 있어서—사흘 안에 갚으리다.\""]], [["‘I dare say I can,’ replied Mr.", "\"아마 할 수 있을 걸세.\""], ["Tupman, in the fulness of his heart.", "텁먼 씨가 충만한 마음으로 대답했다."], ["‘Three days, you say?’", "\"사흘이라 했나?\""]], [["‘Only three days--all over then--no more difficulties.’ Mr.", "\"사흘뿐이오—그럼 다 끝나오—더는 어려움이 없을 거요.\""], ["Tupman counted the money into his companion’s hand, and he dropped it piece by piece into his pocket, as they walked towards the house.", "텁먼 씨가 돈을 세어 동행의 손에 쥐여 주자, 그는 집을 향해 걸으면서 그것을 한 닢 한 닢 자기 주머니에 떨어뜨려 넣었다."]], [["‘Be careful,’ said Mr.", "\"조심하시오.\" 징글 씨가 말했다."], ["Jingle--‘not a look.’", "\"눈길 하나도 주지 말고.\""]], [["‘Not a wink,’ said Mr.", "\"눈 깜빡 하나도.\""], ["Tupman.", "텁먼 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Not a syllable.’", "\"한마디도.\""]], [["‘Not a whisper.’", "\"속삭임 하나도.\""]], [["‘All your attentions to the niece--rather rude, than otherwise, to the aunt--only way of deceiving the old ones.’", "\"당신의 온갖 정성은 죄다 조카에게—고모에게는 차라리 무례할 만큼—그것만이 늙은이들을 속이는 길이오.\""]], [["‘I’ll take care,’ said Mr.", "\"명심하겠네.\""], ["Tupman aloud.", "텁먼 씨가 소리 내어 말했다."]], [["‘And I’ll take care,’ said Mr.", "\"그리고 나도 명심하겠소.\""], ["Jingle internally; and they entered the house.", "징글 씨가 속으로 말했다. 그리고 두 사람은 집 안으로 들어섰다."]], [["The scene of that afternoon was repeated that evening, and on the three afternoons and evenings next ensuing. On the fourth, the host was in high spirits, for he had satisfied himself that there was no ground for the charge against Mr.", "그날 오후의 장면은 그날 저녁에도 되풀이되었고, 이어지는 사흘간의 오후와 저녁마다 거듭되었다."], ["Tupman. So was Mr.", "나흘째 되는 날, 주인은 기분이 매우 좋았으니, 텁먼 씨에 대한 혐의에 아무 근거가 없음을 스스로 납득했기 때문이었다."], ["Tupman, for Mr. Jingle had told him that his affair would soon be brought to a crisis.", "텁먼 씨도 기분이 좋았으니, 징글 씨가 그의 일이 곧 결정적 국면에 이르리라 일러 주었기 때문이었다."], ["So was Mr. Pickwick, for he was seldom otherwise.", "픽윅 씨도 기분이 좋았으니, 그가 그렇지 않은 적은 드물었기 때문이었다."], ["So was not Mr. Snodgrass, for he had grown jealous of Mr.", "스노드그래스 씨는 기분이 좋지 않았으니, 그가 텁먼 씨를 질투하게 되었기 때문이었다."], ["Tupman. So was the old lady, for she had been winning at whist.", "노부인은 기분이 좋았으니, 휘스트에서 이기고 있었기 때문이었다."], ["So were Mr. Jingle and Miss Wardle, for reasons of sufficient importance in this eventful history to be narrated in another chapter.", "징글 씨와 워들 양도 기분이 좋았으니, 그 까닭인즉 이 파란만장한 이야기에서 따로 한 장을 할애해 서술할 만큼 충분히 중요한 것이었다."]], [["# CHAPTER IX. A DISCOVERY AND A CHASE", "# CHAPTER IX. A DISCOVERY AND A CHASE"]], [["The supper was ready laid, the chairs were drawn round the table, bottles, jugs, and glasses were arranged upon the sideboard, and everything betokened the approach of the most convivial period in the whole four-and-twenty hours.", "저녁 식사 준비가 다 되어, 의자들이 식탁 둘레로 끌어당겨져 있었고, 술병과 주전자와 잔들이 사이드보드 위에 가지런히 놓여 있었으니, 모든 것이 하루 스물네 시간 가운데 가장 흥겨운 때가 다가오고 있음을 알리고 있었다."]], [["‘Where’s Rachael?’ said Mr.", "\"레이철은 어디 있지?\""], ["Wardle.", "워들 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Ay, and Jingle?’ added Mr.", "\"아, 그러고 보니 징글은 어디 있소?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 덧붙였다."]], [["‘Dear me,’ said the host, ‘I wonder I haven’t missed him before.", "\"이런,\" 주인이 말했다."], ["Why, I don’t think I’ve heard his voice for two hours at least.", "\"어째서 진작 그가 없는 걸 몰랐는지 모르겠군. 글쎄, 적어도 두 시간은 그의 목소리를 듣지 못한 것 같아."], ["Emily, my dear, ring the bell.’", "에밀리야, 종을 좀 울려라.\""]], [["The bell was rung, and the fat boy appeared.", "종이 울렸고, 뚱뚱한 소년이 나타났다."]], [["‘Where’s Miss Rachael?’ He couldn’t say.", "\"레이철 양은 어디 있느냐?\" 그는 알지 못했다."]], [["‘Where’s Mr. Jingle, then?’ He didn’t know.", "\"그럼 징글 씨는 어디 있느냐?\""], ["Everybody looked surprised.", "그는 모른다고 했다. 모두가 놀란 표정이었다."], ["It was late--past eleven o’clock.", "밤이 깊었다—열한 시가 지나 있었다."], ["Mr. Tupman laughed in his sleeve.", "터프먼 씨는 소매 속에서 혼자 웃었다."], ["They were loitering somewhere, talking about him.", "그들은 어디선가 어슬렁거리며 자기 이야기를 하고 있을 것이다."], ["Ha, ha!", "하, 하!"], ["capital notion that--funny.", "그거 참 기막힌 생각이야—재미있군."]], [["‘Never mind,’ said Wardle, after a short pause.", "\"신경 쓸 것 없네,\" 워들이 잠깐 멈췄다가 말했다."], ["‘They’ll turn up presently, I dare say.", "\"곧 나타나겠지, 틀림없이."], ["I never wait supper for anybody.’", "나는 누구를 기다리느라 저녁을 미루는 법이 없다네.\""]], [["‘Excellent rule, that,’ said Mr.", "\"그거 훌륭한 원칙이군요,\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick--‘admirable.’", "\"참으로 감탄할 만합니다.\""]], [["‘Pray, sit down,’ said the host.", "\"자, 앉으시지요,\" 주인이 말했다."]], [["‘Certainly’ said Mr.", "\"그러지요,\""], ["Pickwick; and down they sat.", "픽윅 씨가 말하니, 모두가 자리에 앉았다."]], [["There was a gigantic round of cold beef on the table, and Mr. Pickwick was supplied with a plentiful portion of it.", "식탁 위에는 거대한 차가운 소고기 덩어리가 놓여 있었고, 픽윅 씨에게도 푸짐한 몫이 돌아갔다."], ["He had raised his fork to his lips, and was on the very point of opening his mouth for the reception of a piece of beef, when the hum of many voices suddenly arose in the kitchen.", "그가 포크를 입술로 들어올리고는 막 입을 벌려 소고기 한 점을 받아들이려는 바로 그 순간, 부엌에서 여러 목소리가 웅성거리는 소리가 갑작스레 일었다."], ["He paused, and laid down his fork. Mr.", "그는 동작을 멈추고 포크를 내려놓았다."], ["Wardle paused too, and insensibly released his hold of the carving-knife, which remained inserted in the beef.", "워들 씨도 멈추고는 자기도 모르게 고기 써는 칼을 쥐었던 손을 놓았는데, 칼은 소고기에 꽂힌 채 그대로 남아 있었다."], ["He looked at Mr. Pickwick.", "그는 픽윅 씨를 바라보았다."], ["Mr. Pickwick looked at him.", "픽윅 씨도 그를 바라보았다."]], [["Heavy footsteps were heard in the passage; the parlour door was suddenly burst open; and the man who had cleaned Mr.", "복도에서 묵직한 발소리가 들렸다."], ["Pickwick’s boots on his first arrival, rushed into the room, followed by the fat boy and all the domestics.", "응접실 문이 갑자기 벌컥 열리더니, 픽윅 씨가 처음 도착했을 때 그의 부츠를 닦아 주었던 사내가 방 안으로 뛰어들었고, 뒤이어 뚱뚱한 소년과 하인들이 모두 따라 들어왔다."]], [["‘What the devil’s the meaning of this?’ exclaimed the host.", "\"이게 도대체 무슨 영문이냐?\" 주인이 외쳤다."]], [["‘The kitchen chimney ain’t a-fire, is it, Emma?’ inquired the old lady.", "\"부엌 굴뚝에 불난 거 아니지, 그렇지, 엠마?\" 노부인이 물었다."]], [["‘Lor, grandma!", "\"어머나, 할머니!"], ["No,’ screamed both the young ladies.", "아니에요,\" 두 젊은 아가씨가 동시에 소리쳤다."]], [["‘What’s the matter?’ roared the master of the house.", "\"무슨 일이냐?\" 집주인이 고함쳤다."]], [["The man gasped for breath, and faintly ejaculated--", "사내는 숨을 헐떡이며 가까스로 말을 토해 냈다."]], [["‘They ha’ gone, mas’r!--gone right clean off, Sir!’ (At this juncture Mr.", "\"그분들이 떠났어유, 나리!—싹 가버렸구먼유, 나리!\""], ["Tupman was observed to lay down his knife and fork, and to turn very pale.)", "(이 대목에서 터프먼 씨가 나이프와 포크를 내려놓고 얼굴이 몹시 창백해지는 것이 보였다.)"]], [["‘Who’s gone?’ said Mr.", "\"누가 갔다는 거냐?\""], ["Wardle fiercely.", "워들 씨가 사납게 물었다."]], [["‘Mus’r Jingle and Miss Rachael, in a po’-chay, from Blue Lion, Muggleton.", "\"징글 씨하구 레이철 양이유, 머글턴 블루 라이언에서 역마차 타구유."], ["I was there; but I couldn’t stop ‘em; so I run off to tell ‘ee.’", "지가 거기 있었는디, 막을 수가 없어서, 그래 알려드릴라구 달려왔구먼유.\""]], [["‘I paid his expenses!’ said Mr.", "\"내가 그놈 여비를 댔는데!\""], ["Tupman, jumping up frantically.", "터프먼 씨가 미친 듯이 벌떡 일어서며 말했다."], ["‘He’s got ten pounds of mine!--stop him!--he’s swindled me!--I won’t bear it!- -I’ll have justice, Pickwick!--I won’t stand it!’ and with sundry incoherent exclamations of the like nature, the unhappy gentleman spun round and round the apartment, in a transport of frenzy.", "\"그놈한테 내 돈 십 파운드가 있어!—저놈을 잡아!—저놈이 날 속였어!—난 못 참아!—정의를 바로 세울 테야, 픽윅!—난 그냥 두지 않겠어!\" 그러고는 이와 비슷한 두서없는 외침들을 잇따라 내지르며, 그 불행한 신사는 광란의 발작에 사로잡혀 방 안을 빙글빙글 돌았다."]], [["‘Lord preserve us!’ ejaculated Mr.", "\"맙소사!\""], ["Pickwick, eyeing the extraordinary gestures of his friend with terrified surprise.", "픽윅 씨가 친구의 기이한 몸짓을 겁에 질린 놀라움으로 바라보며 외쳤다. \"이 사람이 미쳤구나!"], ["‘He’s gone mad! What shall we do?’", "어떻게 하면 좋단 말인가?\""]], [["Do!’ said the stout old host, who regarded only the last words of the sentence.", "\"하면 되지!\" 그 문장의 마지막 말만 귀에 담은 건장한 노주인이 말했다."], ["‘Put the horse in the gig!", "\"이륜마차에 말을 매시오!"], ["I’ll get a chaise at the Lion, and follow ‘em instantly.", "나는 라이언에서 사륜마차를 구해 당장 뒤쫓겠소."], ["Where?’--he exclaimed, as the man ran out to execute the commission--‘where’s that villain, Joe?’", "어디 있느냐?\"—그가 외쳤으니, 사내가 명령을 수행하러 달려 나간 참이었다—\"그 악당 조는 어디 있느냐?\""]], [["‘Here I am!", "\"여기 있구먼유!"], ["but I hain’t a willin,’ replied a voice.", "근디 지는 악당이 아니유,\" 한 목소리가 대답했다."], ["It was the fat boy’s.", "뚱뚱한 소년의 목소리였다."]], [["‘Let me get at him, Pickwick,’ cried Wardle, as he rushed at the ill- starred youth.", "\"저놈한테 좀 가게 해 주시오, 픽윅,\" 워들이 그 운수 사나운 젊은이에게 달려들며 외쳤다."], ["‘He was bribed by that scoundrel, Jingle, to put me on a wrong scent, by telling a cock-and-bull story of my sister and your friend Tupman!’ (Here Mr.", "\"저놈이 그 악당 징글한테 매수당해서, 내 여동생과 자네 친구 터프먼에 관한 터무니없는 거짓말을 늘어놓고는 나를 엉뚱한 데로 따돌린 거요!\""], ["Tupman sank into a chair.) ‘Let me get at him!’", "(이 대목에서 터프먼 씨가 의자에 털썩 주저앉았다.) \"저놈한테 가게 해 주시오!\""]], [["‘Don’t let him!’ screamed all the women, above whose exclamations the blubbering of the fat boy was distinctly audible.", "\"막아요!\" 여자들이 모두 비명을 질렀는데, 그 외침들 너머로 뚱뚱한 소년이 엉엉 우는 소리가 또렷이 들렸다."]], [["‘I won’t be held!’ cried the old man.", "\"날 붙들지 마시오!\" 노인이 소리쳤다."], ["‘Mr. Winkle, take your hands off.", "\"윈클 씨, 손을 떼시오."], ["Mr. Pickwick, let me go, sir!’", "픽윅 씨, 날 놔주시오, 어서!\""]], [["It was a beautiful sight, in that moment of turmoil and confusion, to behold the placid and philosophical expression of Mr. Pickwick’s face, albeit somewhat flushed with exertion, as he stood with his arms firmly clasped round the extensive waist of their corpulent host, thus restraining the impetuosity of his passion, while the fat boy was scratched, and pulled, and pushed from the room by all the females congregated therein.", "소란과 혼란의 그 순간에, 픽윅 씨의 얼굴에 떠오른 평온하고 철학적인 표정을 바라보는 것은 참으로 아름다운 광경이었으니, 비록 안간힘을 쓰느라 다소 상기되긴 했지만, 그는 두 팔로 그 뚱뚱한 주인의 널찍한 허리를 단단히 끌어안은 채 그의 격정의 격렬함을 억누르고 있었고, 그러는 동안 뚱뚱한 소년은 그곳에 모인 모든 여자들에게 할퀴이고, 잡아당겨지고, 떠밀려 방에서 쫓겨났다."], ["He had no sooner released his hold, than the man entered to announce that the gig was ready.", "그가 손을 놓기 무섭게, 그 사내가 들어와 이륜마차가 준비되었음을 알렸다."]], [["‘Don’t let him go alone!’ screamed the females.", "\"저분 혼자 보내지 마요!\" 여자들이 비명을 질렀다."], ["‘He’ll kill somebody!’", "\"누굴 죽이고 말 거예요!\""]], [["‘I’ll go with him,’ said Mr.", "\"내가 함께 가겠습니다,\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘You’re a good fellow, Pickwick,’ said the host, grasping his hand.", "\"자네 참 좋은 사람일세, 픽윅,\" 주인이 그의 손을 꼭 잡으며 말했다."], ["‘Emma, give Mr. Pickwick a shawl to tie round his neck--make haste.", "\"엠마, 픽윅 씨가 목에 두를 숄을 갖다 드려라—서둘러."], ["Look after your grandmother, girls; she has fainted away.", "얘들아, 할머니를 돌봐 드려라. 기절하셨다."], ["Now then, are you ready?’", "자, 그럼, 준비되었소?\""]], [["Mr. Pickwick’s mouth and chin having been hastily enveloped in a large shawl, his hat having been put on his head, and his greatcoat thrown over his arm, he replied in the affirmative.", "픽윅 씨는 입과 턱을 커다란 숄로 황급히 둘둘 감싸고, 모자를 머리에 쓰고, 외투를 팔에 걸치고는 그렇다고 대답했다."]], [["They jumped into the gig.", "두 사람은 이륜마차에 뛰어올랐다. \"고삐를 늦춰라, 톰!\""], ["‘Give her her head, Tom,’ cried the host; and away they went, down the narrow lanes; jolting in and out of the cart- ruts, and bumping up against the hedges on either side, as if they would go to pieces every moment.", "주인이 외쳤다. 그렇게 그들은 좁은 길을 따라 내달렸으니, 수레바퀴 자국을 들락날락 덜컹거리고, 양쪽 산울타리에 쿵쿵 부딪치며, 금방이라도 산산조각이 날 것만 같았다."]], [["‘How much are they ahead?’ shouted Wardle, as they drove up to the door of the Blue Lion, round which a little crowd had collected, late as it was.", "\"얼마나 앞섰소?\" 워들이 외쳤으니, 그들은 블루 라이언의 문 앞으로 마차를 몰아 댔는데, 그 둘레에는 늦은 시각인데도 작은 무리가 모여 있었다."]], [["‘Not above three-quarters of an hour,’ was everybody’s reply.", "\"기껏해야 사십오 분쯤 앞섰을 겁니다,\" 다들 그렇게 대답했다."]], [["‘Chaise-and-four directly!--out with ‘em!", "\"당장 사두마차를! 끌어내시오!"], ["Put up the gig afterwards.’", "이륜마차는 나중에 들여놓고.\""]], [["‘Now, boys!’ cried the landlord--‘chaise-and-four out--make haste--look alive there!’", "\"자, 얘들아!\" 여관 주인이 외쳤다. \"사두마차 끌어내라—서둘러—꾸물대지 마라!\""]], [["Away ran the hostlers and the boys. The lanterns glimmered, as the men ran to and fro; the horses’ hoofs clattered on the uneven paving of the yard; the chaise rumbled as it was drawn out of the coach-house; and all was noise and bustle.", "마부들과 일꾼 소년들이 달려 나갔다. 사람들이 이리저리 뛰어다니자 등불이 깜빡거렸다. 말발굽이 마당의 울퉁불퉁한 포석 위에서 또각거렸다. 마차가 차고에서 끌려나오며 덜커덕거렸다. 온통 소음과 부산함뿐이었다."]], [["‘Now then!--is that chaise coming out to-night?’ cried Wardle.", "\"자, 어떻게 됐나!—그 마차는 오늘 밤 안에 나오는 거냐?\" 워들이 외쳤다."]], [["‘Coming down the yard now, Sir,’ replied the hostler.", "\"지금 마당으로 내려오고 있습니다, 나리,\" 마부가 대답했다."]], [["Out came the chaise--in went the horses--on sprang the boys--in got the travellers.", "마차가 나오고—말들이 매어지고—소년들이 뛰어오르고—여행자들이 올라탔다."]], [["‘Mind--the seven-mile stage in less than half an hour!’ shouted Wardle.", "\"명심해라—칠 마일 구간을 삼십 분 안에!\" 워들이 외쳤다."]], [["‘Off with you!’", "\"자, 출발!\""]], [["The boys applied whip and spur, the waiters shouted, the hostlers cheered, and away they went, fast and furiously.", "소년들이 채찍과 박차를 가하고, 종업원들이 소리치고, 마부들이 환호하니, 그들은 빠르고 맹렬하게 내달렸다."]], [["‘Pretty situation,’ thought Mr.", "\"근사한 처지로군,\""], ["Pickwick, when he had had a moment’s time for reflection.", "픽윅 씨는 잠시 생각할 틈이 생기자 속으로 중얼거렸다."], ["‘Pretty situation for the general chairman of the Pickwick Club.", "\"픽윅 클럽의 회장이라는 사람이 처한 근사한 처지야."], ["Damp chaise--strange horses--fifteen miles an hour--and twelve o’clock at night!’", "축축한 마차에—낯선 말들에—시속 십오 마일에—게다가 한밤중 열두 시라니!\""]], [["For the first three or four miles, not a word was spoken by either of the gentlemen, each being too much immersed in his own reflections to address any observations to his companion.", "처음 삼사 마일 동안은 두 신사 누구도 한마디도 하지 않았으니, 저마다 제 생각에 너무 깊이 빠져 동행에게 무슨 말을 건넬 겨를이 없었다."], ["When they had gone over that much ground, however, and the horses getting thoroughly warmed began to do their work in really good style, Mr. Pickwick became too much exhilarated with the rapidity of the motion, to remain any longer perfectly mute.", "그러나 그만큼 길을 달려, 말들이 완전히 몸이 풀려 정말로 멋들어지게 제 일을 해내기 시작하자, 픽윅 씨는 그 빠른 움직임에 너무도 흥이 올라 더는 입을 다물고 있을 수가 없었다."]], [["‘We’re sure to catch them, I think,’ said he.", "\"우리가 꼭 그들을 따라잡을 것 같소,\" 그가 말했다."]], [["‘Hope so,’ replied his companion.", "\"그러길 바라오,\" 동행이 대답했다."]], [["‘Fine night,’ said Mr.", "\"좋은 밤이로군요,\""], ["Pickwick, looking up at the moon, which was shining brightly.", "픽윅 씨가 환하게 빛나는 달을 올려다보며 말했다."]], [["‘So much the worse,’ returned Wardle; ‘for they’ll have had all the advantage of the moonlight to get the start of us, and we shall lose it.", "\"그만큼 더 나쁘지요,\" 워들이 받았다."], ["It will have gone down in another hour.’", "\"저들은 달빛 덕을 톡톡히 보며 우릴 앞질렀을 테고, 우린 그 덕을 못 보게 될 테니. 한 시간만 지나면 달이 져 버릴 거요.\""]], [["‘It will be rather unpleasant going at this rate in the dark, won’t it?’ inquired Mr.", "\"이런 속도로 어둠 속을 달리려면 꽤 불쾌하지 않겠습니까?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘I dare say it will,’ replied his friend dryly.", "\"아마 그럴 거요,\" 친구가 무뚝뚝하게 대답했다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick’s temporary excitement began to sober down a little, as he reflected upon the inconveniences and dangers of the expedition in which he had so thoughtlessly embarked.", "픽윅 씨의 일시적인 흥분은 차츰 가라앉기 시작했으니, 그가 그토록 경솔하게 뛰어든 이 원정의 불편함과 위험을 곰곰 생각하게 된 까닭이었다."], ["He was roused by a loud shouting of the post-boy on the leader.", "선두 말에 탄 마부 소년이 큰 소리로 외치는 바람에 그는 정신이 번쩍 들었다."]], [["‘Yo-yo-yo-yo-yoe!’ went the first boy.", "\"요-요-요-요-요-에!\" 첫 번째 소년이 외쳤다."]], [["‘Yo-yo-yo-yoe!’ went the second.", "\"요-요-요-요-에!\" 두 번째 소년이 외쳤다."]], [["‘Yo-yo-yo-yoe!’ chimed in old Wardle himself, most lustily, with his head and half his body out of the coach window.", "\"요-요-요-요-에!\" 워들 노인이 직접 우렁차게 받았으니, 머리와 상반신 절반을 마차 창밖으로 내민 채였다."]], [["‘Yo-yo-yo-yoe!’ shouted Mr.", "\"요-요-요-요-에!\""], ["Pickwick, taking up the burden of the cry, though he had not the slightest notion of its meaning or object.", "픽윅 씨도 그 뜻이나 목적은 전혀 알지 못한 채 그 외침을 따라 외쳤다."], ["And amidst the yo-yoing of the whole four, the chaise stopped.", "그렇게 넷이서 다 함께 요-요 하고 외치는 가운데, 마차가 멈춰 섰다."]], [["‘What’s the matter?’ inquired Mr.", "\"무슨 일이오?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘There’s a gate here,’ replied old Wardle.", "\"여기 통행문이 있소,\" 워들 노인이 대답했다."], ["‘We shall hear something of the fugitives.’", "\"도망자들 소식을 좀 들을 수 있을 거요.\""]], [["After a lapse of five minutes, consumed in incessant knocking and shouting, an old man in his shirt and trousers emerged from the turnpike-house, and opened the gate.", "쉴 새 없이 두드리고 외치는 데 오 분을 보낸 뒤에야, 셔츠와 바지 차림의 노인이 통행료 징수소에서 나와 문을 열었다."]], [["‘How long is it since a post-chaise went through here?’ inquired Mr.", "\"여기를 역마차가 지나간 지 얼마나 됐소?\""], ["Wardle.", "워들 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘How long?’\n‘Ah!’", "\"얼마나 됐냐고요?\""]], [["‘Why, I don’t rightly know.", "\"그래!\" \"글쎄, 정확히는 모르겄네유."], ["It worn’t a long time ago, nor it worn’t a short time ago--just between the two, perhaps.’", "오래된 것도 아니구, 그렇다고 얼마 안 된 것도 아니구—아마 그 중간쯤 될라나유.\""]], [["‘Has any chaise been by at all?’", "\"마차가 지나가긴 했소?\""]], [["‘Oh, yes, there’s been a chay by.’", "\"아, 예, 마차가 한 대 지나갔쥬.\""]], [["‘How long ago, my friend,’ interposed Mr.", "\"얼마나 됐소, 친구,\" 픽윅 씨가 끼어들었다."], ["Pickwick; ‘an hour?’", "\"한 시간 됐소?\""]], [["‘Ah, I dare say it might be,’ replied the man.", "\"아, 그쯤 됐을지두 모르쥬,\" 사내가 대답했다."]], [["‘Or two hours?’ inquired the post--boy on the wheeler.", "\"아니면 두 시간 됐나?\" 뒷말에 탄 마부 소년이 물었다."]], [["‘Well, I shouldn’t wonder if it was,’ returned the old man doubtfully.", "\"글쎄유, 그랬다 해두 이상할 거 없쥬,\" 노인이 미심쩍게 받았다."]], [["‘Drive on, boys,’ cried the testy old gentleman; ‘don’t waste any more time with that old idiot!’", "\"몰아라, 얘들아,\" 성마른 노신사가 외쳤다. \"저 늙은 멍청이하고 더는 시간 낭비하지 마라!\""]], [["‘Idiot!’ exclaimed the old man with a grin, as he stood in the middle of the road with the gate half-closed, watching the chaise which rapidly diminished in the increasing distance. ‘No--not much o’ that either; you’ve lost ten minutes here, and gone away as wise as you came, arter all. If every man on the line as has a guinea give him, earns it half as well, you won’t catch t’other chay this side Mich’lmas, old short-and- fat.’ And with another prolonged grin, the old man closed the gate, re- entered his house, and bolted the door after him.", "\"멍청이라구!\" 노인이 씩 웃으며 외쳤으니, 그는 문을 반쯤 닫은 채 길 한복판에 서서, 점점 멀어지며 빠르게 작아지는 마차를 바라보고 있었다. \"아니지—그렇게 멍청한 것두 아니구먼. 당신네는 여기서 십 분을 까먹고두, 결국 올 때만큼이나 헛똑똑이로 가버렸으니 말이여. 이 길에서 일 기니씩 받는 작자가 죄다 그 절반만큼이라두 제값을 하면, 당신네는 미가엘 축일 전에는 저쪽 마차를 못 잡을 거구먼, 이 땅딸막한 영감탱이야.\" 그러고는 또 한 번 길게 씩 웃고 나서, 노인은 문을 닫고 제 집으로 들어가 뒤로 빗장을 질렀다. * 미가엘 축일: 9월 29일. \"그때까지도 못 잡는다\"는 곧 한참 동안 못 잡는다는 비아냥이다."]], [["Meanwhile the chaise proceeded, without any slackening of pace, towards the conclusion of the stage.", "한편 마차는 속도를 조금도 늦추지 않고 구간의 끝을 향해 나아갔다. 워들이 예언한 대로 달은 빠르게 기울어 가고 있었다."], ["The moon, as Wardle had foretold, was rapidly on the wane; large tiers of dark, heavy clouds, which had been gradually overspreading the sky for some time past, now formed one black mass overhead; and large drops of rain which pattered every now and then against the windows of the chaise, seemed to warn the travellers of the rapid approach of a stormy night.", "한동안 차츰 하늘을 뒤덮어 가던 검고 무거운 구름이 겹겹이 쌓여, 이제는 머리 위에 하나의 검은 덩어리를 이루었다. 이따금 마차 창문에 후드득 부딪치는 굵은 빗방울은 폭풍우 치는 밤이 빠르게 다가오고 있음을 여행자들에게 경고하는 듯했다."], ["The wind, too, which was directly against them, swept in furious gusts down the narrow road, and howled dismally through the trees which skirted the pathway.", "바람마저 정면으로 불어와, 좁은 길을 따라 사납게 휘몰아치며, 길가에 늘어선 나무들 사이로 음산하게 울부짖었다."], ["Mr. Pickwick drew his coat closer about him, coiled himself more snugly up into the corner of the chaise, and fell into a sound sleep, from which he was only awakened by the stopping of the vehicle, the sound of the hostler’s bell, and a loud cry of ‘Horses on directly!’", "픽윅 씨는 외투를 몸에 더 단단히 여미고, 마차 구석으로 한층 아늑하게 몸을 웅크리고는 깊은 잠에 빠졌으니, 마차가 멈추는 것과 마부의 종소리, 그리고 \"당장 말을 대령하라!\"는 큰 외침에야 비로소 잠에서 깨어났다."]], [["But here another delay occurred.", "그러나 여기서 또 한 번 지체가 생겼다."], ["The boys were sleeping with such mysterious soundness, that it took five minutes a-piece to wake them.", "소년들이 어찌나 신기하리만치 곤히 잠들었던지, 하나씩 깨우는 데 오 분씩이나 걸렸다."], ["The hostler had somehow or other mislaid the key of the stable, and even when that was found, two sleepy helpers put the wrong harness on the wrong horses, and the whole process of harnessing had to be gone through afresh. Had Mr.", "마부는 어찌 된 영문인지 마구간 열쇠를 어디 두었는지 잊어버렸고, 그것을 찾고 나서도 졸음에 겨운 일꾼 둘이 엉뚱한 말에 엉뚱한 마구를 채우는 바람에, 마구 채우는 일을 처음부터 다시 해야만 했다."], ["Pickwick been alone, these multiplied obstacles would have completely put an end to the pursuit at once, but old Wardle was not to be so easily daunted; and he laid about him with such hearty good-will, cuffing this man, and pushing that; strapping a buckle here, and taking in a link there, that the chaise was ready in a much shorter time than could reasonably have been expected, under so many difficulties.", "픽윅 씨 혼자였다면 이렇게 거듭되는 장애에 추격은 즉시 완전히 끝장나고 말았겠지만, 워들 노인은 그리 쉽게 기죽을 사람이 아니었다. 그는 이 사람을 쥐어박고 저 사람을 떠밀며, 여기서 죔쇠를 채우고 저기서 고리를 조이며, 어찌나 진심 어린 열의로 동분서주했던지, 그 숱한 어려움 속에서도 마차는 응당 예상할 수 있었던 것보다 훨씬 짧은 시간에 채비를 갖추었다."]], [["They resumed their journey; and certainly the prospect before them was by no means encouraging.", "그들은 다시 길을 떠났다. 그러나 앞에 펼쳐진 전망은 결코 희망적이라 할 수 없었다."], ["The stage was fifteen miles long, the night was dark, the wind high, and the rain pouring in torrents.", "구간은 십오 마일이나 되었고, 밤은 어두웠고, 바람은 거셌으며, 비는 억수같이 퍼붓고 있었다."], ["It was impossible to make any great way against such obstacles united; it was hard upon one o’clock already; and nearly two hours were consumed in getting to the end of the stage.", "이런 장애들이 한데 겹친 상황에서는 좀처럼 길을 크게 나아갈 수가 없었다. 이미 한 시가 다 되어 가고 있었고, 그 구간의 끝에 다다르는 데 거의 두 시간이 걸렸다."], ["Here, however, an object presented itself, which rekindled their hopes, and reanimated their drooping spirits.", "그러나 여기서 한 가지 광경이 눈에 띄어, 그들의 희망에 다시 불을 지피고 가라앉았던 기운을 되살아나게 했다."]], [["‘When did this chaise come in?’ cried old Wardle, leaping out of his own vehicle, and pointing to one covered with wet mud, which was standing in the yard.", "\"이 마차는 언제 들어왔소?\" 워들 노인이 자기 마차에서 뛰어내리며 외쳤으니, 마당에 서 있는, 젖은 진흙을 뒤집어쓴 마차 한 대를 가리키고 있었다."]], [["‘Not a quarter of an hour ago, sir,’ replied the hostler, to whom the question was addressed.", "\"십오 분도 채 안 됐습니다, 나리,\" 질문을 받은 마부가 대답했다."]], [["‘Lady and gentleman?’ inquired Wardle, almost breathless with impatience.", "\"숙녀와 신사였소?\" 워들이 조바심에 거의 숨이 넘어갈 듯 물었다."]], [["‘Yes, sir.’", "\"예, 나리.\""]], [["‘Tall gentleman--dress-coat--long legs--thin body?’", "\"키 큰 신사—연미복—긴 다리—마른 몸?\""]], [["‘Yes, sir.’", "\"예, 나리.\""]], [["‘Elderly lady--thin face--rather skinny--eh?’", "\"나이 든 숙녀—여윈 얼굴—다소 깡마르고—응?\""]], [["‘Yes, sir.’", "\"예, 나리.\""]], [["‘By heavens, it’s the couple, Pickwick,’ exclaimed the old gentleman.", "\"맙소사, 바로 그 한 쌍일세, 픽윅,\" 노신사가 외쳤다."]], [["‘Would have been here before,’ said the hostler, ‘but they broke a trace.’", "\"더 일찍 도착했을 텐데,\" 마부가 말했다. \"끄는 가죽끈이 끊어졌더랬습니다.\""]], [["‘’Tis them!’ said Wardle, ‘it is, by Jove!", "\"그들이야!\" 워들이 말했다."], ["Chaise-and-four instantly!", "\"맞아, 정말로! 당장 사두마차를!"], ["We shall catch them yet before they reach the next stage.", "다음 구간에 닿기 전에 아직 따라잡을 수 있어."], ["A guinea a-piece, boys-be alive there--bustle about--there’s good fellows.’", "한 사람당 일 기니다, 얘들아—어서 움직여—부산을 떨어 봐라—착한 녀석들이지.\""]], [["And with such admonitions as these, the old gentleman ran up and down the yard, and bustled to and fro, in a state of excitement which communicated itself to Mr.", "이런 식의 독려를 쏟아내며, 노신사는 마당을 오르내리며 이리저리 부산을 떨었으니, 그 흥분이 픽윅 씨에게도 옮아갔다."], ["Pickwick also; and under the influence of which, that gentleman got himself into complicated entanglements with harness, and mixed up with horses and wheels of chaises, in the most surprising manner, firmly believing that by so doing he was materially forwarding the preparations for their resuming their journey.", "그 영향으로 픽윅 씨도 마구에 복잡하게 뒤엉키고, 더없이 놀라운 방식으로 말들과 마차 바퀴들 사이에 휘말려 들었으니, 그렇게 함으로써 자신이 다시 길을 떠나기 위한 준비를 실질적으로 돕고 있다고 굳게 믿고 있었다."]], [["‘Jump in--jump in!’ cried old Wardle, climbing into the chaise, pulling up the steps, and slamming the door after him.", "\"올라타시오—올라타!\" 워들 노인이 마차에 기어오르며, 발판을 끌어올리고, 뒤로 문을 쾅 닫으며 외쳤다."], ["‘Come along!", "\"어서 갑시다!"], ["Make haste!’ And before Mr.", "서둘러요!\""], ["Pickwick knew precisely what he was about, he felt himself forced in at the other door, by one pull from the old gentleman and one push from the hostler; and off they were again.", "픽윅 씨가 정확히 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 미처 깨닫기도 전에, 노신사의 잡아당김 한 번과 마부의 떠밂 한 번에 그는 반대편 문으로 떠밀려 들어가는 자신을 느꼈다. 그렇게 그들은 다시 출발했다."]], [["‘Ah!", "\"아!"], ["we are moving now,’ said the old gentleman exultingly.", "이제 가는군,\" 노신사가 의기양양하게 말했다."], ["They were indeed, as was sufficiently testified to Mr. Pickwick, by his constant collision either with the hard wood-work of the chaise, or the body of his companion.", "정말로 그러했으니, 픽윅 씨가 마차의 단단한 목재에든 동행의 몸에든 끊임없이 부딪치는 것으로 충분히 입증되었다."]], [["‘Hold up!’ said the stout old Mr.", "\"버티시오!\""], ["Wardle, as Mr. Pickwick dived head foremost into his capacious waistcoat.", "픽윅 씨가 머리부터 곤두박여 자신의 널찍한 조끼 속으로 파고들자 건장한 워들 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘I never did feel such a jolting in my life,’ said Mr.", "\"내 평생 이런 덜컹거림은 겪어 본 적이 없소,\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Never mind,’ replied his companion, ‘it will soon be over.", "\"신경 쓸 것 없소,\" 동행이 대답했다."], ["Steady, steady.’", "\"곧 끝날 거요. 차분히, 차분히.\""]], [["Mr.", "픽윅 씨는 할 수 있는 한 단단히 자기 구석에 몸을 자리 잡았다."], ["Pickwick planted himself into his own corner, as firmly as he could; and on whirled the chaise faster than ever.", "그리고 마차는 그 어느 때보다도 빠르게 내달렸다."]], [["They had travelled in this way about three miles, when Mr. Wardle, who had been looking out of the Window for two or three minutes, suddenly drew in his face, covered with splashes, and exclaimed in breathless eagerness--", "그들이 이런 식으로 약 삼 마일쯤 갔을 때, 이삼 분 동안 창밖을 내다보던 워들 씨가 갑자기 진흙투성이가 된 얼굴을 안으로 들이며, 숨 가쁘게 격앙되어 외쳤다."]], [["‘Here they are!’", "\"저기 있다!\""]], [["Mr. Pickwick thrust his head out of his window.", "픽윅 씨가 창밖으로 고개를 내밀었다."], ["Yes: there was a chaise- and-four, a short distance before them, dashing along at full gallop.", "그렇다. 얼마 떨어지지 않은 앞쪽에 사두마차 한 대가 전속력으로 질주하고 있었다."]], [["‘Go on, go on,’ almost shrieked the old gentleman.", "\"가라, 가!\" 노신사가 거의 비명을 지르듯 외쳤다."], ["‘Two guineas a-piece, boys--don’t let ‘em gain on us--keep it up--keep it up.’", "\"한 사람당 이 기니다, 얘들아—저들이 우리를 따돌리지 못하게 해라—계속 몰아—계속 몰아.\""]], [["The horses in the first chaise started on at their utmost speed; and those in Mr. Wardle’s galloped furiously behind them.", "앞선 마차의 말들이 최고 속력으로 내달리기 시작했고, 워들 씨의 말들도 그 뒤를 맹렬히 질주했다."]], [["‘I see his head,’ exclaimed the choleric old man; ‘damme, I see his head.’", "\"저놈 머리가 보여,\" 성마른 노인이 외쳤다. \"제기랄, 저놈 머리가 보인다.\""]], [["‘So do I’ said Mr.", "\"나도 보이오,\""], ["Pickwick; ‘that’s he.’", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"저게 그자요.\""]], [["Mr. Pickwick was not mistaken.", "픽윅 씨는 잘못 본 것이 아니었다."], ["The countenance of Mr.", "바퀴가 튀긴 진흙으로 온통 뒤덮인 징글 씨의 얼굴이 그의 마차 창문에 또렷이 보였다."], ["Jingle, completely coated with mud thrown up by the wheels, was plainly discernible at the window of his chaise; and the motion of his arm, which was waving violently towards the postillions, denoted that he was encouraging them to increased exertion.", "그리고 마부들을 향해 격렬하게 흔들어 대는 그의 팔짓은, 그가 그들에게 더욱 힘을 내라고 부추기고 있음을 나타냈다."]], [["The interest was intense.", "긴장감은 극에 달했다."], ["Fields, trees, and hedges, seemed to rush past them with the velocity of a whirlwind, so rapid was the pace at which they tore along.", "들판과 나무와 산울타리가 회오리바람 같은 속도로 그들 곁을 휙휙 스쳐 지나가는 듯했으니, 그만큼 그들이 내달리는 속도가 빨랐다."], ["They were close by the side of the first chaise.", "그들은 앞선 마차의 바로 옆에 바짝 따라붙었다."], ["Jingle’s voice could be plainly heard, even above the din of the wheels, urging on the boys.", "바퀴 소리의 소란을 뚫고도 소년들을 다그치는 징글의 목소리가 또렷이 들렸다."], ["Old Mr. Wardle foamed with rage and excitement.", "워들 노인은 분노와 흥분으로 입에 거품을 물었다."], ["He roared out scoundrels and villains by the dozen, clenched his fist and shook it expressively at the object of his indignation; but Mr.", "그는 악당이니 악한이니 하는 욕설을 수십 마디 내질렀고, 주먹을 불끈 쥐어 자기 분노의 대상을 향해 표나게 흔들어 댔다."], ["Jingle only answered with a contemptuous smile, and replied to his menaces by a shout of triumph, as his horses, answering the increased application of whip and spur, broke into a faster gallop, and left the pursuers behind.", "그러나 징글 씨는 경멸하는 미소로만 응수했고, 그의 위협에는 승리의 함성으로 답했으니, 그의 말들이 더욱 거세진 채찍과 박차에 응하여 한층 빠른 질주로 박차고 나가, 추격자들을 뒤에 떨궈 버렸다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick had just drawn in his head, and Mr.", "픽윅 씨가 막 고개를 안으로 들이고, 외치느라 기진맥진한 워들 씨도 그렇게 한 참이었는데, 엄청난 덜컹거림에 두 사람이 마차 앞쪽으로 내동댕이쳐졌다."], ["Wardle, exhausted with shouting, had done the same, when a tremendous jolt threw them forward against the front of the vehicle. There was a sudden bump--a loud crash- -away rolled a wheel, and over went the chaise.", "갑작스레 쿵 하는 충격—요란한 와장창 소리—바퀴 하나가 떨어져 굴러가고, 마차가 뒤집어졌다."]], [["After a very few seconds of bewilderment and confusion, in which nothing but the plunging of horses, and breaking of glass could be made out, Mr.", "당황과 혼란의 아주 짧은 몇 초가 지나는 동안에는 말들이 날뛰는 것과 유리가 깨지는 것밖에 분간할 수 없었는데, 픽윅 씨는 마차의 잔해 속에서 자신이 거칠게 끌려 나오는 것을 느꼈다."], ["Pickwick felt himself violently pulled out from among the ruins of the chaise; and as soon as he had gained his feet, extricated his head from the skirts of his greatcoat, which materially impeded the usefulness of his spectacles, the full disaster of the case met his view.", "그리고 발을 딛고 일어서, 안경의 쓸모를 적잖이 방해하던 외투 자락에서 머리를 빼내자마자, 이 사태의 전모가 그의 눈앞에 드러났다."]], [["Old Mr. Wardle without a hat, and his clothes torn in several places, stood by his side, and the fragments of the chaise lay scattered at their feet.", "워들 노인이 모자도 없이, 옷은 여러 군데 찢긴 채 그의 곁에 서 있었고, 마차의 파편들이 그들 발치에 흩어져 있었다."], ["The post-boys, who had succeeded in cutting the traces, were standing, disfigured with mud and disordered by hard riding, by the horses’ heads.", "가죽끈을 끊는 데 성공한 마부 소년들은, 진흙으로 일그러지고 험한 질주에 흐트러진 몰골로 말들의 머리맡에 서 있었다."], ["About a hundred yards in advance was the other chaise, which had pulled up on hearing the crash.", "백 야드쯤 앞에는 다른 마차가 와장창 소리를 듣고 멈춰 서 있었다."], ["The postillions, each with a broad grin convulsing his countenance, were viewing the adverse party from their saddles, and Mr. Jingle was contemplating the wreck from the coach window, with evident satisfaction.", "마부들은 저마다 얼굴 가득 활짝 웃음을 띤 채 안장 위에서 상대편을 구경하고 있었고, 징글 씨는 마차 창문에서 그 잔해를 역력히 흡족한 표정으로 바라보고 있었다."], ["The day was just breaking, and the whole scene was rendered perfectly visible by the grey light of the morning.", "날이 막 밝아 오고 있어, 잿빛 아침 빛에 온 광경이 완연히 드러나 보였다."]], [["‘Hollo!’ shouted the shameless Jingle, ‘anybody damaged?--elderly gentlemen--no light weights--dangerous work--very.’", "\"여보시오!\" 뻔뻔스러운 징글이 외쳤다. \"다친 사람 없소?—나이 지긋한 신사분들—가벼운 몸도 아니고—위험한 일이지—아주.\""]], [["‘You’re a rascal,’ roared Wardle.", "\"이 악당 놈,\" 워들이 고함쳤다."]], [["‘Ha! ha!’ replied Jingle; and then he added, with a knowing wink, and a jerk of the thumb towards the interior of the chaise--‘I say--she’s very well--desires her compliments--begs you won’t trouble yourself--love to Tuppy--won’t you get up behind?--drive on, boys.’", "\"하! 하!\" 징글이 대답했다. 그러고는 알겠다는 듯 한쪽 눈을 찡긋하고 엄지손가락으로 마차 안쪽을 가리키며 덧붙였다. \"이봐요—그 여자분은 아주 잘 있소—안부 전해 달라더군—부디 수고로이 마시라고—터피한테 사랑한다 전해 주오—뒤에 매달려 타지 않겠소?—몰아라, 얘들아.\""]], [["The postillions resumed their proper attitudes, and away rattled the chaise, Mr. Jingle fluttering in derision a white handkerchief from the coach window.", "마부들이 다시 제 자세를 갖추니, 마차가 덜커덕거리며 내달렸고, 징글 씨는 마차 창문에서 흰 손수건을 조롱하듯 나풀나풀 흔들어 댔다."]], [["Nothing in the whole adventure, not even the upset, had disturbed the calm and equable current of Mr. Pickwick’s temper.", "이 모험 전체에서, 마차가 뒤집힌 일조차도, 픽윅 씨의 차분하고 한결같은 성정의 흐름을 흐트러뜨리지는 못했다."], ["The villainy, however, which could first borrow money of his faithful follower, and then abbreviate his name to ‘Tuppy,’ was more than he could patiently bear.", "그러나 자기의 충실한 추종자에게서 먼저 돈을 빌려 가고, 그런 다음 그의 이름을 \"터피\"라고 줄여 부르는 그 악랄함만은, 그가 차분히 견딜 수 있는 정도를 넘어섰다."], ["He drew his breath hard, and coloured up to the very tips of his spectacles, as he said, slowly and emphatically--", "그는 숨을 거칠게 몰아쉬며 안경테 끝까지 새빨개진 채로, 천천히 그리고 힘주어 말했다."]], [["‘If ever I meet that man again, I’ll--’", "\"내 다시 저자를 만나는 날에는, 기필코—\""]], [["‘Yes, yes,’ interrupted Wardle, ‘that’s all very well; but while we stand talking here, they’ll get their licence, and be married in London.’", "\"그래요, 그래,\" 워들이 가로막았다. \"다 좋은 말이오. 하지만 우리가 여기 서서 이러쿵저러쿵하는 사이에, 저들은 혼인 허가증을 받아 런던에서 결혼해 버릴 거요.\""]], [["Mr. Pickwick paused, bottled up his vengeance, and corked it down.", "픽윅 씨는 말을 멈추고, 복수심을 병에 담아 마개를 단단히 막았다."], ["‘How far is it to the next stage?’ inquired Mr.", "\"다음 구간까지 얼마나 되느냐?\""], ["Wardle, of one of the boys.", "워들 씨가 소년들 중 하나에게 물었다."]], [["‘Six mile, ain’t it, Tom?’", "\"육 마일이지, 안 그래, 톰?\""]], [["‘Rayther better.’", "\"그보다 좀 더 되구먼.\""]], [["‘Rayther better nor six mile, Sir.’", "\"육 마일보다 좀 더 됩니다, 나리.\""]], [["‘Can’t be helped,’ said Wardle, ‘we must walk it, Pickwick.’", "\"어쩔 수 없군,\" 워들이 말했다. \"걸어가야겠소, 픽윅.\""]], [["‘No help for it,’ replied that truly great man.", "\"별 도리가 없지요,\" 그 참으로 위대한 인물이 대답했다."]], [["So sending forward one of the boys on horseback, to procure a fresh chaise and horses, and leaving the other behind to take care of the broken one, Mr. Pickwick and Mr. Wardle set manfully forward on the walk, first tying their shawls round their necks, and slouching down their hats to escape as much as possible from the deluge of rain, which after a slight cessation had again begun to pour heavily down.", "그리하여 소년 하나를 말에 태워 앞서 보내 새 마차와 말을 구해 오게 하고, 다른 하나는 뒤에 남겨 부서진 마차를 지키게 한 뒤, 픽윅 씨와 워들 씨는 사내답게 걷기 시작했으니, 먼저 숄을 목에 두르고 모자를 푹 눌러써서, 잠시 잦아들었다가 다시 억수같이 퍼붓기 시작한 빗줄기를 되도록 피하려 했다."]], [["CHAPTER X. CLEARING UP ALL DOUBTS (IF ANY EXISTED) OF THE DISINTERESTEDNESS OF MR.", "런던에는 옛 여관이 여럿 있으니, 한때는 이름난 역마차들의 본거지였던 곳들이다."], ["A. JINGLE’S CHARACTER\nThere are in London several old inns, once the headquarters of celebrated coaches in the days when coaches performed their journeys in a graver and more solemn manner than they do in these times; but which have now degenerated into little more than the abiding and booking- places of country wagons.", "그 시절에는 마차가 요즘보다 한결 점잖고 엄숙한 태도로 여행을 수행하였으나, 지금은 시골 짐마차들이 머물고 짐을 부치는 곳 정도로 영락하고 말았다."], ["The reader would look in vain for any of these ancient hostelries, among the Golden Crosses and Bull and Mouths, which rear their stately fronts in the improved streets of London.", "개량된 런던 거리에 당당한 정면을 곧추세운 골든 크로스니 불 앤드 마우스니 하는 여관들 가운데서 이런 고풍스러운 객사를 찾으려 한다면 독자는 헛수고만 하게 될 것이다."], ["If he would light upon any of these old places, he must direct his steps to the obscurer quarters of the town, and there in some secluded nooks he will find several, still standing with a kind of gloomy sturdiness, amidst the modern innovations which surround them.", "이런 옛 장소를 만나려거든 도시의 좀 더 후미진 구역으로 발길을 돌려야 하니, 그곳 한적한 모퉁이에 여러 곳이 아직도 남아, 둘레를 에워싼 근대적 혁신들 한가운데서 일종의 음울한 견실함을 지니고 서 있는 것을 보게 될 것이다."]], [["In the Borough especially, there still remain some half-dozen old inns, which have preserved their external features unchanged, and which have escaped alike the rage for public improvement and the encroachments of private speculation.", "특히 버러 지역에는 아직도 대여섯 곳의 옛 여관이 남아 있어, 외관을 변함없이 보존한 채 공공 개량의 열풍과 사적 투기의 잠식을 똑같이 면하였다."], ["Great, rambling queer old places they are, with galleries, and passages, and staircases, wide enough and antiquated enough to furnish materials for a hundred ghost stories, supposing we should ever be reduced to the lamentable necessity of inventing any, and that the world should exist long enough to exhaust the innumerable veracious legends connected with old London Bridge, and its adjacent neighbourhood on the Surrey side.", "그것들은 크고 어수선하고 기묘한 옛 건물들로, 복도와 통로와 계단이 갖추어져 있는데, 그것들이 어찌나 넓고 고색창연한지, 우리가 언젠가 유령 이야기를 백 가지쯤 지어내야 하는 통탄할 처지에 몰린다 하더라도—그리고 세상이 옛 런던 다리와 그 인근 서리 쪽 일대에 얽힌 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 진실한 전설들을 다 써 버릴 만큼 오래 존속한다 하더라도—그 재료가 되기에 충분할 정도였다."]], [["It was in the yard of one of these inns--of no less celebrated a one than the White Hart--that a man was busily employed in brushing the dirt off a pair of boots, early on the morning succeeding the events narrated in the last chapter.", "지난 장에서 이야기한 사건들이 있은 다음 날 이른 아침, 이런 여관들 가운데 하나—다름 아닌 그 이름난 화이트 하트—의 마당에서 한 사내가 부지런히 부츠 한 켤레의 흙을 솔질해 떨어내고 있었다."], ["He was habited in a coarse, striped waistcoat, with black calico sleeves, and blue glass buttons; drab breeches and leggings.", "그는 거친 줄무늬 조끼를 입었는데, 소매는 검은 옥양목이고 단추는 푸른 유리로 되어 있었으며, 황갈색 반바지에 각반을 둘렀다."], ["A bright red handkerchief was wound in a very loose and unstudied style round his neck, and an old white hat was carelessly thrown on one side of his head.", "새빨간 손수건을 아주 헐겁고 아무렇게나 목에 둘렀고, 낡은 흰 모자를 머리 한쪽에 무심히 걸쳐 놓았다."], ["There were two rows of boots before him, one cleaned and the other dirty, and at every addition he made to the clean row, he paused from his work, and contemplated its results with evident satisfaction.", "그의 앞에는 두 줄로 부츠가 놓여 있었으니, 한 줄은 닦은 것이고 다른 줄은 더러운 것이었으며, 닦은 줄에 한 켤레를 보탤 때마다 그는 일손을 멈추고 그 성과를 흐뭇한 기색으로 바라보았다."]], [["The yard presented none of that bustle and activity which are the usual characteristics of a large coach inn.", "그 마당에는 큰 역마차 여관의 일반적인 특징인 그 부산함과 활기가 조금도 없었다."], ["Three or four lumbering wagons, each with a pile of goods beneath its ample canopy, about the height of the second-floor window of an ordinary house, were stowed away beneath a lofty roof which extended over one end of the yard; and another, which was probably to commence its journey that morning, was drawn out into the open space.", "큼직한 짐마차 서너 대가 저마다 그 너른 차양 아래 보통 집 이층 창문 높이쯤 되는 짐 더미를 싣고서, 마당 한쪽 끝에 걸쳐진 높다란 지붕 밑에 들이박혀 있었다. 그리고 또 한 대—아마 그날 아침에 길을 떠날 모양이었다—는 빈터로 끌어내져 있었다."], ["A double tier of bedroom galleries, with old clumsy balustrades, ran round two sides of the straggling area, and a double row of bells to correspond, sheltered from the weather by a little sloping roof, hung over the door leading to the bar and coffee-room.", "낡고 투박한 난간을 두른 이층짜리 침실 회랑이 흩어진 마당의 두 면을 둘러싸고 있었고, 그에 맞추어 이층으로 달린 종들이 비를 막아 주는 작은 비탈 지붕 아래 걸려, 술집과 커피실로 통하는 문 위에 매달려 있었다."], ["Two or three gigs and chaise-carts were wheeled up under different little sheds and pent-houses; and the occasional heavy tread of a cart-horse, or rattling of a chain at the farther end of the yard, announced to anybody who cared about the matter, that the stable lay in that direction.", "이륜마차와 경마차 두세 대가 여기저기 작은 헛간과 차양 아래로 밀어 넣어져 있었고, 마당 저쪽 끝에서 이따금 들려오는 짐말의 무거운 발소리며 사슬 절그럭거리는 소리는, 관심 있는 이라면 누구에게나 마구간이 그쪽에 있음을 알려 주었다."], ["When we add that a few boys in smock-frocks were lying asleep on heavy packages, wool-packs, and other articles that were scattered about on heaps of straw, we have described as fully as need be the general appearance of the yard of the White Hart Inn, High Street, Borough, on the particular morning in question.", "거기에다 작업복을 입은 소년 몇이 무거운 꾸러미며 양모 포대며, 그 밖에 짚 더미 위에 흩어져 놓인 물건들 위에 누워 잠들어 있더라고 덧붙인다면, 바로 그 문제의 아침 버러 하이 스트리트 화이트 하트 여관 마당의 전반적인 모습은 필요한 만큼 충분히 묘사한 셈이 된다."]], [["A loud ringing of one of the bells was followed by the appearance of a smart chambermaid in the upper sleeping gallery, who, after tapping at one of the doors, and receiving a request from within, called over the balustrades--", "종 가운데 하나가 요란하게 울리더니, 위층 침실 회랑에 말쑥한 객실 하녀가 나타났다. 그녀는 어느 문을 똑똑 두드리고 안에서 무언가 부탁을 받고는, 난간 너머로 소리쳐 불렀다—"]], [["‘Sam!’", "\"샘!\""]], [["‘Hollo,’ replied the man with the white hat.", "\"왜 그래.\" 흰 모자를 쓴 사내가 대답했다."]], [["‘Number twenty-two wants his boots.’", "\"이십이 호 손님이 부츠 달래.\""]], [["‘Ask number twenty-two, vether he’ll have ‘em now, or vait till he gets ‘em,’ was the reply.", "\"이십이 호한테 물어봐, 지금 가질 건지 아니면 받을 때까지 기다릴 건지.\" 하는 것이 대답이었다."]], [["‘Come, don’t be a fool, Sam,’ said the girl coaxingly, ‘the gentleman wants his boots directly.’", "\"이봐, 바보같이 굴지 좀 마, 샘.\" 하녀가 어르듯 말했다. \"그 신사분이 부츠를 당장 원하신다니까.\""]], [["‘Well, you are a nice young ‘ooman for a musical party, you are,’ said the boot-cleaner.", "\"흥, 댁은 정말이지 음악회에나 어울릴 참한 아가씨로구만.\" 부츠 닦이가 말했다."], ["‘Look at these here boots--eleven pair o’ boots; and one shoe as belongs to number six, with the wooden leg.", "\"이 부츠들 좀 봐—열한 켤레야. 게다가 나무다리 짚은 육 호 손님 신발 한 짝도 있고."], ["The eleven boots is to be called at half-past eight and the shoe at nine.", "이 열한 켤레는 여덟 시 반에 가져갈 거고, 신발 한 짝은 아홉 시에 가져갈 거야."], ["Who’s number twenty-two, that’s to put all the others out?", "이십이 호가 뭐길래 다른 것들을 다 제쳐 놓으라는 거야? 안 되지, 안 돼."], ["No, no; reg’lar rotation, as Jack Ketch said, ven he tied the men up.", "잭 케치가 사람들 목 매달 때 한 말마따나, 정해진 차례대로 해야지."], ["Sorry to keep you a-waitin’, Sir, but I’ll attend to you directly.’", "기다리시게 해서 죄송합니다, 손님, 곧 댁 차례를 봐 드리겠습니다.\""]], [["Saying which, the man in the white hat set to work upon a top-boot with increased assiduity.", "이렇게 말하면서 흰 모자를 쓴 사내는 한층 더 정성을 들여 긴 부츠 한 짝에 일을 시작했다."]], [["There was another loud ring; and the bustling old landlady of the White Hart made her appearance in the opposite gallery.", "또 한 번 종이 요란하게 울렸다. 그러자 부산스러운 화이트 하트의 늙은 여주인이 맞은편 회랑에 모습을 드러냈다."]], [["‘Sam,’ cried the landlady, ‘where’s that lazy, idle--why, Sam--oh, there you are; why don’t you answer?’", "\"샘.\" 여주인이 외쳤다. \"그 게으르고 빈둥거리는—아니, 샘—오, 거기 있구나. 왜 대답을 안 해?\""]], [["‘Vouldn’t be gen-teel to answer, till you’d done talking,’ replied Sam gruffly.", "\"마님 말씀 다 끝나기도 전에 대답하는 건 점잖지 못한 일이라서요.\" 샘이 퉁명스럽게 대꾸했다."]], [["‘Here, clean these shoes for number seventeen directly, and take ‘em to private sitting-room, number five, first floor.’", "\"여기, 이 신발 당장 닦아서 십칠 호한테 갖다 줘. 일층 오 호 전용 응접실로 가져가.\""]], [["The landlady flung a pair of lady’s shoes into the yard, and bustled away.", "여주인은 숙녀용 신발 한 켤레를 마당으로 휙 던지고는 부산하게 가 버렸다."]], [["‘Number five,’ said Sam, as he picked up the shoes, and taking a piece of chalk from his pocket, made a memorandum of their destination on the soles--‘Lady’s shoes and private sittin’-room!", "\"오 호라.\" 샘이 신발을 집어 들며 말했다."], ["I suppose she didn’t come in the vagin.’", "그리고 주머니에서 분필 한 토막을 꺼내, 신발 밑창에 행선지를 적어 두었다—\"숙녀용 신발에 전용 응접실이라! 짐마차로 오신 건 아니겠군.\""]], [["‘She came in early this morning,’ cried the girl, who was still leaning over the railing of the gallery, ‘with a gentleman in a hackney-coach, and it’s him as wants his boots, and you’d better do ‘em, that’s all about it.’", "\"오늘 아침 일찍 오셨어.\" 아직도 회랑 난간에 기대어 있던 하녀가 외쳤다. \"어떤 신사분하고 삯마차를 타고 오셨는데, 부츠를 원하는 게 바로 그 신사야. 그러니 어서 닦는 게 좋을걸, 그게 전부야.\""]], [["‘Vy didn’t you say so before,’ said Sam, with great indignation, singling out the boots in question from the heap before him.", "\"진작 그렇게 말하지 그랬어.\" 샘이 크게 분개하며, 앞에 쌓인 더미에서 문제의 부츠를 골라냈다."], ["‘For all I know’d he was one o’ the regular threepennies.", "\"나야 그 사람이 으레 삼 펜스짜리 손님 중 하나인 줄로만 알았지."], ["Private room!", "전용 객실이라!"], ["and a lady too!", "게다가 숙녀까지!"], ["If he’s anything of a gen’l’m’n, he’s vurth a shillin’ a day, let alone the arrands.’", "그 사람이 정말 신사라면, 심부름값은 빼고도 하루에 일 실링 값어치는 되겠는걸.\""]], [["Stimulated by this inspiring reflection, Mr. Samuel brushed away with such hearty good-will, that in a few minutes the boots and shoes, with a polish which would have struck envy to the soul of the amiable Mr.", "이렇게 고무적인 생각에 자극받아, 새뮤얼 씨는 어찌나 신명나게 솔질을 해 댔던지, 몇 분 만에 그 부츠와 신발은—화이트 하트에서는 데이 앤드 마틴 구두약을 썼으니, 그 친절한 워런 씨의 영혼에 시샘을 불러일으켰을 만한 광택을 내며—오 호의 문 앞에 당도하였다."], ["Warren (for they used Day & Martin at the White Hart), had arrived at the door of number five.", "* 잭 케치: 17세기 영국의 악명 높은 처형 집행인. 이후 사형 집행인의 대명사가 되었다."]], [["‘Come in,’ said a man’s voice, in reply to Sam’s rap at the door.", "\"들어오게.\" 샘이 문을 두드리자 한 남자의 목소리가 대답했다."], ["Sam made his best bow, and stepped into the presence of a lady and gentleman seated at breakfast.", "샘은 정중하게 절을 하고는, 아침 식사를 하며 앉아 있는 한 숙녀와 신사 앞으로 걸어 들어갔다."], ["Having officiously deposited the gentleman’s boots right and left at his feet, and the lady’s shoes right and left at hers, he backed towards the door.", "그는 부지런히 신사의 부츠를 그 발치 좌우에 놓고, 숙녀의 신발을 그 발치 좌우에 놓은 뒤, 뒷걸음질로 문 쪽으로 물러섰다."]], [["‘Boots,’ said the gentleman.", "\"부츠.\" 신사가 말했다."]], [["‘Sir,’ said Sam, closing the door, and keeping his hand on the knob of the lock.", "\"네.\" 샘이 문을 닫고는, 손은 자물쇠 손잡이에 댄 채 대답했다."]], [["‘Do you know--what’s a-name--Doctors’ Commons?’", "\"자네 혹시 아나—거 뭐라더라—닥터스 커먼스 말일세?\""]], [["‘Yes, Sir.’", "\"네, 알지요.\""]], [["‘Where is it?’", "\"어디 있나?\""]], [["‘Paul’s Churchyard, Sir; low archway on the carriage side, bookseller’s at one corner, hotel on the other, and two porters in the middle as touts for licences.’", "\"폴 교회 뜰입니다. 마차 다니는 쪽으로 나지막한 아치 통로가 있고, 한쪽 모퉁이엔 책방, 다른 쪽엔 호텔, 그 가운데에 허가증 호객꾼 둘이 있지요.\""]], [["‘Touts for licences!’ said the gentleman.", "\"허가증 호객꾼이라!\" 신사가 말했다."]], [["‘Touts for licences,’ replied Sam.", "\"허가증 호객꾼이요.\" 샘이 대답했다."], ["‘Two coves in vhite aprons--touches their hats ven you walk in--“Licence, Sir, licence?”", "\"흰 앞치마를 두른 사내 둘인데—들어가시면 모자를 들어 인사하면서 '허가증요, 나리, 허가증?'"], ["Queer sort, them, and their mas’rs, too, sir--Old Bailey Proctors--and no mistake.’", "한답니다. 묘한 작자들이지요, 그 주인들까지도요, 나리—올드 베일리 변호사들 말입니다—틀림없이요.\""]], [["‘What do they do?’ inquired the gentleman.", "\"그자들이 뭘 하나?\" 신사가 물었다."]], [["‘Do! You, Sir!", "\"하다니요!"], ["That ain’t the worst on it, neither.", "나리, 댁이요! 그게 제일 나쁜 것도 아니에요."], ["They puts things into old gen’l’m’n’s heads as they never dreamed of.", "그자들은 영감님들 머릿속에 꿈에도 생각 못 한 것들을 집어넣지요."], ["My father, Sir, wos a coachman.", "우리 아버지로 말씀드리면, 마부였습니다."], ["A widower he wos, and fat enough for anything--uncommon fat, to be sure.", "홀아비셨는데, 뭐든 못 할 게 없을 만큼 살이 쪘지요—참말이지 유별나게 뚱뚱했어요."], ["His missus dies, and leaves him four hundred pound.", "그 마누라가 죽으면서 사백 파운드를 남겼습니다."], ["Down he goes to the Commons, to see the lawyer and draw the blunt--very smart-- top boots on--nosegay in his button-hole--broad-brimmed tile--green shawl--quite the gen’l’m’n.", "아버지는 변호사를 만나 그 돈을 찾으려고 커먼스로 내려갔지요—아주 멋을 부리고요—긴 부츠를 신고, 단춧구멍엔 꽃다발을 꽂고, 챙 넓은 모자에 초록 숄을 두르고, 영락없는 신사였어요."], ["Goes through the archvay, thinking how he should inwest the money--up comes the touter, touches his hat--“Licence, Sir, licence?”--“What’s that?”", "아치 통로를 지나며 그 돈을 어떻게 굴릴까 궁리하는데—호객꾼이 다가와 모자를 들고 '허가증요, 나리, 허가증?' 하더랍니다.—'그게 뭔가?'"], ["says my father.--“Licence, Sir,”", "아버지가 묻지요.—'허가증입니다요, 나리.'"], ["says he.--“What licence?”", "하니까—'무슨 허가증?'"], ["says my father.--“Marriage licence,”", "아버지가 묻고—'결혼 허가증이지요.'"], ["says the touter.--“Dash my veskit,”", "호객꾼이 말합니다.—'젠장맞을,'"], ["says my father, “I never thought o’ that.”-- “I think you wants one, Sir,”", "아버지가 말하길, '그건 생각도 못 했군.'—'제 보기엔 나리께 하나 필요하실 듯한데요.'"], ["says the touter.", "호객꾼이 말합니다."], ["My father pulls up, and thinks a bit--“No,”", "아버지가 걸음을 멈추고 잠깐 생각하더니—'아니야,'"], ["says he, “damme, I’m too old, b’sides, I’m a many sizes too large,”", "하시며, '제기랄, 난 너무 늙었어. 게다가 몸집이 몇 치수는 너무 크고.'"], ["says he.--“Not a bit on it, Sir,”", "하셨지요.—'천만에요, 나리.'"], ["says the touter.-- “Think not?”", "호객꾼이 말합니다.—'그렇게 생각하나?'"], ["says my father.--“I’m sure not,”", "아버지가 묻고—'틀림없이요.'"], ["says he; “we married a gen’l’m’n twice your size, last Monday.”--“Did you, though?”", "호객꾼이 말하길, '지난 월요일엔 나리보다 두 배는 큰 신사분도 결혼시켜 드렸는걸요.'—'정말인가?' 아버지가 말합니다.—'그럼요, 정말입니다.'"], ["said my father.--“To be sure, we did,”", "호객꾼이 말하길, '나리는 그분에 비하면 애기지요—이쪽입니다, 나리, 이쪽이요!' 하더랍니다."], ["says the touter, “you’re a babby to him-- this way, sir--this way!”--and sure enough my father walks arter him, like a tame monkey behind a horgan, into a little back office, vere a teller sat among dirty papers, and tin boxes, making believe he was busy.", "그래서 정말로 아버지는, 풍금쟁이 뒤를 따르는 길든 원숭이처럼 그 사내 뒤를 따라, 어느 작은 안쪽 사무실로 들어갔지요. 거기엔 서기 하나가 더러운 서류와 양철 상자들 틈에 앉아 바쁜 척하고 있었어요."], ["“Pray take a seat, vile I makes out the affidavit, Sir,”", "'제가 진술서를 작성하는 동안 자리에 앉으시지요, 나리.'"], ["says the lawyer.--“Thank’ee, Sir,”", "변호사가 말합니다.—'고맙소.'"], ["says my father, and down he sat, and stared with all his eyes, and his mouth vide open, at the names on the boxes.", "아버지가 말하고는 앉아서, 두 눈을 부릅뜨고 입은 떡 벌린 채 상자들에 적힌 이름들을 멀거니 쳐다봤지요."], ["“What’s your name, Sir,”", "'성함이 어찌 되십니까, 나리?'"], ["says the lawyer.--“Tony Weller,”", "변호사가 묻습니다.—'토니 웰러요.'"], ["says my father.--“Parish?”", "아버지가 말하지요.—'교구는요?'"], ["says the lawyer.", "변호사가 묻습니다."], ["“Belle Savage,”", "'벨 소비지요.'"], ["says my father; for he stopped there wen he drove up, and he know’d nothing about parishes, he didn’t.--“And what’s the lady’s name?”", "아버지가 말합니다. 마차를 몰고 와서 거기 묵었던 데다, 교구에 대해선 아무것도 몰랐으니까요.—'그럼 부인 되실 분 성함은요?'"], ["says the lawyer.", "변호사가 묻습니다."], ["My father was struck all of a heap.", "아버지는 그만 어안이 벙벙해졌지요."], ["“Blessed if I know,”", "'내 알 게 뭐요.'"], ["says he.--“Not know!”", "하셨어요.—'모르신다고요!'"], ["says the lawyer.--“No more nor you do,”", "변호사가 말합니다.—'댁이나 나나 모르긴 마찬가지지.'"], ["says my father; “can’t I put that in arterwards?”--“Impossible!”", "아버지가 말하길, '그건 나중에 적어 넣으면 안 되겠소?'—'안 됩니다!'"], ["says the lawyer.--“Wery well,”", "변호사가 말합니다.—'그럼 좋소.'"], ["says my father, after he’d thought a moment, “put down Mrs.", "아버지가 잠깐 생각하더니, '클라크 부인이라고 적으시오.'—'어느 클라크요?' 변호사가 펜을 잉크에 적시며 묻습니다.—'도킹의 그랜비 후작 여관, 수전 클라크요.'"], ["Clarke.”--“What Clarke?”", "아버지가 말하지요."], ["says the lawyer, dipping his pen in the ink.--“Susan Clarke, Markis o’ Granby, Dorking,”", "'내가 청하면 날 받아 줄 거요. 아마—한 번도 그 여자한테 말을 꺼낸 적은 없지만, 받아 줄 게 틀림없소.'"], ["says my father; “she’ll have me, if I ask.", "허가증이 작성됐고, 정말로 그 여자가 아버지를 받아 줬지요."], ["I des-say--I never said nothing to her, but she’ll have me, I know.”", "그뿐인가요, 지금도 데리고 살아요. 그런데 난 그 사백 파운드 중 한 푼도 못 받았으니, 더 안된 일이지요."], ["The licence was made out, and she did have him, and what’s more she’s got him now; and I never had any of the four hundred pound, worse luck.", "죄송합니다, 나리.\" 샘이 이야기를 끝내고 말했다."], ["Beg your pardon, sir,’ said Sam, when he had concluded, ‘but wen I gets on this here grievance, I runs on like a new barrow with the wheel greased.’ Having said which, and having paused for an instant to see whether he was wanted for anything more, Sam left the room.", "\"하지만 이 분한 얘기만 꺼냈다 하면, 저는 바퀴에 기름칠한 새 손수레처럼 줄줄 굴러가 버린답니다.\" 이렇게 말하고는, 더 시킬 일이 있는지 보려고 잠깐 멈추었다가, 샘은 방을 나갔다."]], [["‘Half-past nine--just the time--off at once;’ said the gentleman, whom we need hardly introduce as Mr.", "\"아홉 시 반—마침 그 시각이로군—당장 떠나자.\""], ["Jingle.", "신사가 말했으니, 그가 징글 씨임은 굳이 소개할 필요도 없겠다."]], [["‘Time--for what?’ said the spinster aunt coquettishly.", "\"떠나요—어디로요?\" 노처녀 고모가 교태를 부리며 말했다."]], [["‘Licence, dearest of angels--give notice at the church--call you mine, to-morrow’--said Mr.", "\"허가증 받으러, 천사 중에서도 가장 사랑스러운 이여—교회에 통고하고—내일이면 당신을 내 사람이라 부르는 거요.\""], ["Jingle, and he squeezed the spinster aunt’s hand.", "징글 씨가 말하며 노처녀 고모의 손을 꼭 쥐었다."]], [["‘The licence!’ said Rachael, blushing.", "\"허가증요!\" 레이철이 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다."]], [["‘The licence,’ repeated Mr.", "\"허가증.\""], ["Jingle--", "징글 씨가 되뇌었다—"]], [["‘In hurry, post-haste for a licence, In hurry, ding dong I come back.’", "\"허가증 받으러 부랴부랴 서두르고, 부랴부랴 댕그랑댕그랑 돌아오리.\""]], [["‘How you run on,’ said Rachael.", "\"어쩌면 그렇게 말이 술술 나와요.\" 레이철이 말했다."]], [["‘Run on--nothing to the hours, days, weeks, months, years, when we’re united--run on--they’ll fly on--bolt--mizzle--steam-engine--thousand- horse power--nothing to it.’", "\"말이 술술 나온다—우리가 맺어진 뒤의 시간, 날, 주, 달, 해에 비하면 아무것도 아니지—술술 나온다—그것들은 훨훨 날아갈 거요—쏜살같이—슬그머니—증기기관—천 마력—이건 거기 댈 것도 아니야.\""]], [["‘Can’t--can’t we be married before to-morrow morning?’ inquired Rachael.", "\"내일 아침 전에—전에 결혼할 순 없나요?\" 레이철이 물었다."]], [["‘Impossible--can’t be--notice at the church--leave the licence to-day-- ceremony come off to-morrow.’", "\"불가능하오—안 되오—교회에 통고하고—오늘 허가증을 맡겨 두고—예식은 내일 치르는 거요.\""]], [["I am so terrified, lest my brother should discover us!’ said Rachael.", "\"오빠가 우릴 찾아낼까 봐 너무도 무서워요!\" 레이철이 말했다."]], [["‘Discover--nonsense--too much shaken by the break-down--besides--extreme caution--gave up the post-chaise--walked on--took a hackney-coach--came to the Borough--last place in the world that he’d look in--ha! ha!-- capital notion that--very.’", "\"찾아내다니—말도 안 되오—마차가 부서지는 통에 너무 놀라서—게다가—극도로 조심해서—역마차도 포기하고—걸어서—삯마차를 잡고—버러로 왔으니—세상에서 그가 마지막으로 찾아볼 곳이지—하하!—기막힌 묘안이오—그렇고말고.\""]], [["‘Don’t be long,’ said the spinster affectionately, as Mr.", "\"오래 걸리지 마세요.\""], ["Jingle stuck the pinched-up hat on his head.", "노처녀가 다정하게 말하자, 징글 씨는 우그러진 모자를 머리에 눌러썼다."]], [["‘Long away from you?--Cruel charmer,’ and Mr.", "\"당신 곁을 오래 비운다니?—잔인한 매혹의 여인이여.\""], ["Jingle skipped playfully up to the spinster aunt, imprinted a chaste kiss upon her lips, and danced out of the room.", "그리고 징글 씨는 장난스럽게 노처녀 고모에게 깡충 다가가, 그 입술에 정숙한 입맞춤을 찍고는, 춤추듯 방을 나갔다."]], [["‘Dear man!’ said the spinster, as the door closed after him.", "\"고운 분!\" 그의 뒤로 문이 닫히자 노처녀가 말했다."]], [["‘Rum old girl,’ said Mr.", "\"별난 늙은 여자로군.\""], ["Jingle, as he walked down the passage.", "징글 씨가 복도를 걸어 내려가며 말했다."]], [["It is painful to reflect upon the perfidy of our species; and we will not, therefore, pursue the thread of Mr.", "우리 인간 종족의 배신을 곱씹는 일은 괴로운 노릇이다. 그러므로 우리는 징글 씨가 닥터스 커먼스로 향하는 길에 했던 명상의 실마리를 좇지는 않으련다."], ["Jingle’s meditations, as he wended his way to Doctors’ Commons.", "우리의 목적을 위해서는 다만 이것만 이야기하면 족하리라."], ["It will be sufficient for our purpose to relate, that escaping the snares of the dragons in white aprons, who guard the entrance to that enchanted region, he reached the vicar-general’s office in safety and having procured a highly flattering address on parchment, from the Archbishop of Canterbury, to his ‘trusty and well-beloved Alfred Jingle and Rachael Wardle, greeting,’ he carefully deposited the mystic document in his pocket, and retraced his steps in triumph to the Borough.", "그는 그 마법에 걸린 영역의 입구를 지키는 흰 앞치마 두른 용들의 함정을 피해, 무사히 부주교 사무소에 다다랐고, 캔터베리 대주교가 그의 \"신실하고 사랑스러운 알프레드 징글과 레이철 워들에게 보내는 인사\"라는, 더없이 듣기 좋은 양피지 문서를 손에 넣고는, 그 신비로운 서류를 주머니에 조심스레 넣고서, 의기양양하게 버러로 발길을 되돌렸다."]], [["He was yet on his way to the White Hart, when two plump gentleman and one thin one entered the yard, and looked round in search of some authorised person of whom they could make a few inquiries.", "그가 아직 화이트 하트로 돌아가는 길에 있을 때, 통통한 신사 둘과 마른 신사 하나가 마당으로 들어와, 몇 가지 물어볼 만한 권한 있는 자를 찾아 두리번거렸다."], ["Mr.", "마침 새뮤얼 웰러 씨는 그때 채색한 긴 부츠 한 켤레를 광내는 일에 매여 있었으니, 그것은 어느 농부의 소유물이었다."], ["Samuel Weller happened to be at that moment engaged in burnishing a pair of painted tops, the personal property of a farmer who was refreshing himself with a slight lunch of two or three pounds of cold beef and a pot or two of porter, after the fatigues of the Borough market; and to him the thin gentleman straightway advanced.", "그 농부는 버러 시장에서의 노고를 마친 뒤, 차가운 쇠고기 두세 파운드와 포터 한두 잔의 가벼운 점심으로 원기를 돋우고 있었다. 마른 신사가 곧장 그에게 다가왔다."]], [["‘My friend,’ said the thin gentleman.", "\"여보게.\" 마른 신사가 말했다."]], [["‘You’re one o’ the adwice gratis order,’ thought Sam, ‘or you wouldn’t be so wery fond o’ me all at once.’ But he only said--‘Well, Sir.’", "'당신은 공짜 충고 패거리 중 하나로군.' 샘은 속으로 생각했다. '안 그러면 갑자기 나한테 이렇게 살갑게 굴 리가 없지.' 하지만 그는 다만—\"네, 무슨 일이신지요.\" 하고 말했다."]], [["‘My friend,’ said the thin gentleman, with a conciliatory hem--‘have you got many people stopping here now?", "\"여보게.\" 마른 신사가 비위를 맞추듯 헛기침을 하며 말했다."], ["Pretty busy.", "\"지금 여기 묵는 사람이 많은가? 꽤 바쁜가?"], ["Eh?’", "응?\""]], [["Sam stole a look at the inquirer.", "샘은 묻는 이를 슬쩍 훔쳐보았다."], ["He was a little high-dried man, with a dark squeezed-up face, and small, restless, black eyes, that kept winking and twinkling on each side of his little inquisitive nose, as if they were playing a perpetual game of peep-bo with that feature.", "그는 자그마하고 바싹 마른 사내로, 거뭇하고 쪼그라든 얼굴에, 작고 잠시도 가만있지 못하는 검은 눈을 가졌는데, 그 눈은 작은 캐묻는 코의 양옆에서 연신 깜박이고 반짝이며, 마치 그 코하고 끝없이 까꿍 놀이라도 하는 듯했다."], ["He was dressed all in black, with boots as shiny as his eyes, a low white neckcloth, and a clean shirt with a frill to it.", "그는 온통 검은 옷차림에, 부츠는 눈만큼이나 번들거렸고, 낮은 흰 넥타이를 매고, 주름 장식 달린 깨끗한 셔츠를 입었다."], ["A gold watch-chain, and seals, depended from his fob.", "회중시계 주머니에서 금 시곗줄과 인장들이 늘어져 있었다."], ["He carried his black kid gloves in his hands, and not ON them; and as he spoke, thrust his wrists beneath his coat tails, with the air of a man who was in the habit of propounding some regular posers.", "그는 검은 새끼 염소 가죽 장갑을 손에 끼지 않고 손에 들고 있었으며, 말하면서는, 으레 골치 아픈 난제들을 척척 내놓는 데 익숙한 사람 같은 태도로, 손목을 외투 자락 밑으로 찔러 넣었다."]], [["‘Pretty busy, eh?’ said the little man.", "\"꽤 바쁜가, 응?\" 작은 사내가 말했다."]], [["‘Oh, wery well, Sir,’ replied Sam, ‘we shan’t be bankrupts, and we shan’t make our fort’ns.", "\"아, 그저 그렇습죠, 나리.\" 샘이 대답했다."], ["We eats our biled mutton without capers, and don’t care for horse-radish ven ve can get beef.’", "\"파산할 일도 없고, 큰돈을 벌 일도 없지요. 케이퍼 없이 삶은 양고기를 먹고, 쇠고기가 있을 땐 서양고추냉이 따위는 거들떠보지도 않습니다요.\""]], [["‘Ah,’ said the little man, ‘you’re a wag, ain’t you?’", "\"아하.\" 작은 사내가 말했다. \"자네 익살꾼이로군, 그렇지?\""]], [["‘My eldest brother was troubled with that complaint,’ said Sam; ‘it may be catching--I used to sleep with him.’", "\"제 맏형이 그 병으로 고생했지요.\" 샘이 말했다. \"옮는 병인가 봐요—제가 형이랑 한방에서 잤거든요.\""]], [["‘This is a curious old house of yours,’ said the little man, looking round him.", "\"여기는 참 별스러운 옛 건물이로군.\" 작은 사내가 둘레를 둘러보며 말했다."]], [["‘If you’d sent word you was a-coming, we’d ha’ had it repaired;’ replied the imperturbable Sam.", "\"오신다고 미리 기별만 주셨어도 손봐 두었을 텐데요.\" 태연자약한 샘이 대꾸했다."]], [["The little man seemed rather baffled by these several repulses, and a short consultation took place between him and the two plump gentlemen.", "작은 사내는 이 잇따른 퇴짜에 다소 당황한 모양이었다. 그와 통통한 신사 둘 사이에 잠깐 상의가 오갔다."], ["At its conclusion, the little man took a pinch of snuff from an oblong silver box, and was apparently on the point of renewing the conversation, when one of the plump gentlemen, who in addition to a benevolent countenance, possessed a pair of spectacles, and a pair of black gaiters, interfered--", "상의가 끝나자, 작은 사내는 길쭉한 은제 갑에서 코담배를 한 줌 집어 들고는, 막 대화를 다시 시작하려는 참인 듯했다. 그때 통통한 신사 중 하나—자애로운 얼굴에다 안경 한 벌과 검은 각반 한 벌까지 갖춘 이가 끼어들었다—"]], [["‘The fact of the matter is,’ said the benevolent gentleman, ‘that my friend here (pointing to the other plump gentleman) will give you half a guinea, if you’ll answer one or two--’", "\"실은 이런 일이오.\" 자애로운 신사가 말했다. \"여기 내 친구가(다른 통통한 신사를 가리키며) 자네에게 반 기니를 주겠다는 거요, 자네가 한두 가지만 대답해 준다면—\""]], [["‘Now, my dear sir--my dear Sir,’ said the little man, ‘pray, allow me-- my dear Sir, the very first principle to be observed in these cases, is this: if you place the matter in the hands of a professional man, you must in no way interfere in the progress of the business; you must repose implicit confidence in him. Really, Mr.--’ He turned to the other plump gentleman, and said, ‘I forget your friend’s name.’", "\"이런, 친애하는 선생—친애하는 선생.\" 작은 사내가 말했다. \"제발, 좀 봐주십시오—친애하는 선생, 이런 경우에 가장 먼저 지켜야 할 원칙은 이것입니다. 일을 전문가의 손에 맡기셨다면, 그 일이 진행되는 데 결코 끼어들어서는 안 됩니다. 그에게 전적인 신뢰를 두셔야 해요. 정말이지, 미스터—\" 그는 다른 통통한 신사를 돌아보며 말했다. \"친구분 성함을 깜빡했군요.\""]], [["‘Pickwick,’ said Mr.", "\"픽윅이오.\""], ["Wardle, for it was no other than that jolly personage.", "워들 씨가 말했으니, 바로 그 유쾌한 인물이 다름 아니었다."]], [["‘Ah, Pickwick--really Mr.", "\"아, 픽윅—정말이지 미스터 픽윅, 친애하는 선생, 실례지만—저는 법정의 벗으로서 선생의 어떤 사적인 제안이든 기꺼이 받겠습니다만, 반 기니를 제시하는 따위의 환심 사는 논거를 들어 이 사건에 대한 제 처리에 끼어드시는 것이 부당함은 아셔야 합니다. 정말이지, 친애하는 선생, 정말로요.\""], ["Pickwick, my dear Sir, excuse me--I shall be happy to receive any private suggestions of yours, as AMICUS CURIAE, but you must see the impropriety of your interfering with my conduct in this case, with such an AD CAPTANDUM argument as the offer of half a guinea.", "그러고는 작은 사내는 논쟁이라도 하듯 코담배를 한 줌 들이켜고, 자못 심오한 표정을 지었다. * 법정의 벗: 라틴어 amicus curiae."], ["Really, my dear Sir, really;’ and the little man took an argumentative pinch of snuff, and looked very profound.", "소송 당사자가 아니면서 법원에 의견을 제출하는 자. 그 뒤의 '환심 사는 논거'는 라틴어 ad captandum으로, 대중의 환심을 사려는 그럴듯한 논법을 뜻한다."]], [["‘My only wish, Sir,’ said Mr.", "\"제 바람은 다만, 선생,\""], ["Pickwick, ‘was to bring this very unpleasant matter to as speedy a close as possible.’", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"이 매우 불쾌한 일을 되도록 빨리 매듭짓자는 것뿐이었소.\""]], [["‘Quite right--quite right,’ said the little man.", "\"지당하십니다—지당하시고말고요.\" 작은 사내가 말했다."]], [["‘With which view,’ continued Mr.", "\"그런 뜻에서,\""], ["Pickwick, ‘I made use of the argument which my experience of men has taught me is the most likely to succeed in any case.’", "픽윅 씨가 말을 이었다. \"나는 사람을 겪어 본 내 경험상 어떤 경우에든 가장 성공할 법한 논거를 써 본 것이오.\""]], [["‘Ay, ay,’ said the little man, ‘very good, very good, indeed; but you should have suggested it to me.", "\"예, 예.\" 작은 사내가 말했다."], ["My dear sir, I’m quite certain you cannot be ignorant of the extent of confidence which must be placed in professional men.", "\"아주 좋습니다, 참으로 좋아요. 하지만 그건 제게 먼저 귀띔해 주셨어야지요."], ["If any authority can be necessary on such a point, my dear sir, let me refer you to the well-known case in Barnwell and--’", "친애하는 선생, 전문가에게 두어야 할 신뢰가 얼마나 큰 것인지를 선생께서 모르실 리 없다고 저는 확신합니다. 그 점에 무슨 전거가 필요하시다면, 친애하는 선생, 그 유명한 사건, 반웰 대—를 참조하시도록 일러 드리지요—\""]], [["‘Never mind George Barnwell,’ interrupted Sam, who had remained a wondering listener during this short colloquy; ‘everybody knows what sort of a case his was, tho’ it’s always been my opinion, mind you, that the young ‘ooman deserved scragging a precious sight more than he did.", "\"조지 반웰은 신경 쓰지 마쇼.\" 이 짧은 대화 내내 어리둥절한 청자로 가만히 있던 샘이 끼어들었다."], ["Hows’ever, that’s neither here nor there.", "\"그자 사건이 어떤 거였는지야 다들 알지요."], ["You want me to accept of half a guinea.", "하기야 내 생각으론, 잘 들으슈, 그 젊은 여편네야말로 그자보다 곱절은 더 목매달려 마땅했지만 말이오."], ["Wery well, I’m agreeable: I can’t say no fairer than that, can I, sir?’ (Mr.", "어쨌든, 그건 여기서 할 얘긴 아니고요. 댁들은 나더러 반 기니를 받으라는 거지요."], ["Pickwick smiled.) Then the next question is, what the devil do you want with me, as the man said, wen he see the ghost?’", "좋습니다, 받지요. 그보다 더 공정하게 말할 순 없잖소, 안 그렇소, 선생?\"(픽윅 씨가 빙긋 웃었다.) \"그럼 다음 물음은, 유령을 본 사내가 한 말마따나, 대체 나한테 뭘 바라는 거요?\""]], [["‘We want to know--’ said Mr.", "\"우리가 알고 싶은 건—\""], ["Wardle.", "워들 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Now, my dear sir--my dear sir,’ interposed the busy little man.", "\"이런, 친애하는 선생—친애하는 선생.\" 부산스러운 작은 사내가 가로막았다."]], [["Mr. Wardle shrugged his shoulders, and was silent.", "워들 씨는 어깨를 으쓱하고는 입을 다물었다."]], [["‘We want to know,’ said the little man solemnly; ‘and we ask the question of you, in order that we may not awaken apprehensions inside-- we want to know who you’ve got in this house at present?’", "\"우리가 알고 싶은 건,\" 작은 사내가 엄숙하게 말했다. \"그리고 이걸 자네한테 묻는 건, 안에 있는 이들의 경각심을 일깨우지 않으려는 뜻이니—지금 이 집에 묵고 있는 게 누군지 알고 싶네.\""]], [["‘Who there is in the house!’ said Sam, in whose mind the inmates were always represented by that particular article of their costume, which came under his immediate superintendence.", "\"이 집에 누가 있느냐고요!\" 샘이 말했다."], ["‘There’s a vooden leg in number six; there’s a pair of Hessians in thirteen; there’s two pair of halves in the commercial; there’s these here painted tops in the snuggery inside the bar; and five more tops in the coffee-room.’", "그의 머릿속에서 투숙객들은 늘 그가 직접 맡아 보는 그 의복의 특정 품목으로 떠올랐다. \"육 호엔 나무다리가 하나 있고, 십삼 호엔 헤시안 부츠 한 켤레가 있고, 상인용 객실엔 단화 두 켤레가 있고, 바 안쪽 아늑한 방엔 이 채색한 긴 부츠가 있고, 커피실엔 긴 부츠 다섯 켤레가 더 있지요.\""]], [["‘Nothing more?’ said the little man.", "\"그뿐인가?\" 작은 사내가 말했다."]], [["‘Stop a bit,’ replied Sam, suddenly recollecting himself.", "\"잠깐만요.\" 샘이 문득 생각이 났다는 듯 대답했다."], ["‘Yes; there’s a pair of Vellingtons a good deal worn, and a pair o’ lady’s shoes, in number five.’", "\"참, 닳을 대로 닳은 웰링턴 부츠 한 켤레랑 숙녀용 신발 한 켤레가 오 호에 있군요.\""]], [["‘What sort of shoes?’ hastily inquired Wardle, who, together with Mr.", "\"무슨 신발인가?\" 워들이 다급히 물었다."], ["Pickwick, had been lost in bewilderment at the singular catalogue of visitors.", "그는 픽윅 씨와 더불어 그 별난 투숙객 목록에 어리둥절해하고 있던 참이었다."]], [["‘Country make,’ replied Sam.", "\"시골 제품입니다.\" 샘이 대답했다."]], [["‘Any maker’s name?’", "\"제조자 이름은?\""]], [["‘Brown.’\n‘Where of?’", "\"브라운이요.\""]], [["‘Muggleton.", "\"어디 거?\""]], [["‘It is them,’ exclaimed Wardle.", "\"머글턴이지요.\" \"그들이오.\""], ["‘By heavens, we’ve found them.’", "워들이 외쳤다. \"맙소사, 찾았어.\""]], [["‘Hush!’ said Sam.", "\"쉿!\" 샘이 말했다."], ["‘The Vellingtons has gone to Doctors’ Commons.’", "\"그 웰링턴 부츠는 닥터스 커먼스에 갔습니다.\""]], [["‘No,’ said the little man.", "\"설마.\" 작은 사내가 말했다."]], [["‘Yes, for a licence.’", "\"맞아요, 허가증 받으러요.\""]], [["‘We’re in time,’ exclaimed Wardle.", "\"늦지 않았어.\" 워들이 외쳤다."], ["‘Show us the room; not a moment is to be lost.’", "\"그 방을 안내해 주게. 한순간도 지체할 수 없네.\""]], [["‘Pray, my dear sir--pray,’ said the little man; ‘caution, caution.’ He drew from his pocket a red silk purse, and looked very hard at Sam as he drew out a sovereign.", "\"제발, 친애하는 선생—제발.\" 작은 사내가 말했다. \"신중히, 신중히.\" 그는 주머니에서 붉은 비단 지갑을 꺼내고는, 소브린 한 닢을 꺼내며 샘을 뚫어지게 바라보았다."]], [["Sam grinned expressively.", "샘은 의미심장하게 씩 웃었다."]], [["‘Show us into the room at once, without announcing us,’ said the little man, ‘and it’s yours.’", "\"우리를 알리지 말고 곧장 그 방으로 안내하게.\" 작은 사내가 말했다. \"그러면 이건 자네 거야.\""]], [["Sam threw the painted tops into a corner, and led the way through a dark passage, and up a wide staircase.", "샘은 채색한 긴 부츠를 한쪽 구석으로 던져 놓고는, 앞장서서 어두운 복도를 지나 넓은 계단을 올라갔다."], ["He paused at the end of a second passage, and held out his hand.", "그는 두 번째 복도 끝에서 걸음을 멈추고 손을 내밀었다."]], [["‘Here it is,’ whispered the attorney, as he deposited the money on the hand of their guide.", "\"여기 있네.\" 변호사가 안내자의 손에 돈을 얹어 주며 속삭였다."]], [["The man stepped forward for a few paces, followed by the two friends and their legal adviser.", "샘은 몇 걸음 앞으로 나아갔고, 두 친구와 그들의 법률 고문이 뒤를 따랐다."], ["He stopped at a door.", "그가 어느 문 앞에서 멈춰 섰다."]], [["‘Is this the room?’ murmured the little gentleman.", "\"이 방인가?\" 작은 신사가 나직이 물었다."]], [["Sam nodded assent.", "샘이 그렇다는 듯 고개를 끄덕였다."]], [["Old Wardle opened the door; and the whole three walked into the room just as Mr.", "워들 영감이 문을 열었다."], ["Jingle, who had that moment returned, had produced the licence to the spinster aunt.", "그리고 셋 모두 방으로 걸어 들어갔으니, 바로 그 순간 막 돌아온 징글 씨가 노처녀 고모에게 허가증을 꺼내 보이던 참이었다."]], [["The spinster uttered a loud shriek, and throwing herself into a chair, covered her face with her hands.", "노처녀가 날카로운 비명을 지르며 의자에 몸을 던지고는 두 손으로 얼굴을 가렸다."], ["Mr. Jingle crumpled up the licence, and thrust it into his coat pocket.", "징글 씨는 허가증을 구겨 외투 주머니에 쑤셔 넣었다."], ["The unwelcome visitors advanced into the middle of the room.", "달갑잖은 방문객들이 방 한가운데로 나아갔다."]], [["‘You--you are a nice rascal, arn’t you?’ exclaimed Wardle, breathless with passion.", "\"네놈—네놈은 참 멋진 악당이로구나, 안 그러냐?\" 워들이 격분으로 숨을 헐떡이며 외쳤다."]], [["‘My dear Sir, my dear sir,’ said the little man, laying his hat on the table, ‘pray, consider--pray.", "\"친애하는 선생, 친애하는 선생.\" 작은 사내가 모자를 탁자에 내려놓으며 말했다."], ["Defamation of character: action for damages.", "\"제발, 생각하십시오—제발요. 명예 훼손에다, 손해 배상 소송감입니다."], ["Calm yourself, my dear sir, pray--’", "진정하세요, 친애하는 선생, 제발—\""]], [["‘How dare you drag my sister from my house?’ said the old man.", "\"네 어찌 감히 내 집에서 누이를 끌어냈느냐?\" 노인이 말했다."]], [["Ay--ay--very good,’ said the little gentleman, ‘you may ask that.", "\"예—예—아주 좋습니다.\" 작은 신사가 말했다."], ["How dare you, sir?--eh, sir?’", "\"그건 물으셔도 됩니다. 네 어찌 감히 그랬느냐, 이놈?—응, 이놈?\""]], [["‘Who the devil are you?’ inquired Mr.", "\"넌 대체 뭐 하는 놈이냐?\""], ["Jingle, in so fierce a tone, that the little gentleman involuntarily fell back a step or two.", "징글 씨가 어찌나 험악한 어조로 물었던지, 작은 신사는 저도 모르게 한두 걸음 물러섰다."]], [["‘Who is he, you scoundrel,’ interposed Wardle.", "\"이분이 누구냐고, 이 악당아.\" 워들이 끼어들었다."], ["‘He’s my lawyer, Mr. Perker, of Gray’s Inn.", "\"그레이즈 인의 퍼커 변호사, 내 변호사다."], ["Perker, I’ll have this fellow prosecuted-- indicted--I’ll--I’ll--I’ll ruin him.", "퍼커, 이놈을 기소하게—고발해—내가—내가—내가 이놈을 파멸시키고 말겠어."], ["And you,’ continued Mr.", "그리고 너는,\""], ["Wardle, turning abruptly round to his sister--‘you, Rachael, at a time of life when you ought to know better, what do you mean by running away with a vagabond, disgracing your family, and making yourself miserable?", "워들 씨가 누이에게 홱 돌아서며 말을 이었다. \"너, 레이철, 이젠 철이 들 만한 나이에, 떠돌이 놈하고 달아나서, 집안을 욕보이고, 제 신세를 망치다니, 대체 무슨 생각이냐?"], ["Get on your bonnet and come back.", "보닛을 쓰고 돌아가자."], ["Call a hackney-coach there, directly, and bring this lady’s bill, d’ye hear--d’ye hear?’", "거기 당장 삯마차를 불러오고, 이 부인 계산서도 가져와, 알겠나—알겠어?\""]], [["Cert’nly, Sir,’ replied Sam, who had answered Wardle’s violent ringing of the bell with a degree of celerity which must have appeared marvellous to anybody who didn’t know that his eye had been applied to the outside of the keyhole during the whole interview.", "\"알겠습니다, 나리.\" 샘이 대답했다. 그는 워들이 사납게 종을 울리자 어찌나 잽싸게 응했던지, 그 회담 내내 그의 눈이 열쇠 구멍 바깥에 붙어 있었음을 모르는 이라면 그 민첩함이 놀랍게 보였을 것이다."]], [["‘Get on your bonnet,’ repeated Wardle.", "\"보닛을 쓰래도.\" 워들이 되풀이했다."]], [["‘Do nothing of the kind,’ said Jingle.", "\"그러지 마시오.\" 징글이 말했다."], ["‘Leave the room, Sir--no business here--lady’s free to act as she pleases--more than one-and-twenty.’", "\"방을 나가시오, 선생—여기서 당신이 상관할 일은 없소—이 숙녀는 제 뜻대로 행동할 자유가 있고—스물한 살이 넘었소.\""]], [["‘More than one-and-twenty!’ ejaculated Wardle contemptuously.", "\"스물한 살이 넘었다!\" 워들이 경멸하듯 내뱉었다."], ["‘More than one-and-forty!’", "\"마흔하나도 넘었어!\""]], [["‘I ain’t,’ said the spinster aunt, her indignation getting the better of her determination to faint.", "\"안 넘었어요.\" 노처녀 고모가 말했으니, 분개함이 기절하려던 결심을 이겨 버린 것이다."]], [["‘You are,’ replied Wardle; ‘you’re fifty if you’re an hour.’", "\"넘었다.\" 워들이 대꾸했다. \"너는 분명 쉰이야, 한 시간이라도 모자랄 게 없어.\""]], [["Here the spinster aunt uttered a loud shriek, and became senseless.", "이 말에 노처녀 고모는 날카로운 비명을 지르고 정신을 잃었다."]], [["‘A glass of water,’ said the humane Mr.", "\"물 한 잔.\""], ["Pickwick, summoning the landlady.", "인정 많은 픽윅 씨가 여주인을 부르며 말했다."]], [["‘A glass of water!’ said the passionate Wardle. ‘Bring a bucket, and throw it all over her; it’ll do her good, and she richly deserves it.’", "\"물 한 잔이라뇨!\" 격분한 워들이 말했다. \"양동이를 가져와 통째로 끼얹으시오. 그게 저 여자한테 약이 될 거요. 그래도 싸지.\""]], [["‘Ugh, you brute!’ ejaculated the kind-hearted landlady.", "\"어머, 이 짐승 같은 양반!\" 마음씨 고운 여주인이 내뱉었다."], ["‘Poor dear.’ And with sundry ejaculations of ‘Come now, there’s a dear--drink a little of this--it’ll do you good--don’t give way so--there’s a love,’ etc.", "\"가엾어라.\" 그러고는 \"자, 이러세요, 착하지—이걸 좀 드세요—약이 될 거예요—그렇게 정신 놓지 마시고요—착한 분이지\""], ["etc., the landlady, assisted by a chambermaid, proceeded to vinegar the forehead, beat the hands, titillate the nose, and unlace the stays of the spinster aunt, and to administer such other restoratives as are usually applied by compassionate females to ladies who are endeavouring to ferment themselves into hysterics.", "따위 갖은 말을 연발하며, 여주인은 객실 하녀의 도움을 받아 노처녀 고모의 이마에 식초를 바르고, 손을 두드리고, 코를 간질이고, 코르셋 끈을 풀고, 또 동정심 많은 부인네들이 히스테리를 부려 일으켜 보려고 안간힘 쓰는 숙녀들에게 으레 베푸는 갖가지 회복 처치를 시작했다."]], [["‘Coach is ready, Sir,’ said Sam, appearing at the door.", "\"마차 준비됐습니다, 나리.\" 샘이 문가에 나타나며 말했다."]], [["‘Come along,’ cried Wardle.", "\"가자.\" 워들이 외쳤다."], ["‘I’ll carry her downstairs.’", "\"내가 아래층으로 데려가겠다.\""]], [["At this proposition, the hysterics came on with redoubled violence.", "이 제안에, 히스테리는 곱절로 격렬하게 도졌다."]], [["The landlady was about to enter a very violent protest against this proceeding, and had already given vent to an indignant inquiry whether Mr. Wardle considered himself a lord of the creation, when Mr.", "여주인은 이 처사에 대해 매우 격한 항의를 늘어놓으려던 참이었고, 이미 워들 씨가 자기를 만물의 주인쯤으로 여기는 거냐며 분개한 물음을 터뜨린 뒤였다."], ["Jingle interposed--", "그때 징글 씨가 끼어들었다—"]], [["‘Boots,’ said he, ‘get me an officer.’", "\"부츠.\" 그가 말했다. \"경관을 불러오게.\""]], [["‘Stay, stay,’ said little Mr.", "\"잠깐, 잠깐만요.\" 작은 퍼커 씨가 말했다."], ["Perker. ‘Consider, Sir, consider.’", "\"생각하십시오, 선생, 생각하셔야지요.\""]], [["‘I’ll not consider,’ replied Jingle.", "\"생각 안 할 거요.\" 징글이 대꾸했다."], ["‘She’s her own mistress--see who dares to take her away--unless she wishes it.’", "\"이 여자는 제 몸의 주인이니—본인이 원치 않는 한—누가 감히 이 여자를 데려가는지 어디 봅시다.\""]], [["‘I won’t be taken away,’ murmured the spinster aunt.", "\"전 끌려가지 않을 거예요.\" 노처녀 고모가 중얼거렸다."], ["‘I don’t wish it.’ (Here there was a frightful relapse.)", "\"전 그러길 원치 않아요.\"(이 대목에서 끔찍한 발작이 도졌다.)"]], [["‘My dear Sir,’ said the little man, in a low tone, taking Mr.", "\"친애하는 선생.\" 작은 사내가 워들 씨와 픽윅 씨를 따로 데려가 나직한 목소리로 말했다."], ["Wardle and Mr. Pickwick apart--‘my dear Sir, we’re in a very awkward situation.", "\"친애하는 선생, 우린 매우 난처한 처지에 놓였습니다."], ["It’s a distressing case--very; I never knew one more so; but really, my dear sir, really we have no power to control this lady’s actions.", "딱한 사건이에요—참으로요. 저도 이보다 더한 경우는 본 적이 없습니다."], ["I warned you before we came, my dear sir, that there was nothing to look to but a compromise.’", "하지만 정말이지, 친애하는 선생, 정말로 우리에겐 이 숙녀의 행동을 통제할 권한이 없습니다. 오기 전에 미리 일러 드렸지요, 친애하는 선생, 타협 말고는 기대할 게 없다고요.\""]], [["There was a short pause.", "잠시 침묵이 흘렀다."]], [["‘What kind of compromise would you recommend?’ inquired Mr.", "\"어떤 종류의 타협을 권하시오?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘Why, my dear Sir, our friend’s in an unpleasant position--very much so.", "\"글쎄요, 친애하는 선생, 우리 친구분께서 불쾌한 처지에 놓이셨지요—매우 그렇습니다."], ["We must be content to suffer some pecuniary loss.’", "우린 얼마간의 금전적 손실을 감수하기로 만족해야 합니다.\""]], [["‘I’ll suffer any, rather than submit to this disgrace, and let her, fool as she is, be made miserable for life,’ said Wardle.", "\"이 망신을 당하며, 저 애가, 바보 같은 애지만, 평생을 비참하게 사느니, 어떤 손실이라도 감수하겠소.\" 워들이 말했다."]], [["‘I rather think it can be done,’ said the bustling little man.", "\"제 생각엔 해낼 수 있을 듯합니다.\" 부산스러운 작은 사내가 말했다."], ["‘Mr. Jingle, will you step with us into the next room for a moment?’", "\"징글 씨, 잠깐 우리와 옆방으로 가 주시겠소?\""]], [["Mr. Jingle assented, and the quartette walked into an empty apartment.", "징글 씨가 응했고, 넷은 빈방으로 걸어 들어갔다."]], [["‘Now, sir,’ said the little man, as he carefully closed the door, ‘is there no way of accommodating this matter--step this way, sir, for a moment--into this window, Sir, where we can be alone--there, sir, there, pray sit down, sir.", "\"자, 선생.\" 작은 사내가 문을 조심스레 닫으며 말했다."], ["Now, my dear Sir, between you and I, we know very well, my dear Sir, that you have run off with this lady for the sake of her money.", "\"이 일을 매듭지을 방도가 없겠소—이쪽으로 잠깐, 선생—이 창가로요, 선생, 여기선 단둘이 있을 수 있으니—자, 선생, 자, 제발 앉으시오, 선생. 자, 친애하는 선생, 당신과 나 사이의 얘기지만, 우린 아주 잘 알지요, 친애하는 선생, 당신이 이 숙녀의 돈을 노리고 함께 달아났다는 걸 말이오."], ["Don’t frown, Sir, don’t frown; I say, between you and I, we know it.", "찡그리지 마시오, 선생, 찡그리지 마요. 당신과 나 사이의 얘기라고 했잖소, 우린 그걸 알고 있다고."], ["We are both men of the world, and WE know very well that our friends here, are not--eh?’", "우린 둘 다 세상물정에 밝은 사람이고, 우리야 잘 알지, 여기 우리 친구들은 그렇지 못하다는 걸—안 그렇소?\""]], [["Mr.", "징글 씨의 얼굴이 차츰 누그러졌다."], ["Jingle’s face gradually relaxed; and something distantly resembling a wink quivered for an instant in his left eye.", "그리고 어렴풋이 윙크 비슷한 것이 그의 왼쪽 눈에서 한순간 떨렸다."]], [["‘Very good, very good,’ said the little man, observing the impression he had made.", "\"좋습니다, 아주 좋아요.\" 자기가 남긴 인상을 알아챈 작은 사내가 말했다."], ["‘Now, the fact is, that beyond a few hundreds, the lady has little or nothing till the death of her mother--fine old lady, my dear Sir.’", "\"자, 사실인즉, 몇백 파운드 말고는, 이 숙녀는 어머니가 죽기 전엔 가진 게 거의, 아니 전혀 없소—훌륭한 노부인이지요, 친애하는 선생.\""]], [["‘Old,’ said Mr.", "\"늙은이지.\""], ["Jingle briefly but emphatically.", "징글 씨가 짧지만 힘주어 말했다."]], [["‘Why, yes,’ said the attorney, with a slight cough.", "\"뭐, 그렇지요.\" 변호사가 가볍게 헛기침을 하며 말했다."], ["‘You are right, my dear Sir, she is rather old.", "\"옳은 말씀입니다, 친애하는 선생, 제법 연로하시지요."], ["She comes of an old family though, my dear Sir; old in every sense of the word.", "하지만 유서 깊은 가문 출신입니다, 친애하는 선생. 무슨 뜻으로 보나 오래된 집안이지요."], ["The founder of that family came into Kent when Julius Caesar invaded Britain;--only one member of it, since, who hasn’t lived to eighty-five, and he was beheaded by one of the Henrys.", "그 가문의 시조는 율리우스 카이사르가 브리튼을 침공할 때 켄트로 들어왔고, 그 이래로 여든다섯까지 못 산 사람이 단 한 명뿐인데, 그자도 헨리 왕 중 하나에게 목이 잘렸지요."], ["The old lady is not seventy-three now, my dear Sir.’ The little man paused, and took a pinch of snuff.", "그 노부인이 지금 일흔셋도 안 됐습니다, 친애하는 선생.\" 작은 사내가 말을 멈추고 코담배를 한 줌 들이켰다."]], [["‘Well,’ cried Mr.", "\"그래서요.\""], ["Jingle.", "징글 씨가 외쳤다."]], [["‘Well, my dear sir--you don’t take snuff!--ah!", "\"그래서 말입니다, 친애하는 선생—코담배는 안 하시지요!—아!"], ["so much the better-- expensive habit--well, my dear Sir, you’re a fine young man, man of the world--able to push your fortune, if you had capital, eh?’", "그게 낫지요—돈 드는 버릇이니—그래서 말이오, 친애하는 선생, 당신은 멋진 젊은이에다, 세상물정에 밝은 사람이라, 밑천만 있다면 한밑천 일굴 수 있는 분이지요, 안 그렇소?\""]], [["‘Well,’ said Mr.", "\"그래서요.\""], ["Jingle again.", "징글 씨가 또 말했다."]], [["‘Do you comprehend me?’", "\"제 말을 알아들으시겠소?\""]], [["‘Not quite.’", "\"썩 잘은 모르겠는데.\""]], [["‘Don’t you think--now, my dear Sir, I put it to you don’t you think-- that fifty pounds and liberty would be better than Miss Wardle and expectation?’", "\"이렇게 생각하지 않으시오—자, 친애하는 선생, 당신에게 묻습니다—오십 파운드와 자유가, 워들 양과 막연한 기대보다 낫다고 생각지 않으시오?\""]], [["‘Won’t do--not half enough!’ said Mr.", "\"안 되겠소—턱없이 모자라요!\""], ["Jingle, rising.", "징글 씨가 일어서며 말했다."]], [["‘Nay, nay, my dear Sir,’ remonstrated the little attorney, seizing him by the button.", "\"아니, 아니, 친애하는 선생.\" 작은 변호사가 그의 단추를 붙들며 만류했다."], ["‘Good round sum--a man like you could treble it in no time--great deal to be done with fifty pounds, my dear Sir.’", "\"두둑한 액수요—당신 같은 사람이라면 눈 깜짝할 새에 세 배로 불릴 수 있지—오십 파운드로 할 수 있는 일이 얼마나 많은데요, 친애하는 선생.\""]], [["‘More to be done with a hundred and fifty,’ replied Mr.", "\"백오십이면 할 수 있는 일이 더 많지요.\""], ["Jingle coolly.", "징글 씨가 냉랭하게 대꾸했다."]], [["‘Well, my dear Sir, we won’t waste time in splitting straws,’ resumed the little man, ‘say--say--seventy.’", "\"자, 친애하는 선생, 자질구레한 흥정으로 시간을 허비하진 맙시다.\" 작은 사내가 다시 말했다. \"그럼—그럼—칠십으로 합시다.\""]], [["Won’t do,’ said Mr.", "\"안 되겠소.\""], ["Jingle.", "징글 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Don’t go away, my dear sir--pray don’t hurry,’ said the little man.", "\"가지 마시오, 친애하는 선생—제발 서두르지 마요.\" 작은 사내가 말했다."], ["‘Eighty; come: I’ll write you a cheque at once.’", "\"팔십이오. 자, 당장 수표를 끊어 드리리다.\""]], [["‘Won’t do,’ said Mr.", "\"안 되겠소.\""], ["Jingle.", "징글 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Well, my dear Sir, well,’ said the little man, still detaining him; ‘just tell me what will do.’", "\"자, 친애하는 선생, 자.\" 작은 사내가 여전히 그를 붙들며 말했다. \"그럼 얼마면 되겠는지 말해 보시오.\""]], [["‘Expensive affair,’ said Mr. Jingle.", "\"돈 드는 일이지요.\""], ["‘Money out of pocket--posting, nine pounds; licence, three--that’s twelve--compensation, a hundred--hundred and twelve--breach of honour--and loss of the lady--’", "징글 씨가 말했다. \"주머니에서 나간 돈만—마차 삯이 구 파운드, 허가증이 삼 파운드—그게 십이 파운드—위자료가 백—백십이 파운드—명예를 저버린 값에다—숙녀를 잃은 값까지—\""]], [["‘Yes, my dear Sir, yes,’ said the little man, with a knowing look, ‘never mind the last two items.", "\"예, 친애하는 선생, 예.\" 작은 사내가 알겠다는 표정으로 말했다."], ["That’s a hundred and twelve--say a hundred--come.’", "\"마지막 두 항목은 신경 쓰지 맙시다. 그럼 백십이 파운드인데—백으로 합시다—자.\""]], [["‘And twenty,’ said Mr.", "\"이십을 더해서요.\""], ["Jingle.", "징글 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Come, come, I’ll write you a cheque,’ said the little man; and down he sat at the table for that purpose.", "\"자, 자, 수표를 끊어 드리리다.\" 작은 사내가 말하고는, 그러려고 탁자 앞에 앉았다."]], [["‘I’ll make it payable the day after to-morrow,’ said the little man, with a look towards Mr.", "\"모레 지급으로 끊겠소.\" 작은 사내가 워들 씨 쪽을 흘끗 보며 말했다."], ["Wardle; ‘and we can get the lady away, meanwhile.’ Mr.", "\"그동안 우리는 숙녀를 데려갈 수 있고요.\""], ["Wardle sullenly nodded assent.", "워들 씨가 시무룩하게 고개를 끄덕여 동의했다."]], [["‘A hundred,’ said the little man.", "\"백 파운드요.\" 작은 사내가 말했다."]], [["‘And twenty,’ said Mr.", "\"이십을 더해서요.\""], ["Jingle.", "징글 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘My dear Sir,’ remonstrated the little man.", "\"친애하는 선생.\" 작은 사내가 만류했다."]], [["‘Give it him,’ interposed Mr.", "\"줘 버리게.\" 워들 씨가 끼어들었다."], ["Wardle, ‘and let him go.’", "\"그리고 보내 버려.\""]], [["The cheque was written by the little gentleman, and pocketed by Mr. Jingle.", "수표는 작은 신사가 작성했고, 징글 씨가 주머니에 챙겼다."]], [["‘Now, leave this house instantly!’ said Wardle, starting up.", "\"자, 당장 이 집에서 나가게!\" 워들이 벌떡 일어서며 말했다."]], [["‘My dear Sir,’ urged the little man.", "\"친애하는 선생.\" 작은 사내가 다그쳤다."]], [["‘And mind,’ said Mr.", "\"그리고 명심하게.\" 워들 씨가 말했다."], ["Wardle, ‘that nothing should have induced me to make this compromise--not even a regard for my family--if I had not known that the moment you got any money in that pocket of yours, you’d go to the devil faster, if possible, than you would without it--’", "\"이런 타협에 응하게 만든 건—내 집안에 대한 염려조차도—아무것도 없었어. 다만 네놈이 그 주머니에 돈이 들어오는 순간, 돈 없을 때보다, 가능만 하다면, 더 빨리 지옥으로 곤두박질치리라는 걸 알았기에 응한 거다—\""]], [["‘My dear sir,’ urged the little man again.", "\"친애하는 선생.\" 작은 사내가 또 다그쳤다."]], [["‘Be quiet, Perker,’ resumed Wardle.", "\"잠자코 있게, 퍼커.\" 워들이 다시 말했다."], ["‘Leave the room, Sir.’", "\"방을 나가, 이놈.\""]], [["‘Off directly,’ said the unabashed Jingle.", "\"당장 떠나지요.\" 뻔뻔한 징글이 말했다."], ["‘Bye bye, Pickwick.’", "\"잘 있게, 픽윅.\""]], [["If any dispassionate spectator could have beheld the countenance of the illustrious man, whose name forms the leading feature of the title of this work, during the latter part of this conversation, he would have been almost induced to wonder that the indignant fire which flashed from his eyes did not melt the glasses of his spectacles--so majestic was his wrath.", "이 대화의 끝 무렵, 이 작품 제목의 첫머리를 이루는 이름의 그 저명한 인물의 얼굴을, 어느 냉정한 구경꾼이 보았더라면, 그 두 눈에서 번득이는 분노의 불길이 안경알을 녹여 버리지 않은 것이 거의 신기하게 여겨졌으리라—그만큼 그의 노여움은 위풍당당했다."], ["His nostrils dilated, and his fists clenched involuntarily, as he heard himself addressed by the villain.", "그 악당에게 이름이 불리는 것을 듣자, 그의 콧구멍은 벌름거렸고 두 주먹은 저절로 불끈 쥐어졌다."], ["But he restrained himself again- -he did not pulverise him.", "하지만 그는 다시금 자신을 억눌렀다—그자를 가루로 만들어 버리지는 않았다."]], [["‘Here,’ continued the hardened traitor, tossing the licence at Mr.", "\"여기.\" 그 철면피 배신자가 픽윅 씨의 발치에 허가증을 휙 내던지며 말을 이었다."], ["Pickwick’s feet; ‘get the name altered--take home the lady--do for Tuppy.’", "\"이름이나 고치쇼—숙녀를 집에 데려가고—터피한테나 줘 버리쇼.\""]], [["Mr. Pickwick was a philosopher, but philosophers are only men in armour, after all.", "픽윅 씨는 철학자였지만, 철학자라 한들 결국 갑옷 입은 인간일 뿐이다."], ["The shaft had reached him, penetrated through his philosophical harness, to his very heart.", "그 화살은 그에게 닿아, 그의 철학적 갑옷을 꿰뚫고, 바로 그 심장에까지 박혔다."], ["In the frenzy of his rage, he hurled the inkstand madly forward, and followed it up himself.", "격노에 미쳐, 그는 잉크병을 미친 듯이 앞으로 내던지고, 자신도 그 뒤를 따라 돌진했다."], ["But Mr. Jingle had disappeared, and he found himself caught in the arms of Sam.", "그러나 징글 씨는 사라지고 없었고, 그는 자신이 샘의 두 팔에 붙들려 있음을 깨달았다."]], [["‘Hollo,’ said that eccentric functionary, ‘furniter’s cheap where you come from, Sir. Self-acting ink, that ‘ere; it’s wrote your mark upon the wall, old gen’l’m’n. Hold still, Sir; wot’s the use o’ runnin’ arter a man as has made his lucky, and got to t’other end of the Borough by this time?’", "\"이런.\" 그 별난 종업원이 말했다. \"나리 고향에선 가구가 싼가 봅니다요. 그거야말로 저절로 써지는 잉크네요. 벽에다 나리 표시를 적어 놨군요, 영감님. 가만 계세요, 나리. 줄행랑쳐서 이쯤이면 버러 저쪽 끝까지 갔을 사내를 쫓아가 봤자 무슨 소용이래요?\""]], [["Mr. Pickwick’s mind, like those of all truly great men, was open to conviction.", "픽윅 씨의 마음은, 진정으로 위대한 모든 이의 마음이 그렇듯, 깨달음에 열려 있었다."], ["He was a quick and powerful reasoner; and a moment’s reflection sufficed to remind him of the impotency of his rage.", "그는 빠르고 강력한 추론가였다. 잠깐의 성찰만으로도 자신의 분노가 무력함을 깨닫기에 충분했다."], ["It subsided as quickly as it had been roused.", "그것은 솟아오른 만큼이나 빠르게 가라앉았다."], ["He panted for breath, and looked benignantly round upon his friends.", "그는 숨을 헐떡이며, 친구들을 너그러운 눈길로 둘러보았다."]], [["Shall we tell the lamentations that ensued when Miss Wardle found herself deserted by the faithless Jingle?", "신의 없는 징글에게 버림받은 것을 워들 양이 알았을 때 뒤이어진 그 비탄을 우리가 이야기해야 할까?"], ["Shall we extract Mr. Pickwick’s masterly description of that heartrending scene?", "그 가슴 미어지는 광경에 대한 픽윅 씨의 빼어난 묘사를 우리가 발췌해야 할까?"], ["His note- book, blotted with the tears of sympathising humanity, lies open before us; one word, and it is in the printer’s hands.", "공감하는 인간애의 눈물로 얼룩진 그의 수첩이 우리 앞에 펼쳐져 있으니, 한마디만 하면 그것은 인쇄공의 손에 넘어간다."], ["But, no! we will be resolute!", "하지만 안 될 말이다!"], ["We will not wring the public bosom, with the delineation of such suffering!", "우리는 단호하리라! 그런 고통을 묘사함으로써 대중의 가슴을 쥐어짜지는 않으리라!"]], [["Slowly and sadly did the two friends and the deserted lady return next day in the Muggleton heavy coach.", "이튿날, 두 친구와 버림받은 숙녀는 무겁고 굼뜬 머글턴행 마차를 타고 느릿느릿 서글피 돌아왔다."], ["Dimly and darkly had the sombre shadows of a summer’s night fallen upon all around, when they again reached Dingley Dell, and stood within the entrance to Manor Farm.", "그들이 다시 딩글리 델에 다다라 매너 농장 어귀 안에 들어섰을 때는, 여름밤의 음울한 그림자가 사방에 어슴푸레 어둑하게 드리워 있었다."]], [["CHAPTER XI. INVOLVING ANOTHER JOURNEY, AND AN ANTIQUARIAN DISCOVERY; RECORDING MR.", "딩글리 델의 깊은 정적 속에서 조용히 쉬며 보낸 하룻밤과, 이튿날 아침 신선하고 향기로운 공기를 한 시간 동안 들이마신 것은, 픽윅 씨를 최근의 육체적 피로와 정신적 근심의 여파로부터 완전히 회복시켜 주었다."], ["PICKWICK’S DETERMINATION TO BE PRESENT AT AN ELECTION; AND CONTAINING A MANUSCRIPT OF THE OLD CLERGYMAN’S\nA night of quiet and repose in the profound silence of Dingley Dell, and an hour’s breathing of its fresh and fragrant air on the ensuing morning, completely recovered Mr. Pickwick from the effects of his late fatigue of body and anxiety of mind.", "이 저명한 인물은 친구들과 추종자들로부터 꼬박 이틀 동안 떨어져 있었다."], ["That illustrious man had been separated from his friends and followers for two whole days; and it was with a degree of pleasure and delight, which no common imagination can adequately conceive, that he stepped forward to greet Mr.", "그래서 그가 이른 산책에서 돌아오는 길에 윈클 씨와 스노드그래스 씨를 마주쳐 그 신사들을 맞이하러 앞으로 나아갔을 때, 그 기쁨과 환희는 평범한 상상력으로는 도무지 헤아릴 수 없을 정도였다. 기쁨은 서로의 것이었다."], ["Winkle and Mr. Snodgrass, as he encountered those gentlemen on his return from his early walk.", "픽윅 씨의 환히 빛나는 얼굴을 바라보고서 그런 감정을 느끼지 않을 자가 어디 있겠는가?"], ["The pleasure was mutual; for who could ever gaze on Mr. Pickwick’s beaming face without experiencing the sensation?", "하지만 그래도 그의 동료들 위에는 어떤 구름이 드리워져 있는 듯했으니, 그 위대한 인물도 이를 알아차리지 않을 수 없었으되 그 까닭은 도무지 알 수 없었다."], ["But still a cloud seemed to hang over his companions which that great man could not but be sensible of, and was wholly at a loss to account for. There was a mysterious air about them both, as unusual as it was alarming.", "두 사람에게는 무언가 알 수 없는 분위기가 감돌고 있었는데, 그것은 예사롭지 않은 만큼이나 불안을 자아내는 것이었다."]], [["‘And how,’ said Mr.", "\"그래,\""], ["Pickwick, when he had grasped his followers by the hand, and exchanged warm salutations of welcome--‘how is Tupman?’", "픽윅 씨가 추종자들의 손을 맞잡고 따뜻한 환영의 인사를 나눈 뒤 말했다. \"터프먼은 어떻게 지내나?\""]], [["Mr. Winkle, to whom the question was more peculiarly addressed, made no reply.", "이 질문이 특별히 향한 대상이었던 윈클 씨는 아무 대답도 하지 않았다."], ["He turned away his head, and appeared absorbed in melancholy reflection.", "그는 고개를 돌리고 우울한 상념에 잠긴 듯이 보였다."]], [["‘Snodgrass,’ said Mr.", "\"스노드그래스,\" 픽윅 씨가 간절하게 말했다."], ["Pickwick earnestly, ‘how is our friend--he is not ill?’", "\"우리 친구는 어떻게 됐나? 혹시 아픈 건 아니겠지?\""]], [["‘No,’ replied Mr. Snodgrass; and a tear trembled on his sentimental eyelid, like a rain-drop on a window-frame--‘no; he is not ill.’", "\"아닙니다.\" 스노드그래스 씨가 대답했다. 그러자 창틀에 맺힌 빗방울처럼 그의 감상적인 눈꺼풀 위에 눈물 한 방울이 떨렸다. \"아닙니다. 아픈 건 아닙니다.\""]], [["Mr. Pickwick stopped, and gazed on each of his friends in turn.", "픽윅 씨는 걸음을 멈추고 두 친구를 차례로 바라보았다."]], [["‘Winkle--Snodgrass,’ said Mr.", "\"윈클, 스노드그래스,\""], ["Pickwick; ‘what does this mean?", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"이게 무슨 뜻인가?"], ["Where is our friend?", "우리 친구는 어디 있나?"], ["What has happened?", "무슨 일이 일어난 거야?"], ["Speak--I conjure, I entreat--nay, I command you, speak.’", "말하게—간청하네, 애원하네—아니, 명하노니, 말하게.\""]], [["There was a solemnity--a dignity--in Mr. Pickwick’s manner, not to be withstood.", "픽윅 씨의 태도에는 거역할 수 없는 엄숙함이—위엄이—깃들어 있었다."]], [["‘He is gone,’ said Mr.", "\"그는 떠났습니다.\""], ["Snodgrass.", "스노드그래스 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Gone!’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"떠나!\" 픽윅 씨가 소리쳤다."], ["Pickwick. ‘Gone!’", "\"떠나다니!\""]], [["‘Gone,’ repeated Mr.", "\"떠났습니다.\""], ["Snodgrass.", "스노드그래스 씨가 되풀이했다."]], [["‘Where!’ ejaculated Mr.", "\"어디로!\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 외쳤다."]], [["‘We can only guess, from that communication,’ replied Mr.", "\"저희도 이 편지를 보고 짐작할 수밖에 없습니다.\""], ["Snodgrass, taking a letter from his pocket, and placing it in his friend’s hand.", "스노드그래스 씨가 주머니에서 편지 한 통을 꺼내 친구의 손에 쥐여 주며 대답했다."], ["‘Yesterday morning, when a letter was received from Mr. Wardle, stating that you would be home with his sister at night, the melancholy which had hung over our friend during the whole of the previous day, was observed to increase.", "\"어제 아침, 워들 씨로부터 당신이 그의 누이와 함께 밤에 돌아오리라는 편지가 왔을 때, 전날 하루 종일 우리 친구를 덮고 있던 우울함이 더욱 짙어지는 것이 눈에 띄었습니다."], ["He shortly afterwards disappeared: he was missing during the whole day, and in the evening this letter was brought by the hostler from the Crown, at Muggleton.", "그는 곧 사라졌습니다. 하루 종일 보이지 않았고, 저녁이 되어 머글턴의 크라운 여관 마부가 이 편지를 가져왔습니다."], ["It had been left in his charge in the morning, with a strict injunction that it should not be delivered until night.’", "아침에 그에게 맡기면서, 밤이 되기 전에는 절대로 전하지 말라고 단단히 일러두었다더군요.\""]], [["Mr. Pickwick opened the epistle.", "픽윅 씨는 그 편지를 펼쳤다."], ["It was in his friend’s hand-writing, and these were its contents:--", "그것은 친구의 필체로 쓰여 있었고, 내용은 다음과 같았다."]], [["‘MY DEAR PICKWICK,--You, my dear friend, are placed far beyond the reach of many mortal frailties and weaknesses which ordinary people cannot overcome.", "\"친애하는 픽윅에게,—자네는, 나의 친애하는 벗이여, 보통 사람들이 극복하지 못하는 수많은 인간적 약점과 결함이 미치지 못하는 저 높은 곳에 놓여 있는 사람일세."], ["You do not know what it is, at one blow, to be deserted by a lovely and fascinating creature, and to fall a victim to the artifices of a villain, who had the grin of cunning beneath the mask of friendship.", "자네는 단 한 번의 타격으로 사랑스럽고 매혹적인 여인에게 버림받는 것이, 그리고 우정의 가면 아래 교활한 미소를 짓고 있던 악당의 술책에 희생되는 것이 어떤 것인지 모를 걸세."], ["I hope you never may.", "부디 영영 모르기를 바라네."]], [["‘Any letter addressed to me at the Leather Bottle, Cobham, Kent, will be forwarded--supposing I still exist.", "\"코범, 켄트의 레더 보틀로 내게 보내는 편지는 어디로든 전달될 걸세—내가 아직 살아 있다면 말이지."], ["I hasten from the sight of that world, which has become odious to me.", "나는 내게 가증스러워진 저 세상의 광경으로부터 서둘러 떠나네."], ["Should I hasten from it altogether, pity--forgive me.", "만일 내가 그 세상에서 아주 서둘러 떠나 버린다면, 가엾이 여겨 주게—용서해 주게."], ["Life, my dear Pickwick, has become insupportable to me.", "인생은, 친애하는 픽윅이여, 내게 견딜 수 없는 것이 되었네."], ["The spirit which burns within us, is a porter’s knot, on which to rest the heavy load of worldly cares and troubles; and when that spirit fails us, the burden is too heavy to be borne.", "우리 안에서 타오르는 정신이란, 세상의 근심과 고난이라는 무거운 짐을 얹어 받치는 짐꾼의 멜빵일세. 그런데 그 정신이 우리를 저버릴 때, 그 짐은 너무 무거워 도저히 질 수 없게 되네."], ["We sink beneath it.", "우리는 그 아래 가라앉고 마는 것이지."], ["You may tell Rachael--Ah, that name!--", "레이철에게 전해 주게—아, 그 이름!—"]], [["‘We must leave this place directly,’ said Mr.", "\"트레이시 터프먼.\" \"우리는 즉시 이곳을 떠나야 하네.\""], ["Pickwick, as he refolded the note.", "픽윅 씨가 그 쪽지를 다시 접으며 말했다. \"어떤 사정이 있더라도, 이런 일이 일어난 마당에 우리가 여기 머무는 것은 점잖지 못한 일일 걸세."], ["‘It would not have been decent for us to remain here, under any circumstances, after what has happened; and now we are bound to follow in search of our friend.’ And so saying, he led the way to the house.", "그리고 이제 우리는 친구를 찾아 뒤따라 나서야 할 의무가 있네.\" 그렇게 말하며 그는 앞장서서 집으로 향했다."]], [["His intention was rapidly communicated.", "그의 뜻은 신속히 전해졌다."], ["The entreaties to remain were pressing, but Mr. Pickwick was inflexible.", "머물러 달라는 간청이 빗발쳤지만, 픽윅 씨는 단호했다."], ["Business, he said, required his immediate attendance.", "볼일이 있어 당장 가 봐야 한다고 그는 말했다."]], [["The old clergyman was present.", "늙은 목사도 그 자리에 있었다."]], [["‘You are not really going?’ said he, taking Mr.", "\"정말 가시는 겁니까?\""], ["Pickwick aside.", "그가 픽윅 씨를 한쪽으로 데려가며 말했다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick reiterated his former determination.", "픽윅 씨는 앞서의 결심을 거듭 밝혔다."]], [["‘Then here,’ said the old gentleman, ‘is a little manuscript, which I had hoped to have the pleasure of reading to you myself.", "\"그렇다면 여기,\" 노신사가 말했다."], ["I found it on the death of a friend of mine--a medical man, engaged in our county lunatic asylum--among a variety of papers, which I had the option of destroying or preserving, as I thought proper.", "\"작은 원고가 하나 있습니다. 본래는 제가 직접 당신께 읽어 드리는 기쁨을 누리고 싶었지요."], ["I can hardly believe that the manuscript is genuine, though it certainly is not in my friend’s hand.", "제 친구 한 사람이—우리 고을의 정신병원에서 일하던 의사였는데—세상을 떠났을 때, 제가 적당히 처분하거나 보존할 권한이 있던 여러 서류들 가운데서 이것을 발견했습니다."], ["However, whether it be the genuine production of a maniac, or founded upon the ravings of some unhappy being (which I think more probable), read it, and judge for yourself.’", "제 친구의 필체는 분명 아니지만, 이 원고가 진짜인지는 저로서도 도무지 믿기 어렵습니다. 어쨌든 이것이 정말 광인의 손에서 나온 것이든, 아니면 어떤 불행한 자의 헛소리에 바탕을 둔 것이든(저는 후자 쪽이 더 그럴듯하다고 봅니다만), 한번 읽어 보시고 직접 판단해 보십시오.\""]], [["Mr. Pickwick received the manuscript, and parted from the benevolent old gentleman with many expressions of good-will and esteem.", "픽윅 씨는 원고를 받아 들고, 호의와 존경의 말을 거듭 건네며 그 인자한 노신사와 작별했다."]], [["It was a more difficult task to take leave of the inmates of Manor Farm, from whom they had received so much hospitality and kindness.", "매너 농장의 식구들과 헤어지는 것은 더 어려운 일이었으니, 그들에게서 그토록 많은 환대와 친절을 받았기 때문이다."], ["Mr. Pickwick kissed the young ladies--we were going to say, as if they were his own daughters, only, as he might possibly have infused a little more warmth into the salutation, the comparison would not be quite appropriate--hugged the old lady with filial cordiality; and patted the rosy cheeks of the female servants in a most patriarchal manner, as he slipped into the hands of each some more substantial expression of his approval.", "픽윅 씨는 젊은 아가씨들에게 입을 맞추었다—마치 제 친딸들에게 하듯이, 라고 말하려던 참인데, 다만 그가 그 인사에 다소 더 많은 온기를 불어넣었을 법하니, 그 비유는 그리 적절치 않을 것이다—그리고 노부인을 자식 같은 다정함으로 끌어안았으며, 가장 가부장다운 태도로 하녀들의 발그레한 뺨을 토닥이면서, 그 인정의 보다 실질적인 표시를 저마다의 손에 슬쩍 쥐여 주었다."], ["The exchange of cordialities with their fine old host and Mr. Trundle was even more hearty and prolonged; and it was not until Mr.", "그들의 훌륭한 늙은 주인 및 트런들 씨와 나눈 정다운 인사는 한층 더 진심 어렸고 오래 이어졌다."], ["Snodgrass had been several times called for, and at last emerged from a dark passage followed soon after by Emily (whose bright eyes looked unusually dim), that the three friends were enabled to tear themselves from their friendly entertainers.", "스노드그래스 씨를 여러 번 불러서야, 그리고 마침내 그가 어두운 복도에서 모습을 드러내고 곧이어 에밀리(그녀의 빛나는 눈은 유난히 흐릿해 보였다)가 뒤따라 나온 다음에야, 비로소 세 친구는 그 다정한 접대자들에게서 몸을 떼어 낼 수 있었다."], ["Many a backward look they gave at the farm, as they walked slowly away; and many a kiss did Mr.", "천천히 걸어 멀어지면서 그들은 농장을 몇 번이고 돌아보았다."], ["Snodgrass waft in the air, in acknowledgment of something very like a lady’s handkerchief, which was waved from one of the upper windows, until a turn of the lane hid the old house from their sight.", "스노드그래스 씨는 위층 창문 하나에서 흔들리는, 영락없이 숙녀의 손수건처럼 보이는 무언가에 화답하여 공중으로 몇 번이고 입맞춤을 날려 보냈으니, 골목길이 굽이지면서 그 오래된 집이 시야에서 사라질 때까지 그러했다."]], [["At Muggleton they procured a conveyance to Rochester.", "머글턴에서 그들은 로체스터로 가는 마차편을 구했다."], ["By the time they reached the last-named place, the violence of their grief had sufficiently abated to admit of their making a very excellent early dinner; and having procured the necessary information relative to the road, the three friends set forward again in the afternoon to walk to Cobham.", "그 도시에 다다랐을 무렵에는 슬픔의 격렬함이 어지간히 가라앉아, 아주 훌륭한 이른 식사를 할 만했다. 길에 관한 필요한 정보를 얻은 뒤, 세 친구는 오후에 다시 길을 나서서 코범까지 걸어갔다."]], [["A delightful walk it was; for it was a pleasant afternoon in June, and their way lay through a deep and shady wood, cooled by the light wind which gently rustled the thick foliage, and enlivened by the songs of the birds that perched upon the boughs.", "상쾌한 산책이었다. 6월의 화창한 오후였고, 그들의 길은 깊고 그늘진 숲을 지나갔는데, 그 숲은 무성한 잎새들을 부드럽게 스치는 산들바람에 시원했고, 나뭇가지에 앉은 새들의 노래로 활기를 띠었다."], ["The ivy and the moss crept in thick clusters over the old trees, and the soft green turf overspread the ground like a silken mat.", "담쟁이와 이끼가 오래된 나무들 위로 빽빽이 뭉쳐 기어오르고, 부드러운 초록 잔디가 비단 깔개처럼 땅을 뒤덮었다."], ["They emerged upon an open park, with an ancient hall, displaying the quaint and picturesque architecture of Elizabeth’s time.", "이윽고 그들은 탁 트인 공원으로 나왔는데, 그곳에는 엘리자베스 시대의 예스럽고 그림 같은 건축 양식을 뽐내는 오래된 저택이 서 있었다."], ["Long vistas of stately oaks and elm trees appeared on every side; large herds of deer were cropping the fresh grass; and occasionally a startled hare scoured along the ground, with the speed of the shadows thrown by the light clouds which swept across a sunny landscape like a passing breath of summer.", "사방으로 위풍당당한 참나무와 느릅나무가 늘어선 긴 풍경이 펼쳐졌고, 큰 사슴 떼가 싱싱한 풀을 뜯고 있었으며, 이따금 놀란 산토끼 한 마리가, 여름의 한 자락 숨결처럼 햇살 가득한 풍경 위를 스쳐 지나가는 가벼운 구름이 드리우는 그림자만큼이나 빠른 속도로 땅 위를 내달렸다."]], [["‘If this,’ said Mr.", "\"만일 이곳이,\""], ["Pickwick, looking about him--‘if this were the place to which all who are troubled with our friend’s complaint came, I fancy their old attachment to this world would very soon return.’", "픽윅 씨가 주위를 둘러보며 말했다. \"우리 친구의 병을 앓는 모든 이가 찾아오는 곳이라면, 이 세상에 대한 그들의 옛 애착이 아주 금세 되살아나리라고 보네.\""]], [["‘I think so too,’ said Mr.", "\"저도 그렇게 생각합니다.\""], ["Winkle.", "윈클 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘And really,’ added Mr.", "\"그리고 정말이지,\""], ["Pickwick, after half an hour’s walking had brought them to the village, ‘really, for a misanthrope’s choice, this is one of the prettiest and most desirable places of residence I ever met with.’", "픽윅 씨가 반 시간을 걸어 마을에 다다른 뒤 덧붙였다. \"정말이지, 인간 혐오자가 고를 곳치고는, 이곳이야말로 내가 여태껏 만나 본 가장 예쁘고 살 만한 거처 가운데 하나일세.\""]], [["In this opinion also, both Mr. Winkle and Mr.", "이 의견에도 윈클 씨와 스노드그래스 씨 모두 동의를 표했다."], ["Snodgrass expressed their concurrence; and having been directed to the Leather Bottle, a clean and commodious village ale-house, the three travellers entered, and at once inquired for a gentleman of the name of Tupman.", "깨끗하고 널찍한 마을 술집인 레더 보틀로 가는 길을 안내받은 뒤, 세 나그네는 안으로 들어가 곧장 터프먼이라는 이름의 신사를 찾았다."]], [["‘Show the gentlemen into the parlour, Tom,’ said the landlady.", "\"신사분들을 응접실로 모셔라, 톰.\" 안주인이 말했다."]], [["A stout country lad opened a door at the end of the passage, and the three friends entered a long, low-roofed room, furnished with a large number of high-backed leather-cushioned chairs, of fantastic shapes, and embellished with a great variety of old portraits and roughly-coloured prints of some antiquity.", "땅딸막한 시골 총각이 복도 끝의 문을 열었고, 세 친구는 길고 천장이 낮은 방으로 들어섰다. 그 방에는 기묘한 모양에 등받이가 높고 가죽 방석을 댄 의자가 잔뜩 놓여 있었고, 다양한 옛 초상화와 다소 오래된, 거칠게 색칠한 판화들로 꾸며져 있었다."], ["At the upper end of the room was a table, with a white cloth upon it, well covered with a roast fowl, bacon, ale, and et ceteras; and at the table sat Mr.", "방 안쪽 끝에는 흰 식탁보를 덮은 식탁이 있었는데, 통닭구이며 베이컨이며 에일이며 그 밖의 여러 음식들이 그득히 차려져 있었다."], ["Tupman, looking as unlike a man who had taken his leave of the world, as possible.", "그리고 식탁에는 터프먼 씨가 앉아 있었으니, 세상에 작별을 고한 사람과는 도무지 닮은 데가 없는 모습이었다."]], [["On the entrance of his friends, that gentleman laid down his knife and fork, and with a mournful air advanced to meet them.", "친구들이 들어서자 그 신사는 나이프와 포크를 내려놓고, 침울한 표정으로 그들을 맞으러 다가왔다."]], [["‘I did not expect to see you here,’ he said, as he grasped Mr.", "\"여기서 자네들을 보게 될 줄은 몰랐네.\" 그가 픽윅 씨의 손을 맞잡으며 말했다."], ["Pickwick’s hand. ‘It’s very kind.’", "\"정말 고맙군.\""]], [["‘Ah!’ said Mr.", "\"아!\""], ["Pickwick, sitting down, and wiping from his forehead the perspiration which the walk had engendered.", "픽윅 씨가 자리에 앉아 걸어오느라 맺힌 이마의 땀을 닦으며 말했다."], ["‘Finish your dinner, and walk out with me.", "\"식사부터 마치고, 나하고 같이 좀 걷세."], ["I wish to speak to you alone.’", "자네와 단둘이 이야기하고 싶네.\""]], [["Mr. Tupman did as he was desired; and Mr.", "터프먼 씨는 시키는 대로 했다."], ["Pickwick having refreshed himself with a copious draught of ale, waited his friend’s leisure.", "픽윅 씨는 에일을 한 모금 듬뿍 들이켜 기운을 차린 뒤, 친구가 여유를 낼 때까지 기다렸다."], ["The dinner was quickly despatched, and they walked out together.", "식사는 금세 끝났고, 두 사람은 함께 밖으로 나갔다."]], [["For half an hour, their forms might have been seen pacing the churchyard to and fro, while Mr.", "반 시간 동안 그들의 모습은 교회 묘지를 이리저리 거니는 것으로 보였을 텐데, 그동안 픽윅 씨는 친구의 결심을 꺾으려 애쓰고 있었다."], ["Pickwick was engaged in combating his companion’s resolution.", "그의 논변을 다시 옮긴들 소용없는 일이다."], ["Any repetition of his arguments would be useless; for what language could convey to them that energy and force which their great originator’s manner communicated? Whether Mr.", "그 위대한 발설자의 태도가 전하던 그 활력과 힘을, 어떤 언어로 그들에게 전달할 수 있겠는가?"], ["Tupman was already tired of retirement, or whether he was wholly unable to resist the eloquent appeal which was made to him, matters not, he did not resist it at last.", "터프먼 씨가 이미 은둔 생활에 싫증이 났던 것인지, 아니면 그에게 던져진 그 웅변적인 호소를 도무지 뿌리칠 수 없었던 것인지는 중요하지 않다. 어쨌든 그는 끝내 그것을 뿌리치지 못했다."]], [["‘It mattered little to him,’ he said, ‘where he dragged out the miserable remainder of his days; and since his friend laid so much stress upon his humble companionship, he was willing to share his adventures.’", "\"자기에게는,\" 그가 말했다. \"비참한 여생을 어디서 질질 끌며 보내든 별 상관이 없네. 그리고 친구가 이 보잘것없는 동행을 그토록 소중히 여겨 주니, 기꺼이 그의 모험을 함께 나누겠네.\""]], [["Mr.", "픽윅 씨는 미소 지었다."], ["Pickwick smiled; they shook hands, and walked back to rejoin their companions.", "두 사람은 악수했고, 동료들과 다시 합류하러 걸어 돌아갔다."]], [["It was at this moment that Mr. Pickwick made that immortal discovery, which has been the pride and boast of his friends, and the envy of every antiquarian in this or any other country.", "바로 이 순간에 픽윅 씨는 저 불멸의 발견을 이루어 냈으니, 그것은 그의 친구들에게는 자랑이요 긍지였고, 이 나라뿐 아니라 어느 나라의 모든 골동품 애호가에게도 부러움의 대상이었다."], ["They had passed the door of their inn, and walked a little way down the village, before they recollected the precise spot in which it stood.", "그들은 묵고 있던 여관의 문을 지나쳐 마을 길을 조금 내려간 뒤에야, 여관이 정확히 어느 자리에 서 있었는지를 떠올렸다."], ["As they turned back, Mr. Pickwick’s eye fell upon a small broken stone, partially buried in the ground, in front of a cottage door.", "되돌아가는데, 픽윅 씨의 눈이 어느 오두막 문 앞 땅에 일부가 묻힌 작고 깨진 돌 하나에 가 닿았다."], ["He paused.", "그는 걸음을 멈췄다."]], [["‘This is very strange,’ said Mr.", "\"이건 참 이상하군.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘What is strange?’ inquired Mr.", "\"뭐가 이상합니까?\""], ["Tupman, staring eagerly at every object near him, but the right one.", "터프먼 씨가 자기 가까이의 온갖 물건을 열심히 쳐다보면서—정작 그 돌만 빼고—물었다."], ["‘God bless me, what’s the matter?’", "\"이런, 무슨 일입니까?\""]], [["This last was an ejaculation of irrepressible astonishment, occasioned by seeing Mr. Pickwick, in his enthusiasm for discovery, fall on his knees before the little stone, and commence wiping the dust off it with his pocket-handkerchief.", "이 마지막 말은 억누를 수 없는 경악의 외침이었으니, 픽윅 씨가 발견의 열정에 사로잡혀 그 작은 돌 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 주머니 손수건으로 먼지를 닦아 내기 시작하는 것을 보았기 때문이다."]], [["‘There is an inscription here,’ said Mr.", "\"여기 명문(銘文)이 새겨져 있네.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Is it possible?’ said Mr.", "\"그게 가능합니까?\""], ["Tupman.", "터프먼 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘I can discern,’ continued Mr.", "\"내가 알아볼 수 있겠는데,\""], ["Pickwick, rubbing away with all his might, and gazing intently through his spectacles--‘I can discern a cross, and a 13, and then a T.", "픽윅 씨가 온 힘을 다해 문지르며 안경 너머로 뚫어져라 들여다보면서 말을 이었다. \"십자가가 하나 보이고, 13이라는 숫자가 있고, 그다음에 T가 있군."], ["This is important,’ continued Mr.", "이건 중요해.\" 픽윅 씨가 벌떡 일어서며 말을 이었다."], ["Pickwick, starting up.", "\"이건 아주 오래된 명문일세."], ["‘This is some very old inscription, existing perhaps long before the ancient alms-houses in this place.", "어쩌면 이 고장의 저 옛 구빈원보다도 훨씬 전부터 있었을 게야."], ["It must not be lost.’", "이걸 잃어서는 안 돼.\""]], [["He tapped at the cottage door.", "그는 오두막 문을 두드렸다."], ["A labouring man opened it.", "일하던 사내가 문을 열었다."]], [["‘Do you know how this stone came here, my friend?’ inquired the benevolent Mr.", "\"여보게, 이 돌이 어떻게 여기 있게 됐는지 아나?\""], ["Pickwick.", "인자한 픽윅 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘No, I doan’t, Sir,’ replied the man civilly.", "\"아뇨, 모릅니다, 나리.\" 사내가 공손히 대답했다."], ["‘It was here long afore I was born, or any on us.’", "\"이건 제가 태어나기 한참 전부터, 아니 우리 누구든 태어나기 전부터 여기 있었습니다요.\""]], [["Mr. Pickwick glanced triumphantly at his companion.", "픽윅 씨는 의기양양하게 동행을 흘끗 보았다."]], [["‘You--you--are not particularly attached to it, I dare say,’ said Mr.", "\"자네는—자네는—이 돌에 딱히 애착이 있는 건 아니겠지, 짐작건대.\""], ["Pickwick, trembling with anxiety.", "픽윅 씨가 초조함에 떨면서 말했다."], ["‘You wouldn’t mind selling it, now?’", "\"지금 이걸 파는 거, 괜찮겠나?\""]], [["‘Ah!", "\"아!"], ["but who’d buy it?’ inquired the man, with an expression of face which he probably meant to be very cunning.", "하지만 누가 이런 걸 사겠습니까?\" 사내가, 아마도 무척 교활하게 지으려 했을 표정을 띠며 물었다."]], [["‘I’ll give you ten shillings for it, at once,’ said Mr.", "\"내 당장 10실링을 주겠네.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick, ‘if you would take it up for me.’", "\"자네가 나를 위해 이걸 파내 준다면 말일세.\""]], [["The astonishment of the village may be easily imagined, when (the little stone having been raised with one wrench of a spade) Mr. Pickwick, by dint of great personal exertion, bore it with his own hands to the inn, and after having carefully washed it, deposited it on the table.", "(삽으로 한 번 비틀어 그 작은 돌을 들어 올린 뒤) 픽윅 씨가 엄청난 노력을 들여 제 손으로 그것을 여관까지 들고 가, 정성껏 씻은 다음 식탁 위에 내려놓았을 때, 마을 사람들이 얼마나 놀랐을지는 능히 짐작할 수 있다."]], [["The exultation and joy of the Pickwickians knew no bounds, when their patience and assiduity, their washing and scraping, were crowned with success. The stone was uneven and broken, and the letters were straggling and irregular, but the following fragment of an inscription was clearly to be deciphered:--", "픽윅 회원들의 인내와 부지런함, 그 씻고 긁어내는 수고가 성공의 영예로 빛났을 때, 그들의 환희와 기쁨은 끝이 없었다. 돌은 울퉁불퉁하고 깨져 있었으며 글자들은 들쭉날쭉하고 고르지 못했지만, 다음과 같은 명문의 단편을 분명히 판독해 낼 수 있었다. ✝ B I L S T U M P S H I S. M. 표시"]], [["Mr. Pickwick’s eyes sparkled with delight, as he sat and gloated over the treasure he had discovered.", "자신이 발견한 보물을 흐뭇이 바라보며 앉아 있는 픽윅 씨의 두 눈은 환희로 반짝였다."], ["He had attained one of the greatest objects of his ambition.", "그는 자기 야망의 가장 큰 목표 가운데 하나를 이룬 것이다."], ["In a county known to abound in the remains of the early ages; in a village in which there still existed some memorials of the olden time, he--he, the chairman of the Pickwick Club--had discovered a strange and curious inscription of unquestionable antiquity, which had wholly escaped the observation of the many learned men who had preceded him.", "고대의 유물이 많기로 이름난 고을에서, 옛 시절의 기념물이 아직도 더러 남아 있는 마을에서, 그가—그가, 다름 아닌 픽윅 클럽의 회장이—의심할 여지 없는 고대의, 기이하고 진귀한 명문 하나를, 그에 앞선 수많은 학식 있는 인사들의 눈을 죄다 피해 갔던 그것을 발견해 낸 것이다."], ["He could hardly trust the evidence of his senses.", "그는 자기 감각의 증거를 도무지 믿을 수가 없었다."]], [["‘This--this,’ said he, ‘determines me.", "\"이것이—이것이,\" 그가 말했다."], ["We return to town to-morrow.’", "\"나의 결심을 굳히게 하는군. 우리는 내일 시내로 돌아가네.\""]], [["‘To-morrow!’ exclaimed his admiring followers.", "\"내일이라뇨!\" 그를 흠모하는 추종자들이 외쳤다."]], [["‘To-morrow,’ said Mr.", "\"내일.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick. ‘This treasure must be at once deposited where it can be thoroughly investigated and properly understood.", "\"이 보물은 철저히 조사되고 제대로 이해될 수 있는 곳에 즉시 보관되어야 하네."], ["I have another reason for this step.", "이 조치에는 또 다른 이유가 있어."], ["In a few days, an election is to take place for the borough of Eatanswill, at which Mr. Perker, a gentleman whom I lately met, is the agent of one of the candidates.", "며칠 뒤에 이턴스윌 자치구에서 선거가 열릴 텐데, 내가 최근에 만난 신사인 퍼커 씨가 거기서 한 후보의 선거 대리인을 맡고 있네."], ["We will behold, and minutely examine, a scene so interesting to every Englishman.’", "우리는 모든 영국인에게 그토록 흥미로운 광경을 직접 보고 면밀히 살펴볼 걸세.\""]], [["‘We will,’ was the animated cry of three voices.", "\"그러겠습니다.\" 세 목소리가 활기차게 외쳤다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick looked round him.", "픽윅 씨는 둘레를 둘러보았다."], ["The attachment and fervour of his followers lighted up a glow of enthusiasm within him.", "추종자들의 애착과 열의가 그의 마음속에 열정의 불꽃을 환히 지폈다."], ["He was their leader, and he felt it.", "그는 그들의 지도자였고, 그것을 절감했다."]], [["‘Let us celebrate this happy meeting with a convivial glass,’ said he.", "\"이 행복한 만남을 흥겨운 한 잔으로 축하하세.\" 그가 말했다."], ["This proposition, like the other, was received with unanimous applause.", "이 제안은 앞서의 것과 마찬가지로 만장일치의 갈채로 받아들여졌다."], ["Having himself deposited the important stone in a small deal box, purchased from the landlady for the purpose, he placed himself in an arm-chair, at the head of the table; and the evening was devoted to festivity and conversation.", "그는 그 중요한 돌을, 이를 위해 안주인에게서 사들인 작은 전나무 상자에 손수 넣어 두고, 식탁 상석의 안락의자에 자리를 잡았다. 그렇게 저녁은 잔치와 담소에 바쳐졌다."]], [["It was past eleven o’clock--a late hour for the little village of Cobham--when Mr. Pickwick retired to the bedroom which had been prepared for his reception.", "픽윅 씨가 자기를 위해 마련된 침실로 물러간 것은 열한 시가 지나서였으니—작은 마을 코범으로서는 늦은 시각이었다."], ["He threw open the lattice window, and setting his light upon the table, fell into a train of meditation on the hurried events of the two preceding days.", "그는 격자창을 활짝 열고, 촛불을 탁자 위에 놓은 다음, 지난 이틀 동안 숨 가쁘게 일어난 일들에 대한 깊은 상념에 빠져들었다."]], [["The hour and the place were both favourable to contemplation; Mr.", "그 시각과 그 장소는 둘 다 사색에 알맞았다."], ["Pickwick was roused by the church clock striking twelve.", "픽윅 씨는 교회 시계가 열두 시를 치는 소리에 정신이 들었다."], ["The first stroke of the hour sounded solemnly in his ear, but when the bell ceased the stillness seemed insupportable--he almost felt as if he had lost a companion.", "정각을 알리는 첫 타종 소리가 그의 귀에 엄숙하게 울렸으나, 종이 그치자 정적이 견딜 수 없게 느껴졌다—그는 마치 동무 하나를 잃은 것 같은 기분마저 들었다."], ["He was nervous and excited; and hastily undressing himself and placing his light in the chimney, got into bed.", "그는 신경이 곤두서고 들떠 있었다. 그래서 서둘러 옷을 벗고 촛불을 벽난로에 놓은 뒤 침대로 들어갔다."]], [["Every one has experienced that disagreeable state of mind, in which a sensation of bodily weariness in vain contends against an inability to sleep. It was Mr.", "육체적 피로감이 잠을 이루지 못하는 무력함과 헛되이 다투는, 그 언짢은 마음 상태를 누구나 한 번쯤 겪어 보았을 것이다."], ["Pickwick’s condition at this moment: he tossed first on one side and then on the other; and perseveringly closed his eyes as if to coax himself to slumber.", "이 순간 픽윅 씨의 처지가 바로 그러했다. 그는 먼저 이쪽으로 뒤척이다가 또 저쪽으로 뒤척였고, 잠을 어르듯이 끈질기게 눈을 감았다."], ["It was of no use.", "소용없었다."], ["Whether it was the unwonted exertion he had undergone, or the heat, or the brandy-and- water, or the strange bed--whatever it was, his thoughts kept reverting very uncomfortably to the grim pictures downstairs, and the old stories to which they had given rise in the course of the evening.", "그가 겪은 평소답지 않은 수고 탓인지, 더위 탓인지, 브랜디 탄 물 탓인지, 낯선 침대 탓인지—그게 무엇이었든, 그의 생각은 아래층의 그 음산한 그림들과, 그것들이 저녁 내내 불러일으킨 옛이야기들로 자꾸만 거북하게 되돌아갔다."], ["After half an hour’s tumbling about, he came to the unsatisfactory conclusion, that it was of no use trying to sleep; so he got up and partially dressed himself.", "반 시간을 뒤척인 끝에 그는 잠을 청해 봐야 소용없다는 탐탁잖은 결론에 이르렀다. 그래서 일어나 옷을 대강 걸쳤다."], ["Anything, he thought, was better than lying there fancying all kinds of horrors.", "무엇이든, 그는 생각했다, 거기 누워 온갖 무서운 것들을 상상하는 것보다는 나았다."], ["He looked out of the window--it was very dark.", "그는 창밖을 내다보았다—몹시 어두웠다."], ["He walked about the room--it was very lonely.", "그는 방 안을 거닐었다—몹시 적막했다."]], [["He had taken a few turns from the door to the window, and from the window to the door, when the clergyman’s manuscript for the first time entered his head.", "문에서 창으로, 창에서 문으로 몇 차례 오가던 차에, 비로소 목사의 원고가 머릿속에 떠올랐다."], ["It was a good thought.", "좋은 생각이었다."], ["If it failed to interest him, it might send him to sleep.", "흥미를 끌지 못한다 해도, 잠이라도 들게 해 줄 터였다."], ["He took it from his coat pocket, and drawing a small table towards his bedside, trimmed the light, put on his spectacles, and composed himself to read.", "그는 외투 주머니에서 그것을 꺼내, 작은 탁자를 침대 옆으로 끌어당기고는, 촛불 심지를 다듬고 안경을 쓴 다음 읽으려고 자세를 가다듬었다."], ["It was a strange handwriting, and the paper was much soiled and blotted.", "낯선 필체였고, 종이는 몹시 더럽혀지고 얼룩져 있었다."], ["The title gave him a sudden start, too; and he could not avoid casting a wistful glance round the room.", "제목도 그를 흠칫 놀라게 했다. 그는 방 안을 휘 둘러보며 무언가를 갈구하는 듯한 시선을 던지지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Reflecting on the absurdity of giving way to such feelings, however, he trimmed the light again, and read as follows:--", "그러나 그런 감정에 굴복하는 것이 어리석다는 생각이 들어, 다시 촛불 심지를 다듬고 다음과 같이 읽어 내려갔다. 어느 광인의 원고"]], [["# A MADMAN’S MANUSCRIPT", "# A MADMAN’S MANUSCRIPT"]], [["‘Yes!--a madman’s!", "\"그렇다!—광인의 것이다!"], ["How that word would have struck to my heart, many years ago!", "그 단어가 여러 해 전이었다면 내 가슴을 얼마나 후벼 팠으랴!"], ["How it would have roused the terror that used to come upon me sometimes, sending the blood hissing and tingling through my veins, till the cold dew of fear stood in large drops upon my skin, and my knees knocked together with fright!", "그것이 이따금 나를 덮치곤 하던 그 공포를 얼마나 불러일으켰으랴, 피가 쉭쉭 소리를 내며 내 핏줄을 따끔따끔 내달려, 마침내 차가운 두려움의 이슬이 굵은 방울로 내 살갗에 맺히고, 무서움에 두 무릎이 서로 부딪칠 때까지!"], ["I like it now though.", "하지만 지금은 그 말이 좋다."], ["It’s a fine name.", "멋진 이름이지."], ["Show me the monarch whose angry frown was ever feared like the glare of a madman’s eye--whose cord and axe were ever half so sure as a madman’s gripe.", "광인의 눈빛처럼 두려움을 산 성난 찌푸림을 가진 군주를 내게 보여 보라—광인의 움켜쥠만큼 절반이라도 확실한 올가미와 도끼를 가진 군주를."], ["Ho!", "호!"], ["ho!", "호!"], ["It’s a grand thing to be mad!", "미친다는 건 굉장한 일이지!"], ["to be peeped at like a wild lion through the iron bars--to gnash one’s teeth and howl, through the long still night, to the merry ring of a heavy chain and to roll and twine among the straw, transported with such brave music.", "쇠창살 너머로 야생 사자처럼 사람들이 엿보고—길고 고요한 밤 내내 묵직한 쇠사슬의 흥겨운 쟁그랑 소리에 맞추어 이를 갈고 울부짖으며—그 용맹한 음악에 도취되어 짚더미 속에서 뒹굴고 몸을 비틀 수 있다니."], ["Hurrah for the madhouse!", "정신병원이여, 만세!"], ["Oh, it’s a rare place!", "오, 정말 멋진 곳이지!"]], [["‘I remember days when I was afraid of being mad; when I used to start from my sleep, and fall upon my knees, and pray to be spared from the curse of my race; when I rushed from the sight of merriment or happiness, to hide myself in some lonely place, and spend the weary hours in watching the progress of the fever that was to consume my brain. I knew that madness was mixed up with my very blood, and the marrow of my bones! that one generation had passed away without the pestilence appearing among them, and that I was the first in whom it would revive. I knew it must be so: that so it always had been, and so it ever would be: and when I cowered in some obscure corner of a crowded room, and saw men whisper, and point, and turn their eyes towards me, I knew they were telling each other of the doomed madman; and I slunk away again to mope in solitude.", "\"내가 미칠까 봐 두려워하던 나날을 나는 기억한다. 잠에서 깜짝 놀라 깨어나 무릎을 꿇고, 우리 핏줄의 저주를 면하게 해 달라고 기도하던 때를. 흥겨움이나 행복의 광경에서 도망쳐, 어느 외딴곳에 숨어, 내 뇌를 좀먹어 갈 그 열병의 진행을 지켜보며 지친 시간을 보내던 때를. 광기가 바로 내 피와 뼛속 골수에 뒤섞여 있다는 것을 나는 알았다! 한 세대는 그 역병이 나타나지 않은 채 지나갔으나, 그것이 되살아날 첫 사람이 바로 나임을 알았다. 반드시 그러하리라는 것을 나는 알았다. 늘 그래 왔고, 또 늘 그러하리라는 것을. 사람들로 붐비는 방의 어느 후미진 구석에 웅크리고 있을 때, 사람들이 수군대고 손가락질하며 내게로 눈길을 돌리는 것을 보면, 나는 그들이 서로에게 저 운명 지어진 광인의 이야기를 하고 있음을 알았다. 그러면 나는 다시 슬그머니 빠져나가 고독 속에 시름겨워했다."]], [["‘I did this for years; long, long years they were.", "\"나는 여러 해 동안 이렇게 했다. 길고, 긴 세월이었지."], ["The nights here are long sometimes--very long; but they are nothing to the restless nights, and dreadful dreams I had at that time.", "이곳의 밤은 때로 길다—아주 길다. 하지만 그 시절 내가 겪은 잠 못 이루는 밤들과 무시무시한 꿈들에 비하면 아무것도 아니다."], ["It makes me cold to remember them.", "그것들을 떠올리면 몸이 오싹해진다."], ["Large dusky forms with sly and jeering faces crouched in the corners of the room, and bent over my bed at night, tempting me to madness.", "음흉하고 비웃는 얼굴을 한 크고 어슴푸레한 형체들이 방구석에 웅크리고 있다가, 밤이면 내 침대 위로 몸을 굽혀 나를 광기로 꾀어냈다."], ["They told me in low whispers, that the floor of the old house in which my father died, was stained with his own blood, shed by his own hand in raging madness.", "그들은 낮은 속삭임으로, 내 아버지가 돌아가신 그 오래된 집의 마룻바닥이 그분 자신의 피로 물들어 있다고 내게 일러 주었다—광란의 광기 속에서 그분 자신의 손으로 흘린 피로."], ["I drove my fingers into my ears, but they screamed into my head till the room rang with it, that in one generation before him the madness slumbered, but that his grandfather had lived for years with his hands fettered to the ground, to prevent his tearing himself to pieces.", "나는 손가락을 귀에 처박았지만, 그들은 방이 쩌렁쩌렁 울리도록 내 머릿속으로 비명을 질러 댔다, 그분보다 한 세대 앞서서는 광기가 잠들어 있었으나, 그분의 할아버지는 스스로를 갈가리 찢지 못하도록 두 손을 땅에 결박당한 채 여러 해를 살았노라고."], ["I knew they told the truth--I knew it well.", "나는 그들이 진실을 말하고 있음을 알았다—잘 알았다."], ["I had found it out years before, though they had tried to keep it from me.", "그들이 내게 숨기려 했건만, 나는 여러 해 전에 그것을 알아냈던 것이다."], ["Ha!", "하!"], ["ha!", "하!"], ["I was too cunning for them, madman as they thought me.", "그들이 나를 광인으로 여겼어도, 나는 그들보다 한 수 위였지."]], [["‘At last it came upon me, and I wondered how I could ever have feared it.", "\"마침내 그것이 내게 닥쳤고, 나는 내가 어떻게 그토록 그것을 두려워할 수 있었는지 의아했다."], ["I could go into the world now, and laugh and shout with the best among them.", "이제 나는 세상에 나가, 그들 가운데 누구 못지않게 웃고 떠들 수 있었다."], ["I knew I was mad, but they did not even suspect it.", "나는 내가 미쳤음을 알았으나, 그들은 짐작조차 못 했다."], ["How I used to hug myself with delight, when I thought of the fine trick I was playing them after their old pointing and leering, when I was not mad, but only dreading that I might one day become so!", "내가 미치지 않았으되 언젠가 그리 될까 두려워하기만 하던 시절, 그들이 손가락질하고 곁눈질하던 그 일에 대해, 이제 내가 그들에게 멋진 장난을 치고 있다고 생각할 때면, 나는 얼마나 기뻐 나 자신을 끌어안았던가!"], ["And how I used to laugh for joy, when I was alone, and thought how well I kept my secret, and how quickly my kind friends would have fallen from me, if they had known the truth.", "혼자 있을 때면, 내가 비밀을 얼마나 잘 지키는지, 그리고 진실을 알았더라면 나의 다정한 벗들이 얼마나 빨리 내게서 떨어져 나갔을지를 생각하며, 기쁨에 겨워 얼마나 웃어 댔던가!"], ["I could have screamed with ecstasy when I dined alone with some fine roaring fellow, to think how pale he would have turned, and how fast he would have run, if he had known that the dear friend who sat close to him, sharpening a bright, glittering knife, was a madman with all the power, and half the will, to plunge it in his heart.", "어느 호탕하게 떠들어 대는 멋쟁이와 단둘이 식사할 때면, 바로 곁에 앉아 번쩍번쩍 빛나는 칼을 갈고 있는 그 다정한 벗이, 그것을 그의 심장에 꽂을 힘을 온전히 지녔고 그럴 의지도 절반은 지닌 광인임을 그가 알았더라면 얼마나 새파랗게 질리고, 얼마나 잽싸게 달아났을까를 생각하며, 나는 황홀경에 비명이라도 지를 수 있었다."], ["Oh, it was a merry life!", "오, 그것은 흥겨운 삶이었지!"]], [["‘Riches became mine, wealth poured in upon me, and I rioted in pleasures enhanced a thousandfold to me by the consciousness of my well-kept secret.", "\"부가 내 것이 되었고, 재물이 내게 쏟아져 들어왔으며, 나는 잘 간직한 비밀에 대한 의식 덕분에 천 배로 부풀려진 쾌락 속에서 흥청거렸다."], ["I inherited an estate.", "나는 한 영지를 물려받았다."], ["The law--the eagle-eyed law itself--had been deceived, and had handed over disputed thousands to a madman’s hands.", "법이—저 매서운 눈을 가진 법조차도—속아 넘어가, 분쟁의 대상이던 수천 파운드를 광인의 손에 넘겨주고 만 것이다."], ["Where was the wit of the sharp-sighted men of sound mind?", "멀쩡한 정신을 가진 그 날카로운 자들의 재간은 어디 갔던가?"], ["Where the dexterity of the lawyers, eager to discover a flaw?", "허점을 잡아내려 안달하던 변호사들의 기민함은 어디 갔던가?"], ["The madman’s cunning had overreached them all.", "광인의 교활함이 그들 모두를 한 수 앞질렀던 것이다."]], [["‘I had money.", "\"내게는 돈이 있었다."], ["How I was courted! I spent it profusely.", "사람들이 나를 얼마나 떠받들었던가!"], ["How I was praised!", "나는 그것을 흥청망청 썼다."], ["How those three proud, overbearing brothers humbled themselves before me!", "사람들이 나를 얼마나 칭송했던가! 그 거만하고 오만한 세 형제가 내 앞에서 얼마나 굽실거렸던가!"], ["The old, white-headed father, too--such deference--such respect--such devoted friendship--he worshipped me!", "그 늙고 백발이 성성한 아버지도—그 깍듯한 공경—그 존경—그 헌신적인 우정—그는 나를 떠받들었다!"], ["The old man had a daughter, and the young men a sister; and all the five were poor.", "그 노인에게는 딸이 하나 있었고, 그 젊은이들에게는 누이가 있었다. 그리고 다섯 식구 모두가 가난했다."], ["I was rich; and when I married the girl, I saw a smile of triumph play upon the faces of her needy relatives, as they thought of their well-planned scheme, and their fine prize.", "나는 부유했다. 그래서 내가 그 처녀와 혼인했을 때, 나는 그 궁핍한 친척들의 얼굴에 의기양양한 미소가 떠도는 것을 보았으니, 그들은 자신들의 잘 짜인 계략과 멋진 노획물을 떠올리고 있었던 것이다."], ["It was for me to smile.", "정작 미소 지어야 할 사람은 나였다."], ["To smile!", "미소라니!"], ["To laugh outright, and tear my hair, and roll upon the ground with shrieks of merriment.", "큰 소리로 웃고, 머리를 쥐어뜯고, 흥에 겨워 비명을 지르며 땅바닥에서 뒹굴어야 할 사람은."], ["They little thought they had married her to a madman.", "그들은 자기들이 그녀를 광인에게 시집보냈다는 것을 꿈에도 생각지 못했다."]], [["‘Stay.", "\"잠깐."], ["If they had known it, would they have saved her?", "그들이 그것을 알았더라면, 그녀를 구했을까?"], ["A sister’s happiness against her husband’s gold.", "누이의 행복을 그녀 남편의 황금에 견주다니."], ["The lightest feather I blow into the air, against the gay chain that ornaments my body!", "내가 공중에 날려 보내는 가장 가벼운 깃털 한 올을, 내 몸을 장식하는 화려한 사슬에 견주는 격이지!"]], [["‘In one thing I was deceived with all my cunning.", "\"그토록 교활했건만 한 가지에서 나는 속았다."], ["If I had not been mad- -for though we madmen are sharp-witted enough, we get bewildered sometimes--I should have known that the girl would rather have been placed, stiff and cold in a dull leaden coffin, than borne an envied bride to my rich, glittering house.", "만일 내가 미치지 않았더라면—우리 광인들이 머리가 제법 날카롭다 해도, 때로는 갈피를 못 잡곤 하니까—나는 알았어야 했다, 그 처녀가 부럽도록 단장한 신부로서 내 부유하고 번쩍이는 집으로 실려 오느니, 차라리 뻣뻣하고 차갑게 칙칙한 납관 속에 누이는 편을 택했으리라는 것을."], ["I should have known that her heart was with the dark-eyed boy whose name I once heard her breathe in her troubled sleep; and that she had been sacrificed to me, to relieve the poverty of the old, white-headed man and the haughty brothers.", "나는 알았어야 했다, 그녀의 마음이, 한번은 그녀가 뒤척이는 잠결에 그 이름을 읊조리는 것을 내가 들은 적 있는 그 검은 눈의 소년에게 가 있었다는 것을. 그리고 그녀가 그 늙고 백발인 사내와 거만한 형제들의 가난을 덜어 주고자 내게 제물로 바쳐졌다는 것을."]], [["‘I don’t remember forms or faces now, but I know the girl was beautiful.", "\"이제는 형체도 얼굴도 기억나지 않지만, 그 처녀가 아름다웠다는 것만은 안다. 그녀가 아름다웠음을 나는 안다."], ["I know she was; for in the bright moonlight nights, when I start up from my sleep, and all is quiet about me, I see, standing still and motionless in one corner of this cell, a slight and wasted figure with long black hair, which, streaming down her back, stirs with no earthly wind, and eyes that fix their gaze on me, and never wink or close.", "밝은 달빛 비치는 밤이면, 내가 잠에서 깜짝 놀라 깨어나 사방이 고요할 때, 이 감방의 한 구석에 가냘프고 여윈 형체 하나가 가만히 미동도 없이 서 있는 것이 보이니, 등 뒤로 흘러내린 긴 검은 머리칼은 이 세상의 바람이 아닌 것에 흔들리고, 두 눈은 나를 응시한 채 깜빡이지도 감기지도 않는다. 쉿!"], ["Hush!", "이걸 적자니 심장의 피가 얼어붙는다—그 형체는 그녀의 것이다."], ["the blood chills at my heart as I write it down--that form is her’s; the face is very pale, and the eyes are glassy bright; but I know them well.", "얼굴은 몹시 창백하고 두 눈은 유리처럼 번득이지만, 나는 그것들을 잘 안다. 그 형체는 결코 움직이지 않는다."], ["That figure never moves; it never frowns and mouths as others do, that fill this place sometimes; but it is much more dreadful to me, even than the spirits that tempted me many years ago--it comes fresh from the grave; and is so very death-like.", "이따금 이곳을 채우는 다른 자들처럼 찌푸리거나 입을 놀리지도 않는다. 하지만 그것은 내게, 여러 해 전 나를 꾀어내던 그 망령들보다도 훨씬 더 무섭다—그것은 무덤에서 갓 나온 것이라, 그토록 죽음을 닮아 있다."]], [["‘For nearly a year I saw that face grow paler; for nearly a year I saw the tears steal down the mournful cheeks, and never knew the cause.", "\"거의 일 년 동안 나는 그 얼굴이 점점 더 창백해지는 것을 보았다. 거의 일 년 동안 나는 그 슬픈 두 뺨을 타고 눈물이 흘러내리는 것을 보았으되, 그 까닭을 결코 알지 못했다."], ["I found it out at last though.", "하지만 마침내 나는 그것을 알아냈다."], ["They could not keep it from me long.", "그들은 오래도록 내게 그것을 숨길 수 없었다. 그녀는 한 번도 나를 좋아한 적이 없었다."], ["She had never liked me; I had never thought she did: she despised my wealth, and hated the splendour in which she lived; but I had not expected that.", "나도 그녀가 그러리라고는 생각해 본 적이 없었다. 그녀는 내 재물을 경멸했고, 자신이 살아가는 그 호화로움을 증오했다."], ["She loved another.", "하지만 그것까지는 나도 예상치 못했다."], ["This I had never thought of.", "그녀는 다른 사람을 사랑하고 있었던 것이다."], ["Strange feelings came over me, and thoughts, forced upon me by some secret power, whirled round and round my brain.", "이것만은 나도 생각해 본 적이 없었다. 기묘한 감정들이 나를 덮쳐 왔고, 어떤 은밀한 힘에 떠밀린 상념들이 내 머릿속을 빙빙 휘돌았다."], ["I did not hate her, though I hated the boy she still wept for.", "나는 그녀를 미워하지 않았다, 그녀가 여전히 눈물 흘리는 그 소년은 미워했어도."], ["I pitied--yes, I pitied--the wretched life to which her cold and selfish relations had doomed her.", "나는 가엾이 여겼다—그렇다, 가엾이 여겼다—그녀의 차갑고 이기적인 친척들이 그녀에게 운명 지운 그 비참한 삶을."], ["I knew that she could not live long; but the thought that before her death she might give birth to some ill-fated being, destined to hand down madness to its offspring, determined me.", "나는 그녀가 오래 살지 못하리라는 것을 알았다. 하지만 죽기 전에 그녀가, 제 자손에게 광기를 물려줄 운명을 지닌 어떤 불운한 존재를 낳을지도 모른다는 생각이 나의 결심을 굳혔다."], ["I resolved to kill her.", "나는 그녀를 죽이기로 작정했다."]], [["‘For many weeks I thought of poison, and then of drowning, and then of fire.", "\"여러 주 동안 나는 독약을 생각했고, 그다음엔 익사를, 그다음엔 불을 생각했다."], ["A fine sight, the grand house in flames, and the madman’s wife smouldering away to cinders.", "멋진 광경이지, 그 으리으리한 집이 불길에 휩싸이고, 광인의 아내가 잿더미로 타들어 가는 것은."], ["Think of the jest of a large reward, too, and of some sane man swinging in the wind for a deed he never did, and all through a madman’s cunning!", "거기다 두둑한 현상금이라는 농지거리며, 결코 저지른 적 없는 짓 때문에 어느 멀쩡한 사내가 바람결에 매달려 흔들리는 꼴을, 그것도 모두 광인의 교활함 때문에 벌어진 일임을 생각해 보라!"], ["I thought often of this, but I gave it up at last.", "나는 이것을 자주 생각했지만, 끝내 단념했다."], ["Oh!", "오!"], ["the pleasure of stropping the razor day after day, feeling the sharp edge, and thinking of the gash one stroke of its thin, bright edge would make!", "날마다 면도칼을 가죽에 갈며, 그 날카로운 날을 만져 보고, 그 얇고 번득이는 날의 단 한 번 그음이 만들어 낼 깊은 상처를 떠올리는 그 즐거움이라니!"]], [["‘At last the old spirits who had been with me so often before whispered in my ear that the time was come, and thrust the open razor into my hand.", "\"마침내 전에도 그토록 자주 내 곁에 있던 옛 망령들이 내 귀에 때가 왔다고 속삭이며, 펼쳐진 면도칼을 내 손에 쥐여 주었다."], ["I grasped it firmly, rose softly from the bed, and leaned over my sleeping wife.", "나는 그것을 단단히 거머쥐고 침대에서 살그머니 일어나, 잠든 아내 위로 몸을 굽혔다."], ["Her face was buried in her hands.", "그녀의 얼굴은 두 손에 파묻혀 있었다."], ["I withdrew them softly, and they fell listlessly on her bosom.", "나는 그 손을 살며시 치웠고, 손은 맥없이 그녀의 가슴 위로 떨어졌다."], ["She had been weeping; for the traces of the tears were still wet upon her cheek.", "그녀는 울었던 모양이다. 눈물 자국이 아직도 뺨에 축축이 남아 있었으니까."], ["Her face was calm and placid; and even as I looked upon it, a tranquil smile lighted up her pale features.", "그녀의 얼굴은 차분하고 평온했다. 내가 바로 그것을 바라보고 있는 사이에도, 고요한 미소 하나가 그 창백한 얼굴을 환히 밝혔다."], ["I laid my hand softly on her shoulder.", "나는 그녀의 어깨에 살며시 손을 얹었다."], ["She started--it was only a passing dream.", "그녀가 흠칫했다—그저 스쳐 가는 꿈이었을 뿐."], ["I leaned forward again.", "나는 다시 몸을 앞으로 숙였다."], ["She screamed, and woke.", "그녀가 비명을 지르며 깨어났다."]], [["‘One motion of my hand, and she would never again have uttered cry or sound.", "\"내 손이 한 번만 움직였더라면, 그녀는 다시는 울음소리도 그 어떤 소리도 내지 못했으리라."], ["But I was startled, and drew back.", "그러나 나는 흠칫 놀라 물러섰다."], ["Her eyes were fixed on mine.", "그녀의 두 눈이 내 눈에 못 박혀 있었다."], ["I knew not how it was, but they cowed and frightened me; and I quailed beneath them.", "어찌 된 영문인지 알 수 없으나, 그 눈이 나를 위압하고 겁먹게 했다. 나는 그 눈 아래 움츠러들었다."], ["She rose from the bed, still gazing fixedly and steadily on me.", "그녀는 여전히 나를 똑바로, 한결같이 응시한 채 침대에서 일어났다."], ["I trembled; the razor was in my hand, but I could not move.", "나는 떨었다. 손에는 면도칼이 들려 있었으나, 나는 움직일 수가 없었다."], ["She made towards the door.", "그녀는 문 쪽으로 다가갔다."], ["As she neared it, she turned, and withdrew her eyes from my face.", "문에 가까워지자 그녀는 몸을 돌려, 내 얼굴에서 시선을 거두었다."], ["The spell was broken.", "주문이 풀렸다."], ["I bounded forward, and clutched her by the arm.", "나는 앞으로 뛰쳐나가 그녀의 팔을 움켜쥐었다."], ["Uttering shriek upon shriek, she sank upon the ground.", "비명에 비명을 거듭 지르며 그녀는 바닥에 쓰러졌다."]], [["‘Now I could have killed her without a struggle; but the house was alarmed.", "\"이제 나는 몸싸움도 없이 그녀를 죽일 수 있었다. 그러나 집안이 발칵 뒤집혔다."], ["I heard the tread of footsteps on the stairs.", "계단을 밟는 발소리가 들려왔다."], ["I replaced the razor in its usual drawer, unfastened the door, and called loudly for assistance.", "나는 면도칼을 늘 두던 서랍에 도로 넣고, 문을 열어젖힌 다음, 큰 소리로 도움을 청했다."]], [["‘They came, and raised her, and placed her on the bed.", "\"그들이 와서 그녀를 일으켜 침대에 눕혔다."], ["She lay bereft of animation for hours; and when life, look, and speech returned, her senses had deserted her, and she raved wildly and furiously.", "그녀는 여러 시간 동안 의식을 잃고 누워 있었다. 그리고 생기와 눈빛과 말이 되돌아왔을 때, 그녀는 정신이 나가 거칠고 사납게 헛소리를 해 댔다."]], [["‘Doctors were called in--great men who rolled up to my door in easy carriages, with fine horses and gaudy servants.", "\"의사들이 불려 왔다—멋진 말과 화려한 하인을 거느리고 편안한 마차를 타고 내 집 앞에 들이닥치는 거물들이었다."], ["They were at her bedside for weeks.", "그들은 여러 주 동안 그녀의 머리맡을 지켰다."], ["They had a great meeting and consulted together in low and solemn voices in another room.", "그들은 다른 방에서 거창하게 모여 낮고 엄숙한 목소리로 함께 의논했다."], ["One, the cleverest and most celebrated among them, took me aside, and bidding me prepare for the worst, told me--me, the madman!--that my wife was mad.", "그중에 가장 명민하고 가장 이름난 한 사람이 나를 한쪽으로 데려가더니, 최악의 사태에 대비하라면서 내게—나, 광인에게!—내 아내가 미쳤다고 일러 주었다."], ["He stood close beside me at an open window, his eyes looking in my face, and his hand laid upon my arm.", "그는 열린 창가에 내 바로 곁에 서서, 두 눈으로 내 얼굴을 들여다보고 한 손은 내 팔에 얹고 있었다."], ["With one effort, I could have hurled him into the street beneath.", "단 한 번 힘을 쓰면, 나는 그를 아래 거리로 내던질 수 있었을 것이다."], ["It would have been rare sport to have done it; but my secret was at stake, and I let him go.", "그리했더라면 진귀한 놀이가 되었으련만, 내 비밀이 걸려 있었기에 나는 그를 놓아주었다."], ["A few days after, they told me I must place her under some restraint: I must provide a keeper for her. I!", "며칠 뒤 그들은 내게 그녀를 어떤 식으로든 감금해 두어야 한다고, 간수를 한 사람 붙여야 한다고 말했다."], ["I went into the open fields where none could hear me, and laughed till the air resounded with my shouts!", "내가! 나는 아무도 들을 수 없는 탁 트인 들판으로 나가, 공기가 내 고함에 쩌렁쩌렁 울릴 때까지 웃어 댔다!"]], [["‘She died next day.", "\"그녀는 이튿날 죽었다."], ["The white-headed old man followed her to the grave, and the proud brothers dropped a tear over the insensible corpse of her whose sufferings they had regarded in her lifetime with muscles of iron.", "백발의 노인이 그녀를 무덤까지 따라갔고, 거만한 형제들은 그녀가 살아 있을 적엔 그 고통을 쇠심줄 같은 무심함으로 바라보던 주제에, 이제 그 감각 없는 시신 위로 눈물을 떨구었다."], ["All this was food for my secret mirth, and I laughed behind the white handkerchief which I held up to my face, as we rode home, till the tears came into my eyes.", "이 모든 것이 내 은밀한 즐거움의 양식이 되었으니, 우리가 마차로 집에 돌아오는 길에, 나는 얼굴에 받쳐 든 흰 손수건 뒤에서 눈물이 나도록 웃어 댔다."]], [["‘But though I had carried my object and killed her, I was restless and disturbed, and I felt that before long my secret must be known.", "\"하지만 내 목적을 이루어 그녀를 죽이긴 했어도, 나는 안절부절못하고 심란했으며, 머지않아 내 비밀이 드러나고 말리라는 것을 느꼈다."], ["I could not hide the wild mirth and joy which boiled within me, and made me when I was alone, at home, jump up and beat my hands together, and dance round and round, and roar aloud.", "나는 내 안에서 끓어오르는 그 광포한 환희와 기쁨을 감출 수가 없었으니, 그것은 내가 집에 혼자 있을 때면 나를 벌떡 일어나 두 손을 마주치게 하고, 빙글빙글 돌며 춤추게 하고, 큰 소리로 울부짖게 했다."], ["When I went out, and saw the busy crowds hurrying about the streets; or to the theatre, and heard the sound of music, and beheld the people dancing, I felt such glee, that I could have rushed among them, and torn them to pieces limb from limb, and howled in transport.", "밖에 나가 거리를 분주히 오가는 인파를 보거나, 극장에 가서 음악 소리를 듣고 사람들이 춤추는 것을 바라볼 때면, 어찌나 흥겨운지 그들 가운데로 뛰어들어 사지를 갈가리 찢어발기고 황홀경에 울부짖을 수도 있을 것 같았다."], ["But I ground my teeth, and struck my feet upon the floor, and drove my sharp nails into my hands.", "그러나 나는 이를 갈고, 발로 바닥을 구르고, 날카로운 손톱을 손바닥에 처박았다."], ["I kept it down; and no one knew I was a madman yet.", "나는 그것을 억눌렀고, 아직 아무도 내가 광인임을 알지 못했다."]], [["‘I remember--though it’s one of the last things I can remember: for now I mix up realities with my dreams, and having so much to do, and being always hurried here, have no time to separate the two, from some strange confusion in which they get involved--I remember how I let it out at last.", "\"기억난다—그것이 내가 기억할 수 있는 마지막 것들 가운데 하나이긴 하다. 지금은 현실과 꿈이 뒤섞이고, 할 일이 어찌나 많은지 늘 이리로 떠밀려 다니느라, 그 둘이 어떤 기묘한 혼란 속에 뒤엉켜 버려 그것들을 갈라낼 겨를이 없으니 말이다—나는 마침내 그것을 어떻게 발설하고 말았는지를 기억한다."], ["Ha!", "하!"], ["ha!", "하!"], ["I think I see their frightened looks now, and feel the ease with which I flung them from me, and dashed my clenched fist into their white faces, and then flew like the wind, and left them screaming and shouting far behind.", "지금도 그들의 겁에 질린 얼굴이 눈에 선하고, 내가 얼마나 수월하게 그들을 내게서 떨쳐 내고, 움켜쥔 주먹으로 그들의 허연 얼굴을 후려갈겼는지, 그러고는 바람처럼 날아가 그들을 비명 지르고 고함치게 저 뒤에 남겨 두었는지가 느껴진다."], ["The strength of a giant comes upon me when I think of it.", "그것을 떠올리면 거인의 힘이 내게 솟구친다."], ["There--see how this iron bar bends beneath my furious wrench.", "보라—이 쇠막대가 내 광포한 비틀림 아래 어떻게 휘는지를."], ["I could snap it like a twig, only there are long galleries here with many doors--I don’t think I could find my way along them; and even if I could, I know there are iron gates below which they keep locked and barred.", "나무 잔가지처럼 부러뜨릴 수도 있으련만, 다만 이곳에는 문이 많이 달린 긴 회랑들이 있어서—내가 그것들을 따라 길을 찾을 수 있을 것 같지가 않다. 설령 찾을 수 있다 해도, 아래쪽에 그들이 잠그고 빗장을 질러 두는 철문들이 있음을 안다."], ["They know what a clever madman I have been, and they are proud to have me here, to show.", "그들은 내가 얼마나 영리한 광인이었는지를 알기에, 나를 이곳에 두고 구경시키는 것을 자랑스러워한다."]], [["‘Let me see: yes, I had been out.", "\"가만 보자. 그래, 나는 외출했었다."], ["It was late at night when I reached home, and found the proudest of the three proud brothers waiting to see me--urgent business he said: I recollect it well.", "집에 다다랐을 때는 밤이 깊었고, 거만한 세 형제 가운데 가장 거만한 자가 나를 기다리고 있는 것을 보았다—긴한 볼일이 있다고 했다. 나는 그것을 잘 기억한다."], ["I hated that man with all a madman’s hate.", "나는 그 사내를 광인의 온갖 증오를 다해 미워했다."], ["Many and many a time had my fingers longed to tear him.", "몇 번이고 내 손가락은 그를 갈가리 찢고 싶어 안달했다."], ["They told me he was there.", "그들이 그가 와 있다고 내게 알렸다."], ["I ran swiftly upstairs.", "나는 잽싸게 위층으로 뛰어 올라갔다."], ["He had a word to say to me.", "그가 내게 할 말이 있다고 했다."], ["I dismissed the servants.", "나는 하인들을 물렸다."], ["It was late, and we were alone together--for the first time.", "늦은 시각이었고, 우리는 단둘이 있었다—처음으로."]], [["‘I kept my eyes carefully from him at first, for I knew what he little thought--and I gloried in the knowledge--that the light of madness gleamed from them like fire.", "\"나는 처음엔 그에게서 조심스레 눈길을 돌렸으니, 그가 거의 짐작도 못 하는 것을—그리고 나는 그 앎에 의기양양했는데—내 눈에서 광기의 빛이 불처럼 번득이고 있음을 알았기 때문이다."], ["We sat in silence for a few minutes.", "우리는 몇 분 동안 말없이 앉아 있었다."], ["He spoke at last.", "마침내 그가 입을 열었다."], ["My recent dissipation, and strange remarks, made so soon after his sister’s death, were an insult to her memory.", "그의 누이가 죽고 그토록 얼마 되지도 않아 보인 나의 최근의 방탕과 괴이한 언동은 그녀의 추억에 대한 모욕이라고 했다."], ["Coupling together many circumstances which had at first escaped his observation, he thought I had not treated her well.", "처음엔 그의 눈에 띄지 않았던 여러 정황을 한데 꿰맞춰 보니, 내가 그녀를 잘 대해 주지 않았다는 생각이 든다고 했다."], ["He wished to know whether he was right in inferring that I meant to cast a reproach upon her memory, and a disrespect upon her family.", "그는 내가 그녀의 추억에 비난을, 그녀의 가문에 모욕을 던지려 한 것이라고 추론하는 것이 옳은지 알고 싶다고 했다."], ["It was due to the uniform he wore, to demand this explanation.", "자신이 입은 군복에 비추어, 이 해명을 요구하는 것이 마땅하다고 했다."]], [["‘This man had a commission in the army--a commission, purchased with my money, and his sister’s misery!", "\"이 사내는 군대에서 장교 직위를 가지고 있었다—내 돈으로, 그리고 제 누이의 비참함으로 사들인 직위를!"], ["This was the man who had been foremost in the plot to ensnare me, and grasp my wealth.", "이자야말로 나를 옭아매어 내 재산을 거머쥐려는 음모에 앞장섰던 사내였다."], ["This was the man who had been the main instrument in forcing his sister to wed me; well knowing that her heart was given to that puling boy.", "이자야말로 제 누이의 마음이 그 칭얼대는 소년에게 가 있음을 뻔히 알면서도, 그녀를 억지로 내게 시집보내는 데 주된 도구가 되었던 사내였다."], ["Due to his uniform!", "군복에 비추어 마땅하다고!"], ["The livery of his degradation!", "제 타락의 제복에 비추어!"], ["I turned my eyes upon him--I could not help it--but I spoke not a word.", "나는 그에게로 눈길을 돌렸다—그러지 않을 수 없었다—그러나 한마디도 입에 담지 않았다."]], [["‘I saw the sudden change that came upon him beneath my gaze.", "\"나는 내 응시 아래 그에게 닥친 갑작스러운 변화를 보았다."], ["He was a bold man, but the colour faded from his face, and he drew back his chair.", "그는 담대한 사내였으나, 얼굴에서 핏기가 가시며 의자를 뒤로 물렸다. 나는 내 의자를 그에게 더 가까이 끌어당겼다."], ["I dragged mine nearer to him; and I laughed--I was very merry then--I saw him shudder.", "그리고 나는 웃었다—그때 나는 무척 흥겨웠다—그가 몸서리치는 것이 보였다."], ["I felt the madness rising within me.", "나는 광기가 내 안에서 솟구치는 것을 느꼈다."], ["He was afraid of me.", "그는 나를 두려워하고 있었다."]], [["‘“You were very fond of your sister when she was alive,”", "\"'당신은 누이가 살아 있을 적에 그녀를 무척 좋아했지요.'"], ["I said.-- “Very.”", "내가 말했다.—'아주.'"]], [["‘He looked uneasily round him, and I saw his hand grasp the back of his chair; but he said nothing.", "\"그는 불안한 듯 둘레를 둘러보았고, 나는 그의 손이 의자 등받이를 움켜쥐는 것을 보았다. 하지만 그는 아무 말도 하지 않았다."]], [["‘“You villain,”", "\"'이 악당아.' 내가 말했다."], ["said I, “I found you out: I discovered your hellish plots against me; I know her heart was fixed on some one else before you compelled her to marry me.", "'나는 너를 간파했다. 나를 노린 네 흉악한 음모를 알아냈다."], ["I know it--I know it.”", "네가 그녀를 억지로 내게 시집보내기 전에, 그녀의 마음이 이미 다른 누군가에게 못 박혀 있었음을 안다. 나는 안다—나는 안다.'"]], [["‘He jumped suddenly from his chair, brandished it aloft, and bid me stand back--for I took care to be getting closer to him all the time I spoke.", "\"그는 별안간 의자에서 벌떡 일어나 그것을 높이 휘두르며, 내게 물러서라고 외쳤다—내가 말하는 내내 줄곧 그에게 더 가까이 다가가고 있었으니까."]], [["‘I screamed rather than talked, for I felt tumultuous passions eddying through my veins, and the old spirits whispering and taunting me to tear his heart out.", "\"나는 말한다기보다 비명을 질렀으니, 격동하는 격정이 내 핏줄을 따라 소용돌이치는 것을, 그리고 옛 망령들이 그의 심장을 도려내라고 속삭이며 부추기는 것을 느꼈기 때문이다."]], [["‘“Damn you,”", "\"'빌어먹을 놈.'"], ["said I, starting up, and rushing upon him; “I killed her.", "나는 벌떡 일어서며, 그에게 달려들며 말했다. '내가 그녀를 죽였다."], ["I am a madman.", "나는 광인이다."], ["Down with you.", "쓰러져라. 피, 피다!"], ["Blood, blood! I will have it!”", "내 기어이 그것을 보고야 말겠다!'"]], [["‘I turned aside with one blow the chair he hurled at me in his terror, and closed with him; and with a heavy crash we rolled upon the floor together.", "\"나는 그가 공포에 질려 내게 내던진 의자를 단번에 후려쳐 옆으로 쳐냈고, 그와 맞붙었다. 그리고 묵직하게 쿵 하는 소리와 함께 우리는 함께 바닥을 뒹굴었다."]], [["‘It was a fine struggle that; for he was a tall, strong man, fighting for his life; and I, a powerful madman, thirsting to destroy him.", "\"그것은 멋진 몸싸움이었다. 그는 키 크고 힘센 사내로 제 목숨을 걸고 싸웠으니까."], ["I knew no strength could equal mine, and I was right.", "그리고 나는, 그를 파멸시키기를 갈망하는 힘센 광인이었다. 그 어떤 힘도 내 힘에 맞설 수 없음을 나는 알았고, 내 생각이 옳았다."], ["Right again, though a madman!", "광인이건만 또다시 옳았던 것이다!"], ["His struggles grew fainter.", "그의 몸부림이 차츰 약해졌다."], ["I knelt upon his chest, and clasped his brawny throat firmly with both hands.", "나는 그의 가슴 위에 무릎을 꿇고, 그 억센 목을 두 손으로 단단히 졸랐다."], ["His face grew purple; his eyes were starting from his head, and with protruded tongue, he seemed to mock me.", "그의 얼굴이 시퍼레졌다. 두 눈은 머리에서 튀어나올 듯했고, 혀를 쑥 내민 채 나를 비웃는 것만 같았다."], ["I squeezed the tighter.", "나는 더 세게 졸랐다."]], [["‘The door was suddenly burst open with a loud noise, and a crowd of people rushed forward, crying aloud to each other to secure the madman.", "\"문이 요란한 소리와 함께 별안간 벌컥 열리며, 한 무리의 사람들이 서로에게 큰 소리로 저 광인을 붙잡으라고 외치며 앞으로 쏟아져 들어왔다."]], [["‘My secret was out; and my only struggle now was for liberty and freedom.", "\"내 비밀이 드러났다. 이제 나의 유일한 몸부림은 자유를, 해방을 위한 것이었다."], ["I gained my feet before a hand was on me, threw myself among my assailants, and cleared my way with my strong arm, as if I bore a hatchet in my hand, and hewed them down before me.", "누구의 손이 내게 닿기도 전에 나는 두 발로 일어서서, 나를 공격하는 자들 가운데로 몸을 던졌고, 마치 손에 손도끼라도 든 양 억센 팔로 길을 열며 그들을 앞에서 베어 넘겼다."], ["I gained the door, dropped over the banisters, and in an instant was in the street.", "나는 문에 다다라, 난간 너머로 뛰어내렸고, 순식간에 거리로 나섰다."]], [["‘Straight and swift I ran, and no one dared to stop me.", "\"나는 곧장 쏜살같이 내달렸고, 아무도 감히 나를 막지 못했다."], ["I heard the noise of the feet behind, and redoubled my speed.", "등 뒤로 발소리가 들려와, 나는 속도를 곱절로 했다."], ["It grew fainter and fainter in the distance, and at length died away altogether; but on I bounded, through marsh and rivulet, over fence and wall, with a wild shout which was taken up by the strange beings that flocked around me on every side, and swelled the sound, till it pierced the air.", "그 소리는 멀어지며 점점 희미해지다가, 마침내 아주 잦아들어 버렸다. 그러나 나는 늪과 시내를 가로질러, 울타리와 담을 넘어, 거친 함성을 내지르며 껑충껑충 내달렸으니, 그 함성은 사방에서 내게 떼 지어 몰려든 기괴한 존재들에게 받아들여져, 그 소리가 부풀어 올라 마침내 허공을 꿰뚫었다."], ["I was borne upon the arms of demons who swept along upon the wind, and bore down bank and hedge before them, and spun me round and round with a rustle and a speed that made my head swim, until at last they threw me from them with a violent shock, and I fell heavily upon the earth.", "나는 바람을 타고 내달리는 악마들의 팔에 떠받쳐졌으니, 그들은 둑과 산울타리를 앞에서 무너뜨리며, 부스럭거리는 소리와 함께 어찌나 빠르게 나를 빙글빙글 돌렸던지 머리가 어질어질했고, 마침내 그들은 격렬한 충격과 함께 나를 내던졌으며, 나는 땅바닥에 무겁게 떨어졌다."], ["When I woke I found myself here--here in this gray cell, where the sunlight seldom comes, and the moon steals in, in rays which only serve to show the dark shadows about me, and that silent figure in its old corner.", "깨어났을 때 나는 내가 이곳에 있음을 알았다—햇빛은 좀처럼 들지 않고, 달빛이 슬며시 스며드나 그 빛줄기는 그저 내 둘레의 어두운 그림자들과, 제 옛 구석에 선 저 말 없는 형체를 드러내는 데나 쓸모 있는 이 잿빛 감방에."], ["When I lie awake, I can sometimes hear strange shrieks and cries from distant parts of this large place.", "잠들지 못하고 누워 있노라면, 나는 때때로 이 넓은 곳의 먼 구석들에서 들려오는 기괴한 비명과 절규를 들을 수 있다. 그것들이 무엇인지 나는 모른다."], ["What they are, I know not; but they neither come from that pale form, nor does it regard them.", "하지만 그것들은 저 창백한 형체에게서 나오는 것도 아니요, 그 형체가 그것들에 아랑곳하는 것도 아니다."], ["For from the first shades of dusk till the earliest light of morning, it still stands motionless in the same place, listening to the music of my iron chain, and watching my gambols on my straw bed.’", "황혼의 첫 그늘이 질 때부터 아침의 가장 이른 빛이 들 때까지, 그것은 여전히 같은 자리에 미동도 없이 서서, 내 쇠사슬의 음악에 귀 기울이고, 내 짚 침대 위의 까불대는 몸짓을 지켜보고 있으니까.\""]], [["At the end of the manuscript was written, in another hand, this note:--", "원고 끝에는 다른 필체로 이런 주석이 적혀 있었다."]], [["[The unhappy man whose ravings are recorded above, was a melancholy instance of the baneful results of energies misdirected in early life, and excesses prolonged until their consequences could never be repaired.", "[위에 헛소리가 기록된 이 불행한 사내는, 젊은 시절 그릇된 방향으로 쓰인 정력과, 그 결과를 결코 돌이킬 수 없게 될 때까지 이어진 무절제가 빚어낸 해로운 결과의 우울한 한 본보기였다."], ["The thoughtless riot, dissipation, and debauchery of his younger days produced fever and delirium.", "젊은 날의 분별없는 방탕과 난봉과 음탕은 열병과 섬망을 낳았다."], ["The first effects of the latter was the strange delusion, founded upon a well-known medical theory, strongly contended for by some, and as strongly contested by others, that an hereditary madness existed in his family.", "후자의 첫 영향은 어떤 기이한 망상이었으니, 그것은 어떤 이들은 강력히 주장하고 또 어떤 이들은 그만큼 강력히 반박하는, 잘 알려진 한 의학 이론에 바탕을 둔 것으로, 곧 그의 집안에 유전적 광기가 존재한다는 것이었다."], ["This produced a settled gloom, which in time developed a morbid insanity, and finally terminated in raving madness.", "이것이 고착된 우울을 낳았고, 그것이 차차 병적인 정신착란으로 발전하여, 마침내 광란의 광기로 귀결되었다."], ["There is every reason to believe that the events he detailed, though distorted in the description by his diseased imagination, really happened.", "그가 상세히 늘어놓은 사건들은, 그의 병든 상상으로 묘사가 일그러져 있긴 해도, 실제로 일어난 일이라고 믿을 만한 온갖 근거가 있다."], ["It is only matter of wonder to those who were acquainted with the vices of his early career, that his passions, when no longer controlled by reason, did not lead him to the commission of still more frightful deeds.]", "다만 그의 초년 생애의 악덕들을 잘 알던 이들로서는, 이성에 더는 통제되지 않게 된 그의 격정이 그를 한층 더 끔찍한 행위로 몰아가지 않았다는 것만이 놀라울 따름이다.]"]], [["Mr. Pickwick’s candle was just expiring in the socket, as he concluded the perusal of the old clergyman’s manuscript; and when the light went suddenly out, without any previous flicker by way of warning, it communicated a very considerable start to his excited frame.", "픽윅 씨가 늙은 목사의 원고를 다 읽어 갈 무렵, 그의 촛불은 막 받침 구멍에서 꺼져 가고 있었다."], ["Hastily throwing off such articles of clothing as he had put on when he rose from his uneasy bed, and casting a fearful glance around, he once more scrambled hastily between the sheets, and soon fell fast asleep.", "그리고 그 불이, 미리 경고하듯 깜빡이는 일조차 없이 별안간 꺼져 버리자, 그것은 들떠 있던 그의 몸뚱이를 적잖이 흠칫 놀라게 했다. 그는 그 불편한 침대에서 일어났을 때 걸쳐 두었던 옷가지들을 황급히 벗어 던지고, 두려운 눈길을 사방에 던지면서, 다시 한 번 이불 속으로 허둥지둥 기어들었고, 이내 깊이 잠들었다."]], [["The sun was shining brilliantly into his chamber, when he awoke, and the morning was far advanced.", "그가 깨어났을 때 햇빛이 그의 방 안으로 찬란하게 비쳐 들고 있었고, 아침은 이미 한참 무르익어 있었다."], ["The gloom which had oppressed him on the previous night had disappeared with the dark shadows which shrouded the landscape, and his thoughts and feelings were as light and gay as the morning itself.", "전날 밤 그를 짓누르던 음울함은, 풍경을 뒤덮었던 어두운 그림자와 함께 사라졌고, 그의 생각과 기분은 아침 그 자체만큼이나 가볍고 명랑했다."], ["After a hearty breakfast, the four gentlemen sallied forth to walk to Gravesend, followed by a man bearing the stone in its deal box.", "푸짐한 아침 식사를 마친 뒤, 네 신사는 그레이브젠드까지 걸어가려고 힘차게 나섰으니, 전나무 상자에 담긴 그 돌을 든 사내 하나가 뒤따랐다."], ["They reached the town about one o’clock (their luggage they had directed to be forwarded to the city, from Rochester), and being fortunate enough to secure places on the outside of a coach, arrived in London in sound health and spirits, on that same afternoon.", "그들은 한 시쯤 그 읍에 다다랐고(짐은 로체스터에서 시내로 보내도록 일러 두었다), 다행히 마차 바깥쪽 좌석을 잡을 수 있어서, 바로 그날 오후에 건강하고 활기찬 모습으로 런던에 도착했다."]], [["The next three or four days were occupied with the preparations which were necessary for their journey to the borough of Eatanswill.", "이어지는 사나흘은 이턴스윌 자치구로 떠나는 여행에 필요한 준비로 채워졌다."], ["As any references to that most important undertaking demands a separate chapter, we may devote the few lines which remain at the close of this, to narrate, with great brevity, the history of the antiquarian discovery.", "그 더없이 중요한 일에 관한 어떤 언급이든 별도의 장을 요하므로, 우리는 이 장의 끝머리에 남은 몇 줄을, 그 골동품 발견의 내력을 아주 간략히 이야기하는 데 바치기로 하자."]], [["It appears from the Transactions of the Club, then, that Mr. Pickwick lectured upon the discovery at a General Club Meeting, convened on the night succeeding their return, and entered into a variety of ingenious and erudite speculations on the meaning of the inscription.", "그러므로 클럽의 의사록에 따르면, 픽윅 씨는 그들이 돌아온 이튿날 밤에 소집된 클럽 총회에서 그 발견에 관하여 강연했고, 그 명문의 의미에 대해 갖가지 기발하고 박학한 추론을 펼쳤다."], ["It also appears that a skilful artist executed a faithful delineation of the curiosity, which was engraven on stone, and presented to the Royal Antiquarian Society, and other learned bodies: that heart-burnings and jealousies without number were created by rival controversies which were penned upon the subject; and that Mr.", "또한 솜씨 좋은 화공 하나가 그 진귀한 물건을 충실히 묘사한 그림을 그려, 그것을 돌에 새겨서 왕립 골동품 학회와 그 밖의 학술 단체들에 증정한 일도 드러난다. 그 주제를 두고 쓰인 경쟁적 논쟁들로 인해 이루 헤아릴 수 없는 시기와 질투가 일어났으며, 픽윅 씨 자신은 아주 작은 활자로 아흔여섯 면에 걸친, 그 명문에 대한 스물일곱 가지의 서로 다른 판독을 담은 소책자를 써냈다는 것도 드러난다."], ["Pickwick himself wrote a pamphlet, containing ninety-six pages of very small print, and twenty-seven different readings of the inscription: that three old gentlemen cut off their eldest sons with a shilling a-piece for presuming to doubt the antiquity of the fragment; and that one enthusiastic individual cut himself off prematurely, in despair at being unable to fathom its meaning: that Mr.", "노신사 세 사람은 그 단편의 고대성을 감히 의심했다는 이유로 제 맏아들들에게 각기 1실링씩만 떼어 주고 상속에서 내쳤으며, 어느 열성적인 인물 하나는 그 의미를 도무지 헤아릴 수 없다는 절망에 빠져 스스로 제 명을 일찍 끊어 버렸다는 것, 그리고 픽윅 씨는 그 발견을 이루어 낸 공로로 국내외 열일곱 학회의 명예 회원으로 추대되었다는 것도 드러난다."], ["Pickwick was elected an honorary member of seventeen native and foreign societies, for making the discovery: that none of the seventeen could make anything of it; but that all the seventeen agreed it was very extraordinary.", "그 열일곱 학회 가운데 어느 곳도 그것에 대해 무어라 알아내지 못했으나, 열일곱 학회 모두 그것이 참으로 비범한 물건이라는 데는 의견을 같이했다는 것 또한."]], [["Mr. Blotton, indeed--and the name will be doomed to the undying contempt of those who cultivate the mysterious and the sublime--Mr.", "사실 블로턴 씨는—그 이름은 신비롭고 숭고한 것을 가꾸는 자들의 영원한 멸시를 받을 운명에 처할 터인데—그 블로턴 씨는, 우리가 말하건대, 천박한 정신에 특유한 의심과 트집을 부려, 격을 떨어뜨리는 만큼이나 우스꽝스러운 견해를 감히 내놓았다."], ["Blotton, we say, with the doubt and cavilling peculiar to vulgar minds, presumed to state a view of the case, as degrading as ridiculous. Mr.", "블로턴 씨는 불멸의 이름 픽윅의 광채를 흐리려는 비열한 욕심에서, 실제로 몸소 코범까지 여행을 떠났고, 돌아와서는 클럽의 한 연설에서 비아냥대며 이렇게 말했다."], ["Blotton, with a mean desire to tarnish the lustre of the immortal name of Pickwick, actually undertook a journey to Cobham in person, and on his return, sarcastically observed in an oration at the club, that he had seen the man from whom the stone was purchased; that the man presumed the stone to be ancient, but solemnly denied the antiquity of the inscription-- inasmuch as he represented it to have been rudely carved by himself in an idle mood, and to display letters intended to bear neither more or less than the simple construction of--‘BILL STUMPS, HIS MARK’; and that Mr.", "자신이 그 돌을 사들였던 그 사내를 만나 보았는데, 그 사내는 돌이 오래된 것이라는 데는 동의하면서도 그 명문의 고대성만큼은 엄숙히 부인했다는 것이다—왜냐하면 그자는 그것을 자신이 한가한 기분에 직접 거칠게 새긴 것이라고 진술했으며, 그 글자들은 다름 아닌 '빌 스텀프스, 그의 표시'라는 단순한 뜻 그 이상도 그 이하도 나타내려 한 것이 아니라고 했다는 것이다."], ["Stumps, being little in the habit of original composition, and more accustomed to be guided by the sound of words than by the strict rules of orthography, had omitted the concluding ‘L’ of his Christian name.", "그리고 스텀프스 씨는 본디 글을 직접 지어 보는 일에 거의 익숙지 않고, 철자법의 엄정한 규칙보다는 말소리에 이끌리는 데 더 익숙한지라, 제 세례명의 끝 'L'을 빠뜨렸다는 것이었다."]], [["The Pickwick Club (as might have been expected from so enlightened an institution) received this statement with the contempt it deserved, expelled the presumptuous and ill-conditioned Blotton from the society, and voted Mr. Pickwick a pair of gold spectacles, in token of their confidence and approbation: in return for which, Mr.", "픽윅 클럽은 (그토록 계몽된 단체에게서 능히 기대할 법한 대로) 이 진술을 마땅한 멸시로써 받아들였고, 그 주제넘고 성미 고약한 블로턴을 회에서 축출했으며, 신임과 인정의 표시로 픽윅 씨에게 금테 안경 한 벌을 표결로 증정했다."], ["Pickwick caused a portrait of himself to be painted, and hung up in the club room.", "그 답례로 픽윅 씨는 자신의 초상화를 그리게 하여 클럽 방에 걸어 두도록 했다."]], [["Mr.", "블로턴 씨는 쫓겨나기는 했어도 굴복하지는 않았다."], ["Blotton was ejected but not conquered.", "그 역시 국내외 열일곱 학회 앞으로 소책자 한 권을 써 보냈는데, 거기에는 자신이 이미 했던 진술을 되풀이하고, 더 나아가 그 열일곱 학회가 죄다 한낱 '허풍선이들'이라는 자신의 견해를 절반 이상 내비쳤다. 이에 열일곱 학회의 의로운 분노가 들끓어, 새로운 소책자 여러 권이 나타났다."], ["He also wrote a pamphlet, addressed to the seventeen learned societies, native and foreign, containing a repetition of the statement he had already made, and rather more than half intimating his opinion that the seventeen learned societies were so many ‘humbugs.’ Hereupon, the virtuous indignation of the seventeen learned societies being roused, several fresh pamphlets appeared; the foreign learned societies corresponded with the native learned societies; the native learned societies translated the pamphlets of the foreign learned societies into English; the foreign learned societies translated the pamphlets of the native learned societies into all sorts of languages; and thus commenced that celebrated scientific discussion so well known to all men, as the Pickwick controversy.", "국외 학회들은 국내 학회들과 서신을 주고받았고, 국내 학회들은 국외 학회들의 소책자를 영어로 번역했으며, 국외 학회들은 국내 학회들의 소책자를 온갖 언어로 번역했다. 그리하여 모든 사람에게 픽윅 논쟁이라는 이름으로 그토록 잘 알려진, 저 유명한 학술 논쟁이 비롯되었다."]], [["But this base attempt to injure Mr. Pickwick recoiled upon the head of its calumnious author.", "그러나 픽윅 씨를 해치려던 이 비열한 시도는 그 중상모략을 일삼은 장본인 자신의 머리 위로 되돌아왔다."], ["The seventeen learned societies unanimously voted the presumptuous Blotton an ignorant meddler, and forthwith set to work upon more treatises than ever.", "열일곱 학회는 만장일치로 그 주제넘은 블로턴을 무지한 참견꾼이라 의결하고, 곧장 그 어느 때보다도 더 많은 논문을 써 내는 일에 착수했다."], ["And to this day the stone remains, an illegible monument of Mr. Pickwick’s greatness, and a lasting trophy to the littleness of his enemies.", "그리하여 오늘날까지도 그 돌은, 픽윅 씨의 위대함을 보여 주는 판독할 수 없는 기념비로서, 또한 그의 적들의 옹졸함을 영원히 증언하는 전리품으로서 남아 있다."]], [["CHAPTER XII. DESCRIPTIVE OF A VERY IMPORTANT PROCEEDING ON THE PART OF MR.", "고스웰 거리에 있는 픽윅 씨의 거처는 규모는 비록 한정되어 있었으나, 더없이 깔끔하고 안락할 뿐 아니라, 그와 같은 천재성과 관찰력을 갖춘 인물이 머물기에 유난히 안성맞춤이었다."], ["PICKWICK; NO LESS AN EPOCH IN HIS LIFE, THAN IN THIS HISTORY\nMr.", "그의 거실은 이 층 앞쪽, 침실은 삼 층 앞쪽이었다."], ["Pickwick’s apartments in Goswell Street, although on a limited scale, were not only of a very neat and comfortable description, but peculiarly adapted for the residence of a man of his genius and observation. His sitting-room was the first-floor front, his bedroom the second-floor front; and thus, whether he were sitting at his desk in his parlour, or standing before the dressing-glass in his dormitory, he had an equal opportunity of contemplating human nature in all the numerous phases it exhibits, in that not more populous than popular thoroughfare.", "그리하여 그는 응접실의 책상에 앉아 있을 때나, 침실의 화장 거울 앞에 서 있을 때나, 사람이 그다지 많은 것은 아니나 그만큼 번화한 그 거리에서 인간 본성이 드러내는 수많은 국면을 똑같이 관조할 기회를 누렸다."], ["His landlady, Mrs. Bardell--the relict and sole executrix of a deceased custom-house officer--was a comely woman of bustling manners and agreeable appearance, with a natural genius for cooking, improved by study and long practice, into an exquisite talent.", "그의 하숙집 여주인 바델 부인은—작고한 세관 관리의 미망인이자 유일한 유언 집행인으로—부산스러운 거동에 보기 좋은 용모를 지닌 잘생긴 여인이었으며, 본래 타고난 요리 솜씨가 연구와 오랜 실습을 거쳐 절묘한 재능으로 가다듬어져 있었다."], ["There were no children, no servants, no fowls.", "아이도 없고, 하인도 없고, 닭도 없었다."], ["The only other inmates of the house were a large man and a small boy; the first a lodger, the second a production of Mrs. Bardell’s.", "집 안에 사는 다른 사람이라곤 덩치 큰 사내 하나와 자그마한 사내아이 하나뿐이었으니, 앞엣것은 하숙인이요, 뒤엣것은 바델 부인의 소산이었다."], ["The large man was always home precisely at ten o’clock at night, at which hour he regularly condensed himself into the limits of a dwarfish French bedstead in the back parlour; and the infantine sports and gymnastic exercises of Master Bardell were exclusively confined to the neighbouring pavements and gutters.", "덩치 큰 사내는 언제나 밤 열 시 정각에 집에 돌아와, 그 시각이면 어김없이 뒤쪽 응접실에 놓인 난쟁이만 한 프랑스식 침대의 한계 안으로 제 몸을 응축시켰고, 어린 바델 도련님의 유아적 놀이와 체조 운동은 오로지 이웃 보도와 도랑에만 국한되어 있었다."], ["Cleanliness and quiet reigned throughout the house; and in it Mr. Pickwick’s will was law.", "집 안 곳곳에 청결과 정적이 군림했고, 그 안에서 픽윅 씨의 뜻은 곧 법이었다."]], [["To any one acquainted with these points of the domestic economy of the establishment, and conversant with the admirable regulation of Mr. Pickwick’s mind, his appearance and behaviour on the morning previous to that which had been fixed upon for the journey to Eatanswill would have been most mysterious and unaccountable.", "이 집안 살림의 이런 면면을 잘 아는 사람, 그리고 픽윅 씨의 마음이 지닌 감탄스러운 절제와 규율에 정통한 사람이라면, 이턴스윌로 떠나기로 정해 둔 날의 전날 아침에 보인 그의 모습과 거동이야말로 더없이 수수께끼 같고 설명할 길 없는 것으로 비쳤으리라."], ["He paced the room to and fro with hurried steps, popped his head out of the window at intervals of about three minutes each, constantly referred to his watch, and exhibited many other manifestations of impatience very unusual with him.", "그는 황급한 걸음으로 방 안을 이리저리 거닐었고, 한 삼 분 간격으로 창밖에 머리를 쑥 내밀었으며, 끊임없이 시계를 들여다보고, 그 밖에도 평소의 그로서는 자못 드문 초조함의 징후를 여러모로 드러냈다."], ["It was evident that something of great importance was in contemplation, but what that something was, not even Mrs. Bardell had been enabled to discover.", "무언가 대단히 중대한 일이 궁리되고 있음은 분명했으나, 그 무언가가 무엇인지는 바델 부인조차 알아낼 수 없었다."]], [["‘Mrs. Bardell,’ said Mr.", "\"바델 부인.\""], ["Pickwick, at last, as that amiable female approached the termination of a prolonged dusting of the apartment.", "마침내 픽윅 씨가 입을 열었으니, 그 다정한 여인이 방 안 먼지를 오래도록 털어 내는 일이 막바지에 다다른 무렵이었다."]], [["‘Sir,’ said Mrs.", "\"네, 나리.\""], ["Bardell.", "바델 부인이 말했다."]], [["‘Your little boy is a very long time gone.’", "\"댁의 어린 아드님이 나간 지 참 오래되었군요.\""]], [["‘Why it’s a good long way to the Borough, sir,’ remonstrated Mrs.", "\"아니, 자치구까지는 꽤 먼 길이지 않습니까, 나리.\""], ["Bardell.", "바델 부인이 항변했다."]], [["‘Ah,’ said Mr.", "\"아.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick, ‘very true; so it is.’ Mr.", "\"참으로 그렇군요. 정말 그래요.\""], ["Pickwick relapsed into silence, and Mrs. Bardell resumed her dusting.", "픽윅 씨는 다시 침묵에 잠겼고, 바델 부인은 먼지 떨기를 재개했다."]], [["‘Mrs. Bardell,’ said Mr.", "\"바델 부인.\""], ["Pickwick, at the expiration of a few minutes.", "몇 분이 지나서 픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Sir,’ said Mrs.", "\"네, 나리.\""], ["Bardell again.", "바델 부인이 다시 말했다."]], [["‘Do you think it a much greater expense to keep two people, than to keep one?’", "\"두 사람을 건사하는 것이 한 사람을 건사하는 것보다 비용이 훨씬 많이 든다고 생각하시오?\""]], [["‘La, Mr. Pickwick,’ said Mrs.", "\"어머나, 픽윅 씨.\""], ["Bardell, colouring up to the very border of her cap, as she fancied she observed a species of matrimonial twinkle in the eyes of her lodger; ‘La, Mr.", "바델 부인은 모자 가장자리까지 발갛게 물들이며 말했으니, 하숙인의 눈에 어린 일종의 혼인을 암시하는 반짝임을 본 듯한 기분이 든 까닭이었다."], ["Pickwick, what a question!’", "\"어머나, 픽윅 씨, 별 질문을 다 하시네요!\""]], [["‘Well, but do you?’ inquired Mr.", "\"글쎄, 그래도 어떻소?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘That depends,’ said Mrs.", "\"그야 경우에 따라 다르지요.\""], ["Bardell, approaching the duster very near to Mr. Pickwick’s elbow which was planted on the table.", "바델 부인은 먼지떨이를 식탁에 괸 픽윅 씨의 팔꿈치에 바짝 가까이 가져가며 말했다."], ["‘That depends a good deal upon the person, you know, Mr.", "\"그건 사람에 따라 크게 달라지는 거잖아요, 픽윅 씨."], ["Pickwick; and whether it’s a saving and careful person, sir.’", "또 그 사람이 절약하고 알뜰한 사람이냐 아니냐에 따라서요, 나리.\""]], [["‘That’s very true,’ said Mr.", "\"참으로 옳은 말씀이오.\""], ["Pickwick, ‘but the person I have in my eye (here he looked very hard at Mrs.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Bardell) I think possesses these qualities; and has, moreover, a considerable knowledge of the world, and a great deal of sharpness, Mrs.", "\"하나 내가 점찍어 둔 사람은(여기서 그는 바델 부인을 아주 빤히 바라보았다) 그런 자질을 갖추었다고 생각하오. 게다가 세상 물정에 상당한 식견이 있고 매우 영민하지요, 바델 부인."], ["Bardell, which may be of material use to me.’", "그것이 내게 실질적으로 큰 쓸모가 있을 거요.\""]], [["‘La, Mr. Pickwick,’ said Mrs.", "\"어머나, 픽윅 씨.\""], ["Bardell, the crimson rising to her cap- border again.", "바델 부인은 또다시 모자 가장자리까지 붉은빛이 차오르며 말했다."]], [["‘I do,’ said Mr.", "\"정말이오.\""], ["Pickwick, growing energetic, as was his wont in speaking of a subject which interested him--‘I do, indeed; and to tell you the truth, Mrs.", "자기가 흥미를 느끼는 화제를 이야기할 때면 으레 그러하듯, 픽윅 씨는 점점 열기를 더해 가며 말했다. \"정말 그렇소."], ["Bardell, I have made up my mind.’", "솔직히 말씀드리면, 바델 부인, 나는 이미 마음을 굳혔소.\""]], [["‘Dear me, sir,’ exclaimed Mrs.", "\"어머, 나리.\""], ["Bardell.", "바델 부인이 탄성을 질렀다."]], [["‘You’ll think it very strange now,’ said the amiable Mr.", "\"이제 와서 참 이상하다 여기시겠지요.\""], ["Pickwick, with a good-humoured glance at his companion, ‘that I never consulted you about this matter, and never even mentioned it, till I sent your little boy out this morning--eh?’", "다정한 픽윅 씨는 짝지어 앉은 이를 흐뭇한 눈길로 바라보며 말했다. \"내가 이 일을 두고 부인과 한 번도 상의하지 않았고, 오늘 아침 댁의 어린 아드님을 심부름 보낼 때까지 입 밖에도 내지 않았으니 말이오—그렇지요?\""]], [["Mrs. Bardell could only reply by a look.", "바델 부인은 그저 눈빛으로만 대답할 수 있을 따름이었다."], ["She had long worshipped Mr. Pickwick at a distance, but here she was, all at once, raised to a pinnacle to which her wildest and most extravagant hopes had never dared to aspire.", "그녀는 오랫동안 멀찍이서 픽윅 씨를 흠모해 왔건만, 이제 별안간 그녀의 가장 터무니없고 분에 넘치는 희망조차 감히 우러러보지 못했던 절정에 들어 올려진 것이었다."], ["Mr. Pickwick was going to propose--a deliberate plan, too-- sent her little boy to the Borough, to get him out of the way--how thoughtful--how considerate!", "픽윅 씨가 청혼을 하려 하다니—그것도 신중히 짜 둔 계획으로—어린 아들을 방해되지 않게 자치구로 심부름 보내다니—어쩌면 그리도 사려 깊고—어쩌면 그리도 배려심이 깊은가!"]], [["‘Well,’ said Mr.", "\"자.\""], ["Pickwick, ‘what do you think?’", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"어찌 생각하시오?\""]], [["‘Oh, Mr. Pickwick,’ said Mrs.", "\"오, 픽윅 씨.\""], ["Bardell, trembling with agitation, ‘you’re very kind, sir.’", "바델 부인은 격정에 떨며 말했다. \"정말 친절하셔요, 나리.\""]], [["‘It’ll save you a good deal of trouble, won’t it?’ said Mr.", "\"부인의 수고를 꽤나 덜어 드리게 될 거요, 그렇지 않겠소?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Oh, I never thought anything of the trouble, sir,’ replied Mrs.", "\"오, 저는 수고 따위는 한 번도 마음에 둔 적이 없어요, 나리.\""], ["Bardell; ‘and, of course, I should take more trouble to please you then, than ever; but it is so kind of you, Mr.", "바델 부인이 대답했다. \"그리고 물론 그때부터는 나리를 기쁘게 해 드리려고 여느 때보다 더 애쓸 거예요."], ["Pickwick, to have so much consideration for my loneliness.’", "하지만 이렇게 제 외로움을 그토록 헤아려 주시다니, 정말 친절하셔요, 픽윅 씨.\""]], [["‘Ah, to be sure,’ said Mr.", "\"아, 그렇고말고.\""], ["Pickwick; ‘I never thought of that.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"그건 미처 생각지 못했구려."], ["When I am in town, you’ll always have somebody to sit with you.", "내가 시내에 있을 때면 늘 부인 곁에 함께 앉아 있을 사람이 있을 테니."], ["To be sure, so you will.’", "그렇고말고, 정말 그렇게 되겠군요.\""]], [["‘I am sure I ought to be a very happy woman,’ said Mrs.", "\"저는 정말이지 더없이 행복한 여자가 되어 마땅하겠어요.\""], ["Bardell.", "바델 부인이 말했다."]], [["‘And your little boy--’ said Mr.", "\"그리고 댁의 어린 아드님도—\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Bless his heart!’ interposed Mrs.", "\"아유, 우리 귀한 것!\""], ["Bardell, with a maternal sob.", "바델 부인이 모성 어린 흐느낌과 함께 말을 가로챘다."]], [["‘He, too, will have a companion,’ resumed Mr.", "\"그 아이에게도 동무가 생길 거요.\" 픽윅 씨가 말을 이었다."], ["Pickwick, ‘a lively one, who’ll teach him, I’ll be bound, more tricks in a week than he would ever learn in a year.’ And Mr.", "\"활기찬 동무 말이오. 장담하건대, 그 아이가 일 년 걸려도 못 배울 재주를 일주일 만에 더 많이 가르쳐 줄 거요.\""], ["Pickwick smiled placidly.", "그러고서 픽윅 씨는 잔잔하게 미소 지었다."]], [["‘Oh, you dear--’ said Mrs.", "\"오, 당신은 정말 귀한—\""], ["Bardell.", "바델 부인이 말했다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick started.", "픽윅 씨가 흠칫했다."]], [["‘Oh, you kind, good, playful dear,’ said Mrs.", "\"오, 당신은 정말 친절하고 선량하고 다정한 분이셔요.\""], ["Bardell; and without more ado, she rose from her chair, and flung her arms round Mr. Pickwick’s neck, with a cataract of tears and a chorus of sobs.", "바델 부인은 이렇게 말하고는, 더 망설일 것도 없이 의자에서 일어나 픽윅 씨의 목을 두 팔로 휘감았으니, 폭포수 같은 눈물과 흐느낌의 합창이 곁들여졌다."]], [["‘Bless my soul,’ cried the astonished Mr.", "\"이런 맙소사.\""], ["Pickwick; ‘Mrs.", "깜짝 놀란 픽윅 씨가 외쳤다."], ["Bardell, my good woman--dear me, what a situation--pray consider.--Mrs. Bardell, don’t--if anybody should come--’", "\"바델 부인, 이 선량한 여인이여—어이쿠, 이게 무슨 꼴이오—제발 좀 생각해 보시오.—바델 부인, 이러지 마시오—혹시 누가 들어오기라도 하면—\""]], [["‘Oh, let them come,’ exclaimed Mrs.", "\"오, 누구든 들어오라죠.\""], ["Bardell frantically; ‘I’ll never leave you--dear, kind, good soul;’ and, with these words, Mrs.", "바델 부인이 정신없이 외쳤다. \"저는 절대 당신을 떠나지 않을 거예요—다정하고, 친절하고, 선량한 분이여.\""], ["Bardell clung the tighter.", "이렇게 말하며 바델 부인은 더욱 단단히 매달렸다."]], [["‘Mercy upon me,’ said Mr.", "\"이거 참 큰일이군.\""], ["Pickwick, struggling violently, ‘I hear somebody coming up the stairs.", "픽윅 씨가 격렬히 몸부림치며 말했다."], ["Don’t, don’t, there’s a good creature, don’t.’ But entreaty and remonstrance were alike unavailing; for Mrs. Bardell had fainted in Mr.", "\"누가 계단을 올라오는 소리가 들리오."], ["Pickwick’s arms; and before he could gain time to deposit her on a chair, Master Bardell entered the room, ushering in Mr. Tupman, Mr.", "이러지, 이러지 마시오, 착한 사람아, 제발 이러지 마시오.\""], ["Winkle, and Mr. Snodgrass.", "그러나 애원이고 항변이고 죄다 소용없었으니, 바델 부인이 픽윅 씨의 품에서 까무러쳐 버린 데다, 그가 그녀를 의자에 내려놓을 짬을 얻기도 전에 바델 도련님이 방으로 들어서며 터프먼 씨, 윈클 씨, 스노드그래스 씨를 안내해 들였기 때문이다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick was struck motionless and speechless.", "픽윅 씨는 옴짝달싹 못 하고 말문이 막힌 채 우두커니 서 있었다."], ["He stood with his lovely burden in his arms, gazing vacantly on the countenances of his friends, without the slightest attempt at recognition or explanation.", "그는 사랑스러운 짐을 두 팔에 안은 채, 알아보거나 해명하려는 시도라곤 손톱만큼도 하지 못하고, 멍하니 친구들의 얼굴만 바라보았다."], ["They, in their turn, stared at him; and Master Bardell, in his turn, stared at everybody.", "그들 또한 그를 빤히 쳐다보았고, 바델 도련님은 또 그 나름으로 사람마다 빤히 쳐다보았다."]], [["The astonishment of the Pickwickians was so absorbing, and the perplexity of Mr. Pickwick was so extreme, that they might have remained in exactly the same relative situations until the suspended animation of the lady was restored, had it not been for a most beautiful and touching expression of filial affection on the part of her youthful son.", "픽윅 회원들의 경악은 그토록 넋을 앗아갔고, 픽윅 씨의 당혹은 그토록 극심했던지라, 그 여인의 정지된 의식이 되살아날 때까지 그들은 꼭 그대로의 관계 속에 머물러 있었을지도 모른다."], ["Clad in a tight suit of corduroy, spangled with brass buttons of a very considerable size, he at first stood at the door astounded and uncertain; but by degrees, the impression that his mother must have suffered some personal damage pervaded his partially developed mind, and considering Mr.", "그녀의 어린 아들 편에서 더없이 아름답고 가슴 뭉클한 효심의 표현이 없었던들 말이다. 무척이나 큼지막한 놋단추가 박힌 꽉 끼는 코듀로이 옷을 입은 그 아이는 처음엔 어쩔 줄 모른 채 어리둥절하여 문가에 서 있었다."], ["Pickwick as the aggressor, he set up an appalling and semi-earthly kind of howling, and butting forward with his head, commenced assailing that immortal gentleman about the back and legs, with such blows and pinches as the strength of his arm, and the violence of his excitement, allowed.", "그러나 점차, 어머니가 무슨 신체적 위해를 입은 게 틀림없다는 인상이 채 여물지 않은 그의 머릿속에 퍼져 나갔고, 픽윅 씨를 가해자로 단정한 그는 소름 끼치고 거의 이승의 것 같지 않은 울부짖음을 터뜨리더니, 머리를 앞세워 들이받으며, 그 불멸의 신사의 등과 다리 언저리를 제 팔 힘과 격앙된 흥분이 허락하는 만큼의 주먹질과 꼬집기로 공격하기 시작했다."]], [["‘Take this little villain away,’ said the agonised Mr.", "\"이 작은 악당을 데려가시오.\" 고통에 찬 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick, ‘he’s mad.’", "\"이놈이 미쳤소.\""]], [["‘What is the matter?’ said the three tongue-tied Pickwickians.", "\"대체 무슨 일입니까?\" 혀가 굳어 버린 세 픽윅 회원이 말했다."]], [["‘I don’t know,’ replied Mr.", "\"나도 모르오.\""], ["Pickwick pettishly.", "픽윅 씨가 짜증스레 대답했다."], ["‘Take away the boy.’ (Here Mr.", "\"저 아이나 좀 데려가시오.\""], ["Winkle carried the interesting boy, screaming and struggling, to the farther end of the apartment.) ‘Now help me, lead this woman downstairs.’", "(여기서 윈클 씨가 그 흥미로운 아이를, 비명을 지르고 발버둥 치는 채로 방 저쪽 끝으로 데려갔다.) \"자, 이제 나를 좀 도와, 이 부인을 아래층으로 데려다주시오.\""]], [["‘Oh, I am better now,’ said Mrs.", "\"오, 이제 좀 나아졌어요.\""], ["Bardell faintly.", "바델 부인이 가냘프게 말했다."]], [["‘Let me lead you downstairs,’ said the ever-gallant Mr.", "\"제가 아래층까지 모셔다 드리지요.\""], ["Tupman.", "언제나 정중한 터프먼 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Thank you, sir--thank you;’ exclaimed Mrs.", "\"고마워요, 나리—고마워요.\""], ["Bardell hysterically.", "바델 부인이 히스테리에 차 외쳤다."], ["And downstairs she was led accordingly, accompanied by her affectionate son.", "그리하여 그녀는 그 말마따나 아래층으로 인도되었으니, 애정 깊은 아들이 함께했다."]], [["‘I cannot conceive,’ said Mr.", "\"도무지 짐작이 안 가오.\""], ["Pickwick when his friend returned--‘I cannot conceive what has been the matter with that woman.", "친구가 돌아오자 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"저 여인에게 대체 무슨 일이 있었는지 도무지 짐작이 안 가오."], ["I had merely announced to her my intention of keeping a man-servant, when she fell into the extraordinary paroxysm in which you found her.", "나는 그저 남자 하인을 하나 둘 작정이라고 알렸을 뿐인데, 여러분이 본 그 별난 발작을 일으키지 뭐요."], ["Very extraordinary thing.’", "참으로 별난 일이구려.\""]], [["‘Very,’ said his three friends.", "\"참으로요.\" 세 친구가 말했다."]], [["‘Placed me in such an extremely awkward situation,’ continued Mr.", "\"나를 더없이 거북한 처지에 빠뜨려 놓았소.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말을 이었다."]], [["‘Very,’ was the reply of his followers, as they coughed slightly, and looked dubiously at each other.", "\"참으로요.\" 추종자들은 이렇게 대답하며 가볍게 헛기침을 하고는 미심쩍은 눈길로 서로를 바라보았다."]], [["This behaviour was not lost upon Mr.", "이런 태도를 픽윅 씨가 놓칠 리 없었다."], ["Pickwick. He remarked their incredulity.", "그는 그들이 믿지 않음을 알아챘다."], ["They evidently suspected him.", "그들은 분명 그를 의심하고 있었다."]], [["‘There is a man in the passage now,’ said Mr.", "\"지금 복도에 웬 사내가 와 있소.\""], ["Tupman.", "터프먼 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘It’s the man I spoke to you about,’ said Mr.", "\"내가 여러분께 말한 그 사람이오.\""], ["Pickwick; ‘I sent for him to the Borough this morning.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"오늘 아침 자치구로 사람을 보내 불러왔지요."], ["Have the goodness to call him up, Snodgrass.’", "미안하지만 그를 올라오라 불러 주시오, 스노드그래스.\""]], [["Mr. Snodgrass did as he was desired; and Mr. Samuel Weller forthwith presented himself.", "스노드그래스 씨가 시키는 대로 하자, 곧장 새뮤얼 웰러 씨가 모습을 드러냈다."]], [["‘Oh--you remember me, I suppose?’ said Mr.", "\"오—나를 기억하겠지요?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘I should think so,’ replied Sam, with a patronising wink.", "\"그럼요.\" 샘이 짐짓 너그러운 듯 한쪽 눈을 찡긋하며 대답했다."], ["‘Queer start that ‘ere, but he was one too many for you, warn’t he?", "\"거 참 묘한 사건이었습죠. 헌데 그놈이 나리한테 한 수 위였잖습니까, 안 그래요?"], ["Up to snuff and a pinch or two over--eh?’", "어찌나 약빠른지, 거기다 한두 술 더 떴더란 말입죠—그렇잖아요?\""]], [["‘Never mind that matter now,’ said Mr.", "\"그 일은 이제 신경 쓰지 마시게.\""], ["Pickwick hastily; ‘I want to speak to you about something else.", "픽윅 씨가 황급히 말했다. \"다른 일을 두고 자네와 얘기를 좀 하고 싶네."], ["Sit down.’", "앉게나.\""]], [["‘Thank’ee, sir,’ said Sam.", "\"고맙습니다요, 나리.\""], ["And down he sat without further bidding, having previously deposited his old white hat on the landing outside the door.", "샘이 말했다. 그러고는 더 권할 것도 없이 자리에 앉았으니, 그에 앞서 낡은 흰 모자를 문밖 층계참에 내려놓았다."], ["‘’Tain’t a wery good ‘un to look at,’ said Sam, ‘but it’s an astonishin’ ‘un to wear; and afore the brim went, it was a wery handsome tile.", "\"보기엔 그리 좋은 물건이 아닙죠.\" 샘이 말했다."], ["Hows’ever it’s lighter without it, that’s one thing, and every hole lets in some air, that’s another--wentilation gossamer I calls it.’ On the delivery of this sentiment, Mr.", "\"하지만 쓰기엔 기막힌 물건이라, 챙이 나가기 전엔 아주 근사한 모자였습죠. 어쨌든 없으니 머리가 가볍다는 게 하나 좋은 점이고, 구멍마다 바람이 좀 들어온다는 게 또 하나 좋은 점입죠—전 그걸 환기 거미줄이라 부른답니다.\""], ["Weller smiled agreeably upon the assembled Pickwickians.", "이런 소회를 털어놓고서 웰러 씨는 모여 있는 픽윅 회원들을 향해 싹싹하게 미소 지었다."]], [["‘Now with regard to the matter on which I, with the concurrence of these gentlemen, sent for you,’ said Mr.", "\"자, 내가 이 신사분들의 동의를 얻어 자네를 부른 그 용건에 관해서인데.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘That’s the pint, sir,’ interposed Sam; ‘out vith it, as the father said to his child, when he swallowed a farden.’", "\"그게 핵심입죠, 나리.\" 샘이 끼어들었다. \"어서 털어놓으십쇼. 동전 한 닢 삼킨 자식한테 아비가 한 말처럼요.\""]], [["‘We want to know, in the first place,’ said Mr.", "\"우선 첫째로 우리가 알고 싶은 것은.\""], ["Pickwick, ‘whether you have any reason to be discontented with your present situation.’", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"자네가 지금의 처지에 불만을 가질 만한 까닭이 있느냐 하는 것일세.\""]], [["‘Afore I answers that ‘ere question, gen’l’m’n,’ replied Mr.", "\"그 질문에 답하기 전에, 신사 여러분.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다."], ["Weller, ‘I should like to know, in the first place, whether you’re a-goin’ to purwide me with a better?’", "\"저로서는 우선 첫째로 알고 싶은 게 있는데요, 여러분이 저한테 더 나은 자리를 마련해 주실 작정이냐 하는 겁니다요.\""]], [["A sunbeam of placid benevolence played on Mr.", "잔잔한 자애의 햇살이 픽윅 씨의 얼굴에 어리며 그가 말했다."], ["Pickwick’s features as he said, ‘I have half made up my mind to engage you myself.’", "\"내가 직접 자네를 고용하기로 거의 마음을 굳혔네.\""]], [["‘Have you, though?’ said Sam.", "\"정말입니까요?\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick nodded in the affirmative.", "픽윅 씨가 그렇다는 뜻으로 고개를 끄덕였다."]], [["‘Wages?’ inquired Sam.", "\"품삯은요?\" 샘이 물었다."]], [["‘Twelve pounds a year,’ replied Mr.", "\"일 년에 십이 파운드일세.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘Clothes?’", "\"옷은요?\""]], [["‘Two suits.’", "\"두 벌.\""]], [["‘Work?’", "\"일은요?\""]], [["‘To attend upon me; and travel about with me and these gentlemen here.’", "\"나를 시중들고, 나와 여기 이 신사분들과 함께 여기저기 다니는 거지.\""]], [["‘Take the bill down,’ said Sam emphatically.", "\"광고지를 내리쇼.\" 샘이 힘주어 말했다."], ["‘I’m let to a single gentleman, and the terms is agreed upon.’", "\"저는 독신 신사분께 임대되었고, 조건은 합의됐습니다요.\""]], [["‘You accept the situation?’ inquired Mr.", "\"그 자리를 받아들이겠나?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘Cert’nly,’ replied Sam.", "\"여부가 있겠습니까요.\""], ["‘If the clothes fits me half as well as the place, they’ll do.’", "샘이 대답했다. \"옷이 자리의 반만큼이라도 제게 맞아떨어진다면야, 그걸로 충분합죠.\""]], [["‘You can get a character of course?’ said Mr.", "\"물론 신원 보증서는 받을 수 있겠지?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Ask the landlady o’ the White Hart about that, Sir,’ replied Sam.", "\"그건 화이트 하트의 여주인한테 물어보십쇼, 나리.\" 샘이 대답했다."]], [["‘Can you come this evening?’", "\"오늘 저녁에 올 수 있겠나?\""]], [["‘I’ll get into the clothes this minute, if they’re here,’ said Sam, with great alacrity.", "\"옷만 여기 있다면야 당장 이 자리에서 갈아입겠습니다요.\" 샘이 더없이 기민하게 말했다."]], [["‘Call at eight this evening,’ said Mr.", "\"오늘 저녁 여덟 시에 들르게.\""], ["Pickwick; ‘and if the inquiries are satisfactory, they shall be provided.’", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"조회 결과가 만족스러우면 옷을 마련해 주겠네.\""]], [["With the single exception of one amiable indiscretion, in which an assistant housemaid had equally participated, the history of Mr. Weller’s conduct was so very blameless, that Mr.", "보조 가정부도 똑같이 가담했던 어느 다정한 경솔함 하나만을 유일한 예외로 친다면, 웰러 씨의 행실 이력은 그토록 흠잡을 데 없었던지라, 픽윅 씨는 바로 그날 저녁에 고용을 마무리 짓는 것이 충분히 정당하다고 느꼈다."], ["Pickwick felt fully justified in closing the engagement that very evening. With the promptness and energy which characterised not only the public proceedings, but all the private actions of this extraordinary man, he at once led his new attendant to one of those convenient emporiums where gentlemen’s new and second-hand clothes are provided, and the troublesome and inconvenient formality of measurement dispensed with; and before night had closed in, Mr.", "이 비범한 인물의 공적인 처사뿐 아니라 사적인 행동 일체를 특징짓는 그 신속함과 정력으로, 그는 곧장 새 시종을 데리고, 신사용 새 옷과 헌 옷을 갖추어 놓고 치수 재기라는 성가시고 번거로운 격식을 생략해 주는 그런 편리한 상점 가운데 하나로 향했다."], ["Weller was furnished with a grey coat with the P. C.", "그리하여 밤이 채 깃들기도 전에, 웰러 씨는 P."], ["button, a black hat with a cockade to it, a pink striped waistcoat, light breeches and gaiters, and a variety of other necessaries, too numerous to recapitulate.", "C. 단추가 달린 회색 외투, 모표를 단 검은 모자, 분홍 줄무늬 조끼, 밝은색 반바지와 각반, 그 밖에 일일이 늘어놓기엔 너무 많은 갖가지 필수품을 갖추게 되었다."]], [["‘Well,’ said that suddenly-transformed individual, as he took his seat on the outside of the Eatanswill coach next morning; ‘I wonder whether I’m meant to be a footman, or a groom, or a gamekeeper, or a seedsman. I looks like a sort of compo of every one on ‘em. Never mind; there’s a change of air, plenty to see, and little to do; and all this suits my complaint uncommon; so long life to the Pickvicks, says I!’", "\"자아.\" 별안간 탈바꿈한 그 인물은 이튿날 아침 이턴스윌행 마차 바깥자리에 올라앉으며 말했다. \"내가 종복이 되라는 건지, 마부가 되라는 건지, 사냥터지기가 되라는 건지, 씨앗 장수가 되라는 건지 모르겠군. 꼭 그 모든 걸 한데 버무린 잡탕 같은 꼴이야. 뭐 상관없지. 공기도 바뀌고, 볼 것도 많고, 할 일은 적고. 이 모든 게 내 형편에 더없이 잘 맞아떨어지거든. 그러니 픽윅 양반들, 만수무강하시라, 내 말이 그거야!\""]], [["CHAPTER XIII.", "제13장."], ["SOME ACCOUNT OF EATANSWILL; OF THE STATE OF PARTIES THEREIN; AND OF THE ELECTION OF A MEMBER TO SERVE IN PARLIAMENT FOR THAT ANCIENT, LOYAL, AND PATRIOTIC BOROUGH", "이턴스윌에 관한 약간의 기록, 그곳 정당들의 상태, 그리고 그 유서 깊고 충성스럽고 애국적인 자치구를 위해 의회에 봉직할 의원을 뽑는 선거에 관하여"]], [["We will frankly acknowledge that, up to the period of our being first immersed in the voluminous papers of the Pickwick Club, we had never heard of Eatanswill; we will with equal candour admit that we have in vain searched for proof of the actual existence of such a place at the present day.", "우리는 픽윅 클럽의 방대한 문서에 처음 빠져들기 전까지는 이턴스윌이라는 곳을 들어 본 적이 없었음을 솔직히 인정한다. 또한 오늘날 그런 곳이 실제로 존재한다는 증거를 헛되이 찾았음도 같은 솔직함으로 시인한다."], ["Knowing the deep reliance to be placed on every note and statement of Mr. Pickwick’s, and not presuming to set up our recollection against the recorded declarations of that great man, we have consulted every authority, bearing upon the subject, to which we could possibly refer.", "픽윅 씨의 모든 기록과 진술에 깊은 신뢰를 두어야 함을 알기에, 또 그 위대한 인물이 기록으로 남긴 선언에 우리의 기억을 감히 맞세우려 하지 않기에, 우리는 참조할 수 있는 한 이 주제에 관계된 모든 권위 있는 자료를 두루 살폈다."], ["We have traced every name in schedules A and B, without meeting with that of Eatanswill; we have minutely examined every corner of the pocket county maps issued for the benefit of society by our distinguished publishers, and the same result has attended our investigation.", "우리는 명부 갑과 을에 실린 모든 지명을 추적했으나 이턴스윌이라는 이름은 만나지 못했고, 우리의 저명한 출판인들이 사회의 이익을 위해 펴낸 휴대용 군 지도의 구석구석을 면밀히 살폈으나 똑같은 결과만이 우리의 조사를 따라다녔다."], ["We are therefore led to believe that Mr. Pickwick, with that anxious desire to abstain from giving offence to any, and with those delicate feelings for which all who knew him well know he was so eminently remarkable, purposely substituted a fictitious designation, for the real name of the place in which his observations were made.", "그러므로 우리는 픽윅 씨가, 그 누구에게도 불쾌감을 주지 않으려는 그 애타는 바람과, 그를 잘 아는 모든 이가 그가 유난히 두드러지게 지녔음을 아는 그 섬세한 감정으로, 자신이 관찰을 행한 장소의 실제 이름 대신 일부러 가공의 명칭을 갖다 붙였다고 믿게 되었다."], ["We are confirmed in this belief by a little circumstance, apparently slight and trivial in itself, but when considered in this point of view, not undeserving of notice. In Mr.", "우리는 그 자체로는 사소하고 하찮아 보이지만 이런 관점에서 고찰하면 주목할 가치가 없지 않은 작은 정황으로 인해 이 믿음을 더욱 굳히게 되었다."], ["Pickwick’s note-book, we can just trace an entry of the fact, that the places of himself and followers were booked by the Norwich coach; but this entry was afterwards lined through, as if for the purpose of concealing even the direction in which the borough is situated.", "픽윅 씨의 수첩에서 우리는 그와 그의 추종자들의 자리가 노리치 마차에 예약되었다는 사실의 기재를 겨우 알아볼 수 있는데, 이 기재는 나중에 줄이 그어져 지워졌으니, 마치 그 자치구가 위치한 방향마저 감추려는 목적에서인 듯하다."], ["We will not, therefore, hazard a guess upon the subject, but will at once proceed with this history, content with the materials which its characters have provided for us.", "따라서 우리는 이 주제에 대해 함부로 추측을 시도하지 않고, 그 등장인물들이 우리에게 마련해 준 자료에 만족하며 곧바로 이 역사를 이어 가겠다."]], [["It appears, then, that the Eatanswill people, like the people of many other small towns, considered themselves of the utmost and most mighty importance, and that every man in Eatanswill, conscious of the weight that attached to his example, felt himself bound to unite, heart and soul, with one of the two great parties that divided the town--the Blues and the Buffs.", "그러니까 이턴스윌 사람들은, 다른 많은 작은 읍의 사람들과 마찬가지로, 자신들을 더없이 막중하고 막강한 존재로 여겼던 모양이고, 이턴스윌의 모든 사람은 자기 본보기에 실린 무게를 의식하여 읍을 갈라놓은 두 거대 정당 가운데 하나, 즉 블루파 아니면 버프파에 마음과 영혼을 다해 합쳐야 한다고 느꼈던 모양이다."], ["Now the Blues lost no opportunity of opposing the Buffs, and the Buffs lost no opportunity of opposing the Blues; and the consequence was, that whenever the Buffs and Blues met together at public meeting, town-hall, fair, or market, disputes and high words arose between them.", "그런데 블루파는 버프파에 반대할 기회를 결코 놓치지 않았고, 버프파는 블루파에 반대할 기회를 결코 놓치지 않았다. 그 결과, 버프파와 블루파가 공개 집회, 읍사무소, 장터, 또는 시장에서 함께 마주칠 때면 언제나 그들 사이에 다툼과 격한 말이 일어났다."], ["With these dissensions it is almost superfluous to say that everything in Eatanswill was made a party question.", "이런 불화로 인해 이턴스윌에서는 모든 일이 당파 문제로 변했다는 것은 거의 말할 필요도 없다."], ["If the Buffs proposed to new skylight the market-place, the Blues got up public meetings, and denounced the proceeding; if the Blues proposed the erection of an additional pump in the High Street, the Buffs rose as one man and stood aghast at the enormity.", "버프파가 시장에 새 채광창을 달자고 제안하면 블루파는 공개 집회를 열어 그 처사를 규탄했고, 블루파가 큰길에 펌프를 하나 더 세우자고 제안하면 버프파는 한 사람처럼 들고일어나 그 엄청난 일에 아연실색했다."], ["There were Blue shops and Buff shops, Blue inns and Buff inns--there was a Blue aisle and a Buff aisle in the very church itself.", "블루파 가게와 버프파 가게가 있었고, 블루파 여관과 버프파 여관이 있었으며, 바로 그 교회 안에도 블루파 통로와 버프파 통로가 있었다."]], [["Of course it was essentially and indispensably necessary that each of these powerful parties should have its chosen organ and representative: and, accordingly, there were two newspapers in the town--the Eatanswill Gazette and the Eatanswill Independent; the former advocating Blue principles, and the latter conducted on grounds decidedly Buff. Fine newspapers they were. Such leading articles, and such spirited attacks!- -’Our worthless contemporary, the Gazette’--‘That disgraceful and dastardly journal, the Independent’--‘That false and scurrilous print, the Independent’--‘That vile and slanderous calumniator, the Gazette;’ these, and other spirit-stirring denunciations, were strewn plentifully over the columns of each, in every number, and excited feelings of the most intense delight and indignation in the bosoms of the townspeople.", "물론 이 강력한 두 정당이 저마다 선택한 기관지와 대변자를 두는 것은 본질적이고 불가결하게 필요한 일이었다. 그리하여 읍에는 두 신문이 있었으니, 이턴스윌 가제트와 이턴스윌 인디펜던트였다. 전자는 블루파 원칙을 옹호했고, 후자는 단연 버프파의 입장에서 운영되었다. 훌륭한 신문들이었다. 그 무슨 사설들이며, 그 무슨 패기 넘치는 공격들이던가! \"우리의 가치 없는 동시대 신문 가제트,\" \"저 수치스럽고 비겁한 신문 인디펜던트,\" \"저 거짓되고 상스러운 인쇄물 인디펜던트,\" \"저 비열하고 중상모략을 일삼는 비방꾼 가제트.\" 이런 것들과 그 밖의 정신을 들끓게 하는 규탄들이 매 호마다 양쪽 지면 곳곳에 흠뻑 흩뿌려졌고, 읍 사람들의 가슴속에 더없이 강렬한 기쁨과 분노의 감정을 불러일으켰다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick, with his usual foresight and sagacity, had chosen a peculiarly desirable moment for his visit to the borough.", "픽윅 씨는 평소의 선견지명과 명민함으로 이 자치구를 방문하기에 유난히 바람직한 시점을 골랐다."], ["Never was such a contest known.", "일찍이 그런 경합은 없었다."], ["The Honourable Samuel Slumkey, of Slumkey Hall, was the Blue candidate; and Horatio Fizkin, Esq., of Fizkin Lodge, near Eatanswill, had been prevailed upon by his friends to stand forward on the Buff interest.", "슬럼키 홀의 새뮤얼 슬럼키 각하가 블루파 후보였고, 이턴스윌 인근 피즈킨 로지의 호레이쇼 피즈킨 씨가 친구들의 권유에 못 이겨 버프파의 이익을 위해 나섰다."], ["The Gazette warned the electors of Eatanswill that the eyes not only of England, but of the whole civilised world, were upon them; and the Independent imperatively demanded to know, whether the constituency of Eatanswill were the grand fellows they had always taken them for, or base and servile tools, undeserving alike of the name of Englishmen and the blessings of freedom.", "가제트는 이턴스윌의 유권자들에게 영국뿐 아니라 온 문명 세계의 눈이 그들에게 쏠려 있다고 일깨웠고, 인디펜던트는 이턴스윌의 유권자들이 줄곧 여겨 온 대로 훌륭한 인물들인지, 아니면 영국인이라는 이름과 자유의 축복 양쪽 모두에 어울리지 않는 비천하고 비굴한 도구들인지 단호히 알기를 요구했다."], ["Never had such a commotion agitated the town before.", "일찍이 그런 소동이 읍을 뒤흔든 적은 없었다."]], [["It was late in the evening when Mr. Pickwick and his companions, assisted by Sam, dismounted from the roof of the Eatanswill coach.", "픽윅 씨와 그의 일행이 샘의 도움을 받아 이턴스윌 마차 지붕에서 내린 것은 저녁 늦은 시각이었다."], ["Large blue silk flags were flying from the windows of the Town Arms Inn, and bills were posted in every sash, intimating, in gigantic letters, that the Honourable Samuel Slumkey’s committee sat there daily.", "큰 푸른 비단 깃발들이 타운 암스 여관의 창문들에서 나부끼고 있었고, 모든 창틀에는 새뮤얼 슬럼키 각하의 위원회가 그곳에서 날마다 회의를 연다는 것을 거대한 글자로 알리는 벽보가 붙어 있었다."], ["A crowd of idlers were assembled in the road, looking at a hoarse man in the balcony, who was apparently talking himself very red in the face in Mr.", "한 무리의 한가한 사람들이 길에 모여, 발코니에 선 목쉰 사내를 쳐다보고 있었는데, 그 사내는 슬럼키 씨를 위하여 얼굴이 새빨개지도록 떠들어 대는 듯했다."], ["Slumkey’s behalf; but the force and point of whose arguments were somewhat impaired by the perpetual beating of four large drums which Mr. Fizkin’s committee had stationed at the street corner.", "그러나 그 논변의 힘과 요지는 피즈킨 씨의 위원회가 길모퉁이에 배치해 둔 큰 북 네 개를 끊임없이 두드려 대는 통에 다소 손상되었다."], ["There was a busy little man beside him, though, who took off his hat at intervals and motioned to the people to cheer, which they regularly did, most enthusiastically; and as the red-faced gentleman went on talking till he was redder in the face than ever, it seemed to answer his purpose quite as well as if anybody had heard him.", "하지만 그 옆에는 바삐 움직이는 작은 사내가 있어, 이따금 모자를 벗고 사람들에게 환호하라고 손짓을 했는데, 사람들은 어김없이 더없이 열렬하게 그렇게 했다. 그래서 그 얼굴 붉은 신사가 전보다 더 새빨개지도록 계속 떠들어 대니, 그것은 누가 그의 말을 듣기라도 한 것 못지않게 그의 목적에 잘 부합하는 듯했다."]], [["The Pickwickians had no sooner dismounted than they were surrounded by a branch mob of the honest and independent, who forthwith set up three deafening cheers, which being responded to by the main body (for it’s not at all necessary for a crowd to know what they are cheering about), swelled into a tremendous roar of triumph, which stopped even the red- faced man in the balcony.", "픽윅 회원들이 마차에서 내리기가 무섭게 그 정직하고 독립적인 사람들의 한 분파가 그들을 에워싸더니 곧바로 귀가 멍멍해지는 세 차례의 환호를 올렸고, 이에 본대가 화답하자(군중이 무엇에 환호하는지 알 필요는 전혀 없으므로) 그것은 어마어마한 승리의 함성으로 부풀어, 발코니의 그 얼굴 붉은 사내마저 멈춰 세웠다."]], [["‘Hurrah!’ shouted the mob, in conclusion.", "\"만세!\" 군중이 끝맺으며 외쳤다."]], [["‘One cheer more,’ screamed the little fugleman in the balcony, and out shouted the mob again, as if lungs were cast-iron, with steel works.", "\"한 번 더 환호!\" 발코니의 작은 선창자가 비명을 질렀고, 군중은 폐가 무쇠에 강철 부품이라도 박힌 듯이 다시 함성을 질렀다."]], [["‘Slumkey for ever!’ roared the honest and independent.", "\"슬럼키 만세!\" 그 정직하고 독립적인 사람들이 외쳐 댔다."]], [["‘Slumkey for ever!’ echoed Mr.", "\"슬럼키 만세!\""], ["Pickwick, taking off his hat.", "픽윅 씨가 모자를 벗으며 메아리쳤다."]], [["‘No Fizkin!’ roared the crowd.", "\"피즈킨은 안 돼!\" 군중이 외쳐 댔다."]], [["‘Certainly not!’ shouted Mr.", "\"물론 안 되고말고!\" 픽윅 씨가 외쳤다."], ["Pickwick.", "\"만세!\""], ["‘Hurrah!’ And then there was another roaring, like that of a whole menagerie when the elephant has rung the bell for the cold meat.", "그러자 마치 코끼리가 식은 고기를 달라고 종을 울렸을 때의 동물원 전체 같은 또 한 번의 함성이 일었다."]], [["‘Who is Slumkey?’ whispered Mr.", "\"슬럼키가 누구죠?\""], ["Tupman.", "텁먼 씨가 속삭였다."]], [["‘I don’t know,’ replied Mr.", "\"나도 모르오.\""], ["Pickwick, in the same tone.", "픽윅 씨도 같은 어조로 대답했다."], ["‘Hush.", "\"쉿."], ["Don’t ask any questions.", "아무것도 묻지 마시오."], ["It’s always best on these occasions to do what the mob do.’", "이런 자리에서는 군중이 하는 대로 하는 게 언제나 상책이오.\""]], [["‘But suppose there are two mobs?’ suggested Mr.", "\"하지만 군중이 둘이면 어쩌죠?\""], ["Snodgrass.", "스노드그래스 씨가 의견을 냈다."]], [["‘Shout with the largest,’ replied Mr.", "\"가장 큰 쪽과 함께 외치면 되오.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 대답했다."]], [["Volumes could not have said more.", "여러 권의 책으로도 이보다 더 많은 말을 할 수는 없었으리라."]], [["They entered the house, the crowd opening right and left to let them pass, and cheering vociferously.", "그들은 여관 안으로 들어갔고, 군중은 좌우로 갈라져 그들이 지나가도록 길을 내주며 떠들썩하게 환호했다."], ["The first object of consideration was to secure quarters for the night.", "가장 먼저 고려할 일은 하룻밤 묵을 숙소를 확보하는 것이었다."]], [["‘Can we have beds here?’ inquired Mr.", "\"여기 침대를 얻을 수 있겠소?\""], ["Pickwick, summoning the waiter.", "픽윅 씨가 종업원을 부르며 물었다."]], [["‘Don’t know, Sir,’ replied the man; ‘afraid we’re full, sir--I’ll inquire, Sir.’ Away he went for that purpose, and presently returned, to ask whether the gentleman were ‘Blue.’", "\"모르겠습니다, 선생님.\" 사내가 대답했다. \"다 찼을까 봐 걱정입니다, 선생님. 알아보겠습니다, 선생님.\" 그는 그러려고 가더니 이내 돌아와, 그 신사분들이 \"블루파\"인지 물었다."]], [["As neither Mr. Pickwick nor his companions took any vital interest in the cause of either candidate, the question was rather a difficult one to answer. In this dilemma Mr. Pickwick bethought himself of his new friend, Mr. Perker.", "픽윅 씨도 그의 일행도 어느 후보의 대의에든 절실한 관심이 없었기에, 그것은 답하기가 다소 어려운 질문이었다. 이 곤경에서 픽윅 씨는 새로 사귄 친구 퍼커 씨를 떠올렸다."]], [["‘Do you know a gentleman of the name of Perker?’ inquired Mr.", "\"퍼커라는 이름의 신사를 아오?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘Certainly, Sir; Honourable Mr.", "\"물론입죠, 선생님."], ["Samuel Slumkey’s agent.’", "새뮤얼 슬럼키 각하의 대리인이십니다.\""]], [["‘He is Blue, I think?’\n‘Oh, yes, Sir.’", "\"그분은 블루파라고 생각하는데?\""]], [["‘Then we are Blue,’ said Mr.", "\"아, 그렇습니다, 선생님.\" \"그렇다면 우리도 블루파요.\""], ["Pickwick; but observing that the man looked rather doubtful at this accommodating announcement, he gave him his card, and desired him to present it to Mr.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. 그러나 사내가 이 선뜻한 선언에 다소 미심쩍어하는 눈치를 보이자, 그는 자기 명함을 건네며, 혹시 퍼커 씨가 여관에 계시면 곧바로 전해 달라고 일렀다."], ["Perker forthwith, if he should happen to be in the house. The waiter retired; and reappearing almost immediately with a request that Mr.", "종업원은 물러갔다가 거의 곧바로 다시 나타나, 픽윅 씨가 자기를 따라와 주십사 청하더니, 앞장서서 이층의 큰 방으로 안내했다."], ["Pickwick would follow him, led the way to a large room on the first floor, where, seated at a long table covered with books and papers, was Mr. Perker.", "그곳에는 책과 서류로 뒤덮인 긴 탁자 앞에 퍼커 씨가 앉아 있었다."]], [["‘Ah--ah, my dear Sir,’ said the little man, advancing to meet him; ‘very happy to see you, my dear Sir, very.", "\"아, 아, 친애하는 선생,\" 작은 사내가 그를 맞으러 다가오며 말했다."], ["Pray sit down.", "\"만나 뵙게 되어 매우 기쁩니다, 친애하는 선생, 매우요."], ["So you have carried your intention into effect.", "자, 앉으십시오. 그래, 뜻하신 바를 실행에 옮기셨군요."], ["You have come down here to see an election-- eh?’", "선거를 보려고 이리로 내려오신 거지요, 그렇죠?\""]], [["Mr. Pickwick replied in the affirmative.", "픽윅 씨는 그렇다고 대답했다."]], [["‘Spirited contest, my dear sir,’ said the little man.", "\"기운찬 경합입니다, 친애하는 선생.\" 작은 사내가 말했다."]], [["‘I’m delighted to hear it,’ said Mr.", "\"그 말을 들으니 기쁘군요.\""], ["Pickwick, rubbing his hands.", "픽윅 씨가 두 손을 비비며 말했다."], ["‘I like to see sturdy patriotism, on whatever side it is called forth--and so it’s a spirited contest?’", "\"나는 어느 편에서 발휘되든 굳건한 애국심을 보는 것이 좋소. 그래, 기운찬 경합이란 말이지요?\""]], [["‘Oh, yes,’ said the little man, ‘very much so indeed. We have opened all the public-houses in the place, and left our adversary nothing but the beer-shops--masterly stroke of policy that, my dear Sir, eh?’ The little man smiled complacently, and took a large pinch of snuff.", "\"아, 그렇고말고요.\" 작은 사내가 말했다. \"정말 무척이나요. 우리는 이곳의 모든 술집을 다 차지하고, 우리 적수에게는 맥줏집만 남겨 주었지요. 그게 절묘한 정략의 한 수였습니다, 친애하는 선생, 그렇죠?\" 작은 사내는 흡족하게 미소 지으며 코담배를 한 줌 크게 들이켰다."]], [["‘And what are the probabilities as to the result of the contest?’ inquired Mr.", "\"그래 경합의 결과에 대한 전망은 어떻소?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘Why, doubtful, my dear Sir; rather doubtful as yet,’ replied the little man.", "\"글쎄요, 불확실합니다, 친애하는 선생. 아직은 다소 불확실해요.\""], ["‘Fizkin’s people have got three-and-thirty voters in the lock-up coach-house at the White Hart.’", "작은 사내가 대답했다. \"피즈킨 쪽 사람들이 화이트 하트의 잠긴 마차 차고에 표 서른셋을 잡아 두고 있거든요.\""]], [["‘In the coach-house!’ said Mr.", "\"마차 차고에라니!\""], ["Pickwick, considerably astonished by this second stroke of policy.", "픽윅 씨가 이 두 번째 정략의 한 수에 적잖이 놀라 말했다."]], [["‘They keep ‘em locked up there till they want ‘em,’ resumed the little man.", "\"필요할 때까지 거기에 가둬 두는 거지요.\" 작은 사내가 말을 이었다."], ["‘The effect of that is, you see, to prevent our getting at them; and even if we could, it would be of no use, for they keep them very drunk on purpose.", "\"그 효과는, 아시겠지만, 우리가 그들에게 접근하지 못하게 하는 것입니다. 설령 접근할 수 있다 해도 소용이 없을 게, 일부러 잔뜩 취하게 해 두니까요."], ["Smart fellow Fizkin’s agent--very smart fellow indeed.’", "피즈킨의 대리인은 영리한 친구입니다. 정말이지 아주 영리한 친구예요.\""]], [["Mr. Pickwick stared, but said nothing.", "픽윅 씨는 눈을 휘둥그렇게 떴으나 아무 말도 하지 않았다."]], [["‘We are pretty confident, though,’ said Mr.", "\"그래도 우리는 꽤 자신이 있습니다.\""], ["Perker, sinking his voice almost to a whisper.", "퍼커 씨가 목소리를 거의 속삭임으로 낮추며 말했다. \"어젯밤 여기서 작은 다과회를 열었지요."], ["‘We had a little tea-party here, last night--five- and-forty women, my dear sir--and gave every one of ‘em a green parasol when she went away.’", "부인이 마흔다섯 분이었습니다, 친애하는 선생. 그리고 한 분 한 분 돌아갈 때마다 초록색 양산을 하나씩 드렸지요.\""]], [["‘A parasol!’ said Mr.", "\"양산을!\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Fact, my dear Sir, fact.", "\"사실입니다, 친애하는 선생, 사실이에요."], ["Five-and-forty green parasols, at seven and sixpence a-piece.", "초록 양산 마흔다섯 개를, 하나에 칠 실링 육 펜스씩 주고요."], ["All women like finery--extraordinary the effect of those parasols.", "여자들은 다들 고운 것을 좋아하지요. 그 양산들의 효과가 어찌나 대단하던지."], ["Secured all their husbands, and half their brothers-- beats stockings, and flannel, and all that sort of thing hollow.", "그 남편들을 죄다 우리 편으로 끌어들였고, 그 형제들의 절반도 잡았습니다. 양말이니 플란넬이니 그런 따위는 댈 것도 못 됩니다."], ["My idea, my dear Sir, entirely.", "온전히 제 생각이었지요, 친애하는 선생."], ["Hail, rain, or sunshine, you can’t walk half a dozen yards up the street, without encountering half a dozen green parasols.’", "우박이 오나, 비가 오나, 햇볕이 나나, 길을 대여섯 야드도 못 걸어서 초록 양산 대여섯 개를 마주치게 되거든요.\""]], [["Here the little man indulged in a convulsion of mirth, which was only checked by the entrance of a third party.", "여기서 작은 사내는 웃음의 발작에 빠져들었는데, 그것은 제삼자가 들어오면서야 비로소 멎었다."]], [["This was a tall, thin man, with a sandy-coloured head inclined to baldness, and a face in which solemn importance was blended with a look of unfathomable profundity. He was dressed in a long brown surtout, with a black cloth waistcoat, and drab trousers.", "이는 키가 크고 마른 사내였으니, 머리는 모래빛에 벗어질 듯 성기었고, 얼굴에는 엄숙한 위엄과 헤아릴 수 없는 심오함의 기색이 뒤섞여 있었다."], ["A double eyeglass dangled at his waistcoat; and on his head he wore a very low-crowned hat with a broad brim.", "그는 검은 천 조끼와 황갈색 바지 차림에 긴 갈색 프록코트를 입고 있었다."], ["The new-comer was introduced to Mr. Pickwick as Mr.", "조끼에는 겹안경이 달랑거렸고, 머리에는 챙이 넓고 관이 아주 낮은 모자를 쓰고 있었다."], ["Pott, the editor of the Eatanswill Gazette. After a few preliminary remarks, Mr.", "이 새로 온 사람은 픽윅 씨에게 이턴스윌 가제트의 편집인 포트 씨로 소개되었다."], ["Pott turned round to Mr. Pickwick, and said with solemnity--", "몇 마디 의례적인 인사를 나눈 뒤, 포트 씨는 픽윅 씨를 돌아보며 엄숙하게 말했다."]], [["‘This contest excites great interest in the metropolis, sir?’", "\"이번 경합이 수도에서 큰 관심을 불러일으키고 있지요, 선생?\""]], [["‘I believe it does,’ said Mr.", "\"그런 것 같습니다.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘To which I have reason to know,’ said Pott, looking towards Mr.", "\"내가 알 만한 이유가 있는 바로는,\" 포트가 확인을 구하듯 퍼커 씨 쪽을 바라보며 말했다."], ["Perker for corroboration--‘to which I have reason to know that my article of last Saturday in some degree contributed.’", "\"내가 알 만한 이유가 있는 바로는, 지난 토요일 내 기사가 거기에 어느 정도 이바지했지요.\""]], [["‘Not the least doubt of it,’ said the little man.", "\"조금도 의심할 여지가 없습니다.\" 작은 사내가 말했다."]], [["‘The press is a mighty engine, sir,’ said Pott.", "\"언론은 막강한 동력 기관입니다, 선생.\" 포트가 말했다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick yielded his fullest assent to the proposition.", "픽윅 씨는 그 명제에 전적인 동의를 표했다."]], [["‘But I trust, sir,’ said Pott, ‘that I have never abused the enormous power I wield. I trust, sir, that I have never pointed the noble instrument which is placed in my hands, against the sacred bosom of private life, or the tender breast of individual reputation; I trust, sir, that I have devoted my energies to--to endeavours--humble they may be, humble I know they are--to instil those principles of--which--are--’", "\"하지만 나는, 선생,\" 포트가 말했다. \"내가 휘두르는 그 막대한 힘을 결코 남용한 적이 없다고 믿소. 나는, 선생, 내 손에 쥐여진 그 고귀한 도구를 사생활의 신성한 가슴이나 개인의 명예라는 여린 가슴을 겨누어 들이댄 적이 없다고 믿소. 나는, 선생, 내 정력을—그—노력에—비록 보잘것없을지라도, 보잘것없음을 나도 알지만—그—원칙을—그—주입하려는 노력에 바쳐 왔다고 믿소—\""]], [["Here the editor of the Eatanswill Gazette, appearing to ramble, Mr. Pickwick came to his relief, and said--", "여기서 이턴스윌 가제트의 편집인이 두서를 잃은 듯 보이자, 픽윅 씨가 그를 구해 주려 말했다."]], [["‘Certainly.’", "\"물론이지요.\""]], [["‘And what, Sir,’ said Pott--‘what, Sir, let me ask you as an impartial man, is the state of the public mind in London, with reference to my contest with the Independent?’", "\"그래, 선생,\" 포트가 말했다. \"그래, 선생, 공정한 사람으로서 한 가지 여쭙겠는데, 인디펜던트와의 내 경합에 관하여 런던 여론의 상태가 어떻소?\""]], [["‘Greatly excited, no doubt,’ interposed Mr.", "\"틀림없이 크게 들끓고 있겠지요.\""], ["Perker, with a look of slyness which was very likely accidental.", "퍼커 씨가 끼어들었는데, 그 교활한 눈빛은 십중팔구 우연이었으리라."]], [["‘The contest,’ said Pott, ‘shall be prolonged so long as I have health and strength, and that portion of talent with which I am gifted.", "\"그 경합은,\" 포트가 말했다."], ["From that contest, Sir, although it may unsettle men’s minds and excite their feelings, and render them incapable for the discharge of the everyday duties of ordinary life; from that contest, sir, I will never shrink, till I have set my heel upon the Eatanswill Independent.", "\"나에게 건강과 기력이 있고 내가 타고난 그 얼마간의 재능이 있는 한 길이 이어질 것이오. 그 경합으로 사람들의 마음이 흔들리고 감정이 들끓어 평범한 일상의 일을 해낼 수 없게 될지라도, 그 경합에서, 선생, 나는 이턴스윌 인디펜던트를 발뒤꿈치로 짓밟아 버릴 때까지 결코 물러서지 않을 것이오."], ["I wish the people of London, and the people of this country to know, sir, that they may rely upon me--that I will not desert them, that I am resolved to stand by them, Sir, to the last.’", "나는 런던 사람들과 이 나라 사람들이 알아주기를 바라오, 선생, 그들이 나를 믿어도 된다는 것을—내가 그들을 저버리지 않으리라는 것을, 내가 끝까지 그들 곁을 지키기로 결심했다는 것을, 선생.\""]], [["Your conduct is most noble, Sir,’ said Mr.", "\"선생의 처신은 더없이 고결합니다, 선생.\""], ["Pickwick; and he grasped the hand of the magnanimous Pott.", "픽윅 씨가 말하며, 도량 넓은 포트의 손을 꽉 잡았다."]], [["‘You are, sir, I perceive, a man of sense and talent,’ said Mr.", "\"선생은, 보아하니, 분별과 재능을 지닌 분이오.\""], ["Pott, almost breathless with the vehemence of his patriotic declaration.", "포트 씨가 자신의 애국적 선언의 격정에 거의 숨이 차서 말했다."], ["‘I am most happy, sir, to make the acquaintance of such a man.’", "\"그런 분과 사귀게 되어 더없이 기쁩니다, 선생.\""]], [["‘And I,’ said Mr.", "\"그리고 저는,\""], ["Pickwick, ‘feel deeply honoured by this expression of your opinion.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"선생의 그 의견 표명에 깊이 영광을 느낍니다."], ["Allow me, sir, to introduce you to my fellow-travellers, the other corresponding members of the club I am proud to have founded.’", "선생, 제 길동무들이자, 제가 자랑스럽게 창립한 클럽의 다른 통신 회원들을 소개해 드리도록 허락해 주십시오.\""]], [["‘I shall be delighted,’ said Mr.", "\"기꺼이 그러지요.\""], ["Pott.", "포트 씨가 말했다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick withdrew, and returning with his friends, presented them in due form to the editor of the Eatanswill Gazette.", "픽윅 씨는 물러갔다가 친구들과 함께 돌아와, 격식에 맞게 그들을 이턴스윌 가제트의 편집인에게 소개했다."]], [["‘Now, my dear Pott,’ said little Mr.", "\"자, 친애하는 포트,\" 작은 퍼커 씨가 말했다."], ["Perker, ‘the question is, what are we to do with our friends here?’", "\"문제는 여기 우리 친구분들을 어찌해 드리느냐 하는 거요.\""]], [["‘We can stop in this house, I suppose,’ said Mr.", "\"우리는 이 여관에 묵을 수 있겠지요?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Not a spare bed in the house, my dear sir--not a single bed.’", "\"이 집에는 남는 침대가 하나도 없어요, 친애하는 선생—침대 한 개도요.\""]], [["‘Extremely awkward,’ said Mr.", "\"몹시 난처하군요.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Very,’ said his fellow-voyagers.", "\"무척이나요.\" 그의 길동무들이 말했다."]], [["‘I have an idea upon this subject,’ said Mr.", "\"이 일에 대해 한 가지 생각이 있는데,\""], ["Pott, ‘which I think may be very successfully adopted.", "포트 씨가 말했다."], ["They have two beds at the Peacock, and I can boldly say, on behalf of Mrs.", "\"아주 성공적으로 채택될 수 있을 것 같소."], ["Pott, that she will be delighted to accommodate Mr. Pickwick and any one of his friends, if the other two gentlemen and their servant do not object to shifting, as they best can, at the Peacock.’", "피콕에 침대가 둘 있으니, 나는 포트 부인을 대신하여 자신 있게 말할 수 있소, 다른 두 신사분과 그 하인이 피콕에서 형편껏 임시변통하는 데 반대만 안 하신다면, 부인은 픽윅 씨와 그 친구분 한 분을 기꺼이 모실 거라고 말이오.\""]], [["After repeated pressings on the part of Mr. Pott, and repeated protestations on that of Mr. Pickwick that he could not think of incommoding or troubling his amiable wife, it was decided that it was the only feasible arrangement that could be made. So it was made; and after dinner together at the Town Arms, the friends separated, Mr. Tupman and Mr. Snodgrass repairing to the Peacock, and Mr. Pickwick and Mr. Winkle proceeding to the mansion of Mr. Pott; it having been previously arranged that they should all reassemble at the Town Arms in the morning, and accompany the Honourable Samuel Slumkey’s procession to the place of nomination.", "포트 씨가 거듭 권하고 픽윅 씨가 그 상냥한 부인에게 불편이나 폐를 끼칠 수는 없다고 거듭 손사래를 친 끝에, 그것이 이루어질 수 있는 유일한 실행 가능한 방안이라고 결론이 났다. 그리하여 그렇게 정해졌다. 타운 암스에서 함께 저녁을 든 뒤 친구들은 헤어졌으니, 텁먼 씨와 스노드그래스 씨는 피콕으로 향하고, 픽윅 씨와 윈클 씨는 포트 씨의 저택으로 갔다. 그들 모두 아침에 타운 암스에서 다시 모여, 새뮤얼 슬럼키 각하의 행렬을 따라 후보 지명 장소로 가기로 미리 정해 둔 터였다."]], [["Mr. Pott’s domestic circle was limited to himself and his wife.", "포트 씨의 가정 식구는 그 자신과 아내로 한정되어 있었다."], ["All men whom mighty genius has raised to a proud eminence in the world, have usually some little weakness which appears the more conspicuous from the contrast it presents to their general character. If Mr.", "막강한 천재성 덕분에 세상에서 자랑스러운 높은 자리에 오른 모든 사람은 대개 어떤 작은 약점을 지니고 있는데, 그 약점은 그들의 전반적인 성격과 이루는 대조로 인해 한층 더 두드러지게 마련이다."], ["Pott had a weakness, it was, perhaps, that he was rather too submissive to the somewhat contemptuous control and sway of his wife.", "만약 포트 씨에게 약점이 있었다면, 그것은 아마도 아내의 다소 경멸 어린 통제와 지배에 너무 고분고분하다는 점이었으리라."], ["We do not feel justified in laying any particular stress upon the fact, because on the present occasion all Mrs. Pott’s most winning ways were brought into requisition to receive the two gentlemen.", "그러나 우리는 이 사실을 특별히 강조하는 것이 정당하다고 느끼지 않으니, 이날만큼은 포트 부인의 가장 사랑스러운 매력이 모조리 동원되어 두 신사를 맞이했기 때문이다."]], [["‘My dear,’ said Mr.", "\"여보,\""], ["Pott, ‘Mr. Pickwick--Mr.", "포트 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick of London.’", "\"픽윅 씨요—런던의 픽윅 씨.\""]], [["Mrs. Pott received Mr.", "포트 부인은 픽윅 씨가 아버지처럼 손을 꽉 잡는 것을 매혹적인 다정함으로 받아들였다."], ["Pickwick’s paternal grasp of the hand with enchanting sweetness; and Mr. Winkle, who had not been announced at all, sidled and bowed, unnoticed, in an obscure corner.", "그리고 통 소개받지 못한 윈클 씨는 눈에 띄지 않게 으슥한 구석에서 비실비실 다가가 인사를 했다."]], [["‘P.", "\"여보 피,\""], ["my dear’--said Mrs. Pott.", "포트 부인이 말했다."]], [["‘My life,’ said Mr.", "\"왜 그러오, 내 사랑.\""], ["Pott.", "포트 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Pray introduce the other gentleman.’", "\"저 다른 신사분을 좀 소개해 주세요.\""]], [["‘I beg a thousand pardons,’ said Mr.", "\"천만번 용서를 비오.\""], ["Pott. ‘Permit me, Mrs.", "포트 씨가 말했다."], ["Pott, Mr.--’", "\"포트 부인, 이분은—\""]], [["‘Winkle,’ said Mr.", "\"윈클입니다.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Winkle,’ echoed Mr.", "\"윈클.\""], ["Pott; and the ceremony of introduction was complete.", "포트 씨가 되받았고, 이로써 소개의 의식이 완결되었다."]], [["‘We owe you many apologies, ma’am,’ said Mr.", "\"이렇게 갑작스레 댁의 가정 살림을 어지럽혀 드려, 여러모로 송구합니다, 부인.\""], ["Pickwick, ‘for disturbing your domestic arrangements at so short a notice.’", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘I beg you won’t mention it, sir,’ replied the feminine Pott, with vivacity.", "\"그런 말씀 마세요, 선생.\" 여성다운 포트 부인이 발랄하게 대답했다."], ["‘It is a high treat to me, I assure you, to see any new faces; living as I do, from day to day, and week to week, in this dull place, and seeing nobody.’", "\"새로운 얼굴을 뵙는 것은 제게는 더없는 즐거움이랍니다, 정말로요. 저는 이 따분한 곳에서 날마다, 주마다, 아무도 만나지 못한 채 살고 있거든요.\""]], [["‘Nobody, my dear!’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"아무도라니, 여보!\""], ["Pott archly.", "포트 씨가 짐짓 능청을 떨며 외쳤다."]], [["‘Nobody but you,’ retorted Mrs.", "\"당신 말고는 아무도요.\""], ["Pott, with asperity.", "포트 부인이 쌀쌀맞게 받아쳤다."]], [["‘You see, Mr.", "\"보십시오, 픽윅 씨,\""], ["Pickwick,’ said the host in explanation of his wife’s lament, ‘that we are in some measure cut off from many enjoyments and pleasures of which we might otherwise partake.", "주인이 아내의 한탄을 해명하듯 말했다. \"우리는 어느 정도 여러 즐거움과 기쁨에서 동떨어져 있습니다."], ["My public station, as editor of the Eatanswill Gazette, the position which that paper holds in the country, my constant immersion in the vortex of politics--’", "그것들을 누릴 수도 있을 텐데 말이지요. 이턴스윌 가제트 편집인으로서의 제 공적 지위, 그 신문이 이 나라에서 차지하는 위치, 정치의 소용돌이에 끊임없이 몰두하는 일이—\""]], [["‘P.", "\"여보 피—\""], ["my dear--’ interposed Mrs. Pott.", "포트 부인이 끼어들었다."]], [["‘My life--’ said the editor.", "\"내 사랑—\" 편집인이 말했다."]], [["‘I wish, my dear, you would endeavour to find some topic of conversation in which these gentlemen might take some rational interest.’", "\"여보, 부디 이분들이 좀 합리적으로 관심을 가질 만한 화제를 좀 찾아보려고 애써 주세요.\""]], [["‘But, my love,’ said Mr.", "\"하지만, 내 사랑,\""], ["Pott, with great humility, ‘Mr.", "포트 씨가 자못 겸손하게 말했다."], ["Pickwick does take an interest in it.’", "\"픽윅 씨는 거기에 관심을 가지신다오.\""]], [["‘It’s well for him if he can,’ said Mrs.", "\"그분이야 그럴 수 있다면 다행이죠.\""], ["Pott emphatically; ‘I am wearied out of my life with your politics, and quarrels with the Independent, and nonsense.", "포트 부인이 힘주어 말했다. \"저는 당신의 정치니, 인디펜던트와의 다툼이니, 그 허튼소리에 진절머리가 나서 죽겠어요."], ["I am quite astonished, P., at your making such an exhibition of your absurdity.’", "피, 당신이 그렇게 자기 어리석음을 떠벌리시다니 정말 어이가 없네요.\""]], [["‘But, my dear--’ said Mr.", "\"하지만, 여보—\""], ["Pott.", "포트 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Oh, nonsense, don’t talk to me,’ said Mrs.", "\"아, 허튼소리예요, 제게 말 거시지 마세요.\" 포트 부인이 말했다."], ["Pott. ‘Do you play ecarte, Sir?’", "\"에카르테 하실 줄 아세요, 선생?\""]], [["‘I shall be very happy to learn under your tuition,’ replied Mr.", "\"부인의 가르침을 받아 배운다면 더없이 기쁘겠습니다.\""], ["Winkle.", "윈클 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘Well, then, draw that little table into this window, and let me get out of hearing of those prosy politics.’", "\"그럼, 저 작은 탁자를 이 창가로 끌어다 놓으세요. 그 따분한 정치 얘기가 안 들리는 데로 좀 갑시다.\""]], [["‘Jane,’ said Mr.", "\"제인,\""], ["Pott, to the servant who brought in candles, ‘go down into the office, and bring me up the file of the Gazette for eighteen hundred and twenty-six.", "포트 씨가 촛불을 들고 들어온 하녀에게 말했다. \"사무실로 내려가서 천팔백이십육 년도 가제트 합본을 가져오너라."], ["I’ll read you,’ added the editor, turning to Mr.", "읽어 드리지요.\" 편집인이 픽윅 씨를 돌아보며 덧붙였다."], ["Pickwick--‘I’ll just read you a few of the leaders I wrote at that time upon the Buff job of appointing a new tollman to the turnpike here; I rather think they’ll amuse you.’", "\"그때 여기 통행료 징수소에 새 통행료 징수원을 임명한 버프파의 농간에 관해 내가 쓴 사설을 몇 편 읽어 드리리다. 아마 흥미로워하실 겁니다.\""]], [["‘I should like to hear them very much indeed,’ said Mr.", "\"정말이지 꼭 듣고 싶습니다.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["Up came the file, and down sat the editor, with Mr. Pickwick at his side.", "합본이 올라왔고, 편집인은 픽윅 씨를 곁에 두고 자리에 앉았다."]], [["We have in vain pored over the leaves of Mr. Pickwick’s note-book, in the hope of meeting with a general summary of these beautiful compositions.", "우리는 이 아름다운 글들의 전반적인 요약을 만나 보려는 바람으로 픽윅 씨의 수첩 갈피를 헛되이 뒤졌다."], ["We have every reason to believe that he was perfectly enraptured with the vigour and freshness of the style; indeed Mr.", "우리는 그가 그 문체의 활력과 신선함에 완전히 황홀해했으리라고 믿을 만한 온갖 근거를 가지고 있다."], ["Winkle has recorded the fact that his eyes were closed, as if with excess of pleasure, during the whole time of their perusal.", "사실 윈클 씨는 그것들을 낭독하는 내내 픽윅 씨의 눈이, 마치 지나친 기쁨에 겨운 듯, 감겨 있었다는 사실을 기록해 두었다."]], [["The announcement of supper put a stop both to the game of ecarte, and the recapitulation of the beauties of the Eatanswill Gazette. Mrs.", "밤참이 차려졌다는 알림에 에카르테 놀이도, 이턴스윌 가제트의 아름다움에 대한 요약 낭독도 모두 멎었다."], ["Pott was in the highest spirits and the most agreeable humour.", "포트 부인은 더없이 들떠 더없이 유쾌한 기분이었다."], ["Mr. Winkle had already made considerable progress in her good opinion, and she did not hesitate to inform him, confidentially, that Mr.", "윈클 씨는 이미 그녀의 호감을 상당히 얻어 둔 터라, 그녀는 픽윅 씨가 \"유쾌한 노신사 양반\"이라고 그에게 은밀히 일러 주기를 주저하지 않았다."], ["Pickwick was ‘a delightful old dear.’ These terms convey a familiarity of expression, in which few of those who were intimately acquainted with that colossal-minded man, would have presumed to indulge.", "이런 표현은 친밀한 표현법을 담고 있으니, 그 거대한 정신의 소유자를 가까이 알던 이들 가운데 감히 그런 말을 함부로 써 본 사람은 거의 없었을 것이다."], ["We have preserved them, nevertheless, as affording at once a touching and a convincing proof of the estimation in which he was held by every class of society, and the ease with which he made his way to their hearts and feelings.", "그럼에도 우리는 그것을 그대로 보존해 두었으니, 그가 사회의 모든 계층으로부터 받던 존경과, 그가 그들의 마음과 감정 속으로 파고드는 그 수월함을 한꺼번에 보여 주는 감동적이고도 설득력 있는 증거가 되기 때문이다."]], [["It was a late hour of the night--long after Mr. Tupman and Mr.", "밤이 늦은 시각—텁먼 씨와 스노드그래스 씨가 피콕의 가장 깊숙한 구석에서 잠든 지 한참 뒤—에야 두 친구는 잠자리에 들었다."], ["Snodgrass had fallen asleep in the inmost recesses of the Peacock--when the two friends retired to rest. Slumber soon fell upon the senses of Mr.", "윈클 씨의 감각에는 이내 잠이 내려앉았으나, 그의 감정은 들떠 있었고 그의 흠모는 깨어나 있었다."], ["Winkle, but his feelings had been excited, and his admiration roused; and for many hours after sleep had rendered him insensible to earthly objects, the face and figure of the agreeable Mrs. Pott presented themselves again and again to his wandering imagination.", "그리하여 잠이 그를 세상사에 무감각하게 만든 뒤로도 여러 시간 동안, 그 사랑스러운 포트 부인의 얼굴과 모습이 그의 떠도는 상상 속에 거듭거듭 나타났다."]], [["The noise and bustle which ushered in the morning were sufficient to dispel from the mind of the most romantic visionary in existence, any associations but those which were immediately connected with the rapidly-approaching election.", "아침을 알리는 소음과 부산함은, 세상에 둘도 없는 가장 낭만적인 몽상가의 마음에서라도, 빠르게 다가오는 선거와 직접 관련된 것 말고는 어떤 연상도 떨쳐 버리기에 충분했다."], ["The beating of drums, the blowing of horns and trumpets, the shouting of men, and tramping of horses, echoed and re-echoed through the streets from the earliest dawn of day; and an occasional fight between the light skirmishers of either party at once enlivened the preparations, and agreeably diversified their character.", "북 두드리는 소리, 뿔피리와 나팔 부는 소리, 사람들의 외침, 말발굽 소리가 동틀 무렵부터 거리에 메아리치고 또 메아리쳤다. 그리고 양 당파의 경무장 척후병들 사이에서 이따금 벌어지는 싸움질은 그 준비를 활기차게 하는 동시에 그 성격을 흥겹게 다채롭게 했다."]], [["‘Well, Sam,’ said Mr.", "\"그래, 샘,\""], ["Pickwick, as his valet appeared at his bedroom door, just as he was concluding his toilet; ‘all alive to-day, I suppose?’", "픽윅 씨가 막 몸단장을 끝마칠 무렵 침실 문에 시종이 나타나자 말했다. \"오늘은 다들 활기가 넘치겠지?\""]], [["‘Reg’lar game, sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"아주 신납니다, 선생님.\""], ["Weller; ‘our people’s a-collecting down at the Town Arms, and they’re a-hollering themselves hoarse already.’", "웰러 씨가 대답했다. \"우리 편 사람들이 타운 암스 아래에 모여서, 벌써 목이 쉬도록 고함을 질러 대고 있습죠.\""]], [["‘Ah,’ said Mr.", "\"아,\""], ["Pickwick, ‘do they seem devoted to their party, Sam?’", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"다들 자기 당파에 헌신하는 것 같더냐, 샘?\""]], [["‘Never see such dewotion in my life, Sir.’", "\"제 평생 그런 헌신은 본 적이 없습죠, 선생님.\""]], [["‘Energetic, eh?’ said Mr.", "\"기운들이 넘치고, 응?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Uncommon,’ replied Sam; ‘I never see men eat and drink so much afore.", "\"보통이 아닙죠.\" 샘이 대답했다."], ["I wonder they ain’t afeer’d o’ bustin’.’", "\"사람들이 그렇게 먹고 마셔 대는 건 여태 못 봤습니다. 배가 터지는 게 안 무서운가 봐요.\""]], [["‘That’s the mistaken kindness of the gentry here,’ said Mr.", "\"그건 이곳 신사 양반들의 빗나간 친절이지.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Wery likely,’ replied Sam briefly.", "\"그럴 법도 합죠.\" 샘이 짧게 대답했다."]], [["‘Fine, fresh, hearty fellows they seem,’ said Mr.", "\"다들 멀쩡하고 싱싱하고 기운찬 친구들 같구나.\""], ["Pickwick, glancing from the window.", "픽윅 씨가 창밖을 흘끗 보며 말했다."]], [["‘Wery fresh,’ replied Sam; ‘me and the two waiters at the Peacock has been a-pumpin’ over the independent woters as supped there last night.’", "\"아주 싱싱합죠.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"저랑 피콕 종업원 둘이서, 어젯밤 거기서 밤참 먹은 독립 유권자들한테 펌프질을 해 댔거든요.\""]], [["‘Pumping over independent voters!’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"독립 유권자들한테 펌프질을 하다니!\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 외쳤다."]], [["‘Yes,’ said his attendant, ‘every man slept vere he fell down; we dragged ‘em out, one by one, this mornin’, and put ‘em under the pump, and they’re in reg’lar fine order now. Shillin’ a head the committee paid for that ‘ere job.’", "\"네.\" 그의 시종이 말했다. \"다들 쓰러진 자리에서 그대로 잤습죠. 오늘 아침에 하나씩 하나씩 끌어내서 펌프 밑에 갖다 놓았더니, 이제 다들 멀쩡하게 좋은 꼴이 됐습니다. 그 일 하라고 위원회가 한 사람당 일 실링씩 줬습죠.\""]], [["‘Can such things be!’ exclaimed the astonished Mr.", "\"그런 일이 다 있을 수 있다니!\""], ["Pickwick.", "깜짝 놀란 픽윅 씨가 외쳤다."]], [["‘Lord bless your heart, sir,’ said Sam, ‘why where was you half baptised?--that’s nothin’, that ain’t.’", "\"맙소사, 선생님,\" 샘이 말했다. \"도대체 어디서 반쪽짜리 세례라도 받으셨나요? 그건 아무것도 아닙니다요, 그건요.\""]], [["‘Nothing?’said Mr.", "\"아무것도 아니라고?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Nothin’ at all, Sir,’ replied his attendant.", "\"전혀 아무것도 아닙죠, 선생님.\""], ["‘The night afore the last day o’ the last election here, the opposite party bribed the barmaid at the Town Arms, to hocus the brandy-and-water of fourteen unpolled electors as was a-stoppin’ in the house.’", "그의 시종이 대답했다. \"여기서 지난번 선거 마지막 날 전날 밤에는, 반대편 당파가 타운 암스 술집 아가씨를 매수해서, 그 집에 묵고 있던 투표 안 한 유권자 열넷의 브랜디 워터에 약을 탔습죠.\""]], [["‘What do you mean by “hocussing”", "\"브랜디 워터에 '약을 탔다'는 게 무슨 뜻이냐?\""], ["brandy-and-water?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘Puttin’ laud’num in it,’ replied Sam.", "\"아편 팅크를 탄 거죠.\" 샘이 대답했다."], ["‘Blessed if she didn’t send ‘em all to sleep till twelve hours arter the election was over.", "\"글쎄 그 아가씨가 선거 끝나고 열두 시간 뒤까지 그자들을 죄다 곯아떨어지게 만들었지 뭡니까. 시험 삼아 한 사람을 짐수레에 실어서 곤히 잠든 채로 투표소까지 데려갔는데, 소용이 없었어요."], ["They took one man up to the booth, in a truck, fast asleep, by way of experiment, but it was no go--they wouldn’t poll him; so they brought him back, and put him to bed again.’", "투표를 안 받아 주더랍니다. 그래서 도로 데려다가 다시 침대에 눕혔습죠.\""]], [["Strange practices, these,’ said Mr.", "\"참 별난 짓들이로구나.\""], ["Pickwick; half speaking to himself and half addressing Sam.", "픽윅 씨가 절반은 혼잣말로, 절반은 샘에게 말하듯 했다."]], [["‘Not half so strange as a miraculous circumstance as happened to my own father, at an election time, in this wery place, Sir,’ replied Sam.", "\"바로 이곳에서, 선거 때, 제 친아버지한테 일어난 기적 같은 사건에 비하면 절반도 별난 게 아닙죠, 선생님.\" 샘이 대답했다."]], [["‘What was that?’ inquired Mr.", "\"그게 무슨 일이었느냐?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘Why, he drove a coach down here once,’ said Sam; ‘’lection time came on, and he was engaged by vun party to bring down woters from London.", "\"그게요, 아버지가 한번은 이리로 마차를 몰고 왔거든요.\" 샘이 말했다."], ["Night afore he was going to drive up, committee on t’ other side sends for him quietly, and away he goes vith the messenger, who shows him in;- -large room--lots of gen’l’m’n--heaps of papers, pens and ink, and all that ‘ere.", "\"선거철이 닥쳤는데, 한쪽 당파가 아버지를 고용해서 런던에서 유권자들을 실어 오게 했습죠. 마차를 몰고 올라가기로 한 전날 밤, 반대편 위원회가 아버지를 슬그머니 부르더니, 아버지가 심부름꾼을 따라가니까 그자가 안으로 들여보냈답니다."], ["“Ah, Mr. Weller,”", "큰 방에, 신사 양반들이 잔뜩, 서류며 펜이며 잉크며 그런 것들이 산더미였죠."], ["says the gen’l’m’n in the chair, “glad to see you, sir; how are you?”--“Wery well, thank ‘ee, Sir,”", "'아, 웰러 씨,' 의장석에 앉은 신사가 말합니다, '만나서 반갑소, 선생, 어떻게 지내시오?'—'아주 잘 지냅니다, 고맙습니다, 선생.'"], ["says my father; “I hope you’re pretty middlin,”", "아버지가 말했죠. '나리도 그럭저럭 잘 지내시길 바랍니다.'"], ["says he.--“Pretty well, thank’ee, Sir,”", "했답니다.—'그럭저럭 잘 지내오, 고맙소, 선생,'"], ["says the gen’l’m’n; “sit down, Mr.", "신사가 말합니다."], ["Weller--pray sit down, sir.”", "'앉으시오, 웰러 씨—자, 앉으시오, 선생.'"], ["So my father sits down, and he and the gen’l’m’n looks wery hard at each other.", "그래서 아버지가 앉으니까, 아버지하고 그 신사하고 서로 아주 빤히 쳐다봤답니다."], ["“You don’t remember me?”", "'나를 기억 못 하시오?'"], ["said the gen’l’m’n.--“Can’t say I do,”", "신사가 말합니다.—'기억난다고는 못 하겠는뎁쇼.'"], ["says my father.--“Oh, I know you,”", "아버지가 말했죠.—'아, 나는 당신을 아오,'"], ["says the gen’l’m’n: “know’d you when you was a boy,”", "신사가 말합니다. '당신이 꼬마였을 때 알았지요.'"], ["says he.--“Well, I don’t remember you,”", "하더랍니다.—'글쎄, 나는 나리가 기억 안 나는뎁쇼.'"], ["says my father.--“That’s wery odd,”", "아버지가 말했죠.—'그것참 이상하구려.'"], ["says the gen’l’m’n.”--“Wery,”", "신사가 말합니다.—'이상하죠.'"], ["says my father.--“You must have a bad mem’ry, Mr. Weller,”", "아버지가 말했죠.—'기억력이 나쁘신가 보오, 웰러 씨.'"], ["says the gen’l’m’n.--“Well, it is a wery bad ‘un,”", "신사가 말합니다.—'글쎄, 아주 나쁜 놈입죠.'"], ["says my father.--“I thought so,”", "아버지가 말했죠.—'그럴 줄 알았소.'"], ["says the gen’l’m’n.", "신사가 말합니다."], ["So then they pours him out a glass of wine, and gammons him about his driving, and gets him into a reg’lar good humour, and at last shoves a twenty-pound note into his hand.", "그러더니 아버지한테 포도주 한 잔을 따라 주고, 마차 모는 솜씨를 추켜세워 가며 아버지를 아주 기분 좋게 만들어 놓더니, 마침내 아버지 손에 이십 파운드짜리 지폐를 슬쩍 찔러 줬습죠."], ["“It’s a wery bad road between this and London,”", "'여기서 런던까지 길이 아주 험하지요.'"], ["says the gen’l’m’n.--“Here and there it is a heavy road,”", "신사가 말합니다.—'군데군데 무거운 길이긴 합죠.'"], ["says my father.--” ‘Specially near the canal, I think,”", "아버지가 말했죠.—'특히 운하 근처가 그렇겠지요.'"], ["says the gen’l’m’n.--“Nasty bit that ‘ere,”", "신사가 말합니다.—'고약한 구석이죠, 거기가요.'"], ["says my father.-- “Well, Mr. Weller,”", "아버지가 말했죠.—'그런데, 웰러 씨,'"], ["says the gen’l’m’n, “you’re a wery good whip, and can do what you like with your horses, we know.", "신사가 말합니다. '당신은 아주 솜씨 좋은 마부고, 말을 마음대로 부릴 줄 안다는 걸 우리도 알지요."], ["We’re all wery fond o’ you, Mr.", "우리는 다들 당신을 아주 좋아한다오, 웰러 씨."], ["Weller, so in case you should have an accident when you’re bringing these here woters down, and should tip ‘em over into the canal vithout hurtin’ of ‘em, this is for yourself,”", "그러니 혹시 이 유권자들을 실어 내려올 때 사고가 나서, 그 사람들을 다치게 하지 않고 운하에 처박게 되거든, 이건 당신 몫이오.'"], ["says he.--“Gen’l’m’n, you’re wery kind,”", "하더랍니다.—'나리, 참 친절하십니다,' 아버지가 말했죠."], ["says my father, “and I’ll drink your health in another glass of wine,”", "'포도주 한 잔 더 들면서 나리 건강을 위해 마시겠습니다.' 했답니다."], ["says he; vich he did, and then buttons up the money, and bows himself out.", "그래서 그렇게 하고는 돈을 단단히 챙겨 넣고 굽실거리며 나왔습죠. 그런데 믿지 못하실 겁니다, 선생님,\""], ["You wouldn’t believe, sir,’ continued Sam, with a look of inexpressible impudence at his master, ‘that on the wery day as he came down with them woters, his coach was upset on that ‘ere wery spot, and ev’ry man on ‘em was turned into the canal.’", "샘이 주인을 향해 이루 말할 수 없이 뻔뻔한 표정을 지으며 말을 이었다. \"바로 그 유권자들을 싣고 내려온 바로 그날, 아버지 마차가 바로 그 자리에서 뒤집혀서, 그 사람들이 죄다 운하에 처박혀 버렸지 뭡니까.\""]], [["‘And got out again?’ inquired Mr.", "\"그래서 다시 건져 냈느냐?\""], ["Pickwick hastily.", "픽윅 씨가 황급히 물었다."]], [["‘Why,’ replied Sam very slowly, ‘I rather think one old gen’l’m’n was missin’; I know his hat was found, but I ain’t quite certain whether his head was in it or not. But what I look at is the hex-traordinary and wonderful coincidence, that arter what that gen’l’m’n said, my father’s coach should be upset in that wery place, and on that wery day!’", "\"그게요,\" 샘이 아주 느릿느릿 대답했다. \"노신사 한 분이 안 보였던 것 같습니다. 모자는 찾았다는 걸 아는데, 그 안에 머리가 들어 있었는지 어떤지는 잘 모르겠어요. 하지만 제가 주목하는 건, 그 신사가 그런 말을 한 뒤에, 아버지 마차가 바로 그 자리에서, 바로 그날에 뒤집혔다는 그 어마어마하고 놀라운 우연의 일치란 말입죠!\""]], [["‘It is, no doubt, a very extraordinary circumstance indeed,’ said Mr.", "\"그건 의심할 바 없이 참으로 비상한 정황이로구나.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick. ‘But brush my hat, Sam, for I hear Mr.", "\"그건 그렇고 내 모자를 좀 솔질해 다오, 샘."], ["Winkle calling me to breakfast.’", "윈클 씨가 아침을 들자고 나를 부르는 소리가 들리는구나.\""]], [["With these words Mr. Pickwick descended to the parlour, where he found breakfast laid, and the family already assembled.", "이 말과 함께 픽윅 씨는 응접실로 내려갔는데, 거기에는 아침 식사가 차려져 있고 가족들은 이미 모여 있었다."], ["The meal was hastily despatched; each of the gentlemen’s hats was decorated with an enormous blue favour, made up by the fair hands of Mrs. Pott herself; and as Mr.", "식사는 서둘러 끝났다."], ["Winkle had undertaken to escort that lady to a house-top, in the immediate vicinity of the hustings, Mr.", "신사들의 모자에는 저마다 포트 부인이 손수 고운 손으로 만든 거대한 푸른 휘장이 장식되었다."], ["Pickwick and Mr. Pott repaired alone to the Town Arms, from the back window of which, one of Mr.", "그리고 윈클 씨가 그 부인을 후보 지명 연단 바로 근처의 한 지붕 위로 모시기로 맡았으므로, 픽윅 씨와 포트 씨만 함께 타운 암스로 갔다."], ["Slumkey’s committee was addressing six small boys and one girl, whom he dignified, at every second sentence, with the imposing title of ‘Men of Eatanswill,’ whereat the six small boys aforesaid cheered prodigiously.", "그곳 뒤 창문에서는 슬럼키 씨의 위원 한 명이 사내아이 여섯과 계집아이 하나를 향해 연설을 하고 있었는데, 그는 한 문장 걸러 한 번씩 그들에게 \"이턴스윌의 사나이들\"이라는 위풍당당한 칭호를 붙여 주었고, 그러면 앞서 말한 사내아이 여섯이 엄청나게 환호했다."]], [["The stable-yard exhibited unequivocal symptoms of the glory and strength of the Eatanswill Blues.", "마구간 마당은 이턴스윌 블루파의 영광과 위세를 명백히 드러내는 징표들을 펼쳐 보였다."], ["There was a regular army of blue flags, some with one handle, and some with two, exhibiting appropriate devices, in golden characters four feet high, and stout in proportion.", "손잡이가 하나인 것도 있고 둘인 것도 있는 푸른 깃발들이 정규군처럼 늘어서서, 높이 사 피트에 그에 걸맞게 굵직한 황금빛 글자로 알맞은 도안들을 드러내고 있었다."], ["There was a grand band of trumpets, bassoons, and drums, marshalled four abreast, and earning their money, if ever men did, especially the drum-beaters, who were very muscular.", "나팔과 바순과 북으로 이루어진 웅장한 악대가 네 줄로 정렬하여, 일찍이 사람이 제값을 한 적이 있다면 바로 그렇게 제 삯값을 하고 있었으니, 특히 북 치는 자들은 근육이 어찌나 우람했던지."], ["There were bodies of constables with blue staves, twenty committee-men with blue scarfs, and a mob of voters with blue cockades.", "푸른 곤봉을 든 경관 부대들, 푸른 띠를 두른 위원 스무 명, 그리고 푸른 모표를 단 유권자 무리가 있었다. 말을 탄 유권자도 있고 걸어서 온 유권자도 있었다."], ["There were electors on horseback and electors afoot.", "새뮤얼 슬럼키 각하를 위한 사두마차 한 대가 있었고, 그의 친구와 지지자들을 위한 이두마차 네 대가 있었다."], ["There was an open carriage-and-four, for the Honourable Samuel Slumkey; and there were four carriage-and-pair, for his friends and supporters; and the flags were rustling, and the band was playing, and the constables were swearing, and the twenty committee-men were squabbling, and the mob were shouting, and the horses were backing, and the post- boys perspiring; and everybody, and everything, then and there assembled, was for the special use, behoof, honour, and renown, of the Honourable Samuel Slumkey, of Slumkey Hall, one of the candidates for the representation of the borough of Eatanswill, in the Commons House of Parliament of the United Kingdom.", "깃발은 펄럭이고, 악대는 연주하고, 경관들은 욕설을 퍼붓고, 위원 스무 명은 옥신각신하고, 군중은 외쳐 대고, 말들은 뒷걸음치고, 역마차 마부들은 땀을 흘렸다. 그리고 그때 그곳에 모인 모든 사람과 모든 것이, 연합 왕국 의회 하원에서 이턴스윌 자치구의 대표직을 노리는 후보 가운데 한 사람인, 슬럼키 홀의 새뮤얼 슬럼키 각하를 위한 특별한 쓰임과 이익과 명예와 명성을 위한 것이었다."]], [["Loud and long were the cheers, and mighty was the rustling of one of the blue flags, with ‘Liberty of the Press’ inscribed thereon, when the sandy head of Mr.", "아래 군중이 한 창문에서 모래빛 머리의 포트 씨를 알아보았을 때, 환호는 우렁차고 길었으며 \"언론의 자유\"라고 적힌 푸른 깃발 하나의 펄럭임은 우람했다."], ["Pott was discerned in one of the windows, by the mob beneath; and tremendous was the enthusiasm when the Honourable Samuel Slumkey himself, in top-boots, and a blue neckerchief, advanced and seized the hand of the said Pott, and melodramatically testified by gestures to the crowd, his ineffaceable obligations to the Eatanswill Gazette.", "그리고 새뮤얼 슬럼키 각하 본인이 통가죽 장화에 푸른 목도리를 두르고 앞으로 나와 앞서 말한 포트의 손을 잡고서, 자신이 이턴스윌 가제트에 진 잊을 수 없는 은혜를 군중에게 과장된 몸짓으로 증언했을 때, 그 열광은 어마어마했다."]], [["‘Is everything ready?’ said the Honourable Samuel Slumkey to Mr.", "\"모든 게 준비되었소?\""], ["Perker.", "새뮤얼 슬럼키 각하가 퍼커 씨에게 말했다."]], [["‘Everything, my dear Sir,’ was the little man’s reply.", "\"모두요, 친애하는 선생.\" 작은 사내가 대답했다."]], [["‘Nothing has been omitted, I hope?’ said the Honourable Samuel Slumkey.", "\"빠뜨린 건 없겠지요?\" 새뮤얼 슬럼키 각하가 말했다."]], [["‘Nothing has been left undone, my dear sir--nothing whatever.", "\"빠뜨린 건 하나도 없습니다, 친애하는 선생—단 하나도요."], ["There are twenty washed men at the street door for you to shake hands with; and six children in arms that you’re to pat on the head, and inquire the age of; be particular about the children, my dear sir--it has always a great effect, that sort of thing.’", "거리 문 앞에 깨끗이 씻은 사내 스무 명이 선생과 악수하려고 대기하고 있고, 팔에 안긴 아기 여섯이 있으니 머리를 토닥여 주고 나이를 물어봐 주십시오. 아이들에게 신경을 쓰십시오, 친애하는 선생—그런 것이 늘 큰 효과를 내거든요.\""]], [["‘I’ll take care,’ said the Honourable Samuel Slumkey.", "\"명심하겠소.\" 새뮤얼 슬럼키 각하가 말했다."]], [["‘And, perhaps, my dear Sir,’ said the cautious little man, ‘perhaps if you could--I don’t mean to say it’s indispensable--but if you could manage to kiss one of ‘em, it would produce a very great impression on the crowd.’", "\"그리고, 혹시, 친애하는 선생,\" 신중한 작은 사내가 말했다. \"혹시 하실 수 있다면—꼭 필요하다는 말씀은 아닙니다만—그중 하나에게 입을 맞춰 주실 수 있다면, 군중에게 아주 큰 인상을 남길 겁니다.\""]], [["‘Wouldn’t it have as good an effect if the proposer or seconder did that?’ said the Honourable Samuel Slumkey.", "\"제안자나 추천자가 그렇게 해도 똑같이 좋은 효과가 나지 않겠소?\" 새뮤얼 슬럼키 각하가 말했다."]], [["‘Why, I am afraid it wouldn’t,’ replied the agent; ‘if it were done by yourself, my dear Sir, I think it would make you very popular.’", "\"글쎄요, 그렇지는 못할 것 같습니다.\" 대리인이 대답했다. \"선생께서 직접 하신다면, 친애하는 선생, 선생의 인기가 아주 높아질 것 같습니다.\""]], [["‘Very well,’ said the Honourable Samuel Slumkey, with a resigned air, ‘then it must be done.", "\"좋소.\" 새뮤얼 슬럼키 각하가 체념한 듯한 기색으로 말했다."], ["That’s all.’", "\"그럼 해야지요. 그뿐이오.\""]], [["‘Arrange the procession,’ cried the twenty committee-men.", "\"행렬을 정돈하라!\" 위원 스무 명이 외쳤다."]], [["Amidst the cheers of the assembled throng, the band, and the constables, and the committee-men, and the voters, and the horsemen, and the carriages, took their places--each of the two-horse vehicles being closely packed with as many gentlemen as could manage to stand upright in it; and that assigned to Mr. Perker, containing Mr. Pickwick, Mr. Tupman, Mr. Snodgrass, and about half a dozen of the committee besides.", "모여든 무리의 환호 속에, 악대와 경관들과 위원들과 유권자들과 기마병들과 마차들이 저마다 제자리를 잡았다. 이두마차마다 그 안에 똑바로 설 수 있는 한 많은 신사들이 빽빽이 들어찼고, 퍼커 씨에게 배정된 마차에는 픽윅 씨, 텁먼 씨, 스노드그래스 씨, 그리고 그 밖에 위원 여섯 명가량이 탔다."]], [["There was a moment of awful suspense as the procession waited for the Honourable Samuel Slumkey to step into his carriage.", "행렬이 새뮤얼 슬럼키 각하가 마차에 오르기를 기다리는 동안 끔찍한 긴장의 순간이 있었다."], ["Suddenly the crowd set up a great cheering.", "갑자기 군중이 큰 환호를 올렸다."]], [["‘He has come out,’ said little Mr.", "\"각하가 나오셨군.\""], ["Perker, greatly excited; the more so as their position did not enable them to see what was going forward.", "작은 퍼커 씨가 크게 흥분하여 말했는데, 그들의 위치에서는 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 볼 수 없었기에 더욱 그러했다."]], [["Another cheer, much louder.", "또 한 번, 훨씬 더 우렁찬 환호."]], [["‘He has shaken hands with the men,’ cried the little agent.", "\"각하가 사람들과 악수하셨군.\" 작은 대리인이 외쳤다."]], [["Another cheer, far more vehement.", "또 한 번, 한층 더 격렬한 환호."]], [["‘He has patted the babies on the head,’ said Mr.", "\"각하가 아기들 머리를 토닥이셨군.\""], ["Perker, trembling with anxiety.", "퍼커 씨가 불안에 떨며 말했다."]], [["A roar of applause that rent the air.", "공기를 찢는 갈채의 함성."]], [["‘He has kissed one of ‘em!’ exclaimed the delighted little man.", "\"각하가 그중 하나에게 입을 맞추셨군!\" 기뻐 어쩔 줄 모르는 작은 사내가 외쳤다."]], [["A second roar.", "두 번째 함성."]], [["‘He has kissed another,’ gasped the excited manager.", "\"각하가 또 하나에게 입을 맞추셨군.\" 흥분한 관리자가 헐떡이며 말했다."]], [["A third roar.", "세 번째 함성."]], [["‘He’s kissing ‘em all!’ screamed the enthusiastic little gentleman, and hailed by the deafening shouts of the multitude, the procession moved on.", "\"각하가 죄다 입을 맞추고 계셔!\" 열광한 작은 신사가 비명을 질렀고, 군중의 귀가 멍멍해지는 외침에 환영받으며 행렬이 움직이기 시작했다."]], [["How or by what means it became mixed up with the other procession, and how it was ever extricated from the confusion consequent thereupon, is more than we can undertake to describe, inasmuch as Mr. Pickwick’s hat was knocked over his eyes, nose, and mouth, by one poke of a Buff flag- staff, very early in the proceedings.", "그것이 어떻게, 또는 무슨 수로 다른 행렬과 뒤엉키게 되었는지, 그리고 그로 인해 빚어진 혼란에서 도대체 어떻게 빠져나왔는지는 우리가 감히 묘사하겠다고 나설 수 있는 바가 아니니, 픽윅 씨의 모자가 일이 시작되자마자 아주 일찌감치 버프파 깃대에 한 번 쿡 찔려 그의 눈과 코와 입 위로 푹 내려앉아 버렸기 때문이다."], ["He describes himself as being surrounded on every side, when he could catch a glimpse of the scene, by angry and ferocious countenances, by a vast cloud of dust, and by a dense crowd of combatants.", "그는 그 장면을 어쩌다 한 번씩 흘끗 볼 수 있었을 때, 사방이 성나고 사나운 얼굴들과, 광대한 흙먼지 구름과, 빽빽한 싸움꾼 무리에 에워싸여 있더라고 묘사한다."], ["He represents himself as being forced from the carriage by some unseen power, and being personally engaged in a pugilistic encounter; but with whom, or how, or why, he is wholly unable to state.", "그는 자신이 어떤 보이지 않는 힘에 의해 마차에서 떠밀려 나와, 몸소 권투 시합에 휘말렸더라고 진술하는데, 다만 누구와, 어떻게, 또는 왜 그랬는지는 도무지 말할 수가 없다고 한다."], ["He then felt himself forced up some wooden steps by the persons from behind; and on removing his hat, found himself surrounded by his friends, in the very front of the left hand side of the hustings.", "이윽고 그는 뒤에 있는 사람들에게 떠밀려 어떤 나무 계단을 올라가는 자신을 느꼈고, 모자를 벗고 보니 자기 친구들에게 둘러싸인 채 후보 지명 연단 왼편의 맨 앞자리에 있었다."], ["The right was reserved for the Buff party, and the centre for the mayor and his officers; one of whom--the fat crier of Eatanswill--was ringing an enormous bell, by way of commanding silence, while Mr.", "오른편은 버프파 몫으로, 가운데는 시장과 그의 관리들 몫으로 비워져 있었다."], ["Horatio Fizkin, and the Honourable Samuel Slumkey, with their hands upon their hearts, were bowing with the utmost affability to the troubled sea of heads that inundated the open space in front; and from whence arose a storm of groans, and shouts, and yells, and hootings, that would have done honour to an earthquake.", "그 관리들 가운데 하나—이턴스윌의 뚱뚱한 외침꾼—가 정숙을 명하느라 거대한 종을 울리고 있었고, 그동안 호레이쇼 피즈킨 씨와 새뮤얼 슬럼키 각하는 가슴에 손을 얹은 채, 앞쪽 빈터를 가득 메운 머리들의 어수선한 바다를 향해 더없이 상냥하게 절을 하고 있었으니, 그 바다에서는 지진에라도 명예가 될 만한 신음과 외침과 비명과 야유의 폭풍이 일었다."]], [["‘There’s Winkle,’ said Mr.", "\"저기 윈클이 있군.\""], ["Tupman, pulling his friend by the sleeve.", "텁먼 씨가 친구의 소매를 잡아당기며 말했다."]], [["‘Where!’ said Mr.", "\"어디?\""], ["Pickwick, putting on his spectacles, which he had fortunately kept in his pocket hitherto.", "픽윅 씨가, 다행히 그때껏 주머니에 넣어 둔 안경을 쓰며 말했다."]], [["‘There,’ said Mr.", "\"저기,\""], ["Tupman, ‘on the top of that house.’ And there, sure enough, in the leaden gutter of a tiled roof, were Mr.", "텁먼 씨가 말했다. \"저 집 꼭대기에.\""], ["Winkle and Mrs. Pott, comfortably seated in a couple of chairs, waving their handkerchiefs in token of recognition--a compliment which Mr.", "그러자 과연 거기에, 기와지붕의 납 홈통에 윈클 씨와 포트 부인이 의자 두 개에 편안히 앉아, 알아본다는 표시로 손수건을 흔들고 있었다."], ["Pickwick returned by kissing his hand to the lady.", "픽윅 씨는 부인에게 손에 입을 맞춰 보이며 그 인사에 화답했다."]], [["The proceedings had not yet commenced; and as an inactive crowd is generally disposed to be jocose, this very innocent action was sufficient to awaken their facetiousness.", "연설은 아직 시작되지 않았다. 그런데 일거리 없는 군중은 대체로 익살을 부리기 마련이라, 이 더없이 순진한 동작 하나만으로도 그들의 우스개 기질을 일깨우기에 충분했다."]], [["‘Oh, you wicked old rascal,’ cried one voice, ‘looking arter the girls, are you?’", "\"오, 이 고약한 늙은 악당 같으니,\" 한 목소리가 외쳤다. \"처녀들이나 노리고 있는 거요?\""]], [["‘Oh, you wenerable sinner,’ cried another.", "\"오, 이 점잖빼는 죄인 양반아.\" 다른 목소리가 외쳤다."]], [["‘Putting on his spectacles to look at a married ‘ooman!’ said a third.", "\"안경까지 쓰고 유부녀를 쳐다보네!\" 세 번째 목소리가 말했다."]], [["‘I see him a-winkin’ at her, with his wicked old eye,’ shouted a fourth.", "\"저 고약한 늙은 눈으로 부인한테 추파를 던지는 게 보이는구먼.\" 네 번째 목소리가 외쳤다."]], [["‘Look arter your wife, Pott,’ bellowed a fifth--and then there was a roar of laughter.", "\"마누라나 잘 챙기쇼, 포트.\" 다섯 번째 목소리가 고함쳤다. 그러고는 폭소가 터졌다."]], [["As these taunts were accompanied with invidious comparisons between Mr. Pickwick and an aged ram, and several witticisms of the like nature; and as they moreover rather tended to convey reflections upon the honour of an innocent lady, Mr.", "이 조롱들에는 픽윅 씨를 늙은 숫양에 빗대는 험한 비유와, 그와 비슷한 종류의 재담 몇 가지가 곁들여졌고, 게다가 그것들은 죄 없는 한 부인의 명예에 대한 비방까지 은근히 내비치는 경향이 있었기에, 픽윅 씨의 분노는 이만저만이 아니었다."], ["Pickwick’s indignation was excessive; but as silence was proclaimed at the moment, he contented himself by scorching the mob with a look of pity for their misguided minds, at which they laughed more boisterously than ever.", "그러나 바로 그 순간 정숙이 선포되었으므로, 그는 그들의 그릇된 정신을 가엾이 여기는 눈빛으로 군중을 쏘아보는 것으로 만족했는데, 그러자 그들은 전보다 더 떠들썩하게 웃어 댔다."]], [["‘Silence!’ roared the mayor’s attendants.", "\"정숙!\" 시장의 수행원들이 고함쳤다."]], [["‘Whiffin, proclaim silence,’ said the mayor, with an air of pomp befitting his lofty station.", "\"휘핀, 정숙을 선포하라.\" 시장이 자신의 드높은 지위에 걸맞은 위풍 어린 태도로 말했다."], ["In obedience to this command the crier performed another concerto on the bell, whereupon a gentleman in the crowd called out ‘Muffins’; which occasioned another laugh.", "이 명령에 따라 외침꾼은 종으로 또 한 차례 협주곡을 연주했고, 그러자 군중 속의 한 신사가 \"머핀!\" 하고 외쳐 또 한 번 웃음을 자아냈다."]], [["‘Gentlemen,’ said the mayor, at as loud a pitch as he could possibly force his voice to--‘gentlemen.", "\"신사 여러분,\" 시장이 한껏 짜낼 수 있는 가장 큰 목청으로 말했다."], ["Brother electors of the borough of Eatanswill.", "\"신사 여러분. 이턴스윌 자치구의 동료 유권자 여러분."], ["We are met here to-day for the purpose of choosing a representative in the room of our late--’", "우리는 오늘 우리의 작고하신—의 자리를 대신할 대표를 뽑고자 이 자리에 모였습니다—\""]], [["Here the mayor was interrupted by a voice in the crowd.", "여기서 시장은 군중 속의 한 목소리에 말이 끊겼다."]], [["‘Suc-cess to the mayor!’ cried the voice, ‘and may he never desert the nail and sarspan business, as he got his money by.’", "\"시장 만세!\" 그 목소리가 외쳤다. \"그리고 그가 돈을 벌게 해 준 못과 냄비 장사를 결코 저버리지 않기를!\""]], [["This allusion to the professional pursuits of the orator was received with a storm of delight, which, with a bell-accompaniment, rendered the remainder of his speech inaudible, with the exception of the concluding sentence, in which he thanked the meeting for the patient attention with which they heard him throughout--an expression of gratitude which elicited another burst of mirth, of about a quarter of an hour’s duration.", "연설가의 직업적 생업을 이렇게 빗댄 말은 환희의 폭풍으로 받아들여졌고, 그것이 종 반주와 어우러져 그의 연설 나머지를 알아들을 수 없게 만들었으니, 다만 마지막 문장만은 예외였다. 그 문장에서 그는 모임이 처음부터 끝까지 참을성 있게 경청해 준 데 감사를 표했는데, 이 감사의 표현은 약 십오 분간 이어진 또 한 차례의 웃음을 터뜨리게 했다."]], [["Next, a tall, thin gentleman, in a very stiff white neckerchief, after being repeatedly desired by the crowd to ‘send a boy home, to ask whether he hadn’t left his voice under the pillow,’ begged to nominate a fit and proper person to represent them in Parliament.", "다음으로, 아주 빳빳한 흰 목도리를 두른 키 크고 마른 신사가, 군중으로부터 \"베개 밑에 목소리를 두고 오지 않았는지 알아보라고 집에 애를 하나 보내라\"는 말을 거듭 들은 끝에, 의회에서 자신들을 대표할 적격의 인물을 지명하겠다고 청했다."], ["And when he said it was Horatio Fizkin, Esquire, of Fizkin Lodge, near Eatanswill, the Fizkinites applauded, and the Slumkeyites groaned, so long, and so loudly, that both he and the seconder might have sung comic songs in lieu of speaking, without anybody’s being a bit the wiser.", "그리고 그가 그 인물이 이턴스윌 인근 피즈킨 로지의 호레이쇼 피즈킨 씨라고 말하자, 피즈킨파는 갈채를 보내고 슬럼키파는 어찌나 오래, 어찌나 크게 신음을 토했던지, 그와 추천자 둘 다 연설하는 대신 우스개 노래를 불렀더라도 아무도 더 알아채지 못했을 정도였다."]], [["The friends of Horatio Fizkin, Esquire, having had their innings, a little choleric, pink-faced man stood forward to propose another fit and proper person to represent the electors of Eatanswill in Parliament; and very swimmingly the pink-faced gentleman would have got on, if he had not been rather too choleric to entertain a sufficient perception of the fun of the crowd. But after a very few sentences of figurative eloquence, the pink-faced gentleman got from denouncing those who interrupted him in the mob, to exchanging defiances with the gentlemen on the hustings; whereupon arose an uproar which reduced him to the necessity of expressing his feelings by serious pantomime, which he did, and then left the stage to his seconder, who delivered a written speech of half an hour’s length, and wouldn’t be stopped, because he had sent it all to the Eatanswill Gazette, and the Eatanswill Gazette had already printed it, every word.", "호레이쇼 피즈킨 씨의 친구들이 자기네 차례를 다 치르고 나자, 다소 성마른 분홍빛 얼굴의 작은 사내가 앞으로 나서서, 이턴스윌 유권자들을 의회에서 대표할 또 한 사람의 적격 인물을 추천했다. 그리고 이 분홍빛 얼굴의 신사는, 군중의 장난기를 충분히 알아챌 만큼 마음의 여유를 갖기에는 너무 성마르지만 않았더라면, 아주 순조롭게 일을 풀어 나갔을 것이다. 그러나 비유적인 능변을 몇 마디 늘어놓은 끝에, 분홍빛 얼굴의 신사는 군중 속에서 자신의 말을 가로막는 자들을 규탄하던 데서 나아가, 연단 위 신사들과 도전적인 말을 주고받기에 이르렀다. 그러자 큰 소동이 일어, 그는 어쩔 수 없이 자기 감정을 진지한 무언극으로 표현해야 할 지경이 되었고, 실제로 그렇게 한 다음 무대를 추천자에게 넘겼다. 추천자는 반 시간 길이의 원고 연설을 늘어놓았고, 멈추려 하지 않았으니, 그 원고를 죄다 이턴스윌 가제트에 보냈고 이턴스윌 가제트가 이미 그것을 한 글자도 빠짐없이 인쇄해 둔 까닭이었다."]], [["Then Horatio Fizkin, Esquire, of Fizkin Lodge, near Eatanswill, presented himself for the purpose of addressing the electors; which he no sooner did, than the band employed by the Honourable Samuel Slumkey, commenced performing with a power to which their strength in the morning was a trifle; in return for which, the Buff crowd belaboured the heads and shoulders of the Blue crowd; on which the Blue crowd endeavoured to dispossess themselves of their very unpleasant neighbours the Buff crowd; and a scene of struggling, and pushing, and fighting, succeeded, to which we can no more do justice than the mayor could, although he issued imperative orders to twelve constables to seize the ringleaders, who might amount in number to two hundred and fifty, or thereabouts. At all these encounters, Horatio Fizkin, Esquire, of Fizkin Lodge, and his friends, waxed fierce and furious; until at last Horatio Fizkin, Esquire, of Fizkin Lodge, begged to ask his opponent, the Honourable Samuel Slumkey, of Slumkey Hall, whether that band played by his consent; which question the Honourable Samuel Slumkey declining to answer, Horatio Fizkin, Esquire, of Fizkin Lodge, shook his fist in the countenance of the Honourable Samuel Slumkey, of Slumkey Hall; upon which the Honourable Samuel Slumkey, his blood being up, defied Horatio Fizkin, Esquire, to mortal combat. At this violation of all known rules and precedents of order, the mayor commanded another fantasia on the bell, and declared that he would bring before himself, both Horatio Fizkin, Esquire, of Fizkin Lodge, and the Honourable Samuel Slumkey, of Slumkey Hall, and bind them over to keep the peace. Upon this terrific denunciation, the supporters of the two candidates interfered, and after the friends of each party had quarrelled in pairs, for three-quarters of an hour, Horatio Fizkin, Esquire, touched his hat to the Honourable Samuel Slumkey; the Honourable Samuel Slumkey touched his to Horatio Fizkin, Esquire; the band was stopped; the crowd were partially quieted; and Horatio Fizkin, Esquire, was permitted to proceed.", "이윽고 이턴스윌 인근 피즈킨 로지의 호레이쇼 피즈킨 씨가 유권자들에게 연설하고자 모습을 드러냈다. 그가 그러기가 무섭게, 새뮤얼 슬럼키 각하가 고용한 악대가, 그날 아침의 기세는 아무것도 아니었을 만큼의 위력으로 연주를 시작했다. 그 보답으로 버프파 군중은 블루파 군중의 머리와 어깨를 두들겨 댔고, 이에 블루파 군중은 그 더없이 거북한 이웃인 버프파 군중을 떨쳐 내려 애썼다. 그리하여 몸싸움과 밀치기와 주먹다짐의 장면이 이어졌으니, 우리는 그것을 제대로 묘사할 수 없거니와 시장 역시 그러지 못했으니, 비록 그가 경관 열둘에게 주모자들—그 수가 이백오십 명쯤, 아니면 그 언저리쯤 되었을—을 붙잡으라고 단호히 명령하긴 했지만 말이다. 이 모든 충돌에서 피즈킨 로지의 호레이쇼 피즈킨 씨와 그의 친구들은 사납고 격렬해졌다. 마침내 피즈킨 로지의 호레이쇼 피즈킨 씨가 자신의 적수인 슬럼키 홀의 새뮤얼 슬럼키 각하에게, 저 악대가 그의 동의로 연주하는 것인지 여쭙겠다고 청했다. 새뮤얼 슬럼키 각하가 그 질문에 답하기를 거절하자, 피즈킨 로지의 호레이쇼 피즈킨 씨는 슬럼키 홀의 새뮤얼 슬럼키 각하의 면전에 주먹을 흔들어 댔다. 그러자 새뮤얼 슬럼키 각하는 피가 끓어올라, 호레이쇼 피즈킨 씨에게 목숨을 건 결투를 청했다. 알려진 온갖 질서의 규칙과 전례를 이렇게 어기는 데 대해, 시장은 또 한 차례 종으로 환상곡을 연주하라 명하고는, 자신이 피즈킨 로지의 호레이쇼 피즈킨 씨와 슬럼키 홀의 새뮤얼 슬럼키 각하를 함께 자기 앞에 출두시켜, 치안을 지키겠다는 서약을 받겠노라 선언했다. 이 무시무시한 호령에, 두 후보의 지지자들이 끼어들었고, 양 당파의 친구들이 짝을 지어 사십오 분간 다툰 끝에, 호레이쇼 피즈킨 씨가 새뮤얼 슬럼키 각하에게 모자에 손을 대어 인사했고, 새뮤얼 슬럼키 각하도 호레이쇼 피즈킨 씨에게 그렇게 인사했으며, 악대는 멈췄고, 군중은 어느 정도 진정되었으며, 호레이쇼 피즈킨 씨는 연설을 이어 가도록 허락받았다."]], [["The speeches of the two candidates, though differing in every other respect, afforded a beautiful tribute to the merit and high worth of the electors of Eatanswill.", "두 후보의 연설은, 다른 모든 점에서는 서로 달랐으나, 이턴스윌 유권자들의 미덕과 높은 가치에 대한 아름다운 헌사를 바쳤다는 점에서는 같았다."], ["Both expressed their opinion that a more independent, a more enlightened, a more public-spirited, a more noble- minded, a more disinterested set of men than those who had promised to vote for him, never existed on earth; each darkly hinted his suspicions that the electors in the opposite interest had certain swinish and besotted infirmities which rendered them unfit for the exercise of the important duties they were called upon to discharge.", "둘 다, 자기에게 표를 주겠다고 약속한 사람들보다 더 독립적이고, 더 깨인 의식을 지녔으며, 더 공익에 헌신적이고, 더 고결한 정신을 지녔으며, 더 사심 없는 사람들은 이 땅에 일찍이 존재한 적이 없다는 것이 자신의 의견이라고 밝혔다. 그리고 저마다, 반대편을 지지하는 유권자들은 그들이 수행하라고 부름받은 중대한 임무를 행하기에 부적합하게 만드는, 어떤 돼지 같고 우매한 결함들을 지녔다는 의심을 음험하게 내비쳤다."], ["Fizkin expressed his readiness to do anything he was wanted: Slumkey, his determination to do nothing that was asked of him.", "피즈킨은 무엇이든 요청받는 일은 다 할 준비가 되어 있다고 밝혔고, 슬럼키는 무엇이든 요청받는 일은 결코 하지 않겠다는 결심을 밝혔다."], ["Both said that the trade, the manufactures, the commerce, the prosperity of Eatanswill, would ever be dearer to their hearts than any earthly object; and each had it in his power to state, with the utmost confidence, that he was the man who would eventually be returned.", "둘 다, 이턴스윌의 무역과 제조업과 상업과 번영이 이 세상 그 어떤 대상보다도 자기 가슴에 언제나 더 소중하리라고 말했고, 저마다 자신이야말로 결국 당선될 사람이라고 더없이 자신 있게 진술할 수 있는 처지였다."]], [["There was a show of hands; the mayor decided in favour of the Honourable Samuel Slumkey, of Slumkey Hall.", "거수 표결이 있었고, 시장은 슬럼키 홀의 새뮤얼 슬럼키 각하의 손을 들어 주었다."], ["Horatio Fizkin, Esquire, of Fizkin Lodge, demanded a poll, and a poll was fixed accordingly.", "피즈킨 로지의 호레이쇼 피즈킨 씨가 투표를 요구하자, 그에 따라 투표일이 정해졌다."], ["Then a vote of thanks was moved to the mayor for his able conduct in the chair; and the mayor, devoutly wishing that he had had a chair to display his able conduct in (for he had been standing during the whole proceedings), returned thanks.", "그런 다음 의장석에서의 유능한 처신에 대해 시장에게 감사를 표하는 동의가 제기되었고, 시장은 자신의 유능한 처신을 보여 줄 의자가 있었더라면 좋았을 것을 간절히 바라며(그는 의사 진행 내내 서 있었던 까닭이다) 감사로 화답했다."], ["The processions reformed, the carriages rolled slowly through the crowd, and its members screeched and shouted after them as their feelings or caprice dictated.", "행렬은 다시 정돈되었고, 마차들은 군중 사이를 천천히 굴러갔으며, 군중은 저마다의 감정이나 변덕이 시키는 대로 그 뒤를 향해 꽥꽥거리고 고함쳤다."]], [["During the whole time of the polling, the town was in a perpetual fever of excitement.", "투표가 진행되는 내내, 읍은 끊임없는 흥분의 열병에 들떠 있었다."], ["Everything was conducted on the most liberal and delightful scale.", "모든 일이 더없이 후하고 즐거운 규모로 진행되었다."], ["Excisable articles were remarkably cheap at all the public-houses; and spring vans paraded the streets for the accommodation of voters who were seized with any temporary dizziness in the head--an epidemic which prevailed among the electors, during the contest, to a most alarming extent, and under the influence of which they might frequently be seen lying on the pavements in a state of utter insensibility.", "과세 대상 품목들은 모든 술집에서 놀랍도록 쌌다. 그리고 머리에 일시적인 어지럼증을 일으킨 유권자들의 편의를 위해 짐마차들이 거리를 누볐으니, 그 어지럼증은 경합이 벌어지는 동안 유권자들 사이에 더없이 우려스러운 정도로 퍼진 유행병이었고, 그 영향 아래 그들이 완전히 의식을 잃은 채 보도에 누워 있는 모습을 흔히 볼 수 있었다."], ["A small body of electors remained unpolled on the very last day.", "한 작은 무리의 유권자들이 바로 마지막 날까지도 투표하지 않은 채로 남아 있었다."], ["They were calculating and reflecting persons, who had not yet been convinced by the arguments of either party, although they had frequent conferences with each.", "그들은 타산적이고 사려 깊은 사람들로, 양 당파와 자주 회담을 가졌음에도 어느 쪽의 논변에도 아직 설득되지 않은 자들이었다."], ["One hour before the close of the poll, Mr. Perker solicited the honour of a private interview with these intelligent, these noble, these patriotic men.", "투표 마감 한 시간 전, 퍼커 씨는 이 지각 있고, 고결하고, 애국적인 사람들과 사사로이 면담하는 영광을 청했다."], ["It was granted.", "그것은 허락되었다."], ["His arguments were brief but satisfactory.", "그의 논변은 간략했으나 흡족했다."], ["They went in a body to the poll; and when they returned, the Honourable Samuel Slumkey, of Slumkey Hall, was returned also.", "그들은 떼를 지어 투표소로 갔다. 그리고 그들이 돌아왔을 때, 슬럼키 홀의 새뮤얼 슬럼키 각하 또한 당선되었다."]], [["CHAPTER XIV. COMPRISING A BRIEF DESCRIPTION OF THE COMPANY AT THE PEACOCK ASSEMBLED; AND A TALE TOLD BY A BAGMAN\nIt is pleasant to turn from contemplating the strife and turmoil of political existence, to the peaceful repose of private life.", "정치라는 존재의 다툼과 소란을 들여다보다가, 사생활의 평온한 안식으로 눈을 돌리는 것은 유쾌한 일이다."], ["Although in reality no great partisan of either side, Mr. Pickwick was sufficiently fired with Mr.", "실은 어느 편의 열렬한 당파도 아니었으나, 픽윅 씨는 포트 씨의 열정에 충분히 불이 붙어, 지난 장에서 그 자신의 비망록을 토대로 엮어 묘사한 그 일련의 사건에 자신의 모든 시간과 주의를 쏟았다."], ["Pott’s enthusiasm, to apply his whole time and attention to the proceedings, of which the last chapter affords a description compiled from his own memoranda. Nor while he was thus occupied was Mr.", "그가 이렇게 분주한 동안 윈클 씨도 한가로이 지내지는 않았으니, 그의 온 시간은 포트 부인과 함께 즐거운 산책과 짧은 시골 나들이를 다니는 데 바쳐졌다."], ["Winkle idle, his whole time being devoted to pleasant walks and short country excursions with Mrs. Pott, who never failed, when such an opportunity presented itself, to seek some relief from the tedious monotony she so constantly complained of.", "부인은 그런 기회가 생길 때마다 자신이 늘 그토록 불평하던 지루한 단조로움에서 벗어날 위안을 구하기를 결코 빠뜨리지 않았다."], ["The two gentlemen being thus completely domesticated in the editor’s house, Mr. Tupman and Mr.", "이렇게 두 신사가 편집자의 집에 완전히 길들여져 있는 동안, 터프먼 씨와 스노드그래스 씨는 상당 부분 제 나름의 방편에 내맡겨졌다."], ["Snodgrass were in a great measure cast upon their own resources. Taking but little interest in public affairs, they beguiled their time chiefly with such amusements as the Peacock afforded, which were limited to a bagatelle-board in the first floor, and a sequestered skittle-ground in the back yard.", "공적인 일에는 별 흥미가 없었던 터라, 그들은 주로 공작 여관이 제공하는 오락거리로 시간을 보냈는데, 그 오락이라야 이 층에 있는 바가텔 대와 뒷마당의 한갓진 스키틀 마당으로 한정되어 있었다."], ["In the science and nicety of both these recreations, which are far more abstruse than ordinary men suppose, they were gradually initiated by Mr. Weller, who possessed a perfect knowledge of such pastimes.", "이 두 가지 놀이의 기술과 묘리는 보통 사람들이 짐작하는 것보다 훨씬 심오한데, 그들은 그런 소일거리에 완벽한 지식을 갖춘 웰러 씨에게서 차츰 그 묘법을 전수받았다."], ["Thus, notwithstanding that they were in a great measure deprived of the comfort and advantage of Mr. Pickwick’s society, they were still enabled to beguile the time, and to prevent its hanging heavily on their hands.", "그리하여 픽윅 씨와 함께하는 즐거움과 이점을 상당히 빼앗긴 처지였음에도, 그들은 여전히 시간을 보내며 그것이 손안에서 무겁게 늘어지는 것을 막을 수 있었다."]], [["It was in the evening, however, that the Peacock presented attractions which enabled the two friends to resist even the invitations of the gifted, though prosy, Pott. It was in the evening that the ‘commercial room’ was filled with a social circle, whose characters and manners it was the delight of Mr.", "그러나 공작 여관이 이 두 친구로 하여금 재주는 있되 따분한 포트의 초대마저 뿌리치게 할 만큼 매력을 발하는 때는 바로 저녁이었다."], ["Tupman to observe; whose sayings and doings it was the habit of Mr. Snodgrass to note down.", "저녁이 되면 '상인용 객실'은 어떤 사교 모임으로 가득 찼으니, 그 인물됨과 거동을 관찰하는 것이 터프먼 씨의 낙이었고, 그들의 말과 행동을 적어 두는 것이 스노드그래스 씨의 버릇이었다."]], [["Most people know what sort of places commercial rooms usually are.", "상인용 객실이라는 곳이 대개 어떤 곳인지는 대부분 사람들이 안다."], ["That of the Peacock differed in no material respect from the generality of such apartments; that is to say, it was a large, bare-looking room, the furniture of which had no doubt been better when it was newer, with a spacious table in the centre, and a variety of smaller dittos in the corners; an extensive assortment of variously shaped chairs, and an old Turkey carpet, bearing about the same relative proportion to the size of the room, as a lady’s pocket-handkerchief might to the floor of a watch- box.", "공작 여관의 그것도 그런 부류의 방들이 일반적으로 그러한 것과 본질적인 점에서는 다를 바가 없었다. 다시 말해, 그것은 크고 휑한 방으로, 그 가구들은 새것이었을 때는 분명 더 나았을 터였고, 한가운데에는 널찍한 탁자가, 구석마다 그보다 작은 같은 종류의 탁자들이 놓여 있었다."], ["The walls were garnished with one or two large maps; and several weather-beaten rough greatcoats, with complicated capes, dangled from a long row of pegs in one corner.", "갖가지 모양의 의자가 푸짐하게 갖추어져 있었고, 낡은 터키산 양탄자가 깔려 있었는데, 그 양탄자가 방의 크기에 대해 차지하는 비율이란 마치 숙녀의 손수건이 파수막 바닥에 대해 차지하는 비율과 거의 같았다."], ["The mantel-shelf was ornamented with a wooden inkstand, containing one stump of a pen and half a wafer; a road- book and directory; a county history minus the cover; and the mortal remains of a trout in a glass coffin.", "벽에는 큼직한 지도가 한두 장 걸려 장식되어 있었고, 한쪽 구석의 긴 못걸이에는 풍상에 시달린 거친 외투 몇 벌이 복잡한 어깨망토를 단 채 늘어져 있었다."], ["The atmosphere was redolent of tobacco-smoke, the fumes of which had communicated a rather dingy hue to the whole room, and more especially to the dusty red curtains which shaded the windows.", "벽난로 선반은 나무로 된 잉크스탠드로 장식되어 있었는데, 그 안에는 펜 한 자루의 몽당이와 봉랍 반쪽이 들어 있었고, 그 밖에 도로 안내서와 인명록 한 권, 표지가 떨어져 나간 주(州) 역사책 한 권, 그리고 유리 관 속에 든 송어의 유해가 놓여 있었다."], ["On the sideboard a variety of miscellaneous articles were huddled together, the most conspicuous of which were some very cloudy fish-sauce cruets, a couple of driving-boxes, two or three whips, and as many travelling shawls, a tray of knives and forks, and the mustard.", "공기에는 담배 연기 냄새가 배어 있었으니, 그 연기는 방 전체에, 특히 창문을 가린 먼지투성이의 붉은 커튼에 다소 거무칙칙한 빛깔을 입혀 놓았다. 찬장 위에는 잡다한 물건들이 한데 뒤엉켜 쌓여 있었는데, 그 가운데 가장 눈에 띄는 것은 몹시 뿌예진 생선 소스 병 몇 개, 마부석 방석 두어 개, 채찍 두세 개, 그만큼의 여행용 숄, 나이프와 포크가 담긴 쟁반, 그리고 겨자였다."]], [["Here it was that Mr. Tupman and Mr. Snodgrass were seated on the evening after the conclusion of the election, with several other temporary inmates of the house, smoking and drinking.", "선거가 끝난 이튿날 저녁, 터프먼 씨와 스노드그래스 씨가 이 집에 임시로 묵는 다른 몇몇과 함께 담배를 피우고 술을 마시며 앉아 있던 곳이 바로 여기였다."]], [["‘Well, gents,’ said a stout, hale personage of about forty, with only one eye--a very bright black eye, which twinkled with a roguish expression of fun and good-humour, ‘our noble selves, gents.", "\"자, 여러분.\" 마흔 살가량의, 외눈인—그러나 그 눈은 더없이 반짝이는 검은 눈으로, 짓궂은 익살과 호탕함이 어려 반짝이는 눈이었다—건장하고 정정한 사내가 말했다."], ["I always propose that toast to the company, and drink Mary to myself.", "\"우리 고귀한 자신들을 위하여, 여러분. 나는 늘 좌중에 이 건배를 권하고, 메리를 위해서는 혼자 마신다오."], ["Eh, Mary!’", "응, 메리!\""]], [["‘Get along with you, you wretch,’ said the hand-maiden, obviously not ill-pleased with the compliment, however.", "\"저리 가요, 이 못된 양반.\" 하녀가 말했으나, 그 칭찬이 싫지는 않은 기색이 역력했다."]], [["‘Don’t go away, Mary,’ said the black-eyed man.", "\"가지 마, 메리.\" 검은 눈의 사내가 말했다."]], [["‘Let me alone, imperence,’ said the young lady.", "\"좀 내버려 둬요, 뻔뻔하기는.\" 젊은 처녀가 말했다."]], [["‘Never mind,’ said the one-eyed man, calling after the girl as she left the room.", "\"괜찮아.\" 외눈 사내가 방을 나가는 처녀의 뒤에다 대고 외쳤다."], ["‘I’ll step out by and by, Mary.", "\"조금 있다 내가 나가지, 메리."], ["Keep your spirits up, dear.’ Here he went through the not very difficult process of winking upon the company with his solitary eye, to the enthusiastic delight of an elderly personage with a dirty face and a clay pipe.", "기운 내라고, 아가씨.\" 여기서 그는 외눈 하나로 좌중을 향해 윙크하는, 그리 어렵지 않은 동작을 해 보였고, 그것이 더러운 얼굴에 흙으로 빚은 파이프를 문 어느 나이 든 사내를 열광적으로 기쁘게 했다."]], [["‘Rum creeters is women,’ said the dirty-faced man, after a pause.", "\"여자란 별난 족속이야.\" 더러운 얼굴의 사내가 잠시 사이를 두고 말했다."]], [["‘Ah!", "\"아!"], ["no mistake about that,’ said a very red-faced man, behind a cigar.", "그건 틀림없는 말이지.\" 시가를 문 채 매우 붉은 얼굴의 사내가 말했다."]], [["After this little bit of philosophy there was another pause.", "이 자그마한 철학을 늘어놓은 뒤 또 한 차례 침묵이 흘렀다."]], [["‘There’s rummer things than women in this world though, mind you,’ said the man with the black eye, slowly filling a large Dutch pipe, with a most capacious bowl.", "\"그렇지만 말이오, 이 세상엔 여자보다 더 별난 것들도 있다오.\" 검은 눈의 사내가 더없이 넉넉한 통을 가진 큼직한 네덜란드식 파이프에 천천히 담배를 채우며 말했다."]], [["‘Are you married?’ inquired the dirty-faced man.", "\"결혼은 했소?\" 더러운 얼굴의 사내가 물었다."]], [["‘Can’t say I am.’", "\"했다고는 못 하겠소.\""]], [["‘I thought not.’ Here the dirty-faced man fell into ecstasies of mirth at his own retort, in which he was joined by a man of bland voice and placid countenance, who always made it a point to agree with everybody.", "\"안 한 줄 알았소.\" 여기서 더러운 얼굴의 사내는 자기가 한 응수에 황홀할 만큼 흥겨워 웃음을 터뜨렸고, 늘 모든 사람의 말에 맞장구치기를 원칙으로 삼는, 부드러운 목소리와 온화한 낯빛의 사내가 거기에 합세했다."]], [["‘Women, after all, gentlemen,’ said the enthusiastic Mr.", "\"여자란 결국, 여러분.\" 열정적인 스노드그래스 씨가 말했다."], ["Snodgrass, ‘are the great props and comforts of our existence.’", "\"우리 존재의 위대한 버팀목이요 위안입니다.\""]], [["‘So they are,’ said the placid gentleman.", "\"그렇고말고요.\" 온화한 신사가 말했다."]], [["‘When they’re in a good humour,’ interposed the dirty-faced man.", "\"기분이 좋을 때는 말이오.\" 더러운 얼굴의 사내가 끼어들었다."]], [["‘And that’s very true,’ said the placid one.", "\"그것도 참으로 옳은 말이오.\" 온화한 사내가 말했다."]], [["‘I repudiate that qualification,’ said Mr.", "\"나는 그 단서를 거부합니다.\""], ["Snodgrass, whose thoughts were fast reverting to Emily Wardle.", "생각이 어느새 에밀리 와들에게로 빠르게 돌아가고 있던 스노드그래스 씨가 말했다."], ["‘I repudiate it with disdain--with indignation.", "\"나는 그것을 경멸로—분노로 거부합니다."], ["Show me the man who says anything against women, as women, and I boldly declare he is not a man.’ And Mr.", "여자를, 여자라는 이유로 헐뜯는 자가 있거든 내게 데려와 보시오. 나는 그가 사내가 아니라고 당당히 선언하겠소.\""], ["Snodgrass took his cigar from his mouth, and struck the table violently with his clenched fist.", "그러고는 스노드그래스 씨가 입에서 시가를 빼더니 움켜쥔 주먹으로 탁자를 거세게 내리쳤다."]], [["‘That’s good sound argument,’ said the placid man.", "\"건전하고 훌륭한 논리요.\" 온화한 사내가 말했다."]], [["‘Containing a position which I deny,’ interrupted he of the dirty countenance.", "\"내가 부정하는 명제를 담고 있구먼.\" 더러운 낯빛의 사내가 가로막았다."]], [["‘And there’s certainly a very great deal of truth in what you observe too, Sir,’ said the placid gentleman.", "\"그리고 선생 말씀에도 분명 대단히 많은 진실이 담겨 있긴 하지요.\" 온화한 신사가 말했다."]], [["‘Your health, Sir,’ said the bagman with the lonely eye, bestowing an approving nod on Mr.", "\"선생의 건강을 위하여.\""], ["Snodgrass.", "외눈의 외판원이 스노드그래스 씨에게 동의하듯 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다."]], [["Mr. Snodgrass acknowledged the compliment.", "스노드그래스 씨는 그 인사를 받아 답했다."]], [["‘I always like to hear a good argument,’ continued the bagman, ‘a sharp one, like this: it’s very improving; but this little argument about women brought to my mind a story I have heard an old uncle of mine tell, the recollection of which, just now, made me say there were rummer things than women to be met with, sometimes.’", "\"나는 늘 좋은 논쟁을 듣는 걸 즐긴다오.\" 외판원이 말을 이었다. \"이번 같은 날카로운 논쟁 말이오. 매우 유익하지요. 그런데 여자에 관한 이 자그마한 논쟁을 듣다 보니, 내가 옛날에 우리 늙은 삼촌에게서 들은 이야기 하나가 떠오르는구려. 방금 그 이야기가 생각나는 바람에, 더러 여자보다 더 별난 것들과 마주칠 수도 있다고 내가 말한 거라오.\""]], [["‘I should like to hear that same story,’ said the red-faced man with the cigar.", "\"그 이야기라는 걸 들어 보고 싶군요.\" 시가를 문 붉은 얼굴의 사내가 말했다."]], [["‘Should you?’ was the only reply of the bagman, who continued to smoke with great vehemence.", "\"그러시오?\" 외판원은 그 한마디만 던지고는 맹렬한 기세로 계속 담배를 피웠다."]], [["‘So should I,’ said Mr.", "\"나도 그렇소.\""], ["Tupman, speaking for the first time.", "터프먼 씨가 처음으로 입을 열며 말했다."], ["He was always anxious to increase his stock of experience.", "그는 늘 자신의 경험 보따리를 늘리는 데 열심이었다."]], [["‘Should you?", "\"당신도 그렇소?"], ["Well then, I’ll tell it.", "그럼 좋소, 이야기하리다."], ["No, I won’t.", "아니, 그만두겠소."], ["I know you won’t believe it,’ said the man with the roguish eye, making that organ look more roguish than ever.", "당신들이 믿지 않을 게 뻔하니까.\" 짓궂은 눈의 사내가, 그 눈을 어느 때보다 더 짓궂게 보이게 하며 말했다."], ["‘If you say it’s true, of course I shall,’ said Mr.", "\"참말이라고 하시면 당연히 믿겠소.\""], ["Tupman.", "터프먼 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Well, upon that understanding I’ll tell you,’ replied the traveller.", "\"그럼 그 약조 아래 이야기하리다.\" 그 나그네가 대답했다."], ["‘Did you ever hear of the great commercial house of Bilson & Slum?", "\"빌슨 앤드 슬럼이라는 큰 상사(商社) 이야기를 들어 보신 적 있소?"], ["But it doesn’t matter though, whether you did or not, because they retired from business long since.", "하긴 들었든 안 들었든 상관없소, 그들은 오래전에 사업에서 손을 뗐으니까."], ["It’s eighty years ago, since the circumstance happened to a traveller for that house, but he was a particular friend of my uncle’s; and my uncle told the story to me.", "그 상사의 외판원에게 이 일이 일어난 게 팔십 년 전이오만, 그 사람은 우리 삼촌의 각별한 친구였고, 삼촌이 내게 그 이야기를 들려주었지요."], ["It’s a queer name; but he used to call it", "묘한 제목이오만, 삼촌은 그걸 이렇게 부르곤 했소."]], [["and he used to tell it, something in this way.", "외판원의 이야기\n그리고 대강 이런 식으로 들려주곤 했지요."]], [["‘One winter’s evening, about five o’clock, just as it began to grow dusk, a man in a gig might have been seen urging his tired horse along the road which leads across Marlborough Downs, in the direction of Bristol.", "'어느 겨울 저녁, 다섯 시쯤, 막 어스름이 깔리기 시작할 무렵, 한 사내가 이륜마차를 몰며 지친 말을 재촉해 말버러 다운스를 가로질러 브리스틀 쪽으로 난 길을 따라가는 모습을 볼 수 있었을지도 모른다오."], ["I say he might have been seen, and I have no doubt he would have been, if anybody but a blind man had happened to pass that way; but the weather was so bad, and the night so cold and wet, that nothing was out but the water, and so the traveller jogged along in the middle of the road, lonesome and dreary enough.", "볼 수 있었을지도 모른다고 했소만, 장님이 아닌 사람이 그 길을 지나갔다면 틀림없이 보였으리라는 데는 의심의 여지가 없소. 그러나 날씨가 어찌나 사나운지, 또 밤이 어찌나 춥고 축축한지, 바깥에 나도는 거라곤 빗물뿐이었고, 그래서 그 나그네는 길 한복판을 따라 외롭고도 쓸쓸하게 터덜터덜 갔던 거요."], ["If any bagman of that day could have caught sight of the little neck-or-nothing sort of gig, with a clay-coloured body and red wheels, and the vixenish, ill tempered, fast- going bay mare, that looked like a cross between a butcher’s horse and a twopenny post-office pony, he would have known at once, that this traveller could have been no other than Tom Smart, of the great house of Bilson and Slum, Cateaton Street, City.", "그 시절 어느 외판원이라도 진흙빛 차체에 붉은 바퀴를 단, 죽기 살기로 내달리는 듯한 그 자그마한 이륜마차와, 푸줏간 말과 이펜짜리 우편 조랑말을 섞어 놓은 듯한, 성깔 사납고 심술궂고 빨리 달리는 그 밤색 암말을 한번 보기만 했다면, 이 나그네가 바로 시티의 케이턴 가에 있는 빌슨 앤드 슬럼 대상사의 톰 스마트임을 단박에 알아챘을 거요."], ["However, as there was no bagman to look on, nobody knew anything at all about the matter; and so Tom Smart and his clay-coloured gig with the red wheels, and the vixenish mare with the fast pace, went on together, keeping the secret among them, and nobody was a bit the wiser.", "그러나 지켜보는 외판원이 하나도 없었으니, 이 일에 대해 아무도 아무것도 알지 못했고, 그리하여 톰 스마트와 붉은 바퀴를 단 진흙빛 이륜마차와 빨리 달리는 성깔 사나운 암말은 셋이서 비밀을 간직한 채 함께 나아갔고, 아무도 조금도 더 알게 된 바가 없었소."]], [["‘There are many pleasanter places even in this dreary world, than Marlborough Downs when it blows hard; and if you throw in beside, a gloomy winter’s evening, a miry and sloppy road, and a pelting fall of heavy rain, and try the effect, by way of experiment, in your own proper person, you will experience the full force of this observation.", "이 음울한 세상에도 바람이 거셀 때의 말버러 다운스보다 더 유쾌한 곳은 많이 있다오. 게다가 음산한 겨울 저녁과, 진창에 질척이는 길과, 사정없이 퍼붓는 폭우까지 더해서, 시험 삼아 그 효과를 당신 몸으로 직접 겪어 본다면, 이 말이 얼마나 옳은지 절절히 체험하게 될 거요."]], [["‘The wind blew--not up the road or down it, though that’s bad enough, but sheer across it, sending the rain slanting down like the lines they used to rule in the copy-books at school, to make the boys slope well.", "바람이 불었소—길을 따라 위로도 아래로도 아니라, 그것도 충분히 고약하긴 하오만, 길을 정통으로 가로질러 불어서, 비를 비스듬히 후려쳐 내려보냈으니, 마치 학교에서 아이들이 글씨를 잘 비스듬히 쓰게 하려고 습자 공책에 그어 놓던 줄 같았소."], ["For a moment it would die away, and the traveller would begin to delude himself into the belief that, exhausted with its previous fury, it had quietly laid itself down to rest, when, whoo!", "한순간 바람이 잦아들면, 나그네는 바람이 앞서의 광포함에 지쳐 조용히 드러누워 쉬고 있는 거라고 스스로를 속여 믿기 시작하는데, 그때 휘이!"], ["he could hear it growling and whistling in the distance, and on it would come rushing over the hill-tops, and sweeping along the plain, gathering sound and strength as it drew nearer, until it dashed with a heavy gust against horse and man, driving the sharp rain into their ears, and its cold damp breath into their very bones; and past them it would scour, far, far away, with a stunning roar, as if in ridicule of their weakness, and triumphant in the consciousness of its own strength and power.", "멀리서 바람이 으르렁대고 휘파람 부는 소리가 들리는가 싶더니, 그것이 언덕마루를 넘어 휩쓸어 오고 벌판을 쓸며 달려와, 가까워질수록 소리와 기세를 더해, 마침내 묵직한 돌풍이 되어 말과 사람을 후려치며, 날카로운 빗줄기를 그들의 귓속에, 그 차고 축축한 숨결을 그들의 뼛속까지 몰아넣었소. 그러고는 그들을 스쳐 멀리, 저 멀리 귀가 멍멍한 굉음을 내며 내달려 갔으니, 마치 그들의 연약함을 비웃기라도 하듯, 또 제 힘과 위세를 자각하며 의기양양해하듯 말이오."]], [["‘The bay mare splashed away, through the mud and water, with drooping ears; now and then tossing her head as if to express her disgust at this very ungentlemanly behaviour of the elements, but keeping a good pace notwithstanding, until a gust of wind, more furious than any that had yet assailed them, caused her to stop suddenly and plant her four feet firmly against the ground, to prevent her being blown over.", "밤색 암말은 귀를 축 늘어뜨린 채 진흙과 물을 튀기며 첨벙첨벙 나아갔고, 이따금 머리를 내흔드는 것이 마치 이 지극히 무례한 날씨의 거동에 대한 혐오를 드러내는 듯했으나, 그래도 좋은 걸음걸이를 유지하다가, 마침내 그때껏 그들을 덮친 어떤 것보다 더 사나운 돌풍이 불어닥치자 갑자기 멈춰 서서 날아가지 않으려 네 발을 땅에 단단히 버텼소."], ["It’s a special mercy that she did this, for if she had been blown over, the vixenish mare was so light, and the gig was so light, and Tom Smart such a light weight into the bargain, that they must infallibly have all gone rolling over and over together, until they reached the confines of earth, or until the wind fell; and in either case the probability is, that neither the vixenish mare, nor the clay-coloured gig with the red wheels, nor Tom Smart, would ever have been fit for service again.", "암말이 그렇게 한 것은 각별한 자비였소. 만약 정말로 날아가 버렸다면, 성깔 사나운 암말은 어찌나 가벼운지, 이륜마차도 어찌나 가벼운지, 게다가 톰 스마트마저도 그토록 가벼운 몸이라, 세 식구가 영락없이 다 같이 데굴데굴 굴러, 땅끝에 이르거나 바람이 잦아들 때까지 굴렀을 것이고, 어느 쪽이든 그 성깔 사나운 암말도, 붉은 바퀴를 단 진흙빛 이륜마차도, 톰 스마트도, 다시는 쓸모 있게 되지 못했을 게 십중팔구였소."]], [["‘“Well, damn my straps and whiskers,”", "\"이런, 내 가죽끈과 구레나룻을 걸고 빌어먹을.\""], ["says Tom Smart (Tom sometimes had an unpleasant knack of swearing)--“damn my straps and whiskers,”", "톰 스마트가 말했소(톰은 가끔 욕을 입에 다는 고약한 버릇이 있었거든)."], ["says Tom, “if this ain’t pleasant, blow me!”", "\"내 가죽끈과 구레나룻을 걸고 빌어먹을, 이게 즐겁지 않다면, 바람에 날아가 버려라!\""]], [["‘You’ll very likely ask me why, as Tom Smart had been pretty well blown already, he expressed this wish to be submitted to the same process again.", "톰 스마트가 이미 어지간히 바람에 날릴 만큼 날렸으면서 어째서 같은 일을 또 당하기를 바랐는지, 당신들은 십중팔구 내게 그 까닭을 물을 거요."], ["I can’t say--all I know is, that Tom Smart said so--or at least he always told my uncle he said so, and it’s just the same thing.", "나도 모르겠소—내가 아는 거라곤 톰 스마트가 그렇게 말했다는 것뿐이오—적어도 그가 우리 삼촌에게 늘 그렇게 말했다고 했으니, 그게 그거지요."]], [["“‘Blow me,”", "\"바람에 날아가 버려라.\""], ["says Tom Smart; and the mare neighed as if she were precisely of the same opinion.", "톰 스마트가 말하자, 암말이 자기도 꼭 같은 생각이라는 듯 히힝 울었소."]], [["“‘Cheer up, old girl,”", "\"기운 내, 늙은 아가씨.\""], ["said Tom, patting the bay mare on the neck with the end of his whip.", "톰이 채찍 끝으로 밤색 암말의 목을 토닥이며 말했소."], ["“It won’t do pushing on, such a night as this; the first house we come to we’ll put up at, so the faster you go the sooner it’s over.", "\"이런 밤에 억지로 밀고 나가 봐야 소용없어. 처음 닿는 집에 묵을 테니, 네가 빨리 갈수록 더 빨리 끝나는 거다."], ["Soho, old girl--gently--gently.”", "어허, 늙은 아가씨—살살—살살.\""]], [["‘Whether the vixenish mare was sufficiently well acquainted with the tones of Tom’s voice to comprehend his meaning, or whether she found it colder standing still than moving on, of course I can’t say.", "성깔 사나운 암말이 톰의 목소리 가락을 알아들을 만큼 익숙해서 그 뜻을 헤아린 건지, 아니면 가만히 서 있는 것이 움직이는 것보다 더 춥다고 느낀 건지, 그야 물론 나로선 알 수 없소."], ["But I can say that Tom had no sooner finished speaking, than she pricked up her ears, and started forward at a speed which made the clay-coloured gig rattle until you would have supposed every one of the red spokes were going to fly out on the turf of Marlborough Downs; and even Tom, whip as he was, couldn’t stop or check her pace, until she drew up of her own accord, before a roadside inn on the right-hand side of the way, about half a quarter of a mile from the end of the Downs.", "그러나 내가 말할 수 있는 건, 톰이 말을 마치자마자 암말이 귀를 쫑긋 세우고는 어찌나 빠른 속도로 앞으로 내달았던지, 진흙빛 이륜마차가 어찌나 덜컹거렸던지, 붉은 바큇살이 하나하나 다 말버러 다운스의 잔디 위로 튕겨 날아갈 것만 같았다는 거요. 톰이 아무리 채찍질에 이골이 난 사람이라도 그 걸음을 멈추거나 늦추지 못했고, 마침내 암말이 제풀에 멈춰 선 곳은 다운스 끝에서 반의 사분의 일 마일쯤 떨어진, 길 오른편의 한 길가 여관 앞이었소."]], [["‘Tom cast a hasty glance at the upper part of the house as he threw the reins to the hostler, and stuck the whip in the box.", "톰은 마부에게 고삐를 던지고 채찍을 마차 통에 꽂으면서 그 집의 위쪽을 흘긋 살펴보았소."], ["It was a strange old place, built of a kind of shingle, inlaid, as it were, with cross- beams, with gabled-topped windows projecting completely over the pathway, and a low door with a dark porch, and a couple of steep steps leading down into the house, instead of the modern fashion of half a dozen shallow ones leading up to it.", "그것은 기묘한 옛 건물로, 일종의 나무 널판으로 지었는데, 마치 들보를 가로질러 박아 넣은 듯했고, 박공 얹은 창들이 보행로 위로 완전히 튀어나와 있었으며, 어두운 현관이 딸린 낮은 문이 있었고, 요즘 식으로 얕은 계단 대여섯이 위로 이어진 게 아니라, 가파른 계단 두어 단이 집 안으로 내려가게 되어 있었소."], ["It was a comfortable-looking place though, for there was a strong, cheerful light in the bar window, which shed a bright ray across the road, and even lighted up the hedge on the other side; and there was a red flickering light in the opposite window, one moment but faintly discernible, and the next gleaming strongly through the drawn curtains, which intimated that a rousing fire was blazing within.", "그래도 아늑해 보이는 곳이었으니, 술청 창에 강하고 명랑한 불빛이 있어 길 건너로 환한 빛줄기를 뿜어내고 건너편 산울타리까지 비추었으며, 맞은편 창에는 붉게 일렁이는 불빛이 있어, 한순간 어렴풋이 분간될 듯하다가 다음 순간 드리운 커튼 사이로 강하게 빛나, 안에 활활 타오르는 불이 있음을 알려 주었소."], ["Marking these little evidences with the eye of an experienced traveller, Tom dismounted with as much agility as his half- frozen limbs would permit, and entered the house.", "노련한 나그네의 눈으로 이런 자그마한 증거들을 가늠한 톰은, 얼어붙다시피 한 사지가 허락하는 한껏 날렵하게 마차에서 내려 집 안으로 들어갔소."]], [["‘In less than five minutes’ time, Tom was ensconced in the room opposite the bar--the very room where he had imagined the fire blazing--before a substantial, matter-of-fact, roaring fire, composed of something short of a bushel of coals, and wood enough to make half a dozen decent gooseberry bushes, piled half-way up the chimney, and roaring and crackling with a sound that of itself would have warmed the heart of any reasonable man. This was comfortable, but this was not all; for a smartly-dressed girl, with a bright eye and a neat ankle, was laying a very clean white cloth on the table; and as Tom sat with his slippered feet on the fender, and his back to the open door, he saw a charming prospect of the bar reflected in the glass over the chimney-piece, with delightful rows of green bottles and gold labels, together with jars of pickles and preserves, and cheeses and boiled hams, and rounds of beef, arranged on shelves in the most tempting and delicious array. Well, this was comfortable too; but even this was not all--for in the bar, seated at tea at the nicest possible little table, drawn close up before the brightest possible little fire, was a buxom widow of somewhere about eight-and-forty or thereabouts, with a face as comfortable as the bar, who was evidently the landlady of the house, and the supreme ruler over all these agreeable possessions. There was only one drawback to the beauty of the whole picture, and that was a tall man--a very tall man-- in a brown coat and bright basket buttons, and black whiskers and wavy black hair, who was seated at tea with the widow, and who it required no great penetration to discover was in a fair way of persuading her to be a widow no longer, but to confer upon him the privilege of sitting down in that bar, for and during the whole remainder of the term of his natural life.", "채 오 분도 안 되어, 톰은 술청 맞은편 방—그가 불이 활활 타고 있으리라 상상했던 바로 그 방—에 자리 잡고, 든든하고 실속 있게 우렁차게 타오르는 불 앞에 앉아 있었소. 그 불은 한 부셸이 채 안 되는 석탄과, 멀쩡한 구스베리 덤불 대여섯 그루는 만들 만한 장작으로 이루어져, 굴뚝 중턱까지 쌓여 우렁우렁 탁탁 타올랐으니, 그 소리만으로도 웬만큼 정상인 사람의 마음은 절로 데워질 만했소. 이것은 아늑했지만, 이게 다가 아니었소. 맵시 있게 차려입은, 반짝이는 눈과 단정한 발목을 지닌 처녀가 탁자에 아주 깨끗한 흰 식탁보를 깔고 있었으니까. 톰이 실내화 신은 발을 난로 받침대에 올리고 열린 문에 등을 돌린 채 앉아 있자니, 벽난로 위 거울에 비친 술청의 매혹적인 풍경이 눈에 들어왔소. 녹색 병들과 금빛 상표가 줄줄이 늘어선 흐뭇한 광경에다, 절임과 잼이 든 단지들, 치즈와 삶은 햄, 둥글게 만 쇠고기가 선반에 더없이 먹음직스럽고 군침 도는 모양새로 진열되어 있었소. 자, 이것도 아늑했지만, 이것조차 다가 아니었으니—술청 안에, 더없이 아담한 조그만 탁자에 앉아, 더없이 환한 조그만 불 앞에 바싹 다가앉아 차를 마시는, 마흔여덟쯤 되었거나 그 언저리쯤 되어 보이는 풍만한 과부가 있었던 거요. 술청만큼이나 푸근한 얼굴을 한 그 여인은, 분명 이 집의 안주인이자 이 모든 흐뭇한 재산을 다스리는 지존이었소. 이 그림 전체의 아름다움에 단 하나 흠이 있었으니, 그것은 키 큰 사내—아주 키 큰 사내—였소. 갈색 외투에 반짝이는 바구니 모양 단추를 달고, 검은 구레나룻에 물결치는 검은 머리를 한 그자가 과부와 마주 앉아 차를 마시고 있었는데, 그자가 과부를 설득해 더는 과부로 지내지 말고 자기에게 그 술청에 눌러앉을 특권을, 그것도 제 타고난 수명이 다하는 그날까지 통째로 누리게 해 달라고 어지간히 잘 구슬리고 있는 중임을 알아채는 데는 대단한 통찰력이 필요치 않았소."]], [["‘Tom Smart was by no means of an irritable or envious disposition, but somehow or other the tall man with the brown coat and the bright basket buttons did rouse what little gall he had in his composition, and did make him feel extremely indignant, the more especially as he could now and then observe, from his seat before the glass, certain little affectionate familiarities passing between the tall man and the widow, which sufficiently denoted that the tall man was as high in favour as he was in size.", "톰 스마트는 결코 성마르거나 시기심 많은 성미가 아니었소만, 어쩐 일인지 갈색 외투에 반짝이는 바구니 단추를 단 그 키 큰 사내가 그의 천성 속에 든 얼마 안 되는 울화를 들쑤셔, 그를 몹시 분개하게 만들었소. 더구나 거울 앞 제자리에서 이따금, 그 키 큰 사내와 과부 사이에 오가는 어떤 자그마한 다정한 친밀함을 엿볼 수 있었기에 더욱 그러했으니, 그것은 그 키 큰 사내가 키가 큰 만큼이나 총애도 두텁다는 걸 충분히 말해 주었소."], ["Tom was fond of hot punch--I may venture to say he was very fond of hot punch--and after he had seen the vixenish mare well fed and well littered down, and had eaten every bit of the nice little hot dinner which the widow tossed up for him with her own hands, he just ordered a tumbler of it by way of experiment.", "톰은 따끈한 펀치를 좋아했소—아주 좋아했다고 감히 말할 수 있겠소—그래서 성깔 사나운 암말이 잘 먹고 잘 깔린 자리에 누운 걸 보고, 과부가 제 손으로 직접 차려 준 맛깔난 더운 저녁을 한 입도 남김없이 먹어 치운 다음, 그저 시험 삼아 펀치 한 잔을 시켰소."], ["Now, if there was one thing in the whole range of domestic art, which the widow could manufacture better than another, it was this identical article; and the first tumbler was adapted to Tom Smart’s taste with such peculiar nicety, that he ordered a second with the least possible delay.", "그런데 온갖 가사 기예를 통틀어 그 과부가 무엇보다 더 잘 만드는 게 하나 있다면, 그것이 바로 이 펀치였소. 첫 잔이 어찌나 절묘하게 톰 스마트의 입맛에 딱 맞았던지, 그는 한 치의 지체도 없이 두 잔째를 시켰소."], ["Hot punch is a pleasant thing, gentlemen--an extremely pleasant thing under any circumstances--but in that snug old parlour, before the roaring fire, with the wind blowing outside till every timber in the old house creaked again, Tom Smart found it perfectly delightful.", "따끈한 펀치란 유쾌한 것이오, 여러분—어떤 상황에서든 더없이 유쾌한 것이오만—그 아늑한 옛 거실에서, 우렁차게 타는 불 앞에서, 바깥에선 바람이 불어 그 낡은 집의 들보란 들보가 다시금 삐걱대는 가운데, 톰 스마트는 그것이 더없이 흐뭇하게 느껴졌소."], ["He ordered another tumbler, and then another--I am not quite certain whether he didn’t order another after that--but the more he drank of the hot punch, the more he thought of the tall man.", "그는 또 한 잔, 그리고 또 한 잔을 시켰고—그다음에도 한 잔을 더 시키지 않았는지 나는 그리 확신할 수 없소만—따끈한 펀치를 마시면 마실수록 키 큰 사내 생각이 더 났소."]], [["‘“Confound his impudence!”", "\"저 뻔뻔한 작자 같으니!\""], ["said Tom to himself, “what business has he in that snug bar?", "톰이 속으로 말했소. \"저자가 저 아늑한 술청에 무슨 볼일이 있단 말이야?"], ["Such an ugly villain too!”", "게다가 저렇게 못생긴 악당이!\""], ["said Tom.", "톰이 말했소."], ["“If the widow had any taste, she might surely pick up some better fellow than that.”", "\"과부에게 안목이라는 게 조금이라도 있다면, 저것보단 나은 사내를 분명 골라잡을 수 있을 텐데.\""], ["Here Tom’s eye wandered from the glass on the chimney-piece to the glass on the table; and as he felt himself becoming gradually sentimental, he emptied the fourth tumbler of punch and ordered a fifth.", "여기서 톰의 눈은 벽난로 위 거울에서 탁자 위 술잔으로 옮겨 갔고, 차츰 감상에 젖어 드는 것을 느끼며 그는 펀치 넉 잔째를 비우고 다섯 잔째를 시켰소."]], [["‘Tom Smart, gentlemen, had always been very much attached to the public line.", "톰 스마트는 말이오, 여러분, 늘 술장사에 무던히도 마음이 끌렸소."], ["It had been long his ambition to stand in a bar of his own, in a green coat, knee-cords, and tops.", "녹색 외투에 무릎까지 오는 끈바지와 장화 차림으로 제 술청에 떡 버티고 서 보는 것이 오래된 그의 야망이었소."], ["He had a great notion of taking the chair at convivial dinners, and he had often thought how well he could preside in a room of his own in the talking way, and what a capital example he could set to his customers in the drinking department.", "그는 흥겨운 만찬에서 좌장 노릇을 하는 데 거창한 포부가 있었고, 제 방에서 입담으로 좌중을 주재한다면 얼마나 멋들어질지, 또 술 마시는 부문에서 손님들에게 얼마나 본보기가 될지를 자주 떠올리곤 했소."], ["All these things passed rapidly through Tom’s mind as he sat drinking the hot punch by the roaring fire, and he felt very justly and properly indignant that the tall man should be in a fair way of keeping such an excellent house, while he, Tom Smart, was as far off from it as ever.", "톰이 우렁찬 불 앞에서 따끈한 펀치를 마시며 앉아 있는 동안 이 모든 것이 그의 머릿속을 빠르게 스쳐 지나갔고, 그는 저 키 큰 사내는 이토록 훌륭한 집을 차지할 어엿한 길목에 서 있는데 자기, 톰 스마트는 그것에서 여전히 멀찍이 떨어져 있다는 사실에 지극히 정당하고 마땅하게 분개했소."], ["So, after deliberating over the two last tumblers, whether he hadn’t a perfect right to pick a quarrel with the tall man for having contrived to get into the good graces of the buxom widow, Tom Smart at last arrived at the satisfactory conclusion that he was a very ill-used and persecuted individual, and had better go to bed.", "그리하여 마지막 두 잔을 비우며, 저 풍만한 과부의 환심을 용케 사들인 그 키 큰 사내에게 시비를 걸 완벽한 권리가 자기에게 없는 것은 아닌지 곰곰이 따져 본 끝에, 톰 스마트는 마침내 자신이 몹시 부당한 대접을 받고 박해당하는 인간이며, 그러니 자러 가는 편이 낫겠다는 흡족한 결론에 이르렀소."]], [["‘Up a wide and ancient staircase the smart girl preceded Tom, shading the chamber candle with her hand, to protect it from the currents of air which in such a rambling old place might have found plenty of room to disport themselves in, without blowing the candle out, but which did blow it out nevertheless--thus affording Tom’s enemies an opportunity of asserting that it was he, and not the wind, who extinguished the candle, and that while he pretended to be blowing it alight again, he was in fact kissing the girl.", "그 맵시 있는 처녀가 침실용 촛불을 손으로 가린 채 넓고 오래된 계단을 앞장서 톰을 안내했는데, 그렇게 엉성하게 뻗어 나간 낡은 집에서는 바람이 촛불을 끄지 않고도 마음껏 뛰놀 만한 자리가 얼마든지 있었을 텐데도, 바람은 그예 촛불을 꺼 버렸소—그리하여 톰의 원수들에게, 촛불을 끈 것은 바람이 아니라 바로 톰이며, 그가 다시 촛불을 불어 켜는 척하면서 실은 처녀에게 입을 맞추고 있었다고 우길 빌미를 주고 말았소."], ["Be this as it may, another light was obtained, and Tom was conducted through a maze of rooms, and a labyrinth of passages, to the apartment which had been prepared for his reception, where the girl bade him good-night and left him alone.", "어찌 됐든, 다른 불을 마련해, 톰은 미로 같은 방들과 미궁 같은 복도를 지나 그를 위해 준비된 방으로 안내되었고, 거기서 처녀는 그에게 잘 자라는 인사를 하고 그를 혼자 남겨 둔 채 떠났소."]], [["‘It was a good large room with big closets, and a bed which might have served for a whole boarding-school, to say nothing of a couple of oaken presses that would have held the baggage of a small army; but what struck Tom’s fancy most was a strange, grim-looking, high backed chair, carved in the most fantastic manner, with a flowered damask cushion, and the round knobs at the bottom of the legs carefully tied up in red cloth, as if it had got the gout in its toes.", "큼직한 벽장들이 딸린 꽤 넓은 방이었고, 침대는 기숙학교 전교생이 다 써도 될 만했으며, 작은 군대 하나의 짐을 다 넣고도 남을 떡갈나무 옷장 두 개는 말할 것도 없었소. 그러나 톰의 마음을 가장 사로잡은 것은 기묘하고도 음산해 보이는, 등받이가 높은 의자였소."], ["Of any other queer chair, Tom would only have thought it was a queer chair, and there would have been an end of the matter; but there was something about this particular chair, and yet he couldn’t tell what it was, so odd and so unlike any other piece of furniture he had ever seen, that it seemed to fascinate him.", "더없이 환상적인 모양새로 조각되어 있었고, 꽃무늬 다마스크 방석이 놓였으며, 다리 끝의 둥근 혹들은 마치 발가락에 통풍이라도 걸린 양 붉은 천으로 정성스레 싸매여 있었소. 다른 어떤 괴상한 의자였다면 톰은 그저 괴상한 의자로구나 하고 말았을 것이고, 그걸로 일은 끝났을 거요."], ["He sat down before the fire, and stared at the old chair for half an hour.--Damn the chair, it was such a strange old thing, he couldn’t take his eyes off it.", "그러나 이 별난 의자에는 뭔가가 있었으니, 그게 무엇인지 톰 자신도 말할 수 없었소만, 어찌나 야릇하고, 그가 여태 본 어떤 가구와도 어찌나 다르던지, 그를 호리는 것만 같았소. 그는 불 앞에 앉아 반 시간 동안 그 낡은 의자를 빤히 바라보았소—제기랄, 그 의자는 어찌나 기묘한 옛 물건이던지, 그는 거기서 눈을 뗄 수가 없었소."]], [["‘“Well,”", "\"흠.\""], ["said Tom, slowly undressing himself, and staring at the old chair all the while, which stood with a mysterious aspect by the bedside, “I never saw such a rum concern as that in my days.", "천천히 옷을 벗으면서, 침대맡에 신비로운 자태로 서 있는 그 낡은 의자에서 줄곧 눈을 떼지 못한 채 톰이 말했소. \"내 평생 저런 별난 물건은 처음 보는걸."], ["Very odd,”", "참 야릇해.\""], ["said Tom, who had got rather sage with the hot punch--“very odd.”", "따끈한 펀치 덕에 다소 점잖은 현자가 된 톰이 말했소—\"참 야릇해.\""], ["Tom shook his head with an air of profound wisdom, and looked at the chair again.", "톰은 심오한 지혜의 기색으로 고개를 절레절레 흔들고는 다시 의자를 바라보았소."], ["He couldn’t make anything of it though, so he got into bed, covered himself up warm, and fell asleep.", "그래도 도무지 알 수가 없었던지라, 그는 침대로 들어가 몸을 따뜻하게 덮고 잠이 들었소."]], [["‘In about half an hour, Tom woke up with a start, from a confused dream of tall men and tumblers of punch; and the first object that presented itself to his waking imagination was the queer chair.", "반 시간쯤 지나, 톰은 키 큰 사내들과 펀치 잔들이 뒤엉킨 어수선한 꿈에서 화들짝 깨어났소. 그리고 깨어난 그의 머릿속에 가장 먼저 떠오른 것은 그 괴상한 의자였소."]], [["‘“I won’t look at it any more,”", "\"다시는 저걸 안 봐야지.\""], ["said Tom to himself, and he squeezed his eyelids together, and tried to persuade himself he was going to sleep again.", "톰이 속으로 말하며 두 눈꺼풀을 꼭 맞붙이고 다시 잠들려 한다고 스스로를 타일러 보았소. 소용없었소."], ["No use; nothing but queer chairs danced before his eyes, kicking up their legs, jumping over each other’s backs, and playing all kinds of antics.", "눈앞에서 춤추는 거라곤 괴상한 의자들뿐이었으니, 다리를 차올리고, 서로의 등을 뛰어넘고, 온갖 익살을 부려 댔소."]], [["“‘I may as well see one real chair, as two or three complete sets of false ones,”", "\"가짜 의자 두세 벌 전체를 보느니, 진짜 의자 하나를 보는 편이 낫겠군.\""], ["said Tom, bringing out his head from under the bedclothes.", "톰이 이불 밑에서 머리를 빼며 말했소."], ["There it was, plainly discernible by the light of the fire, looking as provoking as ever.", "의자는 거기, 불빛에 또렷이 분간되며, 어느 때보다 더 약 올리는 듯한 모습으로 있었소."]], [["‘Tom gazed at the chair; and, suddenly as he looked at it, a most extraordinary change seemed to come over it.", "톰이 의자를 가만히 바라보는데, 그가 그것을 보고 있노라니 별안간, 더없이 기이한 변화가 의자에 일어나는 듯했소."], ["The carving of the back gradually assumed the lineaments and expression of an old, shrivelled human face; the damask cushion became an antique, flapped waistcoat; the round knobs grew into a couple of feet, encased in red cloth slippers; and the whole chair looked like a very ugly old man, of the previous century, with his arms akimbo.", "등받이의 조각이 차츰 늙고 쪼글쪼글한 사람 얼굴의 이목구비와 표정을 띠어 갔고, 다마스크 방석은 옛날식의 자락 늘어진 조끼가 되었으며, 둥근 혹들은 붉은 천 슬리퍼에 싸인 두 발로 자라났고, 의자 전체가 마치 지난 세기의, 두 팔을 허리에 짚은 몹시 못생긴 늙은이처럼 보였소."], ["Tom sat up in bed, and rubbed his eyes to dispel the illusion.", "톰은 침대에 일어나 앉아 그 환영을 떨치려 눈을 비볐소. 천만에."], ["No. The chair was an ugly old gentleman; and what was more, he was winking at Tom Smart.", "의자는 한 못생긴 노신사였고, 게다가 그 노신사는 톰 스마트를 향해 윙크를 하고 있었소."]], [["‘Tom was naturally a headlong, careless sort of dog, and he had had five tumblers of hot punch into the bargain; so, although he was a little startled at first, he began to grow rather indignant when he saw the old gentleman winking and leering at him with such an impudent air.", "톰은 본래 앞뒤 안 가리고 무모한, 태평한 부류의 사내였고, 게다가 따끈한 펀치를 다섯 잔이나 들이켜기까지 했소. 그래서 처음엔 좀 놀랐으나, 그 노신사가 그토록 뻔뻔한 태도로 윙크를 하고 곁눈질을 하는 걸 보자 다소 분개하기 시작했소."], ["At length he resolved that he wouldn’t stand it; and as the old face still kept winking away as fast as ever, Tom said, in a very angry tone--", "마침내 그는 더는 못 참겠다고 마음먹었고, 그 늙은 얼굴이 여전히 어느 때 못지않게 빠르게 윙크를 해 대는지라, 톰이 몹시 성난 어조로 말했소—"]], [["‘“What the devil are you winking at me for?”", "\"대체 왜 나한테 윙크질이야?\""]], [["‘“Because I like it, Tom Smart,”", "\"내가 좋아서 그런다, 톰 스마트.\""], ["said the chair; or the old gentleman, whichever you like to call him.", "의자가, 아니, 당신들 좋을 대로 부르시오, 노신사가 말했소."], ["He stopped winking though, when Tom spoke, and began grinning like a superannuated monkey.", "톰이 입을 열자 그는 윙크는 멈췄으나, 늙어 빠진 원숭이처럼 히죽거리기 시작했소."]], [["‘“How do you know my name, old nut-cracker face?”", "\"네가 어떻게 내 이름을 알아, 이 호두까기 같은 늙은 얼굴아?\""], ["inquired Tom Smart, rather staggered; though he pretended to carry it off so well.", "톰 스마트가 자못 흔들리면서도, 짐짓 아주 의연한 척하며 물었소."]], [["‘“Come, come, Tom,”", "\"이런, 이런, 톰.\""], ["said the old gentleman, “that’s not the way to address solid Spanish mahogany.", "노신사가 말했소. \"묵직한 스페인산 마호가니한테 그런 식으로 말하는 게 아니지."], ["Damme, you couldn’t treat me with less respect if I was veneered.”", "빌어먹을, 내가 무늬목 베니어였대도 그보다 더 무례하게 굴진 못할 게다.\""], ["When the old gentleman said this, he looked so fierce that Tom began to grow frightened.", "노신사가 이 말을 할 때 어찌나 사납게 노려보던지 톰은 겁이 나기 시작했소."]], [["‘“I didn’t mean to treat you with any disrespect, Sir,”", "\"무례를 범하려던 건 아니었습니다, 어르신.\""], ["said Tom, in a much humbler tone than he had spoken in at first.", "톰이 처음에 말할 때보다 훨씬 겸손한 어조로 말했소."]], [["‘“Well, well,”", "\"음, 음.\""], ["said the old fellow, “perhaps not--perhaps not.", "늙은 양반이 말했소. \"그럴 테지—그럴 테지."], ["Tom--”", "톰—\""]], [["‘“Sir--”", "\"네, 어르신—\""]], [["‘“I know everything about you, Tom; everything.", "\"나는 너에 관해 죄다 안다, 톰. 죄다."], ["You’re very poor, Tom.”", "너는 무척 가난하구나, 톰.\""]], [["‘“I certainly am,”", "\"그건 분명 그렇습니다.\""], ["said Tom Smart.", "톰 스마트가 말했소."], ["“But how came you to know that?”", "\"한데 그걸 어떻게 아셨습니까?\""]], [["‘“Never mind that,”", "\"그건 아무래도 좋아.\""], ["said the old gentleman; “you’re much too fond of punch, Tom.”", "노신사가 말했소. \"너는 펀치를 너무 좋아해, 톰.\""]], [["‘Tom Smart was just on the point of protesting that he hadn’t tasted a drop since his last birthday, but when his eye encountered that of the old gentleman he looked so knowing that Tom blushed, and was silent.", "톰 스마트는 지난 생일 이후로는 한 방울도 입에 댄 적이 없다고 항변하려던 참이었소만, 그의 눈이 노신사의 눈과 마주치자 그 눈이 어찌나 다 안다는 표정이던지, 톰은 얼굴을 붉히고 입을 다물었소."]], [["‘“Tom,”", "\"톰.\""], ["said the old gentleman, “the widow’s a fine woman--remarkably fine woman--eh, Tom?”", "노신사가 말했소. \"그 과부는 참한 여자야—유달리 참한 여자지—안 그러냐, 톰?\""], ["Here the old fellow screwed up his eyes, cocked up one of his wasted little legs, and looked altogether so unpleasantly amorous, that Tom was quite disgusted with the levity of his behaviour-- at his time of life, too!", "여기서 늙은 양반이 두 눈을 찡긋 오므리고 비쩍 마른 자그마한 다리 하나를 까딱 치켜들며, 도무지 그 나이에—그 연배에 말이오!—어찌나 보기 흉하게 색을 밝히는 꼴이던지, 톰은 그 경박한 거동에 진절머리가 났소."]], [["‘“I am her guardian, Tom,”", "\"내가 그 여자의 후견인이다, 톰.\""], ["said the old gentleman.", "노신사가 말했소."]], [["‘“Are you?”", "\"그러십니까?\""], ["inquired Tom Smart.", "톰 스마트가 물었소."]], [["‘“I knew her mother, Tom,”", "\"나는 그 여자의 어미를 알았지, 톰.\""], ["said the old fellow: “and her grandmother.", "늙은 양반이 말했소. \"그리고 그 할미도."], ["She was very fond of me--made me this waistcoat, Tom.”", "그 할미는 나를 무척 아꼈어—내게 이 조끼를 지어 주었지, 톰.\""]], [["‘“Did she?”", "\"그랬습니까?\""], ["said Tom Smart.", "톰 스마트가 말했소."]], [["‘“And these shoes,”", "\"그리고 이 신도.\""], ["said the old fellow, lifting up one of the red cloth mufflers; “but don’t mention it, Tom.", "늙은 양반이 붉은 천 덮개 하나를 들어 올리며 말했소. \"하지만 이건 입 밖에 내지 마라, 톰."], ["I shouldn’t like to have it known that she was so much attached to me.", "그 할미가 나를 그토록 좋아했다는 게 알려지는 건 싫으니까."], ["It might occasion some unpleasantness in the family.”", "집안에 곤란한 일이 생길지도 모르거든.\""], ["When the old rascal said this, he looked so extremely impertinent, that, as Tom Smart afterwards declared, he could have sat upon him without remorse.", "그 늙은 악당이 이 말을 할 때 어찌나 지독히 건방진 꼴이던지, 톰 스마트가 나중에 단언하기를, 그자 위에 가책 없이 털썩 앉아 버릴 수도 있었을 거라 했소."]], [["‘“I have been a great favourite among the women in my time, Tom,”", "\"내가 한창때는 여자들 사이에서 대단한 인기였단다, 톰.\""], ["said the profligate old debauchee; “hundreds of fine women have sat in my lap for hours together.", "그 방탕한 늙은 난봉꾼이 말했소. \"참한 여자들 수백이 내 무릎 위에 몇 시간씩 앉아 있곤 했지."], ["What do you think of that, you dog, eh!”", "그거 어떠냐, 이 녀석아, 응!\""], ["The old gentleman was proceeding to recount some other exploits of his youth, when he was seized with such a violent fit of creaking that he was unable to proceed.", "노신사가 자기 젊은 날의 다른 무용담을 더 늘어놓으려는데, 어찌나 격렬하게 삐걱대는 발작에 사로잡혔던지 더는 말을 잇지 못했소."]], [["‘“Just serves you right, old boy,”", "'쌤통이다, 영감.'"], ["thought Tom Smart; but he didn’t say anything.", "톰 스마트는 속으로 생각했소만, 아무 말도 하지 않았소."]], [["‘“Ah!”", "\"아!\""], ["said the old fellow, “I am a good deal troubled with this now.", "늙은 양반이 말했소. \"요즘은 이게 어지간히 성가셔."], ["I am getting old, Tom, and have lost nearly all my nails.", "나도 늙어 가는구나, 톰, 못이 거의 다 빠졌어."], ["I have had an operation performed, too--a small piece let into my back--and I found it a severe trial, Tom.”", "수술도 한 번 받았다—등에 작은 조각을 끼워 넣는 수술 말이야—그게 어찌나 혹독한 시련이던지, 톰.\""]], [["‘“I dare say you did, Sir,”", "\"그러셨을 겁니다, 어르신.\""], ["said Tom Smart.", "톰 스마트가 말했소."]], [["‘“However,”", "\"하지만.\""], ["said the old gentleman, “that’s not the point.", "노신사가 말했소. \"그건 요점이 아니지."], ["Tom!", "톰!"], ["I want you to marry the widow.”", "나는 네가 그 과부와 결혼했으면 한다.\""]], [["‘“Me, Sir!”", "\"제가요, 어르신!\""], ["said Tom.", "톰이 말했소."]], [["‘“You,”", "\"네가.\""], ["said the old gentleman.", "노신사가 말했소."]], [["‘“Bless your reverend locks,”", "\"고매하신 머리타래에 축복을.\""], ["said Tom (he had a few scattered horse- hairs left)--“bless your reverend locks, she wouldn’t have me.”", "톰이 말했소(그에게도 듬성듬성 말총 몇 가닥이 남아 있었거든)—\"고매하신 머리타래에 축복을, 그 여자는 저 같은 걸 받아 주지 않을 겁니다.\""], ["And Tom sighed involuntarily, as he thought of the bar.", "그러고 톰은 술청을 떠올리며 저도 모르게 한숨을 쉬었소."]], [["‘“Wouldn’t she?”", "\"안 받아 준다고?\""], ["said the old gentleman firmly.", "노신사가 단호하게 말했소."]], [["‘“No, no,”", "\"아니요, 아니요.\""], ["said Tom; “there’s somebody else in the wind.", "톰이 말했소. \"딴 사람이 점찍혀 있단 말입니다."], ["A tall man--a confoundedly tall man--with black whiskers.”", "키 큰 사내—지독하게 키 큰 사내—검은 구레나룻을 한 자가요.\""]], [["‘“Tom,”", "\"톰.\""], ["said the old gentleman; “she will never have him.”", "노신사가 말했소. \"그 여자는 결코 그자를 받아들이지 않아.\""]], [["‘“Won’t she?” said Tom.", "\"안 받아들인다고요?\""], ["“If you stood in the bar, old gentleman, you’d tell another story.”", "톰이 말했소. \"어르신이 그 술청에 서서 보셨다면, 딴소리를 하실 텐데요.\""]], [["‘“Pooh, pooh,”", "\"쳇, 쳇.\""], ["said the old gentleman.", "노신사가 말했소."], ["“I know all about that.”", "\"그것에 관해선 내가 죄다 안다.\""]], [["‘“About what?”", "\"무엇에 관해서요?\""], ["said Tom.", "톰이 말했소."]], [["‘“The kissing behind the door, and all that sort of thing, Tom,”", "\"문 뒤의 입맞춤이며, 그런 따위 죄다 말이야, 톰.\""], ["said the old gentleman.", "노신사가 말했소."], ["And here he gave another impudent look, which made Tom very wroth, because as you all know, gentlemen, to hear an old fellow, who ought to know better, talking about these things, is very unpleasant--nothing more so.", "그러고는 또 한 번 건방진 눈초리를 보냈는데, 그게 톰을 몹시 노엽게 했으니, 여러분도 다들 아시다시피, 마땅히 더 잘 알아야 할 늙은 양반이 그런 일들을 입에 올리는 걸 듣는 건 매우 불쾌한 노릇이오—그보다 더한 노릇도 없지요."]], [["‘“I know all about that, Tom,”", "\"나는 그것에 관해 죄다 안다, 톰.\""], ["said the old gentleman.", "노신사가 말했소."], ["“I have seen it done very often in my time, Tom, between more people than I should like to mention to you; but it never came to anything after all.”", "\"한창때 나는 그런 짓을 무던히도 자주 봤지, 톰, 네게 거명하고 싶지 않은 숱한 사람들 사이에서 말이야. 하지만 결국 아무 결실도 없었어.\""]], [["‘“You must have seen some queer things,”", "\"별별 괴상한 일들을 다 보셨겠군요.\""], ["said Tom, with an inquisitive look.", "톰이 캐묻는 듯한 눈빛으로 말했소."]], [["‘“You may say that, Tom,”", "\"그렇다고 할 수 있지, 톰.\""], ["replied the old fellow, with a very complicated wink.", "늙은 양반이 매우 복잡한 윙크를 하며 대답했소."], ["“I am the last of my family, Tom,”", "\"나는 우리 집안의 마지막 자손이란다, 톰.\""], ["said the old gentleman, with a melancholy sigh.", "노신사가 우울하게 한숨지으며 말했소."]], [["‘“Was it a large one?”", "\"식구가 많았습니까?\""], ["inquired Tom Smart.", "톰 스마트가 물었소."]], [["‘“There were twelve of us, Tom,”", "\"우리는 열둘이었지, 톰.\""], ["said the old gentleman; “fine, straight-backed, handsome fellows as you’d wish to see.", "노신사가 말했소. \"보기 좋게 등이 곧고 잘생긴 녀석들이었어, 누구라도 보고 싶어 할 만큼."], ["None of your modern abortions--all with arms, and with a degree of polish, though I say it that should not, which it would have done your heart good to behold.”", "요즘의 그 기형아들 같은 게 아니라—다들 팔걸이가 있고, 내 입으로 말하긴 뭣하다만, 어찌나 윤이 흐르던지, 그걸 보면 네 마음이 흐뭇했을 게다.\""]], [["‘“And what’s become of the others, Sir?”", "\"그럼 나머지는 어떻게 됐습니까, 어르신?\""], ["asked Tom Smart--", "톰 스마트가 물었소—"]], [["‘The old gentleman applied his elbow to his eye as he replied, “Gone, Tom, gone.", "노신사는 팔꿈치를 눈가에 갖다 대며 대답했소. \"갔지, 톰, 다 갔어."], ["We had hard service, Tom, and they hadn’t all my constitution.", "우리는 고된 일을 겪었고, 톰, 그 녀석들이 다 나만 한 체질을 타고난 건 아니었지."], ["They got rheumatic about the legs and arms, and went into kitchens and other hospitals; and one of ‘em, with long service and hard usage, positively lost his senses--he got so crazy that he was obliged to be burnt.", "다리며 팔에 류머티즘이 와서, 부엌이며 다른 병원들로 들어갔어. 그리고 그중 하나는, 오래 부려지고 험하게 다뤄진 끝에, 정말로 제정신을 잃었지—어찌나 미쳤던지 태워 버릴 수밖에 없었어."], ["Shocking thing that, Tom.”", "그거 끔찍한 일이었지, 톰.\""]], [["‘“Dreadful!”", "\"무서운 일이군요!\""], ["said Tom Smart.", "톰 스마트가 말했소."]], [["‘The old fellow paused for a few minutes, apparently struggling with his feelings of emotion, and then said--", "늙은 양반은 잠시 동안, 북받치는 감정과 싸우는 듯, 말을 멈췄다가 이윽고 입을 열었소—"]], [["‘“However, Tom, I am wandering from the point.", "\"하지만, 톰, 내가 요점에서 벗어나고 있구나."], ["This tall man, Tom, is a rascally adventurer.", "이 키 큰 사내는, 톰, 악당 같은 협잡꾼이야."], ["The moment he married the widow, he would sell off all the furniture, and run away.", "그자는 과부와 결혼하는 순간, 가구를 죄다 팔아 치우고 달아날 거다."], ["What would be the consequence?", "그 결과가 어떻게 되겠느냐?"], ["She would be deserted and reduced to ruin, and I should catch my death of cold in some broker’s shop.”", "그 여자는 버림받아 파산에 이를 것이고, 나는 어느 고물상 가게에서 감기에 걸려 죽고 말겠지.\""]], [["‘“Yes, but--”", "\"네, 하지만—\""]], [["‘“Don’t interrupt me,”", "\"내 말을 끊지 마라.\""], ["said the old gentleman.", "노신사가 말했소."], ["“Of you, Tom, I entertain a very different opinion; for I well know that if you once settled yourself in a public-house, you would never leave it, as long as there was anything to drink within its walls.”", "\"너에 대해서는, 톰, 나는 아주 다른 견해를 품고 있다. 네가 일단 어느 술집에 자리를 잡으면, 그 벽 안에 마실 거리가 있는 한 결코 거기를 떠나지 않으리라는 걸 잘 아니까.\""]], [["‘“I am very much obliged to you for your good opinion, Sir,”", "\"좋게 봐 주셔서 대단히 감사합니다, 어르신.\""], ["said Tom Smart.", "톰 스마트가 말했소."]], [["‘“Therefore,”", "\"그러니,\""], ["resumed the old gentleman, in a dictatorial tone, “you shall have her, and he shall not.”", "노신사가 명령조로 말을 이었소. \"그 여자는 네가 차지하고, 그자는 못 차지하게 될 게다.\""]], [["‘“What is to prevent it?”", "\"무엇으로 그걸 막습니까?\""], ["said Tom Smart eagerly.", "톰 스마트가 다급히 말했소."]], [["‘“This disclosure,”", "\"이 폭로지.\""], ["replied the old gentleman; “he is already married.”", "노신사가 대답했소. \"그자는 이미 결혼한 몸이다.\""]], [["‘“How can I prove it?”", "\"그걸 어떻게 증명합니까?\""], ["said Tom, starting half out of bed.", "톰이 침대에서 반쯤 벌떡 일어나며 말했소."]], [["‘The old gentleman untucked his arm from his side, and having pointed to one of the oaken presses, immediately replaced it, in its old position.", "노신사는 옆구리에서 팔을 빼내, 떡갈나무 옷장 하나를 가리키고는 곧바로 팔을 본디 자리로 되돌려 놓았소."]], [["‘“He little thinks,”", "\"그자는 꿈에도 모르지,\""], ["said the old gentleman, “that in the right-hand pocket of a pair of trousers in that press, he has left a letter, entreating him to return to his disconsolate wife, with six--mark me, Tom--six babes, and all of them small ones.”", "노신사가 말했소. \"저 옷장 속에 있는 바지 한 벌의 오른쪽 주머니에, 자기더러 비탄에 잠긴 아내에게 돌아와 달라고 애원하는 편지를 한 통 두고 왔다는 걸 말이야—여섯이나 되는—명심해라, 톰—여섯이나 되는 갓난것들이 있는데, 죄다 어린것들이지.\""]], [["‘As the old gentleman solemnly uttered these words, his features grew less and less distinct, and his figure more shadowy.", "노신사가 이 말을 엄숙하게 내뱉는 동안, 그의 이목구비는 점점 흐릿해지고 그의 형체는 점점 그림자처럼 변해 갔소."], ["A film came over Tom Smart’s eyes.", "톰 스마트의 눈에 얇은 막이 끼었소."], ["The old man seemed gradually blending into the chair, the damask waistcoat to resolve into a cushion, the red slippers to shrink into little red cloth bags.", "노인은 차츰 의자 속으로 녹아들고, 다마스크 조끼는 방석으로 풀려나고, 붉은 슬리퍼는 작은 붉은 천 주머니로 쪼그라드는 듯했소."], ["The light faded gently away, and Tom Smart fell back on his pillow, and dropped asleep.", "불빛이 부드럽게 사위어 가고, 톰 스마트는 베개에 도로 쓰러져 잠에 빠져들었소."]], [["‘Morning aroused Tom from the lethargic slumber, into which he had fallen on the disappearance of the old man.", "아침이 되자 톰은, 노인이 사라진 뒤 빠져들었던 혼곤한 잠에서 깨어났소."], ["He sat up in bed, and for some minutes vainly endeavoured to recall the events of the preceding night.", "그는 침대에 일어나 앉아, 몇 분 동안 전날 밤의 일들을 떠올리려 헛되이 애썼소."], ["Suddenly they rushed upon him.", "그러다 별안간 그 일들이 그에게 와락 밀려들었소."], ["He looked at the chair; it was a fantastic and grim-looking piece of furniture, certainly, but it must have been a remarkably ingenious and lively imagination, that could have discovered any resemblance between it and an old man.", "그는 의자를 바라보았소—과연 환상적이고 음산해 보이는 가구이긴 했소만, 그것과 늙은이 사이에서 어떤 닮은 점을 찾아낼 수 있었다면 그건 무던히도 기발하고 발랄한 상상력이었음에 틀림없었소."]], [["‘“How are you, old boy?”", "\"잘 잤나, 영감?\""], ["said Tom.", "톰이 말했소."], ["He was bolder in the daylight--most men are.", "그는 대낮엔 더 대담해졌으니—사내들 대부분이 그렇소."]], [["‘The chair remained motionless, and spoke not a word.", "의자는 꼼짝도 않고, 한마디도 하지 않았소."]], [["‘“Miserable morning,”", "\"고약한 아침이군.\""], ["said Tom. No.", "톰이 말했소."], ["The chair would not be drawn into conversation.", "천만에. 의자는 대화에 끌려들 생각이 없었소."]], [["‘“Which press did you point to?--you can tell me that,”", "\"어느 옷장을 가리켰지?—그건 말해 줄 수 있겠지.\""], ["said Tom.", "톰이 말했소."], ["Devil a word, gentlemen, the chair would say.", "한마디도, 여러분, 의자는 입을 떼려 들지 않았소."]], [["‘“It’s not much trouble to open it, anyhow,”", "\"어쨌든 열어 보는 거야 별 수고도 아니지.\""], ["said Tom, getting out of bed very deliberately.", "톰이 자못 신중하게 침대에서 빠져나오며 말했소."], ["He walked up to one of the presses.", "그는 옷장 하나로 걸어갔소."], ["The key was in the lock; he turned it, and opened the door.", "열쇠가 자물쇠에 꽂혀 있었소. 그는 그것을 돌려 문을 열었소."], ["There was a pair of trousers there.", "거기 바지 한 벌이 있었소."], ["He put his hand into the pocket, and drew forth the identical letter the old gentleman had described!", "그는 주머니에 손을 넣어, 노신사가 묘사한 바로 그 편지를 끄집어냈소!"]], [["‘“Queer sort of thing, this,”", "\"이거 참 묘한 일이군.\""], ["said Tom Smart, looking first at the chair and then at the press, and then at the letter, and then at the chair again.", "톰 스마트가 처음엔 의자를, 다음엔 옷장을, 다음엔 편지를, 그러고 다시 의자를 바라보며 말했소."], ["“Very queer,”", "\"참 묘해.\""], ["said Tom.", "톰이 말했소."], ["But, as there was nothing in either, to lessen the queerness, he thought he might as well dress himself, and settle the tall man’s business at once--just to put him out of his misery.", "그러나 어느 쪽에도 그 묘함을 덜어 줄 만한 게 없었던지라, 그는 차라리 옷을 차려입고 키 큰 사내의 일을 당장 처리해 버리는 편이 낫겠다고 생각했소—그자를 비참한 처지에서 벗어나게 해 줄 겸 말이오."]], [["‘Tom surveyed the rooms he passed through, on his way downstairs, with the scrutinising eye of a landlord; thinking it not impossible, that before long, they and their contents would be his property.", "톰은 아래층으로 내려가는 길에 지나치는 방들을, 마치 주인의 매서운 눈으로 찬찬히 살폈소. 머지않아 그 방들과 그 안의 물건들이 제 재산이 되는 게 불가능하지만은 않다고 여기면서 말이오."], ["The tall man was standing in the snug little bar, with his hands behind him, quite at home.", "키 큰 사내는 아담한 조그만 술청에 뒷짐을 진 채, 아주 제 집인 양 서 있었소."], ["He grinned vacantly at Tom.", "그는 톰을 향해 멍하니 히죽 웃었소."], ["A casual observer might have supposed he did it, only to show his white teeth; but Tom Smart thought that a consciousness of triumph was passing through the place where the tall man’s mind would have been, if he had had any.", "무심한 구경꾼이라면 그가 그저 흰 이를 보이려고 그러는 거라 여겼을지 모르오만, 톰 스마트는 그 키 큰 사내에게 정신이라는 게 있었다면 들어앉았을 자리쯤에 승리감이 스쳐 가고 있다고 생각했소."], ["Tom laughed in his face; and summoned the landlady.", "톰은 그자의 면전에 대고 웃고는 안주인을 불렀소."]], [["‘“Good-morning ma’am,”", "\"안녕하십니까, 마님.\""], ["said Tom Smart, closing the door of the little parlour as the widow entered.", "톰 스마트가 과부가 들어오자 조그만 거실의 문을 닫으며 말했소."]], [["‘“Good-morning, Sir,”", "\"안녕하세요, 손님.\""], ["said the widow.", "과부가 말했소."], ["“What will you take for breakfast, sir?”", "\"아침으로 무얼 드시겠어요?\""]], [["‘Tom was thinking how he should open the case, so he made no answer.", "톰은 어떻게 이야기를 꺼낼까 궁리하느라 아무 대답도 하지 않았소."]], [["‘“There’s a very nice ham,”", "\"아주 맛 좋은 햄이 있고요.\""], ["said the widow, “and a beautiful cold larded fowl.", "과부가 말했소. \"기름을 잘 넣은 멋진 냉(冷) 닭고기도 있답니다."], ["Shall I send ‘em in, Sir?”", "들여올까요, 손님?\""]], [["‘These words roused Tom from his reflections.", "이 말이 톰을 상념에서 일깨웠소."], ["His admiration of the widow increased as she spoke.", "그녀가 말할수록 그녀에 대한 그의 감탄은 커져 갔소."], ["Thoughtful creature!", "사려 깊은 사람이로다!"], ["Comfortable provider!", "든든한 살림꾼이로다!"]], [["‘“Who is that gentleman in the bar, ma’am?”", "\"술청에 있는 저 양반은 누굽니까, 마님?\""], ["inquired Tom.", "톰이 물었소."]], [["‘“His name is Jinkins, Sir,”", "\"그분 성함은 징킨스예요, 손님.\""], ["said the widow, slightly blushing.", "과부가 살짝 얼굴을 붉히며 말했소."]], [["‘“He’s a tall man,”", "\"키가 큰 양반이군요.\""], ["said Tom.", "톰이 말했소."]], [["‘“He is a very fine man, Sir,”", "\"아주 훤칠한 분이세요, 손님.\""], ["replied the widow, “and a very nice gentleman.”", "과부가 대답했소. \"그리고 아주 멋진 신사분이고요.\""]], [["‘“Ah!”", "\"아!\""], ["said Tom.", "톰이 말했소."]], [["‘“Is there anything more you want, Sir?”", "\"더 필요하신 거라도 있으세요, 손님?\""], ["inquired the widow, rather puzzled by Tom’s manner.", "톰의 태도에 자못 어리둥절해진 과부가 물었소."]], [["‘“Why, yes,” said Tom.", "\"음, 그렇습니다.\""], ["“My dear ma’am, will you have the kindness to sit down for one moment?”", "톰이 말했소. \"친애하는 마님, 잠깐만 앉아 주시는 친절을 베풀어 주시겠습니까?\""]], [["‘The widow looked much amazed, but she sat down, and Tom sat down too, close beside her.", "과부는 몹시 놀란 기색이었소만, 그래도 자리에 앉았고, 톰도 그녀 바로 곁에 바싹 앉았소."], ["I don’t know how it happened, gentlemen--indeed my uncle used to tell me that Tom Smart said he didn’t know how it happened either--but somehow or other the palm of Tom’s hand fell upon the back of the widow’s hand, and remained there while he spoke.", "그게 어떻게 된 일인지 나는 모르오, 여러분—실은 우리 삼촌이 늘 내게 들려주기를, 톰 스마트도 그게 어떻게 된 일인지 자기도 모르겠다고 했다 하오—그런데 어찌어찌하여 톰의 손바닥이 과부의 손등 위에 떨어졌고, 그가 말하는 내내 거기 머물러 있었소."]], [["‘“My dear ma’am,”", "\"친애하는 마님.\""], ["said Tom Smart--he had always a great notion of committing the amiable--“my dear ma’am, you deserve a very excellent husband--you do indeed.”", "톰 스마트가 말했소—그는 늘 상냥하게 구는 데 거창한 포부가 있었거든—\"친애하는 마님, 당신은 아주 훌륭한 남편을 맞을 자격이 있습니다—정말로 그렇습니다.\""]], [["‘“Lor, Sir!”", "\"어머나, 손님!\""], ["said the widow--as well she might; Tom’s mode of commencing the conversation being rather unusual, not to say startling; the fact of his never having set eyes upon her before the previous night being taken into consideration.", "과부가 말했소—그럴 만도 했지요. 톰이 대화를 시작하는 방식이 자못 별나다 못해 깜짝 놀랄 만했으니, 그가 그 전날 밤 이전엔 그녀에게 눈길 한 번 준 적이 없다는 사실을 감안하면 말이오."], ["“Lor, Sir!”", "\"어머나, 손님!\""]], [["‘“I scorn to flatter, my dear ma’am,”", "\"나는 아첨 따위는 경멸합니다, 친애하는 마님.\""], ["said Tom Smart.", "톰 스마트가 말했소."], ["“You deserve a very admirable husband, and whoever he is, he’ll be a very lucky man.”", "\"당신은 아주 훌륭한 남편을 맞을 자격이 있고, 그게 누구든 그는 아주 운 좋은 사내일 겁니다.\""], ["As Tom said this, his eye involuntarily wandered from the widow’s face to the comfort around him.", "톰이 이 말을 하면서, 그의 눈은 저도 모르게 과부의 얼굴에서 그를 둘러싼 안락함으로 옮겨 갔소."]], [["‘The widow looked more puzzled than ever, and made an effort to rise.", "과부는 어느 때보다 더 어리둥절한 얼굴로 일어서려 했소."], ["Tom gently pressed her hand, as if to detain her, and she kept her seat.", "톰은 그녀를 붙들려는 듯 그녀의 손을 가만히 눌렀고, 그녀는 자리에 그대로 앉아 있었소."], ["Widows, gentlemen, are not usually timorous, as my uncle used to say.", "과부란 말이오, 여러분, 대개 겁이 많지 않다고, 우리 삼촌이 늘 말씀하시곤 했소."]], [["‘“I am sure I am very much obliged to you, Sir, for your good opinion,”", "\"좋게 봐 주셔서 정말 대단히 감사드려요, 손님.\" 풍만한 안주인이 반쯤 웃으며 말했소."], ["said the buxom landlady, half laughing; “and if ever I marry again--”", "\"그리고 만에 하나 제가 다시 결혼하게 된다면—\""]], [["’”If,”", "\"만에 하나라니요.\""], ["said Tom Smart, looking very shrewdly out of the right-hand corner of his left eye.", "톰 스마트가 왼쪽 눈 오른쪽 귀퉁이로 자못 빈틈없이 흘겨보며 말했소."], ["“If--”", "\"만에 하나라니요—\""]], [["“Well,”", "\"그래요.\""], ["said the widow, laughing outright this time, “when I do, I hope I shall have as good a husband as you describe.”", "이번엔 과부가 대놓고 웃으며 말했소. \"제가 그리되거든, 손님 말씀처럼 그렇게 좋은 남편을 만났으면 해요.\""]], [["‘“Jinkins, to wit,”", "\"이를테면 징킨스 말이군요.\""], ["said Tom.", "톰이 말했소."]], [["‘“Lor, sir!”", "\"어머나, 손님!\""], ["exclaimed the widow.", "과부가 소리쳤소."]], [["‘“Oh, don’t tell me,”", "\"아, 내게 말 마십시오.\""], ["said Tom, “I know him.”", "톰이 말했소. \"나는 그자를 압니다.\""]], [["‘“I am sure nobody who knows him, knows anything bad of him,”", "\"그분을 아는 사람치고, 그분에 대해 나쁜 걸 아는 이는 아무도 없을 거예요.\""], ["said the widow, bridling up at the mysterious air with which Tom had spoken.", "톰이 말한 그 알 수 없는 태도에 발끈하며 과부가 말했소."]], [["‘“Hem!”", "\"흠!\""], ["said Tom Smart.", "톰 스마트가 말했소."]], [["‘The widow began to think it was high time to cry, so she took out her handkerchief, and inquired whether Tom wished to insult her, whether he thought it like a gentleman to take away the character of another gentleman behind his back, why, if he had got anything to say, he didn’t say it to the man, like a man, instead of terrifying a poor weak woman in that way; and so forth.", "과부는 이제 슬슬 울 때가 됐다고 여겨, 손수건을 꺼내 들고는, 톰이 자기를 모욕하려는 거냐고, 다른 신사의 인격을 그 등 뒤에서 헐뜯는 게 신사다운 짓이라고 여기느냐고, 아니 할 말이 있으면 사내답게 그 사내에게 직접 말할 일이지 어째서 이런 식으로 가엾고 연약한 여자를 겁주느냐고 따져 물었소, 등등 말이오."]], [["‘“I’ll say it to him fast enough,”", "\"그자에게는 얼마든지 직접 말하지요.\""], ["said Tom, “only I want you to hear it first.”", "톰이 말했소. \"다만 당신이 먼저 들어 주었으면 해서요.\""]], [["‘“What is it?”", "\"그게 뭔데요?\""], ["inquired the widow, looking intently in Tom’s countenance.", "과부가 톰의 얼굴을 뚫어지게 들여다보며 물었소."]], [["‘“I’ll astonish you,”", "\"당신을 깜짝 놀라게 해 드리지요.\""], ["said Tom, putting his hand in his pocket.", "톰이 주머니에 손을 넣으며 말했소."]], [["‘“If it is, that he wants money,”", "\"만약 그게, 그분이 돈을 원한다는 얘기라면,\""], ["said the widow, “I know that already, and you needn’t trouble yourself.”", "과부가 말했소. \"그건 이미 알고 있으니, 수고하실 것 없어요.\""], ["‘“Pooh, nonsense, that’s nothing,”", "\"쳇, 당치 않아요, 그런 건 아무것도 아닙니다.\""], ["said Tom Smart, “I want money.", "톰 스마트가 말했소. \"돈이야 나도 원하는 걸요."], ["‘Tain’t that.”", "그게 아니라요.\""]], [["‘“Oh, dear, what can it be?”", "\"어머, 저런, 대체 뭘까요?\""], ["exclaimed the poor widow.", "가엾은 과부가 소리쳤소."]], [["‘“Don’t be frightened,”", "\"놀라지 마십시오.\""], ["said Tom Smart.", "톰 스마트가 말했소."], ["He slowly drew forth the letter, and unfolded it.", "그는 천천히 편지를 끄집어내 펼쳤소."], ["“You won’t scream?”", "\"비명은 안 지르시겠지요?\""], ["said Tom doubtfully.", "톰이 미심쩍은 듯 말했소."]], [["‘“No, no,”", "\"네, 네.\""], ["replied the widow; “let me see it.”", "과부가 대답했소. \"어디 보여 주세요.\""]], [["‘“You won’t go fainting away, or any of that nonsense?”", "\"기절하거나, 뭐 그런 부질없는 짓은 안 하시겠지요?\""], ["said Tom.", "톰이 말했소."]], [["‘“No, no,”", "\"네, 네.\""], ["returned the widow hastily.", "과부가 다급히 대답했소."]], [["‘“And don’t run out, and blow him up,”", "\"그리고 뛰쳐나가서 그자에게 퍼붓지도 마십시오.\""], ["said Tom; “because I’ll do all that for you.", "톰이 말했소. \"그건 내가 당신을 위해 다 해 드릴 테니까요."], ["You had better not exert yourself.”", "당신은 기력을 빼지 않는 게 좋습니다.\""]], [["‘“Well, well,”", "\"알았어요, 알았어요.\""], ["said the widow, “let me see it.”", "과부가 말했소. \"어디 보여 주세요.\""]], [["‘“I will,”", "\"그러지요.\""], ["replied Tom Smart; and, with these words, he placed the letter in the widow’s hand.", "톰 스마트가 대답하고, 이 말과 함께 편지를 과부의 손에 쥐여 주었소."]], [["‘Gentlemen, I have heard my uncle say, that Tom Smart said the widow’s lamentations when she heard the disclosure would have pierced a heart of stone.", "여러분, 우리 삼촌이 말씀하시기를, 톰 스마트가 그러더랍니다, 그 폭로를 듣고 과부가 터뜨린 통곡은 돌 같은 심장도 꿰뚫었을 거라고요."], ["Tom was certainly very tender-hearted, but they pierced his, to the very core.", "톰은 확실히 마음이 무척 여린 사람이었소만, 그 통곡은 그의 심장을 한복판까지 꿰뚫었소."], ["The widow rocked herself to and fro, and wrung her hands.", "과부는 몸을 앞뒤로 흔들며 두 손을 비틀어 짰소."]], [["‘“Oh, the deception and villainy of the man!”", "\"오, 그 사람의 기만과 악행이라니!\""], ["said the widow.", "과부가 말했소."]], [["‘“Frightful, my dear ma’am; but compose yourself,”", "\"끔찍하지요, 친애하는 마님. 하지만 마음을 가라앉히십시오.\""], ["said Tom Smart.", "톰 스마트가 말했소."]], [["‘“Oh, I can’t compose myself,”", "\"오, 마음을 가라앉힐 수가 없어요.\""], ["shrieked the widow.", "과부가 비명을 질렀소."], ["“I shall never find anyone else I can love so much!”", "\"이만큼 사랑할 수 있는 사람을 다시는 못 찾을 거예요!\""]], [["‘“Oh, yes you will, my dear soul,”", "\"오, 아닙니다, 찾으실 겁니다, 친애하는 분이여.\""], ["said Tom Smart, letting fall a shower of the largest-sized tears, in pity for the widow’s misfortunes.", "톰 스마트가, 과부의 불행을 가엾이 여겨 더없이 큼직한 눈물을 비 오듯 떨구며 말했소."], ["Tom Smart, in the energy of his compassion, had put his arm round the widow’s waist; and the widow, in a passion of grief, had clasped Tom’s hand.", "톰 스마트는 그 연민의 격정 속에 과부의 허리에 팔을 둘렀고, 과부는 비탄의 격정 속에 톰의 손을 꽉 움켜쥐었소."], ["She looked up in Tom’s face, and smiled through her tears.", "그녀는 톰의 얼굴을 올려다보며 눈물 속에 미소 지었소."], ["Tom looked down in hers, and smiled through his.", "톰은 그녀의 얼굴을 내려다보며 눈물 속에 미소 지었소."]], [["‘I never could find out, gentlemen, whether Tom did or did not kiss the widow at that particular moment.", "나는 도무지 알아낼 수가 없었소, 여러분, 그 특별한 순간에 톰이 과부에게 입을 맞추었는지 안 맞추었는지 말이오."], ["He used to tell my uncle he didn’t, but I have my doubts about it.", "톰은 우리 삼촌에게 안 맞추었다고 했다는데, 나는 그게 영 미심쩍소."], ["Between ourselves, gentlemen, I rather think he did.", "우리끼리 얘기지만, 여러분, 나는 그가 맞춘 쪽이라고 짐작하오."]], [["‘At all events, Tom kicked the very tall man out at the front door half an hour later, and married the widow a month after.", "여하튼, 톰은 반 시간 뒤 그 아주 키 큰 사내를 현관문 밖으로 걷어차 냈고, 한 달 뒤 과부와 결혼했소."], ["And he used to drive about the country, with the clay-coloured gig with the red wheels, and the vixenish mare with the fast pace, till he gave up business many years afterwards, and went to France with his wife; and then the old house was pulled down.’", "그리고 그는 붉은 바퀴를 단 진흙빛 이륜마차와 빨리 달리는 성깔 사나운 암말을 몰고 시골을 돌아다니다가, 여러 해 뒤 사업을 접고 아내와 함께 프랑스로 갔소. 그리고 그제야 그 낡은 집은 헐렸소.'"]], [["‘Will you allow me to ask you,’ said the inquisitive old gentleman, ‘what became of the chair?’", "\"한 가지 여쭤도 되겠소이까.\" 캐묻기 좋아하는 노신사가 말했다. \"그 의자는 어떻게 됐소?\""]], [["‘Why,’ replied the one-eyed bagman, ‘it was observed to creak very much on the day of the wedding; but Tom Smart couldn’t say for certain whether it was with pleasure or bodily infirmity. He rather thought it was the latter, though, for it never spoke afterwards.’", "\"음.\" 외눈의 외판원이 대답했다. \"결혼식 날 그게 몹시 삐걱대더라는 게 사람들 눈에 띄었지요. 그렇지만 톰 스마트는 그게 기뻐서였는지 몸이 성치 않아서였는지는 확실히 말할 수 없었소. 그래도 그는 후자 쪽이라고 짐작했소, 그 뒤로 의자가 다시는 입을 떼지 않았으니까요.\""]], [["‘Everybody believed the story, didn’t they?’ said the dirty-faced man, refilling his pipe.", "\"다들 그 이야기를 믿었겠지요?\" 더러운 얼굴의 사내가 파이프를 다시 채우며 말했다."]], [["‘Except Tom’s enemies,’ replied the bagman.", "\"톰의 원수들만 빼고요.\" 외판원이 대답했다."], ["‘Some of ‘em said Tom invented it altogether; and others said he was drunk and fancied it, and got hold of the wrong trousers by mistake before he went to bed.", "\"더러는 톰이 그걸 통째로 지어낸 거라 했고, 또 더러는 톰이 취해서 헛것을 본 데다, 잠자리에 들기 전 실수로 엉뚱한 바지를 집어 든 거라 했지요."], ["But nobody ever minded what they said.’", "하지만 그자들이 하는 말 따위에 신경 쓰는 사람은 아무도 없었소.\""]], [["‘Tom Smart said it was all true?’", "\"톰 스마트는 그게 다 참말이라고 했고요?\""]], [["‘Every word.’", "\"한마디 한마디 다요.\""]], [["‘And your uncle?’", "\"그럼 당신 삼촌은요?\""]], [["‘Every letter.’", "\"글자 하나하나 다요.\""]], [["‘They must have been very nice men, both of ‘em,’ said the dirty-faced man.", "\"둘 다 무척 좋은 사람들이었겠구려.\" 더러운 얼굴의 사내가 말했다."]], [["‘Yes, they were,’ replied the bagman; ‘very nice men indeed!’", "\"그럼요, 그랬지요.\" 외판원이 대답했다. \"정말이지 무척 좋은 사람들이었소!\""]], [["CHAPTER XV. IN WHICH IS GIVEN A FAITHFUL PORTRAITURE OF TWO DISTINGUISHED PERSONS; AND AN ACCURATE DESCRIPTION OF A PUBLIC BREAKFAST IN THEIR HOUSE AND GROUNDS: WHICH PUBLIC BREAKFAST LEADS TO THE RECOGNITION OF AN OLD ACQUAINTANCE, AND THE COMMENCEMENT OF ANOTHER CHAPTER\nMr.", "픽윅 씨의 양심은 피코크에 있는 친구들을 요사이 소홀히 한 일로 그를 다소간 책망하고 있었다."], ["Pickwick’s conscience had been somewhat reproaching him for his recent neglect of his friends at the Peacock; and he was just on the point of walking forth in quest of them, on the third morning after the election had terminated, when his faithful valet put into his hand a card, on which was engraved the following inscription:--", "선거가 끝난 지 사흘째 되는 날 아침, 그가 막 친구들을 찾아 나서려던 참에, 충직한 시종이 그의 손에 명함 한 장을 쥐어 주었다. 거기에는 다음과 같은 문구가 새겨져 있었다.—"]], [["Mrs. Leo Hunter THE DEN.", "리오 헌터 부인 디 덴."], ["EATANSWILL.", "이턴스윌."]], [["‘Person’s a-waitin’,’ said Sam, epigrammatically.", "\"손님이 기다리십니다.\" 샘이 경구처럼 말했다."]], [["‘Does the person want me, Sam?’ inquired Mr.", "\"그 손님이 나를 보러 온 거냐, 샘?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘He wants you partickler; and no one else ‘ll do, as the devil’s private secretary said ven he fetched avay Doctor Faustus,’ replied Mr.", "\"각별히 나리를 찾으십니다. 다른 사람으론 안 된답니다, 악마의 개인 비서가 파우스트 박사를 데리러 왔을 때 한 말마따나요.\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘He.", "\"그라니."], ["Is it a gentleman?’ said Mr.", "신사분이냐?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘A wery good imitation o’ one, if it ain’t,’ replied Mr.", "\"신사가 아니라면, 신사 흉내는 아주 그럴듯하게 내는 양반입죠.\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘But this is a lady’s card,’ said Mr.", "\"하지만 이건 부인의 명함인데.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Given me by a gen’l’m’n, howsoever,’ replied Sam, ‘and he’s a-waitin’ in the drawing-room--said he’d rather wait all day, than not see you.’", "\"그래도 신사분이 제게 건네주셨습니다요.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"지금 응접실에서 기다리고 계세요—나리를 못 뵈느니 차라리 온종일 기다리겠다고 하시던데요.\""]], [["Mr.", "이 결연한 태도를 듣고 픽윅 씨는 응접실로 내려갔다."], ["Pickwick, on hearing this determination, descended to the drawing- room, where sat a grave man, who started up on his entrance, and said, with an air of profound respect:--", "거기에는 근엄한 사내가 앉아 있다가, 그가 들어서자 벌떡 일어나 깊은 경의를 담은 태도로 말했다.—"]], [["‘Mr. Pickwick, I presume?’", "\"픽윅 씨 되시겠지요?\""]], [["‘The same.’", "\"그렇습니다.\""]], [["‘Allow me, Sir, the honour of grasping your hand.", "\"선생, 선생의 손을 잡는 영광을 허락해 주십시오."], ["Permit me, Sir, to shake it,’ said the grave man.", "부디 악수하게 해 주십시오.\" 근엄한 사내가 말했다."]], [["‘Certainly,’ said Mr. Pickwick.", "\"물론입니다.\""], ["The stranger shook the extended hand, and then continued--", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. 낯선 이는 내민 손을 잡아 흔들고는 말을 이었다.—"]], [["‘We have heard of your fame, sir.", "\"선생의 명성을 익히 들었습니다."], ["The noise of your antiquarian discussion has reached the ears of Mrs.", "선생의 고고학 토론에 관한 소문이 리오 헌터 부인의 귀에까지 들어갔지요—제 아내입니다, 선생."], ["Leo Hunter--my wife, sir; I am Mr.", "저는 리오 헌터입니다.\""], ["Leo Hunter’--the stranger paused, as if he expected that Mr. Pickwick would be overcome by the disclosure; but seeing that he remained perfectly calm, proceeded--", "낯선 이는 마치 픽윅 씨가 이 사실에 압도되리라 기대하는 듯 잠시 말을 멈추었으나, 그가 더없이 침착하게 있는 것을 보고는 말을 계속했다.—"]], [["‘My wife, sir--Mrs. Leo Hunter--is proud to number among her acquaintance all those who have rendered themselves celebrated by their works and talents.", "\"제 아내, 선생—리오 헌터 부인—은 자신의 작품과 재능으로 명성을 떨친 모든 이들을 지인으로 두는 것을 자랑으로 여깁니다."], ["Permit me, sir, to place in a conspicuous part of the list the name of Mr. Pickwick, and his brother-members of the club that derives its name from him.’", "선생, 그 명단의 잘 보이는 자리에 픽윅 씨와, 그분의 이름을 딴 클럽의 동료 회원들의 이름을 올리도록 허락해 주십시오.\""]], [["‘I shall be extremely happy to make the acquaintance of such a lady, sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"그런 부인과 가까이 지내게 된다면 더없이 기쁘겠습니다, 선생.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘You shall make it, sir,’ said the grave man.", "\"꼭 그리 되실 겁니다, 선생.\" 근엄한 사내가 말했다."], ["‘To-morrow morning, sir, we give a public breakfast--a fete champetre--to a great number of those who have rendered themselves celebrated by their works and talents.", "\"내일 아침에, 선생, 저희가 공개 조찬을—야외 연회를—엽니다. 자신의 작품과 재능으로 명성을 떨친 수많은 이들을 위해서요."], ["Permit Mrs. Leo Hunter, Sir, to have the gratification of seeing you at the Den.’", "리오 헌터 부인이 디 덴에서 선생을 뵙는 기쁨을 누리게 해 주십시오.\""]], [["‘With great pleasure,’ replied Mr.", "\"기꺼이 그러겠습니다.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘Mrs. Leo Hunter has many of these breakfasts, Sir,’ resumed the new acquaintance--‘“feasts of reason,”", "\"리오 헌터 부인은 이런 조찬을 여러 번 엽니다, 선생.\""], ["sir, “and flows of soul,”", "새 지인이 말을 이었다."], ["as somebody who wrote a sonnet to Mrs.", "\"'이성의 향연'이지요, 선생, '그리고 영혼의 흐름'이고요."], ["Leo Hunter on her breakfasts, feelingly and originally observed.’", "리오 헌터 부인의 조찬에 부치는 소네트를 쓴 어느 분이 절절하고도 독창적으로 표현한 말입니다.\""]], [["‘Was he celebrated for his works and talents?’ inquired Mr.", "\"그분도 작품과 재능으로 명성을 떨친 분입니까?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘He was Sir,’ replied the grave man, ‘all Mrs.", "\"그렇습니다, 선생.\" 근엄한 사내가 대답했다."], ["Leo Hunter’s acquaintances are; it is her ambition, sir, to have no other acquaintance.’", "\"리오 헌터 부인의 지인은 모두 그렇습니다. 다른 부류의 지인은 두지 않는 것이 부인의 포부지요, 선생.\""]], [["‘It is a very noble ambition,’ said Mr.", "\"참으로 고결한 포부로군요.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘When I inform Mrs. Leo Hunter, that that remark fell from your lips, sir, she will indeed be proud,’ said the grave man.", "\"리오 헌터 부인에게 그 말씀이 선생의 입에서 나왔다고 전하면, 부인은 정말로 자랑스러워할 겁니다, 선생.\""], ["‘You have a gentleman in your train, who has produced some beautiful little poems, I think, sir.’", "근엄한 사내가 말했다. \"선생의 일행 중에 아름다운 소품 시들을 지어 낸 신사분이 한 분 계신 걸로 압니다만, 선생.\""]], [["‘My friend Mr. Snodgrass has a great taste for poetry,’ replied Mr.", "\"제 친구 스노드그래스 씨가 시에 대단한 취향을 지니고 있지요.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘So has Mrs. Leo Hunter, Sir.", "\"리오 헌터 부인도 그렇습니다, 선생."], ["She dotes on poetry, sir.", "부인은 시를 끔찍이 좋아합니다, 선생."], ["She adores it; I may say that her whole soul and mind are wound up, and entwined with it.", "시를 흠모하지요. 부인의 온 영혼과 정신이 시로 칭칭 감겨 얽혀 있다 해도 좋을 정도입니다."], ["She has produced some delightful pieces, herself, sir.", "부인 자신도 몇 편의 멋진 작품을 지었습니다, 선생."], ["You may have met with her “Ode to an Expiring Frog,” sir.’", "부인의 '죽어 가는 개구리에게 부치는 송시'를 접해 보셨을지도 모르겠군요, 선생.\""]], [["‘I don’t think I have,’ said Mr.", "\"아마 못 본 것 같습니다.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘You astonish me, Sir,’ said Mr. Leo Hunter.", "\"놀랍군요, 선생.\""], ["‘It created an immense sensation.", "리오 헌터 씨가 말했다."], ["It was signed with an “L”", "\"엄청난 반향을 일으켰는데요."], ["and eight stars, and appeared originally in a lady’s magazine.", "'L'과 별 여덟 개로 서명되어, 처음에는 어느 부인 잡지에 실렸지요."], ["It commenced--", "이렇게 시작합니다.—"]], [["‘“Can I view thee panting, lying On thy stomach, without sighing; Can I unmoved see thee dying On a log Expiring frog!”’", "\"'배를 깔고 누워 헐떡이는 너를 보며 한숨짓지 않을 수 있으랴. 통나무 위에서 죽어 가는 너를 보며 마음이 흔들리지 않을 수 있으랴, 죽어 가는 개구리여!'\""]], [["‘Beautiful!’ said Mr.", "\"아름답군요!\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Fine,’ said Mr.", "\"훌륭하지요.\""], ["Leo Hunter; ‘so simple.’", "리오 헌터 씨가 말했다. \"참으로 단순하고요.\""]], [["‘Very,’ said Mr.", "\"매우 그렇습니다.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘The next verse is still more touching.", "\"다음 연은 한층 더 가슴을 울립니다."], ["Shall I repeat it?’", "읊어 드릴까요?\""]], [["‘If you please,’ said Mr.", "\"그러시지요.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘It runs thus,’ said the grave man, still more gravely.", "\"이렇게 이어집니다.\" 근엄한 사내가 한층 더 근엄하게 말했다."]], [["‘“Say, have fiends in shape of boys, With wild halloo, and brutal noise, Hunted thee from marshy joys, With a dog, Expiring frog!”’", "\"'말해 다오, 소년의 모습을 한 악귀들이 거친 함성과 잔인한 소음으로, 개 한 마리를 데리고 늪지의 기쁨에서 너를 사냥해 쫓아내었느냐, 죽어 가는 개구리여!'\""]], [["‘Finely expressed,’ said Mr.", "\"표현이 훌륭하군요.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘All point, Sir,’ said Mr.", "\"하나하나가 다 핵심이지요, 선생.\""], ["Leo Hunter; ‘but you shall hear Mrs.", "리오 헌터 씨가 말했다."], ["Leo Hunter repeat it. She can do justice to it, Sir.", "\"하지만 리오 헌터 부인이 읊는 것을 들어 보셔야 합니다."], ["She will repeat it, in character, Sir, to-morrow morning.’", "부인은 이 시를 제대로 살려 낼 수 있어요, 선생. 내일 아침에 분장까지 하고 읊을 겁니다, 선생.\""]], [["‘In character!’", "\"분장까지요!\""]], [["‘As Minerva.", "\"미네르바로요."], ["But I forgot--it’s a fancy-dress dejeune.’", "아, 깜빡했군요—분장 무도 조찬이랍니다.\""]], [["‘Dear me,’ said Mr.", "\"이런.\""], ["Pickwick, glancing at his own figure--‘I can’t possibly--’", "픽윅 씨가 자기 몸을 흘끗 내려다보며 말했다. \"저는 도저히—\""]], [["‘Can’t, sir; can’t!’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"못 한다니요, 선생!"], ["Leo Hunter.", "못 한다니요!\""], ["‘Solomon Lucas, the Jew in the High Street, has thousands of fancy-dresses.", "리오 헌터 씨가 외쳤다. \"하이 스트리트의 유대인 솔로몬 루커스가 분장 의상을 수천 벌 갖고 있습니다."], ["Consider, Sir, how many appropriate characters are open for your selection.", "생각해 보십시오, 선생, 선생이 고르실 수 있는 적절한 배역이 얼마나 많은지요."], ["Plato, Zeno, Epicurus, Pythagoras--all founders of clubs.’", "플라톤, 제논, 에피쿠로스, 피타고라스—모두 클럽의 창시자들이지요.\""]], [["‘I know that,’ said Mr.", "\"그건 압니다.\""], ["Pickwick; ‘but as I cannot put myself in competition with those great men, I cannot presume to wear their dresses.’", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"하지만 그런 위인들과 저를 견줄 수는 없으니, 감히 그들의 의상을 걸칠 수는 없습니다.\""]], [["The grave man considered deeply, for a few seconds, and then said--", "근엄한 사내는 몇 초간 깊이 생각하더니 말했다.—"]], [["‘On reflection, Sir, I don’t know whether it would not afford Mrs. Leo Hunter greater pleasure, if her guests saw a gentleman of your celebrity in his own costume, rather than in an assumed one.", "\"다시 생각해 보니, 선생, 손님들이 선생처럼 명성 높은 신사분을 가장 의상이 아니라 본인의 평상복 차림으로 보는 편이 리오 헌터 부인에게 더 큰 기쁨을 줄지도 모르겠습니다."], ["I may venture to promise an exception in your case, sir--yes, I am quite certain that, on behalf of Mrs. Leo Hunter, I may venture to do so.’", "선생의 경우에는 감히 예외를 약속드릴 수 있겠어요, 선생—그렇습니다, 리오 헌터 부인을 대신해 그리해도 좋다고 확신합니다.\""]], [["‘In that case,’ said Mr.", "\"그렇다면,\""], ["Pickwick, ‘I shall have great pleasure in coming.’", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"기꺼이 가도록 하겠습니다.\""]], [["‘But I waste your time, Sir,’ said the grave man, as if suddenly recollecting himself.", "\"하지만 제가 선생의 시간을 빼앗고 있군요, 선생.\" 근엄한 사내가 문득 정신을 차린 듯 말했다."], ["‘I know its value, sir.", "\"그 가치를 잘 압니다, 선생."], ["I will not detain you. I may tell Mrs.", "더는 붙들지 않겠습니다."], ["Leo Hunter, then, that she may confidently expect you and your distinguished friends?", "그럼 리오 헌터 부인에게, 선생과 선생의 저명한 친구분들이 틀림없이 오시리라 안심하고 기다려도 된다고 전해도 되겠지요? 안녕히 계십시오, 선생."], ["Good-morning, Sir, I am proud to have beheld so eminent a personage--not a step sir; not a word.’ And without giving Mr.", "이렇게 걸출한 인물을 뵙게 되어 영광입니다—나오지 마십시오, 선생. 한마디도 마십시오.\""], ["Pickwick time to offer remonstrance or denial, Mr. Leo Hunter stalked gravely away.", "그러고는 픽윅 씨가 만류하거나 사양할 틈도 주지 않고, 리오 헌터 씨는 근엄하게 성큼성큼 걸어 나갔다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick took up his hat, and repaired to the Peacock, but Mr. Winkle had conveyed the intelligence of the fancy-ball there, before him.", "픽윅 씨는 모자를 집어 들고 피코크로 갔으나, 윈클 씨가 그보다 앞서 그곳에 분장 무도회 소식을 전한 뒤였다."]], [["‘Mrs.", "\"포트 부인이 가신답니다.\""], ["Pott’s going,’ were the first words with which he saluted his leader.", "그가 자기 지도자에게 건넨 첫마디는 이것이었다."]], [["‘Is she?’ said Mr.", "\"그래?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘As Apollo,’ replied Winkle.", "\"아폴로로 분장하고요.\" 윈클이 대답했다."], ["‘Only Pott objects to the tunic.’", "\"다만 포트 씨가 튜닉은 안 된다고 하셔서요.\""]], [["‘He is right.", "\"옳은 말이네."], ["He is quite right,’ said Mr.", "지당한 말이야.\""], ["Pickwick emphatically.", "픽윅 씨가 힘주어 말했다."]], [["‘Yes; so she’s going to wear a white satin gown with gold spangles.’", "\"네, 그래서 금박 장식이 달린 흰 새틴 가운을 입으신답니다.\""]], [["‘They’ll hardly know what she’s meant for; will they?’ inquired Mr.", "\"그러면 사람들이 그분이 뭘 표현한 건지 알아보기 힘들지 않을까요?\""], ["Snodgrass.", "스노드그래스 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘Of course they will,’ replied Mr.", "\"당연히 알아보죠.\""], ["Winkle indignantly.", "윈클 씨가 발끈하며 대답했다."], ["‘They’ll see her lyre, won’t they?’", "\"그분의 리라를 보면 알 거 아닙니까?\""]], [["‘True; I forgot that,’ said Mr.", "\"그렇군. 그걸 깜빡했네.\""], ["Snodgrass.", "스노드그래스 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘I shall go as a bandit,’ interposed Mr.", "\"저는 산적으로 가겠습니다.\""], ["Tupman.", "터프먼 씨가 끼어들었다."]], [["‘What!’ said Mr.", "\"뭐라고요!\""], ["Pickwick, with a sudden start.", "픽윅 씨가 화들짝 놀라며 말했다."]], [["‘As a bandit,’ repeated Mr.", "\"산적으로요.\""], ["Tupman, mildly.", "터프먼 씨가 부드럽게 되풀이했다."]], [["‘You don’t mean to say,’ said Mr.", "\"설마,\""], ["Pickwick, gazing with solemn sternness at his friend--‘you don’t mean to say, Mr.", "픽윅 씨가 엄숙하고 준엄한 눈길로 친구를 바라보며 말했다."], ["Tupman, that it is your intention to put yourself into a green velvet jacket, with a two-inch tail?’", "\"설마, 터프먼 씨, 두 치짜리 꼬리가 달린 초록 벨벳 재킷을 걸치겠다는 건 아니겠지요?\""]], [["‘Such is my intention, Sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"바로 그게 제 뜻입니다, 선생.\" 터프먼 씨가 발끈하며 대답했다."], ["Tupman warmly. ‘And why not, sir?’", "\"안 될 게 뭐 있습니까, 선생?\""]], [["‘Because, Sir,’ said Mr.", "\"왜냐하면, 선생,\""], ["Pickwick, considerably excited--‘because you are too old, Sir.’", "픽윅 씨가 상당히 흥분하여 말했다. \"선생은 너무 나이가 많으니까요, 선생.\""]], [["‘Too old!’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"너무 나이가 많다니!\""], ["Tupman.", "터프먼 씨가 외쳤다."]], [["‘And if any further ground of objection be wanting,’ continued Mr.", "\"그리고 반대할 근거가 더 필요하다면,\" 픽윅 씨가 말을 이었다."], ["Pickwick, ‘you are too fat, sir.’", "\"선생은 너무 뚱뚱합니다, 선생.\""]], [["‘Sir,’ said Mr.", "\"선생,\""], ["Tupman, his face suffused with a crimson glow, ‘this is an insult.’", "터프먼 씨가 얼굴이 시뻘겋게 달아올라 말했다. \"이건 모욕입니다.\""]], [["‘Sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"선생,\""], ["Pickwick, in the same tone, ‘it is not half the insult to you, that your appearance in my presence in a green velvet jacket, with a two-inch tail, would be to me.’", "픽윅 씨가 똑같은 어조로 대답했다. \"선생이 두 치짜리 꼬리 달린 초록 벨벳 재킷을 입고 제 앞에 나타나는 것이 제게 주는 모욕에 비하면, 이건 그 절반도 안 되는 모욕입니다.\""]], [["‘Sir,’ said Mr.", "\"선생,\" 터프먼 씨가 말했다."], ["Tupman, ‘you’re a fellow.’", "\"당신은 형편없는 작자요.\""]], [["‘Sir,’ said Mr.", "\"선생,\""], ["Pickwick, ‘you’re another!’", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"당신도 마찬가지요!\""]], [["Mr. Tupman advanced a step or two, and glared at Mr. Pickwick. Mr. Pickwick returned the glare, concentrated into a focus by means of his spectacles, and breathed a bold defiance. Mr. Snodgrass and Mr. Winkle looked on, petrified at beholding such a scene between two such men.", "터프먼 씨가 한두 걸음 다가와 픽윅 씨를 노려보았다. 픽윅 씨도 안경을 통해 초점을 모은 노려보기로 응수하며 당당히 도전의 숨을 내쉬었다. 스노드그래스 씨와 윈클 씨는 그런 두 인물 사이에 벌어진 장면을 보며 돌처럼 굳은 채 지켜보았다."]], [["‘Sir,’ said Mr.", "\"선생,\""], ["Tupman, after a short pause, speaking in a low, deep voice, ‘you have called me old.’", "터프먼 씨가 잠시 멈추었다가 낮고 깊은 목소리로 말했다. \"당신은 나를 늙었다고 했소.\""]], [["‘I have,’ said Mr.", "\"그랬소.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘And fat.’", "\"그리고 뚱뚱하다고.\""]], [["‘I reiterate the charge.’", "\"그 비난을 거듭하겠소.\""]], [["‘And a fellow.’\n‘So you are!’", "\"그리고 형편없는 작자라고.\""]], [["There was a fearful pause.", "\"실제로 그러하니까!\""]], [["‘My attachment to your person, sir,’ said Mr.", "무시무시한 침묵이 흘렀다. \"당신이라는 인물에 대한 제 애착은, 선생,\""], ["Tupman, speaking in a voice tremulous with emotion, and tucking up his wristbands meanwhile, ‘is great--very great--but upon that person, I must take summary vengeance.’", "터프먼 씨가 감정으로 떨리는 목소리로 말하며 그러는 사이 소맷부리를 걷어 올렸다. \"크오—매우 크오—하나 그 인물에게 즉결로 복수를 하지 않을 수 없소.\""]], [["‘Come on, Sir!’ replied Mr. Pickwick.", "\"덤비시오, 선생!\""], ["Stimulated by the exciting nature of the dialogue, the heroic man actually threw himself into a paralytic attitude, confidently supposed by the two bystanders to have been intended as a posture of defence.", "픽윅 씨가 대답했다. 자극적인 대화의 흥분에 휩싸여, 이 영웅적인 사내는 실제로 중풍 걸린 듯한 자세를 취했는데, 두 구경꾼은 그것이 방어 자세로 의도된 것이라고 자신 있게 짐작했다."]], [["‘What!’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"뭐라고요!\" 스노드그래스 씨가 외쳤다."], ["Snodgrass, suddenly recovering the power of speech, of which intense astonishment had previously bereft him, and rushing between the two, at the imminent hazard of receiving an application on the temple from each--‘what!", "극심한 경악으로 잃었던 말의 능력을 문득 되찾고는, 양쪽에서 관자놀이에 한 방씩 맞을 위험을 무릅쓰며 두 사람 사이로 뛰어들었다. \"뭐라고요!"], ["Mr. Pickwick, with the eyes of the world upon you!", "픽윅 씨, 온 세상의 눈이 당신께 쏠려 있는데요!"], ["Mr. Tupman!", "터프먼 씨!"], ["who, in common with us all, derives a lustre from his undying name!", "우리 모두와 마찬가지로 그의 불멸의 이름에서 광채를 얻는 분께서! 부끄러운 줄 아십시오, 두 분!"], ["For shame, gentlemen; for shame.’", "부끄러운 줄 아십시오.\""]], [["The unwonted lines which momentary passion had ruled in Mr. Pickwick’s clear and open brow, gradually melted away, as his young friend spoke, like the marks of a black-lead pencil beneath the softening influence of india-rubber.", "순간적인 격정이 픽윅 씨의 맑고 트인 이마에 그어 놓았던 낯선 주름들이, 젊은 친구가 말하는 동안 차츰 녹아 사라졌으니, 마치 흑연 연필 자국이 지우개의 부드러운 손길 아래 지워지듯 하였다."], ["His countenance had resumed its usual benign expression, ere he concluded.", "그가 말을 마치기도 전에 그의 얼굴은 평소의 인자한 표정을 되찾았다."]], [["‘I have been hasty,’ said Mr.", "\"내가 성급했네.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick, ‘very hasty.", "\"참으로 성급했어."], ["Tupman; your hand.’", "터프먼, 손을 주게.\""]], [["The dark shadow passed from Mr. Tupman’s face, as he warmly grasped the hand of his friend.", "터프먼 씨가 친구의 손을 따뜻하게 맞잡자, 그의 얼굴에서 어두운 그림자가 걷혔다."]], [["‘I have been hasty, too,’ said he.", "\"저도 성급했습니다.\" 그가 말했다."]], [["‘No, no,’ interrupted Mr.", "\"아니, 아닐세.\""], ["Pickwick, ‘the fault was mine.", "픽윅 씨가 가로막았다."], ["You will wear the green velvet jacket?’", "\"내 잘못이었네. 초록 벨벳 재킷을 입겠지?\""]], [["‘No, no,’ replied Mr.", "\"아닙니다, 아니에요.\""], ["Tupman.", "터프먼 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘To oblige me, you will,’ resumed Mr.", "\"내 부탁이니 입어 주게나.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말을 이었다."]], [["‘Well, well, I will,’ said Mr.", "\"좋습니다, 좋아요, 입겠습니다.\""], ["Tupman.", "터프먼 씨가 말했다."]], [["It was accordingly settled that Mr. Tupman, Mr. Winkle, and Mr. Snodgrass, should all wear fancy-dresses. Thus Mr. Pickwick was led by the very warmth of his own good feelings to give his consent to a proceeding from which his better judgment would have recoiled--a more striking illustration of his amiable character could hardly have been conceived, even if the events recorded in these pages had been wholly imaginary.", "이리하여 터프먼 씨, 윈클 씨, 스노드그래스 씨 모두 분장 의상을 입기로 정해졌다. 이렇게 픽윅 씨는 그 자신의 선량한 감정의 열기 그 자체에 이끌려, 보다 나은 분별이라면 움츠러들었을 일에 동의하고 말았다.—그의 사랑스러운 성품을 이보다 더 인상 깊게 보여 주는 예는, 설령 이 지면에 기록된 사건들이 전부 가상의 것이라 해도 좀처럼 떠올리기 어려울 것이다."]], [["Mr. Leo Hunter had not exaggerated the resources of Mr.", "리오 헌터 씨는 솔로몬 루커스 씨의 자원을 과장한 것이 아니었다."], ["Solomon Lucas. His wardrobe was extensive--very extensive--not strictly classical perhaps, not quite new, nor did it contain any one garment made precisely after the fashion of any age or time, but everything was more or less spangled; and what can be prettier than spangles!", "그의 의상 창고는 방대했다—매우 방대했다—어쩌면 엄밀히 고전적이지는 않았고, 아주 새것도 아니었으며, 어느 시대나 시기의 양식을 정확히 따라 지은 옷이라곤 한 벌도 없었으나, 모든 것에 어느 정도씩 금박 장식이 달려 있었다."], ["It may be objected that they are not adapted to the daylight, but everybody knows that they would glitter if there were lamps; and nothing can be clearer than that if people give fancy-balls in the day-time, and the dresses do not show quite as well as they would by night, the fault lies solely with the people who give the fancy-balls, and is in no wise chargeable on the spangles.", "그리고 금박만큼 예쁜 것이 또 어디 있겠는가! 그것이 대낮에는 어울리지 않는다고 이의를 제기할 수도 있겠으나, 등불만 있다면 반짝였으리란 것은 누구나 아는 사실이다."], ["Such was the convincing reasoning of Mr. Solomon Lucas; and influenced by such arguments did Mr.", "그러니 사람들이 대낮에 분장 무도회를 열어 의상이 밤만큼 돋보이지 않는다면, 그 잘못은 오로지 분장 무도회를 여는 사람들에게 있는 것이지, 결코 금박에 물을 수 있는 일이 아니라는 것보다 더 명백한 이치는 없다."], ["Tupman, Mr. Winkle, and Mr.", "이것이 솔로몬 루커스 씨의 설득력 있는 논리였다."], ["Snodgrass engage to array themselves in costumes which his taste and experience induced him to recommend as admirably suited to the occasion.", "그리고 그런 논변에 영향을 받아, 터프먼 씨와 윈클 씨와 스노드그래스 씨는 그의 안목과 경험이 이 행사에 더없이 잘 어울린다고 추천한 의상으로 차려입기로 약속했다."]], [["A carriage was hired from the Town Arms, for the accommodation of the Pickwickians, and a chariot was ordered from the same repository, for the purpose of conveying Mr. and Mrs.", "픽윅 회원들의 편의를 위해 타운 암스에서 마차 한 대를 빌렸고, 같은 곳에서 포트 부부를 리오 헌터 부인의 저택으로 모실 사륜마차도 주문했다."], ["Pott to Mrs. Leo Hunter’s grounds, which Mr.", "포트 씨는 초대받은 데 대한 섬세한 답례로, 이미 이턴스윌 가제트에서 그 조찬이 \"다채롭고 황홀한 마법의 광경을—아름다움과 재능의 어지러운 광채를—후하고 아낌없는 환대의 향연을—무엇보다도, 더없이 정교한 안목으로 누그러뜨린 광휘를, 완벽한 조화와 가장 정숙한 절제로 다듬어진 장식을 펼쳐 보일 것이며, 이에 견주면 전설로 전하는 동방 요정 나라의 화려함조차도, 자신의 시샘의 독으로 감히 더럽히려 드는 신경질적이고 사내답지 못한 자의 마음만큼이나 어둡고 칙칙한 빛깔을 두른 것처럼 보이리니, 그 덕성 높고 지극히 걸출한 부인의 신전에 이 보잘것없는 찬미의 공물이 바쳐지는 것\"이라고 자신 있게 예언해 둔 터였다."], ["Pott, as a delicate acknowledgment of having received an invitation, had already confidently predicted in the Eatanswill Gazette ‘would present a scene of varied and delicious enchantment--a bewildering coruscation of beauty and talent--a lavish and prodigal display of hospitality--above all, a degree of splendour softened by the most exquisite taste; and adornment refined with perfect harmony and the chastest good keeping--compared with which, the fabled gorgeousness of Eastern fairyland itself would appear to be clothed in as many dark and murky colours, as must be the mind of the splenetic and unmanly being who could presume to taint with the venom of his envy, the preparations made by the virtuous and highly distinguished lady at whose shrine this humble tribute of admiration was offered.’ This last was a piece of biting sarcasm against the Independent, who, in consequence of not having been invited at all, had been, through four numbers, affecting to sneer at the whole affair, in his very largest type, with all the adjectives in capital letters.", "이 마지막 대목은 인디펜던트를 겨냥한 신랄한 풍자였으니, 인디펜던트는 아예 초대받지 못한 탓에 네 호에 걸쳐 가장 큰 활자로, 형용사마다 대문자로 박아 가며 이 모든 일을 비웃는 척해 온 터였다."]], [["The morning came: it was a pleasant sight to behold Mr.", "마침내 아침이 밝았다."], ["Tupman in full brigand’s costume, with a very tight jacket, sitting like a pincushion over his back and shoulders, the upper portion of his legs incased in the velvet shorts, and the lower part thereof swathed in the complicated bandages to which all brigands are peculiarly attached.", "터프먼 씨가 완전한 산적 차림으로 있는 모습은 볼 만한 광경이었으니, 아주 꽉 끼는 재킷이 그의 등과 어깨 위에 바늘방석처럼 얹혀 있었고, 다리 윗부분은 벨벳 반바지에 싸여 있었으며, 아랫부분은 모든 산적이 유독 즐겨 두르는 복잡한 붕대로 칭칭 감겨 있었다."], ["It was pleasing to see his open and ingenuous countenance, well mustachioed and corked, looking out from an open shirt collar; and to contemplate the sugar-loaf hat, decorated with ribbons of all colours, which he was compelled to carry on his knee, inasmuch as no known conveyance with a top to it, would admit of any man’s carrying it between his head and the roof.", "트인 셔츠 깃 사이로 콧수염을 잘 기르고 그을음으로 칠한 그의 솔직하고 천진한 얼굴이 내다보는 모습이며, 온갖 색의 리본으로 장식한 설탕 덩이 모양의 모자를 보는 것도 즐거운 일이었으니, 그 모자는 그가 무릎 위에 올려 들고 있을 수밖에 없었다."], ["Equally humorous and agreeable was the appearance of Mr.", "지붕이 있는 어떤 알려진 탈것도 머리와 천장 사이에 그것을 끼우고 탈 수 있게 해 주지 않았기 때문이다."], ["Snodgrass in blue satin trunks and cloak, white silk tights and shoes, and Grecian helmet, which everybody knows (and if they do not, Mr. Solomon Lucas did) to have been the regular, authentic, everyday costume of a troubadour, from the earliest ages down to the time of their final disappearance from the face of the earth.", "마찬가지로 우스꽝스럽고 보기 좋았던 것은 스노드그래스 씨의 모습이었으니, 그는 파란 새틴 짧은 바지와 망토에, 흰 비단 타이츠와 신발을 신고, 그리스식 투구를 썼는데, 그것이 가장 이른 시대부터 음유시인이 지상에서 마침내 자취를 감추던 때까지의, 정식이고 진정한 그들의 일상 복장이었다는 것은 누구나 아는 사실이다(설령 모른다 해도 솔로몬 루커스 씨는 알았다)."], ["All this was pleasant, but this was as nothing compared with the shouting of the populace when the carriage drew up, behind Mr.", "이 모든 것이 흐뭇했으나, 마차가 포트 씨의 사륜마차 뒤에 멈춰 섰을 때 군중이 지른 함성에 비하면 아무것도 아니었다."], ["Pott’s chariot, which chariot itself drew up at Mr. Pott’s door, which door itself opened, and displayed the great Pott accoutred as a Russian officer of justice, with a tremendous knout in his hand--tastefully typical of the stern and mighty power of the Eatanswill Gazette, and the fearful lashings it bestowed on public offenders.", "그 사륜마차는 포트 씨의 문 앞에 멈춰 섰고, 그 문이 열리며 위대한 포트가 모습을 드러냈으니, 그는 러시아 사법 관리 차림으로 손에 어마어마한 채찍을 들고 있었다—이턴스윌 가제트의 준엄하고 막강한 위력과, 그것이 공적 범법자들에게 가하는 무시무시한 매질을 멋들어지게 상징하는 것이었다."]], [["‘Bravo!’ shouted Mr.", "\"브라보!\""], ["Tupman and Mr. Snodgrass from the passage, when they beheld the walking allegory.", "터프먼 씨와 스노드그래스 씨가 그 걸어 다니는 알레고리를 보고 복도에서 외쳤다."]], [["‘Bravo!’ Mr.", "\"브라보!\""], ["Pickwick was heard to exclaim, from the passage.", "픽윅 씨도 복도에서 외치는 소리가 들렸다."]], [["‘Hoo-roar Pott!’ shouted the populace.", "\"포트 만세!\" 군중이 외쳤다."], ["Amid these salutations, Mr. Pott, smiling with that kind of bland dignity which sufficiently testified that he felt his power, and knew how to exert it, got into the chariot.", "이러한 인사 속에서, 포트 씨는 자신의 권력을 느끼고 그것을 행사할 줄 안다는 것을 충분히 드러내는 그런 온화한 위엄의 미소를 띤 채, 사륜마차에 올랐다."]], [["Then there emerged from the house, Mrs. Pott, who would have looked very like Apollo if she hadn’t had a gown on, conducted by Mr.", "이어 집에서 포트 부인이 나왔는데, 가운만 걸치지 않았더라면 영락없는 아폴로처럼 보였을 것이며, 윈클 씨의 안내를 받고 있었다."], ["Winkle, who, in his light-red coat could not possibly have been mistaken for anything but a sportsman, if he had not borne an equal resemblance to a general postman. Last of all came Mr.", "윈클 씨는 연붉은 코트를 입고 있어, 일반 우편배달부와 똑같이 닮아 보이지만 않았더라면 영락없이 사냥꾼으로밖에 보이지 않았을 것이다."], ["Pickwick, whom the boys applauded as loud as anybody, probably under the impression that his tights and gaiters were some remnants of the dark ages; and then the two vehicles proceeded towards Mrs.", "맨 마지막으로 픽윅 씨가 나왔는데, 소년들은 누구 못지않게 크게 그에게 박수를 보냈으니, 아마도 그의 타이츠와 각반을 암흑시대의 잔재쯤으로 여긴 모양이었다. 그러고는 두 대의 탈것이 리오 헌터 부인의 저택을 향해 나아갔다."], ["Leo Hunter’s; Mr. Weller (who was to assist in waiting) being stationed on the box of that in which his master was seated.", "웰러 씨는(시중을 거들기로 되어 있었다) 자기 주인이 탄 탈것의 마부석에 자리를 잡았다."]], [["Every one of the men, women, boys, girls, and babies, who were assembled to see the visitors in their fancy-dresses, screamed with delight and ecstasy, when Mr. Pickwick, with the brigand on one arm, and the troubadour on the other, walked solemnly up the entrance.", "분장 의상을 차려입은 손님들을 보려고 모여든 남자, 여자, 소년, 소녀, 갓난아기 할 것 없이 하나하나가, 픽윅 씨가 한쪽 팔에 산적을, 다른 쪽 팔에 음유시인을 끼고 엄숙하게 입구로 걸어 올라가자 환희와 황홀로 비명을 질렀다."], ["Never were such shouts heard as those which greeted Mr. Tupman’s efforts to fix the sugar-loaf hat on his head, by way of entering the garden in style.", "터프먼 씨가 정원에 멋들어지게 입장하려고 설탕 덩이 모양의 모자를 머리에 고정시키려 애쓰는 모습을 맞이한 함성만큼 대단한 함성은 일찍이 들어 본 적이 없었다."]], [["The preparations were on the most delightful scale; fully realising the prophetic Pott’s anticipations about the gorgeousness of Eastern fairyland, and at once affording a sufficient contradiction to the malignant statements of the reptile Independent.", "준비는 더없이 흐뭇한 규모로 갖추어져 있었으니, 동방 요정 나라의 화려함에 관한 예언가 포트의 기대를 완전히 실현하는 것이자, 동시에 파충류 같은 인디펜던트의 악의에 찬 진술을 충분히 반박해 주는 것이었다. 저택은 넓이가 일 에이커 사 분의 일이 넘었고, 사람들로 가득 차 있었다!"], ["The grounds were more than an acre and a quarter in extent, and they were filled with people!", "그런 미모와 멋과 문학의 향연은 일찍이 없었다. 이턴스윌 가제트의 시 담당을 '맡은'"], ["Never was such a blaze of beauty, and fashion, and literature.", "젊은 숙녀가 술탄 비빈의 차림으로, 서평란을 '맡은'"], ["There was the young lady who ‘did’ the poetry in the Eatanswill Gazette, in the garb of a sultana, leaning upon the arm of the young gentleman who ‘did’ the review department, and who was appropriately habited in a field- marshal’s uniform--the boots excepted.", "젊은 신사의 팔에 기대어 있었는데, 그 신사는 적절하게도 야전 원수의 군복을 차려입고 있었다—장화만 빼고 말이다. 이런 천재들이 무리를 이루고 있었으니, 분별 있는 사람이라면 누구라도 그들을 만나는 것만으로도 충분한 영광이라 여겼을 것이다."], ["There were hosts of these geniuses, and any reasonable person would have thought it honour enough to meet them. But more than these, there were half a dozen lions from London--authors, real authors, who had written whole books, and printed them afterwards--and here you might see ‘em, walking about, like ordinary men, smiling, and talking--aye, and talking pretty considerable nonsense too, no doubt with the benign intention of rendering themselves intelligible to the common people about them.", "그러나 이들 말고도, 런던에서 온 사자가 여섯이나 있었으니—작가들, 진짜 작가들로, 책을 통째로 써내고 그것을 나중에 인쇄까지 한 이들이었다—여기서 당신은 그들이 보통 사람처럼 돌아다니며 웃고 떠드는 것을, 그래, 게다가 꽤나 상당한 헛소리를 떠드는 것을 볼 수 있었으니, 틀림없이 자신들을 둘러싼 보통 사람들이 알아들을 수 있게 하려는 인자한 의도에서였으리라."], ["Moreover, there was a band of music in pasteboard caps; four something-ean singers in the costume of their country, and a dozen hired waiters in the costume of their country--and very dirty costume too. And above all, there was Mrs.", "게다가 판지 모자를 쓴 악단이 있었고, 자기 나라 의상을 입은 네 명의 무슨무슨 출신 가수들과, 자기네 나라 의상—그것도 아주 더러운 의상—을 입은 고용된 웨이터 열두 명이 있었다."], ["Leo Hunter in the character of Minerva, receiving the company, and overflowing with pride and gratification at the notion of having called such distinguished individuals together.", "그리고 무엇보다도, 미네르바로 분장한 리오 헌터 부인이 손님들을 맞이하며, 이토록 저명한 인사들을 한자리에 불러 모았다는 생각에 자부심과 만족으로 넘쳐 있었다."]], [["‘Mr.", "\"픽윅 씨 오셨습니다, 마님.\""], ["Pickwick, ma’am,’ said a servant, as that gentleman approached the presiding goddess, with his hat in his hand, and the brigand and troubadour on either arm.", "픽윅 씨가 모자를 손에 들고 산적과 음유시인을 양팔에 낀 채 주재하는 여신에게 다가가자, 한 하인이 말했다."]], [["‘What!", "\"어머!"], ["Where!’ exclaimed Mrs.", "어디!\""], ["Leo Hunter, starting up, in an affected rapture of surprise.", "리오 헌터 부인이 짐짓 꾸민 놀람의 황홀경에 빠져 벌떡 일어나며 외쳤다."]], [["‘Here,’ said Mr.", "\"여기 있습니다.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Is it possible that I have really the gratification of beholding Mr.", "\"내가 정말로 픽윅 씨 본인을 뵙는 기쁨을 누리는 게 가능한 일인가요!\""], ["Pickwick himself!’ ejaculated Mrs. Leo Hunter.", "리오 헌터 부인이 부르짖었다."]], [["‘No other, ma’am,’ replied Mr. Pickwick, bowing very low.", "\"다름 아닌 본인입니다, 마님.\""], ["‘Permit me to introduce my friends--Mr. Tupman--Mr.", "픽윅 씨가 아주 깊이 허리를 숙이며 대답했다."], ["Winkle--Mr. Snodgrass--to the authoress of “The Expiring Frog.”’", "\"제 친구들을 소개해 드리지요—터프먼 씨—윈클 씨—스노드그래스 씨를—'죽어 가는 개구리'의 작가께.\""]], [["Very few people but those who have tried it, know what a difficult process it is to bow in green velvet smalls, and a tight jacket, and high-crowned hat; or in blue satin trunks and white silks, or knee-cords and top-boots that were never made for the wearer, and have been fixed upon him without the remotest reference to the comparative dimensions of himself and the suit.", "초록 벨벳 반바지에 꽉 끼는 재킷과 높다란 모자 차림으로, 혹은 파란 새틴 짧은 바지와 흰 비단 차림으로, 혹은 결코 입는 이를 위해 만든 것이 아니어서 그와 옷의 상대적 치수를 전혀 고려하지 않고 그에게 끼워 맞춘 무릎 끈 바지와 장화 차림으로 허리를 숙이는 일이 얼마나 어려운 과정인지는, 직접 해 본 사람이 아니고서는 거의 알지 못한다."], ["Never were such distortions as Mr. Tupman’s frame underwent in his efforts to appear easy and graceful--never was such ingenious posturing, as his fancy-dressed friends exhibited.", "편안하고 우아해 보이려 애쓰는 가운데 터프먼 씨의 몸이 겪은 것 같은 기괴한 뒤틀림은 일찍이 없었으며—그의 분장한 친구들이 보여 준 것 같은 기발한 자세 잡기도 일찍이 없었다."]], [["‘Mr. Pickwick,’ said Mrs.", "\"픽윅 씨,\""], ["Leo Hunter, ‘I must make you promise not to stir from my side the whole day.", "리오 헌터 부인이 말했다. \"온종일 제 곁을 떠나지 않겠다고 약속해 주셔야겠어요."], ["There are hundreds of people here, that I must positively introduce you to.’", "여기 제가 꼭 소개해 드려야 할 분들이 수백 명이나 계시거든요.\""]], [["‘You are very kind, ma’am,’ said Mr.", "\"참으로 친절하십니다, 마님.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘In the first place, here are my little girls; I had almost forgotten them,’ said Minerva, carelessly pointing towards a couple of full-grown young ladies, of whom one might be about twenty, and the other a year or two older, and who were dressed in very juvenile costumes--whether to make them look young, or their mamma younger, Mr.", "\"우선, 여기 제 어린 딸들이에요. 하마터면 깜빡할 뻔했네요.\""], ["Pickwick does not distinctly inform us.", "미네르바가 다 자란 두 젊은 숙녀를 무심히 가리키며 말했는데, 한 명은 스무 살쯤, 다른 한 명은 그보다 한두 살 위였으며, 둘 다 매우 앳된 의상을 입고 있었다.—그것이 그들을 어려 보이게 하려는 것인지, 아니면 그들의 엄마를 더 젊어 보이게 하려는 것인지는, 픽윅 씨가 우리에게 분명히 일러 주지 않는다."]], [["‘They are very beautiful,’ said Mr.", "\"참으로 아름다운 따님들입니다.\""], ["Pickwick, as the juveniles turned away, after being presented.", "어린 딸들이 소개를 마친 뒤 돌아서자 픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘They are very like their mamma, Sir,’ said Mr.", "\"어머니를 꼭 닮았지요, 선생.\""], ["Pott, majestically.", "포트 씨가 위엄 있게 말했다."]], [["‘Oh, you naughty man,’ exclaimed Mrs.", "\"어머, 짓궂으셔라.\""], ["Leo Hunter, playfully tapping the editor’s arm with her fan (Minerva with a fan!).", "리오 헌터 부인이 부채로 편집장의 팔을 장난스레 톡 치며 외쳤다(부채를 든 미네르바라니!)."]], [["‘Why now, my dear Mrs.", "\"아니 이런, 친애하는 헌터 부인,\""], ["Hunter,’ said Mr.", "디 덴의 상임 나팔수인 포트 씨가 말했다."], ["Pott, who was trumpeter in ordinary at the Den, ‘you know that when your picture was in the exhibition of the Royal Academy, last year, everybody inquired whether it was intended for you, or your youngest daughter; for you were so much alike that there was no telling the difference between you.’", "\"부인도 아시다시피 작년에 부인의 초상화가 왕립 미술원 전시회에 걸렸을 때, 모두가 그게 부인을 그린 건지 아니면 부인의 막내따님을 그린 건지 물었지요. 두 분이 어찌나 닮았던지 분간할 수가 없었으니까요.\""]], [["‘Well, and if they did, why need you repeat it, before strangers?’ said Mrs.", "\"그래, 그랬다 한들, 굳이 낯선 분들 앞에서 그 말을 되풀이할 필요가 어디 있어요?\""], ["Leo Hunter, bestowing another tap on the slumbering lion of the Eatanswill Gazette.", "리오 헌터 부인이 이턴스윌 가제트의 잠든 사자에게 다시 한 번 톡 치며 말했다."]], [["‘Count, count,’ screamed Mrs.", "\"백작님, 백작님.\""], ["Leo Hunter to a well-whiskered individual in a foreign uniform, who was passing by.", "리오 헌터 부인이 마침 지나가던, 외국 군복을 입고 구레나룻이 멋진 인물에게 외쳤다."]], [["‘Ah!", "\"아!"], ["you want me?’ said the count, turning back.", "저를 부르셨소?\" 백작이 돌아서며 말했다."]], [["‘I want to introduce two very clever people to each other,’ said Mrs.", "\"아주 영리한 두 분을 서로 소개해 드리고 싶어서요.\""], ["Leo Hunter. ‘Mr.", "리오 헌터 부인이 말했다."], ["Pickwick, I have great pleasure in introducing you to Count Smorltork.’ She added in a hurried whisper to Mr.", "\"픽윅 씨, 스모를토크 백작님을 소개해 드리게 되어 더없이 기쁩니다.\""], ["Pickwick--‘The famous foreigner--gathering materials for his great work on England-- hem!--Count Smorltork, Mr. Pickwick.’", "그러고는 픽윅 씨에게 황급히 속삭이며 덧붙였다.—\"유명한 외국분이세요—영국에 관한 대작에 쓸 자료를 모으고 계시지요—흠!—스모를토크 백작님, 픽윅 씨예요.\""]], [["Mr. Pickwick saluted the count with all the reverence due to so great a man, and the count drew forth a set of tablets.", "픽윅 씨는 그토록 위대한 인물에게 마땅한 온갖 경의를 다해 백작에게 인사했고, 백작은 수첩을 한 권 꺼냈다."]], [["‘What you say, Mrs.", "\"뭐라고 하셨소, 헌트 부인?\""], ["Hunt?’ inquired the count, smiling graciously on the gratified Mrs.", "만족스러워하는 리오 헌터 부인에게 점잖게 미소 지으며 백작이 물었다."], ["Leo Hunter, ‘Pig Vig or Big Vig--what you call--lawyer-- eh?", "\"피그 비그요, 빅 비그요—뭐라더라—변호사—응?"], ["I see--that is it.", "알겠소—그거로군."], ["Big Vig’--and the count was proceeding to enter Mr.", "빅 비그.\""], ["Pickwick in his tablets, as a gentleman of the long robe, who derived his name from the profession to which he belonged, when Mrs. Leo Hunter interposed.", "그러고서 백작은 픽윅 씨를, 자기가 속한 직업에서 이름을 따온 법복 신사로 수첩에 적어 넣으려던 참이었는데, 리오 헌터 부인이 끼어들었다."]], [["‘No, no, count,’ said the lady, ‘Pick-wick.’", "\"아니에요, 아니에요, 백작님.\" 부인이 말했다. \"픽—윅이에요.\""]], [["‘Ah, ah, I see,’ replied the count.", "\"아, 아, 알겠소.\" 백작이 대답했다."], ["‘Peek--christian name; Weeks-- surname; good, ver good.", "\"피크—세례명이고, 윅스—성이로군. 좋소, 아주 좋아."], ["Peek Weeks.", "피크 윅스."], ["How you do, Weeks?’", "안녕하시오, 윅스?\""]], [["‘Quite well, I thank you,’ replied Mr.", "\"덕분에 아주 잘 지냅니다.\""], ["Pickwick, with all his usual affability.", "픽윅 씨가 여느 때처럼 상냥하게 대답했다."], ["‘Have you been long in England?’", "\"영국에 오신 지 오래되셨습니까?\""]], [["‘Long--ver long time--fortnight--more.’", "\"오래—아주 오래—두 주—그 이상.\""]], [["‘Do you stay here long?’", "\"여기 오래 머무십니까?\""]], [["‘One week.’", "\"한 주요.\""]], [["‘You will have enough to do,’ said Mr.", "\"그 시간 안에 필요한 자료를 다 모으시려면,\""], ["Pickwick smiling, ‘to gather all the materials you want in that time.’", "픽윅 씨가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"할 일이 꽤 많으시겠습니다.\""]], [["‘Eh, they are gathered,’ said the count.", "\"응, 이미 다 모았소.\" 백작이 말했다."]], [["‘Indeed!’ said Mr.", "\"정말입니까!\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘They are here,’ added the count, tapping his forehead significantly.", "\"여기 다 있소.\" 백작이 자기 이마를 의미심장하게 톡톡 두드리며 덧붙였다."], ["‘Large book at home--full of notes--music, picture, science, potry, poltic; all tings.’", "\"집에 큰 책이 있소—메모로 가득—음악, 그림, 과학, 시, 정치, 만사가.\""]], [["‘The word politics, sir,’ said Mr.", "\"정치라는 말은, 선생,\""], ["Pickwick, ‘comprises in itself, a difficult study of no inconsiderable magnitude.’", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"그 자체로 결코 적지 않은 분량의 어려운 연구를 담고 있지요.\""]], [["‘Ah!’ said the count, drawing out the tablets again, ‘ver good--fine words to begin a chapter.", "\"아!\" 백작이 다시 수첩을 꺼내며 말했다."], ["Chapter forty-seven.", "\"아주 좋소—한 장을 시작하기에 멋진 말이오."], ["Poltics.", "사십칠 장."], ["The word poltic surprises by himself--’ And down went Mr.", "정치. 정치라는 말은 그 자체로 놀라움을—\""], ["Pickwick’s remark, in Count Smorltork’s tablets, with such variations and additions as the count’s exuberant fancy suggested, or his imperfect knowledge of the language occasioned.", "그리고 픽윅 씨의 말은 스모를토크 백작의 수첩 속에 적혀 들어갔으니, 백작의 넘쳐 나는 상상이 일러 주거나 그의 불완전한 언어 지식이 빚어낸 갖가지 변주와 추가가 곁들여졌다."]], [["‘Count,’ said Mrs.", "\"백작님.\""], ["Leo Hunter.", "리오 헌터 부인이 말했다."]], [["‘Mrs.", "\"헌트 부인.\""], ["Hunt,’ replied the count.", "백작이 대답했다."]], [["‘This is Mr. Snodgrass, a friend of Mr. Pickwick’s, and a poet.’", "\"이분은 스노드그래스 씨인데, 픽윅 씨의 친구분이자 시인이세요.\""]], [["‘Stop,’ exclaimed the count, bringing out the tablets once more.", "\"잠깐만.\" 백작이 다시 한 번 수첩을 꺼내며 외쳤다."], ["‘Head, potry--chapter, literary friends--name, Snowgrass; ver good.", "\"항목, 시—장, 문인 친구들—이름, 스노그래스."], ["Introduced to Snowgrass--great poet, friend of Peek Weeks--by Mrs.", "아주 좋소."], ["Hunt, which wrote other sweet poem--what is that name?--Fog--Perspiring Fog--ver good--ver good indeed.’ And the count put up his tablets, and with sundry bows and acknowledgments walked away, thoroughly satisfied that he had made the most important and valuable additions to his stock of information.", "헌트 부인의 소개로 스노그래스를 알게 됨—위대한 시인, 피크 윅스의 친구—부인은 또 다른 감미로운 시를 썼는데—그 제목이 뭐라더라?—포그—땀 흘리는 포그—아주 좋소—참으로 아주 좋아.\" 그러고서 백작은 수첩을 집어넣고는, 여러 차례 절하고 사례의 인사를 하며 걸어갔으니, 자기 정보 창고에 가장 중요하고 값진 추가를 했노라 철석같이 만족해하고 있었다."]], [["‘Wonderful man, Count Smorltork,’ said Mrs.", "\"놀라운 분이세요, 스모를토크 백작님은.\""], ["Leo Hunter.", "리오 헌터 부인이 말했다."]], [["‘Sound philosopher,’ said Mr.", "\"건실한 철학자이지요.\""], ["Pott.", "포트 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Clear-headed, strong-minded person,’ added Mr.", "\"명석하고 의지가 굳센 분입니다.\""], ["Snodgrass.", "스노드그래스 씨가 덧붙였다."]], [["A chorus of bystanders took up the shout of Count Smorltork’s praise, shook their heads sagely, and unanimously cried, ‘Very!’", "곁에 있던 사람들이 일제히 스모를토크 백작을 칭송하는 외침을 받아, 현명하게 고개를 끄덕이며 한목소리로 외쳤다. \"정말로요!\""]], [["As the enthusiasm in Count Smorltork’s favour ran very high, his praises might have been sung until the end of the festivities, if the four something-ean singers had not ranged themselves in front of a small apple-tree, to look picturesque, and commenced singing their national songs, which appeared by no means difficult of execution, inasmuch as the grand secret seemed to be, that three of the something-ean singers should grunt, while the fourth howled.", "스모를토크 백작을 향한 열광이 워낙 드높았던 터라, 만일 무슨무슨 출신의 가수 넷이 그림 같은 모습을 연출하려고 작은 사과나무 앞에 늘어서서, 자기네 민요를 부르기 시작하지만 않았더라면, 그의 찬가는 잔치가 끝날 때까지 이어졌을지도 모른다. 그 민요는 부르기가 결코 어려워 보이지 않았으니, 그 위대한 비결인즉 무슨무슨 출신의 가수 셋이 꿀꿀거리는 동안 넷째가 울부짖는 것이었던 듯하다."], ["This interesting performance having concluded amidst the loud plaudits of the whole company, a boy forthwith proceeded to entangle himself with the rails of a chair, and to jump over it, and crawl under it, and fall down with it, and do everything but sit upon it, and then to make a cravat of his legs, and tie them round his neck, and then to illustrate the ease with which a human being can be made to look like a magnified toad--all which feats yielded high delight and satisfaction to the assembled spectators. After which, the voice of Mrs.", "이 흥미로운 공연이 온 좌중의 우레와 같은 갈채 속에 끝나자, 곧이어 한 소년이 의자의 살에 제 몸을 얽고는, 그것을 뛰어넘고, 그 밑으로 기어들고, 그것과 함께 넘어지고, 그 위에 앉는 것만 빼고는 온갖 짓을 다 하더니, 이어 제 다리로 넥타이를 만들어 목에 두르고, 그러고는 인간이 얼마나 쉽게 거대한 두꺼비처럼 보이게 될 수 있는지를 실연해 보였다—이 모든 묘기가 모여든 구경꾼들에게 크나큰 즐거움과 만족을 안겨 주었다."], ["Pott was heard to chirp faintly forth, something which courtesy interpreted into a song, which was all very classical, and strictly in character, because Apollo was himself a composer, and composers can very seldom sing their own music or anybody else’s, either. This was succeeded by Mrs.", "그러고 나서 포트 부인의 목소리가 희미하게 지저귀듯 흘러나오는 것이 들렸으니, 예의상 노래로 해석된 무언가였는데, 이는 매우 고전적이고 분장에도 엄격히 들어맞는 것이었다."], ["Leo Hunter’s recitation of her far-famed ‘Ode to an Expiring Frog,’ which was encored once, and would have been encored twice, if the major part of the guests, who thought it was high time to get something to eat, had not said that it was perfectly shameful to take advantage of Mrs. Hunter’s good nature.", "아폴로 자신이 작곡가였고, 작곡가란 자기 음악도 남의 음악도 좀처럼 노래할 줄 모르는 법이니까."], ["So although Mrs. Leo Hunter professed her perfect willingness to recite the ode again, her kind and considerate friends wouldn’t hear of it on any account; and the refreshment room being thrown open, all the people who had ever been there before, scrambled in with all possible despatch- -Mrs.", "이어서 리오 헌터 부인이 그 널리 알려진 '죽어 가는 개구리에게 부치는 송시'를 낭송했는데, 한 차례 앙코르를 받았고, 만일 이제 뭔가 먹을 때가 됐다고 여긴 손님들 대다수가 헌터 부인의 호의를 이용해 먹는 것은 정말 부끄러운 일이라고 말하지 않았더라면 두 차례 앙코르를 받았을 것이다."], ["Leo Hunter’s usual course of proceedings being, to issue cards for a hundred, and breakfast for fifty, or in other words to feed only the very particular lions, and let the smaller animals take care of themselves.", "그리하여 리오 헌터 부인은 송시를 다시 낭송할 뜻이 충분히 있노라 공언했으나, 친절하고 사려 깊은 친구들은 무슨 일이 있어도 들으려 하지 않았다. 그리고 다과실이 활짝 열리자, 전에 한 번이라도 거기 와 본 적이 있는 사람들은 모두 가능한 한 서둘러 우르르 밀려들었다.—리오 헌터 부인의 통상적인 방식인즉, 백 명에게 초대장을 보내고 쉰 명분의 조찬을 차리는 것, 다시 말해 아주 특별한 사자들에게만 먹을 것을 주고 작은 짐승들은 알아서 챙기게 두는 것이었다."]], [["‘Where is Mr. Pott?’ said Mrs.", "\"포트 씨는 어디 계세요?\""], ["Leo Hunter, as she placed the aforesaid lions around her.", "리오 헌터 부인이 앞서 말한 사자들을 자기 둘레에 앉히며 말했다."]], [["‘Here I am,’ said the editor, from the remotest end of the room; far beyond all hope of food, unless something was done for him by the hostess.", "\"여기 있습니다.\" 편집장이 방의 가장 먼 끝에서 말했으니, 안주인이 그를 위해 무언가 해 주지 않는 한 음식이라곤 바랄 가망이 전혀 없는 자리였다."]], [["‘Won’t you come up here?’", "\"이리 올라오시지 않겠어요?\""]], [["‘Oh, pray don’t mind him,’ said Mrs.", "\"아, 부디 저이는 신경 쓰지 마세요.\""], ["Pott, in the most obliging voice-- ‘you give yourself a great deal of unnecessary trouble, Mrs.", "포트 부인이 더없이 상냥한 목소리로 말했다. \"공연히 수고를 끼치고 계세요, 헌터 부인."], ["Hunter. You’ll do very well there, won’t you--dear?’", "저이는 거기서도 잘 지낼 거예요, 그렇죠—여보?\""]], [["‘Certainly--love,’ replied the unhappy Pott, with a grim smile.", "\"물론이지—여보.\" 불운한 포트가 음울한 미소를 띠며 대답했다."], ["Alas for the knout!", "아, 그 채찍이여!"], ["The nervous arm that wielded it, with such a gigantic force on public characters, was paralysed beneath the glance of the imperious Mrs. Pott.", "공인들에게 그토록 거대한 위력으로 그것을 휘두르던 그 굳센 팔이, 도도한 포트 부인의 눈길 아래 마비되고 말았다."]], [["Mrs. Leo Hunter looked round her in triumph.", "리오 헌터 부인은 의기양양하게 주위를 둘러보았다."], ["Count Smorltork was busily engaged in taking notes of the contents of the dishes; Mr. Tupman was doing the honours of the lobster salad to several lionesses, with a degree of grace which no brigand ever exhibited before; Mr.", "스모를토크 백작은 접시에 담긴 음식들의 내용을 메모하느라 분주했고, 터프먼 씨는 일찍이 어떤 산적도 보여 준 적 없는 우아함으로 여러 암사자들에게 바닷가재 샐러드를 권하고 있었다."], ["Snodgrass having cut out the young gentleman who cut up the books for the Eatanswill Gazette, was engaged in an impassioned argument with the young lady who did the poetry; and Mr. Pickwick was making himself universally agreeable.", "스노드그래스 씨는 이턴스윌 가제트의 책을 잘게 난도질하던 젊은 신사를 밀어내고는, 시를 담당하던 젊은 숙녀와 열정적인 토론에 빠져 있었으며, 픽윅 씨는 두루 누구에게나 호감을 사고 있었다."], ["Nothing seemed wanting to render the select circle complete, when Mr. Leo Hunter--whose department on these occasions, was to stand about in doorways, and talk to the less important people--suddenly called out--", "이 가려 뽑은 모임을 완벽하게 만드는 데 모자란 것이라곤 아무것도 없어 보였는데, 그때 리오 헌터 씨가—이런 자리에서 그의 소임이란 문간께 서성이며 덜 중요한 사람들과 이야기를 나누는 것이었다—문득 외쳤다.—"]], [["‘My dear; here’s Mr. Charles Fitz-Marshall.’", "\"여보, 여기 찰스 피츠마셜 씨가 왔소.\""]], [["‘Oh dear,’ said Mrs.", "\"어머나,\""], ["Leo Hunter, ‘how anxiously I have been expecting him.", "리오 헌터 부인이 말했다. \"내가 얼마나 애타게 기다렸는지 몰라요."], ["Pray make room, to let Mr. Fitz-Marshall pass.", "부디 길을 비켜서 피츠마셜 씨가 지나가게 해 주세요."], ["Tell Mr. Fitz- Marshall, my dear, to come up to me directly, to be scolded for coming so late.’", "여보, 피츠마셜 씨에게 곧장 내게로 올라오라고 일러 주세요, 이렇게 늦게 온 데 대해 야단을 좀 맞으셔야겠으니.\""]], [["‘Coming, my dear ma’am,’ cried a voice, ‘as quick as I can--crowds of people--full room--hard work--very.’", "\"가요, 친애하는 마님.\" 한 목소리가 외쳤다. \"최대한 빨리 가는 중입니다—사람들이 잔뜩—방이 꽉 차서—힘들군요—매우.\""]], [["Mr. Pickwick’s knife and fork fell from his hand.", "픽윅 씨의 나이프와 포크가 손에서 떨어졌다."], ["He stared across the table at Mr. Tupman, who had dropped his knife and fork, and was looking as if he were about to sink into the ground without further notice.", "그는 식탁 너머로 터프먼 씨를 응시했는데, 터프먼 씨도 나이프와 포크를 떨어뜨린 채, 더는 통보도 없이 그대로 땅속으로 꺼져 들어갈 듯한 표정을 짓고 있었다."]], [["‘Ah!’ cried the voice, as its owner pushed his way among the last five- and-twenty Turks, officers, cavaliers, and Charles the Seconds, that remained between him and the table, ‘regular mangle--Baker’s patent--not a crease in my coat, after all this squeezing--might have “got up my linen”", "\"아!\" 그 목소리가 외쳤으니, 그 주인이 자기와 식탁 사이에 남아 있는 마지막 스물다섯 명의 터키인과 장교와 기사와 찰스 이세들 틈을 비집고 나오면서였다."], ["as I came along--ha! ha!", "\"영락없는 압착기로군—베이커 특허품—이렇게 짓눌렸는데도 코트에 주름 하나 없어—오는 길에 내 속옷에 '풀까지 먹였을'"], ["not a bad idea, that--queer thing to have it mangled when it’s upon one, though--trying process--very.’", "법한데—하하! 나쁘지 않은 생각이군, 그거—하긴 몸에 걸친 채로 압착당하는 건 묘한 일이지—고된 과정이야—매우.\""]], [["With these broken words, a young man dressed as a naval officer made his way up to the table, and presented to the astonished Pickwickians the identical form and features of Mr. Alfred Jingle.", "이렇게 두서없는 말과 함께, 해군 장교 차림을 한 한 젊은 남자가 식탁으로 다가와, 경악한 픽윅 회원들 앞에 바로 그 앨프리드 징글 씨의 모습과 이목구비를 그대로 내보였다."]], [["The offender had barely time to take Mrs. Leo Hunter’s proffered hand, when his eyes encountered the indignant orbs of Mr. Pickwick.", "이 죄인은 리오 헌터 부인이 내민 손을 채 잡기도 전에, 그의 눈이 픽윅 씨의 분노로 이글거리는 눈동자와 마주쳤다."]], [["‘Hollo!’ said Jingle.", "\"이런!\" 징글이 말했다."], ["‘Quite forgot--no directions to postillion--give ‘em at once--back in a minute.’", "\"깜빡 잊었군—마부에게 지시를 안 했어—당장 일러 줘야겠소—금방 돌아오리다.\""]], [["‘The servant, or Mr. Hunter will do it in a moment, Mr.", "\"하인이나 헌터 씨가 곧 처리할 거예요, 피츠마셜 씨.\""], ["Fitz-Marshall,’ said Mrs. Leo Hunter.", "리오 헌터 부인이 말했다."]], [["‘No, no--I’ll do it--shan’t be long--back in no time,’ replied Jingle.", "\"아니, 아니오—내가 하겠소—오래 안 걸리오—곧 돌아오리다.\" 징글이 대답했다."], ["With these words he disappeared among the crowd.", "이 말과 함께 그는 군중 속으로 사라졌다."]], [["‘Will you allow me to ask you, ma’am,’ said the excited Mr.", "\"실례지만 여쭤봐도 되겠습니까, 마님,\""], ["Pickwick, rising from his seat, ‘who that young man is, and where he resides?’", "흥분한 픽윅 씨가 자리에서 일어나며 말했다. \"저 젊은이가 누구이며, 어디에 사는지를요?\""]], [["‘He is a gentleman of fortune, Mr.", "\"재산가이신 신사분이에요, 픽윅 씨.\""], ["Pickwick,’ said Mrs.", "리오 헌터 부인이 말했다."], ["Leo Hunter, ‘to whom I very much want to introduce you.", "\"제가 꼭 소개해 드리고 싶었던 분이지요."], ["The count will be delighted with him.’", "백작님도 저분을 무척 마음에 들어 하실 거예요.\""]], [["‘Yes, yes,’ said Mr.", "\"네, 네.\""], ["Pickwick hastily.", "픽윅 씨가 다급히 말했다."], ["‘His residence--’", "\"그가 사는 곳은—\""]], [["‘Is at present at the Angel at Bury.’", "\"지금은 베리의 에인절에 묵고 계세요.\""]], [["‘At Bury?’", "\"베리에요?\""]], [["‘At Bury St. Edmunds, not many miles from here.", "\"베리 세인트 에드먼즈요, 여기서 몇 마일 안 됩니다."], ["But dear me, Mr. Pickwick, you are not going to leave us; surely Mr.", "그런데 어머나, 픽윅 씨, 저희를 떠나시려는 건 아니겠지요."], ["Pickwick you cannot think of going so soon?’", "설마 픽윅 씨, 이렇게 일찍 가실 생각은 아니시겠지요?\""]], [["But long before Mrs. Leo Hunter had finished speaking, Mr. Pickwick had plunged through the throng, and reached the garden, whither he was shortly afterwards joined by Mr. Tupman, who had followed his friend closely.", "그러나 리오 헌터 부인이 말을 채 마치기도 한참 전에, 픽윅 씨는 인파를 헤치고 뛰어들어 정원에 다다랐으며, 곧이어 친구를 바짝 뒤따라온 터프먼 씨가 그와 합류했다."]], [["‘It’s of no use,’ said Mr.", "\"소용없습니다.\" 터프먼 씨가 말했다."], ["Tupman. ‘He has gone.’", "\"가 버렸어요.\""]], [["‘I know it,’ said Mr.", "\"알아.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick, ‘and I will follow him.’", "\"그러니 내가 그를 뒤쫓겠네.\""]], [["‘Follow him!", "\"뒤쫓다니! 어디로요?\""], ["Where?’ inquired Mr. Tupman.", "터프먼 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘To the Angel at Bury,’ replied Mr.", "\"베리의 에인절로.\""], ["Pickwick, speaking very quickly.", "픽윅 씨가 아주 빠르게 말했다."], ["‘How do we know whom he is deceiving there?", "\"그가 거기서 누구를 속이고 있는지 우리가 어찌 알겠나?"], ["He deceived a worthy man once, and we were the innocent cause.", "그는 전에도 훌륭한 사람 하나를 속였고, 우리가 그 죄 없는 빌미가 되었네."], ["He shall not do it again, if I can help it; I’ll expose him!", "내가 막을 수만 있다면 다시는 그러게 두지 않겠어. 그자의 정체를 폭로하겠네!"], ["Sam!", "샘!"], ["Where’s my servant?’", "내 시종은 어디 있나?\""]], [["‘Here you are, Sir,’ said Mr.", "\"여기 있습니다, 나리.\""], ["Weller, emerging from a sequestered spot, where he had been engaged in discussing a bottle of Madeira, which he had abstracted from the breakfast-table an hour or two before.", "웰러 씨가 한적한 자리에서 모습을 드러내며 말했으니, 한두 시간 전에 조찬 식탁에서 슬쩍 빼돌린 마데이라 한 병을 처리하느라 거기 있던 참이었다. \"여기 나리의 시종이 있습니다요."], ["‘Here’s your servant, Sir. Proud o’ the title, as the living skellinton said, ven they show’d him.’", "그 칭호가 자랑스럽습죠, 살아 있는 해골이 사람들이 자기를 구경시켜 줄 때 한 말마따나요.\""]], [["‘Follow me instantly,’ said Mr.", "\"당장 나를 따라오너라.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick. ‘Tupman, if I stay at Bury, you can join me there, when I write.", "\"터프먼, 내가 베리에 머물게 되면 편지를 쓸 테니 그때 거기서 합류하게나."], ["Till then, good-bye!’", "그때까진, 잘 있게!\""]], [["Remonstrances were useless. Mr. Pickwick was roused, and his mind was made up. Mr. Tupman returned to his companions; and in another hour had drowned all present recollection of Mr. Alfred Jingle, or Mr. Charles Fitz-Marshall, in an exhilarating quadrille and a bottle of champagne. By that time, Mr. Pickwick and Sam Weller, perched on the outside of a stage-coach, were every succeeding minute placing a less and less distance between themselves and the good old town of Bury St. Edmunds.", "만류해 봐야 소용없었다. 픽윅 씨는 분기탱천해 있었고, 마음을 굳힌 터였다. 터프먼 씨는 일행에게 돌아갔고, 한 시간이 채 못 되어 흥겨운 카드리유 한 곡과 샴페인 한 병에 앨프리드 징글 씨든 찰스 피츠마셜 씨든 당장의 기억을 모조리 묻어 버렸다. 그 무렵, 역마차 바깥자리에 올라탄 픽윅 씨와 샘 웰러는, 매분이 지날 때마다 자신들과 그 유서 깊은 옛 도시 베리 세인트 에드먼즈 사이의 거리를 점점 더 좁혀 가고 있었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XVI. TOO FULL OF ADVENTURE TO BE BRIEFLY DESCRIBED", "# CHAPTER XVI. TOO FULL OF ADVENTURE TO BE BRIEFLY DESCRIBED"]], [["There is no month in the whole year in which nature wears a more beautiful appearance than in the month of August.", "일 년 열두 달 가운데 자연이 8월보다 더 아름다운 모습을 띠는 달은 없다."], ["Spring has many beauties, and May is a fresh and blooming month, but the charms of this time of year are enhanced by their contrast with the winter season.", "봄에도 여러 아름다움이 있고, 5월은 싱그럽고 꽃이 만발하는 달이지만, 그 시절의 매력은 겨울철과 대비됨으로써 한층 돋보인다."], ["August has no such advantage.", "8월에는 그런 이점이 없다."], ["It comes when we remember nothing but clear skies, green fields, and sweet-smelling flowers--when the recollection of snow, and ice, and bleak winds, has faded from our minds as completely as they have disappeared from the earth--and yet what a pleasant time it is!", "8월은 우리가 맑은 하늘과 푸른 들판과 향기로운 꽃 말고는 아무것도 기억하지 못할 때—눈과 얼음과 매서운 바람의 기억이 땅에서 자취를 감춘 만큼이나 우리 마음에서도 말끔히 사라졌을 때—찾아온다. 그런데도 얼마나 즐거운 시절인가!"], ["Orchards and cornfields ring with the hum of labour; trees bend beneath the thick clusters of rich fruit which bow their branches to the ground; and the corn, piled in graceful sheaves, or waving in every light breath that sweeps above it, as if it wooed the sickle, tinges the landscape with a golden hue.", "과수원과 옥수수밭은 노동의 콧노래로 울려 퍼지고, 나무들은 가지를 땅까지 휘게 하는 탐스러운 열매 송이의 무게에 휘어진다. 그리고 곡식은 우아한 단으로 쌓여 있거나, 그 위를 스치는 산들바람마다 마치 낫에게 구애라도 하듯 물결치며 풍경을 황금빛으로 물들인다."], ["A mellow softness appears to hang over the whole earth; the influence of the season seems to extend itself to the very wagon, whose slow motion across the well- reaped field is perceptible only to the eye, but strikes with no harsh sound upon the ear.", "온 대지에 그윽한 부드러움이 감도는 듯하다. 계절의 영향은 잘 베어진 들판을 천천히 가로지르는 마차에까지 미치는 듯한데, 그 느린 움직임은 오직 눈에만 보일 뿐 귀에는 어떤 거친 소리도 와닿지 않는다."]], [["As the coach rolls swiftly past the fields and orchards which skirt the road, groups of women and children, piling the fruit in sieves, or gathering the scattered ears of corn, pause for an instant from their labour, and shading the sun-burned face with a still browner hand, gaze upon the passengers with curious eyes, while some stout urchin, too small to work, but too mischievous to be left at home, scrambles over the side of the basket in which he has been deposited for security, and kicks and screams with delight. The reaper stops in his work, and stands with folded arms, looking at the vehicle as it whirls past; and the rough cart-horses bestow a sleepy glance upon the smart coach team, which says as plainly as a horse’s glance can, ‘It’s all very fine to look at, but slow going, over a heavy field, is better than warm work like that, upon a dusty road, after all.’ You cast a look behind you, as you turn a corner of the road. The women and children have resumed their labour; the reaper once more stoops to his work; the cart-horses have moved on; and all are again in motion.", "마차가 길가에 늘어선 들판과 과수원을 빠르게 지나칠 때, 체에 과일을 담거나 흩어진 옥수수 이삭을 줍던 아낙네와 아이들의 무리는 잠시 일손을 멈추고, 햇볕에 그을린 얼굴을 그보다 더 까무잡잡한 손으로 가리며 호기심 어린 눈으로 승객들을 바라본다. 그런가 하면 일하기엔 너무 어리지만 집에 두기엔 너무 짓궂은 어떤 통통한 개구쟁이는 안전하게 담겨 있던 바구니 옆으로 기어 올라 발을 차며 기쁨에 겨워 소리를 지른다. 낫질하던 일꾼은 일손을 멈추고 팔짱을 낀 채 휙 지나가는 마차를 바라보고, 투박한 짐말들은 말끔한 역마차의 말 무리에게 졸린 듯한 눈길을 던지는데, 그 눈길은 말의 눈빛이 말할 수 있는 한 또렷이 이렇게 말하는 듯하다. \"보기엔 다 그럴듯하다만, 따지고 보면 먼지 자욱한 길 위에서 저렇게 땀나게 일하느니 무거운 들판을 느릿느릿 가는 편이 낫지.\" 길모퉁이를 돌면서 뒤를 한번 돌아본다. 아낙네와 아이들은 다시 일을 시작했고, 낫질하던 일꾼은 또다시 일감 위로 허리를 굽혔으며, 짐말들은 앞으로 나아갔다. 모두가 다시 움직이고 있다."]], [["The influence of a scene like this, was not lost upon the well-regulated mind of Mr. Pickwick.", "이런 광경의 영향이 픽윅 씨의 잘 다스려진 마음에 헛되지 않았다."], ["Intent upon the resolution he had formed, of exposing the real character of the nefarious Jingle, in any quarter in which he might be pursuing his fraudulent designs, he sat at first taciturn and contemplative, brooding over the means by which his purpose could be best attained.", "그는 사기 행각을 벌이고 있는 곳이 어디든 비열한 징글의 본색을 폭로하겠다는 결심에 골몰한 채, 처음에는 말없이 깊은 생각에 잠겨, 목적을 가장 잘 이룰 수 있는 방법을 곰곰이 궁리하며 앉아 있었다."], ["By degrees his attention grew more and more attracted by the objects around him; and at last he derived as much enjoyment from the ride, as if it had been undertaken for the pleasantest reason in the world.", "차츰 그의 주의는 주변의 사물에 점점 더 끌렸고, 마침내 그는 마치 세상에서 가장 즐거운 이유로 이 길을 나선 것처럼 마차 여행을 한껏 즐기게 되었다."]], [["‘Delightful prospect, Sam,’ said Mr.", "\"경치가 좋군, 샘.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Beats the chimbley-pots, Sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"굴뚝 항아리보다야 훨씬 낫지요, 나리.\""], ["Weller, touching his hat.", "웰러 씨가 모자에 손을 대며 대답했다."]], [["‘I suppose you have hardly seen anything but chimney-pots and bricks and mortar all your life, Sam,’ said Mr.", "\"자네는 평생 굴뚝 항아리와 벽돌과 모르타르 말고는 거의 본 게 없겠지, 샘.\""], ["Pickwick, smiling.", "픽윅 씨가 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["‘I worn’t always a boots, sir,’ said Mr.", "\"전 늘 구두닦이였던 건 아니랍니다, 나리.\""], ["Weller, with a shake of the head.", "웰러 씨가 고개를 저으며 말했다."], ["‘I wos a vaginer’s boy, once.’", "\"한때는 짐마차꾼 조수였지요.\""]], [["‘When was that?’ inquired Mr.", "\"그게 언제였나?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘When I wos first pitched neck and crop into the world, to play at leap- frog with its troubles,’ replied Sam.", "\"제가 처음으로 세상에 머리부터 내동댕이쳐져, 세상의 온갖 골칫거리와 등넘기 놀이를 하게 됐을 때였죠.\" 샘이 대답했다."], ["‘I wos a carrier’s boy at startin’; then a vaginer’s, then a helper, then a boots.", "\"처음엔 운송꾼 조수였고, 그다음엔 짐마차꾼 조수, 그다음엔 거들이꾼, 그다음엔 구두닦이였습니다."], ["Now I’m a gen’l’m’n’s servant.", "이제는 신사의 하인이지요."], ["I shall be a gen’l’m’n myself one of these days, perhaps, with a pipe in my mouth, and a summer-house in the back-garden.", "언젠가는 저 자신이 신사가 될지도 모릅니다요. 입에 파이프를 물고, 뒤뜰에 정자도 하나 두고 말입니다."], ["Who knows?", "누가 알겠어요?"], ["I shouldn’t be surprised for one.’", "저로선 별로 놀랄 일도 아니지요.\""]], [["‘You are quite a philosopher, Sam,’ said Mr.", "\"자네는 아주 철학자로군, 샘.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘It runs in the family, I b’lieve, sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"그건 집안 내력인가 봅니다, 나리.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다."], ["Weller. ‘My father’s wery much in that line now.", "\"제 아버지도 지금 그쪽으로 아주 정통하시죠."], ["If my mother-in-law blows him up, he whistles.", "새어머니가 아버지한테 바가지를 긁으면, 아버지는 휘파람을 붑니다."], ["She flies in a passion, and breaks his pipe; he steps out, and gets another.", "그러면 어머니는 발끈해서 아버지 파이프를 부러뜨리죠. 아버지는 밖으로 나가 다른 걸 하나 구해 옵니다."], ["Then she screams wery loud, and falls into ‘sterics; and he smokes wery comfortably till she comes to agin.", "그러면 어머니는 아주 큰 소리로 비명을 지르고 발작을 일으키죠. 아버지는 어머니가 다시 정신 차릴 때까지 아주 편안하게 담배를 피웁니다."], ["That’s philosophy, Sir, ain’t it?’", "그게 철학이지요, 나리, 안 그렇습니까?\""]], [["‘A very good substitute for it, at all events,’ replied Mr.", "\"적어도 철학을 아주 잘 대신하는 것이긴 하군.\""], ["Pickwick, laughing.", "픽윅 씨가 웃으며 대답했다."], ["‘It must have been of great service to you, in the course of your rambling life, Sam.’", "\"자네의 떠돌이 인생살이에서 그게 큰 도움이 되었겠어, 샘.\""]], [["‘Service, sir,’ exclaimed Sam.", "\"도움이라뇨, 나리.\""], ["‘You may say that.", "샘이 외쳤다."], ["Arter I run away from the carrier, and afore I took up with the vaginer, I had unfurnished lodgin’s for a fortnight.’", "\"그렇게 말씀하셔도 좋습니다. 운송꾼한테서 도망친 다음, 짐마차꾼한테 가기 전에, 전 두 주 동안 가구 없는 셋방에 묵었습죠.\""]], [["‘Unfurnished lodgings?’ said Mr.", "\"가구 없는 셋방이라고?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Yes--the dry arches of Waterloo Bridge.", "\"네—워털루 다리의 마른 아치 밑이었지요."], ["Fine sleeping-place--vithin ten minutes’ walk of all the public offices--only if there is any objection to it, it is that the sitivation’s rayther too airy.", "잠자리로는 그만이었습니다요—온갖 관공서에서 걸어서 십 분 거리고요—다만 흠을 잡자면, 자리가 좀 지나치게 바람이 잘 통한다는 거랄까요."], ["I see some queer sights there.’", "거기서 별별 희한한 광경을 다 봤습니다.\""]], [["Ah, I suppose you did,’ said Mr.", "\"아, 그랬겠지.\""], ["Pickwick, with an air of considerable interest.", "픽윅 씨가 자못 흥미롭다는 기색으로 말했다."]], [["‘Sights, sir,’ resumed Mr.", "\"광경이라뇨, 나리.\""], ["Weller, ‘as ‘ud penetrate your benevolent heart, and come out on the other side.", "웰러 씨가 다시 입을 열었다. \"나리의 자비로운 가슴을 꿰뚫고 반대편으로 빠져나갈 만한 광경들이었죠."], ["You don’t see the reg’lar wagrants there; trust ‘em, they knows better than that.", "거기서 변변한 부랑자들은 볼 수 없습니다요. 믿으십시오, 그자들은 그보다 더 잘 알거든요."], ["Young beggars, male and female, as hasn’t made a rise in their profession, takes up their quarters there sometimes; but it’s generally the worn-out, starving, houseless creeturs as roll themselves in the dark corners o’ them lonesome places--poor creeturs as ain’t up to the twopenny rope.’", "자기 직업에서 출세하지 못한 젊은 거지들, 남자고 여자고 더러 거기다 잠자리를 틀기도 합죠. 하지만 대개는 그 외롭고 어두운 구석에 몸을 웅크리는 건 닳고 닳아 굶주리고 집 없는 가련한 것들입니다요—이 펜스짜리 밧줄 값도 못 대는 불쌍한 것들 말입니다.\""]], [["‘And pray, Sam, what is the twopenny rope?’ inquired Mr.", "\"그런데 말이지, 샘, 이 펜스짜리 밧줄이라는 게 뭔가?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘The twopenny rope, sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"이 펜스짜리 밧줄이라는 건요, 나리.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다."], ["Weller, ‘is just a cheap lodgin’ house, where the beds is twopence a night.’", "\"그냥 싸구려 여인숙입죠. 거기선 침대가 하룻밤에 이 펜스랍니다.\""]], [["‘What do they call a bed a rope for?’ said Mr.", "\"왜 침대를 밧줄이라고 부르나?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Bless your innocence, sir, that ain’t it,’ replied Sam. ‘Ven the lady and gen’l’m’n as keeps the hot-el first begun business, they used to make the beds on the floor; but this wouldn’t do at no price, ‘cos instead o’ taking a moderate twopenn’orth o’ sleep, the lodgers used to lie there half the day. So now they has two ropes, ‘bout six foot apart, and three from the floor, which goes right down the room; and the beds are made of slips of coarse sacking, stretched across ‘em.’", "\"순진하시기도 하셔라, 나리, 그게 아닙니다요.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"그 호텔을 운영하는 양반네 부부가 처음 장사를 시작했을 때는 침대를 바닥에 깔았습죠. 그런데 그게 도무지 수지가 안 맞았던 게, 손님들이 적당히 이 펜스어치 잠만 자는 게 아니라 한낮까지 거기 드러누워 있었거든요. 그래서 이제는 밧줄을 두 줄 매답니다요. 한 육 피트 간격으로, 바닥에서 삼 피트 높이에다 방을 가로질러 쭉 늘이는 거죠. 그리고 거친 자루천 조각을 그 위에 가로질러 펼쳐 침대를 만드는 겁니다.\""]], [["‘Well,’ said Mr.", "\"그래서?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Well,’ said Mr.", "\"그래서요.\""], ["Weller, ‘the adwantage o’ the plan’s hobvious.", "웰러 씨가 말했다. \"그 방법의 이점이 뻔하지요."], ["At six o’clock every mornin’ they let’s go the ropes at one end, and down falls the lodgers.", "매일 아침 여섯 시면 밧줄의 한쪽 끝을 풀어 버립니다요. 그러면 손님들이 우르르 떨어지죠."], ["Consequence is, that being thoroughly waked, they get up wery quietly, and walk away!’", "그 결과 완전히 잠이 깬 손님들은 아주 조용히 일어나 걸어 나가는 거죠!\""]], [["‘Beg your pardon, sir,’ said Sam, suddenly breaking off in his loquacious discourse.", "\"실례합니다, 나리.\" 샘이 그 수다스러운 이야기를 갑자기 멈추며 말했다."], ["‘Is this Bury St. Edmunds?’", "\"여기가 베리 세인트 에드먼즈입니까?\""]], [["‘It is,’ replied Mr.", "\"그렇다네.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 대답했다."]], [["The coach rattled through the well-paved streets of a handsome little town, of thriving and cleanly appearance, and stopped before a large inn situated in a wide open street, nearly facing the old abbey.", "마차는 번창하고 깔끔한 모습의 아담하고 멋진 도시의 잘 포장된 거리를 덜컹덜컹 지나, 옛 수도원을 거의 마주 보는 넓고 탁 트인 거리에 자리한 큰 여관 앞에 멈췄다."]], [["‘And this,’ said Mr.", "\"그리고 이게.\""], ["Pickwick, looking up.", "픽윅 씨가 올려다보며 말했다."], ["‘Is the Angel!", "\"에인절 여관이로군!"], ["We alight here, Sam.", "여기서 내리세, 샘."], ["But some caution is necessary.", "하지만 좀 조심해야 하네."], ["Order a private room, and do not mention my name.", "개인실을 하나 주문하고, 내 이름은 입에 올리지 말게."], ["You understand.’", "알겠나.\""]], [["‘Right as a trivet, sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"빈틈없이 하겠습니다, 나리.\""], ["Weller, with a wink of intelligence; and having dragged Mr.", "웰러 씨가 알겠다는 듯 눈을 찡긋하며 대답했다."], ["Pickwick’s portmanteau from the hind boot, into which it had been hastily thrown when they joined the coach at Eatanswill, Mr. Weller disappeared on his errand.", "그러고는 이턴스윌에서 마차에 합류할 때 급히 던져 넣었던 뒤쪽 짐칸에서 픽윅 씨의 여행 가방을 끌어낸 뒤, 웰러 씨는 심부름하러 사라졌다."], ["A private room was speedily engaged; and into it Mr. Pickwick was ushered without delay.", "개인실은 곧 잡혔고, 픽윅 씨는 지체 없이 그리로 안내되었다."]], [["‘Now, Sam,’ said Mr.", "\"자, 샘.\""], ["Pickwick, ‘the first thing to be done is to--’", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"제일 먼저 해야 할 일은—\""]], [["Order dinner, Sir,’ interposed Mr.", "\"저녁부터 시키는 겁니다, 나리.\" 웰러 씨가 끼어들었다."], ["Weller. ‘It’s wery late, sir.’", "\"시간이 아주 늦었습니다요, 나리.\""]], [["‘Ah, so it is,’ said Mr.", "\"아, 그렇군.\""], ["Pickwick, looking at his watch.", "픽윅 씨가 시계를 보며 말했다."], ["‘You are right, Sam.’", "\"자네 말이 맞네, 샘.\""]], [["‘And if I might adwise, Sir,’ added Mr.", "\"그리고 제가 한 말씀 올려도 된다면, 나리.\""], ["Weller, ‘I’d just have a good night’s rest arterwards, and not begin inquiring arter this here deep ‘un till the mornin’.", "웰러 씨가 덧붙였다. \"그다음엔 푹 한잠 주무시고, 이 음흉한 자를 캐묻는 일은 아침까지 미루시는 게 좋겠습니다요."], ["There’s nothin’ so refreshen’ as sleep, sir, as the servant girl said afore she drank the egg-cupful of laudanum.’", "잠만큼 기운 차리게 하는 건 없으니까요, 나리, 하녀 아이가 달걀잔으로 아편 한 잔을 들이켜기 전에 했던 말처럼 말입니다.\""]], [["‘I think you are right, Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick.", "\"자네 말이 맞는 것 같네, 샘.\""], ["‘But I must first ascertain that he is in the house, and not likely to go away.’", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"하지만 먼저 그자가 여기 묵고 있는지, 떠날 낌새는 없는지 확인해야겠어.\""]], [["‘Leave that to me, Sir,’ said Sam.", "\"그건 제게 맡기십시오, 나리.\" 샘이 말했다."], ["‘Let me order you a snug little dinner, and make my inquiries below while it’s a-getting ready; I could worm ev’ry secret out O’ the boots’s heart, in five minutes, Sir.’", "\"아담한 저녁을 시켜 드리게 해 주시고, 그게 준비되는 동안 제가 아래층에서 알아보겠습니다요. 구두닦이의 가슴속에서 비밀이란 비밀은 죄다 오 분 안에 끄집어낼 수 있습죠, 나리.\""]], [["Do so,’ said Mr.", "\"그렇게 하게.\""], ["Pickwick; and Mr.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Weller at once retired.", "그러자 웰러 씨는 즉시 물러갔다."]], [["In half an hour, Mr. Pickwick was seated at a very satisfactory dinner; and in three-quarters Mr. Weller returned with the intelligence that Mr. Charles Fitz-Marshall had ordered his private room to be retained for him, until further notice. He was going to spend the evening at some private house in the neighbourhood, had ordered the boots to sit up until his return, and had taken his servant with him.", "삼십 분 뒤 픽윅 씨는 아주 흡족한 저녁상 앞에 앉아 있었고, 사십오 분 뒤 웰러 씨는 찰스 피츠마셜 씨가 추후 통지가 있을 때까지 자기 개인실을 비워 두지 말라고 일러 두었다는 소식을 가지고 돌아왔다. 그자는 근처의 어느 개인 저택에서 저녁을 보낼 예정이었고, 구두닦이에게 자기가 돌아올 때까지 자지 말고 기다리라고 일러 두었으며, 하인을 데리고 갔다고 했다."]], [["‘Now, sir,’ argued Mr.", "\"자, 나리.\""], ["Weller, when he had concluded his report, ‘if I can get a talk with this here servant in the mornin’, he’ll tell me all his master’s concerns.’", "웰러 씨가 보고를 마치고 나서 주장했다. \"제가 아침에 이 하인 녀석하고 얘기를 나눌 수 있다면, 그자가 제 주인의 일이란 일은 죄다 털어놓을 겁니다요.\""]], [["‘How do you know that?’ interposed Mr.", "\"그걸 어찌 아나?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 끼어들었다."]], [["‘Bless your heart, sir, servants always do,’ replied Mr.", "\"세상에, 나리, 하인들은 늘 그러거든요.\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘Oh, ah, I forgot that,’ said Mr.", "\"오, 아, 그걸 깜빡했군.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["‘Well.’", "\"좋아.\""]], [["‘Then you can arrange what’s best to be done, sir, and we can act accordingly.’", "\"그러면 나리께서 어떻게 하는 게 최선인지 정하시고, 저희는 거기에 맞춰 움직이면 됩니다요.\""]], [["As it appeared that this was the best arrangement that could be made, it was finally agreed upon. Mr.", "이것이 세울 수 있는 최선의 방안인 듯했으므로, 결국 그렇게 합의되었다."], ["Weller, by his master’s permission, retired to spend the evening in his own way; and was shortly afterwards elected, by the unanimous voice of the assembled company, into the taproom chair, in which honourable post he acquitted himself so much to the satisfaction of the gentlemen-frequenters, that their roars of laughter and approbation penetrated to Mr.", "웰러 씨는 주인의 허락을 받아 자기 식대로 저녁을 보내러 물러갔고, 곧이어 모여든 일행의 만장일치로 술집 좌장 자리에 추대되었다."], ["Pickwick’s bedroom, and shortened the term of his natural rest by at least three hours.", "그 영예로운 자리에서 그가 어찌나 단골 신사들을 흡족하게 했던지, 그들의 웃음소리와 갈채가 픽윅 씨의 침실까지 뚫고 들어와 그의 자연스러운 휴식 시간을 적어도 세 시간은 단축시켰다."]], [["Early on the ensuing morning, Mr.", "이튿날 아침 일찍, 웰러 씨는 반 페니짜리 물세례의 힘을 빌려(마구간 일을 보는 어린 친구에게 그 동전을 주고는, 완전히 정신이 들 때까지 머리와 얼굴에 물을 퍼붓게 했다) 전날 저녁 흥청거림이 남긴 열기를 떨쳐 내고 있었다."], ["Weller was dispelling all the feverish remains of the previous evening’s conviviality, through the instrumentality of a halfpenny shower-bath (having induced a young gentleman attached to the stable department, by the offer of that coin, to pump over his head and face, until he was perfectly restored), when he was attracted by the appearance of a young fellow in mulberry- coloured livery, who was sitting on a bench in the yard, reading what appeared to be a hymn-book, with an air of deep abstraction, but who occasionally stole a glance at the individual under the pump, as if he took some interest in his proceedings, nevertheless.", "그러던 중 그는 마당 벤치에 앉아 찬송가 책으로 보이는 것을 깊은 상념에 잠긴 듯 읽고 있는, 오디색 제복을 입은 한 젊은 친구의 모습에 눈길이 끌렸다. 그 친구는 그래도 가끔씩 펌프 아래 있는 사람에게 슬쩍슬쩍 눈길을 던졌는데, 마치 그 사람의 행동에 어떤 관심이라도 있는 듯했다."]], [["‘You’re a rum ‘un to look at, you are!’ thought Mr.", "'너 참 희한하게 생겨 먹었구나, 너 말이야!'"], ["Weller, the first time his eyes encountered the glance of the stranger in the mulberry suit, who had a large, sallow, ugly face, very sunken eyes, and a gigantic head, from which depended a quantity of lank black hair.", "웰러 씨는 오디색 옷을 입은 낯선 자의 눈길과 처음 마주쳤을 때 그렇게 생각했다. 그자는 누렇고 큼지막한 못생긴 얼굴에 움푹 꺼진 눈, 그리고 숱 없는 검은 머리가 늘어진 거대한 머리통을 하고 있었다."], ["‘You’re a rum ‘un!’ thought Mr.", "'너 참 희한한 놈이로구나!'"], ["Weller; and thinking this, he went on washing himself, and thought no more about him.", "웰러 씨는 그렇게 생각하고는, 그렇게 생각하며 계속 몸을 씻었고, 더는 그자에 대해 생각하지 않았다."]], [["Still the man kept glancing from his hymn-book to Sam, and from Sam to his hymn-book, as if he wanted to open a conversation.", "그래도 그 사내는 마치 대화를 시작하고 싶기라도 한 듯 찬송가 책에서 샘에게로, 샘에게서 다시 찬송가 책으로 자꾸 눈길을 옮겼다."], ["So at last, Sam, by way of giving him an opportunity, said with a familiar nod--", "그래서 마침내 샘은 기회를 주자는 뜻으로 친근하게 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다."]], [["‘How are you, governor?’", "\"안녕하시오, 양반?\""]], [["‘I am happy to say, I am pretty well, Sir,’ said the man, speaking with great deliberation, and closing the book.", "\"덕분에 꽤 잘 지낸다고 말씀드릴 수 있어 기쁩니다, 선생.\" 그 사내가 아주 신중하게 말하며 책을 덮었다."], ["‘I hope you are the same, Sir?’", "\"선생도 마찬가지이시길 바랍니다.\""]], [["‘Why, if I felt less like a walking brandy-bottle I shouldn’t be quite so staggery this mornin’,’ replied Sam.", "\"글쎄, 내가 걸어 다니는 브랜디 병 같은 기분만 좀 덜했어도, 오늘 아침에 이렇게 비틀거리진 않았을 텐데.\""], ["‘Are you stoppin’ in this house, old ‘un?’", "샘이 대답했다. \"이 집에 묵고 계시오, 양반?\""]], [["The mulberry man replied in the affirmative.", "오디색 옷의 사내는 그렇다고 대답했다."]], [["‘How was it you worn’t one of us, last night?’ inquired Sam, scrubbing his face with the towel.", "\"어쩌다 어젯밤엔 우리 자리에 안 끼셨소?\" 샘이 수건으로 얼굴을 문지르며 물었다."], ["‘You seem one of the jolly sort--looks as conwivial as a live trout in a lime basket,’ added Mr.", "\"흥겨운 부류로 보이는데 말이오—석회 바구니에 든 살아 있는 송어만큼이나 잔치를 즐길 상이구먼.\""], ["Weller, in an undertone.", "웰러 씨가 나직이 덧붙였다."]], [["‘I was out last night with my master,’ replied the stranger.", "\"어젯밤엔 주인 나리와 함께 나가 있었습니다.\" 낯선 자가 대답했다."]], [["‘What’s his name?’ inquired Mr.", "\"그분 이름이 뭐요?\""], ["Weller, colouring up very red with sudden excitement, and the friction of the towel combined.", "웰러 씨가 갑작스러운 흥분과 수건 마찰이 겹쳐 얼굴이 시뻘게지며 물었다."]], [["‘Fitz-Marshall,’ said the mulberry man.", "\"피츠마셜입니다.\" 오디색 옷의 사내가 말했다."]], [["‘Give us your hand,’ said Mr.", "\"악수합시다.\" 웰러 씨가 다가서며 말했다."], ["Weller, advancing; ‘I should like to know you.", "\"당신과 좀 알고 지내고 싶구려."], ["I like your appearance, old fellow.’", "당신 인상이 마음에 드는걸, 이 친구.\""]], [["‘Well, that is very strange,’ said the mulberry man, with great simplicity of manner.", "\"그거 참 묘하군요.\" 오디색 옷의 사내가 아주 순박한 태도로 말했다."], ["‘I like yours so much, that I wanted to speak to you, from the very first moment I saw you under the pump.’", "\"저도 당신 인상이 어찌나 마음에 들던지, 펌프 아래 있는 당신을 처음 본 순간부터 말을 걸고 싶었거든요.\""]], [["Did you though?’", "\"정말이오?\""]], [["‘Upon my word.", "\"정말입니다."], ["Now, isn’t that curious?’", "자, 이상하지 않습니까?\""]], [["‘Wery sing’ler,’ said Sam, inwardly congratulating himself upon the softness of the stranger.", "\"아주 희한하구먼.\" 샘은 그 낯선 자의 무름을 속으로 흐뭇해하며 말했다."], ["‘What’s your name, my patriarch?’", "\"이름이 뭐요, 영감님?\""]], [["‘Job.’", "\"잡이오.\""]], [["‘And a wery good name it is; only one I know that ain’t got a nickname to it.", "\"아주 좋은 이름이구먼. 별명이 붙지 않은 이름은 그것밖에 모르겠소."], ["What’s the other name?’", "다른 이름은 뭐요?\""]], [["‘Trotter,’ said the stranger.", "\"트로터요.\" 낯선 자가 말했다."], ["‘What is yours?’", "\"당신은 뭐요?\""]], [["Sam bore in mind his master’s caution, and replied--", "샘은 주인의 당부를 명심하고 대답했다."]], [["‘My name’s Walker; my master’s name’s Wilkins.", "\"내 이름은 워커요. 우리 주인 이름은 윌킨스고."], ["Will you take a drop o’ somethin’ this mornin’, Mr. Trotter?’", "오늘 아침에 뭐 한잔 드시겠소, 트로터 씨?\""]], [["Mr. Trotter acquiesced in this agreeable proposal; and having deposited his book in his coat pocket, accompanied Mr.", "트로터 씨는 그 반가운 제안에 응했고, 책을 외투 주머니에 넣은 뒤 웰러 씨를 따라 술집으로 갔다."], ["Weller to the tap, where they were soon occupied in discussing an exhilarating compound, formed by mixing together, in a pewter vessel, certain quantities of British Hollands and the fragrant essence of the clove.", "거기서 두 사람은 곧 백랍 그릇에다 일정량의 영국산 진과 향긋한 정향 에센스를 섞어 만든, 기운 돋우는 혼합주를 음미하는 일에 빠져들었다."]], [["‘And what sort of a place have you got?’ inquired Sam, as he filled his companion’s glass, for the second time.", "\"그런데 일자리는 어떻소?\" 샘이 동료의 잔을 두 번째로 채우며 물었다."]], [["‘Bad,’ said Job, smacking his lips, ‘very bad.’", "\"형편없죠.\" 잡이 입맛을 다시며 말했다. \"아주 형편없습니다.\""]], [["‘You don’t mean that?’ said Sam.", "\"설마 그럴 리가?\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["‘I do, indeed.", "\"정말입니다."], ["Worse than that, my master’s going to be married.’", "게다가 그보다 더한 게, 우리 주인 나리가 결혼하려 한다는 겁니다.\""]], [["‘No.’", "\"설마.\""]], [["‘Yes; and worse than that, too, he’s going to run away with an immense rich heiress, from boarding-school.’", "\"정말이오. 그리고 그보다 더한 게, 어마어마하게 부유한 상속녀를, 기숙학교에서 빼내 도망치려 한다는 거죠.\""]], [["‘What a dragon!’ said Sam, refilling his companion’s glass.", "\"그런 못된 놈이!\" 샘이 동료의 잔을 다시 채우며 말했다."], ["‘It’s some boarding-school in this town, I suppose, ain’t it?’ Now, although this question was put in the most careless tone imaginable, Mr.", "\"이 도시 어딘가에 있는 기숙학교겠구먼, 안 그렇소?\""], ["Job Trotter plainly showed by gestures that he perceived his new friend’s anxiety to draw forth an answer to it.", "그런데 이 질문은 상상할 수 있는 한 가장 무심한 어조로 던져졌건만, 잡 트로터 씨는 새 친구가 거기에 대한 답을 끌어내고 싶어 안달하는 것을 알아챘다는 것을 몸짓으로 분명히 드러냈다."], ["He emptied his glass, looked mysteriously at his companion, winked both of his small eyes, one after the other, and finally made a motion with his arm, as if he were working an imaginary pump-handle; thereby intimating that he (Mr.", "그는 잔을 비우고, 의미심장하게 동료를 바라보았으며, 작은 두 눈을 하나씩 차례로 찡긋한 뒤, 마침내 팔을 움직여 마치 보이지 않는 펌프 손잡이를 작동시키는 듯한 동작을 했다."], ["Trotter) considered himself as undergoing the process of being pumped by Mr. Samuel Weller.", "그럼으로써 자기(트로터 씨)가 새뮤얼 웰러 씨에게 펌프질당하는, 즉 정보를 캐내어지는 과정을 겪고 있다고 여긴다는 것을 넌지시 알린 것이었다."]], [["‘No, no,’ said Mr.", "\"아니, 아니죠.\""], ["Trotter, in conclusion, ‘that’s not to be told to everybody.", "트로터 씨가 결론지어 말했다. \"그건 아무한테나 말할 게 못 됩니다."], ["That is a secret--a great secret, Mr.", "그건 비밀이거든요—굉장한 비밀이죠, 워커 씨.\""], ["Walker.’ As the mulberry man said this, he turned his glass upside down, by way of reminding his companion that he had nothing left wherewith to slake his thirst.", "오디색 옷의 사내는 이렇게 말하며, 동료에게 이제 갈증을 풀 만한 것이 남아 있지 않다는 것을 일깨워 주려는 듯 잔을 거꾸로 뒤집었다."], ["Sam observed the hint; and feeling the delicate manner in which it was conveyed, ordered the pewter vessel to be refilled, whereat the small eyes of the mulberry man glistened.", "샘은 그 암시를 알아채고, 그것이 전달된 섬세한 방식을 헤아려, 백랍 그릇을 다시 채우라고 시켰다. 그러자 오디색 옷의 사내의 작은 눈이 반짝였다."]], [["‘And so it’s a secret?’ said Sam.", "\"그래서 그게 비밀이라고?\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["‘I should rather suspect it was,’ said the mulberry man, sipping his liquor, with a complacent face.", "\"그렇다고 봐야겠죠.\" 오디색 옷의 사내가 흡족한 얼굴로 술을 홀짝이며 말했다."]], [["‘I suppose your mas’r’s wery rich?’ said Sam.", "\"당신 주인이 아주 부자인가 보군?\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["Mr.", "트로터 씨는 미소를 지으며, 왼손에 잔을 든 채 오른손으로 그 오디색 형용할 수 없는 옷의 주머니를 또렷이 네 번 두드렸다."], ["Trotter smiled, and holding his glass in his left hand, gave four distinct slaps on the pockets of his mulberry indescribables with his right, as if to intimate that his master might have done the same without alarming anybody much by the chinking of coin.", "마치 자기 주인도 그렇게 했더라면 동전 짤랑거리는 소리로 누구를 크게 놀라게 하는 일 없이 그럴 수 있었을 거라고 넌지시 알리려는 듯이."]], [["‘Ah,’ said Sam, ‘that’s the game, is it?’", "\"아하.\" 샘이 말했다. \"그게 수작이라 이거지?\""]], [["The mulberry man nodded significantly.", "오디색 옷의 사내가 의미심장하게 고개를 끄덕였다."]], [["‘Well, and don’t you think, old feller,’ remonstrated Mr.", "\"그래, 이 친구야, 당신은 이런 생각 안 드시오.\" 웰러 씨가 따지듯 말했다."], ["Weller, ‘that if you let your master take in this here young lady, you’re a precious rascal?’", "\"당신 주인이 이 아가씨를 채 가게 내버려 둔다면, 당신도 아주 못된 악당이라는 생각 말이오?\""]], [["‘I know that,’ said Job Trotter, turning upon his companion a countenance of deep contrition, and groaning slightly, ‘I know that, and that’s what it is that preys upon my mind. But what am I to do?’", "\"저도 압니다.\" 잡 트로터가 동료에게로 깊이 뉘우치는 얼굴을 돌리고 가볍게 신음하며 말했다. \"저도 압니다. 그게 바로 제 마음을 갉아먹는 거죠. 하지만 제가 뭘 어쩌겠습니까?\""]], [["‘Do!’ said Sam; ‘di-wulge to the missis, and give up your master.’", "\"어쩌긴!\" 샘이 말했다. \"마님한테 까발리고, 당신 주인을 버리는 거지.\""]], [["‘Who’d believe me?’ replied Job Trotter.", "\"누가 제 말을 믿겠습니까?\""], ["‘The young lady’s considered the very picture of innocence and discretion.", "잡 트로터가 대답했다. \"그 아가씨는 순결과 신중함의 표본으로 통하거든요."], ["She’d deny it, and so would my master.", "아가씨는 부인할 테고, 우리 주인도 부인할 겁니다."], ["Who’d believe me?", "누가 제 말을 믿겠습니까?"], ["I should lose my place, and get indicted for a conspiracy, or some such thing; that’s all I should take by my motion.’", "전 일자리를 잃을 테고, 음모죄인지 뭔지로 기소되겠죠. 제가 나서서 얻을 거라곤 그게 다입니다.\""]], [["‘There’s somethin’ in that,’ said Sam, ruminating; ‘there’s somethin’ in that.’", "\"거기엔 일리가 있군.\" 샘이 곰곰이 생각하며 말했다. \"거기엔 일리가 있어.\""]], [["‘If I knew any respectable gentleman who would take the matter up,’ continued Mr.", "\"이 일을 떠맡아 줄 점잖은 신사분을 한 분이라도 안다면.\" 트로터 씨가 말을 이었다."], ["Trotter. ‘I might have some hope of preventing the elopement; but there’s the same difficulty, Mr.", "\"그 도망 행각을 막을 가망이 좀 있을지도 모르죠."], ["Walker, just the same.", "하지만 똑같은 어려움이 있단 말입니다, 워커 씨, 꼭 그대로요."], ["I know no gentleman in this strange place; and ten to one if I did, whether he would believe my story.’", "이 낯선 곳에 아는 신사가 한 분도 없으니까요. 설령 안다 한들 그분이 제 이야기를 믿어 줄지는 십중팔구 모를 일이고요.\""]], [["‘Come this way,’ said Sam, suddenly jumping up, and grasping the mulberry man by the arm.", "\"이리 와 보시오.\" 샘이 갑자기 벌떡 일어나 오디색 옷의 사내의 팔을 붙잡으며 말했다."], ["‘My mas’r’s the man you want, I see.’ And after a slight resistance on the part of Job Trotter, Sam led his newly-found friend to the apartment of Mr.", "\"우리 주인 나리가 바로 당신이 찾는 분이오, 알겠소.\""], ["Pickwick, to whom he presented him, together with a brief summary of the dialogue we have just repeated.", "그리고 잡 트로터가 약간 저항했으나, 샘은 새로 사귄 친구를 픽윅 씨의 방으로 데려가, 방금 우리가 옮긴 대화의 짤막한 요약과 함께 그를 픽윅 씨에게 소개했다."]], [["‘I am very sorry to betray my master, sir,’ said Job Trotter, applying to his eyes a pink checked pocket-handkerchief about six inches square.", "\"주인을 배신하게 되어 정말 가슴이 아픕니다, 나리.\" 잡 트로터가 가로세로 육 인치쯤 되는 분홍 체크무늬 손수건을 눈에 갖다 대며 말했다."]], [["‘The feeling does you a great deal of honour,’ replied Mr.", "\"그 마음씨는 자네에게 큰 명예가 되는 일이네.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다."], ["Pickwick; ‘but it is your duty, nevertheless.’", "\"하지만 그래도 그건 자네의 의무야.\""]], [["‘I know it is my duty, Sir,’ replied Job, with great emotion. ‘We should all try to discharge our duty, Sir, and I humbly endeavour to discharge mine, Sir; but it is a hard trial to betray a master, Sir, whose clothes you wear, and whose bread you eat, even though he is a scoundrel, Sir.’", "\"제 의무라는 걸 압니다, 나리.\" 잡이 몹시 격앙되어 대답했다. \"우리는 모두 자기 의무를 다하려고 애써야지요, 나리. 저도 미천하나마 제 의무를 다하려고 애쓰고 있습니다, 나리. 하지만 주인을 배신한다는 건 괴로운 시련입니다, 나리. 그 옷을 입고 그 밥을 얻어먹은 주인을 말입니다. 설령 그가 악당이라 하더라도 말이지요, 나리.\""]], [["‘You are a very good fellow,’ said Mr.", "\"자네는 아주 착한 친구로군.\""], ["Pickwick, much affected; ‘an honest fellow.’", "픽윅 씨가 크게 감동하여 말했다. \"정직한 친구야.\""]], [["‘Come, come,’ interposed Sam, who had witnessed Mr.", "\"자, 자.\""], ["Trotter’s tears with considerable impatience, ‘blow this ‘ere water-cart bis’ness.", "샘이 끼어들었는데, 그는 트로터 씨의 눈물을 자못 못마땅하게 지켜보던 참이었다. \"이 물뿌리개 짓 좀 집어치우쇼."], ["It won’t do no good, this won’t.’", "이런 거 아무 소용 없으니까.\""]], [["‘Sam,’ said Mr.", "\"샘.\""], ["Pickwick reproachfully.", "픽윅 씨가 책망하듯 말했다."], ["‘I am sorry to find that you have so little respect for this young man’s feelings.’", "\"자네가 이 젊은이의 감정을 그렇게 존중하지 않는다니 유감일세.\""]], [["‘His feelin’s is all wery well, Sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"이 친구 감정이야 다 좋습죠, 나리.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다."], ["Weller; ‘and as they’re so wery fine, and it’s a pity he should lose ‘em, I think he’d better keep ‘em in his own buzzum, than let ‘em ewaporate in hot water, ‘specially as they do no good.", "\"그리고 그게 그렇게 아주 곱고, 잃어버리면 아까운 거라면, 제 생각엔 그걸 뜨거운 물에 증발시키느니 자기 가슴속에 간직하는 게 나을 것 같습니다요. 아무 소용도 없으니 더욱이요."], ["Tears never yet wound up a clock, or worked a steam ingin’.", "눈물이 시계를 감아 본 적도, 증기 기관을 돌려 본 적도 없거든요."], ["The next time you go out to a smoking party, young fellow, fill your pipe with that ‘ere reflection; and for the present just put that bit of pink gingham into your pocket.", "다음번에 흡연 파티에 나가거든, 이 친구야, 그 생각을 파이프에 채워 넣으쇼. 그리고 지금은 그 분홍 깅엄 조각이나 주머니에 넣어 두시고."], ["‘Tain’t so handsome that you need keep waving it about, as if you was a tight-rope dancer.’", "그게 줄타기 곡예사라도 되는 양 흔들어 댈 만큼 그리 잘난 물건도 아니니까.\""]], [["‘My man is in the right,’ said Mr.", "\"내 하인 말이 옳네.\""], ["Pickwick, accosting Job, ‘although his mode of expressing his opinion is somewhat homely, and occasionally incomprehensible.’", "픽윅 씨가 잡에게 말을 걸며 말했다. \"비록 의견을 표현하는 방식이 다소 투박하고, 가끔은 알아들을 수 없긴 하지만 말이야.\""]], [["‘He is, sir, very right,’ said Mr.", "\"옳습니다, 나리, 아주 옳은 말씀입니다.\""], ["Trotter, ‘and I will give way no longer.’", "트로터 씨가 말했다. \"더는 무너지지 않겠습니다.\""]], [["Very well,’ said Mr.", "\"좋아.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick. ‘Now, where is this boarding-school?’", "\"자, 그 기숙학교는 어디인가?\""]], [["‘It is a large, old, red brick house, just outside the town, Sir,’ replied Job Trotter.", "\"도시 바로 외곽에 있는, 크고 오래된 붉은 벽돌집입니다, 나리.\" 잡 트로터가 대답했다."]], [["‘And when,’ said Mr.", "\"그러면 언제.\""], ["Pickwick--‘when is this villainous design to be carried into execution--when is this elopement to take place?’", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"이 흉악한 계획이 실행에 옮겨지는가—이 도주극은 언제 벌어지는가?\""]], [["‘To-night, Sir,’ replied Job.", "\"오늘 밤입니다, 나리.\" 잡이 대답했다."]], [["‘To-night!’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"오늘 밤!\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 외쳤다."]], [["‘This very night, sir,’ replied Job Trotter.", "\"바로 오늘 밤입니다, 나리.\" 잡 트로터가 대답했다."], ["‘That is what alarms me so much.’", "\"그래서 제가 이토록 마음을 졸이는 겁니다.\""]], [["‘Instant measures must be taken,’ said Mr.", "\"당장 조치를 취해야겠군.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick. ‘I will see the lady who keeps the establishment immediately.’", "\"그 시설을 운영하는 부인을 즉시 만나야겠어.\""]], [["‘I beg your pardon, Sir,’ said Job, ‘but that course of proceeding will never do.’", "\"죄송합니다만, 나리.\" 잡이 말했다. \"그런 식으로 하시면 절대 안 됩니다.\""]], [["‘Why not?’ inquired Mr.", "\"왜 안 되나?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘My master, sir, is a very artful man.’", "\"우리 주인은, 나리, 아주 교활한 자거든요.\""]], [["‘I know he is,’ said Mr.", "\"교활한 줄은 나도 알지.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘And he has so wound himself round the old lady’s heart, Sir,’ resumed Job, ‘that she would believe nothing to his prejudice, if you went down on your bare knees, and swore it; especially as you have no proof but the word of a servant, who, for anything she knows (and my master would be sure to say so), was discharged for some fault, and does this in revenge.’", "\"그리고 그자가 그 노부인의 마음을 어찌나 단단히 휘감아 놓았는지요, 나리.\" 잡이 말을 이었다. \"나리께서 맨무릎을 꿇고 맹세하신다 해도, 그자에게 불리한 말은 부인이 곧이듣지 않을 겁니다. 더구나 나리께는 하인의 말 말고는 증거가 없으니까요. 부인이 알기로는(그리고 우리 주인이 틀림없이 그렇게 말할 테니), 그 하인은 무슨 잘못으로 쫓겨나서 앙심을 품고 이런 짓을 하는 자일 수도 있는 거죠.\""]], [["‘What had better be done, then?’ said Mr.", "\"그럼 어떻게 하는 게 낫겠나?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Nothing but taking him in the very act of eloping, will convince the old lady, sir,’ replied Job.", "\"그자가 도주하는 바로 그 현장을 잡는 것 말고는, 그 노부인을 납득시킬 길이 없습니다, 나리.\" 잡이 대답했다."]], [["‘All them old cats will run their heads agin milestones,’ observed Mr.", "\"그런 늙은 고양이들은 죄다 이정표에다 머리를 들이박고 마는 법이죠.\""], ["Weller, in a parenthesis.", "웰러 씨가 곁들여 한마디 했다."]], [["‘But this taking him in the very act of elopement, would be a very difficult thing to accomplish, I fear,’ said Mr.", "\"하지만 도주의 바로 그 현장에서 그자를 잡는다는 건, 해내기가 무척 어려운 일일 것 같은데, 걱정이군.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘I don’t know, sir,’ said Mr.", "\"잘 모르겠습니다, 나리.\""], ["Trotter, after a few moments’ reflection.", "트로터 씨가 잠시 생각한 뒤에 말했다."], ["‘I think it might be very easily done.’", "\"제 생각엔 아주 쉽게 해낼 수 있을 것 같습니다.\""]], [["‘How?’ was Mr.", "\"어떻게?\""], ["Pickwick’s inquiry.", "하는 것이 픽윅 씨의 물음이었다."]], [["‘Why,’ replied Mr.", "\"그게요.\""], ["Trotter, ‘my master and I, being in the confidence of the two servants, will be secreted in the kitchen at ten o’clock.", "트로터 씨가 대답했다. \"우리 주인과 저는, 두 하인의 신임을 얻고 있으니, 열 시에 부엌에 숨어 있을 겁니다."], ["When the family have retired to rest, we shall come out of the kitchen, and the young lady out of her bedroom.", "식구들이 잠자리에 들면, 저희는 부엌에서 나오고, 아가씨는 침실에서 나오는 거죠."], ["A post-chaise will be waiting, and away we go.’", "역마차가 대기하고 있을 테고, 그러면 떠나는 겁니다.\""]], [["‘Well?’ said Mr.", "\"그래서?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Well, sir, I have been thinking that if you were in waiting in the garden behind, alone--’", "\"그래서, 나리, 제가 생각해 봤는데요, 만일 나리께서 뒤뜰에서 혼자 기다리고 계신다면—\""]], [["‘Alone,’ said Mr.", "\"혼자라.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["‘Why alone?’", "\"왜 혼자지?\""]], [["‘I thought it very natural,’ replied Job, ‘that the old lady wouldn’t like such an unpleasant discovery to be made before more persons than can possibly be helped.", "\"제 생각엔 그게 아주 자연스러울 것 같아서요.\" 잡이 대답했다."], ["The young lady, too, sir--consider her feelings.’", "\"노부인으로서는 이런 불쾌한 일이 굳이 더 많은 사람 앞에서 들통나는 걸 바라지 않을 테니까요. 아가씨도요, 나리—그 심정을 헤아려 주십시오.\""]], [["‘You are very right,’ said Mr.", "\"자네 말이 아주 옳네.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick. ‘The consideration evinces your delicacy of feeling.", "\"그 배려가 자네의 섬세한 감정을 잘 보여 주는군."], ["Go on; you are very right.’", "계속하게. 자네 말이 아주 옳아.\""]], [["‘Well, sir, I have been thinking that if you were waiting in the back garden alone, and I was to let you in, at the door which opens into it, from the end of the passage, at exactly half-past eleven o’clock, you would be just in the very moment of time to assist me in frustrating the designs of this bad man, by whom I have been unfortunately ensnared.’ Here Mr.", "\"그래서요, 나리, 제가 생각해 봤는데, 만일 나리께서 뒤뜰에서 혼자 기다리고 계시면, 제가 복도 끝에서 그 뜰로 통하는 문을 통해, 정확히 열한 시 반에 나리를 들여보내 드리겠습니다. 그러면 나리께서 바로 그 순간에, 불운하게도 제가 옭매인 이 악한의 계략을 좌절시키는 일을 도와주실 수 있을 겁니다.\""], ["Trotter sighed deeply.", "여기서 트로터 씨는 깊이 한숨을 쉬었다."]], [["‘Don’t distress yourself on that account,’ said Mr.", "\"그 일로 자네가 괴로워할 건 없네.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick; ‘if he had one grain of the delicacy of feeling which distinguishes you, humble as your station is, I should have some hopes of him.’", "\"그자가 비록 자네의 처지는 미천하나 자네에게서 두드러지는 그 섬세한 감정을 단 한 톨이라도 지녔다면, 나는 그자에게도 얼마간 희망을 걸어 보겠네만.\""]], [["Job Trotter bowed low; and in spite of Mr.", "잡 트로터는 깊이 허리를 숙였다."], ["Weller’s previous remonstrance, the tears again rose to his eyes.", "그리고 웰러 씨가 앞서 한 핀잔에도 불구하고, 그의 눈에는 또다시 눈물이 차올랐다."]], [["‘I never see such a feller,’ said Sam, ‘Blessed if I don’t think he’s got a main in his head as is always turned on.’", "\"이런 친구는 처음 보는군.\" 샘이 말했다. \"이 친구 머릿속엔 늘 틀어 놓은 수도관이 박혀 있는 게 틀림없어, 내 장담하지.\""]], [["‘Sam,’ said Mr.", "\"샘.\""], ["Pickwick, with great severity, ‘hold your tongue.’", "픽윅 씨가 자못 엄하게 말했다. \"입 다물게.\""]], [["‘Wery well, sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"잘 알겠습니다, 나리.\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘I don’t like this plan,’ said Mr.", "\"이 계획이 마음에 들지 않는군.\""], ["Pickwick, after deep meditation.", "픽윅 씨가 깊이 숙고한 뒤에 말했다."], ["‘Why cannot I communicate with the young lady’s friends?’", "\"왜 내가 그 아가씨의 친지들과 연락을 취할 수는 없는 건가?\""]], [["‘Because they live one hundred miles from here, sir,’ responded Job Trotter.", "\"그분들은 여기서 백 마일이나 떨어진 곳에 살거든요, 나리.\" 잡 트로터가 응답했다."]], [["‘That’s a clincher,’ said Mr.", "\"그게 결정타로군.\""], ["Weller, aside.", "웰러 씨가 옆에서 한마디 했다."]], [["‘Then this garden,’ resumed Mr.", "\"그럼 그 뜰 말인데.\" 픽윅 씨가 다시 말했다."], ["Pickwick. ‘How am I to get into it?’", "\"거기엔 어떻게 들어가나?\""]], [["‘The wall is very low, sir, and your servant will give you a leg up.’", "\"담이 아주 낮습니다, 나리. 그리고 나리의 하인이 받쳐 올려 드리면 됩니다.\""]], [["My servant will give me a leg up,’ repeated Mr.", "\"내 하인이 나를 받쳐 올린다.\""], ["Pickwick mechanically.", "픽윅 씨가 기계적으로 되뇌었다."], ["‘You will be sure to be near this door that you speak of?’", "\"자네가 말한 그 문 가까이에 틀림없이 있을 거지?\""]], [["‘You cannot mistake it, Sir; it’s the only one that opens into the garden.", "\"틀림없이 알아보실 겁니다, 나리. 뜰로 통하는 문은 그것 하나뿐이거든요."], ["Tap at it when you hear the clock strike, and I will open it instantly.’", "시계 치는 소리가 들리거든 거기를 두드리십시오. 그러면 제가 즉시 열어 드리겠습니다.\""]], [["‘I don’t like the plan,’ said Mr.", "\"이 계획은 마음에 들지 않네.\""], ["Pickwick; ‘but as I see no other, and as the happiness of this young lady’s whole life is at stake, I adopt it.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"하지만 달리 방도가 보이지 않고, 또 이 아가씨의 평생의 행복이 걸려 있으니, 받아들이겠네."], ["I shall be sure to be there.’", "틀림없이 그곳에 가 있겠어.\""]], [["Thus, for the second time, did Mr. Pickwick’s innate good-feeling involve him in an enterprise from which he would most willingly have stood aloof.", "이리하여, 두 번째로, 픽윅 씨의 타고난 선량한 마음이 그를, 그가 가장 기꺼이 멀찍이 물러서 있었을 일에 휘말리게 만들었다."]], [["‘What is the name of the house?’ inquired Mr.", "\"그 집 이름이 뭔가?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘Westgate House, Sir.", "\"웨스트게이트 하우스입니다, 나리."], ["You turn a little to the right when you get to the end of the town; it stands by itself, some little distance off the high road, with the name on a brass plate on the gate.’", "도시 끝에 이르거든 오른쪽으로 조금 도십시오. 큰길에서 조금 떨어진 곳에 외따로 서 있고, 대문에 놋쇠 명패로 이름이 붙어 있습니다.\""]], [["‘I know it,’ said Mr.", "\"알겠네.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick. ‘I observed it once before, when I was in this town.", "\"전에 이 도시에 왔을 때 한번 본 적이 있어."], ["You may depend upon me.’", "날 믿어도 좋네.\""]], [["Mr. Trotter made another bow, and turned to depart, when Mr. Pickwick thrust a guinea into his hand.", "트로터 씨가 또 한 번 절을 하고 떠나려고 돌아서자, 픽윅 씨가 그의 손에 기니 한 닢을 쥐여 주었다."]], [["‘You’re a fine fellow,’ said Mr.", "\"자네는 훌륭한 친구야.\""], ["Pickwick, ‘and I admire your goodness of heart.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"자네의 착한 마음씨가 감탄스럽군."], ["No thanks.", "사례는 됐네."], ["Remember--eleven o’clock.’", "잊지 말게—열한 시야.\""]], [["‘There is no fear of my forgetting it, sir,’ replied Job Trotter.", "\"잊을 염려는 없습니다, 나리.\" 잡 트로터가 대답했다."], ["With these words he left the room, followed by Sam.", "이 말과 함께 그는 방을 나섰고, 샘이 그 뒤를 따랐다."]], [["‘I say,’ said the latter, ‘not a bad notion that ‘ere crying.", "\"이봐.\" 샘이 말했다."], ["I’d cry like a rain-water spout in a shower on such good terms.", "\"그 우는 거 말이오, 제법이던데. 나도 그런 좋은 조건이면 소나기 속 빗물받이 홈통처럼 울어 보겠구먼."], ["How do you do it?’", "어떻게 하는 거요?\""]], [["‘It comes from the heart, Mr.", "\"그건 가슴에서 우러나오는 겁니다, 워커 씨.\""], ["Walker,’ replied Job solemnly.", "잡이 엄숙하게 대답했다."], ["‘Good- morning, sir.’", "\"그럼 안녕히 계십시오, 선생.\""]], [["‘You’re a soft customer, you are; we’ve got it all out o’ you, anyhow,’ thought Mr.", "'당신 참 물러 빠진 양반이구먼. 어쨌든 당신한테서 죄다 캐냈으니 됐어.'"], ["Weller, as Job walked away.", "잡이 걸어가는 동안 웰러 씨는 그렇게 생각했다."]], [["We cannot state the precise nature of the thoughts which passed through Mr. Trotter’s mind, because we don’t know what they were.", "트로터 씨의 머릿속을 스쳐 간 생각이 정확히 어떤 성질의 것이었는지는 우리도 말할 수 없으니, 그게 무엇이었는지 모르기 때문이다."]], [["The day wore on, evening came, and at a little before ten o’clock Sam Weller reported that Mr.", "하루가 저물고 저녁이 찾아왔다."], ["Jingle and Job had gone out together, that their luggage was packed up, and that they had ordered a chaise.", "그리고 열 시가 조금 못 되어 샘 웰러가, 징글 씨와 잡이 함께 나갔으며, 그들의 짐이 꾸려져 있고, 그들이 역마차를 한 대 주문했다고 보고했다."], ["The plot was evidently in execution, as Mr. Trotter had foretold.", "트로터 씨가 예고했던 대로, 음모가 분명 실행에 옮겨지고 있었다."]], [["Half-past ten o’clock arrived, and it was time for Mr. Pickwick to issue forth on his delicate errand.", "열 시 반이 되었고, 픽윅 씨가 그 미묘한 심부름을 위해 나설 시간이 되었다."], ["Resisting Sam’s tender of his greatcoat, in order that he might have no encumbrance in scaling the wall, he set forth, followed by his attendant.", "담을 넘을 때 거추장스러운 것이 없도록, 샘이 외투를 내미는 것을 마다하고, 그는 길을 나섰다. 시종이 그 뒤를 따랐다."]], [["There was a bright moon, but it was behind the clouds.", "환한 달이 떠 있었지만, 구름 뒤에 가려져 있었다."], ["It was a fine dry night, but it was most uncommonly dark.", "비가 오지 않는 좋은 밤이었지만, 유난히 캄캄했다."], ["Paths, hedges, fields, houses, and trees, were enveloped in one deep shade.", "길도, 산울타리도, 들판도, 집도, 나무도 한 겹 짙은 어둠에 휩싸여 있었다. 대기는 후텁지근하고 무더웠으며, 여름 번개가 지평선 끝에서 희미하게 떨렸는데, 그것이 모든 것을 감싼 그 침침한 어둠에 변화를 주는 유일한 광경이었다."], ["The atmosphere was hot and sultry, the summer lightning quivered faintly on the verge of the horizon, and was the only sight that varied the dull gloom in which everything was wrapped--sound there was none, except the distant barking of some restless house-dog.", "소리라곤 없었다. 멀리서 들려오는 어느 안절부절못하는 집 개의 짖는 소리 말고는."]], [["They found the house, read the brass plate, walked round the wall, and stopped at that portion of it which divided them from the bottom of the garden.", "그들은 그 집을 찾아냈고, 놋쇠 명패를 읽었으며, 담을 빙 돌아, 뜰 안쪽과 그들을 갈라놓는 담의 한 지점에 멈춰 섰다."]], [["‘You will return to the inn, Sam, when you have assisted me over,’ said Mr.", "\"내가 넘어가는 것을 도와준 다음에는 여관으로 돌아가게, 샘.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Wery well, Sir.’", "\"잘 알겠습니다, 나리.\""]], [["‘And you will sit up, till I return.’", "\"그리고 내가 돌아올 때까지 자지 말고 기다리게.\""]], [["‘Cert’nly, Sir.’", "\"여부가 있겠습니까, 나리.\""]], [["‘Take hold of my leg; and, when I say “Over,”", "\"내 다리를 붙잡게."], ["raise me gently.’", "그리고 내가 '넘어간다'고 하면, 살살 들어 올리게.\""]], [["‘All right, sir.’", "\"알겠습니다, 나리.\""]], [["Having settled these preliminaries, Mr. Pickwick grasped the top of the wall, and gave the word ‘Over,’ which was literally obeyed.", "이 사전 절차를 마무리하고, 픽윅 씨는 담 꼭대기를 붙잡고 \"넘어간다\"는 말을 내렸다."], ["Whether his body partook in some degree of the elasticity of his mind, or whether Mr. Weller’s notions of a gentle push were of a somewhat rougher description than Mr.", "그 말은 글자 그대로 이행되었다."], ["Pickwick’s, the immediate effect of his assistance was to jerk that immortal gentleman completely over the wall on to the bed beneath, where, after crushing three gooseberry-bushes and a rose- tree, he finally alighted at full length.", "그의 몸이 그의 정신이 지닌 탄력성을 어느 정도 닮았던 것인지, 아니면 살살 밀어 올린다는 웰러 씨의 관념이 픽윅 씨의 것보다 다소 거칠었던 것인지는 모르겠으나, 그 도움의 즉각적인 효과는 그 불멸의 신사를 담 너머로 완전히 휙 넘겨, 아래쪽 화단에 떨어뜨린 것이었다. 거기서 그는 구스베리 덤불 셋과 장미나무 한 그루를 으스러뜨린 뒤, 마침내 큰대자로 착지했다."]], [["‘You ha’n’t hurt yourself, I hope, Sir?’ said Sam, in a loud whisper, as soon as he had recovered from the surprise consequent upon the mysterious disappearance of his master.", "\"다치진 않으셨겠지요, 나리?\" 샘이, 주인이 신비롭게 사라진 데서 온 놀라움에서 정신을 차리자마자, 큰 소리로 속삭이며 말했다."]], [["‘I have not hurt myself, Sam, certainly,’ replied Mr.", "\"나는 분명 안 다쳤네, 샘.\""], ["Pickwick, from the other side of the wall, ‘but I rather think that you have hurt me.’", "픽윅 씨가 담 저편에서 대답했다. \"하지만 아무래도 자네가 나를 다치게 한 것 같군.\""]], [["‘I hope not, Sir,’ said Sam.", "\"안 그러셨길 바랍니다, 나리.\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["‘Never mind,’ said Mr.", "\"괜찮네.\""], ["Pickwick, rising, ‘it’s nothing but a few scratches.", "픽윅 씨가 일어서며 말했다. \"긁힌 데 몇 군데뿐이야."], ["Go away, or we shall be overheard.’", "어서 가게, 안 그러면 우리 소리가 들킬 테니.\""]], [["‘Good-bye, Sir.’", "\"안녕히 계십시오, 나리.\""]], [["‘Good-bye.’", "\"잘 가게.\""]], [["With stealthy steps Sam Weller departed, leaving Mr. Pickwick alone in the garden.", "샘 웰러는 살금살금 걸음으로 떠나며, 픽윅 씨를 뜰에 홀로 남겨 두었다."]], [["Lights occasionally appeared in the different windows of the house, or glanced from the staircases, as if the inmates were retiring to rest.", "집 안의 여러 창문에 이따금 불빛이 나타나거나 계단에서 새어 나왔는데, 마치 식구들이 잠자리에 드는 듯했다."], ["Not caring to go too near the door, until the appointed time, Mr. Pickwick crouched into an angle of the wall, and awaited its arrival.", "정해진 시각이 되기까지는 문에 너무 가까이 가지 않으려고, 픽윅 씨는 담 한 모퉁이에 몸을 웅크리고는 그 시각이 오기를 기다렸다."]], [["It was a situation which might well have depressed the spirits of many a man.", "그것은 능히 많은 사람의 기를 꺾어 놓을 만한 처지였다."], ["Mr. Pickwick, however, felt neither depression nor misgiving.", "그러나 픽윅 씨는 의기소침함도 불안함도 느끼지 않았다."], ["He knew that his purpose was in the main a good one, and he placed implicit reliance on the high-minded Job.", "그는 자기 목적이 근본적으로 선한 것임을 알았고, 고매한 잡을 전적으로 믿었다. 따분한 건 분명했다."], ["It was dull, certainly; not to say dreary; but a contemplative man can always employ himself in meditation.", "음울하다고까지는 않더라도. 하지만 사색을 즐기는 사람은 언제든 명상으로 시간을 보낼 수 있는 법이다."], ["Mr. Pickwick had meditated himself into a doze, when he was roused by the chimes of the neighbouring church ringing out the hour--half-past eleven.", "픽윅 씨가 명상에 잠겨 어느새 꾸벅꾸벅 졸고 있을 때, 이웃 교회의 종소리가 시각을 알리며 울려 퍼져 그를 깨웠다—열한 시 반이었다."]], [["‘That’s the time,’ thought Mr.", "'시간이 됐군.'"], ["Pickwick, getting cautiously on his feet.", "픽윅 씨는 그렇게 생각하며 조심스레 일어섰다."], ["He looked up at the house.", "그는 집을 올려다보았다."], ["The lights had disappeared, and the shutters were closed--all in bed, no doubt.", "불빛은 사라졌고 덧문은 닫혀 있었다—다들 잠자리에 든 게 틀림없었다."], ["He walked on tiptoe to the door, and gave a gentle tap.", "그는 발끝으로 문까지 걸어가 살며시 두드렸다."], ["Two or three minutes passing without any reply, he gave another tap rather louder, and then another rather louder than that.", "이삼 분이 지나도록 아무 대답이 없자, 그는 좀 더 크게 한 번 더 두드렸고, 그다음엔 그보다 좀 더 크게 또 두드렸다."]], [["At length the sound of feet was audible upon the stairs, and then the light of a candle shone through the keyhole of the door.", "마침내 계단 위에서 발소리가 들리고, 이어 촛불 빛이 문의 열쇠 구멍을 통해 비쳤다."], ["There was a good deal of unchaining and unbolting, and the door was slowly opened.", "한참 동안 사슬을 풀고 빗장을 빼는 소리가 나더니, 문이 천천히 열렸다."]], [["Now the door opened outwards; and as the door opened wider and wider, Mr.", "그런데 문은 바깥쪽으로 열렸다. 그리고 문이 점점 더 넓게 열림에 따라, 픽윅 씨는 점점 더 그 뒤로 물러섰다."], ["Pickwick receded behind it, more and more. What was his astonishment when he just peeped out, by way of caution, to see that the person who had opened it was--not Job Trotter, but a servant-girl with a candle in her hand!", "조심하느라 살짝 내다보았을 때, 문을 연 사람이—잡 트로터가 아니라, 손에 촛불을 든 하녀였음을 보았으니, 그의 놀라움이 어떠했겠는가!"], ["Mr. Pickwick drew in his head again, with the swiftness displayed by that admirable melodramatic performer, Punch, when he lies in wait for the flat-headed comedian with the tin box of music.", "픽윅 씨는, 저 훌륭한 멜로드라마 배우 펀치가 양철 음악 상자를 든 머리 납작한 광대를 노리며 매복할 때 보이는 그 날쌤으로, 다시 머리를 쑥 집어넣었다."]], [["‘It must have been the cat, Sarah,’ said the girl, addressing herself to some one in the house.", "\"고양이였나 봐, 세라.\" 하녀가 집 안의 누군가에게 말을 걸며 말했다."], ["‘Puss, puss, puss,--tit, tit, tit.’", "\"야옹아, 야옹아, 야옹아—나비야, 나비야, 나비야.\""]], [["But no animal being decoyed by these blandishments, the girl slowly closed the door, and re-fastened it; leaving Mr.", "하지만 이런 어르는 소리에 꾀여 오는 짐승이 한 마리도 없자, 하녀는 천천히 문을 닫고 다시 걸어 잠갔다."], ["Pickwick drawn up straight against the wall.", "픽윅 씨는 담에 바짝 붙어 곧추선 채 남겨졌다."]], [["‘This is very curious,’ thought Mr.", "'이거 참 묘하군.' 픽윅 씨는 생각했다."], ["Pickwick. ‘They are sitting up beyond their usual hour, I suppose.", "'평소 시간보다 늦게까지 안 자고 있나 보군."], ["Extremely unfortunate, that they should have chosen this night, of all others, for such a purpose-- exceedingly.’ And with these thoughts, Mr.", "하필 다른 날 다 두고 오늘 밤을 그런 일에 골랐다니, 지극히 불운한 일이야—지극히 말이지.'"], ["Pickwick cautiously retired to the angle of the wall in which he had been before ensconced; waiting until such time as he might deem it safe to repeat the signal.", "그리고 이런 생각을 하며, 픽윅 씨는 조심스레, 전에 몸을 숨겼던 담 모퉁이로 물러났다. 다시 신호를 보내도 안전하리라 여겨질 때까지 기다리려는 것이었다."]], [["He had not been here five minutes, when a vivid flash of lightning was followed by a loud peal of thunder that crashed and rolled away in the distance with a terrific noise--then came another flash of lightning, brighter than the other, and a second peal of thunder louder than the first; and then down came the rain, with a force and fury that swept everything before it.", "거기 있은 지 채 오 분도 안 되어, 강렬한 번갯불이 번쩍이더니 요란한 천둥이 뒤따라, 무시무시한 소리를 내며 콰르릉 멀리 굴러갔다. 그러더니 또 한 번, 먼젓번보다 더 밝은 번갯불이 번쩍이고, 처음 것보다 더 큰 두 번째 천둥이 울렸다. 그러더니 모든 것을 휩쓸어 버리는 듯한 기세와 맹렬함으로 비가 쏟아져 내렸다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick was perfectly aware that a tree is a very dangerous neighbour in a thunderstorm.", "픽윅 씨는 뇌우 속에서 나무가 무척 위험한 이웃이라는 것을 익히 알고 있었다."], ["He had a tree on his right, a tree on his left, a third before him, and a fourth behind.", "그의 오른쪽에 나무가 한 그루, 왼쪽에 한 그루, 앞에 세 번째, 뒤에 네 번째가 있었다."], ["If he remained where he was, he might fall the victim of an accident; if he showed himself in the centre of the garden, he might be consigned to a constable.", "있던 자리에 그대로 있으면 사고의 희생자가 될지도 모르고, 뜰 한복판에 모습을 드러내면 순경 손에 넘겨질지도 몰랐다."], ["Once or twice he tried to scale the wall, but having no other legs this time, than those with which Nature had furnished him, the only effect of his struggles was to inflict a variety of very unpleasant gratings on his knees and shins, and to throw him into a state of the most profuse perspiration.", "그는 한두 번 담을 넘으려 해 보았지만, 이번엔 자연이 그에게 준 다리 말고는 다른 다리가 없는지라, 그 안간힘의 유일한 효과라곤 무릎과 정강이에 무척 불쾌한 갖가지 생채기를 내고, 자신을 비 오듯 땀에 젖은 상태로 만든 것뿐이었다."]], [["‘What a dreadful situation,’ said Mr.", "\"끔찍한 처지로군.\""], ["Pickwick, pausing to wipe his brow after this exercise.", "픽윅 씨가 이 운동을 한 뒤에 이마를 닦으려고 멈춰 서서 말했다."], ["He looked up at the house--all was dark.", "그는 집을 올려다보았다—온통 캄캄했다."], ["They must be gone to bed now.", "이제는 다들 잠자리에 들었을 것이다."], ["He would try the signal again.", "다시 신호를 해 보아야겠다."]], [["He walked on tiptoe across the moist gravel, and tapped at the door.", "그는 축축한 자갈 위를 발끝으로 걸어가 문을 두드렸다."], ["He held his breath, and listened at the key-hole.", "그는 숨을 죽이고 열쇠 구멍에 귀를 기울였다."], ["No reply: very odd.", "대답이 없었다."], ["Another knock.", "무척 이상했다."], ["He listened again.", "또 한 번 두드렸다."], ["There was a low whispering inside, and then a voice cried--", "그는 다시 귀를 기울였다. 안에서 낮게 속삭이는 소리가 나더니, 이윽고 한 목소리가 외쳤다."]], [["‘Who’s there?’", "\"누구세요?\""]], [["‘That’s not Job,’ thought Mr.", "'잡이 아니군.'"], ["Pickwick, hastily drawing himself straight up against the wall again.", "픽윅 씨는 다시 황급히 담에 바짝 붙어 곧추서며 생각했다."], ["‘It’s a woman.’", "'여자야.'"]], [["He had scarcely had time to form this conclusion, when a window above stairs was thrown up, and three or four female voices repeated the query--‘Who’s there?’", "그가 이 결론을 채 내릴 새도 없이, 위층 창문 하나가 벌컥 열리더니, 서너 명의 여자 목소리가 그 물음을 되풀이했다—\"누구세요?\""]], [["Mr. Pickwick dared not move hand or foot.", "픽윅 씨는 손도 발도 감히 움직이지 못했다."], ["It was clear that the whole establishment was roused.", "시설 전체가 깨어났다는 것이 분명했다."], ["He made up his mind to remain where he was, until the alarm had subsided; and then by a supernatural effort, to get over the wall, or perish in the attempt.", "그는 소동이 가라앉을 때까지 있던 자리에 그대로 있다가, 그런 다음 초인적인 노력으로 담을 넘거나 그러지 못하면 그 시도 중에 죽기로 마음을 굳혔다."]], [["Like all Mr. Pickwick’s determinations, this was the best that could be made under the circumstances; but, unfortunately, it was founded upon the assumption that they would not venture to open the door again.", "픽윅 씨의 모든 결심이 그렇듯, 이것도 그 상황에서 세울 수 있는 최선의 결심이었다."], ["What was his discomfiture, when he heard the chain and bolts withdrawn, and saw the door slowly opening, wider and wider!", "그러나 불행히도 그것은 그들이 감히 문을 다시 열지는 않으리라는 가정에 근거를 둔 것이었다."], ["He retreated into the corner, step by step; but do what he would, the interposition of his own person, prevented its being opened to its utmost width.", "사슬과 빗장이 풀리는 소리를 듣고, 문이 점점 더 넓게 천천히 열리는 것을 보았을 때, 그의 낭패감이 어떠했겠는가! 그는 한 걸음 한 걸음 모퉁이로 물러섰지만, 아무리 애를 써도 자기 몸이 가로막은 탓에 문이 최대한 넓게 열리지는 못했다."]], [["‘Who’s there?’ screamed a numerous chorus of treble voices from the staircase inside, consisting of the spinster lady of the establishment, three teachers, five female servants, and thirty boarders, all half- dressed and in a forest of curl-papers.", "\"누구세요?\" 안쪽 계단에서 여럿이 합창하듯 새된 목소리가 비명을 질렀다. 그 합창단은 그 시설의 노처녀 원장과 교사 셋, 하녀 다섯, 그리고 기숙생 서른 명으로 이루어져 있었는데, 모두 옷을 반쯤 걸친 채 컬페이퍼를 산더미처럼 말고 있었다."]], [["Of course Mr.", "물론 픽윅 씨는 누구라고 말하지 않았다."], ["Pickwick didn’t say who was there: and then the burden of the chorus changed into--‘Lor!", "그러자 합창의 후렴구가 바뀌었다—\"어머나!"], ["I am so frightened.’", "무서워 죽겠어.\""]], [["‘Cook,’ said the lady abbess, who took care to be on the top stair, the very last of the group--‘cook, why don’t you go a little way into the garden?’", "\"요리사.\" 맨 윗계단에, 그러니까 무리의 맨 끄트머리에 서 있도록 단단히 신경 쓴 원장이 말했다. \"요리사, 자네가 뜰로 조금만 들어가 보지 그래?\""]], [["Please, ma’am, I don’t like,’ responded the cook.", "\"제발요, 마님, 싫습니다.\" 요리사가 응답했다."]], [["‘Lor, what a stupid thing that cook is!’ said the thirty boarders.", "\"어머, 저 요리사 정말 멍청하기도 해라!\" 기숙생 서른 명이 말했다."]], [["‘Cook,’ said the lady abbess, with great dignity; ‘don’t answer me, if you please.", "\"요리사.\" 원장이 자못 위엄 있게 말했다."], ["I insist upon your looking into the garden immediately.’", "\"내게 말대꾸하지 말게나. 당장 뜰을 들여다보라고 명하네.\""]], [["Here the cook began to cry, and the housemaid said it was ‘a shame!’ for which partisanship she received a month’s warning on the spot.", "이에 요리사가 울기 시작하자, 하녀가 \"너무하셔!\"라고 했고, 그렇게 편을 든 죄로 그녀는 그 자리에서 한 달 후 해고 통보를 받았다."]], [["‘Do you hear, cook?’ said the lady abbess, stamping her foot impatiently.", "\"안 들리나, 요리사?\" 원장이 짜증스레 발을 구르며 말했다."]], [["‘Don’t you hear your missis, cook?’ said the three teachers.", "\"마님 말씀이 안 들려, 요리사?\" 교사 셋이 말했다."]], [["‘What an impudent thing that cook is!’ said the thirty boarders.", "\"저 요리사 정말 뻔뻔하기도 해라!\" 기숙생 서른 명이 말했다."]], [["The unfortunate cook, thus strongly urged, advanced a step or two, and holding her candle just where it prevented her from seeing at all, declared there was nothing there, and it must have been the wind.", "이렇게 강하게 떠밀린 불운한 요리사는 한두 걸음 앞으로 나아가, 촛불을 하필 자기가 아무것도 못 보게 가리는 자리에 들고서, 거기엔 아무것도 없으며 바람이었던 게 틀림없다고 단언했다."], ["The door was just going to be closed in consequence, when an inquisitive boarder, who had been peeping between the hinges, set up a fearful screaming, which called back the cook and housemaid, and all the more adventurous, in no time.", "그래서 문을 막 닫으려던 참인데, 경첩 틈으로 엿보고 있던 호기심 많은 기숙생 하나가 무시무시한 비명을 질러 댔고, 그 소리에 요리사와 하녀, 그리고 더 대담한 이들 모두가 순식간에 도로 불려 왔다."]], [["‘What is the matter with Miss Smithers?’ said the lady abbess, as the aforesaid Miss Smithers proceeded to go into hysterics of four young lady power.", "\"스미더스 양이 왜 저러나?\" 원장이 말했다. 앞서 말한 스미더스 양이 젊은 아가씨 넷 분량의 히스테리 발작을 일으키기 시작했던 것이다."]], [["‘Lor, Miss Smithers, dear,’ said the other nine-and-twenty boarders.", "\"어머, 스미더스 양, 얘.\" 나머지 기숙생 스물아홉 명이 말했다."]], [["‘Oh, the man--the man--behind the door!’ screamed Miss Smithers.", "\"아, 남자—남자가—문 뒤에!\" 스미더스 양이 비명을 질렀다."]], [["The lady abbess no sooner heard this appalling cry, than she retreated to her own bedroom, double-locked the door, and fainted away comfortably.", "원장은 이 소름 끼치는 외침을 듣자마자 자기 침실로 물러나, 문을 이중으로 잠그고는 편안하게 기절해 버렸다."], ["The boarders, and the teachers, and the servants, fell back upon the stairs, and upon each other; and never was such a screaming, and fainting, and struggling beheld.", "기숙생들과 교사들과 하인들은 계단으로, 그리고 서로의 몸 위로 뒷걸음질 쳐 나동그라졌다. 그토록 비명을 지르고 기절하고 발버둥 치는 광경은 일찍이 본 적이 없었다."], ["In the midst of the tumult, Mr. Pickwick emerged from his concealment, and presented himself amongst them.", "그 소동의 한복판에서 픽윅 씨가 숨어 있던 곳에서 모습을 드러내, 그들 가운데로 나섰다."]], [["‘Ladies--dear ladies,’ said Mr.", "\"숙녀 여러분—사랑스러운 숙녀 여러분.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Oh. he says we’re dear,’ cried the oldest and ugliest teacher.", "\"어머, 우릴 사랑스럽대.\""], ["‘Oh, the wretch!’", "가장 나이 많고 가장 못생긴 교사가 소리쳤다. \"어머, 저 흉악한 작자!\""]], [["‘Ladies,’ roared Mr.", "\"숙녀 여러분.\""], ["Pickwick, rendered desperate by the danger of his situation.", "자기 처지의 위태로움에 절박해진 픽윅 씨가 고함쳤다. \"내 말을 들어 보시오."], ["‘Hear me. I am no robber.", "나는 강도가 아니오."], ["I want the lady of the house.’", "나는 이 댁의 안주인을 만나러 왔소.\""]], [["‘Oh, what a ferocious monster!’ screamed another teacher.", "\"어머, 정말 흉포한 괴물 같으니!\" 다른 교사가 비명을 질렀다."], ["‘He wants Miss Tomkins.’", "\"톰킨스 양을 노리는 거야.\""]], [["Here there was a general scream.", "이에 일제히 비명이 터져 나왔다."]], [["‘Ring the alarm bell, somebody!’ cried a dozen voices.", "\"누가 경보 종을 울려요!\" 열 몇 개의 목소리가 외쳤다."]], [["‘Don’t--don’t,’ shouted Mr.", "\"안 돼요—안 됩니다.\""], ["Pickwick. ‘Look at me.", "픽윅 씨가 소리쳤다."], ["Do I look like a robber!", "\"나를 보시오."], ["My dear ladies--you may bind me hand and leg, or lock me up in a closet, if you like.", "내가 강도처럼 생겼소! 사랑스러운 숙녀 여러분—원하신다면 내 손발을 묶으셔도, 벽장에 가두셔도 좋소."], ["Only hear what I have got to say--only hear me.’", "다만 내가 할 말만 들어 주시오—내 말만 들어 주시오.\""]], [["‘How did you come in our garden?’ faltered the housemaid.", "\"우리 뜰엔 어떻게 들어오셨어요?\" 하녀가 더듬거리며 말했다."]], [["‘Call the lady of the house, and I’ll tell her everything,’ said Mr.", "\"이 댁 안주인을 불러 주시오. 그러면 그분께 모든 걸 말씀드리겠소.\""], ["Pickwick, exerting his lungs to the utmost pitch.", "픽윅 씨가 폐활량을 최대한 끌어올려 말했다."], ["‘Call her--only be quiet, and call her, and you shall hear everything.’", "\"그분을 불러 주시오—다만 조용히 하시고, 그분을 불러 주시면, 모든 걸 듣게 되실 거요.\""]], [["It might have been Mr. Pickwick’s appearance, or it might have been his manner, or it might have been the temptation--irresistible to a female mind--of hearing something at present enveloped in mystery, that reduced the more reasonable portion of the establishment (some four individuals) to a state of comparative quiet.", "픽윅 씨의 외모 때문이었는지, 그의 태도 때문이었는지, 아니면 지금 신비에 싸인 무언가를 듣게 된다는—여성의 마음으로서는 거부할 수 없는—유혹 때문이었는지는 모르겠으나, 그 시설에서 그나마 분별 있는 이들(네댓 명쯤)이 비교적 잠잠한 상태로 진정되었다."], ["By them it was proposed, as a test of Mr.", "이들은 픽윅 씨의 진실성을 시험하는 방편으로, 그가 즉시 신체의 구속에 따를 것을 제안했다."], ["Pickwick’s sincerity, that he should immediately submit to personal restraint; and that gentleman having consented to hold a conference with Miss Tomkins, from the interior of a closet in which the day boarders hung their bonnets and sandwich-bags, he at once stepped into it, of his own accord, and was securely locked in.", "그리고 그 신사가, 통학생들이 보닛과 샌드위치 가방을 걸어 두는 벽장 안에서 톰킨스 양과 회담을 갖는 데 동의하자, 그는 즉시 제 발로 그 안에 들어갔고, 단단히 갇혔다. 이로써 나머지 사람들도 기운을 차렸다."], ["This revived the others; and Miss Tomkins having been brought to, and brought down, the conference began.", "그리고 톰킨스 양이 정신을 차리고 또 아래층으로 내려오자, 회담이 시작되었다."]], [["‘What did you do in my garden, man?’ said Miss Tomkins, in a faint voice.", "\"내 뜰에서 무얼 했어요, 이 사람아?\" 톰킨스 양이 가냘픈 목소리로 말했다."]], [["‘I came to warn you that one of your young ladies was going to elope to- night,’ replied Mr.", "\"댁의 아가씨들 가운데 한 분이 오늘 밤 도망치려 한다는 것을 경고하러 왔습니다.\""], ["Pickwick, from the interior of the closet.", "픽윅 씨가 벽장 안에서 대답했다."]], [["‘Elope!’ exclaimed Miss Tomkins, the three teachers, the thirty boarders, and the five servants.", "\"도망이라뇨!\" 톰킨스 양과 교사 셋, 기숙생 서른 명, 그리고 하인 다섯이 외쳤다."], ["‘Who with?’", "\"누구하고요?\""]], [["Your friend, Mr. Charles Fitz-Marshall.’", "\"댁의 친구인 찰스 피츠마셜 씨하고요.\""]], [["‘My friend!", "\"내 친구라뇨!"], ["I don’t know any such person.’", "난 그런 사람 모르는데.\""]], [["‘Well, Mr. Jingle, then.’", "\"그럼, 징글 씨하고요.\""]], [["‘I never heard the name in my life.’", "\"그런 이름은 평생 들어 본 적도 없어요.\""]], [["‘Then, I have been deceived, and deluded,’ said Mr. Pickwick.", "\"그렇다면 나는 속고 기만당한 것이군.\""], ["‘I have been the victim of a conspiracy--a foul and base conspiracy.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"나는 음모의 희생양이 된 거요—더럽고 비열한 음모의."], ["Send to the Angel, my dear ma’am, if you don’t believe me.", "내 말이 못 믿기시거든, 부인, 에인절 여관으로 사람을 보내 보십시오."], ["Send to the Angel for Mr. Pickwick’s manservant, I implore you, ma’am.’", "부탁드립니다, 부인, 에인절 여관으로 사람을 보내 픽윅 씨의 하인을 불러 주십시오.\""]], [["‘He must be respectable--he keeps a manservant,’ said Miss Tomkins to the writing and ciphering governess.", "\"점잖은 분인 게 분명해—하인을 두셨다니.\" 톰킨스 양이 글쓰기와 셈하기를 가르치는 가정교사에게 말했다."]], [["‘It’s my opinion, Miss Tomkins,’ said the writing and ciphering governess, ‘that his manservant keeps him, I think he’s a madman, Miss Tomkins, and the other’s his keeper.’", "\"제 생각엔요, 톰킨스 양.\" 글쓰기와 셈하기 가정교사가 말했다. \"그 하인이 저 사람을 두고 있는 것 같아요. 저는 저 사람이 미치광이고, 다른 사람이 그 보호자인 것 같습니다, 톰킨스 양.\""]], [["‘I think you are very right, Miss Gwynn,’ responded Miss Tomkins.", "\"그웬 양 말이 아주 옳은 것 같네요.\" 톰킨스 양이 응답했다."], ["‘Let two of the servants repair to the Angel, and let the others remain here, to protect us.’", "\"하인 둘을 에인절 여관으로 보내고, 나머지는 여기 남아 우릴 지켜요.\""]], [["So two of the servants were despatched to the Angel in search of Mr. Samuel Weller; and the remaining three stopped behind to protect Miss Tomkins, and the three teachers, and the thirty boarders.", "그리하여 하인 둘이 새뮤얼 웰러 씨를 찾아 에인절 여관으로 파견되었고, 나머지 셋은 톰킨스 양과 교사 셋, 기숙생 서른 명을 지키려고 뒤에 남았다."], ["And Mr. Pickwick sat down in the closet, beneath a grove of sandwich-bags, and awaited the return of the messengers, with all the philosophy and fortitude he could summon to his aid.", "그리고 픽윅 씨는 샌드위치 가방이 늘어선 숲 아래 벽장 안에 앉아, 끌어모을 수 있는 온갖 철학과 꿋꿋함으로 심부름꾼들이 돌아오기를 기다렸다."]], [["An hour and a half elapsed before they came back, and when they did come, Mr. Pickwick recognised, in addition to the voice of Mr.", "그들이 돌아오기까지 한 시간 반이 흘렀다."], ["Samuel Weller, two other voices, the tones of which struck familiarly on his ear; but whose they were, he could not for the life of him call to mind.", "그리고 그들이 마침내 왔을 때, 픽윅 씨는 새뮤얼 웰러 씨의 목소리에 더해, 그의 귀에 익숙하게 와닿는 다른 두 목소리를 알아챘다. 하지만 그것이 누구의 목소리인지는 아무리 애를 써도 도무지 떠올릴 수가 없었다."]], [["A very brief conversation ensued.", "아주 짤막한 대화가 오갔다."], ["The door was unlocked. Mr.", "문이 열렸다."], ["Pickwick stepped out of the closet, and found himself in the presence of the whole establishment of Westgate House, Mr Samuel Weller, and--old Wardle, and his destined son-in-law, Mr. Trundle!", "픽윅 씨는 벽장에서 걸어 나와, 웨스트게이트 하우스의 시설 전원과 새뮤얼 웰러 씨, 그리고—워들 영감과 그의 장차 사위가 될 트런들 씨 앞에 서 있는 자신을 발견했다!"]], [["‘My dear friend,’ said Mr.", "\"이보게, 친구.\""], ["Pickwick, running forward and grasping Wardle’s hand, ‘my dear friend, pray, for Heaven’s sake, explain to this lady the unfortunate and dreadful situation in which I am placed.", "픽윅 씨가 앞으로 달려나가 워들의 손을 붙잡으며 말했다. \"이보게, 친구, 제발, 하늘에 맹세코, 내가 처한 이 불운하고 끔찍한 처지를 이 부인께 설명해 주게."], ["You must have heard it from my servant; say, at all events, my dear fellow, that I am neither a robber nor a madman.’", "내 하인한테서 들었을 테지. 아무튼, 이 사람아, 내가 강도도 미치광이도 아니라고 말해 주게.\""]], [["‘I have said so, my dear friend.", "\"이미 그렇게 말했네, 친구."], ["I have said so already,’ replied Mr.", "벌써 그렇게 말했어.\""], ["Wardle, shaking the right hand of his friend, while Mr.", "워들 씨가 친구의 오른손을 흔들며 대답했다."], ["Trundle shook the left.", "트런들 씨는 그의 왼손을 흔들었다."]], [["‘And whoever says, or has said, he is,’ interposed Mr.", "\"그리고 이분이 강도라거나 미치광이라고 말하는 자나, 말했던 자가 있다면.\""], ["Weller, stepping forward, ‘says that which is not the truth, but so far from it, on the contrary, quite the rewerse.", "웰러 씨가 앞으로 나서며 끼어들었다. \"그건 사실이 아닌 걸 말하는 거고, 사실은커녕 정반대로 완전히 거꾸로지요."], ["And if there’s any number o’ men on these here premises as has said so, I shall be wery happy to give ‘em all a wery convincing proof o’ their being mistaken, in this here wery room, if these wery respectable ladies ‘ll have the goodness to retire, and order ‘em up, one at a time.’ Having delivered this defiance with great volubility, Mr.", "그리고 이 건물 안에 그렇게 말한 자들이 몇이 됐든, 저는 이 아주 점잖으신 숙녀분들께서 물러가시고 한 번에 한 놈씩 올려 보내 주시는 친절을 베푸신다면, 바로 이 방에서 그놈들 모두에게 자기들이 틀렸다는 아주 확실한 증거를 제공해 드리는 영광을 누리겠습니다요.\""], ["Weller struck his open palm emphatically with his clenched fist, and winked pleasantly on Miss Tomkins, the intensity of whose horror at his supposing it within the bounds of possibility that there could be any men on the premises of Westgate House Establishment for Young Ladies, it is impossible to describe.", "이 도전장을 무척 거침없이 쏟아낸 뒤, 웰러 씨는 펼친 손바닥을 꽉 쥔 주먹으로 힘차게 내리치고는, 톰킨스 양에게 유쾌하게 눈을 찡긋했다. 그가 웨스트게이트 하우스 영애 양성소의 건물 안에 남자가 있을 가능성의 범위 안에 있다고 상정한 데 대해 톰킨스 양이 느낀 그 격렬한 공포는 이루 다 묘사할 수가 없다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick’s explanation having already been partially made, was soon concluded.", "픽윅 씨의 설명은 이미 부분적으로 이루어졌던지라 곧 마무리되었다."], ["But neither in the course of his walk home with his friends, nor afterwards when seated before a blazing fire at the supper he so much needed, could a single observation be drawn from him.", "그러나 친구들과 함께 집으로 걸어가는 동안에도, 그 뒤 그토록 절실했던 야식을 위해 활활 타오르는 난롯불 앞에 앉았을 때에도, 그에게서는 단 한마디 말도 끌어낼 수 없었다."], ["He seemed bewildered and amazed.", "그는 어리둥절하고 어안이 벙벙해 보였다."], ["Once, and only once, he turned round to Mr. Wardle, and said--", "단 한 번, 오직 한 번, 그는 워들 씨에게로 돌아서서 말했다."]], [["‘How did you come here?’", "\"자네는 어쩌다 여기 온 건가?\""]], [["‘Trundle and I came down here, for some good shooting on the first,’ replied Wardle.", "\"트런들과 내가 초하룻날 사냥 좀 제대로 해 보려고 여기 내려온 걸세.\" 워들이 대답했다."], ["‘We arrived to-night, and were astonished to hear from your servant that you were here too.", "\"오늘 밤에 도착했는데, 자네 하인한테서 자네도 여기 와 있다는 말을 듣고 깜짝 놀랐지. 하지만 자네가 와 있어서 반갑네.\""], ["But I am glad you are,’ said the old fellow, slapping him on the back--‘I am glad you are.", "노인이 그의 등을 탁 치며 말했다. \"와 있어서 반가워."], ["We shall have a jovial party on the first, and we’ll give Winkle another chance--eh, old boy?’", "초하룻날 흥겨운 잔치를 벌이세. 그리고 윙클에게 한 번 더 기회를 주자고—안 그런가, 친구?\""]], [["Mr.", "픽윅 씨는 아무 대답도 하지 않았다."], ["Pickwick made no reply, he did not even ask after his friends at Dingley Dell, and shortly afterwards retired for the night, desiring Sam to fetch his candle when he rung.", "그는 딩글리 델에 있는 친구들의 안부조차 묻지 않았으며, 얼마 안 있어 잠자리에 들기 위해 물러갔다. 그러면서 종을 울리면 샘에게 촛불을 가져오라고 일러 두었다."]], [["The bell did ring in due course, and Mr. Weller presented himself.", "종은 적당한 때에 울렸고, 웰러 씨가 모습을 드러냈다."]], [["‘Sam,’ said Mr.", "\"샘.\""], ["Pickwick, looking out from under the bed-clothes.", "픽윅 씨가 이불 밑에서 내다보며 말했다."]], [["‘Sir,’ said Mr.", "\"네, 나리.\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 말했다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick paused, and Mr. Weller snuffed the candle.", "픽윅 씨가 잠시 말을 멈추자, 웰러 씨는 촛불 심지를 잘랐다."]], [["‘Sam,’ said Mr.", "\"샘.\""], ["Pickwick again, as if with a desperate effort.", "픽윅 씨가 마치 필사적인 노력을 기울이듯 다시 말했다."]], [["‘Sir,’ said Mr.", "\"네, 나리.\""], ["Weller, once more.", "웰러 씨가 또 한 번 말했다."]], [["‘Where is that Trotter?’", "\"그 트로터는 어디 있나?\""]], [["‘Job, sir?’\n‘Yes.", "\"잡 말씀입니까, 나리?\""]], [["‘Gone, sir.’", "\"그래.\""]], [["‘With his master, I suppose?’", "\"갔습니다, 나리.\" \"제 주인하고 함께겠지?\""]], [["‘Friend or master, or whatever he is, he’s gone with him,’ replied Mr.", "\"친구든 주인이든 뭐든 간에, 그자와 함께 갔습니다요, 나리.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다."], ["Weller. ‘There’s a pair on ‘em, sir.’", "\"그 둘은 한 짝패지요, 나리.\""]], [["‘Jingle suspected my design, and set that fellow on you, with this story, I suppose?’ said Mr.", "\"징글이 내 계략을 눈치채고, 그 녀석을 시켜 자네한테 이 이야기를 흘리게 한 거겠지, 그렇지?\""], ["Pickwick, half choking.", "픽윅 씨가 거의 숨이 막힌 채 말했다."]], [["‘Just that, sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"바로 그렇습니다, 나리.\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘It was all false, of course?’", "\"물론 죄다 거짓이었고?\""]], [["‘All, sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"죄다 거짓이었지요, 나리.\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 대답했다."], ["‘Reg’lar do, sir; artful dodge.’", "\"완전히 한 방 먹인 거죠, 나리. 교활한 술책입니다요.\""]], [["‘I don’t think he’ll escape us quite so easily the next time, Sam!’ said Mr.", "\"다음번엔 그자가 우리한테서 그리 쉽게 빠져나가진 못할 걸세, 샘!\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘I don’t think he will, Sir.’", "\"그러지 못할 겁니다, 나리.\""]], [["‘Whenever I meet that Jingle again, wherever it is,’ said Mr.", "\"그 징글을 다시 만나는 날엔, 그게 어디든 간에.\""], ["Pickwick, raising himself in bed, and indenting his pillow with a tremendous blow, ‘I’ll inflict personal chastisement on him, in addition to the exposure he so richly merits.", "픽윅 씨가 침대에서 몸을 일으키며 베개를 엄청난 일격으로 푹 꺼지게 내리치고 말했다. \"그자가 그토록 톡톡히 받아 마땅한 폭로에 더해, 직접 손수 응징을 가하고 말겠어."], ["I will, or my name is not Pickwick.’", "반드시 그러겠어. 안 그러면 내 이름이 픽윅이 아니지.\""]], [["‘And venever I catches hold o’ that there melan-cholly chap with the black hair,’ said Sam, ‘if I don’t bring some real water into his eyes, for once in a way, my name ain’t Weller.", "\"그리고 그 검은 머리의 우중충한 녀석을 제가 붙잡는 날엔.\" 샘이 말했다."], ["Good-night, Sir!’", "\"이번 한 번만큼은 그 눈에서 진짜 물이 나오게 해 주지 못한다면, 제 이름은 웰러가 아닙죠. 안녕히 주무십시오, 나리!\""]], [["CHAPTER XVII. SHOWING THAT AN ATTACK OF RHEUMATISM, IN SOME CASES, ACTS AS A QUICKENER TO INVENTIVE GENIUS\nThe constitution of Mr.", "픽윅 씨의 체질은 상당한 정도의 노고와 피로를 견뎌 낼 수 있었으나, 앞 장에 기록된 그 잊지 못할 밤에 그가 겪은 것과 같은 여러 공격이 한꺼번에 닥치는 데에는 끄떡없지 못했다."], ["Pickwick, though able to sustain a very considerable amount of exertion and fatigue, was not proof against such a combination of attacks as he had undergone on the memorable night, recorded in the last chapter. The process of being washed in the night air, and rough-dried in a closet, is as dangerous as it is peculiar.", "밤공기에 씻기고 벽장 안에서 거칠게 말려지는 과정이란 기이한 만큼이나 위험한 것이다."], ["Mr. Pickwick was laid up with an attack of rheumatism.", "픽윅 씨는 류머티즘 발작으로 자리에 눕고 말았다."]], [["But although the bodily powers of the great man were thus impaired, his mental energies retained their pristine vigour.", "그러나 이 위대한 인물의 육신의 힘이 그렇듯 손상되었음에도, 그의 정신적 활력은 본래의 기운을 그대로 간직하고 있었다."], ["His spirits were elastic; his good-humour was restored.", "그의 기분은 탄력이 있었고, 그의 쾌활함도 되살아났다."], ["Even the vexation consequent upon his recent adventure had vanished from his mind; and he could join in the hearty laughter, which any allusion to it excited in Mr. Wardle, without anger and without embarrassment.", "최근의 모험에서 비롯된 언짢음마저도 그의 마음에서 사라졌으니, 워들 씨가 그 일을 들먹일 때마다 터뜨리는 호탕한 웃음에 그도 노여움 없이, 거북함 없이 함께 어울릴 수 있었다."], ["Nay, more. During the two days Mr.", "아니, 그뿐이 아니었다."], ["Pickwick was confined to bed, Sam was his constant attendant.", "픽윅 씨가 자리에 묶여 있던 이틀 동안 샘은 한결같이 그의 곁을 지켰다."], ["On the first, he endeavoured to amuse his master by anecdote and conversation; on the second, Mr. Pickwick demanded his writing-desk, and pen and ink, and was deeply engaged during the whole day.", "첫째 날, 샘은 일화와 이야기로 주인을 즐겁게 해 주려 애썼고, 둘째 날, 픽윅 씨는 책상과 펜과 잉크를 청하더니 온종일 무언가에 깊이 몰두했다."], ["On the third, being able to sit up in his bedchamber, he despatched his valet with a message to Mr. Wardle and Mr.", "셋째 날, 침실에서 일어나 앉을 수 있게 되자 그는 시종을 보내 워들 씨와 트런들 씨에게 전갈을 전했으니, 그날 저녁 그곳에서 함께 술을 들어 준다면 큰 기쁨이겠다는 내용이었다."], ["Trundle, intimating that if they would take their wine there, that evening, they would greatly oblige him. The invitation was most willingly accepted; and when they were seated over their wine, Mr.", "초대는 더없이 흔쾌히 받아들여졌고, 두 사람이 술잔을 앞에 두고 자리에 앉자, 픽윅 씨는 이런저런 낯을 붉히며 다음과 같은 짤막한 이야기를 내놓았다."], ["Pickwick, with sundry blushes, produced the following little tale, as having been ‘edited’ by himself, during his recent indisposition, from his notes of Mr.", "그것은 그가 최근의 병중에 웰러 씨의 꾸밈없는 구술 기록을 손수 \"편집한\" 것이었다."], ["Weller’s unsophisticated recital.", "교구 서기—참된 사랑의 이야기"]], [["# THE PARISH CLERK A TALE OF TRUE LOVE", "# THE PARISH CLERK A TALE OF TRUE LOVE"]], [["‘Once upon a time, in a very small country town, at a considerable distance from London, there lived a little man named Nathaniel Pipkin, who was the parish clerk of the little town, and lived in a little house in the little High Street, within ten minutes’ walk from the little church; and who was to be found every day, from nine till four, teaching a little learning to the little boys.", "\"옛날 옛적, 런던에서 꽤 멀리 떨어진 아주 작은 시골 마을에 너새니얼 핍킨이라는 작은 사내가 살았으니, 그는 그 작은 마을의 교구 서기였고, 작은 교회에서 걸어서 십 분도 안 되는 작은 한길가의 작은 집에 살았으며, 날마다 아홉 시부터 네 시까지 작은 사내아이들에게 작은 학문을 가르치는 일로 만날 수 있는 사람이었다."], ["Nathaniel Pipkin was a harmless, inoffensive, good-natured being, with a turned-up nose, and rather turned-in legs, a cast in his eye, and a halt in his gait; and he divided his time between the church and his school, verily believing that there existed not, on the face of the earth, so clever a man as the curate, so imposing an apartment as the vestry-room, or so well-ordered a seminary as his own.", "너새니얼 핍킨은 해롭지 않고 거슬리지 않으며 마음씨 고운 사람으로, 위로 들린 코에 안쪽으로 휜 다리, 사팔뜨기 눈에 절뚝거리는 걸음걸이를 지녔다. 그는 교회와 학교 사이로 시간을 나누어 보내며, 이 땅 위에 부목사만큼 똑똑한 사람도, 제의실만큼 위풍당당한 방도, 자기 학교만큼 잘 정돈된 학당도 없다고 진실로 믿었다."], ["Once, and only once, in his life, Nathaniel Pipkin had seen a bishop--a real bishop, with his arms in lawn sleeves, and his head in a wig.", "평생에 단 한 번, 오직 한 번 너새니얼 핍킨은 주교를 본 적이 있었다—두 팔을 고운 모시 소매에 넣고 머리에 가발을 쓴, 진짜 주교 말이다."], ["He had seen him walk, and heard him talk, at a confirmation, on which momentous occasion Nathaniel Pipkin was so overcome with reverence and awe, when the aforesaid bishop laid his hand on his head, that he fainted right clean away, and was borne out of church in the arms of the beadle.", "그는 견진성사 자리에서 그 주교가 걷는 것을 보고 말하는 것을 들었는데, 그 중대한 자리에서 앞서 말한 주교가 그의 머리에 손을 얹자 너새니얼 핍킨은 경외와 두려움에 그만 압도되어 깨끗이 기절해 버렸고, 교회지기의 품에 안겨 교회 밖으로 실려 나왔다."]], [["‘This was a great event, a tremendous era, in Nathaniel Pipkin’s life, and it was the only one that had ever occurred to ruffle the smooth current of his quiet existence, when happening one fine afternoon, in a fit of mental abstraction, to raise his eyes from the slate on which he was devising some tremendous problem in compound addition for an offending urchin to solve, they suddenly rested on the blooming countenance of Maria Lobbs, the only daughter of old Lobbs, the great saddler over the way.", "\"이것이 너새니얼 핍킨의 인생에서 한 큰 사건이요, 어마어마한 시대였으며, 그의 조용한 생애의 잔잔한 물결을 흐트러뜨린 유일한 일이었다. 그러던 어느 화창한 오후, 그가 멍하니 정신을 놓고 있다가, 잘못을 저지른 한 개구쟁이에게 풀게 하려고 복합 덧셈의 어마어마한 문제를 궁리하던 석판에서 눈을 들었을 때, 그의 시선이 문득 길 건너편 큰 마구상 노인 롭스의 외동딸인 마리아 롭스의 꽃다운 얼굴에 가서 멎었다."], ["Now, the eyes of Mr. Pipkin had rested on the pretty face of Maria Lobbs many a time and oft before, at church and elsewhere; but the eyes of Maria Lobbs had never looked so bright, the cheeks of Maria Lobbs had never looked so ruddy, as upon this particular occasion.", "그런데 핍킨 씨의 눈은 그전에도 교회에서나 다른 곳에서나 마리아 롭스의 어여쁜 얼굴에 여러 번 멎은 적이 있었지만, 이날만큼 마리아 롭스의 눈이 그토록 빛나 보인 적도, 마리아 롭스의 두 뺨이 그토록 발그레해 보인 적도 없었다."], ["No wonder then, that Nathaniel Pipkin was unable to take his eyes from the countenance of Miss Lobbs; no wonder that Miss Lobbs, finding herself stared at by a young man, withdrew her head from the window out of which she had been peeping, and shut the casement and pulled down the blind; no wonder that Nathaniel Pipkin, immediately thereafter, fell upon the young urchin who had previously offended, and cuffed and knocked him about to his heart’s content.", "그러니 너새니얼 핍킨이 롭스 양의 얼굴에서 눈을 떼지 못한 것도 이상할 게 없고, 롭스 양이 한 젊은이가 자기를 빤히 쳐다보는 것을 알아채고는 내다보던 창문에서 고개를 거두고 여닫이창을 닫더니 블라인드를 내린 것도 이상할 게 없으며, 그 직후에 너새니얼 핍킨이 앞서 잘못을 저지른 어린 개구쟁이에게 달려들어 마음껏 두들기고 쥐어박은 것도 이상할 게 없다."], ["All this was very natural, and there’s nothing at all to wonder at about it.", "이 모든 것이 지극히 자연스러운 일이라 조금도 이상할 것이 없다."]], [["‘It is matter of wonder, though, that anyone of Mr. Nathaniel Pipkin’s retiring disposition, nervous temperament, and most particularly diminutive income, should from this day forth, have dared to aspire to the hand and heart of the only daughter of the fiery old Lobbs--of old Lobbs, the great saddler, who could have bought up the whole village at one stroke of his pen, and never felt the outlay--old Lobbs, who was well known to have heaps of money, invested in the bank at the nearest market town--who was reported to have countless and inexhaustible treasures hoarded up in the little iron safe with the big keyhole, over the chimney-piece in the back parlour--and who, it was well known, on festive occasions garnished his board with a real silver teapot, cream- ewer, and sugar-basin, which he was wont, in the pride of his heart, to boast should be his daughter’s property when she found a man to her mind.", "\"그러나 정작 이상한 일은, 너새니얼 핍킨 씨처럼 내성적인 성품에 신경질적인 기질에다 무엇보다도 쥐꼬리만 한 수입을 가진 사람이, 이날 이후로 그 불같은 노인 롭스의 외동딸의 손과 마음을 감히 넘본 것이다—롭스, 그 큰 마구상 노인은 펜을 한 번 놀려 온 마을을 통째로 사들이고도 그 지출을 아랑곳하지 않을 만한 사람이었고—가장 가까운 장터 읍내의 은행에 산더미 같은 돈을 넣어 두었다고 익히 알려진 노인이었으며—뒷거실 벽난로 위에 놓인, 큼지막한 열쇠 구멍이 달린 작은 쇠금고 안에 헤아릴 수 없고 마르지 않는 보물을 쟁여 두었다고 소문난 노인이었고—잔치가 있을 때면 식탁을 진짜 은제 찻주전자와 크림 단지와 설탕 그릇으로 장식하던 노인이었으니, 그는 자랑스러운 마음에 딸이 마음에 드는 사내를 만나는 날엔 그것들이 모두 딸의 것이 되리라 으스대곤 했다."], ["I repeat it, to be matter of profound astonishment and intense wonder, that Nathaniel Pipkin should have had the temerity to cast his eyes in this direction.", "거듭 말하거니와, 너새니얼 핍킨이 감히 그 방향으로 눈을 던졌다는 것은 깊은 경악과 강렬한 의아함을 자아내는 일이다."], ["But love is blind; and Nathaniel had a cast in his eye; and perhaps these two circumstances, taken together, prevented his seeing the matter in its proper light.", "그러나 사랑은 눈이 멀게 마련이고, 너새니얼은 사팔뜨기 눈을 가졌으니, 어쩌면 이 두 사정이 합쳐져 그가 사태를 제대로 보지 못하게 했는지도 모른다."]], [["‘Now, if old Lobbs had entertained the most remote or distant idea of the state of the affections of Nathaniel Pipkin, he would just have razed the school-room to the ground, or exterminated its master from the surface of the earth, or committed some other outrage and atrocity of an equally ferocious and violent description; for he was a terrible old fellow, was Lobbs, when his pride was injured, or his blood was up.", "\"한데 만일 노인 롭스가 너새니얼 핍킨의 애정 상태를 조금이라도, 아주 어렴풋이라도 알아챘다면, 그는 학교 건물을 송두리째 땅바닥에 헐어 버리거나, 그 선생을 지상에서 박멸하거나, 그에 못지않게 사납고 난폭한 어떤 폭거와 만행을 저질렀을 것이다. 자존심이 상하거나 피가 끓어오를 때면 롭스란 노인은 끔찍한 사내였으니까."], ["Swear!", "욕설이라니!"], ["Such trains of oaths would come rolling and pealing over the way, sometimes, when he was denouncing the idleness of the bony apprentice with the thin legs, that Nathaniel Pipkin would shake in his shoes with horror, and the hair of the pupils’ heads would stand on end with fright.", "다리가 가느다란 깡마른 도제의 게으름을 호되게 꾸짖을 때면, 때때로 길 건너편까지 욕설의 행렬이 우르르 천둥처럼 굴러와, 너새니얼 핍킨은 공포에 신발 속에서 발을 부들부들 떨었고, 학생들의 머리카락은 겁에 질려 곤두서곤 했다."]], [["‘Well!", "\"자!"], ["Day after day, when school was over, and the pupils gone, did Nathaniel Pipkin sit himself down at the front window, and, while he feigned to be reading a book, throw sidelong glances over the way in search of the bright eyes of Maria Lobbs; and he hadn’t sat there many days, before the bright eyes appeared at an upper window, apparently deeply engaged in reading too.", "날이면 날마다 수업이 끝나고 학생들이 돌아가고 나면, 너새니얼 핍킨은 앞창가에 자리를 잡고 앉아, 책을 읽는 척하면서 마리아 롭스의 빛나는 눈을 찾아 길 건너편으로 곁눈질을 던지곤 했다. 그렇게 앉은 지 며칠이 채 못 되어, 위층 창문에 그 빛나는 눈이 나타났는데, 보아하니 그쪽도 독서에 깊이 빠져 있는 모양이었다."], ["This was delightful, and gladdening to the heart of Nathaniel Pipkin.", "이것은 너새니얼 핍킨의 마음에 더없이 흐뭇하고 기쁜 일이었다."], ["It was something to sit there for hours together, and look upon that pretty face when the eyes were cast down; but when Maria Lobbs began to raise her eyes from her book, and dart their rays in the direction of Nathaniel Pipkin, his delight and admiration were perfectly boundless.", "그 어여쁜 얼굴의 눈이 내리깔려 있을 때라도 거기 몇 시간이고 앉아 바라보는 것만으로도 대단한 일이었지만, 마리아 롭스가 책에서 눈을 들어 그 눈빛을 너새니얼 핍킨 쪽으로 쏘기 시작하면 그의 기쁨과 흠모는 그야말로 끝이 없었다."], ["At last, one day when he knew old Lobbs was out, Nathaniel Pipkin had the temerity to kiss his hand to Maria Lobbs; and Maria Lobbs, instead of shutting the window, and pulling down the blind, kissed hers to him, and smiled.", "마침내 어느 날, 노인 롭스가 외출한 것을 안 너새니얼 핍킨은 대담하게도 마리아 롭스에게 자기 손에 입을 맞춰 보였고, 마리아 롭스는 창문을 닫고 블라인드를 내리는 대신, 그에게 제 손에 입을 맞춰 보이며 미소를 지었다."], ["Upon which Nathaniel Pipkin determined, that, come what might, he would develop the state of his feelings, without further delay.", "이에 너새니얼 핍킨은 무슨 일이 있어도 더 미루지 않고 제 마음의 상태를 펼쳐 보이리라 결심했다."]], [["‘A prettier foot, a gayer heart, a more dimpled face, or a smarter form, never bounded so lightly over the earth they graced, as did those of Maria Lobbs, the old saddler’s daughter.", "\"이보다 더 어여쁜 발, 더 명랑한 마음, 더 보조개진 얼굴, 더 맵시 있는 몸매가 자기가 빛내 주는 땅 위를 그토록 가볍게 뛰노닌 적은 없었으니, 늙은 마구상의 딸 마리아 롭스가 바로 그러했다."], ["There was a roguish twinkle in her sparkling eyes, that would have made its way to far less susceptible bosoms than that of Nathaniel Pipkin; and there was such a joyous sound in her merry laugh, that the sternest misanthrope must have smiled to hear it.", "그 반짝이는 눈에는 짓궂은 빛이 어른거려, 너새니얼 핍킨보다 훨씬 둔감한 가슴이라도 능히 파고들었을 것이고, 그 흥겨운 웃음소리에는 어찌나 즐거운 가락이 있던지 아무리 무뚝뚝한 염세가라도 그것을 들으면 미소 짓지 않을 수 없었을 것이다."], ["Even old Lobbs himself, in the very height of his ferocity, couldn’t resist the coaxing of his pretty daughter; and when she, and her cousin Kate--an arch, impudent-looking, bewitching little person-- made a dead set upon the old man together, as, to say the truth, they very often did, he could have refused them nothing, even had they asked for a portion of the countless and inexhaustible treasures, which were hidden from the light, in the iron safe.", "노인 롭스 자신마저도, 그 사나움이 한창 치솟을 때조차 어여쁜 딸의 어리광에는 당해 낼 수 없었다. 그래서 그녀와 그녀의 사촌 케이트—짓궂고 뻔뻔스러워 보이는, 사람을 호리는 작은 아가씨—가 둘이서 함께 노인을 작정하고 졸라 댈 때면, 사실 그들은 곧잘 그러곤 했는데, 노인은 그들에게 아무것도 거절하지 못했을 것이니, 설령 그들이 빛을 보지 못한 채 쇠금고 안에 숨겨진 그 헤아릴 수 없고 마르지 않는 보물의 일부를 달라고 했더라도 그러했을 것이다."]], [["‘Nathaniel Pipkin’s heart beat high within him, when he saw this enticing little couple some hundred yards before him one summer’s evening, in the very field in which he had many a time strolled about till night-time, and pondered on the beauty of Maria Lobbs.", "\"어느 여름 저녁, 너새니얼 핍킨이 이 매혹적인 작은 두 사람을 백 야드쯤 앞에서 보았을 때 그의 가슴은 높이 뛰었다. 그곳은 바로 그가 여러 번 밤이 깊도록 거닐며 마리아 롭스의 아름다움을 곱씹던 바로 그 들판이었다."], ["But though he had often thought then, how briskly he would walk up to Maria Lobbs and tell her of his passion if he could only meet her, he felt, now that she was unexpectedly before him, all the blood in his body mounting to his face, manifestly to the great detriment of his legs, which, deprived of their usual portion, trembled beneath him.", "그러나 그때마다 그는, 그녀를 만나기만 하면 얼마나 씩씩하게 마리아 롭스에게 다가가 제 연정을 고백하겠노라 곧잘 생각했건만, 막상 그녀가 뜻밖에 눈앞에 나타나자 온몸의 피가 얼굴로 죄다 솟구치는 것을 느꼈고, 그 탓에 평소의 몫을 빼앗긴 두 다리가 그 아래에서 후들거리니, 이는 분명 다리에 큰 손해였다. 그들이 울타리 꽃을 꺾으려, 혹은 새소리를 들으려 멈춰 서면 너새니얼 핍킨도 멈춰 서서 사색에 잠긴 척했는데, 사실 그는 정말로 사색에 잠겨 있었다."], ["When they stopped to gather a hedge flower, or listen to a bird, Nathaniel Pipkin stopped too, and pretended to be absorbed in meditation, as indeed he really was; for he was thinking what on earth he should ever do, when they turned back, as they inevitably must in time, and meet him face to face.", "머잖아 그들이 반드시 그래야 할 테고, 그래서 그들이 돌아서서 얼굴을 마주 보게 되면 도대체 어찌해야 할지를 궁리하고 있었으니 말이다. 그러나 그는 그들에게 다가가기는 두려워하면서도 그들을 시야에서 놓치는 것은 견딜 수 없었다."], ["But though he was afraid to make up to them, he couldn’t bear to lose sight of them; so when they walked faster he walked faster, when they lingered he lingered, and when they stopped he stopped; and so they might have gone on, until the darkness prevented them, if Kate had not looked slyly back, and encouragingly beckoned Nathaniel to advance.", "그래서 그들이 빨리 걸으면 그도 빨리 걸었고, 그들이 머뭇거리면 그도 머뭇거렸으며, 그들이 멈추면 그도 멈췄다. 그렇게 어둠이 그들을 가로막을 때까지 계속 갔을지도 모르나, 케이트가 슬쩍 뒤를 돌아보며 너새니얼에게 어서 오라고 부추기듯 손짓하지 않았던들 그러했을 것이다."], ["There was something in Kate’s manner that was not to be resisted, and so Nathaniel Pipkin complied with the invitation; and after a great deal of blushing on his part, and immoderate laughter on that of the wicked little cousin, Nathaniel Pipkin went down on his knees on the dewy grass, and declared his resolution to remain there for ever, unless he were permitted to rise the accepted lover of Maria Lobbs.", "케이트의 태도에는 거역할 수 없는 무언가가 있었던지라 너새니얼 핍킨은 그 청에 응했다. 그러고는 그가 한바탕 낯을 붉히고, 그 짓궂은 작은 사촌이 한바탕 분에 넘치게 웃어 댄 끝에, 너새니얼 핍킨은 이슬 젖은 풀밭에 무릎을 꿇고, 마리아 롭스의 받아들여진 연인으로 일어서도록 허락받지 않는 한 영원히 그 자리에 머물겠다는 결심을 선언했다."], ["Upon this, the merry laughter of Miss Lobbs rang through the calm evening air--without seeming to disturb it, though; it had such a pleasant sound--and the wicked little cousin laughed more immoderately than before, and Nathaniel Pipkin blushed deeper than ever.", "이에 롭스 양의 흥겨운 웃음소리가 고요한 저녁 공기를 가르며 울려 퍼졌으나—그러면서도 그 공기를 어지럽히지는 않는 듯했으니, 그 소리가 그토록 듣기 좋았던 까닭이다—짓궂은 작은 사촌은 아까보다 더 분에 넘치게 웃어 댔고, 너새니얼 핍킨은 어느 때보다 더 깊이 낯을 붉혔다."], ["At length, Maria Lobbs being more strenuously urged by the love-worn little man, turned away her head, and whispered her cousin to say, or at all events Kate did say, that she felt much honoured by Mr.", "마침내 마리아 롭스는 사랑에 애가 닳은 작은 사내가 더욱 간곡히 졸라 대자 고개를 돌려 사촌에게 대신 말해 달라고 속삭였고, 어쨌거나 케이트가 이렇게 말했다."], ["Pipkin’s addresses; that her hand and heart were at her father’s disposal; but that nobody could be insensible to Mr. Pipkin’s merits.", "마리아는 핍킨 씨의 구애를 무척 영광스럽게 여기며, 자신의 손과 마음은 아버지의 뜻에 달려 있으나, 핍킨 씨의 장점에 무심할 사람은 아무도 없을 것이라고."], ["As all this was said with much gravity, and as Nathaniel Pipkin walked home with Maria Lobbs, and struggled for a kiss at parting, he went to bed a happy man, and dreamed all night long, of softening old Lobbs, opening the strong box, and marrying Maria.", "이 모든 말이 무척이나 진지하게 전해졌고, 너새니얼 핍킨은 마리아 롭스와 함께 집으로 걸어가며 헤어질 적에 입맞춤을 얻으려 애쓴 끝에, 행복한 사내가 되어 잠자리에 들었고, 밤새도록 노인 롭스를 누그러뜨리고, 금고를 열고, 마리아와 결혼하는 꿈을 꾸었다."]], [["The next day, Nathaniel Pipkin saw old Lobbs go out upon his old gray pony, and after a great many signs at the window from the wicked little cousin, the object and meaning of which he could by no means understand, the bony apprentice with the thin legs came over to say that his master wasn’t coming home all night, and that the ladies expected Mr. Pipkin to tea, at six o’clock precisely.", "\"이튿날, 너새니얼 핍킨은 노인 롭스가 그 늙은 잿빛 조랑말을 타고 외출하는 것을 보았고, 짓궂은 작은 사촌이 창문에서 여러 차례 신호를 보냈으나 그 뜻과 의미를 도무지 알아챌 수 없던 차에, 다리가 가느다란 깡마른 도제가 건너와서, 주인이 밤새 집에 돌아오지 않을 것이며, 아가씨들이 핍킨 씨가 정확히 여섯 시에 차를 들러 오기를 기다린다고 전했다."], ["How the lessons were got through that day, neither Nathaniel Pipkin nor his pupils knew any more than you do; but they were got through somehow, and, after the boys had gone, Nathaniel Pipkin took till full six o’clock to dress himself to his satisfaction.", "그날 수업이 어떻게 끝났는지는 너새니얼 핍킨도 그의 학생들도 여러분만큼이나 모를 일이지만, 어쨌든 어떻게든 끝나기는 했고, 아이들이 돌아간 뒤 너새니얼 핍킨은 만족스럽게 차려입느라 여섯 시가 꽉 차도록 시간을 들였다. 입을 옷을 고르는 데 오래 걸려서가 아니었다."], ["Not that it took long to select the garments he should wear, inasmuch as he had no choice about the matter; but the putting of them on to the best advantage, and the touching of them up previously, was a task of no inconsiderable difficulty or importance.", "그 문제에서는 달리 선택의 여지가 없었으니까. 그러나 그 옷을 가장 돋보이게 차려입고, 미리 손질을 해 두는 일은 결코 가볍지 않은 어려움과 중요성을 지닌 과업이었다."]], [["‘There was a very snug little party, consisting of Maria Lobbs and her cousin Kate, and three or four romping, good-humoured, rosy-cheeked girls.", "\"그곳에는 마리아 롭스와 그녀의 사촌 케이트, 그리고 서너 명의 떠들썩하고 명랑하며 볼이 발그레한 처녀들로 이루어진, 아주 아늑한 작은 모임이 있었다."], ["Nathaniel Pipkin had ocular demonstration of the fact, that the rumours of old Lobbs’s treasures were not exaggerated.", "너새니얼 핍킨은 노인 롭스의 보물에 관한 소문이 과장이 아니었다는 사실을 두 눈으로 똑똑히 확인했다."], ["There were the real solid silver teapot, cream-ewer, and sugar-basin, on the table, and real silver spoons to stir the tea with, and real china cups to drink it out of, and plates of the same, to hold the cakes and toast in.", "식탁에는 진짜 묵직한 은제 찻주전자와 크림 단지와 설탕 그릇이 놓여 있었고, 차를 저을 진짜 은수저와, 차를 따라 마실 진짜 도자기 잔과, 케이크와 토스트를 담을 같은 도자기 접시들이 있었다."], ["The only eye-sore in the whole place was another cousin of Maria Lobbs’s, and a brother of Kate, whom Maria Lobbs called “Henry,” and who seemed to keep Maria Lobbs all to himself, up in one corner of the table.", "그 모든 곳에서 단 하나 눈엣가시는 마리아 롭스의 또 다른 사촌이자 케이트의 오라비였으니, 마리아 롭스는 그를 \"헨리\"라 불렀고, 그는 식탁 한쪽 구석에서 마리아 롭스를 온통 제 차지로 붙들어 두는 것 같았다."], ["It’s a delightful thing to see affection in families, but it may be carried rather too far, and Nathaniel Pipkin could not help thinking that Maria Lobbs must be very particularly fond of her relations, if she paid as much attention to all of them as to this individual cousin.", "가족 간의 정을 보는 것은 흐뭇한 일이지만, 그것도 좀 지나치면 곤란한 법인데, 너새니얼 핍킨은 마리아 롭스가 모든 친척에게 이 한 사람의 사촌에게 쏟는 만큼의 관심을 쏟는다면, 그녀는 친척을 유난히도 끔찍이 아끼는 사람임에 틀림없다고 생각하지 않을 수 없었다."], ["After tea, too, when the wicked little cousin proposed a game at blind man’s buff, it somehow or other happened that Nathaniel Pipkin was nearly always blind, and whenever he laid his hand upon the male cousin, he was sure to find that Maria Lobbs was not far off.", "게다가 차를 마신 뒤, 짓궂은 작은 사촌이 술래잡기 놀이를 제안하자, 어찌 된 영문인지 너새니얼 핍킨은 거의 늘 눈을 가린 술래가 되었고, 그가 그 사내 사촌에게 손을 댈 때마다 어김없이 마리아 롭스가 멀지 않은 곳에 있음을 알게 되었다."], ["And though the wicked little cousin and the other girls pinched him, and pulled his hair, and pushed chairs in his way, and all sorts of things, Maria Lobbs never seemed to come near him at all; and once--once--Nathaniel Pipkin could have sworn he heard the sound of a kiss, followed by a faint remonstrance from Maria Lobbs, and a half-suppressed laugh from her female friends.", "그리고 짓궂은 작은 사촌과 다른 처녀들이 그를 꼬집고, 머리카락을 잡아당기고, 그의 앞길에 의자를 밀어 놓는 따위 온갖 짓을 하는데도, 마리아 롭스는 도무지 그의 곁에 오는 법이 없는 것 같았다. 그리고 한 번—단 한 번—너새니얼 핍킨은 분명 입맞춤 소리를 들었고, 뒤이어 마리아 롭스의 희미한 항의와 그녀의 여자 친구들의 반쯤 억눌린 웃음소리를 들었노라 맹세할 수도 있었다."], ["All this was odd--very odd--and there is no saying what Nathaniel Pipkin might or might not have done, in consequence, if his thoughts had not been suddenly directed into a new channel.", "이 모든 것이 야릇했다—참으로 야릇했다—그리고 만일 그의 생각이 갑자기 새로운 물길로 돌려지지 않았던들, 그 결과 너새니얼 핍킨이 무슨 짓을 했을지 안 했을지는 알 수 없는 노릇이다."]], [["‘The circumstance which directed his thoughts into a new channel was a loud knocking at the street door, and the person who made this loud knocking at the street door was no other than old Lobbs himself, who had unexpectedly returned, and was hammering away, like a coffin-maker; for he wanted his supper.", "\"그의 생각을 새로운 물길로 돌린 사정인즉, 거리 쪽 문을 쾅쾅 두드리는 요란한 소리였으니, 그렇게 거리 쪽 문을 요란하게 두드린 사람은 다름 아닌 노인 롭스 자신이었다. 그는 뜻밖에 돌아와 관 짜는 목수처럼 쾅쾅 두들겨 대고 있었으니, 저녁밥을 먹고 싶었던 것이다."], ["The alarming intelligence was no sooner communicated by the bony apprentice with the thin legs, than the girls tripped upstairs to Maria Lobbs’s bedroom, and the male cousin and Nathaniel Pipkin were thrust into a couple of closets in the sitting- room, for want of any better places of concealment; and when Maria Lobbs and the wicked little cousin had stowed them away, and put the room to rights, they opened the street door to old Lobbs, who had never left off knocking since he first began.", "다리가 가느다란 깡마른 도제가 이 놀라운 소식을 전하기가 무섭게, 처녀들은 마리아 롭스의 침실로 종종걸음으로 올라갔고, 사내 사촌과 너새니얼 핍킨은 더 나은 은신처가 없던 터라 거실에 있는 벽장 두 개에 떠밀려 들어갔다. 마리아 롭스와 짓궂은 작은 사촌이 그들을 안에 숨기고 방을 말끔히 정돈한 뒤, 처음 두드리기 시작한 이래 한 번도 멈추지 않은 노인 롭스를 향해 거리 쪽 문을 열어 주었다."]], [["‘Now it did unfortunately happen that old Lobbs being very hungry was monstrous cross.", "\"그런데 공교롭게도 노인 롭스는 몹시 시장한 탓에 무지막지하게 심술이 나 있었다."], ["Nathaniel Pipkin could hear him growling away like an old mastiff with a sore throat; and whenever the unfortunate apprentice with the thin legs came into the room, so surely did old Lobbs commence swearing at him in a most Saracenic and ferocious manner, though apparently with no other end or object than that of easing his bosom by the discharge of a few superfluous oaths.", "너새니얼 핍킨은 그가 목이 쉰 늙은 마스티프처럼 으르렁대는 소리를 들을 수 있었고, 다리가 가느다란 그 가엾은 도제가 방에 들어올 때마다 노인 롭스는 어김없이 더없이 사라센적이고 사나운 투로 그에게 욕을 퍼붓기 시작했는데, 보아하니 군더더기 욕설 몇 마디를 토해 내어 제 가슴을 후련하게 하는 것 말고는 다른 목적이나 의도가 없는 듯했다."], ["At length some supper, which had been warming up, was placed on the table, and then old Lobbs fell to, in regular style; and having made clear work of it in no time, kissed his daughter, and demanded his pipe.", "마침내 데워 두었던 저녁밥이 식탁에 차려졌고, 노인 롭스는 격식대로 먹기 시작하더니, 눈 깜짝할 새에 말끔히 해치우고는 딸에게 입을 맞추고 파이프를 가져오라고 했다."]], [["‘Nature had placed Nathaniel Pipkin’s knees in very close juxtaposition, but when he heard old Lobbs demand his pipe, they knocked together, as if they were going to reduce each other to powder; for, depending from a couple of hooks, in the very closet in which he stood, was a large, brown-stemmed, silver-bowled pipe, which pipe he himself had seen in the mouth of old Lobbs, regularly every afternoon and evening, for the last five years.", "\"자연은 너새니얼 핍킨의 두 무릎을 아주 바싹 붙여 놓았는데, 노인 롭스가 제 파이프를 가져오라고 하는 소리를 듣자 그 두 무릎이 서로 가루로 만들어 버리기라도 할 듯이 맞부딪쳤다. 왜냐하면 그가 서 있는 바로 그 벽장 안에, 갈고리 두 개에 매달려, 갈색 대롱에 은제 통이 달린 커다란 파이프가 걸려 있었으니, 바로 지난 오 년 동안 그가 매일 오후와 저녁마다 어김없이 노인 롭스의 입에 물려 있는 것을 봐 온 그 파이프였기 때문이다."], ["The two girls went downstairs for the pipe, and upstairs for the pipe, and everywhere but where they knew the pipe was, and old Lobbs stormed away meanwhile, in the most wonderful manner.", "두 처녀는 파이프를 찾으러 아래층으로 내려가고 위층으로 올라가고, 정작 파이프가 있는 줄 아는 곳만 빼고 사방을 다 뒤졌으며, 그동안 노인 롭스는 더없이 놀라운 기세로 길길이 날뛰었다."], ["At last he thought of the closet, and walked up to it.", "마침내 그는 벽장을 떠올리고 그쪽으로 걸어갔다."], ["It was of no use a little man like Nathaniel Pipkin pulling the door inwards, when a great strong fellow like old Lobbs was pulling it outwards.", "노인 롭스 같은 덩치 크고 힘센 사내가 문을 바깥쪽으로 잡아당기는데, 너새니얼 핍킨 같은 작은 사내가 문을 안쪽으로 잡아당겨 봐야 아무 소용이 없었다."], ["Old Lobbs gave it one tug, and open it flew, disclosing Nathaniel Pipkin standing bolt upright inside, and shaking with apprehension from head to foot.", "노인 롭스가 한 번 홱 잡아당기자 문이 벌컥 열리며, 안에 빳빳이 곧추서서 머리끝부터 발끝까지 두려움에 떨고 있는 너새니얼 핍킨이 드러났다."], ["Bless us!", "어이쿠!"], ["what an appalling look old Lobbs gave him, as he dragged him out by the collar, and held him at arm’s length.", "노인 롭스가 그의 멱살을 잡아 끌어내어 팔을 쭉 뻗어 붙들고는 그에게 던진 그 무시무시한 눈초리란!"]], [["‘“Why, what the devil do you want here?” said old Lobbs, in a fearful voice.", "\"《그래, 도대체 여기서 뭘 하자는 거냐?》 노인 롭스가 무시무시한 목소리로 말했다."]], [["‘Nathaniel Pipkin could make no reply, so old Lobbs shook him backwards and forwards, for two or three minutes, by way of arranging his ideas for him.", "\"너새니얼 핍킨은 아무 대답도 못 했고, 그러자 노인 롭스는 그의 생각을 정리해 주려는 셈으로 그를 두세 분 동안 앞뒤로 뒤흔들었다."]], [["‘“What do you want here?”", "\"《여기서 뭘 하자는 거냐?》 롭스가 고함쳤다."], ["roared Lobbs; “I suppose you have come after my daughter, now!”", "《내 딸을 노리고 온 게로구나, 그렇지!》"]], [["‘Old Lobbs merely said this as a sneer: for he did not believe that mortal presumption could have carried Nathaniel Pipkin so far.", "\"노인 롭스가 이렇게 말한 것은 그저 비웃으려는 것이었으니, 인간의 주제넘음이 너새니얼 핍킨을 그토록 멀리까지 끌고 갔으리라고는 믿지 않았던 것이다."], ["What was his indignation, when that poor man replied--", "그러니 그 가엾은 사내가 이렇게 대답했을 때 그의 분노가 어떠했겠는가—"]], [["‘“Yes, I did, Mr. Lobbs, I did come after your daughter.", "\"《예, 그렇습니다, 롭스 씨, 저는 따님을 노리고 왔습니다."], ["I love her, Mr. Lobbs.”", "저는 따님을 사랑합니다, 롭스 씨.》"]], [["‘“Why, you snivelling, wry-faced, puny villain,” gasped old Lobbs, paralysed by the atrocious confession; “what do you mean by that?", "\"《이런, 이 훌쩍거리는 비뚜름한 낯짝의 하찮은 악당아.》 노인 롭스는 그 끔찍한 고백에 얼이 빠진 채 헐떡였다."], ["Say this to my face!", "《그게 무슨 소리냐?"], ["Damme, I’ll throttle you!”", "내 얼굴에다 대고 이런 말을 해? 빌어먹을, 네 멱을 비틀어 놓겠다!》"]], [["‘It is by no means improbable that old Lobbs would have carried his threat into execution, in the excess of his rage, if his arm had not been stayed by a very unexpected apparition: to wit, the male cousin, who, stepping out of his closet, and walking up to old Lobbs, said--", "\"노인 롭스가 격노한 나머지 그 위협을 정말로 실행에 옮겼을 법도 했으나, 아주 뜻밖의 출현에 그의 팔이 멈춰 섰으니, 곧 그 사내 사촌이었다. 그가 제 벽장에서 걸어 나와 노인 롭스에게 다가가더니 이렇게 말했다—"]], [["‘“I cannot allow this harmless person, Sir, who has been asked here, in some girlish frolic, to take upon himself, in a very noble manner, the fault (if fault it is) which I am guilty of, and am ready to avow.", "\"《어르신, 어떤 처녀들의 장난질에 불려 와서, 제가 저질렀고 또 기꺼이 인정하려는 잘못(만일 그것이 잘못이라면)을 그토록 갸륵하게 제 탓으로 떠안으려는 이 해롭지 않은 사람을, 저는 그대로 두고 볼 수 없습니다."], ["I love your daughter, sir; and I came here for the purpose of meeting her.”", "저는 따님을 사랑합니다, 어르신. 그리고 저는 그녀를 만나려고 여기 왔습니다.》"]], [["‘Old Lobbs opened his eyes very wide at this, but not wider than Nathaniel Pipkin.", "\"노인 롭스는 이 말에 눈을 휘둥그레 떴으나, 너새니얼 핍킨이 휘둥그레 뜬 것만큼은 아니었다."]], [["‘“You did?” said Lobbs, at last finding breath to speak.", "\"《네가?》 마침내 말할 숨을 되찾은 롭스가 말했다."]], [["‘“I did.”", "\"《제가요.》"]], [["‘“And I forbade you this house, long ago.”", "\"《내가 오래전에 이 집에 발도 들이지 말라고 했거늘.》"]], [["‘“You did, or I should not have been here, clandestinely, to-night.”", "\"《그러셨지요. 그러지 않으셨다면 오늘 밤 제가 몰래 여기 와 있지도 않았겠지요.》"]], [["‘I am sorry to record it of old Lobbs, but I think he would have struck the cousin, if his pretty daughter, with her bright eyes swimming in tears, had not clung to his arm.", "\"노인 롭스에 관해 이렇게 기록하기는 가슴 아프지만, 만일 그 어여쁜 딸이 눈물 어린 빛나는 눈으로 그의 팔에 매달리지 않았던들, 그는 그 사촌을 후려쳤으리라고 나는 생각한다."]], [["‘“Don’t stop him, Maria,”", "\"《아버지를 말리지 마시오, 마리아.》 젊은이가 말했다."], ["said the young man; “if he has the will to strike me, let him.", "《어르신이 나를 칠 마음이라면, 치게 두시오."], ["I would not hurt a hair of his gray head, for the riches of the world.”", "나는 세상의 온갖 재물을 준다 해도 그분의 흰 머리카락 한 올 다치게 하지 않을 것이오.》"]], [["‘The old man cast down his eyes at this reproof, and they met those of his daughter.", "\"노인은 이 꾸짖음에 눈을 내리깔았고, 그 눈이 딸의 눈과 마주쳤다."], ["I have hinted once or twice before, that they were very bright eyes, and, though they were tearful now, their influence was by no means lessened.", "앞서 한두 번 넌지시 일렀듯이 그것은 무척 빛나는 눈이었고, 비록 지금은 눈물에 젖어 있었으나 그 위력은 결코 줄어들지 않았다."], ["Old Lobbs turned his head away, as if to avoid being persuaded by them, when, as fortune would have it, he encountered the face of the wicked little cousin, who, half afraid for her brother, and half laughing at Nathaniel Pipkin, presented as bewitching an expression of countenance, with a touch of slyness in it, too, as any man, old or young, need look upon.", "노인 롭스는 그 눈에 설득당하지 않으려는 듯 고개를 옆으로 돌렸는데, 운명의 장난인지 그만 짓궂은 작은 사촌의 얼굴과 마주쳤다. 그녀는 제 오라비가 반쯤 걱정스럽고 너새니얼 핍킨이 반쯤 우스워서, 늙은이든 젊은이든 어느 사내가 보아도 좋을 만큼, 거기에 약간의 능청까지 깃든 더없이 사람을 호리는 표정을 짓고 있었다."], ["She drew her arm coaxingly through the old man’s, and whispered something in his ear; and do what he would, old Lobbs couldn’t help breaking out into a smile, while a tear stole down his cheek at the same time.", "그녀가 노인의 팔에 제 팔을 어리광 부리듯 끼고는 그의 귀에 무언가 속삭이자, 노인 롭스는 아무리 애써도 빙그레 미소를 터뜨리지 않을 수 없었고, 그와 동시에 한 줄기 눈물이 그의 뺨을 타고 흘러내렸다."]], [["‘Five minutes after this, the girls were brought down from the bedroom with a great deal of giggling and modesty; and while the young people were making themselves perfectly happy, old Lobbs got down the pipe, and smoked it; and it was a remarkable circumstance about that particular pipe of tobacco, that it was the most soothing and delightful one he ever smoked.", "\"이로부터 오 분 뒤, 처녀들이 한바탕 키득거림과 수줍음과 함께 침실에서 아래로 내려왔고, 젊은이들이 더없이 행복해하는 동안 노인 롭스는 파이프를 내려 피웠는데, 그 한 대의 담배에 관해 한 가지 주목할 만한 점은, 그것이 그가 일찍이 피워 본 것 가운데 가장 마음을 누그러뜨리고 가장 흐뭇한 한 대였다는 것이다."]], [["‘Nathaniel Pipkin thought it best to keep his own counsel, and by so doing gradually rose into high favour with old Lobbs, who taught him to smoke in time; and they used to sit out in the garden on the fine evenings, for many years afterwards, smoking and drinking in great state.", "\"너새니얼 핍킨은 제 속을 잠자코 간직하는 것이 상책이라 여겼고, 그렇게 함으로써 차츰 노인 롭스의 두터운 총애를 받게 되었으니, 노인은 머잖아 그에게 담배 피우는 법까지 가르쳐 주었다. 그래서 두 사람은 그 뒤로 여러 해 동안 날씨 좋은 저녁이면 정원에 나앉아 거하게 담배를 피우고 술을 들곤 했다."], ["He soon recovered the effects of his attachment, for we find his name in the parish register, as a witness to the marriage of Maria Lobbs to her cousin; and it also appears, by reference to other documents, that on the night of the wedding he was incarcerated in the village cage, for having, in a state of extreme intoxication, committed sundry excesses in the streets, in all of which he was aided and abetted by the bony apprentice with the thin legs.’", "그는 곧 제 연정의 후유증을 떨쳐 냈으니, 교구 등록부에 마리아 롭스와 그녀의 사촌의 결혼 증인으로 그의 이름이 올라 있는 것이 보이는 까닭이다. 또한 다른 문서들을 참조해 보면, 결혼식 날 밤 그는 만취한 상태로 거리에서 이런저런 난동을 부린 죄로 마을 유치장에 갇혔다고 나오는데, 그 모든 난동에서 그는 다리가 가느다란 깡마른 도제의 거들고 부추김을 받았다고 한다.\""]], [["CHAPTER XVIII. BRIEFLY ILLUSTRATIVE OF TWO POINTS; FIRST, THE POWER OF HYSTERICS, AND, SECONDLY, THE FORCE OF CIRCUMSTANCES\nFor two days after the dejeune at Mrs. Hunter’s, the Pickwickians remained at Eatanswill, anxiously awaiting the arrival of some intelligence from their revered leader. Mr. Tupman and Mr. Snodgrass were once again left to their own means of amusement; for Mr. Winkle, in compliance with a most pressing invitation, continued to reside at Mr. Pott’s house, and to devote his time to the companionship of his amiable lady. Nor was the occasional society of Mr. Pott himself wanting to complete their felicity. Deeply immersed in the intensity of his speculations for the public weal and the destruction of the Independent, it was not the habit of that great man to descend from his mental pinnacle to the humble level of ordinary minds. On this occasion, however, and as if expressly in compliment to any follower of Mr. Pickwick’s, he unbent, relaxed, stepped down from his pedestal, and walked upon the ground, benignly adapting his remarks to the comprehension of the herd, and seeming in outward form, if not in spirit, to be one of them.", "헌터 부인 댁의 조찬이 있은 뒤 이틀 동안, 픽윅 회원들은 이턴스윌에 머무르며 존경하는 지도자로부터 무슨 소식이 도착하기를 애타게 기다렸다. 터프먼 씨와 스노드그래스 씨는 다시 한 번 저마다 알아서 소일거리를 찾아야 하는 처지가 되었으니, 윈클 씨는 더없이 간곡한 초대에 응하여 여전히 포트 씨 댁에 머무르며 그의 상냥한 부인과 어울리는 데 시간을 바치고 있었기 때문이다. 게다가 이따금 포트 씨 자신과의 교제까지 곁들여져 그들의 행복은 완전해졌다. 공공의 복리와 인디펜던트 신문의 파멸을 위한 깊은 궁리에 골몰해 있던 이 위대한 인물은, 자신의 정신적 정점에서 평범한 머리들의 비천한 수준으로 내려오는 법이 좀처럼 없었다. 그러나 이번에는, 마치 픽윅 씨를 따르는 이라면 누구든 각별히 예우하려는 듯, 그는 마음을 풀고 긴장을 늦추며 받침대에서 내려와 땅 위를 걸었고, 무리들이 알아들을 수 있게 자비롭게 자신의 말을 맞추어, 정신은 아닐지언정 겉모습으로는 그들 가운데 하나처럼 보였다."]], [["Such having been the demeanour of this celebrated public character towards Mr. Winkle, it will be readily imagined that considerable surprise was depicted on the countenance of the latter gentleman, when, as he was sitting alone in the breakfast-room, the door was hastily thrown open, and as hastily closed, on the entrance of Mr. Pott, who, stalking majestically towards him, and thrusting aside his proffered hand, ground his teeth, as if to put a sharper edge on what he was about to utter, and exclaimed, in a saw-like voice--", "이 저명한 공인이 윈클 씨를 대하는 태도가 이러했으니, 그가 조찬실에 혼자 앉아 있을 때 문이 황급히 열렸다가 포트 씨가 들어서자마자 또 황급히 닫히고, 그 신사가 위풍당당하게 그를 향해 성큼성큼 다가와 내미는 손을 뿌리치며, 이제 막 내뱉으려는 말에 한층 날을 세우기라도 하듯 이를 갈고는, 톱질하는 듯한 목소리로 외쳤을 때, 후자 신사의 얼굴에 상당한 놀라움이 떠올랐으리라는 것은 쉽사리 짐작이 갈 것이다."]], [["‘Serpent!’", "\"이 뱀 같은 자!\""]], [["‘Sir!’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"이보시오!\""], ["Winkle, starting from his chair.", "윈클 씨가 의자에서 벌떡 일어서며 외쳤다."]], [["‘Serpent, Sir,’ repeated Mr.", "\"뱀 같은 자라고 했소.\""], ["Pott, raising his voice, and then suddenly depressing it: ‘I said, serpent, sir--make the most of it.’", "포트 씨가 목소리를 높였다가 별안간 낮추며 되풀이했다. \"내가 뱀 같은 자라고 했소—마음껏 새겨들으시오.\""]], [["When you have parted with a man at two o’clock in the morning, on terms of the utmost good-fellowship, and he meets you again, at half-past nine, and greets you as a serpent, it is not unreasonable to conclude that something of an unpleasant nature has occurred meanwhile.", "새벽 두 시에 더없이 두터운 우정을 나누며 헤어진 사람이 아홉 시 반에 다시 만나 뱀이라고 인사를 건넨다면, 그사이에 뭔가 불쾌한 일이 일어났으리라 결론짓는 것이 무리는 아닐 것이다."], ["So Mr.", "윈클 씨도 그렇게 생각했다."], ["Winkle thought. He returned Mr.", "그는 포트 씨의 돌 같은 시선을 마주 받았고, 그 신사의 요청에 따라 \"뱀\"이라는 말을 할 수 있는 한 마음껏 새겨 보았다."], ["Pott’s gaze of stone, and in compliance with that gentleman’s request, proceeded to make the most he could of the ‘serpent.’ The most, however, was nothing at all; so, after a profound silence of some minutes’ duration, he said,--", "그러나 마음껏 새긴 결과란 아무것도 아니었다. 그래서 몇 분간의 깊은 침묵 끝에 그가 말했다—"]], [["‘Serpent, Sir!", "\"뱀이라뇨, 이보시오!"], ["Serpent, Mr. Pott!", "뱀이라니, 포트 씨!"], ["What can you mean, Sir?--this is pleasantry.’", "대체 무슨 뜻입니까?—농담이겠지요.\""]], [["‘Pleasantry, sir!’ exclaimed Pott, with a motion of the hand, indicative of a strong desire to hurl the Britannia metal teapot at the head of the visitor.", "\"농담이라!\" 포트가 외치며, 영국제 백랍 찻주전자를 방문객의 머리에 내던지고 싶은 강한 욕구를 드러내는 손짓을 했다."], ["‘Pleasantry, sir!--But--no, I will be calm; I will be calm, Sir;’ in proof of his calmness, Mr.", "\"농담이라니!—하지만—아니, 침착하자, 침착해야지.\""], ["Pott flung himself into a chair, and foamed at the mouth.", "자신의 침착함을 입증하려는 듯, 포트 씨는 의자에 몸을 내던지고는 입에 거품을 물었다."]], [["‘My dear sir,’ interposed Mr.", "\"이보시오, 친애하는 분.\""], ["Winkle.", "윈클 씨가 끼어들었다."]], [["‘DEAR Sir!’ replied Pott.", "\"친애하는 분이라!\" 포트가 대꾸했다."], ["‘How dare you address me, as dear Sir, Sir?", "\"감히 나를 친애하는 분이라 부르다니, 이 사람아?"], ["How dare you look me in the face and do it, sir?’", "감히 내 얼굴을 똑바로 보면서 그렇게 부른단 말이오?\""]], [["‘Well, Sir, if you come to that,’ responded Mr.", "\"글쎄요, 그렇게 나오신다면,\" 윈클 씨가 받아쳤다."], ["Winkle, ‘how dare you look me in the face, and call me a serpent, sir?’", "\"당신이야말로 어떻게 감히 내 얼굴을 똑바로 보면서 나를 뱀이라 부른단 말이오?\""]], [["‘Because you are one,’ replied Mr.", "\"네가 뱀이니까 그러지.\""], ["Pott.", "포트 씨가 대꾸했다."]], [["‘Prove it, Sir,’ said Mr.", "\"증명해 보시오.\" 윈클 씨가 발끈하여 말했다."], ["Winkle warmly. ‘Prove it.’", "\"증명해 보시라고요.\""]], [["A malignant scowl passed over the profound face of the editor, as he drew from his pocket the Independent of that morning; and laying his finger on a particular paragraph, threw the journal across the table to Mr.", "편집장의 음흉한 얼굴 위로 악의에 찬 찡그림이 스쳐 갔다."], ["Winkle.", "그는 주머니에서 그날 아침 자 인디펜던트를 꺼내, 특정 문단에 손가락을 짚고는, 그 신문을 탁자 너머 윈클 씨에게로 던졌다."]], [["That gentleman took it up, and read as follows:--", "윈클 씨는 그것을 집어 들고 다음과 같이 읽었다—"]], [["‘Our obscure and filthy contemporary, in some disgusting observations on the recent election for this borough, has presumed to violate the hallowed sanctity of private life, and to refer in a manner not to be misunderstood, to the personal affairs of our late candidate--aye, and notwithstanding his base defeat, we will add, our future member, Mr.", "\"우리의 보잘것없고 더러운 동업지는, 이 자치구에서 최근 치러진 선거에 대한 역겨운 논평 가운데, 감히 사생활의 신성한 영역을 침범하고, 오해의 여지가 없는 방식으로 우리의 지난번 후보—그렇다, 그의 비열한 패배에도 불구하고, 덧붙이건대 우리의 장차 의원이 되실 분, 피즈킨 씨의 사적인 일을 들먹였다."], ["Fizkin. What does our dastardly contemporary mean?", "비겁한 우리 동업지는 대체 무슨 속셈인가?"], ["What would the ruffian say, if we, setting at naught, like him, the decencies of social intercourse, were to raise the curtain which happily conceals His private life from general ridicule, not to say from general execration?", "만일 우리가 저자처럼 사회적 교제의 예의를 헌신짝처럼 내던지고, 다행히 그자의 사생활을 세상의 조롱은 물론 세상의 저주로부터 가려 주고 있는 장막을 걷어 올린다면, 그 불한당은 무어라 하겠는가?"], ["What, if we were even to point out, and comment on, facts and circumstances, which are publicly notorious, and beheld by every one but our mole-eyed contemporary--what if we were to print the following effusion, which we received while we were writing the commencement of this article, from a talented fellow-townsman and correspondent?", "만일 우리가, 두더지 눈을 한 동업지를 빼고는 누구나 다 아는, 세상에 널리 알려진 사실과 정황을 들추어내어 논평한다면 어떻겠는가—만일 우리가 이 글의 첫머리를 쓰고 있을 때 재능 있는 한 동향인이자 기고가로부터 받은 다음의 일필을 인쇄한다면 어떻겠는가? \"'놋 단지에게 부치는 시구"]], [["‘“Oh Pott!", "\"'오 포트여!"], ["if you’d known How false she’d have grown, When you heard the marriage bells tinkle; You’d have done then, I vow, What you cannot help now,", "그대가 결혼의 종소리 딸랑이는 것을 들었을 때, 그녀가 얼마나 거짓될지 알았더라면, 그대는 그때 했으리라, 내 맹세코, 이제는 어쩔 수 없는 그 일을—'\""]], [["‘What,’ said Mr.", "\"'딸랑'에\""], ["Pott solemnly--‘what rhymes to “tinkle,” villain?’", "포트 씨가 엄숙하게 말했다—\"'딸랑'에 운이 맞는 말이 무엇이냐, 이 악당아?\""]], [["‘What rhymes to tinkle?’ said Mrs.", "\"'딸랑'에 운이 맞는 말이라뇨?\""], ["Pott, whose entrance at the moment forestalled the reply.", "마침 그 순간 들어선 포트 부인이 대답을 가로채며 말했다."], ["‘What rhymes to tinkle?", "\"'딸랑'에 운이 맞는 말이라고요?"], ["Why, Winkle, I should conceive.’ Saying this, Mrs.", "어머, '윙클'이겠지요.\""], ["Pott smiled sweetly on the disturbed Pickwickian, and extended her hand towards him.", "이렇게 말하며 포트 부인은 당혹스러워하는 픽윅 회원에게 다정하게 미소 짓고 손을 내밀었다."], ["The agitated young man would have accepted it, in his confusion, had not Pott indignantly interposed.", "마음이 흔들린 젊은이는 어찌할 바를 모른 채 그 손을 받았을 터인데, 포트가 분개하여 끼어들지 않았더라면 그러했을 것이다."]], [["‘Back, ma’am--back!’ said the editor.", "\"물러서시오, 부인—물러서!\" 편집장이 말했다."], ["‘Take his hand before my very face!’", "\"내 코앞에서 저자의 손을 잡다니!\""]], [["‘Mr.", "\"여보!\""], ["P.!’ said his astonished lady.", "깜짝 놀란 그의 부인이 말했다."]], [["‘Wretched woman, look here,’ exclaimed the husband.", "\"한심한 여자 같으니, 여기를 보시오.\" 남편이 외쳤다."], ["‘Look here, ma’am-- “Lines to a Brass Pot.”", "\"여기를 보란 말이오, 부인—'놋 단지에게 부치는 시구.'"], ["“Brass Pot”; that’s me, ma’am.", "'놋 단지', 그게 바로 나요, 부인."], ["“False she’d have grown”; that’s you, ma’am--you.’ With this ebullition of rage, which was not unaccompanied with something like a tremble, at the expression of his wife’s face, Mr.", "'그녀가 얼마나 거짓될지', 그게 바로 당신이오, 부인—당신 말이오.\""], ["Pott dashed the current number of the Eatanswill Independent at her feet.", "이렇게 분통을 터뜨리는데, 아내의 얼굴 표정을 보고는 어딘가 떨림 같은 것도 섞여 있었으니, 포트 씨는 이번 호 이턴스윌 인디펜던트를 그녀의 발치에 내동댕이쳤다."]], [["‘Upon my word, Sir,’ said the astonished Mrs.", "\"기가 막혀서, 여보.\""], ["Pott, stooping to pick up the paper.", "깜짝 놀란 포트 부인이 신문을 집으려 몸을 굽히며 말했다."], ["‘Upon my word, Sir!’", "\"기가 막혀서, 정말!\""]], [["Mr. Pott winced beneath the contemptuous gaze of his wife.", "포트 씨는 아내의 경멸 어린 시선 아래 움찔했다."], ["He had made a desperate struggle to screw up his courage, but it was fast coming unscrewed again.", "그는 용기를 끌어올리려 필사적으로 안간힘을 썼으나, 그 용기는 다시 빠르게 풀려 나가고 있었다."]], [["There appears nothing very tremendous in this little sentence, ‘Upon my word, sir,’ when it comes to be read; but the tone of voice in which it was delivered, and the look that accompanied it, both seeming to bear reference to some revenge to be thereafter visited upon the head of Pott, produced their effect upon him.", "이 짧은 한마디 \"기가 막혀서, 여보\"라는 말은, 글로만 읽으면 그리 대단해 보이지 않는다. 그러나 그 말이 전달된 목소리의 어조와 거기에 곁들여진 표정은, 둘 다 장차 포트의 머리 위에 내려질 어떤 복수를 가리키는 듯하여, 그에게 효력을 발휘했다."], ["The most unskilful observer could have detected in his troubled countenance, a readiness to resign his Wellington boots to any efficient substitute who would have consented to stand in them at that moment.", "아무리 서툰 관찰자라도 그의 어지러운 얼굴에서, 지금 이 순간 자신의 웰링턴 부츠를 신고 대신 서 주겠다고 할 만한 유능한 대역이 있다면 기꺼이 그 자리를 넘겨주고픈 마음을 읽어 낼 수 있었으리라."]], [["Mrs. Pott read the paragraph, uttered a loud shriek, and threw herself at full length on the hearth-rug, screaming, and tapping it with the heels of her shoes, in a manner which could leave no doubt of the propriety of her feelings on the occasion.", "포트 부인은 그 문단을 읽고는 요란한 비명을 지르며 난로 앞 깔개 위에 길게 몸을 던지더니, 비명을 지르며 구두 뒤축으로 깔개를 두드려 댔는데, 그 모양새는 이 일에 대한 그녀의 감정이 지극히 정당하다는 것을 의심할 여지가 없게 만들었다."]], [["‘My dear,’ said the terrified Pott, ‘I didn’t say I believed it;--I--’ but the unfortunate man’s voice was drowned in the screaming of his partner.", "\"여보,\" 겁에 질린 포트가 말했다. \"내가 그걸 믿는다고는 안 했소—나는—\" 그러나 그 가엾은 사내의 목소리는 아내의 비명에 묻혀 버렸다."]], [["‘Mrs. Pott, let me entreat you, my dear ma’am, to compose yourself,’ said Mr.", "\"포트 부인, 부탁드립니다, 친애하는 부인, 진정하십시오.\""], ["Winkle; but the shrieks and tappings were louder, and more frequent than ever.", "윈클 씨가 말했다. 그러나 비명과 두드림은 그 어느 때보다 더 크고 더 잦아질 뿐이었다."]], [["‘My dear,’ said Mr.", "\"여보,\" 포트 씨가 말했다."], ["Pott, ‘I’m very sorry.", "\"정말 미안하오."], ["If you won’t consider your own health, consider me, my dear.", "당신 건강은 생각 안 하더라도, 나를 좀 생각해 주오, 여보."], ["We shall have a crowd round the house.’ But the more strenuously Mr.", "집 주위에 사람들이 몰려들 거요.\""], ["Pott entreated, the more vehemently the screams poured forth.", "그러나 포트 씨가 간절히 빌면 빌수록 비명은 더욱 격렬하게 쏟아져 나왔다."]], [["Very fortunately, however, attached to Mrs. Pott’s person was a bodyguard of one, a young lady whose ostensible employment was to preside over her toilet, but who rendered herself useful in a variety of ways, and in none more so than in the particular department of constantly aiding and abetting her mistress in every wish and inclination opposed to the desires of the unhappy Pott.", "그런데 아주 다행스럽게도, 포트 부인에게는 한 사람으로 이루어진 호위병이 딸려 있었으니, 표면상 임무는 그녀의 몸단장을 맡아보는 것이었으나, 갖가지 방식으로 제 쓸모를 발휘하던 젊은 처자였다."], ["The screams reached this young lady’s ears in due course, and brought her into the room with a speed which threatened to derange, materially, the very exquisite arrangement of her cap and ringlets.", "그중에서도 가장 두드러진 분야는, 불행한 포트의 바람에 어긋나는 안주인의 온갖 소망과 성향을 끊임없이 거들고 부추기는 일이었다. 비명 소리는 때를 맞추어 이 젊은 처자의 귀에 닿았고, 그녀를 어찌나 빠른 속도로 방 안으로 불러들였던지, 더없이 정교하게 매만진 모자와 곱슬머리가 크게 흐트러질 지경이었다."]], [["‘Oh, my dear, dear mistress!’ exclaimed the bodyguard, kneeling frantically by the side of the prostrate Mrs.", "\"오, 친애하는, 친애하는 마님!\" 호위병이 엎드린 포트 부인 곁에 미친 듯이 무릎 꿇으며 외쳤다."], ["Pott. ‘Oh, my dear mistress, what is the matter?’", "\"오, 친애하는 마님, 무슨 일이세요?\""]], [["‘Your master--your brutal master,’ murmured the patient.", "\"네 주인—저 잔인한 주인이.\" 환자가 중얼거렸다."]], [["Pott was evidently giving way.", "포트는 누가 봐도 무너져 가고 있었다."]], [["‘It’s a shame,’ said the bodyguard reproachfully.", "\"너무하셨어요.\" 호위병이 나무라듯 말했다."], ["‘I know he’ll be the death on you, ma’am.", "\"저분이 마님을 잡고 말 거예요, 정말."], ["Poor dear thing!’", "가엾은 마님 같으니!\""]], [["He gave way more.", "그는 더욱 무너졌다."], ["The opposite party followed up the attack.", "상대편은 공격을 밀어붙였다."]], [["‘Oh, don’t leave me--don’t leave me, Goodwin,’ murmured Mrs.", "\"오, 날 떠나지 마라—날 떠나지 마, 굿윈.\""], ["Pott, clutching at the wrist of the said Goodwin with an hysteric jerk.", "포트 부인이 히스테릭하게 움찔하며 굿윈의 손목을 움켜쥐고 중얼거렸다."], ["‘You’re the only person that’s kind to me, Goodwin.’", "\"내게 잘해 주는 사람은 너밖에 없어, 굿윈.\""]], [["At this affecting appeal, Goodwin got up a little domestic tragedy of her own, and shed tears copiously.", "이 가슴 뭉클한 호소에, 굿윈은 자기 나름의 자그마한 가정 비극을 한바탕 꾸며 내어 눈물을 펑펑 쏟았다."]], [["‘Never, ma’am--never,’ said Goodwin. ‘Oh, sir, you should be careful-- you should indeed; you don’t know what harm you may do missis; you’ll be sorry for it one day, I know--I’ve always said so.’", "\"절대로요, 마님—절대로.\" 굿윈이 말했다. \"오, 나리, 조심하셔야 해요—정말로요. 마님께 얼마나 해를 끼치는지 모르시는 거예요. 언젠가 후회하실 거예요, 정말—제가 늘 그렇게 말씀드렸잖아요.\""]], [["The unlucky Pott looked timidly on, but said nothing.", "운 없는 포트는 겁먹은 눈으로 바라보기만 할 뿐 아무 말도 하지 못했다."]], [["‘Goodwin,’ said Mrs.", "\"굿윈.\""], ["Pott, in a soft voice.", "포트 부인이 부드러운 목소리로 말했다."]], [["‘Ma’am,’ said Goodwin.", "\"네, 마님.\" 굿윈이 말했다."]], [["‘If you only knew how I have loved that man--’", "\"내가 저 사람을 얼마나 사랑했는지 네가 알기만 한다면—\""]], [["Don’t distress yourself by recollecting it, ma’am,’ said the bodyguard.", "\"그걸 떠올리며 마음 아파하지 마세요, 마님.\" 호위병이 말했다."]], [["Pott looked very frightened.", "포트는 몹시 겁에 질린 표정이었다."], ["It was time to finish him.", "그를 끝장낼 때가 되었다."]], [["‘And now,’ sobbed Mrs.", "\"그런데 이제,\" 포트 부인이 흐느꼈다."], ["Pott, ‘now, after all, to be treated in this way; to be reproached and insulted in the presence of a third party, and that party almost a stranger.", "\"이제, 결국, 이런 식으로 취급당하다니. 제삼자가 보는 앞에서, 그것도 거의 낯선 이가 보는 앞에서 비난받고 모욕당하다니."], ["But I will not submit to it!", "하지만 난 이런 일을 참지 않겠어!"], ["Goodwin,’ continued Mrs.", "굿윈,\""], ["Pott, raising herself in the arms of her attendant, ‘my brother, the lieutenant, shall interfere.", "포트 부인이 시중드는 처자의 품에서 몸을 일으키며 말을 이었다. \"내 오빠, 그 중위가 나설 거야."], ["I’ll be separated, Goodwin!’", "난 별거하겠어, 굿윈!\""]], [["‘It would certainly serve him right, ma’am,’ said Goodwin.", "\"그래야 저분이 마땅한 벌을 받지요, 마님.\" 굿윈이 말했다."]], [["Whatever thoughts the threat of a separation might have awakened in Mr. Pott’s mind, he forbore to give utterance to them, and contented himself by saying, with great humility:--", "별거하겠다는 위협이 포트 씨의 마음속에 어떤 생각을 불러일으켰든, 그는 그것을 입 밖에 내기를 삼가고, 지극히 겸손하게 이렇게 말하는 것으로 만족했다—"]], [["‘My dear, will you hear me?’", "\"여보, 내 말 좀 들어 주겠소?\""]], [["A fresh train of sobs was the only reply, as Mrs. Pott grew more hysterical, requested to be informed why she was ever born, and required sundry other pieces of information of a similar description.", "새로운 흐느낌의 행렬만이 유일한 대답이었으니, 포트 부인은 더욱 히스테릭해져서 자신이 대체 왜 태어났는지 알려 달라고 청하고, 그와 비슷한 갖가지 정보들을 요구했다."]], [["‘My dear,’ remonstrated Mr.", "\"여보,\" 포트 씨가 타일렀다."], ["Pott, ‘do not give way to these sensitive feelings.", "\"그런 예민한 감정에 빠지지 마오."], ["I never believed that the paragraph had any foundation, my dear--impossible.", "난 그 문단에 무슨 근거가 있다고는 결코 믿지 않았소, 여보—그럴 리가 없지."], ["I was only angry, my dear--I may say outrageous--with the Independent people for daring to insert it; that’s all.’ Mr.", "난 그저 화가 났을 뿐이오, 여보—격분했다고 해도 좋소—감히 그걸 실은 인디펜던트 사람들한테 말이오. 그뿐이오.\""], ["Pott cast an imploring look at the innocent cause of the mischief, as if to entreat him to say nothing about the serpent.", "포트 씨는 이 사달의 무고한 원인을 향해 애원하는 눈길을 던졌으니, 마치 그 뱀 운운한 일에 대해서는 아무 말도 말아 달라고 간청하는 듯했다."]], [["‘And what steps, sir, do you mean to take to obtain redress?’ inquired Mr.", "\"그렇다면 어떤 조치를 취하셔서 명예를 회복하실 작정입니까?\""], ["Winkle, gaining courage as he saw Pott losing it.", "포트가 용기를 잃는 것을 보고 도리어 용기를 얻은 윈클 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘Oh, Goodwin,’ observed Mrs.", "\"오, 굿윈,\" 포트 부인이 말했다."], ["Pott, ‘does he mean to horsewhip the editor of the Independent--does he, Goodwin?’", "\"저 사람이 인디펜던트 편집장을 채찍질하겠다는 거니—그렇다는 거니, 굿윈?\""]], [["‘Hush, hush, ma’am; pray keep yourself quiet,’ replied the bodyguard.", "\"쉿, 쉿, 마님. 부디 조용히 하세요.\""], ["‘I dare say he will, if you wish it, ma’am.’", "호위병이 대답했다. \"마님이 바라신다면 분명 그러실 거예요, 마님.\""]], [["‘Certainly,’ said Pott, as his wife evinced decided symptoms of going off again.", "\"물론이지.\" 아내가 다시 발작을 일으킬 뚜렷한 징후를 보이자 포트가 말했다."], ["‘Of course I shall.’", "\"당연히 그래야지.\""]], [["‘When, Goodwin--when?’ said Mrs.", "\"언제, 굿윈—언제?\""], ["Pott, still undecided about the going off.", "발작을 일으킬지 말지 여전히 결정짓지 못한 채 포트 부인이 말했다."]], [["‘Immediately, of course,’ said Mr.", "\"물론 당장이오.\" 포트 씨가 말했다."], ["Pott; ‘before the day is out.’", "\"오늘이 가기 전에.\""]], [["‘Oh, Goodwin,’ resumed Mrs.", "\"오, 굿윈,\""], ["Pott, ‘it’s the only way of meeting the slander, and setting me right with the world.’", "포트 부인이 다시 입을 열었다. \"그게 그 중상에 맞서고 세상 앞에서 내 결백을 밝히는 유일한 길이야.\""]], [["‘Certainly, ma’am,’ replied Goodwin.", "\"물론이죠, 마님.\" 굿윈이 대답했다."], ["‘No man as is a man, ma’am, could refuse to do it.’", "\"사내대장부라면 누구도 그걸 마다할 수 없지요, 마님.\""]], [["So, as the hysterics were still hovering about, Mr. Pott said once more that he would do it; but Mrs.", "그리하여 히스테리가 여전히 주위를 맴돌고 있었으므로, 포트 씨는 다시 한 번 그렇게 하겠노라 말했다."], ["Pott was so overcome at the bare idea of having ever been suspected, that she was half a dozen times on the very verge of a relapse, and most unquestionably would have gone off, had it not been for the indefatigable efforts of the assiduous Goodwin, and repeated entreaties for pardon from the conquered Pott; and finally, when that unhappy individual had been frightened and snubbed down to his proper level, Mrs.", "그러나 포트 부인은 단 한 번이라도 의심받았다는 생각만으로도 어찌나 압도되었던지, 예닐곱 번이나 재발 직전에까지 이르렀고, 부지런한 굿윈의 지칠 줄 모르는 노력과 굴복한 포트의 거듭된 용서 간청이 없었더라면 영락없이 발작을 일으켰을 것이다."], ["Pott recovered, and they went to breakfast.", "마침내 그 가엾은 사내가 겁먹고 핀잔을 들으며 제자리로 눌려 내려간 뒤에야 포트 부인은 회복되었고, 그들은 조찬을 들러 갔다."]], [["‘You will not allow this base newspaper slander to shorten your stay here, Mr.", "\"이 비열한 신문 중상 때문에 이곳 체류를 줄이지는 않으실 거지요, 윈클 씨?\""], ["Winkle?’ said Mrs. Pott, smiling through the traces of her tears.", "눈물 자국 사이로 미소 지으며 포트 부인이 말했다."]], [["‘I hope not,’ said Mr.", "\"그러지 않으시길 바라오.\""], ["Pott, actuated, as he spoke, by a wish that his visitor would choke himself with the morsel of dry toast which he was raising to his lips at the moment, and so terminate his stay effectually.", "포트 씨가 말했는데, 말하는 그 순간 그의 마음은, 방문객이 마침 입가로 들어 올리던 마른 토스트 조각에 목이 막혀 그렇게 체류를 확실히 끝내 버렸으면 하는 바람에 사로잡혀 있었다. \"그러지 않으시길 바라오.\""]], [["‘I hope not.’\n‘You are very good,’ said Mr. Winkle; ‘but a letter has been received from Mr.", "\"친절하시군요.\""], ["Pickwick--so I learn by a note from Mr.", "윈클 씨가 말했다."], ["Tupman, which was brought up to my bedroom door, this morning--in which he requests us to join him at Bury to-day; and we are to leave by the coach at noon.’", "\"하지만 픽윅 씨로부터 편지가 한 통 왔습니다—오늘 아침 침실 문 앞으로 전해진 터프먼 씨의 쪽지로 알게 된 일인데—그분이 오늘 베리에서 합류하자고 청하셨더군요. 정오에 마차로 떠나야 합니다.\""]], [["‘But you will come back?’ said Mrs.", "\"하지만 돌아오시겠지요?\""], ["Pott.", "포트 부인이 말했다."]], [["‘Oh, certainly,’ replied Mr.", "\"오, 물론이지요.\""], ["Winkle.", "윈클 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘You are quite sure?’ said Mrs.", "\"정말 확실하지요?\""], ["Pott, stealing a tender look at her visitor.", "포트 부인이 방문객에게 다정한 눈길을 슬쩍 던지며 말했다."]], [["‘Quite,’ responded Mr.", "\"확실합니다.\""], ["Winkle.", "윈클 씨가 대답했다."]], [["The breakfast passed off in silence, for each of the party was brooding over his, or her, own personal grievances. Mrs.", "조찬은 침묵 속에 지나갔으니, 일행 저마다 자신의 개인적인 불만을 곱씹고 있었기 때문이다."], ["Pott was regretting the loss of a beau; Mr. Pott his rash pledge to horsewhip the Independent; Mr.", "포트 부인은 멋진 사내를 잃는 것을 애석해했고, 포트 씨는 인디펜던트를 채찍질하겠다는 경솔한 맹세를, 윈클 씨는 자신이 무고하게 이토록 거북한 처지에 놓인 것을 한탄했다."], ["Winkle his having innocently placed himself in so awkward a situation. Noon approached, and after many adieux and promises to return, he tore himself away.", "정오가 다가왔고, 숱한 작별 인사와 돌아오겠다는 약속을 남긴 뒤, 그는 가까스로 몸을 떼어 떠났다."]], [["‘If he ever comes back, I’ll poison him,’ thought Mr.", "'저자가 다시 돌아오기만 하면, 독을 먹여 버리겠어.'"], ["Pott, as he turned into the little back office where he prepared his thunderbolts.", "포트 씨는 자신의 벼락을 벼리는 자그마한 뒷사무실로 들어서며 생각했다."]], [["‘If I ever do come back, and mix myself up with these people again,’ thought Mr.", "'내가 다시 돌아와서 이 사람들과 또 얽히게 된다면,'"], ["Winkle, as he wended his way to the Peacock, ‘I shall deserve to be horsewhipped myself--that’s all.’", "윈클 씨는 피콕 여관으로 발걸음을 옮기며 생각했다. '나야말로 채찍질당해 마땅하지—그뿐이야.'"]], [["His friends were ready, the coach was nearly so, and in half an hour they were proceeding on their journey, along the road over which Mr.", "그의 친구들은 채비를 마쳤고, 마차도 거의 채비가 되어 있었으며, 반 시간 뒤 그들은 길을 따라 여정에 올랐으니, 그 길은 픽윅 씨와 샘이 얼마 전에 지나온 바로 그 길이었다."], ["Pickwick and Sam had so recently travelled, and of which, as we have already said something, we do not feel called upon to extract Mr. Snodgrass’s poetical and beautiful description.", "그 길에 대해서는 이미 어느 정도 이야기한 바 있으므로, 스노드그래스 씨의 시적이고 아름다운 묘사를 굳이 옮겨야 한다고는 느끼지 않는다."]], [["Mr. Weller was standing at the door of the Angel, ready to receive them, and by that gentleman they were ushered to the apartment of Mr. Pickwick, where, to the no small surprise of Mr. Winkle and Mr. Snodgrass, and the no small embarrassment of Mr. Tupman, they found old Wardle and Trundle.", "웰러 씨가 엔젤 여관 문간에 서서 그들을 맞을 채비를 하고 있었고, 그 신사의 안내로 그들은 픽윅 씨의 방으로 들어갔는데, 거기서 윈클 씨와 스노드그래스 씨는 적잖이 놀랐고 터프먼 씨는 적잖이 당황했으니, 늙은 와들과 트런들이 와 있었던 것이다."]], [["‘How are you?’ said the old man, grasping Mr.", "\"잘들 지냈는가?\" 노인이 터프먼 씨의 손을 잡으며 말했다."], ["Tupman’s hand. ‘Don’t hang back, or look sentimental about it; it can’t be helped, old fellow.", "\"뒤로 빼지도 말고, 그 일로 감상에 젖지도 말게."], ["For her sake, I wish you’d had her; for your own, I’m very glad you have not.", "어쩔 수 없는 일이었네, 이 사람아. 그녀를 위해서는 자네가 그녀를 얻었더라면 싶지만, 자네 자신을 위해서는 그러지 않은 게 천만다행일세."], ["A young fellow like you will do better one of these days, eh?’ With this conclusion, Wardle slapped Mr.", "자네 같은 젊은이는 머잖아 더 나은 사람을 만날 게야, 안 그런가?\""], ["Tupman on the back, and laughed heartily.", "이렇게 말을 맺으며 와들은 터프먼 씨의 등을 철썩 치고 호탕하게 웃었다."]], [["‘Well, and how are you, my fine fellows?’ said the old gentleman, shaking hands with Mr.", "\"그래, 자네들은 어떤가, 멋진 친구들?\""], ["Winkle and Mr. Snodgrass at the same time.", "노신사가 윈클 씨와 스노드그래스 씨와 동시에 악수를 나누며 말했다."], ["‘I have just been telling Pickwick that we must have you all down at Christmas.", "\"방금 픽윅한테 말하던 참인데, 크리스마스에는 자네들 모두 우리 집에 내려와야 하네."], ["We’re going to have a wedding--a real wedding this time.’", "결혼식이 있을 거야—이번에는 진짜 결혼식이지.\""]], [["‘A wedding!’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"결혼식이라뇨!\""], ["Snodgrass, turning very pale.", "스노드그래스 씨가 새파랗게 질려 외쳤다."]], [["‘Yes, a wedding.", "\"그래, 결혼식. 하지만 놀라지 말게.\""], ["But don’t be frightened,’ said the good-humoured old man; ‘it’s only Trundle there, and Bella.’", "마음씨 좋은 노인이 말했다. \"저기 저 트런들과 벨라뿐이니까.\""]], [["‘Oh, is that all?’ said Mr.", "\"오, 그게 다입니까?\""], ["Snodgrass, relieved from a painful doubt which had fallen heavily on his breast.", "스노드그래스 씨가, 그의 가슴을 무겁게 짓누르던 괴로운 의심에서 벗어나 말했다."], ["‘Give you joy, Sir.", "\"축하드립니다."], ["How is Joe?’", "조는 어떻습니까?\""]], [["‘Very well,’ replied the old gentleman.", "\"아주 잘 지내네.\" 노신사가 대답했다."], ["‘Sleepy as ever.’", "\"여전히 잠만 자지.\""]], [["‘And your mother, and the clergyman, and all of ‘em?’", "\"어머님과 목사님, 그리고 다들 어떠십니까?\""]], [["‘Quite well.’", "\"다들 잘 지내네.\""]], [["‘Where,’ said Mr.", "\"그,\""], ["Tupman, with an effort--‘where is--she, Sir?’ and he turned away his head, and covered his eyes with his hand.", "터프먼 씨가 안간힘을 쓰며 말했다—\"그분은 어디 계십니까, 어르신?\" 그러고는 고개를 돌리며 손으로 두 눈을 가렸다."]], [["‘She!’ said the old gentleman, with a knowing shake of the head.", "\"그분이라!\" 노신사가 무언가 알겠다는 듯 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 말했다."], ["‘Do you mean my single relative--eh?’", "\"내 결혼 안 한 친척을 말하는 건가—응?\""]], [["Mr. Tupman, by a nod, intimated that his question applied to the disappointed Rachael.", "터프먼 씨는 고갯짓으로, 자신의 물음이 실연당한 레이철에게 향한 것임을 알렸다."]], [["‘Oh, she’s gone away,’ said the old gentleman.", "\"오, 그 사람은 떠났네.\" 노신사가 말했다."], ["‘She’s living at a relation’s, far enough off.", "\"멀찍이 떨어진 친척 집에서 살고 있지."], ["She couldn’t bear to see the girls, so I let her go.", "처녀들 보는 걸 견디지 못하길래 내가 보내 줬네."], ["But come! Here’s the dinner.", "하지만 자, 저녁 식사가 나왔구먼."], ["You must be hungry after your ride.", "마차를 타고 왔으니 다들 시장할 게야."], ["I am, without any ride at all; so let us fall to.’", "나는 마차를 타지 않고도 시장하니, 어서들 들세나.\""]], [["Ample justice was done to the meal; and when they were seated round the table, after it had been disposed of, Mr. Pickwick, to the intense horror and indignation of his followers, related the adventure he had undergone, and the success which had attended the base artifices of the diabolical Jingle.", "식사는 충분히 대접받았고, 식사가 치워진 뒤 그들이 탁자에 둘러앉자, 픽윅 씨는 추종자들이 격렬한 공포와 분노에 휩싸이는 가운데, 자신이 겪은 모험과 그 악마 같은 징글의 비열한 술책이 거둔 성공을 이야기했다."]], [["‘And the attack of rheumatism which I caught in that garden,’ said Mr.", "\"그리고 그 정원에서 걸린 류머티즘 발작으로,\" 픽윅 씨가 끝맺으며 말했다."], ["Pickwick, in conclusion, ‘renders me lame at this moment.’", "\"지금 이 순간에도 다리를 절고 있다네.\""]], [["‘I, too, have had something of an adventure,’ said Mr.", "\"저도 모험이라 할 만한 일을 좀 겪었습니다.\""], ["Winkle, with a smile; and, at the request of Mr.", "윈클 씨가 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["Pickwick, he detailed the malicious libel of the Eatanswill Independent, and the consequent excitement of their friend, the editor.", "그러고는 픽윅 씨의 청에 따라, 이턴스윌 인디펜던트의 악의에 찬 명예 훼손과 그로 인한 그들의 친구, 그 편집장의 흥분을 자세히 들려주었다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick’s brow darkened during the recital.", "이야기가 진행되는 동안 픽윅 씨의 이마가 어두워졌다."], ["His friends observed it, and, when Mr. Winkle had concluded, maintained a profound silence.", "그의 친구들도 그것을 알아채고, 윈클 씨가 이야기를 마치자 깊은 침묵을 지켰다."], ["Mr. Pickwick struck the table emphatically with his clenched fist, and spoke as follows:--", "픽윅 씨는 꽉 쥔 주먹으로 탁자를 힘주어 내리치고는 다음과 같이 말했다—"]], [["‘Is it not a wonderful circumstance,’ said Mr.", "\"이 어찌 놀라운 노릇이 아닌가.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick, ‘that we seem destined to enter no man’s house without involving him in some degree of trouble?", "\"우리는 어느 집에 들든 그 주인을 얼마간 곤경에 끌어들이지 않고는 못 배기는 운명을 타고난 듯하니 말일세."], ["Does it not, I ask, bespeak the indiscretion, or, worse than that, the blackness of heart--that I should say so!--of my followers, that, beneath whatever roof they locate, they disturb the peace of mind and happiness of some confiding female?", "묻건대, 내 추종자들이 어떤 지붕 아래 머무르든 어느 순진한 여인의 마음의 평화와 행복을 어지럽히고 마는 것은, 분별없음을, 아니 그보다 더 나쁘게는—내가 이런 말을 하다니!—마음의 시커멈을 드러내는 것이 아닌가?"], ["Is it not, I say--’", "이것이야말로—\""]], [["Mr. Pickwick would in all probability have gone on for some time, had not the entrance of Sam, with a letter, caused him to break off in his eloquent discourse.", "픽윅 씨는 십중팔구 한참을 더 늘어놓았을 터인데, 샘이 편지 한 통을 들고 들어서는 바람에 그 웅변적인 일장 연설을 중단하게 되었다."], ["He passed his handkerchief across his forehead, took off his spectacles, wiped them, and put them on again; and his voice had recovered its wonted softness of tone when he said--", "그는 손수건으로 이마를 닦고, 안경을 벗어 닦은 뒤 다시 쓰고는, 여느 때의 부드러운 어조를 되찾은 목소리로 말했다—"]], [["‘What have you there, Sam?’", "\"거기 든 게 무엇인가, 샘?\""]], [["‘Called at the post-office just now, and found this here letter, as has laid there for two days,’ replied Mr.", "\"방금 우체국에 들렀더니 이 편지가 이틀이나 놓여 있더군요.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다."], ["Weller. ‘It’s sealed vith a vafer, and directed in round hand.’", "\"봉랍 대신 풀로 봉해져 있고, 둥근 글씨로 수신인이 적혀 있습니다.\""]], [["‘I don’t know this hand,’ said Mr.", "\"이 글씨는 모르겠는데.\""], ["Pickwick, opening the letter.", "픽윅 씨가 편지를 열며 말했다."], ["‘Mercy on us!", "\"맙소사!"], ["what’s this?", "이게 무슨 일인가?"], ["It must be a jest; it--it--can’t be true.’", "농담이렷다. 이건—이건—사실일 리가 없어.\""]], [["‘What’s the matter?’ was the general inquiry.", "\"무슨 일입니까?\" 모두가 물었다."]], [["‘Nobody dead, is there?’ said Wardle, alarmed at the horror in Mr.", "\"누가 죽기라도 한 건 아니지요?\""], ["Pickwick’s countenance.", "픽윅 씨의 얼굴에 떠오른 공포에 놀란 와들이 말했다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick made no reply, but, pushing the letter across the table, and desiring Mr. Tupman to read it aloud, fell back in his chair with a look of vacant astonishment quite alarming to behold.", "픽윅 씨는 아무 대답도 하지 않고, 탁자 너머로 편지를 밀어 놓으며 터프먼 씨에게 큰 소리로 읽어 달라고 청하고는, 보기에도 섬뜩할 만큼 멍하니 놀란 표정으로 의자에 털썩 기댔다."]], [["Mr. Tupman, with a trembling voice, read the letter, of which the following is a copy:--", "터프먼 씨가 떨리는 목소리로 편지를 읽었으니, 그 사본은 다음과 같다—"]], [["Freeman’s Court, Cornhill, August 28th, 1827.", "콘힐, 프리먼스 코트, 1827년 8월 28일."]], [["Bardell against Pickwick.", "바델 대 픽윅 건. 귀하께,"]], [["Sir,\nHaving been instructed by Mrs.", "저희는 마사 바델 부인으로부터 혼인 약속 위반에 대하여 귀하를 상대로 소송을 제기하라는 위임을 받았으며, 원고는 그 손해액을 천오백 파운드로 산정하고 있습니다."], ["Martha Bardell to commence an action against you for a breach of promise of marriage, for which the plaintiff lays her damages at fifteen hundred pounds, we beg to inform you that a writ has been issued against you in this suit in the Court of Common Pleas; and request to know, by return of post, the name of your attorney in London, who will accept service thereof.", "이에 본 소송에 관하여 민사 법원에서 귀하를 상대로 영장이 발부되었음을 알려 드리며, 그 송달을 접수할 런던 측 귀하의 대리인 이름을 회신 편으로 알려 주시기를 청하는 바입니다."]], [["We are, Sir, Your obedient servants, Dodson & Fogg.", "귀하의 충실한 종복, 도드슨 앤드 포그 올림."]], [["Mr. Samuel Pickwick.", "새뮤얼 픽윅 귀하."]], [["There was something so impressive in the mute astonishment with which each man regarded his neighbour, and every man regarded Mr. Pickwick, that all seemed afraid to speak.", "저마다 옆 사람을 바라보고, 또 모두가 픽윅 씨를 바라보던 그 말없는 경악에는 어딘가 깊은 인상을 주는 데가 있어, 다들 입을 떼기를 두려워하는 듯했다."], ["The silence was at length broken by Mr. Tupman.", "마침내 그 침묵을 깬 것은 터프먼 씨였다."]], [["‘Dodson and Fogg,’ he repeated mechanically.", "\"도드슨 앤드 포그.\" 그가 기계적으로 되뇌었다."]], [["‘Bardell and Pickwick,’ said Mr.", "\"바델 대 픽윅.\""], ["Snodgrass, musing.", "스노드그래스 씨가 골똘히 생각하며 말했다."]], [["‘Peace of mind and happiness of confiding females,’ murmured Mr.", "\"순진한 여인의 마음의 평화와 행복.\""], ["Winkle, with an air of abstraction.", "윈클 씨가 넋이 나간 듯 중얼거렸다."]], [["‘It’s a conspiracy,’ said Mr.", "\"음모일세.\""], ["Pickwick, at length recovering the power of speech; ‘a base conspiracy between these two grasping attorneys, Dodson and Fogg.", "마침내 말할 기운을 되찾은 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"탐욕스러운 저 두 변호사, 도드슨과 포그가 꾸민 비열한 음모야."], ["Mrs. Bardell would never do it;--she hasn’t the heart to do it;--she hasn’t the case to do it.", "바델 부인은 결코 이런 짓을 할 리가 없네—그럴 마음도 없고—그럴 만한 사정도 없어."], ["Ridiculous--ridiculous.’", "우습군—우스워.\""]], [["Of her heart,’ said Wardle, with a smile, ‘you should certainly be the best judge.", "\"그녀의 마음에 대해서야,\" 와들이 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["I don’t wish to discourage you, but I should certainly say that, of her case, Dodson and Fogg are far better judges than any of us can be.’", "\"분명 자네가 가장 잘 아는 판관이겠지. 자네 기를 꺾고 싶지는 않네만, 그녀의 사정에 대해서라면, 도드슨과 포그가 우리 누구보다도 훨씬 더 잘 아는 판관이라고 분명히 말할 수 있겠네.\""]], [["‘It’s a vile attempt to extort money,’ said Mr.", "\"돈을 뜯어내려는 비열한 수작일세.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘I hope it is,’ said Wardle, with a short, dry cough.", "\"그러길 바라네.\" 와들이 짧고 메마른 기침을 하며 말했다."]], [["‘Who ever heard me address her in any way but that in which a lodger would address his landlady?’ continued Mr.", "\"하숙인이 집주인 여자에게 건넬 법한 말 말고, 내가 그녀에게 다른 식으로 말하는 걸 들은 사람이 누가 있단 말인가?\""], ["Pickwick, with great vehemence.", "픽윅 씨가 무서운 기세로 말을 이었다."], ["‘Who ever saw me with her?", "\"내가 그녀와 함께 있는 걸 본 사람이 누가 있나?"], ["Not even my friends here--’", "여기 내 친구들조차도—\""]], [["‘Except on one occasion,’ said Mr.", "\"한 번 빼고요.\""], ["Tupman.", "터프먼 씨가 말했다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick changed colour.", "픽윅 씨의 얼굴빛이 변했다."]], [["‘Ah,’ said Mr.", "\"아하,\""], ["Wardle. ‘Well, that’s important.", "와들 씨가 말했다."], ["There was nothing suspicious then, I suppose?’", "\"음, 그건 중요하군. 그때 의심스러운 점은 없었겠지?\""]], [["Mr.", "터프먼 씨가 겁먹은 눈으로 자신의 지도자를 슬쩍 보았다."], ["Tupman glanced timidly at his leader.", "\"글쎄요,\" 그가 말했다."], ["‘Why,’ said he, ‘there was nothing suspicious; but--I don’t know how it happened, mind--she certainly was reclining in his arms.’", "\"의심스러운 점은 없었습니다. 하지만—어쩌다 그렇게 됐는지는 모르겠는데, 아셔야 할 게—그분이 분명 그녀를 품에 안고 있었습니다.\""]], [["‘Gracious powers!’ ejaculated Mr.", "\"맙소사!\""], ["Pickwick, as the recollection of the scene in question struck forcibly upon him; ‘what a dreadful instance of the force of circumstances!", "픽윅 씨가 문제의 그 장면이 또렷이 떠오르자 부르짖었다. \"정황의 위력이 빚어낸 이 무서운 본보기라니!"], ["So she was--so she was.’", "그래, 그랬지—그랬어.\""]], [["‘And our friend was soothing her anguish,’ said Mr.", "\"그리고 우리의 친구께서는 그녀의 고뇌를 달래 주고 계셨고요.\""], ["Winkle, rather maliciously.", "윈클 씨가 다소 짓궂게 말했다."]], [["‘So I was,’ said Mr.", "\"그래, 그랬네.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["‘I don’t deny it.", "\"부인하지 않겠네."], ["So I was.’", "그래, 그랬어.\""]], [["‘Hollo!’ said Wardle; ‘for a case in which there’s nothing suspicious, this looks rather queer--eh, Pickwick? Ah, sly dog--sly dog!’ and he laughed till the glasses on the sideboard rang again.", "\"이런!\" 와들이 말했다. \"의심스러운 점이라곤 없는 사건치고는, 이거 영 수상쩍어 보이는데—안 그런가, 픽윅? 아하, 능구렁이 같으니—능구렁이!\" 그러고는 식기대 위의 잔들이 다시 울리도록 한바탕 웃어 댔다."]], [["‘What a dreadful conjunction of appearances!’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"이 무슨 끔찍한 정황의 결합이란 말인가!\""], ["Pickwick, resting his chin upon his hands.", "픽윅 씨가 두 손에 턱을 괴며 외쳤다."], ["‘Winkle--Tupman--I beg your pardon for the observations I made just now.", "\"윈클—터프먼—방금 내가 한 말은 사과하겠네."], ["We are all the victims of circumstances, and I the greatest.’ With this apology Mr.", "우리는 모두 정황의 희생양이고, 그중에서도 내가 가장 큰 희생양일세.\""], ["Pickwick buried his head in his hands, and ruminated; while Wardle measured out a regular circle of nods and winks, addressed to the other members of the company.", "이렇게 사과하고 픽윅 씨는 두 손에 얼굴을 묻고 깊은 생각에 잠겼으며, 와들은 좌중의 다른 회원들을 향해 고갯짓과 윙크를 한 바퀴 빙 돌려 보냈다."]], [["‘I’ll have it explained, though,’ said Mr.", "\"하지만 기어이 해명을 듣고야 말겠네.\""], ["Pickwick, raising his head and hammering the table.", "픽윅 씨가 고개를 들고 탁자를 두드리며 말했다."], ["‘I’ll see this Dodson and Fogg!", "\"이 도드슨인지 포그인지를 만나 보겠어!"], ["I’ll go to London to-morrow.’", "내일 런던으로 가겠네.\""]], [["‘Not to-morrow,’ said Wardle; ‘you’re too lame.’", "\"내일은 안 되네.\" 와들이 말했다. \"다리를 너무 절잖나.\""]], [["‘Well, then, next day.’", "\"그럼, 그다음 날.\""]], [["‘Next day is the first of September, and you’re pledged to ride out with us, as far as Sir Geoffrey Manning’s grounds at all events, and to meet us at lunch, if you don’t take the field.’", "\"그다음 날은 9월 1일이고, 자네는 우리와 함께 적어도 제프리 매닝 경의 영지까지는 나가기로 약속했네. 사냥에 나서지 않더라도 점심때 우리와 만나기로 했고.\""]], [["‘Well, then, the day after,’ said Mr.", "\"그럼, 그 다음다음 날.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick; ‘Thursday.--Sam!’", "\"목요일에.—샘!\""]], [["‘Sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"네, 어르신.\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘Take two places outside to London, on Thursday morning, for yourself and me.’", "\"목요일 아침에 런던행 마차 바깥 자리 두 개를 잡아 주게, 자네와 나 몫으로.\""]], [["‘Wery well, Sir.’", "\"잘 알겠습니다, 어르신.\""]], [["Mr. Weller left the room, and departed slowly on his errand, with his hands in his pocket and his eyes fixed on the ground.", "웰러 씨는 방을 나가, 두 손을 주머니에 찌르고 두 눈을 땅에 박은 채, 느릿느릿 심부름을 하러 떠났다."]], [["‘Rum feller, the hemperor,’ said Mr.", "\"별난 양반이라니까, 우리 황제 폐하께서는.\""], ["Weller, as he walked slowly up the street.", "웰러 씨가 거리를 천천히 걸어 올라가며 말했다."], ["‘Think o’ his makin’ up to that ‘ere Mrs. Bardell--vith a little boy, too!", "\"저 바델 부인한테 수작을 걸다니—게다가 어린 사내애까지 딸린 여자한테 말이야!"], ["Always the vay vith these here old ‘uns howsoever, as is such steady goers to look at.", "하지만 늘 이런 식이지, 보기엔 점잖고 진중한 이런 늙은 양반들이 말이야."], ["I didn’t think he’d ha’ done it, though--I didn’t think he’d ha’ done it!’ Moralising in this strain, Mr.", "그래도 그분이 그런 짓을 했을 줄은 몰랐는데—정말 그랬을 줄은 몰랐어!\""], ["Samuel Weller bent his steps towards the booking-office.", "이런 투로 도덕적 사색을 펼치며, 새뮤얼 웰러 씨는 마차 예약소를 향해 발걸음을 옮겼다."]], [["# CHAPTER XIX. A PLEASANT DAY WITH AN UNPLEASANT TERMINATION", "# CHAPTER XIX. A PLEASANT DAY WITH AN UNPLEASANT TERMINATION"]], [["The birds, who, happily for their own peace of mind and personal comfort, were in blissful ignorance of the preparations which had been making to astonish them, on the first of September, hailed it, no doubt, as one of the pleasantest mornings they had seen that season.", "새들은 — 다행히도 제 마음의 평화와 몸의 안락을 위하여 — 자기들을 놀라게 하려고 마련된 채비를 까맣게 모르는 복된 무지 속에서, 9월 첫날을 분명 그 계절에 보아 온 가장 상쾌한 아침의 하나로 반겼을 것이다."], ["Many a young partridge who strutted complacently among the stubble, with all the finicking coxcombry of youth, and many an older one who watched his levity out of his little round eye, with the contemptuous air of a bird of wisdom and experience, alike unconscious of their approaching doom, basked in the fresh morning air with lively and blithesome feelings, and a few hours afterwards were laid low upon the earth.", "그루터기 사이를 젊음의 온갖 까다로운 멋부림으로 우쭐대며 거닐던 어린 자고새도 많았고, 그 경박함을 지혜와 경험을 갖춘 새다운 경멸의 태도로 작고 동그란 눈으로 지켜보던 나이 든 자고새도 많았으나, 다 같이 다가오는 운명을 모른 채 발랄하고 명랑한 기분으로 싱그러운 아침 공기 속에 몸을 쬐다가, 몇 시간 뒤에는 땅바닥에 쓰러져 버렸다. 그러나 우리가 너무 감상에 젖고 있다."], ["But we grow affecting: let us proceed.", "이야기를 계속하자."]], [["In plain commonplace matter-of-fact, then, it was a fine morning--so fine that you would scarcely have believed that the few months of an English summer had yet flown by.", "그렇다면 평범하고 꾸밈없는 사실대로 말하건대, 화창한 아침이었다 — 어찌나 화창한지 영국 여름의 그 몇 달이 벌써 지나갔다고는 도저히 믿기 어려울 정도였다."], ["Hedges, fields, and trees, hill and moorland, presented to the eye their ever-varying shades of deep rich green; scarce a leaf had fallen, scarce a sprinkle of yellow mingled with the hues of summer, warned you that autumn had begun.", "산울타리며 들판이며 나무며, 언덕과 황야가, 짙고 그윽한 초록의 끊임없이 변하는 색조를 눈앞에 펼쳐 보였다. 떨어진 잎은 거의 없었고, 여름의 빛깔에 섞여 가을이 시작되었음을 알려 줄 노란 기운도 거의 흩뿌려져 있지 않았다."], ["The sky was cloudless; the sun shone out bright and warm; the songs of birds, the hum of myriads of summer insects, filled the air; and the cottage gardens, crowded with flowers of every rich and beautiful tint, sparkled, in the heavy dew, like beds of glittering jewels.", "하늘은 구름 한 점 없었고, 해는 밝고 따뜻하게 빛났다. 새들의 노래와 무수한 여름 벌레들의 윙윙거림이 공기를 가득 채웠고, 온갖 그윽하고 아름다운 빛깔의 꽃들로 빼곡한 오두막 정원들은 짙은 이슬 속에서 반짝이는 보석을 깔아 놓은 화단처럼 반짝였다."], ["Everything bore the stamp of summer, and none of its beautiful colour had yet faded from the die.", "모든 것이 여름의 도장을 찍고 있었고, 그 아름다운 빛깔 가운데 어느 하나도 아직 그 색판에서 바래지 않았다."]], [["Such was the morning, when an open carriage, in which were three Pickwickians (Mr. Snodgrass having preferred to remain at home), Mr.", "이런 아침에, 픽윅 회원 셋(스노드그래스 씨는 집에 남기를 택했다)과 워들 씨와 트런들 씨가 탄 무개 마차가, 마부 옆 좌석에 샘 웰러를 앉히고서, 길가의 문 옆에 멈춰 섰다."], ["Wardle, and Mr. Trundle, with Sam Weller on the box beside the driver, pulled up by a gate at the roadside, before which stood a tall, raw- boned gamekeeper, and a half-booted, leather-legginged boy, each bearing a bag of capacious dimensions, and accompanied by a brace of pointers.", "그 문 앞에는 키 크고 뼈만 앙상한 사냥터지기 한 명과, 반장화에 가죽 각반을 두른 소년 하나가 서 있었는데, 둘 다 넉넉한 크기의 자루를 하나씩 메고 포인터 두 마리를 데리고 있었다."]], [["‘I say,’ whispered Mr.", "\"이봐요,\""], ["Winkle to Wardle, as the man let down the steps, ‘they don’t suppose we’re going to kill game enough to fill those bags, do they?’", "사내가 발판을 내리자 윈클 씨가 워들에게 속삭였다. \"설마 우리가 저 자루를 채울 만큼 사냥감을 잡으리라고 생각하는 건 아니겠죠?\""]], [["‘Fill them!’ exclaimed old Wardle.", "\"채운다고!\" 워들 영감이 외쳤다."], ["‘Bless you, yes!", "\"원, 그럼 채우고말고!"], ["You shall fill one, and I the other; and when we’ve done with them, the pockets of our shooting-jackets will hold as much more.’", "자네가 하나 채우고, 내가 다른 하나를 채울 걸세. 그러고도 다 채우고 나면 우리 사냥 재킷 주머니에도 그만큼 더 들어갈 테고.\""]], [["Mr. Winkle dismounted without saying anything in reply to this observation; but he thought within himself, that if the party remained in the open air, till he had filled one of the bags, they stood a considerable chance of catching colds in their heads.", "윈클 씨는 이 말에 아무 대꾸도 하지 않고 마차에서 내렸으나, 속으로는 만일 일행이 자기가 자루 하나를 채울 때까지 바깥 공기 속에 머물러 있어야 한다면 머리에 고뿔이 들 가능성이 상당하리라고 생각했다."]], [["‘Hi, Juno, lass-hi, old girl; down, Daph, down,’ said Wardle, caressing the dogs.", "\"이리 와, 주노, 아가야 — 이리 와, 늙은 것아. 엎드려, 다프, 엎드려.\""], ["‘Sir Geoffrey still in Scotland, of course, Martin?’", "워들이 개들을 어루만지며 말했다. \"제프리 경께서는 아직 스코틀랜드에 계시겠지, 마틴?\""]], [["The tall gamekeeper replied in the affirmative, and looked with some surprise from Mr. Winkle, who was holding his gun as if he wished his coat pocket to save him the trouble of pulling the trigger, to Mr. Tupman, who was holding his as if he was afraid of it--as there is no earthly reason to doubt he really was.", "키 큰 사냥터지기는 그렇다고 대답하면서, 마치 코트 주머니가 방아쇠 당기는 수고를 덜어 주기를 바라기라도 하는 듯 총을 쥐고 있는 윈클 씨로부터, 마치 총을 무서워하는 듯이 — 정말로 그랬다는 데 의심할 여지가 조금도 없거니와 — 총을 쥐고 있는 터프먼 씨에게로, 다소 놀란 듯 눈길을 옮겼다."]], [["‘My friends are not much in the way of this sort of thing yet, Martin,’ said Wardle, noticing the look.", "\"내 친구들은 아직 이런 일에 익숙지가 않다네, 마틴.\" 워들이 그 눈길을 알아채고 말했다."], ["‘Live and learn, you know.", "\"살아가며 배우는 거지, 안 그런가."], ["They’ll be good shots one of these days.", "머지않아 명사수가 될 걸세."], ["I beg my friend Winkle’s pardon, though; he has had some practice.’", "그래도 내 친구 윈클에게는 미안하군. 이 사람은 연습을 좀 했거든.\""]], [["Mr. Winkle smiled feebly over his blue neckerchief in acknowledgment of the compliment, and got himself so mysteriously entangled with his gun, in his modest confusion, that if the piece had been loaded, he must inevitably have shot himself dead upon the spot.", "윈클 씨는 그 칭찬을 받아들인다는 뜻으로 파란 목도리 너머로 힘없이 미소 지었는데, 겸손한 당혹감에 어찌나 알 수 없이 총과 뒤엉켜 버렸던지, 만일 총에 탄알이 장전되어 있었더라면 그 자리에서 자기 몸을 쏘아 즉사하고 말았을 것임에 틀림없었다."]], [["‘You mustn’t handle your piece in that ‘ere way, when you come to have the charge in it, Sir,’ said the tall gamekeeper gruffly; ‘or I’m damned if you won’t make cold meat of some on us.’", "\"총알을 재고 나면 총을 그딴 식으로 다루시면 안 됩니다요, 나리.\" 키 큰 사냥터지기가 퉁명스레 말했다. \"안 그러면 영락없이 우리 중 누구 하나를 싸늘한 고깃덩이로 만들어 놓고 마실 게요.\""]], [["Mr. Winkle, thus admonished, abruptly altered his position, and in so doing, contrived to bring the barrel into pretty smart contact with Mr. Weller’s head.", "이렇게 주의를 받은 윈클 씨는 황급히 자세를 바꾸었는데, 그러는 와중에 총신을 웰러 씨의 머리에 제법 세게 부딪치고 말았다."]], [["‘Hollo!’ said Sam, picking up his hat, which had been knocked off, and rubbing his temple.", "\"이런!\" 샘이 떨어진 모자를 주워 들고 관자놀이를 문지르며 말했다."], ["‘Hollo, sir!", "\"이런, 나리!"], ["if you comes it this vay, you’ll fill one o’ them bags, and something to spare, at one fire.’", "이런 식으로 가시다간 한 발에 저 자루 중 하나는 채우고도 남겠습니다요.\""]], [["Here the leather-legginged boy laughed very heartily, and then tried to look as if it was somebody else, whereat Mr. Winkle frowned majestically.", "이 말에 가죽 각반을 두른 소년이 아주 진심으로 웃어 댔다가는, 마치 웃은 게 딴 사람인 양 시치미를 떼려 애썼고, 그 바람에 윈클 씨가 위엄 있게 눈살을 찌푸렸다."]], [["‘Where did you tell the boy to meet us with the snack, Martin?’ inquired Wardle.", "\"그 소년더러 어디로 새참을 가지고 우리한테 오라고 일렀나, 마틴?\" 워들이 물었다."]], [["‘Side of One-tree Hill, at twelve o’clock, Sir.’", "\"외나무 언덕 기슭으로, 열두 시에 오라 했습니다요, 나리.\""]], [["‘That’s not Sir Geoffrey’s land, is it?’", "\"거긴 제프리 경의 땅은 아니지, 그렇지?\""]], [["‘No, Sir; but it’s close by it.", "\"네, 나리. 하지만 바로 옆이지요."], ["It’s Captain Boldwig’s land; but there’ll be nobody to interrupt us, and there’s a fine bit of turf there.’", "볼드위그 대위의 땅인데, 거기선 우릴 방해할 사람도 없고, 잔디밭도 썩 좋습니다요.\""]], [["‘Very well,’ said old Wardle.", "\"좋아.\" 워들 영감이 말했다."], ["‘Now the sooner we’re off the better.", "\"자, 이제 빨리 떠날수록 좋겠군."], ["Will you join us at twelve, then, Pickwick?’", "그럼 열두 시에 우리한테 합류하겠나, 픽윅?\""]], [["Mr. Pickwick was particularly desirous to view the sport, the more especially as he was rather anxious in respect of Mr.", "픽윅 씨는 그 사냥을 구경하고 싶은 마음이 각별했는데, 특히나 윈클 씨의 목숨과 팔다리가 다소 걱정스러웠기 때문이다."], ["Winkle’s life and limbs. On so inviting a morning, too, it was very tantalising to turn back, and leave his friends to enjoy themselves.", "게다가 이토록 마음을 끄는 아침에 돌아서서 친구들이 즐기도록 내버려 두는 것은 무척이나 애가 타는 일이었다."], ["It was, therefore, with a very rueful air that he replied--", "그래서 그는 몹시 서글픈 표정으로 대답했다."]], [["‘Why, I suppose I must.’", "\"글쎄, 그래야겠지요.\""]], [["‘Ain’t the gentleman a shot, Sir?’ inquired the long gamekeeper.", "\"저 신사분은 사냥꾼이 아니십니까요, 나리?\" 키 큰 사냥터지기가 물었다."]], [["‘No,’ replied Wardle; ‘and he’s lame besides.’", "\"아닐세.\" 워들이 대답했다. \"게다가 다리도 절고.\""]], [["‘I should very much like to go,’ said Mr.", "\"나도 정말 가고 싶은데요.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick--‘very much.’", "\"정말이지.\""]], [["There was a short pause of commiseration.", "잠시 동정 어린 침묵이 흘렀다."]], [["‘There’s a barrow t’other side the hedge,’ said the boy.", "\"산울타리 저편에 외바퀴 손수레가 하나 있습니다요.\" 소년이 말했다."], ["‘If the gentleman’s servant would wheel along the paths, he could keep nigh us, and we could lift it over the stiles, and that.’", "\"저 신사분의 하인이 길을 따라 그걸 밀고 오면, 우리 가까이 따라올 수 있고요, 층계 디딤대 같은 데선 우리가 들어 넘겨 드리면 됩지요.\""]], [["‘The wery thing,’ said Mr.", "\"바로 그거다.\""], ["Weller, who was a party interested, inasmuch as he ardently longed to see the sport.", "웰러 씨가 말했는데, 그도 사냥을 보고 싶은 마음이 간절했던 터라 이해관계가 걸린 당사자였다."], ["‘The wery thing.", "\"바로 그거야."], ["Well said, Smallcheek; I’ll have it out in a minute.’", "잘 말했다, 볼따구. 내 금방 끌어내 오마.\""]], [["But here a difficulty arose.", "그러나 여기서 난관이 생겼다."], ["The long gamekeeper resolutely protested against the introduction into a shooting party, of a gentleman in a barrow, as a gross violation of all established rules and precedents.", "키 큰 사냥터지기가, 외바퀴 손수레에 신사를 태워 사냥 행렬에 끼워 넣는 것은 확립된 온갖 규칙과 선례를 노골적으로 위반하는 짓이라며 단호히 반대하고 나선 것이다."]], [["It was a great objection, but not an insurmountable one.", "큰 반대였으나, 넘지 못할 것은 아니었다."], ["The gamekeeper having been coaxed and feed, and having, moreover, eased his mind by ‘punching’ the head of the inventive youth who had first suggested the use of the machine, Mr.", "사냥터지기를 어르고 돈도 좀 쥐여 주고, 게다가 그 기계를 쓰자고 맨 처음 제안한 발명심 강한 젊은 녀석의 머리통을 \"쥐어박아\""], ["Pickwick was placed in it, and off the party set; Wardle and the long gamekeeper leading the way, and Mr.", "속을 좀 푼 뒤에, 픽윅 씨는 그 수레에 앉혀졌고, 일행은 출발했다."], ["Pickwick in the barrow, propelled by Sam, bringing up the rear.", "워들과 키 큰 사냥터지기가 앞장서고, 손수레에 탄 픽윅 씨를 샘이 밀며 후미를 맡았다."]], [["‘Stop, Sam,’ said Mr.", "\"멈춰, 샘.\""], ["Pickwick, when they had got half across the first field.", "첫 번째 들판을 절반쯤 가로질렀을 때 픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘What’s the matter now?’ said Wardle.", "\"이번엔 또 뭐요?\" 워들이 말했다."]], [["‘I won’t suffer this barrow to be moved another step,’ said Mr.", "\"이 손수레가 한 발짝도 더 움직이는 걸 두고 보지 않겠습니다.\""], ["Pickwick, resolutely, ‘unless Winkle carries that gun of his in a different manner.’", "픽윅 씨가 단호히 말했다. \"윈클이 저 총을 다른 식으로 들지 않는 한은요.\""]], [["‘How am I to carry it?’ said the wretched Winkle.", "\"그럼 대체 어떻게 들라는 거요?\" 가엾은 윈클이 말했다."]], [["‘Carry it with the muzzle to the ground,’ replied Mr.", "\"총구를 땅으로 향하게 들어요.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘It’s so unsportsmanlike,’ reasoned Winkle.", "\"그건 영 사냥꾼답지 못한데요.\" 윈클이 따졌다."]], [["‘I don’t care whether it’s unsportsmanlike or not,’ replied Mr.", "\"사냥꾼답건 말건 난 상관 안 합니다.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다."], ["Pickwick; ‘I am not going to be shot in a wheel-barrow, for the sake of appearances, to please anybody.’", "\"남들 보기 좋자고, 누구 비위 맞추자고 외바퀴 수레에서 총 맞아 죽을 생각은 없으니까요.\""]], [["‘I know the gentleman’ll put that ‘ere charge into somebody afore he’s done,’ growled the long man.", "\"저 신사분이 끝장 보기 전에 누구한테든 그 총알을 박아 넣고 말 게라니까요.\" 키 큰 사내가 으르렁거리듯 말했다."]], [["‘Well, well--I don’t mind,’ said poor Winkle, turning his gun-stock uppermost--‘there.’", "\"알았어요, 알았어 — 난 상관없어요.\" 가엾은 윈클이 개머리판을 위로 가게 돌려 들며 말했다. \"자, 됐죠.\""]], [["‘Anythin’ for a quiet life,’ said Mr.", "\"조용히 살자면 뭔들 못 하겠습니까요.\""], ["Weller; and on they went again.", "웰러 씨가 말했다. 그리고 그들은 다시 나아갔다."]], [["‘Stop!’ said Mr.", "\"멈춰!\""], ["Pickwick, after they had gone a few yards farther.", "몇 야드 더 갔을 때 픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘What now?’ said Wardle.", "\"이번엔 또 뭐요?\" 워들이 말했다."]], [["‘That gun of Tupman’s is not safe: I know it isn’t,’ said Mr.", "\"터프먼의 저 총은 안전하지가 않아요. 분명 안전하지 않다니까요.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Eh?", "\"네?"], ["What!", "뭐라고요!"], ["not safe?’ said Mr.", "안전하지 않다고요?\""], ["Tupman, in a tone of great alarm.", "터프먼 씨가 몹시 놀란 목소리로 말했다."]], [["‘Not as you are carrying it,’ said Mr.", "\"당신이 들고 있는 그 꼴로는요.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick. ‘I am very sorry to make any further objection, but I cannot consent to go on, unless you carry it as Winkle does his.’", "\"또 이의를 제기해서 정말 미안하지만, 윈클이 자기 총을 드는 식으로 당신도 들지 않는 한, 더 가는 데 동의할 수 없어요.\""]], [["‘I think you had better, sir,’ said the long gamekeeper, ‘or you’re quite as likely to lodge the charge in yourself as in anything else.’", "\"그러시는 게 좋겠는뎁쇼, 나리.\" 키 큰 사냥터지기가 말했다. \"안 그러면 무엇보다도 당신 자신한테 그 총알을 박을 공산이 큽니다요.\""]], [["Mr.", "터프먼 씨는 더없이 친절하고 급하게 자기 총을 요구받은 자세로 옮겼고, 일행은 다시 나아갔다."], ["Tupman, with the most obliging haste, placed his piece in the position required, and the party moved on again; the two amateurs marching with reversed arms, like a couple of privates at a royal funeral.", "두 아마추어는, 왕실 장례식의 사병 두 명처럼 총을 거꾸로 멘 채 행진했다."]], [["The dogs suddenly came to a dead stop, and the party advancing stealthily a single pace, stopped too.", "개들이 갑자기 딱 멈춰 섰고, 일행도 살금살금 한 발짝 나아갔다가 역시 멈춰 섰다."]], [["‘What’s the matter with the dogs’ legs?’ whispered Mr.", "\"저 개들 다리가 왜 저래요?\" 윈클 씨가 속삭였다."], ["Winkle. ‘How queer they’re standing.’", "\"서 있는 꼴이 어찌 저리 이상하담.\""]], [["‘Hush, can’t you?’ replied Wardle softly.", "\"쉿, 좀 가만히 못 있겠나?\" 워들이 나직이 대답했다."], ["‘Don’t you see, they’re making a point?’", "\"안 보이나, 저것들이 포인트를 잡고 있는 거잖나?\""]], [["‘Making a point!’ said Mr.", "\"포인트를 잡는다고요!\""], ["Winkle, staring about him, as if he expected to discover some particular beauty in the landscape, which the sagacious animals were calling special attention to.", "윈클 씨가, 마치 저 영리한 짐승들이 특별히 주목을 끌고 있는 무슨 빼어난 풍경이라도 발견하리라 기대하는 양 사방을 두리번거리며 말했다."], ["‘Making a point!", "\"포인트를 잡는다니!"], ["What are they pointing at?’", "뭘 가리키고 있다는 거예요?\""]], [["‘Keep your eyes open,’ said Wardle, not heeding the question in the excitement of the moment.", "\"눈 똑바로 떠.\" 워들이 그 순간의 흥분으로 질문에는 아랑곳하지 않고 말했다."], ["‘Now then.’", "\"자, 간다.\""]], [["There was a sharp whirring noise, that made Mr. Winkle start back as if he had been shot himself.", "날카롭게 푸드덕거리는 소리가 나, 윈클 씨가 마치 자기가 총에 맞기라도 한 양 흠칫 물러섰다."], ["Bang, bang, went a couple of guns--the smoke swept quickly away over the field, and curled into the air.", "탕, 탕, 총 두 자루가 울렸다 — 연기가 들판 위로 재빨리 휩쓸려 가더니 공중으로 말려 올랐다."]], [["‘Where are they!’ said Mr.", "\"어디 있어요!\""], ["Winkle, in a state of the highest excitement, turning round and round in all directions.", "윈클 씨가 잔뜩 흥분한 상태로, 사방으로 빙글빙글 돌며 말했다. \"어디 있냐고요?"], ["‘Where are they? Tell me when to fire.", "언제 쏘면 되는지 말해 줘요."], ["Where are they--where are they?’", "어디 있어요 — 어디 있냐고요?\""]], [["‘Where are they!’ said Wardle, taking up a brace of birds which the dogs had deposited at his feet.", "\"어디 있냐고!\" 워들이 개들이 제 발치에 물어다 놓은 새 두 마리를 집어 들며 말했다."], ["‘Why, here they are.’", "\"원, 여기 있잖나.\""]], [["‘No, no; I mean the others,’ said the bewildered Winkle.", "\"아니, 아니요. 난 다른 것들 말이에요.\" 어리둥절해진 윈클이 말했다."]], [["‘Far enough off, by this time,’ replied Wardle, coolly reloading his gun.", "\"지금쯤이면 멀찌감치 갔겠지.\" 워들이 태연히 총을 다시 장전하며 대답했다."]], [["‘We shall very likely be up with another covey in five minutes,’ said the long gamekeeper.", "\"오 분쯤 뒤면 십중팔구 또 한 무리를 만나게 될 겁니다요.\" 키 큰 사냥터지기가 말했다."], ["‘If the gentleman begins to fire now, perhaps he’ll just get the shot out of the barrel by the time they rise.’", "\"저 신사분이 지금부터 쏘기 시작하시면, 새들이 날아오를 때쯤에는 총구에서 막 탄알이 나올 겝니다요.\""]], [["‘Ha!", "\"하! 하!"], ["ha!", "하!\""], ["ha!’ roared Mr. Weller.", "웰러 씨가 폭소를 터뜨렸다."]], [["‘Sam,’ said Mr.", "\"샘.\""], ["Pickwick, compassionating his follower’s confusion and embarrassment.", "픽윅 씨가 자기 하인의 혼란과 당혹을 가엾이 여기며 말했다. \"네, 나리.\""]], [["‘Don’t laugh.’", "\"웃지 마라.\""]], [["‘Certainly not, Sir.’ So, by way of indemnification, Mr.", "\"여부가 있겠습니까요, 나리.\""], ["Weller contorted his features from behind the wheel-barrow, for the exclusive amusement of the boy with the leggings, who thereupon burst into a boisterous laugh, and was summarily cuffed by the long gamekeeper, who wanted a pretext for turning round, to hide his own merriment.", "그래서 웰러 씨는 그 보상으로, 외바퀴 손수레 뒤에 숨어 오로지 각반을 두른 소년만 즐기라고 얼굴을 일그러뜨려 보였고, 소년은 이에 요란하게 웃음을 터뜨렸다가, 제 즐거움을 감추려고 돌아설 핑계가 필요했던 키 큰 사냥터지기에게 즉결로 한 대 쥐어박혔다."]], [["‘Bravo, old fellow!’ said Wardle to Mr.", "\"잘했네, 이 친구!\" 워들이 터프먼 씨에게 말했다."], ["Tupman; ‘you fired that time, at all events.’", "\"어쨌든 이번엔 발사는 했군그래.\""]], [["‘Oh, yes,’ replied Mr.", "\"오, 그럼요.\""], ["Tupman, with conscious pride.", "터프먼 씨가 흐뭇한 자부심으로 대답했다."], ["‘I let it off.’", "\"쏘아 버렸지요.\""]], [["‘Well done.", "\"잘했어."], ["You’ll hit something next time, if you look sharp.", "다음번엔 눈 똑바로 뜨면 뭐라도 맞힐 걸세."], ["Very easy, ain’t it?’", "아주 쉽지, 안 그런가?\""]], [["‘Yes, it’s very easy,’ said Mr.", "\"네, 아주 쉽군요.\" 터프먼 씨가 말했다."], ["Tupman. ‘How it hurts one’s shoulder, though.", "\"그런데 어깨가 어찌 이리 아픈지."], ["It nearly knocked me backwards.", "하마터면 뒤로 나자빠질 뻔했어요."], ["I had no idea these small firearms kicked so.’", "이런 작은 총이 이렇게 반동이 셀 줄은 몰랐네요.\""]], [["‘Ah,’ said the old gentleman, smiling, ‘you’ll get used to it in time.", "\"아.\" 영감이 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["Now then--all ready--all right with the barrow there?’", "\"차차 익숙해질 걸세. 자, 그럼 — 다들 준비됐나 — 거기 손수레는 괜찮나?\""]], [["‘All right, Sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"괜찮습니다요, 나리.\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘Come along, then.’", "\"그럼 가세.\""]], [["‘Hold hard, Sir,’ said Sam, raising the barrow.", "\"꽉 잡으십쇼, 나리.\" 샘이 손수레를 들어 올리며 말했다."]], [["‘Aye, aye,’ replied Mr.", "\"아무렴, 아무렴.\""], ["Pickwick; and on they went, as briskly as need be.", "픽윅 씨가 대답했다. 그리고 그들은 더없이 활기차게 나아갔다."]], [["‘Keep that barrow back now,’ cried Wardle, when it had been hoisted over a stile into another field, and Mr.", "\"이제 그 손수레는 뒤에 세워 두게.\""], ["Pickwick had been deposited in it once more.", "손수레가 층계 디딤대를 넘어 또 다른 들판으로 들어 올려지고 픽윅 씨가 다시 그 안에 앉혀진 뒤, 워들이 외쳤다."]], [["‘All right, sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"알겠습니다요, 나리.\""], ["Weller, pausing.", "웰러 씨가 멈춰 서며 대답했다."]], [["‘Now, Winkle,’ said the old gentleman, ‘follow me softly, and don’t be too late this time.’", "\"자, 윈클.\" 영감이 말했다. \"조용히 날 따라오게. 그리고 이번엔 너무 늦지 말고.\""]], [["‘Never fear,’ said Mr.", "\"걱정 마세요.\" 윈클 씨가 말했다."], ["Winkle. ‘Are they pointing?’", "\"포인트를 잡고 있나요?\""]], [["‘No, no; not now.", "\"아니, 아니."], ["Quietly now, quietly.’ On they crept, and very quietly they would have advanced, if Mr.", "지금은 아닐세. 자, 조용히, 조용히.\""], ["Winkle, in the performance of some very intricate evolutions with his gun, had not accidentally fired, at the most critical moment, over the boy’s head, exactly in the very spot where the tall man’s brain would have been, had he been there instead.", "그들은 살금살금 기어 나아갔고, 정말 아주 조용히 전진했을 텐데, 다만 윈클 씨가 자기 총으로 몹시 복잡한 동작을 하던 중, 가장 결정적인 순간에 어쩌다 그만 소년의 머리 위로 — 만일 그 키 큰 사내가 거기 대신 있었다면 바로 그의 골이 있었을 자리로 — 총을 쏘아 버리고 말았다."]], [["‘Why, what on earth did you do that for?’ said old Wardle, as the birds flew unharmed away.", "\"아니, 대체 왜 그런 짓을 한 건가?\" 새들이 다친 데 없이 날아가 버리자 워들 영감이 말했다."]], [["‘I never saw such a gun in my life,’ replied poor Mr.", "\"내 평생 이런 총은 처음 봅니다.\""], ["Winkle, looking at the lock, as if that would do any good.", "가엾은 윈클 씨가, 마치 그래 봐야 무슨 소용이라도 있는 양 격발 장치를 들여다보며 대답했다."], ["‘It goes off of its own accord.", "\"제멋대로 발사돼 버려요."], ["It will do it.’", "자꾸 그런다니까요.\""]], [["‘Will do it!’ echoed Wardle, with something of irritation in his manner.", "\"자꾸 그런다고!\" 워들이 다소 짜증 섞인 투로 되받았다."], ["‘I wish it would kill something of its own accord.’", "\"그놈이 제멋대로 뭐라도 좀 죽여 주면 좋겠구먼.\""]], [["‘It’ll do that afore long, Sir,’ observed the tall man, in a low, prophetic voice.", "\"머지않아 그럴 겝니다요, 나리.\" 키 큰 사내가 나직이 예언하는 목소리로 말했다."]], [["‘What do you mean by that observation, Sir?’ inquired Mr.", "\"그 말이 무슨 뜻이오, 응?\""], ["Winkle, angrily.", "윈클 씨가 화를 내며 물었다."]], [["‘Never mind, Sir, never mind,’ replied the long gamekeeper; ‘I’ve no family myself, sir; and this here boy’s mother will get something handsome from Sir Geoffrey, if he’s killed on his land. Load again, Sir, load again.’", "\"신경 쓰지 마십쇼, 나리, 신경 쓰지 마세요.\" 키 큰 사냥터지기가 대답했다. \"전 제 식솔도 없는 사람입니다요, 나리. 그리고 이 소년의 어미야, 만일 이 녀석이 제프리 경의 땅에서 죽으면 경한테서 후한 보상을 받을 테고요. 다시 장전하십쇼, 나리, 다시 장전하시라고요.\""]], [["‘Take away his gun,’ cried Mr.", "\"저 사람 총을 치워 버려요.\""], ["Pickwick from the barrow, horror-stricken at the long man’s dark insinuations.", "키 큰 사내의 음산한 암시에 질겁한 픽윅 씨가 손수레에서 외쳤다."], ["‘Take away his gun, do you hear, somebody?’", "\"저 사람 총을 치우란 말이오, 안 들려요, 누구든?\""]], [["Nobody, however, volunteered to obey the command; and Mr. Winkle, after darting a rebellious glance at Mr. Pickwick, reloaded his gun, and proceeded onwards with the rest.", "그러나 아무도 그 명령에 자청해서 따르지 않았고, 윈클 씨는 픽윅 씨에게 반항하는 눈길을 한 번 쏘아 던진 뒤, 자기 총을 다시 장전하고는 나머지 일행과 함께 앞으로 나아갔다."]], [["We are bound, on the authority of Mr. Pickwick, to state, that Mr.", "우리는 픽윅 씨의 권위에 의거하여, 터프먼 씨의 처신 방식이 윈클 씨가 취한 것보다 훨씬 더 신중하고 사려 깊었음을 밝히지 않을 수 없다."], ["Tupman’s mode of proceeding evinced far more of prudence and deliberation, than that adopted by Mr. Winkle.", "그렇다고 이것이 사냥에 관련된 모든 일에 있어 후자 신사가 지닌 대단한 권위를 결코 깎아내리는 것은 아니다."], ["Still, this by no means detracts from the great authority of the latter gentleman, on all matters connected with the field; because, as Mr. Pickwick beautifully observes, it has somehow or other happened, from time immemorial, that many of the best and ablest philosophers, who have been perfect lights of science in matters of theory, have been wholly unable to reduce them to practice.", "왜냐하면, 픽윅 씨가 멋지게 지적하듯, 까마득한 옛날부터 어쩐 일인지, 이론상으로는 학문의 완전한 등불이라 할 더없이 훌륭하고 유능한 철학자들 가운데 많은 이들이 그것을 실천으로 옮기는 데는 전혀 무력했던 일이 곧잘 있어 왔기 때문이다."]], [["Mr. Tupman’s process, like many of our most sublime discoveries, was extremely simple.", "터프먼 씨의 방식은, 우리의 가장 숭고한 발견들 가운데 많은 것이 그러하듯, 지극히 단순했다."], ["With the quickness and penetration of a man of genius, he had at once observed that the two great points to be attained were-- first, to discharge his piece without injury to himself, and, secondly, to do so, without danger to the bystanders--obviously, the best thing to do, after surmounting the difficulty of firing at all, was to shut his eyes firmly, and fire into the air.", "천재다운 기민함과 통찰력으로, 그는 도달해야 할 두 가지 큰 요점을 단번에 간파했던 것이다 — 첫째는 자기 자신을 다치지 않게 하면서 총을 발사하는 것이요, 둘째는 그러면서도 곁에 선 이들에게 위험을 끼치지 않는 것이었다. 분명히, 총을 쏜다는 난관 자체를 넘어선 다음에 할 수 있는 최선책은, 두 눈을 꼭 감고 공중을 향해 쏘는 것이었다."]], [["On one occasion, after performing this feat, Mr. Tupman, on opening his eyes, beheld a plump partridge in the act of falling, wounded, to the ground.", "한번은 이 묘기를 펼친 뒤 터프먼 씨가 눈을 떠 보니, 통통한 자고새 한 마리가 부상을 입고 땅으로 떨어지는 참이었다."], ["He was on the point of congratulating Mr. Wardle on his invariable success, when that gentleman advanced towards him, and grasped him warmly by the hand.", "그는 워들 씨에게 그 변함없는 성공을 축하해 주려던 참이었는데, 바로 그 신사가 그에게로 다가와 그의 손을 따뜻하게 움켜쥐는 것이었다."]], [["‘Tupman,’ said the old gentleman, ‘you singled out that particular bird?’", "\"터프먼.\" 영감이 말했다. \"자네가 바로 저 새를 골라서 쐈지?\""]], [["‘No,’ said Mr.", "\"아니에요.\" 터프먼 씨가 말했다."], ["Tupman--‘no.’", "\"아닙니다.\""]], [["‘You did,’ said Wardle.", "\"맞아.\" 워들이 말했다."], ["‘I saw you do it--I observed you pick him out--I noticed you, as you raised your piece to take aim; and I will say this, that the best shot in existence could not have done it more beautifully.", "\"내가 자네가 그러는 걸 봤어 — 자네가 그놈을 골라내는 걸 보았다고 — 자네가 겨누려고 총을 들어 올릴 때 내가 눈여겨봤거든. 그리고 내 한마디 하건대, 세상에 둘도 없는 명사수라도 그보다 더 멋들어지게는 못 했을 걸세."], ["You are an older hand at this than I thought you, Tupman; you have been out before.’", "자넨 내가 생각했던 것보다 이 방면에 노련한 솜씨꾼이군그래, 터프먼. 전에도 나가 본 적이 있었어.\""]], [["It was in vain for Mr. Tupman to protest, with a smile of self-denial, that he never had.", "터프먼 씨가 결코 그런 적 없노라고 겸양의 미소를 띠며 항변해 봐야 헛일이었다."], ["The very smile was taken as evidence to the contrary; and from that time forth his reputation was established.", "바로 그 미소가 그 반대의 증거로 받아들여졌고, 그때부터 그의 명성은 확립되었다."], ["It is not the only reputation that has been acquired as easily, nor are such fortunate circumstances confined to partridge-shooting.", "그렇게 쉽사리 얻어진 명성이 이것뿐만도 아니거니와, 그런 운수 좋은 정황이 자고새 사냥에만 국한된 것도 아니다."]], [["Meanwhile, Mr. Winkle flashed, and blazed, and smoked away, without producing any material results worthy of being noted down; sometimes expending his charge in mid-air, and at others sending it skimming along so near the surface of the ground as to place the lives of the two dogs on a rather uncertain and precarious tenure.", "그러는 사이에 윈클 씨는 번쩍이고 불을 뿜고 연기를 피워 댔으나, 적어 둘 만한 이렇다 할 성과는 하나도 내지 못했다."], ["As a display of fancy- shooting, it was extremely varied and curious; as an exhibition of firing with any precise object, it was, upon the whole, perhaps a failure.", "어떤 때는 탄알을 허공에 흩뿌렸고, 또 어떤 때는 땅 표면에 바싹 붙여 스치듯 날려서, 두 마리 개의 목숨을 자못 불확실하고 위태로운 처지에 놓이게 했다."], ["It is an established axiom, that ‘every bullet has its billet.’ If it apply in an equal degree to shot, those of Mr.", "묘기 사격의 시범으로 치자면 그것은 지극히 다채롭고 진기했으나, 어떤 특정한 표적을 겨눈 사격의 시연으로 치자면, 대체로 보아 실패라 할 만했다."], ["Winkle were unfortunate foundlings, deprived of their natural rights, cast loose upon the world, and billeted nowhere.", "\"총알마다 제 묵을 자리가 있다\"는 것은 확립된 격언이다. 만일 그것이 산탄에도 똑같이 적용된다면, 윈클 씨의 산탄들은 타고난 권리를 빼앗긴 채 세상에 내던져져 어디에도 묵을 자리를 얻지 못한 불운한 미아들이었다."]], [["‘Well,’ said Wardle, walking up to the side of the barrow, and wiping the streams of perspiration from his jolly red face; ‘smoking day, isn’t it?’", "\"이거.\" 워들이 손수레 옆으로 걸어와, 그 유쾌하고 불그레한 얼굴에서 줄줄 흐르는 땀을 닦으며 말했다. \"찌는 날이로군, 안 그런가?\""]], [["‘It is, indeed,’ replied Mr.", "\"정말 그렇군요.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다."], ["Pickwick. The sun is tremendously hot, even to me.", "\"햇볕이 이 나한테도 어마어마하게 뜨겁군요."], ["I don’t know how you must feel it.’", "당신은 얼마나 덥게 느껴질지 모르겠어요.\""]], [["‘Why,’ said the old gentleman, ‘pretty hot.", "\"글쎄.\" 영감이 말했다."], ["It’s past twelve, though.", "\"꽤나 덥지. 하지만 벌써 열두 시가 지났네."], ["You see that green hill there?’", "저기 저 푸른 언덕 보이나?\""]], [["‘Certainly.’", "\"보이지요.\""]], [["‘That’s the place where we are to lunch; and, by Jove, there’s the boy with the basket, punctual as clockwork!’", "\"저기가 우리가 점심을 들 곳일세. 그리고 어럽쇼, 저기 그 소년이 바구니를 들고 있군, 시계처럼 정확하게 말이야!\""]], [["‘So he is,’ said Mr.", "\"정말 그렇군요.\""], ["Pickwick, brightening up.", "픽윅 씨가 환해지며 말했다."], ["‘Good boy, that.", "\"착한 소년이로군."], ["I’ll give him a shilling, presently.", "이따 내 그 애한테 1실링 줘야겠어."], ["Now, then, Sam, wheel away.’", "자, 그럼, 샘, 밀고 가게.\""]], [["‘Hold on, sir,’ said Mr.", "\"꽉 잡으십쇼, 나리.\""], ["Weller, invigorated with the prospect of refreshments.", "먹을거리에 대한 기대로 기운이 솟은 웰러 씨가 말했다."], ["‘Out of the vay, young leathers.", "\"비켜라, 가죽 각반 꼬마야."], ["If you walley my precious life don’t upset me, as the gen’l’m’n said to the driver when they was a-carryin’ him to Tyburn.’ And quickening his pace to a sharp run, Mr.", "너 만약에 내 귀하신 목숨을 소중히 여긴다면 날 엎지 말거라, 마치 타이번으로 끌려가던 그 신사 양반이 마부한테 한 말처럼 말이다.\""], ["Weller wheeled his master nimbly to the green hill, shot him dexterously out by the very side of the basket, and proceeded to unpack it with the utmost despatch.", "그러고는 걸음을 빠른 달음질로 재촉하며, 웰러 씨는 주인을 잽싸게 푸른 언덕으로 밀고 가, 바로 바구니 옆에다 솜씨 좋게 쏟아 내려놓고는, 더없이 부랴부랴 바구니를 풀기 시작했다. * 타이번: 옛 런던의 처형장."]], [["‘Weal pie,’ said Mr.", "\"송아지 고기 파이로군.\""], ["Weller, soliloquising, as he arranged the eatables on the grass.", "웰러 씨가 풀밭에 먹을거리를 늘어놓으며 혼잣말로 중얼거렸다."], ["‘Wery good thing is weal pie, when you know the lady as made it, and is quite sure it ain’t kittens; and arter all though, where’s the odds, when they’re so like weal that the wery piemen themselves don’t know the difference?’", "\"송아지 고기 파이야말로 아주 좋은 거지, 그걸 만든 부인을 알고 그게 새끼고양이가 아니라는 걸 확실히 알 때는 말이야. 하긴 그게 어쨌든, 송아지 고기랑 어찌나 똑같은지 파이 장수 자신들도 그 차이를 모를 지경인데 무슨 상관이람?\""]], [["‘Don’t they, Sam?’ said Mr.", "\"모른단 말이냐, 샘?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Not they, sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"모르고말굽쇼, 나리.\""], ["Weller, touching his hat.", "웰러 씨가 모자에 손을 갖다 대며 대답했다."], ["‘I lodged in the same house vith a pieman once, sir, and a wery nice man he was--reg’lar clever chap, too--make pies out o’ anything, he could.", "\"한번은 어떤 파이 장수랑 같은 집에서 하숙한 적이 있는데요, 나리, 아주 좋은 사람이었지요 — 게다가 영락없는 솜씨꾼이라, 뭐로든 파이를 만들어 낼 줄 알았답니다."], ["“What a number o’ cats you keep, Mr. Brooks,”", "'브룩스 씨, 고양이를 어찌 이리 많이 기르슈?'"], ["says I, when I’d got intimate with him.", "하고 그 사람이랑 친해졌을 때 제가 물었지요."], ["“Ah,”", "'아,'"], ["says he, “I do--a good many,”", "하고 그가 말합디다, '많이 기르지요,'"], ["says he, “You must be wery fond o’ cats,”", "하고요. '고양이를 어지간히 좋아하시나 보네,'"], ["says I. “Other people is,”", "하고 제가 그랬더니, '남들도 그렇지요,'"], ["says he, a-winkin’ at me; “they ain’t in season till the winter though,”", "하면서 저한테 눈을 찡긋합디다. '그래도 그놈들은 겨울이 돼야 제철이라오,'"], ["says he.", "그래요."], ["“Not in season!”", "'제철이 아니라뇨!'"], ["says I. “No,”", "제가 그랬죠."], ["says he, “fruits is in, cats is out.”", "'그렇소,' 그가 말합디다, '과일이 들면 고양이는 나가는 거요.'"], ["“Why, what do you mean?”", "'아니, 그게 무슨 말이오?'"], ["says I. “Mean!”", "제가 그랬더니, '말이냐고요!'"], ["says he.", "그래요."], ["“That I’ll never be a party to the combination o’ the butchers, to keep up the price o’ meat,”", "'내 결단코 고깃값을 떠받치려는 푸줏간들 작당에는 끼지 않으리란 거지요,'"], ["says he.", "하고요."], ["“Mr. Weller,”", "'웰러 씨,'"], ["says he, a-squeezing my hand wery hard, and vispering in my ear--“don’t mention this here agin--but it’s the seasonin’ as does it.", "하고 그가 제 손을 아주 꽉 쥐고 제 귀에 대고 속삭이는 거예요 — '이건 다시는 입 밖에 내지 마쇼 — 하지만 결국 양념이 다 하는 거라오."], ["They’re all made o’ them noble animals,”", "죄다 저 고귀한 짐승들로 만드는 거지요,'"], ["says he, a-pointin’ to a wery nice little tabby kitten, “and I seasons ‘em for beefsteak, weal or kidney, ‘cording to the demand.", "하고 아주 예쁘장한 작은 얼룩 새끼고양이를 가리키면서 말합디다, '난 수요에 따라 그놈들을 비프스테이크용으로, 송아지 고기용으로, 콩팥용으로 양념을 하지요."], ["And more than that,”", "그뿐인 줄 아쇼,'"], ["says he, “I can make a weal a beef- steak, or a beef-steak a kidney, or any one on ‘em a mutton, at a minute’s notice, just as the market changes, and appetites wary!”’", "그래요, '난 시장이 변하고 입맛이 바뀌는 대로, 눈 깜짝할 새에 송아지 고기를 비프스테이크로, 비프스테이크를 콩팥으로, 그것들 중 뭐든 양고기로 만들 수도 있다오!' 합디다.\""]], [["‘He must have been a very ingenious young man, that, Sam,’ said Mr.", "\"아주 재간 있는 젊은이였나 보구나, 그자가, 샘.\""], ["Pickwick, with a slight shudder.", "픽윅 씨가 살짝 몸서리치며 말했다."]], [["‘Just was, sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"그렇고말굽쇼, 나리.\""], ["Weller, continuing his occupation of emptying the basket, ‘and the pies was beautiful.", "웰러 씨가 계속해서 바구니를 비우며 대답했다. \"게다가 파이는 끝내줬답니다."], ["Tongue--, well that’s a wery good thing when it ain’t a woman’s.", "우설 — 음, 그것도 여자 혀만 아니면 아주 좋은 거지요."], ["Bread--knuckle o’ ham, reg’lar picter--cold beef in slices, wery good.", "빵 — 햄 정강이, 그림 같습니다 — 차가운 소고기 저민 것, 아주 좋고요."], ["What’s in them stone jars, young touch-and-go?’", "저 돌단지엔 뭐가 들었느냐, 위험천만한 꼬마야?\""]], [["‘Beer in this one,’ replied the boy, taking from his shoulder a couple of large stone bottles, fastened together by a leathern strap--‘cold punch in t’other.’", "\"이건 맥주고요.\" 소년이 어깨에서, 가죽끈으로 함께 묶인 큼직한 돌병 두 개를 내리며 대답했다. \"저건 차가운 펀치예요.\""]], [["‘And a wery good notion of a lunch it is, take it altogether,’ said Mr.", "\"이만하면 점심으로 아주 그럴듯한 생각이로군, 죄다 합쳐 놓고 보니.\""], ["Weller, surveying his arrangement of the repast with great satisfaction.", "웰러 씨가 자기가 차려 놓은 식사를 크게 만족스러워하며 둘러보고 말했다."], ["‘Now, gen’l’m’n, “fall on,”", "\"자, 신사 여러분, '돌격하시지요,'"], ["as the English said to the French when they fixed bagginets.’", "마치 영국군이 총검을 꽂았을 때 프랑스군한테 한 말처럼요.\""]], [["It needed no second invitation to induce the party to yield full justice to the meal; and as little pressing did it require to induce Mr. Weller, the long gamekeeper, and the two boys, to station themselves on the grass, at a little distance, and do good execution upon a decent proportion of the viands.", "일행더러 그 식사에 한껏 정의를 베풀라고 두 번 청할 필요는 없었고, 웰러 씨와 키 큰 사냥터지기와 두 소년더러 조금 떨어진 풀밭에 자리 잡고 음식의 상당한 몫을 거뜬히 해치우라고 권하는 데도 별 강권이 필요 없었다."], ["An old oak afforded a pleasant shelter to the group, and a rich prospect of arable and meadow land, intersected with luxuriant hedges, and richly ornamented with wood, lay spread out before them.", "늙은 떡갈나무 한 그루가 이 무리에게 기분 좋은 그늘을 드리워 주었고, 무성한 산울타리로 갈리고 숲으로 그윽하게 단장된 경작지와 목초지의 풍요로운 전경이 그들 앞에 펼쳐져 있었다."]], [["‘This is delightful--thoroughly delightful!’ said Mr.", "\"이거 참 즐겁군요 — 더할 나위 없이 즐거워요!\""], ["Pickwick; the skin of whose expressive countenance was rapidly peeling off, with exposure to the sun.", "픽윅 씨가 말했는데, 그의 표정 풍부한 얼굴 가죽은 햇볕에 노출되어 빠르게 벗겨지고 있었다."]], [["‘So it is--so it is, old fellow,’ replied Wardle.", "\"그렇지 — 그렇고말고, 이 친구.\" 워들이 대답했다."], ["‘Come; a glass of punch!’", "\"자, 펀치 한 잔!\""]], [["‘With great pleasure,’ said Mr.", "\"기꺼이요.\""], ["Pickwick; the satisfaction of whose countenance, after drinking it, bore testimony to the sincerity of the reply.", "픽윅 씨가 말했고, 그것을 마신 뒤 그의 얼굴에 떠오른 만족감이 그 대답의 진심을 증언해 주었다."]], [["‘Good,’ said Mr.", "\"좋군요.\""], ["Pickwick, smacking his lips.", "픽윅 씨가 입맛을 다시며 말했다."], ["‘Very good.", "\"아주 좋아요."], ["I’ll take another.", "한 잔 더 들겠어요."], ["Cool; very cool.", "시원하군요, 아주 시원해요."], ["Come, gentlemen,’ continued Mr.", "자, 신사 여러분.\""], ["Pickwick, still retaining his hold upon the jar, ‘a toast.", "픽윅 씨가 여전히 단지를 손에서 놓지 않고 말을 이었다. \"건배합시다."], ["Our friends at Dingley Dell.’", "딩글리 델의 우리 친구들을 위하여.\""]], [["The toast was drunk with loud acclamations.", "건배는 우렁찬 환호 속에 이루어졌다."]], [["‘I’ll tell you what I shall do, to get up my shooting again,’ said Mr.", "\"내가 사격 솜씨를 다시 키우려면 어떻게 할지 말씀드리지요.\""], ["Winkle, who was eating bread and ham with a pocket-knife.", "주머니칼로 빵과 햄을 먹고 있던 윈클 씨가 말했다."], ["‘I’ll put a stuffed partridge on the top of a post, and practise at it, beginning at a short distance, and lengthening it by degrees.", "\"기둥 꼭대기에 박제 자고새를 하나 올려놓고, 가까운 거리에서 시작해서 차츰 거리를 늘려 가며 연습할 겁니다."], ["I understand it’s capital practice.’", "그게 아주 훌륭한 연습이라더군요.\""]], [["‘I know a gen’l’man, Sir,’ said Mr. Weller, ‘as did that, and begun at two yards; but he never tried it on agin; for he blowed the bird right clean away at the first fire, and nobody ever seed a feather on him arterwards.’", "\"제가 어떤 신사분을 아는데요, 나리.\" 웰러 씨가 말했다. \"그 양반이 그걸 했지요, 2야드에서 시작해서요. 하지만 다시는 해 보지 않았습니다. 첫 발에 그 새를 깨끗이 날려 버려서, 그 뒤로 깃털 하나 본 사람이 없었거든요.\""]], [["‘Sam,’ said Mr.", "\"샘.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"네, 나리.\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘Have the goodness to reserve your anecdotes till they are called for.’", "\"네 일화는 청할 때까지 좀 아껴 두는 친절을 베풀어 다오.\""]], [["‘Cert’nly, sir.’", "\"여부가 있겠습니까요, 나리.\""]], [["Here Mr. Weller winked the eye which was not concealed by the beer-can he was raising to his lips, with such exquisite facetiousness, that the two boys went into spontaneous convulsions, and even the long man condescended to smile.", "이때 웰러 씨가, 입술로 가져가던 맥주 깡통에 가려지지 않은 한쪽 눈을 어찌나 절묘하게 익살스럽게 찡긋했던지, 두 소년이 절로 자지러졌고, 심지어 키 큰 사내까지 황송하게도 미소를 지었다."]], [["‘Well, that certainly is most capital cold punch,’ said Mr.", "\"음, 이건 정말이지 더없이 훌륭한 차가운 펀치로군요.\""], ["Pickwick, looking earnestly at the stone bottle; ‘and the day is extremely warm, and--Tupman, my dear friend, a glass of punch?’", "픽윅 씨가 돌병을 골똘히 바라보며 말했다. \"게다가 날은 몹시 덥고, 또 — 터프먼, 이보게, 펀치 한 잔?\""]], [["‘With the greatest delight,’ replied Mr.", "\"더없이 기쁘게요.\""], ["Tupman; and having drank that glass, Mr.", "터프먼 씨가 대답했다."], ["Pickwick took another, just to see whether there was any orange peel in the punch, because orange peel always disagreed with him; and finding that there was not, Mr. Pickwick took another glass to the health of their absent friend, and then felt himself imperatively called upon to propose another in honour of the punch-compounder, unknown.", "그리고 그 잔을 비우자 픽윅 씨는, 펀치에 오렌지 껍질이 들었는지 그저 알아보려고 — 오렌지 껍질은 늘 그에게 받지 않았던 까닭에 — 또 한 잔을 들었고, 들어 있지 않음을 확인하자 픽윅 씨는 자리에 없는 친구의 건강을 위해 또 한 잔을 들었으며, 그러고 나니 누군지 모를 펀치 제조자를 기려 또 한 잔을 권해야 한다는 절박한 의무감이 들었다."]], [["This constant succession of glasses produced considerable effect upon Mr.", "이렇게 잔이 끊임없이 이어지자 픽윅 씨에게는 상당한 효과가 나타났다."], ["Pickwick; his countenance beamed with the most sunny smiles, laughter played around his lips, and good-humoured merriment twinkled in his eye. Yielding by degrees to the influence of the exciting liquid, rendered more so by the heat, Mr.", "그의 얼굴은 더없이 화창한 미소로 빛났고, 입가에는 웃음이 어렸으며, 눈에는 사람 좋은 즐거움이 반짝였다."], ["Pickwick expressed a strong desire to recollect a song which he had heard in his infancy, and the attempt proving abortive, sought to stimulate his memory with more glasses of punch, which appeared to have quite a contrary effect; for, from forgetting the words of the song, he began to forget how to articulate any words at all; and finally, after rising to his legs to address the company in an eloquent speech, he fell into the barrow, and fast asleep, simultaneously.", "차츰차츰, 그 자극적인 음료의 영향에 — 더위로 한층 더해진 그 영향에 — 굴복한 픽윅 씨는, 어릴 적에 들었던 노래 한 곡을 떠올리고 싶다는 강한 바람을 내비쳤고, 그 시도가 헛수고로 돌아가자 펀치를 더 들어 기억을 자극하려 했는데, 그것은 정반대의 효과를 내는 듯했다. 노랫말을 잊어버리던 그가, 이젠 아무 말이나마 또박또박 발음하는 법조차 잊기 시작했고, 마침내는 좌중을 향해 유창한 연설을 하겠다고 두 다리로 일어섰다가 손수레 안으로 쓰러짐과 동시에 곤히 잠들어 버렸다."]], [["The basket having been repacked, and it being found perfectly impossible to awaken Mr. Pickwick from his torpor, some discussion took place whether it would be better for Mr.", "바구니를 다시 챙기고 나서도 픽윅 씨를 그 혼곤한 잠에서 깨우기란 도무지 불가능하다는 것이 드러나자, 웰러 씨가 주인을 다시 밀고 돌아가는 편이 나을지, 아니면 다들 돌아갈 채비가 될 때까지 그를 있는 자리에 두는 편이 나을지를 두고 약간의 논의가 벌어졌다."], ["Weller to wheel his master back again, or to leave him where he was, until they should all be ready to return. The latter course was at length decided on; and as the further expedition was not to exceed an hour’s duration, and as Mr.", "결국 후자 쪽으로 결정이 났다."], ["Weller begged very hard to be one of the party, it was determined to leave Mr. Pickwick asleep in the barrow, and to call for him on their return.", "이후의 사냥 행차가 한 시간을 넘기지 않을 터인 데다, 웰러 씨가 일행에 끼게 해 달라고 몹시 간곡히 청했으므로, 픽윅 씨는 손수레 안에서 잠들게 둔 채 돌아오는 길에 데리러 오기로 정해졌다."], ["So away they went, leaving Mr. Pickwick snoring most comfortably in the shade.", "그리하여 그들은, 그늘에서 더없이 편안하게 코를 고는 픽윅 씨를 남겨 두고 떠났다."]], [["That Mr. Pickwick would have continued to snore in the shade until his friends came back, or, in default thereof, until the shades of evening had fallen on the landscape, there appears no reasonable cause to doubt; always supposing that he had been suffered to remain there in peace.", "픽윅 씨가 친구들이 돌아올 때까지, 혹은 그들이 돌아오지 않을 경우 저녁 어스름이 풍경에 깔릴 때까지 그늘에서 계속 코를 골았으리라는 데에는 — 그가 평온하게 거기 머물도록 내버려졌으리라고 가정한다면 — 의심할 만한 합당한 이유가 전혀 없어 보인다."], ["But he was not suffered to remain there in peace.", "그러나 그는 평온하게 거기 머물도록 내버려지지 않았다."], ["And this was what prevented him.", "그리고 그것을 가로막은 것은 다음과 같은 일이었다."]], [["Captain Boldwig was a little fierce man in a stiff black neckerchief and blue surtout, who, when he did condescend to walk about his property, did it in company with a thick rattan stick with a brass ferrule, and a gardener and sub-gardener with meek faces, to whom (the gardeners, not the stick) Captain Boldwig gave his orders with all due grandeur and ferocity; for Captain Boldwig’s wife’s sister had married a marquis, and the captain’s house was a villa, and his land ‘grounds,’ and it was all very high, and mighty, and great.", "볼드위그 대위는 빳빳한 검은 목도리와 푸른 프록코트 차림의 자그마하고 사나운 사내였는데, 황송하게도 자기 영지를 거닐 때면 놋쇠 물미를 댄 굵은 등나무 지팡이와 함께, 그리고 온순한 얼굴을 한 정원사와 보조 정원사를 거느리고 다녔으며, 볼드위그 대위는 그들에게(지팡이가 아니라 정원사들에게) 온갖 위엄과 사나움을 갖추어 명령을 내렸다. 볼드위그 대위 부인의 언니가 후작과 결혼했고, 대위의 집은 별장이며, 그의 땅은 \"장원\"이고, 그러니 그것이 다 대단하고 위세 등등하고 굉장한 것이었기 때문이다."]], [["Mr.", "픽윅 씨가 잠든 지 반 시간도 채 안 되었을 때, 자그마한 볼드위그 대위가 두 정원사를 거느리고, 자기 덩치와 위신이 허락하는 한 빠른 걸음으로 성큼성큼 걸어왔다."], ["Pickwick had not been asleep half an hour when little Captain Boldwig, followed by the two gardeners, came striding along as fast as his size and importance would let him; and when he came near the oak tree, Captain Boldwig paused and drew a long breath, and looked at the prospect as if he thought the prospect ought to be highly gratified at having him to take notice of it; and then he struck the ground emphatically with his stick, and summoned the head-gardener.", "그리고 떡갈나무 가까이에 이르자 볼드위그 대위는 걸음을 멈추고 길게 숨을 들이쉬며, 마치 이 경치가 자기 같은 사람의 주목을 받게 된 것을 더없이 흡족해해야 마땅하다고 여기는 양 경치를 바라보았다. 그러고는 지팡이로 땅을 힘주어 내리치고 우두머리 정원사를 불렀다."]], [["‘Hunt,’ said Captain Boldwig.", "\"헌트.\" 볼드위그 대위가 말했다."]], [["‘Yes, Sir,’ said the gardener.", "\"네, 나리.\" 정원사가 말했다."]], [["‘Roll this place to-morrow morning--do you hear, Hunt?’", "\"내일 아침 이곳을 골라 다지게 — 알겠나, 헌트?\""]], [["‘Yes, Sir.’", "\"네, 나리.\""]], [["‘And take care that you keep this place in good order--do you hear, Hunt?’", "\"그리고 이곳을 잘 관리하도록 신경 써 — 알겠나, 헌트?\""]], [["‘Yes, Sir.’", "\"네, 나리.\""]], [["‘And remind me to have a board done about trespassers, and spring guns, and all that sort of thing, to keep the common people out.", "\"그리고 무단출입과 자동 발사 총 따위에 관한 푯말을 세우라고 일러 주게, 천한 것들이 못 들어오게 말이야."], ["Do you hear, Hunt; do you hear?’", "알겠나, 헌트, 알겠어?\""]], [["‘I’ll not forget it, Sir.’", "\"잊지 않겠습니다, 나리.\""]], [["‘I beg your pardon, Sir,’ said the other man, advancing, with his hand to his hat.", "\"실례합니다, 나리.\" 다른 사내가 모자에 손을 갖다 대며 앞으로 나서서 말했다."]], [["‘Well, Wilkins, what’s the matter with you?’ said Captain Boldwig.", "\"무슨 일인가, 윌킨스, 뭐가 잘못됐나?\" 볼드위그 대위가 말했다."]], [["‘I beg your pardon, sir--but I think there have been trespassers here to-day.’", "\"실례합니다만, 나리 — 오늘 여기 무단출입자들이 있었던 것 같습니다.\""]], [["‘Ha!’ said the captain, scowling around him.", "\"하!\" 대위가 주위를 노려보며 말했다."]], [["‘Yes, sir--they have been dining here, I think, sir.’", "\"네, 나리 — 여기서 식사를 한 모양입니다, 나리.\""]], [["‘Why, damn their audacity, so they have,’ said Captain Boldwig, as the crumbs and fragments that were strewn upon the grass met his eye.", "\"이런, 저놈들의 뻔뻔함을 봐라, 정말 그랬군.\" 풀밭에 흩어진 빵 부스러기와 음식 찌꺼기가 그의 눈에 들어오자 볼드위그 대위가 말했다."], ["‘They have actually been devouring their food here.", "\"실제로 여기서 음식을 먹어 치웠어."], ["I wish I had the vagabonds here!’ said the captain, clenching the thick stick.", "그 부랑자 놈들을 여기서 잡았더라면!\" 대위가 굵은 지팡이를 움켜쥐며 말했다."]], [["‘I wish I had the vagabonds here,’ said the captain wrathfully.", "\"그 부랑자 놈들을 잡았더라면.\" 대위가 분노에 차 말했다."]], [["‘Beg your pardon, sir,’ said Wilkins, ‘but--’", "\"실례합니다, 나리.\" 윌킨스가 말했다. \"그런데—\""]], [["‘But what?", "\"그런데 뭐?"], ["Eh?’ roared the captain; and following the timid glance of Wilkins, his eyes encountered the wheel-barrow and Mr.", "응?\" 대위가 고함쳤다."], ["Pickwick.", "그리고 윌킨스의 겁먹은 시선을 좇아가던 그의 눈이 외바퀴 손수레와 픽윅 씨와 마주쳤다."]], [["‘Who are you, you rascal?’ said the captain, administering several pokes to Mr.", "\"넌 누구냐, 이 악당아?\" 대위가 굵은 지팡이로 픽윅 씨의 몸을 몇 차례 쿡쿡 찌르며 말했다."], ["Pickwick’s body with the thick stick. ‘What’s your name?’", "\"네 이름이 뭐야?\""]], [["‘Cold punch,’ murmured Mr.", "\"차가운 펀치.\""], ["Pickwick, as he sank to sleep again.", "픽윅 씨가 다시 잠 속으로 가라앉으며 중얼거렸다."]], [["‘What?’ demanded Captain Boldwig.", "\"뭐라고?\" 볼드위그 대위가 다그쳤다."]], [["No reply.", "대답이 없었다."]], [["‘What did he say his name was?’ asked the captain.", "\"제 이름이 뭐라고 하던가?\" 대위가 물었다."]], [["‘Punch, I think, sir,’ replied Wilkins.", "\"펀치라고 한 것 같습니다, 나리.\" 윌킨스가 대답했다."]], [["‘That’s his impudence--that’s his confounded impudence,’ said Captain Boldwig.", "\"저게 저놈의 무례함이야 — 저게 저 빌어먹을 무례함이라고.\" 볼드위그 대위가 말했다."], ["‘He’s only feigning to be asleep now,’ said the captain, in a high passion.", "\"지금 그저 잠든 척하는 거야.\" 대위가 격분하여 말했다."], ["‘He’s drunk; he’s a drunken plebeian.", "\"취했어, 술 취한 천민이야."], ["Wheel him away, Wilkins, wheel him away directly.’", "밀어 내, 윌킨스, 당장 밀어 내라고.\""]], [["Where shall I wheel him to, sir?’ inquired Wilkins, with great timidity.", "\"어디로 밀고 갈까요, 나리?\" 윌킨스가 몹시 겁먹은 채 물었다."]], [["‘Wheel him to the devil,’ replied Captain Boldwig.", "\"악마한테로 밀고 가.\" 볼드위그 대위가 대답했다."]], [["‘Very well, sir,’ said Wilkins.", "\"알겠습니다, 나리.\" 윌킨스가 말했다."]], [["‘Stay,’ said the captain.", "\"잠깐.\" 대위가 말했다."]], [["Wilkins stopped accordingly.", "윌킨스가 그에 따라 멈춰 섰다."]], [["‘Wheel him,’ said the captain--‘wheel him to the pound; and let us see whether he calls himself Punch when he comes to himself.", "\"이놈을 밀어다가.\" 대위가 말했다."], ["He shall not bully me--he shall not bully me.", "\"유치장으로 밀어다 넣어. 그리고 제정신이 들었을 때도 제 이름을 펀치라고 하는지 어디 두고 보자고."], ["Wheel him away.’", "감히 날 윽박지르려 들다니 — 어림없지, 날 윽박질러? 밀어 내.\""]], [["Away Mr. Pickwick was wheeled in compliance with this imperious mandate; and the great Captain Boldwig, swelling with indignation, proceeded on his walk.", "이 위압적인 명령에 따라 픽윅 씨는 밀려갔고, 위대한 볼드위그 대위는 분개로 부풀어 오른 채 산책을 계속했다."]], [["Inexpressible was the astonishment of the little party when they returned, to find that Mr. Pickwick had disappeared, and taken the wheel-barrow with him.", "저 작은 일행이 돌아와서 픽윅 씨가 외바퀴 손수레를 끌고 사라져 버린 것을 알았을 때의 경악이란 이루 말로 다 할 수 없었다."], ["It was the most mysterious and unaccountable thing that was ever heard of.", "그것은 일찍이 들어 본 적 없는 더없이 불가사의하고 설명할 길 없는 일이었다."], ["For a lame man to have got upon his legs without any previous notice, and walked off, would have been most extraordinary; but when it came to his wheeling a heavy barrow before him, by way of amusement, it grew positively miraculous.", "다리를 저는 사람이 아무런 예고도 없이 두 다리로 일어나 걸어 가 버렸다는 것만 해도 더없이 놀라운 일이었을 텐데, 게다가 무거운 손수레를 앞세워 밀고 가며 그것을 소일거리로 삼았다는 데 이르러서는, 그야말로 기적이라 할 만했다."], ["They searched every nook and corner round, together and separately; they shouted, whistled, laughed, called--and all with the same result.", "그들은 주위의 구석구석을 함께 또 따로 뒤졌다. 소리치고 휘파람 불고 웃고 부르고 했으나 — 모두 한결같은 결과였다."], ["Mr. Pickwick was not to be found.", "픽윅 씨는 찾을 수가 없었다."], ["After some hours of fruitless search, they arrived at the unwelcome conclusion that they must go home without him.", "몇 시간을 헛되이 찾아 헤맨 끝에, 그들은 그를 두고 집에 돌아가야 한다는 달갑지 않은 결론에 이르렀다."]], [["Meanwhile Mr. Pickwick had been wheeled to the Pound, and safely deposited therein, fast asleep in the wheel-barrow, to the immeasurable delight and satisfaction not only of all the boys in the village, but three-fourths of the whole population, who had gathered round, in expectation of his waking.", "그러는 사이 픽윅 씨는 유치장으로 밀려가, 외바퀴 손수레 안에 곤히 잠든 채 그 안에 무사히 안치되었는데, 이는 마을의 모든 소년들뿐 아니라 그가 깨어나기를 기대하며 둘러 모여든 전체 주민의 4분의 3에게도 더없는 기쁨과 만족을 안겨 주었다."], ["If their most intense gratification had been awakened by seeing him wheeled in, how many hundredfold was their joy increased when, after a few indistinct cries of ‘Sam!’ he sat up in the barrow, and gazed with indescribable astonishment on the faces before him.", "그가 밀려 들어오는 것을 보고 그토록 강렬한 즐거움을 맛본 그들이었으니, 몇 마디 알아들을 수 없는 \"샘!\" 소리를 내뱉고 나서 그가 손수레 안에서 일어나 앉아 눈앞의 얼굴들을 형언할 수 없는 경악으로 바라보았을 때, 그들의 기쁨이 몇백 곱절로 커졌으랴."]], [["A general shout was of course the signal of his having woke up; and his involuntary inquiry of ‘What’s the matter?’ occasioned another, louder than the first, if possible.", "물론 그가 깨어난 것을 알리는 신호는 일제히 터진 함성이었고, 그의 무심코 던진 \"무슨 일이오?\"라는 물음은, 가능하다면 첫 번째보다 더 큰 또 한 차례의 함성을 불러일으켰다."]], [["‘Here’s a game!’ roared the populace.", "\"구경거리 났다!\" 군중이 떠들썩하게 외쳤다."]], [["‘Where am I?’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"내가 어디 있는 거요?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 외쳤다."]], [["‘In the pound,’ replied the mob.", "\"유치장이오.\" 군중이 대답했다."]], [["‘How came I here?", "\"내가 어쩌다 여기 왔지?"], ["What was I doing?", "내가 뭘 하고 있었나?"], ["Where was I brought from?’", "어디서 끌려온 거야?\""]], [["Boldwig!", "\"볼드위그!"], ["Captain Boldwig!’ was the only reply.", "볼드위그 대위!\"가 유일한 대답이었다."]], [["‘Let me out,’ cried Mr.", "\"내보내 주시오.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 외쳤다."], ["‘Where’s my servant?", "\"내 하인은 어디 있소?"], ["Where are my friends?’", "내 친구들은 어디 있냐고?\""]], [["‘You ain’t got no friends.", "\"당신한텐 친구 따위 없어. 와아!\""], ["Hurrah!’ Then there came a turnip, then a potato, and then an egg; with a few other little tokens of the playful disposition of the many-headed.", "그러더니 무 한 개가 날아오고, 이어 감자 한 개, 그다음엔 달걀 한 개가 날아왔으며, 그 밖에도 머리 여럿 달린 군중의 장난기 어린 성품을 보여 주는 자잘한 표시들이 몇 가지 더 날아왔다."]], [["How long this scene might have lasted, or how much Mr. Pickwick might have suffered, no one can tell, had not a carriage, which was driving swiftly by, suddenly pulled up, from whence there descended old Wardle and Sam Weller, the former of whom, in far less time than it takes to write it, if not to read it, had made his way to Mr.", "이 광경이 얼마나 오래 이어졌을지, 또 픽윅 씨가 얼마나 더 고초를 겪었을지는, 마침 빠르게 지나가던 마차 한 대가 갑자기 멈춰 서지 않았더라면 아무도 알 수 없었으리라."], ["Pickwick’s side, and placed him in the vehicle, just as the latter had concluded the third and last round of a single combat with the town-beadle.", "그 마차에서 워들 영감과 샘 웰러가 내렸는데, 전자는 — 읽는 데는 몰라도 적는 데 걸리는 시간보다 훨씬 짧은 새에 — 픽윅 씨 곁으로 길을 헤치고 나아가 그를 마차에 태웠다. 바로 그때 픽윅 씨는 마을 교구 관리와의 일대일 격투에서 세 번째이자 마지막 라운드를 막 끝낸 참이었다."]], [["‘Run to the justice’s!’ cried a dozen voices.", "\"치안판사한테 달려가!\" 열댓 명의 목소리가 외쳤다."]], [["‘Ah, run avay,’ said Mr.", "\"아, 달려가렴.\""], ["Weller, jumping up on the box.", "웰러 씨가 마부석으로 뛰어오르며 말했다."], ["‘Give my compliments--Mr. Veller’s compliments--to the justice, and tell him I’ve spiled his beadle, and that, if he’ll swear in a new ‘un, I’ll come back again to-morrow and spile him.", "\"내 인사 전해라 — 웰러 씨의 인사를 — 치안판사한테 전하고, 내가 그 양반 교구 관리를 망가뜨려 놨다고, 그리고 만약에 그가 새 놈을 하나 선서시켜 앉히면 내일 다시 와서 그놈도 망가뜨려 주겠다고 일러라."], ["Drive on, old feller.’", "몰아라, 이 영감님아.\""]], [["‘I’ll give directions for the commencement of an action for false imprisonment against this Captain Boldwig, directly I get to London,’ said Mr.", "\"런던에 도착하는 즉시 이 볼드위그 대위를 상대로 불법 감금 소송을 시작하라고 지시하겠습니다.\""], ["Pickwick, as soon as the carriage turned out of the town.", "마차가 마을을 벗어나자마자 픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘We were trespassing, it seems,’ said Wardle.", "\"우리가 무단출입을 한 모양이던데.\" 워들이 말했다."]], [["‘I don’t care,’ said Mr.", "\"상관없어요.\""], ["Pickwick, ‘I’ll bring the action.’", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"소송을 걸겠습니다.\""]], [["‘No, you won’t,’ said Wardle.", "\"아니, 안 그럴 걸세.\" 워들이 말했다."]], [["‘I will, by--’ But as there was a humorous expression in Wardle’s face, Mr.", "\"걸 겁니다, 맹세코—\" 그러나 워들의 얼굴에 익살스러운 표정이 어려 있는 것을 보고 픽윅 씨는 말을 멈추고 물었다."], ["Pickwick checked himself, and said, ‘Why not?’", "\"왜 안 된다는 거지요?\""]], [["‘Because,’ said old Wardle, half-bursting with laughter, ‘because they might turn on some of us, and say we had taken too much cold punch.’", "\"왜냐하면,\" 워들 영감이 웃음을 터뜨릴 듯 말했다. \"왜냐하면 그쪽에서 도리어 우리 중 몇몇한테 화살을 돌려, 우리가 차가운 펀치를 너무 많이 마셨다고 할지도 모르니까.\""]], [["Do what he would, a smile would come into Mr.", "아무리 안 그러려 해도 픽윅 씨의 얼굴에는 미소가 번지고 말았다."], ["Pickwick’s face; the smile extended into a laugh; the laugh into a roar; the roar became general.", "그 미소는 웃음으로 번지고, 웃음은 폭소로 번졌으며, 폭소는 모두에게로 번졌다."], ["So, to keep up their good-humour, they stopped at the first roadside tavern they came to, and ordered a glass of brandy-and-water all round, with a magnum of extra strength for Mr. Samuel Weller.", "그래서 그들은 그 좋은 기분을 이어 가려고, 처음 만난 길가의 선술집에 들러 모두에게 브랜디 워터 한 잔씩을, 그리고 새뮤얼 웰러 씨에게는 특별히 독한 큰 병 하나를 주문했다."]], [["CHAPTER XX. SHOWING HOW DODSON AND FOGG WERE MEN OF BUSINESS, AND THEIR CLERKS MEN OF PLEASURE; AND HOW AN AFFECTING INTERVIEW TOOK PLACE BETWEEN MR. WELLER AND HIS LONG-LOST PARENT; SHOWING ALSO WHAT CHOICE SPIRITS ASSEMBLED AT THE MAGPIE AND STUMP, AND WHAT A CAPITAL CHAPTER THE NEXT ONE WILL BE\nIn the ground-floor front of a dingy house, at the very farthest end of Freeman’s Court, Cornhill, sat the four clerks of Messrs. Dodson & Fogg, two of his Majesty’s attorneys of the courts of King’s Bench and Common Pleas at Westminster, and solicitors of the High Court of Chancery--the aforesaid clerks catching as favourable glimpses of heaven’s light and heaven’s sun, in the course of their daily labours, as a man might hope to do, were he placed at the bottom of a reasonably deep well; and without the opportunity of perceiving the stars in the day-time, which the latter secluded situation affords.", "콘힐의 프리먼스 코트 맨 끝, 어느 우중충한 집의 일층 앞방에는 도드슨 앤드 포그 사무소의 사무원 네 사람이 앉아 있었으니, 이들은 웨스트민스터의 국왕좌부 법원과 민사 법원에 소속된 폐하의 변호사 두 분이자 고등 형평법원의 사무 변호사들이었다—앞서 말한 사무원들로 말하자면, 매일같이 일하는 동안 하늘의 빛과 하늘의 해를 어쩌다 엿보는 정도가, 적당히 깊은 우물 바닥에 놓인 사람이 바랄 법한 만큼에 지나지 않았으며, 더구나 그 깊숙한 자리라면 한낮에도 별을 볼 기회가 있으련만 그런 기회조차 없었다."]], [["The clerks’ office of Messrs. Dodson & Fogg was a dark, mouldy, earthy- smelling room, with a high wainscotted partition to screen the clerks from the vulgar gaze, a couple of old wooden chairs, a very loud-ticking clock, an almanac, an umbrella-stand, a row of hat-pegs, and a few shelves, on which were deposited several ticketed bundles of dirty papers, some old deal boxes with paper labels, and sundry decayed stone ink bottles of various shapes and sizes.", "도드슨 앤드 포그 사무소의 사무원실은 어둡고 곰팡내 나는, 흙냄새 풍기는 방으로, 사무원들을 속된 시선으로부터 가려 주는 높은 판벽 칸막이와 낡은 나무 의자 두 개, 몹시 시끄럽게 똑딱거리는 시계 하나, 달력 한 부, 우산꽂이 하나, 모자걸이 한 줄, 그리고 선반 몇 개가 있었는데, 그 선반에는 꼬리표를 단 더러운 서류 묶음 여러 개와 종이 라벨이 붙은 낡은 전나무 상자 몇 개, 그리고 갖가지 모양과 크기의 삭아 빠진 돌 잉크병 여럿이 놓여 있었다."], ["There was a glass door leading into the passage which formed the entrance to the court, and on the outer side of this glass door, Mr. Pickwick, closely followed by Sam Weller, presented himself on the Friday morning succeeding the occurrence of which a faithful narration is given in the last chapter.", "그 안뜰로 들어가는 입구를 이루는 복도로 통하는 유리문이 하나 있었는데, 지난 장에서 충실히 서술한 사건이 있고 난 뒤 금요일 아침, 픽윅 씨가 샘 웰러를 바짝 뒤에 거느린 채 그 유리문 바깥쪽에 모습을 드러냈다."]], [["‘Come in, can’t you!’ cried a voice from behind the partition, in reply to Mr.", "\"들어와요, 못 들어와!\""], ["Pickwick’s gentle tap at the door. And Mr.", "픽윅 씨가 문을 가볍게 두드리자 칸막이 뒤에서 한 목소리가 외쳤다."], ["Pickwick and Sam entered accordingly.", "그리하여 픽윅 씨와 샘은 안으로 들어갔다."]], [["‘Mr. Dodson or Mr.", "\"도드슨 씨나 포그 씨 계십니까?\""], ["Fogg at home, sir?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick, gently, advancing, hat in hand, towards the partition.", "픽윅 씨가 모자를 손에 든 채 칸막이 쪽으로 다가가며 점잖게 물었다."]], [["‘Mr. Dodson ain’t at home, and Mr.", "\"도드슨 씨는 안 계시고, 포그 씨는 특별히 바쁘십니다.\""], ["Fogg’s particularly engaged,’ replied the voice; and at the same time the head to which the voice belonged, with a pen behind its ear, looked over the partition, and at Mr.", "그 목소리가 대답했다."], ["Pickwick.", "그와 동시에 그 목소리의 주인인 머리통이, 귀 뒤에 펜을 꽂은 채 칸막이 너머로 픽윅 씨를 내다보았다."]], [["It was a ragged head, the sandy hair of which, scrupulously parted on one side, and flattened down with pomatum, was twisted into little semi- circular tails round a flat face ornamented with a pair of small eyes, and garnished with a very dirty shirt collar, and a rusty black stock.", "그것은 텁수룩한 머리통이었는데, 모래빛 머리카락은 한쪽으로 정성스레 가르마를 타 포마드로 납작하게 눌러 붙였고, 작은 두 눈으로 장식되고 몹시 더러운 셔츠 깃과 녹슨 듯한 검정 목도리로 마무리된 납작한 얼굴 둘레로 작은 반원형 꼬랑지처럼 비틀려 말려 있었다."]], [["‘Mr. Dodson ain’t at home, and Mr.", "\"도드슨 씨는 안 계시고, 포그 씨는 특별히 바쁘십니다.\""], ["Fogg’s particularly engaged,’ said the man to whom the head belonged.", "그 머리통의 주인이 말했다."]], [["‘When will Mr. Dodson be back, sir?’ inquired Mr.", "\"도드슨 씨는 언제쯤 돌아오십니까?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘Can’t say.’", "\"몰라요.\""]], [["‘Will it be long before Mr. Fogg is disengaged, Sir?’", "\"포그 씨가 한가해지려면 오래 걸리겠습니까?\""]], [["‘Don’t know.’", "\"모르죠.\""]], [["Here the man proceeded to mend his pen with great deliberation, while another clerk, who was mixing a Seidlitz powder, under cover of the lid of his desk, laughed approvingly.", "여기서 그 사내는 자기 펜을 아주 느긋하게 고치기 시작했고, 그동안 책상 뚜껑에 가려진 채 자이들리츠 가루약을 타고 있던 또 다른 사무원이 동의하듯 웃었다."]], [["‘I think I’ll wait,’ said Mr. Pickwick.", "\"기다려야겠군요.\""], ["There was no reply; so Mr.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick sat down unbidden, and listened to the loud ticking of the clock and the murmured conversation of the clerks.", "아무 대답도 없었으므로, 픽윅 씨는 권하지도 않았는데 자리에 앉아 시계의 요란한 똑딱거림과 사무원들의 소곤대는 대화에 귀를 기울였다."]], [["‘That was a game, wasn’t it?’ said one of the gentlemen, in a brown coat and brass buttons, inky drabs, and bluchers, at the conclusion of some inaudible relation of his previous evening’s adventures.", "\"한판 제대로였지, 안 그래?\" 갈색 외투에 놋쇠 단추를 달고, 잉크 얼룩이 진 누런 바지에 단화를 신은 신사들 가운데 하나가, 들리지 않는 전날 저녁 모험담을 마무리 지으면서 말했다."]], [["‘Devilish good--devilish good,’ said the Seidlitz-powder man.", "\"끝내줬지—끝내줬어.\" 자이들리츠 가루약을 타던 사내가 말했다."]], [["‘Tom Cummins was in the chair,’ said the man with the brown coat.", "\"톰 커민스가 의장석에 앉았었어.\" 갈색 외투의 사내가 말했다."], ["‘It was half-past four when I got to Somers Town, and then I was so uncommon lushy, that I couldn’t find the place where the latch-key went in, and was obliged to knock up the old ‘ooman.", "\"내가 서머스 타운에 닿았을 때가 네 시 반이었는데, 그때쯤엔 어찌나 곤드레만드레였던지 열쇠 구멍 자리도 못 찾아서 결국 노친네를 깨워야 했지 뭐야."], ["I say, I wonder what old Fogg ‘ud say, if he knew it.", "그나저나 영감 포그가 이 일을 알면 뭐라 할지 궁금하군."], ["I should get the sack, I s’pose--eh?’", "잘리겠지, 아마—응?\""]], [["At this humorous notion, all the clerks laughed in concert.", "이 우스운 생각에 사무원들이 일제히 웃음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["‘There was such a game with Fogg here, this mornin’,’ said the man in the brown coat, ‘while Jack was upstairs sorting the papers, and you two were gone to the stamp-office.", "\"오늘 아침에 여기서 포그 영감이랑 한바탕 제대로 있었지.\" 갈색 외투의 사내가 말했다."], ["Fogg was down here, opening the letters when that chap as we issued the writ against at Camberwell, you know, came in--what’s his name again?’", "\"잭이 위층에서 서류 정리를 하고, 너희 둘은 인지 사무소에 가 있던 동안 말이야. 포그가 여기 내려와서 편지를 뜯고 있는데, 왜 우리가 캠버웰에서 영장 발부한 그 친구 있잖아, 그 사람이 들어온 거야—이름이 뭐였더라?\""]], [["‘Ramsey,’ said the clerk who had spoken to Mr.", "\"램지.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨에게 말을 건넸던 사무원이 말했다."]], [["‘Ah, Ramsey--a precious seedy-looking customer.", "\"아, 램지—꽤나 추레하게 생긴 손님이지."], ["“Well, sir,”", "'자, 선생,'"], ["says old Fogg, looking at him very fierce--you know his way--“well, Sir, have you come to settle?”", "포그 영감이 그를 아주 사납게 노려보며 말하는 거야—그 양반 버릇 알잖아—'자, 선생, 갚으러 오셨소?'"], ["“Yes, I have, sir,”", "'예, 그렇습니다, 선생.'"], ["said Ramsey, putting his hand in his pocket, and bringing out the money, “the debt’s two pound ten, and the costs three pound five, and here it is, Sir;”", "램지가 주머니에 손을 넣어 돈을 꺼내며 말했지. '빚이 2파운드 10실링이고, 비용이 3파운드 5실링, 여기 있습니다, 선생.'"], ["and he sighed like bricks, as he lugged out the money, done up in a bit of blotting-paper.", "그러면서 압지 조각에 싸인 돈을 끌어내는데, 벽돌 무너지듯 한숨을 푹푹 쉬더라고."], ["Old Fogg looked first at the money, and then at him, and then he coughed in his rum way, so that I knew something was coming.", "포그 영감이 먼저 돈을 보고, 다음엔 그 사람을 보고, 그러더니 그 묘한 식으로 기침을 하는 거야."], ["“You don’t know there’s a declaration filed, which increases the costs materially, I suppose,”", "그래서 나는 뭔가 일이 벌어지겠구나 했지. '소장이 접수되어 비용이 상당히 불어난 걸 모르나 보군.'"], ["said Fogg. “You don’t say that, sir,”", "포그가 말하니까, '그럴 리가요, 선생.'"], ["said Ramsey, starting back; “the time was only out last night, Sir.”", "램지가 흠칫 물러서며 말하더군. '기한이 어젯밤에야 끝났는데요, 선생.'"], ["“I do say it, though,”", "'내 말이 맞소.'"], ["said Fogg, “my clerk’s just gone to file it.", "포그가 말했지. '내 사무원이 방금 접수하러 갔거든."], ["Hasn’t Mr. Jackson gone to file that declaration in Bullman and Ramsey, Mr.", "위크스 군, 잭슨 군이 불먼 대 램지 건 소장을 접수하러 가지 않았나?'"], ["Wicks?” Of course I said yes, and then Fogg coughed again, and looked at Ramsey.", "물론 나는 그렇다고 했고, 그러자 포그가 다시 기침을 하고 램지를 보았어."], ["“My God!”", "'맙소사!'"], ["said Ramsey; “and here have I nearly driven myself mad, scraping this money together, and all to no purpose.”", "램지가 말하더군. '이 돈 긁어모으느라 미칠 지경이었는데, 죄다 헛수고였단 말이오.'"], ["“None at all,”", "'그렇소, 죄다 헛수고지.'"], ["said Fogg coolly; “so you had better go back and scrape some more together, and bring it here in time.”", "포그가 냉정하게 말했어. '그러니 돌아가서 더 긁어모아서 제때 가져오는 게 좋겠소.'"], ["“I can’t get it, by God!”", "'도저히 못 구하겠소, 맙소사!'"], ["said Ramsey, striking the desk with his fist.", "램지가 주먹으로 책상을 치며 말했지. '나한테 윽박지르지 마시오, 선생.'"], ["“Don’t bully me, sir,”", "포그가 일부러 발끈하면서 말했어."], ["said Fogg, getting into a passion on purpose.", "'윽박지르는 게 아닙니다, 선생.' 램지가 말했지."], ["“I am not bullying you, sir,”", "'윽박지르고 있잖소.' 포그가 말했어."], ["said Ramsey. “You are,”", "'나가시오, 선생."], ["said Fogg; “get out, sir; get out of this office, Sir, and come back, Sir, when you know how to behave yourself.”", "이 사무실에서 나가시오, 선생. 그리고 처신을 어떻게 해야 하는지 알게 되거든 다시 오시오, 선생.'"], ["Well, Ramsey tried to speak, but Fogg wouldn’t let him, so he put the money in his pocket, and sneaked out.", "자, 램지가 뭐라 말하려 했지만 포그가 도무지 틈을 안 주니, 결국 돈을 도로 주머니에 넣고 슬그머니 나갔지."], ["The door was scarcely shut, when old Fogg turned round to me, with a sweet smile on his face, and drew the declaration out of his coat pocket.", "문이 채 닫히기도 전에 포그 영감이 얼굴 가득 달콤한 미소를 띠고 나를 돌아보더니, 외투 주머니에서 소장을 꺼내는 거야."], ["“Here, Wicks,”", "'자, 위크스,'"], ["says Fogg, “take a cab, and go down to the Temple as quick as you can, and file that.", "포그가 말하길, '마차 잡아타고 되도록 빨리 템플로 내려가서 이걸 접수하게. 비용은 떼일 염려 없어."], ["The costs are quite safe, for he’s a steady man with a large family, at a salary of five-and-twenty shillings a week, and if he gives us a warrant of attorney, as he must in the end, I know his employers will see it paid; so we may as well get all we can get out of him, Mr.", "그 사람은 식솔이 많고 주급 25실링 받는 착실한 사람이거든. 결국엔 어쩔 수 없이 위임장을 써 줄 텐데, 그러면 고용주들이 분명히 갚도록 해 줄 거야."], ["Wicks; it’s a Christian act to do it, Mr.", "그러니 우리는 받아낼 수 있는 건 죄다 받아내는 게 좋단 말일세, 위크스 군."], ["Wicks, for with his large family and small income, he’ll be all the better for a good lesson against getting into debt--won’t he, Mr.", "이건 기독교인다운 처사야, 위크스 군. 식솔은 많고 벌이는 적으니, 빚지지 말라는 따끔한 교훈을 받는 편이 그 사람한테도 외려 이로울 테니까—안 그런가, 위크스 군, 안 그래?'—그러고는 어찌나 사람 좋게 웃으며 나가던지, 보기만 해도 흐뭇하더라니까."], ["Wicks, won’t he?”-- and he smiled so good-naturedly as he went away, that it was delightful to see him.", "정말이지 일에 밝은 분이라니까.\""], ["He is a capital man of business,’ said Wicks, in a tone of the deepest admiration, ‘capital, isn’t he?’", "위크스가 더없이 깊은 감탄이 어린 어조로 말했다. \"기막힌 분 아냐, 응?\""]], [["The other three cordially subscribed to this opinion, and the anecdote afforded the most unlimited satisfaction.", "나머지 세 사람도 이 의견에 진심으로 동조했고, 그 일화는 더없이 한량없는 만족을 안겨 주었다."]], [["‘Nice men these here, Sir,’ whispered Mr.", "\"여기 사람들 멋지죠, 나리.\""], ["Weller to his master; ‘wery nice notion of fun they has, Sir.’", "웰러 씨가 주인에게 속삭였다. \"놀음에 대해선 아주 멋진 생각을 가졌네요, 나리.\""]], [["Mr. Pickwick nodded assent, and coughed to attract the attention of the young gentlemen behind the partition, who, having now relaxed their minds by a little conversation among themselves, condescended to take some notice of the stranger.", "픽윅 씨는 고개를 끄덕여 동의를 표하고는, 칸막이 뒤 젊은 신사들의 주의를 끌려고 기침을 했는데, 이제 자기들끼리 잠시 잡담을 나누며 머리를 식힌 그들은 그 낯선 사람에게 어느 정도 관심을 두는 것을 너그러이 허락했다."]], [["‘I wonder whether Fogg’s disengaged now?’ said Jackson.", "\"포그가 이제 한가한지 모르겠군.\" 잭슨이 말했다."]], [["‘I’ll see,’ said Wicks, dismounting leisurely from his stool.", "\"내가 보고 오지.\" 위크스가 의자에서 느릿느릿 내려오며 말했다."], ["‘What name shall I tell Mr. Fogg?’", "\"포그 씨한테 성함을 뭐라고 전할까요?\""]], [["‘Pickwick,’ replied the illustrious subject of these memoirs.", "\"픽윅이오.\" 이 회고록의 저명한 주인공이 대답했다."]], [["Mr. Jackson departed upstairs on his errand, and immediately returned with a message that Mr. Fogg would see Mr. Pickwick in five minutes; and having delivered it, returned again to his desk.", "잭슨 씨가 그 심부름으로 위층에 올라갔다가 곧장 돌아와, 포그 씨가 오 분 뒤에 픽윅 씨를 만나겠다는 전갈을 전하고는, 그 말을 전한 뒤 다시 자기 책상으로 돌아갔다."]], [["‘What did he say his name was?’ whispered Wicks.", "\"이름이 뭐랬더라?\" 위크스가 속삭였다."]], [["‘Pickwick,’ replied Jackson; ‘it’s the defendant in Bardell and Pickwick.’", "\"픽윅.\" 잭슨이 대답했다. \"바델 대 픽윅 건의 피고야.\""]], [["A sudden scraping of feet, mingled with the sound of suppressed laughter, was heard from behind the partition.", "칸막이 뒤에서 발을 끄는 소리가 별안간 나더니, 억눌린 웃음소리와 뒤섞여 들려왔다."]], [["‘They’re a-twiggin’ of you, Sir,’ whispered Mr.", "\"저 사람들이 나리를 훑어보고 있어요, 나리.\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 속삭였다."]], [["‘Twigging of me, Sam!’ replied Mr.", "\"날 훑어봐, 샘!\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다."], ["Pickwick; ‘what do you mean by twigging me?’", "\"날 훑어본다는 게 무슨 말이냐?\""]], [["Mr. Weller replied by pointing with his thumb over his shoulder, and Mr.", "웰러 씨는 어깨너머로 엄지손가락을 들어 가리키는 것으로 대답을 대신했고, 픽윅 씨가 고개를 들어 보니, 사무원 넷 모두가 더없이 즐거워하는 표정으로 나무 칸막이 너머로 머리를 쑥 내민 채, 여성의 마음을 농락하고 여성의 행복을 어지럽혔다고 여겨지는 자의 풍채와 전반적 외양을 샅샅이 뜯어보고 있다는 흐뭇한 사실을 깨달았다."], ["Pickwick, on looking up, became sensible of the pleasing fact, that all the four clerks, with countenances expressive of the utmost amusement, and with their heads thrust over the wooden screen, were minutely inspecting the figure and general appearance of the supposed trifler with female hearts, and disturber of female happiness. On his looking up, the row of heads suddenly disappeared, and the sound of pens travelling at a furious rate over paper, immediately succeeded.", "그가 고개를 들자 그 한 줄로 늘어선 머리통들이 별안간 사라지고, 곧이어 펜이 종이 위를 맹렬한 속도로 내달리는 소리가 뒤따랐다."]], [["A sudden ring at the bell which hung in the office, summoned Mr. Jackson to the apartment of Fogg, from whence he came back to say that he (Fogg) was ready to see Mr. Pickwick if he would step upstairs.", "사무실에 매달린 종이 별안간 울리자 잭슨 씨가 포그의 방으로 불려 갔고, 그는 거기서 돌아와 그분(포그)이 위층으로 올라오시면 픽윅 씨를 만날 준비가 되었다고 전했다."]], [["Upstairs Mr. Pickwick did step accordingly, leaving Sam Weller below.", "이에 픽윅 씨는 샘 웰러를 아래에 남겨 둔 채 위층으로 올라갔다."], ["The room door of the one-pair back, bore inscribed in legible characters the imposing words, ‘Mr.", "한 층 위 뒤편 방문에는 알아보기 쉬운 글자로 '포그 씨'라는 위풍당당한 글귀가 적혀 있었다."], ["Fogg’; and, having tapped thereat, and been desired to come in, Jackson ushered Mr. Pickwick into the presence.", "그 문을 두드리고 들어오라는 말을 듣자, 잭슨은 픽윅 씨를 그 앞으로 안내했다."]], [["‘Is Mr.", "\"도드슨 씨 계신가?\""], ["Dodson in?’ inquired Mr. Fogg.", "포그 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘Just come in, Sir,’ replied Jackson.", "\"방금 들어오셨습니다, 선생님.\" 잭슨이 대답했다."]], [["‘Ask him to step here.’", "\"이리 좀 오시라 하게.\""]], [["‘Yes, sir.’ Exit Jackson.", "\"예, 선생님.\" 잭슨 퇴장."]], [["‘Take a seat, sir,’ said Fogg; ‘there is the paper, sir; my partner will be here directly, and we can converse about this matter, sir.’", "\"앉으시지요, 선생.\" 포그가 말했다. \"신문이 여기 있습니다, 선생. 제 동업자가 곧 올 테니, 이 일에 관해 이야기를 나눌 수 있을 겁니다, 선생.\""]], [["Mr.", "픽윅 씨는 자리에 앉아 신문을 집어 들었으나, 그것을 읽는 대신 신문 위로 흘끔 넘겨다보며 그 일에 밝은 사내를 살폈다."], ["Pickwick took a seat and the paper, but, instead of reading the latter, peeped over the top of it, and took a survey of the man of business, who was an elderly, pimply-faced, vegetable-diet sort of man, in a black coat, dark mixture trousers, and small black gaiters; a kind of being who seemed to be an essential part of the desk at which he was writing, and to have as much thought or feeling.", "그는 여드름투성이 얼굴에 채식이나 할 법한 나이 든 사내로, 검정 외투에 어두운 혼방 바지를 입고 작은 검정 각반을 차고 있었으니, 그가 글을 쓰고 있는 책상의 없어서는 안 될 일부인 듯, 그 책상만큼이나 생각도 감정도 없어 보이는 부류의 인간이었다."]], [["After a few minutes’ silence, Mr. Dodson, a plump, portly, stern-looking man, with a loud voice, appeared; and the conversation commenced.", "몇 분간의 침묵 끝에, 포동포동하고 풍채 좋으며 근엄한 얼굴에 목소리가 우렁찬 도드슨 씨가 나타났고, 대화가 시작되었다."]], [["‘This is Mr.", "\"이분이 픽윅 씨요.\""], ["Pickwick,’ said Fogg.", "포그가 말했다."]], [["‘Ah!", "\"아!"], ["You are the defendant, Sir, in Bardell and Pickwick?’ said Dodson.", "당신이 바델 대 픽윅 건의 피고로군요, 선생?\" 도드슨이 말했다."]], [["‘I am, sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"그렇습니다, 선생.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘Well, sir,’ said Dodson, ‘and what do you propose?’", "\"자, 선생,\" 도드슨이 말했다. \"그래, 어떻게 하시겠다는 거요?\""]], [["‘Ah!’ said Fogg, thrusting his hands into his trousers’ pockets, and throwing himself back in his chair, ‘what do you propose, Mr Pickwick?’", "\"아!\" 포그가 두 손을 바지 주머니에 찔러 넣고 의자에 몸을 젖히며 말했다. \"어떻게 하시겠다는 겁니까, 픽윅 씨?\""]], [["‘Hush, Fogg,’ said Dodson, ‘let me hear what Mr.", "\"쉿, 포그.\" 도드슨이 말했다."], ["Pickwick has to say.’", "\"픽윅 씨가 할 말을 들어 봅시다.\""]], [["‘I came, gentlemen,’ said Mr.", "\"내가 여기 온 것은, 여러분,\""], ["Pickwick, gazing placidly on the two partners, ‘I came here, gentlemen, to express the surprise with which I received your letter of the other day, and to inquire what grounds of action you can have against me.’", "픽윅 씨가 두 동업자를 차분히 바라보며 말했다. \"내가 여기 온 것은, 여러분, 며칠 전 보내신 편지를 받고 느낀 놀라움을 표하고, 또 당신들이 나를 상대로 무슨 소송 근거를 가질 수 있는지 묻기 위해서요.\""]], [["‘Grounds of--’ Fogg had ejaculated this much, when he was stopped by Dodson.", "\"소송 근거라니—\" 포그가 여기까지 내뱉었을 때 도드슨이 그를 막았다."]], [["‘Mr.", "\"포그 군,\""], ["Fogg,’ said Dodson, ‘I am going to speak.’", "도드슨이 말했다. \"내가 말하겠네.\""]], [["I beg your pardon, Mr.", "\"실례했소, 도드슨 씨.\""], ["Dodson,’ said Fogg.", "포그가 말했다."]], [["‘For the grounds of action, sir,’ continued Dodson, with moral elevation in his air, ‘you will consult your own conscience and your own feelings. We, Sir, we, are guided entirely by the statement of our client. That statement, Sir, may be true, or it may be false; it may be credible, or it may be incredible; but, if it be true, and if it be credible, I do not hesitate to say, Sir, that our grounds of action, Sir, are strong, and not to be shaken. You may be an unfortunate man, Sir, or you may be a designing one; but if I were called upon, as a juryman upon my oath, Sir, to express an opinion of your conduct, Sir, I do not hesitate to assert that I should have but one opinion about it.’ Here Dodson drew himself up, with an air of offended virtue, and looked at Fogg, who thrust his hands farther in his pockets, and nodding his head sagely, said, in a tone of the fullest concurrence, ‘Most certainly.’", "\"소송 근거로 말할 것 같으면, 선생,\" 도드슨이 도덕적으로 고매한 기색을 띠며 말을 이었다. \"당신 자신의 양심과 당신 자신의 감정에 물어보시오. 우리로 말하면, 선생, 우리는 전적으로 의뢰인의 진술에 따를 뿐이오. 그 진술은, 선생, 참일 수도 거짓일 수도 있고, 믿을 만할 수도 믿지 못할 수도 있소. 허나 그것이 참이고 또 믿을 만한 것이라면, 나는 서슴없이 말하겠소, 선생, 우리의 소송 근거는, 선생, 탄탄하여 흔들리지 않는다고 말이오. 당신은 불운한 사람일 수도, 음흉한 사람일 수도 있소. 허나 내가 선서한 배심원으로서, 선생, 당신 행실에 대한 의견을 표하라는 요청을 받는다면, 선생, 나는 서슴없이 단언하건대 그에 대해 단 하나의 의견밖에 갖지 않을 거요.\" 여기서 도드슨은 모욕당한 미덕의 기색으로 몸을 곧추세우고 포그를 바라보았는데, 포그는 두 손을 주머니에 더 깊이 찔러 넣고 슬기로운 듯 고개를 끄덕이며 더없이 동조하는 어조로 말했다. \"두말할 나위 없지요.\""]], [["‘Well, Sir,’ said Mr.", "\"그렇다면, 선생,\""], ["Pickwick, with considerable pain depicted in his countenance, ‘you will permit me to assure you that I am a most unfortunate man, so far as this case is concerned.’", "픽윅 씨가 얼굴에 상당한 고통을 드러내며 말했다. \"당신들에게 한 가지 분명히 말해 두건대, 적어도 이 사건에 관한 한 나는 더없이 불운한 사람이오.\""]], [["‘I hope you are, Sir,’ replied Dodson; ‘I trust you may be, Sir.", "\"그러시기를 바라오, 선생.\" 도드슨이 대답했다."], ["If you are really innocent of what is laid to your charge, you are more unfortunate than I had believed any man could possibly be.", "\"그러시기를 진정 바라오, 선생. 당신이 받은 혐의에 대해 정말로 결백하다면, 당신은 내가 사람으로서 가능하리라 믿었던 것보다 더 불운한 사람이오."], ["What do you say, Mr. Fogg?’", "어떻게 생각하나, 포그 군?\""]], [["‘I say precisely what you say,’ replied Fogg, with a smile of incredulity.", "\"나는 당신이 말한 그대로 생각하오.\" 포그가 못 믿겠다는 미소를 띠며 대답했다."]], [["‘The writ, Sir, which commences the action,’ continued Dodson, ‘was issued regularly.", "\"소송을 개시하는 영장은, 선생,\" 도드슨이 말을 이었다."], ["Mr.", "\"정식으로 발부되었소."], ["Fogg, where is the Praecipe book?’", "포그 군, 그 소환 명령부가 어디 있나?\""]], [["‘Here it is,’ said Fogg, handing over a square book, with a parchment cover.", "\"여기 있소.\" 포그가 양피지 표지를 댄 네모난 장부를 건네며 말했다."]], [["‘Here is the entry,’ resumed Dodson.", "\"여기 기재가 있소.\" 도드슨이 다시 말했다."], ["‘“Middlesex, Capias MARTHA BARDELL, WIDOW, v.", "\"'미들섹스, 신병 구인. 과부 마사 바델, 새뮤얼 픽윅을 상대로."], ["SAMUEL PICKWICK. Damages £1500.", "손해 배상 1500파운드."], ["Dodson & Fogg for the plaintiff, Aug.", "원고 대리인 도드슨 앤드 포그, 1827년 8월 28일.' 모두 정식이오, 선생."], ["28, 1827.”", "완벽하게.\""], ["All regular, Sir; perfectly.’ Dodson coughed and looked at Fogg, who said ‘Perfectly,’ also.", "도드슨이 기침을 하고 포그를 바라보자, 포그 역시 \"완벽하게요.\" 하고 말했다."], ["And then they both looked at Mr. Pickwick.", "그러고 나서 두 사람 모두 픽윅 씨를 바라보았다."]], [["‘I am to understand, then,’ said Mr.", "\"그렇다면,\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick, ‘that it really is your intention to proceed with this action?’", "\"정말로 이 소송을 진행할 의사라는 것으로 이해하면 되겠소?\""]], [["‘Understand, sir!--that you certainly may,’ replied Dodson, with something as near a smile as his importance would allow.", "\"이해라니, 선생—그야 분명히 그렇게 이해하셔도 좋소.\" 도드슨이 자신의 위엄이 허락하는 한 미소에 가장 가까운 무엇을 지으며 대답했다."]], [["‘And that the damages are actually laid at fifteen hundred pounds?’ said Mr.", "\"그리고 손해 배상액이 실제로 천오백 파운드로 책정되었다는 거요?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘To which understanding you may add my assurance, that if we could have prevailed upon our client, they would have been laid at treble the amount, sir,’ replied Dodson.", "\"그 이해에 한 가지 더 보태드리자면, 우리가 의뢰인을 설득할 수만 있었다면 그 액수의 세 배로 책정되었을 거라는 점을 장담하오, 선생.\" 도드슨이 대답했다."]], [["‘I believe Mrs.", "\"하지만 바델 부인이 특별히 한 말씀이 있었지요.\""], ["Bardell specially said, however,’ observed Fogg, glancing at Dodson, ‘that she would not compromise for a farthing less.’", "포그가 도드슨을 흘끗 보며 말했다. \"단 한 푼이라도 적게는 절대 합의하지 않겠다고요.\""]], [["‘Unquestionably,’ replied Dodson sternly.", "\"두말할 나위 없소.\" 도드슨이 근엄하게 대답했다."], ["For the action was only just begun; and it wouldn’t have done to let Mr. Pickwick compromise it then, even if he had been so disposed.", "소송은 이제 막 시작되었을 뿐이었고, 설령 픽윅 씨가 그럴 마음이 있었다 한들 지금 그가 합의하도록 두는 것은 될 일이 아니었던 것이다."]], [["‘As you offer no terms, sir,’ said Dodson, displaying a slip of parchment in his right hand, and affectionately pressing a paper copy of it, on Mr.", "\"당신이 아무 조건도 제시하지 않으니, 선생,\" 도드슨이 오른손에 든 양피지 한 장을 펼쳐 보이며, 왼손으로는 그 종이 사본을 픽윅 씨에게 다정하게 들이밀면서 말했다."], ["Pickwick with his left, ‘I had better serve you with a copy of this writ, sir.", "\"이 영장의 사본을 송달하는 편이 낫겠소, 선생."], ["Here is the original, sir.’", "여기 원본이오, 선생.\""]], [["‘Very well, gentlemen, very well,’ said Mr.", "\"좋소, 여러분, 아주 좋소.\""], ["Pickwick, rising in person and wrath at the same time; ‘you shall hear from my solicitor, gentlemen.’", "픽윅 씨가 몸과 함께 노기까지 일으키며 말했다. \"내 사무 변호사로부터 연락이 갈 거요, 여러분.\""]], [["‘We shall be very happy to do so,’ said Fogg, rubbing his hands.", "\"기꺼이 받겠소이다.\" 포그가 두 손을 비비며 말했다."]], [["‘Very,’ said Dodson, opening the door.", "\"기꺼이.\" 도드슨이 문을 열며 말했다."]], [["‘And before I go, gentlemen,’ said the excited Mr.", "\"가기 전에 한마디 하겠소, 여러분,\""], ["Pickwick, turning round on the landing, ‘permit me to say, that of all the disgraceful and rascally proceedings--’", "흥분한 픽윅 씨가 층계참에서 돌아서며 말했다. \"온갖 수치스럽고 악랄한 처사 중에서도—\""]], [["‘Stay, sir, stay,’ interposed Dodson, with great politeness.", "\"잠깐, 선생, 잠깐만.\" 도드슨이 더없이 정중하게 끼어들었다."], ["‘Mr. Jackson!", "\"잭슨 군!"], ["Mr. Wicks!’", "위크스 군!\""]], [["‘Sir,’ said the two clerks, appearing at the bottom of the stairs.", "\"예, 선생님.\" 두 사무원이 계단 아래에 나타나 말했다."]], [["‘I merely want you to hear what this gentleman says,’ replied Dodson.", "\"이 신사분이 하는 말을 그저 자네들이 들어 두었으면 해서 그러네.\""], ["‘Pray, go on, sir--disgraceful and rascally proceedings, I think you said?’", "도드슨이 대답했다. \"어서 계속하시오, 선생—수치스럽고 악랄한 처사라 하셨던 것 같은데?\""]], [["‘I did,’ said Mr.", "\"그렇소.\""], ["Pickwick, thoroughly roused.", "픽윅 씨가 단단히 격분하여 말했다."], ["‘I said, Sir, that of all the disgraceful and rascally proceedings that ever were attempted, this is the most so.", "\"내가 말했소, 선생, 일찍이 꾀해진 온갖 수치스럽고 악랄한 처사 중에서도 이것이 가장 심하다고 말이오."], ["I repeat it, sir.’", "다시 한번 말하오, 선생.\""]], [["‘You hear that, Mr.", "\"들었지, 위크스 군.\""], ["Wicks,’ said Dodson.", "도드슨이 말했다."]], [["‘You won’t forget these expressions, Mr.", "\"이 표현들을 잊지 않겠지, 잭슨 군?\""], ["Jackson?’ said Fogg.", "포그가 말했다."]], [["‘Perhaps you would like to call us swindlers, sir,’ said Dodson.", "\"우리를 사기꾼이라 부르고 싶으실 테지요, 선생.\" 도드슨이 말했다."], ["‘Pray do, Sir, if you feel disposed; now pray do, Sir.’", "\"어서 그러시오, 선생, 그러고 싶으시거든. 자, 어서 그러시오, 선생.\""]], [["‘I do,’ said Mr.", "\"그러겠소.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["‘You are swindlers.’", "\"당신들은 사기꾼이오.\""]], [["‘Very good,’ said Dodson.", "\"아주 좋습니다.\" 도드슨이 말했다."], ["‘You can hear down there, I hope, Mr. Wicks?’", "\"거기서 잘 들리겠지, 위크스 군?\""]], [["‘Oh, yes, Sir,’ said Wicks.", "\"오, 예, 선생님.\" 위크스가 말했다."]], [["‘You had better come up a step or two higher, if you can’t,’ added Mr.", "\"잘 안 들리거든 한두 계단 더 올라오는 게 좋을 거야.\" 포그 씨가 덧붙였다."], ["Fogg. ‘Go on, Sir; do go on.", "\"계속하시오, 선생, 어서 계속하시오."], ["You had better call us thieves, Sir; or perhaps You would like to assault one of us.", "우리를 도둑놈이라 부르시는 게 좋겠소, 선생. 아니면 우리 둘 중 하나를 한 대 치고 싶으실 테지."], ["Pray do it, Sir, if you would; we will not make the smallest resistance.", "어서 그러시오, 선생, 그러고 싶으시거든. 우리는 조금도 저항하지 않겠소."], ["Pray do it, Sir.’", "어서 그러시오, 선생.\""]], [["As Fogg put himself very temptingly within the reach of Mr. Pickwick’s clenched fist, there is little doubt that that gentleman would have complied with his earnest entreaty, but for the interposition of Sam, who, hearing the dispute, emerged from the office, mounted the stairs, and seized his master by the arm.", "포그가 픽윅 씨의 움켜쥔 주먹 사정거리 안으로 아주 솔깃하게 제 몸을 들이밀었으니, 그 신사가 그 간곡한 청을 따랐으리라는 것은 거의 의심할 여지가 없었으나, 다툼 소리를 듣고 사무실에서 나와 계단을 올라온 샘이 주인의 팔을 붙든 덕분에 그 일은 일어나지 않았다."]], [["‘You just come away,’ said Mr.", "\"그냥 가시지요.\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 말했다."], ["‘Battledore and shuttlecock’s a wery good game, vhen you ain’t the shuttlecock and two lawyers the battledores, in which case it gets too excitin’ to be pleasant.", "\"배틀도어 앤드 셔틀콕도 좋은 놀이긴 합니다만, 나리가 셔틀콕이고 변호사 둘이 배틀도어 채일 때는 얘기가 다르죠. 그런 경우엔 너무 흥분돼서 도무지 즐겁질 않거든요."], ["Come avay, Sir.", "가시지요, 나리."], ["If you want to ease your mind by blowing up somebody, come out into the court and blow up me; but it’s rayther too expensive work to be carried on here.’", "누굴 한바탕 깨부숴서 속을 풀고 싶으시거든, 안뜰로 나가서 저를 깨부수십쇼. 하지만 여기서 그걸 계속하기엔 비용이 너무 많이 들거든요.\""]], [["And without the slightest ceremony, Mr. Weller hauled his master down the stairs, and down the court, and having safely deposited him in Cornhill, fell behind, prepared to follow whithersoever he should lead.", "그리고 웰러 씨는 조금의 예의도 차리지 않고 주인을 계단 아래로, 또 안뜰 밖으로 끌어내고는, 콘힐에 무사히 내려놓고 나서, 어디로 이끌든 따라갈 채비를 하고 뒤로 물러섰다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick walked on abstractedly, crossed opposite the Mansion House, and bent his steps up Cheapside.", "픽윅 씨는 멍하니 걸어 맨션 하우스 맞은편을 가로질러 칩사이드 쪽으로 발길을 옮겼다."], ["Sam began to wonder where they were going, when his master turned round, and said--", "샘이 대체 어디로 가는 건가 궁금해하기 시작할 무렵, 주인이 돌아서며 말했다—"]], [["‘Sam, I will go immediately to Mr. Perker’s.’", "\"샘, 곧장 퍼커 씨에게 가야겠다.\""]], [["‘That’s just exactly the wery place vere you ought to have gone last night, Sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"그게 바로 어젯밤 진작에 가셨어야 할 곳입니다, 나리.\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘I think it is, Sam,’ said Mr.", "\"그런 것 같구나, 샘.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘I know it is,’ said Mr.", "\"틀림없습니다, 나리.\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Well, well, Sam,’ replied Mr.", "\"그래, 그래, 샘.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다."], ["Pickwick, ‘we will go there at once; but first, as I have been rather ruffled, I should like a glass of brandy- and-water warm, Sam.", "\"당장 거기로 가자. 하지만 그전에, 내가 좀 심란해졌으니 따끈한 브랜디 물 한 잔을 마셨으면 싶구나, 샘."], ["Where can I have it, Sam?’", "어디서 마실 수 있겠느냐, 샘?\""]], [["Mr. Weller’s knowledge of London was extensive and peculiar.", "런던에 대한 웰러 씨의 지식은 폭넓고도 독특했다."], ["He replied, without the slightest consideration--", "그는 조금의 망설임도 없이 대답했다—"]], [["‘Second court on the right hand side--last house but vun on the same side the vay--take the box as stands in the first fireplace, ‘cos there ain’t no leg in the middle o’ the table, which all the others has, and it’s wery inconvenient.’", "\"오른편 두 번째 안뜰로 들어가서—같은 쪽 끝에서 두 번째 집입니다요—첫 번째 벽난로 옆에 있는 자리를 잡으십쇼. 거긴 다른 탁자들엔 다 있는 가운데 다리가 없어서, 그게 아주 불편하거든요.\""]], [["Mr. Pickwick observed his valet’s directions implicitly, and bidding Sam follow him, entered the tavern he had pointed out, where the hot brandy- and-water was speedily placed before him; while Mr.", "픽윅 씨는 시종의 지시를 고스란히 따랐고, 샘에게 따라오라 이르고는 그가 가리킨 선술집으로 들어갔는데, 그곳에서는 따끈한 브랜디 물이 곧장 그의 앞에 놓였다."], ["Weller, seated at a respectful distance, though at the same table with his master, was accommodated with a pint of porter.", "한편 웰러 씨는 주인과 같은 탁자에 앉되 공손하게 거리를 둔 자리에 앉아 포터 한 파인트를 대접받았다."]], [["The room was one of a very homely description, and was apparently under the especial patronage of stage-coachmen; for several gentleman, who had all the appearance of belonging to that learned profession, were drinking and smoking in the different boxes.", "그 방은 매우 소박한 부류였으며, 보아하니 역마차 마부들의 각별한 단골집인 듯했다. 그 학식 있는 직업에 속한 듯한 풍채의 신사 여럿이 여러 자리에서 술을 마시고 담배를 피우고 있었기 때문이다."], ["Among the number was one stout, red-faced, elderly man, in particular, seated in an opposite box, who attracted Mr. Pickwick’s attention.", "그 무리 중에서도 특히 맞은편 자리에 앉은, 풍채 좋고 얼굴이 붉으며 나이 든 사내 하나가 픽윅 씨의 주의를 끌었다."], ["The stout man was smoking with great vehemence, but between every half-dozen puffs, he took his pipe from his mouth, and looked first at Mr. Weller and then at Mr.", "그 풍채 좋은 사내는 몹시 격렬하게 담배를 피웠는데, 여섯 모금쯤 빨 때마다 한 번씩 입에서 파이프를 떼고는 먼저 웰러 씨를, 그다음엔 픽윅 씨를 바라보았다."], ["Pickwick. Then, he would bury in a quart pot, as much of his countenance as the dimensions of the quart pot admitted of its receiving, and take another look at Sam and Mr.", "그러고는 1쿼트들이 술 단지가 받아들일 수 있는 만큼 얼굴을 그 안에 파묻었다가, 다시 샘과 픽윅 씨를 한 번 바라보았다."], ["Pickwick. Then he would take another half-dozen puffs with an air of profound meditation and look at them again.", "그러고는 깊은 사색에 잠긴 기색으로 또 여섯 모금쯤 빨고는 그들을 다시 바라보았다."], ["At last the stout man, putting up his legs on the seat, and leaning his back against the wall, began to puff at his pipe without leaving off at all, and to stare through the smoke at the new-comers, as if he had made up his mind to see the most he could of them.", "마침내 그 풍채 좋은 사내는 두 다리를 자리에 올리고 등을 벽에 기대더니, 조금도 멈추지 않고 파이프를 빨아대며, 마치 이들을 최대한 자세히 봐 두기로 작정한 듯 연기 너머로 새로 온 사람들을 빤히 응시하기 시작했다."]], [["At first the evolutions of the stout man had escaped Mr. Weller’s observation, but by degrees, as he saw Mr.", "처음에는 그 풍채 좋은 사내의 동작이 웰러 씨의 눈에 띄지 않았으나, 차츰 주인 픽윅 씨의 눈이 이따금 그쪽으로 향하는 것을 보고는, 자기도 같은 방향을 바라보기 시작하면서 동시에 손으로 눈에 그늘을 만들었으니, 마치 앞에 있는 대상을 어렴풋이 알아보고 그 정체를 확실히 가려내려는 듯했다."], ["Pickwick’s eyes every now and then turning towards him, he began to gaze in the same direction, at the same time shading his eyes with his hand, as if he partially recognised the object before him, and wished to make quite sure of its identity.", "그러나 그의 의심은 곧 풀렸다."], ["His doubts were speedily dispelled, however; for the stout man having blown a thick cloud from his pipe, a hoarse voice, like some strange effort of ventriloquism, emerged from beneath the capacious shawls which muffled his throat and chest, and slowly uttered these sounds--‘Wy, Sammy!’", "풍채 좋은 사내가 파이프에서 짙은 연기 구름을 내뿜더니, 목과 가슴을 휘감은 넉넉한 숄 아래에서 무슨 기이한 복화술의 안간힘 같은 쉰 목소리가 새어 나와, 이런 소리를 느릿느릿 내뱉었기 때문이다—\"아니, 새미!\""]], [["‘Who’s that, Sam?’ inquired Mr.", "\"저 사람이 누구냐, 샘?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘Why, I wouldn’t ha’ believed it, Sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"이거 참, 믿기지가 않는군요, 나리.\""], ["Weller, with astonished eyes.", "웰러 씨가 놀란 눈으로 대답했다."], ["‘It’s the old ‘un.’", "\"우리 영감이네요.\""]], [["‘Old one,’ said Mr.", "\"영감이라니,\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["‘What old one?’", "\"무슨 영감 말이냐?\""]], [["‘My father, sir,’ replied Mr. Weller.", "\"제 아버지요, 나리.\""], ["‘How are you, my ancient?’ And with this beautiful ebullition of filial affection, Mr.", "웰러 씨가 대답했다. \"어떻게 지내셨어요, 노친네?\""], ["Weller made room on the seat beside him, for the stout man, who advanced pipe in mouth and pot in hand, to greet him.", "그리고 이 아름다운 효심의 분출과 함께 웰러 씨는 자기 옆자리를 비워 주었고, 풍채 좋은 사내는 파이프를 입에 물고 술 단지를 손에 든 채 그를 맞으러 다가왔다."]], [["‘Wy, Sammy,’ said the father, ‘I ha’n’t seen you, for two year and better.’", "\"아니, 새미,\" 아버지가 말했다. \"이 년 하고도 더 너를 못 봤구나.\""]], [["‘Nor more you have, old codger,’ replied the son.", "\"그러게요, 어쩌다 보니 그랬네요, 노친네.\" 아들이 대답했다."], ["‘How’s mother-in-law?’", "\"새어머니는 어때요?\""]], [["‘Wy, I’ll tell you what, Sammy,’ said Mr.", "\"이봐, 새미, 내 말 좀 들어 봐라.\" 웰러 영감이 자못 엄숙한 태도로 말했다."], ["Weller, senior, with much solemnity in his manner; ‘there never was a nicer woman as a widder, than that ‘ere second wentur o’ mine--a sweet creetur she was, Sammy; all I can say on her now, is, that as she was such an uncommon pleasant widder, it’s a great pity she ever changed her condition.", "\"과부로 말할 것 같으면 그 두 번째 마누라보다 더 괜찮은 여자는 없었지—참 사랑스러운 사람이었어, 새미. 지금 그 여자에 대해 내가 할 수 있는 말이라곤, 그토록 보기 드물게 상냥한 과부였으니 신세를 바꾼 게 참으로 애석하다는 것뿐이야."], ["She don’t act as a vife, Sammy.’", "마누라 노릇은 영 못 하거든, 새미.\""]], [["Don’t she, though?’ inquired Mr.", "\"정말요?\""], ["Weller, junior.", "웰러 군이 물었다."]], [["The elder Mr.", "웰러 영감은 고개를 절레절레 저으며 한숨과 함께 대답했다."], ["Weller shook his head, as he replied with a sigh, ‘I’ve done it once too often, Sammy; I’ve done it once too often.", "\"내가 한 번 더 저질러 버린 거야, 새미. 한 번 더 저지른 거지."], ["Take example by your father, my boy, and be wery careful o’ widders all your life, ‘specially if they’ve kept a public-house, Sammy.’ Having delivered this parental advice with great pathos, Mr.", "네 아비를 본보기 삼아라, 얘야. 그리고 평생 과부를 아주 조심하렷다."], ["Weller, senior, refilled his pipe from a tin box he carried in his pocket; and, lighting his fresh pipe from the ashes of the old One, commenced smoking at a great rate.", "특히 술집을 해 본 과부라면 더더욱이지, 새미.\" 이 아비다운 충고를 자못 비장하게 전한 뒤, 웰러 영감은 주머니에 지니고 다니던 양철통에서 파이프를 다시 채우고는, 묵은 파이프의 재로 새 파이프에 불을 붙여 맹렬한 속도로 담배를 피우기 시작했다."]], [["‘Beg your pardon, sir,’ he said, renewing the subject, and addressing Mr.", "\"실례합니다, 선생.\" 그는 한참 뜸을 들인 뒤 그 화제를 다시 꺼내며 픽윅 씨에게 말을 건넸다."], ["Pickwick, after a considerable pause, ‘nothin’ personal, I hope, sir; I hope you ha’n’t got a widder, sir.’", "\"별다른 뜻은 없습니다요, 선생. 선생께선 과부를 두고 계시진 않겠지요, 선생?\""]], [["‘Not I,’ replied Mr.", "\"아니요.\""], ["Pickwick, laughing; and while Mr.", "픽윅 씨가 웃으며 대답했다."], ["Pickwick laughed, Sam Weller informed his parent in a whisper, of the relation in which he stood towards that gentleman.", "픽윅 씨가 웃는 동안, 샘 웰러는 아버지에게 자기가 그 신사와 어떤 관계인지를 속삭여 일러 주었다."]], [["‘Beg your pardon, sir,’ said Mr.", "\"실례했습니다, 선생.\""], ["Weller, senior, taking off his hat, ‘I hope you’ve no fault to find with Sammy, Sir?’", "웰러 영감이 모자를 벗으며 말했다. \"새미한테 흠잡으실 일은 없겠지요, 선생?\""]], [["‘None whatever,’ said Mr.", "\"전혀 없소이다.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Wery glad to hear it, sir,’ replied the old man; ‘I took a good deal o’ pains with his eddication, sir; let him run in the streets when he was wery young, and shift for hisself. It’s the only way to make a boy sharp, sir.’", "\"그 말씀 들으니 참 기쁩니다요, 선생.\" 노인이 대답했다. \"제가 저 녀석 교육에 꽤나 공을 들였거든요, 선생. 아주 어렸을 때부터 거리를 쏘다니며 제 앞가림은 제가 하게 내버려뒀지요. 그게 사내아이를 영리하게 만드는 유일한 방법이거든요, 선생.\""]], [["‘Rather a dangerous process, I should imagine,’ said Mr.", "\"꽤나 위험한 방법인 듯싶군요.\""], ["Pickwick, with a smile.", "픽윅 씨가 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["‘And not a wery sure one, neither,’ added Mr.", "\"그렇다고 아주 확실한 방법도 아니죠.\" 웰러 씨가 덧붙였다."], ["Weller; ‘I got reg’larly done the other day.’", "\"얼마 전에 저도 제대로 한 방 먹었거든요.\""]], [["‘No!’ said his father.", "\"설마!\" 그의 아버지가 말했다."]], [["‘I did,’ said the son; and he proceeded to relate, in as few words as possible, how he had fallen a ready dupe to the stratagems of Job Trotter.", "\"먹었다니까요.\" 아들이 말했다. 그러고는 자기가 어떻게 잡 트로터의 계략에 호락호락 넘어갔는지를 되도록 간략한 말로 이야기하기 시작했다."]], [["Mr. Weller, senior, listened to the tale with the most profound attention, and, at its termination, said--", "웰러 영감은 더없이 깊은 주의를 기울여 그 이야기를 듣고는, 끝나자 이렇게 말했다—"]], [["‘Worn’t one o’ these chaps slim and tall, with long hair, and the gift o’ the gab wery gallopin’?’", "\"그 패거리 중 하나가 호리호리하고 키 크고, 머리는 길고, 입담이 아주 청산유수 같지 않더냐?\""]], [["Mr.", "픽윅 씨는 마지막 묘사 항목을 썩 알아듣지는 못했으나, 첫 부분은 이해했으므로 짐작으로 \"그렇소.\""], ["Pickwick did not quite understand the last item of description, but, comprehending the first, said ‘Yes,’ at a venture.", "하고 대답했다."]], [["‘T’ other’s a black-haired chap in mulberry livery, with a wery large head?’", "\"다른 하나는 머리 검은 녀석으로 오디빛 제복을 입고, 머리통이 아주 크지요?\""]], [["‘Yes, yes, he is,’ said Mr.", "\"그렇소, 그렇소, 맞소.\""], ["Pickwick and Sam, with great earnestness.", "픽윅 씨와 샘이 자못 진지하게 말했다."]], [["‘Then I know where they are, and that’s all about it,’ said Mr.", "\"그럼 그놈들이 어디 있는지 내가 알지, 그게 다일세.\""], ["Weller; ‘they’re at Ipswich, safe enough, them two.’", "웰러 씨가 말했다. \"그 둘은 입스위치에 있어, 틀림없이.\""]], [["‘No!’ said Mr.", "\"설마!\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Fact,’ said Mr.", "\"사실입니다요.\""], ["Weller, ‘and I’ll tell you how I know it.", "웰러 씨가 말했다. \"어떻게 아는지 말씀드리죠."], ["I work an Ipswich coach now and then for a friend o’ mine.", "제가 가끔 친구 대신 입스위치 마차를 몹니다."], ["I worked down the wery day arter the night as you caught the rheumatic, and at the Black Boy at Chelmsford--the wery place they’d come to--I took ‘em up, right through to Ipswich, where the man-servant--him in the mulberries--told me they was a-goin’ to put up for a long time.’", "선생께서 류머티즘에 걸리신 그 밤 바로 다음 날 마차를 몰고 내려갔는데, 첼름스퍼드의 블랙 보이 여관에서—바로 그놈들이 와 있던 곳이죠—그 둘을 태워서 입스위치까지 곧장 데려다줬거든요. 거기서 그 하인 녀석이—오디빛 옷 입은 놈 말입니다—한동안 묵을 거라고 하더군요.\""]], [["‘I’ll follow him,’ said Mr.", "\"그놈을 쫓아가겠다.\""], ["Pickwick; ‘we may as well see Ipswich as any other place.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"입스위치도 다른 데 못지않게 가 볼 만하지."], ["I’ll follow him.’", "그놈을 쫓아가겠다.\""]], [["‘You’re quite certain it was them, governor?’ inquired Mr.", "\"그놈들이 틀림없었어요, 영감님?\""], ["Weller, junior.", "웰러 군이 물었다."]], [["‘Quite, Sammy, quite,’ replied his father, ‘for their appearance is wery sing’ler; besides that ‘ere, I wondered to see the gen’l’m’n so formiliar with his servant; and, more than that, as they sat in the front, right behind the box, I heerd ‘em laughing and saying how they’d done old Fireworks.’", "\"틀림없어, 새미, 틀림없고말고.\" 그의 아버지가 대답했다. \"생김새가 아주 별나거든. 게다가 그 신사 양반이 하인이랑 어찌나 허물없이 구는지 의아하기도 했고, 더구나 그놈들이 마부석 바로 뒤 앞자리에 앉아서 늙은 폭죽을 어떻게 골탕 먹였느니 하며 웃고 떠드는 걸 들었으니까.\""]], [["‘Old who?’ said Mr.", "\"늙은 누구라고요?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Old Fireworks, Sir; by which, I’ve no doubt, they meant you, Sir.’", "\"늙은 폭죽요, 선생. 그게 분명 선생을 두고 한 말일 겁니다요, 선생.\""]], [["There is nothing positively vile or atrocious in the appellation of ‘old Fireworks,’ but still it is by no means a respectful or flattering designation. The recollection of all the wrongs he had sustained at Jingle’s hands, had crowded on Mr.", "'늙은 폭죽'이라는 호칭에 딱히 흉악하거나 잔악한 데는 없으나, 그렇다 해도 결코 존경스럽거나 듣기 좋은 명칭은 아니었다."], ["Pickwick’s mind, the moment Mr. Weller began to speak; it wanted but a feather to turn the scale, and ‘old Fireworks’ did it.", "웰러 씨가 말을 꺼내는 순간, 징글의 손에 당했던 온갖 부당한 일들의 기억이 픽윅 씨의 마음속에 밀려들었으니, 저울추를 기울이는 데는 깃털 하나면 족했는데, '늙은 폭죽'이 바로 그 깃털 노릇을 했다."]], [["‘I’ll follow him,’ said Mr.", "\"그놈을 쫓아가겠다.\""], ["Pickwick, with an emphatic blow on the table.", "픽윅 씨가 탁자를 힘껏 내려치며 말했다."]], [["‘I shall work down to Ipswich the day arter to-morrow, Sir,’ said Mr.", "\"제가 모레 입스위치까지 마차를 몰고 내려갑니다요, 선생.\" 웰러 영감이 말했다."], ["Weller the elder, ‘from the Bull in Whitechapel; and if you really mean to go, you’d better go with me.’", "\"화이트채플의 불 여관에서요. 정말로 가실 생각이시면 저와 함께 가시는 게 좋겠습니다.\""]], [["‘So we had,’ said Mr.", "\"그러는 게 좋겠군.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick; ‘very true; I can write to Bury, and tell them to meet me at Ipswich.", "\"지당한 말이오. 베리에 편지를 써서 입스위치로 나를 만나러 오라 이르면 되겠소."], ["We will go with you.", "당신과 함께 가겠소."], ["But don’t hurry away, Mr.", "하지만 서둘러 가지 마시오, 웰러 씨."], ["Weller; won’t you take anything?’", "뭐 좀 들지 않겠소?\""]], [["‘You’re wery good, Sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"참 친절하십니다요, 선생.\""], ["W., stopping short;--‘perhaps a small glass of brandy to drink your health, and success to Sammy, Sir, wouldn’t be amiss.’", "웰러 씨가 우뚝 멈춰 서며 대답했다.—\"브랜디 작은 잔으로 한 잔, 선생의 건강과 새미의 성공을 빌며 마시는 것쯤이야 나쁠 것 없겠지요, 선생.\""]], [["‘Certainly not,’ replied Mr.", "\"물론 나쁠 것 없소이다.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘A glass of brandy here!’ The brandy was brought; and Mr.", "\"여기 브랜디 한 잔!\""], ["Weller, after pulling his hair to Mr. Pickwick, and nodding to Sam, jerked it down his capacious throat as if it had been a small thimbleful.", "브랜디가 나왔고, 웰러 씨는 픽윅 씨에게 머리카락을 잡아당겨 인사하고 샘에게 고개를 끄덕인 뒤, 마치 골무 한 모금밖에 안 되는 양 그것을 자기 널찍한 목구멍으로 꿀꺽 털어 넣었다."]], [["‘Well done, father,’ said Sam, ‘take care, old fellow, or you’ll have a touch of your old complaint, the gout.’", "\"잘하셨어요, 아버지.\" 샘이 말했다. \"조심하세요, 노친네. 안 그러면 옛날 지병인 통풍이 도질 테니까요.\""]], [["‘I’ve found a sov’rin’ cure for that, Sammy,’ said Mr.", "\"그건 내가 특효약을 찾아냈지, 새미.\""], ["Weller, setting down the glass.", "웰러 씨가 잔을 내려놓으며 말했다."]], [["‘A sovereign cure for the gout,’ said Mr.", "\"통풍 특효약이라고요?\""], ["Pickwick, hastily producing his note-book--‘what is it?’", "픽윅 씨가 황급히 수첩을 꺼내며 말했다.—\"그게 뭐요?\""]], [["‘The gout, Sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"통풍 말입니다요, 선생.\""], ["Weller, ‘the gout is a complaint as arises from too much ease and comfort.", "웰러 씨가 대답했다. \"통풍이란 건 너무 편하고 안락해서 생기는 병이거든요."], ["If ever you’re attacked with the gout, sir, jist you marry a widder as has got a good loud woice, with a decent notion of usin’ it, and you’ll never have the gout agin.", "혹여 통풍에 걸리시거든, 선생, 목소리 크고 그걸 쓸 줄도 제법 아는 과부랑 그저 결혼하십쇼. 그럼 다시는 통풍에 안 걸리실 겁니다."], ["It’s a capital prescription, sir.", "기막힌 처방이죠, 선생."], ["I takes it reg’lar, and I can warrant it to drive away any illness as is caused by too much jollity.’ Having imparted this valuable secret, Mr.", "저는 그걸 꼬박꼬박 복용하는데, 너무 흥청댄 탓에 생긴 병이라면 무엇이든 싹 쫓아낸다고 보증할 수 있습니다요.\""], ["Weller drained his glass once more, produced a laboured wink, sighed deeply, and slowly retired.", "이 값진 비방을 전한 뒤, 웰러 씨는 잔을 한 번 더 비우고는 힘겹게 한쪽 눈을 찡긋해 보이고, 깊은 한숨을 내쉬며 천천히 물러갔다."]], [["‘Well, what do you think of what your father says, Sam?’ inquired Mr.", "\"자, 네 아버지 말씀을 어떻게 생각하느냐, 샘?\""], ["Pickwick, with a smile.", "픽윅 씨가 미소 지으며 물었다."]], [["‘Think, Sir!’ replied Mr.", "\"어떻게 생각하느냐고요, 나리!\""], ["Weller; ‘why, I think he’s the wictim o’ connubiality, as Blue Beard’s domestic chaplain said, vith a tear of pity, ven he buried him.’", "웰러 씨가 대답했다. \"글쎄요, 푸른 수염의 전속 목사가 그를 묻으면서 가엾어 눈물 한 방울 흘리며 말했듯이, 아버지는 부부 생활의 희생양이로구나 싶습니다요.\""]], [["There was no replying to this very apposite conclusion, and, therefore, Mr. Pickwick, after settling the reckoning, resumed his walk to Gray’s Inn.", "이 더없이 적절한 결론에는 달리 대꾸할 말이 없었으므로, 픽윅 씨는 셈을 치른 뒤 그레이즈 인을 향한 발걸음을 다시 옮겼다."], ["By the time he reached its secluded groves, however, eight o’clock had struck, and the unbroken stream of gentlemen in muddy high-lows, soiled white hats, and rusty apparel, who were pouring towards the different avenues of egress, warned him that the majority of the offices had closed for that day.", "그러나 그곳의 외진 숲에 다다랐을 무렵엔 이미 여덟 시를 알리는 종이 울렸고, 진흙투성이 반장화에 더러워진 흰 모자를 쓰고 낡은 옷차림을 한 신사들이 여러 출구의 길목으로 끊임없이 쏟아져 나오는 행렬은, 그날 대부분의 사무실이 문을 닫았음을 그에게 일러 주었다."]], [["After climbing two pairs of steep and dirty stairs, he found his anticipations were realised. Mr.", "가파르고 더러운 계단을 두 층 오른 뒤, 그는 자기 예상이 들어맞았음을 알았다."], ["Perker’s ‘outer door’ was closed; and the dead silence which followed Mr. Weller’s repeated kicks thereat, announced that the officials had retired from business for the night.", "퍼커 씨의 '바깥문'은 닫혀 있었고, 웰러 씨가 거기를 거듭 발로 찼으나 뒤따른 죽은 듯한 정적은 직원들이 그날 밤 일을 접고 물러갔음을 알려 주었다."]], [["‘This is pleasant, Sam,’ said Mr.", "\"이거 난감하구나, 샘.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick; ‘I shouldn’t lose an hour in seeing him; I shall not be able to get one wink of sleep to-night, I know, unless I have the satisfaction of reflecting that I have confided this matter to a professional man.’", "\"그를 만나는 데 한 시간도 허비하고 싶지 않은데. 이 일을 전문가에게 맡겼다는 생각으로 마음을 놓지 못한다면, 오늘 밤 나는 한숨도 못 잘 게 뻔하다.\""]], [["‘Here’s an old ‘ooman comin’ upstairs, sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"노파 하나가 계단을 올라오는데요, 나리.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다."], ["Weller; ‘p’raps she knows where we can find somebody.", "\"어쩌면 누굴 찾을 수 있는지 알지도 모르죠."], ["Hollo, old lady, vere’s Mr. Perker’s people?’", "여보쇼, 할멈, 퍼커 씨 사무소 사람들은 어디 있소?\""]], [["‘Mr. Perker’s people,’ said a thin, miserable-looking old woman, stopping to recover breath after the ascent of the staircase--‘Mr.", "\"퍼커 씨 사무소 사람들이라.\""], ["Perker’s people’s gone, and I’m a-goin’ to do the office out.’", "비쩍 마르고 초라해 보이는 노파가 계단을 오른 뒤 숨을 고르려 멈춰 서며 말했다.—\"퍼커 씨 사무소 사람들은 다 갔고, 나는 이제 사무실 청소를 하려는 참이오.\""]], [["Are you Mr. Perker’s servant?’ inquired Mr.", "\"당신이 퍼커 씨의 하인이오?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘I am Mr.", "\"나는 퍼커 씨의 세탁부요.\""], ["Perker’s laundress,’ replied the woman.", "여자가 대답했다."]], [["‘Ah,’ said Mr.", "\"아,\" 픽윅 씨가 샘에게 반쯤 돌아서서 말했다."], ["Pickwick, half aside to Sam, ‘it’s a curious circumstance, Sam, that they call the old women in these inns, laundresses.", "\"참 묘한 일이로구나, 샘. 이런 법학원에서는 노파들을 세탁부라 부르니 말이다."], ["I wonder what’s that for?’", "대체 왜 그러는지 모르겠구나.\""]], [["‘’Cos they has a mortal awersion to washing anythin’, I suppose, Sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"뭐든 빠는 걸 죽도록 싫어해서 그런 것 아니겠습니까, 나리.\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘I shouldn’t wonder,’ said Mr.", "\"그럴 만도 하구나.\""], ["Pickwick, looking at the old woman, whose appearance, as well as the condition of the office, which she had by this time opened, indicated a rooted antipathy to the application of soap and water; ‘do you know where I can find Mr.", "픽윅 씨가 그 노파를 바라보며 말했는데, 그녀의 행색은 물론이거니와 이제 막 그녀가 연 사무실의 상태로 보아도 비누와 물을 들이대는 일에 뿌리 깊은 반감을 품고 있음이 드러났다."], ["Perker, my good woman?’", "\"퍼커 씨를 어디서 찾을 수 있는지 아시오, 할멈?\""]], [["‘No, I don’t,’ replied the old woman gruffly; ‘he’s out o’ town now.’", "\"아니, 몰라요.\" 노파가 퉁명스레 대답했다. \"지금 시외에 나가 계시니까.\""]], [["‘That’s unfortunate,’ said Mr.", "\"그것참 안됐군.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick; ‘where’s his clerk?", "\"그의 사무원은 어디 있소?"], ["Do you know?’", "아시오?\""]], [["‘Yes, I know where he is, but he won’t thank me for telling you,’ replied the laundress.", "\"예, 그 사람이 어디 있는지는 알지만, 알려 줘도 그쪽이 나한테 고마워하진 않을 거요.\" 세탁부가 대답했다."]], [["‘I have very particular business with him,’ said Mr.", "\"그와 아주 긴한 볼일이 있소.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Won’t it do in the morning?’ said the woman.", "\"아침에 보면 안 되겠소?\" 여자가 말했다."]], [["‘Not so well,’ replied Mr.", "\"그러기엔 마땅찮소.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘Well,’ said the old woman, ‘if it was anything very particular, I was to say where he was, so I suppose there’s no harm in telling.", "\"그럼,\" 노파가 말했다."], ["If you just go to the Magpie and Stump, and ask at the bar for Mr.", "\"아주 긴한 일이거든 그 사람 있는 데를 일러 주라고 했으니, 알려 줘도 별 탈은 없겠지."], ["Lowten, they’ll show you in to him, and he’s Mr.", "매그파이 앤드 스텀프에 가서 바에서 로튼 씨를 찾으면, 그 사람한테 안내해 줄 거요."], ["Perker’s clerk.’", "그 사람이 퍼커 씨의 사무원이오.\""]], [["With this direction, and having been furthermore informed that the hostelry in question was situated in a court, happy in the double advantage of being in the vicinity of Clare Market, and closely approximating to the back of New Inn, Mr. Pickwick and Sam descended the rickety staircase in safety, and issued forth in quest of the Magpie and Stump.", "이런 안내를 받고, 또 그 술집이 클레어 시장 근처에 있으면서 동시에 뉴 인 뒤편과 바짝 맞닿아 있다는 이중의 이점을 누리는 어느 안뜰에 자리 잡고 있다는 추가 정보를 듣고서, 픽윅 씨와 샘은 삐걱대는 계단을 무사히 내려와 매그파이 앤드 스텀프를 찾아 나섰다."]], [["This favoured tavern, sacred to the evening orgies of Mr. Lowten and his companions, was what ordinary people would designate a public-house.", "로튼 씨와 그의 패거리가 저녁마다 벌이는 잔치에 바쳐진 이 특별한 선술집은, 보통 사람들이라면 그저 술집이라 부를 만한 곳이었다."], ["That the landlord was a man of money-making turn was sufficiently testified by the fact of a small bulkhead beneath the tap-room window, in size and shape not unlike a sedan-chair, being underlet to a mender of shoes: and that he was a being of a philanthropic mind was evident from the protection he afforded to a pieman, who vended his delicacies without fear of interruption, on the very door-step.", "주인이 돈벌이에 밝은 사람이라는 것은, 술청 창문 아래의 작은 칸막이 공간이—크기나 모양이 가마와 별반 다르지 않은—구두 수선공에게 세놓아져 있다는 사실로 충분히 입증되었다. 또 그가 박애심을 지닌 사람이라는 것은, 바로 그 문간 계단에서 방해받을 염려 없이 별미를 파는 파이 장수에게 그가 베풀어 준 비호로 분명히 드러났다."], ["In the lower windows, which were decorated with curtains of a saffron hue, dangled two or three printed cards, bearing reference to Devonshire cider and Dantzic spruce, while a large blackboard, announcing in white letters to an enlightened public, that there were 500,000 barrels of double stout in the cellars of the establishment, left the mind in a state of not unpleasing doubt and uncertainty as to the precise direction in the bowels of the earth, in which this mighty cavern might be supposed to extend.", "사프란빛 커튼으로 꾸며진 아래층 창문에는 데번셔 사과주와 단치히 가문비주를 가리키는 인쇄된 카드 두세 장이 매달려 있었고, 한편 흰 글씨로 깨인 대중에게 이 가게의 지하 저장고에 이중 흑맥주 50만 통이 있노라 알리는 커다란 흑판은, 이 거대한 동굴이 땅속 어느 방향으로 뻗어 있으리라 짐작해야 할지에 대해 마음을 그리 불쾌하지만은 않은 의혹과 불확실 속에 남겨 두었다."], ["When we add that the weather-beaten signboard bore the half- obliterated semblance of a magpie intently eyeing a crooked streak of brown paint, which the neighbours had been taught from infancy to consider as the ‘stump,’ we have said all that need be said of the exterior of the edifice.", "풍상에 시달린 간판이 한 마리 까치가 갈색 페인트로 그은 구부정한 줄을—이웃들이 어릴 적부터 '그루터기'로 여기도록 배워 온 그것을—골똘히 노려보는 반쯤 지워진 형상을 담고 있었다고 덧붙이면, 이 건물의 외관에 관해 굳이 더 할 말은 다 한 셈이다."]], [["On Mr. Pickwick’s presenting himself at the bar, an elderly female emerged from behind the screen therein, and presented herself before him.", "픽윅 씨가 바에 나타나자, 그 안의 가림막 뒤에서 나이 든 여자 하나가 나와 그의 앞에 모습을 드러냈다."]], [["‘Is Mr. Lowten here, ma’am?’ inquired Mr.", "\"로튼 씨가 여기 계십니까, 부인?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘Yes, he is, Sir,’ replied the landlady.", "\"예, 계세요, 선생님.\" 여주인이 대답했다."], ["‘Here, Charley, show the gentleman in to Mr. Lowten.’", "\"얘, 찰리, 이 신사분을 로튼 씨한테로 모셔라.\""]], [["‘The gen’l’m’n can’t go in just now,’ said a shambling pot-boy, with a red head, ‘cos’ Mr.", "\"이 신사분은 지금은 들어가실 수 없어요.\" 빨강 머리의 어기적거리는 술집 사환이 말했다."], ["Lowten’s a-singin’ a comic song, and he’ll put him out.", "\"로튼 씨가 익살스러운 노래를 부르고 계셔서요. 들어가면 흥을 깰 거예요."], ["He’ll be done directly, Sir.’", "곧 끝나실 거예요, 선생님.\""]], [["The red-headed pot-boy had scarcely finished speaking, when a most unanimous hammering of tables, and jingling of glasses, announced that the song had that instant terminated; and Mr.", "빨강 머리 사환이 미처 말을 끝맺기도 전에, 더없이 일사불란하게 탁자를 두드리고 잔을 쨍그랑대는 소리가 그 노래가 방금 끝났음을 알렸다."], ["Pickwick, after desiring Sam to solace himself in the tap, suffered himself to be conducted into the presence of Mr. Lowten.", "그리하여 픽윅 씨는 샘에게 술청에서 한잔 즐기라 이른 뒤, 로튼 씨 앞으로 안내받아 들어갔다."]], [["At the announcement of ‘A gentleman to speak to you, Sir,’ a puffy-faced young man, who filled the chair at the head of the table, looked with some surprise in the direction from whence the voice proceeded; and the surprise seemed to be by no means diminished, when his eyes rested on an individual whom he had never seen before.", "\"신사 한 분이 말씀 나누자 하십니다, 선생님.\" 하는 통보에, 탁자 상석에 앉아 있던 부어오른 얼굴의 젊은이가 그 목소리가 난 쪽으로 다소 놀란 듯 바라보았다. 그리고 그 놀라움은 그의 눈길이 일찍이 본 적 없는 한 인물에 가닿았을 때 결코 줄어드는 것 같지 않았다."]], [["‘I beg your pardon, Sir,’ said Mr.", "\"실례합니다, 선생.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick, ‘and I am very sorry to disturb the other gentlemen, too, but I come on very particular business; and if you will suffer me to detain you at this end of the room for five minutes, I shall be very much obliged to you.’", "\"다른 신사분들까지 방해하게 되어 매우 죄송합니다만, 아주 긴한 볼일로 왔습니다. 이 방 이쪽 끝에서 오 분만 시간을 내주신다면 대단히 감사하겠습니다.\""]], [["The puffy-faced young man rose, and drawing a chair close to Mr. Pickwick in an obscure corner of the room, listened attentively to his tale of woe.", "부어오른 얼굴의 젊은이는 일어나 방 한쪽 어두운 구석에서 픽윅 씨 곁으로 의자를 바짝 끌어다 놓고, 그의 딱한 사연에 귀를 기울였다."]], [["‘Ah,’ he said, when Mr.", "\"아,\""], ["Pickwick had concluded, ‘Dodson and Fogg--sharp practice theirs--capital men of business, Dodson and Fogg, sir.’", "픽윅 씨가 이야기를 끝맺자 그가 말했다. \"도드슨 앤드 포그라—약삭빠른 수완이지요, 그들은—일에 밝은 사람들입니다, 도드슨 앤드 포그는, 선생.\""]], [["Mr. Pickwick admitted the sharp practice of Dodson and Fogg, and Lowten resumed.", "픽윅 씨는 도드슨 앤드 포그의 약삭빠른 수완을 인정했고, 로튼은 말을 이었다."]], [["‘Perker ain’t in town, and he won’t be, neither, before the end of next week; but if you want the action defended, and will leave the copy with me, I can do all that’s needful till he comes back.’", "\"퍼커는 시내에 안 계시고, 다음 주말 전엔 돌아오지도 않으실 겁니다. 하지만 소송을 방어하실 생각이고 그 사본을 저한테 맡겨 두신다면, 그분이 돌아오실 때까지 필요한 일은 제가 다 해 둘 수 있습니다.\""]], [["‘That’s exactly what I came here for,’ said Mr.", "\"바로 그 일로 여기 온 겁니다.\""], ["Pickwick, handing over the document.", "픽윅 씨가 그 서류를 건네며 말했다."], ["‘If anything particular occurs, you can write to me at the post-office, Ipswich.’", "\"긴한 일이 생기면 입스위치 우체국으로 편지하시면 됩니다.\""]], [["‘That’s all right,’ replied Mr.", "\"그럼 됐습니다.\""], ["Perker’s clerk; and then seeing Mr.", "퍼커 씨의 사무원이 대답했다."], ["Pickwick’s eye wandering curiously towards the table, he added, ‘will you join us, for half an hour or so?", "그러고는 픽윅 씨의 눈이 호기심 어린 듯 탁자 쪽으로 향하는 것을 보고 덧붙였다. \"삼십 분쯤 저희와 어울리지 않으시렵니까?"], ["We are capital company here to- night.", "오늘 밤 여기 모인 사람들이 아주 좋은 친구들이거든요."], ["There’s Samkin and Green’s managing-clerk, and Smithers and Price’s chancery, and Pimkin and Thomas’s out o’ doors--sings a capital song, he does--and Jack Bamber, and ever so many more.", "샘킨 앤드 그린의 사무장도 있고, 스미더스 앤드 프라이스의 형평법원 담당도 있고, 핌킨 앤드 토머스의 외근 사무원도—그 사람 노래 기막히게 잘 부르죠—거기다 잭 뱀버에, 그 밖에도 아주 많습니다."], ["You’re come out of the country, I suppose.", "시골에서 올라오신 것 같은데."], ["Would you like to join us?’", "함께 어울리시겠어요?\""]], [["Mr. Pickwick could not resist so tempting an opportunity of studying human nature.", "픽윅 씨는 인간 본성을 연구할 이토록 솔깃한 기회를 뿌리칠 수 없었다."], ["He suffered himself to be led to the table, where, after having been introduced to the company in due form, he was accommodated with a seat near the chairman and called for a glass of his favourite beverage.", "그는 탁자로 이끌려 가, 정식으로 좌중에 소개된 뒤 의장 곁의 자리를 권받았고, 자기가 좋아하는 음료 한 잔을 청했다."]], [["A profound silence, quite contrary to Mr. Pickwick’s expectation, succeeded.", "픽윅 씨의 예상과는 사뭇 다르게, 깊은 침묵이 뒤따랐다."]], [["‘You don’t find this sort of thing disagreeable, I hope, sir?’ said his right hand neighbour, a gentleman in a checked shirt and Mosaic studs, with a cigar in his mouth.", "\"이런 분위기가 거북하진 않으시겠지요, 선생?\" 그의 오른편 이웃이 말했는데, 체크무늬 셔츠에 모자이크 장식 단추를 달고 입에 시가를 문 신사였다."]], [["‘Not in the least,’ replied Mr.", "\"조금도 그렇지 않소.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다."], ["Pickwick; ‘I like it very much, although I am no smoker myself.’", "\"나는 담배를 피우진 않습니다만, 이런 자리를 아주 좋아합니다.\""]], [["‘I should be very sorry to say I wasn’t,’ interposed another gentleman on the opposite side of the table.", "\"나는 안 피운다고는 말도 못 하겠소.\" 탁자 맞은편의 또 다른 신사가 끼어들었다."], ["‘It’s board and lodgings to me, is smoke.’", "\"담배는 내게 숙식이나 마찬가지거든.\""]], [["Mr. Pickwick glanced at the speaker, and thought that if it were washing too, it would be all the better.", "픽윅 씨는 그 말한 이를 흘끔 보며, 만약 빨래까지 그렇다면 더 좋으련만 하고 생각했다."]], [["Here there was another pause. Mr.", "여기서 또 한 번 침묵이 흘렀다."], ["Pickwick was a stranger, and his coming had evidently cast a damp upon the party.", "픽윅 씨는 낯선 사람이었고, 그의 등장은 분명 좌중에 찬물을 끼얹은 셈이었다."]], [["‘Mr.", "\"그런디 씨가 여러분께 노래 한 곡 들려주실 겁니다.\""], ["Grundy’s going to oblige the company with a song,’ said the chairman.", "의장이 말했다."]], [["‘No, he ain’t,’ said Mr.", "\"아니, 안 부를 거요.\""], ["Grundy.", "그런디 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Why not?’ said the chairman.", "\"왜 안 부르시오?\" 의장이 말했다."]], [["‘Because he can’t,’ said Mr.", "\"못 부르니까요.\""], ["Grundy.", "그런디 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘You had better say he won’t,’ replied the chairman.", "\"안 부르겠다고 하는 게 낫겠소.\" 의장이 대꾸했다."]], [["‘Well, then, he won’t,’ retorted Mr.", "\"그럼, 안 부르겠소.\""], ["Grundy. Mr.", "그런디 씨가 받아쳤다."], ["Grundy’s positive refusal to gratify the company occasioned another silence.", "좌중을 즐겁게 해 주기를 한사코 거부하는 그런디 씨 탓에 또다시 침묵이 찾아왔다."]], [["‘Won’t anybody enliven us?’ said the chairman, despondingly.", "\"아무도 우릴 좀 흥겹게 해 주지 않으려오?\" 의장이 풀죽은 목소리로 말했다."]], [["‘Why don’t you enliven us yourself, Mr.", "\"의장 양반이 직접 흥을 돋우시지 그래요?\""], ["Chairman?’ said a young man with a whisker, a squint, and an open shirt collar (dirty), from the bottom of the table.", "탁자 끝자리에서 구레나룻에 사팔뜨기 눈, (지저분한) 풀어 헤친 셔츠 깃을 한 젊은이가 말했다."]], [["‘Hear!", "\"옳소!"], ["hear!’ said the smoking gentleman, in the Mosaic jewellery.", "옳소!\" 모자이크 장신구를 단, 담배 피우는 신사가 말했다."]], [["‘Because I only know one song, and I have sung it already, and it’s a fine of “glasses round”", "\"나는 노래 한 곡밖에 모르고, 그것도 이미 불렀거든. 그리고 한 곳에서 같은 노래를 한 밤에 두 번 부르면 '한 바퀴 술 돌리기'"], ["to sing the same song twice in a night,’ replied the chairman.", "벌금이 있단 말이오.\" 의장이 대답했다."]], [["This was an unanswerable reply, and silence prevailed again.", "이것은 반박할 수 없는 대답이었고, 다시금 침묵이 자리를 지배했다."]], [["‘I have been to-night, gentlemen,’ said Mr.", "\"여러분, 저는 오늘 밤,\""], ["Pickwick, hoping to start a subject which all the company could take a part in discussing, ‘I have been to-night, in a place which you all know very well, doubtless, but which I have not been in for some years, and know very little of; I mean Gray’s Inn, gentlemen.", "픽윅 씨가 좌중 모두가 함께 논할 만한 화제를 꺼내 보려는 마음으로 말했다. \"저는 오늘 밤, 여러분 모두가 분명 잘 아시겠지만 저로서는 몇 해 동안 가 본 적이 없어 거의 알지 못하는 곳에 다녀왔습니다."], ["Curious little nooks in a great place, like London, these old inns are.’", "그레이즈 인 말입니다, 여러분. 런던같이 큰 곳에 자리한 이런 오래된 법학원들은 참으로 묘한 작은 구석들이지요.\""]], [["‘By Jove!’ said the chairman, whispering across the table to Mr.", "\"이런!\" 의장이 탁자 너머로 픽윅 씨에게 속삭였다."], ["Pickwick, ‘you have hit upon something that one of us, at least, would talk upon for ever.", "\"당신이 우리 중 적어도 한 사람은 끝없이 떠들어 댈 화제를 건드리셨군요. 잭 뱀버 영감을 끌어내실 거예요."], ["You’ll draw old Jack Bamber out; he was never heard to talk about anything else but the inns, and he has lived alone in them till he’s half crazy.’", "저 양반은 법학원 말고 다른 얘기를 하는 걸 도무지 들어 본 적이 없죠. 거기서 혼자 살다 보니 반쯤 정신이 나가 버린 양반이거든요.\""]], [["The individual to whom Lowten alluded, was a little, yellow, high- shouldered man, whose countenance, from his habit of stooping forward when silent, Mr. Pickwick had not observed before.", "로튼이 가리킨 인물은 키 작고 누르스름하며 어깨가 올라간 사내로, 말이 없을 때면 앞으로 구부정하게 숙이는 버릇 탓에 픽윅 씨가 미처 그 얼굴을 못 알아본 터였다."], ["He wondered, though, when the old man raised his shrivelled face, and bent his gray eye upon him, with a keen inquiring look, that such remarkable features could have escaped his attention for a moment.", "그러나 막상 그 노인이 쪼글쪼글한 얼굴을 들어 회색 눈을 픽윅 씨에게 박으며 날카롭게 캐묻는 눈초리를 보냈을 때, 픽윅 씨는 그토록 두드러진 이목구비가 어떻게 한순간이라도 자기 눈을 비껴갔을까 의아했다."], ["There was a fixed grim smile perpetually on his countenance; he leaned his chin on a long, skinny hand, with nails of extraordinary length; and as he inclined his head to one side, and looked keenly out from beneath his ragged gray eyebrows, there was a strange, wild slyness in his leer, quite repulsive to behold.", "그의 얼굴에는 굳어 버린 음산한 미소가 끊임없이 어려 있었고, 그는 유난히 길게 자란 손톱이 달린 길고 앙상한 손에 턱을 괴고 있었다. 그가 고개를 한쪽으로 갸웃하고 텁수룩한 회색 눈썹 아래로 날카롭게 내다보았을 때, 그 곁눈질에는 보기에 자못 역겨운, 기이하고 사나운 교활함이 어려 있었다."]], [["This was the figure that now started forward, and burst into an animated torrent of words.", "이제 앞으로 불쑥 나선 것이 바로 이 인물이었고, 그는 활기 넘치는 말의 격류를 터뜨렸다."], ["As this chapter has been a long one, however, and as the old man was a remarkable personage, it will be more respectful to him, and more convenient to us, to let him speak for himself in a fresh one.", "그러나 이 장이 길어졌고, 또 그 노인이 주목할 만한 인물인 만큼, 그를 위해서도 더 정중하고 우리에게도 더 편하도록, 그가 새로운 장에서 스스로 이야기하게 두기로 한다."]], [["CHAPTER XXI.", "\"아하!\" 앞 장 끝머리에 그 태도와 외양이 간략히 묘사된 그 노인이 말했다."], ["IN WHICH THE OLD MAN LAUNCHES FORTH INTO HIS FAVOURITE THEME, AND RELATES A STORY ABOUT A QUEER CLIENT", "\"아하! 누가 그 여관들 이야기를 하고 있었던가?\""]], [["‘Aha!’ said the old man, a brief description of whose manner and appearance concluded the last chapter, ‘aha!", "\"제가 했습니다.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다."], ["who was talking about the inns?’", "\"그곳들이 참으로 별난 옛 장소라고 말하던 참이었지요.\""]], [["‘I was, Sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"당신이!\""], ["Pickwick--‘I was observing what singular old places they are.’\n‘You!’ said the old man contemptuously.", "노인이 경멸하듯 말했다."], ["‘What do you know of the time when young men shut themselves up in those lonely rooms, and read and read, hour after hour, and night after night, till their reason wandered beneath their midnight studies; till their mental powers were exhausted; till morning’s light brought no freshness or health to them; and they sank beneath the unnatural devotion of their youthful energies to their dry old books?", "\"젊은이들이 그 적막한 방에 틀어박혀 읽고 또 읽으며, 한 시간 또 한 시간, 밤이 가고 또 밤이 와도, 마침내 그 한밤의 학업 아래 이성이 헤매기 시작하고, 정신의 힘이 다 닳아 없어지며, 아침의 빛이 와도 그들에게 아무런 생기도 건강도 가져다주지 못한 채, 젊은 기력을 메마른 옛 책들에 부자연스레 바친 끝에 스러져 가던 그 시절을 당신이 무엇을 안단 말이오?"], ["Coming down to a later time, and a very different day, what do you know of the gradual sinking beneath consumption, or the quick wasting of fever--the grand results of “life” and dissipation--which men have undergone in these same rooms?", "좀 더 뒤의 시대, 아주 다른 시절로 내려와서, 이 똑같은 방에서 사람들이 겪어 온 폐병의 점진적 침몰이나 열병의 빠른 소진—'인생'과 방탕의 거창한 결말—에 대해 당신이 무엇을 안단 말이오?"], ["How many vain pleaders for mercy, do you think, have turned away heart-sick from the lawyer’s office, to find a resting-place in the Thames, or a refuge in the jail?", "자비를 헛되이 빌던 자들 가운데 얼마나 많은 이가 변호사 사무실에서 가슴이 무너진 채 돌아서서, 템스강에서 안식처를, 아니면 감옥에서 피난처를 찾았는지 짐작이나 하시오?"], ["They are no ordinary houses, those.", "그곳들은 예사로운 집이 아니오."], ["There is not a panel in the old wainscotting, but what, if it were endowed with the powers of speech and memory, could start from the wall, and tell its tale of horror--the romance of life, Sir, the romance of life!", "그 낡은 벽판자에 박힌 판자 한 장이라도, 만약 말하고 기억하는 힘을 부여받는다면, 벽에서 튀어나와 제 끔찍한 이야기를 들려줄 수 있을 것이오—인생의 로맨스 말이오, 선생, 인생의 로맨스!"], ["Common-place as they may seem now, I tell you they are strange old places, and I would rather hear many a legend with a terrific-sounding name, than the true history of one old set of chambers.’", "지금은 아무리 평범해 보일지언정, 말하건대 그곳들은 기이한 옛 장소요. 나는 무시무시한 이름이 붙은 전설을 숱하게 듣느니, 차라리 어느 낡은 방 한 채의 참된 역사를 듣는 편이 낫겠소.\""]], [["There was something so odd in the old man’s sudden energy, and the subject which had called it forth, that Mr.", "노인의 갑작스러운 격정과, 그것을 불러일으킨 화제에는 어딘가 너무도 기묘한 데가 있어서, 픽윅 씨는 대꾸할 말을 미처 준비하지 못했다."], ["Pickwick was prepared with no observation in reply; and the old man checking his impetuosity, and resuming the leer, which had disappeared during his previous excitement, said--", "그러자 노인은 그 격렬함을 누그러뜨리고, 앞서의 흥분 동안 사라졌던 곁눈질을 다시 되살리며 말했다."]], [["‘Look at them in another light--their most common-place and least romantic.", "\"또 다른 측면에서 그곳들을 보시오—가장 평범하고 가장 로맨스가 없는 면을."], ["What fine places of slow torture they are!", "얼마나 훌륭한 더딘 고문의 장소들인지!"], ["Think of the needy man who has spent his all, beggared himself, and pinched his friends, to enter the profession, which is destined never to yield him a morsel of bread.", "가진 것을 다 써 버리고, 스스로 거지가 되고, 친구들의 것까지 졸라내어 이 직업에 발을 들였으나, 끝내 빵 한 조각도 안겨 주지 않을 운명인 그 궁핍한 사내를 생각해 보시오."], ["The waiting--the hope--the disappointment--the fear--the misery-- the poverty--the blight on his hopes, and end to his career--the suicide perhaps, or the shabby, slipshod drunkard.", "그 기다림—희망—실망—두려움—비참—가난—꺾여 버린 그의 희망과 끝장난 그의 경력—어쩌면 자살, 아니면 추레하고 칠칠치 못한 술꾼이 되는 것."], ["Am I not right about them?’ And the old man rubbed his hands, and leered as if in delight at having found another point of view in which to place his favourite subject.", "내 말이 그르오?\" 그러더니 노인은 두 손을 비비며, 자기가 좋아하는 화제를 놓아둘 또 다른 시각을 찾아낸 것이 기쁜 듯 곁눈질했다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick eyed the old man with great curiosity, and the remainder of the company smiled, and looked on in silence.", "픽윅 씨는 큰 호기심으로 노인을 바라보았고, 나머지 일행은 미소를 띤 채 말없이 지켜보았다."]], [["‘Talk of your German universities,’ said the little old man.", "\"당신네 독일 대학들 이야기를 하지만.\" 자그마한 노인이 말했다."], ["‘Pooh, pooh!", "\"쳇, 쳇!"], ["there’s romance enough at home without going half a mile for it; only people never think of it.’", "반 마일도 가지 않고 바로 우리 안방에 로맨스가 넘쳐 나는데, 다만 사람들이 도무지 그걸 생각지 않을 뿐이오.\""]], [["‘I never thought of the romance of this particular subject before, certainly,’ said Mr.", "\"확실히 저는 이 특별한 화제의 로맨스를 전에는 생각해 본 적이 없군요.\""], ["Pickwick, laughing.", "픽윅 씨가 웃으며 말했다."]], [["‘To be sure you didn’t,’ said the little old man; ‘of course not.", "\"물론 안 했겠지요.\" 자그마한 노인이 말했다."], ["As a friend of mine used to say to me, “What is there in chambers in particular?”", "\"당연하지. 내 친구 하나가 늘 내게 하던 말이 있소."], ["“Queer old places,” said I.", "'대체 방이라는 게 뭐 특별할 게 있나?'"], ["“Not at all,”", "'기이한 옛 장소지.'"], ["said he.", "내가 말했소."], ["“Lonely,”", "'천만에.'"], ["said I.", "그가 말했소."], ["“Not a bit of it,”", "'쓸쓸하지.' 내가 말했소."], ["said he.", "'조금도.'"], ["He died one morning of apoplexy, as he was going to open his outer door.", "그가 말했소. 그 친구는 어느 날 아침 바깥문을 열려다가 졸중으로 죽었소."], ["Fell with his head in his own letter-box, and there he lay for eighteen months.", "머리를 제 편지함에 처박은 채 쓰러져서는, 거기 열여덟 달을 누워 있었지."], ["Everybody thought he’d gone out of town.’", "다들 그가 마을을 떠난 줄 알았소.\""]], [["‘And how was he found out at last?’ inquired Mr.", "\"그래서 결국 어떻게 발견됐습니까?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘The benchers determined to have his door broken open, as he hadn’t paid any rent for two years.", "\"이 년 동안 집세를 한 푼도 내지 않으니, 법학원 간부들이 그의 문을 부수기로 했지."], ["So they did.", "그래서 그렇게 했소."], ["Forced the lock; and a very dusty skeleton in a blue coat, black knee-shorts, and silks, fell forward in the arms of the porter who opened the door.", "자물쇠를 비틀어 부수니, 푸른 외투에 검은 무릎바지, 비단 양말을 신은 먼지투성이 해골 하나가 문을 연 짐꾼의 팔 안으로 앞으로 고꾸라졌소."], ["Queer, that.", "기이하지, 그게."], ["Rather, perhaps; rather, eh?’ The little old man put his head more on one side, and rubbed his hands with unspeakable glee.", "좀 그렇지 않소, 그렇지 않소, 응?\" 자그마한 노인은 고개를 더욱 한쪽으로 기울이고, 이루 말할 수 없는 희열에 젖어 두 손을 비볐다."]], [["‘I know another case,’ said the little old man, when his chuckles had in some degree subsided.", "\"또 다른 사례를 알고 있소.\" 그의 낄낄거림이 어느 정도 가라앉자 자그마한 노인이 말했다."], ["‘It occurred in Clifford’s Inn.", "\"클리퍼드 법학원에서 일어난 일이오."], ["Tenant of a top set--bad character--shut himself up in his bedroom closet, and took a dose of arsenic.", "맨 위층 방의 세입자였는데—평판이 나쁜 자였소—제 침실 벽장에 틀어박혀 비소를 한 모금 들이켰소."], ["The steward thought he had run away: opened the door, and put a bill up.", "관리인은 그가 달아난 줄 알고, 문을 열고 세놓는다는 안내문을 붙였지."], ["Another man came, took the chambers, furnished them, and went to live there.", "또 다른 사내가 와서 그 방을 빌리고, 가구를 들이고, 거기 들어가 살았소."], ["Somehow or other he couldn’t sleep--always restless and uncomfortable.", "그런데 웬일인지 그는 잠을 잘 수가 없었지—늘 안절부절못하고 불편했소."], ["“Odd,” says he.", "'이상하군.'"], ["“I’ll make the other room my bedchamber, and this my sitting-room.”", "그가 말했소. '다른 방을 침실로 하고, 이 방을 거실로 삼아야겠어.'"], ["He made the change, and slept very well at night, but suddenly found that, somehow, he couldn’t read in the evening: he got nervous and uncomfortable, and used to be always snuffing his candles and staring about him.", "그렇게 바꾸고 나니 밤엔 아주 잘 잤소만, 문득 어찌 된 일인지 저녁에 책을 읽을 수 없게 됐음을 알아챘소. 신경이 곤두서고 불편해져서, 늘 촛불 심지를 다듬으며 주위를 두리번거리곤 했지."], ["“I can’t make this out,”", "'도무지 영문을 모르겠군.'"], ["said he, when he came home from the play one night, and was drinking a glass of cold grog, with his back to the wall, in order that he mightn’t be able to fancy there was any one behind him--“I can’t make it out,”", "어느 날 밤 연극을 보고 돌아와 차가운 그로그 한 잔을 마시며—누가 등 뒤에 있다는 상상을 못 하도록 벽에 등을 대고 앉아—그가 말했소. '도무지 영문을 모르겠어.'"], ["said he; and just then his eyes rested on the little closet that had been always locked up, and a shudder ran through his whole frame from top to toe.", "바로 그때 그의 눈이 늘 잠겨 있던 그 작은 벽장에 멎었고, 머리끝부터 발끝까지 온몸에 전율이 흘렀소. '전에도 이 이상한 느낌을 받았어.'"], ["“I have felt this strange feeling before,”", "그가 말했소. '저 벽장에 뭔가 잘못된 게 있다는 생각을 떨칠 수가 없군.'"], ["said he, “I cannot help thinking there’s something wrong about that closet.”", "그는 안간힘을 다해 용기를 끌어모아, 부지깽이로 한두 번 내리쳐 자물쇠를 박살내고 문을 열었소."], ["He made a strong effort, plucked up his courage, shivered the lock with a blow or two of the poker, opened the door, and there, sure enough, standing bolt upright in the corner, was the last tenant, with a little bottle clasped firmly in his hand, and his face--well!’ As the little old man concluded, he looked round on the attentive faces of his wondering auditory with a smile of grim delight.", "그랬더니 과연, 구석에 꼿꼿이 서 있는 것은 다름 아닌 지난 세입자였는데, 작은 병을 손에 꽉 움켜쥐고, 그 얼굴은—이런!\" 자그마한 노인은 이야기를 맺으며, 놀라움에 귀 기울이는 청중의 얼굴들을 둘러보며 음산한 희열의 미소를 지었다."]], [["‘What strange things these are you tell us of, Sir,’ said Mr.", "\"참으로 이상한 이야기들을 들려주시는군요, 선생.\""], ["Pickwick, minutely scanning the old man’s countenance, by the aid of his glasses.", "픽윅 씨가 안경의 힘을 빌려 노인의 안색을 세세히 살피며 말했다."]], [["‘Strange!’ said the little old man.", "\"이상하다고!\" 자그마한 노인이 말했다."], ["‘Nonsense; you think them strange, because you know nothing about it.", "\"허튼소리. 당신이 그것들을 이상하게 여기는 건 그에 대해 아무것도 모르기 때문이오."], ["They are funny, but not uncommon.’", "그건 우스운 일이지, 드문 일이 아니라오.\""]], [["‘Funny!’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"우스운 일이라고요!\""], ["Pickwick involuntarily.", "픽윅 씨가 저도 모르게 외쳤다."]], [["‘Yes, funny, are they not?’ replied the little old man, with a diabolical leer; and then, without pausing for an answer, he continued--", "\"그렇소, 우습지 않소?\" 자그마한 노인이 악마 같은 곁눈질로 대답하더니, 대답을 기다리지도 않고 말을 이었다."]], [["‘I knew another man--let me see--forty years ago now--who took an old, damp, rotten set of chambers, in one of the most ancient inns, that had been shut up and empty for years and years before.", "\"또 다른 사내를 알았소—가만있자—이젠 사십 년 전 일이군—그 사내가 가장 오래된 여관들 가운데 한 곳에서, 여러 해 동안 닫힌 채 텅 비어 있던 낡고 축축하고 썩어 가는 방 한 채를 빌렸소. 그곳에 대해서는 노파들의 이야기가 잔뜩 돌았고, 결코 유쾌한 곳이라고는 할 수 없었지."], ["There were lots of old women’s stories about the place, and it certainly was very far from being a cheerful one; but he was poor, and the rooms were cheap, and that would have been quite a sufficient reason for him, if they had been ten times worse than they really were.", "하지만 그는 가난했고 방은 쌌으니, 설령 실제보다 열 배 더 형편없었던들 그에겐 그것으로 충분한 이유가 됐을 거요. 그는 그곳에 있던 곰팡내 나는 비품 몇 가지를 떠맡아야 했는데, 그 가운데 큼지막한 유리문이 달리고 안쪽에 초록색 휘장이 쳐진, 육중하고 거추장스러운 서류용 나무 진열장이 있었소."], ["He was obliged to take some mouldering fixtures that were on the place, and, among the rest, was a great lumbering wooden press for papers, with large glass doors, and a green curtain inside; a pretty useless thing for him, for he had no papers to put in it; and as to his clothes, he carried them about with him, and that wasn’t very hard work, either.", "그에겐 거의 쓸모없는 물건이었지. 거기 넣을 서류가 없었으니까."], ["Well, he had moved in all his furniture--it wasn’t quite a truck-full--and had sprinkled it about the room, so as to make the four chairs look as much like a dozen as possible, and was sitting down before the fire at night, drinking the first glass of two gallons of whisky he had ordered on credit, wondering whether it would ever be paid for, and if so, in how many years’ time, when his eyes encountered the glass doors of the wooden press.", "옷으로 말하자면 그는 그것을 몸에 지니고 다녔고, 그것도 그리 힘든 일은 아니었소. 자, 그는 가구를 모조리 들여놓았는데—짐수레 하나에도 차지 않았지—의자 넷이 되도록 열두 개처럼 보이게끔 방 안에 흩어 놓고는, 밤에 난롯불 앞에 앉아, 외상으로 주문한 위스키 두 갤런 가운데 첫 잔을 마시며, 이게 과연 값이 치러질는지, 치러진다면 몇 년 뒤에나 그리될지를 궁리하고 있었소."], ["“Ah,”", "그때 그의 눈이 나무 진열장의 유리문에 맞닥뜨렸소. '아.'"], ["says he, “if I hadn’t been obliged to take that ugly article at the old broker’s valuation, I might have got something comfortable for the money.", "그가 말했소. '저 추한 물건을 늙은 중개인이 매긴 값에 떠맡지만 않았던들, 그 돈으로 뭔가 안락한 걸 장만했을 텐데."], ["I’ll tell you what it is, old fellow,”", "이것 봐, 친구.' 달리 말 붙일 데가 없어 진열장을 향해 소리 내어 말했소."], ["he said, speaking aloud to the press, having nothing else to speak to, “if it wouldn’t cost more to break up your old carcass, than it would ever be worth afterward, I’d have a fire out of you in less than no time.”", "'네 늙은 몸뚱이를 쪼개는 데 드는 비용이, 그 뒤에 그게 가질 값어치보다 더 들지만 않는다면야, 당장에라도 너로 불을 피울 텐데.' 그 말을 채 끝내기도 전에, 그 궤짝 속에서 희미한 신음 같은 소리가 새어 나오는 듯했소."], ["He had hardly spoken the words, when a sound resembling a faint groan, appeared to issue from the interior of the case.", "처음엔 깜짝 놀랐지만, 잠시 곰곰 생각해 보니 옆방에서 밖에 나가 식사를 하고 온 어느 젊은이일 거라 여기고는, 두 발을 난로 가리개 위에 올리고 불을 쑤시려 부지깽이를 들어 올렸소. 그 순간 그 소리가 다시 울렸고, 유리문 하나가 천천히 열리더니, 더럽고 해진 옷차림에 창백하고 수척한 형체가 진열장 안에 꼿꼿이 서 있는 것이 드러났소."], ["It startled him at first, but thinking, on a moment’s reflection, that it must be some young fellow in the next chamber, who had been dining out, he put his feet on the fender, and raised the poker to stir the fire.", "그 형체는 키가 크고 야위었으며, 얼굴에는 근심과 불안이 어려 있었지. 하지만 그 살갗의 빛깔과, 온몸의 수척하고 이승의 것 같지 않은 모습에는, 이 세상 어떤 존재도 띤 적 없는 무엇인가가 있었소."], ["At that moment, the sound was repeated; and one of the glass doors slowly opening, disclosed a pale and emaciated figure in soiled and worn apparel, standing erect in the press.", "'당신은 누구요?' 새 세입자가 새파랗게 질리면서도, 부지깽이를 손에 겨누어 형체의 얼굴을 제법 정확히 조준하며 말했소."], ["The figure was tall and thin, and the countenance expressive of care and anxiety; but there was something in the hue of the skin, and gaunt and unearthly appearance of the whole form, which no being of this world was ever seen to wear.", "'당신은 누구요?' '그 부지깽이를 내게 던지지 마시오.'"], ["“Who are you?”", "형체가 대답했소."], ["said the new tenant, turning very pale; poising the poker in his hand, however, and taking a very decent aim at the countenance of the figure.", "'제아무리 정확히 겨누어 던진들, 그것은 아무 저항 없이 나를 꿰뚫고 지나가 뒤의 나무에다 그 힘을 다 쏟을 뿐이오. 나는 영혼이오.'"], ["“Who are you?”", "'그래, 대체 여기서 무엇을 바라는 거요?' 세입자가 더듬거렸소."], ["“Don’t throw that poker at me,”", "'이 방에서.' 유령이 대답했소."], ["replied the form; “if you hurled it with ever so sure an aim, it would pass through me, without resistance, and expend its force on the wood behind.", "'나의 세속적 파멸이 이루어졌고, 나와 내 자식들이 거지가 되었소. 이 진열장에는 여러 해 동안 쌓여 온, 길고도 긴 소송의 서류들이 보관되어 있었소."], ["I am a spirit.” “And pray, what do you want here?”", "이 방에서, 내가 슬픔과 오래 미루어진 희망 끝에 죽고 나자, 교활한 독수리 두 마리가, 내가 비참한 일생 동안 다투어 온 그 재산을 나누어 가졌고, 끝내 내 불행한 후손들에겐 단 한 푼도 남지 않았소."], ["faltered the tenant. “In this room,”", "나는 그자들을 이곳에서 겁주어 쫓아냈고, 그날 이후로 밤이면—내가 이 땅을 다시 찾을 수 있는 유일한 때라오—오래 끌어온 내 비참의 무대를 어슬렁거려 왔소."], ["replied the apparition, “my worldly ruin was worked, and I and my children beggared.", "이 거처는 내 것이오. 내게 비워 주시오.'"], ["In this press, the papers in a long, long suit, which accumulated for years, were deposited.", "'굳이 여기 나타나야 하겠다면야.' 유령의 그 지루한 진술 동안 마음을 추스를 틈을 얻은 세입자가 말했소."], ["In this room, when I had died of grief, and long-deferred hope, two wily harpies divided the wealth for which I had contested during a wretched existence, and of which, at last, not one farthing was left for my unhappy descendants.", "'더없이 기꺼이 점유권을 내드리리다. 다만 허락하신다면 한 가지 물어보고 싶소.'"], ["I terrified them from the spot, and since that day have prowled by night--the only period at which I can revisit the earth--about the scenes of my long-protracted misery.", "'말하시오.' 유령이 엄하게 말했소."], ["This apartment is mine: leave it to me.”", "'저.' 세입자가 말했소."], ["“If you insist upon making your appearance here,”", "'이 말을 당신께 개인적으로 적용하는 건 아니오. 내가 들어 본 대부분의 유령에게 똑같이 해당하는 말이니까."], ["said the tenant, who had had time to collect his presence of mind during this prosy statement of the ghost’s, “I shall give up possession with the greatest pleasure; but I should like to ask you one question, if you will allow me.”", "하지만 내가 보기엔 좀 앞뒤가 안 맞는 듯하오. 당신은 이 땅의 가장 아름다운 곳들을 찾아갈 기회가 있을 텐데—당신에게 공간이란 아무것도 아닐 테니 말이오—어째서 늘 하필 가장 비참했던 바로 그 장소들로만 정확히 돌아오는 거요?'"], ["“Say on,”", "'아이고, 그건 참으로 옳은 말이오."], ["said the apparition sternly.", "전에는 그런 생각을 미처 못 했군.' 유령이 말했소."], ["“Well,”", "'보시다시피, 선생.'"], ["said the tenant, “I don’t apply the observation personally to you, because it is equally applicable to most of the ghosts I ever heard of; but it does appear to me somewhat inconsistent, that when you have an opportunity of visiting the fairest spots of earth--for I suppose space is nothing to you--you should always return exactly to the very places where you have been most miserable.”", "세입자가 말을 이었소. '여긴 아주 불편한 방이오."], ["“Egad, that’s very true; I never thought of that before,”", "저 진열장 꼴을 보아하니 빈대가 전혀 없지도 않을 것 같소. 그러니 정말이지 훨씬 더 안락한 거처를 찾으실 수 있을 거요."], ["said the ghost. “You see, Sir,”", "게다가 런던의 기후는 지극히 불쾌하기 짝이 없으니 말할 것도 없고요.'"], ["pursued the tenant, “this is a very uncomfortable room.", "'당신 말이 참으로 옳소, 선생.' 유령이 정중하게 말했소."], ["From the appearance of that press, I should be disposed to say that it is not wholly free from bugs; and I really think you might find much more comfortable quarters: to say nothing of the climate of London, which is extremely disagreeable.”", "'지금껏 그런 생각이 떠오른 적이 없었구려. 당장 공기를 바꿔 보리다.'—그리고 실은, 그가 말하는 동안 이미 사라지기 시작하더니, 다리는 아예 완전히 자취를 감추었소."], ["“You are very right, Sir,”", "'그리고, 선생.' 세입자가 그를 향해 외쳤소."], ["said the ghost politely, “it never struck me till now; I’ll try change of air directly”--and, in fact, he began to vanish as he spoke; his legs, indeed, had quite disappeared.", "'낡고 텅 빈 집들을 출몰하는 일에 지금 골몰해 계신 다른 신사 숙녀분들께, 다른 데 가면 훨씬 더 편안하리라고 일러 주실 친절을 베푸신다면, 사회에 참으로 큰 은덕을 베푸시는 거요.' '그러리다.'"], ["“And if, Sir,”", "유령이 대답했소."], ["said the tenant, calling after him, “if you would have the goodness to suggest to the other ladies and gentlemen who are now engaged in haunting old empty houses, that they might be much more comfortable elsewhere, you will confer a very great benefit on society.”", "'우리는 어지간히 둔한 작자들임이 틀림없소—참으로 둔한 작자들 말이오. 어떻게 우리가 그토록 멍청할 수 있었는지 도무지 모르겠구려.'"], ["“I will,”", "이 말과 함께 영혼은 사라졌소."], ["replied the ghost; “we must be dull fellows--very dull fellows, indeed; I can’t imagine how we can have been so stupid.”", "그리고 자못 주목할 만한 것은.\" 노인이 식탁을 빈틈없이 둘러보며 덧붙였다."], ["With these words, the spirit disappeared; and what is rather remarkable,’ added the old man, with a shrewd look round the table, ‘he never came back again.’", "\"그가 두 번 다시 돌아오지 않았다는 거요.\""]], [["‘That ain’t bad, if it’s true,’ said the man in the Mosaic studs, lighting a fresh cigar.", "\"사실이라면 그리 나쁘지 않군요.\" 모자이크 장식 단추를 단 사내가 새 시가에 불을 붙이며 말했다."]], [["‘If!’ exclaimed the old man, with a look of excessive contempt. ‘I suppose,’ he added, turning to Lowten, ‘he’ll say next, that my story about the queer client we had, when I was in an attorney’s office, is not true either--I shouldn’t wonder.’", "\"사실이라면이라고!\" 노인이 지극한 경멸의 표정으로 외쳤다. \"내 짐작에는.\" 그가 로튼을 돌아보며 덧붙였다. \"이 친구는 다음엔, 내가 변호사 사무실에 있을 적에 우리에게 찾아온 그 기이한 의뢰인 이야기도 사실이 아니라고 할 게요—그래도 난 놀라지 않겠지만.\""]], [["‘I shan’t venture to say anything at all about it, seeing that I never heard the story,’ observed the owner of the Mosaic decorations.", "\"저야 그 이야기를 들어 본 적이 없으니, 그것에 대해선 아무 말도 감히 못 하지요.\" 모자이크 장식의 소유자가 말했다."]], [["‘I wish you would repeat it, Sir,’ said Mr.", "\"그 이야기를 한번 들려주시지요, 선생.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Ah, do,’ said Lowten, ‘nobody has heard it but me, and I have nearly forgotten it.’", "\"아, 그래요.\" 로튼이 말했다. \"그건 저 말고는 아무도 못 들었는데, 저마저 거의 잊어버렸으니까요.\""]], [["The old man looked round the table, and leered more horribly than ever, as if in triumph, at the attention which was depicted in every face.", "노인은 식탁을 둘러보았고, 모든 얼굴에 그려진 주목에 의기양양한 듯, 어느 때보다 더 소름 끼치게 곁눈질했다."], ["Then rubbing his chin with his hand, and looking up to the ceiling as if to recall the circumstances to his memory, he began as follows:--", "그러고는 손으로 턱을 문지르고, 그 정황들을 기억에서 불러내려는 듯 천장을 올려다보며, 다음과 같이 시작했다. 기이한 의뢰인에 관한 노인의 이야기"]], [["# THE OLD MAN’S TALE ABOUT THE QUEER CLIENT", "# THE OLD MAN’S TALE ABOUT THE QUEER CLIENT"]], [["‘It matters little,’ said the old man, ‘where, or how, I picked up this brief history.", "\"내가 이 짤막한 사연을 어디서, 어떻게 주워듣게 됐는지는 별로 중요치 않소.\""], ["If I were to relate it in the order in which it reached me, I should commence in the middle, and when I had arrived at the conclusion, go back for a beginning.", "노인이 말했다. \"이것이 내게 전해진 순서대로 이야기하려 든다면, 중간에서 시작해 결말에 이르렀다가 다시 처음을 찾아 거슬러 가야 할 거요."], ["It is enough for me to say that some of its circumstances passed before my own eyes; for the remainder I know them to have happened, and there are some persons yet living, who will remember them but too well.", "그저 그 정황의 일부는 내 눈앞에서 지나간 일이라는 것, 나머지는 실제로 일어난 일임을 내가 안다는 것, 그리고 그것을 너무도 잘 기억할 사람들이 아직 더러 살아 있다는 것만 말해 두면 되겠소."]], [["‘In the Borough High Street, near St. George’s Church, and on the same side of the way, stands, as most people know, the smallest of our debtors’ prisons, the Marshalsea.", "자치구 큰길, 세인트 조지 교회 가까이, 그 길의 같은 편에, 대다수 사람들이 알다시피, 우리네 채무자 감옥 가운데 가장 작은 마셜시가 서 있소."], ["Although in later times it has been a very different place from the sink of filth and dirt it once was, even its improved condition holds out but little temptation to the extravagant, or consolation to the improvident.", "후대에 와서는 한때의 그 오물과 더러움의 소굴과는 사뭇 다른 곳이 되었지만, 개선된 지금의 상태조차도 낭비하는 자에게 별다른 유혹이 되지 못하고, 앞날을 헤아리지 못하는 자에게 별다른 위안이 되지 못하오."], ["The condemned felon has as good a yard for air and exercise in Newgate, as the insolvent debtor in the Marshalsea Prison.", "사형을 선고받은 중죄인이 뉴게이트에서 누리는 바람 쐬고 운동할 마당이, 마셜시 감옥의 지급 불능 채무자가 누리는 것만큼이나 좋다오."], ["[Better.", "[더 낫지."], ["But this is past, in a better age, and the prison exists no longer.]", "하지만 이는 지난 일, 더 나은 시대에 지나간 일이고, 그 감옥은 이제 존재하지 않소.]"]], [["‘It may be my fancy, or it may be that I cannot separate the place from the old recollections associated with it, but this part of London I cannot bear.", "내 공상일지도 모르고, 아니면 그곳을 그에 얽힌 옛 기억들로부터 떼어 낼 수 없어서일지도 모르나, 나는 런던의 이 일대를 견딜 수가 없소. 거리는 넓고 가게들은 널찍하며, 지나가는 마차 소리와 끊임없이 흐르는 인파의 발소리—교통의 온갖 분주한 소리들이 아침부터 한밤중까지 거기서 울리지만, 그 둘레의 골목들은 누추하고 답답하오."], ["The street is broad, the shops are spacious, the noise of passing vehicles, the footsteps of a perpetual stream of people--all the busy sounds of traffic, resound in it from morn to midnight; but the streets around are mean and close; poverty and debauchery lie festering in the crowded alleys; want and misfortune are pent up in the narrow prison; an air of gloom and dreariness seems, in my eyes at least, to hang about the scene, and to impart to it a squalid and sickly hue.", "가난과 방탕이 붐비는 골목에 곪아 들고, 궁핍과 불운이 좁은 감옥에 갇혀 있소. 적어도 내 눈에는, 음울하고 황량한 기운이 그 풍경에 감돌면서, 거기에 더럽고 병약한 빛깔을 입히는 것만 같소."]], [["‘Many eyes, that have long since been closed in the grave, have looked round upon that scene lightly enough, when entering the gate of the old Marshalsea Prison for the first time; for despair seldom comes with the first severe shock of misfortune.", "그 옛 마셜시 감옥의 문으로 처음 들어설 때, 지금은 무덤 속에 닫힌 지 오래된 숱한 눈들이 그 풍경을 제법 가볍게 둘러보았을 거요. 절망이란 불운의 첫 호된 충격과 함께 찾아오는 일이 드무니까."], ["A man has confidence in untried friends, he remembers the many offers of service so freely made by his boon companions when he wanted them not; he has hope--the hope of happy inexperience--and however he may bend beneath the first shock, it springs up in his bosom, and flourishes there for a brief space, until it droops beneath the blight of disappointment and neglect.", "사람은 시험해 보지 않은 친구들에 대한 믿음을 품고, 자신이 도움을 바라지 않을 때 술친구들이 그토록 선뜻 베풀던 숱한 봉사의 제안을 떠올리며, 희망을—행복한 무경험의 희망을—품소. 첫 충격에 아무리 무너진다 한들, 그것은 가슴속에서 다시 솟아올라 잠시 동안 거기서 피어나다가, 마침내 실망과 외면의 시들음 아래 고개를 떨구지."], ["How soon have those same eyes, deeply sunken in the head, glared from faces wasted with famine, and sallow from confinement, in days when it was no figure of speech to say that debtors rotted in prison, with no hope of release, and no prospect of liberty!", "채무자들이 풀려날 가망도, 자유의 전망도 없이 감옥에서 썩어 간다는 말이 결코 비유가 아니던 시절, 그 똑같은 눈들이 머리 깊숙이 꺼져 들어, 굶주림에 야위고 감금으로 누렇게 뜬 얼굴에서 이글거리기까지는 얼마나 빨랐던가!"], ["The atrocity in its full extent no longer exists, but there is enough of it left to give rise to occurrences that make the heart bleed.", "그 잔혹함이 온전한 정도로는 더 이상 존재하지 않으나, 가슴을 피 흘리게 하는 일들을 일으키기에 족할 만큼은 아직 남아 있소."]], [["‘Twenty years ago, that pavement was worn with the footsteps of a mother and child, who, day by day, so surely as the morning came, presented themselves at the prison gate; often after a night of restless misery and anxious thoughts, were they there, a full hour too soon, and then the young mother turning meekly away, would lead the child to the old bridge, and raising him in her arms to show him the glistening water, tinted with the light of the morning’s sun, and stirring with all the bustling preparations for business and pleasure that the river presented at that early hour, endeavour to interest his thoughts in the objects before him.", "이십 년 전, 그 보도는 한 어머니와 아이의 발소리에 닳아 있었소. 그들은 날마다, 아침이 오면 어김없이 감옥 문 앞에 모습을 드러냈지."], ["But she would quickly set him down, and hiding her face in her shawl, give vent to the tears that blinded her; for no expression of interest or amusement lighted up his thin and sickly face.", "안식 없는 비참과 근심 어린 생각으로 지새운 밤 끝에, 꼬박 한 시간이나 일찍 거기 와 있는 일도 잦았소. 그러면 젊은 어머니는 온순히 돌아서서 아이를 옛 다리로 데려가, 두 팔로 안아 올려 반짝이는 강물을—아침 햇살로 물들고, 그 이른 시각 강이 펼쳐 보이는 사업과 즐거움의 온갖 분주한 채비로 일렁이는 그 물을—보여 주며, 눈앞의 것들에 아이의 마음을 끌어 보려 애썼소."], ["His recollections were few enough, but they were all of one kind--all connected with the poverty and misery of his parents.", "하지만 그녀는 곧 아이를 내려놓고, 숄에 얼굴을 묻은 채, 눈을 가리는 눈물을 쏟아 내곤 했소. 아이의 여위고 병약한 얼굴에는 흥미나 즐거움의 빛이 도무지 어리지 않았으니까."], ["Hour after hour had he sat on his mother’s knee, and with childish sympathy watched the tears that stole down her face, and then crept quietly away into some dark corner, and sobbed himself to sleep.", "그 아이의 기억은 몇 개 되지 않았으나, 죄다 한 가지뿐이었소—모두가 제 부모의 가난과 비참에 얽힌 것이었지. 그 아이는 한 시간 또 한 시간 어머니 무릎에 앉아, 어린아이다운 연민으로 어머니 얼굴을 타고 흐르는 눈물을 지켜보다가, 어느 어두운 구석으로 조용히 기어가 흐느끼다 잠들곤 했소."], ["The hard realities of the world, with many of its worst privations--hunger and thirst, and cold and want--had all come home to him, from the first dawnings of reason; and though the form of childhood was there, its light heart, its merry laugh, and sparkling eyes were wanting.", "세상의 모진 현실은, 그 가장 혹독한 결핍의 여러 가지—굶주림과 목마름, 추위와 궁핍—와 더불어, 이성이 처음 동트던 때부터 그 아이에게 죄다 다가와 있었소. 그리고 어린아이의 형상은 거기 있었으나, 그 가벼운 마음도, 그 명랑한 웃음도, 반짝이는 눈동자도 없었소."]], [["‘The father and mother looked on upon this, and upon each other, with thoughts of agony they dared not breathe in words.", "아버지와 어머니는 이것을 바라보았고, 또 서로를 바라보았소. 차마 말로 내뱉지 못할 고뇌의 생각을 품은 채로."], ["The healthy, strong- made man, who could have borne almost any fatigue of active exertion, was wasting beneath the close confinement and unhealthy atmosphere of a crowded prison.", "활발히 일하는 데서 오는 거의 어떤 피로도 견딜 수 있었을 건강하고 다부진 사내는, 붐비는 감옥의 갑갑한 감금과 건강에 해로운 공기 아래 쇠약해져 갔소."], ["The slight and delicate woman was sinking beneath the combined effects of bodily and mental illness.", "가냘프고 연약한 여인은 육체와 정신의 병이 한데 미친 영향 아래 가라앉아 갔소."], ["The child’s young heart was breaking.", "아이의 어린 가슴은 무너지고 있었소."]], [["‘Winter came, and with it weeks of cold and heavy rain.", "겨울이 왔고, 그와 함께 몇 주에 걸친 춥고 거센 비가 왔소."], ["The poor girl had removed to a wretched apartment close to the spot of her husband’s imprisonment; and though the change had been rendered necessary by their increasing poverty, she was happier now, for she was nearer him.", "그 가엾은 여인은 남편이 갇힌 곳 바로 가까이의 누추한 거처로 옮겨 갔소. 그 이사는 날로 깊어 가는 가난 탓에 어쩔 수 없이 하게 된 것이었으나, 그녀는 이제 남편에게 더 가까웠으니 더 행복했소."], ["For two months, she and her little companion watched the opening of the gate as usual.", "두 달 동안 그녀와 그 어린 동행은 여느 때처럼 문 열리는 것을 지켜보았소."], ["One day she failed to come, for the first time.", "어느 날 그녀는 처음으로 오지 못했소."], ["Another morning arrived, and she came alone.", "또 다른 아침이 왔고, 그녀는 혼자 왔소."], ["The child was dead.", "아이가 죽은 것이었소."]], [["‘They little know, who coldly talk of the poor man’s bereavements, as a happy release from pain to the departed, and a merciful relief from expense to the survivor--they little know, I say, what the agony of those bereavements is.", "가난한 자의 사별을, 떠난 이에게는 고통에서 벗어나는 행복한 해방이요 남은 이에게는 비용에서 벗어나는 자비로운 안도라고 차갑게 입에 올리는 자들은, 그 사별의 고뇌가 어떤 것인지를 거의 모르오—거의 모른다고 나는 말하오."], ["A silent look of affection and regard when all other eyes are turned coldly away--the consciousness that we possess the sympathy and affection of one being when all others have deserted us--is a hold, a stay, a comfort, in the deepest affliction, which no wealth could purchase, or power bestow.", "다른 모든 눈이 차갑게 돌아설 때 보내오는 애정과 존중의 말 없는 눈길, 다른 모든 이가 우리를 버렸을 때 한 존재의 연민과 애정을 우리가 지니고 있다는 그 의식—그것은 가장 깊은 고난 속에서, 어떤 재물로도 살 수 없고 어떤 권력으로도 베풀 수 없는 의지처요, 버팀목이요, 위안이라오."], ["The child had sat at his parents’ feet for hours together, with his little hands patiently folded in each other, and his thin wan face raised towards them.", "그 아이는 부모의 발치에 몇 시간이고 함께 앉아, 작은 두 손을 참을성 있게 맞잡고, 여위고 핼쑥한 얼굴을 그들을 향해 쳐든 채 있곤 했소."], ["They had seen him pine away, from day to day; and though his brief existence had been a joyless one, and he was now removed to that peace and rest which, child as he was, he had never known in this world, they were his parents, and his loss sank deep into their souls.", "그들은 그 아이가 날이면 날마다 시들어 가는 것을 지켜보았소. 그 짧은 생애가 기쁨이라곤 없는 것이었고, 이제는 어린아이였음에도 이 세상에서 결코 알지 못했던 그 평화와 안식으로 옮겨 갔다 한들, 그들은 그 아이의 부모였고, 그 상실은 그들 영혼 깊숙이 가라앉았소."]], [["‘It was plain to those who looked upon the mother’s altered face, that death must soon close the scene of her adversity and trial.", "어머니의 변해 버린 얼굴을 바라본 이들에게는, 죽음이 머지않아 그녀의 역경과 시련의 무대를 닫으리라는 것이 분명했소."], ["Her husband’s fellow-prisoners shrank from obtruding on his grief and misery, and left to himself alone, the small room he had previously occupied in common with two companions.", "남편의 동료 죄수들은 그의 슬픔과 비참을 방해하기를 꺼려, 그가 전에 두 동료와 함께 쓰던 작은 방을 그 혼자에게 내주었소."], ["She shared it with him; and lingering on without pain, but without hope, her life ebbed slowly away.", "그녀는 그와 그 방을 함께 썼고, 고통 없이, 그러나 희망도 없이 명을 이어 가다가, 그 생명이 천천히 스러져 갔소."]], [["‘She had fainted one evening in her husband’s arms, and he had borne her to the open window, to revive her with the air, when the light of the moon falling full upon her face, showed him a change upon her features, which made him stagger beneath her weight, like a helpless infant.", "어느 저녁 그녀는 남편의 품에서 정신을 잃었고, 그는 그녀를 되살리려 바람을 쐬게 하려고 열린 창가로 그녀를 안고 갔소. 그때 달빛이 그녀의 얼굴에 가득 떨어지며, 그에게 그녀의 이목구비에 어린 변화를 보여 주었으니, 그는 무력한 갓난아이처럼 그녀의 무게 아래 비틀거렸소."]], [["‘“Set me down, George,”", "'나를 내려 줘요, 조지.'"], ["she said faintly.", "그녀가 힘없이 말했소."], ["He did so, and seating himself beside her, covered his face with his hands, and burst into tears.", "그가 그렇게 하고는, 그녀 곁에 앉아 두 손으로 얼굴을 가린 채 울음을 터뜨렸소."]], [["‘“It is very hard to leave you, George,”", "'당신을 두고 가기가 너무도 힘드네요, 조지.'"], ["she said; “but it is God’s will, and you must bear it for my sake.", "그녀가 말했소. '하지만 이건 하느님의 뜻이니, 나를 위해서라도 견뎌야 해요."], ["Oh!", "오!"], ["how I thank Him for having taken our boy!", "우리 아이를 데려가 주신 그분께 얼마나 감사한지!"], ["He is happy, and in heaven now.", "그 애는 이제 행복하게, 천국에 있어요."], ["What would he have done here, without his mother!”", "여기서 엄마 없이 그 애가 어찌 살았겠어요!'"]], [["‘“You shall not die, Mary, you shall not die;”", "'당신은 죽지 않을 거야, 메리, 죽지 않아.'"], ["said the husband, starting up.", "남편이 벌떡 일어서며 말했소."], ["He paced hurriedly to and fro, striking his head with his clenched fists; then reseating himself beside her, and supporting her in his arms, added more calmly, “Rouse yourself, my dear girl.", "그는 주먹 쥔 손으로 제 머리를 치며 황급히 이리저리 거닐다가, 다시 그녀 곁에 앉아 그녀를 두 팔로 받치며 한결 차분히 덧붙였소. '정신 차려요, 여보."], ["Pray, pray do.", "제발, 제발 그래 줘요."], ["You will revive yet.”", "당신은 아직 회복할 거야.'"]], [["‘“Never again, George; never again,”", "'다신 안 돼요, 조지. 다신.'"], ["said the dying woman.", "죽어 가는 여인이 말했소."], ["“Let them lay me by my poor boy now, but promise me, that if ever you leave this dreadful place, and should grow rich, you will have us removed to some quiet country churchyard, a long, long way off--very far from here-- where we can rest in peace.", "'이제 나를 우리 가엾은 아이 곁에 뉘여 줘요. 다만 약속해 줘요, 언젠가 당신이 이 끔찍한 곳을 떠나 부유해지거든, 우리를 어딘가 조용한 시골 교회 묘지로, 아주 멀리, 멀리—여기서 아주 먼 곳으로 옮겨 주겠다고."], ["Dear George, promise me you will.”", "우리가 평화로이 쉴 수 있는 곳으로. 사랑하는 조지, 그러겠다고 약속해 줘요.'"]], [["‘“I do, I do,”", "'약속해, 약속하지.'"], ["said the man, throwing himself passionately on his knees before her.", "사내가 그녀 앞에 격정에 차 무릎을 던지며 말했소."], ["“Speak to me, Mary, another word; one look--but one!”", "'내게 말해 줘요, 메리, 한마디만 더. 눈길 한 번만—단 한 번만!'"]], [["‘He ceased to speak: for the arm that clasped his neck grew stiff and heavy.", "그는 말을 멈췄소. 그의 목을 감았던 팔이 뻣뻣하고 무거워졌기 때문이오."], ["A deep sigh escaped from the wasted form before him; the lips moved, and a smile played upon the face; but the lips were pallid, and the smile faded into a rigid and ghastly stare.", "그의 앞에 있는 쇠잔한 형체에서 깊은 한숨이 새어 나왔고, 입술이 움직이며 얼굴에 미소가 어렸소. 하지만 그 입술은 핏기 없이 창백했고, 그 미소는 굳어 버린 섬뜩한 응시로 잦아들었소."], ["He was alone in the world.", "그는 이 세상에 홀로 남았소."]], [["‘That night, in the silence and desolation of his miserable room, the wretched man knelt down by the dead body of his wife, and called on God to witness a terrible oath, that from that hour, he devoted himself to revenge her death and that of his child; that thenceforth to the last moment of his life, his whole energies should be directed to this one object; that his revenge should be protracted and terrible; that his hatred should be undying and inextinguishable; and should hunt its object through the world.", "그날 밤, 그 비참한 방의 정적과 적막 속에서, 그 가련한 사내는 아내의 시신 곁에 무릎을 꿇고, 하느님을 증인으로 삼아 무서운 맹세를 외쳤소. 그 시각부터 그는 아내와 아이의 죽음을 복수하는 데 자신을 바치겠노라고, 그날 이후 제 생의 마지막 순간까지 온 기력을 이 한 가지 목표에 쏟겠노라고, 그 복수는 길게 끌며 무시무시하리라고, 그 증오는 죽지도 꺼지지도 않은 채 세상 끝까지 그 대상을 쫓으리라고."]], [["‘The deepest despair, and passion scarcely human, had made such fierce ravages on his face and form, in that one night, that his companions in misfortune shrank affrighted from him as he passed by.", "그 단 하룻밤에, 더없이 깊은 절망과 인간의 것이라곤 거의 할 수 없는 격정이 그의 얼굴과 몸에 어찌나 사납게 자국을 남겼던지, 그가 지나갈 때면 불운을 함께한 동료들이 겁에 질려 그에게서 움츠러들었소."], ["His eyes were bloodshot and heavy, his face a deadly white, and his body bent as if with age.", "그의 눈은 핏발이 서고 무거웠으며, 얼굴은 죽은 듯이 희었고, 몸은 노쇠한 듯 굽어 있었소."], ["He had bitten his under lip nearly through in the violence of his mental suffering, and the blood which had flowed from the wound had trickled down his chin, and stained his shirt and neckerchief.", "정신적 고통의 격렬함 속에서 그는 아랫입술을 거의 깨물어 뚫어 놓았고, 그 상처에서 흐른 피가 턱을 타고 흘러내려 셔츠와 목수건을 물들였소."], ["No tear, or sound of complaint escaped him; but the unsettled look, and disordered haste with which he paced up and down the yard, denoted the fever which was burning within.", "눈물 한 방울, 한탄의 소리 한 마디 새어 나오지 않았으나, 그가 마당을 오르내리는 동요된 눈빛과 흐트러진 조급함은 그 안에서 타오르는 열병을 드러내고 있었소."]], [["‘It was necessary that his wife’s body should be removed from the prison, without delay.", "아내의 시신을 지체 없이 감옥에서 내가야 했소."], ["He received the communication with perfect calmness, and acquiesced in its propriety.", "그는 그 통보를 더없이 차분하게 받아들였고, 그것이 마땅함을 수긍했소."], ["Nearly all the inmates of the prison had assembled to witness its removal; they fell back on either side when the widower appeared; he walked hurriedly forward, and stationed himself, alone, in a little railed area close to the lodge gate, from whence the crowd, with an instinctive feeling of delicacy, had retired.", "감옥의 거의 모든 수감자가 그 운구를 지켜보려 모여들었는데, 홀아비가 나타나자 그들은 양옆으로 물러섰소. 그는 황급히 앞으로 걸어 나가, 수위실 문 가까이 작게 난간으로 둘린 구역에 홀로 자리를 잡았소."], ["The rude coffin was borne slowly forward on men’s shoulders.", "군중은 본능적인 배려의 정으로 거기서 물러나 있었지."], ["A dead silence pervaded the throng, broken only by the audible lamentations of the women, and the shuffling steps of the bearers on the stone pavement.", "거친 관이 사람들의 어깨에 얹혀 천천히 앞으로 옮겨졌소. 무리 사이에 죽은 듯한 정적이 감돌았고, 그것을 깨뜨리는 것이라곤 여인들의 또렷한 통곡과, 돌바닥을 끄는 운구꾼들의 발소리뿐이었소."], ["They reached the spot where the bereaved husband stood: and stopped.", "그들은 사별한 남편이 서 있는 곳에 이르러—멈춰 섰소."], ["He laid his hand upon the coffin, and mechanically adjusting the pall with which it was covered, motioned them onward.", "그는 관에 손을 얹고, 그 위에 덮인 관보를 기계적으로 가다듬더니, 그들에게 계속 가라고 손짓했소."], ["The turnkeys in the prison lobby took off their hats as it passed through, and in another moment the heavy gate closed behind it.", "그것이 지나갈 때 감옥 현관의 간수들은 모자를 벗었고, 다음 순간 육중한 문이 그 뒤로 닫혔소."], ["He looked vacantly upon the crowd, and fell heavily to the ground.", "그는 멍하니 군중을 바라보더니, 땅바닥에 무겁게 쓰러졌소."]], [["‘Although for many weeks after this, he was watched, night and day, in the wildest ravings of fever, neither the consciousness of his loss, nor the recollection of the vow he had made, ever left him for a moment.", "이 일이 있고 여러 주 동안, 그는 더없이 거친 열병의 헛소리 속에서 밤낮으로 지켜졌으나, 자신의 상실에 대한 의식도, 자신이 한 맹세에 대한 기억도, 한순간도 그를 떠나지 않았소."], ["Scenes changed before his eyes, place succeeded place, and event followed event, in all the hurry of delirium; but they were all connected in some way with the great object of his mind.", "그의 눈앞에서 장면들이 바뀌고, 장소가 장소를 잇고, 사건이 사건을 뒤따르며 섬망의 온갖 분주함이 펼쳐졌으나, 그것들은 모두 어떤 식으로든 그의 마음의 그 거대한 목표와 이어져 있었소."], ["He was sailing over a boundless expanse of sea, with a blood-red sky above, and the angry waters, lashed into fury beneath, boiling and eddying up, on every side.", "그는 가없는 바다 위를 항해하고 있었고, 위로는 핏빛 붉은 하늘이, 아래로는 광란에 채찍질당한 성난 물결이 사방에서 끓어오르며 소용돌이쳤소."], ["There was another vessel before them, toiling and labouring in the howling storm; her canvas fluttering in ribbons from the mast, and her deck thronged with figures who were lashed to the sides, over which huge waves every instant burst, sweeping away some devoted creatures into the foaming sea.", "앞에는 또 다른 배 한 척이 울부짖는 폭풍 속에서 안간힘 쓰며 고생하고 있었소. 그 돛은 돛대에서 누더기처럼 펄럭였고, 갑판에는 양옆에 몸을 묶은 형체들이 빽빽이 들어차 있었는데, 그 위로 시시각각 거대한 파도가 부서지며 가엾은 몇몇을 거품 이는 바다로 휩쓸어 갔소."], ["Onward they bore, amidst the roaring mass of water, with a speed and force which nothing could resist; and striking the stem of the foremost vessel, crushed her beneath their keel.", "무엇으로도 막을 수 없는 속도와 힘으로, 그들은 포효하는 물 더미 한가운데를 헤치고 앞으로 나아가, 맨 앞 배의 이물을 들이받아 제 용골 아래 그것을 으스러뜨렸소."], ["From the huge whirlpool which the sinking wreck occasioned, arose a shriek so loud and shrill-- the death-cry of a hundred drowning creatures, blended into one fierce yell--that it rung far above the war-cry of the elements, and echoed, and re-echoed till it seemed to pierce air, sky, and ocean.", "가라앉는 난파선이 일으킨 거대한 소용돌이로부터 비명이 솟았는데—익사하는 백 명의 단말마가 하나의 사나운 절규로 한데 뒤섞인 그것은—어찌나 크고 날카롭던지, 자연의 전투 함성 위로 까마득히 울려 퍼지며 메아리치고 또 메아리쳐, 공기와 하늘과 대양을 꿰뚫는 듯했소."], ["But what was that--that old gray head that rose above the water’s surface, and with looks of agony, and screams for aid, buffeted with the waves!", "한데 저것은 무엇인가—물 위로 솟아올라, 고뇌의 표정과 도와 달라는 비명으로 파도와 싸우는 저 늙은 잿빛 머리는!"], ["One look, and he had sprung from the vessel’s side, and with vigorous strokes was swimming towards it.", "한 번 보더니, 그는 뱃전에서 뛰어내려, 힘찬 팔놀림으로 그것을 향해 헤엄쳐 갔소."], ["He reached it; he was close upon it.", "그것에 다다랐고, 바싹 다가갔소."], ["They were his features.", "그것은 바로 그자의 얼굴이었소."], ["The old man saw him coming, and vainly strove to elude his grasp.", "노인은 그가 다가오는 것을 보고, 그 손아귀를 벗어나려 헛되이 발버둥쳤소."], ["But he clasped him tight, and dragged him beneath the water.", "하지만 그는 노인을 꽉 붙잡아 물 밑으로 끌고 내려갔소."], ["Down, down with him, fifty fathoms down; his struggles grew fainter and fainter, until they wholly ceased.", "아래로, 아래로, 그와 함께 쉰 길 아래로. 노인의 발버둥은 점점 희미해지다가 마침내 완전히 멎었소."], ["He was dead; he had killed him, and had kept his oath.", "노인은 죽었소. 그는 노인을 죽였고, 제 맹세를 지켰소."]], [["‘He was traversing the scorching sands of a mighty desert, barefoot and alone.", "그는 맨발에 홀로, 어느 광대한 사막의 타는 듯한 모래를 가로질러 나아가고 있었소."], ["The sand choked and blinded him; its fine thin grains entered the very pores of his skin, and irritated him almost to madness.", "모래가 그를 숨 막히게 하고 눈멀게 했으며, 그 곱고 가는 알갱이들이 살갗의 모공 속까지 파고들어 그를 거의 미칠 지경으로 들볶았소."], ["Gigantic masses of the same material, carried forward by the wind, and shone through by the burning sun, stalked in the distance like pillars of living fire.", "바람에 실려 앞으로 떠밀려 가고, 타는 듯한 태양에 꿰뚫린 똑같은 물질의 거대한 덩어리들이 멀리서 살아 있는 불기둥처럼 우뚝우뚝 걸어 다녔소."], ["The bones of men, who had perished in the dreary waste, lay scattered at his feet; a fearful light fell on everything around; so far as the eye could reach, nothing but objects of dread and horror presented themselves.", "그 황량한 불모지에서 스러진 사람들의 뼈가 그의 발치에 흩어져 있었고, 무시무시한 빛이 주위 만물에 떨어졌소. 눈이 닿는 데까지, 두려움과 공포의 대상밖에는 아무것도 나타나지 않았소."], ["Vainly striving to utter a cry of terror, with his tongue cleaving to his mouth, he rushed madly forward.", "혀가 입천장에 들러붙은 채 공포의 비명을 지르려 헛되이 애쓰며, 그는 미친 듯이 앞으로 내달렸소."], ["Armed with supernatural strength, he waded through the sand, until, exhausted with fatigue and thirst, he fell senseless on the earth.", "초자연적인 힘으로 무장한 채, 그는 모래를 헤치고 나아가다가, 마침내 피로와 갈증에 지쳐 정신을 잃고 땅바닥에 쓰러졌소."], ["What fragrant coolness revived him; what gushing sound was that?", "무슨 향기로운 서늘함이 그를 되살렸는가. 저 콸콸 솟는 소리는 무엇이었던가."], ["Water!", "물이었소!"], ["It was indeed a well; and the clear fresh stream was running at his feet.", "그것은 과연 우물이었고, 맑고 시원한 물줄기가 그의 발치에 흐르고 있었소."], ["He drank deeply of it, and throwing his aching limbs upon the bank, sank into a delicious trance.", "그는 그것을 깊이 들이켜고, 욱신거리는 사지를 둑에 던지더니, 황홀한 혼몽 속으로 가라앉았소."], ["The sound of approaching footsteps roused him.", "다가오는 발소리가 그를 깨웠소."], ["An old gray-headed man tottered forward to slake his burning thirst.", "잿빛 머리의 노인 하나가 타는 듯한 갈증을 축이려 비틀비틀 다가왔소."], ["It was he again!", "또 그자였소!"], ["He wound his arms round the old man’s body, and held him back.", "그는 두 팔로 노인의 몸을 휘감아 끌어당겨 막았소."], ["He struggled, and shrieked for water--for but one drop of water to save his life!", "노인은 발버둥치며 물을 달라고 비명을 질렀소—제 목숨을 구할 단 한 방울의 물을!"], ["But he held the old man firmly, and watched his agonies with greedy eyes; and when his lifeless head fell forward on his bosom, he rolled the corpse from him with his feet.", "하지만 그는 노인을 단단히 붙잡고, 게걸스러운 눈으로 그 고통을 지켜보았소. 그리고 노인의 생기 없는 머리가 그의 가슴 위로 앞으로 떨어지자, 그는 발로 그 시신을 제게서 굴려 냈소."]], [["‘When the fever left him, and consciousness returned, he awoke to find himself rich and free, to hear that the parent who would have let him die in jail--would! who had let those who were far dearer to him than his own existence die of want, and sickness of heart that medicine cannot cure--had been found dead in his bed of down.", "열병이 그를 떠나고 의식이 돌아왔을 때, 그는 깨어나 자신이 부유하고 자유로운 몸이 되었음을, 그리고 자신을 감옥에서 죽게 내버려 두려 했던—기어이!—제 목숨보다 훨씬 더 소중했던 이들을 궁핍과, 약으로도 못 고칠 마음의 병으로 죽게 내버려 둔 그 어버이가, 제 깃털 침대에서 죽은 채로 발견되었다는 소식을 들었소."], ["He had had all the heart to leave his son a beggar, but proud even of his health and strength, had put off the act till it was too late, and now might gnash his teeth in the other world, at the thought of the wealth his remissness had left him.", "그자는 제 아들을 거지로 남길 만한 모진 마음을 충분히 품고 있었으나, 제 건강과 기력을 자랑스러워한 나머지 그 일을 미루다가, 마침내 때를 놓쳤고, 이제 저세상에서 제 태만이 그에게 남겨 준 재산을 생각하며 이를 갈지도 모를 노릇이었소."], ["He awoke to this, and he awoke to more.", "그는 이 사실에 눈떴고, 그 이상의 것에도 눈떴소."], ["To recollect the purpose for which he lived, and to remember that his enemy was his wife’s own father--the man who had cast him into prison, and who, when his daughter and her child sued at his feet for mercy, had spurned them from his door.", "자신이 무엇을 위해 살아가는지 그 목적을 다시 떠올렸고, 자신의 원수가 다름 아닌 제 아내의 친아버지—자신을 감옥에 처넣은 자요, 제 딸과 그 손주가 발치에서 자비를 빌었을 때 문전에서 그들을 박차 버린 자—임을 기억해 냈소."], ["Oh, how he cursed the weakness that prevented him from being up, and active, in his scheme of vengeance!", "오, 그가 일어나 제 복수의 계획에 부지런히 나서지 못하게 막은 그 쇠약함을 그는 얼마나 저주했던가!"]], [["‘He caused himself to be carried from the scene of his loss and misery, and conveyed to a quiet residence on the sea-coast; not in the hope of recovering his peace of mind or happiness, for both were fled for ever; but to restore his prostrate energies, and meditate on his darling object.", "그는 자신을 그 상실과 비참의 무대에서 옮겨, 해안가의 조용한 거처로 데려가게 했소. 마음의 평화나 행복을 되찾으려는 바람에서가 아니었으니, 그 둘은 다 영영 달아나 버렸기 때문이오."], ["And here, some evil spirit cast in his way the opportunity for his first, most horrible revenge.", "다만 무너진 기력을 되살리고, 제 간절한 목표를 골똘히 궁리하기 위해서였소. 그리고 여기서, 어떤 악령이 그가 첫 번째이자 가장 끔찍한 복수를 행할 기회를 그의 앞에 던져 놓았소."]], [["‘It was summer-time; and wrapped in his gloomy thoughts, he would issue from his solitary lodgings early in the evening, and wandering along a narrow path beneath the cliffs, to a wild and lonely spot that had struck his fancy in his ramblings, seat himself on some fallen fragment of the rock, and burying his face in his hands, remain there for hours-- sometimes until night had completely closed in, and the long shadows of the frowning cliffs above his head cast a thick, black darkness on every object near him.", "여름철이었소. 그는 음울한 생각에 잠긴 채, 저녁 일찍 외딴 거처에서 나와, 절벽 아래 좁은 길을 따라, 떠돌다 마음에 든 거칠고 호젓한 곳까지 거닐어 가서, 떨어져 나온 바위 조각에 앉아 두 손에 얼굴을 묻은 채 몇 시간이고 거기 머물곤 했소—때로는 밤이 완전히 깔리고, 머리 위 우뚝 솟은 절벽의 긴 그림자가 그 가까이 만물 위로 짙고 검은 어둠을 드리울 때까지."]], [["‘He was seated here, one calm evening, in his old position, now and then raising his head to watch the flight of a sea-gull, or carry his eye along the glorious crimson path, which, commencing in the middle of the ocean, seemed to lead to its very verge where the sun was setting, when the profound stillness of the spot was broken by a loud cry for help; he listened, doubtful of his having heard aright, when the cry was repeated with even greater vehemence than before, and, starting to his feet, he hastened in the direction whence it proceeded.", "어느 고요한 저녁, 그는 옛 자세 그대로 여기 앉아, 이따금 고개를 들어 갈매기의 비상을 바라보거나, 대양 한복판에서 시작해 해가 지는 바로 그 끝자락까지 이어지는 듯한 그 찬란한 진홍빛 길을 따라 눈길을 옮기고 있었소. 그때 그곳의 깊은 정적을 깨뜨리며 도와 달라는 큰 외침이 들려왔소. 그는 제가 제대로 들었는지 미심쩍어하며 귀를 기울였는데, 그 외침이 전보다 한층 더 격렬하게 되풀이되자, 그는 벌떡 일어나 그 소리가 나는 쪽으로 서둘러 갔소."]], [["‘The tale told itself at once: some scattered garments lay on the beach; a human head was just visible above the waves at a little distance from the shore; and an old man, wringing his hands in agony, was running to and fro, shrieking for assistance.", "그 사연은 한눈에 절로 드러났소. 흩어진 옷가지 몇 점이 모래밭에 놓여 있었고, 사람의 머리 하나가 해안에서 조금 떨어진 파도 위로 간신히 보였으며, 한 노인이 고뇌에 차 두 손을 비틀며 이리저리 뛰어다니면서 도움을 구해 비명을 지르고 있었소."], ["The invalid, whose strength was now sufficiently restored, threw off his coat, and rushed towards the sea, with the intention of plunging in, and dragging the drowning man ashore.", "이제 기력이 어지간히 회복된 그 병약자는 외투를 벗어 던지고, 물속에 뛰어들어 익사하는 자를 해안으로 끌어내려는 작정으로 바다를 향해 내달렸소."]], [["‘“Hasten here, Sir, in God’s name; help, help, sir, for the love of Heaven.", "'이리로 서둘러 오시오, 선생, 하느님의 이름으로. 도와주시오, 도와주시오, 선생, 하늘의 사랑으로."], ["He is my son, Sir, my only son!”", "저 애는 제 아들이오, 선생, 제 외아들이오!'"], ["said the old man frantically, as he advanced to meet him.", "노인이 그를 맞으러 다가오며 미친 듯이 말했소."], ["“My only son, Sir, and he is dying before his father’s eyes!”", "'제 외아들이오, 선생, 그런데 제 아비의 눈앞에서 죽어 가고 있소!'"]], [["‘At the first word the old man uttered, the stranger checked himself in his career, and, folding his arms, stood perfectly motionless.", "노인이 입을 떼어 첫 마디를 내뱉는 순간, 그 낯선 사내는 내닫던 걸음을 멈추고, 두 팔을 포갠 채 미동도 없이 우뚝 섰소."]], [["‘“Great God!”", "'아아 하느님!'"], ["exclaimed the old man, recoiling, “Heyling!”", "노인이 뒷걸음치며 외쳤소. '헤일링!'"]], [["‘The stranger smiled, and was silent.", "낯선 사내는 미소 지을 뿐 말이 없었소."]], [["‘“Heyling!”", "'헤일링!'"], ["said the old man wildly; “my boy, Heyling, my dear boy, look, look!”", "노인이 사납게 말했소. '내 아들, 헤일링, 내 사랑하는 아들, 저기, 저기 좀 봐!'"], ["Gasping for breath, the miserable father pointed to the spot where the young man was struggling for life.", "숨을 헐떡이며, 그 비참한 아비는 젊은이가 목숨을 부지하려 발버둥치는 곳을 가리켰소."]], [["‘“Hark!”", "'들어 봐!'"], ["said the old man.", "노인이 말했소."], ["“He cries once more.", "'저 애가 또 한 번 외쳐."], ["He is alive yet.", "아직 살아 있어."], ["Heyling, save him, save him!”", "헤일링, 저 애를 구해 줘, 구해 줘!'"]], [["‘The stranger smiled again, and remained immovable as a statue.", "낯선 사내는 다시 미소 지었고, 조각상처럼 꼼짝도 하지 않았소."]], [["‘“I have wronged you,” shrieked the old man, falling on his knees, and clasping his hands together. “Be revenged; take my all, my life; cast me into the water at your feet, and, if human nature can repress a struggle, I will die, without stirring hand or foot. Do it, Heyling, do it, but save my boy; he is so young, Heyling, so young to die!”", "'내가 자네에게 잘못했네.' 노인이 무릎을 꿇고 두 손을 맞잡으며 비명을 질렀소. '복수하게. 내 모든 것을, 내 목숨을 가져가게. 자네 발치의 물속에 나를 던져 넣게. 그리고 인간의 본성이 발버둥을 억누를 수 있다면, 나는 손발 하나 까딱 않고 죽겠네. 그리하게, 헤일링, 그리하게. 다만 내 아들을 구해 줘. 저 애는 너무 어려, 헤일링, 죽기엔 너무 어려!'"]], [["‘“Listen,”", "'들어 보시오.'"], ["said the stranger, grasping the old man fiercely by the wrist; “I will have life for life, and here is one.", "낯선 사내가 노인의 손목을 사납게 움켜쥐며 말했소. '나는 목숨에는 목숨을 받겠소."], ["My child died, before his father’s eyes, a far more agonising and painful death than that young slanderer of his sister’s worth is meeting while I speak.", "그리고 여기 하나가 있소. 내 자식은 제 아비의 눈앞에서, 제 누이의 가치를 헐뜯던 저 젊은 비방자가 내가 말하는 지금 맞이하고 있는 것보다 훨씬 더 고통스럽고 괴로운 죽음을 맞아 죽었소."], ["You laughed--laughed in your daughter’s face, where death had already set his hand--at our sufferings, then.", "그때 당신은 웃었소—이미 죽음이 손을 얹은 당신 딸의 얼굴에 대고, 우리의 고통을 비웃으며 웃었소."], ["What think you of them now!", "이제 그 고통을 어찌 생각하오!"], ["See there, see there!”", "저기를 보시오, 저기를!'"]], [["‘As the stranger spoke, he pointed to the sea.", "낯선 사내가 말하며 바다를 가리켰소."], ["A faint cry died away upon its surface; the last powerful struggle of the dying man agitated the rippling waves for a few seconds; and the spot where he had gone down into his early grave, was undistinguishable from the surrounding water.", "희미한 외침 하나가 그 수면 위에서 잦아들었고, 죽어 가는 사내의 마지막 안간힘이 잔물결을 몇 초간 흔들었소. 그리고 그가 이른 무덤으로 가라앉은 그 자리는 둘레의 물과 분간할 수 없게 되었소."]], [["‘Three years had elapsed, when a gentleman alighted from a private carriage at the door of a London attorney, then well known as a man of no great nicety in his professional dealings, and requested a private interview on business of importance.", "삼 년이 흘렀을 무렵, 한 신사가 자가용 마차에서 내려, 당시 직업적 거래에서 그리 깐깐하지 않은 사람으로 잘 알려져 있던 런던의 한 변호사 집 문 앞에 서서, 중요한 용무로 사적인 면담을 청했소."], ["Although evidently not past the prime of life, his face was pale, haggard, and dejected; and it did not require the acute perception of the man of business, to discern at a glance, that disease or suffering had done more to work a change in his appearance, than the mere hand of time could have accomplished in twice the period of his whole life.", "분명 인생의 한창때를 넘기지 않았는데도, 그의 얼굴은 창백하고 초췌하며 풀이 죽어 있었소. 질병이나 고통이 그의 평생을 두 배로 합친 세월 동안 한낱 시간의 손이 이룰 수 있었을 것보다 더 큰 변화를 그의 외양에 빚어냈음을 한눈에 알아보는 데는, 사업가의 예리한 통찰력까지는 필요치 않았소."]], [["‘“I wish you to undertake some legal business for me,”", "'나를 위해 어떤 법률 사무를 맡아 주셨으면 하오.'"], ["said the stranger.", "낯선 사내가 말했소."]], [["‘The attorney bowed obsequiously, and glanced at a large packet which the gentleman carried in his hand.", "변호사는 굽실거리며 절하고, 그 신사가 손에 든 큼지막한 꾸러미를 흘끗 보았소."], ["His visitor observed the look, and proceeded.", "손님은 그 눈길을 알아채고 말을 이었소."]], [["‘“It is no common business,”", "'예사로운 일이 아니오.'"], ["said he; “nor have these papers reached my hands without long trouble and great expense.”", "그가 말했소. '또한 이 서류들이 오랜 수고와 막대한 비용 없이 내 손에 들어온 것도 아니오.'"]], [["‘The attorney cast a still more anxious look at the packet; and his visitor, untying the string that bound it, disclosed a quantity of promissory notes, with copies of deeds, and other documents.", "변호사는 그 꾸러미를 더욱 갈망에 찬 눈길로 던졌소. 그리고 손님은 그것을 묶은 끈을 풀어, 한 무더기의 약속어음과, 증서 사본들과 그 밖의 문서들을 드러냈소."]], [["‘“Upon these papers,”", "'이 서류들로.'"], ["said the client, “the man whose name they bear, has raised, as you will see, large sums of money, for years past.", "의뢰인이 말했소. '여기 이름이 적힌 사내가, 보시다시피 지난 여러 해 동안 거액의 돈을 빌렸소."], ["There was a tacit understanding between him and the men into whose hands they originally went--and from whom I have by degrees purchased the whole, for treble and quadruple their nominal value--that these loans should be from time to time renewed, until a given period had elapsed.", "그와, 이 어음들이 원래 넘어갔던 자들—나는 그들로부터 명목 가치의 세 배, 네 배를 주고 차츰차츰 전부를 사들였소—사이에는 암묵적인 양해가 있었소. 정해진 기한이 지날 때까지 이 차용을 그때그때 갱신해 주기로 한 거요."], ["Such an understanding is nowhere expressed.", "그런 양해는 어디에도 명시되어 있지 않소."], ["He has sustained many losses of late; and these obligations accumulating upon him at once, would crush him to the earth.”", "그는 근래 많은 손실을 입었소. 그러니 이 채무들이 한꺼번에 그에게 쌓이면, 그를 땅바닥에 짓뭉개 버릴 거요.'"]], [["‘“The whole amount is many thousands of pounds,”", "'총액이 수천 파운드에 달하는군요.'"], ["said the attorney, looking over the papers.", "변호사가 서류를 훑어보며 말했소."]], [["‘“It is,”", "'그렇소.'"], ["said the client.", "의뢰인이 말했소."]], [["‘“What are we to do?”", "'우리가 어찌하면 되겠습니까?'"], ["inquired the man of business.", "사업가가 물었소."]], [["‘“Do!”", "'어찌하다니!'"], ["replied the client, with sudden vehemence.", "의뢰인이 갑작스러운 격정으로 대답했소."], ["“Put every engine of the law in force, every trick that ingenuity can devise and rascality execute; fair means and foul; the open oppression of the law, aided by all the craft of its most ingenious practitioners.", "'법의 온갖 장치를 다 동원하고, 교묘한 머리로 짜낼 수 있고 악랄함으로 실행할 수 있는 온갖 술수를 다 쓰시오. 정당한 수단이든 부정한 수단이든."], ["I would have him die a harassing and lingering death.", "법의 노골적인 압제에다, 그 가장 교활한 종사자들의 온갖 술책을 더하시오."], ["Ruin him, seize and sell his lands and goods, drive him from house and home, and drag him forth a beggar in his old age, to die in a common jail.”", "나는 그자가 시달리며 더디게 끌려가는 죽음을 맞기를 바라오. 그를 파멸시키고, 그의 토지와 재물을 압류해 팔아 치우고, 그를 집과 가정에서 내쫓고, 늘그막에 거지로 끌어내어 흔한 감옥에서 죽게 하시오.'"]], [["‘“But the costs, my dear Sir, the costs of all this,”", "'하지만 비용이, 선생, 이 모든 일의 비용이 문제입니다.'"], ["reasoned the attorney, when he had recovered from his momentary surprise.", "변호사가 순간의 놀라움에서 헤어나자 따져 말했소."], ["“If the defendant be a man of straw, who is to pay the costs, Sir?”", "'피고가 빈털터리라면, 그 비용은 누가 치릅니까, 선생?'"]], [["‘“Name any sum,”", "'액수를 말해 보시오.'"], ["said the stranger, his hand trembling so violently with excitement, that he could scarcely hold the pen he seized as he spoke-- “any sum, and it is yours.", "낯선 사내가 말했는데, 그 손이 흥분으로 어찌나 격하게 떨리던지, 말하면서 움켜쥔 펜을 거의 쥐고 있을 수가 없었소—'얼마든, 그것은 당신 것이오."], ["Don’t be afraid to name it, man.", "겁내지 말고 액수를 말하시오, 이 사람아."], ["I shall not think it dear, if you gain my object.”", "내 목표를 이루어 준다면, 나는 그것을 비싸다 여기지 않으리다.'"]], [["‘The attorney named a large sum, at hazard, as the advance he should require to secure himself against the possibility of loss; but more with the view of ascertaining how far his client was really disposed to go, than with any idea that he would comply with the demand.", "변호사는 손실의 가능성에 대비해 자신을 지키기 위해 요구할 선금이라며 큰 액수를 되는대로 말했소. 하지만 그것은 그가 그 요구에 응하리라는 생각에서라기보다는, 자신의 의뢰인이 실제로 어디까지 갈 작정인지를 알아보려는 뜻에서였소."], ["The stranger wrote a cheque upon his banker, for the whole amount, and left him.", "낯선 사내는 그 전액에 대해 제 은행 앞으로 수표를 끊어 주고 떠났소."]], [["‘The draft was duly honoured, and the attorney, finding that his strange client might be safely relied upon, commenced his work in earnest. For more than two years afterwards, Mr.", "그 어음은 어김없이 결제되었고, 변호사는 자신의 기이한 의뢰인이 믿고 의지해도 안전함을 알고는 본격적으로 일에 착수했소."], ["Heyling would sit whole days together, in the office, poring over the papers as they accumulated, and reading again and again, his eyes gleaming with joy, the letters of remonstrance, the prayers for a little delay, the representations of the certain ruin in which the opposite party must be involved, which poured in, as suit after suit, and process after process, was commenced.", "그 뒤로 이 년 넘게, 헤일링 씨는 며칠씩 내리 사무실에 앉아, 쌓여 가는 서류들을 들여다보고, 또 소송이 줄을 잇고 절차가 절차를 잇따라 시작될 때마다 쏟아져 들어오는 항의의 편지들, 잠시만 미뤄 달라는 간청들, 상대편이 틀림없이 빠져들 파멸에 대한 호소들을, 기쁨에 눈을 번득이며 읽고 또 읽었소."], ["To all applications for a brief indulgence, there was but one reply--the money must be paid.", "잠깐의 관용을 구하는 온갖 청원에 돌아오는 대답은 단 하나뿐이었소—돈을 갚아야 한다."], ["Land, house, furniture, each in its turn, was taken under some one of the numerous executions which were issued; and the old man himself would have been immured in prison had he not escaped the vigilance of the officers, and fled.", "토지, 집, 가구가, 발부된 숱한 강제 집행 영장 가운데 하나하나에 따라 차례차례 압류되었소. 그리고 그 노인 자신도, 관리들의 감시를 피해 달아나지 않았더라면 감옥에 갇혔을 거요."]], [["‘The implacable animosity of Heyling, so far from being satiated by the success of his persecution, increased a hundredfold with the ruin he inflicted.", "헤일링의 가차 없는 적의는, 박해의 성공으로 누그러지기는커녕, 그가 끼친 파멸과 더불어 백 배로 커졌소."], ["On being informed of the old man’s flight, his fury was unbounded.", "노인이 달아났다는 소식을 듣자, 그의 분노는 끝이 없었소."], ["He gnashed his teeth with rage, tore the hair from his head, and assailed with horrid imprecations the men who had been intrusted with the writ.", "그는 격분하여 이를 갈고, 제 머리털을 쥐어뜯었으며, 영장을 맡았던 자들에게 끔찍한 저주를 퍼부었소."], ["He was only restored to comparative calmness by repeated assurances of the certainty of discovering the fugitive.", "도망자를 반드시 찾아내겠다는 거듭된 다짐을 받고서야 그는 겨우 어느 정도 진정되었소."], ["Agents were sent in quest of him, in all directions; every stratagem that could be invented was resorted to, for the purpose of discovering his place of retreat; but it was all in vain.", "사방으로 그를 찾는 대리인들이 보내졌고, 그의 은신처를 알아낼 목적으로 궁리해 낼 수 있는 온갖 책략이 동원되었으나, 모두 헛수고였소."], ["Half a year had passed over, and he was still undiscovered.", "반년이 지나도록 그는 여전히 발견되지 않았소."]], [["‘At length late one night, Heyling, of whom nothing had been seen for many weeks before, appeared at his attorney’s private residence, and sent up word that a gentleman wished to see him instantly.", "마침내 어느 늦은 밤, 그 전 여러 주 동안 도무지 모습을 보이지 않던 헤일링이 변호사의 사저에 나타나, 한 신사가 즉시 만나기를 청한다고 위로 전갈을 올렸소."], ["Before the attorney, who had recognised his voice from above stairs, could order the servant to admit him, he had rushed up the staircase, and entered the drawing-room pale and breathless.", "위층에서 그 목소리를 알아챈 변호사가 하인에게 그를 들이라 명하기도 전에, 그는 이미 계단을 뛰어 올라와, 창백하고 숨 가쁜 모습으로 응접실에 들어섰소."], ["Having closed the door, to prevent being overheard, he sank into a chair, and said, in a low voice--", "엿들리지 않도록 문을 닫고, 그는 의자에 털썩 주저앉아 낮은 목소리로 말했소."]], [["‘“Hush!", "'쉿!"], ["I have found him at last.”", "마침내 그자를 찾아냈소.'"]], [["‘“No!”", "'설마!'"], ["said the attorney.", "변호사가 말했소."], ["“Well done, my dear sir, well done.”", "'잘하셨소, 선생, 참 잘하셨소.'"]], [["‘“He lies concealed in a wretched lodging in Camden Town,”", "'그자는 캠던 타운의 누추한 거처에 숨어 있소.'"], ["said Heyling.", "헤일링이 말했소."], ["“Perhaps it is as well we did lose sight of him, for he has been living alone there, in the most abject misery, all the time, and he is poor--very poor.”", "'어쩌면 우리가 그자를 시야에서 놓친 게 차라리 잘된 일인지도 모르오. 그동안 내내 그자는 거기서 더없이 비참하게 홀로 살아왔고, 가난하니까—몹시도 가난하니까.'"]], [["‘“Very good,”", "'아주 좋군요.'"], ["said the attorney.", "변호사가 말했소."], ["“You will have the caption made to- morrow, of course?”", "'물론 내일 체포 영장을 집행하시겠지요?'"]], [["‘“Yes,”", "'그렇소.'"], ["replied Heyling.", "헤일링이 대답했소. '잠깐!"], ["“Stay!", "아니오!"], ["No! The next day.", "모레로 합시다."], ["You are surprised at my wishing to postpone it,”", "내가 그것을 미루려는 게 당신은 의아하겠지.'"], ["he added, with a ghastly smile; “but I had forgotten.", "그가 섬뜩한 미소를 지으며 덧붙였소. '하지만 깜빡했군."], ["The next day is an anniversary in his life: let it be done then.”", "모레는 그자의 생애에서 어떤 기념일이오. 그날 하도록 합시다.'"]], [["‘“Very good,”", "'아주 좋군요.'"], ["said the attorney.", "변호사가 말했소."], ["“Will you write down instructions for the officer?”", "'관리에게 줄 지시 사항을 적어 주시겠습니까?'"]], [["‘“No; let him meet me here, at eight in the evening, and I will accompany him myself.”", "'아니오. 그자를 여기로, 저녁 여덟 시에 나와 만나게 하시오. 내가 직접 그자와 동행하리다.'"]], [["‘They met on the appointed night, and, hiring a hackney-coach, directed the driver to stop at that corner of the old Pancras Road, at which stands the parish workhouse.", "그들은 약속한 밤에 만났고, 삯마차를 빌려, 옛 팽크러스 가의 모퉁이—교구 구빈원이 서 있는 그 자리에 세우라고 마부에게 지시했소."], ["By the time they alighted there, it was quite dark; and, proceeding by the dead wall in front of the Veterinary Hospital, they entered a small by-street, which is, or was at that time, called Little College Street, and which, whatever it may be now, was in those days a desolate place enough, surrounded by little else than fields and ditches.", "그들이 거기서 내렸을 무렵엔 이미 사방이 캄캄했소. 그리고 수의(獸醫) 병원 앞의 막다른 담을 따라 나아가, 그들은 리틀 칼리지 가라고 불리는—혹은 그 당시에 그렇게 불리던—작은 골목길로 들어섰는데, 그곳은 지금이야 어떨지 몰라도 그 시절엔 들판과 도랑 말고는 거의 아무것도 없이 둘러싸인, 제법 황량한 곳이었소."]], [["‘Having drawn the travelling-cap he had on half over his face, and muffled himself in his cloak, Heyling stopped before the meanest-looking house in the street, and knocked gently at the door.", "쓰고 있던 여행 모자를 얼굴 절반쯤까지 내려쓰고 망토로 몸을 감싼 채, 헤일링은 그 거리에서 가장 초라해 보이는 집 앞에 멈춰 서서 문을 가만히 두드렸소."], ["It was at once opened by a woman, who dropped a curtsey of recognition, and Heyling, whispering the officer to remain below, crept gently upstairs, and, opening the door of the front room, entered at once.", "곧 한 여인이 문을 열고 알아본다는 듯 무릎을 굽혀 인사했고, 헤일링은 관리에게 아래에 있으라고 속삭이고는, 조용히 위층으로 살금살금 올라가, 앞방 문을 열고 곧장 들어섰소."]], [["‘The object of his search and his unrelenting animosity, now a decrepit old man, was seated at a bare deal table, on which stood a miserable candle.", "그의 추적과 가차 없는 적의의 대상은, 이제 노쇠한 늙은이가 되어, 보잘것없는 양초 하나가 놓인 휑한 전나무 탁자에 앉아 있었소."], ["He started on the entrance of the stranger, and rose feebly to his feet.", "그는 낯선 사내가 들어서자 흠칫하더니, 힘없이 일어섰소."]], [["‘“What now, what now?”", "'무슨 일이오, 무슨 일이오?'"], ["said the old man.", "노인이 말했소."], ["“What fresh misery is this?", "'이번엔 또 무슨 새로운 고통이오?"], ["What do you want here?”", "여기서 무엇을 바라는 거요?'"]], [["‘“A word with you,” replied Heyling.", "'당신과 할 말이 있소.'"], ["As he spoke, he seated himself at the other end of the table, and, throwing off his cloak and cap, disclosed his features.", "헤일링이 대답했소. 그러고는 탁자 반대편 끝에 앉아, 망토와 모자를 벗어 던져 제 얼굴을 드러냈소."]], [["‘The old man seemed instantly deprived of speech.", "노인은 순간 말문이 막힌 듯했소."], ["He fell backward in his chair, and, clasping his hands together, gazed on the apparition with a mingled look of abhorrence and fear.", "그는 의자에 뒤로 넘어지며, 두 손을 맞잡고, 혐오와 두려움이 뒤섞인 눈으로 그 유령 같은 자를 응시했소."]], [["‘“This day six years,”", "'육 년 전 오늘.'"], ["said Heyling, “I claimed the life you owed me for my child’s.", "헤일링이 말했소. '나는 내 자식의 목숨 값으로 당신이 내게 진 목숨을 요구했소."], ["Beside the lifeless form of your daughter, old man, I swore to live a life of revenge.", "당신 딸의 생기 없는 시신 곁에서, 늙은이여, 나는 복수의 삶을 살겠다고 맹세했소."], ["I have never swerved from my purpose for a moment’s space; but if I had, one thought of her uncomplaining, suffering look, as she drooped away, or of the starving face of our innocent child, would have nerved me to my task.", "나는 단 한순간도 그 목적에서 벗어난 적이 없소. 설령 벗어났던들, 그녀가 시들어 갈 때의 그 불평 없이 고통받던 눈길을, 또 우리 죄 없는 아이의 굶주린 얼굴을 한 번만 떠올렸다면, 그것이 나를 다시 내 과업으로 다잡아 세웠을 거요."], ["My first act of requital you well remember: this is my last.”", "내 첫 번째 보복의 행위를 당신은 잘 기억할 거요. 이것이 내 마지막 행위요.'"]], [["‘The old man shivered, and his hands dropped powerless by his side.", "노인은 부르르 떨었고, 두 손이 옆구리에 맥없이 떨어졌소."]], [["‘“I leave England to-morrow,”", "'나는 내일 잉글랜드를 떠나오.'"], ["said Heyling, after a moment’s pause.", "헤일링이 잠시 멈췄다가 말했소."], ["“To- night I consign you to the living death to which you devoted her--a hopeless prison--”", "'오늘 밤, 나는 당신이 그녀를 몰아넣었던 그 산 채로의 죽음으로—희망 없는 감옥으로 당신을 넘기겠소—'"]], [["‘He raised his eyes to the old man’s countenance, and paused.", "그는 노인의 얼굴로 눈을 들어 올리다가, 말을 멈췄소."], ["He lifted the light to his face, set it gently down, and left the apartment.", "그는 양초를 그 얼굴로 들어 올렸다가, 가만히 내려놓고는, 그 방을 떠났소."]], [["‘“You had better see to the old man,”", "'저 노인을 좀 살펴보는 게 좋겠소.'"], ["he said to the woman, as he opened the door, and motioned the officer to follow him into the street.", "그가 문을 열며 여인에게 말하고는, 관리에게 자기를 따라 거리로 나오라고 손짓했소."], ["“I think he is ill.”", "'아무래도 아픈 것 같소.'"], ["The woman closed the door, ran hastily upstairs, and found him lifeless.", "여인은 문을 닫고 황급히 위층으로 뛰어 올라가, 그가 숨이 끊긴 것을 발견했소."]], [["‘Beneath a plain gravestone, in one of the most peaceful and secluded churchyards in Kent, where wild flowers mingle with the grass, and the soft landscape around forms the fairest spot in the garden of England, lie the bones of the young mother and her gentle child.", "켄트의 가장 평화롭고 외진 교회 묘지 가운데 한 곳, 들꽃이 풀과 어우러지고, 둘레의 부드러운 풍경이 잉글랜드라는 정원에서 가장 아름다운 자리를 이루는 그곳, 소박한 묘석 아래 그 젊은 어머니와 그 가녀린 아이의 뼈가 누워 있소."], ["But the ashes of the father do not mingle with theirs; nor, from that night forward, did the attorney ever gain the remotest clue to the subsequent history of his queer client.’", "하지만 아버지의 유해는 그들의 것과 섞이지 않았소. 또한 그날 밤 이후로, 변호사는 자신의 기이한 의뢰인의 그 이후 행적에 대해 단 한 가닥의 실마리도 끝내 얻지 못했소.\""]], [["As the old man concluded his tale, he advanced to a peg in one corner, and taking down his hat and coat, put them on with great deliberation; and, without saying another word, walked slowly away.", "노인은 이야기를 맺고는, 한쪽 구석의 못으로 다가가 거기 걸린 모자와 외투를 내려, 더없이 느긋하게 그것들을 걸쳐 입었소. 그러고는 한마디 말도 없이 천천히 걸어 나갔다."], ["As the gentleman with the Mosaic studs had fallen asleep, and the major part of the company were deeply occupied in the humorous process of dropping melted tallow-grease into his brandy-and-water, Mr. Pickwick departed unnoticed, and having settled his own score, and that of Mr.", "모자이크 장식 단추를 단 신사는 잠이 들어 버렸고, 일행의 대부분은 그의 브랜디 탄 물에 녹은 양초 기름을 떨어뜨리는 우스꽝스러운 작업에 깊이 빠져 있었으므로, 픽윅 씨는 아무에게도 들키지 않고 자리를 떴다."], ["Weller, issued forth, in company with that gentleman, from beneath the portal of the Magpie and Stump.", "그리고 자신의 계산과 웰러 씨의 계산을 치른 뒤, 그 신사와 함께 매그파이 앤드 스텀프의 정문 아래로 걸어 나왔다."]], [["CHAPTER XXII. MR.", "「그게 자네 주인 짐인가, 새미?」"], ["PICKWICK JOURNEYS TO IPSWICH AND MEETS WITH A ROMANTIC ADVENTURE WITH A MIDDLE-AGED LADY IN YELLOW CURL-PAPERS", "웰러 씨가 화이트채플의 불 여관 마당으로 여행 가방 하나와 작은 트렁크 하나를 들고 들어오는 사랑하는 아들에게 물었다."]], [["That ‘ere your governor’s luggage, Sammy?’ inquired Mr. Weller of his affectionate son, as he entered the yard of the Bull Inn, Whitechapel, with a travelling-bag and a small portmanteau.", "「그보다 더 형편없는 짐작도 할 수 있었을 텐데, 영감님.」"], ["‘You might ha’ made a worser guess than that, old feller,’ replied Mr. Weller the younger, setting down his burden in the yard, and sitting himself down upon it afterwards.", "아들 웰러 씨가 짐을 마당에 내려놓고 그 위에 털썩 주저앉으며 대답했다."], ["‘The governor hisself’ll be down here presently.’", "「주인 양반은 곧 여기로 내려오실 거예요.」"]], [["‘He’s a-cabbin’ it, I suppose?’ said the father.", "「마차 타고 오시는 모양이지?」 아버지가 물었다."]], [["‘Yes, he’s a havin’ two mile o’ danger at eight-pence,’ responded the son.", "「예, 8펜스에 2마일짜리 위험을 즐기고 계시죠.」 아들이 대답했다."], ["‘How’s mother-in-law this mornin’?’", "「새어머니는 오늘 아침 어떠세요?」"]], [["‘Queer, Sammy, queer,’ replied the elder Mr.", "「영 시원찮아, 새미, 시원찮아.」"], ["Weller, with impressive gravity.", "아버지 웰러 씨가 자못 엄숙한 어조로 대답했다."], ["‘She’s been gettin’ rayther in the Methodistical order lately, Sammy; and she is uncommon pious, to be sure.", "「요즘 들어 점점 메소디스트풍으로 돌아서고 있어, 새미. 게다가 어찌나 신앙심이 깊은지 말도 못 한다."], ["She’s too good a creetur for me, Sammy.", "나한테는 과분한 사람이야, 새미."], ["I feel I don’t deserve her.’", "내가 그 사람을 누릴 자격이 없다는 생각이 들어.」"]], [["‘Ah,’ said Mr. Samuel.", "「아.」"], ["‘that’s wery self-denyin’ o’ you.’", "새뮤얼 씨가 말했다. 「그거 참 대단한 극기네요.」"]], [["‘Wery,’ replied his parent, with a sigh.", "「대단하지.」 그의 아버지가 한숨을 쉬며 대답했다."], ["‘She’s got hold o’ some inwention for grown-up people being born again, Sammy--the new birth, I think they calls it.", "「다 자란 어른이 다시 태어난다는 무슨 발명품에 빠졌어, 새미. 그 사람들이 거듭남이라고 부르는 거지, 아마."], ["I should wery much like to see that system in haction, Sammy.", "그 방식이 실제로 돌아가는 걸 한번 보고 싶구나, 새미."], ["I should wery much like to see your mother-in-law born again.", "자네 새어머니가 다시 태어나는 걸 정말 보고 싶어."], ["Wouldn’t I put her out to nurse!’", "그러면 내가 그 사람을 유모한테 갖다 맡길 텐데!」"]], [["‘What do you think them women does t’other day,’ continued Mr.", "「엊그제 그 여편네들이 무슨 짓을 했는지 알겠나.」"], ["Weller, after a short pause, during which he had significantly struck the side of his nose with his forefinger some half-dozen times.", "웰러 씨가 잠깐 말을 멈추고는 집게손가락으로 콧등을 대여섯 번 의미심장하게 두드린 뒤 말을 이었다."], ["‘What do you think they does, t’other day, Sammy?’", "「엊그제 그 여편네들이 무슨 짓을 했는지 알겠나, 새미?」"]], [["‘Don’t know,’ replied Sam, ‘what?’", "「몰라요.」 샘이 대답했다. 「뭔데요?」"]], [["‘Goes and gets up a grand tea drinkin’ for a feller they calls their shepherd,’ said Mr.", "「자기네가 목자라고 부르는 작자를 위해서 대단한 다과회를 열었지 뭐냐.」 웰러 씨가 말했다."], ["Weller. ‘I was a-standing starin’ in at the pictur shop down at our place, when I sees a little bill about it; “tickets half-a-crown.", "「내가 우리 동네 그림 가게 앞에 서서 안을 멀거니 들여다보고 있는데, 그것에 관한 작은 광고지가 눈에 띄더라고."], ["All applications to be made to the committee.", "‘입장권 반 크라운. 모든 신청은 위원회로."], ["Secretary, Mrs. Weller”; and when I got home there was the committee a-sittin’ in our back parlour.", "간사, 웰러 부인.’ 그러고 집에 가 보니 위원회가 우리 집 뒤채 응접실에 떡하니 앉아 있더라."], ["Fourteen women; I wish you could ha’ heard ‘em, Sammy.", "여편네가 열넷이야. 자네도 그 소리를 들었어야 하는 건데, 새미."], ["There they was, a-passin’ resolutions, and wotin’ supplies, and all sorts o’ games.", "거기 앉아서 결의안을 통과시키고, 자금을 표결하고, 별의별 놀음을 다 하더라고."], ["Well, what with your mother-in-law a-worrying me to go, and what with my looking for’ard to seein’ some queer starts if I did, I put my name down for a ticket; at six o’clock on the Friday evenin’ I dresses myself out wery smart, and off I goes with the old ‘ooman, and up we walks into a fust-floor where there was tea-things for thirty, and a whole lot o’ women as begins whisperin’ to one another, and lookin’ at me, as if they’d never seen a rayther stout gen’l’m’n of eight-and-fifty afore.", "그런데 자네 새어머니는 가자고 나를 들들 볶지, 또 가면 무슨 희한한 구경거리가 생길까 싶어 나도 기대가 되지, 그래서 입장권에 이름을 올렸지. 금요일 저녁 여섯 시에 아주 말쑥하게 차려입고는 마누라랑 같이 나서서, 이층으로 올라가니까 거기 서른 명분 찻잔이 차려져 있고, 여편네들이 떼로 모여서 서로 귓속말을 주고받으면서, 쉰여덟 먹은 좀 뚱뚱한 신사를 난생처음 본다는 듯이 나를 쳐다보더라고."], ["By and by, there comes a great bustle downstairs, and a lanky chap with a red nose and a white neckcloth rushes up, and sings out, “Here’s the shepherd a-coming to wisit his faithful flock;”", "이윽고 아래층에서 한바탕 소란이 일더니, 빨간 코에 흰 목도리를 두른 비쩍 마른 작자가 후다닥 올라와서 외치는 거야."], ["and in comes a fat chap in black, vith a great white face, a-smilin’ avay like clockwork. Such goin’s on, Sammy!", "‘목자께서 충실한 양 떼를 찾아오십니다.’ 그러고는 검은 옷을 입은 뚱뚱한 작자가, 큼지막한 흰 얼굴로, 태엽 인형처럼 싱글싱글 웃으면서 들어오더라고."], ["“The kiss of peace,”", "그 꼴이라니, 새미!"], ["says the shepherd; and then he kissed the women all round, and ven he’d done, the man vith the red nose began.", "‘평화의 입맞춤이오.’ 목자가 말하더니 여편네들한테 한 사람씩 빙 돌아가며 입을 맞추는 거야."], ["I was just a-thinkin’ whether I hadn’t better begin too--‘specially as there was a wery nice lady a-sittin’ next me--ven in comes the tea, and your mother-in-law, as had been makin’ the kettle bile downstairs.", "그게 끝나니까 빨간 코 작자가 시작하더라고. 나도 시작하는 게 낫지 않을까 막 생각하던 참이었어—특히 내 옆에 아주 참한 부인이 앉아 있었거든—그런데 그때 차가 들어왔어, 자네 새어머니가 아래층에서 주전자를 끓이고 있었지."], ["At it they went, tooth and nail.", "그러더니 다들 죽기 살기로 달려들더라."], ["Such a precious loud hymn, Sammy, while the tea was a brewing; such a grace, such eatin’ and drinkin’!", "차가 우러나는 동안 어찌나 요란하게 찬송가를 부르던지, 새미. 그 감사 기도하며, 그 먹고 마시는 꼴하며!"], ["I wish you could ha’ seen the shepherd walkin’ into the ham and muffins.", "자네도 그 목자가 햄이랑 머핀을 해치우는 걸 봤어야 하는데."], ["I never see such a chap to eat and drink-- never.", "그렇게 먹고 마시는 작자는 생전 처음 봤다—정말로."], ["The red-nosed man warn’t by no means the sort of person you’d like to grub by contract, but he was nothin’ to the shepherd.", "그 빨간 코 작자도 도급으로 먹여 살리고 싶은 부류는 결코 아니었지만, 목자에 비하면 아무것도 아니었어."], ["Well; arter the tea was over, they sang another hymn, and then the shepherd began to preach: and wery well he did it, considerin’ how heavy them muffins must have lied on his chest.", "그래, 차가 끝나니까 찬송가를 한 곡 더 부르고, 목자가 설교를 시작했지. 그 머핀이 가슴에 얼마나 묵직하게 얹혔을지 생각하면 제법 잘하더라고."], ["Presently he pulls up, all of a sudden, and hollers out, “Where is the sinner; where is the mis’rable sinner?”", "한참 하다가 갑자기 딱 멈추더니 고래고래 소리치는 거야."], ["Upon which, all the women looked at me, and began to groan as if they was a-dying. I thought it was rather sing’ler, but howsoever, I says nothing.", "‘죄인은 어디 있는가, 비참한 죄인은 어디 있는가?’ 그러자 여편네들이 죄다 나를 쳐다보면서 죽어 가는 사람들처럼 신음을 내기 시작하더라."], ["Presently he pulls up again, and lookin’ wery hard at me, says, “Where is the sinner; where is the mis’rable sinner?”", "좀 묘하다 싶었지만, 어쨌든 나는 아무 말도 안 했어. 그런데 한참 있다가 또 멈추더니 나를 빤히 노려보면서 말하는 거야."], ["and all the women groans again, ten times louder than afore. I got rather savage at this, so I takes a step or two for’ard and says, “My friend,”", "‘죄인은 어디 있는가, 비참한 죄인은 어디 있는가?’ 그러자 여편네들이 아까보다 열 배는 더 크게 또 신음을 내더라."], ["says I, “did you apply that ‘ere obserwation to me?”", "이쯤 되니 나도 슬슬 사나워져서, 한두 발짝 앞으로 나서서 말했지."], ["‘Stead of beggin’ my pardon as any gen’l’m’n would ha’ done, he got more abusive than ever:--called me a wessel, Sammy--a wessel of wrath--and all sorts o’ names.", "‘여보쇼.’ 내가 말했어. ‘그 말씀, 나한테 한 거요?’ 그런데 점잖은 신사라면 으레 그랬을 것처럼 사과를 하기는커녕, 더 험한 욕을 퍼붓더라고—나더러 그릇이라더라, 새미—진노의 그릇이라며—별의별 욕을 다 하더라고."], ["So my blood being reg’larly up, I first gave him two or three for himself, and then two or three more to hand over to the man with the red nose, and walked off.", "그래서 나도 피가 제대로 거꾸로 솟아서, 우선 그 작자한테 두세 대 안기고, 그다음에 빨간 코 작자한테 전하라고 두세 대 더 안기고는 걸어 나왔지."], ["I wish you could ha’ heard how the women screamed, Sammy, ven they picked up the shepherd from underneath the table--Hollo!", "자네도 그 여편네들이 어떻게 비명을 질렀는지 들었어야 하는데, 새미, 식탁 밑에서 그 목자를 끌어냈을 때 말이야—이런!"], ["here’s the governor, the size of life.’", "저기 주인 양반이 오시는구나, 실물 크기로 말이야.」"]], [["As Mr. Weller spoke, Mr. Pickwick dismounted from a cab, and entered the yard.", "웰러 씨가 이렇게 말하는 동안 픽윅 씨가 마차에서 내려 마당으로 들어섰다."]], [["‘Fine mornin’, Sir,’ said Mr.", "「좋은 아침입니다, 나리.」"], ["Weller, senior.", "아버지 웰러 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Beautiful indeed,’ replied Mr.", "「참으로 아름다운 아침이군.」"], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘Beautiful indeed,’ echoes a red-haired man with an inquisitive nose and green spectacles, who had unpacked himself from a cab at the same moment as Mr.", "「참으로 아름다운 아침입니다.」 캐묻기 좋아할 것 같은 코에 초록색 안경을 쓴 붉은 머리 사내가 따라 말했는데, 그는 픽윅 씨와 같은 순간에 마차에서 몸을 빼낸 참이었다."], ["Pickwick. ‘Going to Ipswich, Sir?’", "「입스위치로 가십니까, 선생?」"]], [["‘I am,’ replied Mr.", "「그렇소.」"], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘Extraordinary coincidence.", "「기막힌 우연이로군요."], ["So am I.’", "저도 그렇습니다.」"]], [["Mr. Pickwick bowed.", "픽윅 씨는 고개를 숙여 인사했다."]], [["‘Going outside?’ said the red-haired man.", "「바깥자리에 타십니까?」 붉은 머리 사내가 물었다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick bowed again.", "픽윅 씨는 다시 고개를 숙였다."]], [["‘Bless my soul, how remarkable--I am going outside, too,’ said the red- haired man; ‘we are positively going together.’ And the red-haired man, who was an important-looking, sharp-nosed, mysterious-spoken personage, with a bird-like habit of giving his head a jerk every time he said anything, smiled as if he had made one of the strangest discoveries that ever fell to the lot of human wisdom.", "「이런 세상에, 참으로 놀랍군요—저도 바깥자리에 탑니다.」 붉은 머리 사내가 말했다. 「우리는 영락없이 동행하게 되는군요.」 그 붉은 머리 사내는 거드름을 피우는 듯한 인상에 코가 뾰족하고 말투가 알쏭달쏭한 인물이었는데, 무슨 말을 할 때마다 새처럼 고개를 홱홱 젖히는 버릇이 있었고, 인간의 지혜에 일찍이 떨어진 적 없는 가장 기이한 발견이라도 해낸 듯이 빙긋 웃었다."]], [["‘I am happy in the prospect of your company, Sir,’ said Mr.", "「선생과 동행하게 되어 기쁩니다.」"], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Ah,’ said the new-comer, ‘it’s a good thing for both of us, isn’t it?", "「아.」 새로 온 사내가 말했다."], ["Company, you see--company--is--is--it’s a very different thing from solitude--ain’t it?’", "「우리 둘 다한테 좋은 일이지요, 안 그렇습니까? 동행이라는 건, 보십시오—동행이라는 건—혼자인 것과는—참 다른 거지요, 안 그래요?」"]], [["‘There’s no denying that ‘ere,’ said Mr.", "「그건 부인할 수 없는 노릇이죠.」"], ["Weller, joining in the conversation, with an affable smile.", "웰러 씨가 싹싹한 미소를 지으며 대화에 끼어들었다."], ["‘That’s what I call a self-evident proposition, as the dog’s-meat man said, when the housemaid told him he warn’t a gentleman.’", "「그게 바로 제가 말하는 자명한 명제라는 겁니다요, 개고기 장수가 하녀더러 자기는 신사가 아니라는 말을 들었을 때 한 말처럼요.」"]], [["‘Ah,’ said the red-haired man, surveying Mr.", "「아.」"], ["Weller from head to foot with a supercilious look.", "붉은 머리 사내가 멸시하는 눈초리로 웰러 씨를 머리끝부터 발끝까지 훑어보며 말했다."], ["‘Friend of yours, sir?’", "「선생의 친구분이오?」"]], [["‘Not exactly a friend,’ replied Mr.", "「친구라고 하기는 좀 그렇소.」"], ["Pickwick, in a low tone.", "픽윅 씨가 낮은 목소리로 대답했다."], ["‘The fact is, he is my servant, but I allow him to take a good many liberties; for, between ourselves, I flatter myself he is an original, and I am rather proud of him.’", "「사실은 내 하인인데, 내가 꽤 많은 무례를 허용해 주고 있지요. 우리끼리 얘기지만, 이 친구가 보기 드문 인물이라고 자부하고 있고, 또 이 친구가 제법 자랑스럽기도 하거든요.」"]], [["‘Ah,’ said the red-haired man, ‘that, you see, is a matter of taste. I am not fond of anything original; I don’t like it; don’t see the necessity for it. What’s your name, sir?’", "「아.」 붉은 머리 사내가 말했다. 「그건, 보십시오, 취향 문제지요. 나는 보기 드문 것이라면 뭐든 좋아하지 않습니다. 마음에 안 들어요. 그게 왜 필요한지 모르겠소. 성함이 어떻게 되십니까, 선생?」"]], [["‘Here is my card, sir,’ replied Mr.", "「여기 제 명함이 있습니다.」"], ["Pickwick, much amused by the abruptness of the question, and the singular manner of the stranger.", "픽윅 씨가 질문의 느닷없음과 낯선 이의 별난 태도에 적잖이 흥겨워하며 대답했다."]], [["‘Ah,’ said the red-haired man, placing the card in his pocket-book, ‘Pickwick; very good.", "「아.」"], ["I like to know a man’s name, it saves so much trouble.", "붉은 머리 사내가 명함을 수첩에 끼워 넣으며 말했다."], ["That’s my card, sir.", "「픽윅이라. 아주 좋군요."], ["Magnus, you will perceive, sir--Magnus is my name.", "나는 사람 이름을 알아 두는 걸 좋아합니다, 수고를 많이 덜어 주거든요. 그게 제 명함입니다, 선생."], ["It’s rather a good name, I think, sir.’", "매그너스, 보시다시피요—매그너스가 제 이름입니다. 제법 좋은 이름이라고 생각해요, 선생.」"]], [["‘A very good name, indeed,’ said Mr.", "「참으로 좋은 이름이군요.」"], ["Pickwick, wholly unable to repress a smile.", "픽윅 씨가 도저히 미소를 억누르지 못한 채 말했다."]], [["‘Yes, I think it is,’ resumed Mr. Magnus.", "「예, 저도 그렇게 생각합니다.」"], ["‘There’s a good name before it, too, you will observe.", "매그너스 씨가 말을 이었다. 「그 앞에 붙은 이름도 좋지요, 보십시오."], ["Permit me, sir--if you hold the card a little slanting, this way, you catch the light upon the up-stroke.", "잠깐만요, 선생—명함을 이렇게 약간 비스듬히 들면, 올려 쓴 획에 빛이 걸립니다."], ["There--Peter Magnus--sounds well, I think, sir.’", "자—피터 매그너스—듣기 좋지요, 그렇지 않습니까, 선생?」"]], [["‘Very,’ said Mr.", "「아주 그렇소.」"], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Curious circumstance about those initials, sir,’ said Mr.", "「그 머리글자에 묘한 점이 있습니다, 선생.」 매그너스 씨가 말했다."], ["Magnus. ‘You will observe--P.M.--post meridian.", "「보십시오—P.M.—오후라는 뜻이지요."], ["In hasty notes to intimate acquaintance, I sometimes sign myself “Afternoon.”", "친한 사이에 급하게 쪽지를 쓸 때면 가끔 ‘오후’라고 서명하곤 합니다."], ["It amuses my friends very much, Mr. Pickwick.’", "친구들이 아주 재미있어해요, 픽윅 씨.」"]], [["‘It is calculated to afford them the highest gratification, I should conceive,’ said Mr.", "「그들에게 더없는 흡족함을 안겨 주게끔 짜인 것이겠지요.」"], ["Pickwick, rather envying the ease with which Mr. Magnus’s friends were entertained.", "픽윅 씨가, 매그너스 씨의 친구들이 그토록 손쉽게 즐거워한다는 것에 다소 부러움을 느끼며 말했다."]], [["‘Now, gen’l’m’n,’ said the hostler, ‘coach is ready, if you please.’", "「자, 신사분들.」 마부가 말했다. 「준비되시면, 마차가 떠납니다.」"]], [["‘Is all my luggage in?’ inquired Mr.", "「제 짐은 다 실렸습니까?」"], ["Magnus.", "매그너스 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘All right, sir.’", "「다 됐습니다, 선생.」"]], [["‘Is the red bag in?’", "「빨간 가방은 실렸나요?」"]], [["‘All right, Sir.’", "「됐습니다, 선생.」"]], [["‘And the striped bag?’", "「줄무늬 가방은요?」"]], [["‘Fore boot, Sir.’", "「앞쪽 짐칸에요, 선생.」"]], [["‘And the brown-paper parcel?’", "「갈색 종이 꾸러미는요?」"]], [["‘Under the seat, Sir.’", "「좌석 밑에요, 선생.」"]], [["‘And the leather hat-box?’", "「가죽 모자 상자는요?」"]], [["‘They’re all in, Sir.’", "「전부 다 실렸습니다, 선생.」"]], [["‘Now, will you get up?’ said Mr.", "「자, 이제 올라타시겠습니까?」"], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Excuse me,’ replied Magnus, standing on the wheel.", "「실례합니다.」 매그너스가 바퀴 위에 올라선 채 대답했다."], ["‘Excuse me, Mr. Pickwick.", "「실례합니다, 픽윅 씨."], ["I cannot consent to get up, in this state of uncertainty.", "이런 불확실한 상태로는 올라탈 수가 없군요."], ["I am quite satisfied from that man’s manner, that the leather hat-box is not in.’", "저 사람 태도로 보아 가죽 모자 상자가 실리지 않았다고 확신이 듭니다.」"]], [["The solemn protestations of the hostler being wholly unavailing, the leather hat-box was obliged to be raked up from the lowest depth of the boot, to satisfy him that it had been safely packed; and after he had been assured on this head, he felt a solemn presentiment, first, that the red bag was mislaid, and next that the striped bag had been stolen, and then that the brown-paper parcel ‘had come untied.’ At length when he had received ocular demonstration of the groundless nature of each and every of these suspicions, he consented to climb up to the roof of the coach, observing that now he had taken everything off his mind, he felt quite comfortable and happy.", "마부의 엄숙한 항변도 아무 소용이 없어, 결국 가죽 모자 상자를 짐칸 맨 밑바닥에서 끄집어내어 그것이 무사히 실렸음을 그에게 납득시켜야 했다. 그 점에 대해 확신을 얻고 나자, 그는 이번엔 엄숙한 예감이 들어, 처음엔 빨간 가방이 잘못 놓였고, 다음엔 줄무늬 가방을 도둑맞았으며, 그다음엔 갈색 종이 꾸러미가 ‘풀려 버렸다’고 했다. 마침내 이 의심들 하나하나가 모두 근거 없는 것임을 눈으로 직접 확인하고 나서야, 그는 마차 지붕에 올라가기로 했고, 이제 마음의 짐을 다 덜었으니 아주 편안하고 행복하다고 말했다."]], [["‘You’re given to nervousness, ain’t you, Sir?’ inquired Mr.", "「선생은 신경이 예민한 편이시지요, 안 그렇습니까, 선생?」"], ["Weller, senior, eyeing the stranger askance, as he mounted to his place.", "아버지 웰러 씨가, 낯선 이가 제자리에 오르는 동안 곁눈질로 그를 보며 물었다."]], [["‘Yes; I always am rather about these little matters,’ said the stranger, ‘but I am all right now--quite right.’", "「예, 이런 자잘한 일에는 늘 좀 그렇습니다.」 낯선 이가 말했다. 「하지만 이제 괜찮아요—아주 괜찮습니다.」"]], [["‘Well, that’s a blessin’, said Mr.", "「그거 참 다행이군요.」"], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 말했다."], ["‘Sammy, help your master up to the box; t’other leg, Sir, that’s it; give us your hand, Sir.", "「새미, 주인 어른께서 마부석에 오르시는 걸 도와드려라. 그쪽 다리요, 선생, 그렇지."], ["Up with you.", "손을 주십시오, 선생."], ["You was a lighter weight when you was a boy, sir.’", "자, 올라가시죠. 어릴 적엔 훨씬 가벼우셨을 텐데요, 선생.」"]], [["True enough, that, Mr. Weller,’ said the breathless Mr.", "「딱 맞는 말이군, 웰러.」"], ["Pickwick good- humouredly, as he took his seat on the box beside him.", "숨을 헐떡이는 픽윅 씨가 그의 옆 마부석에 앉으며 흔쾌히 말했다."]], [["‘Jump up in front, Sammy,’ said Mr.", "「앞쪽에 올라타라, 새미.」 웰러 씨가 말했다."], ["Weller. ‘Now Villam, run ‘em out.", "「자, 빌럼, 말들을 몰아라."], ["Take care o’ the archvay, gen’l’m’n.", "아치 길 조심하시고요, 신사분들."], ["“Heads,” as the pieman says.", "‘머리 조심’, 파이 장수 말마따나요."], ["That’ll do, Villam.", "됐다, 빌럼."], ["Let ‘em alone.’ And away went the coach up Whitechapel, to the admiration of the whole population of that pretty densely populated quarter.", "내버려 두어라.」 그러자 마차는 화이트채플 거리를 따라 달려 나갔고, 꽤나 인구가 빽빽한 그 동네 주민 전체의 감탄을 자아냈다."]], [["‘Not a wery nice neighbourhood, this, Sir,’ said Sam, with a touch of the hat, which always preceded his entering into conversation with his master.", "「그리 좋은 동네는 아니군요, 선생.」 샘이 주인과 대화를 시작하기에 앞서 늘 그러듯 모자에 손을 살짝 대며 말했다."]], [["‘It is not indeed, Sam,’ replied Mr.", "「정말 그렇구나, 샘.」"], ["Pickwick, surveying the crowded and filthy street through which they were passing.", "픽윅 씨가, 자기들이 지나고 있는 붐비고 더러운 거리를 둘러보며 대답했다."]], [["‘It’s a wery remarkable circumstance, Sir,’ said Sam, ‘that poverty and oysters always seem to go together.’", "「참 묘한 일입니다요, 선생.」 샘이 말했다. 「가난과 굴은 늘 짝을 지어 다니는 것 같단 말이죠.」"]], [["‘I don’t understand you, Sam,’ said Mr.", "「무슨 소린지 모르겠구나, 샘.」"], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘What I mean, sir,’ said Sam, ‘is, that the poorer a place is, the greater call there seems to be for oysters.", "「제 말은요, 선생.」 샘이 말했다."], ["Look here, sir; here’s a oyster-stall to every half-dozen houses.", "「동네가 가난하면 할수록 굴을 찾는 소리가 더 커지는 것 같다는 겁니다요. 보십시오, 선생."], ["The street’s lined vith ‘em.", "집 여섯 채마다 굴 좌판이 하나씩 있어요."], ["Blessed if I don’t think that ven a man’s wery poor, he rushes out of his lodgings, and eats oysters in reg’lar desperation.’", "거리에 쭉 늘어서 있다고요. 사람이 아주 가난해지면 하숙방에서 뛰쳐나와 영락없이 자포자기 심정으로 굴을 먹어 치우는 거라고, 저는 그렇게 생각합니다요.」"]], [["‘To be sure he does,’ said Mr.", "「당연히 그렇지.」"], ["Weller, senior; ‘and it’s just the same vith pickled salmon!’", "아버지 웰러 씨가 말했다. 「절인 연어도 꼭 마찬가지란다!」"]], [["‘Those are two very remarkable facts, which never occurred to me before,’ said Mr. Pickwick.", "「그건 두 가지 다 참으로 주목할 만한 사실이군, 전에는 미처 생각해 본 적이 없었어.」"], ["‘The very first place we stop at, I’ll make a note of them.’", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. 「우리가 처음 멈추는 곳에서 그것들을 적어 두어야겠다.」"]], [["By this time they had reached the turnpike at Mile End; a profound silence prevailed until they had got two or three miles farther on, when Mr.", "이즈음 그들은 마일 엔드의 통행세 징수소에 다다랐다."], ["Weller, senior, turning suddenly to Mr. Pickwick, said--", "그 뒤로 두세 마일을 더 갈 때까지 깊은 침묵이 감돌았는데, 그때 아버지 웰러 씨가 갑자기 픽윅 씨를 돌아보며 말했다."]], [["‘Wery queer life is a pike-keeper’s, sir.’", "「참 묘한 인생이지요, 통행세지기란 게 말입니다, 선생.」"]], [["‘A what?’ said Mr.", "「뭐라고?」"], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘A pike-keeper.’", "「통행세지기 말입니다.」"]], [["‘What do you mean by a pike-keeper?’ inquired Mr.", "「통행세지기라는 게 무슨 뜻이오?」"], ["Peter Magnus.", "피터 매그너스 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘The old ‘un means a turnpike-keeper, gen’l’m’n,’ observed Mr.", "「이 영감님은 통행세 징수소를 지키는 사람을 말하는 겁니다, 신사분들.」"], ["Samuel Weller, in explanation.", "새뮤얼 웰러 씨가 설명조로 거들었다."]], [["‘Oh,’ said Mr.", "「아.」"], ["Pickwick, ‘I see.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Yes; very curious life.", "「알겠네. 그래, 참 묘한 인생이지."], ["Very uncomfortable.’", "무척 불편하기도 하고.」"]], [["‘They’re all on ‘em men as has met vith some disappointment in life,’ said Mr.", "「그자들은 죄다 인생에서 무슨 좌절을 겪은 사람들이지요.」"], ["Weller, senior.", "아버지 웰러 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Ay, ay,’ said Mr.", "「그렇지, 그렇지.」"], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Yes.", "「예."], ["Consequence of vich, they retires from the world, and shuts themselves up in pikes; partly with the view of being solitary, and partly to rewenge themselves on mankind by takin’ tolls.’", "그래서 세상을 등지고 징수소에 틀어박히는 거지요. 한편으로는 혼자 있고 싶어서고, 또 한편으로는 통행세를 거두어서 인류에게 복수하려는 거지요.」"]], [["‘Dear me,’ said Mr.", "「저런.」 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick, ‘I never knew that before.’", "「그건 전에 미처 몰랐군.」"]], [["‘Fact, Sir,’ said Mr.", "「사실입니다, 선생.」"], ["Weller; ‘if they was gen’l’m’n, you’d call ‘em misanthropes, but as it is, they only takes to pike-keepin’.’", "웰러 씨가 말했다. 「그자들이 신사였다면 인간 혐오자라고 불렀겠지만, 사정이 사정인지라 그저 통행세지기 노릇을 하는 거지요.」"]], [["With such conversation, possessing the inestimable charm of blending amusement with instruction, did Mr. Weller beguile the tediousness of the journey, during the greater part of the day. Topics of conversation were never wanting, for even when any pause occurred in Mr. Weller’s loquacity, it was abundantly supplied by the desire evinced by Mr. Magnus to make himself acquainted with the whole of the personal history of his fellow-travellers, and his loudly-expressed anxiety at every stage, respecting the safety and well-being of the two bags, the leather hat-box, and the brown-paper parcel.", "이렇듯 재미와 가르침을 함께 버무리는 더없는 매력을 지닌 대화로, 웰러 씨는 하루의 대부분에 걸쳐 여행의 지루함을 달래 주었다. 화제는 결코 동나는 법이 없었으니, 웰러 씨의 수다가 잠시 멎을 때면, 동행들의 신상 내력을 죄다 알고 싶어 하는 매그너스 씨의 욕구가, 또 정거장마다 두 가방과 가죽 모자 상자와 갈색 종이 꾸러미의 안전과 무사함에 대해 큰 소리로 표하는 그의 근심이 그 빈자리를 넉넉히 채워 주었기 때문이다."]], [["In the main street of Ipswich, on the left-hand side of the way, a short distance after you have passed through the open space fronting the Town Hall, stands an inn known far and wide by the appellation of the Great White Horse, rendered the more conspicuous by a stone statue of some rampacious animal with flowing mane and tail, distantly resembling an insane cart-horse, which is elevated above the principal door.", "입스위치의 큰길에서, 길의 왼편으로, 시청 앞 광장을 지나고 나서 조금 더 가면, 멀리까지 그레이트 화이트 호스라는 이름으로 알려진 여관이 서 있다. 그 여관은 큰 문 위에 높이 솟은, 갈기와 꼬리를 휘날리는 어떤 사납기 그지없는 짐승의 돌 조각상 덕분에 한층 눈에 띄는데, 그 조각상은 멀리서 보면 미쳐 버린 짐마차 말을 어렴풋이 닮았다."], ["The Great White Horse is famous in the neighbourhood, in the same degree as a prize ox, or a county-paper-chronicled turnip, or unwieldy pig--for its enormous size.", "그레이트 화이트 호스는 그 일대에서 유명한데, 상을 탄 황소나 지방 신문에 실린 무 또는 거대한 돼지가 유명한 것과 똑같은 까닭—곧 어마어마한 크기 때문이다."], ["Never was such labyrinths of uncarpeted passages, such clusters of mouldy, ill-lighted rooms, such huge numbers of small dens for eating or sleeping in, beneath any one roof, as are collected together between the four walls of the Great White Horse at Ipswich.", "카펫도 깔리지 않은 복도의 미로며, 곰팡이 슬고 어두컴컴한 방들의 무리며, 먹거나 잠자기 위한 작은 굴 같은 방들의 엄청난 수가, 입스위치의 그레이트 화이트 호스의 네 벽 안에 한데 모여 있는 것만큼 하나의 지붕 아래 모여 있는 곳은 일찍이 없었다."]], [["It was at the door of this overgrown tavern that the London coach stopped, at the same hour every evening; and it was from this same London coach that Mr.", "런던발 마차가 저녁마다 같은 시각에 멈추어 서는 곳이 바로 이 터무니없이 커진 여관의 문 앞이었다."], ["Pickwick, Sam Weller, and Mr. Peter Magnus dismounted, on the particular evening to which this chapter of our history bears reference.", "그리고 우리 이야기의 이 장이 가리키는 바로 그 저녁에, 픽윅 씨와 샘 웰러와 피터 매그너스 씨가 내린 곳도 바로 이 런던발 마차였다."]], [["‘Do you stop here, sir?’ inquired Mr.", "「여기 묵으십니까, 선생?」"], ["Peter Magnus, when the striped bag, and the red bag, and the brown-paper parcel, and the leather hat- box, had all been deposited in the passage.", "피터 매그너스 씨가, 줄무늬 가방과 빨간 가방과 갈색 종이 꾸러미와 가죽 모자 상자가 모두 복도에 내려놓아지자 물었다."], ["‘Do you stop here, sir?’", "「여기 묵으십니까, 선생?」"]], [["‘I do,’ said Mr.", "「그렇소.」"], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Dear me,’ said Mr.", "「이런 세상에.」"], ["Magnus, ‘I never knew anything like these extraordinary coincidences.", "매그너스 씨가 말했다. 「이렇게 기막힌 우연은 처음 봅니다."], ["Why, I stop here too.", "아니, 저도 여기 묵거든요."], ["I hope we dine together?’", "함께 저녁을 드실 수 있겠지요?」"]], [["‘With pleasure,’ replied Mr. Pickwick.", "「기꺼이 그러지요.」"], ["‘I am not quite certain whether I have any friends here or not, though.", "픽윅 씨가 대답했다. 「다만 여기 제 친구들이 와 있는지 어떤지 확실치가 않군요."], ["Is there any gentleman of the name of Tupman here, waiter?’", "여기 텁먼이라는 이름의 신사가 와 있소, 종업원?」"]], [["A corpulent man, with a fortnight’s napkin under his arm, and coeval stockings on his legs, slowly desisted from his occupation of staring down the street, on this question being put to him by Mr.", "뚱뚱한 사내 하나가, 보름은 묵었을 행주를 겨드랑이에 끼고, 그와 동년배쯤 되어 보이는 양말을 신은 채, 픽윅 씨가 이 질문을 던지자 거리를 멀거니 내려다보던 일을 천천히 멈추었다."], ["Pickwick; and, after minutely inspecting that gentleman’s appearance, from the crown of his hat to the lowest button of his gaiters, replied emphatically--", "그러고는 모자 꼭대기부터 각반의 맨 아래 단추까지 그 신사의 행색을 세세히 뜯어본 뒤, 단호하게 대답했다. 「없습니다!」"]], [["‘Nor any gentleman of the name of Snodgrass?’ inquired Mr.", "「스노드그래스라는 이름의 신사도 없소?」 픽윅 씨가 물었다."], ["Pickwick.", "「없습니다!」"]], [["‘No!’", "「윈클도?」"]], [["‘Nor Winkle?’", "「없습니다!」"]], [["‘No!’\n‘My friends have not arrived to-day, Sir,’ said Mr.", "「내 친구들이 오늘 도착하지 않았군요, 선생.」 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick. ‘We will dine alone, then.", "「그러면 우리끼리 저녁을 듭시다."], ["Show us a private room, waiter.’", "별실로 안내해 주게, 종업원.」"]], [["On this request being preferred, the corpulent man condescended to order the boots to bring in the gentlemen’s luggage; and preceding them down a long, dark passage, ushered them into a large, badly-furnished apartment, with a dirty grate, in which a small fire was making a wretched attempt to be cheerful, but was fast sinking beneath the dispiriting influence of the place.", "이 요청이 들어가자, 뚱뚱한 사내는 황송하게도 구두닦이에게 신사분들의 짐을 들여오라고 명한 뒤, 그들을 앞장세워 길고 어두운 복도를 따라 내려가, 더러운 화로가 놓인 크고 가구가 변변찮은 방으로 안내했다. 그 화로에서는 작은 불이 즐거워 보이려고 가련하게 애쓰고 있었으나, 그 장소의 의기소침한 기운에 짓눌려 빠르게 사그라지고 있었다."], ["After the lapse of an hour, a bit of fish and a steak was served up to the travellers, and when the dinner was cleared away, Mr. Pickwick and Mr.", "한 시간이 지난 뒤 여행객들에게 생선 한 토막과 스테이크가 차려졌고, 저녁상이 치워지자 픽윅 씨와 피터 매그너스 씨는 의자를 불가로 끌어당겼다."], ["Peter Magnus drew their chairs up to the fire, and having ordered a bottle of the worst possible port wine, at the highest possible price, for the good of the house, drank brandy-and-water for their own.", "그러고는 여관을 위하는 셈치고 가능한 한 형편없는 포트와인 한 병을 가능한 한 비싼 값에 주문한 뒤, 자기들이 마실 것으로는 브랜디에 물을 탄 것을 마셨다."]], [["Mr. Peter Magnus was naturally of a very communicative disposition, and the brandy-and-water operated with wonderful effect in warming into life the deepest hidden secrets of his bosom. After sundry accounts of himself, his family, his connections, his friends, his jokes, his business, and his brothers (most talkative men have a great deal to say about their brothers), Mr. Peter Magnus took a view of Mr. Pickwick through his coloured spectacles for several minutes, and then said, with an air of modesty--", "피터 매그너스 씨는 천성이 매우 말이 많은 사람이었는데, 브랜디에 물을 탄 것이 놀라운 효력을 발휘하여 그의 가슴 깊이 숨겨 둔 비밀들에까지 생기를 불어넣어 데워 놓았다. 자기 자신과 가족과 인척과 친구들과 농담과 사업과 형제들(말이 많은 사람들은 대개 자기 형제들에 관해 할 말이 아주 많다)에 관해 이런저런 이야기를 늘어놓은 뒤, 피터 매그너스 씨는 색안경 너머로 몇 분 동안 픽윅 씨를 바라보더니, 겸손한 기색을 띠고 말했다."]], [["‘And what do you think--what do you think, Mr. Pickwick--I have come down here for?’", "「그런데 어떻게 생각하십니까—대체 어떻게 생각하시느냐고요, 픽윅 씨—제가 여기에 무엇 때문에 내려왔다고요?」"]], [["‘Upon my word,’ said Mr.", "「솔직히 말해서.」 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick, ‘it is wholly impossible for me to guess; on business, perhaps.’", "「도무지 짐작할 수가 없군요. 사업 때문이려나요.」"]], [["‘Partly right, Sir,’ replied Mr.", "「반은 맞고요, 선생.」"], ["Peter Magnus, ‘but partly wrong at the same time; try again, Mr.", "피터 매그너스 씨가 대답했다. 「하지만 동시에 반은 틀렸습니다."], ["Pickwick.’", "다시 한번 맞혀 보시지요, 픽윅 씨.」"]], [["‘Really,’ said Mr.", "「정말이지.」 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick, ‘I must throw myself on your mercy, to tell me or not, as you may think best; for I should never guess, if I were to try all night.’", "「말씀하시든 말든 좋으실 대로 하시도록 선생의 자비에 맡겨야겠군요. 밤새 맞혀 본다 한들 도저히 알아낼 수가 없겠으니 말입니다.」"]], [["‘Why, then, he-he-he!’ said Mr.", "「그럼, 그러면, 헤헤헤!」"], ["Peter Magnus, with a bashful titter, ‘what should you think, Mr.", "피터 매그너스 씨가 수줍게 킥킥거리며 말했다."], ["Pickwick, if I had come down here to make a proposal, Sir, eh?", "「픽윅 씨, 제가 여기에 청혼을 하러 내려왔다면 어떻게 생각하시겠습니까, 선생, 예?"], ["He, he, he!’", "헤, 헤, 헤!」"]], [["‘Think!", "「생각이라!"], ["That you are very likely to succeed,’ replied Mr.", "성공하실 가능성이 매우 높다고 보지요.」"], ["Pickwick, with one of his beaming smiles.", "픽윅 씨가 그 특유의 환한 미소를 지으며 대답했다."]], [["‘Ah!’ said Mr.", "「아!」 매그너스 씨가 말했다."], ["Magnus. ‘But do you really think so, Mr.", "「하지만 정말 그렇게 생각하십니까, 픽윅 씨?"], ["Pickwick? Do you, though?’", "정말요?」"]], [["‘Certainly,’ said Mr.", "「물론이지요.」"], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘No; but you’re joking, though.’", "「아니, 농담하시는 거죠.」"]], [["‘I am not, indeed.’", "「정말이지 농담이 아닙니다.」"]], [["‘Why, then,’ said Mr.", "「그럼, 그렇다면.」"], ["Magnus, ‘to let you into a little secret, I think so too.", "매그너스 씨가 말했다. 「작은 비밀을 하나 털어놓자면, 저도 그렇게 생각합니다."], ["I don’t mind telling you, Mr. Pickwick, although I’m dreadful jealous by nature--horrid--that the lady is in this house.’ Here Mr.", "픽윅 씨, 제가 천성이 끔찍하게 질투가 많은 사람이긴 합니다만—지독하지요—말씀드려도 상관없겠지요, 그 부인이 이 여관에 있습니다.」"], ["Magnus took off his spectacles, on purpose to wink, and then put them on again.", "여기서 매그너스 씨는 윙크를 하려고 일부러 안경을 벗었다가 다시 썼다."]], [["‘That’s what you were running out of the room for, before dinner, then, so often,’ said Mr.", "「그래서 저녁 식사 전에 그렇게 자주 방을 들락날락하셨던 거로군요.」"], ["Pickwick archly.", "픽윅 씨가 짓궂게 말했다."]], [["‘Hush!", "「쉿!"], ["Yes, you’re right, that was it; not such a fool as to see her, though.’\n‘No!’", "예, 맞습니다, 바로 그거였어요. 그래도 그 부인을 만날 만큼 어리석지는 않지요.」"]], [["‘No; wouldn’t do, you know, after having just come off a journey.", "「안 만나신다고요!」 「예."], ["Wait till to-morrow, sir; double the chance then.", "그래서야 안 되지요, 아시다시피, 막 여행에서 내린 참인데요."], ["Mr.", "내일까지 기다려야지요, 선생."], ["Pickwick, Sir, there is a suit of clothes in that bag, and a hat in that box, which, I expect, in the effect they will produce, will be invaluable to me, sir.’", "그러면 가능성이 두 배는 되니까요. 픽윅 씨, 선생, 저 가방 안에 옷 한 벌이 있고 저 상자 안에 모자가 하나 있는데, 그것들이 자아낼 효과로 따지면 제게 더없이 귀중한 것이 되리라 기대합니다, 선생.」"]], [["‘Indeed!’ said Mr.", "「그렇습니까!」"], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Yes; you must have observed my anxiety about them to-day.", "「예. 오늘 그것들에 대한 제 염려를 분명 보셨겠지요."], ["I do not believe that such another suit of clothes, and such a hat, could be bought for money, Mr. Pickwick.’", "그만한 옷 한 벌과 그만한 모자는 돈을 주고도 살 수 없으리라고 봅니다, 픽윅 씨.」"]], [["Mr. Pickwick congratulated the fortunate owner of the irresistible garments on their acquisition; and Mr. Peter Magnus remained a few moments apparently absorbed in contemplation.", "픽윅 씨는 그 거부할 수 없는 의복을 손에 넣은 행운의 주인에게 축하를 건넸고, 피터 매그너스 씨는 잠시 무언가에 골똘히 잠겨 있는 듯했다."]], [["‘She’s a fine creature,’ said Mr.", "「그분은 참한 사람입니다.」"], ["Magnus.", "매그너스 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Is she?’ said Mr.", "「그렇습니까?」"], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Very,’ said Mr.", "「아주요.」 매그너스 씨가 말했다."], ["Magnus. ‘Very.", "「아주 그렇지요."], ["She lives about twenty miles from here, Mr. Pickwick.", "여기서 한 이십 마일 떨어진 곳에 삽니다, 픽윅 씨."], ["I heard she would be here to-night and all to-morrow forenoon, and came down to seize the opportunity.", "오늘 밤과 내일 오전 내내 여기 와 있을 거라는 소식을 듣고, 그 기회를 잡으려고 내려온 거지요."], ["I think an inn is a good sort of a place to propose to a single woman in, Mr.", "미혼 여성에게 청혼하기에는 여관이 좋은 곳이라고 봅니다, 픽윅 씨."], ["Pickwick. She is more likely to feel the loneliness of her situation in travelling, perhaps, than she would be at home.", "집에 있을 때보다 여행 중일 때 자기 처지의 외로움을 더 느낄 법하잖습니까."], ["What do you think, Mr. Pickwick?’", "어떻게 생각하십니까, 픽윅 씨?」"]], [["‘I think it is very probable,’ replied that gentleman.", "「충분히 그럴 법하다고 봅니다.」 그 신사가 대답했다."]], [["‘I beg your pardon, Mr.", "「실례합니다만, 픽윅 씨.」"], ["Pickwick,’ said Mr.", "피터 매그너스 씨가 말했다."], ["Peter Magnus, ‘but I am naturally rather curious; what may you have come down here for?’", "「제가 천성이 좀 호기심이 많아서요. 선생께서는 무엇 때문에 여기 내려오셨는지요?」"]], [["‘On a far less pleasant errand, Sir,’ replied Mr.", "「훨씬 덜 유쾌한 용무 때문이지요, 선생.」"], ["Pickwick, the colour mounting to his face at the recollection.", "픽윅 씨가, 그 기억에 얼굴이 붉어지며 대답했다."], ["‘I have come down here, Sir, to expose the treachery and falsehood of an individual, upon whose truth and honour I placed implicit reliance.’", "「저는 여기에, 선생, 제가 그 진실함과 명예를 전적으로 믿었던 한 인간의 배신과 거짓을 폭로하러 내려온 것입니다.」"]], [["‘Dear me,’ said Mr.", "「이런.」"], ["Peter Magnus, ‘that’s very unpleasant.", "피터 매그너스 씨가 말했다. 「그것 참 불쾌한 일이군요."], ["It is a lady, I presume?", "여자분이겠지요, 아마? 예?"], ["Eh? ah!", "아하!"], ["Sly, Mr. Pickwick, sly.", "능청스러우시군요, 픽윅 씨, 능청맞으세요."], ["Well, Mr. Pickwick, sir, I wouldn’t probe your feelings for the world.", "자, 픽윅 씨, 선생, 무슨 일이 있어도 선생의 심정을 캐묻지는 않겠습니다."], ["Painful subjects, these, sir, very painful.", "고통스러운 화제지요, 선생, 무척 고통스러운."], ["Don’t mind me, Mr. Pickwick, if you wish to give vent to your feelings.", "저는 신경 쓰지 마시고, 픽윅 씨, 심정을 토로하고 싶으시면 그렇게 하십시오."], ["I know what it is to be jilted, Sir; I have endured that sort of thing three or four times.’", "저도 차인다는 게 어떤 건지 압니다, 선생. 그런 일을 서너 번은 견뎌 봤거든요.」"]], [["‘I am much obliged to you, for your condolence on what you presume to be my melancholy case,’ said Mr.", "「제 우울한 처지라고 짐작하시는 일에 대한 위로의 말씀, 대단히 고맙습니다.」"], ["Pickwick, winding up his watch, and laying it on the table, ‘but--’", "픽윅 씨가 회중시계의 태엽을 감아 탁자 위에 내려놓으며 말했다. 「하지만—」"]], [["‘No, no,’ said Mr.", "「아니, 아닙니다.」 피터 매그너스 씨가 말했다."], ["Peter Magnus, ‘not a word more; it’s a painful subject.", "「더는 한마디도 마세요. 고통스러운 화제니까요."], ["I see, I see.", "압니다, 알아요."], ["What’s the time, Mr. Pickwick?’", "지금 몇 시입니까, 픽윅 씨?」"]], [["Past twelve.’", "「열두 시가 넘었군요.」"]], [["‘Dear me, it’s time to go to bed.", "「이런, 잠자리에 들 시간이군요."], ["It will never do, sitting here.", "이렇게 앉아 있어서는 안 되겠어요."], ["I shall be pale to-morrow, Mr. Pickwick.’", "내일 얼굴이 창백해질 겁니다, 픽윅 씨.」"]], [["At the bare notion of such a calamity, Mr.", "그런 참사를 떠올리기만 해도 견딜 수 없었던지, 피터 매그너스 씨는 객실 담당 하녀를 부르려고 종을 울렸다."], ["Peter Magnus rang the bell for the chambermaid; and the striped bag, the red bag, the leathern hat- box, and the brown-paper parcel, having been conveyed to his bedroom, he retired in company with a japanned candlestick, to one side of the house, while Mr. Pickwick, and another japanned candlestick, were conducted through a multitude of tortuous windings, to another.", "줄무늬 가방과 빨간 가방과 가죽 모자 상자와 갈색 종이 꾸러미가 그의 침실로 옮겨지고 나자, 그는 옻칠한 촛대 하나를 벗 삼아 여관의 한쪽으로 물러갔고, 한편 픽윅 씨는 또 다른 옻칠한 촛대 하나와 함께, 구불구불 꼬인 수많은 길을 거쳐 또 다른 쪽으로 안내되었다."]], [["‘This is your room, sir,’ said the chambermaid.", "「여기가 손님 방입니다, 선생.」 객실 담당 하녀가 말했다."]], [["‘Very well,’ replied Mr.", "「좋군.」"], ["Pickwick, looking round him.", "픽윅 씨가 주위를 둘러보며 대답했다."], ["It was a tolerably large double-bedded room, with a fire; upon the whole, a more comfortable-looking apartment than Mr. Pickwick’s short experience of the accommodations of the Great White Horse had led him to expect.", "그 방은 침대가 둘 놓인 제법 큰 방으로 불이 피워져 있었으니, 전체적으로 보아 픽윅 씨가 그레이트 화이트 호스의 숙박 설비를 짧게나마 겪어 본 끝에 기대했던 것보다 한결 안락해 보이는 방이었다."]], [["‘Nobody sleeps in the other bed, of course,’ said Mr.", "「물론 다른 침대에는 아무도 자지 않겠지.」"], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Very good.", "「예, 아무도 없습니다, 선생.」"], ["Tell my servant to bring me up some hot water at half-past eight in the morning, and that I shall not want him any more to-night.’", "「아주 좋아. 내 하인에게 아침 여덟 시 반에 더운물을 좀 가져오라고, 그리고 오늘 밤에는 더 부를 일이 없다고 전해 주게.」"]], [["‘Yes, Sir,’ and bidding Mr.", "「예, 선생.」"], ["Pickwick good-night, the chambermaid retired, and left him alone.", "그러고는 객실 담당 하녀가 픽윅 씨에게 안녕히 주무시라고 인사한 뒤 물러가, 그를 혼자 남겨 두었다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick sat himself down in a chair before the fire, and fell into a train of rambling meditations.", "픽윅 씨는 불 앞 의자에 앉아 두서없는 명상의 흐름에 빠져들었다."], ["First he thought of his friends, and wondered when they would join him; then his mind reverted to Mrs.", "처음에는 친구들 생각을 하며 그들이 언제 합류할지 궁금해했다."], ["Martha Bardell; and from that lady it wandered, by a natural process, to the dingy counting-house of Dodson & Fogg.", "그다음 그의 마음은 마사 바델 부인에게로 돌아갔고, 그 부인에게서 자연스러운 흐름을 따라 도드슨 앤드 포그의 칙칙한 사무실로 흘러갔다."], ["From Dodson & Fogg’s it flew off at a tangent, to the very centre of the history of the queer client; and then it came back to the Great White Horse at Ipswich, with sufficient clearness to convince Mr. Pickwick that he was falling asleep.", "도드슨 앤드 포그에서 그것은 옆으로 휙 튀어 그 괴상한 의뢰인 이야기의 한복판으로 갔다가, 이윽고 다시 입스위치의 그레이트 화이트 호스로 돌아왔는데, 그 또렷함이 충분하여 픽윅 씨는 자기가 잠들고 있음을 깨달을 수 있었다."], ["So he roused himself, and began to undress, when he recollected he had left his watch on the table downstairs.", "그래서 그는 정신을 차리고 옷을 벗기 시작하다가, 회중시계를 아래층 탁자에 두고 왔다는 사실이 떠올랐다."]], [["Now this watch was a special favourite with Mr. Pickwick, having been carried about, beneath the shadow of his waistcoat, for a greater number of years than we feel called upon to state at present.", "그런데 이 회중시계는 픽윅 씨가 각별히 아끼는 물건이었으니, 지금 이 자리에서 굳이 밝힐 필요가 있다고 여겨지지 않을 만큼 오랜 세월 동안 그의 조끼 그늘 아래 지니고 다닌 것이었다."], ["The possibility of going to sleep, unless it were ticking gently beneath his pillow, or in the watch-pocket over his head, had never entered Mr. Pickwick’s brain.", "그것이 베개 밑에서나 머리맡 시계 주머니에서 조용히 째깍거리지 않는다면 잠들 수 있으리라는 생각은 픽윅 씨의 머릿속에 한 번도 떠오른 적이 없었다."], ["So as it was pretty late now, and he was unwilling to ring his bell at that hour of the night, he slipped on his coat, of which he had just divested himself, and taking the japanned candlestick in his hand, walked quietly downstairs.", "그래서 지금은 꽤 늦은 시각인 데다 이런 야밤에 종을 울려 사람을 부르고 싶지도 않았으므로, 그는 방금 벗어 둔 외투를 다시 걸치고, 옻칠한 촛대를 손에 든 채 조용히 아래층으로 내려갔다."]], [["The more stairs Mr. Pickwick went down, the more stairs there seemed to be to descend, and again and again, when Mr.", "픽윅 씨가 계단을 내려가면 내려갈수록 내려가야 할 계단이 더 많아지는 것만 같았고, 픽윅 씨가 어느 좁은 복도에 들어서서 일층에 다다랐다고 스스로를 축하하려 할 때마다, 또 한 줄기 계단이 그의 놀란 눈앞에 거듭거듭 나타나곤 했다."], ["Pickwick got into some narrow passage, and began to congratulate himself on having gained the ground-floor, did another flight of stairs appear before his astonished eyes.", "마침내 그는 이 여관에 들어설 때 본 기억이 있는 돌 깔린 현관에 이르렀다."], ["At last he reached a stone hall, which he remembered to have seen when he entered the house.", "복도란 복도는 죄다 뒤졌고, 방이란 방은 죄다 들여다보았다."], ["Passage after passage did he explore; room after room did he peep into; at length, as he was on the point of giving up the search in despair, he opened the door of the identical room in which he had spent the evening, and beheld his missing property on the table.", "마침내 절망에 빠져 수색을 포기하려던 바로 그 순간, 그는 자기가 저녁을 보냈던 바로 그 방의 문을 열었고, 탁자 위에서 잃어버린 자기 물건을 발견했다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick seized the watch in triumph, and proceeded to retrace his steps to his bedchamber.", "픽윅 씨는 의기양양하게 시계를 움켜쥐고는 침실로 발걸음을 되짚어 가기 시작했다."], ["If his progress downward had been attended with difficulties and uncertainty, his journey back was infinitely more perplexing.", "내려올 때의 여정이 어려움과 불확실함을 동반한 것이었다면, 돌아가는 길은 그보다 한없이 더 헷갈렸다."], ["Rows of doors, garnished with boots of every shape, make, and size, branched off in every possible direction.", "온갖 모양과 제작 방식과 크기의 구두들로 장식된 문들이 줄지어, 생각할 수 있는 온갖 방향으로 갈라져 나갔다."], ["A dozen times did he softly turn the handle of some bedroom door which resembled his own, when a gruff cry from within of ‘Who the devil’s that?’ or ‘What do you want here?’ caused him to steal away, on tiptoe, with a perfectly marvellous celerity.", "그는 열두 번이나 자기 방과 비슷한 어느 침실 문의 손잡이를 살며시 돌렸는데, 그때마다 안에서 「대체 누구야?」라거나 「여기서 뭘 하는 거야?」라는 퉁명스러운 외침이 들려와, 그는 참으로 경이로울 만큼 날쌔게 발끝으로 살금살금 달아나곤 했다."], ["He was reduced to the verge of despair, when an open door attracted his attention.", "그가 절망의 벼랑 끝까지 내몰렸을 때, 열린 문 하나가 그의 눈길을 끌었다."], ["He peeped in. Right at last!", "그는 안을 들여다보았다."], ["There were the two beds, whose situation he perfectly remembered, and the fire still burning.", "드디어 맞았다! 그 위치를 똑똑히 기억하는 두 침대가 거기 있었고, 불도 여전히 타고 있었다."], ["His candle, not a long one when he first received it, had flickered away in the drafts of air through which he had passed and sank into the socket as he closed the door after him.", "처음 받았을 때도 그리 길지 않았던 그의 촛불은, 그가 지나온 외풍 속에서 깜박이며 사그라들다가, 그가 등 뒤로 문을 닫는 순간 촛대 구멍 속으로 폭 꺼지고 말았다."], ["‘No matter,’ said Mr.", "「상관없어.」"], ["Pickwick, ‘I can undress myself just as well by the light of the fire.’", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. 「불빛만으로도 옷 정도는 거뜬히 벗을 수 있지.」"]], [["The bedsteads stood one on each side of the door; and on the inner side of each was a little path, terminating in a rush-bottomed chair, just wide enough to admit of a person’s getting into or out of bed, on that side, if he or she thought proper.", "침대들은 문 양쪽에 하나씩 놓여 있었고, 각 침대의 안쪽으로는 골풀 방석을 깐 의자에서 끝나는 작은 통로가 나 있었는데, 그 통로는 본인이 원한다면 그쪽으로 침대에 들거나 나올 수 있을 만큼만 폭이 넓었다."], ["Having carefully drawn the curtains of his bed on the outside, Mr. Pickwick sat down on the rush-bottomed chair, and leisurely divested himself of his shoes and gaiters.", "픽윅 씨는 침대의 바깥쪽 휘장을 조심스럽게 친 뒤, 골풀 방석 의자에 앉아 느긋하게 구두와 각반을 벗었다."], ["He then took off and folded up his coat, waistcoat, and neckcloth, and slowly drawing on his tasselled nightcap, secured it firmly on his head, by tying beneath his chin the strings which he always had attached to that article of dress.", "그러고는 외투와 조끼와 목도리를 벗어 개켜 놓고, 술 달린 잠잘 때 쓰는 모자를 천천히 쓴 뒤, 그 의복에 늘 달아 두던 끈을 턱 아래로 매어 머리에 단단히 고정시켰다."], ["It was at this moment that the absurdity of his recent bewilderment struck upon his mind. Throwing himself back in the rush- bottomed chair, Mr.", "바로 이 순간, 조금 전 자기가 겪은 갈팡질팡함이 얼마나 우스꽝스러운 것이었는지가 그의 마음에 와닿았다."], ["Pickwick laughed to himself so heartily, that it would have been quite delightful to any man of well-constituted mind to have watched the smiles that expanded his amiable features as they shone forth from beneath the nightcap.", "픽윅 씨는 골풀 방석 의자에 몸을 뒤로 젖히고는 어찌나 진심으로 혼자 웃었던지, 잠잘 때 쓰는 모자 아래로 환히 빛나며 그 선량한 얼굴을 활짝 펴는 미소를 지켜본다면, 제대로 된 마음을 지닌 사람이라면 누구라도 무척 흐뭇했을 것이다."]], [["‘It is the best idea,’ said Mr.", "「이건 정말 기막힌 일이로군.」"], ["Pickwick to himself, smiling till he almost cracked the nightcap strings--‘it is the best idea, my losing myself in this place, and wandering about these staircases, that I ever heard of.", "픽윅 씨가 잠잘 때 쓰는 모자 끈이 끊어질 만큼 미소를 지으며 혼잣말을 했다. 「이런 데서 길을 잃고 이 계단들을 헤매고 다니다니, 이건 내가 들어 본 것 중에서도 가장 기막힌 일이야."], ["Droll, droll, very droll.’ Here Mr.", "우습군, 우스워, 정말 우스워.」"], ["Pickwick smiled again, a broader smile than before, and was about to continue the process of undressing, in the best possible humour, when he was suddenly stopped by a most unexpected interruption: to wit, the entrance into the room of some person with a candle, who, after locking the door, advanced to the dressing-table, and set down the light upon it.", "여기서 픽윅 씨는 아까보다 더 활짝 다시 미소를 짓고는, 더없이 유쾌한 기분으로 옷 벗는 일을 마저 계속하려던 참이었는데, 더없이 뜻밖의 방해—곧 어떤 사람이 촛불을 들고 방으로 들어와, 문을 잠근 뒤 화장대로 다가가 그 위에 불빛을 내려놓는 일—로 인해 갑작스레 멈추고 말았다."]], [["The smile that played on Mr. Pickwick’s features was instantaneously lost in a look of the most unbounded and wonder-stricken surprise.", "픽윅 씨의 얼굴에 어리던 미소는 끝없이 놀라고 경악한 표정 속으로 순식간에 사라져 버렸다."], ["The person, whoever it was, had come in so suddenly and with so little noise, that Mr. Pickwick had had no time to call out, or oppose their entrance.", "그 사람은, 누구인지는 몰라도, 어찌나 갑작스럽게, 어찌나 소리 없이 들어왔던지, 픽윅 씨는 소리를 지를 새도, 그 입장을 막을 새도 없었다."], ["Who could it be?", "누구일까?"], ["A robber?", "강도일까?"], ["Some evil-minded person who had seen him come upstairs with a handsome watch in his hand, perhaps.", "어쩌면 자기가 멋진 회중시계를 손에 들고 위층으로 올라오는 것을 본 어떤 악한일지도 몰랐다."], ["What was he to do?", "어떻게 해야 한단 말인가?"]], [["The only way in which Mr. Pickwick could catch a glimpse of his mysterious visitor with the least danger of being seen himself, was by creeping on to the bed, and peeping out from between the curtains on the opposite side.", "픽윅 씨가 자신이 들킬 위험을 가장 적게 무릅쓰면서 그 수수께끼 같은 방문객을 슬쩍 엿볼 수 있는 유일한 방법은, 침대 위로 기어 올라가 반대쪽 휘장 사이로 내다보는 것이었다."], ["To this manoeuvre he accordingly resorted.", "그리하여 그는 이 책략에 의지했다."], ["Keeping the curtains carefully closed with his hand, so that nothing more of him could be seen than his face and nightcap, and putting on his spectacles, he mustered up courage and looked out.", "얼굴과 잠잘 때 쓰는 모자 말고는 자기 모습이 더 보이지 않도록 휘장을 손으로 조심스럽게 닫아 쥔 채, 안경을 끼고는 용기를 그러모아 밖을 내다보았다."]], [["Mr.", "픽윅 씨는 공포와 경악으로 거의 까무러칠 뻔했다."], ["Pickwick almost fainted with horror and dismay.", "화장대 거울 앞에 노란 컬페이퍼를 만 중년 부인 하나가 서서, 부인들이 ‘뒷머리’라고 부르는 것을 부지런히 빗질하고 있었다."], ["Standing before the dressing-glass was a middle-aged lady, in yellow curl-papers, busily engaged in brushing what ladies call their ‘back-hair.’ However the unconscious middle-aged lady came into that room, it was quite clear that she contemplated remaining there for the night; for she had brought a rushlight and shade with her, which, with praiseworthy precaution against fire, she had stationed in a basin on the floor, where it was glimmering away, like a gigantic lighthouse in a particularly small piece of water.", "그 의식하지 못하는 중년 부인이 어떻게 이 방에 들어왔든 간에, 그녀가 이곳에서 하룻밤을 보낼 작정이라는 것만은 분명했으니, 골풀 양초와 갓을 가지고 들어와서는, 화재에 대한 칭찬할 만한 조심성을 발휘하여 그것을 바닥의 대야 안에 세워 두었기 때문이다. 거기서 그것은 유난히 작은 물웅덩이 속에 선 거대한 등대처럼 가물거리고 있었다."]], [["‘Bless my soul!’ thought Mr.", "「맙소사!」 픽윅 씨가 생각했다."], ["Pickwick, ‘what a dreadful thing!’", "「이거 큰일이로군!」"]], [["‘Hem!’ said the lady; and in went Mr.", "「에헴!」 부인이 말했다."], ["Pickwick’s head with automaton- like rapidity.", "그러자 픽윅 씨의 머리가 자동인형 같은 속도로 쑥 들어갔다."]], [["‘I never met with anything so awful as this,’ thought poor Mr.", "「이렇게 끔찍한 일은 난생처음 겪는군.」"], ["Pickwick, the cold perspiration starting in drops upon his nightcap.", "가엾은 픽윅 씨는 잠잘 때 쓰는 모자 위로 식은땀이 방울방울 솟는 것을 느끼며 생각했다. 「난생처음이야."], ["‘Never. This is fearful.’", "이거 무서운 일이로군.」"]], [["It was quite impossible to resist the urgent desire to see what was going forward.", "지금 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 보고 싶은 절박한 욕구를 도저히 억누를 수 없었다."], ["So out went Mr. Pickwick’s head again.", "그래서 픽윅 씨의 머리가 다시 쑥 나왔다."], ["The prospect was worse than before.", "광경은 아까보다 더 나빴다."], ["The middle-aged lady had finished arranging her hair; had carefully enveloped it in a muslin nightcap with a small plaited border; and was gazing pensively on the fire.", "중년 부인은 머리 손질을 마치고, 작은 주름 가두리가 달린 모슬린 잠잘 때 쓰는 모자로 그것을 조심스럽게 감싼 뒤, 시름에 잠긴 듯 불을 바라보고 있었다."]], [["‘This matter is growing alarming,’ reasoned Mr.", "「이 일이 점점 심상찮게 되어 가는군.」"], ["Pickwick with himself.", "픽윅 씨가 혼자 따져 보았다."], ["‘I can’t allow things to go on in this way.", "「일이 이런 식으로 흘러가게 둘 수는 없어."], ["By the self-possession of that lady, it is clear to me that I must have come into the wrong room.", "저 부인이 저토록 침착한 걸 보니, 내가 방을 잘못 들어온 것이 분명해."], ["If I call out she’ll alarm the house; but if I remain here the consequences will be still more frightful.’", "내가 소리를 지르면 부인이 온 집 안을 놀라게 할 테고, 그렇다고 여기 그대로 있으면 그 결과는 더더욱 끔찍할 거야.」"]], [["Mr. Pickwick, it is quite unnecessary to say, was one of the most modest and delicate-minded of mortals.", "굳이 말할 것도 없이, 픽윅 씨는 인간들 가운데 가장 정숙하고 섬세한 마음을 지닌 사람 중 하나였다."], ["The very idea of exhibiting his nightcap to a lady overpowered him, but he had tied those confounded strings in a knot, and, do what he would, he couldn’t get it off.", "한 부인에게 자기 잠잘 때 쓰는 모자를 내보인다는 생각만으로도 그는 견딜 수가 없었으나, 그 망할 끈을 매듭으로 묶어 둔 탓에, 아무리 애를 써도 벗을 수가 없었다."], ["The disclosure must be made.", "정체를 밝혀야만 했다."], ["There was only one other way of doing it.", "그렇게 하는 다른 방법은 단 하나뿐이었다."], ["He shrunk behind the curtains, and called out very loudly--", "그는 휘장 뒤로 몸을 움츠리고는 아주 큰 소리로 외쳤다."]], [["‘Ha-hum!’", "「하-험!」"]], [["That the lady started at this unexpected sound was evident, by her falling up against the rushlight shade; that she persuaded herself it must have been the effect of imagination was equally clear, for when Mr.", "이 뜻밖의 소리에 부인이 흠칫 놀란 것은 그녀가 골풀 양초 갓에 부딪혀 비틀거린 것으로 보아 분명했다."], ["Pickwick, under the impression that she had fainted away stone-dead with fright, ventured to peep out again, she was gazing pensively on the fire as before.", "또 그것이 상상의 장난임에 틀림없다고 그녀가 스스로를 납득시켰다는 것도 똑같이 분명했으니, 그녀가 공포로 새파랗게 질려 까무러쳤으리라 생각한 픽윅 씨가 다시 한번 밖을 내다보았을 때, 그녀는 아까처럼 시름에 잠긴 듯 불을 바라보고 있었기 때문이다."]], [["‘Most extraordinary female this,’ thought Mr.", "「참으로 별난 여자로군.」"], ["Pickwick, popping in again.", "픽윅 씨가 다시 머리를 쑥 집어넣으며 생각했다."], ["‘Ha-hum!’", "「하-험!」"]], [["These last sounds, so like those in which, as legends inform us, the ferocious giant Blunderbore was in the habit of expressing his opinion that it was time to lay the cloth, were too distinctly audible to be again mistaken for the workings of fancy.", "이 마지막 소리는, 전설이 우리에게 일러 주는 바, 사나운 거인 블런더보어가 이제 식탁보를 깔 때가 되었다는 자기 견해를 표할 때 즐겨 내던 소리와 너무도 비슷한 것이어서, 또렷이 들렸으므로 다시 공상의 장난으로 오해받기에는 너무도 분명했다."]], [["‘Gracious Heaven!’ said the middle-aged lady, ‘what’s that?’", "「세상에!」 중년 부인이 말했다. 「저게 뭐지?」"]], [["‘It’s--it’s--only a gentleman, ma’am,’ said Mr.", "「그건—그건—그저 신사일 뿐입니다, 부인.」"], ["Pickwick, from behind the curtains.", "픽윅 씨가 휘장 뒤에서 말했다."]], [["‘A gentleman!’ said the lady, with a terrific scream.", "「신사라니!」 부인이 무시무시한 비명을 지르며 말했다."]], [["‘It’s all over!’ thought Mr.", "「이제 다 끝났구나!」"], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 생각했다."]], [["‘A strange man!’ shrieked the lady.", "「낯선 남자야!」 부인이 새된 소리로 외쳤다."], ["Another instant and the house would be alarmed.", "한순간만 더 지나면 온 집 안이 발칵 뒤집힐 판이었다."], ["Her garments rustled as she rushed towards the door.", "그녀가 문 쪽으로 달려가자 그녀의 옷자락이 바스락거렸다."]], [["‘Ma’am,’ said Mr.", "「부인.」"], ["Pickwick, thrusting out his head in the extremity of his desperation, ‘ma’am!’", "픽윅 씨가 절박함의 극에 달하여 머리를 쑥 내밀며 말했다. 「부인!」"]], [["Now, although Mr. Pickwick was not actuated by any definite object in putting out his head, it was instantaneously productive of a good effect.", "그런데 픽윅 씨가 머리를 내민 데에 어떤 분명한 목적이 있었던 것은 아니지만, 그것은 즉각 좋은 효과를 낳았다."], ["The lady, as we have already stated, was near the door.", "앞서 말했듯이 부인은 문 가까이에 있었다."], ["She must pass it, to reach the staircase, and she would most undoubtedly have done so by this time, had not the sudden apparition of Mr. Pickwick’s nightcap driven her back into the remotest corner of the apartment, where she stood staring wildly at Mr.", "계단으로 가려면 그 문을 지나야 했는데, 픽윅 씨의 잠잘 때 쓰는 모자가 별안간 나타난 통에 그녀가 방의 가장 먼 구석으로 떠밀려 가지만 않았더라면, 그녀는 분명 이때쯤 이미 그렇게 했을 것이다."], ["Pickwick, while Mr. Pickwick in his turn stared wildly at her.", "그곳에서 그녀는 픽윅 씨를 사납게 노려보며 서 있었고, 픽윅 씨 또한 그녀를 사납게 노려보았다."]], [["‘Wretch,’ said the lady, covering her eyes with her hands, ‘what do you want here?’", "「파렴치한.」 부인이 두 손으로 눈을 가리며 말했다. 「여기서 뭘 하려는 거예요?」"]], [["‘Nothing, ma’am; nothing whatever, ma’am,’ said Mr.", "「아무것도, 부인. 아무것도 하려는 게 아닙니다, 부인.」"], ["Pickwick earnestly.", "픽윅 씨가 간절하게 말했다."]], [["‘Nothing!’ said the lady, looking up.", "「아무것도라니!」 부인이 고개를 들며 말했다."]], [["‘Nothing, ma’am, upon my honour,’ said Mr.", "「아무것도 없습니다, 부인, 제 명예를 걸고서요.」"], ["Pickwick, nodding his head so energetically, that the tassel of his nightcap danced again.", "픽윅 씨가 어찌나 힘차게 고개를 끄덕였던지 잠잘 때 쓰는 모자의 술이 다시 춤을 추었다."], ["‘I am almost ready to sink, ma’am, beneath the confusion of addressing a lady in my nightcap (here the lady hastily snatched off hers), but I can’t get it off, ma’am (here Mr.", "「부인, 저는 잠잘 때 쓰는 모자를 쓴 채 부인께 말을 건네는 이 당혹스러움에 거의 쓰러질 지경입니다(여기서 부인이 황급히 자기 모자를 벗어 던졌다)."], ["Pickwick gave it a tremendous tug, in proof of the statement).", "하지만 이걸 벗을 수가 없군요, 부인(여기서 픽윅 씨는 그 말이 사실임을 입증하려는 듯 모자를 어마어마하게 잡아당겼다)."], ["It is evident to me, ma’am, now, that I have mistaken this bedroom for my own.", "부인, 이제 제게 분명해진 것은, 제가 이 침실을 제 방으로 착각했다는 겁니다."], ["I had not been here five minutes, ma’am, when you suddenly entered it.’", "부인, 여기 들어온 지 오 분도 채 안 되었는데, 부인께서 별안간 들어오셨던 거지요.」"]], [["‘If this improbable story be really true, Sir,’ said the lady, sobbing violently, ‘you will leave it instantly.’", "「이 있을 법하지 않은 이야기가 정말 사실이라면, 선생.」 부인이 격렬하게 흐느끼며 말했다. 「당장 이 방에서 나가실 테죠.」"]], [["‘I will, ma’am, with the greatest pleasure,’ replied Mr.", "「나가지요, 부인, 더없이 기꺼이요.」"], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘Instantly, sir,’ said the lady.", "「당장이요, 선생.」 부인이 말했다."]], [["‘Certainly, ma’am,’ interposed Mr.", "「물론입니다, 부인.」 픽윅 씨가 아주 재빨리 끼어들었다."], ["Pickwick, very quickly.", "「물론이지요, 부인."], ["‘Certainly, ma’am.", "저는—저는—참으로 죄송합니다, 부인.」"], ["I--I--am very sorry, ma’am,’ said Mr.", "픽윅 씨가 침대 발치에 모습을 드러내며 말했다."], ["Pickwick, making his appearance at the bottom of the bed, ‘to have been the innocent occasion of this alarm and emotion; deeply sorry, ma’am.’", "「이 놀람과 동요의 죄 없는 빌미가 된 것이 말입니다. 깊이 죄송합니다, 부인.」"]], [["The lady pointed to the door.", "부인이 문을 가리켰다."], ["One excellent quality of Mr. Pickwick’s character was beautifully displayed at this moment, under the most trying circumstances.", "더없이 곤혹스러운 상황에서, 바로 이 순간 픽윅 씨 성품의 한 가지 빼어난 자질이 아름답게 드러났다."], ["Although he had hastily put on his hat over his nightcap, after the manner of the old patrol; although he carried his shoes and gaiters in his hand, and his coat and waistcoat over his arm; nothing could subdue his native politeness.", "그가 옛 야경꾼처럼 잠잘 때 쓰는 모자 위에 황급히 모자를 덮어쓰긴 했어도, 구두와 각반을 손에 들고 외투와 조끼를 팔에 걸치고 있긴 해도, 그 무엇도 그의 타고난 예의 바름을 누를 수는 없었다."]], [["‘I am exceedingly sorry, ma’am,’ said Mr.", "「대단히 죄송합니다, 부인.」"], ["Pickwick, bowing very low.", "픽윅 씨가 아주 깊이 고개를 숙이며 말했다."]], [["‘If you are, Sir, you will at once leave the room,’ said the lady.", "「죄송하시다면, 선생, 당장 이 방에서 나가실 테죠.」 부인이 말했다."]], [["‘Immediately, ma’am; this instant, ma’am,’ said Mr.", "「즉시요, 부인. 이 순간 당장요, 부인.」"], ["Pickwick, opening the door, and dropping both his shoes with a crash in so doing.", "픽윅 씨가 문을 열며 말했는데, 그러는 사이 구두 두 짝을 와장창 떨어뜨리고 말았다."]], [["‘I trust, ma’am,’ resumed Mr.", "「바라옵건대, 부인.」"], ["Pickwick, gathering up his shoes, and turning round to bow again--‘I trust, ma’am, that my unblemished character, and the devoted respect I entertain for your sex, will plead as some slight excuse for this--’ But before Mr.", "픽윅 씨가 구두를 주워 들고 다시 고개를 숙이려 돌아서며 말을 이었다. 「바라옵건대, 부인, 저의 티끌 하나 없는 인품과, 제가 부인 같은 여성분들에 대해 품은 헌신적인 존경심이, 이 일에 대한 약간의 변명거리나마 되어 주기를—」"], ["Pickwick could conclude the sentence, the lady had thrust him into the passage, and locked and bolted the door behind him.", "그러나 픽윅 씨가 그 문장을 마치기도 전에, 부인은 그를 복도로 떠밀어 내고는 등 뒤로 문을 잠그고 빗장을 질러 버렸다."]], [["Whatever grounds of self-congratulation Mr. Pickwick might have for having escaped so quietly from his late awkward situation, his present position was by no means enviable.", "조금 전의 난처한 처지에서 그토록 조용히 빠져나온 데 대해 픽윅 씨가 아무리 스스로를 축하할 근거가 있다 한들, 지금의 그의 형편은 결코 부러워할 만한 것이 아니었다."], ["He was alone, in an open passage, in a strange house in the middle of the night, half dressed; it was not to be supposed that he could find his way in perfect darkness to a room which he had been wholly unable to discover with a light, and if he made the slightest noise in his fruitless attempts to do so, he stood every chance of being shot at, and perhaps killed, by some wakeful traveller.", "그는 한밤중에, 낯선 집의, 트인 복도에, 옷도 반쯤만 걸친 채 홀로 있었다. 불빛을 들고서도 도무지 찾지 못했던 방을 칠흑 같은 어둠 속에서 찾아갈 수 있으리라고는 도무지 기대할 수 없었고, 그렇게 헛되이 더듬는 중에 조금이라도 소리를 낸다면, 잠 못 든 어떤 여행객에게 총을 맞고, 어쩌면 죽임을 당할 위험이 다분했다."], ["He had no resource but to remain where he was until daylight appeared.", "날이 밝을 때까지 있던 자리에 머무는 것 말고는 달리 방도가 없었다."], ["So after groping his way a few paces down the passage, and, to his infinite alarm, stumbling over several pairs of boots in so doing, Mr. Pickwick crouched into a little recess in the wall, to wait for morning, as philosophically as he might.", "그리하여 복도를 따라 몇 걸음 더듬어 가다가, 그러는 와중에 구두 여러 켤레에 걸려 넘어져 한없이 놀란 픽윅 씨는, 벽의 작은 우묵한 곳으로 몸을 웅크려 들어가, 되도록 의연하게 아침을 기다렸다."]], [["He was not destined, however, to undergo this additional trial of patience; for he had not been long ensconced in his present concealment when, to his unspeakable horror, a man, bearing a light, appeared at the end of the passage.", "그러나 그는 이 인내의 추가 시련을 겪을 운명은 아니었으니, 그가 지금의 은신처에 몸을 숨긴 지 얼마 되지 않아, 말로 다 할 수 없는 공포 속에서, 불빛을 든 한 사내가 복도 끝에 나타났기 때문이다."], ["His horror was suddenly converted into joy, however, when he recognised the form of his faithful attendant.", "그러나 그가 자기 충실한 시종의 모습을 알아보자, 그 공포는 별안간 기쁨으로 바뀌었다."], ["It was indeed Mr. Samuel Weller, who after sitting up thus late, in conversation with the boots, who was sitting up for the mail, was now about to retire to rest.", "그는 과연 새뮤얼 웰러 씨였으니, 우편 마차를 기다리느라 자지 않고 있던 구두닦이와 이렇게 늦은 시각까지 이야기를 나누다가, 이제 막 잠자리에 들려던 참이었다."]], [["‘Sam,’ said Mr.", "「샘.」"], ["Pickwick, suddenly appearing before him, ‘where’s my bedroom?’", "픽윅 씨가 그의 앞에 불쑥 나타나며 말했다. 「내 침실이 어디냐?」"]], [["Mr.", "웰러 씨는 더없이 분명한 놀라움을 담아 주인을 빤히 쳐다보았다."], ["Weller stared at his master with the most emphatic surprise; and it was not until the question had been repeated three several times, that he turned round, and led the way to the long-sought apartment.", "그러고는 그 질문을 세 차례나 거듭한 뒤에야 비로소 몸을 돌려, 그토록 오래 찾아 헤매던 방으로 앞장서 안내했다."]], [["‘Sam,’ said Mr.", "「샘.」"], ["Pickwick, as he got into bed, ‘I have made one of the most extraordinary mistakes to-night, that ever were heard of.’", "침대에 들면서 픽윅 씨가 말했다. 「오늘 밤 나는 일찍이 들어 본 적도 없는 가장 기막힌 실수를 하나 저질렀구나.」"]], [["‘Wery likely, Sir,’ replied Mr.", "「충분히 그러실 만하지요, 선생.」"], ["Weller drily.", "웰러 씨가 무뚝뚝하게 대답했다."]], [["‘But of this I am determined, Sam,’ said Mr.", "「하지만 이것만은 단단히 마음먹었다, 샘.」"], ["Pickwick; ‘that if I were to stop in this house for six months, I would never trust myself about it, alone, again.’", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. 「이 집에서 여섯 달을 묵는다 해도, 다시는 이 집 안을 혼자서 돌아다니는 일에 나 자신을 맡기지 않으리라는 것을.」"]], [["‘That’s the wery prudentest resolution as you could come to, Sir,’ replied Mr.", "「그게 선생께서 내리실 수 있는 가장 신중한 결심입지요, 선생.」 웰러 씨가 대답했다."], ["Weller. ‘You rayther want somebody to look arter you, Sir, when your judgment goes out a wisitin’.’", "「선생께서는 판단력이 잠깐 마실이라도 나갈 때면, 누가 곁에서 좀 보살펴 드릴 사람이 꽤나 필요하시거든요, 선생.」"]], [["‘What do you mean by that, Sam?’ said Mr.", "「그게 무슨 소리냐, 샘?」 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick. He raised himself in bed, and extended his hand, as if he were about to say something more; but suddenly checking himself, turned round, and bade his valet ‘Good- night.’", "그는 침대에서 몸을 일으켜 손을 뻗으며, 무언가 더 말하려는 듯하더니, 별안간 스스로를 다잡고는 몸을 돌려, 시종에게 「잘 자게」라고 일렀다."]], [["‘Good-night, Sir,’ replied Mr. Weller.", "「안녕히 주무십시오, 선생.」"], ["He paused when he got outside the door--shook his head--walked on--stopped--snuffed the candle--shook his head again--and finally proceeded slowly to his chamber, apparently buried in the profoundest meditation.", "웰러 씨가 대답했다. 그는 문밖으로 나가서 걸음을 멈추더니—고개를 절레절레 흔들고—다시 걸어가다가—멈춰 서서—촛불 심지를 다듬고—또다시 고개를 절레절레 흔들고는—마침내 더없이 깊은 명상에 잠긴 듯, 천천히 자기 방으로 걸어갔다."]], [["CHAPTER XXIII. IN WHICH MR. SAMUEL WELLER BEGINS TO DEVOTE HIS ENERGIES TO THE RETURN MATCH BETWEEN HIMSELF AND MR. TROTTER\nIn a small room in the vicinity of the stableyard, betimes in the morning, which was ushered in by Mr. Pickwick’s adventure with the middle--aged lady in the yellow curl-papers, sat Mr. Weller, senior, preparing himself for his journey to London. He was sitting in an excellent attitude for having his portrait taken; and here it is.", "픽윅 씨가 노란 컬페이퍼를 만 중년 부인과 모험을 겪던 그 아침이 밝아 올 무렵, 마구간 마당 가까이에 있는 작은 방 안에서, 웰러 영감은 런던으로 떠날 채비를 차리며 앉아 있었다. 그는 초상화를 그리기에 더없이 좋은 자세로 앉아 있었으니, 여기 그 모습이 있다."]], [["It is very possible that at some earlier period of his career, Mr. Weller’s profile might have presented a bold and determined outline.", "웰러 씨의 옆얼굴은 그의 인생 어느 이른 시기에는 어쩌면 굳세고 단호한 윤곽을 드러냈을지도 모른다."], ["His face, however, had expanded under the influence of good living, and a disposition remarkable for resignation; and its bold, fleshy curves had so far extended beyond the limits originally assigned them, that unless you took a full view of his countenance in front, it was difficult to distinguish more than the extreme tip of a very rubicund nose.", "그러나 그의 얼굴은 잘 먹고 사는 생활과, 체념이라는 점에서 유난스러운 기질의 영향 아래 부풀어 올랐고, 그 굳세고 살집 좋은 곡선들이 본래 정해졌던 한계를 한참 넘어 뻗어 나간 탓에, 정면에서 그 얼굴 전체를 똑바로 바라보지 않는 한 그저 아주 불그스레한 코의 맨 끄트머리만 겨우 가려낼 수 있을 정도였다."], ["His chin, from the same cause, had acquired the grave and imposing form which is generally described by prefixing the word ‘double’ to that expressive feature; and his complexion exhibited that peculiarly mottled combination of colours which is only to be seen in gentlemen of his profession, and in underdone roast beef.", "그의 턱은 같은 이유로 그 인상적인 부위 앞에 흔히 '이중'이라는 말을 붙여 묘사하곤 하는 그 묵직하고 위풍당당한 형태를 갖추게 되었으며, 그의 안색은 그의 직업에 종사하는 신사들과 덜 익힌 로스트비프에서나 볼 수 있는, 그 독특하게 얼룩덜룩한 색의 조합을 드러내고 있었다."], ["Round his neck he wore a crimson travelling-shawl, which merged into his chin by such imperceptible gradations, that it was difficult to distinguish the folds of the one, from the folds of the other.", "목에는 진홍색 여행용 숄을 두르고 있었는데, 그것이 어찌나 알아차릴 수 없을 만큼 점진적으로 턱과 뒤섞여 들어가던지, 어느 쪽 주름이 숄의 주름이고 어느 쪽이 턱의 주름인지 분간하기 어려울 지경이었다."], ["Over this, he mounted a long waistcoat of a broad pink-striped pattern, and over that again, a wide- skirted green coat, ornamented with large brass buttons, whereof the two which garnished the waist, were so far apart, that no man had ever beheld them both at the same time.", "그 위로 그는 넓은 분홍 줄무늬 무늬의 긴 조끼를 걸쳤고, 다시 그 위로는 커다란 놋쇠 단추들로 장식한 자락 넓은 초록색 코트를 걸쳤는데, 그 가운데 허리께를 장식한 두 단추는 어찌나 멀리 떨어져 있던지 그 둘을 동시에 본 사람은 일찍이 아무도 없었다."], ["His hair, which was short, sleek, and black, was just visible beneath the capacious brim of a low-crowned brown hat.", "그의 머리카락은 짧고 매끄럽고 검었으며, 챙이 넓고 우듬지가 낮은 갈색 모자의 너른 챙 아래로 겨우 보일락 말락 했다."], ["His legs were encased in knee-cord breeches, and painted top- boots; and a copper watch-chain, terminating in one seal, and a key of the same material, dangled loosely from his capacious waistband.", "그의 다리는 무릎까지 오는 골덴 반바지와 칠한 톱부츠로 감싸여 있었고, 구리 시곗줄 하나가 인장 하나와 같은 재질의 열쇠 하나로 끝나며 그의 너른 허리띠에서 헐겁게 늘어져 달랑거리고 있었다."]], [["We have said that Mr. Weller was engaged in preparing for his journey to London--he was taking sustenance, in fact.", "우리는 웰러 씨가 런던으로 떠날 채비에 여념이 없었다고 말했거니와—실은 그가 음식을 섭취하는 중이었다."], ["On the table before him, stood a pot of ale, a cold round of beef, and a very respectable-looking loaf, to each of which he distributed his favours in turn, with the most rigid impartiality.", "그의 앞 탁자 위에는 에일 한 단지와 차가운 소고기 한 덩이, 그리고 매우 점잖아 보이는 빵 한 덩어리가 놓여 있었는데, 그는 더없이 엄격한 공평함으로 그 각각에 차례로 자신의 은혜를 베풀고 있었다."], ["He had just cut a mighty slice from the latter, when the footsteps of somebody entering the room, caused him to raise his head; and he beheld his son.", "그가 막 그 빵에서 큼직한 한 조각을 잘라 냈을 때, 누군가 방으로 들어오는 발소리에 그는 고개를 들었고, 자기 아들의 모습을 보았다."]], [["‘Mornin’, Sammy!’ said the father.", "\"안녕, 새미!\" 아버지가 말했다."]], [["The son walked up to the pot of ale, and nodding significantly to his parent, took a long draught by way of reply.", "아들은 에일 단지로 걸어가더니, 부모에게 의미심장하게 고개를 끄덕여 보이고는, 대답 삼아 한 모금 길게 들이켰다."]], [["‘Wery good power o’ suction, Sammy,’ said Mr.", "\"제법 빨아들이는 힘이 좋구나, 새미.\""], ["Weller the elder, looking into the pot, when his first-born had set it down half empty.", "웰러 영감이, 맏아들이 단지를 반쯤 비워 내려놓자 단지 속을 들여다보며 말했다."], ["‘You’d ha’ made an uncommon fine oyster, Sammy, if you’d been born in that station o’ life.’", "\"새미, 네가 만약 그런 처지로 태어났더라면, 보기 드물게 훌륭한 굴이 됐을 게다.\""]], [["‘Yes, I des-say, I should ha’ managed to pick up a respectable livin’,’ replied Sam applying himself to the cold beef, with considerable vigour.", "\"예, 그렇겠지요. 점잖게 입에 풀칠은 했을 겁니다.\" 샘은 상당한 기세로 차가운 소고기에 달려들며 대답했다."]], [["‘I’m wery sorry, Sammy,’ said the elder Mr.", "\"새미, 난 정말 유감스럽다.\""], ["Weller, shaking up the ale, by describing small circles with the pot, preparatory to drinking.", "웰러 영감이, 마시기 전에 단지로 작은 원을 그리며 에일을 흔들어 섞으면서 말했다."], ["‘I’m wery sorry, Sammy, to hear from your lips, as you let yourself be gammoned by that ‘ere mulberry man.", "\"새미, 그 오디색 옷 입은 작자한테 네가 속아 넘어갔다는 말을 네 입으로 듣다니, 정말 유감스럽다."], ["I always thought, up to three days ago, that the names of Veller and gammon could never come into contract, Sammy, never.’", "사흘 전까지만 해도 난 늘 웰러라는 이름하고 속아 넘어간다는 게 한데 어울릴 일은 결코 없으리라 생각했다, 새미, 결코.\""]], [["‘Always exceptin’ the case of a widder, of course,’ said Sam.", "\"물론 과부 경우는 늘 예외로 치고 말이죠.\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["‘Widders, Sammy,’ replied Mr.", "\"과부들 말이냐, 새미.\""], ["Weller, slightly changing colour.", "웰러 씨가 살짝 안색을 바꾸며 대답했다."], ["‘Widders are ‘ceptions to ev’ry rule.", "\"과부들이야 어떤 규칙에든 예외지."], ["I have heerd how many ordinary women one widder’s equal to in pint o’ comin’ over you.", "사람 홀려 넘기는 점에서 과부 하나가 보통 여자 몇 명에 맞먹는지 들은 적이 있다."], ["I think it’s five-and- twenty, but I don’t rightly know vether it ain’t more.’", "스물다섯이었던 것 같은데, 그보다 더는 아닌지는 똑똑히 모르겠구나.\""]], [["‘Well; that’s pretty well,’ said Sam.", "\"흠, 그 정도면 제법이네요.\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["‘Besides,’ continued Mr.", "\"게다가,\""], ["Weller, not noticing the interruption, ‘that’s a wery different thing.", "웰러 씨가 끼어든 말은 못 들은 체하고 이어 갔다. \"그건 아주 다른 얘기다."], ["You know what the counsel said, Sammy, as defended the gen’l’m’n as beat his wife with the poker, venever he got jolly.", "새미, 술만 거나해지면 부지깽이로 마누라를 패던 신사를 변호한 변호사가 뭐라고 했는지 너도 알지."], ["“And arter all, my Lord,”", "'재판장님, 결국 따지고 보면,'"], ["says he, “it’s a amiable weakness.”", "그가 말했지, '그건 사랑스러운 약점입니다.'"], ["So I says respectin’ widders, Sammy, and so you’ll say, ven you gets as old as me.’", "그러니 새미, 과부들에 대해선 나도 그렇게 말하는 거고, 너도 내 나이쯤 되면 그렇게 말하게 될 게다.\""]], [["‘I ought to ha’ know’d better, I know,’ said Sam.", "\"제가 좀 더 분별이 있었어야 했지요, 압니다.\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["‘Ought to ha’ know’d better!’ repeated Mr.", "\"분별이 있었어야 했다고!\""], ["Weller, striking the table with his fist.", "웰러 씨가 주먹으로 탁자를 치며 되풀이했다."], ["‘Ought to ha’ know’d better!", "\"분별이 있었어야 했다고!"], ["why, I know a young ‘un as hasn’t had half nor quarter your eddication--as hasn’t slept about the markets, no, not six months--who’d ha’ scorned to be let in, in such a vay; scorned it, Sammy.’ In the excitement of feeling produced by this agonising reflection, Mr.", "아니, 난 네 교육의 절반은커녕 사분의 일도 못 받은 어린놈을 아는데—시장 바닥에서 떠돌며 잠자 본 적도 없고, 그래, 여섯 달도 안 되는 놈이지만—그런 식으로 속아 넘어가는 건 경멸했을 게다. 경멸했을 거야, 새미.\""], ["Weller rang the bell, and ordered an additional pint of ale.", "이 가슴 아픈 회상이 불러일으킨 감정의 격앙 속에서, 웰러 씨는 종을 울려 에일 한 파인트를 더 시켰다."]], [["‘Well, it’s no use talking about it now,’ said Sam.", "\"뭐, 지금 와서 그 얘길 해 봐야 소용없죠.\" 샘이 말했다."], ["‘It’s over, and can’t be helped, and that’s one consolation, as they always says in Turkey, ven they cuts the wrong man’s head off.", "\"다 끝난 일이고, 어쩔 수도 없고, 그게 그나마 위안이에요. 터키에서 엉뚱한 사람 목을 베고 나서 늘 하는 말처럼 말이죠."], ["It’s my innings now, gov’nor, and as soon as I catches hold o’ this ‘ere Trotter, I’ll have a good ‘un.’", "이제 제 차례예요, 아버지. 이 트로터란 작자만 붙잡으면 한판 제대로 할 겁니다.\""]], [["‘I hope you will, Sammy.", "\"그러길 바란다, 새미."], ["I hope you will,’ returned Mr. Weller.", "그러길 바라.\""], ["‘Here’s your health, Sammy, and may you speedily vipe off the disgrace as you’ve inflicted on the family name.’ In honour of this toast Mr.", "웰러 씨가 대답했다. \"네 건강을 위하여, 새미, 그리고 네가 집안 이름에 끼친 그 망신을 어서 씻어 내기를.\""], ["Weller imbibed at a draught, at least two-thirds of a newly-arrived pint, and handed it over to his son, to dispose of the remainder, which he instantaneously did.", "이 건배를 기리며 웰러 씨는 새로 도착한 파인트의 적어도 삼분의 이는 단숨에 들이켜고는, 나머지를 처리하라고 아들에게 건넸으며, 아들은 그것을 즉각 해치웠다."]], [["‘And now, Sammy,’ said Mr.", "\"자 이제, 새미.\""], ["Weller, consulting a large double-faced silver watch that hung at the end of the copper chain.", "웰러 씨가 구리 줄 끝에 매달린 커다란 양면짜리 은시계를 들여다보며 말했다."], ["‘Now it’s time I was up at the office to get my vay-bill and see the coach loaded; for coaches, Sammy, is like guns--they requires to be loaded with wery great care, afore they go off.’", "\"이제 사무소에 올라가 운행표를 받고 마차에 짐 싣는 걸 봐야 할 시간이구나. 새미, 마차란 건 총하고 같아서 말이다—발사되기 전에 아주 조심스럽게 장전해야 하거든.\""]], [["At this parental and professional joke, Mr. Weller, junior, smiled a filial smile.", "이 아버지답고 직업적인 농담에 웰러 2세는 효성스러운 미소를 지었다."], ["His revered parent continued in a solemn tone--", "그의 존경하는 아버지는 엄숙한 어조로 말을 이어 갔다."]], [["‘I’m a-goin’ to leave you, Samivel, my boy, and there’s no telling ven I shall see you again.", "\"난 이제 널 떠난다, 새뮤얼아, 내 아들아. 그리고 언제 또 널 보게 될지는 알 수 없구나."], ["Your mother-in-law may ha’ been too much for me, or a thousand things may have happened by the time you next hears any news o’ the celebrated Mr. Veller o’ the Bell Savage.", "네 새어미가 날 너무 들들 볶았을 수도 있고, 네가 그 유명한 벨 새비지의 웰러 씨 소식을 다음번에 듣게 될 즈음엔 천 가지 일이 일어나 있을지도 모르지."], ["The family name depends wery much upon you, Samivel, and I hope you’ll do wot’s right by it.", "집안 이름은 네게 크게 달려 있다, 새뮤얼아, 부디 그 이름에 옳은 일을 해 주길 바란다."], ["Upon all little pints o’ breedin’, I know I may trust you as vell as if it was my own self.", "자잘한 예절 같은 거야 내 자신만큼이나 너를 믿을 수 있다는 걸 안다."], ["So I’ve only this here one little bit of adwice to give you.", "그러니 딱 한 가지 작은 충고만 해 두마."], ["If ever you gets to up’ards o’ fifty, and feels disposed to go a-marryin’ anybody--no matter who--jist you shut yourself up in your own room, if you’ve got one, and pison yourself off hand.", "혹시라도 네가 쉰 살이 넘어서 누구든—누구든 상관없다—결혼할 마음이 들거든, 방이 하나 있으면 네 방에 틀어박혀서, 당장 독약을 먹고 죽어라."], ["Hangin’s wulgar, so don’t you have nothin’ to say to that.", "목매다는 건 천박하니 그런 건 얼씬도 못 하게 해라."], ["Pison yourself, Samivel, my boy, pison yourself, and you’ll be glad on it arterwards.’ With these affecting words, Mr.", "독약을 먹어라, 새뮤얼아, 내 아들아, 독약을 먹어. 나중에 잘했다 싶을 게다.\""], ["Weller looked steadfastly on his son, and turning slowly upon his heel, disappeared from his sight.", "이 감동적인 말과 함께, 웰러 씨는 아들을 물끄러미 바라보더니, 천천히 발꿈치를 돌려 그의 시야에서 사라졌다."]], [["In the contemplative mood which these words had awakened, Mr. Samuel Weller walked forth from the Great White Horse when his father had left him; and bending his steps towards St.", "이 말들이 불러일으킨 사색에 잠긴 기분으로, 새뮤얼 웰러 씨는 아버지가 떠난 뒤 그레이트 화이트 호스를 나섰다."], ["Clement’s Church, endeavoured to dissipate his melancholy, by strolling among its ancient precincts.", "그는 세인트 클레먼트 교회 쪽으로 걸음을 옮기며, 그 유서 깊은 경내를 거니는 것으로 우울함을 떨쳐 보려 애썼다."], ["He had loitered about, for some time, when he found himself in a retired spot--a kind of courtyard of venerable appearance--which he discovered had no other outlet than the turning by which he had entered.", "한동안 어슬렁거리던 그는 어느 외진 곳에 이르렀는데—고풍스러운 모습의 안마당 같은 곳이었다—그곳에는 자신이 들어온 모퉁이 말고는 다른 출구가 없다는 것을 알게 되었다."], ["He was about retracing his steps, when he was suddenly transfixed to the spot by a sudden appearance; and the mode and manner of this appearance, we now proceed to relate.", "그가 막 발길을 되돌리려는 참에, 별안간 나타난 어떤 광경에 그 자리에 못 박힌 듯 멈춰 섰다. 이 출현의 양상과 방식을 이제부터 이야기하기로 한다."]], [["Mr. Samuel Weller had been staring up at the old brick houses now and then, in his deep abstraction, bestowing a wink upon some healthy- looking servant girl as she drew up a blind, or threw open a bedroom window, when the green gate of a garden at the bottom of the yard opened, and a man having emerged therefrom, closed the green gate very carefully after him, and walked briskly towards the very spot where Mr. Weller was standing.", "새뮤얼 웰러 씨는 깊은 멍한 생각에 잠겨 이따금 그 오래된 벽돌집들을 올려다보며, 건강해 보이는 어느 하녀가 블라인드를 올리거나 침실 창문을 활짝 열 때마다 그녀에게 한 번씩 윙크를 던지고 있었는데, 그때 마당 안쪽에 있는 어느 정원의 초록색 문이 열리더니, 한 남자가 거기서 나와 등 뒤로 그 초록색 문을 아주 조심스럽게 닫고는, 웰러 씨가 서 있는 바로 그 자리를 향해 빠른 걸음으로 걸어왔다."]], [["Now, taking this, as an isolated fact, unaccompanied by any attendant circumstances, there was nothing very extraordinary in it; because in many parts of the world men do come out of gardens, close green gates after them, and even walk briskly away, without attracting any particular share of public observation.", "자, 이것을 다른 어떤 정황도 곁들이지 않은 하나의 동떨어진 사실로만 본다면, 거기엔 그다지 별날 것이 없었다. 세상 여러 곳에서 사람들은 정원에서 나와 등 뒤로 초록색 문을 닫고, 심지어 빠른 걸음으로 걸어가기까지 하지만, 대중의 각별한 주목을 끄는 일이란 전혀 없으니 말이다."], ["It is clear, therefore, that there must have been something in the man, or in his manner, or both, to attract Mr. Weller’s particular notice.", "그러므로 그 남자에게든, 그의 거동에든, 아니면 그 둘 다에든, 웰러 씨의 특별한 주의를 끌 만한 무언가가 분명 있었음에 틀림없다."], ["Whether there was, or not, we must leave the reader to determine, when we have faithfully recorded the behaviour of the individual in question.", "그것이 있었는지 없었는지는, 우리가 문제의 그 인물의 행동을 충실히 기록한 다음, 독자의 판단에 맡기기로 하자."]], [["When the man had shut the green gate after him, he walked, as we have said twice already, with a brisk pace up the courtyard; but he no sooner caught sight of Mr.", "그 남자는 등 뒤로 초록색 문을 닫고서, 이미 두 번이나 말했듯이, 빠른 걸음으로 안마당을 가로질러 걸어왔다."], ["Weller than he faltered, and stopped, as if uncertain, for the moment, what course to adopt.", "그러나 웰러 씨를 보자마자 그는 주춤거리며 멈춰 섰는데, 마치 그 순간 어떤 길을 택해야 할지 잠시 망설이는 듯했다."], ["As the green gate was closed behind him, and there was no other outlet but the one in front, however, he was not long in perceiving that he must pass Mr. Samuel Weller to get away.", "그러나 초록색 문이 등 뒤로 닫혀 있고 앞쪽 하나 말고는 다른 출구가 없었으므로, 그는 빠져나가려면 새뮤얼 웰러 씨를 지나쳐야 한다는 것을 알아차리는 데 오래 걸리지 않았다."], ["He therefore resumed his brisk pace, and advanced, staring straight before him.", "그리하여 그는 다시 빠른 걸음을 되찾아, 똑바로 앞만 응시하며 다가왔다."], ["The most extraordinary thing about the man was, that he was contorting his face into the most fearful and astonishing grimaces that ever were beheld.", "그 남자에게서 가장 별난 점은, 일찍이 본 적 없는 더없이 무시무시하고 놀라운 찡그림으로 얼굴을 일그러뜨리고 있다는 것이었다."], ["Nature’s handiwork never was disguised with such extraordinary artificial carving, as the man had overlaid his countenance with in one moment.", "그 남자가 한순간에 자기 얼굴 위에 덧씌운 것만큼 그토록 기상천외한 인공의 조각으로 자연의 솜씨를 위장한 적은 일찍이 없었다."]], [["‘Well!’ said Mr.", "\"이런!\""], ["Weller to himself, as the man approached.", "웰러 씨는 남자가 다가오자 속으로 중얼거렸다."], ["‘This is wery odd.", "\"이거 참 묘하군."], ["I could ha’ swore it was him.’", "그 작자라고 맹세라도 할 수 있었는데.\""]], [["Up came the man, and his face became more frightfully distorted than ever, as he drew nearer.", "남자가 다가왔고, 가까이 올수록 그의 얼굴은 그 어느 때보다 더 무섭게 일그러졌다."]], [["‘I could take my oath to that ‘ere black hair and mulberry suit,’ said Mr.", "\"저 검은 머리하고 오디색 옷에는 맹세를 걸 수 있겠는데.\" 웰러 씨가 말했다."], ["Weller; ‘only I never see such a face as that afore.’", "\"다만 저런 얼굴은 여태 본 적이 없단 말이야.\""]], [["As Mr. Weller said this, the man’s features assumed an unearthly twinge, perfectly hideous.", "웰러 씨가 이렇게 말하는 사이, 남자의 얼굴이 완전히 흉측한, 이 세상 것 같지 않은 경련으로 뒤틀렸다."], ["He was obliged to pass very near Sam, however, and the scrutinising glance of that gentleman enabled him to detect, under all these appalling twists of feature, something too like the small eyes of Mr. Job Trotter to be easily mistaken.", "그러나 그는 샘의 아주 가까이를 지나칠 수밖에 없었고, 그 신사의 꼼꼼히 살피는 눈길은 그 소름 끼치는 온갖 표정의 뒤틀림 아래서, 잡 트로터 씨의 그 작은 눈과 너무도 닮아 쉽게 착각할 수 없는 무언가를 알아차릴 수 있게 해 주었다."]], [["‘Hollo, you Sir!’ shouted Sam fiercely.", "\"어이, 거기 양반!\" 샘이 사납게 외쳤다."]], [["The stranger stopped.", "낯선 자는 멈춰 섰다."]], [["‘Hollo!’ repeated Sam, still more gruffly.", "\"어이!\" 샘이 한층 더 퉁명스럽게 되풀이했다."]], [["The man with the horrible face looked, with the greatest surprise, up the court, and down the court, and in at the windows of the houses-- everywhere but at Sam Weller--and took another step forward, when he was brought to again by another shout.", "그 끔찍한 얼굴의 남자는 더없이 놀란 기색으로 마당 위쪽을 보고, 마당 아래쪽을 보고, 집들의 창문 안을 들여다보고—샘 웰러만 빼고 사방을 두리번거리더니—한 발짝 더 앞으로 내디뎠는데, 또 한 번의 고함에 다시 멈춰 섰다."]], [["‘Hollo, you sir!’ said Sam, for the third time.", "\"어이, 거기 양반!\" 샘이 세 번째로 말했다."]], [["There was no pretending to mistake where the voice came from now, so the stranger, having no other resource, at last looked Sam Weller full in the face.", "이제는 그 목소리가 어디서 나오는지 모르는 척할 도리가 없었으므로, 낯선 자는 달리 방도가 없어 마침내 샘 웰러의 얼굴을 똑바로 바라보았다."]], [["‘It won’t do, Job Trotter,’ said Sam.", "\"안 통해, 잡 트로터.\" 샘이 말했다."], ["‘Come!", "\"어서!"], ["None o’ that ‘ere nonsense.", "그런 수작은 그만둬."], ["You ain’t so wery ‘andsome that you can afford to throw avay many o’ your good looks.", "넌 그렇게 잘생긴 얼굴도 아니라서 그 멀쩡한 인상을 함부로 내던질 처지가 못 돼."], ["Bring them ‘ere eyes o’ yourn back into their proper places, or I’ll knock ‘em out of your head.", "그 눈깔 제자리로 되돌려 놓으라고, 안 그러면 머리통에서 아예 빼 버릴 테니까."], ["D’ye hear?’", "알아들어?\""]], [["As Mr. Weller appeared fully disposed to act up to the spirit of this address, Mr.", "웰러 씨가 이 말의 취지대로 행동할 작정이 완연해 보이자, 트로터 씨는 차츰 얼굴이 본래의 표정을 되찾게 내버려 두더니, 기쁨에 찬 듯 화들짝 놀라며 외쳤다."], ["Trotter gradually allowed his face to resume its natural expression; and then giving a start of joy, exclaimed, ‘What do I see?", "\"이게 누구신가요?"], ["Mr. Walker!’", "워커 씨!\""]], [["‘Ah,’ replied Sam.", "\"아.\" 샘이 대답했다."], ["‘You’re wery glad to see me, ain’t you?’", "\"날 만나서 무척 반갑단 말이지, 안 그래?\""]], [["‘Glad!’ exclaimed Job Trotter; ‘Oh, Mr.", "\"반갑다마다요!\" 잡 트로터가 외쳤다."], ["Walker, if you had but known how I have looked forward to this meeting!", "\"오, 워커 씨, 제가 이 만남을 얼마나 고대했는지 아셨더라면!"], ["It is too much, Mr.", "너무 벅찹니다, 워커 씨."], ["Walker; I cannot bear it, indeed I cannot.’ And with these words, Mr.", "견딜 수가 없어요, 정말 견딜 수가 없습니다.\""], ["Trotter burst into a regular inundation of tears, and, flinging his arms around those of Mr. Weller, embraced him closely, in an ecstasy of joy.", "그러고는 이 말과 함께, 트로터 씨는 그야말로 눈물의 홍수를 터뜨리며, 두 팔을 웰러 씨의 팔에 휘감고는 기쁨의 황홀경에 젖어 그를 꼭 껴안았다."]], [["‘Get off!’ cried Sam, indignant at this process, and vainly endeavouring to extricate himself from the grasp of his enthusiastic acquaintance.", "\"떨어져!\" 샘이 이 과정에 분개하여 외치며, 열렬한 지인의 포옹에서 벗어나려 헛되이 애를 썼다."], ["‘Get off, I tell you.", "\"떨어지라니까."], ["What are you crying over me for, you portable engine?’", "왜 나한테 매달려서 우는 거야, 이 들고 다니는 펌프 같은 놈아?\""]], [["‘Because I am so glad to see you,’ replied Job Trotter, gradually releasing Mr.", "\"당신을 만나서 너무도 기쁘기 때문이지요.\""], ["Weller, as the first symptoms of his pugnacity disappeared.", "잡 트로터가, 웰러 씨의 호전적인 기색이 처음으로 누그러지자 차츰 그를 놓아주며 대답했다."], ["‘Oh, Mr. Walker, this is too much.’", "\"오, 워커 씨, 너무 벅찹니다.\""]], [["‘Too much!’ echoed Sam, ‘I think it is too much--rayther!", "\"벅차다고!\" 샘이 받아 말했다."], ["Now, what have you got to say to me, eh?’", "\"내가 보기엔 너무 벅찬 것 같군—그것도 좀! 자, 나한테 할 말이 뭐야, 응?\""]], [["Mr.", "트로터 씨는 아무 대답도 하지 않았다."], ["Trotter made no reply; for the little pink pocket-handkerchief was in full force.", "그 작은 분홍색 손수건이 한창 위력을 발휘하고 있었기 때문이다."]], [["‘What have you got to say to me, afore I knock your head off?’ repeated Mr.", "\"내가 네 머리통을 박살 내기 전에, 나한테 할 말이 뭐냐고?\""], ["Weller, in a threatening manner.", "웰러 씨가 위협적인 태도로 되풀이했다."]], [["‘Eh!’ said Mr.", "\"네?\""], ["Trotter, with a look of virtuous surprise.", "트로터 씨가 더없이 고결하게 놀란 표정으로 말했다."]], [["‘What have you got to say to me?’", "\"나한테 할 말이 뭐냐고?\""]], [["‘I, Mr. Walker!’", "\"제가요, 워커 씨!\""]], [["‘Don’t call me Valker; my name’s Veller; you know that vell enough.", "\"날 워커라고 부르지 마. 내 이름은 웰러야."], ["What have you got to say to me?’", "너도 잘 알면서. 나한테 할 말이 뭐냐고?\""]], [["‘Bless you, Mr. Walker--Weller, I mean--a great many things, if you will come away somewhere, where we can talk comfortably.", "\"이런, 워커 씨—웰러 씨 말입니다—할 말이 아주 많지요, 어디 편하게 얘기할 만한 데로 가신다면요."], ["If you knew how I have looked for you, Mr. Weller--’", "제가 당신을 얼마나 찾아 헤맸는지 아신다면, 웰러 씨—\""]], [["‘Wery hard, indeed, I s’pose?’ said Sam drily.", "\"무척 애써서 찾았겠지, 안 그래?\" 샘이 무뚝뚝하게 말했다."]], [["‘Very, very, Sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"무척, 무척요, 나리.\""], ["Trotter, without moving a muscle of his face.", "트로터 씨가 얼굴 근육 하나 까딱하지 않고 대답했다."], ["‘But shake hands, Mr. Weller.’", "\"하지만 악수를 청하지요, 웰러 씨.\""]], [["Sam eyed his companion for a few seconds, and then, as if actuated by a sudden impulse, complied with his request.", "샘은 몇 초간 동행을 빤히 바라보더니, 갑작스러운 충동에 이끌린 듯 그의 청에 응했다."]], [["‘How,’ said Job Trotter, as they walked away, ‘how is your dear, good master?", "\"그래.\" 잡 트로터가 걸음을 옮기며 말했다."], ["Oh, he is a worthy gentleman, Mr. Weller!", "\"당신의 그 친절하고 훌륭하신 주인 어른은 어떻게 지내십니까?"], ["I hope he didn’t catch cold, that dreadful night, Sir.’", "오, 정말 훌륭한 신사분이시지요, 웰러 씨! 그 끔찍하던 밤에 감기는 안 드셨길 바랍니다, 나리.\""]], [["There was a momentary look of deep slyness in Job Trotter’s eye, as he said this, which ran a thrill through Mr. Weller’s clenched fist, as he burned with a desire to make a demonstration on his ribs.", "잡 트로터가 이 말을 할 때 그의 눈에 깊은 능청스러움이 순간적으로 스쳤고, 그것은 웰러 씨의 움켜쥔 주먹에 짜릿한 떨림을 일으켜, 그는 그자의 갈비뼈에 한 방 시연을 해 보이고 싶은 욕망으로 달아올랐다."], ["Sam constrained himself, however, and replied that his master was extremely well.", "그러나 샘은 자신을 억누르고는, 주인 어른은 더없이 평안하시다고 대답했다."]], [["‘Oh, I am so glad,’ replied Mr.", "\"오, 정말 다행입니다.\" 트로터 씨가 대답했다."], ["Trotter; ‘is he here?’", "\"여기 계신가요?\""]], [["‘Is yourn?’ asked Sam, by way of reply.", "\"네 주인은?\" 샘이 대답 삼아 물었다."]], [["‘Oh, yes, he is here, and I grieve to say, Mr.", "\"오, 네, 여기 계시지요."], ["Weller, he is going on worse than ever.’", "그런데 말씀드리기 가슴 아프지만, 웰러 씨, 그분은 그 어느 때보다도 더 못된 짓을 하고 계십니다.\""]], [["‘Ah, ah!’ said Sam.", "\"아하, 아하!\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["‘Oh, shocking--terrible!’", "\"오, 충격적이에요—끔찍합니다!\""]], [["‘At a boarding-school?’ said Sam.", "\"기숙 여학교에서?\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["‘No, not at a boarding-school,’ replied Job Trotter, with the same sly look which Sam had noticed before; ‘not at a boarding-school.’", "\"아뇨, 기숙 여학교는 아닙니다.\" 잡 트로터가, 샘이 앞서 알아챘던 그 능청스러운 표정으로 대답했다. \"기숙 여학교는 아니에요.\""]], [["‘At the house with the green gate?’ said Sam, eyeing his companion closely.", "\"초록색 문 달린 그 집에서?\" 샘이 동행을 빤히 살피며 말했다."]], [["‘No, no--oh, not there,’ replied Job, with a quickness very unusual to him, ‘not there.’", "\"아뇨, 아뇨—오, 거기가 아니에요.\" 잡이 그답지 않게 재빠른 어조로 대답했다. \"거기가 아닙니다.\""]], [["‘What was you a-doin’ there?’ asked Sam, with a sharp glance.", "\"넌 거기서 뭘 하고 있었는데?\" 샘이 날카로운 눈길로 물었다."], ["‘Got inside the gate by accident, perhaps?’", "\"어쩌다 우연히 그 문 안으로 들어갔나 보지?\""]], [["‘Why, Mr.", "\"이런, 웰러 씨.\" 잡이 대답했다."], ["Weller,’ replied Job, ‘I don’t mind telling you my little secrets, because, you know, we took such a fancy for each other when we first met.", "\"당신한테는 제 자잘한 비밀을 털어놓아도 상관없지요. 왜냐면, 아시다시피, 우린 처음 만났을 때 서로한테 그렇게 정이 들었으니까요."], ["You recollect how pleasant we were that morning?’", "그날 아침 우리가 얼마나 즐거웠는지 기억하시지요?\""]], [["‘Oh, yes,’ said Sam, impatiently.", "\"오, 그래.\" 샘이 짜증스럽게 말했다."], ["‘I remember.", "\"기억하지."], ["Well?’", "그래서?\""]], [["‘Well,’ replied Job, speaking with great precision, and in the low tone of a man who communicates an important secret; ‘in that house with the green gate, Mr.", "\"그래서요.\" 잡이 대단히 정확한 말투로, 중대한 비밀을 전하는 사람의 나직한 어조로 말했다."], ["Weller, they keep a good many servants.’", "\"그 초록색 문 달린 집에는요, 웰러 씨, 하인을 꽤 많이 두고 있답니다.\""]], [["‘So I should think, from the look on it,’ interposed Sam.", "\"그 집 꼴을 보아하니 그럴 것 같더군.\" 샘이 끼어들었다."]], [["‘Yes,’ continued Mr.", "\"그렇지요.\""], ["Trotter, ‘and one of them is a cook, who has saved up a little money, Mr.", "트로터 씨가 이어 갔다. \"그리고 그중 하나가 요리사인데, 돈을 조금 모아 두었답니다, 웰러 씨."], ["Weller, and is desirous, if she can establish herself in life, to open a little shop in the chandlery way, you see.’", "그래서 살림을 꾸릴 수 있게 되면, 잡화점이라도 작게 하나 열고 싶어 하지요.\" \"그렇군.\""]], [["Yes.’\n‘Yes, Mr. Weller.", "\"네, 웰러 씨."], ["Well, Sir, I met her at a chapel that I go to; a very neat little chapel in this town, Mr.", "그런데 나리, 제가 다니는 예배당에서 그 여자를 만났습니다. 이 고장에 있는 아주 아담하고 작은 예배당이지요, 웰러 씨."], ["Weller, where they sing the number four collection of hymns, which I generally carry about with me, in a little book, which you may perhaps have seen in my hand--and I got a little intimate with her, Mr.", "거기선 찬송가 제4집을 부르는데, 저는 그걸 늘 작은 책자에 담아 가지고 다닌답니다. 어쩌면 제 손에 들린 걸 보셨을지도 모르지요—그래서 그 여자랑 좀 가까워졌고, 거기서부터 우리 사이에 친분이 싹텄지요."], ["Weller, and from that, an acquaintance sprung up between us, and I may venture to say, Mr. Weller, that I am to be the chandler.’", "그래서 감히 말씀드리자면, 웰러 씨, 그 잡화상은 제가 되게 생겼습니다.\""]], [["‘Ah, and a wery amiable chandler you’ll make,’ replied Sam, eyeing Job with a side look of intense dislike.", "\"아, 그것 참 사랑스러운 잡화상이 되겠군.\" 샘이 강렬한 혐오의 곁눈질로 잡을 바라보며 대답했다."]], [["‘The great advantage of this, Mr.", "\"이 일의 가장 큰 이점은요, 웰러 씨.\""], ["Weller,’ continued Job, his eyes filling with tears as he spoke, ‘will be, that I shall be able to leave my present disgraceful service with that bad man, and to devote myself to a better and more virtuous life; more like the way in which I was brought up, Mr.", "잡이 말하면서 눈에 눈물을 글썽이며 이어 갔다. \"지금 그 못된 자 밑에서 하고 있는 이 치욕스러운 시중살이를 그만두고, 더 낫고 더 덕스러운 삶에 저를 바칠 수 있게 된다는 거지요."], ["Weller.’", "제가 자라 온 방식에 더 가까운 그런 삶 말입니다, 웰러 씨.\""]], [["‘You must ha’ been wery nicely brought up,’ said Sam.", "\"넌 무척 곱게 자랐나 보군.\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["‘Oh, very, Mr. Weller, very,’ replied Job.", "\"오, 무척요, 웰러 씨, 무척이요.\""], ["At the recollection of the purity of his youthful days, Mr.", "잡이 대답했다."], ["Trotter pulled forth the pink handkerchief, and wept copiously.", "자기 어린 시절의 순결함을 떠올리며 트로터 씨는 분홍색 손수건을 꺼내 들고 펑펑 울었다."]], [["‘You must ha’ been an uncommon nice boy, to go to school vith,’ said Sam.", "\"넌 함께 학교 다니기에 보기 드물게 좋은 아이였겠어.\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["‘I was, sir,’ replied Job, heaving a deep sigh; ‘I was the idol of the place.’", "\"그랬지요, 나리.\" 잡이 깊은 한숨을 내쉬며 대답했다. \"전 그곳의 우상이었답니다.\""]], [["‘Ah,’ said Sam, ‘I don’t wonder at it.", "\"아.\" 샘이 말했다."], ["What a comfort you must ha’ been to your blessed mother.’", "\"그럴 만도 하지. 네 복된 어머니한테 넌 얼마나 큰 위안이었겠어.\""]], [["At these words, Mr. Job Trotter inserted an end of the pink handkerchief into the corner of each eye, one after the other, and began to weep copiously.", "이 말에 잡 트로터 씨는 분홍색 손수건 끝을 양쪽 눈가에 차례로 하나씩 갖다 대고는 펑펑 울기 시작했다."]], [["‘Wot’s the matter with the man,’ said Sam, indignantly.", "\"이 작자 왜 이래.\" 샘이 분개하여 말했다."], ["‘Chelsea water- works is nothin’ to you.", "\"첼시 수도장이 너한테는 명함도 못 내밀겠군."], ["What are you melting vith now?", "이번엔 뭣 때문에 녹아내리는 거야?"], ["The consciousness o’ willainy?’", "악행을 저질렀다는 양심 때문에?\""]], [["‘I cannot keep my feelings down, Mr.", "\"제 감정을 억누를 수가 없습니다, 웰러 씨.\""], ["Weller,’ said Job, after a short pause.", "잡이 잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤 말했다."], ["‘To think that my master should have suspected the conversation I had with yours, and so dragged me away in a post-chaise, and after persuading the sweet young lady to say she knew nothing of him, and bribing the school-mistress to do the same, deserted her for a better speculation!", "\"제 주인이, 제가 당신 주인과 나눈 대화를 의심해서, 저를 역마차에 태워 끌고 가 버리고, 그 사랑스러운 젊은 아가씨를 설득해 자기는 아무것도 모른다고 말하게 하고, 여교장에게 뇌물을 먹여 똑같이 하게 해 놓고는, 더 짭짤한 투기 거리를 찾아 그 아가씨를 버렸다고 생각하면!"], ["Oh!", "오!"], ["Mr. Weller, it makes me shudder.’", "웰러 씨, 몸서리가 쳐집니다.\""]], [["‘Oh, that was the vay, was it?’ said Mr.", "\"오, 그게 사정이었구먼, 그래?\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘To be sure it was,’ replied Job.", "\"두말할 것 없이 그렇지요.\" 잡이 대답했다."]], [["‘Vell,’ said Sam, as they had now arrived near the hotel, ‘I vant to have a little bit o’ talk with you, Job; so if you’re not partickler engaged, I should like to see you at the Great White Horse to-night, somewheres about eight o’clock.’", "\"그럼.\" 샘이, 이제 호텔 가까이에 다다랐으므로 말했다. \"너랑 좀 얘기를 나누고 싶은데, 잡. 그러니 특별히 바쁜 일이 없으면, 오늘 밤 여덟 시쯤 그레이트 화이트 호스에서 너를 보고 싶다.\""]], [["‘I shall be sure to come,’ said Job.", "\"꼭 오겠습니다.\" 잡이 말했다."]], [["‘Yes, you’d better,’ replied Sam, with a very meaning look, ‘or else I shall perhaps be askin’ arter you, at the other side of the green gate, and then I might cut you out, you know.’", "\"그래, 그러는 게 좋을 거야.\" 샘이 자못 의미심장한 표정으로 대답했다. \"안 그러면 어쩌면 내가 저 초록색 문 너머에서 너를 찾게 될지도 모르거든. 그러면 내가 너를 가로채 버릴 수도 있고 말이지, 알겠어?\""]], [["‘I shall be sure to be with you, sir,’ said Mr.", "\"꼭 함께하겠습니다, 나리.\""], ["Trotter; and wringing Sam’s hand with the utmost fervour, he walked away.", "트로터 씨가 말하고는, 더없이 열렬하게 샘의 손을 꼭 쥔 뒤 걸어갔다."]], [["‘Take care, Job Trotter, take care,’ said Sam, looking after him, ‘or I shall be one too many for you this time.", "\"조심해라, 잡 트로터, 조심해.\" 샘이 그의 뒷모습을 바라보며 말했다."], ["I shall, indeed.’ Having uttered this soliloquy, and looked after Job till he was to be seen no more, Mr.", "\"안 그러면 이번엔 내가 너한테 한 수 위가 될 테니까. 정말로 그럴 거야.\""], ["Weller made the best of his way to his master’s bedroom.", "이 혼잣말을 내뱉고, 잡이 더는 보이지 않을 때까지 그 뒷모습을 바라본 뒤, 웰러 씨는 부지런히 주인의 침실로 향했다."]], [["‘It’s all in training, Sir,’ said Sam.", "\"전부 준비 중입니다, 나리.\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["‘What’s in training, Sam?’ inquired Mr.", "\"뭐가 준비 중이라는 거냐, 샘?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘I’ve found ‘em out, Sir,’ said Sam.", "\"그자들을 찾아냈습니다, 나리.\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["‘Found out who?’", "\"누굴 찾아냈다고?\""]], [["‘That ‘ere queer customer, and the melan-cholly chap with the black hair.’", "\"그 수상쩍은 작자하고, 검은 머리의 그 우울한 녀석 말입니다.\""]], [["‘Impossible, Sam!’ said Mr.", "\"그럴 리가, 샘!\""], ["Pickwick, with the greatest energy.", "픽윅 씨가 더없는 기세로 말했다."], ["‘Where are they, Sam: where are they?’", "\"그들이 어디 있느냐, 샘. 어디 있어?\""]], [["‘Hush, hush!’ replied Mr.", "\"쉿, 쉿!\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다."], ["Weller; and as he assisted Mr. Pickwick to dress, he detailed the plan of action on which he proposed to enter.", "그러고는 픽윅 씨가 옷 입는 것을 거들면서, 자신이 착수하고자 하는 행동 계획을 자세히 일러 주었다."]], [["‘But when is this to be done, Sam?’ inquired Mr.", "\"하지만 이건 언제 하게 되는 거냐, 샘?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘All in good time, Sir,’ replied Sam.", "\"다 때가 되면요, 나리.\" 샘이 대답했다."]], [["Whether it was done in good time, or not, will be seen hereafter.", "그것이 제때에 이루어졌는지 아닌지는 이후에 밝혀질 것이다."]], [["CHAPTER XXIV. WHEREIN MR. PETER MAGNUS GROWS JEALOUS, AND THE MIDDLE- AGED LADY APPREHENSIVE, WHICH BRINGS THE PICKWICKIANS WITHIN THE GRASP OF THE LAW\nWhen Mr. Pickwick descended to the room in which he and Mr. Peter Magnus had spent the preceding evening, he found that gentleman with the major part of the contents of the two bags, the leathern hat-box, and the brown-paper parcel, displaying to all possible advantage on his person, while he himself was pacing up and down the room in a state of the utmost excitement and agitation.", "피터 매그너스 씨가 그와 전날 저녁을 함께 보냈던 방으로 픽윅 씨가 내려가 보니, 그 신사는 두 개의 가방과 가죽 모자 상자와 갈색 종이 꾸러미에 들어 있던 물건들의 대부분을 자기 몸에 가능한 한 돋보이게 걸치고서, 더없이 흥분하고 동요한 상태로 방 안을 이리저리 거닐고 있었다."]], [["‘Good-morning, Sir,’ said Mr.", "\"좋은 아침입니다, 선생.\""], ["Peter Magnus.", "피터 매그너스 씨가 말했다."], ["‘What do you think of this, Sir?’", "\"이거 어떻게 보십니까, 선생?\""]], [["‘Very effective indeed,’ replied Mr.", "\"정말 아주 효과적이군요.\""], ["Pickwick, surveying the garments of Mr. Peter Magnus with a good-natured smile.", "픽윅 씨가 사람 좋은 미소를 띠고 피터 매그너스 씨의 의복을 살펴보며 대답했다."]], [["‘Yes, I think it’ll do,’ said Mr.", "\"네, 이만하면 될 것 같습니다.\""], ["Magnus. ‘Mr.", "매그너스 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick, Sir, I have sent up my card.’", "\"픽윅 씨, 선생, 제 명함을 올려 보냈습니다.\""]], [["‘Have you?’ said Mr.", "\"그러셨습니까?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘And the waiter brought back word, that she would see me at eleven--at eleven, Sir; it only wants a quarter now.’", "\"그랬더니 종업원이 답을 가지고 돌아왔는데, 열한 시에 저를 만나 주시겠다는 겁니다—열한 시에요, 선생. 지금 십오 분밖에 안 남았습니다.\""]], [["‘Very near the time,’ said Mr.", "\"시간이 다 됐군요.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Yes, it is rather near,’ replied Mr.", "\"네, 꽤 가까워졌지요.\" 매그너스 씨가 대답했다."], ["Magnus, ‘rather too near to be pleasant--eh! Mr.", "\"마음 편하기엔 좀 지나치게 가까워졌어요—그렇지 않습니까!"], ["Pickwick, sir?’", "픽윅 씨, 선생?\""]], [["‘Confidence is a great thing in these cases,’ observed Mr.", "\"이런 경우엔 자신감이 큰 힘이 됩니다.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘I believe it is, Sir,’ said Mr.", "\"그런 것 같습니다, 선생.\""], ["Peter Magnus.", "피터 매그너스 씨가 말했다."], ["‘I am very confident, Sir. Really, Mr.", "\"저는 아주 자신이 있습니다, 선생."], ["Pickwick, I do not see why a man should feel any fear in such a case as this, sir.", "정말이지, 픽윅 씨, 이런 경우에 사내가 무슨 두려움을 느낄 까닭이 있는지 저는 모르겠어요, 선생."], ["What is it, Sir?", "무엇이 문제입니까, 선생?"], ["There’s nothing to be ashamed of; it’s a matter of mutual accommodation, nothing more.", "부끄러워할 것이라곤 없지요. 서로의 편의를 위한 일일 뿐, 그 이상도 그 이하도 아닙니다."], ["Husband on one side, wife on the other.", "한쪽에는 남편, 다른 쪽에는 아내."], ["That’s my view of the matter, Mr. Pickwick.’", "그게 이 문제에 대한 제 견해입니다, 픽윅 씨.\""]], [["‘It is a very philosophical one,’ replied Mr.", "\"아주 철학적인 견해로군요.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다."], ["Pickwick. ‘But breakfast is waiting, Mr.", "\"하지만 아침 식사가 기다리고 있습니다, 매그너스 씨."], ["Magnus. Come.’", "갑시다.\""]], [["Down they sat to breakfast, but it was evident, notwithstanding the boasting of Mr.", "두 사람은 아침 식사를 하러 앉았다."], ["Peter Magnus, that he laboured under a very considerable degree of nervousness, of which loss of appetite, a propensity to upset the tea-things, a spectral attempt at drollery, and an irresistible inclination to look at the clock, every other second, were among the principal symptoms.", "그러나 피터 매그너스 씨의 큰소리에도 불구하고, 그가 상당한 정도의 긴장에 시달리고 있다는 것이 역력했으니, 식욕의 상실, 찻잔을 엎으려는 경향, 익살을 부려 보려는 유령 같은 시도, 그리고 매 순간마다 시계를 들여다보려는 억누를 수 없는 충동 따위가 그 주요 증상 가운데 들어 있었다."]], [["‘He-he-he,’ tittered Mr.", "\"헤헤헤.\""], ["Magnus, affecting cheerfulness, and gasping with agitation.", "매그너스 씨가 명랑한 척하면서, 동요로 숨을 헐떡이며 킥킥거렸다."], ["‘It only wants two minutes, Mr.", "\"이제 이 분밖에 안 남았습니다, 픽윅 씨."], ["Pickwick. Am I pale, Sir?’", "저 창백해 보입니까, 선생?\""]], [["‘Not very,’ replied Mr.", "\"그리 심하진 않습니다.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 대답했다."]], [["There was a brief pause.", "잠시 짤막한 침묵이 흘렀다."]], [["‘I beg your pardon, Mr. Pickwick; but have you ever done this sort of thing in your time?’ said Mr.", "\"실례합니다만, 픽윅 씨, 혹시 살아오시면서 이런 일을 해 보신 적이 있으십니까?\""], ["Magnus.", "매그너스 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘You mean proposing?’ said Mr.", "\"청혼 말씀이십니까?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Yes.’", "\"네.\""]], [["‘Never,’ said Mr.", "\"전혀 없습니다.\""], ["Pickwick, with great energy, ‘never.’", "픽윅 씨가 대단한 기세로 말했다. \"전혀요.\""]], [["‘You have no idea, then, how it’s best to begin?’ said Mr.", "\"그러면 어떻게 시작하는 것이 가장 좋은지 짐작도 못 하시겠군요?\""], ["Magnus.", "매그너스 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Why,’ said Mr.", "\"글쎄요.\""], ["Pickwick, ‘I may have formed some ideas upon the subject, but, as I have never submitted them to the test of experience, I should be sorry if you were induced to regulate your proceedings by them.’", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"그 문제에 대해 어떤 생각을 떠올려 본 적은 있을지 모르나, 한 번도 경험의 시험에 부쳐 본 적이 없으니, 만약 당신이 그것에 따라 일을 진행하시게 된다면 제가 송구스러울 것입니다.\""]], [["‘I should feel very much obliged to you, for any advice, Sir,’ said Mr.", "\"무슨 조언이든 주시면 대단히 감사하겠습니다, 선생.\""], ["Magnus, taking another look at the clock, the hand of which was verging on the five minutes past.", "매그너스 씨가 말하며 다시 시계를 한번 보았는데, 바늘은 막 오 분이 지나려는 참이었다."]], [["‘Well, sir,’ said Mr.", "\"그러면, 선생.\""], ["Pickwick, with the profound solemnity with which that great man could, when he pleased, render his remarks so deeply impressive.", "픽윅 씨가, 그 위대한 인물이 마음만 먹으면 자신의 말을 그토록 깊이 인상적으로 만들 수 있는 그 심오한 엄숙함으로 말했다."], ["‘I should commence, sir, with a tribute to the lady’s beauty and excellent qualities; from them, Sir, I should diverge to my own unworthiness.’", "\"저라면, 선생, 먼저 그 숙녀의 아름다움과 훌륭한 자질에 대한 찬사로 시작하겠습니다. 그러고 나서, 선생, 거기서 저 자신의 부족함으로 화제를 옮기겠습니다.\""]], [["‘Very good,’ said Mr.", "\"아주 좋습니다.\""], ["Magnus.", "매그너스 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Unworthiness for her only, mind, sir,’ resumed Mr.", "\"오직 그분에게만 부족하다는 것이지요, 명심하십시오, 선생.\""], ["Pickwick; ‘for to show that I was not wholly unworthy, sir, I should take a brief review of my past life, and present condition.", "픽윅 씨가 이었다. \"제가 전적으로 부족한 인물은 아님을 보이기 위해, 선생, 저는 제 지난 삶과 현재의 처지를 간략히 되돌아보겠습니다."], ["I should argue, by analogy, that to anybody else, I must be a very desirable object.", "유추를 통해, 다른 누구에게라면 제가 아주 바람직한 상대일 것이라고 주장하겠습니다."], ["I should then expatiate on the warmth of my love, and the depth of my devotion.", "그러고 나서 제 사랑의 뜨거움과 헌신의 깊이를 펼쳐 보이겠습니다."], ["Perhaps I might then be tempted to seize her hand.’", "어쩌면 그쯤에서 그분의 손을 잡고 싶은 유혹을 느낄지도 모르지요.\""]], [["‘Yes, I see,’ said Mr.", "\"네, 알겠습니다.\""], ["Magnus; ‘that would be a very great point.’", "매그너스 씨가 말했다. \"그게 아주 중요한 대목이겠군요.\""]], [["‘I should then, Sir,’ continued Mr.", "\"그러고 나서, 선생.\""], ["Pickwick, growing warmer as the subject presented itself in more glowing colours before him--‘I should then, Sir, come to the plain and simple question, “Will you have me?”", "픽윅 씨가, 화제가 더욱 찬란한 빛깔로 눈앞에 떠오를수록 점점 열을 올리며 계속했다. \"그러고 나서, 선생, 저는 솔직하고 단순한 질문에 이르겠습니다."], ["I think I am justified in assuming that upon this, she would turn away her head.’", "'저를 받아 주시겠습니까?' 이쯤에서 그분이 고개를 돌리리라고 가정해도 무방하리라 봅니다.\""]], [["‘You think that may be taken for granted?’ said Mr.", "\"그게 당연한 거라고 보십니까?\" 매그너스 씨가 말했다."], ["Magnus; ‘because, if she did not do that at the right place, it would be embarrassing.’", "\"왜냐하면 만약 그분이 적절한 대목에서 그렇게 하지 않으면 난처해질 테니까요.\""]], [["‘I think she would,’ said Mr. Pickwick.", "\"그렇게 하리라 봅니다.\""], ["‘Upon this, sir, I should squeeze her hand, and I think--I think, Mr.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"그러면, 선생, 저는 그분의 손을 꼭 쥐겠습니다."], ["Magnus--that after I had done that, supposing there was no refusal, I should gently draw away the handkerchief, which my slight knowledge of human nature leads me to suppose the lady would be applying to her eyes at the moment, and steal a respectful kiss. I think I should kiss her, Mr.", "그리고 제 생각엔—제 생각엔, 매그너스 씨—그렇게 한 다음, 거절이 없다고 가정하면, 그 순간 그분이 눈가에 갖다 대고 있으리라고—제 얕은 인간 본성 지식이 그렇게 짐작하게 합니다만—그 손수건을 살며시 치우고, 정중하게 살짝 입을 맞추겠습니다."], ["Magnus; and at this particular point, I am decidedly of opinion that if the lady were going to take me at all, she would murmur into my ears a bashful acceptance.’", "제 생각엔 그분에게 입을 맞출 것 같습니다, 매그너스 씨. 그리고 바로 이 대목에서, 만약 그분이 저를 받아들일 마음이 조금이라도 있다면, 수줍은 승낙을 제 귀에 속삭일 것이라고 저는 단연코 확신합니다.\""]], [["Mr. Magnus started; gazed on Mr.", "매그너스 씨는 흠칫 놀랐다."], ["Pickwick’s intelligent face, for a short time in silence; and then (the dial pointing to the ten minutes past) shook him warmly by the hand, and rushed desperately from the room.", "그러고는 잠시 말없이 픽윅 씨의 영민한 얼굴을 바라보다가, (시계 바늘이 십 분 지난 지점을 가리키자) 그의 손을 따뜻하게 흔들고는 필사적으로 방에서 뛰쳐나갔다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick had taken a few strides to and fro; and the small hand of the clock following the latter part of his example, had arrived at the figure which indicates the half-hour, when the door suddenly opened. He turned round to meet Mr. Peter Magnus, and encountered, in his stead, the joyous face of Mr. Tupman, the serene countenance of Mr. Winkle, and the intellectual lineaments of Mr. Snodgrass.", "픽윅 씨가 몇 걸음 이리저리 거닐고 있는 동안, 시계의 작은 바늘도 그가 보인 마지막 본보기를 따라, 삼십 분을 가리키는 숫자에 다다랐을 무렵, 갑자기 문이 열렸다. 그는 피터 매그너스 씨를 맞으려고 돌아섰으나, 그 대신 마주친 것은 터프먼 씨의 즐거운 얼굴, 윈클 씨의 평온한 표정, 그리고 스노드그래스 씨의 지적인 이목구비였다."]], [["As Mr. Pickwick greeted them, Mr. Peter Magnus tripped into the room.", "픽윅 씨가 그들에게 인사하고 있을 때, 피터 매그너스 씨가 종종걸음으로 방에 들어왔다."]], [["‘My friends, the gentleman I was speaking of--Mr.", "\"여러분, 제가 말씀드렸던 그 신사분—매그너스 씨입니다.\""], ["Magnus,’ said Mr. Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Your servant, gentlemen,’ said Mr.", "\"안녕하십니까, 여러분.\""], ["Magnus, evidently in a high state of excitement; ‘Mr.", "매그너스 씨가, 분명히 한껏 흥분한 상태로 말했다."], ["Pickwick, allow me to speak to you one moment, sir.’", "\"픽윅 씨, 잠시만 말씀 좀 나누겠습니다, 선생.\""]], [["As he said this, Mr. Magnus harnessed his forefinger to Mr. Pickwick’s buttonhole, and, drawing him to a window recess, said--", "이렇게 말하면서 매그너스 씨는 자기 집게손가락을 픽윅 씨의 단춧구멍에 걸고는, 그를 창가 구석으로 끌고 가서 말했다—"]], [["‘Congratulate me, Mr.", "\"축하해 주십시오, 픽윅 씨."], ["Pickwick; I followed your advice to the very letter.’", "저는 당신의 조언을 글자 그대로 따랐습니다.\""]], [["‘And it was all correct, was it?’ inquired Mr.", "\"그래서 다 잘되었습니까?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘It was, Sir.", "\"그렇습니다, 선생."], ["Could not possibly have been better,’ replied Mr.", "그보다 더 잘될 수는 없었을 겁니다.\""], ["Magnus. ‘Mr.", "매그너스 씨가 대답했다."], ["Pickwick, she is mine.’", "\"픽윅 씨, 그분은 제 사람이 되었습니다.\""]], [["‘I congratulate you, with all my heart,’ replied Mr.", "\"진심으로 축하드립니다.\""], ["Pickwick, warmly shaking his new friend by the hand.", "픽윅 씨가 새 친구의 손을 따뜻하게 흔들며 대답했다."]], [["‘You must see her. Sir,’ said Mr.", "\"그분을 꼭 만나 보셔야 합니다, 선생.\""], ["Magnus; ‘this way, if you please.", "매그너스 씨가 말했다."], ["Excuse us for one instant, gentlemen.’ Hurrying on in this way, Mr.", "\"이쪽입니다, 괜찮으시다면. 잠깐만 실례하겠습니다, 여러분.\""], ["Peter Magnus drew Mr. Pickwick from the room.", "이렇게 서두르며 피터 매그너스 씨는 픽윅 씨를 방에서 끌고 나갔다."], ["He paused at the next door in the passage, and tapped gently thereat.", "그는 복도의 다음 문 앞에서 멈추더니, 그 문을 가볍게 두드렸다."]], [["‘Come in,’ said a female voice.", "\"들어오세요.\" 여인의 목소리가 말했다."], ["And in they went.", "그래서 두 사람은 안으로 들어갔다."]], [["‘Miss Witherfield,’ said Mr.", "\"위더필드 양.\" 매그너스 씨가 말했다."], ["Magnus, ‘allow me to introduce my very particular friend, Mr. Pickwick.", "\"제 아주 각별한 친구 픽윅 씨를 소개해 드리겠습니다."], ["Mr. Pickwick, I beg to make you known to Miss Witherfield.’", "픽윅 씨, 위더필드 양을 소개해 드리겠습니다.\""]], [["The lady was at the upper end of the room. As Mr.", "그 숙녀는 방의 안쪽 끝에 있었다."], ["Pickwick bowed, he took his spectacles from his waistcoat pocket, and put them on; a process which he had no sooner gone through, than, uttering an exclamation of surprise, Mr. Pickwick retreated several paces, and the lady, with a half-suppressed scream, hid her face in her hands, and dropped into a chair; whereupon Mr.", "픽윅 씨가 고개를 숙이면서 조끼 주머니에서 안경을 꺼내 썼는데, 그 과정을 마치자마자 픽윅 씨는 놀라움의 탄성을 내지르며 몇 걸음 뒤로 물러섰고, 그 숙녀는 반쯤 억누른 비명과 함께 두 손에 얼굴을 묻고 의자에 풀썩 주저앉았다."], ["Peter Magnus was stricken motionless on the spot, and gazed from one to the other, with a countenance expressive of the extremities of horror and surprise.", "그 광경에 피터 매그너스 씨는 그 자리에 못 박힌 듯 꼼짝 못 하고 서서, 공포와 놀라움의 극치를 드러내는 표정으로 두 사람을 번갈아 바라보았다."]], [["This certainly was, to all appearance, very unaccountable behaviour; but the fact is, that Mr. Pickwick no sooner put on his spectacles, than he at once recognised in the future Mrs.", "이것은 분명, 겉보기에는, 매우 알 수 없는 행동이었다."], ["Magnus the lady into whose room he had so unwarrantably intruded on the previous night; and the spectacles had no sooner crossed Mr. Pickwick’s nose, than the lady at once identified the countenance which she had seen surrounded by all the horrors of a nightcap.", "그러나 사실인즉, 픽윅 씨는 안경을 쓰자마자 곧바로 미래의 매그너스 부인에게서 전날 밤 그토록 무엄하게 침입했던 그 방의 숙녀를 알아보았고, 그 안경이 픽윅 씨의 코에 걸리자마자 그 숙녀는 잠자리 모자의 온갖 끔찍함에 둘러싸여 있던 그 얼굴을 곧바로 알아본 것이었다."], ["So the lady screamed, and Mr. Pickwick started.", "그래서 숙녀는 비명을 질렀고, 픽윅 씨는 흠칫 놀랐던 것이다."]], [["‘Mr. Pickwick!’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"픽윅 씨!\""], ["Magnus, lost in astonishment, ‘what is the meaning of this, Sir?", "매그너스 씨가 경악에 빠져 외쳤다. \"이게 무슨 뜻입니까, 선생?"], ["What is the meaning of it, Sir?’ added Mr.", "이게 무슨 뜻이냐고요, 선생?\""], ["Magnus, in a threatening, and a louder tone.", "매그너스 씨가 위협적이고 더 큰 목소리로 덧붙였다."]], [["‘Sir,’ said Mr.", "\"선생.\""], ["Pickwick, somewhat indignant at the very sudden manner in which Mr.", "픽윅 씨가, 피터 매그너스 씨가 그토록 갑작스럽게 자신을 명령조로 활용한 데에 다소 분개하여 말했다."], ["Peter Magnus had conjugated himself into the imperative mood, ‘I decline answering that question.’", "\"저는 그 질문에 답하기를 거절합니다.\""]], [["‘You decline it, Sir?’ said Mr.", "\"거절하신다고요, 선생?\""], ["Magnus.", "매그너스 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘I do, Sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"그렇습니다, 선생.\""], ["Pickwick; ‘I object to say anything which may compromise that lady, or awaken unpleasant recollections in her breast, without her consent and permission.’", "픽윅 씨가 대답했다. \"저 숙녀분의 입장을 곤란하게 하거나, 그분의 가슴에 불쾌한 기억을 일깨울 만한 어떤 말도, 그분의 동의와 허락 없이는 하지 않겠습니다.\""]], [["‘Miss Witherfield,’ said Mr.", "\"위더필드 양.\""], ["Peter Magnus, ‘do you know this person?’", "피터 매그너스 씨가 말했다. \"이 사람을 아십니까?\""]], [["‘Know him!’ repeated the middle-aged lady, hesitating.", "\"이 사람을 아느냐고요!\" 중년 숙녀가 머뭇거리며 되뇌었다."]], [["‘Yes, know him, ma’am; I said know him,’ replied Mr.", "\"네, 아느냐고요, 부인. 안다고 했습니다.\""], ["Magnus, with ferocity.", "매그너스 씨가 사납게 대답했다."]], [["‘I have seen him,’ replied the middle-aged lady.", "\"본 적은 있어요.\" 중년 숙녀가 대답했다."]], [["‘Where?’ inquired Mr.", "\"어디서요?\" 매그너스 씨가 물었다."], ["Magnus, ‘where?’", "\"어디서 말입니까?\""]], [["‘That,’ said the middle-aged lady, rising from her seat, and averting her head--‘that I would not reveal for worlds.’", "\"그건,\" 중년 숙녀가 자리에서 일어서며 고개를 돌리고 말했다. \"무슨 일이 있어도 밝히지 않겠어요.\""]], [["‘I understand you, ma’am,’ said Mr.", "\"이해합니다, 부인.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick, ‘and respect your delicacy; it shall never be revealed by me depend upon it.’", "\"그리고 그 조심스러움을 존중합니다. 제 입으로는 결코 밝히지 않을 테니, 안심하십시오.\""]], [["‘Upon my word, ma’am,’ said Mr.", "\"이거 참, 부인.\""], ["Magnus, ‘considering the situation in which I am placed with regard to yourself, you carry this matter off with tolerable coolness--tolerable coolness, ma’am.’", "매그너스 씨가 말했다. \"제가 당신과 관련해 처해 있는 처지를 생각하면, 부인께서 이 일을 꽤나 태연하게 넘기시는군요—꽤나 태연하게요, 부인.\""]], [["‘Cruel Mr.", "\"잔인하신 매그너스 씨!\""], ["Magnus!’ said the middle-aged lady; here she wept very copiously indeed.", "중년 숙녀가 말했다. 여기서 그녀는 정말로 펑펑 울었다."]], [["‘Address your observations to me, sir,’ interposed Mr.", "\"하실 말씀은 저에게 하십시오, 선생.\" 픽윅 씨가 끼어들었다."], ["Pickwick; ‘I alone am to blame, if anybody be.’", "\"누가 탓을 받아야 한다면, 그건 오직 저뿐입니다.\""]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["you alone are to blame, are you, sir?’ said Mr.", "오직 당신만 탓을 받아야 한다고요, 선생?\" 매그너스 씨가 말했다."], ["Magnus; ‘I--I--see through this, sir.", "\"저—저는—이게 다 보입니다, 선생."], ["You repent of your determination now, do you?’", "이제 와서 당신의 결심을 후회하시는 거군요, 그렇죠?\""]], [["‘My determination!’ said Mr.", "\"제 결심이라니요!\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Your determination, Sir.", "\"당신의 결심 말입니다, 선생."], ["Oh!", "오!"], ["don’t stare at me, Sir,’ said Mr.", "저를 빤히 노려보지 마십시오, 선생.\""], ["Magnus; ‘I recollect your words last night, Sir.", "매그너스 씨가 말했다. \"어젯밤 당신이 한 말을 기억합니다, 선생."], ["You came down here, sir, to expose the treachery and falsehood of an individual on whose truth and honour you had placed implicit reliance--eh?’ Here Mr.", "당신은 이리로 내려오신 거지요, 선생, 당신이 그 진실과 명예를 전적으로 신뢰했던 어떤 인물의 배신과 거짓을 폭로하러—그렇죠?\""], ["Peter Magnus indulged in a prolonged sneer; and taking off his green spectacles-- which he probably found superfluous in his fit of jealousy--rolled his little eyes about, in a manner frightful to behold.", "여기서 피터 매그너스 씨는 길게 비웃음을 흘리고는, 아마도 질투의 발작 속에서 거추장스럽게 느껴졌을 초록빛 안경을 벗어 들고, 보기에도 끔찍한 모양으로 작은 두 눈을 이리저리 굴렸다."]], [["‘Eh?’ said Mr.", "\"그렇죠?\""], ["Magnus; and then he repeated the sneer with increased effect.", "매그너스 씨가 말했다. 그러고는 한층 효과를 더해 다시 비웃었다."], ["‘But you shall answer it, Sir.’", "\"하지만 그 대가를 치르셔야 할 겁니다, 선생.\""]], [["‘Answer what?’ said Mr.", "\"무슨 대가 말입니까?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Never mind, sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"신경 쓰지 마십시오, 선생.\""], ["Magnus, striding up and down the room.", "매그너스 씨가 방 안을 성큼성큼 오가며 대답했다."], ["‘Never mind.’", "\"신경 쓰지 마십시오.\""]], [["There must be something very comprehensive in this phrase of ‘Never mind,’ for we do not recollect to have ever witnessed a quarrel in the street, at a theatre, public room, or elsewhere, in which it has not been the standard reply to all belligerent inquiries.", "이 '신경 쓰지 마십시오'라는 말에는 무언가 대단히 포괄적인 데가 있는 것이 틀림없으니, 우리는 거리에서든, 극장에서든, 공공장소에서든, 그 어디에서든 다툼을 목격할 때마다 이 말이 모든 호전적인 질문에 대한 표준적인 대답이 아니었던 적을 본 기억이 없다."], ["‘Do you call yourself a gentleman, sir?’--‘Never mind, sir.’", "\"당신, 자기가 신사라고 생각하시오, 선생?\"—\"신경 쓰지 마시오, 선생.\""]], [["Did I offer to say anything to the young woman, sir?’--‘Never mind, sir.’", "\"내가 그 젊은 여인에게 무슨 말이라도 건넸소, 선생?\"—\"신경 쓰지 마시오, 선생.\""]], [["Do you want your head knocked up against that wall, sir?’--‘Never mind, sir.’ It is observable, too, that there would appear to be some hidden taunt in this universal ‘Never mind,’ which rouses more indignation in the bosom of the individual addressed, than the most lavish abuse could possibly awaken.", "\"당신, 저 벽에다 머리를 처박히고 싶소, 선생?\"—\"신경 쓰지 마시오, 선생.\" 또한 주목할 만한 것은, 이 어디서나 통하는 '신경 쓰지 마시오' 속에는 무언가 숨겨진 조롱이 들어 있는 듯하여, 아무리 입에 담을 수 있는 욕설을 마구 퍼붓는다 한들 그것이 일깨울 수 있는 것보다도 더 큰 분개를 그 말을 듣는 사람의 가슴속에 불러일으킨다는 점이다."]], [["We do not mean to assert that the application of this brevity to himself, struck exactly that indignation to Mr.", "우리는 이 짧은 말이 자신에게 쓰였을 때 픽윅 씨의 영혼에, 천박한 가슴이라면 틀림없이 불러일으켰을 바로 그런 분개를 정확히 일으켰다고 주장하려는 것은 아니다."], ["Pickwick’s soul, which it would infallibly have roused in a vulgar breast.", "우리는 다만 픽윅 씨가 방문을 열고 불쑥, \"터프먼, 이리 오게!\""], ["We merely record the fact that Mr. Pickwick opened the room door, and abruptly called out, ‘Tupman, come here!’", "하고 외쳤다는 사실을 기록할 뿐이다."]], [["Mr. Tupman immediately presented himself, with a look of very considerable surprise.", "터프먼 씨가 자못 놀란 표정으로 곧장 나타났다."]], [["‘Tupman,’ said Mr.", "\"터프먼.\""], ["Pickwick, ‘a secret of some delicacy, in which that lady is concerned, is the cause of a difference which has just arisen between this gentleman and myself.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"저 숙녀분과 관련된, 다소 민감한 비밀 하나가 방금 이 신사분과 나 사이에 일어난 의견 차이의 원인일세."], ["When I assure him, in your presence, that it has no relation to himself, and is not in any way connected with his affairs, I need hardly beg you to take notice that if he continue to dispute it, he expresses a doubt of my veracity, which I shall consider extremely insulting.’ As Mr.", "내가 자네 앞에서, 이것이 저 신사분과는 아무 관련이 없으며 그의 일과는 어떤 식으로도 얽혀 있지 않다고 단언하는 마당에, 만약 저분이 계속 그것을 의심한다면, 그것은 내 진실성을 의심하는 것이니, 나는 그것을 극히 모욕적으로 여기리라는 점을 자네가 유념해 주기를 굳이 청할 필요도 없겠지.\""], ["Pickwick said this, he looked encyclopedias at Mr. Peter Magnus.", "픽윅 씨는 이렇게 말하면서 백과사전만큼이나 의미심장한 눈길로 피터 매그너스 씨를 바라보았다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick’s upright and honourable bearing, coupled with that force and energy of speech which so eminently distinguished him, would have carried conviction to any reasonable mind; but, unfortunately, at that particular moment, the mind of Mr.", "픽윅 씨의 곧고 명예로운 태도에, 그를 그토록 두드러지게 돋보이게 하는 그 힘차고 정력적인 화술이 더해졌으니, 이성적인 사람이라면 누구든 납득시켰을 것이다."], ["Peter Magnus was in anything but reasonable order. Consequently, instead of receiving Mr.", "그러나 불행히도 바로 그 순간 피터 매그너스 씨의 정신은 이성적인 상태와는 거리가 멀었다."], ["Pickwick’s explanation as he ought to have done, he forthwith proceeded to work himself into a red-hot, scorching, consuming passion, and to talk about what was due to his own feelings, and all that sort of thing; adding force to his declamation by striding to and fro, and pulling his hair-- amusements which he would vary occasionally, by shaking his fist in Mr.", "따라서 그는 마땅히 받아들였어야 할 픽윅 씨의 해명을 받아들이는 대신, 곧장 시뻘겋게 달아오른, 타는 듯한, 모든 것을 삼킬 듯한 격정 속으로 자신을 몰아넣으며, 자기 감정에 대한 도리가 어떻고 하는 그런 따위의 이야기를 늘어놓기 시작했다."], ["Pickwick’s philanthropic countenance.", "그러면서 이리저리 성큼성큼 거닐고 제 머리카락을 쥐어뜯는 것으로 그 열변에 힘을 보탰으니—이런 놀이는 이따금씩 픽윅 씨의 박애로운 얼굴 앞에 주먹을 흔들어 대는 것으로 변화를 주었다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick, in his turn, conscious of his own innocence and rectitude, and irritated by having unfortunately involved the middle-aged lady in such an unpleasant affair, was not so quietly disposed as was his wont. The consequence was, that words ran high, and voices higher; and at length Mr. Magnus told Mr. Pickwick he should hear from him; to which Mr. Pickwick replied, with laudable politeness, that the sooner he heard from him the better; whereupon the middle-aged lady rushed in terror from the room, out of which Mr. Tupman dragged Mr. Pickwick, leaving Mr. Peter Magnus to himself and meditation.", "이번에는 픽윅 씨가, 제 결백과 올바름을 자각하고 있으면서도, 불운하게 중년 숙녀를 이런 불쾌한 일에 끌어들이고 만 데에 화가 나서, 평소만큼 차분하게 굴지 못했다. 그 결과 말이 거칠어지고 목소리는 더욱 높아졌으며, 마침내 매그너스 씨는 픽윅 씨에게 곧 자신의 연락을 받게 될 것이라고 말했고, 이에 픽윅 씨는 칭찬할 만한 정중함으로, 그 연락이 빠를수록 좋다고 대답했다. 그러자 중년 숙녀는 겁에 질려 방에서 뛰쳐나갔고, 터프먼 씨는 거기서 픽윅 씨를 끌어내어, 피터 매그너스 씨를 홀로 명상에 잠기게 내버려 두었다."]], [["If the middle-aged lady had mingled much with the busy world, or had profited at all by the manners and customs of those who make the laws and set the fashions, she would have known that this sort of ferocity is the most harmless thing in nature; but as she had lived for the most part in the country, and never read the parliamentary debates, she was little versed in these particular refinements of civilised life.", "만약 그 중년 숙녀가 분주한 세상과 많이 어울려 보았거나, 법을 만들고 유행을 정하는 자들의 풍습과 관습에서 조금이라도 득을 본 적이 있었다면, 이런 종류의 사나움이야말로 세상에서 가장 무해한 것임을 알았을 것이다."], ["Accordingly, when she had gained her bedchamber, bolted herself in, and began to meditate on the scene she had just witnessed, the most terrific pictures of slaughter and destruction presented themselves to her imagination; among which, a full-length portrait of Mr.", "그러나 그녀는 거의 평생을 시골에서 살았고 의회 토론을 읽은 적도 없었기에, 문명 생활의 이런 특정한 세련됨에는 거의 정통하지 못했다."], ["Peter Magnus borne home by four men, with the embellishment of a whole barrelful of bullets in his left side, was among the very least. The more the middle- aged lady meditated, the more terrified she became; and at length she determined to repair to the house of the principal magistrate of the town, and request him to secure the persons of Mr.", "따라서 그녀가 제 침실로 들어가 안에서 빗장을 지르고, 방금 목격한 장면을 곰곰이 생각하기 시작하자, 살육과 파멸의 더없이 무시무시한 그림들이 그녀의 상상 속에 떠올랐으니, 그 가운데 피터 매그너스 씨가 네 사내에게 떠메여 집으로 옮겨지고, 그의 왼쪽 옆구리에 총알 한 통이 통째로 박힌 장식이 더해진 전신 초상화 따위는 가장 약과에 속하는 것이었다."], ["Pickwick and Mr. Tupman without delay.", "중년 숙녀는 생각하면 할수록 더욱 겁에 질렸고, 마침내 그 고을의 으뜸 치안판사 댁으로 가서, 픽윅 씨와 터프먼 씨의 신병을 지체 없이 확보해 달라고 청하기로 결심했다."]], [["To this decision the middle-aged lady was impelled by a variety of considerations, the chief of which was the incontestable proof it would afford of her devotion to Mr. Peter Magnus, and her anxiety for his safety.", "중년 숙녀가 이런 결심에 이르게 된 데에는 여러 가지 고려가 작용했는데, 그중 으뜸은 그것이 피터 매그너스 씨에 대한 자신의 헌신과 그의 안전에 대한 염려를 반박할 수 없게 증명해 주리라는 점이었다."], ["She was too well acquainted with his jealous temperament to venture the slightest allusion to the real cause of her agitation on beholding Mr.", "그녀는 그의 질투심 많은 기질을 너무도 잘 알고 있었기에, 픽윅 씨를 보고 동요한 진짜 원인을 조금이라도 내비칠 엄두를 내지 못했다."], ["Pickwick; and she trusted to her own influence and power of persuasion with the little man, to quell his boisterous jealousy, supposing that Mr. Pickwick were removed, and no fresh quarrel could arise.", "그래서 그녀는 픽윅 씨가 치워지고 새로운 다툼이 일어날 일이 없어진다면, 자신이 그 작은 사내에게 미치는 영향력과 설득력으로 그의 떠들썩한 질투를 가라앉힐 수 있으리라 믿었다."], ["Filled with these reflections, the middle-aged lady arrayed herself in her bonnet and shawl, and repaired to the mayor’s dwelling straightway.", "이런 생각들로 가득 찬 중년 숙녀는 보닛과 숄을 차려입고는, 곧장 시장의 거처로 향했다."]], [["Now George Nupkins, Esquire, the principal magistrate aforesaid, was as grand a personage as the fastest walker would find out, between sunrise and sunset, on the twenty-first of June, which being, according to the almanacs, the longest day in the whole year, would naturally afford him the longest period for his search.", "그런데 앞서 말한 그 으뜸 치안판사인 조지 너프킨스 님은, 가장 발 빠른 보행자라도 유월 이십일일 해 뜰 녘부터 해 질 녘 사이에 찾아낼 수 있을 만큼 대단한 인물이었으니—그날은 책력에 따르면 일 년 중 가장 긴 날이라, 자연히 그를 찾는 데 가장 긴 시간을 허락해 줄 터였다."], ["On this particular morning, Mr.", "바로 그날 아침, 너프킨스 씨는 더없이 흥분하고 격앙된 상태였으니, 고을에 반란이 일어났던 까닭이다."], ["Nupkins was in a state of the utmost excitement and irritation, for there had been a rebellion in the town; all the day-scholars at the largest day-school had conspired to break the windows of an obnoxious apple-seller, and had hooted the beadle and pelted the constabulary--an elderly gentleman in top-boots, who had been called out to repress the tumult, and who had been a peace-officer, man and boy, for half a century at least. And Mr.", "가장 큰 주간 학교의 통학생들이 죄다 공모하여 눈엣가시 같은 사과 장수의 창문을 깨뜨렸고, 교구 관리에게 야유를 퍼붓고 경찰관에게 돌팔매질을 했으니—그 경찰관이란 장화를 신은 노신사로, 소동을 진압하라고 불려 나온 자였으며, 적어도 반세기 동안 어릴 때부터 줄곧 치안 관리로 지내 온 사람이었다."], ["Nupkins was sitting in his easy-chair, frowning with majesty, and boiling with rage, when a lady was announced on pressing, private, and particular business. Mr.", "그래서 너프킨스 씨는 안락의자에 앉아 위엄 있게 인상을 찌푸리며 분노로 끓어오르고 있었는데, 그때 한 숙녀가 긴급하고 사사로우며 특별한 용무로 왔다는 전갈이 전해졌다."], ["Nupkins looked calmly terrible, and commanded that the lady should be shown in; which command, like all the mandates of emperors, and magistrates, and other great potentates of the earth, was forthwith obeyed; and Miss Witherfield, interestingly agitated, was ushered in accordingly.", "너프킨스 씨는 침착하면서도 무시무시한 표정을 짓고는 그 숙녀를 들이라고 명했다. 그 명령은, 황제와 치안판사와 그 밖에 이 땅의 위대한 권력자들의 모든 분부가 그러하듯, 즉각 받들어졌고, 흥미롭게 동요한 위더필드 양이 그에 따라 안으로 안내되었다."]], [["‘Muzzle!’ said the magistrate.", "\"머즐!\" 치안판사가 말했다."]], [["Muzzle was an undersized footman, with a long body and short legs.", "머즐은 몸통은 길고 다리는 짧은, 키 작은 하인이었다."]], [["‘Muzzle!’", "\"머즐!\""]], [["Yes, your Worship.’", "\"네, 나리.\""]], [["‘Place a chair, and leave the room.’", "\"의자를 놓고 방에서 나가게.\""]], [["‘Yes, your Worship.’", "\"네, 나리.\""]], [["‘Now, ma’am, will you state your business?’ said the magistrate.", "\"자, 부인, 용건을 말씀해 보시겠습니까?\" 치안판사가 말했다."]], [["‘It is of a very painful kind, Sir,’ said Miss Witherfield.", "\"매우 괴로운 종류의 일입니다, 나리.\" 위더필드 양이 말했다."]], [["‘Very likely, ma’am,’ said the magistrate.", "\"그럴 법도 하지요, 부인.\" 치안판사가 말했다."], ["‘Compose your feelings, ma’am.’ Here Mr.", "\"마음을 가라앉히십시오, 부인.\""], ["Nupkins looked benignant.", "여기서 너프킨스 씨는 인자한 표정을 지었다."], ["‘And then tell me what legal business brings you here, ma’am.’ Here the magistrate triumphed over the man; and he looked stern again.", "\"그러고 나서 어떤 법적인 용무로 여기 오셨는지 말씀해 주시지요, 부인.\" 여기서 치안판사는 인간적인 면을 누르고 직무자로서 의기양양해져, 다시 근엄한 표정을 지었다."]], [["‘It is very distressing to me, Sir, to give this information,’ said Miss Witherfield, ‘but I fear a duel is going to be fought here.’", "\"이 정보를 알려 드리는 것이 저로서는 매우 괴롭습니다, 나리.\" 위더필드 양이 말했다. \"하지만 여기서 결투가 벌어질 것 같아 두렵습니다.\""]], [["‘Here, ma’am?’ said the magistrate.", "\"여기서요, 부인?\" 치안판사가 말했다."], ["‘Where, ma’am?’", "\"어디서 말입니까, 부인?\""]], [["‘In Ipswich.’", "\"입스위치에서요.\""]], [["In Ipswich, ma’am!", "\"입스위치에서라니요, 부인!"], ["A duel in Ipswich!’ said the magistrate, perfectly aghast at the notion.", "입스위치에서 결투라니!\" 치안판사가 그 생각에 완전히 아연실색하여 말했다."], ["‘Impossible, ma’am; nothing of the kind can be contemplated in this town, I am persuaded.", "\"있을 수 없는 일입니다, 부인. 이 고을에서는 그런 일이 도모될 수 없다고 저는 확신합니다."], ["Bless my soul, ma’am, are you aware of the activity of our local magistracy?", "맙소사, 부인, 우리 지방 치안 당국의 활약상을 알고 계십니까?"], ["Do you happen to have heard, ma’am, that I rushed into a prize-ring on the fourth of May last, attended by only sixty special constables; and, at the hazard of falling a sacrifice to the angry passions of an infuriated multitude, prohibited a pugilistic contest between the Middlesex Dumpling and the Suffolk Bantam?", "혹시 들으신 적이 있습니까, 부인, 제가 지난 오월 사일에, 단 예순 명의 특별 순경만을 거느리고 권투 시합장으로 뛰어들어, 격분한 군중의 성난 격정에 희생될 위험을 무릅쓰고, 미들섹스 덤플링과 서퍽 밴텀 사이의 권투 시합을 금지시켰다는 것을요? 입스위치에서 결투라니요, 부인?"], ["A duel in Ipswich, ma’am?", "저는 생각지 않습니다—저는 도무지 생각할 수 없습니다.\""], ["I don’t think--I do not think,’ said the magistrate, reasoning with himself, ‘that any two men can have had the hardihood to plan such a breach of the peace, in this town.’", "치안판사가 혼자 따져 보며 말했다. \"이 고을에서 어떤 두 사람이 그런 치안 문란을 꾸밀 만큼 대담했으리라고는요.\""]], [["‘My information is, unfortunately, but too correct,’ said the middle- aged lady; ‘I was present at the quarrel.’", "\"제 정보는, 안타깝게도, 지나치리만큼 정확합니다.\" 중년 숙녀가 말했다. \"제가 그 다툼 자리에 있었습니다.\""]], [["‘It’s a most extraordinary thing,’ said the astounded magistrate.", "\"참으로 기이한 일이로군요.\" 깜짝 놀란 치안판사가 말했다."], ["‘Muzzle!’", "\"머즐!\""]], [["‘Yes, your Worship.’", "\"네, 나리.\""]], [["‘Send Mr. Jinks here, directly!", "\"징크스 씨를 당장 이리로 보내게!"], ["Instantly.’", "즉시.\""]], [["‘Yes, your Worship.’", "\"네, 나리.\""]], [["Muzzle retired; and a pale, sharp-nosed, half-fed, shabbily-clad clerk, of middle age, entered the room.", "머즐이 물러갔고, 창백하고 코가 뾰족하며 영양 상태가 부실하고 초라한 옷차림을 한 중년의 서기가 방으로 들어왔다."]], [["‘Mr.", "\"징크스 씨.\""], ["Jinks,’ said the magistrate.", "치안판사가 말했다."], ["‘Mr. Jinks.’", "\"징크스 씨.\""]], [["‘Sir,’ said Mr.", "\"네, 나리.\""], ["Jinks.", "징크스 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘This lady, Mr. Jinks, has come here, to give information of an intended duel in this town.’", "\"이 부인께서, 징크스 씨, 이 고을에서 계획된 결투에 관한 정보를 알리러 오셨네.\""]], [["Mr. Jinks, not knowing exactly what to do, smiled a dependent’s smile.", "징크스 씨는 정확히 어떻게 해야 할지 몰라, 아랫사람다운 미소를 지었다."]], [["‘What are you laughing at, Mr.", "\"무엇이 우습소, 징크스 씨?\""], ["Jinks?’ said the magistrate.", "치안판사가 말했다."]], [["Mr. Jinks looked serious instantly.", "징크스 씨는 즉시 진지한 표정을 지었다."]], [["‘Mr.", "\"징크스 씨.\""], ["Jinks,’ said the magistrate, ‘you’re a fool.’", "치안판사가 말했다. \"자네는 바보일세.\""]], [["Mr. Jinks looked humbly at the great man, and bit the top of his pen.", "징크스 씨는 그 위대한 인물을 겸손하게 바라보며, 펜 끝을 깨물었다."]], [["‘You may see something very comical in this information, Sir--but I can tell you this, Mr.", "\"이 정보에서 자네는 무언가 아주 우스운 점을 볼지도 모르지, 선생—하지만 내가 말해 두건대, 징크스 씨, 자네에게는 웃을 거리가 거의 없네.\""], ["Jinks, that you have very little to laugh at,’ said the magistrate.", "치안판사가 말했다."]], [["The hungry-looking Jinks sighed, as if he were quite aware of the fact of his having very little indeed to be merry about; and, being ordered to take the lady’s information, shambled to a seat, and proceeded to write it down.", "굶주린 듯한 징크스는, 자신에게 즐거워할 거리가 정말이지 거의 없다는 사실을 잘 알고 있다는 듯 한숨을 내쉬었다. 그러고는 숙녀의 정보를 받아 적으라는 명을 받자, 비척비척 자리로 가서 그것을 받아 적기 시작했다."]], [["‘This man, Pickwick, is the principal, I understand?’ said the magistrate, when the statement was finished.", "\"제가 이해하기로는, 이 픽윅이라는 자가 주범이지요?\" 진술이 끝나자 치안판사가 말했다."]], [["‘He is,’ said the middle-aged lady.", "\"그렇습니다.\" 중년 숙녀가 말했다."]], [["‘And the other rioter--what’s his name, Mr. Jinks?’", "\"그리고 다른 폭도는—그자 이름이 뭐였소, 징크스 씨?\""]], [["‘Tupman, Sir.’", "\"터프먼입니다, 나리.\""]], [["Tupman is the second?’", "\"터프먼이 입회인인가?\""]], [["‘Yes.’", "\"네.\""]], [["‘The other principal, you say, has absconded, ma’am?’", "\"또 다른 주범은, 말씀하시기를, 달아났다고요, 부인?\""]], [["‘Yes,’ replied Miss Witherfield, with a short cough.", "\"네.\" 위더필드 양이 짧게 헛기침을 하며 대답했다."]], [["‘Very well,’ said the magistrate.", "\"좋습니다.\" 치안판사가 말했다."], ["‘These are two cut-throats from London, who have come down here to destroy his Majesty’s population, thinking that at this distance from the capital, the arm of the law is weak and paralysed.", "\"이자들은 런던에서 온 두 명의 살인배로, 수도에서 이만큼 떨어진 곳이라면 법의 팔이 약하고 마비되어 있으리라 여기고, 폐하의 백성을 해치러 이리로 내려온 것이오."], ["They shall be made an example of.", "본보기로 삼아 주겠소."], ["Draw up the warrants, Mr. Jinks.", "영장을 작성하게, 징크스 씨."], ["Muzzle!’", "머즐!\""]], [["‘Yes, your Worship.’", "\"네, 나리.\""]], [["‘Is Grummer downstairs?’", "\"그러머는 아래층에 있는가?\""]], [["‘Yes, your Worship.’", "\"네, 나리.\""]], [["‘Send him up.’", "\"그를 올려 보내게.\""]], [["The obsequious Muzzle retired, and presently returned, introducing the elderly gentleman in the top-boots, who was chiefly remarkable for a bottle-nose, a hoarse voice, a snuff-coloured surtout, and a wandering eye.", "굽실거리는 머즐이 물러갔다가, 이내 돌아오며 장화를 신은 그 노신사를 안내해 들였는데, 그자는 무엇보다도 술병 모양의 코, 쉰 목소리, 코담배 빛깔의 외투, 그리고 두리번거리는 눈으로 두드러지는 인물이었다."]], [["‘Grummer,’ said the magistrate.", "\"그러머.\" 치안판사가 말했다."]], [["‘Your Wash-up.’", "\"넵, 나리.\""]], [["‘Is the town quiet now?’", "\"고을은 이제 잠잠해졌는가?\""]], [["‘Pretty well, your Wash-up,’ replied Grummer.", "\"꽤 잠잠합니다요, 나리.\" 그러머가 대답했다."], ["‘Pop’lar feeling has in a measure subsided, consekens o’ the boys having dispersed to cricket.’", "\"민중의 감정이 어느 정도 가라앉았는데요, 사내애들이 크리켓 하러 흩어진 덕분입죠.\""]], [["‘Nothing but vigorous measures will do in these times, Grummer,’ said the magistrate, in a determined manner.", "\"이런 시대엔 단호한 조치만이 통하네, 그러머.\" 치안판사가 단호한 태도로 말했다."], ["‘If the authority of the king’s officers is set at naught, we must have the riot act read.", "\"국왕의 관리들의 권위가 무시당한다면, 우리는 폭동 진압령을 낭독해야 하네."], ["If the civil power cannot protect these windows, Grummer, the military must protect the civil power, and the windows too.", "만약 민간 권력이 이 창문들을 보호하지 못한다면, 그러머, 군대가 민간 권력을 보호해야 하고, 창문들도 보호해야 하네."], ["I believe that is a maxim of the constitution, Mr. Jinks?’", "그것이 헌법의 금언이라고 보는데, 징크스 씨?\""]], [["Certainly, sir,’ said Jinks.", "\"물론입니다, 나리.\" 징크스가 말했다."]], [["‘Very good,’ said the magistrate, signing the warrants.", "\"아주 좋아.\" 치안판사가 영장에 서명하며 말했다."], ["‘Grummer, you will bring these persons before me, this afternoon.", "\"그러머, 오늘 오후에 이자들을 내 앞으로 데려오게."], ["You will find them at the Great White Horse.", "그레이트 화이트 호스에 가면 그들을 찾을 수 있을 걸세."], ["You recollect the case of the Middlesex Dumpling and the Suffolk Bantam, Grummer?’", "미들섹스 덤플링과 서퍽 밴텀 사건을 기억하지, 그러머?\""]], [["Mr. Grummer intimated, by a retrospective shake of the head, that he should never forget it--as indeed it was not likely he would, so long as it continued to be cited daily.", "그러머 씨는 회상하듯 고개를 가로저어, 그 일을 결코 잊지 못하리라는 뜻을 내비쳤으니—사실 그 일이 날마다 거론되는 한, 그가 잊을 리도 없었다."]], [["‘This is even more unconstitutional,’ said the magistrate; ‘this is even a greater breach of the peace, and a grosser infringement of his Majesty’s prerogative.", "\"이번 일은 그보다 한층 더 헌법에 어긋나는 일이네.\" 치안판사가 말했다."], ["I believe duelling is one of his Majesty’s most undoubted prerogatives, Mr.", "\"이건 한층 더 큰 치안 문란이고, 폐하의 대권을 한층 더 심하게 침해하는 일일세."], ["Jinks?’", "결투란 폐하의 가장 의심할 바 없는 대권 가운데 하나라고 보는데, 징크스 씨?\""]], [["‘Expressly stipulated in Magna Charta, sir,’ said Mr.", "\"마그나 카르타에 명시적으로 규정되어 있습니다, 나리.\""], ["Jinks.", "징크스 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘One of the brightest jewels in the British crown, wrung from his Majesty by the barons, I believe, Mr.", "\"영국 왕관의 가장 빛나는 보석 가운데 하나로, 귀족들이 폐하로부터 쟁취해 낸 것이라고 보는데, 징크스 씨?\""], ["Jinks?’ said the magistrate.", "치안판사가 말했다."]], [["‘Just so, Sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"바로 그렇습니다, 나리.\""], ["Jinks.", "징크스 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘Very well,’ said the magistrate, drawing himself up proudly, ‘it shall not be violated in this portion of his dominions.", "\"아주 좋아.\" 치안판사가 자랑스럽게 몸을 곧추세우며 말했다."], ["Grummer, procure assistance, and execute these warrants with as little delay as possible.", "\"그것이 짐의 영토 이 부분에서 침해당하는 일은 없을 것이다. 그러머, 인력을 구해서, 되도록 지체 없이 이 영장들을 집행하게."], ["Muzzle!’", "머즐!\""]], [["‘Yes, your Worship.’", "\"네, 나리.\""]], [["‘Show the lady out.’", "\"숙녀분을 배웅해 드리게.\""]], [["Miss Witherfield retired, deeply impressed with the magistrate’s learning and research; Mr.", "위더필드 양은 치안판사의 학식과 박식함에 깊은 감명을 받고 물러갔다."], ["Nupkins retired to lunch; Mr.", "너프킨스 씨는 점심을 들러 물러갔다."], ["Jinks retired within himself--that being the only retirement he had, except the sofa- bedstead in the small parlour which was occupied by his landlady’s family in the daytime--and Mr.", "징크스 씨는 제 안으로 물러갔으니—낮 동안에는 집주인의 식구들이 차지하는 작은 응접실의 소파 겸 침대를 제외하면, 그것이 그가 가진 유일한 안식처였다."], ["Grummer retired, to wipe out, by his mode of discharging his present commission, the insult which had been fastened upon himself, and the other representative of his Majesty--the beadle--in the course of the morning.", "그리고 그러머 씨는, 그날 아침 자신과 폐하의 또 다른 대리인—교구 관리—에게 가해진 모욕을, 이번 임무를 수행하는 방식으로 씻어 내고자 물러갔다."]], [["While these resolute and determined preparations for the conservation of the king’s peace were pending, Mr.", "국왕의 치안 보전을 위한 이 결연하고 단호한 준비가 진행되는 동안, 픽윅 씨와 그의 친구들은, 진행 중인 이 엄청난 사건들을 전혀 알지 못한 채, 조용히 만찬 자리에 앉아 있었다."], ["Pickwick and his friends, wholly unconscious of the mighty events in progress, had sat quietly down to dinner; and very talkative and companionable they all were.", "그리고 그들은 모두 매우 이야기가 많고 사이좋게 어울렸다."], ["Mr. Pickwick was in the very act of relating his adventure of the preceding night, to the great amusement of his followers, Mr.", "픽윅 씨가 마침 전날 밤의 모험담을 들려주어 추종자들, 특히 터프먼 씨를 크게 즐겁게 해 주고 있는데, 그때 문이 열리며 다소 험상궂은 얼굴 하나가 방 안을 들여다보았다."], ["Tupman especially, when the door opened, and a somewhat forbidding countenance peeped into the room. The eyes in the forbidding countenance looked very earnestly at Mr.", "그 험상궂은 얼굴의 두 눈은 몇 초 동안 픽윅 씨를 매우 진지하게 바라보았고, 겉보기에는 그 조사에 만족한 듯했다."], ["Pickwick, for several seconds, and were to all appearance satisfied with their investigation; for the body to which the forbidding countenance belonged, slowly brought itself into the apartment, and presented the form of an elderly individual in top-boots--not to keep the reader any longer in suspense, in short, the eyes were the wandering eyes of Mr. Grummer, and the body was the body of the same gentleman.", "그 험상궂은 얼굴이 달려 있는 몸뚱이가 천천히 방 안으로 들어와, 장화를 신은 한 노인의 형상을 드러냈기 때문이다—독자를 더 이상 마음 졸이게 하지 않자면, 요컨대 그 두 눈은 그러머 씨의 두리번거리는 눈이었고, 그 몸뚱이는 바로 그 신사의 몸뚱이였다."]], [["Mr. Grummer’s mode of proceeding was professional, but peculiar.", "그러머 씨의 일 처리 방식은 직업적이었으나, 별난 데가 있었다."], ["His first act was to bolt the door on the inside; his second, to polish his head and countenance very carefully with a cotton handkerchief; his third, to place his hat, with the cotton handkerchief in it, on the nearest chair; and his fourth, to produce from the breast-pocket of his coat a short truncheon, surmounted by a brazen crown, with which he beckoned to Mr. Pickwick with a grave and ghost-like air.", "그의 첫 번째 행동은 안에서 문에 빗장을 지르는 것이었고, 두 번째는 면 손수건으로 자신의 머리와 얼굴을 매우 정성스레 닦는 것이었으며, 세 번째는 모자를 그 안에 면 손수건을 넣은 채 가장 가까운 의자에 놓는 것이었고, 네 번째는 외투 가슴 주머니에서 놋쇠 왕관이 얹힌 짧은 곤봉을 꺼내, 그것으로 엄숙하고 유령 같은 태도로 픽윅 씨에게 까딱 손짓하는 것이었다."]], [["Mr. Snodgrass was the first to break the astonished silence.", "놀란 침묵을 처음 깬 것은 스노드그래스 씨였다."], ["He looked steadily at Mr.", "그는 잠깐 동안 그러머 씨를 빤히 바라보다가, 힘주어 말했다."], ["Grummer for a brief space, and then said emphatically, ‘This is a private room, Sir.", "\"여기는 사적인 방입니다, 선생."], ["A private room.’", "사적인 방이라고요.\""]], [["Mr. Grummer shook his head, and replied, ‘No room’s private to his Majesty when the street door’s once passed.", "그러머 씨는 고개를 가로저으며 대답했다."], ["That’s law.", "\"일단 길가 문턱을 넘고 나면 폐하에게는 사적인 방이란 없소."], ["Some people maintains that an Englishman’s house is his castle.", "그게 법이오. 어떤 사람들은 영국인의 집은 그의 성채라고 우기지."], ["That’s gammon.’", "그건 헛소리요.\""]], [["The Pickwickians gazed on each other with wondering eyes.", "픽윅 회원들은 놀란 눈으로 서로를 바라보았다."]], [["‘Which is Mr.", "\"누가 터프먼 씨요?\""], ["Tupman?’ inquired Mr. Grummer.", "그러머 씨가 물었다."], ["He had an intuitive perception of Mr. Pickwick; he knew him at once.", "그는 픽윅 씨에 대해서는 직관적인 감이 있어, 단번에 그를 알아보았다."]], [["‘My name’s Tupman,’ said that gentleman.", "\"내가 터프먼이오.\" 그 신사가 말했다."]], [["‘My name’s Law,’ said Mr.", "\"내 이름은 법이오.\""], ["Grummer.", "그러머 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘What?’ said Mr.", "\"뭐라고요?\""], ["Tupman.", "터프먼 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Law,’ replied Mr.", "\"법이오.\" 그러머 씨가 대답했다."], ["Grummer--‘Law, civil power, and exekative; them’s my titles; here’s my authority.", "\"법, 민간 권력, 그리고 집행권. 그게 내 직함이오."], ["Blank Tupman, blank Pickwick--against the peace of our sufferin’ lord the king--stattit in the case made and purwided--and all regular.", "여기 내 권한이 있고. 백지 터프먼, 백지 픽윅—우리의 수난하시는 군주 폐하의 평안을 거스른 죄로—해당 사건에 대해 제정되고 규정된 법령에 따라—모두 정식이오."], ["I apprehend you Pickwick!", "픽윅, 그대를 체포하오!"], ["Tupman--the aforesaid.’", "터프먼—앞서 말한 자도.\""]], [["‘What do you mean by this insolence?’ said Mr.", "\"이 무례함은 대체 무슨 뜻이오?\""], ["Tupman, starting up; ‘leave the room!’", "터프먼 씨가 벌떡 일어서며 말했다. \"방에서 나가시오!\""]], [["‘Hollo,’ said Mr.", "\"여보게.\""], ["Grummer, retreating very expeditiously to the door, and opening it an inch or two, ‘Dubbley.’", "그러머 씨가 아주 잽싸게 문 쪽으로 물러나 문을 한두 치쯤 열며 말했다. \"더블리.\""]], [["‘Well,’ said a deep voice from the passage.", "\"네.\" 복도에서 굵은 목소리가 들려왔다."]], [["‘Come for’ard, Dubbley.’", "\"앞으로 나오게, 더블리.\""]], [["At the word of command, a dirty-faced man, something over six feet high, and stout in proportion, squeezed himself through the half-open door (making his face very red in the process), and entered the room.", "그 명령 한마디에, 키가 육 척이 조금 넘고 그에 걸맞게 몸집이 우람한, 얼굴이 더러운 사내가 반쯤 열린 문 사이로 몸을 비집고 들어와(그 과정에서 얼굴이 시뻘게진 채), 방으로 들어섰다."]], [["‘Is the other specials outside, Dubbley?’ inquired Mr.", "\"나머지 특별 순경들은 밖에 있는가, 더블리?\""], ["Grummer.", "그러머 씨가 물었다."]], [["Mr. Dubbley, who was a man of few words, nodded assent.", "말수가 적은 사내인 더블리 씨는 고개를 끄덕여 그렇다고 했다."]], [["‘Order in the diwision under your charge, Dubbley,’ said Mr.", "\"자네 휘하의 분대를 들여보내게, 더블리.\""], ["Grummer.", "그러머 씨가 말했다."]], [["Mr. Dubbley did as he was desired; and half a dozen men, each with a short truncheon and a brass crown, flocked into the room.", "더블리 씨가 시키는 대로 했고, 저마다 짧은 곤봉과 놋쇠 왕관을 든 사내 여섯 명이 우르르 방으로 몰려 들어왔다."], ["Mr. Grummer pocketed his staff, and looked at Mr.", "그러머 씨는 제 곤봉을 주머니에 넣고 더블리 씨를 바라보았다."], ["Dubbley; Mr. Dubbley pocketed his staff and looked at the division; the division pocketed their staves and looked at Messrs.", "더블리 씨는 제 곤봉을 주머니에 넣고 분대를 바라보았다."], ["Tupman and Pickwick.", "분대는 저마다 곤봉을 주머니에 넣고 터프먼 씨와 픽윅 씨를 바라보았다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick and his followers rose as one man.", "픽윅 씨와 그의 추종자들은 한 사람처럼 일제히 일어섰다."]], [["‘What is the meaning of this atrocious intrusion upon my privacy?’ said Mr.", "\"내 사생활에 대한 이 흉악한 침입은 무슨 뜻이오?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Who dares apprehend me?’ said Mr.", "\"누가 감히 나를 체포한단 말이오?\""], ["Tupman.", "터프먼 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘What do you want here, scoundrels?’ said Mr.", "\"여기서 무엇을 원하는 거요, 이 악당들아?\""], ["Snodgrass.", "스노드그래스 씨가 말했다."]], [["Mr. Winkle said nothing, but he fixed his eyes on Grummer, and bestowed a look upon him, which, if he had had any feeling, must have pierced his brain.", "윈클 씨는 아무 말도 하지 않았으나, 두 눈을 그러머에게 고정시키고는 그에게 한 눈길을 던졌으니, 만약 그자에게 조금이라도 감각이 있었다면 그 눈길은 틀림없이 그의 뇌를 꿰뚫었을 것이다."], ["As it was, however, it had no visible effect on him whatever.", "그러나 실상은, 그것이 그자에게 눈에 띄는 영향이라곤 전혀 미치지 못했다."]], [["When the executive perceived that Mr. Pickwick and his friends were disposed to resist the authority of the law, they very significantly turned up their coat sleeves, as if knocking them down in the first instance, and taking them up afterwards, were a mere professional act which had only to be thought of to be done, as a matter of course.", "집행관들은 픽윅 씨와 그의 친구들이 법의 권위에 저항할 기색임을 알아채자, 매우 의미심장하게 외투 소맷자락을 걷어 올렸으니, 마치 우선 그들을 때려눕히고 나중에 거두어들이는 일이란, 그저 떠올리기만 하면 당연히 해치울 수 있는 단순한 직업적 행위라는 듯했다."], ["This demonstration was not lost upon Mr. Pickwick.", "이 시위는 픽윅 씨에게 헛되지 않았다."], ["He conferred a few moments with Mr. Tupman apart, and then signified his readiness to proceed to the mayor’s residence, merely begging the parties then and there assembled, to take notice, that it was his firm intention to resent this monstrous invasion of his privileges as an Englishman, the instant he was at liberty; whereat the parties then and there assembled laughed very heartily, with the single exception of Mr.", "그는 잠시 터프먼 씨와 따로 의논한 뒤, 시장의 거처로 가겠다는 뜻을 밝혔는데, 다만 그 자리에 모인 자들에게, 자유의 몸이 되는 순간 영국인으로서의 자기 특권에 대한 이 괴이한 침해를 응징하리라는 것이 자신의 확고한 의도임을 유념해 두라고 청할 뿐이었다."], ["Grummer, who seemed to consider that any slight cast upon the divine right of magistrates was a species of blasphemy not to be tolerated.", "그러자 그 자리에 모인 자들은, 치안판사의 신성한 권리에 가해진 어떤 사소한 흠집도 용납할 수 없는 일종의 신성모독으로 여기는 듯한 그러머 씨 한 사람을 빼고는, 모두 매우 신나게 웃어 댔다."]], [["But when Mr. Pickwick had signified his readiness to bow to the laws of his country, and just when the waiters, and hostlers, and chambermaids, and post-boys, who had anticipated a delightful commotion from his threatened obstinacy, began to turn away, disappointed and disgusted, a difficulty arose which had not been foreseen. With every sentiment of veneration for the constituted authorities, Mr. Pickwick resolutely protested against making his appearance in the public streets, surrounded and guarded by the officers of justice, like a common criminal. Mr. Grummer, in the then disturbed state of public feeling (for it was half-holiday, and the boys had not yet gone home), as resolutely protested against walking on the opposite side of the way, and taking Mr. Pickwick’s parole that he would go straight to the magistrate’s; and both Mr. Pickwick and Mr. Tupman as strenuously objected to the expense of a post-coach, which was the only respectable conveyance that could be obtained. The dispute ran high, and the dilemma lasted long; and just as the executive were on the point of overcoming Mr. Pickwick’s objection to walking to the magistrate’s, by the trite expedient of carrying him thither, it was recollected that there stood in the inn yard, an old sedan-chair, which, having been originally built for a gouty gentleman with funded property, would hold Mr. Pickwick and Mr. Tupman, at least as conveniently as a modern post-chaise. The chair was hired, and brought into the hall; Mr. Pickwick and Mr. Tupman squeezed themselves inside, and pulled down the blinds; a couple of chairmen were speedily found; and the procession started in grand order. The specials surrounded the body of the vehicle; Mr. Grummer and Mr. Dubbley marched triumphantly in front; Mr. Snodgrass and Mr. Winkle walked arm-in-arm behind; and the unsoaped of Ipswich brought up the rear.", "그러나 픽윅 씨가 제 나라의 법에 복종하겠다는 뜻을 밝히고, 그가 위협하던 고집에서 즐거운 소동을 기대했던 종업원들과 마부들과 객실 하녀들과 우편 마차 소년들이 막 실망하고 못마땅해하며 돌아서기 시작하던 바로 그때, 미처 예견하지 못했던 난관이 생겼다. 픽윅 씨는 구성된 당국에 대한 온갖 경의를 품으면서도, 흔한 범죄자처럼 사법 관리들에게 둘러싸여 호위받으며 공공 거리에 모습을 드러내는 것에 대해서는 단호히 항의했다. 그러머 씨는, 당시 동요된 민심 상태로 보아(반공일이라 사내애들이 아직 집에 가지 않은 까닭이었다), 길 건너편으로 걷거나 픽윅 씨가 곧장 치안판사에게 가겠다는 서약을 받는 것에 대해 마찬가지로 단호히 항의했다. 그리고 픽윅 씨와 터프먼 씨는 둘 다, 구할 수 있는 유일한 점잖은 탈것인 역마차의 비용에 대해 한사코 반대했다. 논쟁은 격해지고 진퇴양난은 오래 끌었다. 그리고 집행관들이 픽윅 씨를 그냥 떠메어 치안판사에게 데려가는 진부한 방편으로 그의 도보 항의를 막 꺾으려던 참에, 여관 마당에 낡은 가마 하나가 서 있다는 것이 떠올랐으니, 원래 공채를 가진 통풍 환자 신사를 위해 지어진 것이라, 적어도 요즘의 역마차만큼은 편안하게 픽윅 씨와 터프먼 씨를 태울 수 있을 터였다. 가마를 빌려 현관 홀로 들여왔다. 픽윅 씨와 터프먼 씨가 그 안으로 몸을 비집고 들어가 가리개를 내렸다. 가마꾼 두 명이 곧 구해졌고, 행렬은 당당한 격식을 갖추어 출발했다. 특별 순경들은 가마의 몸체를 에워쌌고, 그러머 씨와 더블리 씨는 의기양양하게 앞장서 행진했으며, 스노드그래스 씨와 윈클 씨는 팔짱을 끼고 뒤따랐고, 입스위치의 씻지 않은 무리가 후미를 맡았다."]], [["The shopkeepers of the town, although they had a very indistinct notion of the nature of the offence, could not but be much edified and gratified by this spectacle.", "고을의 상인들은, 그 죄목의 성격에 대해서는 매우 막연한 관념밖에 없었으나, 이 광경에 크게 교화되고 흡족해하지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Here was the strong arm of the law, coming down with twenty gold-beater force, upon two offenders from the metropolis itself; the mighty engine was directed by their own magistrate, and worked by their own officers; and both the criminals, by their united efforts, were securely shut up, in the narrow compass of one sedan-chair.", "여기 법의 강력한 팔이, 다름 아닌 수도에서 온 두 죄인 위로 금박공 스무 명의 힘으로 내려치고 있었다. 그 강력한 기계는 그들 자신의 치안판사가 지휘하고 그들 자신의 관리들이 작동시키는 것이었으며, 두 죄인은 그 합심한 노력 덕에 가마 한 채의 비좁은 공간 속에 단단히 갇혀 있었다."], ["Many were the expressions of approval and admiration which greeted Mr.", "그러머 씨가 곤봉을 손에 들고 행렬을 이끌자, 그를 맞이하는 찬동과 감탄의 표현이 숱하게 쏟아졌다."], ["Grummer, as he headed the cavalcade, staff in hand; loud and long were the shouts raised by the unsoaped; and amidst these united testimonials of public approbation, the procession moved slowly and majestically along.", "씻지 않은 무리가 지르는 함성은 크고도 길었다. 그리고 이렇게 하나로 모인 대중의 갈채 속에서, 행렬은 천천히 그리고 위풍당당하게 나아갔다."]], [["Mr. Weller, habited in his morning jacket, with the black calico sleeves, was returning in a rather desponding state from an unsuccessful survey of the mysterious house with the green gate, when, raising his eyes, he beheld a crowd pouring down the street, surrounding an object which had very much the appearance of a sedan-chair.", "웰러 씨는, 검은 옥양목 소매가 달린 아침나절 웃옷을 입고서, 초록색 대문이 달린 그 수수께끼 같은 집을 살피러 갔다가 허탕을 치고 다소 풀이 죽은 채로 돌아오던 길에, 눈을 들어 보니 군중이 거리로 쏟아져 내려오며 어떤 물체를 에워싸고 있는데, 그것이 영락없이 가마처럼 보였다."], ["Willing to divert his thoughts from the failure of his enterprise, he stepped aside to see the crowd pass; and finding that they were cheering away, very much to their own satisfaction, forthwith began (by way of raising his spirits) to cheer too, with all his might and main.", "자신의 일이 실패한 데서 생각을 돌리고 싶었던 그는, 군중이 지나가는 것을 보려고 옆으로 비켜섰다. 그러다 그들이 저희끼리 매우 흡족해하며 환호성을 지르고 있는 것을 보자, (기운을 북돋울 셈으로) 곧장 그도 있는 힘껏 환호하기 시작했다."]], [["Mr. Grummer passed, and Mr. Dubbley passed, and the sedan passed, and the bodyguard of specials passed, and Sam was still responding to the enthusiastic cheers of the mob, and waving his hat about as if he were in the very last extreme of the wildest joy (though, of course, he had not the faintest idea of the matter in hand), when he was suddenly stopped by the unexpected appearance of Mr. Winkle and Mr. Snodgrass.", "그러머 씨가 지나가고, 더블리 씨가 지나가고, 가마가 지나가고, 특별 순경 호위대가 지나가는 동안에도, 샘은 여전히 군중의 열광적인 환호에 화답하며, (물론 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지는 전혀 짐작도 못 한 채) 더없이 광적인 기쁨의 마지막 극한에 이른 것처럼 모자를 휘둘러 대고 있었는데, 그때 윈클 씨와 스노드그래스 씨가 뜻밖에 나타나는 바람에 그는 갑자기 멈춰 섰다."]], [["‘What’s the row, gen’l’m’n?", "\"무슨 소동입니까, 나리들?\""], ["’cried Sam.", "샘이 외쳤다."], ["‘Who have they got in this here watch-box in mournin’?’", "\"상복 입은 이 파수막 같은 데에 누구를 집어넣은 겁니까?\""]], [["Both gentlemen replied together, but their words were lost in the tumult.", "두 신사가 동시에 대답했으나, 그들의 말은 소란 속에 묻혀 버렸다."]], [["‘Who is it?’ cried Sam again.", "\"누굽니까?\" 샘이 다시 외쳤다."]], [["Once more was a joint reply returned; and, though the words were inaudible, Sam saw by the motion of the two pairs of lips that they had uttered the magic word ‘Pickwick.’", "또 한 번 두 사람이 함께 대답을 했는데, 비록 말소리는 들리지 않았으나, 샘은 두 쌍의 입술이 움직이는 모양을 보고 그들이 마법의 단어 '픽윅'을 내뱉었음을 알아챘다."]], [["This was enough.", "그것으로 충분했다."], ["In another minute Mr. Weller had made his way through the crowd, stopped the chairmen, and confronted the portly Grummer.", "다음 순간 웰러 씨는 군중을 헤치고 나아가 가마꾼들을 멈춰 세우고, 뚱뚱한 그러머와 정면으로 맞섰다."]], [["‘Hollo, old gen’l’m’n!’ said Sam.", "\"이보쇼, 영감님!\" 샘이 말했다."], ["‘Who have you got in this here conweyance?’", "\"이 탈것에 누굴 태운 거요?\""]], [["‘Stand back,’ said Mr.", "\"물러서라.\""], ["Grummer, whose dignity, like the dignity of a great many other men, had been wondrously augmented by a little popularity.", "그러머 씨가 말했는데, 그의 위엄은 다른 숱한 사내들의 위엄이 그러하듯, 약간의 인기로 놀랍도록 부풀어 있었다."]], [["‘Knock him down, if he don’t,’ said Mr.", "\"안 물러서면 때려눕혀.\""], ["Dubbley.", "더블리 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘I’m wery much obliged to you, old gen’l’m’n,’ replied Sam, ‘for consulting my conwenience, and I’m still more obliged to the other gen’l’m’n, who looks as if he’d just escaped from a giant’s carrywan, for his wery ‘andsome suggestion; but I should prefer your givin’ me a answer to my question, if it’s all the same to you.--How are you, Sir?’ This last observation was addressed with a patronising air to Mr. Pickwick, who was peeping through the front window.", "\"내 편의를 챙겨 주셔서 대단히 고맙소, 영감님.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"그리고 거인의 순회 마차에서 막 도망쳐 나온 것 같은 저 양반한테는, 그 아주 멋들어진 제안에 더더욱 고맙고. 하지만 댁만 괜찮으시다면, 내 질문에 대답이나 좀 해 주시는 게 좋겠소.—안녕하십니까, 선생?\" 이 마지막 말은 앞쪽 창으로 내다보고 있던 픽윅 씨에게 너그럽게 거드름 피우는 투로 건넨 것이었다."]], [["Mr. Grummer, perfectly speechless with indignation, dragged the truncheon with the brass crown from its particular pocket, and flourished it before Sam’s eyes.", "분개하여 완전히 말문이 막힌 그러머 씨는, 놋쇠 왕관이 달린 곤봉을 그 특별한 주머니에서 끌어내어 샘의 눈앞에 휘둘러 보였다."]], [["‘Ah,’ said Sam, ‘it’s wery pretty, ‘specially the crown, which is uncommon like the real one.’", "\"아.\" 샘이 말했다. \"거 참 예쁘구려, 특히 그 왕관이 말이오. 진짜하고 똑 닮은 게 보통 아니구려.\""]], [["‘Stand back!’ said the outraged Mr.", "\"물러서라!\""], ["Grummer. By way of adding force to the command, he thrust the brass emblem of royalty into Sam’s neckcloth with one hand, and seized Sam’s collar with the other--a compliment which Mr.", "격분한 그러머 씨가 말했다."], ["Weller returned by knocking him down out of hand, having previously with the utmost consideration, knocked down a chairman for him to lie upon.", "그 명령에 힘을 더할 셈으로, 그는 한 손으로 그 왕권의 놋쇠 표상을 샘의 목도리에 들이밀고, 다른 손으로 샘의 멱살을 움켜쥐었다—이에 웰러 씨는 그를 즉석에서 때려눕히는 것으로 그 인사에 답했는데, 그에 앞서 더없이 세심하게도, 그가 깔고 누울 가마꾼 하나를 먼저 때려눕혀 두었다."]], [["Whether Mr. Winkle was seized with a temporary attack of that species of insanity which originates in a sense of injury, or animated by this display of Mr.", "윈클 씨가, 모욕감에서 비롯되는 그런 종류의 일시적인 광기 발작에 사로잡힌 것인지, 아니면 웰러 씨의 이 용맹스러운 시범에 고무된 것인지는 확실치 않다."], ["Weller’s valour, is uncertain; but certain it is, that he no sooner saw Mr. Grummer fall than he made a terrific onslaught on a small boy who stood next him; whereupon Mr.", "그러나 확실한 것은, 그가 그러머 씨가 쓰러지는 것을 보자마자 곁에 서 있던 한 어린 소년에게 무시무시하게 달려들었다는 것이다."], ["Snodgrass, in a truly Christian spirit, and in order that he might take no one unawares, announced in a very loud tone that he was going to begin, and proceeded to take off his coat with the utmost deliberation.", "이에 스노드그래스 씨는, 참으로 그리스도교적인 정신으로, 또 누구도 불시에 덮치지 않으려고, 매우 큰 소리로 자신이 이제 시작하겠노라고 알린 다음, 더없이 신중하게 외투를 벗기 시작했다."], ["He was immediately surrounded and secured; and it is but common justice both to him and Mr.", "그는 즉시 둘러싸여 제압당했다."], ["Winkle to say, that they did not make the slightest attempt to rescue either themselves or Mr.", "그와 윈클 씨 모두를 위해 공정하게 말해 두자면, 그들은 자신들이든 웰러 씨든 구하려고 조금도 시도하지 않았다."], ["Weller; who, after a most vigorous resistance, was overpowered by numbers and taken prisoner.", "그 웰러 씨는, 더없이 격렬하게 저항한 끝에, 수에 눌려 제압당하고 포로가 되었다."], ["The procession then reformed; the chairmen resumed their stations; and the march was re- commenced.", "그러고 나서 행렬은 다시 정렬했다. 가마꾼들은 제자리로 돌아갔고, 행진이 다시 시작되었다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick’s indignation during the whole of this proceeding was beyond all bounds.", "이 모든 과정 동안 픽윅 씨의 분개는 그 끝을 알 수 없을 지경이었다."], ["He could just see Sam upsetting the specials, and flying about in every direction; and that was all he could see, for the sedan doors wouldn’t open, and the blinds wouldn’t pull up. At length, with the assistance of Mr.", "그는 샘이 특별 순경들을 자빠뜨리며 사방으로 날뛰는 것을 겨우 볼 수 있었는데, 그것이 그가 볼 수 있는 전부였으니, 가마 문은 열리지 않고 가리개는 올라가지 않았기 때문이다."], ["Tupman, he managed to push open the roof; and mounting on the seat, and steadying himself as well as he could, by placing his hand on that gentleman’s shoulder, Mr.", "마침내 그는 터프먼 씨의 도움을 받아 지붕을 밀어 열어젖히는 데 성공했다. 그러고는 좌석 위에 올라서서, 그 신사의 어깨에 한 손을 짚고 되도록 몸을 가눈 채, 픽윅 씨는 군중을 향해 연설하기 시작했다."], ["Pickwick proceeded to address the multitude; to dwell upon the unjustifiable manner in which he had been treated; and to call upon them to take notice that his servant had been first assaulted.", "자신이 당한 부당하기 그지없는 대우를 역설하고, 자신의 하인이 먼저 폭행당했다는 점을 유념해 달라고 그들에게 호소했다."], ["In this order they reached the magistrate’s house; the chairmen trotting, the prisoners following, Mr. Pickwick oratorising, and the crowd shouting.", "이런 대형으로 그들은 치안판사의 집에 다다랐으니, 가마꾼들은 종종걸음을 치고, 죄수들은 뒤따르고, 픽윅 씨는 열변을 토하고, 군중은 함성을 질렀다."]], [["CHAPTER XXV. SHOWING, AMONG A VARIETY OF PLEASANT MATTERS, HOW MAJESTIC AND IMPARTIAL MR. NUPKINS WAS; AND HOW MR. WELLER RETURNED MR. JOB TROTTER’S SHUTTLECOCK AS HEAVILY AS IT CAME--WITH ANOTHER MATTER, WHICH WILL BE FOUND IN ITS PLACE\nViolent was Mr. Weller’s indignation as he was borne along; numerous were the allusions to the personal appearance and demeanour of Mr. Grummer and his companion; and valorous were the defiances to any six of the gentlemen present, in which he vented his dissatisfaction. Mr. Snodgrass and Mr. Winkle listened with gloomy respect to the torrent of eloquence which their leader poured forth from the sedan-chair, and the rapid course of which not all Mr. Tupman’s earnest entreaties to have the lid of the vehicle closed, were able to check for an instant. But Mr. Weller’s anger quickly gave way to curiosity when the procession turned down the identical courtyard in which he had met with the runaway Job Trotter; and curiosity was exchanged for a feeling of the most gleeful astonishment, when the all-important Mr. Grummer, commanding the sedan-bearers to halt, advanced with dignified and portentous steps to the very green gate from which Job Trotter had emerged, and gave a mighty pull at the bell-handle which hung at the side thereof. The ring was answered by a very smart and pretty-faced servant-girl, who, after holding up her hands in astonishment at the rebellious appearance of the prisoners, and the impassioned language of Mr. Pickwick, summoned Mr. Muzzle. Mr. Muzzle opened one half of the carriage gate, to admit the sedan, the captured ones, and the specials; and immediately slammed it in the faces of the mob, who, indignant at being excluded, and anxious to see what followed, relieved their feelings by kicking at the gate and ringing the bell, for an hour or two afterwards. In this amusement they all took part by turns, except three or four fortunate individuals, who, having discovered a grating in the gate, which commanded a view of nothing, stared through it with the indefatigable perseverance with which people will flatten their noses against the front windows of a chemist’s shop, when a drunken man, who has been run over by a dog-cart in the street, is undergoing a surgical inspection in the back-parlour.", "웰러 씨는 끌려가는 내내 격렬하게 분개했다. 그루머 씨와 그 일행의 생김새와 태도에 대한 빈정거림이 줄을 이었고, 여기 모인 신사들 가운데 여섯쯤은 한꺼번에 덤벼도 좋다는 용맹한 도전으로 그는 자신의 불만을 토해 냈다. 스노드그래스 씨와 윈클 씨는, 그들의 지도자가 가마 안에서 쏟아 내는 웅변의 격류에 침울한 경의를 표하며 귀를 기울였는데, 그 거침없는 흐름은 가마 뚜껑을 닫게 해 달라는 터프먼 씨의 간곡한 애원으로도 한순간조차 막을 수 없었다. 그러나 행렬이, 그가 달아나던 잡 트로터와 마주쳤던 바로 그 안마당으로 꺾어 들자, 웰러 씨의 분노는 이내 호기심에 자리를 내주었다. 그리고 그 무엇보다도 중요한 그루머 씨가 가마꾼들에게 멈추라 명한 뒤, 위엄 있고 거창한 걸음걸이로, 잡 트로터가 빠져나왔던 바로 그 초록 대문 앞까지 나아가, 그 옆에 매달린 종 손잡이를 힘껏 잡아당겼을 때, 호기심은 더없이 흥겨운 경악의 감정으로 바뀌었다. 종소리에 응답하여 나온 것은 아주 맵시 있고 예쁜 얼굴의 하녀였는데, 그 여자는 죄인들의 반항적인 행색과 픽윅 씨의 격앙된 언사에 놀라 두 손을 치켜올린 뒤, 머즐 씨를 불렀다. 머즐 씨는 마차 대문의 한쪽을 열어 가마와 붙잡힌 자들과 특별 순경들을 들이고는, 곧장 그것을 군중의 면전에서 쾅 닫아 버렸다. 따돌림을 당해 분개한 데다 그다음에 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 보고 싶어 안달이 난 군중은, 그 뒤로 한두 시간 동안 대문을 발로 차고 종을 울려 대며 분풀이를 했다. 이 놀이에는 모두가 돌아가며 참여했는데, 다만 서너 명의 운 좋은 자들만은 예외였으니, 그들은 대문에서 아무것도 보이지 않는 쪽을 향한 쇠살창을 발견하고는, 길에서 개수레에 치인 술 취한 사내가 어느 약방 뒷방에서 외과 진찰을 받고 있을 때 사람들이 그 약방 앞 유리창에 코를 납작하게 들이대며 보이는 그런 지칠 줄 모르는 끈기로, 그 살창 사이로 빤히 들여다보았다."]], [["At the foot of a flight of steps, leading to the house door, which was guarded on either side by an American aloe in a green tub, the sedan- chair stopped. Mr.", "집 현관문으로 이어지는 계단 발치에서 가마가 멈추었는데, 그 문은 양옆으로 초록 통에 심긴 미국산 알로에가 지키고 있었다."], ["Pickwick and his friends were conducted into the hall, whence, having been previously announced by Muzzle, and ordered in by Mr. Nupkins, they were ushered into the worshipful presence of that public-spirited officer.", "픽윅 씨와 그 친구들은 현관 홀로 안내되었고, 거기서 머즐이 미리 알린 뒤 너프킨스 씨의 지시에 따라, 그 공공심에 불타는 관리의 존엄한 면전으로 인도되었다."]], [["The scene was an impressive one, well calculated to strike terror to the hearts of culprits, and to impress them with an adequate idea of the stern majesty of the law.", "그 광경은 인상적이었으니, 죄인들의 가슴에 공포를 심고 법의 준엄한 위엄에 관한 적절한 관념을 새겨 넣기에 알맞게 꾸며진 것이었다."], ["In front of a big book-case, in a big chair, behind a big table, and before a big volume, sat Mr. Nupkins, looking a full size larger than any one of them, big as they were.", "큼직한 책장 앞, 큼직한 의자에, 큼직한 탁자 뒤, 큼직한 책을 앞에 두고 너프킨스 씨가 앉아 있었는데, 그 모두가 큼직했음에도 그는 그 가운데 어느 것보다도 한 치수쯤 더 커 보였다."], ["The table was adorned with piles of papers; and above the farther end of it, appeared the head and shoulders of Mr. Jinks, who was busily engaged in looking as busy as possible.", "탁자에는 서류 더미가 장식처럼 쌓여 있었고, 그 저편 끝 너머로 징크스 씨의 머리와 어깨가 나타났는데, 그는 될 수 있는 대로 바빠 보이려 애쓰느라 분주했다."], ["The party having all entered, Muzzle carefully closed the door, and placed himself behind his master’s chair to await his orders.", "일행이 모두 들어서자 머즐은 조심스레 문을 닫고, 주인의 의자 뒤에 자리를 잡고 분부를 기다렸다."], ["Mr. Nupkins threw himself back with thrilling solemnity, and scrutinised the faces of his unwilling visitors.", "너프킨스 씨는 오싹할 만큼 엄숙하게 몸을 뒤로 젖히고는, 마지못해 끌려온 방문객들의 얼굴을 찬찬히 뜯어보았다."]], [["‘Now, Grummer, who is that person?’ said Mr.", "\"자, 그루머, 저 사람은 누구냐?\""], ["Nupkins, pointing to Mr.", "너프킨스 씨가 픽윅 씨를 가리키며 말했다."], ["Pickwick, who, as the spokesman of his friends, stood hat in hand, bowing with the utmost politeness and respect.", "픽윅 씨는 친구들의 대변자로서 모자를 손에 들고 더없이 공손하고 정중하게 허리를 굽히고 서 있었다."]], [["‘This here’s Pickvick, your Wash-up,’ said Grummer.", "\"이 사람이 픽윅입니다요, 나리.\" 그루머가 말했다."]], [["‘Come, none o’ that ‘ere, old Strike-a-light,’ interposed Mr.", "\"자, 그런 소리 마쇼, 이 늙은 부싯돌 양반.\""], ["Weller, elbowing himself into the front rank.", "웰러 씨가 팔꿈치로 비집고 맨 앞줄로 나서며 끼어들었다."], ["‘Beg your pardon, sir, but this here officer o’ yourn in the gambooge tops, ‘ull never earn a decent livin’ as a master o’ the ceremonies any vere.", "\"실례합니다, 나리. 그렇지만 등황색 장화를 신은 이 나리의 부하 양반은 어디 가서도 의전 담당으로는 변변한 밥벌이를 못 할 겁니다."], ["This here, sir’ continued Mr.", "자, 이분이 말입니다, 나리.\""], ["Weller, thrusting Grummer aside, and addressing the magistrate with pleasant familiarity, ‘this here is S. Pickvick, Esquire; this here’s Mr.", "웰러 씨는 그루머를 옆으로 밀치고, 유쾌한 허물없음으로 치안판사를 향해 말을 이었다."], ["Tupman; that ‘ere’s Mr. Snodgrass; and farder on, next him on the t’other side, Mr.", "\"이분이 바로 S."], ["Winkle--all wery nice gen’l’m’n, Sir, as you’ll be wery happy to have the acquaintance on; so the sooner you commits these here officers o’ yourn to the tread-mill for a month or two, the sooner we shall begin to be on a pleasant understanding.", "픽윅 님이시고, 이분이 터프먼 씨, 저분이 스노드그래스 씨, 그리고 더 가서 그 반대쪽 옆이 윈클 씨—모두 아주 훌륭한 신사분들이라, 나리께서도 알게 되시면 무척 기쁘실 겁니다. 그러니 나리께서 이 부하 양반들을 한두 달쯤 디딜방아 형틀로 빨리 보내시면 보내실수록, 우리가 유쾌하게 서로를 이해하기 시작하는 것도 그만큼 빨라질 겁니다."], ["Business first, pleasure arterwards, as King Richard the Third said when he stabbed the t’other king in the Tower, afore he smothered the babbies.’", "일이 먼저, 즐거움은 나중이라고, 리처드 3세께서 갓난애들 숨통을 막기 전에 탑에서 다른 임금을 찔러 죽이며 하신 말씀처럼 말입죠.\""]], [["At the conclusion of this address, Mr. Weller brushed his hat with his right elbow, and nodded benignly to Jinks, who had heard him throughout with unspeakable awe.", "이 연설을 끝맺으며 웰러 씨는 오른쪽 팔꿈치로 모자를 쓱 닦고는, 처음부터 끝까지 형언할 수 없는 경외감에 차서 그의 말을 들었던 징크스에게 인자하게 고개를 끄덕여 보였다."]], [["‘Who is this man, Grummer?’ said the magistrate.", "\"이 사람은 누구냐, 그루머?\" 치안판사가 말했다."]], [["‘Wery desp’rate ch’racter, your Wash-up,’ replied Grummer.", "\"아주 위험천만한 자입니다요, 나리.\" 그루머가 대답했다."], ["‘He attempted to rescue the prisoners, and assaulted the officers; so we took him into custody, and brought him here.’", "\"죄인들을 빼내려고 시도하고 관리들을 폭행했습니다요. 그래서 저희가 신병을 확보하여 여기로 데려왔습니다요.\""]], [["‘You did quite right,’ replied the magistrate.", "\"잘했다.\" 치안판사가 대답했다."], ["‘He is evidently a desperate ruffian.’", "\"딱 보아도 위험한 불한당이로군.\""]], [["‘He is my servant, Sir,’ said Mr.", "\"이 사람은 제 하인입니다, 나리.\""], ["Pickwick angrily.", "픽윅 씨가 화가 나서 말했다."]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["he is your servant, is he?’ said Mr. Nupkins.", "이자가 당신 하인이라, 그렇소?\""], ["‘A conspiracy to defeat the ends of justice, and murder its officers.", "너프킨스 씨가 말했다. \"정의의 목적을 좌절시키고 그 관리들을 살해하려는 음모로군."], ["Pickwick’s servant.", "픽윅의 하인이라."], ["Put that down, Mr. Jinks.’", "그것을 적어 두시오, 징크스 씨.\""]], [["Mr. Jinks did so.", "징크스 씨가 그대로 적었다."]], [["‘What’s your name, fellow?’ thundered Mr.", "\"이름이 뭐냐, 이놈아?\""], ["Nupkins.", "너프킨스 씨가 우레같이 호통쳤다."]], [["‘Veller,’ replied Sam.", "\"웰러요.\" 샘이 대답했다."]], [["‘A very good name for the Newgate Calendar,’ said Mr.", "\"뉴게이트 죄수 명부에 딱 어울리는 이름이로군.\""], ["Nupkins.", "너프킨스 씨가 말했다."]], [["This was a joke; so Jinks, Grummer, Dubbley, all the specials, and Muzzle, went into fits of laughter of five minutes’ duration.", "이것은 농담이었다. 그래서 징크스, 그루머, 더블리, 모든 특별 순경들, 그리고 머즐이 오 분 동안이나 폭소를 터뜨렸다."]], [["‘Put down his name, Mr.", "\"이름을 적으시오, 징크스 씨.\""], ["Jinks,’ said the magistrate.", "치안판사가 말했다."]], [["‘Two L’s, old feller,’ said Sam.", "\"L이 두 개요, 영감.\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["Here an unfortunate special laughed again, whereupon the magistrate threatened to commit him instantly.", "여기서 운 나쁜 특별 순경 하나가 또 웃자, 치안판사가 당장 그를 구류시키겠노라 으름장을 놓았다."], ["It is a dangerous thing to laugh at the wrong man, in these cases.", "이런 경우에는 엉뚱한 사람을 보고 웃는 것이 위험한 일이다."]], [["‘Where do you live?’ said the magistrate.", "\"어디에 사느냐?\" 치안판사가 말했다."]], [["‘Vere ever I can,’ replied Sam.", "\"살 수 있는 데면 어디든요.\" 샘이 대답했다."]], [["‘Put down that, Mr.", "\"그것을 적으시오, 징크스 씨.\""], ["Jinks,’ said the magistrate, who was fast rising into a rage.", "점점 분노가 치미는 치안판사가 말했다."]], [["‘Score it under,’ said Sam.", "\"아래에 죽 그어 두쇼.\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["‘He is a vagabond, Mr.", "\"이자는 부랑자요, 징크스 씨.\""], ["Jinks,’ said the magistrate.", "치안판사가 말했다."], ["‘He is a vagabond on his own statement,--is he not, Mr. Jinks?’", "\"제 입으로 한 진술로 보아도 부랑자란 말이오—그렇지 않소, 징크스 씨?\""]], [["‘Certainly, Sir.’", "\"분명히 그렇습니다, 나리.\""]], [["‘Then I’ll commit him--I’ll commit him as such,’ said Mr.", "\"그렇다면 이자를 구류시키겠다—그런 자로서 구류시키겠다.\""], ["Nupkins.", "너프킨스 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘This is a wery impartial country for justice, ‘said Sam.’ There ain’t a magistrate goin’ as don’t commit himself twice as he commits other people.’", "\"여긴 정의에 관한 한 아주 공평한 나라로구먼.\" 샘이 말했다. \"남을 구류시키는 만큼 제 몸도 두 배로 망신시키지 않는 치안판사가 없으니 말이야.\""]], [["At this sally another special laughed, and then tried to look so supernaturally solemn, that the magistrate detected him immediately.", "이 익살에 또 다른 특별 순경이 웃었다가, 무섭도록 엄숙한 표정을 지으려 애썼는데, 그 바람에 치안판사가 곧바로 그를 적발해 냈다."]], [["‘Grummer,’ said Mr.", "\"그루머.\""], ["Nupkins, reddening with passion, ‘how dare you select such an inefficient and disreputable person for a special constable, as that man?", "너프킨스 씨가 분노로 얼굴이 벌게져서 말했다. \"어찌 감히 저런 무능하고 평판 나쁜 자를 특별 순경으로 골라 뽑았느냐?"], ["How dare you do it, Sir?’", "어찌 감히 그런 짓을 했느냐, 이놈아?\""]], [["‘I am very sorry, your Wash-up,’ stammered Grummer.", "\"대단히 죄송합니다요, 나리.\" 그루머가 더듬거렸다."]], [["‘Very sorry!’ said the furious magistrate.", "\"대단히 죄송하다고!\" 격분한 치안판사가 말했다."], ["‘You shall repent of this neglect of duty, Mr.", "\"이 직무 태만을 후회하게 될 것이다, 그루머 씨."], ["Grummer; you shall be made an example of.", "본보기로 삼겠다. 저자의 봉을 빼앗아라."], ["Take that fellow’s staff away.", "저자는 취했다."], ["He’s drunk. You’re drunk, fellow.’", "너 취했구나, 이놈아.\""]], [["‘I am not drunk, your Worship,’ said the man.", "\"저는 취하지 않았습니다요, 나리.\" 그 사내가 말했다."]], [["‘You are drunk,’ returned the magistrate.", "\"너는 취했다.\" 치안판사가 받아쳤다."], ["‘How dare you say you are not drunk, Sir, when I say you are?", "\"내가 취했다는데 어찌 감히 취하지 않았다고 말하느냐, 이놈아?"], ["Doesn’t he smell of spirits, Grummer?’", "저자한테 술 냄새가 나지 않느냐, 그루머?\""]], [["‘Horrid, your Wash-up,’ replied Grummer, who had a vague impression that there was a smell of rum somewhere.", "\"지독하게 납니다요, 나리.\" 어딘가에서 럼주 냄새가 난다는 어렴풋한 인상을 받은 그루머가 대답했다."]], [["‘I knew he did,’ said Mr. Nupkins.", "\"그럴 줄 알았다.\""], ["‘I saw he was drunk when he first came into the room, by his excited eye.", "너프킨스 씨가 말했다. \"저자가 방에 처음 들어왔을 때 흥분한 눈을 보고 취한 줄 알았지."], ["Did you observe his excited eye, Mr. Jinks?’", "저자의 흥분한 눈을 보았소, 징크스 씨?\""]], [["‘Certainly, Sir.’", "\"분명히 보았습니다, 나리.\""]], [["‘I haven’t touched a drop of spirits this morning,’ said the man, who was as sober a fellow as need be.", "\"저는 오늘 아침 술이라곤 한 방울도 입에 안 댔습니다요.\" 더할 나위 없이 말짱하게 정신이 든 그 사내가 말했다."]], [["‘How dare you tell me a falsehood?’ said Mr.", "\"어찌 감히 내게 거짓말을 하느냐?\""], ["Nupkins.", "너프킨스 씨가 말했다."], ["‘Isn’t he drunk at this moment, Mr. Jinks?’", "\"이자가 지금 이 순간 취해 있지 않소, 징크스 씨?\""]], [["‘Certainly, Sir,’ replied Jinks.", "\"분명히 그렇습니다, 나리.\" 징크스가 대답했다."]], [["‘Mr.", "\"징크스 씨.\""], ["Jinks,’ said the magistrate, ‘I shall commit that man for contempt.", "치안판사가 말했다. \"저자를 모욕죄로 구류시키겠소."], ["Make out his committal, Mr. Jinks.’", "구류장을 작성하시오, 징크스 씨.\""]], [["And committed the special would have been, only Jinks, who was the magistrate’s adviser (having had a legal education of three years in a country attorney’s office), whispered the magistrate that he thought it wouldn’t do; so the magistrate made a speech, and said, that in consideration of the special’s family, he would merely reprimand and discharge him.", "그래서 그 특별 순경은 구류될 뻔했으나, 다만 치안판사의 자문역인 징크스가(시골 변호사 사무소에서 삼 년간 법률 교육을 받은 적이 있었다) 그래서는 안 될 것 같다고 치안판사에게 속삭인 덕에 모면했다. 그리하여 치안판사는 일장 연설을 늘어놓으며, 그 특별 순경의 가족을 헤아려 그저 견책하고 방면하겠노라 말했다."], ["Accordingly, the special was abused, vehemently, for a quarter of an hour, and sent about his business; and Grummer, Dubbley, Muzzle, and all the other specials, murmured their admiration of the magnanimity of Mr. Nupkins.", "따라서 그 특별 순경은 십오 분 동안 격렬하게 욕을 먹고는 제 일을 보러 쫓겨났고, 그루머, 더블리, 머즐, 그리고 다른 모든 특별 순경들은 너프킨스 씨의 도량에 감탄하여 웅성거렸다."]], [["‘Now, Mr.", "\"자, 징크스 씨.\""], ["Jinks,’ said the magistrate, ‘swear Grummer.’", "치안판사가 말했다. \"그루머에게 선서를 시키시오.\""]], [["Grummer was sworn directly; but as Grummer wandered, and Mr. Nupkins’s dinner was nearly ready, Mr. Nupkins cut the matter short, by putting leading questions to Grummer, which Grummer answered as nearly in the affirmative as he could. So the examination went off, all very smooth and comfortable, and two assaults were proved against Mr. Weller, and a threat against Mr. Winkle, and a push against Mr. Snodgrass. When all this was done to the magistrate’s satisfaction, the magistrate and Mr. Jinks consulted in whispers.", "그루머는 즉시 선서를 했다. 그러나 그루머가 이야기를 종잡지 못하고 헤매는 데다 너프킨스 씨의 만찬이 거의 준비된 터라, 너프킨스 씨는 그루머에게 유도 질문을 던져 일을 빨리 매듭지었고, 그루머는 할 수 있는 한 긍정에 가깝게 대답했다. 그리하여 심문은 아주 매끄럽고 편안하게 진행되어, 웰러 씨에게는 두 건의 폭행이, 윈클 씨에게는 협박이, 스노드그래스 씨에게는 밀친 행위가 입증되었다. 이 모든 일이 치안판사가 만족할 만큼 마무리되자, 치안판사와 징크스 씨는 속삭이며 의논했다."]], [["The consultation having lasted about ten minutes, Mr.", "그 의논이 십 분쯤 이어진 뒤, 징크스 씨는 탁자 끝 자기 자리로 물러났다."], ["Jinks retired to his end of the table; and the magistrate, with a preparatory cough, drew himself up in his chair, and was proceeding to commence his address, when Mr. Pickwick interposed.", "그리고 치안판사가 예비 삼아 헛기침을 하고 의자에 몸을 곧추세우며 일장 연설을 시작하려는 참에, 픽윅 씨가 끼어들었다."]], [["‘I beg your pardon, sir, for interrupting you,’ said Mr.", "\"말씀을 끊어 죄송합니다, 나리.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick; ‘but before you proceed to express, and act upon, any opinion you may have formed on the statements which have been made here, I must claim my right to be heard so far as I am personally concerned.’", "\"그렇지만 여기서 이루어진 진술들에 대해 나리께서 형성하신 어떤 의견을 표명하고 그에 따라 처분하시기 전에, 저는 제 개인에 관련된 한 발언할 권리가 있음을 주장하지 않을 수 없습니다.\""]], [["‘Hold your tongue, Sir,’ said the magistrate peremptorily.", "\"입 다무시오.\" 치안판사가 단호하게 말했다."]], [["‘I must submit to you, Sir--’ said Mr.", "\"제가 나리께 말씀드려야 할 것은—\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Hold your tongue, sir,’ interposed the magistrate, ‘or I shall order an officer to remove you.’", "\"입 다무시오.\" 치안판사가 끼어들었다. \"안 그러면 관리를 시켜 당신을 끌어내게 하겠소.\""]], [["‘You may order your officers to do whatever you please, Sir,’ said Mr.", "\"나리께서 좋으실 대로 관리들에게 무슨 일이든 명하셔도 좋습니다, 나리.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick; ‘and I have no doubt, from the specimen I have had of the subordination preserved amongst them, that whatever you order, they will execute, Sir; but I shall take the liberty, Sir, of claiming my right to be heard, until I am removed by force.’", "\"그리고 그들 사이에 유지되는 복종의 표본을 제가 이미 보았으니, 나리께서 무엇을 명하시든 그들이 실행하리라는 것은 의심치 않습니다. 그러나 저는 외람되나마, 나리, 힘으로 끌려 나가기 전까지는 발언할 권리를 주장하겠습니다.\""]], [["‘Pickvick and principle!’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"픽윅과 원칙이다!\""], ["Weller, in a very audible voice.", "웰러 씨가 아주 또렷한 목소리로 외쳤다."]], [["‘Sam, be quiet,’ said Mr.", "\"샘, 조용히 해라.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Dumb as a drum vith a hole in it, Sir,’ replied Sam.", "\"구멍 뚫린 북처럼 벙어리가 되겠습니다, 나리.\" 샘이 대답했다."]], [["Mr. Nupkins looked at Mr.", "너프킨스 씨는 픽윅 씨가 이토록 유례없는 대담함을 드러내는 데 강렬한 경악의 눈빛으로 그를 바라보았다."], ["Pickwick with a gaze of intense astonishment, at his displaying such unwonted temerity; and was apparently about to return a very angry reply, when Mr. Jinks pulled him by the sleeve, and whispered something in his ear.", "그리고 막 몹시 화난 대답을 돌려주려는 듯했는데, 그때 징크스 씨가 그의 소매를 잡아당기며 귀에 무언가를 속삭였다."], ["To this, the magistrate returned a half- audible answer, and then the whispering was renewed.", "이에 치안판사는 반쯤 들릴락 말락 한 대답을 했고, 그러자 속삭임이 다시 이어졌다."], ["Jinks was evidently remonstrating.", "징크스가 항변하고 있는 것이 분명했다."]], [["At length the magistrate, gulping down, with a very bad grace, his disinclination to hear anything more, turned to Mr.", "마침내 치안판사는 더 듣고 싶지 않은 마음을 아주 마지못한 태도로 꿀꺽 삼키고는, 픽윅 씨를 향해 날카롭게 말했다."], ["Pickwick, and said sharply, ‘What do you want to say?’", "\"무슨 말이 하고 싶소?\""]], [["‘First,’ said Mr.", "\"먼저,\""], ["Pickwick, sending a look through his spectacles, under which even Nupkins quailed, ‘first, I wish to know what I and my friend have been brought here for?’", "픽윅 씨가 안경 너머로 한 줄기 시선을 보내며 말했는데, 그 앞에서는 너프킨스마저 움찔했다. \"먼저, 저와 제 친구가 무슨 이유로 여기 끌려왔는지 알고 싶습니다.\""]], [["‘Must I tell him?’ whispered the magistrate to Jinks.", "\"이자에게 말해 주어야 하오?\" 치안판사가 징크스에게 속삭였다."]], [["‘I think you had better, sir,’ whispered Jinks to the magistrate.", "\"말씀하시는 편이 나을 듯합니다, 나리.\" 징크스가 치안판사에게 속삭였다."]], [["‘An information has been sworn before me,’ said the magistrate, ‘that it is apprehended you are going to fight a duel, and that the other man, Tupman, is your aider and abettor in it.", "\"내 앞에서 선서된 고발이 있었소.\" 치안판사가 말했다."], ["Therefore--eh, Mr.", "\"당신이 결투를 하려 한다는 혐의가 있고, 다른 사람, 터프먼이 그 일에서 당신의 조력자이자 교사자라는 것이오."], ["Jinks?’", "따라서—응, 징크스 씨?\""]], [["‘Certainly, sir.’", "\"분명히 그렇습니다, 나리.\""]], [["‘Therefore, I call upon you both, to--I think that’s the course, Mr. Jinks?’", "\"따라서 나는 당신네 두 사람에게 명하노니—그게 절차이지요, 징크스 씨?\""]], [["‘Certainly, Sir.’", "\"분명히 그렇습니다, 나리.\""]], [["‘To--to--what, Mr.", "\"명하노니—명하노니—무엇이오, 징크스 씨?\""], ["Jinks?’ said the magistrate pettishly.", "치안판사가 짜증스럽게 말했다."]], [["‘To find bail, sir.’", "\"보증인을 세우라는 것입니다, 나리.\""]], [["‘Yes.", "\"그렇지."], ["Therefore, I call upon you both--as I was about to say when I was interrupted by my clerk--to find bail.’", "따라서 나는 당신네 두 사람에게 명하노니—내 서기가 끼어들기 전에 막 말하려던 대로—보증인을 세우시오.\""]], [["Good bail,’ whispered Mr.", "\"건실한 보증인입니다.\""], ["Jinks.", "징크스 씨가 속삭였다."]], [["‘I shall require good bail,’ said the magistrate.", "\"건실한 보증인을 요구하겠소.\" 치안판사가 말했다."]], [["‘Town’s-people,’ whispered Jinks.", "\"이 고장 사람이어야 합니다.\" 징크스가 속삭였다."]], [["‘They must be townspeople,’ said the magistrate.", "\"이 고장 사람이어야 하오.\" 치안판사가 말했다."]], [["‘Fifty pounds each,’ whispered Jinks, ‘and householders, of course.’", "\"각각 오십 파운드씩입니다.\" 징크스가 속삭였다. \"물론 가구주여야 하고요.\""]], [["‘I shall require two sureties of fifty pounds each,’ said the magistrate aloud, with great dignity, ‘and they must be householders, of course.’", "\"각각 오십 파운드의 보증인 두 사람을 요구하겠소.\" 치안판사가 큰 위엄을 담아 소리 내어 말했다. \"그리고 물론 가구주여야 하오.\""]], [["‘But bless my heart, Sir,’ said Mr. Pickwick, who, together with Mr.", "\"하지만 맙소사, 나리.\""], ["Tupman, was all amazement and indignation; ‘we are perfect strangers in this town.", "터프먼 씨와 더불어 온통 경악과 분개에 휩싸인 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"우리는 이 고장에서 완전히 낯선 사람들입니다."], ["I have as little knowledge of any householders here, as I have intention of fighting a duel with anybody.’", "여기 어느 가구주에 대해서도 아는 바가 없는 것이, 누구와 결투할 생각이 없는 것과 똑같습니다.\""]], [["‘I dare say,’ replied the magistrate, ‘I dare say--don’t you, Mr.", "\"그렇겠지요.\" 치안판사가 대답했다."], ["Jinks?’", "\"그렇겠지요—그렇지 않소, 징크스 씨?\""]], [["‘Certainly, Sir.’", "\"분명히 그렇습니다, 나리.\""]], [["‘Have you anything more to say?’ inquired the magistrate.", "\"더 할 말이 있소?\" 치안판사가 물었다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick had a great deal more to say, which he would no doubt have said, very little to his own advantage, or the magistrate’s satisfaction, if he had not, the moment he ceased speaking, been pulled by the sleeve by Mr.", "픽윅 씨는 할 말이 훨씬 더 많았고, 의심할 바 없이 그 말을 했을 것이며, 그것은 자기 자신에게 거의 득이 되지 않았을뿐더러 치안판사를 만족시키지도 못했을 것이다."], ["Weller, with whom he was immediately engaged in so earnest a conversation, that he suffered the magistrate’s inquiry to pass wholly unnoticed. Mr.", "다만 그가 말을 멈춘 순간 웰러 씨가 그의 소매를 잡아당겼고, 그가 곧장 너무도 진지한 대화에 빠져든 나머지 치안판사의 질문을 전혀 알아채지 못한 채 흘려보냈기에 그렇지 않았다."], ["Nupkins was not the man to ask a question of the kind twice over; and so, with another preparatory cough, he proceeded, amidst the reverential and admiring silence of the constables, to pronounce his decision.", "너프킨스 씨는 그런 종류의 질문을 두 번 되풀이할 사람이 아니었다. 그래서 그는 또 한 번 예비 삼아 헛기침을 하고는, 순경들의 경건하고 감탄에 찬 침묵 가운데서 자신의 판결을 선고하기 시작했다."]], [["He should fine Weller two pounds for the first assault, and three pounds for the second.", "그는 웰러에게 첫 번째 폭행에 대해 이 파운드, 두 번째 폭행에 대해 삼 파운드의 벌금을 부과하겠다고 했다."], ["He should fine Winkle two pounds, and Snodgrass one pound, besides requiring them to enter into their own recognisances to keep the peace towards all his Majesty’s subjects, and especially towards his liege servant, Daniel Grummer.", "윈클에게는 이 파운드, 스노드그래스에게는 일 파운드의 벌금을 부과하고, 아울러 그들에게 폐하의 모든 백성, 특히 그의 충실한 종 대니얼 그루머에 대해 평화를 지키겠다는 서약서를 작성하도록 요구하겠다고 했다."], ["Pickwick and Tupman he had already held to bail.", "픽윅과 터프먼은 이미 보증 처분에 부쳤다."]], [["Immediately on the magistrate ceasing to speak, Mr. Pickwick, with a smile mantling on his again good-humoured countenance, stepped forward, and said--", "치안판사가 말을 마치자마자, 픽윅 씨는 다시 온화해진 얼굴에 미소를 가득 띠고 앞으로 나서서 말했다."]], [["‘I beg the magistrate’s pardon, but may I request a few minutes’ private conversation with him, on a matter of deep importance to himself?’", "\"치안판사님께 송구하오나, 나리 자신에게 대단히 중요한 일에 관하여 잠시 단둘이 이야기를 나눌 수 있도록 청해도 되겠습니까?\""]], [["‘What?’ said the magistrate.", "\"뭐라고?\" 치안판사가 말했다."], ["Mr. Pickwick repeated his request.", "픽윅 씨가 청을 되풀이했다."]], [["‘This is a most extraordinary request,’ said the magistrate.", "\"이것은 참으로 별난 요청이로군.\" 치안판사가 말했다."], ["‘A private interview?’", "\"단둘이 면담하자고?\""]], [["‘A private interview,’ replied Mr.", "\"단둘이 면담하자는 것입니다.\""], ["Pickwick firmly; ‘only, as a part of the information which I wish to communicate is derived from my servant, I should wish him to be present.’", "픽윅 씨가 단호히 대답했다. \"다만 제가 전하려는 정보의 일부가 제 하인에게서 나온 것이라, 그가 함께 있었으면 합니다.\""]], [["The magistrate looked at Mr. Jinks; Mr.", "치안판사는 징크스 씨를 보았고, 징크스 씨는 치안판사를 보았으며, 관리들은 어리둥절하여 서로를 마주 보았다."], ["Jinks looked at the magistrate; the officers looked at each other in amazement.", "너프킨스 씨가 갑자기 창백해졌다. 저 웰러라는 자가 후회의 순간에, 자신을 암살하려는 어떤 은밀한 음모를 폭로하려는 것일까?"], ["Mr. Nupkins turned suddenly pale.", "그것은 무시무시한 생각이었다."], ["Could the man Weller, in a moment of remorse, have divulged some secret conspiracy for his assassination? It was a dreadful thought.", "그는 공인이었다."], ["He was a public man; and he turned paler, as he thought of Julius Caesar and Mr. Perceval.", "그리고 율리우스 카이사르와 퍼시벌 씨를 떠올리며 그는 더욱 창백해졌다."]], [["The magistrate looked at Mr. Pickwick again, and beckoned Mr. Jinks.", "치안판사는 다시 픽윅 씨를 보고는 징크스 씨를 손짓해 불렀다."]], [["‘What do you think of this request, Mr.", "\"이 요청을 어떻게 생각하오, 징크스 씨?\""], ["Jinks?’ murmured Mr. Nupkins.", "너프킨스 씨가 중얼거렸다."]], [["Mr. Jinks, who didn’t exactly know what to think of it, and was afraid he might offend, smiled feebly, after a dubious fashion, and, screwing up the corners of his mouth, shook his head slowly from side to side.", "이를 정확히 어떻게 생각해야 할지 알 수 없었고 행여 비위를 거스를까 두려웠던 징크스 씨는, 어정쩡한 투로 힘없이 미소를 짓고는 입꼬리를 비틀며 천천히 고개를 좌우로 저었다."]], [["‘Mr.", "\"징크스 씨.\""], ["Jinks,’ said the magistrate gravely, ‘you are an ass.’", "치안판사가 근엄하게 말했다. \"당신은 멍청이요.\""]], [["At this little expression of opinion, Mr. Jinks smiled again--rather more feebly than before--and edged himself, by degrees, back into his own corner.", "이 짤막한 견해 표명에 징크스 씨는 다시 미소를 지었는데—아까보다도 한층 더 힘없이—그러고는 조금씩 슬금슬금 제 구석 자리로 물러났다."]], [["Mr. Nupkins debated the matter within himself for a few seconds, and then, rising from his chair, and requesting Mr. Pickwick and Sam to follow him, led the way into a small room which opened into the justice- parlour. Desiring Mr. Pickwick to walk to the upper end of the little apartment, and holding his hand upon the half-closed door, that he might be able to effect an immediate escape, in case there was the least tendency to a display of hostilities, Mr. Nupkins expressed his readiness to hear the communication, whatever it might be.", "너프킨스 씨는 몇 초 동안 속으로 그 문제를 따져 본 뒤, 의자에서 일어나 픽윅 씨와 샘에게 따라오라 청하고는, 재판정 응접실로 통하는 작은 방으로 앞장서 갔다. 픽윅 씨에게 그 작은 방의 안쪽 끝으로 가라 이르고, 조금이라도 적대적인 기색이 드러나는 경우 즉시 도망칠 수 있도록 반쯤 닫힌 문에 손을 얹은 채, 너프킨스 씨는 그 전갈이 무엇이든 들을 준비가 되어 있노라 밝혔다."]], [["‘I will come to the point at once, sir,’ said Mr.", "\"단도직입으로 말씀드리겠습니다, 나리.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick; ‘it affects yourself and your credit materially.", "\"이것은 나리 자신과 나리의 신용에 중대하게 관련된 일입니다."], ["I have every reason to believe, Sir, that you are harbouring in your house a gross impostor!’", "저로서는 나리께서 댁에 어느 지독한 사기꾼을 숨겨 두고 계시다고 믿을 만한 온갖 이유가 있습니다, 나리!\""]], [["‘Two,’ interrupted Sam.", "\"둘입니다.\" 샘이 끼어들었다."], ["‘Mulberry agin all natur, for tears and willainny!’", "\"눈물과 악행으로 치면 천하무적인 멀버리 양반이죠!\""]], [["‘Sam,’ said Mr.", "\"샘.\""], ["Pickwick, ‘if I am to render myself intelligible to this gentleman, I must beg you to control your feelings.’", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"내가 이 신사분께 내 말을 알아듣게 하려면, 네가 감정을 좀 다스려 주어야겠다.\""]], [["‘Wery sorry, Sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"대단히 죄송합니다, 나리.\""], ["Weller; ‘but when I think o’ that ‘ere Job, I can’t help opening the walve a inch or two.’", "웰러 씨가 대답했다. \"하지만 저 잡이라는 놈을 생각하면, 밸브를 한두 치 열지 않고는 못 배기겠습니다.\""]], [["‘In one word, Sir,’ said Mr.", "\"한마디로 말씀드리겠습니다, 나리.\""], ["Pickwick, ‘is my servant right in suspecting that a certain Captain Fitz-Marshall is in the habit of visiting here?", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"어떤 피츠마셜 대위라는 자가 이곳에 드나드는 버릇이 있다고 의심하는 제 하인의 말이 옳습니까?"], ["Because,’ added Mr. Pickwick, as he saw that Mr.", "왜냐하면,\""], ["Nupkins was about to offer a very indignant interruption, ‘because if he be, I know that person to be a--’", "픽윅 씨는 너프킨스 씨가 막 몹시 분개하여 끼어들려는 것을 보고 덧붙였다. \"왜냐하면 그렇다면, 저는 그 사람이—\""]], [["‘Hush, hush,’ said Mr.", "\"쉿, 쉿.\""], ["Nupkins, closing the door.", "너프킨스 씨가 문을 닫으며 말했다."], ["‘Know him to be what, Sir?’", "\"그 사람이 무엇임을 안다는 거요?\""]], [["‘An unprincipled adventurer--a dishonourable character--a man who preys upon society, and makes easily-deceived people his dupes, Sir; his absurd, his foolish, his wretched dupes, Sir,’ said the excited Mr.", "\"파렴치한 협잡꾼—명예라곤 없는 자—사회를 등쳐 먹으며 잘 속아 넘어가는 사람들을 제 봉으로 삼는 자입니다, 나리. 그 어이없고, 어리석고, 가련한 봉들 말입니다, 나리.\""], ["Pickwick.", "흥분한 픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Dear me,’ said Mr.", "\"이런 세상에.\""], ["Nupkins, turning very red, and altering his whole manner directly.", "너프킨스 씨가 얼굴이 새빨개지며 태도를 단번에 바꾸어 말했다."], ["‘Dear me, Mr.--’", "\"이런 세상에, 저—\""]], [["‘Pickvick,’ said Sam.", "\"픽윅입니다.\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["‘Pickwick,’ said the magistrate, ‘dear me, Mr.", "\"픽윅 씨.\" 치안판사가 말했다."], ["Pickwick--pray take a seat--you cannot mean this?", "\"이런 세상에, 픽윅 씨—부디 앉으시오—설마 그럴 리가?"], ["Captain Fitz-Marshall!’", "피츠마셜 대위가!\""]], [["‘Don’t call him a cap’en,’ said Sam, ‘nor Fitz-Marshall neither; he ain’t neither one nor t’other. He’s a strolling actor, he is, and his name’s Jingle; and if ever there was a wolf in a mulberry suit, that ‘ere Job Trotter’s him.’", "\"대위라고 부르지 마쇼.\" 샘이 말했다. \"피츠마셜도 아니고요. 그자는 이것도 저것도 아닙니다. 그자는 떠돌이 배우고, 이름은 징글입니다. 그리고 멀버리 양복을 입은 늑대가 세상에 있었다면, 그 잡 트로터라는 놈이 바로 그놈입니다.\""]], [["‘It is very true, Sir,’ said Mr.", "\"정말입니다, 나리.\""], ["Pickwick, replying to the magistrate’s look of amazement; ‘my only business in this town, is to expose the person of whom we now speak.’", "픽윅 씨가 치안판사의 경악한 표정에 응하여 말했다. \"제가 이 고장에 온 유일한 용무는, 지금 우리가 말하는 그 인물의 정체를 폭로하는 것입니다.\""]], [["Mr. Pickwick proceeded to pour into the horror-stricken ear of Mr. Nupkins, an abridged account of all Mr. Jingle’s atrocities. He related how he had first met him; how he had eloped with Miss Wardle; how he had cheerfully resigned the lady for a pecuniary consideration; how he had entrapped himself into a lady’s boarding-school at midnight; and how he (Mr. Pickwick) now felt it his duty to expose his assumption of his present name and rank.", "픽윅 씨는 너프킨스 씨의 경악한 귀에다 징글 씨의 온갖 악행을 추려 들려주기 시작했다. 처음에 그를 어떻게 만났는지, 그가 워들 양과 어떻게 사랑의 도피를 했는지, 금전적 대가를 받고 어떻게 흔쾌히 그 숙녀를 포기했는지, 한밤중에 어느 숙녀 기숙학교로 어떻게 자신을 끌어들였는지, 그리고 그(픽윅 씨)가 이제 그자가 지금의 이름과 신분을 사칭하는 것을 폭로하는 일을 자신의 의무로 여긴다는 것을 그는 이야기했다."]], [["As the narrative proceeded, all the warm blood in the body of Mr. Nupkins tingled up into the very tips of his ears.", "이야기가 진행됨에 따라, 너프킨스 씨의 몸속에 흐르는 뜨거운 피가 죄다 귓불 끝까지 화끈하게 솟아올랐다."], ["He had picked up the captain at a neighbouring race-course. Charmed with his long list of aristocratic acquaintance, his extensive travel, and his fashionable demeanour, Mrs.", "그는 이웃 경마장에서 그 대위를 주워 온 터였다."], ["Nupkins and Miss Nupkins had exhibited Captain Fitz- Marshall, and quoted Captain Fitz-Marshall, and hurled Captain Fitz- Marshall at the devoted heads of their select circle of acquaintance, until their bosom friends, Mrs. Porkenham and the Misses Porkenhams, and Mr.", "그의 장황한 귀족 지인 명단과 광범위한 여행 경력, 세련된 태도에 매료되어, 너프킨스 부인과 너프킨스 양은 피츠마셜 대위를 전시하고 다녔고, 피츠마셜 대위를 입에 올렸으며, 자기네가 엄선한 지인들의 충실한 머리 위로 피츠마셜 대위를 내던지고 다녀, 결국 그들의 절친한 벗인 포컨엄 부인과 포컨엄 자매, 그리고 시드니 포컨엄 씨가 질투와 절망으로 터질 지경에 이르렀다."], ["Sidney Porkenham, were ready to burst with jealousy and despair. And now, to hear, after all, that he was a needy adventurer, a strolling player, and if not a swindler, something so very like it, that it was hard to tell the difference!", "그런데 이제 와서, 결국 그자가 빈털터리 협잡꾼이요, 떠돌이 배우요, 사기꾼이 아니라 해도 그와 너무도 비슷해서 차이를 가려내기 어려운 자라는 말을 듣다니!"], ["Heavens!", "맙소사!"], ["what would the Porkenhams say!", "포컨엄 일가가 뭐라고 할까!"], ["What would be the triumph of Mr. Sidney Porkenham when he found that his addresses had been slighted for such a rival!", "자기 청혼이 그런 연적 때문에 외면당했음을 알게 된다면 시드니 포컨엄 씨가 얼마나 의기양양해할까!"], ["How should he, Nupkins, meet the eye of old Porkenham at the next quarter-sessions!", "그, 너프킨스가 다음 분기 순회재판에서 늙은 포컨엄의 눈을 어떻게 마주한단 말인가!"], ["And what a handle would it be for the opposition magisterial party if the story got abroad!", "그리고 이 이야기가 새어 나가면 반대편 치안판사 일파에게 얼마나 좋은 빌미가 될 것인가!"]], [["‘But after all,’ said Mr.", "\"하지만 결국에는,\""], ["Nupkins, brightening for a moment, after a long pause; ‘after all, this is a mere statement.", "한참 말이 없다가 잠시 얼굴이 환해지며 너프킨스 씨가 말했다. \"결국에는, 이것은 한낱 진술에 불과하지 않소."], ["Captain Fitz-Marshall is a man of very engaging manners, and, I dare say, has many enemies.", "피츠마셜 대위는 사람을 끄는 매력이 아주 많은 사람이고, 짐작컨대 적도 많을 게요."], ["What proof have you of the truth of these representations?’", "이 주장들이 사실이라는 무슨 증거가 있소?\""]], [["‘Confront me with him,’ said Mr.", "\"저를 그자와 대면시키십시오.\""], ["Pickwick, ‘that is all I ask, and all I require.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"그것이 제가 청하는 전부이자 요구하는 전부입니다."], ["Confront him with me and my friends here; you will want no further proof.’", "그자를 저와 여기 제 친구들과 대면시키십시오. 그러면 더 이상의 증거는 필요 없을 것입니다.\""]], [["‘Why,’ said Mr.", "\"음,\""], ["Nupkins, ‘that might be very easily done, for he will be here to-night, and then there would be no occasion to make the matter public, just--just--for the young man’s own sake, you know.", "너프킨스 씨가 말했다. \"그거야 아주 쉽게 할 수 있겠소."], ["I--I--should like to consult Mrs.", "그자가 오늘 밤 여기 올 테니 말이오."], ["Nupkins on the propriety of the step, in the first instance, though. At all events, Mr.", "그렇게 하면 이 일을 굳이 세상에 알릴 필요가 없겠지, 그저—그저—그 젊은이 자신을 위해서라도 말이오, 아시겠소."], ["Pickwick, we must despatch this legal business before we can do anything else.", "다만 나는—나는—먼저 그 조치가 타당한지 너프킨스 부인과 상의하고 싶소. 어쨌든, 픽윅 씨, 다른 무엇을 하기에 앞서 이 법적인 일부터 처리해야 하오."], ["Pray step back into the next room.’", "부디 옆방으로 다시 들어가 주시오.\""]], [["Into the next room they went.", "그들은 옆방으로 들어갔다."]], [["‘Grummer,’ said the magistrate, in an awful voice.", "\"그루머.\" 치안판사가 무시무시한 목소리로 말했다."]], [["‘Your Wash-up,’ replied Grummer, with the smile of a favourite.", "\"예, 나리.\" 그루머가 총애받는 자의 미소를 띠며 대답했다."]], [["‘Come, come, Sir,’ said the magistrate sternly, ‘don’t let me see any of this levity here.", "\"이봐, 이봐.\" 치안판사가 준엄하게 말했다."], ["It is very unbecoming, and I can assure you that you have very little to smile at.", "\"여기서 그런 경박한 짓일랑 보이지 말게. 그건 아주 꼴사납고, 단언컨대 자네가 웃을 일이라곤 거의 없네."], ["Was the account you gave me just now strictly true?", "자네가 방금 내게 해 준 진술이 한 치도 틀림없이 사실이었나?"], ["Now be careful, sir!’", "자, 조심하게, 이 사람아!\""]], [["Your Wash-up,’ stammered Grummer, ‘I-’", "\"나리.\" 그루머가 더듬거렸다. \"저는—\""]], [["‘Oh, you are confused, are you?’ said the magistrate.", "\"오, 자네 당황하고 있군, 그렇지?\" 치안판사가 말했다."], ["‘Mr. Jinks, you observe this confusion?’", "\"징크스 씨, 이 당황한 모습이 보이오?\""]], [["‘Certainly, Sir,’ replied Jinks.", "\"분명히 보입니다, 나리.\" 징크스가 대답했다."]], [["‘Now,’ said the magistrate, ‘repeat your statement, Grummer, and again I warn you to be careful.", "\"자.\" 치안판사가 말했다."], ["Mr.", "\"진술을 되풀이해 보게, 그루머."], ["Jinks, take his words down.’", "다시 한번 조심하라고 경고하네. 징크스 씨, 그자의 말을 받아 적으시오.\""]], [["The unfortunate Grummer proceeded to re-state his complaint, but, what between Mr. Jinks’s taking down his words, and the magistrate’s taking them up, his natural tendency to rambling, and his extreme confusion, he managed to get involved, in something under three minutes, in such a mass of entanglement and contradiction, that Mr. Nupkins at once declared he didn’t believe him. So the fines were remitted, and Mr. Jinks found a couple of bail in no time. And all these solemn proceedings having been satisfactorily concluded, Mr. Grummer was ignominiously ordered out--an awful instance of the instability of human greatness, and the uncertain tenure of great men’s favour.", "불운한 그루머는 자기 고발을 다시 진술하기 시작했으나, 징크스 씨가 그의 말을 받아 적는 데다 치안판사가 그것을 꼬집어 따지고, 게다가 천성적으로 횡설수설하는 버릇과 극도의 당황이 겹쳐, 그는 채 삼 분도 안 되어 그토록 엄청난 뒤엉킴과 모순의 덩어리에 휘말린 나머지, 너프킨스 씨는 당장 그를 믿지 못하겠노라 선언해 버렸다. 그리하여 벌금은 면제되었고, 징크스 씨는 순식간에 보증인 두 사람을 구해 냈다. 그리고 이 모든 엄숙한 절차가 흡족하게 마무리되자, 그루머 씨는 치욕스럽게 밖으로 쫓겨났으니—인간의 위대함이 얼마나 덧없는지, 그리고 위대한 자들의 총애가 얼마나 불안정한 것인지를 보여 주는 무서운 본보기였다."]], [["Mrs. Nupkins was a majestic female in a pink gauze turban and a light brown wig. Miss Nupkins possessed all her mamma’s haughtiness without the turban, and all her ill-nature without the wig; and whenever the exercise of these two amiable qualities involved mother and daughter in some unpleasant dilemma, as they not infrequently did, they both concurred in laying the blame on the shoulders of Mr. Nupkins. Accordingly, when Mr. Nupkins sought Mrs. Nupkins, and detailed the communication which had been made by Mr. Pickwick, Mrs. Nupkins suddenly recollected that she had always expected something of the kind; that she had always said it would be so; that her advice was never taken; that she really did not know what Mr. Nupkins supposed she was; and so forth.", "너프킨스 부인은 분홍 사 터번을 쓰고 연갈색 가발을 얹은 위엄 있는 여인이었다. 너프킨스 양은 터번을 빼고 어머니의 도도함을 죄다 물려받았고, 가발을 빼고 어머니의 심술을 죄다 물려받았다. 그리고 이 두 가지 사랑스러운 자질을 발휘하다가 모녀가 어떤 불쾌한 곤경에 빠질 때면—적잖이 그런 일이 있었다—그들은 한결같이 너프킨스 씨의 어깨에 그 책임을 지우는 데 의기투합했다. 따라서 너프킨스 씨가 너프킨스 부인을 찾아가 픽윅 씨가 전한 이야기를 소상히 늘어놓자, 너프킨스 부인은 자기가 늘 그런 일이 있으리라 예상했었다는 것, 늘 그렇게 될 거라고 말했었다는 것, 자기 충고는 결코 받아들여지지 않는다는 것, 너프킨스 씨가 도대체 자기를 무엇으로 여기는지 정말 모르겠다는 것 따위를 문득 떠올렸다."]], [["‘The idea!’ said Miss Nupkins, forcing a tear of very scanty proportions into the corner of each eye; ‘the idea of my being made such a fool of!’", "\"기가 막혀!\" 너프킨스 양이 양쪽 눈꼬리에 아주 인색한 분량의 눈물을 억지로 짜내며 말했다. \"내가 이런 바보 취급을 당하다니, 기가 막혀!\""]], [["‘Ah!", "\"아!"], ["you may thank your papa, my dear,’ said Mrs.", "그건 네 아빠한테 고마워해야지, 얘야.\" 너프킨스 부인이 말했다."], ["Nupkins; ‘how I have implored and begged that man to inquire into the captain’s family connections; how I have urged and entreated him to take some decisive step!", "\"내가 저 양반더러 대위의 가문 내력을 알아보라고 얼마나 사정하고 애원했는지. 무슨 결단을 내리라고 얼마나 다그치고 간청했는지!"], ["I am quite certain nobody would believe it--quite.’", "아무도 안 믿을 거야, 정말—정말로.\""]], [["‘But, my dear,’ said Mr.", "\"하지만, 여보.\""], ["Nupkins.", "너프킨스 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Don’t talk to me, you aggravating thing, don’t!’ said Mrs.", "\"내게 말 걸지 말아요, 이 속 터지게 하는 양반아, 말 걸지 마요!\""], ["Nupkins.", "너프킨스 부인이 말했다."]], [["‘My love,’ said Mr.", "\"여보.\""], ["Nupkins, ‘you professed yourself very fond of Captain Fitz-Marshall.", "너프킨스 씨가 말했다. \"당신이 피츠마셜 대위를 무척 좋아한다고 공언했잖소."], ["You have constantly asked him here, my dear, and you have lost no opportunity of introducing him elsewhere.’", "당신이 줄곧 그자를 여기로 초대했고, 다른 데서도 그자를 소개할 기회를 한 번도 놓치지 않았잖소, 여보.\""]], [["‘Didn’t I say so, Henrietta?’ cried Mrs.", "\"내가 그렇게 말했지, 헨리에타?\""], ["Nupkins, appealing to her daughter with the air of a much-injured female.", "너프킨스 부인이 몹시 억울한 여인의 태도로 딸에게 호소하며 외쳤다."], ["‘Didn’t I say that your papa would turn round and lay all this at my door?", "\"네 아빠가 돌변해서 이 모든 걸 내 탓으로 돌릴 거라고 내가 말했지? 내가 그렇게 말했지?\""], ["Didn’t I say so?’ Here Mrs. Nupkins sobbed.", "여기서 너프킨스 부인은 흐느꼈다."]], [["‘Oh, pa!’ remonstrated Miss Nupkins.", "\"오, 아빠!\" 너프킨스 양이 항변했다."], ["And here she sobbed too.", "그리고 여기서 그녀도 흐느꼈다."]], [["‘Isn’t it too much, when he has brought all this disgrace and ridicule upon us, to taunt me with being the cause of it?’ exclaimed Mrs.", "\"이 모든 망신과 웃음거리를 우리에게 안겨 놓고도, 그게 내 탓이라고 빈정거리다니, 너무하지 않니?\""], ["Nupkins.", "너프킨스 부인이 외쳤다."]], [["‘How can we ever show ourselves in society!’ said Miss Nupkins.", "\"우리가 어떻게 사교계에 얼굴을 내밀어요!\" 너프킨스 양이 말했다."]], [["‘How can we face the Porkenhams?’ cried Mrs.", "\"포컨엄 일가를 어떻게 봐!\""], ["Nupkins.", "너프킨스 부인이 외쳤다."]], [["‘Or the Griggs!’ cried Miss Nupkins.", "\"아니면 그릭스 일가는요!\" 너프킨스 양이 외쳤다."]], [["‘Or the Slummintowkens!’ cried Mrs. Nupkins.", "\"아니면 슬러민토우큰 일가는!\""], ["‘But what does your papa care!", "너프킨스 부인이 외쳤다."], ["What is it to him!’ At this dreadful reflection, Mrs.", "\"하지만 네 아빠가 무슨 상관이람! 저 양반한테 그게 무슨 대수겠어!\""], ["Nupkins wept mental anguish, and Miss Nupkins followed on the same side.", "이 끔찍한 생각에 너프킨스 부인은 마음의 고통으로 울었고, 너프킨스 양도 같은 편에서 뒤따라 울었다."]], [["Mrs. Nupkins’s tears continued to gush forth, with great velocity, until she had gained a little time to think the matter over; when she decided, in her own mind, that the best thing to do would be to ask Mr.", "너프킨스 부인의 눈물은 그 문제를 곰곰이 생각할 짬을 좀 벌 때까지 엄청난 기세로 계속 솟구쳤다."], ["Pickwick and his friends to remain until the captain’s arrival, and then to give Mr. Pickwick the opportunity he sought.", "그러다 그녀는 속으로 결론을 내렸으니, 가장 좋은 방도는 픽윅 씨와 그 친구들에게 대위가 도착할 때까지 머물러 달라고 청한 뒤, 픽윅 씨에게 그가 바라는 기회를 주는 것이라고."], ["If it appeared that he had spoken truly, the captain could be turned out of the house without noising the matter abroad, and they could easily account to the Porkenhams for his disappearance, by saying that he had been appointed, through the Court influence of his family, to the governor-generalship of Sierra Leone, of Saugur Point, or any other of those salubrious climates which enchant Europeans so much, that when they once get there, they can hardly ever prevail upon themselves to come back again.", "만일 그가 사실을 말한 것으로 드러나면, 일을 세상에 떠벌리지 않고도 대위를 집에서 내쫓을 수 있고, 그자가 가문의 궁정 연줄을 통해 시에라리온이나 사우거 곶이나, 혹은 유럽인들을 어찌나 홀리는지 한번 발을 들이면 좀처럼 돌아올 마음을 못 내게 만드는 그런 다른 어느 쾌적한 풍토의 총독으로 임명되었다고 말함으로써, 그가 사라진 것을 포컨엄 일가에게 쉽게 둘러댈 수 있을 터였다."]], [["When Mrs. Nupkins dried up her tears, Miss Nupkins dried up hers, and Mr. Nupkins was very glad to settle the matter as Mrs. Nupkins had proposed. So Mr. Pickwick and his friends, having washed off all marks of their late encounter, were introduced to the ladies, and soon afterwards to their dinner; and Mr. Weller, whom the magistrate, with his peculiar sagacity, had discovered in half an hour to be one of the finest fellows alive, was consigned to the care and guardianship of Mr. Muzzle, who was specially enjoined to take him below, and make much of him.", "너프킨스 부인이 눈물을 거두자 너프킨스 양도 눈물을 거두었고, 너프킨스 씨는 너프킨스 부인이 제안한 대로 일을 매듭짓게 되어 무척 기뻤다. 그리하여 픽윅 씨와 그 친구들은 조금 전 다툼의 흔적을 죄다 씻어 낸 뒤 부인들에게 소개되었고, 이내 만찬 자리로 안내되었다. 그리고 치안판사가 특유의 통찰력으로 반 시간 만에 세상에 둘도 없는 멋진 사내라고 알아본 웰러 씨는, 머즐 씨의 보살핌과 보호에 맡겨졌으니, 머즐 씨는 특별히 그를 아래층으로 데려가 융숭히 대접하라는 분부를 받았다."]], [["‘How de do, sir?’ said Mr.", "\"안녕하시오, 선생?\""], ["Muzzle, as he conducted Mr. Weller down the kitchen stairs.", "머즐 씨가 웰러 씨를 부엌 계단 아래로 안내하며 말했다."]], [["‘Why, no considerable change has taken place in the state of my system, since I see you cocked up behind your governor’s chair in the parlour, a little vile ago,’ replied Sam.", "\"뭐, 조금 전에 응접실에서 당신네 영감 의자 뒤에 떡하니 버티고 있던 걸 본 뒤로 내 몸 상태에 별다른 변화는 없소이다.\" 샘이 대답했다."]], [["‘You will excuse my not taking more notice of you then,’ said Mr.", "\"그때 당신을 더 살갑게 못 챙긴 건 양해해 주시오.\" 머즐 씨가 말했다."], ["Muzzle. ‘You see, master hadn’t introduced us, then.", "\"보다시피 그땐 주인 어른이 우릴 소개해 주지 않았으니 말이오."], ["Lord, how fond he is of you, Mr. Weller, to be sure!’", "아이고, 주인 어른이 당신을 어찌나 좋아하시는지, 웰러 씨, 정말이지!\""]], [["‘Ah!’ said Sam, ‘what a pleasant chap he is!’", "\"아!\" 샘이 말했다. \"참 유쾌한 양반이지!\""]], [["‘Ain’t he?’ replied Mr.", "\"안 그렇소?\""], ["Muzzle.", "머즐 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘So much humour,’ said Sam.", "\"익살도 어찌나 풍부한지.\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["‘And such a man to speak,’ said Mr.", "\"게다가 말솜씨는 또 어떻고.\" 머즐 씨가 말했다."], ["Muzzle. ‘How his ideas flow, don’t they?’", "\"생각이 어찌나 술술 흘러나오는지, 안 그렇소?\""]], [["‘Wonderful,’ replied Sam; ‘they comes a-pouring out, knocking each other’s heads so fast, that they seems to stun one another; you hardly know what he’s arter, do you?’", "\"기가 막히지.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"콸콸 쏟아져 나오면서 서로 머리를 어찌나 빨리 부딪치는지, 저희끼리 기절을 시키는 것 같다니까. 당최 무슨 말을 하려는 건지 알 수가 없지, 안 그렇소?\""]], [["That’s the great merit of his style of speaking,’ rejoined Mr.", "\"그게 바로 그분 말씨의 큰 장점이지.\" 머즐 씨가 받았다."], ["Muzzle. ‘Take care of the last step, Mr.", "\"마지막 계단을 조심하시오, 웰러 씨."], ["Weller. Would you like to wash your hands, sir, before we join the ladies?", "부인들과 합석하기 전에 손이나 좀 씻으시겠소, 선생?"], ["Here’s a sink, with the water laid on, Sir, and a clean jack towel behind the door.’", "여기 물 받아 둔 개수통이 있고, 문 뒤에 깨끗한 수건이 걸려 있소이다.\""]], [["‘Ah!", "\"아!"], ["perhaps I may as well have a rinse,’ replied Mr.", "한번 헹구는 것도 좋겠구먼.\""], ["Weller, applying plenty of yellow soap to the towel, and rubbing away till his face shone again.", "웰러 씨가 노란 비누를 수건에 듬뿍 묻혀 얼굴이 다시 번들번들 빛날 때까지 문질러 대며 대답했다."], ["‘How many ladies are there?’", "\"부인이 몇이나 되오?\""]], [["‘Only two in our kitchen,’ said Mr.", "\"우리 부엌엔 둘뿐이오.\" 머즐 씨가 말했다."], ["Muzzle; ‘cook and ‘ouse-maid.", "\"요리사와 가정부 말이오."], ["We keep a boy to do the dirty work, and a gal besides, but they dine in the wash’us.’", "궂은일 할 사내아이 하나와 계집아이도 하나 더 두긴 했지만, 그것들은 빨래방에서 밥을 먹소.\""]], [["‘Oh, they dines in the wash’us, do they?’ said Mr.", "\"오, 그것들은 빨래방에서 먹는다, 그렇소?\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Yes,’ replied Mr.", "\"그렇소.\""], ["Muzzle, ‘we tried ‘em at our table when they first come, but we couldn’t keep ‘em.", "머즐 씨가 대답했다. \"처음 왔을 때는 우리 식탁에서 먹여 봤는데, 도무지 두고 볼 수가 없더이다."], ["The gal’s manners is dreadful vulgar; and the boy breathes so very hard while he’s eating, that we found it impossible to sit at table with him.’", "계집아이는 행실이 끔찍하게 천하고, 사내아이는 먹는 동안 숨을 어찌나 거칠게 몰아쉬는지, 같은 식탁에 앉아 있기가 도저히 불가능하더란 말이오.\""]], [["‘Young grampus!’ said Mr.", "\"새끼 고래 같은 놈이로구먼!\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Oh, dreadful,’ rejoined Mr.", "\"오, 끔찍하지.\" 머즐 씨가 받았다."], ["Muzzle; ‘but that is the worst of country service, Mr.", "\"하지만 그게 시골 하인살이의 가장 못된 점이라오, 웰러 씨."], ["Weller; the juniors is always so very savage.", "어린것들은 늘 그렇게 야만스럽거든."], ["This way, sir, if you please, this way.’", "이쪽이오, 선생, 괜찮다면 이쪽으로.\""]], [["Preceding Mr. Weller, with the utmost politeness, Mr. Muzzle conducted him into the kitchen.", "머즐 씨는 더없이 정중하게 웰러 씨를 앞장세워 부엌으로 안내했다."]], [["‘Mary,’ said Mr.", "\"메리.\""], ["Muzzle to the pretty servant-girl, ‘this is Mr.", "머즐 씨가 그 예쁜 하녀에게 말했다."], ["Weller; a gentleman as master has sent down, to be made as comfortable as possible.’", "\"이분은 웰러 씨인데, 주인 어른이 될 수 있는 대로 편히 모시라고 아래로 보내신 분이오.\""]], [["‘And your master’s a knowin’ hand, and has just sent me to the right place,’ said Mr.", "\"그리고 당신네 주인 어른은 사정에 밝은 양반이라, 나를 딱 옳은 곳으로 보내셨군요.\""], ["Weller, with a glance of admiration at Mary.", "웰러 씨가 메리에게 감탄의 눈길을 던지며 말했다."], ["‘If I wos master o’ this here house, I should alvays find the materials for comfort vere Mary wos.’", "\"내가 만약 이 집 주인이라면, 편안함의 재료는 늘 메리 양이 있는 곳에서 찾을 거요.\""]], [["Lor, Mr.", "\"어머, 웰러 씨도!\""], ["Weller!’ said Mary blushing.", "메리가 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다."]], [["‘Well, I never!’ ejaculated the cook.", "\"아유, 별꼴이야!\" 요리사가 소리쳤다."]], [["‘Bless me, cook, I forgot you,’ said Mr.", "\"이런, 요리사 양반, 깜박했군.\""], ["Muzzle. ‘Mr.", "머즐 씨가 말했다."], ["Weller, let me introduce you.’", "\"웰러 씨, 소개해 드리리다.\""]], [["‘How are you, ma’am?’ said Mr.", "\"안녕하시오, 부인?\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 말했다."], ["‘Wery glad to see you, indeed, and hope our acquaintance may be a long ‘un, as the gen’l’m’n said to the fi’ pun’ note.’", "\"참으로 반갑소이다. 그리고 우리 사귐이 오래가길 바라오, 5파운드짜리 지폐한테 그 신사가 말했듯이 말이지.\""]], [["When this ceremony of introduction had been gone through, the cook and Mary retired into the back kitchen to titter, for ten minutes; then returning, all giggles and blushes, they sat down to dinner.", "이 소개 의식이 다 끝나자, 요리사와 메리는 뒷부엌으로 물러가 십 분 동안 킥킥거리다가, 온통 깔깔거리며 얼굴을 붉힌 채 돌아와 만찬에 둘러앉았다."]], [["Mr. Weller’s easy manners and conversational powers had such irresistible influence with his new friends, that before the dinner was half over, they were on a footing of perfect intimacy, and in possession of a full account of the delinquency of Job Trotter.", "웰러 씨의 편안한 태도와 입담은 새로 사귄 벗들에게 어찌나 거부할 수 없는 영향을 미쳤던지, 만찬이 반도 채 끝나기 전에 그들은 완전히 허물없는 사이가 되었고, 잡 트로터의 비행에 관한 자초지종을 죄다 알게 되었다."]], [["‘I never could a-bear that Job,’ said Mary.", "\"난 그 잡이라는 작자를 도무지 못 참겠더라고요.\" 메리가 말했다."]], [["‘No more you never ought to, my dear,’ replied Mr.", "\"그야 절대 참아선 안 되지, 아가씨.\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘Why not?’ inquired Mary.", "\"왜요?\" 메리가 물었다."]], [["‘’Cos ugliness and svindlin’ never ought to be formiliar with elegance and wirtew,’ replied Mr.", "\"왜냐면 추함과 사기 행각이 우아함과 미덕과 친해서는 절대 안 되니까.\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 대답했다."], ["‘Ought they, Mr. Muzzle?’", "\"안 그렇소, 머즐 씨?\""]], [["‘Not by no means,’ replied that gentleman.", "\"결코 안 되지.\" 그 신사가 대답했다."]], [["Here Mary laughed, and said the cook had made her; and the cook laughed, and said she hadn’t.", "여기서 메리가 웃으며 요리사 때문에 웃었다고 말하자, 요리사는 웃으며 자기는 아니라고 했다."]], [["‘I ha’n’t got a glass,’ said Mary.", "\"전 잔이 없어요.\" 메리가 말했다."]], [["‘Drink with me, my dear,’ said Mr. Weller.", "\"나하고 같이 마십시다, 아가씨.\""], ["‘Put your lips to this here tumbler, and then I can kiss you by deputy.’", "웰러 씨가 말했다. \"이 잔에 입술을 대요, 그럼 내가 대리로 키스할 수 있으니.\""]], [["‘For shame, Mr.", "\"창피하게, 웰러 씨도!\""], ["Weller!’ said Mary.", "메리가 말했다."]], [["‘What’s a shame, my dear?’", "\"뭐가 창피하단 거요, 아가씨?\""]], [["‘Talkin’ in that way.’", "\"그런 식으로 말하는 거요.\""]], [["‘Nonsense; it ain’t no harm.", "\"실없는 소리. 해로울 거 하나 없소."], ["It’s natur; ain’t it, cook?’", "자연스러운 거지, 안 그렇소, 요리사 양반?\""]], [["‘Don’t ask me, imperence,’ replied the cook, in a high state of delight; and hereupon the cook and Mary laughed again, till what between the beer, and the cold meat, and the laughter combined, the latter young lady was brought to the verge of choking--an alarming crisis from which she was only recovered by sundry pats on the back, and other necessary attentions, most delicately administered by Mr.", "\"내게 묻지 말아요, 뻔뻔한 양반.\" 요리사가 한껏 기뻐하며 대답했다."], ["Samuel Weller.", "그러자 요리사와 메리가 또 웃어 댔는데, 맥주에다 차가운 고기에다 웃음까지 겹쳐, 메리 양은 그만 숨이 막힐 지경에 이르렀다—샘 웰러 씨가 더없이 섬세하게 등을 몇 차례 토닥이고 그 밖의 필요한 시중을 든 덕에 가까스로 회복된, 아찔한 위기였다."]], [["In the midst of all this jollity and conviviality, a loud ring was heard at the garden gate, to which the young gentleman who took his meals in the wash-house, immediately responded. Mr. Weller was in the height of his attentions to the pretty house-maid; Mr. Muzzle was busy doing the honours of the table; and the cook had just paused to laugh, in the very act of raising a huge morsel to her lips; when the kitchen door opened, and in walked Mr. Job Trotter.", "이 모든 흥겨움과 화기애애함 한가운데서, 정원 문에서 요란한 종소리가 들렸고, 빨래방에서 끼니를 때우는 그 젊은 양반이 즉시 응답하러 갔다. 웰러 씨는 예쁜 가정부에게 한창 정성을 쏟고 있었고, 머즐 씨는 식탁에서 주인 노릇 하기에 바빴으며, 요리사는 마침 커다란 음식 조각을 입으로 가져가던 바로 그 순간에 웃느라 멈춰 있었다—바로 그때 부엌문이 열리고 잡 트로터 씨가 걸어 들어왔다."]], [["We have said in walked Mr. Job Trotter, but the statement is not distinguished by our usual scrupulous adherence to fact.", "잡 트로터 씨가 걸어 들어왔다고 우리는 말했으나, 이 진술은 우리가 늘 사실에 꼼꼼히 충실한 것과는 사뭇 다르다."], ["The door opened and Mr. Trotter appeared.", "문이 열리고 트로터 씨가 나타났다."], ["He would have walked in, and was in the very act of doing so, indeed, when catching sight of Mr. Weller, he involuntarily shrank back a pace or two, and stood gazing on the unexpected scene before him, perfectly motionless with amazement and terror.", "그는 걸어 들어오려 했고, 실로 막 그렇게 하려던 참이었으나, 웰러 씨를 발견하는 순간 자기도 모르게 한두 걸음 움찔 물러서더니, 경악과 공포로 완전히 꼼짝 못 한 채, 눈앞에 펼쳐진 뜻밖의 광경을 응시하며 서 있었다."]], [["‘Here he is!’ said Sam, rising with great glee.", "\"여기 왔네!\" 샘이 더없이 신이 나서 일어나며 말했다."], ["‘Why we were that wery moment a-speaking o’ you.", "\"아니, 우리가 바로 그 순간에 자네 얘길 하고 있었거든."], ["How are you?", "잘 지냈나?"], ["Where have you been?", "어디 갔다 왔어?"], ["Come in.’", "어서 들어와.\""]], [["Laying his hand on the mulberry collar of the unresisting Job, Mr.", "웰러 씨는 저항 없는 잡의 멀버리 옷깃에 손을 얹고 그를 부엌 안으로 끌고 들어왔다."], ["Weller dragged him into the kitchen; and, locking the door, handed the key to Mr. Muzzle, who very coolly buttoned it up in a side pocket.", "그러고는 문을 잠그고 열쇠를 머즐 씨에게 건네자, 머즐 씨는 아주 태연하게 그것을 옆 호주머니에 넣고 단추를 채웠다."]], [["‘Well, here’s a game!’ cried Sam.", "\"자, 이거 한판 벌어졌구먼!\""], ["‘Only think o’ my master havin’ the pleasure o’ meeting yourn upstairs, and me havin’ the joy o’ meetin’ you down here.", "샘이 외쳤다. \"글쎄 우리 주인 어른이 위층에서 자네 주인 만나는 기쁨을 누리시고, 나는 여기 아래서 자네 만나는 즐거움을 누리다니 말이야."], ["How are you gettin’ on, and how is the chandlery bis’ness likely to do?", "어떻게 지내나? 그 양초 가게 장사는 잘될 것 같은가?"], ["Well, I am so glad to see you.", "아, 자네를 보니 어찌나 반가운지."], ["How happy you look.", "어찌나 행복해 보이는지."], ["It’s quite a treat to see you; ain’t it, Mr. Muzzle?’", "자네를 보는 게 그야말로 큰 즐거움이야, 안 그렇소, 머즐 씨?\""]], [["‘Quite,’ said Mr.", "\"그렇소.\""], ["Muzzle.", "머즐 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘So cheerful he is!’ said Sam.", "\"어찌나 명랑한지!\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["‘In such good spirits!’ said Muzzle.", "\"기분이 어찌나 좋아 보이는지!\" 머즐이 말했다."]], [["‘And so glad to see us--that makes it so much more comfortable,’ said Sam.", "\"게다가 우릴 보고 어찌나 반가워하는지—그러니 한결 더 흐뭇하단 말이야.\" 샘이 말했다."], ["‘Sit down; sit down.’", "\"앉아, 앉으라고.\""]], [["Mr. Trotter suffered himself to be forced into a chair by the fireside. He cast his small eyes, first on Mr. Weller, and then on Mr. Muzzle, but said nothing.", "트로터 씨는 떠밀려 난롯가 의자에 앉혀지도록 자신을 내맡겼다. 그는 그 자그마한 눈을 먼저 웰러 씨에게, 그다음 머즐 씨에게 던졌으나, 아무 말도 하지 않았다."]], [["‘Well, now,’ said Sam, ‘afore these here ladies, I should jest like to ask you, as a sort of curiosity, whether you don’t consider yourself as nice and well-behaved a young gen’l’m’n, as ever used a pink check pocket-handkerchief, and the number four collection?’", "\"자, 그럼.\" 샘이 말했다. \"여기 이 부인들 앞에서, 그저 호기심 삼아 자네한테 좀 묻고 싶은데 말이야, 자네는 분홍 체크 손수건과 4번 헌금 바구니를 써 본 그 어떤 젊은 양반 못지않게 자네 스스로가 곱고 예의 바른 젊은 양반이라고 여기지 않나?\""]], [["‘And as was ever a-going to be married to a cook,’ said that lady indignantly.", "\"게다가 요리사하고 결혼까지 하려던 양반 아니랍니까.\" 그 부인이 분개하여 말했다."], ["‘The willin!’", "\"그 악당이!\""]], [["‘And leave off his evil ways, and set up in the chandlery line arterwards,’ said the housemaid.", "\"그러고는 못된 짓을 청산하고 나중에 양초 장사를 차리겠다던 양반이고요.\" 가정부가 말했다."]], [["‘Now, I’ll tell you what it is, young man,’ said Mr.", "\"자, 내 말 들어 봐, 젊은이.\" 머즐 씨가 방금 한 두 마디 언급에 격분하여 근엄하게 말했다."], ["Muzzle solemnly, enraged at the last two allusions, ‘this here lady (pointing to the cook) keeps company with me; and when you presume, Sir, to talk of keeping chandlers’ shops with her, you injure me in one of the most delicatest points in which one man can injure another.", "\"여기 이 부인이(요리사를 가리키며) 나와 사귀고 있단 말이야. 그런데 자네가, 선생, 감히 이 사람과 양초 가게를 차리겠다는 둥 떠벌리면, 그건 한 사내가 다른 사내를 해칠 수 있는 가장 미묘한 대목에서 나를 해치는 거란 말이야."], ["Do you understand that, Sir?’", "알아듣겠나, 선생?\""]], [["Here Mr. Muzzle, who had a great notion of his eloquence, in which he imitated his master, paused for a reply.", "여기서 자기 웅변에 대단한 자부심을 품고 그 점에서 주인을 흉내 내던 머즐 씨가, 대답을 기다리며 잠시 말을 멈추었다."]], [["But Mr. Trotter made no reply.", "그러나 트로터 씨는 아무 대답도 하지 않았다."], ["So Mr. Muzzle proceeded in a solemn manner--", "그래서 머즐 씨는 근엄한 태도로 말을 이었다."]], [["‘It’s very probable, sir, that you won’t be wanted upstairs for several minutes, Sir, because my master is at this moment particularly engaged in settling the hash of your master, Sir; and therefore you’ll have leisure, Sir, for a little private talk with me, Sir.", "\"십중팔구, 선생, 자네는 몇 분 동안은 위층에서 부르지 않을 걸세, 선생. 왜냐하면 우리 주인 어른이 지금 이 순간 자네 주인의 죽을 끓여 매듭짓느라 각별히 바쁘시거든, 선생."], ["Do you understand that, Sir?’", "그러니 자네한텐, 선생, 나하고 잠시 단둘이 얘기할 짬이 있을 걸세, 선생. 알아듣겠나, 선생?\""]], [["Mr. Muzzle again paused for a reply; and again Mr. Trotter disappointed him.", "머즐 씨가 다시 대답을 기다리며 멈췄으나, 트로터 씨는 또다시 그를 실망시켰다."]], [["‘Well, then,’ said Mr.", "\"자, 그럼.\""], ["Muzzle, ‘I’m very sorry to have to explain myself before ladies, but the urgency of the case will be my excuse.", "머즐 씨가 말했다. \"부인들 앞에서 내 뜻을 분명히 밝혀야 하는 게 참 유감이지만, 사정이 급하니 그것으로 양해를 구하겠네."], ["The back kitchen’s empty, Sir.", "뒷부엌이 비어 있어, 선생."], ["If you will step in there, Sir, Mr. Weller will see fair, and we can have mutual satisfaction till the bell rings.", "자네가 그리로 들어가 준다면, 선생, 웰러 씨가 공정하게 지켜볼 테고, 우리는 종이 울릴 때까지 서로 만족스럽게 결판을 낼 수 있을 거야."], ["Follow me, Sir!’", "날 따라오게, 선생!\""]], [["As Mr. Muzzle uttered these words, he took a step or two towards the door; and, by way of saving time, began to pull off his coat as he walked along.", "머즐 씨는 이 말을 하면서 문 쪽으로 한두 걸음 내딛었고, 시간을 아끼느라 걸어가며 윗도리를 벗기 시작했다."]], [["Now, the cook no sooner heard the concluding words of this desperate challenge, and saw Mr. Muzzle about to put it into execution, than she uttered a loud and piercing shriek; and rushing on Mr.", "자, 요리사는 이 무시무시한 도전의 끝맺는 말을 듣고 머즐 씨가 막 그것을 실행에 옮기려는 것을 보자마자, 크고 날카로운 비명을 질렀다."], ["Job Trotter, who rose from his chair on the instant, tore and buffeted his large flat face, with an energy peculiar to excited females, and twining her hands in his long black hair, tore therefrom about enough to make five or six dozen of the very largest-sized mourning-rings.", "그러고는 즉각 의자에서 일어선 잡 트로터 씨에게 달려들어, 흥분한 여성 특유의 기세로 그의 크고 넓적한 얼굴을 할퀴고 쥐어박았으며, 두 손을 그의 긴 검은 머리칼에 휘감아 가장 큰 치수의 추모 반지를 대여섯 다스는 만들 만큼의 머리털을 잡아 뽑았다."], ["Having accomplished this feat with all the ardour which her devoted love for Mr.", "머즐 씨를 향한 헌신적인 사랑에서 우러난 온갖 열정으로 이 위업을 이뤄 낸 그녀는 비틀거리며 물러섰다."], ["Muzzle inspired, she staggered back; and being a lady of very excitable and delicate feelings, she instantly fell under the dresser, and fainted away.", "그리고 몹시 흥분 잘하고 섬세한 감정의 숙녀인지라, 그녀는 즉각 찬장 밑으로 쓰러져 기절해 버렸다."]], [["At this moment, the bell rang.", "이 순간, 종이 울렸다."]], [["‘That’s for you, Job Trotter,’ said Sam; and before Mr.", "\"자넬 부르는 거야, 잡 트로터.\" 샘이 말했다."], ["Trotter could offer remonstrance or reply--even before he had time to stanch the wounds inflicted by the insensible lady--Sam seized one arm and Mr. Muzzle the other, and one pulling before, and the other pushing behind, they conveyed him upstairs, and into the parlour.", "그리고 트로터 씨가 항변이나 대답을 할 새도 없이—기절한 숙녀가 입힌 상처를 지혈할 틈조차 없이—샘이 한쪽 팔을, 머즐 씨가 다른 쪽 팔을 붙잡고, 하나는 앞에서 끌고 하나는 뒤에서 밀며, 그를 위층 응접실로 데려갔다."]], [["It was an impressive tableau.", "인상적인 활인화였다."], ["Alfred Jingle, Esquire, alias Captain Fitz-Marshall, was standing near the door with his hat in his hand, and a smile on his face, wholly unmoved by his very unpleasant situation. Confronting him, stood Mr.", "앨프리드 징글 님, 일명 피츠마셜 대위는 모자를 손에 들고 얼굴에 미소를 띤 채, 자신의 매우 곤란한 처지에는 조금도 동요하지 않고 문 가까이에 서 있었다."], ["Pickwick, who had evidently been inculcating some high moral lesson; for his left hand was beneath his coat tail, and his right extended in air, as was his wont when delivering himself of an impressive address. At a little distance, stood Mr.", "그와 마주하여 픽윅 씨가 서 있었는데, 그는 분명 어떤 고매한 도덕적 교훈을 가르치던 참이었으니, 왼손은 윗도리 자락 밑에, 오른손은 허공으로 뻗고 있었던바, 인상적인 연설을 토해 낼 때면 늘 그러던 자세였다."], ["Tupman with indignant countenance, carefully held back by his two younger friends; at the farther end of the room were Mr.", "조금 떨어진 곳에는 터프먼 씨가 분개한 얼굴로, 두 젊은 친구에게 조심스레 제지당한 채 서 있었다."], ["Nupkins, Mrs. Nupkins, and Miss Nupkins, gloomily grand and savagely vexed.", "그리고 방 저편 끝에는 너프킨스 씨와 너프킨스 부인, 너프킨스 양이 침울하게 도도하고 사납게 속상해하며 서 있었다."]], [["‘What prevents me,’ said Mr.", "\"무엇이 나를 막는가.\""], ["Nupkins, with magisterial dignity, as Job was brought in--‘what prevents me from detaining these men as rogues and impostors?", "잡이 끌려 들어오자 너프킨스 씨가 치안판사다운 위엄으로 말했다. \"무엇이 나를 막아, 이자들을 악당이요 사기꾼으로 구류하지 못하게 하는가?"], ["It is a foolish mercy.", "어리석은 자비로다."], ["What prevents me?’", "무엇이 나를 막는가?\""]], [["‘Pride, old fellow, pride,’ replied Jingle, quite at his ease.", "\"자존심이지, 영감, 자존심.\" 징글이 아주 느긋하게 대답했다."], ["‘Wouldn’t do--no go--caught a captain, eh?--ha!", "\"안 될걸—틀렸어—대위를 하나 낚았겠다, 응?—하!"], ["ha!", "하!"], ["very good--husband for daughter--biter bit--make it public--not for worlds--look stupid--very!’", "아주 좋아—딸 사윗감으로 말이지—물려던 자가 물렸군—세상에 알린다—천만에—멍청해 보일걸—아주!\""]], [["‘Wretch,’ said Mr.", "\"비열한 놈.\""], ["Nupkins, ‘we scorn your base insinuations.’", "너프킨스 씨가 말했다. \"우리는 네놈의 천박한 암시 따위 경멸한다.\""]], [["‘I always hated him,’ added Henrietta.", "\"전 처음부터 저 사람이 싫었어요.\" 헨리에타가 덧붙였다."]], [["‘Oh, of course,’ said Jingle.", "\"오, 그렇겠지.\" 징글이 말했다."], ["‘Tall young man--old lover--Sidney Porkenham--rich--fine fellow--not so rich as captain, though, eh?--turn him away--off with him--anything for captain--nothing like captain anywhere--all the girls--raving mad--eh, Job, eh?’", "\"키 큰 젊은이—옛 연인—시드니 포컨엄—부자—훌륭한 친구—그래도 대위만큼 부자는 아니지, 응?—그자를 내쳐라—꺼지게 해—대위를 위해서라면 뭐든지—대위만 한 게 어디 없으니—계집애들 죄다—미쳐 날뛰지—응, 잡, 응?\""]], [["Here Mr. Jingle laughed very heartily; and Job, rubbing his hands with delight, uttered the first sound he had given vent to since he entered the house--a low, noiseless chuckle, which seemed to intimate that he enjoyed his laugh too much, to let any of it escape in sound.", "여기서 징글 씨는 아주 진심으로 껄껄 웃었고, 잡은 기뻐서 두 손을 비비며, 집에 들어온 뒤로 처음으로 소리를 냈으니—낮고 소리 없는 킬킬거림이었는데, 그것은 자기 웃음을 너무도 만끽한 나머지 그 어느 한 조각도 소리로 새어 나가게 하지 않으려는 듯했다."]], [["‘Mr.", "\"너프킨스 씨.\""], ["Nupkins,’ said the elder lady,’ this is not a fit conversation for the servants to overhear.", "나이 든 부인이 말했다. \"이건 하인들이 엿들을 만한 대화가 아니에요."], ["Let these wretches be removed.’", "저 비열한 것들을 내보내요.\""]], [["‘Certainly, my dear,’ Said Mr.", "\"물론이오, 여보.\" 너프킨스 씨가 말했다."], ["Nupkins. ‘Muzzle!’", "\"머즐!\""]], [["‘Your Worship.’", "\"예, 나리.\""]], [["‘Open the front door.’", "\"현관문을 열어라.\""]], [["‘Yes, your Worship.’", "\"예, 나리.\""]], [["‘Leave the house!’ said Mr.", "\"이 집에서 나가라!\""], ["Nupkins, waving his hand emphatically.", "너프킨스 씨가 단호하게 손을 내저으며 말했다."]], [["Jingle smiled, and moved towards the door.", "징글이 미소를 짓고는 문 쪽으로 움직였다."]], [["‘Stay!’ said Mr.", "\"멈추시오!\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Jingle stopped.", "징글이 멈췄다."]], [["‘I might,’ said Mr.", "\"나는,\""], ["Pickwick, ‘have taken a much greater revenge for the treatment I have experienced at your hands, and that of your hypocritical friend there.’", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"당신과 저기 저 위선적인 당신 친구의 손에 겪은 처사에 대해 훨씬 더 큰 복수를 할 수도 있었소.\""]], [["Job Trotter bowed with great politeness, and laid his hand upon his heart.", "잡 트로터가 더없이 정중하게 절을 하며 가슴에 손을 얹었다."]], [["‘I say,’ said Mr.", "\"내 말은,\" 픽윅 씨가 차츰 화가 치밀어 말했다."], ["Pickwick, growing gradually angry, ‘that I might have taken a greater revenge, but I content myself with exposing you, which I consider a duty I owe to society.", "\"내가 더 큰 복수를 할 수도 있었지만, 당신의 정체를 폭로하는 것으로 만족한다는 것이오. 나는 그것을 사회에 진 의무로 여기오."], ["This is a leniency, Sir, which I hope you will remember.’", "이것은 관용이오, 선생. 부디 그것을 기억해 두기 바라오.\""]], [["When Mr. Pickwick arrived at this point, Job Trotter, with facetious gravity, applied his hand to his ear, as if desirous not to lose a syllable he uttered.", "픽윅 씨가 이 대목에 이르자, 잡 트로터는 익살스러운 엄숙함으로 손을 귀에 갖다 댔으니, 그가 내뱉는 한 음절도 놓치지 않으려는 듯했다."]], [["‘And I have only to add, sir,’ said Mr.", "\"그리고 한마디만 덧붙이겠소, 선생.\""], ["Pickwick, now thoroughly angry, ‘that I consider you a rascal, and a--a--ruffian--and--and worse than any man I ever saw, or heard of, except that pious and sanctified vagabond in the mulberry livery.’", "이제 완전히 화가 난 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"나는 당신을 악당이요, 또—또—불한당이요—또—또 멀버리 제복을 입은 저 경건하고 신성한 척하는 부랑자를 빼고는, 내가 보았거나 들었던 그 누구보다도 더 못된 자로 여기는 바이오.\""]], [["‘Ha!", "\"하! 하!\""], ["ha!’ said Jingle, ‘good fellow, Pickwick--fine heart--stout old boy--but must not be passionate--bad thing, very--bye, bye--see you again some day--keep up your spirits--now, Job--trot!’", "징글이 말했다. \"좋은 친구야, 픽윅—마음씨 곱고—다부진 영감이지—하지만 격해지면 안 돼—나쁜 거야, 아주—잘 있어, 잘 있어—언젠가 또 봅시다—기운 내고—자, 잡—종종걸음!\""]], [["With these words, Mr. Jingle stuck on his hat in his old fashion, and strode out of the room. Job Trotter paused, looked round, smiled and then with a bow of mock solemnity to Mr. Pickwick, and a wink to Mr. Weller, the audacious slyness of which baffles all description, followed the footsteps of his hopeful master.", "이 말과 함께 징글 씨는 예의 그 방식대로 모자를 척 눌러쓰고 성큼성큼 방을 나섰다. 잡 트로터는 잠시 멈춰 둘러보고는 미소를 짓더니, 픽윅 씨에게는 가짜 엄숙함을 담은 절을, 웰러 씨에게는 그 뻔뻔한 능청스러움이 도무지 형언할 길 없는 윙크를 보내고, 제 알량한 주인의 발자취를 뒤따라갔다."]], [["‘Sam,’ said Mr.", "\"샘.\""], ["Pickwick, as Mr.", "웰러 씨가 따라 나서려 하자 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Weller was following.", "\"예, 나리.\""]], [["‘Sir.’\nStay here.’", "\"여기 있거라.\""]], [["Mr. Weller seemed uncertain.", "웰러 씨는 머뭇거리는 듯했다."]], [["‘Stay here,’ repeated Mr.", "\"여기 있거라.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 되풀이했다."]], [["‘Mayn’t I polish that ‘ere Job off, in the front garden?’ said Mr.", "\"저 잡이라는 놈을 앞뜰에서 한번 손봐 줘도 안 되겠습니까, 나리?\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Certainly not,’ replied Mr.", "\"절대 안 된다.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘Mayn’t I kick him out o’ the gate, Sir?’ said Mr.", "\"문밖으로 걷어차 내쫓는 것도 안 됩니까, 나리?\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Not on any account,’ replied his master.", "\"무슨 일이 있어도 안 된다.\" 그의 주인이 대답했다."]], [["For the first time since his engagement, Mr. Weller looked, for a moment, discontented and unhappy. But his countenance immediately cleared up; for the wily Mr. Muzzle, by concealing himself behind the street door, and rushing violently out, at the right instant, contrived with great dexterity to overturn both Mr. Jingle and his attendant, down the flight of steps, into the American aloe tubs that stood beneath.", "고용된 이래 처음으로, 웰러 씨는 한순간 불만스럽고 언짢은 표정을 지었다. 그러나 그의 얼굴은 곧장 환해졌으니, 약삭빠른 머즐 씨가 현관문 뒤에 몸을 숨겼다가 바로 알맞은 순간에 사납게 뛰쳐나와, 더없이 솜씨 좋게 징글 씨와 그 종자를 둘 다 계단 아래로, 그 밑에 놓인 미국산 알로에 통 속으로 굴러떨어뜨리는 데 성공했기 때문이다."]], [["‘Having discharged my duty, Sir,’ said Mr.", "\"제 의무를 다했으니, 나리.\" 픽윅 씨가 너프킨스 씨에게 말했다."], ["Pickwick to Mr. Nupkins, ‘I will, with my friends, bid you farewell.", "\"저는 제 친구들과 더불어 작별을 고하겠습니다."], ["While we thank you for such hospitality as we have received, permit me to assure you, in our joint names, that we should not have accepted it, or have consented to extricate ourselves in this way, from our previous dilemma, had we not been impelled by a strong sense of duty.", "저희가 받은 환대에 감사드리면서도, 저희 모두의 이름으로 단언하건대, 저희가 강한 의무감에 떠밀리지 않았던들, 저희는 그 환대를 받아들이지도, 앞서의 곤경에서 이런 식으로 벗어나는 데 동의하지도 않았을 것입니다. 저희는 내일 런던으로 돌아갑니다."], ["We return to London to-morrow. Your secret is safe with us.’", "나리의 비밀은 저희가 안전하게 지켜 드리겠습니다.\""]], [["Having thus entered his protest against their treatment of the morning, Mr. Pickwick bowed low to the ladies, and notwithstanding the solicitations of the family, left the room with his friends.", "이렇듯 그날 아침의 처사에 대한 항의를 표한 뒤, 픽윅 씨는 부인들에게 깊이 절하고는, 가족의 만류에도 불구하고 친구들과 함께 방을 나섰다."]], [["‘Get your hat, Sam,’ said Mr.", "\"네 모자를 가져오너라, 샘.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘It’s below stairs, Sir,’ said Sam, and he ran down after it.", "\"아래층에 있습니다, 나리.\" 샘이 말하고는 그것을 가지러 뛰어 내려갔다."]], [["Now, there was nobody in the kitchen, but the pretty housemaid; and as Sam’s hat was mislaid, he had to look for it, and the pretty housemaid lighted him.", "자, 부엌에는 그 예쁜 가정부 말고는 아무도 없었다. 그리고 샘의 모자가 어디 놓였는지 알 수 없었던 터라 그것을 찾아야 했고, 예쁜 가정부가 불을 비춰 주었다."], ["They had to look all over the place for the hat.", "그들은 온 사방을 뒤져 모자를 찾아야 했다."], ["The pretty housemaid, in her anxiety to find it, went down on her knees, and turned over all the things that were heaped together in a little corner by the door.", "예쁜 가정부는 그것을 찾으려는 조바심에 무릎을 꿇고 앉아, 문 옆 작은 구석에 쌓여 있는 온갖 물건을 들춰 보았다."], ["It was an awkward corner.", "거북한 구석이었다."], ["You couldn’t get at it without shutting the door first.", "먼저 문을 닫지 않고는 거기에 손이 닿지 않았다."]], [["‘Here it is,’ said the pretty housemaid.", "\"여기 있네요.\" 예쁜 가정부가 말했다."], ["‘This is it, ain’t it?’", "\"이거죠, 그렇죠?\""]], [["‘Let me look,’ said Sam.", "\"어디 봅시다.\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["The pretty housemaid had stood the candle on the floor; and, as it gave a very dim light, Sam was obliged to go down on his knees before he could see whether it really was his own hat or not.", "예쁜 가정부는 촛불을 바닥에 세워 두었는데, 불빛이 아주 흐릿했던 탓에 샘은 그것이 정말 자기 모자인지 아닌지 보려면 무릎을 꿇어야 했다."], ["It was a remarkably small corner, and so--it was nobody’s fault but the man’s who built the house--Sam and the pretty housemaid were necessarily very close together.", "유난히 좁은 구석이었고, 그래서—집을 지은 사람 말고는 누구의 잘못도 아니었으나—샘과 예쁜 가정부는 어쩔 수 없이 아주 바싹 붙어 있게 되었다."]], [["‘Yes, this is it,’ said Sam.", "\"맞소, 이거요.\" 샘이 말했다."], ["‘Good-bye!’", "\"잘 있어요!\""]], [["‘Good-bye!’ said the pretty housemaid.", "\"잘 가요!\" 예쁜 가정부가 말했다."]], [["‘Good-bye!’ said Sam; and as he said it, he dropped the hat that had cost so much trouble in looking for.", "\"잘 있어요!\" 샘이 말했다. 그리고 그렇게 말하면서, 그토록 애써 찾아낸 모자를 떨어뜨렸다."]], [["‘How awkward you are,’ said the pretty housemaid.", "\"어쩜 그리 칠칠치 못해요.\" 예쁜 가정부가 말했다."], ["‘You’ll lose it again, if you don’t take care.’", "\"조심하지 않으면 또 잃어버릴 거예요.\""]], [["So just to prevent his losing it again, she put it on for him.", "그래서 그가 또 잃어버리지 않도록, 그녀가 직접 그것을 그에게 씌워 주었다."]], [["Whether it was that the pretty housemaid’s face looked prettier still, when it was raised towards Sam’s, or whether it was the accidental consequence of their being so near to each other, is matter of uncertainty to this day; but Sam kissed her.", "예쁜 가정부의 얼굴이 샘의 얼굴을 향해 들어 올려졌을 때 한층 더 예뻐 보였기 때문인지, 아니면 둘이 그토록 가까이 있던 데서 비롯된 우연한 결과였는지는 오늘날까지도 알 수 없는 일이다. 그러나 샘은 그녀에게 입을 맞추었다."]], [["‘You don’t mean to say you did that on purpose,’ said the pretty housemaid, blushing.", "\"설마 일부러 그런 건 아니겠죠.\" 예쁜 가정부가 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다."]], [["‘No, I didn’t then,’ said Sam; ‘but I will now.’\nSo he kissed her again.", "\"아니, 그땐 일부러는 아니었소.\" 샘이 말했다. \"하지만 이번엔 일부러 하겠소.\""]], [["‘Sam!’ said Mr. Pickwick, calling over the banisters.", "그러고는 다시 그녀에게 입을 맞추었다."]], [["‘Coming, Sir,’ replied Sam, running upstairs.", "\"샘!\" 픽윅 씨가 난간 너머로 부르며 말했다."]], [["‘How long you have been!’ said Mr.", "\"갑니다, 나리.\""], ["Pickwick.", "샘이 위층으로 뛰어 올라가며 대답했다."]], [["‘There was something behind the door, Sir, which perwented our getting it open, for ever so long, Sir,’ replied Sam.", "\"어찌 그리 오래 걸렸느냐!\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"문 뒤에 뭔가가 있어서, 한참 동안 도무지 열 수가 없었습니다, 나리.\" 샘이 대답했다."]], [["And this was the first passage of Mr. Weller’s first love.", "그리고 이것이 웰러 씨의 첫사랑의 첫 대목이었다."]], [["CHAPTER XXVI. WHICH CONTAINS A BRIEF ACCOUNT OF THE PROGRESS OF THE ACTION OF BARDELL AGAINST PICKWICK\nHaving accomplished the main end and object of his journey, by the exposure of Jingle, Mr.", "징글의 정체를 폭로함으로써 여행의 주된 목적과 목표를 이룬 픽윅 씨는, 그동안 도드슨과 포그 두 사람이 자신을 상대로 취해 온 소송 절차를 알아보기 위해 즉시 런던으로 돌아가기로 결심했다."], ["Pickwick resolved on immediately returning to London, with the view of becoming acquainted with the proceedings which had been taken against him, in the meantime, by Messrs. Dodson and Fogg.", "자신의 성격다운 온갖 정력과 결단력으로 이 결심을 실행에 옮긴 그는, 앞의 두 장에서 자세히 서술한 잊지 못할 사건들이 있은 다음 날 아침, 입스위치를 떠나는 첫 마차의 뒷좌석에 올라탔다."], ["Acting upon this resolution with all the energy and decision of his character, he mounted to the back seat of the first coach which left Ipswich on the morning after the memorable occurrences detailed at length in the two preceding chapters; and accompanied by his three friends, and Mr. Samuel Weller, arrived in the metropolis, in perfect health and safety, the same evening.", "그리고 세 친구와 새뮤얼 웰러 씨를 대동하고 그날 저녁 더없이 건강하고 무사하게 수도에 도착했다."]], [["Here the friends, for a short time, separated. Messrs.", "여기서 친구들은 잠시 동안 헤어졌다."], ["Tupman, Winkle, and Snodgrass repaired to their several homes to make such preparations as might be requisite for their forthcoming visit to Dingley Dell; and Mr. Pickwick and Sam took up their present abode in very good, old- fashioned, and comfortable quarters, to wit, the George and Vulture Tavern and Hotel, George Yard, Lombard Street.", "터프먼 씨, 윈클 씨, 스노드그래스 씨는 곧 있을 딩글리 델 방문에 필요한 준비를 하기 위해 각자의 집으로 향했고, 픽윅 씨와 샘은 매우 훌륭하고 고풍스러우며 안락한 숙소, 즉 롬바드 가 조지 야드에 있는 조지 앤드 벌처 여관 겸 호텔에 당분간 거처를 정했다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick had dined, finished his second pint of particular port, pulled his silk handkerchief over his head, put his feet on the fender, and thrown himself back in an easy-chair, when the entrance of Mr. Weller with his carpet-bag, aroused him from his tranquil meditation.", "픽윅 씨는 저녁 식사를 마치고 특제 포트와인 두 번째 잔까지 비운 뒤, 비단 손수건을 머리 위에 끌어 덮고 발을 난로 받침대에 올린 채 안락의자에 몸을 깊숙이 기대고 있었는데, 그때 웰러 씨가 융단 가방을 들고 들어오는 바람에 고요한 명상에서 깨어났다."]], [["‘Sam,’ said Mr.", "\"샘.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Sir,’ said Mr.", "\"네, 어르신.\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘I have just been thinking, Sam,’ said Mr.", "\"방금 생각해 보았는데 말이다, 샘.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick, ‘that having left a good many things at Mrs. Bardell’s, in Goswell Street, I ought to arrange for taking them away, before I leave town again.’", "\"고스웰 가에 있는 바델 부인 댁에 물건을 꽤 많이 두고 왔으니, 다시 시내를 떠나기 전에 그것들을 가져올 채비를 해 두어야겠다.\""]], [["‘Wery good, sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"아주 좋습니다, 어르신.\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘I could send them to Mr. Tupman’s, for the present, Sam,’ continued Mr.", "\"당장은 그것들을 터프먼 씨 댁으로 보내 두면 되겠다, 샘.\""], ["Pickwick, ‘but before we take them away, it is necessary that they should be looked up, and put together.", "픽윅 씨가 말을 이었다. \"하지만 가져오기 전에, 물건들을 찾아내어 한데 모아 두어야 한다."], ["I wish you would step up to Goswell Street, Sam, and arrange about it.’", "네가 고스웰 가에 좀 가서 그 일을 처리해 주었으면 한다, 샘.\""]], [["‘At once, Sir?’ inquired Mr.", "\"지금 당장 말씀입니까, 어르신?\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘At once,’ replied Mr.", "\"당장 가거라.\""], ["Pickwick. ‘And stay, Sam,’ added Mr.", "픽윅 씨가 대답했다."], ["Pickwick, pulling out his purse, ‘there is some rent to pay.", "\"그리고 잠깐, 샘.\" 픽윅 씨가 지갑을 꺼내며 덧붙였다."], ["The quarter is not due till Christmas, but you may pay it, and have done with it.", "\"치를 집세가 좀 있다. 분기 집세는 크리스마스까지는 만기가 아니다만, 미리 치러서 끝내 두어도 좋다."], ["A month’s notice terminates my tenancy.", "한 달 전 통지로 내 세입이 끝나게 된다."], ["Here it is, written out.", "여기 그 통지서를 적어 두었다."], ["Give it, and tell Mrs. Bardell she may put a bill up, as soon as she likes.’", "이것을 건네고, 바델 부인에게 언제든 원할 때 세놓는다는 광고를 붙여도 된다고 전하거라.\""]], [["‘Wery good, sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"아주 좋습니다, 어르신.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다."], ["Weller; ‘anythin’ more, sir?’", "\"그 밖에 더 하실 말씀은요, 어르신?\""]], [["‘Nothing more, Sam.’", "\"더 없다, 샘.\""]], [["Mr. Weller stepped slowly to the door, as if he expected something more; slowly opened it, slowly stepped out, and had slowly closed it within a couple of inches, when Mr. Pickwick called out--", "웰러 씨는 무언가 더 있기를 기대하기라도 하듯 천천히 문 쪽으로 걸어가, 천천히 문을 열고, 천천히 밖으로 나가서는, 천천히 문을 두어 인치쯤 남기고 닫았는데, 그때 픽윅 씨가 불러 세웠다."]], [["‘Sam.’", "\"샘.\""]], [["‘Yes, sir,’ said Mr.", "\"네, 어르신.\""], ["Weller, stepping quickly back, and closing the door behind him.", "웰러 씨가 얼른 되돌아와 등 뒤로 문을 닫으며 말했다."]], [["‘I have no objection, Sam, to your endeavouring to ascertain how Mrs. Bardell herself seems disposed towards me, and whether it is really probable that this vile and groundless action is to be carried to extremity.", "\"샘, 나는 네가 바델 부인 본인이 나를 어떻게 여기는 듯한지, 그리고 이 비열하고 근거 없는 소송이 정말로 끝까지 갈 가능성이 있는지를 알아보려 애쓰는 데 반대하지 않는다."], ["I say I do not object to you doing this, if you wish it, Sam,’ said Mr.", "네가 원한다면 그렇게 하는 데 반대하지 않는다는 말이다, 샘.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["Sam gave a short nod of intelligence, and left the room. Mr.", "샘은 알아들었다는 듯 짧게 고개를 끄덕이고 방을 나섰다."], ["Pickwick drew the silk handkerchief once more over his head, And composed himself for a nap.", "픽윅 씨는 다시 한번 비단 손수건을 머리 위로 끌어 덮고 한숨 잘 채비를 했다."], ["Mr. Weller promptly walked forth, to execute his commission.", "웰러 씨는 곧장 자신의 임무를 수행하러 걸어 나갔다."]], [["It was nearly nine o’clock when he reached Goswell Street.", "그가 고스웰 가에 다다랐을 때는 거의 아홉 시였다."], ["A couple of candles were burning in the little front parlour, and a couple of caps were reflected on the window-blind.", "자그마한 앞쪽 거실에는 촛불 두 자루가 타고 있었고, 창문 블라인드에는 모자 두 개가 비쳐 있었다."], ["Mrs. Bardell had got company.", "바델 부인에게 손님이 와 있었던 것이다."]], [["Mr. Weller knocked at the door, and after a pretty long interval-- occupied by the party without, in whistling a tune, and by the party within, in persuading a refractory flat candle to allow itself to be lighted--a pair of small boots pattered over the floor-cloth, and Master Bardell presented himself.", "웰러 씨가 문을 두드리자, 꽤 긴 시간이 흐른 뒤에야—그동안 밖에 있는 쪽에서는 가락을 휘파람으로 불고, 안에 있는 쪽에서는 말을 안 듣는 평평한 양초가 불 켜지기를 받아들이도록 구슬리느라 시간이 걸렸는데—자그마한 장화 한 켤레가 바닥 깔개 위를 타닥타닥 걸어왔고, 바델 도련님이 모습을 드러냈다."]], [["‘Well, young townskip,’ said Sam, ‘how’s mother?’", "\"어이, 꼬마 동네 양반.\" 샘이 말했다. \"어머니는 좀 어떠시냐?\""]], [["‘She’s pretty well,’ replied Master Bardell, ‘so am I.’", "\"잘 지내세요.\" 바델 도련님이 대답했다. \"저도 그렇고요.\""]], [["‘Well, that’s a mercy,’ said Sam; ‘tell her I want to speak to her, will you, my hinfant fernomenon?’", "\"그것참 다행이로구나.\" 샘이 말했다. \"어머니께 내가 좀 뵙고 싶다고 전해 주겠니, 우리 천재 신동 아기씨?\""]], [["Master Bardell, thus adjured, placed the refractory flat candle on the bottom stair, and vanished into the front parlour with his message.", "이렇게 부탁을 받은 바델 도련님은 말 안 듣는 평평한 양초를 맨 아래 계단에 내려놓고, 전갈을 들고 앞쪽 거실로 사라졌다."]], [["The two caps, reflected on the window-blind, were the respective head- dresses of a couple of Mrs. Bardell’s most particular acquaintance, who had just stepped in, to have a quiet cup of tea, and a little warm supper of a couple of sets of pettitoes and some toasted cheese. The cheese was simmering and browning away, most delightfully, in a little Dutch oven before the fire; the pettitoes were getting on deliciously in a little tin saucepan on the hob; and Mrs. Bardell and her two friends were getting on very well, also, in a little quiet conversation about and concerning all their particular friends and acquaintance; when Master Bardell came back from answering the door, and delivered the message intrusted to him by Mr. Samuel Weller.", "창문 블라인드에 비쳤던 두 모자는, 바델 부인의 가장 각별한 지인 두 사람이 각각 쓴 머리 장식이었으니, 그들은 조용히 차나 한잔 마시고, 돼지 족발 두 벌과 구운 치즈로 따끈한 야식이나 좀 들려고 막 들른 참이었다. 치즈는 난로 앞 작은 더치 오븐 속에서 더없이 먹음직스럽게 보글보글 끓으며 노릇노릇 익어 가고 있었고, 족발은 화덕 선반 위 작은 양철 냄비 속에서 맛있게 익어 가고 있었으며, 바델 부인과 두 친구 또한 자기들의 각별한 친구들과 지인들에 관한 조용한 잡담을 나누며 매우 사이좋게 지내고 있던 차에, 바델 도련님이 문에 응대하고 돌아와 새뮤얼 웰러 씨가 맡긴 전갈을 전했다."]], [["‘Mr. Pickwick’s servant!’ said Mrs.", "\"픽윅 씨의 하인이라고!\""], ["Bardell, turning pale.", "바델 부인이 새파랗게 질리며 말했다."]], [["‘Bless my soul!’ said Mrs.", "\"어머나 세상에!\""], ["Cluppins.", "클러핀스 부인이 말했다."]], [["‘Well, I raly would not ha’ believed it, unless I had ha’ happened to ha’ been here!’ said Mrs.", "\"글쎄, 내가 마침 여기 있지 않았더라면 정말이지 믿지 못했을 거예요!\""], ["Sanders.", "샌더스 부인이 말했다."]], [["Mrs. Cluppins was a little, brisk, busy-looking woman; Mrs. Sanders was a big, fat, heavy-faced personage; and the two were the company.", "클러핀스 부인은 작고 활발하며 분주해 보이는 여자였고, 샌더스 부인은 크고 뚱뚱하며 우둔한 얼굴을 한 인물이었으니, 이 둘이 손님이었다."]], [["Mrs. Bardell felt it proper to be agitated; and as none of the three exactly knew whether under existing circumstances, any communication, otherwise than through Dodson & Fogg, ought to be held with Mr.", "바델 부인은 동요하는 것이 마땅하다고 느꼈다."], ["Pickwick’s servant, they were all rather taken by surprise.", "그리고 세 사람 중 누구도 지금 상황에서 도드슨과 포그를 거치지 않고 픽윅 씨의 하인과 어떤 식으로든 이야기를 나누어도 되는 것인지 정확히 알지 못했으므로, 모두가 다소 당황했다."], ["In this state of indecision, obviously the first thing to be done, was to thump the boy for finding Mr.", "이런 우유부단한 상태에서, 가장 먼저 해야 할 일은 분명, 웰러 씨를 문간에서 맞아들인 죄로 아이를 한 대 쥐어박는 것이었다."], ["Weller at the door. So his mother thumped him, and he cried melodiously.", "그래서 어머니가 아이를 쥐어박았고, 아이는 가락을 맞춰 울어 댔다."]], [["‘Hold your noise--do--you naughty creetur!’ said Mrs.", "\"시끄럽게 좀 굴지 마라—제발—이 못된 녀석아!\""], ["Bardell.", "바델 부인이 말했다."]], [["‘Yes; don’t worrit your poor mother,’ said Mrs.", "\"그래, 가엾은 어머니를 속 썩이지 말거라.\""], ["Sanders.", "샌더스 부인이 말했다."]], [["‘She’s quite enough to worrit her, as it is, without you, Tommy,’ said Mrs.", "\"안 그래도 어머니는 너 말고도 속 썩일 일이 충분하단다, 토미.\""], ["Cluppins, with sympathising resignation.", "클러핀스 부인이 동정 어린 체념의 어조로 말했다."]], [["‘Ah!", "\"아!"], ["worse luck, poor lamb!’ said Mrs.", "더 안된 일이지, 가엾은 어린양!\""], ["Sanders.", "샌더스 부인이 말했다."]], [["At all which moral reflections, Master Bardell howled the louder.", "이 모든 교훈적인 훈계를 들을 때마다, 바델 도련님은 더욱 크게 울부짖었다."]], [["‘Now, what shall I do?’ said Mrs.", "\"자, 이제 어쩌면 좋죠?\""], ["Bardell to Mrs. Cluppins.", "바델 부인이 클러핀스 부인에게 물었다."]], [["‘I think you ought to see him,’ replied Mrs.", "\"내 생각엔 그자를 만나 봐야 할 것 같아요.\" 클러핀스 부인이 대답했다."], ["Cluppins. ‘But on no account without a witness.’", "\"하지만 절대로 증인 없이는 안 돼요.\""]], [["‘I think two witnesses would be more lawful,’ said Mrs.", "\"증인은 둘이 있어야 더 법에 맞을 것 같은데요.\""], ["Sanders, who, like the other friend, was bursting with curiosity.", "다른 친구와 마찬가지로 호기심으로 터질 듯한 샌더스 부인이 말했다."]], [["‘Perhaps he’d better come in here,’ said Mrs.", "\"이리로 들어오라고 하는 게 낫겠어요.\""], ["Bardell.", "바델 부인이 말했다."]], [["‘To be sure,’ replied Mrs.", "\"물론이지요.\" 클러핀스 부인이 그 생각에 얼른 매달리며 대답했다."], ["Cluppins, eagerly catching at the idea; ‘walk in, young man; and shut the street door first, please.’", "\"들어와요, 젊은이. 그리고 먼저 현관문부터 좀 닫고요.\""]], [["Mr. Weller immediately took the hint; and presenting himself in the parlour, explained his business to Mrs.", "웰러 씨는 즉시 그 뜻을 알아차렸다."], ["Bardell thus--", "그리고 거실에 모습을 드러내고는, 자신의 용건을 바델 부인에게 이렇게 설명했다."]], [["‘Wery sorry to ‘casion any personal inconwenience, ma’am, as the housebreaker said to the old lady when he put her on the fire; but as me and my governor ‘s only jest come to town, and is jest going away agin, it can’t be helped, you see.’", "\"개인적으로 폐를 끼치게 되어 대단히 죄송합니다, 부인. 강도가 노부인을 불 위에 올려놓으면서 한 말마따나 말입니다. 하지만 저와 제 주인 어른이 막 시내에 온 데다 또 막 떠나려는 참이라, 보시다시피 어쩔 수가 없습니다요.\""]], [["‘Of course, the young man can’t help the faults of his master,’ said Mrs.", "\"물론, 젊은이가 주인의 잘못을 어쩔 수는 없지요.\""], ["Cluppins, much struck by Mr. Weller’s appearance and conversation.", "웰러 씨의 외모와 말솜씨에 자못 감명받은 클러핀스 부인이 말했다."]], [["‘Certainly not,’ chimed in Mrs.", "\"그럼요, 그렇고말고요.\""], ["Sanders, who, from certain wistful glances at the little tin saucepan, seemed to be engaged in a mental calculation of the probable extent of the pettitoes, in the event of Sam’s being asked to stop to supper.", "샌더스 부인이 맞장구를 쳤는데, 그녀는 작은 양철 냄비를 향해 무언가 아쉬운 눈길을 던지는 것으로 보아, 샘이 야식을 들고 가라는 권유를 받을 경우 족발이 어느 정도까지 줄어들지를 마음속으로 계산하고 있는 듯했다."]], [["‘So all I’ve come about, is jest this here,’ said Sam, disregarding the interruption; ‘first, to give my governor’s notice--there it is.", "\"그래서 제가 온 용건은 그저 이런 것입니다.\" 샘이 그 끼어드는 말은 아랑곳 않고 말했다."], ["Secondly, to pay the rent--here it is.", "\"첫째, 제 주인 어른의 통지를 전하는 것—여기 있습니다."], ["Thirdly, to say as all his things is to be put together, and give to anybody as we sends for ‘em.", "둘째, 집세를 치르는 것—여기 있습니다. 셋째, 주인 어른의 물건을 모두 한데 모아 두었다가 우리가 사람을 보내면 누구에게든 내어 달라는 것."], ["Fourthly, that you may let the place as soon as you like--and that’s all.’", "넷째, 부인께서 언제든 원하시는 대로 이 집을 세놓으셔도 된다는 것—이상입니다.\""]], [["‘Whatever has happened,’ said Mrs.", "\"무슨 일이 있었든 간에,\" 바델 부인이 말했다."], ["Bardell, ‘I always have said, and always will say, that in every respect but one, Mr. Pickwick has always behaved himself like a perfect gentleman.", "\"저는 늘 말해 왔고 앞으로도 늘 말하겠지만, 딱 한 가지만 빼면 모든 면에서 픽윅 씨는 언제나 완벽한 신사답게 처신하셨어요."], ["His money always as good as the bank--always.’", "그분 돈은 언제나 은행만큼이나 확실했지요—언제나요.\""]], [["As Mrs. Bardell said this, she applied her handkerchief to her eyes, and went out of the room to get the receipt.", "바델 부인은 이렇게 말하며 손수건을 눈가에 갖다 대고는, 영수증을 가지러 방을 나갔다."]], [["Sam well knew that he had only to remain quiet, and the women were sure to talk; so he looked alternately at the tin saucepan, the toasted cheese, the wall, and the ceiling, in profound silence.", "샘은 자기가 그저 가만히 있기만 하면 여자들이 틀림없이 떠들어 댈 것임을 잘 알고 있었다. 그래서 그는 양철 냄비, 구운 치즈, 벽, 천장을 번갈아 바라보며 깊은 침묵을 지켰다."]], [["‘Poor dear!’ said Mrs.", "\"가엾은 양반!\""], ["Cluppins.", "클러핀스 부인이 말했다."]], [["‘Ah, poor thing!’ replied Mrs.", "\"아, 가엾은 사람!\""], ["Sanders.", "샌더스 부인이 응수했다."]], [["Sam said nothing.", "샘은 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["He saw they were coming to the subject.", "그들이 본론으로 다가오고 있음을 알아챘던 것이다."]], [["‘I raly cannot contain myself,’ said Mrs.", "\"그런 위증을 생각하면 정말이지 견딜 수가 없어요.\""], ["Cluppins, ‘when I think of such perjury.", "클러핀스 부인이 말했다."], ["I don’t wish to say anything to make you uncomfortable, young man, but your master’s an old brute, and I wish I had him here to tell him so.’", "\"젊은이를 불편하게 할 말은 하고 싶지 않지만, 자네 주인은 늙은 짐승이고, 그 작자가 여기 있다면 면전에 대고 그렇게 말해 주고 싶군요.\""]], [["I wish you had,’ said Sam.", "\"여기 계셨더라면 좋았겠네요.\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["‘To see how dreadful she takes on, going moping about, and taking no pleasure in nothing, except when her friends comes in, out of charity, to sit with her, and make her comfortable,’ resumed Mrs.", "\"그 부인이 얼마나 끔찍하게 시름에 잠겨 있는지, 멍하니 돌아다니며 아무것에도 즐거움을 못 느끼고, 오직 친구들이 자비심으로 찾아와 곁에 앉아 마음을 달래 줄 때나 좀 나아지는지를 본다면—\""], ["Cluppins, glancing at the tin saucepan and the Dutch oven, ‘it’s shocking!’", "클러핀스 부인이 양철 냄비와 더치 오븐을 흘끔거리며 말을 이었다. \"정말 충격적이라니까요!\""]], [["‘Barbareous,’ said Mrs.", "\"야만적이지요.\""], ["Sanders.", "샌더스 부인이 말했다."]], [["‘And your master, young man!", "\"게다가 자네 주인 말이에요, 젊은이!"], ["A gentleman with money, as could never feel the expense of a wife, no more than nothing,’ continued Mrs.", "돈 많은 신사라서 아내 두는 비용쯤은 아무것도 아닌 듯 전혀 부담도 안 될 양반이.\""], ["Cluppins, with great volubility; ‘why there ain’t the faintest shade of an excuse for his behaviour!", "클러핀스 부인이 매우 거침없이 말을 이었다. \"그자의 행동에는 변명할 구석이 털끝만큼도 없어요!"], ["Why don’t he marry her?’", "어째서 그 부인과 결혼하지 않는 거죠?\""]], [["‘Ah,’ said Sam, ‘to be sure; that’s the question.’", "\"아.\" 샘이 말했다. \"물론이죠. 바로 그게 문제지요.\""]], [["‘Question, indeed,’ retorted Mrs.", "\"문제고말고요.\" 클러핀스 부인이 받아쳤다."], ["Cluppins, ‘she’d question him, if she’d my spirit.", "\"내가 가진 기개가 그 부인에게 있었다면, 그 작자를 문초했을 거예요. 하지만 어쨌든, 우리 여자들에게도 법이라는 게 있지요."], ["Hows’ever, there is law for us women, mis’rable creeturs as they’d make us, if they could; and that your master will find out, young man, to his cost, afore he’s six months older.’", "그자들은 할 수만 있다면 우리를 비참한 처지로 만들려 들겠지만요. 그리고 그건 자네 주인이, 젊은이, 반년이 채 지나기 전에 호된 대가를 치르고서 알게 될 거예요.\""]], [["At this consolatory reflection, Mrs. Cluppins bridled up, and smiled at Mrs. Sanders, who smiled back again.", "이 위안 어린 훈계를 하면서, 클러핀스 부인은 거드름을 피우며 샌더스 부인에게 미소를 지었고, 샌더스 부인도 마주 미소로 답했다."]], [["‘The action’s going on, and no mistake,’ thought Sam, as Mrs.", "'소송은 진행 중이로군, 틀림없어.'"], ["Bardell re-entered with the receipt.", "바델 부인이 영수증을 들고 다시 들어올 때 샘은 그렇게 생각했다."]], [["‘Here’s the receipt, Mr.", "\"여기 영수증이에요, 웰러 씨.\" 바델 부인이 말했다."], ["Weller,’ said Mrs.", "\"그리고 여기 거스름돈이고요."], ["Bardell, ‘and here’s the change, and I hope you’ll take a little drop of something to keep the cold out, if it’s only for old acquaintance’ sake, Mr. Weller.’", "그리고 추위도 막을 겸 뭐라도 한 모금 드셨으면 좋겠어요, 옛정을 봐서라도요, 웰러 씨.\""]], [["Sam saw the advantage he should gain, and at once acquiesced; whereupon Mrs.", "샘은 자기가 얻게 될 이점을 알아채고 즉시 응했다. 그러자 바델 부인은 작은 벽장에서 검은 병 하나와 포도주 잔 하나를 꺼냈다."], ["Bardell produced, from a small closet, a black bottle and a wine- glass; and so great was her abstraction, in her deep mental affliction, that, after filling Mr. Weller’s glass, she brought out three more wine- glasses, and filled them too.", "그런데 깊은 심적 고통으로 인한 그녀의 정신없음이 어찌나 심했던지, 웰러 씨의 잔을 채운 뒤에 포도주 잔 세 개를 더 꺼내어 거기에도 가득 따랐다."]], [["‘Lauk, Mrs.", "\"어머나, 바델 부인.\""], ["Bardell,’ said Mrs.", "클러핀스 부인이 말했다."], ["Cluppins, ‘see what you’ve been and done!’", "\"당신이 무슨 짓을 했는지 좀 봐요!\""]], [["‘Well, that is a good one!’ ejaculated Mrs.", "\"어머, 이거 정말 우습네!\""], ["Sanders.", "샌더스 부인이 탄성을 질렀다."]], [["‘Ah, my poor head!’ said Mrs.", "\"아, 내 가엾은 정신머리!\""], ["Bardell, with a faint smile.", "바델 부인이 희미한 미소를 띠며 말했다."]], [["Sam understood all this, of course, so he said at once, that he never could drink before supper, unless a lady drank with him.", "샘은 물론 이 모든 것을 알아챘으므로, 곧바로 자기는 숙녀 분이 함께 마시지 않는 한 결코 야식 전에는 술을 못 마신다고 말했다."], ["A great deal of laughter ensued, and Mrs. Sanders volunteered to humour him, so she took a slight sip out of her glass.", "한바탕 웃음이 터졌고, 샌더스 부인이 그의 비위를 맞춰 주겠다고 자청하여 자기 잔에서 살짝 한 모금 마셨다."], ["Then Sam said it must go all round, so they all took a slight sip. Then little Mrs.", "그러자 샘은 모두 돌아가며 마셔야 한다고 했고, 그래서 다들 살짝 한 모금씩 마셨다."], ["Cluppins proposed as a toast, ‘Success to Bardell agin Pickwick’; and then the ladies emptied their glasses in honour of the sentiment, and got very talkative directly.", "그러고 나서 작은 클러핀스 부인이 \"바델 대 픽윅 소송의 승리를 위하여\"라는 건배사를 제안했고, 그러자 숙녀들은 그 취지를 기려 잔을 비웠으며, 곧장 매우 수다스러워졌다."]], [["‘I suppose you’ve heard what’s going forward, Mr.", "\"무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 들으셨겠죠, 웰러 씨?\""], ["Weller?’ said Mrs. Bardell.", "바델 부인이 말했다."]], [["‘I’ve heerd somethin’ on it,’ replied Sam.", "\"그것에 대해 좀 듣긴 했습니다.\" 샘이 대답했다."]], [["‘It’s a terrible thing to be dragged before the public, in that way, Mr.", "\"그런 식으로 세상 사람들 앞에 끌려 나가는 건 끔찍한 일이에요, 웰러 씨.\""], ["Weller,’ said Mrs.", "바델 부인이 말했다."], ["Bardell; ‘but I see now, that it’s the only thing I ought to do, and my lawyers, Mr.", "\"하지만 이제 알겠어요, 이게 내가 해야 할 단 하나의 길이라는 걸요."], ["Dodson and Fogg, tell me that, with the evidence as we shall call, we must succeed.", "그리고 제 변호사인 도드슨 씨와 포그 씨가 그러는데, 우리가 부를 증거를 가지고서라면 틀림없이 이긴다고 해요."], ["I don’t know what I should do, Mr. Weller, if I didn’t.’", "만약 못 이긴다면, 웰러 씨, 저는 어찌해야 할지 모르겠어요.\""]], [["The mere idea of Mrs. Bardell’s failing in her action, affected Mrs.", "바델 부인이 소송에서 진다는 생각만으로도 샌더스 부인은 어찌나 깊이 충격을 받았던지, 즉시 자기 잔을 다시 채워 다시 비울 수밖에 없었다."], ["Sanders so deeply, that she was under the necessity of refilling and re- emptying her glass immediately; feeling, as she said afterwards, that if she hadn’t had the presence of mind to do so, she must have dropped.", "나중에 그녀가 말하기를, 그렇게 할 마음의 침착함이 없었더라면 자기는 그 자리에서 쓰러지고 말았으리라는 것이었다."]], [["‘Ven is it expected to come on?’ inquired Sam.", "\"언제쯤 재판이 열릴 것 같습니까?\" 샘이 물었다."]], [["‘Either in February or March,’ replied Mrs.", "\"2월 아니면 3월쯤일 거예요.\""], ["Bardell.", "바델 부인이 대답했다."]], [["‘What a number of witnesses there’ll be, won’t there?’ said Mrs.", "\"증인이 얼마나 많이 나올까요, 안 그래요?\""], ["Cluppins.", "클러핀스 부인이 말했다."]], [["‘Ah!", "\"아!"], ["won’t there!’ replied Mrs.", "그렇고말고요!\""], ["Sanders.", "샌더스 부인이 응수했다."]], [["‘And won’t Mr. Dodson and Fogg be wild if the plaintiff shouldn’t get it?’ added Mrs.", "\"그리고 만약 원고가 이기지 못하면 도드슨 씨와 포그 씨가 얼마나 길길이 날뛸까요.\""], ["Cluppins, ‘when they do it all on speculation!’", "클러핀스 부인이 덧붙였다. \"이 일을 죄다 성공 보수로만 맡아 했으니 말이에요!\""]], [["‘Ah!", "\"아!"], ["won’t they!’ said Mrs.", "그렇고말고요!\""], ["Sanders.", "샌더스 부인이 말했다."]], [["‘But the plaintiff must get it,’ resumed Mrs.", "\"하지만 원고가 틀림없이 이길 거예요.\""], ["Cluppins.", "클러핀스 부인이 말을 이었다."]], [["‘I hope so,’ said Mrs.", "\"그러길 바라요.\""], ["Bardell.", "바델 부인이 말했다."]], [["‘Oh, there can’t be any doubt about it,’ rejoined Mrs.", "\"오, 그건 의심할 여지가 없어요.\""], ["Sanders.", "샌더스 부인이 거들었다."]], [["‘Vell,’ said Sam, rising and setting down his glass, ‘all I can say is, that I vish you may get it.’", "\"그럼.\" 샘이 일어서서 잔을 내려놓으며 말했다. \"제가 드릴 말씀은 그저, 부인께서 부디 이기시기를 바란다는 것뿐입니다.\""]], [["‘Thank’ee, Mr. Weller,’ said Mrs.", "\"고마워요, 웰러 씨.\""], ["Bardell fervently.", "바델 부인이 간절히 말했다."]], [["‘And of them Dodson and Foggs, as does these sort o’ things on spec,’ continued Mr.", "\"그리고 이런 일을 성공 보수로 맡아 하는 그 도드슨이니 포그니 하는 작자들이며,\""], ["Weller, ‘as vell as for the other kind and gen’rous people o’ the same purfession, as sets people by the ears, free gratis for nothin’, and sets their clerks to work to find out little disputes among their neighbours and acquaintances as vants settlin’ by means of lawsuits--all I can say o’ them is, that I vish they had the reward I’d give ‘em.’", "웰러 씨가 말을 이었다. \"또 그와 같은 직업에 종사하면서 친절하고 너그러운 척하며, 공짜로 거저 사람들을 서로 싸움 붙이고, 자기 서기들을 시켜 이웃이나 지인들 사이에 소송으로 해결해야 할 자그마한 분쟁거리를 찾아내게 하는 그 종류의 사람들 말입니다만—제가 그들에 대해 드릴 수 있는 말은 그저, 제가 그들에게 주고 싶은 보상을 그들이 받았으면 좋겠다는 것뿐입니다.\""]], [["‘Ah, I wish they had the reward that every kind and generous heart would be inclined to bestow upon them!’ said the gratified Mrs.", "\"아, 친절하고 너그러운 마음을 가진 사람이라면 누구나 그들에게 베풀고 싶어 할 그런 보상을 그들이 받았으면 좋겠어요!\""], ["Bardell.", "흡족해진 바델 부인이 말했다."]], [["‘Amen to that,’ replied Sam, ‘and a fat and happy liven’ they’d get out of it!", "\"그 말씀에 아멘입니다.\" 샘이 대답했다."], ["Wish you good-night, ladies.’", "\"그러면 그자들은 거기서 살지고 행복한 생계를 얻게 될 테니까요! 그럼 안녕히 주무십시오, 숙녀 여러분.\""]], [["To the great relief of Mrs.", "샌더스 부인으로서는 무척 다행스럽게도, 샘은 안주인 쪽에서 족발이나 구운 치즈에 관해 아무런 언급도 없이 떠나도록 허락받았다."], ["Sanders, Sam was allowed to depart without any reference, on the part of the hostess, to the pettitoes and toasted cheese; to which the ladies, with such juvenile assistance as Master Bardell could afford, soon afterwards rendered the amplest justice-- indeed they wholly vanished before their strenuous exertions.", "그리고 그 음식들에는, 바델 도련님이 거들 수 있는 어린아이다운 도움까지 더해, 숙녀들이 곧이어 더없이 충분한 정당한 대접을 해 주었다—실로 그것들은 그들의 맹렬한 분투 앞에 깡그리 사라져 버렸다."]], [["Mr. Weller wended his way back to the George and Vulture, and faithfully recounted to his master, such indications of the sharp practice of Dodson & Fogg, as he had contrived to pick up in his visit to Mrs.", "웰러 씨는 조지 앤드 벌처로 발걸음을 되돌려, 바델 부인 댁을 방문하는 동안 용케 알아낸 도드슨과 포그의 교활한 술책의 정황들을 주인에게 충실히 전했다."], ["Bardell’s. An interview with Mr.", "이튿날 퍼커 씨와의 면담은 웰러 씨의 진술을 충분히 확증하고도 남았다."], ["Perker, next day, more than confirmed Mr. Weller’s statement; and Mr.", "그리하여 픽윅 씨는, 두어 석 달 뒤에는 혼인 약속 위반으로 인해 입은 손해에 대한 배상 청구 소송이 민사 법원에서 공개적으로 재판에 부쳐지리라는 유쾌한 예상을 안고, 딩글리 델에서의 크리스마스 방문을 준비할 수밖에 없었다."], ["Pickwick was fain to prepare for his Christmas visit to Dingley Dell, with the pleasant anticipation that some two or three months afterwards, an action brought against him for damages sustained by reason of a breach of promise of marriage, would be publicly tried in the Court of Common Pleas; the plaintiff having all the advantages derivable, not only from the force of circumstances, but from the sharp practice of Dodson & Fogg to boot.", "원고는 정황의 위력에서 비롯되는 온갖 이점뿐 아니라, 거기에 더해 도드슨과 포그의 교활한 술책에서 비롯되는 이점까지 모조리 누리고 있었다."]], [["CHAPTER XXVII. SAMUEL WELLER MAKES A PILGRIMAGE TO DORKING, AND BEHOLDS HIS MOTHER-IN-LAW\nThere still remaining an interval of two days before the time agreed upon for the departure of the Pickwickians to Dingley Dell, Mr.", "픽윅 회원들이 딩글리 델로 출발하기로 약속한 날까지는 아직 이틀의 여유가 남아 있었으므로, 웰러 씨는 이른 점심을 먹은 뒤 조지 앤드 벌처의 뒷방에 자리를 잡고 앉아 자신의 시간을 가장 잘 보낼 방도를 곰곰이 궁리하였다."], ["Weller sat himself down in a back room at the George and Vulture, after eating an early dinner, to muse on the best way of disposing of his time.", "유난히 화창한 날이었다. 그런데 그 문제를 마음속으로 십 분도 채 굴려 보기 전에, 그는 갑자기 효심과 애정에 사로잡혔다."], ["It was a remarkably fine day; and he had not turned the matter over in his mind ten minutes, when he was suddenly stricken filial and affectionate; and it occurred to him so strongly that he ought to go down and see his father, and pay his duty to his mother-in-law, that he was lost in astonishment at his own remissness in never thinking of this moral obligation before.", "마땅히 내려가서 아버지를 뵙고 새어머니께 도리를 다해야 한다는 생각이 어찌나 강하게 떠오르던지, 이제껏 이런 도덕적 의무를 한 번도 떠올려 보지 못한 자신의 게으름에 그는 그만 망연자실하고 말았다."], ["Anxious to atone for his past neglect without another hour’s delay, he straightway walked upstairs to Mr. Pickwick, and requested leave of absence for this laudable purpose.", "한 시간이라도 더 미루지 않고 지난날의 소홀함을 메우려는 마음이 간절하여, 그는 곧장 위층 픽윅 씨에게로 걸어 올라가 이 갸륵한 목적을 위한 휴가를 청하였다."]], [["‘Certainly, Sam, certainly,’ said Mr.", "\"물론이지, 샘, 물론이고말고,\""], ["Pickwick, his eyes glistening with delight at this manifestation of filial feeling on the part of his attendant; ‘certainly, Sam.’", "픽윅 씨는 자기 시중꾼에게서 이런 효심의 발로가 나타난 것에 기뻐서 두 눈을 반짝이며 말하였다. \"물론이지, 샘.\""]], [["Mr. Weller made a grateful bow.", "웰러 씨는 고마워하며 고개를 숙여 인사하였다."]], [["‘I am very glad to see that you have so high a sense of your duties as a son, Sam,’ said Mr.", "\"네가 아들로서의 도리를 그토록 높이 여기는 것을 보니 나는 무척 기쁘구나, 샘,\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말하였다."]], [["‘I always had, sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"늘 그래 왔습니다요, 나리,\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 대답하였다."]], [["‘That’s a very gratifying reflection, Sam,’ said Mr.", "\"그것 참 흐뭇한 생각이로구나, 샘,\""], ["Pickwick approvingly.", "픽윅 씨가 흡족해하며 말하였다."]], [["‘Wery, Sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"그렇습죠, 나리,\""], ["Weller; ‘if ever I wanted anythin’ o’ my father, I always asked for it in a wery ‘spectful and obligin’ manner.", "웰러 씨가 대답하였다. \"저는 아버지한테 뭐가 필요하면 늘 아주 공손하고 싹싹하게 청을 드렸습니다요."], ["If he didn’t give it me, I took it, for fear I should be led to do anythin’ wrong, through not havin’ it.", "그래도 안 주시면, 그걸 못 가져서 무슨 나쁜 짓이라도 저지를까 봐 제가 그냥 가져왔습죠."], ["I saved him a world o’ trouble this vay, Sir.’", "이런 식으로 저는 아버지의 골칫거리를 엄청나게 덜어 드렸습니다요, 나리.\""]], [["‘That’s not precisely what I meant, Sam,’ said Mr.", "\"내가 말한 건 꼭 그런 뜻이 아니었다, 샘,\""], ["Pickwick, shaking his head, with a slight smile.", "픽윅 씨가 가벼운 미소를 띠며 고개를 저었다."]], [["‘All good feelin’, sir--the wery best intentions, as the gen’l’m’n said ven he run away from his wife ‘cos she seemed unhappy with him,’ replied Mr.", "\"다 좋은 마음에서 한 거죠, 나리—더할 나위 없이 좋은 뜻이었습니다요, 자기 곁에서 마누라가 불행해 보인다고 마누라를 버리고 도망친 신사 양반이 한 말처럼요,\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 대답하였다."]], [["‘You may go, Sam,’ said Mr.", "\"가도 좋다, 샘,\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말하였다."]], [["‘Thank’ee, Sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"고맙습니다요, 나리,\""], ["Weller; and having made his best bow, and put on his best clothes, Sam planted himself on the top of the Arundel coach, and journeyed on to Dorking.", "웰러 씨가 대답하였다. 그리고 정중하게 절을 올리고 가장 좋은 옷을 차려입은 뒤, 샘은 어런들 마차 지붕 위에 자리를 잡고 도킹을 향해 길을 떠났다."]], [["The Marquis of Granby, in Mrs. Weller’s time, was quite a model of a roadside public-house of the better class--just large enough to be convenient, and small enough to be snug.", "마퀴스 오브 그랜비는 웰러 부인이 운영하던 시절에 더 나은 부류의 길가 선술집의 본보기라 할 만한 곳이었다—편리할 만큼은 충분히 크고, 아늑할 만큼은 충분히 작았다."], ["On the opposite side of the road was a large sign-board on a high post, representing the head and shoulders of a gentleman with an apoplectic countenance, in a red coat with deep blue facings, and a touch of the same blue over his three- cornered hat, for a sky.", "길 건너편에는 높은 기둥에 큼직한 간판이 걸려 있었는데, 거기에는 짙은 파란 깃을 단 붉은 외투 차림에, 중풍 든 듯한 얼굴을 한 신사의 머리와 어깨가 그려져 있었고, 삼각모 위로도 같은 파란빛을 살짝 칠해 하늘을 표현해 놓았다."], ["Over that again were a pair of flags; beneath the last button of his coat were a couple of cannon; and the whole formed an expressive and undoubted likeness of the Marquis of Granby of glorious memory.", "그 위로는 다시 깃발 한 쌍이 있었고, 외투의 맨 아랫단추 밑에는 대포 두 문이 자리했으니, 그 전체가 영광스러운 기억 속의 마퀴스 오브 그랜비를 표현한, 의심할 바 없이 생생한 초상이었다."], ["The bar window displayed a choice collection of geranium plants, and a well-dusted row of spirit phials.", "술집 창문에는 제라늄 화분이 정성껏 골라 진열되어 있었고, 먼지 하나 없이 닦인 양주병들이 한 줄로 늘어서 있었다."], ["The open shutters bore a variety of golden inscriptions, eulogistic of good beds and neat wines; and the choice group of countrymen and hostlers lounging about the stable door and horse-trough, afforded presumptive proof of the excellent quality of the ale and spirits which were sold within.", "활짝 열린 덧문에는 좋은 침대와 산뜻한 포도주를 칭송하는 금빛 글귀가 갖가지로 적혀 있었으며, 마구간 문과 말 물통 주위를 어슬렁거리는 시골 사람들과 마부들의 그럴듯한 무리는 그 안에서 파는 에일과 양주가 일품이라는 사실을 그럴싸하게 증언하고 있었다."], ["Sam Weller paused, when he dismounted from the coach, to note all these little indications of a thriving business, with the eye of an experienced traveller; and having done so, stepped in at once, highly satisfied with everything he had observed.", "샘 웰러는 마차에서 내리며 잠시 멈춰 서서, 노련한 여행자의 눈으로 장사가 잘되고 있음을 보여 주는 이 자잘한 징표들을 모두 살폈다. 그러고 나서 자기가 본 모든 것에 흡족해하며 곧장 안으로 들어섰다."]], [["‘Now, then!’ said a shrill female voice the instant Sam thrust his head in at the door, ‘what do you want, young man?’", "\"자, 이봐요!\" 샘이 문 안으로 머리를 들이미는 순간, 날카로운 여자 목소리가 들려왔다. \"뭘 원하는 거예요, 젊은이?\""]], [["Sam looked round in the direction whence the voice proceeded.", "샘은 목소리가 나는 쪽으로 둘러보았다."], ["It came from a rather stout lady of comfortable appearance, who was seated beside the fireplace in the bar, blowing the fire to make the kettle boil for tea.", "그것은 편안해 보이는 인상의 다소 통통한 부인에게서 나온 것이었는데, 부인은 술집 벽난로 곁에 앉아 차 끓일 주전자 물을 데우려고 불을 불고 있었다."], ["She was not alone; for on the other side of the fireplace, sitting bolt upright in a high-backed chair, was a man in threadbare black clothes, with a back almost as long and stiff as that of the chair itself, who caught Sam’s most particular and especial attention at once.", "부인은 혼자가 아니었다. 벽난로 반대편 등받이 높은 의자에 꼿꼿이 앉아 있는 사내가 있었으니, 닳아빠진 검은 옷을 입은 그자는 등이 의자 등받이만큼이나 길고 뻣뻣해서, 단번에 샘의 각별하고도 특별한 주목을 끌었다."]], [["He was a prim-faced, red-nosed man, with a long, thin countenance, and a semi-rattlesnake sort of eye--rather sharp, but decidedly bad.", "그는 새침한 얼굴에 코가 붉고, 길고 여윈 인상에다 반쯤 방울뱀 같은 눈—다소 날카로우면서도 분명히 음흉한 눈—을 지닌 사내였다."], ["He wore very short trousers, and black cotton stockings, which, like the rest of his apparel, were particularly rusty.", "그는 아주 짧은 바지에 검은 무명 양말을 신고 있었는데, 그 양말은 나머지 옷가지와 마찬가지로 유난히 낡아빠져 있었다."], ["His looks were starched, but his white neckerchief was not, and its long limp ends straggled over his closely-buttoned waistcoat in a very uncouth and unpicturesque fashion.", "그의 표정은 빳빳하게 풀먹인 듯했으나, 흰 목도리만은 그렇지 못해, 그 길고 축 처진 끝자락이 단단히 단추 채운 조끼 위로 아주 보기 흉하고 멋없게 늘어져 있었다."], ["A pair of old, worn, beaver gloves, a broad-brimmed hat, and a faded green umbrella, with plenty of whalebone sticking through the bottom, as if to counterbalance the want of a handle at the top, lay on a chair beside him; and, being disposed in a very tidy and careful manner, seemed to imply that the red-nosed man, whoever he was, had no intention of going away in a hurry.", "낡고 닳은 비버 가죽 장갑 한 켤레와 챙 넓은 모자, 그리고 빛바랜 초록색 우산—맨 위에 손잡이가 없는 것을 메우기라도 하려는 듯 바닥으로는 고래수염이 잔뜩 삐져나온 우산—이 그 곁의 의자 위에 놓여 있었다. 그것들이 아주 단정하고 조심스럽게 정돈되어 있는 것을 보니, 이 붉은 코 사내가 누구든 간에 서둘러 떠날 생각은 없는 듯하였다."]], [["To do the red-nosed man justice, he would have been very far from wise if he had entertained any such intention; for, to judge from all appearances, he must have been possessed of a most desirable circle of acquaintance, if he could have reasonably expected to be more comfortable anywhere else.", "붉은 코 사내를 공정하게 평하자면, 그가 그런 생각을 품었다면 그건 결코 현명한 일이 못 되었을 것이다. 무릇 모든 정황으로 미루어 보건대, 그가 다른 어디에서든 이보다 더 편안할 거라 기대할 수 있었다면, 그는 더없이 바람직한 교우 관계를 누리고 있었음에 틀림없으니 말이다."], ["The fire was blazing brightly under the influence of the bellows, and the kettle was singing gaily under the influence of both.", "풀무질 덕분에 불은 환하게 타오르고 있었고, 그 둘의 영향으로 주전자는 흥겹게 노래하고 있었다."], ["A small tray of tea-things was arranged on the table; a plate of hot buttered toast was gently simmering before the fire; and the red-nosed man himself was busily engaged in converting a large slice of bread into the same agreeable edible, through the instrumentality of a long brass toasting-fork.", "작은 쟁반에 찻그릇이 차려져 있었고, 뜨겁게 버터 바른 토스트 한 접시가 불 앞에서 부드럽게 지글거리고 있었다. 그리고 붉은 코 사내는 긴 놋쇠 토스트 포크를 매개 삼아, 두툼한 빵 한 조각을 바로 그 먹음직스러운 음식으로 바꾸는 일에 분주하였다."], ["Beside him stood a glass of reeking hot pine-apple rum-and-water, with a slice of lemon in it; and every time the red-nosed man stopped to bring the round of toast to his eye, with the view of ascertaining how it got on, he imbibed a drop or two of the hot pine-apple rum-and-water, and smiled upon the rather stout lady, as she blew the fire.", "그의 곁에는 김이 무럭무럭 나는 뜨거운 파인애플 럼주 물 한 잔이 레몬 한 조각과 함께 놓여 있었는데, 붉은 코 사내는 토스트가 어떻게 구워지는지 살피려고 빵 한 바퀴를 눈앞으로 가져올 때마다, 그 뜨거운 파인애플 럼주 물을 한두 모금씩 들이켜고는, 불을 불고 있는 다소 통통한 부인을 향해 미소를 지었다."]], [["Sam was so lost in the contemplation of this comfortable scene, that he suffered the first inquiry of the rather stout lady to pass unheeded.", "샘은 이 안락한 광경을 들여다보는 데 어찌나 빠져 있었던지, 다소 통통한 부인의 첫 질문을 흘려듣고 말았다."], ["It was not until it had been twice repeated, each time in a shriller tone, that he became conscious of the impropriety of his behaviour.", "그 질문이 두 번이나 되풀이되고, 그때마다 더 날카로운 목소리로 거듭되고 나서야, 그는 비로소 자기 행동이 무례했음을 깨달았다."]], [["‘Governor in?’ inquired Sam, in reply to the question.", "\"주인장 계십니까?\" 샘이 그 물음에 대답 삼아 물었다."]], [["‘No, he isn’t,’ replied Mrs.", "\"아니요, 안 계세요,\""], ["Weller; for the rather stout lady was no other than the quondam relict and sole executrix of the dead-and-gone Mr.", "웰러 부인이 대답하였다. 그 다소 통통한 부인이란 다름 아닌, 세상을 떠난 클라크 씨의 옛 미망인이자 유일한 유언집행인이었다."], ["Clarke; ‘no, he isn’t, and I don’t expect him, either.’", "\"아니요, 안 계시고, 오시리라 기대하지도 않아요.\""]], [["‘I suppose he’s drivin’ up to-day?’ said Sam.", "\"오늘 마차 몰고 올라오시지 않습니까요?\" 샘이 말하였다."]], [["‘He may be, or he may not,’ replied Mrs.", "\"올지도 모르고, 안 올지도 모르죠,\""], ["Weller, buttering the round of toast which the red-nosed man had just finished.", "웰러 부인이 붉은 코 사내가 막 구워 낸 토스트에 버터를 바르며 대답하였다."], ["‘I don’t know, and, what’s more, I don’t care.--Ask a blessin’, Mr. Stiggins.’", "\"나는 모르고, 게다가 알 바도 아니에요.—축복 기도를 올려 주세요, 스티긴스 씨.\""]], [["The red-nosed man did as he was desired, and instantly commenced on the toast with fierce voracity.", "붉은 코 사내는 청을 받은 대로 하더니, 곧장 무서운 식탐으로 토스트를 먹기 시작하였다."]], [["The appearance of the red-nosed man had induced Sam, at first sight, to more than half suspect that he was the deputy-shepherd of whom his estimable parent had spoken.", "붉은 코 사내의 생김새는 샘으로 하여금 첫눈에, 그자가 자신의 존경하는 아버지가 말한 적 있는 그 부목자(副牧者)가 아닐까 하는 의심을 절반 넘게 품게 하였다."], ["The moment he saw him eat, all doubt on the subject was removed, and he perceived at once that if he purposed to take up his temporary quarters where he was, he must make his footing good without delay.", "그자가 먹는 모습을 보는 순간, 그 점에 관한 모든 의심이 사라지고, 샘은 곧 만약 자기가 지금 있는 이곳에 잠시 머물 작정이라면 지체 없이 자기 자리를 단단히 굳혀 두어야 함을 깨달았다."], ["He therefore commenced proceedings by putting his arm over the half-door of the bar, coolly unbolting it, and leisurely walking in.", "그래서 그는 술집 반쪽 문 너머로 팔을 뻗어 태연하게 빗장을 풀고는 느긋하게 안으로 걸어 들어가는 것으로 일을 시작하였다."]], [["‘Mother-in-law,’ said Sam, ‘how are you?’", "\"새어머니,\" 샘이 말하였다. \"안녕하셨습니까?\""]], [["‘Why, I do believe he is a Weller!’ said Mrs.", "\"어머, 이 사람 정말 웰러 집안 사람이네!\""], ["W., raising her eyes to Sam’s face, with no very gratified expression of countenance.", "웰러 부인이 그다지 반갑지 않은 표정으로 샘의 얼굴을 올려다보며 말하였다."]], [["‘I rayther think he is,’ said the imperturbable Sam; ‘and I hope this here reverend gen’l’m’n ‘ll excuse me saying that I wish I was the Weller as owns you, mother-in-law.’", "\"저도 그런 것 같습니다요,\" 태연한 샘이 말하였다. \"그리고 이 점잖으신 목사 양반께서 부디 너그러이 봐주시기 바랍니다만, 저는 차라리 제가 어머니를 차지한 바로 그 웰러였으면 좋겠습니다요, 새어머니.\""]], [["This was a double-barrelled compliment.", "이것은 일거양득의 찬사였다."], ["It implied that Mrs. Weller was a most agreeable female, and also that Mr.", "웰러 부인이 더없이 매력적인 여인이라는 뜻이면서, 동시에 스티긴스 씨가 성직자다운 풍모를 지녔다는 뜻이기도 했다."], ["Stiggins had a clerical appearance.", "그것은 즉시 눈에 띄는 효과를 냈다."], ["It made a visible impression at once; and Sam followed up his advantage by kissing his mother-in-law.", "그리고 샘은 새어머니에게 입을 맞추는 것으로 그 우세를 한층 밀어붙였다."]], [["‘Get along with you!’ said Mrs.", "\"저리 비켜요!\""], ["Weller, pushing him away.", "웰러 부인이 그를 밀어내며 말하였다."]], [["‘For shame, young man!’ said the gentleman with the red nose.", "\"부끄러운 줄 아시오, 젊은이!\" 붉은 코 신사가 말하였다."]], [["‘No offence, sir, no offence,’ replied Sam; ‘you’re wery right, though; it ain’t the right sort o’ thing, ven mothers-in-law is young and good- looking, is it, Sir?’", "\"기분 상하시게 할 뜻은 없었습니다요, 나리, 정말입니다요,\" 샘이 대답하였다. \"그렇지만 나리 말씀이 지당하십니다요. 새어머니가 젊고 인물이 고우실 때는 그게 마땅한 일이 아니지요, 안 그렇습니까요, 나리?\""]], [["‘It’s all vanity,’ said Mr.", "\"모든 것이 헛되도다,\""], ["Stiggins.", "스티긴스 씨가 말하였다."]], [["‘Ah, so it is,’ said Mrs.", "\"아, 그렇고말고요,\""], ["Weller, setting her cap to rights.", "웰러 부인이 모자를 바로잡으며 말하였다."]], [["Sam thought it was, too, but he held his peace.", "샘도 그렇다고 생각했지만, 입을 다물었다."]], [["The deputy-shepherd seemed by no means best pleased with Sam’s arrival; and when the first effervescence of the compliment had subsided, even Mrs.", "부목자는 샘이 온 것이 도무지 달갑지 않은 눈치였다. 그리고 찬사의 첫 거품이 가라앉고 나자, 웰러 부인조차 샘이 없었더라면 조금도 불편할 게 없었으리라는 듯한 표정이었다."], ["Weller looked as if she could have spared him without the smallest inconvenience. However, there he was; and as he couldn’t be decently turned out, they all three sat down to tea.", "그러나 어쨌든 그는 거기 있었고, 점잖게 내쫓을 수도 없는 노릇이라, 셋은 모두 자리에 앉아 차를 마셨다."]], [["‘And how’s father?’ said Sam.", "\"그래, 아버지는 어떻습니까요?\" 샘이 말하였다."]], [["At this inquiry, Mrs. Weller raised her hands, and turned up her eyes, as if the subject were too painful to be alluded to.", "이 물음에, 웰러 부인은 마치 그 화제가 입에 올리기에 너무도 고통스럽다는 듯, 두 손을 들어 올리고 눈을 치떴다."]], [["Mr. Stiggins groaned.", "스티긴스 씨가 신음 소리를 냈다."]], [["‘What’s the matter with that ‘ere gen’l’m’n?’ inquired Sam.", "\"저 양반 무슨 일이랍니까요?\" 샘이 물었다."]], [["‘He’s shocked at the way your father goes on in,’ replied Mrs.", "\"네 아버지가 처신하는 꼴에 충격을 받으신 거란다,\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 부인이 대답하였다."]], [["‘Oh, he is, is he?’ said Sam.", "\"아, 그렇습니까요?\" 샘이 말하였다."]], [["‘And with too good reason,’ added Mrs.", "\"게다가 그럴 만한 이유가 너무도 충분하지,\""], ["Weller gravely.", "웰러 부인이 엄숙하게 덧붙였다."]], [["Mr. Stiggins took up a fresh piece of toast, and groaned heavily.", "스티긴스 씨는 새 토스트 한 조각을 집어 들고 묵직하게 신음하였다."]], [["‘He is a dreadful reprobate,’ said Mrs.", "\"그 사람은 끔찍한 난봉꾼이야,\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 부인이 말하였다."]], [["‘A man of wrath!’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"진노의 사람이라!\" 스티긴스 씨가 외쳤다."], ["Stiggins. He took a large semi-circular bite out of the toast, and groaned again.", "그는 토스트를 반원 모양으로 큼직하게 한 입 베어 물고는 다시 신음하였다."]], [["Sam felt very strongly disposed to give the reverend Mr.", "샘은 목사 스티긴스 씨에게 신음할 거리를 한번 만들어 주고 싶은 마음이 굴뚝같았으나, 그 충동을 억누르고 그저 이렇게만 물었다."], ["Stiggins something to groan for, but he repressed his inclination, and merely asked, ‘What’s the old ‘un up to now?’", "\"영감님이 지금 무슨 짓을 하고 계십니까요?\""]], [["‘Up to, indeed!’ said Mrs.", "\"무슨 짓이라니, 원!\""], ["Weller, ‘Oh, he has a hard heart.", "웰러 부인이 말하였다. \"오, 그이는 마음이 모질어."], ["Night after night does this excellent man--don’t frown, Mr.", "밤이면 밤마다 이 훌륭한 분이—얼굴 찌푸리지 마세요, 스티긴스 씨."], ["Stiggins; I will say you are an excellent man--come and sit here, for hours together, and it has not the least effect upon him.’", "당신은 정말 훌륭한 분이라고 나는 말할 거예요—여기 와서 몇 시간씩 앉아 계신데도, 그이한테는 눈곱만큼도 효과가 없으니.\""]], [["Well, that is odd,’ said Sam; ‘it ‘ud have a wery considerable effect upon me, if I wos in his place; I know that.’", "\"거참, 그것 이상하네요,\" 샘이 말하였다. \"제가 그 양반 처지였더라면 저한테는 아주 상당한 효과가 있었을 텐데 말입니다요. 그건 제가 압니다요.\""]], [["‘The fact is, my young friend,’ said Mr.", "\"실은 말이오, 젊은 친구,\""], ["Stiggins solemnly, ‘he has an obderrate bosom.", "스티긴스 씨가 엄숙하게 말하였다."], ["Oh, my young friend, who else could have resisted the pleading of sixteen of our fairest sisters, and withstood their exhortations to subscribe to our noble society for providing the infant negroes in the West Indies with flannel waistcoats and moral pocket- handkerchiefs?’", "\"그분은 완고한 가슴을 지녔소이다. 오, 젊은 친구여, 그분이 아니라면 그 누가, 우리의 가장 어여쁜 자매 열여섯 분의 간청을 뿌리치고, 서인도제도의 검둥이 아기들에게 플란넬 조끼와 도덕적 손수건을 마련해 주려는 우리의 고귀한 협회에 기부하라는 그분들의 권유를 끝까지 거부할 수 있었겠소?\""]], [["‘What’s a moral pocket-ankercher?’ said Sam; ‘I never see one o’ them articles o’ furniter.’", "\"도덕적 손수건이라는 게 뭡니까요?\" 샘이 말하였다. \"저는 그런 가구 같은 물건은 본 적이 없는데요.\""]], [["‘Those which combine amusement With instruction, my young friend,’ replied Mr.", "\"오락과 교훈을 한데 아우른 것이라오, 젊은 친구,\" 스티긴스 씨가 대답하였다."], ["Stiggins, ‘blending select tales with wood-cuts.’", "\"엄선한 이야기에 목판화를 곁들인 것이지요.\""]], [["‘Oh, I know,’ said Sam; ‘them as hangs up in the linen-drapers’ shops, with beggars’ petitions and all that ‘ere upon ‘em?’", "\"아, 알겠습니다요,\" 샘이 말하였다. \"포목점에다 걸어 놓는, 거지들 청원서 같은 게 잔뜩 박혀 있는 그런 거 말입니까요?\""]], [["Mr. Stiggins began a third round of toast, and nodded assent.", "스티긴스 씨는 세 번째 토스트를 먹기 시작하며 그렇다고 고개를 끄덕였다."]], [["‘And he wouldn’t be persuaded by the ladies, wouldn’t he?’ said Sam.", "\"그래서 그 부인들 말에도 넘어가지 않으셨다는 겁니까요?\" 샘이 말하였다."]], [["‘Sat and smoked his pipe, and said the infant negroes were--what did he say the infant negroes were?’ said Mrs.", "\"앉아서 파이프 담배만 피우면서, 검둥이 아기들이란—그이가 검둥이 아기들이 뭐라고 했더라?\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 부인이 말하였다."]], [["‘Little humbugs,’ replied Mr.", "\"하찮은 사기꾼들이라 했지요,\""], ["Stiggins, deeply affected.", "스티긴스 씨가 깊이 마음 상한 표정으로 대답하였다."]], [["‘Said the infant negroes were little humbugs,’ repeated Mrs.", "\"검둥이 아기들이 하찮은 사기꾼들이라 했다지,\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 부인이 되풀이하였다."], ["And they both groaned at the atrocious conduct of the elder Mr. Weller.", "그리고 두 사람은 늙은 웰러 씨의 흉악한 행실에 함께 신음하였다."]], [["A great many more iniquities of a similar nature might have been disclosed, only the toast being all eaten, the tea having got very weak, and Sam holding out no indications of meaning to go, Mr. Stiggins suddenly recollected that he had a most pressing appointment with the shepherd, and took himself off accordingly.", "이와 비슷한 죄상들이 훨씬 더 많이 폭로될 수도 있었으나, 토스트가 다 떨어지고 차는 아주 싱거워진 데다, 샘이 갈 기색이라곤 조금도 내비치지 않자, 스티긴스 씨는 문득 자기가 목자와 더없이 긴급한 약속이 있음을 떠올리고 그에 따라 자리를 떠났다."]], [["The tea-things had been scarcely put away, and the hearth swept up, when the London coach deposited Mr.", "찻그릇이 채 치워지고 난로 바닥이 쓸리기도 전에, 런던행 마차가 늙은 웰러 씨를 문간에 부려 놓았다."], ["Weller, senior, at the door; his legs deposited him in the bar; and his eyes showed him his son.", "그의 두 다리는 그를 술집 안에 부려 놓았고, 그의 두 눈은 그에게 아들을 보여 주었다."]], [["‘What, Sammy!’ exclaimed the father.", "\"아니, 새미!\" 아버지가 외쳤다."]], [["‘What, old Nobs!’ ejaculated the son.", "\"아니, 영감님!\" 아들이 소리쳤다."], ["And they shook hands heartily.", "그리고 둘은 정답게 악수를 나누었다."]], [["‘Wery glad to see you, Sammy,’ said the elder Mr.", "\"너를 보니 참 반갑구나, 새미,\""], ["Weller, ‘though how you’ve managed to get over your mother-in-law, is a mystery to me.", "늙은 웰러 씨가 말하였다. \"그나저나 네가 새어머니를 어떻게 구워삶았는지는 나한테는 수수께끼야."], ["I only vish you’d write me out the receipt, that’s all.’", "그 비법을 좀 적어 줬으면 좋겠다, 그뿐이야.\""]], [["‘Hush!’ said Sam, ‘she’s at home, old feller.’", "\"쉿!\" 샘이 말하였다. \"어머니 집에 계세요, 영감님.\""]], [["She ain’t vithin hearin’,’ replied Mr.", "\"들릴 데 있는 게 아니야,\" 웰러 씨가 대답하였다."], ["Weller; ‘she always goes and blows up, downstairs, for a couple of hours arter tea; so we’ll just give ourselves a damp, Sammy.’", "\"네 어머니는 차 마신 뒤에 늘 아래층에 내려가서 두어 시간씩 화풀이를 하거든. 그러니 우리 한잔 축이자꾸나, 새미.\""]], [["Saying this, Mr. Weller mixed two glasses of spirits-and-water, and produced a couple of pipes.", "이렇게 말하면서 웰러 씨는 양주에 물을 탄 술을 두 잔 만들고, 파이프 두 개를 꺼냈다."], ["The father and son sitting down opposite each other; Sam on one side of the fire, in the high-backed chair, and Mr. Weller, senior, on the other, in an easy ditto, they proceeded to enjoy themselves with all due gravity.", "아버지와 아들은 마주 보고 앉았으니, 샘은 불 한쪽 편의 등받이 높은 의자에 앉고, 늙은 웰러 씨는 반대편의 안락의자에 앉아, 둘은 마땅한 진중함을 갖추고 한껏 즐기기 시작하였다."]], [["‘Anybody been here, Sammy?’ asked Mr.", "\"누구 왔다 갔니, 새미?\""], ["Weller, senior, dryly, after a long silence.", "늙은 웰러 씨가 한참 침묵하다가 무심하게 물었다."]], [["Sam nodded an expressive assent.", "샘은 의미심장하게 고개를 끄덕여 그렇다고 답하였다."]], [["‘Red-nosed chap?’ inquired Mr.", "\"코 빨간 녀석이지?\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 물었다."]], [["Sam nodded again.", "샘은 다시 고개를 끄덕였다."]], [["‘Amiable man that ‘ere, Sammy,’ said Mr.", "\"붙임성 좋은 사람이지, 그 녀석,\""], ["Weller, smoking violently.", "웰러 씨가 거세게 담배를 피우며 말하였다."]], [["‘Seems so,’ observed Sam.", "\"그런 것 같습디다요,\" 샘이 말하였다."]], [["‘Good hand at accounts,’ said Mr.", "\"셈에 밝은 친구야,\" 웰러 씨가 말하였다."], ["Weller.", "\"그렇습니까요?\""], ["‘Is he?’ said Sam.", "샘이 말하였다."]], [["‘Borrows eighteenpence on Monday, and comes on Tuesday for a shillin’ to make it up half-a-crown; calls again on Vensday for another half-crown to make it five shillin’s; and goes on, doubling, till he gets it up to a five pund note in no time, like them sums in the ‘rithmetic book ‘bout the nails in the horse’s shoes, Sammy.’", "\"월요일에 십팔 펜스를 꿔 가고는, 화요일에 와서 일 실링을 더해 반 크라운을 채우고, 수요일에 다시 와서 반 크라운을 더 받아 오 실링을 만들고, 그렇게 자꾸 곱절로 늘려 가다 보면 눈 깜짝할 새에 오 파운드짜리 지폐까지 채운다니까, 새미. 산수책에 나오는 그 말발굽 못 셈하는 문제처럼 말이야.\""]], [["Sam intimated by a nod that he recollected the problem alluded to by his parent.", "샘은 아버지가 말하는 그 문제를 기억한다는 뜻으로 고개를 끄덕였다."]], [["‘So you vouldn’t subscribe to the flannel veskits?’ said Sam, after another interval of smoking.", "\"그래서 플란넬 조끼에는 기부를 안 하셨군요?\" 한참 더 담배를 피우다가 샘이 말하였다."]], [["‘Cert’nly not,’ replied Mr.", "\"당연히 안 했지,\""], ["Weller; ‘what’s the good o’ flannel veskits to the young niggers abroad?", "웰러 씨가 대답하였다. \"저 멀리 있는 어린 검둥이들한테 플란넬 조끼가 무슨 소용이람?"], ["But I’ll tell you what it is, Sammy,’ said Mr.", "그렇지만 내가 무슨 말을 할까, 새미,\""], ["Weller, lowering his voice, and bending across the fireplace; ‘I’d come down wery handsome towards strait veskits for some people at home.’", "웰러 씨가 목소리를 낮추고 벽난로 너머로 몸을 기울이며 말하였다. \"집 안에 있는 어떤 사람들한테 구속복을 입히는 일이라면 내 아주 후하게 내놓겠다.\""]], [["As Mr.", "* 구속복: 원문 strait veskits(straitwaistcoats), 광인을 묶어 두는 옷."], ["Weller said this, he slowly recovered his former position, and winked at his first-born, in a profound manner.", "플란넬 조끼(flannel veskits)와 말장난."]], [["‘It cert’nly seems a queer start to send out pocket-’ankerchers to people as don’t know the use on ‘em,’ observed Sam.", "웰러 씨는 이렇게 말하며 천천히 본래 자세로 돌아오더니, 자기 맏아들에게 의미심장하게 한쪽 눈을 찡긋해 보였다."]], [["‘They’re alvays a-doin’ some gammon of that sort, Sammy,’ replied his father.", "\"손수건 쓸 줄도 모르는 사람들한테 손수건을 보낸다는 건 확실히 이상한 짓 같습니다요,\" 샘이 말하였다."], ["‘T’other Sunday I wos walkin’ up the road, wen who should I see, a-standin’ at a chapel door, with a blue soup-plate in her hand, but your mother-in-law!", "\"그자들은 늘 그런 식으로 무슨 수작을 부린다니까, 새미,\" 그의 아버지가 대답하였다."], ["I werily believe there was change for a couple o’ suv’rins in it, then, Sammy, all in ha’pence; and as the people come out, they rattled the pennies in it, till you’d ha’ thought that no mortal plate as ever was baked, could ha’ stood the wear and tear.", "\"지난 일요일에 내가 한길을 걸어 올라가는데, 글쎄 누가 예배당 문간에 파란 수프 접시를 손에 들고 서 있나 했더니, 바로 네 새어머니였단 말이야! 그 접시 안에는 그때 일 파운드짜리 둘은 족히 거슬러 줄 만큼 잔돈이 들어 있었을걸, 새미, 죄다 반 페니짜리로 말이야."], ["What d’ye think it was all for?’", "그리고 사람들이 나오면서 그 안에 페니를 떨그럭떨그럭 던져 넣는데, 이제껏 구워 낸 그 어떤 접시도 그 닳고 부대끼는 걸 못 견딜 거라 싶을 정도였어. 그게 다 뭣 때문이었는지 알겠니?\""]], [["‘For another tea-drinkin’, perhaps,’ said Sam.", "\"또 차 모임 하려는 거였겠죠,\" 샘이 말하였다."]], [["‘Not a bit on it,’ replied the father; ‘for the shepherd’s water-rate, Sammy.’", "\"천만에,\" 아버지가 대답하였다. \"목자의 수도세 때문이었어, 새미.\""]], [["‘The shepherd’s water-rate!’ said Sam.", "\"목자의 수도세요!\" 샘이 말하였다."]], [["‘Ay,’ replied Mr.", "\"그래,\""], ["Weller, ‘there was three quarters owin’, and the shepherd hadn’t paid a farden, not he--perhaps it might be on account that the water warn’t o’ much use to him, for it’s wery little o’ that tap he drinks, Sammy, wery; he knows a trick worth a good half-dozen of that, he does.", "웰러 씨가 대답하였다. \"세 분기치가 밀려 있었는데, 목자란 작자는 한 푼도 안 냈거든, 정말 안 냈어—어쩌면 물이 그 작자한테는 별 쓸모가 없어서 그랬는지도 모르지, 그 양반은 그 수도꼭지 물은 아주 조금밖에 안 마시니까, 새미, 아주 조금."], ["Hows’ever, it warn’t paid, and so they cuts the water off.", "그 양반은 그보다 여섯 배는 나은 수작을 알고 있거든, 암."], ["Down goes the shepherd to chapel, gives out as he’s a persecuted saint, and says he hopes the heart of the turncock as cut the water off, ‘ll be softened, and turned in the right vay, but he rayther thinks he’s booked for somethin’ uncomfortable.", "어쨌든 돈을 안 내니까, 그래서 물을 끊어 버렸지. 목자가 예배당으로 내려가서는 자기가 박해받는 성도라고 떠벌리고, 물 끊은 수도 검침원의 마음이 누그러져서 올바른 길로 돌아서기를 바란다고 하면서도, 그자는 아무래도 불편한 데로 가도록 예약된 것 같다고 말하지."], ["Upon this, the women calls a meetin’, sings a hymn, wotes your mother-in-law into the chair, wolunteers a collection next Sunday, and hands it all over to the shepherd.", "그러자 여자들이 모임을 열어 찬송가를 부르고, 네 새어머니를 의장으로 뽑고, 다음 일요일에 헌금을 걷자고 자청해서는, 그걸 죄다 목자한테 넘겨준 거야. 그래서 그 작자가 그 여자들한테서 평생 수도회사에 거저 다닐 만큼 충분히 뜯어내지 못했다면,\""], ["And if he ain’t got enough out on ‘em, Sammy, to make him free of the water company for life,’ said Mr.", "웰러 씨가 결론지어 말하였다. \"내가 네덜란드 놈이고 너도 네덜란드 놈인 거다, 그뿐이야.\""], ["Weller, in conclusion, ‘I’m one Dutchman, and you’re another, and that’s all about it.’", "* 네덜란드 놈: I'm a Dutchman, 영어 관용표현으로 \"그렇지 않다면 내 손에 장을 지진다\" 정도의 강한 부정."]], [["Mr. Weller smoked for some minutes in silence, and then resumed--", "웰러 씨는 몇 분 동안 말없이 담배를 피우다가, 다시 입을 열었다—"]], [["‘The worst o’ these here shepherds is, my boy, that they reg’larly turns the heads of all the young ladies, about here.", "\"이 동네 목자들의 가장 못된 점은, 얘야, 그자들이 이 근방 젊은 아가씨들의 머리를 죄다 돌게 만든다는 거야."], ["Lord bless their little hearts, they thinks it’s all right, and don’t know no better; but they’re the wictims o’ gammon, Samivel, they’re the wictims o’ gammon.’", "가엾게도 그 어린것들은 다 옳은 일인 줄 알고 더 나은 걸 모르지. 하지만 그 애들은 수작의 희생자야, 새뮤얼, 수작의 희생자라고.\""]], [["‘I s’pose they are,’ said Sam.", "\"그런 것 같습니다요,\" 샘이 말하였다."]], [["‘Nothin’ else,’ said Mr.", "\"바로 그거지,\""], ["Weller, shaking his head gravely; ‘and wot aggrawates me, Samivel, is to see ‘em a-wastin’ all their time and labour in making clothes for copper-coloured people as don’t want ‘em, and taking no notice of flesh-coloured Christians as do.", "웰러 씨가 무겁게 고개를 저으며 말하였다. \"그리고 나를 약 오르게 하는 건, 새뮤얼, 그자들이 제 시간과 노력을 다 허비해 가며 필요로 하지도 않는 구릿빛 사람들한테 줄 옷을 만들면서, 정작 옷이 필요한 살빛 기독교인들은 거들떠보지도 않는 꼴을 보는 거야."], ["If I’d my vay, Samivel, I’d just stick some o’ these here lazy shepherds behind a heavy wheelbarrow, and run ‘em up and down a fourteen-inch-wide plank all day.", "내 마음대로 할 수만 있다면, 새뮤얼, 이 게으른 목자 놈들을 무거운 외바퀴 손수레 뒤에 매달아 놓고, 폭 십사 인치짜리 널빤지 위를 하루 종일 오르락내리락 굴려 댈 텐데."], ["That ‘ud shake the nonsense out of ‘em, if anythin’ vould.’", "그러면 그 헛소리가 쏙 빠질 거야, 뭐라도 빠진다면 말이지.\""]], [["Mr. Weller, having delivered this gentle recipe with strong emphasis, eked out by a variety of nods and contortions of the eye, emptied his glass at a draught, and knocked the ashes out of his pipe, with native dignity.", "웰러 씨는 이 점잖은 처방을, 강한 어조에다 갖가지 고갯짓과 눈을 찡그리는 표정까지 곁들여 늘어놓고는, 한입에 잔을 비우고, 타고난 위엄을 갖추어 파이프의 재를 털어 냈다."]], [["He was engaged in this operation, when a shrill voice was heard in the passage.", "그가 이 일을 하고 있을 때, 복도에서 날카로운 목소리가 들려왔다."]], [["‘Here’s your dear relation, Sammy,’ said Mr. Weller; and Mrs.", "\"네 사랑하는 친척분이 오신다, 새미,\""], ["W. hurried into the room.", "웰러 씨가 말하자, 웰러 부인이 황급히 방으로 들어왔다."]], [["‘Oh, you’ve come back, have you!’ said Mrs.", "\"오, 돌아오셨군요, 그래요!\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 부인이 말하였다."]], [["‘Yes, my dear,’ replied Mr.", "\"그래, 여보,\""], ["Weller, filling a fresh pipe.", "웰러 씨가 새 파이프에 담배를 채우며 대답하였다."]], [["‘Has Mr. Stiggins been back?’ said Mrs.", "\"스티긴스 씨는 다시 다녀가셨나요?\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 부인이 말하였다."]], [["‘No, my dear, he hasn’t,’ replied Mr.", "\"아니, 여보, 안 왔어,\""], ["Weller, lighting the pipe by the ingenious process of holding to the bowl thereof, between the tongs, a red-hot coal from the adjacent fire; and what’s more, my dear, I shall manage to surwive it, if he don’t come back at all.’", "웰러 씨가 옆 난로에서 시뻘겋게 단 석탄 한 조각을 부젓가락 사이에 끼워 파이프 통에 갖다 대는 기발한 방법으로 불을 붙이며 대답하였다. \"게다가 말이지, 여보, 그자가 영영 안 돌아온다 해도 나는 그럭저럭 견디며 살아남을 거요.\""]], [["‘Ugh, you wretch!’ said Mrs.", "\"으, 이 망할 인간!\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 부인이 말하였다."]], [["‘Thank’ee, my love,’ said Mr.", "\"고맙구려, 내 사랑,\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 말하였다."]], [["‘Come, come, father,’ said Sam, ‘none o’ these little lovin’s afore strangers.", "\"자, 자, 아버지,\" 샘이 말하였다."], ["Here’s the reverend gen’l’m’n a-comin’ in now.’", "\"남들 앞에서 그런 알콩달콩한 짓 그만하세요. 저기 목사 양반이 지금 들어오십니다요.\""]], [["At this announcement, Mrs. Weller hastily wiped off the tears which she had just begun to force on; and Mr.", "이 알림에, 웰러 부인은 막 짜내기 시작한 눈물을 황급히 닦아 냈다."], ["W. drew his chair sullenly into the chimney-corner.", "그리고 웰러 씨는 부루퉁하게 자기 의자를 굴뚝 모퉁이로 끌어당겼다."]], [["Mr. Stiggins was easily prevailed on to take another glass of the hot pine-apple rum-and-water, and a second, and a third, and then to refresh himself with a slight supper, previous to beginning again.", "스티긴스 씨는 뜨거운 파인애플 럼주 물을 한 잔 더, 또 두 잔째, 세 잔째 들도록 쉽게 꾀임에 넘어갔고, 그런 다음 다시 시작하기에 앞서 가벼운 저녁식사로 원기를 돋우었다."], ["He sat on the same side as Mr.", "그는 늙은 웰러 씨와 같은 편에 앉았다."], ["Weller, senior; and every time he could contrive to do so, unseen by his wife, that gentleman indicated to his son the hidden emotions of his bosom, by shaking his fist over the deputy-shepherd’s head; a process which afforded his son the most unmingled delight and satisfaction, the more especially as Mr.", "그리고 아내 눈에 띄지 않게 그렇게 할 수 있을 때마다, 웰러 씨는 부목자의 머리 위로 주먹을 흔들어 자기 가슴속에 숨은 감정을 아들에게 내비쳤다."], ["Stiggins went on, quietly drinking the hot pine-apple rum-and-water, wholly unconscious of what was going forward.", "이 동작은 아들에게 더없이 순수한 기쁨과 만족을 안겨 주었으니, 특히 스티긴스 씨가 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 전혀 모른 채 조용히 뜨거운 파인애플 럼주 물을 들이켜고 있었기에 더욱 그러하였다."]], [["The major part of the conversation was confined to Mrs.", "대화의 대부분은 웰러 부인과 목사 스티긴스 씨에게 국한되었다."], ["Weller and the reverend Mr. Stiggins; and the topics principally descanted on, were the virtues of the shepherd, the worthiness of his flock, and the high crimes and misdemeanours of everybody beside--dissertations which the elder Mr.", "그리고 주로 늘어놓은 화제란 목자의 덕성, 그 양 떼의 갸륵함, 그리고 그 밖의 모든 사람들의 중대한 죄악과 비행이었으니—이런 일장연설을, 늙은 웰러 씨는 워커라는 이름의 신사에 대한 반쯤 억눌린 언급이나 그와 비슷한 갖가지 곁말로 이따금 끼어들어 가로막았다."], ["Weller occasionally interrupted by half-suppressed references to a gentleman of the name of Walker, and other running commentaries of the same kind.", "* 워커(Walker): 당대 영어 속어로, 믿기 힘든 헛소리에 대한 야유성 추임새."]], [["At length Mr. Stiggins, with several most indubitable symptoms of having quite as much pine-apple rum-and-water about him as he could comfortably accommodate, took his hat, and his leave; and Sam was, immediately afterwards, shown to bed by his father.", "마침내 스티긴스 씨는 자기가 편안히 감당할 수 있는 만큼의 파인애플 럼주 물을 이미 몸에 채웠다는, 의심할 여지 없는 여러 징후를 보이며 모자를 집어 들고 작별을 고하였다."], ["The respectable old gentleman wrung his hand fervently, and seemed disposed to address some observation to his son; but on Mrs.", "그리고 그 직후 샘은 아버지의 안내를 받아 잠자리로 갔다."], ["Weller advancing towards him, he appeared to relinquish that intention, and abruptly bade him good-night.", "점잖은 노신사는 아들의 손을 뜨겁게 부여잡고, 무언가 한마디 일러 주려는 기색이었으나, 웰러 부인이 그를 향해 다가오자 그 뜻을 접은 듯, 불쑥 잘 자라는 인사를 건넸다."]], [["Sam was up betimes next day, and having partaken of a hasty breakfast, prepared to return to London.", "이튿날 샘은 일찌감치 일어나, 서둘러 아침을 먹고 런던으로 돌아갈 채비를 하였다."], ["He had scarcely set foot without the house, when his father stood before him.", "그가 미처 집 밖으로 발을 내딛기도 전에, 아버지가 그의 앞에 서 있었다."]], [["‘Goin’, Sammy?’ inquired Mr.", "\"가는 거냐, 새미?\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘Off at once,’ replied Sam.", "\"곧장 떠납니다요,\" 샘이 대답하였다."]], [["‘I vish you could muffle that ‘ere Stiggins, and take him vith you,’ said Mr.", "\"저 스티긴스란 놈을 자루에 처넣어서 너랑 같이 데려갔으면 좋겠다,\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 말하였다."]], [["‘I am ashamed on you!’ said Sam reproachfully; ‘what do you let him show his red nose in the Markis o’ Granby at all, for?’", "\"부끄러운 줄 아세요!\" 샘이 나무라듯 말하였다. \"대체 왜 그 작자가 마퀴스 오브 그랜비에 그 빨간 코를 들이밀도록 내버려 두시는 겁니까요?\""]], [["Mr. Weller the elder fixed on his son an earnest look, and replied, ‘’Cause I’m a married man, Samivel, ‘cause I’m a married man.", "늙은 웰러 씨는 아들에게 진지한 눈길을 던지고 대답하였다."], ["Ven you’re a married man, Samivel, you’ll understand a good many things as you don’t understand now; but vether it’s worth while goin’ through so much, to learn so little, as the charity-boy said ven he got to the end of the alphabet, is a matter o’ taste.", "\"내가 결혼한 몸이니까 그러는 거야, 새뮤얼, 내가 결혼한 몸이니까. 너도 결혼한 몸이 되면, 새뮤얼, 지금은 모르는 많은 것들을 알게 될 거야."], ["I rayther think it isn’t.’", "하지만 그렇게 많은 걸 겪어 가면서 그토록 적은 걸 배우는 게 과연 그만한 값어치가 있는지는, 자선학교 아이가 알파벳 끝까지 다 떼고 나서 한 말마따나, 취향 나름인 게지. 내 생각엔 아무래도 값어치가 없는 것 같다만.\""]], [["Well,’ said Sam, ‘good-bye.’", "\"자,\" 샘이 말하였다. \"안녕히 계세요.\""]], [["‘Tar, tar, Sammy,’ replied his father.", "\"잘 가라, 잘 가, 새미,\" 그의 아버지가 대답하였다."]], [["‘I’ve only got to say this here,’ said Sam, stopping short, ‘that if I was the properiator o’ the Markis o’ Granby, and that ‘ere Stiggins came and made toast in my bar, I’d--’", "\"저는 이 말씀만 드리겠습니다요,\" 샘이 우뚝 멈춰 서며 말하였다. \"제가 만약 마퀴스 오브 그랜비의 주인이고, 그 스티긴스란 놈이 와서 제 술집에서 토스트나 굽고 있다면, 저라면—\""]], [["‘What?’ interposed Mr.", "\"뭘?\""], ["Weller, with great anxiety.", "웰러 씨가 잔뜩 조바심을 내며 끼어들었다."], ["‘What?’", "\"뭘 어쩐다고?\""]], [["‘Pison his rum-and-water,’ said Sam.", "\"그놈 럼주 물에 독을 타겠습니다요,\" 샘이 말하였다."]], [["‘No!’ said Mr.", "\"안 돼!\""], ["Weller, shaking his son eagerly by the hand, ‘would you raly, Sammy-would you, though?’", "웰러 씨가 아들의 손을 와락 흔들며 말하였다. \"정말로 그러겠니, 새미—정말 그럴 테냐?\""]], [["‘I would,’ said Sam.", "\"그러겠습니다요,\" 샘이 말하였다."], ["‘I wouldn’t be too hard upon him at first.", "\"그래도 처음부터 너무 모질게 굴진 않겠습니다요."], ["I’d drop him in the water-butt, and put the lid on; and if I found he was insensible to kindness, I’d try the other persvasion.’", "일단 물통에 빠뜨리고 뚜껑을 덮어 두죠. 그래서 그놈이 친절에도 꿈쩍 않는다 싶으면, 그때 다른 방법으로 설득해 보겠습니다요.\""]], [["The elder Mr. Weller bestowed a look of deep, unspeakable admiration on his son, and, having once more grasped his hand, walked slowly away, revolving in his mind the numerous reflections to which his advice had given rise.", "늙은 웰러 씨는 아들에게 깊고도 형언할 수 없는 감탄의 눈길을 보내고는, 다시 한번 그 손을 부여잡은 뒤, 자기 충고가 불러일으킨 갖가지 상념을 마음속으로 곱씹으며 천천히 걸어갔다."]], [["Sam looked after him, until he turned a corner of the road; and then set forward on his walk to London.", "샘은 아버지가 한길 모퉁이를 돌아 사라질 때까지 그 뒷모습을 바라보았다. 그러고 나서 런던으로 걷는 길에 나섰다."], ["He meditated at first, on the probable consequences of his own advice, and the likelihood of his father’s adopting it.", "그는 처음에는 자기 충고가 가져올 법한 결과와, 아버지가 그것을 받아들일 가능성에 대해 곰곰이 생각하였다."], ["He dismissed the subject from his mind, however, with the consolatory reflection that time alone would show; and this is the reflection we would impress upon the reader.", "그러나 그는 오직 시간만이 알려 주리라는 위안 어린 생각으로 그 문제를 머릿속에서 떨쳐 냈다. 그리고 이것이야말로 우리가 독자에게 새겨 두고 싶은 생각이기도 하다."]], [["CHAPTER XXVIII.", "제28장."], ["A GOOD-HUMOURED CHRISTMAS CHAPTER, CONTAINING AN ACCOUNT OF A WEDDING, AND SOME OTHER SPORTS BESIDE: WHICH ALTHOUGH IN THEIR WAY, EVEN AS GOOD CUSTOMS AS MARRIAGE ITSELF, ARE NOT QUITE SO RELIGIOUSLY KEPT UP, IN THESE DEGENERATE TIMES", "결혼식에 관한 기록과 그 밖의 몇 가지 놀이를 담은 유쾌한 크리스마스 장이니, 그 놀이들은 그 나름으로 결혼 자체만큼이나 좋은 풍습이지만, 이 타락한 시대에는 그만큼 경건하게 지켜지지는 않는다"]], [["As brisk as bees, if not altogether as light as fairies, did the four Pickwickians assemble on the morning of the twenty-second day of December, in the year of grace in which these, their faithfully-recorded adventures, were undertaken and accomplished.", "요정처럼 가뿐하지는 않았더라도 꿀벌만큼이나 활기차게, 네 명의 픽윅 회원들은 12월 22일 아침에 모여들었다. 충실히 기록된 그들의 모험이 시작되고 이루어진 바로 그 은총의 해였다."], ["Christmas was close at hand, in all his bluff and hearty honesty; it was the season of hospitality, merriment, and open-heartedness; the old year was preparing, like an ancient philosopher, to call his friends around him, and amidst the sound of feasting and revelry to pass gently and calmly away.", "크리스마스가 그 무뚝뚝하고도 정 깊은 정직함을 온통 띤 채 코앞에 다가와 있었다. 그것은 환대와 즐거움과 활짝 열린 마음의 계절이었다."], ["Gay and merry was the time; and right gay and merry were at least four of the numerous hearts that were gladdened by its coming.", "묵은해는 마치 옛 철학자처럼 벗들을 둘레에 불러 모으고, 잔치와 환락의 소리 가운데서 부드럽고 고요하게 스러질 채비를 하고 있었다. 흥겹고 즐거운 때였으니, 그 도래로 기뻐진 수많은 가슴들 가운데 적어도 네 가슴은 참으로 흥겹고 즐거웠다."]], [["And numerous indeed are the hearts to which Christmas brings a brief season of happiness and enjoyment.", "그리고 실로 크리스마스가 짧은 행복과 기쁨의 한 철을 가져다주는 가슴들은 수없이 많다."], ["How many families, whose members have been dispersed and scattered far and wide, in the restless struggles of life, are then reunited, and meet once again in that happy state of companionship and mutual goodwill, which is a source of such pure and unalloyed delight; and one so incompatible with the cares and sorrows of the world, that the religious belief of the most civilised nations, and the rude traditions of the roughest savages, alike number it among the first joys of a future condition of existence, provided for the blessed and happy!", "삶의 쉼 없는 분투 속에서 식구들이 멀리멀리 흩어지고 떨어졌던 그 많은 가정들이 이때 다시 모여, 그토록 순수하고 티 없는 기쁨의 원천이 되는, 그리고 세상의 근심과 슬픔과는 도무지 어울리지 않는 그 행복한 동무됨과 서로를 향한 선의의 상태 속에서 다시 한 번 만난다. 그 기쁨은 세상의 근심과 슬픔과 너무도 맞지 않기에, 가장 문명화된 나라들의 종교적 신앙도, 가장 거친 야만인들의 투박한 전승도, 한결같이 그것을 복되고 행복한 자들을 위해 마련된 내세의 첫 기쁨들 가운데 하나로 꼽는다."], ["How many old recollections, and how many dormant sympathies, does Christmas time awaken!", "크리스마스철은 얼마나 많은 옛 추억을, 또 얼마나 많은 잠든 정을 일깨우는가!"]], [["We write these words now, many miles distant from the spot at which, year after year, we met on that day, a merry and joyous circle.", "우리는 지금 이 글을 쓰고 있는바, 해마다 그날이면 우리가 흥겹고 즐거운 무리를 이루어 만나던 그 자리로부터 수십 리 떨어진 곳에 있다."], ["Many of the hearts that throbbed so gaily then, have ceased to beat; many of the looks that shone so brightly then, have ceased to glow; the hands we grasped, have grown cold; the eyes we sought, have hid their lustre in the grave; and yet the old house, the room, the merry voices and smiling faces, the jest, the laugh, the most minute and trivial circumstances connected with those happy meetings, crowd upon our mind at each recurrence of the season, as if the last assemblage had been but yesterday!", "그때 그토록 흥겹게 두근거리던 많은 가슴들이 이제는 멎었고, 그때 그토록 환히 빛나던 많은 눈길들이 이제는 광채를 잃었으며, 우리가 맞잡았던 손들은 차갑게 식었고, 우리가 찾던 눈들은 무덤 속에 그 광채를 감추었다. 그러나 그 옛집, 그 방, 흥겨운 목소리들과 미소 짓던 얼굴들, 농담과 웃음, 그 행복한 만남에 얽힌 더없이 사소하고 자질구레한 정황들이, 그 계절이 돌아올 적마다 마치 마지막 모임이 어제 일이었던 것처럼 우리 마음에 밀려든다!"], ["Happy, happy Christmas, that can win us back to the delusions of our childish days; that can recall to the old man the pleasures of his youth; that can transport the sailor and the traveller, thousands of miles away, back to his own fireside and his quiet home!", "행복하고 행복한 크리스마스여, 우리를 어린 시절의 헛된 꿈으로 되돌려 줄 수 있고, 노인에게 젊은 날의 즐거움을 되살려 줄 수 있으며, 수천 리 떨어진 뱃사람과 나그네를 제 화롯가와 고요한 집으로 실어다 줄 수 있는 크리스마스여!"]], [["But we are so taken up and occupied with the good qualities of this saint Christmas, that we are keeping Mr.", "그러나 우리는 이 성자 크리스마스의 미덕에 너무도 사로잡히고 빠져들어, 픽윅 씨와 그 친구들을 머글턴행 마차 바깥 추위 속에 세워 둔 채 기다리게 하고 있다."], ["Pickwick and his friends waiting in the cold on the outside of the Muggleton coach, which they have just attained, well wrapped up in great-coats, shawls, and comforters.", "그들은 외투며 숄이며 목도리로 단단히 싸맨 채 방금 그 마차에 막 올라탄 참이다."], ["The portmanteaus and carpet-bags have been stowed away, and Mr.", "여행 가방과 융단 가방은 다 실어 두었고, 웰러 씨와 차장은 앞칸에 엄청나게 큰 대구 한 마리를 밀어 넣으려 애쓰고 있다."], ["Weller and the guard are endeavouring to insinuate into the fore- boot a huge cod-fish several sizes too large for it--which is snugly packed up, in a long brown basket, with a layer of straw over the top, and which has been left to the last, in order that he may repose in safety on the half-dozen barrels of real native oysters, all the property of Mr.", "그 대구는 앞칸에 비해 몇 치수나 더 컸는데, 긴 갈색 바구니 속에 짚을 한 켜 덮어 아늑하게 담겨 있었고, 맨 마지막에 싣도록 남겨 두었으니, 픽윅 씨의 소유인 진짜 자연산 굴 여섯 통이 그 칸 바닥에 가지런히 정돈되어 있는 그 위에 안전하게 누여 두기 위함이었다. 웰러 씨와 차장이 대구를 앞칸에 밀어 넣으려고, 머리부터 넣어 보고, 다음엔 꼬리부터, 다음엔 위가 위로 가게, 다음엔 아래가 위로 가게, 다음엔 옆으로, 다음엔 길게 눕혀 가며 온갖 수를 다 쓰는 동안, 픽윅 씨의 낯빛에 떠오른 관심은 더없이 강렬했다."], ["Pickwick, which have been arranged in regular order at the bottom of the receptacle. The interest displayed in Mr.", "그 굽힘 없는 대구는 그 모든 술책에 완강히 버텼으나, 마침내 차장이 우연히 바구니 한복판을 들이치자 대구는 별안간 앞칸 속으로 사라졌고, 그와 함께 차장 자신의 머리와 어깨도 같이 사라졌다."], ["Pickwick’s countenance is most intense, as Mr. Weller and the guard try to squeeze the cod-fish into the boot, first head first, and then tail first, and then top upward, and then bottom upward, and then side-ways, and then long-ways, all of which artifices the implacable cod-fish sturdily resists, until the guard accidentally hits him in the very middle of the basket, whereupon he suddenly disappears into the boot, and with him, the head and shoulders of the guard himself, who, not calculating upon so sudden a cessation of the passive resistance of the cod-fish, experiences a very unexpected shock, to the unsmotherable delight of all the porters and bystanders.", "대구의 소극적 저항이 그토록 갑작스레 멎으리라곤 미처 헤아리지 못했던 그는 자못 뜻밖의 충격을 겪었으니, 짐꾼들과 구경꾼들 모두는 도저히 참을 수 없는 환희에 빠졌다."], ["Upon this, Mr. Pickwick smiles with great good-humour, and drawing a shilling from his waistcoat pocket, begs the guard, as he picks himself out of the boot, to drink his health in a glass of hot brandy-and-water; at which the guard smiles too, and Messrs.", "이에 픽윅 씨는 자못 너그러운 마음으로 미소 지으며 조끼 주머니에서 1실링을 꺼내, 앞칸에서 기어 나오는 차장에게 뜨거운 브랜디 물 한 잔으로 제 건강을 위해 마셔 달라고 청했다."], ["Snodgrass, Winkle, and Tupman, all smile in company. The guard and Mr.", "그 말에 차장도 미소 짓고, 스노드그래스 씨와 윈클 씨와 텁먼 씨도 다 같이 미소 지었다."], ["Weller disappear for five minutes, most probably to get the hot brandy-and-water, for they smell very strongly of it, when they return, the coachman mounts to the box, Mr.", "차장과 웰러 씨는 오 분쯤 사라졌는데, 십중팔구 뜨거운 브랜디 물을 마시러 간 것이었다. 돌아왔을 때 그 냄새가 자못 진동했기 때문이다."], ["Weller jumps up behind, the Pickwickians pull their coats round their legs and their shawls over their noses, the helpers pull the horse-cloths off, the coachman shouts out a cheery ‘All right,’ and away they go.", "마부가 마부석에 올라타고, 웰러 씨가 뒤로 뛰어오르고, 픽윅 회원들이 외투를 다리에 두르고 숄을 코까지 끌어 올리고, 일꾼들이 말 덮개를 벗기고, 마부가 기운차게 \"출발!\" 하고 외치자 그들은 떠나갔다."]], [["They have rumbled through the streets, and jolted over the stones, and at length reach the wide and open country.", "그들은 거리를 덜컹덜컹 지나고 돌길 위를 흔들흔들 넘어, 마침내 드넓고 탁 트인 시골에 다다랐다."], ["The wheels skim over the hard and frosty ground; and the horses, bursting into a canter at a smart crack of the whip, step along the road as if the load behind them-- coach, passengers, cod-fish, oyster-barrels, and all--were but a feather at their heels.", "바퀴들이 단단하고 서리 내린 땅 위를 미끄러져 갔고, 채찍이 날카롭게 한 번 울리자 말들은 구보로 내달아, 마치 그 뒤에 실린 짐 ― 마차며 승객이며 대구며 굴통이며 죄다 ― 이 제 발꿈치에 매달린 깃털 한 올에 지나지 않는다는 듯 길을 따라 성큼성큼 나아갔다."], ["They have descended a gentle slope, and enter upon a level, as compact and dry as a solid block of marble, two miles long.", "그들은 완만한 비탈을 내려가 단단한 대리석 덩이처럼 다부지고 메마른, 이 마일이나 뻗은 평지로 들어섰다."], ["Another crack of the whip, and on they speed, at a smart gallop, the horses tossing their heads and rattling the harness, as if in exhilaration at the rapidity of the motion; while the coachman, holding whip and reins in one hand, takes off his hat with the other, and resting it on his knees, pulls out his handkerchief, and wipes his forehead, partly because he has a habit of doing it, and partly because it’s as well to show the passengers how cool he is, and what an easy thing it is to drive four-in-hand, when you have had as much practice as he has.", "채찍이 또 한 번 울리자 그들은 날쌘 질주로 내달았고, 말들은 그 빠른 움직임에 신이 난 듯 머리를 흔들고 마구를 절그럭거렸다. 한편 마부는 채찍과 고삐를 한 손에 쥔 채 다른 손으로 모자를 벗어 무릎에 얹고는 손수건을 꺼내 이마를 닦았으니, 한편으로는 그것이 버릇이기 때문이요, 한편으로는 자기가 얼마나 태평한지, 그리고 자기만큼 숙달이 되면 사두마차를 모는 게 얼마나 쉬운 일인지를 승객들에게 보여 주는 편이 좋기 때문이었다."], ["Having done this very leisurely (otherwise the effect would be materially impaired), he replaces his handkerchief, pulls on his hat, adjusts his gloves, squares his elbows, cracks the whip again, and on they speed, more merrily than before.", "이 일을 자못 느긋하게 마치고 나서(안 그러면 그 효과가 크게 줄어들 테니), 그는 손수건을 도로 넣고, 모자를 쓰고, 장갑을 매만지고, 팔꿈치를 떡 벌리고, 다시 채찍을 울리니, 그들은 전보다 더 흥겹게 내달았다."]], [["A few small houses, scattered on either side of the road, betoken the entrance to some town or village.", "길 양편에 드문드문 흩어진 자그마한 집 몇 채가 어느 읍이나 마을의 어귀임을 알렸다."], ["The lively notes of the guard’s key- bugle vibrate in the clear cold air, and wake up the old gentleman inside, who, carefully letting down the window-sash half-way, and standing sentry over the air, takes a short peep out, and then carefully pulling it up again, informs the other inside that they’re going to change directly; on which the other inside wakes himself up, and determines to postpone his next nap until after the stoppage.", "차장의 건반 나팔에서 흘러나오는 경쾌한 가락이 맑고 차가운 공기 속에 울려 안에 탄 노신사를 깨웠으니, 그는 창틀을 조심스레 절반쯤 내리고 공기를 향해 보초라도 서듯 잠깐 밖을 내다보고는, 다시 조심스레 그것을 올리며 같이 안에 탄 다른 이에게 곧 말을 바꿔 맬 거라고 일러 주었다. 그 말에 다른 이는 잠을 깨고는 다음 낮잠은 정차 후로 미루기로 작정했다."], ["Again the bugle sounds lustily forth, and rouses the cottager’s wife and children, who peep out at the house door, and watch the coach till it turns the corner, when they once more crouch round the blazing fire, and throw on another log of wood against father comes home; while father himself, a full mile off, has just exchanged a friendly nod with the coachman, and turned round to take a good long stare at the vehicle as it whirls away.", "또다시 나팔이 우렁차게 울려 오두막집 아낙과 아이들을 일깨우니, 그들은 집 문가로 빼꼼 내다보며 마차가 모퉁이를 돌 때까지 지켜보다가, 다시금 활활 타는 불가에 둘러앉아 아버지가 집에 올 것에 대비해 장작 한 개비를 더 던져 넣었다. 한편 아버지 자신은 족히 일 마일 떨어진 곳에서 막 마부와 다정하게 고개를 끄덕여 인사를 나누고는, 휭 하니 내달아 가는 마차를 오래도록 물끄러미 바라보려고 몸을 돌린 참이었다."]], [["And now the bugle plays a lively air as the coach rattles through the ill-paved streets of a country town; and the coachman, undoing the buckle which keeps his ribands together, prepares to throw them off the moment he stops.", "이제 마차가 어느 시골 읍의 험하게 깔린 거리를 덜컹덜컹 지나는 동안 나팔이 경쾌한 가락을 연주했다. 마부는 고삐들을 한데 묶어 두는 버클을 풀어, 멈추는 순간 곧바로 고삐를 벗어 던질 채비를 했다."], ["Mr. Pickwick emerges from his coat collar, and looks about him with great curiosity; perceiving which, the coachman informs Mr.", "픽윅 씨는 외투 깃에서 얼굴을 내밀고 자못 호기심 어린 눈으로 주위를 둘러보았다."], ["Pickwick of the name of the town, and tells him it was market-day yesterday, both of which pieces of information Mr.", "그것을 알아챈 마부가 픽윅 씨에게 그 읍의 이름을 일러 주고 어제가 장날이었다고 말해 주니, 픽윅 씨는 그 두 가지 정보를 동행한 승객들에게 그대로 전했다."], ["Pickwick retails to his fellow-passengers; whereupon they emerge from their coat collars too, and look about them also.", "그러자 그들도 외투 깃에서 얼굴을 내밀고 역시 주위를 둘러보았다."], ["Mr. Winkle, who sits at the extreme edge, with one leg dangling in the air, is nearly precipitated into the street, as the coach twists round the sharp corner by the cheesemonger’s shop, and turns into the market-place; and before Mr.", "한쪽 다리를 허공에 늘어뜨린 채 맨 가장자리에 앉아 있던 윈클 씨는, 마차가 치즈 가게 옆 날카로운 모퉁이를 휙 돌아 장터로 접어들 때 하마터면 거리로 곤두박질칠 뻔했다."], ["Snodgrass, who sits next to him, has recovered from his alarm, they pull up at the inn yard where the fresh horses, with cloths on, are already waiting.", "그의 옆에 앉은 스노드그래스 씨가 놀란 가슴을 채 진정하기도 전에, 마차는 새 말들이 덮개를 두른 채 이미 기다리고 있는 여관 마당에 멈춰 섰다."], ["The coachman throws down the reins and gets down himself, and the other outside passengers drop down also; except those who have no great confidence in their ability to get up again; and they remain where they are, and stamp their feet against the coach to warm them--looking, with longing eyes and red noses, at the bright fire in the inn bar, and the sprigs of holly with red berries which ornament the window.", "마부가 고삐를 내던지고 제가 먼저 내려서고, 다른 바깥쪽 승객들도 따라 내렸다. 다만 다시 올라탈 자신이 없는 이들은 빼고였으니, 그들은 제자리에 남아 발을 마차에 굴러 몸을 녹였고 ― 갈망 어린 눈과 빨개진 코로, 여관 술청의 환한 불과 창을 장식한 빨간 열매 달린 호랑가시나무 가지를 바라보았다."]], [["But the guard has delivered at the corn-dealer’s shop, the brown paper packet he took out of the little pouch which hangs over his shoulder by a leathern strap; and has seen the horses carefully put to; and has thrown on the pavement the saddle which was brought from London on the coach roof; and has assisted in the conference between the coachman and the hostler about the gray mare that hurt her off fore-leg last Tuesday; and he and Mr.", "그러나 차장은 가죽끈으로 어깨에 걸친 자그마한 주머니에서 꺼낸 갈색 종이 꾸러미를 곡물상에게 건네주었고, 말들을 조심스레 매는 것을 지켜보았으며, 런던에서 마차 지붕에 실어 온 안장을 보도에 내던졌고, 지난 화요일에 오른쪽 앞다리를 다친 잿빛 암말에 관해 마부와 마부 조수가 나누는 의논을 거들었다."], ["Weller are all right behind, and the coachman is all right in front, and the old gentleman inside, who has kept the window down full two inches all this time, has pulled it up again, and the cloths are off, and they are all ready for starting, except the ‘two stout gentlemen,’ whom the coachman inquires after with some impatience.", "이제 그와 웰러 씨는 뒤쪽에서 만반의 준비가 되었고, 마부는 앞쪽에서 만반의 준비가 되었고, 그동안 내내 창을 꼬박 두 치나 내려 두고 있던 안쪽 노신사는 그것을 다시 올렸고, 덮개도 벗겨졌으니, \"건장한 두 신사\"만 빼고는 모두 출발 준비가 끝났다."], ["Hereupon the coachman, and the guard, and Sam Weller, and Mr.", "마부는 그 두 사람을 다소 조바심을 내며 찾았다."], ["Winkle, and Mr. Snodgrass, and all the hostlers, and every one of the idlers, who are more in number than all the others put together, shout for the missing gentlemen as loud as they can bawl.", "이에 마부와 차장과 샘 웰러와 윈클 씨와 스노드그래스 씨와 마부 조수들 전부와, 다 합친 수보다도 많은 빈둥거리는 구경꾼들 하나하나가 사라진 신사들을 목청껏 불러 댔다."], ["A distant response is heard from the yard, and Mr. Pickwick and Mr.", "마당 쪽에서 멀리 응답이 들려오더니, 픽윅 씨와 텁먼 씨가 숨을 헐떡이며 그리로 달려 내려왔다."], ["Tupman come running down it, quite out of breath, for they have been having a glass of ale a-piece, and Mr.", "둘이 각각 에일 한 잔씩을 들이켠 데다, 픽윅 씨의 손가락이 어찌나 시렸던지 그 값을 치를 6펜스를 찾는 데만 꼬박 오 분이 걸린 탓이었다."], ["Pickwick’s fingers are so cold that he has been full five minutes before he could find the sixpence to pay for it.", "마부가 타이르듯 \"자 그럼, 신사 양반들\" 하고 외치자 차장이 그것을 따라 외쳤다."], ["The coachman shouts an admonitory ‘Now then, gen’l’m’n,’ the guard re-echoes it; the old gentleman inside thinks it a very extraordinary thing that people will get down when they know there isn’t time for it; Mr.", "안쪽 노신사는 시간이 없는 줄 빤히 알면서도 사람들이 굳이 내리려 든다는 게 참으로 별난 일이라고 생각했다. 픽윅 씨가 한쪽으로 끙끙대며 올라타고, 텁먼 씨가 다른 쪽으로 올라타고, 윈클 씨가 \"출발!\""], ["Pickwick struggles up on one side, Mr. Tupman on the other; Mr.", "하고 외치자 그들은 떠났다."], ["Winkle cries ‘All right’; and off they start. Shawls are pulled up, coat collars are readjusted, the pavement ceases, the houses disappear; and they are once again dashing along the open road, with the fresh clear air blowing in their faces, and gladdening their very hearts within them.", "숄을 끌어 올리고, 외투 깃을 다시 여미고, 보도가 끝나고, 집들이 사라지고, 그들은 다시 한 번 탁 트인 길을 따라 내달았으니, 상쾌하고 맑은 공기가 얼굴에 불어와 그들 가슴속 깊은 곳까지 기쁘게 했다."]], [["Such was the progress of Mr. Pickwick and his friends by the Muggleton Telegraph, on their way to Dingley Dell; and at three o’clock that afternoon they all stood high and dry, safe and sound, hale and hearty, upon the steps of the Blue Lion, having taken on the road quite enough of ale and brandy, to enable them to bid defiance to the frost that was binding up the earth in its iron fetters, and weaving its beautiful network upon the trees and hedges.", "머글턴 텔레그래프 마차로 딩글리 델로 향하는 픽윅 씨와 그 친구들의 여정은 이러했다."], ["Mr. Pickwick was busily engaged in counting the barrels of oysters and superintending the disinterment of the cod-fish, when he felt himself gently pulled by the skirts of the coat.", "그날 오후 세 시에 그들은 모두 블루 라이언 여관 계단 위에 높직하고 뽀송하게, 무사하고 멀쩡하게, 정정하고 기운차게 서 있었으니, 길에서 에일과 브랜디를 넉넉히 들이켠 덕에 대지를 쇠사슬로 옭아매고 나무며 산울타리에 아름다운 그물을 짜 놓는 서리에 능히 맞설 만했다."], ["Looking round, he discovered that the individual who resorted to this mode of catching his attention was no other than Mr.", "픽윅 씨가 굴통을 세고 대구를 파내는 일을 감독하느라 분주하던 차에, 누군가 외투 자락을 살그머니 잡아당기는 것을 느꼈다."], ["Wardle’s favourite page, better known to the readers of this unvarnished history, by the distinguishing appellation of the fat boy.", "돌아보니 이런 식으로 그의 주의를 끌려 한 그 사람은 다름 아닌 워들 씨의 총애받는 시동, 이 꾸밈없는 역사의 독자들에게 뚱보 소년이라는 별난 호칭으로 더 잘 알려진 바로 그 아이였다."]], [["‘Aha!’ said Mr.", "\"아하!\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Aha!’ said the fat boy.", "\"아하!\" 뚱보 소년이 말했다."]], [["As he said it, he glanced from the cod-fish to the oyster-barrels, and chuckled joyously.", "그렇게 말하면서 그는 대구에서 굴통으로 눈길을 옮기며 흐뭇하게 빙긋거렸다."], ["He was fatter than ever.", "그는 전보다 더 뚱뚱해져 있었다."]], [["‘Well, you look rosy enough, my young friend,’ said Mr.", "\"이런, 자네 혈색이 아주 좋아 보이는구먼, 젊은 친구.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘I’ve been asleep, right in front of the taproom fire,’ replied the fat boy, who had heated himself to the colour of a new chimney-pot, in the course of an hour’s nap.", "\"술청 불 바로 앞에서 자고 있었거든요.\" 뚱보 소년이 대답했다."], ["‘Master sent me over with the chay-cart, to carry your luggage up to the house.", "그는 한 시간 낮잠을 자는 동안 새 굴뚝 단지 빛깔로 달아올라 있었다."], ["He’d ha’ sent some saddle-horses, but he thought you’d rather walk, being a cold day.’", "\"주인 어른이 짐을 댁으로 실어 가라고 저를 마차에 태워 보내셨어요. 안장말도 몇 마리 보내려고 하셨는데, 추운 날이니 걸으시는 편이 낫겠다고 생각하셨어요.\""]], [["‘Yes, yes,’ said Mr.", "\"그래, 그래.\""], ["Pickwick hastily, for he remembered how they had travelled over nearly the same ground on a previous occasion.", "픽윅 씨가 서둘러 말했으니, 지난번에 거의 같은 길을 어떻게 지났던가가 떠올랐던 것이다."], ["‘Yes, we would rather walk.", "\"그래, 우리는 걷는 편이 낫겠네."], ["Here, Sam!’", "이보게, 샘!\""]], [["‘Sir,’ said Mr.", "\"예, 나리.\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Help Mr. Wardle’s servant to put the packages into the cart, and then ride on with him.", "\"워들 씨네 하인이 짐을 마차에 싣는 걸 거들고, 그런 다음 같이 타고 오게."], ["We will walk forward at once.’", "우리는 곧장 앞서 걸어가겠네.\""]], [["Having given this direction, and settled with the coachman, Mr.", "이렇게 일러두고 마부와 셈을 치른 뒤, 픽윅 씨와 세 친구는 들판을 가로지르는 오솔길로 접어들어 활기차게 걸어 떠났다."], ["Pickwick and his three friends struck into the footpath across the fields, and walked briskly away, leaving Mr.", "웰러 씨와 뚱보 소년만 처음으로 단둘이 마주 보게 남겨 둔 채였다."], ["Weller and the fat boy confronted together for the first time.", "셈은 한마디 말도 없이 자못 놀란 눈으로 뚱보 소년을 바라보더니, 짐을 후딱후딱 마차에 챙겨 싣기 시작했다."], ["Sam looked at the fat boy with great astonishment, but without saying a word; and began to stow the luggage rapidly away in the cart, while the fat boy stood quietly by, and seemed to think it a very interesting sort of thing to see Mr. Weller working by himself.", "그동안 뚱보 소년은 곁에 가만히 서서, 웰러 씨가 혼자 일하는 꼴을 구경하는 게 자못 흥미로운 일이라도 되는 양 여기는 듯했다."]], [["‘There,’ said Sam, throwing in the last carpet-bag, ‘there they are!’", "\"자.\" 마지막 융단 가방을 던져 넣으며 셈이 말했다. \"다 됐다!\""]], [["‘Yes,’ said the fat boy, in a very satisfied tone, ‘there they are.’", "\"그래요.\" 뚱보 소년이 자못 흡족한 투로 말했다. \"다 됐네요.\""]], [["‘Vell, young twenty stun,’ said Sam, ‘you’re a nice specimen of a prize boy, you are!’", "\"이봐, 스무 관짜리 젊은 양반.\" 셈이 말했다. \"넌 정말이지 우량아 표본으로 딱이로구나!\""]], [["Thank’ee,’ said the fat boy.", "\"고맙수다.\" 뚱보 소년이 말했다."]], [["‘You ain’t got nothin’ on your mind as makes you fret yourself, have you?’ inquired Sam.", "\"마음에 걸려서 끙끙대게 만드는 일 같은 건 없겠지, 응?\" 셈이 물었다."]], [["‘Not as I knows on,’ replied the fat boy.", "\"제가 알기론 없는데요.\" 뚱보 소년이 대답했다."]], [["‘I should rayther ha’ thought, to look at you, that you was a-labourin’ under an unrequited attachment to some young ‘ooman,’ said Sam.", "\"널 보고 있자니, 난 차라리 네가 어느 처녀한테 짝사랑이라도 앓고 있는 줄로 알았다.\" 셈이 말했다."]], [["The fat boy shook his head.", "뚱보 소년이 고개를 가로저었다."]], [["‘Vell,’ said Sam, ‘I am glad to hear it.", "\"이런.\" 셈이 말했다."], ["Do you ever drink anythin’?’", "\"그 말 들으니 다행이군. 넌 뭘 좀 마시기는 하냐?\""]], [["‘I likes eating better,’ replied the boy.", "\"전 먹는 게 더 좋아요.\" 소년이 대답했다."]], [["‘Ah,’ said Sam, ‘I should ha’ s’posed that; but what I mean is, should you like a drop of anythin’ as’d warm you? but I s’pose you never was cold, with all them elastic fixtures, was you?’", "\"아.\" 셈이 말했다. \"그럴 줄 알았다. 한데 내 말은, 몸을 좀 녹여 줄 만한 걸 한 방울 마시고 싶지 않냐는 거다. 하긴 너야 그 탄력 좋은 살집을 다 갖췄으니 추운 적이 없었겠지, 안 그러냐?\""]], [["‘Sometimes,’ replied the boy; ‘and I likes a drop of something, when it’s good.’", "\"가끔은요.\" 소년이 대답했다. \"그리고 좋은 거라면 한 방울쯤 마시는 거 좋아해요.\""]], [["‘Oh, you do, do you?’ said Sam, ‘come this way, then!’", "\"오, 그러냐, 그래?\" 셈이 말했다. \"그럼 이리 와라!\""]], [["The Blue Lion tap was soon gained, and the fat boy swallowed a glass of liquor without so much as winking--a feat which considerably advanced him in Mr.", "블루 라이언 술청에 곧 다다랐고, 뚱보 소년은 눈 하나 깜빡 않고 술 한 잔을 들이켰다."], ["Weller’s good opinion. Mr.", "그 솜씨에 웰러 씨의 호감이 적잖이 올라갔다."], ["Weller having transacted a similar piece of business on his own account, they got into the cart.", "웰러 씨도 제 몫으로 같은 일을 처리한 뒤, 둘은 마차에 올라탔다."]], [["‘Can you drive?’ said the fat boy.", "\"몰 줄 알아요?\" 뚱보 소년이 말했다."]], [["‘I should rayther think so,’ replied Sam.", "\"그렇다고 봐야지.\" 셈이 대답했다."]], [["‘There, then,’ said the fat boy, putting the reins in his hand, and pointing up a lane, ‘it’s as straight as you can go; you can’t miss it.’", "\"그럼 자요.\" 뚱보 소년이 셈의 손에 고삐를 쥐여 주고 골목 위쪽을 가리키며 말했다. \"곧장 가기만 하면 돼요. 절대 못 놓쳐요.\""]], [["With these words, the fat boy laid himself affectionately down by the side of the cod-fish, and, placing an oyster-barrel under his head for a pillow, fell asleep instantaneously.", "이 말과 함께 뚱보 소년은 대구 옆에 다정스레 드러눕더니, 굴통 하나를 베개 삼아 머리 밑에 받치고는 그 자리에서 곧바로 잠들어 버렸다."]], [["‘Well,’ said Sam, ‘of all the cool boys ever I set my eyes on, this here young gen’l’m’n is the coolest.", "\"이거 참.\" 셈이 말했다."], ["Come, wake up, young dropsy!’", "\"내 평생 본 태평스런 녀석 중에서도 이 어린 양반이 제일 태평하네. 자, 일어나, 이 물오른 녀석아!\""]], [["But as young dropsy evinced no symptoms of returning animation, Sam Weller sat himself down in front of the cart, and starting the old horse with a jerk of the rein, jogged steadily on, towards the Manor Farm.", "그러나 물오른 녀석은 다시 살아날 기색을 도무지 보이지 않으니, 셈 웰러는 마차 앞쪽에 자리를 잡고 앉아 고삐를 한 번 홱 당겨 늙은 말을 출발시키고는, 매너 농장을 향해 꾸준히 덜컹덜컹 나아갔다."]], [["Meanwhile, Mr. Pickwick and his friends having walked their blood into active circulation, proceeded cheerfully on.", "그러는 동안 픽윅 씨와 그 친구들은 걸어서 피를 활발히 돌게 한 뒤 즐겁게 나아갔다."], ["The paths were hard; the grass was crisp and frosty; the air had a fine, dry, bracing coldness; and the rapid approach of the gray twilight (slate-coloured is a better term in frosty weather) made them look forward with pleasant anticipation to the comforts which awaited them at their hospitable entertainer’s.", "길은 단단했고, 풀은 빳빳하게 서리를 머금었으며, 공기는 곱고 메마른, 정신을 번쩍 들게 하는 찬 기운을 띠고 있었다. 그리고 잿빛 황혼(서리 내린 날씨엔 슬레이트 빛이라는 말이 더 낫겠다)이 빠르게 다가오니, 그들은 환대 잘하는 주인의 집에서 기다리고 있을 안락함을 흐뭇한 기대로 고대하게 되었다."], ["It was the sort of afternoon that might induce a couple of elderly gentlemen, in a lonely field, to take off their greatcoats and play at leap-frog in pure lightness of heart and gaiety; and we firmly believe that had Mr.", "그것은 외딴 들판에서 노신사 둘이 순전히 마음의 가벼움과 흥겨움에 겨워 외투를 벗어 던지고 등 짚고 뛰어넘기 놀이라도 하게 만들 법한 그런 오후였다. 우리는 만약 그 순간 텁먼 씨가 \"등을 대 주마\""], ["Tupman at that moment proffered ‘a back,’ Mr. Pickwick would have accepted his offer with the utmost avidity.", "하고 나섰더라면 픽윅 씨가 더없이 간절하게 그 제의를 받아들였으리라고 굳게 믿는다."]], [["However, Mr.", "그러나 텁먼 씨는 그런 편의를 자청하지 않았으니, 친구들은 흥겹게 담소하며 계속 걸었다."], ["Tupman did not volunteer any such accommodation, and the friends walked on, conversing merrily.", "그들이 건너야 할 어느 골목으로 접어들 때, 여러 목소리가 그들의 귀에 와락 밀려들었다. 그것이 누구의 목소리인지 짐작해 볼 겨를도 없이, 그들은 도착을 기다리고 있던 무리의 한복판으로 곧장 걸어 들어갔다."], ["As they turned into a lane they had to cross, the sound of many voices burst upon their ears; and before they had even had time to form a guess to whom they belonged, they walked into the very centre of the party who were expecting their arrival--a fact which was first notified to the Pickwickians, by the loud ‘Hurrah,’ which burst from old Wardle’s lips, when they appeared in sight.", "그 사실은 그들이 시야에 들어선 순간 워들 영감의 입에서 터져 나온 우렁찬 \"만세!\" 소리로 픽윅 회원들에게 처음 알려졌다."]], [["First, there was Wardle himself, looking, if that were possible, more jolly than ever; then there were Bella and her faithful Trundle; and, lastly, there were Emily and some eight or ten young ladies, who had all come down to the wedding, which was to take place next day, and who were in as happy and important a state as young ladies usually are, on such momentous occasions; and they were, one and all, startling the fields and lanes, far and wide, with their frolic and laughter.", "먼저 워들 자신이 있었으니, 가능한 일이라면 전보다 더 명랑해 보였다. 다음엔 벨라와 그녀의 충실한 트런들이 있었고, 마지막으로 에밀리와 여덟이나 열쯤 되는 젊은 숙녀들이 있었다. 이들은 모두 이튿날 치러질 결혼식을 보러 내려와 있었고, 그런 중대한 행사 때 젊은 숙녀들이 으레 그렇듯 행복하고 자못 거드름 피우는 상태였다. 그들은 너 나 할 것 없이 멀고 넓게 들판과 골목을 그들의 장난과 웃음으로 뒤흔들고 있었다."]], [["The ceremony of introduction, under such circumstances, was very soon performed, or we should rather say that the introduction was soon over, without any ceremony at all.", "그런 상황에서 소개의 격식은 아주 금세 치러졌으니, 차라리 아무런 격식도 없이 소개가 금세 끝나 버렸다고 말하는 편이 낫겠다."], ["In two minutes thereafter, Mr. Pickwick was joking with the young ladies who wouldn’t come over the stile while he looked--or who, having pretty feet and unexceptionable ankles, preferred standing on the top rail for five minutes or so, declaring that they were too frightened to move--with as much ease and absence of reserve or constraint, as if he had known them for life.", "그로부터 이 분 뒤, 픽윅 씨는 자기가 보고 있는 동안엔 디딤대를 넘어오려 하지 않는 ― 혹은 예쁜 발과 나무랄 데 없는 발목을 가진지라 맨 윗단에 오 분쯤 서 있는 편을 택하며 너무 무서워 꼼짝 못하겠다고 우기는 ― 젊은 숙녀들과, 마치 평생을 알고 지낸 사이인 양 거리낌이나 어색함 없이 편안하게 농을 주고받고 있었다."], ["It is worthy of remark, too, that Mr. Snodgrass offered Emily far more assistance than the absolute terrors of the stile (although it was full three feet high, and had only a couple of stepping-stones) would seem to require; while one black-eyed young lady in a very nice little pair of boots with fur round the top, was observed to scream very loudly, when Mr.", "또한 주목할 만한 일은, 스노드그래스 씨가 에밀리에게, 그 디딤대의 정녕 무서운 정도가(비록 그것이 족히 석 자 높이에 디딤돌이라곤 두어 개뿐이었지만) 요구할 법한 것보다 훨씬 더 많은 도움을 베풀었다는 것이다."], ["Winkle offered to help her over.", "한편 위쪽에 모피가 둘린 자못 멋진 작은 부츠를 신은 어느 검은 눈의 젊은 숙녀는, 윈클 씨가 넘는 것을 도와주겠다고 나서자 자못 크게 비명을 지른 것이 목격되었다."]], [["All this was very snug and pleasant.", "이 모든 것이 자못 아늑하고 즐거웠다."], ["And when the difficulties of the stile were at last surmounted, and they once more entered on the open field, old Wardle informed Mr. Pickwick how they had all been down in a body to inspect the furniture and fittings-up of the house, which the young couple were to tenant, after the Christmas holidays; at which communication Bella and Trundle both coloured up, as red as the fat boy after the taproom fire; and the young lady with the black eyes and the fur round the boots, whispered something in Emily’s ear, and then glanced archly at Mr.", "마침내 디딤대의 난관이 극복되고 그들이 다시 한 번 탁 트인 들판으로 들어섰을 때, 워들 영감은 픽윅 씨에게, 크리스마스 휴가가 지난 뒤 그 젊은 부부가 살게 될 집의 가구와 비품을 다 같이 떼 지어 내려가 둘러보고 왔노라고 일러 주었다."], ["Snodgrass; to which Emily responded that she was a foolish girl, but turned very red, notwithstanding; and Mr.", "그 말에 벨라와 트런들은 둘 다 술청 불 앞에 있던 뚱보 소년처럼 새빨갛게 낯을 붉혔다."], ["Snodgrass, who was as modest as all great geniuses usually are, felt the crimson rising to the crown of his head, and devoutly wished, in the inmost recesses of his own heart, that the young lady aforesaid, with her black eyes, and her archness, and her boots with the fur round the top, were all comfortably deposited in the adjacent county.", "또 검은 눈에 부츠에 모피를 두른 젊은 숙녀가 에밀리의 귀에 뭐라고 속삭이고는 짓궂게 스노드그래스 씨를 흘끔 보니, 에밀리는 그녀더러 바보 같은 애라고 응수했지만 그럼에도 자못 빨개졌다. 그리고 위대한 천재들이 으레 그렇듯 겸손한 스노드그래스 씨는 정수리까지 붉은 기운이 차오르는 것을 느끼며, 제 마음속 깊디깊은 곳에서, 앞서 말한 그 젊은 숙녀가 검은 눈도 짓궂음도 위쪽에 모피 둘린 부츠도 죄다, 아예 이웃 고을에 편안히 가 있었으면 좋겠다고 간절히 바랐다."]], [["But if they were social and happy outside the house, what was the warmth and cordiality of their reception when they reached the farm!", "집 밖에서도 그토록 정겹고 행복했다면, 농장에 다다랐을 때의 환영은 또 얼마나 따뜻하고 정겨웠겠는가!"], ["The very servants grinned with pleasure at sight of Mr.", "하인들조차 픽윅 씨를 보고 기뻐서 활짝 웃었다."], ["Pickwick; and Emma bestowed a half-demure, half-impudent, and all-pretty look of recognition, on Mr. Tupman, which was enough to make the statue of Bonaparte in the passage, unfold his arms, and clasp her within them.", "그리고 엠마는 텁먼 씨에게 반은 새침하고 반은 발칙하며 죄다 사랑스러운 알은체의 눈길을 보냈으니, 복도에 선 보나파르트 석상이라도 팔짱을 풀고 그녀를 끌어안게 만들 만한 것이었다."]], [["The old lady was seated with customary state in the front parlour, but she was rather cross, and, by consequence, most particularly deaf.", "노부인은 앞 객실에 여느 때처럼 위엄 있게 앉아 있었으나, 다소 심사가 뒤틀려 있었고, 그 탓에 유난히도 귀가 먹어 있었다."], ["She never went out herself, and like a great many other old ladies of the same stamp, she was apt to consider it an act of domestic treason, if anybody else took the liberty of doing what she couldn’t.", "그녀 자신은 결코 바깥출입을 하지 않았으니, 같은 부류의 다른 노부인들 다수가 그렇듯, 누구든 자기가 할 수 없는 일을 제멋대로 하면 그것을 집안에 대한 반역 행위쯤으로 여기기 쉬웠다."], ["So, bless her old soul, she sat as upright as she could, in her great chair, and looked as fierce as might be--and that was benevolent after all.", "그러니 그 늙은 영혼에게 복이 있기를, 그녀는 큰 의자에 될 수 있는 한 꼿꼿이 앉아 더없이 사납게 보이려 했으나 ― 그래 봤자 결국은 인자해 보였다."]], [["‘Mother,’ said Wardle, ‘Mr.", "\"어머니.\" 워들이 말했다."], ["Pickwick.", "\"픽윅 씨예요."], ["You recollect him?’", "기억나시죠?\""]], [["‘Never mind,’ replied the old lady, with great dignity.", "\"신경 쓰지 마라.\" 노부인이 자못 위엄 있게 대답했다."], ["‘Don’t trouble Mr. Pickwick about an old creetur like me.", "\"나 같은 늙은이 일로 픽윅 씨를 성가시게 하지 마."], ["Nobody cares about me now, and it’s very nat’ral they shouldn’t.’ Here the old lady tossed her head, and smoothed down her lavender-coloured silk dress with trembling hands.", "이제 아무도 날 챙기지 않으니, 그게 당연한 일이지.\" 여기서 노부인은 고개를 휙 젖히고는 떨리는 손으로 라벤더 빛 비단 드레스를 매만져 내렸다."]], [["‘Come, come, ma’am,’ said Mr.", "\"이런, 이런, 부인.\""], ["Pickwick, ‘I can’t let you cut an old friend in this way.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"이런 식으로 옛 친구를 모른 체하게 둘 수는 없지요."], ["I have come down expressly to have a long talk, and another rubber with you; and we’ll show these boys and girls how to dance a minuet, before they’re eight-and-forty hours older.’", "저는 부인과 오래도록 이야기도 나누고 카드도 한판 더 하려고 일부러 내려온 겁니다. 그리고 우리 둘이서 이 사내애들과 계집애들에게, 마흔여덟 시간이 더 지나기 전에 미뉴에트 추는 법을 보여 줍시다.\""]], [["The old lady was rapidly giving way, but she did not like to do it all at once; so she only said, ‘Ah!", "노부인은 빠르게 마음이 누그러지고 있었으나, 한꺼번에 그러기는 싫었던지라, 그저 \"아! 안 들려요!\""], ["I can’t hear him!’", "하고만 말했다."]], [["‘Nonsense, mother,’ said Wardle.", "\"무슨 말씀이세요, 어머니.\" 워들이 말했다."], ["‘Come, come, don’t be cross, there’s a good soul.", "\"자, 자, 토라지지 마세요, 착한 분이시잖아요."], ["Recollect Bella; come, you must keep her spirits up, poor girl.’", "벨라를 생각하세요. 자, 저 가엾은 애의 기운을 북돋워 주셔야지요.\""]], [["The good old lady heard this, for her lip quivered as her son said it.", "선량한 노부인은 이 말을 들었으니, 아들이 그 말을 하자 입술이 떨렸기 때문이다."], ["But age has its little infirmities of temper, and she was not quite brought round yet.", "그러나 나이에는 사소한 심술의 약점이 따르는 법이라, 그녀는 아직 완전히 풀리지는 않았다."], ["So, she smoothed down the lavender-coloured dress again, and turning to Mr. Pickwick said, ‘Ah, Mr.", "그래서 라벤더 빛 드레스를 다시 한 번 매만져 내리고는 픽윅 씨를 향해 돌아앉아 말했다."], ["Pickwick, young people was very different, when I was a girl.’", "\"아, 픽윅 씨, 내가 처녀였을 적엔 젊은이들이 아주 달랐다오.\""]], [["‘No doubt of that, ma’am,’ said Mr.", "\"의심할 바 없지요, 부인.\""], ["Pickwick, ‘and that’s the reason why I would make much of the few that have any traces of the old stock’--and saying this, Mr.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"그러니 옛 혈통의 자취를 조금이라도 지닌 몇 안 되는 이들을 제가 그토록 귀히 여기는 것이지요.\""], ["Pickwick gently pulled Bella towards him, and bestowing a kiss upon her forehead, bade her sit down on the little stool at her grandmother’s feet.", "이렇게 말하며 픽윅 씨는 벨라를 가만히 제 쪽으로 끌어당겨 이마에 입을 맞추고는, 할머니 발치의 작은 걸상에 앉으라고 일렀다."], ["Whether the expression of her countenance, as it was raised towards the old lady’s face, called up a thought of old times, or whether the old lady was touched by Mr.", "노부인의 얼굴을 향해 들어 올려진 그녀의 표정이 옛 시절의 생각을 불러일으켰던 것인지, 아니면 노부인이 픽윅 씨의 다정한 선량함에 감동했던 것인지, 그 까닭이 무엇이었든 간에 그녀는 끝내 마음이 녹아내렸다."], ["Pickwick’s affectionate good- nature, or whatever was the cause, she was fairly melted; so she threw herself on her granddaughter’s neck, and all the little ill-humour evaporated in a gush of silent tears.", "그래서 손녀의 목을 끌어안았고, 그 모든 자그마한 심술은 소리 없는 눈물의 봇물 속에 다 씻겨 사라졌다."]], [["A happy party they were, that night.", "그날 밤 그들은 행복한 무리였다."], ["Sedate and solemn were the score of rubbers in which Mr. Pickwick and the old lady played together; uproarious was the mirth of the round table.", "픽윅 씨와 노부인이 함께한 스무 판 남짓의 카드놀이는 점잖고 엄숙했으며, 둥근 식탁의 흥겨움은 떠들썩했다."], ["Long after the ladies had retired, did the hot elder wine, well qualified with brandy and spice, go round, and round, and round again; and sound was the sleep and pleasant were the dreams that followed.", "숙녀들이 물러간 한참 뒤까지도, 브랜디와 향료를 넉넉히 탄 뜨거운 엘더베리 술이 돌고, 또 돌고, 거듭 돌았다."], ["It is a remarkable fact that those of Mr.", "그 뒤에 이어진 잠은 깊었고 꿈은 즐거웠다."], ["Snodgrass bore constant reference to Emily Wardle; and that the principal figure in Mr. Winkle’s visions was a young lady with black eyes, and arch smile, and a pair of remarkably nice boots with fur round the tops.", "주목할 만한 사실은, 스노드그래스 씨의 꿈은 끊임없이 에밀리 워들을 향했다는 것이요, 윈클 씨의 환상 속 주역은 검은 눈에 짓궂은 미소를 띠고 위쪽에 모피가 둘린 자못 멋진 부츠 한 켤레를 신은 젊은 숙녀였다는 것이다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick was awakened early in the morning, by a hum of voices and a pattering of feet, sufficient to rouse even the fat boy from his heavy slumbers.", "픽윅 씨는 이른 아침에, 여러 목소리의 웅성거림과 발소리의 자박거림에 잠이 깼으니, 그것은 뚱보 소년조차 그 깊은 잠에서 깨워 낼 만한 것이었다."], ["He sat up in bed and listened.", "그는 침대에 일어나 앉아 귀를 기울였다."], ["The female servants and female visitors were running constantly to and fro; and there were such multitudinous demands for hot water, such repeated outcries for needles and thread, and so many half-suppressed entreaties of ‘Oh, do come and tie me, there’s a dear!’ that Mr.", "하녀들과 여자 손님들이 쉴 새 없이 이리저리 뛰어다녔다. 그리고 뜨거운 물을 찾는 외침이 어찌나 빗발치고, 바늘과 실을 찾는 부르짖음이 어찌나 거듭되며, \"오, 좀 와서 매 줘, 제발!\""], ["Pickwick in his innocence began to imagine that something dreadful must have occurred--when he grew more awake, and remembered the wedding.", "하는 반쯤 죽인 애원이 어찌나 많았던지, 순진한 픽윅 씨는 무슨 끔찍한 일이라도 벌어진 게 틀림없다고 상상하기 시작했다 ― 그러다 정신이 더 들면서 결혼식이 생각났다."], ["The occasion being an important one, he dressed himself with peculiar care, and descended to the breakfast- room.", "중대한 행사인지라 그는 유난히 공들여 옷을 차려입고는 아침 식사방으로 내려갔다."]], [["There were all the female servants in a bran new uniform of pink muslin gowns with white bows in their caps, running about the house in a state of excitement and agitation which it would be impossible to describe.", "거기엔 하녀들이 죄다 분홍 모슬린 가운에 모자엔 흰 리본을 단 산뜻한 새 제복 차림으로, 도저히 형용할 수 없는 흥분과 동요의 상태로 집 안을 뛰어다니고 있었다."], ["The old lady was dressed out in a brocaded gown, which had not seen the light for twenty years, saving and excepting such truant rays as had stolen through the chinks in the box in which it had been laid by, during the whole time.", "노부인은 이십 년 동안 빛을 보지 못한 양단 가운을 차려입었으니, 다만 그동안 내내 그것을 넣어 둔 상자 틈새로 슬며시 새어 든 길 잃은 빛줄기 몇 가닥만이 예외였다."], ["Mr. Trundle was in high feather and spirits, but a little nervous withal.", "트런들 씨는 자못 의기양양하고 기운이 넘쳤으나, 그러면서도 다소 긴장해 있었다."], ["The hearty old landlord was trying to look very cheerful and unconcerned, but failing signally in the attempt.", "정 많은 늙은 집주인은 자못 명랑하고 태연한 척하려 했으나, 그 시도에 보기 좋게 실패하고 있었다."], ["All the girls were in tears and white muslin, except a select two or three, who were being honoured with a private view of the bride and bridesmaids, upstairs.", "처녀들은 위층에서 신부와 들러리들을 따로 구경하는 영예를 누리고 있던 특별히 선택된 두엇을 빼고는, 죄다 눈물에 젖은 채 흰 모슬린을 입고 있었다."], ["All the Pickwickians were in most blooming array; and there was a terrific roaring on the grass in front of the house, occasioned by all the men, boys, and hobbledehoys attached to the farm, each of whom had got a white bow in his button-hole, and all of whom were cheering with might and main; being incited thereto, and stimulated therein by the precept and example of Mr.", "픽윅 회원들은 모두 더없이 화사한 차림이었다. 그리고 집 앞 잔디밭에서는 무시무시한 함성이 울려 퍼졌으니, 농장에 딸린 사내들과 소년들과 어중간한 풋내기들이 죄다 단춧구멍에 흰 리본을 달고 있던 차에, 하나같이 있는 힘껏 환호성을 질러 댄 탓이었다."], ["Samuel Weller, who had managed to become mighty popular already, and was as much at home as if he had been born on the land.", "그들을 그렇게 부추기고 북돋운 것은 새뮤얼 웰러 씨의 가르침과 본보기였으니, 그는 벌써 대단히 인기를 얻는 데 성공하여, 마치 이 땅에서 나고 자란 사람처럼 너무도 편안해 보였다."]], [["A wedding is a licensed subject to joke upon, but there really is no great joke in the matter after all;--we speak merely of the ceremony, and beg it to be distinctly understood that we indulge in no hidden sarcasm upon a married life.", "결혼식은 농담거리로 삼아도 좋다고 허락된 주제이지만, 사실 따지고 보면 그리 큰 농담거리는 못 된다. 우리는 그저 그 의식을 두고 하는 말이며, 결혼 생활에 대해 무슨 숨은 빈정거림을 늘어놓는 것이 결코 아님을 분명히 이해해 주기를 청한다."], ["Mixed up with the pleasure and joy of the occasion, are the many regrets at quitting home, the tears of parting between parent and child, the consciousness of leaving the dearest and kindest friends of the happiest portion of human life, to encounter its cares and troubles with others still untried and little known--natural feelings which we would not render this chapter mournful by describing, and which we should be still more unwilling to be supposed to ridicule.", "그 행사의 기쁨과 즐거움에 뒤섞여 있는 것은, 집을 떠나는 데 따르는 숱한 아쉬움이요, 부모와 자식이 헤어지며 흘리는 눈물이요, 인생에서 가장 행복한 한 시절의 더없이 사랑스럽고 다정한 벗들을 떠나, 아직 시험해 보지 못하고 거의 알지도 못하는 다른 이들과 더불어 그 근심과 시련을 맞으러 간다는 자각이다 ― 그것들은 자연스러운 감정이거니와, 우리는 그것을 묘사하여 이 장을 구슬프게 만들고 싶지 않으며, 그것을 비웃는다고 여겨지는 것은 더더욱 원치 않는다."]], [["Let us briefly say, then, that the ceremony was performed by the old clergyman, in the parish church of Dingley Dell, and that Mr. Pickwick’s name is attached to the register, still preserved in the vestry thereof; that the young lady with the black eyes signed her name in a very unsteady and tremulous manner; that Emily’s signature, as the other bridesmaid, is nearly illegible; that it all went off in very admirable style; that the young ladies generally thought it far less shocking than they had expected; and that although the owner of the black eyes and the arch smile informed Mr.", "그러니 간략히 말하자면, 그 의식은 딩글리 델의 교구 교회에서 그 늙은 목사에 의해 치러졌고, 픽윅 씨의 이름이 그 교회 제의실에 아직도 보존되어 있는 혼인 명부에 적혀 있으며, 검은 눈의 젊은 숙녀는 자못 떨리고 불안정한 필체로 제 이름을 서명했고, 또 다른 들러리인 에밀리의 서명은 거의 알아볼 수 없을 지경이며, 모든 것이 자못 훌륭하게 치러졌고, 젊은 숙녀들은 대체로 그것이 예상했던 것보다 훨씬 덜 충격적이라고 여겼으며, 또 검은 눈과 짓궂은 미소의 임자가 워들 씨에게 자기는 결코 그런 끔찍한 일은 견뎌 내지 못할 것이 분명하다고 일러두었으나, 우리는 그녀가 잘못 알았다고 생각할 더없이 확실한 근거를 가지고 있다."], ["Wardle that she was sure she could never submit to anything so dreadful, we have the very best reasons for thinking she was mistaken. To all this, we may add, that Mr.", "이 모든 것에 덧붙여 말할 수 있는 것은, 픽윅 씨가 신부에게 맨 처음으로 입맞춤을 한 사람이라는 것, 그리고 그러면서 그녀의 목에 값진 금시계와 금줄을 걸어 주었는데, 그것은 보석상 말고는 어떤 인간의 눈도 일찍이 본 적이 없는 물건이었다는 것이다."], ["Pickwick was the first who saluted the bride, and that in so doing he threw over her neck a rich gold watch and chain, which no mortal eyes but the jeweller’s had ever beheld before. Then, the old church bell rang as gaily as it could, and they all returned to breakfast.", "그러고 나서 늙은 교회 종이 더없이 흥겹게 울렸고, 그들은 모두 아침을 먹으러 돌아왔다."]], [["‘Vere does the mince-pies go, young opium-eater?’ said Mr.", "\"민스파이는 어디 놓냐, 이 어린 아편쟁이야?\""], ["Weller to the fat boy, as he assisted in laying out such articles of consumption as had not been duly arranged on the previous night.", "웰러 씨가 전날 밤에 제대로 차려 두지 못한 먹을거리들을 늘어놓는 것을 거들며 뚱보 소년에게 말했다."]], [["The fat boy pointed to the destination of the pies.", "뚱보 소년은 파이를 놓을 자리를 가리켰다."]], [["‘Wery good,’ said Sam, ‘stick a bit o’ Christmas in ‘em.", "\"아주 좋아.\" 셈이 말했다."], ["T’other dish opposite.", "\"거기다 크리스마스 장식을 한 조각 꽂아라."], ["There; now we look compact and comfortable, as the father said ven he cut his little boy’s head off, to cure him o’ squintin’.’", "다른 접시는 맞은편에. 옳지, 이제 우리가 보기에 아담하고 편안하구먼, 사팔뜨기 고치겠다고 어린 아들 머리를 댕강 잘라 버린 아버지 말마따나 말이야.\""]], [["As Mr. Weller made the comparison, he fell back a step or two, to give full effect to it, and surveyed the preparations with the utmost satisfaction.", "웰러 씨는 이 비유를 하면서, 그 효과를 한껏 살리려고 한두 걸음 뒤로 물러서서는 더없이 흡족한 눈으로 차려 둔 것을 둘러보았다."]], [["‘Wardle,’ said Mr.", "\"워들.\""], ["Pickwick, almost as soon as they were all seated, ‘a glass of wine in honour of this happy occasion!’", "다들 자리에 앉기가 무섭게 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"이 기쁜 행사를 기려 술 한 잔!\""]], [["‘I shall be delighted, my boy,’ said Wardle.", "\"기꺼이 들지, 이 사람아.\" 워들이 말했다."], ["‘Joe--damn that boy, he’s gone to sleep.’", "\"조 ― 빌어먹을 녀석, 또 잠들었구먼.\""]], [["No, I ain’t, sir,’ replied the fat boy, starting up from a remote corner, where, like the patron saint of fat boys--the immortal Horner-- he had been devouring a Christmas pie, though not with the coolness and deliberation which characterised that young gentleman’s proceedings.", "\"아니에요, 나리.\" 뚱보 소년이 외딴 구석에서 벌떡 일어나며 대답했다. 거기서 그는 뚱보 소년들의 수호성인 ― 불멸의 호너 ― 처럼 크리스마스 파이를 게걸스레 먹어 치우고 있었으니, 다만 그 젊은 신사의 행적을 특징짓는 그 침착함과 신중함은 없었다."]], [["‘Fill Mr. Pickwick’s glass.’", "\"픽윅 씨 잔을 채워라.\""]], [["‘Yes, sir.’", "\"예, 나리.\""]], [["The fat boy filled Mr. Pickwick’s glass, and then retired behind his master’s chair, from whence he watched the play of the knives and forks, and the progress of the choice morsels from the dishes to the mouths of the company, with a kind of dark and gloomy joy that was most impressive.", "뚱보 소년은 픽윅 씨의 잔을 채우고는 주인 의자 뒤로 물러나, 거기서 나이프와 포크의 놀림이며, 접시에서 일행의 입으로 옮겨 가는 맛난 음식들의 행로를, 더없이 인상적인 어둡고 음울한 기쁨으로 지켜보았다."]], [["‘God bless you, old fellow!’ said Mr.", "\"신의 가호가 함께하길, 이 친구야!\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Same to you, my boy,’ replied Wardle; and they pledged each other, heartily.", "\"자네도 그러하길, 이 사람아.\" 워들이 대답했다. 그리고 그들은 진심으로 서로를 위해 축배를 들었다."]], [["‘Mrs. Wardle,’ said Mr.", "\"워들 부인.\""], ["Pickwick, ‘we old folks must have a glass of wine together, in honour of this joyful event.’", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"우리 같은 늙은이들은 이 기쁜 경사를 기려 함께 술 한 잔 들어야지요.\""]], [["The old lady was in a state of great grandeur just then, for she was sitting at the top of the table in the brocaded gown, with her newly- married granddaughter on one side, and Mr.", "노부인은 그때 자못 위엄을 갖춘 상태였으니, 양단 가운을 입고 식탁 상석에 앉아, 한쪽엔 갓 결혼한 손녀를, 다른 쪽엔 고기를 썰어 줄 픽윅 씨를 거느리고 있었던 까닭이다."], ["Pickwick on the other, to do the carving. Mr.", "픽윅 씨는 자못 큰 소리로 말한 것이 아니었으나 그녀는 단번에 알아듣고는, 그의 만수무강과 행복을 비는 술을 한 잔 가득 들이켰다."], ["Pickwick had not spoken in a very loud tone, but she understood him at once, and drank off a full glass of wine to his long life and happiness; after which the worthy old soul launched forth into a minute and particular account of her own wedding, with a dissertation on the fashion of wearing high-heeled shoes, and some particulars concerning the life and adventures of the beautiful Lady Tollimglower, deceased; at all of which the old lady herself laughed very heartily indeed, and so did the young ladies too, for they were wondering among themselves what on earth grandma was talking about.", "그러고 나서 그 갸륵한 노인은 자신의 결혼식에 관한 시시콜콜하고 자세한 이야기를 늘어놓기 시작했으니, 굽 높은 신을 신는 유행에 관한 일장 논설과, 작고한 아름다운 톨림글로어 부인의 생애와 모험에 관한 몇 가지 자세한 사연을 곁들였다. 그 모든 것에 노부인 자신은 실로 자못 흥겹게 웃어 댔고, 젊은 숙녀들도 따라 웃었으니, 그들은 자기들끼리 도대체 할머니가 무슨 이야기를 하고 있는 건지 궁금해하던 참이었다."], ["When they laughed, the old lady laughed ten times more heartily, and said that these always had been considered capital stories, which caused them all to laugh again, and put the old lady into the very best of humours.", "그들이 웃자 노부인은 열 곱절은 더 흥겹게 웃으며, 이건 늘 일품 이야기로 쳐 주던 것이라고 말했고, 그 말에 그들은 다 같이 또 웃어, 노부인을 더없이 좋은 기분으로 만들어 주었다."], ["Then the cake was cut, and passed through the ring; the young ladies saved pieces to put under their pillows to dream of their future husbands on; and a great deal of blushing and merriment was thereby occasioned.", "그러고 나서 케이크를 잘라 반지 사이로 통과시켰다. 젊은 숙녀들은 미래의 남편을 꿈에서 보려고 베개 밑에 둘 조각들을 챙겼고, 그리하여 자못 많은 낯붉힘과 웃음판이 벌어졌다."]], [["‘Mr. Miller,’ said Mr.", "\"밀러 씨.\""], ["Pickwick to his old acquaintance, the hard-headed gentleman, ‘a glass of wine?’", "픽윅 씨가 옛 지인인 그 고집스러운 신사에게 말했다. \"술 한 잔?\""]], [["‘With great satisfaction, Mr.", "\"더없는 만족과 함께 들지요, 픽윅 씨.\""], ["Pickwick,’ replied the hard-headed gentleman solemnly.", "고집스러운 신사가 엄숙하게 대답했다."]], [["‘You’ll take me in?’ said the benevolent old clergyman.", "\"저도 끼워 주시겠소?\" 인자한 늙은 목사가 말했다."]], [["‘And me,’ interposed his wife.", "\"저도요.\" 그의 아내가 끼어들었다."]], [["‘And me, and me,’ said a couple of poor relations at the bottom of the table, who had eaten and drunk very heartily, and laughed at everything.", "\"저도, 저도요.\" 식탁 끝에 앉은 가난한 친척 두 사람이 말했다. 그들은 자못 진탕 먹고 마시고 무엇에든 다 웃어 대던 이들이었다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick expressed his heartfelt delight at every additional suggestion; and his eyes beamed with hilarity and cheerfulness.", "픽윅 씨는 그렇게 더해지는 제안마다 진심 어린 기쁨을 드러냈고, 그의 눈은 흥겨움과 명랑함으로 빛났다."]], [["‘Ladies and gentlemen,’ said Mr.", "\"신사 숙녀 여러분.\""], ["Pickwick, suddenly rising.", "픽윅 씨가 별안간 일어서며 말했다."]], [["‘Hear, hear!", "\"옳소, 옳소!"], ["Hear, hear!", "옳소, 옳소!"], ["Hear, hear!’ cried Mr.", "옳소, 옳소!\""], ["Weller, in the excitement of his feelings.", "웰러 씨가 북받치는 감정에 못 이겨 외쳤다."]], [["‘Call in all the servants,’ cried old Wardle, interposing to prevent the public rebuke which Mr.", "\"하인들을 죄다 불러들이게.\""], ["Weller would otherwise most indubitably have received from his master.", "워들 영감이 외쳤으니, 안 그랬다면 웰러 씨가 그 주인에게서 틀림없이 받았을 공개적인 꾸지람을 막으려고 끼어든 것이었다."], ["‘Give them a glass of wine each to drink the toast in.", "\"저마다 술 한 잔씩 들려 이 축배를 같이 들게 하게."], ["Now, Pickwick.’", "자, 픽윅.\""]], [["Amidst the silence of the company, the whispering of the women-servants, and the awkward embarrassment of the men, Mr. Pickwick proceeded--", "일행의 침묵과 하녀들의 소곤거림과 사내들의 어색한 쭈뼛거림 가운데서, 픽윅 씨는 말을 이어 갔다."]], [["‘Ladies and gentlemen--no, I won’t say ladies and gentlemen, I’ll call you my friends, my dear friends, if the ladies will allow me to take so great a liberty--’", "\"신사 숙녀 여러분 ― 아니, 신사 숙녀라고는 하지 않겠습니다. 숙녀분들께서 제가 그토록 큰 무례를 범하도록 허락해 주신다면, 여러분을 제 친구들, 제 소중한 친구들이라고 부르겠습니다 ―\""]], [["Here Mr. Pickwick was interrupted by immense applause from the ladies, echoed by the gentlemen, during which the owner of the eyes was distinctly heard to state that she could kiss that dear Mr.", "여기서 픽윅 씨는 숙녀들의 엄청난 박수에 말이 끊겼고, 신사들이 그것을 받아 따라 했으니, 그 와중에 그 눈의 임자가 저 사랑스러운 픽윅 씨에게 입을 맞출 수도 있겠노라고 말하는 것이 또렷이 들렸다."], ["Pickwick. Whereupon Mr.", "이에 윈클 씨가 정중하게, 그 일을 대리인이 대신 할 수는 없겠느냐고 물으니, 검은 눈의 젊은 숙녀는 \"저리 가세요\""], ["Winkle gallantly inquired if it couldn’t be done by deputy: to which the young lady with the black eyes replied ‘Go away,’ and accompanied the request with a look which said as plainly as a look could do, ‘if you can.’", "하고 대답하면서, 그 청에다 \"할 수 있으면 해 보든가\" 하는 말을 눈빛으로 할 수 있는 한 분명하게 곁들였다."]], [["‘My dear friends,’ resumed Mr.", "\"제 소중한 친구들이여.\""], ["Pickwick, ‘I am going to propose the health of the bride and bridegroom--God bless ‘em (cheers and tears).", "픽윅 씨가 다시 말을 이었다. \"저는 신랑 신부의 건강을 위해 축배를 제의하려 합니다 ― 신의 가호가 그들에게 있기를(환호와 눈물)."], ["My young friend, Trundle, I believe to be a very excellent and manly fellow; and his wife I know to be a very amiable and lovely girl, well qualified to transfer to another sphere of action the happiness which for twenty years she has diffused around her, in her father’s house.", "제 젊은 친구 트런들은 제가 믿건대 자못 훌륭하고 사내다운 친구입니다. 그리고 그의 아내는 제가 알건대 자못 상냥하고 사랑스러운 처녀이니, 이십 년 동안 아버지의 집에서 둘레에 베풀어 온 그 행복을 또 다른 활동의 영역으로 옮길 자격이 충분합니다."], ["(Here, the fat boy burst forth into stentorian blubberings, and was led forth by the coat collar, by Mr.", "(여기서 뚱보 소년이 우렁차게 엉엉 울음을 터뜨려, 웰러 씨가 그의 외투 깃을 잡아 밖으로 끌어냈다.) 저는 바랍니다.\""], ["Weller.) I wish,’ added Mr.", "픽윅 씨가 덧붙였다."], ["Pickwick-- ‘I wish I was young enough to be her sister’s husband (cheers), but, failing that, I am happy to be old enough to be her father; for, being so, I shall not be suspected of any latent designs when I say, that I admire, esteem, and love them both (cheers and sobs).", "\"저는 그녀의 동생의 남편이 될 만큼 젊었으면 좋겠습니다(환호). 그러나 그러지 못하니, 그녀의 아버지가 될 만큼 늙은 것을 기쁘게 여깁니다."], ["The bride’s father, our good friend there, is a noble person, and I am proud to know him (great uproar).", "그러한즉, 제가 두 사람 다 흠모하고 존경하며 사랑한다고 말할 때(환호와 흐느낌), 무슨 숨은 속셈이 있다고 의심받지는 않을 테니까요."], ["He is a kind, excellent, independent-spirited, fine- hearted, hospitable, liberal man (enthusiastic shouts from the poor relations, at all the adjectives; and especially at the two last).", "신부의 아버지, 저기 계신 우리의 선량한 친구는 고결한 분이며, 저는 그분을 아는 것이 자랑스럽습니다(큰 소란). 그분은 친절하고, 훌륭하고, 독립된 기개를 지녔으며, 마음이 고운, 환대 잘하고, 너그러운 분입니다(그 모든 형용사마다, 특히 마지막 둘에 가난한 친척들의 열광적인 함성이 터졌다)."], ["That his daughter may enjoy all the happiness, even he can desire; and that he may derive from the contemplation of her felicity all the gratification of heart and peace of mind which he so well deserves, is, I am persuaded, our united wish.", "그분의 따님이, 그분 자신이 바랄 수 있는 모든 행복을 누리기를, 그리고 그분이 따님의 행복을 바라보는 데서 그토록 받아 마땅한 마음의 온갖 흡족함과 평안을 얻으시기를 비는 것이, 저는 확신하건대, 우리 모두의 한결같은 소원입니다."], ["So, let us drink their healths, and wish them prolonged life, and every blessing!’", "그러니 그들의 건강을 위해 마시고, 그들의 장수와 온갖 축복을 빕시다!\""]], [["Mr. Pickwick concluded amidst a whirlwind of applause; and once more were the lungs of the supernumeraries, under Mr. Weller’s command, brought into active and efficient operation. Mr. Wardle proposed Mr. Pickwick; Mr. Pickwick proposed the old lady. Mr. Snodgrass proposed Mr. Wardle; Mr. Wardle proposed Mr. Snodgrass. One of the poor relations proposed Mr. Tupman, and the other poor relation proposed Mr. Winkle; all was happiness and festivity, until the mysterious disappearance of both the poor relations beneath the table, warned the party that it was time to adjourn.", "픽윅 씨는 박수갈채의 회오리 가운데서 말을 맺었다. 그리고 다시 한 번 웰러 씨의 지휘 아래, 임시 동원된 사람들의 허파가 활발하고도 능률적으로 작동에 들어갔다. 워들 씨가 픽윅 씨를 위해 축배를 제의했고, 픽윅 씨가 노부인을 위해 제의했다. 스노드그래스 씨가 워들 씨를 위해 제의했고, 워들 씨가 스노드그래스 씨를 위해 제의했다. 가난한 친척 하나가 텁먼 씨를 위해 제의했고, 다른 가난한 친척이 윈클 씨를 위해 제의했다. 모든 것이 행복과 잔치판이었으니, 마침내 두 가난한 친척이 둘 다 식탁 밑으로 알 수 없이 사라져 버린 것이, 이제 파할 때가 되었음을 일행에게 일러 주었다."]], [["At dinner they met again, after a five-and-twenty mile walk, undertaken by the males at Wardle’s recommendation, to get rid of the effects of the wine at breakfast.", "저녁 식사 때 그들은 다시 만났으니, 그 사이 사내들이 아침 술기운을 떨쳐 내려고 워들의 권유로 이십오 마일 도보 행군에 나섰던 것이다."], ["The poor relations had kept in bed all day, with the view of attaining the same happy consummation, but, as they had been unsuccessful, they stopped there.", "가난한 친척들은 같은 행복한 결말에 이르고자 종일 침대에 누워 있었으나, 성공하지 못한 까닭에 거기 그대로 머물러 있었다."], ["Mr. Weller kept the domestics in a state of perpetual hilarity; and the fat boy divided his time into small alternate allotments of eating and sleeping.", "웰러 씨는 하인들을 끊임없는 흥겨움의 상태에 빠뜨려 놓았고, 뚱보 소년은 먹기와 잠자기를 자그맣게 번갈아 나눠 가며 시간을 보냈다."]], [["The dinner was as hearty an affair as the breakfast, and was quite as noisy, without the tears.", "저녁 식사는 아침 식사만큼이나 푸짐한 자리였고, 눈물만 빠졌을 뿐 꼭 그만큼 떠들썩했다."], ["Then came the dessert and some more toasts.", "그다음엔 후식과 몇 차례 더 이어진 축배가 있었다."], ["Then came the tea and coffee; and then, the ball.", "그다음엔 차와 커피가, 그리고 그다음엔 무도회가 있었다."]], [["The best sitting-room at Manor Farm was a good, long, dark-panelled room with a high chimney-piece, and a capacious chimney, up which you could have driven one of the new patent cabs, wheels and all.", "매너 농장의 가장 좋은 거실은, 높직한 벽난로 선반과 널찍한 굴뚝을 갖춘, 길고 어두운 판벽 두른 좋은 방이었다. 그 굴뚝은 새로 나온 특허 마차를 바퀴까지 통째로 몰고 올라갈 수 있을 만큼 컸다."], ["At the upper end of the room, seated in a shady bower of holly and evergreens were the two best fiddlers, and the only harp, in all Muggleton.", "방 위쪽 끝에는 호랑가시나무와 상록수로 그늘진 정자처럼 꾸민 자리에, 머글턴 전체에서 으뜸가는 바이올린 주자 둘과 단 하나뿐인 하프가 앉아 있었다."], ["In all sorts of recesses, and on all kinds of brackets, stood massive old silver candlesticks with four branches each.", "온갖 벽감과 온갖 받침 위에는, 저마다 네 갈래로 갈라진 묵직한 옛 은촛대가 서 있었다."], ["The carpet was up, the candles burned bright, the fire blazed and crackled on the hearth, and merry voices and light-hearted laughter rang through the room.", "양탄자는 걷어 두었고, 촛불은 환히 타올랐고, 화롯불은 벽난로에서 활활 타며 탁탁거렸고, 흥겨운 목소리와 명랑한 웃음이 방 안에 울려 퍼졌다."], ["If any of the old English yeomen had turned into fairies when they died, it was just the place in which they would have held their revels.", "만약 옛 잉글랜드 자영농 중 누군가가 죽어서 요정이 되었다면, 바로 이런 곳이 그들이 잔치를 벌일 만한 자리였을 것이다."]], [["If anything could have added to the interest of this agreeable scene, it would have been the remarkable fact of Mr. Pickwick’s appearing without his gaiters, for the first time within the memory of his oldest friends.", "이 유쾌한 정경의 흥취에 무언가를 더할 수 있는 것이 있었다면, 그것은 픽윅 씨가, 그의 가장 오랜 친구들의 기억으로도 처음으로, 각반 없이 나타났다는 주목할 만한 사실이었으리라."]], [["‘You mean to dance?’ said Wardle.", "\"춤추실 작정이오?\" 워들이 말했다."]], [["‘Of course I do,’ replied Mr.", "\"물론이지.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다."], ["Pickwick. ‘Don’t you see I am dressed for the purpose?’ Mr.", "\"보다시피 내가 그러려고 차려입지 않았소?\""], ["Pickwick called attention to his speckled silk stockings, and smartly tied pumps.", "픽윅 씨는 자기가 신은 얼룩무늬 비단 양말과 맵시 있게 끈을 묶은 펌프스 구두에 눈길을 모았다."]], [["‘You in silk stockings!’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"당신이 비단 양말을!\""], ["Tupman jocosely.", "텁먼 씨가 농조로 외쳤다."]], [["‘And why not, sir--why not?’ said Mr.", "\"왜 안 되겠소, 이 사람아 ― 왜 안 돼?\""], ["Pickwick, turning warmly upon him.", "픽윅 씨가 발끈하며 그를 향해 돌아서며 말했다."]], [["‘Oh, of course there is no reason why you shouldn’t wear them,’ responded Mr.", "\"오, 물론 당신이 그걸 신으면 안 될 이유야 없지요.\""], ["Tupman.", "텁먼 씨가 응수했다."]], [["‘I imagine not, sir--I imagine not,’ said Mr.", "\"나도 없다고 보오, 이 사람아 ― 없다고 보오.\""], ["Pickwick, in a very peremptory tone.", "픽윅 씨가 자못 단호한 어조로 말했다."]], [["Mr.", "텁먼 씨는 웃어 볼까 했으나, 이게 진지한 문제임을 알아챘다."], ["Tupman had contemplated a laugh, but he found it was a serious matter; so he looked grave, and said they were a pretty pattern.", "그래서 정색하고는, 무늬가 예쁘다고 말했다."]], [["‘I hope they are,’ said Mr.", "\"그러길 바라오.\""], ["Pickwick, fixing his eyes upon his friend.", "픽윅 씨가 친구에게 눈을 박으며 말했다."], ["‘You see nothing extraordinary in the stockings, as stockings, I trust, Sir?’", "\"양말을 양말로서 볼 때, 거기에 별난 데라곤 없을 줄로 믿소만, 그렇지 않소?\""]], [["‘Certainly not.", "\"물론이지요."], ["Oh, certainly not,’ replied Mr. Tupman.", "오, 물론 없고말고요.\""], ["He walked away; and Mr.", "텁먼 씨가 대답했다."], ["Pickwick’s countenance resumed its customary benign expression.", "그는 자리를 떴고, 픽윅 씨의 낯빛은 여느 때의 인자한 표정으로 돌아갔다."]], [["‘We are all ready, I believe,’ said Mr.", "\"우리 다 준비된 것 같소이다.\""], ["Pickwick, who was stationed with the old lady at the top of the dance, and had already made four false starts, in his excessive anxiety to commence.", "픽윅 씨가 말했으니, 그는 노부인과 함께 춤의 맨 앞자리에 서 있었고, 시작하고 싶은 지나친 조바심에 벌써 네 번이나 헛출발을 한 참이었다."]], [["‘Then begin at once,’ said Wardle.", "\"그럼 당장 시작하시오.\" 워들이 말했다."], ["‘Now!’", "\"자!\""]], [["Up struck the two fiddles and the one harp, and off went Mr.", "바이올린 둘과 하프 하나가 울려 퍼지자, 픽윅 씨가 손을 엇잡는 동작으로 막 나아가려는데, 사방에서 일제히 손뼉을 치며 \"멈춰요, 멈춰!\""], ["Pickwick into hands across, when there was a general clapping of hands, and a cry of ‘Stop, stop!’", "하는 외침이 일었다."]], [["‘What’s the matter?’ said Mr.", "\"무슨 일이오?\""], ["Pickwick, who was only brought to, by the fiddles and harp desisting, and could have been stopped by no other earthly power, if the house had been on fire.", "픽윅 씨가 말했으니, 그는 바이올린과 하프가 멎고서야 겨우 정신이 들었지, 집에 불이 났다 한들 이 세상 어떤 힘으로도 그를 멈출 수 없었을 것이다."]], [["‘Where’s Arabella Allen?’ cried a dozen voices.", "\"아라벨라 앨런은 어딨소?\" 열댓 목소리가 외쳤다."]], [["‘And Winkle?’ added Mr.", "\"그리고 윈클은?\""], ["Tupman.", "텁먼 씨가 덧붙였다."]], [["‘Here we are!’ exclaimed that gentleman, emerging with his pretty companion from the corner; as he did so, it would have been hard to tell which was the redder in the face, he or the young lady with the black eyes.", "\"여기 있소!\" 그 신사가 그의 어여쁜 동행과 함께 구석에서 나오며 외쳤다. 그렇게 나올 때, 그와 검은 눈의 젊은 숙녀 중 누구의 얼굴이 더 빨갰는지 가려내기란 어려운 일이었으리라."]], [["‘What an extraordinary thing it is, Winkle,’ said Mr.", "\"참 별난 일이로군, 윈클.\""], ["Pickwick, rather pettishly, ‘that you couldn’t have taken your place before.’", "픽윅 씨가 다소 부루퉁하게 말했다. \"진작 제자리에 와 있을 수가 없었다니 말이오.\""]], [["‘Not at all extraordinary,’ said Mr.", "\"전혀 별난 일이 아닙니다.\""], ["Winkle.", "윈클 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Well,’ said Mr.", "\"글쎄.\""], ["Pickwick, with a very expressive smile, as his eyes rested on Arabella, ‘well, I don’t know that it was extraordinary, either, after all.’", "픽윅 씨가, 아라벨라에게 눈길을 멈춘 채 자못 의미심장한 미소를 띠며 말했다. \"글쎄, 따지고 보면 그게 그리 별난 일이었는지도 잘 모르겠군그래.\""]], [["However, there was no time to think more about the matter, for the fiddles and harp began in real earnest. Away went Mr.", "그러나 그 일을 더 생각할 겨를은 없었으니, 바이올린과 하프가 본격적으로 시작한 까닭이다."], ["Pickwick--hands across--down the middle to the very end of the room, and half-way up the chimney, back again to the door--poussette everywhere--loud stamp on the ground--ready for the next couple--off again--all the figure over once more--another stamp to beat out the time--next couple, and the next, and the next again--never was such going; at last, after they had reached the bottom of the dance, and full fourteen couple after the old lady had retired in an exhausted state, and the clergyman’s wife had been substituted in her stead, did that gentleman, when there was no demand whatever on his exertions, keep perpetually dancing in his place, to keep time to the music, smiling on his partner all the while with a blandness of demeanour which baffles all description.", "픽윅 씨가 내달았다 ― 손을 엇잡고 ― 한가운데를 따라 방 끝까지, 그리고 굴뚝 절반쯤까지 올라갔다가, 다시 문 쪽으로 돌아오고 ― 도처에서 푸세트를 추고 ― 바닥을 쾅 구르고 ― 다음 짝을 맞을 채비를 하고 ― 다시 내달았다 ― 그 도형 전체를 한 번 더 거치고 ― 박자를 맞추려 또 한 번 쾅 구르고 ― 다음 짝, 또 다음 짝, 또 그다음 짝 ― 일찍이 그런 신명은 없었다. 마침내 그들이 춤의 맨 끝에 다다르고, 노부인이 기진하여 물러나고 그 자리에 목사 부인이 대신 들어선 뒤로도 족히 열네 짝이 지난 다음에도, 그 신사는 정작 제 수고가 전혀 필요 없는 마당에까지, 음악에 박자를 맞추느라 제자리에서 쉼 없이 춤을 추며, 그 내내 짝을 향해 도무지 형용할 수 없는 부드러운 태도로 미소 지었다."]], [["Long before Mr. Pickwick was weary of dancing, the newly-married couple had retired from the scene.", "픽윅 씨가 춤에 싫증을 내기 훨씬 전에, 갓 결혼한 부부는 그 자리에서 물러갔다."], ["There was a glorious supper downstairs, notwithstanding, and a good long sitting after it; and when Mr.", "그래도 아래층에는 성대한 야식이 있었고, 그 뒤에는 한참 오래도록 앉아 있는 자리가 이어졌다."], ["Pickwick awoke, late the next morning, he had a confused recollection of having, severally and confidentially, invited somewhere about five-and-forty people to dine with him at the George and Vulture, the very first time they came to London; which Mr.", "그리고 이튿날 아침 늦게 픽윅 씨가 잠에서 깼을 때, 그는 어렴풋한 기억으로, 어딘가에서 한 사람 한 사람 따로따로 은밀하게, 마흔다섯 명쯤 되는 사람들을, 그들이 런던에 오는 바로 그 첫날 조지 앤드 벌처 여관에서 자기와 함께 식사하자고 초대했다는 것을 떠올렸다."], ["Pickwick rightly considered a pretty certain indication of his having taken something besides exercise, on the previous night.", "픽윅 씨는 이것을, 그가 전날 밤에 운동 말고도 무언가를 더 들었다는 자못 확실한 징표로 옳게 여겼다."]], [["‘And so your family has games in the kitchen to-night, my dear, has they?’ inquired Sam of Emma.", "\"그래, 자네 댁에서는 오늘 밤 부엌에서 놀이를 한다지, 응, 그렇지?\" 셈이 엠마에게 물었다."]], [["‘Yes, Mr.", "\"네, 웰러 씨.\""], ["Weller,’ replied Emma; ‘we always have on Christmas Eve.", "엠마가 대답했다. \"우린 크리스마스이브엔 늘 그래요."], ["Master wouldn’t neglect to keep it up on any account.’", "주인 어른은 무슨 일이 있어도 그걸 거르지 않으세요.\""]], [["‘Your master’s a wery pretty notion of keeping anythin’ up, my dear,’ said Mr.", "\"자네 주인 어른은 뭐든 챙기는 데 아주 멋진 생각을 갖고 계신 분이야, 아가씨.\""], ["Weller; ‘I never see such a sensible sort of man as he is, or such a reg’lar gen’l’m’n.’", "웰러 씨가 말했다. \"내 그분만큼 분별 있는 사람도, 그분만큼 진짜배기 신사도 본 적이 없네.\""]], [["Oh, that he is!’ said the fat boy, joining in the conversation; ‘don’t he breed nice pork!’ The fat youth gave a semi-cannibalic leer at Mr.", "\"오, 그렇고말고요!\" 뚱보 소년이 대화에 끼어들며 말했다."], ["Weller, as he thought of the roast legs and gravy.", "\"그분이 키우는 돼지고기는 정말 좋다니까요!\" 뚱보 젊은이는 구운 다리와 육즙을 떠올리며 웰러 씨를 향해 반쯤 식인종 같은 곁눈질을 보냈다."]], [["‘Oh, you’ve woke up, at last, have you?’ said Sam.", "\"오, 드디어 깼냐, 응?\" 셈이 말했다."]], [["The fat boy nodded.", "뚱보 소년이 고개를 끄덕였다."]], [["‘I’ll tell you what it is, young boa-constructer,’ said Mr.", "\"내 한마디 해 두마, 이 어린 보아뱀아.\""], ["Weller impressively; ‘if you don’t sleep a little less, and exercise a little more, wen you comes to be a man you’ll lay yourself open to the same sort of personal inconwenience as was inflicted on the old gen’l’m’n as wore the pigtail.’", "웰러 씨가 자못 인상 깊게 말했다. \"네가 잠을 좀 덜 자고 운동을 좀 더 하지 않으면, 어른이 됐을 때 변발 늘어뜨린 그 노신사 양반이 당한 거랑 똑같은 신변의 불편을 당하기 십상이야.\""]], [["‘What did they do to him?’ inquired the fat boy, in a faltering voice.", "\"그 사람한테 무슨 짓을 했는데요?\" 뚱보 소년이 떨리는 목소리로 물었다."]], [["‘I’m a-going to tell you,’ replied Mr.", "\"이제 말해 주마.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다."], ["Weller; ‘he was one o’ the largest patterns as was ever turned out--reg’lar fat man, as hadn’t caught a glimpse of his own shoes for five-and-forty year.’", "\"그분은 여태껏 빚어낸 것 중에 가장 큰 표본 중 하나였지 ― 진짜배기 뚱뚱한 양반이라, 마흔다섯 해 동안 제 발끝을 한 번도 본 적이 없었어.\""]], [["‘Lor!’ exclaimed Emma.", "\"어머나!\" 엠마가 외쳤다."]], [["‘No, that he hadn’t, my dear,’ said Mr.", "\"그래, 한 번도 못 봤지, 아가씨.\""], ["Weller; ‘and if you’d put an exact model of his own legs on the dinin’-table afore him, he wouldn’t ha’ known ‘em.", "웰러 씨가 말했다. \"그리고 그분 자기 다리를 똑같이 본떠 만들어 눈앞 식탁에 올려놨대도, 자기 거라곤 알아보지 못했을 거야."], ["Well, he always walks to his office with a wery handsome gold watch-chain hanging out, about a foot and a quarter, and a gold watch in his fob pocket as was worth--I’m afraid to say how much, but as much as a watch can be--a large, heavy, round manufacter, as stout for a watch, as he was for a man, and with a big face in proportion.", "자, 그분은 늘 한 자 반쯤 늘어뜨린 아주 근사한 금시곗줄에다, 안주머니엔 값나가는 금시계를 차고 사무실로 걸어갔는데 ― 얼마짜리라고 말하기도 겁이 나지만, 시계로서 값나갈 수 있는 만큼은 나가는 ― 크고 묵직하고 둥근 물건이라, 시계로 치면 그분이 사람으로 치는 만큼이나 굵직했고, 거기 걸맞게 큼직한 문자판이 달려 있었지. '그 시계 가지고 다니지 않는 게 좋을걸,'"], ["“You’d better not carry that ‘ere watch,”", "그 노신사의 친구들이 말했어."], ["says the old gen’l’m’n’s friends, “you’ll be robbed on it,”", "'도둑맞을 거야,' 하고 말이야."], ["says they.", "'내가?'"], ["“Shall I?”", "그분이 말했지."], ["says he. “Yes, you will,”", "'그래, 자네 그럴 거야,'"], ["says they.", "친구들이 말했어."], ["“Well,”", "'글쎄,'"], ["says he, “I should like to see the thief as could get this here watch out, for I’m blessed if I ever can, it’s such a tight fit,”", "그분이 말했지. '이 시계를 빼낼 수 있는 도둑이 있다면 어디 한번 보고 싶군."], ["says he, “and wenever I vants to know what’s o’clock, I’m obliged to stare into the bakers’ shops,” he says.", "나도 도무지 못 빼낼 만큼 어찌나 꽉 끼는지, 시간이 알고 싶을 때마다 빵집 진열창을 들여다봐야 할 판이거든,'"], ["Well, then he laughs as hearty as if he was a-goin’ to pieces, and out he walks agin with his powdered head and pigtail, and rolls down the Strand with the chain hangin’ out furder than ever, and the great round watch almost bustin’ through his gray kersey smalls.", "하고 말이야. 그러고는 마치 산산조각이라도 날 듯이 너털웃음을 웃고는, 분 바른 머리에 변발을 늘어뜨리고 다시 걸어 나가, 시곗줄을 그 어느 때보다 더 길게 늘어뜨린 채, 그 크고 둥근 시계가 잿빛 커지 바지를 거의 뚫고 나올 듯한 모양새로 스트랜드 거리를 어슬렁어슬렁 내려갔지."], ["There warn’t a pickpocket in all London as didn’t take a pull at that chain, but the chain ‘ud never break, and the watch ‘ud never come out, so they soon got tired of dragging such a heavy old gen’l’m’n along the pavement, and he’d go home and laugh till the pigtail wibrated like the penderlum of a Dutch clock.", "런던 천지 어느 소매치기 하나 그 줄을 잡아당겨 보지 않은 자가 없었지만, 줄은 절대 끊기지 않고 시계는 절대 빠져나오지 않으니, 그들은 그 묵직한 노신사를 보도 위로 끌고 다니는 데 곧 진력이 났고, 그분은 집에 가서 변발이 네덜란드 시계 추처럼 부르르 떨릴 만큼 웃어 댔지."], ["At last, one day the old gen’l’m’n was a-rollin’ along, and he sees a pickpocket as he know’d by sight, a-coming up, arm in arm with a little boy with a wery large head.", "그러던 어느 날, 그 노신사가 어슬렁거리며 가다가, 낯이 익은 어떤 소매치기 하나가 머리가 아주 큰 어린애와 팔짱을 끼고 오는 걸 봤어."], ["“Here’s a game,”", "'재미있게 됐군,'"], ["says the old gen’l’m’n to himself, “they’re a-goin’ to have another try, but it won’t do!”", "노신사가 속으로 말했지. '또 한번 해 보려는 모양인데, 어림없지!'"], ["So he begins a- chucklin’ wery hearty, wen, all of a sudden, the little boy leaves hold of the pickpocket’s arm, and rushes head foremost straight into the old gen’l’m’n’s stomach, and for a moment doubles him right up with the pain.", "그러고는 자못 흥겹게 낄낄대기 시작했는데, 별안간 그 어린애가 소매치기 팔을 놓더니 머리부터 곧장 노신사 배때기로 들이받아, 한순간 그 아픔에 그분을 그대로 허리가 꺾이게 만들었어."], ["“Murder!”", "'사람 살려!'"], ["says the old gen’l’m’n.", "노신사가 외쳤지."], ["“All right, Sir,”", "'됐습니다, 나리,'"], ["says the pickpocket, a-wisperin’ in his ear.", "소매치기가 그분 귀에 대고 속삭였어."], ["And wen he come straight agin, the watch and chain was gone, and what’s worse than that, the old gen’l’m’n’s digestion was all wrong ever afterwards, to the wery last day of his life; so just you look about you, young feller, and take care you don’t get too fat.’", "그리고 그분이 다시 몸을 곧추세웠을 땐, 시계도 줄도 사라지고 없었고, 그보다 더 나쁜 건, 그 뒤로 노신사의 소화가 평생 마지막 날까지 죄다 엉망이 돼 버렸다는 거야. 그러니 너도 정신 똑바로 차리고, 이 젊은 친구야, 너무 뚱뚱해지지 않게 조심해라.\""]], [["As Mr.", "웰러 씨가 이 교훈적인 이야기를 매듭짓자 ― 뚱보 소년은 자못 감명받은 듯했다 ― 셋은 다 함께 큰 부엌으로 갔다."], ["Weller concluded this moral tale, with which the fat boy appeared much affected, they all three repaired to the large kitchen, in which the family were by this time assembled, according to annual custom on Christmas Eve, observed by old Wardle’s forefathers from time immemorial.", "거기엔 이때쯤 식구들이 다 모여 있었으니, 워들 영감의 조상들이 아득한 옛날부터 지켜 온, 크리스마스이브의 해마다의 관습을 따른 것이었다."]], [["From the centre of the ceiling of this kitchen, old Wardle had just suspended, with his own hands, a huge branch of mistletoe, and this same branch of mistletoe instantaneously gave rise to a scene of general and most delightful struggling and confusion; in the midst of which, Mr.", "이 부엌의 천장 한복판에, 워들 영감은 방금 제 손으로 커다란 겨우살이 가지 하나를 매달아 놓은 참이었고, 바로 이 겨우살이 가지가 즉각 온통 더없이 유쾌한 다툼과 소란의 한 장면을 빚어냈다."], ["Pickwick, with a gallantry that would have done honour to a descendant of Lady Tollimglower herself, took the old lady by the hand, led her beneath the mystic branch, and saluted her in all courtesy and decorum.", "그 와중에 픽윅 씨는, 톨림글로어 부인 자신의 후손에게도 손색없을 정중함으로, 노부인의 손을 잡아 그 신비로운 가지 아래로 인도하고는, 더없는 예의와 격식을 갖추어 그녀에게 입을 맞추었다."], ["The old lady submitted to this piece of practical politeness with all the dignity which befitted so important and serious a solemnity, but the younger ladies, not being so thoroughly imbued with a superstitious veneration for the custom, or imagining that the value of a salute is very much enhanced if it cost a little trouble to obtain it, screamed and struggled, and ran into corners, and threatened and remonstrated, and did everything but leave the room, until some of the less adventurous gentlemen were on the point of desisting, when they all at once found it useless to resist any longer, and submitted to be kissed with a good grace. Mr.", "노부인은 그토록 중대하고 엄숙한 의례에 걸맞은 온갖 위엄을 갖추어 이 실천적 예법의 행위에 응했으나, 그 관습에 대한 미신적 숭배에 그만큼 푹 젖어 있지 않았거나, 아니면 입맞춤의 가치란 그것을 얻는 데 약간의 수고가 든다면 한껏 높아진다고 여긴 젊은 숙녀들은, 비명을 지르고 발버둥치고 구석으로 달아나고 으름장을 놓고 항의를 하며, 방을 나가는 것만 빼고는 별짓을 다 하다가, 덜 모험적인 신사 몇몇이 막 단념하려는 참에, 다들 별안간 더 이상 버텨 봐야 소용없음을 깨닫고는 흔쾌히 입맞춤을 받아들였다."], ["Winkle kissed the young lady with the black eyes, and Mr. Snodgrass kissed Emily; and Mr.", "윈클 씨는 검은 눈의 젊은 숙녀에게 입을 맞췄고, 스노드그래스 씨는 에밀리에게 입을 맞췄다."], ["Weller, not being particular about the form of being under the mistletoe, kissed Emma and the other female servants, just as he caught them.", "그리고 겨우살이 아래에 있어야 한다는 형식에 그리 까다롭지 않은 웰러 씨는, 엠마와 다른 하녀들을 붙잡는 족족 입을 맞췄다."], ["As to the poor relations, they kissed everybody, not even excepting the plainer portions of the young lady visitors, who, in their excessive confusion, ran right under the mistletoe, as soon as it was hung up, without knowing it!", "가난한 친척들로 말하자면, 그들은 누구에게나 다 입을 맞췄으니, 젊은 숙녀 손님들 중 다소 못생긴 축들조차 예외가 아니었다. 그 숙녀들은 겨우살이가 매달리기가 무섭게 너무도 허둥대다가 저도 모르게 그 바로 아래로 달려 들어가 버렸던 것이다!"], ["Wardle stood with his back to the fire, surveying the whole scene, with the utmost satisfaction; and the fat boy took the opportunity of appropriating to his own use, and summarily devouring, a particularly fine mince-pie, that had been carefully put by, for somebody else.", "워들은 불에 등을 댄 채 서서, 더없는 흡족함으로 그 모든 정경을 둘러보았다. 그리고 뚱보 소년은 그 틈을 타, 누군가 다른 사람을 위해 고이 챙겨 두었던 유난히 좋은 민스파이 하나를 제 것으로 가로채어 단숨에 먹어 치웠다."]], [["Now, the screaming had subsided, and faces were in a glow, and curls in a tangle, and Mr.", "이제 비명은 가라앉았고, 얼굴들은 발그레 달아올랐고, 머리채는 헝클어졌다."], ["Pickwick, after kissing the old lady as before mentioned, was standing under the mistletoe, looking with a very pleased countenance on all that was passing around him, when the young lady with the black eyes, after a little whispering with the other young ladies, made a sudden dart forward, and, putting her arm round Mr.", "픽윅 씨는 앞서 말한 대로 노부인에게 입을 맞춘 뒤 겨우살이 아래에 서서, 자못 흐뭇한 낯빛으로 둘레에서 벌어지는 모든 일을 바라보고 있었다. 그때 검은 눈의 젊은 숙녀가 다른 젊은 숙녀들과 잠깐 속닥거리더니, 별안간 앞으로 휙 달려 나와, 픽윅 씨의 목에 팔을 두르고는 그의 왼뺨에 다정하게 입을 맞추었다."], ["Pickwick’s neck, saluted him affectionately on the left cheek; and before Mr. Pickwick distinctly knew what was the matter, he was surrounded by the whole body, and kissed by every one of them.", "그리고 픽윅 씨가 무슨 영문인지 채 또렷이 알기도 전에, 그는 그 무리 전체에 둘러싸여, 그들 하나하나에게서 입맞춤을 받았다."]], [["It was a pleasant thing to see Mr. Pickwick in the centre of the group, now pulled this way, and then that, and first kissed on the chin, and then on the nose, and then on the spectacles, and to hear the peals of laughter which were raised on every side; but it was a still more pleasant thing to see Mr.", "무리 한복판에 선 픽윅 씨가, 이리 끌리고 저리 끌리며, 턱에 입맞춤을 받고, 다음엔 코에, 다음엔 안경에 입맞춤을 받는 꼴을 보고, 사방에서 터져 나오는 웃음의 폭소를 듣는 것은 유쾌한 일이었다."], ["Pickwick, blinded shortly afterwards with a silk handkerchief, falling up against the wall, and scrambling into corners, and going through all the mysteries of blind-man’s buff, with the utmost relish for the game, until at last he caught one of the poor relations, and then had to evade the blind-man himself, which he did with a nimbleness and agility that elicited the admiration and applause of all beholders.", "그러나 그보다 더 유쾌한 것은, 잠시 뒤 비단 손수건으로 눈을 가린 픽윅 씨가 벽에 가서 부딪히고, 구석으로 기어들고, 그 놀이에 더없는 흥을 붙여 술래잡기의 온갖 신비를 다 거치다가, 마침내 가난한 친척 하나를 붙잡고는 이번엔 술래 자신을 따돌려야 하게 되자, 모든 구경꾼의 감탄과 갈채를 자아낼 만한 날쌤과 민첩함으로 그것을 해내는 꼴을 보는 것이었다."], ["The poor relations caught the people who they thought would like it, and, when the game flagged, got caught themselves.", "가난한 친척들은 좋아할 성싶은 사람들을 붙잡았고, 놀이가 시들해지면 제가 붙잡혀 주었다."], ["When they all tired of blind-man’s buff, there was a great game at snap- dragon, and when fingers enough were burned with that, and all the raisins were gone, they sat down by the huge fire of blazing logs to a substantial supper, and a mighty bowl of wassail, something smaller than an ordinary wash-house copper, in which the hot apples were hissing and bubbling with a rich look, and a jolly sound, that were perfectly irresistible.", "그들이 모두 술래잡기에 싫증이 났을 때, 스냅드래건이라는 큰 놀이판이 벌어졌고, 거기서 충분히 많은 손가락을 데고 건포도가 죄다 동나자, 그들은 활활 타오르는 장작불의 거대한 불가에 둘러앉아 푸짐한 야식과, 여느 세탁장 구리솥보다 조금 작을 만한 거대한 와사일 사발을 들었다. 그 사발 속에서는 뜨거운 사과들이 쉭쉭거리고 보글거리며, 도무지 뿌리칠 수 없는 먹음직스러운 빛깔과 흥겨운 소리를 내고 있었다."]], [["‘This,’ said Mr.", "\"이것이.\" 픽윅 씨가 둘레를 둘러보며 말했다."], ["Pickwick, looking round him, ‘this is, indeed, comfort.’ ‘Our invariable custom,’ replied Mr.", "\"이것이 진정한 안락이로구려.\" \"우리 한결같은 관습이지요.\""], ["Wardle.", "워들 씨가 대답했다."], ["‘Everybody sits down with us on Christmas Eve, as you see them now--servants and all; and here we wait, until the clock strikes twelve, to usher Christmas in, and beguile the time with forfeits and old stories.", "\"크리스마스이브엔 누구나 다, 지금 자네가 보는 대로 우리와 함께 앉습니다 ― 하인들까지 죄다 말이오. 그리고 우리는 여기서 시계가 열두 시를 칠 때까지 기다리며 크리스마스를 맞아들이고, 벌금 놀이와 옛이야기로 시간을 보내지요."], ["Trundle, my boy, rake up the fire.’", "트런들, 이 사람아, 불을 좀 돋워 주게.\""]], [["Up flew the bright sparks in myriads as the logs were stirred.", "장작을 휘젓자 환한 불똥이 무수히 솟아올랐다."], ["The deep red blaze sent forth a rich glow, that penetrated into the farthest corner of the room, and cast its cheerful tint on every face.", "짙붉은 불꽃이 풍성한 빛을 내뿜어 방의 가장 먼 구석까지 스며들고, 그 명랑한 빛깔을 모든 얼굴에 드리웠다."]], [["‘Come,’ said Wardle, ‘a song--a Christmas song!", "\"자.\" 워들이 말했다."], ["I’ll give you one, in default of a better.’", "\"노래 ― 크리스마스 노래! 더 나은 게 없으니, 내가 한 곡 들려주리다.\""]], [["‘Bravo!’ said Mr.", "\"좋소!\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Fill up,’ cried Wardle.", "\"잔을 채우게.\" 워들이 외쳤다."], ["‘It will be two hours, good, before you see the bottom of the bowl through the deep rich colour of the wassail; fill up all round, and now for the song.’", "\"그 와사일의 짙고 풍성한 빛깔 너머로 사발 바닥을 보게 되기까지는 족히 두 시간은 걸릴 거요. 다들 잔을 채우고, 자 이제 노래요.\""]], [["Thus saying, the merry old gentleman, in a good, round, sturdy voice, commenced without more ado--", "이렇게 말하며, 그 흥겨운 노신사는 좋고 우렁차고 다부진 목소리로 더 지체 없이 시작했다. 크리스마스 캐럴"]], [["# A CHRISTMAS CAROL", "# A CHRISTMAS CAROL"]], [["‘I care not for Spring; on his fickle wing Let the blossoms and buds be borne; He woos them amain with his treacherous rain, And he scatters them ere the morn.", "\"봄 따위 나는 아랑곳없네. 그 변덕스런 날개에 / 꽃송이와 봉오리일랑 실려 가라지."], ["An inconstant elf, he knows not himself, Nor his own changing mind an hour, He’ll smile in your face, and, with wry grimace, He’ll wither your youngest flower.", "그는 음흉한 비로 그것들을 한껏 꾀어내고는 / 동트기도 전에 흩뿌려 버린다네. 변덕스런 요정이라, 제 자신도 / 한 시간이면 바뀌는 제 마음도 알지 못하니, 네 얼굴을 향해 미소 짓다가도, 찡그린 상으로 / 네 가장 어린 꽃을 시들게 하리라."]], [["‘Let the Summer sun to his bright home run, He shall never be sought by me; When he’s dimmed by a cloud I can laugh aloud And care not how sulky he be!", "\"여름 해일랑 그 밝은 집으로 내달려 가라지, / 나는 결코 그를 찾지 않으리. 구름에 그가 흐려질 때 나는 큰 소리로 웃을 수 있으니 / 그가 아무리 시무룩한들 무슨 상관이랴!"], ["For his darling child is the madness wild That sports in fierce fever’s train; And when love is too strong, it don’t last long, As many have found to their pain.", "그의 귀염둥이 자식이란 사나운 열병의 행렬에 / 날뛰는 거친 광기인 것을. 그리고 사랑이 너무 강하면, 오래가지 못하는 법이니, / 많은 이가 제 아픔으로 알아낸 바라네."]], [["‘A mild harvest night, by the tranquil light Of the modest and gentle moon, Has a far sweeter sheen for me, I ween, Than the broad and unblushing noon.", "\"고요한 추수의 밤, 정숙하고 부드러운 / 달의 잔잔한 빛 아래라면, 내 생각에 그것은 넓고 부끄럼 없는 한낮보다 / 내게 훨씬 더 고운 광채를 띤다네."], ["But every leaf awakens my grief, As it lieth beneath the tree; So let Autumn air be never so fair, It by no means agrees with me.", "허나 잎새마다 내 슬픔을 일깨우니, / 그것이 나무 아래 누워 있는 까닭이라. 그러니 가을 공기 아무리 고운들, / 그것은 결코 내겐 맞지 않다네."]], [["‘But my song I troll out, for Christmas Stout, The hearty, the true, and the bold; A bumper I drain, and with might and main Give three cheers for this Christmas old!", "\"허나 내 노래를 소리 높여 부르노라, 든든한 크리스마스를 위해, / 정 깊고, 참되고, 담대한 그를 위해. 가득 찬 잔을 비우고, 있는 힘껏 / 이 늙은 크리스마스를 위해 세 번 환호하리!"], ["We’ll usher him in with a merry din That shall gladden his joyous heart, And we’ll keep him up, while there’s bite or sup, And in fellowship good, we’ll part.", "우리는 흥겨운 떠들썩함으로 그를 맞아들여 / 그 기쁜 가슴을 즐겁게 하고, 한 입, 한 모금 남아 있는 동안 그를 받들다가, / 좋은 벗됨 속에 작별하리라."], ["‘In his fine honest pride, he scorns to hide One jot of his hard-weather scars; They’re no disgrace, for there’s much the same trace On the cheeks of our bravest tars.", "그 곱고 정직한 긍지로, 그는 모진 비바람의 흉터를 / 한 점도 감추기를 마다하니, 그건 부끄러움이 아니라네, 우리의 가장 용감한 뱃사람들의 / 뺨에도 꼭 같은 자취가 숱하니."], ["Then again I sing till the roof doth ring And it echoes from wall to wall--To the stout old wight, fair welcome to-night, As the King of the Seasons all!’", "그러니 다시 한 번 노래하리, 지붕이 울리고 / 벽에서 벽으로 메아리칠 때까지 ― 그 든든한 옛 호걸에게, 오늘 밤 어서 오시라고, / 모든 계절의 임금님이시여!\""]], [["This song was tumultuously applauded--for friends and dependents make a capital audience--and the poor relations, especially, were in perfect ecstasies of rapture.", "이 노래는 떠들썩한 갈채를 받았으니 ― 벗들과 식솔들이야말로 일품 청중인 까닭이다 ― 특히 가난한 친척들은 완전한 환희의 황홀경에 빠져 있었다."], ["Again was the fire replenished, and again went the wassail round.", "다시 불을 돋우었고, 다시 와사일이 한 바퀴 돌았다."]], [["‘How it snows!’ said one of the men, in a low tone.", "\"눈이 어찌나 내리는지!\" 사내들 중 하나가 낮은 목소리로 말했다."]], [["‘Snows, does it?’ said Wardle.", "\"눈이 내려, 그래?\" 워들이 말했다."]], [["‘Rough, cold night, Sir,’ replied the man; ‘and there’s a wind got up, that drifts it across the fields, in a thick white cloud.’", "\"모질고 추운 밤입니다, 나리.\" 그 사내가 대답했다. \"게다가 바람이 일어, 눈을 들판 너머로 짙은 흰 구름처럼 몰아가고 있어요.\""]], [["‘What does Jem say?’ inquired the old lady.", "\"젬이 뭐라는 거냐?\" 노부인이 물었다."], ["‘There ain’t anything the matter, is there?’", "\"무슨 일이 있는 건 아니지, 응?\""]], [["‘No, no, mother,’ replied Wardle; ‘he says there’s a snowdrift, and a wind that’s piercing cold.", "\"아니에요, 아니에요, 어머니.\" 워들이 대답했다."], ["I should know that, by the way it rumbles in the chimney.’", "\"눈보라가 치고, 바람이 살을 에듯 차다는군요. 굴뚝에서 우르릉거리는 소리를 들으니 저도 알겠어요.\""]], [["‘Ah!’ said the old lady, ‘there was just such a wind, and just such a fall of snow, a good many years back, I recollect--just five years before your poor father died. It was a Christmas Eve, too; and I remember that on that very night he told us the story about the goblins that carried away old Gabriel Grub.’", "\"아!\" 노부인이 말했다. \"꼭 이런 바람, 꼭 이런 눈이 내린 적이 있었지, 여러 해 전에, 내 기억나는구나 ― 네 가엾은 아버지가 돌아가시기 꼭 다섯 해 전에. 그날도 크리스마스이브였지. 그리고 기억하기로, 바로 그날 밤에 그이가 도깨비들이 늙은 게이브리얼 그럽을 데려가 버린 이야기를 우리에게 들려줬단다.\""]], [["‘The story about what?’ said Mr.", "\"무엇에 관한 이야기라고요?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Oh, nothing, nothing,’ replied Wardle.", "\"오, 아무것도, 아무것도 아니오.\""], ["‘About an old sexton, that the good people down here suppose to have been carried away by goblins.’", "워들이 대답했다. \"이 고장 선량한 사람들이 도깨비들에게 끌려갔다고 여기는 어느 늙은 교회지기에 관한 이야기지요.\""]], [["‘Suppose!’ ejaculated the old lady.", "\"여긴다고!\" 노부인이 내뱉었다."], ["‘Is there anybody hardy enough to disbelieve it?", "\"그걸 안 믿을 만큼 간 큰 사람이 어디 있단 말이냐?"], ["Suppose!", "여긴다니!"], ["Haven’t you heard ever since you were a child, that he was carried away by the goblins, and don’t you know he was?’", "너도 어렸을 적부터, 그이가 도깨비들에게 끌려갔다는 걸 줄곧 들어 오지 않았느냐, 그리고 그게 사실인 줄 너도 알지 않느냐?\""]], [["‘Very well, mother, he was, if you like,’ said Wardle laughing.", "\"알았어요, 어머니, 그러셨다 치죠, 어머니가 그러길 바라신다면.\" 워들이 웃으며 말했다."], ["‘He was carried away by goblins, Pickwick; and there’s an end of the matter.’", "\"그분은 도깨비들에게 끌려갔습니다, 픽윅. 자, 그걸로 그 일은 끝입니다.\""]], [["‘No, no,’ said Mr.", "\"아니, 아니지요.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick, ‘not an end of it, I assure you; for I must hear how, and why, and all about it.’", "\"끝이 아닙니다, 정말이지. 어떻게, 왜, 그리고 그에 관한 모든 것을 저는 꼭 들어야겠소.\""]], [["Wardle smiled, as every head was bent forward to hear, and filling out the wassail with no stinted hand, nodded a health to Mr. Pickwick, and began as follows--", "워들은 모두가 들으려고 고개를 앞으로 숙이자 빙긋 웃고는, 인색하지 않은 손길로 와사일을 따라 채우고, 픽윅 씨에게 고개를 끄덕여 건강을 빌고는, 다음과 같이 이야기를 시작했다."]], [["But bless our editorial heart, what a long chapter we have been betrayed into!", "그러나 우리의 편집자 된 가슴에 복이 있기를, 우리가 얼마나 긴 장에 그만 빠져들고 말았는가!"], ["We had quite forgotten all such petty restrictions as chapters, we solemnly declare.", "우리는 장이라느니 하는 그런 자질구레한 제약 따위는 깡그리 잊고 있었노라고, 우리는 엄숙히 고백한다."], ["So here goes, to give the goblin a fair start in a new one.", "그러니 자 이렇게, 도깨비에게 새 장에서 정정당당한 출발을 안겨 주기로 하자."], ["A clear stage and no favour for the goblins, ladies and gentlemen, if you please.", "도깨비들을 위해 무대를 비워 두고 봐주기란 없기를, 신사 숙녀 여러분, 부디 그리해 주시기를."]], [["# CHAPTER XXIX. THE STORY OF THE GOBLINS WHO STOLE A SEXTON", "# CHAPTER XXIX. THE STORY OF THE GOBLINS WHO STOLE A SEXTON"]], [["‘In an old abbey town, down in this part of the country, a long, long while ago--so long, that the story must be a true one, because our great-grandfathers implicitly believed it--there officiated as sexton and grave-digger in the churchyard, one Gabriel Grub.", "\"아주 오래전, 이 고장 어느 옛 수도원 도시에서—얼마나 오래전인고 하니, 우리 증조부들이 그 이야기를 곧이곧대로 믿었을 만큼이나 오래전이라, 그러니 그 이야기는 틀림없이 사실일 게요—개브리얼 그럽이라는 이가 교회 묘지의 교회지기 겸 무덤 파는 일꾼으로 일하고 있었더랍니다. 사람이 교회지기라 해서, 또 늘 죽음의 표상들에 둘러싸여 산다 해서, 반드시 침울하고 우울한 사람이 되어야 한다는 법은 결코 없지요."], ["It by no means follows that because a man is a sexton, and constantly surrounded by the emblems of mortality, therefore he should be a morose and melancholy man; your undertakers are the merriest fellows in the world; and I once had the honour of being on intimate terms with a mute, who in private life, and off duty, was as comical and jocose a little fellow as ever chirped out a devil-may-care song, without a hitch in his memory, or drained off a good stiff glass without stopping for breath.", "장의사들로 말하자면 세상에서 가장 유쾌한 친구들이거든요. 나도 한때 어느 상여꾼과 가까이 지내는 영광을 누린 적이 있는데, 그자는 사생활에서, 그러니까 일을 쉴 때면, 기억에 막힘 한 번 없이 '될 대로 되라'는 노래를 신나게 뽑아내거나, 숨 한 번 돌리지 않고 독한 술 한 잔을 쭉 들이켜는, 그런 익살스럽고 우스꽝스러운 작달막한 친구였답니다."], ["But notwithstanding these precedents to the contrary, Gabriel Grub was an ill-conditioned, cross-grained, surly fellow--a morose and lonely man, who consorted with nobody but himself, and an old wicker bottle which fitted into his large deep waistcoat pocket--and who eyed each merry face, as it passed him by, with such a deep scowl of malice and ill- humour, as it was difficult to meet without feeling something the worse for.", "하지만 이런 반대되는 선례들에도 불구하고, 개브리얼 그럽은 심사가 고약하고 비뚤어지고 무뚝뚝한 자였고—침울하고 외로운 사람이라, 제 자신과, 그리고 크고 깊은 조끼 주머니에 꼭 들어맞는 낡은 고리버들 술병 말고는 아무와도 어울리지 않았으며—제 곁을 스쳐 지나가는 유쾌한 얼굴들마다 어찌나 깊은 악의와 심술의 눈초리로 쏘아보던지, 그 시선을 마주하고서 무언가 언짢은 기분이 들지 않기란 좀처럼 어려운 일이었지요."]], [["‘A little before twilight, one Christmas Eve, Gabriel shouldered his spade, lighted his lantern, and betook himself towards the old churchyard; for he had got a grave to finish by next morning, and, feeling very low, he thought it might raise his spirits, perhaps, if he went on with his work at once.", "어느 크리스마스이브, 땅거미가 지기 조금 전에, 개브리얼은 삽을 둘러메고 등불을 켜고는 옛 교회 묘지로 향했답니다. 이튿날 아침까지 무덤 하나를 마저 파 놓아야 했고, 기분이 몹시 가라앉아 있던 터라, 어쩌면 당장 일을 시작하면 기운이 좀 날까 싶었던 게지요."], ["As he went his way, up the ancient street, he saw the cheerful light of the blazing fires gleam through the old casements, and heard the loud laugh and the cheerful shouts of those who were assembled around them; he marked the bustling preparations for next day’s cheer, and smelled the numerous savoury odours consequent thereupon, as they steamed up from the kitchen windows in clouds.", "그가 오래된 거리를 따라 올라가는 길에, 활활 타오르는 난롯불의 정겨운 빛이 낡은 여닫이창 너머로 새어 나오는 것을 보았고, 그 둘레에 모여 앉은 이들의 큰 웃음소리와 흥겨운 환성을 들었답니다. 이튿날 잔치를 위해 분주히 채비하는 모습을 눈여겨보았고, 거기서 비롯되어 부엌 창문으로 구름처럼 무럭무럭 피어오르는 갖가지 군침 도는 냄새를 맡았지요."], ["All this was gall and wormwood to the heart of Gabriel Grub; and when groups of children bounded out of the houses, tripped across the road, and were met, before they could knock at the opposite door, by half a dozen curly-headed little rascals who crowded round them as they flocked upstairs to spend the evening in their Christmas games, Gabriel smiled grimly, and clutched the handle of his spade with a firmer grasp, as he thought of measles, scarlet fever, thrush, whooping-cough, and a good many other sources of consolation besides.", "이 모든 것이 개브리얼 그럽의 마음에는 쓸개즙이요 쓴 쑥이었습니다. 그리고 아이들 무리가 집에서 우르르 뛰쳐나와 길을 가로질러 종종거리며 건너가, 미처 맞은편 문을 두드리기도 전에, 곱슬머리 꼬마 녀석 대여섯이 우르르 몰려나와 그들을 맞이하고는 다 함께 위층으로 몰려 올라가 크리스마스 놀이로 저녁을 보내려 할 때, 개브리얼은 홍역, 성홍열, 아구창, 백일해, 그리고 그 밖에도 위안이 될 만한 여러 가지를 떠올리며 음산하게 미소 짓고는 삽자루를 한층 단단히 움켜쥐었답니다."]], [["‘In this happy frame of mind, Gabriel strode along, returning a short, sullen growl to the good-humoured greetings of such of his neighbours as now and then passed him, until he turned into the dark lane which led to the churchyard.", "이렇게 흐뭇한 심정으로 개브리얼은 성큼성큼 걸으며, 이따금 곁을 지나가는 이웃들이 상냥하게 건네는 인사에 짤막하고 시무룩한 으르렁거림으로 응수하다가, 마침내 교회 묘지로 이어지는 어두운 골목으로 접어들었습니다."], ["Now, Gabriel had been looking forward to reaching the dark lane, because it was, generally speaking, a nice, gloomy, mournful place, into which the townspeople did not much care to go, except in broad daylight, and when the sun was shining; consequently, he was not a little indignant to hear a young urchin roaring out some jolly song about a merry Christmas, in this very sanctuary which had been called Coffin Lane ever since the days of the old abbey, and the time of the shaven-headed monks.", "그런데 개브리얼은 그 어두운 골목에 다다르기를 손꼽아 기다리고 있었으니, 대체로 그곳은 사람들이 환한 대낮, 해가 쨍쨍할 때가 아니고서는 별로 들어가기를 꺼리는, 음침하고 으스스하고 처량한 좋은 장소였기 때문이지요. 그러니, 옛 수도원 시절부터, 머리를 빡빡 깎은 수도승들의 시대로부터 줄곧 관(棺) 골목이라 불려 온 바로 이 성역에서, 어린 개구쟁이 녀석 하나가 즐거운 크리스마스를 노래하는 흥겨운 가락을 목청껏 외쳐 대는 것을 듣고는 적잖이 분개했답니다."], ["As Gabriel walked on, and the voice drew nearer, he found it proceeded from a small boy, who was hurrying along, to join one of the little parties in the old street, and who, partly to keep himself company, and partly to prepare himself for the occasion, was shouting out the song at the highest pitch of his lungs.", "개브리얼이 계속 걸어가고 그 목소리가 점점 가까워지자, 그것이 어느 작은 사내아이에게서 나오는 것임을 알게 되었지요. 그 아이는 옛 거리의 어느 모임에 끼려고 부랴부랴 가던 길이었는데, 한편으로는 제 자신에게 동무가 되어 주려고, 또 한편으로는 그 자리를 위해 미리 채비하려고, 노래를 폐가 터져라 목청껏 외쳐 대고 있었던 겁니다."], ["So Gabriel waited until the boy came up, and then dodged him into a corner, and rapped him over the head with his lantern five or six times, just to teach him to modulate his voice.", "그래서 개브리얼은 아이가 다가오기를 기다렸다가, 그 녀석을 한 모퉁이로 몰아넣고는 목소리 좀 가다듬는 법을 가르쳐 주려고 등불로 그 머리통을 대여섯 번 후려쳤답니다."], ["And as the boy hurried away with his hand to his head, singing quite a different sort of tune, Gabriel Grub chuckled very heartily to himself, and entered the churchyard, locking the gate behind him.", "그러자 아이는 머리에 손을 얹고 사뭇 다른 곡조를 부르며 부랴부랴 달아났고, 개브리얼 그럽은 속으로 아주 흐뭇하게 낄낄거리며 교회 묘지로 들어가 등 뒤로 문을 잠갔지요."]], [["‘He took off his coat, set down his lantern, and getting into the unfinished grave, worked at it for an hour or so with right good-will.", "그는 외투를 벗고 등불을 내려놓은 다음, 아직 마저 파지 못한 무덤 속으로 들어가 한 시간 남짓 신명나게 일했답니다."], ["But the earth was hardened with the frost, and it was no very easy matter to break it up, and shovel it out; and although there was a moon, it was a very young one, and shed little light upon the grave, which was in the shadow of the church.", "그러나 땅은 서리에 꽁꽁 얼어붙어, 그것을 깨뜨려 퍼내기란 그리 쉬운 일이 아니었지요. 게다가 달이 떠 있긴 했으나 갓 뜬 초승달이라, 교회 그늘에 묻힌 무덤에는 빛을 거의 비추지 못했습니다."], ["At any other time, these obstacles would have made Gabriel Grub very moody and miserable, but he was so well pleased with having stopped the small boy’s singing, that he took little heed of the scanty progress he had made, and looked down into the grave, when he had finished work for the night, with grim satisfaction, murmuring as he gathered up his things--", "다른 때 같았으면 이런 장애들 탓에 개브리얼 그럽은 몹시 침울하고 비참한 기분에 빠졌겠지만, 그 어린 사내아이의 노래를 그치게 한 일이 어찌나 흡족하던지, 작업이 더디게 진척된 것 따위는 별로 개의치 않았답니다. 그날 밤 일을 마치고 나서, 그는 음산한 만족감을 품고 무덤 속을 내려다보며, 연장을 주섬주섬 챙기면서 이렇게 흥얼거렸지요."]], [["Brave lodgings for one, brave lodgings for one, A few feet of cold earth, when life is done; A stone at the head, a stone at the feet, A rich, juicy meal for the worms to eat; Rank grass overhead, and damp clay around, Brave lodgings for one, these, in holy ground!", "한 사람을 위한 멋진 거처, 한 사람을 위한 멋진 거처, 목숨이 다하면 얻는 차디찬 흙 몇 자(尺); 머리맡에 돌 하나, 발치에 돌 하나, 구더기들이 먹어 치울 기름지고 통통한 한 끼 식사; 머리 위엔 무성한 잡초, 둘레엔 축축한 진흙, 거룩한 땅에 마련된 한 사람을 위한 멋진 거처, 이거로구나!"]], [["‘“Ho!", "'호!"], ["ho!”", "호!'"], ["laughed Gabriel Grub, as he sat himself down on a flat tombstone which was a favourite resting-place of his, and drew forth his wicker bottle.", "개브리얼 그럽은 자기가 즐겨 쉬던 자리인 납작한 묘비 위에 걸터앉아 고리버들 술병을 꺼내 들며 웃었습니다. '크리스마스에 관(棺)이라니!"], ["“A coffin at Christmas!", "크리스마스 선물이로다!"], ["A Christmas box!", "호!"], ["Ho!", "호!"], ["ho! ho!”", "호!'"]], [["‘“Ho!", "'호!"], ["ho!", "호!"], ["ho!”", "호!'"], ["repeated a voice which sounded close behind him.", "바로 등 뒤에서 어떤 목소리가 그 말을 따라 했습니다."]], [["‘Gabriel paused, in some alarm, in the act of raising the wicker bottle to his lips, and looked round.", "개브리얼은 고리버들 술병을 입술로 가져가던 손길을 멈추고, 다소 놀라 주위를 둘러보았지요."], ["The bottom of the oldest grave about him was not more still and quiet than the churchyard in the pale moonlight.", "곁에 있는 가장 오래된 무덤의 밑바닥조차도, 창백한 달빛 아래 펼쳐진 그 교회 묘지보다 더 고요하고 적막하지는 않았습니다."], ["The cold hoar frost glistened on the tombstones, and sparkled like rows of gems, among the stone carvings of the old church.", "차디찬 흰 서리가 묘비들 위에서 반짝였고, 옛 교회의 돌 조각들 사이에서 보석을 줄지어 박은 듯 빛났지요."], ["The snow lay hard and crisp upon the ground; and spread over the thickly-strewn mounds of earth, so white and smooth a cover that it seemed as if corpses lay there, hidden only by their winding sheets.", "눈은 땅 위에 단단하고 바삭하게 깔려 있었고, 빽빽이 흩어진 흙더미들 위로 어찌나 희고 매끈한 덮개를 펼쳐 놓았던지, 마치 시신들이 그 아래 누워, 오직 수의(壽衣)에만 가려진 채 거기 묻혀 있는 듯했습니다."], ["Not the faintest rustle broke the profound tranquillity of the solemn scene.", "그 엄숙한 정경의 깊디깊은 적막을 깨뜨리는 것은 아주 희미한 바스락 소리 하나조차 없었지요."], ["Sound itself appeared to be frozen up, all was so cold and still.", "모든 것이 어찌나 차갑고 고요하던지, 소리 그 자체가 얼어붙어 버린 것만 같았답니다."]], [["‘“It was the echoes,”", "'메아리였구나.'"], ["said Gabriel Grub, raising the bottle to his lips again.", "개브리얼 그럽은 다시 술병을 입술로 가져가며 말했습니다."]], [["‘“It was not,”", "'아니거든.'"], ["said a deep voice.", "어떤 굵직한 목소리가 말했지요."]], [["‘Gabriel started up, and stood rooted to the spot with astonishment and terror; for his eyes rested on a form that made his blood run cold.", "개브리얼은 벌떡 일어나, 놀라움과 공포에 그 자리에 못 박힌 듯 서 버렸습니다. 그의 시선이 머문 형상이 그의 피를 얼어붙게 했기 때문이지요."]], [["‘Seated on an upright tombstone, close to him, was a strange, unearthly figure, whom Gabriel felt at once, was no being of this world.", "곧추선 묘비 하나 위에, 그의 바로 곁에, 기이하고 이 세상 것 같지 않은 형상 하나가 앉아 있었는데, 개브리얼은 그것이 이 세상의 존재가 아님을 단번에 느꼈답니다."], ["His long, fantastic legs which might have reached the ground, were cocked up, and crossed after a quaint, fantastic fashion; his sinewy arms were bare; and his hands rested on his knees.", "땅에 닿았을 법한 길고 괴상한 다리를 위로 추켜올려 묘하고 기괴한 모양새로 꼬고 있었고, 힘줄이 불거진 두 팔은 맨살을 드러냈으며, 두 손은 무릎 위에 얹혀 있었지요."], ["On his short, round body, he wore a close covering, ornamented with small slashes; a short cloak dangled at his back; the collar was cut into curious peaks, which served the goblin in lieu of ruff or neckerchief; and his shoes curled up at his toes into long points.", "짧고 둥근 몸통에는 자잘하게 칼집을 낸 장식이 달린 몸에 꼭 맞는 옷을 걸쳤고, 등 뒤로는 짧은 망토가 늘어져 있었습니다. 옷깃은 기묘한 뾰족 모양으로 잘려 있어, 도깨비에게 주름 깃이나 목도리 노릇을 대신해 주었지요."], ["On his head, he wore a broad-brimmed sugar-loaf hat, garnished with a single feather.", "신발은 발끝이 길게 위로 말려 올라가 뾰족했답니다. 머리에는 깃털 하나를 꽂아 장식한, 챙이 넓은 원뿔형 모자를 쓰고 있었습니다."], ["The hat was covered with the white frost; and the goblin looked as if he had sat on the same tombstone very comfortably, for two or three hundred years.", "그 모자는 흰 서리로 뒤덮여 있어, 도깨비는 마치 그 똑같은 묘비 위에 이삼백 년 동안 아주 편안히 앉아 있었던 것처럼 보였지요. 그는 꼼짝 않고 완벽히 가만히 앉아 있었습니다."], ["He was sitting perfectly still; his tongue was put out, as if in derision; and he was grinning at Gabriel Grub with such a grin as only a goblin could call up.", "혀를 쑥 내밀어 마치 비웃기라도 하는 듯했고, 오직 도깨비만이 지을 수 있는 그런 히죽거리는 웃음으로 개브리얼 그럽을 향해 싱글거리고 있었답니다."]], [["‘“It was not the echoes,”", "'메아리가 아니라니까.'"], ["said the goblin.", "도깨비가 말했습니다."]], [["‘Gabriel Grub was paralysed, and could make no reply.", "개브리얼 그럽은 온몸이 마비되어 아무런 대꾸도 할 수 없었지요."]], [["‘“What do you do here on Christmas Eve?”", "'크리스마스이브에 여기서 무얼 하는 게냐?'"], ["said the goblin sternly.", "도깨비가 준엄하게 말했습니다."]], [["‘“I came to dig a grave, Sir,”", "'무덤을 파러 왔습니다요, 나리.'"], ["stammered Gabriel Grub.", "개브리얼 그럽이 말을 더듬었지요."]], [["‘“What man wanders among graves and churchyards on such a night as this?”", "'이런 밤에 무덤과 교회 묘지를 헤매고 다니는 자는 어떤 인간이냐?'"], ["cried the goblin.", "도깨비가 외쳤습니다."]], [["‘“Gabriel Grub!", "'개브리얼 그럽!"], ["Gabriel Grub!”", "개브리얼 그럽!'"], ["screamed a wild chorus of voices that seemed to fill the churchyard.", "교회 묘지를 가득 메우는 듯한 사나운 목소리들이 한꺼번에 비명처럼 외쳤지요."], ["Gabriel looked fearfully round--nothing was to be seen.", "개브리얼은 겁에 질려 주위를 둘러보았으나—아무것도 보이지 않았습니다."]], [["‘“What have you got in that bottle?”", "'그 병 속에 든 게 무엇이냐?'"], ["said the goblin.", "도깨비가 말했습니다."]], [["‘“Hollands, sir,”", "'네덜란드 진(酒)입니다요, 나리.'"], ["replied the sexton, trembling more than ever; for he had bought it of the smugglers, and he thought that perhaps his questioner might be in the excise department of the goblins.", "교회지기는 어느 때보다 더 떨면서 대답했지요. 그것을 밀수꾼들에게서 산 터라, 어쩌면 자기에게 묻는 이가 도깨비들의 세무서 소속일지도 모른다는 생각이 들었기 때문입니다."]], [["‘“Who drinks Hollands alone, and in a churchyard, on such a night as this?”", "'이런 밤에 교회 묘지에서 혼자 네덜란드 진을 마시는 자는 누구냐?'"], ["said the goblin.", "도깨비가 말했습니다."]], [["‘“Gabriel Grub!", "'개브리얼 그럽!"], ["Gabriel Grub!”", "개브리얼 그럽!'"], ["exclaimed the wild voices again.", "사나운 목소리들이 다시 외쳤지요."]], [["‘The goblin leered maliciously at the terrified sexton, and then raising his voice, exclaimed--", "도깨비는 겁에 질린 교회지기를 심술궂게 곁눈질하더니, 목소리를 높여 이렇게 외쳤습니다."]], [["‘“And who, then, is our fair and lawful prize?”", "'그렇다면, 우리의 정당하고 마땅한 노획물은 누구냐?'"]], [["‘To this inquiry the invisible chorus replied, in a strain that sounded like the voices of many choristers singing to the mighty swell of the old church organ--a strain that seemed borne to the sexton’s ears upon a wild wind, and to die away as it passed onward; but the burden of the reply was still the same, “Gabriel Grub!", "이 물음에, 보이지 않는 합창이 응답했는데, 그것은 옛 교회 오르간의 우렁찬 울림에 맞추어 수많은 성가대원들이 노래하는 목소리처럼 들렸지요—사나운 바람을 타고 교회지기의 귓전에 실려 오다가, 스쳐 지나가며 잦아드는 듯한 가락이었습니다. 그러나 그 응답의 후렴은 여전히 똑같았으니, '개브리얼 그럽!"], ["Gabriel Grub!”", "개브리얼 그럽!'이었지요."]], [["‘The goblin grinned a broader grin than before, as he said, “Well, Gabriel, what do you say to this?”", "도깨비는 전보다 더 활짝 히죽거리며 말했습니다. '자, 개브리얼, 이것에 대해 뭐라고 할 테냐?'"]], [["‘The sexton gasped for breath.", "교회지기는 숨이 막혀 헐떡였지요."]], [["‘“What do you think of this, Gabriel?”", "'이것을 어찌 생각하느냐, 개브리얼?'"], ["said the goblin, kicking up his feet in the air on either side of the tombstone, and looking at the turned-up points with as much complacency as if he had been contemplating the most fashionable pair of Wellingtons in all Bond Street.", "도깨비가 묘비 양옆으로 두 발을 허공에 차올리며, 그 위로 말려 올라간 신발 끝을, 마치 본드 가(街)의 가장 유행하는 웰링턴 부츠 한 켤레라도 흐뭇이 바라보듯 흡족하게 들여다보며 말했습니다."]], [["‘“It’s--it’s--very curious, Sir,”", "'그게—그게—참 묘하군요, 나리.' 교회지기가 겁에 질려 반쯤 죽은 채로 대답했지요."], ["replied the sexton, half dead with fright; “very curious, and very pretty, but I think I’ll go back and finish my work, Sir, if you please.”", "'참 묘하고, 참 예쁩니다요. 하지만 나리만 괜찮으시다면 저는 돌아가서 제 일을 마저 끝내야 할 것 같습니다요, 나리.'"]], [["‘“Work!”", "'일이라!'"], ["said the goblin, “what work?”", "도깨비가 말했습니다. '무슨 일?'"]], [["‘“The grave, Sir; making the grave,”", "'무덤 말입니다요, 나리. 무덤을 파는 일이요.'"], ["stammered the sexton.", "교회지기가 더듬거렸지요."]], [["‘“Oh, the grave, eh?”", "'아, 무덤이라, 응?'"], ["said the goblin; “who makes graves at a time when all other men are merry, and takes a pleasure in it?”", "도깨비가 말했습니다. '다른 사람들이 다 흥겨워하는 때에 무덤을 파면서 그걸 즐기는 자는 누구냐?'"]], [["‘Again the mysterious voices replied, “Gabriel Grub!", "다시 그 알 수 없는 목소리들이 응답했지요. '개브리얼 그럽!"], ["Gabriel Grub!”", "개브리얼 그럽!'"]], [["‘“I am afraid my friends want you, Gabriel,”", "'아무래도 내 친구들이 너를 원하는 모양이다, 개브리얼.'"], ["said the goblin, thrusting his tongue farther into his cheek than ever--and a most astonishing tongue it was--“I’m afraid my friends want you, Gabriel,”", "도깨비가 어느 때보다 더 깊숙이 혀를 볼 안쪽으로 밀어 넣으며 말했습니다—참으로 기막힌 혀였지요—'아무래도 내 친구들이 너를 원하는 모양이야, 개브리얼.'"], ["said the goblin.", "도깨비가 말했습니다."]], [["‘“Under favour, Sir,” replied the horror-stricken sexton, “I don’t think they can, Sir; they don’t know me, Sir; I don’t think the gentlemen have ever seen me, Sir.”", "'황송하오나, 나리,' 공포에 사로잡힌 교회지기가 대답했지요. '그럴 리가 없습니다요, 나리. 그분들은 저를 모릅니다요, 나리. 그분들이 저를 본 적이 있을 리 없다고 생각합니다요, 나리.'"]], [["‘“Oh, yes, they have,”", "'아니, 본 적이 있고말고.'"], ["replied the goblin; “we know the man with the sulky face and grim scowl, that came down the street to-night, throwing his evil looks at the children, and grasping his burying-spade the tighter.", "도깨비가 대답했습니다. '오늘 밤 거리를 내려오며 아이들에게 사악한 눈초리를 던지고 무덤 파는 삽을 한층 단단히 움켜쥐던, 그 시무룩한 낯짝에 음산한 눈초리를 한 사내를 우리는 알지."], ["We know the man who struck the boy in the envious malice of his heart, because the boy could be merry, and he could not.", "아이는 흥겨울 수 있는데 자기는 그러지 못하는 까닭에, 시샘하는 마음의 악의로 그 아이를 후려친 사내를 우리는 알아."], ["We know him, we know him.”", "우리는 그를 알아, 우리는 그를 안다고.'"]], [["‘Here, the goblin gave a loud, shrill laugh, which the echoes returned twentyfold; and throwing his legs up in the air, stood upon his head, or rather upon the very point of his sugar-loaf hat, on the narrow edge of the tombstone, whence he threw a Somerset with extraordinary agility, right to the sexton’s feet, at which he planted himself in the attitude in which tailors generally sit upon the shop-board.", "여기서 도깨비는 크고 날카로운 웃음을 터뜨렸고, 메아리가 그것을 스무 배로 되돌려 주었지요. 그러고는 두 다리를 허공으로 차올려 머리로, 아니 정확히는 그 원뿔형 모자의 바로 그 뾰족한 끝으로, 묘비의 좁은 가장자리 위에 물구나무를 섰답니다. 거기서 놀라운 날렵함으로 공중제비를 넘어, 곧장 교회지기의 발치에 내려서더니, 양복장이들이 흔히 작업대 위에 앉는 자세로 척 자리를 잡았지요."]], [["‘“I--I--am afraid I must leave you, Sir,”", "'저—저는 아무래도 나리 곁을 떠나야 할 것 같습니다요, 나리.'"], ["said the sexton, making an effort to move.", "교회지기가 움직여 보려 애쓰며 말했습니다."]], [["‘“Leave us!”", "'우리를 떠나겠다고!'"], ["said the goblin, “Gabriel Grub going to leave us.", "도깨비가 말했지요. '개브리얼 그럽이 우리를 떠나겠다는군."], ["Ho!", "호!"], ["ho!", "호!"], ["ho!”", "호!'"]], [["‘As the goblin laughed, the sexton observed, for one instant, a brilliant illumination within the windows of the church, as if the whole building were lighted up; it disappeared, the organ pealed forth a lively air, and whole troops of goblins, the very counterpart of the first one, poured into the churchyard, and began playing at leap-frog with the tombstones, never stopping for an instant to take breath, but “overing”", "도깨비가 웃는 순간, 교회지기는 한 찰나, 교회 창문 안쪽에 마치 건물 전체가 환히 밝혀진 듯 눈부신 불빛이 비치는 것을 보았답니다. 그 빛은 사라졌고, 오르간이 경쾌한 가락을 울려 퍼뜨렸으며, 첫 번째 도깨비와 똑 닮은 도깨비 떼거리가 교회 묘지로 쏟아져 들어와 묘비들을 놓고 등 짚고 뛰어넘기 놀이를 시작했지요."], ["the highest among them, one after the other, with the most marvellous dexterity.", "한순간도 숨 돌리려 멈추는 법 없이, 그 가운데 가장 높은 묘비들을 차례차례, 더없이 놀라운 솜씨로 훌쩍훌쩍 뛰어넘었습니다."], ["The first goblin was a most astonishing leaper, and none of the others could come near him; even in the extremity of his terror the sexton could not help observing, that while his friends were content to leap over the common-sized gravestones, the first one took the family vaults, iron railings and all, with as much ease as if they had been so many street-posts.", "첫 번째 도깨비는 더없이 놀라운 도약 솜씨를 지녀, 다른 누구도 그를 따를 수 없었지요. 극도의 공포에 사로잡힌 와중에도 교회지기는, 다른 친구들은 보통 크기의 묘비들을 뛰어넘는 것으로 만족하는 데 반해, 첫 번째 도깨비는 가족 납골당을, 쇠 울타리까지 통째로, 마치 그것들이 한낱 길거리 기둥이나 되는 양 거뜬히 뛰어넘는 것을 눈여겨보지 않을 수 없었답니다."]], [["‘At last the game reached to a most exciting pitch; the organ played quicker and quicker, and the goblins leaped faster and faster, coiling themselves up, rolling head over heels upon the ground, and bounding over the tombstones like footballs.", "마침내 그 놀이가 더없이 흥겨운 절정에 다다랐지요. 오르간은 점점 더 빠르게 연주되었고, 도깨비들은 점점 더 빠르게 뛰어, 몸을 둥글게 말고, 땅바닥에서 곤두박질치며 데굴데굴 구르고, 묘비들을 축구공처럼 펄쩍펄쩍 뛰어넘었습니다."], ["The sexton’s brain whirled round with the rapidity of the motion he beheld, and his legs reeled beneath him, as the spirits flew before his eyes; when the goblin king, suddenly darting towards him, laid his hand upon his collar, and sank with him through the earth.", "교회지기의 머릿속은 그가 바라보는 그 빠른 움직임에 어지러이 빙빙 돌았고, 그 정령들이 눈앞에서 날아다니는 통에 다리가 후들거렸지요. 그때 도깨비 왕이 별안간 그를 향해 쏜살같이 달려들더니, 그의 옷깃에 손을 얹고는 함께 땅속으로 가라앉았답니다."]], [["‘When Gabriel Grub had had time to fetch his breath, which the rapidity of his descent had for the moment taken away, he found himself in what appeared to be a large cavern, surrounded on all sides by crowds of goblins, ugly and grim; in the centre of the room, on an elevated seat, was stationed his friend of the churchyard; and close behind him stood Gabriel Grub himself, without power of motion.", "개브리얼 그럽이, 너무 빠른 추락에 한순간 잃었던 숨을 겨우 돌릴 만한 틈을 얻고 보니, 자기가 커다란 동굴인 듯한 곳에 와 있고, 사방이 추하고 음산한 도깨비 떼거리로 둘러싸여 있는 것을 알게 되었지요. 그 방 한가운데, 높직한 자리 위에는 교회 묘지에서 만났던 그 친구가 떡하니 앉아 있었고, 그 바로 뒤에는 개브리얼 그럽 자신이 꼼짝달싹 못 한 채 서 있었습니다."]], [["‘“Cold to-night,”", "'오늘 밤은 춥군.'"], ["said the king of the goblins, “very cold.", "도깨비 왕이 말했습니다. '아주 추워."], ["A glass of something warm here!”", "여기 따뜻한 걸로 한 잔!'"]], [["‘At this command, half a dozen officious goblins, with a perpetual smile upon their faces, whom Gabriel Grub imagined to be courtiers, on that account, hastily disappeared, and presently returned with a goblet of liquid fire, which they presented to the king.", "이 명령이 떨어지자, 얼굴에 영원토록 미소를 띤 도깨비 대여섯이—개브리얼 그럽은 그래서 그들이 신하들이려니 짐작했지요—부랴부랴 사라지더니, 이내 액체로 된 불이 담긴 잔을 들고 돌아와 왕에게 바쳤답니다."]], [["‘“Ah!”", "'아!'"], ["cried the goblin, whose cheeks and throat were transparent, as he tossed down the flame, “this warms one, indeed!", "뺨과 목구멍이 훤히 비치는 도깨비가 그 불꽃을 쭉 들이켜고는 외쳤지요. '이거야말로 정말 몸을 덥혀 주는구나!"], ["Bring a bumper of the same, for Mr. Grub.”", "같은 걸로 그럽 씨에게도 한 잔 가득 갖다주어라.'"]], [["‘It was in vain for the unfortunate sexton to protest that he was not in the habit of taking anything warm at night; one of the goblins held him while another poured the blazing liquid down his throat; the whole assembly screeched with laughter, as he coughed and choked, and wiped away the tears which gushed plentifully from his eyes, after swallowing the burning draught.", "불운한 교회지기가 밤에는 따뜻한 걸 마시는 버릇이 없노라고 항변해 봐야 헛일이었지요. 도깨비 하나가 그를 붙들고 다른 하나가 그 활활 타는 액체를 목구멍에 부어 넣었고, 그가 콜록거리며 캑캑대고, 그 타는 듯한 음료를 삼킨 뒤 두 눈에서 펑펑 솟구쳐 나오는 눈물을 닦아 내자, 그 자리에 모인 무리 전체가 깔깔거리며 웃어 댔습니다."]], [["‘“And now,”", "'자 이제,'"], ["said the king, fantastically poking the taper corner of his sugar-loaf hat into the sexton’s eye, and thereby occasioning him the most exquisite pain; “and now, show the man of misery and gloom, a few of the pictures from our own great storehouse!”", "왕이 원뿔형 모자의 뾰족한 끝을 짓궂게 교회지기의 눈에 쿡 찔러 넣어 더없이 지독한 통증을 안기며 말했지요. '자 이제, 이 비참하고 음울한 사내에게 우리 자신의 저 위대한 보고(寶庫)에서 그림 몇 점을 보여 주어라!'"]], [["‘As the goblin said this, a thick cloud which obscured the remoter end of the cavern rolled gradually away, and disclosed, apparently at a great distance, a small and scantily furnished, but neat and clean apartment.", "도깨비가 이 말을 하자, 동굴의 저 안쪽 끝을 가리고 있던 짙은 구름이 차츰 걷히더니, 보아하니 아주 먼 곳에, 작고 가구도 변변찮지만 정갈하고 깨끗한 방 하나가 드러났습니다."], ["A crowd of little children were gathered round a bright fire, clinging to their mother’s gown, and gambolling around her chair.", "한 무리의 어린아이들이 환한 난롯불 둘레에 모여, 어머니의 치맛자락에 매달리고, 그 의자 둘레에서 깡충깡충 뛰놀고 있었지요."], ["The mother occasionally rose, and drew aside the window-curtain, as if to look for some expected object; a frugal meal was ready spread upon the table; and an elbow chair was placed near the fire.", "어머니는 이따금 일어나 창문 커튼을 한쪽으로 젖히며, 마치 기다리던 무언가를 살피는 듯했습니다. 검소한 한 끼 식사가 식탁 위에 차려져 있었고, 난롯가에는 팔걸이의자 하나가 놓여 있었지요."], ["A knock was heard at the door; the mother opened it, and the children crowded round her, and clapped their hands for joy, as their father entered.", "문 두드리는 소리가 들리자, 어머니가 문을 열었고, 아버지가 들어서자 아이들이 그 둘레에 우르르 몰려들며 기뻐서 손뼉을 쳤답니다."], ["He was wet and weary, and shook the snow from his garments, as the children crowded round him, and seizing his cloak, hat, stick, and gloves, with busy zeal, ran with them from the room.", "그는 젖고 지쳐 있었고, 옷에서 눈을 털어 냈지요. 아이들이 그 둘레에 몰려들어, 그의 망토, 모자, 지팡이, 장갑을 분주한 열성으로 받아 들고는 그것들을 들고 방에서 우르르 달려 나갔습니다."], ["Then, as he sat down to his meal before the fire, the children climbed about his knee, and the mother sat by his side, and all seemed happiness and comfort.", "그러고는 그가 난롯가에 앉아 식사를 들 때, 아이들이 그의 무릎 위로 기어오르고, 어머니가 그 곁에 앉으니, 모든 것이 행복하고 안락해 보였지요."]], [["‘But a change came upon the view, almost imperceptibly.", "그러나 그 광경에 변화가 일었는데, 거의 알아채지 못할 만큼 슬그머니였습니다. 장면은 작은 침실로 바뀌었고, 거기서 가장 곱고 가장 어린 아이가 죽어 가고 있었지요."], ["The scene was altered to a small bedroom, where the fairest and youngest child lay dying; the roses had fled from his cheek, and the light from his eye; and even as the sexton looked upon him with an interest he had never felt or known before, he died.", "그 뺨에서는 장밋빛이 가셨고, 두 눈에서는 빛이 사라졌습니다. 교회지기가 전에는 한 번도 느껴 보지도 알지도 못했던 관심을 품고 그 아이를 바라보는 바로 그 순간에, 아이는 숨을 거두었지요."], ["His young brothers and sisters crowded round his little bed, and seized his tiny hand, so cold and heavy; but they shrank back from its touch, and looked with awe on his infant face; for calm and tranquil as it was, and sleeping in rest and peace as the beautiful child seemed to be, they saw that he was dead, and they knew that he was an angel looking down upon, and blessing them, from a bright and happy Heaven.", "어린 남동생들과 여동생들이 그 작은 침대 둘레에 몰려들어, 그토록 차갑고 무거운 그 조그만 손을 붙잡았으나, 그 감촉에 움찔 뒤로 물러나며 그 아기 같은 얼굴을 경외에 차서 바라보았답니다. 그 얼굴이 그토록 차분하고 평온하며, 그 아름다운 아이가 안식과 평화 속에 잠들어 있는 듯 보였지만, 아이들은 그가 죽었음을 보았고, 또 그가 환하고 행복한 천국에서 자기들을 내려다보며 축복해 주는 천사가 되었음을 알았기 때문이지요."]], [["‘Again the light cloud passed across the picture, and again the subject changed.", "다시 엷은 구름이 그림 위를 스쳐 지나가더니, 다시 장면이 바뀌었습니다. 이제 아버지와 어머니는 늙고 무력해졌고, 그들 둘레에 있는 이들의 수는 절반도 더 줄어들었지요."], ["The father and mother were old and helpless now, and the number of those about them was diminished more than half; but content and cheerfulness sat on every face, and beamed in every eye, as they crowded round the fireside, and told and listened to old stories of earlier and bygone days.", "그러나 그들이 난롯가에 모여 옛 지난날의 이야기를 들려주고 또 귀 기울이는 동안, 만족과 흐뭇함이 모든 얼굴에 깃들고 모든 눈에서 빛났답니다. 천천히 그리고 평화롭게, 아버지는 무덤 속으로 가라앉았고, 머지않아 그의 온갖 근심과 고생을 함께 나누던 이가 안식의 자리로 그를 뒤따랐지요."], ["Slowly and peacefully, the father sank into the grave, and, soon after, the sharer of all his cares and troubles followed him to a place of rest.", "아직 살아남은 몇 안 되는 이들은 그 무덤 곁에 무릎 꿇고, 그것을 덮은 푸른 잔디를 눈물로 적셨습니다. 그러고는 일어나, 슬프고 애달프게 발길을 돌렸으나, 쓰라린 울부짖음이나 절망에 찬 통곡과는 거리가 멀었지요."], ["The few who yet survived them, kneeled by their tomb, and watered the green turf which covered it with their tears; then rose, and turned away, sadly and mournfully, but not with bitter cries, or despairing lamentations, for they knew that they should one day meet again; and once more they mixed with the busy world, and their content and cheerfulness were restored.", "언젠가 다시 만나리라는 것을 알았기 때문입니다. 그리하여 그들은 다시 한번 분주한 세상 속에 섞여 들었고, 그들의 만족과 흐뭇함은 되살아났지요."], ["The cloud settled upon the picture, and concealed it from the sexton’s view.", "구름이 그림 위에 내려앉아, 그것을 교회지기의 시야에서 가려 버렸답니다."]], [["‘“What do you think of that?”", "'저것을 어찌 생각하느냐?'"], ["said the goblin, turning his large face towards Gabriel Grub.", "도깨비가 그 커다란 얼굴을 개브리얼 그럽 쪽으로 돌리며 말했습니다."]], [["‘Gabriel murmured out something about its being very pretty, and looked somewhat ashamed, as the goblin bent his fiery eyes upon him.", "개브리얼은 참 예쁘다는 둥 무어라 우물우물 중얼거렸고, 도깨비가 그 불타는 눈을 자기에게 쏘아붙이자 다소 부끄러운 기색이었지요."]], [["‘“You a miserable man!”", "'네가 비참한 사람이라고!' 도깨비가 지독한 경멸의 어조로 말했습니다."], ["said the goblin, in a tone of excessive contempt.", "'네가!' 더 무어라 덧붙이려는 듯했으나, 분개에 목이 메어 말이 막히자, 그 무척이나 나긋나긋한 다리 하나를 들어 올려, 겨냥을 확실히 하려고 머리 위로 잠시 휘두르더니, 개브리얼 그럽에게 보기 좋게 한 방 걷어찼지요."], ["“You!”", "그러자 곧바로 시중들던 도깨비들이 죄다 그 가엾은 교회지기 둘레에 몰려들어, 인정사정없이 그를 걷어찼답니다."], ["He appeared disposed to add more, but indignation choked his utterance, so he lifted up one of his very pliable legs, and, flourishing it above his head a little, to insure his aim, administered a good sound kick to Gabriel Grub; immediately after which, all the goblins in waiting crowded round the wretched sexton, and kicked him without mercy, according to the established and invariable custom of courtiers upon earth, who kick whom royalty kicks, and hug whom royalty hugs.", "왕이 걷어차는 자를 함께 걷어차고, 왕이 껴안는 자를 함께 껴안는, 지상 신하들의 확립되고 변함없는 관습을 따라서 말이지요."]], [["‘“Show him some more!”", "'더 보여 주어라!'"], ["said the king of the goblins.", "도깨비 왕이 말했습니다."]], [["‘At these words, the cloud was dispelled, and a rich and beautiful landscape was disclosed to view--there is just such another, to this day, within half a mile of the old abbey town.", "이 말에 구름이 흩어지더니, 풍요롭고 아름다운 풍경이 시야에 드러났지요—바로 그런 풍경이 오늘날까지도 그 옛 수도원 도시에서 반 마일 이내에 펼쳐져 있답니다."], ["The sun shone from out the clear blue sky, the water sparkled beneath his rays, and the trees looked greener, and the flowers more gay, beneath its cheering influence.", "해가 맑고 푸른 하늘에서 빛났고, 그 빛살 아래 물이 반짝였으며, 그 정겨운 기운 아래 나무들은 한층 푸르러 보이고 꽃들은 한층 화사해 보였지요."], ["The water rippled on with a pleasant sound, the trees rustled in the light wind that murmured among their leaves, the birds sang upon the boughs, and the lark carolled on high her welcome to the morning.", "물은 기분 좋은 소리를 내며 졸졸 흘렀고, 나무들은 그 잎새 사이로 속삭이는 산들바람에 살랑거렸으며, 새들은 나뭇가지 위에서 노래했고, 종달새는 높은 하늘에서 아침을 반기는 노래를 지저귀었습니다. 그래요, 아침이었지요."], ["Yes, it was morning; the bright, balmy morning of summer; the minutest leaf, the smallest blade of grass, was instinct with life.", "여름의 환하고 향긋한 아침이었습니다. 가장 자그마한 잎새 하나, 가장 작은 풀잎 하나에도 생명이 가득했지요."], ["The ant crept forth to her daily toil, the butterfly fluttered and basked in the warm rays of the sun; myriads of insects spread their transparent wings, and revelled in their brief but happy existence.", "개미는 그날의 노역을 위해 기어 나왔고, 나비는 따스한 햇살 속에 팔랑거리며 몸을 데웠으며, 무수한 곤충들이 투명한 날개를 펼치고 짧지만 행복한 삶을 만끽했답니다."], ["Man walked forth, elated with the scene; and all was brightness and splendour.", "사람은 그 정경에 마음이 들떠 걸어 나왔고, 모든 것이 환하고 찬란했지요."]], [["’”You a miserable man!”", "'네가 비참한 사람이라고!'"], ["said the king of the goblins, in a more contemptuous tone than before.", "도깨비 왕이 전보다 더 경멸에 찬 어조로 말했습니다."], ["And again the king of the goblins gave his leg a flourish; again it descended on the shoulders of the sexton; and again the attendant goblins imitated the example of their chief.", "그리고 다시 도깨비 왕이 다리를 휘둘렀고, 다시 그것이 교회지기의 어깨 위로 내리쳤으며, 다시 시중드는 도깨비들이 우두머리의 본을 따라 했지요."]], [["‘Many a time the cloud went and came, and many a lesson it taught to Gabriel Grub, who, although his shoulders smarted with pain from the frequent applications of the goblins’ feet thereunto, looked on with an interest that nothing could diminish. He saw that men who worked hard, and earned their scanty bread with lives of labour, were cheerful and happy; and that to the most ignorant, the sweet face of Nature was a never-failing source of cheerfulness and joy. He saw those who had been delicately nurtured, and tenderly brought up, cheerful under privations, and superior to suffering, that would have crushed many of a rougher grain, because they bore within their own bosoms the materials of happiness, contentment, and peace. He saw that women, the tenderest and most fragile of all God’s creatures, were the oftenest superior to sorrow, adversity, and distress; and he saw that it was because they bore, in their own hearts, an inexhaustible well-spring of affection and devotion. Above all, he saw that men like himself, who snarled at the mirth and cheerfulness of others, were the foulest weeds on the fair surface of the earth; and setting all the good of the world against the evil, he came to the conclusion that it was a very decent and respectable sort of world after all. No sooner had he formed it, than the cloud which had closed over the last picture, seemed to settle on his senses, and lull him to repose. One by one, the goblins faded from his sight; and, as the last one disappeared, he sank to sleep.", "구름은 몇 번이고 갔다가 다시 왔고, 그때마다 개브리얼 그럽에게 숱한 가르침을 일깨워 주었답니다. 그는 비록 도깨비들이 거기다 거듭 발길질을 해 대는 통에 어깨가 아파 쑤셨지만, 무엇으로도 줄일 수 없는 관심을 품고 바라보았지요. 그는 보았습니다. 고되게 일하며 노역의 나날로 변변찮은 빵을 벌어먹는 사람들이 흐뭇하고 행복하다는 것을. 또 가장 무지한 이에게조차 자연의 정겨운 얼굴이 끊임없이 솟아나는 흐뭇함과 기쁨의 원천이라는 것을. 그는 보았지요. 곱게 길러지고 다정히 자란 이들이, 더 거친 천성의 많은 이들을 짓눌렀을 궁핍 속에서도 흐뭇하고, 고통을 넘어서 있는 것을. 그들이 제 가슴속에 행복과 만족과 평화의 재료를 품고 있었기 때문이지요. 그는 보았습니다. 하느님의 모든 피조물 가운데 가장 다정하고 가장 연약한 여인들이, 슬픔과 역경과 고난을 가장 자주 넘어서 있다는 것을. 또 그것은 그들이 제 가슴속에 마르지 않는 애정과 헌신의 샘을 품고 있었기 때문임을 보았지요. 무엇보다도 그는 보았답니다. 자기처럼 남들의 흥겨움과 즐거움에 으르렁대는 자들이야말로 이 아름다운 대지의 표면에 돋은 가장 더러운 잡초라는 것을. 그리고 세상의 온갖 선(善)을 악(惡)에 견주어 저울질해 본 끝에, 그는 결국 이 세상이 제법 괜찮고 훌륭한 곳이라는 결론에 이르렀지요. 그가 그 생각을 채 굳히기가 무섭게, 마지막 그림 위로 닫혀 든 그 구름이 그의 감각 위에 내려앉아 그를 잠으로 달래는 듯했습니다. 하나둘씩 도깨비들이 그의 시야에서 흐려져 갔고, 마지막 하나가 사라지자 그는 깊은 잠 속으로 가라앉았지요."]], [["‘The day had broken when Gabriel Grub awoke, and found himself lying at full length on the flat gravestone in the churchyard, with the wicker bottle lying empty by his side, and his coat, spade, and lantern, all well whitened by the last night’s frost, scattered on the ground.", "개브리얼 그럽이 깨어났을 때는 날이 밝아 있었고, 자기가 교회 묘지의 그 납작한 묘비 위에 큰대자로 누워 있는 것을 알게 되었답니다. 곁에는 텅 빈 고리버들 술병이 나뒹굴었고, 외투와 삽과 등불은 죄다 간밤의 서리에 허옇게 덮인 채 땅바닥에 흩어져 있었지요."], ["The stone on which he had first seen the goblin seated, stood bolt upright before him, and the grave at which he had worked, the night before, was not far off.", "그가 처음 도깨비가 앉아 있는 것을 보았던 그 돌은 그의 앞에 빳빳이 곧추서 있었고, 간밤에 그가 파던 무덤도 멀지 않은 곳에 있었습니다."], ["At first, he began to doubt the reality of his adventures, but the acute pain in his shoulders when he attempted to rise, assured him that the kicking of the goblins was certainly not ideal.", "처음에 그는 자기가 겪은 모험이 정말이었는지 의심하기 시작했으나, 일어서려 하자 어깨가 쑤시는 그 날카로운 통증이, 도깨비들의 발길질이 결코 헛것이 아니었음을 일러 주었지요."], ["He was staggered again, by observing no traces of footsteps in the snow on which the goblins had played at leap-frog with the gravestones, but he speedily accounted for this circumstance when he remembered that, being spirits, they would leave no visible impression behind them.", "도깨비들이 묘비를 놓고 등 짚고 뛰어넘기 놀이를 하던 그 눈밭에 발자국 하나 보이지 않는 것을 보고 다시 한번 어리둥절했으나, 그들이 정령인지라 눈에 보이는 자취 따위는 남기지 않으리라는 데 생각이 미치자 이내 그 일을 납득했답니다."], ["So, Gabriel Grub got on his feet as well as he could, for the pain in his back; and, brushing the frost off his coat, put it on, and turned his face towards the town.", "그리하여 개브리얼 그럽은 등이 아픈 와중에도 그럭저럭 몸을 일으켜, 외투에 묻은 서리를 털어 내고는 그것을 걸치고, 도시 쪽으로 얼굴을 돌렸지요."]], [["‘But he was an altered man, and he could not bear the thought of returning to a place where his repentance would be scoffed at, and his reformation disbelieved.", "그러나 그는 달라진 사람이었고, 자기의 뉘우침이 비웃음을 사고 자기의 개심(改心)이 믿어지지 않을 그런 곳으로 돌아간다는 생각을 견딜 수 없었답니다."], ["He hesitated for a few moments; and then turned away to wander where he might, and seek his bread elsewhere.", "그는 잠시 망설였지요. 그러고는 발길을 돌려, 어디로든 정처 없이 떠돌며 다른 곳에서 밥벌이를 구하기로 했습니다."]], [["‘The lantern, the spade, and the wicker bottle were found, that day, in the churchyard.", "그날, 등불과 삽과 고리버들 술병이 교회 묘지에서 발견되었지요. 처음에는 교회지기의 운명을 두고 별별 추측이 다 있었으나, 이내 그가 도깨비들에게 끌려갔다는 것으로 결론이 났답니다."], ["There were a great many speculations about the sexton’s fate, at first, but it was speedily determined that he had been carried away by the goblins; and there were not wanting some very credible witnesses who had distinctly seen him whisked through the air on the back of a chestnut horse blind of one eye, with the hind-quarters of a lion, and the tail of a bear.", "게다가 그가 한쪽 눈이 먼 밤색 말 등에 실려 공중을 휙 가로질러 가는 것을 똑똑히 보았다는, 자못 믿을 만한 목격자들도 없지 않았지요. 그 말은 사자의 뒷다리에 곰의 꼬리를 달고 있었다더군요."], ["At length all this was devoutly believed; and the new sexton used to exhibit to the curious, for a trifling emolument, a good-sized piece of the church weathercock which had been accidentally kicked off by the aforesaid horse in his aerial flight, and picked up by himself in the churchyard, a year or two afterwards.", "마침내 이 모든 이야기가 독실하게 믿어졌고, 새 교회지기는 호기심 많은 이들에게 약간의 돈을 받고, 앞서 말한 그 말이 공중을 날다가 우연히 걷어차 떨어뜨린 교회 풍향계의 제법 큼직한 조각 하나를 보여 주곤 했답니다. 그 조각은 일이 년 뒤에 그 자신이 교회 묘지에서 주워 온 것이라고 했지요."]], [["‘Unfortunately, these stories were somewhat disturbed by the unlooked- for reappearance of Gabriel Grub himself, some ten years afterwards, a ragged, contented, rheumatic old man. He told his story to the clergyman, and also to the mayor; and in course of time it began to be received as a matter of history, in which form it has continued down to this very day. The believers in the weathercock tale, having misplaced their confidence once, were not easily prevailed upon to part with it again, so they looked as wise as they could, shrugged their shoulders, touched their foreheads, and murmured something about Gabriel Grub having drunk all the Hollands, and then fallen asleep on the flat tombstone; and they affected to explain what he supposed he had witnessed in the goblin’s cavern, by saying that he had seen the world, and grown wiser. But this opinion, which was by no means a popular one at any time, gradually died off; and be the matter how it may, as Gabriel Grub was afflicted with rheumatism to the end of his days, this story has at least one moral, if it teach no better one--and that is, that if a man turn sulky and drink by himself at Christmas time, he may make up his mind to be not a bit the better for it: let the spirits be never so good, or let them be even as many degrees beyond proof, as those which Gabriel Grub saw in the goblin’s cavern.’", "공교롭게도, 이 이야기들은 한 십 년쯤 뒤에 개브리얼 그럽 본인이 뜻밖에 다시 나타나는 바람에 다소 어수선해졌답니다. 그는 누덕누덕한 차림에, 만족스러워 보이는, 류머티즘을 앓는 노인이 되어 있었지요. 그는 자기 이야기를 목사에게, 또 시장에게도 들려주었고, 세월이 흐르면서 그것은 하나의 역사적 사실로 받아들여지기 시작했으며, 바로 오늘날까지도 그런 형태로 전해져 내려오고 있답니다. 풍향계 이야기를 믿던 이들은 한 번 자기들 믿음을 엉뚱한 데 두었던 터라, 그것을 좀처럼 다시 놓으려 하지 않았지요. 그래서 그들은 짐짓 아는 체하는 표정을 지으며 어깨를 으쓱하고, 이마를 손가락으로 톡톡 두드리고는, 개브리얼 그럽이 네덜란드 진을 죄다 마셔 버리고 그 납작한 묘비 위에서 곯아떨어진 것이라고 무어라 중얼거렸답니다. 그리고 그가 도깨비 동굴에서 보았다고 여기는 것들을, 그가 세상 구경을 하고 더 지혜로워진 것이라고 설명하는 척했지요. 그러나 어느 때고 결코 널리 받아들여진 적 없던 이 견해는 차츰 사그라들고 말았습니다. 그리고 사정이야 어떻든 간에, 개브리얼 그럽이 평생토록 류머티즘에 시달렸으니, 이 이야기는 더 나은 교훈을 가르치지는 못할지언정 적어도 하나의 교훈은 담고 있는 셈이지요. 그것인즉, 사람이 크리스마스철에 시무룩해져서 혼자 술을 마신다면, 그것으로 조금도 나아질 게 없으리라 각오하는 편이 좋다는 것입니다. 그 술이 제아무리 좋은 것이든, 또 개브리얼 그럽이 도깨비 동굴에서 본 그 술처럼 표준 도수를 제아무리 여러 배나 웃도는 것이든 말이지요.'"]], [["CHAPTER XXX.", "「자, 샘.」"], ["HOW THE PICKWICKIANS MADE AND CULTIVATED THE ACQUAINTANCE OF A COUPLE OF NICE YOUNG MEN BELONGING TO ONE OF THE LIBERAL PROFESSIONS; HOW THEY DISPORTED THEMSELVES ON THE ICE; AND HOW THEIR VISIT CAME TO A CONCLUSION\nWell, Sam,’ said Mr.", "크리스마스 아침, 그 총애받는 하인이 따뜻한 물을 들고 침실로 들어오자 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick, as that favoured servitor entered his bed-chamber, with his warm water, on the morning of Christmas Day, ‘still frosty?’", "「아직도 서리가 내렸나?」"]], [["‘Water in the wash-hand basin’s a mask o’ ice, Sir,’ responded Sam.", "「세면대 물이 얼음 가면을 뒤집어썼습니다요, 나리.」 샘이 대답했다."]], [["‘Severe weather, Sam,’ observed Mr.", "「혹독한 날씨로군, 샘.」"], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Fine time for them as is well wropped up, as the Polar bear said to himself, ven he was practising his skating,’ replied Mr.", "「몸을 단단히 싸맨 자들한테는 좋은 시절이죠, 북극곰이 스케이트를 연습하면서 혼잣말로 그랬듯이 말입니다.」"], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘I shall be down in a quarter of an hour, Sam,’ said Mr.", "「십오 분 뒤에 내려가겠네, 샘.」"], ["Pickwick, untying his nightcap.", "픽윅 씨가 잠잘 때 쓰는 모자를 풀며 말했다."]], [["‘Wery good, sir,’ replied Sam.", "「아주 좋습니다요, 나리.」 샘이 대답했다."], ["‘There’s a couple o’ sawbones downstairs.’", "「아래층에 톱쟁이가 두 명 와 있습니다.」"]], [["‘A couple of what!’ exclaimed Mr.", "「두 명의 뭐라고!」"], ["Pickwick, sitting up in bed.", "픽윅 씨가 침대에서 벌떡 일어나 앉으며 외쳤다."]], [["‘A couple o’ sawbones,’ said Sam.", "「톱쟁이 두 명요.」 샘이 말했다."]], [["‘What’s a sawbones?’ inquired Mr.", "「톱쟁이가 뭔가?」 픽윅 씨가 물었다."], ["Pickwick, not quite certain whether it was a live animal, or something to eat.", "그것이 살아 있는 동물인지, 먹는 것인지 도무지 확신이 서지 않았다."]], [["‘What!", "「아니!"], ["Don’t you know what a sawbones is, sir?’ inquired Mr.", "톱쟁이가 뭔지 모르십니까요, 나리?」 웰러 씨가 물었다."], ["Weller. ‘I thought everybody know’d as a sawbones was a surgeon.’", "「톱쟁이가 외과 의사라는 건 누구나 다 아는 줄 알았는데요.」"]], [["‘Oh, a surgeon, eh?’ said Mr.", "「오, 외과 의사라, 응?」"], ["Pickwick, with a smile.", "픽윅 씨가 미소를 띠며 말했다."]], [["‘Just that, sir,’ replied Sam.", "「바로 그겁니다요, 나리.」"], ["‘These here ones as is below, though, ain’t reg’lar thoroughbred sawbones; they’re only in trainin’.’", "샘이 대답했다. 「하지만 저 아래 있는 것들은 정식 순종 톱쟁이는 아니고, 그냥 훈련 중인 놈들입니다.」"]], [["In other words they’re medical students, I suppose?’ said Mr.", "「다시 말해 의대생들이라는 거지, 그런가?」"], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["Sam Weller nodded assent.", "샘 웰러는 그렇다는 듯 고개를 끄덕였다."]], [["‘I am glad of it,’ said Mr.", "「반가운 일이군.」"], ["Pickwick, casting his nightcap energetically on the counterpane.", "픽윅 씨가 잠잘 때 쓰는 모자를 침대보 위에 힘차게 내던지며 말했다."], ["‘They are fine fellows--very fine fellows; with judgments matured by observation and reflection; and tastes refined by reading and study.", "「훌륭한 친구들이지—아주 훌륭한 친구들이야. 관찰과 성찰로 판단력이 무르익고, 독서와 학문으로 취향이 세련된 자들이지."], ["I am very glad of it.’", "정말 반가운 일이야.」"]], [["‘They’re a-smokin’ cigars by the kitchen fire,’ said Sam.", "「부엌 난롯가에서 시가를 피우고 있습니다요.」 샘이 말했다."]], [["‘Ah!’ observed Mr.", "「아!」"], ["Pickwick, rubbing his hands, ‘overflowing with kindly feelings and animal spirits.", "픽윅 씨가 두 손을 비비며 말했다. 「다정한 정과 활기가 넘쳐흐르는 게로군."], ["Just what I like to see.’", "내가 보고 싶어 하던 바로 그 모습이야.」"]], [["And one on ‘em,’ said Sam, not noticing his master’s interruption, ‘one on ‘em’s got his legs on the table, and is a-drinking brandy neat, vile the t’other one--him in the barnacles--has got a barrel o’ oysters atween his knees, which he’s a-openin’ like steam, and as fast as he eats ‘em, he takes a aim vith the shells at young dropsy, who’s a sittin’ down fast asleep, in the chimbley corner.’", "「그리고 한 놈은요,」 샘이 주인의 말 끊김에는 아랑곳하지 않고 말했다. 「한 놈은 다리를 탁자에 올려놓고 브랜디를 물도 안 타고 들이켜고 있고, 다른 놈은—안경 낀 놈 말입니다—무릎 사이에 굴 한 통을 끼고서 김 나듯이 후딱후딱 까고 있는데, 까먹는 족족 굴 껍데기를 굴뚝 모퉁이에 앉아서 곯아떨어진 어린 수종 환자 놈한테 겨눠서 던지고 있습니다요.」"]], [["‘Eccentricities of genius, Sam,’ said Mr.", "「천재의 기벽이지, 샘.」 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick. ‘You may retire.’", "「물러가도 좋네.」"]], [["Sam did retire accordingly. Mr.", "샘은 그에 따라 물러갔다."], ["Pickwick at the expiration of the quarter of an hour, went down to breakfast.", "십오 분이 지나자 픽윅 씨는 아침을 먹으러 아래층으로 내려갔다."]], [["‘Here he is at last!’ said old Mr.", "「드디어 오셨군!」 늙은 워들 씨가 말했다."], ["Wardle. ‘Pickwick, this is Miss Allen’s brother, Mr.", "「픽윅, 이쪽은 앨런 양의 오빠인 벤저민 앨런 씨라네."], ["Benjamin Allen. Ben we call him, and so may you, if you like.", "우린 그를 벤이라 부르는데, 자네도 원한다면 그렇게 불러도 좋네."], ["This gentleman is his very particular friend, Mr.--’", "이 신사분은 그의 아주 절친한 친구인—」"]], [["‘Mr. Bob Sawyer,’ interposed Mr. Benjamin Allen; whereupon Mr. Bob Sawyer and Mr. Benjamin Allen laughed in concert.", "「밥 소여 씨입니다.」 벤저민 앨런 씨가 끼어들었고, 그러자 밥 소여 씨와 벤저민 앨런 씨가 한목소리로 웃음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick bowed to Bob Sawyer, and Bob Sawyer bowed to Mr. Pickwick. Bob and his very particular friend then applied themselves most assiduously to the eatables before them; and Mr. Pickwick had an opportunity of glancing at them both.", "픽윅 씨가 밥 소여에게 인사하자, 밥 소여도 픽윅 씨에게 인사했다. 밥과 그의 아주 절친한 친구는 그러고 나서 눈앞에 놓인 먹을 것에 무척이나 부지런히 달려들었고, 픽윅 씨는 두 사람을 찬찬히 살펴볼 기회를 얻었다."]], [["Mr. Benjamin Allen was a coarse, stout, thick-set young man, with black hair cut rather short, and a white face cut rather long.", "벤저민 앨런 씨는 거칠고 통통하며 다부진 체격의 청년으로, 검은 머리는 다소 짧게 깎았고 흰 얼굴은 다소 길었다."], ["He was embellished with spectacles, and wore a white neckerchief.", "안경으로 멋을 부렸고 흰 목도리를 둘렀다."], ["Below his single-breasted black surtout, which was buttoned up to his chin, appeared the usual number of pepper-and-salt coloured legs, terminating in a pair of imperfectly polished boots.", "턱까지 단추를 채운 외줄 단추의 검은 프록코트 아래로는 으레 그렇듯 후추소금색 다리 한 쌍이 드러났고, 그 끝에는 어설프게 닦은 부츠 한 켤레가 달려 있었다."], ["Although his coat was short in the sleeves, it disclosed no vestige of a linen wristband; and although there was quite enough of his face to admit of the encroachment of a shirt collar, it was not graced by the smallest approach to that appendage.", "코트는 소매가 짧았지만 셔츠 소맷부리의 흔적은 조금도 비치지 않았고, 얼굴은 셔츠 깃이 침범할 만한 여유가 충분했음에도 그런 부속물의 그림자조차 드리워지지 않았다."], ["He presented, altogether, rather a mildewy appearance, and emitted a fragrant odour of full-flavoured Cubas.", "그는 전체적으로 다소 곰팡내 나는 인상을 풍겼으며, 향이 풍부한 쿠바 시가의 그윽한 냄새를 발산하고 있었다."]], [["Mr. Bob Sawyer, who was habited in a coarse, blue coat, which, without being either a greatcoat or a surtout, partook of the nature and qualities of both, had about him that sort of slovenly smartness, and swaggering gait, which is peculiar to young gentlemen who smoke in the streets by day, shout and scream in the same by night, call waiters by their Christian names, and do various other acts and deeds of an equally facetious description.", "밥 소여 씨는 거친 푸른색 코트를 입고 있었는데, 그것은 외투도 프록코트도 아니면서 둘의 성질과 특성을 동시에 지닌 옷이었다."], ["He wore a pair of plaid trousers, and a large, rough, double-breasted waistcoat; out of doors, he carried a thick stick with a big top.", "그에게는 낮에 거리에서 담배를 피우고 밤에 같은 거리에서 고함치고 소리 지르며 웨이터를 세례명으로 부르고 그 밖에도 비슷한 정도의 우스꽝스러운 짓거리를 두루 일삼는 젊은 신사 특유의, 그 헝클어진 멋스러움과 으스대는 걸음걸이가 있었다. 그는 격자무늬 바지를 입고 크고 거친 더블 단추의 조끼를 걸쳤다."], ["He eschewed gloves, and looked, upon the whole, something like a dissipated Robinson Crusoe.", "밖에 나갈 때는 머리가 굵직한 두툼한 지팡이를 들고 다녔다. 장갑은 멀리했고, 전체적으로 어딘가 방탕한 로빈슨 크루소 같아 보였다."]], [["Such were the two worthies to whom Mr. Pickwick was introduced, as he took his seat at the breakfast-table on Christmas morning.", "이것이 크리스마스 아침 아침 식탁에 자리를 잡은 픽윅 씨에게 소개된 두 인물이었다."]], [["‘Splendid morning, gentlemen,’ said Mr.", "「멋진 아침이군요, 신사분들.」"], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["Mr. Bob Sawyer slightly nodded his assent to the proposition, and asked Mr. Benjamin Allen for the mustard.", "밥 소여 씨는 그 말에 가볍게 고개를 끄덕여 동의를 표하고는 벤저민 앨런 씨에게 겨자를 좀 건네 달라고 했다."]], [["‘Have you come far this morning, gentlemen?’ inquired Mr.", "「오늘 아침 멀리서들 오셨습니까, 신사분들?」"], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘Blue Lion at Muggleton,’ briefly responded Mr.", "「머글턴의 블루 라이언에서요.」"], ["Allen.", "앨런 씨가 짧게 대답했다."]], [["‘You should have joined us last night,’ said Mr.", "「어젯밤에 우리와 합류하셨어야지요.」"], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘So we should,’ replied Bob Sawyer, ‘but the brandy was too good to leave in a hurry; wasn’t it, Ben?’", "「그랬어야 했죠.」 밥 소여가 대답했다. 「하지만 브랜디가 너무 좋아서 서둘러 자리를 뜰 수가 없었거든요. 안 그런가, 벤?」"]], [["‘Certainly,’ said Mr.", "「그렇고말고.」 벤저민 앨런 씨가 말했다."], ["Benjamin Allen; ‘and the cigars were not bad, or the pork-chops either; were they, Bob?’", "「시가도 나쁘지 않았고, 돼지고기 갈비도 그랬지. 안 그런가, 밥?」"]], [["‘Decidedly not,’ said Bob.", "「두말할 것도 없지.」"], ["The particular friends resumed their attack upon the breakfast, more freely than before, as if the recollection of last night’s supper had imparted a new relish to the meal.", "밥이 말했다. 절친한 두 친구는 마치 어젯밤 저녁 식사의 추억이 이 식사에 새로운 풍미를 더해 주기라도 한 듯, 전보다 한층 거리낌 없이 아침 식사 공략을 재개했다."]], [["‘Peg away, Bob,’ said Mr.", "「쉬지 말고 해치워, 밥.」"], ["Allen, to his companion, encouragingly.", "앨런 씨가 동무를 격려하듯 말했다."]], [["‘So I do,’ replied Bob Sawyer.", "「하고 있어.」 밥 소여가 대답했다."], ["And so, to do him justice, he did.", "그리고 정말이지, 그의 명예를 위해 말하건대, 그는 그렇게 하고 있었다."]], [["‘Nothing like dissecting, to give one an appetite,’ said Mr.", "「식욕을 돋우는 데는 해부만 한 게 없지.」"], ["Bob Sawyer, looking round the table.", "밥 소여 씨가 식탁을 둘러보며 말했다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick slightly shuddered.", "픽윅 씨는 가볍게 몸서리쳤다."]], [["‘By the bye, Bob,’ said Mr.", "「그건 그렇고, 밥.」"], ["Allen, ‘have you finished that leg yet?’", "앨런 씨가 말했다. 「그 다리는 다 끝냈나?」"]], [["‘Nearly,’ replied Sawyer, helping himself to half a fowl as he spoke.", "「거의.」 소여가 그렇게 말하면서 닭 반 마리를 자기 접시에 덜었다."], ["‘It’s a very muscular one for a child’s.’", "「어린애 것치고는 근육이 아주 많은 다리야.」"]], [["Is it?’ inquired Mr.", "「그런가?」"], ["Allen carelessly.", "앨런 씨가 무심하게 물었다."]], [["‘Very,’ said Bob Sawyer, with his mouth full.", "「아주.」 밥 소여가 입 가득 음식을 문 채 말했다."]], [["‘I’ve put my name down for an arm at our place,’ said Mr.", "「난 우리 데서 팔 하나에 이름을 올려뒀어.」"], ["Allen.", "앨런 씨가 말했다."], ["‘We’re clubbing for a subject, and the list is nearly full, only we can’t get hold of any fellow that wants a head.", "「시신 하나를 다 같이 추렴하는 중인데, 명단이 거의 다 찼지. 다만 머리를 원하는 친구를 도무지 못 구하고 있어."], ["I wish you’d take it.’", "자네가 좀 맡아 줬으면 하는데.」"]], [["‘No,’ replied ‘Bob Sawyer; ‘can’t afford expensive luxuries.’", "「싫어.」 밥 소여가 대답했다. 「값비싼 사치는 감당이 안 돼.」"]], [["‘Nonsense!’ said Allen.", "「말도 안 되는 소리!」 앨런이 말했다."]], [["‘Can’t, indeed,’ rejoined Bob Sawyer, ‘I wouldn’t mind a brain, but I couldn’t stand a whole head.’", "「정말이지 못 해.」 밥 소여가 받았다. 「뇌라면야 마다하지 않겠지만, 머리통 전체는 못 견뎌.」"]], [["Hush, hush, gentlemen, pray,’ said Mr.", "「쉿, 쉿, 신사분들, 제발.」 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick, ‘I hear the ladies.’", "「숙녀분들 오시는 소리가 들립니다.」"]], [["As Mr. Pickwick spoke, the ladies, gallantly escorted by Messrs. Snodgrass, Winkle, and Tupman, returned from an early walk.", "픽윅 씨가 말하는 사이, 숙녀들이 스노드그래스, 윈클, 터프먼 씨의 정중한 호위를 받으며 이른 아침 산책에서 돌아왔다."]], [["‘Why, Ben!’ said Arabella, in a tone which expressed more surprise than pleasure at the sight of her brother.", "「어머, 벤!」 아라벨라가 오빠를 본 반가움보다는 놀라움이 더 짙게 묻어나는 어조로 말했다."]], [["‘Come to take you home to-morrow,’ replied Benjamin.", "「내일 너를 집으로 데려가려고 왔다.」 벤저민이 대답했다."]], [["Mr. Winkle turned pale.", "윈클 씨의 얼굴이 창백해졌다."]], [["‘Don’t you see Bob Sawyer, Arabella?’ inquired Mr.", "「밥 소여가 안 보이니, 아라벨라?」"], ["Benjamin Allen, somewhat reproachfully.", "벤저민 앨런 씨가 다소 나무라는 듯 물었다."], ["Arabella gracefully held out her hand, in acknowledgment of Bob Sawyer’s presence.", "아라벨라는 밥 소여가 있음을 알은체하며 우아하게 손을 내밀었다."], ["A thrill of hatred struck to Mr. Winkle’s heart, as Bob Sawyer inflicted on the proffered hand a perceptible squeeze.", "밥 소여가 내민 그 손을 눈에 띄게 꽉 쥐자, 증오의 전율이 윈클 씨의 가슴을 찔렀다."]], [["‘Ben, dear!’ said Arabella, blushing; ‘have--have--you been introduced to Mr.", "「벤 오빠!」 아라벨라가 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다."], ["Winkle?’", "「윈클 씨와—인사—인사는 나눴어?」"]], [["‘I have not been, but I shall be very happy to be, Arabella,’ replied her brother gravely. Here Mr.", "「아직 못 나눴지만, 그렇게 된다면 더없이 기쁠 게다, 아라벨라.」"], ["Allen bowed grimly to Mr. Winkle, while Mr.", "오빠가 근엄하게 대답했다."], ["Winkle and Mr. Bob Sawyer glanced mutual distrust out of the corners of their eyes.", "이때 앨런 씨가 윈클 씨에게 떨떠름하게 인사했고, 윈클 씨와 밥 소여 씨는 곁눈질로 서로에 대한 불신을 주고받았다."]], [["The arrival of the two new visitors, and the consequent check upon Mr. Winkle and the young lady with the fur round her boots, would in all probability have proved a very unpleasant interruption to the hilarity of the party, had not the cheerfulness of Mr. Pickwick, and the good humour of the host, been exerted to the very utmost for the common weal. Mr. Winkle gradually insinuated himself into the good graces of Mr. Benjamin Allen, and even joined in a friendly conversation with Mr. Bob Sawyer; who, enlivened with the brandy, and the breakfast, and the talking, gradually ripened into a state of extreme facetiousness, and related with much glee an agreeable anecdote, about the removal of a tumour on some gentleman’s head, which he illustrated by means of an oyster-knife and a half-quartern loaf, to the great edification of the assembled company. Then the whole train went to church, where Mr. Benjamin Allen fell fast asleep; while Mr. Bob Sawyer abstracted his thoughts from worldly matters, by the ingenious process of carving his name on the seat of the pew, in corpulent letters of four inches long.", "새로 온 두 손님의 도착과, 그로 인해 윈클 씨와 부츠에 모피를 두른 젊은 숙녀에게 가해진 제약은, 만약 픽윅 씨의 쾌활함과 주인장의 너그러운 기분이 공동의 안녕을 위해 한껏 발휘되지 않았더라면 십중팔구 일행의 즐거움에 무척 불쾌한 훼방이 되었을 것이다. 윈클 씨는 차츰 벤저민 앨런 씨의 호감 속으로 슬며시 파고들었고, 심지어 밥 소여 씨와 다정한 대화까지 나누기에 이르렀다. 밥 소여는 브랜디와 아침 식사와 잡담으로 기운이 돋아 점차 극도로 익살스러운 상태로 무르익었고, 어떤 신사의 머리에 난 종양을 제거한 일에 관한 유쾌한 일화를 무척 신이 나서 들려주었는데, 그것을 굴 까는 칼과 4분의 1짜리 빵 반 덩이를 동원해 설명하니 모여 있던 일행에게는 큰 교화가 되었다. 그러고 나서 일행 전체가 교회에 갔는데, 거기서 벤저민 앨런 씨는 깊이 곯아떨어졌고, 밥 소여 씨는 신도석 자리에 4인치 길이의 통통한 글자로 제 이름을 새기는 기발한 방식으로 세속의 일에서 마음을 떼어 놓았다."]], [["‘Now,’ said Wardle, after a substantial lunch, with the agreeable items of strong beer and cherry-brandy, had been done ample justice to, ‘what say you to an hour on the ice?", "「자.」 진한 맥주와 체리 브랜디라는 흐뭇한 곁들이가 딸린 푸짐한 점심을 충분히 즐긴 뒤 워들이 말했다."], ["We shall have plenty of time.’", "「얼음 위에서 한 시간 보내는 건 어떻소? 시간은 넉넉하니.」"]], [["‘Capital!’ said Mr.", "「근사하군요!」"], ["Benjamin Allen.", "벤저민 앨런 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Prime!’ ejaculated Mr.", "「최고예요!」"], ["Bob Sawyer.", "밥 소여 씨가 내뱉었다."]], [["‘You skate, of course, Winkle?’ said Wardle.", "「당연히 스케이트는 타시겠지요, 윈클?」 워들이 말했다."]], [["‘Ye-yes; oh, yes,’ replied Mr.", "「네—네, 아, 네.」 윈클 씨가 대답했다."], ["Winkle. ‘I--I--am rather out of practice.’", "「제가—제가—연습이 좀 부족하긴 합니다.」"]], [["‘Oh, do skate, Mr.", "「오, 꼭 타세요, 윈클 씨.」"], ["Winkle,’ said Arabella.", "아라벨라가 말했다."], ["‘I like to see it so much.’", "「전 그걸 보는 게 무척 좋거든요.」"]], [["‘Oh, it is so graceful,’ said another young lady.", "「오, 정말 우아하죠.」 다른 젊은 숙녀가 말했다."]], [["A third young lady said it was elegant, and a fourth expressed her opinion that it was ‘swan-like.’", "세 번째 젊은 숙녀는 그것이 고상하다고 했고, 네 번째 숙녀는 그것이 ‘백조 같다’는 의견을 표했다."]], [["‘I should be very happy, I’m sure,’ said Mr.", "「정말이지 무척 기쁠 겁니다.」 윈클 씨가 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다."], ["Winkle, reddening; ‘but I have no skates.’", "「하지만 제겐 스케이트가 없습니다.」"]], [["This objection was at once overruled.", "이 반론은 즉각 기각되었다."], ["Trundle had a couple of pair, and the fat boy announced that there were half a dozen more downstairs; whereat Mr.", "트런들에게 두 켤레가 있었고, 뚱뚱한 소년이 아래층에 여섯 켤레는 더 있다고 알렸다."], ["Winkle expressed exquisite delight, and looked exquisitely uncomfortable.", "그러자 윈클 씨는 더할 나위 없는 기쁨을 표하면서도, 더할 나위 없이 불편한 표정을 지었다."]], [["Old Wardle led the way to a pretty large sheet of ice; and the fat boy and Mr. Weller, having shovelled and swept away the snow which had fallen on it during the night, Mr.", "늙은 워들이 앞장서서 꽤 넓은 얼음판으로 일행을 이끌었다."], ["Bob Sawyer adjusted his skates with a dexterity which to Mr. Winkle was perfectly marvellous, and described circles with his left leg, and cut figures of eight, and inscribed upon the ice, without once stopping for breath, a great many other pleasant and astonishing devices, to the excessive satisfaction of Mr.", "뚱뚱한 소년과 웰러 씨가 밤사이 그 위에 내려앉은 눈을 삽으로 퍼내고 쓸어낸 뒤, 밥 소여 씨는 윈클 씨가 보기에 완전히 경이로운 솜씨로 스케이트를 맞춰 신고는, 왼쪽 다리로 원을 그리고 8자를 새기며, 한 번도 숨을 돌리려 멈추는 일 없이 얼음 위에 그 밖에도 유쾌하고 놀라운 무늬를 잔뜩 그려 보였다."], ["Pickwick, Mr. Tupman, and the ladies; which reached a pitch of positive enthusiasm, when old Wardle and Benjamin Allen, assisted by the aforesaid Bob Sawyer, performed some mystic evolutions, which they called a reel.", "픽윅 씨와 터프먼 씨와 숙녀들은 더없이 흡족해했고, 늙은 워들과 벤저민 앨런이 앞서 말한 밥 소여의 도움을 받아 그들이 ‘릴’이라 부르는 어떤 신비로운 회전 동작을 선보이자 그 만족은 그야말로 열광의 절정에 이르렀다."]], [["All this time, Mr. Winkle, with his face and hands blue with the cold, had been forcing a gimlet into the sole of his feet, and putting his skates on, with the points behind, and getting the straps into a very complicated and entangled state, with the assistance of Mr. Snodgrass, who knew rather less about skates than a Hindoo. At length, however, with the assistance of Mr. Weller, the unfortunate skates were firmly screwed and buckled on, and Mr. Winkle was raised to his feet.", "그러는 내내 윈클 씨는 얼굴과 손이 추위로 시퍼레진 채, 자기 발바닥에 송곳을 비집어 넣으며 스케이트를 신느라 애쓰고 있었는데, 날 끝을 뒤로 돌려 신질 않나, 스케이트라면 힌두인보다도 잘 모르는 스노드그래스 씨의 도움을 받아 끈을 아주 복잡하고 뒤엉킨 상태로 만들어 놓질 않나, 야단이었다. 그러다 마침내 웰러 씨의 도움으로 그 불운한 스케이트가 단단히 조여지고 채워졌고, 윈클 씨는 일으켜 세워졌다."]], [["‘Now, then, Sir,’ said Sam, in an encouraging tone; ‘off vith you, and show ‘em how to do it.’", "「자, 이제, 나리.」 샘이 격려하는 어조로 말했다. 「출발하셔서 저 사람들한테 어떻게 타는 건지 보여 주십쇼.」"]], [["‘Stop, Sam, stop!’ said Mr.", "「멈춰, 샘, 멈춰!」"], ["Winkle, trembling violently, and clutching hold of Sam’s arms with the grasp of a drowning man.", "윈클 씨가 격렬하게 떨면서, 물에 빠진 사람처럼 샘의 팔을 움켜쥐며 말했다."], ["‘How slippery it is, Sam!’", "「어찌나 미끄러운지, 샘!」"]], [["‘Not an uncommon thing upon ice, Sir,’ replied Mr.", "「얼음 위에서야 드문 일은 아닙죠, 나리.」 웰러 씨가 대답했다."], ["Weller. ‘Hold up, Sir!’", "「똑바로 서십쇼, 나리!」"]], [["This last observation of Mr. Weller’s bore reference to a demonstration Mr. Winkle made at the instant, of a frantic desire to throw his feet in the air, and dash the back of his head on the ice.", "웰러 씨의 이 마지막 말은, 바로 그 순간 윈클 씨가 두 발을 허공으로 내던지고 뒤통수를 얼음에 처박으려는 듯한 미친 욕망을 드러내 보인 데 대한 것이었다."]], [["‘These--these--are very awkward skates; ain’t they, Sam?’ inquired Mr.", "「이—이건—참 다루기 거북한 스케이트로군. 안 그런가, 샘?」"], ["Winkle, staggering.", "윈클 씨가 비틀거리며 물었다."]], [["‘I’m afeerd there’s a orkard gen’l’m’n in ‘em, Sir,’ replied Sam.", "「아무래도 그 안에 거북한 신사 양반이 들어 있는 것 같습니다요, 나리.」 샘이 대답했다."]], [["‘Now, Winkle,’ cried Mr.", "「자, 윈클.」"], ["Pickwick, quite unconscious that there was anything the matter.", "무슨 문제가 있는지 전혀 모른 채 픽윅 씨가 외쳤다. 「자, 오게."], ["‘Come; the ladies are all anxiety.’", "숙녀분들이 모두 애타게 기다리고 있네.」"]], [["‘Yes, yes,’ replied Mr.", "「예, 예.」"], ["Winkle, with a ghastly smile.", "윈클 씨가 무시무시한 미소를 지으며 대답했다."], ["‘I’m coming.’", "「갑니다.」"]], [["‘Just a-goin’ to begin,’ said Sam, endeavouring to disengage himself.", "「막 시작하려는 참입니다요.」 샘이 몸을 빼내려 애쓰며 말했다."], ["‘Now, Sir, start off!’", "「자, 나리, 출발하십쇼!」"]], [["‘Stop an instant, Sam,’ gasped Mr.", "「잠깐만, 샘.」"], ["Winkle, clinging most affectionately to Mr. Weller.", "윈클 씨가 더없이 다정하게 웰러 씨에게 매달리며 헐떡였다."], ["‘I find I’ve got a couple of coats at home that I don’t want, Sam.", "「집에 보니 내가 안 쓰는 코트가 두 벌 있더군, 샘."], ["You may have them, Sam.’", "자네가 가져도 좋네, 샘.」"]], [["‘Thank’ee, Sir,’ replied Mr.", "「감사합니다요, 나리.」"], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘Never mind touching your hat, Sam,’ said Mr.", "「모자에 손 올려 인사할 것 없네, 샘.」"], ["Winkle hastily.", "윈클 씨가 황급히 말했다."], ["‘You needn’t take your hand away to do that.", "「그러느라 손을 뗄 필요는 없어."], ["I meant to have given you five shillings this morning for a Christmas box, Sam.", "오늘 아침 크리스마스 선물로 자네한테 5실링을 주려고 했었네, 샘."], ["I’ll give it you this afternoon, Sam.’", "오후에 주지, 샘.」"]], [["‘You’re wery good, sir,’ replied Mr.", "「참 너그러우십니다요, 나리.」"], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘Just hold me at first, Sam; will you?’ said Mr. Winkle.", "「처음엔 그냥 날 좀 잡아 주게, 샘, 그래 주겠나?」"], ["‘There--that’s right.", "윈클 씨가 말했다."], ["I shall soon get in the way of it, Sam.", "「거기—그래, 됐어. 곧 요령을 익힐 거야, 샘."], ["Not too fast, Sam; not too fast.’", "너무 빠르지 않게, 샘. 너무 빠르지 않게.」"]], [["Mr. Winkle, stooping forward, with his body half doubled up, was being assisted over the ice by Mr. Weller, in a very singular and un-swan-like manner, when Mr. Pickwick most innocently shouted from the opposite bank--", "윈클 씨가 몸을 반쯤 구부리고 앞으로 숙인 채, 도무지 백조 같지 않은 아주 기묘한 모양새로 웰러 씨에게 의지해 얼음 위를 건너가고 있을 때, 픽윅 씨가 더없이 천진하게 맞은편 둔덕에서 소리쳤다—\n「샘!」"]], [["‘Sam!’\n‘Sir?’", "「예, 나리?」"]], [["‘Here.", "「이리 와."], ["I want you.’", "자네가 필요해.」"]], [["‘Let go, Sir,’ said Sam.", "「놓으십쇼, 나리.」 샘이 말했다."], ["‘Don’t you hear the governor a-callin’?", "「주인 나리가 부르시는 거 안 들립니까?"], ["Let go, sir.’", "놓으십쇼, 나리.」"]], [["With a violent effort, Mr. Weller disengaged himself from the grasp of the agonised Pickwickian, and, in so doing, administered a considerable impetus to the unhappy Mr.", "웰러 씨는 격렬하게 힘을 주어 괴로워하는 픽윅 회원의 손아귀에서 몸을 빼냈는데, 그러면서 불운한 윈클 씨에게 상당한 추진력을 가하고 말았다."], ["Winkle. With an accuracy which no degree of dexterity or practice could have insured, that unfortunate gentleman bore swiftly down into the centre of the reel, at the very moment when Mr.", "어떤 솜씨나 연습으로도 결코 보장할 수 없을 만큼의 정확함으로, 그 불운한 신사는 밥 소여 씨가 비할 데 없이 아름다운 묘기를 부리고 있던 바로 그 순간에 릴의 한가운데로 쏜살같이 돌진했다."], ["Bob Sawyer was performing a flourish of unparalleled beauty. Mr.", "윈클 씨가 그에게 사납게 부딪치자, 두 사람은 요란한 소리와 함께 묵직하게 나동그라졌다."], ["Winkle struck wildly against him, and with a loud crash they both fell heavily down. Mr.", "픽윅 씨가 그 자리로 달려갔다."], ["Pickwick ran to the spot. Bob Sawyer had risen to his feet, but Mr.", "밥 소여는 일어섰지만, 윈클 씨는 스케이트를 신은 채로 그런 짓을 할 만큼 어리석지 않았다."], ["Winkle was far too wise to do anything of the kind, in skates. He was seated on the ice, making spasmodic efforts to smile; but anguish was depicted on every lineament of his countenance.", "그는 얼음 위에 앉은 채 발작하듯 미소를 지어 보이려 애썼지만, 그의 얼굴 윤곽 하나하나에는 고통이 새겨져 있었다."]], [["‘Are you hurt?’ inquired Mr.", "「다치셨소?」"], ["Benjamin Allen, with great anxiety.", "벤저민 앨런 씨가 몹시 걱정하며 물었다."]], [["‘Not much,’ said Mr.", "「많이는 아닙니다.」"], ["Winkle, rubbing his back very hard.", "윈클 씨가 등을 아주 세게 문지르며 말했다."]], [["‘I wish you’d let me bleed you,’ said Mr.", "「제가 피를 좀 뽑아 드렸으면 합니다만.」"], ["Benjamin, with great eagerness.", "벤저민 씨가 무척 열렬하게 말했다."]], [["‘No, thank you,’ replied Mr.", "「아뇨, 괜찮습니다.」"], ["Winkle hurriedly.", "윈클 씨가 황급히 대답했다."]], [["‘I really think you had better,’ said Allen.", "「정말이지 그러시는 편이 좋을 것 같은데요.」 앨런이 말했다."]], [["‘Thank you,’ replied Mr.", "「고맙습니다만,」 윈클 씨가 대답했다."], ["Winkle; ‘I’d rather not.’", "「사양하겠습니다.」"]], [["‘What do you think, Mr.", "「픽윅 씨, 당신 생각은 어떻습니까?」"], ["Pickwick?’ inquired Bob Sawyer.", "밥 소여가 물었다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick was excited and indignant.", "픽윅 씨는 흥분했고 분개해 있었다."], ["He beckoned to Mr.", "그는 웰러 씨에게 손짓하고는 엄한 목소리로 말했다."], ["Weller, and said in a stern voice, ‘Take his skates off.’", "「저 사람 스케이트를 벗기게.」"]], [["‘No; but really I had scarcely begun,’ remonstrated Mr.", "「아니, 하지만 정말이지 전 이제 막 시작했을 뿐인데요.」"], ["Winkle.", "윈클 씨가 항의했다."]], [["‘Take his skates off,’ repeated Mr.", "「저 사람 스케이트를 벗기게.」"], ["Pickwick firmly.", "픽윅 씨가 단호하게 되풀이했다."]], [["The command was not to be resisted.", "그 명령에는 거역할 수가 없었다."], ["Mr. Winkle allowed Sam to obey it, in silence.", "윈클 씨는 묵묵히 샘이 그 명을 따르도록 내버려 두었다."]], [["‘Lift him up,’ said Mr.", "「일으켜 세우게.」"], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Sam assisted him to rise.", "샘이 그를 일으키도록 거들었다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick retired a few paces apart from the bystanders; and, beckoning his friend to approach, fixed a searching look upon him, and uttered in a low, but distinct and emphatic tone, these remarkable words--", "픽윅 씨는 구경꾼들로부터 몇 걸음 떨어진 곳으로 물러나, 친구에게 다가오라고 손짓한 뒤, 그를 꿰뚫어 보듯 응시하면서 낮지만 또렷하고 힘 있는 어조로 이 주목할 만한 말을 내뱉었다—"]], [["‘You’re a humbug, sir.’", "「자네는 사기꾼일세.」"]], [["A what?’ said Mr.", "「뭐라고요?」"], ["Winkle, starting.", "윈클 씨가 흠칫하며 말했다."]], [["‘A humbug, Sir.", "「사기꾼이라고."], ["I will speak plainer, if you wish it.", "원한다면 더 분명하게 말해 주지."], ["An impostor, sir.’", "협잡꾼이야, 자네는.」"]], [["With those words, Mr. Pickwick turned slowly on his heel, and rejoined his friends.", "그 말과 함께 픽윅 씨는 천천히 발꿈치를 돌려 친구들에게로 되돌아갔다."]], [["While Mr. Pickwick was delivering himself of the sentiment just recorded, Mr.", "픽윅 씨가 방금 적은 그 소회를 토로하고 있는 동안, 웰러 씨와 뚱뚱한 소년은 힘을 합쳐 미끄럼 길을 닦아 놓고는 그 위에서 아주 능숙하고 멋들어진 솜씨로 몸을 놀리고 있었다."], ["Weller and the fat boy, having by their joint endeavours cut out a slide, were exercising themselves thereupon, in a very masterly and brilliant manner. Sam Weller, in particular, was displaying that beautiful feat of fancy-sliding which is currently denominated ‘knocking at the cobbler’s door,’ and which is achieved by skimming over the ice on one foot, and occasionally giving a postman’s knock upon it with the other.", "특히 샘 웰러는 흔히 ‘구둣방 문 두드리기’라 불리는 그 아름다운 묘기 미끄럼을 선보이고 있었는데, 그것은 한 발로 얼음 위를 미끄러져 가다가 이따금 다른 발로 우편배달부처럼 얼음을 똑똑 두드리는 식으로 해내는 것이었다."], ["It was a good long slide, and there was something in the motion which Mr. Pickwick, who was very cold with standing still, could not help envying.", "꽤 길고 멋진 미끄럼 길이었고, 가만히 서 있느라 무척 추웠던 픽윅 씨로서는 그 움직임에 어딘가 부러움을 느끼지 않을 수 없는 데가 있었다."]], [["‘It looks a nice warm exercise that, doesn’t it?’ he inquired of Wardle, when that gentleman was thoroughly out of breath, by reason of the indefatigable manner in which he had converted his legs into a pair of compasses, and drawn complicated problems on the ice.", "「저거 보기 좋고 몸도 데워지는 운동 같지 않소?」 픽윅 씨가 워들에게 물었다. 워들이 두 다리를 컴퍼스 한 쌍으로 만들어 얼음 위에 복잡한 도형을 지칠 줄 모르고 그려 댄 통에 완전히 숨이 차 있을 때였다."]], [["‘Ah, it does, indeed,’ replied Wardle.", "「아, 정말 그렇소.」 워들이 대답했다."], ["‘Do you slide?’", "「미끄럼 탈 줄 아시오?」"]], [["‘I used to do so, on the gutters, when I was a boy,’ replied Mr.", "「어릴 적엔 도랑에서 타곤 했지요.」"], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘Try it now,’ said Wardle.", "「지금 한번 해 보시오.」 워들이 말했다."]], [["‘Oh, do, please, Mr.", "「오, 제발, 픽윅 씨, 그러세요!」"], ["Pickwick!’ cried all the ladies.", "숙녀들이 모두 외쳤다."]], [["‘I should be very happy to afford you any amusement,’ replied Mr.", "「여러분께 즐거움을 드릴 수 있다면 무척 기쁘겠소만,」"], ["Pickwick, ‘but I haven’t done such a thing these thirty years.’", "픽윅 씨가 대답했다. 「그런 걸 안 해 본 지가 삼십 년이오.」"]], [["‘Pooh!", "「쳇!"], ["pooh!", "쳇!"], ["Nonsense!’ said Wardle, dragging off his skates with the impetuosity which characterised all his proceedings.", "말도 안 되는 소리!」 워들이, 그의 모든 행동을 특징짓는 그 성급함으로 스케이트를 휙 벗어 던지며 말했다."], ["‘Here; I’ll keep you company; come along!’ And away went the good-tempered old fellow down the slide, with a rapidity which came very close upon Mr.", "「자, 내가 함께해 드리리다. 갑시다!」"], ["Weller, and beat the fat boy all to nothing.", "그러더니 마음씨 좋은 늙은이는 미끄럼 길을 따라 내달렸는데, 그 속도가 웰러 씨에 거의 육박하고 뚱뚱한 소년을 완전히 압도할 정도였다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick paused, considered, pulled off his gloves and put them in his hat; took two or three short runs, baulked himself as often, and at last took another run, and went slowly and gravely down the slide, with his feet about a yard and a quarter apart, amidst the gratified shouts of all the spectators.", "픽윅 씨는 잠시 멈춰 곰곰이 생각하더니, 장갑을 벗어 모자 속에 넣고는, 두세 번 짧게 달려 보았다가 그만큼 자주 멈칫거렸고, 마침내 다시 한번 달려나가, 두 발을 1야드 4분의 1쯤 벌린 채 천천히 그리고 진지하게 미끄럼 길을 내려갔으니, 그 사이 구경꾼 모두가 흡족해하며 환호성을 질렀다."]], [["‘Keep the pot a-bilin’, Sir!’ said Sam; and down went Wardle again, and then Mr. Pickwick, and then Sam, and then Mr.", "「불 꺼뜨리지 마십쇼, 나리!」"], ["Winkle, and then Mr.", "샘이 말했다."], ["Bob Sawyer, and then the fat boy, and then Mr. Snodgrass, following closely upon each other’s heels, and running after each other with as much eagerness as if their future prospects in life depended on their expedition.", "그러자 워들이 다시 내려갔고, 그다음엔 픽윅 씨가, 그다음엔 샘이, 그다음엔 윈클 씨가, 그다음엔 밥 소여 씨가, 그다음엔 뚱뚱한 소년이, 그다음엔 스노드그래스 씨가, 서로의 발꿈치를 바짝 뒤따르며, 마치 앞으로의 인생 전망이 그 빠르기에 달려 있기라도 한 듯 열심히 서로를 뒤쫓아 달렸다."]], [["It was the most intensely interesting thing, to observe the manner in which Mr.", "픽윅 씨가 이 의식에서 제 몫을 해내는 모습을 지켜보는 것은 더없이 흥미로운 일이었다."], ["Pickwick performed his share in the ceremony; to watch the torture of anxiety with which he viewed the person behind, gaining upon him at the imminent hazard of tripping him up; to see him gradually expend the painful force he had put on at first, and turn slowly round on the slide, with his face towards the point from which he had started; to contemplate the playful smile which mantled on his face when he had accomplished the distance, and the eagerness with which he turned round when he had done so, and ran after his predecessor, his black gaiters tripping pleasantly through the snow, and his eyes beaming cheerfulness and gladness through his spectacles.", "뒤에 오는 사람이 자칫 그를 넘어뜨릴 위험을 무릅쓰며 점점 따라붙는 것을 바라보는 그 애타는 고뇌, 처음에 잔뜩 준 그 안간힘을 차츰 다 써 버리고는 출발한 지점을 향해 미끄럼 길 위에서 천천히 몸을 돌리는 모습, 그 거리를 다 해냈을 때 얼굴에 어리는 장난스러운 미소, 그러고 나서 몸을 돌려 앞사람을 뒤쫓아 달릴 때의 그 열성, 검은 각반이 눈 위로 경쾌하게 종종거리고 안경 너머로 두 눈이 명랑함과 기쁨을 발하던 그 모습을 음미하는 것 말이다."], ["And when he was knocked down (which happened upon the average every third round), it was the most invigorating sight that can possibly be imagined, to behold him gather up his hat, gloves, and handkerchief, with a glowing countenance, and resume his station in the rank, with an ardour and enthusiasm that nothing Could abate.", "그리고 그가 넘어졌을 때면(평균 세 바퀴마다 한 번씩 일어난 일이었다), 발그레 상기된 얼굴로 모자와 장갑과 손수건을 주워 모아, 그 무엇으로도 가라앉힐 수 없는 열의와 열정으로 다시 대열에 제자리를 잡는 그를 바라보는 것은 상상할 수 있는 가장 활기를 북돋우는 광경이었다."]], [["The sport was at its height, the sliding was at the quickest, the laughter was at the loudest, when a sharp smart crack was heard.", "놀이가 한창 무르익고, 미끄럼이 가장 빨라지고, 웃음소리가 가장 커졌을 때, 날카롭고 짤막하게 쩍 갈라지는 소리가 들렸다."], ["There was a quick rush towards the bank, a wild scream from the ladies, and a shout from Mr. Tupman.", "둔덕을 향해 황급히 내달리는 무리, 숙녀들의 거친 비명, 그리고 터프먼 씨의 고함이 일었다."], ["A large mass of ice disappeared; the water bubbled up over it; Mr.", "커다란 얼음덩이가 사라지고, 물이 그 위로 부글부글 솟구쳐 올랐다."], ["Pickwick’s hat, gloves, and handkerchief were floating on the surface; and this was all of Mr. Pickwick that anybody could see.", "픽윅 씨의 모자와 장갑과 손수건이 수면 위에 떠 있었으니, 누구든 볼 수 있는 픽윅 씨의 흔적이라고는 그것이 전부였다."]], [["Dismay and anguish were depicted on every countenance; the males turned pale, and the females fainted; Mr.", "낙담과 고뇌가 모든 얼굴에 새겨졌다. 남자들은 창백해지고 여자들은 기절했다."], ["Snodgrass and Mr. Winkle grasped each other by the hand, and gazed at the spot where their leader had gone down, with frenzied eagerness; while Mr.", "스노드그래스 씨와 윈클 씨는 서로의 손을 맞잡고, 그들의 지도자가 빠져든 그 자리를 미친 듯한 간절함으로 응시했다."], ["Tupman, by way of rendering the promptest assistance, and at the same time conveying to any persons who might be within hearing, the clearest possible notion of the catastrophe, ran off across the country at his utmost speed, screaming ‘Fire!’ with all his might.", "한편 터프먼 씨는 가장 신속한 도움을 베푸는 동시에, 소리가 닿는 곳에 있을지 모를 누구에게나 이 참사를 더없이 분명하게 알리고자, 온 힘을 다해 「불이야!」를 외쳐 대며 들판을 가로질러 죽을힘을 다해 내달렸다."]], [["It was at this moment, when old Wardle and Sam Weller were approaching the hole with cautious steps, and Mr. Benjamin Allen was holding a hurried consultation with Mr. Bob Sawyer on the advisability of bleeding the company generally, as an improving little bit of professional practice--it was at this very moment, that a face, head, and shoulders, emerged from beneath the water, and disclosed the features and spectacles of Mr. Pickwick.", "늙은 워들과 샘 웰러가 조심스러운 걸음으로 그 구멍에 다가가고, 벤저민 앨런 씨가 직업적 실습을 좀 향상시킬 작은 기회로서 일행 전원의 피를 뽑는 것이 좋을지를 두고 밥 소여 씨와 황급히 의논하던 바로 그 순간이었다—바로 그 순간에 얼굴과 머리와 어깨가 물속에서 솟아올라 픽윅 씨의 이목구비와 안경을 드러냈다."]], [["‘Keep yourself up for an instant--for only one instant!’ bawled Mr.", "「잠깐만 몸을 띄우고 계세요—단 한순간만요!」"], ["Snodgrass.", "스노드그래스 씨가 고래고래 외쳤다."]], [["‘Yes, do; let me implore you--for my sake!’ roared Mr.", "「그래요, 그러세요. 제발 부탁입니다—저를 위해서라도요!」"], ["Winkle, deeply affected.", "윈클 씨가 깊이 감동한 채 울부짖었다."], ["The adjuration was rather unnecessary; the probability being, that if Mr. Pickwick had declined to keep himself up for anybody else’s sake, it would have occurred to him that he might as well do so, for his own.", "그 간청은 다소 불필요한 것이었으니, 십중팔구 픽윅 씨가 다른 누군가를 위해서 몸을 띄우기를 거부했다 한들, 적어도 제 자신을 위해서라면 그렇게 해 두는 편이 낫겠다는 생각쯤은 들었을 것이기 때문이다."]], [["‘Do you feel the bottom there, old fellow?’ said Wardle.", "「거기 바닥에 발이 닿소, 친구?」 워들이 말했다."]], [["‘Yes, certainly,’ replied Mr.", "「응, 물론이오.」"], ["Pickwick, wringing the water from his head and face, and gasping for breath.", "픽윅 씨가 머리와 얼굴에서 물을 짜내며 숨을 헐떡이며 대답했다."], ["‘I fell upon my back.", "「등으로 떨어졌소."], ["I couldn’t get on my feet at first.’", "처음엔 도무지 일어설 수가 없었다오.」"]], [["The clay upon so much of Mr.", "픽윅 씨의 코트에서 아직 드러나 있는 부분에 묻은 진흙이 이 말의 정확함을 증언해 주었다."], ["Pickwick’s coat as was yet visible, bore testimony to the accuracy of this statement; and as the fears of the spectators were still further relieved by the fat boy’s suddenly recollecting that the water was nowhere more than five feet deep, prodigies of valour were performed to get him out.", "그리고 뚱뚱한 소년이 그곳 물이 어디든 5피트보다 깊지 않다는 것을 불현듯 떠올린 덕에 구경꾼들의 두려움이 한층 더 가신 가운데, 그를 끌어내기 위한 용맹의 위업이 펼쳐졌다."], ["After a vast quantity of splashing, and cracking, and struggling, Mr. Pickwick was at length fairly extricated from his unpleasant position, and once more stood on dry land.", "첨벙거리고 우지끈거리고 버둥거리기를 한참 거듭한 끝에, 픽윅 씨는 마침내 그 불쾌한 처지에서 무사히 빠져나와 다시 한번 마른 땅 위에 섰다."]], [["‘Oh, he’ll catch his death of cold,’ said Emily.", "「오, 감기로 돌아가시겠어요.」 에밀리가 말했다."]], [["‘Dear old thing!’ said Arabella.", "「가엾어라!」 아라벨라가 말했다."], ["‘Let me wrap this shawl round you, Mr. Pickwick.’", "「이 숄을 둘러 드릴게요, 픽윅 씨.」"]], [["‘Ah, that’s the best thing you can do,’ said Wardle; ‘and when you’ve got it on, run home as fast as your legs can carry you, and jump into bed directly.’", "「아, 그게 가장 좋은 방법이오.」 워들이 말했다. 「그걸 두르거든 다리야 날 살려라 하고 집으로 내달려서 곧장 침대로 뛰어드시오.」"]], [["A dozen shawls were offered on the instant. Three or four of the thickest having been selected, Mr.", "순식간에 숄 열두 장이 내밀어졌다."], ["Pickwick was wrapped up, and started off, under the guidance of Mr. Weller; presenting the singular phenomenon of an elderly gentleman, dripping wet, and without a hat, with his arms bound down to his sides, skimming over the ground, without any clearly-defined purpose, at the rate of six good English miles an hour.", "그중 가장 두꺼운 서너 장이 골라지고, 픽윅 씨는 그것에 둘둘 싸여 웰러 씨의 안내를 받아 출발했으니, 흠뻑 젖은 데다 모자도 없고 두 팔은 옆구리에 묶인 채, 뚜렷한 목적도 없이 시속 6 잉글랜드 마일은 족히 되는 속도로 땅 위를 미끄러져 가는 노신사라는 진기한 광경을 선사했다."]], [["But Mr. Pickwick cared not for appearances in such an extreme case, and urged on by Sam Weller, he kept at the very top of his speed until he reached the door of Manor Farm, where Mr.", "그러나 픽윅 씨는 이런 극단적인 상황에서 남의 눈 따위는 개의치 않았고, 샘 웰러의 재촉을 받으며 매너 농장 문 앞에 다다를 때까지 최고 속력을 유지했다."], ["Tupman had arrived some five minutes before, and had frightened the old lady into palpitations of the heart by impressing her with the unalterable conviction that the kitchen chimney was on fire--a calamity which always presented itself in glowing colours to the old lady’s mind, when anybody about her evinced the smallest agitation.", "그곳에는 터프먼 씨가 한 5분 먼저 도착해 있었는데, 부엌 굴뚝에 불이 났다는 흔들림 없는 확신을 노부인의 머릿속에 심어 놓아 그녀를 심장이 두근거리도록 겁먹게 만든 참이었다—그것은 주위의 누군가가 조금이라도 동요를 내비치기만 하면 늘 노부인의 마음속에 선명한 빛깔로 떠오르는 재앙이었다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick paused not an instant until he was snug in bed.", "픽윅 씨는 아늑하게 침대에 누울 때까지 한순간도 멈추지 않았다."], ["Sam Weller lighted a blazing fire in the room, and took up his dinner; a bowl of punch was carried up afterwards, and a grand carouse held in honour of his safety.", "샘 웰러가 방에 활활 타는 불을 지피고 그의 저녁을 날라 왔다. 이어 펀치 한 그릇이 올라왔고, 그의 무사함을 기리는 거창한 술잔치가 벌어졌다."], ["Old Wardle would not hear of his rising, so they made the bed the chair, and Mr. Pickwick presided.", "늙은 워들은 그가 일어나려는 것을 도무지 들으려 하지 않았으므로, 그들은 침대를 의자 삼았고 픽윅 씨가 좌장을 맡았다."], ["A second and a third bowl were ordered in; and when Mr.", "두 번째, 세 번째 그릇이 주문되어 들어왔다."], ["Pickwick awoke next morning, there was not a symptom of rheumatism about him; which proves, as Mr. Bob Sawyer very justly observed, that there is nothing like hot punch in such cases; and that if ever hot punch did fail to act as a preventive, it was merely because the patient fell into the vulgar error of not taking enough of it.", "그리고 픽윅 씨가 이튿날 아침 잠에서 깼을 때 그에게는 류머티즘의 기미라곤 한 점도 없었으니, 이는 밥 소여 씨가 아주 지당하게 일렀듯, 이런 경우엔 뜨거운 펀치만 한 것이 없음을, 그리고 혹여 뜨거운 펀치가 예방제로서 효험을 보이지 못한 적이 있었다면 그것은 단지 환자가 그것을 충분히 마시지 않는다는 저속한 오류에 빠졌기 때문임을 입증하는 것이다."]], [["The jovial party broke up next morning.", "흥겨운 일행은 이튿날 아침 해산했다."], ["Breakings-up are capital things in our school-days, but in after life they are painful enough.", "헤어짐이란 학창 시절에는 더없이 멋진 일이지만, 그 후의 삶에서는 꽤나 고통스러운 법이다."], ["Death, self-interest, and fortune’s changes, are every day breaking up many a happy group, and scattering them far and wide; and the boys and girls never come back again.", "죽음과 사리사욕과 운명의 변천은 날마다 수많은 행복한 무리를 흩어 놓고 사방으로 멀리 갈라놓으며, 그 소년 소녀들은 결코 다시 돌아오지 않는다."], ["We do not mean to say that it was exactly the case in this particular instance; all we wish to inform the reader is, that the different members of the party dispersed to their several homes; that Mr. Pickwick and his friends once more took their seats on the top of the Muggleton coach; and that Arabella Allen repaired to her place of destination, wherever it might have been--we dare say Mr.", "우리는 이 특정한 경우가 정확히 그러했다고 말하려는 것은 아니다."], ["Winkle knew, but we confess we don’t--under the care and guardianship of her brother Benjamin, and his most intimate and particular friend, Mr. Bob Sawyer.", "다만 독자에게 알리고 싶은 것은, 일행의 여러 구성원이 각자의 집으로 흩어졌다는 것, 픽윅 씨와 그 친구들이 다시 한번 머글턴행 마차 지붕 위에 자리를 잡았다는 것, 그리고 아라벨라 앨런이 그녀의 오빠 벤저민과 그의 더없이 친밀하고 절친한 친구인 밥 소여 씨의 보살핌과 보호 아래, 그곳이 어디였든 간에 그녀의 목적지로 향했다는 것이다—윈클 씨는 알고 있었으리라 짐작되지만, 우리는 솔직히 모른다."]], [["Before they separated, however, that gentleman and Mr. Benjamin Allen drew Mr. Pickwick aside with an air of some mystery; and Mr. Bob Sawyer, thrusting his forefinger between two of Mr. Pickwick’s ribs, and thereby displaying his native drollery, and his knowledge of the anatomy of the human frame, at one and the same time, inquired--", "그러나 헤어지기 전에, 그 신사와 벤저민 앨런 씨는 어딘가 은밀한 기색으로 픽윅 씨를 한쪽으로 끌고 갔다. 그리고 밥 소여 씨는 픽윅 씨의 갈비뼈 두 대 사이로 집게손가락을 쑤셔 넣어, 타고난 익살과 인체 해부에 관한 지식을 동시에 내보이면서 물었다—"]], [["‘I say, old boy, where do you hang out?’ Mr.", "「이봐요, 영감님, 어디 묵고 계시오?」"], ["Pickwick replied that he was at present suspended at the George and Vulture.", "픽윅 씨는 현재 조지 앤드 벌처에 매달려 있다고 대답했다."]], [["‘I wish you’d come and see me,’ said Bob Sawyer.", "「저를 한번 보러 와 주셨으면 합니다.」 밥 소여가 말했다."]], [["‘Nothing would give me greater pleasure,’ replied Mr.", "「그보다 더 큰 기쁨은 없을 거요.」"], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘There’s my lodgings,’ said Mr. Bob Sawyer, producing a card.", "「제 하숙집은 여깁니다.」"], ["‘Lant Street, Borough; it’s near Guy’s, and handy for me, you know.", "밥 소여 씨가 명함 한 장을 꺼내며 말했다. 「버러의 랜트 가입니다."], ["Little distance after you’ve passed St.", "가이 병원 근처라서 저한테는 편하지요."], ["George’s Church--turns out of the High Street on the right hand side the way.’", "세인트 조지 교회를 지나서 조금만 가면 됩니다—큰길에서 오른쪽으로 빠지는 길입죠.」"]], [["‘I shall find it,’ said Mr.", "「찾아가지요.」"], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Come on Thursday fortnight, and bring the other chaps with you,’ said Mr.", "「두 주 뒤 목요일에 오시고, 다른 친구분들도 데려오세요.」 밥 소여 씨가 말했다."], ["Bob Sawyer; ‘I’m going to have a few medical fellows that night.’", "「그날 밤엔 의학 하는 친구들 몇을 부를 참이거든요.」"]], [["Mr. Pickwick expressed the pleasure it would afford him to meet the medical fellows; and after Mr.", "픽윅 씨는 그 의학 하는 친구들을 만나는 것이 자신에게 얼마나 큰 기쁨이 될지를 표했다."], ["Bob Sawyer had informed him that he meant to be very cosy, and that his friend Ben was to be one of the party, they shook hands and separated.", "그리고 밥 소여 씨가 아주 아늑하게 차릴 작정이며 친구 벤도 일행 중 하나가 될 것이라고 알린 뒤, 그들은 악수를 나누고 헤어졌다."]], [["We feel that in this place we lay ourself open to the inquiry whether Mr. Winkle was whispering, during this brief conversation, to Arabella Allen; and if so, what he said; and furthermore, whether Mr. Snodgrass was conversing apart with Emily Wardle; and if so, what he said. To this, we reply, that whatever they might have said to the ladies, they said nothing at all to Mr. Pickwick or Mr. Tupman for eight-and-twenty miles, and that they sighed very often, refused ale and brandy, and looked gloomy. If our observant lady readers can deduce any satisfactory inferences from these facts, we beg them by all means to do so.", "여기서 우리는, 이 짧은 대화가 오가는 동안 윈클 씨가 아라벨라 앨런에게 속삭이고 있었는지, 그렇다면 무어라 말했는지, 더 나아가 스노드그래스 씨가 에밀리 워들과 따로 떨어져 이야기를 나누고 있었는지, 그렇다면 그가 무어라 말했는지 하는 물음에 스스로를 내맡기게 됨을 느낀다. 이에 대해 우리는 이렇게 답하노라—그들이 숙녀들에게 무어라 말했든 간에, 픽윅 씨나 터프먼 씨에게는 28마일을 가는 내내 한마디도 하지 않았으며, 그들은 무척 자주 한숨을 내쉬고 에일과 브랜디를 마다하며 침울한 표정을 지었다고. 만약 우리의 관찰력 깊은 여성 독자들이 이 사실들로부터 어떤 흡족한 추론을 이끌어 낼 수 있다면, 부디 마음껏 그리하시기를 청하는 바이다."]], [["CHAPTER XXXI. WHICH IS ALL ABOUT THE LAW, AND SUNDRY GREAT AUTHORITIES LEARNED THEREIN\nScattered about, in various holes and corners of the Temple, are certain dark and dirty chambers, in and out of which, all the morning in vacation, and half the evening too in term time, there may be seen constantly hurrying with bundles of papers under their arms, and protruding from their pockets, an almost uninterrupted succession of lawyers’ clerks.", "템플 곳곳의 갖가지 구멍과 모퉁이에 어둡고 지저분한 사무실들이 흩어져 있는데, 그 안팎으로 휴정기에는 오전 내내, 개정기에는 저녁때까지도 절반은, 팔 밑에 서류 뭉치를 끼고 주머니에서도 서류가 삐죽이 비어져 나온 변호사 사무원들이 거의 끊임없이 줄지어 분주히 오가는 모습을 볼 수 있다."], ["There are several grades of lawyers’ clerks.", "변호사 사무원에도 여러 등급이 있다."], ["There is the articled clerk, who has paid a premium, and is an attorney in perspective, who runs a tailor’s bill, receives invitations to parties, knows a family in Gower Street, and another in Tavistock Square; who goes out of town every long vacation to see his father, who keeps live horses innumerable; and who is, in short, the very aristocrat of clerks.", "우선 수습 사무원이 있으니, 그는 권리금을 치렀고 장차 변호사가 될 사람으로, 양복점에 외상을 달아 놓고 파티 초대를 받으며, 가워 가에 한 집, 태비스톡 광장에 또 한 집 아는 가족을 두고 있고, 긴 휴정기마다 시골로 가서 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 살아 있는 말을 기르는 제 아버지를 만나 뵙는, 한마디로 사무원들 가운데 단연 귀족이다."], ["There is the salaried clerk--out of door, or in door, as the case may be--who devotes the major part of his thirty shillings a week to his Personal pleasure and adornments, repairs half-price to the Adelphi Theatre at least three times a week, dissipates majestically at the cider cellars afterwards, and is a dirty caricature of the fashion which expired six months ago.", "다음으로 봉급 사무원이 있으니, 경우에 따라 외근이거나 내근이거나 하는데, 주급 삼십 실링의 대부분을 제 향락과 치장에 쏟아붓고, 적어도 일주일에 세 번은 반값으로 아델파이 극장에 드나들며, 그 뒤에는 사과주 지하술집에서 위엄 있게 흥청거리고, 반년 전에 사라진 유행을 흉내 낸 지저분한 풍자화 같은 자다."], ["There is the middle-aged copying clerk, with a large family, who is always shabby, and often drunk.", "또 처자식 많은 중년의 필사 사무원이 있으니, 그는 늘 행색이 초라하고 자주 술에 취해 있다."], ["And there are the office lads in their first surtouts, who feel a befitting contempt for boys at day-schools, club as they go home at night, for saveloys and porter, and think there’s nothing like ‘life.’ There are varieties of the genus, too numerous to recapitulate, but however numerous they may be, they are all to be seen, at certain regulated business hours, hurrying to and from the places we have just mentioned.", "그리고 처음으로 외투를 걸친 사무실 심부름꾼 소년들이 있으니, 그들은 주간 학교에 다니는 아이들을 그럴싸하게 깔보고, 밤에 집으로 돌아갈 때면 돈을 추렴해 소시지와 흑맥주를 사 먹으며, '인생'만 한 것이 없다고 여긴다. 이 종류에는 일일이 다 헤아릴 수 없을 만큼 변종이 많지만, 아무리 많다 해도 그들은 모두 정해진 업무 시간이면 방금 말한 그 장소들을 오가며 분주히 움직이는 모습으로 볼 수 있다."]], [["These sequestered nooks are the public offices of the legal profession, where writs are issued, judgments signed, declarations filed, and numerous other ingenious machines put in motion for the torture and torment of His Majesty’s liege subjects, and the comfort and emolument of the practitioners of the law.", "이 외딴 구석들이야말로 법조계의 공공 사무소이니, 영장이 발부되고 판결이 서명되며 소장이 접수되고, 그 밖에도 폐하의 충성스러운 신민을 괴롭히고 고통스럽게 하는 한편 법조인에게는 편안함과 보수를 안겨 주는 갖가지 교묘한 기계 장치가 작동되는 곳이다."], ["They are, for the most part, low- roofed, mouldy rooms, where innumerable rolls of parchment, which have been perspiring in secret for the last century, send forth an agreeable odour, which is mingled by day with the scent of the dry-rot, and by night with the various exhalations which arise from damp cloaks, festering umbrellas, and the coarsest tallow candles.", "그곳들은 대개 천장이 낮고 곰팡내 나는 방으로, 지난 한 세기 동안 남몰래 땀을 흘려 온 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 양피지 두루마리가 그윽한 냄새를 풍기는데, 그 냄새는 낮에는 마른 부패 냄새와 뒤섞이고, 밤에는 축축한 외투와 곪아 가는 우산, 그리고 가장 조악한 수지 양초에서 피어오르는 갖가지 냄새와 뒤섞인다."]], [["About half-past seven o’clock in the evening, some ten days or a fortnight after Mr. Pickwick and his friends returned to London, there hurried into one of these offices, an individual in a brown coat and brass buttons, whose long hair was scrupulously twisted round the rim of his napless hat, and whose soiled drab trousers were so tightly strapped over his Blucher boots, that his knees threatened every moment to start from their concealment.", "픽윅 씨와 그의 친구들이 런던으로 돌아온 지 열흘 내지 보름쯤 지난 어느 날 저녁 일곱 시 반쯤, 이런 사무소 가운데 한 곳으로 한 사내가 분주히 들어왔으니, 갈색 외투에 놋쇠 단추를 단 그자는 긴 머리카락을 보풀 다 빠진 모자챙에 정성스레 둘둘 감아 두었고, 때 묻은 황갈색 바지를 블뤼허 부츠 위로 어찌나 팽팽하게 졸라매었던지 무릎이 금방이라도 가려진 데서 튀어나올 것만 같았다."], ["He produced from his coat pockets a long and narrow strip of parchment, on which the presiding functionary impressed an illegible black stamp.", "그는 외투 주머니에서 길고 좁은 양피지 한 장을 꺼냈고, 주재하던 직원이 거기에 알아볼 수 없는 검은 도장을 찍었다."], ["He then drew forth four scraps of paper, of similar dimensions, each containing a printed copy of the strip of parchment with blanks for a name; and having filled up the blanks, put all the five documents in his pocket, and hurried away.", "그러자 그는 같은 크기의 종잇조각 넉 장을 꺼냈는데, 저마다 그 양피지의 내용을 인쇄해 베껴 놓고 이름 적을 빈칸을 둔 것이었다. 그는 빈칸을 채운 뒤 다섯 장의 서류를 모두 주머니에 넣고 부랴부랴 사라졌다."]], [["The man in the brown coat, with the cabalistic documents in his pocket, was no other than our old acquaintance Mr. Jackson, of the house of Dodson & Fogg, Freeman’s Court, Cornhill.", "주머니에 그 비밀스러운 서류를 넣은 갈색 외투의 사내는 다름 아닌 우리의 옛 친구, 콘힐의 프리먼 광장에 있는 도드슨 앤드 포그 사무소의 잭슨 씨였다."], ["Instead of returning to the office whence he came, however, he bent his steps direct to Sun Court, and walking straight into the George and Vulture, demanded to know whether one Mr. Pickwick was within.", "그러나 그는 자기가 나온 사무소로 돌아가지 않고 곧장 선 광장 쪽으로 발걸음을 옮겼고, 곧장 조지 앤드 벌처 여관으로 걸어 들어가 픽윅이라는 분이 안에 계신지 알고 싶다고 청했다."]], [["‘Call Mr.", "\"톰, 픽윅 씨 하인을 불러와요.\""], ["Pickwick’s servant, Tom,’ said the barmaid of the George and Vulture.", "조지 앤드 벌처의 술집 아가씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Don’t trouble yourself,’ said Mr. Jackson.", "\"수고하실 것 없습니다.\""], ["‘I’ve come on business.", "잭슨 씨가 말했다."], ["If you’ll show me Mr.", "\"일이 있어 왔으니까요."], ["Pickwick’s room I’ll step up myself.’", "픽윅 씨 방을 알려 주시면 제가 직접 올라가지요.\""]], [["‘What name, Sir?’ said the waiter.", "\"성함이 어떻게 되시는지요?\" 종업원이 물었다."]], [["‘Jackson,’ replied the clerk.", "\"잭슨이오.\" 사무원이 대답했다."]], [["The waiter stepped upstairs to announce Mr. Jackson; but Mr. Jackson saved him the trouble by following close at his heels, and walking into the apartment before he could articulate a syllable.", "종업원이 잭슨 씨를 알리려고 위층으로 올라갔으나, 잭슨 씨는 그의 발꿈치를 바짝 따라가 그가 미처 한마디도 내뱉기 전에 방 안으로 걸어 들어감으로써 그 수고를 덜어 주었다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick had, that day, invited his three friends to dinner; they were all seated round the fire, drinking their wine, when Mr. Jackson presented himself, as above described.", "픽윅 씨는 그날 세 친구를 만찬에 초대해 두었는데, 잭슨 씨가 앞서 말한 대로 모습을 드러냈을 때 그들은 모두 난롯가에 둘러앉아 포도주를 마시고 있었다."]], [["‘How de do, sir?’ said Mr.", "\"안녕하십니까, 선생?\""], ["Jackson, nodding to Mr. Pickwick.", "잭슨 씨가 픽윅 씨에게 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다."]], [["That gentleman bowed, and looked somewhat surprised, for the physiognomy of Mr. Jackson dwelt not in his recollection.", "픽윅 씨는 인사를 하면서도 다소 놀란 듯한 표정을 지었으니, 잭슨 씨의 인상은 그의 기억 속에 남아 있지 않았기 때문이다."]], [["‘I have called from Dodson and Fogg’s,’ said Mr.", "\"도드슨 앤드 포그 사무소에서 왔습니다.\""], ["Jackson, in an explanatory tone.", "잭슨 씨가 설명하는 투로 말했다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick roused at the name.", "픽윅 씨는 그 이름에 정신이 번쩍 들었다."], ["‘I refer you to my attorney, Sir; Mr.", "\"내 변호사에게 가시오, 선생."], ["Perker, of Gray’s Inn,’ said he.", "그레이즈 인의 퍼커 씨 말이오.\""], ["‘Waiter, show this gentleman out.’", "그가 말했다. \"종업원, 이분을 모셔 내가시오.\""]], [["‘Beg your pardon, Mr.", "\"실례합니다만, 픽윅 씨.\""], ["Pickwick,’ said Jackson, deliberately depositing his hat on the floor, and drawing from his pocket the strip of parchment.", "잭슨이 모자를 차분히 바닥에 내려놓고 주머니에서 양피지를 꺼내며 말했다."], ["‘But personal service, by clerk or agent, in these cases, you know, Mr.", "\"이런 경우에는 사무원이나 대리인이 직접 송달을 해야 하거든요, 픽윅 씨."], ["Pickwick--nothing like caution, sir, in all legal forms--eh?’", "아시다시피, 모든 법적 절차에서는 신중함만 한 것이 없지요, 안 그렇습니까?\""]], [["Here Mr.", "여기서 잭슨 씨는 양피지에 눈길을 던지더니, 두 손을 탁자에 짚고 사람을 끌어당기는 듯 설득력 있는 미소를 띠며 주위를 둘러보고 말했다."], ["Jackson cast his eye on the parchment; and, resting his hands on the table, and looking round with a winning and persuasive smile, said, ‘Now, come; don’t let’s have no words about such a little matter as this.", "\"자, 어서요. 이렇게 사소한 일을 가지고 이러쿵저러쿵하지 맙시다."], ["Which of you gentlemen’s name’s Snodgrass?’", "여러분 가운데 스노드그래스라는 분이 어느 분이십니까?\""]], [["At this inquiry, Mr. Snodgrass gave such a very undisguised and palpable start, that no further reply was needed.", "이 물음에 스노드그래스 씨가 어찌나 노골적이고 뚜렷하게 움찔하던지, 더 이상 대답할 필요도 없었다."]], [["‘Ah!", "\"아!"], ["I thought so,’ said Mr.", "그러실 줄 알았습니다.\""], ["Jackson, more affably than before.", "잭슨 씨가 아까보다 더 상냥하게 말했다."], ["‘I’ve a little something to trouble you with, Sir.’", "\"선생께 폐를 좀 끼칠 일이 하나 있습니다.\""]], [["‘Me!’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"나한테!\""], ["Snodgrass.", "스노드그래스 씨가 외쳤다."]], [["‘It’s only a subpoena in Bardell and Pickwick on behalf of the plaintiff,’ replied Jackson, singling out one of the slips of paper, and producing a shilling from his waistcoat pocket.", "\"바델 대 픽윅 소송에서 원고 측을 위한 소환장일 뿐입니다.\" 잭슨이 종잇조각 가운데 하나를 골라내고 조끼 주머니에서 실링 한 닢을 꺼내며 대답했다."], ["‘It’ll come on, in the settens after Term: fourteenth of Febooary, we expect; we’ve marked it a special jury cause, and it’s only ten down the paper.", "\"개정기가 끝난 뒤 회기 중에 열릴 겁니다. 우리 예상으로는 이월 십사일이지요."], ["That’s yours, Mr. Snodgrass.’ As Jackson said this, he presented the parchment before the eyes of Mr.", "특별 배심 사건으로 표시해 두었고, 명단에서 열 번째밖에 안 됩니다."], ["Snodgrass, and slipped the paper and the shilling into his hand.", "이게 선생 것입니다, 스노드그래스 씨.\" 잭슨은 이렇게 말하면서 양피지를 스노드그래스 씨의 눈앞에 들이밀고는 종이와 실링을 그의 손에 슬쩍 쥐여 주었다."]], [["Mr. Tupman had witnessed this process in silent astonishment, when Jackson, turning sharply upon him, said--", "터프먼 씨가 말없이 놀라며 이 과정을 지켜보고 있는데, 잭슨이 그에게 홱 돌아서며 말했다."]], [["‘I think I ain’t mistaken when I say your name’s Tupman, am I?’", "\"선생 성함이 터프먼이라고 해도 틀린 건 아니겠지요, 그렇죠?\""]], [["Mr. Tupman looked at Mr. Pickwick; but, perceiving no encouragement in that gentleman’s widely-opened eyes to deny his name, said--", "터프먼 씨는 픽윅 씨를 바라보았으나, 그 신사의 휘둥그레진 눈에서 제 이름을 부인하라는 기색을 찾지 못하자 이렇게 말했다."]], [["‘Yes, my name is Tupman, Sir.’", "\"그렇소, 내 이름은 터프먼이오, 선생.\""]], [["‘And that other gentleman’s Mr.", "\"그리고 저 다른 신사분은 윈클 씨이신 것 같은데요?\""], ["Winkle, I think?’ said Jackson. Mr.", "잭슨이 말했다."], ["Winkle faltered out a reply in the affirmative; and both gentlemen were forthwith invested with a slip of paper, and a shilling each, by the dexterous Mr. Jackson.", "윈클 씨가 더듬더듬 그렇다고 대답하자, 솜씨 좋은 잭슨 씨는 곧바로 두 신사에게 각각 종잇조각 한 장과 실링 한 닢씩을 안겨 주었다."]], [["‘Now,’ said Jackson, ‘I’m afraid you’ll think me rather troublesome, but I want somebody else, if it ain’t inconvenient.", "\"자,\" 잭슨이 말했다."], ["I have Samuel Weller’s name here, Mr.", "\"제가 좀 성가시게 군다고 여기실까 봐 걱정입니다만, 불편하지 않으시다면 한 분 더 뵈어야겠습니다."], ["Pickwick.’", "여기 새뮤얼 웰러라는 이름이 적혀 있습니다, 픽윅 씨.\""]], [["‘Send my servant here, waiter,’ said Mr.", "\"종업원, 내 하인을 이리로 보내 주시오.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["The waiter retired, considerably astonished, and Mr. Pickwick motioned Jackson to a seat.", "종업원은 적잖이 놀란 채 물러갔고, 픽윅 씨는 잭슨에게 자리에 앉으라고 손짓했다."]], [["There was a painful pause, which was at length broken by the innocent defendant.", "괴로운 침묵이 흘렀고, 마침내 그 침묵을 깨뜨린 것은 결백한 피고였다."]], [["‘I suppose, Sir,’ said Mr.", "\"내 짐작에는, 선생,\""], ["Pickwick, his indignation rising while he spoke--‘I suppose, Sir, that it is the intention of your employers to seek to criminate me upon the testimony of my own friends?’", "픽윅 씨가 말하면서 점점 분노가 치밀어 올랐다. \"내 짐작에는, 선생, 당신네 고용주들의 의도가 내 친구들의 증언을 이용해 나를 죄인으로 몰려는 것 같소만?\""]], [["Mr. Jackson struck his forefinger several times against the left side of his nose, to intimate that he was not there to disclose the secrets of the prison house, and playfully rejoined--", "잭슨 씨는 자기가 감옥의 비밀을 누설하러 온 사람이 아님을 내비치려는 듯 집게손가락으로 코 왼쪽을 몇 번 두드리더니 짓궂게 받아쳤다."]], [["‘Not knowin’, can’t say.’", "\"모르는 일이니 말씀드릴 수 없지요.\""]], [["‘For what other reason, Sir,’ pursued Mr.", "\"그렇다면 다른 무슨 이유로,\" 픽윅 씨가 계속했다."], ["Pickwick, ‘are these subpoenas served upon them, if not for this?’", "\"이런 소환장들이 그들에게 송달되겠소, 그게 아니라면 말이오?\""]], [["‘Very good plant, Mr.", "\"수가 아주 좋습니다, 픽윅 씨.\""], ["Pickwick,’ replied Jackson, slowly shaking his head.", "잭슨이 천천히 고개를 가로저으며 대답했다."], ["‘But it won’t do.", "\"하지만 안 통합니다."], ["No harm in trying, but there’s little to be got out of me.’", "시도하는 거야 나쁠 것 없지만, 저한테서는 캐낼 게 별로 없을 겁니다.\""]], [["Here Mr. Jackson smiled once more upon the company, and, applying his left thumb to the tip of his nose, worked a visionary coffee-mill with his right hand, thereby performing a very graceful piece of pantomime (then much in vogue, but now, unhappily, almost obsolete) which was familiarly denominated ‘taking a grinder.’", "여기서 잭슨 씨는 일행을 향해 다시 한번 미소를 짓더니, 왼손 엄지를 코끝에 갖다 대고 오른손으로 허공의 커피 분쇄기를 돌리는 시늉을 하며 더없이 우아한 무언극(그때는 크게 유행했으나 지금은 안타깝게도 거의 자취를 감춘) 한 토막을 선보였으니, 그것은 흔히 '맷돌질하기'라고 불리던 것이었다."]], [["‘No, no, Mr.", "\"아닙니다, 아니에요, 픽윅 씨.\""], ["Pickwick,’ said Jackson, in conclusion; ‘Perker’s people must guess what we’ve served these subpoenas for.", "잭슨이 끝맺으며 말했다. \"퍼커네 사람들이 우리가 왜 이 소환장들을 송달했는지 알아맞혀야겠지요."], ["If they can’t, they must wait till the action comes on, and then they’ll find out.’", "못 알아맞히면 소송이 시작될 때까지 기다렸다가 그때 가서 알게 될 겁니다.\""]], [["Mr. Pickwick bestowed a look of excessive disgust on his unwelcome visitor, and would probably have hurled some tremendous anathema at the heads of Messrs. Dodson & Fogg, had not Sam’s entrance at the instant interrupted him.", "픽윅 씨는 반갑잖은 방문객에게 극도의 혐오가 담긴 눈길을 던졌고, 마침 샘이 들어와 가로막지 않았더라면 도드슨 앤드 포그 두 분의 머리 위에 어떤 무시무시한 저주를 퍼부었을지도 모른다."]], [["‘Samuel Weller?’ said Mr.", "\"새뮤얼 웰러?\""], ["Jackson, inquiringly.", "잭슨 씨가 묻듯이 말했다."]], [["‘Vun o’ the truest things as you’ve said for many a long year,’ replied Sam, in a most composed manner.", "\"여러 해 만에 처음으로 맞는 말씀을 하나 하셨네요.\" 샘이 더없이 침착한 태도로 대답했다."]], [["‘Here’s a subpoena for you, Mr.", "\"여기 소환장이 있소, 웰러 씨.\""], ["Weller,’ said Jackson.", "잭슨이 말했다."]], [["‘What’s that in English?’ inquired Sam.", "\"그게 사람 말로 하면 뭐요?\" 샘이 물었다."]], [["‘Here’s the original,’ said Jackson, declining the required explanation.", "\"여기 원본이 있소.\" 잭슨은 요구받은 설명을 거절하며 말했다."]], [["‘Which?’ said Sam.", "\"어느 거요?\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["‘This,’ replied Jackson, shaking the parchment.", "\"이거요.\" 잭슨이 양피지를 흔들며 대답했다."]], [["‘Oh, that’s the ‘rig’nal, is it?’ said Sam.", "\"오, 그게 원본입니까?\" 샘이 말했다."], ["‘Well, I’m wery glad I’ve seen the ‘rig’nal, ‘cos it’s a gratifyin’ sort o’ thing, and eases vun’s mind so much.’", "\"음, 원본을 보게 되어 아주 기쁘네요. 흐뭇한 일이고, 마음이 한결 놓이거든요.\""]], [["‘And here’s the shilling,’ said Jackson.", "\"그리고 여기 실링도 있소.\" 잭슨이 말했다."], ["‘It’s from Dodson and Fogg’s.’", "\"도드슨 앤드 포그가 주는 거요.\""]], [["‘And it’s uncommon handsome o’ Dodson and Fogg, as knows so little of me, to come down vith a present,’ said Sam.", "\"저를 그렇게나 모르시는 도드슨 앤드 포그가 선물을 다 내려 주시다니 참으로 후하시네요.\" 샘이 말했다."], ["‘I feel it as a wery high compliment, sir; it’s a wery honorable thing to them, as they knows how to reward merit werever they meets it.", "\"아주 높은 칭찬으로 여기겠습니다, 선생. 어디서든 공로를 만나면 그걸 보상할 줄 아시니, 그분들로서는 참 명예로운 일이지요."], ["Besides which, it’s affectin’ to one’s feelin’s.’", "게다가 사람 마음을 다 뭉클하게 합니다그려.\""]], [["As Mr. Weller said this, he inflicted a little friction on his right eyelid, with the sleeve of his coat, after the most approved manner of actors when they are in domestic pathetics.", "웰러 씨는 이렇게 말하면서 가정극의 비장한 장면에 임한 배우들이 으레 쓰는 가장 그럴듯한 방식으로 외투 소매로 오른쪽 눈꺼풀을 살짝 비볐다."]], [["Mr. Jackson seemed rather puzzled by Sam’s proceedings; but, as he had served the subpoenas, and had nothing more to say, he made a feint of putting on the one glove which he usually carried in his hand, for the sake of appearances; and returned to the office to report progress.", "잭슨 씨는 샘의 행동에 다소 어리둥절한 듯했으나, 소환장을 다 송달했고 더 할 말도 없는지라, 체면을 차리려고 늘 손에 들고 다니던 장갑 한 짝을 끼는 시늉을 하고는 진행 상황을 보고하러 사무소로 돌아갔다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick slept little that night; his memory had received a very disagreeable refresher on the subject of Mrs.", "픽윅 씨는 그날 밤 잠을 거의 이루지 못했으니, 바델 부인의 소송 건에 대한 그의 기억이 매우 불쾌하게 되살아났기 때문이다."], ["Bardell’s action. He breakfasted betimes next morning, and, desiring Sam to accompany him, set forth towards Gray’s Inn Square.", "그는 이튿날 아침 일찍 식사를 마치고 샘에게 따라오라 이른 뒤 그레이즈 인 광장 쪽으로 길을 나섰다."]], [["‘Sam!’ said Mr.", "\"샘!\""], ["Pickwick, looking round, when they got to the end of Cheapside.", "칩사이드 끝에 이르렀을 때 픽윅 씨가 주위를 둘러보며 말했다."]], [["‘Sir?’ said Sam, stepping up to his master.", "\"네, 나리?\" 샘이 주인에게 다가서며 말했다."]], [["‘Which way?’", "\"어느 쪽이냐?\""]], [["Up Newgate Street.’", "\"뉴게이트 가로 올라가십니다.\""]], [["Mr. Pickwick did not turn round immediately, but looked vacantly in Sam’s face for a few seconds, and heaved a deep sigh.", "픽윅 씨는 곧바로 돌아서지 않고 잠시 멍하니 샘의 얼굴을 바라보다가 깊은 한숨을 내쉬었다."]], [["‘What’s the matter, sir?’ inquired Sam.", "\"무슨 일이십니까, 나리?\" 샘이 물었다."]], [["‘This action, Sam,’ said Mr.", "\"이 소송 말이다, 샘.\""], ["Pickwick, ‘is expected to come on, on the fourteenth of next month.’", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"다음 달 십사일에 열린다는구나.\""]], [["Remarkable coincidence that ‘ere, sir,’ replied Sam.", "\"그것참 묘한 우연이네요, 나리.\" 샘이 대답했다."]], [["‘Why remarkable, Sam?’ inquired Mr.", "\"왜 묘하다는 거냐, 샘?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘Walentine’s day, sir,’ responded Sam; ‘reg’lar good day for a breach o’ promise trial.’", "\"밸런타인 날이거든요, 나리.\" 샘이 응했다. \"혼인 약속 위반 재판에는 더없이 잘 어울리는 날이지요.\""]], [["Mr. Weller’s smile awakened no gleam of mirth in his master’s countenance.", "웰러 씨의 미소도 주인의 얼굴에는 한 줄기 기쁨의 빛조차 불러일으키지 못했다."], ["Mr. Pickwick turned abruptly round, and led the way in silence.", "픽윅 씨는 휙 돌아서서 말없이 앞장섰다."]], [["They had walked some distance, Mr. Pickwick trotting on before, plunged in profound meditation, and Sam following behind, with a countenance expressive of the most enviable and easy defiance of everything and everybody, when the latter, who was always especially anxious to impart to his master any exclusive information he possessed, quickened his pace until he was close at Mr.", "그들은 얼마간 걸어갔는데, 픽윅 씨는 깊은 사색에 잠긴 채 앞서 종종걸음을 쳤고, 샘은 무엇이든 누구든 가장 부럽고도 태평하게 무시하는 표정으로 뒤를 따랐다."], ["Pickwick’s heels; and, pointing up at a house they were passing, said--", "그러다 샘은—그는 늘 자기만 아는 정보를 주인에게 일러 주고 싶어 안달이었으므로—걸음을 빨리해 픽윅 씨의 발꿈치 바로 뒤까지 따라붙더니, 지나치던 어느 집을 가리키며 말했다."]], [["‘Wery nice pork-shop that ‘ere, sir.’", "\"저거 아주 근사한 돼지고기 가게입니다요, 나리.\""]], [["‘Yes, it seems so,’ said Mr.", "\"그래, 그런 것 같구나.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Celebrated sassage factory,’ said Sam.", "\"이름난 소시지 공장이지요.\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["‘Is it?’ said Mr.", "\"그러냐?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Is it!’ reiterated Sam, with some indignation; ‘I should rayther think it was.", "\"그러냐고요!\" 샘이 다소 분개하며 되받았다."], ["Why, sir, bless your innocent eyebrows, that’s where the mysterious disappearance of a ‘spectable tradesman took place four years ago.’", "\"그렇고말고요. 아니, 나리, 그 순진한 눈썹에 복이나 받으십시오, 사 년 전에 어느 점잖은 상인이 수상쩍게 사라진 데가 바로 저깁니다요.\""]], [["‘You don’t mean to say he was burked, Sam?’ said Mr.", "\"설마 그 사람이 살해당해 시신을 팔아넘겨졌다는 건 아니겠지, 샘?\""], ["Pickwick, looking hastily round.", "픽윅 씨가 황급히 주위를 둘러보며 말했다."]], [["‘No, I don’t indeed, sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"아니요, 정말 그런 건 아닙니다, 나리.\""], ["Weller, ‘I wish I did; far worse than that.", "웰러 씨가 대답했다. \"차라리 그랬으면 좋겠습니다."], ["He was the master o’ that ‘ere shop, sir, and the inwentor o’ the patent-never-leavin’-off sassage steam-ingin, as ‘ud swaller up a pavin’ stone if you put it too near, and grind it into sassages as easy as if it was a tender young babby.", "그보다 훨씬 끔찍하지요. 그 사람은 저 가게 주인이었는데요, 나리, 특허 받은 '결코-멈추지-않는'"], ["Wery proud o’ that machine he was, as it was nat’ral he should be, and he’d stand down in the celler a-lookin’ at it wen it was in full play, till he got quite melancholy with joy.", "소시지 증기 기계를 발명한 사람이었지요. 그 기계가 어찌나 대단한지, 포석을 너무 가까이 갖다 대면 통째로 삼켜서는 연한 갓난아기를 다루듯 손쉽게 소시지로 갈아 버렸답니다."], ["A wery happy man he’d ha’ been, Sir, in the procession o’ that ‘ere ingin and two more lovely hinfants besides, if it hadn’t been for his wife, who was a most owdacious wixin.", "그 사람은 그 기계를 무척 자랑스러워했지요, 그럴 만도 했고요. 기계가 한창 돌아갈 때면 지하실에 내려가 서서 그걸 바라보다가 기쁨에 겨워 아주 우울해질 지경이었답니다."], ["She was always a-follerin’ him about, and dinnin’ in his ears, till at last he couldn’t stand it no longer.", "그 기계 하고, 거기다 사랑스러운 어린것이 둘이나 더 있었으니, 나리, 그 사람은 그야말로 행복한 사람이었을 겁니다. 그 아내만 아니었더라면요."], ["“I’ll tell you what it is, my dear,”", "그 여편네가 아주 발칙한 표독한 여자였거든요."], ["he says one day; “if you persewere in this here sort of amusement,”", "늘 남편을 졸졸 따라다니며 귀에다 잔소리를 퍼부어 댔으니, 마침내 그 사람도 더는 못 견디게 됐지요. '여보, 내 분명히 말해 두겠는데,'"], ["he says, “I’m blessed if I don’t go away to ‘Merriker; and that’s all about it.”", "어느 날 그 사람이 그럽니다. '당신이 이런 식의 재미를 계속 부린다면, 내가 미국으로 떠나 버려도 난 모르겠소."], ["“You’re a idle willin,”", "그게 다요.'"], ["says she, “and I wish the ‘Merrikins joy of their bargain.”", "'당신은 게으른 망나니야,' 여편네가 그러죠."], ["Arter which she keeps on abusin’ of him for half an hour, and then runs into the little parlour behind the shop, sets to a-screamin’, says he’ll be the death on her, and falls in a fit, which lasts for three good hours--one o’ them fits wich is all screamin’ and kickin’.", "'미국 사람들이 당신 같은 걸 차지하게 됐으니 좋아라 하겠네.' 그러고는 반 시간 동안 그 사람을 욕해 대다가 가게 뒤편 작은 거실로 뛰어 들어가 비명을 질러 대며, 당신이 날 죽이려 든다고 하더니, 발작을 일으켜 꼬박 세 시간이나 가는 겁니다."], ["Well, next mornin’, the husband was missin’.", "온통 비명에 발길질뿐인 그런 발작 중 하나였지요."], ["He hadn’t taken nothin’ from the till--hadn’t even put on his greatcoat--so it was quite clear he warn’t gone to ‘Merriker.", "자, 그런데 이튿날 아침, 남편이 사라진 거예요. 금고에서 한 푼도 가져가지 않았고, 외투조차 걸치지 않았으니, 미국으로 가지 않은 건 분명했지요."], ["Didn’t come back next day; didn’t come back next week; missis had bills printed, sayin’ that, if he’d come back, he should be forgiven everythin’ (which was very liberal, seein’ that he hadn’t done nothin’ at all); the canals was dragged, and for two months arterwards, wenever a body turned up, it was carried, as a reg’lar thing, straight off to the sassage shop.", "다음 날도 안 돌아오고, 다음 주에도 안 돌아오자, 마누라가 전단을 인쇄해서, 돌아오기만 하면 다 용서하겠다고 했답니다(아주 너그러운 일이었지요, 그 사람이 아무 잘못도 하지 않은 걸 생각하면요). 운하란 운하는 죄다 훑었고, 그 뒤로 두 달 동안은 시신이 떠오를 때마다 으레 곧장 소시지 가게로 실어 갔답니다."], ["Hows’ever, none on ‘em answered; so they gave out that he’d run away, and she kep’ on the bis’ness.", "하지만 그 가운데 들어맞는 건 하나도 없었지요. 그래서 사람들은 그가 달아났다고 결론짓고, 마누라는 계속 장사를 했어요."], ["One Saturday night, a little, thin, old gen’l’m’n comes into the shop in a great passion and says, “Are you the missis o’ this here shop?”", "그런데 어느 토요일 밤, 작고 비쩍 마른 늙은 신사 양반이 잔뜩 화가 난 채 가게로 들어와서 그럽니다. '당신이 이 가게 주인 마누라요?'"], ["“Yes, I am,”", "'네, 그런데요.' 여편네가 그래요."], ["says she.", "'음, 부인,'"], ["“Well, ma’am,”", "그 양반이 그러죠. '그럼 잠깐 들러 한마디 하리다."], ["says he, “then I’ve just looked in to say that me and my family ain’t a-goin’ to be choked for nothin’; and more than that, ma’am,”", "나와 내 가족은 까닭 없이 목이 막혀 죽지는 않겠소이다. 게다가 부인,'"], ["he says, “you’ll allow me to observe that as you don’t use the primest parts of the meat in the manafacter o’ sassages, I’d think you’d find beef come nearly as cheap as buttons.”", "그 양반이 그래요. '한마디 덧붙이자면, 소시지를 만드는 데 고기에서 제일 좋은 부위를 안 쓰는 모양인데, 그렇다면 소고기가 단추만큼이나 싸게 먹힐 것 같소이다.'"], ["“As buttons, Sir!” says she.", "'단추라니요, 선생!'"], ["“Buttons, ma’am,”", "여편네가 그래요."], ["says the little, old gentleman, unfolding a bit of paper, and showin’ twenty or thirty halves o’ buttons.", "'단추 말이오, 부인.' 작고 늙은 신사 양반이 종잇조각을 펼쳐 스무 개에서 서른 개쯤 되는 반 토막 난 단추들을 보여 주며 그럽니다."], ["“Nice seasonin’ for sassages, is trousers’ buttons, ma’am.”", "'바지 단추란 게 소시지 양념으로는 아주 그만이로군요, 부인.'"], ["“They’re my husband’s buttons!”", "'그거 우리 남편 단추예요!'"], ["says the widder beginnin’ to faint, “What!”", "과부가 기절하기 시작하며 그래요. '뭐라고요!'"], ["screams the little old gen’l’m’n, turnin’ wery pale.", "작고 늙은 신사 양반이 새파랗게 질려 소리칩니다. '이제야 다 알겠어.'"], ["“I see it all,”", "과부가 그래요."], ["says the widder; “in a fit of temporary insanity he rashly converted hisself into sassages!”", "'일시적인 정신착란을 일으켜서 무모하게도 제 몸을 소시지로 만들어 버린 거야!' 정말 그랬던 겁니다, 나리.\""], ["And so he had, Sir,’ said Mr. Weller, looking steadily into Mr.", "웰러 씨가 픽윅 씨의 공포에 질린 얼굴을 빤히 들여다보며 말했다."], ["Pickwick’s horror-stricken countenance, ‘or else he’d been draw’d into the ingin; but however that might ha’ been, the little, old gen’l’m’n, who had been remarkably partial to sassages all his life, rushed out o’ the shop in a wild state, and was never heerd on arterwards!’", "\"아니면 기계에 빨려 들어갔거나요. 어느 쪽이었든 간에, 평생 소시지를 끔찍이 좋아하던 그 작고 늙은 신사 양반은 미친 듯이 가게를 뛰쳐나가서는 그 뒤로 다시는 소식이 없었답니다!\""]], [["The relation of this affecting incident of private life brought master and man to Mr. Perker’s chambers.", "사생활의 이 가슴 아픈 사건을 늘어놓는 동안 주인과 하인은 퍼커 씨의 사무실에 다다랐다."], ["Lowten, holding the door half open, was in conversation with a rustily-clad, miserable-looking man, in boots without toes and gloves without fingers.", "라우튼이 문을 반쯤 연 채로, 발가락 없는 부츠에 손가락 없는 장갑을 낀, 녹슨 듯 초라한 차림의 비참한 행색을 한 사내와 이야기를 나누고 있었다. 야위고 근심에 찌든 그의 얼굴에는 궁핍과 고통의—거의 절망에 가까운—흔적이 서려 있었다."], ["There were traces of privation and suffering--almost of despair--in his lank and care-worn countenance; he felt his poverty, for he shrank to the dark side of the staircase as Mr. Pickwick approached.", "그는 제 가난을 의식하는지, 픽윅 씨가 다가가자 계단의 어두운 쪽으로 몸을 움츠렸다."]], [["‘It’s very unfortunate,’ said the stranger, with a sigh.", "\"참으로 딱하게 됐군요.\" 그 낯선 사내가 한숨을 쉬며 말했다."]], [["‘Very,’ said Lowten, scribbling his name on the doorpost with his pen, and rubbing it out again with the feather.", "\"딱하지요.\" 라우튼이 펜으로 문기둥에 제 이름을 끄적이고는 깃촉으로 다시 지우며 말했다."], ["‘Will you leave a message for him?’", "\"그분께 전할 말이라도 남기시겠습니까?\""]], [["‘When do you think he’ll be back?’ inquired the stranger.", "\"언제쯤 돌아오실 것 같소?\" 낯선 사내가 물었다."]], [["‘Quite uncertain,’ replied Lowten, winking at Mr.", "\"전혀 알 수 없습니다.\""], ["Pickwick, as the stranger cast his eyes towards the ground.", "라우튼은 낯선 사내가 눈길을 바닥으로 떨구는 사이 픽윅 씨에게 눈을 찡긋하며 대답했다."]], [["‘You don’t think it would be of any use my waiting for him?’ said the stranger, looking wistfully into the office.", "\"내가 기다린다 한들 아무 소용 없을 것 같소?\" 낯선 사내가 간절한 눈빛으로 사무실 안을 들여다보며 말했다."]], [["‘Oh, no, I’m sure it wouldn’t,’ replied the clerk, moving a little more into the centre of the doorway.", "\"아, 그럼요, 소용없을 게 분명합니다.\" 사무원이 문간 한가운데로 조금 더 옮겨 서며 대답했다."], ["‘He’s certain not to be back this week, and it’s a chance whether he will be next; for when Perker once gets out of town, he’s never in a hurry to come back again.’", "\"이번 주에는 틀림없이 안 돌아오실 거고, 다음 주에 돌아오실지도 장담 못 합니다. 퍼커 씨는 한번 시내를 벗어나면 좀처럼 서둘러 돌아오시는 법이 없거든요.\""]], [["‘Out of town!’ said Mr.", "\"시내를 벗어났다고요!\""], ["Pickwick; ‘dear me, how unfortunate!’", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"이런, 참 딱하게 됐군요!\""]], [["‘Don’t go away, Mr.", "\"가지 마십시오, 픽윅 씨.\""], ["Pickwick,’ said Lowten, ‘I’ve got a letter for you.’ The stranger, seeming to hesitate, once more looked towards the ground, and the clerk winked slyly at Mr.", "라우튼이 말했다. \"선생께 드릴 편지가 있습니다.\""], ["Pickwick, as if to intimate that some exquisite piece of humour was going forward, though what it was Mr.", "낯선 사내는 망설이는 듯 다시 한번 바닥 쪽으로 눈길을 던졌고, 사무원은 무슨 절묘한 익살이 벌어지고 있다는 듯 픽윅 씨에게 슬쩍 눈을 찡긋했다."], ["Pickwick could not for the life of him divine.", "그러나 그게 무엇인지 픽윅 씨로서는 도무지 짐작할 수 없었다."]], [["‘Step in, Mr.", "\"들어오십시오, 픽윅 씨.\""], ["Pickwick,’ said Lowten.", "라우튼이 말했다."], ["‘Well, will you leave a message, Mr. Watty, or will you call again?’", "\"자, 전할 말을 남기시겠습니까, 와티 씨, 아니면 다시 들르시겠습니까?\""]], [["‘Ask him to be so kind as to leave out word what has been done in my business,’ said the man; ‘for God’s sake don’t neglect it, Mr.", "\"제발 부탁이니 제 일이 어떻게 됐는지 한 말씀 남겨 주십사 그분께 청해 주시오.\" 사내가 말했다."], ["Lowten.’", "\"제발 부탁이니 잊지 말아 주시오, 라우튼 씨.\""]], [["‘No, no; I won’t forget it,’ replied the clerk.", "\"네, 네, 잊지 않겠습니다.\" 사무원이 대답했다."], ["‘Walk in, Mr. Pickwick.", "\"들어오십시오, 픽윅 씨."], ["Good-morning, Mr.", "안녕히 가십시오, 와티 씨."], ["Watty; it’s a fine day for walking, isn’t it?’ Seeing that the stranger still lingered, he beckoned Sam Weller to follow his master in, and shut the door in his face.", "산책하기 좋은 날씨네요, 그렇지 않습니까?\" 낯선 사내가 여전히 머뭇거리는 것을 보고, 사무원은 샘 웰러에게 주인을 따라 들어오라고 손짓하고는 그의 면전에서 문을 닫았다."]], [["‘There never was such a pestering bankrupt as that since the world began, I do believe!’ said Lowten, throwing down his pen with the air of an injured man.", "\"세상이 생긴 이래로 저렇게 사람 귀찮게 구는 파산자는 또 없을 겁니다, 정말이지!\" 라우튼이 모욕당한 사람의 표정으로 펜을 내던지며 말했다."], ["‘His affairs haven’t been in Chancery quite four years yet, and I’m d----d if he don’t come worrying here twice a week.", "\"저 사람 사건이 형평법원에 걸린 지 아직 사 년도 안 됐는데, 일주일에 두 번씩 여기 와서 들볶지 뭡니까, 제기랄. 이쪽으로 오시지요, 픽윅 씨."], ["Step this way, Mr.", "퍼커 씨는 안에 계십니다. 선생을 만나 주실 겁니다, 제가 압니다."], ["Pickwick.", "지독하게 춥군요.\""], ["Perker is in, and he’ll see you, I know.", "그가 짜증스레 덧붙였다. \"저런 꾀죄죄한 부랑자랑 시간이나 허비하며 저 문간에 서 있자니 말입니다!\""], ["Devilish cold,’ he added pettishly, ‘standing at that door, wasting one’s time with such seedy vagabonds!’ Having very vehemently stirred a particularly large fire with a particularly small poker, the clerk led the way to his principal’s private room, and announced Mr. Pickwick.", "사무원은 유달리 작은 부지깽이로 유달리 큰 불을 사납게 휘젓고는 자기 상관의 개인 방으로 앞장서 가서 픽윅 씨가 왔음을 알렸다."]], [["‘Ah, my dear Sir,’ said little Mr.", "\"아, 친애하는 선생.\""], ["Perker, bustling up from his chair.", "작은 체구의 퍼커 씨가 의자에서 부산스레 일어나며 말했다."], ["‘Well, my dear sir, and what’s the news about your matter, eh?", "\"자, 친애하는 선생, 선생 일은 좀 어찌 돼 갑니까, 응?"], ["Anything more about our friends in Freeman’s Court?", "프리먼 광장의 우리 친구들에 관해 더 들으신 거라도 있습니까?"], ["They’ve not been sleeping, I know that.", "그들이 손 놓고 있지는 않았을 겁니다, 그건 제가 압니다."], ["Ah, they’re very smart fellows; very smart, indeed.’", "아, 아주 영민한 친구들이지요. 정말이지 아주 영민해요.\""]], [["As the little man concluded, he took an emphatic pinch of snuff, as a tribute to the smartness of Messrs. Dodson and Fogg.", "작은 사내는 말을 맺으며 도드슨과 포그 두 분의 영민함에 바치는 헌사인 양 코담배를 힘주어 한 줌 들이마셨다."]], [["‘They are great scoundrels,’ said Mr.", "\"그들은 대단한 악당들이오.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Aye, aye,’ said the little man; ‘that’s a matter of opinion, you know, and we won’t dispute about terms; because of course you can’t be expected to view these subjects with a professional eye. Well, we’ve done everything that’s necessary. I have retained Serjeant Snubbin.’", "\"아, 그렇지요.\" 작은 사내가 말했다. \"그거야 보기 나름이지요. 그러니 표현을 두고 다투지는 맙시다. 선생께서야 당연히 이런 일들을 전문가의 눈으로 보실 수는 없을 테니까요. 자, 필요한 건 다 해 두었습니다. 스너빈 변호인을 선임했지요.\""]], [["‘Is he a good man?’ inquired Mr.", "\"실력 있는 사람이오?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘Good man!’ replied Perker; ‘bless your heart and soul, my dear Sir, Serjeant Snubbin is at the very top of his profession.", "\"실력 있는 사람이냐고요!\" 퍼커가 대답했다."], ["Gets treble the business of any man in court--engaged in every case.", "\"이런, 친애하는 선생, 마음과 영혼에 복이나 받으십시오, 스너빈 변호인은 그 직업의 정점에 있는 분입니다."], ["You needn’t mention it abroad; but we say--we of the profession--that Serjeant Snubbin leads the court by the nose.’", "법정의 그 누구보다도 세 배나 많은 사건을 맡고, 어느 사건에나 관여하지요. 밖에 나가 말씀하실 건 없습니다만, 우리끼리는—우리 같은 동업자들끼리는—스너빈 변호인이 법정을 제멋대로 쥐락펴락한다고들 합니다.\""]], [["The little man took another pinch of snuff as he made this communication, and nodded mysteriously to Mr. Pickwick.", "작은 사내는 이 말을 전하며 코담배를 또 한 줌 들이마시고는 픽윅 씨에게 의미심장하게 고개를 끄덕였다."]], [["‘They have subpoenaed my three friends,’ said Mr.", "\"그들이 내 친구 셋에게 소환장을 보냈소.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Ah!", "\"아!"], ["of course they would,’ replied Perker.", "당연히 그러겠지요.\" 퍼커가 대답했다."], ["‘Important witnesses; saw you in a delicate situation.’", "\"중요한 증인들이니까요. 선생이 미묘한 상황에 처한 걸 본 사람들 아닙니까.\""]], [["‘But she fainted of her own accord,’ said Mr.", "\"하지만 그 여자가 제멋대로 기절한 거요.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick. ‘She threw herself into my arms.’", "\"내 품으로 제가 알아서 뛰어든 거란 말이오.\""]], [["‘Very likely, my dear Sir,’ replied Perker; ‘very likely and very natural.", "\"충분히 그럴 법한 일이지요, 친애하는 선생.\" 퍼커가 대답했다."], ["Nothing more so, my dear Sir, nothing.", "\"충분히 그럴 법하고 아주 자연스러운 일입니다. 그보다 더 자연스러운 일도 없지요, 친애하는 선생, 없고말고요."], ["But who’s to prove it?’", "하지만 그걸 누가 입증해 주겠습니까?\""]], [["‘They have subpoenaed my servant, too,’ said Mr.", "\"그들은 내 하인에게도 소환장을 보냈소.\""], ["Pickwick, quitting the other point; for there Mr. Perker’s question had somewhat staggered him.", "픽윅 씨가 그 점은 접어 두고 말했으니, 거기서 퍼커 씨의 질문이 그를 다소 당황하게 했기 때문이다."]], [["‘Sam?’ said Perker.", "\"샘 말입니까?\" 퍼커가 말했다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick replied in the affirmative.", "픽윅 씨가 그렇다고 대답했다."]], [["‘Of course, my dear Sir; of course.", "\"당연하지요, 친애하는 선생, 당연합니다."], ["I knew they would.", "그럴 줄 알았어요."], ["I could have told you that, a month ago.", "한 달 전에도 말씀드릴 수 있었습니다."], ["You know, my dear Sir, if you will take the management of your affairs into your own hands after entrusting them to your solicitor, you must also take the consequences.’ Here Mr.", "아시다시피, 친애하는 선생, 일을 변호사에게 맡겨 놓고서도 그 처리를 굳이 손수 떠맡으신다면, 그 결과 또한 떠안으셔야 하는 법이지요.\""], ["Perker drew himself up with conscious dignity, and brushed some stray grains of snuff from his shirt frill.", "여기서 퍼커 씨는 자못 위엄을 차리며 몸을 곧추세우고는 셔츠 주름 장식에 떨어진 코담배 가루 몇 알을 털어 냈다."]], [["‘And what do they want him to prove?’ asked Mr.", "\"그래서 그들이 그에게서 입증하려는 게 뭐요?\""], ["Pickwick, after two or three minutes’ silence.", "이삼 분 침묵한 뒤 픽윅 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘That you sent him up to the plaintiff ‘s to make some offer of a compromise, I suppose,’ replied Perker.", "\"선생께서 그를 원고에게 보내 무슨 화해 제안을 하게 했다는 것이겠지요, 아마도요.\" 퍼커가 대답했다."], ["‘It don’t matter much, though; I don’t think many counsel could get a great deal out of him.’", "\"하지만 그건 별로 대수롭지 않습니다. 그에게서 많은 걸 캐낼 수 있는 변호사는 그리 많지 않을 테니까요.\""]], [["‘I don’t think they could,’ said Mr.", "\"나도 그렇게 생각하오.\""], ["Pickwick, smiling, despite his vexation, at the idea of Sam’s appearance as a witness.", "픽윅 씨가 샘이 증인으로 나설 모습을 떠올리며, 짜증 속에서도 미소를 지으며 말했다."], ["‘What course do we pursue?’", "\"그럼 우리는 어떤 방침을 취하오?\""]], [["‘We have only one to adopt, my dear Sir,’ replied Perker; ‘cross-examine the witnesses; trust to Snubbin’s eloquence; throw dust in the eyes of the judge; throw ourselves on the jury.’", "\"우리가 취할 길은 단 하나뿐입니다, 친애하는 선생.\" 퍼커가 대답했다. \"증인들을 반대신문하고, 스너빈의 웅변에 기대고, 판사의 눈에 먼지를 뿌리고, 배심원들에게 우리를 맡기는 것이지요.\""]], [["‘And suppose the verdict is against me?’ said Mr.", "\"그래서 평결이 내게 불리하게 나오면?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["Mr. Perker smiled, took a very long pinch of snuff, stirred the fire, shrugged his shoulders, and remained expressively silent.", "퍼커 씨는 미소를 짓고 코담배를 아주 길게 한 줌 들이마시더니, 불을 휘젓고 어깨를 으쓱하고는 의미심장하게 침묵을 지켰다."]], [["‘You mean that in that case I must pay the damages?’ said Mr.", "\"그 경우엔 내가 손해 배상금을 물어야 한다는 뜻이오?\""], ["Pickwick, who had watched this telegraphic answer with considerable sternness.", "이 무언의 답을 자못 엄숙하게 지켜보던 픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["Perker gave the fire another very unnecessary poke, and said, ‘I am afraid so.’", "퍼커는 또다시 전혀 쓸데없이 불을 한 번 쑤시고는 말했다. \"유감스럽게도 그렇습니다.\""]], [["‘Then I beg to announce to you my unalterable determination to pay no damages whatever,’ said Mr.", "\"그렇다면 분명히 알려 드리리다. 나는 손해 배상금을 단 한 푼도 물지 않기로 단단히 마음먹었소.\""], ["Pickwick, most emphatically.", "픽윅 씨가 더없이 단호하게 말했다."], ["‘None, Perker.", "\"한 푼도요, 퍼커."], ["Not a pound, not a penny of my money, shall find its way into the pockets of Dodson and Fogg.", "내 돈은 단 일 파운드도, 단 일 페니도 도드슨과 포그의 주머니로 들어가는 일은 없을 것이오."], ["That is my deliberate and irrevocable determination.’ Mr.", "이것이 신중히 내린, 결코 번복할 수 없는 결심이오.\""], ["Pickwick gave a heavy blow on the table before him, in confirmation of the irrevocability of his intention.", "픽윅 씨는 제 결심이 번복될 수 없음을 확인하듯 앞에 놓인 탁자를 세게 내리쳤다."]], [["‘Very well, my dear Sir, very well,’ said Perker.", "\"좋습니다, 친애하는 선생, 좋아요.\" 퍼커가 말했다."], ["‘You know best, of course.’", "\"물론 선생이 제일 잘 아시겠지요.\""]], [["‘Of course,’ replied Mr.", "\"물론이오.\""], ["Pickwick hastily.", "픽윅 씨가 황급히 대답했다."], ["‘Where does Serjeant Snubbin live?’", "\"스너빈 변호인은 어디 사오?\""]], [["In Lincoln’s Inn Old Square,’ replied Perker.", "\"링컨스 인 올드 광장에 삽니다.\" 퍼커가 대답했다."]], [["‘I should like to see him,’ said Mr.", "\"그를 만나고 싶소.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘See Serjeant Snubbin, my dear Sir!’ rejoined Perker, in utter amazement.", "\"스너빈 변호인을 만나시겠다고요, 친애하는 선생!\" 퍼커가 어처구니없다는 듯 되받았다."], ["‘Pooh, pooh, my dear Sir, impossible.", "\"쯧쯧, 친애하는 선생, 그건 불가능합니다."], ["See Serjeant Snubbin!", "스너빈 변호인을 만나다니요!"], ["Bless you, my dear Sir, such a thing was never heard of, without a consultation fee being previously paid, and a consultation fixed.", "이런, 친애하는 선생, 그런 일은 들어 본 적도 없습니다. 상담료를 미리 치르고 상담 일정을 잡지 않고서는요."], ["It couldn’t be done, my dear Sir; it couldn’t be done.’", "안 될 일입니다, 친애하는 선생. 안 될 일이에요.\""]], [["Mr.", "그러나 픽윅 씨는 그 일이 될 수 있을 뿐만 아니라 반드시 되어야 한다고 마음을 굳혔다."], ["Pickwick, however, had made up his mind not only that it could be done, but that it should be done; and the consequence was, that within ten minutes after he had received the assurance that the thing was impossible, he was conducted by his solicitor into the outer office of the great Serjeant Snubbin himself.", "그리하여 그 일이 불가능하다는 다짐을 들은 지 십 분도 채 안 되어, 그는 자기 변호사의 안내를 받아 다름 아닌 위대한 스너빈 변호인의 바깥 사무실로 들어서게 되었다."]], [["It was an uncarpeted room of tolerable dimensions, with a large writing- table drawn up near the fire, the baize top of which had long since lost all claim to its original hue of green, and had gradually grown gray with dust and age, except where all traces of its natural colour were obliterated by ink-stains.", "그곳은 카펫이 깔리지 않은 제법 넓은 방으로, 난로 가까이에 커다란 필기용 탁자가 놓여 있었는데, 그 탁자에 씌운 모직 천은 본래의 초록빛을 주장할 자격을 잃은 지 오래여서, 잉크 얼룩으로 본래 색깔의 흔적마저 지워진 자리를 빼고는 먼지와 세월에 차츰 잿빛으로 바래 있었다."], ["Upon the table were numerous little bundles of papers tied with red tape; and behind it, sat an elderly clerk, whose sleek appearance and heavy gold watch-chain presented imposing indications of the extensive and lucrative practice of Mr. Serjeant Snubbin.", "탁자 위에는 붉은 끈으로 묶인 작은 서류 뭉치들이 잔뜩 널려 있었고, 그 뒤에는 한 늙은 사무원이 앉아 있었으니, 그의 매끈한 행색과 묵직한 금시계 줄은 스너빈 변호인의 폭넓고 짭짤한 일거리를 위풍당당하게 드러내고 있었다."]], [["‘Is the Serjeant in his room, Mr.", "\"변호인께서는 방에 계십니까, 맬러드 씨?\""], ["Mallard?’ inquired Perker, offering his box with all imaginable courtesy.", "퍼커가 더없이 정중하게 코담배갑을 내밀며 물었다."]], [["‘Yes, he is,’ was the reply, ‘but he’s very busy. Look here; not an opinion given yet, on any one of these cases; and an expedition fee paid with all of ‘em.’ The clerk smiled as he said this, and inhaled the pinch of snuff with a zest which seemed to be compounded of a fondness for snuff and a relish for fees.", "\"네, 계십니다.\" 대답이 돌아왔다. \"하지만 무척 바쁘십니다. 보십시오, 이 사건들 가운데 어느 하나에도 아직 의견서를 안 내셨는데, 다들 신속 처리비까지 치렀거든요.\" 사무원은 이렇게 말하며 미소를 짓고는, 코담배를 좋아하는 마음과 수수료를 즐기는 마음이 한데 어우러진 듯한 흐뭇함으로 코담배를 한 줌 들이마셨다."]], [["‘Something like practice that,’ said Perker.", "\"제법 일이 많으신 모양이군요.\" 퍼커가 말했다."]], [["‘Yes,’ said the barrister’s clerk, producing his own box, and offering it with the greatest cordiality; ‘and the best of it is, that as nobody alive except myself can read the serjeant’s writing, they are obliged to wait for the opinions, when he has given them, till I have copied ‘em, ha-ha-ha!’", "\"네.\" 변호인의 사무원이 제 코담배갑을 꺼내 더없이 다정하게 내밀며 말했다. \"그리고 무엇보다 좋은 건, 저 말고는 살아 있는 어느 누구도 변호인의 글씨를 못 읽으니, 변호인이 의견서를 내 주셔도 제가 그걸 베껴 쓸 때까지 다들 꼼짝없이 기다려야 한다는 거지요, 하하하!\""]], [["‘Which makes good for we know who, besides the serjeant, and draws a little more out of the clients, eh?’ said Perker; ‘Ha, ha, ha!’ At this the serjeant’s clerk laughed again--not a noisy boisterous laugh, but a silent, internal chuckle, which Mr.", "\"그러면 변호인 말고도 우리가 다 아는 누군가한테 득이 되고, 의뢰인들한테서 조금 더 우려낼 수 있겠군요, 응?\""], ["Pickwick disliked to hear.", "퍼커가 말했다. \"하, 하, 하!\""], ["When a man bleeds inwardly, it is a dangerous thing for himself; but when he laughs inwardly, it bodes no good to other people.", "이 말에 변호인의 사무원이 다시 웃었으니, 떠들썩하게 요란한 웃음이 아니라 소리 없이 속으로 킬킬대는 웃음이었는데, 픽윅 씨는 그 소리를 듣기 싫어했다. 사람이 속으로 피를 흘리는 것은 제 몸에 위험한 일이지만, 속으로 웃는 것은 남들에게 좋은 조짐이 아니다."]], [["‘You haven’t made me out that little list of the fees that I’m in your debt, have you?’ said Perker.", "\"제가 갚아야 할 수수료 목록은 아직 안 만들어 주셨지요?\" 퍼커가 말했다."]], [["‘No, I have not,’ replied the clerk.", "\"네, 아직 안 만들었습니다.\" 사무원이 대답했다."]], [["‘I wish you would,’ said Perker.", "\"좀 만들어 주셨으면 합니다.\""], ["‘Let me have them, and I’ll send you a cheque.", "퍼커가 말했다. \"그걸 주시면 수표를 보내 드리지요."], ["But I suppose you’re too busy pocketing the ready money, to think of the debtors, eh?", "하지만 현찰 챙기느라 너무 바빠서 빚진 사람들 생각할 겨를이 없으신 모양이군요, 응?"], ["ha, ha, ha!’ This sally seemed to tickle the clerk amazingly, and he once more enjoyed a little quiet laugh to himself.", "하, 하, 하!\" 이 농담이 사무원을 굉장히 즐겁게 한 듯, 그는 또 한 번 혼자 조용히 웃음을 즐겼다."]], [["‘But, Mr.", "\"한데 맬러드 씨, 친애하는 벗이여.\""], ["Mallard, my dear friend,’ said Perker, suddenly recovering his gravity, and drawing the great man’s great man into a Corner, by the lappel of his coat; ‘you must persuade the Serjeant to see me, and my client here.’", "퍼커가 갑자기 진지한 표정을 되찾고는 그 위대한 분의 위대한 분을 외투 깃을 잡아 한쪽 구석으로 끌고 가며 말했다. \"변호인께서 저와 여기 제 의뢰인을 만나 주시도록 설득해 주셔야겠습니다.\""]], [["‘Come, come,’ said the clerk, ‘that’s not bad either.", "\"이런, 이런.\""], ["See the Serjeant!", "사무원이 말했다."], ["come, that’s too absurd.’ Notwithstanding the absurdity of the proposal, however, the clerk allowed himself to be gently drawn beyond the hearing of Mr.", "\"그것도 제법 괜찮은 말씀이군요. 변호인을 만난다니요!"], ["Pickwick; and after a short conversation conducted in whispers, walked softly down a little dark passage, and disappeared into the legal luminary’s sanctum, whence he shortly returned on tiptoe, and informed Mr.", "원, 너무 터무니없습니다.\""], ["Perker and Mr. Pickwick that the Serjeant had been prevailed upon, in violation of all established rules and customs, to admit them at once.", "그러나 그 제안이 터무니없음에도 불구하고, 사무원은 픽윅 씨의 귀에 들리지 않는 곳까지 슬슬 끌려가도록 내버려 두었고, 속닥속닥 짧게 이야기를 나눈 뒤 어두운 작은 복도를 살금살금 내려가더니 그 법조계 명사의 성소 안으로 사라졌다가, 이내 발끝으로 살그머니 돌아와 퍼커 씨와 픽윅 씨에게, 변호인이 모든 확립된 규칙과 관례를 어기고 두 사람을 당장 들이도록 설득되었다고 알렸다."]], [["Mr. Serjeant Snubbins was a lantern-faced, sallow-complexioned man, of about five-and-forty, or--as the novels say--he might be fifty.", "스너빈 변호인은 얼굴이 등잔처럼 길쭉하고 안색이 누르께한 사내로, 마흔다섯쯤 되었거나—소설에서 으레 말하듯—쉰쯤 되었을 수도 있었다."], ["He had that dull-looking, boiled eye which is often to be seen in the heads of people who have applied themselves during many years to a weary and laborious course of study; and which would have been sufficient, without the additional eyeglass which dangled from a broad black riband round his neck, to warn a stranger that he was very near-sighted.", "그는 여러 해 동안 고되고 힘겨운 학업에 매달려 온 사람들의 머리에서 흔히 볼 수 있는, 흐리멍덩하고 삶은 듯한 눈을 가지고 있었으니, 그것만으로도—목에 두른 넓은 검은 리본에 매달린 안경이 없더라도—낯선 이에게 그가 몹시 근시임을 알리기에 충분했다."], ["His hair was thin and weak, which was partly attributable to his having never devoted much time to its arrangement, and partly to his having worn for five- and-twenty years the forensic wig which hung on a block beside him.", "그의 머리카락은 가늘고 힘이 없었는데, 이는 한편으로는 그가 머리 손질에 시간을 들인 적이 없기 때문이요, 한편으로는 이십오 년 동안 옆에 놓인 받침대에 걸려 있는 법정용 가발을 써 온 탓이었다."], ["The marks of hairpowder on his coat-collar, and the ill-washed and worse tied white neckerchief round his throat, showed that he had not found leisure since he left the court to make any alteration in his dress; while the slovenly style of the remainder of his costume warranted the inference that his personal appearance would not have been very much improved if he had.", "외투 깃에 묻은 가발 분 자국과, 목에 두른 잘 빨지도 못하고 더 엉성하게 맨 흰 목도리를 보면, 그가 법정을 나선 뒤 옷차림에 손볼 겨를조차 없었음을 알 수 있었다. 한편 나머지 차림새가 칠칠치 못한 것으로 미루어, 설령 손을 봤다 한들 그의 외양이 크게 나아지지는 않았으리라 짐작할 만했다."], ["Books of practice, heaps of papers, and opened letters, were scattered over the table, without any attempt at order or arrangement; the furniture of the room was old and rickety; the doors of the book-case were rotting in their hinges; the dust flew out from the carpet in little clouds at every step; the blinds were yellow with age and dirt; the state of everything in the room showed, with a clearness not to be mistaken, that Mr.", "실무 서적과 서류 더미, 뜯어 놓은 편지들이 정돈하거나 배열하려는 어떤 시도도 없이 탁자 위에 어지러이 흩어져 있었고, 방의 가구는 낡고 삐걱거렸으며, 책장 문은 경첩에서 썩어 가고 있었다. 걸음을 옮길 때마다 카펫에서 먼지가 작은 구름처럼 풀썩풀썩 피어올랐고, 차양은 세월과 때로 누렇게 바래 있었다."], ["Serjeant Snubbin was far too much occupied with his professional pursuits to take any great heed or regard of his personal comforts.", "방 안 모든 것의 상태가, 의심할 여지없이 분명하게, 스너빈 변호인이 제 직업적 일에 너무도 몰두한 나머지 제 개인적 안락 따위에는 그다지 마음 쓰거나 신경 쓸 겨를이 없음을 보여 주고 있었다."]], [["The Serjeant was writing when his clients entered; he bowed abstractedly when Mr.", "변호인은 의뢰인들이 들어왔을 때 글을 쓰고 있었다."], ["Pickwick was introduced by his solicitor; and then, motioning them to a seat, put his pen carefully in the inkstand, nursed his left leg, and waited to be spoken to.", "그는 픽윅 씨가 그의 변호사에게 소개되자 건성으로 고개를 숙였고, 이어 두 사람에게 자리에 앉으라 손짓하고는 펜을 조심스레 잉크병에 꽂은 뒤, 왼쪽 다리를 끌어안고 누가 말을 걸어 주기를 기다렸다."]], [["‘Mr.", "\"픽윅 씨가 바델 대 픽윅 소송의 피고입니다, 스너빈 변호인.\""], ["Pickwick is the defendant in Bardell and Pickwick, Serjeant Snubbin,’ said Perker.", "퍼커가 말했다."]], [["‘I am retained in that, am I?’ said the Serjeant.", "\"내가 그 사건을 맡았던가요?\" 변호인이 말했다."]], [["‘You are, Sir,’ replied Perker.", "\"그렇습니다, 선생.\" 퍼커가 대답했다."]], [["The Serjeant nodded his head, and waited for something else.", "변호인은 고개를 끄덕이고는 또 다른 무언가를 기다렸다."]], [["‘Mr.", "\"픽윅 씨가 변호인을 꼭 찾아뵙고 싶어 하셨습니다, 스너빈 변호인.\" 퍼커가 말했다."], ["Pickwick was anxious to call upon you, Serjeant Snubbin,’ said Perker, ‘to state to you, before you entered upon the case, that he denies there being any ground or pretence whatever for the action against him; and that unless he came into court with clean hands, and without the most conscientious conviction that he was right in resisting the plaintiff’s demand, he would not be there at all.", "\"변호인께서 사건에 착수하시기 전에 말씀드리고 싶으셨던 거지요. 자기를 상대로 한 이 소송에는 어떤 근거나 구실도 전혀 없음을 부인하신다고요."], ["I believe I state your views correctly; do I not, my dear Sir?’ said the little man, turning to Mr.", "그리고 자신이 깨끗한 손으로, 또 원고의 요구에 맞서는 것이 옳다는 더없이 양심적인 확신을 품고 법정에 서는 것이 아니라면, 애초에 법정에 나가지도 않겠다고요. 제가 선생의 뜻을 정확히 전하고 있는 거지요?"], ["Pickwick.", "그렇지 않습니까, 친애하는 선생?\" 작은 사내가 픽윅 씨를 돌아보며 말했다."]], [["‘Quite so,’ replied that gentleman.", "\"바로 그렇소.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다."]], [["Mr. Serjeant Snubbin unfolded his glasses, raised them to his eyes; and, after looking at Mr. Pickwick for a few seconds with great curiosity, turned to Mr. Perker, and said, smiling slightly as he spoke--", "스너빈 변호인은 안경을 펴서 눈에 갖다 대고는, 잠시 큰 호기심을 품고 픽윅 씨를 바라보더니, 퍼커 씨에게 돌아서며 말하는 내내 가볍게 미소를 지으며 말했다."]], [["‘Has Mr. Pickwick a strong case?’", "\"픽윅 씨는 사건이 유리합니까?\""]], [["The attorney shrugged his shoulders.", "변호사는 어깨를 으쓱했다."]], [["‘Do you propose calling witnesses?’", "\"증인을 부를 작정이오?\" \"아닙니다.\""]], [["‘No.’\nThe smile on the Serjeant’s countenance became more defined; he rocked his leg with increased violence; and, throwing himself back in his easy- chair, coughed dubiously.", "변호인의 얼굴에 떠오른 미소가 한층 또렷해졌다. 그는 더욱 사납게 다리를 흔들더니, 안락의자에 몸을 뒤로 젖히고는 미심쩍은 듯 기침을 했다."]], [["These tokens of the Serjeant’s presentiments on the subject, slight as they were, were not lost on Mr. Pickwick.", "이 문제에 대한 변호인의 예감을 드러내는 이런 징표들은, 비록 사소하기는 했으나, 픽윅 씨에게 그냥 지나쳐지지 않았다."], ["He settled the spectacles, through which he had attentively regarded such demonstrations of the barrister’s feelings as he had permitted himself to exhibit, more firmly on his nose; and said with great energy, and in utter disregard of all Mr. Perker’s admonitory winkings and frownings--", "그는 변호인이 스스로 드러내도록 허락한 만큼의 감정 표시를 주의 깊게 살피던 안경을 코 위에 더 단단히 고쳐 쓰고는, 퍼커 씨가 보내는 온갖 경고의 눈짓과 찌푸림을 전혀 아랑곳하지 않은 채 자못 힘차게 말했다."]], [["‘My wishing to wait upon you, for such a purpose as this, Sir, appears, I have no doubt, to a gentleman who sees so much of these matters as you must necessarily do, a very extraordinary circumstance.’", "\"제가 이런 목적으로 선생을 찾아뵙고자 한 것이, 이런 일들을 으레 그토록 많이 보아 오셨을 신사분께는, 의심할 바 없이 매우 유별난 일로 비치리라 봅니다.\""]], [["The Serjeant tried to look gravely at the fire, but the smile came back again.", "변호인은 진지하게 불을 바라보려 했으나, 미소가 다시 떠올랐다."]], [["‘Gentlemen of your profession, Sir,’ continued Mr.", "\"선생 같은 직업을 가진 분들은,\" 픽윅 씨가 계속했다."], ["Pickwick, ‘see the worst side of human nature.", "\"인간 본성의 가장 추악한 면을 보십니다."], ["All its disputes, all its ill-will and bad blood, rise up before you.", "인간의 온갖 분쟁, 온갖 악의와 반목이 선생 앞에 펼쳐지지요."], ["You know from your experience of juries (I mean no disparagement to you, or them) how much depends upon effect; and you are apt to attribute to others, a desire to use, for purposes of deception and self-interest, the very instruments which you, in pure honesty and honour of purpose, and with a laudable desire to do your utmost for your client, know the temper and worth of so well, from constantly employing them yourselves.", "선생은 배심원에 대한 경험으로(선생이나 배심원들을 깎아내리려는 뜻은 결코 아닙니다) 효과라는 것이 얼마나 큰 비중을 차지하는지 아십니다. 그리고 선생은, 순수한 정직과 명예로운 목적으로, 의뢰인을 위해 최선을 다하려는 갸륵한 마음으로, 또 늘 손수 다루어 오신 까닭에 그 본성과 가치를 너무도 잘 알고 계신 바로 그 수단들을, 남들도 속임수와 사리사욕을 위해 쓰려 든다고 여기기 쉽습니다."], ["I really believe that to this circumstance may be attributed the vulgar but very general notion of your being, as a body, suspicious, distrustful, and over-cautious.", "정말이지 저는, 선생들이 한 집단으로서 의심 많고 불신에 차 있으며 지나치게 조심스럽다는 저속하지만 매우 일반적인 통념이 바로 이 정황에서 비롯되었다고 믿습니다."], ["Conscious as I am, sir, of the disadvantage of making such a declaration to you, under such circumstances, I have come here, because I wish you distinctly to understand, as my friend Mr.", "이런 상황에서 선생께 이런 선언을 하는 것이 불리하다는 걸 잘 알면서도, 저는 여기 왔습니다. 제 벗 퍼커 씨가 말씀드렸듯이, 제게 씌워진 거짓 혐의에 저는 결백하다는 것을 선생께서 분명히 이해해 주시기를 바라기 때문입니다."], ["Perker has said, that I am innocent of the falsehood laid to my charge; and although I am very well aware of the inestimable value of your assistance, Sir, I must beg to add, that unless you sincerely believe this, I would rather be deprived of the aid of your talents than have the advantage of them.’", "또한 선생의 도움이 더없이 값진 것임을 잘 알면서도, 한 말씀 덧붙이지 않을 수 없습니다. 선생께서 이를 진심으로 믿어 주시지 않는다면, 저는 차라리 선생의 재능이 주는 이점을 누리느니 그 도움을 받지 못하는 편을 택하겠습니다.\""]], [["Long before the close of this address, which we are bound to say was of a very prosy character for Mr. Pickwick, the Serjeant had relapsed into a state of abstraction.", "우리로서는 픽윅 씨치고는 매우 장황한 연설이었다고 말하지 않을 수 없는 이 일장 연설이 채 끝나기도 훨씬 전에, 변호인은 이미 멍한 상태로 빠져들어 있었다."], ["After some minutes, however, during which he had reassumed his pen, he appeared to be again aware of the presence of his clients; raising his head from the paper, he said, rather snappishly--", "그러나 몇 분 뒤, 그사이 다시 펜을 집어 들었던 그는 의뢰인들이 곁에 있음을 다시금 깨달은 듯, 종이에서 고개를 들고 다소 퉁명스럽게 말했다."]], [["‘Who is with me in this case?’", "\"이 사건에 나와 함께하는 사람이 누구지요?\""]], [["‘Mr.", "\"펑키 씨입니다, 스너빈 변호인.\""], ["Phunky, Serjeant Snubbin,’ replied the attorney.", "변호사가 대답했다."]], [["‘Phunky--Phunky,’ said the Serjeant, ‘I never heard the name before.", "\"펑키, 펑키.\" 변호인이 말했다."], ["He must be a very young man.’", "\"그런 이름은 전에 들어 본 적이 없는데. 아주 젊은 사람이겠군요.\""]], [["‘Yes, he is a very young man,’ replied the attorney.", "\"네, 아주 젊은 사람입니다.\" 변호사가 대답했다."], ["‘He was only called the other day.", "\"얼마 전에야 변호사 자격을 얻었지요."], ["Let me see--he has not been at the Bar eight years yet.’", "어디 보자, 법정에 선 지 아직 팔 년도 안 됐습니다.\""]], [["‘Ah, I thought not,’ said the Serjeant, in that sort of pitying tone in which ordinary folks would speak of a very helpless little child.", "\"아, 그러리라 생각했소.\" 변호인이, 보통 사람들이 아주 가엾고 무력한 어린아이를 두고 말할 법한 그런 측은해하는 투로 말했다."], ["‘Mr. Mallard, send round to Mr.--Mr.--’", "\"맬러드 씨, 사람을 보내 미스터—미스터—\""]], [["Phunky’s--Holborn Court, Gray’s Inn,’ interposed Perker.", "\"펑키 씨네에요."], ["(Holborn Court, by the bye, is South Square now.)--‘Mr.", "그레이즈 인의 홀번 광장입니다.\" 퍼커가 끼어들었다."], ["Phunky, and say I should be glad if he’d step here, a moment.’", "(그런데 홀번 광장은 지금은 사우스 광장이다.) \"펑키 씨에게, 잠깐 이리로 와 주면 고맙겠다고 전해 주십시오.\""]], [["Mr. Mallard departed to execute his commission; and Serjeant Snubbin relapsed into abstraction until Mr. Phunky himself was introduced.", "맬러드 씨는 그 분부를 수행하러 떠났고, 스너빈 변호인은 펑키 씨가 직접 안내되어 들어올 때까지 다시 멍한 상태에 빠져들었다."]], [["Although an infant barrister, he was a full-grown man.", "비록 풋내기 변호사였으나, 그는 다 자란 성인이었다."], ["He had a very nervous manner, and a painful hesitation in his speech; it did not appear to be a natural defect, but seemed rather the result of timidity, arising from the consciousness of being ‘kept down’ by want of means, or interest, or connection, or impudence, as the case might be.", "그는 몹시 신경질적인 태도에다 말을 할 때면 괴로울 만큼 더듬거렸는데, 그것은 타고난 결함이라기보다는, 형편이든 인맥이든 연줄이든 뻔뻔함이든, 그 무엇이 부족하여 '눌려 지낸다'는 의식에서 비롯된 소심함의 결과인 듯했다."], ["He was overawed by the Serjeant, and profoundly courteous to the attorney.", "그는 변호인 앞에서는 주눅이 들었고, 변호사에게는 더없이 깍듯했다."]], [["‘I have not had the pleasure of seeing you before, Mr.", "\"전에 뵌 적이 없었던 것 같군요, 펑키 씨.\""], ["Phunky,’ said Serjeant Snubbin, with haughty condescension.", "스너빈 변호인이 거만하게 생색을 내며 말했다."]], [["Mr. Phunky bowed.", "펑키 씨는 고개를 숙였다."], ["He had had the pleasure of seeing the Serjeant, and of envying him too, with all a poor man’s envy, for eight years and a quarter.", "그는 팔 년 하고도 사분기 동안, 가난한 사람 특유의 온갖 부러움을 품고서, 변호인을 뵙고 또 그를 부러워하는 기쁨을 누려 왔던 것이다."]], [["‘You are with me in this case, I understand?’ said the Serjeant.", "\"이 사건에 나와 함께하신다고 알고 있는데, 맞소?\" 변호인이 말했다."]], [["If Mr.", "펑키 씨가 부유한 사람이었다면, 즉시 사무원을 불러 일깨워 달라 했을 것이다."], ["Phunky had been a rich man, he would have instantly sent for his clerk to remind him; if he had been a wise one, he would have applied his forefinger to his forehead, and endeavoured to recollect, whether, in the multiplicity of his engagements, he had undertaken this one or not; but as he was neither rich nor wise (in this sense, at all events) he turned red, and bowed.", "현명한 사람이었다면, 집게손가락을 이마에 갖다 대고 자신의 그 숱한 일거리 가운데 이 사건을 맡았는지 안 맡았는지 떠올리려 애썼을 것이다. 그러나 그는 (적어도 이런 면에서는) 부유하지도 현명하지도 못했으므로, 얼굴이 빨개진 채 고개를 숙였다."]], [["‘Have you read the papers, Mr.", "\"서류는 읽어 보셨소, 펑키 씨?\""], ["Phunky?’ inquired the Serjeant.", "변호인이 물었다."]], [["Here again, Mr. Phunky should have professed to have forgotten all about the merits of the case; but as he had read such papers as had been laid before him in the course of the action, and had thought of nothing else, waking or sleeping, throughout the two months during which he had been retained as Mr.", "여기서도 펑키 씨는 이 사건의 시시비비에 대해 까맣게 잊었노라 둘러댔어야 했다."], ["Serjeant Snubbin’s junior, he turned a deeper red and bowed again.", "그러나 그는 소송이 진행되는 동안 자기 앞에 놓인 서류라는 서류는 죄다 읽었고, 스너빈 변호인의 후배 변호사로 선임된 두 달 내내 깨어 있을 때나 잠잘 때나 그 생각밖에 하지 않았던 터라, 더욱 붉게 얼굴을 물들이며 다시 고개를 숙였다."]], [["‘This is Mr.", "\"이분이 픽윅 씨요.\""], ["Pickwick,’ said the Serjeant, waving his pen in the direction in which that gentleman was standing.", "변호인이 그 신사가 서 있는 쪽으로 펜을 흔들며 말했다."]], [["Mr. Phunky bowed to Mr. Pickwick, with a reverence which a first client must ever awaken; and again inclined his head towards his leader.", "펑키 씨는 첫 의뢰인이라면 으레 불러일으키기 마련인 그런 경의를 담아 픽윅 씨에게 고개를 숙였고, 다시 자기 선임자 쪽으로 머리를 기울였다."]], [["‘Perhaps you will take Mr. Pickwick away,’ said the Serjeant, ‘and--and- -and--hear anything Mr.", "\"펑키 씨가 픽윅 씨를 모시고 나가서,\""], ["Pickwick may wish to communicate.", "변호인이 말했다."], ["We shall have a consultation, of course.’ With that hint that he had been interrupted quite long enough, Mr.", "\"그리고—그리고—그리고—픽윅 씨가 전하고 싶어 하시는 것은 무엇이든 들어 주시는 게 좋겠소."], ["Serjeant Snubbin, who had been gradually growing more and more abstracted, applied his glass to his eyes for an instant, bowed slightly round, and was once more deeply immersed in the case before him, which arose out of an interminable lawsuit, originating in the act of an individual, deceased a century or so ago, who had stopped up a pathway leading from some place which nobody ever came from, to some other place which nobody ever went to.", "물론 우리는 상담을 하게 될 테고요.\" 충분히 오래 방해받았다는 그 암시와 함께, 점점 더 멍한 상태로 빠져들고 있던 스너빈 변호인은 안경을 잠시 눈에 갖다 대고는 주위를 향해 가볍게 고개를 숙인 뒤, 다시금 눈앞의 사건에 깊이 몰두했으니, 그 사건이란 한 세기쯤 전에 죽은 어느 인물의 행위에서 비롯된 끝없는 소송에서 생겨난 것으로, 그 인물은 아무도 오지 않는 어딘가에서 아무도 가지 않는 또 다른 어딘가로 이어지는 오솔길을 막아 버렸던 것이다."]], [["Mr. Phunky would not hear of passing through any door until Mr.", "펑키 씨는 픽윅 씨와 그의 변호사가 먼저 지나갈 때까지는 어떤 문도 결코 먼저 지나가려 하지 않았으므로, 그들이 광장으로 나오기까지는 한참이 걸렸다."], ["Pickwick and his solicitor had passed through before him, so it was some time before they got into the Square; and when they did reach it, they walked up and down, and held a long conference, the result of which was, that it was a very difficult matter to say how the verdict would go; that nobody could presume to calculate on the issue of an action; that it was very lucky they had prevented the other party from getting Serjeant Snubbin; and other topics of doubt and consolation, common in such a position of affairs.", "그리고 마침내 광장에 다다르자, 그들은 이리저리 거닐며 긴 회담을 나누었는데, 그 결론인즉, 평결이 어떻게 날지 말하기는 매우 어려운 일이라는 것, 누구도 소송의 결과를 감히 예측할 수 없다는 것, 상대편이 스너빈 변호인을 선임하지 못하게 막은 것은 매우 운 좋은 일이라는 것, 그 밖에 이런 처지에서 흔히 나누는 갖가지 의구심과 위안의 화제들이었다."]], [["Mr. Weller was then roused by his master from a sweet sleep of an hour’s duration; and, bidding adieu to Lowten, they returned to the city.", "그러고 나서 웰러 씨는 한 시간에 걸친 달콤한 잠에서 주인에게 깨워졌고, 라우튼에게 작별을 고한 뒤 그들은 도심으로 돌아왔다."]], [["CHAPTER XXXII. DESCRIBES, FAR MORE FULLY THAN THE COURT NEWSMAN EVER DID, A BACHELOR’S PARTY, GIVEN BY MR.", "버러의 랜트 거리에는 어떤 고요함이 깃들어 있어 영혼에 잔잔한 우수를 드리운다."], ["BOB SAWYER AT HIS LODGINGS IN THE BOROUGH\nThere is a repose about Lant Street, in the Borough, which sheds a gentle melancholy upon the soul.", "이 거리에는 언제나 세놓을 집이 꽤 많다."], ["There are always a good many houses to let in the street: it is a by-street too, and its dulness is soothing.", "게다가 뒷골목이어서 그 적막함이 마음을 달래 준다."], ["A house in Lant Street would not come within the denomination of a first- rate residence, in the strict acceptation of the term; but it is a most desirable spot nevertheless.", "랜트 거리의 집이란, 말의 엄밀한 뜻을 따지자면 일류 주거지에 드는 부류는 결코 아니지만, 그래도 더없이 바람직한 곳이다."], ["If a man wished to abstract himself from the world--to remove himself from within the reach of temptation--to place himself beyond the possibility of any inducement to look out of the window--we should recommend him by all means go to Lant Street.", "만일 어떤 사람이 세상으로부터 자기를 떼어 놓고 싶다면—유혹의 손길이 닿지 않는 곳으로 몸을 옮기고 싶다면—창밖을 내다보고 싶어질 어떤 유인조차 생길 수 없는 곳에 자기를 두고 싶다면, 우리는 무슨 일이 있어도 랜트 거리로 가라고 권하겠다."]], [["In this happy retreat are colonised a few clear-starchers, a sprinkling of journeymen bookbinders, one or two prison agents for the Insolvent Court, several small housekeepers who are employed in the Docks, a handful of mantua-makers, and a seasoning of jobbing tailors.", "이 행복한 은신처에는 풀 먹여 다림질하는 여인 몇과, 제본 직공 몇 사람, 파산법원의 감옥 대리인 한둘, 부두에서 일하는 자잘한 살림꾼 여럿, 망토 짓는 여인 한 줌, 그리고 임시 양복쟁이 약간이 자리 잡고 산다."], ["The majority of the inhabitants either direct their energies to the letting of furnished apartments, or devote themselves to the healthful and invigorating pursuit of mangling.", "주민의 대다수는 가구 딸린 셋방을 놓는 일에 정력을 쏟거나, 빨래를 짜고 다리는 건강하고 활기찬 생업에 몸을 바친다."], ["The chief features in the still life of the street are green shutters, lodging-bills, brass door-plates, and bell-handles; the principal specimens of animated nature, the pot-boy, the muffin youth, and the baked-potato man.", "이 거리의 정물(靜物) 중 으뜸가는 특징은 초록색 덧문, 셋방 안내문, 놋쇠 문패, 그리고 초인종 손잡이이고, 살아 움직이는 자연의 주요 표본은 술집 사환과 머핀 파는 소년과 군고구마 장수다."], ["The population is migratory, usually disappearing on the verge of quarter-day, and generally by night.", "인구는 떠돌이라 대개 분기 결산일* 어름이면 사라지는데, 흔히 밤을 틈탄다. 폐하의 세입은 이 행복한 골짜기에서는 좀처럼 거두어지지 않는다."], ["His Majesty’s revenues are seldom collected in this happy valley; the rents are dubious; and the water communication is very frequently cut off.", "집세는 미덥지 못하고, 수도 공급은 매우 자주 끊긴다. * 분기 결산일: 집세 따위를 치르는 분기별 정해진 날."]], [["Mr. Bob Sawyer embellished one side of the fire, in his first-floor front, early on the evening for which he had invited Mr.", "밥 소여 씨는 픽윅 씨를 초대한 그날 저녁 일찌감치, 자기 이층 앞방의 난롯가 한쪽을 차지하고 앉았고, 벤 앨런 씨가 다른 한쪽을 차지했다."], ["Pickwick, and Mr. Ben Allen the other.", "손님 맞을 채비는 다 끝난 듯했다."], ["The preparations for the reception of visitors appeared to be completed. The umbrellas in the passage had been heaped into the little corner outside the back-parlour door; the bonnet and shawl of the landlady’s servant had been removed from the bannisters; there were not more than two pairs of pattens on the street-door mat; and a kitchen candle, with a very long snuff, burned cheerfully on the ledge of the staircase window.", "복도의 우산들은 뒷거실 문밖 자그마한 구석에 쌓아 두었고, 안주인 하녀의 보닛과 숄은 난간에서 치워졌으며, 현관 매트 위에는 덧신이 두 켤레밖에 남아 있지 않았고, 심지가 아주 길게 자란 부엌 양초 하나가 층계 창턱에서 명랑하게 타고 있었다."], ["Mr. Bob Sawyer had himself purchased the spirits at a wine vaults in High Street, and had returned home preceding the bearer thereof, to preclude the possibility of their delivery at the wrong house.", "밥 소여 씨는 직접 하이 거리의 술 창고에서 독한 술을 사 와서, 엉뚱한 집에 배달될 가능성을 미연에 막으려고 그것을 나르는 사람보다 앞장서서 집으로 돌아왔다."], ["The punch was ready-made in a red pan in the bedroom; a little table, covered with a green baize cloth, had been borrowed from the parlour, to play at cards on; and the glasses of the establishment, together with those which had been borrowed for the occasion from the public-house, were all drawn up in a tray, which was deposited on the landing outside the door.", "펀치는 침실의 빨간 냄비에 미리 만들어 두었고, 카드놀이를 할 작은 탁자 하나는 초록색 베이즈 천을 씌워 거실에서 빌려 왔으며, 이 집의 술잔들은 이번 일을 위해 술집에서 빌려 온 잔들과 함께 모두 쟁반에 가지런히 올려 문밖 층계참에 놓아 두었다."]], [["Notwithstanding the highly satisfactory nature of all these arrangements, there was a cloud on the countenance of Mr. Bob Sawyer, as he sat by the fireside.", "이 모든 준비가 더없이 흡족하게 갖추어졌건만, 난롯가에 앉은 밥 소여 씨의 얼굴에는 먹구름이 끼어 있었다."], ["There was a sympathising expression, too, in the features of Mr. Ben Allen, as he gazed intently on the coals, and a tone of melancholy in his voice, as he said, after a long silence--", "벤 앨런 씨의 얼굴에도 동정 어린 표정이 어려 있어, 그는 석탄불을 골똘히 바라보다가 오랜 침묵 끝에 우수 어린 목소리로 말했다."]], [["‘Well, it is unlucky she should have taken it in her head to turn sour, just on this occasion.", "\"음, 하필 이런 날 그 여자가 심사가 뒤틀릴 생각을 하다니 운도 없군."], ["She might at least have waited till to-morrow.’", "적어도 내일까지는 기다려 줄 수도 있었을 텐데.\""]], [["‘That’s her malevolence--that’s her malevolence,’ returned Mr.", "\"그게 그 여자 심술이지—그게 다 그 여자 심술이라고.\" 밥 소여 씨가 격하게 받았다."], ["Bob Sawyer vehemently.", "\"그 여자가 그러는 거야."], ["‘She says that if I can afford to give a party I ought to be able to pay her confounded “little bill.”’", "내가 파티를 열 형편이 되면 자기 그 망할 '소액 청구서'쯤은 갚을 수 있어야 하는 거 아니냐고 말이야.\""]], [["How long has it been running?’ inquired Mr.", "\"그게 얼마나 밀린 건데?\" 벤 앨런 씨가 물었다."], ["Ben Allen. A bill, by the bye, is the most extraordinary locomotive engine that the genius of man ever produced.", "그건 그렇고, 청구서란 인간의 천재성이 일찍이 만들어 낸 가장 비상한 기관차다."], ["It would keep on running during the longest lifetime, without ever once stopping of its own accord.", "그것은 한 사람의 가장 긴 일생 내내 굴러가면서도 제 발로 한 번도 멈추는 법이 없다."]], [["‘Only a quarter, and a month or so,’ replied Mr.", "\"한 분기에 한 달쯤 더 됐을까.\""], ["Bob Sawyer.", "밥 소여 씨가 대답했다."]], [["Ben Allen coughed hopelessly, and directed a searching look between the two top bars of the stove.", "벤 앨런은 가망 없다는 듯 기침을 하고, 난로의 위쪽 살 두 개 사이를 샅샅이 살피는 눈길을 보냈다."]], [["‘It’ll be a deuced unpleasant thing if she takes it into her head to let out, when those fellows are here, won’t it?’ said Mr.", "\"저 친구들이 와 있을 때 그 여자가 떠들어 댈 생각을 하면 정말 망할 만큼 불쾌한 일일 텐데, 안 그래?\""], ["Ben Allen at length.", "마침내 벤 앨런 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Horrible,’ replied Bob Sawyer, ‘horrible.’", "\"끔찍하지.\" 밥 소여가 대답했다. \"끔찍하고말고.\""]], [["A low tap was heard at the room door. Mr.", "방문을 가볍게 두드리는 소리가 들렸다."], ["Bob Sawyer looked expressively at his friend, and bade the tapper come in; whereupon a dirty, slipshod girl in black cotton stockings, who might have passed for the neglected daughter of a superannuated dustman in very reduced circumstances, thrust in her head, and said--", "밥 소여 씨는 친구를 의미심장하게 바라보고는 두드린 사람더러 들어오라고 했다. 그러자 검은 무명 양말을 신은, 꾀죄죄하고 단정치 못한 처녀 하나가—몹시 영락한 처지에 놓인 늙은 청소부의 돌보지 못한 딸쯤으로 보일 만한 모습이었다—고개를 디밀고 말했다."]], [["‘Please, Mister Sawyer, Missis Raddle wants to speak to you.’", "\"저, 소여 선생님, 래들 마님이 선생님하고 할 말이 있으시대요.\""]], [["Before Mr.", "밥 소여 씨가 미처 대꾸하기도 전에, 처녀는 마치 누가 뒤에서 거칠게 잡아챈 듯 홱 하고 갑자기 사라졌다."], ["Bob Sawyer could return any answer, the girl suddenly disappeared with a jerk, as if somebody had given her a violent pull behind; this mysterious exit was no sooner accomplished, than there was another tap at the door--a smart, pointed tap, which seemed to say, ‘Here I am, and in I’m coming.’", "이 알 수 없는 퇴장이 이루어지자마자 문에서 또 한 번 두드리는 소리가 났다—날카롭고 또렷한 노크였는데, 마치 \"나 여기 있고, 이제 들어간다.\"라고 말하는 듯했다."]], [["Mr.", "밥 소여 씨는 비굴한 두려움이 어린 눈으로 친구를 흘끗 보고는 다시 한 번 외쳤다."], ["Bob Sawyer glanced at his friend with a look of abject apprehension, and once more cried, ‘Come in.’", "\"들어오시오.\""]], [["The permission was not at all necessary, for, before Mr.", "그 허락은 전혀 필요치 않았다."], ["Bob Sawyer had uttered the words, a little, fierce woman bounced into the room, all in a tremble with passion, and pale with rage.", "밥 소여 씨가 그 말을 채 내뱉기도 전에, 자그마하고 사나운 여자 하나가 방 안으로 뛰어들었기 때문이다. 격분으로 온몸을 떨고, 분노로 새파랗게 질린 채였다."]], [["‘Now, Mr.", "\"자, 소여 선생,\""], ["Sawyer,’ said the little, fierce woman, trying to appear very calm, ‘if you’ll have the kindness to settle that little bill of mine I’ll thank you, because I’ve got my rent to pay this afternoon, and my landlord’s a-waiting below now.’ Here the little woman rubbed her hands, and looked steadily over Mr.", "그 자그맣고 사나운 여자가 무척 침착해 보이려 애쓰며 말했다. \"내 그 소액 청구서를 친절히 갚아 주신다면 고맙겠어요."], ["Bob Sawyer’s head, at the wall behind him.", "오늘 오후에 집세를 내야 하는데, 집주인이 지금 아래층에서 기다리고 있거든요.\" 여기서 그 자그마한 여자는 두 손을 비비며 밥 소여 씨의 머리 너머 뒤쪽 벽을 뚫어지게 바라보았다."]], [["‘I am very sorry to put you to any inconvenience, Mrs.", "\"불편을 끼쳐 드려서 정말 죄송합니다, 래들 부인.\""], ["Raddle,’ said Bob Sawyer deferentially, ‘but--’", "밥 소여가 공손하게 말했다. \"하지만—\""]], [["‘Oh, it isn’t any inconvenience,’ replied the little woman, with a shrill titter.", "\"오, 불편이랄 것도 없어요.\" 그 자그마한 여자가 새된 소리로 키득거리며 받았다."], ["‘I didn’t want it particular before to-day; leastways, as it has to go to my landlord directly, it was as well for you to keep it as me.", "\"오늘 전까지는 딱히 급할 것도 없었으니까요. 적어도, 어차피 바로 집주인한테 넘어갈 돈이니, 내가 쥐고 있으나 선생이 쥐고 있으나 매한가지였죠."], ["You promised me this afternoon, Mr.", "선생이 오늘 오후에 약속하지 않았어요, 소여 선생."], ["Sawyer, and every gentleman as has ever lived here, has kept his word, Sir, as of course anybody as calls himself a gentleman does.’ Mrs.", "여기 살다 간 신사분들은 누구든 다 약속을 지켰답니다, 선생. 물론 스스로를 신사라 부르는 사람이라면 누구나 그러는 법이지요.\""], ["Raddle tossed her head, bit her lips, rubbed her hands harder, and looked at the wall more steadily than ever. It was plain to see, as Mr.", "래들 부인은 고개를 홱 젖히고, 입술을 깨물고, 두 손을 더 세차게 비비며, 그 어느 때보다 더 뚫어지게 벽을 바라보았다."], ["Bob Sawyer remarked in a style of Eastern allegory on a subsequent occasion, that she was ‘getting the steam up.’", "밥 소여 씨가 훗날 어느 자리에서 동방식 우화풍으로 평했듯, 그 여자가 \"증기를 끌어올리고\" 있다는 것은 누가 봐도 뻔했다."]], [["‘I am very sorry, Mrs.", "\"정말 죄송합니다, 래들 부인.\""], ["Raddle,’ said Bob Sawyer, with all imaginable humility, ‘but the fact is, that I have been disappointed in the City to-day.’--Extraordinary place that City.", "밥 소여가 상상할 수 있는 한껏 겸손하게 말했다. \"하지만 실은, 오늘 시내에서 일이 어긋났지 뭡니까.\"—참으로 별난 곳이다, 그 시내라는 데는."], ["An astonishing number of men always are getting disappointed there.", "거기서는 놀랄 만큼 많은 사람들이 늘 일이 어긋난다."]], [["‘Well, Mr. Sawyer,’ said Mrs.", "\"그래서요, 소여 선생,\""], ["Raddle, planting herself firmly on a purple cauliflower in the Kidderminster carpet, ‘and what’s that to me, Sir?’", "래들 부인이 키더민스터 카펫의 보라색 콜리플라워 무늬 위에 두 발을 단단히 디디고 서서 말했다. \"그게 나하고 무슨 상관이죠, 선생?\""]], [["‘I--I--have no doubt, Mrs.", "\"저는—저는—틀림없습니다, 래들 부인.\""], ["Raddle,’ said Bob Sawyer, blinking this last question, ‘that before the middle of next week we shall be able to set ourselves quite square, and go on, on a better system, afterwards.’", "밥 소여가 이 마지막 질문을 슬쩍 피하며 말했다. \"다음 주 중반 전에는 우리가 셈을 말끔히 맞출 수 있을 거고, 그 뒤로는 더 나은 방식으로 해 나갈 수 있을 겁니다.\""]], [["This was all Mrs. Raddle wanted.", "이것이야말로 래들 부인이 원하던 전부였다."], ["She had bustled up to the apartment of the unlucky Bob Sawyer, so bent upon going into a passion, that, in all probability, payment would have rather disappointed her than otherwise. She was in excellent order for a little relaxation of the kind, having just exchanged a few introductory compliments with Mr.", "부인은 운수 사나운 밥 소여의 방으로 부산하게 올라오면서 한바탕 성을 낼 작정을 단단히 굳히고 있었던 터라, 십중팔구 돈을 받았다면 오히려 김이 빠졌을 정도였다."], ["R. in the front kitchen.", "부인은 앞쪽 부엌에서 래들 씨와 막 서두 인사 격으로 몇 마디 입씨름을 나눈 참이라, 이런 종류의 자그마한 여흥을 즐기기에는 더없이 좋은 상태였다."]], [["‘Do you suppose, Mr. Sawyer,’ said Mrs.", "\"소여 선생, 당신 생각엔 말이에요,\""], ["Raddle, elevating her voice for the information of the neighbours--‘do you suppose that I’m a-going day after day to let a fellar occupy my lodgings as never thinks of paying his rent, nor even the very money laid out for the fresh butter and lump sugar that’s bought for his breakfast, and the very milk that’s took in, at the street door?", "래들 부인이 이웃들 들으라고 목청을 높이며 말했다. \"내가 날이면 날마다, 집세 낼 생각은커녕 아침 식사로 산 신선한 버터와 각설탕 값, 현관에서 받아 들이는 우유 값조차 낼 생각이 없는 작자한테 내 셋방을 내주고 있을 거라고 생각해요?"], ["Do you suppose a hard-working and industrious woman as has lived in this street for twenty year (ten year over the way, and nine year and three-quarters in this very house) has nothing else to do but to work herself to death after a parcel of lazy idle fellars, that are always smoking and drinking, and lounging, when they ought to be glad to turn their hands to anything that would help ‘em to pay their bills?", "이 거리에서 이십 년을(건너편에서 십 년, 바로 이 집에서 구 년 하고 사분의 삼 년) 살아온, 부지런하고 열심히 일하는 여자가, 청구서 갚는 데 보탬이 될 일이라면 무슨 일이든 기꺼이 손을 대야 마땅할 마당에 늘 담배나 피우고 술이나 마시고 빈둥거리기만 하는 게으르고 나태한 작자들 뒤치다꺼리하느라 죽도록 일하는 것 말고는 달리 할 일이 없을 거라고 생각해요?"], ["Do you--’", "당신 생각엔—\""]], [["‘My good soul,’ interposed Mr.", "\"이보세요, 부인.\""], ["Benjamin Allen soothingly.", "벤저민 앨런 씨가 달래듯 끼어들었다."]], [["‘Have the goodness to keep your observashuns to yourself, Sir, I beg,’ said Mrs.", "\"선생, 제발 부탁이니 그 소견은 혼자 간직하시지요.\""], ["Raddle, suddenly arresting the rapid torrent of her speech, and addressing the third party with impressive slowness and solemnity.", "래들 부인이 거침없이 쏟아 내던 말의 물줄기를 별안간 멈추고는, 위압적인 느릿함과 엄숙함으로 제삼자를 향해 말했다."], ["‘I am not aweer, Sir, that you have any right to address your conversation to me.", "\"선생, 나는 당신이 내게 말을 걸 권리가 있는 줄은 미처 몰랐는데요."], ["I don’t think I let these apartments to you, Sir.’", "내가 이 방을 당신한테 세준 것 같지는 않은데, 선생.\""]], [["‘No, you certainly did not,’ said Mr.", "\"네, 분명 그러지 않으셨지요.\""], ["Benjamin Allen.", "벤저민 앨런 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Very good, Sir,’ responded Mrs.", "\"아주 좋아요, 선생.\""], ["Raddle, with lofty politeness.", "래들 부인이 도도하고 정중하게 응수했다."], ["‘Then p’raps, Sir, you’ll confine yourself to breaking the arms and legs of the poor people in the hospitals, and keep yourself to yourself, Sir, or there may be some persons here as will make you, Sir.’", "\"그렇다면 선생, 당신은 병원에 있는 가엾은 사람들 팔다리나 부러뜨리는 일에나 매달리고, 자기 일은 자기나 챙기시지요, 선생. 안 그러면 여기엔 당신을 그렇게 만들어 줄 사람들이 있을지도 모르니까요, 선생.\""]], [["‘But you are such an unreasonable woman,’ remonstrated Mr.", "\"하지만 부인은 너무도 막무가내시군요.\""], ["Benjamin Allen.", "벤저민 앨런 씨가 항변했다."]], [["‘I beg your parding, young man,’ said Mrs.", "\"실례지만, 젊은이.\""], ["Raddle, in a cold perspiration of anger.", "래들 부인이 분노로 식은땀을 흘리며 말했다."], ["‘But will you have the goodness just to call me that again, sir?’", "\"방금 그 말, 한 번만 더 친절히 해 주시겠어요, 선생?\""]], [["‘I didn’t make use of the word in any invidious sense, ma’am,’ replied Mr.", "\"무슨 악의가 있어서 그 말을 쓴 건 아닙니다, 부인.\""], ["Benjamin Allen, growing somewhat uneasy on his own account.", "벤저민 앨런 씨가 자기 처지도 좀 거북해지면서 대답했다."]], [["‘I beg your parding, young man,’ demanded Mrs.", "\"실례지만, 젊은이.\""], ["Raddle, in a louder and more imperative tone.", "래들 부인이 한층 크고 명령조인 어조로 다그쳤다."], ["‘But who do you call a woman?", "\"방금 누구더러 막무가내라는 거죠?"], ["Did you make that remark to me, sir?’", "그 말을 나한테 한 거예요, 선생?\""]], [["‘Why, bless my heart!’ said Mr.", "\"원, 세상에!\""], ["Benjamin Allen.", "벤저민 앨런 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Did you apply that name to me, I ask of you, sir?’ interrupted Mrs.", "\"그 호칭을 나한테 갖다 붙인 거냐고 묻잖아요, 선생?\""], ["Raddle, with intense fierceness, throwing the door wide open.", "래들 부인이 사납기 그지없는 기세로 가로막으며 문을 활짝 열어젖혔다."]], [["‘Why, of course I did,’ replied Mr.", "\"그야, 물론 그랬습니다.\""], ["Benjamin Allen.", "벤저민 앨런 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘Yes, of course you did,’ said Mrs.", "\"그래요, 물론 그랬겠지요.\""], ["Raddle, backing gradually to the door, and raising her voice to its loudest pitch, for the special behoof of Mr. Raddle in the kitchen.", "래들 부인이 슬금슬금 문 쪽으로 물러서며, 부엌에 있는 래들 씨더러 특별히 들으라고 목청을 한껏 높여 말했다."], ["‘Yes, of course you did!", "\"그래요, 물론 그랬겠지!"], ["And everybody knows that they may safely insult me in my own ‘ouse while my husband sits sleeping downstairs, and taking no more notice than if I was a dog in the streets.", "누구나 알다시피, 내 남편이 아래층에서 자빠져 자면서 내가 길거리의 개라도 되는 양 거들떠보지도 않는 동안에는, 다들 내 집에서 마음 놓고 나를 모욕해도 되는 거지. 저 사람은 부끄러운 줄 알아야 해요(여기서 래들 부인이 흐느꼈다)."], ["He ought to be ashamed of himself (here Mrs.", "셋방 망신이나 시키는, 살아 있는 사람 몸뚱이나 째고 자르는 풋내기 패거리한테 제 아내가 이런 꼴을 당하도록 내버려 두다니(또 한 번 흐느꼈다), 온갖 행패에 고스란히 노출되도록 내버려 두다니."], ["Raddle sobbed) to allow his wife to be treated in this way by a parcel of young cutters and carvers of live people’s bodies, that disgraces the lodgings (another sob), and leaving her exposed to all manner of abuse; a base, faint-hearted, timorous wretch, that’s afraid to come upstairs, and face the ruffinly creatures--that’s afraid--that’s afraid to come!’ Mrs.", "천하고, 비겁하고, 겁 많은 작자, 위층에 올라와서 저 불한당 같은 것들 앞에 나설 엄두도 못 내는—엄두도—엄두도 못 내는!\" 래들 부인은 이 빈정거림을 되풀이한 것이 그 반쪽을 분기시켰는지 어떤지 귀 기울여 들으려 잠깐 멈추었다가, 효험이 없자 헤아릴 수 없이 흐느끼며 층계를 내려가기 시작했다."], ["Raddle paused to listen whether the repetition of the taunt had roused her better half; and finding that it had not been successful, proceeded to descend the stairs with sobs innumerable; when there came a loud double knock at the street door; whereupon she burst into an hysterical fit of weeping, accompanied with dismal moans, which was prolonged until the knock had been repeated six times, when, in an uncontrollable burst of mental agony, she threw down all the umbrellas, and disappeared into the back parlour, closing the door after her with an awful crash.", "그때 현관문에서 쾅쾅, 큰 노크 소리가 두 번 났다. 그러자 부인은 처량한 신음과 함께 발작적인 통곡을 터뜨렸고, 그것은 노크가 여섯 번 되풀이될 때까지 이어지다가, 마침내 걷잡을 수 없는 정신적 고뇌가 폭발한 끝에 우산을 죄다 내동댕이치고 뒷거실로 사라지면서 무시무시한 굉음과 함께 문을 쾅 닫았다."]], [["‘Does Mr. Sawyer live here?’ said Mr.", "\"여기가 소여 씨 사는 집이오?\""], ["Pickwick, when the door was opened.", "문이 열리자 픽윅 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘Yes,’ said the girl, ‘first floor. It’s the door straight afore you, when you gets to the top of the stairs.’ Having given this instruction, the handmaid, who had been brought up among the aboriginal inhabitants of Southwark, disappeared, with the candle in her hand, down the kitchen stairs, perfectly satisfied that she had done everything that could possibly be required of her under the circumstances.", "\"네.\" 처녀가 말했다. \"이층이에요. 층계 꼭대기에 올라가면 바로 앞에 보이는 문이에요.\" 이렇게 일러 준 뒤, 서더크의 토박이들 틈에서 자란 그 하녀는 양초를 손에 든 채 부엌 층계 아래로 사라졌다. 이런 상황에서 자기에게 요구될 만한 일은 모두 다 했다는 더없는 만족감에 젖어서였다."]], [["Mr. Snodgrass, who entered last, secured the street door, after several ineffectual efforts, by putting up the chain; and the friends stumbled upstairs, where they were received by Mr.", "맨 나중에 들어온 스노드그래스 씨는 몇 번 헛수고를 한 끝에 사슬을 걸어 현관문을 잠그는 데 성공했다."], ["Bob Sawyer, who had been afraid to go down, lest he should be waylaid by Mrs.", "그러고 나서 친구들은 비틀거리며 층계를 올라갔는데, 거기서 밥 소여 씨의 영접을 받았다."], ["Raddle.", "그는 래들 부인에게 길목에서 붙잡힐까 봐 아래층으로 내려갈 엄두를 못 냈던 것이다."]], [["‘How are you?’ said the discomfited student.", "\"안녕들 하십니까?\""], ["‘Glad to see you--take care of the glasses.’ This caution was addressed to Mr.", "풀이 죽은 의학도가 말했다. \"만나서 반갑소—잔들 조심하시고요.\""], ["Pickwick, who had put his hat in the tray.", "이 주의는 모자를 쟁반에 올려놓은 픽윅 씨를 향한 것이었다."]], [["‘Dear me,’ said Mr.", "\"이런.\""], ["Pickwick, ‘I beg your pardon.’", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"미안하오.\""]], [["‘Don’t mention it, don’t mention it,’ said Bob Sawyer.", "\"별말씀을, 별말씀을요.\" 밥 소여가 말했다."], ["‘I’m rather confined for room here, but you must put up with all that, when you come to see a young bachelor.", "\"여기 방이 좀 비좁긴 해도, 미혼 청년을 보러 오신 거니 그 정도는 참아 주셔야지요. 어서 들어오십시오."], ["Walk in. You’ve seen this gentleman before, I think?’ Mr.", "이 신사분과는 전에 뵌 적이 있으시죠?\""], ["Pickwick shook hands with Mr. Benjamin Allen, and his friends followed his example.", "픽윅 씨는 벤저민 앨런 씨와 악수했고, 친구들도 그를 따라 했다."], ["They had scarcely taken their seats when there was another double knock.", "그들이 자리에 채 앉기도 전에 또 한 번 쾅쾅, 노크 소리가 두 번 났다."]], [["‘I hope that’s Jack Hopkins!’ said Mr.", "\"잭 홉킨스이길 바라는데!\""], ["Bob Sawyer.", "밥 소여 씨가 말했다."], ["‘Hush.", "\"쉿."], ["Yes, it is.", "그래, 맞아."], ["Come up, Jack; come up.’", "올라와, 잭. 올라오라고.\""]], [["A heavy footstep was heard upon the stairs, and Jack Hopkins presented himself.", "층계에서 묵직한 발소리가 들리더니 잭 홉킨스가 모습을 드러냈다."], ["He wore a black velvet waistcoat, with thunder-and-lightning buttons; and a blue striped shirt, with a white false collar.", "그는 번개무늬 단추가 달린 검은 벨벳 조끼에, 흰 가짜 깃을 단 푸른 줄무늬 셔츠를 입고 있었다."]], [["‘You’re late, Jack?’ said Mr.", "\"늦었군, 잭?\""], ["Benjamin Allen.", "벤저민 앨런 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Been detained at Bartholomew’s,’ replied Hopkins.", "\"바살러뮤 병원에 붙들려 있었어.\" 홉킨스가 대답했다."]], [["‘Anything new?’", "\"뭐 새로운 거라도?\""]], [["‘No, nothing particular.", "\"아니, 별다른 건 없고."], ["Rather a good accident brought into the casualty ward.’", "응급 병동에 꽤 괜찮은 사고가 하나 실려 왔지.\""]], [["‘What was that, sir?’ inquired Mr.", "\"그게 뭐였습니까, 선생?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘Only a man fallen out of a four pair of stairs’ window; but it’s a very fair case indeed.’", "\"그저 사 층 창문에서 떨어진 사내일 뿐인데, 정말이지 아주 좋은 사례예요.\""]], [["‘Do you mean that the patient is in a fair way to recover?’ inquired Mr.", "\"환자가 회복될 가망이 좋다는 말씀입니까?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘No,’ replied Mr.", "\"아니요.\""], ["Hopkins carelessly.", "홉킨스 씨가 무심하게 대답했다."], ["‘No, I should rather say he wouldn’t.", "\"아니요, 오히려 가망이 없다고 해야겠지요."], ["There must be a splendid operation, though, to-morrow-- magnificent sight if Slasher does it.’", "그래도 내일 굉장한 수술이 있을 거예요—슬래셔가 집도하면 장관이거든요.\""]], [["‘You consider Mr. Slasher a good operator?’ said Mr.", "\"슬래셔 씨를 솜씨 좋은 집도의로 보시나 보군요?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Best alive,’ replied Hopkins.", "\"살아 있는 사람 중에 으뜸이죠.\""], ["‘Took a boy’s leg out of the socket last week--boy ate five apples and a gingerbread cake--exactly two minutes after it was all over, boy said he wouldn’t lie there to be made game of, and he’d tell his mother if they didn’t begin.’", "홉킨스가 대답했다. \"지난주에는 어떤 소년의 다리를 관절에서 떼어 냈는데—그 애가 사과를 다섯 개에 생강 케이크를 하나 먹고는—수술이 다 끝나고 딱 이 분 뒤에, 자기를 놀림감으로 만들려고 여기 누워 있는 게 아니라며, 당장 시작하지 않으면 엄마한테 이르겠다고 했다니까요.\""]], [["‘Dear me!’ said Mr.", "\"이런!\""], ["Pickwick, astonished.", "픽윅 씨가 놀라며 말했다."]], [["‘Pooh!", "\"흥!"], ["That’s nothing, that ain’t,’ said Jack Hopkins.", "그 정도야 아무것도 아니죠.\" 잭 홉킨스가 말했다."], ["‘Is it, Bob?’", "\"안 그래, 밥?\""]], [["‘Nothing at all,’ replied Mr.", "\"아무것도 아니지.\""], ["Bob Sawyer.", "밥 소여 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘By the bye, Bob,’ said Hopkins, with a scarcely perceptible glance at Mr.", "\"그러고 보니, 밥,\" 홉킨스가 픽윅 씨의 주의 깊은 얼굴을 거의 알아챌 수 없을 만큼 슬쩍 흘끔거리며 말했다."], ["Pickwick’s attentive face, ‘we had a curious accident last night.", "\"어젯밤에 묘한 사고가 하나 있었어."], ["A child was brought in, who had swallowed a necklace.’", "목걸이를 삼킨 아이가 하나 실려 왔지.\""]], [["‘Swallowed what, Sir?’ interrupted Mr.", "\"뭘 삼켰다고요, 선생?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 가로막았다."]], [["‘A necklace,’ replied Jack Hopkins.", "\"목걸이요.\" 잭 홉킨스가 대답했다."], ["‘Not all at once, you know, that would be too much--you couldn’t swallow that, if the child did--eh, Mr.", "\"한꺼번에는 아니고요, 아시잖습니까. 그건 무리지요—그 애가 삼켰다 해도 선생님은 못 삼키실 겁니다—안 그래요, 픽윅 씨?"], ["Pickwick? ha, ha!’ Mr.", "하하!\""], ["Hopkins appeared highly gratified with his own pleasantry, and continued--‘No, the way was this.", "홉킨스 씨는 제 농담에 무척 흡족해하며 말을 이었다. \"아니요, 사정은 이렇습니다."], ["Child’s parents were poor people who lived in a court.", "그 애 부모는 어느 골목에 사는 가난한 사람들이었어요."], ["Child’s eldest sister bought a necklace--common necklace, made of large black wooden beads.", "그 애의 맏누이가 목걸이를 하나 샀는데—큼지막한 검은 나무 구슬로 만든 흔한 목걸이였죠."], ["Child being fond of toys, cribbed the necklace, hid it, played with it, cut the string, and swallowed a bead.", "그 애가 장난감을 좋아해서 그 목걸이를 슬쩍 훔쳐다 감추고 가지고 놀다가, 끈을 끊고 구슬을 하나 삼켜 버렸지요."], ["Child thought it capital fun, went back next day, and swallowed another bead.’", "그 애는 그게 기막힌 놀이라고 여기고는, 이튿날 또 가서 구슬을 하나 더 삼켰어요.\""]], [["‘Bless my heart,’ said Mr.", "\"세상에.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick, ‘what a dreadful thing!", "\"그 무슨 끔찍한 일이오!"], ["I beg your pardon, Sir.", "미안하오, 선생."], ["Go on.’", "계속하시오.\""]], [["‘Next day, child swallowed two beads; the day after that, he treated himself to three, and so on, till in a week’s time he had got through the necklace--five-and-twenty beads in all.", "\"그다음 날엔 구슬을 두 개 삼켰고, 그 이튿날엔 자기한테 세 개를 대접했고, 그렇게 계속하다가 일주일 만에 목걸이 하나를 다 해치웠어요—구슬이 죄다 합쳐서 스물다섯 개였지요."], ["The sister, who was an industrious girl, and seldom treated herself to a bit of finery, cried her eyes out, at the loss of the necklace; looked high and low for it; but, I needn’t say, didn’t find it.", "그 누이는 부지런한 처녀라 변변한 장신구 하나 사 본 적이 거의 없었던 터라, 목걸이를 잃고는 눈이 빠지도록 울었어요. 위아래로 샅샅이 찾아봤지만, 말할 것도 없이 못 찾았지요."], ["A few days afterwards, the family were at dinner--baked shoulder of mutton, and potatoes under it--the child, who wasn’t hungry, was playing about the room, when suddenly there was heard a devil of a noise, like a small hailstorm.", "며칠 뒤에 식구들이 저녁을 먹는데—양 어깨살 구이에, 그 밑에 감자를 깔았더랬죠—배가 안 고팠던 그 애가 방을 이리저리 돌아다니고 있는데, 별안간 작은 우박 폭풍 같은 요란한 소리가 들리는 거예요."], ["“Don’t do that, my boy,”", "'그러지 마라, 얘야.'"], ["said the father.", "아버지가 말했죠."], ["“I ain’t a-doin’ nothing,”", "'난 아무것도 안 했는데요.'"], ["said the child.", "아이가 말했어요."], ["“Well, don’t do it again,”", "'그래, 다시는 그러지 마라.'"], ["said the father.", "아버지가 말했죠."], ["There was a short silence, and then the noise began again, worse than ever.", "잠깐 조용하더니, 그러고는 그 소리가 전보다 더 심하게 또 나기 시작했어요. '내가 한 말 안 들으면, 얘야,'"], ["“If you don’t mind what I say, my boy,”", "아버지가 말했죠. '돼지 멱따는 소리 한 번 낼 새도 없이 침대에 처박힐 줄 알아라.'"], ["said the father, “you’ll find yourself in bed, in something less than a pig’s whisper.”", "아버지가 말을 잘 듣게 하려고 아이를 흔들어 댔더니, 일찍이 아무도 들어 본 적 없는 덜그럭 소리가 나는 거예요. '아니, 빌어먹을, 이거 아이 뱃속에 들었잖아!'"], ["He gave the child a shake to make him obedient, and such a rattling ensued as nobody ever heard before.", "아버지가 말했죠. '이놈이 엉뚱한 데에 후두염*이 걸렸구나!'"], ["“Why, damme, it’s in the child!”", "'아니에요, 아버지.' 아이가 울먹이며 말했어요."], ["said the father, “he’s got the croup in the wrong place!”", "'그건 목걸이예요. 제가 삼켰어요, 아버지.'—아버지는 아이를 번쩍 안아 들고 병원으로 달려갔는데, 가는 내내 덜컹거릴 때마다 그 애 뱃속 구슬들이 달가닥거렸고, 사람들은 그 유별난 소리가 어디서 나는지 보려고 하늘을 올려다보고 지하실을 내려다보고 했지요."], ["“No, I haven’t, father,”", "그 애는 지금 병원에 있어요.\""], ["said the child, beginning to cry, “it’s the necklace; I swallowed it, father.”--The father caught the child up, and ran with him to the hospital; the beads in the boy’s stomach rattling all the way with the jolting; and the people looking up in the air, and down in the cellars, to see where the unusual sound came from.", "잭 홉킨스가 말했다. \"그런데 돌아다닐 때마다 어찌나 빌어먹게 요란한 소리를 내는지, 환자들이 깰까 봐 야경꾼 외투로 그 애를 둘둘 감싸 두어야 할 지경이랍니다.\""], ["He’s in the hospital now,’ said Jack Hopkins, ‘and he makes such a devil of a noise when he walks about, that they’re obliged to muffle him in a watchman’s coat, for fear he should wake the patients.’", "* 후두염: 원문에서 아버지는 '뱃속에서 나는 덜그럭 소리'를 후두에 생긴 병으로 착각한다."]], [["‘That’s the most extraordinary case I ever heard of,’ said Mr.", "\"내 평생 들어 본 것 중에 가장 비상한 사례로구려.\""], ["Pickwick, with an emphatic blow on the table.", "픽윅 씨가 탁자를 힘주어 한 번 내리치며 말했다."]], [["‘Oh, that’s nothing,’ said Jack Hopkins.", "\"오, 그 정도야 아무것도 아니죠.\" 잭 홉킨스가 말했다."], ["‘Is it, Bob?’", "\"안 그래, 밥?\""]], [["‘Certainly not,’ replied Bob Sawyer.", "\"물론 아니지.\" 밥 소여가 대답했다."]], [["‘Very singular things occur in our profession, I can assure you, Sir,’ said Hopkins.", "\"우리 직업에는 정말 별난 일들이 일어난답니다, 선생, 장담하지요.\" 홉킨스가 말했다."]], [["‘So I should be disposed to imagine,’ replied Mr.", "\"그럴 거라고 짐작이 가는군요.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 대답했다."]], [["Another knock at the door announced a large-headed young man in a black wig, who brought with him a scorbutic youth in a long stock.", "문에서 또 한 번 노크가 나더니, 검은 가발을 쓴 머리 큰 청년 하나가 들어왔는데, 긴 목도리를 두른 괴혈병 기색의 젊은이를 데리고 왔다. 그다음에 온 사람은 분홍 닻 무늬로 장식한 셔츠를 입은 신사였고, 그 뒤를 도금한 시곗줄을 단 창백한 젊은이가 바짝 따라왔다."], ["The next comer was a gentleman in a shirt emblazoned with pink anchors, who was closely followed by a pale youth with a plated watchguard.", "깨끗한 아마포 옷에 천 장화를 신은 새침한 인물이 도착하자 모임이 완성되었다. 초록색 베이즈를 씌운 작은 탁자를 끌어내고, 펀치 첫 분량을 흰 주전자에 담아 들여왔다."], ["The arrival of a prim personage in clean linen and cloth boots rendered the party complete.", "그러고 나서 이어진 세 시간은 한 다스에 육 펜스를 거는 뱅테욍\\에 바쳐졌는데, 도중에 단 한 번, 괴혈병 기색의 젊은이와 분홍 닻 무늬 신사 사이에 사소한 다툼이 있어 중단되었다. 그 와중에 괴혈병 기색의 젊은이는 희망의 표상을 단 신사의 코를 비틀어 주고 싶다는 불타는 욕망을 내비쳤고, 이에 대해 그 인물은, 괴혈병 기색의 성마른 젊은 신사한테서든 머리 달린 다른 어떤 사람한테서든, 거저 주는 '건방진 소리'\\*는 결단코 받아들일 뜻이 없음을 표명했다."], ["The little table with the green baize cover was wheeled out; the first instalment of punch was brought in, in a white jug; and the succeeding three hours were devoted to Vingt-et-un at sixpence a dozen, which was only once interrupted by a slight dispute between the scorbutic youth and the gentleman with the pink anchors; in the course of which, the scorbutic youth intimated a burning desire to pull the nose of the gentleman with the emblems of hope; in reply to which, that individual expressed his decided unwillingness to accept of any ‘sauce’ on gratuitous terms, either from the irascible young gentleman with the scorbutic countenance, or any other person who was ornamented with a head.", "* 뱅테욍: 카드놀이의 일종(블랙잭). \\\\ '건방진 소리': 'sauce'에는 '소스'와 '건방진 말대꾸'라는 두 뜻이 있다."]], [["When the last ‘natural’ had been declared, and the profit and loss account of fish and sixpences adjusted, to the satisfaction of all parties, Mr.", "마지막 '내추럴'*이 선언되고, 생선 패와 육 펜스의 손익 계산이 모두의 만족스럽게 정산되자, 밥 소여 씨는 종을 울려 야식을 청했고, 손님들은 그것이 준비되는 동안 구석으로 비집고 들어갔다."], ["Bob Sawyer rang for supper, and the visitors squeezed themselves into corners while it was getting ready.", "* 내추럴: 카드놀이에서 처음 두 장으로 21을 만드는 패."]], [["It was not so easily got ready as some people may imagine.", "야식은 더러 생각하는 것만큼 쉽게 준비되지는 않았다."], ["First of all, it was necessary to awaken the girl, who had fallen asleep with her face on the kitchen table; this took a little time, and, even when she did answer the bell, another quarter of an hour was consumed in fruitless endeavours to impart to her a faint and distant glimmering of reason.", "우선, 부엌 탁자에 얼굴을 묻고 잠들어 버린 처녀를 깨워야 했다. 여기에 시간이 좀 걸렸고, 마침내 종소리에 응답한 뒤에도 그 애에게 어렴풋하고 아득한 분별의 빛 한 가닥이나마 일깨우려는 헛된 노력에 또 십오 분이 허비되었다."], ["The man to whom the order for the oysters had been sent, had not been told to open them; it is a very difficult thing to open an oyster with a limp knife and a two-pronged fork; and very little was done in this way.", "굴을 가져오라고 시킨 사람에게는 그것을 까 두라는 말을 미처 하지 않은 터였다. 흐물거리는 칼과 두 갈래 포크로 굴을 까는 일이란 여간 어려운 게 아니어서, 이쪽 일은 거의 진척이 없었다."], ["Very little of the beef was done either; and the ham (which was also from the German-sausage shop round the corner) was in a similar predicament.", "쇠고기도 거의 익지 않았고, 햄(이것도 모퉁이의 독일식 소시지 가게에서 사 온 것이었다) 역시 비슷한 처지였다."], ["However, there was plenty of porter in a tin can; and the cheese went a great way, for it was very strong.", "그래도 양철통에 든 흑맥주는 넉넉했고, 치즈는 워낙 독해서 멀리까지 갔다."], ["So upon the whole, perhaps, the supper was quite as good as such matters usually are.", "그러니 전체적으로 보면, 야식은 그런 자리에서 흔히 보는 정도로는 그럭저럭 괜찮았다고 할 만했다."]], [["After supper, another jug of punch was put upon the table, together with a paper of cigars, and a couple of bottles of spirits.", "야식이 끝나고, 펀치를 담은 주전자가 하나 더, 시가 한 봉지, 그리고 독한 술 두 병과 함께 탁자에 올랐다."], ["Then there was an awful pause; and this awful pause was occasioned by a very common occurrence in this sort of place, but a very embarrassing one notwithstanding.", "그러고 나서 무시무시한 침묵이 찾아왔는데, 이 무시무시한 침묵은 이런 곳에서 흔히 있는 일이 빚어낸 것이었으나, 그럼에도 무척 난처한 것이었다."]], [["The fact is, the girl was washing the glasses.", "실은, 처녀가 술잔들을 씻고 있었다."], ["The establishment boasted four: we do not record the circumstance as at all derogatory to Mrs.", "이 집이 자랑하는 잔은 넉 개였다. 우리는 이 사정을 결코 래들 부인의 흠으로 적는 것이 아니다."], ["Raddle, for there never was a lodging-house yet, that was not short of glasses.", "잔이 모자라지 않은 셋방집이란 일찍이 없었으니까."], ["The landlady’s glasses were little, thin, blown-glass tumblers, and those which had been borrowed from the public-house were great, dropsical, bloated articles, each supported on a huge gouty leg.", "안주인의 잔은 작고 얇은, 입으로 불어 만든 텀블러였고, 술집에서 빌려 온 잔은 크고 부석부석하게 부푼 물건으로, 저마다 통풍에 걸린 듯 굵직한 다리 하나로 받쳐져 있었다. 이것만으로도 모임의 사람들에게 사정의 진상을 알아채게 하기에 충분했을 테지만, 만능 잡역부 처녀는 어느 신사든 이 문제로 행여 오해를 품지 못하도록 미연에 막아 버렸다."], ["This would have been in itself sufficient to have possessed the company with the real state of affairs; but the young woman of all work had prevented the possibility of any misconception arising in the mind of any gentleman upon the subject, by forcibly dragging every man’s glass away, long before he had finished his beer, and audibly stating, despite the winks and interruptions of Mr. Bob Sawyer, that it was to be conveyed downstairs, and washed forthwith.", "그가 맥주를 다 마시기도 훨씬 전에 사내들 각자의 잔을 억지로 끌어가면서, 밥 소여 씨가 눈짓을 하고 말을 가로막는데도 아랑곳없이, 잔을 아래층으로 가져가 당장 씻어야 한다고 또렷이 말해 댔던 것이다."]], [["It is a very ill wind that blows nobody any good.", "누구에게도 득이 되지 않는 바람은 정말 고약한 바람이다.* 천 장화를 신은 새침한 사내는 그 돌림판\\\\ 놀이가 이어지는 내내 농담 한마디 던져 보려다 번번이 실패했는데, 이제 기회를 잡고는 그것을 놓치지 않았다."], ["The prim man in the cloth boots, who had been unsuccessfully attempting to make a joke during the whole time the round game lasted, saw his opportunity, and availed himself of it.", "잔들이 사라지는 순간, 그는 이름이 기억나지 않는 어느 위대한 공인이, 자기로서는 끝내 누구인지 알아낼 수 없었던 또 다른 저명하고 이름난 인물에게 유달리 멋들어진 응수를 한 이야기를 길게 풀어 놓기 시작했다."], ["The instant the glasses disappeared, he commenced a long story about a great public character, whose name he had forgotten, making a particularly happy reply to another eminent and illustrious individual whom he had never been able to identify.", "그는 지금 다루는 일화와 멀찍이 얽힌 여러 부수적 정황을 꽤 길게, 무척 세세하게 늘어놓았으나, 죽었다 깨도 그 순간 정작 그 일화가 무엇이었는지는 도무지 기억해 낼 수 없었다. 지난 십 년간 그 이야기를 큰 갈채를 받으며 들려주는 것이 버릇이었는데도 말이다."], ["He enlarged at some length and with great minuteness upon divers collateral circumstances, distantly connected with the anecdote in hand, but for the life of him he couldn’t recollect at that precise moment what the anecdote was, although he had been in the habit of telling the story with great applause for the last ten years.", "* 누구에게도 득이 되지 않는 바람: \"어느 바람이든 누군가에게는 득이 된다\"는 영어 속담을 뒤집은 표현. \\\\ 돌림판 놀이: 여럿이 돌아가며 하는 카드놀이."]], [["‘Dear me,’ said the prim man in the cloth boots, ‘it is a very extraordinary circumstance.’", "\"이런.\" 천 장화를 신은 새침한 사내가 말했다. \"참으로 비상한 일이로군요.\""]], [["‘I am sorry you have forgotten it,’ said Mr.", "\"잊으셨다니 안타깝습니다.\""], ["Bob Sawyer, glancing eagerly at the door, as he thought he heard the noise of glasses jingling; ‘very sorry.’", "밥 소여 씨가, 잔들이 짤랑거리는 소리를 들은 듯해서 문 쪽을 안달하며 흘끔거리면서 말했다. \"참 안타깝네요.\""]], [["‘So am I,’ responded the prim man, ‘because I know it would have afforded so much amusement. Never mind; I dare say I shall manage to recollect it, in the course of half an hour or so.’", "\"저도 그렇습니다.\" 새침한 사내가 응수했다. \"정말 큰 즐거움을 드렸을 이야기라는 걸 아니까요. 괜찮습니다. 한 삼십 분쯤 지나면 어떻게든 기억해 낼 수 있을 듯하군요.\""]], [["The prim man arrived at this point just as the glasses came back, when Mr. Bob Sawyer, who had been absorbed in attention during the whole time, said he should very much like to hear the end of it, for, so far as it went, it was, without exception, the very best story he had ever heard.", "새침한 사내가 이 대목에 이르렀을 때 마침 잔들이 돌아왔는데, 그 내내 정신을 빼앗긴 채 귀 기울이던 밥 소여 씨는, 자기가 들은 한에서 그것이 단연코 여태 들어 본 가장 훌륭한 이야기였으니, 그 결말을 꼭 듣고 싶다고 말했다."]], [["The sight of the tumblers restored Bob Sawyer to a degree of equanimity which he had not possessed since his interview with his landlady.", "텀블러들이 눈에 들어오자 밥 소여는 안주인과의 면담 이래 줄곧 잃었던 평정을 어느 정도 되찾았다."], ["His face brightened up, and he began to feel quite convivial.", "그의 얼굴이 환해지고, 그는 자못 흥겨워지기 시작했다."]], [["‘Now, Betsy,’ said Mr.", "\"자, 베치.\" 밥 소여 씨가 더없이 상냥하게 말하면서, 동시에 처녀가 탁자 한가운데에 그러모아 놓은 잔들의 어수선한 작은 무리를 흩어 놓았다."], ["Bob Sawyer, with great suavity, and dispersing, at the same time, the tumultuous little mob of glasses the girl had collected in the centre of the table--‘now, Betsy, the warm water; be brisk, there’s a good girl.’", "\"자, 베치, 따뜻한 물 좀. 빨리, 착하지.\""]], [["‘You can’t have no warm water,’ replied Betsy.", "\"따뜻한 물은 없는데요.\" 베치가 대답했다."]], [["‘No warm water!’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"따뜻한 물이 없다고!\""], ["Bob Sawyer.", "밥 소여 씨가 소리쳤다."]], [["‘No,’ said the girl, with a shake of the head which expressed a more decided negative than the most copious language could have conveyed.", "\"네.\" 처녀가 고개를 절레절레 저으며 말했는데, 그 몸짓은 아무리 풍부한 말로도 전할 수 없을 만큼 단호한 부정을 나타냈다."], ["‘Missis Raddle said you warn’t to have none.’", "\"래들 마님이 선생님한테 물은 한 방울도 안 된다고 하셨어요.\""]], [["The surprise depicted on the countenances of his guests imparted new courage to the host.", "손님들의 얼굴에 떠오른 놀란 기색이 주인에게 새로운 용기를 불어넣었다."]], [["‘Bring up the warm water instantly--instantly!’ said Mr.", "\"당장 따뜻한 물을 가져와—당장!\""], ["Bob Sawyer, with desperate sternness.", "밥 소여 씨가 필사적으로 엄하게 말했다."]], [["‘No.", "\"아뇨. 못 해요.\""], ["I can’t,’ replied the girl; ‘Missis Raddle raked out the kitchen fire afore she went to bed, and locked up the kittle.’", "처녀가 대답했다. \"래들 마님이 주무시기 전에 부엌 불을 다 긁어내 버리시고 주전자도 잠가 두셨거든요.\""]], [["‘Oh, never mind; never mind.", "\"오, 괜찮네, 괜찮아."], ["Pray don’t disturb yourself about such a trifle,’ said Mr.", "그런 사소한 일로 마음 쓰지 마시오.\""], ["Pickwick, observing the conflict of Bob Sawyer’s passions, as depicted in his countenance, ‘cold water will do very well.’", "밥 소여의 격정이 얼굴에 드러나며 부딪치는 것을 본 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"찬물이면 충분하오.\""]], [["‘Oh, admirably,’ said Mr.", "\"오, 더할 나위 없지요.\""], ["Benjamin Allen.", "벤저민 앨런 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘My landlady is subject to some slight attacks of mental derangement,’ remarked Bob Sawyer, with a ghastly smile; ‘I fear I must give her warning.’", "\"우리 안주인이 가벼운 정신 착란 발작을 좀 일으키곤 해서요.\" 밥 소여가 섬뜩한 미소를 띠며 말했다. \"아무래도 통고를 해야겠습니다.\""]], [["‘No, don’t,’ said Ben Allen.", "\"아니, 그러지 마.\" 벤 앨런이 말했다."]], [["‘I fear I must,’ said Bob, with heroic firmness.", "\"아무래도 그래야겠어.\" 밥이 영웅적인 단호함으로 말했다."], ["‘I’ll pay her what I owe her, and give her warning to-morrow morning.’ Poor fellow!", "\"빚진 걸 갚고, 내일 아침에 나가라고 통고할 거야.\" 가엾은 친구!"], ["how devoutly he wished he could!", "그게 정말 가능하기를 그가 얼마나 간절히 바랐던가!"]], [["Mr.", "이 마지막 타격을 받고도 기운을 차려 보려는 밥 소여 씨의 마음 시린 안간힘은 좌중에 의기소침한 기운을 옮겨 놓았다."], ["Bob Sawyer’s heart-sickening attempts to rally under this last blow, communicated a dispiriting influence to the company, the greater part of whom, with the view of raising their spirits, attached themselves with extra cordiality to the cold brandy-and-water, the first perceptible effects of which were displayed in a renewal of hostilities between the scorbutic youth and the gentleman in the shirt.", "그 대다수는 흥을 돋워 보겠다고 찬 브랜디 물에 한층 정답게 매달렸는데, 그 첫 번째 알아챌 만한 효과는 괴혈병 기색의 젊은이와 셔츠 입은 신사 사이에 적대 행위가 재개된 것으로 나타났다."], ["The belligerents vented their feelings of mutual contempt, for some time, in a variety of frownings and snortings, until at last the scorbutic youth felt it necessary to come to a more explicit understanding on the matter; when the following clear understanding took place.", "두 교전자는 한동안 갖가지 찡그림과 콧방귀로 서로에 대한 경멸의 감정을 토해 내다가, 마침내 괴혈병 기색의 젊은이가 이 문제를 좀 더 명확히 매듭지을 필요를 느꼈다. 그리하여 다음과 같은 명료한 합의가 이루어졌다."]], [["‘Sawyer,’ said the scorbutic youth, in a loud voice.", "\"소여.\" 괴혈병 기색의 젊은이가 큰 소리로 말했다."]], [["‘Well, Noddy,’ replied Mr.", "\"왜 그래, 노디.\""], ["Bob Sawyer.", "밥 소여 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘I should be very sorry, Sawyer,’ said Mr. Noddy, ‘to create any unpleasantness at any friend’s table, and much less at yours, Sawyer-- very; but I must take this opportunity of informing Mr.", "\"소여, 나는 어느 친구의 식탁에서든 불쾌한 일을 일으키는 건 정말 유감스럽게 여기고, 더구나 자네 식탁에서야 말할 것도 없네, 소여—정말로 그래."], ["Gunter that he is no gentleman.’", "하지만 나는 이 기회를 빌려 건터 씨에게, 당신은 신사가 아니라고 알려 줘야겠어.\""]], [["‘And I should be very sorry, Sawyer, to create any disturbance in the street in which you reside,’ said Mr.", "\"그리고 소여, 나도 자네가 사는 거리에서 소동을 일으키는 건 정말 유감스럽게 여기네만,\""], ["Gunter, ‘but I’m afraid I shall be under the necessity of alarming the neighbours by throwing the person who has just spoken, out o’ window.’", "건터 씨가 말했다. \"방금 말한 자를 창밖으로 내던져 이웃들을 놀라게 할 수밖에 없을 듯하군.\""]], [["‘What do you mean by that, sir?’ inquired Mr.", "\"그게 무슨 소리요, 선생?\""], ["Noddy.", "노디 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘What I say, Sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"말 그대로요, 선생.\""], ["Gunter.", "건터 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘I should like to see you do it, Sir,’ said Mr.", "\"어디 한번 해 보시지, 선생.\""], ["Noddy.", "노디 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘You shall feel me do it in half a minute, Sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"삼십 초 안에 내가 하는 걸 몸소 느끼게 될 거요, 선생.\""], ["Gunter.", "건터 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘I request that you’ll favour me with your card, Sir,’ said Mr.", "\"당신 명함을 좀 주시지요, 선생.\""], ["Noddy.", "노디 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘I’ll do nothing of the kind, Sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"그런 짓은 절대 안 하겠소, 선생.\""], ["Gunter.", "건터 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘Why not, Sir?’ inquired Mr.", "\"왜 안 되는데요, 선생?\""], ["Noddy.", "노디 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘Because you’ll stick it up over your chimney-piece, and delude your visitors into the false belief that a gentleman has been to see you, Sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"그걸 벽난로 선반 위에 꽂아 두고, 신사가 자기를 찾아온 적이 있다는 헛된 믿음으로 손님들을 속일 테니까요, 선생.\""], ["Gunter.", "건터 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘Sir, a friend of mine shall wait on you in the morning,’ said Mr.", "\"선생, 내일 아침에 내 친구가 당신을 찾아갈 거요.\""], ["Noddy.", "노디 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Sir, I’m very much obliged to you for the caution, and I’ll leave particular directions with the servant to lock up the spoons,’ replied Mr.", "\"선생, 미리 일러 주셔서 정말 고맙소. 하인한테 숟가락을 잘 챙겨 두라고 특별히 단단히 일러 두겠소.\""], ["Gunter.", "건터 씨가 대답했다."]], [["At this point the remainder of the guests interposed, and remonstrated with both parties on the impropriety of their conduct; on which Mr. Noddy begged to state that his father was quite as respectable as Mr. Gunter’s father; to which Mr. Gunter replied that his father was to the full as respectable as Mr. Noddy’s father, and that his father’s son was as good a man as Mr. Noddy, any day in the week. As this announcement seemed the prelude to a recommencement of the dispute, there was another interference on the part of the company; and a vast quantity of talking and clamouring ensued, in the course of which Mr. Noddy gradually allowed his feelings to overpower him, and professed that he had ever entertained a devoted personal attachment towards Mr. Gunter. To this Mr. Gunter replied that, upon the whole, he rather preferred Mr. Noddy to his own brother; on hearing which admission, Mr. Noddy magnanimously rose from his seat, and proffered his hand to Mr. Gunter. Mr. Gunter grasped it with affecting fervour; and everybody said that the whole dispute had been conducted in a manner which was highly honourable to both parties concerned.", "이 대목에서 나머지 손님들이 끼어들어, 두 사람 모두에게 그 행동거지의 온당치 못함을 들어 타일렀다. 그러자 노디 씨는 자기 아버지도 건터 씨 아버지 못지않게 훌륭한 분이라고 밝혔고, 이에 건터 씨는 자기 아버지도 노디 씨 아버지에 조금도 못지않게 훌륭한 분이며, 그 아버지의 아들은 노디 씨에 비해 어느 날을 잡아도 손색없는 사내라고 응수했다. 이 선언이 다툼을 다시 시작하는 서막처럼 보였으므로 좌중이 또 한 번 끼어들었고, 엄청난 양의 말과 떠들썩한 소동이 이어졌다. 그 와중에 노디 씨는 차츰 감정에 북받쳐, 자기는 건터 씨에게 늘 헌신적인 개인적 애정을 품어 왔노라고 고백했다. 이에 건터 씨는, 전체적으로 보아 자기는 친형제보다 차라리 노디 씨가 더 좋다고 응수했다. 이 고백을 듣자 노디 씨는 너그럽게 자리에서 일어나 건터 씨에게 손을 내밀었다. 건터 씨는 가슴 뭉클한 열정으로 그 손을 맞잡았고, 모두가 이 다툼 전체가 두 당사자 모두에게 더없이 명예로운 방식으로 처리되었다고 입을 모았다."]], [["‘Now,’ said Jack Hopkins, ‘just to set us going again, Bob, I don’t mind singing a song.’ And Hopkins, incited thereto by tumultuous applause, plunged himself at once into ‘The King, God bless him,’ which he sang as loud as he could, to a novel air, compounded of the ‘Bay of Biscay,’ and ‘A Frog he would.’ The chorus was the essence of the song; and, as each gentleman sang it to the tune he knew best, the effect was very striking indeed.", "\"자,\" 잭 홉킨스가 말했다. \"분위기를 다시 띄울 겸, 밥, 내가 노래 한 곡 부르는 것도 괜찮겠지.\" 그러고는 우레 같은 갈채에 부추겨진 홉킨스가 단숨에 '국왕 폐하, 신의 가호를'에 뛰어들어, '비스케이 만'과 '개구리 한 마리가'를 섞어 만든 새로운 곡조에 맞춰 목청껏 불러 댔다. 노래의 정수는 후렴이었는데, 신사들 저마다 자기가 가장 잘 아는 가락으로 그것을 부르는 통에 그 효과는 참으로 충격적이었다."]], [["It was at the end of the chorus to the first verse, that Mr. Pickwick held up his hand in a listening attitude, and said, as soon as silence was restored--", "첫째 절 후렴이 끝날 무렵, 픽윅 씨가 귀를 기울이는 자세로 손을 들어 올리고는, 조용해지자마자 말했다."]], [["‘Hush!", "\"쉿!"], ["I beg your pardon.", "미안합니다."], ["I thought I heard somebody calling from upstairs.’", "위층에서 누가 부르는 소리를 들은 것 같아서요.\""]], [["A profound silence immediately ensued; and Mr. Bob Sawyer was observed to turn pale.", "곧바로 깊은 침묵이 뒤따랐고, 밥 소여 씨의 얼굴이 새파랗게 질리는 것이 눈에 띄었다."]], [["‘I think I hear it now,’ said Mr. Pickwick.", "\"지금 또 들리는 것 같소.\""], ["‘Have the goodness to open the door.’", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"문 좀 열어 주시겠소.\""]], [["The door was no sooner opened than all doubt on the subject was removed.", "문이 열리기가 무섭게 그 문제에 관한 의심은 모두 가셨다."]], [["‘Mr.", "\"소여 씨!"], ["Sawyer! Mr.", "소여 씨!\""], ["Sawyer!’ screamed a voice from the two-pair landing.", "삼 층 층계참에서 어떤 목소리가 비명을 질렀다."]], [["‘It’s my landlady,’ said Bob Sawyer, looking round him with great dismay.", "\"우리 안주인이오.\" 밥 소여가 몹시 당황하여 둘러보며 말했다."], ["‘Yes, Mrs. Raddle.’", "\"네, 래들 부인.\""]], [["‘What do you mean by this, Mr.", "\"이게 대체 무슨 짓이에요, 소여 씨?\""], ["Sawyer?’ replied the voice, with great shrillness and rapidity of utterance.", "그 목소리가 무척 새되고 빠른 말투로 응수했다."], ["‘Ain’t it enough to be swindled out of one’s rent, and money lent out of pocket besides, and abused and insulted by your friends that dares to call themselves men, without having the house turned out of the window, and noise enough made to bring the fire-engines here, at two o’clock in the morning?--Turn them wretches away.’", "\"집세를 떼이고, 게다가 내 돈까지 빌려주고, 사내라고 감히 자처하는 당신 친구들한테 욕을 먹고 모욕을 당하는 것도 모자라서, 새벽 두 시에 집을 창밖으로 뒤집어엎고 소방차라도 불러올 만큼 시끄럽게 굴어야겠어요?—저 못된 것들을 내쫓아요.\""]], [["‘You ought to be ashamed of yourselves,’ said the voice of Mr.", "\"당신들 부끄러운 줄 알아야 하오.\""], ["Raddle, which appeared to proceed from beneath some distant bed-clothes.", "래들 씨의 목소리가 말했는데, 어디 먼 이불 속에서 새어 나오는 듯했다."]], [["‘Ashamed of themselves!’ said Mrs.", "\"부끄러운 줄 알라고!\" 래들 부인이 말했다."], ["Raddle. ‘Why don’t you go down and knock ‘em every one downstairs?", "\"당신은 어째서 내려가서 저것들을 하나하나 층계 아래로 처박지 않아요?"], ["You would if you was a man.’", "당신이 사내라면 그렇게 했을 거 아니에요.\""]], [["I should if I was a dozen men, my dear,’ replied Mr.", "\"내가 열두 사람이라면 그랬을 거요, 여보.\""], ["Raddle pacifically, ‘but they have the advantage of me in numbers, my dear.’", "래들 씨가 누그러뜨리려는 듯 대답했다. \"하지만 머릿수에서 저쪽이 나보다 한 수 위라오, 여보.\""]], [["‘Ugh, you coward!’ replied Mrs.", "\"흥, 이 겁쟁이!\""], ["Raddle, with supreme contempt.", "래들 부인이 더없는 경멸로 응수했다."], ["‘Do you mean to turn them wretches out, or not, Mr. Sawyer?’", "\"저 못된 것들을 내쫓을 거예요, 말 거예요, 소여 씨?\""]], [["‘They’re going, Mrs. Raddle, they’re going,’ said the miserable Bob.", "\"가는 중입니다, 래들 부인, 가는 중이에요.\""], ["‘I am afraid you’d better go,’ said Mr.", "가엾은 밥이 말했다. \"아무래도 자네들 가는 게 좋겠어.\""], ["Bob Sawyer to his friends.", "밥 소여 씨가 친구들에게 말했다."], ["‘I thought you were making too much noise.’", "\"내 생각에도 우리가 너무 시끄럽게 굴었어.\""]], [["‘It’s a very unfortunate thing,’ said the prim man.", "\"이거 참 불운한 일이군요.\" 새침한 사내가 말했다."], ["‘Just as we were getting so comfortable too!’ The prim man was just beginning to have a dawning recollection of the story he had forgotten.", "\"마침 이렇게 편안해지던 참인데!\" 새침한 사내는 막 그 잊어버렸던 이야기가 어렴풋이 떠오르기 시작한 참이었다."]], [["‘It’s hardly to be borne,’ said the prim man, looking round.", "\"이건 도저히 견딜 수 없는 일이오.\" 새침한 사내가 둘러보며 말했다."], ["‘Hardly to be borne, is it?’", "\"도저히 견딜 수 없죠, 안 그렇습니까?\""]], [["‘Not to be endured,’ replied Jack Hopkins; ‘let’s have the other verse, Bob.", "\"참을 수 없지요.\" 잭 홉킨스가 대답했다."], ["Come, here goes!’", "\"나머지 절을 부르세, 밥. 자, 간다!\""]], [["‘No, no, Jack, don’t,’ interposed Bob Sawyer; ‘it’s a capital song, but I am afraid we had better not have the other verse.", "\"안 돼, 안 돼, 잭, 그러지 마.\" 밥 소여가 끼어들었다."], ["They are very violent people, the people of the house.’", "\"기막힌 노래이긴 한데, 아무래도 나머지 절은 부르지 않는 게 좋겠어. 이 집 사람들이 아주 사나운 사람들이거든.\""]], [["‘Shall I step upstairs, and pitch into the landlord?’ inquired Hopkins, ‘or keep on ringing the bell, or go and groan on the staircase?", "\"내가 위층에 올라가서 그 집주인 양반을 한 대 갈겨 줄까?\" 홉킨스가 물었다."], ["You may command me, Bob.’", "\"아니면 종을 계속 울려 댈까, 아니면 층계에 가서 신음 소리라도 낼까? 자네가 시키는 대로 하지, 밥.\""]], [["‘I am very much indebted to you for your friendship and good-nature, Hopkins,’ said the wretched Mr.", "\"자네의 우정과 호의에 정말 큰 신세를 지는군, 홉킨스.\" 비참해진 밥 소여 씨가 말했다."], ["Bob Sawyer, ‘but I think the best plan to avoid any further dispute is for us to break up at once.’", "\"하지만 더 이상의 다툼을 피하는 데는 우리가 당장 자리를 파하는 게 상책일 것 같네.\""]], [["‘Now, Mr.", "\"자, 소여 씨.\""], ["Sawyer,’ screamed the shrill voice of Mrs.", "래들 부인의 새된 목소리가 비명을 질렀다."], ["Raddle, ‘are them brutes going?’", "\"저 짐승 같은 것들이 가고 있어요?\""]], [["‘They’re only looking for their hats, Mrs.", "\"모자를 찾고 있을 뿐입니다, 래들 부인.\""], ["Raddle,’ said Bob; ‘they are going directly.’", "밥이 말했다. \"곧 갈 겁니다.\""]], [["‘Going!’ said Mrs.", "\"가긴!\""], ["Raddle, thrusting her nightcap over the banisters just as Mr. Pickwick, followed by Mr.", "래들 부인이, 픽윅 씨가 터프먼 씨를 뒤에 거느리고 거실에서 막 나오는 바로 그 순간에 난간 너머로 나이트캡을 쑥 내밀며 말했다."], ["Tupman, emerged from the sitting- room. ‘Going!", "\"가긴!"], ["what did they ever come for?’", "대체 무엇 하러 온 거야?\""]], [["‘My dear ma’am,’ remonstrated Mr.", "\"이보세요, 부인.\""], ["Pickwick, looking up.", "픽윅 씨가 고개를 들고 항변했다."]], [["‘Get along with you, old wretch!’ replied Mrs.", "\"썩 꺼져요, 이 늙은 망나니!\""], ["Raddle, hastily withdrawing the nightcap.", "래들 부인이 황급히 나이트캡을 도로 거두며 응수했다."], ["‘Old enough to be his grandfather, you willin!", "\"저 사람 할아버지뻘이나 되는 게, 이 능구렁이 같으니!"], ["You’re worse than any of ‘em.’", "당신이 저것들 중에 제일 고약해.\""]], [["Mr. Pickwick found it in vain to protest his innocence, so hurried downstairs into the street, whither he was closely followed by Mr.", "픽윅 씨는 자기 결백을 항변해 봐야 소용없음을 깨닫고 서둘러 층계를 내려가 거리로 나갔다."], ["Tupman, Mr. Winkle, and Mr.", "그 뒤를 터프먼 씨와 윈클 씨와 스노드그래스 씨가 바짝 따랐다."], ["Snodgrass. Mr.", "술기운과 격앙으로 처량하게 풀이 죽은 벤 앨런 씨는 런던 다리까지 그들과 동행했는데, 걷는 도중에 윈클 씨에게—비밀을 털어놓기에 유달리 적임인 인물이라 여겨—자기 누이 아라벨라의 애정을 넘본다면 밥 소여 씨를 제외한 어떤 신사든 그 목을 따고 말리라 결심했노라고 속내를 털어놓았다."], ["Ben Allen, who was dismally depressed with spirits and agitation, accompanied them as far as London Bridge, and in the course of the walk confided to Mr. Winkle, as an especially eligible person to intrust the secret to, that he was resolved to cut the throat of any gentleman, except Mr.", "오라비로서 이 괴로운 의무를 마땅한 단호함으로 수행하겠다는 결의를 밝히고 나서, 그는 와락 눈물을 쏟더니 모자를 눈가까지 눌러쓰고, 되는대로 길을 되짚어 가서는, 버러 시장 사무소 문을 쾅쾅 두드렸다가 계단에서 잠깐씩 졸기를 번갈아 하며 동틀 녘까지 그러고 있었다."], ["Bob Sawyer, who should aspire to the affections of his sister Arabella. Having expressed his determination to perform this painful duty of a brother with proper firmness, he burst into tears, knocked his hat over his eyes, and, making the best of his way back, knocked double knocks at the door of the Borough Market office, and took short naps on the steps alternately, until daybreak, under the firm impression that he lived there, and had forgotten the key.", "자기가 거기 사는데 열쇠를 잊어버렸다고 굳게 믿으면서 말이다."]], [["The visitors having all departed, in compliance with the rather pressing request of Mrs. Raddle, the luckless Mr. Bob Sawyer was left alone, to meditate on the probable events of to-morrow, and the pleasures of the evening.", "손님들이 래들 부인의 자못 절박한 요청에 따라 모두 떠나 버리자, 운수 사나운 밥 소여 씨만 홀로 남아, 내일 벌어질 법한 일들과 그날 저녁의 즐거움을 곰곰이 곱씹게 되었다."]], [["CHAPTER XXXIII. MR.", "이 진실한 이야기의 독자들이라면 우리만큼이나 잘 알고 있겠지만, 2월 13일 아침은 바델 부인의 소송 재판이 열리기로 정해진 날의 바로 전날이었으니, 그날은 새뮤얼 웰러 씨에게 몹시 바쁜 시간이었다."], ["WELLER THE ELDER DELIVERS SOME CRITICAL SENTIMENTS RESPECTING LITERARY COMPOSITION; AND, ASSISTED BY HIS SON SAMUEL, PAYS A SMALL INSTALMENT OF RETALIATION TO THE ACCOUNT OF THE REVEREND GENTLEMAN WITH THE RED NOSE\nThe morning of the thirteenth of February, which the readers of this authentic narrative know, as well as we do, to have been the day immediately preceding that which was appointed for the trial of Mrs.", "그는 아침 아홉 시부터 오후 두 시까지, 양 끝 시각을 모두 포함하여, 조지 앤드 벌처 여관에서 퍼커 씨의 사무실로, 다시 그 반대로 끊임없이 오가는 일에 매달려 있었다."], ["Bardell’s action, was a busy time for Mr.", "사실 무슨 할 일이 있어서가 아니었다."], ["Samuel Weller, who was perpetually engaged in travelling from the George and Vulture to Mr.", "협의는 이미 끝났고, 채택할 소송 진행 방향도 최종적으로 결정된 터였다."], ["Perker’s chambers and back again, from and between the hours of nine o’clock in the morning and two in the afternoon, both inclusive. Not that there was anything whatever to be done, for the consultation had taken place, and the course of proceeding to be adopted, had been finally determined on; but Mr.", "다만 픽윅 씨가 극도의 흥분 상태에 빠져, 자기 변호사에게 끊임없이 짧은 쪽지를 보내기를 고집했던 것인데, 그 쪽지란 그저 \"퍼커 씨에게."], ["Pickwick being in a most extreme state of excitement, persevered in constantly sending small notes to his attorney, merely containing the inquiry, ‘Dear Perker.", "모든 일이 잘되어 가고 있소?\" 하는 물음만을 담은 것이었고, 거기에 퍼커 씨는 한결같이 \"픽윅 씨에게."], ["Is all going on well?’ to which Mr.", "더할 나위 없이 잘되고 있습니다\""], ["Perker invariably forwarded the reply, ‘Dear Pickwick.", "하는 답을 보내 왔다."], ["As well as possible’; the fact being, as we have already hinted, that there was nothing whatever to go on, either well or ill, until the sitting of the court on the following morning.", "사실은 우리가 이미 넌지시 비친 대로, 이튿날 아침 법정이 열리기 전까지는 잘되든 못되든 진행될 일이라곤 전혀 없었던 것이다."]], [["But people who go voluntarily to law, or are taken forcibly there, for the first time, may be allowed to labour under some temporary irritation and anxiety; and Sam, with a due allowance for the frailties of human nature, obeyed all his master’s behests with that imperturbable good- humour and unruffable composure which formed one of his most striking and amiable characteristics.", "그러나 자진해서든 강제로든 난생처음 법정에 가는 사람이라면 한동안 신경과민과 불안에 시달리는 것쯤은 너그러이 봐줄 수 있는 법이다. 그리고 샘은 인간 본성의 약점에 대한 마땅한 헤아림으로, 그 누구도 흔들 수 없는 차분한 쾌활함과 한결같은 평정심으로 주인의 모든 분부를 따랐으니, 이는 그의 가장 두드러지고 사랑스러운 특성 가운데 하나였다."]], [["Sam had solaced himself with a most agreeable little dinner, and was waiting at the bar for the glass of warm mixture in which Mr.", "샘은 더없이 흡족한 점심으로 자신을 위로한 뒤, 픽윅 씨가 아침 산책의 피로를 씻어 내라고 권한 따끈한 혼합주 한 잔을 바에서 기다리고 있었다. 그때 키가 삼 피트쯤 되어 보이는 어린 사내아이 하나가 털모자를 쓰고 퍼스티언 작업복을 걸치고 조지 앤드 벌처 여관의 복도로 들어왔는데, 그 차림새는 머지않아 마구간지기의 지위에 오르겠다는 가상한 포부를 드러내고 있었다."], ["Pickwick had requested him to drown the fatigues of his morning’s walks, when a young boy of about three feet high, or thereabouts, in a hairy cap and fustian overalls, whose garb bespoke a laudable ambition to attain in time the elevation of an hostler, entered the passage of the George and Vulture, and looked first up the stairs, and then along the passage, and then into the bar, as if in search of somebody to whom he bore a commission; whereupon the barmaid, conceiving it not improbable that the said commission might be directed to the tea or table spoons of the establishment, accosted the boy with--", "아이는 먼저 계단 위를 올려다보고, 다음으로 복도를 따라 살펴보고, 또 바 안을 들여다보았으니, 마치 무슨 심부름을 전할 사람을 찾는 듯한 모양새였다. 그러자 바의 아가씨는 그 심부름이라는 것이 가게의 찻숟가락이나 식탁용 숟가락을 노리는 것일지도 모른다고 여겨, 아이에게 말을 걸었다—"]], [["‘Now, young man, what do you want?’", "\"자, 젊은이, 무슨 일이지?\""]], [["‘Is there anybody here, named Sam?’ inquired the youth, in a loud voice of treble quality.", "\"여기에 샘이라는 사람 있어요?\" 그 아이가 새된 고음으로 큰 소리를 내며 물었다."]], [["‘What’s the t’other name?’ said Sam Weller, looking round.", "\"성은 뭐냐?\" 샘 웰러가 돌아보며 말했다."]], [["‘How should I know?’ briskly replied the young gentleman below the hairy cap.", "\"그걸 내가 어떻게 알아요?\" 털모자 아래의 젊은 신사가 잽싸게 대꾸했다."]], [["‘You’re a sharp boy, you are,’ said Mr.", "\"너 참 똘똘한 녀석이로구나.\""], ["Weller; ‘only I wouldn’t show that wery fine edge too much, if I was you, in case anybody took it off.", "웰러 씨가 말했다. \"다만 내가 너라면 그 아주 잘 선 날을 너무 드러내지는 않겠어."], ["What do you mean by comin’ to a hot-el, and asking arter Sam, vith as much politeness as a vild Indian?’", "누가 그걸 갈아 없애 버릴까 봐 말이지. 호-텔에 들어와서 야생 인디언만큼이나 공손하게 샘을 찾는 건 또 무슨 뜻이냐?\""]], [["‘’Cos an old gen’l’m’n told me to,’ replied the boy.", "\"어떤 늙은 신사분이 그러라고 시켰거든요.\" 아이가 대답했다."]], [["‘What old gen’l’m’n?’ inquired Sam, with deep disdain.", "\"어떤 늙은 신사 말이냐?\" 샘이 깊은 경멸을 담아 물었다."]], [["‘Him as drives a Ipswich coach, and uses our parlour,’ rejoined the boy.", "\"입스위치 마차를 모는 분이요, 우리 가게 응접실을 쓰는 분이요.\""], ["‘He told me yesterday mornin’ to come to the George and Wultur this arternoon, and ask for Sam.’", "아이가 받아 말했다. \"어제 아침에 저더러 오늘 오후에 조지 앤드 벌처로 가서 샘을 찾으라고 하셨어요.\""]], [["‘It’s my father, my dear,’ said Mr.", "\"우리 아버지로구나, 아가씨.\""], ["Weller, turning with an explanatory air to the young lady in the bar; ‘blessed if I think he hardly knows wot my other name is.", "웰러 씨가 바의 젊은 아가씨를 향해 설명하는 투로 돌아서며 말했다. \"내 성이 뭔지도 거의 모르시는 것 같네, 분명히."], ["Well, young brockiley sprout, wot then?’", "자, 어린 브로콜리 새싹아, 그래서 어쩌라는 거냐?\""]], [["‘Why then,’ said the boy, ‘you was to come to him at six o’clock to our ‘ouse, ‘cos he wants to see you--Blue Boar, Leaden’all Markit.", "\"그러니까요,\" 아이가 말했다."], ["Shall I say you’re comin’?’", "\"아저씨가 여섯 시에 우리 가게로 그분한테 오래요, 그분이 아저씨를 보고 싶어 하니까요—리든홀 시장의 블루 보어요. 온다고 전할까요?\""]], [["‘You may wenture on that ‘ere statement, Sir,’ replied Sam.", "\"그렇게 전해도 좋다, 도련님.\" 샘이 대답했다."], ["And thus empowered, the young gentleman walked away, awakening all the echoes in George Yard as he did so, with several chaste and extremely correct imitations of a drover’s whistle, delivered in a tone of peculiar richness and volume.", "이렇게 권한을 받은 젊은 신사는 걸어가면서, 유난히 풍부하고 우렁찬 가락으로 가축몰이꾼의 휘파람을 몇 차례 더없이 정확하고 깔끔하게 흉내 내어, 조지 야드의 메아리란 메아리를 모조리 깨워 놓고 떠났다."]], [["Mr. Weller having obtained leave of absence from Mr.", "웰러 씨는 픽윅 씨에게서 외출 허락을 얻었는데, 픽윅 씨는 그때의 흥분과 근심 상태에서 혼자 남겨지는 것을 결코 언짢아하지 않았다."], ["Pickwick, who, in his then state of excitement and worry, was by no means displeased at being left alone, set forth, long before the appointed hour, and having plenty of time at his disposal, sauntered down as far as the Mansion House, where he paused and contemplated, with a face of great calmness and philosophy, the numerous cads and drivers of short stages who assemble near that famous place of resort, to the great terror and confusion of the old-lady population of these realms.", "그는 정해진 시각보다 한참 일찍 나섰고, 시간이 넉넉했으므로 맨션 하우스까지 어슬렁어슬렁 걸어 내려가, 그 유명한 집결지 근처에 모여 이 나라 노부인 무리에게 큰 공포와 혼란을 안기는 수많은 마차 호객꾼과 단거리 마차 마부들을 더없이 차분하고 철학적인 표정으로 바라보았다."], ["Having loitered here, for half an hour or so, Mr. Weller turned, and began wending his way towards Leadenhall Market, through a variety of by-streets and courts.", "한 삼십 분쯤 여기서 어정거린 뒤, 웰러 씨는 발길을 돌려 여러 골목과 안마당을 지나 리든홀 시장 쪽으로 걸어가기 시작했다."], ["As he was sauntering away his spare time, and stopped to look at almost every object that met his gaze, it is by no means surprising that Mr.", "그는 남는 시간을 어슬렁거리며 흘려보내고 있었고 눈에 띄는 거의 모든 것 앞에 멈춰 서서 구경했으므로, 웰러 씨가 어느 작은 문구점 겸 판화 가게 진열창 앞에서 걸음을 멈춘 것은 조금도 놀랄 일이 아니었다."], ["Weller should have paused before a small stationer’s and print- seller’s window; but without further explanation it does appear surprising that his eyes should have no sooner rested on certain pictures which were exposed for sale therein, than he gave a sudden start, smote his right leg with great vehemence, and exclaimed, with energy, ‘if it hadn’t been for this, I should ha’ forgot all about it, till it was too late!’", "그러나 더 이상의 설명 없이 보면, 거기에 팔려고 내놓은 어떤 그림들에 그의 눈길이 닿자마자, 그가 갑자기 흠칫 놀라 오른쪽 다리를 세차게 치며 기운차게 외친 것은 자못 놀라워 보인다. \"이게 아니었으면 너무 늦을 때까지 까맣게 잊고 있을 뻔했네!\""]], [["The particular picture on which Sam Weller’s eyes were fixed, as he said this, was a highly-coloured representation of a couple of human hearts skewered together with an arrow, cooking before a cheerful fire, while a male and female cannibal in modern attire, the gentleman being clad in a blue coat and white trousers, and the lady in a deep red pelisse with a parasol of the same, were approaching the meal with hungry eyes, up a serpentine gravel path leading thereunto.", "샘 웰러가 이렇게 말할 때 그의 눈이 못 박힌 그 그림은, 화살에 꼬치처럼 꿰인 두 개의 사람 심장이 활활 타오르는 불 위에서 구워지고 있는 것을 화려한 색채로 그린 것이었다. 한편 현대식 차림을 한 남녀 식인종이—신사는 파란 코트에 흰 바지를 입고 숙녀는 진홍색 펠리스에 같은 빛깔의 양산을 들고—거기로 이어지는 구불구불한 자갈길을 따라 굶주린 눈빛으로 그 식사에 다가가고 있었다."], ["A decidedly indelicate young gentleman, in a pair of wings and nothing else, was depicted as superintending the cooking; a representation of the spire of the church in Langham Place, London, appeared in the distance; and the whole formed a ‘valentine,’ of which, as a written inscription in the window testified, there was a large assortment within, which the shopkeeper pledged himself to dispose of, to his countrymen generally, at the reduced rate of one-and-sixpence each.", "날개 한 쌍만 달고 그 밖에는 아무것도 걸치지 않은, 누가 봐도 점잖지 못한 젊은 신사가 그 요리를 감독하는 모습으로 그려져 있었고, 멀리 런던 랭엄 플레이스 교회의 첨탑이 보였다. 그리고 그 전체가 하나의 \"밸런타인\"을 이루고 있었는데, 진열창에 적힌 안내문이 증언하듯, 가게 안에는 이런 것이 잔뜩 갖추어져 있어, 가게 주인은 동포 여러분께 한 장당 일 실링 육 펜스의 할인가로 처분할 것을 다짐하고 있었다."]], [["‘I should ha’ forgot it; I should certainly ha’ forgot it!’ said Sam; so saying, he at once stepped into the stationer’s shop, and requested to be served with a sheet of the best gilt-edged letter-paper, and a hard- nibbed pen which could be warranted not to splutter.", "\"잊고 있을 뻔했네, 정말 까맣게 잊고 있을 뻔했어!\" 샘이 말했다."], ["These articles having been promptly supplied, he walked on direct towards Leadenhall Market at a good round pace, very different from his recent lingering one.", "이렇게 말하면서 그는 곧장 문구점 안으로 들어가, 최상품 금테 편지지 한 장과 결코 잉크가 튀지 않는다고 보증할 수 있는 단단한 펜촉 하나를 달라고 했다."], ["Looking round him, he there beheld a signboard on which the painter’s art had delineated something remotely resembling a cerulean elephant with an aquiline nose in lieu of trunk.", "이 물건들이 즉시 마련되자, 그는 최근의 느릿느릿한 걸음과는 사뭇 다른, 빠르고 활기찬 걸음으로 곧장 리든홀 시장을 향해 걸어갔다. 주위를 둘러보다가 그는 거기서 간판 하나를 보았는데, 화가의 솜씨로 코끼리 비슷한 무엇이 그려져 있었으되, 코 대신 매부리코가 달린 하늘빛 코끼리 같은 것이었다."], ["Rightly conjecturing that this was the Blue Boar himself, he stepped into the house, and inquired concerning his parent.", "이것이 바로 블루 보어임에 틀림없다고 옳게 짐작하고, 그는 가게 안으로 들어가 자기 아버지의 소식을 물었다."]], [["‘He won’t be here this three-quarters of an hour or more,’ said the young lady who superintended the domestic arrangements of the Blue Boar.", "\"그분은 앞으로 사십오 분이나 그 이상은 더 있어야 오실 텐데요.\" 블루 보어의 집안일을 맡아보는 젊은 아가씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Wery good, my dear,’ replied Sam.", "\"아주 좋아요, 아가씨.\""], ["‘Let me have nine-penn’oth o’ brandy- and-water luke, and the inkstand, will you, miss?’", "샘이 대답했다. \"미지근한 브랜디 워터 구 펜스어치하고 잉크병 좀 갖다주겠어요, 아가씨?\""]], [["The brandy-and-water luke, and the inkstand, having been carried into the little parlour, and the young lady having carefully flattened down the coals to prevent their blazing, and carried away the poker to preclude the possibility of the fire being stirred, without the full privity and concurrence of the Blue Boar being first had and obtained, Sam Weller sat himself down in a box near the stove, and pulled out the sheet of gilt-edged letter-paper, and the hard-nibbed pen.", "미지근한 브랜디 워터와 잉크병이 작은 응접실로 들어왔고, 젊은 아가씨가 석탄이 활활 타오르지 않도록 조심스레 눌러 다지고, 블루 보어의 충분한 양해와 동의를 먼저 얻지 않고서는 불을 휘저을 가능성을 아예 막으려고 부지깽이를 치워 버린 뒤, 샘 웰러는 난로 가까운 칸막이 자리에 앉아 금테 편지지와 단단한 펜촉을 꺼냈다."], ["Then looking carefully at the pen to see that there were no hairs in it, and dusting down the table, so that there might be no crumbs of bread under the paper, Sam tucked up the cuffs of his coat, squared his elbows, and composed himself to write.", "그러고는 펜에 털이 끼지 않았는지 꼼꼼히 살피고, 종이 밑에 빵 부스러기가 없도록 식탁을 털어 낸 다음, 샘은 코트 소맷부리를 걷어 올리고 팔꿈치를 떡 벌리고는, 마음을 가다듬고 쓰기 시작했다."]], [["To ladies and gentlemen who are not in the habit of devoting themselves practically to the science of penmanship, writing a letter is no very easy task; it being always considered necessary in such cases for the writer to recline his head on his left arm, so as to place his eyes as nearly as possible on a level with the paper, and, while glancing sideways at the letters he is constructing, to form with his tongue imaginary characters to correspond. These motions, although unquestionably of the greatest assistance to original composition, retard in some degree the progress of the writer; and Sam had unconsciously been a full hour and a half writing words in small text, smearing out wrong letters with his little finger, and putting in new ones which required going over very often to render them visible through the old blots, when he was roused by the opening of the door and the entrance of his parent.", "서법(書法)이라는 학문에 실제로 몸 바쳐 본 적이 없는 신사 숙녀에게 편지를 쓴다는 것은 결코 쉬운 일이 아니다. 그런 경우에는 으레 쓰는 사람이 왼팔에 머리를 기대어 두 눈을 가능한 한 종이와 같은 높이에 두고, 자기가 만들고 있는 글자들을 곁눈질하면서, 거기에 맞춰 혀로 가상의 글자들을 빚어내는 것이 필요하다고 여겨지는 법이다. 이런 동작들은 의심할 바 없이 독창적인 글짓기에 더없이 큰 도움이 되긴 하지만, 어느 정도 쓰는 이의 진척을 더디게 한다. 그리하여 샘은 자기도 모르게 꼬박 한 시간 반 동안이나, 작은 글씨로 단어들을 쓰고, 잘못 쓴 글자를 새끼손가락으로 문질러 지우고, 새 글자를 써 넣되 옛 얼룩 사이로 드러나 보이게 하려고 몇 번씩 덧칠해야 했다. 그러던 차에 문이 열리고 자기 아버지가 들어오는 바람에 정신이 번쩍 들었다."]], [["‘Vell, Sammy,’ said the father.", "\"잘 있었냐, 새미.\" 아버지가 말했다."]], [["‘Vell, my Prooshan Blue,’ responded the son, laying down his pen.", "\"안녕하셨어요, 우리 프러시안 블루.\" 아들이 펜을 내려놓으며 대꾸했다."], ["‘What’s the last bulletin about mother-in-law?’", "\"새어머니에 관한 최신 소식은 어떻게 됐어요?\""]], [["‘Mrs. Veller passed a very good night, but is uncommon perwerse, and unpleasant this mornin’.", "\"웰러 부인께서는 밤은 아주 잘 보내셨으나, 오늘 아침엔 유난히 심술궂고 까칠하시다."], ["Signed upon oath, Tony Veller, Esquire.", "토니 웰러 경(卿), 선서하고 서명함."], ["That’s the last vun as was issued, Sammy,’ replied Mr.", "그게 가장 최근에 나온 거다, 새미.\""], ["Weller, untying his shawl.", "웰러 씨가 목도리를 풀며 대답했다."]], [["‘No better yet?’ inquired Sam.", "\"아직도 나아진 게 없어요?\" 샘이 물었다."]], [["‘All the symptoms aggerawated,’ replied Mr.", "\"증세가 죄다 더 악화됐다.\""], ["Weller, shaking his head.", "웰러 씨가 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 대답했다."], ["‘But wot’s that, you’re a-doin’ of?", "\"그런데 너 그거 뭘 하고 있는 거냐?"], ["Pursuit of knowledge under difficulties, Sammy?’", "역경 속에서 학문을 추구하는 거냐, 새미?\""]], [["‘I’ve done now,’ said Sam, with slight embarrassment; ‘I’ve been a- writin’.’", "\"이제 다 했어요.\" 샘이 약간 멋쩍어하며 말했다. \"편지를 쓰고 있었어요.\""]], [["‘So I see,’ replied Mr.", "\"그래 보이는구나.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다."], ["Weller. ‘Not to any young ‘ooman, I hope, Sammy?’", "\"설마 어떤 젊은 아가씨한테 쓴 건 아니겠지, 새미?\""]], [["‘Why, it’s no use a-sayin’ it ain’t,’ replied Sam; ‘it’s a walentine.’", "\"아니라고 해 봐야 소용없죠.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"밸런타인이에요.\""]], [["‘A what!’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"뭐라고!\""], ["Weller, apparently horror-stricken by the word.", "웰러 씨가 그 말에 질겁한 듯 외쳤다."]], [["‘A walentine,’ replied Sam.", "\"밸런타인이라고요.\" 샘이 대답했다."]], [["‘Samivel, Samivel,’ said Mr.", "\"새미블, 새미블아.\""], ["Weller, in reproachful accents, ‘I didn’t think you’d ha’ done it.", "웰러 씨가 나무라는 어조로 말했다. \"네가 이럴 줄은 몰랐다."], ["Arter the warnin’ you’ve had o’ your father’s wicious propensities; arter all I’ve said to you upon this here wery subject; arter actiwally seein’ and bein’ in the company o’ your own mother-in-law, vich I should ha’ thought wos a moral lesson as no man could never ha’ forgotten to his dyin’ day!", "네 애비의 못된 성벽(性癖)에 관해 그토록 경고를 받았으면서, 내가 바로 이 문제에 대해 너한테 그토록 일러 줬으면서, 게다가 네 새어머니를 직접 보고 함께 지내고도 말이지. 그건 어떤 사내도 죽는 날까지 결코 잊을 수 없는 도덕적 교훈이라고 생각했건만!"], ["I didn’t think you’d ha’ done it, Sammy, I didn’t think you’d ha’ done it!’ These reflections were too much for the good old man.", "네가 이럴 줄은 몰랐다, 새미, 네가 이럴 줄은 정말 몰랐어!\" 이런 생각들은 마음씨 착한 노인에게 너무 벅찼다."], ["He raised Sam’s tumbler to his lips and drank off its contents.", "그는 샘의 잔을 입에 들어 올려 그 안의 것을 단숨에 들이켰다."]], [["‘Wot’s the matter now?’ said Sam.", "\"이제 또 왜 그러세요?\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["‘Nev’r mind, Sammy,’ replied Mr. Weller, ‘it’ll be a wery agonisin’ trial to me at my time of life, but I’m pretty tough, that’s vun consolation, as the wery old turkey remarked wen the farmer said he wos afeerd he should be obliged to kill him for the London market.’", "\"신경 쓸 거 없다, 새미.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다. \"이 나이에 나한텐 참으로 고통스러운 시련이 되겠지만, 그래도 내가 꽤 질기다는 게 한 가지 위안이긴 하다. 농부가 런던 시장에 내다 팔려면 어쩔 수 없이 너를 잡아야겠다고 하니까, 아주 늙은 칠면조가 그러더라지. '난 꽤 질겨서요' 하고 말이야.\""]], [["‘Wot’ll be a trial?’ inquired Sam.", "\"뭐가 시련이 된다는 거예요?\" 샘이 물었다."]], [["‘To see you married, Sammy--to see you a dilluded wictim, and thinkin’ in your innocence that it’s all wery capital,’ replied Mr. Weller.", "\"네가 결혼하는 걸 보는 거 말이다, 새미—네가 속아 넘어간 희생자가 돼서, 순진하게도 그게 다 아주 멋진 일이라고 여기는 꼴을 보는 거 말이야.\""], ["‘It’s a dreadful trial to a father’s feelin’s, that ‘ere, Sammy--’", "웰러 씨가 대답했다. \"그게 애비 심정엔 끔찍한 시련이라고, 새미—\""]], [["‘Nonsense,’ said Sam.", "\"말도 안 돼요.\" 샘이 말했다."], ["‘I ain’t a-goin’ to get married, don’t you fret yourself about that; I know you’re a judge of these things.", "\"전 결혼 안 할 거니까 그런 거 가지고 애태우지 마세요. 아버지가 이런 일엔 도가 텄다는 거 잘 알아요."], ["Order in your pipe and I’ll read you the letter.", "파이프나 시키세요. 편지를 읽어 드릴 테니까."], ["There!’", "자!\""]], [["We cannot distinctly say whether it was the prospect of the pipe, or the consolatory reflection that a fatal disposition to get married ran in the family, and couldn’t be helped, which calmed Mr.", "웰러 씨의 마음을 가라앉히고 그의 슬픔을 잦아들게 한 것이, 파이프를 피우게 된다는 기대였는지, 아니면 결혼하고야 마는 치명적인 기질이 집안 내력이라 어쩔 도리가 없다는 위안의 생각이었는지는 우리도 또렷이 말할 수 없다."], ["Weller’s feelings, and caused his grief to subside.", "차라리 이 두 가지 위안의 원천을 합친 데서 그 결과가 나왔다고 보는 편이 옳을 듯하다."], ["We should be rather disposed to say that the result was attained by combining the two sources of consolation, for he repeated the second in a low tone, very frequently; ringing the bell meanwhile, to order in the first.", "그가 두 번째 것을 낮은 목소리로 아주 자주 되뇌면서, 그러는 사이 종을 울려 첫 번째 것을 시켰으니 말이다. 이윽고 그는 겉옷을 벗고, 파이프에 불을 붙인 다음, 불기운을 흠뻑 느끼는 동시에 벽난로 선반에 기댈 수 있도록 불을 등지고 그 앞에 자리를 잡았다."], ["He then divested himself of his upper coat; and lighting the pipe and placing himself in front of the fire with his back towards it, so that he could feel its full heat, and recline against the mantel-piece at the same time, turned towards Sam, and, with a countenance greatly mollified by the softening influence of tobacco, requested him to ‘fire away.’", "그러고는 샘 쪽으로 돌아서서, 담배의 누그러뜨리는 영향으로 한결 부드러워진 얼굴로, 그에게 \"어디 한번 쏴 봐라\" 하고 청했다."]], [["Sam dipped his pen into the ink to be ready for any corrections, and began with a very theatrical air--", "샘은 어떤 수정에도 대비하려고 펜을 잉크에 적시고는, 자못 연극적인 태도로 시작했다—"]], [["‘“Lovely--“’", "\"'사랑스러운—'\""]], [["‘Stop,’ said Mr.", "\"잠깐.\""], ["Weller, ringing the bell.", "웰러 씨가 종을 울리며 말했다."], ["‘A double glass o’ the inwariable, my dear.’", "\"늘 마시던 그거 두 잔 분량으로다가, 아가씨.\""]], [["‘Very well, Sir,’ replied the girl; who with great quickness appeared, vanished, returned, and disappeared.", "\"알겠습니다, 손님.\" 그 아가씨가 대답했다. 그녀는 무척 날쌔게 나타났다가 사라지고, 돌아왔다가 다시 자취를 감췄다."]], [["‘They seem to know your ways here,’ observed Sam.", "\"여기선 아버지 습관을 잘 아는 것 같네요.\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["‘Yes,’ replied his father, ‘I’ve been here before, in my time.", "\"그렇지.\" 그의 아버지가 대답했다."], ["Go on, Sammy.’", "\"나도 한창때 여기 와 본 적이 있으니까. 계속해라, 새미.\""]], [["‘“Lovely creetur,”’ repeated Sam.", "\"'사랑스러운 그대여,'\" 샘이 되풀이했다."]], [["‘’Tain’t in poetry, is it?’ interposed his father.", "\"그거 시는 아니겠지, 응?\" 그의 아버지가 끼어들었다."]], [["‘No, no,’ replied Sam.", "\"아니에요, 아니에요.\" 샘이 대답했다."]], [["‘Wery glad to hear it,’ said Mr.", "\"그거 들으니 아주 다행이다.\" 웰러 씨가 말했다."], ["Weller.", "\"시란 건 부자연스러운 거야."], ["‘Poetry’s unnat’ral; no man ever talked poetry ‘cept a beadle on boxin’-day, or Warren’s blackin’, or Rowland’s oil, or some of them low fellows; never you let yourself down to talk poetry, my boy.", "권투의 날 교회 머슴이나, 워런 구두약이나, 롤런드 기름이나, 그런 천박한 부류 말고는 시로 말하는 놈을 본 적이 없다. 절대로 시 따위로 말하면서 네 격을 떨어뜨리지 마라, 얘야."], ["Begin agin, Sammy.’", "다시 시작해라, 새미.\""]], [["Mr. Weller resumed his pipe with critical solemnity, and Sam once more commenced, and read as follows:", "웰러 씨는 비평가다운 엄숙함으로 다시 파이프를 물었고, 샘은 또 한 번 시작하여 다음과 같이 읽었다—"]], [["‘“Lovely creetur I feel myself a damned--“’", "\"'사랑스러운 그대여 나는 빌어먹을—'\""]], [["That ain’t proper,’ said Mr.", "\"그건 적절치 않다.\""], ["Weller, taking his pipe from his mouth.", "웰러 씨가 입에서 파이프를 떼며 말했다."]], [["‘No; it ain’t “damned,”’ observed Sam, holding the letter up to the light, ‘it’s “shamed,”", "\"아니, '빌어먹을'이 아니에요.\" 샘이 편지를 불빛에 비춰 보며 말했다."], ["there’s a blot there--“I feel myself ashamed.”’", "\"'부끄러운'이에요, 거기 얼룩이 있어요—'나는 부끄러움을 느낍니다.'\""]], [["‘Wery good,’ said Mr.", "\"아주 좋다.\" 웰러 씨가 말했다."], ["Weller. ‘Go on.’", "\"계속해라.\""]], [["‘Feel myself ashamed, and completely cir--’ I forget what this here word is,’ said Sam, scratching his head with the pen, in vain attempts to remember.", "\"'부끄러움을 느끼며, 완전히 제—' 이 단어가 뭔지 까먹었네.\" 샘이 펜으로 머리를 긁으며 기억해 내려고 헛되이 애쓰며 말했다."]], [["‘Why don’t you look at it, then?’ inquired Mr.", "\"그럼 들여다보면 되잖냐?\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘So I am a-lookin’ at it,’ replied Sam, ‘but there’s another blot.", "\"그러니까 들여다보고 있는 거잖아요.\" 샘이 대답했다."], ["Here’s a “c,”", "\"근데 또 얼룩이 있어요."], ["and a “i,” and a “d.”’", "여기 'ㅈ'하고 'ㅔ'하고 'ㅎ'이 있는데.\""]], [["‘Circumwented, p’raps,’ suggested Mr.", "\"'제압당했다'겠지, 아마.\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 넌지시 일러 줬다."]], [["‘No, it ain’t that,’ said Sam, ‘“circumscribed”; that’s it.’", "\"아니, 그게 아니에요.\" 샘이 말했다. \"'제한되어'예요, 그거예요.\""]], [["‘That ain’t as good a word as “circumwented,”", "\"그건 '제압당했다'만큼 좋은 단어는 아니다, 새미.\""], ["Sammy,’ said Mr. Weller gravely.", "웰러 씨가 진지하게 말했다."]], [["‘Think not?’ said Sam.", "\"그렇게 생각하세요?\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["‘Nothin’ like it,’ replied his father.", "\"비교도 안 되지.\" 그의 아버지가 대답했다."]], [["‘But don’t you think it means more?’ inquired Sam.", "\"하지만 이게 뜻이 더 깊다고 생각 안 하세요?\" 샘이 물었다."]], [["‘Vell p’raps it’s a more tenderer word,’ said Mr.", "\"흠, 어쩌면 더 부드러운 단어일지도 모르겠다.\""], ["Weller, after a few moments’ reflection.", "웰러 씨가 잠시 생각한 뒤에 말했다."], ["‘Go on, Sammy.’", "\"계속해라, 새미.\""]], [["‘“Feel myself ashamed and completely circumscribed in a-dressin’ of you, for you are a nice gal and nothin’ but it.”’", "\"'당신께 말을 거는 데 있어 부끄러움을 느끼며 완전히 제한되어 있습니다, 당신은 멋진 아가씨고 그 외엔 아무것도 아니니까요.'\""]], [["‘That’s a wery pretty sentiment,’ said the elder Mr.", "\"그것 참 아주 예쁜 정서로구나.\""], ["Weller, removing his pipe to make way for the remark.", "늙은 웰러 씨가 그 말을 하려고 파이프를 입에서 떼며 말했다."]], [["‘Yes, I think it is rayther good,’ observed Sam, highly flattered.", "\"네, 저도 제법 괜찮다고 생각해요.\" 샘이 한껏 우쭐해하며 말했다."]], [["‘Wot I like in that ‘ere style of writin’,’ said the elder Mr.", "\"그런 식의 글쓰기에서 내가 마음에 드는 건 말이다,\""], ["Weller, ‘is, that there ain’t no callin’ names in it--no Wenuses, nor nothin’ o’ that kind.", "늙은 웰러 씨가 말했다. \"거기엔 별명 부르기가 없다는 거다—비너스니 뭐니 그런 게 없잖냐."], ["Wot’s the good o’ callin’ a young ‘ooman a Wenus or a angel, Sammy?’", "젊은 아가씨를 비너스니 천사니 하고 부르는 게 무슨 소용이냐, 새미?\""]], [["‘Ah!", "\"아!"], ["what, indeed?’ replied Sam.", "정말, 무슨 소용이겠어요?\" 샘이 대답했다."]], [["‘You might jist as well call her a griffin, or a unicorn, or a king’s arms at once, which is wery well known to be a collection o’ fabulous animals,’ added Mr.", "\"그럴 바엔 차라리 그 여자를 그리핀이라든가, 유니콘이라든가, 아니면 아예 왕가의 문장(紋章)이라고 부르는 편이 낫지. 그건 다들 잘 알다시피 허구의 동물들을 모아 놓은 거니까 말이다.\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 덧붙였다."]], [["‘Just as well,’ replied Sam.", "\"그러는 편이 낫겠죠.\" 샘이 대답했다."]], [["‘Drive on, Sammy,’ said Mr.", "\"몰아붙여라, 새미.\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 말했다."]], [["Sam complied with the request, and proceeded as follows; his father continuing to smoke, with a mixed expression of wisdom and complacency, which was particularly edifying.", "샘은 그 청에 따라 다음과 같이 이어 갔고, 그의 아버지는 지혜와 흐뭇함이 뒤섞인, 자못 교화적인 표정으로 계속 담배를 피웠다."]], [["‘“Afore I see you, I thought all women was alike.”’", "\"'당신을 보기 전에는 여자들이 다 똑같은 줄 알았습니다.'\""]], [["‘So they are,’ observed the elder Mr.", "\"맞는 말이지.\""], ["Weller parenthetically.", "늙은 웰러 씨가 곁다리로 한마디 했다."]], [["‘“But now,”’ continued Sam, ‘“now I find what a reg’lar soft-headed, inkred’lous turnip I must ha’ been; for there ain’t nobody like you, though I like you better than nothin’ at all.” I thought it best to make that rayther strong,’ said Sam, looking up.", "\"'하지만 이제,'\" 샘이 이어 갔다. \"'이제는 제가 얼마나 멍청하고 잘 속아 넘어가는 순무 같은 놈이었는지 알게 됐습니다. 당신 같은 사람은 아무도 없으니까요. 그래도 저는 아무것도 없는 것보다는 당신을 더 좋아합니다.' 그 부분은 좀 강하게 하는 게 좋겠다 싶었어요.\" 샘이 고개를 들며 말했다."]], [["Mr. Weller nodded approvingly, and Sam resumed.", "웰러 씨가 흡족하게 고개를 끄덕이자, 샘이 다시 이어 갔다."]], [["‘“So I take the privilidge of the day, Mary, my dear--as the gen’l’m’n in difficulties did, ven he valked out of a Sunday--to tell you that the first and only time I see you, your likeness was took on my hart in much quicker time and brighter colours than ever a likeness was took by the profeel macheen (wich p’raps you may have heerd on Mary my dear) altho it does finish a portrait and put the frame and glass on complete, with a hook at the end to hang it up by, and all in two minutes and a quarter.”’", "\"'그래서 저는 이 날의 특권을 누리기로 합니다, 메리, 내 사랑—곤경에 처한 그 신사가 일요일에 걸어 나갔을 때 그랬던 것처럼 말이죠—당신께 말씀드리려고요. 당신을 처음이자 단 한 번 보았던 그때, 당신의 모습이 제 가슴에 새겨졌는데, 그 어떤 옆얼굴 그리는 기계가 새긴 것보다(메리, 내 사랑, 당신도 그 기계 얘긴 들어 봤을지 모르겠네요) 훨씬 빠른 시간에 훨씬 밝은 색으로 새겨졌습니다. 비록 그 기계가 초상화를 완성해서 액자와 유리까지 다 끼우고, 걸 수 있게 끝에 고리까지 달아 주는 걸 단 이 분 십오 초 만에 다 해내긴 하지만요.'\""]], [["‘I am afeerd that werges on the poetical, Sammy,’ said Mr.", "\"그건 시적인 데로 흐르는 것 같아 걱정이구나, 새미.\""], ["Weller dubiously.", "웰러 씨가 미심쩍어하며 말했다."]], [["‘No, it don’t,’ replied Sam, reading on very quickly, to avoid contesting the point--", "\"아니에요, 안 그래요.\" 샘이 그 점을 두고 다투기 싫어 아주 빠르게 읽어 나가며 대답했다—"]], [["‘“Except of me Mary my dear as your walentine and think over what I’ve said.--My dear Mary I will now conclude.”", "\"'메리, 내 사랑, 저를 당신의 밸런타인으로 받아 주시고 제가 한 말을 곰곰이 생각해 주세요.—내 사랑 메리, 이제 이만 줄이겠습니다.'"], ["That’s all,’ said Sam.", "그게 다예요.\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["‘That’s rather a Sudden pull-up, ain’t it, Sammy?’ inquired Mr.", "\"그건 좀 갑작스럽게 멈추는 것 아니냐, 새미?\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘Not a bit on it,’ said Sam; ‘she’ll vish there wos more, and that’s the great art o’ letter-writin’.’", "\"전혀 아니에요.\" 샘이 말했다. \"그 여자는 더 있었으면 하고 바랄 거예요. 그게 바로 편지 쓰기의 위대한 기술이라고요.\""]], [["‘Well,’ said Mr.", "\"흠,\" 웰러 씨가 말했다."], ["Weller, ‘there’s somethin’ in that; and I wish your mother-in-law ‘ud only conduct her conwersation on the same gen-teel principle.", "\"거기엔 일리가 있구나. 네 새어머니도 그 똑같은 우아한 원칙으로 대화를 좀 이끌어 갔으면 좋겠다만."], ["Ain’t you a-goin’ to sign it?’", "서명은 안 할 거냐?\""]], [["‘That’s the difficulty,’ said Sam; ‘I don’t know what to sign it.’", "\"그게 문제예요.\" 샘이 말했다. \"뭐라고 서명해야 할지 모르겠어요.\""]], [["‘Sign it--“Veller”,’ said the oldest surviving proprietor of that name.", "\"'웰러'라고 서명해라.\" 그 성을 가진 현존하는 가장 오래된 소유자가 말했다."]], [["‘Won’t do,’ said Sam.", "\"안 돼요.\" 샘이 말했다."], ["‘Never sign a walentine with your own name.’", "\"밸런타인에는 절대 자기 본명으로 서명하지 않는 법이에요.\""]], [["‘Sign it “Pickwick,” then,’ said Mr.", "\"그럼 '픽윅'이라고 서명해라.\""], ["Weller; ‘it’s a wery good name, and a easy one to spell.’", "웰러 씨가 말했다. \"아주 좋은 이름이고, 철자 쓰기도 쉽잖냐.\""]], [["The wery thing,’ said Sam.", "\"바로 그거예요.\" 샘이 말했다."], ["‘I could end with a werse; what do you think?’", "\"시 한 구절로 끝맺을 수도 있는데, 어떻게 생각하세요?\""]], [["‘I don’t like it, Sam,’ rejoined Mr.", "\"난 별로다, 샘.\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 받아 말했다."], ["‘I never know’d a respectable coachman as wrote poetry, ‘cept one, as made an affectin’ copy o’ werses the night afore he was hung for a highway robbery; and he wos only a Cambervell man, so even that’s no rule.’", "\"시 쓰는 점잖은 마부는 단 하나밖에 못 봤다. 그것도 노상강도 죄로 교수형 당하기 전날 밤에 가슴 저미는 시 한 편을 지은 놈인데, 그자도 고작 캠버웰 출신이었으니, 그것조차 통례라곤 할 수 없지.\""]], [["But Sam was not to be dissuaded from the poetical idea that had occurred to him, so he signed the letter--", "그러나 샘은 자기에게 떠오른 시적인 발상을 단념할 마음이 없었으므로, 편지에 이렇게 서명했다—"]], [["‘Your love-sick Pickwick.’", "\"상사병에 걸린 당신의 픽윅.\""]], [["And having folded it, in a very intricate manner, squeezed a downhill direction in one corner: ‘To Mary, Housemaid, at Mr.", "그러고는 아주 복잡한 방식으로 편지를 접어, 한쪽 귀퉁이에 비뚤배뚤 내리막으로 수신처를 쑤셔 넣었다."], ["Nupkins’s, Mayor’s, Ipswich, Suffolk’; and put it into his pocket, wafered, and ready for the general post.", "\"서퍽주 입스위치, 시장 너프킨스 씨 댁, 가정부 메리에게.\" 그러고는 봉인용 풀로 붙여 일반 우편으로 보낼 채비를 갖추어 주머니에 넣었다."], ["This important business having been transacted, Mr. Weller the elder proceeded to open that, on which he had summoned his son.", "이 중대한 일을 처리하고 나자, 늙은 웰러 씨는 자기가 아들을 부른 그 용건을 꺼내기 시작했다."]], [["‘The first matter relates to your governor, Sammy,’ said Mr. Weller.", "\"첫째 용건은 네 주인 나리에 관한 거다, 새미.\""], ["‘He’s a-goin’ to be tried to-morrow, ain’t he?’", "웰러 씨가 말했다. \"내일 재판을 받는다지, 그렇지?\""]], [["‘The trial’s a-comin’ on,’ replied Sam.", "\"재판이 다가오고 있죠.\" 샘이 대답했다."]], [["‘Vell,’ said Mr.", "\"음,\" 웰러 씨가 말했다."], ["Weller, ‘Now I s’pose he’ll want to call some witnesses to speak to his character, or p’rhaps to prove a alleybi.", "\"그럼 이제 나리께서 인품을 증언해 줄 증인을 좀 부르고 싶어 하실 테고, 아니면 알리바이를 입증하려 하실지도 모르지."], ["I’ve been a- turnin’ the bis’ness over in my mind, and he may make his-self easy, Sammy.", "내가 이 일을 마음속으로 곰곰이 굴려 봤는데, 나리께서 마음 푹 놓으셔도 된다, 새미."], ["I’ve got some friends as’ll do either for him, but my adwice ‘ud be this here--never mind the character, and stick to the alleybi.", "둘 중 어느 쪽이든 나리를 위해 해 줄 친구들이 나한테 있으니까. 하지만 내 충고는 이거다—인품 따위는 신경 쓰지 말고 알리바이로 밀어붙이라는 거야."], ["Nothing like a alleybi, Sammy, nothing.’ Mr.", "알리바이만 한 게 없다, 새미, 그만한 게 없어.\""], ["Weller looked very profound as he delivered this legal opinion; and burying his nose in his tumbler, winked over the top thereof, at his astonished son.", "웰러 씨는 이 법적 견해를 펼치면서 자못 심오한 표정을 지었고, 잔에 코를 처박더니 그 너머로 어리둥절해하는 아들을 향해 한쪽 눈을 찡긋했다."]], [["‘Why, what do you mean?’ said Sam; ‘you don’t think he’s a-goin’ to be tried at the Old Bailey, do you?’", "\"아니, 무슨 말씀이세요?\" 샘이 말했다. \"설마 나리가 올드 베일리에서 재판받는다고 생각하시는 건 아니죠?\""]], [["‘That ain’t no part of the present consideration, Sammy,’ replied Mr.", "\"그건 지금 고려할 사항이 아니다, 새미.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다."], ["Weller. ‘Verever he’s a-goin’ to be tried, my boy, a alleybi’s the thing to get him off.", "\"나리가 어디서 재판받든, 얘야, 그분을 빼내는 데는 알리바이가 제일이다."], ["Ve got Tom Vildspark off that ‘ere manslaughter, with a alleybi, ven all the big vigs to a man said as nothing couldn’t save him.", "우리가 톰 와일드스파크를 그 과실치사에서 알리바이로 빼냈잖냐, 그 거물 양반들이 하나같이 그놈을 구할 길이 없다고 했는데도 말이다."], ["And my ‘pinion is, Sammy, that if your governor don’t prove a alleybi, he’ll be what the Italians call reg’larly flummoxed, and that’s all about it.’", "그래서 내 생각엔, 새미, 네 주인 나리가 알리바이를 입증하지 못하면, 이탈리아 사람들 말로 아주 옴짝달싹 못 하게 될 거라는 거지, 그게 다야.\""]], [["As the elder Mr. Weller entertained a firm and unalterable conviction that the Old Bailey was the supreme court of judicature in this country, and that its rules and forms of proceeding regulated and controlled the practice of all other courts of justice whatsoever, he totally disregarded the assurances and arguments of his son, tending to show that the alibi was inadmissible; and vehemently protested that Mr.", "늙은 웰러 씨는 올드 베일리가 이 나라의 최고 사법 법원이며, 그 규칙과 소송 절차가 다른 모든 사법 법원의 관행을 규제하고 통제한다는 굳고 흔들림 없는 확신을 품고 있었으므로, 알리바이는 받아들여질 수 없다는 것을 보여 주려는 아들의 장담과 논거를 완전히 무시하고, 픽윅 씨가 \"희생양이 되고 있다\"라고 격렬히 항변했다."], ["Pickwick was being ‘wictimised.’ Finding that it was of no use to discuss the matter further, Sam changed the subject, and inquired what the second topic was, on which his revered parent wished to consult him.", "이 문제를 더 논해 봐야 소용없다는 것을 알고, 샘은 화제를 바꾸어, 자기의 존경하는 부친이 자기와 상의하고 싶어 한 두 번째 화제가 무엇인지 물었다."]], [["‘That’s a pint o’ domestic policy, Sammy,’ said Mr.", "\"그건 집안 정책에 관한 문제다, 새미.\" 웰러 씨가 말했다."], ["Weller. ‘This here Stiggins--’", "\"이 스티긴스라는 자가—\""]], [["‘Red-nosed man?’ inquired Sam.", "\"빨간 코의 사내요?\" 샘이 물었다."]], [["‘The wery same,’ replied Mr.", "\"바로 그자다.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다."], ["Weller. ‘This here red-nosed man, Sammy, wisits your mother-in-law vith a kindness and constancy I never see equalled.", "\"이 빨간 코의 사내가 말이다, 새미, 네 새어머니를 찾아오는데, 그 친절함과 한결같음이란 게 내가 일찍이 견줄 만한 걸 본 적이 없을 정도야."], ["He’s sitch a friend o’ the family, Sammy, that wen he’s avay from us, he can’t be comfortable unless he has somethin’ to remember us by.’", "어찌나 우리 집안의 벗인지, 새미, 우리한테서 떨어져 있으면 우리를 기억할 만한 뭔가를 갖지 않고선 도통 편안해하질 못한단 말이지.\""]], [["‘And I’d give him somethin’ as ‘ud turpentine and beeswax his memory for the next ten years or so, if I wos you,’ interposed Sam.", "\"제가 아버지라면 그자의 기억력을 앞으로 한 십 년쯤은 테레빈유와 밀랍으로 잘 닦아 줄 만한 걸 한 방 먹여 주겠어요.\" 샘이 끼어들었다."]], [["‘Stop a minute,’ said Mr.", "\"잠깐만.\" 웰러 씨가 말했다."], ["Weller; ‘I wos a-going to say, he always brings now, a flat bottle as holds about a pint and a half, and fills it vith the pine-apple rum afore he goes avay.’", "\"내가 하려던 말은, 그자가 이제 항상 한 파인트 반쯤 들어가는 납작한 병을 가져와서, 떠나기 전에 거기다 파인애플 럼을 채운다는 거다.\""]], [["‘And empties it afore he comes back, I s’pose?’ said Sam.", "\"그러고는 돌아오기 전에 다 비우겠죠, 아마?\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["‘Clean!’ replied Mr.", "\"싹 비우지!\""], ["Weller; ‘never leaves nothin’ in it but the cork and the smell; trust him for that, Sammy.", "웰러 씨가 대답했다. \"그 안에 코르크 마개랑 냄새 말곤 아무것도 안 남긴다, 그 점은 그자한테 맡겨 둬도 된다, 새미."], ["Now, these here fellows, my boy, are a-goin’ to-night to get up the monthly meetin’ o’ the Brick Lane Branch o’ the United Grand Junction Ebenezer Temperance Association.", "자, 그런데 이 작자들이 말이다, 얘야, 오늘 밤에 통합 대(大) 연합 에버니저 금주 협회 브릭 레인 지부의 월례 모임을 연다는 거다."], ["Your mother-in-law wos a-goin’, Sammy, but she’s got the rheumatics, and can’t; and I, Sammy--I’ve got the two tickets as wos sent her.’ Mr.", "네 새어머니가 가려고 했는데, 새미, 류머티즘에 걸려서 못 가게 됐어. 그래서 내가, 새미—그이한테 보내온 표 두 장을 내가 손에 넣었다.\""], ["Weller communicated this secret with great glee, and winked so indefatigably after doing so, that Sam began to think he must have got the Tic Doloureux in his right eyelid.", "웰러 씨는 이 비밀을 잔뜩 신이 나서 전했고, 그러고 나서 어찌나 지칠 줄 모르고 눈을 찡긋대던지, 샘은 아버지가 오른쪽 눈꺼풀에 안면 신경통이라도 걸린 게 아닌가 생각하기 시작했다."]], [["‘Well?’ said that young gentleman.", "\"그래서요?\" 그 젊은 신사가 말했다."]], [["‘Well,’ continued his progenitor, looking round him very cautiously, ‘you and I’ll go, punctiwal to the time.", "\"그래서,\" 그의 아버지가 무척 조심스레 주위를 둘러보며 이어 갔다."], ["The deputy-shepherd won’t, Sammy; the deputy-shepherd won’t.’ Here Mr.", "\"너랑 나랑 시간 딱 맞춰서 가는 거다. 부목사 양반은 못 갈 거야, 새미."], ["Weller was seized with a paroxysm of chuckles, which gradually terminated in as near an approach to a choke as an elderly gentleman can, with safety, sustain.", "부목사 양반은 못 가.\" 여기서 웰러 씨는 발작적인 킬킬거림에 사로잡혔고, 그것은 차츰차츰 노신사가 안전하게 견뎌 낼 수 있는 한도에서 거의 질식에 가까운 지경에 이르렀다."]], [["‘Well, I never see sitch an old ghost in all my born days,’ exclaimed Sam, rubbing the old gentleman’s back, hard enough to set him on fire with the friction.", "\"아니, 평생 살면서 이런 늙은 도깨비는 처음 보네요.\" 샘이 노신사의 등을 어찌나 세게 문질렀던지 마찰로 불이라도 날 지경이었다."], ["‘What are you a-laughin’ at, corpilence?’", "\"뭐가 그렇게 우스워요, 뚱보 영감님?\""]], [["‘Hush!", "\"쉿!"], ["Sammy,’ said Mr.", "새미.\""], ["Weller, looking round him with increased caution, and speaking in a whisper.", "웰러 씨가 한층 조심스레 주위를 둘러보며 속삭이는 목소리로 말했다. \"옥스퍼드 로드에서 일하는 데다 온갖 수작에 도가 튼 내 친구 두 명이, 그 부목사 양반을 단단히 끌고 다니고 있다, 새미."], ["‘Two friends o’ mine, as works the Oxford Road, and is up to all kinds o’ games, has got the deputy- shepherd safe in tow, Sammy; and ven he does come to the Ebenezer Junction (vich he’s sure to do: for they’ll see him to the door, and shove him in, if necessary), he’ll be as far gone in rum-and-water, as ever he wos at the Markis o’ Granby, Dorkin’, and that’s not sayin’ a little neither.’ And with this, Mr.", "그래서 그자가 에버니저 정거장에 정말로 오게 되면(틀림없이 올 거다. 그 친구들이 그자를 문 앞까지 데려다 주고, 필요하면 떠밀어 넣을 거니까), 그자는 도킹의 마키스 오브 그랜비에서 그랬던 만큼이나 럼 워터에 흠뻑 취해 있을 거다."], ["Weller once more laughed immoderately, and once more relapsed into a state of partial suffocation, in consequence.", "그것도 결코 적게 말하는 게 아니지.\" 그러고 나서 웰러 씨는 또 한 번 걷잡을 수 없이 웃어 댔고, 그 결과 또 한 번 반쯤 질식한 상태에 빠져들었다."]], [["Nothing could have been more in accordance with Sam Weller’s feelings than the projected exposure of the real propensities and qualities of the red-nosed man; and it being very near the appointed hour of meeting, the father and son took their way at once to Brick Lane, Sam not forgetting to drop his letter into a general post-office as they walked along.", "빨간 코의 사내가 지닌 진짜 성벽과 본바탕을 까발리려는 이 계획만큼 샘 웰러의 마음에 쏙 드는 것도 없었다. 모임이 약속된 시각에 무척 임박했으므로, 부자는 곧장 브릭 레인 쪽으로 길을 잡았고, 걸어가는 동안 샘은 자기 편지를 일반 우체국에 떨어뜨리는 것을 잊지 않았다."]], [["The monthly meetings of the Brick Lane Branch of the United Grand Junction Ebenezer Temperance Association were held in a large room, pleasantly and airily situated at the top of a safe and commodious ladder.", "통합 대 연합 에버니저 금주 협회 브릭 레인 지부의 월례 모임은, 안전하고 널찍한 사다리 꼭대기에 쾌적하고 바람 잘 통하게 자리 잡은 큰 방에서 열렸다."], ["The president was the straight-walking Mr. Anthony Humm, a converted fireman, now a schoolmaster, and occasionally an itinerant preacher; and the secretary was Mr.", "의장은 곧은 걸음걸이의 앤서니 험 씨였는데, 회심한 소방관 출신으로 지금은 학교 교사이자 이따금 순회 설교자였다."], ["Jonas Mudge, chandler’s shopkeeper, an enthusiastic and disinterested vessel, who sold tea to the members.", "그리고 서기는 양초 가게 주인인 조나스 머지 씨였는데, 열성적이고 사심 없는 그릇으로, 회원들에게 차를 파는 사람이었다."], ["Previous to the commencement of business, the ladies sat upon forms, and drank tea, till such time as they considered it expedient to leave off; and a large wooden money-box was conspicuously placed upon the green baize cloth of the business-table, behind which the secretary stood, and acknowledged, with a gracious smile, every addition to the rich vein of copper which lay concealed within.", "일이 시작되기에 앞서, 부인들은 긴 의자에 앉아, 이제 그만두는 게 좋겠다 싶을 때까지 차를 마셨다. 그리고 큰 나무 돈 상자 하나가 사무용 탁자의 초록색 베이즈 천 위에 눈에 잘 띄게 놓여 있었으니, 서기는 그 뒤에 서서, 그 안에 숨겨진 풍부한 구리 광맥에 새로 더해지는 한 닢 한 닢마다 자애로운 미소로 답례했다."]], [["On this particular occasion the women drank tea to a most alarming extent; greatly to the horror of Mr.", "이 특별한 자리에서 여자들은 더없이 놀라운 정도로 차를 마셔 댔으니, 늙은 웰러 씨로서는 여간 질겁할 일이 아니었다."], ["Weller, senior, who, utterly regardless of all Sam’s admonitory nudgings, stared about him in every direction with the most undisguised astonishment.", "그는 샘이 아무리 옆구리를 쿡쿡 찔러 가며 주의를 줘도 도무지 아랑곳없이, 더없이 노골적인 경악의 표정으로 사방을 두리번거렸다."]], [["‘Sammy,’ whispered Mr.", "\"새미,\" 웰러 씨가 속삭였다."], ["Weller, ‘if some o’ these here people don’t want tappin’ to-morrow mornin’, I ain’t your father, and that’s wot it is.", "\"이 사람들 중 몇몇은 내일 아침에 마개 따 줘야 할 판 아니냐, 안 그럼 내가 네 애비가 아니다, 그게 사실이라니까."], ["Why, this here old lady next me is a-drowndin’ herself in tea.’", "아니, 내 옆에 이 노부인은 차에 빠져 죽고 있구나.\""]], [["Be quiet, can’t you?’ murmured Sam.", "\"좀 가만히 계실 수 없어요?\" 샘이 중얼거렸다."]], [["‘Sam,’ whispered Mr.", "\"샘,\""], ["Weller, a moment afterwards, in a tone of deep agitation, ‘mark my vords, my boy.", "웰러 씨가 잠시 후 깊이 동요한 어조로 속삭였다. \"내 말 명심해라, 얘야."], ["If that ‘ere secretary fellow keeps on for only five minutes more, he’ll blow hisself up with toast and water.’", "저기 저 서기 녀석이 단 오 분만 더 저러고 있으면, 토스트 워터로 제 몸을 터뜨려 버릴 거다.\""]], [["‘Well, let him, if he likes,’ replied Sam; ‘it ain’t no bis’ness o’ yourn.’", "\"그럼 그러게 두세요, 그자가 그러고 싶다면요.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"아버지가 상관할 바 아니잖아요.\""]], [["‘If this here lasts much longer, Sammy,’ said Mr.", "\"이게 좀 더 오래가면, 새미,\""], ["Weller, in the same low voice, ‘I shall feel it my duty, as a human bein’, to rise and address the cheer.", "웰러 씨가 똑같이 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"나는 인간으로서 일어나 의장한테 한마디 하는 게 내 의무라고 느낄 것 같다."], ["There’s a young ‘ooman on the next form but two, as has drunk nine breakfast cups and a half; and she’s a-swellin’ wisibly before my wery eyes.’", "두 줄 건너 긴 의자에 젊은 아가씨가 아침 찻잔으로 아홉 잔 반을 마셨는데, 내 바로 눈앞에서 눈에 띄게 부풀어 오르고 있단 말이지.\""]], [["There is little doubt that Mr. Weller would have carried his benevolent intention into immediate execution, if a great noise, occasioned by putting up the cups and saucers, had not very fortunately announced that the tea-drinking was over.", "찻잔과 받침을 치우면서 난 큰 소리가, 다행스럽게도 차 마시기가 끝났음을 알려 주지 않았더라면, 웰러 씨가 그 자비로운 의도를 당장 실행에 옮겼으리라는 것은 의심할 여지가 거의 없다."], ["The crockery having been removed, the table with the green baize cover was carried out into the centre of the room, and the business of the evening was commenced by a little emphatic man, with a bald head and drab shorts, who suddenly rushed up the ladder, at the imminent peril of snapping the two little legs incased in the drab shorts, and said--", "그릇이 치워지자, 초록색 베이즈 천을 덮은 탁자가 방 한가운데로 옮겨졌고, 그날 저녁의 일은, 대머리에 황갈색 반바지를 입은 자그마하고 힘 있는 사내가 시작했는데, 그는 황갈색 반바지에 싸인 가느다란 두 다리가 부러질 만큼 위태롭게 갑작스레 사다리를 뛰어 올라와 이렇게 말했다—"]], [["‘Ladies and gentlemen, I move our excellent brother, Mr. Anthony Humm, into the chair.’", "\"신사 숙녀 여러분, 저는 우리의 훌륭하신 형제, 앤서니 험 씨를 의장으로 추대합니다.\""]], [["The ladies waved a choice selection of pocket-handkerchiefs at this proposition; and the impetuous little man literally moved Mr.", "부인들은 이 제안에 엄선한 손수건들을 흔들어 댔다."], ["Humm into the chair, by taking him by the shoulders and thrusting him into a mahogany-frame which had once represented that article of furniture.", "그리고 그 성마른 자그마한 사내는, 험 씨의 어깨를 붙잡아 한때 의장석이라는 가구를 이루었던 마호가니 틀 속으로 밀어 넣음으로써, 말 그대로 험 씨를 의장석으로 옮겨 놓았다."], ["The waving of handkerchiefs was renewed; and Mr.", "손수건 흔들기가 다시 이어졌다."], ["Humm, who was a sleek, white-faced man, in a perpetual perspiration, bowed meekly, to the great admiration of the females, and formally took his seat. Silence was then proclaimed by the little man in the drab shorts, and Mr.", "그리고 매끄럽고 핼쑥한 얼굴에 늘 땀을 흘리는 험 씨는 온순하게 절을 했으니, 여자들의 큰 감탄을 자아냈고, 정식으로 자리에 앉았다."], ["Humm rose and said--That, with the permission of his Brick Lane Branch brothers and sisters, then and there present, the secretary would read the report of the Brick Lane Branch committee; a proposition which was again received with a demonstration of pocket-handkerchiefs.", "그러자 황갈색 반바지의 자그마한 사내가 정숙을 선포했고, 험 씨가 일어나 말했다—그곳에 모인 브릭 레인 지부의 형제자매들이 허락한다면, 서기가 브릭 레인 지부 위원회의 보고서를 낭독하겠다는 것이었다. 이 제안은 또 한 번 손수건의 시위로 환영받았다."]], [["The secretary having sneezed in a very impressive manner, and the cough which always seizes an assembly, when anything particular is going to be done, having been duly performed, the following document was read:", "서기가 자못 인상적인 방식으로 재채기를 하고, 무슨 특별한 일이 벌어지려 할 때면 으레 회중을 사로잡는 그 헛기침이 마땅히 행해진 뒤에, 다음 문서가 낭독되었다—"]], [["‘REPORT OF THE COMMITTEE OF THE BRICK LANE BRANCH OF THE UNITED GRAND JUNCTION EBENEZER TEMPERANCE ASSOCIATION", "\"통합 대 연합 에버니저 금주 협회 브릭 레인 지부 위원회 보고서"]], [["‘Your committee have pursued their grateful labours during the past month, and have the unspeakable pleasure of reporting the following additional cases of converts to Temperance.", "\"귀 위원회는 지난 한 달 동안 그 고마운 수고를 계속해 왔으며, 다음과 같이 금주로 전향한 추가 사례들을 보고하게 되어 이루 말할 수 없는 기쁨을 느끼는 바입니다."]], [["‘H.", "\"재단사 H."], ["Walker, tailor, wife, and two children.", "워커, 아내, 그리고 두 자녀. 형편이 더 나았을 때, 끊임없이 에일과 맥주를 마시는 습관이 있었음을 인정함."], ["When in better circumstances, owns to having been in the constant habit of drinking ale and beer; says he is not certain whether he did not twice a week, for twenty years, taste “dog’s nose,”", "이십 년간 일주일에 두 번 '도그스 노즈'를 맛보지 않았는지 확신할 수 없다고 함. 귀 위원회가 조사한바, 그것은 따뜻한 포터, 축축한 설탕, 진, 육두구를 섞어 만든 것이라 함(어느 나이 든 여성에게서 신음 소리와 '바로 그래요!'"], ["which your committee find upon inquiry, to be compounded of warm porter, moist sugar, gin, and nutmeg (a groan, and ‘So it is!’ from an elderly female).", "하는 외침). 지금은 실직하고 무일푼임."], ["Is now out of work and penniless; thinks it must be the porter (cheers) or the loss of the use of his right hand; is not certain which, but thinks it very likely that, if he had drunk nothing but water all his life, his fellow-workman would never have stuck a rusty needle in him, and thereby occasioned his accident (tremendous cheering).", "그게 포터 탓이 틀림없다고 생각함(환호). 아니면 오른손을 못 쓰게 된 탓이라고 함."], ["Has nothing but cold water to drink, and never feels thirsty (great applause).", "어느 쪽인지 확실치는 않으나, 평생 물밖에 마시지 않았더라면 동료 일꾼이 녹슨 바늘로 자기를 찔러 그 사고를 일으키는 일은 결코 없었으리라고 생각함(엄청난 환호). 마실 것은 찬물밖에 없으며, 결코 목마름을 느끼지 않음(큰 박수)."]], [["‘Betsy Martin, widow, one child, and one eye.", "\"과부 벳시 마틴, 자녀 하나, 외눈 하나."], ["Goes out charing and washing, by the day; never had more than one eye, but knows her mother drank bottled stout, and shouldn’t wonder if that caused it (immense cheering).", "날품으로 청소와 빨래일을 나감. 눈은 처음부터 하나밖에 없었으나, 어머니가 병맥주 스타우트를 마셨다는 것을 알고 있으며, 그게 원인이래도 놀랍지 않다고 함(엄청난 환호)."], ["Thinks it not impossible that if she had always abstained from spirits she might have had two eyes by this time (tremendous applause).", "늘 독한 술을 멀리했더라면 지금쯤 두 눈을 가졌을 수도 있다는 게 불가능한 일은 아니라고 생각함(어마어마한 박수)."], ["Used, at every place she went to, to have eighteen-pence a day, a pint of porter, and a glass of spirits; but since she became a member of the Brick Lane Branch, has always demanded three-and-sixpence (the announcement of this most interesting fact was received with deafening enthusiasm).", "일하러 가는 곳마다 하루 일 실링 육 펜스, 포터 한 파인트, 독주 한 잔을 받곤 했으나, 브릭 레인 지부 회원이 된 뒤로는 늘 삼 실링 육 펜스를 요구해 왔다고 함(이 더없이 흥미로운 사실의 발표는 귀를 멍하게 하는 열광으로 환영받음)."]], [["‘Henry Beller was for many years toast-master at various corporation dinners, during which time he drank a great deal of foreign wine; may sometimes have carried a bottle or two home with him; is not quite certain of that, but is sure if he did, that he drank the contents.", "\"헨리 벨러는 여러 해 동안 갖가지 시(市) 당국 만찬에서 건배 진행을 맡았으며, 그동안 외국산 포도주를 엄청나게 마셨음. 때로는 한두 병을 집에 가져갔을 수도 있으나, 그 점은 확실치 않다고 함."], ["Feels very low and melancholy, is very feverish, and has a constant thirst upon him; thinks it must be the wine he used to drink (cheers).", "그러나 만약 그랬다면 그 내용물을 다 마셨으리라는 것만은 확실하다고 함. 몹시 처지고 우울하며, 열이 심하고, 끊임없이 목마름에 시달림."], ["Is out of employ now; and never touches a drop of foreign wine by any chance (tremendous plaudits).", "그게 예전에 마시던 포도주 탓이 틀림없다고 생각함(환호). 지금은 실직 상태이며, 어떤 일이 있어도 외국산 포도주는 한 방울도 입에 대지 않음(엄청난 갈채)."]], [["‘Thomas Burton is purveyor of cat’s meat to the Lord Mayor and Sheriffs, and several members of the Common Council (the announcement of this gentleman’s name was received with breathless interest).", "\"토머스 버턴은 시장님과 주(州) 장관들, 그리고 시 참사회 의원 여러 명에게 고양이 먹이를 납품하는 사람임(이 신사의 이름 발표는 숨죽인 관심으로 환영받음). 나무 의족이 하나 있음."], ["Has a wooden leg; finds a wooden leg expensive, going over the stones; used to wear second-hand wooden legs, and drink a glass of hot gin-and-water regularly every night--sometimes two (deep sighs).", "돌길을 다니다 보니 나무 의족 값이 많이 든다는 것을 알게 됨. 예전엔 중고 나무 의족을 차고, 매일 밤 규칙적으로 뜨거운 진 워터 한 잔씩을—때로는 두 잔씩 마시곤 했음(깊은 한숨)."], ["Found the second-hand wooden legs split and rot very quickly; is firmly persuaded that their constitution was undermined by the gin-and-water (prolonged cheering).", "중고 나무 의족이 아주 빨리 갈라지고 썩는 것을 발견함. 그 의족들의 체질이 진 워터에 의해 약해진 것이라고 굳게 믿음(길게 이어진 환호)."], ["Buys new wooden legs now, and drinks nothing but water and weak tea.", "지금은 새 나무 의족을 사며, 물과 묽은 차밖에 마시지 않음."], ["The new legs last twice as long as the others used to do, and he attributes this solely to his temperate habits (triumphant cheers).’", "새 의족은 예전 것보다 두 배나 오래가는데, 이것이 오로지 자신의 절제하는 습관 덕분이라고 봄(의기양양한 환호).\""]], [["Anthony Humm now moved that the assembly do regale itself with a song.", "앤서니 험은 이제 회중이 노래 한 곡으로 즐기자고 발의했다."], ["With a view to their rational and moral enjoyment, Brother Mordlin had adapted the beautiful words of ‘Who hasn’t heard of a Jolly Young Waterman?’ to the tune of the Old Hundredth, which he would request them to join him in singing (great applause).", "회중의 이성적이고 도덕적인 즐거움을 위하여, 모들린 형제가 '유쾌한 젊은 뱃사공을 모르는 이 누구랴?'라는 아름다운 노랫말을 '구(舊) 백 편'의 가락에 맞춰 편곡했으니, 자기와 함께 그것을 부르는 데 동참해 주기를 청한다는 것이었다(큰 박수)."], ["He might take that opportunity of expressing his firm persuasion that the late Mr. Dibdin, seeing the errors of his former life, had written that song to show the advantages of abstinence.", "그는 이 기회를 빌려, 작고하신 디브딘 씨가 자신의 지난 삶의 잘못을 깨닫고, 절제의 이로움을 보여 주려고 그 노래를 지었다는 자신의 굳은 확신을 표명하고자 했다."], ["It was a temperance song (whirlwinds of cheers).", "그것은 금주 노래였다(회오리바람 같은 환호)."], ["The neatness of the young man’s attire, the dexterity of his feathering, the enviable state of mind which enabled him in the beautiful words of the poet, to", "젊은이의 단정한 옷차림, 노 젓는 솜씨, 그리고 시인의 아름다운 말마따나 그로 하여금"]], [["‘Row along, thinking of nothing at all,’", "\"아무 생각 없이 노 저어 가게\""]], [["all combined to prove that he must have been a water-drinker (cheers).", "한 그 부러운 마음 상태—이 모든 것이 한데 모여, 그가 물 마시는 사람임에 틀림없다는 것을 입증했다(환호)."], ["Oh, what a state of virtuous jollity! (rapturous cheering).", "오, 이 얼마나 덕스럽고 유쾌한 상태인가!(황홀한 환호)."], ["And what was the young man’s reward?", "그리고 그 젊은이가 받은 보상은 무엇이었던가?"], ["Let all young men present mark this:", "여기 계신 모든 젊은이는 이것을 새겨들으라—"]], [["‘The maidens all flocked to his boat so readily.’", "\"처녀들이 모두 그의 배로 그토록 선뜻 모여들었네.\""]], [["(Loud cheers, in which the ladies joined.) What a bright example!", "(우렁찬 환호, 거기에 부인들도 가담함.) 이 얼마나 빛나는 본보기인가!"], ["The sisterhood, the maidens, flocking round the young waterman, and urging him along the stream of duty and of temperance.", "자매들이, 처녀들이, 젊은 뱃사공 주위로 모여들어 그를 의무와 절제의 물줄기를 따라 떠밀어 주었던 것이다."], ["But, was it the maidens of humble life only, who soothed, consoled, and supported him?", "그러나 그를 어루만지고 위로하고 떠받쳐 준 것이 비천한 신분의 처녀들뿐이었던가?"], ["No!", "아니다!"]], [["‘He was always first oars with the fine city ladies.’", "\"그는 우아한 도시 숙녀들과도 늘 으뜸 노잡이였네.\""]], [["(Immense cheering.) The soft sex to a man--he begged pardon, to a female--rallied round the young waterman, and turned with disgust from the drinker of spirits (cheers).", "(엄청난 환호.) 그 연약한 성(性)이 하나같이—그는 사죄하건대, 여성이 하나같이—젊은 뱃사공 주위로 결집했고, 독주 마시는 자에게서는 진저리치며 등을 돌렸던 것이다(환호)."], ["The Brick Lane Branch brothers were watermen (cheers and laughter).", "브릭 레인 지부 형제들은 뱃사공이었다(환호와 웃음)."], ["That room was their boat; that audience were the maidens; and he (Mr. Anthony Humm), however unworthily, was ‘first oars’ (unbounded applause).", "이 방은 그들의 배요, 이 청중은 그 처녀들이요, 그리고 그(앤서니 험 씨)는, 비록 변변치 못하나마, '으뜸 노잡이'였다(끝없는 박수)."]], [["‘Wot does he mean by the soft sex, Sammy?’ inquired Mr.", "\"연약한 성이라는 게 무슨 뜻이냐, 새미?\""], ["Weller, in a whisper.", "웰러 씨가 속삭이며 물었다."]], [["‘The womin,’ said Sam, in the same tone.", "\"여자들요.\" 샘이 같은 어조로 말했다."]], [["‘He ain’t far out there, Sammy,’ replied Mr.", "\"거기선 그자가 영 틀린 말은 아니구나, 새미.\""], ["Weller; ‘they must be a soft sex--a wery soft sex, indeed--if they let themselves be gammoned by such fellers as him.’", "웰러 씨가 대답했다. \"여자들이 분명 연약한 성이긴 하지—아주 연약한 성이고말고—저런 작자들한테 속아 넘어가게 제 몸을 내맡길 정도라면 말이야.\""]], [["Any further observations from the indignant old gentleman were cut short by the announcement of the song, which Mr. Anthony Humm gave out two lines at a time, for the information of such of his hearers as were unacquainted with the legend.", "격분한 노신사의 더 이상의 논평은, 노래가 발표되면서 끊겨 버렸으니, 앤서니 험 씨는 그 노래의 사연을 모르는 청중을 위해 한 번에 두 줄씩 일러 주었다."], ["While it was being sung, the little man with the drab shorts disappeared; he returned immediately on its conclusion, and whispered Mr. Anthony Humm, with a face of the deepest importance.", "노래가 불리는 동안, 황갈색 반바지의 자그마한 사내가 사라졌다가, 노래가 끝나자마자 곧장 돌아와, 더없이 중대한 표정으로 앤서니 험 씨에게 귀엣말을 했다."]], [["‘My friends,’ said Mr.", "\"여러분,\""], ["Humm, holding up his hand in a deprecatory manner, to bespeak the silence of such of the stout old ladies as were yet a line or two behind; ‘my friends, a delegate from the Dorking Branch of our society, Brother Stiggins, attends below.’", "험 씨가, 아직 한두 줄 뒤처진 뚱뚱한 노부인들의 정숙을 청하려고 만류하는 몸짓으로 손을 들어 올리며 말했다. \"여러분, 우리 협회 도킹 지부의 대표인 스티긴스 형제가 아래에 와 계십니다.\""]], [["Out came the pocket-handkerchiefs again, in greater force than ever; for Mr.", "다시 손수건들이 나왔으니, 그 어느 때보다 기세 좋았다."], ["Stiggins was excessively popular among the female constituency of Brick Lane.", "스티긴스 씨는 브릭 레인의 여성 회원들 사이에서 대단히 인기가 높았던 것이다."]], [["‘He may approach, I think,’ said Mr.", "\"올라오셔도 될 것 같습니다.\""], ["Humm, looking round him, with a fat smile.", "험 씨가 살진 미소를 띠고 주위를 둘러보며 말했다."], ["‘Brother Tadger, let him come forth and greet us.’", "\"태저 형제, 그분을 모셔 올라와 우리에게 인사시켜 주세요.\""]], [["The little man in the drab shorts who answered to the name of Brother Tadger, bustled down the ladder with great speed, and was immediately afterwards heard tumbling up with the Reverend Mr. Stiggins.", "태저 형제라는 이름에 답한 황갈색 반바지의 자그마한 사내는 무척 빠르게 사다리를 부산히 내려갔고, 곧이어 스티긴스 목사와 함께 비틀비틀 올라오는 소리가 들렸다."]], [["‘He’s a-comin’, Sammy,’ whispered Mr.", "\"올라온다, 새미.\""], ["Weller, purple in the countenance with suppressed laughter.", "웰러 씨가 억누른 웃음으로 얼굴이 시뻘게져 속삭였다."]], [["‘Don’t say nothin’ to me,’ replied Sam, ‘for I can’t bear it.", "\"저한테 아무 말도 하지 마세요.\" 샘이 대답했다."], ["He’s close to the door.", "\"도저히 못 참겠으니까요."], ["I hear him a-knockin’ his head again the lath and plaster now.’", "그자가 문 바로 앞에 와 있네요. 지금 욋가지하고 회반죽 벽에 머리를 쿵쿵 박는 소리가 들려요.\""]], [["As Sam Weller spoke, the little door flew open, and Brother Tadger appeared, closely followed by the Reverend Mr.", "샘 웰러가 말하는 사이, 작은 문이 활짝 열리며 태저 형제가 나타났고, 바로 뒤에 스티긴스 목사가 따라왔다."], ["Stiggins, who no sooner entered, than there was a great clapping of hands, and stamping of feet, and flourishing of handkerchiefs; to all of which manifestations of delight, Brother Stiggins returned no other acknowledgment than staring with a wild eye, and a fixed smile, at the extreme top of the wick of the candle on the table, swaying his body to and fro, meanwhile, in a very unsteady and uncertain manner.", "그가 들어서기가 무섭게 요란한 박수와 발 구르는 소리와 손수건 흔들기가 터져 나왔다. 그러나 이 모든 환희의 표시에 스티긴스 형제는, 탁자 위 촛불 심지의 맨 꼭대기를 광기 어린 눈과 굳은 미소로 응시하며, 그러는 동안 자기 몸을 무척 불안하고 종잡을 수 없는 모양새로 앞뒤로 흔드는 것 말고는 아무런 답례도 하지 않았다."]], [["‘Are you unwell, Brother Stiggins?’ whispered Mr.", "\"몸이 편찮으십니까, 스티긴스 형제?\""], ["Anthony Humm.", "앤서니 험 씨가 속삭였다."]], [["‘I am all right, Sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"멀쩡합니다, 선생.\""], ["Stiggins, in a tone in which ferocity was blended with an extreme thickness of utterance; ‘I am all right, Sir.’", "스티긴스 씨가, 사나움과 극도로 굳은 발음이 뒤섞인 어조로 대답했다. \"멀쩡하다고요, 선생.\""]], [["‘Oh, very well,’ rejoined Mr.", "\"오, 그렇군요.\""], ["Anthony Humm, retreating a few paces.", "앤서니 험 씨가 몇 걸음 물러서며 받아 말했다."]], [["‘I believe no man here has ventured to say that I am not all right, Sir?’ said Mr.", "\"여기 있는 누구도 감히 내가 멀쩡하지 않다고는 못 했을 텐데요, 선생?\""], ["Stiggins.", "스티긴스 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Oh, certainly not,’ said Mr.", "\"오, 물론입니다, 아무도요.\""], ["Humm.", "험 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘I should advise him not to, Sir; I should advise him not,’ said Mr.", "\"안 그러는 게 좋을 거요, 선생. 안 그러는 게 좋을 거라고 충고하겠소, 선생.\""], ["Stiggins.", "스티긴스 씨가 말했다."]], [["By this time the audience were perfectly silent, and waited with some anxiety for the resumption of business.", "이쯤 되자 청중은 완전히 조용해졌고, 다소 불안한 마음으로 일이 재개되기를 기다렸다."]], [["‘Will you address the meeting, brother?’ said Mr.", "\"모임을 향해 한 말씀 하시겠습니까, 형제?\""], ["Humm, with a smile of invitation.", "험 씨가 권유하는 미소를 띠고 말했다."]], [["‘No, sir,’ rejoined Mr.", "\"아니오, 선생.\" 스티긴스 씨가 받아 말했다."], ["Stiggins; ‘No, sir.", "\"아니오, 선생."], ["I will not, sir.’", "안 하겠소, 선생.\""]], [["The meeting looked at each other with raised eyelids; and a murmur of astonishment ran through the room.", "모임에 참석한 이들은 눈꺼풀을 치켜뜨고 서로를 바라보았고, 경악의 웅성거림이 방 안을 휩쓸었다."]], [["‘It’s my opinion, sir,’ said Mr.", "\"내 의견은 말이오, 선생,\""], ["Stiggins, unbuttoning his coat, and speaking very loudly--‘it’s my opinion, sir, that this meeting is drunk, sir.", "스티긴스 씨가 코트 단추를 풀며 무척 큰 소리로 말했다. \"내 의견은, 선생, 이 모임이 취해 있다는 거요, 선생."], ["Brother Tadger, sir!’ said Mr.", "태저 형제, 선생!\""], ["Stiggins, suddenly increasing in ferocity, and turning sharp round on the little man in the drab shorts, ‘you are drunk, sir!’ With this, Mr.", "스티긴스 씨가 갑자기 사나움을 더하며 황갈색 반바지의 자그마한 사내에게 홱 돌아서서 말했다. \"당신이 취했소, 선생!\""], ["Stiggins, entertaining a praiseworthy desire to promote the sobriety of the meeting, and to exclude therefrom all improper characters, hit Brother Tadger on the summit of the nose with such unerring aim, that the drab shorts disappeared like a flash of lightning.", "이렇게 말하면서 스티긴스 씨는, 모임의 금주를 도모하고 거기서 모든 부적절한 인물을 배제하려는 가상한 욕심을 품고, 태저 형제의 콧등 꼭대기를 어찌나 정확히 겨냥하여 후려쳤던지, 황갈색 반바지가 번갯불처럼 사라져 버렸다."], ["Brother Tadger had been knocked, head first, down the ladder.", "태저 형제가 머리부터 사다리 아래로 처박힌 것이다."]], [["Upon this, the women set up a loud and dismal screaming; and rushing in small parties before their favourite brothers, flung their arms around them to preserve them from danger.", "이에 여자들은 요란하고 처량한 비명을 질러 댔고, 작은 무리를 지어 자기들이 좋아하는 형제들 앞으로 달려가, 위험에서 그들을 지키려고 그들에게 두 팔을 둘러 감았다."], ["An instance of affection, which had nearly proved fatal to Humm, who, being extremely popular, was all but suffocated, by the crowd of female devotees that hung about his neck, and heaped caresses upon him.", "그러한 애정의 한 사례는 험에게 거의 치명적일 뻔했으니, 그는 워낙 인기가 많아, 그의 목에 매달려 애무를 퍼붓는 여성 신도들의 무리에 거의 질식할 지경이었다."], ["The greater part of the lights were quickly put out, and nothing but noise and confusion resounded on all sides.", "등불의 대부분이 금세 꺼졌고, 사방에 시끄러운 소리와 혼란만이 울려 퍼졌다."]], [["‘Now, Sammy,’ said Mr.", "\"자, 새미,\""], ["Weller, taking off his greatcoat with much deliberation, ‘just you step out, and fetch in a watchman.’", "웰러 씨가 무척 신중하게 외투를 벗으며 말했다. \"너 어서 나가서, 야경꾼 한 명 데려와라.\""]], [["‘And wot are you a-goin’ to do, the while?’ inquired Sam.", "\"그러는 동안 아버진 뭘 하시려고요?\" 샘이 물었다."]], [["‘Never you mind me, Sammy,’ replied the old gentleman; ‘I shall ockipy myself in havin’ a small settlement with that ‘ere Stiggins.’ Before Sam could interfere to prevent it, his heroic parent had penetrated into a remote corner of the room, and attacked the Reverend Mr.", "\"넌 신경 쓸 거 없다, 새미.\" 노신사가 대답했다."], ["Stiggins with manual dexterity.", "\"난 저기 저 스티긴스랑 자그마한 결판을 내느라 바쁠 테니까.\" 샘이 끼어들어 그것을 막을 새도 없이, 그의 영웅적인 부친은 방의 외진 구석으로 파고들어, 스티긴스 목사에게 능숙한 주먹질로 달려들었다."]], [["‘Come off!’ said Sam.", "\"그만두세요!\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["‘Come on!’ cried Mr.", "\"덤벼라!\" 웰러 씨가 외쳤다."], ["Weller; and without further invitation he gave the Reverend Mr. Stiggins a preliminary tap on the head, and began dancing round him in a buoyant and cork-like manner, which in a gentleman at his time of life was a perfect marvel to behold.", "그러고는 더 청할 것도 없이 스티긴스 목사의 머리를 한 대 선제로 톡 치고는, 그 주위를 통통 튀듯, 코르크처럼 가볍게 춤추며 돌기 시작했으니, 그 연배의 신사에게서 그것은 보기에 그야말로 경이로운 광경이었다."]], [["Finding all remonstrances unavailing, Sam pulled his hat firmly on, threw his father’s coat over his arm, and taking the old man round the waist, forcibly dragged him down the ladder, and into the street; never releasing his hold, or permitting him to stop, until they reached the corner.", "아무리 타일러도 소용없다는 것을 알고, 샘은 모자를 단단히 눌러쓰고, 아버지의 코트를 팔에 걸친 다음, 노인의 허리를 붙잡아 힘껏 사다리 아래로, 그리고 거리로 끌어냈다. 모퉁이에 이를 때까지 결코 붙든 손을 놓지도, 아버지를 멈춰 세우게 하지도 않았다."], ["As they gained it, they could hear the shouts of the populace, who were witnessing the removal of the Reverend Mr. Stiggins to strong lodgings for the night, and could hear the noise occasioned by the dispersion in various directions of the members of the Brick Lane Branch of the United Grand Junction Ebenezer Temperance Association.", "모퉁이에 다다랐을 때, 그들은 스티긴스 목사가 그날 밤 묵을 튼튼한 숙소로 끌려가는 광경을 지켜보는 군중의 외침 소리를 들을 수 있었고, 또한 통합 대 연합 에버니저 금주 협회 브릭 레인 지부 회원들이 사방으로 흩어지면서 일으키는 소란도 들을 수 있었다."]], [["CHAPTER XXXIV. IS WHOLLY DEVOTED TO A FULL AND FAITHFUL REPORT OF THE MEMORABLE TRIAL OF BARDELL AGAINST PICKWICK\nI wonder what the foreman of the jury, whoever he’ll be, has got for breakfast,’ said Mr.", "이번 2월 14일의 중대한 아침, 대화를 이어 가려는 듯 스노드그래스 씨가 입을 열었다."], ["Snodgrass, by way of keeping up a conversation on the eventful morning of the fourteenth of February.", "\"배심원장이—누가 됐든 간에—아침으로 뭘 먹었을지 궁금하군요.\""]], [["‘Ah!’ said Perker, ‘I hope he’s got a good one.’", "\"아!\" 퍼커가 말했다. \"잘 챙겨 먹었기를 바라야지요.\""]], [["Why so?’ inquired Mr.", "\"어째서요?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘Highly important--very important, my dear Sir,’ replied Perker.", "\"매우 중요한 일입니다—아주 중요한 일이지요, 친애하는 선생.\""], ["‘A good, contented, well-breakfasted juryman is a capital thing to get hold of.", "퍼커가 대답했다. \"잘 먹어 만족한 배심원을 만나는 것보다 더 좋은 일은 없습니다."], ["Discontented or hungry jurymen, my dear sir, always find for the plaintiff.’", "불만에 차 있거나 굶주린 배심원은, 친애하는 선생, 언제나 원고에게 유리한 평결을 내리거든요.\""]], [["‘Bless my heart,’ said Mr.", "\"이런 세상에.\""], ["Pickwick, looking very blank, ‘what do they do that for?’", "픽윅 씨가 몹시 멍한 표정을 지으며 말했다. \"어째서 그런답니까?\""]], [["‘Why, I don’t know,’ replied the little man coolly; ‘saves time, I suppose.", "\"글쎄요, 저도 잘 모르겠습니다.\" 작은 사내는 태연히 대답했다."], ["If it’s near dinner-time, the foreman takes out his watch when the jury has retired, and says, “Dear me, gentlemen, ten minutes to five, I declare!", "\"시간을 아끼려고 그러는 것이겠지요. 저녁 식사 시간이 가까우면, 배심원들이 물러난 뒤 배심원장이 시계를 꺼내 보고는 이러는 겁니다."], ["I dine at five, gentlemen.” “So do I,”", "'이런, 여러분, 벌써 다섯 시 십 분 전이로군요!"], ["says everybody else, except two men who ought to have dined at three and seem more than half disposed to stand out in consequence.", "나는 다섯 시에 저녁을 든답니다, 여러분.' 그러면 다들 '나도 그렇소'라고 하지요."], ["The foreman smiles, and puts up his watch:--“Well, gentlemen, what do we say, plaintiff or defendant, gentlemen?", "다만 세 시에 저녁을 들었어야 할 두 사람만은 그 때문에 끝까지 버틸 기미를 절반 넘게 보입니다. 배심원장은 빙긋 웃으며 시계를 도로 넣고 말하지요."], ["I rather think, so far as I am concerned, gentlemen,--I say, I rather think--but don’t let that influence you--I rather think the plaintiff’s the man.”", "'자, 여러분, 우리가 어떻게 정할까요, 원고요 피고요, 여러분? 내 생각으로는, 어디까지나 나로서는 말입니다, 여러분—말하건대, 내 생각엔—하지만 그게 여러분에게 영향을 미치게 하진 마십시오—내 생각엔 원고 쪽이 옳은 것 같소.'"], ["Upon this, two or three other men are sure to say that they think so too--as of course they do; and then they get on very unanimously and comfortably.", "그러면 두세 사람이 분명 자기들도 그렇게 생각한다고 말하지요—물론 그러고말고요. 그렇게 해서 아주 만장일치로, 아주 편안하게 일이 흘러가는 겁니다."], ["Ten minutes past nine!’ said the little man, looking at his watch.", "아홉 시 십 분이로군요!\" 작은 사내가 시계를 보며 말했다."], ["‘Time we were off, my dear sir; breach of promise trial-court is generally full in such cases.", "\"떠날 시간입니다, 친애하는 선생. 약혼 불이행 재판이 열리는 법정은 이런 사건엔 으레 만원이지요."], ["You had better ring for a coach, my dear sir, or we shall be rather late.’", "마차를 부르시는 게 좋겠습니다, 친애하는 선생. 안 그러면 좀 늦겠어요.\""]], [["Mr. Pickwick immediately rang the bell, and a coach having been procured, the four Pickwickians and Mr.", "픽윅 씨가 즉시 종을 울렸고, 마차가 마련되자 네 명의 픽윅 회원과 퍼커 씨가 그 안에 자리를 잡고 길드홀로 달려갔다."], ["Perker ensconced themselves therein, and drove to Guildhall; Sam Weller, Mr. Lowten, and the blue bag, following in a cab.", "샘 웰러와 라우튼 씨, 그리고 파란 가방은 삯마차를 타고 뒤따랐다."]], [["‘Lowten,’ said Perker, when they reached the outer hall of the court, ‘put Mr.", "\"라우튼,\" 법정 바깥 홀에 이르렀을 때 퍼커가 말했다."], ["Pickwick’s friends in the students’ box; Mr. Pickwick himself had better sit by me.", "\"픽윅 씨의 친구분들은 방청석에 모시고, 픽윅 씨는 내 옆에 앉으시는 게 좋겠네."], ["This way, my dear sir, this way.’ Taking Mr.", "이쪽입니다, 친애하는 선생, 이쪽으로.\""], ["Pickwick by the coat sleeve, the little man led him to the low seat just beneath the desks of the King’s Counsel, which is constructed for the convenience of attorneys, who from that spot can whisper into the ear of the leading counsel in the case, any instructions that may be necessary during the progress of the trial.", "작은 사내는 픽윅 씨의 외투 소매를 잡고, 칙선 변호사들의 책상 바로 아래에 있는 낮은 자리로 그를 이끌었다. 그곳은 사무 변호사들의 편의를 위해 마련된 자리로, 거기서는 재판이 진행되는 동안 필요한 지시 사항을 사건 주임 변호사의 귀에 속삭여 줄 수 있었다."], ["The occupants of this seat are invisible to the great body of spectators, inasmuch as they sit on a much lower level than either the barristers or the audience, whose seats are raised above the floor.", "이 자리에 앉은 사람들은 대다수 방청객의 눈에는 보이지 않았으니, 변호사들이나 청중보다 훨씬 낮은 곳에 앉아 있기 때문이었다. 변호사석이나 방청석은 바닥보다 높이 솟아 있었다."], ["Of course they have their backs to both, and their faces towards the judge.", "물론 그들은 양쪽에 등을 돌리고 판사를 마주 보고 앉아 있었다."]], [["‘That’s the witness-box, I suppose?’ said Mr.", "\"저게 증인석이겠지요?\""], ["Pickwick, pointing to a kind of pulpit, with a brass rail, on his left hand.", "픽윅 씨가 왼편에 있는, 놋쇠 난간이 달린 설교단 비슷한 것을 가리키며 말했다."]], [["‘That’s the witness-box, my dear sir,’ replied Perker, disinterring a quantity of papers from the blue bag, which Lowten had just deposited at his feet.", "\"저게 증인석입니다, 친애하는 선생.\" 라우튼이 막 발치에 내려놓은 파란 가방에서 잔뜩 서류를 파내며 퍼커가 대답했다."]], [["‘And that,’ said Mr.", "\"그러면 저건,\""], ["Pickwick, pointing to a couple of enclosed seats on his right, ‘that’s where the jurymen sit, is it not?’", "픽윅 씨가 오른편의 막힌 자리 둘을 가리키며 말했다. \"배심원들이 앉는 곳이지요, 그렇지 않습니까?\""]], [["‘The identical place, my dear Sir,’ replied Perker, tapping the lid of his snuff-box.", "\"바로 그곳입니다, 친애하는 선생.\" 퍼커가 코담배갑 뚜껑을 톡톡 두드리며 대답했다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick stood up in a state of great agitation, and took a glance at the court.", "픽윅 씨는 몹시 불안한 상태로 일어서서 법정을 한 차례 둘러보았다."], ["There were already a pretty large sprinkling of spectators in the gallery, and a numerous muster of gentlemen in wigs, in the barristers’ seats, who presented, as a body, all that pleasing and extensive variety of nose and whisker for which the Bar of England is so justly celebrated.", "방청석에는 이미 꽤 많은 구경꾼들이 흩어져 있었고, 변호사석에는 가발을 쓴 신사들이 수두룩하게 모여 있었으니, 그들은 한 무리로서 영국 법조계가 그토록 정당하게 명성을 떨치는, 그 유쾌하고도 다양한 갖가지 코와 구레나룻을 죄다 갖추고 있었다. 변론서를 들고 갈 일이 있는 신사들은 되도록 눈에 띄게 그것을 들고 다녔고, 이따금 그것으로 코를 긁어 그 사실을 더 강하게 구경꾼들의 눈에 새겨 넣었다."], ["Such of the gentlemen as had a brief to carry, carried it in as conspicuous a manner as possible, and occasionally scratched their noses therewith, to impress the fact more strongly on the observation of the spectators.", "보여 줄 변론서가 없는 다른 신사들은 뒤쪽에 빨간 딱지가 붙고, 설익은 파이 껍질 빛깔의 표지를 댄—전문 용어로 '법률용 송아지 가죽'이라 불리는—멋진 8절판 책들을 옆구리에 끼고 있었다."], ["Other gentlemen, who had no briefs to show, carried under their arms goodly octavos, with a red label behind, and that under-done-pie-crust-coloured cover, which is technically known as ‘law calf.’ Others, who had neither briefs nor books, thrust their hands into their pockets, and looked as wise as they conveniently could; others, again, moved here and there with great restlessness and earnestness of manner, content to awaken thereby the admiration and astonishment of the uninitiated strangers.", "변론서도 책도 없는 다른 이들은 두 손을 주머니에 찔러 넣고서 되도록 현명해 보이는 표정을 지었다. 또 다른 이들은 안절부절못하며 자못 진지한 태도로 여기저기 오가면서, 그것으로써 사정을 모르는 낯선 이들의 감탄과 놀라움을 자아내는 데 만족했다."], ["The whole, to the great wonderment of Mr. Pickwick, were divided into little groups, who were chatting and discussing the news of the day in the most unfeeling manner possible--just as if no trial at all were coming on.", "픽윅 씨가 크게 놀란 것은, 그 전부가 작은 무리들로 나뉘어, 마치 재판 따위는 닥쳐오지도 않는다는 듯, 더없이 무심한 태도로 그날의 소식을 떠들고 논하고 있다는 점이었다."]], [["A bow from Mr. Phunky, as he entered, and took his seat behind the row appropriated to the King’s Counsel, attracted Mr.", "펑키 씨가 들어와 칙선 변호사들에게 배정된 줄 뒤에 자리를 잡으면서 인사를 보냈고, 그것이 픽윅 씨의 주의를 끌었다."], ["Pickwick’s attention; and he had scarcely returned it, when Mr. Serjeant Snubbin appeared, followed by Mr.", "픽윅 씨가 미처 답례를 다 하기도 전에 스너빈 변호사가 나타났다."], ["Mallard, who half hid the Serjeant behind a large crimson bag, which he placed on his table, and, after shaking hands with Perker, withdrew.", "그 뒤를 맬러드 씨가 따랐는데, 그가 커다란 진홍색 가방을 책상 위에 내려놓는 바람에 변호사의 모습이 반쯤 가려졌다. 맬러드 씨는 퍼커와 악수를 나눈 뒤 물러갔다."], ["Then there entered two or three more Serjeants; and among them, one with a fat body and a red face, who nodded in a friendly manner to Mr. Serjeant Snubbin, and said it was a fine morning.", "이어서 두세 명의 변호사가 더 들어왔는데, 그 가운데 몸집이 뚱뚱하고 얼굴이 불그레한 자가 스너빈 변호사에게 다정하게 고갯짓하며 좋은 아침이라고 인사했다."]], [["‘Who’s that red-faced man, who said it was a fine morning, and nodded to our counsel?’ whispered Mr.", "\"좋은 아침이라며 우리 변호사에게 고갯짓한 저 얼굴 붉은 사람은 누굽니까?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 속삭였다."]], [["‘Mr. Serjeant Buzfuz,’ replied Perker.", "\"버즈퍼즈 변호사입니다.\""], ["‘He’s opposed to us; he leads on the other side.", "퍼커가 대답했다. \"우리 쪽 반대편이지요."], ["That gentleman behind him is Mr.", "상대측 주임 변호사입니다."], ["Skimpin, his junior.’", "그 뒤에 있는 신사는 그의 부변호사인 스킴핀 씨입니다.\""]], [["Mr. Pickwick was on the point of inquiring, with great abhorrence of the man’s cold-blooded villainy, how Mr.", "픽윅 씨는 그 사내의 냉혈한 같은 악랄함에 큰 혐오를 느끼며, 상대측 변호사인 버즈퍼즈 변호사가 어떻게 감히 자기 측 변호사인 스너빈 변호사에게 좋은 아침이라고 말할 수 있는지 막 따져 물으려던 참이었다."], ["Serjeant Buzfuz, who was counsel for the opposite party, dared to presume to tell Mr.", "그때 변호사들이 일제히 일어서고 법정 관리들이 \"정숙!\""], ["Serjeant Snubbin, who was counsel for him, that it was a fine morning, when he was interrupted by a general rising of the barristers, and a loud cry of ‘Silence!’ from the officers of the court.", "하고 큰 소리로 외치는 바람에 말이 끊겼다."], ["Looking round, he found that this was caused by the entrance of the judge.", "둘러보니 그것은 판사가 입정하기 때문이었다."]], [["Mr. Justice Stareleigh (who sat in the absence of the Chief Justice, occasioned by indisposition) was a most particularly short man, and so fat, that he seemed all face and waistcoat.", "스테어리 판사는(수석 판사가 몸이 편치 않아 자리를 비운 탓에 대신 앉은 것이었다) 유독 키가 작은 사내였고, 어찌나 뚱뚱한지 온통 얼굴과 조끼뿐인 것 같았다."], ["He rolled in, upon two little turned legs, and having bobbed gravely to the Bar, who bobbed gravely to him, put his little legs underneath his table, and his little three-cornered hat upon it; and when Mr.", "그는 깎아 만든 듯한 작은 두 다리로 굴러들어 와, 법조인들에게 엄숙히 고개를 까딱했고—그들도 그에게 엄숙히 고개를 까딱했다—작은 다리를 책상 밑에 넣고 작은 삼각모를 책상 위에 올려놓았다."], ["Justice Stareleigh had done this, all you could see of him was two queer little eyes, one broad pink face, and somewhere about half of a big and very comical-looking wig.", "스테어리 판사가 이렇게 하고 나니, 그에게서 볼 수 있는 것이라곤 괴상한 작은 두 눈과 널찍한 분홍빛 얼굴 하나, 그리고 크고 매우 우스꽝스러워 보이는 가발의 절반쯤뿐이었다."]], [["The judge had no sooner taken his seat, than the officer on the floor of the court called out ‘Silence!’ in a commanding tone, upon which another officer in the gallery cried ‘Silence!’ in an angry manner, whereupon three or four more ushers shouted ‘Silence!’ in a voice of indignant remonstrance.", "판사가 자리에 앉기 무섭게 법정 바닥에 있던 관리가 명령조로 \"정숙!\"이라 외쳤고, 그러자 방청석에 있던 또 다른 관리가 성난 투로 \"정숙!\"이라 외쳤으며, 이어서 서너 명의 정리들이 분개해 항의하는 목소리로 \"정숙!\"이라 고함쳤다."], ["This being done, a gentleman in black, who sat below the judge, proceeded to call over the names of the jury; and after a great deal of bawling, it was discovered that only ten special jurymen were present.", "이 일이 끝나자, 판사 아래에 앉아 있던 검은 옷의 신사가 배심원들의 이름을 부르기 시작했다. 한참을 외쳐 댄 끝에, 특별 배심원이 열 명밖에 출석하지 않은 사실이 드러났다."], ["Upon this, Mr. Serjeant Buzfuz prayed a tales; the gentleman in black then proceeded to press into the special jury, two of the common jurymen; and a greengrocer and a chemist were caught directly.", "이에 버즈퍼즈 변호사가 보충 배심을 청했고, 검은 옷의 신사는 일반 배심원 가운데 둘을 특별 배심에 밀어 넣었으니, 청과상 한 명과 약제사 한 명이 곧장 붙잡혔다."]], [["‘Answer to your names, gentlemen, that you may be sworn,’ said the gentleman in black.", "\"선서하실 수 있도록, 호명에 답하십시오, 여러분.\" 검은 옷의 신사가 말했다."], ["‘Richard Upwitch.’", "\"리처드 업위치.\""]], [["‘Here,’ said the greengrocer.", "\"여기 있습니다.\" 청과상이 말했다."]], [["‘Thomas Groffin.’", "\"토머스 그로핀.\""]], [["‘Here,’ said the chemist.", "\"여기 있습니다.\" 약제사가 말했다."]], [["‘Take the book, gentlemen.", "\"성경을 받으십시오, 여러분."], ["You shall well and truly try--’", "두 분은 성실하고 진실하게 심리하실 것이며—\""]], [["‘I beg this court’s pardon,’ said the chemist, who was a tall, thin, yellow-visaged man, ‘but I hope this court will excuse my attendance.’", "\"법정에 죄송합니다만,\" 키가 크고 마른, 누런 낯빛의 사내인 약제사가 말했다. \"법정께서 제 출석을 면해 주시기를 바랍니다.\""]], [["‘On what grounds, Sir?’ said Mr.", "\"무슨 근거로 그러시오, 선생?\""], ["Justice Stareleigh.", "스테어리 판사가 말했다."]], [["‘I have no assistant, my Lord,’ said the chemist.", "\"저는 조수가 없습니다, 판사님.\" 약제사가 말했다."]], [["‘I can’t help that, Sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"그건 내가 어쩔 수 없는 일이오, 선생.\""], ["Justice Stareleigh.", "스테어리 판사가 대답했다."], ["‘You should hire one.’", "\"한 사람 고용하시오.\""]], [["‘I can’t afford it, my Lord,’ rejoined the chemist.", "\"그럴 형편이 못 됩니다, 판사님.\" 약제사가 받아쳤다."]], [["‘Then you ought to be able to afford it, Sir,’ said the judge, reddening; for Mr.", "\"그러면 그럴 형편이 되어야 마땅하오, 선생.\" 판사가 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다."], ["Justice Stareleigh’s temper bordered on the irritable, and brooked not contradiction.", "스테어리 판사의 성미는 짜증에 가까웠고, 반박을 참지 못했다."]], [["‘I know I ought to do, if I got on as well as I deserved; but I don’t, my Lord,’ answered the chemist.", "\"제가 받아 마땅한 만큼 잘되었다면 마땅히 그래야겠지요. 하지만 그렇질 못합니다, 판사님.\" 약제사가 대답했다."]], [["‘Swear the gentleman,’ said the judge peremptorily.", "\"저 신사에게 선서를 시키시오.\" 판사가 단호하게 말했다."]], [["The officer had got no further than the ‘You shall well and truly try,’ when he was again interrupted by the chemist.", "관리가 \"두 분은 성실하고 진실하게 심리하실 것이며—\"라는 데까지 갔을 때, 또다시 약제사가 끼어들었다."]], [["‘I am to be sworn, my Lord, am I?’ said the chemist.", "\"저더러 선서를 하라는 거지요, 판사님? 그렇습니까?\" 약제사가 말했다."]], [["‘Certainly, sir,’ replied the testy little judge.", "\"물론이오, 선생.\" 짜증 난 작은 판사가 대답했다."]], [["‘Very well, my Lord,’ replied the chemist, in a resigned manner.", "\"좋습니다, 판사님.\" 약제사가 체념한 듯 대답했다."], ["‘Then there’ll be murder before this trial’s over; that’s all.", "\"그러면 이 재판이 끝나기 전에 살인이 날 겁니다. 그뿐입니다."], ["Swear me, if you please, Sir;’ and sworn the chemist was, before the judge could find words to utter.", "그럼 선서를 시키십시오, 부디.\" 판사가 입을 열 말을 미처 찾기도 전에 약제사는 선서를 마쳤다."]], [["‘I merely wanted to observe, my Lord,’ said the chemist, taking his seat with great deliberation, ‘that I’ve left nobody but an errand-boy in my shop. He is a very nice boy, my Lord, but he is not acquainted with drugs; and I know that the prevailing impression on his mind is, that Epsom salts means oxalic acid; and syrup of senna, laudanum. That’s all, my Lord.’ With this, the tall chemist composed himself into a comfortable attitude, and, assuming a pleasant expression of countenance, appeared to have prepared himself for the worst.", "\"제가 그저 말씀드리고 싶었던 건, 판사님,\" 약제사가 아주 신중하게 자리에 앉으며 말했다. \"제 가게에 심부름꾼 소년 하나밖에 남겨 두지 않았다는 겁니다. 아주 착한 아이지요, 판사님. 하지만 약에 대해선 잘 모릅니다. 게다가 그 아이 머릿속에는 엡솜소금이 옥살산이고, 센나 시럽이 아편팅크라는 생각이 박혀 있는 게 분명합니다. 그뿐입니다, 판사님.\" 이렇게 말하고 키 큰 약제사는 편안한 자세를 취하더니, 유쾌한 표정을 지으며 최악의 사태에 대비한 듯이 보였다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick was regarding the chemist with feelings of the deepest horror, when a slight sensation was perceptible in the body of the court; and immediately afterwards Mrs.", "픽윅 씨가 더없이 깊은 공포의 심정으로 약제사를 바라보고 있을 때, 법정 안에 가벼운 동요가 일었다."], ["Bardell, supported by Mrs. Cluppins, was led in, and placed, in a drooping state, at the other end of the seat on which Mr.", "그러더니 곧 바델 부인이 클러핀스 부인의 부축을 받으며 끌려 들어와, 픽윅 씨가 앉은 자리의 반대쪽 끝에 풀 죽은 모습으로 앉혀졌다."], ["Pickwick sat. An extra-sized umbrella was then handed in by Mr.", "이어서 도드슨 씨가 특대 크기의 우산을 건넸고, 포그 씨가 나막신 한 켤레를 건넸으니, 두 사람 모두 그 자리를 위해 더없이 동정 어린 우울한 표정을 지어 두었다."], ["Dodson, and a pair of pattens by Mr.", "그다음에 샌더스 부인이 어린 바델을 데리고 나타났다."], ["Fogg, each of whom had prepared a most sympathising and melancholy face for the occasion. Mrs.", "제 아이를 보자 바델 부인은 흠칫 놀랐다."], ["Sanders then appeared, leading in Master Bardell. At sight of her child, Mrs.", "갑자기 정신을 차린 그녀는 미친 듯이 아이에게 입을 맞췄다."], ["Bardell started; suddenly recollecting herself, she kissed him in a frantic manner; then relapsing into a state of hysterical imbecility, the good lady requested to be informed where she was. In reply to this, Mrs.", "그러더니 다시 히스테릭한 얼빠진 상태로 빠져들어, 이 선량한 부인은 자기가 어디에 있느냐고 물었다."], ["Cluppins and Mrs. Sanders turned their heads away and wept, while Messrs.", "이에 클러핀스 부인과 샌더스 부인은 고개를 돌리고 흐느꼈고, 도드슨 씨와 포그 씨는 원고에게 마음을 가라앉히라고 간청했다."], ["Dodson and Fogg entreated the plaintiff to compose herself. Serjeant Buzfuz rubbed his eyes very hard with a large white handkerchief, and gave an appealing look towards the jury, while the judge was visibly affected, and several of the beholders tried to cough down their emotion.", "버즈퍼즈 변호사는 큼지막한 흰 손수건으로 눈을 몹시 비비며 배심원들을 향해 호소하는 눈길을 던졌고, 판사는 눈에 띄게 감동한 듯했으며, 여러 방청객이 기침으로 감정을 눌러 보려 애썼다."]], [["‘Very good notion that indeed,’ whispered Perker to Mr.", "\"참으로 기막힌 착상이로군요.\" 퍼커가 픽윅 씨에게 속삭였다."], ["Pickwick.", "\"대단한 자들입니다, 저 도드슨과 포그는."], ["‘Capital fellows those Dodson and Fogg; excellent ideas of effect, my dear Sir, excellent.’", "효과를 내는 데 탁월한 발상을 지녔지요, 친애하는 선생. 탁월합니다.\""]], [["As Perker spoke, Mrs. Bardell began to recover by slow degrees, while Mrs.", "퍼커가 말하는 동안, 바델 부인은 천천히 조금씩 정신을 차리기 시작했고, 클러핀스 부인은 어린 바델의 단추들과 그 각각이 끼워질 단춧구멍을 꼼꼼히 살핀 뒤, 아이를 어머니 앞쪽 법정 바닥에 세워 놓았다—거기서라면 판사와 배심원 모두의 충분한 연민과 동정을 자아내지 않을 수 없는, 그야말로 좌중을 압도하는 위치였다."], ["Cluppins, after a careful survey of Master Bardell’s buttons and the button-holes to which they severally belonged, placed him on the floor of the court in front of his mother--a commanding position in which he could not fail to awaken the full commiseration and sympathy of both judge and jury. This was not done without considerable opposition, and many tears, on the part of the young gentleman himself, who had certain inward misgivings that the placing him within the full glare of the judge’s eye was only a formal prelude to his being immediately ordered away for instant execution, or for transportation beyond the seas, during the whole term of his natural life, at the very least.", "이 일은 적잖은 반발과, 어린 신사 본인의 숱한 눈물 없이는 이루어지지 못했으니, 아이는 자기를 판사의 눈길이 환히 쏟아지는 곳에 세워 놓는 것이, 곧 즉결 처형이나, 적어도 평생토록 바다 너머로 유배되도록 명령받기 직전의 형식적인 서막에 불과하다는 어떤 내심의 불안을 품고 있었던 것이다."]], [["‘Bardell and Pickwick,’ cried the gentleman in black, calling on the case, which stood first on the list.", "\"바델 대 픽윅.\" 검은 옷의 신사가, 명단 첫머리에 오른 사건을 호명하며 외쳤다."]], [["‘I am for the plaintiff, my Lord,’ said Mr.", "\"원고 측을 맡고 있습니다, 판사님.\""], ["Serjeant Buzfuz.", "버즈퍼즈 변호사가 말했다."]], [["‘Who is with you, Brother Buzfuz?’ said the judge.", "\"누가 함께하시오, 버즈퍼즈 형제?\" 판사가 말했다."], ["Mr. Skimpin bowed, to intimate that he was.", "스킴핀 씨가 자기라는 뜻으로 고개를 숙였다."]], [["‘I appear for the defendant, my Lord,’ said Mr.", "\"피고 측을 맡고 있습니다, 판사님.\""], ["Serjeant Snubbin.", "스너빈 변호사가 말했다."]], [["‘Anybody with you, Brother Snubbin?’ inquired the court.", "\"누가 함께하시오, 스너빈 형제?\" 법정이 물었다."]], [["‘Mr.", "\"펑키 씨입니다, 판사님.\""], ["Phunky, my Lord,’ replied Serjeant Snubbin.", "스너빈 변호사가 대답했다."]], [["‘Serjeant Buzfuz and Mr.", "\"원고 측에 버즈퍼즈 변호사와 스킴핀 씨.\" 판사가 수첩에 이름을 적으면서, 쓰는 대로 읽으며 말했다."], ["Skimpin for the plaintiff,’ said the judge, writing down the names in his note-book, and reading as he wrote; ‘for the defendant, Serjeant Snubbin and Mr. Monkey.’", "\"피고 측에 스너빈 변호사와 멍키 씨.\""]], [["‘Beg your Lordship’s pardon, Phunky.’", "\"송구하오나 판사님, 펑키입니다.\""]], [["‘Oh, very good,’ said the judge; ‘I never had the pleasure of hearing the gentleman’s name before.’ Here Mr.", "\"오, 좋소.\""], ["Phunky bowed and smiled, and the judge bowed and smiled too, and then Mr.", "판사가 말했다. \"전에는 그 신사의 이름을 들어 본 적이 없었구려.\""], ["Phunky, blushing into the very whites of his eyes, tried to look as if he didn’t know that everybody was gazing at him, a thing which no man ever succeeded in doing yet, or in all reasonable probability, ever will.", "이에 펑키 씨가 고개를 숙이며 미소를 지었고, 판사도 고개를 숙이며 미소를 지었으며, 그러고 나서 펑키 씨는 눈 흰자위까지 새빨개지면서, 모두가 자기를 쳐다보고 있다는 사실을 모르는 척하려 애썼다—이는 일찍이 그 누구도 성공한 적이 없었고, 합리적인 개연성으로 보아 앞으로도 결코 성공하지 못할 일이었다."]], [["‘Go on,’ said the judge.", "\"진행하시오.\" 판사가 말했다."]], [["The ushers again called silence, and Mr.", "정리들이 다시 정숙을 외쳤고, 스킴핀 씨가 '사건 모두진술'에 들어갔다."], ["Skimpin proceeded to ‘open the case’; and the case appeared to have very little inside it when he had opened it, for he kept such particulars as he knew, completely to himself, and sat down, after a lapse of three minutes, leaving the jury in precisely the same advanced stage of wisdom as they were in before.", "그런데 그가 모두진술을 마치고 보니 그 안에는 알맹이가 거의 없는 듯했으니, 그는 자기가 아는 자세한 내용을 전부 혼자만 간직한 채, 삼 분쯤 지나서 자리에 앉아 버렸고, 배심원들은 이전과 정확히 똑같은 정도의 지혜에 머문 채 남겨졌다."], ["Serjeant Buzfuz then rose with all the majesty and dignity which the grave nature of the proceedings demanded, and having whispered to Dodson, and conferred briefly with Fogg, pulled his gown over his shoulders, settled his wig, and addressed the jury.", "그러자 버즈퍼즈 변호사가, 이 절차의 엄숙한 성격이 요구하는 온갖 위엄과 품위를 갖추어 일어섰다. 그는 도드슨에게 속삭이고 포그와 잠시 의논한 뒤, 가운을 어깨 위로 끌어당기고 가발을 바로잡고서 배심원들에게 변론을 시작했다."]], [["Serjeant Buzfuz began by saying, that never, in the whole course of his professional experience--never, from the very first moment of his applying himself to the study and practice of the law--had he approached a case with feelings of such deep emotion, or with such a heavy sense of the responsibility imposed upon him--a responsibility, he would say, which he could never have supported, were he not buoyed up and sustained by a conviction so strong, that it amounted to positive certainty that the cause of truth and justice, or, in other words, the cause of his much-injured and most oppressed client, must prevail with the high- minded and intelligent dozen of men whom he now saw in that box before him.", "버즈퍼즈 변호사는 먼저, 자신의 직업 생활을 통틀어—법을 공부하고 실무에 임하기 시작한 바로 그 첫 순간 이래로—이토록 깊은 감회를 품고, 혹은 자신에게 지워진 책임에 대해 이토록 무거운 느낌을 안고 사건에 임한 적이 결코 없었다고 말했다. 그 책임이란, 만일 한 가지 확신—실로 적극적인 확실성에 이를 만큼 강한 확신, 곧 진실과 정의의 대의, 다시 말해 더없이 부당하게 상처 입고 더없이 억압받은 자신의 의뢰인의 대의가, 지금 자기 앞 저 배심석에서 보고 있는 고결하고 지성 있는 열두 명의 사람들 앞에서 반드시 승리하리라는 확신—에 의해 떠받쳐지고 지탱되지 않았더라면, 결코 감당할 수 없었을 책임이라고 그는 말했다."]], [["Counsel usually begin in this way, because it puts the jury on the very best terms with themselves, and makes them think what sharp fellows they must be.", "변호사들이 으레 이런 식으로 시작하는 까닭은, 그것이 배심원들을 더없이 흐뭇한 기분에 젖게 하여, 자기들이 얼마나 명민한 사람들인가 생각하게 만들기 때문이다."], ["A visible effect was produced immediately, several jurymen beginning to take voluminous notes with the utmost eagerness.", "그 효과는 즉시 눈에 띄게 나타났으니, 몇몇 배심원은 더없이 열심히 장황한 기록을 적기 시작했다."]], [["‘You have heard from my learned friend, gentlemen,’ continued Serjeant Buzfuz, well knowing that, from the learned friend alluded to, the gentlemen of the jury had heard just nothing at all--‘you have heard from my learned friend, gentlemen, that this is an action for a breach of promise of marriage, in which the damages are laid at £1,500.", "\"여러분은 저의 학식 있는 동료에게서 들으셨습니다.\" 버즈퍼즈 변호사가 말을 이었다."], ["But you have not heard from my learned friend, inasmuch as it did not come within my learned friend’s province to tell you, what are the facts and circumstances of the case.", "사실 방금 언급한 그 학식 있는 동료에게서 배심원 여러분이 들은 것이라곤 아무것도 없다는 사실을 그는 익히 알고 있었다. \"여러분은 저의 학식 있는 동료에게서, 이것이 약혼 불이행에 관한 소송이며, 그 손해 배상액이 1,500파운드로 산정되어 있다는 것을 들으셨습니다."], ["Those facts and circumstances, gentlemen, you shall hear detailed by me, and proved by the unimpeachable female whom I will place in that box before you.’", "그러나 여러분은 저의 학식 있는 동료에게서, 이 사건의 사실과 정황이 무엇인지는 듣지 못하셨으니, 그것을 말씀드리는 것은 저의 학식 있는 동료의 소관이 아니었기 때문입니다. 그 사실과 정황을, 여러분, 제가 상세히 진술해 드리고, 또 제가 저 증인석에 세울 흠잡을 데 없는 부인을 통해 입증해 보이겠습니다.\""]], [["Here, Mr.", "여기서 버즈퍼즈 변호사는 '증인석'이라는 말에 엄청난 강세를 주며 책상을 힘껏 내리쳐 우렁찬 소리를 냈고, 도드슨과 포그를 흘끗 보았다."], ["Serjeant Buzfuz, with a tremendous emphasis on the word ‘box,’ smote his table with a mighty sound, and glanced at Dodson and Fogg, who nodded admiration of the Serjeant, and indignant defiance of the defendant.", "두 사람은 변호사에 대한 감탄과 피고에 대한 분개 어린 적의를 담아 고개를 끄덕였다."]], [["‘The plaintiff, gentlemen,’ continued Serjeant Buzfuz, in a soft and melancholy voice, ‘the plaintiff is a widow; yes, gentlemen, a widow.", "\"원고는, 여러분,\" 버즈퍼즈 변호사가 부드럽고 우울한 목소리로 말을 이었다."], ["The late Mr.", "\"원고는 미망인입니다."], ["Bardell, after enjoying, for many years, the esteem and confidence of his sovereign, as one of the guardians of his royal revenues, glided almost imperceptibly from the world, to seek elsewhere for that repose and peace which a custom-house can never afford.’", "그렇습니다, 여러분, 미망인이지요. 고인이 된 바델 씨는 여러 해 동안 국왕의 세입을 지키는 수호자의 한 사람으로서 군주의 존경과 신임을 누리다가, 거의 알아차릴 수 없을 만큼 조용히 이 세상을 빠져나가, 세관 따위는 결코 줄 수 없는 그 안식과 평화를 다른 곳에서 구하고자 떠났습니다.\""]], [["At this pathetic description of the decease of Mr. Bardell, who had been knocked on the head with a quart-pot in a public-house cellar, the learned serjeant’s voice faltered, and he proceeded, with emotion--", "선술집 지하 저장고에서 일 쿼트들이 술병에 머리를 얻어맞아 죽은 바델 씨의 이 비통한 사망 묘사 대목에서, 학식 있는 변호사의 목소리는 떨렸고, 그는 북받치는 감정으로 말을 이었다—"]], [["‘Some time before his death, he had stamped his likeness upon a little boy. With this little boy, the only pledge of her departed exciseman, Mrs.", "\"그는 죽기 얼마 전, 한 어린 사내아이에게 자신의 모습을 새겨 놓았습니다."], ["Bardell shrank from the world, and courted the retirement and tranquillity of Goswell Street; and here she placed in her front parlour window a written placard, bearing this inscription--“Apartments furnished for a single gentleman.", "떠난 세무 관리가 남긴 유일한 정표인 이 어린 사내아이와 함께, 바델 부인은 세상으로부터 움츠러들어 가스웰 거리의 은거와 평온을 찾았습니다. 그리고 그녀는 이곳 앞쪽 거실 창문에 다음과 같은 문구가 적힌 벽보를 붙였습니다—'독신 신사를 위한 가구 딸린 셋방 있음."], ["Inquire within.”’ Here Serjeant Buzfuz paused, while several gentlemen of the jury took a note of the document.", "안에서 문의 바람.'\" 여기서 버즈퍼즈 변호사는 잠시 멈추었고, 그동안 몇몇 배심원이 그 문서를 받아 적었다."]], [["‘There is no date to that, is there?’ inquired a juror.", "\"거기엔 날짜가 없지요, 그렇지요?\" 한 배심원이 물었다."]], [["‘There is no date, gentlemen,’ replied Serjeant Buzfuz; ‘but I am instructed to say that it was put in the plaintiff’s parlour window just this time three years.", "\"날짜는 없습니다, 여러분.\" 버즈퍼즈 변호사가 대답했다."], ["I entreat the attention of the jury to the wording of this document--“Apartments furnished for a single gentleman”!", "\"그러나 제가 지시받은 바로는, 그것이 원고의 거실 창문에 붙은 것은 바로 삼 년 전 이맘때였습니다."], ["Mrs. Bardell’s opinions of the opposite sex, gentlemen, were derived from a long contemplation of the inestimable qualities of her lost husband.", "저는 배심원 여러분께서 이 문서의 문구에 주목해 주시기를 간청합니다—'독신 신사를 위한 가구 딸린 셋방 있음'!"], ["She had no fear, she had no distrust, she had no suspicion; all was confidence and reliance. “Mr.", "바델 부인이 이성에 대해 가졌던 견해는, 여러분, 잃어버린 남편의 더없이 값진 품성을 오래 음미한 데서 비롯된 것입니다."], ["Bardell,” said the widow--“Mr.", "그녀에게는 두려움도, 불신도, 의심도 없었습니다."], ["Bardell was a man of honour, Mr.", "모든 것이 신뢰요 의지였지요."], ["Bardell was a man of his word, Mr. Bardell was no deceiver, Mr.", "'바델 씨는,'"], ["Bardell was once a single gentleman himself; to single gentlemen I look for protection, for assistance, for comfort, and for consolation; in single gentlemen I shall perpetually see something to remind me of what Mr.", "미망인은 말했습니다—'바델 씨는 명예로운 사람이었고, 바델 씨는 약속을 지키는 사람이었으며, 바델 씨는 남을 속이는 사람이 아니었고, 바델 씨도 한때는 독신 신사였다. 나는 독신 신사들에게서 보호와 도움과 위안과 위로를 구하리라."], ["Bardell was when he first won my young and untried affections; to a single gentleman, then, shall my lodgings be let.”", "독신 신사들에게서 나는, 바델 씨가 나의 젊고 순진한 애정을 처음 얻었을 때 어떠했는지를 떠올리게 하는 무언가를 끊임없이 보게 되리라."], ["Actuated by this beautiful and touching impulse (among the best impulses of our imperfect nature, gentlemen), the lonely and desolate widow dried her tears, furnished her first floor, caught her innocent boy to her maternal bosom, and put the bill up in her parlour window.", "그러니 내 셋방은 독신 신사에게 세놓으리라.' 이 아름답고 가슴 뭉클한 충동에 이끌려(우리 불완전한 본성 가운데 가장 선한 충동의 하나이지요, 여러분), 외롭고 쓸쓸한 미망인은 눈물을 닦고, 이 층을 꾸미고, 죄 없는 아이를 모성의 가슴에 끌어안고서, 거실 창문에 그 벽보를 붙였습니다."], ["Did it remain there long? No.", "그것이 오래 거기 붙어 있었을까요?"], ["The serpent was on the watch, the train was laid, the mine was preparing, the sapper and miner was at work.", "아닙니다. 뱀이 노리고 있었고, 화약 도화선이 깔렸으며, 지뢰가 준비되고 있었고, 공병과 굴착병이 일에 착수해 있었습니다."], ["Before the bill had been in the parlour window three days--three days, gentlemen--a being, erect upon two legs, and bearing all the outward semblance of a man, and not of a monster, knocked at the door of Mrs. Bardell’s house.", "그 벽보가 거실 창문에 붙은 지 사흘도 채 되지 않아—사흘입니다, 여러분—두 다리로 곧추선, 괴물이 아니라 사람의 온갖 겉모습을 갖춘 한 존재가 바델 부인 집의 문을 두드렸습니다."], ["He inquired within--he took the lodgings; and on the very next day he entered into possession of them.", "그는 안에서 문의했고—그 셋방을 빌렸습니다. 그리고 바로 그 이튿날 그곳을 차지하게 되었지요."], ["This man was Pickwick--Pickwick, the defendant.’", "이 사내가 바로 픽윅—피고 픽윅이었습니다.\""]], [["Serjeant Buzfuz, who had proceeded with such volubility that his face was perfectly crimson, here paused for breath.", "버즈퍼즈 변호사는 어찌나 청산유수로 진행했던지 얼굴이 완전히 진홍빛이 되어, 여기서 숨을 돌리려 멈추었다."], ["The silence awoke Mr. Justice Stareleigh, who immediately wrote down something with a pen without any ink in it, and looked unusually profound, to impress the jury with the belief that he always thought most deeply with his eyes shut.", "그 정적이 스테어리 판사를 깨웠고, 판사는 즉시 잉크도 묻히지 않은 펜으로 뭔가를 적으며, 자기는 늘 눈을 감을 때 가장 깊이 생각한다는 믿음을 배심원들에게 심어 주려고 유난히 심오한 표정을 지었다."], ["Serjeant Buzfuz proceeded--", "버즈퍼즈 변호사가 말을 이었다—"]], [["‘Of this man Pickwick I will say little; the subject presents but few attractions; and I, gentlemen, am not the man, nor are you, gentlemen, the men, to delight in the contemplation of revolting heartlessness, and of systematic villainy.’", "\"이 사내 픽윅에 관해서는 별로 말하지 않겠습니다. 이 주제에는 끌릴 만한 점이 거의 없으니까요. 그리고 저는, 여러분, 그리고 여러분도, 여러분, 혐오스러운 무정함과 체계적인 악행을 음미하며 즐길 그런 사람들이 아닙니다.\""]], [["Here Mr. Pickwick, who had been writhing in silence for some time, gave a violent start, as if some vague idea of assaulting Serjeant Buzfuz, in the august presence of justice and law, suggested itself to his mind. An admonitory gesture from Perker restrained him, and he listened to the learned gentleman’s continuation with a look of indignation, which contrasted forcibly with the admiring faces of Mrs. Cluppins and Mrs. Sanders.", "여기서, 한동안 말없이 몸을 비비 꼬고 있던 픽윅 씨가 격렬하게 흠칫했으니, 마치 정의와 법의 위엄 어린 면전에서 버즈퍼즈 변호사에게 폭행을 가하겠다는 어떤 막연한 생각이 머릿속에 떠오른 듯했다. 퍼커의 만류하는 몸짓이 그를 제지했고, 그는 학식 있는 신사가 말을 잇는 것을, 클러핀스 부인과 샌더스 부인의 감탄 어린 얼굴과는 극명하게 대비되는 분개의 표정으로 듣고 있었다."]], [["‘I say systematic villainy, gentlemen,’ said Serjeant Buzfuz, looking through Mr.", "\"체계적인 악행이라고 저는 말합니다, 여러분.\" 버즈퍼즈 변호사가 픽윅 씨를 꿰뚫어 보듯 하면서, 그를 향해 쏘아붙이며 말했다."], ["Pickwick, and talking at him; ‘and when I say systematic villainy, let me tell the defendant Pickwick, if he be in court, as I am informed he is, that it would have been more decent in him, more becoming, in better judgment, and in better taste, if he had stopped away.", "\"그리고 체계적인 악행이라 말하는 김에, 피고 픽윅에게—제가 들은 바로는 그가 법정에 있다 하니—이 말을 해 두겠습니다. 그가 차라리 나오지 않았더라면 더 점잖고, 더 합당하고, 더 분별 있고, 더 품위 있는 처사였을 거라고요."], ["Let me tell him, gentlemen, that any gestures of dissent or disapprobation in which he may indulge in this court will not go down with you; that you will know how to value and how to appreciate them; and let me tell him further, as my Lord will tell you, gentlemen, that a counsel, in the discharge of his duty to his client, is neither to be intimidated nor bullied, nor put down; and that any attempt to do either the one or the other, or the first, or the last, will recoil on the head of the attempter, be he plaintiff or be he defendant, be his name Pickwick, or Noakes, or Stoakes, or Stiles, or Brown, or Thompson.’", "그리고 여러분께도 말씀드리건대, 그가 이 법정에서 부리는 그 어떤 부정이나 못마땅함의 몸짓도 여러분에게 통하지 않을 것이며, 여러분은 그것을 어떻게 평가하고 어떻게 헤아려야 하는지 아실 겁니다. 또한 그에게 더 말해 두겠습니다—판사님께서도 여러분께 말씀하시겠지만—변호사란, 의뢰인에 대한 자기 직무를 수행함에 있어, 위협받지도, 윽박질리지도, 짓눌리지도 않으며, 그 어느 하나라도, 또는 그중 처음 것이든 마지막 것이든 시도하려는 자에게는, 그가 원고이든 피고이든, 그 이름이 픽윅이든 노크스든 스토크스든 스타일스든 브라운이든 톰슨이든, 그 시도가 시도한 자의 머리 위로 되돌아오리라는 것을요.\""]], [["This little divergence from the subject in hand, had, of course, the intended effect of turning all eyes to Mr.", "이렇게 본론에서 잠시 벗어난 것은 물론 의도한 대로 모든 눈을 픽윅 씨에게 돌리는 효과를 냈다."], ["Pickwick. Serjeant Buzfuz, having partially recovered from the state of moral elevation into which he had lashed himself, resumed--", "버즈퍼즈 변호사는 스스로 채찍질해 몰아넣었던 도덕적 격앙 상태에서 어느 정도 회복하고 나서 다시 말을 이었다—"]], [["‘I shall show you, gentlemen, that for two years, Pickwick continued to reside constantly, and without interruption or intermission, at Mrs.", "\"여러분께 보여 드리겠습니다. 두 해 동안 픽윅이 끊임없이, 중단이나 단절 없이, 바델 부인의 집에 계속 거주했다는 것을요."], ["Bardell’s house. I shall show you that Mrs.", "또한 그 기간 내내 바델 부인이 그의 시중을 들고, 그의 안락을 살피고, 그의 식사를 짓고, 빨랫감이 밖으로 나갈 때면 세탁부에게 보낼 그의 속옷을 챙겨 주고, 그것이 돌아오면 깁고 말려 입을 수 있도록 손질해 주었으며, 한마디로 그의 더없는 신뢰와 믿음을 누렸다는 것도 보여 드리겠습니다."], ["Bardell, during the whole of that time, waited on him, attended to his comforts, cooked his meals, looked out his linen for the washerwoman when it went abroad, darned, aired, and prepared it for wear, when it came home, and, in short, enjoyed his fullest trust and confidence.", "또한 그가 여러 차례 그녀의 어린 아들에게 반 페니씩을, 어떤 때는 심지어 육 펜스씩을 주었다는 것을 보여 드리겠습니다. 그리고 저의 학식 있는 동료가 도저히 약화시키거나 반박할 수 없는 증언을 지닌 한 증인을 통해, 그가 한번은 그 아이의 머리를 쓰다듬으며 요즘 '앨리 토어'나 '커머니'(둘 다 이 고장 청소년들이 무척 귀히 여기는 구슬의 특정 종류라고 들었습니다)를 좀 땄느냐고 묻고는, 다음과 같은 주목할 만한 표현을 썼다는 것을 입증해 보이겠습니다."], ["I shall show you that, on many occasions, he gave halfpence, and on some occasions even sixpences, to her little boy; and I shall prove to you, by a witness whose testimony it will be impossible for my learned friend to weaken or controvert, that on one occasion he patted the boy on the head, and, after inquiring whether he had won any “alley tors”", "'또 다른 아버지가 생기면 어떻겠니?' 또한 여러분께 입증해 보이겠습니다."], ["or “commoneys”", "약 일 년 전, 픽윅이 마치 점차 제 의뢰인에게서 손을 끊으려는 의도로, 오랫동안 집을 비우기 시작했다는 것을요. 그러나 동시에 보여 드리겠습니다."], ["lately (both of which I understand to be a particular species of marbles much prized by the youth of this town), made use of this remarkable expression, “How should you like to have another father?”", "그 결심이 그때만 해도 충분히 강하지 못했거나, 혹은—그에게 그런 게 있다면—더 선한 감정이 이겼거나, 혹은 제 의뢰인의 매력과 재능이 그의 사내답지 못한 의도를 이겨 냈다는 것을, 다음 사실을 입증함으로써 보여 드리겠습니다. 한번은 그가 시골에서 돌아왔을 때, 그가 그녀에게 분명하게, 말로써 청혼했다는 것을요."], ["I shall prove to you, gentlemen, that about a year ago, Pickwick suddenly began to absent himself from home, during long intervals, as if with the intention of gradually breaking off from my client; but I shall show you also, that his resolution was not at that time sufficiently strong, or that his better feelings conquered, if better feelings he has, or that the charms and accomplishments of my client prevailed against his unmanly intentions, by proving to you, that on one occasion, when he returned from the country, he distinctly and in terms, offered her marriage: previously, however, taking special care that there would be no witness to their solemn contract; and I am in a situation to prove to you, on the testimony of three of his own friends--most unwilling witnesses, gentlemen--most unwilling witnesses--that on that morning he was discovered by them holding the plaintiff in his arms, and soothing her agitation by his caresses and endearments.’", "다만 그에 앞서, 그들의 엄숙한 약속에 증인이 없도록 각별히 신경을 썼습니다. 그리고 저는 그 자신의 친구 셋의 증언으로—더없이 마지못해 나선 증인들이지요, 여러분—더없이 마지못해 나선 증인들이지만—그날 아침 그가 원고를 두 팔에 안고서 애무와 다정한 말로 그녀의 동요를 달래고 있는 것을 그들이 발견했다는 것을 입증할 위치에 있습니다.\""]], [["A visible impression was produced upon the auditors by this part of the learned Serjeant’s address.", "학식 있는 변호사의 변론 가운데 이 대목에서 청중에게 눈에 띄는 인상이 새겨졌다."], ["Drawing forth two very small scraps of paper, he proceeded--", "그는 아주 작은 종잇조각 둘을 꺼내 들며 말을 이었다—"]], [["‘And now, gentlemen, but one word more.", "\"자, 여러분, 이제 한마디만 더 하겠습니다."], ["Two letters have passed between these parties, letters which are admitted to be in the handwriting of the defendant, and which speak volumes, indeed.", "이 두 사람 사이에 편지 두 통이 오갔으니, 피고의 필적임이 인정된 편지들로서, 실로 많은 것을 말해 줍니다."], ["The letters, too, bespeak the character of the man.", "더욱이 이 편지들은 그 사내의 성품을 드러냅니다."], ["They are not open, fervent, eloquent epistles, breathing nothing but the language of affectionate attachment.", "이것들은 오로지 애정 어린 정의 언어만을 토로하는, 솔직하고 열렬하고 유려한 서한이 아닙니다."], ["They are covert, sly, underhanded communications, but, fortunately, far more conclusive than if couched in the most glowing language and the most poetic imagery--letters that must be viewed with a cautious and suspicious eye--letters that were evidently intended at the time, by Pickwick, to mislead and delude any third parties into whose hands they might fall.", "은밀하고, 교활하고, 음흉한 통신이지요. 그러나 다행히도, 더없이 불타는 듯한 언어와 더없이 시적인 표현으로 적혀 있을 때보다 훨씬 더 결정적입니다—조심스럽고 의심 어린 눈으로 살펴야 할 편지들이요—픽윅이 당시에, 그것이 손에 들어갈 수 있는 제삼자라면 누구든 오도하고 현혹하려고 명백히 의도한 편지들입니다."], ["Let me read the first: “Garraways, twelve o’clock.", "첫 번째 것을 읽어 보겠습니다. '개러웨이즈, 열두 시."], ["Dear Mrs. B.--Chops and tomato sauce.", "친애하는 비 부인—양갈비와 토마토소스."], ["Yours, Pickwick.”", "그대의, 픽윅.'"], ["Gentlemen, what does this mean?", "여러분, 이게 무슨 뜻이겠습니까?"], ["Chops and tomato sauce.", "양갈비와 토마토소스."], ["Yours, Pickwick! Chops!", "그대의, 픽윅!"], ["Gracious heavens!", "양갈비라니! 맙소사!"], ["and tomato sauce!", "게다가 토마토소스라니!"], ["Gentlemen, is the happiness of a sensitive and confiding female to be trifled away, by such shallow artifices as these?", "여러분, 예민하고 사람을 믿는 한 여인의 행복이 이런 얄팍한 술책으로 농락당해도 좋단 말입니까? 다음 것에는 날짜가 전혀 없으니, 그 자체로 의심스럽습니다."], ["The next has no date whatever, which is in itself suspicious.", "'친애하는 비 부인, 내일까지는 집에 들어가지 못하겠소."], ["“Dear Mrs.", "느림보 마차.'"], ["B., I shall not be at home till to-morrow.", "그러고는 이 매우 주목할 만한 표현이 뒤따릅니다. '보온팬은 걱정하지 마시오.'"], ["Slow coach.”", "보온팬이라니!"], ["And then follows this very remarkable expression.", "아니, 여러분, 누가 보온팬 따위를 걱정한단 말입니까?"], ["“Don’t trouble yourself about the warming-pan.” The warming-pan!", "보온팬으로 인해 남자든 여자든 마음의 평화가 깨지거나 어지럽혀진 적이 언제 있었답니까?"], ["Why, gentlemen, who does trouble himself about a warming-pan?", "보온팬은 그 자체로 무해하고 유용하며, 덧붙이자면, 여러분, 위안을 주는 가재도구가 아닙니까?"], ["When was the peace of mind of man or woman broken or disturbed by a warming-pan, which is in itself a harmless, a useful, and I will add, gentlemen, a comforting article of domestic furniture? Why is Mrs.", "어째서 바델 부인은 이 보온팬에 대해 마음을 졸이지 말라고 그토록 간절히 당부받는 것일까요."], ["Bardell so earnestly entreated not to agitate herself about this warming-pan, unless (as is no doubt the case) it is a mere cover for hidden fire--a mere substitute for some endearing word or promise, agreeably to a preconcerted system of correspondence, artfully contrived by Pickwick with a view to his contemplated desertion, and which I am not in a condition to explain?", "그것이(틀림없이 그러하듯) 숨겨진 불길을 가리는 한낱 위장에 불과한 것이 아니라면—픽윅이 작정한 배신을 염두에 두고 교묘히 꾸며 낸, 미리 짜인 통신 방식에 따라, 어떤 다정한 말이나 약속을 대신하는 한낱 대용물이 아니라면 말입니다. 그리고 그것이 무엇인지는 제가 설명할 처지가 못 됩니다."], ["And what does this allusion to the slow coach mean?", "그렇다면 이 '느림보 마차' 운운하는 것은 또 무슨 뜻이겠습니까?"], ["For aught I know, it may be a reference to Pickwick himself, who has most unquestionably been a criminally slow coach during the whole of this transaction, but whose speed will now be very unexpectedly accelerated, and whose wheels, gentlemen, as he will find to his cost, will very soon be greased by you!’", "제가 아는 한, 그것은 픽윅 자신을 가리키는 것일 수 있습니다. 그는 이 사건 전체를 통틀어 두말할 나위 없이 죄스럽도록 느려 터진 마차였으나, 이제 그 속도가 매우 뜻밖에도 빨라질 것이며, 그의 수레바퀴는, 여러분, 그가 호되게 깨닫게 되겠지만, 머지않아 여러분에 의해 기름칠을 받게 될 것입니다!\""]], [["Mr.", "버즈퍼즈 변호사는 여기서 잠시 멈춰, 배심원들이 자기 농담에 미소 짓는지 살펴보았다."], ["Serjeant Buzfuz paused in this place, to see whether the jury smiled at his joke; but as nobody took it but the greengrocer, whose sensitiveness on the subject was very probably occasioned by his having subjected a chaise-cart to the process in question on that identical morning, the learned Serjeant considered it advisable to undergo a slight relapse into the dismals before he concluded.", "그러나 그것을 알아들은 자라곤 청과상뿐이었으니—그가 이 문제에 예민했던 것은 십중팔구 바로 그날 아침에 자기 손수레에 바로 그 작업을 했던 탓이리라—학식 있는 변호사는 변론을 마무리하기 전에 잠시 다시 침울한 기분에 빠져드는 것이 상책이라 여겼다."]], [["‘But enough of this, gentlemen,’ said Mr.", "\"하지만 이 이야기는 이만하면 됐습니다, 여러분.\" 버즈퍼즈 변호사가 말했다."], ["Serjeant Buzfuz, ‘it is difficult to smile with an aching heart; it is ill jesting when our deepest sympathies are awakened.", "\"아픈 마음으로 미소 짓기란 어려운 일이지요. 우리의 가장 깊은 연민이 일깨워질 때 농담하기란 온당치 못한 일입니다."], ["My client’s hopes and prospects are ruined, and it is no figure of speech to say that her occupation is gone indeed.", "제 의뢰인의 희망과 앞날은 망가졌으니, 그녀의 생업이 실로 사라졌다고 해도 그것은 결코 비유적인 표현이 아닙니다."], ["The bill is down--but there is no tenant.", "벽보는 내려졌으나—세입자는 없습니다."], ["Eligible single gentlemen pass and repass--but there is no invitation for to inquire within or without.", "적격의 독신 신사들이 오가지만—안에서든 밖에서든 문의하라는 권유는 없습니다."], ["All is gloom and silence in the house; even the voice of the child is hushed; his infant sports are disregarded when his mother weeps; his “alley tors” and his “commoneys”", "집 안은 온통 음울함과 침묵뿐이고, 아이의 목소리마저 잠잠합니다."], ["are alike neglected; he forgets the long familiar cry of “knuckle down,”", "어머니가 우는데 그 어린것의 놀이가 무슨 소용이겠습니까."], ["and at tip-cheese, or odd and even, his hand is out.", "그의 '앨리 토어'도 '커머니'도 한결같이 내버려져 있습니다."], ["But Pickwick, gentlemen, Pickwick, the ruthless destroyer of this domestic oasis in the desert of Goswell Street--Pickwick who has choked up the well, and thrown ashes on the sward--Pickwick, who comes before you to-day with his heartless tomato sauce and warming-pans--Pickwick still rears his head with unblushing effrontery, and gazes without a sigh on the ruin he has made.", "그는 오래 친숙했던 '엄지로 받쳐!'라는 외침마저 잊었고, 막대 치기 놀이나 홀짝 놀이에서도 솜씨가 무뎌졌습니다. 그러나 픽윅은, 여러분, 픽윅은, 가스웰 거리라는 사막의 이 가정이라는 오아시스를 무자비하게 파괴한 자—우물을 막아 버리고, 잔디밭에 재를 끼얹은 픽윅—그 무정한 토마토소스와 보온팬을 들고 오늘 여러분 앞에 나선 픽윅—그런 픽윅이 여전히 낯빛 하나 바꾸지 않는 뻔뻔함으로 고개를 쳐들고서, 자기가 저질러 놓은 폐허를 한숨 한 번 없이 바라보고 있습니다."], ["Damages, gentlemen--heavy damages is the only punishment with which you can visit him; the only recompense you can award to my client.", "손해 배상이, 여러분—무거운 손해 배상이야말로 여러분이 그에게 내릴 수 있는 유일한 벌이요, 제 의뢰인에게 베풀 수 있는 유일한 보상입니다."], ["And for those damages she now appeals to an enlightened, a high-minded, a right- feeling, a conscientious, a dispassionate, a sympathising, a contemplative jury of her civilised countrymen.’ With this beautiful peroration, Mr.", "그리고 그 손해 배상을 위해 그녀는 이제, 계몽되고, 고결하고, 올바른 정서를 지니고, 양심적이고, 냉정하고, 동정심 있고, 사려 깊은, 문명화된 동포로 이루어진 배심원 여러분께 호소하는 바입니다.\""], ["Serjeant Buzfuz sat down, and Mr. Justice Stareleigh woke up.", "이 아름다운 결론과 함께 버즈퍼즈 변호사가 자리에 앉았고, 스테어리 판사는 잠에서 깼다."]], [["‘Call Elizabeth Cluppins,’ said Serjeant Buzfuz, rising a minute afterwards, with renewed vigour.", "\"엘리자베스 클러핀스를 부르시오.\" 버즈퍼즈 변호사가 잠시 후, 새로운 기운을 차려 일어서며 말했다."]], [["The nearest usher called for Elizabeth Tuppins; another one, at a little distance off, demanded Elizabeth Jupkins; and a third rushed in a breathless state into King Street, and screamed for Elizabeth Muffins till he was hoarse.", "가장 가까이 있던 정리가 엘리자베스 터핀스를 불렀고, 조금 떨어진 또 다른 정리가 엘리자베스 줍킨스를 찾았으며, 세 번째 정리는 숨이 차도록 킹 거리로 뛰쳐나가 목이 쉬도록 엘리자베스 머핀스를 외쳐 댔다."]], [["Meanwhile Mrs. Cluppins, with the combined assistance of Mrs. Bardell, Mrs. Sanders, Mr. Dodson, and Mr. Fogg, was hoisted into the witness- box; and when she was safely perched on the top step, Mrs. Bardell stood on the bottom one, with the pocket-handkerchief and pattens in one hand, and a glass bottle that might hold about a quarter of a pint of smelling-salts in the other, ready for any emergency. Mrs. Sanders, whose eyes were intently fixed on the judge’s face, planted herself close by, with the large umbrella, keeping her right thumb pressed on the spring with an earnest countenance, as if she were fully prepared to put it up at a moment’s notice.", "그러는 사이 클러핀스 부인은 바델 부인, 샌더스 부인, 도드슨 씨, 포그 씨의 합심한 도움으로 증인석에 올려졌다. 그녀가 맨 윗단에 무사히 자리를 잡자, 바델 부인은 맨 아랫단에 서서, 한 손에는 손수건과 나막신을, 다른 손에는 사 분의 일 파인트쯤 들어갈 각성제 병을 들고 만일의 사태에 대비했다. 판사의 얼굴에 눈을 골똘히 고정한 샌더스 부인은 커다란 우산을 들고 바짝 곁에 자리 잡았으니, 오른손 엄지로 우산 용수철을 누른 채 진지한 표정을 짓고 있어, 마치 한순간이라도 즉시 우산을 펼칠 만반의 준비가 된 듯했다."]], [["‘Mrs.", "\"클러핀스 부인,\""], ["Cluppins,’ said Serjeant Buzfuz, ‘pray compose yourself, ma’am.’ Of course, directly Mrs.", "버즈퍼즈 변호사가 말했다. \"부디 마음을 가라앉히십시오, 부인.\""], ["Cluppins was desired to compose herself, she sobbed with increased vehemence, and gave divers alarming manifestations of an approaching fainting fit, or, as she afterwards said, of her feelings being too many for her.", "물론 클러핀스 부인은 마음을 가라앉히라는 청을 받자마자 더욱 격하게 흐느꼈고, 곧 기절할 듯한—혹은 그녀가 나중에 표현한 대로, 감정이 북받쳐 주체할 수 없는—여러 가지 놀라운 징후를 보였다."]], [["‘Do you recollect, Mrs.", "\"기억하십니까, 클러핀스 부인?\""], ["Cluppins,’ said Serjeant Buzfuz, after a few unimportant questions--‘do you recollect being in Mrs.", "버즈퍼즈 변호사가 몇 가지 사소한 질문을 던진 뒤에 말했다."], ["Bardell’s back one pair of stairs, on one particular morning in July last, when she was dusting Pickwick’s apartment?’", "\"지난 7월의 어느 날 아침, 바델 부인이 픽윅 씨의 방을 청소하고 있을 때, 부인께서 바델 부인 댁 뒤편 한 층 위 방에 계셨던 것을 기억하십니까?\""]], [["‘Yes, my Lord and jury, I do,’ replied Mrs.", "\"예, 판사님과 배심원 여러분, 기억합니다.\""], ["Cluppins.", "클러핀스 부인이 대답했다."]], [["‘Mr. Pickwick’s sitting-room was the first-floor front, I believe?’", "\"픽윅 씨의 거실은 이 층 앞쪽이었지요, 제가 알기로는?\""]], [["‘Yes, it were, Sir,’ replied Mrs.", "\"예, 그랬지요, 선생님.\""], ["Cluppins.", "클러핀스 부인이 대답했다."]], [["‘What were you doing in the back room, ma’am?’ inquired the little judge.", "\"부인은 뒷방에서 무엇을 하고 계셨소?\" 작은 판사가 물었다."]], [["‘My Lord and jury,’ said Mrs.", "\"판사님과 배심원 여러분,\""], ["Cluppins, with interesting agitation, ‘I will not deceive you.’", "클러핀스 부인이 흥미로운 동요를 보이며 말했다. \"여러분을 속이지 않겠습니다.\""]], [["‘You had better not, ma’am,’ said the little judge.", "\"그러지 않는 게 좋소, 부인.\" 작은 판사가 말했다."]], [["‘I was there,’ resumed Mrs.", "\"저는 거기 있었습니다,\""], ["Cluppins, ‘unbeknown to Mrs.", "클러핀스 부인이 말을 이었다."], ["Bardell; I had been out with a little basket, gentlemen, to buy three pound of red kidney pertaties, which was three pound tuppence ha’penny, when I see Mrs.", "\"바델 부인 모르게요. 저는 작은 바구니를 들고 빨강강낭콩 감자 세 파운드를 사러 나갔었는데요, 여러분, 그게 세 파운드에 이 펜스 반이었지요."], ["Bardell’s street door on the jar.’", "그러다 바델 부인 댁 대문이 빠끔히 열려 있는 걸 봤지요.\""]], [["‘On the what?’ exclaimed the little judge.", "\"무엇 위에 열려 있다고요?\" 작은 판사가 외쳤다."]], [["‘Partly open, my Lord,’ said Serjeant Snubbin.", "\"조금 열려 있었다는 겁니다, 판사님.\" 스너빈 변호사가 말했다."]], [["‘She said on the jar,’ said the little judge, with a cunning look.", "\"부인은 빠끔히라고 했소만.\" 작은 판사가 영악한 표정으로 말했다."]], [["‘It’s all the same, my Lord,’ said Serjeant Snubbin.", "\"매한가지입니다, 판사님.\" 스너빈 변호사가 말했다."], ["The little judge looked doubtful, and said he’d make a note of it.", "작은 판사는 미심쩍은 표정을 짓더니, 그것을 기록해 두겠다고 말했다."], ["Mrs. Cluppins then resumed--", "그러자 클러핀스 부인이 다시 말을 이었다—"]], [["‘I walked in, gentlemen, just to say good-mornin’, and went, in a permiscuous manner, upstairs, and into the back room.", "\"저는 그저 안녕하시냐고 인사나 하려고 들어갔지요, 여러분. 그러고는 두서없이 위층으로, 뒷방으로 올라갔습니다."], ["Gentlemen, there was the sound of voices in the front room, and--’", "여러분, 앞방에서 말소리가 들렸는데요, 그러니까—\""]], [["‘And you listened, I believe, Mrs.", "\"그래서 부인은 엿들으셨겠지요, 그렇지요, 클러핀스 부인?\""], ["Cluppins?’ said Serjeant Buzfuz.", "버즈퍼즈 변호사가 말했다."]], [["‘Beggin’ your pardon, Sir,’ replied Mrs.", "\"외람되지만, 선생님,\""], ["Cluppins, in a majestic manner, ‘I would scorn the haction.", "클러핀스 부인이 위엄 있는 태도로 대답했다. \"저는 그런 짓을 경멸합니다."], ["The voices was very loud, Sir, and forced themselves upon my ear.’", "말소리가 아주 컸지요, 선생님. 제 귀에 절로 들이닥쳤어요.\""]], [["‘Well, Mrs.", "\"좋습니다, 클러핀스 부인."], ["Cluppins, you were not listening, but you heard the voices.", "부인은 엿들으신 게 아니라, 말소리를 들으신 거지요."], ["Was one of those voices Pickwick’s?’", "그 목소리 가운데 하나가 픽윅의 것이었습니까?\""]], [["‘Yes, it were, Sir.’ And Mrs. Cluppins, after distinctly stating that Mr.", "\"예, 그랬지요, 선생님.\""], ["Pickwick addressed himself to Mrs. Bardell, repeated by slow degrees, and by dint of many questions, the conversation with which our readers are already acquainted.", "그리고 클러핀스 부인은 픽윅 씨가 바델 부인에게 말을 건넸다고 분명히 밝힌 뒤, 우리 독자들이 이미 알고 있는 그 대화를 천천히, 숱한 질문을 거쳐 가며 되풀이했다."]], [["The jury looked suspicious, and Mr. Serjeant Buzfuz smiled as he sat down.", "배심원들은 미심쩍어하는 표정을 지었고, 버즈퍼즈 변호사는 자리에 앉으며 미소를 지었다."], ["They looked positively awful when Serjeant Snubbin intimated that he should not cross-examine the witness, for Mr. Pickwick wished it to be distinctly stated that it was due to her to say, that her account was in substance correct.", "스너빈 변호사가 이 증인을 반대 신문하지 않겠다는 뜻을 밝히자 배심원들의 표정은 그야말로 험악해졌으니, 픽윅 씨가 그녀의 진술이 본질적으로 정확하다고 말해 주는 것이 그녀에 대한 도리라는 점을 분명히 밝혀 두기를 원했기 때문이다."]], [["Mrs. Cluppins having once broken the ice, thought it a favourable opportunity for entering into a short dissertation on her own domestic affairs; so she straightway proceeded to inform the court that she was the mother of eight children at that present speaking, and that she entertained confident expectations of presenting Mr.", "일단 말문을 튼 클러핀스 부인은 이때야말로 자기 집안 사정에 관한 짤막한 논설을 펼칠 절호의 기회라 여겼다."], ["Cluppins with a ninth, somewhere about that day six months. At this interesting point, the little judge interposed most irascibly; and the effect of the interposition was, that both the worthy lady and Mrs.", "그래서 그녀는 곧장 법정을 향해, 자기가 지금 말하는 이 순간에 여덟 아이의 어머니이며, 앞으로 여섯 달쯤 뒤 그 무렵에는 클러핀스 씨에게 아홉 번째 아이를 안겨 드릴 것을 확실히 기대하고 있노라고 알리기 시작했다."], ["Sanders were politely taken out of court, under the escort of Mr. Jackson, without further parley.", "바로 이 흥미로운 대목에서 작은 판사가 더없이 짜증스럽게 끼어들었고, 그 끼어듦의 결과로, 이 훌륭한 부인과 샌더스 부인 둘 다 더 이상의 실랑이 없이 잭슨 씨의 호위 아래 정중히 법정 밖으로 데려가졌다."]], [["‘Nathaniel Winkle!’ said Mr.", "\"너새니얼 윈클!\""], ["Skimpin.", "스킴핀 씨가 외쳤다."]], [["‘Here!’ replied a feeble voice.", "\"여기 있습니다!\" 가냘픈 목소리가 대답했다."], ["Mr. Winkle entered the witness-box, and having been duly sworn, bowed to the judge with considerable deference.", "윈클 씨가 증인석에 들어서서 정식으로 선서를 마친 뒤, 자못 공손하게 판사에게 고개를 숙였다."]], [["‘Don’t look at me, Sir,’ said the judge sharply, in acknowledgment of the salute; ‘look at the jury.’", "\"날 보지 마시오, 선생.\" 판사가 그 인사에 대한 응답으로 날카롭게 말했다. \"배심원을 보시오.\""]], [["Mr.", "윈클 씨는 명령에 따라, 배심원이 있을 법한 자리라고 생각되는 곳을 바라보았다."], ["Winkle obeyed the mandate, and looked at the place where he thought it most probable the jury might be; for seeing anything in his then state of intellectual complication was wholly out of the question.", "그가 처한 그 지적 혼란 상태로는 무엇이든 분간하는 것이 전혀 불가능했기 때문이다."]], [["Mr. Winkle was then examined by Mr.", "이어서 윈클 씨는 스킴핀 씨의 신문을 받았다."], ["Skimpin, who, being a promising young man of two or three-and-forty, was of course anxious to confuse a witness who was notoriously predisposed in favour of the other side, as much as he could.", "스킴핀 씨는 마흔두세 살가량의 장래가 촉망되는 젊은이로서, 물론 상대편에 유리하게 기울어 있기로 소문난 증인을 되도록 혼란에 빠뜨리고 싶어 안달이었다."]], [["‘Now, Sir,’ said Mr.", "\"자, 선생,\""], ["Skimpin, ‘have the goodness to let his Lordship know what your name is, will you?’ and Mr.", "스킴핀 씨가 말했다. \"친절히 판사님께 선생의 이름이 무엇인지 알려 주시겠소?\""], ["Skimpin inclined his head on one side to listen with great sharpness to the answer, and glanced at the jury meanwhile, as if to imply that he rather expected Mr. Winkle’s natural taste for perjury would induce him to give some name which did not belong to him.", "그러고는 스킴핀 씨가 답을 매우 날카롭게 들으려는 듯 고개를 한쪽으로 기울였고, 그러는 동안 배심원들을 흘끗 보았으니, 마치 윈클 씨의 타고난 위증 취향이 그로 하여금 자기 것이 아닌 어떤 이름을 대게 하리라고 은근히 예상한다는 듯한 눈치였다."]], [["‘Winkle,’ replied the witness.", "\"윈클입니다.\" 증인이 대답했다."]], [["‘What’s your Christian name, Sir?’ angrily inquired the little judge.", "\"세례명은 무엇이오, 선생?\" 작은 판사가 성을 내며 물었다."]], [["‘Nathaniel, Sir.’", "\"너새니얼입니다, 선생.\""]], [["‘Daniel--any other name?’", "\"대니얼—다른 이름은 또 없소?\""]], [["‘Nathaniel, sir--my Lord, I mean.’", "\"너새니얼입니다, 선생—아니, 판사님이라는 뜻입니다.\""]], [["‘Nathaniel Daniel, or Daniel Nathaniel?’", "\"너새니얼 대니얼이오, 아니면 대니얼 너새니얼이오?\""]], [["‘No, my Lord, only Nathaniel--not Daniel at all.’", "\"아닙니다, 판사님. 그냥 너새니얼입니다—대니얼은 전혀 아닙니다.\""]], [["‘What did you tell me it was Daniel for, then, sir?’ inquired the judge.", "\"그러면 어째서 나한테 대니얼이라고 했소, 선생?\" 판사가 물었다."]], [["‘I didn’t, my Lord,’ replied Mr.", "\"그러지 않았습니다, 판사님.\""], ["Winkle.", "윈클 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘You did, Sir,’ replied the judge, with a severe frown.", "\"했소, 선생.\" 판사가 매섭게 눈살을 찌푸리며 대답했다."], ["‘How could I have got Daniel on my notes, unless you told me so, Sir?’", "\"선생이 그렇게 말하지 않았다면, 내가 어떻게 메모에 대니얼이라고 적었겠소, 선생?\""]], [["This argument was, of course, unanswerable.", "이 논리에는 물론 대꾸할 도리가 없었다."]], [["‘Mr. Winkle has rather a short memory, my Lord,’ interposed Mr.", "\"윈클 씨는 기억력이 좀 짧은 모양입니다, 판사님.\""], ["Skimpin, with another glance at the jury.", "스킴핀 씨가 다시 한번 배심원들을 흘끗 보며 끼어들었다."], ["‘We shall find means to refresh it before we have quite done with him, I dare say.’", "\"이 증인을 마저 다루기 전에 그 기억을 되살릴 방도를 찾게 되겠지요, 아마도요.\""]], [["‘You had better be careful, Sir,’ said the little judge, with a sinister look at the witness.", "\"조심하는 게 좋을 거요, 선생.\" 작은 판사가 증인을 음흉하게 노려보며 말했다."]], [["Poor Mr. Winkle bowed, and endeavoured to feign an easiness of manner, which, in his then state of confusion, gave him rather the air of a disconcerted pickpocket.", "가엾은 윈클 씨는 고개를 숙이며 태연한 척하려 애썼으나, 그가 처한 그 혼란 상태에서는 오히려 당황한 소매치기 같은 인상을 풍겼다."]], [["‘Now, Mr.", "\"자, 윈클 씨,\""], ["Winkle,’ said Mr.", "스킴핀 씨가 말했다."], ["Skimpin, ‘attend to me, if you please, Sir; and let me recommend you, for your own sake, to bear in mind his Lordship’s injunctions to be careful.", "\"부디 제 말에 주의를 기울이시오, 선생. 그리고 선생 자신을 위해, 조심하라는 판사님의 당부를 명심하시기를 권하는 바이오."], ["I believe you are a particular friend of Mr. Pickwick, the defendant, are you not?’", "선생은 피고 픽윅 씨의 각별한 친구로 아는데, 그렇지 않소?\""]], [["‘I have known Mr. Pickwick now, as well as I recollect at this moment, nearly--’", "\"제가 픽윅 씨를 알아 온 것은, 지금 이 순간 기억하기로는, 거의—\""]], [["‘Pray, Mr. Winkle, do not evade the question.", "\"제발, 윈클 씨, 질문을 회피하지 마시오."], ["Are you, or are you not, a particular friend of the defendant’s?’", "선생은 피고의 각별한 친구요, 아니오?\""]], [["‘I was just about to say, that--’", "\"제가 막 말씀드리려던 건—\""]], [["‘Will you, or will you not, answer my question, Sir?’", "\"제 질문에 답하시겠소, 안 하시겠소, 선생?\""]], [["If you don’t answer the question, you’ll be committed, Sir,’ interposed the little judge, looking over his note-book.", "\"질문에 답하지 않으면, 구금될 거요, 선생.\" 작은 판사가 자기 수첩 너머로 들여다보며 끼어들었다."]], [["‘Come, Sir,’ said Mr.", "\"자, 선생,\""], ["Skimpin, ‘yes or no, if you please.’", "스킴핀 씨가 말했다. \"예 또는 아니오로, 부디.\""]], [["‘Yes, I am,’ replied Mr.", "\"예, 그렇습니다.\""], ["Winkle.", "윈클 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘Yes, you are.", "\"예, 그렇다."], ["And why couldn’t you say that at once, Sir?", "그러면 어째서 그 말을 진작 못 했소, 선생?"], ["Perhaps you know the plaintiff too?", "어쩌면 원고도 아시겠구려?"], ["Eh, Mr. Winkle?’", "그렇지요, 윈클 씨?\""]], [["‘I don’t know her; I’ve seen her.’", "\"그분을 알지는 못합니다. 본 적은 있지요.\""]], [["‘Oh, you don’t know her, but you’ve seen her?", "\"오, 알지는 못하지만 본 적은 있다?"], ["Now, have the goodness to tell the gentlemen of the jury what you mean by that, Mr. Winkle.’", "자, 친절히 배심원 여러분께 그게 무슨 뜻인지 말씀해 주시오, 윈클 씨.\""]], [["‘I mean that I am not intimate with her, but I have seen her when I went to call on Mr. Pickwick, in Goswell Street.’", "\"제 말은, 그분과 친밀한 사이는 아니지만, 가스웰 거리로 픽윅 씨를 찾아갔을 때 본 적이 있다는 겁니다.\""]], [["‘How often have you seen her, Sir?’", "\"몇 번이나 보셨소, 선생?\""]], [["‘How often?’", "\"몇 번이냐고요?\""]], [["‘Yes, Mr. Winkle, how often?", "\"그렇소, 윈클 씨, 몇 번이오?"], ["I’ll repeat the question for you a dozen times, if you require it, Sir.’ And the learned gentleman, with a firm and steady frown, placed his hands on his hips, and smiled suspiciously to the jury.", "원하신다면 질문을 열두 번이라도 되풀이해 드리리다, 선생.\" 그러고는 학식 있는 신사가 단호하고 흔들림 없이 눈살을 찌푸린 채, 두 손을 허리에 얹고서 배심원들을 향해 미심쩍게 미소 지었다."]], [["On this question there arose the edifying brow-beating, customary on such points. First of all, Mr.", "이 질문을 두고, 이런 대목에서 으레 벌어지는 그 유익한 윽박지름이 시작되었다."], ["Winkle said it was quite impossible for him to say how many times he had seen Mrs. Bardell.", "우선 윈클 씨는 바델 부인을 몇 번 보았는지 도저히 말할 수 없다고 했다."], ["Then he was asked if he had seen her twenty times, to which he replied, ‘Certainly--more than that.’ Then he was asked whether he hadn’t seen her a hundred times-- whether he couldn’t swear that he had seen her more than fifty times-- whether he didn’t know that he had seen her at least seventy-five times, and so forth; the satisfactory conclusion which was arrived at, at last, being, that he had better take care of himself, and mind what he was about.", "그러자 스무 번은 보았느냐는 질문을 받았고, 이에 그는 \"물론이지요—그보다는 많습니다\"라고 답했다. 이어서 백 번은 보지 않았느냐—쉰 번 넘게 보았다고 맹세할 수 없느냐—적어도 일흔다섯 번은 보았다는 걸 알고 있지 않느냐는 등의 질문이 이어졌다."], ["The witness having been by these means reduced to the requisite ebb of nervous perplexity, the examination was continued as follows--", "마침내 도달한 흡족한 결론이란, 그가 몸조심을 하고 자기 처신에 유의하는 게 좋겠다는 것이었다. 이런 수단으로 증인이 필요한 만큼 신경이 곤두선 당혹 상태에 이르자, 신문은 다음과 같이 이어졌다—"]], [["‘Pray, Mr. Winkle, do you remember calling on the defendant Pickwick at these apartments in the plaintiff’s house in Goswell Street, on one particular morning, in the month of July last?’", "\"제발, 윈클 씨, 지난 7월의 어느 날 아침, 가스웰 거리에 있는 원고의 집 그 셋방으로 피고 픽윅을 찾아갔던 것을 기억하시오?\""]], [["‘Yes, I do.’", "\"예, 기억합니다.\""]], [["‘Were you accompanied on that occasion by a friend of the name of Tupman, and another by the name of Snodgrass?’", "\"그때 텁먼이라는 이름의 친구와 스노드그래스라는 이름의 또 다른 친구가 동행했소?\""]], [["‘Yes, I was.’", "\"예, 그랬습니다.\""]], [["‘Are they here?’", "\"그들이 여기 있소?\""]], [["Yes, they are,’ replied Mr.", "\"예, 있습니다.\""], ["Winkle, looking very earnestly towards the spot where his friends were stationed.", "윈클 씨가 자기 친구들이 자리한 곳을 자못 간절히 바라보며 대답했다."]], [["‘Pray attend to me, Mr. Winkle, and never mind your friends,’ said Mr.", "\"제발 제 말에 주의하시고, 친구분들은 신경 쓰지 마시오, 윈클 씨.\""], ["Skimpin, with another expressive look at the jury.", "스킴핀 씨가 다시금 배심원들을 향해 의미심장한 눈길을 보내며 말했다."], ["‘They must tell their stories without any previous consultation with you, if none has yet taken place (another look at the jury).", "\"그들은, 아직 그런 일이 없었다면, 선생과 사전에 일절 상의하지 않고 자기 이야기를 해야 하니까요(다시 배심원들을 향한 눈길)."], ["Now, Sir, tell the gentlemen of the jury what you saw on entering the defendant’s room, on this particular morning.", "자, 선생, 배심원 여러분께 이 특정한 아침에 피고의 방에 들어섰을 때 무엇을 보았는지 말씀하시오."], ["Come; out with it, Sir; we must have it, sooner or later.’", "자, 어서 털어놓으시오, 선생. 우린 늦든 빠르든 그것을 들어야 하오.\""]], [["‘The defendant, Mr. Pickwick, was holding the plaintiff in his arms, with his hands clasping her waist,’ replied Mr.", "\"피고 픽윅 씨가 원고를 두 팔로 안고서, 두 손으로 그녀의 허리를 감싸고 있었습니다.\""], ["Winkle with natural hesitation, ‘and the plaintiff appeared to have fainted away.’", "윈클 씨가 자연스러운 망설임을 보이며 대답했다. \"그리고 원고는 기절한 듯 보였습니다.\""]], [["‘Did you hear the defendant say anything?’", "\"피고가 무슨 말을 하는 것을 들었소?\""]], [["‘I heard him call Mrs. Bardell a good creature, and I heard him ask her to compose herself, for what a situation it was, if anybody should come, or words to that effect.’", "\"그가 바델 부인을 착한 사람이라 부르는 것을 들었고, 누가 들어오기라도 하면 이게 무슨 꼴이냐며, 마음을 진정하라고 그녀에게 청하는 것을, 혹은 그 비슷한 말을 들었습니다.\""]], [["‘Now, Mr. Winkle, I have only one more question to ask you, and I beg you to bear in mind his Lordship’s caution.", "\"자, 윈클 씨, 이제 한 가지만 더 묻겠소."], ["Will you undertake to swear that Pickwick, the defendant, did not say on the occasion in question-- “My dear Mrs.", "부디 판사님의 주의를 명심하시오. 선생은 문제의 그때 피고 픽윅이—'친애하는 바델 부인, 당신은 착한 사람이오."], ["Bardell, you’re a good creature; compose yourself to this situation, for to this situation you must come,” or words to that effect?’", "이 상황에 마음을 맞추시오, 당신은 이 상황에 이르게 될 테니'라거나, 그 비슷한 말을 하지 않았다고 맹세할 수 있겠소?\""]], [["‘I--I didn’t understand him so, certainly,’ said Mr.", "\"저는—저는 분명히 그렇게 이해하진 않았습니다.\""], ["Winkle, astounded on this ingenious dove-tailing of the few words he had heard.", "윈클 씨가, 자기가 들은 몇 마디 말을 이렇게 교묘하게 짜 맞춘 데에 어안이 벙벙해져서 말했다."], ["‘I was on the staircase, and couldn’t hear distinctly; the impression on my mind is--’", "\"저는 계단에 있어서 똑똑히 들을 수 없었습니다. 제 머릿속에 남은 인상으로는—\""]], [["‘The gentlemen of the jury want none of the impressions on your mind, Mr.", "\"배심원 여러분은 선생의 머릿속에 남은 인상 따위는 원치 않으십니다, 윈클 씨."], ["Winkle, which I fear would be of little service to honest, straightforward men,’ interposed Mr.", "그런 건 정직하고 솔직한 사람들에게 별 도움이 안 될 것 같으니까요.\" 스킴핀 씨가 끼어들었다."], ["Skimpin. ‘You were on the staircase, and didn’t distinctly hear; but you will not swear that Pickwick did not make use of the expressions I have quoted?", "\"선생은 계단에 있어서 똑똑히 듣지 못했다, 하지만 선생은 픽윅이 제가 인용한 그 표현을 쓰지 않았다고 맹세하지는 못하겠다?"], ["Do I understand that?’", "제가 그렇게 이해하면 되겠소?\""]], [["‘No, I will not,’ replied Mr.", "\"아니요, 못 하겠습니다.\""], ["Winkle; and down sat Mr.", "윈클 씨가 대답했다."], ["Skimpin with a triumphant countenance.", "그러자 스킴핀 씨가 의기양양한 표정으로 자리에 앉았다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick’s case had not gone off in so particularly happy a manner, up to this point, that it could very well afford to have any additional suspicion cast upon it.", "픽윅 씨의 사건은 여기까지 그다지 순조롭게 흘러오지 못한지라, 추가로 의심을 뒤집어쓸 여유 따위는 없었다."], ["But as it could afford to be placed in a rather better light, if possible, Mr. Phunky rose for the purpose of getting something important out of Mr.", "그러나 가능하다면 좀 더 나은 쪽으로 비춰 볼 여지는 있었으므로, 펑키 씨가 반대 신문에서 윈클 씨에게 무언가 중요한 것을 끌어낼 요량으로 일어섰다."], ["Winkle in cross-examination. Whether he did get anything important out of him, will immediately appear.", "그가 과연 무언가 중요한 것을 끌어냈는지는 곧 드러날 것이다."]], [["‘I believe, Mr. Winkle,’ said Mr.", "\"제가 알기로, 윈클 씨,\""], ["Phunky, ‘that Mr.", "펑키 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick is not a young man?’", "\"픽윅 씨는 젊은이가 아니지요?\""]], [["‘Oh, no,’ replied Mr.", "\"오, 아닙니다.\""], ["Winkle; ‘old enough to be my father.’", "윈클 씨가 대답했다. \"제 아버지뻘은 되십니다.\""]], [["‘You have told my learned friend that you have known Mr. Pickwick a long time.", "\"선생은 저의 학식 있는 동료에게, 픽윅 씨를 오래 알아 왔다고 말씀하셨소."], ["Had you ever any reason to suppose or believe that he was about to be married?’", "그가 결혼할 참이라고 추측하거나 믿을 만한 어떤 이유라도 있었던 적이 있소?\""]], [["‘Oh, no; certainly not;’ replied Mr.", "\"오, 아닙니다. 결코 없었지요.\""], ["Winkle with so much eagerness, that Mr. Phunky ought to have got him out of the box with all possible dispatch.", "윈클 씨가 어찌나 열을 올려 대답했던지, 펑키 씨로서는 되도록 서둘러 그를 증인석에서 내보냈어야 마땅했다."], ["Lawyers hold that there are two kinds of particularly bad witnesses--a reluctant witness, and a too-willing witness; it was Mr.", "변호사들은 유독 나쁜 증인에 두 종류가 있다고 본다—마지못해 말하는 증인과 지나치게 잘 협조하는 증인이다."], ["Winkle’s fate to figure in both characters.", "그런데 윈클 씨는 그 두 가지 역할을 다 떠맡는 것이 운명이었다."]], [["‘I will even go further than this, Mr.", "\"이보다 더 나아가 보겠습니다, 윈클 씨.\""], ["Winkle,’ continued Mr. Phunky, in a most smooth and complacent manner.", "펑키 씨가 더없이 부드럽고 흡족한 태도로 말을 이었다."], ["‘Did you ever see anything in Mr. Pickwick’s manner and conduct towards the opposite sex, to induce you to believe that he ever contemplated matrimony of late years, in any case?’", "\"선생은 픽윅 씨가 이성을 대하는 태도와 행동에서, 그가 근년 들어 어떤 경우에든 결혼을 염두에 두었다고 믿게 할 만한 무언가를 본 적이 있소?\""]], [["‘Oh, no; certainly not,’ replied Mr.", "\"오, 아닙니다. 결코 없습니다.\""], ["Winkle.", "윈클 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘Has his behaviour, when females have been in the case, always been that of a man, who, having attained a pretty advanced period of life, content with his own occupations and amusements, treats them only as a father might his daughters?’", "\"여성이 관련된 자리에서 그의 행동은 늘, 인생의 꽤 지긋한 시기에 이르러 자기 일과 즐거움에 만족하며, 여성을 오직 아버지가 딸을 대하듯 대하는 사람의 그것이었소?\""]], [["‘Not the least doubt of it,’ replied Mr.", "\"조금도 의심할 여지가 없습니다.\""], ["Winkle, in the fulness of his heart.", "윈클 씨가 진심을 다해 대답했다."], ["‘That is--yes--oh, yes--certainly.’", "\"그러니까—예—오, 예—물론입니다.\""]], [["‘You have never known anything in his behaviour towards Mrs. Bardell, or any other female, in the least degree suspicious?’ said Mr.", "\"선생은 그가 바델 부인이나 그 어떤 다른 여성을 대하는 행동에서, 조금이라도 의심스러운 점을 알아챈 적이 결코 없소?\""], ["Phunky, preparing to sit down; for Serjeant Snubbin was winking at him.", "펑키 씨가 자리에 앉으려 채비하며 말했다. 스너빈 변호사가 그에게 눈짓을 보내고 있었던 것이다."]], [["‘N-n-no,’ replied Mr.", "\"어—없—없습니다.\""], ["Winkle, ‘except on one trifling occasion, which, I have no doubt, might be easily explained.’", "윈클 씨가 대답했다. \"다만 한 가지 사소한 경우가 있긴 한데요, 그건 틀림없이 쉽게 해명될 수 있을 겁니다.\""]], [["Now, if the unfortunate Mr. Phunky had sat down when Serjeant Snubbin had winked at him, or if Serjeant Buzfuz had stopped this irregular cross-examination at the outset (which he knew better than to do; observing Mr. Winkle’s anxiety, and well knowing it would, in all probability, lead to something serviceable to him), this unfortunate admission would not have been elicited. The moment the words fell from Mr. Winkle’s lips, Mr. Phunky sat down, and Serjeant Snubbin rather hastily told him he might leave the box, which Mr. Winkle prepared to do with great readiness, when Serjeant Buzfuz stopped him.", "자, 만일 불운한 펑키 씨가 스너빈 변호사가 눈짓을 보냈을 때 자리에 앉았더라면, 혹은 버즈퍼즈 변호사가 이 변칙적인 반대 신문을 처음부터 막았더라면(그러나 그는 그러지 않을 만큼 영악했으니, 윈클 씨의 안달하는 모습을 보고서 그것이 십중팔구 자기에게 쓸모 있는 무언가로 이어지리라는 것을 익히 알았던 것이다), 이 불운한 시인은 끌려 나오지 않았을 것이다. 윈클 씨의 입에서 그 말이 떨어지는 순간, 펑키 씨는 자리에 앉았고, 스너빈 변호사는 다소 황급히 그에게 증인석에서 내려가도 좋다고 일렀다. 윈클 씨가 더없이 선뜻 그러려는 참에, 버즈퍼즈 변호사가 그를 멈춰 세웠다."]], [["‘Stay, Mr.", "\"잠깐, 윈클 씨, 잠깐!\""], ["Winkle, stay!’ said Serjeant Buzfuz, ‘will your Lordship have the goodness to ask him, what this one instance of suspicious behaviour towards females on the part of this gentleman, who is old enough to be his father, was?’", "버즈퍼즈 변호사가 말했다. \"판사님, 친절을 베푸시어, 자기 아버지뻘은 되는 이 신사가 여성을 대하는 의심스러운 행동의 그 한 가지 사례가 무엇이었는지 그에게 물어봐 주시겠습니까?\""]], [["‘You hear what the learned counsel says, Sir,’ observed the judge, turning to the miserable and agonised Mr.", "\"학식 있는 변호사가 하는 말을 들으셨소, 선생.\" 판사가 비참하고 괴로워하는 윈클 씨를 돌아보며 말했다."], ["Winkle. ‘Describe the occasion to which you refer.’", "\"선생이 말한 그 경우를 설명하시오.\""]], [["‘My Lord,’ said Mr.", "\"판사님,\""], ["Winkle, trembling with anxiety, ‘I--I’d rather not.’", "윈클 씨가 불안에 떨며 말했다. \"저—저는 말하지 않는 편이 좋겠습니다.\""]], [["‘Perhaps so,’ said the little judge; ‘but you must.’", "\"그렇겠지요.\" 작은 판사가 말했다. \"하지만 말해야 하오.\""]], [["Amid the profound silence of the whole court, Mr. Winkle faltered out, that the trifling circumstance of suspicion was Mr.", "법정 전체의 깊은 정적 속에서, 윈클 씨는 더듬더듬 털어놓았다."], ["Pickwick’s being found in a lady’s sleeping-apartment at midnight; which had terminated, he believed, in the breaking off of the projected marriage of the lady in question, and had led, he knew, to the whole party being forcibly carried before George Nupkins, Esq., magistrate and justice of the peace, for the borough of Ipswich!", "그 사소한 의심의 정황이란, 픽윅 씨가 한밤중에 어느 부인의 침실에서 발견된 일이었으며, 그 일은 그가 알기로 문제의 그 부인이 예정했던 결혼이 깨지는 것으로 끝났고, 또한 그가 아는 바로는 일행 전체가 입스위치 자치구의 치안 판사이자 치안관인 조지 너프킨스 님 앞으로 강제로 끌려가는 결과로 이어졌다는 것이었다!"]], [["‘You may leave the box, Sir,’ said Serjeant Snubbin.", "\"증인석에서 내려가도 좋소, 선생.\" 스너빈 변호사가 말했다."], ["Mr. Winkle did leave the box, and rushed with delirious haste to the George and Vulture, where he was discovered some hours after, by the waiter, groaning in a hollow and dismal manner, with his head buried beneath the sofa cushions.", "윈클 씨는 정말로 증인석에서 내려와, 정신없이 허둥지둥 조지 앤드 벌처로 달려갔으며, 몇 시간 뒤 웨이터에게 발견되었으니, 머리를 소파 쿠션 밑에 처박은 채 공허하고 음울한 소리로 끙끙대고 있었다."]], [["Tracy Tupman, and Augustus Snodgrass, were severally called into the box; both corroborated the testimony of their unhappy friend; and each was driven to the verge of desperation by excessive badgering.", "이어서 트레이시 텁먼과 오거스터스 스노드그래스가 차례로 증인석에 불려 나왔다. 둘 다 불운한 친구의 증언을 뒷받침했으며, 각자 지나친 윽박지름에 시달려 절망의 벼랑 끝으로 내몰렸다."]], [["Susannah Sanders was then called, and examined by Serjeant Buzfuz, and cross-examined by Serjeant Snubbin.", "다음으로 수재나 샌더스가 불려 나와, 버즈퍼즈 변호사의 신문을 받고 스너빈 변호사의 반대 신문을 받았다."], ["Had always said and believed that Pickwick would marry Mrs. Bardell; knew that Mrs.", "그녀는 픽윅이 바델 부인과 결혼하리라고 늘 말하고 또 믿어 왔다."], ["Bardell’s being engaged to Pickwick was the current topic of conversation in the neighbourhood, after the fainting in July; had been told it herself by Mrs. Mudberry which kept a mangle, and Mrs.", "7월의 그 기절 사건 이후, 바델 부인이 픽윅과 약혼했다는 것이 동네에 떠도는 이야깃거리였음을 알고 있었다."], ["Bunkin which clear-starched, but did not see either Mrs. Mudberry or Mrs.", "그것은 직접 다림질 일을 하는 머드베리 부인과 풀 먹이는 일을 하는 벙킨 부인에게서 들은 것이지만, 머드베리 부인도 벙킨 부인도 법정에는 보이지 않았다."], ["Bunkin in court. Had heard Pickwick ask the little boy how he should like to have another father.", "픽윅이 어린 아들에게 또 다른 아버지가 생기면 어떻겠느냐고 묻는 것을 들었다."], ["Did not know that Mrs. Bardell was at that time keeping company with the baker, but did know that the baker was then a single man and is now married.", "당시 바델 부인이 빵집 주인과 사귀고 있었는지는 몰랐으나, 그 빵집 주인이 당시에는 독신이었고 지금은 결혼했다는 것은 안다."], ["Couldn’t swear that Mrs. Bardell was not very fond of the baker, but should think that the baker was not very fond of Mrs.", "바델 부인이 그 빵집 주인을 무척 좋아하지 않았다고 단언할 수는 없으나, 빵집 주인은 바델 부인을 그리 좋아하지 않았으리라 생각하니, 그렇지 않았다면 다른 사람과 결혼하지 않았을 것이기 때문이다."], ["Bardell, or he wouldn’t have married somebody else. Thought Mrs.", "바델 부인이 7월의 그날 아침 기절한 것은 픽윅이 그녀에게 날을 잡으라고 청했기 때문이라 생각한다."], ["Bardell fainted away on the morning in July, because Pickwick asked her to name the day: knew that she (witness) fainted away stone dead when Mr. Sanders asked her to name the day, and believed that everybody as called herself a lady would do the same, under similar circumstances.", "그녀(증인)도 샌더스 씨가 날을 잡으라고 청했을 때 죽은 듯이 기절했음을 알고 있으며, 자기를 숙녀라 일컫는 사람이라면 누구든 비슷한 상황에서 똑같이 할 것이라 믿는다."], ["Heard Pickwick ask the boy the question about the marbles, but upon her oath did not know the difference between an ‘alley tor’ and a ‘commoney.’", "픽윅이 아이에게 구슬에 관한 질문을 하는 것을 들었으나, 맹세코 '앨리 토어'와 '커머니'의 차이는 모른다."]], [["By the court.--During the period of her keeping company with Mr. Sanders, had received love letters, like other ladies.", "법정의 질문에 대하여—샌더스 씨와 사귀던 동안 다른 숙녀들처럼 연애편지를 받았다."], ["In the course of their correspondence Mr. Sanders had often called her a ‘duck,’ but never ‘chops,’ nor yet ‘tomato sauce.’ He was particularly fond of ducks.", "편지를 주고받는 동안 샌더스 씨는 그녀를 종종 '오리'라 불렀으나, '양갈비'라 부른 적은 없었고 '토마토소스'라 부른 적도 없었다."], ["Perhaps if he had been as fond of chops and tomato sauce, he might have called her that, as a term of affection.", "그는 오리를 유난히 좋아했다. 어쩌면 그가 양갈비와 토마토소스를 그만큼 좋아했더라면, 애정의 표현으로 그녀를 그렇게 불렀을지도 모른다."]], [["Serjeant Buzfuz now rose with more importance than he had yet exhibited, if that were possible, and vociferated; ‘Call Samuel Weller.’", "이제 버즈퍼즈 변호사가, 가능하다면, 지금껏 보인 것보다 더 거드름을 피우며 일어서서 고함쳤다. \"새뮤얼 웰러를 부르시오.\""]], [["It was quite unnecessary to call Samuel Weller; for Samuel Weller stepped briskly into the box the instant his name was pronounced; and placing his hat on the floor, and his arms on the rail, took a bird’s- eye view of the Bar, and a comprehensive survey of the Bench, with a remarkably cheerful and lively aspect.", "새뮤얼 웰러를 부를 필요는 전혀 없었다. 새뮤얼 웰러는 자기 이름이 불리는 순간 잽싸게 증인석에 들어섰으니, 모자를 바닥에 놓고 두 팔을 난간에 얹은 채, 유난히 쾌활하고 발랄한 표정으로 법조계를 한눈에 내려다보고 재판석을 두루 둘러보았던 것이다."]], [["‘What’s your name, sir?’ inquired the judge.", "\"이름이 무엇이오, 선생?\" 판사가 물었다."]], [["‘Sam Weller, my Lord,’ replied that gentleman.", "\"샘 웰러입니다, 판사님.\" 그 신사가 대답했다."]], [["‘Do you spell it with a “V”", "\"철자를 'V'로 쓰오, 아니면 'W'로 쓰오?\""], ["or a “W”?’ inquired the judge.", "판사가 물었다."]], [["‘That depends upon the taste and fancy of the speller, my Lord,’ replied Sam; ‘I never had occasion to spell it more than once or twice in my life, but I spells it with a “V.”’", "\"그건 쓰는 사람의 취향과 기분에 달렸지요, 판사님.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"평생 한두 번밖에 그걸 써 볼 일이 없었습니다만, 저는 'V'로 씁니다.\""]], [["Here a voice in the gallery exclaimed aloud, ‘Quite right too, Samivel, quite right.", "이때 방청석에서 한 목소리가 크게 외쳤다. \"옳소, 새미빌, 옳고말고."], ["Put it down a “we,” my Lord, put it down a “we.”’", "'we'로 적으시오, 판사님, 'we'로 적으시오.\""]], [["Who is that, who dares address the court?’ said the little judge, looking up.", "\"누구냐, 감히 법정에 말을 거는 자가?\" 작은 판사가 고개를 들며 말했다."], ["‘Usher.’", "\"정리.\""]], [["‘Yes, my Lord.’", "\"예, 판사님.\""]], [["‘Bring that person here instantly.’", "\"저 사람을 당장 이리 데려오너라.\""]], [["‘Yes, my Lord.’", "\"예, 판사님.\""]], [["But as the usher didn’t find the person, he didn’t bring him; and, after a great commotion, all the people who had got up to look for the culprit, sat down again.", "그러나 정리가 그 사람을 찾지 못했으니, 데려오지도 못했다. 한바탕 소동이 인 끝에, 그 죄인을 찾으려고 일어섰던 사람들이 모두 도로 자리에 앉았다."], ["The little judge turned to the witness as soon as his indignation would allow him to speak, and said--", "작은 판사는 분이 가라앉아 말을 할 수 있게 되자마자 증인을 돌아보며 말했다—"]], [["‘Do you know who that was, sir?’", "\"저게 누구인지 아오, 선생?\""]], [["‘I rayther suspect it was my father, my lord,’ replied Sam.", "\"제 아버지가 아닌가 싶습니다만, 판사님.\" 샘이 대답했다."]], [["‘Do you see him here now?’ said the judge.", "\"지금 여기 보이오?\" 판사가 말했다."]], [["‘No, I don’t, my Lord,’ replied Sam, staring right up into the lantern at the roof of the court.", "\"아니요, 안 보입니다, 판사님.\" 샘이 법정 지붕의 채광창을 똑바로 올려다보며 대답했다."]], [["‘If you could have pointed him out, I would have committed him instantly,’ said the judge.", "\"그를 가리킬 수 있었다면, 당장 구금했을 것이오.\" 판사가 말했다."], ["Sam bowed his acknowledgments and turned, with unimpaired cheerfulness of countenance, towards Serjeant Buzfuz.", "샘은 감사의 뜻으로 고개를 숙이고는, 변함없이 쾌활한 표정으로 버즈퍼즈 변호사를 향해 돌아섰다."]], [["‘Now, Mr.", "\"자, 웰러 씨.\""], ["Weller,’ said Serjeant Buzfuz.", "버즈퍼즈 변호사가 말했다."]], [["‘Now, sir,’ replied Sam.", "\"자, 선생.\" 샘이 대답했다."]], [["‘I believe you are in the service of Mr. Pickwick, the defendant in this case?", "\"내가 알기로 선생은 이 사건의 피고 픽윅 씨를 모시고 있지요?"], ["Speak up, if you please, Mr. Weller.’", "크게 말씀하시오, 부디, 웰러 씨.\""]], [["‘I mean to speak up, Sir,’ replied Sam; ‘I am in the service o’ that ‘ere gen’l’man, and a wery good service it is.’", "\"크게 말할 작정입니다, 선생.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"저는 그 양반을 모시고 있고요, 아주 좋은 일자리지요.\""]], [["‘Little to do, and plenty to get, I suppose?’ said Serjeant Buzfuz, with jocularity.", "\"할 일은 적고 받는 건 많겠구려?\" 버즈퍼즈 변호사가 농담조로 말했다."]], [["‘Oh, quite enough to get, Sir, as the soldier said ven they ordered him three hundred and fifty lashes,’ replied Sam.", "\"오, 받는 건 넉넉합니다요, 선생. 그 병사가 채찍 삼백쉰 대를 맞으라는 명령을 받았을 때 한 말마따나요.\" 샘이 대답했다."]], [["‘You must not tell us what the soldier, or any other man, said, Sir,’ interposed the judge; ‘it’s not evidence.’", "\"그 병사가, 혹은 다른 누가 무슨 말을 했는지 우리한테 말해선 안 되오, 선생.\" 판사가 끼어들었다. \"그건 증거가 아니오.\""]], [["‘Wery good, my Lord,’ replied Sam.", "\"잘 알겠습니다, 판사님.\" 샘이 대답했다."]], [["‘Do you recollect anything particular happening on the morning when you were first engaged by the defendant; eh, Mr.", "\"선생이 피고에게 처음 고용된 날 아침에 무언가 특별한 일이 있었던 걸 기억하오, 응, 웰러 씨?\""], ["Weller?’ said Serjeant Buzfuz.", "버즈퍼즈 변호사가 말했다."]], [["‘Yes, I do, sir,’ replied Sam.", "\"예, 기억합니다, 선생.\" 샘이 대답했다."]], [["‘Have the goodness to tell the jury what it was.’", "\"친절히 그게 무엇이었는지 배심원에게 말씀하시오.\""]], [["‘I had a reg’lar new fit out o’ clothes that mornin’, gen’l’men of the jury,’ said Sam, ‘and that was a wery partickler and uncommon circumstance vith me in those days.’", "\"그날 아침 제가 옷을 한 벌 새로 쫙 빼입었지요, 배심원 여러분.\" 샘이 말했다. \"그건 그 시절 저한테 아주 특별하고 흔치 않은 일이었습니다요.\""]], [["Hereupon there was a general laugh; and the little judge, looking with an angry countenance over his desk, said, ‘You had better be careful, Sir.’", "이에 좌중에 한바탕 웃음이 일었고, 작은 판사가 성난 표정으로 책상 너머를 노려보며 말했다. \"조심하는 게 좋을 거요, 선생.\""]], [["‘So Mr.", "\"그때 픽윅 씨도 그렇게 말씀하셨습니다요, 판사님.\" 샘이 대답했다."], ["Pickwick said at the time, my Lord,’ replied Sam; ‘and I was wery careful o’ that ‘ere suit o’ clothes; wery careful indeed, my Lord.’", "\"그래서 저는 그 옷을 아주 조심해서 다뤘지요. 정말 아주 조심했습니다요, 판사님.\""]], [["The judge looked sternly at Sam for full two minutes, but Sam’s features were so perfectly calm and serene that the judge said nothing, and motioned Serjeant Buzfuz to proceed.", "판사는 족히 이 분 동안 샘을 엄하게 노려보았으나, 샘의 표정이 어찌나 완벽하게 차분하고 평온했던지, 판사는 아무 말도 못 하고 버즈퍼즈 변호사에게 진행하라고 손짓했다."]], [["‘Do you mean to tell me, Mr.", "\"내게 말하려는 거요, 웰러 씨?\""], ["Weller,’ said Serjeant Buzfuz, folding his arms emphatically, and turning half-round to the jury, as if in mute assurance that he would bother the witness yet--‘do you mean to tell me, Mr.", "버즈퍼즈 변호사가 두 팔을 단호하게 팔짱 끼고 반쯤 배심원 쪽으로 돌아서며 말했으니, 마치 자기가 이 증인을 기어이 곤란하게 만들겠다고 말없이 다짐하는 듯했다."], ["Weller, that you saw nothing of this fainting on the part of the plaintiff in the arms of the defendant, which you have heard described by the witnesses?’", "\"내게 말하려는 거요, 웰러 씨, 선생이 증인들의 입을 통해 묘사된 것을 들은 그 일, 곧 원고가 피고의 품에서 기절한 그 일을 선생은 전혀 보지 못했단 말이오?\""]], [["Certainly not,’ replied Sam; ‘I was in the passage till they called me up, and then the old lady was not there.’", "\"못 봤고말고요.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"저는 불려 올라갈 때까지 복도에 있었고, 그러고 나니 그 노부인은 거기 안 계셨지요.\""]], [["‘Now, attend, Mr.", "\"자, 주목하시오, 웰러 씨.\""], ["Weller,’ said Serjeant Buzfuz, dipping a large pen into the inkstand before him, for the purpose of frightening Sam with a show of taking down his answer.", "버즈퍼즈 변호사가 샘의 답변을 받아 적는 시늉을 해 그를 겁주려고, 앞에 놓인 잉크병에 큼지막한 펜을 적시며 말했다."], ["‘You were in the passage, and yet saw nothing of what was going forward.", "\"선생은 복도에 있었으면서도 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 전혀 보지 못했다."], ["Have you a pair of eyes, Mr. Weller?’", "선생, 눈은 두 개 달려 있소, 웰러 씨?\""]], [["‘Yes, I have a pair of eyes,’ replied Sam, ‘and that’s just it. If they wos a pair o’ patent double million magnifyin’ gas microscopes of hextra power, p’raps I might be able to see through a flight o’ stairs and a deal door; but bein’ only eyes, you see, my wision ‘s limited.’", "\"예, 눈은 두 개 달려 있습죠.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"바로 그게 문제지요. 그게 만일 특별히 성능 좋은, 특허받은 이백만 배 확대 가스 현미경 한 쌍이었다면야, 어쩌면 계단 한 층과 두꺼운 널문도 꿰뚫어 볼 수 있었을지도 모릅니다요. 하지만 그저 눈일 뿐이니, 아시다시피 제 시야엔 한계가 있는 거지요.\""]], [["At this answer, which was delivered without the slightest appearance of irritation, and with the most complete simplicity and equanimity of manner, the spectators tittered, the little judge smiled, and Serjeant Buzfuz looked particularly foolish.", "조금도 짜증 내는 기색 없이, 더없이 천연덕스럽고 평온한 태도로 던진 이 답변에, 방청객들은 킥킥거렸고, 작은 판사는 미소를 지었으며, 버즈퍼즈 변호사는 유난히 멍청해 보였다."], ["After a short consultation with Dodson & Fogg, the learned Serjeant again turned towards Sam, and said, with a painful effort to conceal his vexation, ‘Now, Mr.", "도드슨 및 포그와 잠시 의논한 뒤, 학식 있는 변호사는 다시 샘을 향해 돌아서서, 분함을 감추려 애쓰는 괴로운 표정으로 말했다."], ["Weller, I’ll ask you a question on another point, if you please.’", "\"자, 웰러 씨, 괜찮다면 다른 점에 관해 한 가지 묻겠소.\""]], [["‘If you please, Sir,’ rejoined Sam, with the utmost good-humour.", "\"좋습니다요, 선생.\" 샘이 더없이 좋은 기분으로 받았다."]], [["‘Do you remember going up to Mrs. Bardell’s house, one night in November last?’", "\"지난 11월의 어느 밤, 바델 부인 댁에 올라갔던 것을 기억하오?\""]], [["Oh, yes, wery well.’", "\"오, 예, 아주 잘 기억합니다.\""]], [["‘Oh, you do remember that, Mr.", "\"오, 그건 기억하는구려, 웰러 씨.\""], ["Weller,’ said Serjeant Buzfuz, recovering his spirits; ‘I thought we should get at something at last.’", "버즈퍼즈 변호사가 기운을 차리며 말했다. \"마침내 뭔가 건질 게 있겠다 싶었소.\""]], [["‘I rayther thought that, too, sir,’ replied Sam; and at this the spectators tittered again.", "\"저도 그럴 줄 알았습니다요, 선생.\" 샘이 대답했다. 이에 방청객들이 또 킥킥거렸다."]], [["‘Well; I suppose you went up to have a little talk about this trial--eh, Mr.", "\"자, 내 생각에 선생은 이 재판에 관해 잠깐 이야기를 나누려고 올라간 거겠지, 응, 웰러 씨?\""], ["Weller?’ said Serjeant Buzfuz, looking knowingly at the jury.", "버즈퍼즈 변호사가 배심원들을 의미심장하게 바라보며 말했다."]], [["‘I went up to pay the rent; but we did get a-talkin’ about the trial,’ replied Sam.", "\"집세를 내러 올라갔지요. 하지만 재판에 관해 얘기를 나누긴 했습니다요.\" 샘이 대답했다."]], [["‘Oh, you did get a-talking about the trial,’ said Serjeant Buzfuz, brightening up with the anticipation of some important discovery.", "\"오, 재판에 관해 이야기를 나눴구려.\" 버즈퍼즈 변호사가 무언가 중대한 발견을 기대하며 기색이 밝아져 말했다."], ["‘Now, what passed about the trial; will you have the goodness to tell us, Mr.", "\"자, 재판에 관해 무슨 말이 오갔소?"], ["Weller’?’", "친절히 우리에게 말씀해 주시겠소, 웰러 씨?\""]], [["‘Vith all the pleasure in life, sir,’ replied Sam.", "\"기꺼이 그러지요, 선생.\" 샘이 대답했다."], ["‘Arter a few unimportant obserwations from the two wirtuous females as has been examined here to-day, the ladies gets into a very great state o’ admiration at the honourable conduct of Mr.", "\"오늘 여기서 신문받은 그 정숙한 두 부인이 대수롭잖은 얘기를 몇 마디 한 뒤에, 그 부인들이 도드슨 씨하고 포그 씨의—지금 선생 곁에 앉아 계신 저 두 양반의—훌륭한 처사에 아주 크게 감탄하는 상태에 빠졌습죠.\""], ["Dodson and Fogg--them two gen’l’men as is settin’ near you now.’ This, of course, drew general attention to Dodson & Fogg, who looked as virtuous as possible.", "이 말은 물론 좌중의 시선을 도드슨 및 포그에게로 끌어모았고, 두 사람은 더없이 고결해 보이는 표정을 지었다."]], [["‘The attorneys for the plaintiff,’ said Mr.", "\"원고 측 사무 변호사들 말이오.\""], ["Serjeant Buzfuz.", "버즈퍼즈 변호사가 말했다."], ["‘Well!", "\"좋소!"], ["They spoke in high praise of the honourable conduct of Messrs. Dodson and Fogg, the attorneys for the plaintiff, did they?’", "그들이 원고 측 사무 변호사인 도드슨 및 포그 씨의 훌륭한 처사를 높이 칭찬했다는 거지요?\""]], [["‘Yes,’ said Sam, ‘they said what a wery gen’rous thing it was o’ them to have taken up the case on spec, and to charge nothing at all for costs, unless they got ‘em out of Mr.", "\"예.\" 샘이 말했다."], ["Pickwick.’", "\"그 두 양반이 이 사건을 성공 보수로만 맡고, 픽윅 씨한테서 받아 내지 못하면 소송 비용을 한 푼도 안 받기로 한 게 참 너그러운 일이라고들 했습죠.\""]], [["At this very unexpected reply, the spectators tittered again, and Dodson & Fogg, turning very red, leaned over to Serjeant Buzfuz, and in a hurried manner whispered something in his ear.", "이 더없이 뜻밖의 대답에 방청객들이 또 킥킥거렸고, 도드슨과 포그는 얼굴이 새빨개져서 버즈퍼즈 변호사에게 몸을 기울이고는 황급히 그의 귀에 무언가를 속삭였다."]], [["‘You are quite right,’ said Serjeant Buzfuz aloud, with affected composure.", "\"지당한 말씀이오.\" 버즈퍼즈 변호사가 짐짓 침착한 척하며 큰 소리로 말했다."], ["‘It’s perfectly useless, my Lord, attempting to get at any evidence through the impenetrable stupidity of this witness.", "\"판사님, 이 증인의 뚫을 수 없는 우둔함을 통해 무슨 증거를 끌어내려 애쓰는 것은 완전히 헛수고입니다."], ["I will not trouble the court by asking him any more questions.", "더는 그에게 질문을 하여 법정을 번거롭게 하지 않겠습니다."], ["Stand down, sir.’", "내려가시오, 선생.\""]], [["‘Would any other gen’l’man like to ask me anythin’?’ inquired Sam, taking up his hat, and looking round most deliberately.", "\"또 다른 어느 양반이 저한테 뭔가 묻고 싶으십니까?\" 샘이 모자를 집어 들며 더없이 느긋하게 둘러보며 물었다."]], [["‘Not I, Mr.", "\"난 됐소, 웰러 씨, 고맙소.\""], ["Weller, thank you,’ said Serjeant Snubbin, laughing.", "스너빈 변호사가 웃으며 말했다."]], [["‘You may go down, sir,’ said Serjeant Buzfuz, waving his hand impatiently.", "\"내려가도 좋소, 선생.\" 버즈퍼즈 변호사가 짜증스레 손을 내저으며 말했다."], ["Sam went down accordingly, after doing Messrs. Dodson & Fogg’s case as much harm as he conveniently could, and saying just as little respecting Mr.", "샘은 그에 따라 내려갔으니, 도드슨 및 포그 씨의 소송에 되도록 해를 끼치고, 픽윅 씨에 관해서는 되도록 말을 아낀 뒤였다."], ["Pickwick as might be, which was precisely the object he had had in view all along.", "그것이야말로 처음부터 그가 노린 바로 그 목적이었다."]], [["‘I have no objection to admit, my Lord,’ said Serjeant Snubbin, ‘if it will save the examination of another witness, that Mr.", "\"인정하는 데 이의가 없습니다, 판사님.\" 스너빈 변호사가 말했다."], ["Pickwick has retired from business, and is a gentleman of considerable independent property.’", "\"또 다른 증인의 신문을 덜 수 있다면 말입니다만, 픽윅 씨가 사업에서 은퇴했으며, 상당한 독립적 자산을 지닌 신사라는 점 말입니다.\""]], [["‘Very well,’ said Serjeant Buzfuz, putting in the two letters to be read, ‘then that’s my case, my Lord.’", "\"좋소.\" 버즈퍼즈 변호사가 낭독될 두 통의 편지를 제출하며 말했다. \"그러면 이것으로 저의 입증을 마칩니다, 판사님.\""]], [["Serjeant Snubbin then addressed the jury on behalf of the defendant; and a very long and a very emphatic address he delivered, in which he bestowed the highest possible eulogiums on the conduct and character of Mr.", "이어서 스너빈 변호사가 피고를 위해 배심원들에게 변론했으니, 더없이 길고 더없이 힘주어 변론을 펼치며, 그 안에서 픽윅 씨의 행실과 인품에 더없이 높은 찬사를 바쳤다."], ["Pickwick; but inasmuch as our readers are far better able to form a correct estimate of that gentleman’s merits and deserts, than Serjeant Snubbin could possibly be, we do not feel called upon to enter at any length into the learned gentleman’s observations.", "그러나 우리 독자들은 그 신사의 장점과 됨됨이를 스너빈 변호사가 도저히 할 수 있는 것보다 훨씬 더 정확히 가늠할 수 있는 처지이므로, 우리는 그 학식 있는 신사의 변론을 길게 늘어놓을 필요를 느끼지 못한다."], ["He attempted to show that the letters which had been exhibited, merely related to Mr. Pickwick’s dinner, or to the preparations for receiving him in his apartments on his return from some country excursion.", "그는 제출된 편지들이 한낱 픽윅 씨의 식사에 관한 것이거나, 그가 어느 시골 나들이에서 돌아올 때 그의 방에서 맞이할 준비에 관한 것에 불과하다는 것을 보여 주려 애썼다."], ["It is sufficient to add in general terms, that he did the best he could for Mr.", "대략적으로 덧붙이자면, 그는 픽윅 씨를 위해 할 수 있는 최선을 다했다고 하는 것으로 충분하다."], ["Pickwick; and the best, as everybody knows, on the infallible authority of the old adage, could do no more.", "그리고 그 최선이란, 누구나 알다시피 그 옛 격언의 틀림없는 권위에 따르면, 그 이상은 할 수 없는 법이다."]], [["Mr. Justice Stareleigh summed up, in the old-established and most approved form.", "스테어리 판사는 오랜 관례에 따른, 더없이 인정받는 방식으로 사건을 정리했다."], ["He read as much of his notes to the jury as he could decipher on so short a notice, and made running-comments on the evidence as he went along. If Mrs.", "그는 그토록 짧은 시간에 해독할 수 있는 만큼의 자기 메모를 배심원들에게 읽어 주었고, 읽어 나가면서 증거에 즉석 논평을 곁들였다."], ["Bardell were right, it was perfectly clear that Mr. Pickwick was wrong, and if they thought the evidence of Mrs.", "만일 바델 부인이 옳다면 픽윅 씨가 그르다는 것이 더없이 분명하며, 만일 그들이 클러핀스 부인의 증언을 믿을 만하다고 여긴다면 그것을 믿을 것이요, 그렇지 않다면야 믿지 않을 것이다."], ["Cluppins worthy of credence they would believe it, and, if they didn’t, why, they wouldn’t. If they were satisfied that a breach of promise of marriage had been committed they would find for the plaintiff with such damages as they thought proper; and if, on the other hand, it appeared to them that no promise of marriage had ever been given, they would find for the defendant with no damages at all.", "만일 그들이 약혼 불이행이 저질러졌다고 납득한다면, 그들은 적절하다고 여기는 손해 배상과 함께 원고에게 유리한 평결을 내릴 것이요, 반면에 결혼 약속이 애초에 전혀 주어진 적이 없다고 그들에게 여겨진다면, 손해 배상은 일절 없이 피고에게 유리한 평결을 내릴 것이다."], ["The jury then retired to their private room to talk the matter over, and the judge retired to his private room, to refresh himself with a mutton chop and a glass of sherry.", "그런 다음 배심원들은 그 문제를 의논하려고 자기들의 밀실로 물러갔고, 판사는 양갈비 한 점과 셰리주 한 잔으로 원기를 돋우려고 자기 밀실로 물러갔다."]], [["An anxious quarter of a hour elapsed; the jury came back; the judge was fetched in.", "애가 타는 십오 분이 흘렀다. 배심원들이 돌아왔고, 판사가 불려 들어왔다."], ["Mr. Pickwick put on his spectacles, and gazed at the foreman with an agitated countenance and a quickly-beating heart.", "픽윅 씨는 안경을 쓰고, 동요하는 표정과 빠르게 뛰는 가슴으로 배심원장을 바라보았다."]], [["‘Gentlemen,’ said the individual in black, ‘are you all agreed upon your verdict?’", "\"여러분,\" 검은 옷의 인물이 말했다. \"평결에 모두 합의하셨습니까?\""]], [["‘We are,’ replied the foreman.", "\"합의했습니다.\" 배심원장이 대답했다."]], [["‘Do you find for the plaintiff, gentlemen, or for the defendant?’", "\"원고에게 유리한 평결입니까, 여러분, 아니면 피고에게 유리한 평결입니까?\""]], [["For the plaintiff.’", "\"원고에게 유리한 평결입니다.\""]], [["‘With what damages, gentlemen?’", "\"손해 배상액은 얼마입니까, 여러분?\""]], [["‘Seven hundred and fifty pounds.’", "\"칠백오십 파운드입니다.\""]], [["Mr.", "픽윅 씨는 안경을 벗어, 알을 정성껏 닦고, 그것을 안경집에 접어 넣어 주머니에 넣었다."], ["Pickwick took off his spectacles, carefully wiped the glasses, folded them into their case, and put them in his pocket; then, having", "그러고는,"]], [["drawn on his gloves with great nicety, and stared at the foreman all the while, he mechanically followed Mr. Perker and the blue bag out of court.", "장갑을 대단히 정성스럽게 끼고서 그동안 내내 배심장을 노려보던 그는, 기계적으로 퍼커 씨와 푸른 가방을 따라 법정을 나섰다."]], [["They stopped in a side room while Perker paid the court fees; and here, Mr. Pickwick was joined by his friends.", "퍼커가 법정 수수료를 치르는 동안 그들은 곁방에 들렀고, 여기서 픽윅 씨는 친구들과 합류했다."], ["Here, too, he encountered Messrs. Dodson & Fogg, rubbing their hands with every token of outward satisfaction.", "그리고 여기서 그는 도드슨과 포그 두 사람과도 마주쳤는데, 그들은 겉으로 드러나는 온갖 만족의 기색을 띠며 두 손을 비비고 있었다."]], [["‘Well, gentlemen,’ said Mr.", "\"그래, 여러분.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Well, Sir,’ said Dodson, for self and partner.", "\"그래요, 선생.\" 도드슨이 자신과 동업자를 대표해서 말했다."]], [["‘You imagine you’ll get your costs, don’t you, gentlemen?’ said Mr.", "\"당신들 소송 비용을 받아 낼 거라고 생각하나 본데, 그렇소, 여러분?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["Fogg said they thought it rather probable.", "포그는 그렇게 될 가능성이 꽤 높다고 생각한다고 말했다."], ["Dodson smiled, and said they’d try.", "도드슨은 미소를 지으며 한번 해 보겠다고 말했다."]], [["‘You may try, and try, and try again, Messrs.", "\"해 보고, 또 해 보고, 또다시 해 보시오, 도드슨, 포그 두 분.\""], ["Dodson and Fogg,’ said Mr.", "픽윅 씨가 격렬하게 말했다."], ["Pickwick vehemently, ‘but not one farthing of costs or damages do you ever get from me, if I spend the rest of my existence in a debtor’s prison.’", "\"하지만 내가 남은 평생을 채무자 감옥에서 보낸다 할지라도, 당신들은 나한테서 비용이든 손해배상이든 단 한 푼도 받아 내지 못할 거요.\""]], [["‘Ha!", "\"하!"], ["ha!’ laughed Dodson.", "하!\" 도드슨이 웃었다."], ["‘You’ll think better of that, before next term, Mr. Pickwick.’", "\"다음 개정기 전에 생각이 달라지실 겁니다, 픽윅 씨.\""]], [["‘He, he, he!", "\"헤, 헤, 헤!"], ["We’ll soon see about that, Mr.", "곧 알게 되겠지요, 픽윅 씨.\""], ["Pickwick,’ grinned Fogg.", "포그가 이를 드러내며 히죽거렸다."]], [["Speechless with indignation, Mr. Pickwick allowed himself to be led by his solicitor and friends to the door, and there assisted into a hackney-coach, which had been fetched for the purpose, by the ever- watchful Sam Weller.", "분노로 말문이 막힌 픽윅 씨는 변호사와 친구들에게 이끌려 문간까지 갔고, 거기서 늘 빈틈없이 살피던 샘 웰러가 미리 불러다 놓은 삯마차에 오르는 것을 부축받았다."]], [["Sam had put up the steps, and was preparing to jump upon the box, when he felt himself gently touched on the shoulder; and, looking round, his father stood before him.", "샘은 발판을 올려놓고 마부석으로 뛰어오를 채비를 하던 차에, 누군가 어깨를 살며시 건드리는 것을 느꼈다. 돌아보니 그의 아버지가 앞에 서 있었다."], ["The old gentleman’s countenance wore a mournful expression, as he shook his head gravely, and said, in warning accents--", "노신사의 얼굴에는 애처로운 표정이 어려 있었고, 그는 엄숙하게 고개를 가로저으며 타이르는 어조로 말했다."]], [["‘I know’d what ‘ud come o’ this here mode o’ doin’ bisness.", "\"이런 식으로 일을 처리하면 이렇게 될 줄 내 진작 알았다."], ["Oh, Sammy, Sammy, vy worn’t there a alleybi!’", "아이고, 새미야, 새미야, 어째서 알리바이가 없었더란 말이냐!\""]], [["CHAPTER XXXV. IN WHICH MR.", "\"하지만 분명히, 선생님,\""], ["PICKWICK THINKS HE HAD BETTER GO TO BATH; AND GOES ACCORDINGLY\nBut surely, my dear sir,’ said little Perker, as he stood in Mr.", "재판 다음 날 아침 픽윅 씨의 방에 서 있던 작은 퍼커가 말했다."], ["Pickwick’s apartment on the morning after the trial, ‘surely you don’t really mean--really and seriously now, and irritation apart--that you won’t pay these costs and damages?’", "\"분명히 정말로 그러시는 건 아니겠지요—이제 진심으로, 진지하게, 흥분은 빼고 말입니다만—이 소송 비용과 손해배상금을 안 내시겠다는 말씀은 정말 아니시겠지요?\""]], [["‘Not one halfpenny,’ said Mr.", "\"반 페니도 못 낸다네.\""], ["Pickwick firmly; ‘not one halfpenny.’", "픽윅 씨가 단호히 말했다. \"반 페니도 못 내.\""]], [["‘Hooroar for the principle, as the money-lender said ven he vouldn’t renew the bill,’ observed Mr.", "\"원칙을 위하여 만세, 라고 어떤 고리대금업자가 어음을 갱신해 주지 않을 때 말했다죠.\""], ["Weller, who was clearing away the breakfast-things.", "아침 식기를 치우던 웰러 씨가 한마디 했다."]], [["‘Sam,’ said Mr.", "\"샘,\""], ["Pickwick, ‘have the goodness to step downstairs.’", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"수고스럽지만 아래층으로 좀 내려가 주게.\""]], [["‘Cert’nly, sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"알겠습니다요, 나리.\""], ["Weller; and acting on Mr.", "웰러 씨가 대답했다."], ["Pickwick’s gentle hint, Sam retired.", "그리고 픽윅 씨의 부드러운 암시를 받아들여 샘은 물러갔다."]], [["‘No, Perker,’ said Mr.", "\"안 되네, 퍼커.\""], ["Pickwick, with great seriousness of manner, ‘my friends here have endeavoured to dissuade me from this determination, but without avail.", "픽윅 씨가 매우 진지한 태도로 말했다. \"여기 내 친구들이 이 결심을 단념시키려 애썼지만 헛수고였네."], ["I shall employ myself as usual, until the opposite party have the power of issuing a legal process of execution against me; and if they are vile enough to avail themselves of it, and to arrest my person, I shall yield myself up with perfect cheerfulness and content of heart.", "나는 상대편이 나에게 법적 집행 절차를 발부할 권한을 가질 때까지 평소대로 지내겠네. 그리고 만약 그들이 그 권한을 이용해서 내 몸을 잡아 가둘 만큼 비열하다면, 나는 더없이 명랑하고 흡족한 마음으로 순순히 응하겠네."], ["When can they do this?’", "그자들이 언제 그렇게 할 수 있나?\""]], [["‘They can issue execution, my dear Sir, for the amount of the damages and taxed costs, next term,’ replied Perker, ‘just two months hence, my dear sir.’", "\"손해배상금과 산정된 소송 비용에 대해서는 다음 개정기에 집행을 발부할 수 있습니다, 선생님.\" 퍼커가 대답했다. \"지금부터 꼭 두 달 뒤지요, 선생님.\""]], [["‘Very good,’ said Mr.", "\"아주 좋네.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick. ‘Until that time, my dear fellow, let me hear no more of the matter.", "\"그때까지는, 이 사람아, 이 문제에 대해서 더는 듣고 싶지 않네."], ["And now,’ continued Mr.", "자 그럼,\""], ["Pickwick, looking round on his friends with a good-humoured smile, and a sparkle in the eye which no spectacles could dim or conceal, ‘the only question is, Where shall we go next?’", "픽윅 씨는 어떤 안경으로도 흐리거나 가릴 수 없는 눈빛의 반짝임과 더불어 유쾌한 미소를 띠고 친구들을 둘러보며 말을 이었다. \"이제 유일한 문제는, 다음엔 어디로 갈까 하는 거네.\""]], [["Mr. Tupman and Mr.", "터프먼 씨와 스노드그래스 씨는 친구의 영웅적 태도에 너무 감동한 나머지 아무 대답도 내놓지 못했다."], ["Snodgrass were too much affected by their friend’s heroism to offer any reply. Mr.", "윈클 씨는 재판에서 자기가 한 증언의 기억에서 아직 충분히 회복되지 못해 어떤 주제에 대해서도 한마디 할 처지가 못 되었다."], ["Winkle had not yet sufficiently recovered the recollection of his evidence at the trial, to make any observation on any subject, so Mr. Pickwick paused in vain.", "그래서 픽윅 씨는 헛되이 잠시 기다렸다."]], [["‘Well,’ said that gentleman, ‘if you leave me to suggest our destination, I say Bath.", "\"자,\" 그 신사가 말했다."], ["I think none of us have ever been there.’", "\"행선지를 정하는 일을 나에게 맡긴다면, 나는 배스라고 말하겠네. 우리 중 아무도 거기 가 본 적이 없는 것 같으니 말일세.\""]], [["Nobody had; and as the proposition was warmly seconded by Perker, who considered it extremely probable that if Mr.", "아무도 없었다."], ["Pickwick saw a little change and gaiety he would be inclined to think better of his determination, and worse of a debtor’s prison, it was carried unanimously; and Sam was at once despatched to the White Horse Cellar, to take five places by the half-past seven o’clock coach, next morning.", "그리고 그 제안은 퍼커가 따뜻이 거들었으니, 그는 픽윅 씨가 약간의 변화와 흥겨움을 맛본다면 자기 결심에 대해서는 다시 생각해 보고 채무자 감옥에 대해서는 더 나쁘게 여기게 될 가능성이 매우 크다고 보았던 것이다. 그리하여 만장일치로 가결되었고, 샘은 즉시 화이트 호스 셀러로 보내져 이튿날 아침 일곱 시 반 마차의 다섯 자리를 예약하게 되었다."]], [["There were just two places to be had inside, and just three to be had out; so Sam Weller booked for them all, and having exchanged a few compliments with the booking-office clerk on the subject of a pewter half-crown which was tendered him as a portion of his ‘change,’ walked back to the George and Vulture, where he was pretty busily employed until bed-time in reducing clothes and linen into the smallest possible compass, and exerting his mechanical genius in constructing a variety of ingenious devices for keeping the lids on boxes which had neither locks nor hinges.", "마침 안쪽에 두 자리, 바깥쪽에 세 자리가 있었다. 그래서 샘 웰러는 그것을 모두 예약하고, '거스름돈'의 일부랍시고 건네받은 백랍으로 된 가짜 반 크라운 동전을 두고 예약 사무소 직원과 몇 마디 인사치레를 주고받은 뒤, 조지 앤드 벌처로 걸어 돌아왔다. 거기서 그는 잠자리에 들 때까지 꽤 분주히 일했으니, 옷가지와 속옷을 가능한 한 작게 줄여 넣고, 자물쇠도 경첩도 없는 상자의 뚜껑을 닫아 두기 위한 갖가지 기발한 장치를 고안하는 데 그의 기계적 천재성을 발휘했다."]], [["The next was a very unpropitious morning for a journey--muggy, damp, and drizzly.", "이튿날 아침은 여행하기에 매우 좋지 못한 날씨였다—후텁지근하고, 축축하고, 가랑비가 내렸다."], ["The horses in the stages that were going out, and had come through the city, were smoking so, that the outside passengers were invisible.", "도시를 지나 나가는 역마차의 말들은 어찌나 김을 뿜어 대는지 바깥쪽 승객들이 보이지 않을 정도였다."], ["The newspaper-sellers looked moist, and smelled mouldy; the wet ran off the hats of the orange-vendors as they thrust their heads into the coach windows, and diluted the insides in a refreshing manner.", "신문팔이들은 눅눅해 보였고 곰팡내가 났다. 오렌지 장수들이 마차 창문으로 머리를 들이밀 때 그들의 모자에서 빗물이 흘러내려 상쾌하게도 안쪽 승객들을 적셨다."], ["The Jews with the fifty-bladed penknives shut them up in despair; the men with the pocket-books made pocket-books of them. Watch-guards and toasting-forks were alike at a discount, and pencil-cases and sponges were a drug in the market.", "칼날이 쉰 개나 달린 주머니칼을 파는 유대인들은 절망 속에 그것을 접어 넣었고, 수첩을 파는 사내들은 자기네 자신이 수첩 꼴이 되었다.* 시곗줄과 토스트용 포크는 똑같이 헐값이었고, 연필집과 스펀지는 시장에서 거저나 다름없었다."]], [["Leaving Sam Weller to rescue the luggage from the seven or eight porters who flung themselves savagely upon it, the moment the coach stopped, and finding that they were about twenty minutes too early, Mr.", "* 수첩 꼴: 비에 흠뻑 젖어 후줄근해진 모습을 빗댄 말장난."], ["Pickwick and his friends went for shelter into the travellers’ room--the last resource of human dejection.", "마차가 멈추는 순간 거칠게 달려들어 짐에 매달리는 일고여덟 명의 짐꾼들로부터 짐을 구해 내는 일은 샘 웰러에게 맡기고, 자기들이 한 이십 분쯤 너무 일찍 왔다는 것을 안 픽윅 씨와 친구들은 비를 피하러 여행객 대기실로 들어갔다—인간의 낙심이 마지막으로 기댈 곳이었다."]], [["The travellers’ room at the White Horse Cellar is of course uncomfortable; it would be no travellers’ room if it were not.", "화이트 호스 셀러의 여행객 대기실은 물론 불편하다. 불편하지 않다면 여행객 대기실이랄 수도 없을 것이다."], ["It is the right-hand parlour, into which an aspiring kitchen fireplace appears to have walked, accompanied by a rebellious poker, tongs, and shovel.", "그곳은 오른편 응접실인데, 야심만만한 부엌 벽난로가 반항적인 부지깽이며 부젓가락이며 부삽을 거느리고 거기로 걸어 들어온 듯한 모양새였다."], ["It is divided into boxes, for the solitary confinement of travellers, and is furnished with a clock, a looking-glass, and a live waiter, which latter article is kept in a small kennel for washing glasses, in a corner of the apartment.", "그 방은 여행객들의 독방 감금을 위해 칸막이로 나뉘어 있었고, 시계 하나와 거울 하나, 그리고 살아 있는 웨이터 하나가 비치되어 있었다. 마지막 물건은 방 한구석의 유리잔 닦는 작은 개집 같은 데에 보관되어 있었다."]], [["One of these boxes was occupied, on this particular occasion, by a stern-eyed man of about five-and-forty, who had a bald and glossy forehead, with a good deal of black hair at the sides and back of his head, and large black whiskers.", "이 칸막이 자리들 가운데 하나를 이날따라 마흔다섯 살가량 된, 눈매가 험상궂은 사내가 차지하고 있었다. 그는 대머리에 반들거리는 이마를 가졌으며, 머리 양옆과 뒤쪽에는 검은 머리카락이 꽤 많고, 크고 검은 구레나룻을 길렀다."], ["He was buttoned up to the chin in a brown coat; and had a large sealskin travelling-cap, and a greatcoat and cloak, lying on the seat beside him. He looked up from his breakfast as Mr.", "그는 갈색 외투를 턱까지 단추로 채워 입었고, 옆자리에는 큼직한 물범 가죽 여행 모자와 두꺼운 외투와 망토가 놓여 있었다."], ["Pickwick entered, with a fierce and peremptory air, which was very dignified; and, having scrutinised that gentleman and his companions to his entire satisfaction, hummed a tune, in a manner which seemed to say that he rather suspected somebody wanted to take advantage of him, but it wouldn’t do.", "그는 픽윅 씨가 들어서자 아침 식사에서 눈을 들어, 매우 위엄 있어 보이는 사납고 단호한 태도로 쳐다보았다. 그리고 그 신사와 그 일행을 흡족할 때까지 샅샅이 뜯어본 다음, 누군가 자기를 이용해 먹으려는 게 아닐까 의심스럽지만 그렇게는 안 될 거라고 말하는 듯한 투로 콧노래를 흥얼거렸다."]], [["‘Waiter,’ said the gentleman with the whiskers.", "\"웨이터.\" 구레나룻 신사가 말했다."]], [["‘Sir?’ replied a man with a dirty complexion, and a towel of the same, emerging from the kennel before mentioned.", "\"예, 손님?\" 더러운 안색에다 같은 색깔의 수건을 든 사내가 앞서 말한 개집에서 나오며 대답했다."]], [["‘Some more toast.’", "\"토스트 좀 더.\""]], [["‘Yes, sir.’", "\"예, 손님.\""]], [["‘Buttered toast, mind,’ said the gentleman fiercely.", "\"버터 바른 토스트로, 알겠나.\" 신사가 사납게 말했다."]], [["‘Directly, sir,’ replied the waiter.", "\"곧 가져오겠습니다, 손님.\" 웨이터가 대답했다."]], [["The gentleman with the whiskers hummed a tune in the same manner as before, and pending the arrival of the toast, advanced to the front of the fire, and, taking his coat tails under his arms, looked at his boots and ruminated.", "구레나룻 신사는 아까와 똑같은 식으로 콧노래를 흥얼거리고는, 토스트가 오기를 기다리는 동안 난롯불 앞으로 나아가 외투 자락을 팔 밑에 끼고서 자기 부츠를 내려다보며 생각에 잠겼다."]], [["‘I wonder whereabouts in Bath this coach puts up,’ said Mr.", "\"이 마차가 배스 어디쯤에 서는지 궁금하군.\""], ["Pickwick, mildly addressing Mr. Winkle.", "픽윅 씨가 윈클 씨에게 부드럽게 말을 건넸다."]], [["‘Hum--eh--what’s that?’ said the strange man.", "\"흠—어—그게 무슨 말이오?\" 낯선 사내가 말했다."]], [["‘I made an observation to my friend, sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"제 친구에게 한마디 했을 뿐입니다, 선생님.\""], ["Pickwick, always ready to enter into conversation.", "언제나 대화에 끼어들 준비가 되어 있는 픽윅 씨가 대답했다."], ["‘I wondered at what house the Bath coach put up.", "\"배스행 마차가 어느 여관에 서는지 궁금해서요."], ["Perhaps you can inform me.’", "혹시 알려 주실 수 있을까 하고요.\""]], [["Are you going to Bath?’ said the strange man.", "\"배스에 가시오?\" 낯선 사내가 말했다."]], [["‘I am, sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"갑니다, 선생님.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘And those other gentlemen?’", "\"저 다른 신사분들도?\""]], [["‘They are going also,’ said Mr.", "\"그들도 갑니다.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Not inside--I’ll be damned if you’re going inside,’ said the strange man.", "\"안쪽은 아니겠지—당신들이 안쪽에 탄다면 내 목을 걸겠소.\" 낯선 사내가 말했다."]], [["‘Not all of us,’ said Mr.", "\"우리 모두가 타는 건 아닙니다.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘No, not all of you,’ said the strange man emphatically.", "\"그렇지, 당신들 모두는 아니지.\" 낯선 사내가 힘주어 말했다."], ["‘I’ve taken two places.", "\"내가 두 자리를 잡아 놨소."], ["If they try to squeeze six people into an infernal box that only holds four, I’ll take a post-chaise and bring an action.", "만약 네 명밖에 안 들어가는 그 빌어먹을 칸에 여섯을 욱여넣으려 든다면, 나는 역마차를 따로 빌리고 소송을 걸겠소. 나는 내 차비를 다 냈소."], ["I’ve paid my fare.", "그렇게는 안 되지."], ["It won’t do; I told the clerk when I took my places that it wouldn’t do.", "자리를 잡을 때 직원에게 그렇게는 안 된다고 말했소. 이런 일이 벌어진다는 걸 나도 아오."], ["I know these things have been done.", "매일같이 벌어진다는 걸 알지."], ["I know they are done every day; but I never was done, and I never will be.", "하지만 나는 한 번도 당한 적이 없고, 앞으로도 당하지 않을 거요. 나를 가장 잘 아는 자들이 그걸 가장 잘 알지."], ["Those who know me best, best know it; crush me!’ Here the fierce gentleman rang the bell with great violence, and told the waiter he’d better bring the toast in five seconds, or he’d know the reason why.", "빌어먹을!\" 여기서 그 사나운 신사는 종을 몹시 거칠게 울려 대고는, 웨이터에게 오 초 안에 토스트를 가져오는 게 좋을 거라고, 안 그러면 그 이유를 알게 될 거라고 말했다."]], [["‘My good sir,’ said Mr.", "\"이보세요, 선생님.\""], ["Pickwick, ‘you will allow me to observe that this is a very unnecessary display of excitement.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"한 말씀 드리는 걸 허락해 주신다면, 이건 아주 불필요한 흥분의 표출입니다."], ["I have only taken places inside for two.’", "저는 안쪽에 두 자리만 잡았으니까요.\""]], [["‘I am glad to hear it,’ said the fierce man.", "\"그 말을 들으니 반갑소.\" 사나운 사내가 말했다."], ["‘I withdraw my expressions.", "\"내 말을 거두겠소."], ["I tender an apology.", "사과를 드리지요."], ["There’s my card.", "여기 내 명함이오."], ["Give me your acquaintance.’", "나와 친분을 맺어 주시오.\""]], [["‘With great pleasure, Sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"기꺼이 그러지요, 선생님.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다."], ["Pickwick. ‘We are to be fellow- travellers, and I hope we shall find each other’s society mutually agreeable.’", "\"우리는 같이 여행하게 될 처지이니, 서로의 동행이 피차 즐거운 것이 되기를 바랍니다.\""]], [["‘I hope we shall,’ said the fierce gentleman.", "\"그러기를 바라오.\" 사나운 신사가 말했다."], ["‘I know we shall.", "\"그렇게 될 줄 아오."], ["I like your looks; they please me.", "당신 인상이 마음에 드오. 흡족하오."], ["Gentlemen, your hands and names.", "신사 여러분, 손과 이름을 주시오."], ["Know me.’", "나를 알아 두시오.\""]], [["Of course, an interchange of friendly salutations followed this gracious speech; and the fierce gentleman immediately proceeded to inform the friends, in the same short, abrupt, jerking sentences, that his name was Dowler; that he was going to Bath on pleasure; that he was formerly in the army; that he had now set up in business as a gentleman; that he lived upon the profits; and that the individual for whom the second place was taken, was a personage no less illustrious than Mrs.", "물론 이 정중한 말에 뒤이어 우호적인 인사가 오갔다."], ["Dowler, his lady wife.", "그리고 그 사나운 신사는 곧바로, 똑같이 짧고 퉁명스럽고 끊어지는 듯한 문장으로, 자기 이름은 다울러이며, 즐기러 배스에 가는 길이고, 예전에는 군에 있었으며, 지금은 신사로서 사업을 차렸고, 그 이익으로 먹고살며, 두 번째 자리를 잡은 그 인물은 다름 아닌 자기 아내 다울러 부인이라는 저명한 분이라고 친구들에게 알렸다."]], [["‘She’s a fine woman,’ said Mr.", "\"훌륭한 여자요.\" 다울러 씨가 말했다."], ["Dowler. ‘I am proud of her.", "\"나는 그녀가 자랑스럽소."], ["I have reason.’", "그럴 만한 이유가 있지.\""]], [["‘I hope I shall have the pleasure of judging,’ said Mr.", "\"제가 직접 판단하는 기쁨을 누리게 되기를 바랍니다.\""], ["Pickwick, with a smile.", "픽윅 씨가 미소를 띠고 말했다."]], [["‘You shall,’ replied Dowler.", "\"그러실 거요.\" 다울러가 대답했다."], ["‘She shall know you.", "\"그녀가 당신을 알게 될 거요."], ["She shall esteem you.", "당신을 존중하게 될 거요. 나는 별난 상황에서 그녀에게 구애했소."], ["I courted her under singular circumstances.", "무모한 맹세로 그녀를 얻었지. 이렇게 말이오."], ["I won her through a rash vow.", "나는 그녀를 보았소. 나는 그녀를 사랑했소."], ["Thus.", "나는 청혼했소."], ["I saw her; I loved her; I proposed; she refused me.--“You love another?”--“Spare my blushes.”--“I know him.”--“You do.”--“Very good; if he remains here, I’ll skin him.”’", "그녀는 나를 거절했소.—'다른 사람을 사랑하시오?'—'제 얼굴 붉히는 일을 면하게 해 주세요.'—'그자를 내가 아오.'—'아시는군요.'—'아주 좋소. 그자가 여기 남는다면, 내가 그자의 가죽을 벗겨 버리겠소.'\""]], [["‘Lord bless me!’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"맙소사!\""], ["Pickwick involuntarily.", "픽윅 씨가 저도 모르게 외쳤다."]], [["‘Did you skin the gentleman, Sir?’ inquired Mr.", "\"그 신사 양반의 가죽을 벗기셨습니까, 선생님?\""], ["Winkle, with a very pale face.", "윈클 씨가 몹시 창백한 얼굴로 물었다."]], [["‘I wrote him a note, I said it was a painful thing.", "\"그자에게 쪽지를 썼소. 괴로운 일이라고 했지."], ["And so it was.’", "정말 그랬소.\""]], [["‘Certainly,’ interposed Mr.", "\"그렇고말고요.\""], ["Winkle.", "윈클 씨가 끼어들었다."]], [["‘I said I had pledged my word as a gentleman to skin him.", "\"나는 신사로서 그자의 가죽을 벗기겠다고 내 말로 맹세했다고 했소."], ["My character was at stake.", "내 명예가 걸린 일이었지."], ["I had no alternative.", "달리 방법이 없었소."], ["As an officer in His Majesty’s service, I was bound to skin him.", "국왕 폐하의 군에 복무하는 장교로서, 나는 그자의 가죽을 벗길 의무가 있었소."], ["I regretted the necessity, but it must be done.", "그 불가피함이 유감스러웠지만, 해야만 하는 일이었소."], ["He was open to conviction.", "그자는 설득에 귀를 기울였소."], ["He saw that the rules of the service were imperative.", "군의 규율이 절대적이라는 것을 알아본 거지. 그자는 달아났소."], ["He fled. I married her.", "나는 그녀와 결혼했고."], ["Here’s the coach.", "마차가 왔군."], ["That’s her head.’", "저게 그녀의 머리요.\""]], [["As Mr. Dowler concluded, he pointed to a stage which had just driven up, from the open window of which a rather pretty face in a bright blue bonnet was looking among the crowd on the pavement, most probably for the rash man himself.", "다울러 씨는 말을 마치며, 마침 막 들어온 역마차를 가리켰다."], ["Mr. Dowler paid his bill, and hurried out with his travelling cap, coat, and cloak; and Mr.", "그 열린 창문으로 밝은 파란색 보닛을 쓴 제법 예쁜 얼굴이 보도 위 군중 사이를 살펴보고 있었는데, 십중팔구 그 무모한 사내, 곧 자기를 찾는 것이리라."], ["Pickwick and his friends followed to secure their places.", "다울러 씨는 계산을 치르고 여행 모자와 외투와 망토를 챙겨 서둘러 나갔다. 그리고 픽윅 씨와 친구들도 자리를 잡으러 뒤따라 나갔다."]], [["Mr. Tupman and Mr. Snodgrass had seated themselves at the back part of the coach; Mr. Winkle had got inside; and Mr. Pickwick was preparing to follow him, when Sam Weller came up to his master, and whispering in his ear, begged to speak to him, with an air of the deepest mystery.", "터프먼 씨와 스노드그래스 씨는 마차의 뒤쪽에 자리를 잡았고, 윈클 씨는 안쪽에 올라탔으며, 픽윅 씨도 그를 뒤따르려는 참이었다. 그때 샘 웰러가 주인에게 다가와 귀에 대고 속삭이며, 더없이 은밀한 태도로 잠깐 이야기를 나누고 싶다고 청했다."]], [["‘Well, Sam,’ said Mr.", "\"그래, 샘.\""], ["Pickwick, ‘what’s the matter now?’", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"이번엔 또 무슨 일인가?\""]], [["‘Here’s rayther a rum go, sir,’ replied Sam.", "\"이거 좀 묘한 일입니다요, 나리.\" 샘이 대답했다."]], [["‘What?’ inquired Mr.", "\"뭔데?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘This here, Sir,’ rejoined Sam.", "\"이거 말입니다요, 나리.\" 샘이 대꾸했다."], ["‘I’m wery much afeerd, sir, that the properiator o’ this here coach is a playin’ some imperence vith us.’", "\"제 생각엔 말입니다요, 이 마차 주인이 우리한테 무슨 건방진 수작을 부리는 게 아닌가 아주 걱정이 됩니다요.\""]], [["‘How is that, Sam?’ said Mr.", "\"어째서 그러는가, 샘?\""], ["Pickwick; ‘aren’t the names down on the way-bill?’", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"운행표에 이름이 적혀 있지 않던가?\""]], [["‘The names is not only down on the vay-bill, Sir,’ replied Sam, ‘but they’ve painted vun on ‘em up, on the door o’ the coach.’ As Sam spoke, he pointed to that part of the coach door on which the proprietor’s name usually appears; and there, sure enough, in gilt letters of a goodly size, was the magic name of Pickwick!", "\"이름이 운행표에 적혀 있을 뿐만 아니라요, 나리,\" 샘이 대답했다. \"그중 하나를 마차 문짝에다 칠해 놨지 뭡니까요.\" 샘은 말하면서, 보통 마차 주인의 이름이 적히는 마차 문짝 부분을 가리켰다. 그러자 거기에는 정말로, 큼지막한 금빛 글자로 마법 같은 이름 픽윅이 적혀 있었다!"]], [["‘Dear me,’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"이런.\""], ["Pickwick, quite staggered by the coincidence; ‘what a very extraordinary thing!’", "픽윅 씨가 그 우연의 일치에 어지간히 놀라 외쳤다. \"참으로 별난 일이로군!\""]], [["‘Yes, but that ain’t all,’ said Sam, again directing his master’s attention to the coach door; ‘not content vith writin’ up “Pick-wick,”", "\"네, 그런데 그게 다가 아닙니다요.\" 샘이 다시 주인의 주의를 마차 문짝으로 돌리며 말했다."], ["they puts “Moses”", "\"픽윅이라고 써 놓는 걸로 만족하지 못하고, 그 앞에 모지스라고 붙여 놨지 뭡니까요."], ["afore it, vich I call addin’ insult to injury, as the parrot said ven they not only took him from his native land, but made him talk the English langwidge arterwards.’", "이건 다친 데 모욕을 더하는 격이라고 저는 말하겠습니다요. 그 앵무새가 한 말마따나요, 사람들이 그놈을 제 고향에서 데려온 것도 모자라 그 뒤에 영어까지 지껄이게 만들었다는 거 말입니다요.\""]], [["‘It’s odd enough, certainly, Sam,’ said Mr.", "\"확실히 충분히 이상한 일이구나, 샘.\""], ["Pickwick; ‘but if we stand talking here, we shall lose our places.’", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"하지만 우리가 여기 서서 떠들고 있다가는 자리를 놓치겠다.\""]], [["‘Wot, ain’t nothin’ to be done in consequence, sir?’ exclaimed Sam, perfectly aghast at the coolness with which Mr.", "\"아니, 그 결과로 아무것도 안 하실 겁니까요, 나리?\""], ["Pickwick prepared to ensconce himself inside.", "픽윅 씨가 안쪽에 자리 잡으려고 태연하게 준비하는 그 침착함에 완전히 기겁한 샘이 외쳤다."]], [["‘Done!’ said Mr.", "\"한다고!\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["‘What should be done?’", "\"뭘 한단 말이냐?\""]], [["Ain’t nobody to be whopped for takin’ this here liberty, sir?’ said Mr.", "\"이런 무례를 저질렀는데 누구 한 놈 두들겨 패 줘야 하는 거 아닙니까요, 나리?\""], ["Weller, who had expected that at least he would have been commissioned to challenge the guard and the coachman to a pugilistic encounter on the spot.", "웰러 씨가 말했다. 그는 적어도 자기가 그 자리에서 차장과 마부에게 주먹다짐을 신청하는 임무라도 맡으리라 기대했던 것이다."]], [["‘Certainly not,’ replied Mr.", "\"절대 아니다.\" 픽윅 씨가 간곡히 대답했다."], ["Pickwick eagerly; ‘not on any account.", "\"무슨 일이 있어도 안 된다."], ["Jump up to your seat directly.’", "당장 네 자리로 올라가거라.\""]], [["‘I am wery much afeered,’ muttered Sam to himself, as he turned away, ‘that somethin’ queer’s come over the governor, or he’d never ha’ stood this so quiet.", "\"제 생각엔 말이야,\" 샘은 돌아서며 혼잣말로 중얼거렸다."], ["I hope that ‘ere trial hasn’t broke his spirit, but it looks bad, wery bad.’ Mr.", "\"나리한테 뭔가 묘한 게 씌었어. 안 그러면 이걸 이렇게 조용히 넘기실 리가 없지."], ["Weller shook his head gravely; and it is worthy of remark, as an illustration of the manner in which he took this circumstance to heart, that he did not speak another word until the coach reached the Kensington turnpike.", "그 빌어먹을 재판이 나리 기개를 꺾어 놓지 않았어야 할 텐데, 영 안 좋아 보여, 아주 안 좋아.\" 웰러 씨는 심각하게 고개를 저었다."], ["Which was so long a time for him to remain taciturn, that the fact may be considered wholly unprecedented.", "그리고 그가 이 일을 얼마나 가슴에 새겼는지를 보여 주는 예로서 기록해 둘 만한 것은, 마차가 켄싱턴 통행 요금소에 이를 때까지 그가 한마디도 더 하지 않았다는 점이다. 그가 그토록 오래 입을 다물고 있었다는 것은, 가히 전례 없는 일이라 할 만했다."]], [["Nothing worthy of special mention occurred during the journey. Mr. Dowler related a variety of anecdotes, all illustrative of his own personal prowess and desperation, and appealed to Mrs. Dowler in corroboration thereof; when Mrs. Dowler invariably brought in, in the form of an appendix, some remarkable fact or circumstance which Mr. Dowler had forgotten, or had perhaps through modesty, omitted; for the addenda in every instance went to show that Mr. Dowler was even a more wonderful fellow than he made himself out to be. Mr. Pickwick and Mr. Winkle listened with great admiration, and at intervals conversed with Mrs. Dowler, who was a very agreeable and fascinating person. So, what between Mr. Dowler’s stories, and Mrs. Dowler’s charms, and Mr. Pickwick’s good-humour, and Mr. Winkle’s good listening, the insides contrived to be very companionable all the way.", "여행 중에 특별히 언급할 만한 일은 일어나지 않았다. 다울러 씨는 갖가지 일화를 늘어놓았는데, 하나같이 자기 자신의 용맹과 결사의 기개를 보여 주는 것들이었고, 그 입증을 위해 다울러 부인에게 동의를 구했다. 그러면 다울러 부인은 어김없이 부록 형식으로, 다울러 씨가 잊었거나 혹은 어쩌면 겸손해서 빠뜨린 어떤 놀라운 사실이나 정황을 끼워 넣었다. 그 추가 내용은 매번 다울러 씨가 자기가 스스로 내세우는 것보다도 한층 더 놀라운 사내임을 드러내는 것이었기 때문이다. 픽윅 씨와 윈클 씨는 크게 감탄하며 귀를 기울였고, 틈틈이 다울러 부인과 이야기를 나누었으니, 그녀는 매우 상냥하고 매력적인 사람이었다. 그리하여 다울러 씨의 이야기와 다울러 부인의 매력과 픽윅 씨의 유쾌한 기분과 윈클 씨의 훌륭한 경청 덕분에, 안쪽 승객들은 가는 내내 그럭저럭 아주 화기애애하게 지냈다."]], [["The outsides did as outsides always do.", "바깥쪽 승객들은 바깥쪽 승객들이 언제나 그러하듯 굴었다."], ["They were very cheerful and talkative at the beginning of every stage, and very dismal and sleepy in the middle, and very bright and wakeful again towards the end.", "그들은 매 구간 시작에는 아주 명랑하고 수다스러웠고, 중간에는 아주 침울하고 졸렸으며, 끝 무렵에는 다시 아주 활기차고 정신이 말짱했다."], ["There was one young gentleman in an India-rubber cloak, who smoked cigars all day; and there was another young gentleman in a parody upon a greatcoat, who lighted a good many, and feeling obviously unsettled after the second whiff, threw them away when he thought nobody was looking at him.", "인도 고무 망토를 걸친 한 젊은 신사는 온종일 시가를 피웠고, 외투랍시고 우스꽝스럽게 흉내 낸 것을 걸친 또 다른 젊은 신사는 시가를 여러 개 붙였다가 두 모금 빨고 나서는 분명 속이 거북한 듯, 아무도 자기를 안 본다 싶을 때 던져 버렸다."], ["There was a third young man on the box who wished to be learned in cattle; and an old one behind, who was familiar with farming.", "마부석에는 가축에 정통하고 싶어 하는 세 번째 젊은이가 있었고, 뒤쪽에는 농사일에 밝은 노인이 있었다."], ["There was a constant succession of Christian names in smock-frocks and white coats, who were invited to have a ‘lift’ by the guard, and who knew every horse and hostler on the road and off it; and there was a dinner which would have been cheap at half-a-crown a mouth, if any moderate number of mouths could have eaten it in the time. And at seven o’clock P.M.", "작업복과 흰 외투를 걸친 채 세례명으로 불리는 자들이 끊임없이 줄을 이었는데, 그들은 차장에게서 '태워 주겠다'는 권유를 받았고, 길 위에서나 길 밖에서나 말이며 마부며 모르는 게 없었다."], ["Mr. Pickwick and his friends, and Mr.", "그리고 만약 어지간한 수의 입들이 그 시간 안에 먹어 치울 수만 있었다면 한 입에 반 크라운이라도 싸다 할 만찬이 있었다."], ["Dowler and his wife, respectively retired to their private sitting-rooms at the White Hart Hotel, opposite the Great Pump Room, Bath, where the waiters, from their costume, might be mistaken for Westminster boys, only they destroy the illusion by behaving themselves much better.", "그리하여 저녁 일곱 시에 픽윅 씨와 친구들, 그리고 다울러 씨와 그의 아내는 각각, 배스의 대온천장 맞은편에 있는 화이트 하트 호텔의 자기네 전용 거실로 물러났다. 그곳 웨이터들은 차림새로 보아 웨스트민스터 학교 학생들로 오인받을 만했으나, 다만 훨씬 더 점잖게 처신함으로써 그 착각을 깨뜨렸다."]], [["Breakfast had scarcely been cleared away on the succeeding morning, when a waiter brought in Mr. Dowler’s card, with a request to be allowed permission to introduce a friend.", "이튿날 아침 식사가 채 치워지기도 전에, 웨이터 하나가 다울러 씨의 명함을 들여왔는데, 친구 한 사람을 소개하도록 허락해 달라는 청이 적혀 있었다."], ["Mr. Dowler at once followed up the delivery of the card, by bringing himself and the friend also.", "다울러 씨는 명함을 들여보낸 데 곧바로 뒤이어, 자기 자신과 그 친구를 함께 데리고 들어왔다."]], [["The friend was a charming young man of not much more than fifty, dressed in a very bright blue coat with resplendent buttons, black trousers, and the thinnest possible pair of highly-polished boots.", "그 친구는 쉰을 그리 넘기지 않은 매력적인 젊은이로, 번쩍이는 단추가 달린 매우 산뜻한 파란색 외투에 검은 바지를 입고, 한껏 광을 낸 더없이 얇은 부츠를 신고 있었다."], ["A gold eye-glass was suspended from his neck by a short, broad, black ribbon; a gold snuff-box was lightly clasped in his left hand; gold rings innumerable glittered on his fingers; and a large diamond pin set in gold glistened in his shirt frill.", "짧고 넓은 검은 리본으로 목에 매단 금테 안경이 늘어뜨려져 있었고, 금 코담배갑이 왼손에 가볍게 쥐여 있었으며, 손가락마다 셀 수 없이 많은 금반지가 반짝였고, 셔츠의 주름 장식에는 금에 박은 커다란 다이아몬드 핀이 빛났다."], ["He had a gold watch, and a gold curb chain with large gold seals; and he carried a pliant ebony cane with a gold top.", "그는 금시계와, 큼직한 금 도장이 달린 금 사슬을 지녔으며, 금 손잡이가 달린 낭창낭창한 흑단 지팡이를 들고 다녔다."], ["His linen was of the very whitest, finest, and stiffest; his wig of the glossiest, blackest, and curliest.", "그의 속옷은 더없이 희고, 곱고, 빳빳했으며, 그의 가발은 더없이 반들거리고, 검고, 곱슬곱슬했다."], ["His snuff was princes’ mixture; his scent bouquet du roi.", "그의 코담배는 프린스 믹스처였고, 향수는 부케 뒤 루아였다."], ["His features were contracted into a perpetual smile; and his teeth were in such perfect order that it was difficult at a small distance to tell the real from the false.", "그의 얼굴은 영원한 미소로 오므라져 있었고, 그의 이는 어찌나 가지런한지 조금만 떨어져서는 진짜와 가짜를 분간하기 어려울 정도였다."]], [["‘Mr. Pickwick,’ said Mr.", "\"픽윅 씨.\""], ["Dowler; ‘my friend, Angelo Cyrus Bantam, Esquire, M.C.; Bantam; Mr.", "다울러 씨가 말했다. \"제 친구, 안젤로 사이러스 밴텀 님, 의전관이올시다."], ["Pickwick.", "밴텀, 픽윅 씨라네."], ["Know each other.’", "서로 인사하시오.\""]], [["‘Welcome to Ba--ath, Sir.", "\"배스에 오신 것을 환영합니다, 선생님."], ["This is indeed an acquisition.", "이건 참으로 큰 소득입니다."], ["Most welcome to Ba--ath, sir.", "배스에 더없이 환영합니다, 선생님."], ["It is long--very long, Mr. Pickwick, since you drank the waters.", "오래되었군요—참 오래되었습니다, 픽윅 씨, 선생께서 이 온천물을 마신 지가요."], ["It appears an age, Mr. Pickwick.", "한 세월은 된 것 같습니다, 픽윅 씨."], ["Re-markable!’", "놀라운 일입니다!\""]], [["Such were the expressions with which Angelo Cyrus Bantam, Esquire, M.C., took Mr.", "안젤로 사이러스 밴텀 의전관 님이 픽윅 씨의 손을 잡으며 한 말들이 그러했다."], ["Pickwick’s hand; retaining it in his, meantime, and shrugging up his shoulders with a constant succession of bows, as if he really could not make up his mind to the trial of letting it go again.", "그동안 그는 그 손을 자기 손에 쥔 채로, 마치 그 손을 다시 놓는 시련을 정말이지 도무지 결단할 수 없다는 듯, 연거푸 절을 하며 어깨를 으쓱거렸다."]], [["‘It is a very long time since I drank the waters, certainly,’ replied Mr.", "\"제가 이 온천물을 마신 지가 아주 오래된 것은 확실합니다.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다."], ["Pickwick; ‘for, to the best of my knowledge, I was never here before.’", "\"왜냐하면 제가 아는 한, 저는 여기 와 본 적이 한 번도 없으니까요.\""]], [["‘Never in Ba--ath, Mr.", "\"배스에 와 보신 적이 없다니요, 픽윅 씨!\""], ["Pickwick!’ exclaimed the Grand Master, letting the hand fall in astonishment.", "그 위대한 의전관이 놀라 손을 떨어뜨리며 외쳤다."], ["‘Never in Ba--ath! He!", "\"배스에 와 보신 적이 없다니!"], ["he!", "헤!"], ["Mr.", "헤!"], ["Pickwick, you are a wag.", "픽윅 씨, 농담도 잘하십니다."], ["Not bad, not bad.", "나쁘지 않군요, 나쁘지 않아요."], ["Good, good.", "좋습니다, 좋아요."], ["He!", "헤!"], ["he!", "헤!"], ["he!", "헤!"], ["Re-markable!’", "놀랍습니다!\""]], [["‘To my shame, I must say that I am perfectly serious,’ rejoined Mr.", "\"부끄러운 일입니다만, 저는 완전히 진지하다는 말씀을 드려야겠습니다.\" 픽윅 씨가 대꾸했다."], ["Pickwick. ‘I really never was here before.’", "\"저는 정말로 여기 와 본 적이 한 번도 없습니다.\""]], [["‘Oh, I see,’ exclaimed the Grand Master, looking extremely pleased; ‘yes, yes--good, good--better and better.", "\"오, 알겠습니다.\" 그 위대한 의전관이 몹시 흡족해하는 표정으로 외쳤다."], ["You are the gentleman of whom we have heard.", "\"그래요, 그래—좋습니다, 좋아요—점점 더 좋습니다. 선생이 바로 저희가 소문으로 듣던 그분이시군요."], ["Yes; we know you, Mr.", "그래요, 저희는 선생을 압니다, 픽윅 씨."], ["Pickwick; we know you.’", "저희는 선생을 알지요.\""]], [["‘The reports of the trial in those confounded papers,’ thought Mr.", "'그 빌어먹을 신문들에 난 재판 기사로군.' 픽윅 씨는 생각했다."], ["Pickwick. ‘They have heard all about me.’", "'나에 관한 걸 죄다 들었구나.'"]], [["You are the gentleman residing on Clapham Green,’ resumed Bantam, ‘who lost the use of his limbs from imprudently taking cold after port wine; who could not be moved in consequence of acute suffering, and who had the water from the king’s bath bottled at one hundred and three degrees, and sent by wagon to his bedroom in town, where he bathed, sneezed, and the same day recovered.", "\"선생은 클래펌 그린에 사시는 그 신사분이지요.\" 밴텀이 다시 말을 이었다."], ["Very remarkable!’", "\"포트와인을 드신 뒤 경솔하게 감기에 걸려 사지를 못 쓰게 되셨고, 그 결과 극심한 고통으로 몸을 움직일 수 없게 되어, 킹스 배스의 온천물을 화씨 103도로 병에 담게 하여 짐마차로 도시의 침실까지 보내게 한 다음, 거기서 목욕을 하고 재채기를 하고는 그날로 회복하셨다지요. 참으로 놀라운 일입니다!\""]], [["Mr. Pickwick acknowledged the compliment which the supposition implied, but had the self-denial to repudiate it, notwithstanding; and taking advantage of a moment’s silence on the part of the M.C., begged to introduce his friends, Mr. Tupman, Mr. Winkle, and Mr. Snodgrass. An introduction which overwhelmed the M.C. with delight and honour.", "픽윅 씨는 그 추측이 함축한 칭찬을 받아들이긴 했으나, 그럼에도 그것을 부인하는 극기심을 발휘했다. 그리고 의전관이 잠시 침묵하는 틈을 타, 자기 친구들인 터프먼 씨, 윈클 씨, 스노드그래스 씨를 소개하기를 청했다. 그 소개는 의전관을 기쁨과 영광으로 압도했다."]], [["‘Bantam,’ said Mr.", "\"밴텀,\""], ["Dowler, ‘Mr.", "다울러 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick and his friends are strangers.", "\"픽윅 씨와 그 친구들은 이곳이 처음이라네."], ["They must put their names down.", "이름을 등록해야 하네."], ["Where’s the book?’", "그 명부는 어디 있나?\""]], [["‘The register of the distinguished visitors in Ba--ath will be at the Pump Room this morning at two o’clock,’ replied the M.C.", "\"배스를 찾은 귀빈 명부는 오늘 아침 두 시에 대온천장에 비치될 겁니다.\" 의전관이 대답했다."], ["‘Will you guide our friends to that splendid building, and enable me to procure their autographs?’", "\"우리 친구분들을 그 훌륭한 건물로 안내해 주셔서, 제가 그분들의 서명을 받을 수 있도록 해 주시겠습니까?\""]], [["‘I will,’ rejoined Dowler.", "\"그러지.\" 다울러가 대꾸했다."], ["‘This is a long call.", "\"방문이 길어졌군."], ["It’s time to go.", "갈 시간일세."], ["I shall be here again in an hour.", "한 시간 뒤에 다시 오겠소."], ["Come.’", "가세.\""]], [["‘This is a ball-night,’ said the M.C., again taking Mr.", "\"오늘은 무도회가 있는 밤입니다.\" 의전관이 일어서며 다시 픽윅 씨의 손을 잡고 말했다."], ["Pickwick’s hand, as he rose to go.", "\"배스의 무도회 밤은 낙원에서 떼어 온 순간들이지요."], ["‘The ball-nights in Ba--ath are moments snatched from paradise; rendered bewitching by music, beauty, elegance, fashion, etiquette, and--and--above all, by the absence of tradespeople, who are quite inconsistent with paradise, and who have an amalgamation of themselves at the Guildhall every fortnight, which is, to say the least, remarkable.", "음악과 미모와 우아함과 유행과 예절로, 그리고—그리고—무엇보다도 상인들이 없음으로 인해 황홀해지는 순간들입니다. 상인들이란 낙원과는 도무지 어울리지 않는 자들이고, 그자들은 격주로 길드홀에서 자기네끼리 한데 뭉치는데, 그건 좋게 말해도 놀라운 일이지요."], ["Good-bye, good-bye!’ and protesting all the way downstairs that he was most satisfied, and most delighted, and most overpowered, and most flattered, Angelo Cyrus Bantam, Esquire, M.C., stepped into a very elegant chariot that waited at the door, and rattled off.", "안녕히 계십시오, 안녕히!\" 그리하여 계단을 내려가는 내내 자기는 더없이 만족스럽고, 더없이 기쁘고, 더없이 압도되었으며, 더없이 영광스럽다고 거듭 말하면서, 안젤로 사이러스 밴텀 의전관 님은 문 앞에서 기다리던 매우 우아한 사륜마차에 올라타 덜커덩거리며 떠나갔다."]], [["At the appointed hour, Mr. Pickwick and his friends, escorted by Dowler, repaired to the Assembly Rooms, and wrote their names down in the book-- an instance of condescension at which Angelo Bantam was even more overpowered than before.", "정해진 시각에 픽윅 씨와 친구들은 다울러의 안내를 받아 집회장으로 가서 명부에 자기네 이름을 적었다—이 황송한 처사에 안젤로 밴텀은 전보다도 더욱 압도되었다."], ["Tickets of admission to that evening’s assembly were to have been prepared for the whole party, but as they were not ready, Mr. Pickwick undertook, despite all the protestations to the contrary of Angelo Bantam, to send Sam for them at four o’clock in the afternoon, to the M.C.’s house in Queen Square.", "그날 저녁 모임의 입장권은 일행 전부를 위해 미리 준비되었어야 했으나, 아직 마련되지 않았기에, 픽윅 씨는 안젤로 밴텀이 한사코 만류하는데도 불구하고, 오후 네 시에 퀸 광장에 있는 의전관의 집으로 샘을 보내 입장권을 받아 오게 하기로 했다."], ["Having taken a short walk through the city, and arrived at the unanimous conclusion that Park Street was very much like the perpendicular streets a man sees in a dream, which he cannot get up for the life of him, they returned to the White Hart, and despatched Sam on the errand to which his master had pledged him.", "시내를 잠시 거닐고 나서, 파크 거리가 꿈에서 보는 가파른 거리—아무리 애를 써도 도무지 올라갈 수 없는 그런 거리—와 매우 닮았다는 만장일치의 결론에 이른 뒤, 그들은 화이트 하트로 돌아왔고, 주인이 그에게 약속한 그 심부름을 위해 샘을 보냈다."]], [["Sam Weller put on his hat in a very easy and graceful manner, and, thrusting his hands in his waistcoat pockets, walked with great deliberation to Queen Square, whistling as he went along, several of the most popular airs of the day, as arranged with entirely new movements for that noble instrument the organ, either mouth or barrel.", "샘 웰러는 아주 편안하고 우아한 태도로 모자를 쓰고, 두 손을 조끼 주머니에 찔러 넣은 채 매우 느긋하게 퀸 광장으로 걸어갔다. 그러면서 그는 당대 가장 인기 있는 곡조 여러 개를, 입으로 부는 것이든 손잡이를 돌리는 것이든 저 고귀한 악기인 오르간을 위해 완전히 새로운 악장으로 편곡한 양 휘파람으로 불며 갔다."], ["Arriving at the number in Queen Square to which he had been directed, he left off whistling and gave a cheerful knock, which was instantaneously answered by a powdered-headed footman in gorgeous livery, and of symmetrical stature.", "안내받은 퀸 광장의 그 번지에 이르자, 그는 휘파람을 멈추고 명랑하게 문을 두드렸고, 즉각 화려한 제복을 입은, 균형 잡힌 체격에 머리에 분을 바른 하인이 응답했다."]], [["‘Is this here Mr.", "\"여기가 밴텀 씨 댁이오, 영감?\""], ["Bantam’s, old feller?’ inquired Sam Weller, nothing abashed by the blaze of splendour which burst upon his sight in the person of the powdered-headed footman with the gorgeous livery.", "샘 웰러가 화려한 제복을 입고 머리에 분을 바른 하인이라는 형상으로 눈앞에 펼쳐진 그 휘황한 광채에 조금도 주눅 들지 않고 물었다."]], [["‘Why, young man?’ was the haughty inquiry of the powdered-headed footman.", "\"무슨 일이오, 젊은이?\" 머리에 분을 바른 하인이 거만하게 물었다."]], [["‘’Cos if it is, jist you step in to him with that ‘ere card, and say Mr. Veller’s a-waitin’, will you?’ said Sam.", "\"여기가 거기라면, 이 명함을 들고 그분께 들어가서, 웰러 씨가 기다린다고 전해 주시겠소?\""], ["And saying it, he very coolly walked into the hall, and sat down.", "샘이 말했다. 그렇게 말하면서 그는 아주 태연하게 현관으로 걸어 들어가 앉았다."]], [["The powdered-headed footman slammed the door very hard, and scowled very grandly; but both the slam and the scowl were lost upon Sam, who was regarding a mahogany umbrella-stand with every outward token of critical approval.", "머리에 분을 바른 하인은 문을 몹시 세게 쾅 닫고, 매우 거창하게 인상을 찌푸렸다. 그러나 그 쾅 소리도 찌푸린 인상도 샘에게는 헛수고였으니, 그는 마호가니 우산꽂이를 비평적 찬탄의 온갖 외적 표시를 띠고 살펴보고 있었다."]], [["Apparently his master’s reception of the card had impressed the powdered-headed footman in Sam’s favour, for when he came back from delivering it, he smiled in a friendly manner, and said that the answer would be ready directly.", "명함에 대한 주인의 응대가 머리에 분을 바른 하인에게 샘에 대한 호의를 심어 준 듯했다. 명함을 전하고 돌아온 그가 다정한 태도로 미소를 지으며, 답장이 곧 준비될 거라고 말했기 때문이다."]], [["‘Wery good,’ said Sam.", "\"아주 좋소.\""], ["‘Tell the old gen’l’m’n not to put himself in a perspiration.", "샘이 말했다. \"그 영감님더러 진땀 빼지 마시라고 전하시오."], ["No hurry, six-foot.", "서두를 거 없소, 키다리 양반."], ["I’ve had my dinner.’", "나는 점심을 먹었으니.\""]], [["‘You dine early, sir,’ said the powdered-headed footman.", "\"점심을 일찍 드시는군요, 손님.\" 머리에 분을 바른 하인이 말했다."]], [["‘I find I gets on better at supper when I does,’ replied Sam.", "\"그래야 저녁이 더 잘 넘어가더라고요.\" 샘이 대답했다."]], [["‘Have you been long in Bath, sir?’ inquired the powdered-headed footman.", "\"배스에 오래 계셨습니까, 손님?\" 머리에 분을 바른 하인이 물었다."], ["‘I have not had the pleasure of hearing of you before.’", "\"전에 손님에 대한 소문을 들어 본 기쁨이 없어서요.\""]], [["‘I haven’t created any wery surprisin’ sensation here, as yet,’ rejoined Sam, ‘for me and the other fash’nables only come last night.’", "\"아직 여기서 무슨 대단한 화제를 일으키진 못했소.\" 샘이 대꾸했다. \"나하고 다른 멋쟁이분들은 어젯밤에야 막 왔으니 말이오.\""]], [["‘Nice place, Sir,’ said the powdered-headed footman.", "\"좋은 곳입니다, 손님.\" 머리에 분을 바른 하인이 말했다."]], [["‘Seems so,’ observed Sam.", "\"그런 것 같소.\" 샘이 한마디 했다."]], [["‘Pleasant society, sir,’ remarked the powdered-headed footman.", "\"기분 좋은 사교계지요, 손님.\" 머리에 분을 바른 하인이 말했다."], ["‘Very agreeable servants, sir.’", "\"아주 상냥한 하인들이 있고요, 손님.\""]], [["‘I should think they wos,’ replied Sam.", "\"그럴 거라고 생각했소.\" 샘이 대답했다."], ["‘Affable, unaffected, say- nothin’-to-nobody sorts o’ fellers.’", "\"싹싹하고, 꾸밈없고, 아무한테도-아무 말도-안 하는 그런 부류들 말이지.\""]], [["‘Oh, very much so, indeed, sir,’ said the powdered-headed footman, taking Sam’s remarks as a high compliment.", "\"오, 정말 그렇습니다, 손님.\" 샘의 말을 더없는 칭찬으로 받아들인 머리에 분을 바른 하인이 말했다."], ["‘Very much so indeed.", "\"정말 그렇고말고요."], ["Do you do anything in this way, Sir?’ inquired the tall footman, producing a small snuff-box with a fox’s head on the top of it.", "손님께서는 이쪽으로 뭐 좀 하십니까?\" 키다리 하인이 위에 여우 머리가 달린 작은 코담배갑을 꺼내며 물었다."]], [["‘Not without sneezing,’ replied Sam.", "\"재채기 없이는 못 하오.\" 샘이 대답했다."]], [["‘Why, it is difficult, sir, I confess,’ said the tall footman.", "\"음, 그게 어렵긴 합니다, 손님, 인정하지요.\" 키다리 하인이 말했다."], ["‘It may be done by degrees, Sir.", "\"차츰차츰 하면 됩니다, 손님."], ["Coffee is the best practice.", "커피가 제일 좋은 연습이지요."], ["I carried coffee, Sir, for a long time.", "저는 한동안 커피를 가지고 다녔습니다, 손님."], ["It looks very like rappee, sir.’", "그게 라피 담배랑 아주 비슷해 보이거든요.\""]], [["Here, a sharp peal at the bell reduced the powdered-headed footman to the ignominious necessity of putting the fox’s head in his pocket, and hastening with a humble countenance to Mr.", "이때 종이 날카롭게 울려 대는 바람에, 머리에 분을 바른 하인은 여우 머리를 주머니에 넣고 겸손한 낯빛으로 밴텀 씨의 '서재'로 서둘러 가야 하는 굴욕스러운 처지가 되었다."], ["Bantam’s ‘study.’ By the bye, who ever knew a man who never read or wrote either, who hadn’t got some small back parlour which he would call a study!", "그건 그렇고, 글을 읽지도 쓰지도 않으면서도 굳이 '서재'라고 부르는 작은 뒷방 하나쯤은 가지고 있지 않은 사람을 본 적이 있는가!"]], [["‘There is the answer, sir,’ said the powdered-headed footman.", "\"답장이 여기 있습니다, 손님.\" 머리에 분을 바른 하인이 말했다."], ["‘I’m afraid you’ll find it inconveniently large.’", "\"불편하실 만큼 크진 않을까 걱정입니다만.\""]], [["‘Don’t mention it,’ said Sam, taking a letter with a small enclosure.", "\"그런 말씀 마시오.\" 샘이 작은 동봉물이 든 편지를 받으며 말했다."], ["‘It’s just possible as exhausted natur’ may manage to surwive it.’", "\"기진맥진한 자연이 그럭저럭 이걸 견뎌 낼 수 있을지도 모르지요.\""]], [["‘I hope we shall meet again, Sir,’ said the powdered-headed footman, rubbing his hands, and following Sam out to the door-step.", "\"또 뵙기를 바랍니다, 손님.\" 머리에 분을 바른 하인이 두 손을 비비며 샘을 따라 문 앞 계단까지 나오며 말했다."]], [["‘You are wery obligin’, sir,’ replied Sam.", "\"참 친절하시오, 선생.\" 샘이 대답했다."], ["‘Now, don’t allow yourself to be fatigued beyond your powers; there’s a amiable bein’.", "\"자, 기운을 넘어설 만큼 너무 무리하지는 마시오. 참으로 사랑스러운 분이군."], ["Consider what you owe to society, and don’t let yourself be injured by too much work.", "당신이 사회에 진 빚을 생각하시고, 너무 많은 일로 몸 상하게 하지 마시오. 동포들을 위해서, 되도록 조용히 지내시오."], ["For the sake o’ your feller-creeturs, keep yourself as quiet as you can; only think what a loss you would be!’ With these pathetic words, Sam Weller departed.", "당신을 잃는다면 그 손실이 얼마나 클지 생각해 보시오!\" 이 가슴 절절한 말과 함께, 샘 웰러는 떠나갔다."]], [["‘A very singular young man that,’ said the powdered-headed footman, looking after Mr.", "\"참 별난 젊은이로군.\""], ["Weller, with a countenance which clearly showed he could make nothing of him.", "머리에 분을 바른 하인이 웰러 씨의 뒷모습을 바라보며 말했는데, 그 표정은 도무지 그를 이해할 수 없다는 것을 분명히 드러냈다."]], [["Sam said nothing at all.", "샘은 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["He winked, shook his head, smiled, winked again; and, with an expression of countenance which seemed to denote that he was greatly amused with something or other, walked merrily away.", "그는 한쪽 눈을 찡긋하고, 고개를 흔들고, 미소 짓고, 다시 한쪽 눈을 찡긋했다. 그러고는 뭔가에 몹시 즐거워하는 듯한 표정을 띠고서 명랑하게 걸어가 버렸다."]], [["At precisely twenty minutes before eight o’clock that night, Angelo Cyrus Bantam, Esq., the Master of the Ceremonies, emerged from his chariot at the door of the Assembly Rooms in the same wig, the same teeth, the same eye-glass, the same watch and seals, the same rings, the same shirt-pin, and the same cane.", "바로 그날 밤 여덟 시 이십 분 전, 의전관인 안젤로 사이러스 밴텀 님은, 똑같은 가발, 똑같은 이, 똑같은 안경, 똑같은 시계와 도장, 똑같은 반지들, 똑같은 셔츠 핀, 똑같은 지팡이 차림으로, 집회장 문 앞에서 자기 사륜마차에서 내려섰다."], ["The only observable alterations in his appearance were, that he wore a brighter blue coat, with a white silk lining, black tights, black silk stockings, and pumps, and a white waistcoat, and was, if possible, just a thought more scented.", "그의 차림새에서 눈에 띄게 달라진 점이라곤, 흰 비단 안감을 댄 한층 더 산뜻한 파란색 외투에 검은 짧은 바지와 검은 비단 양말과 펌프스를 신고, 흰 조끼를 입었으며, 가능하다면, 향수를 딱 한 점쯤 더 뿌렸다는 것뿐이었다."]], [["Thus attired, the Master of the Ceremonies, in strict discharge of the important duties of his all-important office, planted himself in the room to receive the company.", "이렇게 차려입고서, 의전관은 그 막중하기 그지없는 직책의 중요한 임무를 엄정히 수행하고자, 손님들을 맞이하러 홀에 자리를 잡았다."]], [["Bath being full, the company, and the sixpences for tea, poured in, in shoals.", "배스가 만원이었던 터라, 손님들과 차 값 6펜스 동전이 떼를 지어 쏟아져 들어왔다."], ["In the ballroom, the long card-room, the octagonal card-room, the staircases, and the passages, the hum of many voices, and the sound of many feet, were perfectly bewildering.", "무도회장과 기다란 카드실, 팔각형 카드실, 계단, 복도에서 수많은 목소리의 웅성거림과 수많은 발소리가 정신을 아득하게 했다."], ["Dresses rustled, feathers waved, lights shone, and jewels sparkled.", "드레스가 바스락거리고, 깃털이 나부끼고, 불빛이 빛나고, 보석이 반짝였다."], ["There was the music--not of the quadrille band, for it had not yet commenced; but the music of soft, tiny footsteps, with now and then a clear, merry laugh--low and gentle, but very pleasant to hear in a female voice, whether in Bath or elsewhere.", "음악이 있었으니—사각무 악단의 음악은 아니었으니 아직 시작되지 않은 까닭이요—부드럽고 자그마한 발소리의 음악이었고, 이따금 맑고 즐거운 웃음소리가 섞였으니, 그 웃음은 낮고 부드러웠으나 여성의 목소리로 듣기에는 매우 듣기 좋았으니, 배스에서든 다른 곳에서든 마찬가지였다."], ["Brilliant eyes, lighted up with pleasurable expectation, gleamed from every side; and, look where you would, some exquisite form glided gracefully through the throng, and was no sooner lost, than it was replaced by another as dainty and bewitching.", "즐거운 기대로 빛나는 영롱한 눈동자들이 사방에서 반짝였고, 어디를 둘러보든 어떤 우아한 자태가 인파 사이를 매끄럽게 미끄러져 가다가, 사라지기가 무섭게 그에 못지않게 앙증맞고 매혹적인 또 다른 자태로 대체되곤 했다."]], [["In the tea-room, and hovering round the card-tables, were a vast number of queer old ladies, and decrepit old gentlemen, discussing all the small talk and scandal of the day, with a relish and gusto which sufficiently bespoke the intensity of the pleasure they derived from the occupation.", "다실 안과 카드 탁자 주위를 맴도는 곳에는, 그날의 온갖 잡담과 추문을 논하는 별난 노부인들과 노쇠한 노신사들이 어마어마하게 많았는데, 그 음미하는 맛과 흥취가 그들이 그 소일거리에서 얻는 즐거움이 얼마나 강렬한지를 충분히 말해 주었다."], ["Mingled with these groups, were three or four match-making mammas, appearing to be wholly absorbed by the conversation in which they were taking part, but failing not from time to time to cast an anxious sidelong glance upon their daughters, who, remembering the maternal injunction to make the best use of their youth, had already commenced incipient flirtations in the mislaying scarves, putting on gloves, setting down cups, and so forth; slight matters apparently, but which may be turned to surprisingly good account by expert practitioners.", "이 무리들 사이에 섞여 있는 것은 서너 명의 혼사 주선에 열중한 어머니들이었으니, 그들은 자기가 끼어든 대화에 온통 몰두한 듯이 보였으나, 때때로 딸들에게 근심 어린 곁눈질을 던지는 것을 잊지 않았다. 그 딸들은 젊음을 한껏 활용하라는 어머니의 당부를 기억하여, 벌써 풋풋한 추파 던지기에 들어가 있었으니, 스카프를 흘리고, 장갑을 끼고, 찻잔을 내려놓고 하는 따위였다—겉보기엔 사소한 일들이나, 능숙한 솜씨꾼들의 손에서는 놀랍도록 큰 효과를 낼 수 있는 것들이었다."]], [["Lounging near the doors, and in remote corners, were various knots of silly young men, displaying various varieties of puppyism and stupidity; amusing all sensible people near them with their folly and conceit; and happily thinking themselves the objects of general admiration--a wise and merciful dispensation which no good man will quarrel with.", "문 가까이며 외진 구석에서 어슬렁거리는 것은 어리석은 젊은이들의 여러 무리였으니, 갖가지 종류의 멋부림과 우둔함을 뽐내며, 자기네 곁에 있는 분별 있는 모든 이들을 그 어리석음과 자만으로 즐겁게 해 주었고, 다행히도 자기네가 만인의 찬탄의 대상이라고 여기고 있었으니—이는 어떤 선량한 사람도 시비 걸지 않을 현명하고 자비로운 섭리였다."]], [["And lastly, seated on some of the back benches, where they had already taken up their positions for the evening, were divers unmarried ladies past their grand climacteric, who, not dancing because there were no partners for them, and not playing cards lest they should be set down as irretrievably single, were in the favourable situation of being able to abuse everybody without reflecting on themselves.", "그리고 마지막으로, 저녁 내내 이미 자리를 잡은 뒤쪽 의자 몇 군데에 앉아 있는 것은, 한창때를 넘긴 미혼의 여러 부인들이었으니, 그들은 상대가 없어 춤을 추지 않았고, 돌이킬 수 없이 노처녀로 낙인찍힐까 두려워 카드도 하지 않았기에, 자기 자신은 돌아보지 않고서 누구든 헐뜯을 수 있는 유리한 처지에 있었다."], ["In short, they could abuse everybody, because everybody was there.", "요컨대, 그들은 누구든 헐뜯을 수 있었으니, 모두가 거기 있었던 까닭이다."], ["It was a scene of gaiety, glitter, and show; of richly-dressed people, handsome mirrors, chalked floors, girandoles and wax-candles; and in all parts of the scene, gliding from spot to spot in silent softness, bowing obsequiously to this party, nodding familiarly to that, and smiling complacently on all, was the sprucely-attired person of Angelo Cyrus Bantam, Esquire, the Master of the Ceremonies.", "그것은 화려함과 광채와 볼거리의 광경이었고, 호화롭게 차려입은 사람들과 멋진 거울들과 백묵 가루를 뿌린 마룻바닥과 가지촛대와 밀랍 양초의 광경이었다. 그리고 그 광경 곳곳에서, 소리 없이 부드럽게 이곳저곳을 미끄러져 다니며, 이쪽 무리에게는 굽실굽실 절하고, 저쪽에게는 허물없이 고갯짓하고, 모두에게 흡족하게 미소 짓는 것은, 말끔하게 차려입은 의전관 안젤로 사이러스 밴텀 님의 모습이었다."]], [["‘Stop in the tea-room.", "\"다실에서 멈추시오."], ["Take your sixpenn’orth. Then lay on hot water, and call it tea.", "6펜스어치를 받으시오."], ["Drink it,’ said Mr. Dowler, in a loud voice, directing Mr.", "그런 다음 뜨거운 물을 부어 그걸 차라 부르시오."], ["Pickwick, who advanced at the head of the little party, with Mrs. Dowler on his arm.", "그걸 드시오.\""], ["Into the tea-room Mr. Pickwick turned; and catching sight of him, Mr.", "다울러 씨가 큰 소리로, 다울러 부인을 팔에 끼고 작은 일행의 선두에 서서 나아가는 픽윅 씨에게 일러 주며 말했다."], ["Bantam corkscrewed his way through the crowd and welcomed him with ecstasy.", "픽윅 씨는 다실로 들어섰고, 그를 발견한 밴텀 씨는 인파를 코르크 마개 따듯 비집고 나아와 황홀하게 그를 맞이했다."]], [["‘My dear Sir, I am highly honoured.", "\"선생님, 더없는 영광입니다."], ["Ba--ath is favoured. Mrs.", "배스가 은총을 입었군요."], ["Dowler, you embellish the rooms.", "다울러 부인, 부인께서 이 방들을 빛내 주십니다."], ["I congratulate you on your feathers.", "그 깃털을 축하드립니다."], ["Re- markable!’", "놀랍습니다!\""]], [["‘Anybody here?’ inquired Dowler suspiciously.", "\"여기 누구 와 있나?\" 다울러가 미심쩍은 듯 물었다."]], [["‘Anybody!", "\"누구라니요!"], ["The elite of Ba--ath. Mr.", "배스의 최상류층이지요."], ["Pickwick, do you see the old lady in the gauze turban?’", "픽윅 씨, 저 사 모자를 쓴 노부인이 보이십니까?\""]], [["‘The fat old lady?’ inquired Mr.", "\"저 뚱뚱한 노부인 말입니까?\""], ["Pickwick innocently.", "픽윅 씨가 순진하게 물었다."]], [["‘Hush, my dear sir--nobody’s fat or old in Ba--ath.", "\"쉿, 선생님—배스에서는 누구도 뚱뚱하거나 늙지 않았답니다."], ["That’s the Dowager Lady Snuphanuph.’", "저분이 스누퍼누프 미망인 마님이십니다.\""]], [["‘Is it, indeed?’ said Mr.", "\"정말입니까?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘No less a person, I assure you,’ said the Master of the Ceremonies.", "\"바로 그분이올시다, 장담합니다.\" 의전관이 말했다."], ["‘Hush.", "\"쉿."], ["Draw a little nearer, Mr. Pickwick.", "좀 더 가까이 오십시오, 픽윅 씨."], ["You see the splendidly- dressed young man coming this way?’", "이쪽으로 오고 있는 저 호화롭게 차려입은 젊은이가 보이십니까?\""]], [["‘The one with the long hair, and the particularly small forehead?’ inquired Mr.", "\"머리가 길고 이마가 유난히 좁은 저 사람 말입니까?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘The same.", "\"바로 그자입니다."], ["The richest young man in Ba--ath at this moment.", "지금 이 순간 배스에서 가장 부유한 젊은이지요."], ["Young Lord Mutanhed.’", "젊은 머탄헤드 경이올시다.\""]], [["‘You don’t say so?’ said Mr.", "\"설마요?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Yes.", "\"그렇습니다."], ["You’ll hear his voice in a moment, Mr.", "곧 그분 목소리를 듣게 되실 겁니다, 픽윅 씨."], ["Pickwick. He’ll speak to me.", "그분이 제게 말을 걸 테니까요."], ["The other gentleman with him, in the red under-waistcoat and dark moustache, is the Honourable Mr. Crushton, his bosom friend.", "그분과 함께 있는, 붉은 속조끼에 검은 콧수염을 기른 저 다른 신사분은 크러시턴 님이신데, 그분의 절친한 벗이지요."], ["How do you do, my Lord?’", "안녕하십니까, 경?\""]], [["‘Veway hot, Bantam,’ said his Lordship.", "\"무척 덥구먼, 밴텀.\" 경께서 말했다."]], [["‘It is very warm, my Lord,’ replied the M.C.", "\"정말 무척 따뜻합니다, 경.\" 의전관이 대답했다."]], [["‘Confounded,’ assented the Honourable Mr.", "\"지독하지.\""], ["Crushton.", "크러시턴 님이 동의했다."]], [["‘Have you seen his Lordship’s mail-cart, Bantam?’ inquired the Honourable Mr. Crushton, after a short pause, during which young Lord Mutanhed had been endeavouring to stare Mr.", "\"경의 우편 마차를 보셨습니까, 밴텀?\""], ["Pickwick out of countenance, and Mr. Crushton had been reflecting what subject his Lordship could talk about best.", "크러시턴 님이 잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤 물었는데, 그동안 젊은 머탄헤드 경은 픽윅 씨를 빤히 쳐다보아 무안하게 만들려 애쓰고 있었고, 크러시턴 씨는 경께서 어떤 화제를 가장 잘 떠들 수 있을지 궁리하고 있었다."]], [["‘Dear me, no,’ replied the M.C.", "\"이런, 못 봤습니다.\" 의전관이 대답했다."], ["‘A mail-cart!", "\"우편 마차라니!"], ["What an excellent idea.", "참으로 훌륭한 발상이십니다."], ["Re-markable!’", "놀랍습니다!\""]], [["‘Gwacious heavens!’ said his Lordship, ‘I thought evewebody had seen the new mail-cart; it’s the neatest, pwettiest, gwacefullest thing that ever wan upon wheels. Painted wed, with a cweam piebald.’", "\"맙소사!\" 경께서 말했다. \"다들 그 새 우편 마차를 본 줄 알았는데. 바퀴로 굴러간 것 중에 그렇게 산뜻하고 예쁘고 우아한 건 없지. 빨간색으로 칠하고, 크림빛 얼룩말을 매달았다네.\""]], [["‘With a real box for the letters, and all complete,’ said the Honourable Mr.", "\"편지를 넣는 진짜 함까지 달려서, 완전히 다 갖춰져 있지요.\""], ["Crushton.", "크러시턴 님이 말했다."]], [["‘And a little seat in fwont, with an iwon wail, for the dwiver,’ added his Lordship.", "\"그리고 앞쪽엔 마부를 위한 작은 자리가, 쇠 난간을 둘러서 있다네.\" 경께서 덧붙였다."], ["‘I dwove it over to Bwistol the other morning, in a cwimson coat, with two servants widing a quarter of a mile behind; and confound me if the people didn’t wush out of their cottages, and awest my pwogwess, to know if I wasn’t the post.", "\"요전 날 아침엔 진홍색 외투를 입고 그걸 몰아 브리스틀까지 갔는데, 하인 둘이 사 분의 일 마일쯤 뒤에서 따라왔지. 그런데 빌어먹을, 사람들이 오두막에서 뛰쳐나와 내 갈 길을 막고서, 내가 우편배달부가 아니냐고 묻지 뭔가."], ["Glorwious--glorwious!’", "영광이었지—영광이었어!\""]], [["At this anecdote his Lordship laughed very heartily, as did the listeners, of course.", "이 일화에 경께서 무척 호탕하게 웃었고, 물론 듣던 이들도 그랬다."], ["Then, drawing his arm through that of the obsequious Mr. Crushton, Lord Mutanhed walked away.", "그러고는 굽실거리는 크러시턴 씨의 팔에 자기 팔을 끼고, 머탄헤드 경은 걸어가 버렸다."]], [["‘Delightful young man, his Lordship,’ said the Master of the Ceremonies.", "\"참 유쾌한 젊은이지요, 경께서는.\" 의전관이 말했다."]], [["‘So I should think,’ rejoined Mr.", "\"그런 것 같군요.\""], ["Pickwick drily.", "픽윅 씨가 무뚝뚝하게 대꾸했다."]], [["The dancing having commenced, the necessary introductions having been made, and all preliminaries arranged, Angelo Bantam rejoined Mr. Pickwick, and led him into the card-room.", "춤이 시작되고, 필요한 소개가 다 이루어지고, 모든 사전 준비가 마련되자, 안젤로 밴텀은 픽윅 씨에게 다시 합류하여 그를 카드실로 데려갔다."]], [["Just at the very moment of their entrance, the Dowager Lady Snuphanuph and two other ladies of an ancient and whist-like appearance, were hovering over an unoccupied card-table; and they no sooner set eyes upon Mr.", "그들이 들어선 바로 그 순간, 스누퍼누프 미망인 마님과, 고색창연하고 휘스트깨나 칠 듯한 풍모의 다른 두 부인이 비어 있는 카드 탁자 위를 맴돌고 있었다."], ["Pickwick under the convoy of Angelo Bantam, than they exchanged glances with each other, seeing that he was precisely the very person they wanted, to make up the rubber.", "그들은 안젤로 밴텀의 호위를 받는 픽윅 씨에게 눈길이 닿기가 무섭게, 그가 바로 자기네가 러버 판을 채우는 데 필요한 바로 그 사람임을 알아보고서 서로 눈짓을 주고받았다."]], [["‘My dear Bantam,’ said the Dowager Lady Snuphanuph coaxingly, ‘find us some nice creature to make up this table; there’s a good soul.’ Mr. Pickwick happened to be looking another way at the moment, so her Ladyship nodded her head towards him, and frowned expressively.", "\"밴텀, 이 친구.\" 스누퍼누프 미망인 마님이 어르듯 말했다. \"이 탁자를 채울 멋진 분을 좀 찾아 주시오. 착한 양반이니.\" 마침 그 순간 픽윅 씨가 다른 쪽을 보고 있었던 터라, 마님께서는 그를 향해 고갯짓을 하고 의미심장하게 눈살을 찌푸렸다."]], [["‘My friend Mr. Pickwick, my Lady, will be most happy, I am sure, remarkably so,’ said the M.C., taking the hint.", "\"제 친구 픽윅 씨가, 마님, 더없이 기뻐할 겁니다, 분명히, 대단히 기뻐할 거예요.\""], ["‘Mr. Pickwick, Lady Snuphanuph--Mrs.", "그 암시를 알아챈 의전관이 말했다."], ["Colonel Wugsby--Miss Bolo.’", "\"픽윅 씨, 스누퍼누프 마님—웍스비 대령 부인—볼로 양이올시다.\""]], [["Mr. Pickwick bowed to each of the ladies, and, finding escape impossible, cut. Mr. Pickwick and Miss Bolo against Lady Snuphanuph and Mrs. Colonel Wugsby.", "픽윅 씨는 부인들에게 일일이 절을 하고, 빠져나갈 수 없음을 알고서 패를 갈랐다. 픽윅 씨와 볼로 양이 스누퍼누프 마님과 웍스비 대령 부인을 상대하게 되었다."]], [["As the trump card was turned up, at the commencement of the second deal, two young ladies hurried into the room, and took their stations on either side of Mrs. Colonel Wugsby’s chair, where they waited patiently until the hand was over.", "두 번째 패를 돌리기 시작할 때 으뜸패가 뒤집히는 순간, 두 젊은 숙녀가 서둘러 방으로 들어와 웍스비 대령 부인의 의자 양옆에 자리를 잡고, 그 판이 끝날 때까지 참을성 있게 기다렸다."]], [["‘Now, Jane,’ said Mrs.", "\"자, 제인.\""], ["Colonel Wugsby, turning to one of the girls, ‘what is it?’", "웍스비 대령 부인이 두 처녀 중 하나에게 돌아서며 말했다. \"무슨 일이냐?\""]], [["I came to ask, ma, whether I might dance with the youngest Mr.", "\"엄마, 막내 크롤리 씨와 춤을 춰도 되는지 여쭤보러 왔어요.\""], ["Crawley,’ whispered the prettier and younger of the two.", "둘 중 더 예쁘고 어린 쪽이 속삭였다."]], [["‘Good God, Jane, how can you think of such things?’ replied the mamma indignantly.", "\"맙소사, 제인, 어떻게 그런 생각을 할 수 있니?\" 어머니가 분개하여 대답했다."], ["‘Haven’t you repeatedly heard that his father has eight hundred a year, which dies with him?", "\"그 사람 아버지가 연 팔백 파운드 수입이 있는데 그게 본인 대에서 끊긴다는 말을 누누이 듣지 않았니?"], ["I am ashamed of you.", "네가 부끄럽구나."], ["Not on any account.’", "무슨 일이 있어도 안 된다.\""]], [["‘Ma,’ whispered the other, who was much older than her sister, and very insipid and artificial, ‘Lord Mutanhed has been introduced to me.", "\"엄마,\" 다른 쪽이 속삭였는데, 그녀는 동생보다 훨씬 나이가 많고 매우 맥없고 꾸민 티가 났다."], ["I said I thought I wasn’t engaged, ma.’", "\"머탄헤드 경께서 제게 소개되었어요. 제 생각엔 약속이 없는 것 같다고 말씀드렸어요, 엄마.\""]], [["‘You’re a sweet pet, my love,’ replied Mrs.", "\"넌 참 사랑스러운 내 보배다, 얘야.\""], ["Colonel Wugsby, tapping her daughter’s cheek with her fan, ‘and are always to be trusted.", "웍스비 대령 부인이 부채로 딸의 뺨을 톡톡 두드리며 대답했다."], ["He’s immensely rich, my dear.", "\"언제나 믿음직스럽지."], ["Bless you!’ With these words Mrs.", "그분은 어마어마하게 부자란다, 얘야."], ["Colonel Wugsby kissed her eldest daughter most affectionately, and frowning in a warning manner upon the other, sorted her cards.", "복 받으렴!\" 이 말과 함께 웍스비 대령 부인은 큰딸에게 더없이 다정하게 입을 맞추고, 다른 딸에게는 경고하듯 눈살을 찌푸리고서, 자기 패를 추렸다."]], [["Poor Mr. Pickwick!", "가엾은 픽윅 씨!"], ["he had never played with three thorough-paced female card-players before.", "그는 노련하기 짝이 없는 세 여성 카드꾼과 함께 쳐 본 적이 한 번도 없었다."], ["They were so desperately sharp, that they quite frightened him.", "그들은 어찌나 지독하게 날카로운지 그는 아주 겁이 났다."], ["If he played a wrong card, Miss Bolo looked a small armoury of daggers; if he stopped to consider which was the right one, Lady Snuphanuph would throw herself back in her chair, and smile with a mingled glance of impatience and pity to Mrs. Colonel Wugsby, at which Mrs.", "그가 잘못된 패를 내면 볼로 양은 단검 한 무더기 같은 눈빛을 보냈고, 그가 멈춰 서서 어느 패가 옳은지 곰곰이 생각하면 스누퍼누프 마님은 의자에 몸을 젖히고 웍스비 대령 부인에게 짜증과 연민이 뒤섞인 눈길로 미소 지었으며, 그러면 웍스비 대령 부인은 어깨를 으쓱하고 기침을 했으니, 마치 저 양반이 도대체 시작이나 할는지 모르겠다고 말하는 듯했다."], ["Colonel Wugsby would shrug up her shoulders, and cough, as much as to say she wondered whether he ever would begin. Then, at the end of every hand, Miss Bolo would inquire with a dismal countenance and reproachful sigh, why Mr.", "그러고는 매 판이 끝날 때마다 볼로 양은 침울한 낯빛과 책망 어린 한숨으로, 픽윅 씨가 왜 그 다이아몬드를 받아 내지 않았는지, 혹은 클럽을 내지 않았는지, 혹은 스페이드를 끊지 않았는지, 혹은 하트로 수를 부리지 않았는지, 혹은 으뜸패를 거쳐 내지 않았는지, 혹은 에이스를 내지 않았는지, 혹은 킹에 맞춰 내지 않았는지, 그 비슷한 무언가를 묻곤 했다."], ["Pickwick had not returned that diamond, or led the club, or roughed the spade, or finessed the heart, or led through the honour, or brought out the ace, or played up to the king, or some such thing; and in reply to all these grave charges, Mr. Pickwick would be wholly unable to plead any justification whatever, having by this time forgotten all about the game.", "그리고 이 모든 엄중한 추궁에 대해, 픽윅 씨는 이쯤 되자 게임에 관한 것을 죄다 잊어버린 터라, 무슨 변명도 전혀 내놓을 수가 없었다."], ["People came and looked on, too, which made Mr. Pickwick nervous.", "게다가 사람들이 와서 구경까지 하는 바람에 픽윅 씨는 긴장이 됐다."], ["Besides all this, there was a great deal of distracting conversation near the table, between Angelo Bantam and the two Misses Matinter, who, being single and singular, paid great court to the Master of the Ceremonies, in the hope of getting a stray partner now and then.", "이 모든 것에 더하여, 탁자 가까이서는 안젤로 밴텀과 두 마틴터 양 사이에 정신 사나운 대화가 잔뜩 오갔으니, 그들은 미혼인 데다 별나기까지 한지라, 이따금 짝 없는 상대라도 하나 얻을까 하는 희망에서 의전관에게 한껏 비위를 맞추고 있었다."], ["All these things, combined with the noises and interruptions of constant comings in and goings out, made Mr.", "끊임없는 출입의 소음과 방해가 더해진 이 모든 것이 픽윅 씨가 패를 다소 서투르게 치게 만들었다."], ["Pickwick play rather badly; the cards were against him, also; and when they left off at ten minutes past eleven, Miss Bolo rose from the table considerably agitated, and went straight home, in a flood of tears and a sedan-chair.", "패도 그에게 불리했다. 그리고 그들이 열한 시 십 분에 판을 끝냈을 때, 볼로 양은 상당히 격앙되어 탁자에서 일어나, 눈물을 펑펑 쏟으며 가마를 타고 곧장 집으로 가 버렸다."]], [["Being joined by his friends, who one and all protested that they had scarcely ever spent a more pleasant evening, Mr. Pickwick accompanied them to the White Hart, and having soothed his feelings with something hot, went to bed, and to sleep, almost simultaneously.", "친구들이 합류했는데, 하나같이 이보다 더 유쾌한 저녁을 보낸 적이 거의 없다고 입을 모으는 가운데, 픽윅 씨는 그들과 함께 화이트 하트로 갔고, 뜨거운 무언가로 마음을 달랜 다음, 잠자리에 들면서 거의 동시에 잠이 들었다."]], [["CHAPTER XXXVI. THE CHIEF FEATURES OF WHICH WILL BE FOUND TO BE AN AUTHENTIC VERSION OF THE LEGEND OF PRINCE BLADUD, AND A MOST EXTRAORDINARY CALAMITY THAT BEFELL MR.", "픽윅 씨는 적어도 두 달은 배스에 머물 생각이었으므로, 그동안 자신과 친구들이 쓸 개인 숙소를 빌려 두는 편이 좋겠다고 판단했다."], ["WINKLE\nAs Mr. Pickwick contemplated a stay of at least two months in Bath, he deemed it advisable to take private lodgings for himself and friends for that period; and as a favourable opportunity offered for their securing, on moderate terms, the upper portion of a house in the Royal Crescent, which was larger than they required, Mr.", "마침 로열 크레센트에 있는 어느 집의 위층을 적당한 조건으로 확보할 좋은 기회가 생겼는데, 그 공간은 필요 이상으로 넓었으므로, 다울러 부부가 침실 하나와 거실 하나를 맡아 부담을 덜어 주겠다고 제안했다."], ["and Mrs. Dowler offered to relieve them of a bedroom and sitting-room.", "이 제안은 곧바로 받아들여졌고, 사흘 만에 그들은 모두 새 거처에 자리를 잡았으며, 그때부터 픽윅 씨는 더없는 부지런함으로 광천수를 마시기 시작했다."], ["This proposition was at once accepted, and in three days’ time they were all located in their new abode, when Mr. Pickwick began to drink the waters with the utmost assiduity.", "픽윅 씨는 체계적으로 그것을 마셨다."], ["Mr. Pickwick took them systematically.", "아침 식사 전에 4분의 1파인트를 마시고 언덕을 올라갔다가, 아침 식사 후에 또 4분의 1파인트를 마시고 언덕을 내려왔다."], ["He drank a quarter of a pint before breakfast, and then walked up a hill; and another quarter of a pint after breakfast, and then walked down a hill; and, after every fresh quarter of a pint, Mr. Pickwick declared, in the most solemn and emphatic terms, that he felt a great deal better; whereat his friends were very much delighted, though they had not been previously aware that there was anything the matter with him.", "그리고 새로 4분의 1파인트를 마실 때마다, 픽윅 씨는 더없이 엄숙하고 단호한 어조로 자신이 훨씬 나아진 것 같다고 선언했고, 그러면 친구들은 무척 기뻐했다—비록 그들은 그 전까지 그에게 어디 아픈 데가 있는 줄도 몰랐지만."]], [["The Great Pump Room is a spacious saloon, ornamented with Corinthian pillars, and a music-gallery, and a Tompion clock, and a statue of Nash, and a golden inscription, to which all the water-drinkers should attend, for it appeals to them in the cause of a deserving charity.", "대(大)펌프 룸은 코린트식 기둥과 음악 회랑, 톰피온 시계, 내시의 조각상, 그리고 모든 물 마시는 이들이 주목해야 할 금빛 명문(銘文)으로 장식된 널찍한 살롱인데, 그 명문은 마땅히 도울 만한 자선 사업을 위해 그들에게 호소하는 글이다."], ["There is a large bar with a marble vase, out of which the pumper gets the water; and there are a number of yellow-looking tumblers, out of which the company get it; and it is a most edifying and satisfactory sight to behold the perseverance and gravity with which they swallow it.", "그곳에는 대리석 항아리가 놓인 커다란 바가 있어 물 따르는 사람이 거기서 물을 길어 내고, 노르스름한 텀블러가 여럿 있어 손님들이 거기에 받아 마시는데, 그들이 얼마나 끈기 있고 진지하게 그것을 삼키는지를 바라보는 것은 더없이 교훈적이고 만족스러운 광경이다."], ["There are baths near at hand, in which a part of the company wash themselves; and a band plays afterwards, to congratulate the remainder on their having done so.", "가까운 곳에는 목욕탕이 있어 손님 중 일부가 몸을 씻고, 그러고 나면 악단이 연주하여 그들이 그 일을 해낸 것을 나머지 사람들과 함께 축하해 준다."], ["There is another pump room, into which infirm ladies and gentlemen are wheeled, in such an astonishing variety of chairs and chaises, that any adventurous individual who goes in with the regular number of toes, is in imminent danger of coming out without them; and there is a third, into which the quiet people go, for it is less noisy than either.", "또 다른 펌프 룸도 있는데, 거기에는 병약한 신사 숙녀들이 어찌나 놀랍도록 다양한 의자와 가마에 실려 들어오는지, 정상적인 수의 발가락을 갖추고 들어간 모험적인 인물은 그것들을 잃은 채 나올 위험이 코앞에 닥칠 정도이다. 그리고 세 번째 펌프 룸도 있어, 조용한 사람들이 그리로 가는데, 그곳이 다른 두 곳보다 덜 시끄럽기 때문이다."], ["There is an immensity of promenading, on crutches and off, with sticks and without, and a great deal of conversation, and liveliness, and pleasantry.", "목발을 짚거나 짚지 않거나, 지팡이를 들거나 들지 않거나 하며 거니는 사람들이 어마어마하게 많고, 대화와 활기와 익살도 무척 풍성하다."]], [["Every morning, the regular water-drinkers, Mr. Pickwick among the number, met each other in the pump room, took their quarter of a pint, and walked constitutionally.", "매일 아침, 정규로 물을 마시는 이들은—픽윅 씨도 그 가운데 하나였다—펌프 룸에서 서로 만나, 4분의 1파인트씩 마시고는 건강을 위해 산책했다."], ["At the afternoon’s promenade, Lord Mutanhed, and the Honourable Mr. Crushton, the Dowager Lady Snuphanuph, Mrs.", "오후의 산책 때에는 무탄헤드 경과 명예로운 크러시턴 씨, 미망인 스누퍼누프 부인, 워그스비 대령 부인, 그 밖의 온갖 지체 높은 사람들과 아침에 물을 마신 모든 이들이 성대한 회합을 이루며 만났다."], ["Colonel Wugsby, and all the great people, and all the morning water-drinkers, met in grand assemblage. After this, they walked out, or drove out, or were pushed out in bath-chairs, and met one another again.", "그러고 나면 그들은 걸어서 나가거나, 마차를 타고 나가거나, 목욕 의자에 밀려 나가서는 다시 서로 만났다."], ["After this, the gentlemen went to the reading-rooms, and met divisions of the mass.", "그러고 나면 신사들은 열람실로 가서 무리의 일부와 만났다."], ["After this, they went home.", "그러고 나면 집으로 돌아갔다."], ["If it were theatre-night, perhaps they met at the theatre; if it were assembly-night, they met at the rooms; and if it were neither, they met the next day.", "연극이 있는 밤이면 어쩌면 극장에서 만나고, 무도회가 있는 밤이면 무도장에서 만났으며, 둘 다 아니면 이튿날 만났다."], ["A very pleasant routine, with perhaps a slight tinge of sameness.", "어쩌면 약간의 단조로운 기색이 감돌긴 했지만, 무척 즐거운 일과였다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick was sitting up by himself, after a day spent in this manner, making entries in his journal, his friends having retired to bed, when he was roused by a gentle tap at the room door.", "이런 식으로 하루를 보낸 어느 날, 친구들은 모두 잠자리에 들고 픽윅 씨 혼자 늦게까지 앉아 일기에 기록을 적고 있었는데, 방문을 가볍게 두드리는 소리에 정신이 들었다."]], [["‘Beg your pardon, Sir,’ said Mrs.", "\"실례합니다, 선생님.\""], ["Craddock, the landlady, peeping in; ‘but did you want anything more, sir?’", "집주인 크래덕 부인이 안을 들여다보며 말했다. \"혹시 더 필요하신 것이라도 있으신지요, 선생님?\""]], [["‘Nothing more, ma’am,’ replied Mr.", "\"더 필요한 건 없습니다, 부인.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘My young girl is gone to bed, Sir,’ said Mrs.", "\"제 어린 하녀는 잠자리에 들었고요, 선생님.\""], ["Craddock; ‘and Mr.", "크래덕 부인이 말했다."], ["Dowler is good enough to say that he’ll sit up for Mrs.", "\"다울러 씨께서 친절하게도 다울러 부인을 기다려 늦게까지 앉아 있겠다고 하셨어요. 파티가 늦게야 끝날 것 같으니까요."], ["Dowler, as the party isn’t expected to be over till late; so I was thinking that if you wanted nothing more, Mr. Pickwick, I would go to bed.’", "그래서 저는 픽윅 선생님께서 더 필요하신 게 없으시다면 저도 자러 갈까 생각하고 있었답니다.\""]], [["‘By all means, ma’am,’ replied Mr.", "\"얼마든지 그러십시오, 부인.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘Wish you good-night, Sir,’ said Mrs.", "\"안녕히 주무세요, 선생님.\""], ["Craddock.", "크래덕 부인이 말했다."]], [["‘Good-night, ma’am,’ rejoined Mr.", "\"안녕히 주무십시오, 부인.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 화답했다."]], [["Mrs. Craddock closed the door, and Mr. Pickwick resumed his writing.", "크래덕 부인이 문을 닫았고, 픽윅 씨는 다시 글쓰기를 이어 갔다."]], [["In half an hour’s time the entries were concluded.", "반 시간쯤 지나 기록이 마무리되었다."], ["Mr. Pickwick carefully rubbed the last page on the blotting-paper, shut up the book, wiped his pen on the bottom of the inside of his coat tail, and opened the drawer of the inkstand to put it carefully away.", "픽윅 씨는 마지막 장(張)을 압지로 정성껏 문지르고, 책을 덮은 뒤, 펜을 자기 코트 자락 안쪽 밑단에 닦고는, 그것을 잘 넣어 두려고 잉크스탠드 서랍을 열었다."], ["There were a couple of sheets of writing-paper, pretty closely written over, in the inkstand drawer, and they were folded so, that the title, which was in a good round hand, was fully disclosed to him.", "잉크스탠드 서랍 안에는 꽤 빽빽이 글이 적힌 편지지 두어 장이 들어 있었는데, 제법 둥글둥글한 좋은 필체로 쓴 제목이 그에게 온전히 드러나도록 접혀 있었다."], ["Seeing from this, that it was no private document; and as it seemed to relate to Bath, and was very short: Mr. Pick-wick unfolded it, lighted his bedroom candle that it might burn up well by the time he finished; and drawing his chair nearer the fire, read as follows--", "그것을 보고 사적인 문서가 아님을 알았고, 배스에 관한 것인 듯하며 길이도 무척 짧았으므로, 픽윅 씨는 그것을 펼치고, 다 읽을 즈음 잘 타오르도록 침실 양초에 불을 붙인 뒤, 의자를 난로 가까이 끌어당기고 다음과 같이 읽어 나갔다—"]], [["# THE TRUE LEGEND OF PRINCE BLADUD", "# THE TRUE LEGEND OF PRINCE BLADUD"]], [["‘Less than two hundred years ago, on one of the public baths in this city, there appeared an inscription in honour of its mighty founder, the renowned Prince Bladud.", "블래더드 왕자의 참된 전설\n\"이백 년이 채 되지 않은 옛날, 이 도시의 공중목욕탕 중 하나에는 그 위대한 창건자, 이름 높은 블래더드 왕자를 기리는 명문이 새겨져 있었다."], ["That inscription is now erased.", "그 명문은 이제 지워졌다."]], [["‘For many hundred years before that time, there had been handed down, from age to age, an old legend, that the illustrious prince being afflicted with leprosy, on his return from reaping a rich harvest of knowledge in Athens, shunned the court of his royal father, and consorted moodily with husbandman and pigs.", "\"그보다 수백 년 전부터, 한 시대에서 다음 시대로 옛 전설 하나가 전해 내려왔으니, 그 명망 높은 왕자가 아테네에서 풍성한 지식의 수확을 거두고 돌아오던 중 나병에 걸려, 부왕(父王)의 궁정을 피하고 침울하게 농부와 돼지들과 어울려 지냈다는 이야기였다."], ["Among the herd (so said the legend) was a pig of grave and solemn countenance, with whom the prince had a fellow-feeling--for he too was wise--a pig of thoughtful and reserved demeanour; an animal superior to his fellows, whose grunt was terrible, and whose bite was sharp.", "그 무리 가운데에는(전설은 이렇게 말한다) 진지하고 엄숙한 표정을 한 돼지 한 마리가 있었으니, 왕자는 그 돼지에게 동질감을 느꼈다—그 또한 현명했기 때문이다—사려 깊고 과묵한 태도를 지닌 돼지였고, 동료들보다 뛰어난 짐승이었으며, 그 꿀꿀거리는 소리는 무시무시했고 무는 힘은 날카로웠다."], ["The young prince sighed deeply as he looked upon the countenance of the majestic swine; he thought of his royal father, and his eyes were bedewed with tears.", "젊은 왕자는 그 위풍당당한 돼지의 얼굴을 바라보며 깊이 한숨을 쉬었다. 부왕을 떠올리자 그의 두 눈은 눈물로 젖어 들었다."]], [["‘This sagacious pig was fond of bathing in rich, moist mud.", "\"이 영리한 돼지는 기름지고 축축한 진흙 속에서 목욕하기를 좋아했다."], ["Not in summer, as common pigs do now, to cool themselves, and did even in those distant ages (which is a proof that the light of civilisation had already begun to dawn, though feebly), but in the cold, sharp days of winter.", "요즘 흔한 돼지들이 그러하고 또 그 먼 옛날에도 그러했던 것처럼(이는 비록 미약하게나마 문명의 빛이 이미 동트기 시작했다는 증거다) 여름철에 몸을 식히기 위해서가 아니라, 춥고 매서운 겨울날에 그리했다."], ["His coat was ever so sleek, and his complexion so clear, that the prince resolved to essay the purifying qualities of the same water that his friend resorted to.", "그 돼지의 털은 늘 어찌나 매끄럽고 그 살빛은 어찌나 맑았던지, 왕자는 자기 친구가 즐겨 찾는 바로 그 물의 정화하는 효능을 시험해 보기로 결심했다."], ["He made the trial.", "그는 시험해 보았다."], ["Beneath that black mud, bubbled the hot springs of Bath.", "그 검은 진흙 아래에서 배스의 온천이 부글부글 솟아오르고 있었다."], ["He washed, and was cured.", "그는 몸을 씻었고, 병이 나았다."], ["Hastening to his father’s court, he paid his best respects, and returning quickly hither, founded this city and its famous baths.", "부왕의 궁정으로 서둘러 가 최고의 경의를 표한 뒤, 재빨리 이곳으로 돌아와 이 도시와 그 유명한 목욕탕을 세웠다."]], [["‘He sought the pig with all the ardour of their early friendship--but, alas!", "\"그는 옛 우정의 온갖 열정을 다해 그 돼지를 찾았으나—아아!"], ["the waters had been his death.", "그 물이 돼지의 죽음이 되고 말았다."], ["He had imprudently taken a bath at too high a temperature, and the natural philosopher was no more!", "돼지는 경솔하게도 너무 높은 온도의 목욕을 했고, 그 자연철학자는 더 이상 이 세상 존재가 아니었다!"], ["He was succeeded by Pliny, who also fell a victim to his thirst for knowledge.", "그의 뒤를 이은 것은 플리니우스였으니, 그 또한 지식을 향한 갈증의 희생양이 되었다."]], [["‘This was the legend.", "\"이상이 그 전설이었다."], ["Listen to the true one.", "이제 참된 이야기를 들어 보라."]], [["‘A great many centuries since, there flourished, in great state, the famous and renowned Lud Hudibras, king of Britain.", "\"아주 여러 세기 전, 영국의 왕 그 유명하고 이름난 러드 휴디브래스가 크나큰 위세를 떨치며 번영했다."], ["He was a mighty monarch.", "그는 강대한 군주였다."], ["The earth shook when he walked--he was so very stout.", "그가 걸으면 대지가 흔들렸으니—그가 너무도 뚱뚱했기 때문이다."], ["His people basked in the light of his countenance--it was so red and glowing.", "그의 백성은 그의 얼굴빛 속에서 햇볕을 쬐었으니—그 얼굴이 너무도 붉고 이글거렸기 때문이다."], ["He was, indeed, every inch a king.", "그는 참으로 어느 모로 보나 왕이었다."], ["And there were a good many inches of him, too, for although he was not very tall, he was a remarkable size round, and the inches that he wanted in height, he made up in circumference.", "게다가 그에게는 '모(吋)'가 꽤 여럿 있었으니, 키가 그리 크지는 않았어도 둘레로는 놀랄 만한 크기였고, 키에서 모자란 모는 둘레로 채웠던 것이다."], ["If any degenerate monarch of modern times could be in any way compared with him, I should say the venerable King Cole would be that illustrious potentate.", "현대의 타락한 군주 가운데 누구라도 그에게 견줄 만한 자가 있다면, 나는 저 유서 깊은 콜 왕이야말로 그 이름난 권력자라고 말하겠다."]], [["‘This good king had a queen, who eighteen years before, had had a son, who was called Bladud.", "\"이 선량한 왕에게는 왕비가 있었으니, 그 왕비는 십팔 년 전에 아들을 하나 낳았고 그 이름을 블래더드라 했다. 그는 열 살이 될 때까지 부왕의 영토에 있는 예비 학교로 보내졌다가, 그 후 믿을 만한 사자(使者)의 손에 맡겨져 아테네의 마무리 학교로 파견되었다."], ["He was sent to a preparatory seminary in his father’s dominions until he was ten years old, and was then despatched, in charge of a trusty messenger, to a finishing school at Athens; and as there was no extra charge for remaining during the holidays, and no notice required previous to the removal of a pupil, there he remained for eight long years, at the expiration of which time, the king his father sent the lord chamberlain over, to settle the bill, and to bring him home; which, the lord chamberlain doing, was received with shouts, and pensioned immediately.", "그리고 방학 동안 머물러도 추가 비용이 들지 않았고, 학생을 데려갈 때 사전 통지도 필요치 않았으므로, 그는 그곳에 여덟 해 동안이나 머물렀으며, 그 기간이 끝나자 부왕은 시종장(侍從長)을 보내 청구서를 정산하고 그를 집으로 데려오게 했다. 시종장이 그 일을 해내자, 그는 환호 속에 영접받고 즉시 연금을 받았다."]], [["‘When King Lud saw the prince his son, and found he had grown up such a fine young man, he perceived what a grand thing it would be to have him married without delay, so that his children might be the means of perpetuating the glorious race of Lud, down to the very latest ages of the world.", "\"러드 왕은 아들인 왕자를 보고 그가 그토록 훌륭한 청년으로 자란 것을 알자, 지체 없이 그를 결혼시키는 것이 얼마나 멋진 일일지를 깨달았으니, 그리하면 그 자손들이 러드의 영광스러운 혈통을 세상 가장 후세에 이르도록 영구히 이어 갈 방편이 될 터였다."], ["With this view, he sent a special embassy, composed of great noblemen who had nothing particular to do, and wanted lucrative employment, to a neighbouring king, and demanded his fair daughter in marriage for his son; stating at the same time that he was anxious to be on the most affectionate terms with his brother and friend, but that if they couldn’t agree in arranging this marriage, he should be under the unpleasant necessity of invading his kingdom and putting his eyes out.", "이런 생각으로 그는, 특별히 할 일이 없으며 돈벌이 되는 일자리를 원하는 위대한 귀족들로 구성된 특별 사절단을 이웃 나라 왕에게 보내어, 자기 아들의 아내로 그 아리따운 딸을 달라고 요구했다. 그러면서 동시에 자신은 형제이자 벗인 그와 더없이 다정한 사이로 지내고 싶지만, 만일 이 혼사를 주선하는 데 뜻이 맞지 않는다면 부득이 그의 왕국을 침공하여 그의 두 눈을 후벼 파야 하는 달갑잖은 처지에 놓일 것이라고 밝혔다."], ["To this, the other king (who was the weaker of the two) replied that he was very much obliged to his friend and brother for all his goodness and magnanimity, and that his daughter was quite ready to be married, whenever Prince Bladud liked to come and fetch her.", "이에 대해 다른 왕은(둘 중 더 약한 쪽이었다) 자신의 벗이자 형제인 그의 온갖 호의와 도량에 깊이 감사하며, 블래더드 왕자가 언제든 와서 딸을 데려가고자 하면 자기 딸은 결혼할 준비가 충분히 되어 있다고 답했다."]], [["‘This answer no sooner reached Britain, than the whole nation was transported with joy.", "\"이 답이 영국에 닿기가 무섭게 온 나라가 기쁨에 휩싸였다."], ["Nothing was heard, on all sides, but the sounds of feasting and revelry--except the chinking of money as it was paid in by the people to the collector of the royal treasures, to defray the expenses of the happy ceremony.", "사방에서 들리는 것이라곤 잔치와 흥청거림의 소리뿐이었다—다만 그 행복한 의식의 비용을 치르고자 백성들이 왕실 재고 징수관에게 바치는 돈이 짤랑이는 소리만은 예외였다."], ["It was upon this occasion that King Lud, seated on the top of his throne in full council, rose, in the exuberance of his feelings, and commanded the lord chief justice to order in the richest wines and the court minstrels--an act of graciousness which has been, through the ignorance of traditionary historians, attributed to King Cole, in those celebrated lines in which his Majesty is represented as", "바로 이 자리에서 러드 왕은 어전 회의 중 옥좌 꼭대기에 앉았다가 벅찬 감정에 못 이겨 일어나, 대법관에게 가장 값진 포도주와 궁정 악사들을 들이라 명했으니—이 너그러운 처사는 전승 사가(史家)들의 무지로 인해 콜 왕에게 돌려졌던바, 그 폐하가 다음과 같이 묘사되는 저 유명한 시구가 그것이다."]], [["Calling for his pipe, and calling for his pot, And calling for his fiddlers three.", "파이프를 청하고, 술단지를 청하며, 세 명의 바이올린 연주자를 청하셨네."]], [["Which is an obvious injustice to the memory of King Lud, and a dishonest exaltation of the virtues of King Cole.", "이는 러드 왕의 기억에 대한 명백한 부당함이요, 콜 왕의 미덕을 부정직하게 떠받드는 일이다."]], [["‘But, in the midst of all this festivity and rejoicing, there was one individual present, who tasted not when the sparkling wines were poured forth, and who danced not, when the minstrels played.", "\"그러나 이 모든 잔치와 환희 한가운데, 반짝이는 포도주가 따라질 때 입에 대지 않고, 악사들이 연주할 때 춤추지 않는 인물이 하나 있었다."], ["This was no other than Prince Bladud himself, in honour of whose happiness a whole people were, at that very moment, straining alike their throats and purse- strings.", "그는 다름 아닌 블래더드 왕자 본인이었으니, 바로 그 순간 온 백성이 그의 행복을 기리며 목청과 지갑 끈을 한꺼번에 쥐어짜고 있던 그 장본인이었다."], ["The truth was, that the prince, forgetting the undoubted right of the minister for foreign affairs to fall in love on his behalf, had, contrary to every precedent of policy and diplomacy, already fallen in love on his own account, and privately contracted himself unto the fair daughter of a noble Athenian.", "사실인즉, 왕자는 외무대신이 자기를 대신해 사랑에 빠질 의심할 바 없는 권리를 가졌다는 것을 잊고서, 정치와 외교의 온갖 선례에 어긋나게 이미 자기 멋대로 사랑에 빠져, 어느 고귀한 아테네인의 아리따운 딸과 남몰래 혼약을 맺어 둔 것이었다."]], [["‘Here we have a striking example of one of the manifold advantages of civilisation and refinement.", "\"여기서 우리는 문명과 세련됨이 지닌 수많은 이점 가운데 하나의 두드러진 본보기를 본다."], ["If the prince had lived in later days, he might at once have married the object of his father’s choice, and then set himself seriously to work, to relieve himself of the burden which rested heavily upon him.", "만일 왕자가 후세에 살았더라면, 그는 아버지가 택한 상대와 곧장 결혼한 뒤, 자기를 무겁게 짓누르는 그 짐을 덜고자 본격적으로 일에 착수할 수 있었으리라."], ["He might have endeavoured to break her heart by a systematic course of insult and neglect; or, if the spirit of her sex, and a proud consciousness of her many wrongs had upheld her under this ill-treatment, he might have sought to take her life, and so get rid of her effectually.", "체계적인 모욕과 무관심의 과정을 통해 그녀의 가슴을 찢어 놓으려 애쓸 수도 있었을 테고, 혹은 여성 특유의 기개와 자신이 당한 숱한 부당함에 대한 도도한 자각이 이런 학대 속에서도 그녀를 지탱해 주었다면, 그녀의 목숨을 빼앗아 깨끗이 그녀를 치워 버리려 들었을지도 모른다."], ["But neither mode of relief suggested itself to Prince Bladud; so he solicited a private audience, and told his father.", "그러나 이 두 가지 해소 방법 어느 것도 블래더드 왕자에게는 떠오르지 않았다. 그래서 그는 단독 알현을 청하여 부왕에게 사실을 털어놓았다."]], [["‘It is an old prerogative of kings to govern everything but their passions.", "\"제 격정을 빼고는 무엇이든 다스리는 것이 왕의 오랜 특권이다."], ["King Lud flew into a frightful rage, tossed his crown up to the ceiling, and caught it again--for in those days kings kept their crowns on their heads, and not in the Tower--stamped the ground, rapped his forehead, wondered why his own flesh and blood rebelled against him, and, finally, calling in his guards, ordered the prince away to instant Confinement in a lofty turret; a course of treatment which the kings of old very generally pursued towards their sons, when their matrimonial inclinations did not happen to point to the same quarter as their own.", "러드 왕은 무시무시한 격노에 사로잡혀 왕관을 천장까지 던졌다가 다시 받았으니—그 시절에는 왕들이 왕관을 탑(塔)이 아니라 머리에 두고 있었기 때문이다—땅을 쾅쾅 구르고, 제 이마를 두드리며, 어찌하여 제 살붙이가 자신에게 반역하는지 의아해하다가, 마침내 호위병들을 불러들여 왕자를 즉각 높다란 망루에 가두라 명했으니, 이는 옛 왕들이 자기 아들들의 혼인 의향이 자신과 같은 쪽을 향하지 않을 때 흔히 취하던 처사였다."]], [["‘When Prince Bladud had been shut up in the lofty turret for the greater part of a year, with no better prospect before his bodily eyes than a stone wall, or before his mental vision than prolonged imprisonment, he naturally began to ruminate on a plan of escape, which, after months of preparation, he managed to accomplish; considerately leaving his dinner- knife in the heart of his jailer, lest the poor fellow (who had a family) should be considered privy to his flight, and punished accordingly by the infuriated king.", "\"블래더드 왕자가 한 해의 거의 대부분을 그 높다란 망루에 갇혀, 육신의 눈앞에는 돌벽보다 나은 전망이 없고, 마음의 눈앞에는 기약 없는 감금보다 나은 전망이 없게 되자, 그는 자연히 탈출 계획을 궁리하기 시작했고, 여러 달의 준비 끝에 그것을 용케 실행해 냈다. 그러면서 사려 깊게도 자신의 만찬용 칼을 간수의 심장에 꽂아 두었으니, 행여 그 가엾은 사람이(처자식이 있었다) 그의 도주에 가담한 것으로 여겨져 격분한 왕에게 합당한 벌을 받지 않게 하려는 배려였다."]], [["‘The monarch was frantic at the loss of his son.", "\"군주는 아들을 잃고 미칠 듯이 날뛰었다."], ["He knew not on whom to vent his grief and wrath, until fortunately bethinking himself of the lord chamberlain who had brought him home, he struck off his pension and his head together.", "누구에게 자신의 비통과 분노를 쏟아 내야 할지 알 수 없던 차에, 다행히 왕자를 집으로 데려왔던 시종장이 생각나, 그자의 연금과 머리를 한꺼번에 잘라 버렸다."]], [["‘Meanwhile, the young prince, effectually disguised, wandered on foot through his father’s dominions, cheered and supported in all his hardships by sweet thoughts of the Athenian maid, who was the innocent cause of his weary trials.", "\"한편, 감쪽같이 변장한 젊은 왕자는 부왕의 영토를 걸어서 떠돌았으니, 자신의 고단한 시련의 죄 없는 빌미가 된 그 아테네 처녀에 대한 달콤한 생각이 온갖 고생 속에서도 그를 북돋우고 떠받쳐 주었다."], ["One day he stopped to rest in a country village; and seeing that there were gay dances going forward on the green, and gay faces passing to and fro, ventured to inquire of a reveller who stood near him, the reason for this rejoicing.", "어느 날 그는 한 시골 마을에서 쉬려고 걸음을 멈췄는데, 풀밭에서 흥겨운 춤이 벌어지고 명랑한 얼굴들이 오가는 것을 보고는, 가까이 서 있던 한 흥겨운 사람에게 이 환희의 까닭을 감히 물어보았다."]], [["‘“Know you not, O stranger,”", "\"'아니, 그대는 모르시오, 오 나그네여,'"], ["was the reply, “of the recent proclamation of our gracious king?”", "하는 대답이 돌아왔다. '우리 자비로우신 왕께서 최근에 내리신 포고를 말이오?'"]], [["‘“Proclamation! No.", "\"'포고라니!"], ["What proclamation?”", "모르오."], ["rejoined the prince--for he had travelled along the by and little-frequented ways, and knew nothing of what had passed upon the public roads, such as they were.", "무슨 포고 말이오?' 왕자가 되물었다—그가 한적하고 인적 드문 샛길로만 다녀, 그런 큰길에서 무슨 일이 일어났는지 전혀 몰랐기 때문이다."]], [["‘“Why,”", "\"'아니 글쎄,' 농부가 대답했다."], ["replied the peasant, “the foreign lady that our prince wished to wed, is married to a foreign noble of her own country, and the king proclaims the fact, and a great public festival besides; for now, of course, Prince Bladud will come back and marry the lady his father chose, who they say is as beautiful as the noonday sun.", "'우리 왕자께서 혼인하려 하시던 외국 아가씨가 제 나라의 외국 귀족과 결혼해 버려서, 왕께서 그 사실을 포고하시고 게다가 큰 공공 축제까지 여신 거요. 이제 당연히 블래더드 왕자께서 돌아오셔서 부왕께서 택하신 아가씨와 혼인하실 테니까요."], ["Your health, sir.", "그 아가씨가 한낮의 태양처럼 아름답다고들 합디다."], ["God save the king!”", "자, 당신의 건강을 위하여, 신께서 왕을 지켜 주시기를!'"]], [["‘The prince remained to hear no more.", "\"왕자는 더 듣고 있지 않았다."], ["He fled from the spot, and plunged into the thickest recesses of a neighbouring wood.", "그는 그 자리에서 달아나, 이웃한 숲의 가장 깊은 후미진 곳으로 뛰어들었다."], ["On, on, he wandered, night and day; beneath the blazing sun, and the cold pale moon; through the dry heat of noon, and the damp cold of night; in the gray light of morn, and the red glare of eve.", "그는 밤낮으로 계속 떠돌았다. 이글거리는 태양 아래, 차고 창백한 달 아래, 한낮의 메마른 더위를 뚫고, 밤의 축축한 추위를 뚫고, 아침의 잿빛 어스름 속에서, 저녁의 붉은 노을 속에서."], ["So heedless was he of time or object, that being bound for Athens, he wandered as far out of his way as Bath.", "그는 시간이나 목적지에 어찌나 무심했던지, 아테네를 향해 가던 것이 길을 벗어나 배스까지나 헤매어 왔다."]], [["‘There was no city where Bath stands, then.", "\"그때는 배스가 서 있는 자리에 도시라곤 없었다."], ["There was no vestige of human habitation, or sign of man’s resort, to bear the name; but there was the same noble country, the same broad expanse of hill and dale, the same beautiful channel stealing on, far away, the same lofty mountains which, like the troubles of life, viewed at a distance, and partially obscured by the bright mist of its morning, lose their ruggedness and asperity, and seem all ease and softness.", "그 이름으로 불릴 만한 인간 거주지의 흔적도, 사람들이 모여드는 기색도 없었다. 그러나 거기에는 똑같은 고귀한 들판이 있었고, 언덕과 골짜기가 똑같이 너르게 펼쳐져 있었으며, 똑같이 아름다운 물줄기가 저 멀리로 슬며시 흘러갔고, 인생의 시련이 멀리서 바라보고 그 아침의 환한 안개에 부분적으로 가려졌을 때 그 험준함과 거침을 잃고 온통 편안하고 부드러워 보이듯, 똑같이 우뚝 솟은 산들이 있었다."], ["Moved by the gentle beauty of the scene, the prince sank upon the green turf, and bathed his swollen feet in his tears.", "그 경치의 그윽한 아름다움에 마음이 움직여, 왕자는 푸른 잔디 위에 주저앉아 부르튼 두 발을 자신의 눈물로 적셨다."]], [["‘“Oh!”", "\"'오!'"], ["said the unhappy Bladud, clasping his hands, and mournfully raising his eyes towards the sky, “would that my wanderings might end here!", "불행한 블래더드가 두 손을 맞잡고 슬프게 두 눈을 하늘로 들어 올리며 말했다. '내 방랑이 여기서 끝날 수 있다면!"], ["Would that these grateful tears with which I now mourn hope misplaced, and love despised, might flow in peace for ever!”", "그릇된 희망과 멸시받은 사랑을 이제 애도하는 이 고마운 눈물이 영원히 평화로이 흐를 수 있다면!'"]], [["‘The wish was heard.", "\"그 소원은 들어졌다."], ["It was in the time of the heathen deities, who used occasionally to take people at their words, with a promptness, in some cases, extremely awkward.", "그때는 이교의 신들이 다스리던 시절이었으니, 그들은 이따금 사람들의 말을 곧이곧대로 받아들이곤 했고, 어떤 경우에는 그 신속함이 지극히 곤란하기도 했다."], ["The ground opened beneath the prince’s feet; he sank into the chasm; and instantaneously it closed upon his head for ever, save where his hot tears welled up through the earth, and where they have continued to gush forth ever since.", "왕자의 발밑에서 땅이 갈라졌고, 그는 그 틈으로 가라앉았으며, 그 순간 그것이 영영 그의 머리 위로 닫혔으니, 다만 그의 뜨거운 눈물이 땅을 뚫고 솟아오른 곳만은 예외였고, 그곳에서 그 눈물은 그 이래로 줄곧 솟구쳐 나오고 있다."]], [["‘It is observable that, to this day, large numbers of elderly ladies and gentlemen who have been disappointed in procuring partners, and almost as many young ones who are anxious to obtain them, repair annually to Bath to drink the waters, from which they derive much strength and comfort.", "\"눈여겨볼 만한 것은, 오늘날까지도 짝을 구하지 못해 실망한 수많은 노년의 신사 숙녀들과, 짝을 얻고자 애태우는 그에 못지않게 많은 젊은이들이, 해마다 배스로 와서 그 물을 마시고 거기서 큰 힘과 위안을 얻는다는 사실이다."], ["This is most complimentary to the virtue of Prince Bladud’s tears, and strongly corroborative of the veracity of this legend.’", "이는 블래더드 왕자의 눈물이 지닌 효험에 더없이 찬사를 보내는 일이요, 이 전설의 진실성을 강력히 뒷받침해 주는 일이다.\""]], [["Mr. Pickwick yawned several times when he had arrived at the end of this little manuscript, carefully refolded, and replaced it in the inkstand drawer, and then, with a countenance expressive of the utmost weariness, lighted his chamber candle, and went upstairs to bed.", "픽윅 씨는 이 짤막한 원고의 끝에 이르렀을 때 여러 차례 하품을 하고는, 그것을 조심스레 다시 접어 잉크스탠드 서랍에 도로 넣은 뒤, 더없는 피로가 어린 얼굴로 침실 양초에 불을 붙이고 잠자리에 들려고 위층으로 올라갔다."]], [["He stopped at Mr. Dowler’s door, according to custom, and knocked to say good-night.", "그는 늘 하던 대로 다울러 씨의 방문 앞에 멈춰, 잘 자라고 인사하려 노크했다."]], [["‘Ah!’ said Dowler, ‘going to bed?", "\"아!\" 다울러가 말했다."], ["I wish I was.", "\"주무시러 가시오?"], ["Dismal night.", "나도 그랬으면 좋겠소. 음울한 밤이군."], ["Windy; isn’t it?’", "바람이 부오. 안 그렇소?\""]], [["‘Very,’ said Mr.", "\"몹시 그렇군요.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["‘Good-night.’\n‘Good-night.’", "\"안녕히 주무십시오.\""]], [["Mr. Pickwick went to his bedchamber, and Mr.", "\"안녕히 주무시오.\""], ["Dowler resumed his seat before the fire, in fulfilment of his rash promise to sit up till his wife came home.", "픽윅 씨는 침실로 갔고, 다울러 씨는 아내가 집에 돌아올 때까지 깨어 있겠다는 경솔한 약속을 지키느라 다시 난로 앞 자리에 앉았다."]], [["There are few things more worrying than sitting up for somebody, especially if that somebody be at a party.", "누군가를 기다리며 깨어 앉아 있는 것보다 더 애를 태우는 일은 흔치 않으니, 그 누군가가 파티에 가 있다면 더욱 그러하다."], ["You cannot help thinking how quickly the time passes with them, which drags so heavily with you; and the more you think of this, the more your hopes of their speedy arrival decline.", "자기에게는 그토록 더디게 흘러가는 시간이 그들에게는 얼마나 빨리 지나가고 있을까 하는 생각을 떨칠 수가 없고, 이 생각을 하면 할수록 그들이 곧 도착하리라는 희망은 점점 줄어든다."], ["Clocks tick so loud, too, when you are sitting up alone, and you seem as if you had an under-garment of cobwebs on.", "게다가 혼자 깨어 앉아 있으면 시계가 어찌나 크게 째깍거리는지, 마치 거미줄로 짠 속옷을 걸친 것만 같다."], ["First, something tickles your right knee, and then the same sensation irritates your left.", "처음에는 무언가가 오른쪽 무릎을 간질이고, 그다음에는 똑같은 감각이 왼쪽 무릎을 괴롭힌다."], ["You have no sooner changed your position, than it comes again in the arms; when you have fidgeted your limbs into all sorts of queer shapes, you have a sudden relapse in the nose, which you rub as if to rub it off--as there is no doubt you would, if you could.", "자세를 바꾸기가 무섭게 그 느낌이 이번에는 두 팔에 찾아온다. 사지를 온갖 괴상한 모양으로 비비 꼬고 나면, 느닷없이 코가 다시 도지는데, 그것을 비벼 떼어 내기라도 할 듯이 문질러 댄다—실제로 할 수만 있다면 그렇게 하리라는 것은 의심할 바 없다."], ["Eyes, too, are mere personal inconveniences; and the wick of one candle gets an inch and a half long, while you are snuffing the other.", "두 눈 또한 한낱 개인적인 불편거리에 지나지 않고, 한쪽 촛불의 심지를 다듬는 동안 다른 쪽 촛불 심지는 1.5인치나 길게 자라 버린다."], ["These, and various other little nervous annoyances, render sitting up for a length of time after everybody else has gone to bed, anything but a cheerful amusement.", "이런저런 자잘한 신경질적 성가심들이, 다른 사람들이 모두 잠자리에 든 뒤 오래도록 깨어 앉아 있는 일을 결코 유쾌한 소일거리라 할 수 없게 만든다."]], [["This was just Mr. Dowler’s opinion, as he sat before the fire, and felt honestly indignant with all the inhuman people at the party who were keeping him up.", "이것이 바로, 난로 앞에 앉아 자기를 잠 못 이루게 붙잡아 두고 있는 그 파티의 비정한 사람들 모두에게 진심으로 분개하던 다울러 씨의 심정이었다."], ["He was not put into better humour either, by the reflection that he had taken it into his head, early in the evening, to think he had got an ache there, and so stopped at home.", "게다가 저녁 일찍 자신이 거기 어딘가 아픈 데가 있다는 생각이 들어 그래서 집에 남았다는 사실을 곱씹어 보아도 그의 기분은 조금도 나아지지 않았다."], ["At length, after several droppings asleep, and fallings forward towards the bars, and catchings backward soon enough to prevent being branded in the face, Mr. Dowler made up his mind that he would throw himself on the bed in the back room and think--not sleep, of course.", "마침내, 여러 번 깜빡 졸다가 난로 살 쪽으로 앞으로 고꾸라지고, 얼굴에 낙인이 찍히기 직전에 가까스로 뒤로 몸을 젖히기를 거듭한 끝에, 다울러 씨는 안쪽 방의 침대에 몸을 던지고 생각이나 하기로—물론 잠은 자지 않고—마음먹었다."]], [["‘I’m a heavy sleeper,’ said Mr.", "\"난 잠귀가 어두워.\""], ["Dowler, as he flung himself on the bed.", "다울러 씨가 침대에 몸을 던지며 말했다."], ["‘I must keep awake.", "\"깨어 있어야 해."], ["I suppose I shall hear a knock here.", "여기 노크 소리는 들리겠지. 그래."], ["Yes. I thought so.", "그럴 줄 알았어."], ["I can hear the watchman.", "야경꾼 소리가 들리는군."], ["There he goes.", "저기 가는군."], ["Fainter now, though.", "그래도 이젠 좀 희미해졌어."], ["A little fainter.", "조금 더 희미해졌고."], ["He’s turning the corner.", "모퉁이를 돌고 있군."], ["Ah!’ When Mr.", "아!\""], ["Dowler arrived at this point, he turned the corner at which he had been long hesitating, and fell fast asleep.", "다울러 씨가 이 지점에 이르렀을 때, 그는 오래도록 망설이고 있던 그 모퉁이를 돌아 깊이 잠들어 버렸다."]], [["Just as the clock struck three, there was blown into the crescent a sedan-chair with Mrs. Dowler inside, borne by one short, fat chairman, and one long, thin one, who had had much ado to keep their bodies perpendicular: to say nothing of the chair.", "마침 시계가 세 시를 칠 무렵, 크레센트 안으로 가마 하나가 바람에 떠밀리듯 들어왔으니, 안에는 다울러 부인이 타고 있었고, 한 명은 키 작고 뚱뚱하며 다른 한 명은 키 크고 비쩍 마른 두 가마꾼이 그것을 메고 있었는데, 두 사람은 가마는 말할 것도 없고 제 몸뚱이를 똑바로 가누는 데에도 여간 애를 먹지 않았다."], ["But on that high ground, and in the crescent, which the wind swept round and round as if it were going to tear the paving stones up, its fury was tremendous.", "그러나 그 높은 지대, 게다가 바람이 마치 포석을 죄다 뜯어 올리려는 듯 빙빙 휘몰아치는 그 크레센트에서는, 바람의 기세가 어마어마했다."], ["They were very glad to set the chair down, and give a good round loud double-knock at the street door.", "그들은 가마를 내려놓고 현관문을 두 번씩 우렁차게 쾅쾅 두드릴 수 있게 되자 무척 기뻐했다."]], [["They waited some time, but nobody came.", "그들은 한참을 기다렸으나, 아무도 나오지 않았다."]], [["‘Servants is in the arms o’ Porpus, I think,’ said the short chairman, warming his hands at the attendant link-boy’s torch.", "\"하인들이 잠의 신 모르페우스 품에 안겨 있나 보군.\" 키 작은 가마꾼이 곁에 선 횃불 든 소년의 횃불에 두 손을 녹이며 말했다."]], [["‘I wish he’d give ‘em a squeeze and wake ‘em,’ observed the long one.", "\"그자가 놈들을 한번 꽉 쥐어서 깨워 줬으면 좋겠구먼.\" 키 큰 가마꾼이 한마디 했다."]], [["‘Knock again, will you, if you please,’ cried Mrs.", "\"미안하지만, 한 번 더 두드려 주겠어요?\""], ["Dowler from the chair.", "다울러 부인이 가마 안에서 외쳤다."], ["‘Knock two or three times, if you please.’", "\"미안하지만 두세 번 두드려 줘요.\""]], [["The short man was quite willing to get the job over, as soon as possible; so he stood on the step, and gave four or five most startling double-knocks, of eight or ten knocks a-piece, while the long man went into the road, and looked up at the windows for a light.", "키 작은 사내는 되도록 빨리 이 일을 끝내고 싶은 마음이 굴뚝같았으므로, 계단에 올라서서 한 번에 여덟 번이고 열 번이고 두드리는 식으로 더없이 깜짝 놀랄 만한 노크를 네댓 차례나 해 댔고, 그동안 키 큰 사내는 길로 나가 불빛이 켜지는지 창문을 올려다보았다."]], [["Nobody came.", "아무도 나오지 않았다."], ["It was all as silent and dark as ever.", "모든 것이 여전히 고요하고 어두웠다."]], [["‘Dear me!’ said Mrs.", "\"이런!\" 다울러 부인이 말했다."], ["Dowler. ‘You must knock again, if you please.’", "\"미안하지만 한 번 더 두드려 줘야겠어요.\""]], [["There ain’t a bell, is there, ma’am?’ said the short chairman.", "\"초인종은 없습니까, 부인?\" 키 작은 가마꾼이 말했다."]], [["‘Yes, there is,’ interposed the link-boy, ‘I’ve been a-ringing at it ever so long.’", "\"아뇨, 있어요.\" 횃불 든 소년이 끼어들었다. \"제가 아까부터 한참이나 그걸 당겨 댔는걸요.\""]], [["‘It’s only a handle,’ said Mrs.", "\"그건 그냥 손잡이일 뿐이에요.\""], ["Dowler, ‘the wire’s broken.’", "다울러 부인이 말했다. \"줄이 끊어졌거든요.\""]], [["‘I wish the servants’ heads wos,’ growled the long man.", "\"하인 놈들 머리통이 끊어졌으면 좋겠구먼.\" 키 큰 사내가 으르렁거렸다."]], [["‘I must trouble you to knock again, if you please,’ said Mrs.", "\"미안하지만 한 번 더 두드려 주는 수고를 끼쳐야겠어요.\""], ["Dowler, with the utmost politeness.", "다울러 부인이 더없이 정중하게 말했다."]], [["The short man did knock again several times, without producing the smallest effect.", "키 작은 사내는 과연 여러 차례 다시 두드렸으나, 아무런 효과도 거두지 못했다."], ["The tall man, growing very impatient, then relieved him, and kept on perpetually knocking double-knocks of two loud knocks each, like an insane postman.", "그러자 몹시 조바심이 난 키 큰 사내가 그와 교대하여, 미친 우체부처럼 두 번씩 우렁차게 쾅쾅 노크를 끊임없이 해 댔다."]], [["At length Mr. Winkle began to dream that he was at a club, and that the members being very refractory, the chairman was obliged to hammer the table a good deal to preserve order; then he had a confused notion of an auction room where there were no bidders, and the auctioneer was buying everything in; and ultimately he began to think it just within the bounds of possibility that somebody might be knocking at the street door.", "마침내 윈클 씨는 자기가 어느 클럽에 와 있는데, 회원들이 어찌나 말을 안 듣는지 의장이 질서를 잡으려고 탁자를 어지간히 두드려야 한다는 꿈을 꾸기 시작했다."], ["To make quite certain, however, he remained quiet in bed for ten minutes or so, and listened; and when he had counted two or three-and- thirty knocks, he felt quite satisfied, and gave himself a great deal of credit for being so wakeful.", "그러다가 입찰자가 하나도 없어 경매인이 모든 것을 떠안아 사들이는 경매장에 와 있는 듯한 어렴풋한 생각이 들었고, 종내에는 누군가 현관문을 두드리고 있을지도 모른다는 것이 그럭저럭 가능성의 범위 안에 든다고 여기기 시작했다. 그러나 확실히 하려고 그는 십 분쯤 침대에 가만히 누운 채 귀를 기울였고, 노크 소리를 서른두세 번쯤 헤아리고 나자 완전히 납득했으며, 자신이 이토록 잠귀가 밝은 것을 두고 스스로를 대단히 대견스러워했다."]], [["‘Rap rap-rap rap-rap rap-ra, ra, ra, ra, ra, rap!’ went the knocker.", "\"쾅 쾅—쾅 쾅—쾅 쾅—쿵, 쿵, 쿵, 쿵, 쿵, 쾅!\" 노커가 울려 댔다."]], [["Mr. Winkle jumped out of bed, wondering very much what could possibly be the matter, and hastily putting on his stockings and slippers, folded his dressing-gown round him, lighted a flat candle from the rush-light that was burning in the fireplace, and hurried downstairs.", "윈클 씨는 도대체 무슨 일일까 몹시 의아해하며 침대에서 벌떡 뛰어내려, 부랴부랴 양말과 슬리퍼를 신고, 실내복을 몸에 둘둘 두른 뒤, 벽난로에서 타고 있던 골풀 등불로 납작한 양초에 불을 붙이고는 서둘러 아래층으로 내려갔다."]], [["‘Here’s somebody comin’ at last, ma’am,’ said the short chairman.", "\"드디어 누가 나오나 봐요, 부인.\" 키 작은 가마꾼이 말했다."]], [["‘I wish I wos behind him vith a bradawl,’ muttered the long one.", "\"내가 송곳을 들고 저놈 뒤에 있었으면 좋겠구먼.\" 키 큰 사내가 중얼거렸다."]], [["‘Who’s there?’ cried Mr.", "\"누구시오?\""], ["Winkle, undoing the chain.", "윈클 씨가 사슬을 풀며 외쳤다."]], [["‘Don’t stop to ask questions, cast-iron head,’ replied the long man, with great disgust, taking it for granted that the inquirer was a footman; ‘but open the door.’", "\"질문 따위 하느라 멈추지 말고, 이 무쇠 대가리야.\" 키 큰 사내가 묻는 자를 으레 하인으로 단정하고는 잔뜩 못마땅한 투로 대꾸했다. \"문이나 열어.\""]], [["‘Come, look sharp, timber eyelids,’ added the other encouragingly.", "\"자, 어서 서둘러라, 나무 눈꺼풀아.\" 다른 사내가 부추기듯 덧붙였다."]], [["Mr. Winkle, being half asleep, obeyed the command mechanically, opened the door a little, and peeped out.", "윈클 씨는 반쯤 잠든 상태였으므로 기계적으로 그 명령에 따라, 문을 조금 열고 밖을 내다보았다."], ["The first thing he saw, was the red glare of the link-boy’s torch.", "그가 맨 처음 본 것은 횃불 든 소년의 횃불이 내뿜는 붉은 불빛이었다."], ["Startled by the sudden fear that the house might be on fire, he hastily threw the door wide open, and holding the candle above his head, stared eagerly before him, not quite certain whether what he saw was a sedan-chair or a fire-engine.", "집에 불이 난 것은 아닐까 하는 느닷없는 두려움에 깜짝 놀라, 그는 황급히 문을 활짝 열어젖히고, 양초를 머리 위로 치켜든 채 앞을 뚫어지게 노려보았으니, 자기가 본 것이 가마인지 소방차인지 영 확신이 서지 않았다."], ["At this instant there came a violent gust of wind; the light was blown out; Mr. Winkle felt himself irresistibly impelled on to the steps; and the door blew to, with a loud crash.", "바로 그 순간 거센 돌풍이 휘몰아쳐 불이 꺼졌고, 윈클 씨는 거역할 수 없는 힘에 떠밀려 계단 위로 떠밀려 나갔으며, 문은 요란한 소리를 내며 쾅 닫혀 버렸다."]], [["‘Well, young man, now you have done it!’ said the short chairman.", "\"이런, 젊은 양반, 이제 일을 제대로 저질러 놨구먼!\" 키 작은 가마꾼이 말했다."]], [["Mr. Winkle, catching sight of a lady’s face at the window of the sedan, turned hastily round, plied the knocker with all his might and main, and called frantically upon the chairman to take the chair away again.", "윈클 씨는 가마 창문에 비친 한 여인의 얼굴을 언뜻 보고는 황급히 돌아서서, 있는 힘껏 노커를 두드려 대며 가마꾼들에게 가마를 도로 치워 달라고 미친 듯이 외쳤다."]], [["‘Take it away, take it away,’ cried Mr.", "\"치워 줘요, 치워 줘요.\""], ["Winkle.", "윈클 씨가 외쳤다."], ["‘Here’s somebody coming out of another house; put me into the chair.", "\"저기 다른 집에서 누가 나오고 있어요. 나를 가마 안에 넣어 줘요."], ["Hide me!", "나를 숨겨 줘요!"], ["Do something with me!’", "어떻게 좀 해 줘요!\""]], [["All this time he was shivering with cold; and every time he raised his hand to the knocker, the wind took the dressing-gown in a most unpleasant manner.", "이러는 내내 그는 추위에 덜덜 떨고 있었고, 노커에 손을 들어 올릴 때마다 바람이 더없이 고약하게 실내복을 들춰 댔다."]], [["‘The people are coming down the crescent now.", "\"이젠 사람들이 크레센트를 내려오고 있어요."], ["There are ladies with ‘em; cover me up with something.", "부인들도 함께 있고요. 뭐라도 나를 좀 덮어 줘요."], ["Stand before me!’ roared Mr. Winkle.", "내 앞을 막아서 줘요!\""], ["But the chairmen were too much exhausted with laughing to afford him the slightest assistance, and the ladies were every moment approaching nearer and nearer.", "윈클 씨가 고함쳤다. 그러나 가마꾼들은 너무 웃느라 기진맥진하여 그에게 손톱만큼의 도움도 줄 수 없었고, 부인들은 시시각각 점점 더 가까이 다가오고 있었다."]], [["Mr. Winkle gave a last hopeless knock; the ladies were only a few doors off.", "윈클 씨가 마지막으로 절망적인 노크를 했다."], ["He threw away the extinguished candle, which, all this time he had held above his head, and fairly bolted into the sedan-chair where Mrs.", "부인들은 불과 몇 집 떨어진 곳까지 와 있었다."], ["Dowler was.", "그는 이러는 내내 머리 위로 치켜들고 있던 꺼진 양초를 내던지고, 다울러 부인이 타고 있는 가마 속으로 곧장 뛰어들었다."]], [["Now, Mrs. Craddock had heard the knocking and the voices at last; and, only waiting to put something smarter on her head than her nightcap, ran down into the front drawing-room to make sure that it was the right party. Throwing up the window-sash as Mr. Winkle was rushing into the chair, she no sooner caught sight of what was going forward below, than she raised a vehement and dismal shriek, and implored Mr. Dowler to get up directly, for his wife was running away with another gentleman.", "한편 크래덕 부인은 마침내 노크 소리와 말소리를 듣고, 잠자리 모자보다 좀 더 그럴듯한 것을 머리에 쓰느라 잠깐 멈춘 것 말고는 곧장 앞쪽 응접실로 달려 내려와, 정말로 기다리던 사람들인지 확인하려 했다. 윈클 씨가 막 가마 안으로 뛰어들고 있을 때 창틀을 밀어 올리던 그녀는, 아래에서 벌어지고 있는 일을 보자마자 격렬하고 처참한 비명을 지르며, 부인이 다른 신사와 함께 달아나고 있으니 당장 일어나라고 다울러 씨에게 애원했다."]], [["Upon this, Mr. Dowler bounced off the bed as abruptly as an India-rubber ball, and rushing into the front room, arrived at one window just as Mr. Pickwick threw up the other, when the first object that met the gaze of both, was Mr. Winkle bolting into the sedan-chair.", "이 말에 다울러 씨는 고무공처럼 별안간 침대에서 튀어 올라, 앞방으로 달려가 한쪽 창문에 다다랐는데, 마침 픽윅 씨가 다른 쪽 창문을 밀어 올린 순간이었으니, 두 사람의 눈에 맨 처음 들어온 것은 가마 안으로 뛰어드는 윈클 씨였다."]], [["‘Watchman,’ shouted Dowler furiously, ‘stop him--hold him--keep him tight--shut him in, till I come down.", "\"야경꾼!\" 다울러가 노발대발하며 고함쳤다."], ["I’ll cut his throat--give me a knife--from ear to ear, Mrs.", "\"저놈을 멈춰—붙잡아—꽉 붙들어—내가 내려갈 때까지 가둬 둬."], ["Craddock--I will!’ And breaking from the shrieking landlady, and from Mr.", "내가 저놈 목을 따 버릴 테다—칼을 가져와—이쪽 귀에서 저쪽 귀까지, 크래덕 부인—내 그렇게 하고 말겠소!\""], ["Pickwick, the indignant husband seized a small supper-knife, and tore into the street.", "그리고 비명을 질러 대는 집주인 여자와 픽윅 씨에게서 몸을 뿌리치고는, 분개한 남편은 작은 만찬용 칼 하나를 움켜쥐고 거리로 뛰쳐나갔다."]], [["But Mr. Winkle didn’t wait for him.", "그러나 윈클 씨는 그를 기다리지 않았다."], ["He no sooner heard the horrible threat of the valorous Dowler, than he bounced out of the sedan, quite as quickly as he had bounced in, and throwing off his slippers into the road, took to his heels and tore round the crescent, hotly pursued by Dowler and the watchman.", "그 용맹한 다울러의 끔찍한 위협을 듣자마자, 그는 뛰어들었던 것만큼이나 잽싸게 가마에서 튀어나와, 슬리퍼를 길바닥에 벗어 던지고는 부리나케 줄행랑을 쳐 크레센트를 빙 돌아 내달렸으니, 다울러와 야경꾼이 맹렬히 그 뒤를 쫓았다."], ["He kept ahead; the door was open as he came round the second time; he rushed in, slammed it in Dowler’s face, mounted to his bedroom, locked the door, piled a wash-hand-stand, chest of drawers, and a table against it, and packed up a few necessaries ready for flight with the first ray of morning.", "그는 계속 앞서 달렸다. 두 번째로 돌아왔을 때 문이 열려 있어, 그는 안으로 뛰어들어 다울러의 코앞에서 문을 쾅 닫고, 침실로 올라가 문을 잠그고는, 세면대와 서랍장과 탁자를 그 앞에 쌓아 올린 뒤, 동이 트는 첫 햇살과 함께 달아날 채비로 몇 가지 필수품을 챙겨 꾸렸다."]], [["Dowler came up to the outside of the door; avowed, through the keyhole, his steadfast determination of cutting Mr.", "다울러는 문 바깥까지 올라와, 열쇠 구멍을 통해 이튿날 윈클 씨의 목을 따고 말겠다는 확고한 결심을 천명했다."], ["Winkle’s throat next day; and, after a great confusion of voices in the drawing-room, amidst which that of Mr. Pickwick was distinctly heard endeavouring to make peace, the inmates dispersed to their several bed-chambers, and all was quiet once more.", "그러고는 응접실에서 한바탕 어수선하게 목소리들이 뒤엉킨 끝에—그 가운데 화해를 붙이려 애쓰는 픽윅 씨의 목소리가 또렷이 들렸다—거주자들은 저마다의 침실로 흩어졌고, 다시 한번 모든 것이 고요해졌다."]], [["It is not unlikely that the inquiry may be made, where Mr. Weller was, all this time?", "이 모든 일이 벌어지는 동안 웰러 씨는 어디 있었는지 묻고 싶어질 법도 하다."], ["We will state where he was, in the next chapter.", "그가 어디 있었는지는 다음 장에서 밝히겠다."]], [["CHAPTER XXXVII. HONOURABLY ACCOUNTS FOR MR.", "「웰러 씨」"], ["WELLER’S ABSENCE, BY DESCRIBING A SOIREE TO WHICH HE WAS INVITED AND WENT; ALSO RELATES HOW HE WAS ENTRUSTED BY MR. PICKWICK WITH A PRIVATE MISSION OF DELICACY AND IMPORTANCE\nMr.", "하고 크래덕 부인이 바로 이 다사다난한 날 아침에 말했다."], ["Weller,’ said Mrs. Craddock, upon the morning of this very eventful day, ‘here’s a letter for you.’", "\"당신한테 편지가 한 통 왔어요.\""]], [["‘Wery odd that,’ said Sam; ‘I’m afeerd there must be somethin’ the matter, for I don’t recollect any gen’l’m’n in my circle of acquaintance as is capable o’ writin’ one.’", "\"그것참 묘한 일이군요.\" 샘이 말했다. \"뭔가 일이 잘못된 게 아닌가 걱정되는데요. 제 아는 사람들 가운데 편지 한 장이라도 쓸 줄 아는 신사 양반은 떠오르질 않아서 말입니다.\""]], [["‘Perhaps something uncommon has taken place,’ observed Mrs.", "\"뭔가 흔치 않은 일이 일어났는지도 모르죠.\""], ["Craddock.", "크래덕 부인이 한마디 했다."]], [["‘It must be somethin’ wery uncommon indeed, as could perduce a letter out o’ any friend o’ mine,’ replied Sam, shaking his head dubiously; ‘nothin’ less than a nat’ral conwulsion, as the young gen’l’m’n observed ven he wos took with fits.", "\"제 친구들 가운데 누군가한테서 편지가 나올 정도라면, 그건 정말로 어지간히 흔치 않은 일이어야 할 겁니다.\" 샘이 미심쩍게 고개를 저으며 대답했다."], ["It can’t be from the gov’ner,’ said Sam, looking at the direction.", "\"발작을 일으킨 그 젊은 신사 양반이 말한 대로, 천재지변쯤은 돼야 한다는 거죠. 영감님한테서 온 건 아닐 테고요.\""], ["‘He always prints, I know, ‘cos he learnt writin’ from the large bills in the booking-offices.", "샘이 수신인 주소를 들여다보며 말했다. \"영감님은 늘 활자체로 쓰시거든요, 글씨를 매표소의 큼직한 광고판에서 배우셨으니까요."], ["It’s a wery strange thing now, where this here letter can ha’ come from.’", "지금으로선 이 편지가 대체 어디서 왔는지 알 수가 없으니, 거참 이상한 노릇이네요.\""]], [["As Sam said this, he did what a great many people do when they are uncertain about the writer of a note--looked at the seal, and then at the front, and then at the back, and then at the sides, and then at the superscription; and, as a last resource, thought perhaps he might as well look at the inside, and try to find out from that.", "샘은 이렇게 말하면서, 쪽지를 쓴 사람이 누구인지 확신이 서지 않을 때 수많은 사람들이 하는 짓을 했다—봉인을 보고, 그다음에 앞면을 보고, 그다음에 뒷면을 보고, 그다음에 옆면들을 보고, 그다음에 겉봉의 글씨를 보았다. 그러다가 마지막 수단으로, 차라리 안쪽을 들여다보고 거기서 알아내는 편이 낫겠다고 생각했다."]], [["‘It’s wrote on gilt-edged paper,’ said Sam, as he unfolded it, ‘and sealed in bronze vax vith the top of a door key.", "\"금테 두른 종이에 썼군.\" 샘이 편지를 펼치며 말했다."], ["Now for it.’ And, with a very grave face, Mr.", "\"그리고 청동색 밀랍에다 문 열쇠 대가리로 봉인을 했고. 자, 이제 읽어 보자.\""], ["Weller slowly read as follows--", "그러고는 웰러 씨가 자못 근엄한 얼굴로 천천히 다음과 같이 읽어 내려갔다—"]], [["‘A select company of the Bath footmen presents their compliments to Mr.", "\"배스의 정선된 하인 일동은 웰러 씨께 인사를 전하며, 오늘 저녁 친목 만찬회에 왕림하시는 기쁨을 청하나이다."], ["Weller, and requests the pleasure of his company this evening, to a friendly swarry, consisting of a boiled leg of mutton with the usual trimmings.", "만찬은 양다리 삶은 요리에 으레 곁들이는 것들로 이루어져 있나이다."], ["The swarry to be on table at half-past nine o’clock punctually.’", "만찬은 정각 아홉 시 반에 식탁에 오를 것이옵니다.\""]], [["This was inclosed in another note, which ran thus--", "이것은 또 다른 쪽지 안에 동봉되어 있었는데, 그 내용은 이러했다—"]], [["‘Mr. John Smauker, the gentleman who had the pleasure of meeting Mr. Weller at the house of their mutual acquaintance, Mr. Bantam, a few days since, begs to inclose Mr. Weller the herewith invitation. If Mr. Weller will call on Mr. John Smauker at nine o’clock, Mr. John Smauker will have the pleasure of introducing Mr. Weller.", "\"존 스모커 씨는, 며칠 전 두 사람의 공통된 지인이신 밴텀 씨 댁에서 웰러 씨를 만나 뵙는 기쁨을 누렸던 신사인바, 이에 웰러 씨께 초대장을 동봉하여 보내드리는 바입니다. 만약 웰러 씨께서 아홉 시에 존 스모커 씨를 찾아주신다면, 존 스모커 씨는 웰러 씨를 소개해 드리는 기쁨을 누리게 될 것입니다."]], [["(Signed) ‘John Smauker.’", "(서명) 존 스모커.\""]], [["The envelope was directed to blank Weller, Esq., at Mr. Pickwick’s; and in a parenthesis, in the left hand corner, were the words ‘airy bell,’ as an instruction to the bearer.", "봉투에는 픽윅 씨 댁의 모씨 웰러 귀하라고 적혀 있었고, 왼쪽 귀퉁이에는 괄호 안에 \"지하 출입구 초인종\"이라는 말이 심부름꾼에게 주는 안내로 적혀 있었다."]], [["‘Vell,’ said Sam, ‘this is comin’ it rayther powerful, this is.", "\"그것참.\" 샘이 말했다."], ["I never heerd a biled leg o’ mutton called a swarry afore.", "\"이건 좀 거창하게 나오는걸. 양다리 삶은 걸 만찬회라고 부르는 건 여태 들어 본 적이 없어."], ["I wonder wot they’d call a roast one.’", "그럼 구운 다리는 뭐라고 부를는지 궁금하군.\""]], [["However, without waiting to debate the point, Sam at once betook himself into the presence of Mr. Pickwick, and requested leave of absence for that evening, which was readily granted.", "그러나 그 점을 두고 따져 볼 겨를도 없이, 샘은 곧장 픽윅 씨 앞으로 나아가 그날 저녁의 외출 허락을 청했고, 허락은 선뜻 내려졌다."], ["With this permission and the street-door key, Sam Weller issued forth a little before the appointed time, and strolled leisurely towards Queen Square, which he no sooner gained than he had the satisfaction of beholding Mr.", "이 허락과 길거리로 통하는 대문 열쇠를 받아 든 채, 샘 웰러는 약속 시간보다 조금 일찍 나섰고, 퀸 광장 쪽으로 느긋하게 거닐어 갔다."], ["John Smauker leaning his powdered head against a lamp-post at a short distance off, smoking a cigar through an amber tube.", "광장에 다다르자마자 그는 존 스모커 씨가 조금 떨어진 곳의 가로등 기둥에 분 바른 머리를 기댄 채 호박색 물부리로 시가를 피우고 있는 모습을 보는 만족을 누렸다."]], [["‘How do you do, Mr. Weller?’ said Mr.", "\"안녕하시오, 웰러 씨?\""], ["John Smauker, raising his hat gracefully with one hand, while he gently waved the other in a condescending manner.", "존 스모커 씨가 한 손으로는 우아하게 모자를 들어 올리고 다른 한 손으로는 점잖게 거들먹거리며 부드럽게 흔들면서 말했다."], ["‘How do you do, Sir?’", "\"어떻게 지내시오, 선생?\""]], [["‘Why, reasonably conwalessent,’ replied Sam.", "\"뭐, 그럭저럭 회복 중입니다.\" 샘이 대답했다."], ["‘How do you find yourself, my dear feller?’", "\"댁은 어떻게 지내시나요, 친애하는 양반?\""]], [["‘Only so so,’ said Mr.", "\"그저 그렇소.\""], ["John Smauker.", "존 스모커 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Ah, you’ve been a-workin’ too hard,’ observed Sam. ‘I was fearful you would; it won’t do, you know; you must not give way to that ‘ere uncompromisin’ spirit o’ yourn.’", "\"아, 너무 무리하게 일을 하셨군요.\" 샘이 한마디 했다. \"그러실까 봐 걱정했습니다. 그러시면 안 되지요, 아시잖아요. 그 타협을 모르는 기질에 너무 자신을 내맡기시면 안 됩니다.\""]], [["‘It’s not so much that, Mr.", "\"그런 것보다는요, 웰러 씨,\""], ["Weller,’ replied Mr.", "존 스모커 씨가 대답했다."], ["John Smauker, ‘as bad wine; I’m afraid I’ve been dissipating.’", "\"나쁜 포도주 탓입니다. 아무래도 제가 방탕하게 지낸 모양이에요.\""]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오! 그게 원인이군요, 그렇죠?\""], ["that’s it, is it?’ said Sam; ‘that’s a wery bad complaint, that.’", "샘이 말했다. \"그거참 아주 고약한 병이올시다.\""]], [["‘And yet the temptation, you see, Mr.", "\"하지만 그 유혹이라는 게 말이오, 웰러 씨.\""], ["Weller,’ observed Mr. John Smauker.", "존 스모커 씨가 한마디 했다."]], [["‘Ah, to be sure,’ said Sam.", "\"아, 그렇고말고요.\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["‘Plunged into the very vortex of society, you know, Mr.", "\"사교계의 한복판 소용돌이 속에 빠져 있으니 말이오, 웰러 씨.\""], ["Weller,’ said Mr. John Smauker, with a sigh.", "존 스모커 씨가 한숨을 쉬며 말했다."]], [["‘Dreadful, indeed!’ rejoined Sam.", "\"정말이지 끔찍한 노릇이지요!\" 샘이 맞장구쳤다."]], [["‘But it’s always the way,’ said Mr.", "\"하지만 늘 그런 법이라오.\" 존 스모커 씨가 말했다."], ["John Smauker; ‘if your destiny leads you into public life, and public station, you must expect to be subjected to temptations which other people is free from, Mr. Weller.’", "\"운명이 당신을 공적인 생활과 공적인 지위로 이끈다면, 다른 사람들은 면제받는 유혹에 시달릴 각오를 해야 하는 거지요, 웰러 씨.\""]], [["‘Precisely what my uncle said, ven he vent into the public line,’ remarked Sam, ‘and wery right the old gen’l’m’n wos, for he drank hisself to death in somethin’ less than a quarter.’", "\"우리 삼촌이 술집 일에 발을 들였을 때 한 말이 바로 그거였어요.\" 샘이 한마디 했다. \"그리고 그 영감님 말씀이 아주 옳았지요. 석 달도 채 안 돼서 스스로 술을 퍼마시다 죽었으니까요.\""]], [["Mr.", "존 스모커 씨는 자신과 그 작고한 신사 양반 사이에 어떤 견줌이 그어지는 것에 깊은 분개를 내비쳤다."], ["John Smauker looked deeply indignant at any parallel being drawn between himself and the deceased gentleman in question; but, as Sam’s face was in the most immovable state of calmness, he thought better of it, and looked affable again.", "하지만 샘의 얼굴이 도무지 흔들림 없는 평온의 상태에 있었으므로, 그는 마음을 고쳐먹고 다시 상냥한 표정을 지었다."]], [["‘Perhaps we had better be walking,’ said Mr.", "\"슬슬 걸어가는 편이 낫겠소.\""], ["Smauker, consulting a copper timepiece which dwelt at the bottom of a deep watch-pocket, and was raised to the surface by means of a black string, with a copper key at the other end.", "스모커 씨가 깊은 시계 주머니 바닥에 들어앉아 있다가 검은 끈—그 끝에는 구리 열쇠가 달려 있었다—의 도움으로 수면 위로 끌어올려진 구리 시계를 들여다보며 말했다."]], [["‘P’raps we had,’ replied Sam, ‘or they’ll overdo the swarry, and that’ll spile it.’", "\"그러는 편이 낫겠네요.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"안 그러면 만찬이 너무 익어서 못 쓰게 될 테니까요.\""]], [["‘Have you drank the waters, Mr.", "\"광천수는 마셔 보셨소, 웰러 씨?\""], ["Weller?’ inquired his companion, as they walked towards High Street.", "그들이 하이 스트리트 쪽으로 걸어가는 동안 동행이 물었다."]], [["‘Once,’ replied Sam.", "\"한 번요.\" 샘이 대답했다."]], [["‘What did you think of ‘em, Sir?’", "\"어떻게 생각하셨소, 선생?\""]], [["‘I thought they was particklery unpleasant,’ replied Sam.", "\"유난히 역겹다고 생각했지요.\" 샘이 대답했다."]], [["‘Ah,’ said Mr.", "\"아,\""], ["John Smauker, ‘you disliked the killibeate taste, perhaps?’", "존 스모커 씨가 말했다. \"그 철분 맛이 마음에 안 드셨던 모양이군요?\""]], [["‘I don’t know much about that ‘ere,’ said Sam.", "\"그게 뭔지는 잘 모르겠습니다만.\" 샘이 말했다."], ["‘I thought they’d a wery strong flavour o’ warm flat irons.’", "\"따뜻한 다리미 냄새가 아주 진하게 난다고 생각했지요.\""]], [["‘That is the killibeate, Mr. Weller,’ observed Mr.", "\"그게 바로 철분이라는 거요, 웰러 씨.\""], ["John Smauker contemptuously.", "존 스모커 씨가 깔보듯 한마디 했다."]], [["‘Well, if it is, it’s a wery inexpressive word, that’s all,’ said Sam.", "\"뭐, 그렇다면 그건 영 뜻을 알 수 없는 단어로군요, 그뿐입니다.\" 샘이 말했다."], ["‘It may be, but I ain’t much in the chimical line myself, so I can’t say.’ And here, to the great horror of Mr.", "\"그럴지도 모르지만, 저야 화학 쪽으로는 별로 아는 게 없으니 뭐라 할 수 없지요.\""], ["John Smauker, Sam Weller began to whistle.", "그러더니 여기서, 존 스모커 씨가 몹시 질겁하게도, 샘 웰러는 휘파람을 불기 시작했다."]], [["‘I beg your pardon, Mr. Weller,’ said Mr.", "\"죄송합니다만, 웰러 씨.\""], ["John Smauker, agonised at the exceeding ungenteel sound, ‘will you take my arm?’", "존 스모커 씨가 그 지극히 천박한 소리에 괴로워하며 말했다. \"제 팔을 잡으시겠소?\""]], [["‘Thank’ee, you’re wery good, but I won’t deprive you of it,’ replied Sam.", "\"고맙습니다만, 친절은 고마우나 그 팔을 뺏지는 않겠습니다.\" 샘이 대답했다."], ["‘I’ve rayther a way o’ putting my hands in my pockets, if it’s all the same to you.’ As Sam said this, he suited the action to the word, and whistled far louder than before.", "\"저는 손을 주머니에 찔러 넣는 버릇이 좀 있어서요, 댁만 괜찮으시다면 말입니다.\" 샘은 이렇게 말하면서 말에 행동을 맞추었고, 아까보다 훨씬 더 크게 휘파람을 불었다."]], [["‘This way,’ said his new friend, apparently much relieved as they turned down a by-street; ‘we shall soon be there.’", "\"이쪽이오.\" 그의 새 친구가, 그들이 골목길로 접어들 때 눈에 띄게 안도하며 말했다. \"곧 도착할 거요.\""]], [["‘Shall we?’ said Sam, quite unmoved by the announcement of his close vicinity to the select footmen of Bath.", "\"그래요?\" 배스의 정선된 하인들 가까이에 와 있다는 알림에도 전혀 동요하지 않은 채 샘이 말했다."]], [["‘Yes,’ said Mr.", "\"그렇소.\""], ["John Smauker.", "존 스모커 씨가 말했다."], ["‘Don’t be alarmed, Mr. Weller.’", "\"겁먹지 마시오, 웰러 씨.\""]], [["‘Oh, no,’ said Sam.", "\"오, 아닙니다.\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["‘You’ll see some very handsome uniforms, Mr.", "\"아주 멋진 제복들을 보게 될 거요, 웰러 씨.\""], ["Weller,’ continued Mr.", "존 스모커 씨가 말을 이었다."], ["John Smauker; ‘and perhaps you’ll find some of the gentlemen rather high at first, you know, but they’ll soon come round.’", "\"그리고 처음에는 신사분들 몇몇이 좀 콧대를 세울지도 모릅니다만, 아시잖소, 금세 누그러질 거요.\""]], [["‘That’s wery kind on ‘em,’ replied Sam.", "\"그것참 친절한 분들이군요.\" 샘이 대답했다."]], [["‘And you know,’ resumed Mr.", "\"그리고 말이오,\""], ["John Smauker, with an air of sublime protection--‘you know, as you’re a stranger, perhaps, they’ll be rather hard upon you at first.’", "존 스모커 씨가 한껏 고상하게 비호하는 태도로 다시 말을 이었다. \"아시다시피 당신은 처음 온 사람이니, 어쩌면 처음에는 좀 호되게 굴지도 몰라요.\""]], [["‘They won’t be wery cruel, though, will they?’ inquired Sam.", "\"그래도 너무 잔인하게 굴진 않겠지요, 안 그래요?\" 샘이 물었다."]], [["‘No, no,’ replied Mr.", "\"아니, 아니오.\""], ["John Smauker, pulling forth the fox’s head, and taking a gentlemanly pinch.", "존 스모커 씨가 여우 머리 장식 코담뱃갑을 꺼내 신사답게 한 줌 집으며 대답했다."], ["‘There are some funny dogs among us, and they will have their joke, you know; but you mustn’t mind ‘em, you mustn’t mind ‘em.’", "\"우리 가운데 우스꽝스러운 친구들이 몇 있어서, 아시다시피, 그들 나름의 농을 칠 거요. 하지만 마음에 두면 안 됩니다, 마음에 두면 안 돼요.\""]], [["‘I’ll try and bear up agin such a reg’lar knock down o’ talent,’ replied Sam.", "\"그런 어지간한 재기의 한 방쯤이야 견뎌 보도록 애써 보겠습니다.\" 샘이 대답했다."]], [["‘That’s right,’ said Mr.", "\"그게 옳소.\""], ["John Smauker, putting forth his fox’s head, and elevating his own; ‘I’ll stand by you.’", "존 스모커 씨가 여우 머리 코담뱃갑을 내밀고 제 머리를 치켜들며 말했다. \"내가 당신 편을 들어 주리다.\""]], [["By this time they had reached a small greengrocer’s shop, which Mr.", "이즈음 그들은 자그마한 청과물 가게에 다다랐는데, 존 스모커 씨가 그 안으로 들어섰고 샘이 그 뒤를 따랐다."], ["John Smauker entered, followed by Sam, who, the moment he got behind him, relapsed into a series of the very broadest and most unmitigated grins, and manifested other demonstrations of being in a highly enviable state of inward merriment.", "샘은 그의 등 뒤로 가는 순간, 더할 나위 없이 큼지막하고 거리낌 없는 싱글거림을 잇따라 터뜨리며, 속으로 더없이 부러울 만큼 즐거운 상태에 있음을 드러내는 갖가지 표시를 내보였다."]], [["Crossing the greengrocer’s shop, and putting their hats on the stairs in the little passage behind it, they walked into a small parlour; and here the full splendour of the scene burst upon Mr.", "청과물 가게를 가로질러, 그 뒤쪽 작은 통로의 계단 위에 모자를 놓아두고서, 그들은 작은 응접실로 걸어 들어갔다."], ["Weller’s view.", "그리고 여기서 그 광경의 온전한 화려함이 웰러 씨의 눈앞에 와락 펼쳐졌다."]], [["A couple of tables were put together in the middle of the parlour, covered with three or four cloths of different ages and dates of washing, arranged to look as much like one as the circumstances of the case would allow.", "응접실 한가운데에는 탁자 두 개가 맞붙여져 있었는데, 그 위에는 빨아 온 햇수와 시기가 제각각인 천 서너 장이 덮여 있었고, 사정이 허락하는 한 한 장처럼 보이도록 배치되어 있었다. 그 위에는 예닐곱 명 몫의 나이프와 포크가 놓여 있었다."], ["Upon these were laid knives and forks for six or eight people.", "나이프 손잡이는 더러는 초록, 더러는 빨강, 몇 개는 노랑이었다. 그리고 포크가 죄다 검정이었으므로 색깔의 어우러짐이 자못 눈길을 끌었다."], ["Some of the knife handles were green, others red, and a few yellow; and as all the forks were black, the combination of colours was exceedingly striking.", "그에 걸맞은 수의 손님 몫 접시들이 난로 가리개 뒤에서 데워지고 있었고, 손님들 자신은 그 앞에서 몸을 녹이고 있었다. 그 가운데 우두머리이자 가장 중요해 보이는 이는, 긴 자락이 달린 새빨간 코트에 선명한 붉은 반바지를 입고 삼각모를 쓴 다소 뚱뚱한 신사였다."], ["Plates for a corresponding number of guests were warming behind the fender; and the guests themselves were warming before it: the chief and most important of whom appeared to be a stoutish gentleman in a bright crimson coat with long tails, vividly red breeches, and a cocked hat, who was standing with his back to the fire, and had apparently just entered, for besides retaining his cocked hat on his head, he carried in his hand a high stick, such as gentlemen of his profession usually elevate in a sloping position over the roofs of carriages.", "그는 불을 등지고 서 있었는데, 보아하니 막 들어온 듯했다. 머리에 삼각모를 그대로 쓰고 있을 뿐 아니라, 손에는 그의 직업에 속하는 신사들이 으레 마차 지붕 위로 비스듬히 치켜드는 긴 막대기를 쥐고 있었기 때문이다."]], [["‘Smauker, my lad, your fin,’ said the gentleman with the cocked hat.", "\"스모커, 이 친구, 자네 손 좀 주게.\" 삼각모를 쓴 신사가 말했다."]], [["Mr. Smauker dovetailed the top joint of his right-hand little finger into that of the gentleman with the cocked hat, and said he was charmed to see him looking so well.", "스모커 씨는 자기 오른손 새끼손가락 맨 윗마디를 삼각모를 쓴 신사의 그것에 맞물려 끼우고는, 그가 그렇게 좋아 보이니 반갑다고 말했다."]], [["‘Well, they tell me I am looking pretty blooming,’ said the man with the cocked hat, ‘and it’s a wonder, too. I’ve been following our old woman about, two hours a day, for the last fortnight; and if a constant contemplation of the manner in which she hooks-and-eyes that infernal lavender-coloured old gown of hers behind, isn’t enough to throw anybody into a low state of despondency for life, stop my quarter’s salary.’", "\"뭐, 내가 제법 혈색이 좋아 보인다고들 하더군.\" 삼각모를 쓴 사내가 말했다. \"그것도 신통한 일이지. 지난 보름 동안 하루에 두 시간씩 우리 마님을 졸졸 따라다녔거든. 그 망할 라벤더색 헌 가운을 등 뒤에서 갈고리단추로 채우는 꼴을 줄곧 바라보고 있노라면, 그것만으로도 누구든 평생 의기소침의 구렁텅이에 빠뜨리기에 충분하지 않다면, 내 석 달 치 봉급을 떼 가도 좋아.\""]], [["At this, the assembled selections laughed very heartily; and one gentleman in a yellow waistcoat, with a coach-trimming border, whispered a neighbour in green-foil smalls, that Tuckle was in spirits to-night.", "이 말에 모인 정선된 인사들이 아주 호탕하게 웃었다. 그리고 마부 장식 테두리가 둘러진 노란 조끼를 입은 신사 하나가, 초록 금박을 입힌 반바지 차림의 옆 사람에게, 오늘 밤 터클이 기분이 좋군, 하고 속삭였다."]], [["‘By the bye,’ said Mr.", "\"그건 그렇고,\""], ["Tuckle, ‘Smauker, my boy, you--’ The remainder of the sentence was forwarded into Mr.", "터클 씨가 말했다. \"스모커, 이 사람, 자네는—\""], ["John Smauker’s ear, by whisper.", "그 문장의 나머지는 속삭임으로 존 스모커 씨의 귀에 전해졌다."]], [["‘Oh, dear me, I quite forgot,’ said Mr.", "\"오, 이런, 깜빡 잊고 있었군요.\""], ["John Smauker.", "존 스모커 씨가 말했다."], ["‘Gentlemen, my friend Mr. Weller.’", "\"여러분, 제 친구 웰러 씨입니다.\""]], [["‘Sorry to keep the fire off you, Weller,’ said Mr.", "\"불을 가려서 미안하오, 웰러.\""], ["Tuckle, with a familiar nod.", "터클 씨가 허물없이 고개를 까딱이며 말했다."], ["‘Hope you’re not cold, Weller.’", "\"춥지는 않기를 바라오, 웰러.\""]], [["‘Not by no means, Blazes,’ replied Sam.", "\"천만에요, 불꽃 양반.\" 샘이 대답했다."], ["‘It ‘ud be a wery chilly subject as felt cold wen you stood opposite.", "\"댁 맞은편에 서 있으면서 추위를 타는 작자가 있다면 그거야말로 어지간히 냉랭한 인물일 테죠."], ["You’d save coals if they put you behind the fender in the waitin’-room at a public office, you would.’", "관청 대기실 난로 가리개 뒤에 댁을 갖다 놓으면 석탄값을 아끼겠습니다그려.\""]], [["As this retort appeared to convey rather a personal allusion to Mr. Tuckle’s crimson livery, that gentleman looked majestic for a few seconds, but gradually edging away from the fire, broke into a forced smile, and said it wasn’t bad.", "이 응수가 터클 씨의 진홍색 제복을 두고 다소 인신공격적인 빗댐을 담은 듯 보였으므로, 그 신사는 몇 초 동안 위엄을 부렸으나, 차츰 불에서 옆으로 비켜서며 억지 미소를 짓고는 나쁘지 않다고 말했다."]], [["‘Wery much obliged for your good opinion, sir,’ replied Sam.", "\"좋게 봐 주셔서 대단히 고맙습니다, 선생.\" 샘이 대답했다."], ["‘We shall get on by degrees, I des-say.", "\"차츰차츰 친해지겠지요, 아마."], ["We’ll try a better one by and bye.’", "이따가 더 나은 농담을 한번 해 보지요.\""]], [["At this point the conversation was interrupted by the arrival of a gentleman in orange-coloured plush, accompanied by another selection in purple cloth, with a great extent of stocking.", "이 대목에서 대화는 오렌지빛 플러시 천을 입은 신사 하나가 도착하는 바람에 끊겼는데, 그는 보랏빛 천을 두르고 양말을 넓게 드러낸 또 다른 정선된 인사를 데리고 왔다."], ["The new-comers having been welcomed by the old ones, Mr. Tuckle put the question that supper be ordered in, which was carried unanimously.", "새로 온 이들이 먼저 와 있던 이들의 환영을 받자, 터클 씨가 저녁 식사를 들여오도록 하자는 안건을 내놓았고, 그것은 만장일치로 가결되었다."]], [["The greengrocer and his wife then arranged upon the table a boiled leg of mutton, hot, with caper sauce, turnips, and potatoes. Mr.", "그러자 청과물 가게 주인과 그의 아내가 양다리 삶은 요리를 뜨끈하게, 케이퍼 소스와 순무와 감자를 곁들여 식탁에 차렸다."], ["Tuckle took the chair, and was supported at the other end of the board by the gentleman in orange plush.", "터클 씨가 상석에 앉았고, 식탁 반대쪽 끝에서는 오렌지빛 플러시 천을 입은 신사가 받쳐 주었다."], ["The greengrocer put on a pair of wash-leather gloves to hand the plates with, and stationed himself behind Mr. Tuckle’s chair.", "청과물 가게 주인은 접시를 건네주려고 무두질한 가죽 장갑 한 켤레를 끼고 터클 씨 의자 뒤에 자리를 잡았다."]], [["‘Harris,’ said Mr.", "\"해리스.\""], ["Tuckle, in a commanding tone.", "터클 씨가 명령조로 말했다."]], [["‘Sir,’ said the greengrocer.", "\"예, 나리.\" 청과물 가게 주인이 말했다."]], [["‘Have you got your gloves on?’", "\"장갑은 꼈나?\""]], [["Yes, Sir.’", "\"예, 나리.\""]], [["‘Then take the kiver off.’", "\"그럼 뚜껑을 벗기게.\""]], [["‘Yes, Sir.’", "\"예, 나리.\""]], [["The greengrocer did as he was told, with a show of great humility, and obsequiously handed Mr.", "청과물 가게 주인은 자못 공손한 태도로 시키는 대로 했고, 굽실거리며 터클 씨에게 고기 써는 칼을 건넸다."], ["Tuckle the carving-knife; in doing which, he accidentally gaped.", "그렇게 하다가 그는 그만 무심코 하품을 했다."]], [["‘What do you mean by that, Sir?’ said Mr.", "\"그게 무슨 짓인가, 이 사람아?\""], ["Tuckle, with great asperity.", "터클 씨가 자못 매섭게 말했다."]], [["‘I beg your pardon, Sir,’ replied the crestfallen greengrocer, ‘I didn’t mean to do it, Sir; I was up very late last night, Sir.’", "\"죄송합니다, 나리.\" 풀 죽은 청과물 가게 주인이 대답했다. \"일부러 그런 게 아닙니다, 나리. 어젯밤에 아주 늦게까지 깨어 있었거든요, 나리.\""]], [["‘I tell you what my opinion of you is, Harris,’ said Mr.", "\"내가 자네를 어떻게 보는지 말해 주지, 해리스.\""], ["Tuckle, with a most impressive air, ‘you’re a wulgar beast.’", "터클 씨가 더없이 위압적인 태도로 말했다. \"자네는 천박한 짐승이야.\""]], [["‘I hope, gentlemen,’ said Harris, ‘that you won’t be severe with me, gentlemen.", "\"부디 여러분,\" 해리스가 말했다."], ["I am very much obliged to you indeed, gentlemen, for your patronage, and also for your recommendations, gentlemen, whenever additional assistance in waiting is required.", "\"저한테 너무 엄하게 굴지 말아 주십쇼, 여러분. 여러분의 후원에, 또 시중드는 일손이 더 필요할 때마다 해 주시는 추천에, 저는 정말이지 대단히 감사드리고 있습니다, 여러분."], ["I hope, gentlemen, I give satisfaction.’", "부디 제가 만족스럽게 해 드리고 있기를 바랍니다, 여러분.\""]], [["‘No, you don’t, Sir,’ said Mr.", "\"아니, 그렇지 않네, 이 사람아.\" 터클 씨가 말했다."], ["Tuckle. ‘Very far from it, Sir.’", "\"그러기는커녕 어림도 없지, 이 사람아.\""]], [["‘We consider you an inattentive reskel,’ said the gentleman in the orange plush.", "\"우리는 자네를 부주의한 불한당으로 여기네.\" 오렌지빛 플러시 천을 입은 신사가 말했다."]], [["‘And a low thief,’ added the gentleman in the green-foil smalls.", "\"게다가 저열한 도둑놈이지.\" 초록 금박 반바지를 입은 신사가 덧붙였다."]], [["‘And an unreclaimable blaygaird,’ added the gentleman in purple.", "\"또 구제 불능의 악당이고.\" 보랏빛 옷을 입은 신사가 덧붙였다."]], [["The poor greengrocer bowed very humbly while these little epithets were bestowed upon him, in the true spirit of the very smallest tyranny; and when everybody had said something to show his superiority, Mr.", "가엾은 청과물 가게 주인은 가장 옹졸한 폭정의 참된 정신으로 이런 자그마한 욕설들이 그에게 퍼부어지는 동안 아주 공손하게 머리를 조아렸다."], ["Tuckle proceeded to carve the leg of mutton, and to help the company.", "그리고 모두가 저마다 자신의 우월함을 내보일 만한 말을 한마디씩 하고 나자, 터클 씨가 양다리를 썰어 일동에게 나누어 주기 시작했다."]], [["This important business of the evening had hardly commenced, when the door was thrown briskly open, and another gentleman in a light-blue suit, and leaden buttons, made his appearance.", "저녁의 이 중대한 일이 막 시작되었나 싶을 때, 문이 활기차게 벌컥 열리며, 또 다른 신사 하나가 연푸른 양복에 납빛 단추를 달고 나타났다."]], [["‘Against the rules,’ said Mr.", "\"규칙 위반이야.\" 터클 씨가 말했다."], ["Tuckle. ‘Too late, too late.’", "\"너무 늦었어, 너무 늦었다고.\""]], [["‘No, no; positively I couldn’t help it,’ said the gentleman in blue.", "\"아니, 아니. 정말이지 어쩔 수 없었다네.\""], ["‘I appeal to the company.", "푸른 옷을 입은 신사가 말했다."], ["An affair of gallantry now, an appointment at the theayter.’", "\"여러분께 호소하겠네. 글쎄 연애 사건이라, 극장에서 만나기로 약속한 게 있었거든.\""]], [["‘Oh, that indeed,’ said the gentleman in the orange plush.", "\"오, 그거라면야.\" 오렌지빛 플러시 천을 입은 신사가 말했다."]], [["‘Yes; raly now, honour bright,’ said the man in blue.", "\"그렇다네. 정말이지, 명예를 걸고 하는 말이야.\""], ["‘I made a promese to fetch our youngest daughter at half-past ten, and she is such an uncauminly fine gal, that I raly hadn’t the ‘art to disappint her.", "푸른 옷을 입은 사내가 말했다. \"우리 집 막내딸을 열 시 반에 데리러 가겠다고 약속을 했거든."], ["No offence to the present company, Sir, but a petticut, sir--a petticut, Sir, is irrevokeable.’", "그런데 그 아가씨가 어찌나 보기 드물게 고운 처자인지, 정말이지 차마 그 애를 실망시킬 마음이 안 나더라고. 여기 계신 분들께 무례하려는 건 아니네만, 선생, 치마폭이란—치마폭이란, 선생, 돌이킬 수 없는 거란 말이지.\""]], [["‘I begin to suspect there’s something in that quarter,’ said Tuckle, as the new-comer took his seat next Sam, ‘I’ve remarked, once or twice, that she leans very heavy on your shoulder when she gets in and out of the carriage.’", "\"그쪽에 뭔가 있는 게 아닌가 슬슬 의심이 드는걸.\" 새로 온 이가 샘 옆에 자리를 잡자 터클이 말했다. \"한두 번 눈여겨봤는데, 그 아가씨가 마차에서 타고 내릴 때 자네 어깨에 아주 무겁게 기대더란 말이야.\""]], [["‘Oh, raly, raly, Tuckle, you shouldn’t,’ said the man in blue.", "\"오, 정말, 정말이지, 터클, 그러면 못쓰네.\" 푸른 옷을 입은 사내가 말했다."], ["‘It’s not fair.", "\"그건 공정하지 못해."], ["I may have said to one or two friends that she wos a very divine creechure, and had refused one or two offers without any hobvus cause, but--no, no, no, indeed, Tuckle--before strangers, too--it’s not right-- you shouldn’t.", "내가 한두 친구한테 그 처자가 아주 신성한 여인이며 별 뚜렷한 까닭도 없이 한두 번 청혼을 거절했다고 말했을지는 몰라도, 그래도—아니, 아니, 정말 아니야, 터클—더구나 모르는 사람들 앞에서—그건 옳지 않아—그러면 못써."], ["Delicacy, my dear friend, delicacy!’ And the man in blue, pulling up his neckerchief, and adjusting his coat cuffs, nodded and frowned as if there were more behind, which he could say if he liked, but was bound in honour to suppress.", "신중함이야, 친애하는 친구, 신중함이라고!\" 그러고는 푸른 옷을 입은 사내가 목도리를 끌어올리고 소맷부리를 매만지면서, 마치 마음만 먹으면 더 할 말이 있지만 명예를 걸고 입을 다물고 있다는 듯 고개를 끄덕이며 눈살을 찌푸렸다."]], [["The man in blue being a light-haired, stiff-necked, free and easy sort of footman, with a swaggering air and pert face, had attracted Mr.", "푸른 옷을 입은 사내는 머리칼이 옅고 목이 뻣뻣하며 거리낌 없고 무람없는 부류의 하인으로, 으스대는 태도에 건방진 얼굴을 하고 있어서 처음부터 웰러 씨의 각별한 주목을 끌었다."], ["Weller’s special attention at first, but when he began to come out in this way, Sam felt more than ever disposed to cultivate his acquaintance; so he launched himself into the conversation at once, with characteristic independence.", "그런데 그가 이런 식으로 본색을 드러내기 시작하자, 샘은 그 어느 때보다도 더 그와 사귀어 두고 싶은 마음이 일었다. 그래서 그는 특유의 거침없음으로 단숨에 대화에 뛰어들었다."]], [["‘Your health, Sir,’ said Sam.", "\"건강을 위하여, 선생.\" 샘이 말했다."], ["‘I like your conversation much.", "\"댁의 이야기가 아주 마음에 듭니다."], ["I think it’s wery pretty.’", "정말 근사하다고 생각해요.\""]], [["At this the man in blue smiled, as if it were a compliment he was well used to; but looked approvingly on Sam at the same time, and said he hoped he should be better acquainted with him, for without any flattery at all he seemed to have the makings of a very nice fellow about him, and to be just the man after his own heart.", "이 말에 푸른 옷을 입은 사내는, 흔히 들어 익숙한 칭찬이라는 듯 미소 지었다. 그러나 동시에 샘을 흐뭇하게 바라보면서, 더 잘 알고 지내게 되기를 바란다고 말했다. 빈말이 아니라 그에게는 아주 괜찮은 친구가 될 만한 자질이 엿보이고, 꼭 제 마음에 드는 사람인 듯하다는 것이었다."]], [["‘You’re wery good, sir,’ said Sam.", "\"친절하시네요, 선생.\" 샘이 말했다."], ["‘What a lucky feller you are!’", "\"댁은 참 운 좋은 양반이올시다!\""]], [["‘How do you mean?’ inquired the gentleman in blue.", "\"그게 무슨 뜻이오?\" 푸른 옷을 입은 신사가 물었다."]], [["‘That ‘ere young lady,’ replied Sam.", "\"그 아가씨 말입니다.\" 샘이 대답했다."], ["‘She knows wot’s wot, she does.", "\"그 처자는 뭘 좀 아는 양반이거든요, 그렇고말고요."], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["I see.’ Mr.", "알겠어요.\""], ["Weller closed one eye, and shook his head from side to side, in a manner which was highly gratifying to the personal vanity of the gentleman in blue.", "웰러 씨가 한쪽 눈을 감고 머리를 좌우로 흔들었는데, 그 모양이 푸른 옷을 입은 신사의 개인적 허영심을 자못 흡족하게 했다."]], [["‘I’m afraid you’re a cunning fellow, Mr.", "\"댁은 아무래도 능구렁이 같은 양반이군요, 웰러 씨.\""], ["Weller,’ said that individual.", "그 인물이 말했다."]], [["‘No, no,’ said Sam.", "\"아니, 아닙니다.\""], ["‘I leave all that ‘ere to you.", "샘이 말했다. \"그런 건 죄다 댁한테 맡기렵니다."], ["It’s a great deal more in your way than mine, as the gen’l’m’n on the right side o’ the garden vall said to the man on the wrong un, ven the mad bull vos a- comin’ up the lane.’", "그건 저보다 댁한테 훨씬 어울리는 일이지요. 정원 담장 옳은 쪽에 있던 신사 양반이, 미친 황소가 골목길로 올라오고 있을 때, 담장 그른 쪽에 있던 사람한테 한 말마따나요.\""]], [["‘Well, well, Mr.", "\"뭐, 뭐, 웰러 씨.\" 푸른 옷을 입은 신사가 말했다."], ["Weller,’ said the gentleman in blue, ‘I think she has remarked my air and manner, Mr. Weller.’", "\"내 생각엔 그 아가씨가 내 분위기와 몸가짐을 눈여겨본 것 같소, 웰러 씨.\""]], [["‘I should think she couldn’t wery well be off o’ that,’ said Sam.", "\"그걸 못 본 척하기란 어지간히 어려웠을 겁니다.\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["‘Have you any little thing of that kind in hand, sir?’ inquired the favoured gentleman in blue, drawing a toothpick from his waistcoat pocket.", "\"댁도 그런 자그마한 일이 손안에 하나쯤 있소, 선생?\" 호의를 입은 푸른 옷의 신사가 조끼 주머니에서 이쑤시개를 꺼내며 물었다."]], [["‘Not exactly,’ said Sam.", "\"꼭 그렇지는 않습니다.\" 샘이 말했다."], ["‘There’s no daughters at my place, else o’ course I should ha’ made up to vun on ‘em.", "\"제가 있는 데는 딸들이 없거든요. 아니면 물론 그중 하나한테 수작을 걸어 봤겠지요."], ["As it is, I don’t think I can do with anythin’ under a female markis.", "사정이 이러하니, 저는 여성 후작쯤은 돼야 만족할 수 있을 것 같습니다."], ["I might keep up with a young ‘ooman o’ large property as hadn’t a title, if she made wery fierce love to me.", "작위는 없어도 재산이 많은 젊은 여인이 저한테 아주 열렬히 사랑을 퍼부어 준다면 그럭저럭 어울려 볼 수는 있겠지만요."], ["Not else.’", "그게 아니면 안 돼요.\""]], [["‘Of course not, Mr.", "\"물론이지요, 웰러 씨.\""], ["Weller,’ said the gentleman in blue, ‘one can’t be troubled, you know; and we know, Mr.", "푸른 옷을 입은 신사가 말했다. \"성가신 일에 매일 수야 없지요, 아시잖소."], ["Weller--we, who are men of the world--that a good uniform must work its way with the women, sooner or later.", "그리고 우리는 압니다, 웰러 씨—세상 물정에 밝은 우리 같은 사람들은요—멋진 제복은 머지않아 여인들 사이에서 제 몫을 해낸다는 것을 말이오."], ["In fact, that’s the only thing, between you and me, that makes the service worth entering into.’", "사실 우리끼리 얘기지만, 그게 이 일에 발을 들일 만한 가치가 있게 만드는 유일한 거지요.\""]], [["‘Just so,’ said Sam.", "\"바로 그렇습니다.\" 샘이 말했다."], ["‘That’s it, o’ course.’", "\"그게 다지요, 물론입니다.\""]], [["When this confidential dialogue had gone thus far, glasses were placed round, and every gentleman ordered what he liked best, before the public-house shut up.", "이 은밀한 대화가 여기까지 이르렀을 때, 잔이 돌려졌고, 신사들은 저마다 술집 문 닫기 전에 제일 좋아하는 것을 시켰다."], ["The gentleman in blue, and the man in orange, who were the chief exquisites of the party, ordered ‘cold shrub and water,’ but with the others, gin-and-water, sweet, appeared to be the favourite beverage.", "일행 가운데 으뜸가는 멋쟁이인 푸른 옷의 신사와 오렌지빛 옷의 사내는 \"찬 과실주에 물을 탄 것\"을 시켰으나, 나머지 사람들 사이에서는 달콤한 진토닉이 가장 인기 있는 음료인 듯했다."], ["Sam called the greengrocer a ‘desp’rate willin,’ and ordered a large bowl of punch--two circumstances which seemed to raise him very much in the opinion of the selections.", "샘은 청과물 가게 주인을 \"지독한 악당\"이라고 부르며 큼지막한 펀치 한 사발을 시켰는데—이 두 가지 일이 정선된 인사들의 평가에서 그의 주가를 상당히 끌어올린 듯했다."]], [["‘Gentlemen,’ said the man in blue, with an air of the most consummate dandyism, ‘I’ll give you the ladies; come.’", "\"여러분,\" 푸른 옷을 입은 사내가 더없이 완벽한 멋쟁이의 태도로 말했다. \"숙녀분들을 위하여 건배하겠소. 자.\""]], [["‘Hear, hear!’ said Sam.", "\"옳소, 옳소!\" 샘이 말했다."], ["‘The young mississes.’", "\"젊은 마님들을 위하여.\""]], [["Here there was a loud cry of ‘Order,’ and Mr.", "이때 \"질서!\""], ["John Smauker, as the gentleman who had introduced Mr. Weller into that company, begged to inform him that the word he had just made use of, was unparliamentary.", "하고 외치는 소리가 크게 일었고, 웰러 씨를 그 자리에 소개한 신사로서 존 스모커 씨가, 그가 방금 쓴 그 말은 의회 규칙에 어긋난다고 알려 주었다."]], [["‘Which word was that ‘ere, Sir?’ inquired Sam.", "\"그게 어떤 말이었나요, 선생?\" 샘이 물었다."]], [["‘Mississes, Sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"마님들이라는 말이오, 선생.\""], ["John Smauker, with an alarming frown.", "존 스모커 씨가 섬뜩하게 눈살을 찌푸리며 대답했다."], ["‘We don’t recognise such distinctions here.’", "\"여기서는 그런 구별을 인정하지 않소.\""]], [["‘Oh, wery good,’ said Sam; ‘then I’ll amend the obserwation and call ‘em the dear creeturs, if Blazes vill allow me.’", "\"오, 아주 좋습니다.\" 샘이 말했다. \"그럼 그 말을 고쳐서 그분들을 사랑스러운 여인들이라고 부르지요, 불꽃 양반이 허락해 주신다면 말입니다.\""]], [["Some doubt appeared to exist in the mind of the gentleman in the green- foil smalls, whether the chairman could be legally appealed to, as ‘Blazes,’ but as the company seemed more disposed to stand upon their own rights than his, the question was not raised.", "초록 금박 반바지를 입은 신사의 마음속에는, 의장을 \"불꽃 양반\"이라 부르며 호소하는 것이 적법한지 어떤지 다소 의문이 이는 듯했다. 하지만 일동이 그의 권리보다는 자신들의 권리를 내세우는 쪽으로 더 기운 듯했으므로, 그 문제는 제기되지 않았다."], ["The man with the cocked hat breathed short, and looked long at Sam, but apparently thought it as well to say nothing, in case he should get the worst of it.", "삼각모를 쓴 사내는 숨을 가쁘게 몰아쉬며 샘을 오래도록 노려보았으나, 자칫 봉변을 당할까 봐 아무 말도 안 하는 편이 낫겠다고 여긴 듯했다."], ["After a short silence, a gentleman in an embroidered coat reaching down to his heels, and a waistcoat of the same which kept one half of his legs warm, stirred his gin-and-water with great energy, and putting himself upon his feet, all at once by a violent effort, said he was desirous of offering a few remarks to the company, whereupon the person in the cocked hat had no doubt that the company would be very happy to hear any remarks that the man in the long coat might wish to offer.", "잠시 침묵이 흐른 뒤, 발뒤꿈치까지 내려오는 수놓은 코트와, 다리의 절반을 따뜻하게 덮어 주는 똑같은 천의 조끼를 입은 신사 하나가, 진토닉을 힘차게 휘젓더니, 별안간 안간힘을 써서 벌떡 일어서서는, 일동에게 몇 마디 의견을 내고 싶다고 말했다. 그러자 삼각모를 쓴 인물은, 긴 코트를 입은 사내가 내놓고자 하는 어떤 의견이든 일동이 기꺼이 듣고 싶어 할 것이 분명하다고 했다."]], [["‘I feel a great delicacy, gentlemen, in coming for’ard,’ said the man in the long coat, ‘having the misforchune to be a coachman, and being only admitted as a honorary member of these agreeable swarrys, but I do feel myself bound, gentlemen--drove into a corner, if I may use the expression--to make known an afflicting circumstance which has come to my knowledge; which has happened I may say within the soap of my everyday contemplation.", "\"여러분, 저는 나서기가 몹시 조심스럽습니다.\" 긴 코트를 입은 사내가 말했다."], ["Gentlemen, our friend Mr.", "\"불행히도 마부 신세인 데다, 이 유쾌한 만찬회에 명예 회원으로만 받아들여진 처지인지라 말입니다."], ["Whiffers (everybody looked at the individual in orange), our friend Mr.", "하지만 여러분, 저는 제 자신이—이런 표현을 써도 된다면, 구석에 몰린 듯한 심정으로—제가 알게 된 어떤 통탄할 사정을 알려야 할 의무를 느낍니다. 말하자면 제 일상의 사색이 미치는 비누 거리 안에서 일어난 일이지요."], ["Whiffers has resigned.’", "여러분, 우리 친구 위퍼스 씨가(모두가 오렌지빛 옷을 입은 인물을 바라보았다), 우리 친구 위퍼스 씨가 사직했습니다.\""]], [["Universal astonishment fell upon the hearers.", "만장의 경악이 듣는 이들을 덮쳤다."], ["Each gentleman looked in his neighbour’s face, and then transferred his glance to the upstanding coachman.", "신사들은 저마다 옆 사람의 얼굴을 보고는, 이어 그 시선을 벌떡 일어선 마부에게로 옮겼다."]], [["‘You may well be sapparised, gentlemen,’ said the coachman.", "\"여러분, 놀라시는 것도 무리가 아닙니다.\" 마부가 말했다."], ["‘I will not wenchure to state the reasons of this irrepairabel loss to the service, but I will beg Mr.", "\"저는 이 직업계에 돌이킬 수 없는 손실이 된 이번 일의 이유를 감히 말씀드리지는 못하겠습니다만, 위퍼스 씨더러 직접 그 이유를 말씀해 주십사 청하겠습니다."], ["Whiffers to state them himself, for the improvement and imitation of his admiring friends.’", "그를 흠모하는 벗들의 발전과 본보기를 위해서 말이지요.\""]], [["The suggestion being loudly approved of, Mr. Whiffers explained.", "이 제안이 큰 소리로 찬동을 얻자, 위퍼스 씨가 사정을 설명했다."], ["He said he certainly could have wished to have continued to hold the appointment he had just resigned.", "그는 분명 자기가 방금 사직한 그 자리를 계속 지키고 싶었노라고 말했다."], ["The uniform was extremely rich and expensive, the females of the family was most agreeable, and the duties of the situation was not, he was bound to say, too heavy; the principal service that was required of him, being, that he should look out of the hall window as much as possible, in company with another gentleman, who had also resigned.", "제복은 더없이 호화롭고 값진 것이었으며, 그 집안의 여인들은 더없이 상냥했고, 그 자리의 직무도, 정말이지 너무 고되지는 않았다고 했다. 그에게 요구된 주된 일이라고는, 역시 사직한 또 다른 신사와 함께, 되도록 자주 현관 창밖을 내다보는 것이었으니까."], ["He could have wished to have spared that company the painful and disgusting detail on which he was about to enter, but as the explanation had been demanded of him, he had no alternative but to state, boldly and distinctly, that he had been required to eat cold meat.", "그는 자신이 이제부터 늘어놓으려는 그 고통스럽고 역겨운 자초지종으로부터 일동을 면해 주고 싶었으나, 해명을 요구받은 이상, 대담하고도 또렷이 밝히는 것 말고는 달리 도리가 없다고 했다—그는 식은 고기를 먹으라는 요구를 받았던 것이다."]], [["It is impossible to conceive the disgust which this avowal awakened in the bosoms of the hearers.", "이 고백이 듣는 이들의 가슴속에 불러일으킨 혐오감은 이루 헤아릴 수 없다."], ["Loud cries of ‘Shame,’ mingled with groans and hisses, prevailed for a quarter of an hour.", "\"수치다!\" 하는 큰 외침이 신음과 야유에 뒤섞여 십오 분 동안이나 이어졌다."]], [["Mr. Whiffers then added that he feared a portion of this outrage might be traced to his own forbearing and accommodating disposition.", "이어 위퍼스 씨는, 이 만행의 일부는 자기 자신의 참고 맞춰 주는 성품에서 비롯되었을지도 모른다고 덧붙였다."], ["He had a distinct recollection of having once consented to eat salt butter, and he had, moreover, on an occasion of sudden sickness in the house, so far forgotten himself as to carry a coal-scuttle up to the second floor.", "그는 한때 짠 버터를 먹는 데 동의했던 일을 또렷이 기억하고 있으며, 게다가 집안에 갑작스레 환자가 생겼던 어느 때에는, 분수도 잊고서 석탄통을 이 층까지 들고 올라간 일까지 있었다는 것이다."], ["He trusted he had not lowered himself in the good opinion of his friends by this frank confession of his faults; and he hoped the promptness with which he had resented the last unmanly outrage on his feelings, to which he had referred, would reinstate him in their good opinion, if he had.", "그는 자신의 잘못에 대한 이 솔직한 고백으로 인해 벗들이 자기를 보는 좋은 평판이 깎이지 않았기를 바라며, 또 만약 깎였다 하더라도, 방금 언급한, 자신의 감정에 가해진 그 마지막 비열한 만행에 자기가 보인 단호한 분노로 인해 다시금 좋은 평판을 되찾기를 바란다고 했다."]], [["Mr. Whiffers’s address was responded to, with a shout of admiration, and the health of the interesting martyr was drunk in a most enthusiastic manner; for this, the martyr returned thanks, and proposed their visitor, Mr.", "위퍼스 씨의 연설에는 감탄의 함성으로 화답이 일었고, 그 흥미로운 순교자의 건강을 위해 더없이 열렬하게 건배가 이루어졌다."], ["Weller--a gentleman whom he had not the pleasure of an intimate acquaintance with, but who was the friend of Mr. John Smauker, which was a sufficient letter of recommendation to any society of gentlemen whatever, or wherever.", "이에 순교자는 사례하고서, 그들의 손님 웰러 씨를 위해 건배하자고 제안했다—친밀하게 알고 지내는 기쁨은 누리지 못한 신사이나, 존 스모커 씨의 친구이니, 그것이야말로 어떤 신사들의 모임이든, 그것이 어디에 있든 충분한 추천장이 되는 것이었다."], ["On this account, he should have been disposed to have given Mr. Weller’s health with all the honours, if his friends had been drinking wine; but as they were taking spirits by way of a change, and as it might be inconvenient to empty a tumbler at every toast, he should propose that the honours be understood.", "이러한 까닭에, 그는 벗들이 포도주를 마시고 있었다면 모든 예우를 갖추어 웰러 씨의 건강을 위해 건배하고 싶었을 것이나, 기분 전환 삼아 다들 독한 술을 들고 있는 데다, 건배할 때마다 잔을 비우는 것이 거북할 수도 있으니, 예우는 마음으로 갖춘 것으로 여기자고 제안했다."]], [["At the conclusion of this speech, everybody took a sip in honour of Sam; and Sam having ladled out, and drunk, two full glasses of punch in honour of himself, returned thanks in a neat speech.", "이 연설이 끝나자, 모두가 샘을 위하여 한 모금씩 들이켰다. 그리고 샘은 자신을 위하여 펀치 두 잔을 가득 떠서 들이켜고는, 말끔한 연설로 사례했다."]], [["‘Wery much obliged to you, old fellers,’ said Sam, ladling away at the punch in the most unembarrassed manner possible, ‘for this here compliment; which, comin’ from sich a quarter, is wery overvelmin’. I’ve heered a good deal on you as a body, but I will say, that I never thought you was sich uncommon nice men as I find you air. I only hope you’ll take care o’ yourselves, and not compromise nothin’ o’ your dignity, which is a wery charmin’ thing to see, when one’s out a- walkin’, and has always made me wery happy to look at, ever since I was a boy about half as high as the brass-headed stick o’ my wery respectable friend, Blazes, there. As to the wictim of oppression in the suit o’ brimstone, all I can say of him, is, that I hope he’ll get jist as good a berth as he deserves; in vitch case it’s wery little cold swarry as ever he’ll be troubled with agin.’", "\"대단히 고맙습니다, 오랜 친구분들.\" 샘이 더없이 거리낌 없는 태도로 펀치를 연신 퍼 담으며 말했다. \"이렇게 분에 넘치는 찬사를 보내 주셔서 말입니다. 이런 데서 나오는 칭찬이고 보니 그저 황송할 따름이지요. 저는 여러분에 대해 한 집단으로서 익히 들어 왔습니다만, 이렇게 막상 뵙고 보니 여러분이 이토록 보기 드물게 좋은 분들일 줄은 미처 생각도 못 했다고 말씀드리지 않을 수 없습니다. 다만 부디 몸 잘 챙기시고, 그 위엄을 조금도 손상시키지 마시기를 바랄 뿐입니다. 그 위엄이라는 게, 길을 거닐다 보면 정말이지 보기에 매력적인 것이어서, 제가 저기 계신 더없이 존경스러운 제 친구 불꽃 양반의 놋쇠 머리 막대기의 절반쯤밖에 안 되던 꼬맹이 시절부터 늘 그걸 바라보는 것이 저를 아주 행복하게 해 주었지요. 유황빛 양복을 입은 저 핍박의 희생자 양반으로 말하자면, 제가 그분께 드릴 수 있는 말은, 부디 그분이 마땅히 받을 만한 좋은 자리를 얻으시기를 바란다는 것뿐입니다. 그렇게만 된다면, 그분이 다시는 식은 만찬 따위로 시달릴 일은 거의 없을 테니까요.\""]], [["Here Sam sat down with a pleasant smile, and his speech having been vociferously applauded, the company broke up.", "여기서 샘은 유쾌한 미소를 지으며 자리에 앉았고, 그의 연설이 떠나갈 듯한 갈채를 받자, 일동은 자리를 파했다."]], [["‘Wy, you don’t mean to say you’re a-goin’ old feller?’ said Sam Weller to his friend, Mr.", "\"아니, 설마 가시려는 건 아니겠죠, 오랜 친구 양반?\""], ["John Smauker.", "샘 웰러가 그의 친구 존 스모커 씨에게 말했다."]], [["‘I must, indeed,’ said Mr.", "\"정말 가야 하오.\" 스모커 씨가 말했다."], ["Smauker; ‘I promised Bantam.’", "\"밴텀과 약속을 했거든요.\""]], [["‘Oh, wery well,’ said Sam; ‘that’s another thing.", "\"오, 그렇군요.\" 샘이 말했다."], ["P’raps he’d resign if you disappinted him.", "\"그건 또 다른 문제지요. 댁이 바람을 맞히면 그분이 사직이라도 하실지 모르니까요."], ["You ain’t a-goin’, Blazes?’", "불꽃 양반은 안 가시겠죠?\""]], [["‘Yes, I am,’ said the man with the cocked hat.", "\"아니, 나도 가야겠소.\" 삼각모를 쓴 사내가 말했다."]], [["‘Wot, and leave three-quarters of a bowl of punch behind you!’ said Sam; ‘nonsense, set down agin.’", "\"아니, 펀치를 사발 가득 사 분의 삼이나 남겨 두고요!\" 샘이 말했다. \"말도 안 됩니다, 도로 앉으세요.\""]], [["Mr. Tuckle was not proof against this invitation.", "터클 씨는 이 권유를 당해 내지 못했다."], ["He laid aside the cocked hat and stick which he had just taken up, and said he would have one glass, for good fellowship’s sake.", "그는 방금 집어 든 삼각모와 막대기를 다시 내려놓고는, 좋은 우정을 위해 한 잔 들겠노라고 말했다."]], [["As the gentleman in blue went home the same way as Mr. Tuckle, he was prevailed upon to stop too.", "푸른 옷을 입은 신사는 터클 씨와 가는 길이 같았으므로, 그 역시 못 이기는 척 머물렀다."], ["When the punch was about half gone, Sam ordered in some oysters from the green-grocer’s shop; and the effect of both was so extremely exhilarating, that Mr. Tuckle, dressed out with the cocked hat and stick, danced the frog hornpipe among the shells on the table, while the gentleman in blue played an accompaniment upon an ingenious musical instrument formed of a hair-comb upon a curl-paper.", "펀치가 절반쯤 비었을 때, 샘이 청과물 가게에서 굴을 좀 들여오게 했는데, 그 두 가지의 효과가 어찌나 흥을 돋우었던지, 터클 씨는 삼각모와 막대기로 차려입은 채 식탁 위 굴 껍데기 사이에서 개구리 혼파이프 춤을 추었고, 그동안 푸른 옷을 입은 신사는 곱슬머리 마는 종이에 머리빗을 댄, 기발한 악기로 반주를 넣었다."], ["At last, when the punch was all gone, and the night nearly so, they sallied forth to see each other home. Mr.", "마침내 펀치가 죄다 비고 밤도 거의 다 지나갔을 때, 그들은 서로 바래다주려고 우르르 밖으로 나섰다."], ["Tuckle no sooner got into the open air, than he was seized with a sudden desire to lie on the curbstone; Sam thought it would be a pity to contradict him, and so let him have his own way.", "터클 씨는 바깥 공기를 쐬자마자 별안간 길가 연석 위에 드러눕고 싶은 욕구에 사로잡혔다. 샘은 그를 거스르는 것이 딱하다 싶어 그가 하고 싶은 대로 내버려 두었다."], ["As the cocked hat would have been spoiled if left there, Sam very considerately flattened it down on the head of the gentleman in blue, and putting the big stick in his hand, propped him up against his own street-door, rang the bell, and walked quietly home.", "삼각모를 거기 두면 못 쓰게 될 터였으므로, 샘은 자못 사려 깊게 그것을 푸른 옷을 입은 신사의 머리에 납작하게 눌러 씌우고, 큼지막한 막대기를 그의 손에 쥐여 준 다음, 그를 그의 집 대문에 기대 세워 두고 초인종을 울리고는, 조용히 집으로 걸어갔다."]], [["At a much earlier hour next morning than his usual time of rising, Mr. Pickwick walked downstairs completely dressed, and rang the bell.", "이튿날 아침, 픽윅 씨는 여느 때 일어나는 시각보다 훨씬 이른 시각에 옷을 말끔히 차려입고 아래층으로 내려와 초인종을 울렸다."]], [["‘Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick, when Mr.", "\"샘,\""], ["Weller appeared in reply to the summons, ‘shut the door.’", "부름에 응해 웰러 씨가 나타나자 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"문을 닫게.\""]], [["Mr. Weller did so.", "웰러 씨는 그렇게 했다."]], [["‘There was an unfortunate occurrence here, last night, Sam,’ said Mr.", "\"간밤에 여기서 불행한 일이 하나 있었네, 샘.\""], ["Pickwick, ‘which gave Mr.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Winkle some cause to apprehend violence from Mr. Dowler.’", "\"그 일로 윈클 씨가 다울러 씨한테서 봉변을 당할까 봐 다소 우려할 만한 까닭이 생겼지.\""]], [["‘So I’ve heerd from the old lady downstairs, Sir,’ replied Sam.", "\"그 얘기라면 아래층 노부인한테서 들었습니다, 선생님.\" 샘이 대답했다."]], [["‘And I’m sorry to say, Sam,’ continued Mr.", "\"그런데 안타깝게도 말일세, 샘,\" 픽윅 씨가 더없이 난처한 얼굴로 말을 이었다."], ["Pickwick, with a most perplexed countenance, ‘that in dread of this violence, Mr. Winkle has gone away.’", "\"이 봉변이 두려운 나머지 윈클 씨가 떠나 버렸다네.\""]], [["‘Gone avay!’ said Sam.", "\"떠나 버렸다고요!\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["‘Left the house early this morning, without the slightest previous communication with me,’ replied Mr.", "\"오늘 아침 일찌감치, 나한테 미리 한마디 귀띔도 없이 집을 나갔다네.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다."], ["Pickwick. ‘And is gone, I know not where.’", "\"그리고 어디로 갔는지 나는 알 수가 없어.\""]], [["‘He should ha’ stopped and fought it out, Sir,’ replied Sam contemptuously.", "\"그냥 남아서 끝장을 봤어야지요, 선생님.\" 샘이 깔보듯 대답했다."], ["‘It wouldn’t take much to settle that ‘ere Dowler, Sir.’", "\"그 다울러라는 작자쯤 처치하는 데는 별것 안 들 텐데요, 선생님.\""]], [["‘Well, Sam,’ said Mr.", "\"글쎄, 샘,\""], ["Pickwick, ‘I may have my doubts of his great bravery and determination also.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"나도 그의 대단한 용기와 결단력에 대해서는 의심이 들지도 모르겠네."], ["But however that may be, Mr. Winkle is gone.", "하지만 어찌 됐든, 윈클 씨는 가 버렸어."], ["He must be found, Sam.", "그를 찾아내야 하네, 샘."], ["Found and brought back to me.’", "찾아내서 나한테 데려와야 해.\""]], [["And s’pose he won’t come back, Sir?’ said Sam.", "\"그런데 그분이 돌아오지 않겠다고 하면요, 선생님?\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["‘He must be made, Sam,’ said Mr.", "\"오게 만들어야지, 샘.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Who’s to do it, Sir?’ inquired Sam, with a smile.", "\"누가 그 일을 합니까, 선생님?\" 샘이 미소 지으며 물었다."]], [["‘You,’ replied Mr.", "\"자네지.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘Wery good, Sir.’", "\"아주 좋습니다, 선생님.\""]], [["With these words Mr. Weller left the room, and immediately afterwards was heard to shut the street door.", "이 말과 함께 웰러 씨는 방을 나갔고, 곧이어 그가 대문을 닫는 소리가 들렸다."], ["In two hours’ time he returned with so much coolness as if he had been despatched on the most ordinary message possible, and brought the information that an individual, in every respect answering Mr. Winkle’s description, had gone over to Bristol that morning, by the branch coach from the Royal Hotel.", "두 시간쯤 지나서 그는, 더없이 평범한 심부름이라도 다녀온 양 어찌나 태연한지 모를 모습으로 돌아와서는, 모든 점에서 윈클 씨의 인상착의에 들어맞는 한 인물이 그날 아침 로열 호텔에서 출발하는 지선 마차를 타고 브리스틀로 건너갔다는 정보를 가져왔다."]], [["‘Sam,’ said Mr.", "\"샘,\""], ["Pickwick, grasping his hand, ‘you’re a capital fellow; an invaluable fellow.", "픽윅 씨가 그의 손을 부여잡으며 말했다. \"자네는 훌륭한 친구야."], ["You must follow him, Sam.’", "더할 나위 없이 소중한 친구일세. 자네가 그를 뒤쫓아야 하네, 샘.\""]], [["‘Cert’nly, Sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"물론이지요, 선생님.\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘The instant you discover him, write to me immediately, Sam,’ said Mr.", "\"그를 찾아내는 즉시 나한테 곧바로 편지를 쓰게, 샘.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick. ‘If he attempts to run away from you, knock him down, or lock him up.", "\"그가 자네한테서 달아나려고 하거든, 때려눕히든지 가둬 버리게."], ["You have my full authority, Sam.’", "자네한테 전권을 주는 걸세, 샘.\""]], [["‘I’ll be wery careful, sir,’ rejoined Sam.", "\"아주 조심하겠습니다, 선생님.\" 샘이 응했다."]], [["‘You’ll tell him,’ said Mr.", "\"그에게 전하게,\""], ["Pickwick, ‘that I am highly excited, highly displeased, and naturally indignant, at the very extraordinary course he has thought proper to pursue.’", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"그가 적절하다고 여겨 취한 이 지극히 유별난 처사에, 내가 몹시 격앙되고, 몹시 언짢고, 당연히 분개하고 있다고 말일세.\""]], [["‘I will, Sir,’ replied Sam.", "\"그러겠습니다, 선생님.\" 샘이 대답했다."]], [["‘You’ll tell him,’ said Mr.", "\"그에게 전하게,\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick, ‘that if he does not come back to this very house, with you, he will come back with me, for I will come and fetch him.’", "\"만약 그가 자네와 함께 바로 이 집으로 돌아오지 않는다면, 나와 함께 돌아오게 될 거라고. 내가 직접 가서 데려올 테니 말일세.\""]], [["‘I’ll mention that ‘ere, Sir,’ rejoined Sam.", "\"그 말씀을 전하겠습니다, 선생님.\" 샘이 응했다."]], [["‘You think you can find him, Sam?’ said Mr.", "\"자네는 그를 찾아낼 수 있겠나, 샘?\""], ["Pickwick, looking earnestly in his face.", "픽윅 씨가 그의 얼굴을 진지하게 들여다보며 말했다."]], [["‘Oh, I’ll find him if he’s anyvere,’ rejoined Sam, with great confidence.", "\"오, 그분이 어디 있기만 하다면 찾아내고말고요.\" 샘이 자못 자신만만하게 응했다."]], [["‘Very well,’ said Mr. Pickwick.", "\"아주 좋아.\""], ["‘Then the sooner you go the better.’", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"그럼 빨리 갈수록 좋겠지.\""]], [["With these instructions, Mr. Pickwick placed a sum of money in the hands of his faithful servitor, and ordered him to start for Bristol immediately, in pursuit of the fugitive.", "이런 지시와 함께, 픽윅 씨는 충직한 하인의 손에 얼마간의 돈을 쥐여 주고는, 그에게 즉시 브리스틀로 떠나 달아난 자를 뒤쫓으라고 일렀다."]], [["Sam put a few necessaries in a carpet-bag, and was ready for starting.", "샘은 여행용 가방에 필요한 물건 몇 가지를 챙겨 넣고 떠날 채비를 마쳤다."], ["He stopped when he had got to the end of the passage, and walking quietly back, thrust his head in at the parlour door.", "그런데 통로 끝까지 갔다가 걸음을 멈추고, 조용히 되돌아와서는 응접실 문 안으로 머리를 들이밀었다."]], [["‘Sir,’ whispered Sam.", "\"선생님.\" 샘이 속삭였다."]], [["‘Well, Sam,’ said Mr.", "\"왜 그러나, 샘.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘I fully understands my instructions, do I, Sir?’ inquired Sam.", "\"제 지시를 제가 다 잘 알아들었지요, 선생님?\" 샘이 물었다."]], [["‘I hope so,’ said Mr.", "\"그러기를 바라네.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘It’s reg’larly understood about the knockin’ down, is it, Sir?’ inquired Sam.", "\"그 때려눕히는 일에 대해선 확실히 정해진 거지요, 선생님?\" 샘이 물었다."]], [["‘Perfectly,’ replied Pickwick.", "\"완벽하게 정해졌네.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다."], ["‘Thoroughly.", "\"철저하게 말이야."], ["Do what you think necessary.", "필요하다고 생각하는 대로 하게."], ["You have my orders.’", "자네한테 명령을 내렸으니.\""]], [["Sam gave a nod of intelligence, and withdrawing his head from the door, set forth on his pilgrimage with a light heart.", "샘은 알아들었다는 뜻으로 고개를 끄덕이고는, 문에서 머리를 거두며, 가벼운 마음으로 자신의 순례 길에 올랐다."]], [["CHAPTER XXXVIII. HOW MR.", "이미 묘사한 방식과 형태로 로열 크레슨트의 주민들을 놀라게 했던 그 예사롭지 않은 소동과 소란의 불운한 장본인이었던 별 박복한 신사는, 큰 혼란과 불안 속에 하룻밤을 보낸 뒤, 친구들이 아직 잠들어 있는 지붕 아래를 떠나, 어디로 가는지도 모른 채 길을 나섰다."], ["WINKLE, WHEN HE STEPPED OUT OF THE FRYING-PAN, WALKED GENTLY AND COMFORTABLY INTO THE FIRE\nThe ill-starred gentleman who had been the unfortunate cause of the unusual noise and disturbance which alarmed the inhabitants of the Royal Crescent in manner and form already described, after passing a night of great confusion and anxiety, left the roof beneath which his friends still slumbered, bound he knew not whither. The excellent and considerate feelings which prompted Mr.", "윈클 씨로 하여금 이런 조치를 취하게 만든 그 훌륭하고도 사려 깊은 마음은 아무리 높이 평가하고 아무리 따뜻하게 칭송해도 지나치지 않을 것이다."], ["Winkle to take this step can never be too highly appreciated or too warmly extolled.", "\"만일,\" 하고 윈클 씨는 혼자 추론했다."], ["‘If,’ reasoned Mr. Winkle with himself--‘if this Dowler attempts (as I have no doubt he will) to carry into execution his threat of personal violence against myself, it will be incumbent on me to call him out.", "\"만일 이 다울러가 (틀림없이 그러리라 의심치 않는바) 나에게 신체적 폭력을 가하겠다는 위협을 실행에 옮기려 든다면, 나로서는 그에게 결투를 신청하지 않을 수 없게 된다."], ["He has a wife; that wife is attached to, and dependent on him.", "그에게는 아내가 있다. 그 아내는 그를 사랑하고 그에게 의지하고 있다."], ["Heavens!", "맙소사!"], ["If I should kill him in the blindness of my wrath, what would be my feelings ever afterwards!’ This painful consideration operated so powerfully on the feelings of the humane young man, as to cause his knees to knock together, and his countenance to exhibit alarming manifestations of inward emotion.", "만일 내가 분노에 눈이 멀어 그를 죽이기라도 한다면, 그 후로 내 심정이 어떠하겠는가!\" 이 고통스러운 생각이 이 인정 많은 청년의 감정에 어찌나 강하게 작용했던지, 그의 두 무릎은 서로 부딪쳤고, 그의 얼굴은 내면의 격정을 드러내는 놀라운 징후들을 나타냈다."], ["Impelled by such reflections, he grasped his carpet-bag, and creeping stealthily downstairs, shut the detestable street door with as little noise as possible, and walked off.", "그러한 상념에 떠밀려, 그는 자신의 융단 가방을 움켜쥐고는, 살금살금 아래층으로 내려가, 그 가증스러운 거리 쪽 문을 되도록 소리 안 나게 닫고 걸어 나갔다."], ["Bending his steps towards the Royal Hotel, he found a coach on the point of starting for Bristol, and, thinking Bristol as good a place for his purpose as any other he could go to, he mounted the box, and reached his place of destination in such time as the pair of horses, who went the whole stage and back again, twice a day or more, could be reasonably supposed to arrive there.", "로열 호텔 쪽으로 발걸음을 옮기다가, 그는 마침 브리스틀로 출발하려는 마차를 발견했고, 브리스틀이 자기 목적에 다른 어느 곳 못지않게 적당하다고 여겨, 마부석에 올라탔다. 그리하여 하루에 두 번 이상 그 구간을 왕복하는 두 마리 말이 도착하리라고 합리적으로 기대할 만한 시간 안에 목적지에 다다랐다."]], [["He took up his quarters at the Bush, and designing to postpone any communication by letter with Mr. Pickwick until it was probable that Mr.", "그는 부시 여관에 거처를 정했고, 다울러 씨의 분노가 어느 정도 가라앉았으리라 짐작될 때까지 픽윅 씨에게 편지로 알리는 일을 미루기로 작정한 뒤, 도시를 구경하러 나섰는데, 그 도시는 그가 여태껏 본 어느 곳보다도 한층 더 지저분하다는 인상을 주었다."], ["Dowler’s wrath might have in some degree evaporated, walked forth to view the city, which struck him as being a shade more dirty than any place he had ever seen.", "부두와 선박들을 살피고 대성당을 둘러본 다음, 그는 클리프턴으로 가는 길을 물었고, 그쪽으로 안내받아 가르쳐 준 노선을 따라갔다."], ["Having inspected the docks and shipping, and viewed the cathedral, he inquired his way to Clifton, and being directed thither, took the route which was pointed out to him.", "그러나 브리스틀의 보도가 지상에서 가장 넓거나 깨끗한 편이 못 되는 것처럼, 그 거리들 또한 도통 가장 곧거나 가장 덜 복잡한 편이 못 되었다."], ["But as the pavements of Bristol are not the widest or cleanest upon earth, so its streets are not altogether the straightest or least intricate; and Mr. Winkle, being greatly puzzled by their manifold windings and twistings, looked about him for a decent shop in which he could apply afresh for counsel and instruction.", "그래서 윈클 씨는 그 갖가지 굽이와 비틀림에 크게 어리둥절해져, 다시 한번 길 안내와 조언을 청할 만한 변변한 가게가 없는지 주위를 둘러보았다."]], [["His eye fell upon a newly-painted tenement which had been recently converted into something between a shop and a private house, and which a red lamp, projecting over the fanlight of the street door, would have sufficiently announced as the residence of a medical practitioner, even if the word ‘Surgery’ had not been inscribed in golden characters on a wainscot ground, above the window of what, in times bygone, had been the front parlour.", "그의 눈길은 새로 페인트칠을 한 한 건물에 떨어졌는데, 그것은 최근에 가게와 개인 주택의 중간쯤 되는 무언가로 개조된 것이었다. 거리 쪽 문 채광창 위로 튀어나온 붉은 등 하나만으로도 그곳이 의사의 거처임을 충분히 알려 주었을 터인데, 설령 지난날에는 응접실이었던 곳의 창문 위쪽 징두리널 바탕에 금빛 글자로 '수술'이라는 단어가 새겨져 있지 않았더라도 그러했을 것이다."], ["Thinking this an eligible place wherein to make his inquiries, Mr.", "여기가 자기 질문을 던지기에 적합한 곳이라 여기고, 윈클 씨는 금빛 표딱지가 붙은 서랍들과 병들이 놓인 작은 가게 안으로 들어섰다."], ["Winkle stepped into the little shop where the gilt- labelled drawers and bottles were; and finding nobody there, knocked with a half-crown on the counter, to attract the attention of anybody who might happen to be in the back parlour, which he judged to be the innermost and peculiar sanctum of the establishment, from the repetition of the word surgery on the door--painted in white letters this time, by way of taking off the monotony.", "그러나 거기에는 아무도 없었으므로, 그는 안쪽 응접실에 혹 있을지 모를 누군가의 주의를 끌려고 반 크라운짜리 동전으로 계산대를 두드렸다. 그 응접실이 이 가게의 가장 깊숙하고도 특별한 성소이리라고 짐작한 것은, 문에 다시금 '수술'이라는 단어가—이번에는 단조로움을 덜고자 흰 글자로—적혀 있었기 때문이다."]], [["At the first knock, a sound, as of persons fencing with fire-irons, which had until now been very audible, suddenly ceased; at the second, a studious-looking young gentleman in green spectacles, with a very large book in his hand, glided quietly into the shop, and stepping behind the counter, requested to know the visitor’s pleasure.", "첫 노크 소리에, 그때까지 또렷이 들려오던, 부지깽이로 펜싱이라도 하는 듯한 소리가 갑자기 멎었다. 두 번째 노크 소리에, 초록색 안경을 쓰고 매우 커다란 책 한 권을 손에 든, 학구적으로 보이는 한 젊은 신사가 조용히 가게 안으로 미끄러져 들어와, 계산대 뒤로 돌아가 손님의 용건을 알고 싶다고 청했다."]], [["‘I am sorry to trouble you, Sir,’ said Mr.", "\"폐를 끼쳐 죄송합니다만, 선생님,\""], ["Winkle, ‘but will you have the goodness to direct me to--’", "윈클 씨가 말했다. \"괜찮으시다면 제게 길을 좀 가르쳐 주시겠습니까—\""]], [["‘Ha!", "\"하!"], ["ha!", "하!"], ["ha!’ roared the studious young gentleman, throwing the large book up into the air, and catching it with great dexterity at the very moment when it threatened to smash to atoms all the bottles on the counter.", "하!\" 그 학구적인 젊은 신사가 큰 소리로 웃으며, 커다란 책을 공중으로 던져 올렸다가, 그것이 계산대 위의 모든 병을 산산조각 내려는 바로 그 순간에 대단한 솜씨로 낚아챘다."], ["‘Here’s a start!’", "\"이거 한판 놀랄 일인데!\""]], [["There was, without doubt; for Mr.", "과연 놀랄 일이었다."], ["Winkle was so very much astonished at the extraordinary behaviour of the medical gentleman, that he involuntarily retreated towards the door, and looked very much disturbed at his strange reception.", "윈클 씨는 그 의사 양반의 비상한 행동에 어찌나 깜짝 놀랐던지, 저도 모르게 문 쪽으로 물러섰고, 자신을 맞이하는 그 괴이한 태도에 몹시 당혹스러운 표정을 지었다."]], [["‘What, don’t you know me?’ said the medical gentleman.", "\"아니, 나를 모른단 말이오?\" 의사 양반이 말했다."]], [["Mr. Winkle murmured, in reply, that he had not that pleasure.", "윈클 씨는 그런 영광은 누리지 못했노라고 중얼거리며 대답했다."]], [["‘Why, then,’ said the medical gentleman, ‘there are hopes for me yet; I may attend half the old women in Bristol, if I’ve decent luck. Get out, you mouldy old villain, get out!’ With this adjuration, which was addressed to the large book, the medical gentleman kicked the volume with remarkable agility to the farther end of the shop, and, pulling off his green spectacles, grinned the identical grin of Robert Sawyer, Esquire, formerly of Guy’s Hospital in the Borough, with a private residence in Lant Street.", "\"그렇다면,\" 의사 양반이 말했다. \"내게도 아직 희망이 있군. 운만 어지간히 따라 주면 브리스틀의 늙은 여편네 절반은 진료할 수 있겠어. 꺼져라, 이 곰팡내 나는 늙은 악당아, 꺼져!\" 커다란 책을 향해 던진 이 호통과 함께, 의사 양반은 놀라운 민첩함으로 그 책을 발로 차서 가게 저 끝으로 날려 보냈고, 초록색 안경을 벗어 던지더니, 일찍이 자치구의 가이 병원에 적을 두었으며 랜트 거리에 개인 거처를 두었던 로버트 소여 씨 바로 그 사람의 그 웃음을 씩 지어 보였다."]], [["‘You don’t mean to say you weren’t down upon me?’ said Mr.", "\"나를 알아보지 못했다는 말은 아니겠죠?\""], ["Bob Sawyer, shaking Mr. Winkle’s hand with friendly warmth.", "보브 소여 씨가 다정한 온기로 윈클 씨의 손을 흔들며 말했다."]], [["‘Upon my word I was not,’ replied Mr.", "\"정말이지 못 알아봤습니다.\""], ["Winkle, returning his pressure.", "윈클 씨가 그의 악수에 응하며 대답했다."]], [["‘I wonder you didn’t see the name,’ said Bob Sawyer, calling his friend’s attention to the outer door, on which, in the same white paint, were traced the words ‘Sawyer, late Nockemorf.’", "\"이름을 못 봤다니 이상하군요.\" 보브 소여가 바깥쪽 문으로 친구의 주의를 끌며 말했는데, 그 문에는 똑같은 흰 페인트로 '소여, 전 녹케모프'라는 글자가 새겨져 있었다."]], [["‘It never caught my eye,’ returned Mr.", "\"그건 제 눈에 안 띄었어요.\""], ["Winkle.", "윈클 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘Lord, if I had known who you were, I should have rushed out, and caught you in my arms,’ said Bob Sawyer; ‘but upon my life, I thought you were the King’s-taxes.’", "\"이런, 당신인 줄 알았더라면, 뛰쳐나가 두 팔로 끌어안았을 텐데,\" 보브 소여가 말했다. \"그런데 정말이지, 나는 당신이 국세청 사람인 줄로만 알았다니까요.\""]], [["‘No!’ said Mr.", "\"설마요!\""], ["Winkle.", "윈클 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘I did, indeed,’ responded Bob Sawyer, ‘and I was just going to say that I wasn’t at home, but if you’d leave a message I’d be sure to give it to myself; for he don’t know me; no more does the Lighting and Paving.", "\"정말 그랬다니까요.\" 보브 소여가 응수했다."], ["I think the Church-rates guesses who I am, and I know the Water-works does, because I drew a tooth of his when I first came down here.", "\"마침 집에 없다고 말하려던 참이었어요. 그래도 전할 말씀이 있으면 내가 꼭 나 자신에게 전해 주겠노라고 말이오."], ["But come in, come in!’ Chattering in this way, Mr.", "그자는 나를 모르거든요."], ["Bob Sawyer pushed Mr.", "가로등·포장 관리국도 마찬가지고."], ["Winkle into the back room, where, amusing himself by boring little circular caverns in the chimney-piece with a red-hot poker, sat no less a person than Mr.", "교회세 쪽은 내가 누군지 어렴풋이 짐작하는 것 같고, 수도국 쪽은 분명히 알지요. 왜냐하면 내가 여기 처음 내려왔을 때 그 사람 이를 하나 뽑아 줬으니까."], ["Benjamin Allen.", "어쨌든 들어와요, 들어와!\" 이런 식으로 재잘대며, 보브 소여 씨는 윈클 씨를 안쪽 방으로 밀어 넣었는데, 거기에는 다름 아닌 벤저민 앨런 씨가, 시뻘겋게 달군 부지깽이로 벽난로 선반에 자그마한 둥근 동굴들을 뚫으며 혼자 즐기고 앉아 있었다."]], [["‘Well!’ said Mr. Winkle.", "\"이야!\""], ["‘This is indeed a pleasure I did not expect.", "윈클 씨가 말했다. \"이거 정말 뜻밖의 기쁨이군요."], ["What a very nice place you have here!’", "참으로 멋진 곳을 가지셨네요!\""]], [["‘Pretty well, pretty well,’ replied Bob Sawyer.", "\"그저 그렇소, 그저 그래요.\" 보브 소여가 대답했다."], ["‘I passed, soon after that precious party, and my friends came down with the needful for this business; so I put on a black suit of clothes, and a pair of spectacles, and came here to look as solemn as I could.’", "\"그 진귀한 파티가 있고 나서 얼마 안 되어 시험에 통과했고, 친구들이 이 사업에 필요한 돈을 대 줘서 말이오. 그래서 검은 양복 한 벌에 안경 한 쌍을 걸치고 여기 내려와, 되도록 근엄해 보이려고 하는 거랍니다.\""]], [["‘And a very snug little business you have, no doubt?’ said Mr.", "\"그리고 분명 아주 아늑하고 자그마한 사업을 하고 계시겠죠?\""], ["Winkle knowingly.", "윈클 씨가 아는 체하며 말했다."]], [["‘Very,’ replied Bob Sawyer.", "\"아주 그렇소.\" 보브 소여가 대답했다."], ["‘So snug, that at the end of a few years you might put all the profits in a wine-glass, and cover ‘em over with a gooseberry leaf.’", "\"어찌나 아늑한지, 몇 해가 지나고 나면 그동안 번 이문을 전부 포도주잔 하나에 담아 까치밥나무 잎사귀 한 장으로 덮어 둘 수 있을 정도라오.\""]], [["You cannot surely mean that?’ said Mr.", "\"설마 진심으로 하는 말은 아니겠죠?\" 윈클 씨가 말했다."], ["Winkle. ‘The stock itself--’", "\"재고만 해도—\""]], [["Dummies, my dear boy,’ said Bob Sawyer; ‘half the drawers have nothing in ‘em, and the other half don’t open.’", "\"눈속임이라오, 이 친구야.\" 보브 소여가 말했다. \"서랍의 절반은 안에 아무것도 없고, 나머지 절반은 아예 열리지도 않소.\""]], [["‘Nonsense!’ said Mr.", "\"말도 안 돼요!\""], ["Winkle.", "윈클 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Fact--honour!’ returned Bob Sawyer, stepping out into the shop, and demonstrating the veracity of the assertion by divers hard pulls at the little gilt knobs on the counterfeit drawers.", "\"사실이오—명예를 걸고!\" 보브 소여가 응수하며, 가게로 나가서 가짜 서랍에 달린 자그마한 금빛 손잡이들을 몇 번이고 세게 잡아당겨 그 단언이 참임을 입증해 보였다."], ["‘Hardly anything real in the shop but the leeches, and they are second-hand.’", "\"이 가게에 진짜라곤 거머리밖에 없는데, 그것도 중고품이라오.\""]], [["‘I shouldn’t have thought it!’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"전혀 그런 줄 몰랐는데요!\""], ["Winkle, much surprised.", "윈클 씨가 몹시 놀라 외쳤다."]], [["‘I hope not,’ replied Bob Sawyer, ‘else where’s the use of appearances, eh?", "\"몰랐어야지요.\" 보브 소여가 대답했다."], ["But what will you take?", "\"안 그러면 겉모양을 꾸민 보람이 어디 있겠소, 안 그래요?"], ["Do as we do?", "한데 뭘 좀 드시겠소?"], ["That’s right.", "우리 하는 대로 하시겠소?"], ["Ben, my fine fellow, put your hand into the cupboard, and bring out the patent digester.’", "그게 좋지요. 벤, 이 멋진 친구야, 찬장에 손 넣어서 특허 소화제 좀 꺼내 주게.\""]], [["Mr. Benjamin Allen smiled his readiness, and produced from the closet at his elbow a black bottle half full of brandy.", "벤저민 앨런 씨는 기꺼이 그러겠다는 듯 미소 지으며, 팔꿈치 옆 벽장에서 브랜디가 반쯤 든 검은 병 하나를 꺼냈다."]], [["‘You don’t take water, of course?’ said Bob Sawyer.", "\"물은 안 타시겠죠, 물론?\" 보브 소여가 말했다."]], [["‘Thank you,’ replied Mr.", "\"고맙습니다만,\""], ["Winkle. ‘It’s rather early.", "윈클 씨가 대답했다."], ["I should like to qualify it, if you have no objection.’", "\"시간이 좀 이르니까요. 괜찮으시다면 좀 묽게 타고 싶습니다.\""]], [["‘None in the least, if you can reconcile it to your conscience,’ replied Bob Sawyer, tossing off, as he spoke, a glass of the liquor with great relish.", "\"양심에 거리낌만 없다면야 조금도 상관없소.\" 보브 소여가, 말하는 동안에도 그 술 한 잔을 아주 맛나게 쭉 들이켜며 대답했다."], ["‘Ben, the pipkin!’", "\"벤, 그 작은 냄비!\""]], [["Mr. Benjamin Allen drew forth, from the same hiding-place, a small brass pipkin, which Bob Sawyer observed he prided himself upon, particularly because it looked so business-like.", "벤저민 앨런 씨는 같은 은닉처에서 자그마한 놋쇠 냄비 하나를 꺼냈는데, 보브 소여는 그것이 특히나 사업장다워 보인다는 이유로 자랑스럽게 여긴다고 말했다."], ["The water in the professional pipkin having been made to boil, in course of time, by various little shovelfuls of coal, which Mr. Bob Sawyer took out of a practicable window-seat, labelled ‘Soda Water,’ Mr.", "보브 소여 씨가 '소다수'라고 표딱지를 붙인, 열리게 되어 있는 창가 의자에서 꺼낸 자그마한 석탄 몇 삽으로 그 직업용 냄비 속 물을 한참 끓여 내자, 윈클 씨는 자기 브랜디에 그것을 섞었다."], ["Winkle adulterated his brandy; and the conversation was becoming general, when it was interrupted by the entrance into the shop of a boy, in a sober gray livery and a gold- laced hat, with a small covered basket under his arm, whom Mr.", "그리고 대화가 두루 무르익어 가던 차에, 수수한 회색 제복에 금줄 두른 모자를 쓰고, 뚜껑 덮인 자그마한 바구니를 옆구리에 낀 한 소년이 가게로 들어서면서 대화가 끊겼다. 보브 소여 씨가 그 소년을 즉시 불러 세웠다."], ["Bob Sawyer immediately hailed with, ‘Tom, you vagabond, come here.’", "\"톰, 이 부랑아 녀석, 이리 와.\""]], [["The boy presented himself accordingly.", "소년이 그에 맞춰 앞으로 나섰다."]], [["‘You’ve been stopping to “over” all the posts in Bristol, you idle young scamp!’ said Mr.", "\"브리스틀의 기둥이란 기둥은 죄다 멈춰 서서 '뛰어넘기'를 하고 다녔지, 이 게으른 망나니 녀석!\""], ["Bob Sawyer.", "보브 소여 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘No, sir, I haven’t,’ replied the boy.", "\"아닙니다, 선생님, 안 그랬어요.\" 소년이 대답했다."]], [["‘You had better not!’ said Mr.", "\"안 그러는 게 좋을 거다!\""], ["Bob Sawyer, with a threatening aspect.", "보브 소여 씨가 위협하는 기색으로 말했다."], ["‘Who do you suppose will ever employ a professional man, when they see his boy playing at marbles in the gutter, or flying the garter in the horse-road?", "\"심부름하는 녀석이 도랑에서 구슬치기를 하거나 한길에서 가터놀이를 하는 꼴을 보고서, 그 어느 누가 의사 양반에게 일을 맡기겠느냐?"], ["Have you no feeling for your profession, you groveller?", "너는 네 직업에 대한 자긍심도 없느냐, 이 비굴한 녀석아?"], ["Did you leave all the medicine?’", "약은 다 전했냐?\""]], [["Yes, Sir.’", "\"네, 선생님.\""]], [["‘The powders for the child, at the large house with the new family, and the pills to be taken four times a day at the ill-tempered old gentleman’s with the gouty leg?’", "\"새 식구 들어온 큰 저택에 보내는 아이 가루약하고, 통풍 든 다리에 성질 고약한 노신사한테 하루 네 번 먹으라는 알약 말이다?\""]], [["‘Yes, sir.’", "\"네, 선생님.\""]], [["‘Then shut the door, and mind the shop.’", "\"그럼 문 닫고 가게나 지키고 있어라.\""]], [["‘Come,’ said Mr.", "\"이거 보세요,\""], ["Winkle, as the boy retired, ‘things are not quite so bad as you would have me believe, either.", "소년이 물러가자 윈클 씨가 말했다. \"내게 믿게 하려던 만큼 형편이 그리 나쁜 것도 아니잖아요."], ["There is some medicine to be sent out.’", "그래도 내보낼 약이 좀 있지 않습니까.\""]], [["Mr. Bob Sawyer peeped into the shop to see that no stranger was within hearing, and leaning forward to Mr. Winkle, said, in a low tone--", "보브 소여 씨는 엿듣고 있는 낯선 이가 없는지 가게 안을 슬쩍 살피고는, 윈클 씨 쪽으로 몸을 기울이며 나직한 목소리로 말했다—"]], [["‘He leaves it all at the wrong houses.’", "\"그 녀석은 그걸 죄다 엉뚱한 집에 두고 온다오.\""]], [["Mr. Winkle looked perplexed, and Bob Sawyer and his friend laughed.", "윈클 씨가 어리둥절한 표정을 짓자, 보브 소여와 그의 친구가 웃었다."]], [["‘Don’t you see?’ said Bob.", "\"모르겠소?\" 보브가 말했다."], ["‘He goes up to a house, rings the area bell, pokes a packet of medicine without a direction into the servant’s hand, and walks off.", "\"그 녀석이 어느 집에 가서 지하실 초인종을 울리고, 받는 사람 이름도 안 적힌 약 꾸러미를 하인 손에 쥐여 주고는 가 버린단 말이오. 하인은 그걸 식당으로 가져가지."], ["Servant takes it into the dining-parlour; master opens it, and reads the label: “Draught to be taken at bedtime--pills as before--lotion as usual--the powder.", "주인이 그걸 뜯어 표딱지를 읽어요. '취침 시 복용할 물약—알약은 종전대로—물약은 평소대로—가루약."], ["From Sawyer’s, late Nockemorf’s.", "소여, 전 녹케모프 약방에서."], ["Physicians’ prescriptions carefully prepared,” and all the rest of it.", "의사 처방전 정성껏 조제함', 그 밖에 어쩌고저쩌고 다 적혀 있지."], ["Shows it to his wife--she reads the label; it goes down to the servants- -they read the label.", "그걸 아내한테 보여 주면, 아내가 표딱지를 읽고, 그게 하인들한테까지 내려가서, 그들도 표딱지를 읽소. 이튿날 그 녀석이 와서 이러지."], ["Next day, boy calls: “Very sorry--his mistake-- immense business--great many parcels to deliver--Mr. Sawyer’s compliments--late Nockemorf.”", "'대단히 죄송합니다—저의 실수입니다—워낙 일이 많아서—배달할 꾸러미가 무척 많거든요—소여 씨가 안부 전하랍니다—전 녹케모프 약방요.'"], ["The name gets known, and that’s the thing, my boy, in the medical way.", "그렇게 이름이 알려지는 거요. 그게 바로 의술 쪽에서 통하는 비결이지, 이 친구야."], ["Bless your heart, old fellow, it’s better than all the advertising in the world.", "정말이지, 이 사람아, 그게 세상 어떤 광고보다도 낫소."], ["We have got one four-ounce bottle that’s been to half the houses in Bristol, and hasn’t done yet.’", "4온스짜리 병 하나는 브리스틀 집들의 절반은 다녀왔는데도 아직도 끝이 안 났다오.\""]], [["‘Dear me, I see,’ observed Mr.", "\"이런, 알겠어요.\" 윈클 씨가 말했다."], ["Winkle; ‘what an excellent plan!’", "\"정말 기막힌 방법이군요!\""]], [["‘Oh, Ben and I have hit upon a dozen such,’ replied Bob Sawyer, with great glee.", "\"오, 벤하고 나는 그런 수법을 열두 가지나 생각해 냈다오.\" 보브 소여가 신이 나서 대답했다."], ["‘The lamplighter has eighteenpence a week to pull the night- bell for ten minutes every time he comes round; and my boy always rushes into the church just before the psalms, when the people have got nothing to do but look about ‘em, and calls me out, with horror and dismay depicted on his countenance.", "\"가로등지기한테는 일주일에 1실링 6펜스를 주고, 순찰 돌 때마다 야간 초인종을 십 분씩 잡아당기게 하지. 그리고 우리 심부름 녀석은 늘 찬송가 부르기 직전에 교회로 뛰어 들어가는데, 사람들이 마침 두리번거리는 것 말고는 할 일이 없을 그때, 얼굴에 공포와 낭패를 잔뜩 그린 채로 나를 불러내는 거요."], ["“Bless my soul,”", "그러면 다들 이러지."], ["everybody says, “somebody taken suddenly ill!", "'아이고 저런, 누가 갑자기 병이 났나 보군!"], ["Sawyer, late Nockemorf, sent for.", "소여, 전 녹케모프 약방을 부르러 왔구먼."], ["What a business that young man has!”’", "저 젊은 양반은 일이 어찌나 많은지!'\""]], [["At the termination of this disclosure of some of the mysteries of medicine, Mr. Bob Sawyer and his friend, Ben Allen, threw themselves back in their respective chairs, and laughed boisterously.", "이렇게 의술의 비밀 몇 가지를 폭로한 끝에, 보브 소여 씨와 그의 친구 벤 앨런은 저마다 의자에 등을 기대고 떠들썩하게 웃어 댔다."], ["When they had enjoyed the joke to their heart’s content, the discourse changed to topics in which Mr. Winkle was more immediately interested.", "마음껏 그 농담을 즐기고 나자, 화제는 윈클 씨가 한결 직접적으로 관심을 가진 주제로 옮겨 갔다."]], [["We think we have hinted elsewhere, that Mr. Benjamin Allen had a way of becoming sentimental after brandy.", "벤저민 앨런 씨가 브랜디를 마신 뒤에는 감상적이 되는 버릇이 있다는 점은, 우리가 다른 데서 이미 넌지시 일러 둔 듯하다."], ["The case is not a peculiar one, as we ourself can testify, having, on a few occasions, had to deal with patients who have been afflicted in a similar manner. At this precise period of his existence, Mr.", "그런 경우는 별스러운 일이 아니니, 우리 자신도 몇 차례 그와 비슷한 증세에 시달리는 환자들을 다뤄 본 적이 있어 증언할 수 있다."], ["Benjamin Allen had perhaps a greater predisposition to maudlinism than he had ever known before; the cause of which malady was briefly this. He had been staying nearly three weeks with Mr.", "그의 생애에서 바로 이 무렵, 벤저민 앨런 씨는 일찍이 알았던 것보다도 어쩌면 더 심하게 감상에 빠지기 쉬운 상태였는데, 그 병의 원인은 간단히 이러했다."], ["Bob Sawyer; Mr. Bob Sawyer was not remarkable for temperance, nor was Mr.", "그는 보브 소여 씨와 거의 삼 주 가까이 함께 지내고 있었다."], ["Benjamin Allen for the ownership of a very strong head; the consequence was that, during the whole space of time just mentioned, Mr.", "보브 소여 씨는 절제로 이름난 사람이 아니었고, 벤저민 앨런 씨 역시 머리가 썩 튼튼한 편이 못 되었다."], ["Benjamin Allen had been wavering between intoxication partial, and intoxication complete.", "그 결과, 방금 말한 그 기간 내내 벤저민 앨런 씨는 절반쯤 취한 상태와 완전히 취한 상태 사이를 오락가락하고 있었던 것이다."]], [["‘My dear friend,’ said Mr. Ben Allen, taking advantage of Mr.", "\"이보게, 친구,\""], ["Bob Sawyer’s temporary absence behind the counter, whither he had retired to dispense some of the second-hand leeches, previously referred to; ‘my dear friend, I am very miserable.’", "하고 벤 앨런 씨가, 앞서 말한 그 중고 거머리를 좀 내주려고 계산대 뒤로 잠시 자리를 비운 보브 소여 씨의 부재를 틈타 말했다. \"이보게, 친구, 나는 무척 비참하다네.\""]], [["Mr. Winkle professed his heartfelt regret to hear it, and begged to know whether he could do anything to alleviate the sorrows of the suffering student.", "윈클 씨는 그 말을 듣고 진심으로 안됐다는 뜻을 표하며, 고통받는 이 학도의 슬픔을 덜어 줄 무슨 일이라도 자기가 할 수 있는지 알고 싶다고 청했다."]], [["‘Nothing, my dear boy, nothing,’ said Ben.", "\"아무것도 없네, 이 사람아, 아무것도.\""], ["‘You recollect Arabella, Winkle?", "벤이 말했다. \"아라벨라 기억하나, 윈클?"], ["My sister Arabella--a little girl, Winkle, with black eyes--when we were down at Wardle’s?", "내 누이동생 아라벨라 말일세—자그마한 처녀였지, 윈클, 검은 눈을 한—우리가 와들네 집에 내려가 있었을 때 말이야."], ["I don’t know whether you happened to notice her--a nice little girl, Winkle.", "자네가 그 애를 눈여겨봤는지는 모르겠네만—참 어여쁜 처녀였지, 윈클."], ["Perhaps my features may recall her countenance to your recollection?’", "어쩌면 내 얼굴 생김새가 그 애 얼굴을 자네 기억에 떠올려 줄지도 모르겠군?\""]], [["Mr. Winkle required nothing to recall the charming Arabella to his mind; and it was rather fortunate he did not, for the features of her brother Benjamin would unquestionably have proved but an indifferent refresher to his memory.", "윈클 씨는 그 사랑스러운 아라벨라를 마음속에 떠올리는 데 아무것도 필요치 않았다."], ["He answered, with as much calmness as he could assume, that he perfectly remembered the young lady referred to, and sincerely trusted she was in good health.", "그게 도리어 다행이었으니, 그녀의 오라비 벤저민의 얼굴 생김새는 분명 그의 기억을 되살려 주는 데 영 신통찮은 자극제였을 것이기 때문이다. 그는 애써 침착함을 가장하여, 말씀하신 그 젊은 숙녀를 똑똑히 기억하며, 그녀가 부디 건강하기를 진심으로 바란다고 대답했다."]], [["‘Our friend Bob is a delightful fellow, Winkle,’ was the only reply of Mr.", "\"우리 친구 보브는 유쾌한 친구지, 윈클.\""], ["Ben Allen.", "벤 앨런 씨의 대답이라곤 이것뿐이었다."]], [["‘Very,’ said Mr.", "\"아주 그렇죠.\""], ["Winkle, not much relishing this close connection of the two names.", "윈클 씨가, 두 이름이 이렇게 가깝게 엮이는 것이 그리 달갑지 않은 채로 말했다."]], [["‘I designed ‘em for each other; they were made for each other, sent into the world for each other, born for each other, Winkle,’ said Mr.", "\"나는 그 둘을 짝지어 줄 작정이었네. 그 둘은 서로를 위해 만들어졌고, 서로를 위해 세상에 보내졌고, 서로를 위해 태어난 사이라고, 윈클.\""], ["Ben Allen, setting down his glass with emphasis.", "벤 앨런 씨가 잔을 힘주어 내려놓으며 말했다."], ["‘There’s a special destiny in the matter, my dear sir; there’s only five years’ difference between ‘em, and both their birthdays are in August.’", "\"이건 특별한 운명이 깃든 일이라네, 이 사람아. 둘은 나이 차가 다섯 살밖에 안 나고, 둘 다 생일이 팔월이거든.\""]], [["Mr. Winkle was too anxious to hear what was to follow to express much wonderment at this extraordinary coincidence, marvellous as it was; so Mr.", "윈클 씨는 그다음에 무슨 말이 이어질지 너무도 듣고 싶은 나머지, 그 비범한 우연이 아무리 놀랍다 한들 별로 놀라움을 드러내지 못했다."], ["Ben Allen, after a tear or two, went on to say that, notwithstanding all his esteem and respect and veneration for his friend, Arabella had unaccountably and undutifully evinced the most determined antipathy to his person.", "그래서 벤 앨런 씨는 눈물 한두 방울을 흘린 뒤, 자기가 친구에게 품은 그 모든 존경과 경의와 흠모에도 불구하고, 아라벨라가 알 수 없게도, 또 도리에 어긋나게도, 그의 인품에 더없이 단호한 반감을 드러냈노라고 말을 이었다."]], [["‘And I think,’ said Mr.", "\"그래서 내 생각엔,\""], ["Ben Allen, in conclusion.", "벤 앨런 씨가 결론짓듯 말했다."], ["‘I think there’s a prior attachment.’", "\"내 생각엔 이미 마음 둔 사람이 따로 있는 것 같네.\""]], [["‘Have you any idea who the object of it might be?’ asked Mr.", "\"그 상대가 누구일지 짐작 가는 데가 있습니까?\""], ["Winkle, with great trepidation.", "윈클 씨가 몹시 떨면서 물었다."]], [["Mr. Ben Allen seized the poker, flourished it in a warlike manner above his head, inflicted a savage blow on an imaginary skull, and wound up by saying, in a very expressive manner, that he only wished he could guess; that was all.", "벤 앨런 씨는 부지깽이를 거머쥐고는, 그것을 머리 위로 호전적으로 휘두르며, 상상 속 두개골에 사나운 일격을 가하더니, 매우 의미심장한 태도로 마무리하기를, 그저 그게 누군지 짐작이라도 갈 수 있으면 좋으련만, 그뿐이라고 했다."]], [["‘I’d show him what I thought of him,’ said Mr.", "\"내가 그놈을 어떻게 생각하는지 똑똑히 보여 줄 텐데.\""], ["Ben Allen.", "벤 앨런 씨가 말했다."], ["And round went the poker again, more fiercely than before.", "그러면서 부지깽이가 다시 한번, 아까보다도 더 사납게 휘둘러졌다."]], [["All this was, of course, very soothing to the feelings of Mr.", "이 모든 것이 물론 윈클 씨의 심정에는 무척이나 위안이 되었으니, 그는 몇 분 동안 잠자코 있었다."], ["Winkle, who remained silent for a few minutes; but at length mustered up resolution to inquire whether Miss Allen was in Kent.", "그러나 마침내 결심을 다잡고, 앨런 양이 켄트에 있느냐고 물었다."]], [["‘No, no,’ said Mr.", "\"아니, 아닐세.\" 벤 앨런 씨가 부지깽이를 옆으로 치워 놓고 자못 교활한 표정을 지으며 말했다."], ["Ben Allen, laying aside the poker, and looking very cunning; ‘I didn’t think Wardle’s exactly the place for a headstrong girl; so, as I am her natural protector and guardian, our parents being dead, I have brought her down into this part of the country to spend a few months at an old aunt’s, in a nice, dull, close place.", "\"고집 센 처녀에겐 와들네 집이 꼭 알맞은 데라곤 생각지 않았네. 그래서, 부모님이 돌아가신 마당에 내가 그 애의 마땅한 보호자요 후견인인지라, 그 애를 이 지방으로 데려와서, 어느 늙은 고모님 댁의, 아주 따분하고 답답한 곳에서 몇 달 지내게 하고 있다네."], ["I think that will cure her, my boy.", "그러면 그 애가 나으리라 보네, 이 사람아."], ["If it doesn’t, I’ll take her abroad for a little while, and see what that’ll do.’", "그래도 안 되면, 잠시 외국에 데려가서 그게 어떤지 두고 볼 참이야.\""]], [["‘Oh, the aunt’s is in Bristol, is it?’ faltered Mr.", "\"오, 그 고모님 댁이 브리스틀에 있는 거군요?\""], ["Winkle.", "윈클 씨가 더듬거렸다."]], [["‘No, no, not in Bristol,’ replied Mr.", "\"아니, 아니, 브리스틀은 아닐세.\""], ["Ben Allen, jerking his thumb over his right shoulder; ‘over that way--down there.", "벤 앨런 씨가 오른쪽 어깨 너머로 엄지손가락을 홱 젖히며 대답했다. \"저쪽이지—저 아래 말이야."], ["But, hush, here’s Bob.", "그런데 쉿, 보브가 오네."], ["Not a word, my dear friend, not a word.’", "한마디도 말게, 이 사람아, 한마디도.\""]], [["Short as this conversation was, it roused in Mr. Winkle the highest degree of excitement and anxiety.", "이 대화는 짧았지만, 윈클 씨의 마음속에 더없이 높은 정도의 흥분과 불안을 불러일으켰다."], ["The suspected prior attachment rankled in his heart.", "이미 마음 둔 사람이 있으리라는 그 의심이 그의 가슴속에 사무쳤다."], ["Could he be the object of it?", "혹 그 상대가 자기일 수 있을까?"], ["Could it be for him that the fair Arabella had looked scornfully on the sprightly Bob Sawyer, or had he a successful rival?", "어여쁜 아라벨라가 발랄한 보브 소여를 멸시하듯 바라본 것이 혹 자기 때문이었을까, 아니면 자기에게 성공한 연적이 따로 있는 것일까? 그는 어떤 대가를 치르든 그녀를 만나 보기로 결심했다."], ["He determined to see her, cost what it might; but here an insurmountable objection presented itself, for whether the explanatory ‘over that way,’ and ‘down there,’ of Mr. Ben Allen, meant three miles off, or thirty, or three hundred, he could in no wise guess.", "그러나 여기서 넘을 수 없는 난관이 하나 나타났으니, 벤 앨런 씨가 설명조로 한 그 '저쪽'이며 '저 아래'라는 말이 삼 마일을 뜻하는지, 삼십 마일을 뜻하는지, 아니면 삼백 마일을 뜻하는지 그로서는 도무지 짐작할 길이 없었던 것이다."]], [["But he had no opportunity of pondering over his love just then, for Bob Sawyer’s return was the immediate precursor of the arrival of a meat-pie from the baker’s, of which that gentleman insisted on his staying to partake.", "하지만 당장은 자기 연정을 곰곰이 곱씹을 겨를이 없었으니, 보브 소여가 돌아온 직후 곧바로 빵집에서 고기 파이가 도착했고, 그 양반이 함께 남아 들자고 우긴 까닭이다."], ["The cloth was laid by an occasional charwoman, who officiated in the capacity of Mr. Bob Sawyer’s housekeeper; and a third knife and fork having been borrowed from the mother of the boy in the gray livery (for Mr.", "보브 소여 씨의 가정부 노릇을 하는 한 임시 잡역부 여자가 식탁보를 깔았고, 회색 제복을 입은 그 소년의 어머니한테서 세 번째 칼과 포크를 빌려 온 뒤(소여 씨의 살림살이는 아직 옹색한 규모로 꾸려지고 있었으므로), 그들은 자리에 앉아 식사를 했다."], ["Sawyer’s domestic arrangements were as yet conducted on a limited scale), they sat down to dinner; the beer being served up, as Mr.", "맥주는, 소여 씨의 말마따나 '제 본래의 백랍 잔에 담겨'"], ["Sawyer remarked, ‘in its native pewter.’", "나왔다."]], [["After dinner, Mr. Bob Sawyer ordered in the largest mortar in the shop, and proceeded to brew a reeking jorum of rum-punch therein, stirring up and amalgamating the materials with a pestle in a very creditable and apothecary-like manner.", "식사 후, 보브 소여 씨는 가게에서 제일 큰 약절구를 들여오게 하더니, 그 안에서 김이 모락모락 나는 럼 펀치 한 사발을 빚기 시작했는데, 약공이 같은 솜씨로 자못 그럴듯하게 재료를 휘저어 섞어 냈다."], ["Mr. Sawyer, being a bachelor, had only one tumbler in the house, which was assigned to Mr.", "소여 씨는 독신이라 집에 텀블러가 하나밖에 없었는데, 그것은 손님에 대한 예우로 윈클 씨에게 배정되었고, 벤 앨런 씨에게는 좁은 끝에 코르크를 박은 깔때기가 주어졌으며, 보브 소여는 약제사들이 처방을 조제할 때 액체 약을 따라 재는 데 흔히 쓰는, 입구가 넓고 갖가지 알 수 없는 부호들이 새겨진 그 수정 그릇 가운데 하나로 만족했다."], ["Winkle as a compliment to the visitor, Mr. Ben Allen being accommodated with a funnel with a cork in the narrow end, and Bob Sawyer contented himself with one of those wide-lipped crystal vessels inscribed with a variety of cabalistic characters, in which chemists are wont to measure out their liquid drugs in compounding prescriptions.", "이런 준비가 갖추어지자, 펀치를 맛보아 훌륭하다는 평을 내렸다."], ["These preliminaries adjusted, the punch was tasted, and pronounced excellent; and it having been arranged that Bob Sawyer and Ben Allen should be considered at liberty to fill twice to Mr. Winkle’s once, they started fair, with great satisfaction and good-fellowship.", "그리고 보브 소여와 벤 앨런은 윈클 씨가 한 잔 채울 동안 두 잔씩 채워도 무방하다고 정해 두고서, 다 같이 큰 만족과 우애 속에 공평하게 시작했다."]], [["There was no singing, because Mr. Bob Sawyer said it wouldn’t look professional; but to make amends for this deprivation there was so much talking and laughing that it might have been heard, and very likely was, at the end of the street.", "노래는 없었으니, 보브 소여 씨가 그건 의사다워 보이지 않는다고 했기 때문이다."], ["Which conversation materially lightened the hours and improved the mind of Mr.", "그러나 그 박탈을 벌충하느라 떠들고 웃는 소리가 어찌나 컸던지, 거리 끝까지 들렸을 만했고, 십중팔구 실제로도 들렸을 것이다."], ["Bob Sawyer’s boy, who, instead of devoting the evening to his ordinary occupation of writing his name on the counter, and rubbing it out again, peeped through the glass door, and thus listened and looked on at the same time.", "그 대화는 보브 소여 씨네 심부름 소년의 시간을 한결 가볍게 해 주었고 그의 머리도 깨우쳐 주었으니, 그 소년은 평소 하던 대로 계산대에 제 이름을 썼다 다시 지우는 일로 저녁을 보내는 대신, 유리문 너머로 엿보며 듣는 동시에 구경까지 했던 것이다."]], [["The mirth of Mr. Bob Sawyer was rapidly ripening into the furious, Mr. Ben Allen was fast relapsing into the sentimental, and the punch had well-nigh disappeared altogether, when the boy hastily running in, announced that a young woman had just come over, to say that Sawyer late Nockemorf was wanted directly, a couple of streets off. This broke up the party. Mr. Bob Sawyer, understanding the message, after some twenty repetitions, tied a wet cloth round his head to sober himself, and, having partially succeeded, put on his green spectacles and issued forth. Resisting all entreaties to stay till he came back, and finding it quite impossible to engage Mr. Ben Allen in any intelligible conversation on the subject nearest his heart, or indeed on any other, Mr. Winkle took his departure, and returned to the Bush.", "보브 소여 씨의 흥취가 빠르게 광적으로 무르익어 가고, 벤 앨런 씨는 다시금 감상에 빠져들며, 펀치는 거의 다 동나 갈 무렵, 심부름 소년이 황급히 뛰어 들어와 알리기를, 한 젊은 여자가 방금 건너와서 두 블록 떨어진 곳에 소여, 전 녹케모프 약방을 당장 찾는다고 전했다는 것이었다. 이로써 모임이 파했다. 보브 소여 씨는 그 전갈을 스무 번쯤 되풀이해 듣고서야 알아듣고는, 정신을 차리려고 젖은 헝겊을 머리에 동여맸고, 어느 정도 효험을 본 뒤 초록색 안경을 쓰고 나섰다. 돌아올 때까지 기다리라는 온갖 간청을 뿌리치고, 또 벤 앨런 씨를 붙들고 자기 마음에 가장 사무친 화제로든 아니 실은 그 어떤 화제로든 알아들을 만한 대화를 나누기란 도무지 불가능함을 알아챈 윈클 씨는, 자리를 떠나 부시 여관으로 돌아왔다."]], [["The anxiety of his mind, and the numerous meditations which Arabella had awakened, prevented his share of the mortar of punch producing that effect upon him which it would have had under other circumstances.", "마음속 불안과 아라벨라가 불러일으킨 숱한 상념 탓에, 그가 마신 그 약절구 펀치는 다른 상황에서라면 미쳤을 효과를 그에게 미치지 못했다."], ["So, after taking a glass of soda-water and brandy at the bar, he turned into the coffee-room, dispirited rather than elevated by the occurrences of the evening.", "그래서 그는 바에서 소다수에 브랜디를 탄 술 한 잔을 마신 뒤, 그날 저녁의 일들로 고양되기는커녕 도리어 풀이 죽은 채 커피실로 들어갔다."]], [["Sitting in front of the fire, with his back towards him, was a tallish gentleman in a greatcoat: the only other occupant of the room.", "벽난로 앞에, 그에게 등을 돌리고 앉아 있는 것은, 두꺼운 외투를 걸친 키가 큰 편인 한 신사였으니, 그 방에 있는 다른 사람은 그뿐이었다."], ["It was rather a cool evening for the season of the year, and the gentleman drew his chair aside to afford the new-comer a sight of the fire.", "그해 그 계절치고는 꽤 쌀쌀한 저녁이라, 그 신사는 새로 들어온 사람에게 불을 쬘 수 있도록 의자를 옆으로 비켜 놓았다."], ["What were Mr. Winkle’s feelings when, in doing so, he disclosed to view the face and figure of the vindictive and sanguinary Dowler!", "그런데 그렇게 하면서 그 복수심에 불타고 피에 굶주린 다울러의 얼굴과 모습이 드러났을 때, 윈클 씨의 심정이 어떠했겠는가!"]], [["Mr. Winkle’s first impulse was to give a violent pull at the nearest bell-handle, but that unfortunately happened to be immediately behind Mr.", "윈클 씨의 첫 충동은 가장 가까운 초인종 손잡이를 힘껏 잡아당기는 것이었으나, 공교롭게도 그것은 마침 다울러 씨의 머리 바로 뒤에 있었다."], ["Dowler’s head. He had made one step towards it, before he checked himself.", "그는 그쪽으로 한 발짝 내디뎠다가 스스로를 제지했다."], ["As he did so, Mr. Dowler very hastily drew back.", "그가 그러는 사이, 다울러 씨가 몹시 황급히 뒤로 물러섰다."]], [["‘Mr. Winkle, Sir.", "\"윈클 씨."], ["Be calm.", "진정하시오."], ["Don’t strike me.", "나를 치지 마시오."], ["I won’t bear it.", "나는 참지 않겠소."], ["A blow!", "주먹질이라니!"], ["Never!’ said Mr.", "절대 안 되오!\""], ["Dowler, looking meeker than Mr. Winkle had expected in a gentleman of his ferocity.", "다울러 씨가, 그 사나운 성격의 신사에게서 윈클 씨가 예상했던 것보다 한결 온순한 얼굴로 말했다."]], [["‘A blow, Sir?’ stammered Mr.", "\"주먹질이라뇨, 선생님?\""], ["Winkle.", "윈클 씨가 더듬거렸다."]], [["‘A blow, Sir,’ replied Dowler.", "\"주먹질 말이오, 선생.\" 다울러가 대답했다."], ["‘Compose your feelings.", "\"마음을 가라앉히시오. 앉으시오."], ["Sit down. Hear me.’", "내 말을 들어 보시오.\""]], [["‘Sir,’ said Mr.", "\"선생님,\""], ["Winkle, trembling from head to foot, ‘before I consent to sit down beside, or opposite you, without the presence of a waiter, I must be secured by some further understanding.", "윈클 씨가 머리끝부터 발끝까지 떨면서 말했다. \"급사도 없는 자리에서 당신 곁에, 혹은 당신 맞은편에 앉기로 응하기 전에, 나는 무언가 더 분명한 다짐으로 안심을 얻어야겠소."], ["You used a threat against me last night, Sir, a dreadful threat, Sir.’ Here Mr.", "당신은 어젯밤 나에게 위협을 가했소, 선생, 끔찍한 위협을 말이오.\""], ["Winkle turned very pale indeed, and stopped short.", "여기서 윈클 씨는 정말이지 새파랗게 질려 말을 뚝 끊었다."]], [["‘I did,’ said Dowler, with a countenance almost as white as Mr.", "\"그랬소.\" 다울러가, 윈클 씨 못지않게 새하얀 얼굴로 말했다."], ["Winkle’s. ‘Circumstances were suspicious.", "\"정황이 수상했소."], ["They have been explained.", "그게 해명되었소."], ["I respect your bravery.", "나는 당신의 용기를 존경하오."], ["Your feeling is upright.", "당신의 마음은 올곧소."], ["Conscious innocence.", "떳떳한 결백 말이오."], ["There’s my hand.", "자, 내 손이오."], ["Grasp it.’", "잡으시오.\""]], [["‘Really, Sir,’ said Mr.", "\"실은, 선생님,\""], ["Winkle, hesitating whether to give his hand or not, and almost fearing that it was demanded in order that he might be taken at an advantage, ‘really, Sir, I--’", "윈클 씨가 손을 내밀어야 할지 말아야 할지 망설이며, 혹 자기가 허를 찔리게 하려고 손을 청하는 게 아닐까 거의 두려워하며 말했다. \"실은, 선생님, 저는—\""]], [["‘I know what you mean,’ interposed Dowler.", "\"무슨 말인지 알겠소.\" 다울러가 가로막았다."], ["‘You feel aggrieved.", "\"억울하게 느끼는 거지."], ["Very natural.", "지극히 당연하오."], ["So should I.", "나라도 그랬을 거요."], ["I was wrong.", "내가 잘못했소."], ["I beg your pardon.", "사과하오. 우리 친구가 됩시다."], ["Be friendly.", "나를 용서하시오.\""], ["Forgive me.’ With this, Dowler fairly forced his hand upon Mr. Winkle, and shaking it with the utmost vehemence, declared he was a fellow of extreme spirit, and he had a higher opinion of him than ever.", "이 말과 함께, 다울러는 거의 강제로 자기 손을 윈클 씨의 손에 쥐여 주고는, 더없이 격렬하게 그것을 흔들어 대며, 윈클 씨야말로 더없이 기개 있는 사람이며 자기는 어느 때보다도 그를 높이 평가하게 되었노라고 선언했다."]], [["‘Now,’ said Dowler, ‘sit down.", "\"자,\" 다울러가 말했다."], ["Relate it all.", "\"앉으시오. 다 이야기해 보시오."], ["How did you find me?", "어떻게 나를 찾았소?"], ["When did you follow?", "언제부터 뒤를 따라왔소?"], ["Be frank.", "솔직히 말해 보시오."], ["Tell me.’", "내게 들려주시오.\""]], [["‘It’s quite accidental,’ replied Mr.", "\"순전히 우연입니다.\""], ["Winkle, greatly perplexed by the curious and unexpected nature of the interview.", "윈클 씨가, 이 만남의 야릇하고도 뜻밖인 성격에 몹시 당혹스러워하며 대답했다."], ["‘Quite.’", "\"순전히요.\""]], [["‘Glad of it,’ said Dowler.", "\"다행이오.\" 다울러가 말했다."], ["‘I woke this morning. I had forgotten my threat.", "\"오늘 아침 잠에서 깼더니, 내가 한 위협을 까맣게 잊고 있었소."], ["I laughed at the accident.", "그 일을 떠올리며 웃었지."], ["I felt friendly.", "친근한 마음이 들었소."], ["I said so.’", "그렇게 말했다오.\""]], [["‘To whom?’ inquired Mr.", "\"누구한테요?\""], ["Winkle.", "윈클 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘To Mrs. Dowler.", "\"다울러 부인한테."], ["“You made a vow,”", "'당신, 맹세를 했잖아요'"], ["said she. “I did,”", "하고 그녀가 말합디다."], ["said I.", "'그랬지'"], ["“It was a rash one,”", "하고 내가 말했소. '그건 경솔한 맹세였어요'"], ["said she. “It was,”", "하고 그녀가 말했소."], ["said I.", "'그랬지'"], ["“I’ll apologise.", "하고 내가 말했소. '내가 사과하리다."], ["Where is he?”’", "그 사람 어디 있소?'\""]], [["‘Who?’ inquired Mr.", "\"누구 말이오?\""], ["Winkle.", "윈클 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘You,’ replied Dowler.", "\"당신 말이오.\" 다울러가 대답했다."], ["‘I went downstairs.", "\"나는 아래층으로 내려갔소."], ["You were not to be found.", "당신은 어디서도 찾을 수 없었지."], ["Pickwick looked gloomy.", "픽윅은 침울한 표정이었소."], ["Shook his head. Hoped no violence would be committed.", "고개를 절레절레 흔들면서, 무슨 폭력 사태가 벌어지지 않기를 바란다고 합디다."], ["I saw it all.", "나는 전부 알아챘소."], ["You felt yourself insulted.", "당신은 모욕을 당했다고 느꼈던 거요."], ["You had gone, for a friend perhaps.", "친구를 데리러, 어쩌면 갔겠지."], ["Possibly for pistols.", "어쩌면 권총을 구하러 갔는지도."], ["“High spirit,”", "'대단한 기개야'"], ["said I.", "하고 내가 말했소."], ["“I admire him.”’", "'나는 그를 존경하오' 하고.\""]], [["Mr. Winkle coughed, and beginning to see how the land lay, assumed a look of importance.", "윈클 씨는 헛기침을 하더니, 사정이 어떻게 돌아가는지 알아채기 시작하며, 거드름 피우는 표정을 지었다."]], [["‘I left a note for you,’ resumed Dowler.", "\"나는 당신 앞으로 쪽지를 남겼소.\" 다울러가 말을 이었다."], ["‘I said I was sorry.", "\"미안하다고 적었지."], ["So I was.", "정말 그랬소."], ["Pressing business called me here.", "급한 용무가 있어 나는 이리로 왔던 거요."], ["You were not satisfied.", "당신은 흡족하지 않았던 거고."], ["You followed.", "그래서 뒤따라왔지."], ["You required a verbal explanation.", "직접 말로 듣는 해명을 원했던 거요."], ["You were right.", "그건 옳았소."], ["It’s all over now.", "이제 다 끝났소."], ["My business is finished.", "내 용무는 마무리됐소."], ["I go back to-morrow.", "나는 내일 돌아가오."], ["Join me.’", "같이 갑시다.\""]], [["As Dowler progressed in his explanation, Mr. Winkle’s countenance grew more and more dignified.", "다울러가 해명을 이어 갈수록, 윈클 씨의 표정은 점점 더 위엄을 띠어 갔다."], ["The mysterious nature of the commencement of their conversation was explained; Mr.", "두 사람 대화가 처음에 어찌하여 그토록 알쏭달쏭하게 시작되었는지가 이제 밝혀졌다."], ["Dowler had as great an objection to duelling as himself; in short, this blustering and awful personage was one of the most egregious cowards in existence, and interpreting Mr.", "다울러 씨도 그 자신 못지않게 결투를 질색했던 것이다."], ["Winkle’s absence through the medium of his own fears, had taken the same step as himself, and prudently retired until all excitement of feeling should have subsided.", "요컨대 이 허세 가득하고 무시무시한 인물은 세상에 둘도 없는 지독한 겁쟁이 가운데 하나여서, 윈클 씨의 부재를 제 두려움이라는 잣대로 해석한 나머지, 자기와 똑같은 조치를 취해, 감정의 흥분이 죄다 가라앉을 때까지 신중하게 몸을 피해 있었던 것이다."]], [["As the real state of the case dawned upon Mr. Winkle’s mind, he looked very terrible, and said he was perfectly satisfied; but at the same time, said so with an air that left Mr.", "사태의 진상이 윈클 씨의 머릿속에 차츰 밝아 오자, 그는 자못 무시무시한 표정을 지으며 자기는 더없이 흡족하다고 말했다."], ["Dowler no alternative but to infer that if he had not been, something most horrible and destructive must inevitably have occurred.", "그러나 동시에, 그렇게 말하는 그의 태도는, 만일 자기가 흡족하지 않았더라면 무언가 더없이 끔찍하고 파괴적인 일이 틀림없이 벌어졌으리라고 다울러 씨가 미루어 짐작하지 않을 수 없게 하는 것이었다."], ["Mr. Dowler appeared to be impressed with a becoming sense of Mr.", "다울러 씨는 윈클 씨의 도량과 너그러움에 걸맞은 감명을 받은 듯했다."], ["Winkle’s magnanimity and condescension; and the two belligerents parted for the night, with many protestations of eternal friendship.", "그리하여 두 교전자는 영원한 우정을 거듭 다짐하며 그날 밤 헤어졌다."]], [["About half-past twelve o’clock, when Mr.", "열두 시 반쯤, 윈클 씨가 첫잠의 더없는 호사에 이십 분쯤 흠뻑 빠져 있을 때, 그는 자기 방문을 쾅쾅 두드리는 요란한 소리에 별안간 깨어났다."], ["Winkle had been revelling some twenty minutes in the full luxury of his first sleep, he was suddenly awakened by a loud knocking at his chamber door, which, being repeated with increased vehemence, caused him to start up in bed, and inquire who was there, and what the matter was.", "그 소리가 한층 더 격렬하게 되풀이되자, 그는 침대에서 벌떡 일어나 거기 누구냐고, 무슨 일이냐고 물었다."]], [["‘Please, Sir, here’s a young man which says he must see you directly,’ responded the voice of the chambermaid.", "\"저, 선생님, 여기 젊은 사람이 와서 당장 선생님을 뵈어야 한다는데요.\" 객실 하녀의 목소리가 대답했다."]], [["‘A young man!’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"젊은 사람이라고!\""], ["Winkle.", "윈클 씨가 외쳤다."]], [["‘No mistake about that ‘ere, Sir,’ replied another voice through the keyhole; ‘and if that wery same interestin’ young creetur ain’t let in vithout delay, it’s wery possible as his legs vill enter afore his countenance.’ The young man gave a gentle kick at one of the lower panels of the door, after he had given utterance to this hint, as if to add force and point to the remark.", "\"그 점에 대해선 추호도 틀림없습니다, 나리.\" 또 다른 목소리가 열쇠 구멍을 통해 대답했다. \"그리고 만약 바로 그 흥미로운 젊은 양반을 지체 없이 들이지 않으시면, 그 양반 얼굴보다 다리가 먼저 들어올지도 모르는 일이고말굽쇼.\" 젊은 사람은 이 암시를 입 밖에 내고 나서, 그 말에 무게와 요점을 더하기라도 하려는 듯, 문 아래쪽 널빤지 하나를 슬쩍 걷어찼다."]], [["‘Is that you, Sam?’ inquired Mr.", "\"자네인가, 샘?\""], ["Winkle, springing out of bed.", "윈클 씨가 침대에서 펄쩍 뛰어 일어나며 물었다."]], [["‘Quite unpossible to identify any gen’l’m’n vith any degree o’ mental satisfaction, vithout lookin’ at him, Sir,’ replied the voice dogmatically.", "\"어떤 신사분이든 직접 쳐다보지 않고서야 마음에 흡족할 만큼 알아본다는 건 도무지 불가능한 노릇입죠, 나리.\" 그 목소리가 단정적으로 대답했다."]], [["Mr. Winkle, not much doubting who the young man was, unlocked the door; which he had no sooner done than Mr.", "그 젊은 사람이 누군지 의심할 여지가 별로 없었던 윈클 씨는 문을 열었다."], ["Samuel Weller entered with great precipitation, and carefully relocking it on the inside, deliberately put the key in his waistcoat pocket; and, after surveying Mr.", "그가 문을 열기가 무섭게 새뮤얼 웰러 씨가 몹시 황급히 들어와서는, 안쪽에서 도로 정성껏 문을 잠그더니, 천연덕스럽게 열쇠를 조끼 주머니에 넣었다."], ["Winkle from head to foot, said--", "그러고는 윈클 씨를 머리끝부터 발끝까지 훑어본 뒤 말했다—"]], [["‘You’re a wery humorous young gen’l’m’n, you air, Sir!’", "\"나리는 참 익살맞은 젊은 신사분이십니다그려, 그렇고말굽쇼, 나리!\""]], [["‘What do you mean by this conduct, Sam?’ inquired Mr.", "\"이게 무슨 행동인가, 샘?\""], ["Winkle indignantly.", "윈클 씨가 분개하여 물었다."], ["‘Get out, sir, this instant.", "\"당장 나가게, 이 사람아."], ["What do you mean, Sir?’", "무슨 뜻인가, 이 사람아?\""]], [["‘What do I mean,’ retorted Sam; ‘come, Sir, this is rayther too rich, as the young lady said when she remonstrated with the pastry-cook, arter he’d sold her a pork pie as had got nothin’ but fat inside.", "\"무슨 뜻이냐굽쇼,\" 샘이 응수했다."], ["What do I mean!", "\"이거 보십쇼, 나리, 이건 좀 너무 기름진뎁쇼, 어느 젊은 아가씨가 빵집 주인한테 따지면서 한 말마따나요, 그 양반이 속에 비곗덩어리밖에 안 든 돼지고기 파이를 팔아먹고 나서 말입죠."], ["Well, that ain’t a bad ‘un, that ain’t.’", "무슨 뜻이냐고요! 거 참, 그것도 제법 그럴듯한 농담입니다요, 그렇고말굽쇼.\""]], [["‘Unlock that door, and leave this room immediately, Sir,’ said Mr.", "\"그 문을 열고 당장 이 방에서 나가게, 이 사람아.\""], ["Winkle.", "윈클 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘I shall leave this here room, sir, just precisely at the wery same moment as you leaves it,’ responded Sam, speaking in a forcible manner, and seating himself with perfect gravity.", "\"제가 이 방을 나가는 건요, 나리, 딱 나리께서 나가시는 바로 그 순간에 나갈 겁니다요.\" 샘이 단호한 어조로 말하며, 더없이 진지하게 자리에 앉았다."], ["‘If I find it necessary to carry you away, pick-a-back, o’ course I shall leave it the least bit o’ time possible afore you; but allow me to express a hope as you won’t reduce me to extremities; in saying wich, I merely quote wot the nobleman said to the fractious pennywinkle, ven he vouldn’t come out of his shell by means of a pin, and he conseqvently began to be afeered that he should be obliged to crack him in the parlour door.’ At the end of this address, which was unusually lengthy for him, Mr.", "\"만약 나리를 들쳐 업고 모셔 가야 할 형편이 되면야, 물론 나리보다 아주 조금 먼저 나가게 되겠습죠. 하지만 부디 저를 극단으로 몰아가지 않으시길 바랍니다요."], ["Weller planted his hands on his knees, and looked full in Mr.", "이렇게 말씀드리는 건, 그 귀족 양반이 성질 고약한 경단고둥한테 한 말을 그냥 인용한 것뿐입니다요."], ["Winkle’s face, with an expression of countenance which showed that he had not the remotest intention of being trifled with.", "핀으로 쑤셔도 껍데기에서 안 나오니까, 결국 응접실 문짝에 끼워 깨뜨려야 하나 보다 걱정하기 시작했을 때 말입죠.\" 그로서는 유난히 긴 이 연설을 마치고서, 웰러 씨는 두 손을 무릎에 짚고, 자기가 만만하게 놀아날 생각이 추호도 없음을 똑똑히 드러내는 표정으로 윈클 씨의 얼굴을 정면으로 마주 보았다."]], [["‘You’re a amiably-disposed young man, Sir, I don’t think,’ resumed Mr.", "\"나리는 참으로 상냥한 마음씨를 지닌 젊은 분이올시다, 그것도 못 믿겠습니다만,\""], ["Weller, in a tone of moral reproof, ‘to go inwolving our precious governor in all sorts o’ fanteegs, wen he’s made up his mind to go through everythink for principle.", "웰러 씨가 도덕적인 책망조로 말을 이었다. \"우리 귀하신 주인 어른이 신념을 위해 무슨 일이든 다 감당하기로 마음을 굳히셨는데, 그분을 온갖 골칫거리에 끌어들이시다니요."], ["You’re far worse nor Dodson, Sir; and as for Fogg, I consider him a born angel to you!’ Mr.", "나리는 도드슨보다도 한참 더 못된 분이십니다요, 나리."], ["Weller having accompanied this last sentiment with an emphatic slap on each knee, folded his arms with a look of great disgust, and threw himself back in his chair, as if awaiting the criminal’s defence.", "그리고 포그로 말할 것 같으면, 저는 그자가 나리에 비하면 타고난 천사라고 봅니다요!\" 웰러 씨는 이 마지막 소회에 양 무릎을 철썩 내려치며 힘을 실은 뒤, 몹시 역겨운 듯한 표정으로 팔짱을 끼고는, 죄인의 변론을 기다리기라도 하듯 의자에 등을 기댔다."]], [["‘My good fellow,’ said Mr.", "\"여보게, 친구,\""], ["Winkle, extending his hand--his teeth chattering all the time he spoke, for he had been standing, during the whole of Mr.", "하고 윈클 씨가 손을 내밀며 말했다—웰러 씨의 일장 훈계 내내 그는 잠옷 차림으로 서 있었던 터라, 말하는 동안 줄곧 이가 딱딱 부딪쳤다—\"여보게, 친구, 나는 자네가 내 훌륭한 친구에게 품은 충정을 존경하네."], ["Weller’s lecture, in his night-gear--‘my good fellow, I respect your attachment to my excellent friend, and I am very sorry indeed to have added to his causes for disquiet.", "그리고 그분의 근심거리를 하나 더 보탠 것을 정말이지 깊이 뉘우치네."], ["There, Sam, there!’", "자, 샘, 자!\""]], [["‘Well,’ said Sam, rather sulkily, but giving the proffered hand a respectful shake at the same time--‘well, so you ought to be, and I am very glad to find you air; for, if I can help it, I won’t have him put upon by nobody, and that’s all about it.’", "\"음,\" 샘이 다소 뚱한 채로, 그러나 동시에 내민 손을 공손히 흔들며 말했다. \"음, 그러셔야 마땅합죠. 그리고 정말로 그러신다니 저도 무척 기쁩니다요. 왜냐하면, 제가 막을 수만 있다면, 누구도 그분을 함부로 못 하게 할 작정이거든요. 뭐, 그게 다입니다요.\""]], [["‘Certainly not, Sam,’ said Mr.", "\"물론 그래야지, 샘.\" 윈클 씨가 말했다."], ["Winkle. ‘There!", "\"자!"], ["Now go to bed, Sam, and we’ll talk further about this in the morning.’", "이제 자러 가게, 샘. 이 일은 아침에 더 이야기하세.\""]], [["‘I’m wery sorry,’ said Sam, ‘but I can’t go to bed.’", "\"정말 죄송합니다만,\" 샘이 말했다. \"저는 자러 갈 수가 없습니다요.\""]], [["‘Not go to bed!’ repeated Mr.", "\"자러 갈 수 없다고!\""], ["Winkle.", "윈클 씨가 되뇌었다."]], [["‘No,’ said Sam, shaking his head.", "\"네.\" 샘이 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 말했다."], ["‘Can’t be done.’", "\"그건 안 될 일입니다요.\""]], [["‘You don’t mean to say you’re going back to-night, Sam?’ urged Mr.", "\"설마 오늘 밤에 돌아간다는 말은 아니겠지, 샘?\""], ["Winkle, greatly surprised.", "윈클 씨가 몹시 놀라 다그쳤다."]], [["‘Not unless you particklerly wish it,’ replied Sam; ‘but I mustn’t leave this here room.", "\"나리께서 특별히 그러길 바라지 않으시면 안 갑니다요.\" 샘이 대답했다."], ["The governor’s orders wos peremptory.’", "\"하지만 저는 이 방을 떠나선 안 됩니다요. 주인 어른의 명령이 지엄하셨거든요.\""]], [["‘Nonsense, Sam,’ said Mr. Winkle, ‘I must stop here two or three days; and more than that, Sam, you must stop here too, to assist me in gaining an interview with a young lady--Miss Allen, Sam; you remember her--whom I must and will see before I leave Bristol.’", "\"말도 안 되네, 샘.\" 윈클 씨가 말했다. \"나는 여기서 이삼 일은 머물러야 하네. 그뿐만 아니라, 샘, 자네도 여기 머물러서, 내가 어느 젊은 숙녀와 만나도록 도와줘야 하네—앨런 양 말일세, 샘. 자네도 그분을 기억하겠지—나는 브리스틀을 떠나기 전에 반드시 그분을 만나야 하고, 또 만나고야 말 것이네.\""]], [["But in reply to each of these positions, Sam shook his head with great firmness, and energetically replied, ‘It can’t be done.’", "그러나 이 주장 하나하나에 대해, 샘은 자못 단호하게 고개를 흔들며 힘주어 대답했다. \"그건 안 될 일입니다요.\""]], [["After a great deal of argument and representation on the part of Mr.", "하지만 윈클 씨가 한참을 따지고 설득하고, 또 다울러와의 만남에서 있었던 일을 죄다 털어놓고 나자, 샘이 흔들리기 시작했다."], ["Winkle, however, and a full disclosure of what had passed in the interview with Dowler, Sam began to waver; and at length a compromise was effected, of which the following were the main and principal conditions:--", "그리하여 마침내 하나의 타협이 이루어졌는데, 그 주된 골자는 다음과 같았다."]], [["That Sam should retire, and leave Mr. Winkle in the undisturbed possession of his apartment, on the condition that he had permission to lock the door on the outside, and carry off the key; provided always, that in the event of an alarm of fire, or other dangerous contingency, the door should be instantly unlocked.", "샘은 물러가서 윈클 씨가 방을 거리낌 없이 차지하도록 내버려 두되, 그 대신 바깥에서 문을 잠그고 열쇠를 가져갈 권한을 갖는다."], ["That a letter should be written to Mr. Pickwick early next morning, and forwarded per Dowler, requesting his consent to Sam and Mr.", "단, 화재 경보가 울리거나 그 밖에 위험한 사태가 벌어질 경우에는 즉시 문을 연다."], ["Winkle’s remaining at Bristol, for the purpose and with the object already assigned, and begging an answer by the next coach--, if favourable, the aforesaid parties to remain accordingly, and if not, to return to Bath immediately on the receipt thereof.", "이튿날 아침 일찍 픽윅 씨 앞으로 편지 한 통을 써서 다울러 편에 부쳐, 앞서 정한 목적과 취지로 샘과 윈클 씨가 브리스틀에 머무는 데 대한 동의를 청하고, 다음 마차 편으로 답신을 구한다. 만일 허락이 떨어지면 앞서 말한 당사자들은 그대로 머물고, 그렇지 않으면 답신을 받는 즉시 배스로 돌아간다."], ["And, lastly, that Mr. Winkle should be understood as distinctly pledging himself not to resort to the window, fireplace, or other surreptitious mode of escape in the meanwhile.", "그리고 마지막으로, 윈클 씨는 그동안 창문이나 벽난로, 혹은 그 밖의 은밀한 도주 수단에 의지하지 않겠다고 분명히 서약한 것으로 한다."], ["These stipulations having been concluded, Sam locked the door and departed.", "이 조항들이 매듭지어지자, 샘은 문을 잠그고 떠나갔다."]], [["He had nearly got downstairs, when he stopped, and drew the key from his pocket.", "그는 거의 아래층까지 다 내려왔다가, 걸음을 멈추고 주머니에서 열쇠를 꺼냈다."]], [["‘I quite forgot about the knockin’ down,’ said Sam, half turning back.", "\"때려눕히는 일을 깜빡했네.\" 샘이 반쯤 돌아서며 말했다."], ["‘The governor distinctly said it was to be done.", "\"주인 어른께서 분명히 그렇게 하라고 하셨는데."], ["Amazin’ stupid o’ me, that ‘ere!", "거 참, 어지간히 멍청하구먼, 내가! 뭐 상관없지.\""], ["Never mind,’ said Sam, brightening up, ‘it’s easily done to- morrow, anyvays.’", "샘이 표정이 밝아지며 말했다. \"어쨌거나 그건 내일 하면 쉬운 일이니까.\""]], [["Apparently much consoled by this reflection, Mr. Weller once more deposited the key in his pocket, and descending the remainder of the stairs without any fresh visitations of conscience, was soon, in common with the other inmates of the house, buried in profound repose.", "이 생각에 적잖이 위안을 얻은 듯, 웰러 씨는 다시 한번 열쇠를 주머니에 집어넣고, 더는 양심의 가책을 받지 않고 남은 계단을 마저 내려가, 이내 그 집의 다른 식구들과 마찬가지로 깊은 잠 속에 파묻혔다."]], [["CHAPTER XXXIX. MR. SAMUEL WELLER, BEING INTRUSTED WITH A MISSION OF LOVE, PROCEEDS TO EXECUTE IT; WITH WHAT SUCCESS WILL HEREINAFTER APPEAR\nDuring the whole of next day, Sam kept Mr. Winkle steadily in sight, fully determined not to take his eyes off him for one instant, until he should receive express instructions from the fountain-head. However disagreeable Sam’s very close watch and great vigilance were to Mr. Winkle, he thought it better to bear with them, than, by any act of violent opposition, to hazard being carried away by force, which Mr. Weller more than once strongly hinted was the line of conduct that a strict sense of duty prompted him to pursue. There is little reason to doubt that Sam would very speedily have quieted his scruples, by bearing Mr. Winkle back to Bath, bound hand and foot, had not Mr. Pickwick’s prompt attention to the note, which Dowler had undertaken to deliver, forestalled any such proceeding. In short, at eight o’clock in the evening, Mr. Pickwick himself walked into the coffee-room of the Bush Tavern, and told Sam with a smile, to his very great relief, that he had done quite right, and it was unnecessary for him to mount guard any longer.", "이튿날 내내 샘은 윈클 씨를 한시도 놓치지 않으려고 단단히 마음먹은 채 그를 줄곧 지켜보았으니, 우두머리로부터 분명한 지시를 받을 때까지는 단 한순간도 그에게서 눈을 떼지 않겠다고 작정한 터였다. 샘의 바짝 붙은 감시와 대단한 경계가 윈클 씨에게는 아무리 불쾌했다 하더라도, 그는 격렬히 저항하는 어떤 행동을 했다가 힘에 의해 끌려가는 위험을 무릅쓰느니 차라리 그것을 참는 편이 낫다고 생각했다. 웰러 씨가 한 번 이상 강하게 내비친 바로는, 그것이야말로 엄격한 의무감이 그에게 따르라고 재촉하는 행동 방침이었던 것이다. 다우러가 전하기로 맡았던 쪽지에 픽윅 씨가 즉각 주의를 기울이지 않았더라면, 샘이 윈클 씨를 손발을 꽁꽁 묶어 배스로 도로 떠메고 가서 자신의 거리낌을 아주 신속히 가라앉혔으리라는 것은 의심할 여지가 거의 없다. 요컨대 저녁 여덟 시에 픽윅 씨가 직접 부시 여관의 커피룸으로 걸어 들어와, 샘에게 미소를 지으며 그가 아주 잘했고 더 이상 보초를 설 필요가 없다고 말해 주어, 샘은 크게 안도했다."]], [["‘I thought it better to come myself,’ said Mr. Pickwick, addressing Mr.", "\"내가 직접 오는 편이 낫겠다고 생각했네.\""], ["Winkle, as Sam disencumbered him of his great-coat and travelling-shawl, ‘to ascertain, before I gave my consent to Sam’s employment in this matter, that you are quite in earnest and serious, with respect to this young lady.’", "샘이 외투와 여행용 숄을 벗겨 주는 동안 픽윅 씨가 윈클 씨를 향해 말했다. \"이 일에 샘을 부리는 것에 동의하기에 앞서, 자네가 그 젊은 숙녀와 관련해 정말로 진지하고 진심인지 확인하려고 말일세.\""]], [["‘Serious, from my heart--from my soul!’ returned Mr.", "\"진심입니다, 가슴 깊이에서—제 영혼에서요!\""], ["Winkle, with great energy.", "윈클 씨가 대단한 기세로 대답했다."]], [["‘Remember,’ said Mr.", "\"기억하게.\""], ["Pickwick, with beaming eyes, ‘we met her at our excellent and hospitable friend’s, Winkle.", "픽윅 씨가 환히 빛나는 눈으로 말했다. \"우리는 그 훌륭하고 환대 깊은 친구의 집에서 그녀를 만났네, 윈클."], ["It would be an ill return to tamper lightly, and without due consideration, with this young lady’s affections.", "충분한 숙고 없이 가볍게 이 젊은 숙녀의 애정을 가지고 장난친다면 그것은 못된 보답이 될 걸세."], ["I’ll not allow that, sir.", "나는 그것을 용납하지 않겠네, 자네."], ["I’ll not allow it.’", "용납하지 않겠어.\""]], [["‘I have no such intention, indeed,’ exclaimed Mr. Winkle warmly.", "\"정말 저는 그런 의도가 전혀 없습니다.\""], ["‘I have considered the matter well, for a long time, and I feel that my happiness is bound up in her.’", "윈클 씨가 열렬히 외쳤다. \"저는 오랫동안 이 문제를 잘 숙고했고, 제 행복이 그녀에게 달려 있음을 느낍니다.\""]], [["‘That’s wot we call tying it up in a small parcel, sir,’ interposed Mr.", "\"그게 바로 우리가 말하는 행복을 조그만 꾸러미에 동여매는 일이라는 거죠, 나리.\""], ["Weller, with an agreeable smile.", "웰러 씨가 유쾌한 미소를 지으며 끼어들었다."]], [["Mr. Winkle looked somewhat stern at this interruption, and Mr.", "이 끼어듦에 윈클 씨가 다소 엄한 표정을 짓자, 픽윅 씨가 화를 내며 자기 하인에게 인간 본성의 가장 좋은 감정 가운데 하나를 가지고 농담하지 말라고 요청했다."], ["Pickwick angrily requested his attendant not to jest with one of the best feelings of our nature; to which Sam replied, ‘That he wouldn’t, if he was aware on it; but there were so many on ‘em, that he hardly know’d which was the best ones wen he heerd ‘em mentioned.’", "이에 샘은 \"그게 좋은 감정인 줄 알았더라면 그러지 않았을 텐데, 그런 감정이 하도 많아서 누가 그것들을 입에 올리는 걸 들을 때 어느 게 제일 좋은 건지 도통 분간을 못 하겠다.\"라고 대꾸했다."]], [["Mr. Winkle then recounted what had passed between himself and Mr.", "이어 윈클 씨는 아라벨라와 관련해 자신과 벤 앨런 씨 사이에 오간 일을 자세히 이야기했고, 자신의 목적은 그 젊은 숙녀와 만나 자신의 연정을 정식으로 털어놓는 것이라고 밝혔으며, 앞서 말한 벤이 흘린 어떤 모호한 암시와 중얼거림에 근거하여, 그녀가 지금 어디에 갇혀 있든 그곳은 다운스 근처 어딘가라는 확신을 표명했다."], ["Ben Allen, relative to Arabella; stated that his object was to gain an interview with the young lady, and make a formal disclosure of his passion; and declared his conviction, founded on certain dark hints and mutterings of the aforesaid Ben, that, wherever she was at present immured, it was somewhere near the Downs. And this was his whole stock of knowledge or suspicion on the subject.", "그리고 이것이 그가 이 문제에 관해 알거나 짐작하고 있는 전부였다."]], [["With this very slight clue to guide him, it was determined that Mr. Weller should start next morning on an expedition of discovery; it was also arranged that Mr. Pickwick and Mr. Winkle, who were less confident of their powers, should parade the town meanwhile, and accidentally drop in upon Mr. Bob Sawyer in the course of the day, in the hope of seeing or hearing something of the young lady’s whereabouts.", "이처럼 지극히 빈약한 단서를 길잡이로 삼아, 웰러 씨가 이튿날 아침 탐색 원정에 나서기로 결정되었다. 또한 자신들의 능력에 자신이 덜한 픽윅 씨와 윈클 씨는 그동안 시내를 돌아다니다가 그날 중에 우연인 척 보브 소여 씨를 들러, 그 젊은 숙녀의 행방에 관해 무언가 보거나 듣게 되기를 기대하기로 정해졌다."]], [["Accordingly, next morning, Sam Weller issued forth upon his quest, in no way daunted by the very discouraging prospect before him; and away he walked, up one street and down another--we were going to say, up one hill and down another, only it’s all uphill at Clifton--without meeting with anything or anybody that tended to throw the faintest light on the matter in hand.", "그리하여 이튿날 아침, 샘 웰러는 앞에 놓인 무척이나 맥 빠지는 전망에도 조금도 기죽지 않고 탐색에 나섰다. 그는 한 거리를 올라갔다가 다른 거리를 내려가며 걸었으니—한 언덕을 올라갔다 다른 언덕을 내려갔다고 말하려 했으나, 클리프턴에서는 죄다 오르막이라 그럴 수가 없다—다루는 일에 가장 희미한 빛이라도 던져 줄 만한 무엇이나 누구와도 마주치지 못했다."], ["Many were the colloquies into which Sam entered with grooms who were airing horses on roads, and nursemaids who were airing children in lanes; but nothing could Sam elicit from either the first- mentioned or the last, which bore the slightest reference to the object of his artfully-prosecuted inquiries.", "샘이 길에서 말을 산책시키는 마부들이며 골목에서 아이들을 바람 쐬게 하는 보모들과 나눈 대화는 수없이 많았으나, 앞에 말한 부류든 뒤에 말한 부류든 어느 쪽에게서도 그가 교묘하게 진행하는 탐문의 대상과 조금이라도 관련된 것은 아무것도 캐낼 수 없었다."], ["There were a great many young ladies in a great many houses, the greater part whereof were shrewdly suspected by the male and female domestics to be deeply attached to somebody, or perfectly ready to become so, if opportunity afforded.", "숱한 집에 숱한 젊은 숙녀들이 있었고, 그 대부분은 남녀 하인들에게서 누군가에게 깊이 마음을 두고 있거나, 기회만 닿으면 기꺼이 그리되리라는 예리한 의심을 사고 있었다."], ["But as none among these young ladies was Miss Arabella Allen, the information left Sam at exactly the old point of wisdom at which he had stood before.", "그러나 그 젊은 숙녀들 가운데 아라벨라 앨런 양은 없었으므로, 그 정보는 샘을 이전에 서 있던 바로 그 지혜의 지점에 그대로 남겨 두었다."]], [["Sam struggled across the Downs against a good high wind, wondering whether it was always necessary to hold your hat on with both hands in that part of the country, and came to a shady by-place, about which were sprinkled several little villas of quiet and secluded appearance.", "샘은 제법 거센 바람을 거슬러 다운스를 가로질러 헤쳐 나가며, 이 고장에서는 늘 두 손으로 모자를 붙들고 있어야 하는 건지 궁금해하다가, 그늘진 외진 곳에 이르렀는데, 그 주위로는 조용하고 한적해 보이는 작은 별장 몇 채가 흩어져 있었다."], ["Outside a stable door at the bottom of a long back lane without a thoroughfare, a groom in undress was idling about, apparently persuading himself that he was doing something with a spade and a wheel-barrow.", "막다른 긴 뒷골목 끝에 있는 마구간 문밖에서, 작업복 차림의 마부 하나가 빈둥거리며, 삽 한 자루와 외바퀴 손수레로 무언가 하고 있노라고 스스로 믿게 만드는 듯했다."], ["We may remark, in this place, that we have scarcely ever seen a groom near a stable, in his lazy moments, who has not been, to a greater or less extent, the victim of this singular delusion.", "여기서 한마디 해 두자면, 우리는 마구간 근처에서 한가한 시간을 보내는 마부치고 정도의 차이는 있을지언정 이 묘한 망상의 희생양이 아닌 자를 거의 본 적이 없다."]], [["Sam thought he might as well talk to this groom as to any one else, especially as he was very tired with walking, and there was a good large stone just opposite the wheel-barrow; so he strolled down the lane, and, seating himself on the stone, opened a conversation with the ease and freedom for which he was remarkable.", "샘은 어차피 다른 누구에게 말을 거나 이 마부에게 말을 거나 매한가지라고 생각했는데, 더욱이 그는 걸어 다니느라 몹시 지쳐 있었고, 외바퀴 손수레 바로 맞은편에 제법 큼직한 돌 하나가 있었던 것이다. 그래서 그는 골목을 어슬렁어슬렁 내려가, 그 돌에 앉아, 그 특유의 편안함과 거리낌 없음으로 대화를 텄다."]], [["‘Mornin’, old friend,’ said Sam.", "\"안녕하슈, 친구 양반.\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["‘Arternoon, you mean,’ replied the groom, casting a surly look at Sam.", "\"오후라고 해야지.\" 마부가 샘에게 퉁명스러운 눈길을 던지며 대꾸했다."]], [["‘You’re wery right, old friend,’ said Sam; ‘I do mean arternoon.", "\"맞는 말이오, 친구 양반.\" 샘이 말했다."], ["How are you?’", "\"내 말은 오후 안녕이오. 어떻게 지내시오?\""]], [["‘Why, I don’t find myself much the better for seeing of you,’ replied the ill-tempered groom.", "\"글쎄, 댁을 본다고 내 기분이 그닥 나아질 것 같진 않은데.\" 성질 고약한 마부가 대꾸했다."]], [["‘That’s wery odd--that is,’ said Sam, ‘for you look so uncommon cheerful, and seem altogether so lively, that it does vun’s heart good to see you.’", "\"그것참 이상하구먼.\" 샘이 말했다. \"댁은 어찌나 보기 드물게 명랑해 보이고 통 활기차 보이는지, 댁을 보고 있으면 사람 마음이 다 흐뭇해지는데 말이오.\""]], [["The surly groom looked surlier still at this, but not sufficiently so to produce any effect upon Sam, who immediately inquired, with a countenance of great anxiety, whether his master’s name was not Walker.", "이 말에 퉁명스러운 마부는 한층 더 퉁명스러운 표정을 지었으나, 샘에게 무슨 영향을 줄 만큼은 못 되었고, 샘은 곧장 대단히 근심스러운 얼굴로, 댁 주인장 성함이 워커가 아니냐고 물었다."]], [["‘No, it ain’t,’ said the groom.", "\"아니, 아닌데.\" 마부가 말했다."]], [["‘Nor Brown, I s’pose?’ said Sam.", "\"그럼 브라운도 아니겠구먼?\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["‘No, it ain’t.’", "\"아니, 아니야.\""]], [["‘Nor Vilson?’", "\"윌슨도 아니고?\""]], [["‘No; nor that either,’ said the groom.", "\"아니, 그것도 아니야.\" 마부가 말했다."]], [["‘Vell,’ replied Sam, ‘then I’m mistaken, and he hasn’t got the honour o’ my acquaintance, which I thought he had. Don’t wait here out o’ compliment to me,’ said Sam, as the groom wheeled in the barrow, and prepared to shut the gate. ‘Ease afore ceremony, old boy; I’ll excuse you.’", "\"흠.\" 샘이 대꾸했다. \"그럼 내가 잘못 안 거고, 그 양반이 나랑 알고 지내는 영광은 없는 거구먼. 그런 줄로 알았는데 말이오. 나한테 예의 차리느라 여기 서 있을 거 없소.\" 마부가 손수레를 밀고 들어가며 문을 닫을 채비를 하자 샘이 말했다. \"격식보다 편한 게 낫지, 이 양반아. 내 봐줄 테니.\""]], [["‘I’d knock your head off for half-a-crown,’ said the surly groom, bolting one half of the gate.", "\"반 크라운만 주면 댁 대가리를 날려 버리겠는데.\" 퉁명스러운 마부가 문짝 한쪽에 빗장을 지르며 말했다."]], [["‘Couldn’t afford to have it done on those terms,’ rejoined Sam.", "\"그런 조건으로 그 짓을 시킬 여유는 없소.\" 샘이 대꾸했다."], ["‘It ‘ud be worth a life’s board wages at least, to you, and ‘ud be cheap at that.", "\"그건 적어도 댁한테 평생 치 식비 수당만큼 값나가는 일일 텐데, 그 값에 한다면 거저나 다름없는 거지."], ["Make my compliments indoors.", "안에다 내 안부 전해 주쇼."], ["Tell ‘em not to vait dinner for me, and say they needn’t mind puttin’ any by, for it’ll be cold afore I come in.’", "나 기다려 가며 저녁 차리지 말라고 하고, 따로 챙겨 둘 것도 없다고 해 주쇼. 내가 들어갈 즈음엔 다 식어 있을 테니까.\""]], [["In reply to this, the groom waxing very wroth, muttered a desire to damage somebody’s person; but disappeared without carrying it into execution, slamming the door angrily after him, and wholly unheeding Sam’s affectionate request, that he would leave him a lock of his hair before he went.", "이에 대한 대답으로 마부는 잔뜩 노발대발하며 누군가의 신체에 손상을 입히고 싶다는 뜻을 중얼거렸으나, 그것을 실행에 옮기지는 않은 채 사라졌으니, 화가 나서 문을 쾅 닫고는, 가기 전에 머리카락 한 줌만 남겨 두고 가 달라는 샘의 다정한 부탁을 깡그리 무시했다."]], [["Sam continued to sit on the large stone, meditating upon what was best to be done, and revolving in his mind a plan for knocking at all the doors within five miles of Bristol, taking them at a hundred and fifty or two hundred a day, and endeavouring to find Miss Arabella by that expedient, when accident all of a sudden threw in his way what he might have sat there for a twelvemonth and yet not found without it.", "샘은 큼직한 돌에 계속 앉아, 어떻게 하는 게 최선일지 곰곰이 궁리하며, 브리스틀 반경 5마일 안의 모든 집 문을 하루에 백오십 채니 이백 채니 하는 식으로 차례차례 두드려, 그 방편으로 아라벨라 양을 찾아내 보려는 계획을 머릿속에서 굴리고 있었는데, 그때 우연이 느닷없이 그의 길에 던져 준 것이라곤, 그것 없이는 일 년을 앉아 있어도 찾아내지 못했을 바로 그것이었다."]], [["Into the lane where he sat, there opened three or four garden gates, belonging to as many houses, which though detached from each other, were only separated by their gardens.", "그가 앉아 있는 골목으로는 서너 채의 집에 딸린 정원 문 서너 개가 나 있었는데, 그 집들은 서로 떨어져 있었으나 오로지 정원으로만 갈라져 있었다."], ["As these were large and long, and well planted with trees, the houses were not only at some distance off, but the greater part of them were nearly concealed from view.", "그 정원들이 넓고 길었으며 나무가 빽빽이 심겨 있었던지라, 집들은 어느 정도 떨어져 있을 뿐 아니라 대부분이 거의 시야에서 가려져 있었다."], ["Sam was sitting with his eyes fixed upon the dust-heap outside the next gate to that by which the groom had disappeared, profoundly turning over in his mind the difficulties of his present undertaking, when the gate opened, and a female servant came out into the lane to shake some bedside carpets.", "샘은 마부가 사라진 문 바로 옆 문밖에 쌓인 쓰레기 더미에 눈길을 고정한 채, 지금 떠맡은 일의 어려움을 깊이 곱씹고 있었는데, 그때 그 문이 열리며 하녀 하나가 침대맡 양탄자 몇 장을 털러 골목으로 나왔다."]], [["Sam was so very busy with his own thoughts, that it is probable he would have taken no more notice of the young woman than just raising his head and remarking that she had a very neat and pretty figure, if his feelings of gallantry had not been most strongly roused by observing that she had no one to help her, and that the carpets seemed too heavy for her single strength.", "샘은 자기 생각에 어찌나 깊이 빠져 있었던지, 그 여자에 대해서도 그저 고개를 들어 그녀의 몸매가 무척 단정하고 예쁘다고 여기는 것 이상의 관심은 두지 않았을 것이다. 다만 그녀를 도와줄 사람이 아무도 없고 양탄자가 그녀 혼자 힘으로는 너무 무거워 보인다는 것을 알아챈 순간, 그의 기사도 정신이 가장 강하게 동했다."], ["Mr. Weller was a gentleman of great gallantry in his own way, and he no sooner remarked this circumstance than he hastily rose from the large stone, and advanced towards her.", "웰러 씨는 그 나름으로 기사도가 대단한 신사였던지라, 이 정황을 알아채자마자 큼직한 돌에서 황급히 일어나 그녀에게 다가갔다."]], [["‘My dear,’ said Sam, sliding up with an air of great respect, ‘you’ll spile that wery pretty figure out o’ all perportion if you shake them carpets by yourself.", "\"아가씨.\" 샘이 대단히 정중한 태도로 미끄러지듯 다가가며 말했다."], ["Let me help you.’", "\"그 양탄자를 혼자 털다간 그 아주 예쁜 몸매를 영 어울리잖게 망쳐 놓을 거요. 내가 도와드리리다.\""]], [["The young lady, who had been coyly affecting not to know that a gentleman was so near, turned round as Sam spoke--no doubt (indeed she said so, afterwards) to decline this offer from a perfect stranger--when instead of speaking, she started back, and uttered a half-suppressed scream.", "신사가 그토록 가까이 있는 줄 모르는 척 새침을 떨고 있던 젊은 숙녀는, 샘이 말하자 돌아섰으니—틀림없이 (사실 그녀는 나중에 그렇게 말했다) 생판 모르는 남자의 이 제안을 거절하려던 것이었으나—말을 꺼내는 대신, 그녀는 흠칫 뒤로 물러서며 반쯤 억눌린 비명을 질렀다."], ["Sam was scarcely less staggered, for in the countenance of the well-shaped female servant, he beheld the very features of his valentine, the pretty housemaid from Mr. Nupkins’s.", "샘도 못지않게 휘청했으니, 맵시 있는 하녀의 얼굴에서 다름 아닌 자신의 연인, 너프킨스 씨 댁의 예쁜 하녀의 바로 그 이목구비를 본 것이다."]], [["‘Wy, Mary, my dear!’ said Sam.", "\"아니, 메리, 내 사랑!\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["‘Lauk, Mr.", "\"어머나, 웰러 씨.\""], ["Weller,’ said Mary, ‘how you do frighten one!’", "메리가 말했다. \"사람을 어쩜 그렇게 놀래키세요!\""]], [["Sam made no verbal answer to this complaint, nor can we precisely say what reply he did make.", "샘은 이 불평에 말로 대답하지 않았고, 정확히 그가 무슨 대답을 했는지는 우리도 말할 수 없다."], ["We merely know that after a short pause Mary said, ‘Lor, do adun, Mr.", "우리가 아는 것이라곤, 잠시 뒤 메리가 \"어머, 그만 좀 하세요, 웰러 씨!\""], ["Weller!’ and that his hat had fallen off a few moments before--from both of which tokens we should be disposed to infer that one kiss, or more, had passed between the parties.", "하고 말했다는 것, 그리고 그의 모자가 몇 순간 전에 벗겨져 떨어졌다는 것뿐이다—이 두 가지 정황으로 미루어, 두 사람 사이에 입맞춤이 한 번, 아니 그 이상 오갔으리라고 짐작할 만하다."]], [["‘Why, how did you come here?’ said Mary, when the conversation to which this interruption had been offered, was resumed.", "\"근데, 여기 어떻게 오셨어요?\" 끼어들었던 일로 끊겼던 대화가 다시 이어졌을 때 메리가 말했다."]], [["‘O’ course I came to look arter you, my darlin’,’ replied Mr.", "\"당연히 당신을 찾으러 왔지, 내 사랑.\""], ["Weller; for once permitting his passion to get the better of his veracity.", "웰러 씨가 대답했으니, 이번만큼은 자신의 정열이 진실함을 이기도록 내버려 둔 것이었다."]], [["‘And how did you know I was here?’ inquired Mary.", "\"근데 제가 여기 있는 줄은 어떻게 아셨어요?\" 메리가 물었다."], ["‘Who could have told you that I took another service at Ipswich, and that they afterwards moved all the way here?", "\"제가 입스위치에서 다른 집으로 옮겼고, 그러고 나서 그 집이 여기까지 멀리 이사 왔다는 걸 누가 말해 줄 수 있었겠어요?"], ["Who could have told you that, Mr. Weller?’", "그걸 누가 말해 줄 수 있었겠어요, 웰러 씨?\""]], [["‘Ah, to be sure,’ said Sam, with a cunning look, ‘that’s the pint.", "\"아, 그렇지.\" 샘이 능청스러운 표정으로 말했다."], ["Who could ha’ told me?’", "\"그게 요점이지. 누가 나한테 말해 줄 수 있었을까?\""]], [["‘It wasn’t Mr.", "\"머즐 씨는 아니죠, 그렇죠?\""], ["Muzzle, was it?’ inquired Mary.", "메리가 물었다."]], [["‘Oh, no.’ replied Sam, with a solemn shake of the head, ‘it warn’t him.’", "\"오, 아니지.\" 샘이 엄숙하게 고개를 저으며 대답했다. \"그 사람은 아니야.\""]], [["‘It must have been the cook,’ said Mary.", "\"그럼 요리사가 틀림없어요.\" 메리가 말했다."]], [["‘O’ course it must,’ said Sam.", "\"당연히 그렇겠지.\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["‘Well, I never heard the like of that!’ exclaimed Mary.", "\"어머, 그런 얘긴 난생처음 들어 봐요!\" 메리가 외쳤다."]], [["‘No more did I,’ said Sam.", "\"나도 마찬가지야.\""], ["‘But Mary, my dear’--here Sam’s manner grew extremely affectionate--‘Mary, my dear, I’ve got another affair in hand as is wery pressin’.", "샘이 말했다. \"근데 메리, 내 사랑\"—여기서 샘의 태도가 무척 다정해졌다—\"메리, 내 사랑, 나한테 또 한 가지 아주 급한 일이 있어."], ["There’s one o’ my governor’s friends--Mr. Winkle, you remember him?’", "우리 주인 양반 친구분 중 하나가—윈클 씨라고, 기억하지?\""]], [["‘Him in the green coat?’ said Mary.", "\"녹색 코트 입은 그분요?\" 메리가 말했다."], ["‘Oh, yes, I remember him.’", "\"아, 네, 기억해요.\""]], [["‘Well,’ said Sam, ‘he’s in a horrid state o’ love; reg’larly comfoozled, and done over vith it.’", "\"그게 말이지.\" 샘이 말했다. \"그분이 끔찍하게 사랑에 빠졌거든. 아주 정신이 혼미해져서, 완전히 맥을 못 추고 있어.\""]], [["‘Lor!’ interposed Mary.", "\"어머나!\" 메리가 끼어들었다."]], [["‘Yes,’ said Sam; ‘but that’s nothin’ if we could find out the young ‘ooman;’ and here Sam, with many digressions upon the personal beauty of Mary, and the unspeakable tortures he had experienced since he last saw her, gave a faithful account of Mr.", "\"그래.\" 샘이 말했다."], ["Winkle’s present predicament.", "\"근데 그 젊은 아가씨를 찾을 수만 있으면 그쯤은 아무것도 아니지.\" 그러고 나서 샘은 메리의 미모와, 그녀를 마지막으로 본 이후 자신이 겪은 이루 말할 수 없는 고통에 관해 숱하게 곁가지를 치면서, 윈클 씨가 지금 처한 곤경을 충실히 들려주었다."]], [["‘Well,’ said Mary, ‘I never did!’", "\"어머, 세상에!\" 메리가 말했다."]], [["‘O’ course not,’ said Sam, ‘and nobody never did, nor never vill neither; and here am I a-walkin’ about like the wandering Jew--a sportin’ character you have perhaps heerd on Mary, my dear, as vos alvays doin’ a match agin’ time, and never vent to sleep--looking arter this here Miss Arabella Allen.’", "\"당연히 처음 듣겠지.\" 샘이 말했다. \"그런 일은 아무도 들어 본 적 없고, 앞으로도 없을 테니까. 그래서 내가 방랑하는 유대인처럼 돌아다니고 있는 거야—아마 들어 봤을 텐데 메리, 늘 시간과 경주를 벌이면서 잠 한숨 안 자고 다닌다는 그 유명한 작자 말이야—이 아라벨라 앨런 양인지 하는 사람을 찾으면서.\""]], [["‘Miss who?’ said Mary, in great astonishment.", "\"누구 양이라고요?\" 메리가 몹시 놀라며 말했다."]], [["‘Miss Arabella Allen,’ said Sam.", "\"아라벨라 앨런 양.\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["‘Goodness gracious!’ said Mary, pointing to the garden door which the sulky groom had locked after him.", "\"어머나 세상에!\" 메리가 그 뚱한 마부가 잠가 둔 정원 문을 가리키며 말했다."], ["‘Why, it’s that very house; she’s been living there these six weeks.", "\"아니, 바로 저 집이에요. 그분이 거기서 산 지 육 주나 됐어요."], ["Their upper house-maid, which is lady’s- maid too, told me all about it over the wash-house palin’s before the family was out of bed, one mornin’.’", "어느 날 아침, 그 집 식구들이 아직 잠자리에서 일어나기도 전에, 거기 수석 하녀가, 시녀 일도 같이 하는 사람인데, 세탁장 울타리 너머로 저한테 그 얘기를 다 해 줬거든요.\""]], [["‘Wot, the wery next door to you?’ said Sam.", "\"뭐라고, 너희 집 바로 옆집이라고?\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["‘The very next,’ replied Mary.", "\"바로 옆집요.\" 메리가 대답했다."]], [["Mr.", "이 소식을 듣고 웰러 씨는 어찌나 깊이 마음이 북받쳤던지, 어쩔 수 없이 자신의 어여쁜 정보 제공자에게 매달려 몸을 지탱해야 했다."], ["Weller was so deeply overcome on receiving this intelligence that he found it absolutely necessary to cling to his fair informant for support; and divers little love passages had passed between them, before he was sufficiently collected to return to the subject.", "그가 다시 본론으로 돌아갈 만큼 정신을 추스르기까지, 두 사람 사이에는 자잘한 사랑의 실랑이가 몇 차례 오갔다."]], [["‘Vell,’ said Sam at length, ‘if this don’t beat cock-fightin’ nothin’ never vill, as the lord mayor said, ven the chief secretary o’ state proposed his missis’s health arter dinner.", "\"흠.\" 마침내 샘이 말했다."], ["That wery next house!", "\"이거야말로 닭싸움보다 더 기막힌 일인데, 이걸 능가할 게 없겠어—만찬 뒤에 국무 장관이 자기 마님 건강을 위해 건배하자고 했을 때 시장 나리가 한 말마따나 말이야."], ["Wy, I’ve got a message to her as I’ve been a-trying all day to deliver.’", "바로 그 옆집이라니! 아니, 내가 그 아가씨한테 전할 전갈이 있어서 온종일 전하려고 애썼는데 말이야.\""]], [["‘Ah,’ said Mary, ‘but you can’t deliver it now, because she only walks in the garden in the evening, and then only for a very little time; she never goes out, without the old lady.’", "\"아.\" 메리가 말했다. \"하지만 지금은 전할 수 없어요. 그분은 저녁에만 정원을 거니는 데다, 그것도 아주 잠깐뿐이거든요. 늙은 마님 없이는 절대 밖에 안 나가요.\""]], [["Sam ruminated for a few moments, and finally hit upon the following plan of operations; that he should return just at dusk--the time at which Arabella invariably took her walk--and, being admitted by Mary into the garden of the house to which she belonged, would contrive to scramble up the wall, beneath the overhanging boughs of a large pear-tree, which would effectually screen him from observation; would there deliver his message, and arrange, if possible, an interview on behalf of Mr.", "샘은 잠시 곰곰이 생각하다가, 마침내 다음과 같은 작전 계획을 떠올렸다. 즉, 그가 해 질 녘—아라벨라가 으레 산책하는 시각—에 맞춰 돌아와, 메리가 일하는 집 정원으로 그녀의 안내를 받아 들어간 다음, 커다란 배나무의 늘어진 가지들 아래로 담장을 기어올라 가는데, 그 가지들이 그를 남의 눈에서 확실히 가려 줄 터였다."], ["Winkle for the ensuing evening at the same hour.", "거기서 그가 전갈을 전하고, 가능하다면 윈클 씨를 위해 이튿날 저녁 같은 시각에 만남을 주선하기로 한 것이다."], ["Having made this arrangement with great despatch, he assisted Mary in the long-deferred occupation of shaking the carpets.", "이 계획을 아주 잽싸게 마련한 뒤, 그는 메리가 한참 미뤄 두었던 양탄자 터는 일을 거들었다."]], [["It is not half as innocent a thing as it looks, that shaking little pieces of carpet--at least, there may be no great harm in the shaking, but the folding is a very insidious process.", "조그만 양탄자 조각들을 터는 그 일이란, 보이는 것처럼 그렇게 순진한 일이 결코 아니다—적어도 터는 데에는 별 해될 것이 없을지 모르나, 개키는 일이야말로 무척 음흉한 과정이다."], ["So long as the shaking lasts, and the two parties are kept the carpet’s length apart, it is as innocent an amusement as can well be devised; but when the folding begins, and the distance between them gets gradually lessened from one half its former length to a quarter, and then to an eighth, and then to a sixteenth, and then to a thirty-second, if the carpet be long enough, it becomes dangerous.", "터는 일이 계속되어 두 사람이 양탄자 길이만큼 떨어져 있는 동안은, 그야말로 더없이 순진한 놀이라 할 만하다. 그러나 개키기가 시작되어 둘 사이의 거리가 본래 길이의 절반에서 차츰 사분의 일로, 다시 팔분의 일로, 다시 십육분의 일로, 다시 양탄자가 충분히 길다면 삼십이분의 일로 줄어들면, 그것은 위험해진다."], ["We do not know, to a nicety, how many pieces of carpet were folded in this instance, but we can venture to state that as many pieces as there were, so many times did Sam kiss the pretty housemaid.", "이 경우에 정확히 몇 조각의 양탄자가 개켜졌는지 우리는 콕 집어 알지 못하나, 그 조각의 수가 몇이었든 그만큼 샘이 그 예쁜 하녀에게 입을 맞추었으리라고는 감히 단언할 수 있다."]], [["Mr. Weller regaled himself with moderation at the nearest tavern until it was nearly dusk, and then returned to the lane without the thoroughfare.", "웰러 씨는 거의 해 질 녘이 될 때까지 가장 가까운 술집에서 절도 있게 한잔 즐긴 뒤, 막다른 골목으로 돌아왔다."], ["Having been admitted into the garden by Mary, and having received from that lady sundry admonitions concerning the safety of his limbs and neck, Sam mounted into the pear-tree, to wait until Arabella should come into sight.", "메리의 안내로 정원에 들어가, 그 숙녀에게서 자기 팔다리와 목의 안전에 관한 갖가지 당부를 들은 다음, 샘은 아라벨라가 시야에 나타날 때까지 기다리려고 배나무 위로 올라갔다."]], [["He waited so long without this anxiously-expected event occurring, that he began to think it was not going to take place at all, when he heard light footsteps upon the gravel, and immediately afterwards beheld Arabella walking pensively down the garden.", "그토록 기다려 마지않던 일이 일어나지 않은 채 어찌나 오래 기다렸던지, 그는 그 일이 영 일어나지 않을 모양이라고 생각하기 시작했는데, 그때 자갈 위로 가벼운 발소리가 들리더니, 곧이어 아라벨라가 정원을 따라 시름에 잠긴 채 걸어 내려오는 것이 보였다."], ["As soon as she came nearly below the tree, Sam began, by way of gently indicating his presence, to make sundry diabolical noises similar to those which would probably be natural to a person of middle age who had been afflicted with a combination of inflammatory sore throat, croup, and whooping-cough, from his earliest infancy.", "그녀가 나무 거의 바로 아래까지 다가오자, 샘은 자기가 있음을 넌지시 알릴 셈으로, 갓난아기 적부터 인후염과 후두염과 백일해를 한꺼번에 앓아 온 중년 남자에게나 어울릴 법한 갖가지 끔찍한 소리를 내기 시작했다."]], [["Upon this, the young lady cast a hurried glance towards the spot whence the dreadful sounds proceeded; and her previous alarm being not at all diminished when she saw a man among the branches, she would most certainly have decamped, and alarmed the house, had not fear fortunately deprived her of the power of moving, and caused her to sink down on a garden seat, which happened by good luck to be near at hand.", "이에 그 젊은 숙녀는 그 무시무시한 소리가 나는 쪽으로 황급히 눈길을 던졌다. 나뭇가지 사이에서 남자 하나를 보았을 때 그녀의 앞선 놀라움이 조금도 누그러지지 않았으니, 그녀는 틀림없이 줄행랑을 쳐서 온 집 안을 깨워 놓았을 것이다. 다행히 공포가 그녀에게서 움직일 힘을 앗아 가, 마침 운 좋게도 가까이에 있던 정원 의자에 주저앉게 만들지 않았더라면 말이다."]], [["‘She’s a-goin’ off,’ soliloquised Sam in great perplexity.", "\"기절하시는구먼.\" 샘이 몹시 난처해하며 혼잣말을 했다."], ["‘Wot a thing it is, as these here young creeturs will go a-faintin’ avay just ven they oughtn’t to.", "\"거참 이상한 일이지, 이 젊은 처자들은 꼭 기절하면 안 될 때 기절을 한단 말이야."], ["Here, young ‘ooman, Miss Sawbones, Mrs. Vinkle, don’t!’", "이봐요, 아가씨, 톱장이 아가씨, 윈클 부인, 그러지 마쇼!\""]], [["Whether it was the magic of Mr. Winkle’s name, or the coolness of the open air, or some recollection of Mr.", "아라벨라를 되살린 것이 윈클 씨라는 이름의 마력이었는지, 바깥 공기의 서늘함이었는지, 아니면 웰러 씨의 목소리에 대한 어떤 기억이었는지는 중요하지 않다."], ["Weller’s voice, that revived Arabella, matters not.", "그녀는 고개를 들고 나른하게 물었다."], ["She raised her head and languidly inquired, ‘Who’s that, and what do you want?’", "\"거기 누구예요, 뭘 원하는 거예요?\""]], [["‘Hush,’ said Sam, swinging himself on to the wall, and crouching there in as small a compass as he could reduce himself to, ‘only me, miss, only me.’", "\"쉿.\" 샘이 담장 위로 몸을 휙 올려, 될 수 있는 대로 작게 몸을 웅크리며 말했다. \"저예요, 아가씨, 저뿐이라고요.\""]], [["‘Mr.", "\"픽윅 씨네 하인이군요!\""], ["Pickwick’s servant!’ said Arabella earnestly.", "아라벨라가 진지하게 말했다."]], [["‘The wery same, miss,’ replied Sam.", "\"바로 그렇습니다, 아가씨.\""], ["‘Here’s Mr.", "샘이 대답했다."], ["Vinkle reg’larly sewed up vith desperation, miss.’", "\"윈클 씨가 절망에 빠져 아주 단단히 꿰매어진 꼴이 됐답니다, 아가씨.\""]], [["‘Ah!’ said Arabella, drawing nearer the wall.", "\"아!\" 아라벨라가 담장 가까이 다가서며 말했다."]], [["‘Ah, indeed,’ said Sam. ‘Ve thought ve should ha’ been obliged to strait-veskit him last night; he’s been a-ravin’ all day; and he says if he can’t see you afore to-morrow night’s over, he vishes he may be somethin’ unpleasanted if he don’t drownd hisself.’", "\"아, 정말입니다.\" 샘이 말했다. \"저희가 어젯밤엔 그분한테 구속복을 입혀야 하나 싶었다니까요. 온종일 헛소리를 해 대시거든요. 내일 밤이 다 가기 전에 아가씨를 못 보면, 물에 빠져 죽지 않으면 무슨 험한 꼴이라도 당하고 싶다고 그러십니다.\""]], [["‘Oh, no, no, Mr.", "\"오, 안 돼요, 안 돼, 웰러 씨!\""], ["Weller!’ said Arabella, clasping her hands.", "아라벨라가 두 손을 맞잡으며 말했다."]], [["‘That’s wot he says, miss,’ replied Sam coolly.", "\"그분이 그렇게 말씀하셨어요, 아가씨.\" 샘이 차분히 대답했다."], ["‘He’s a man of his word, and it’s my opinion he’ll do it, miss.", "\"약속을 지키는 분이고, 제 생각엔 정말로 그러실 겁니다, 아가씨."], ["He’s heerd all about you from the sawbones in barnacles.’", "그분은 안경 쓴 톱장이한테서 아가씨 얘기를 다 들으셨거든요.\""]], [["‘From my brother!’ said Arabella, having some faint recognition of Sam’s description.", "\"오라버니한테서요!\" 아라벨라가 샘의 묘사에서 어렴풋이 누군지 알아채며 말했다."]], [["‘I don’t rightly know which is your brother, miss,’ replied Sam.", "\"어느 분이 아가씨 오라버니인지는 정확히 모르겠습니다, 아가씨.\" 샘이 대답했다."], ["‘Is it the dirtiest vun o’ the two?’", "\"둘 중에 더 꾀죄죄한 쪽인가요?\""]], [["‘Yes, yes, Mr.", "\"네, 네, 웰러 씨.\""], ["Weller,’ returned Arabella, ‘go on.", "아라벨라가 대꾸했다. \"계속하세요."], ["Make haste, pray.’", "제발 어서요.\""]], [["‘Well, miss,’ said Sam, ‘he’s heerd all about it from him; and it’s the gov’nor’s opinion that if you don’t see him wery quick, the sawbones as we’ve been a-speakin’ on, ‘ull get as much extra lead in his head as’ll rayther damage the dewelopment o’ the orgins if they ever put it in spirits artervards.’", "\"그러니까요, 아가씨.\" 샘이 말했다. \"그분이 그한테서 자초지종을 다 들으셨고요. 주인 양반 생각으로는, 아가씨가 그분을 아주 빨리 안 만나시면, 우리가 얘기하던 그 톱장이 머리통에 납이 좀 넉넉히 더 박혀서, 나중에 알코올에 담가 보존이라도 하려 들 때 장기들 상태에 적잖이 해가 될 거랍니다.\""]], [["‘Oh, what can I do to prevent these dreadful quarrels!’ exclaimed Arabella.", "\"오, 이 끔찍한 다툼을 막으려면 제가 뭘 할 수 있을까요!\" 아라벨라가 외쳤다."]], [["‘It’s the suspicion of a priory ‘tachment as is the cause of it all,’ replied Sam.", "\"이게 다 옛정 운운하는 의심 때문에 벌어진 일입니다.\" 샘이 대답했다."], ["‘You’d better see him, miss.’", "\"그분을 만나 보시는 게 좋겠어요, 아가씨.\""]], [["‘But how?--where?’ cried Arabella.", "\"하지만 어떻게요?—어디서요?\" 아라벨라가 외쳤다."], ["‘I dare not leave the house alone.", "\"저는 혼자서는 감히 집 밖에 못 나가요."], ["My brother is so unkind, so unreasonable!", "오라버니가 어찌나 매정하고, 어찌나 막무가내인지!"], ["I know how strange my talking thus to you may appear, Mr.", "제가 이렇게 당신께 말하는 게 얼마나 이상해 보일지 알아요, 웰러 씨."], ["Weller, but I am very, very unhappy--’ and here poor Arabella wept so bitterly that Sam grew chivalrous.", "하지만 저는 정말, 정말 불행해요—\" 그러고는 가엾은 아라벨라가 어찌나 서럽게 울던지 샘은 기사도가 솟구쳤다."]], [["‘It may seem wery strange talkin’ to me about these here affairs, miss,’ said Sam, with great vehemence; ‘but all I can say is, that I’m not only ready but villin’ to do anythin’ as’ll make matters agreeable; and if chuckin’ either o’ them sawboneses out o’ winder ‘ull do it, I’m the man.’ As Sam Weller said this, he tucked up his wristbands, at the imminent hazard of falling off the wall in so doing, to intimate his readiness to set to work immediately.", "\"아가씨가 이런 일들을 저한테 말씀하시는 게 무척 이상해 보일지 모릅니다, 아가씨.\" 샘이 대단한 열의를 담아 말했다. \"하지만 제가 드릴 수 있는 말은, 저는 일이 잘 풀리게 할 거라면 무엇이든 할 준비가 됐을 뿐 아니라 기꺼이 하겠다는 겁니다. 그리고 그 톱장이들 중 누구든 창밖으로 내던지는 게 도움이 된다면, 제가 바로 그 일을 할 사람입니다.\" 샘 웰러는 이렇게 말하면서, 당장이라도 일에 착수할 채비가 됐음을 내비치려고, 그러다 담장에서 떨어질 위험을 무릅쓰면서까지 소맷부리를 걷어붙였다."]], [["Flattering as these professions of good feeling were, Arabella resolutely declined (most unaccountably, as Sam thought) to avail herself of them.", "이러한 선의의 다짐이 아무리 흐뭇한 것이었어도, 아라벨라는 (샘이 보기엔 도무지 영문 모를 일이었지만) 단호히 그것을 받아들이기를 사양했다."], ["For some time she strenuously refused to grant Mr.", "한동안 그녀는 샘이 그토록 애처롭게 청한 윈클 씨와의 만남을 허락하기를 한사코 거절했다."], ["Winkle the interview Sam had so pathetically requested; but at length, when the conversation threatened to be interrupted by the unwelcome arrival of a third party, she hurriedly gave him to understand, with many professions of gratitude, that it was barely possible she might be in the garden an hour later, next evening.", "그러나 마침내, 반갑지 않은 제삼자의 출현으로 대화가 끊길 위기에 처하자, 그녀는 거듭 고마움을 표하면서, 이튿날 저녁 한 시간쯤 늦게라면 어쩌면 정원에 나와 있을 수도 있겠다고 황급히 일러 주었다. 샘은 이를 완벽히 알아들었다."], ["Sam understood this perfectly well; and Arabella, bestowing upon him one of her sweetest smiles, tripped gracefully away, leaving Mr. Weller in a state of very great admiration of her charms, both personal and mental.", "아라벨라는 그에게 더없이 사랑스러운 미소를 하나 지어 보이고는, 우아하게 종종걸음으로 사라져, 웰러 씨를 그녀의 매력에 대한—외모와 마음씨 양쪽 다에 대한—깊은 감탄에 젖게 한 채 남겨 두었다."]], [["Having descended in safety from the wall, and not forgotten to devote a few moments to his own particular business in the same department, Mr. Weller then made the best of his way back to the Bush, where his prolonged absence had occasioned much speculation and some alarm.", "담장에서 무사히 내려온 뒤, 같은 부문에서 자신만의 사사로운 용무에 몇 순간을 바치는 것도 잊지 않은 채, 웰러 씨는 부시 여관으로 부지런히 돌아갔으니, 그곳에서는 그의 길어진 부재가 갖은 추측과 약간의 불안을 자아내고 있었다."]], [["‘We must be careful,’ said Mr.", "\"우리는 조심해야 하네.\""], ["Pickwick, after listening attentively to Sam’s tale, ‘not for our sakes, but for that of the young lady.", "픽윅 씨가 샘의 이야기를 주의 깊게 들은 뒤 말했다. \"우리 자신을 위해서가 아니라 그 젊은 숙녀를 위해서 말일세."], ["We must be very cautious.’", "우리는 아주 신중해야 해.\""]], [["‘We!’ said Mr.", "\"우리라고요!\""], ["Winkle, with marked emphasis.", "윈클 씨가 두드러지게 힘주어 말했다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick’s momentary look of indignation at the tone of this remark, subsided into his characteristic expression of benevolence, as he replied--", "이 말의 어조에 픽윅 씨의 얼굴에 잠깐 떠올랐던 분개의 빛은, 이내 그의 특유의 자비로운 표정으로 가라앉았고, 그가 대답했다—"]], [["‘We, Sir!", "\"우리 말일세, 자네!"], ["I shall accompany you.’", "내가 자네와 동행하겠네.\""]], [["‘You!’ said Mr.", "\"선생님이요!\""], ["Winkle.", "윈클 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘I,’ replied Mr.", "\"내가.\""], ["Pickwick mildly.", "픽윅 씨가 온화하게 대답했다."], ["‘In affording you this interview, the young lady has taken a natural, perhaps, but still a very imprudent step.", "\"이 만남을 자네에게 허락함으로써, 그 젊은 숙녀는 어쩌면 자연스러운, 그러나 그래도 무척 경솔한 발걸음을 내디딘 걸세."], ["If I am present at the meeting--a mutual friend, who is old enough to be the father of both parties--the voice of calumny can never be raised against her hereafter.’", "내가 그 만남 자리에 함께 있다면—두 사람 모두의 아버지뻘은 됨 직한 공동의 친구로서 말일세—이후로 그녀를 두고 중상의 목소리가 결코 일지 못할 걸세.\""]], [["Mr. Pickwick’s eyes lightened with honest exultation at his own foresight, as he spoke thus.", "이렇게 말하면서 픽윅 씨의 두 눈은 자신의 선견지명에 대한 정직한 기쁨으로 빛났다."], ["Mr. Winkle was touched by this little trait of his delicate respect for the young protegee of his friend, and took his hand with a feeling of regard, akin to veneration.", "윈클 씨는 친구의 젊은 피보호자에 대한 이 섬세한 존중의 작은 표시에 감동하여, 숭배에 가까운 경애의 마음으로 그의 손을 잡았다."]], [["‘You SHALL go,’ said Mr.", "\"꼭 가셔야 합니다.\""], ["Winkle.", "윈클 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘I will,’ said Mr. Pickwick.", "\"가겠네.\""], ["‘Sam, have my greatcoat and shawl ready, and order a conveyance to be at the door to-morrow evening, rather earlier than is absolutely necessary, in order that we may be in good time.’", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"샘, 내 외투와 숄을 챙겨 두고, 내일 저녁 문 앞에 탈것을 대기시키되, 우리가 넉넉히 시간을 맞출 수 있게 꼭 필요한 시각보다 다소 일찍 오도록 일러 두게.\""]], [["Mr. Weller touched his hat, as an earnest of his obedience, and withdrew to make all needful preparations for the expedition.", "웰러 씨는 복종의 표시로 모자에 손을 갖다 댄 뒤, 원정에 필요한 모든 준비를 하러 물러갔다."]], [["The coach was punctual to the time appointed; and Mr. Weller, after duly installing Mr.", "마차는 정한 시각에 어김없이 도착했다."], ["Pickwick and Mr. Winkle inside, took his seat on the box by the driver.", "웰러 씨는 픽윅 씨와 윈클 씨를 안에 제대로 앉힌 다음, 마부 옆 마부석에 자리를 잡았다."], ["They alighted, as had been agreed on, about a quarter of a mile from the place of rendezvous, and desiring the coachman to await their return, proceeded the remaining distance on foot.", "그들은 약속한 대로 만남의 장소에서 사 분의 일 마일쯤 떨어진 곳에서 내려, 마부에게 자신들이 돌아올 때까지 기다리라고 이른 뒤, 나머지 거리는 걸어서 나아갔다."]], [["It was at this stage of the undertaking that Mr. Pickwick, with many smiles and various other indications of great self-satisfaction, produced from one of his coat pockets a dark lantern, with which he had specially provided himself for the occasion, and the great mechanical beauty of which he proceeded to explain to Mr. Winkle, as they walked along, to the no small surprise of the few stragglers they met.", "일이 이 단계에 이르렀을 때 픽윅 씨는, 숱한 미소와 더불어 대단한 자기만족을 드러내는 갖가지 표시와 함께, 외투 주머니 하나에서 이 일을 위해 특별히 마련해 둔 어둠 등을 꺼내, 그 대단한 기계적 정교함을 길을 가면서 윈클 씨에게 설명하기 시작했으니, 그들이 마주친 몇 안 되는 행인들이 적잖이 놀란 것은 말할 것도 없었다."]], [["‘I should have been the better for something of this kind, in my last garden expedition, at night; eh, Sam?’ said Mr.", "* 어둠 등: 빛을 한 방향으로만 비추고 가릴 수 있게 만든 휴대용 등. \"지난번 밤중에 정원 원정을 갔을 때 이런 게 있었으면 좋았을 텐데 말이야."], ["Pickwick, looking good- humouredly round at his follower, who was trudging behind.", "안 그런가, 샘?\" 픽윅 씨가 뒤에서 터덜터덜 따라오는 하인을 기분 좋게 돌아보며 말했다."]], [["‘Wery nice things, if they’re managed properly, Sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"제대로만 다루면 아주 좋은 물건이죠, 나리.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다."], ["Weller; ‘but wen you don’t want to be seen, I think they’re more useful arter the candle’s gone out, than wen it’s alight.’", "\"하지만 남 눈에 안 띄고 싶을 땐, 제 생각엔 불이 켜져 있을 때보다 촛불이 꺼진 다음에 더 쓸모가 있을 것 같은데요.\""]], [["Mr. Pickwick appeared struck by Sam’s remarks, for he put the lantern into his pocket again, and they walked on in silence.", "픽윅 씨는 샘의 말에 적잖이 마음이 찔린 듯, 등을 도로 주머니에 집어넣었고, 두 사람은 말없이 걸어갔다."]], [["‘Down here, Sir,’ said Sam.", "\"이쪽으로 내려가시죠, 나리.\""], ["‘Let me lead the way.", "샘이 말했다. \"제가 앞장서겠습니다."], ["This is the lane, Sir.’", "이게 그 골목입니다, 나리.\""]], [["Down the lane they went, and dark enough it was. Mr.", "그들은 골목을 내려갔는데, 골목은 충분히 어두웠다."], ["Pickwick brought out the lantern, once or twice, as they groped their way along, and threw a very brilliant little tunnel of light before them, about a foot in diameter.", "픽윅 씨가 더듬더듬 길을 헤쳐 가며 한두 차례 등을 꺼내, 지름이 한 자쯤 되는 아주 환한 빛의 작은 터널을 앞쪽으로 비추었다."], ["It was very pretty to look at, but seemed to have the effect of rendering surrounding objects rather darker than before.", "보기에는 무척 예뻤으나, 주위의 사물들을 도리어 전보다 더 어둡게 만드는 효과가 있는 듯했다."]], [["At length they arrived at the large stone.", "마침내 그들은 큼직한 돌에 이르렀다."], ["Here Sam recommended his master and Mr. Winkle to seat themselves, while he reconnoitred, and ascertained whether Mary was yet in waiting.", "여기서 샘은 주인과 윈클 씨에게 앉아 있으라고 권하고, 자신은 정찰을 나가 메리가 벌써 기다리고 있는지 확인했다."]], [["After an absence of five or ten minutes, Sam returned to say that the gate was opened, and all quiet.", "오 분인지 십 분인지 자리를 비운 뒤, 샘은 돌아와 문이 열렸고 모든 게 조용하다고 알렸다."], ["Following him with stealthy tread, Mr. Pickwick and Mr.", "그를 따라 살금살금 발을 옮겨, 픽윅 씨와 윈클 씨는 이내 정원 안에 들어서 있었다."], ["Winkle soon found themselves in the garden.", "여기서 모두가 \"쉿!\""], ["Here everybody said, ‘Hush!’ a good many times; and that being done, no one seemed to have any very distinct apprehension of what was to be done next.", "하고 여러 차례 말했고, 그러고 나자 다음에 무엇을 해야 할지에 대해서는 아무도 그다지 또렷한 생각이 없는 듯했다."]], [["‘Is Miss Allen in the garden yet, Mary?’ inquired Mr.", "\"앨런 양이 벌써 정원에 나와 있나, 메리?\""], ["Winkle, much agitated.", "윈클 씨가 몹시 안절부절못하며 물었다."]], [["‘I don’t know, sir,’ replied the pretty housemaid.", "\"모르겠어요, 나리.\""], ["‘The best thing to be done, sir, will be for Mr.", "예쁜 하녀가 대답했다."], ["Weller to give you a hoist up into the tree, and perhaps Mr. Pickwick will have the goodness to see that nobody comes up the lane, while I watch at the other end of the garden.", "\"제일 좋은 방법은요, 나리, 웰러 씨가 나리를 나무 위로 밀어 올려 드리고, 픽윅 씨께서는 친절하게 골목으로 아무도 안 오는지 봐 주시고, 그동안 제가 정원 저쪽 끝에서 망을 보는 거예요."], ["Goodness gracious, what’s that?’", "어머나 세상에, 저게 뭐예요?\""]], [["‘That ‘ere blessed lantern ‘ull be the death on us all,’ exclaimed Sam peevishly.", "\"저 망할 놈의 등 때문에 우리 다 죽게 생겼네.\" 샘이 짜증스럽게 외쳤다."], ["‘Take care wot you’re a-doin’ on, sir; you’re a-sendin’ a blaze o’ light, right into the back parlour winder.’", "\"뭘 하시는 건지 조심하세요, 나리. 뒷방 창문으로 곧장 한바탕 빛을 쏘아 보내고 계시잖아요.\""]], [["‘Dear me!’ said Mr.", "\"이런!\""], ["Pickwick, turning hastily aside, ‘I didn’t mean to do that.’", "픽윅 씨가 황급히 옆으로 돌아서며 말했다. \"그럴 생각은 아니었는데.\""]], [["‘Now, it’s in the next house, sir,’ remonstrated Sam.", "\"이번엔 옆집으로 가는데요, 나리.\" 샘이 항의했다."]], [["‘Bless my heart!’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"맙소사!\""], ["Pickwick, turning round again.", "픽윅 씨가 다시 빙 돌아서며 외쳤다."]], [["‘Now, it’s in the stable, and they’ll think the place is afire,’ said Sam.", "\"이번엔 마구간이에요, 불난 줄 알겠어요.\" 샘이 말했다."], ["‘Shut it up, sir, can’t you?’", "\"그거 좀 닫으시라니까요, 나리, 못 닫으세요?\""]], [["‘It’s the most extraordinary lantern I ever met with, in all my life!’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"내 평생 만나 본 중에 가장 별난 등이로구먼!\""], ["Pickwick, greatly bewildered by the effects he had so unintentionally produced.", "픽윅 씨가, 자신이 그토록 본의 아니게 만들어 낸 효과에 크게 어리둥절해하며 외쳤다."], ["‘I never saw such a powerful reflector.’", "\"이렇게 강력한 반사경은 본 적이 없네.\""]], [["‘It’ll be vun too powerful for us, if you keep blazin’ avay in that manner, sir,’ replied Sam, as Mr.", "\"그렇게 계속 빛을 쏘아 대시다간 우리한테 너무 과하게 강력한 물건이 되겠는데요, 나리.\""], ["Pickwick, after various unsuccessful efforts, managed to close the slide.", "픽윅 씨가 갖은 헛수고 끝에 가까스로 덮개를 닫는 동안 샘이 대답했다."], ["‘There’s the young lady’s footsteps.", "\"아가씨 발소리가 나네요."], ["Now, Mr. Winkle, sir, up vith you.’", "자, 윈클 씨, 나리, 올라가시죠.\""]], [["‘Stop, stop!’ said Mr.", "\"잠깐, 잠깐!\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick, ‘I must speak to her first.", "\"내가 먼저 그녀와 이야기를 해야겠네."], ["Help me up, Sam.’", "좀 밀어 올려 주게, 샘.\""]], [["‘Gently, Sir,’ said Sam, planting his head against the wall, and making a platform of his back.", "\"살살요, 나리.\" 샘이 자기 머리를 담장에 대고 등으로 발판을 만들며 말했다."], ["‘Step atop o’ that ‘ere flower-pot, Sir.", "\"저기 저 화분 위에 발을 디디세요, 나리."], ["Now then, up vith you.’", "자, 그럼 올라가시죠.\""]], [["‘I’m afraid I shall hurt you, Sam,’ said Mr.", "\"내가 자네를 다치게 할까 봐 걱정이네, 샘.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Never mind me, Sir,’ replied Sam.", "\"저는 신경 쓰지 마세요, 나리.\" 샘이 대답했다."], ["‘Lend him a hand, Mr. Winkle, sir.", "\"손 좀 빌려주세요, 윈클 씨, 나리."], ["Steady, sir, steady!", "가만히, 나리, 가만히!"], ["That’s the time o’ day!’", "그렇지, 바로 그겁니다!\""]], [["As Sam spoke, Mr. Pickwick, by exertions almost supernatural in a gentleman of his years and weight, contrived to get upon Sam’s back; and Sam gently raising himself up, and Mr.", "샘이 말하는 동안, 픽윅 씨는 그 나이와 몸무게의 신사로서는 거의 초인적인 분투 끝에 가까스로 샘의 등에 올라탔다."], ["Pickwick holding on fast by the top of the wall, while Mr. Winkle clasped him tight by the legs, they contrived by these means to bring his spectacles just above the level of the coping.", "샘이 가만히 몸을 일으키고, 픽윅 씨가 담장 꼭대기를 단단히 붙잡고, 그동안 윈클 씨가 그의 두 다리를 꽉 끌어안아, 이런 방법으로 그들은 그의 안경을 담장 갓돌 높이 바로 위까지 올려놓는 데 성공했다."]], [["‘My dear,’ said Mr.", "\"아가씨.\""], ["Pickwick, looking over the wall, and catching sight of Arabella, on the other side, ‘don’t be frightened, my dear, it’s only me.’ ‘Oh, pray go away, Mr.", "픽윅 씨가 담장 너머를 넘겨다보다가 반대편에 있는 아라벨라를 발견하고 말했다. \"겁내지 마세요, 아가씨, 저뿐입니다.\""], ["Pickwick,’ said Arabella.", "\"오, 제발 가세요, 픽윅 씨.\""], ["‘Tell them all to go away.", "아라벨라가 말했다. \"다들 가라고 하세요."], ["I am so dreadfully frightened.", "저는 너무너무 무서워요."], ["Dear, dear Mr. Pickwick, don’t stop there.", "친애하는, 친애하는 픽윅 씨, 거기 계시지 마세요."], ["You’ll fall down and kill yourself, I know you will.’", "떨어져서 목숨을 잃으실 거예요, 분명히 그러실 거예요.\""]], [["‘Now, pray don’t alarm yourself, my dear,’ said Mr.", "\"자, 제발 놀라지 마세요, 아가씨.\""], ["Pickwick soothingly.", "픽윅 씨가 달래듯 말했다."], ["‘There is not the least cause for fear, I assure you.", "\"조금도 두려워할 까닭이 없습니다, 정말입니다."], ["Stand firm, Sam,’ said Mr.", "단단히 버텨 주게, 샘.\""], ["Pickwick, looking down.", "픽윅 씨가 아래를 내려다보며 말했다."]], [["‘All right, sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"괜찮습니다, 나리.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다."], ["Weller. ‘Don’t be longer than you can conweniently help, sir.", "\"편하게 봐주실 수 있는 것보다 더 오래 걸리진 마세요, 나리."], ["You’re rayther heavy.’", "좀 무거우시거든요.\""]], [["‘Only another moment, Sam,’ replied Mr.", "\"잠깐이면 되네, 샘.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘I merely wished you to know, my dear, that I should not have allowed my young friend to see you in this clandestine way, if the situation in which you are placed had left him any alternative; and, lest the impropriety of this step should cause you any uneasiness, my love, it may be a satisfaction to you, to know that I am present.", "\"그저 아가씨가 알아 두셨으면 해서요. 아가씨가 처한 처지가 제 젊은 친구에게 다른 선택의 여지를 남겨 두지 않았더라면, 저는 그가 이렇게 은밀한 방식으로 아가씨를 만나도록 결코 허락하지 않았을 겁니다."], ["That’s all, my dear.’", "그리고 이 발걸음의 온당치 못함이 아가씨에게 어떤 불편함을 끼치지 않도록, 내 사랑, 제가 함께 있다는 것을 아시는 게 위안이 될 듯합니다. 그게 전부입니다, 아가씨.\""]], [["‘Indeed, Mr. Pickwick, I am very much obliged to you for your kindness and consideration,’ replied Arabella, drying her tears with her handkerchief.", "\"정말이지, 픽윅 씨, 친절과 배려에 무척 감사드려요.\""], ["She would probably have said much more, had not Mr.", "아라벨라가 손수건으로 눈물을 닦으며 대답했다."], ["Pickwick’s head disappeared with great swiftness, in consequence of a false step on Sam’s shoulder which brought him suddenly to the ground.", "그녀는 아마 훨씬 더 많은 말을 했을 테지만, 샘의 어깨를 잘못 디딘 탓에 픽윅 씨가 갑자기 땅바닥으로 떨어지면서 그의 머리가 대단히 잽싸게 사라져 버렸다."], ["He was up again in an instant however; and bidding Mr.", "그러나 그는 순식간에 다시 일어났다."], ["Winkle make haste and get the interview over, ran out into the lane to keep watch, with all the courage and ardour of youth.", "그러고는 윈클 씨에게 어서 서둘러 만남을 끝내라 이르고는, 젊은이다운 온갖 용기와 열의로 골목으로 달려 나가 망을 보았다."], ["Mr. Winkle himself, inspired by the occasion, was on the wall in a moment, merely pausing to request Sam to be careful of his master.", "윈클 씨 본인은 이 상황에 고무되어 눈 깜짝할 새 담장 위에 올라가, 다만 샘에게 주인을 잘 보살피라고 부탁하느라 잠시 멈추었을 뿐이었다."]], [["‘I’ll take care on him, sir,’ replied Sam.", "\"제가 잘 모실 테니까요, 나리.\" 샘이 대답했다."], ["‘Leave him to me.’", "\"저한테 맡기세요.\""]], [["‘Where is he?", "\"그분은 어디 계셔?"], ["What’s he doing, Sam?’ inquired Mr.", "뭘 하고 계시지, 샘?\""], ["Winkle.", "윈클 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘Bless his old gaiters,’ rejoined Sam, looking out at the garden door.", "\"그 양반 낡은 각반에 복이 깃들기를.\" 샘이 정원 문밖을 내다보며 대꾸했다."], ["‘He’s a-keepin’ guard in the lane vith that ‘ere dark lantern, like a amiable Guy Fawkes!", "\"저 어둠 등을 들고 골목에서 망을 보고 계시네요, 무슨 상냥한 가이 포크스처럼 말이죠!"], ["I never see such a fine creetur in my days.", "내 평생 저렇게 훌륭한 양반은 본 적이 없어요."], ["Blessed if I don’t think his heart must ha’ been born five-and-twenty year arter his body, at least!’", "분명코 저분 심장은 적어도 몸뚱이보다 스물다섯 해는 늦게 태어났을 거라니까!\""]], [["Mr. Winkle stayed not to hear the encomium upon his friend. He had dropped from the wall; thrown himself at Arabella’s feet; and by this time was pleading the sincerity of his passion with an eloquence worthy even of Mr. Pickwick himself.", "* 가이 포크스: 1605년 영국 의회 폭파를 꾀한 화약 음모 사건의 인물로, 등불을 들고 지하실에 숨어 있던 모습으로 흔히 그려진다."]], [["While these things were going on in the open air, an elderly gentleman of scientific attainments was seated in his library, two or three houses off, writing a philosophical treatise, and ever and anon moistening his clay and his labours with a glass of claret from a venerable-looking bottle which stood by his side.", "윈클 씨는 친구에 대한 그 찬사를 끝까지 듣고 있지 않았다. 그는 이미 담장에서 뛰어내려, 아라벨라의 발치에 몸을 던졌고, 이즈음에는 픽윅 씨 본인에게도 손색없을 만한 웅변으로 자신의 정열이 진실함을 호소하고 있었다."], ["In the agonies of composition, the elderly gentleman looked sometimes at the carpet, sometimes at the ceiling, and sometimes at the wall; and when neither carpet, ceiling, nor wall afforded the requisite degree of inspiration, he looked out of the window.", "이런 일들이 야외에서 벌어지고 있는 동안, 두세 집 떨어진 곳에서는 과학적 소양을 갖춘 한 노신사가 서재에 앉아 철학 논문을 쓰면서, 이따금 곁에 놓인 고풍스러워 보이는 병에서 클라레 한 잔을 따라 자기 흙덩이며 노고를 적시고 있었다. 글쓰기의 고통 속에서 노신사는 때로는 양탄자를, 때로는 천장을, 때로는 벽을 바라보았고, 양탄자도 천장도 벽도 필요한 만큼의 영감을 주지 못하면, 창밖을 내다보았다."]], [["In one of these pauses of invention, the scientific gentleman was gazing abstractedly on the thick darkness outside, when he was very much surprised by observing a most brilliant light glide through the air, at a short distance above the ground, and almost instantaneously vanish.", "이렇게 착상이 멎은 어느 한순간, 그 과학적 신사가 바깥의 짙은 어둠을 멍하니 응시하고 있었는데, 지면에서 조금 떨어진 높이로 더없이 환한 빛 하나가 공중을 미끄러져 지나가더니 거의 순식간에 사라지는 것을 보고 그는 몹시 놀랐다."], ["After a short time the phenomenon was repeated, not once or twice, but several times; at last the scientific gentleman, laying down his pen, began to consider to what natural causes these appearances were to be assigned.", "잠시 뒤 그 현상이 되풀이되었는데, 한두 번이 아니라 여러 번이었다. 마침내 그 과학적 신사는 펜을 내려놓고, 이 출현들을 어떤 자연적 원인에 돌려야 할지 궁리하기 시작했다."]], [["They were not meteors; they were too low.", "그것들은 유성이 아니었다. 너무 낮았다."], ["They were not glow-worms; they were too high.", "반딧불이도 아니었다. 너무 높았다."], ["They were not will-o’-the-wisps; they were not fireflies; they were not fireworks.", "도깨비불도 아니었고, 개똥벌레도 아니었으며, 불꽃놀이도 아니었다."], ["What could they be?", "대체 무엇일 수 있단 말인가?"], ["Some extraordinary and wonderful phenomenon of nature, which no philosopher had ever seen before; something which it had been reserved for him alone to discover, and which he should immortalise his name by chronicling for the benefit of posterity.", "어떤 철학자도 일찍이 본 적 없는, 자연의 어떤 비범하고 경이로운 현상, 오직 그 혼자만이 발견하도록 남겨져 있었던 무엇이며, 후세의 이익을 위해 그것을 기록함으로써 자신의 이름을 불멸케 할 무엇이었다."], ["Full of this idea, the scientific gentleman seized his pen again, and committed to paper sundry notes of these unparalleled appearances, with the date, day, hour, minute, and precise second at which they were visible: all of which were to form the data of a voluminous treatise of great research and deep learning, which should astonish all the atmospherical wiseacres that ever drew breath in any part of the civilised globe.", "이 생각에 사로잡혀, 그 과학적 신사는 다시 펜을 집어 들고, 이 유례없는 출현들에 관한 갖가지 기록을, 그것들이 보인 날짜와 요일, 시각, 분, 그리고 정확한 초까지 곁들여 종이에 적어 두었다. 이 모든 것이, 문명 세계 어느 구석에서든 일찍이 숨 쉬어 본 적 있는 모든 대기학 풋내기 학자들을 깜짝 놀라게 할, 방대한 연구와 깊은 학식이 담긴 논문의 자료가 될 터였다."]], [["He threw himself back in his easy-chair, wrapped in contemplations of his future greatness.", "그는 안락의자에 등을 기댄 채 장차 자신이 누릴 위대함에 대한 상념에 잠겼다."], ["The mysterious light appeared more brilliantly than before, dancing, to all appearance, up and down the lane, crossing from side to side, and moving in an orbit as eccentric as comets themselves.", "그 신비로운 빛은 전보다 더 찬란하게 나타나, 보아하니 골목을 오르내리며 춤추듯, 이쪽저쪽을 가로지르며, 혜성 그 자체만큼이나 변덕스러운 궤도를 그리며 움직였다."]], [["The scientific gentleman was a bachelor.", "그 과학적 신사는 독신이었다."], ["He had no wife to call in and astonish, so he rang the bell for his servant.", "불러들여 놀라게 해 줄 아내가 없었던지라, 그는 종을 울려 하인을 불렀다."]], [["‘Pruffle,’ said the scientific gentleman, ‘there is something very extraordinary in the air to-night? Did you see that?’ said the scientific gentleman, pointing out of the window, as the light again became visible.", "\"프러플.\" 과학적 신사가 말했다. \"오늘 밤 공중에 아주 비범한 무언가가 있네. 자네도 저걸 봤나?\" 빛이 다시 보이게 되자 그가 창밖을 가리키며 말했다."]], [["‘Yes, I did, Sir.’", "\"네, 봤습니다, 나리.\""]], [["‘What do you think of it, Pruffle?’", "\"저게 뭐라고 생각하나, 프러플?\""]], [["‘Think of it, Sir?’", "\"저걸 뭐라고 생각하느냐고요, 나리?\""]], [["‘Yes.", "\"그래."], ["You have been bred up in this country.", "자네는 이 고장에서 나고 자랐지 않은가."], ["What should you say was the cause for those lights, now?’", "저 빛들의 원인이 무엇이라고 말하겠나, 어디?\""]], [["The scientific gentleman smilingly anticipated Pruffle’s reply that he could assign no cause for them at all.", "그 과학적 신사는, 자기로서는 그 원인을 도무지 댈 수 없노라는 프러플의 대답을 미소 지으며 미리 예상하고 있었다."], ["Pruffle meditated.", "프러플은 곰곰이 생각했다."]], [["‘I should say it was thieves, Sir,’ said Pruffle at length.", "\"제 생각엔 도둑들 같습니다, 나리.\" 마침내 프러플이 말했다."]], [["‘You’re a fool, and may go downstairs,’ said the scientific gentleman.", "\"자네는 바보일세, 내려가도 좋네.\" 과학적 신사가 말했다."]], [["‘Thank you, Sir,’ said Pruffle.", "\"고맙습니다, 나리.\" 프러플이 말했다."], ["And down he went.", "그러고는 내려갔다."]], [["But the scientific gentleman could not rest under the idea of the ingenious treatise he had projected being lost to the world, which must inevitably be the case if the speculation of the ingenious Mr. Pruffle were not stifled in its birth.", "그러나 그 과학적 신사는, 자신이 구상한 그 기발한 논문이 세상에서 사라져 버릴지 모른다는 생각에 가만히 있을 수가 없었으니, 기발한 프러플 씨의 추측이 그 싹부터 짓눌리지 않는다면 그런 일은 반드시 벌어지고 말 터였다."], ["He put on his hat and walked quickly down the garden, determined to investigate the matter to the very bottom.", "그는 모자를 쓰고 정원으로 빠르게 걸어 내려가, 이 문제를 끝까지 파헤쳐 보기로 작정했다."]], [["Now, shortly before the scientific gentleman walked out into the garden, Mr. Pickwick had run down the lane as fast as he could, to convey a false alarm that somebody was coming that way; occasionally drawing back the slide of the dark lantern to keep himself from the ditch.", "그런데 그 과학적 신사가 정원으로 나오기 얼마 전, 픽윅 씨는 누군가 그쪽으로 오고 있다는 거짓 경보를 전하려고 골목을 있는 힘껏 달려 내려왔으니, 도랑에 빠지지 않으려고 이따금 어둠 등의 덮개를 도로 열었던 것이다."], ["The alarm was no sooner given, than Mr.", "경보가 떨어지기가 무섭게 윈클 씨는 담장을 기어 넘어 돌아왔고 아라벨라는 집 안으로 달려 들어갔다."], ["Winkle scrambled back over the wall, and Arabella ran into the house; the garden gate was shut, and the three adventurers were making the best of their way down the lane, when they were startled by the scientific gentleman unlocking his garden gate.", "정원 문이 닫혔고, 세 모험가가 골목을 부지런히 내려가던 차에, 그 과학적 신사가 자기 정원 문을 따는 바람에 그들은 흠칫 놀랐다."]], [["‘Hold hard,’ whispered Sam, who was, of course, the first of the party.", "\"멈추세요.\" 물론 일행 중 맨 앞에 있던 샘이 속삭였다."], ["‘Show a light for just vun second, Sir.’", "\"딱 일 초만 불을 비춰 주세요, 나리.\""]], [["Mr. Pickwick did as he was desired, and Sam, seeing a man’s head peeping out very cautiously within half a yard of his own, gave it a gentle tap with his clenched fist, which knocked it, with a hollow sound, against the gate. Having performed this feat with great suddenness and dexterity, Mr. Weller caught Mr. Pickwick up on his back, and followed Mr. Winkle down the lane at a pace which, considering the burden he carried, was perfectly astonishing.", "픽윅 씨가 시키는 대로 하자, 샘은 자기 머리에서 반 야드도 안 되는 곳에서 한 남자의 머리가 무척 조심스럽게 빼꼼 내미는 것을 보고는, 그것을 꽉 쥔 주먹으로 가볍게 한 대 쳤으니, 그 머리는 텅 빈 소리를 내며 문에 부딪쳤다. 이 묘기를 대단히 갑작스럽고 능숙하게 해치운 뒤, 웰러 씨는 픽윅 씨를 들쳐 업고, 짊어진 짐을 생각하면 그야말로 놀라운 속도로 윈클 씨를 뒤따라 골목을 내려갔다."]], [["‘Have you got your vind back agin, Sir,’ inquired Sam, when they had reached the end.", "\"숨은 다시 돌아오셨나요, 나리?\" 골목 끝에 다다랐을 때 샘이 물었다."]], [["‘Quite. Quite, now,’ replied Mr.", "\"아주, 아주 좋아졌네, 이제.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘Then come along, Sir,’ said Sam, setting his master on his feet again.", "\"그럼 가시죠, 나리.\" 샘이 주인을 다시 발로 서게 하며 말했다."], ["‘Come betveen us, sir.", "\"우리 사이로 들어오세요, 나리."], ["Not half a mile to run.", "달릴 거리는 반 마일도 안 됩니다."], ["Think you’re vinnin’ a cup, sir.", "우승컵을 따는 중이라고 생각하세요, 나리."], ["Now for it.’", "자, 갑니다.\""]], [["Thus encouraged, Mr. Pickwick made the very best use of his legs.", "이렇게 격려받아, 픽윅 씨는 자기 두 다리를 더없이 잘 놀렸다."], ["It may be confidently stated that a pair of black gaiters never got over the ground in better style than did those of Mr. Pickwick on this memorable occasion.", "그 기억할 만한 날 픽윅 씨의 검정 각반보다 더 멋진 모양새로 땅을 가로질러 본 검정 각반은 일찍이 없었노라고 자신 있게 말할 수 있다."]], [["The coach was waiting, the horses were fresh, the roads were good, and the driver was willing.", "마차가 기다리고 있었고, 말들은 기운이 넘쳤으며, 길은 좋았고, 마부는 기꺼이 나섰다."], ["The whole party arrived in safety at the Bush before Mr. Pickwick had recovered his breath.", "일행 전원은 픽윅 씨가 숨을 채 고르기도 전에 무사히 부시 여관에 도착했다."]], [["‘In with you at once, sir,’ said Sam, as he helped his master out.", "\"당장 안으로 들어가시죠, 나리.\" 샘이 주인을 부축해 내리며 말했다."], ["‘Don’t stop a second in the street, arter that ‘ere exercise.", "\"그렇게 운동을 하신 뒤에 길거리에서 일 초도 멈춰 계시면 안 됩니다."], ["Beg your pardon, sir,’ continued Sam, touching his hat as Mr.", "실례합니다, 나리.\" 윈클 씨가 내리자 샘이 모자에 손을 갖다 대며 말을 이었다."], ["Winkle descended, ‘hope there warn’t a priory ‘tachment, sir?’", "\"옛정 운운하는 거리낌 같은 건 없었기를 바랍니다, 나리?\""]], [["Mr.", "윈클 씨는 자신의 미천한 친구의 손을 꽉 잡고는 그의 귀에 대고 속삭였다."], ["Winkle grasped his humble friend by the hand, and whispered in his ear, ‘It’s all right, Sam; quite right.’ Upon which Mr.", "\"다 잘됐네, 샘. 아주 잘됐어.\""], ["Weller struck three distinct blows upon his nose in token of intelligence, smiled, winked, and proceeded to put the steps up, with a countenance expressive of lively satisfaction.", "이에 웰러 씨는 알았다는 표시로 자기 코를 또렷하게 세 번 두드리고, 미소 짓고, 한쪽 눈을 찡긋한 다음, 활기찬 만족이 어린 얼굴로 발판을 올리기 시작했다."]], [["As to the scientific gentleman, he demonstrated, in a masterly treatise, that these wonderful lights were the effect of electricity; and clearly proved the same by detailing how a flash of fire danced before his eyes when he put his head out of the gate, and how he received a shock which stunned him for a quarter of an hour afterwards; which demonstration delighted all the scientific associations beyond measure, and caused him to be considered a light of science ever afterwards.", "그 과학적 신사로 말하자면, 그는 한 편의 명저에서 이 경이로운 빛들이 전기의 작용이었음을 증명해 보였고, 자신이 문밖으로 머리를 내밀었을 때 한 줄기 불꽃이 눈앞에서 춤추던 일이며, 그 뒤로 십오 분 동안 자신을 멍하게 만든 충격을 받은 일을 상세히 서술함으로써 그것을 명백히 입증했다. 이 논증은 모든 과학 협회를 더없이 기쁘게 했고, 이후로 그를 과학의 등불로 여겨지게 만들었다."]], [["CHAPTER XL. INTRODUCES MR. PICKWICK TO A NEW AND NOT UNINTERESTING SCENE IN THE GREAT DRAMA OF LIFE\nThe remainder of the period which Mr. Pickwick had assigned as the duration of the stay at Bath passed over without the occurrence of anything material. Trinity term commenced. On the expiration of its first week, Mr. Pickwick and his friends returned to London; and the former gentleman, attended of course by Sam, straightway repaired to his old quarters at the George and Vulture.", "픽윅 씨가 배스에 머물기로 정해 두었던 기간의 나머지는 별다른 일 없이 지나갔다. 삼위일체 개정기가 시작되었다. 그 첫 주가 끝나자 픽윅 씨와 그의 친구들은 런던으로 돌아왔고, 픽윅 씨는 물론 샘을 거느리고 곧장 조지 앤드 벌처에 있는 그의 옛 숙소로 향했다."]], [["On the third morning after their arrival, just as all the clocks in the city were striking nine individually, and somewhere about nine hundred and ninety-nine collectively, Sam was taking the air in George Yard, when a queer sort of fresh-painted vehicle drove up, out of which there jumped with great agility, throwing the reins to a stout man who sat beside him, a queer sort of gentleman, who seemed made for the vehicle, and the vehicle for him.", "* 삼위일체 개정기: 영국 법원의 개정 기간 중 하나로, 삼위일체 주일 이후에 열렸다. 그들이 도착한 지 사흘째 되는 아침, 시내의 모든 시계가 저마다 아홉 시를 치고, 다 합치면 어림잡아 구백구십아홉 번쯤 울리고 있을 때, 샘이 조지 야드에서 바람을 쐬고 있는데, 갓 칠한 듯한 묘한 종류의 마차 한 대가 다가오더니, 그 안에서 무척 날렵하게 한 사람이 뛰어내리며 옆자리에 앉아 있던 건장한 사내에게 고삐를 던졌으니, 그는 마차를 위해 만들어진 듯하고 마차는 그를 위해 만들어진 듯한, 묘한 종류의 신사였다."]], [["The vehicle was not exactly a gig, neither was it a stanhope.", "그 마차는 정확히 말해 이륜마차도 아니었고, 스탠호프도 아니었다."], ["It was not what is currently denominated a dog-cart, neither was it a taxed cart, nor a chaise-cart, nor a guillotined cabriolet; and yet it had something of the character of each and every of these machines.", "흔히 말하는 도그카트도 아니었고, 과세 짐마차도 아니었으며, 짐수레도 아니었고, 위쪽이 잘려 나간 이인승 마차도 아니었다. 그러면서도 이 모든 기계 하나하나의 특징을 조금씩 지니고 있었다."], ["It was painted a bright yellow, with the shafts and wheels picked out in black; and the driver sat in the orthodox sporting style, on cushions piled about two feet above the rail.", "그것은 밝은 노란색으로 칠해져 있었고, 끌채와 바퀴는 검은색으로 도드라지게 그려져 있었다. 마부는 정통 스포츠 양식으로, 난간보다 두 자쯤 위로 쌓아 올린 방석 위에 앉아 있었다."], ["The horse was a bay, a well-looking animal enough; but with something of a flash and dog-fighting air about him, nevertheless, which accorded both with the vehicle and his master.", "말은 적갈색으로, 제법 잘생긴 짐승이었으나, 그럼에도 어딘가 번지르르하고 투견판 분위기 같은 것이 풍겼으니, 그것은 마차와 그 주인 양쪽에 다 어울렸다."]], [["The master himself was a man of about forty, with black hair, and carefully combed whiskers.", "주인 자신은 마흔쯤 된 사내로, 검은 머리에 정성껏 빗어 넘긴 구레나룻을 기르고 있었다."], ["He was dressed in a particularly gorgeous manner, with plenty of articles of jewellery about him--all about three sizes larger than those which are usually worn by gentlemen--and a rough greatcoat to crown the whole.", "그는 유난히 화려한 차림이었고, 몸에는 보석붙이를 잔뜩 달고 있었는데—모두 신사들이 보통 차고 다니는 것보다 세 치수쯤 큰 것들이었다—그 모든 것 위에 거친 외투를 걸쳐 마무리를 지었다."], ["Into one pocket of this greatcoat, he thrust his left hand the moment he dismounted, while from the other he drew forth, with his right, a very bright and glaring silk handkerchief, with which he whisked a speck or two of dust from his boots, and then, crumpling it in his hand, swaggered up the court.", "그는 마차에서 내리자마자 이 외투의 한쪽 주머니에 왼손을 찔러 넣고, 다른 주머니에서 오른손으로 무척 밝고 번쩍이는 비단 손수건을 꺼내, 그것으로 자기 장화에서 먼지 한두 점을 털어 내더니, 그것을 손안에 구겨 쥐고는 으스대며 안마당으로 걸어 올라갔다."]], [["It had not escaped Sam’s attention that, when this person dismounted, a shabby-looking man in a brown greatcoat shorn of divers buttons, who had been previously slinking about, on the opposite side of the way, crossed over, and remained stationary close by.", "이 사람이 마차에서 내릴 때, 앞서 길 건너편에서 어슬렁거리며 단추가 여럿 떨어져 나간 갈색 외투를 입은 초라한 행색의 사내 하나가 길을 건너와 바로 곁에 멈춰 선 것이 샘의 눈을 벗어나지는 않았다."], ["Having something more than a suspicion of the object of the gentleman’s visit, Sam preceded him to the George and Vulture, and, turning sharp round, planted himself in the centre of the doorway.", "그 신사가 찾아온 목적에 대해 의심을 넘어선 무엇을 품고 있던 샘은 그보다 앞서 조지 앤드 벌처로 들어가, 홱 돌아서서 문간 한가운데에 떡 버티고 섰다."]], [["‘Now, my fine fellow!’ said the man in the rough coat, in an imperious tone, attempting at the same time to push his way past.", "\"이봐, 젊은이!\" 거친 외투를 입은 사내가 위압적인 어조로 말하면서, 동시에 밀치고 지나가려 했다."]], [["‘Now, Sir, wot’s the matter?’ replied Sam, returning the push with compound interest.", "\"이봐요, 선생, 무슨 일이슈?\" 샘이 그 떠밂을 이자까지 쳐서 되갚으며 대꾸했다."]], [["‘Come, none of this, my man; this won’t do with me,’ said the owner of the rough coat, raising his voice, and turning white.", "\"이봐, 그만두게, 이 사람아. 나한테는 그게 안 통해.\""], ["‘Here, Smouch!’", "거친 외투의 임자가 목소리를 높이며 새파랗게 질려 말했다. \"여기, 스마우치!\""]], [["‘Well, wot’s amiss here?’ growled the man in the brown coat, who had been gradually sneaking up the court during this short dialogue.", "\"왜, 무슨 일이오?\" 갈색 외투를 입은 사내가 으르렁거리며 말했으니, 그는 이 짧은 대화가 오가는 동안 슬금슬금 안마당으로 다가와 있었다."]], [["‘Only some insolence of this young man’s,’ said the principal, giving Sam another push.", "\"이 젊은 녀석이 좀 무례하게 굴어서 그래.\" 우두머리가 샘을 한 번 더 밀치며 말했다."]], [["‘Come, none o’ this gammon,’ growled Smouch, giving him another, and a harder one.", "\"자, 그딴 수작 부리지 마.\" 스마우치가 으르렁거리며 그를 또 한 번, 그것도 더 세게 밀쳤다."]], [["This last push had the effect which it was intended by the experienced Mr.", "이 마지막 떠밂은 노련한 스마우치 씨가 의도한 대로의 효과를 냈다."], ["Smouch to produce; for while Sam, anxious to return the compliment, was grinding that gentleman’s body against the door-post, the principal crept past, and made his way to the bar, whither Sam, after bandying a few epithetical remarks with Mr. Smouch, followed at once.", "샘이 그 신사에게 인사를 되갚고 싶어 그자의 몸을 문기둥에 짓이기고 있는 사이, 우두머리가 슬그머니 지나쳐 술청으로 길을 냈으니, 샘은 스마우치 씨와 몇 마디 욕설을 주고받은 뒤 곧장 그 뒤를 따랐다."]], [["‘Good-morning, my dear,’ said the principal, addressing the young lady at the bar, with Botany Bay ease, and New South Wales gentility; ‘which is Mr.", "\"안녕하시오, 아가씨.\" 우두머리가 보터니 만의 느긋함과 뉴사우스웨일스의 점잖음으로 술청에 있는 젊은 여자에게 말을 건넸다."], ["Pickwick’s room, my dear?’", "\"픽윅 씨 방이 어디요, 아가씨?\""]], [["‘Show him up,’ said the barmaid to a waiter, without deigning another look at the exquisite, in reply to his inquiry.", "* 보터니 만, 뉴사우스웨일스: 오스트레일리아의 죄수 유형지로, 화자의 출신을 빈정대는 표현이다."]], [["The waiter led the way upstairs as he was desired, and the man in the rough coat followed, with Sam behind him, who, in his progress up the staircase, indulged in sundry gestures indicative of supreme contempt and defiance, to the unspeakable gratification of the servants and other lookers-on.", "\"안내해 드려요.\""], ["Mr.", "술청 아가씨가 그 멋쟁이의 물음에 답하면서, 그를 두 번 다시 쳐다볼 것도 없다는 듯 웨이터에게 말했다."], ["Smouch, who was troubled with a hoarse cough, remained below, and expectorated in the passage.", "웨이터가 일러 준 대로 앞장서서 위층으로 길을 안내했고, 거친 외투를 입은 사내가 그 뒤를 따랐으며, 샘이 그 뒤를 따랐으니, 샘은 계단을 올라가는 동안 더할 나위 없는 경멸과 도전의 갖가지 몸짓을 마음껏 보여, 하인들과 구경하던 다른 이들을 이루 말할 수 없이 흐뭇하게 했다. 쉰 기침으로 고생하던 스마우치 씨는 아래에 남아 통로에다 가래를 뱉었다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick was fast asleep in bed, when his early visitor, followed by Sam, entered the room.", "이른 방문객이 샘을 뒤따라 방에 들어섰을 때 픽윅 씨는 침대에서 곤히 잠들어 있었다."], ["The noise they made, in so doing, awoke him.", "그들이 그러면서 낸 소리에 그는 잠을 깼다."]], [["‘Shaving-water, Sam,’ said Mr.", "\"면도할 물, 샘.\""], ["Pickwick, from within the curtains.", "픽윅 씨가 휘장 안에서 말했다."]], [["‘Shave you directly, Mr.", "\"당장 면도해 드리지요, 픽윅 씨.\""], ["Pickwick,’ said the visitor, drawing one of them back from the bed’s head.", "방문객이 침대 머리맡의 휘장 하나를 젖히며 말했다."], ["‘I’ve got an execution against you, at the suit of Bardell.--Here’s the warrant.--Common Pleas.--Here’s my card.", "\"당신을 상대로 한 집행 영장을 가지고 왔소, 바델의 소송 건으로요.—여기 영장이오.—민사 법원이오.—여기 내 명함이오."], ["I suppose you’ll come over to my house.’ Giving Mr.", "우리 집으로 가 주셔야겠소이다.\""], ["Pickwick a friendly tap on the shoulder, the sheriff’s officer (for such he was) threw his card on the counterpane, and pulled a gold toothpick from his waistcoat pocket.", "픽윅 씨의 어깨를 다정하게 한 번 툭 치고는, 그 집행관은(그가 바로 그런 사람이었다) 명함을 침대보 위에 던지고 조끼 주머니에서 금이쑤시개를 꺼냈다."]], [["‘Namby’s the name,’ said the sheriff’s deputy, as Mr.", "\"이름은 냄비요.\""], ["Pickwick took his spectacles from under the pillow, and put them on, to read the card.", "픽윅 씨가 베개 밑에서 안경을 꺼내 쓰고 명함을 읽는 동안, 그 집달리가 말했다."], ["‘Namby, Bell Alley, Coleman Street.’", "\"콜먼 가, 벨 골목, 냄비요.\""]], [["At this point, Sam Weller, who had had his eyes fixed hitherto on Mr. Namby’s shining beaver, interfered.", "이 대목에서, 그때까지 냄비 씨의 번쩍이는 비버 모자에 눈을 박고 있던 샘 웰러가 끼어들었다."]], [["‘Are you a Quaker?’ said Sam.", "\"당신, 퀘이커요?\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["‘I’ll let you know I am, before I’ve done with you,’ replied the indignant officer.", "* 퀘이커: 모자를 벗지 않는 관습으로 알려진 종파로, 모자를 쓴 채 있는 냄비를 빈정대는 말이다. \"내가 너랑 끝장을 보기 전에 내가 퀘이커인지 아닌지 알려 주마.\""], ["‘I’ll teach you manners, my fine fellow, one of these fine mornings.’", "분노한 집행관이 대꾸했다. \"조만간 어느 좋은 아침에 너한테 예의범절을 가르쳐 주마, 이 녀석아.\""]], [["‘Thank’ee,’ said Sam.", "\"고맙수.\" 샘이 말했다."], ["‘I’ll do the same to you.", "\"나도 똑같이 해 드리지."], ["Take your hat off.’ With this, Mr.", "모자 벗으시오.\""], ["Weller, in the most dexterous manner, knocked Mr. Namby’s hat to the other side of the room, with such violence, that he had very nearly caused him to swallow the gold toothpick into the bargain.", "이 말과 함께 웰러 씨는 더없이 능란한 솜씨로 냄비 씨의 모자를 방 저쪽으로 후려쳐 날렸으니, 그 기세가 어찌나 거셌던지 하마터면 그가 금이쑤시개까지 덤으로 삼켜 버릴 뻔했다."]], [["‘Observe this, Mr. Pickwick,’ said the disconcerted officer, gasping for breath.", "\"이걸 똑똑히 봐 두시오, 픽윅 씨.\""], ["‘I’ve been assaulted in the execution of my dooty by your servant in your chamber.", "당황한 집행관이 숨을 헐떡이며 말했다. \"내가 당신 방에서 당신 하인에게 직무 집행 중에 폭행을 당했소."], ["I’m in bodily fear.", "신변에 위협을 느끼오."], ["I call you to witness this.’", "이걸 증인으로 서 주시오.\""]], [["‘Don’t witness nothin’, Sir,’ interposed Sam.", "\"아무것도 증언하지 마쇼, 선생.\" 샘이 끼어들었다."], ["‘Shut your eyes up tight, Sir.", "\"눈을 꼭 감으쇼, 선생."], ["I’d pitch him out o’ winder, only he couldn’t fall far enough, ‘cause o’ the leads outside.’", "창밖으로 내던져 버리고 싶지만, 바깥 납지붕 때문에 충분히 멀리 떨어지지도 못할 테니 관두지.\""]], [["‘Sam,’ said Mr.", "\"샘.\""], ["Pickwick, in an angry voice, as his attendant made various demonstrations of hostilities, ‘if you say another word, or offer the slightest interference with this person, I discharge you that instant.’", "픽윅 씨가 그의 시종이 적대적인 갖가지 시위를 벌이자 성난 목소리로 말했다. \"네가 한마디만 더 하거나, 이 사람에게 조금이라도 간섭하려 들면, 그 즉시 너를 내보내겠다.\""]], [["‘But, Sir!’ said Sam.", "\"하지만, 어르신!\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["‘Hold your tongue,’ interposed Mr.", "\"입 다물어라.\" 픽윅 씨가 끼어들었다."], ["Pickwick. ‘Take that hat up again.’", "\"저 모자 도로 주워 드려라.\""]], [["But this Sam flatly and positively refused to do; and, after he had been severely reprimanded by his master, the officer, being in a hurry, condescended to pick it up himself, venting a great variety of threats against Sam meanwhile, which that gentleman received with perfect composure, merely observing that if Mr. Namby would have the goodness to put his hat on again, he would knock it into the latter end of next week.", "그러나 샘은 이를 딱 잘라 단호히 거부했고, 주인에게 호되게 꾸지람을 들은 뒤, 집행관은 바쁜 처지라 황송하게도 손수 모자를 주워 들면서, 그러는 내내 샘에게 온갖 위협을 늘어놓았으니, 그 신사는 그것을 완벽한 침착함으로 받아넘기며, 다만 냄비 씨가 모자를 다시 쓰는 친절을 베풀어만 주신다면 그것을 다음 주 끝물까지 날려 버리겠노라고 한마디 했을 따름이다."], ["Mr. Namby, perhaps thinking that such a process might be productive of inconvenience to himself, declined to offer the temptation, and, soon after, called up Smouch.", "냄비 씨는 그런 절차가 자신에게 불편을 초래할지도 모른다고 생각했는지, 그 유혹을 내밀기를 사양했고, 곧이어 스마우치를 불러올렸다."], ["Having informed him that the capture was made, and that he was to wait for the prisoner until he should have finished dressing, Namby then swaggered out, and drove away.", "체포가 이루어졌으며 죄수가 옷을 다 입을 때까지 기다리고 있으라고 그에게 일러 준 뒤, 냄비는 으스대며 나가 마차를 몰고 떠나 버렸다."], ["Smouch, requesting Mr. Pickwick in a surly manner ‘to be as alive as he could, for it was a busy time,’ drew up a chair by the door and sat there, until he had finished dressing.", "스마우치는 픽윅 씨에게 무뚝뚝한 태도로 \"지금이 바쁜 때이니 되도록 빨리 서두르시오\"라고 청하고는, 문 옆에 의자를 끌어다 놓고, 그가 옷을 다 입을 때까지 거기 앉아 있었다."], ["Sam was then despatched for a hackney-coach, and in it the triumvirate proceeded to Coleman Street.", "그러고 나서 샘은 삯마차를 부르러 보내졌고, 세 사람은 그것을 타고 콜먼 가로 향했다."], ["It was fortunate the distance was short; for Mr. Smouch, besides possessing no very enchanting conversational powers, was rendered a decidedly unpleasant companion in a limited space, by the physical weakness to which we have elsewhere adverted.", "거리가 짧았던 것이 다행이었으니, 스마우치 씨는 그다지 매혹적인 대화 능력을 지니지 못한 데다, 우리가 다른 데서 언급한 바 있는 신체적 약점 탓에 좁은 공간 안에서는 결정적으로 불쾌한 동행이 되었기 때문이다."]], [["The coach having turned into a very narrow and dark street, stopped before a house with iron bars to all the windows; the door-posts of which were graced by the name and title of ‘Namby, Officer to the Sheriffs of London’; the inner gate having been opened by a gentleman who might have passed for a neglected twin-brother of Mr.", "마차는 무척 좁고 어두운 거리로 접어들더니, 창마다 쇠창살이 둘린 집 앞에 멈춰 섰다. 그 집 문기둥은 \"냄비, 런던 주 장관 직속 집행관\"이라는 이름과 직함으로 장식되어 있었다."], ["Smouch, and who was endowed with a large key for the purpose, Mr. Pickwick was shown into the ‘coffee-room.’", "안쪽 문은 스마우치 씨의 방치된 쌍둥이 형제라 해도 통할 만한 신사가 열어 주었는데, 그는 그 목적을 위해 큼직한 열쇠 하나를 지니고 있었으며, 픽윅 씨는 \"커피실\"로 안내되었다."]], [["This coffee-room was a front parlour, the principal features of which were fresh sand and stale tobacco smoke.", "이 커피실은 앞쪽 응접실로, 그 주된 특징이라면 갓 깐 모래와 케케묵은 담배 연기였다."], ["Mr. Pickwick bowed to the three persons who were seated in it when he entered; and having despatched Sam for Perker, withdrew into an obscure corner, and looked thence with some curiosity upon his new companions.", "픽윅 씨는 들어설 때 거기 앉아 있던 세 사람에게 인사를 건넸고, 샘을 퍼커에게 보내고 나서 으슥한 구석으로 물러나, 거기서 다소 호기심 어린 눈으로 자신의 새 동료들을 바라보았다."]], [["One of these was a mere boy of nineteen or twenty, who, though it was yet barely ten o’clock, was drinking gin-and-water, and smoking a cigar- -amusements to which, judging from his inflamed countenance, he had devoted himself pretty constantly for the last year or two of his life.", "이들 가운데 하나는 열아홉이나 스무 살밖에 안 된 한낱 소년이었는데, 아직 열 시도 채 안 되었건만 진토닉을 마시고 시가를 피우고 있었으니—그 벌겋게 달아오른 얼굴로 미루어 보건대, 지난 한두 해 동안 줄곧 이 오락에 꽤나 골몰해 온 모양이었다."], ["Opposite him, engaged in stirring the fire with the toe of his right boot, was a coarse, vulgar young man of about thirty, with a sallow face and harsh voice; evidently possessed of that knowledge of the world, and captivating freedom of manner, which is to be acquired in public-house parlours, and at low billiard tables.", "그 맞은편에서는, 오른쪽 장화 끝으로 불을 쑤석거리고 있는, 누르께한 얼굴에 거친 목소리를 가진 서른쯤 된 상스럽고 천박한 젊은이가 있었으니, 술집 응접실과 싸구려 당구대에서 얻을 수 있는 그런 세상 물정과 사람을 홀리는 거리낌 없는 태도를 분명히 지니고 있었다."], ["The third tenant of the apartment was a middle-aged man in a very old suit of black, who looked pale and haggard, and paced up and down the room incessantly; stopping, now and then, to look with great anxiety out of the window as if he expected somebody, and then resuming his walk.", "그 방의 세 번째 거주자는 무척 낡은 검은 양복을 입은 중년 사내로, 창백하고 초췌한 얼굴로 쉴 새 없이 방 안을 오락가락하다가, 이따금 멈춰 서서 누군가를 기다리기라도 하듯 무척 초조하게 창밖을 내다보고는, 다시 걸음을 이어 갔다."]], [["‘You’d better have the loan of my razor this morning, Mr.", "\"오늘 아침에는 제 면도칼을 빌려 쓰시는 게 좋겠수다, 에어슬리 씨.\""], ["Ayresleigh,’ said the man who was stirring the fire, tipping the wink to his friend the boy.", "불을 쑤석거리던 사내가 그의 친구인 소년에게 눈을 찡긋해 보이며 말했다."]], [["‘Thank you, no, I shan’t want it; I expect I shall be out, in the course of an hour or so,’ replied the other in a hurried manner. Then, walking again up to the window, and once more returning disappointed, he sighed deeply, and left the room; upon which the other two burst into a loud laugh.", "\"고맙소만, 됐소. 필요 없을 거요. 한 시간 남짓이면 나갈 것 같으니까.\" 상대가 다급한 태도로 대꾸했다. 그러고는 다시 창가로 걸어가, 또 한 번 실망한 채 돌아오더니, 깊이 한숨을 내쉬고 방을 나가 버렸다. 그러자 나머지 두 사람은 와락 큰 소리로 웃음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["‘Well, I never saw such a game as that,’ said the gentleman who had offered the razor, whose name appeared to be Price.", "\"이거 원, 저런 꼴은 난생처음 보네.\" 면도칼을 권했던 신사가 말했으니, 그의 이름은 프라이스인 듯했다."], ["‘Never!’ Mr.", "\"정말이지!\""], ["Price confirmed the assertion with an oath, and then laughed again, when of course the boy (who thought his companion one of the most dashing fellows alive) laughed also.", "프라이스 씨는 욕설을 한마디 보태어 그 단언을 다지고는 다시 웃었으며, 물론 그러자 소년도(자기 동료를 세상에서 가장 멋진 친구 가운데 하나로 여기던) 따라 웃었다."]], [["‘You’d hardly think, would you now,’ said Price, turning towards Mr.", "\"당신은 잘 짐작도 못 하실 거요, 안 그렇소?\" 프라이스가 픽윅 씨 쪽으로 돌아서며 말했다."], ["Pickwick, ‘that that chap’s been here a week yesterday, and never once shaved himself yet, because he feels so certain he’s going out in half an hour’s time, thinks he may as well put it off till he gets home?’", "\"저 친구가 어제로 여기 들어온 지 일주일째인데, 아직 한 번도 면도를 안 했다는 거 말이오. 삼십 분이면 나갈 거라고 어찌나 굳게 믿는지, 집에 가서 할 때까지 미뤄 두는 편이 낫겠다 싶은 거지요?\""]], [["‘Poor man!’ said Mr. Pickwick.", "\"가엾은 사람이군요!\""], ["‘Are his chances of getting out of his difficulties really so great?’", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"정말로 그가 곤경에서 벗어날 가능성이 그렇게나 크답니까?\""]], [["‘Chances be d----d,’ replied Price; ‘he hasn’t half the ghost of one.", "\"가능성이라니, 제기랄.\" 프라이스가 대꾸했다."], ["I wouldn’t give that for his chance of walking about the streets this time ten years.’ With this, Mr.", "\"그런 건 손톱만큼도 없소. 지금부터 십 년 뒤 그가 거리를 활보할 가능성에 나라면 이것만큼도 안 걸겠소.\""], ["Price snapped his fingers contemptuously, and rang the bell.", "이 말과 함께 프라이스 씨는 경멸하듯 손가락을 딱 튕기고는 종을 울렸다."]], [["‘Give me a sheet of paper, Crookey,’ said Mr.", "\"종이 한 장 주게, 크루키.\" 프라이스 씨가 시중꾼에게 말했으니, 그자는 차림새며 전반적인 행색이 파산한 유리장이와 지급 불능에 빠진 가축 장수 사이쯤 되어 보였다."], ["Price to the attendant, who in dress and general appearance looked something between a bankrupt glazier, and a drover in a state of insolvency; ‘and a glass of brandy- and-water, Crookey, d’ye hear?", "\"그리고 브랜디토닉 한 잔도, 크루키, 알겠나? 우리 아버지한테 편지를 쓸 참인데, 자극제가 좀 있어야겠어."], ["I’m going to write to my father, and I must have a stimulant, or I shan’t be able to pitch it strong enough into the old boy.’ At this facetious speech, the young boy, it is almost needless to say, was fairly convulsed.", "안 그러면 그 영감을 충분히 세게 몰아붙일 수가 없거든.\" 이 익살스러운 말에, 그 어린 소년이 그야말로 자지러졌으리라는 것은 굳이 말할 것도 없다."]], [["‘That’s right,’ said Mr.", "\"그래야지.\" 프라이스 씨가 말했다."], ["Price. ‘Never say die.", "\"결코 죽는소리 하지 말 것."], ["All fun, ain’t it?’", "다 장난질이지, 안 그래?\""]], [["‘Prime!’ said the young gentleman.", "\"끝내주죠!\" 그 젊은 신사가 말했다."]], [["‘You’ve got some spirit about you, you have,’ said Price.", "\"자넨 기개가 좀 있어, 그래.\" 프라이스가 말했다."], ["‘You’ve seen something of life.’", "\"세상을 좀 본 게야.\""]], [["‘I rather think I have!’ replied the boy.", "\"그런 것 같습니다!\" 소년이 대꾸했다."], ["He had looked at it through the dirty panes of glass in a bar door.", "그는 술집 문의 더러운 유리창 너머로 세상을 본 것이었다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick, feeling not a little disgusted with this dialogue, as well as with the air and manner of the two beings by whom it had been carried on, was about to inquire whether he could not be accommodated with a private sitting-room, when two or three strangers of genteel appearance entered, at sight of whom the boy threw his cigar into the fire, and whispering to Mr.", "이 대화에, 그리고 그것을 주고받은 두 인간의 태도와 분위기에 적잖이 넌더리가 난 픽윅 씨는, 따로 쓸 수 있는 응접실은 없겠느냐고 물어보려던 참인데, 점잖은 행색의 낯선 사람 두셋이 들어왔으니, 그들을 보자 소년은 시가를 불 속에 던져 버리고, 프라이스 씨에게 저들이 자기 일을 \"다 해결해\""], ["Price that they had come to ‘make it all right’ for him, joined them at a table in the farther end of the room.", "주러 왔다고 속삭이고는, 방 저편 끝의 탁자에 앉은 그들과 합류했다."]], [["It would appear, however, that matters were not going to be made all right quite so speedily as the young gentleman anticipated; for a very long conversation ensued, of which Mr.", "그러나 일은 그 젊은 신사가 기대한 만큼 그렇게 빨리 다 해결될 것 같지는 않아 보였다."], ["Pickwick could not avoid hearing certain angry fragments regarding dissolute conduct, and repeated forgiveness.", "무척 긴 대화가 이어졌고, 픽윅 씨는 방탕한 행실과 거듭된 용서에 관한 성난 단편들을 더러 듣지 않을 수 없었기 때문이다."], ["At last, there were very distinct allusions made by the oldest gentleman of the party to one Whitecross Street, at which the young gentleman, notwithstanding his primeness and his spirit, and his knowledge of life into the bargain, reclined his head upon the table, and howled dismally.", "마침내 일행 중 가장 나이 든 신사가 화이트크로스 가의 어떤 곳을 무척 분명하게 들먹였으니, 그러자 그 젊은 신사는 자신의 끝내주는 기개에도, 그 패기에도, 게다가 세상 물정에 대한 식견에도 아랑곳없이, 머리를 탁자에 처박고 처량하게 울부짖었다."]], [["Very much satisfied with this sudden bringing down of the youth’s valour, and this effectual lowering of his tone, Mr.", "* 화이트크로스 가: 채무자 감옥이 있던 곳으로, 그곳에 수감되리라는 위협을 가리킨다."], ["Pickwick rang the bell, and was shown, at his own request, into a private room furnished with a carpet, table, chairs, sideboard and sofa, and ornamented with a looking-glass, and various old prints. Here he had the advantage of hearing Mrs.", "이렇게 젊은이의 기세가 별안간 꺾이고, 그 거드름이 효과적으로 꺾인 것에 무척 흡족해진 픽윅 씨는, 종을 울려 자신의 요청대로 양탄자와 탁자, 의자, 찬장, 소파가 갖추어지고, 거울과 갖가지 오래된 판화로 장식된 개인실로 안내되었다."], ["Namby’s performance on a square piano overhead, while the breakfast was getting ready; when it came, Mr.", "여기서 그는 아침 식사가 차려지는 동안 위층에서 냄비 부인이 사각 피아노를 연주하는 것을 들을 호사를 누렸다."], ["Perker came too.", "식사가 나왔을 때 퍼커 씨도 함께 왔다."]], [["‘Aha, my dear sir,’ said the little man, ‘nailed at last, eh?", "\"아하, 친애하는 선생.\" 그 작은 사내가 말했다."], ["Come, come, I’m not sorry for it either, because now you’ll see the absurdity of this conduct.", "\"마침내 붙들리셨군요, 응? 자, 자, 저도 그게 섭섭하지만은 않습니다."], ["I’ve noted down the amount of the taxed costs and damages for which the ca-sa was issued, and we had better settle at once and lose no time.", "이제야 선생께서 이 행동이 얼마나 어처구니없는지 아실 테니까요. 이번 신병 구속 영장이 발부된 명목인 과세 비용과 손해 배상액을 제가 적어 두었으니, 지체 없이 당장 정산하는 편이 낫겠습니다."], ["Namby is come home by this time, I dare say.", "지금쯤이면 냄비도 집에 돌아왔겠지요, 아마."], ["What say you, my dear sir?", "어떻게 하시겠습니까, 친애하는 선생?"], ["Shall I draw a cheque, or will you?’ The little man rubbed his hands with affected cheerfulness as he said this, but glancing at Mr.", "제가 수표를 발행할까요, 아니면 선생께서 하시겠습니까?\""], ["Pickwick’s countenance, could not forbear at the same time casting a desponding look towards Sam Weller.", "그 작은 사내는 이렇게 말하며 짐짓 명랑한 척 두 손을 비볐으나, 픽윅 씨의 표정을 흘끔 보고는 동시에 샘 웰러 쪽으로 낙담한 눈길을 던지지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["‘Perker,’ said Mr.", "\"퍼커.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick, ‘let me hear no more of this, I beg.", "\"이 이야기는 더 이상 듣지 않게 해 주게, 부탁이네."], ["I see no advantage in staying here, so I shall go to prison to-night.’", "여기 머물러 봐야 아무 득도 없어 보이니, 오늘 밤 감옥으로 가겠네.\""]], [["‘You can’t go to Whitecross Street, my dear Sir,’ said Perker.", "\"화이트크로스 가로 가셔서는 안 됩니다, 친애하는 선생.\" 퍼커가 말했다."], ["‘Impossible!", "\"안 됩니다!"], ["There are sixty beds in a ward; and the bolt’s on, sixteen hours out of the four-and-twenty.’", "한 방에 침대가 예순 개나 되고, 빗장은 하루 스물네 시간 가운데 열여섯 시간이나 걸려 있습니다.\""]], [["‘I would rather go to some other place of confinement if I can,’ said Mr.", "\"가능하다면 다른 어떤 구금 장소로 가는 편이 좋겠네.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick. ‘If not, I must make the best I can of that.’", "\"그럴 수 없다면 그곳에서나마 최선을 다해 보아야겠지.\""]], [["‘You can go to the Fleet, my dear Sir, if you’re determined to go somewhere,’ said Perker.", "\"기어이 어딘가로 가시겠다면, 플리트로 가실 수 있습니다, 친애하는 선생.\" 퍼커가 말했다."]], [["‘That’ll do,’ said Mr. Pickwick.", "\"그거면 됐네.\""], ["‘I’ll go there directly I have finished my breakfast.’", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"아침 식사를 마치는 대로 곧장 그리로 가겠네.\""]], [["‘Stop, stop, my dear Sir; not the least occasion for being in such a violent hurry to get into a place that most other men are as eager to get out of,’ said the good-natured little attorney.", "\"잠깐, 잠깐만요, 친애하는 선생. 다른 대부분의 사람들이 그토록 빠져나오려고 안달하는 곳에 그렇게 맹렬히 서둘러 들어가실 까닭은 조금도 없습니다.\""], ["‘We must have a habeas-corpus.", "마음씨 좋은 그 작은 변호사가 말했다."], ["There’ll be no judge at chambers till four o’clock this afternoon.", "\"인신 보호 영장을 받아야 합니다. 오늘 오후 네 시 전에는 판사실에 판사가 없을 겁니다."], ["You must wait till then.’", "그때까지 기다리셔야 합니다.\""]], [["‘Very good,’ said Mr.", "\"아주 좋네.\""], ["Pickwick, with unmoved patience.", "픽윅 씨가 흔들림 없는 인내심으로 말했다."], ["‘Then we will have a chop here, at two.", "\"그러면 두 시에 여기서 갈비를 좀 먹세."], ["See about it, Sam, and tell them to be punctual.’", "그 일을 좀 챙겨라, 샘. 그리고 시간을 어기지 말라고 일러 두어라.\""]], [["Mr. Pickwick remaining firm, despite all the remonstrances and arguments of Perker, the chops appeared and disappeared in due course; he was then put into another hackney coach, and carried off to Chancery Lane, after waiting half an hour or so for Mr.", "퍼커의 온갖 항의와 설득에도 불구하고 픽윅 씨가 단호한 태도를 굽히지 않자, 갈비는 제때에 나왔다가 사라졌다."], ["Namby, who had a select dinner-party and could on no account be disturbed before.", "그러고 나서 그는 또 다른 삯마차에 태워져 챈서리 레인으로 끌려갔으니, 그 전에 냄비 씨를 삼십 분쯤 기다려야 했는데, 그는 엄선한 만찬 모임을 벌이고 있던 터라 그 전에는 무슨 일이 있어도 방해받을 수 없었기 때문이다."]], [["There were two judges in attendance at Serjeant’s Inn--one King’s Bench, and one Common Pleas--and a great deal of business appeared to be transacting before them, if the number of lawyer’s clerks who were hurrying in and out with bundles of papers, afforded any test.", "서전트 인에는 판사 두 사람이 출석해 있었으니—하나는 왕좌 법원, 하나는 민사 법원이었다—그들 앞에서는 적잖은 일이 처리되고 있는 듯했으니, 서류 뭉치를 들고 부산히 드나드는 법률 사무원들의 수가 무슨 잣대라도 된다면 말이다."], ["When they reached the low archway which forms the entrance to the inn, Perker was detained a few moments parlaying with the coachman about the fare and the change; and Mr.", "그들이 그 법학원 입구를 이루는 나지막한 아치문에 이르렀을 때, 퍼커는 마부와 삯과 거스름돈을 두고 흥정하느라 잠시 붙들려 있었다."], ["Pickwick, stepping to one side to be out of the way of the stream of people that were pouring in and out, looked about him with some curiosity.", "그래서 픽윅 씨는 드나드는 사람들의 물결을 피하려고 한쪽으로 비켜서서, 다소 호기심 어린 눈으로 주위를 둘러보았다."]], [["The people that attracted his attention most, were three or four men of shabby-genteel appearance, who touched their hats to many of the attorneys who passed, and seemed to have some business there, the nature of which Mr.", "그의 주의를 가장 끈 사람들은 초라하지만 점잖은 행색의 사내 서넛이었는데, 지나가는 변호사들 여럿에게 모자를 살짝 들어 인사하는 것이 거기서 무슨 볼일이 있는 듯했으나, 그 일의 성격을 픽윅 씨로서는 짐작할 수 없었다. 그들은 묘하게 생긴 친구들이었다."], ["Pickwick could not divine. They were curious-looking fellows.", "하나는 낡아 빛바랜 검정 옷에 흰 목수건을 두른, 호리호리하고 다리를 좀 저는 사내였다."], ["One was a slim and rather lame man in rusty black, and a white neckerchief; another was a stout, burly person, dressed in the same apparel, with a great reddish-black cloth round his neck; a third was a little weazen, drunken-looking body, with a pimply face.", "또 하나는 똑같은 옷차림에 목둘레에 불그스레한 검정 천을 두른, 떡 벌어지고 우람한 자였다. 세 번째는 여드름투성이 얼굴에, 술에 찌든 듯한, 자그맣고 비쩍 마른 자였다."], ["They were loitering about, with their hands behind them, and now and then with an anxious countenance whispered something in the ear of some of the gentlemen with papers, as they hurried by. Mr.", "그들은 뒷짐을 진 채 어슬렁거리다가, 이따금 초조한 낯빛으로, 서류를 든 신사들이 바삐 지나갈 때 그 귀에 대고 무언가를 속삭였다."], ["Pickwick remembered to have very often observed them lounging under the archway when he had been walking past; and his curiosity was quite excited to know to what branch of the profession these dingy-looking loungers could possibly belong.", "픽윅 씨는 자신이 그 앞을 지나다닐 때 그들이 아치문 아래에서 빈둥거리는 것을 무척 자주 본 적이 있음을 떠올렸다. 그래서 이 추레해 보이는 빈둥거리는 자들이 도대체 그 직업의 어느 갈래에 속할 수 있는지 알고 싶은 호기심이 잔뜩 일었다."]], [["He was about to propound the question to Namby, who kept close beside him, sucking a large gold ring on his little finger, when Perker bustled up, and observing that there was no time to lose, led the way into the inn. As Mr.", "그는 새끼손가락에 낀 큼직한 금반지를 빨면서 그의 곁에 바짝 붙어 있던 냄비에게 그 물음을 던지려던 참인데, 퍼커가 부산히 다가와 지체할 시간이 없다고 하면서 앞장서서 법학원 안으로 길을 안내했다."], ["Pickwick followed, the lame man stepped up to him, and civilly touching his hat, held out a written card, which Mr. Pickwick, not wishing to hurt the man’s feelings by refusing, courteously accepted and deposited in his waistcoat pocket.", "픽윅 씨가 뒤를 따르는데, 다리를 저는 사내가 그에게 다가와 공손히 모자를 살짝 들고는 글씨가 적힌 명함 한 장을 내밀었으니, 픽윅 씨는 거절해서 그 사내의 기분을 상하게 하고 싶지 않아 정중히 그것을 받아 조끼 주머니에 넣었다."]], [["‘Now,’ said Perker, turning round before he entered one of the offices, to see that his companions were close behind him.", "\"자.\" 퍼커가 사무실 하나로 들어가기 전에 돌아서서, 동행들이 바로 뒤에 따라붙었는지 확인하며 말했다."], ["‘In here, my dear sir.", "\"이리로 드시지요, 친애하는 선생."], ["Hallo, what do you want?’", "아니, 자넨 뭘 원하나?\""]], [["This last question was addressed to the lame man, who, unobserved by Mr. Pickwick, made one of the party.", "이 마지막 물음은 다리를 저는 사내에게 던진 것이었으니, 그는 픽윅 씨도 모르는 새에 일행에 끼어 있었다."], ["In reply to it, the lame man touched his hat again, with all imaginable politeness, and motioned towards Mr. Pickwick.", "그에 대한 대답으로, 다리를 저는 사내는 상상할 수 있는 한 더없이 공손하게 다시 모자를 살짝 들고는 픽윅 씨 쪽을 가리켰다."]], [["‘No, no,’ said Perker, with a smile.", "\"아니, 아니야.\" 퍼커가 미소를 지으며 말했다."], ["‘We don’t want you, my dear friend, we don’t want you.’", "\"우린 자네가 필요 없네, 이보게, 자네가 필요 없어.\""]], [["‘I beg your pardon, sir,’ said the lame man.", "\"실례합니다, 나리.\" 다리를 저는 사내가 말했다."], ["‘The gentleman took my card.", "\"이 신사분께서 제 명함을 받으셨습니다."], ["I hope you will employ me, sir.", "저를 써 주시길 바랍니다, 나리."], ["The gentleman nodded to me.", "이 신사분께서 제게 고개를 끄덕이셨어요."], ["I’ll be judged by the gentleman himself.", "이 신사분 본인의 판단에 맡기겠습니다."], ["You nodded to me, sir?’", "제게 고개를 끄덕이셨지요, 나리?\""]], [["‘Pooh, pooh, nonsense.", "\"쳇, 쳇, 말도 안 되는 소리."], ["You didn’t nod to anybody, Pickwick?", "자넨 아무에게도 고개를 안 끄덕였지, 픽윅?"], ["A mistake, a mistake,’ said Perker.", "착각이야, 착각.\" 퍼커가 말했다."]], [["‘The gentleman handed me his card,’ replied Mr.", "\"이 신사분이 내게 자기 명함을 건넸네.\""], ["Pickwick, producing it from his waistcoat pocket.", "픽윅 씨가 조끼 주머니에서 그것을 꺼내며 대꾸했다."], ["‘I accepted it, as the gentleman seemed to wish it--in fact I had some curiosity to look at it when I should be at leisure.", "\"이 신사분이 그러길 바라는 듯하기에 받았네.—사실 짬이 날 때 한번 들여다보고 싶은 호기심이 좀 있었거든."], ["I--’", "나는—\""]], [["The little attorney burst into a loud laugh, and returning the card to the lame man, informing him it was all a mistake, whispered to Mr. Pickwick as the man turned away in dudgeon, that he was only a bail.", "그 작은 변호사가 와락 큰 소리로 웃음을 터뜨리며 명함을 다리를 저는 사내에게 돌려주고, 그것이 다 착각이라고 일러 주고는, 사내가 분이 나서 돌아서는 사이 픽윅 씨에게 그자가 한낱 보석 보증인일 뿐이라고 속삭였다."]], [["‘A what!’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"한낱 뭐라고!\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 소리쳤다."]], [["‘A bail,’ replied Perker.", "\"보석 보증인이지요.\""], ["‘A bail!’", "퍼커가 대꾸했다."]], [["Yes, my dear sir--half a dozen of ‘em here.", "\"보석 보증인이라니!\" \"그렇습니다, 친애하는 선생—여기 그런 자들이 대여섯은 됩니다."], ["Bail you to any amount, and only charge half a crown.", "액수가 얼마든 선생을 위해 보증을 서 주고, 고작 반 크라운만 받지요."], ["Curious trade, isn’t it?’ said Perker, regaling himself with a pinch of snuff.", "묘한 장사지요, 그렇잖습니까?\" 퍼커가 코담배를 한 줌 즐기며 말했다."]], [["‘What!", "\"뭐라고요!"], ["Am I to understand that these men earn a livelihood by waiting about here, to perjure themselves before the judges of the land, at the rate of half a crown a crime?’ exclaimed Mr.", "그러니까 이 사람들이 여기서 어슬렁거리며 한 건당 반 크라운씩 받고 이 나라의 판사들 앞에서 위증을 하는 것으로 생계를 꾸린다고 이해하면 되는 겁니까?\""], ["Pickwick, quite aghast at the disclosure.", "픽윅 씨가 그 폭로에 그만 아연실색하여 소리쳤다."]], [["‘Why, I don’t exactly know about perjury, my dear sir,’ replied the little gentleman.", "\"글쎄요, 위증이라고까지는 잘 모르겠습니다만, 친애하는 선생.\" 그 작은 신사가 대꾸했다."], ["‘Harsh word, my dear sir, very harsh word indeed.", "\"험한 말씀입니다, 친애하는 선생, 참으로 험한 말씀이에요."], ["It’s a legal fiction, my dear sir, nothing more.’ Saying which, the attorney shrugged his shoulders, smiled, took a second pinch of snuff, and led the way into the office of the judge’s clerk.", "그건 법률상의 의제일 뿐, 그 이상 아무것도 아닙니다, 친애하는 선생.\" 이렇게 말하면서 변호사는 어깨를 으쓱하고는 미소를 짓고, 코담배를 한 줌 더 들이마시고는, 앞장서서 판사 서기의 사무실로 길을 안내했다."]], [["This was a room of specially dirty appearance, with a very low ceiling and old panelled walls; and so badly lighted, that although it was broad day outside, great tallow candles were burning on the desks.", "이곳은 유난히 더러운 행색의 방으로, 천장이 무척 낮고 벽은 오래된 판자로 덧대어져 있었으며, 어찌나 조명이 형편없던지 바깥은 대낮인데도 책상 위에는 굵직한 수지 양초들이 타오르고 있었다."], ["At one end, was a door leading to the judge’s private apartment, round which were congregated a crowd of attorneys and managing clerks, who were called in, in the order in which their respective appointments stood upon the file.", "한쪽 끝에는 판사의 개인실로 통하는 문이 있었고, 그 둘레에는 변호사와 사무 주임들이 한 무리 모여 있었으니, 그들은 각자의 약속이 명부에 오른 순서대로 불려 들어갔다."], ["Every time this door was opened to let a party out, the next party made a violent rush to get in; and, as in addition to the numerous dialogues which passed between the gentlemen who were waiting to see the judge, a variety of personal squabbles ensued between the greater part of those who had seen him, there was as much noise as could well be raised in an apartment of such confined dimensions.", "한 무리를 내보내려고 이 문이 열릴 때마다, 다음 무리가 들어가려고 맹렬히 돌진했다. 거기다 판사를 만나려고 기다리는 신사들끼리 오가는 무수한 대화에 더해, 그를 만나고 나온 사람들 대부분 사이에서 갖가지 개인적인 말다툼까지 벌어졌으니, 그렇게 비좁은 방 안에서 일으킬 수 있는 한 가장 요란한 소음이 일었다."]], [["Nor were the conversations of these gentlemen the only sounds that broke upon the ear.", "이 신사들의 대화만이 귀를 때리는 유일한 소리는 아니었다."], ["Standing on a box behind a wooden bar at another end of the room was a clerk in spectacles who was ‘taking the affidavits’; large batches of which were, from time to time, carried into the private room by another clerk for the judge’s signature.", "방의 또 다른 끝에서, 나무 난간 뒤 상자 위에 올라선 안경 쓴 서기 하나가 \"선서 진술서를 받고\" 있었으니, 그것은 한 무더기씩 이따금 또 다른 서기에 의해 판사의 서명을 받으러 개인실로 옮겨졌다."], ["There were a large number of attorneys’ clerks to be sworn, and it being a moral impossibility to swear them all at once, the struggles of these gentlemen to reach the clerk in spectacles, were like those of a crowd to get in at the pit door of a theatre when Gracious Majesty honours it with its presence.", "선서를 해야 할 변호사 사무원들이 워낙 많았고, 그들 모두에게 한꺼번에 선서를 시키기란 도덕적으로 불가능한 일이었던지라, 그 안경 쓴 서기에게 다가가려는 이 신사들의 몸부림은, 자비로우신 폐하께서 왕림하시는 영광을 베푸실 때 극장의 일반석 입구로 비집고 들어가려는 군중의 그것과 같았다."], ["Another functionary, from time to time, exercised his lungs in calling over the names of those who had been sworn, for the purpose of restoring to them their affidavits after they had been signed by the judge, which gave rise to a few more scuffles; and all these things going on at the same time, occasioned as much bustle as the most active and excitable person could desire to behold.", "또 다른 직원 하나는 판사의 서명을 받은 뒤 선서 진술서를 돌려주려고, 이따금 선서를 마친 자들의 이름을 부르느라 폐를 혹사했으니, 이는 또 몇 차례의 실랑이를 불러일으켰다. 그리고 이 모든 일이 동시에 벌어지면서, 더없이 활동적이고 흥분 잘하는 사람이라도 흡족하게 구경할 만큼의 소란이 일었다."], ["There were yet another class of persons--those who were waiting to attend summonses their employers had taken out, which it was optional to the attorney on the opposite side to attend or not--and whose business it was, from time to time, to cry out the opposite attorney’s name; to make certain that he was not in attendance without their knowledge.", "또 다른 부류의 사람들도 있었으니—자기 고용주가 신청한 소환에 출석하려고 기다리는 자들로, 그 소환에는 상대편 변호사가 출석하든 말든 자유였으며—그들이 할 일은 이따금 상대편 변호사의 이름을 외쳐, 그가 자기들 모르는 새에 출석해 있지는 않은지 확인하는 것이었다."]], [["For example. Leaning against the wall, close beside the seat Mr.", "이를테면 이런 식이었다."], ["Pickwick had taken, was an office-lad of fourteen, with a tenor voice; near him a common-law clerk with a bass one.", "픽윅 씨가 자리 잡은 좌석 바로 옆 벽에 기대어, 테너 목소리를 가진 열네 살짜리 사무소 사환이 있었다. 그 근처에는 베이스 목소리를 가진 보통법 사무원이 있었다."]], [["A clerk hurried in with a bundle of papers, and stared about him.", "한 사무원이 서류 뭉치를 들고 황급히 들어와 두리번거렸다."]], [["‘Sniggle and Blink,’ cried the tenor.", "\"스니글 앤드 블링크.\" 테너가 외쳤다."]], [["‘Porkin and Snob,’ growled the bass.", "\"포킨 앤드 스놉.\" 베이스가 으르렁거렸다."]], [["‘Stumpy and Deacon,’ said the new-comer.", "\"스텀피 앤드 디컨.\" 새로 온 자가 말했다."]], [["Nobody answered; the next man who came in, was bailed by the whole three; and he in his turn shouted for another firm; and then somebody else roared in a loud voice for another; and so forth.", "아무도 대답하지 않았다. 다음에 들어온 사람은 셋 모두에게 호명되었다. 그러자 그도 자기 차례에 또 다른 회사 이름을 외쳤고, 그러자 누군가 또 다른 회사 이름을 큰 소리로 외쳐 댔으며, 이런 식이었다."]], [["All this time, the man in the spectacles was hard at work, swearing the clerks; the oath being invariably administered, without any effort at punctuation, and usually in the following terms:--", "이 내내, 안경 쓴 사내는 사무원들에게 선서를 시키느라 분주히 일하고 있었으니, 그 선서는 어김없이 구두점이라고는 찍을 생각도 없이, 대개 다음과 같은 식으로 집행되었다.—"]], [["‘Take the book in your right hand this is your name and hand-writing you swear that the contents of this your affidavit are true so help you God a shilling you must get change I haven’t got it.’", "\"오른손에 성서를 잡으시오 이게 당신 이름이고 필적이오 당신은 이 선서 진술서의 내용이 사실임을 맹세하니 그러므로 하느님이 도우시리라 일 실링이오 잔돈은 알아서 마련하시오 나한테는 없으니.\""]], [["‘Well, Sam,’ said Mr.", "\"이보게, 샘.\""], ["Pickwick, ‘I suppose they are getting the Habeas- corpus ready?’", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"인신 보호 영장인지 뭔지를 준비하고 있는 모양이지?\""]], [["‘Yes,’ said Sam, ‘and I vish they’d bring out the have-his-carcase.", "\"네.\" 샘이 말했다."], ["It’s wery unpleasant keepin’ us vaitin’ here.", "\"어서 그 시체-있나 보자 영장인지 뭔지를 가져왔으면 좋겠네요."], ["I’d ha’ got half a dozen have- his-carcases ready, pack’d up and all, by this time.’", "우릴 여기 이렇게 기다리게 하다니 참 고약하지 뭡니까. 저 같으면 이맘때쯤이면 시체-있나 보자 영장을 대여섯 장은 다 준비해서, 포장까지 싹 해 놓았을 텐데요.\""]], [["What sort of cumbrous and unmanageable machine, Sam Weller imagined a habeas-corpus to be, does not appear; for Perker, at that moment, walked up and took Mr. Pickwick away.", "* 샘은 'habeas-corpus'(인신 보호 영장)를 'have-his-carcase'(그자의 시체를 가져와라)로 잘못 알아들어 부린 말장난이다."]], [["The usual forms having been gone through, the body of Samuel Pickwick was soon afterwards confided to the custody of the tipstaff, to be by him taken to the warden of the Fleet Prison, and there detained until the amount of the damages and costs in the action of Bardell against Pickwick was fully paid and satisfied.", "샘 웰러가 인신 보호 영장을 어떤 종류의 다루기 거추장스럽고 거북한 기계로 상상했는지는 드러나지 않는다. 바로 그 순간 퍼커가 다가와 픽윅 씨를 데려갔기 때문이다. 통상의 절차가 다 끝나자, 새뮤얼 픽윅의 신병은 곧이어 집달관에게 인도되어, 그에 의해 플리트 감옥의 간수장에게 넘겨졌으니, 바델 대 픽윅 소송의 손해 배상액과 비용 전액이 완납되어 변제될 때까지 그곳에 구금될 것이었다."]], [["‘And that,’ said Mr.", "\"그러자면,\""], ["Pickwick, laughing, ‘will be a very long time.", "픽윅 씨가 웃으며 말했다. \"아주 오랜 시간이 걸리겠지."], ["Sam, call another hackney-coach.", "샘, 삯마차를 또 한 대 불러라."], ["Perker, my dear friend, good-bye.’", "퍼커, 친애하는 친구여, 잘 있게.\""]], [["‘I shall go with you, and see you safe there,’ said Perker.", "\"제가 함께 가서 무사히 도착하시는 걸 보겠습니다.\" 퍼커가 말했다."]], [["‘Indeed,’ replied Mr.", "\"실은,\" 픽윅 씨가 대꾸했다."], ["Pickwick, ‘I would rather go without any other attendant than Sam.", "\"샘 말고는 다른 누구의 시중도 없이 가는 편이 좋겠네."], ["As soon as I get settled, I will write and let you know, and I shall expect you immediately.", "자리를 잡는 대로 편지를 써서 알려 줄 테니, 그러면 즉시 와 주게나."], ["Until then, good-bye.’", "그때까지, 잘 있게.\""]], [["As Mr. Pickwick said this, he got into the coach which had by this time arrived, followed by the tipstaff.", "픽윅 씨는 이렇게 말하면서 그새 도착해 있던 마차에 올랐고, 집달관이 뒤를 따랐다."], ["Sam having stationed himself on the box, it rolled away.", "샘이 마부석에 자리 잡자, 마차는 굴러 떠나갔다."]], [["‘A most extraordinary man that!’ said Perker, as he stopped to pull on his gloves.", "\"참으로 별난 양반이로군!\" 퍼커가 장갑을 끼느라 멈춰 서서 말했다."]], [["‘What a bankrupt he’d make, Sir,’ observed Mr.", "\"파산자로 치면 어땠겠습니까, 선생님.\""], ["Lowten, who was standing near.", "가까이 서 있던 로턴 씨가 한마디 했다."], ["‘How he would bother the commissioners!", "\"파산 위원들을 얼마나 곤란하게 했을까요!"], ["He’d set ‘em at defiance if they talked of committing him, Sir.’", "그들이 그를 수감하겠다고 말이라도 꺼냈다간, 보란 듯이 들이받았을 겁니다, 선생님.\""]], [["The attorney did not appear very much delighted with his clerk’s professional estimate of Mr. Pickwick’s character, for he walked away without deigning any reply.", "변호사는 픽윅 씨의 인품에 대한 자기 사무원의 그런 직업적 평가가 그다지 마음에 들지 않는 듯, 아무 대꾸도 황송히 베풀지 않은 채 걸어가 버렸다."]], [["The hackney-coach jolted along Fleet Street, as hackney-coaches usually do.", "삯마차는 삯마차가 흔히 그러하듯 덜커덩거리며 플리트 가를 따라 나아갔다."], ["The horses ‘went better’, the driver said, when they had anything before them (they must have gone at a most extraordinary pace when there was nothing), and so the vehicle kept behind a cart; when the cart stopped, it stopped; and when the cart went on again, it did the same.", "마부의 말로는 앞에 무언가 있을 때 말들이 \"더 잘 갔다\"는데(아무것도 없을 때는 어지간히 무시무시한 속도로 달렸을 게 틀림없다), 그래서 마차는 짐수레 한 대 뒤를 따라갔다. 짐수레가 멈추면 마차도 멈췄고, 짐수레가 다시 가면 마차도 그리했다."], ["Mr. Pickwick sat opposite the tipstaff; and the tipstaff sat with his hat between his knees, whistling a tune, and looking out of the coach window.", "픽윅 씨는 집달관 맞은편에 앉아 있었고, 집달관은 무릎 사이에 모자를 끼운 채 가락을 휘파람으로 불며 마차 창밖을 내다보고 앉아 있었다."]], [["Time performs wonders.", "세월은 기적을 일으킨다."], ["By the powerful old gentleman’s aid, even a hackney-coach gets over half a mile of ground.", "그 막강한 노신사의 도움으로, 삯마차조차 반 마일의 거리를 헤쳐 나간다."], ["They stopped at length, and Mr. Pickwick alighted at the gate of the Fleet.", "마침내 그들은 멈춰 섰고, 픽윅 씨는 플리트 정문 앞에서 내렸다."]], [["The tipstaff, just looking over his shoulder to see that his charge was following close at his heels, preceded Mr.", "집달관은 자기가 맡은 자가 바로 발뒤꿈치에 따라오는지 어깨너머로 흘끔 살피고는, 픽윅 씨보다 앞서 감옥 안으로 들어갔다."], ["Pickwick into the prison; turning to the left, after they had entered, they passed through an open door into a lobby, from which a heavy gate, opposite to that by which they had entered, and which was guarded by a stout turnkey with the key in his hand, led at once into the interior of the prison.", "들어선 뒤 왼쪽으로 돌아, 열린 문을 지나 로비로 들어섰는데, 그곳에서는 그들이 들어온 문의 맞은편에 있는, 열쇠를 손에 든 건장한 간수가 지키고 선 묵직한 문이 곧장 감옥 내부로 통했다."]], [["Here they stopped, while the tipstaff delivered his papers; and here Mr.", "여기서 그들은 멈춰 섰고, 그동안 집달관은 자기 서류를 인계했다."], ["Pickwick was apprised that he would remain, until he had undergone the ceremony, known to the initiated as ‘sitting for your portrait.’", "그리고 여기서 픽윅 씨는, 그 방면에 정통한 자들에게 \"초상화 모델로 앉기\"라고 알려진 의식을 치를 때까지 머물러 있어야 한다는 통보를 받았다."]], [["‘Sitting for my portrait?’ said Mr.", "\"초상화 모델로 앉는다고요?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Having your likeness taken, sir,’ replied the stout turnkey.", "\"당신 얼굴 모습을 떠 두는 겁니다, 나리.\" 건장한 간수가 대꾸했다."]], [["‘We’re capital hands at likenesses here.", "\"우린 여기서 얼굴 떠내는 데 솜씨가 일품이지요."], ["Take ‘em in no time, and always exact.", "눈 깜짝할 새에 떠내고, 언제나 정확합니다."], ["Walk in, sir, and make yourself at home.’", "들어오세요, 나리, 편히 앉으시고요.\""]], [["Mr. Pickwick complied with the invitation, and sat himself down; when Mr.", "픽윅 씨는 그 권유에 따라 자리에 앉았다."], ["Weller, who stationed himself at the back of the chair, whispered that the sitting was merely another term for undergoing an inspection by the different turnkeys, in order that they might know prisoners from visitors.", "그러자 의자 등받이 뒤에 자리 잡은 웰러 씨가, 모델로 앉는 것이란 여러 간수들에게 검사를 받는 것을 달리 일컫는 말일 뿐이며, 그래야 그들이 죄수와 방문객을 구분할 수 있다고 속삭였다."]], [["‘Well, Sam,’ said Mr.", "\"음, 샘.\""], ["Pickwick, ‘then I wish the artists would come.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"그럼 화가들이 어서 왔으면 좋겠구나."], ["This is rather a public place.’", "여기는 사람이 좀 많은 곳이로다.\""]], [["‘They von’t be long, Sir, I des-say,’ replied Sam.", "\"오래 안 걸릴 겁니다, 어르신, 아마요.\" 샘이 대꾸했다."], ["‘There’s a Dutch clock, sir.’", "\"저기 네덜란드제 시계가 있네요, 어르신.\""]], [["‘So I see,’ observed Mr.", "\"그래, 보이는구나.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 한마디 했다."]], [["‘And a bird-cage, sir,’ says Sam.", "\"그리고 새장도 있어요, 어르신.\""], ["‘Veels vithin veels, a prison in a prison.", "샘이 말했다. \"바퀴 속의 바퀴랄까, 감옥 속의 감옥이지요."], ["Ain’t it, Sir?’", "안 그렇습니까, 어르신?\""]], [["As Mr. Weller made this philosophical remark, Mr.", "웰러 씨가 이 철학적인 한마디를 하는 동안, 픽윅 씨는 자기 모델 앉기가 시작되었음을 알아챘다."], ["Pickwick was aware that his sitting had commenced. The stout turnkey having been relieved from the lock, sat down, and looked at him carelessly, from time to time, while a long thin man who had relieved him, thrust his hands beneath his coat tails, and planting himself opposite, took a good long view of him.", "자물쇠 당번에서 풀려난 건장한 간수가 자리에 앉아, 이따금 무심하게 그를 쳐다보았고, 그를 교대한 키 크고 마른 사내는 두 손을 외투 자락 밑에 찔러 넣고 맞은편에 떡 버티고 서서 그를 한참 동안 찬찬히 살펴보았다."], ["A third rather surly-looking gentleman, who had apparently been disturbed at his tea, for he was disposing of the last remnant of a crust and butter when he came in, stationed himself close to Mr.", "다소 무뚝뚝해 보이는 세 번째 신사는, 들어올 때 빵 껍질에 버터 바른 것의 마지막 조각을 처치하고 있었던 것으로 보아 차를 마시다 방해받은 모양인데, 픽윅 씨 바로 곁에 자리 잡고는 두 손을 허리에 짚은 채 그를 면밀히 검사했다."], ["Pickwick; and, resting his hands on his hips, inspected him narrowly; while two others mixed with the group, and studied his features with most intent and thoughtful faces.", "한편 다른 두 사람도 그 무리에 끼어, 더없이 골똘하고 신중한 낯빛으로 그의 이목구비를 연구했다."], ["Mr. Pickwick winced a good deal under the operation, and appeared to sit very uneasily in his chair; but he made no remark to anybody while it was being performed, not even to Sam, who reclined upon the back of the chair, reflecting, partly on the situation of his master, and partly on the great satisfaction it would have afforded him to make a fierce assault upon all the turnkeys there assembled, one after the other, if it were lawful and peaceable so to do.", "픽윅 씨는 그 작업 내내 꽤나 움찔거렸고, 의자에 무척 불편하게 앉아 있는 듯했으나, 그것이 진행되는 동안 누구에게도, 심지어 샘에게조차 아무 말도 하지 않았으니, 샘은 의자 등받이에 기댄 채, 한편으로는 주인의 처지를 곰곰이 생각하고, 한편으로는 거기 모인 모든 간수들을 하나씩 차례로 사납게 들이받을 수 있다면—만약 그렇게 하는 것이 합법적이고 평화로운 일이기만 하다면—얼마나 큰 만족을 안겨 줄까를 곱씹고 있었다."]], [["At length the likeness was completed, and Mr. Pickwick was informed that he might now proceed into the prison.", "마침내 얼굴 모습 떠내기가 완료되었고, 픽윅 씨는 이제 감옥 안으로 들어가도 좋다는 통보를 받았다."]], [["‘Where am I to sleep to-night?’ inquired Mr.", "\"오늘 밤은 어디서 자야 하오?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘Why, I don’t rightly know about to-night,’ replied the stout turnkey.", "\"글쎄요, 오늘 밤은 잘 모르겠습니다.\" 건장한 간수가 대꾸했다."], ["‘You’ll be chummed on somebody to-morrow, and then you’ll be all snug and comfortable.", "\"내일이면 누군가와 한방을 쓰게 배정될 테니, 그러면 아늑하고 편안하게 지내실 겁니다."], ["The first night’s generally rather unsettled, but you’ll be set all squares to-morrow.’", "첫날 밤은 으레 좀 어수선하지만, 내일이면 다 자리가 잡힐 겁니다.\""]], [["After some discussion, it was discovered that one of the turnkeys had a bed to let, which Mr. Pickwick could have for that night.", "이런저런 의논 끝에, 간수들 중 하나에게 세놓을 침대가 하나 있어, 픽윅 씨가 그날 밤 그것을 쓸 수 있음이 드러났다."], ["He gladly agreed to hire it.", "그는 기꺼이 그것을 빌리기로 했다."]], [["‘If you’ll come with me, I’ll show it you at once,’ said the man.", "\"저랑 같이 가시면 당장 보여 드리지요.\" 그 사내가 말했다."], ["‘It ain’t a large ‘un; but it’s an out-and-outer to sleep in.", "\"큰 건 아니지만, 자는 데는 끝내주는 자립니다."], ["This way, sir.’", "이쪽입니다, 나리.\""]], [["They passed through the inner gate, and descended a short flight of steps.", "그들은 안쪽 문을 지나 짧은 계단을 내려갔다."], ["The key was turned after them; and Mr.", "그들 뒤에서 열쇠가 돌아갔다."], ["Pickwick found himself, for the first time in his life, within the walls of a debtors’ prison.", "그리하여 픽윅 씨는 난생처음으로 채무자 감옥의 담장 안에 있는 자신을 발견했다."]], [["CHAPTER XLI. WHAT BEFELL MR. PICKWICK WHEN HE GOT INTO THE FLEET; WHAT PRISONERS HE SAW THERE, AND HOW HE PASSED THE NIGHT\nMr. Tom Roker, the gentleman who had accompanied Mr. Pickwick into the prison, turned sharp round to the right when he got to the bottom of the little flight of steps, and led the way, through an iron gate which stood open, and up another short flight of steps, into a long narrow gallery, dirty and low, paved with stone, and very dimly lighted by a window at each remote end.", "픽윅 씨를 따라 감옥으로 들어온 신사인 톰 로커 씨는, 그 짧은 층계의 맨 아래에 이르자 오른쪽으로 홱 돌아서서, 열려 있는 철문을 지나 또 다른 짧은 층계를 올라가, 더럽고 천장이 낮으며 돌로 포장된, 그리고 양쪽 먼 끝에 난 창 하나로만 매우 흐릿하게 밝혀진 길고 좁은 복도로 앞장서 안내했다."]], [["‘This,’ said the gentleman, thrusting his hands into his pockets, and looking carelessly over his shoulder to Mr.", "\"여기가,\" 신사는 두 손을 주머니에 찔러 넣고 어깨너머로 픽윅 씨를 무심히 돌아보며 말했다."], ["Pickwick--‘this here is the hall flight.’", "\"여기가 바로 일층 복도지요.\""]], [["‘Oh,’ replied Mr.", "\"오,\""], ["Pickwick, looking down a dark and filthy staircase, which appeared to lead to a range of damp and gloomy stone vaults, beneath the ground, ‘and those, I suppose, are the little cellars where the prisoners keep their small quantities of coals.", "픽윅 씨는 어둡고 더러운 계단을 내려다보며 대답했는데, 그 계단은 땅 밑으로 죽 이어진 축축하고 음침한 돌 지하실로 통하는 것처럼 보였다. \"그리고 저것들은, 짐작컨대, 죄수들이 자기네 얼마 안 되는 석탄을 보관하는 작은 광들이겠군요."], ["Unpleasant places to have to go down to; but very convenient, I dare say.’", "내려가야 할 일이 생기면 영 불쾌한 곳이지만, 어쨌든 아주 편리하겠습니다.\""]], [["‘Yes, I shouldn’t wonder if they was convenient,’ replied the gentleman, ‘seeing that a few people live there, pretty snug.", "\"네, 편리하다고 해도 놀랄 일은 아니죠,\" 신사가 대답했다."], ["That’s the Fair, that is.’", "\"거기엔 사람 몇이 살거든요, 꽤나 아늑하게 말입니다. 저게 박람횟간(폐어)이지요, 저게요.\""]], [["‘My friend,’ said Mr.", "\"이보시오,\""], ["Pickwick, ‘you don’t really mean to say that human beings live down in those wretched dungeons?’", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"정말로 저 비참한 지하 감방에 사람들이 산다는 말을 하는 건 아니겠지요?\""]], [["‘Don’t I?’ replied Mr.", "\"그게 아니라뇨?\""], ["Roker, with indignant astonishment; ‘why shouldn’t I?’", "로커 씨가 분개하여 놀라며 대답했다. \"안 될 게 뭐 있습니까?\""]], [["‘Live!--live down there!’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"산다고요!—저 아래에서 산다고요!\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 외쳤다."]], [["‘Live down there!", "\"저 아래에서 살죠!"], ["Yes, and die down there, too, very often!’ replied Mr.", "네, 게다가 저 아래에서 죽기도 합니다, 아주 흔히요!\" 로커 씨가 대답했다."], ["Roker; ‘and what of that?", "\"그게 어쨌다는 겁니까?"], ["Who’s got to say anything agin it?", "누가 그걸 두고 뭐라 할 수 있단 말입니까?"], ["Live down there!", "저 아래에서 살죠!"], ["Yes, and a wery good place it is to live in, ain’t it?’", "네, 살기에 아주 좋은 곳이라니까요, 안 그래요?\""]], [["As Roker turned somewhat fiercely upon Mr. Pickwick in saying this, and moreover muttered in an excited fashion certain unpleasant invocations concerning his own eyes, limbs, and circulating fluids, the latter gentleman deemed it advisable to pursue the discourse no further.", "로커가 이 말을 하면서 픽윅 씨를 향해 다소 사납게 돌아섰고, 더욱이 자기 눈과 사지와 혈액에 관한 어떤 불쾌한 저주들을 흥분한 어조로 중얼거렸으므로, 픽윅 씨는 이 이야기를 더 이어가지 않는 편이 현명하겠다고 판단했다."], ["Mr. Roker then proceeded to mount another staircase, as dirty as that which led to the place which has just been the subject of discussion, in which ascent he was closely followed by Mr.", "로커 씨는 그러고 나서 또 다른 계단을 오르기 시작했는데, 방금 화제가 된 그 장소로 통하던 계단만큼이나 더러웠다."], ["Pickwick and Sam.", "그가 그 계단을 오르는 동안 픽윅 씨와 샘이 바짝 뒤를 따랐다."]], [["‘There,’ said Mr.", "\"자,\""], ["Roker, pausing for breath when they reached another gallery of the same dimensions as the one below, ‘this is the coffee- room flight; the one above’s the third, and the one above that’s the top; and the room where you’re a-going to sleep to-night is the warden’s room, and it’s this way--come on.’ Having said all this in a breath, Mr.", "아래층 복도와 똑같은 크기의 또 다른 복도에 이르자 로커 씨가 숨을 돌리려 멈춰 서며 말했다. \"여기가 커피실 복도고, 그 위층이 삼층, 그 위가 꼭대기층입니다."], ["Roker mounted another flight of stairs with Mr.", "그리고 오늘 밤 당신이 잘 방은 간수실인데, 이쪽입니다—오시죠.\""], ["Pickwick and Sam Weller following at his heels.", "이 모든 말을 단숨에 내뱉고서, 로커 씨는 또 한 차례 계단을 올랐고, 픽윅 씨와 샘 웰러가 그 뒤를 바짝 따랐다."]], [["These staircases received light from sundry windows placed at some little distance above the floor, and looking into a gravelled area bounded by a high brick wall, with iron chevaux-de-frise at the top. This area, it appeared from Mr.", "이 계단들은 바닥에서 조금 위에 자리한 여러 창을 통해 빛을 받아들였는데, 그 창들은 높은 벽돌담으로 둘러싸인 자갈 마당을 내다보고 있었고, 담 꼭대기에는 쇠 방어책(슈보드프리즈)이 박혀 있었다."], ["Roker’s statement, was the racket- ground; and it further appeared, on the testimony of the same gentleman, that there was a smaller area in that portion of the prison which was nearest Farringdon Street, denominated and called ‘the Painted Ground,’ from the fact of its walls having once displayed the semblance of various men-of-war in full sail, and other artistical effects achieved in bygone times by some imprisoned draughtsman in his leisure hours.", "이 마당은, 로커 씨의 말에 따르면, 라켓 경기장이었다. 그리고 같은 신사의 증언에 따르면, 감옥의 패링던 거리에 가장 가까운 부분에는 더 작은 마당이 있었는데, '채색 마당'이라 불렸으니, 그 담벼락이 한때 돛을 활짝 편 갖가지 군함의 형상과, 옛 시절 어느 수감된 도안공이 한가한 시간에 그려 놓은 그 밖의 예술적 솜씨들을 드러냈던 까닭에서였다."]], [["Having communicated this piece of information, apparently more for the purpose of discharging his bosom of an important fact, than with any specific view of enlightening Mr. Pickwick, the guide, having at length reached another gallery, led the way into a small passage at the extreme end, opened a door, and disclosed an apartment of an appearance by no means inviting, containing eight or nine iron bedsteads.", "이런 정보를 일러 준 것은, 픽윅 씨를 깨우치려는 어떤 구체적인 의도에서라기보다는, 분명 중요한 사실 하나를 가슴에서 털어 버리려는 목적에서였던 듯한데, 안내인은 마침내 또 다른 복도에 이르자 맨 끝의 작은 통로로 앞장서 들어가 문 하나를 열었고, 결코 마음 끌리는 모습이라고는 할 수 없는, 여덟아홉 개의 쇠 침대틀이 놓인 방을 드러냈다."]], [["‘There,’ said Mr.", "\"자,\""], ["Roker, holding the door open, and looking triumphantly round at Mr.", "로커 씨가 문을 잡고서 의기양양하게 픽윅 씨를 돌아보며 말했다."], ["Pickwick, ‘there’s a room!’", "\"방이올시다!\""]], [["Mr. Pickwick’s face, however, betokened such a very trifling portion of satisfaction at the appearance of his lodging, that Mr. Roker looked, for a reciprocity of feeling, into the countenance of Samuel Weller, who, until now, had observed a dignified silence.", "그러나 픽윅 씨의 얼굴은 자기 숙소의 꼴에 대해 지극히 하찮은 정도의 만족만을 드러냈으므로, 로커 씨는 감정의 공감을 얻으려고 새뮤얼 웰러의 얼굴을 들여다보았는데, 웰러는 그때까지 위엄 있는 침묵을 지키고 있었다."]], [["‘There’s a room, young man,’ observed Mr.", "\"방이 있구먼, 젊은이,\""], ["Roker.", "로커 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘I see it,’ replied Sam, with a placid nod of the head.", "\"보입니다,\" 샘은 평온하게 고개를 끄덕이며 대답했다."]], [["‘You wouldn’t think to find such a room as this in the Farringdon Hotel, would you?’ said Mr.", "\"패링던 호텔에서 이런 방을 찾으리라곤 생각 못 했을걸, 안 그래?\""], ["Roker, with a complacent smile.", "로커 씨가 흡족한 미소를 지으며 말했다."]], [["To this Mr. Weller replied with an easy and unstudied closing of one eye; which might be considered to mean, either that he would have thought it, or that he would not have thought it, or that he had never thought anything at all about it, as the observer’s imagination suggested. Having executed this feat, and reopened his eye, Mr. Weller proceeded to inquire which was the individual bedstead that Mr. Roker had so flatteringly described as an out-and-outer to sleep in.", "이에 웰러 씨는 한쪽 눈을 편안하고 꾸밈없이 감는 것으로 대답했는데, 그것은 그가 그렇게 생각했으리라는 뜻으로도, 그렇게 생각 못 했으리라는 뜻으로도, 또는 그 일에 대해 애초에 아무 생각도 해본 적 없다는 뜻으로도 받아들일 수 있었으니, 보는 이의 상상이 일러주는 대로였다. 이 묘기를 해내고 다시 눈을 뜬 뒤, 웰러 씨는 로커 씨가 그토록 아첨하듯 잠자기에 더없이 그만이라고 묘사한 그 침대틀이 어느 것이냐고 물었다."]], [["‘That’s it,’ replied Mr.", "\"저것일세,\""], ["Roker, pointing to a very rusty one in a corner.", "로커 씨가 한구석에 놓인 매우 녹슨 침대를 가리키며 대답했다."], ["‘It would make any one go to sleep, that bedstead would, whether they wanted to or not.’", "\"저 침대틀은 누구라도 잠들게 만들지, 본인이 원하든 원치 않든 말이야.\""]], [["‘I should think,’ said Sam, eyeing the piece of furniture in question with a look of excessive disgust--‘I should think poppies was nothing to it.’", "\"제 생각엔,\" 샘이 문제의 그 가구를 극도의 혐오가 담긴 눈길로 바라보며 말했다. \"양귀비도 저것에 비하면 아무것도 아닐 것 같습니다요.\""]], [["‘Nothing at all,’ said Mr.", "\"아무것도 아니지,\""], ["Roker.", "로커 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘And I s’pose,’ said Sam, with a sidelong glance at his master, as if to see whether there were any symptoms of his determination being shaken by what passed, ‘I s’pose the other gen’l’men as sleeps here are gen’l’men.’", "\"그리고 짐작컨대,\" 샘은 방금 오간 이야기로 주인의 결심이 흔들리는 기미가 있는지 살피려는 듯 주인 쪽을 곁눈질하며 말했다. \"여기서 자는 다른 신사분들도 신사분들이겠지요.\""]], [["‘Nothing but it,’ said Mr.", "\"바로 그렇지,\" 로커 씨가 말했다."], ["Roker. ‘One of ‘em takes his twelve pints of ale a day, and never leaves off smoking even at his meals.’", "\"그중 하나는 하루에 에일 열두 파인트를 마시고, 식사 때조차 담배를 손에서 놓지 않는다네.\""]], [["‘He must be a first-rater,’ said Sam.", "\"대단한 일급 인물이겠습니다요,\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["‘A1,’ replied Mr.", "\"최고급이지,\""], ["Roker.", "로커 씨가 대답했다."]], [["Nothing daunted, even by this intelligence, Mr. Pickwick smilingly announced his determination to test the powers of the narcotic bedstead for that night; and Mr.", "이런 소식에도 전혀 기죽지 않고, 픽윅 씨는 그날 밤 그 마취제 같은 침대틀의 위력을 시험해 보겠노라고 미소 지으며 알렸다."], ["Roker, after informing him that he could retire to rest at whatever hour he thought proper, without any further notice or formality, walked off, leaving him standing with Sam in the gallery.", "그리고 로커 씨는, 별다른 통지나 격식 없이 아무 때나 마음 내키는 시각에 잠자리에 들어도 된다고 일러 준 뒤, 픽윅 씨를 샘과 함께 복도에 세워 둔 채 떠나갔다."]], [["It was getting dark; that is to say, a few gas jets were kindled in this place which was never light, by way of compliment to the evening, which had set in outside.", "날이 저물고 있었다. 다시 말해, 결코 밝은 적 없는 이곳에도 바깥에 깃든 저녁에 대한 예의로 가스등 몇 개가 켜졌다."], ["As it was rather warm, some of the tenants of the numerous little rooms which opened into the gallery on either hand, had set their doors ajar.", "날씨가 다소 따뜻했으므로, 양쪽으로 복도와 통하는 수많은 작은 방들의 거주자 가운데 몇몇은 문을 반쯤 열어 두고 있었다."], ["Mr. Pickwick peeped into them as he passed along, with great curiosity and interest.", "픽윅 씨는 지나가면서 큰 호기심과 흥미를 가지고 그 방들을 들여다보았다."], ["Here, four or five great hulking fellows, just visible through a cloud of tobacco smoke, were engaged in noisy and riotous conversation over half-emptied pots of beer, or playing at all-fours with a very greasy pack of cards.", "여기서는, 담배 연기 구름 사이로 간신히 보이는 덩치 큰 사내 네댓 명이 절반쯤 비운 맥주 단지를 놓고 시끄럽고 떠들썩한 대화를 나누거나, 몹시 때 묻은 카드 한 벌로 올포어즈 놀이를 하고 있었다."], ["In the adjoining room, some solitary tenant might be seen poring, by the light of a feeble tallow candle, over a bundle of soiled and tattered papers, yellow with dust and dropping to pieces from age, writing, for the hundredth time, some lengthened statement of his grievances, for the perusal of some great man whose eyes it would never reach, or whose heart it would never touch.", "그 옆방에서는, 어떤 외로운 거주자가 희미한 수지 양초 불빛에 의지해, 먼지로 누렇게 바래고 세월에 낡아 부스러져 가는 더럽고 너덜너덜한 서류 뭉치를 들여다보며, 백 번째로 자기 고충을 길게 늘어놓는 진술서를 쓰고 있는 모습이 보였으니, 그것은 결코 그 눈에 닿지 않을, 혹은 그 가슴을 결코 움직이지 못할 어느 높으신 분이 읽어 주기를 바라며 쓰는 것이었다."], ["In a third, a man, with his wife and a whole crowd of children, might be seen making up a scanty bed on the ground, or upon a few chairs, for the younger ones to pass the night in.", "세 번째 방에서는, 한 사내가 아내와 한 무리의 아이들과 함께, 어린것들이 밤을 보내도록 바닥이나 의자 몇 개 위에 빈약한 잠자리를 마련하고 있었다."], ["And in a fourth, and a fifth, and a sixth, and a seventh, the noise, and the beer, and the tobacco smoke, and the cards, all came over again in greater force than before.", "그리고 네 번째, 다섯 번째, 여섯 번째, 일곱 번째 방에서는, 소음과 맥주와 담배 연기와 카드가 모두 전보다 더 큰 기세로 되풀이되고 있었다."]], [["In the galleries themselves, and more especially on the stair-cases, there lingered a great number of people, who came there, some because their rooms were empty and lonesome, others because their rooms were full and hot; the greater part because they were restless and uncomfortable, and not possessed of the secret of exactly knowing what to do with themselves.", "복도 자체에도, 특히 계단에는 수많은 사람이 어슬렁거리고 있었으니, 어떤 이는 자기 방이 비어 적막해서, 어떤 이는 자기 방이 꽉 차서 더워서 나와 있었다. 그러나 대부분은 안절부절못하고 불편한 데다, 자기 자신을 정확히 어떻게 다루어야 할지 그 비결을 알지 못해서였다."], ["There were many classes of people here, from the labouring man in his fustian jacket, to the broken-down spendthrift in his shawl dressing-gown, most appropriately out at elbows; but there was the same air about them all--a kind of listless, jail-bird, careless swagger, a vagabondish who’s-afraid sort of bearing, which is wholly indescribable in words, but which any man can understand in one moment if he wish, by setting foot in the nearest debtors’ prison, and looking at the very first group of people he sees there, with the same interest as Mr.", "여기에는 여러 부류의 사람들이 있었으니, 면포 재킷을 입은 노동자에서부터, 더없이 어울리게도 팔꿈치가 해진 숄 가운을 걸친 영락한 낭비가에 이르기까지 다양했다. 그러나 그들 모두에게는 똑같은 분위기가 감돌았으니—일종의 나른하고, 옥살이에 찌든, 무심한 거들먹거림, 떠돌이 같은 \"그래서 어쩔 건데\""], ["Pickwick did.", "하는 태도였다. 그것은 말로는 도저히 표현할 수 없는 것이지만, 누구든 원한다면 가장 가까운 채무자 감옥에 발을 들여, 픽윅 씨가 그러했던 것과 똑같은 흥미를 가지고 거기서 처음 눈에 띄는 사람 무리를 바라보기만 하면, 단번에 이해할 수 있는 것이었다."]], [["‘It strikes me, Sam,’ said Mr.", "\"내 생각엔 말일세, 샘,\""], ["Pickwick, leaning over the iron rail at the stair-head, ‘it strikes me, Sam, that imprisonment for debt is scarcely any punishment at all.’", "픽윅 씨가 계단참의 쇠 난간에 기대며 말했다. \"내 생각엔, 샘, 빚 때문에 갇히는 일은 도무지 형벌이라고 할 게 못 되는 것 같네.\""]], [["‘Think not, sir?’ inquired Mr.", "\"그렇게 생각 안 하십니까요, 나리?\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘You see how these fellows drink, and smoke, and roar,’ replied Mr.", "\"이 친구들이 어떻게 마시고, 담배 피우고, 떠들어 대는지 보게나,\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다."], ["Pickwick. ‘It’s quite impossible that they can mind it much.’", "\"이들이 이걸 크게 괴로워한다는 건 도무지 있을 수 없는 일이야.\""]], [["‘Ah, that’s just the wery thing, Sir,’ rejoined Sam, ‘they don’t mind it; it’s a reg’lar holiday to them--all porter and skittles. It’s the t’other vuns as gets done over vith this sort o’ thing; them down- hearted fellers as can’t svig avay at the beer, nor play at skittles neither; them as vould pay if they could, and gets low by being boxed up. I’ll tell you wot it is, sir; them as is always a-idlin’ in public- houses it don’t damage at all, and them as is alvays a-workin’ wen they can, it damages too much. “It’s unekal,” as my father used to say wen his grog worn’t made half-and-half: “it’s unekal, and that’s the fault on it.”’", "\"아, 바로 그게 문제입죠, 나리,\" 샘이 받아 말했다. \"저들은 괴로워하지 않습니다요. 저들한텐 아주 휴일이나 다름없지요—온통 흑맥주에 구주희(스키틀) 놀이죠. 이런 식의 일로 망가지는 건 딴 사람들이올시다. 맥주를 들이켜지도 못하고 구주희를 치지도 못하는, 그 풀 죽은 친구들 말입죠. 돈을 낼 수 있다면 낼 텐데, 갇혀 있는 바람에 풀이 죽어 버린 사람들요. 제가 말씀드리자면 이렇습니다, 나리. 늘 술집에서 빈둥거리는 자들한테는 이게 조금도 해가 안 되고, 할 수 있을 때면 늘 일하는 자들한테는 지나치게 해가 됩지요. '이건 공평치가 않아,' 제 아버지가 그로그가 반반으로 안 섞였을 때 늘 하시던 말씀이올시다. '이건 공평치가 않아, 그게 흠이지.'\""]], [["‘I think you’re right, Sam,’ said Mr.", "\"자네 말이 맞는 것 같네, 샘,\""], ["Pickwick, after a few moments’ reflection, ‘quite right.’", "잠시 생각에 잠겼다가 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"아주 옳은 말이야.\""]], [["‘P’raps, now and then, there’s some honest people as likes it,’ observed Mr.", "\"어쩌면, 이따금, 이걸 좋아하는 정직한 사람도 더러 있긴 합죠,\" 웰러 씨가 곰곰이 생각하는 어조로 말했다."], ["Weller, in a ruminative tone, ‘but I never heerd o’ one as I can call to mind, ‘cept the little dirty-faced man in the brown coat; and that was force of habit.’", "\"하지만 제가 떠올릴 수 있는 사람으로는, 갈색 외투를 입은 얼굴 작고 더러운 사내 말고는 한 번도 들어 본 적이 없습니다요. 게다가 그것도 습관의 힘이었습죠.\""]], [["‘And who was he?’ inquired Mr.", "\"그래, 그가 누구였나?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘Wy, that’s just the wery point as nobody never know’d,’ replied Sam.", "\"아, 바로 그게 아무도 한 번도 알아내지 못한 점입죠,\" 샘이 대답했다."]], [["‘But what did he do?’", "\"그런데 그가 뭘 했단 말인가?\""]], [["‘Wy, he did wot many men as has been much better know’d has done in their time, Sir,’ replied Sam, ‘he run a match agin the constable, and vun it.’", "\"아, 그자는 그자보다 훨씬 더 이름난 많은 사람들이 한때 했던 일을 했습죠, 나리,\" 샘이 대답했다. \"집행관과 시합을 벌여서, 이겼지요.\""]], [["‘In other words, I suppose,’ said Mr.", "\"바꿔 말하면, 짐작컨대,\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick, ‘he got into debt.’", "\"빚을 졌다는 거군.\""]], [["‘Just that, Sir,’ replied Sam, ‘and in course o’ time he come here in consekens.", "\"바로 그렇습니다요, 나리,\" 샘이 대답했다."], ["It warn’t much--execution for nine pound nothin’, multiplied by five for costs; but hows’ever here he stopped for seventeen year.", "\"그러다 때가 되니 그 결과로 여기 들어온 거죠. 큰 빚도 아니었습니다—집행이 구 파운드에 한 푼도 안 되는 거였고, 거기에 비용으로 다섯 배가 곱해졌습죠."], ["If he got any wrinkles in his face, they were stopped up vith the dirt, for both the dirty face and the brown coat wos just the same at the end o’ that time as they wos at the beginnin’.", "그래도 어쨌든 그자는 여기서 십칠 년을 머물렀습니다요. 얼굴에 주름이 잡혔다 해도 때로 메워졌을 겁니다, 그 더러운 얼굴이나 갈색 외투나 그 세월이 끝날 무렵에도 처음과 똑같았으니까요."], ["He wos a wery peaceful, inoffendin’ little creetur, and wos alvays a-bustlin’ about for somebody, or playin’ rackets and never vinnin’; till at last the turnkeys they got quite fond on him, and he wos in the lodge ev’ry night, a-chattering vith ‘em, and tellin’ stories, and all that ‘ere.", "그자는 아주 온순하고 남한테 해 끼치지 않는 작은 사람이었고, 늘 누군가를 위해 부산을 떨거나 라켓을 치면서도 한 번도 이기는 법이 없었습죠. 그러다 마침내 간수들이 그자를 아주 좋아하게 됐고, 그자는 밤마다 수위실에 와서 그들과 잡담을 나누고 이야기를 들려주고 그러곤 했답니다."], ["Vun night he wos in there as usual, along vith a wery old friend of his, as wos on the lock, ven he says all of a sudden, “I ain’t seen the market outside, Bill,”", "어느 날 밤 그자가 여느 때처럼 거기 들어가, 자물쇠를 맡고 있던 아주 오랜 친구 하나와 같이 있다가, 갑자기 이러는 겁니다, '난 십칠 년 동안 바깥 시장을 못 봤어, 빌,'"], ["he says (Fleet Market wos there at that time)--“I ain’t seen the market outside, Bill,” he says, “for seventeen year.”", "하고요 (그때 거기엔 플리트 시장이 있었거든요)—'난 십칠 년 동안 바깥 시장을 못 봤어, 빌,'"], ["“I know you ain’t,”", "하고요. '안 봤겠지,'"], ["says the turnkey, smoking his pipe.", "간수가 파이프를 피우며 말합니다."], ["“I should like to see it for a minit, Bill,” he says.", "'잠깐만이라도 그걸 보고 싶어, 빌,'"], ["“Wery probable,”", "그자가 말합니다."], ["says the turnkey, smoking his pipe wery fierce, and making believe he warn’t up to wot the little man wanted.", "'그럴 만도 하지,' 간수가 파이프를 아주 사납게 빨며, 작은 사내가 뭘 원하는지 모르는 척하며 말합니다."], ["“Bill,”", "'빌,'"], ["says the little man, more abrupt than afore, “I’ve got the fancy in my head.", "작은 사내가 아까보다 더 퉁명스럽게 말합니다. '머릿속에 그 생각이 들었어."], ["Let me see the public streets once more afore I die; and if I ain’t struck with apoplexy, I’ll be back in five minits by the clock.”", "죽기 전에 한 번만 더 거리를 보게 해 줘. 졸중이라도 일어나지 않는 한, 시계로 오 분 안에 돌아올게.'"], ["“And wot ‘ud become o’ me if you wos struck with apoplexy?”", "'그럼 네가 졸중이라도 일어나면 내 꼴은 어떻게 되고?'"], ["said the turnkey.", "간수가 말합니다."], ["“Wy,”", "'아니,'"], ["says the little creetur, “whoever found me, ‘ud bring me home, for I’ve got my card in my pocket, Bill,”", "작은 사내가 말합니다. '누가 날 발견하든 집으로 데려다줄 거야, 내 주머니에 명함이 있으니까, 빌,'"], ["he says, “No.", "그자가 말합니다. '커피실 복도 20호'라고 말이죠."], ["20, Coffee-room Flight”: and that wos true, sure enough, for wen he wanted to make the acquaintance of any new-comer, he used to pull out a little limp card vith them words on it and nothin’ else; in consideration of vich, he vos alvays called Number Tventy.", "그런데 그건 정말 사실이었습죠, 새로 온 사람과 사귀고 싶을 때면 그자는 그 글자만 적히고 다른 건 아무것도 없는 작고 흐물흐물한 명함을 꺼내 들곤 했으니까요. 그래서 그자는 늘 20호라고 불렸답니다."], ["The turnkey takes a fixed look at him, and at last he says in a solemn manner, “Tventy,”", "간수가 그자를 빤히 바라보다가 마침내 엄숙한 태도로 말합니다, '20호,' 그가 말합니다."], ["he says, “I’ll trust you; you Won’t get your old friend into trouble.”", "'내 자넬 믿겠네. 자넨 오랜 친구를 곤경에 빠뜨리진 않겠지.'"], ["“No, my boy; I hope I’ve somethin’ better behind here,”", "'암, 그래야지, 친구. 내 가슴속엔 그보다 나은 게 있다고 믿네,'"], ["says the little man; and as he said it he hit his little vesket wery hard, and then a tear started out o’ each eye, which wos wery extraordinary, for it wos supposed as water never touched his face.", "작은 사내가 말합니다. 그러고는 그 말을 하면서 자기 작은 조끼를 세게 두드렸고, 그러자 두 눈에서 눈물이 한 방울씩 솟아났는데, 그건 아주 비상한 일이었습죠, 물이 그자 얼굴에 닿은 적이 없다고들 여겼으니까요."], ["He shook the turnkey by the hand; out he vent--’", "그자는 간수와 악수를 했고, 밖으로 나갔습니다—\""]], [["‘And never came back again,’ said Mr.", "\"그러곤 다시는 돌아오지 않았겠지,\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Wrong for vunce, sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"한 번은 틀리셨습니다요, 나리,\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다."], ["Weller, ‘for back he come, two minits afore the time, a-bilin’ with rage, sayin’ how he’d been nearly run over by a hackney-coach that he warn’t used to it; and he was blowed if he wouldn’t write to the lord mayor.", "\"그자는 약속한 시간보다 이 분 일찍 돌아왔거든요, 분통이 끓어올라서요. 자기가 익숙지 않은 삯마차에 치일 뻔했다며, 시장(市長) 어른께 편지를 쓰지 않으면 자기가 사람도 아니라고 했답니다."], ["They got him pacified at last; and for five years arter that, he never even so much as peeped out o’ the lodge gate.’", "마침내 사람들이 그자를 진정시켰고, 그 후 오 년 동안 그자는 수위실 문밖을 한 번도 내다보지조차 않았습죠.\""]], [["‘At the expiration of that time he died, I suppose,’ said Mr.", "\"그 기간이 끝나고 죽었겠군, 짐작컨대,\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘No, he didn’t, Sir,’ replied Sam.", "\"아닙니다, 안 죽었습니다요, 나리,\" 샘이 대답했다."], ["‘He got a curiosity to go and taste the beer at a new public-house over the way, and it wos such a wery nice parlour, that he took it into his head to go there every night, which he did for a long time, always comin’ back reg’lar about a quarter of an hour afore the gate shut, which was all wery snug and comfortable.", "\"그자는 길 건너 새로 생긴 술집에 가서 맥주 맛을 보고 싶다는 호기심이 일었는데, 그 응접실이 어찌나 좋던지 밤마다 거기 가기로 마음먹었고, 오랫동안 그렇게 했답니다. 늘 문 닫기 십오 분쯤 전에 규칙적으로 돌아오곤 했으니, 모든 게 아주 아늑하고 편안했죠."], ["At last he began to get so precious jolly, that he used to forget how the time vent, or care nothin’ at all about it, and he went on gettin’ later and later, till vun night his old friend wos just a-shuttin’ the gate-- had turned the key in fact--wen he come up.", "그러다 마침내 그자는 너무도 흥겨워진 나머지, 시간이 어떻게 가는지 잊거나 그에 대해 전혀 신경 쓰지 않게 됐고, 점점 더 늦어지다가, 어느 날 밤 그자의 오랜 친구가 막 문을 닫으려던 참에—사실은 열쇠를 돌려 버린 참에—그자가 올라온 겁니다. '잠깐만, 빌,'"], ["“Hold hard, Bill,”", "그자가 말합니다."], ["he says. “Wot, ain’t you come home yet, Tventy?”", "'뭐야, 아직 안 들어왔었어, 20호?'"], ["says the turnkey, “I thought you wos in, long ago.”", "간수가 말합니다. '난 자네가 진작 들어온 줄 알았는데.'"], ["“No, I wasn’t,”", "'아니, 안 들어왔어,'"], ["says the little man, with a smile.", "작은 사내가 미소 지으며 말합니다."], ["“Well, then, I’ll tell you wot it is, my friend,”", "'그럼, 이보게 친구, 내 말하겠네,'"], ["says the turnkey, openin’ the gate wery slow and sulky, “it’s my ‘pinion as you’ve got into bad company o’ late, which I’m wery sorry to see.", "간수가 문을 아주 천천히 시무룩하게 열며 말합니다. '내 생각엔 자네가 요즘 나쁜 패거리와 어울리게 된 것 같아, 그걸 보니 아주 안타깝군."], ["Now, I don’t wish to do nothing harsh,”", "자, 난 모진 짓은 하고 싶지 않네,'"], ["he says, “but if you can’t confine yourself to steady circles, and find your vay back at reg’lar hours, as sure as you’re a-standin’ there, I’ll shut you out altogether!”", "그가 말합니다. '하지만 자네가 점잖은 모임으로 처신을 한정하고 규칙적인 시간에 돌아올 수 없다면, 자네가 거기 서 있는 게 분명한 것만큼이나 분명하게, 내 자넬 아주 내쫓아 버리겠네!'"], ["The little man was seized vith a wiolent fit o’ tremblin’, and never vent outside the prison walls artervards!’", "작은 사내는 심한 떨림 발작에 사로잡혔고, 그 후로는 다시는 감옥 담장 밖으로 나가지 않았답니다!\""]], [["As Sam concluded, Mr. Pickwick slowly retraced his steps downstairs.", "샘이 이야기를 마치자, 픽윅 씨는 천천히 다시 계단을 내려갔다."], ["After a few thoughtful turns in the Painted Ground, which, as it was now dark, was nearly deserted, he intimated to Mr. Weller that he thought it high time for him to withdraw for the night; requesting him to seek a bed in some adjacent public-house, and return early in the morning, to make arrangements for the removal of his master’s wardrobe from the George and Vulture.", "이제 어두워져서 거의 인적이 끊긴 채색 마당을 생각에 잠겨 몇 바퀴 돈 뒤, 그는 웰러 씨에게 이제 밤을 위해 물러갈 때가 된 것 같다고 알리며, 인근 술집에서 잠자리를 구하고, 아침 일찍 돌아와 주인의 옷가지를 조지 앤드 벌처에서 옮겨 올 채비를 하라고 일렀다."], ["This request Mr. Samuel Weller prepared to obey, with as good a grace as he could assume, but with a very considerable show of reluctance nevertheless.", "새뮤얼 웰러 씨는 이 부탁을 가능한 한 좋은 낯빛으로 따르려 했으나, 그럼에도 상당히 마지못한 기색을 드러냈다."], ["He even went so far as to essay sundry ineffectual hints regarding the expediency of stretching himself on the gravel for that night; but finding Mr. Pickwick obstinately deaf to any such suggestions, finally withdrew.", "그는 심지어 그날 밤 자갈밭에 몸을 뉘는 편이 좋겠다는 갖가지 부질없는 암시를 슬쩍 던져 보기까지 했으나, 픽윅 씨가 그런 제안에는 한사코 귀를 막고 있는 것을 알고는, 마침내 물러갔다."]], [["There is no disguising the fact that Mr. Pickwick felt very low-spirited and uncomfortable--not for lack of society, for the prison was very full, and a bottle of wine would at once have purchased the utmost good- fellowship of a few choice spirits, without any more formal ceremony of introduction; but he was alone in the coarse, vulgar crowd, and felt the depression of spirits and sinking of heart, naturally consequent on the reflection that he was cooped and caged up, without a prospect of liberation.", "픽윅 씨가 몹시 의기소침하고 불편함을 느꼈다는 사실은 감출 수 없다—사람들과 어울릴 데가 없어서는 아니었으니, 감옥은 사람들로 매우 붐볐고, 포도주 한 병이면 더 이상의 격식 차린 소개 절차 없이도 당장에 몇몇 별난 부류의 더없는 친교를 살 수 있었을 것이다."], ["As to the idea of releasing himself by ministering to the sharpness of Dodson & Fogg, it never for an instant entered his thoughts.", "그러나 그는 거칠고 천박한 군중 속에서 혼자였고, 풀려날 가망도 없이 갇히고 우리에 넣어졌다는 생각에서 자연히 따라오는, 그 의기소침과 가슴이 가라앉는 느낌을 맛보고 있었다. 도드슨과 포그의 모진 요구를 들어줌으로써 스스로 풀려나겠다는 생각은, 단 한순간도 그의 머릿속에 떠오르지 않았다."]], [["In this frame of mind he turned again into the coffee-room gallery, and walked slowly to and fro.", "이런 심정으로 그는 다시 커피실 복도로 들어서서 천천히 이리저리 거닐었다."], ["The place was intolerably dirty, and the smell of tobacco smoke perfectly suffocating.", "그곳은 견딜 수 없을 만큼 더러웠고, 담배 연기 냄새는 완전히 숨이 막힐 지경이었다."], ["There was a perpetual slamming and banging of doors as the people went in and out; and the noise of their voices and footsteps echoed and re-echoed through the passages constantly.", "사람들이 드나들면서 끊임없이 문을 쾅쾅 여닫았고, 그들의 목소리와 발소리가 통로를 따라 쉴 새 없이 메아리치고 또 메아리쳤다."], ["A young woman, with a child in her arms, who seemed scarcely able to crawl, from emaciation and misery, was walking up and down the passage in conversation with her husband, who had no other place to see her in. As they passed Mr.", "한 젊은 여인이, 야위고 비참한 탓에 거의 기지도 못할 듯한 아이를 품에 안고서, 통로를 오르내리며 남편과 이야기를 나누고 있었으니, 남편으로서는 그녀를 만날 다른 곳이 없었던 것이다."], ["Pickwick, he could hear the female sob bitterly; and once she burst into such a passion of grief, that she was compelled to lean against the wall for support, while the man took the child in his arms, and tried to soothe her.", "그들이 픽윅 씨 곁을 지날 때, 그는 그 여인이 비통하게 흐느끼는 소리를 들을 수 있었다. 그리고 한번은 그녀가 너무도 격렬한 슬픔에 북받쳐, 몸을 지탱하려고 벽에 기대지 않을 수 없었고, 그동안 남편은 아이를 팔에 안고 그녀를 달래려 애썼다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick’s heart was really too full to bear it, and he went upstairs to bed.", "픽윅 씨의 가슴은 정말이지 그것을 견디기에는 너무나 벅찼고, 그는 잠자리에 들려고 위층으로 올라갔다."]], [["Now, although the warder’s room was a very uncomfortable one (being, in every point of decoration and convenience, several hundred degrees inferior to the common infirmary of a county jail), it had at present the merit of being wholly deserted save by Mr.", "자, 비록 간수실은 매우 불편한 방이었지만(장식과 편의의 모든 점에서 어느 주(州) 감옥의 일반 병동보다도 몇백 도는 못한 방이었지만), 지금으로서는 픽윅 씨 자신을 제외하고는 완전히 비어 있다는 장점이 있었다."], ["Pickwick himself. So, he sat down at the foot of his little iron bedstead, and began to wonder how much a year the warder made out of the dirty room.", "그래서 그는 자신의 작은 쇠 침대틀 발치에 앉아, 간수가 이 더러운 방으로 한 해에 얼마나 버는지 궁금해하기 시작했다."], ["Having satisfied himself, by mathematical calculation, that the apartment was about equal in annual value to the freehold of a small street in the suburbs of London, he took to wondering what possible temptation could have induced a dingy-looking fly that was crawling over his pantaloons, to come into a close prison, when he had the choice of so many airy situations--a course of meditation which led him to the irresistible conclusion that the insect was insane.", "수학적 계산으로, 이 방의 연간 가치가 런던 변두리 작은 거리 하나의 자유 보유권과 대략 맞먹는다는 결론에 만족한 뒤, 그는 자기 바지 위를 기어 다니는 거무튀튀한 파리 한 마리가, 그토록 통풍 좋은 곳을 골라 갈 수 있었으면서 도대체 무슨 유혹에 이끌려 이 답답한 감옥에 들어왔을까 궁금해하기 시작했다—이 명상의 흐름은 결국 그 곤충이 미쳤다는 거부할 수 없는 결론으로 그를 이끌었다."], ["After settling this point, he began to be conscious that he was getting sleepy; whereupon he took his nightcap out of the pocket in which he had had the precaution to stow it in the morning, and, leisurely undressing himself, got into bed and fell asleep.", "이 점을 매듭짓고 나자, 그는 자신이 졸음에 겨워지는 것을 의식하기 시작했다. 그래서 그는 아침에 신중하게 챙겨 넣어 둔 주머니에서 잠자리 모자를 꺼냈고, 느긋하게 옷을 벗고 침대에 들어가 잠이 들었다."]], [["‘Bravo!", "\"브라보!"], ["Heel over toe--cut and shuffle--pay away at it, Zephyr!", "뒤꿈치를 돌리고—발끝을 차고 끌어라—한껏 해 봐라, 제피로스!"], ["I’m smothered if the opera house isn’t your proper hemisphere.", "오페라 극장이 네 본고장이 아니라면 내가 숨이 막혀 죽지. 계속해라!"], ["Keep it up!", "만세!\""], ["Hooray!’ These expressions, delivered in a most boisterous tone, and accompanied with loud peals of laughter, roused Mr. Pickwick from one of those sound slumbers which, lasting in reality some half-hour, seem to the sleeper to have been protracted for three weeks or a month.", "더없이 떠들썩한 어조로 내뱉어지고 요란한 웃음소리가 곁들여진 이 외침은, 실제로는 반 시간쯤 지속되었으나 잠든 이에게는 삼 주나 한 달은 이어진 듯이 느껴지는 그런 깊은 잠 가운데 하나로부터 픽윅 씨를 깨웠다."]], [["The voice had no sooner ceased than the room was shaken with such violence that the windows rattled in their frames, and the bedsteads trembled again.", "그 목소리가 멎자마자 방은 어찌나 격렬하게 흔들렸던지 창문이 창틀에서 덜컹거렸고 침대틀들이 거듭 떨렸다."], ["Mr. Pickwick started up, and remained for some minutes fixed in mute astonishment at the scene before him.", "픽윅 씨는 벌떡 일어나, 눈앞의 광경에 말없이 놀란 채 몇 분 동안 꼼짝 못 하고 있었다."]], [["On the floor of the room, a man in a broad-skirted green coat, with corduroy knee-smalls and gray cotton stockings, was performing the most popular steps of a hornpipe, with a slang and burlesque caricature of grace and lightness, which, combined with the very appropriate character of his costume, was inexpressibly absurd.", "방바닥에서, 자락 넓은 초록색 외투에 코르덴 무릎 반바지와 회색 무명 양말을 신은 한 사내가, 호른파이프의 가장 인기 있는 스텝들을, 우아함과 경쾌함을 속되고 익살스럽게 흉내 내며 추고 있었는데, 그의 의상이 지닌 더없이 어울리는 성격과 어우러져 형언할 수 없을 만큼 우스꽝스러웠다. 또 다른 사내는, 분명 몹시 취해서 아마도 동료들에게 침대로 떠밀려 들어간 듯한데, 시트 사이에 일어나 앉아, 자기가 기억해 낼 수 있는 만큼의 희극 노래를 더없이 감상에 젖은 감정과 표정으로 흥얼거리고 있었다."], ["Another man, evidently very drunk, who had probably been tumbled into bed by his companions, was sitting up between the sheets, warbling as much as he could recollect of a comic song, with the most intensely sentimental feeling and expression; while a third, seated on one of the bedsteads, was applauding both performers with the air of a profound connoisseur, and encouraging them by such ebullitions of feeling as had already roused Mr. Pickwick from his sleep.", "한편 세 번째 사내는, 침대틀 하나에 앉아, 깊은 감식가의 태도로 두 연기자에게 박수를 보내고, 이미 픽윅 씨를 잠에서 깨운 그런 감정의 발산으로 그들을 부추기고 있었다."]], [["This last man was an admirable specimen of a class of gentry which never can be seen in full perfection but in such places--they may be met with, in an imperfect state, occasionally about stable-yards and Public- houses; but they never attain their full bloom except in these hot-beds, which would almost seem to be considerately provided by the legislature for the sole purpose of rearing them.", "이 마지막 사내는, 그런 장소에서가 아니고서는 도저히 완전한 형태로 볼 수 없는 부류의 신사를 보여 주는 훌륭한 표본이었다—이들은 이따금 마구간 마당이나 술집 근처에서 불완전한 상태로 마주칠 수는 있으나, 마치 입법부가 오로지 이들을 길러 내려는 목적으로 사려 깊게 마련해 둔 듯한 이런 온상에서가 아니고서는 결코 만개에 이르지 못한다."]], [["He was a tall fellow, with an olive complexion, long dark hair, and very thick bushy whiskers meeting under his chin.", "그는 키 큰 사내로, 올리브빛 안색에, 길고 검은 머리카락과, 턱 밑에서 만나는 매우 무성하고 텁수룩한 구레나룻을 지니고 있었다."], ["He wore no neckerchief, as he had been playing rackets all day, and his open shirt collar displayed their full luxuriance.", "그는 종일 라켓을 친 탓에 목수건을 두르지 않았고, 풀어헤친 셔츠 깃은 그 구레나룻의 무성함을 한껏 드러내고 있었다."], ["On his head he wore one of the common eighteenpenny French skull-caps, with a gaudy tassel dangling therefrom, very happily in keeping with a common fustian coat.", "머리에는 흔한 십팔 펜스짜리 프랑스식 챙 없는 모자를 쓰고 있었는데, 거기 매달린 화려한 술이 흔한 면포 외투와 더없이 잘 어울렸다."], ["His legs, which, being long, were afflicted with weakness, graced a pair of Oxford- mixture trousers, made to show the full symmetry of those limbs.", "그의 다리는, 길긴 했으나 허약함에 시달려, 그 사지의 균형을 온전히 드러내도록 지은 옥스퍼드 혼방 바지로 멋을 부리고 있었다."], ["Being somewhat negligently braced, however, and, moreover, but imperfectly buttoned, they fell in a series of not the most graceful folds over a pair of shoes sufficiently down at heel to display a pair of very soiled white stockings.", "그러나 다소 아무렇게나 멜빵을 매고, 게다가 단추도 제대로 채우지 않아서, 바지는 결코 가장 우아하달 수 없는 여러 주름을 이루며, 뒤축이 충분히 닳아 매우 더러운 흰 양말 한 켤레가 드러나 보이는 구두 위로 흘러내려 있었다."], ["There was a rakish, vagabond smartness, and a kind of boastful rascality, about the whole man, that was worth a mine of gold.", "사내 전체에는 방탕하고 떠돌이 같은 멋과, 일종의 허세 부리는 악당의 기질이 감돌았으니, 그것은 금광 하나만큼의 값어치가 있는 것이었다."]], [["This figure was the first to perceive that Mr.", "이 인물이 픽윅 씨가 지켜보고 있다는 것을 가장 먼저 알아챘다."], ["Pickwick was looking on; upon which he winked to the Zephyr, and entreated him, with mock gravity, not to wake the gentleman.", "그러자 그는 제피로스에게 눈짓하며, 짐짓 엄숙한 척하면서 저 신사를 깨우지 말라고 간청했다."]], [["‘Why, bless the gentleman’s honest heart and soul!’ said the Zephyr, turning round and affecting the extremity of surprise; ‘the gentleman is awake.", "\"아니, 저 신사분의 정직한 가슴과 영혼에 축복을!\" 제피로스가 돌아서서 극도로 놀란 척하며 말했다."], ["Hem, Shakespeare!", "\"신사분이 깨셨네."], ["How do you do, Sir?", "어흠, 셰익스피어 양반!"], ["How is Mary and Sarah, sir?", "안녕하십니까, 나리? 메리와 새러는 안녕하신지요, 나리?"], ["and the dear old lady at home, Sir?", "그리고 댁의 사랑스러운 노부인께서는요, 나리?"], ["Will you have the kindness to put my compliments into the first little parcel you’re sending that way, sir, and say that I would have sent ‘em before, only I was afraid they might be broken in the wagon, sir?’", "그쪽으로 보내실 첫 번째 작은 꾸러미에 제 안부를 넣어 주시는 친절을 베풀어 주시겠습니까, 나리? 진작 보냈을 텐데 마차에서 깨질까 봐 겁이 났다고 전해 주시고요, 나리?\""]], [["‘Don’t overwhelm the gentlemen with ordinary civilities when you see he’s anxious to have something to drink,’ said the gentleman with the whiskers, with a jocose air.", "\"신사분이 마실 것을 간절히 바라는 게 보이는데도 흔해 빠진 인사로 신사분을 질리게 하지 말게나,\" 구레나룻 난 신사가 익살스러운 태도로 말했다."], ["‘Why don’t you ask the gentleman what he’ll take?’", "\"신사분께 뭘 드시겠느냐고 여쭤보지 그러나?\""]], [["‘Dear me, I quite forgot,’ replied the other.", "\"이런, 까맣게 잊었네그려,\" 다른 사내가 대답했다."], ["‘What will you take, sir?", "\"뭘 드시겠습니까, 나리?"], ["Will you take port wine, sir, or sherry wine, sir?", "포트와인을 드시겠습니까, 나리, 아니면 셰리주를 드시겠습니까, 나리?"], ["I can recommend the ale, sir; or perhaps you’d like to taste the porter, sir?", "에일을 권해 드릴 수 있습니다, 나리. 아니면 흑맥주를 맛보고 싶으신지요, 나리?"], ["Allow me to have the felicity of hanging up your nightcap, Sir.’", "나리의 잠자리 모자를 걸어 드리는 영광을 누리게 해 주십시오, 나리.\""]], [["With this, the speaker snatched that article of dress from Mr. Pickwick’s head, and fixed it in a twinkling on that of the drunken man, who, firmly impressed with the belief that he was delighting a numerous assembly, continued to hammer away at the comic song in the most melancholy strains imaginable.", "이 말과 함께, 말하던 자는 그 의복을 픽윅 씨의 머리에서 낚아채, 눈 깜짝할 사이에 술 취한 사내의 머리에 씌웠는데, 그 사내는 자기가 수많은 청중을 즐겁게 하고 있다고 굳게 믿으며, 상상할 수 있는 가장 구슬픈 가락으로 희극 노래를 계속 두들겨 대고 있었다."]], [["Taking a man’s nightcap from his brow by violent means, and adjusting it on the head of an unknown gentleman, of dirty exterior, however ingenious a witticism in itself, is unquestionably one of those which come under the denomination of practical jokes.", "사람의 잠자리 모자를 그 이마에서 난폭한 수단으로 벗겨내, 더러운 행색의 낯선 신사 머리에 씌우는 것은, 그 자체로 아무리 기발한 익살이라 한들, 의문의 여지 없이 짓궂은 장난이라는 명칭에 드는 것 가운데 하나다."], ["Viewing the matter precisely in this light, Mr. Pickwick, without the slightest intimation of his purpose, sprang vigorously out of bed, struck the Zephyr so smart a blow in the chest as to deprive him of a considerable portion of the commodity which sometimes bears his name, and then, recapturing his nightcap, boldly placed himself in an attitude of defence.", "이 일을 바로 그런 관점에서 본 픽윅 씨는, 자신의 의도를 조금도 내비치지 않은 채, 침대에서 힘차게 뛰쳐나와, 제피로스의 가슴에 어찌나 매서운 일격을 가했던지 그가 때때로 자기 이름으로 불리는 그 물건의 상당 부분을 빼앗겼고, 그러고는 자신의 잠자리 모자를 되찾아, 대담하게 방어 자세를 취했다."]], [["‘Now,’ said Mr.", "\"자,\" 픽윅 씨가 흥분 못지않게 그토록 많은 기운을 쏟은 탓에 숨을 헐떡이며 말했다."], ["Pickwick, gasping no less from excitement than from the expenditure of so much energy, ‘come on--both of you--both of you!’ With this liberal invitation the worthy gentleman communicated a revolving motion to his clenched fists, by way of appalling his antagonists with a display of science.", "\"덤벼라—둘 다—둘 다 말이다!\" 이 호기로운 초대와 함께 이 훌륭한 신사는 꽉 쥔 주먹에 빙글빙글 도는 동작을 부여했으니, 적들을 그 기술의 과시로 질겁하게 하려는 것이었다."]], [["It might have been Mr. Pickwick’s very unexpected gallantry, or it might have been the complicated manner in which he had got himself out of bed, and fallen all in a mass upon the hornpipe man, that touched his adversaries.", "픽윅 씨의 더없이 뜻밖의 용감함 때문이었는지, 아니면 그가 침대에서 빠져나와 호른파이프 춘 사내 위로 한 덩어리가 되어 쓰러진 그 복잡한 방식 때문이었는지, 어쨌든 그의 적들은 마음이 움직였다."], ["Touched they were; for, instead of then and there making an attempt to commit man-slaughter, as Mr. Pickwick implicitly believed they would have done, they paused, stared at each other a short time, and finally laughed outright.", "마음이 움직인 것은 사실이었으니, 픽윅 씨가 틀림없이 그러리라 굳게 믿었던 것처럼 그 자리에서 살인을 저지르려 시도하는 대신, 그들은 멈춰 서서, 잠시 서로를 빤히 쳐다보다가, 마침내 큰 소리로 웃어 버렸기 때문이다."]], [["‘Well, you’re a trump, and I like you all the better for it,’ said the Zephyr.", "\"이거 참, 당신은 호걸이오. 그래서 더 마음에 드는구려,\""], ["‘Now jump into bed again, or you’ll catch the rheumatics.", "제피로스가 말했다. \"자, 다시 침대로 뛰어드시오, 안 그러면 류머티즘에 걸릴 거요."], ["No malice, I hope?’ said the man, extending a hand the size of the yellow clump of fingers which sometimes swings over a glover’s door.", "악의는 없으시겠지요?\" 사내가 손을 내밀며 말했는데, 그 손은 때때로 장갑 가게 문 위에 매달려 흔들리는 누런 손가락 뭉치만 한 크기였다."]], [["‘Certainly not,’ said Mr.", "\"물론 없소,\""], ["Pickwick, with great alacrity; for, now that the excitement was over, he began to feel rather cool about the legs.", "픽윅 씨가 아주 선뜻 말했다. 이제 흥분이 가라앉고 나니 다리가 다소 서늘해지는 것을 느끼기 시작했기 때문이다."]], [["‘Allow me the H-onour,’ said the gentleman with the whiskers, presenting his dexter hand, and aspirating the h.", "\"제게 여 — 영광을 주십시오,\" 구레나룻 난 신사가 오른손을 내밀며, h 음을 거세게 발음하여 말했다."]], [["‘With much pleasure, sir,’ said Mr.", "\"매우 기쁘오, 선생,\""], ["Pickwick; and having executed a very long and solemn shake, he got into bed again.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. 그리고 아주 길고 엄숙하게 악수를 나눈 뒤, 다시 침대에 들었다."]], [["‘My name is Smangle, sir,’ said the man with the whiskers.", "\"제 이름은 스맹글입니다, 선생,\" 구레나룻 난 사내가 말했다."]], [["‘Oh,’ said Mr.", "\"오,\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Mine is Mivins,’ said the man in the stockings.", "\"제 것은 미빈스올시다,\" 양말 신은 사내가 말했다."]], [["‘I am delighted to hear it, sir,’ said Mr.", "\"반갑게 듣소이다, 선생,\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Hem,’ coughed Mr.", "\"어흠,\""], ["Smangle.", "스맹글 씨가 헛기침을 했다."]], [["‘Did you speak, sir?’ said Mr.", "\"무슨 말씀 하셨소, 선생?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘No, I did not, sir,’ said Mr.", "\"아니요, 안 했습니다, 선생,\""], ["Smangle.", "스맹글 씨가 말했다."]], [["All this was very genteel and pleasant; and, to make matters still more comfortable, Mr.", "이 모든 것이 매우 점잖고 유쾌했다."], ["Smangle assured Mr. Pickwick a great many more times that he entertained a very high respect for the feelings of a gentleman; which sentiment, indeed, did him infinite credit, as he could be in no wise supposed to understand them.", "그리고 일을 한층 더 화기애애하게 만들려고, 스맹글 씨는 자기가 신사의 감정에 대해 매우 높은 경의를 품고 있노라고 픽윅 씨에게 여러 번 거듭 장담했는데, 이 정서는 실로 그에게 무한한 명예가 되는 것이었으니, 그가 그 감정을 도무지 이해할 리 없다고 여겨졌기 때문이다."]], [["‘Are you going through the court, sir?’ inquired Mr.", "\"법정을 거치실 겁니까, 선생?\""], ["Smangle.", "스맹글 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘Through the what?’ said Mr.", "\"무엇을 거친다고요?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Through the court--Portugal Street--the Court for Relief of--you know.’", "\"법정 말입니다—포르투갈 거리—구제를 위한 법정—아시잖습니까.\""]], [["‘Oh, no,’ replied Mr.", "\"오, 아니오,\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다."], ["Pickwick. ‘No, I am not.’", "\"아니, 그렇지 않소.\""]], [["‘Going out, perhaps?’ suggested Mr.", "\"그럼, 어쩌면 나가실 참인가요?\""], ["Mivins.", "미빈스 씨가 넌지시 물었다."]], [["‘I fear not,’ replied Mr.", "\"그렇지도 않을 것 같소,\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 대답했다."], ["‘I refuse to pay some damages, and am here in consequence.’", "\"내가 어떤 손해 배상금을 내기를 거부하여, 그 결과 여기 있는 것이오.\""]], [["‘Ah,’ said Mr.", "\"아,\""], ["Smangle, ‘paper has been my ruin.’", "스맹글 씨가 말했다. \"종이가 제 파멸이었지요.\""]], [["‘A stationer, I presume, Sir?’ said Mr.", "\"문구상이셨던 모양이군요, 선생?\""], ["Pickwick innocently.", "픽윅 씨가 순진하게 말했다."]], [["‘Stationer!", "\"문구상!"], ["No, no; confound and curse me!", "아니, 아닙니다. 빌어먹을, 저주받을!"], ["Not so low as that.", "그렇게까지 천하진 않습니다."], ["No trade.", "장사는 안 했어요."], ["When I say paper, I mean bills.’", "제가 종이라고 할 때는, 어음을 말하는 겁니다.\""]], [["‘Oh, you use the word in that sense.", "\"오, 그런 뜻으로 그 말을 쓰는군요. 알겠소,\""], ["I see,’ said Mr. Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Damme!", "\"제기랄!"], ["A gentleman must expect reverses,’ said Smangle.", "신사라면 역경을 각오해야지요,\" 스맹글이 말했다."], ["‘What of that?", "\"그게 어쨌단 말입니까?"], ["Here am I in the Fleet Prison.", "여기 제가 플리트 감옥에 있습니다."], ["Well; good.", "좋아요, 좋아."], ["What then?", "그래서 어떻단 말입니까?"], ["I’m none the worse for that, am I?’", "그렇다고 제가 조금이라도 못해진 건 아니지 않습니까, 안 그래요?\""]], [["‘Not a bit,’ replied Mr.", "\"조금도 그렇지 않소,\""], ["Mivins. And he was quite right; for, so far from Mr.", "미빈스 씨가 대답했다."], ["Smangle being any the worse for it, he was something the better, inasmuch as to qualify himself for the place, he had attained gratuitous possession of certain articles of jewellery, which, long before that, had found their way to the pawnbroker’s.", "그의 말은 아주 옳았으니, 스맹글 씨가 그 때문에 조금이라도 못해지기는커녕, 오히려 다소 나아진 셈이었기 때문이다. 이 자리에 들어올 자격을 갖추려고, 그는 어떤 장신구 몇 점을 거저 손에 넣었는데, 그것들은 그보다 훨씬 전에 이미 전당포로 흘러 들어가 있었던 것이다."]], [["‘Well; but come,’ said Mr.", "\"자, 그건 그렇고,\" 스맹글 씨가 말했다."], ["Smangle; ‘this is dry work.", "\"이거 목마른 일이올시다."], ["Let’s rinse our mouths with a drop of burnt sherry; the last-comer shall stand it, Mivins shall fetch it, and I’ll help to drink it.", "구운 셰리주 한 모금으로 입가심이나 합시다. 마지막에 온 분이 한턱내고, 미빈스가 가져오고, 나는 마시는 걸 거들지요."], ["That’s a fair and gentlemanlike division of labour, anyhow.", "어쨌거나 그게 공정하고 신사다운 분업 아니겠습니까."], ["Curse me!’", "제기랄!\""]], [["Unwilling to hazard another quarrel, Mr. Pickwick gladly assented to the proposition, and consigned the money to Mr. Mivins, who, as it was nearly eleven o’clock, lost no time in repairing to the coffee-room on his errand.", "또 한 차례의 다툼을 무릅쓰고 싶지 않았던 픽윅 씨는 그 제안에 기꺼이 동의하고, 미빈스 씨에게 돈을 맡겼는데, 미빈스는 거의 열한 시가 다 된지라 지체 없이 그 심부름을 하러 커피실로 향했다."]], [["‘I say,’ whispered Smangle, the moment his friend had left the room; ‘what did you give him?’", "\"이보시오,\" 친구가 방을 나서자마자 스맹글이 속삭였다. \"저자에게 얼마를 주셨습니까?\""]], [["‘Half a sovereign,’ said Mr.", "\"반 소버린이오,\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘He’s a devilish pleasant gentlemanly dog,’ said Mr.", "\"저자는 지독히 유쾌하고 신사다운 친구지요,\""], ["Smangle;--‘infernal pleasant.", "스맹글 씨가 말했다. \"지독하게 유쾌해요."], ["I don’t know anybody more so; but--’ Here Mr.", "저보다 더한 사람은 모릅니다. 하지만—\""], ["Smangle stopped short, and shook his head dubiously.", "여기서 스맹글 씨는 말을 뚝 끊고, 미심쩍은 듯 고개를 저었다."]], [["‘You don’t think there is any probability of his appropriating the money to his own use?’ said Mr.", "\"그 돈을 자기 용도로 가로챌 가능성이 있다고 생각하는 건 아니겠지요?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Oh, no!", "\"오, 아니요!"], ["Mind, I don’t say that; I expressly say that he’s a devilish gentlemanly fellow,’ said Mr.", "명심하십시오, 그런 말은 안 합니다. 저자가 지독히 신사다운 친구라고 분명히 말씀드리는 겁니다,\""], ["Smangle.", "스맹글 씨가 말했다."], ["‘But I think, perhaps, if somebody went down, just to see that he didn’t dip his beak into the jug by accident, or make some confounded mistake in losing the money as he came upstairs, it would be as well.", "\"하지만 제 생각엔, 어쩌면, 누가 내려가서, 저자가 어쩌다 단지에 부리를 담그지나 않는지, 또는 위층으로 올라오다 빌어먹을 실수로 돈을 잃어버리지나 않는지 살펴보는 편이 좋을 듯합니다."], ["Here, you sir, just run downstairs, and look after that gentleman, will you?’", "이보게, 거기 자네, 잠깐 아래층으로 달려 내려가서 저 신사분을 좀 살피고 와 주겠나?\""]], [["This request was addressed to a little timid-looking, nervous man, whose appearance bespoke great poverty, and who had been crouching on his bedstead all this while, apparently stupefied by the novelty of his situation.", "이 부탁은 행색이 몹시 가난해 보이는, 작고 소심하고 신경질적인 사내에게 한 것이었는데, 그는 그동안 내내 자기 침대틀에 웅크리고 앉아, 분명 자신의 처지가 새로운 데 어리둥절해 있는 듯했다."]], [["‘You know where the coffee-room is,’ said Smangle; ‘just run down, and tell that gentleman you’ve come to help him up with the jug. Or--stop-- I’ll tell you what--I’ll tell you how we’ll do him,’ said Smangle, with a cunning look.", "\"커피실이 어딘지 알지,\" 스맹글이 말했다. \"냉큼 내려가서, 저 신사분께 단지 드는 걸 거들러 왔다고 말하게. 아니—잠깐—내가 일러 주지—우리가 저자를 어떻게 골탕 먹일지 일러 주겠습니다,\" 스맹글이 교활한 표정으로 말했다."]], [["‘How?’ said Mr.", "\"어떻게요?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Send down word that he’s to spend the change in cigars.", "\"거스름돈은 시가에 쓰라고 전갈을 보내는 거지요."], ["Capital thought.", "기막힌 생각입니다."], ["Run and tell him that; d’ye hear?", "가서 저자에게 그렇게 전하게, 알겠나?"], ["They shan’t be wasted,’ continued Smangle, turning to Mr.", "시가를 헛되이 쓰진 않을 겁니다,\" 스맹글이 픽윅 씨를 돌아보며 말을 이었다."], ["Pickwick. ‘I’ll smoke ‘em.’", "\"내가 그걸 피울 테니까요.\""]], [["This manoeuvring was so exceedingly ingenious and, withal, performed with such immovable composure and coolness, that Mr. Pickwick would have had no wish to disturb it, even if he had had the power.", "이 술책이 어찌나 지극히 기발하고, 게다가 어찌나 미동도 없는 침착함과 냉정함으로 행해졌던지, 픽윅 씨는 설령 그럴 힘이 있었다 해도 그것을 방해하고 싶은 마음이 들지 않았을 것이다."], ["In a short time Mr. Mivins returned, bearing the sherry, which Mr.", "얼마 안 가 미빈스 씨가 셰리주를 들고 돌아왔고, 스맹글 씨가 그것을 금이 간 작은 머그잔 두 개에 따랐다."], ["Smangle dispensed in two little cracked mugs; considerately remarking, with reference to himself, that a gentleman must not be particular under such circumstances, and that, for his part, he was not too proud to drink out of the jug.", "그는 자기 자신에 관해서는, 이런 상황에서 신사라면 까다롭게 굴어서는 안 되며, 자기로 말하자면 단지째 마시는 것을 그리 자존심 상해하지 않는다고 사려 깊게 말했다."], ["In which, to show his sincerity, he forthwith pledged the company in a draught which half emptied it.", "그러고는 자신의 진심을 보이려고, 곧바로 단지를 반쯤 비우는 들이켬으로 일동을 위해 축배를 들었다."]], [["An excellent understanding having been by these means promoted, Mr. Smangle proceeded to entertain his hearers with a relation of divers romantic adventures in which he had been from time to time engaged, involving various interesting anecdotes of a thoroughbred horse, and a magnificent Jewess, both of surpassing beauty, and much coveted by the nobility and gentry of these kingdoms.", "이런 수단으로 더없이 훌륭한 화목이 조성되자, 스맹글 씨는 자기가 때때로 겪었던 갖가지 낭만적인 모험에 관한 이야기로 청중을 즐겁게 하기 시작했는데, 거기에는 순종 말 한 마리와 화려한 유대인 여인에 관한 여러 흥미로운 일화가 얽혀 있었으니, 둘 다 빼어나게 아름다워 이 왕국의 귀족과 신사들이 몹시 탐냈다는 것이었다."]], [["Long before these elegant extracts from the biography of a gentleman were concluded, Mr. Mivins had betaken himself to bed, and had set in snoring for the night, leaving the timid stranger and Mr. Pickwick to the full benefit of Mr. Smangle’s experiences.", "한 신사의 전기에서 발췌한 이 우아한 이야기들이 채 끝나기 한참 전에, 미빈스 씨는 이미 잠자리에 들어 밤새 코를 골기 시작했고, 소심한 낯선 이와 픽윅 씨만이 스맹글 씨의 경험담의 온전한 혜택을 누리도록 남겨졌다."]], [["Nor were the two last-named gentlemen as much edified as they might have been by the moving passages narrated. Mr. Pickwick had been in a state of slumber for some time, when he had a faint perception of the drunken man bursting out afresh with the comic song, and receiving from Mr. Smangle a gentle intimation, through the medium of the water-jug, that his audience was not musically disposed. Mr. Pickwick then once again dropped off to sleep, with a confused consciousness that Mr. Smangle was still engaged in relating a long story, the chief point of which appeared to be that, on some occasion particularly stated and set forth, he had ‘done’ a bill and a gentleman at the same time.", "게다가 마지막에 언급된 이 두 신사도, 그 감동적인 이야기 대목들로 마땅히 받을 만큼 교화되지는 못했다. 픽윅 씨는 한동안 졸음 속에 빠져 있었는데, 그때 술 취한 사내가 다시금 희극 노래를 터뜨리는 것과, 청중이 음악적 기분이 아니라는 점을 스맹글 씨가 물 단지를 매개로 부드럽게 일러 주는 것을 어렴풋이 알아챘다. 그러고 나서 픽윅 씨는 다시 한 번 잠에 빠져들었는데, 스맹글 씨가 여전히 긴 이야기를 늘어놓고 있다는 어렴풋한 의식이 남아 있었으니, 그 이야기의 요점은, 특별히 명시되고 자세히 진술된 어떤 경우에, 그가 어음 한 장과 신사 한 사람을 동시에 '해치웠다'는 것인 듯했다."]], [["CHAPTER XLII. ILLUSTRATIVE, LIKE THE PRECEDING ONE, OF THE OLD PROVERB, THAT ADVERSITY BRINGS A MAN ACQUAINTED WITH STRANGE BEDFELLOWS-- LIKEWISE CONTAINING MR. PICKWICK’S EXTRAORDINARY AND STARTLING ANNOUNCEMENT TO MR. SAMUEL WELLER\nWhen Mr. Pickwick opened his eyes next morning, the first object upon which they rested was Samuel Weller, seated upon a small black portmanteau, intently regarding, apparently in a condition of profound abstraction, the stately figure of the dashing Mr. Smangle; while Mr. Smangle himself, who was already partially dressed, was seated on his bedstead, occupied in the desperately hopeless attempt of staring Mr. Weller out of countenance. We say desperately hopeless, because Sam, with a comprehensive gaze which took in Mr. Smangle’s cap, feet, head, face, legs, and whiskers, all at the same time, continued to look steadily on, with every demonstration of lively satisfaction, but with no more regard to Mr. Smangle’s personal sentiments on the subject than he would have displayed had he been inspecting a wooden statue, or a straw-embowelled Guy Fawkes.", "픽윅 씨가 이튿날 아침 눈을 떴을 때, 그의 눈에 가장 먼저 들어온 것은 작은 검정 여행 가방 위에 걸터앉아, 겉보기에 깊은 상념에 잠긴 채로, 화려한 스맹글 씨의 당당한 모습을 골똘히 바라보고 있는 새뮤얼 웰러였다. 한편 스맹글 씨 본인은 이미 어느 정도 옷을 갖춰 입은 채 자기 침대에 앉아, 웰러 씨를 노려보아 무안하게 만들려는, 절망적일 만큼 가망 없는 시도에 골몰하고 있었다. 절망적일 만큼 가망 없다고 말하는 까닭은, 샘이 스맹글 씨의 모자며 발이며 머리며 얼굴이며 다리며 구레나룻을 한꺼번에 다 담아내는 포괄적인 눈길로, 흡족한 기색을 한껏 드러내며 꾸준히 바라보면서도, 그 일에 관한 스맹글 씨 개인의 감정에 대해서는, 나무로 깎은 조각상이나 짚을 속에 채운 가이 포크스 인형을 살펴볼 때만큼의 관심밖에 보이지 않았기 때문이다."]], [["‘Well; will you know me again?’ said Mr.", "\"그래, 다음에 또 날 알아보겠소?\""], ["Smangle, with a frown.", "스맹글 씨가 인상을 찌푸리며 말했다."]], [["‘I’d svear to you anyveres, Sir,’ replied Sam cheerfully.", "\"어디서든 당신이라고 맹세할 수 있겠습니다, 선생님.\" 샘이 명랑하게 대답했다."]], [["‘Don’t be impertinent to a gentleman, Sir,’ said Mr.", "\"신사한테 무례하게 굴지 마시오, 이 사람아.\""], ["Smangle.", "스맹글 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Not on no account,’ replied Sam.", "\"천만의 말씀입니다.\" 샘이 대답했다."], ["‘If you’ll tell me wen he wakes, I’ll be upon the wery best extra-super behaviour!’ This observation, having a remote tendency to imply that Mr.", "\"그분이 언제 깨시는지 알려 주시면, 제가 더할 나위 없이 극상으로 예의 바르게 굴겠습니다요!\""], ["Smangle was no gentleman, kindled his ire.", "이 말은 스맹글 씨가 신사가 아니라는 뜻을 은근히 내비치는 구석이 있어서, 그의 화를 돋우었다."]], [["‘Mivins!’ said Mr.", "\"마이빈스!\""], ["Smangle, with a passionate air.", "스맹글 씨가 발끈한 기색으로 말했다."]], [["‘What’s the office?’ replied that gentleman from his couch.", "\"무슨 일이오?\" 그 신사가 자기 잠자리에서 대답했다."]], [["‘Who the devil is this fellow?’", "\"대체 이 작자는 누구요?\""]], [["‘’Gad,’ said Mr.", "\"맙소사,\""], ["Mivins, looking lazily out from under the bed-clothes, ‘I ought to ask you that.", "마이빈스 씨가 이불 밑에서 게으르게 내다보며 말했다. \"그건 내가 당신한테 물어야 할 말이지."], ["Hasn’t he any business here?’", "여기 무슨 볼일이 있다는 거요?\""]], [["‘No,’ replied Mr.", "\"없소.\""], ["Smangle.", "스맹글 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘Then knock him downstairs, and tell him not to presume to get up till I come and kick him,’ rejoined Mr.", "\"그럼 계단 아래로 차 버리고, 내가 가서 발길질해 줄 때까지 함부로 일어나지 말라고 일러 두시오.\""], ["Mivins; with this prompt advice that excellent gentleman again betook himself to slumber.", "마이빈스 씨가 응수했다. 이 신속한 충고를 던진 다음, 그 훌륭한 신사는 다시 잠 속으로 빠져들었다."]], [["The conversation exhibiting these unequivocal symptoms of verging on the personal, Mr. Pickwick deemed it a fit point at which to interpose.", "이 대화가 인신공격으로 치닫는 명백한 조짐을 보이자, 픽윅 씨는 끼어들기에 알맞은 대목이라고 판단했다."]], [["‘Sam,’ said Mr.", "\"샘.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Sir,’ rejoined that gentleman.", "\"예, 선생님.\" 그가 응답했다."]], [["‘Has anything new occurred since last night?’", "\"간밤 이후로 무슨 새로운 일이라도 있었나?\""]], [["‘Nothin’ partickler, sir,’ replied Sam, glancing at Mr.", "\"별일 없습니다요, 선생님.\""], ["Smangle’s whiskers; ‘the late prewailance of a close and confined atmosphere has been rayther favourable to the growth of veeds, of an alarmin’ and sangvinary natur; but vith that ‘ere exception things is quiet enough.’", "샘이 스맹글 씨의 구레나룻을 흘끗 보며 대답했다. \"근래에 답답하고 갑갑한 공기가 만연한 탓에, 놀랍고도 살벌한 성질의 잡초들이 자라기에는 다소 알맞은 형편이었습니다만, 그 예외만 빼면 모든 게 꽤 잠잠합니다요.\""]], [["‘I shall get up,’ said Mr.", "\"일어나야겠다.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick; ‘give me some clean things.’", "\"깨끗한 옷가지를 좀 가져다주게.\""]], [["Whatever hostile intentions Mr. Smangle might have entertained, his thoughts were speedily diverted by the unpacking of the portmanteau; the contents of which appeared to impress him at once with a most favourable opinion, not only of Mr.", "스맹글 씨가 어떤 적대적 의도를 품고 있었든 간에, 여행 가방을 푸는 광경에 그의 생각은 금세 다른 데로 쏠렸다."], ["Pickwick, but of Sam also, who, he took an early opportunity of declaring in a tone of voice loud enough for that eccentric personage to overhear, was a regular thoroughbred original, and consequently the very man after his own heart.", "가방 속의 물건들은 단번에 그에게 픽윅 씨뿐 아니라 샘에 대해서도 더없이 호의적인 인상을 심어 준 모양이었다. 그는 그 별난 인물이 엿들을 수 있을 만큼 충분히 큰 목소리로, 샘이야말로 순종 중의 순종이요, 따라서 자기 마음에 꼭 드는 사람이라고 일찌감치 선언할 기회를 잡았다."], ["As to Mr. Pickwick, the affection he conceived for him knew no limits.", "픽윅 씨로 말하자면, 그가 픽윅 씨에게 품은 애정에는 한계가 없었다."]], [["‘Now is there anything I can do for you, my dear Sir?’ said Smangle.", "\"자, 제가 뭐 도와드릴 일이라도 있을까요, 선생님?\" 스맹글이 말했다."]], [["‘Nothing that I am aware of, I am obliged to you,’ replied Mr.", "\"내가 아는 한은 없소만, 고맙소.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘No linen that you want sent to the washerwoman’s?", "\"세탁부에게 보낼 빨랫감은 없으십니까?"], ["I know a delightful washerwoman outside, that comes for my things twice a week; and, by Jove!--how devilish lucky!--this is the day she calls.", "바깥에 아주 마음에 드는 세탁부를 한 사람 아는데, 일주일에 두 번씩 제 빨랫감을 가지러 옵니다. 그런데, 이런!—이렇게 운이 좋을 데가!—바로 오늘이 그 여자가 오는 날입니다."], ["Shall I put any of those little things up with mine?", "그 자잘한 것들 중에 몇 개를 제 것과 함께 부쳐 드릴까요?"], ["Don’t say anything about the trouble.", "수고로움 따위는 말씀도 마십시오."], ["Confound and curse it!", "빌어먹을!"], ["if one gentleman under a cloud is not to put himself a little out of the way to assist another gentleman in the same condition, what’s human nature?’", "곤경에 처한 신사 하나가 같은 처지의 또 다른 신사를 돕기 위해 약간의 수고도 마다한다면, 그게 무슨 인간의 본성이겠습니까?\""]], [["Thus spake Mr. Smangle, edging himself meanwhile as near as possible to the portmanteau, and beaming forth looks of the most fervent and disinterested friendship.", "스맹글 씨는 이렇게 말하면서, 그동안 슬금슬금 여행 가방에 되도록 가까이 다가가, 더없이 열렬하고 사심 없는 우정의 눈빛을 환히 발산하고 있었다."]], [["‘There’s nothing you want to give out for the man to brush, my dear creature, is there?’ resumed Smangle.", "\"이보게, 사람을 시켜 솔질해 둘 만한 것은 없는가, 응?\" 스맹글이 말을 이었다."]], [["‘Nothin’ whatever, my fine feller,’ rejoined Sam, taking the reply into his own mouth.", "\"전혀 없습니다요, 멋쟁이 양반.\" 샘이 그 대답을 자기 입으로 가로채며 응수했다."], ["‘P’raps if vun of us wos to brush, without troubling the man, it ‘ud be more agreeable for all parties, as the schoolmaster said when the young gentleman objected to being flogged by the butler.’", "\"사람을 번거롭게 할 것 없이 우리 중 누가 솔질을 한다면, 모두에게 더 좋을 겁니다. 집사한테 매를 맞기 싫다고 도련님이 투정하니까 선생님이 한 말처럼 말입니다요.\""]], [["‘And there’s nothing I can send in my little box to the washer-woman’s, is there?’ said Smangle, turning from Sam to Mr.", "\"그럼 제 작은 상자에 넣어 세탁부에게 보낼 만한 건 없습니까?\""], ["Pickwick, with an air of some discomfiture.", "스맹글이 다소 무안해진 기색으로 샘에게서 픽윅 씨에게로 돌아서며 말했다."]], [["‘Nothin’ whatever, Sir,’ retorted Sam; ‘I’m afeered the little box must be chock full o’ your own as it is.’", "\"전혀 없습니다요, 선생님.\" 샘이 받아쳤다. \"그 작은 상자는 안 그래도 선생님 것만으로 꽉 차 있을 것 같아 걱정인뎁쇼.\""]], [["This speech was accompanied with such a very expressive look at that particular portion of Mr. Smangle’s attire, by the appearance of which the skill of laundresses in getting up gentlemen’s linen is generally tested, that he was fain to turn upon his heel, and, for the present at any rate, to give up all design on Mr.", "이 말과 더불어 샘이 스맹글 씨의 옷차림 가운데서도 바로 그 부분—세탁부가 신사의 빨랫감을 손질하는 솜씨가 대개 그것으로 가늠되는 부분—을 향해 어찌나 의미심장한 눈길을 보냈는지, 스맹글은 발길을 돌릴 수밖에 없었고, 적어도 당분간은 픽윅 씨의 지갑과 옷장에 대한 모든 계획을 단념할 수밖에 없었다."], ["Pickwick’s purse and wardrobe. He accordingly retired in dudgeon to the racket-ground, where he made a light and whole-some breakfast on a couple of the cigars which had been purchased on the previous night.", "그리하여 그는 골이 나서 운동장으로 물러갔는데, 거기서 전날 밤에 사 둔 시가 두 개로 가볍고 속 편한 아침 식사를 때웠다."]], [["Mr.", "담배를 피우지 않는 데다, 잡화점에 진 자잘한 외상 또한 석판 맨 아래까지 적혀 내려가 결국 반대편으로 '넘어가'"], ["Mivins, who was no smoker, and whose account for small articles of chandlery had also reached down to the bottom of the slate, and been ‘carried over’ to the other side, remained in bed, and, in his own words, ‘took it out in sleep.’", "버린 마이빈스 씨는 침대에 그대로 누운 채, 그 자신의 표현을 빌리자면 '잠으로 때웠다'."]], [["After breakfasting in a small closet attached to the coffee-room, which bore the imposing title of the Snuggery, the temporary inmate of which, in consideration of a small additional charge, had the unspeakable advantage of overhearing all the conversation in the coffee-room aforesaid; and, after despatching Mr. Weller on some necessary errands, Mr. Pickwick repaired to the lodge, to consult Mr. Roker concerning his future accommodation.", "커피실에 딸린 작은 골방—'스너거리'라는 거창한 이름이 붙어 있고, 거기 잠시 머무는 이는 약간의 추가 요금을 내는 대가로 앞서 말한 커피실의 모든 대화를 엿들을 수 있다는 말할 수 없는 이점을 누리는 곳—에서 아침을 든 뒤, 그리고 웰러 씨를 몇 가지 필요한 심부름에 내보낸 뒤, 픽윅 씨는 앞으로 묵을 거처에 관해 의논하려고 관리실로 향했다."]], [["‘Accommodation, eh?’ said that gentleman, consulting a large book.", "\"거처라, 응?\" 그 신사가 큼직한 장부를 들여다보며 말했다."], ["‘Plenty of that, Mr. Pickwick.", "\"그거야 얼마든지 있지요, 픽윅 씨."], ["Your chummage ticket will be on twenty- seven, in the third.’", "당신 동숙 배정표는 삼층 이십칠 호로 나올 겁니다.\""]], [["‘Oh,’ said Mr.", "\"오,\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["‘My what, did you say?’", "\"내 뭐라고 하셨소?\""]], [["‘Your chummage ticket,’ replied Mr.", "\"당신 동숙 배정표 말입니다.\" 로커 씨가 대답했다."], ["Roker; ‘you’re up to that?’", "\"그게 무슨 뜻인지 아시지요?\""]], [["‘Not quite,’ replied Mr.", "\"잘은 모르겠소만.\""], ["Pickwick, with a smile.", "픽윅 씨가 미소 지으며 대답했다."]], [["‘Why,’ said Mr.", "\"이런,\" 로커 씨가 말했다."], ["Roker, ‘it’s as plain as Salisbury.", "\"솔즈베리 평원처럼 빤한 얘기 아닙니까."], ["You’ll have a chummage ticket upon twenty-seven in the third, and them as is in the room will be your chums.’", "당신은 삼층 이십칠 호로 동숙 배정표를 받게 되고, 그 방에 있는 자들이 당신의 동숙인이 되는 거지요.\""]], [["‘Are there many of them?’ inquired Mr.", "\"그 사람들이 많소?\""], ["Pickwick dubiously.", "픽윅 씨가 미심쩍어하며 물었다."]], [["‘Three,’ replied Mr.", "\"셋입니다.\""], ["Roker.", "로커 씨가 대답했다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick coughed.", "픽윅 씨는 기침을 했다."]], [["‘One of ‘em’s a parson,’ said Mr.", "\"그중 하나는 목사고,\""], ["Roker, filling up a little piece of paper as he spoke; ‘another’s a butcher.’", "로커 씨가 말하면서 작은 종이쪽지를 채워 넣었다. \"또 하나는 푸주한이지요.\""]], [["‘Eh?’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"네?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 외쳤다."]], [["‘A butcher,’ repeated Mr.", "\"푸주한 말입니다.\""], ["Roker, giving the nib of his pen a tap on the desk to cure it of a disinclination to mark.", "로커 씨가 되풀이하며, 글씨가 잘 안 나오는 것을 고쳐 보려고 펜촉을 책상에 톡톡 두드렸다."], ["‘What a thorough-paced goer he used to be sure-ly!", "\"그 친구, 정말이지 못 말릴 한량이었지요!"], ["You remember Tom Martin, Neddy?’ said Roker, appealing to another man in the lodge, who was paring the mud off his shoes with a five-and-twenty-bladed pocket-knife.", "자네 톰 마틴 기억하나, 네디?\" 로커가 관리실의 다른 사내에게 물었는데, 그 사내는 스물다섯 개의 날이 달린 주머니칼로 신발에서 진흙을 깎아 내고 있었다."]], [["‘I should think so,’ replied the party addressed, with a strong emphasis on the personal pronoun.", "\"기억하다마다.\" 그 말을 들은 자가 인칭대명사에 잔뜩 힘을 주어 대답했다."]], [["‘Bless my dear eyes!’ said Mr.", "\"이런, 세상에!\""], ["Roker, shaking his head slowly from side to side, and gazing abstractedly out of the grated windows before him, as if he were fondly recalling some peaceful scene of his early youth; ‘it seems but yesterday that he whopped the coal-heaver down Fox-under- the-Hill by the wharf there.", "로커 씨가 고개를 천천히 좌우로 저으며, 마치 젊은 시절의 어느 평화로운 정경을 애틋하게 떠올리기라도 하듯 앞에 놓인 쇠창살 창밖을 멍하니 바라보며 말했다. \"그 친구가 저기 부두 옆 폭스언더더힐에서 석탄 부리는 인부를 두들겨 패던 게 꼭 어제 일 같은데."], ["I think I can see him now, a-coming up the Strand between the two street-keepers, a little sobered by the bruising, with a patch o’ winegar and brown paper over his right eyelid, and that ‘ere lovely bulldog, as pinned the little boy arterwards, a-following at his heels.", "지금도 눈에 선하구먼. 두들겨 맞은 덕에 술이 좀 깬 채로, 거리 단속꾼 둘 사이에 끼여, 오른쪽 눈꺼풀에 식초 적신 갈색 종이를 붙이고, 나중에 그 어린애를 물어뜯은 그 사랑스러운 불도그를 발치에 달고서, 스트랜드 거리를 올라오던 모습이 말이야."], ["What a rum thing time is, ain’t it, Neddy?’", "세월이란 거 참 묘한 거 아닌가, 응, 네디?\""]], [["The gentleman to whom these observations were addressed, who appeared of a taciturn and thoughtful cast, merely echoed the inquiry; Mr.", "이 말을 들은 신사는 과묵하고 사색적인 기질로 보였는데, 그저 그 물음을 따라 되뇔 뿐이었다."], ["Roker, shaking off the poetical and gloomy train of thought into which he had been betrayed, descended to the common business of life, and resumed his pen.", "로커 씨는 자신이 잠시 빠져들었던 시적이고 음울한 상념의 흐름을 떨쳐 내고는, 다시 일상사로 내려와 펜을 들었다."]], [["‘Do you know what the third gentlemen is?’ inquired Mr.", "\"세 번째 신사는 어떤 사람인지 아시오?\""], ["Pickwick, not very much gratified by this description of his future associates.", "픽윅 씨가 앞으로 함께 지낼 동료들에 관한 이 설명에 그다지 흡족하지 못한 채 물었다."]], [["‘What is that Simpson, Neddy?’ said Mr.", "\"그 심슨이란 작자는 뭐 하던 사람이지, 네디?\""], ["Roker, turning to his companion.", "로커 씨가 동료에게 돌아서며 말했다."]], [["‘What Simpson?’ said Neddy.", "\"어느 심슨 말이오?\" 네디가 말했다."]], [["‘Why, him in twenty-seven in the third, that this gentleman’s going to be chummed on.’", "\"왜, 삼층 이십칠 호에 있는 자, 이 신사분이 동숙하게 될 그 자 말이야.\""]], [["‘Oh, him!’ replied Neddy; ‘he’s nothing exactly.", "\"오, 그 작자!\" 네디가 대답했다."], ["He was a horse chaunter: he’s a leg now.’", "\"이렇다 할 게 없는 자요. 전에는 말 사기꾼이었는데, 지금은 노름꾼이지요.\""]], [["‘Ah, so I thought,’ rejoined Mr.", "\"아, 그럴 줄 알았어.\""], ["Roker, closing the book, and placing the small piece of paper in Mr. Pickwick’s hands.", "로커 씨가 장부를 덮고 작은 종이쪽지를 픽윅 씨의 손에 쥐여 주며 응수했다."], ["‘That’s the ticket, sir.’", "\"이게 배정표입니다, 선생님.\""]], [["Very much perplexed by this summary disposition of this person, Mr. Pickwick walked back into the prison, revolving in his mind what he had better do.", "이 인물에 대한 이런 즉결 처리에 몹시 어리둥절해진 픽윅 씨는, 어찌하는 게 좋을지 마음속으로 궁리하며 감옥 안으로 되돌아 걸어갔다."], ["Convinced, however, that before he took any other steps it would be advisable to see, and hold personal converse with, the three gentlemen with whom it was proposed to quarter him, he made the best of his way to the third flight.", "그러나 다른 어떤 조치를 취하기에 앞서, 함께 묵게 될 세 신사를 직접 만나 이야기를 나눠 보는 것이 현명하리라 판단하고, 서둘러 삼층으로 올라갔다."]], [["After groping about in the gallery for some time, attempting in the dim light to decipher the numbers on the different doors, he at length appealed to a pot-boy, who happened to be pursuing his morning occupation of gleaning for pewter.", "복도에서 한동안 더듬거리며, 흐릿한 빛 속에서 여러 문에 적힌 번호를 알아보려 애쓰던 끝에, 그는 마침 아침 일과인 백랍 그릇 거두는 일에 나선 술집 사환에게 물어보았다."]], [["‘Which is twenty-seven, my good fellow?’ said Mr.", "\"여보게, 이십칠 호가 어디인가?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Five doors farther on,’ replied the pot-boy.", "\"다섯 칸 더 가시면 됩니다.\" 사환이 대답했다."], ["‘There’s the likeness of a man being hung, and smoking the while, chalked outside the door.’", "\"교수형 당하면서 그 와중에 담배를 피우는 사내 모습이 문밖에 분필로 그려져 있습니다요.\""]], [["Guided by this direction, Mr. Pickwick proceeded slowly along the gallery until he encountered the ‘portrait of a gentleman,’ above described, upon whose countenance he tapped, with the knuckle of his forefinger--gently at first, and then audibly.", "이 안내에 따라 픽윅 씨는 복도를 따라 천천히 나아가, 앞서 말한 '신사의 초상'을 마주칠 때까지 걸었다."], ["After repeating this process several times without effect, he ventured to open the door and peep in.", "그러고는 그 얼굴 부분을 집게손가락 마디로 두드렸는데—처음에는 가만히, 그다음에는 소리가 나게 두드렸다. 이 과정을 여러 번 되풀이해도 아무 반응이 없자, 그는 큰맘 먹고 문을 열어 안을 들여다보았다."]], [["There was only one man in the room, and he was leaning out of window as far as he could without overbalancing himself, endeavouring, with great perseverance, to spit upon the crown of the hat of a personal friend on the parade below.", "방 안에는 단 한 사람뿐이었는데, 그는 몸이 넘어가지 않을 만큼 최대한 창밖으로 몸을 내밀고, 아래 운동장에 있는 어느 친구의 모자 꼭대기에 침을 뱉으려고 대단한 끈기로 애쓰고 있었다."], ["As neither speaking, coughing, sneezing, knocking, nor any other ordinary mode of attracting attention, made this person aware of the presence of a visitor, Mr. Pickwick, after some delay, stepped up to the window, and pulled him gently by the coat tail.", "말을 걸어도, 기침을 해도, 재채기를 해도, 문을 두드려도, 그 밖에 주의를 끄는 다른 어떤 흔한 방법도 이 사람에게 방문객이 와 있음을 알리지 못하자, 픽윅 씨는 한참 머뭇거린 끝에 창가로 다가가 그의 외투 자락을 가만히 잡아당겼다."], ["The individual brought in his head and shoulders with great swiftness, and surveying Mr. Pickwick from head to foot, demanded in a surly tone what the-- something beginning with a capital H--he wanted.", "그 사내는 머리와 어깨를 매우 잽싸게 안으로 들이밀고는, 픽윅 씨를 머리끝부터 발끝까지 훑어보더니, 무뚝뚝한 어조로 대문자 H로 시작하는—무슨 일로 왔느냐고 물었다."]], [["‘I believe,’ said Mr.", "\"제가 보기엔,\""], ["Pickwick, consulting his ticket--‘I believe this is twenty-seven in the third?’", "픽윅 씨가 배정표를 들여다보며 말했다. \"여기가 삼층 이십칠 호인 것 같은데요?\""]], [["‘Well?’ replied the gentleman.", "\"그래서요?\" 그 신사가 대답했다."]], [["‘I have come here in consequence of receiving this bit of paper,’ rejoined Mr.", "\"이 종이쪽지를 받고 여기로 온 겁니다.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 응수했다."]], [["‘Hand it over,’ said the gentleman.", "\"이리 줘 보시오.\" 그 신사가 말했다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick complied.", "픽윅 씨는 그대로 따랐다."]], [["‘I think Roker might have chummed you somewhere else,’ said Mr.", "\"로커가 당신을 다른 데로 보냈으면 좋았을 텐데.\""], ["Simpson (for it was the leg), after a very discontented sort of a pause.", "심슨 씨가(바로 그 노름꾼이었다) 몹시 못마땅한 듯 잠시 뜸을 들인 후에 말했다."]], [["Mr.", "픽윅 씨도 그렇게 생각했다."], ["Pickwick thought so also; but, under all the circumstances, he considered it a matter of sound policy to be silent.", "그러나 모든 정황을 고려할 때, 잠자코 있는 편이 현명한 처사라고 여겼다."]], [["Mr. Simpson mused for a few moments after this, and then, thrusting his head out of the window, gave a shrill whistle, and pronounced some word aloud, several times.", "심슨 씨는 그러고 나서 잠시 골똘히 생각하더니, 창밖으로 고개를 내밀고 날카롭게 휘파람을 불며 무슨 말인가를 큰 소리로 여러 번 외쳤다."], ["What the word was, Mr. Pickwick could not distinguish; but he rather inferred that it must be some nickname which distinguished Mr.", "그 말이 무엇인지 픽윅 씨는 알아들을 수 없었으나, 아래 운동장의 수많은 신사들이 곧바로 \"푸주한!\""], ["Martin, from the fact of a great number of gentlemen on the ground below, immediately proceeding to cry ‘Butcher!’ in imitation of the tone in which that useful class of society are wont, diurnally, to make their presence known at area railings.", "하고 외치기 시작하는 것으로 미루어—그 유익한 부류가 매일같이 지하실 난간에서 자기들이 왔음을 알릴 때 쓰는 어조를 흉내 내며—그것이 마틴 씨를 가리키는 무슨 별명이리라고 짐작했다."]], [["Subsequent occurrences confirmed the accuracy of Mr.", "뒤이은 일들로 픽윅 씨의 짐작이 정확했음이 확인되었다."], ["Pickwick’s impression; for, in a few seconds, a gentleman, prematurely broad for his years, clothed in a professional blue jean frock and top-boots with circular toes, entered the room nearly out of breath, closely followed by another gentleman in very shabby black, and a sealskin cap.", "몇 초 뒤, 나이에 비해 너무 일찍 떡 벌어진 신사 하나가, 직업용 푸른 진 작업복에 코가 둥근 장화 차림으로 거의 숨이 차서 방에 들어왔고, 그 뒤를 몹시 초라한 검은 옷에 물개 가죽 모자를 쓴 또 다른 신사가 바짝 뒤따라 들어왔다."], ["The latter gentleman, who fastened his coat all the way up to his chin by means of a pin and a button alternately, had a very coarse red face, and looked like a drunken chaplain; which, indeed, he was.", "뒤의 신사는 핀과 단추를 번갈아 써서 외투를 턱밑까지 단단히 여미고 있었는데, 매우 거친 붉은 얼굴이 술 취한 목사처럼 보였고, 실제로도 그러했다."]], [["These two gentlemen having by turns perused Mr. Pickwick’s billet, the one expressed his opinion that it was ‘a rig,’ and the other his conviction that it was ‘a go.’ Having recorded their feelings in these very intelligible terms, they looked at Mr.", "이 두 신사가 번갈아 픽윅 씨의 쪽지를 찬찬히 읽고 나서, 한 사람은 그것이 '장난질'이라는 의견을 내놓았고, 다른 한 사람은 그것이 '제대로 된 일'이라는 확신을 내비쳤다."], ["Pickwick and each other in awkward silence.", "이렇게 매우 알아듣기 쉬운 말로 자기들 감정을 기록한 뒤, 그들은 픽윅 씨와 서로를 어색한 침묵 속에 바라보았다."]], [["‘It’s an aggravating thing, just as we got the beds so snug,’ said the chaplain, looking at three dirty mattresses, each rolled up in a blanket; which occupied one corner of the room during the day, and formed a kind of slab, on which were placed an old cracked basin, ewer, and soap-dish, of common yellow earthenware, with a blue flower--‘very aggravating.’", "\"이거 골치 아프게 됐는걸, 이제 막 잠자리를 아늑하게 꾸며 놨는데.\" 목사가, 각각 담요에 둘둘 말린 세 개의 더러운 매트리스를 바라보며 말했다. 그 매트리스들은 낮 동안 방 한구석을 차지하고서 일종의 받침대 노릇을 했는데, 그 위에는 파란 꽃무늬가 그려진 흔한 노란 질그릇으로 된 금 간 낡은 대야와 물주전자와 비누 접시가 놓여 있었다. \"정말 골치 아프게 됐어.\""]], [["Mr. Martin expressed the same opinion in rather stronger terms; Mr.", "마틴 씨는 다소 더 거센 말로 같은 의견을 표했다."], ["Simpson, after having let a variety of expletive adjectives loose upon society without any substantive to accompany them, tucked up his sleeves, and began to wash the greens for dinner.", "심슨 씨는 명사 한 마디도 붙이지 않은 채 갖가지 욕설 형용사를 세상에 마구 풀어 놓은 다음, 소매를 걷어붙이고 저녁거리로 쓸 채소를 씻기 시작했다."]], [["While this was going on, Mr. Pickwick had been eyeing the room, which was filthily dirty, and smelt intolerably close.", "이러는 동안 픽윅 씨는 방 안을 둘러보고 있었는데, 방은 더럽기 짝이 없었고 참을 수 없을 만큼 답답한 냄새가 났다."], ["There was no vestige of either carpet, curtain, or blind.", "양탄자도, 커튼도, 차양도 흔적조차 없었다."], ["There was not even a closet in it.", "벽장 하나도 없었다."], ["Unquestionably there were but few things to put away, if there had been one; but, however few in number, or small in individual amount, still, remnants of loaves and pieces of cheese, and damp towels, and scrags of meat, and articles of wearing apparel, and mutilated crockery, and bellows without nozzles, and toasting-forks without prongs, do present somewhat of an uncomfortable appearance when they are scattered about the floor of a small apartment, which is the common sitting and sleeping room of three idle men.", "설령 벽장이 있었던들 치워 넣을 물건이라곤 거의 없었으리라는 건 의심할 여지가 없었다. 그러나 수가 아무리 적고 하나하나가 아무리 보잘것없다 한들, 빵 부스러기며 치즈 조각이며 축축한 수건이며 고기 자투리며 입던 옷가지며 깨진 그릇이며 주둥이 떨어진 풀무며 살이 빠진 토스트 포크가, 세 한량의 공동 거실 겸 침실인 좁은 방바닥에 여기저기 흩어져 있는 모습은, 그래도 어딘가 불편한 광경을 자아내기 마련이었다."]], [["‘I suppose this can be managed somehow,’ said the butcher, after a pretty long silence.", "\"이 일은 어떻게든 처리할 수 있을 것 같소만.\" 푸주한이 꽤 오랜 침묵 끝에 말했다."], ["‘What will you take to go out?’", "\"나가시는 데 얼마면 되겠소?\""]], [["I beg your pardon,’ replied Mr.", "\"미안하지만,\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다."], ["Pickwick. ‘What did you say?", "\"뭐라고 하셨소?"], ["I hardly understand you.’", "무슨 말인지 잘 모르겠소만.\""]], [["‘What will you take to be paid out?’ said the butcher.", "\"빚을 치르고 나가시는 데 얼마면 되겠냐는 말이오.\" 푸주한이 말했다."], ["‘The regular chummage is two-and-six.", "\"정해진 동숙료는 이 실링 육 펜스요."], ["Will you take three bob?’", "삼 실링이면 되겠소?\""]], [["‘And a bender,’ suggested the clerical gentleman.", "\"한턱 내는 것도 곁들여서.\" 목사 양반이 덧붙였다."]], [["‘Well, I don’t mind that; it’s only twopence a piece more,’ said Mr.", "\"음, 그건 나도 상관없소. 한 사람당 이 펜스 더 얹는 것뿐이니.\""], ["Martin. ‘What do you say, now?", "마틴 씨가 말했다."], ["We’ll pay you out for three-and-sixpence a week.", "\"자, 어떠시오? 일주일에 삼 실링 육 펜스에 당신을 빼 드리겠소."], ["Come!’", "자!\""]], [["‘And stand a gallon of beer down,’ chimed in Mr.", "\"맥주 한 갤런도 내놓으시고.\" 심슨 씨가 거들었다."], ["Simpson. ‘There!’", "\"그렇지!\""]], [["‘And drink it on the spot,’ said the chaplain.", "\"그리고 그 자리에서 마셔 버리고.\" 목사가 말했다."], ["‘Now!’", "\"자, 어서!\""]], [["‘I really am so wholly ignorant of the rules of this place,’ returned Mr.", "\"내가 이곳 규칙에 워낙 깜깜이라,\" 픽윅 씨가 응수했다."], ["Pickwick, ‘that I do not yet comprehend you.", "\"아직 무슨 말씀인지 모르겠소."], ["Can I live anywhere else?", "내가 다른 데서 지낼 수도 있단 말이오?"], ["I thought I could not.’", "그럴 수 없는 줄 알았는데.\""]], [["At this inquiry Mr. Martin looked, with a countenance of excessive surprise, at his two friends, and then each gentleman pointed with his right thumb over his left shoulder.", "이 물음에 마틴 씨는 몹시 놀란 표정으로 두 친구를 바라보았고, 그러자 세 신사는 저마다 오른손 엄지손가락으로 왼쪽 어깨 너머를 가리켰다."], ["This action imperfectly described in words by the very feeble term of ‘over the left,’ when performed by any number of ladies or gentlemen who are accustomed to act in unison, has a very graceful and airy effect; its expression is one of light and playful sarcasm.", "'뒷전으로'라는 매우 빈약한 말로밖에 글로는 어설프게 설명되지 않는 이 동작은, 한데 어울려 행동하는 데 익숙한 여러 신사 숙녀가 행할 때면 매우 우아하고 경쾌한 효과를 내는데, 그 의미는 가볍고 장난스러운 빈정거림이다."]], [["‘Can you!’ repeated Mr.", "\"할 수 있느냐고요!\""], ["Martin, with a smile of pity.", "마틴 씨가 가엾다는 듯 미소를 지으며 되풀이했다."]], [["‘Well, if I knew as little of life as that, I’d eat my hat and swallow the buckle whole,’ said the clerical gentleman.", "\"음, 내가 그렇게 세상 물정을 모른다면, 내 모자를 먹어 치우고 버클까지 통째로 삼키겠소.\" 목사 양반이 말했다."]], [["‘So would I,’ added the sporting one solemnly.", "\"나도 그러겠소.\" 그 노름꾼 신사가 엄숙하게 덧붙였다."]], [["After this introductory preface, the three chums informed Mr. Pickwick, in a breath, that money was, in the Fleet, just what money was out of it; that it would instantly procure him almost anything he desired; and that, supposing he had it, and had no objection to spend it, if he only signified his wish to have a room to himself, he might take possession of one, furnished and fitted to boot, in half an hour’s time.", "이런 서두를 늘어놓은 다음, 세 동숙인은 한목소리로 픽윅 씨에게 일러 주기를, 플리트 안에서도 돈은 바깥에서나 다름없는 것이라, 원하는 것은 거의 무엇이든 당장 마련해 줄 수 있으며, 만약 그에게 돈이 있고 또 그것을 쓰는 데 거리낌이 없다면, 혼자 쓸 방을 갖고 싶다는 뜻만 비치면 삼십 분 안에 가구까지 갖춰져 딸린 방 하나를 차지할 수 있다고 했다."]], [["With this the parties separated, very much to their common satisfaction; Mr.", "이로써 양측은 서로 크게 만족하며 헤어졌다."], ["Pickwick once more retracing his steps to the lodge, and the three companions adjourning to the coffee-room, there to spend the five shillings which the clerical gentleman had, with admirable prudence and foresight, borrowed of him for the purpose.", "픽윅 씨는 다시 한 번 관리실로 발길을 돌렸고, 세 동료는 커피실로 자리를 옮겨, 목사 양반이 감탄스러운 신중함과 선견지명으로 바로 그 목적을 위해 픽윅 씨에게서 빌린 오 실링을 거기서 써 버리기로 했다."]], [["‘I knowed it!’ said Mr.", "\"내 그럴 줄 알았지!\""], ["Roker, with a chuckle, when Mr. Pickwick stated the object with which he had returned.", "픽윅 씨가 되돌아온 목적을 밝히자 로커 씨가 빙긋이 웃으며 말했다."], ["‘Didn’t I say so, Neddy?’", "\"내가 그렇다고 안 했나, 네디?\""]], [["The philosophical owner of the universal penknife growled an affirmative.", "만능 주머니칼의 철학적 주인은 끙 하고 동의의 소리를 냈다."]], [["‘I knowed you’d want a room for yourself, bless you!’ said Mr.", "\"당신이 혼자 쓸 방을 원할 줄 알았다니까요, 정말이지!\" 로커 씨가 말했다."], ["Roker. ‘Let me see.", "\"어디 봅시다."], ["You’ll want some furniture.", "가구가 좀 필요하시겠지요."], ["You’ll hire that of me, I suppose?", "그건 나한테서 빌리실 거고요?"], ["That’s the reg’lar thing.’", "그게 통상적인 방식이거든요.\""]], [["‘With great pleasure,’ replied Mr.", "\"기꺼이 그러지요.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘There’s a capital room up in the coffee-room flight, that belongs to a Chancery prisoner,’ said Mr.", "\"커피실 쪽 계단 위에 아주 훌륭한 방이 하나 있는데, 형평법원 죄수 거랍니다.\" 로커 씨가 말했다."], ["Roker. ‘It’ll stand you in a pound a week.", "\"일주일에 일 파운드 들 겁니다."], ["I suppose you don’t mind that?’", "그쯤은 괜찮으시겠지요?\""]], [["‘Not at all,’ said Mr.", "\"전혀 상관없소.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Just step there with me,’ said Roker, taking up his hat with great alacrity; ‘the matter’s settled in five minutes.", "\"나랑 같이 그리로 가 보십시다.\" 로커가 몹시 잽싸게 모자를 집어 들며 말했다."], ["Lord!", "\"일은 오 분이면 결판납니다."], ["why didn’t you say at first that you was willing to come down handsome?’", "원, 처음부터 통 크게 내겠다고 말씀하시지 그러셨소!\""]], [["The matter was soon arranged, as the turnkey had foretold.", "간수가 미리 말한 대로 일은 금세 마무리되었다."], ["The Chancery prisoner had been there long enough to have lost his friends, fortune, home, and happiness, and to have acquired the right of having a room to himself. As he laboured, however, under the inconvenience of often wanting a morsel of bread, he eagerly listened to Mr.", "그 형평법원 죄수는 친구도, 재산도, 집도, 행복도 다 잃을 만큼, 그리고 혼자 쓸 방을 가질 권리를 얻을 만큼 오래 그곳에 있어 온 터였다."], ["Pickwick’s proposal to rent the apartment, and readily covenanted and agreed to yield him up the sole and undisturbed possession thereof, in consideration of the weekly payment of twenty shillings; from which fund he furthermore contracted to pay out any person or persons that might be chummed upon it.", "그러나 그는 빵 한 조각이 아쉬울 때가 잦은 불편을 겪고 있었던지라, 방을 세놓으라는 픽윅 씨의 제안에 귀가 솔깃했고, 매주 이십 실링을 받는 대가로 그 방을 오로지 픽윅 씨 혼자 방해받지 않고 차지하도록 넘겨주기로 선뜻 계약하고 합의했다. 더 나아가 그는 그 돈으로, 그 방에 동숙하게 될지도 모르는 어떤 사람이든 빚을 치르고 내보내 주기로도 약정했다."]], [["As they struck the bargain, Mr. Pickwick surveyed him with a painful interest.", "흥정을 매듭짓는 동안, 픽윅 씨는 가슴 아픈 심정으로 그를 살펴보았다."], ["He was a tall, gaunt, cadaverous man, in an old greatcoat and slippers, with sunken cheeks, and a restless, eager eye.", "그는 키가 크고 수척하며 송장 같은 사내로, 낡은 외투에 슬리퍼 차림이었고, 두 볼은 움푹 꺼지고 눈은 안절부절 갈망에 차 있었다."], ["His lips were bloodless, and his bones sharp and thin.", "입술에는 핏기가 없었고 뼈는 날카롭고 앙상했다."], ["God help him!", "가엾어라!"], ["the iron teeth of confinement and privation had been slowly filing him down for twenty years.", "감금과 결핍의 무쇠 이빨이 이십 년 세월에 걸쳐 그를 서서히 갈아 깎아 온 것이었다."]], [["‘And where will you live meanwhile, Sir?’ said Mr.", "\"그동안 선생은 어디서 지내시려오?\""], ["Pickwick, as he laid the amount of the first week’s rent, in advance, on the tottering table.", "픽윅 씨가 흔들거리는 탁자 위에 첫 주 방세를 선불로 내려놓으며 말했다."]], [["The man gathered up the money with a trembling hand, and replied that he didn’t know yet; he must go and see where he could move his bed to.", "그 사내는 떨리는 손으로 돈을 그러모으며, 아직 모르겠다고, 침대를 어디로 옮길 수 있을지 가서 알아봐야겠다고 대답했다."]], [["‘I am afraid, sir,’ said Mr.", "\"내 염려가 됩니다, 선생.\""], ["Pickwick, laying his hand gently and compassionately on his arm--‘I am afraid you will have to live in some noisy, crowded place.", "픽윅 씨가 그의 팔에 가만히 측은한 손길을 얹으며 말했다. \"선생이 어딘가 시끄럽고 북적이는 데서 지내셔야 할까 봐 말이오."], ["Now, pray, consider this room your own when you want quiet, or when any of your friends come to see you.’", "그러니 부디, 조용히 있고 싶으실 때나 친구분들이 찾아오실 때면 이 방을 선생 방으로 여기십시오.\""]], [["‘Friends!’ interposed the man, in a voice which rattled in his throat.", "\"친구!\" 그 사내가, 목구멍에서 그르렁거리는 목소리로 끼어들었다."], ["‘if I lay dead at the bottom of the deepest mine in the world; tight screwed down and soldered in my coffin; rotting in the dark and filthy ditch that drags its slime along, beneath the foundations of this prison; I could not be more forgotten or unheeded than I am here.", "\"내가 이 세상에서 가장 깊은 광산 바닥에 죽어 누워, 관 속에 단단히 못 박히고 납땜되어, 이 감옥 토대 밑으로 진창을 끌고 흐르는 어둡고 더러운 도랑 속에서 썩어 간다 한들, 지금 여기 있는 것보다 더 잊히거나 외면당할 수는 없을 거요. 나는 죽은 사람이오."], ["I am a dead man; dead to society, without the pity they bestow on those whose souls have passed to judgment.", "사회에 대해 죽은 사람이지요. 영혼이 심판대로 넘어간 자들에게 베푸는 그 동정조차 받지 못하는 처지요."], ["Friends to see me!", "친구가 날 찾아온다니!"], ["My God!", "맙소사!"], ["I have sunk, from the prime of life into old age, in this place, and there is not one to raise his hand above my bed when I lie dead upon it, and say, “It is a blessing he is gone!”’", "나는 한창나이에서 노년에 이르기까지 이곳에서 가라앉아 왔건만, 내가 침대에 죽어 누우면 내 머리맡에 손을 들어 '그가 떠났으니 다행이다!' 하고 말해 줄 이 하나 없소이다.\""]], [["The excitement, which had cast an unwonted light over the man’s face, while he spoke, subsided as he concluded; and pressing his withered hands together in a hasty and disordered manner, he shuffled from the room.", "말하는 동안 그 사내의 얼굴에 예사롭지 않은 빛을 드리웠던 흥분은, 말을 마치자 가라앉았다. 그는 시들어 빠진 두 손을 황급히 흐트러진 모양새로 맞잡고는, 발을 질질 끌며 방을 나갔다."]], [["‘Rides rather rusty,’ said Mr.", "\"좀 까칠하게 구는구먼요.\""], ["Roker, with a smile.", "로커 씨가 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["‘Ah!", "\"아!"], ["they’re like the elephants.", "저런 자들은 코끼리 같지요."], ["They feel it now and then, and it makes ‘em wild!’", "이따금 그게 느껴지면, 그러면 사나워지는 겁니다!\""]], [["Having made this deeply-sympathising remark, Mr. Roker entered upon his arrangements with such expedition, that in a short time the room was furnished with a carpet, six chairs, a table, a sofa bedstead, a tea- kettle, and various small articles, on hire, at the very reasonable rate of seven-and-twenty shillings and sixpence per week.", "이렇게 깊은 동정을 담은 말을 한 다음, 로커 씨는 어찌나 척척 일을 처리했는지, 얼마 안 가 그 방은 양탄자, 의자 여섯, 탁자, 소파 겸 침대, 찻주전자, 그리고 갖가지 자잘한 물건들로 갖춰졌는데, 일주일에 이십칠 실링 육 펜스라는 매우 합리적인 세로 빌린 것이었다."]], [["‘Now, is there anything more we can do for you?’ inquired Mr.", "\"자, 또 뭐 해 드릴 일이 있을까요?\""], ["Roker, looking round with great satisfaction, and gaily chinking the first week’s hire in his closed fist.", "로커 씨가 매우 흡족한 듯 방을 둘러보며, 그리고 첫 주 임대료를 움켜쥔 주먹 안에서 흥겹게 절렁거리며 물었다."]], [["‘Why, yes,’ said Mr.", "\"음, 그렇소.\""], ["Pickwick, who had been musing deeply for some time.", "한동안 깊이 생각에 잠겨 있던 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["‘Are there any people here who run on errands, and so forth?’", "\"여기 심부름 따위를 다니는 사람이 있소?\""]], [["‘Outside, do you mean?’ inquired Mr.", "\"바깥 심부름 말씀입니까?\""], ["Roker.", "로커 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘Yes.", "\"그렇소."], ["I mean who are able to go outside.", "바깥에 나갈 수 있는 사람 말이오."], ["Not prisoners.’", "죄수가 아닌 사람.\""]], [["‘Yes, there is,’ said Roker.", "\"예, 있습니다.\" 로커가 말했다."], ["‘There’s an unfortunate devil, who has got a friend on the poor side, that’s glad to do anything of that sort.", "\"빈민 구역에 친구가 한 사람 있는, 불쌍한 작자가 하나 있는데, 그런 일이라면 뭐든 기꺼이 합니다."], ["He’s been running odd jobs, and that, for the last two months.", "지난 두 달 동안 잡일이며 그런 걸 해 오고 있지요."], ["Shall I send him?’", "그 사람을 보내 드릴까요?\""]], [["‘If you please,’ rejoined Mr.", "\"그래 주시오.\" 픽윅 씨가 응수했다."], ["Pickwick. ‘Stay; no.", "\"가만—아니오."], ["The poor side, you say?", "빈민 구역이라 하셨소?"], ["I should like to see it.", "그곳을 한번 보고 싶구려."], ["I’ll go to him myself.’", "내가 직접 그 사람한테 가 보겠소.\""]], [["The poor side of a debtor’s prison is, as its name imports, that in which the most miserable and abject class of debtors are confined.", "채무자 감옥의 빈민 구역이란, 그 이름이 뜻하는 대로, 가장 비참하고 가장 비천한 부류의 채무자들이 갇혀 있는 곳이다. 빈민 구역에 등록한 죄수는 방세도 동숙료도 내지 않는다."], ["A prisoner having declared upon the poor side, pays neither rent nor chummage.", "감옥에 들어오고 나갈 때 무는 비용도 그만큼 줄어들고, 약간의 식량을 나눠 받을 자격이 생기는데, 그 식량을 마련하기 위해 자선심 깊은 몇몇 사람들이 때때로 유언으로 얼마 안 되는 유산을 남겨 두었다."], ["His fees, upon entering and leaving the jail, are reduced in amount, and he becomes entitled to a share of some small quantities of food: to provide which, a few charitable persons have, from time to time, left trifling legacies in their wills.", "우리 독자 대부분은 기억하겠지만, 불과 몇 해 전까지만 해도 플리트 감옥 담장에는 일종의 쇠우리가 있었고, 그 안에는 굶주린 표정의 사내가 한 사람 자리하고 있어, 이따금 돈통을 절렁절렁 흔들며 구슬픈 목소리로 외치곤 했다. \"제발, 가엾은 채무자들을 생각해 주십시오."], ["Most of our readers will remember, that, until within a very few years past, there was a kind of iron cage in the wall of the Fleet Prison, within which was posted some man of hungry looks, who, from time to time, rattled a money-box, and exclaimed in a mournful voice, ‘Pray, remember the poor debtors; pray remember the poor debtors.’ The receipts of this box, when there were any, were divided among the poor prisoners; and the men on the poor side relieved each other in this degrading office.", "제발 가엾은 채무자들을 생각해 주십시오.\" 이 돈통에 들어온 돈은, 들어온 게 있을 때면, 가난한 죄수들에게 나눠 주었고, 빈민 구역의 사내들은 이 비참한 임무를 서로 번갈아 맡았다."]], [["Although this custom has been abolished, and the cage is now boarded up, the miserable and destitute condition of these unhappy persons remains the same.", "이 관습은 폐지되었고 우리는 이제 그 쇠우리에 판자를 둘러막았지만, 이 가련한 사람들의 비참하고 궁핍한 처지는 여전히 그대로다."], ["We no longer suffer them to appeal at the prison gates to the charity and compassion of the passersby; but we still leave unblotted the leaves of our statute book, for the reverence and admiration of succeeding ages, the just and wholesome law which declares that the sturdy felon shall be fed and clothed, and that the penniless debtor shall be left to die of starvation and nakedness.", "우리는 이제 그들이 감옥 문에서 지나가는 행인들의 자비와 연민에 호소하도록 내버려 두지 않는다. 그러나 우리는 여전히, 후세의 존경과 찬탄을 받도록, 법전의 책장에 한 점 얼룩도 없이 남겨 두고 있으니, 그 정의롭고 유익한 법이란 곧, 억센 중죄인은 먹이고 입혀야 하나, 한 푼 없는 채무자는 굶주림과 헐벗음 속에 죽도록 내버려 두어야 한다고 선언하는 법이다."], ["This is no fiction.", "이것은 결코 지어낸 이야기가 아니다."], ["Not a week passes over our head, but, in every one of our prisons for debt, some of these men must inevitably expire in the slow agonies of want, if they were not relieved by their fellow-prisoners.", "채무자를 가두는 우리의 감옥마다, 동료 죄수들이 구제해 주지 않는다면, 이런 사람들 가운데 누군가가 결핍의 느린 고통 속에서 숨을 거두지 않고 지나가는 한 주가 없다."]], [["Turning these things in his mind, as he mounted the narrow staircase at the foot of which Roker had left him, Mr.", "로커가 픽윅 씨를 남겨 두고 떠난 그 좁은 계단을 올라가며 이런 것들을 마음속으로 곱씹다가, 픽윅 씨는 차츰 끓어 넘치는 지경에 이르렀다."], ["Pickwick gradually worked himself to the boiling-over point; and so excited was he with his reflections on this subject, that he had burst into the room to which he had been directed, before he had any distinct recollection, either of the place in which he was, or of the object of his visit.", "이 문제에 관한 상념에 어찌나 격앙되었던지, 그는 자신이 어디에 있는지, 무슨 일로 찾아왔는지조차 똑똑히 떠올리기도 전에, 안내받은 그 방으로 불쑥 뛰어들고 말았다."]], [["The general aspect of the room recalled him to himself at once; but he had no sooner cast his eye on the figure of a man who was brooding over the dusty fire, than, letting his hat fall on the floor, he stood perfectly fixed and immovable with astonishment.", "방의 전반적인 모습이 그를 단번에 정신 들게 했다. 그러나 먼지투성이 난롯불 앞에서 깊은 시름에 잠겨 있는 한 사내의 모습에 눈길이 닿기가 무섭게, 그는 모자를 바닥에 떨어뜨린 채, 놀라움에 그대로 못 박힌 듯 꼼짝도 하지 못하고 서 있었다."]], [["Yes; in tattered garments, and without a coat; his common calico shirt, yellow and in rags; his hair hanging over his face; his features changed with suffering, and pinched with famine--there sat Mr.", "그렇다. 누덕누덕 해진 옷에 외투도 걸치지 못한 채, 흔한 옥양목 셔츠는 누렇게 바래 너덜너덜하고, 머리카락은 얼굴 위로 늘어지고, 이목구비는 고통으로 일그러지고 굶주림으로 핼쑥해진—거기 앨프리드 징글 씨가 앉아 있었다."], ["Alfred Jingle; his head resting on his hands, his eyes fixed upon the fire, and his whole appearance denoting misery and dejection!", "그는 두 손에 머리를 받치고 눈은 불을 응시한 채, 온몸으로 비참함과 낙담을 드러내고 있었다!"]], [["Near him, leaning listlessly against the wall, stood a strong-built countryman, flicking with a worn-out hunting-whip the top-boot that adorned his right foot; his left being thrust into an old slipper.", "그의 곁에는, 벽에 맥없이 기대선 채, 건장한 시골 사내 하나가, 닳아 빠진 사냥용 채찍으로 오른발을 장식한 장화를 툭툭 치고 있었다. 그의 왼발은 낡은 슬리퍼에 꿰어져 있었다."], ["Horses, dogs, and drink had brought him there, pell-mell.", "말과 개와 술이 그를 한꺼번에 이리로 몰아넣었던 것이다."], ["There was a rusty spur on the solitary boot, which he occasionally jerked into the empty air, at the same time giving the boot a smart blow, and muttering some of the sounds by which a sportsman encourages his horse.", "그 외짝 장화에는 녹슨 박차가 달려 있었는데, 그는 이따금 그것을 허공으로 휙 차올리는 동시에 장화를 세게 두드리며, 사냥꾼이 말을 다그칠 때 내는 소리를 몇 마디 중얼거렸다."], ["He was riding, in imagination, some desperate steeplechase at that moment.", "그는 그 순간, 상상 속에서 어떤 무모한 장애물 경마를 달리고 있었던 것이다."], ["Poor wretch!", "가엾은 자여!"], ["He never rode a match on the swiftest animal in his costly stud, with half the speed at which he had torn along the course that ended in the Fleet.", "그가 값비싼 마구간의 가장 빠른 짐승에 올라 시합을 치를 때도, 플리트로 끝나 버린 그 코스를 내달리던 속도의 절반에도 미치지 못했으리라."]], [["On the opposite side of the room an old man was seated on a small wooden box, with his eyes riveted on the floor, and his face settled into an expression of the deepest and most hopeless despair.", "방 맞은편에는 노인 하나가 작은 나무 상자 위에 앉아, 눈을 바닥에 못 박은 채, 얼굴은 더없이 깊고 가망 없는 절망의 표정으로 굳어 있었다."], ["A young girl--his little grand-daughter--was hanging about him, endeavouring, with a thousand childish devices, to engage his attention; but the old man neither saw nor heard her.", "어린 소녀 하나가—그의 어린 손녀가—그에게 매달려, 온갖 어린애다운 갖은 수를 써 가며 그의 주의를 끌어 보려 애쓰고 있었지만, 노인은 그 아이를 보지도 듣지도 못했다."], ["The voice that had been music to him, and the eyes that had been light, fell coldly on his senses.", "한때 그에게 음악이었던 목소리도, 한때 그에게 빛이었던 눈동자도, 이제 그의 감각에 차갑게 떨어질 뿐이었다."], ["His limbs were shaking with disease, and the palsy had fastened on his mind.", "그의 사지는 병으로 떨리고 있었고, 마비가 그의 정신마저 사로잡고 있었다."]], [["There were two or three other men in the room, congregated in a little knot, and noiselessly talking among themselves.", "방에는 두세 사람이 더 있어, 작은 무리를 이루어 모여 자기들끼리 소리 없이 이야기를 나누고 있었다."], ["There was a lean and haggard woman, too--a prisoner’s wife--who was watering, with great solicitude, the wretched stump of a dried-up, withered plant, which, it was plain to see, could never send forth a green leaf again--too true an emblem, perhaps, of the office she had come there to discharge.", "야위고 초췌한 여인도 하나 있었는데—어느 죄수의 아내였다—그녀는 바싹 마르고 시들어 버린 식물의 가련한 그루터기에 정성스레 물을 주고 있었다. 그 식물은 한눈에 보아도 다시는 푸른 잎을 틔우지 못할 것이 분명했으니—어쩌면 그녀가 이곳에 와서 떠맡은 그 소임을 너무도 잘 나타내는 표상이었으리라."]], [["Such were the objects which presented themselves to Mr. Pickwick’s view, as he looked round him in amazement.", "픽윅 씨가 놀라움 속에 주위를 둘러보았을 때 그의 눈앞에 나타난 것들은 그러했다."], ["The noise of some one stumbling hastily into the room, roused him.", "누군가가 허둥지둥 방으로 비틀거리며 들어오는 소리에 그는 정신이 들었다."], ["Turning his eyes towards the door, they encountered the new-comer; and in him, through his rags and dirt, he recognised the familiar features of Mr. Job Trotter.", "문 쪽으로 눈을 돌리자 새로 들어온 자와 시선이 마주쳤는데, 그 누더기와 때 너머로 그는 낯익은 잡 트로터 씨의 이목구비를 알아보았다."]], [["‘Mr.", "\"픽윅 씨!\""], ["Pickwick!’ exclaimed Job aloud.", "잡이 큰 소리로 외쳤다."]], [["‘Eh?’ said Jingle, starting from his seat.", "\"네?\" 징글이 자리에서 벌떡 일어나며 말했다."]], [["‘Mr ----!", "\"픽—!"], ["So it is--queer place--strange things--serves me right--very.’ Mr.", "정말 그렇군—묘한 곳—이상한 일—꼴좋다—참으로.\""], ["Jingle thrust his hands into the place where his trousers pockets used to be, and, dropping his chin upon his breast, sank back into his chair.", "징글 씨는 바지 주머니가 있던 자리에 두 손을 찔러 넣고, 턱을 가슴께로 떨군 채 도로 의자에 주저앉았다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick was affected; the two men looked so very miserable.", "픽윅 씨는 마음이 동했다."], ["The sharp, involuntary glance Jingle had cast at a small piece of raw loin of mutton, which Job had brought in with him, said more of their reduced state than two hours’ explanation could have done.", "두 사람의 몰골이 너무도 비참해 보였기 때문이다. 잡이 들고 들어온 작은 양 허릿살 한 조각을 향해 징글이 자기도 모르게 던진 날카로운 눈길은, 두 시간에 걸친 설명보다도 그들의 영락한 처지를 더 많이 말해 주었다."], ["Mr. Pickwick looked mildly at Jingle, and said--", "픽윅 씨는 부드럽게 징글을 바라보며 말했다."]], [["‘I should like to speak to you in private.", "\"긴히 할 얘기가 있소."], ["Will you step out for an instant?’", "잠깐 나와 주겠소?\""]], [["‘Certainly,’ said Jingle, rising hastily.", "\"물론이지요.\" 징글이 황급히 일어서며 말했다."], ["‘Can’t step far--no danger of overwalking yourself here--spike park--grounds pretty--romantic, but not extensive--open for public inspection--family always in town-- housekeeper desperately careful--very.’", "\"멀리는 못 가지만—여기선 너무 걸어 다닐 염려는 없으니—철창 정원—풍경 좋고—낭만적이나 넓지는 않고—일반 공개—주인은 늘 도시에 나가 있고—가정부는 끔찍이도 꼼꼼하고—참으로.\""]], [["‘You have forgotten your coat,’ said Mr.", "\"외투를 두고 왔구려.\""], ["Pickwick, as they walked out to the staircase, and closed the door after them.", "그들이 계단으로 걸어 나와 등 뒤로 문을 닫을 때 픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Eh?’ said Jingle.", "\"네?\" 징글이 말했다."], ["‘Spout--dear relation--uncle Tom--couldn’t help it-- must eat, you know.", "\"전당포에—소중한 친척—톰 삼촌—어쩔 수 없었지요—먹어야 하니까요."], ["Wants of nature--and all that.’", "본능의 요구—뭐 그런 거지요.\""]], [["‘What do you mean?’", "\"무슨 말이오?\""]], [["‘Gone, my dear sir--last coat--can’t help it.", "\"가 버렸지요, 선생님—마지막 외투—어쩔 수 없었습니다."], ["Lived on a pair of boots, whole fortnight.", "장화 한 켤레로 꼬박 두 주를 버텼지요."], ["Silk umbrella--ivory handle--week--fact--honour--ask Job--knows it.’", "비단 우산—상아 손잡이—한 주—사실입니다—명예를 걸고—잡한테 물어보세요—알고 있으니까.\""]], [["‘Lived for three weeks upon a pair of boots, and a silk umbrella with an ivory handle!’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"장화 한 켤레와 상아 손잡이가 달린 비단 우산으로 세 주를 버텼다고!\" 픽윅 씨가 외쳤다."], ["Pickwick, who had only heard of such things in shipwrecks or read of them in Constable’s Miscellany.", "그는 그런 일을 난파선 이야기에서나 들었거나 콘스터블 잡지에서나 읽어 보았을 뿐이었다."]], [["‘True,’ said Jingle, nodding his head.", "\"사실입니다.\" 징글이 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다."], ["‘Pawnbroker’s shop--duplicates here--small sums--mere nothing--all rascals.’", "\"전당포—전당표가 여기 있지요—푼돈—아무것도 아닌 액수—죄다 악당들이라.\""]], [["‘Oh,’ said Mr.", "\"오,\""], ["Pickwick, much relieved by this explanation; ‘I understand you.", "픽윅 씨가 이 설명에 크게 안도하며 말했다. \"알겠소."], ["You have pawned your wardrobe.’", "옷가지를 전당 잡혔다는 말이군.\""]], [["‘Everything--Job’s too--all shirts gone--never mind--saves washing.", "\"전부 다—잡 것도—셔츠도 다 가 버렸지요—상관없습니다—빨래 수고를 더는 셈이니."], ["Nothing soon--lie in bed--starve--die--inquest--little bone-house--poor prisoner--common necessaries--hush it up--gentlemen of the jury-- warden’s tradesmen--keep it snug--natural death--coroner’s order-- workhouse funeral--serve him right--all over--drop the curtain.’", "곧 아무것도 안 남겠지요—침대에 누워—굶고—죽고—검시—작은 영안실—가엾은 죄수—최소한의 필수품—쉬쉬하고—배심원 여러분—교도소장 단골 상인들—조용히 덮고—자연사—검시관 명령—구빈원 장례—꼴좋다—다 끝나고—막을 내리고.\""]], [["Jingle delivered this singular summary of his prospects in life, with his accustomed volubility, and with various twitches of the countenance to counterfeit smiles.", "징글은 자기 인생의 앞날에 관한 이 기이한 요약을, 늘 그렇듯 거침없이 늘어놓으며, 미소를 꾸며 내려고 얼굴을 갖가지로 씰룩거렸다."], ["Mr. Pickwick easily perceived that his recklessness was assumed, and looking him full, but not unkindly, in the face, saw that his eyes were moist with tears.", "픽윅 씨는 그의 무모함이 가장된 것임을 어렵잖게 알아챘고, 그의 얼굴을 정면으로, 그러나 모질지 않게 들여다보다가, 그의 눈이 눈물로 젖어 있는 것을 보았다."]], [["‘Good fellow,’ said Jingle, pressing his hand, and turning his head away.", "\"좋은 분이지요.\" 징글이 그의 손을 꼭 쥐고 고개를 돌리며 말했다."], ["‘Ungrateful dog--boyish to cry--can’t help it--bad fever--weak-- ill--hungry.", "\"배은망덕한 놈—사내답잖게 울다니—어쩔 수가 없네요—고약한 열병—몸이 약해져—병들고—굶주려서."], ["Deserved it all--but suffered much--very.’ Wholly unable to keep up appearances any longer, and perhaps rendered worse by the effort he had made, the dejected stroller sat down on the stairs, and, covering his face with his hands, sobbed like a child.", "죄다 자업자득—허나 어지간히 고생했지요—참으로.\" 더는 도무지 겉치레를 유지할 수 없었던 데다, 어쩌면 그 안간힘 때문에 한층 더 무너져 버린 이 낙담한 떠돌이 배우는, 계단에 주저앉아 두 손에 얼굴을 묻고 어린아이처럼 흐느꼈다."]], [["‘Come, come,’ said Mr.", "\"자, 자,\""], ["Pickwick, with considerable emotion, ‘we will see what can be done, when I know all about the matter.", "픽윅 씨가 적잖이 감정에 북받쳐 말했다. \"내가 사정을 다 알게 되면, 무엇을 할 수 있을지 알아봅시다."], ["Here, Job; where is that fellow?’", "이보게, 잡, 그 친구는 어디 있나?\""]], [["‘Here, sir,’ replied Job, presenting himself on the staircase.", "\"여기 있습니다, 선생님.\" 잡이 계단에 모습을 드러내며 대답했다."], ["We have described him, by the bye, as having deeply-sunken eyes, in the best of times.", "그런데 앞서 말했듯, 우리는 그를 가장 형편이 좋을 때조차 눈이 움푹 꺼진 자로 묘사한 바 있다."], ["In his present state of want and distress, he looked as if those features had gone out of town altogether.", "지금의 궁핍하고 곤궁한 처지에서는, 마치 그 두 눈이 아예 도시 밖으로 떠나 버린 것처럼 보였다."]], [["‘Here, sir,’ cried Job.", "\"여기 있습니다, 선생님.\" 잡이 외쳤다."]], [["‘Come here, sir,’ said Mr.", "\"이리 오게.\""], ["Pickwick, trying to look stern, with four large tears running down his waistcoat.", "픽윅 씨가, 조끼 위로 굵은 눈물 네 줄기를 흘리면서, 짐짓 엄한 표정을 지으려 애쓰며 말했다."], ["‘Take that, sir.’", "\"이걸 받게.\""]], [["Take what?", "무엇을 받으라는 것인가?"], ["In the ordinary acceptation of such language, it should have been a blow.", "이런 말이 흔히 쓰이는 뜻대로라면, 그것은 한 대 갈기는 일이어야 했다."], ["As the world runs, it ought to have been a sound, hearty cuff; for Mr.", "세상 돌아가는 이치로 보자면, 그것은 묵직하고 호된 따귀여야 마땅했다."], ["Pickwick had been duped, deceived, and wronged by the destitute outcast who was now wholly in his power. Must we tell the truth?", "픽윅 씨는 지금 완전히 자기 손아귀에 들어온 이 궁핍한 떠돌이에게 속고, 기만당하고, 부당한 일을 당해 왔기 때문이다."], ["It was something from Mr.", "사실대로 말해야 할까?"], ["Pickwick’s waistcoat pocket, which chinked as it was given into Job’s hand, and the giving of which, somehow or other imparted a sparkle to the eye, and a swelling to the heart, of our excellent old friend, as he hurried away.", "그것은 픽윅 씨의 조끼 주머니에서 나온 무엇이었으니, 잡의 손에 건네질 때 절렁 소리를 냈다. 그리고 그것을 건네는 일은, 어찌 된 영문인지, 우리의 훌륭한 옛 친구가 황급히 자리를 뜰 때 그 눈에는 반짝임을, 그 가슴에는 벅참을 안겨 주었다."]], [["Sam had returned when Mr. Pickwick reached his own room, and was inspecting the arrangements that had been made for his comfort, with a kind of grim satisfaction which was very pleasant to look upon.", "픽윅 씨가 자기 방에 이르렀을 때 샘은 이미 돌아와 있었고, 주인의 편의를 위해 마련된 것들을 일종의 시무룩한 만족감을 띤 채 살피고 있었는데, 그 모습이 보기에 무척 즐거웠다."], ["Having a decided objection to his master’s being there at all, Mr. Weller appeared to consider it a high moral duty not to appear too much pleased with anything that was done, said, suggested, or proposed.", "주인이 애초에 이런 곳에 있는 것 자체에 단호히 반대하던 웰러 씨는, 행해지거나 말해지거나 제안되거나 권해진 그 무엇에도 너무 기뻐하는 기색을 보이지 않는 것이 자신의 높은 도덕적 의무라고 여기는 모양이었다."]], [["‘Well, Sam,’ said Mr.", "\"그래, 샘.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Well, sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"예, 선생님.\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘Pretty comfortable now, eh, Sam?’", "\"이만하면 꽤 편안하지, 응, 샘?\""]], [["‘Pretty vell, sir,’ responded Sam, looking round him in a disparaging manner.", "\"꽤 괜찮습니다요, 선생님.\" 샘이 깔보는 듯한 태도로 주위를 둘러보며 응답했다."]], [["‘Have you seen Mr. Tupman and our other friends?’", "\"터프먼 씨와 다른 친구들은 만났나?\""]], [["‘Yes, I have seen ‘em, sir, and they’re a-comin’ to-morrow, and wos wery much surprised to hear they warn’t to come to-day,’ replied Sam.", "\"예, 만나 뵈었습니다, 선생님. 내일 오시겠답니다. 오늘은 못 오신다는 말을 듣고 다들 무척 놀라시던뎁쇼.\" 샘이 대답했다."]], [["‘You have brought the things I wanted?’", "\"내가 부탁한 물건들은 가져왔고?\""]], [["Mr. Weller in reply pointed to various packages which he had arranged, as neatly as he could, in a corner of the room.", "웰러 씨는 대답 대신, 자기가 되도록 가지런히 방 한구석에 정돈해 둔 여러 꾸러미를 가리켰다."]], [["‘Very well, Sam,’ said Mr.", "\"좋아, 샘.\""], ["Pickwick, after a little hesitation; ‘listen to what I am going to say, Sam.’", "픽윅 씨가 잠시 머뭇거린 뒤에 말했다. \"내가 하려는 말을 잘 들어 보게, 샘.\""]], [["‘Cert’nly, Sir,’ rejoined Mr.", "\"물론입죠, 선생님.\" 웰러 씨가 응수했다."], ["Weller; ‘fire away, Sir.’", "\"어서 말씀하십시오, 선생님.\""]], [["‘I have felt from the first, Sam,’ said Mr.", "\"나는 처음부터 느껴 왔네, 샘.\""], ["Pickwick, with much solemnity, ‘that this is not the place to bring a young man to.’", "픽윅 씨가 자못 엄숙하게 말했다. \"여기가 젊은이를 데려올 곳이 못 된다고 말일세.\""]], [["‘Nor an old ‘un neither, Sir,’ observed Mr.", "\"늙은이를 데려올 곳도 못 됩니다요, 선생님.\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 한마디 했다."]], [["‘You’re quite right, Sam,’ said Mr.", "\"네 말이 맞다, 샘.\""], ["Pickwick; ‘but old men may come here through their own heedlessness and unsuspicion, and young men may be brought here by the selfishness of those they serve.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"하지만 늙은이야 제 부주의와 방심 탓에 여기 올 수 있다손 쳐도, 젊은이는 제가 섬기는 자들의 이기심 때문에 여기 끌려오는 것일세."], ["It is better for those young men, in every point of view, that they should not remain here.", "어느 모로 보나, 그런 젊은이들은 여기 머물지 않는 편이 낫네."], ["Do you understand me, Sam?’", "내 말 알아듣겠나, 샘?\""]], [["‘Vy no, Sir, I do not,’ replied Mr.", "\"아뇨, 선생님, 모르겠습니다요.\""], ["Weller doggedly.", "웰러 씨가 고집스럽게 대답했다."]], [["‘Try, Sam,’ said Mr.", "\"잘 생각해 보게, 샘.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Vell, sir,’ rejoined Sam, after a short pause, ‘I think I see your drift; and if I do see your drift, it’s my ‘pinion that you’re a-comin’ it a great deal too strong, as the mail-coachman said to the snowstorm, ven it overtook him.’", "\"음, 선생님.\" 샘이 잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤 응수했다. \"선생님 속뜻을 알 것도 같습니다요. 그리고 제가 그 속뜻을 제대로 헤아린 거라면, 제 생각엔 선생님이 너무 심하게 밀어붙이시는 겁니다요. 우편 마차 마부가 눈보라에 따라잡혔을 때 그 눈보라더러 한 말처럼 말입죠.\""]], [["‘I see you comprehend me, Sam,’ said Mr.", "\"내 말을 알아들었구나, 샘.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["‘Independently of my wish that you should not be idling about a place like this, for years to come, I feel that for a debtor in the Fleet to be attended by his manservant is a monstrous absurdity.", "\"네가 앞으로 여러 해 동안 이런 곳에서 빈둥거리지 않았으면 하는 내 바람은 차치하더라도, 플리트의 채무자가 제 하인의 시중을 받는다는 것은 터무니없이 어이없는 일이라고 느낀다네. 샘,\""], ["Sam,’ said Mr.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick, ‘for a time you must leave me.’", "\"한동안 너는 나를 떠나 있어야겠다.\""]], [["‘Oh, for a time, eh, sir?’ rejoined Mr.", "\"오, 한동안이라고요, 선생님?\""], ["Weller rather sarcastically.", "웰러 씨가 다소 빈정거리듯 응수했다."]], [["‘Yes, for the time that I remain here,’ said Mr.", "\"그래, 내가 여기 있는 동안만일세.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick. ‘Your wages I shall continue to pay.", "\"네 품삯은 계속 줄 것이야."], ["Any one of my three friends will be happy to take you, were it only out of respect to me.", "내 세 친구 가운데 누구라도, 나를 봐서라도 기꺼이 너를 거둘 게다."], ["And if I ever do leave this place, Sam,’ added Mr.", "그리고 내가 만에 하나 이곳을 벗어나게 된다면, 샘,\""], ["Pickwick, with assumed cheerfulness--‘if I do, I pledge you my word that you shall return to me instantly.’", "픽윅 씨가 짐짓 명랑한 척하며 덧붙였다. \"그렇게 되면, 너는 즉시 내게 돌아오게 되리라고 내 약속하마.\""]], [["‘Now I’ll tell you wot it is, Sir,’ said Mr.", "\"그럼 제가 말씀드리겠습니다, 선생님.\""], ["Weller, in a grave and solemn voice.", "웰러 씨가 진지하고 엄숙한 목소리로 말했다."], ["‘This here sort o’ thing won’t do at all, so don’t let’s hear no more about it.’", "\"이런 식은 전혀 안 될 일입니다요. 그러니 다시는 그런 말씀 듣지 맙시다요.\""]], [["I am serious, and resolved, Sam,’ said Mr.", "\"나는 진심이고, 또 굳게 마음먹었다, 샘.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘You air, air you, sir?’ inquired Mr.", "\"그러시단 말이지요, 선생님?\" 웰러 씨가 단호히 물었다."], ["Weller firmly.", "\"아주 좋습니다요, 선생님."], ["‘Wery good, Sir; then so am I.’", "그럼 저도 그렇습니다요.\""]], [["Thus speaking, Mr. Weller fixed his hat on his head with great precision, and abruptly left the room.", "이렇게 말하고 나서 웰러 씨는 매우 정확하게 모자를 머리에 눌러쓰더니, 불쑥 방을 나가 버렸다."]], [["‘Sam!’ cried Mr.", "\"샘!\""], ["Pickwick, calling after him, ‘Sam!", "픽윅 씨가 그를 뒤따라 부르며 외쳤다. \"샘!"], ["Here!’", "이리 오너라!\""]], [["But the long gallery ceased to re-echo the sound of footsteps.", "그러나 긴 복도는 더 이상 발소리를 되울리지 않았다."], ["Sam Weller was gone.", "샘 웰러는 가 버린 것이다."]], [["# CHAPTER XLIII. SHOWING HOW MR. SAMUEL WELLER GOT INTO DIFFICULTIES", "# CHAPTER XLIII. SHOWING HOW MR. SAMUEL WELLER GOT INTO DIFFICULTIES"]], [["In a lofty room, ill-lighted and worse ventilated, situated in Portugal Street, Lincoln’s Inn Fields, there sit nearly the whole year round, one, two, three, or four gentlemen in wigs, as the case may be, with little writing-desks before them, constructed after the fashion of those used by the judges of the land, barring the French polish.", "링컨스 인 필즈의 포르투갈 거리에 자리한, 높직하고 조명은 어두우며 환기는 그보다 더 나쁜 어느 방에는, 거의 한 해 내내 가발을 쓴 신사 한 명, 두 명, 세 명, 또는 네 명이—그때그때 형편에 따라—앉아 있는데, 그들 앞에는 이 나라 판사들이 쓰는 것을 본떠 만든 작은 필기용 책상이 놓여 있다. 다만 프랑스식 광택칠만은 빠져 있다."], ["There is a box of barristers on their right hand; there is an enclosure of insolvent debtors on their left; and there is an inclined plane of most especially dirty faces in their front.", "그들의 오른편에는 법정 변호사들이 들어앉은 칸막이가 있고, 왼편에는 파산 채무자들이 가둬진 울이 있으며, 정면에는 유독 지저분한 얼굴들이 비탈을 이루며 늘어서 있다."], ["These gentlemen are the Commissioners of the Insolvent Court, and the place in which they sit, is the Insolvent Court itself.", "이 신사들이 바로 파산법원의 위원들이고, 그들이 앉아 있는 곳이 바로 파산법원 그 자체다."]], [["It is, and has been, time out of mind, the remarkable fate of this court to be, somehow or other, held and understood, by the general consent of all the destitute shabby-genteel people in London, as their common resort, and place of daily refuge.", "이 법원에는 기억할 수 없을 만큼 먼 옛날부터 한 가지 기묘한 운명이 따라붙어 있으니, 어찌 된 영문인지 런던의 가난하고 행색은 초라하나 신사연하는 모든 자들의 공통된 집합소이자 매일의 피난처로, 그들 모두의 묵계에 따라 통하고 또 그렇게 받아들여진다는 것이다."], ["It is always full.", "이곳은 늘 만원이다."], ["The steams of beer and spirits perpetually ascend to the ceiling, and, being condensed by the heat, roll down the walls like rain; there are more old suits of clothes in it at one time, than will be offered for sale in all Houndsditch in a twelvemonth; more unwashed skins and grizzly beards than all the pumps and shaving-shops between Tyburn and Whitechapel could render decent, between sunrise and sunset.", "맥주와 독주의 김이 끊임없이 천장으로 올라가고, 열기에 응결되어 비처럼 벽을 타고 흘러내린다. 한 해 동안 하운즈디치 전체에서 팔리려고 내놓일 헌 옷보다도 더 많은 헌 옷가지가 이곳에는 한꺼번에 들어차 있고, 해 뜰 녘부터 해 질 녘까지 타이번과 화이트채플 사이의 모든 펌프와 이발소를 동원해도 말끔히 단장할 수 없을 만큼 많은 씻지 않은 살갗과 희끗한 수염이 이곳에 모여 있다."]], [["It must not be supposed that any of these people have the least shadow of business in, or the remotest connection with, the place they so indefatigably attend.", "이 사람들 가운데 누구라도 그토록 부지런히 드나드는 그곳에 일말의 볼일이 있거나 조금이라도 관계가 있으리라고 생각해서는 안 된다."], ["If they had, it would be no matter of surprise, and the singularity of the thing would cease.", "만일 그런 게 있었다면 놀랄 일도 아니고, 그 기이함도 사라졌을 것이다."], ["Some of them sleep during the greater part of the sitting; others carry small portable dinners wrapped in pocket-handkerchiefs or sticking out of their worn-out pockets, and munch and listen with equal relish; but no one among them was ever known to have the slightest personal interest in any case that was ever brought forward.", "그들 중 어떤 이는 개정 시간 대부분을 잠으로 보내고, 또 어떤 이는 손수건에 싼 작은 도시락을 들고 다니거나 다 해진 주머니에 삐죽 꽂은 채로, 오물오물 먹으면서 똑같은 흥미로 귀를 기울인다. 그러나 그들 가운데 누구도 일찍이 상정된 어떤 사건에 조금이라도 개인적 이해관계가 있었던 적은 없다."], ["Whatever they do, there they sit from the first moment to the last.", "무엇을 하든 그들은 처음 순간부터 마지막 순간까지 그곳에 앉아 있다."], ["When it is heavy, rainy weather, they all come in, wet through; and at such times the vapours of the court are like those of a fungus-pit.", "날이 궂고 비가 내리면 그들은 모두 흠뻑 젖은 채로 들어오는데, 그럴 때면 법정 안의 수증기는 버섯 재배 구덩이의 그것과 다름없다."]], [["A casual visitor might suppose this place to be a temple dedicated to the Genius of Seediness.", "우연히 들른 방문객이라면 이곳이 초라함의 수호신에게 바쳐진 신전이라 여길 법하다."], ["There is not a messenger or process-server attached to it, who wears a coat that was made for him; not a tolerably fresh, or wholesome-looking man in the whole establishment, except a little white-headed apple-faced tipstaff, and even he, like an ill- conditioned cherry preserved in brandy, seems to have artificially dried and withered up into a state of preservation to which he can lay no natural claim.", "이곳에 딸린 전령이나 송달리 가운데 자기 몸에 맞게 지은 외투를 걸친 자는 하나도 없고, 그나마 봐줄 만큼 생기 있거나 건강해 보이는 사람이라곤 이 건물 전체를 통틀어 머리가 희고 얼굴이 사과 같은 작은 법정 정리 하나뿐이다. 그러나 그조차도 브랜디에 절인 상태 나쁜 버찌처럼, 본래 그에게 주장할 권리가 없는 보존 상태에 이르도록 인위적으로 바싹 말라 쪼그라든 듯 보인다."], ["The very barristers’ wigs are ill-powdered, and their curls lack crispness.", "법정 변호사들의 가발마저 분이 형편없이 발려 있고, 그 곱슬머리에는 빳빳함이 없다."]], [["But the attorneys, who sit at a large bare table below the commissioners, are, after all, the greatest curiosities.", "그러나 위원들 아래쪽의 크고 휑한 탁자에 앉은 사무 변호사들이야말로 결국 가장 큰 구경거리다."], ["The professional establishment of the more opulent of these gentlemen, consists of a blue bag and a boy; generally a youth of the Jewish persuasion.", "이 신사들 중에서도 더 넉넉한 자의 직업적 살림살이라야 푸른 가방 하나와 사환 아이 하나로 이루어지는데, 그 아이는 대개 유대교를 믿는 소년이다."], ["They have no fixed offices, their legal business being transacted in the parlours of public-houses, or the yards of prisons, whither they repair in crowds, and canvass for customers after the manner of omnibus cads.", "그들에게는 고정된 사무실이 없으니, 법률 업무는 술집의 응접실이나 감옥의 마당에서 처리되며, 그들은 떼를 지어 그리로 몰려가 합승마차의 호객꾼처럼 손님을 끌어모은다."], ["They are of a greasy and mildewed appearance; and if they can be said to have any vices at all, perhaps drinking and cheating are the most conspicuous among them.", "그들은 기름지고 곰팡내 나는 행색을 하고 있는데, 그들에게 무슨 악덕이라 할 만한 것이 있다면 아마 술 마시는 일과 속이는 일이 그 가운데 가장 두드러질 것이다."], ["Their residences are usually on the outskirts of ‘the Rules,’ chiefly lying within a circle of one mile from the obelisk in St.", "그들의 거처는 대개 '거주 허가 구역' 변두리에 있으며, 주로 세인트 조지스 필즈의 오벨리스크에서 반경 1마일 안에 자리 잡고 있다."], ["George’s Fields. Their looks are not prepossessing, and their manners are peculiar.", "그들의 인상은 호감을 주지 못하고, 그 거동은 별나다."]], [["Mr. Solomon Pell, one of this learned body, was a fat, flabby, pale man, in a surtout which looked green one minute, and brown the next, with a velvet collar of the same chameleon tints.", "이 유식한 무리의 한 사람인 솔로몬 펠 씨는 살집이 무르고 축 늘어진 창백한 사내로, 한순간은 초록빛으로 보이다가 다음 순간엔 갈색으로 보이는 외투를 걸쳤는데, 그 벨벳 깃 역시 똑같은 카멜레온 색조를 띠고 있었다."], ["His forehead was narrow, his face wide, his head large, and his nose all on one side, as if Nature, indignant with the propensities she observed in him in his birth, had given it an angry tweak which it had never recovered.", "그의 이마는 좁고 얼굴은 넓으며 머리는 컸고, 코는 온통 한쪽으로 쏠려 있어, 마치 자연이 그가 태어날 때 그에게서 본 성벽에 격분하여 코를 화김에 한번 비틀어 놓았는데 그것이 영영 회복되지 못한 것 같았다."], ["Being short-necked and asthmatic, however, he respired principally through this feature; so, perhaps, what it wanted in ornament, it made up in usefulness.", "그러나 목이 짧고 천식기가 있던 터라 그는 주로 이 기관으로 숨을 쉬었으니, 어쩌면 그 코가 꾸밈에서 모자란 것을 쓸모로 메운 셈인지도 모른다."]], [["‘I’m sure to bring him through it,’ said Mr.", "\"제가 틀림없이 그분을 무사히 통과시켜 드리지요.\""], ["Pell.", "펠 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Are you, though?’ replied the person to whom the assurance was pledged.", "\"정말 그렇겠소?\" 그 장담을 받은 사람이 대꾸했다."]], [["‘Certain sure,’ replied Pell; ‘but if he’d gone to any irregular practitioner, mind you, I wouldn’t have answered for the consequences.’", "\"확실히 틀림없습니다.\" 펠이 대답했다. \"하지만 말이죠, 그분이 무자격 변호사한테 갔더라면, 그 결과는 제가 책임 못 졌을 겝니다.\""]], [["‘Ah!’ said the other, with open mouth.", "\"아!\" 상대가 입을 벌린 채 말했다."]], [["‘No, that I wouldn’t,’ said Mr.", "\"그럼요, 못 책임지고말고요.\""], ["Pell; and he pursed up his lips, frowned, and shook his head mysteriously.", "펠 씨가 말했다. 그러고는 입술을 오므리고 눈살을 찌푸리며 알 수 없다는 듯 고개를 저었다."]], [["Now, the place where this discourse occurred was the public-house just opposite to the Insolvent Court; and the person with whom it was held was no other than the elder Mr.", "그런데 이 대화가 오간 곳은 바로 파산법원 맞은편에 있는 술집이었고, 대화 상대는 다름 아닌 웰러 영감이었다."], ["Weller, who had come there, to comfort and console a friend, whose petition to be discharged under the act, was to be that day heard, and whose attorney he was at that moment consulting.", "그는 법령에 따라 면책 처분을 받게 해 달라는 청원이 그날 심리될 예정인 친구를 위로하고 달래러 그곳에 왔으며, 마침 그 친구의 사무 변호사와 상의하고 있던 참이었다."]], [["‘And vere is George?’ inquired the old gentleman.", "\"그래, 조지는 어디 있소?\" 노신사가 물었다."]], [["Mr. Pell jerked his head in the direction of a back parlour, whither Mr.", "펠 씨가 뒤편 응접실 쪽으로 고갯짓을 했고, 웰러 씨가 곧장 그리로 가자, 직업적 형제 대여섯 명이 그의 도착을 반긴다는 표시로 더없이 따뜻하고 살가운 태도로 그를 맞았다."], ["Weller at once repairing, was immediately greeted in the warmest and most flattering manner by some half-dozen of his professional brethren, in token of their gratification at his arrival. The insolvent gentleman, who had contracted a speculative but imprudent passion for horsing long stages, which had led to his present embarrassments, looked extremely well, and was soothing the excitement of his feelings with shrimps and porter.", "장거리 역마차에 투기적이지만 경솔한 열정을 품었다가 지금의 곤경에 빠진 그 파산 신사는 안색이 무척 좋았고, 새우와 흑맥주로 들뜬 마음을 달래고 있었다."]], [["The salutation between Mr. Weller and his friends was strictly confined to the freemasonry of the craft; consisting of a jerking round of the right wrist, and a tossing of the little finger into the air at the same time.", "웰러 씨와 그 벗들 사이에 오간 인사는 그 업종 종사자들만의 비밀스러운 의식에 엄격히 국한된 것으로, 오른 손목을 홱 돌리는 동작과 동시에 새끼손가락을 공중으로 튕겨 올리는 것으로 이루어졌다."], ["We once knew two famous coachmen (they are dead now, poor fellows) who were twins, and between whom an unaffected and devoted attachment existed.", "우리는 일찍이 유명한 마부 둘을 알았는데(가엾게도 지금은 둘 다 세상을 떠났다) 그들은 쌍둥이로, 서로 간에 꾸밈없고 헌신적인 정이 오갔다."], ["They passed each other on the Dover road, every day, for twenty-four years, never exchanging any other greeting than this; and yet, when one died, the other pined away, and soon afterwards followed him!", "그들은 이십사 년 동안 매일같이 도버 가도에서 서로를 스쳐 지나갔으되 이 인사 말고는 어떤 다른 인사도 나누지 않았다. 그런데도 한 사람이 죽자 다른 한 사람은 시름시름 야위어 가더니 머지않아 그의 뒤를 따랐다!"]], [["‘Vell, George,’ said Mr.", "\"그래, 조지,\""], ["Weller senior, taking off his upper coat, and seating himself with his accustomed gravity.", "웰러 영감이 겉옷을 벗으며 늘 그렇듯 점잖게 자리에 앉으며 말했다. \"어떻게 됐나?"], ["‘How is it? All right behind, and full inside?’", "뒤는 다 괜찮고, 안은 꽉 찼나?\""]], [["‘All right, old feller,’ replied the embarrassed gentleman.", "\"다 괜찮네, 이 친구야.\" 곤경에 빠진 신사가 대답했다."]], [["‘Is the gray mare made over to anybody?’ inquired Mr.", "\"잿빛 암말은 누구한테 넘겼고?\""], ["Weller anxiously.", "웰러 씨가 근심스레 물었다."]], [["George nodded in the affirmative.", "조지가 그렇다고 고개를 끄덕였다."]], [["‘Vell, that’s all right,’ said Mr.", "\"그래, 그럼 됐네.\" 웰러 씨가 말했다."], ["Weller. ‘Coach taken care on, also?’", "\"마차도 잘 처리했고?\""]], [["‘Con-signed in a safe quarter,’ replied George, wringing the heads off half a dozen shrimps, and swallowing them without any more ado.", "\"안전한 데다 위탁했네.\" 조지가 새우 대여섯 마리의 머리를 비틀어 떼고는 더 군말 없이 삼키며 대답했다."]], [["‘Wery good, wery good,’ said Mr. Weller.", "\"아주 좋아, 아주 좋아.\""], ["‘Alvays see to the drag ven you go downhill.", "웰러 씨가 말했다. \"내리막길 갈 땐 늘 제동장치를 살피게."], ["Is the vay-bill all clear and straight for’erd?’", "운송장은 다 깔끔하고 떳떳하고?\""]], [["‘The schedule, sir,’ said Pell, guessing at Mr.", "\"진술 명세서 말씀이군요.\" 펠 씨가 웰러 씨의 뜻을 짐작하고 말했다."], ["Weller’s meaning, ‘the schedule is as plain and satisfactory as pen and ink can make it.’", "\"그 명세서는 펜과 잉크로 만들 수 있는 한 더없이 명료하고 흠잡을 데 없습니다.\""]], [["Mr. Weller nodded in a manner which bespoke his inward approval of these arrangements; and then, turning to Mr. Pell, said, pointing to his friend George--", "웰러 씨는 이 처리에 속으로 흡족해한다는 듯 고개를 끄덕이고는, 펠 씨를 향해 돌아서서 친구 조지를 가리키며 말했다."]], [["‘Ven do you take his cloths off?’", "\"이 친구 옷은 언제 벗기시오?\""]], [["‘Why,’ replied Mr.", "\"음,\" 펠 씨가 대답했다."], ["Pell, ‘he stands third on the opposed list, and I should think it would be his turn in about half an hour.", "\"그분은 이의 신청자 명단에서 세 번째에 있으니, 한 삼십 분쯤 지나면 차례가 올 듯합니다."], ["I told my clerk to come over and tell us when there was a chance.’", "제 사무원더러 차례가 올 만하면 건너와서 알려 달라고 일러 뒀습니다.\""]], [["Mr. Weller surveyed the attorney from head to foot with great admiration, and said emphatically--", "웰러 씨가 그 사무 변호사를 머리끝부터 발끝까지 크게 감탄하며 훑어보고는 힘주어 말했다."]], [["‘And what’ll you take, sir?’", "\"그래, 한잔 뭘로 드시겠소, 선생?\""]], [["‘Why, really,’ replied Mr.", "\"아니, 정말이지,\""], ["Pell, ‘you’re very--.", "펠 씨가 대답했다."], ["Upon my word and honour, I’m not in the habit of--.", "\"참으로 친절하시군요—. 맹세코 저는 평소에 그런 걸—."], ["It’s so very early in the morning, that, actually, I am almost--.", "아침나절이 워낙 일러서 사실 저는 거의—."], ["Well, you may bring me threepenn’orth of rum, my dear.’", "음, 럼주 삼 펜스어치만 가져다주오, 아가씨.\""]], [["The officiating damsel, who had anticipated the order before it was given, set the glass of spirits before Pell, and retired.", "주문이 떨어지기도 전에 미리 짐작했던 시중드는 처녀가 펠 앞에 독주 잔을 놓고는 물러갔다."]], [["‘Gentlemen,’ said Mr.", "\"여러분,\""], ["Pell, looking round upon the company, ‘success to your friend!", "펠 씨가 좌중을 둘러보며 말했다. \"여러분 친구의 성공을 위하여!"], ["I don’t like to boast, gentlemen; it’s not my way; but I can’t help saying, that, if your friend hadn’t been fortunate enough to fall into hands that--But I won’t say what I was going to say.", "자랑하기는 싫습니다, 여러분. 그건 제 방식이 아니지요."], ["Gentlemen, my service to you.’ Having emptied the glass in a twinkling, Mr.", "하지만 한마디 안 할 수가 없군요. 만일 여러분 친구가 운 좋게도 다음과 같은 손에 떨어지지 않았더라면—아니, 하려던 말은 안 하겠습니다."], ["Pell smacked his lips, and looked complacently round on the assembled coachmen, who evidently regarded him as a species of divinity.", "여러분, 여러분께 인사 올립니다.\" 눈 깜짝할 사이에 잔을 비운 펠 씨는 입맛을 다시며 둘러앉은 마부들을 흐뭇하게 둘러보았는데, 그들은 분명 그를 일종의 신적 존재로 여기고 있었다."]], [["‘Let me see,’ said the legal authority.", "\"가만있자,\" 그 법률 권위자가 말했다."], ["‘What was I a-saying, gentlemen?’", "\"내가 무슨 말을 하고 있었더라, 여러분?\""]], [["‘I think you was remarkin’ as you wouldn’t have no objection to another o’ the same, Sir,’ said Mr.", "\"제 생각엔 똑같은 걸로 한 잔 더 들어도 사양 안 하시겠다고 말씀하시던 참이었지요, 선생.\""], ["Weller, with grave facetiousness.", "웰러 씨가 점잖은 익살을 부리며 말했다."]], [["‘Ha, ha!’ laughed Mr. Pell.", "\"하하!\""], ["‘Not bad, not bad.", "펠 씨가 웃었다."], ["A professional man, too!", "\"나쁘지 않군, 나쁘지 않아."], ["At this time of the morning, it would be rather too good a--Well, I don’t know, my dear--you may do that again, if you please.", "게다가 전문직 종사자가 말이오! 이 아침나절에 그건 좀 지나치게 좋은—음, 글쎄, 아가씨—원한다면 다시 한 잔 가져다줘도 좋겠소."], ["Hem!’", "험!\""]], [["This last sound was a solemn and dignified cough, in which Mr. Pell, observing an indecent tendency to mirth in some of his auditors, considered it due to himself to indulge.", "이 마지막 소리는 엄숙하고 위엄 있는 헛기침이었으니, 펠 씨는 청중 가운데 몇몇에게서 점잖지 못하게 웃으려는 기미를 알아채고는, 스스로의 체면을 위해 그렇게 해 두는 것이 마땅하다고 여긴 것이었다."]], [["‘The late Lord Chancellor, gentlemen, was very fond of me,’ said Mr.", "\"고인이 되신 대법관께서는 여러분, 저를 무척 아끼셨지요.\""], ["Pell.", "펠 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘And wery creditable in him, too,’ interposed Mr.", "\"그것도 그분으로선 무척 갸륵한 일이었구려.\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 끼어들었다."]], [["‘Hear, hear,’ assented Mr.", "\"옳소, 옳소.\""], ["Pell’s client.", "펠 씨의 의뢰인이 맞장구쳤다."], ["‘Why shouldn’t he be?", "\"안 그럴 까닭이 없지요?\""]], [["‘Ah!", "\"아!"], ["Why, indeed!’ said a very red-faced man, who had said nothing yet, and who looked extremely unlikely to say anything more.", "그렇고말고요!\" 그때껏 한마디도 않던, 앞으로도 더 말할 것 같지 않아 보이는 얼굴이 무척 붉은 사내가 말했다."], ["‘Why shouldn’t he?’", "\"안 그럴 까닭이 어디 있겠소?\""]], [["A murmur of assent ran through the company.", "좌중에 동의의 웅성거림이 번졌다."]], [["‘I remember, gentlemen,’ said Mr.", "\"기억합니다, 여러분,\" 펠 씨가 말했다."], ["Pell, ‘dining with him on one occasion; there was only us two, but everything as splendid as if twenty people had been expected--the great seal on a dumb-waiter at his right hand, and a man in a bag-wig and suit of armour guarding the mace with a drawn sword and silk stockings--which is perpetually done, gentlemen, night and day; when he said, “Pell,”", "\"한번은 그분과 만찬을 함께했지요. 우리 둘뿐이었는데도 마치 스무 명이 올 것처럼 모든 게 호화로웠습니다—그분 오른편의 식기 받침대 위에는 옥새가 놓여 있고, 가발에 갑옷을 차려입은 자가 칼을 빼 들고 비단 양말을 신은 채 권표를 지키고 서 있었지요—여러분, 그건 밤낮없이 늘 그렇게 한답니다."], ["he said, “no false delicacy, Pell.", "그때 그분이 말씀하시길, '펠,' 하시더니, '괜한 겸양은 부리지 말게, 펠."], ["You’re a man of talent; you can get anybody through the Insolvent Court, Pell; and your country should be proud of you.”", "자넨 재능 있는 사람이야. 자넨 누구든 파산법원을 통과시킬 수 있어, 펠."], ["Those were his very words.", "자네 나라가 자네를 자랑스러워해야 마땅하네.' 바로 그 말씀이셨습니다."], ["“My Lord,”", "'대법관님,'"], ["I said, “you flatter me.”--“Pell,”", "제가 말했지요, '과찬이십니다.'—'펠,'"], ["he said, “if I do, I’m damned.”’", "그분이 말씀하셨습니다, '내가 과찬한다면 내 목을 내놓겠네.'\""]], [["‘Did he say that?’ inquired Mr.", "\"그분이 그렇게 말씀하셨소?\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘He did,’ replied Pell.", "\"그러셨지요.\" 펠이 대답했다."]], [["‘Vell, then,’ said Mr.", "\"그렇다면,\" 웰러 씨가 말했다."], ["Weller, ‘I say Parliament ought to ha’ took it up; and if he’d been a poor man, they would ha’ done it.’", "\"내 생각엔 의회가 그 일을 따졌어야 했소. 그분이 가난뱅이였다면 그렇게 했을 거요.\""]], [["‘But, my dear friend,’ argued Mr.", "\"하지만 여보게,\" 펠 씨가 항변했다."], ["Pell, ‘it was in confidence.’", "\"그건 비밀히 하신 말씀이었소.\""]], [["‘In what?’ said Mr.", "\"뭐라고요?\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘In confidence.’", "\"비밀히 하신 말씀이라고.\""]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["wery good,’ replied Mr.", "그럼 됐소이다.\""], ["Weller, after a little reflection.", "웰러 씨가 잠시 곰곰 생각하더니 대꾸했다."], ["‘If he damned hisself in confidence, o’ course that was another thing.’", "\"비밀히 자기 목을 걸겠다 하셨다면야 그건 또 다른 얘기지.\""]], [["‘Of course it was,’ said Mr.", "\"물론 그렇지요.\" 펠 씨가 말했다."], ["Pell. ‘The distinction’s obvious, you will perceive.’", "\"그 차이는 명백하니, 보시면 아실 겝니다.\""]], [["‘Alters the case entirely,’ said Mr.", "\"사정이 완전히 달라지는구려.\" 웰러 씨가 말했다."], ["Weller. ‘Go on, Sir.’", "\"계속하시오, 선생.\""]], [["No, I will not go on, Sir,’ said Mr.", "\"아니요, 더는 계속하지 않겠습니다, 선생.\""], ["Pell, in a low and serious tone.", "펠 씨가 낮고 진지한 어조로 말했다."], ["‘You have reminded me, Sir, that this conversation was private--private and confidential, gentlemen.", "\"선생께서는 이 대화가 사사로운 것이었음을—사사롭고 은밀한 것이었음을 일깨워 주셨습니다, 여러분."], ["Gentlemen, I am a professional man.", "여러분, 저는 전문직 종사자입니다."], ["It may be that I am a good deal looked up to, in my profession--it may be that I am not.", "어쩌면 제가 제 직업에서 상당히 존경받는 사람일 수도—아닐 수도 있겠지요."], ["Most people know. I say nothing.", "대개는 다들 압니다."], ["Observations have already been made, in this room, injurious to the reputation of my noble friend.", "저는 아무 말 않겠습니다. 이 방에서 이미 제 고귀한 벗의 명성을 해치는 발언들이 나왔습니다."], ["You will excuse me, gentlemen; I was imprudent.", "양해해 주시지요, 여러분. 제가 경솔했습니다."], ["I feel that I have no right to mention this matter without his concurrence.", "그분의 동의 없이 이 문제를 입에 올릴 권리가 제게 없음을 느낍니다."], ["Thank you, Sir; thank you.’ Thus delivering himself, Mr.", "고맙소, 선생. 고맙소이다.\""], ["Pell thrust his hands into his pockets, and, frowning grimly around, rattled three halfpence with terrible determination.", "이렇게 일장 연설을 마친 펠 씨는 두 손을 주머니에 찔러 넣고 험상궂게 좌중을 노려보며, 무시무시한 결의로 반 페니짜리 동전 세 닢을 짤랑거렸다."]], [["This virtuous resolution had scarcely been formed, when the boy and the blue bag, who were inseparable companions, rushed violently into the room, and said (at least the boy did, for the blue bag took no part in the announcement) that the case was coming on directly.", "이 갸륵한 결심이 채 다잡아지기도 전에, 떼려야 뗄 수 없는 동행인 사환 아이와 푸른 가방이 방 안으로 우당탕 뛰어들더니, 사건이 곧 시작된다고 알렸다(적어도 아이가 그랬다는 말이지, 푸른 가방은 그 통보에 한몫하지 않았으니까)."], ["The intelligence was no sooner received than the whole party hurried across the street, and began to fight their way into court--a preparatory ceremony, which has been calculated to occupy, in ordinary cases, from twenty-five minutes to thirty.", "이 소식이 전해지자마자 일행 전체가 길을 건너 서둘러 가서 법정 안으로 비집고 들어가려 다투기 시작했는데, 이 예비 의식은 보통의 경우 이십오 분에서 삼십 분쯤 걸리는 것으로 따져진다."]], [["Mr. Weller, being stout, cast himself at once into the crowd, with the desperate hope of ultimately turning up in some place which would suit him.", "웰러 씨는 몸집이 튼실한지라 단번에 군중 속으로 몸을 던졌으니, 결국엔 자기에게 알맞은 어느 자리쯤에 떠오르리라는 필사적인 기대를 품고서였다."], ["His success was not quite equal to his expectations; for having neglected to take his hat off, it was knocked over his eyes by some unseen person, upon whose toes he had alighted with considerable force.", "그러나 그 성공은 그의 기대에 그리 미치지 못했으니, 모자 벗는 것을 잊은 탓에, 그가 적잖은 힘으로 발을 디뎌 밟아 버린 보이지 않는 어떤 자가 그의 모자를 눈 위까지 푹 내려쳐 버린 것이었다."], ["Apparently this individual regretted his impetuosity immediately afterwards, for, muttering an indistinct exclamation of surprise, he dragged the old man out into the hall, and, after a violent struggle, released his head and face.", "그자는 곧바로 자기 성급함을 후회했던 모양인지, 알아들을 수 없는 놀람의 외마디를 중얼거리며 노인을 복도로 끌어내더니, 한바탕 격렬한 씨름 끝에 그의 머리와 얼굴을 자유롭게 풀어 주었다."]], [["‘Samivel!’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"새뮤얼!\""], ["Weller, when he was thus enabled to behold his rescuer.", "웰러 씨가 이렇게 하여 자기 구원자를 보게 되자 외쳤다. 샘이 고개를 끄덕였다."]], [["Sam nodded. ‘You’re a dutiful and affectionate little boy, you are, ain’t you,’ said Mr.", "\"넌 효성스럽고 다정한 꼬맹이로구나, 안 그러냐,\""], ["Weller, ‘to come a-bonnetin’ your father in his old age?’", "웰러 씨가 말했다. \"늘그막에 든 아비 모자를 푹 눌러 씌우러 오다니 말이다.\""]], [["‘How should I know who you wos?’ responded the son.", "\"내가 누군지 어떻게 알았겠소?\""], ["‘Do you s’pose I wos to tell you by the weight o’ your foot?’", "아들이 대꾸했다. \"발 무게로 아버지를 알아보길 바랐소?\""]], [["‘Vell, that’s wery true, Sammy,’ replied Mr.", "\"음, 그건 참 맞는 말이다, 새미.\""], ["Weller, mollified at once; ‘but wot are you a-doin’ on here?", "웰러 씨가 단번에 누그러져 대답했다. \"한데 여긴 무슨 일로 왔느냐?"], ["Your gov’nor can’t do no good here, Sammy.", "네 주인 양반은 여기서 아무 득 볼 게 없다, 새미."], ["They won’t pass that werdick, they won’t pass it, Sammy.’ And Mr.", "저들은 그 평결을 통과시키지 않을 게야, 안 시킬 게다, 새미.\""], ["Weller shook his head with legal solemnity.", "그러고는 웰러 씨가 법정 같은 엄숙함으로 고개를 저었다."]], [["‘Wot a perwerse old file it is!’ exclaimed Sam, ‘always a-goin’ on about werdicks and alleybis and that.", "\"참 별난 영감탱이네!\" 샘이 소리쳤다."], ["Who said anything about the werdick?’", "\"맨날 평결이니 알리바이니 그딴 소리만 늘어놓고. 누가 평결 얘기를 했수?\""]], [["Mr. Weller made no reply, but once more shook his head most learnedly.", "웰러 씨는 아무 대꾸도 않고 다시 한번 더없이 유식한 척 고개를 저었다."]], [["‘Leave off rattlin’ that ‘ere nob o’ yourn, if you don’t want it to come off the springs altogether,’ said Sam impatiently, ‘and behave reasonable.", "\"그 골통일랑 짤랑대는 거 그만두쇼, 통째로 용수철에서 빠지길 바라지 않거든.\" 샘이 참다못해 말했다."], ["I vent all the vay down to the Markis o’ Granby, arter you, last night.’", "\"그리고 사리에 맞게 처신 좀 하쇼. 어젯밤 아버지 찾으러 마퀴스 오브 그랜비까지 내처 갔다 왔수.\""]], [["‘Did you see the Marchioness o’ Granby, Sammy?’ inquired Mr.", "\"마쇼니스 오브 그랜비는 만나 봤느냐, 새미?\""], ["Weller, with a sigh.", "웰러 씨가 한숨을 쉬며 물었다."]], [["‘Yes, I did,’ replied Sam.", "\"네, 만나 봤수.\" 샘이 대답했다."]], [["‘How wos the dear creetur a-lookin’?’", "\"그 가여운 사람은 어떤 낯빛이더냐?\""]], [["‘Wery queer,’ said Sam.", "\"아주 이상합디다.\""], ["‘I think she’s a-injurin’ herself gradivally vith too much o’ that ‘ere pine-apple rum, and other strong medicines of the same natur.’", "샘이 말했다. \"내 생각엔 그 파인애플 럼주랑 비슷한 독한 약들을 너무 들이켜서 차츰 제 몸을 해치고 있는 것 같습디다.\""]], [["‘You don’t mean that, Sammy?’ said the senior earnestly.", "\"설마 그런 건 아니겠지, 새미?\" 영감이 진지하게 말했다."]], [["‘I do, indeed,’ replied the junior.", "\"정말이라니까요.\" 아들이 대답했다."]], [["Mr. Weller seized his son’s hand, clasped it, and let it fall.", "웰러 씨가 아들의 손을 잡고 꽉 쥐었다가 떨어뜨렸다."], ["There was an expression on his countenance in doing so--not of dismay or apprehension, but partaking more of the sweet and gentle character of hope.", "그러는 그의 얼굴에는 어떤 표정이 어렸으니—낙담이나 우려가 아니라, 오히려 희망의 달콤하고 부드러운 빛깔에 더 가까운 것이었다."], ["A gleam of resignation, and even of cheerfulness, passed over his face too, as he slowly said, ‘I ain’t quite certain, Sammy; I wouldn’t like to say I wos altogether positive, in case of any subsekent disappointment, but I rayther think, my boy, I rayther think, that the shepherd’s got the liver complaint!’", "체념의, 아니 심지어 명랑함의 어렴풋한 빛까지도 그의 얼굴을 스쳐 지나갔으니, 그가 천천히 입을 떼며 말하길, \"확실친 않다, 새미. 나중에 행여 실망할까 싶어 아주 단정한다고는 말 못 하겠다만, 그래도 내 생각엔, 얘야, 내 생각엔, 그 목자 양반이 간에 병이 든 것 같구나!\""]], [["‘Does he look bad?’ inquired Sam.", "\"안색이 나쁘던가요?\" 샘이 물었다."]], [["‘He’s uncommon pale,’ replied his father, ‘’cept about the nose, which is redder than ever.", "\"유난히 핼쑥하더라.\" 아비가 대답했다."], ["His appetite is wery so-so, but he imbibes wonderful.’", "\"코빼기만 빼고 말이다, 그건 그 어느 때보다 더 붉었지. 입맛은 그저 그렇다만, 들이켜는 건 기막히게 잘하더라.\""]], [["Some thoughts of the rum appeared to obtrude themselves on Mr.", "이렇게 말하면서 럼주 생각이 웰러 씨의 마음에 끼어든 모양이었다."], ["Weller’s mind, as he said this; for he looked gloomy and thoughtful; but he very shortly recovered, as was testified by a perfect alphabet of winks, in which he was only wont to indulge when particularly pleased.", "그가 침울하고 골똘한 표정을 지었으니 말이다. 그러나 그는 이내 본정신을 되찾았으니, 유달리 흡족할 때만 부리곤 하는 온갖 종류의 눈짓을 완벽한 알파벳처럼 늘어놓은 것이 그 증거였다."]], [["‘Vell, now,’ said Sam, ‘about my affair.", "\"자, 그럼,\" 샘이 말했다."], ["Just open them ears o’ yourn, and don’t say nothin’ till I’ve done.’ With this preface, Sam related, as succinctly as he could, the last memorable conversation he had had with Mr.", "\"내 일 얘기로 갑시다. 그 귀나 좀 활짝 열고, 내 말 끝날 때까지 아무 말 마쇼.\""], ["Pickwick.", "이런 서두를 떼고서 샘은 픽윅 씨와 마지막으로 나눈 기억할 만한 대화를 되도록 간추려 들려주었다."]], [["‘Stop there by himself, poor creetur!’ exclaimed the elder Mr.", "\"혼자 거기 남으시겠다니, 가여운 양반!\" 웰러 영감이 외쳤다."], ["Weller, ‘without nobody to take his part!", "\"편들어 줄 사람 하나 없이!"], ["It can’t be done, Samivel, it can’t be done.’", "그건 안 될 일이다, 새뮤얼, 안 될 일이야.\""]], [["‘O’ course it can’t,’ asserted Sam: ‘I know’d that, afore I came.’", "\"당연히 안 되지요.\" 샘이 단언했다. \"오기 전부터 그건 알고 있었수.\""]], [["Why, they’ll eat him up alive, Sammy,’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"이런, 저들이 그 양반을 산 채로 잡아먹을 게다, 새미.\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 외쳤다."]], [["Sam nodded his concurrence in the opinion.", "샘은 그 의견에 동의한다는 듯 고개를 끄덕였다."]], [["‘He goes in rayther raw, Sammy,’ said Mr.", "\"그 양반은 좀 설익은 채로 들어가시는 거다, 새미,\""], ["Weller metaphorically, ‘and he’ll come out, done so ex-ceedin’ brown, that his most formiliar friends won’t know him.", "웰러 씨가 비유적으로 말했다. \"그리고 나올 땐 어찌나 바싹 익어 나오시는지, 가장 친한 벗들도 못 알아볼 게다."], ["Roast pigeon’s nothin’ to it, Sammy.’", "비둘기 구이쯤은 그에 비하면 아무것도 아니지, 새미.\""]], [["Again Sam Weller nodded.", "샘 웰러가 다시 고개를 끄덕였다."]], [["‘It oughtn’t to be, Samivel,’ said Mr.", "\"그래선 안 된다, 새뮤얼.\""], ["Weller gravely.", "웰러 씨가 엄숙하게 말했다."]], [["‘It mustn’t be,’ said Sam.", "\"그래서야 안 되지요.\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["‘Cert’nly not,’ said Mr.", "\"암, 안 되고말고.\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Vell now,’ said Sam, ‘you’ve been a-prophecyin’ away, wery fine, like a red-faced Nixon, as the sixpenny books gives picters on.’", "\"자, 그럼,\" 샘이 말했다. \"아버진 마치 육 펜스짜리 책에 그림으로 나오는 그 얼굴 붉은 닉슨처럼 그럴듯하게 예언만 늘어놓고 있잖수.\""]], [["‘Who wos he, Sammy?’ inquired Mr.", "\"그게 누구냐, 새미?\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘Never mind who he was,’ retorted Sam; ‘he warn’t a coachman; that’s enough for you.’", "\"누군진 신경 쓰지 마쇼.\" 샘이 받아쳤다. \"마부는 아니었으니, 그거면 아버진 충분하지.\""]], [["I know’d a ostler o’ that name,’ said Mr.", "\"그 이름 가진 마구간지기는 하나 알았었다만.\""], ["Weller, musing.", "웰러 씨가 곰곰 생각하며 말했다."]], [["‘It warn’t him,’ said Sam.", "\"그 사람 아니우.\""], ["‘This here gen’l’m’n was a prophet.’", "샘이 말했다. \"이 양반은 예언자였수.\""]], [["‘Wot’s a prophet?’ inquired Mr.", "\"예언자가 뭐냐?\""], ["Weller, looking sternly on his son.", "웰러 씨가 아들을 매섭게 바라보며 물었다."]], [["‘Wy, a man as tells what’s a-goin’ to happen,’ replied Sam.", "\"앞으로 무슨 일이 일어날지 말해 주는 사람이지요.\" 샘이 대답했다."]], [["‘I wish I’d know’d him, Sammy,’ said Mr. Weller.", "\"그 사람을 알았더라면 좋았을걸, 새미.\""], ["‘P’raps he might ha’ throw’d a small light on that ‘ere liver complaint as we wos a-speakin’ on, just now.", "웰러 씨가 말했다. \"아까 우리가 얘기하던 그 간 병에 대해 작게나마 빛을 비춰 줬을지도 모르잖느냐."], ["Hows’ever, if he’s dead, and ain’t left the bisness to nobody, there’s an end on it.", "하기야, 그 사람이 죽었고 그 장사를 아무한테도 안 물려줬다면야, 그걸로 끝이지."], ["Go on, Sammy,’ said Mr.", "계속해라, 새미.\""], ["Weller, with a sigh.", "웰러 씨가 한숨을 쉬며 말했다."]], [["‘Well,’ said Sam, ‘you’ve been a-prophecyin’ avay about wot’ll happen to the gov’ner if he’s left alone.", "\"좋수,\" 샘이 말했다."], ["Don’t you see any way o’ takin’ care on him?’", "\"아버진 주인 양반이 혼자 남으면 무슨 일이 닥칠지 그럴듯하게 예언만 늘어놨잖수. 그분을 돌볼 무슨 방도가 안 보이우?\""]], [["‘No, I don’t, Sammy,’ said Mr.", "\"안 보인다, 새미.\""], ["Weller, with a reflective visage.", "웰러 씨가 골똘한 낯으로 말했다."]], [["‘No vay at all?’ inquired Sam.", "\"전혀 방도가 없수?\" 샘이 물었다."]], [["‘No vay,’ said Mr.", "\"방도가 없어.\" 웰러 씨가 말했다."], ["Weller, ‘unless’--and a gleam of intelligence lighted up his countenance as he sank his voice to a whisper, and applied his mouth to the ear of his offspring--‘unless it is getting him out in a turn-up bedstead, unbeknown to the turnkeys, Sammy, or dressin’ him up like a old ‘ooman vith a green wail.’", "\"다만—\" 그러더니 슬기의 빛이 그의 얼굴을 환히 밝혔으니, 그는 목소리를 속삭임으로 낮추고 입을 제 자식의 귀에 갖다 대며—\"다만, 간수들 몰래 접이침대에 넣어서 그 양반을 빼내거나, 초록 너울 두른 노파처럼 변장시키는 거라면 또 모르지, 새미.\""]], [["Sam Weller received both of these suggestions with unexpected contempt, and again propounded his question.", "샘 웰러는 이 두 제안을 다 뜻밖일 만큼 경멸하며 받아들이고는, 다시 한번 자기 질문을 던졌다."]], [["‘No,’ said the old gentleman; ‘if he von’t let you stop there, I see no vay at all.", "\"아니다.\" 노신사가 말했다."], ["It’s no thoroughfare, Sammy, no thoroughfare.’", "\"그 양반이 널 거기 남게 하지 않는다면, 도무지 방도가 안 보인다. 막다른 길이야, 새미, 막다른 길.\""]], [["‘Well, then, I’ll tell you wot it is,’ said Sam, ‘I’ll trouble you for the loan of five-and-twenty pound.’", "\"그럼, 좋수, 내가 말하리다.\" 샘이 말했다. \"스물다섯 파운드만 빌려 주쇼.\""]], [["‘Wot good’ll that do?’ inquired Mr.", "\"그게 무슨 소용이냐?\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘Never mind,’ replied Sam.", "\"신경 쓰지 마쇼.\" 샘이 대답했다."], ["‘P’raps you may ask for it five minits arterwards; p’raps I may say I von’t pay, and cut up rough.", "\"오 분쯤 지나서 아버지가 그 돈 내놓으라 할지도 모르고, 어쩌면 내가 안 갚겠다며 험하게 굴지도 모르지."], ["You von’t think o’ arrestin’ your own son for the money, and sendin’ him off to the Fleet, will you, you unnat’ral wagabone?’", "설마 돈 때문에 친아들을 잡아다 플리트로 보낼 생각이야 안 하시겠죠, 이 비정한 늙은 떠돌이 양반?\""]], [["At this reply of Sam’s, the father and son exchanged a complete code of telegraph nods and gestures, after which, the elder Mr. Weller sat himself down on a stone step and laughed till he was purple.", "샘의 이 대답에 아비와 아들은 완벽한 전신 신호 같은 끄덕임과 손짓을 주고받았고, 그러고 나서 웰러 영감은 돌계단에 주저앉아 얼굴이 시뻘게지도록 웃어 댔다."]], [["‘Wot a old image it is!’ exclaimed Sam, indignant at this loss of time.", "\"참 별난 우상이네!\" 샘이 시간을 허비하는 데 화가 나서 외쳤다."], ["‘What are you a-settin’ down there for, con-wertin’ your face into a street-door knocker, wen there’s so much to be done.", "\"할 일이 산더민데 거기 주저앉아 얼굴을 대문 두드리개로 만들고 뭐 하는 거유."], ["Where’s the money?’", "돈은 어디 있수?\""]], [["‘In the boot, Sammy, in the boot,’ replied Mr.", "\"장화 속에 있다, 새미, 장화 속에.\""], ["Weller, composing his features.", "웰러 씨가 얼굴을 추스르며 대답했다."], ["‘Hold my hat, Sammy.’", "\"내 모자 좀 들어라, 새미.\""]], [["Having divested himself of this encumbrance, Mr. Weller gave his body a sudden wrench to one side, and by a dexterous twist, contrived to get his right hand into a most capacious pocket, from whence, after a great deal of panting and exertion, he extricated a pocket-book of the large octavo size, fastened by a huge leathern strap.", "이 거추장스러운 물건을 벗어 던진 웰러 씨는 몸을 한쪽으로 홱 비틀더니, 능숙한 뒤틀림으로 오른손을 더없이 넉넉한 주머니에 집어넣었고, 한바탕 헐떡이며 용을 쓴 끝에 거대한 가죽끈으로 채운 큼지막한 팔절판 크기의 수첩을 끄집어냈다."], ["From this ledger he drew forth a couple of whiplashes, three or four buckles, a little sample-bag of corn, and, finally, a small roll of very dirty bank-notes, from which he selected the required amount, which he handed over to Sam.", "그는 이 장부에서 채찍 끈 두 가닥, 버클 서너 개, 곡식 견본이 든 작은 자루 하나를 차례로 꺼내고, 마지막으로 아주 더러운 지폐 한 뭉치를 꺼내, 거기서 필요한 액수를 골라 샘에게 건넸다."]], [["‘And now, Sammy,’ said the old gentleman, when the whip-lashes, and the buckles, and the samples, had been all put back, and the book once more deposited at the bottom of the same pocket, ‘now, Sammy, I know a gen’l’m’n here, as’ll do the rest o’ the bisness for us, in no time--a limb o’ the law, Sammy, as has got brains like the frogs, dispersed all over his body, and reachin’ to the wery tips of his fingers; a friend of the Lord Chancellorship’s, Sammy, who’d only have to tell him what he wanted, and he’d lock you up for life, if that wos all.’", "\"자, 새미,\" 채찍 끈과 버클과 견본들을 도로 다 넣고 수첩을 다시 그 주머니 밑바닥에 넣고서 노신사가 말했다. \"자, 새미, 여기 내가 아는 신사 양반이 하나 있는데, 나머지 일은 그분이 단숨에 처리해 줄 게다—법률 나부랭이지, 새미, 개구리처럼 온몸에 뇌수가 흩어져 손가락 끄트머리까지 닿아 있는 양반이야. 대법관님의 친구인데, 새미, 자기가 뭘 원하는지 그분한테 말만 하면, 그게 다라면 널 평생 가둬 둘 수도 있는 양반이다.\""]], [["‘I say,’ said Sam, ‘none o’ that.’", "\"이봐요,\" 샘이 말했다. \"그런 짓은 안 돼요.\""]], [["‘None o’ wot?’ inquired Mr.", "\"무슨 짓 말이냐?\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘Wy, none o’ them unconstitootional ways o’ doin’ it,’ retorted Sam.", "\"왜, 그런 위헌적인 방식으로 하는 거 말이우.\" 샘이 받아쳤다."], ["‘The have-his-carcass, next to the perpetual motion, is vun of the blessedest things as wos ever made.", "\"인신 보호 영장이란 건 영구 운동 다음으로 여태 만들어진 것 중에 가장 복된 물건이오."], ["I’ve read that ‘ere in the newspapers wery of’en.’", "그 얘긴 신문에서 아주 자주 읽었수.\""]], [["‘Well, wot’s that got to do vith it?’ inquired Mr.", "\"그래, 그게 이 일이랑 무슨 상관이냐?\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘Just this here,’ said Sam, ‘that I’ll patronise the inwention, and go in, that vay.", "\"바로 이거지요.\" 샘이 말했다."], ["No visperin’s to the Chancellorship--I don’t like the notion.", "\"내가 그 발명품을 애용해서, 그 방식으로 들어가겠다는 거요."], ["It mayn’t be altogether safe, vith reference to gettin’ out agin.’", "대법관님께 귓속말 따위는 안 해요—그런 생각은 마음에 안 드오. 도로 나오는 거에 관해선, 그게 영 안전하지 않을지도 모르니까.\""]], [["Deferring to his son’s feeling upon this point, Mr. Weller at once sought the erudite Solomon Pell, and acquainted him with his desire to issue a writ, instantly, for the sum of twenty-five pounds, and costs of process; to be executed without delay upon the body of one Samuel Weller; the charges thereby incurred, to be paid in advance to Solomon Pell.", "이 점에서 아들의 뜻을 따라, 웰러 씨는 곧장 박식한 솔로몬 펠을 찾아가, 스물다섯 파운드의 금액과 소송 비용에 대해 당장 영장을 발부하고 싶다는 바람을 알렸으니, 그것은 새뮤얼 웰러라는 자의 신병에 대해 지체 없이 집행되어야 하며, 그로 인해 발생하는 비용은 솔로몬 펠에게 선금으로 지불하겠다고 했다."]], [["The attorney was in high glee, for the embarrassed coach-horser was ordered to be discharged forthwith.", "그 사무 변호사는 더없이 기뻐했으니, 곤경에 빠진 그 역마차 임대업자에게 즉시 면책 처분이 내려졌기 때문이었다."], ["He highly approved of Sam’s attachment to his master; declared that it strongly reminded him of his own feelings of devotion to his friend, the Chancellor; and at once led the elder Mr. Weller down to the Temple, to swear the affidavit of debt, which the boy, with the assistance of the blue bag, had drawn up on the spot.", "그는 샘이 주인에게 보이는 애착을 크게 칭찬하며, 그것이 자기가 벗인 대법관에게 품었던 헌신의 감정을 절절히 떠올리게 한다고 단언하고는, 곧장 웰러 영감을 템플로 데려가 채무 진술서에 선서하게 했으니, 그 진술서는 사환 아이가 푸른 가방의 도움을 받아 그 자리에서 작성한 것이었다."]], [["Meanwhile, Sam, having been formally introduced to the whitewashed gentleman and his friends, as the offspring of Mr. Weller, of the Belle Savage, was treated with marked distinction, and invited to regale himself with them in honour of the occasion--an invitation which he was by no means backward in accepting.", "그동안 샘은 벨 소비지의 웰러 씨의 자제분이라며 그 면책된 신사와 그 벗들에게 정식으로 소개되어 각별한 대접을 받았고, 이 일을 기념해 함께 한잔하자는 권유를 받았으니—샘은 결코 그 권유를 마다하지 않았다."]], [["The mirth of gentlemen of this class is of a grave and quiet character, usually; but the present instance was one of peculiar festivity, and they relaxed in proportion.", "이런 부류 신사들의 유흥은 으레 진중하고 조용한 성격을 띠지만, 이번 경우는 유달리 흥겨운 자리였던지라 그들도 그에 걸맞게 마음을 풀어놓았다."], ["After some rather tumultuous toasting of the Chief Commissioner and Mr. Solomon Pell, who had that day displayed such transcendent abilities, a mottled-faced gentleman in a blue shawl proposed that somebody should sing a song.", "그날 그토록 탁월한 솜씨를 발휘한 수석 위원과 솔로몬 펠 씨를 위해 다소 떠들썩하게 건배를 든 뒤, 푸른 숄을 두른 얼룩덜룩한 얼굴의 신사가 누군가 노래 한 곡 불러야 한다고 제안했다."], ["The obvious suggestion was, that the mottled-faced gentleman, being anxious for a song, should sing it himself; but this the mottled-faced gentleman sturdily, and somewhat offensively, declined to do.", "노래가 그토록 듣고 싶다면 그 얼룩덜룩한 얼굴의 신사가 직접 부르는 것이 당연한 제안이었으나, 그 얼룩덜룩한 얼굴의 신사는 완강히, 그것도 다소 무례하게 그러기를 거절했다."], ["Upon which, as is not unusual in such cases, a rather angry colloquy ensued.", "그러자 이런 경우에 흔히 그렇듯 다소 험악한 입씨름이 벌어졌다."]], [["‘Gentlemen,’ said the coach-horser, ‘rather than disturb the harmony of this delightful occasion, perhaps Mr.", "\"여러분,\" 그 역마차 임대업자가 말했다."], ["Samuel Weller will oblige the company.’", "\"이 즐거운 자리의 화기를 깨느니, 차라리 새뮤얼 웰러 씨가 우리 좌중을 위해 한 곡 들려주시면 어떻겠소.\""]], [["‘Raly, gentlemen,’ said Sam, ‘I’m not wery much in the habit o’ singin’ without the instrument; but anythin’ for a quiet life, as the man said wen he took the sitivation at the lighthouse.’", "\"참말로요, 여러분,\" 샘이 말했다. \"저는 악기 없이 노래하는 데는 그리 익숙지 않습니다만, 조용히 살 수만 있다면 뭐든 하지요, 등대지기 자리를 맡으면서 그 사내가 한 말마따나 말입니다.\""]], [["With this prelude, Mr. Samuel Weller burst at once into the following wild and beautiful legend, which, under the impression that it is not generally known, we take the liberty of quoting.", "이런 서곡과 함께 새뮤얼 웰러 씨가 곧장 다음과 같은 거칠고도 아름다운 전설을 우렁차게 뽑아냈으니, 그것이 널리 알려지지 않았으리라는 생각에 우리는 외람되이 그것을 옮겨 적는 바이다."], ["We would beg to call particular attention to the monosyllable at the end of the second and fourth lines, which not only enables the singer to take breath at those points, but greatly assists the metre.", "둘째 행과 넷째 행 끝의 단음절에 특히 주목해 주시기를 청하노니, 그것은 부르는 이가 그 지점에서 숨을 고르게 해 줄 뿐 아니라 운율에도 크게 보탬이 되는 까닭이다. 로맨스"]], [["# I", "# I"]], [["Bold Turpin vunce, on Hounslow Heath, His bold mare Bess bestrode--er; Ven there he see’d the Bishop’s coach A-coming along the road--er.", "1\n대담한 터핀이 한번은 하운즐로 황야에서, 그의 대담한 암말 베스에 올라타—니. 거기서 주교의 마차가 길을 따라 오는 걸 봤다—니."], ["So he gallops close to the ‘orse’s legs, And he claps his head vithin; And the Bishop says, ‘Sure as eggs is eggs, This here’s the bold Turpin!’", "그래서 그는 말다리 곁으로 바싹 달려가, 제 머리를 안으로 쑥 들이미니. 주교가 말하길, \"달걀이 달걀인 게 분명하듯, 이자가 바로 그 대담한 터핀이로다!\""]], [["And the Bishop says, ‘Sure as eggs is eggs, This here’s the bold Turpin!’", "후렴\n그리고 주교가 말하길, \"달걀이 달걀인 게 분명하듯, 이자가 바로 그 대담한 터핀이로다!\""]], [["# II", "# II"]], [["Says Turpin, ‘You shall eat your words, With a sarse of leaden bul-- let;’ So he puts a pistol to his mouth, And he fires it down his gul-- let.", "2\n터핀이 말하길, \"네놈 한 말 도로 처먹게 해 주마, 납 총알 양념을 곁들여—서.\" 그러고 권총을 그자 입에 들이대고, 그자 목구멍에 대고 쏘아 댔—네."], ["The coachman he not likin’ the job, Set off at full gal-lop, But Dick put a couple of balls in his nob, And perwailed on him to stop.", "마부는 그 일이 못마땅하여, 냅다 줄행랑을 놓았다만, 딕이 그자 골통에 총알 두 방을 박아 넣고, 그자를 멈추도록 설득해 냈—네."]], [["CHORUS (sarcastically)", "후렴 (비꼬듯이)"]], [["But Dick put a couple of balls in his nob, And perwailed on him to stop.", "하지만 딕이 그자 골통에 총알 두 방을 박아 넣고, 그자를 멈추도록 설득해 냈네."]], [["‘I maintain that that ‘ere song’s personal to the cloth,’ said the mottled-faced gentleman, interrupting it at this point.", "\"내 주장컨대 그 노래는 성직자를 빗댄 거요.\" 얼룩덜룩한 얼굴의 신사가 여기서 노래를 가로막으며 말했다."], ["‘I demand the name o’ that coachman.’", "\"그 마부 이름을 대시오.\""]], [["‘Nobody know’d,’ replied Sam.", "\"아무도 몰랐다오.\" 샘이 대답했다."], ["‘He hadn’t got his card in his pocket.’", "\"주머니에 명함을 안 넣고 있었거든요.\""]], [["‘I object to the introduction o’ politics,’ said the mottled-faced gentleman. ‘I submit that, in the present company, that ‘ere song’s political; and, wot’s much the same, that it ain’t true. I say that that coachman did not run away; but that he died game--game as pheasants; and I won’t hear nothin’ said to the contrairey.’", "\"나는 정치 끌어들이는 데 반대요.\" 얼룩덜룩한 얼굴의 신사가 말했다. \"내 단언컨대, 지금 이 자리에서 그 노래는 정치적이오. 그리고 그와 매한가지로, 사실도 아니오. 내 말하건대 그 마부는 도망치지 않았소. 그자는 꿩처럼 용맹하게—용맹하게 죽었소. 그 반대로 말하는 건 한마디도 못 듣겠소.\""]], [["As the mottled-faced gentleman spoke with great energy and determination, and as the opinions of the company seemed divided on the subject, it threatened to give rise to fresh altercation, when Mr. Weller and Mr. Pell most opportunely arrived.", "얼룩덜룩한 얼굴의 신사가 워낙 기운차고 단호하게 말하는 데다 좌중의 의견이 그 문제를 두고 갈린 듯하여, 새 입씨름이 벌어질 판이었는데, 그때 웰러 씨와 펠 씨가 더없이 때맞춰 도착했다."]], [["‘All right, Sammy,’ said Mr.", "\"다 됐다, 새미.\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘The officer will be here at four o’clock,’ said Mr.", "\"집행관이 네 시에 올 겁니다.\" 펠 씨가 말했다."], ["Pell. ‘I suppose you won’t run away meanwhile, eh?", "\"설마 그때까지 도망치진 않겠지요, 응?"], ["Ha!", "하!"], ["ha!’", "하!\""]], [["‘P’raps my cruel pa ‘ull relent afore then,’ replied Sam, with a broad grin.", "\"어쩌면 그 전에 우리 무정한 아버지가 마음을 누그러뜨릴지도 모르죠.\" 샘이 활짝 웃으며 대답했다. \"천만에.\" 웰러 영감이 말했다."]], [["‘Not I,’ said the elder Mr.", "\"좀 그래 줘 봐요.\""], ["Weller.", "샘이 말했다."]], [["‘Do,’ said Sam.", "\"무슨 일이 있어도 안 된다.\""], ["‘Not on no account,’ replied the inexorable creditor.", "그 가차 없는 채권자가 대답했다."]], [["‘I’ll give bills for the amount, at sixpence a month,’ said Sam.", "\"한 달에 육 펜스씩 어음으로 갚으리다.\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["‘I won’t take ‘em,’ said Mr.", "\"그딴 건 안 받는다.\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Ha, ha, ha!", "\"하, 하, 하!"], ["very good, very good,’ said Mr.", "좋습니다, 좋아요.\""], ["Solomon Pell, who was making out his little bill of costs; ‘a very amusing incident indeed!", "자기 비용 청구서를 작성하던 솔로몬 펠 씨가 말했다. \"참으로 재미난 일화로군요!"], ["Benjamin, copy that.’ And Mr.", "벤저민, 이거 베껴 두게.\""], ["Pell smiled again, as he called Mr. Weller’s attention to the amount.", "그러고는 펠 씨가 다시 미소를 지으며 웰러 씨에게 그 액수를 일러 주었다."]], [["‘Thank you, thank you,’ said the professional gentleman, taking up another of the greasy notes as Mr.", "\"고맙습니다, 고맙습니다.\" 웰러 씨가 수첩에서 꺼내 주는 기름진 지폐를 또 한 장 받아 들며 그 전문직 신사가 말했다."], ["Weller took it from the pocket-book. ‘Three ten and one ten is five.", "\"삼 파운드 십에 일 파운드 십이면 오 파운드로군요."], ["Much obliged to you, Mr. Weller.", "대단히 고맙습니다, 웰러 씨."], ["Your son is a most deserving young man, very much so indeed, Sir.", "자제분은 더없이 갸륵한 청년이십니다, 정말 그렇습니다, 선생."], ["It’s a very pleasant trait in a young man’s character, very much so,’ added Mr.", "젊은이의 성품 가운데 참으로 보기 좋은 자질이지요, 정말 그렇습니다.\""], ["Pell, smiling smoothly round, as he buttoned up the money.", "펠 씨가 돈을 단추 채워 넣으며 빙긋 좌중을 둘러보며 덧붙였다."]], [["‘Wot a game it is!’ said the elder Mr.", "\"이거 참 별난 노릇 아닌가!\""], ["Weller, with a chuckle.", "웰러 영감이 빙긋 웃으며 말했다."], ["‘A reg’lar prodigy son!’", "\"영락없는 천재(天才) 아들이로구먼!\""]], [["‘Prodigal--prodigal son, Sir,’ suggested Mr.", "\"탕자(蕩子)—탕자 아들이겠지요, 선생.\""], ["Pell, mildly.", "펠 씨가 부드럽게 일러 주었다."]], [["‘Never mind, Sir,’ said Mr.", "\"신경 쓰지 마쇼, 선생.\""], ["Weller, with dignity.", "웰러 씨가 점잖게 말했다."], ["‘I know wot’s o’clock, Sir.", "\"내 사정은 내가 다 압니다, 선생."], ["Wen I don’t, I’ll ask you, Sir.’", "모를 때면 내가 선생한테 물어보지요, 선생.\""]], [["By the time the officer arrived, Sam had made himself so extremely popular, that the congregated gentlemen determined to see him to prison in a body.", "집행관이 도착할 무렵 샘은 어찌나 인기를 끌었던지, 모여든 신사들이 다 함께 그를 감옥까지 바래다주기로 작정했다."], ["So off they set; the plaintiff and defendant walking arm in arm, the officer in front, and eight stout coachmen bringing up the rear.", "그리하여 그들은 출발했으니, 원고와 피고가 팔짱을 끼고 걷고, 집행관이 앞장서고, 건장한 마부 여덟이 뒤를 따랐다."], ["At Serjeant’s Inn Coffee-house the whole party halted to refresh, and, the legal arrangements being completed, the procession moved on again.", "서전트 인 커피점에서 일행 전체가 멈춰 기운을 차렸고, 법적 절차가 마무리되자 행렬은 다시 움직였다."]], [["Some little commotion was occasioned in Fleet Street, by the pleasantry of the eight gentlemen in the flank, who persevered in walking four abreast; it was also found necessary to leave the mottled-faced gentleman behind, to fight a ticket-porter, it being arranged that his friends should call for him as they came back.", "플리트 거리에서는 옆구리에 선 여덟 신사가 끝까지 네 명씩 나란히 걷겠다고 고집을 부리는 익살 탓에 자그마한 소동이 빚어졌다. 또 얼룩덜룩한 얼굴의 신사는 짐꾼과 한판 붙겠다고 해서 뒤에 남겨 둘 수밖에 없었으니, 그의 벗들이 돌아오는 길에 그를 데려가기로 합의했다."], ["Nothing but these little incidents occurred on the way.", "가는 길에 일어난 일이라곤 이런 자질구레한 사건들뿐이었다."], ["When they reached the gate of the Fleet, the cavalcade, taking the time from the plaintiff, gave three tremendous cheers for the defendant, and, after having shaken hands all round, left him.", "그들이 플리트 정문에 다다르자, 그 기마행렬은 원고를 따라 박자를 맞춰 피고를 위해 우렁찬 만세 삼창을 외쳤고, 모두 두루 악수를 나눈 뒤 그를 두고 떠났다."]], [["Sam, having been formally delivered into the warder’s custody, to the intense astonishment of Roker, and to the evident emotion of even the phlegmatic Neddy, passed at once into the prison, walked straight to his master’s room, and knocked at the door.", "샘은 정식으로 간수의 보호 아래 넘겨졌으니, 로커는 몹시 놀랐고, 무뚝뚝한 네디조차 눈에 띄게 감동한 가운데, 곧장 감옥 안으로 들어가 주인의 방으로 곧장 걸어가 문을 두드렸다."]], [["‘Come in,’ said Mr.", "\"들어오게.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["Sam appeared, pulled off his hat, and smiled.", "샘이 모습을 드러내며 모자를 벗고 미소를 지었다."]], [["‘Ah, Sam, my good lad!’ said Mr.", "\"아, 샘, 이 착한 친구야!\""], ["Pickwick, evidently delighted to see his humble friend again; ‘I had no intention of hurting your feelings yesterday, my faithful fellow, by what I said.", "보잘것없는 벗을 다시 보게 되어 분명 기뻐하며 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"어제 내가 한 말로 자네 마음을 상하게 할 생각은 없었네, 충직한 친구."], ["Put down your hat, Sam, and let me explain my meaning, a little more at length.’", "모자를 내려놓게, 샘. 내 뜻을 좀 더 자세히 설명해 주지.\""]], [["‘Won’t presently do, sir?’ inquired Sam.", "\"이따 하면 안 되겠습니까요, 나리?\" 샘이 물었다."]], [["‘Certainly,’ said Mr.", "\"되고말고.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick; ‘but why not now?’", "\"한데 왜 지금은 안 되나?\""]], [["‘I’d rayther not now, sir,’ rejoined Sam.", "\"지금은 좀 안 하는 게 좋겠습니다요, 나리.\" 샘이 응수했다."]], [["‘Why?’ inquired Mr.", "\"어째서?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘’Cause--’ said Sam, hesitating.", "\"그게—\" 샘이 머뭇거리며 말했다."]], [["‘Because of what?’ inquired Mr.", "\"무엇 때문에 그러나?\""], ["Pickwick, alarmed at his follower’s manner.", "부하의 태도에 놀란 픽윅 씨가 물었다."], ["‘Speak out, Sam.’", "\"터놓고 말해 보게, 샘.\""]], [["‘’Cause,’ rejoined Sam--‘’cause I’ve got a little bisness as I want to do.’", "\"그게요,\" 샘이 응수했다. \"처리하고 싶은 자그마한 볼일이 좀 있어서요.\""]], [["‘What business?’ inquired Mr.", "\"무슨 볼일인가?\""], ["Pickwick, surprised at Sam’s confused manner.", "샘의 당황한 태도에 의아해하며 픽윅 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘Nothin’ partickler, Sir,’ replied Sam.", "\"별 대단한 건 아닙니다요, 나리.\" 샘이 대답했다."]], [["‘Oh, if it’s nothing particular,’ said Mr.", "\"오, 별 대단한 게 아니라면,\""], ["Pickwick, with a smile, ‘you can speak with me first.’", "픽윅 씨가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"먼저 나하고 얘기해도 되겠군.\""]], [["‘I think I’d better see arter it at once,’ said Sam, still hesitating.", "\"제 생각엔 그 일을 곧장 살펴보는 게 낫겠습니다요.\" 여전히 머뭇거리며 샘이 말했다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick looked amazed, but said nothing.", "픽윅 씨는 어이없다는 표정을 지었으나 아무 말도 하지 않았다."]], [["‘The fact is--’ said Sam, stopping short.", "\"실은—\" 샘이 말하다 뚝 멈췄다."]], [["‘Well!’ said Mr.", "\"이런!\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick. ‘Speak out, Sam.’", "\"터놓고 말해 보게, 샘.\""]], [["‘Why, the fact is,’ said Sam, with a desperate effort, ‘perhaps I’d better see arter my bed afore I do anythin’ else.’", "\"그게, 실은요,\" 샘이 필사적으로 용을 쓰며 말했다. \"다른 일을 하기 전에 제 잠자리부터 먼저 살펴보는 게 낫겠습니다요.\""]], [["‘Your bed!’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"자네 잠자리라니!\""], ["Pickwick, in astonishment.", "픽윅 씨가 깜짝 놀라 외쳤다."]], [["‘Yes, my bed, Sir,’ replied Sam, ‘I’m a prisoner.", "\"네, 제 잠자리요, 나리.\" 샘이 대답했다."], ["I was arrested this here wery arternoon for debt.’", "\"저는 죄수입니다요. 바로 오늘 오후에 빚 때문에 잡혀 왔습죠.\""]], [["‘You arrested for debt!’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"자네가 빚 때문에 잡혀 왔다고!\""], ["Pickwick, sinking into a chair.", "픽윅 씨가 의자에 털썩 주저앉으며 외쳤다."]], [["‘Yes, for debt, Sir,’ replied Sam.", "\"네, 빚 때문에요, 나리.\" 샘이 대답했다."], ["‘And the man as puts me in, ‘ull never let me out till you go yourself.’", "\"그리고 저를 집어넣은 사람은 나리께서 직접 나가시기 전엔 절대 저를 안 내보내 줄 겁니다요.\""]], [["‘Bless my heart and soul!’ ejaculated Mr.", "\"맙소사, 어찌 이런 일이!\" 픽윅 씨가 부르짖었다."], ["Pickwick. ‘What do you mean?’", "\"그게 무슨 소린가?\""]], [["‘Wot I say, Sir,’ rejoined Sam.", "\"제가 말씀드린 그대롭니다요, 나리.\" 샘이 응수했다."], ["‘If it’s forty years to come, I shall be a prisoner, and I’m very glad on it; and if it had been Newgate, it would ha’ been just the same.", "\"앞으로 사십 년이 걸린대도 저는 죄수로 남을 겁니다요. 그리고 저는 그게 무척 기쁩니다요."], ["Now the murder’s out, and, damme, there’s an end on it!’", "설령 그게 뉴게이트였대도 매한가지였을 겁니다요. 자, 이제 비밀은 다 드러났고, 제기랄, 그걸로 끝입죠!\""]], [["With these words, which he repeated with great emphasis and violence, Sam Weller dashed his hat upon the ground, in a most unusual state of excitement; and then, folding his arms, looked firmly and fixedly in his master’s face.", "이 말들을 무척 힘주어, 격렬하게 거듭 내뱉으며, 샘 웰러는 좀처럼 보기 드물게 흥분한 상태로 모자를 땅바닥에 내동댕이쳤다. 그러고는 팔짱을 끼고 주인의 얼굴을 굳세고도 흔들림 없이 똑바로 바라보았다."]], [["CHAPTER XLIV. TREATS OF DIVERS LITTLE MATTERS WHICH OCCURRED IN THE FLEET, AND OF MR. WINKLE’S MYSTERIOUS BEHAVIOUR; AND SHOWS HOW THE POOR CHANCERY PRISONER OBTAINED HIS RELEASE AT LAST\nMr. Pickwick felt a great deal too much touched by the warmth of Sam’s attachment, to be able to exhibit any manifestation of anger or displeasure at the precipitate course he had adopted, in voluntarily consigning himself to a debtor’s prison for an indefinite period. The only point on which he persevered in demanding an explanation, was, the name of Sam’s detaining creditor; but this Mr. Weller as perseveringly withheld.", "픽윅 씨는 샘이 보여 준 애정의 따뜻함에 너무도 깊이 감동한 나머지, 빚쟁이 감옥에 무기한으로 제 발로 들어가 버린 그 성급한 처사에 대해 노여움이나 불쾌함을 조금도 드러낼 수가 없었다. 그가 끝까지 설명을 요구한 단 한 가지는 샘을 붙잡아 둔 채권자의 이름이었으나, 웰러 씨는 그만큼이나 끈질기게 그것을 밝히지 않았다."]], [["‘It ain’t o’ no use, sir,’ said Sam, again and again; ‘he’s a malicious, bad-disposed, vorldly-minded, spiteful, windictive creetur, with a hard heart as there ain’t no soft’nin’, as the wirtuous clergyman remarked of the old gen’l’m’n with the dropsy, ven he said, that upon the whole he thought he’d rayther leave his property to his vife than build a chapel vith it.’", "\"그래 봐야 아무 소용 없습니다, 나리.\" 샘은 거듭거듭 말했다. \"그 작자는 악의에 차고, 심보 고약하고, 세속적이고, 앙심 많고, 보복심 강한 놈이라, 마음이 어찌나 모진지 도무지 누그러지질 않습니다. 그 덕망 있는 목사 양반이 수종증 걸린 노신사를 두고 한 말마따나지요. 그 양반은 이렇게 말했더랬습니다. 곰곰이 생각해 보니 그 재산으로 예배당을 짓느니 차라리 마누라한테 물려주는 편이 낫겠다고 말입니다.\""]], [["‘But consider, Sam,’ Mr.", "\"하지만 생각해 보게, 샘.\" 픽윅 씨가 타일렀다."], ["Pickwick remonstrated, ‘the sum is so small that it can very easily be paid; and having made up my mind that you shall stop with me, you should recollect how much more useful you would be, if you could go outside the walls.’", "\"그 액수가 워낙 적어서 아주 쉽게 갚을 수 있어. 게다가 자네가 나와 함께 있어야 한다고 내가 마음을 정한 이상, 자네가 담장 바깥으로 나갈 수 있다면 훨씬 더 쓸모가 있으리라는 걸 자네도 떠올려야 하지 않겠나.\""]], [["Wery much obliged to you, sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"대단히 감사합니다요, 나리.\" 웰러 씨가 진지하게 대답했다."], ["Weller gravely; ‘but I’d rayther not.’", "\"하지만 전 그러지 않는 편이 낫겠습니다.\""]], [["‘Rather not do what, Sam?’", "\"무얼 그러지 않는 편이 낫다는 건가, 샘?\""]], [["‘Wy, I’d rayther not let myself down to ask a favour o’ this here unremorseful enemy.’", "\"아, 저 무자비한 원수 놈한테 부탁이나 하려고 제 자신을 낮추는 짓은 안 하는 게 낫겠다는 겁니다요.\""]], [["‘But it is no favour asking him to take his money, Sam,’ reasoned Mr.", "\"하지만 그자더러 자기 돈을 받아 가라고 하는 건 부탁이 아니잖나, 샘.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 논리를 폈다."]], [["‘Beg your pardon, sir,’ rejoined Sam, ‘but it ‘ud be a wery great favour to pay it, and he don’t deserve none; that’s where it is, sir.’", "\"죄송합니다만, 나리.\" 샘이 받아쳤다. \"그걸 갚아 준다는 게 그자한테는 엄청나게 큰 호의가 될 텐데, 그자는 그런 호의를 받을 자격이 없습니다. 바로 그게 문제입니다, 나리.\""]], [["Here Mr. Pickwick, rubbing his nose with an air of some vexation, Mr. Weller thought it prudent to change the theme of the discourse.", "이 대목에서 픽윅 씨가 다소 짜증스러운 기색으로 코를 문지르자, 웰러 씨는 화제를 바꾸는 편이 현명하겠다고 생각했다."]], [["‘I takes my determination on principle, Sir,’ remarked Sam, ‘and you takes yours on the same ground; wich puts me in mind o’ the man as killed his-self on principle, wich o’ course you’ve heerd on, Sir.’ Mr. Weller paused when he arrived at this point, and cast a comical look at his master out of the corners of his eyes.", "\"전 원칙에 따라 제 결심을 세운 겁니다, 나리.\" 샘이 말했다. \"나리께서도 같은 근거에서 결심을 세우신 거고요. 이걸 보니 원칙에 따라 자살한 사내가 생각나는군요. 물론 나리께서도 들어 보셨겠지요.\" 웰러 씨는 이 대목에 이르자 말을 멈추고, 눈꼬리로 주인을 슬쩍 익살스러운 눈길을 던졌다."]], [["‘There is no “of course” in the case, Sam,’ said Mr.", "\"그 일에는 '물론'이랄 게 없네, 샘.\""], ["Pickwick, gradually breaking into a smile, in spite of the uneasiness which Sam’s obstinacy had given him.", "샘의 고집이 안겨 준 불편함에도 불구하고 픽윅 씨는 차츰 미소를 띠며 말했다."], ["‘The fame of the gentleman in question, never reached my ears.’", "\"그 양반의 명성은 내 귀에 닿은 적이 한 번도 없으니까.\""]], [["‘No, sir!’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"아니, 그럴 리가요, 나리!\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 외쳤다."], ["‘You astonish me, Sir; he wos a clerk in a gov’ment office, sir.’", "\"정말 놀랍습니다, 나리. 그 양반은 관청 서기였는데요, 나리.\""]], [["‘Was he?’ said Mr.", "\"그랬나?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Yes, he wos, Sir,’ rejoined Mr.", "\"네, 그랬습니다, 나리.\" 웰러 씨가 받았다."], ["Weller; ‘and a wery pleasant gen’l’m’n too--one o’ the precise and tidy sort, as puts their feet in little India-rubber fire-buckets wen it’s wet weather, and never has no other bosom friends but hare-skins; he saved up his money on principle, wore a clean shirt ev’ry day on principle; never spoke to none of his relations on principle, ‘fear they shou’d want to borrow money of him; and wos altogether, in fact, an uncommon agreeable character.", "\"게다가 아주 유쾌한 양반이었지요. 깔끔하고 단정한 부류라, 비 오는 날이면 발을 조그만 고무 양동이에 집어넣고, 토끼 가죽 말고는 가슴에 품는 친구라곤 없는 사람이었습니다."], ["He had his hair cut on principle vunce a fortnight, and contracted for his clothes on the economic principle--three suits a year, and send back the old uns.", "원칙에 따라 돈을 모았고, 원칙에 따라 매일 깨끗한 셔츠를 입었고, 원칙에 따라 친척이라곤 누구하고도 말을 섞지 않았습니다. 혹시라도 자기한테 돈을 꾸려 들까 봐서요."], ["Being a wery reg’lar gen’l’m’n, he din’d ev’ry day at the same place, where it was one-and-nine to cut off the joint, and a wery good one-and- nine’s worth he used to cut, as the landlord often said, with the tears a-tricklin’ down his face, let alone the way he used to poke the fire in the vinter time, which wos a dead loss o’ four-pence ha’penny a day, to say nothin’ at all o’ the aggrawation o’ seein’ him do it.", "한마디로 보기 드물게 사람 좋은 위인이었지요. 원칙에 따라 보름에 한 번 머리를 깎았고, 절약 원칙에 따라 옷을 계약해서 맞췄습니다."], ["So uncommon grand with it too!", "일 년에 양복 세 벌, 헌 것은 돌려보내는 식으로요. 워낙 규칙적인 양반이라 매일 같은 데서 식사를 했는데, 거기서는 고기를 잘라 주는 데 일 실링 구 펜스를 받았습니다."], ["“Post arter the next gen’l’m’n,”", "그런데 그 양반이 잘라 먹는 양이 일 실링 구 펜스 값을 톡톡히 했지요. 주인장이 얼굴에 눈물을 줄줄 흘리며 자주 하던 말이었습니다."], ["he sings out ev’ry day ven he comes in.", "겨울철에 불을 쑤석거리는 버릇은 또 어떻고요. 그건 하루에 사 펜스 반의 순손실이었습니다."], ["“See arter the TIMES, Thomas; let me look at the MORNIN’ HERALD, when it’s out o’ hand; don’t forget to bespeak the CHRONICLE; and just bring the ‘TIZER, vill you:”", "그 짓을 지켜보는 짜증스러움은 말할 것도 없고요. 게다가 어찌나 거드름을 피우던지!"], ["and then he’d set vith his eyes fixed on the clock, and rush out, just a quarter of a minit ‘fore the time to waylay the boy as wos a-comin’ in with the evenin’ paper, which he’d read with sich intense interest and persewerance as worked the other customers up to the wery confines o’ desperation and insanity, ‘specially one i-rascible old gen’l’m’n as the vaiter wos always obliged to keep a sharp eye on, at sich times, fear he should be tempted to commit some rash act with the carving-knife.", "'다음 손님 차례 끝나면 이리 주게.' 하고 들어올 때마다 외쳐 댔지요."], ["Vell, Sir, here he’d stop, occupyin’ the best place for three hours, and never takin’ nothin’ arter his dinner, but sleep, and then he’d go away to a coffee- house a few streets off, and have a small pot o’ coffee and four crumpets, arter wich he’d walk home to Kensington and go to bed.", "'타임스 좀 챙겨 주게, 토머스. 모닝 헤럴드는 손에서 놓이면 내가 보겠네."], ["One night he wos took very ill; sends for a doctor; doctor comes in a green fly, with a kind o’ Robinson Crusoe set o’ steps, as he could let down wen he got out, and pull up arter him wen he got in, to perwent the necessity o’ the coachman’s gettin’ down, and thereby undeceivin’ the public by lettin’ ‘em see that it wos only a livery coat as he’d got on, and not the trousers to match.", "크로니클 예약하는 거 잊지 말고, 타이저도 가져다주게나.' 그러고는 시계에 눈을 못 박은 채 앉아 있다가, 시간 십오 초 전이면 후닥닥 뛰쳐나가서 석간을 들고 들어오는 소년을 길목에서 가로챘습니다."], ["“Wot’s the matter?” says the doctor.", "그러고는 어찌나 열중해서, 또 어찌나 끈질기게 읽어 대던지 다른 손님들은 절망과 광기의 막바지로 내몰릴 지경이었지요."], ["“Wery ill,” says the patient.", "특히 성마른 노신사 하나가 그랬는데, 종업원은 그럴 때마다 그 양반을 눈여겨봐야 했습니다."], ["“Wot have you been a-eatin’ on?”", "혹시라도 고기 써는 칼로 무슨 무모한 짓을 저지를까 봐서요. 어쨌든, 나리, 그 양반은 거기 죽치고 앉아 제일 좋은 자리를 세 시간씩 차지하고는, 식사 후엔 잠 말고 아무것도 더 들지 않았습니다."], ["says the doctor. “Roast weal,”", "그러고 나서 몇 블록 떨어진 커피점으로 가서 커피 작은 주전자 하나에 크럼펫 네 개를 먹고는, 켄싱턴까지 걸어가 잠자리에 들었지요."], ["says the patient.", "그러던 어느 밤 그 양반이 몹시 앓아누웠습니다."], ["“Wot’s the last thing you dewoured?”", "의사를 불렀더니 의사가 초록색 마차를 타고 왔는데, 로빈슨 크루소 식 발판이 달린 마차였습니다. 내릴 때 펼쳐 놓고 탈 때 다시 접어 올리는 식이라, 마부가 내려올 필요가 없었지요."], ["says the doctor. “Crumpets,”", "그래야 마부가 걸친 게 제복 윗도리뿐이고 거기 어울리는 바지는 없다는 걸 들켜서 대중이 속는 일이 없을 테니까요."], ["says the patient.", "'뭐가 문젭니까?' 하고 의사가 물었습니다."], ["“That’s it!”", "'몹시 아픕니다.'"], ["says the doctor.", "하고 환자가 답했지요. '뭘 드셨습니까?'"], ["“I’ll send you a box of pills directly, and don’t you never take no more of ‘em,”", "하고 의사가 물었습니다. '구운 송아지 고기요.'"], ["he says. “No more o’ wot?”", "하고 환자가 답했지요."], ["says the patient- -“pills?”", "'마지막으로 삼킨 게 뭡니까?' 하고 의사가 물었습니다."], ["“No; crumpets,”", "'크럼펫이요.' 하고 환자가 답했지요."], ["says the doctor. “Wy?”", "'바로 그겁니다!'"], ["says the patient, starting up in bed; “I’ve eat four crumpets, ev’ry night for fifteen year, on principle.”", "하고 의사가 말했습니다. '당장 알약 한 상자를 보내 드릴 테니, 다시는 그걸 들지 마십시오.'"], ["“Well, then, you’d better leave ‘em off, on principle,”", "하고 말했지요. '뭘 더 안 든다고요?'"], ["says the doctor.", "하고 환자가 물었습니다."], ["“Crumpets is not wholesome, Sir,”", "'알약을요?' '아니요, 크럼펫 말입니다.'"], ["says the doctor, wery fierce.", "하고 의사가 답했지요. '왜요?'"], ["“But they’re so cheap,”", "하고 환자가 침대에서 벌떡 일어나며 물었습니다."], ["says the patient, comin’ down a little, “and so wery fillin’ at the price.”", "'난 십오 년 동안 매일 밤 원칙에 따라 크럼펫을 네 개씩 먹었단 말입니다.' '그렇다면, 원칙에 따라 끊으시는 게 낫겠군요.'"], ["“They’d be dear to you, at any price; dear if you wos paid to eat ‘em,”", "하고 의사가 말했습니다. '크럼펫은 몸에 좋지 않습니다, 선생.'"], ["says the doctor.", "하고 의사가 아주 사납게 말했지요."], ["“Four crumpets a night,”", "'하지만 워낙 싸잖습니까.'"], ["he says, “vill do your business in six months!”", "하고 환자가 조금 누그러지며 말했습니다. '그 값에 그렇게 든든하기도 하고요.'"], ["The patient looks him full in the face, and turns it over in his mind for a long time, and at last he says, “Are you sure o’ that ‘ere, Sir?”", "'그건 어떤 값에도 비싼 겁니다. 거저 먹으래도 비싸지요.'"], ["“I’ll stake my professional reputation on it,”", "하고 의사가 말했습니다. '하룻밤에 크럼펫 네 개면,'"], ["says the doctor.", "하고 그가 말했습니다."], ["“How many crumpets, at a sittin’, do you think ‘ud kill me off at once?”", "'여섯 달이면 당신 끝장입니다!' 환자는 의사의 얼굴을 정면으로 바라보며 한참 동안 머릿속으로 곱씹어 보더니, 마침내 이렇게 말했습니다."], ["says the patient.", "'그게 정말 확실합니까, 선생?'"], ["“I don’t know,”", "'내 직업적 명예를 걸지요.'"], ["says the doctor.", "하고 의사가 답했습니다."], ["“Do you think half-a-crown’s wurth ‘ud do it?”", "'한자리에서 크럼펫을 몇 개나 먹으면 단번에 죽을 것 같습니까?' 하고 환자가 물었습니다."], ["says the patient.", "'모르겠습니다.' 하고 의사가 답했지요."], ["“I think it might,”", "'반 크라운어치면 되겠습니까?' 하고 환자가 물었습니다."], ["says the doctor.", "'될 것 같군요.'"], ["“Three shillins’ wurth ‘ud be sure to do it, I s’pose?”", "하고 의사가 답했습니다. '삼 실링어치면 확실히 되겠지요?'"], ["says the patient.", "하고 환자가 물었습니다."], ["“Certainly,”", "'물론이지요.'"], ["says the doctor. “Wery good,”", "하고 의사가 답했습니다."], ["says the patient; “good-night.”", "'아주 좋습니다.' 하고 환자가 말했지요."], ["Next mornin’ he gets up, has a fire lit, orders in three shillins’ wurth o’ crumpets, toasts ‘em all, eats ‘em all, and blows his brains out.’", "'안녕히 가십시오.' 이튿날 아침 그 양반은 일어나서 불을 지피게 하고, 삼 실링어치 크럼펫을 들이라고 시켜서는, 죄다 구워, 죄다 먹어 치우고, 제 머리통을 날려 버렸습니다.\""]], [["‘What did he do that for?’ inquired Mr.", "\"그자는 왜 그런 짓을 한 건가?\""], ["Pickwick abruptly; for he was considerably startled by this tragical termination of the narrative.", "픽윅 씨가 불쑥 물었다. 이야기의 비극적 결말에 적잖이 놀란 탓이었다."]], [["‘Wot did he do it for, Sir?’ reiterated Sam.", "\"왜 그랬냐고요, 나리?\" 샘이 되받았다."], ["‘Wy, in support of his great principle that crumpets wos wholesome, and to show that he wouldn’t be put out of his way for nobody!’", "\"아, 크럼펫이 몸에 좋다는 자기 위대한 원칙을 떠받치려고, 그리고 자기는 누구 때문에도 제 방식을 굽히지 않는다는 걸 보여 주려고 그런 겁니다요!\""]], [["With such like shiftings and changings of the discourse, did Mr. Weller meet his master’s questioning on the night of his taking up his residence in the Fleet.", "이런 식으로 화제를 요리조리 돌리고 바꾸어 가며, 웰러 씨는 플리트에 거처를 잡은 그날 밤 주인의 추궁을 받아넘겼다."], ["Finding all gentle remonstrance useless, Mr. Pickwick at length yielded a reluctant consent to his taking lodgings by the week, of a bald-headed cobbler, who rented a small slip room in one of the upper galleries.", "온건한 타이름이 죄다 소용없음을 깨달은 픽윅 씨는 마침내 마지못해 동의하여, 샘이 대머리 구두장이에게서 주 단위로 셋방을 빌리는 것을 허락했다."], ["To this humble apartment Mr. Weller moved a mattress and bedding, which he hired of Mr.", "그 구두장이는 위층 회랑 가운데 한 곳에 딸린 비좁은 골방을 세 들어 살고 있었다."], ["Roker; and, by the time he lay down upon it at night, was as much at home as if he had been bred in the prison, and his whole family had vegetated therein for three generations.", "이 초라한 방으로 웰러 씨는 로커 씨에게서 빌린 매트리스와 침구를 옮겨 왔고, 밤이 되어 그 위에 누울 무렵에는 마치 감옥에서 자라나 온 집안이 삼대에 걸쳐 그곳에서 풀처럼 자라난 듯이 편안한 기분이 되었다."]], [["‘Do you always smoke arter you goes to bed, old cock?’ inquired Mr.", "\"영감님은 잠자리에 든 다음에도 늘 담배를 피웁니까, 영감?\""], ["Weller of his landlord, when they had both retired for the night.", "둘 다 밤을 맞아 자리에 든 뒤, 웰러 씨가 집주인에게 물었다."]], [["‘Yes, I does, young bantam,’ replied the cobbler.", "\"그렇다네, 젊은 친구.\" 구두장이가 답했다."]], [["‘Will you allow me to in-quire wy you make up your bed under that ‘ere deal table?’ said Sam.", "\"저 송판 식탁 밑에다 잠자리를 까는 이유를 좀 여쭤봐도 되겠습니까?\" 샘이 물었다."]], [["‘’Cause I was always used to a four-poster afore I came here, and I find the legs of the table answer just as well,’ replied the cobbler.", "\"여기 오기 전엔 늘 사주식 침대를 썼는데, 식탁 다리가 꼭 그만큼 구실을 해 주거든.\" 구두장이가 답했다."]], [["‘You’re a character, sir,’ said Sam.", "\"영감님은 별난 인물(character)이시군요.\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["‘I haven’t got anything of the kind belonging to me,’ rejoined the cobbler, shaking his head; ‘and if you want to meet with a good one, I’m afraid you’ll find some difficulty in suiting yourself at this register office.’", "\"그런 건 내게 딸려 있질 않아.\" 구두장이가 고개를 저으며 받았다. \"좋은 추천장(character)을 만나고 싶거든, 이 직업소개소에서 자네 마음에 맞는 걸 찾긴 좀 어려울 거야.\" * character: '인물'과 '신원 보증서·추천장' 두 뜻을 함께 쓴 말장난."]], [["The above short dialogue took place as Mr. Weller lay extended on his mattress at one end of the room, and the cobbler on his, at the other; the apartment being illumined by the light of a rush-candle, and the cobbler’s pipe, which was glowing below the table, like a red-hot coal.", "위의 짧은 대화는 웰러 씨가 방 한쪽 끝에 깔린 매트리스에 길게 뻗고, 구두장이는 반대편 끝의 제 자리에 누운 채로 오간 것이었다."], ["The conversation, brief as it was, predisposed Mr.", "방은 골풀 양초의 불빛과, 식탁 아래에서 시뻘건 숯덩이처럼 이글거리는 구두장이의 담뱃대로 밝혀져 있었다."], ["Weller strongly in his landlord’s favour; and, raising himself on his elbow, he took a more lengthened survey of his appearance than he had yet had either time or inclination to make.", "짧긴 했어도 그 대화는 웰러 씨로 하여금 집주인에게 강하게 호감을 품게 했고, 그는 팔꿈치를 짚고 몸을 일으켜, 여태까지 시간도 마음도 없어 못 했던 만큼 좀 더 찬찬히 그의 모습을 살펴보았다."]], [["He was a sallow man--all cobblers are; and had a strong bristly beard-- all cobblers have.", "그는 누르께한 얼굴을 한 사내였다. 구두장이란 다 그렇다."], ["His face was a queer, good-tempered, crooked-featured piece of workmanship, ornamented with a couple of eyes that must have worn a very joyous expression at one time, for they sparkled yet.", "그리고 빳빳한 수염이 더부룩했다. 구두장이란 다 그렇다."], ["The man was sixty, by years, and Heaven knows how old by imprisonment, so that his having any look approaching to mirth or contentment, was singular enough.", "그의 얼굴은 묘하고 사람 좋고 이목구비가 비뚜름한 작품이었는데, 한때는 무척 즐거운 표정을 띠었음에 틀림없는 두 눈으로 장식되어 있었다. 그 눈은 아직도 반짝였기 때문이다."], ["He was a little man, and, being half doubled up as he lay in bed, looked about as long as he ought to have been without his legs.", "사내는 햇수로 예순이었고, 수감 햇수로는 하늘만이 알 만큼 늙어 있었으니, 그가 즐거움이나 만족에 가까운 표정을 조금이라도 띠고 있다는 건 어지간히 별난 일이었다."], ["He had a great red pipe in his mouth, and was smoking, and staring at the rush-light, in a state of enviable placidity.", "그는 키가 작은 사내였고, 침대에 누워 몸을 반으로 접고 있으니 다리를 뺀 키만큼이나 짧아 보였다. 입에는 커다란 빨간 담뱃대를 물고서, 부러울 만큼 평온한 상태로 담배를 피우며 골풀 양초를 바라보고 있었다."]], [["‘Have you been here long?’ inquired Sam, breaking the silence which had lasted for some time.", "\"여기 오래 계셨습니까?\" 한동안 이어진 침묵을 깨고 샘이 물었다."]], [["‘Twelve year,’ replied the cobbler, biting the end of his pipe as he spoke.", "\"십이 년일세.\" 구두장이가 담뱃대 끝을 깨물며 답했다."]], [["‘Contempt?’ inquired Sam.", "\"법정모독으로요?\" 샘이 물었다."]], [["The cobbler nodded.", "구두장이가 고개를 끄덕였다."]], [["‘Well, then,’ said Sam, with some sternness, ‘wot do you persevere in bein’ obstinit for, vastin’ your precious life away, in this here magnified pound? Wy don’t you give in, and tell the Chancellorship that you’re wery sorry for makin’ his court contemptible, and you won’t do so no more?’", "\"그럼 말입니다.\" 샘이 다소 엄한 어조로 말했다. \"대체 뭐 하러 고집을 부려서, 이 부풀려 놓은 가축우리에서 귀한 인생을 허비하고 계십니까? 왜 항복하고 대법관 양반한테 '법정을 모독해서 정말 죄송하고, 다신 안 그러겠습니다.' 하고 말하지 않으시는 겁니까?\""]], [["The cobbler put his pipe in the corner of his mouth, while he smiled, and then brought it back to its old place again; but said nothing.", "구두장이는 담뱃대를 입꼬리로 옮겨 물고 빙긋 웃더니, 다시 본래 자리로 가져왔다. 그러나 아무 말도 하지 않았다."]], [["‘Wy don’t you?’ said Sam, urging his question strenuously.", "\"왜 안 그러십니까?\" 샘이 끈질기게 다그쳐 물었다."]], [["‘Ah,’ said the cobbler, ‘you don’t quite understand these matters.", "\"아.\" 구두장이가 말했다."], ["What do you suppose ruined me, now?’", "\"자네는 이런 일들을 잘 모르는군. 대체 뭐가 날 망쳤다고 생각하나?\""]], [["‘Wy,’ said Sam, trimming the rush-light, ‘I s’pose the beginnin’ wos, that you got into debt, eh?’", "\"글쎄요.\" 샘이 골풀 양초의 심지를 다듬으며 말했다. \"처음엔 빚을 진 데서 비롯됐겠지요, 안 그렇습니까?\""]], [["‘Never owed a farden,’ said the cobbler; ‘try again.’", "\"한 푼도 빚진 적 없네.\" 구두장이가 말했다. \"다시 맞혀 보게.\""]], [["‘Well, perhaps,’ said Sam, ‘you bought houses, wich is delicate English for goin’ mad; or took to buildin’, wich is a medical term for bein’ incurable.’", "\"그럼, 아마.\" 샘이 말했다. \"집을 사셨겠지요. 그건 미쳐 간다는 말의 점잖은 영어 표현이지요. 아니면 집 짓는 일에 빠지셨거나요. 그건 회복 불능이라는 뜻의 의학 용어고요.\""]], [["The cobbler shook his head and said, ‘Try again.’", "구두장이는 고개를 저으며 말했다. \"다시 맞혀 보게.\""]], [["‘You didn’t go to law, I hope?’ said Sam suspiciously.", "\"설마 소송을 거신 건 아니겠지요?\" 샘이 미심쩍게 물었다."]], [["‘Never in my life,’ replied the cobbler.", "\"평생 한 번도.\" 구두장이가 답했다."], ["‘The fact is, I was ruined by having money left me.’", "\"사실인즉, 난 유산을 물려받는 바람에 망했네.\""]], [["‘Come, come,’ said Sam, ‘that von’t do.", "\"이런, 이런.\" 샘이 말했다."], ["I wish some rich enemy ‘ud try to vork my destruction in that ‘ere vay.", "\"그건 안 통하는 소립니다."], ["I’d let him.’", "부자 원수 놈이 있어서 저를 그런 식으로 망쳐 주겠다면, 저는 기꺼이 망해 드리겠습니다요.\""]], [["‘Oh, I dare say you don’t believe it,’ said the cobbler, quietly smoking his pipe.", "\"오, 자네가 안 믿는 모양이군.\" 구두장이가 조용히 담뱃대를 빨며 말했다."], ["‘I wouldn’t if I was you; but it’s true for all that.’", "\"나라도 안 믿겠지. 하지만 그래도 사실이라네.\""]], [["‘How wos it?’ inquired Sam, half induced to believe the fact already, by the look the cobbler gave him.", "\"어떻게 된 겁니까?\" 구두장이가 던진 눈빛에 벌써 절반쯤 그 말을 믿게 된 샘이 물었다."]], [["‘Just this,’ replied the cobbler; ‘an old gentleman that I worked for, down in the country, and a humble relation of whose I married--she’s dead, God bless her, and thank Him for it!--was seized with a fit and went off.’", "\"바로 이렇다네.\" 구두장이가 답했다. \"내가 시골에서 일해 주던 노신사가 있었는데, 그 양반의 미천한 친척과 내가 결혼했지. 그 사람은 죽었네. 하느님이 보살피시기를, 또 그렇게 해 주신 데 감사하기를! 어쨌든 그 노신사가 발작을 일으켜 가 버렸네.\""]], [["‘Where?’ inquired Sam, who was growing sleepy after the numerous events of the day.", "\"어디로요?\" 그날 하루의 숱한 일들로 졸음이 밀려오던 샘이 물었다."]], [["‘How should I know where he went?’ said the cobbler, speaking through his nose in an intense enjoyment of his pipe.", "\"그 양반이 어디로 갔는지 낸들 어찌 아나?\" 담뱃대를 한껏 음미하며 코맹맹이 소리로 구두장이가 말했다."], ["‘He went off dead.’", "\"죽어서 가 버렸다는 말일세.\""]], [["‘Oh, that indeed,’ said Sam.", "\"아, 그렇군요.\" 샘이 말했다."], ["‘Well?’", "\"그래서요?\""]], [["‘Well,’ said the cobbler, ‘he left five thousand pound behind him.’", "\"그래서.\" 구두장이가 말했다. \"그 양반이 오천 파운드를 남겼네.\""]], [["‘And wery gen-teel in him so to do,’ said Sam.", "\"그렇게 하시다니 어지간히 점잖은 양반이군요.\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["‘One of which,’ continued the cobbler, ‘he left to me, ‘cause I married his relation, you see.’", "\"그중 천 파운드를.\" 구두장이가 이었다. \"내게 남겼네. 내가 자기 친척과 결혼했으니까 말이야.\""]], [["‘Wery good,’ murmured Sam.", "\"아주 좋습니다.\" 샘이 중얼거렸다."]], [["‘And being surrounded by a great number of nieces and nevys, as was always quarrelling and fighting among themselves for the property, he makes me his executor, and leaves the rest to me in trust, to divide it among ‘em as the will prowided.’", "\"그런데 그 양반한테는 조카딸이며 조카아들이 잔뜩 있어서, 늘 재산을 두고 저희끼리 다투고 싸워 댔거든. 그래서 그 양반은 날 유언 집행인으로 삼고, 나머지를 신탁으로 내게 맡겨, 유언에 정해진 대로 그자들 사이에 나눠 주게 했지.\""]], [["‘Wot do you mean by leavin’ it on trust?’ inquired Sam, waking up a little.", "\"신탁으로 남긴다는 게 무슨 말입니까?\" 샘이 살짝 정신이 들며 물었다."], ["‘If it ain’t ready-money, were’s the use on it?’", "\"현찰이 아니면, 그게 무슨 소용입니까?\""]], [["‘It’s a law term, that’s all,’ said the cobbler.", "\"법률 용어일 뿐일세.\" 구두장이가 말했다."]], [["‘I don’t think that,’ said Sam, shaking his head.", "\"그렇게는 생각 안 됩니다.\" 샘이 고개를 저으며 말했다."], ["‘There’s wery little trust at that shop.", "\"그 가게엔 믿을 만한 게 별로 없거든요."], ["Hows’ever, go on.’", "어쨌든 계속하시지요.\""]], [["Well,’ said the cobbler, ‘when I was going to take out a probate of the will, the nieces and nevys, who was desperately disappointed at not getting all the money, enters a caveat against it.’", "* 신탁(trust): 영어 trust에는 '신탁'과 '믿음'의 뜻이 함께 있어, 샘이 말장난을 한 것. \"그래서.\" 구두장이가 말했다. \"내가 유언 검인을 받으려 하니까, 그 돈을 전부 차지하지 못해 잔뜩 실망한 조카딸이며 조카아들이 거기에 이의신청을 걸었네.\""]], [["What’s that?’ inquired Sam.", "\"그게 뭡니까?\" 샘이 물었다."]], [["‘A legal instrument, which is as much as to say, it’s no go,’ replied the cobbler.", "\"법적 절차인데, 한마디로 안 된다는 소리지.\" 구두장이가 답했다."]], [["‘I see,’ said Sam, ‘a sort of brother-in-law o’ the have-his-carcass.", "\"알겠습니다.\" 샘이 말했다."], ["Well.’", "\"인신보호영장의 처남쯤 되는 거군요. 계속하시지요.\""]], [["‘But,’ continued the cobbler, ‘finding that they couldn’t agree among themselves, and consequently couldn’t get up a case against the will, they withdrew the caveat, and I paid all the legacies. I’d hardly done it, when one nevy brings an action to set the will aside. The case comes on, some months afterwards, afore a deaf old gentleman, in a back room somewhere down by Paul’s Churchyard; and arter four counsels had taken a day a-piece to bother him regularly, he takes a week or two to consider, and read the evidence in six volumes, and then gives his judgment that how the testator was not quite right in his head, and I must pay all the money back again, and all the costs. I appealed; the case come on before three or four very sleepy gentlemen, who had heard it all before in the other court, where they’re lawyers without work; the only difference being, that, there, they’re called doctors, and in the other place delegates, if you understand that; and they very dutifully confirmed the decision of the old gentleman below. After that, we went into Chancery, where we are still, and where I shall always be. My lawyers have had all my thousand pound long ago; and what between the estate, as they call it, and the costs, I’m here for ten thousand, and shall stop here, till I die, mending shoes. Some gentlemen have talked of bringing it before Parliament, and I dare say would have done it, only they hadn’t time to come to me, and I hadn’t power to go to them, and they got tired of my long letters, and dropped the business. And this is God’s truth, without one word of suppression or exaggeration, as fifty people, both in this place and out of it, very well know.’", "\"그런데.\" 구두장이가 이었다. \"저희끼리도 의견이 안 맞아서 결국 유언에 맞설 소송을 꾸리지 못하자, 그자들은 이의신청을 거둬들였고, 난 유산을 죄다 나눠 줬네. 그걸 막 끝내자마자 조카아들 하나가 유언을 무효로 돌리려고 소송을 걸었지. 몇 달 뒤에 그 사건이 폴 성당 묘지 근처 어디 뒷방에서 귀먹은 노신사 앞에 올라갔네. 그러고는 변호사 넷이 하루씩 차례로 그 양반을 정성껏 괴롭히고 나니, 그 양반이 일이 주를 들여 곰곰이 따져 보고 여섯 권짜리 증거를 읽어 본 다음, 판결을 내렸지. 유언자가 머리가 온전치 못했으니, 내가 돈을 죄다 도로 물어내고 비용도 다 물어야 한다고 말이야. 난 항소했네. 사건은 아주 졸린 신사 서넛 앞에 올라갔는데, 그자들은 다른 법정에서 그걸 이미 다 들은 자들이었지. 거긴 일거리 없는 변호사들 자리거든. 유일한 차이라면 거기선 그자들을 박사라고 부르고 다른 데선 위원이라고 부른다는 것뿐이야. 무슨 말인지 알겠나. 어쨌든 그자들은 아주 충실하게 아래 노신사의 판결을 확정했네. 그런 다음 우린 형평법원으로 넘어갔고, 지금도 거기 있고, 난 영영 거기 있을 걸세. 변호사들은 내 천 파운드를 벌써 오래전에 다 가져갔지. 게다가 그자들이 재산이라고 부르는 것과 비용을 다 합쳐서, 난 만 파운드 빚으로 여기 들어와 있고, 죽을 때까지 여기서 구두나 꿰매고 있을 걸세. 어떤 신사 양반들이 이 일을 의회에 올리겠다고 말한 적도 있고, 정말 그랬을지도 몰라. 다만 그자들은 내게 올 시간이 없었고, 난 그자들한테 갈 힘이 없었고, 그자들은 내 긴 편지에 진절머리가 나서 그 일을 그만뒀지. 이게 하느님께 맹세코 사실이라네. 한마디도 숨기거나 부풀린 데 없이 말이야. 이곳 안팎의 쉰 명이 다 잘 알고 있는 일이지.\""]], [["The cobbler paused to ascertain what effect his story had produced on Sam; but finding that he had dropped asleep, knocked the ashes out of his pipe, sighed, put it down, drew the bed-clothes over his head, and went to sleep, too.", "구두장이는 제 이야기가 샘에게 어떤 영향을 주었는지 확인하려고 말을 멈췄으나, 그가 잠들어 버린 것을 알자 담뱃대의 재를 털어 내고, 한숨을 쉬고, 담뱃대를 내려놓고, 이불을 머리까지 끌어 올리고는, 자기도 잠이 들었다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick was sitting at breakfast, alone, next morning (Sam being busily engaged in the cobbler’s room, polishing his master’s shoes and brushing the black gaiters) when there came a knock at the door, which, before Mr.", "이튿날 아침 픽윅 씨는 혼자 식탁에 앉아 아침을 먹고 있었는데(샘은 구두장이의 방에서 주인의 구두를 닦고 검은 각반을 솔질하느라 분주했다), 그때 문을 두드리는 소리가 났다."], ["Pickwick could cry ‘Come in!’ was followed by the appearance of a head of hair and a cotton-velvet cap, both of which articles of dress he had no difficulty in recognising as the personal property of Mr.", "픽윅 씨가 \"들어오시오!\""], ["Smangle.", "하고 외치기도 전에, 그 뒤를 이어 한 무더기의 머리털과 무명 벨벳 모자가 나타났는데, 그 두 가지 복장은 어렵잖게 스맹글 씨의 개인 소유물임을 알아볼 수 있었다."]], [["‘How are you?’ said that worthy, accompanying the inquiry with a score or two of nods; ‘I say--do you expect anybody this morning? Three men-- devilish gentlemanly fellows--have been asking after you downstairs, and knocking at every door on the hall flight; for which they’ve been most infernally blown up by the collegians that had the trouble of opening ‘em.’", "\"안녕하시오?\" 그 훌륭한 양반이 스무 번 남짓 고개를 끄덕이며 이렇게 물었다. \"이봐요, 오늘 아침에 누구 올 사람이라도 있소? 사내 셋이, 지독히도 신사다운 작자들이, 아래층에서 당신을 찾으며 복도 층 문이란 문은 죄다 두드려 댔다오. 그 바람에 문을 열어 주느라 고생한 동료 수감자들한테 엄청나게 욕을 먹었지요.\""]], [["‘Dear me!", "\"이런!"], ["How very foolish of them,’ said Mr. Pickwick, rising.", "그 친구들도 어지간히 어리석군요.\""], ["‘Yes; I have no doubt they are some friends whom I rather expected to see, yesterday.’", "픽윅 씨가 일어서며 말했다. \"그래요, 틀림없이 어제 좀 만날까 했던 친구들일 겁니다.\""]], [["‘Friends of yours!’ exclaimed Smangle, seizing Mr.", "\"당신 친구들이라고요!\" 스맹글이 픽윅 씨의 손을 덥석 잡으며 외쳤다."], ["Pickwick by the hand.", "\"더 말할 것 없소."], ["‘Say no more. Curse me, they’re friends of mine from this minute, and friends of Mivins’s, too.", "빌어먹을, 이 순간부터 그들은 내 친구이기도 하고, 마이빈스의 친구이기도 하오."], ["Infernal pleasant, gentlemanly dog, Mivins, isn’t he?’ said Smangle, with great feeling.", "마이빈스란 작자, 지독히도 유쾌하고 신사다운 녀석이지요, 안 그렇소?\" 스맹글이 자못 감격에 차서 말했다."]], [["‘I know so little of the gentleman,’ said Mr.", "\"제가 그분에 대해 아는 게 너무 적어서요.\""], ["Pickwick, hesitating, ‘that I--’", "픽윅 씨가 머뭇거리며 말했다. \"그러니 저는—\""]], [["‘I know you do,’ interrupted Smangle, clasping Mr.", "\"알지요, 잘 알고말고요.\" 스맹글이 픽윅 씨의 어깨를 부여잡으며 말을 가로챘다."], ["Pickwick by the shoulder.", "\"차차 더 잘 알게 될 거요."], ["‘You shall know him better.", "그 친구한테 푹 빠질 거외다."], ["You’ll be delighted with him.", "그 사람으로 말하자면, 선생.\""], ["That man, Sir,’ said Smangle, with a solemn countenance, ‘has comic powers that would do honour to Drury Lane Theatre.’", "스맹글이 엄숙한 얼굴로 말했다. \"드루리 레인 극장에서도 빛을 발할 만한 희극적 재능을 지녔지요.\""]], [["‘Has he indeed?’ said Mr.", "\"정말 그렇습니까?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Ah, by Jove he has!’ replied Smangle.", "\"아, 맹세코 그렇소!\" 스맹글이 답했다."], ["‘Hear him come the four cats in the wheel-barrow--four distinct cats, sir, I pledge you my honour.", "\"외바퀴 손수레에 탄 고양이 네 마리 흉내를 내는 걸 한번 들어 보시오. 또렷이 구별되는 고양이 네 마리를, 명예를 걸고 장담하리다."], ["Now you know that’s infernal clever!", "자, 그게 지독히도 영리한 솜씨 아니겠소!"], ["Damme, you can’t help liking a man, when you see these traits about him.", "빌어먹을, 그런 면모를 보면 그 친구를 좋아하지 않을 수가 없다오."], ["He’s only one fault--that little failing I mentioned to you, you know.’", "한 가지 흠이라면, 아까 내가 당신한테 말한 그 사소한 결점뿐이지요.\""]], [["As Mr. Smangle shook his head in a confidential and sympathising manner at this juncture, Mr.", "이 대목에서 스맹글 씨가 은밀하고도 동정 어린 태도로 고개를 가로젓자, 픽윅 씨는 자기가 무어라 한마디 해야 할 처지임을 느끼고 \"아!\""], ["Pickwick felt that he was expected to say something, so he said, ‘Ah!’ and looked restlessly at the door.", "하고는 안절부절 문 쪽을 바라보았다."]], [["‘Ah!’ echoed Mr.", "\"아!\""], ["Smangle, with a long-drawn sigh.", "스맹글 씨도 길게 한숨을 내쉬며 똑같이 받았다."], ["‘He’s delightful company, that man is, sir.", "\"그 사람으로 말하자면 더없이 유쾌한 동무라오, 선생."], ["I don’t know better company anywhere; but he has that one drawback.", "어디서도 그보다 나은 동무는 못 봤소. 다만 그 한 가지 결점이 있을 뿐이지요."], ["If the ghost of his grandfather, Sir, was to rise before him this minute, he’d ask him for the loan of his acceptance on an eightpenny stamp.’", "만약 지금 이 순간 그의 할아버지 유령이 눈앞에 솟아오른다면, 그자는 팔 펜스짜리 인지를 붙인 어음에 배서나 좀 해 달라고 부탁할 위인이외다.\""]], [["Dear me!’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"이런!\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 외쳤다."]], [["‘Yes,’ added Mr.", "\"그렇소.\""], ["Smangle; ‘and if he’d the power of raising him again, he would, in two months and three days from this time, to renew the bill!’", "스맹글 씨가 덧붙였다. \"그리고 그 유령을 다시 불러낼 힘이 있다면, 지금부터 두 달 사흘 뒤에 어음을 갱신하려고 또 불러낼 자란 말이오!\""]], [["‘Those are very remarkable traits,’ said Mr.", "\"그것 참 놀라운 면모로군요.\""], ["Pickwick; ‘but I’m afraid that while we are talking here, my friends may be in a state of great perplexity at not finding me.’", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"하지만 우리가 여기서 이야기하는 동안 제 친구들이 절 못 찾아 몹시 난처해하고 있을까 봐 걱정입니다.\""]], [["‘I’ll show ‘em the way,’ said Smangle, making for the door.", "\"내가 길을 안내하리다.\" 스맹글이 문 쪽으로 향하며 말했다."], ["‘Good-day.", "\"그럼 또 봅시다."], ["I won’t disturb you while they’re here, you know.", "그들이 여기 있는 동안은 방해하지 않으리다, 알겠소."], ["By the bye--’", "아 참, 그런데—\""]], [["As Smangle pronounced the last three words, he stopped suddenly, reclosed the door which he had opened, and, walking softly back to Mr. Pickwick, stepped close up to him on tiptoe, and said, in a very soft whisper--", "스맹글은 이 마지막 세 마디를 내뱉다 말고 문득 멈춰 서더니, 방금 연 문을 다시 닫고는, 사뿐사뿐 픽윅 씨에게 되돌아와 발끝으로 바싹 다가서서 아주 부드러운 속삭임으로 말했다."]], [["‘You couldn’t make it convenient to lend me half-a-crown till the latter end of next week, could you?’", "\"다음 주 후반까지 반 크라운만 좀 융통해 주실 형편이 안 되겠소?\""]], [["Mr.", "픽윅 씨는 웃음을 참기 힘들었으나, 가까스로 진지한 표정을 지키며 동전을 꺼내어 스맹글 씨의 손바닥에 놓아 주었다."], ["Pickwick could scarcely forbear smiling, but managing to preserve his gravity, he drew forth the coin, and placed it in Mr.", "그러자 그 신사는 깊은 비밀을 암시하는 양 연신 고개를 끄덕이고 눈을 찡긋거리며, 세 낯선 이를 찾으러 사라졌다가 이내 그들을 데리고 돌아왔다. 그리고 갚는 걸 잊지 않겠노라는 픽윅 씨에 대한 다짐으로 세 번 기침하고 세 번 고개를 끄덕인 뒤, 붙임성 있는 태도로 두루 악수를 나누고는 마침내 자리를 떴다."], ["Smangle’s palm; upon which, that gentleman, with many nods and winks, implying profound mystery, disappeared in quest of the three strangers, with whom he presently returned; and having coughed thrice, and nodded as many times, as an assurance to Mr.", "\"여보게들.\" 픽윅 씨가 터프먼 씨, 윈클 씨, 스노드그래스 씨와 차례로 악수하며 말했다."], ["Pickwick that he would not forget to pay, he shook hands all round, in an engaging manner, and at length took himself off.", "문제의 세 방문객은 바로 그들이었다. \"이렇게 보니 정말 기쁘네.\""]], [["‘My dear friends,’ said Mr. Pickwick, shaking hands alternately with Mr. Tupman, Mr. Winkle, and Mr. Snodgrass, who were the three visitors in question, ‘I am delighted to see you.’", "세 사람은 깊이 감격했다. 터프먼 씨는 애통하게 고개를 가로저었고, 스노드그래스 씨는 감정을 감추지 않고 손수건을 꺼냈으며, 윈클 씨는 창가로 물러나 소리 내어 코를 훌쩍였다."]], [["The triumvirate were much affected.", "\"안녕하십니까, 신사 여러분.\""], ["Mr. Tupman shook his head deploringly, Mr.", "마침 그때 구두와 각반을 들고 들어온 샘이 말했다."], ["Snodgrass drew forth his handkerchief, with undisguised emotion; and Mr.", "\"우울일랑 떨쳐 버리십시다. 학교 여선생이 죽었을 때 꼬마가 한 말마따나 말입니다."], ["Winkle retired to the window, and sniffed aloud.", "학교(college)에 오신 걸 환영합니다, 신사 여러분.\""]], [["‘Mornin’, gen’l’m’n,’ said Sam, entering at the moment with the shoes and gaiters. ‘Avay vith melincholly, as the little boy said ven his schoolmissus died. Velcome to the college, gen’l’m’n.’", "* college: 당시 채무자 감옥을 '칼리지(college)'라 익살스럽게 부른 데서 온 말."]], [["‘This foolish fellow,’ said Mr.", "\"이 어리석은 친구가 말일세.\""], ["Pickwick, tapping Sam on the head as he knelt down to button up his master’s gaiters--‘this foolish fellow has got himself arrested, in order to be near me.’", "픽윅 씨가, 주인의 각반 단추를 채우려고 무릎을 꿇은 샘의 머리를 톡톡 두드리며 말했다. \"이 어리석은 친구가 나와 가까이 있겠다고 일부러 붙잡혀 들어왔다네.\""]], [["‘What!’ exclaimed the three friends.", "\"뭐라고요!\" 세 친구가 외쳤다."]], [["‘Yes, gen’l’m’n,’ said Sam, ‘I’m a--stand steady, sir, if you please-- I’m a prisoner, gen’l’m’n. Con-fined, as the lady said.’", "\"그렇습니다, 신사 여러분.\" 샘이 말했다. \"저는, 가만히 좀 계세요, 나리, 부탁드립니다, 저는 죄수입니다, 신사 여러분. 갇힌 몸이지요. 그 부인이 말한 것처럼요.\""]], [["‘A prisoner!’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"죄수라고!\""], ["Winkle, with unaccountable vehemence.", "윈클 씨가 영문 모를 격렬함으로 외쳤다."]], [["‘Hollo, sir!’ responded Sam, looking up.", "\"이런, 나리!\" 샘이 고개를 들며 받았다."], ["‘Wot’s the matter, Sir?’", "\"무슨 일이십니까, 나리?\""]], [["‘I had hoped, Sam, that--Nothing, nothing,’ said Mr.", "\"난 자네가, 샘, 그러기를 바랐는데, 아니, 아무것도 아닐세, 아무것도.\""], ["Winkle precipitately.", "윈클 씨가 황급히 말했다."]], [["There was something so very abrupt and unsettled in Mr. Winkle’s manner, that Mr. Pickwick involuntarily looked at his two friends for an explanation.", "윈클 씨의 태도에 어딘가 너무도 불쑥스럽고 불안정한 데가 있어서, 픽윅 씨는 저도 모르게 설명을 구하듯 다른 두 친구를 바라보았다."]], [["‘We don’t know,’ said Mr.", "\"저희도 모릅니다.\""], ["Tupman, answering this mute appeal aloud.", "이 무언의 호소에 터프먼 씨가 소리 내어 답했다."], ["‘He has been much excited for two days past, and his whole demeanour very unlike what it usually is.", "\"그는 지난 이틀 동안 몹시 들떠 있었고, 전체적으로 평소와는 영 딴판입니다."], ["We feared there must be something the matter, but he resolutely denies it.’", "무슨 일이 있는 게 분명하다고 걱정했지만, 본인은 한사코 아니라고만 합니다.\""]], [["‘No, no,’ said Mr.", "\"아닙니다, 아니에요.\""], ["Winkle, colouring beneath Mr. Pickwick’s gaze; ‘there is really nothing.", "픽윅 씨의 시선 아래 얼굴을 붉히며 윈클 씨가 말했다."], ["I assure you there is nothing, my dear sir.", "\"정말 아무 일도 없습니다."], ["It will be necessary for me to leave town, for a short time, on private business, and I had hoped to have prevailed upon you to allow Sam to accompany me.’", "단언컨대 아무 일도 없어요, 선생님. 다만 제가 개인적인 용무로 잠시 런던을 떠나야 할 일이 있어서, 선생님께서 샘이 저와 동행하도록 허락해 주십사 부탁드리려 했던 겁니다.\""]], [["Mr. Pickwick looked more astonished than before.", "픽윅 씨는 아까보다 더 놀란 표정을 지었다."]], [["‘I think,’ faltered Mr.", "\"제 생각엔.\""], ["Winkle, ‘that Sam would have had no objection to do so; but, of course, his being a prisoner here, renders it impossible.", "윈클 씨가 더듬거렸다. \"샘도 그러는 데 반대하지 않았을 듯합니다만, 물론 그가 여기 죄수로 잡혀 있으니 그건 불가능하지요."], ["So I must go alone.’", "그러니 저 혼자 가야겠습니다.\""]], [["As Mr. Winkle said these words, Mr.", "윈클 씨가 이 말을 하는 동안, 픽윅 씨는 다소 놀라며 느꼈는데, 각반을 만지던 샘의 손가락이, 마치 적잖이 놀라거나 흠칫한 듯 떨리고 있었다."], ["Pickwick felt, with some astonishment, that Sam’s fingers were trembling at the gaiters, as if he were rather surprised or startled. Sam looked up at Mr.", "샘도 윈클 씨가 말을 마치자 그를 올려다보았다."], ["Winkle, too, when he had finished speaking; and though the glance they exchanged was instantaneous, they seemed to understand each other.", "그리고 두 사람이 주고받은 눈길은 순식간이었으나, 서로 뜻을 알아챈 듯했다."]], [["‘Do you know anything of this, Sam?’ said Mr.", "\"자네, 이 일에 대해 무얼 좀 아나, 샘?\""], ["Pickwick sharply.", "픽윅 씨가 날카롭게 물었다."]], [["‘No, I don’t, sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"아니요, 모릅니다, 나리.\""], ["Weller, beginning to button with extraordinary assiduity.", "웰러 씨가 유난스러운 부지런함으로 단추를 채우기 시작하며 답했다."]], [["‘Are you sure, Sam?’ said Mr.", "\"확실한가, 샘?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Wy, sir,’ responded Mr.", "\"글쎄요, 나리.\""], ["Weller; ‘I’m sure so far, that I’ve never heerd anythin’ on the subject afore this moment.", "웰러 씨가 받았다. \"지금 이 순간까지 그 일에 대해 한마디도 들은 적 없다는 것만큼은 확실합니다."], ["If I makes any guess about it,’ added Sam, looking at Mr.", "혹 제가 짐작하는 바가 있다 해도.\" 샘이 윈클 씨를 바라보며 덧붙였다."], ["Winkle, ‘I haven’t got any right to say what it is, ‘fear it should be a wrong ‘un.’", "\"그게 뭔지 함부로 말할 권리는 없습니다. 틀린 짐작일까 봐서요.\""]], [["‘I have no right to make any further inquiry into the private affairs of a friend, however intimate a friend,’ said Mr.", "\"아무리 가까운 친구라 한들, 친구의 사사로운 일을 더 캐물을 권리는 내게 없네.\" 짧은 침묵 끝에 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick, after a short silence; ‘at present let me merely say, that I do not understand this at all.", "\"지금은 다만 이 말만 해 두겠네. 나로선 이게 도무지 이해가 안 가는군."], ["There. We have had quite enough of the subject.’", "자, 이 이야기는 충분히 했으니 그만하지.\""]], [["Thus expressing himself, Mr. Pickwick led the conversation to different topics, and Mr.", "이렇게 말하고 나서 픽윅 씨는 화제를 다른 데로 돌렸고, 윈클 씨도 차츰 마음이 편해 보였으나, 여전히 완전히 편하기에는 멀었다."], ["Winkle gradually appeared more at ease, though still very far from being completely so.", "다들 나눌 이야기가 어찌나 많던지 오전이 순식간에 지나갔다."], ["They had all so much to converse about, that the morning very quickly passed away; and when, at three o’clock, Mr. Weller produced upon the little dining-table, a roast leg of mutton and an enormous meat-pie, with sundry dishes of vegetables, and pots of porter, which stood upon the chairs or the sofa bedstead, or where they could, everybody felt disposed to do justice to the meal, notwithstanding that the meat had been purchased, and dressed, and the pie made, and baked, at the prison cookery hard by.", "그리고 세 시에 웰러 씨가 작은 식탁 위에 양고기 구운 다리와 거대한 고기 파이, 거기에 갖가지 채소 접시와 흑맥주 단지 몇 개를 차려 놓자—단지들은 의자며 소파 겸용 침대며 놓일 만한 데마다 놓였다—고기는 바로 옆 감옥 조리장에서 사다 손질한 것이고 파이도 거기서 만들어 구운 것이었음에도, 다들 식사를 제대로 즐길 마음이 생겼다."]], [["To these succeeded a bottle or two of very good wine, for which a messenger was despatched by Mr. Pickwick to the Horn Coffee-house, in Doctors’ Commons.", "여기에 더해 아주 좋은 포도주 한두 병이 곁들여졌는데, 그것을 가지러 픽윅 씨는 닥터스 코먼스에 있는 혼 커피점으로 심부름꾼을 보냈다."], ["The bottle or two, indeed, might be more properly described as a bottle or six, for by the time it was drunk, and tea over, the bell began to ring for strangers to withdraw.", "사실 한두 병이라기보다는 한 댓 병이라 하는 편이 더 정확하겠다. 그 술을 다 비우고 차까지 마실 무렵에는, 면회객들더러 물러가라는 종이 울리기 시작했으니까."]], [["But, if Mr. Winkle’s behaviour had been unaccountable in the morning, it became perfectly unearthly and solemn when, under the influence of his feelings, and his share of the bottle or six, he prepared to take leave of his friend. He lingered behind, until Mr. Tupman and Mr. Snodgrass had disappeared, and then fervently clenched Mr. Pickwick’s hand, with an expression of face in which deep and mighty resolve was fearfully blended with the very concentrated essence of gloom.", "그러나 윈클 씨의 행동이 아침에도 영문 모를 것이었다면, 자기 감정과 한 댓 병 가운데 제 몫의 영향 아래서 친구와 작별하려 할 즈음에는 완전히 섬뜩하고 비장해졌다. 그는 터프먼 씨와 스노드그래스 씨가 사라질 때까지 뒤에 남았다가, 픽윅 씨의 손을 열렬히 움켜쥐었는데, 그 얼굴 표정에는 깊고 거창한 결의가 더없이 응축된 우수의 정수와 무섭게 뒤섞여 있었다."]], [["‘Good-night, my dear Sir!’ said Mr.", "\"안녕히 주무십시오, 선생님!\""], ["Winkle between his set teeth.", "윈클 씨가 이를 악문 채 말했다."]], [["‘Bless you, my dear fellow!’ replied the warm-hearted Mr.", "\"잘 가게, 여보게!\""], ["Pickwick, as he returned the pressure of his young friend’s hand.", "따뜻한 마음의 픽윅 씨가 젊은 친구의 손을 마주 쥐며 답했다."]], [["‘Now then!’ cried Mr.", "\"어이, 이제 그만!\""], ["Tupman from the gallery.", "회랑에서 터프먼 씨가 외쳤다."]], [["‘Yes, yes, directly,’ replied Mr.", "\"그래, 그래, 곧 가네.\" 윈클 씨가 답했다."], ["Winkle. ‘Good-night!’", "\"안녕히 주무십시오!\""]], [["‘Good-night,’ said Mr.", "\"잘 가게.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["There was another good-night, and another, and half a dozen more after that, and still Mr. Winkle had fast hold of his friend’s hand, and was looking into his face with the same strange expression.", "또 한 번의 작별 인사가 오갔고, 또 한 번, 그러고도 대여섯 번이 더 이어졌지만, 윈클 씨는 여전히 친구의 손을 단단히 붙잡은 채, 한결같은 그 묘한 표정으로 그의 얼굴을 들여다보고 있었다."]], [["‘Is anything the matter?’ said Mr.", "\"무슨 일이라도 있나?\""], ["Pickwick at last, when his arm was quite sore with shaking.", "마침내 픽윅 씨가, 흔들어 댄 통에 팔이 아주 욱신거리게 되자 물었다."]], [["‘Nothing,’ said Mr.", "\"아무 일 없습니다.\""], ["Winkle.", "윈클 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Well then, good-night,’ said Mr.", "\"그럼 잘 가게.\""], ["Pickwick, attempting to disengage his hand.", "픽윅 씨가 손을 빼려 애쓰며 말했다."]], [["‘My friend, my benefactor, my honoured companion,’ murmured Mr.", "\"제 벗이시여, 제 은인이시여, 제가 존경하는 동무시여.\""], ["Winkle, catching at his wrist.", "윈클 씨가 그의 손목을 붙들며 중얼거렸다."], ["‘Do not judge me harshly; do not, when you hear that, driven to extremity by hopeless obstacles, I--’", "\"저를 모질게 판단하지 말아 주십시오. 가망 없는 장애에 밀려 막다른 데까지 내몰린 제가, 그러니까 제가 그러했다는 걸 들으시더라도—\""]], [["‘Now then,’ said Mr.", "\"어이, 이제 그만.\""], ["Tupman, reappearing at the door.", "터프먼 씨가 다시 문간에 나타나며 말했다."], ["‘Are you coming, or are we to be locked in?’", "\"올 텐가, 아니면 우리가 갇혀 버려야 하나?\""]], [["‘Yes, yes, I am ready,’ replied Mr.", "\"그래, 그래, 갈 준비됐네.\" 윈클 씨가 답했다."], ["Winkle. And with a violent effort he tore himself away.", "그러고는 격렬하게 안간힘을 쓰며 몸을 뿌리쳤다."]], [["As Mr. Pickwick was gazing down the passage after them in silent astonishment, Sam Weller appeared at the stair-head, and whispered for one moment in Mr. Winkle’s ear.", "픽윅 씨가 말없이 놀란 채 복도 너머로 그들의 뒷모습을 바라보고 있을 때, 샘 웰러가 계단 위쪽에 나타나 한순간 윈클 씨의 귀에 대고 무언가를 속삭였다."]], [["‘Oh, certainly, depend upon me,’ said that gentleman aloud.", "\"아, 물론이지, 나만 믿게.\" 그 신사가 소리 내어 말했다."]], [["‘Thank’ee, sir.", "\"고맙습니다, 나리."], ["You won’t forget, sir?’ said Sam.", "잊지 않으실 거지요, 나리?\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["‘Of course not,’ replied Mr.", "\"물론 잊지 않네.\""], ["Winkle.", "윈클 씨가 답했다."]], [["‘Wish you luck, Sir,’ said Sam, touching his hat.", "\"행운을 빕니다, 나리.\" 샘이 모자에 손을 갖다 대며 말했다."], ["‘I should very much liked to ha’ joined you, Sir; but the gov’nor, o’ course, is paramount.’", "\"저도 정말이지 따라가고 싶었습니다만, 나리, 물론 주인 어른이 먼저지요.\""]], [["‘It is very much to your credit that you remain here,’ said Mr.", "\"자네가 여기 남는다는 건 자네에게 큰 명예일세.\" 윈클 씨가 말했다."], ["Winkle. With these words they disappeared down the stairs.", "이 말과 함께 그들은 계단 아래로 사라졌다."]], [["‘Very extraordinary,’ said Mr.", "\"참으로 별일이군.\""], ["Pickwick, going back into his room, and seating himself at the table in a musing attitude.", "픽윅 씨가 방으로 돌아와 생각에 잠긴 자세로 식탁에 앉으며 말했다."], ["‘What can that young man be going to do?’", "\"저 젊은 친구가 대체 무얼 하려는 걸까?\""]], [["He had sat ruminating about the matter for some time, when the voice of Roker, the turnkey, demanded whether he might come in.", "그가 한동안 그 일을 곱씹으며 앉아 있는데, 간수 로커의 목소리가 들어가도 되겠느냐고 물었다."]], [["‘By all means,’ said Mr.", "\"얼마든지요.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘I’ve brought you a softer pillow, Sir,’ said Mr.", "\"더 부드러운 베개를 가져왔습니다, 나리.\""], ["Roker, ‘instead of the temporary one you had last night.’", "로커 씨가 말했다. \"어젯밤에 쓰신 임시 베개 대신에요.\""]], [["‘Thank you,’ said Mr.", "\"고맙소.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick. ‘Will you take a glass of wine?’", "\"포도주 한 잔 하겠소?\""]], [["‘You’re wery good, Sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"친절도 하십니다, 나리.\""], ["Roker, accepting the proffered glass.", "내미는 잔을 받으며 로커 씨가 답했다."], ["‘Yours, sir.’", "\"나리를 위하여.\""]], [["‘Thank you,’ said Mr.", "\"고맙소.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘I’m sorry to say that your landlord’s wery bad to-night, Sir,’ said Roker, setting down the glass, and inspecting the lining of his hat preparatory to putting it on again.", "\"안된 말이지만, 오늘 밤 나리 집주인이 아주 위독합니다, 나리.\" 로커가 잔을 내려놓고, 다시 쓰려고 모자 안쪽 안감을 살펴보며 말했다."]], [["‘What!", "\"뭐라고요!"], ["The Chancery prisoner!’ exclaimed Mr.", "그 형평법원 죄수 말이오!\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 외쳤다."]], [["‘He won’t be a Chancery prisoner wery long, Sir,’ replied Roker, turning his hat round, so as to get the maker’s name right side upwards, as he looked into it.", "\"그 양반은 형평법원 죄수로 그리 오래 있진 못할 겁니다, 나리.\" 로커가 모자를 빙 돌려, 들여다보면서 제조자 이름이 똑바로 위로 가도록 맞추며 답했다."]], [["‘You make my blood run cold,’ said Mr.", "\"당신 말에 등골이 서늘해지는군요.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["‘What do you mean?’", "\"무슨 뜻이오?\""]], [["‘He’s been consumptive for a long time past,’ said Mr.", "\"그 양반은 오래전부터 폐병을 앓아 왔는데요.\""], ["Roker, ‘and he’s taken wery bad in the breath to-night.", "로커 씨가 말했다. \"오늘 밤 숨쉬기가 아주 나빠졌습니다."], ["The doctor said, six months ago, that nothing but change of air could save him.’", "의사가 여섯 달 전에 말하기를, 공기를 바꾸는 것 말고는 그 양반을 살릴 길이 없다고 했지요.\""]], [["‘Great Heaven!’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"이런 맙소사!\" 픽윅 씨가 외쳤다."], ["Pickwick; ‘has this man been slowly murdered by the law for six months?’", "\"이 사람이 여섯 달 동안 법에 의해 서서히 살해당해 왔단 말이오?\""]], [["‘I don’t know about that,’ replied Roker, weighing the hat by the brim in both hands.", "\"그건 잘 모르겠습니다.\" 로커가 모자챙을 두 손으로 잡고 무게를 가늠하며 답했다."], ["‘I suppose he’d have been took the same, wherever he was.", "\"그 양반은 어디 있었어도 똑같이 됐을 겁니다. 오늘 아침 의무실로 옮겨졌습니다."], ["He went into the infirmary, this morning; the doctor says his strength is to be kept up as much as possible; and the warden’s sent him wine and broth and that, from his own house.", "의사 말로는 되도록 기력을 지켜 주어야 한다더군요. 그래서 교도소장이 자기 집에서 포도주랑 죽이랑 그런 걸 보내 줬습니다."], ["It’s not the warden’s fault, you know, sir.’", "이건 교도소장 탓이 아닙니다, 아시겠지요, 나리.\""]], [["‘Of course not,’ replied Mr.", "\"물론 아니지요.\""], ["Pickwick hastily.", "픽윅 씨가 황급히 답했다."]], [["‘I’m afraid, however,’ said Roker, shaking his head, ‘that it’s all up with him.", "\"하지만 안됐지만.\" 로커가 고개를 가로저으며 말했다."], ["I offered Neddy two six-penn’orths to one upon it just now, but he wouldn’t take it, and quite right.", "\"그 양반은 이제 끝장난 것 같습니다. 방금 네디한테 한쪽에 육 펜스어치 둘을 걸고 내기를 걸었더니, 받질 않더군요."], ["Thank’ee, Sir.", "잘한 일이지요."], ["Good-night, sir.’", "고맙습니다, 나리. 안녕히 주무십시오, 나리.\""]], [["‘Stay,’ said Mr.", "\"잠깐.\""], ["Pickwick earnestly.", "픽윅 씨가 간곡하게 말했다."], ["‘Where is this infirmary?’", "\"그 의무실이 어디요?\""]], [["‘Just over where you slept, sir,’ replied Roker.", "\"나리께서 주무신 바로 위입니다, 나리.\""], ["‘I’ll show you, if you like to come.’ Mr.", "로커가 답했다. \"오시려거든 안내해 드리지요.\""], ["Pickwick snatched up his hat without speaking, and followed at once.", "픽윅 씨는 아무 말 없이 모자를 집어 들고 곧장 뒤따랐다."]], [["The turnkey led the way in silence; and gently raising the latch of the room door, motioned Mr.", "간수는 말없이 앞장섰다. 그러고는 방문 빗장을 가만히 들어 올리며, 픽윅 씨에게 들어가라고 손짓했다."], ["Pickwick to enter. It was a large, bare, desolate room, with a number of stump bedsteads made of iron, on one of which lay stretched the shadow of a man--wan, pale, and ghastly.", "그곳은 넓고 휑하고 황량한 방으로, 철제 막침대가 여러 개 놓여 있었고, 그중 하나에 한 사내의 그림자가 길게 뻗어 누워 있었다."], ["His breathing was hard and thick, and he moaned painfully as it came and went.", "핏기 없고, 창백하고, 송장 같았다. 그의 숨결은 거칠고 탁했으며, 숨이 들고 날 때마다 고통스럽게 신음했다."], ["At the bedside sat a short old man in a cobbler’s apron, who, by the aid of a pair of horn spectacles, was reading from the Bible aloud.", "침대 머리맡에는 구두장이 앞치마를 두른 키 작은 노인이 앉아, 뿔테 안경의 도움을 받아 성경을 소리 내어 읽고 있었다."], ["It was the fortunate legatee.", "그 행운의 유산 상속인이었다."]], [["The sick man laid his hand upon his attendant’s arm, and motioned him to stop.", "병든 사내가 손을 들어 곁을 지키는 이의 팔에 얹고는, 그만 읽으라고 손짓했다."], ["He closed the book, and laid it on the bed.", "그는 책을 덮어 침대 위에 내려놓았다."]], [["‘Open the window,’ said the sick man.", "\"창문을 열어 주게.\" 병든 사내가 말했다."]], [["He did so.", "노인이 그렇게 했다."], ["The noise of carriages and carts, the rattle of wheels, the cries of men and boys, all the busy sounds of a mighty multitude instinct with life and occupation, blended into one deep murmur, floated into the room.", "마차와 짐수레의 소음, 바퀴 덜컹거리는 소리, 사내들과 소년들의 외침, 삶과 일로 약동하는 거대한 군중의 온갖 분주한 소리가 한데 어우러져 깊은 웅성거림이 되어 방 안으로 떠밀려 들어왔다."], ["Above the hoarse loud hum, arose, from time to time, a boisterous laugh; or a scrap of some jingling song, shouted forth, by one of the giddy crowd, would strike upon the ear, for an instant, and then be lost amidst the roar of voices and the tramp of footsteps; the breaking of the billows of the restless sea of life, that rolled heavily on, without.", "그 쉰 듯한 큰 웅성거림 위로 이따금 떠들썩한 웃음소리가 솟아올랐고, 들뜬 군중 가운데 누군가가 외쳐 부르는 어떤 흥얼대는 노랫가락 한 토막이 한순간 귀를 스쳤다가, 목소리들의 포효와 발걸음 소리 속에 묻혀 사라지곤 했다. 바깥에서 묵직하게 굽이쳐 가는, 쉴 줄 모르는 삶의 바다, 그 물결이 부서지는 소리였다."], ["These are melancholy sounds to a quiet listener at any time; but how melancholy to the watcher by the bed of death!", "이런 것들은 언제 들어도 조용한 듣는 이에게 우울한 소리이건만, 죽음의 침상을 지키는 자에게는 그 얼마나 더 우울한가!"]], [["‘There is no air here,’ said the man faintly.", "\"여긴 공기가 없네.\" 사내가 가냘프게 말했다."], ["‘The place pollutes it.", "\"이곳이 공기를 더럽혀."], ["It was fresh round about, when I walked there, years ago; but it grows hot and heavy in passing these walls.", "몇 해 전 저 바깥을 거닐 때만 해도 사방이 신선했지. 그런데 이 담장을 지나오면서 뜨겁고 답답해진다네."], ["I cannot breathe it.’", "도저히 숨을 쉴 수가 없어.\""]], [["‘We have breathed it together, for a long time,’ said the old man.", "\"우리 함께 오래도록 그걸 들이마셔 오지 않았나.\" 노인이 말했다."], ["‘Come, come.’", "\"자, 자, 마음을 가라앉히게.\""]], [["There was a short silence, during which the two spectators approached the bed.", "잠시 침묵이 흘렀고, 그 사이에 두 구경꾼이 침대로 다가갔다."], ["The sick man drew a hand of his old fellow-prisoner towards him, and pressing it affectionately between both his own, retained it in his grasp.", "병든 사내는 늙은 옥살이 동무의 손 하나를 제 쪽으로 끌어당겨, 두 손 사이에 다정히 감싸 쥐고는, 그대로 놓지 않았다."]], [["‘I hope,’ he gasped after a while, so faintly that they bent their ears close over the bed to catch the half-formed sounds his pale lips gave vent to--‘I hope my merciful Judge will bear in mind my heavy punishment on earth.", "\"바라건대.\" 그가 잠시 후 헐떡이며 말했는데, 어찌나 가냘픈지 두 사람은 그 창백한 입술이 토해 내는 어렴풋한 소리를 받아들으려고 침대 위로 귀를 바싹 숙였다."], ["Twenty years, my friend, twenty years in this hideous grave!", "\"바라건대 자비로우신 심판자께서 이 땅에서 내가 받은 무거운 형벌을 헤아려 주시기를."], ["My heart broke when my child died, and I could not even kiss him in his little coffin.", "이십 년일세, 친구, 이 끔찍한 무덤에서 이십 년이야! 내 아이가 죽었을 때 내 가슴은 무너졌고, 그 조그만 관 속의 아이에게 입맞춤조차 할 수 없었네."], ["My loneliness since then, in all this noise and riot, has been very dreadful.", "그 후로 이 온갖 소음과 소동 속에서 느낀 외로움은 참으로 끔찍했지."], ["May God forgive me!", "하느님, 저를 용서하소서!"], ["He has seen my solitary, lingering death.’", "그분은 외롭게, 더디게 죽어 가는 내 모습을 지켜보셨네.\""]], [["He folded his hands, and murmuring something more they could not hear, fell into a sleep--only a sleep at first, for they saw him smile.", "그는 두 손을 모으고, 그들이 알아들을 수 없는 무언가를 더 중얼거리더니, 잠에 빠져들었다. 처음엔 그저 잠일 뿐이었으니, 그가 미소 짓는 것을 그들이 보았기 때문이다."]], [["They whispered together for a little time, and the turnkey, stooping over the pillow, drew hastily back.", "그들은 잠시 함께 소곤거렸고, 간수가 베개 위로 몸을 숙였다가 황급히 물러섰다."], ["‘He has got his discharge, by G--!’ said the man.", "\"하느님 맙소사, 이 양반 석방됐소!\" 사내가 말했다."]], [["He had.", "그는 정말 그러했다."], ["But he had grown so like death in life, that they knew not when he died.", "그러나 살아 있으면서도 너무나 죽음과 닮아 있었던 터라, 그들은 그가 언제 죽었는지조차 알지 못했다."]], [["CHAPTER XLV. DESCRIPTIVE OF AN AFFECTING INTERVIEW BETWEEN MR. SAMUEL WELLER AND A FAMILY PARTY. MR. PICKWICK MAKES A TOUR OF THE DIMINUTIVE WORLD HE INHABITS, AND RESOLVES TO MIX WITH IT, IN FUTURE, AS LITTLE AS POSSIBLE\nA few mornings after his incarceration, Mr. Samuel Weller, having arranged his master’s room with all possible care, and seen him comfortably seated over his books and papers, withdrew to employ himself for an hour or two to come, as he best could. It was a fine morning, and it occurred to Sam that a pint of porter in the open air would lighten his next quarter of an hour or so, as well as any little amusement in which he could indulge.", "새뮤얼 웰러 씨가 수감된 지 며칠 지난 어느 날 아침, 그는 주인의 방을 할 수 있는 한 정성껏 정돈하고, 주인이 책과 서류를 앞에 두고 편안히 자리 잡은 것을 확인한 다음, 앞으로 한두 시간을 제 나름대로 보내려고 물러났다. 화창한 아침이었고, 샘은 한 잔의 포터를 야외에서 마시면 어떤 자잘한 오락 못지않게 다음 십오 분쯤을 가볍게 만들어 주리라는 생각이 들었다."]], [["Having arrived at this conclusion, he betook himself to the tap.", "이런 결론에 다다른 그는 술집 쪽으로 발걸음을 옮겼다."], ["Having purchased the beer, and obtained, moreover, the day-but-one-before- yesterday’s paper, he repaired to the skittle-ground, and seating himself on a bench, proceeded to enjoy himself in a very sedate and methodical manner.", "맥주를 사고, 게다가 그저께 신문까지 한 부 손에 넣은 그는 스키틀 놀이터로 가서 벤치에 앉아 매우 차분하고 체계적인 방식으로 즐기기 시작했다."]], [["First of all, he took a refreshing draught of the beer, and then he looked up at a window, and bestowed a platonic wink on a young lady who was peeling potatoes thereat.", "우선 그는 시원하게 맥주를 한 모금 들이켰고, 그런 다음 창문을 올려다보며 거기서 감자 껍질을 벗기고 있는 젊은 아가씨에게 플라토닉한 윙크를 던졌다."], ["Then he opened the paper, and folded it so as to get the police reports outwards; and this being a vexatious and difficult thing to do, when there is any wind stirring, he took another draught of the beer when he had accomplished it.", "그러고는 신문을 펼쳐 경찰 보도가 바깥쪽으로 오도록 접었는데, 바람이 조금이라도 부는 날에는 이것이 짜증나고 어려운 일인지라, 그 일을 해내고 나서는 맥주를 또 한 모금 들이켰다."], ["Then, he read two lines of the paper, and stopped short to look at a couple of men who were finishing a game at rackets, which, being concluded, he cried out ‘wery good,’ in an approving manner, and looked round upon the spectators, to ascertain whether their sentiments coincided with his own.", "그런 다음 신문을 두 줄 읽다가, 라켓 경기를 마무리하던 두 사내를 보려고 멈춰 섰는데, 경기가 끝나자 그는 흡족한 태도로 \"아주 좋군\" 하고 외치고는, 구경꾼들의 감정이 자신의 것과 일치하는지 확인하려고 둘러보았다."], ["This involved the necessity of looking up at the windows also; and as the young lady was still there, it was an act of common politeness to wink again, and to drink to her good health in dumb show, in another draught of the beer, which Sam did; and having frowned hideously upon a small boy who had noted this latter proceeding with open eyes, he threw one leg over the other, and, holding the newspaper in both hands, began to read in real earnest.", "이것은 또 창문을 올려다보는 일도 수반했고, 젊은 아가씨가 아직 거기 있었으므로, 다시 윙크를 하고 또 한 모금의 맥주로 무언극처럼 그녀의 건강을 위해 건배하는 것은 흔한 예의의 행위였으니, 샘은 그렇게 했다. 그리고 이 마지막 거동을 눈을 동그랗게 뜨고 지켜본 어린 소년에게 무섭게 인상을 찌푸린 뒤, 그는 한쪽 다리를 다른 쪽 위로 척 올리고는, 양손으로 신문을 든 채 진지하게 읽기 시작했다."]], [["He had hardly composed himself into the needful state of abstraction, when he thought he heard his own name proclaimed in some distant passage.", "그가 필요한 만큼의 몰입 상태에 채 가라앉기도 전에, 어딘가 먼 통로에서 자기 이름이 외쳐지는 것을 들은 듯했다."], ["Nor was he mistaken, for it quickly passed from mouth to mouth, and in a few seconds the air teemed with shouts of ‘Weller!’", "잘못 들은 것도 아니었으니, 그 소리는 곧 입에서 입으로 옮아가더니 몇 초 만에 \"웰러!\" 하는 외침이 공기를 가득 메웠다."]], [["Here!’ roared Sam, in a stentorian voice.", "\"여기!\" 샘이 우렁찬 목소리로 고함쳤다."], ["‘Wot’s the matter?", "\"무슨 일이오?"], ["Who wants him?", "누가 그를 찾소?"], ["Has an express come to say that his country house is afire?’", "시골 별장에 불이 났다는 급보라도 왔소?\""]], [["‘Somebody wants you in the hall,’ said a man who was standing by.", "\"홀에서 누가 자네를 찾고 있네.\" 곁에 서 있던 한 사내가 말했다."]], [["‘Just mind that ‘ere paper and the pot, old feller, will you?’ said Sam.", "\"여보게, 저 신문하고 술잔 좀 봐 주겠나?\" 샘이 말했다."], ["‘I’m a-comin’.", "\"나 가는 중일세."], ["Blessed, if they was a-callin’ me to the bar, they couldn’t make more noise about it!’", "제기랄, 나를 법정으로 불러내는 거라 해도 이보다 더 야단법석을 떨진 못하겠군!\""]], [["Accompanying these words with a gentle rap on the head of the young gentleman before noticed, who, unconscious of his close vicinity to the person in request, was screaming ‘Weller!’ with all his might, Sam hastened across the ground, and ran up the steps into the hall.", "이 말과 더불어, 앞서 언급한 젊은 신사 — 정작 찾는 그 인물이 바로 곁에 있는 줄도 모르고 온 힘을 다해 \"웰러!\"를 외쳐 대던 — 의 머리를 가볍게 툭 쳐 주고는, 샘은 마당을 가로질러 서둘러 가서 계단을 뛰어 올라 홀로 들어섰다."], ["Here, the first object that met his eyes was his beloved father sitting on a bottom stair, with his hat in his hand, shouting out ‘Weller!’ in his very loudest tone, at half-minute intervals.", "거기서 그의 눈에 처음 들어온 것은, 사랑하는 아버지가 맨 아래 계단에 모자를 손에 든 채 앉아서 삼십 초 간격으로 가장 큰 목소리로 \"웰러!\"를 외쳐 대고 있는 광경이었다."]], [["‘Wot are you a-roarin’ at?’ said Sam impetuously, when the old gentleman had discharged himself of another shout; ‘making yourself so precious hot that you looks like a aggrawated glass-blower.", "\"뭘 그렇게 고래고래 외쳐 대는 거요?\" 노신사가 또 한 차례 고함을 토해 냈을 때 샘이 발끈하여 말했다."], ["Wot’s the matter?’", "\"그렇게 잔뜩 달아올라서는 꼭 성난 유리 부는 직공처럼 보이오. 무슨 일이오?\""]], [["‘Aha!’ replied the old gentleman, ‘I began to be afeerd that you’d gone for a walk round the Regency Park, Sammy.’", "\"아하!\" 노신사가 대답했다. \"네가 리젠시 공원을 한 바퀴 산책하러 간 게 아닌가 걱정되기 시작하던 참이다, 새미.\""]], [["‘Come,’ said Sam, ‘none o’ them taunts agin the wictim o’ avarice, and come off that ‘ere step.", "\"이봐요,\" 샘이 말했다."], ["Wot are you a-settin’ down there for?", "\"탐욕의 희생자한테 그런 비아냥은 그만두고, 그 계단에서 좀 내려오시오. 거기 왜 앉아 계신 거요?"], ["I don’t live there.’", "내가 거기 사는 것도 아닌데.\""]], [["‘I’ve got such a game for you, Sammy,’ said the elder Mr.", "\"너한테 보여 줄 재미난 게 있단다, 새미.\""], ["Weller, rising.", "웰러 영감이 일어서며 말했다."]], [["‘Stop a minit,’ said Sam, ‘you’re all vite behind.’", "\"잠깐만,\" 샘이 말했다. \"등이 온통 허옇소.\""]], [["‘That’s right, Sammy, rub it off,’ said Mr.", "\"그래, 됐다, 새미, 털어 내거라.\""], ["Weller, as his son dusted him.", "아들이 먼지를 털어 주자 웰러 씨가 말했다."], ["‘It might look personal here, if a man walked about with vitevash on his clothes, eh, Sammy?’", "\"옷에 회반죽을 묻히고 돌아다니면 여기선 좀 사적인 의미로 비칠지도 모르니까 말이다, 안 그러냐, 새미?\""]], [["As Mr. Weller exhibited in this place unequivocal symptoms of an approaching fit of chuckling, Sam interposed to stop it.", "웰러 씨가 이 대목에서 곧 킥킥대는 발작이 닥쳐올 명백한 징후를 드러내자, 샘이 끼어들어 그것을 막았다."]], [["‘Keep quiet, do,’ said Sam, ‘there never vos such a old picter-card born.", "\"가만히 좀 계시오, 정말.\" 샘이 말했다."], ["Wot are you bustin’ vith, now?’", "\"이런 늙은 그림딱지 같은 양반은 세상에 또 없을 거요. 지금 또 뭐 때문에 터지려는 거요?\""]], [["‘Sammy,’ said Mr.", "\"새미,\""], ["Weller, wiping his forehead, ‘I’m afeerd that vun o’ these days I shall laugh myself into a appleplexy, my boy.’", "웰러 씨가 이마를 닦으며 말했다. \"이러다 언젠가는 내가 웃다가 중풍에 걸릴까 걱정이다, 얘야.\""]], [["‘Vell, then, wot do you do it for?’ said Sam.", "\"그럼, 도대체 왜 그러시오?\" 샘이 말했다."], ["‘Now, then, wot have you got to say?’", "\"자, 그래서, 무슨 할 말이 있는 거요?\""]], [["‘Who do you think’s come here with me, Samivel?’ said Mr.", "\"누가 나하고 같이 여기 왔는지 알겠느냐, 새미벨?\""], ["Weller, drawing back a pace or two, pursing up his mouth, and extending his eyebrows.", "웰러 씨가 한두 걸음 물러나 입을 오므리고 눈썹을 치켜올리며 말했다."]], [["‘Pell?’ said Sam.", "\"펠이오?\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["Mr. Weller shook his head, and his red cheeks expanded with the laughter that was endeavouring to find a vent.", "웰러 씨는 고개를 저었고, 그의 붉은 뺨은 터져 나갈 구멍을 찾고 있던 웃음으로 부풀어 올랐다."]], [["‘Mottled-faced man, p’raps?’ asked Sam.", "\"얼룩진 얼굴의 사내인가?\" 샘이 물었다."]], [["Again Mr. Weller shook his head.", "웰러 씨는 다시 고개를 저었다."]], [["‘Who then?’asked Sam.", "\"그럼 누구요?\" 샘이 물었다."]], [["‘Your mother-in-law,’ said Mr.", "\"네 새어머니다.\" 웰러 씨가 말했다."], ["Weller; and it was lucky he did say it, or his cheeks must inevitably have cracked, from their most unnatural distension.", "그가 그 말을 한 것은 다행이었으니, 그러지 않았더라면 더없이 부자연스럽게 부푼 그의 뺨이 영락없이 터지고 말았을 것이다."]], [["‘Your mother-in-law, Sammy,’ said Mr.", "\"네 새어머니다, 새미,\" 웰러 씨가 말했다."], ["Weller, ‘and the red-nosed man, my boy; and the red-nosed man.", "\"그리고 빨간 코의 사내다, 얘야. 빨간 코의 사내 말이다."], ["Ho!", "호!"], ["ho!", "호!"], ["ho!’", "호!\""]], [["With this, Mr. Weller launched into convulsions of laughter, while Sam regarded him with a broad grin gradually over-spreading his whole countenance.", "이 말과 함께 웰러 씨는 발작적인 웃음 속으로 빠져들었고, 샘은 차츰 온 얼굴에 함박웃음이 번져 가는 채로 그를 바라보았다."]], [["‘They’ve come to have a little serious talk with you, Samivel,’ said Mr.", "\"그들이 너하고 진지한 이야기를 좀 하러 온 거다, 새미벨.\""], ["Weller, wiping his eyes.", "웰러 씨가 눈물을 닦으며 말했다."], ["‘Don’t let out nothin’ about the unnat’ral creditor, Sammy.’", "\"그 못된 채권자에 대해선 한마디도 흘리지 마라, 새미.\""]], [["‘Wot, don’t they know who it is?’ inquired Sam.", "\"뭐요, 그게 누군지 모른단 말이오?\" 샘이 물었다."]], [["‘Not a bit on it,’ replied his father.", "\"눈곱만큼도 모르지.\" 아버지가 대답했다."]], [["‘Vere are they?’ said Sam, reciprocating all the old gentleman’s grins.", "\"그들이 어디 있소?\" 샘이 노신사의 온갖 함박웃음을 똑같이 되받으며 말했다."]], [["‘In the snuggery,’ rejoined Mr.", "\"아늑한 방에.\" 웰러 씨가 대꾸했다."], ["Weller. ‘Catch the red-nosed man a-goin’ anyvere but vere the liquors is; not he, Samivel, not he.", "\"빨간 코 사내가 술 있는 데 말고 어디 다른 데 가 있을 리 있겠느냐."], ["Ve’d a wery pleasant ride along the road from the Markis this mornin’, Sammy,’ said Mr.", "천만에, 새미벨, 그럴 리 없지. 오늘 아침 마키스에서 여기까지 길을 따라 꽤나 유쾌하게 달려왔단다, 새미.\""], ["Weller, when he felt himself equal to the task of speaking in an articulate manner.", "웰러 씨는 또박또박 말할 수 있을 만큼 추슬렀다고 느끼자 말했다."], ["‘I drove the old piebald in that ‘ere little chay- cart as belonged to your mother-in-law’s first wenter, into vich a harm- cheer wos lifted for the shepherd; and I’m blessed,’ said Mr.", "\"네 새어머니의 첫 남편 거였던 그 자그마한 이륜마차에 늙은 얼룩말을 매고 몰았는데, 거기에다 목자님을 위해 안락의자를 하나 들어 올려 실었지. 그런데 정말이지,\""], ["Weller, with a look of deep scorn--‘I’m blessed if they didn’t bring a portable flight o’ steps out into the road a-front o’ our door for him, to get up by.’", "웰러 씨가 깊은 경멸의 표정을 지으며 말했다. \"정말이지, 그자가 올라타기 좋으라고 우리 집 문 앞 길거리까지 휴대용 계단을 들고 나오는 게 아니냐 말이다.\""]], [["‘You don’t mean that?’ said Sam.", "\"설마요?\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["‘I do mean that, Sammy,’ replied his father, ‘and I vish you could ha’ seen how tight he held on by the sides wen he did get up, as if he wos afeerd o’ being precipitayted down full six foot, and dashed into a million hatoms. He tumbled in at last, however, and avay ve vent; and I rayther think--I say I rayther think, Samivel--that he found his-self a little jolted ven ve turned the corners.’", "\"정말 그랬다니까, 새미.\" 아버지가 대답했다. \"그자가 막상 올라탈 때, 마치 꼬박 육 피트 아래로 곤두박질쳐서 백만 조각으로 박살날까 봐 두려운 듯이, 양옆을 얼마나 꽉 붙잡던지 네가 봤어야 하는데. 그래도 마침내 굴러떨어지듯 올라탔고, 우리는 출발했지. 그리고 내 생각엔 — 내 말은, 새미벨, 내 생각엔 — 모퉁이를 돌 때마다 그자가 좀 덜컹거렸을 게다.\""]], [["‘Wot, I s’pose you happened to drive up agin a post or two?’ said Sam.", "\"뭐, 기둥 한두 개에 부딪치도록 모셨다는 거요?\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["‘I’m afeerd,’ replied Mr.", "\"그게 걱정이다,\" 웰러 씨가 황홀하게 윙크를 연발하며 대답했다."], ["Weller, in a rapture of winks--‘I’m afeerd I took vun or two on ‘em, Sammy; he wos a-flyin’ out o’ the arm-cheer all the way.’", "\"한두 개쯤 들이받은 것 같아서 말이다, 새미. 그자는 내내 안락의자에서 튕겨 나가고 있었거든.\""]], [["Here the old gentleman shook his head from side to side, and was seized with a hoarse internal rumbling, accompanied with a violent swelling of the countenance, and a sudden increase in the breadth of all his features; symptoms which alarmed his son not a little.", "여기서 노신사는 고개를 좌우로 흔들더니, 얼굴이 격렬하게 부풀고 이목구비가 죄다 별안간 넓어지는 것을 동반한 채, 목구멍 깊은 데서 쉰 우르릉 소리에 사로잡혔는데, 그 징후에 아들은 적잖이 놀랐다."]], [["‘Don’t be frightened, Sammy, don’t be frightened,’ said the old gentleman, when by dint of much struggling, and various convulsive stamps upon the ground, he had recovered his voice.", "\"놀라지 마라, 새미, 놀라지 마.\" 노신사가 한참을 버둥거리고 땅을 발작적으로 몇 번 쾅쾅 구른 끝에 목소리를 되찾고서 말했다."], ["‘It’s only a kind o’ quiet laugh as I’m a-tryin’ to come, Sammy.’", "\"그저 조용한 웃음이란 걸 한번 해 보려는 것뿐이다, 새미.\""]], [["‘Well, if that’s wot it is,’ said Sam, ‘you’d better not try to come it agin.", "\"음, 그런 거라면,\" 샘이 말했다."], ["You’ll find it rayther a dangerous inwention.’", "\"다시는 해 보지 않는 게 좋겠소. 꽤나 위험한 발명품이라는 걸 알게 될 거요.\""]], [["‘Don’t you like it, Sammy?’ inquired the old gentleman.", "\"마음에 안 드냐, 새미?\" 노신사가 물었다."]], [["‘Not at all,’ replied Sam.", "\"전혀요.\" 샘이 대답했다."]], [["‘Well,’ said Mr.", "\"음,\" 웰러 씨가 여전히 뺨에 눈물을 줄줄 흘리며 말했다."], ["Weller, with the tears still running down his cheeks, ‘it ‘ud ha’ been a wery great accommodation to me if I could ha’ done it, and ‘ud ha’ saved a good many vords atween your mother-in-law and me, sometimes; but I’m afeerd you’re right, Sammy, it’s too much in the appleplexy line--a deal too much, Samivel.’", "\"내가 그걸 할 수만 있었더라면 나한테 아주 큰 도움이 됐을 거고, 가끔 네 새어머니하고 나 사이에 오갈 말도 꽤나 아낄 수 있었을 텐데. 하지만 네 말이 맞는 것 같다, 새미, 그건 너무 중풍 쪽에 가깝구나 — 너무 지나치게 그쪽이야, 새미벨.\""]], [["This conversation brought them to the door of the snuggery, into which Sam--pausing for an instant to look over his shoulder, and cast a sly leer at his respected progenitor, who was still giggling behind--at once led the way.", "이 대화로 그들은 아늑한 방 문 앞에 다다랐고, 샘은 — 어깨너머로 돌아보며 여태 뒤에서 킥킥대고 있는 존경하는 아버지에게 짓궂은 곁눈질을 한 번 던지려고 잠시 멈춘 뒤 — 곧장 앞장서 들어갔다."]], [["‘Mother-in-law,’ said Sam, politely saluting the lady, ‘wery much obliged to you for this here wisit.--Shepherd, how air you?’", "\"새어머니,\" 샘이 부인에게 정중히 인사하며 말했다. \"이렇게 와 주셔서 정말 고맙습니다. — 목자님, 안녕하시오?\""]], [["‘Oh, Samuel!’ said Mrs.", "\"오, 새뮤얼!\" 웰러 부인이 말했다."], ["Weller. ‘This is dreadful.’", "\"끔찍하구나.\""]], [["‘Not a bit on it, mum,’ replied Sam.--‘Is it, shepherd?’", "\"전혀 아닙니다, 부인.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"안 그렇소, 목자님?\""]], [["Mr. Stiggins raised his hands, and turned up his eyes, until the whites- -or rather the yellows--were alone visible; but made no reply in words.", "스티긴스 씨는 두 손을 들어 올리고 눈을 치켜떠, 흰자위 — 아니 차라리 누런자위 — 만 보일 지경이었으나, 말로는 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았다."]], [["‘Is this here gen’l’m’n troubled with any painful complaint?’ said Sam, looking to his mother-in-law for explanation.", "\"이 신사분께선 무슨 괴로운 병이라도 앓고 계십니까?\" 샘이 설명을 구하듯 새어머니를 바라보며 말했다."]], [["‘The good man is grieved to see you here, Samuel,’ replied Mrs.", "\"이 선량한 분은 네가 여기 있는 걸 보고 비통해하시는 거다, 새뮤얼.\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 부인이 대답했다."]], [["‘Oh, that’s it, is it?’ said Sam.", "\"오, 그게 그거였구나, 그렇소?\""], ["‘I was afeerd, from his manner, that he might ha’ forgotten to take pepper vith that ‘ere last cowcumber he eat.", "샘이 말했다. \"저분 태도를 보고는, 마지막으로 드신 그 오이에 후추 뿌리는 걸 깜빡하신 게 아닌가 했지요."], ["Set down, Sir, ve make no extra charge for settin’ down, as the king remarked wen he blowed up his ministers.’", "앉으시오, 선생. 앉는다고 추가 요금은 안 받습니다, 왕이 신하들을 닦아세우며 말한 것처럼요.\""]], [["‘Young man,’ said Mr.", "\"젊은이,\""], ["Stiggins ostentatiously, ‘I fear you are not softened by imprisonment.’", "스티긴스 씨가 거드름을 피우며 말했다. \"투옥으로도 자네가 누그러지지 않은 것 같아 걱정이군.\""]], [["‘Beg your pardon, Sir,’ replied Sam; ‘wot wos you graciously pleased to hobserve?’", "\"실례합니다, 선생,\" 샘이 대답했다. \"황송하게도 무어라 분부하셨소?\""]], [["‘I apprehend, young man, that your nature is no softer for this chastening,’ said Mr.", "\"내가 보기에 젊은이, 자네 성정이 이 징벌로도 조금도 부드러워지지 않은 것 같네.\""], ["Stiggins, in a loud voice.", "스티긴스 씨가 큰 소리로 말했다."]], [["‘Sir,’ replied Sam, ‘you’re wery kind to say so.", "\"선생,\" 샘이 대답했다."], ["I hope my natur is NOT a soft vun, Sir.", "\"그렇게 말씀해 주시니 참 친절하시오."], ["Wery much obliged to you for your good opinion, Sir.’", "내 성정이 부드러운 게 아니길 바랍니다, 선생. 좋게 봐 주셔서 정말 고맙소, 선생.\""]], [["At this point of the conversation, a sound, indecorously approaching to a laugh, was heard to proceed from the chair in which the elder Mr. Weller was seated; upon which Mrs.", "대화가 이 대목에 이르렀을 때, 웃음에 위태로울 만큼 가까운 소리가 웰러 영감이 앉아 있던 의자에서 새어 나오는 것이 들렸다."], ["Weller, on a hasty consideration of all the circumstances of the case, considered it her bounden duty to become gradually hysterical.", "그러자 웰러 부인은 사정의 모든 정황을 황급히 헤아린 끝에, 차츰 히스테리에 빠지는 것이 자기의 마땅한 의무라 여겼다."]], [["‘Weller,’ said Mrs. W.", "\"웰러,\""], ["(the old gentleman was seated in a corner); ‘Weller!", "웰러 부인이 말했다(노신사는 구석에 앉아 있었다). \"웰러!"], ["Come forth.’", "나오세요.\""]], [["‘Wery much obleeged to you, my dear,’ replied Mr.", "\"정말 고맙구려, 여보,\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다."], ["Weller; ‘but I’m quite comfortable vere I am.’", "\"하지만 난 여기가 아주 편안하다오.\""]], [["Upon this, Mrs. Weller burst into tears.", "이에 웰러 부인은 울음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["‘Wot’s gone wrong, mum?’ said Sam.", "\"뭐가 잘못된 거요, 부인?\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["‘Oh, Samuel!’ replied Mrs.", "\"오, 새뮤얼!\" 웰러 부인이 대답했다."], ["Weller, ‘your father makes me wretched.", "\"네 아버지가 나를 비참하게 만드는구나."], ["Will nothing do him good?’", "저이한테는 무엇으로도 도움이 안 될까?\""]], [["‘Do you hear this here?’ said Sam.", "\"이 말 들었소?\" 샘이 말했다."], ["‘Lady vants to know vether nothin’ ‘ull do you good.’", "\"부인께서 무엇으로도 당신한테 도움이 안 될지 알고 싶어 하시오.\""]], [["‘Wery much indebted to Mrs. Weller for her po-lite inquiries, Sammy,’ replied the old gentleman.", "\"웰러 부인의 정중한 물음에 참으로 신세를 졌구나, 새미.\""], ["‘I think a pipe vould benefit me a good deal.", "노신사가 대답했다. \"내 생각엔 담배 한 대가 나한테 꽤나 도움이 될 것 같다."], ["Could I be accommodated, Sammy?’", "그걸 좀 누릴 수 있겠느냐, 새미?\""]], [["Here Mrs. Weller let fall some more tears, and Mr. Stiggins groaned.", "여기서 웰러 부인은 또 몇 방울 눈물을 떨어뜨렸고, 스티긴스 씨는 신음했다."]], [["‘Hollo!", "\"이런!"], ["Here’s this unfortunate gen’l’m’n took ill agin,’ said Sam, looking round.", "이 가엾은 신사분이 또 편찮으신가 보군.\" 샘이 둘러보며 말했다."], ["‘Vere do you feel it now, sir?’", "\"이제 어디가 아프시오, 선생?\""]], [["‘In the same place, young man,’ rejoined Mr.", "\"같은 자리라네, 젊은이,\" 스티긴스 씨가 대꾸했다."], ["Stiggins, ‘in the same place.’", "\"같은 자리야.\""]], [["‘Vere may that be, Sir?’ inquired Sam, with great outward simplicity.", "\"그게 어디일까요, 선생?\" 샘이 겉으로는 더없이 순진하게 물었다."]], [["‘In the buzzim, young man,’ replied Mr.", "\"가슴속이라네, 젊은이.\""], ["Stiggins, placing his umbrella on his waistcoat.", "스티긴스 씨가 우산을 조끼에 갖다 대며 대답했다."]], [["At this affecting reply, Mrs. Weller, being wholly unable to suppress her feelings, sobbed aloud, and stated her conviction that the red-nosed man was a saint; whereupon Mr. Weller, senior, ventured to suggest, in an undertone, that he must be the representative of the united parishes of St. Simon Without and St. Walker Within.", "이 가슴 저미는 대답에 웰러 부인은 도무지 감정을 억누를 수 없어 큰 소리로 흐느꼈고, 빨간 코의 사내가 성인(聖人)이라고 확신한다고 말했다. 그러자 웰러 영감은 나직한 소리로, 저자는 바깥 성 시몬 교구와 안쪽 성 워커 교구를 합친 교구의 대표가 틀림없다고 넌지시 끼어들었다."]], [["‘I’m afeered, mum,’ said Sam, ‘that this here gen’l’m’n, with the twist in his countenance, feels rather thirsty, with the melancholy spectacle afore him.", "\"걱정이군요, 부인,\" 샘이 말했다."], ["Is it the case, mum?’", "\"얼굴이 일그러진 이 신사분께서 눈앞의 우울한 광경 탓에 좀 목이 마르신 모양입니다. 그렇습니까, 부인?\""]], [["The worthy lady looked at Mr.", "그 갸륵한 부인은 대답을 구하려고 스티긴스 씨를 쳐다보았다."], ["Stiggins for a reply; that gentleman, with many rollings of the eye, clenched his throat with his right hand, and mimicked the act of swallowing, to intimate that he was athirst.", "그 신사는 눈알을 여러 번 굴리며 오른손으로 목을 움켜쥐고 무언가를 삼키는 시늉을 하여, 자기가 목마르다는 것을 넌지시 알렸다."]], [["‘I am afraid, Samuel, that his feelings have made him so indeed,’ said Mrs.", "\"안타깝게도, 새뮤얼, 정말로 감정 탓에 그리되신 것 같구나.\""], ["Weller mournfully.", "웰러 부인이 애처롭게 말했다."]], [["‘Wot’s your usual tap, sir?’ replied Sam.", "\"평소에 즐기시는 술은 무엇이오, 선생?\" 샘이 대답했다."]], [["‘Oh, my dear young friend,’ replied Mr.", "\"오, 내 친애하는 젊은 벗이여,\" 스티긴스 씨가 대답했다."], ["Stiggins, ‘all taps is vanities!’", "\"온갖 술이란 다 헛된 것이라네!\""]], [["‘Too true, too true, indeed,’ said Mrs.", "\"참으로 옳아요, 참으로 옳아요.\""], ["Weller, murmuring a groan, and shaking her head assentingly.", "웰러 부인이 신음을 중얼거리며 고개를 끄덕여 동의하며 말했다."]], [["‘Well,’ said Sam, ‘I des-say they may be, sir; but wich is your partickler wanity? Wich wanity do you like the flavour on best, sir?’", "\"음,\" 샘이 말했다. \"그럴지도 모르겠습니다, 선생. 하지만 어느 게 선생께서 특히 좋아하시는 헛된 것이오? 어느 헛된 것의 풍미를 제일 좋아하시오, 선생?\""]], [["‘Oh, my dear young friend,’ replied Mr.", "\"오, 내 친애하는 젊은 벗이여,\" 스티긴스 씨가 대답했다."], ["Stiggins, ‘I despise them all.", "\"나는 그 모두를 경멸한다네."], ["If,’ said Mr.", "만약,\""], ["Stiggins--‘if there is any one of them less odious than another, it is the liquor called rum.", "스티긴스 씨가 말했다. \"만약 그중에 다른 것보다 덜 역겨운 게 하나 있다면, 그건 럼이라 부르는 술이지."], ["Warm, my dear young friend, with three lumps of sugar to the tumbler.’", "따뜻하게, 친애하는 젊은 벗이여, 잔마다 설탕 세 덩이를 넣어서 말일세.\""]], [["‘Wery sorry to say, sir,’ said Sam, ‘that they don’t allow that particular wanity to be sold in this here establishment.’", "\"이런 말씀 드리기 정말 안타깝지만, 선생,\" 샘이 말했다. \"이 시설에서는 그 특정한 헛된 것을 파는 걸 허락하지 않습니다.\""]], [["‘Oh, the hardness of heart of these inveterate men!’ ejaculated Mr.", "\"오, 이 고질적인 자들의 모진 마음이여!\" 스티긴스 씨가 외쳤다."], ["Stiggins. ‘Oh, the accursed cruelty of these inhuman persecutors!’", "\"오, 이 비인간적인 박해자들의 저주받을 잔혹함이여!\""]], [["With these words, Mr.", "이 말과 함께 스티긴스 씨는 다시 눈을 치떴고, 우산으로 가슴을 두들겼다."], ["Stiggins again cast up his eyes, and rapped his breast with his umbrella; and it is but justice to the reverend gentleman to say, that his indignation appeared very real and unfeigned indeed.", "그리고 이 목사 양반에게 공정을 기하자면, 그의 분개는 참으로 진실하고 꾸밈없어 보였다고 말해 둘 만하다."]], [["After Mrs. Weller and the red-nosed gentleman had commented on this inhuman usage in a very forcible manner, and had vented a variety of pious and holy execrations against its authors, the latter recommended a bottle of port wine, warmed with a little water, spice, and sugar, as being grateful to the stomach, and savouring less of vanity than many other compounds. It was accordingly ordered to be prepared, and pending its preparation the red-nosed man and Mrs. Weller looked at the elder W. and groaned.", "웰러 부인과 빨간 코 신사가 이 비인간적인 처우에 대해 자못 강력한 어조로 논평하고, 그 장본인들을 향해 갖가지 경건하고 거룩한 저주를 퍼붓고 난 뒤, 후자는 포도주 한 병에 물과 향신료와 설탕을 조금 넣어 데운 것을 권했으니, 위에 좋고 여느 혼합주보다 헛됨의 맛이 덜하다는 것이었다. 그리하여 그것을 준비하라는 분부가 내려졌고, 그것이 마련되는 동안 빨간 코의 사내와 웰러 부인은 웰러 영감을 바라보며 신음했다."]], [["‘Well, Sammy,’ said the gentleman, ‘I hope you’ll find your spirits rose by this here lively wisit.", "\"음, 새미,\" 노신사가 말했다."], ["Wery cheerful and improvin’ conwersation, ain’t it, Sammy?’", "\"이번의 이 유쾌한 방문으로 네 기분이 좀 나아졌으면 좋겠구나. 아주 명랑하고 유익한 대화 아니냐, 새미?\""]], [["‘You’re a reprobate,’ replied Sam; ‘and I desire you won’t address no more o’ them ungraceful remarks to me.’", "\"못된 인간이오.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"그리고 그런 꼴사나운 말을 나한테 더는 하지 마시오.\""]], [["So far from being edified by this very proper reply, the elder Mr. Weller at once relapsed into a broad grin; and this inexorable conduct causing the lady and Mr.", "이 지극히 온당한 대꾸에 교화되기는커녕, 웰러 영감은 곧장 다시 함박웃음에 빠져들었다."], ["Stiggins to close their eyes, and rock themselves to and fro on their chairs, in a troubled manner, he furthermore indulged in several acts of pantomime, indicative of a desire to pummel and wring the nose of the aforesaid Stiggins, the performance of which, appeared to afford him great mental relief. The old gentleman very narrowly escaped detection in one instance; for Mr.", "그리고 이 막무가내의 행동이 부인과 스티긴스 씨로 하여금 눈을 감고 의자에서 괴로운 듯 앞뒤로 몸을 흔들게 하자, 그는 한술 더 떠 앞서 말한 스티긴스의 코를 후려치고 비틀어 주고 싶다는 뜻을 담은 몇 가지 무언극을 연출했는데, 그 연기를 펼치는 것이 그에게 큰 정신적 위안을 주는 듯했다."], ["Stiggins happening to give a start on the arrival of the negus, brought his head in smart contact with the clenched fist with which Mr. Weller had been describing imaginary fireworks in the air, within two inches of his ear, for some minutes.", "노신사는 한번은 하마터면 들킬 뻔했으니, 마침 니거스 술이 도착하자 스티긴스 씨가 흠칫 놀라는 바람에, 웰러 씨가 몇 분 동안 그의 귀에서 두 인치도 안 되는 거리에서 허공에 상상의 불꽃놀이를 그리던 그 움켜쥔 주먹에 머리를 정통으로 부딪쳤기 때문이다."]], [["‘Wot are you a-reachin’ out, your hand for the tumbler in that ‘ere sawage way for?’ said Sam, with great promptitude.", "\"뭘 그렇게 야만스럽게 잔을 향해 손을 뻗는 거요?\" 샘이 잽싸게 말했다."], ["‘Don’t you see you’ve hit the gen’l’m’n?’", "\"신사분을 친 게 안 보이오?\""]], [["‘I didn’t go to do it, Sammy,’ said Mr.", "\"일부러 그런 게 아니다, 새미.\""], ["Weller, in some degree abashed by the very unexpected occurrence of the incident.", "웰러 씨가 그 뜻밖의 사건이 벌어진 데에 다소 무안해하며 말했다."]], [["‘Try an in’ard application, sir,’ said Sam, as the red-nosed gentleman rubbed his head with a rueful visage.", "\"내복용으로 한번 드셔 보시오, 선생.\" 빨간 코 신사가 처량한 얼굴로 머리를 문지르자 샘이 말했다."], ["‘Wot do you think o’ that, for a go o’ wanity, warm, Sir?’", "\"이건 어떻소, 따뜻한 헛된 것 한 잔으로 말이오, 선생?\""]], [["Mr. Stiggins made no verbal answer, but his manner was expressive.", "스티긴스 씨는 말로는 아무 대답도 하지 않았으나, 그 태도가 웅변적이었다."], ["He tasted the contents of the glass which Sam had placed in his hand, put his umbrella on the floor, and tasted it again, passing his hand placidly across his stomach twice or thrice; he then drank the whole at a breath, and smacking his lips, held out the tumbler for more.", "그는 샘이 손에 쥐여 준 잔의 내용물을 맛보고, 우산을 바닥에 내려놓고는 다시 맛보았으며, 두세 번 손으로 배를 차분히 쓰다듬었다. 그러고는 단숨에 전부 들이켜고 입맛을 다신 다음, 더 달라고 잔을 내밀었다."]], [["Nor was Mrs. Weller behind-hand in doing justice to the composition.", "웰러 부인 또한 그 혼합주에 마땅한 예우를 다하는 데 뒤지지 않았다."], ["The good lady began by protesting that she couldn’t touch a drop--then took a small drop--then a large drop--then a great many drops; and her feelings being of the nature of those substances which are powerfully affected by the application of strong waters, she dropped a tear with every drop of negus, and so got on, melting the feelings down, until at length she had arrived at a very pathetic and decent pitch of misery.", "그 갸륵한 부인은 한 방울도 입에 댈 수 없다고 항변하는 것으로 시작하더니 — 그러고는 조금 한 모금 — 다음엔 큰 한 모금 — 그다음엔 아주 여러 모금을 들었다. 그리고 그녀의 감정이란 독한 술이 닿으면 강하게 영향을 받는 그런 물질의 성질을 띤지라, 니거스 한 모금마다 눈물 한 방울을 떨구었고, 그렇게 감정을 녹여 내려가다가 마침내 자못 애처롭고 점잖은 비탄의 경지에 이르렀다."]], [["The elder Mr. Weller observed these signs and tokens with many manifestations of disgust, and when, after a second jug of the same, Mr. Stiggins began to sigh in a dismal manner, he plainly evinced his disapprobation of the whole proceedings, by sundry incoherent ramblings of speech, among which frequent angry repetitions of the word ‘gammon’ were alone distinguishable to the ear.", "웰러 영감은 이런 조짐과 징후를 갖은 혐오의 표시와 함께 지켜보았고, 같은 술을 두 잔째 들이켠 스티긴스 씨가 처량한 모양새로 한숨짓기 시작하자, 그는 갖가지 두서없는 횡설수설로 이 모든 거동에 대한 못마땅함을 분명히 드러냈는데, 그중에서 귀에 알아들을 수 있는 것이라고는 \"허튼소리\"라는 말을 성난 어조로 자주 되뇌는 것뿐이었다."]], [["‘I’ll tell you wot it is, Samivel, my boy,’ whispered the old gentleman into his son’s ear, after a long and steadfast contemplation of his lady and Mr.", "\"내가 무슨 말 하려는지 알겠느냐, 새미벨, 얘야.\" 노신사가 자기 부인과 스티긴스 씨를 오래도록 한결같이 바라본 끝에 아들의 귀에 대고 속삭였다."], ["Stiggins; ‘I think there must be somethin’ wrong in your mother- in-law’s inside, as vell as in that o’ the red-nosed man.’", "\"내 생각엔 네 새어머니의 뱃속에도, 저 빨간 코 사내의 뱃속에도 뭔가 잘못된 게 있는 게 틀림없다.\""]], [["‘Wot do you mean?’ said Sam.", "\"무슨 말이오?\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["‘I mean this here, Sammy,’ replied the old gentleman, ‘that wot they drink, don’t seem no nourishment to ‘em; it all turns to warm water, and comes a-pourin’ out o’ their eyes. ‘Pend upon it, Sammy, it’s a constitootional infirmity.’", "\"내 말은 이거다, 새미,\" 노신사가 대답했다. \"저들이 마시는 게 아무 자양분도 안 되는 것 같단 말이다. 죄다 미지근한 물로 변해서 눈에서 줄줄 쏟아져 나오니 말이야. 틀림없다, 새미, 이건 체질적인 결함이야.\""]], [["Mr. Weller delivered this scientific opinion with many confirmatory frowns and nods; which, Mrs.", "웰러 씨는 이 과학적인 견해를 갖가지 확신에 찬 찌푸림과 끄덕임으로 전했는데, 웰러 부인이 그것을 알아채고는 그것이 자기든 스티긴스 씨든, 아니면 둘 다든 무언가 깎아내리는 의미를 담고 있다고 단정하여, 한없이 더 나빠지려던 참이었다."], ["Weller remarking, and concluding that they bore some disparaging reference either to herself or to Mr. Stiggins, or to both, was on the point of becoming infinitely worse, when Mr.", "바로 그때 스티긴스 씨가 간신히 다리로 일어서더니, 좌중을 위해, 특히 새뮤얼 씨를 위해 교화적인 설교를 펼치기 시작했다."], ["Stiggins, getting on his legs as well as he could, proceeded to deliver an edifying discourse for the benefit of the company, but more especially of Mr. Samuel, whom he adjured in moving terms to be upon his guard in that sink of iniquity into which he was cast; to abstain from all hypocrisy and pride of heart; and to take in all things exact pattern and copy by him (Stiggins), in which case he might calculate on arriving, sooner or later at the comfortable conclusion, that, like him, he was a most estimable and blameless character, and that all his acquaintances and friends were hopelessly abandoned and profligate wretches.", "그는 새뮤얼에게 감동적인 어조로, 그가 던져진 이 죄악의 소굴에서 경계를 게을리하지 말고, 온갖 위선과 마음의 교만을 멀리하며, 만사에 자기(스티긴스)를 정확한 본보기와 모범으로 삼으라고 간곡히 권했으니, 그리하면 자기처럼 그도 머잖아 자기가 더없이 존경할 만하고 흠 없는 인물이며 자기의 모든 지인과 벗들은 가망 없이 타락하고 방탕한 자들이라는 흐뭇한 결론에 이르리라 기대할 수 있다는 것이었다."], ["Which consideration, he said, could not but afford him the liveliest satisfaction.", "그 생각은, 그의 말로는, 자기에게 더없이 생생한 만족을 안겨 주지 않을 수 없다는 것이었다."]], [["He furthermore conjured him to avoid, above all things, the vice of intoxication, which he likened unto the filthy habits of swine, and to those poisonous and baleful drugs which being chewed in the mouth, are said to filch away the memory.", "그는 더 나아가, 무엇보다도 만취의 악덕을 피하라고 그에게 간청했으니, 그것을 돼지의 더러운 습성에, 그리고 입에 씹으면 기억을 슬쩍 앗아 간다는 그 독하고 해로운 약물에 빗대었다."], ["At this point of his discourse, the reverend and red-nosed gentleman became singularly incoherent, and staggering to and fro in the excitement of his eloquence, was fain to catch at the back of a chair to preserve his perpendicular.", "설교의 이 대목에 이르러 빨간 코의 목사 양반은 유난히 두서가 없어졌고, 자기 웅변의 흥분에 휩싸여 이리저리 비틀거리다가, 수직을 유지하려고 의자 등받이를 부득이 붙잡아야 했다."]], [["Mr. Stiggins did not desire his hearers to be upon their guard against those false prophets and wretched mockers of religion, who, without sense to expound its first doctrines, or hearts to feel its first principles, are more dangerous members of society than the common criminal; imposing, as they necessarily do, upon the weakest and worst informed, casting scorn and contempt on what should be held most sacred, and bringing into partial disrepute large bodies of virtuous and well- conducted persons of many excellent sects and persuasions.", "스티긴스 씨는, 종교의 첫 교리를 풀이할 분별도 그 첫 원리를 느낄 마음도 없으면서, 여느 범죄자보다 사회에 더 위험한 자들인 그 거짓 예언자와 종교를 조롱하는 가련한 자들 — 가장 약하고 가장 무지한 이들을 으레 기만하고, 가장 신성하게 여겨야 할 것에 멸시와 경멸을 퍼부으며, 훌륭한 여러 종파와 신조에 속한 덕망 높고 행실 바른 수많은 이들을 부분적인 오명에 빠뜨리는 자들 — 에 대해 청중에게 경계하라고는 하지 않았다."], ["But as he leaned over the back of the chair for a considerable time, and closing one eye, winked a good deal with the other, it is presumed that he thought all this, but kept it to himself.", "다만 그가 한참 동안 의자 등받이에 기대어 한쪽 눈을 감고 다른 쪽 눈으로 한참 윙크를 해 댔으므로, 짐작건대 그는 이 모든 것을 생각하면서도 혼자 마음에만 담아 두었던 듯하다."]], [["During the delivery of the oration, Mrs.", "연설이 펼쳐지는 동안 웰러 부인은 문단이 끝날 때마다 흐느끼며 울었다."], ["Weller sobbed and wept at the end of the paragraphs; while Sam, sitting cross-legged on a chair and resting his arms on the top rail, regarded the speaker with great suavity and blandness of demeanour; occasionally bestowing a look of recognition on the old gentleman, who was delighted at the beginning, and went to sleep about half-way.", "한편 샘은 의자에 책상다리를 하고 앉아 등받이 맨 윗단에 팔을 얹은 채, 더없이 부드럽고 온화한 태도로 연설자를 바라보며, 이따금 노신사에게 알아본다는 눈길을 보내곤 했는데, 그 노신사는 처음엔 좋아하다가 중간쯤에 잠이 들어 버렸다."]], [["‘Brayvo; wery pretty!’ said Sam, when the red-nosed man having finished, pulled his worn gloves on, thereby thrusting his fingers through the broken tops till the knuckles were disclosed to view.", "\"브라보, 아주 멋지오!\" 빨간 코의 사내가 설교를 마치고는 닳아빠진 장갑을 끼면서, 그 바람에 터진 손가락 끝으로 손가락을 밀어 넣어 마디까지 드러내자 샘이 말했다."], ["‘Wery pretty.’", "\"아주 멋지오.\""]], [["‘I hope it may do you good, Samuel,’ said Mrs.", "\"네게 도움이 되기를 바란다, 새뮤얼.\""], ["Weller solemnly.", "웰러 부인이 엄숙하게 말했다."]], [["‘I think it vill, mum,’ replied Sam.", "\"도움이 될 것 같습니다, 부인.\" 샘이 대답했다."]], [["‘I wish I could hope that it would do your father good,’ said Mrs.", "\"네 아버지한테도 도움이 되리라 바랄 수 있으면 좋으련만.\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 부인이 말했다."]], [["‘Thank’ee, my dear,’ said Mr.", "\"고맙구려, 여보.\""], ["Weller, senior.", "웰러 영감이 말했다."], ["‘How do you find yourself arter it, my love?’", "\"그걸 듣고 나니 당신 기분은 좀 어떻소, 내 사랑?\""]], [["‘Scoffer!’ exclaimed Mrs.", "\"조롱꾼!\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 부인이 외쳤다."]], [["‘Benighted man!’ said the Reverend Mr.", "\"미망에 빠진 자여!\""], ["Stiggins.", "스티긴스 목사가 말했다."]], [["‘If I don’t get no better light than that ‘ere moonshine o’ yourn, my worthy creetur,’ said the elder Mr.", "\"내가 당신의 그 달빛 같은 헛소리보다 더 나은 빛을 얻지 못한다면, 갸륵한 양반,\""], ["Weller, ‘it’s wery likely as I shall continey to be a night coach till I’m took off the road altogether.", "웰러 영감이 말했다. \"나는 영영 길에서 쫓겨날 때까지 밤마차 신세를 면치 못할 것 같구려."], ["Now, Mrs. We, if the piebald stands at livery much longer, he’ll stand at nothin’ as we go back, and p’raps that ‘ere harm-cheer ‘ull be tipped over into some hedge or another, with the shepherd in it.’", "자, 웰러 부인, 저 얼룩말이 마구간에 더 오래 서 있으면, 돌아갈 때 한 발짝도 안 움직이려 할 거고, 그러면 저 안락의자가 목자님을 태운 채 어느 산울타리엔가 처박힐지도 모르겠소.\""]], [["At this supposition, the Reverend Mr. Stiggins, in evident consternation, gathered up his hat and umbrella, and proposed an immediate departure, to which Mrs.", "이 추측에 스티긴스 목사는 역력히 당황하여 모자와 우산을 챙겨 들고 즉시 떠나자고 제안했고, 웰러 부인도 이에 동의했다."], ["Weller assented. Sam walked with them to the lodge gate, and took a dutiful leave.", "샘은 정문 수위실까지 그들을 배웅하며 공손하게 작별했다."]], [["‘A-do, Samivel,’ said the old gentleman.", "\"안녕, 새미벨.\" 노신사가 말했다."]], [["‘Wot’s a-do?’ inquired Sammy.", "\"'안녕'이 뭐요?\" 새미가 물었다."]], [["‘Well, good-bye, then,’ said the old gentleman.", "\"음, 그럼, 잘 있거라.\" 노신사가 말했다."]], [["‘Oh, that’s wot you’re aimin’ at, is it?’ said Sam.", "\"오, 그게 하려던 말이오?\" 샘이 말했다."], ["‘Good-bye!’", "\"안녕히 가시오!\""]], [["‘Sammy,’ whispered Mr.", "\"새미,\""], ["Weller, looking cautiously round; ‘my duty to your gov’nor, and tell him if he thinks better o’ this here bis’ness, to com-moonicate vith me.", "웰러 씨가 조심스레 둘러보며 속삭였다. \"네 주인 나리께 안부 전하고, 만약 이번 일을 다시 생각해 보실 마음이 있으시면 나한테 연락하시라고 해라."], ["Me and a cab’net-maker has dewised a plan for gettin’ him out.", "나하고 가구장이 하나가 나리를 빼낼 묘안을 짜냈단다."], ["A pianner, Samivel--a pianner!’ said Mr.", "피아노다, 새미벨 — 피아노!\""], ["Weller, striking his son on the chest with the back of his hand, and falling back a step or two.", "웰러 씨가 손등으로 아들의 가슴을 치고는 한두 걸음 물러서며 말했다."]], [["‘Wot do you mean?’ said Sam.", "\"무슨 말이오?\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["‘A pianner-forty, Samivel,’ rejoined Mr.", "\"피아노포르테 말이다, 새미벨,\" 웰러 씨가 한층 더 비밀스러운 태도로 대꾸했다."], ["Weller, in a still more mysterious manner, ‘as he can have on hire; vun as von’t play, Sammy.’", "\"빌려 올 수 있는 거 말이다. 소리는 안 나는 걸로, 새미.\""]], [["‘And wot ‘ud be the good o’ that?’ said Sam.", "\"그게 무슨 소용이 있소?\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["‘Let him send to my friend, the cabinet-maker, to fetch it back, Sammy,’ replied Mr.", "\"내 친구 가구장이한테 그걸 도로 가져가라고 사람을 보내시라는 거다, 새미.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다."], ["Weller. ‘Are you avake, now?’", "\"이제 알아듣겠느냐?\""]], [["‘No,’ rejoined Sam.", "\"아니오.\" 샘이 대꾸했다."]], [["‘There ain’t no vurks in it,’ whispered his father.", "\"그 안에 아무 장치도 없는 거야.\" 아버지가 속삭였다."], ["‘It ‘ull hold him easy, vith his hat and shoes on, and breathe through the legs, vich his holler.", "\"나리가 모자랑 신발을 신은 채로도 넉넉히 들어가실 거고, 다리 구멍으로 숨을 쉬면 돼. 그게 텅 비어 있거든."], ["Have a passage ready taken for ‘Merriker.", "아메리카로 가는 배편을 미리 잡아 두고."], ["The ‘Merrikin gov’ment will never give him up, ven vunce they find as he’s got money to spend, Sammy. Let the gov’nor stop there, till Mrs.", "아메리카 정부는 나리한테 쓸 돈이 있다는 걸 알기만 하면 결코 넘겨주지 않을 거다, 새미."], ["Bardell’s dead, or Mr. Dodson and Fogg’s hung (wich last ewent I think is the most likely to happen first, Sammy), and then let him come back and write a book about the ‘Merrikins as’ll pay all his expenses and more, if he blows ‘em up enough.’", "바델 부인이 죽거나 도드슨과 포그 씨가 교수형에 처해질 때까지(내 생각엔 이 마지막 일이 먼저 일어날 가능성이 제일 크다만, 새미) 나리를 거기 머물게 하고, 그런 다음 돌아와서 아메리카 사람들에 관한 책을 한 권 쓰시면, 그들을 실컷 헐뜯기만 하면 경비를 다 대고도 남을 게다.\""]], [["Mr. Weller delivered this hurried abstract of his plot with great vehemence of whisper; and then, as if fearful of weakening the effect of the tremendous communication by any further dialogue, he gave the coachman’s salute, and vanished.", "웰러 씨는 이 서두른 음모의 개요를 자못 격렬한 속삭임으로 전했고, 그런 다음 이 어마어마한 통보의 효과를 더 이상의 대화로 약화시킬까 두려운 듯, 마부식 경례를 붙이고는 사라졌다."]], [["Sam had scarcely recovered his usual composure of countenance, which had been greatly disturbed by the secret communication of his respected relative, when Mr. Pickwick accosted him.", "존경하는 친척의 비밀스러운 통보에 적잖이 흐트러졌던 평소의 얼굴빛을 샘이 채 되찾기도 전에, 픽윅 씨가 그에게 말을 걸었다."]], [["‘Sam,’ said that gentleman.", "\"샘.\" 그 신사가 말했다."]], [["‘Sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"네, 나리.\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘I am going for a walk round the prison, and I wish you to attend me.", "\"감옥을 한 바퀴 산책하려는데, 네가 따라와 주었으면 한다."], ["I see a prisoner we know coming this way, Sam,’ said Mr.", "우리가 아는 죄수 하나가 이쪽으로 오는 게 보이는구나, 샘.\""], ["Pickwick, smiling.", "픽윅 씨가 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["‘Wich, Sir?’ inquired Mr.", "\"어느 분 말입니까, 나리?\""], ["Weller; ‘the gen’l’m’n vith the head o’ hair, or the interestin’ captive in the stockin’s?’", "웰러 씨가 물었다. \"머리숱 많은 그 신사분이오, 아니면 양말 차림의 흥미로운 그 포로 말입니까?\""]], [["‘Neither,’ rejoined Mr. Pickwick.", "\"둘 다 아니다.\""], ["‘He is an older friend of yours, Sam.’", "픽윅 씨가 대꾸했다. \"네 더 오랜 친구다, 샘.\""]], [["‘O’ mine, Sir?’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"제 친구요, 나리?\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 외쳤다."]], [["‘You recollect the gentleman very well, I dare say, Sam,’ replied Mr.", "\"분명히 그 신사를 아주 잘 기억할 게다, 샘,\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다."], ["Pickwick, ‘or else you are more unmindful of your old acquaintances than I think you are.", "\"그렇지 않다면 내가 생각하는 것보다 네가 옛 지인에 더 무심한 거겠지."], ["Hush!", "쉿!"], ["not a word, Sam; not a syllable.", "한마디도, 샘. 한 음절도 하지 마라."], ["Here he is.’", "저기 오는구나.\""]], [["As Mr. Pickwick spoke, Jingle walked up.", "픽윅 씨가 말하는 사이, 징글이 다가왔다."], ["He looked less miserable than before, being clad in a half-worn suit of clothes, which, with Mr. Pickwick’s assistance, had been released from the pawnbroker’s.", "그는 픽윅 씨의 도움으로 전당포에서 되찾은, 절반쯤 닳은 옷 한 벌을 입고 있어, 전보다는 덜 비참해 보였다."], ["He wore clean linen too, and had had his hair cut.", "깨끗한 속옷도 입고 머리도 깎았다."], ["He was very pale and thin, however; and as he crept slowly up, leaning on a stick, it was easy to see that he had suffered severely from illness and want, and was still very weak.", "그러나 몹시 창백하고 야위었으며, 지팡이에 기대어 천천히 기어오듯 다가오는 것을 보면, 그가 병과 궁핍으로 혹독하게 고생했고 아직도 매우 쇠약하다는 것을 쉬이 알 수 있었다."], ["He took off his hat as Mr. Pickwick saluted him, and seemed much humbled and abashed at the sight of Sam Weller.", "그는 픽윅 씨가 인사하자 모자를 벗었고, 샘 웰러를 보고는 몹시 풀이 죽고 무안해하는 듯했다."]], [["Following close at his heels, came Mr. Job Trotter, in the catalogue of whose vices, want of faith and attachment to his companion could at all events find no place.", "그의 바로 뒤를 바짝 따라 잡 트로터 씨가 왔는데, 그의 악덕 목록에 동료에 대한 신의와 헌신의 결여만큼은 도무지 자리할 곳이 없었다."], ["He was still ragged and squalid, but his face was not quite so hollow as on his first meeting with Mr. Pickwick, a few days before.", "그는 여전히 누더기를 걸치고 꾀죄죄했으나, 며칠 전 픽윅 씨와 처음 만났을 때만큼 얼굴이 그리 홀쭉하지는 않았다."], ["As he took off his hat to our benevolent old friend, he murmured some broken expressions of gratitude, and muttered something about having been saved from starving.", "그는 우리의 인자한 옛 친구에게 모자를 벗어 들며 띄엄띄엄 감사의 말을 중얼거렸고, 굶어 죽을 뻔한 것을 구원받았다는 둥 무언가를 웅얼거렸다."]], [["‘Well, well,’ said Mr.", "\"됐네, 됐어.\""], ["Pickwick, impatiently interrupting him, ‘you can follow with Sam.", "픽윅 씨가 그의 말을 조급하게 가로막으며 말했다. \"자네는 샘하고 따라오게."], ["I want to speak to you, Mr. Jingle.", "나는 자네와 이야기를 좀 하고 싶네, 징글 군."], ["Can you walk without his arm?’", "그의 팔에 기대지 않고 걸을 수 있겠나?\""]], [["‘Certainly, sir--all ready--not too fast--legs shaky--head queer--round and round--earthquaky sort of feeling--very.’", "\"물론입죠, 선생 — 다 준비됐고 — 너무 빠르지만 않게 — 다리가 후들거리고 — 머리가 이상하고 — 빙글빙글 — 지진이 난 듯한 느낌이 — 아주.\""]], [["‘Here, give me your arm,’ said Mr.", "\"자, 내 팔을 잡게.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘No, no,’ replied Jingle; ‘won’t indeed--rather not.’", "\"아뇨, 아뇨.\" 징글이 대답했다. \"정말 안 됩니다 — 차라리 사양하겠습니다.\""]], [["‘Nonsense,’ said Mr.", "\"쓸데없는 소리.\""], ["Pickwick; ‘lean upon me, I desire, Sir.’", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"내게 기대게, 그러길 바라네, 선생.\""]], [["Seeing that he was confused and agitated, and uncertain what to do, Mr. Pickwick cut the matter short by drawing the invalided stroller’s arm through his, and leading him away, without saying another word about it.", "그가 당황하고 동요하여 어쩔 줄 몰라 하는 것을 보고, 픽윅 씨는 그 병약한 떠돌이의 팔을 자기 팔에 끼워 더는 그 일에 대해 한마디도 하지 않고 데리고 감으로써 문제를 단숨에 매듭지었다."]], [["During the whole of this time the countenance of Mr. Samuel Weller had exhibited an expression of the most overwhelming and absorbing astonishment that the imagination can portray.", "이 모든 시간 내내 새뮤얼 웰러 씨의 얼굴에는 상상으로 그려 낼 수 있는 가장 압도적이고 넋을 빼앗는 경악의 표정이 떠올라 있었다."], ["After looking from Job to Jingle, and from Jingle to Job in profound silence, he softly ejaculated the words, ‘Well, I am damn’d!’ which he repeated at least a score of times; after which exertion, he appeared wholly bereft of speech, and again cast his eyes, first upon the one and then upon the other, in mute perplexity and bewilderment.", "깊은 침묵 속에 잡에게서 징글로, 징글에게서 잡으로 눈길을 옮긴 끝에, 그는 나직이 \"이런, 기가 막혀 죽겠네!\"라는 말을 내뱉었는데, 그 말을 적어도 스무 번은 되뇌었다. 그러고 나서는 완전히 말문이 막힌 듯, 다시 한쪽에서 다른 쪽으로 눈길을 던지며 말없이 어리둥절해하고 어쩔 줄 몰라 했다."]], [["‘Now, Sam!’ said Mr.", "\"자, 샘!\""], ["Pickwick, looking back.", "픽윅 씨가 뒤돌아보며 말했다."]], [["‘I’m a-comin’, sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"가는 중입니다, 나리.\""], ["Weller, mechanically following his master; and still he lifted not his eyes from Mr.", "웰러 씨가 기계적으로 주인을 따르며 대답했다."], ["Job Trotter, who walked at his side in silence.", "그러면서도 여전히 자기 곁에서 말없이 걷고 있는 잡 트로터 씨에게서 눈을 떼지 못했다."]], [["Job kept his eyes fixed on the ground for some time.", "잡은 한동안 눈을 땅에 박고 있었다."], ["Sam, with his glued to Job’s countenance, ran up against the people who were walking about, and fell over little children, and stumbled against steps and railings, without appearing at all sensible of it, until Job, looking stealthily up, said--", "잡의 얼굴에 눈을 붙박은 샘은, 주위를 거니는 사람들과 부딪치고, 어린아이들에게 발이 걸려 넘어지고, 계단과 난간에 비틀거리면서도 도무지 그것을 의식하지 못하는 듯하더니, 마침내 잡이 슬그머니 눈을 들어 말했다 —"]], [["‘How do you do, Mr. Weller?’", "\"안녕하시오, 웰러 씨?\""]], [["‘It is him!’ exclaimed Sam; and having established Job’s identity beyond all doubt, he smote his leg, and vented his feelings in a long, shrill whistle.", "\"그자가 맞군!\" 샘이 외쳤다. 그리고 잡의 신원을 추호의 의심도 없이 확인하고서, 그는 제 다리를 철썩 치며 길고 날카로운 휘파람으로 감정을 토해 냈다."]], [["‘Things has altered with me, sir,’ said Job.", "\"내 처지가 달라졌소, 선생.\" 잡이 말했다."]], [["‘I should think they had,’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"그런 것 같군.\""], ["Weller, surveying his companion’s rags with undisguised wonder.", "웰러 씨가 동료의 누더기를 노골적인 경탄으로 훑어보며 외쳤다."], ["‘This is rayther a change for the worse, Mr. Trotter, as the gen’l’m’n said, wen he got two doubtful shillin’s and sixpenn’orth o’ pocket-pieces for a good half-crown.’", "\"이거 꽤나 나쁜 쪽으로 바뀐 거구려, 트로터 씨, 멀쩡한 반 크라운을 주고 미심쩍은 실링 두 닢에 육 펜스어치 가짜 동전을 받은 신사가 말한 것처럼 말이오.\""]], [["‘It is indeed,’ replied Job, shaking his head.", "\"정말 그렇소.\" 잡이 고개를 저으며 대답했다."], ["‘There is no deception now, Mr.", "\"이젠 속임수가 없소, 웰러 씨."], ["Weller.", "눈물이란,\""], ["Tears,’ said Job, with a look of momentary slyness-- ‘tears are not the only proofs of distress, nor the best ones.’", "잡이 잠깐 교활한 기색을 띠며 말했다. \"눈물이란 고통의 유일한 증거도, 최선의 증거도 아니지요.\""]], [["‘No, they ain’t,’ replied Sam expressively.", "\"그렇소, 아니고말고.\" 샘이 의미심장하게 대답했다."]], [["‘They may be put on, Mr.", "\"눈물은 꾸며 낼 수도 있소, 웰러 씨.\""], ["Weller,’ said Job.", "잡이 말했다."]], [["‘I know they may,’ said Sam; ‘some people, indeed, has ‘em always ready laid on, and can pull out the plug wenever they likes.’", "\"그럴 수 있다는 거 나도 알지요.\" 샘이 말했다. \"실은 어떤 자들은 늘 눈물을 준비해 두었다가, 마음 내킬 때마다 마개를 뽑아낼 수 있더군요.\""]], [["‘Yes,’ replied Job; ‘but these sort of things are not so easily counterfeited, Mr.", "\"그렇소.\" 잡이 대답했다."], ["Weller, and it is a more painful process to get them up.’ As he spoke, he pointed to his sallow, sunken cheeks, and, drawing up his coat sleeve, disclosed an arm which looked as if the bone could be broken at a touch, so sharp and brittle did it appear, beneath its thin covering of flesh.", "\"하지만 이런 것들은 그리 쉽게 꾸며 낼 수 없고, 그걸 만들어 내려면 더 고통스러운 과정을 거쳐야 하지요.\" 그렇게 말하며 그는 자기의 누렇고 움푹 꺼진 뺨을 가리키고는, 외투 소매를 걷어 올려 한쪽 팔을 드러냈는데, 그 팔은 얇은 살가죽 아래로 어찌나 앙상하고 부서질 듯해 보이던지, 손만 대도 뼈가 부러질 것 같았다."]], [["‘Wot have you been a-doin’ to yourself?’ said Sam, recoiling.", "\"대체 자네 몸에 무슨 짓을 한 거요?\" 샘이 흠칫 물러서며 말했다."]], [["‘Nothing,’ replied Job.", "\"아무것도 안 했소.\" 잡이 대답했다."]], [["‘Nothin’!’ echoed Sam.", "\"아무것도!\" 샘이 따라 외쳤다."]], [["‘I have been doin’ nothing for many weeks past,’ said Job; and eating and drinking almost as little.’", "\"여러 주째 아무것도 안 했소.\" 잡이 말했다. \"먹고 마시는 것도 거의 그만큼 안 했고요.\""]], [["Sam took one comprehensive glance at Mr. Trotter’s thin face and wretched apparel; and then, seizing him by the arm, commenced dragging him away with great violence.", "샘은 트로터 씨의 야윈 얼굴과 비참한 행색을 한 번에 죽 훑어보더니, 그의 팔을 붙잡고는 자못 거칠게 끌고 가기 시작했다."]], [["‘Where are you going, Mr.", "\"어디로 가는 거요, 웰러 씨?\""], ["Weller?’ said Job, vainly struggling in the powerful grasp of his old enemy.", "잡이 옛 적수의 억센 손아귀에서 부질없이 발버둥치며 말했다."]], [["‘Come on,’ said Sam; ‘come on!’ He deigned no further explanation till they reached the tap, and then called for a pot of porter, which was speedily produced.", "\"따라오시오,\" 샘이 말했다. \"따라와요!\" 그는 술집에 다다를 때까지 더 이상의 설명을 해 주지 않았고, 그러고는 포터 한 잔을 청하니 곧 나왔다."]], [["‘Now,’ said Sam, ‘drink that up, ev’ry drop on it, and then turn the pot upside down, to let me see as you’ve took the medicine.’", "\"자,\" 샘이 말했다. \"그걸 다 마시시오, 한 방울도 남기지 말고. 그런 다음 잔을 거꾸로 뒤집어, 약을 다 먹었는지 내게 보여 주시오.\""]], [["‘But, my dear Mr.", "\"하지만, 친애하는 웰러 씨.\""], ["Weller,’ remonstrated Job.", "잡이 항변했다."]], [["‘Down vith it!’ said Sam peremptorily.", "\"쭉 들이켜요!\" 샘이 단호하게 말했다."]], [["Thus admonished, Mr. Trotter raised the pot to his lips, and, by gentle and almost imperceptible degrees, tilted it into the air.", "이렇게 다그침을 받자 트로터 씨는 잔을 입술로 가져가, 부드럽고 거의 알아챌 수 없는 정도로 차츰 허공으로 기울였다."], ["He paused once, and only once, to draw a long breath, but without raising his face from the vessel, which, in a few moments thereafter, he held out at arm’s length, bottom upward.", "그는 단 한 번, 오직 한 번 긴 숨을 들이쉬려고 멈추었으나, 잔에서 얼굴을 떼지는 않았고, 그러고 나서 잠시 뒤 잔을 팔 길이만큼 쭉 뻗어 바닥이 위로 가도록 들어 보였다."], ["Nothing fell upon the ground but a few particles of froth, which slowly detached themselves from the rim, and trickled lazily down.", "바닥에 떨어진 것이라곤, 천천히 잔 가장자리에서 떨어져 나와 게으르게 흘러내리는 거품 몇 알갱이뿐이었다."]], [["‘Well done!’ said Sam.", "\"잘했소!\" 샘이 말했다."], ["‘How do you find yourself arter it?’", "\"그러고 나니 기분이 좀 어떻소?\""]], [["‘Better, Sir.", "\"낫소, 선생."], ["I think I am better,’ responded Job.", "나아진 것 같소.\" 잡이 대답했다."]], [["‘O’ course you air,’ said Sam argumentatively.", "\"당연히 그렇겠지요.\" 샘이 따지듯 말했다."], ["‘It’s like puttin’ gas in a balloon.", "\"기구에 가스를 넣는 거랑 같소."], ["I can see with the naked eye that you gets stouter under the operation.", "그 작업으로 당신이 더 통통해지는 걸 맨눈으로도 알겠소."], ["Wot do you say to another o’ the same dimensions?’", "같은 크기로 한 잔 더 어떻소?\""]], [["‘I would rather not, I am much obliged to you, Sir,’ replied Job--‘much rather not.’", "\"사양하겠소, 정말 고맙소, 선생.\" 잡이 대답했다. \"정말이지 사양하겠소.\""]], [["‘Vell, then, wot do you say to some wittles?’ inquired Sam.", "\"그럼, 먹을 거라도 어떻소?\" 샘이 물었다."]], [["‘Thanks to your worthy governor, Sir,’ said Mr.", "\"당신의 갸륵한 주인 나리 덕분에, 선생,\""], ["Trotter, ‘we have half a leg of mutton, baked, at a quarter before three, with the potatoes under it to save boiling.’", "트로터 씨가 말했다. \"우리는 양 다리 반 짝을 구워서 세 시 십오 분 전에 먹는데, 삶는 수고를 덜려고 그 밑에 감자를 깔아 두지요.\""]], [["‘Wot!", "\"뭐!"], ["Has he been a-purwidin’ for you?’ asked Sam emphatically.", "그분이 당신들을 먹여 살리고 있었단 말이오?\" 샘이 힘주어 물었다."]], [["‘He has, Sir,’ replied Job.", "\"그렇소, 선생.\" 잡이 대답했다."], ["‘More than that, Mr. Weller; my master being very ill, he got us a room--we were in a kennel before--and paid for it, Sir; and come to look at us, at night, when nobody should know.", "\"그뿐 아니라, 웰러 씨, 내 주인이 몹시 편찮으셔서, 그분이 우리에게 방을 하나 마련해 주셨소 — 전엔 개집 같은 데 있었는데 말이오 — 그 방값을 치러 주시고, 아무도 모를 밤에 우리를 보러 오셨소."], ["Mr.", "웰러 씨,\""], ["Weller,’ said Job, with real tears in his eyes, for once, ‘I could serve that gentleman till I fell down dead at his feet.’", "잡이 처음으로 진짜 눈물을 눈에 글썽이며 말했다. \"나는 그 신사분 발치에 쓰러져 죽을 때까지라도 그분을 섬길 수 있소.\""]], [["‘I say!’ said Sam, ‘I’ll trouble you, my friend!", "\"이봐요!\" 샘이 말했다."], ["None o’ that!’", "\"미안하지만, 이 친구야! 그런 소리 마시오!\""]], [["Job Trotter looked amazed.", "잡 트로터는 어리둥절한 표정을 지었다."]], [["‘None o’ that, I say, young feller,’ repeated Sam firmly.", "\"그런 소리 말라니까, 젊은 친구.\" 샘이 단호히 되풀이했다."], ["‘No man serves him but me.", "\"나 말고는 아무도 그분을 섬기지 못하오."], ["And now we’re upon it, I’ll let you into another secret besides that,’ said Sam, as he paid for the beer.", "그리고 이왕 말이 나온 김에, 그것 말고 또 다른 비밀 하나를 알려 주리다.\" 샘이 맥줏값을 치르며 말했다."], ["‘I never heerd, mind you, or read of in story-books, nor see in picters, any angel in tights and gaiters--not even in spectacles, as I remember, though that may ha’ been done for anythin’ I know to the contrairey--but mark my vords, Job Trotter, he’s a reg’lar thoroughbred angel for all that; and let me see the man as wenturs to tell me he knows a better vun.’ With this defiance, Mr.", "\"내 분명히 말하건대, 나는 타이츠와 각반을 차림한 천사 따위는 들어 본 적도, 이야기책에서 읽은 적도, 그림에서 본 적도 없소 — 내 기억으론 안경을 낀 천사조차 없었지만, 혹 어딘가에 그런 게 있었을지도 모르니 단정은 못 하겠소 — 그렇지만 내 말 잘 들으시오, 잡 트로터, 그분은 그럼에도 불구하고 어엿한 순종 천사요. 그분보다 더 나은 천사를 안다고 감히 내게 말할 자가 있거든 어디 한번 데려와 보시오.\""], ["Weller buttoned up his change in a side pocket, and, with many confirmatory nods and gestures by the way, proceeded in search of the subject of discourse.", "이 도전과 함께 웰러 씨는 거스름돈을 옆주머니에 챙겨 넣고는, 길을 가면서 갖가지 확신에 찬 끄덕임과 몸짓을 곁들이며, 화제의 주인공을 찾아 나섰다."]], [["They found Mr.", "그들은 픽윅 씨가 징글과 함께 자못 진지하게 이야기를 나누며, 라켓 놀이터에 모여 있는 무리들에게는 눈길 한 번 주지 않고 있는 것을 발견했다."], ["Pickwick, in company with Jingle, talking very earnestly, and not bestowing a look on the groups who were congregated on the racket-ground; they were very motley groups too, and worth the looking at, if it were only in idle curiosity.", "그 무리들 또한 매우 잡다하여, 그저 한가한 호기심에서라도 볼 만한 가치가 있었다."]], [["‘Well,’ said Mr.", "\"음,\""], ["Pickwick, as Sam and his companion drew nigh, ‘you will see how your health becomes, and think about it meanwhile.", "샘과 그의 동료가 가까이 다가오자 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"자네 건강이 어떻게 되는지 지켜보고, 그동안 그 일에 대해 생각해 보게."], ["Make the statement out for me when you feel yourself equal to the task, and I will discuss the subject with you when I have considered it.", "자네가 할 만하다고 느낄 때 내게 진술서를 작성해 주면, 나도 그것을 숙고한 뒤에 그 문제를 자네와 논의하겠네."], ["Now, go to your room.", "자, 이제 방으로 가게."], ["You are tired, and not strong enough to be out long.’", "자네는 지쳤고, 오래 밖에 있을 만큼 튼튼하지 못하니.\""]], [["Mr. Alfred Jingle, without one spark of his old animation--with nothing even of the dismal gaiety which he had assumed when Mr. Pickwick first stumbled on him in his misery--bowed low without speaking, and, motioning to Job not to follow him just yet, crept slowly away.", "앨프리드 징글 씨는, 옛 활기라곤 한 점도 없이 — 픽윅 씨가 처음 그의 비참한 처지에 맞닥뜨렸을 때 그가 꾸며 보였던 그 음울한 명랑함조차 없이 — 말없이 깊이 허리를 숙이고는, 잡에게 아직 따라오지 말라고 손짓하며, 천천히 기어가듯 물러갔다."]], [["‘Curious scene this, is it not, Sam?’ said Mr.", "\"묘한 광경이지, 그렇지 않으냐, 샘?\""], ["Pickwick, looking good- humouredly round.", "픽윅 씨가 흐뭇한 듯 둘러보며 말했다."]], [["‘Wery much so, Sir,’ replied Sam.", "\"아주 그렇습니다, 나리.\" 샘이 대답했다."], ["‘Wonders ‘ull never cease,’ added Sam, speaking to himself.", "\"놀라운 일은 끝이 없군.\" 샘이 혼잣말로 덧붙였다."], ["‘I’m wery much mistaken if that ‘ere Jingle worn’t a-doin somethin’ in the water-cart way!’", "\"저 징글이 물뿌리개 수레질 비슷한 짓을 하고 있었던 게 아니라면 내가 단단히 잘못 본 거지!\""]], [["The area formed by the wall in that part of the Fleet in which Mr.", "* 물뿌리개 수레질: 우는 척하며 눈물을 흘리는 것을 빗댄 샘의 농. 픽윅 씨가 서 있던 플리트 감옥의 그 구역에서 담장으로 둘러싸인 공간은 마침 좋은 라켓 코트가 될 만큼 널찍했다."], ["Pickwick stood was just wide enough to make a good racket-court; one side being formed, of course, by the wall itself, and the other by that portion of the prison which looked (or rather would have looked, but for the wall) towards St. Paul’s Cathedral.", "한쪽은 물론 담장 자체로 이루어졌고, 다른 한쪽은 세인트폴 대성당 쪽을 바라보는(아니, 차라리 담장만 없었더라면 바라보았을) 감옥의 그 부분으로 이루어져 있었다."], ["Sauntering or sitting about, in every possible attitude of listless idleness, were a great number of debtors, the major part of whom were waiting in prison until their day of ‘going up’ before the Insolvent Court should arrive; while others had been remanded for various terms, which they were idling away as they best could.", "무기력한 권태의 온갖 자세로 어슬렁거리거나 앉아 있는 채무자들이 수두룩했는데, 그 대부분은 파산 법정에 '출두할' 날이 오기를 감옥에서 기다리는 자들이었고, 나머지는 여러 기간 동안 재구류되어 그 시간을 제 나름대로 빈둥거리며 흘려보내고 있었다."], ["Some were shabby, some were smart, many dirty, a few clean; but there they all lounged, and loitered, and slunk about with as little spirit or purpose as the beasts in a menagerie.", "어떤 이는 초라했고 어떤 이는 멀끔했으며, 많은 이가 더럽고 몇몇은 깨끗했다. 그러나 그들은 모두 거기서, 짐승 우리 속의 짐승들만큼이나 기운도 목적도 없이, 빈둥대고 어슬렁대고 슬그머니 돌아다니고 있었다."]], [["Lolling from the windows which commanded a view of this promenade were a number of persons, some in noisy conversation with their acquaintance below, others playing at ball with some adventurous throwers outside, others looking on at the racket-players, or watching the boys as they cried the game.", "이 산책로가 내려다보이는 창문에서 몸을 내밀고 있는 이들도 여럿이었으니, 어떤 이는 아래쪽 지인들과 시끌벅적 이야기를 나누고, 어떤 이는 바깥에서 과감하게 공을 던지는 자들과 공놀이를 하고, 어떤 이는 라켓 치는 자들을 구경하거나 점수를 외치는 소년들을 지켜보고 있었다."], ["Dirty, slipshod women passed and repassed, on their way to the cooking-house in one corner of the yard; children screamed, and fought, and played together, in another; the tumbling of the skittles, and the shouts of the players, mingled perpetually with these and a hundred other sounds; and all was noise and tumult--save in a little miserable shed a few yards off, where lay, all quiet and ghastly, the body of the Chancery prisoner who had died the night before, awaiting the mockery of an inquest.", "더럽고 추레한 차림의 여인들이 마당 한구석의 취사장으로 오가며 지나치고 또 지나쳤다. 또 다른 구석에서는 아이들이 비명을 지르고, 싸우고, 함께 뛰놀았다."], ["The body!", "스키틀이 쓰러지는 소리와 노는 자들의 고함이 이것들, 그리고 백 가지 다른 소리들과 끊임없이 뒤섞였다."], ["It is the lawyer’s term for the restless, whirling mass of cares and anxieties, affections, hopes, and griefs, that make up the living man.", "모든 것이 소음과 소란이었다 — 다만 몇 야드 떨어진 어느 초라한 작은 헛간만은 예외였으니, 거기에는 전날 밤에 죽은 형평법원 죄수의 시신이 온통 고요하고 섬뜩하게 누운 채, 검시라는 조롱극을 기다리고 있었다. 시신!"], ["The law had his body; and there it lay, clothed in grave-clothes, an awful witness to its tender mercy.", "그것은, 살아 있는 사람을 이루는 그 쉴 새 없이 소용돌이치는 근심과 불안, 애정과 희망과 슬픔의 덩어리를 가리키는 법률가의 용어다. 법은 그의 시신을 차지했고, 그것은 거기, 수의를 입은 채 누워, 그 법의 자비로운 인정에 대한 끔찍한 증인이 되어 있었다."]], [["‘Would you like to see a whistling-shop, Sir?’ inquired Job Trotter.", "\"휘파람 가게를 한번 보시겠습니까, 나리?\" 잡 트로터가 물었다."]], [["‘What do you mean?’ was Mr.", "\"그게 무슨 말인가?\""], ["Pickwick’s counter inquiry.", "픽윅 씨가 되물었다."]], [["‘A vistlin’ shop, Sir,’ interposed Mr.", "\"휘파람 가게입니다, 나리.\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 끼어들었다."]], [["‘What is that, Sam?--A bird-fancier’s?’ inquired Mr.", "\"그게 뭐냐, 샘? 새 장수냐?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘Bless your heart, no, Sir,’ replied Job; ‘a whistling-shop, Sir, is where they sell spirits.’ Mr.", "\"천만에요, 나리.\" 잡이 대답했다."], ["Job Trotter briefly explained here, that all persons, being prohibited under heavy penalties from conveying spirits into debtors’ prisons, and such commodities being highly prized by the ladies and gentlemen confined therein, it had occurred to some speculative turnkey to connive, for certain lucrative considerations, at two or three prisoners retailing the favourite article of gin, for their own profit and advantage.", "\"휘파람 가게란, 나리, 술을 파는 곳입죠.\" 여기서 잡 트로터 씨는, 모든 사람이 무거운 벌칙 아래 채무자 감옥으로 술을 들여오는 것이 금지되어 있고, 그런 물건이 갇혀 있는 신사 숙녀들에게 매우 귀히 여겨지는지라, 어느 약삭빠른 간수가 일정한 짭짤한 대가를 받고, 죄수 두세 명이 자기 이득과 잇속을 위해 인기 품목인 진을 소매로 파는 것을 눈감아 주기에 이르렀다고 간략히 설명했다."]], [["‘This plan, you see, Sir, has been gradually introduced into all the prisons for debt,’ said Mr.", "\"이 방식이, 보시다시피, 나리, 채무자 감옥 전체에 차츰 도입되었습죠.\""], ["Trotter.", "트로터 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘And it has this wery great advantage,’ said Sam, ‘that the turnkeys takes wery good care to seize hold o’ ev’rybody but them as pays ‘em, that attempts the willainy, and wen it gets in the papers they’re applauded for their wigilance; so it cuts two ways--frightens other people from the trade, and elewates their own characters.’", "\"게다가 이건 아주 큰 이점이 있지요.\" 샘이 말했다. \"간수들이, 그 못된 짓을 시도하는 자들 가운데 자기들한테 돈 바치는 자만 빼고 모조리 잡아들이는 데 아주 정성을 쏟는다는 거요. 그래서 그게 신문에 나면 그자들은 경계심이 투철하다고 칭송을 받지요. 그러니 일거양득이오 — 다른 자들이 그 장사를 못 하게 겁주고, 제 평판은 높이는 거지요.\""]], [["‘Exactly so, Mr.", "\"바로 그렇소, 웰러 씨.\""], ["Weller,’ observed Job.", "잡이 말했다."]], [["‘Well, but are these rooms never searched to ascertain whether any spirits are concealed in them?’ said Mr.", "\"한데, 이 방들은 술이 숨겨져 있는지 알아보려고 수색당하는 일이 결코 없는가?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Cert’nly they are, Sir,’ replied Sam; ‘but the turnkeys knows beforehand, and gives the word to the wistlers, and you may wistle for it wen you go to look.’", "\"물론 수색당하지요, 나리.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"하지만 간수들이 미리 알고 휘파람장이들한테 귀띔을 해 주니, 막상 찾으러 가면 헛물만 켜게 되지요.\""]], [["By this time, Job had tapped at a door, which was opened by a gentleman with an uncombed head, who bolted it after them when they had walked in, and grinned; upon which Job grinned, and Sam also; whereupon Mr.", "이쯤에서 잡이 어느 문을 두드리자, 헝클어진 머리의 신사가 문을 열었고, 그들이 안으로 걸어 들어가자 뒤에서 빗장을 지르고는 씩 웃었다. 그러자 잡도 씩 웃었고, 샘도 마찬가지였다."], ["Pickwick, thinking it might be expected of him, kept on smiling to the end of the interview.", "이에 픽윅 씨도, 자기에게도 그런 것이 기대될지 모른다는 생각에, 면담이 끝날 때까지 줄곧 미소를 짓고 있었다."]], [["The gentleman with the uncombed head appeared quite satisfied with this mute announcement of their business, and, producing a flat stone bottle, which might hold about a couple of quarts, from beneath his bedstead, filled out three glasses of gin, which Job Trotter and Sam disposed of in a most workmanlike manner.", "헝클어진 머리의 신사는 그들의 용건을 알리는 이 무언의 통고에 꽤나 만족한 듯, 침대틀 밑에서 두 쿼트쯤 들어갈 만한 납작한 돌병을 꺼내더니 진 세 잔을 따랐고, 잡 트로터와 샘은 그것을 더없이 능숙한 솜씨로 해치웠다."]], [["‘Any more?’ said the whistling gentleman.", "\"더 드릴까요?\" 휘파람장이 신사가 말했다."]], [["‘No more,’ replied Job Trotter.", "\"그만하면 됐소.\" 잡 트로터가 대답했다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick paid, the door was unbolted, and out they came; the uncombed gentleman bestowing a friendly nod upon Mr.", "픽윅 씨가 값을 치르자 문의 빗장이 풀렸고, 그들은 밖으로 나왔다."], ["Roker, who happened to be passing at the moment.", "헝클어진 머리의 신사는 마침 그때 지나가던 로커 씨에게 다정하게 고개를 끄덕여 보였다."]], [["From this spot, Mr. Pickwick wandered along all the galleries, up and down all the staircases, and once again round the whole area of the yard.", "이 자리에서 픽윅 씨는 모든 복도를 따라 거닐었고, 모든 계단을 오르내렸으며, 다시 한 번 마당 전체를 한 바퀴 돌았다."], ["The great body of the prison population appeared to be Mivins, and Smangle, and the parson, and the butcher, and the leg, over and over, and over again.", "감옥 인구의 대다수는 미빈스와 스맹글, 그리고 목사와 푸주한과 사기꾼이, 몇 번이고 거듭거듭 되풀이되는 듯했다."], ["There were the same squalor, the same turmoil and noise, the same general characteristics, in every corner; in the best and the worst alike.", "어느 구석에서나, 가장 좋은 곳에서나 가장 나쁜 곳에서나 똑같이, 같은 추레함, 같은 소란과 소음, 같은 일반적인 특징들이 있었다."], ["The whole place seemed restless and troubled; and the people were crowding and flitting to and fro, like the shadows in an uneasy dream.", "그곳 전체가 쉴 새 없고 어수선해 보였고, 사람들은 불안한 꿈속의 그림자들처럼 이리저리 북적이며 어른거렸다."]], [["‘I have seen enough,’ said Mr.", "\"이만하면 충분히 보았다.\""], ["Pickwick, as he threw himself into a chair in his little apartment.", "픽윅 씨가 자기의 작은 방에서 의자에 몸을 던지며 말했다."], ["‘My head aches with these scenes, and my heart too.", "\"이런 광경들로 머리가 아프고, 가슴도 아프구나."], ["Henceforth I will be a prisoner in my own room.’", "이제부터 나는 내 방 안의 죄수가 되겠다.\""]], [["And Mr. Pickwick steadfastly adhered to this determination.", "그리고 픽윅 씨는 이 결심을 굳건히 지켰다."], ["For three long months he remained shut up, all day; only stealing out at night to breathe the air, when the greater part of his fellow-prisoners were in bed or carousing in their rooms.", "석 달이라는 긴 세월 동안 그는 하루 종일 틀어박혀 지냈고, 다만 동료 죄수들 대부분이 잠자리에 들거나 제 방에서 흥청대고 있을 밤에만 공기를 쐬러 몰래 빠져나왔다."], ["His health was beginning to suffer from the closeness of the confinement, but neither the often-repeated entreaties of Perker and his friends, nor the still more frequently- repeated warnings and admonitions of Mr. Samuel Weller, could induce him to alter one jot of his inflexible resolution.", "갇힌 채 지내는 답답함에 그의 건강이 상하기 시작했으나, 퍼커와 그의 친구들이 거듭거듭 간청해도, 또 새뮤얼 웰러 씨가 그보다 더 자주 거듭한 경고와 충고도, 그의 굽힐 줄 모르는 결심을 털끝만큼도 바꾸게 하지는 못했다."]], [["CHAPTER XLVI. RECORDS A TOUCHING ACT OF DELICATE FEELING, NOT UNMIXED WITH PLEASANTRY, ACHIEVED AND PERFORMED BY Messrs.", "7월이 다 저물기 한 주 전쯤, 번호는 기록되지 않은 어느 삯마차 한 대가 고스웰 거리를 빠른 속도로 올라가는 것이 보였다."], ["DODSON AND FOGG\nIt was within a week of the close of the month of July, that a hackney cabriolet, number unrecorded, was seen to proceed at a rapid pace up Goswell Street; three people were squeezed into it besides the driver, who sat in his own particular little dickey at the side; over the apron were hung two shawls, belonging to two small vixenish-looking ladies under the apron; between whom, compressed into a very small compass, was stowed away, a gentleman of heavy and subdued demeanour, who, whenever he ventured to make an observation, was snapped up short by one of the vixenish ladies before-mentioned.", "마차에는 마부 외에도 세 사람이 끼여 타고 있었는데, 마부는 옆쪽에 따로 마련된 자기만의 작은 마부석에 앉아 있었다. 가리개 위로는 두 장의 숄이 걸쳐져 있었고, 그것은 가리개 아래에 앉은 사납게 생긴 두 자그마한 여인의 것이었다."], ["Lastly, the two vixenish ladies and the heavy gentleman were giving the driver contradictory directions, all tending to the one point, that he should stop at Mrs.", "그 두 여인 사이에는 무겁고 풀죽은 태도의 한 신사가 아주 비좁은 공간에 꾸겨넣어져 있었는데, 그는 어쩌다 한마디 입을 떼어 보려 할 때마다 앞서 말한 사나운 여인들 가운데 하나에게 단번에 말허리를 잘렸다."], ["Bardell’s door; which the heavy gentleman, in direct opposition to, and defiance of, the vixenish ladies, contended was a green door and not a yellow one.", "마지막으로, 그 사나운 두 여인과 무거운 신사는 마부에게 서로 모순되는 지시들을 내리고 있었으나, 결국 모두 한 가지로 귀결되었으니, 바델 부인의 집 문 앞에 세우라는 것이었다. 다만 무거운 신사는 사나운 여인들과 정면으로 맞서며, 그것이 노란 문이 아니라 초록 문이라고 우겼다."]], [["‘Stop at the house with a green door, driver,’ said the heavy gentleman.", "\"마부 양반, 초록 문 있는 집에 세워 주시오.\" 무거운 신사가 말했다."]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["You perwerse creetur!’ exclaimed one of the vixenish ladies.", "이 못된 양반아!\" 사나운 여인들 가운데 하나가 외쳤다."], ["‘Drive to the ‘ouse with the yellow door, cabmin.’", "\"노란 문 있는 집으로 몰아요, 마부 양반.\""]], [["Upon this the cabman, who in a sudden effort to pull up at the house with the green door, had pulled the horse up so high that he nearly pulled him backward into the cabriolet, let the animal’s fore-legs down to the ground again, and paused.", "이 말에 마부는, 초록 문 있는 집 앞에 급히 세우려고 한껏 고삐를 잡아챈 나머지 말을 거의 마차 안으로 자빠뜨릴 뻔했다가, 다시 말의 앞다리를 땅바닥에 내려놓고는 멈춰 섰다."]], [["‘Now vere am I to pull up?’ inquired the driver.", "\"그래, 어디다 세우란 말이오?\" 마부가 물었다."], ["‘Settle it among yourselves.", "\"자기들끼리 정하시구려."], ["All I ask is, vere?’", "내가 묻는 건 그저, 어디냐 그거요?\""]], [["Here the contest was renewed with increased violence; and the horse being troubled with a fly on his nose, the cabman humanely employed his leisure in lashing him about on the head, on the counter-irritation principle.", "여기서 다툼은 한층 더 격렬하게 다시 불붙었고, 말은 코에 앉은 파리 때문에 성가셔하니, 마부는 인정 깊게도 그 짬을 이용해, 자극을 다른 데로 돌린다는 원리에 따라 말의 머리를 채찍으로 후려갈겼다."]], [["‘Most wotes carries the day!’ said one of the vixenish ladies at length.", "\"표가 많은 쪽이 이기는 거지!\" 마침내 사나운 여인들 가운데 하나가 말했다."], ["‘The ‘ouse with the yellow door, cabman.’", "\"노란 문 있는 집이에요, 마부 양반.\""]], [["But after the cabriolet had dashed up, in splendid style, to the house with the yellow door, ‘making,’ as one of the vixenish ladies triumphantly said, ‘acterrally more noise than if one had come in one’s own carriage,’ and after the driver had dismounted to assist the ladies in getting out, the small round head of Master Thomas Bardell was thrust out of the one-pair window of a house with a red door, a few numbers off.", "그러나 삯마차가 노란 문 있는 집 앞으로, 사나운 여인들 가운데 하나가 의기양양하게 말한 대로 \"마치 자기 마차를 타고 온 것보다도 더 요란한 소리를 내며\" 멋들어지게 달려가 멈춘 뒤, 마부가 여인들이 내리는 것을 거들려고 마부석에서 내려온 다음에야, 몇 집 떨어진 빨간 문 있는 집의 이 층 창밖으로 토머스 바델 도련님의 작고 둥근 머리가 쑥 내밀어졌다."]], [["‘Aggrawatin’ thing!’ said the vixenish lady last-mentioned, darting a withering glance at the heavy gentleman.", "\"기막힐 노릇이네!\" 마지막으로 언급된 사나운 여인이 무거운 신사에게 쏘아붙이는 눈길을 던지며 말했다."]], [["‘My dear, it’s not my fault,’ said the gentleman.", "\"여보, 내 잘못이 아니잖소.\" 신사가 말했다."]], [["‘Don’t talk to me, you creetur, don’t,’ retorted the lady.", "\"나한테 말 걸지 마요, 이 양반아, 걸지 말라고요.\" 여인이 되받았다."], ["‘The house with the red door, cabmin.", "\"노란 문이 아니라 빨간 문 있는 집이에요, 마부 양반."], ["Oh!", "오!"], ["If ever a woman was troubled with a ruffinly creetur, that takes a pride and a pleasure in disgracing his wife on every possible occasion afore strangers, I am that woman!’", "세상에 어떤 여자가 이런 망나니 같은 양반 때문에 속을 썩었을까, 틈만 나면 낯선 사람들 앞에서 제 마누라 망신 주는 걸 자랑이자 낙으로 아는 양반이라니, 그게 바로 나라고요!\""]], [["‘You ought to be ashamed of yourself, Raddle,’ said the other little woman, who was no other than Mrs.", "\"부끄러운 줄 알아야지, 래들 양반.\""], ["Cluppins.", "다른 자그마한 여인이 말했는데, 그는 다름 아닌 클러핀스 부인이었다."]], [["‘What have I been a-doing of?’ asked Mr.", "\"내가 뭘 했다고 그러오?\""], ["Raddle.", "래들 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘Don’t talk to me, don’t, you brute, for fear I should be perwoked to forgit my sect and strike you!’ said Mrs.", "\"나한테 말 걸지 마요, 걸지 말라니까, 이 짐승 같은 양반아, 내가 성질을 못 이겨 여자 체면도 잊고 당신을 치기라도 할까 봐 그래요!\""], ["Raddle.", "래들 부인이 말했다."]], [["While this dialogue was going on, the driver was most ignominiously leading the horse, by the bridle, up to the house with the red door, which Master Bardell had already opened.", "이 대화가 오가는 동안, 마부는 더없이 창피스러운 모양새로 말굴레를 잡아끌며 빨간 문 있는 집으로 데려갔는데, 그 문은 이미 바델 도련님이 열어 놓은 터였다."], ["Here was a mean and low way of arriving at a friend’s house!", "친구 집에 도착하는 데 이 얼마나 초라하고 격 떨어지는 방식이란 말인가!"], ["No dashing up, with all the fire and fury of the animal; no jumping down of the driver; no loud knocking at the door; no opening of the apron with a crash at the very last moment, for fear of the ladies sitting in a draught; and then the man handing the shawls out, afterwards, as if he were a private coachman!", "말의 온갖 기세와 사나움을 안고 멋지게 달려와 멈추는 일도 없고, 마부가 펄쩍 뛰어내리는 일도 없고, 요란하게 문을 두드리는 일도 없고, 행여 여인들이 외풍을 맞을세라 맨 마지막 순간에 가리개를 와락 젖히는 일도 없고, 그런 다음 마부가 마치 전속 마부라도 되는 양 숄을 건네주는 일도 없었다!"], ["The whole edge of the thing had been taken off--it was flatter than walking.", "멋이라는 멋은 죄다 깎여 나갔으니 — 차라리 걸어오느니만도 못한 밋밋한 도착이었다."]], [["‘Well, Tommy,’ said Mrs.", "\"자, 토미야,\""], ["Cluppins, ‘how’s your poor dear mother?’", "클러핀스 부인이 말했다. \"네 가엾은 엄마는 어떠시니?\""]], [["‘Oh, she’s very well,’ replied Master Bardell.", "\"아, 아주 잘 계세요.\" 바델 도련님이 대답했다."], ["‘She’s in the front parlour, all ready.", "\"앞쪽 응접실에 다 차려 놓고 계세요."], ["I’m ready too, I am.’ Here Master Bardell put his hands in his pockets, and jumped off and on the bottom step of the door.", "나도 준비됐어요, 나도요.\" 여기서 바델 도련님은 두 손을 주머니에 찔러 넣고 문간 맨 아랫계단을 폴짝폴짝 오르내렸다."]], [["‘Is anybody else a-goin’, Tommy?’ said Mrs.", "\"또 누가 가니, 토미야?\""], ["Cluppins, arranging her pelerine.", "클러핀스 부인이 어깨걸이를 매만지며 말했다."]], [["‘Mrs.", "\"샌더스 부인이 가요, 그분이요.\""], ["Sanders is going, she is,’ replied Tommy; ‘I’m going too, I am.’", "토미가 대답했다. \"나도 가요, 나도요.\""]], [["‘Drat the boy,’ said little Mrs.", "\"이 녀석 좀 봐.\" 자그마한 클러핀스 부인이 말했다."], ["Cluppins. ‘He thinks of nobody but himself.", "\"제 생각밖에 안 하네."], ["Here, Tommy, dear.’", "이리 와 보렴, 토미야.\""]], [["‘Well,’ said Master Bardell.", "\"왜요?\" 바델 도련님이 말했다."]], [["‘Who else is a-goin’, lovey?’ said Mrs.", "\"또 누가 가니, 얘야?\""], ["Cluppins, in an insinuating manner.", "클러핀스 부인이 살살 구슬리는 투로 물었다."]], [["‘Oh! Mrs.", "\"아!"], ["Rogers is a-goin’,’ replied Master Bardell, opening his eyes very wide as he delivered the intelligence.", "로저스 부인도 가요.\" 바델 도련님이 그 소식을 전하며 눈을 휘둥그레 뜨고 대답했다."]], [["‘What?", "\"뭐?"], ["The lady as has taken the lodgings!’ ejaculated Mrs.", "세든 그 부인 말이냐!\""], ["Cluppins.", "클러핀스 부인이 소리쳤다."]], [["Master Bardell put his hands deeper down into his pockets, and nodded exactly thirty-five times, to imply that it was the lady-lodger, and no other.", "바델 도련님은 두 손을 주머니에 더 깊숙이 찔러 넣고는, 그게 바로 세 들어 사는 그 부인이지 다른 사람이 아니라는 뜻으로, 정확히 서른다섯 번 고개를 끄덕였다."]], [["‘Bless us!’ said Mrs.", "\"어머나!\" 클러핀스 부인이 말했다."], ["Cluppins. ‘It’s quite a party!’", "\"이거 아주 잔치로구나!\""]], [["‘Ah, if you knew what was in the cupboard, you’d say so,’ replied Master Bardell.", "\"아, 찬장 안에 뭐가 있는지 아주머니가 안다면 그렇게 말할걸요.\" 바델 도련님이 대답했다."]], [["‘What is there, Tommy?’ said Mrs.", "\"뭐가 있는데, 토미야?\""], ["Cluppins coaxingly.", "클러핀스 부인이 어르듯이 말했다."], ["‘You’ll tell me, Tommy, I know.’", "\"나한테는 말해 주겠지, 토미야, 그렇지?\""]], [["No, I won’t,’ replied Master Bardell, shaking his head, and applying himself to the bottom step again.", "\"아니요, 안 할래요.\" 바델 도련님이 고개를 저으며 대답하고는 다시 아랫계단으로 매달렸다."]], [["‘Drat the child!’ muttered Mrs.", "\"이 녀석 좀 봐!\" 클러핀스 부인이 중얼거렸다."], ["Cluppins. ‘What a prowokin’ little wretch it is!", "\"어찌 이리 약 올리는 망나니람!"], ["Come, Tommy, tell your dear Cluppy.’", "자, 토미야, 너의 다정한 클러피 아주머니한테 말해 주렴.\""]], [["‘Mother said I wasn’t to,’ rejoined Master Bardell, ‘I’m a-goin’ to have some, I am.’ Cheered by this prospect, the precocious boy applied himself to his infantile treadmill, with increased vigour.", "\"엄마가 말하지 말랬어요.\" 바델 도련님이 받아쳤다. \"나도 좀 먹을 거예요, 나도요.\" 그런 전망에 신이 난 그 조숙한 소년은 한층 더 힘차게 제 어린애다운 발판 오르내리기에 몰두했다."]], [["The above examination of a child of tender years took place while Mr. and Mrs. Raddle and the cab-driver were having an altercation concerning the fare, which, terminating at this point in favour of the cabman, Mrs. Raddle came up tottering.", "이렇듯 어린아이를 상대로 캐묻는 일이 벌어지는 동안, 래들 씨 부부와 마부는 삯을 두고 옥신각신하고 있었는데, 그 다툼이 마부 쪽에 유리하게 마무리되자 래들 부인이 비틀거리며 다가왔다."]], [["‘Lauk, Mary Ann!", "\"어머, 메리 앤! 무슨 일이야?\""], ["what’s the matter?’ said Mrs. Cluppins.", "클러핀스 부인이 말했다."]], [["‘It’s put me all over in such a tremble, Betsy,’ replied Mrs.", "\"온몸이 다 이렇게 떨려, 벳시.\""], ["Raddle.", "래들 부인이 대답했다."], ["‘Raddle ain’t like a man; he leaves everythink to me.’", "\"래들은 사내답지가 못해. 뭐든지 다 나한테 떠넘긴다니까.\""]], [["This was scarcely fair upon the unfortunate Mr. Raddle, who had been thrust aside by his good lady in the commencement of the dispute, and peremptorily commanded to hold his tongue.", "이는 불운한 래들 씨에게 도무지 공정한 처사가 못 되었으니, 그는 다툼이 시작되자마자 그 착하신 부인에게 옆으로 떠밀려, 입 닥치고 있으라는 단호한 명을 받았던 터였다."], ["He had no opportunity of defending himself, however, for Mrs. Raddle gave unequivocal signs of fainting; which, being perceived from the parlour window, Mrs.", "그러나 그는 변명할 기회조차 얻지 못했으니, 래들 부인이 영락없이 기절할 조짐을 보였기 때문이다."], ["Bardell, Mrs. Sanders, the lodger, and the lodger’s servant, darted precipitately out, and conveyed her into the house, all talking at the same time, and giving utterance to various expressions of pity and condolence, as if she were one of the most suffering mortals on earth.", "그 광경을 응접실 창에서 본 바델 부인과 샌더스 부인, 세 든 부인과 그 부인의 하녀가 부리나케 뛰쳐나와, 마치 그녀가 이 세상에서 가장 고통받는 사람이기라도 한 양 저마다 동시에 떠들어 대며 갖은 동정과 위로의 말을 쏟아 내면서 그녀를 집 안으로 옮겼다."], ["Being conveyed into the front parlour, she was there deposited on a sofa; and the lady from the first floor running up to the first floor, returned with a bottle of sal-volatile, which, holding Mrs. Raddle tight round the neck, she applied in all womanly kindness and pity to her nose, until that lady with many plunges and struggles was fain to declare herself decidedly better.", "앞쪽 응접실로 옮겨진 그녀는 거기 소파에 뉘어졌고, 이 층에 사는 부인이 이 층으로 달려 올라가더니 각성제 한 병을 들고 돌아와, 래들 부인의 목을 단단히 끌어안은 채 더없이 여성스러운 친절과 연민으로 그것을 그녀의 코에 갖다 댔으니, 마침내 그 부인은 몇 번이나 몸을 들썩이고 버둥거린 끝에 자기가 확실히 좀 나아졌다고 인정하지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["‘Ah, poor thing!’ said Mrs.", "\"아, 가엾어라!\" 로저스 부인이 말했다."], ["Rogers, ‘I know what her feelin’s is, too well.’", "\"저분 심정이 어떨지 나도 너무 잘 알아요.\""]], [["Ah, poor thing!", "\"아, 가엾어라!"], ["so do I,’ said Mrs.", "나도 알지요.\""], ["Sanders; and then all the ladies moaned in unison, and said they knew what it was, and they pitied her from their hearts, they did.", "샌더스 부인이 말했다. 그러자 모든 부인이 한목소리로 신음하며, 그게 어떤 심정인지 자기들도 안다고, 진심으로 그녀를 가엾게 여긴다고, 정말로 그렇다고 말했다."], ["Even the lodger’s little servant, who was thirteen years old and three feet high, murmured her sympathy.", "열세 살에 키가 석 자밖에 안 되는 세 든 부인의 어린 하녀조차 동정의 말을 중얼거렸다."]], [["‘But what’s been the matter?’ said Mrs.", "\"그런데 대체 무슨 일이 있었던 거예요?\""], ["Bardell.", "바델 부인이 말했다."]], [["‘Ah, what has decomposed you, ma’am?’ inquired Mrs.", "\"아, 부인, 뭐가 부인을 이리 흐트러뜨려 놓았나요?\""], ["Rogers.", "로저스 부인이 물었다."]], [["‘I have been a good deal flurried,’ replied Mrs.", "\"제가 몹시 마음이 산란했답니다.\""], ["Raddle, in a reproachful manner.", "래들 부인이 나무라는 투로 대답했다."], ["Thereupon the ladies cast indignant glances at Mr. Raddle.", "그러자 부인들은 래들 씨에게 분개한 눈길을 던졌다."]], [["‘Why, the fact is,’ said that unhappy gentleman, stepping forward, ‘when we alighted at this door, a dispute arose with the driver of the cabrioily--’ A loud scream from his wife, at the mention of this word, rendered all further explanation inaudible.", "\"실은 말이죠,\" 그 불행한 신사가 앞으로 나서며 말했다. \"이 집 앞에서 우리가 내릴 때, 그 삯마차 모는 양반하고 다툼이 일어나서—\" 이 말이 나오자마자 그의 아내가 큰 소리로 비명을 지르는 바람에 그 뒤의 설명은 도무지 들리지 않게 되었다."]], [["‘You’d better leave us to bring her round, Raddle,’ said Mrs.", "\"래들 양반, 우리한테 맡기고 자리를 비워 주는 게 좋겠어요.\" 클러핀스 부인이 말했다."], ["Cluppins. ‘She’ll never get better as long as you’re here.’", "\"당신이 여기 있는 한 절대 나아지질 않을 테니까요.\""]], [["All the ladies concurred in this opinion; so Mr. Raddle was pushed out of the room, and requested to give himself an airing in the back yard.", "부인들 모두 이 의견에 동의했고, 그리하여 래들 씨는 방에서 떠밀려 나가 뒷마당에서 바람이나 쐬고 오라는 요청을 받았다."], ["Which he did for about a quarter of an hour, when Mrs. Bardell announced to him with a solemn face that he might come in now, but that he must be very careful how he behaved towards his wife.", "그는 십오 분쯤 그렇게 했는데, 그 무렵 바델 부인이 엄숙한 얼굴로, 이제 들어와도 되지만 아내를 대할 때는 행동을 아주 조심해야 한다고 일러 주었다."], ["She knew he didn’t mean to be unkind; but Mary Ann was very far from strong, and, if he didn’t take care, he might lose her when he least expected it, which would be a very dreadful reflection for him afterwards; and so on.", "그가 불친절하게 굴려는 게 아닌 줄은 안다, 하지만 메리 앤은 결코 튼튼한 사람이 못 되니, 조심하지 않으면 전혀 뜻하지 않은 때에 그녀를 잃을 수도 있고, 그러면 두고두고 끔찍한 회한으로 남을 거라는 둥 그런 말이었다."], ["All this, Mr. Raddle heard with great submission, and presently returned to the parlour in a most lamb-like manner.", "이 모든 말을 래들 씨는 더없이 고분고분하게 듣고는, 곧 더없이 양처럼 순한 태도로 응접실로 돌아왔다."]], [["‘Why, Mrs. Rogers, ma’am,’ said Mrs.", "\"어머, 로저스 부인,\""], ["Bardell, ‘you’ve never been introduced, I declare!", "바델 부인이 말했다. \"정말이지 아직 인사도 못 나누셨네요!"], ["Mr. Raddle, ma’am; Mrs.", "이쪽은 래들 씨예요, 부인."], ["Cluppins, ma’am; Mrs.", "이쪽은 클러핀스 부인이고요."], ["Raddle, ma’am.’", "이쪽은 래들 부인이에요, 부인.\""]], [["‘Which is Mrs. Cluppins’s sister,’ suggested Mrs.", "\"클러핀스 부인의 언니 되시는 분이지요.\""], ["Sanders.", "샌더스 부인이 덧붙였다."]], [["‘Oh, indeed!’ said Mrs.", "\"아, 그러시군요!\""], ["Rogers graciously; for she was the lodger, and her servant was in waiting, so she was more gracious than intimate, in right of her position.", "로저스 부인이 점잖게 말했다. 그도 그럴 것이 그녀는 세 든 부인이었고 그녀의 하녀까지 대기하고 있었으니, 자기 처지에 걸맞게 친근하기보다는 점잖은 편이었다."], ["‘Oh, indeed!’", "\"아, 그러시군요!\""]], [["Mrs. Raddle smiled sweetly, Mr. Raddle bowed, and Mrs. Cluppins said, ‘she was sure she was very happy to have an opportunity of being known to a lady which she had heerd so much in favour of, as Mrs. Rogers.’ A compliment which the last-named lady acknowledged with graceful condescension.", "래들 부인은 상냥하게 미소 지었고, 래들 씨는 고개를 숙여 인사했으며, 클러핀스 부인은 \"로저스 부인처럼 그토록 좋은 평판을 많이 들어 온 분과 알고 지낼 기회를 얻게 되어 참으로 기쁘다\"고 말했다. 마지막에 언급된 그 부인은 우아하게 겸양을 베풀며 그 인사치레를 받아들였다."]], [["‘Well, Mr. Raddle,’ said Mrs.", "\"자, 래들 양반,\""], ["Bardell; ‘I’m sure you ought to feel very much honoured at you and Tommy being the only gentlemen to escort so many ladies all the way to the Spaniards, at Hampstead.", "바델 부인이 말했다. \"당신과 토미가 이렇게 많은 부인들을 햄프스테드의 스패니어즈까지 죽 모셔 가는 유일한 신사분들이니, 정말 무척 영광스럽게 여기셔야 할 거예요."], ["Don’t you think he ought, Mrs. Rogers, ma’am?’", "그렇지 않나요, 로저스 부인?\""]], [["Oh, certainly, ma’am,’ replied Mrs.", "\"아, 그럼요, 부인.\""], ["Rogers; after whom all the other ladies responded, ‘Oh, certainly.’", "로저스 부인이 대답했고, 그 뒤를 따라 다른 부인들도 모두 \"아, 그럼요\" 하고 화답했다."]], [["‘Of course I feel it, ma’am,’ said Mr.", "\"물론 영광으로 여기고말고요, 부인.\""], ["Raddle, rubbing his hands, and evincing a slight tendency to brighten up a little.", "래들 씨가 두 손을 비비며, 조금 기운이 살아나려는 기색을 보이며 말했다."], ["‘Indeed, to tell you the truth, I said, as we was a-coming along in the cabrioily--’", "\"사실 솔직히 말씀드리자면, 그 삯마차를 타고 오면서 제가 그랬지요—\""]], [["At the recapitulation of the word which awakened so many painful recollections, Mrs. Raddle applied her handkerchief to her eyes again, and uttered a half-suppressed scream; so that Mrs. Bardell frowned upon Mr. Raddle, to intimate that he had better not say anything more, and desired Mrs. Rogers’s servant, with an air, to ‘put the wine on.’", "그토록 많은 괴로운 기억을 불러일으키는 그 말이 또다시 되풀이되자, 래들 부인은 다시 손수건을 눈가에 갖다 대며 반쯤 억누른 비명을 내질렀다. 그러자 바델 부인은 래들 씨에게 더는 아무 말도 하지 않는 게 좋겠다는 뜻으로 눈살을 찌푸려 보이고는, 짐짓 점잔을 빼며 로저스 부인의 하녀에게 \"포도주를 내오라\"고 일렀다."]], [["This was the signal for displaying the hidden treasures of the closet, which comprised sundry plates of oranges and biscuits, and a bottle of old crusted port--that at one-and-nine--with another of the celebrated East India sherry at fourteen-pence, which were all produced in honour of the lodger, and afforded unlimited satisfaction to everybody. After great consternation had been excited in the mind of Mrs.", "이는 찬장에 감추어 둔 보물들을 펼쳐 놓으라는 신호였으니, 거기에는 오렌지와 비스킷 몇 접시, 그리고 한 병에 일 실링 구 펜스짜리 묵은 포트와인 한 병과, 십사 펜스짜리 그 유명한 동인도산 셰리주 한 병이 들어 있었는데, 이 모두가 세 든 부인을 기려 내어진 것이었고 모두에게 한없는 만족을 안겨 주었다."], ["Cluppins, by an attempt on the part of Tommy to recount how he had been cross-examined regarding the cupboard then in action (which was fortunately nipped in the bud by his imbibing half a glass of the old crusted ‘the wrong way,’ and thereby endangering his life for some seconds), the party walked forth in quest of a Hampstead stage.", "토미가, 지금 다들 마시고 있는 그 찬장에 관해 자기가 어떻게 캐물음을 당했는지 떠벌리려는 바람에 클러핀스 부인의 마음에 큰 동요가 일었으나(다행히도 토미가 묵은 포트와인 반 잔을 \"엉뚱한 데로\" 넘기는 바람에 몇 초간 목숨이 위태로워진 덕에 그 일은 싹부터 잘려 나갔다), 일행은 햄프스테드행 합승마차를 찾아 나섰다."], ["This was soon found, and in a couple of hours they all arrived safely in the Spaniards Tea-gardens, where the luckless Mr.", "마차는 곧 찾아냈고, 두어 시간 만에 모두 무사히 스패니어즈 다원에 도착했다."], ["Raddle’s very first act nearly occasioned his good lady a relapse; it being neither more nor less than to order tea for seven, whereas (as the ladies one and all remarked), what could have been easier than for Tommy to have drank out of anybody’s cup--or everybody’s, if that was all--when the waiter wasn’t looking, which would have saved one head of tea, and the tea just as good!", "그런데 불운한 래들 씨가 거기서 맨 처음 한 행동이 하마터면 그 착하신 부인을 다시 도지게 할 뻔했으니, 그것은 다름 아니라 일곱 사람 몫의 차를 주문한 일이었다. 부인들이 너 나 할 것 없이 지적한 대로, 종업원이 안 볼 때 토미가 아무 잔에서나—그것뿐이라면 모든 잔에서—마시면 그만 아닌가, 그러면 차 한 사람 몫이 절약되고 차 맛도 똑같았을 텐데 말이다!"]], [["However, there was no help for it, and the tea-tray came, with seven cups and saucers, and bread-and-butter on the same scale. Mrs. Bardell was unanimously voted into the chair, and Mrs. Rogers being stationed on her right hand, and Mrs. Raddle on her left, the meal proceeded with great merriment and success.", "그러나 어쩔 도리가 없었고, 차 쟁반이 일곱 개의 잔과 받침과, 거기에 맞춘 양의 버터 바른 빵과 함께 나왔다. 바델 부인이 만장일치로 좌장으로 추대되었고, 로저스 부인이 그녀의 오른편에, 래들 부인이 왼편에 자리를 잡으니, 차 모임은 큰 흥겨움과 성공 속에서 진행되었다."]], [["‘How sweet the country is, to be sure!’ sighed Mrs.", "\"시골은 정말이지 어찌나 상쾌한지!\" 로저스 부인이 한숨지었다."], ["Rogers; ‘I almost wish I lived in it always.’", "\"차라리 늘 시골에서 살았으면 싶을 정도예요.\""]], [["‘Oh, you wouldn’t like that, ma’am,’ replied Mrs.", "\"오, 그건 마음에 안 드실 거예요, 부인.\""], ["Bardell, rather hastily; for it was not at all advisable, with reference to the lodgings, to encourage such notions; ‘you wouldn’t like it, ma’am.’", "바델 부인이 다소 황급히 대답했으니, 세입자 문제를 생각하면 그런 생각을 부추기는 일은 결코 바람직하지 않았던 것이다. \"마음에 안 드실 거예요, 부인.\""]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["I should think you was a deal too lively and sought after, to be content with the country, ma’am,’ said little Mrs.", "부인은 너무 발랄하시고 찾는 분도 많으셔서, 시골에 만족하실 분이 아닌 것 같아요, 부인.\""], ["Cluppins.", "자그마한 클러핀스 부인이 말했다."]], [["‘Perhaps I am, ma’am.", "\"그럴지도 모르죠, 부인."], ["Perhaps I am,’ sighed the first-floor lodger.", "그럴지도 모르죠.\" 이 층에 든 부인이 한숨지었다."]], [["‘For lone people as have got nobody to care for them, or take care of them, or as have been hurt in their mind, or that kind of thing,’ observed Mr.", "\"자기를 아껴 줄 사람도, 보살펴 줄 사람도 없는 외로운 사람들이라든가, 마음에 상처를 입은 사람들이라든가, 그런 부류한테는요,\""], ["Raddle, plucking up a little cheerfulness, and looking round, ‘the country is all very well.", "래들 씨가 조금 기운을 차리고 좌중을 둘러보며 말을 꺼냈다. \"시골이 아주 그만이지요."], ["The country for a wounded spirit, they say.’", "시골은 상처받은 영혼을 위한 곳이라고들 하니까요.\""]], [["Now, of all things in the world that the unfortunate man could have said, any would have been preferable to this. Of course Mrs.", "그런데 그 불운한 사내가 할 수 있는 세상의 온갖 말 가운데, 차라리 다른 어떤 말이라도 이보다는 나았을 것이다."], ["Bardell burst into tears, and requested to be led from the table instantly; upon which the affectionate child began to cry too, most dismally.", "당연히 바델 부인은 울음을 터뜨리며 당장 식탁에서 데려가 달라고 청했고, 그러자 그 애틋한 아이도 더없이 처량하게 따라 울기 시작했다."]], [["‘Would anybody believe, ma’am,’ exclaimed Mrs.", "\"세상에 누가 믿겠어요, 부인,\""], ["Raddle, turning fiercely to the first-floor lodger, ‘that a woman could be married to such a unmanly creetur, which can tamper with a woman’s feelings as he does, every hour in the day, ma’am?’", "래들 부인이 이 층에 든 부인을 향해 사납게 돌아서며 외쳤다. \"여자가 이렇게 사내답지 못한 양반하고 결혼했다는 걸요, 하루도 빠짐없이 시도 때도 없이 여자의 마음을 함부로 들쑤셔 대는 양반하고 말예요, 부인?\""]], [["‘My dear,’ remonstrated Mr.", "\"여보,\" 래들 씨가 항변했다."], ["Raddle, ‘I didn’t mean anything, my dear.’", "\"난 아무 뜻도 없었소, 여보.\""]], [["‘You didn’t mean!’ repeated Mrs.", "\"뜻이 없었다고요!\""], ["Raddle, with great scorn and contempt.", "래들 부인이 잔뜩 비웃고 멸시하며 되받았다."], ["‘Go away.", "\"저리 가요."], ["I can’t bear the sight on you, you brute.’", "당신 꼴은 보기도 싫으니까, 이 짐승 같은 양반아.\""]], [["‘You must not flurry yourself, Mary Ann,’ interposed Mrs.", "\"마음을 산란하게 하면 안 돼요, 메리 앤.\" 클러핀스 부인이 끼어들었다."], ["Cluppins. ‘You really must consider yourself, my dear, which you never do.", "\"정말이지 네 몸을 좀 생각해야지, 얘야, 넌 도무지 안 하니까."], ["Now go away, Raddle, there’s a good soul, or you’ll only aggravate her.’", "자, 저리 가요, 래들 양반, 착한 양반이라면 말예요, 안 그러면 부인 속만 더 뒤집어 놓을 테니까.\""]], [["‘You had better take your tea by yourself, Sir, indeed,’ said Mrs.", "\"정말이지 차는 따로 드시는 게 좋겠어요, 선생님.\""], ["Rogers, again applying the smelling-bottle.", "로저스 부인이 다시 각성제 병을 갖다 대며 말했다."]], [["Mrs. Sanders, who, according to custom, was very busy with the bread- and-butter, expressed the same opinion, and Mr. Raddle quietly retired.", "평소 버릇대로 버터 바른 빵에 여념이 없던 샌더스 부인도 같은 의견을 내놓았고, 래들 씨는 조용히 물러갔다."]], [["After this, there was a great hoisting up of Master Bardell, who was rather a large size for hugging, into his mother’s arms, in which operation he got his boots in the tea-board, and occasioned some confusion among the cups and saucers.", "이 일이 있은 뒤, 끌어안기에는 덩치가 제법 큰 편인 바델 도련님을 그 어머니의 품으로 끌어올리는 큰 작업이 벌어졌는데, 그 와중에 도련님의 장화가 차 쟁반에 닿는 바람에 잔과 받침 사이에 약간의 소동이 일었다."], ["But that description of fainting fits, which is contagious among ladies, seldom lasts long; so when he had been well kissed, and a little cried over, Mrs. Bardell recovered, set him down again, wondering how she could have been so foolish, and poured out some more tea.", "그러나 부인들 사이에 전염되곤 하는 그런 종류의 기절 발작은 좀처럼 오래가지 않는 법, 그리하여 도련님이 실컷 입맞춤을 받고 그 위로 눈물도 조금 뿌려진 다음, 바델 부인은 정신을 차리고 그를 도로 내려놓으며 자기가 어쩌면 그리 어리석을 수 있었나 의아해하더니, 차를 좀 더 따랐다."]], [["It was at this moment, that the sound of approaching wheels was heard, and that the ladies, looking up, saw a hackney-coach stop at the garden gate.", "바로 이 순간, 다가오는 바퀴 소리가 들려왔고, 부인들이 고개를 들어 보니 삯마차 한 대가 다원 정문 앞에 멈춰 서 있었다."]], [["‘More company!’ said Mrs.", "\"손님이 또 오네!\""], ["Sanders.", "샌더스 부인이 말했다."]], [["‘It’s a gentleman,’ said Mrs.", "\"신사분이에요.\""], ["Raddle.", "래들 부인이 말했다."]], [["‘Well, if it ain’t Mr. Jackson, the young man from Dodson and Fogg’s!’ cried Mrs.", "\"어머, 저거 도드슨 앤드 포그 사무소의 젊은이, 잭슨 씨 아니에요!\""], ["Bardell. ‘Why, gracious!", "바델 부인이 외쳤다."], ["Surely Mr.", "\"어머나, 세상에!"], ["Pickwick can’t have paid the damages.’", "설마 픽윅 씨가 손해 배상금을 치른 건 아니겠죠.\""]], [["‘Or hoffered marriage!’ said Mrs.", "\"아니면 결혼 신청을 했다든가!\""], ["Cluppins.", "클러핀스 부인이 말했다."]], [["‘Dear me, how slow the gentleman is,’ exclaimed Mrs.", "\"어쩌면, 저 신사분은 어찌 저리 굼뜰까.\" 로저스 부인이 외쳤다."], ["Rogers. ‘Why doesn’t he make haste!’", "\"왜 서두르질 않는 거람!\""]], [["As the lady spoke these words, Mr.", "부인이 이 말을 하는 동안, 잭슨 씨는 마차에서 몸을 돌렸으니, 그는 거기서 검은 각반을 두른 허름한 사내에게 몇 마디 건네던 참이었는데, 그 사내는 굵은 물푸레나무 지팡이를 손에 들고 막 마차에서 내린 터였다."], ["Jackson turned from the coach where he had been addressing some observations to a shabby man in black leggings, who had just emerged from the vehicle with a thick ash stick in his hand, and made his way to the place where the ladies were seated; winding his hair round the brim of his hat, as he came along.", "잭슨 씨는 부인들이 앉아 있는 곳으로 걸어오며, 오는 내내 모자챙에 머리카락을 빙빙 감았다."]], [["‘Is anything the matter?", "\"무슨 일이라도 있나요?"], ["Has anything taken place, Mr.", "무슨 일이 생긴 거예요, 잭슨 씨?\""], ["Jackson?’ said Mrs. Bardell eagerly.", "바델 부인이 다급히 말했다."]], [["‘Nothing whatever, ma’am,’ replied Mr. Jackson.", "\"아무 일도 없습니다, 부인.\""], ["‘How de do, ladies?", "잭슨 씨가 대답했다."], ["I have to ask pardon, ladies, for intruding--but the law, ladies--the law.’ With this apology Mr.", "\"안녕하십니까, 부인들? 불쑥 끼어들어 송구합니다, 부인들—하지만 법이 그래서요, 부인들—법 때문에 말입니다.\""], ["Jackson smiled, made a comprehensive bow, and gave his hair another wind. Mrs.", "이렇게 양해를 구하며 잭슨 씨는 미소 짓고, 좌중을 두루 향해 인사를 건네고는, 다시 한 번 머리카락을 빙 감았다."], ["Rogers whispered Mrs. Raddle that he was really an elegant young man.", "로저스 부인은 래들 부인에게 그가 정말 우아한 청년이라고 귓속말을 했다."]], [["‘I called in Goswell Street,’ resumed Mr.", "\"고스웰 거리에 들렀다가,\" 잭슨 씨가 말을 이었다."], ["Jackson, ‘and hearing that you were here, from the slavey, took a coach and came on.", "\"하녀한테서 부인이 여기 계시다는 말을 듣고, 마차를 잡아 곧장 달려왔습니다."], ["Our people want you down in the city directly, Mrs. Bardell.’", "저희 사무소 분들이 부인께서 당장 시내로 와 주셨으면 하십니다, 바델 부인.\""]], [["‘Lor!’ ejaculated that lady, starting at the sudden nature of the communication.", "\"어머나!\" 그 부인이 그 통보의 갑작스러움에 흠칫 놀라며 소리쳤다."]], [["‘Yes,’ said Mr.", "\"네.\""], ["Jackson, biting his lip.", "잭슨 씨가 입술을 깨물며 말했다."], ["‘It’s very important and pressing business, which can’t be postponed on any account.", "\"아주 중대하고 급한 일이라, 무슨 일이 있어도 미룰 수 없는 거랍니다."], ["Indeed, Dodson expressly said so to me, and so did Fogg.", "정말이지 도드슨 씨가 저한테 분명히 그렇게 말씀하셨고, 포그 씨도 그러셨고요."], ["I’ve kept the coach on purpose for you to go back in.’", "부인이 타고 돌아가시라고 일부러 마차를 잡아 두었습니다.\""]], [["‘How very strange!’ exclaimed Mrs.", "\"참 이상도 하네요!\""], ["Bardell.", "바델 부인이 외쳤다."]], [["The ladies agreed that it was very strange, but were unanimously of opinion that it must be very important, or Dodson & Fogg would never have sent; and further, that the business being urgent, she ought to repair to Dodson & Fogg’s without any delay.", "부인들은 정말이지 이상한 일이라는 데 동의했으나, 한목소리로, 분명 아주 중대한 일일 거라고, 안 그러면 도드슨 앤드 포그가 사람을 보냈을 리 없다고 의견을 모았다. 더 나아가, 그 일이 급하니 부인은 지체 없이 도드슨 앤드 포그 사무소로 가야 한다고 했다."]], [["There was a certain degree of pride and importance about being wanted by one’s lawyers in such a monstrous hurry, that was by no means displeasing to Mrs. Bardell, especially as it might be reasonably supposed to enhance her consequence in the eyes of the first-floor lodger.", "자기 변호사들이 그토록 어마어마하게 다급히 자기를 찾는다는 데에는 어느 정도 으쓱하고 대단해지는 느낌이 있어, 바델 부인에게는 결코 싫지 않은 일이었으니, 더구나 그것은 이 층에 든 부인의 눈에 자기 위상을 한층 높여 주리라고 충분히 짐작할 만했다."], ["She simpered a little, affected extreme vexation and hesitation, and at last arrived at the conclusion that she supposed she must go.", "그녀는 살짝 새침을 떨며, 짐짓 몹시 난처하고 망설이는 척하다가, 마침내 아무래도 가야겠다는 결론에 이르렀다."]], [["‘But won’t you refresh yourself after your walk, Mr.", "\"하지만 걸어오셨는데 뭐라도 좀 드시지 않겠어요, 잭슨 씨?\""], ["Jackson?’ said Mrs. Bardell persuasively.", "바델 부인이 권하듯이 말했다."]], [["‘Why, really there ain’t much time to lose,’ replied Jackson; ‘and I’ve got a friend here,’ he continued, looking towards the man with the ash stick.", "\"실은 시간이 그리 많지 않습니다.\" 잭슨이 대답했다. \"게다가 일행이 한 사람 있어서요.\" 그는 물푸레나무 지팡이를 든 사내 쪽을 바라보며 말을 이었다."]], [["‘Oh, ask your friend to come here, Sir,’ said Mrs.", "\"오, 일행분도 이리 오시라고 하세요, 선생님.\" 바델 부인이 말했다."], ["Bardell. ‘Pray ask your friend here, Sir.’", "\"부디 일행분도 이리로 청하세요, 선생님.\""]], [["‘Why, thank’ee, I’d rather not,’ said Mr.", "\"아니요, 고맙습니다만, 그러지 않는 게 낫겠습니다.\""], ["Jackson, with some embarrassment of manner.", "잭슨 씨가 다소 거북한 태도로 말했다."], ["‘He’s not much used to ladies’ society, and it makes him bashful.", "\"저 친구가 부인들과 어울리는 데 그리 익숙지 않아서, 그러면 부끄러워한답니다."], ["If you’ll order the waiter to deliver him anything short, he won’t drink it off at once, won’t he!--only try him!’ Mr.", "종업원더러 독한 걸로 뭐든 갖다주라고 시키시면, 그 친구가 그걸 단번에 들이켜진 않을걸요, 안 그러겠어요!—한번 시험해 보세요!\""], ["Jackson’s fingers wandered playfully round his nose at this portion of his discourse, to warn his hearers that he was speaking ironically.", "잭슨 씨는 이 대목에서 손가락을 코 언저리로 장난스레 놀려, 자기가 빈정대는 말을 하고 있음을 듣는 이들에게 일러 주었다."]], [["The waiter was at once despatched to the bashful gentleman, and the bashful gentleman took something; Mr. Jackson also took something, and the ladies took something, for hospitality’s sake. Mr. Jackson then said he was afraid it was time to go; upon which, Mrs. Sanders, Mrs. Cluppins, and Tommy (who it was arranged should accompany Mrs. Bardell, leaving the others to Mr. Raddle’s protection), got into the coach.", "종업원이 곧장 그 수줍은 신사에게 보내졌고, 수줍은 신사는 무언가를 받아 마셨다. 잭슨 씨도 무언가를 마셨고, 부인들도 접대하는 뜻에서 무언가를 마셨다. 그러자 잭슨 씨가 이제 갈 시간인 것 같다고 말했고, 그 말에 샌더스 부인과 클러핀스 부인과 토미가(이들은 바델 부인을 따라가고 나머지 사람들은 래들 씨의 보호에 맡기기로 정해진 터였다) 마차에 올랐다."]], [["‘Isaac,’ said Jackson, as Mrs.", "\"아이작,\""], ["Bardell prepared to get in, looking up at the man with the ash stick, who was seated on the box, smoking a cigar.", "바델 부인이 막 마차에 오르려 할 때, 잭슨이 마부석에 앉아 시가를 피우던 물푸레나무 지팡이의 사내를 올려다보며 말했다."]], [["‘Well?’", "\"왜?\""]], [["‘This is Mrs. Bardell.’", "\"이분이 바델 부인이오.\""]], [["‘Oh, I know’d that long ago,’ said the man.", "\"아, 그건 진작에 알았소.\" 사내가 말했다."]], [["Mrs. Bardell got in, Mr.", "바델 부인이 올라타고, 그 뒤를 이어 잭슨 씨가 올라타니, 마차는 떠났다."], ["Jackson got in after her, and away they drove. Mrs.", "바델 부인은 잭슨 씨의 일행이 한 말을 곱씹어 보지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Bardell could not help ruminating on what Mr. Jackson’s friend had said.", "약빠른 사람들이지, 그 변호사들이란."], ["Shrewd creatures, those lawyers. Lord bless us, how they find people out!", "어머나, 어쩜 그리도 사람을 잘 알아본담!"]], [["‘Sad thing about these costs of our people’s, ain’t it,’ said Jackson, when Mrs. Cluppins and Mrs.", "\"우리 사무소 분들 그 소송 비용 문제가 딱하게 됐어요, 안 그래요.\""], ["Sanders had fallen asleep; ‘your bill of costs, I mean.’", "클러핀스 부인과 샌더스 부인이 잠들자 잭슨이 말했다. \"부인의 소송 비용 청구서 말입니다.\""]], [["‘I’m very sorry they can’t get them,’ replied Mrs.", "\"그분들이 그걸 못 받게 돼서 정말 안됐어요.\" 바델 부인이 대답했다."], ["Bardell. ‘But if you law gentlemen do these things on speculation, why you must get a loss now and then, you know.’", "\"하지만 변호사 양반들이 이런 일을 투기 삼아 하는 거라면, 이따금 손해도 봐야 하는 거지요, 아시다시피요.\""]], [["‘You gave them a cognovit for the amount of your costs, after the trial, I’m told!’ said Jackson.", "\"재판이 끝난 뒤에 부인이 소송 비용 액수만큼 채무 인정서를 그분들한테 써 주셨다고 들었는데요!\" 잭슨이 말했다."]], [["‘Yes.", "\"네."], ["Just as a matter of form,’ replied Mrs.", "그저 형식상으로요.\""], ["Bardell.", "바델 부인이 대답했다."]], [["‘Certainly,’ replied Jackson drily.", "\"그럼요.\" 잭슨이 무덤덤하게 대답했다."], ["‘Quite a matter of form.", "\"순전히 형식이지요."], ["Quite.’", "순전히요.\""]], [["On they drove, and Mrs.", "* 채무 인정서: 원어는 cognovit."], ["Bardell fell asleep.", "피고가 원고의 청구를 인정하여 법적 집행이 가능하도록 작성하는 문서."], ["She was awakened, after some time, by the stopping of the coach.", "마차는 계속 달렸고, 바델 부인은 잠이 들었다. 얼마쯤 지난 뒤, 그녀는 마차가 멈추는 바람에 잠에서 깨어났다."]], [["‘Bless us!’ said the lady.", "\"어머나!\" 부인이 말했다."], ["‘Are we at Freeman’s Court?’", "\"여기가 프리먼스 코트인가요?\""]], [["‘We’re not going quite so far,’ replied Jackson.", "\"거기까지 가는 건 아닙니다.\" 잭슨이 대답했다."], ["‘Have the goodness to step out.’", "\"어서 내리시지요.\""]], [["Mrs. Bardell, not yet thoroughly awake, complied.", "아직 잠이 채 깨지 않은 바델 부인은 시키는 대로 했다."], ["It was a curious place: a large wall, with a gate in the middle, and a gas-light burning inside.", "묘한 곳이었다. 커다란 담벼락 한가운데에 문이 하나 나 있고, 그 안쪽에 가스등이 타고 있었다."]], [["‘Now, ladies,’ cried the man with the ash stick, looking into the coach, and shaking Mrs.", "\"자, 부인들,\" 물푸레나무 지팡이의 사내가 마차 안을 들여다보며, 샌더스 부인을 흔들어 깨우면서 외쳤다."], ["Sanders to wake her, ‘Come!’ Rousing her friend, Mrs.", "\"어서요!\""], ["Sanders alighted. Mrs.", "친구를 흔들어 깨운 샌더스 부인이 내렸다."], ["Bardell, leaning on Jackson’s arm, and leading Tommy by the hand, had already entered the porch.", "바델 부인은 잭슨의 팔에 기대고 토미의 손을 잡은 채, 이미 현관에 들어선 뒤였다."], ["They followed.", "부인들이 그 뒤를 따랐다."]], [["The room they turned into was even more odd-looking than the porch.", "그들이 들어선 방은 현관보다도 더 괴상한 모양새였다."], ["Such a number of men standing about!", "그렇게나 많은 사내들이 서성거리고 있다니!"], ["And they stared so!", "게다가 어찌나 빤히 쳐다보던지!"]], [["‘What place is this?’ inquired Mrs.", "\"여기가 무슨 곳이죠?\""], ["Bardell, pausing.", "바델 부인이 멈춰 서며 물었다."]], [["‘Only one of our public offices,’ replied Jackson, hurrying her through a door, and looking round to see that the other women were following.", "\"그저 저희 관청 가운데 하나일 뿐입니다.\" 잭슨이 대답하며 그녀를 어느 문으로 황급히 밀어넣고는, 다른 부인들이 따라오는지 둘러보았다."], ["‘Look sharp, Isaac!’", "\"서둘러, 아이작!\""]], [["‘Safe and sound,’ replied the man with the ash stick.", "\"무사히 다 데려왔소.\" 물푸레나무 지팡이의 사내가 대답했다."], ["The door swung heavily after them, and they descended a small flight of steps.", "문이 그들 뒤로 묵직하게 닫혔고, 그들은 짤막한 계단을 내려갔다."]], [["‘Here we are at last.", "\"마침내 다 왔습니다."], ["All right and tight, Mrs.", "만사형통, 바델 부인!\""], ["Bardell!’ said Jackson, looking exultingly round.", "잭슨이 의기양양하게 둘러보며 말했다."]], [["‘What do you mean?’ said Mrs.", "\"무슨 말이에요?\""], ["Bardell, with a palpitating heart.", "바델 부인이 가슴이 두근거리며 말했다."]], [["‘Just this,’ replied Jackson, drawing her a little on one side; ‘don’t be frightened, Mrs. Bardell.", "\"바로 이런 겁니다.\""], ["There never was a more delicate man than Dodson, ma’am, or a more humane man than Fogg.", "잭슨이 그녀를 한쪽으로 살짝 끌어당기며 대답했다. \"겁먹지 마세요, 바델 부인."], ["It was their duty in the way of business, to take you in execution for them costs; but they were anxious to spare your feelings as much as they could.", "도드슨 씨보다 더 섬세한 분도, 포그 씨보다 더 인정 많은 분도 세상에 없답니다, 부인. 그분들로서는 업무상, 그 소송 비용 때문에 부인을 잡아들이는 게 의무였지요."], ["What a comfort it must be, to you, to think how it’s been done!", "하지만 그분들은 되도록이면 부인의 심정을 헤아려 드리려고 무척 마음을 쓰셨답니다. 일이 어떻게 처리됐는지 생각하면 부인께 얼마나 위안이 될지요!"], ["This is the Fleet, ma’am.", "여기는 플리트입니다, 부인."], ["Wish you good-night, Mrs. Bardell.", "안녕히 주무세요, 바델 부인."], ["Good-night, Tommy!’", "잘 자라, 토미!\""]], [["As Jackson hurried away in company with the man with the ash stick another man, with a key in his hand, who had been looking on, led the bewildered female to a second short flight of steps leading to a doorway. Mrs.", "잭슨이 물푸레나무 지팡이의 사내와 함께 황급히 자리를 뜨는 사이, 곁에서 지켜보던 또 다른 사내가, 손에 열쇠를 들고서, 어리둥절해하는 그 여인을 어느 문간으로 이어지는 두 번째 짤막한 계단으로 이끌었다."], ["Bardell screamed violently; Tommy roared; Mrs. Cluppins shrunk within herself; and Mrs.", "바델 부인은 격렬하게 비명을 질렀고, 토미는 울부짖었고, 클러핀스 부인은 잔뜩 움츠러들었으며, 샌더스 부인은 더 따질 것도 없이 줄행랑을 쳤다."], ["Sanders made off, without more ado. For there stood the injured Mr.", "거기에는 억울하게 당한 픽윅 씨가 밤마다의 산책으로 바람을 쐬며 서 있었고, 그 곁에는 새뮤얼 웰러가 기대어 있었던 것이다."], ["Pickwick, taking his nightly allowance of air; and beside him leant Samuel Weller, who, seeing Mrs. Bardell, took his hat off with mock reverence, while his master turned indignantly on his heel.", "새뮤얼은 바델 부인을 보자 짐짓 공손한 척 모자를 벗었고, 한편 그의 주인은 분개하여 홱 발길을 돌렸다."]], [["‘Don’t bother the woman,’ said the turnkey to Weller; ‘she’s just come in.’", "\"저 여자분을 괴롭히지 마시오.\" 간수가 웰러에게 말했다. \"방금 들어온 사람이오.\""]], [["‘A prisoner!’ said Sam, quickly replacing his hat.", "\"죄수라고요!\" 샘이 얼른 다시 모자를 쓰며 말했다."], ["‘Who’s the plaintives?", "\"원고가 누구요?"], ["What for?", "무슨 죄목으로요?"], ["Speak up, old feller.’", "어서 말해 보쇼, 영감님.\""]], [["‘Dodson and Fogg,’ replied the man; ‘execution on cognovit for costs.’", "\"도드슨 앤드 포그요.\" 사내가 대답했다. \"소송 비용에 대한 채무 인정서에 따른 집행이오.\""]], [["‘Here, Job, Job!’ shouted Sam, dashing into the passage.", "\"이봐, 잡, 잡!\" 샘이 복도로 뛰어들며 소리쳤다."], ["‘Run to Mr. Perker’s, Job.", "\"퍼커 씨한테 달려가, 잡."], ["I want him directly.", "그분이 당장 필요해."], ["I see some good in this.", "이거 뭔가 잘 풀릴 것 같단 말이야."], ["Here’s a game.", "한바탕 볼만하겠는걸."], ["Hooray!", "만세!"], ["vere’s the gov’nor?’", "주인 어른은 어디 계시오?\""]], [["But there was no reply to these inquiries, for Job had started furiously off, the instant he received his commission, and Mrs. Bardell had fainted in real downright earnest.", "그러나 이 물음들에는 아무 대답이 없었으니, 잡은 임무를 받자마자 부리나케 달려 나간 뒤였고, 바델 부인은 정말로 영락없이 기절해 버린 뒤였다."]], [["CHAPTER XLVII. IS CHIEFLY DEVOTED TO MATTERS OF BUSINESS, AND THE TEMPORAL ADVANTAGE OF DODSON AND FOGG--MR. WINKLE REAPPEARS UNDER EXTRAORDINARY CIRCUMSTANCES--MR. PICKWICK’S BENEVOLENCE PROVES STRONGER THAN HIS OBSTINACY\nJob Trotter, abating nothing of his speed, ran up Holborn, sometimes in the middle of the road, sometimes on the pavement, sometimes in the gutter, as the chances of getting along varied with the press of men, women, children, and coaches, in each division of the thoroughfare, and, regardless of all obstacles stopped not for an instant until he reached the gate of Gray’s Inn. Notwithstanding all the expedition he had used, however, the gate had been closed a good half-hour when he reached it, and by the time he had discovered Mr. Perker’s laundress, who lived with a married daughter, who had bestowed her hand upon a non-resident waiter, who occupied the one-pair of some number in some street closely adjoining to some brewery somewhere behind Gray’s Inn Lane, it was within fifteen minutes of closing the prison for the night. Mr. Lowten had still to be ferreted out from the back parlour of the Magpie and Stump; and Job had scarcely accomplished this object, and communicated Sam Weller’s message, when the clock struck ten.", "잡 트로터는 조금도 속도를 늦추지 않고 홀번 거리를 내달렸는데, 길의 각 구역마다 남자와 여자, 아이들, 그리고 마차들이 붐비는 정도에 따라 빠져나갈 틈이 달라졌으므로, 때로는 길 한복판으로, 때로는 인도 위로, 때로는 도랑 속으로 뛰었고, 온갖 장애물에 아랑곳하지 않은 채 그레이즈 인의 문에 다다를 때까지 한순간도 멈추지 않았다. 그러나 그토록 서둘렀음에도 그가 도착했을 때 문은 이미 족히 삼십 분 전에 닫혀 있었고, 게다가 퍼커 씨의 세탁부를 찾아냈을 무렵에는—그 세탁부는 어느 출퇴근하는 웨이터에게 손을 내준 시집간 딸과 함께 살았고, 그 웨이터는 그레이즈 인 레인 뒤편 어딘가 어느 양조장에 바싹 붙은 어느 거리 어느 번지의 이층 단칸방을 차지하고 있었다—밤을 위해 감옥이 닫히기까지 십오 분도 채 남지 않은 시각이었다. 게다가 매그파이 앤드 스텀프의 뒷방에서 라우튼 씨를 또 끄집어내야 했는데, 잡이 겨우 이 일을 해내고 샘 웰러의 전갈을 전했을 때 시계가 열 시를 쳤다."]], [["‘There,’ said Lowten, ‘it’s too late now. You can’t get in to-night; you’ve got the key of the street, my friend.’", "\"이런,\" 라우튼이 말했다. \"이젠 너무 늦었어. 오늘 밤엔 못 들어가. 자넨 거리 열쇠를 받은 셈이군, 친구.\""]], [["‘Never mind me,’ replied Job.", "\"저는 신경 쓰지 마십시오,\""], ["‘I can sleep anywhere.", "잡이 대답했다."], ["But won’t it be better to see Mr.", "\"저야 어디서든 잘 수 있습니다."], ["Perker to-night, so that we may be there, the first thing in the morning?’", "하지만 오늘 밤에 퍼커 씨를 뵙는 편이 낫지 않겠습니까? 그래야 아침에 제일 먼저 거기 가 있을 수 있을 테니까요.\""]], [["‘Why,’ responded Lowten, after a little consideration, ‘if it was in anybody else’s case, Perker wouldn’t be best pleased at my going up to his house; but as it’s Mr.", "\"음,\" 라우튼이 잠시 생각한 끝에 대답했다."], ["Pickwick’s, I think I may venture to take a cab and charge it to the office.’ Deciding on this line of conduct, Mr.", "\"다른 사람 일이라면 퍼커 씨는 내가 자기 집까지 찾아오는 걸 그리 달가워하지 않을 거야. 하지만 픽윅 씨 일이니, 마차를 한 대 잡아서 사무소 앞으로 청구해도 괜찮겠지.\""], ["Lowten took up his hat, and begging the assembled company to appoint a deputy-chairman during his temporary absence, led the way to the nearest coach-stand.", "이렇게 행동 방침을 정한 라우튼 씨는 모자를 집어 들고, 모여 있던 사람들에게 자신이 잠시 자리를 비운 동안 부의장을 한 명 뽑으라고 청한 뒤, 가장 가까운 마차 정류장으로 앞장섰다."], ["Summoning the cab of most promising appearance, he directed the driver to repair to Montague Place, Russell Square.", "가장 그럴듯해 보이는 마차를 불러 세운 그는 마부에게 러셀 광장 몬터규 플레이스로 가라고 일렀다."]], [["Mr. Perker had had a dinner-party that day, as was testified by the appearance of lights in the drawing-room windows, the sound of an improved grand piano, and an improvable cabinet voice issuing therefrom, and a rather overpowering smell of meat which pervaded the steps and entry.", "퍼커 씨는 그날 만찬 모임을 열고 있었는데, 응접실 창에 불빛이 보이는 것이며, 거기서 흘러나오는 어느 고급 그랜드 피아노 소리와 더 갈고닦을 여지가 있는 어느 실내악풍 목소리, 그리고 계단과 현관에 자욱이 퍼진 다소 압도적인 고기 냄새가 이를 증언하고 있었다."], ["In fact, a couple of very good country agencies happening to come up to town, at the same time, an agreeable little party had been got together to meet them, comprising Mr. Snicks, the Life Office Secretary, Mr.", "사실인즉슨, 매우 좋은 지방 대리점 두 곳이 마침 동시에 상경하는 바람에, 그들을 맞이하기 위해 유쾌하고 자그마한 모임이 꾸려졌던 것이다."], ["Prosee, the eminent counsel, three solicitors, one commissioner of bankrupts, a special pleader from the Temple, a small-eyed peremptory young gentleman, his pupil, who had written a lively book about the law of demises, with a vast quantity of marginal notes and references; and several other eminent and distinguished personages.", "그 자리에는 생명보험사무소 비서인 스닉스 씨, 저명한 법정 변호사 프로지 씨, 사무 변호사 세 사람, 파산 관재인 한 명, 템플 소속의 특별 변호사 한 명, 그리고 그의 제자인 눈이 작고 단호한 젊은 신사가 있었는데, 이 제자는 부동산 양도법에 관해 방대한 분량의 방주와 참조를 곁들인 발랄한 책을 쓴 자였다. 그 밖에도 저명하고 명망 높은 여러 인사들이 있었다."], ["From this society, little Mr. Perker detached himself, on his clerk being announced in a whisper; and repairing to the dining-room, there found Mr.", "이 모임에서 작달막한 퍼커 씨는 자기 서기가 왔다는 전갈을 귓속말로 듣고 슬그머니 빠져나와, 식당으로 가서 라우튼 씨와 잡 트로터가 부엌 양초 불빛 아래 매우 흐릿하고 그림자처럼 서 있는 것을 발견했다."], ["Lowten and Job Trotter looking very dim and shadowy by the light of a kitchen candle, which the gentleman who condescended to appear in plush shorts and cottons for a quarterly stipend, had, with a becoming contempt for the clerk and all things appertaining to ‘the office,’ placed upon the table.", "그 양초는, 분기마다 받는 봉급을 위해 플러시 반바지와 무명 양말 차림으로 황송하게 등장하시는 신사 양반이 서기를 비롯해 '사무소'에 관련된 모든 것에 대한 합당한 경멸을 담아 탁자 위에 놓아둔 것이었다."]], [["‘Now, Lowten,’ said little Mr.", "\"자, 라우튼,\""], ["Perker, shutting the door, ‘what’s the matter?", "작달막한 퍼커 씨가 문을 닫으며 말했다."], ["No important letter come in a parcel, is there?’", "\"무슨 일인가? 소포에 중요한 편지라도 온 건 아니겠지?\""]], [["‘No, Sir,’ replied Lowten.", "\"아닙니다, 나리,\" 라우튼이 대답했다."], ["‘This is a messenger from Mr. Pickwick, Sir.’", "\"이 사람은 픽윅 씨가 보낸 전령입니다, 나리.\""]], [["‘From Pickwick, eh?’ said the little man, turning quickly to Job.", "\"픽윅한테서, 응?\" 작달막한 사내가 재빨리 잡 쪽으로 돌아서며 말했다."], ["‘Well, what is it?’", "\"그래, 무슨 일인가?\""]], [["‘Dodson and Fogg have taken Mrs.", "\"도드슨과 포그가 소송 비용 때문에 바델 부인을 구금했습니다, 나리,\""], ["Bardell in execution for her costs, Sir,’ said Job.", "잡이 말했다."]], [["‘No!’ exclaimed Perker, putting his hands in his pockets, and reclining against the sideboard.", "\"설마!\" 퍼커가 두 손을 주머니에 찔러 넣고 찬장에 기대며 소리쳤다."]], [["‘Yes,’ said Job.", "\"사실입니다,\""], ["‘It seems they got a cognovit out of her, for the amount of ‘em, directly after the trial.’", "잡이 말했다. \"재판이 끝나자마자 그 비용 액수에 대한 채무 인낙서를 부인한테서 받아냈던 모양입니다.\""]], [["‘By Jove!’ said Perker, taking both hands out of his pockets, and striking the knuckles of his right against the palm of his left, emphatically, ‘those are the cleverest scamps I ever had anything to do with!’", "\"세상에!\" 퍼커가 두 손을 주머니에서 빼내며, 오른손 마디를 왼손 손바닥에 힘주어 탁 치며 말했다. \"내가 여태 상대해 본 자들 중에 가장 영악한 악당들이로군!\""]], [["‘The sharpest practitioners I ever knew, Sir,’ observed Lowten.", "\"제가 알기로 가장 약삭빠른 자들입니다, 나리,\" 라우튼이 한마디 보탰다."]], [["‘Sharp!’ echoed Perker.", "\"약삭빠르다!\" 퍼커가 되받았다."], ["‘There’s no knowing where to have them.’", "\"어디서 그들의 꼬리를 잡아야 할지 도무지 알 수가 없다니까.\""]], [["‘Very true, Sir, there is not,’ replied Lowten; and then, both master and man pondered for a few seconds, with animated countenances, as if they were reflecting upon one of the most beautiful and ingenious discoveries that the intellect of man had ever made.", "\"맞는 말씀입니다, 나리, 정말 그렇습니다,\" 라우튼이 대답했다."], ["When they had in some measure recovered from their trance of admiration, Job Trotter discharged himself of the rest of his commission.", "그러고는 주인과 하인이 둘 다 몇 초 동안 생기 도는 얼굴로 골똘히 생각에 잠겼는데, 마치 인간의 지성이 일찍이 이뤄 낸 가장 아름답고 기발한 발견 가운데 하나를 음미하고 있는 듯했다. 어느 정도 그 감탄의 황홀경에서 깨어났을 때, 잡 트로터는 나머지 임무를 마저 전했다."], ["Perker nodded his head thoughtfully, and pulled out his watch.", "퍼커는 생각에 잠긴 듯 고개를 끄덕이더니 회중시계를 꺼냈다."]], [["‘At ten precisely, I will be there,’ said the little man.", "\"정각 열 시에 거기로 가겠네,\" 작달막한 사내가 말했다."], ["‘Sam is quite right.", "\"샘이 아주 옳아."], ["Tell him so.", "그렇게 전해."], ["Will you take a glass of wine, Lowten?’", "라우튼, 포도주 한 잔 들겠나?\""]], [["No, thank you, Sir.’", "\"아닙니다, 고맙습니다, 나리.\""]], [["‘You mean yes, I think,’ said the little man, turning to the sideboard for a decanter and glasses.", "\"내 생각엔 그렇다는 뜻인 것 같은데,\" 작달막한 사내가 술병과 잔을 가지러 찬장 쪽으로 돌아서며 말했다."]], [["As Lowten did mean yes, he said no more on the subject, but inquired of Job, in an audible whisper, whether the portrait of Perker, which hung opposite the fireplace, wasn’t a wonderful likeness, to which Job of course replied that it was.", "라우튼이 정말로 그렇다는 뜻이었으므로, 그는 그 일에 대해 더 말하지 않고, 들릴 만한 귓속말로 잡에게, 벽난로 맞은편에 걸린 퍼커 씨의 초상화가 놀랄 만큼 닮지 않았느냐고 물었고, 잡은 물론 그렇다고 대답했다."], ["The wine being by this time poured out, Lowten drank to Mrs. Perker and the children, and Job to Perker.", "이때쯤 포도주가 따라졌고, 라우튼은 퍼커 부인과 아이들을 위해, 잡은 퍼커를 위해 건배했다."], ["The gentleman in the plush shorts and cottons considering it no part of his duty to show the people from the office out, consistently declined to answer the bell, and they showed themselves out.", "플러시 반바지와 무명 양말 차림의 신사 양반은 사무소에서 온 사람들을 배웅하는 것이 자기 임무의 일부가 아니라고 여겨 한결같이 초인종에 응하기를 거부했고, 그래서 그들은 알아서 나갔다."], ["The attorney betook himself to his drawing-room, the clerk to the Magpie and Stump, and Job to Covent Garden Market to spend the night in a vegetable basket.", "변호사는 응접실로, 서기는 매그파이 앤드 스텀프로, 그리고 잡은 코번트 가든 시장으로 가서 채소 바구니 속에서 밤을 보냈다."]], [["Punctually at the appointed hour next morning, the good-humoured little attorney tapped at Mr. Pickwick’s door, which was opened with great alacrity by Sam Weller.", "이튿날 아침, 약속한 시각 정각에 그 쾌활하고 작달막한 변호사가 픽윅 씨의 방문을 두드렸고, 샘 웰러가 매우 민첩하게 그 문을 열었다."]], [["‘Mr. Perker, sir,’ said Sam, announcing the visitor to Mr.", "\"퍼커 씨가 오셨습니다, 나리,\""], ["Pickwick, who was sitting at the window in a thoughtful attitude.", "샘이 말하며, 생각에 잠긴 자세로 창가에 앉아 있던 픽윅 씨에게 손님을 알렸다."], ["‘Wery glad you’ve looked in accidentally, Sir.", "\"우연히 들러 주셔서 정말 기쁩니다, 나리."], ["I rather think the gov’nor wants to have a word and a half with you, Sir.’", "제 짐작에 어르신께서 나리와 한두 마디 나누고 싶어 하시는 것 같습니다, 나리.\""]], [["Perker bestowed a look of intelligence on Sam, intimating that he understood he was not to say he had been sent for; and beckoning him to approach, whispered briefly in his ear.", "퍼커는 샘에게 알겠다는 눈짓을 보냈는데, 자기가 부름을 받고 왔다는 말을 해서는 안 된다는 걸 알아들었다는 뜻이었다. 그러고는 샘에게 가까이 오라고 손짓하더니 그의 귀에 짤막하게 속삭였다."]], [["‘You don’t mean that ‘ere, Sir?’ said Sam, starting back in excessive surprise.", "\"설마 정말 그러신 건 아니죠, 나리?\" 샘이 너무 놀란 나머지 뒤로 펄쩍 물러서며 말했다."]], [["Perker nodded and smiled.", "퍼커는 고개를 끄덕이며 미소를 지었다."]], [["Mr. Samuel Weller looked at the little lawyer, then at Mr. Pickwick, then at the ceiling, then at Perker again; grinned, laughed outright, and finally, catching up his hat from the carpet, without further explanation, disappeared.", "새뮤얼 웰러 씨는 작달막한 변호사를 보다가, 다음엔 픽윅 씨를, 다음엔 천장을, 다음엔 다시 퍼커를 보더니, 씩 웃다가 큰 소리로 웃음을 터뜨렸고, 마침내 더 이상의 설명도 없이 양탄자에서 모자를 집어 들고는 사라져 버렸다."]], [["‘What does this mean?’ inquired Mr.", "\"이게 무슨 뜻이오?\""], ["Pickwick, looking at Perker with astonishment.", "픽윅 씨가 놀라서 퍼커를 바라보며 물었다."], ["‘What has put Sam into this extraordinary state?’", "\"무엇이 샘을 저런 별난 상태로 만든 거요?\""]], [["‘Oh, nothing, nothing,’ replied Perker.", "\"오, 아무것도 아닙니다, 아무것도,\" 퍼커가 대답했다."], ["‘Come, my dear Sir, draw up your chair to the table.", "\"자, 친애하는 선생, 의자를 탁자 쪽으로 당기시지요."], ["I have a good deal to say to you.’", "드릴 말씀이 꽤 많습니다.\""]], [["‘What papers are those?’ inquired Mr.", "\"그 서류들은 뭐요?\" 픽윅 씨가 물었다."], ["Pickwick, as the little man deposited on the table a small bundle of documents tied with red tape.", "작달막한 사내가 빨간 끈으로 묶인 작은 서류 뭉치를 탁자 위에 내려놓던 참이었다."]], [["‘The papers in Bardell and Pickwick,’ replied Perker, undoing the knot with his teeth.", "\"바델 대 픽윅 사건의 서류입니다,\" 퍼커가 이로 매듭을 풀며 대답했다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick grated the legs of his chair against the ground; and throwing himself into it, folded his hands and looked sternly--if Mr. Pickwick ever could look sternly--at his legal friend.", "픽윅 씨는 의자 다리를 바닥에 박박 긁으며 끌었고, 의자에 털썩 몸을 던지고 두 손을 맞잡은 채 자신의 법률가 친구를 엄하게—픽윅 씨가 엄하게 볼 수 있는 사람이라면—노려보았다."]], [["‘You don’t like to hear the name of the cause?’ said the little man, still busying himself with the knot.", "\"그 사건 이름을 듣는 게 싫으신 게로군요?\" 작달막한 사내가 여전히 매듭과 씨름하며 말했다."]], [["‘No, I do not indeed,’ replied Mr.", "\"그렇소, 정말이지 싫소,\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘Sorry for that,’ resumed Perker, ‘because it will form the subject of our conversation.’", "\"그것 참 안됐습니다,\" 퍼커가 말을 이었다. \"바로 그게 우리 대화의 주제가 될 테니까요.\""]], [["‘I would rather that the subject should be never mentioned between us, Perker,’ interposed Mr.", "\"그 주제는 우리 사이에 결코 입에 올리지 않았으면 하오, 퍼커,\""], ["Pickwick hastily.", "픽윅 씨가 황급히 끼어들었다."]], [["‘Pooh, pooh, my dear Sir,’ said the little man, untying the bundle, and glancing eagerly at Mr.", "\"쯧쯧, 친애하는 선생,\" 작달막한 사내가 뭉치를 풀며, 눈 가장자리로 픽윅 씨를 간절히 곁눈질하며 말했다."], ["Pickwick out of the corners of his eyes.", "\"이 얘긴 꼭 해야 합니다. 일부러 이러려고 온걸요."], ["‘It must be mentioned. I have come here on purpose.", "자, 제 말을 들으실 준비가 되셨습니까, 친애하는 선생?"], ["Now, are you ready to hear what I have to say, my dear Sir?", "급할 거 없습니다. 준비가 안 되셨다면 기다릴 수 있어요."], ["No hurry; if you are not, I can wait.", "여기 오늘 아침 신문이 있으니까요."], ["I have this morning’s paper here.", "선생 시간에 맞추겠습니다."], ["Your time shall be mine.", "자, 보십시오!\""], ["There!’ Hereupon, the little man threw one leg over the other, and made a show of beginning to read with great composure and application.", "그러면서 작달막한 사내는 한쪽 다리를 다른 쪽 다리 위에 포개고, 자못 침착하고 열성적으로 신문을 읽기 시작하는 시늉을 했다."]], [["‘Well, well,’ said Mr.", "\"알았소, 알았어,\""], ["Pickwick, with a sigh, but softening into a smile at the same time.", "픽윅 씨가 한숨을 쉬면서도 동시에 미소로 누그러지며 말했다."], ["‘Say what you have to say; it’s the old story, I suppose?’", "\"할 말이 있으면 하시오. 늘 하던 얘기겠지, 아마?\""]], [["‘With a difference, my dear Sir; with a difference,’ rejoined Perker, deliberately folding up the paper and putting it into his pocket again.", "\"조금 다릅니다, 친애하는 선생."], ["‘Mrs.", "조금 달라요,\""], ["Bardell, the plaintiff in the action, is within these walls, Sir.’", "퍼커가 신문을 천천히 접어 다시 주머니에 넣으며 대꾸했다. \"이 소송의 원고인 바델 부인이 지금 이 담장 안에 있습니다, 선생.\""]], [["‘I know it,’ was Mr.", "\"알고 있소,\""], ["Pickwick’s reply.", "픽윅 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘Very good,’ retorted Perker.", "\"좋습니다,\" 퍼커가 받아쳤다."], ["‘And you know how she comes here, I suppose; I mean on what grounds, and at whose suit?’", "\"그럼 그 부인이 어떻게 여기 오게 됐는지도 아시겠지요? 무슨 사유로, 누구의 소송으로 왔는지 말입니다.\""]], [["‘Yes; at least I have heard Sam’s account of the matter,’ said Mr.", "\"그렇소. 적어도 샘이 그 일을 설명하는 걸 들었소,\""], ["Pickwick, with affected carelessness.", "픽윅 씨가 짐짓 무심한 척하며 말했다."]], [["‘Sam’s account of the matter,’ replied Perker, ‘is, I will venture to say, a perfectly correct one.", "\"샘의 설명은,\" 퍼커가 대답했다."], ["Well now, my dear Sir, the first question I have to ask, is, whether this woman is to remain here?’", "\"감히 말씀드리건대 완벽하게 정확한 것입니다. 자, 친애하는 선생, 제가 여쭤야 할 첫 번째 질문은, 이 여자가 여기 그대로 머물러야 하느냐는 것입니다.\""]], [["‘To remain here!’ echoed Mr.", "\"여기 그대로 머문다고!\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 되뇌었다."]], [["‘To remain here, my dear Sir,’ rejoined Perker, leaning back in his chair and looking steadily at his client.", "\"여기 그대로 머문다는 것입니다, 친애하는 선생,\" 퍼커가 의자에 등을 기대고 자기 의뢰인을 가만히 바라보며 대꾸했다."]], [["‘How can you ask me?’ said that gentleman.", "\"어찌 내게 그걸 묻소?\" 그 신사가 말했다."], ["‘It rests with Dodson and Fogg; you know that very well.’", "\"그건 도드슨과 포그에게 달린 일이오. 당신도 그건 잘 알지 않소.\""]], [["‘I know nothing of the kind,’ retorted Perker firmly.", "\"전 그런 건 전혀 모르겠습니다,\" 퍼커가 단호히 받아쳤다."], ["‘It does not rest with Dodson and Fogg; you know the men, my dear Sir, as well as I do.", "\"그건 도드슨과 포그에게 달린 일이 아닙니다. 친애하는 선생, 선생도 그자들을 저만큼 잘 아시잖습니까."], ["It rests solely, wholly, and entirely with you.’", "그건 오로지, 전적으로, 온전히 선생께 달린 일입니다.\""]], [["‘With me!’ ejaculated Mr.", "\"내게!\""], ["Pickwick, rising nervously from his chair, and reseating himself directly afterwards.", "픽윅 씨가 신경질적으로 의자에서 벌떡 일어났다가 곧바로 다시 앉으며 내뱉었다."]], [["The little man gave a double-knock on the lid of his snuff-box, opened it, took a great pinch, shut it up again, and repeated the words, ‘With you.’", "작달막한 사내는 코담뱃갑 뚜껑을 두 번 똑똑 두드리고는 갑을 열어 코담배를 한 줌 듬뿍 집은 뒤 다시 닫고, \"선생께\"라는 말을 되풀이했다."]], [["‘I say, my dear Sir,’ resumed the little man, who seemed to gather confidence from the snuff--‘I say, that her speedy liberation or perpetual imprisonment rests with you, and with you alone. Hear me out, my dear Sir, if you please, and do not be so very energetic, for it will only put you into a perspiration and do no good whatever. I say,’ continued Perker, checking off each position on a different finger, as he laid it down--‘I say that nobody but you can rescue her from this den of wretchedness; and that you can only do that, by paying the costs of this suit--both of plaintive and defendant--into the hands of these Freeman Court sharks. Now pray be quiet, my dear sir.’", "\"제 말씀은, 친애하는 선생,\" 코담배에서 자신감을 얻은 듯한 작달막한 사내가 말을 이었다. \"제 말씀은, 그 여자가 곧 풀려나느냐 영영 갇혀 있느냐가 선생께, 오직 선생께만 달려 있다는 것입니다. 부디 제 말을 끝까지 들어 주십시오, 친애하는 선생. 그리고 그렇게 흥분하지 마십시오. 땀만 나고 아무 도움도 안 됩니다. 제 말씀은,\" 퍼커가 한 가지 논점을 꺼낼 때마다 손가락을 하나씩 꼽으며 말을 이었다. \"이 비참함의 소굴에서 그 여자를 구해 낼 수 있는 사람은 선생밖에 없다는 것입니다. 그리고 선생께서 그렇게 하실 수 있는 유일한 방법은, 이 소송 비용—원고 측과 피고 측 모두의 비용—을 저 프리먼 코트의 상어 같은 자들 손에 치르는 것뿐입니다. 자, 부디 진정하십시오, 친애하는 선생.\""]], [["Mr. Pickwick, whose face had been undergoing most surprising changes during this speech, and was evidently on the verge of a strong burst of indignation, calmed his wrath as well as he could.", "이 말이 이어지는 동안 픽윅 씨의 얼굴은 놀라우리만치 다양하게 변해 갔고, 분명 격한 분노가 터질락 말락 한 지경이었으나, 그는 있는 힘껏 노여움을 가라앉혔다."], ["Perker, strengthening his argumentative powers with another pinch of snuff, proceeded--", "퍼커는 코담배를 한 줌 더 집어 논변의 힘을 보강한 뒤 말을 이었다."]], [["‘I have seen the woman, this morning.", "\"오늘 아침에 제가 그 여자를 만났습니다. 비용을 치르면, 손해 배상에 대한 완전한 면제와 청산을 얻을 수 있습니다."], ["By paying the costs, you can obtain a full release and discharge from the damages; and further--this I know is a far greater object of consideration with you, my dear sir--a voluntary statement, under her hand, in the form of a letter to me, that this business was, from the very first, fomented, and encouraged, and brought about, by these men, Dodson and Fogg; that she deeply regrets ever having been the instrument of annoyance or injury to you; and that she entreats me to intercede with you, and implore your pardon.’", "게다가—이건 분명 선생께서 훨씬 더 중하게 여기실 바이지만—그 여자가 친필 서명을 한 자발적 진술서, 그러니까 제게 보내는 편지 형식의 진술서를 받을 수 있습니다. 그 내용인즉, 이 사건은 애초부터 도드슨과 포그라는 자들에 의해 부추겨지고 조장되고 꾸며진 것이라는 점, 그 여자가 선생을 괴롭히고 해를 끼치는 도구가 되었던 것을 깊이 뉘우친다는 점, 그리고 제게 선생을 중재해 달라, 선생의 용서를 구해 달라 간청한다는 점입니다.\""]], [["‘If I pay her costs for her,’ said Mr.", "\"내가 그 여자의 비용을 대신 치러 준다면 말이지,\""], ["Pickwick indignantly.", "픽윅 씨가 분개해 말했다."], ["‘A valuable document, indeed!’", "\"참으로 값진 문서로군!\""]], [["‘No “if” in the case, my dear Sir,’ said Perker triumphantly.", "\"이 경우엔 '~한다면'이란 게 없습니다, 친애하는 선생,\""], ["‘There is the very letter I speak of.", "퍼커가 의기양양하게 말했다. \"여기 바로 제가 말씀드린 그 편지가 있습니다."], ["Brought to my office by another woman at nine o’clock this morning, before I had set foot in this place, or held any communication with Mrs.", "오늘 아침 아홉 시에, 제가 이 자리에 발을 들이거나 바델 부인과 어떤 연락도 주고받기 전에, 다른 여자가 제 사무소로 가져온 것입니다. 명예를 걸고 맹세합니다.\""], ["Bardell, upon my honour.’ Selecting the letter from the bundle, the little lawyer laid it at Mr. Pickwick’s elbow, and took snuff for two consecutive minutes, without winking.", "작달막한 변호사는 뭉치에서 그 편지를 골라내 픽윅 씨의 팔꿈치 옆에 놓고는, 눈도 깜빡이지 않은 채 꼬박 이 분 동안 코담배를 들이마셨다."]], [["‘Is this all you have to say to me?’ inquired Mr.", "\"내게 할 말이라는 게 이게 다요?\""], ["Pickwick mildly.", "픽윅 씨가 부드럽게 물었다."]], [["‘Not quite,’ replied Perker. ‘I cannot undertake to say, at this moment, whether the wording of the cognovit, the nature of the ostensible consideration, and the proof we can get together about the whole conduct of the suit, will be sufficient to justify an indictment for conspiracy. I fear not, my dear Sir; they are too clever for that, I doubt. I do mean to say, however, that the whole facts, taken together, will be sufficient to justify you, in the minds of all reasonable men. And now, my dear Sir, I put it to you. This one hundred and fifty pounds, or whatever it may be--take it in round numbers--is nothing to you. A jury had decided against you; well, their verdict is wrong, but still they decided as they thought right, and it is against you. You have now an opportunity, on easy terms, of placing yourself in a much higher position than you ever could, by remaining here; which would only be imputed, by people who didn’t know you, to sheer dogged, wrongheaded, brutal obstinacy; nothing else, my dear Sir, believe me. Can you hesitate to avail yourself of it, when it restores you to your friends, your old pursuits, your health and amusements; when it liberates your faithful and attached servant, whom you otherwise doom to imprisonment for the whole of your life; and above all, when it enables you to take the very magnanimous revenge--which I know, my dear sir, is one after your own heart--of releasing this woman from a scene of misery and debauchery, to which no man should ever be consigned, if I had my will, but the infliction of which on any woman, is even more frightful and barbarous. Now I ask you, my dear sir, not only as your legal adviser, but as your very true friend, will you let slip the occasion of attaining all these objects, and doing all this good, for the paltry consideration of a few pounds finding their way into the pockets of a couple of rascals, to whom it makes no manner of difference, except that the more they gain, the more they’ll seek, and so the sooner be led into some piece of knavery that must end in a crash? I have put these considerations to you, my dear Sir, very feebly and imperfectly, but I ask you to think of them. Turn them over in your mind as long as you please. I wait here most patiently for your answer.’", "\"꼭 그렇진 않습니다,\" 퍼커가 대답했다. \"이 순간에 제가 장담할 수는 없습니다만, 채무 인낙서의 문구, 표면상의 약인의 성격, 그리고 이 소송의 전 과정에 관해 우리가 그러모을 수 있는 증거가, 공모 혐의로 기소하기에 충분할지 어떨지는 모르겠습니다. 안 될까 걱정입니다, 친애하는 선생. 그러기엔 그자들이 너무 영악하니까요, 아무래도. 하지만 이것만은 말씀드리겠습니다. 모든 사실을 종합해 보면, 분별 있는 모든 사람의 마음속에서 선생을 정당화하기에 충분하다는 것을요. 자, 친애하는 선생, 이제 솔직히 여쭙겠습니다. 이 백오십 파운드, 혹은 얼마가 됐든—어림수로 잡아서요—그건 선생께 아무것도 아닙니다. 배심원단이 선생께 불리한 평결을 내렸지요. 그래요, 그들의 평결은 잘못됐습니다만, 그래도 그들은 옳다고 생각한 대로 결정한 것이고, 그건 어쨌든 선생께 불리합니다. 지금 선생께는 손쉬운 조건으로, 여기 그냥 머무는 것으로는 결코 이를 수 없는 훨씬 더 높은 위치에 자신을 세울 기회가 있습니다. 여기 머무는 것은, 선생을 모르는 사람들 눈에는 그저 완고하고 고집스럽고 무지막지한 외곬으로밖에 비치지 않을 겁니다. 다른 그 무엇도 아니에요, 친애하는 선생, 제 말을 믿으십시오. 이 기회를 잡으시는 데 망설일 까닭이 있겠습니까? 그것은 선생을 친구들에게, 옛 일들에게, 건강과 즐거움에게 되돌려 줍니다. 또 그것은 선생의 충실하고 헌신적인 하인을—그렇지 않으면 선생께서 평생 옥살이를 선고하시는 셈인 그 하인을—풀어 줍니다. 그리고 무엇보다도, 그것은 선생께 더없이 도량 넓은 복수를—제가 알기로, 친애하는 선생, 이건 바로 선생 마음에 꼭 드는 종류의 것입니다만—할 수 있게 해 줍니다. 곧, 이 여자를 비참함과 타락의 현장에서 풀어 주는 일 말입니다. 제 뜻대로라면 그 어떤 남자도 그런 곳에 보내져선 안 되거니와, 하물며 여자에게 그것을 가하는 일은 한층 더 끔찍하고 야만적이지요. 자, 친애하는 선생, 이제 여쭙겠습니다. 선생의 법률 고문으로서뿐 아니라 진정한 벗으로서 여쭙는 것입니다만, 이 모든 목적을 이루고 이 모든 선을 행할 이 기회를, 고작 몇 파운드가 두 악당의 주머니로 흘러 들어가는 것을 막겠다는 그 하찮은 이유로 흘려보내시겠습니까? 그 몇 파운드란 그자들에겐 아무런 차이도 만들지 못합니다. 그저 더 많이 챙길수록 더 많이 탐낼 테고, 그러다 그만큼 더 빨리 어떤 악행에 빠져들어 결국 파국으로 끝날 뿐이지요. 친애하는 선생, 제가 이 여러 사정을 매우 서툴고 불완전하게 말씀드렸습니다만, 부디 곰곰이 생각해 보시기를 청합니다. 마음속에서 얼마든지 오래 굴려 보십시오. 저는 여기서 더없이 참을성 있게 선생의 대답을 기다리겠습니다.\""]], [["Before Mr. Pickwick could reply, before Mr. Perker had taken one twentieth part of the snuff with which so unusually long an address imperatively required to be followed up, there was a low murmuring of voices outside, and then a hesitating knock at the door.", "픽윅 씨가 미처 대답하기도 전에, 그리고 퍼커 씨가 이토록 유난히 긴 연설 뒤에 반드시 뒤따라야 할 코담배의 이십분의 일도 채 들이마시기 전에, 밖에서 나직한 웅성거림이 일더니 머뭇거리는 노크 소리가 문에서 났다."]], [["‘Dear, dear,’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"어허, 이런,\""], ["Pickwick, who had been evidently roused by his friend’s appeal; ‘what an annoyance that door is!", "친구의 호소에 분명히 마음이 움직였던 픽윅 씨가 소리쳤다. \"저 문이란 게 어찌 이리 성가신지!"], ["Who is that?’", "거 누구요?\""]], [["‘Me, Sir,’ replied Sam Weller, putting in his head.", "\"접니다, 나리,\" 샘 웰러가 머리를 들이밀며 대답했다."]], [["‘I can’t speak to you just now, Sam,’ said Mr. Pickwick.", "\"지금은 자네와 얘기할 수 없네, 샘,\""], ["‘I am engaged at this moment, Sam.’", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"지금 바쁜 참일세, 샘.\""]], [["‘Beg your pardon, Sir,’ rejoined Mr.", "\"죄송합니다, 나리,\" 웰러 씨가 대꾸했다."], ["Weller. ‘But here’s a lady here, Sir, as says she’s somethin’ wery partickler to disclose.’", "\"하지만 여기 어떤 부인이 와 계신데요, 나리, 아주 특별히 털어놓을 게 있다고 하십니다.\""]], [["‘I can’t see any lady,’ replied Mr.", "\"어떤 부인도 만날 수 없네,\""], ["Pickwick, whose mind was filled with visions of Mrs. Bardell.", "머릿속이 바델 부인의 환영으로 가득한 픽윅 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘I wouldn’t make too sure o’ that, Sir,’ urged Mr.", "\"그건 너무 장담 마십시오, 나리,\""], ["Weller, shaking his head.", "웰러 씨가 고개를 저으며 다그쳤다."], ["‘If you know’d who was near, sir, I rayther think you’d change your note; as the hawk remarked to himself vith a cheerful laugh, ven he heerd the robin-redbreast a-singin’ round the corner.’", "\"누가 가까이 와 있는지 아신다면, 나리, 제 생각엔 곡조를 바꾸실 것 같습니다요. 매가 모퉁이 돌아서 울새가 지저귀는 소리를 듣고는 흐뭇하게 웃으며 혼잣말하던 것처럼 말입니다.\""]], [["‘Who is it?’ inquired Mr.", "\"그게 누군가?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘Will you see her, Sir?’ asked Mr.", "\"만나 보시겠습니까, 나리?\""], ["Weller, holding the door in his hand as if he had some curious live animal on the other side.", "웰러 씨가 마치 문 저쪽에 무슨 신기한 산짐승이라도 있는 양 문을 손으로 잡은 채 물었다."]], [["‘I suppose I must,’ said Mr.", "\"만나야겠지, 아무래도,\""], ["Pickwick, looking at Perker.", "픽윅 씨가 퍼커를 바라보며 말했다."]], [["‘Well then, all in to begin!’ cried Sam.", "\"그럼, 다들 들어와서 시작합시다!\""], ["‘Sound the gong, draw up the curtain, and enter the two conspiraytors.’", "샘이 외쳤다. \"징을 울리고, 막을 걷고, 두 공모자께서 등장하시오.\""]], [["As Sam Weller spoke, he threw the door open, and there rushed tumultuously into the room, Mr.", "샘 웰러가 이렇게 말하면서 문을 활짝 열어젖히자, 너새니얼 윈클 씨가 와락 방 안으로 뛰어들어 왔는데, 그 뒤로 어느 젊은 숙녀의 손을 잡아 이끌고 있었다."], ["Nathaniel Winkle, leading after him by the hand, the identical young lady who at Dingley Dell had worn the boots with the fur round the tops, and who, now a very pleasing compound of blushes and confusion, and lilac silk, and a smart bonnet, and a rich lace veil, looked prettier than ever.", "바로 딩글리 델에서 윗부분에 털을 두른 부츠를 신었던 그 숙녀로, 이제는 발그레한 홍조와 수줍음, 연보랏빛 비단과 멋진 보닛, 그리고 값진 레이스 베일이 어우러진 더없이 사랑스러운 모습으로, 어느 때보다도 예뻐 보였다."]], [["‘Miss Arabella Allen!’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"아라벨라 앨런 양!\""], ["Pickwick, rising from his chair.", "픽윅 씨가 의자에서 일어서며 소리쳤다."]], [["‘No,’ replied Mr.", "\"아닙니다,\""], ["Winkle, dropping on his knees.", "윈클 씨가 무릎을 꿇으며 대답했다."], ["‘Mrs. Winkle.", "\"윈클 부인입니다."], ["Pardon, my dear friend, pardon!’", "용서하십시오, 친애하는 친구여, 용서해 주십시오!\""]], [["Mr.", "픽윅 씨는 자기 감각이 일러 주는 바를 좀처럼 믿을 수가 없었고, 퍼커의 미소 짓는 얼굴이 보태 주는 뒷받침의 증언과, 뒤편에 실제로 와 있는 샘과 예쁜 하녀의 모습이 아니었다면 아마 믿지 않았을 것이다."], ["Pickwick could scarcely believe the evidence of his senses, and perhaps would not have done so, but for the corroborative testimony afforded by the smiling countenance of Perker, and the bodily presence, in the background, of Sam and the pretty housemaid; who appeared to contemplate the proceedings with the liveliest satisfaction.", "샘과 하녀는 이 일이 벌어지는 것을 더없이 흐뭇하게 지켜보고 있는 듯했다."]], [["‘Oh, Mr.", "\"오, 픽윅 씨!\""], ["Pickwick!’ said Arabella, in a low voice, as if alarmed at the silence.", "아라벨라가 그 침묵에 겁이라도 난 듯 나직한 목소리로 말했다."], ["‘Can you forgive my imprudence?’", "\"제 경솔함을 용서해 주실 수 있나요?\""]], [["Mr. Pickwick returned no verbal response to this appeal; but he took off his spectacles in great haste, and seizing both the young lady’s hands in his, kissed her a great number of times--perhaps a greater number than was absolutely necessary--and then, still retaining one of her hands, told Mr. Winkle he was an audacious young dog, and bade him get up. This, Mr. Winkle, who had been for some seconds scratching his nose with the brim of his hat, in a penitent manner, did; whereupon Mr. Pickwick slapped him on the back several times, and then shook hands heartily with Perker, who, not to be behind-hand in the compliments of the occasion, saluted both the bride and the pretty housemaid with right good-will, and, having wrung Mr. Winkle’s hand most cordially, wound up his demonstrations of joy by taking snuff enough to set any half-dozen men with ordinarily-constructed noses, a-sneezing for life.", "픽윅 씨는 이 호소에 말로는 아무런 대답도 하지 않았으나, 황급히 안경을 벗고는 젊은 숙녀의 두 손을 자기 손에 잡아 여러 번 입을 맞추었는데—어쩌면 꼭 필요한 것보다 더 여러 번이었을 것이다—그러고는 여전히 그녀의 한 손을 쥔 채, 윈클 씨더러 대담한 강아지 같은 놈이라며 어서 일어나라고 일렀다. 몇 초 동안 참회하는 태도로 모자 챙으로 코를 긁어 대던 윈클 씨가 일어서자, 픽윅 씨는 그의 등을 여러 번 철썩 두드린 뒤 퍼커와 진심으로 악수를 나누었다. 퍼커도 이런 경사의 인사에 뒤지지 않으려고, 신부와 예쁜 하녀에게 한껏 호의를 담아 인사를 건넸으며, 윈클 씨의 손을 더없이 정답게 꽉 쥔 뒤, 평범하게 생긴 코를 가진 사내 대여섯쯤은 평생 재채기하게 만들 만큼의 코담배를 들이마시는 것으로 기쁨의 표현을 마무리했다."]], [["‘Why, my dear girl,’ said Mr.", "\"이런, 얘야,\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick, ‘how has all this come about?", "\"이게 다 어찌 된 일이냐?"], ["Come!", "자!"], ["Sit down, and let me hear it all.", "앉아라, 그리고 다 들려다오."], ["How well she looks, doesn’t she, Perker?’ added Mr.", "정말 좋아 보이지 않소, 퍼커?\""], ["Pickwick, surveying Arabella’s face with a look of as much pride and exultation, as if she had been his daughter.", "픽윅 씨가 마치 아라벨라가 제 딸이라도 되는 양 자랑스럽고 의기양양한 눈빛으로 그녀의 얼굴을 살피며 덧붙였다."]], [["‘Delightful, my dear Sir,’ replied the little man.", "\"근사합니다, 친애하는 선생,\""], ["‘If I were not a married man myself, I should be disposed to envy you, you dog.’ Thus expressing himself, the little lawyer gave Mr.", "작달막한 사내가 대답했다. \"저도 유부남만 아니라면, 자네를 시샘하고 싶을 지경이야, 이 능청맞은 친구.\""], ["Winkle a poke in the chest, which that gentleman reciprocated; after which they both laughed very loudly, but not so loudly as Mr.", "이렇게 말하면서 작달막한 변호사는 윈클 씨의 가슴을 쿡 찔렀고, 윈클 씨도 그대로 되갚았다."], ["Samuel Weller, who had just relieved his feelings by kissing the pretty housemaid under cover of the cupboard door.", "그러고는 둘 다 매우 큰 소리로 웃었으나, 마침 찬장 문 뒤에서 예쁜 하녀에게 입을 맞춰 자기 감정을 막 풀어낸 새뮤얼 웰러 씨만큼 크지는 않았다."]], [["‘I can never be grateful enough to you, Sam, I am sure,’ said Arabella, with the sweetest smile imaginable.", "\"제가 아무리 감사해도 모자랄 거예요, 샘, 정말이에요,\" 아라벨라가 더없이 다정한 미소를 지으며 말했다."], ["‘I shall not forget your exertions in the garden at Clifton.’", "\"클리프턴 정원에서 애써 주신 일을 잊지 않을게요.\""]], [["‘Don’t say nothin’ wotever about it, ma’am,’ replied Sam.", "\"그런 건 아무 말씀 마십시오, 마님,\" 샘이 대답했다."], ["‘I only assisted natur, ma’am; as the doctor said to the boy’s mother, after he’d bled him to death.’", "\"전 그저 자연을 거들어 드렸을 뿐인걸요, 마님. 의사가 사내아이를 피 빼다가 죽여 놓고 그 어미한테 한 말처럼요.\""]], [["‘Mary, my dear, sit down,’ said Mr.", "\"메리, 얘야, 앉으렴,\""], ["Pickwick, cutting short these compliments.", "픽윅 씨가 이 인사치레들을 끊으며 말했다."], ["‘Now then; how long have you been married, eh?’", "\"자, 그래. 결혼한 지는 얼마나 됐느냐, 응?\""]], [["Arabella looked bashfully at her lord and master, who replied, ‘Only three days.’", "아라벨라가 수줍게 자기 주인이자 남편을 바라보자, 그가 대답했다. \"고작 사흘입니다.\""]], [["‘Only three days, eh?’ said Mr.", "\"고작 사흘이라고, 응?\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick. ‘Why, what have you been doing these three months?’", "\"이런, 그럼 지난 석 달 동안엔 대체 뭘 한 게냐?\""]], [["‘Ah, to be sure!’ interposed Perker; ‘come, account for this idleness.", "\"아, 그러게나 말일세!\""], ["You see Mr.", "퍼커가 끼어들었다."], ["Pickwick’s only astonishment is, that it wasn’t all over, months ago.’", "\"어디, 그 빈둥거린 까닭을 좀 대 보게나. 보다시피 픽윅 씨가 놀라는 건 다름 아니라, 그게 벌써 몇 달 전에 다 끝나지 않았느냐는 것이거든.\""]], [["‘Why the fact is,’ replied Mr.", "\"실은 이렇습니다,\""], ["Winkle, looking at his blushing young wife, ‘that I could not persuade Bella to run away, for a long time.", "윈클 씨가 발그레해진 어린 아내를 바라보며 대답했다. \"벨라를 함께 달아나자고 설득하는 데 오랜 시간이 걸렸습니다."], ["And when I had persuaded her, it was a long time more before we could find an opportunity.", "그리고 겨우 설득한 뒤에도, 기회를 잡기까지 또 한참이 걸렸지요."], ["Mary had to give a month’s warning, too, before she could leave her place next door, and we couldn’t possibly have done it without her assistance.’", "메리도 옆집 일자리를 그만두려면 한 달 전에 미리 알려야 했는데, 그녀의 도움 없이는 도저히 해낼 수가 없었거든요.\""]], [["Upon my word,’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"이런, 참,\""], ["Pickwick, who by this time had resumed his spectacles, and was looking from Arabella to Winkle, and from Winkle to Arabella, with as much delight depicted in his countenance as warmheartedness and kindly feeling can communicate to the human face-- ‘upon my word!", "픽윅 씨가 소리쳤다. 이때쯤 그는 다시 안경을 쓰고서, 따뜻한 마음과 친절한 정이 인간의 얼굴에 전할 수 있는 온갖 기쁨을 표정 가득 담은 채, 아라벨라에서 윈클로, 윈클에서 다시 아라벨라로 번갈아 보고 있었다."], ["you seem to have been very systematic in your proceedings.", "\"이런, 참! 자네들 꽤 빈틈없이 일을 꾸민 모양이로군."], ["And is your brother acquainted with all this, my dear?’", "그래, 얘야, 네 오라버니는 이걸 다 알고 있느냐?\""]], [["‘Oh, no, no,’ replied Arabella, changing colour.", "\"오, 아니에요, 아니에요,\" 아라벨라가 안색을 바꾸며 대답했다."], ["‘Dear Mr. Pickwick, he must only know it from you--from your lips alone.", "\"친애하는 픽윅 씨, 오라버니는 오직 선생님을 통해서만—오직 선생님 입을 통해서만 알아야 해요."], ["He is so violent, so prejudiced, and has been so--so anxious in behalf of his friend, Mr.", "오라버니는 워낙 불같고, 워낙 편견이 심한 데다, 자기 친구 소여 씨를 위해서 어찌나—어찌나 마음을 써 왔는지,\""], ["Sawyer,’ added Arabella, looking down, ‘that I fear the consequences dreadfully.’", "아라벨라가 고개를 떨구며 덧붙였다. \"그래서 그 결과가 정말 끔찍하게 두려워요.\""]], [["‘Ah, to be sure,’ said Perker gravely.", "\"아, 그렇고말고,\" 퍼커가 진지하게 말했다."], ["‘You must take this matter in hand for them, my dear sir.", "\"이 일은 선생께서 저 사람들을 위해 떠맡으셔야 합니다, 친애하는 선생."], ["These young men will respect you, when they would listen to nobody else.", "저 젊은이들은 다른 누구의 말도 듣지 않을 때조차 선생은 존중할 겁니다."], ["You must prevent mischief, my dear Sir.", "화를 미연에 막으셔야 합니다, 친애하는 선생."], ["Hot blood, hot blood.’ And the little man took a warning pinch, and shook his head doubtfully.", "뜨거운 피, 뜨거운 피니까요.\" 그러면서 작달막한 사내는 경계의 코담배를 한 줌 집어 들이마시고는 미심쩍은 듯 고개를 저었다."]], [["‘You forget, my love,’ said Mr.", "\"잊으셨군요, 얘야,\""], ["Pickwick gently, ‘you forget that I am a prisoner.’", "픽윅 씨가 부드럽게 말했다. \"내가 죄수 신세라는 걸 잊은 게로구나.\""]], [["‘No, indeed I do not, my dear Sir,’ replied Arabella.", "\"아니에요, 정말이지 잊지 않았어요, 친애하는 선생님,\""], ["‘I never have forgotten it.", "아라벨라가 대답했다."], ["I have never ceased to think how great your sufferings must have been in this shocking place.", "\"한 번도 잊은 적 없어요. 이 끔찍한 곳에서 선생님께서 얼마나 큰 고초를 겪으셨을지 늘 생각해 왔는걸요."], ["But I hoped that what no consideration for yourself would induce you to do, a regard to our happiness might.", "다만, 선생님 자신을 위해서는 결코 하지 않으실 일을, 저희의 행복을 헤아리는 마음에서라면 해 주실까 바랐던 거예요."], ["If my brother hears of this, first, from you, I feel certain we shall be reconciled.", "만약 제 오라버니가 이 일을 선생님께 먼저 듣게 된다면, 저희가 화해하리라고 확신해요."], ["He is my only relation in the world, Mr.", "오라버니는 이 세상에서 제 유일한 혈육이에요, 픽윅 씨."], ["Pickwick, and unless you plead for me, I fear I have lost even him.", "선생님께서 저를 위해 변호해 주시지 않는다면, 저는 그 오라버니마저 잃은 게 아닐까 두려워요."], ["I have done wrong, very, very wrong, I know.’ Here poor Arabella hid her face in her handkerchief, and wept bitterly.", "제가 잘못했어요, 정말, 정말 잘못한 거 알아요.\" 이쯤에서 가엾은 아라벨라는 손수건에 얼굴을 묻고 서럽게 울었다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick’s nature was a good deal worked upon, by these same tears; but when Mrs.", "픽윅 씨의 천성은 바로 이 눈물에 적잖이 마음이 흔들렸다."], ["Winkle, drying her eyes, took to coaxing and entreating in the sweetest tones of a very sweet voice, he became particularly restless, and evidently undecided how to act, as was evinced by sundry nervous rubbings of his spectacle-glasses, nose, tights, head, and gaiters.", "그러나 윈클 부인이 눈물을 닦고는, 더없이 고운 목소리의 가장 달콤한 어조로 어르고 간청하기 시작하자, 그는 유난히 안절부절못하며, 어찌해야 할지 갈피를 못 잡는 기색이 역력했는데, 안경알이며 코, 타이츠, 머리, 각반을 초조하게 자꾸 문지르는 모습이 이를 잘 드러냈다."]], [["Taking advantage of these symptoms of indecision, Mr. Perker (to whom, it appeared, the young couple had driven straight that morning) urged with legal point and shrewdness that Mr. Winkle, senior, was still unacquainted with the important rise in life’s flight of steps which his son had taken; that the future expectations of the said son depended entirely upon the said Winkle, senior, continuing to regard him with undiminished feelings of affection and attachment, which it was very unlikely he would, if this great event were long kept a secret from him; that Mr. Pickwick, repairing to Bristol to seek Mr. Allen, might, with equal reason, repair to Birmingham to seek Mr. Winkle, senior; lastly, that Mr. Winkle, senior, had good right and title to consider Mr. Pickwick as in some degree the guardian and adviser of his son, and that it consequently behoved that gentleman, and was indeed due to his personal character, to acquaint the aforesaid Winkle, senior, personally, and by word of mouth, with the whole circumstances of the case, and with the share he had taken in the transaction.", "이 결단을 못 내리는 기색을 틈타, 퍼커 씨는—그날 아침 이 젊은 부부가 곧장 그에게로 마차를 몰고 왔던 모양이다—법률가다운 정연함과 빈틈없음으로 다음과 같이 다그쳤다. 곧, 아버지 윈클 씨는 아들이 인생의 계단에서 밟아 올라간 그 중대한 진전을 아직 모르고 있다는 것, 위에 말한 아들의 장래 기대는 전적으로 위에 말한 아버지 윈클 씨가 그를 줄어듦 없는 애정과 친애의 정으로 계속 대해 주느냐에 달려 있는데, 이 큰 일이 오래도록 비밀에 부쳐진다면 그가 그러기는 매우 어려우리라는 것, 픽윅 씨가 앨런 씨를 찾아 브리스틀로 가실 수 있다면, 같은 이치로 아버지 윈클 씨를 찾아 버밍엄으로도 가실 수 있으리라는 것, 끝으로, 아버지 윈클 씨는 픽윅 씨를 어느 정도 자기 아들의 후견인이자 조언자로 여길 만한 충분한 권리와 자격이 있으며, 따라서 그 신사가 위에 말한 아버지 윈클 씨를 직접, 입으로, 이 사건의 모든 정황과 자신이 이 일에 맡았던 몫에 관해 알려 드리는 것이 마땅하고, 실로 그 인품에 비추어 당연한 도리라는 것이었다."]], [["Mr. Tupman and Mr. Snodgrass arrived, most opportunely, in this stage of the pleadings, and as it was necessary to explain to them all that had occurred, together with the various reasons pro and con, the whole of the arguments were gone over again, after which everybody urged every argument in his own way, and at his own length. And, at last, Mr. Pickwick, fairly argued and remonstrated out of all his resolutions, and being in imminent danger of being argued and remonstrated out of his wits, caught Arabella in his arms, and declaring that she was a very amiable creature, and that he didn’t know how it was, but he had always been very fond of her from the first, said he could never find it in his heart to stand in the way of young people’s happiness, and they might do with him as they pleased.", "이 변론이 이쯤 진행되었을 때, 더없이 알맞게도 터프먼 씨와 스노드그래스 씨가 도착했고, 그들에게 그동안 일어난 모든 일과 더불어 찬반 양쪽의 갖가지 이유를 설명해야 했으므로 논의가 처음부터 다시 한 차례 되풀이되었으며, 그 뒤로는 저마다 자기 방식대로, 자기가 원하는 만큼 길게 온갖 논거를 다그쳤다. 그리고 마침내 픽윅 씨는 자신의 모든 결심을 깨끗이 논파당하고 따져 물림당한 끝에, 이러다 제정신마저 논파당해 잃을 위기에 임박하자, 아라벨라를 품에 끌어안으며, 그녀가 참으로 사랑스러운 사람이라는 것, 어찌 된 영문인지 자신도 모르겠으나 처음부터 늘 그녀를 무척 아껴 왔다는 것을 밝히고는, 젊은이들의 행복을 가로막을 마음은 도저히 들지 않는다며, 자네들 마음대로 나를 부려도 좋다고 말했다."]], [["Mr.", "이 양보를 듣고 웰러 씨가 맨 먼저 한 일은, 잡 트로터를 그 명망 높은 펠 씨에게로 급파하여, 이 편지를 지닌 자에게 정식 석방 서류를 내어 주라는 위임장을 전한 것이었다."], ["Weller’s first act, on hearing this concession, was to despatch Job Trotter to the illustrious Mr.", "그 석방 서류란, 그의 신중한 부친이 만일 어느 때고 비상시에 필요할 경우에 대비해 선견지명으로 그 박식한 신사의 손에 미리 맡겨 둔 것이었다."], ["Pell, with an authority to deliver to the bearer the formal discharge which his prudent parent had had the foresight to leave in the hands of that learned gentleman, in case it should be, at any time, required on an emergency; his next proceeding was, to invest his whole stock of ready-money in the purchase of five- and-twenty gallons of mild porter, which he himself dispensed on the racket-ground to everybody who would partake of it; this done, he hurra’d in divers parts of the building until he lost his voice, and then quietly relapsed into his usual collected and philosophical condition.", "그가 다음으로 한 일은, 가진 현금을 몽땅 털어 순한 흑맥주 스물다섯 갤런을 사들인 뒤, 라켓 운동장에서 마시고자 하는 모든 이에게 손수 따라 돌린 것이었다. 이를 마치고 나서 그는 건물 여기저기를 돌며 목이 쉴 때까지 만세를 부르고는, 조용히 평소의 침착하고 사색적인 상태로 되돌아갔다."]], [["At three o’clock that afternoon, Mr. Pickwick took a last look at his little room, and made his way, as well as he could, through the throng of debtors who pressed eagerly forward to shake him by the hand, until he reached the lodge steps.", "그날 오후 세 시에 픽윅 씨는 자신의 자그마한 방을 마지막으로 둘러본 뒤, 앞다투어 밀려들어 그와 악수하려는 채무자들 무리를 헤치며 가까스로 길을 내어 마침내 수위실 계단에 이르렀다."], ["He turned here, to look about him, and his eye lightened as he did so.", "그는 거기서 걸음을 멈추고 주위를 둘러보았고, 그렇게 둘러보는 동안 그의 눈이 환해졌다."], ["In all the crowd of wan, emaciated faces, he saw not one which was not happier for his sympathy and charity.", "창백하고 야윈 얼굴들로 가득한 그 군중 속에서, 그의 동정과 자선 덕분에 더 행복해지지 않은 얼굴은 단 하나도 보이지 않았던 것이다."]], [["‘Perker,’ said Mr.", "\"퍼커,\""], ["Pickwick, beckoning one young man towards him, ‘this is Mr.", "픽윅 씨가 한 젊은이를 손짓해 부르며 말했다."], ["Jingle, whom I spoke to you about.’", "\"이 사람이 내가 당신에게 말했던 그 징글이오.\""]], [["‘Very good, my dear Sir,’ replied Perker, looking hard at Jingle.", "\"좋습니다, 친애하는 선생,\" 퍼커가 징글을 빤히 쳐다보며 대답했다."], ["‘You will see me again, young man, to-morrow.", "\"젊은이, 내일 나를 다시 보게 될 걸세."], ["I hope you may live to remember and feel deeply, what I shall have to communicate, Sir.’", "내가 자네에게 전할 말을, 부디 오래 살아 깊이 새기고 절감하기를 바라네.\""]], [["Jingle bowed respectfully, trembled very much as he took Mr. Pickwick’s proffered hand, and withdrew.", "징글은 공손히 고개를 숙였고, 픽윅 씨가 내민 손을 잡을 때 몹시 떨더니, 물러갔다."]], [["‘Job you know, I think?’ said Mr.", "\"잡은 자네도 아는 사이지, 아마?\""], ["Pickwick, presenting that gentleman.", "픽윅 씨가 그 신사를 소개하며 말했다."]], [["‘I know the rascal,’ replied Perker good-humouredly.", "\"그 악당이라면 알지요,\" 퍼커가 너그럽게 대답했다."], ["‘See after your friend, and be in the way to-morrow at one.", "\"자네 친구를 잘 돌보고, 내일 한 시에 나타나도록 하게."], ["Do you hear?", "알겠나?"], ["Now, is there anything more?’", "자, 그 밖에 더 있습니까?\""]], [["‘Nothing,’ rejoined Mr.", "\"없소,\" 픽윅 씨가 대꾸했다."], ["Pickwick. ‘You have delivered the little parcel I gave you for your old landlord, Sam?’", "\"샘, 자네 옛 주인 양반에게 전하라고 내가 준 그 작은 꾸러미는 전했나?\""]], [["‘I have, Sir,’ replied Sam.", "\"전했습니다, 나리,\" 샘이 대답했다."], ["‘He bust out a-cryin’, Sir, and said you wos wery gen’rous and thoughtful, and he only wished you could have him innockilated for a gallopin’ consumption, for his old friend as had lived here so long wos dead, and he’d noweres to look for another.’", "\"그분이 울음을 터뜨리시더니, 나리가 아주 후하고 사려 깊은 분이라며, 다만 자기를 급성 폐병에 걸리게 좀 해 주셨으면 좋겠다고 하셨습니다요. 이리 오래 같이 살던 옛 친구가 죽고 나니, 새 친구를 어디서 찾아야 할지 도무지 막막하다면서요.\""]], [["Poor fellow, poor fellow!’ said Mr.", "\"가엾은 사람, 가엾은 사람!\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick. ‘God bless you, my friends!’", "\"다들 신의 가호가 있기를, 친구들!\""]], [["As Mr. Pickwick uttered this adieu, the crowd raised a loud shout.", "픽윅 씨가 이 작별 인사를 입에 올리자, 군중은 우렁찬 함성을 질렀다."], ["Many among them were pressing forward to shake him by the hand again, when he drew his arm through Perker’s, and hurried from the prison, far more sad and melancholy, for the moment, than when he had first entered it.", "그들 가운데 많은 이가 다시 그와 악수하려고 앞으로 밀려들고 있었으나, 그는 자기 팔을 퍼커의 팔에 끼고는 감옥을 서둘러 빠져나왔는데, 그 순간만큼은 처음 그곳에 들어왔을 때보다 훨씬 더 슬프고 우울했다."], ["Alas! how many sad and unhappy beings had he left behind!", "아아, 그가 뒤에 남겨 두고 떠난 슬프고 불행한 사람들이 얼마나 많았던가!"]], [["A happy evening was that for at least one party in the George and Vulture; and light and cheerful were two of the hearts that emerged from its hospitable door next morning.", "조지 앤드 벌처에서 적어도 한 무리에게는 그날 저녁이 행복한 저녁이었다. 그리고 이튿날 아침 그 정겨운 문을 나선 두 사람의 마음은 가볍고 명랑했다."], ["The owners thereof were Mr. Pickwick and Sam Weller, the former of whom was speedily deposited inside a comfortable post-coach, with a little dickey behind, in which the latter mounted with great agility.", "그 주인공은 픽윅 씨와 샘 웰러였는데, 앞 사람은 이내 안락한 역마차 안에 자리 잡았고, 뒤쪽에는 작은 마부석이 달려 있어 뒷사람이 그 위에 날렵하게 올라탔다."]], [["‘Sir,’ called out Mr.", "\"나리,\""], ["Weller to his master.", "웰러 씨가 주인을 향해 소리쳤다."]], [["‘Well, Sam,’ replied Mr.", "\"왜 그러나, 샘,\""], ["Pickwick, thrusting his head out of the window.", "픽윅 씨가 창밖으로 머리를 내밀며 대답했다."]], [["‘I wish them horses had been three months and better in the Fleet, Sir.’", "\"저 말들이 플리트에서 석 달 넘게 갇혀 있었더라면 좋았겠습니다요, 나리.\""]], [["‘Why, Sam?’ inquired Mr.", "\"어째서, 샘?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘Wy, Sir,’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"아, 나리,\""], ["Weller, rubbing his hands, ‘how they would go if they had been!’", "웰러 씨가 두 손을 비비며 외쳤다. \"그랬다면 저놈들이 얼마나 잘 내달리겠습니까요!\""]], [["CHAPTER XLVIII. RELATES HOW MR. PICKWICK, WITH THE ASSISTANCE OF SAMUEL WELLER, ESSAYED TO SOFTEN THE HEART OF MR. BENJAMIN ALLEN, AND TO MOLLIFY THE WRATH OF MR. ROBERT SAWYER\nMr. Ben Allen and Mr. Bob Sawyer sat together in the little surgery behind the shop, discussing minced veal and future prospects, when the discourse, not unnaturally, turned upon the practice acquired by Bob the aforesaid, and his present chances of deriving a competent independence from the honourable profession to which he had devoted himself.", "벤 앨런 씨와 보브 소여 씨는 가게 뒤편의 작은 진료실에 함께 앉아 다진 송아지 고기와 앞으로의 전망을 두고 이야기를 나누고 있었는데, 화제는 자연스럽게도 앞서 말한 보브가 일궈 놓은 진료와, 그가 몸 바친 그 명예로운 직업에서 넉넉한 자립을 끌어낼 그의 현재 가능성으로 옮겨 갔다."]], [["‘Which, I think,’ observed Mr.", "\"내 생각에는 말이지,\""], ["Bob Sawyer, pursuing the thread of the subject--‘which, I think, Ben, are rather dubious.’", "보브 소여 씨가 화제의 실마리를 이어 가며 말했다. \"내 생각에는, 벤, 그게 좀 미심쩍단 말이야.\""]], [["‘What’s rather dubious?’ inquired Mr.", "\"뭐가 좀 미심쩍다는 거야?\""], ["Ben Allen, at the same time sharpening his intellect with a draught of beer.", "벤 앨런 씨가 맥주 한 모금으로 자기 지력을 가다듬으며 물었다."], ["‘What’s dubious?’", "\"뭐가 미심쩍다는 거지?\""]], [["‘Why, the chances,’ responded Mr.", "\"아니, 그 가능성 말이야.\""], ["Bob Sawyer.", "보브 소여 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘I forgot,’ said Mr.", "\"잊어버렸군,\""], ["Ben Allen.", "벤 앨런 씨가 말했다."], ["‘The beer has reminded me that I forgot, Bob--yes; they are dubious.’", "\"맥주를 보니 내가 잊고 있었던 게 떠오르는걸, 보브—그래, 그것들은 미심쩍지.\""]], [["‘It’s wonderful how the poor people patronise me,’ said Mr. Bob Sawyer reflectively.", "\"가난한 사람들이 나를 얼마나 끔찍이도 찾아 주는지 놀라울 정도야,\""], ["‘They knock me up, at all hours of the night; they take medicine to an extent which I should have conceived impossible; they put on blisters and leeches with a perseverance worthy of a better cause; they make additions to their families, in a manner which is quite awful.", "보브 소여 씨가 생각에 잠긴 듯 말했다. \"한밤중 아무 때나 나를 두들겨 깨우지, 약은 내가 도저히 불가능하다 여겼을 만큼 잔뜩 먹어 대고, 발포고와 거머리를 더 나은 일에나 어울릴 끈기로 붙여 대고, 또 식구는 정말 무시무시한 방식으로 불려 댄단 말이야."], ["Six of those last-named little promissory notes, all due on the same day, Ben, and all intrusted to me!’", "마지막에 말한 그 자그마한 약속어음이 여섯 장이나 되는데, 죄다 같은 날짜에 만기가 돌아오고, 죄다 나한테 맡겨졌단 말이야, 벤!\""]], [["‘It’s very gratifying, isn’t it?’ said Mr.", "\"참 흐뭇한 일 아닌가?\""], ["Ben Allen, holding his plate for some more minced veal.", "벤 앨런 씨가 다진 송아지 고기를 좀 더 달라고 접시를 내밀며 말했다."]], [["‘Oh, very,’ replied Bob; ‘only not quite so much so as the confidence of patients with a shilling or two to spare would be.", "\"오, 아주 그렇지,\" 보브가 대답했다."], ["This business was capitally described in the advertisement, Ben.", "\"다만 한두 실링쯤 여윳돈이 있는 환자들의 신뢰만큼 흐뭇하진 않을 뿐이야."], ["It is a practice, a very extensive practice--and that’s all.’", "이 사업은 광고에서 기막히게 잘 묘사돼 있었지, 벤. 진료, 그것도 아주 광범위한 진료야—그게 다라네.\""]], [["‘Bob,’ said Mr.", "\"보브,\""], ["Ben Allen, laying down his knife and fork, and fixing his eyes on the visage of his friend, ‘Bob, I’ll tell you what it is.’", "벤 앨런 씨가 나이프와 포크를 내려놓고 친구의 얼굴에 눈길을 박으며 말했다. \"보브, 내가 어떻게 된 건지 말해 주지.\""]], [["‘What is it?’ inquired Mr.", "\"어떻게 된 건데?\""], ["Bob Sawyer.", "보브 소여 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘You must make yourself, with as little delay as possible, master of Arabella’s one thousand pounds.’", "\"자네는 되도록 지체 없이 어래벨러의 천 파운드를 손에 넣어야 해.\""]], [["‘Three per cent.", "\"잉글랜드 은행 총재 회사의 장부, 또는 장부들에 지금 그녀 이름으로 올라 있는 3퍼센트 통합 은행 연금 말이지,\""], ["consolidated bank annuities, now standing in her name in the book or books of the governor and company of the Bank of England,’ added Bob Sawyer, in legal phraseology.", "보브 소여가 법률 용어로 덧붙였다."]], [["‘Exactly so,’ said Ben.", "\"바로 그거야,\" 벤이 말했다."], ["‘She has it when she comes of age, or marries.", "\"성년이 되거나 결혼하면 그 돈이 그녀 차지가 돼."], ["She wants a year of coming of age, and if you plucked up a spirit she needn’t want a month of being married.’", "성년까지는 한 해가 모자라지만, 자네가 기운만 좀 내면 결혼까지는 한 달도 모자랄 게 없어.\""]], [["‘She’s a very charming and delightful creature,’ quoth Mr.", "\"그녀는 아주 매력적이고 즐거운 사람이지,\""], ["Robert Sawyer, in reply; ‘and has only one fault that I know of, Ben.", "로버트 소여 씨가 대답했다. \"그리고 내가 아는 한 흠이라곤 하나뿐이야, 벤."], ["It happens, unfortunately, that that single blemish is a want of taste.", "불행히도 그 하나뿐인 결점이 안목이 없다는 거지."], ["She don’t like me.’", "그녀가 나를 좋아하질 않거든.\""]], [["‘It’s my opinion that she don’t know what she does like,’ said Mr.", "\"그 애는 자기가 뭘 좋아하는지도 모른다는 게 내 생각이야,\""], ["Ben Allen contemptuously.", "벤 앨런 씨가 경멸하듯 말했다."]], [["‘Perhaps not,’ remarked Mr. Bob Sawyer.", "\"그럴지도 모르지,\""], ["‘But it’s my opinion that she does know what she doesn’t like, and that’s of more importance.’", "보브 소여 씨가 말했다. \"하지만 그녀가 뭘 안 좋아하는지는 안다는 게 내 생각이고, 그게 더 중요한 문제거든.\""]], [["‘I wish,’ said Mr.", "\"내 소원은 말이야,\""], ["Ben Allen, setting his teeth together, and speaking more like a savage warrior who fed on raw wolf’s flesh which he carved with his fingers, than a peaceable young gentleman who ate minced veal with a knife and fork--‘I wish I knew whether any rascal really has been tampering with her, and attempting to engage her affections.", "벤 앨런 씨가 이를 악물고, 나이프와 포크로 다진 송아지 고기를 먹는 점잖은 청년이라기보다 손가락으로 잘라 낸 날 늑대 고기를 뜯어 먹는 야만 전사처럼 말했다. \"어떤 악당이라도 정말로 그 애를 꾀어내려 들고, 그 애 마음을 사로잡으려 했는지 알아내는 거야."], ["I think I should assassinate him, Bob.’", "그런 놈이라면 내가 암살해 버려야겠지, 보브.\""]], [["‘I’d put a bullet in him, if I found him out,’ said Mr.", "\"내가 그놈을 찾아내면 총알을 한 방 박아 넣겠어,\""], ["Sawyer, stopping in the course of a long draught of beer, and looking malignantly out of the porter pot.", "소여 씨가 맥주를 길게 들이켜다 말고 멈추어, 흑맥주 잔 너머로 사납게 노려보며 말했다."], ["‘If that didn’t do his business, I’d extract it afterwards, and kill him that way.’", "\"그래도 끝장이 안 나면, 그 뒤에 총알을 도로 꺼내서 그 방법으로 죽여 버리지.\""]], [["Mr. Benjamin Allen gazed abstractedly on his friend for some minutes in silence, and then said--", "벤저민 앨런 씨는 몇 분 동안 말없이 멍하니 친구를 바라보다가 이윽고 입을 열었다—"]], [["‘You have never proposed to her, point-blank, Bob?’", "\"자네 한 번도 그 애한테 단도직입으로 청혼한 적이 없지, 보브?\""]], [["‘No.", "\"없어."], ["Because I saw it would be of no use,’ replied Mr.", "소용없을 게 뻔히 보였으니까,\""], ["Robert Sawyer.", "로버트 소여 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘You shall do it, before you are twenty-four hours older,’ retorted Ben, with desperate calmness.", "\"자네는 스물네 시간이 더 지나기 전에 그렇게 해야 해,\" 벤이 필사적인 침착함으로 받아쳤다."], ["‘She shall have you, or I’ll know the reason why.", "\"그 애는 자네 사람이 되든지, 아니면 내가 그 까닭을 알아내고 말 거야."], ["I’ll exert my authority.’", "내 권위를 발휘하겠어.\""]], [["‘Well,’ said Mr.", "\"음,\""], ["Bob Sawyer, ‘we shall see.’", "보브 소여 씨가 말했다. \"두고 보자고.\""]], [["‘We shall see, my friend,’ replied Mr.", "\"두고 보세, 이 사람아,\""], ["Ben Allen fiercely.", "벤 앨런 씨가 사납게 대답했다."], ["He paused for a few seconds, and added in a voice broken by emotion, ‘You have loved her from a child, my friend.", "그는 몇 초간 말을 멈추었다가, 감정에 북받쳐 갈라진 목소리로 덧붙였다. \"자네는 어릴 때부터 그 애를 사랑했지, 이 사람아."], ["You loved her when we were boys at school together, and, even then, she was wayward and slighted your young feelings.", "우리가 함께 학교에 다니던 소년 시절에도 자네는 그 애를 사랑했어. 그런데 그때조차 그 애는 변덕스럽게도 자네의 어린 마음을 무시했지."], ["Do you recollect, with all the eagerness of a child’s love, one day pressing upon her acceptance, two small caraway-seed biscuits and one sweet apple, neatly folded into a circular parcel with the leaf of a copy-book?’", "기억나나, 어린아이의 사랑이 지닌 온갖 열성으로, 어느 날 작은 캐러웨이 씨 비스킷 두 개와 달콤한 사과 한 개를 공책 잎으로 동그랗게 곱게 싸서 그 애더러 받으라고 들이밀었던 일이?\""]], [["‘I do,’ replied Bob Sawyer.", "\"기억나지,\" 보브 소여가 대답했다."]], [["‘She slighted that, I think?’ said Ben Allen.", "\"그 애가 그걸 무시했지, 아마?\" 벤 앨런이 말했다."]], [["‘She did,’ rejoined Bob.", "\"그랬지,\" 보브가 받았다."], ["‘She said I had kept the parcel so long in the pockets of my corduroys, that the apple was unpleasantly warm.’", "\"내가 그 꾸러미를 코듀로이 바지 주머니에 너무 오래 넣고 다녀서 사과가 불쾌하게 미지근해졌다고 그러더군.\""]], [["‘I remember,’ said Mr.", "\"기억나,\""], ["Allen gloomily.", "앨런 씨가 침울하게 말했다."], ["‘Upon which we ate it ourselves, in alternate bites.’", "\"그래서 우리 둘이 번갈아 한 입씩 베어 먹어 치웠지.\""]], [["Bob Sawyer intimated his recollection of the circumstance last alluded to, by a melancholy frown; and the two friends remained for some time absorbed, each in his own meditations.", "보브 소여는 방금 언급된 그 정황을 기억한다는 뜻을 우울한 찡그림으로 내비쳤고, 두 친구는 한동안 각자 자기 상념에 잠겨 있었다."]], [["While these observations were being exchanged between Mr. Bob Sawyer and Mr.", "보브 소여 씨와 벤저민 앨런 씨 사이에 이런 말이 오가는 동안, 그리고 회색 제복을 입은 소년이 평소와 달리 식사가 길어지는 것을 의아해하며 이따금 유리문 쪽으로 불안한 눈길을 던지면서, 결국 자기 몫의 갈망에 돌아올 다진 송아지 고기의 양을 두고 마음속 불안에 시달리고 있는 동안, 브리스틀 거리를 따라 점잖게 굴러가는 것이 있었으니, 그것은 음울한 초록빛으로 칠한 개인용 마차였다."], ["Benjamin Allen; and while the boy in the gray livery, marvelling at the unwonted prolongation of the dinner, cast an anxious look, from time to time, towards the glass door, distracted by inward misgivings regarding the amount of minced veal which would be ultimately reserved for his individual cravings; there rolled soberly on through the streets of Bristol, a private fly, painted of a sad green colour, drawn by a chubby sort of brown horse, and driven by a surly-looking man with his legs dressed like the legs of a groom, and his body attired in the coat of a coachman.", "통통한 갈색 말이 끌고, 다리는 마부 종자의 다리처럼 차려입었으되 몸통에는 마부의 외투를 걸친 무뚝뚝한 인상의 남자가 몰고 있었다."], ["Such appearances are common to many vehicles belonging to, and maintained by, old ladies of economic habits; and in this vehicle sat an old lady who was its mistress and proprietor.", "이런 행색은 검소한 살림을 하는 노부인들이 소유하고 건사하는 많은 탈것에서 흔한 것이었고, 이 탈것에는 그 주인이자 소유주인 노부인이 타고 있었다."]], [["‘Martin!’ said the old lady, calling to the surly man, out of the front window.", "\"마틴!\" 노부인이 앞쪽 창밖으로 무뚝뚝한 남자를 부르며 말했다."]], [["‘Well?’ said the surly man, touching his hat to the old lady.", "\"예?\" 무뚝뚝한 남자가 노부인에게 모자에 손을 대며 말했다."]], [["‘Mr.", "\"소여 씨 댁으로.\""], ["Sawyer’s,’ said the old lady.", "노부인이 말했다."]], [["‘I was going there,’ said the surly man.", "\"가던 참입니다.\" 무뚝뚝한 남자가 말했다."]], [["The old lady nodded the satisfaction which this proof of the surly man’s foresight imparted to her feelings; and the surly man giving a smart lash to the chubby horse, they all repaired to Mr. Bob Sawyer’s together.", "노부인은 무뚝뚝한 남자의 이 선견지명이 그녀의 마음에 안겨 준 만족을 끄덕임으로 표했고, 무뚝뚝한 남자가 통통한 말에게 채찍을 매섭게 한 번 휘두르자 그들 모두는 함께 보브 소여 씨 댁으로 향했다."]], [["‘Martin!’ said the old lady, when the fly stopped at the door of Mr.", "\"마틴!\""], ["Robert Sawyer, late Nockemorf.", "마차가 옛 노케모프 가게의 로버트 소여 씨 댁 문 앞에 멈추자 노부인이 말했다."]], [["‘Well?’ said Martin.", "\"예?\" 마틴이 말했다."]], [["‘Ask the lad to step out, and mind the horse.’", "\"저 아이더러 나와서 말을 보라고 해.\""]], [["‘I’m going to mind the horse myself,’ said Martin, laying his whip on the roof of the fly.", "\"제가 직접 말을 보겠습니다,\" 마틴이 채찍을 마차 지붕 위에 올려놓으며 말했다."]], [["‘I can’t permit it, on any account,’ said the old lady; ‘your testimony will be very important, and I must take you into the house with me.", "\"그건 무슨 일이 있어도 허락할 수 없네,\" 노부인이 말했다."], ["You must not stir from my side during the whole interview.", "\"자네 증언이 아주 중요할 테니 자네를 데리고 안으로 들어가야 해. 면담이 진행되는 내내 내 곁을 떠나서는 안 돼."], ["Do you hear?’", "알아듣겠나?\""]], [["‘I hear,’ replied Martin.", "\"알아듣습니다,\" 마틴이 대답했다."]], [["‘Well; what are you stopping for?’", "\"그래, 뭘 꾸물거리는 게야?\""]], [["‘Nothing,’ replied Martin.", "\"아무것도 아닙니다,\""], ["So saying, the surly man leisurely descended from the wheel, on which he had been poising himself on the tops of the toes of his right foot, and having summoned the boy in the gray livery, opened the coach door, flung down the steps, and thrusting in a hand enveloped in a dark wash-leather glove, pulled out the old lady with as much unconcern in his manner as if she were a bandbox.", "마틴이 대답했다. 그렇게 말하면서 무뚝뚝한 남자는 오른발 발끝으로 균형을 잡고 올라타 있던 바퀴에서 느긋이 내려와, 회색 제복의 소년을 불러 놓고 마차 문을 열고 발판을 내린 다음, 짙은 색 섀미 가죽 장갑을 낀 손을 들이밀어, 마치 그녀가 무슨 모자 상자라도 되는 양 태연한 태도로 노부인을 끌어냈다."]], [["‘Dear me!’ exclaimed the old lady.", "\"어머나!\" 노부인이 외쳤다."], ["‘I am so flurried, now I have got here, Martin, that I’m all in a tremble.’", "\"이제 막상 여기 도착하고 나니, 마틴, 마음이 어찌나 어수선한지 온몸이 떨리는구나.\""]], [["Mr. Martin coughed behind the dark wash-leather gloves, but expressed no sympathy; so the old lady, composing herself, trotted up Mr. Bob Sawyer’s steps, and Mr. Martin followed. Immediately on the old lady’s entering the shop, Mr. Benjamin Allen and Mr. Bob Sawyer, who had been putting the spirits-and-water out of sight, and upsetting nauseous drugs to take off the smell of the tobacco smoke, issued hastily forth in a transport of pleasure and affection.", "마틴 씨는 짙은 색 섀미 가죽 장갑 뒤에서 기침을 했을 뿐 아무 동정도 내비치지 않았으므로, 노부인은 마음을 추슬러 보브 소여 씨 댁 계단을 종종걸음으로 올라갔고 마틴 씨가 뒤따랐다. 노부인이 가게에 들어서기가 무섭게, 술과 물을 안 보이는 데로 치우고 담배 연기 냄새를 없애려 역겨운 약품을 엎질러 두던 벤저민 앨런 씨와 보브 소여 씨가 기쁨과 애정에 들떠 황급히 나왔다."]], [["‘My dear aunt,’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"사랑하는 이모님,\" 벤 앨런 씨가 외쳤다."], ["Ben Allen, ‘how kind of you to look in upon us! Mr.", "\"저희를 들여다봐 주시다니 어찌나 친절하신지요!"], ["Sawyer, aunt; my friend Mr.", "소여 씨입니다, 이모님."], ["Bob Sawyer whom I have spoken to you about, regarding--you know, aunt.’ And here Mr.", "제가 이모님께 말씀드렸던 제 친구 보브 소여 씨인데, 바로—아시잖아요, 이모님.\""], ["Ben Allen, who was not at the moment extraordinarily sober, added the word ‘Arabella,’ in what was meant to be a whisper, but which was an especially audible and distinct tone of speech which nobody could avoid hearing, if anybody were so disposed.", "그러고는 마침 그리 멀쩡한 정신이 아니었던 벤 앨런 씨가 \"어래벨러\"라는 말을 속삭인다고 한 것이, 실은 누구라도 마음만 먹으면 안 들을 도리가 없을 만큼 유난히 또렷하고 분명한 어조로 덧붙였다."]], [["‘My dear Benjamin,’ said the old lady, struggling with a great shortness of breath, and trembling from head to foot, ‘don’t be alarmed, my dear, but I think I had better speak to Mr.", "\"사랑하는 벤저민,\" 노부인이 심한 숨 가쁨과 씨름하고 머리끝부터 발끝까지 떨면서 말했다."], ["Sawyer, alone, for a moment.", "\"놀라지 마라, 얘야, 하지만 내 생각에는 잠깐 소여 씨와 단둘이 이야기하는 게 좋을 것 같구나."], ["Only for one moment.’", "잠깐이면 돼.\""]], [["‘Bob,’ said Mr.", "\"보브,\""], ["Allen, ‘will you take my aunt into the surgery?’", "앨런 씨가 말했다. \"이모님을 진료실로 모셔다드리겠나?\""]], [["‘Certainly,’ responded Bob, in a most professional voice.", "\"물론이지,\" 보브가 더없이 직업적인 목소리로 대답했다."], ["‘Step this way, my dear ma’am.", "\"이쪽으로 오시지요, 사모님."], ["Don’t be frightened, ma’am.", "겁내지 마세요, 사모님."], ["We shall be able to set you to rights in a very short time, I have no doubt, ma’am. Here, my dear ma’am.", "아주 짧은 시간 안에 멀쩡하게 해 드릴 수 있을 겁니다, 틀림없이요, 사모님."], ["Now then!’ With this, Mr.", "자, 이쪽입니다, 사모님."], ["Bob Sawyer having handed the old lady to a chair, shut the door, drew another chair close to her, and waited to hear detailed the symptoms of some disorder from which he saw in perspective a long train of profits and advantages.", "그럼!\" 이렇게 말하며 보브 소여 씨는 노부인을 의자에 앉히고 문을 닫은 다음, 다른 의자를 그녀 가까이 끌어다 놓고, 앞으로 길게 이어질 이득과 혜택의 행렬이 내다보이는 어떤 질환의 증상이 상세히 설명되기를 기다렸다."]], [["The first thing the old lady did, was to shake her head a great many times, and began to cry.", "노부인이 맨 처음 한 일은 고개를 여러 번 가로젓는 것이었고, 그러더니 울기 시작했다."]], [["‘Nervous,’ said Bob Sawyer complacently.", "\"신경성이군요,\" 보브 소여가 흐뭇하게 말했다."], ["‘Camphor-julep and water three times a day, and composing draught at night.’", "\"장뇌 물약과 물을 하루 세 번, 그리고 밤에는 진정제를 드시지요.\""]], [["‘I don’t know how to begin, Mr.", "\"어떻게 말을 꺼내야 할지 모르겠네요, 소여 씨,\""], ["Sawyer,’ said the old lady.", "노부인이 말했다."], ["‘It is so very painful and distressing.’", "\"너무나 괴롭고 가슴 아픈 일이라서요.\""]], [["‘You need not begin, ma’am,’ rejoined Mr.", "\"말씀을 꺼내실 필요 없습니다, 사모님,\""], ["Bob Sawyer.", "보브 소여 씨가 받았다."], ["‘I can anticipate all you would say.", "\"하실 말씀을 제가 다 짐작할 수 있으니까요."], ["The head is in fault.’", "머리에 탈이 난 거지요.\""]], [["‘I should be very sorry to think it was the heart,’ said the old lady, with a slight groan.", "\"심장이 아니길 정말 바라고 있어요,\" 노부인이 가벼운 신음과 함께 말했다."]], [["‘Not the slightest danger of that, ma’am,’ replied Bob Sawyer.", "\"그럴 위험은 조금도 없습니다, 사모님,\" 보브 소여가 대답했다."], ["‘The stomach is the primary cause.’", "\"위장이 일차적 원인이지요.\""]], [["‘Mr.", "\"소여 씨!\""], ["Sawyer!’ exclaimed the old lady, starting.", "노부인이 흠칫하며 외쳤다."]], [["‘Not the least doubt of it, ma’am,’ rejoined Bob, looking wondrous wise.", "\"조금도 의심할 여지가 없습니다, 사모님,\" 보브가 놀랍도록 현명한 표정을 지으며 받았다."], ["‘Medicine, in time, my dear ma’am, would have prevented it all.’", "\"제때에 약을 드셨더라면, 사모님, 이 모든 걸 막을 수 있었을 텐데요.\""]], [["‘Mr.", "\"소여 씨,\""], ["Sawyer,’ said the old lady, more flurried than before, ‘this conduct is either great impertinence to one in my situation, Sir, or it arises from your not understanding the object of my visit.", "노부인이 아까보다 더 어수선해진 채 말했다. \"이런 태도는 나 같은 처지의 사람에게 큰 무례이거나, 아니면 선생이 내 방문의 목적을 이해하지 못한 데서 비롯된 거예요."], ["If it had been in the power of medicine, or any foresight I could have used, to prevent what has occurred, I should certainly have done so.", "약의 힘으로든, 내가 쓸 수 있었던 어떤 선견지명으로든 벌어진 일을 막을 수 있었다면, 나는 분명히 그렇게 했을 거예요."], ["I had better see my nephew at once,’ said the old lady, twirling her reticule indignantly, and rising as she spoke.", "차라리 곧장 조카를 만나 보는 게 낫겠어요,\" 노부인이 분개하여 손지갑을 빙빙 돌리며 말하더니, 말하면서 일어섰다."]], [["‘Stop a moment, ma’am,’ said Bob Sawyer; ‘I’m afraid I have not understood you.", "\"잠깐만요, 사모님,\" 보브 소여가 말했다."], ["What is the matter, ma’am?’", "\"제가 사모님 말씀을 이해하지 못한 듯합니다. 무슨 일이신가요, 사모님?\""]], [["‘My niece, Mr.", "\"제 조카딸 말이에요, 소여 씨,\""], ["Sawyer,’ said the old lady: ‘your friend’s sister.’", "노부인이 말했다. \"선생 친구의 누이동생요.\""]], [["‘Yes, ma’am,’ said Bob, all impatience; for the old lady, although much agitated, spoke with the most tantalising deliberation, as old ladies often do.", "\"예, 사모님,\" 보브가 잔뜩 안달이 나서 말했다."], ["‘Yes, ma’am.’", "노부인이, 몹시 동요한 와중에도 노부인들이 흔히 그러듯 사람 애간장을 태울 만큼 느릿느릿 말했기 때문이다. \"예, 사모님.\""]], [["‘Left my home, Mr.", "\"사흘 전에, 소여 씨, 제 집을 떠났어요."], ["Sawyer, three days ago, on a pretended visit to my sister, another aunt of hers, who keeps the large boarding-school, just beyond the third mile-stone, where there is a very large laburnum-tree and an oak gate,’ said the old lady, stopping in this place to dry her eyes.", "제 여동생, 그 애한테는 또 다른 이모인데, 세 번째 이정표 바로 너머에서 큰 기숙학교를 하는, 아주 커다란 금사슬나무 한 그루와 떡갈나무 대문이 있는 그곳을 찾아간다는 핑계로 말이지요,\" 노부인이 여기서 말을 멈추고 눈물을 닦았다."]], [["‘Oh, devil take the laburnum-tree, ma’am!’ said Bob, quite forgetting his professional dignity in his anxiety.", "\"오, 금사슬나무 따위 악마한테나 가라죠, 사모님!\" 보브가 안달이 난 나머지 직업적 위엄을 까맣게 잊고 말했다."], ["‘Get on a little faster; put a little more steam on, ma’am, pray.’", "\"좀 빨리 진행하시지요. 증기를 좀 더 올려 주세요, 사모님, 제발.\""]], [["‘This morning,’ said the old lady slowly--‘this morning, she--’", "\"오늘 아침에,\" 노부인이 느릿느릿 말했다. \"오늘 아침에 그 애가—\""]], [["‘She came back, ma’am, I suppose,’ said Bob, with great animation.", "\"돌아왔겠지요, 사모님, 아마?\" 보브가 잔뜩 활기를 띠며 말했다."], ["‘Did she come back?’", "\"돌아왔나요?\""]], [["‘No, she did not; she wrote,’ replied the old lady.", "\"아니요, 안 돌아왔어요. 편지를 보냈답니다,\" 노부인이 대답했다."]], [["‘What did she say?’ inquired Bob eagerly.", "\"뭐라고 썼던가요?\" 보브가 다급하게 물었다."]], [["‘She said, Mr.", "\"이렇게 썼더군요, 소여 씨,\""], ["Sawyer,’ replied the old lady--‘and it is this I want to prepare Benjamin’s mind for, gently and by degrees; she said that she was--I have got the letter in my pocket, Mr.", "노부인이 대답했다. \"—그리고 이것이야말로 제가 벤저민의 마음을 부드럽게, 차근차근 준비시키고 싶은 바인데요."], ["Sawyer, but my glasses are in the carriage, and I should only waste your time if I attempted to point out the passage to you, without them; she said, in short, Mr.", "그 애가 쓰기를 자기가—편지는 주머니에 들어 있어요, 소여 씨, 하지만 안경이 마차에 있어서, 안경 없이 그 대목을 짚어 드리려 한다면 선생 시간만 허비하게 될 거예요."], ["Sawyer, that she was married.’", "한마디로, 소여 씨, 그 애가 결혼했다고 썼답니다.\""]], [["What!’ said, or rather shouted, Mr.", "\"뭐라고요!\""], ["Bob Sawyer.", "보브 소여 씨가 말했다기보다는 고함을 질렀다."]], [["‘Married,’ repeated the old lady.", "\"결혼했다고요,\" 노부인이 되풀이했다."]], [["Mr.", "보브 소여 씨는 더 들으려 멈추지 않고, 진료실에서 바깥 가게로 뛰쳐나가 우렁찬 목소리로 외쳤다."], ["Bob Sawyer stopped to hear no more; but darting from the surgery into the outer shop, cried in a stentorian voice, ‘Ben, my boy, she’s bolted!’", "\"벤, 이 친구야, 그 애가 달아났어!\""]], [["Mr. Ben Allen, who had been slumbering behind the counter, with his head half a foot or so below his knees, no sooner heard this appalling communication, than he made a precipitate rush at Mr.", "머리를 무릎보다 한 반 자쯤 아래로 떨군 채 계산대 뒤에서 졸고 있던 벤 앨런 씨는 이 소름 끼치는 전갈을 듣자마자 마틴 씨에게 곧장 돌진하여, 그 과묵한 종자의 목수건에 손을 비틀어 넣고는, 그 자리에서 그를 목 졸라 버리겠다는 친절한 의향을 표명했다."], ["Martin, and, twisting his hand in the neck-cloth of that taciturn servitor, expressed an obliging intention of choking him where he stood. This intention, with a promptitude often the effect of desperation, he at once commenced carrying into execution, with much vigour and surgical skill.", "이 의향을, 흔히 절망에서 비롯되는 그 신속함으로 그는 곧장, 대단한 기운과 외과적 솜씨로 실행에 옮기기 시작했다."]], [["Mr. Martin, who was a man of few words and possessed but little power of eloquence or persuasion, submitted to this operation with a very calm and agreeable expression of countenance, for some seconds; finding, however, that it threatened speedily to lead to a result which would place it beyond his power to claim any wages, board or otherwise, in all time to come, he muttered an inarticulate remonstrance and felled Mr.", "말수가 적고 웅변이나 설득의 힘이라고는 거의 없는 사람이었던 마틴 씨는, 몇 초 동안은 아주 침착하고 흡족한 표정으로 이 수술을 받아들였다."], ["Benjamin Allen to the ground. As that gentleman had his hands entangled in his cravat, he had no alternative but to follow him to the floor.", "그러나 그것이 머지않아 자신이 앞으로 영영 어떤 품삯이나, 식비든 무엇이든 청구할 수 없게 만들 결과로 이어질 조짐이 보이자, 그는 알아들을 수 없는 항의를 중얼거리며 벤저민 앨런 씨를 바닥에 메다꽂았다."], ["There they both lay struggling, when the shop door opened, and the party was increased by the arrival of two most unexpected visitors, to wit, Mr.", "그 신사는 손이 그의 목도리에 엉켜 있었으므로 그를 따라 바닥에 함께 쓰러지는 수밖에 없었다."], ["Pickwick and Mr. Samuel Weller.", "둘이 거기 누워 뒤엉켜 다투고 있는데 가게 문이 열리더니, 더없이 뜻밖의 방문객 둘, 곧 픽윅 씨와 새뮤얼 웰러 씨의 도착으로 그 무리가 불어났다."]], [["The impression at once produced on Mr. Weller’s mind by what he saw, was, that Mr.", "자기 눈으로 본 광경이 웰러 씨의 마음에 곧바로 안긴 인상은, 마틴 씨가 옛 노케모프의 소여 진료소에 고용되어 독한 약을 먹거나, 발작을 일으켜 실험 대상이 되거나, 어떤 새 해독제의 효능을 시험하려고 이따금 독을 삼키거나, 의학이라는 위대한 학문을 증진하고 그곳 두 젊은 시술자의 가슴에 타오르는 열렬한 탐구 정신을 만족시키기 위해 이런저런 일을 하는 처지라는 것이었다."], ["Martin was hired by the establishment of Sawyer, late Nockemorf, to take strong medicine, or to go into fits and be experimentalised upon, or to swallow poison now and then with the view of testing the efficacy of some new antidotes, or to do something or other to promote the great science of medicine, and gratify the ardent spirit of inquiry burning in the bosoms of its two young professors. So, without presuming to interfere, Sam stood perfectly still, and looked on, as if he were mightily interested in the result of the then pending experiment.", "그래서 샘은 감히 끼어들 생각도 없이, 마치 지금 진행 중인 실험의 결과에 몹시 관심이 있다는 듯 완벽하게 가만히 서서 지켜보고만 있었다."], ["Not so, Mr. Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨는 그렇지 않았다."], ["He at once threw himself on the astonished combatants, with his accustomed energy, and loudly called upon the bystanders to interpose.", "그는 곧장, 평소의 기운으로 어리둥절해하는 두 격투가에게 몸을 던지며, 구경꾼들을 향해 큰 소리로 끼어들어 말려 달라고 외쳤다."]], [["This roused Mr. Bob Sawyer, who had been hitherto quite paralysed by the frenzy of his companion.", "이것이 그때까지 동료의 광기에 완전히 마비되어 있던 보브 소여 씨를 깨어나게 했다."], ["With that gentleman’s assistance, Mr. Pickwick raised Ben Allen to his feet.", "그 신사의 도움으로 픽윅 씨는 벤 앨런을 일으켜 세웠다."], ["Mr. Martin finding himself alone on the floor, got up, and looked about him.", "마틴 씨는 바닥에 혼자 남은 것을 알고는 일어나 주위를 둘러보았다."]], [["‘Mr. Allen,’ said Mr.", "\"앨런 씨,\""], ["Pickwick, ‘what is the matter, Sir?’", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"무슨 일이오, 선생?\""]], [["‘Never mind, Sir!’ replied Mr.", "\"신경 쓰지 마시오, 선생!\""], ["Allen, with haughty defiance.", "앨런 씨가 오만하게 대들 듯 대답했다."]], [["‘What is it?’ inquired Mr.", "\"무슨 일이오?\""], ["Pickwick, looking at Bob Sawyer.", "픽윅 씨가 보브 소여를 바라보며 물었다."], ["‘Is he unwell?’", "\"이분이 편찮으신가?\""]], [["Before Bob could reply, Mr. Ben Allen seized Mr.", "보브가 대답하기도 전에 벤 앨런 씨가 픽윅 씨의 손을 붙잡고 슬픔에 찬 어조로 중얼거렸다."], ["Pickwick by the hand, and murmured, in sorrowful accents, ‘My sister, my dear Sir; my sister.’", "\"제 누이동생입니다, 선생님. 제 누이동생요.\""]], [["‘Oh, is that all!’ said Mr.", "\"오, 그게 다요!\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick. ‘We shall easily arrange that matter, I hope.", "\"그 문제라면 쉽게 정리할 수 있으리라 보오."], ["Your sister is safe and well, and I am here, my dear Sir, to--’", "누이동생분은 무사하고 잘 있소. 그래서 내가 여기 온 것이오, 선생, 바로—\""]], [["‘Sorry to do anythin’ as may cause an interruption to such wery pleasant proceedin’s, as the king said wen he dissolved the parliament,’ interposed Mr.", "\"임금이 의회를 해산할 때 한 말마따나, 그렇게 아주 기분 좋은 진행을 방해하게 돼서 죄송합니다만,\""], ["Weller, who had been peeping through the glass door; ‘but there’s another experiment here, sir.", "유리문 너머로 들여다보고 있던 웰러 씨가 끼어들었다. \"여기 또 다른 실험이 있습니다요, 나리."], ["Here’s a wenerable old lady a-- lyin’ on the carpet waitin’ for dissection, or galwinism, or some other rewivin’ and scientific inwention.’", "여기 점잖은 노부인 한 분이 양탄자 위에 누워서 해부나, 전기 자극이나, 그 밖에 무슨 사람 되살리는 과학적 발명을 기다리고 계신뎁쇼.\""]], [["‘I forgot,’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"아차, 잊었군,\""], ["Ben Allen.", "벤 앨런 씨가 외쳤다."], ["‘It is my aunt.’", "\"제 이모님입니다.\""]], [["‘Dear me!’ said Mr.", "\"저런!\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick. ‘Poor lady!", "\"가엾은 부인!"], ["Gently Sam, gently.’", "살살, 샘, 살살.\""]], [["‘Strange sitivation for one o’ the family,’ observed Sam Weller, hoisting the aunt into a chair.", "\"집안 한 분이 처하기엔 묘한 처지군입쇼,\" 샘 웰러가 이모를 의자에 들어 올리며 말했다."], ["‘Now depitty sawbones, bring out the wollatilly!’", "\"자, 부원장 돌팔이 선생님, 휘발성 약 좀 가져와 보슈!\""]], [["The latter observation was addressed to the boy in gray, who, having handed over the fly to the care of the street-keeper, had come back to see what all the noise was about. Between the boy in gray, and Mr.", "뒤의 말은 회색 옷의 소년을 두고 한 것이었는데, 그 소년은 마차를 거리 관리인에게 맡겨 두고 무슨 소란인가 보려고 돌아온 참이었다."], ["Bob Sawyer, and Mr. Benjamin Allen (who having frightened his aunt into a fainting fit, was affectionately solicitous for her recovery) the old lady was at length restored to consciousness; then Mr.", "회색 옷의 소년과 보브 소여 씨, 그리고 (이모를 놀라게 해 기절시켜 놓고는 그 회복을 애정 어린 마음으로 안달하던) 벤저민 앨런 씨, 이들 사이에서 노부인은 마침내 의식을 되찾았다."], ["Ben Allen, turning with a puzzled countenance to Mr. Pickwick, asked him what he was about to say, when he had been so alarmingly interrupted.", "그러자 벤 앨런 씨가 어리둥절한 얼굴로 픽윅 씨를 돌아보며, 그렇게 놀랍게 말이 끊겼을 때 무슨 말을 하려던 참이었는지 물었다."]], [["‘We are all friends here, I presume?’ said Mr.", "\"여기 있는 사람들은 다 한편이라고 봐도 되겠지요?\""], ["Pickwick, clearing his voice, and looking towards the man of few words with the surly countenance, who drove the fly with the chubby horse.", "픽윅 씨가 목청을 가다듬고, 통통한 말이 끄는 마차를 몬, 무뚝뚝한 얼굴의 말수 적은 사내 쪽을 바라보며 말했다."]], [["This reminded Mr. Bob Sawyer that the boy in gray was looking on, with eyes wide open, and greedy ears.", "이 말에 보브 소여 씨는 회색 옷의 소년이 눈을 휘둥그레 뜨고 귀를 탐욕스럽게 세운 채 구경하고 있다는 것을 떠올렸다."], ["The incipient chemist having been lifted up by his coat collar, and dropped outside the door, Bob Sawyer assured Mr. Pickwick that he might speak without reserve.", "그 풋내기 약제사가 외투 깃이 잡혀 들려서 문밖으로 내던져진 뒤에야, 보브 소여는 픽윅 씨에게 거리낌 없이 말씀하셔도 된다고 일러 주었다."]], [["‘Your sister, my dear Sir,’ said Mr.", "\"누이동생분은, 선생,\" 픽윅 씨가 벤저민 앨런을 향해 돌아서며 말했다."], ["Pickwick, turning to Benjamin Allen, ‘is in London; well and happy.’", "\"런던에 있소. 잘 있고 행복하오.\""]], [["‘Her happiness is no object to me, sir,’ said Benjamin Allen, with a flourish of the hand.", "\"그 애의 행복 따위 제게는 아무 상관 없습니다, 선생님,\" 벤저민 앨런이 손을 휘저으며 말했다."]], [["‘Her husband is an object to me, Sir,’ said Bob Sawyer.", "\"그 애 남편은 나한테 상관이 있소, 선생,\" 보브 소여가 말했다."], ["‘He shall be an object to me, sir, at twelve paces, and a pretty object I’ll make of him, sir--a mean-spirited scoundrel!’ This, as it stood, was a very pretty denunciation, and magnanimous withal; but Mr.", "\"그놈은 나한테 상관있는 표적이 될 거요, 선생, 열두 보 거리에서 말이오. 내가 그놈을 아주 볼만한 표적으로 만들어 주지요, 선생—그 비열한 악당 놈을!\""], ["Bob Sawyer rather weakened its effect, by winding up with some general observations concerning the punching of heads and knocking out of eyes, which were commonplace by comparison.", "이 정도라면 그 자체로 아주 그럴듯한 규탄이었고 더구나 통이 컸지만, 보브 소여 씨는 머리통을 후려치고 눈깔을 뽑아 버리겠다는, 그에 비하면 진부하기 짝이 없는 일반적 소견으로 끝맺어 그 효과를 다소 약화시켰다."]], [["‘Stay, sir,’ said Mr.", "\"가만, 선생,\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick; ‘before you apply those epithets to the gentleman in question, consider, dispassionately, the extent of his fault, and above all remember that he is a friend of mine.’", "\"문제의 그 신사에게 그런 별칭을 갖다 붙이기 전에, 그의 잘못의 정도를 냉정하게 헤아려 보시오. 그리고 무엇보다도 그가 내 친구라는 점을 기억하시오.\""]], [["‘What!’ said Mr.", "\"뭐라고요!\""], ["Bob Sawyer.", "보브 소여 씨가 말했다."], ["‘His name!’ cried Ben Allen.", "\"그 이름!\" 벤 앨런이 외쳤다."], ["‘His name!’", "\"그자의 이름을!\""]], [["‘Mr. Nathaniel Winkle,’ said Mr.", "\"너새니얼 윈클 씨요,\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["Mr. Benjamin Allen deliberately crushed his spectacles beneath the heel of his boot, and having picked up the pieces, and put them into three separate pockets, folded his arms, bit his lips, and looked in a threatening manner at the bland features of Mr. Pickwick.", "벤저민 앨런 씨는 신중하게 자기 안경을 장화 뒤축으로 짓밟아 부순 다음, 그 조각을 주워 세 군데 주머니에 나누어 넣고는, 팔짱을 끼고 입술을 깨물며 픽윅 씨의 온화한 이목구비를 위협적인 태도로 노려보았다."]], [["‘Then it’s you, is it, Sir, who have encouraged and brought about this match?’ inquired Mr.", "\"그렇다면 이 혼사를 부추기고 성사시킨 게 바로 당신이군요, 선생, 그렇소?\""], ["Benjamin Allen at length.", "벤저민 앨런 씨가 마침내 물었다."]], [["‘And it’s this gentleman’s servant, I suppose,’ interrupted the old lady, ‘who has been skulking about my house, and endeavouring to entrap my servants to conspire against their mistress.--Martin!’", "\"그리고 제 집 주변을 어슬렁거리며 제 하인들을 꾀어 안주인을 거역하는 음모를 꾸미게 하려던 게 이 신사분의 하인이로군요, 아마.—마틴!\" 노부인이 끼어들었다."]], [["‘Well?’ said the surly man, coming forward.", "\"예?\" 무뚝뚝한 남자가 앞으로 나서며 말했다."]], [["‘Is that the young man you saw in the lane, whom you told me about, this morning?’", "\"오늘 아침에 자네가 골목길에서 봤다고 내게 말한 그 젊은이가 바로 저자인가?\""]], [["Mr. Martin, who, as it has already appeared, was a man of few words, looked at Sam Weller, nodded his head, and growled forth, ‘That’s the man.’ Mr.", "이미 드러났듯 말수가 적은 사람이었던 마틴 씨는 샘 웰러를 보고 고개를 끄덕이며 으르렁대듯 내뱉었다."], ["Weller, who was never proud, gave a smile of friendly recognition as his eyes encountered those of the surly groom, and admitted in courteous terms, that he had ‘knowed him afore.’", "\"저 사람 맞습니다.\" 도무지 잘난 척이라곤 모르던 웰러 씨는 무뚝뚝한 마부와 눈이 마주치자 다정한 알은체의 미소를 짓고는, 그를 \"전에 알던 사이\"라고 정중한 말로 인정했다."]], [["‘And this is the faithful creature,’ exclaimed Mr. Ben Allen, ‘whom I had nearly suffocated!--Mr.", "\"그러니까 이 사람이 바로, 제가 하마터면 질식시킬 뻔한 그 충직한 사람이로군요!\""], ["Pickwick, how dare you allow your fellow to be employed in the abduction of my sister?", "벤 앨런 씨가 외쳤다. \"픽윅 씨, 어찌 감히 댁의 하인을 내 누이동생을 유괴하는 데 쓰도록 내버려 두셨소?"], ["I demand that you explain this matter, sir.’", "이 일을 해명하실 것을 요구합니다, 선생.\""]], [["‘Explain it, sir!’ cried Bob Sawyer fiercely.", "\"해명하시오, 선생!\" 보브 소여가 사납게 외쳤다."]], [["‘It’s a conspiracy,’ said Ben Allen.", "\"음모야,\" 벤 앨런이 말했다."]], [["‘A regular plant,’ added Mr.", "\"제대로 짠 함정이지,\""], ["Bob Sawyer.", "보브 소여 씨가 덧붙였다."]], [["‘A disgraceful imposition,’ observed the old lady.", "\"수치스러운 사기예요,\" 노부인이 한마디 했다."]], [["‘Nothing but a do,’ remarked Martin.", "\"순전히 속임수일 뿐이오,\" 마틴이 말했다."]], [["‘Pray hear me,’ urged Mr.", "\"부디 내 말을 들어 보시오,\""], ["Pickwick, as Mr.", "픽윅 씨가 다그쳤다."], ["Ben Allen fell into a chair that patients were bled in, and gave way to his pocket-handkerchief.", "그동안 벤 앨런 씨는 환자들의 피를 뽑던 의자에 털썩 주저앉아 손수건에 얼굴을 묻고 흐느꼈다. \"나는 이 일에서 어떤 도움도 준 적이 없소, 다만 내가 막을 수 없었던 그 두 젊은이의 면담 한 차례에 함께 있었던 것뿐이오."], ["‘I have rendered no assistance in this matter, beyond being present at one interview between the young people which I could not prevent, and from which I conceived my presence would remove any slight colouring of impropriety that it might otherwise have had; this is the whole share I have had in the transaction, and I had no suspicion that an immediate marriage was even contemplated.", "그리고 내가 자리에 있으면 그 면담에 자칫 깃들 수 있는 부적절함의 미묘한 기색을 없앨 수 있으리라 여겼던 것이오. 이것이 이 일에서 내가 맡은 몫의 전부이며, 나는 당장의 결혼이 계획되고 있으리라고는 짐작조차 못 했소."], ["Though, mind,’ added Mr.", "다만 말이오,\""], ["Pickwick, hastily checking himself--‘mind, I do not say I should have prevented it, if I had known that it was intended.’", "픽윅 씨가 황급히 자제하며 덧붙였다. \"다만, 그것이 계획돼 있는 줄 알았다 한들 내가 그것을 막았으리라고는 말하지 않겠소.\""]], [["‘You hear that, all of you; you hear that?’ said Mr.", "\"다들 들었지요, 여러분, 다들 들으셨소?\""], ["Benjamin Allen.", "벤저민 앨런 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘I hope they do,’ mildly observed Mr.", "\"부디 들었길 바라오,\" 픽윅 씨가 주위를 둘러보며 온화하게 말했다."], ["Pickwick, looking round, ‘and,’ added that gentleman, his colour mounting as he spoke, ‘I hope they hear this, Sir, also.", "\"그리고,\" 그 신사가 말하면서 얼굴빛이 점점 달아오르며 덧붙였다."], ["That from what has been stated to me, sir, I assert that you were by no means justified in attempting to force your sister’s inclinations as you did, and that you should rather have endeavoured by your kindness and forbearance to have supplied the place of other nearer relations whom she had never known, from a child.", "\"이것도 부디 들으시길 바라오, 선생. 내게 진술된 바로 미루어, 나는 단언하건대 당신이 그렇게 누이동생의 마음을 강제하려 든 것은 결코 정당하지 못했으며, 오히려 당신은 친절과 인내로써, 그 애가 어릴 때부터 한 번도 알지 못했던 더 가까운 다른 친척들의 자리를 대신해 주려고 애썼어야 마땅했소."], ["As regards my young friend, I must beg to add, that in every point of worldly advantage he is, at least, on an equal footing with yourself, if not on a much better one, and that unless I hear this question discussed with becoming temper and moderation, I decline hearing any more said upon the subject.’", "내 젊은 친구로 말하자면, 한마디 덧붙이지 않을 수 없는데, 세속적 이점이라는 모든 점에서 그는 적어도 당신과 대등하며, 훨씬 더 나은 처지에 있다고 해도 좋소. 그러니 이 문제를 합당한 절제와 온건함으로 논하는 것을 듣지 못한다면, 나는 이 주제에 관해 더 이상 듣기를 거절하겠소.\""]], [["‘I wish to make a wery few remarks in addition to wot has been put for’ard by the honourable gen’l’m’n as has jist give over,’ said Mr.", "\"방금 말씀을 마치신 명예로운 신사분께서 내놓으신 바에 더해 저도 몇 마디 아주 약간만 보태고 싶은뎁쇼,\" 웰러 씨가 앞으로 나서며 말했다."], ["Weller, stepping forth, ‘wich is this here: a indiwidual in company has called me a feller.’", "\"다름이 아니라 이겁니다요. 이 자리에 계신 어느 분이 절 보고 '놈'이라고 하셨거든요.\""]], [["‘That has nothing whatever to do with the matter, Sam,’ interposed Mr.", "\"그건 이 문제와 아무 상관도 없는 일이다, 샘,\" 픽윅 씨가 끼어들었다."], ["Pickwick. ‘Pray hold your tongue.’", "\"제발 입 좀 다물어라.\""]], [["‘I ain’t a-goin’ to say nothin’ on that ‘ere pint, sir,’ replied Sam, ‘but merely this here.", "\"그 점에 대해선 아무 말도 안 하렵니다요, 나리,\" 샘이 대답했다."], ["P’raps that gen’l’m’n may think as there wos a priory ‘tachment; but there worn’t nothin’ o’ the sort, for the young lady said in the wery beginnin’ o’ the keepin’ company, that she couldn’t abide him.", "\"그저 이것만 말씀드리지요. 어쩌면 저 신사분께선 무슨 옛정이 있었던 게 아닌가 생각하실지 모릅니다만, 그런 건 전혀 없었습니다요."], ["Nobody’s cut him out, and it ‘ud ha’ been jist the wery same for him if the young lady had never seen Mr.", "왜냐면 그 아가씨가 사귀기 시작한 바로 그 처음부터 그분을 도무지 못 견디겠다고 하셨거든요. 아무도 그분을 밀어낸 게 아니고, 설사 그 아가씨가 윈클 씨를 한 번도 못 봤더라도 그분한텐 사정이 똑같았을 겁니다요."], ["Vinkle. That’s what I wished to say, sir, and I hope I’ve now made that ‘ere gen’l’m’n’s mind easy.", "그게 제가 말씀드리고 싶었던 거고요, 나리, 이제 저 신사분 마음이 편해졌기를 바랍니다요.\""]], [["A short pause followed these consolatory remarks of Mr. Weller. Then Mr. Ben Allen rising from his chair, protested that he would never see Arabella’s face again; while Mr. Bob Sawyer, despite Sam’s flattering assurance, vowed dreadful vengeance on the happy bridegroom.", "웰러 씨의 이 위로하는 말 뒤에 잠깐 침묵이 흘렀다. 이윽고 벤 앨런 씨가 의자에서 일어서며 다시는 어래벨러의 얼굴을 보지 않겠다고 단언했고, 한편 보브 소여 씨는 샘의 듣기 좋은 장담에도 아랑곳없이 그 행복한 신랑에게 무시무시한 복수를 하겠다고 맹세했다."]], [["But, just when matters were at their height, and threatening to remain so, Mr.", "그러나 사태가 절정에 이르러 그대로 머물 조짐을 보이던 바로 그때, 픽윅 씨는 노부인이라는 강력한 조력자를 얻었다."], ["Pickwick found a powerful assistant in the old lady, who, evidently much struck by the mode in which he had advocated her niece’s cause, ventured to approach Mr. Benjamin Allen with a few comforting reflections, of which the chief were, that after all, perhaps, it was well it was no worse; the least said the soonest mended, and upon her word she did not know that it was so very bad after all; what was over couldn’t be begun, and what couldn’t be cured must be endured; with various other assurances of the like novel and strengthening description.", "노부인은 그가 자기 조카딸의 처지를 옹호한 방식에 분명 크게 감동한 듯, 위로가 될 만한 몇 마디 소회를 들고 벤저민 앨런 씨에게 다가가 보았는데, 그 요지인즉, 어쨌거나 그래도 이만하기 다행인지 모른다, 말을 적게 할수록 빨리 아물 것이다, 정말이지 따지고 보면 그렇게 나쁜 일도 아닌 것 같다, 지나간 일은 다시 시작할 수 없고 고칠 수 없는 일은 견뎌야 하는 법이다, 라는 것이었고, 그 밖에도 그처럼 참신하고 기운을 북돋우는 갖가지 장담을 곁들였다."], ["To all of these, Mr. Benjamin Allen replied that he meant no disrespect to his aunt, or anybody there, but if it were all the same to them, and they would allow him to have his own way, he would rather have the pleasure of hating his sister till death, and after it.", "이 모든 말에 벤저민 앨런 씨는, 이모님이나 여기 계신 누구에게도 무례를 끼칠 뜻은 없으나, 다들 상관없다면, 그리고 자기 뜻대로 하도록 허락해 준다면, 자기는 차라리 누이동생을 죽을 때까지, 아니 죽은 뒤에도 미워하는 즐거움을 누리고 싶다고 대답했다."]], [["At length, when this determination had been announced half a hundred times, the old lady suddenly bridling up and looking very majestic, wished to know what she had done that no respect was to be paid to her years or station, and that she should be obliged to beg and pray, in that way, of her own nephew, whom she remembered about five-and-twenty years before he was born, and whom she had known, personally, when he hadn’t a tooth in his head; to say nothing of her presence on the first occasion of his having his hair cut, and assistance at numerous other times and ceremonies during his babyhood, of sufficient importance to found a claim upon his affection, obedience, and sympathies, for ever.", "마침내 이 결심을 쉰 번쯤 되풀이해 선언하고 났을 때, 노부인이 별안간 발끈하며 자못 위엄 있는 모습으로, 도대체 내가 무슨 짓을 했길래 내 나이나 지위에 아무 존경도 표하지 않는 거냐, 그래서 내가 이런 식으로 내 친조카에게 빌고 사정해야 하는 거냐, 나는 이 애가 태어나기 한 스물다섯 해 전부터 기억하고, 입에 이 하나 안 났을 때부터 직접 알고 지냈으며, 이 애가 처음 머리를 깎던 자리에도 있어 주었고, 갓난쟁이 시절 그 밖에 수많은 때와 의식에서 거들어 주어 그 애정과 순종과 동정을 영원토록 요구할 만한 권리를 충분히 세워 둔 사람인데, 라고 알고 싶어 했다."]], [["While the good lady was bestowing this objurgation on Mr. Ben Allen, Bob Sawyer and Mr. Pickwick had retired in close conversation to the inner room, where Mr. Sawyer was observed to apply himself several times to the mouth of a black bottle, under the influence of which, his features gradually assumed a cheerful and even jovial expression. And at last he emerged from the room, bottle in hand, and, remarking that he was very sorry to say he had been making a fool of himself, begged to propose the health and happiness of Mr. and Mrs. Winkle, whose felicity, so far from envying, he would be the first to congratulate them upon. Hearing this, Mr. Ben Allen suddenly arose from his chair, and, seizing the black bottle, drank the toast so heartily, that, the liquor being strong, he became nearly as black in the face as the bottle. Finally, the black bottle went round till it was empty, and there was so much shaking of hands and interchanging of compliments, that even the metal-visaged Mr. Martin condescended to smile.", "그 선량한 부인이 벤 앨런 씨에게 이런 질책을 퍼붓고 있는 동안, 보브 소여와 픽윅 씨는 은밀한 대화를 나누며 안쪽 방으로 물러나 있었는데, 거기서 소여 씨는 검은 병 주둥이에 여러 차례 입을 갖다 대는 것이 목격되었고, 그 영향으로 그의 표정은 차츰 명랑하다 못해 흥겨운 빛마저 띠게 되었다. 그러고는 마침내 병을 손에 든 채 방에서 나와, 자기가 바보짓을 했다고 말하게 되어 몹시 유감이라면서, 윈클 부부의 건강과 행복을 위해 건배할 것을 청했다. 그들의 행복을 시기하기는커녕 자기야말로 맨 먼저 그들을 축하하겠노라는 것이었다. 이 말을 듣자 벤 앨런 씨가 별안간 의자에서 일어나 검은 병을 움켜쥐고 어찌나 진심으로 건배를 들이켰던지, 술이 독한 탓에 그의 얼굴은 거의 병만큼이나 시커메지고 말았다. 마침내 검은 병은 텅 빌 때까지 돌고 돌았고, 어찌나 악수를 나누고 인사치레를 주고받았던지, 쇠붙이 같은 낯짝의 마틴 씨조차 황송하게도 미소를 지을 정도였다."]], [["‘And now,’ said Bob Sawyer, rubbing his hands, ‘we’ll have a jolly night.’", "\"자 이제,\" 보브 소여가 두 손을 비비며 말했다. \"유쾌한 밤을 보냅시다.\""]], [["‘I am sorry,’ said Mr.", "\"미안하지만,\""], ["Pickwick, ‘that I must return to my inn.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"나는 여관으로 돌아가야 하오."], ["I have not been accustomed to fatigue lately, and my journey has tired me exceedingly.’", "요즘 피곤한 일에 익숙지 않은 데다, 이번 여정이 나를 몹시 지치게 했소.\""]], [["‘You’ll take some tea, Mr.", "\"차라도 좀 드시고 가시지요, 픽윅 씨?\""], ["Pickwick?’ said the old lady, with irresistible sweetness.", "노부인이 거부할 수 없는 다정함으로 말했다."]], [["‘Thank you, I would rather not,’ replied that gentleman.", "\"고맙소만, 안 드는 게 낫겠소,\" 그 신사가 대답했다."], ["The truth is, that the old lady’s evidently increasing admiration was Mr. Pickwick’s principal inducement for going away.", "사실인즉, 노부인의 흠모가 눈에 띄게 짙어 가는 것이야말로 픽윅 씨가 자리를 뜨려는 주된 이유였다."], ["He thought of Mrs. Bardell; and every glance of the old lady’s eyes threw him into a cold perspiration.", "그는 바델 부인을 떠올렸고, 노부인의 눈길이 한 번 던져질 때마다 식은땀이 흘렀다."]], [["As Mr. Pickwick could by no means be prevailed upon to stay, it was arranged at once, on his own proposition, that Mr.", "픽윅 씨를 도무지 머물게 할 수 없었으므로, 그 자신의 제안에 따라 곧장 정해진 바, 벤저민 앨런 씨가 윈클 노인을 향한 픽윅 씨의 여정에 동행하기로 하고, 마차는 이튿날 아침 아홉 시에 문 앞에 대기로 했다."], ["Benjamin Allen should accompany him on his journey to the elder Mr. Winkle’s, and that the coach should be at the door, at nine o’clock next morning.", "그러고 나서 그는 작별을 고하고, 새뮤얼 웰러를 거느린 채 부시 여관으로 향했다."], ["He then took his leave, and, followed by Samuel Weller, repaired to the Bush. It is worthy of remark, that Mr.", "한 가지 적어 둘 만한 것은, 마틴 씨가 헤어지면서 샘과 악수할 때 그의 얼굴이 끔찍하게 일그러지더니, 동시에 미소와 욕설을 한꺼번에 터뜨렸다는 점이다."], ["Martin’s face was horribly convulsed as he shook hands with Sam at parting, and that he gave vent to a smile and an oath simultaneously; from which tokens it has been inferred by those who were best acquainted with that gentleman’s peculiarities, that he expressed himself much pleased with Mr. Weller’s society, and requested the honour of his further acquaintance.", "이러한 징표로 미루어, 그 신사의 별난 기질을 가장 잘 아는 이들은 그가 웰러 씨와 어울린 것이 무척 흡족했으며 앞으로도 더 사귀는 영광을 누리고 싶다는 뜻을 표한 것이라고 추측했다."]], [["‘Shall I order a private room, Sir?’ inquired Sam, when they reached the Bush.", "\"독방을 잡을까요, 나리?\" 부시 여관에 이르자 샘이 물었다."]], [["‘Why, no, Sam,’ replied Mr.", "\"아니다, 샘,\""], ["Pickwick; ‘as I dined in the coffee-room, and shall go to bed soon, it is hardly worth while.", "픽윅 씨가 대답했다. \"식사는 커피실에서 했고 곧 잠자리에 들 테니, 굳이 그럴 것까지 없겠다."], ["See who there is in the travellers’ room, Sam.’", "여행자실에 누가 있는지 보고 오너라, 샘.\""]], [["Mr. Weller departed on his errand, and presently returned to say that there was only a gentleman with one eye; and that he and the landlord were drinking a bowl of bishop together.", "웰러 씨가 그 심부름을 하러 갔다가 곧 돌아와, 외눈박이 신사 한 분뿐인데 그와 여관 주인이 함께 비숍을 한 사발 마시고 있다고 전했다."]], [["‘I will join them,’ said Mr.", "\"내가 합석하지,\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘He’s a queer customer, the vun-eyed vun, sir,’ observed Mr.", "\"별난 손님입니다요, 그 외눈박이 양반은요, 나리,\""], ["Weller, as he led the way.", "웰러 씨가 앞장서며 말했다."], ["‘He’s a-gammonin’ that ‘ere landlord, he is, sir, till he don’t rightly know wether he’s a-standing on the soles of his boots or the crown of his hat.’", "\"여관 주인을 어찌나 구워삶고 있는지요, 나리, 주인 양반이 지금 자기가 장화 밑창을 딛고 서 있는지 모자 꼭대기를 딛고 서 있는지도 제대로 모를 지경입니다요.\""]], [["The individual to whom this observation referred, was sitting at the upper end of the room when Mr.", "이 말이 가리킨 사람은 픽윅 씨가 들어섰을 때 방 위쪽 끝에 앉아, 커다란 네덜란드식 파이프를 피우면서 여관 주인의 둥근 얼굴에 외눈을 골똘히 박고 있었다."], ["Pickwick entered, and was smoking a large Dutch pipe, with his eye intently fixed on the round face of the landlord; a jolly-looking old personage, to whom he had recently been relating some tale of wonder, as was testified by sundry disjointed exclamations of, ‘Well, I wouldn’t have believed it!", "명랑해 보이는 노인인 주인에게 그가 방금 어떤 기이한 이야기를 들려준 참이라는 것은, \"글쎄, 도무지 못 믿겠는걸! 내 평생 들어 본 중에 가장 이상한 이야기야!"], ["The strangest thing I ever heard!", "그게 가능하리라곤 상상도 못 했네!\""], ["Couldn’t have supposed it possible!’ and other expressions of astonishment which burst spontaneously from his lips, as he returned the fixed gaze of the one-eyed man.", "따위의 여러 토막 난 감탄사와, 외눈박이 사내의 응시에 마주하며 그의 입에서 절로 터져 나온 그 밖의 갖가지 놀라움의 표현으로 입증되고 있었다."]], [["‘Servant, sir,’ said the one-eyed man to Mr.", "\"안녕하시오, 선생,\" 외눈박이 사내가 픽윅 씨에게 말했다."], ["Pickwick. ‘Fine night, sir.’", "\"좋은 밤이올시다, 선생.\""]], [["‘Very much so indeed,’ replied Mr.", "\"정말이지 그렇구려,\""], ["Pickwick, as the waiter placed a small decanter of brandy, and some hot water before him.", "픽윅 씨가 대답하는 동안 종업원이 그의 앞에 작은 브랜디 병과 뜨거운 물을 좀 놓아 주었다."]], [["While Mr. Pickwick was mixing his brandy-and-water, the one-eyed man looked round at him earnestly, from time to time, and at length said--", "픽윅 씨가 브랜디에 물을 타는 동안, 외눈박이 사내가 이따금 그를 진지하게 둘러보더니 마침내 입을 열었다—"]], [["‘I think I’ve seen you before.’", "\"전에 당신을 본 적이 있는 것 같소만.\""]], [["‘I don’t recollect you,’ rejoined Mr.", "\"나는 당신이 기억나지 않는구려,\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 받았다."]], [["‘I dare say not,’ said the one-eyed man.", "\"그렇겠지요,\" 외눈박이 사내가 말했다."], ["‘You didn’t know me, but I knew two friends of yours that were stopping at the Peacock at Eatanswill, at the time of the election.’", "\"당신은 나를 몰랐지만, 나는 선거 때 이턴스윌의 피콕 여관에 묵고 있던 당신 친구 두 분을 알았소.\""]], [["‘Oh, indeed!’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"오, 그래요!\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 외쳤다."]], [["‘Yes,’ rejoined the one-eyed man.", "\"그렇소,\" 외눈박이 사내가 받았다."], ["‘I mentioned a little circumstance to them about a friend of mine of the name of Tom Smart.", "\"내가 그들에게 톰 스마트라는 이름의 내 친구에 관한 사소한 이야기 하나를 들려주었지요."], ["Perhaps you’ve heard them speak of it.’", "아마 그들이 그 이야기를 하는 걸 들어 보셨을 거요.\""]], [["‘Often,’ rejoined Mr.", "\"자주 들었소,\""], ["Pickwick, smiling.", "픽윅 씨가 미소 지으며 받았다."], ["‘He was your uncle, I think?’", "\"그분이 당신 삼촌이었지요, 아마?\""]], [["‘No, no; only a friend of my uncle’s,’ replied the one-eyed man.", "\"아니, 아니오. 그저 제 삼촌의 친구였을 뿐이지요,\" 외눈박이 사내가 대답했다."]], [["‘He was a wonderful man, that uncle of yours, though,’ remarked the landlord shaking his head.", "\"하지만 그 삼촌 되시는 분은 대단한 분이었지요,\" 여관 주인이 고개를 가로저으며 한마디 했다."]], [["‘Well, I think he was; I think I may say he was,’ answered the one-eyed man.", "\"음, 그랬다고 봅니다. 그랬다고 말해도 좋겠지요,\""], ["‘I could tell you a story about that same uncle, gentlemen, that would rather surprise you.’", "외눈박이 사내가 대답했다. \"그 똑같은 삼촌에 관해 한 가지 이야기를 들려드릴 수 있는데, 여러분, 꽤 놀라실 거외다.\""]], [["‘Could you?’ said Mr.", "\"그래요?\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick. ‘Let us hear it, by all means.’", "\"부디 들어 봅시다.\""]], [["The one-eyed bagman ladled out a glass of negus from the bowl, and drank it; smoked a long whiff out of the Dutch pipe; and then, calling to Sam Weller who was lingering near the door, that he needn’t go away unless he wanted to, because the story was no secret, fixed his eye upon the landlord’s, and proceeded, in the words of the next chapter.", "외눈박이 외판원은 사발에서 니거스 한 잔을 국자로 떠서 마시고, 네덜란드식 파이프를 길게 한 모금 빨더니, 문 가까이 서성거리던 샘 웰러에게, 이 이야기는 비밀이 아니니 가고 싶지 않거든 굳이 가지 않아도 된다고 일러 준 다음, 여관 주인의 얼굴에 외눈을 박은 채 다음 장에 적힌 말로 이야기를 시작했다."]], [["# CHAPTER XLIX. CONTAINING THE STORY OF THE BAGMAN’S UNCLE", "# 제17장. 류머티즘의 발작이 어떤 경우에는 창의적 천재성을 촉진하는 작용을 한다는 것을 보여 주는 장"]], [["My uncle, gentlemen,’ said the bagman, ‘was one of the merriest, pleasantest, cleverest fellows, that ever lived.", "신사 여러분, 제 삼촌으로 말씀드리자면,\" 외판원이 말했다, \"이제껏 살았던 사람들 가운데 가장 유쾌하고, 가장 상냥하고, 가장 영리한 분들 중 한 분이었습니다."], ["I wish you had known him, gentlemen.", "여러분이 그분을 아셨더라면 좋았을 텐데요, 신사 여러분."], ["On second thoughts, gentlemen, I don’t wish you had known him, for if you had, you would have been all, by this time, in the ordinary course of nature, if not dead, at all events so near it, as to have taken to stopping at home and giving up company, which would have deprived me of the inestimable pleasure of addressing you at this moment.", "다시 생각해 보니, 신사 여러분, 여러분이 그분을 아셨더라면 하고 바라지 않겠습니다. 만약 아셨더라면 여러분은 지금쯤 자연의 순리에 따라, 죽지는 않았더라도 적어도 그에 가까워져서, 집에만 틀어박혀 사람들과의 교제를 끊으셨을 테고, 그러면 저는 이 순간 여러분께 말씀드리는 값으로 따질 수 없는 기쁨을 빼앗기고 말았을 테니까요."], ["Gentlemen, I wish your fathers and mothers had known my uncle.", "신사 여러분, 차라리 여러분의 아버님과 어머님께서 제 삼촌을 아셨더라면 좋았을 겁니다."], ["They would have been amazingly fond of him, especially your respectable mothers; I know they would.", "그분을 무척이나 좋아하셨을 테지요, 특히 여러분의 점잖으신 어머님들께서요. 틀림없이 그러셨을 겁니다."], ["If any two of his numerous virtues predominated over the many that adorned his character, I should say they were his mixed punch and his after-supper song.", "그분의 인품을 꾸미던 수많은 미덕 가운데서도 어느 둘이 다른 많은 것을 압도했다고 한다면, 저는 그것이 그분이 만든 혼합 펀치와 저녁 식사 후의 노래였다고 말씀드리겠습니다."], ["Excuse my dwelling on these melancholy recollections of departed worth; you won’t see a man like my uncle every day in the week.", "떠나간 분의 미덕에 관한 이런 우울한 회상에 제가 잠겨 있는 것을 용서해 주십시오. 제 삼촌 같은 분은 일주일 내내 매일같이 만날 수 있는 사람이 아니니까요."]], [["‘I have always considered it a great point in my uncle’s character, gentlemen, that he was the intimate friend and companion of Tom Smart, of the great house of Bilson and Slum, Cateaton Street, City.", "\"저는 늘 제 삼촌의 인품에서 큰 자랑거리로 여겨 온 것이 있습니다, 신사 여러분. 그분이 시티의 케이트아턴 거리에 있는 대상회 빌슨 앤드 슬럼의 톰 스마트와 막역한 벗이자 동무였다는 점입니다."], ["My uncle collected for Tiggin and Welps, but for a long time he went pretty near the same journey as Tom; and the very first night they met, my uncle took a fancy for Tom, and Tom took a fancy for my uncle.", "제 삼촌은 티긴 앤드 웰프스를 위해 수금을 다니셨지만, 오랫동안 톰과 거의 같은 노선을 도셨습니다. 두 분이 만난 바로 첫날 밤, 제 삼촌은 톰이 마음에 들었고, 톰은 제 삼촌이 마음에 들었지요."], ["They made a bet of a new hat before they had known each other half an hour, who should brew the best quart of punch and drink it the quickest.", "두 분은 서로 알게 된 지 반 시간도 채 되지 않아 새 모자를 걸고 내기를 했습니다. 누가 더 좋은 펀치 한 쿼트를 만들고, 그것을 누가 더 빨리 마시느냐 하는 것이었지요."], ["My uncle was judged to have won the making, but Tom Smart beat him in the drinking by about half a salt-spoonful.", "만드는 쪽은 제 삼촌이 이긴 것으로 판정이 났지만, 마시는 쪽에서는 톰 스마트가 소금 숟가락 반 술쯤 차이로 그분을 이겼습니다."], ["They took another quart apiece to drink each other’s health in, and were staunch friends ever afterwards.", "두 분은 서로의 건강을 빌며 또 한 쿼트씩을 마셨고, 그 후로는 변함없는 벗이 되었지요."], ["There’s a destiny in these things, gentlemen; we can’t help it.", "이런 일들에는 운명이라는 것이 있는 법입니다, 신사 여러분. 어쩔 수 없는 일이지요."]], [["‘In personal appearance, my uncle was a trifle shorter than the middle size; he was a thought stouter too, than the ordinary run of people, and perhaps his face might be a shade redder.", "\"외양으로 말하자면, 제 삼촌은 중키보다 약간 작은 편이었습니다. 보통 사람들보다 조금 더 통통했고, 어쩌면 얼굴빛도 한층 더 붉었을지 모르겠습니다."], ["He had the jolliest face you ever saw, gentleman: something like Punch, with a handsome nose and chin; his eyes were always twinkling and sparkling with good-humour; and a smile--not one of your unmeaning wooden grins, but a real, merry, hearty, good-tempered smile--was perpetually on his countenance.", "여러분이 이제껏 보신 중에 가장 쾌활한 얼굴을 하고 계셨지요, 신사 여러분. 펀치 인형 비슷한데, 잘생긴 코와 턱이 있었습니다."], ["He was pitched out of his gig once, and knocked, head first, against a milestone.", "두 눈은 늘 호의로 반짝거리며 빛났고, 미소가—저 무의미하고 나무토막 같은 히죽거림이 아니라, 진짜로 즐겁고 진심 어리고 너그러운 미소가—그분 얼굴에 항상 어려 있었지요. 한번은 마차에서 튕겨 나가 머리부터 이정표에 부딪치신 적이 있습니다."], ["There he lay, stunned, and so cut about the face with some gravel which had been heaped up alongside it, that, to use my uncle’s own strong expression, if his mother could have revisited the earth, she wouldn’t have known him.", "그분은 기절한 채 거기 누워 계셨는데, 옆에 쌓여 있던 자갈에 얼굴이 어찌나 긁혔던지, 제 삼촌 자신의 강렬한 표현을 빌리자면, 그분 어머니가 다시 이 세상에 오셨더라도 그분을 알아보지 못하셨을 거랍니다. 사실 이 문제를 곰곰이 따져 보면, 신사 여러분, 저는 어머니가 정말로 못 알아보셨을 거라고 거의 확신합니다."], ["Indeed, when I come to think of the matter, gentlemen, I feel pretty sure she wouldn’t, for she died when my uncle was two years and seven months old, and I think it’s very likely that, even without the gravel, his top-boots would have puzzled the good lady not a little; to say nothing of his jolly red face.", "어머니는 제 삼촌이 두 살 일곱 달 되던 때에 돌아가셨으니까요. 그리고 제 생각엔 자갈이 없었더라도 그분의 통가죽 장화만으로도 그 선량한 부인을 적잖이 어리둥절하게 했을 것 같습니다."], ["However, there he lay, and I have heard my uncle say, many a time, that the man said who picked him up that he was smiling as merrily as if he had tumbled out for a treat, and that after they had bled him, the first faint glimmerings of returning animation, were his jumping up in bed, bursting out into a loud laugh, kissing the young woman who held the basin, and demanding a mutton chop and a pickled walnut.", "그분의 쾌활하게 붉은 얼굴은 말할 것도 없고요. 아무튼 그분은 거기 누워 계셨는데, 저는 제 삼촌이 여러 번 하시는 말씀을 들었습니다."], ["He was very fond of pickled walnuts, gentlemen.", "그분을 일으켜 세운 사람 말로는, 그분이 마치 한턱 얻어먹으려고 굴러떨어진 것처럼 즐겁게 미소 짓고 있었다는군요. 그리고 사람들이 그분의 피를 뽑고 나자, 의식이 돌아오는 첫 희미한 기미라는 것이 침대에서 벌떡 일어나 큰 소리로 웃음을 터뜨리고, 대야를 들고 있던 젊은 여자에게 입을 맞추고는 양고기 갈비와 호두 절임을 달라고 한 것이었답니다."], ["He said he always found that, taken without vinegar, they relished the beer.", "그분은 호두 절임을 무척 좋아하셨지요, 신사 여러분. 식초를 치지 않고 먹으면 맥주 맛이 한층 살아난다는 걸 늘 알게 되었다고 하셨습니다."]], [["‘My uncle’s great journey was in the fall of the leaf, at which time he collected debts, and took orders, in the north; going from London to Edinburgh, from Edinburgh to Glasgow, from Glasgow back to Edinburgh, and thence to London by the smack.", "\"제 삼촌의 큰 여정은 낙엽이 지는 가을철에 있었습니다. 그때 그분은 북쪽에서 빚을 거두고 주문을 받으셨지요."], ["You are to understand that his second visit to Edinburgh was for his own pleasure.", "런던에서 에든버러로, 에든버러에서 글래스고로, 글래스고에서 다시 에든버러로, 거기서 다시 소형 범선을 타고 런던으로 돌아오셨습니다."], ["He used to go back for a week, just to look up his old friends; and what with breakfasting with this one, lunching with that, dining with the third, and supping with another, a pretty tight week he used to make of it.", "두 번째로 에든버러를 찾으신 것은 그분 자신의 즐거움을 위해서였다는 점을 알아 두셔야 합니다. 그분은 옛 친구들을 만나려고 일주일쯤 다시 그곳에 가시곤 했지요."], ["I don’t know whether any of you, gentlemen, ever partook of a real substantial hospitable Scotch breakfast, and then went out to a slight lunch of a bushel of oysters, a dozen or so of bottled ale, and a noggin or two of whiskey to close up with.", "그런데 이 친구와 아침을 먹고, 저 친구와 점심을 먹고, 세 번째 친구와 저녁을 먹고, 또 다른 친구와 야식을 먹다 보면, 꽤나 빡빡한 일주일을 보내곤 하셨습니다. 신사 여러분 가운데 누구라도 진짜 푸짐하고 후한 스코틀랜드식 아침 식사를 드신 다음, 다시 굴 한 부셸과 병맥주 열두어 병에다 마무리로 위스키 한두 잔을 곁들이는 가벼운 점심을 드시러 나가 본 적이 있는지 모르겠습니다."], ["If you ever did, you will agree with me that it requires a pretty strong head to go out to dinner and supper afterwards.", "만약 한 번이라도 그러신 적이 있다면, 그러고 나서 다시 저녁과 야식을 먹으러 나가려면 꽤나 강한 머리가 필요하다는 데 제 의견에 동의하실 겁니다."]], [["‘But bless your hearts and eyebrows, all this sort of thing was nothing to my uncle!", "\"하지만, 가슴과 눈썹에 복이 있기를! 이런 종류의 일은 제 삼촌에게는 아무것도 아니었습니다!"], ["He was so well seasoned, that it was mere child’s play.", "그분은 워낙 단련이 잘 되어 있어서 그저 애들 장난에 불과했지요."], ["I have heard him say that he could see the Dundee people out, any day, and walk home afterwards without staggering; and yet the Dundee people have as strong heads and as strong punch, gentlemen, as you are likely to meet with, between the poles.", "저는 그분이 던디 사람들과 어느 날이든 끝까지 술자리를 함께한 다음 비틀거리지 않고 걸어서 집에 돌아올 수 있다고 말씀하시는 걸 들었습니다. 그런데 던디 사람들로 말하자면, 신사 여러분, 여러분이 지구의 양극 사이 어디에서 만나게 될 만한 이들 중에 머리도 가장 강하고 펀치도 가장 독한 자들입니다."], ["I have heard of a Glasgow man and a Dundee man drinking against each other for fifteen hours at a sitting.", "저는 글래스고 사람 하나와 던디 사람 하나가 한자리에 앉아 열다섯 시간 동안 서로 술 대결을 벌였다는 이야기를 들은 적이 있습니다."], ["They were both suffocated, as nearly as could be ascertained, at the same moment, but with this trifling exception, gentlemen, they were not a bit the worse for it.", "확인할 수 있는 한 두 사람은 거의 같은 순간에 질식했다고 하는데, 그 사소한 예외만 빼면, 신사 여러분, 두 사람은 그 일로 조금도 더 나빠지지 않았답니다."]], [["‘One night, within four-and-twenty hours of the time when he had settled to take shipping for London, my uncle supped at the house of a very old friend of his, a Bailie Mac something and four syllables after it, who lived in the old town of Edinburgh.", "\"어느 날 밤, 그분이 런던행 배를 타기로 정한 시각을 스물네 시간 앞두고, 제 삼촌은 아주 오랜 친구의 집에서 야식을 드셨습니다. 그 친구는 매크 무엇이라는, 그 뒤에 음절 네 개가 붙는 이름의 시의원이었는데, 에든버러의 구시가지에 살고 있었지요."], ["There were the bailie’s wife, and the bailie’s three daughters, and the bailie’s grown-up son, and three or four stout, bushy eye-browed, canny, old Scotch fellows, that the bailie had got together to do honour to my uncle, and help to make merry.", "그곳에는 시의원의 아내와, 시의원의 세 딸과, 다 큰 시의원의 아들과, 그리고 제 삼촌을 위해 시의원이 한자리에 불러 모아 흥을 돋우는 데 한몫하게 한, 건장하고 눈썹이 덥수룩하며 신중한 스코틀랜드 노인 서넛이 있었습니다."], ["It was a glorious supper.", "그것은 굉장한 야식이었지요."], ["There was kippered salmon, and Finnan haddocks, and a lamb’s head, and a haggis--a celebrated Scotch dish, gentlemen, which my uncle used to say always looked to him, when it came to table, very much like a Cupid’s stomach--and a great many other things besides, that I forget the names of, but very good things, notwithstanding.", "훈제 연어가 있었고, 피넌 대구가 있었고, 양 머리 요리가 있었고, 해기스가—이건 유명한 스코틀랜드 요리인데, 신사 여러분, 제 삼촌은 그것이 식탁에 오르면 늘 큐피드의 위장처럼 보인다고 하셨지요—그 밖에도 제가 이름은 잊어버렸지만 그래도 아주 훌륭한 음식들이 많이 있었습니다."], ["The lassies were pretty and agreeable; the bailie’s wife was one of the best creatures that ever lived; and my uncle was in thoroughly good cue.", "처녀들은 예쁘고 상냥했고, 시의원의 아내는 이제껏 살았던 사람들 중 가장 좋은 분이었으며, 제 삼촌은 더할 나위 없이 흥이 올라 있었습니다."], ["The consequence of which was, that the young ladies tittered and giggled, and the old lady laughed out loud, and the bailie and the other old fellows roared till they were red in the face, the whole mortal time.", "그 결과, 젊은 아가씨들은 키득키득 웃었고, 노부인은 큰 소리로 웃어 댔으며, 시의원과 다른 노인들은 그 내내 얼굴이 벌게지도록 껄껄대고 웃었지요. 야식 후에 각자가 위스키 토디를 몇 잔이나 마셨는지는 정확히 기억나지 않습니다만, 이것만은 압니다."], ["I don’t quite recollect how many tumblers of whiskey- toddy each man drank after supper; but this I know, that about one o’clock in the morning, the bailie’s grown-up son became insensible while attempting the first verse of “Willie brewed a peck o’ maut”; and he having been, for half an hour before, the only other man visible above the mahogany, it occurred to my uncle that it was almost time to think about going, especially as drinking had set in at seven o’clock, in order that he might get home at a decent hour.", "새벽 한 시쯤 시의원의 다 큰 아들이 「윌리가 보리 한 펙으로 술을 빚었네」의 첫 소절을 부르려다가 정신을 잃었고, 그가 그 반 시간 전부터 마호가니 식탁 위로 모습을 드러낸 유일한 다른 남자였던지라, 제 삼촌은 슬슬 갈 때를 생각해야겠다는 생각이 드셨습니다. 더구나 술자리가 일곱 시에 시작되었으니, 점잖은 시각에 집에 돌아가려면 말이지요."], ["But, thinking it might not be quite polite to go just then, my uncle voted himself into the chair, mixed another glass, rose to propose his own health, addressed himself in a neat and complimentary speech, and drank the toast with great enthusiasm.", "하지만 바로 그때 떠나는 것이 그다지 예의에 맞지 않을지 모른다고 생각하신 제 삼촌은, 스스로를 좌장으로 추대하고, 한 잔을 더 섞은 다음, 일어서서 자신의 건강을 위한 건배를 제의하고, 단정하고 정중한 연설로 자기 자신에게 인사를 건넨 뒤, 큰 열의를 담아 그 건배를 들이켰습니다."], ["Still nobody woke; so my uncle took a little drop more--neat this time, to prevent the toddy from disagreeing with him-- and, laying violent hands on his hat, sallied forth into the street.", "그래도 아무도 깨어나지 않았지요. 그래서 제 삼촌은 한 방울 더—이번에는 토디가 속에 거슬리지 않도록 그냥 위스키만—드신 다음, 모자를 거칠게 움켜잡고 거리로 나섰습니다."]], [["‘It was a wild, gusty night when my uncle closed the bailie’s door, and settling his hat firmly on his head to prevent the wind from taking it, thrust his hands into his pockets, and looking upward, took a short survey of the state of the weather.", "\"제 삼촌이 시의원의 집 문을 닫고, 바람에 모자가 날아가지 않도록 머리에 단단히 눌러쓰고, 두 손을 주머니에 찔러 넣은 채 위를 올려다보며 날씨 상태를 잠깐 살폈을 때, 그것은 거칠고 돌풍이 부는 밤이었습니다."], ["The clouds were drifting over the moon at their giddiest speed; at one time wholly obscuring her; at another, suffering her to burst forth in full splendour and shed her light on all the objects around; anon, driving over her again, with increased velocity, and shrouding everything in darkness.", "구름은 더없이 어지러운 속도로 달을 가로질러 흘러갔습니다. 어느 때는 달을 완전히 가렸다가, 또 어느 때는 달이 온전한 광채로 터져 나와 주위의 온갖 것에 빛을 뿌리도록 내버려 두었다가, 곧 다시 한층 빨라진 속도로 달 위를 내달려 모든 것을 어둠 속에 감추었지요."], ["“Really, this won’t do,”", "'정말이지, 이건 안 되겠는걸.'"], ["said my uncle, addressing himself to the weather, as if he felt himself personally offended.", "제 삼촌은 마치 개인적으로 모욕이라도 당한 듯 날씨를 향해 말했습니다."], ["“This is not at all the kind of thing for my voyage.", "'내 항해에는 도무지 어울리지 않는 날씨야."], ["It will not do at any price,”", "어떤 값을 치르더라도 안 되겠어.'"], ["said my uncle, very impressively.", "하고 제 삼촌은 아주 단호하게 말했지요."], ["Having repeated this, several times, he recovered his balance with some difficulty--for he was rather giddy with looking up into the sky so long--and walked merrily on.", "이 말을 여러 번 되풀이하신 다음, 그분은 다소 어렵사리 균형을 되찾았습니다—하늘을 너무 오래 올려다본 탓에 좀 어지러웠던 거지요—그러고는 즐겁게 걸어 나아갔습니다."]], [["‘The bailie’s house was in the Canongate, and my uncle was going to the other end of Leith Walk, rather better than a mile’s journey.", "\"시의원의 집은 캐넌게이트에 있었고, 제 삼촌은 리스 워크의 반대편 끝으로 가는 길이었습니다. 1마일이 조금 넘는 여정이었지요."], ["On either side of him, there shot up against the dark sky, tall, gaunt, straggling houses, with time-stained fronts, and windows that seemed to have shared the lot of eyes in mortals, and to have grown dim and sunken with age.", "그분 양옆으로는 검은 하늘을 향해 우뚝 솟은, 키 크고 앙상하며 제멋대로 뻗은 집들이 솟아 있었습니다. 세월에 얼룩진 정면과, 사람의 눈이 겪는 운명을 함께 나눈 듯 나이 들어 흐려지고 움푹 꺼진 창문을 단 집들이었지요."], ["Six, seven, eight storey high, were the houses; storey piled upon storey, as children build with cards--throwing their dark shadows over the roughly paved road, and making the dark night darker.", "그 집들은 여섯, 일곱, 여덟 층 높이였습니다. 아이들이 카드로 쌓아 올리듯 층 위에 층이 포개져 있어, 거칠게 포장된 길 위로 어두운 그림자를 드리우며 어두운 밤을 더욱 어둡게 만들었지요."], ["A few oil lamps were scattered at long distances, but they only served to mark the dirty entrance to some narrow close, or to show where a common stair communicated, by steep and intricate windings, with the various flats above.", "기름 등불 몇 개가 멀찍멀찍 떨어진 채 흩어져 있었지만, 그것들은 그저 어느 좁은 골목길의 더러운 입구를 표시하거나, 가파르고 복잡하게 굽이진 계단이 위쪽의 여러 층과 통하는 곳을 보여 줄 뿐이었습니다."], ["Glancing at all these things with the air of a man who had seen them too often before, to think them worthy of much notice now, my uncle walked up the middle of the street, with a thumb in each waistcoat pocket, indulging from time to time in various snatches of song, chanted forth with such good-will and spirit, that the quiet honest folk started from their first sleep and lay trembling in bed till the sound died away in the distance; when, satisfying themselves that it was only some drunken ne’er-do-weel finding his way home, they covered themselves up warm and fell asleep again.", "이 모든 것을, 너무 자주 보아 와서 이제는 별로 주목할 가치가 없다고 여기는 사람의 태도로 슬쩍 훑어보며, 제 삼촌은 양쪽 조끼 주머니에 엄지손가락을 하나씩 찔러 넣고 거리 한복판을 걸어 올라갔습니다. 이따금 노래의 이런저런 토막을 흥얼거렸는데, 어찌나 신명과 기운을 담아 불러 댔던지, 조용하고 정직한 사람들이 첫잠에서 깜짝 놀라 깨어나 그 소리가 멀리서 잦아들 때까지 침대에서 떨며 누워 있다가, 그저 어느 술 취한 건달이 제 집을 찾아가는 소리일 뿐이라고 스스로를 안심시키고는, 다시 이불을 따뜻하게 뒤집어쓰고 잠들곤 했지요."]], [["‘I am particular in describing how my uncle walked up the middle of the street, with his thumbs in his waistcoat pockets, gentlemen, because, as he often used to say (and with great reason too) there is nothing at all extraordinary in this story, unless you distinctly understand at the beginning, that he was not by any means of a marvellous or romantic turn.", "\"제가 제 삼촌이 조끼 주머니에 엄지손가락을 찔러 넣고 거리 한복판을 걸어 올라갔다는 것을 이렇게 자세히 묘사하는 데는 까닭이 있습니다, 신사 여러분. 그분이 자주 하시던 말씀처럼(그리고 그건 충분히 일리 있는 말씀이지요), 이 이야기에는 조금도 비범한 구석이 없거든요. 다만 그분이 결코 기이하거나 낭만적인 기질의 인물이 아니었다는 것을 처음부터 분명히 이해하셔야 합니다."]], [["‘Gentlemen, my uncle walked on with his thumbs in his waistcoat pockets, taking the middle of the street to himself, and singing, now a verse of a love song, and then a verse of a drinking one, and when he was tired of both, whistling melodiously, until he reached the North Bridge, which, at this point, connects the old and new towns of Edinburgh. Here he stopped for a minute, to look at the strange, irregular clusters of lights piled one above the other, and twinkling afar off so high, that they looked like stars, gleaming from the castle walls on the one side and the Calton Hill on the other, as if they illuminated veritable castles in the air; while the old picturesque town slept heavily on, in gloom and darkness below: its palace and chapel of Holyrood, guarded day and night, as a friend of my uncle’s used to say, by old Arthur’s Seat, towering, surly and dark, like some gruff genius, over the ancient city he has watched so long. I say, gentlemen, my uncle stopped here, for a minute, to look about him; and then, paying a compliment to the weather, which had a little cleared up, though the moon was sinking, walked on again, as royally as before; keeping the middle of the road with great dignity, and looking as if he would very much like to meet with somebody who would dispute possession of it with him. There was nobody at all disposed to contest the point, as it happened; and so, on he went, with his thumbs in his waistcoat pockets, like a lamb.", "\"신사 여러분, 제 삼촌은 조끼 주머니에 엄지손가락을 찔러 넣고 거리 한복판을 독차지한 채 걸었습니다. 이번에는 연가의 한 소절을, 다음에는 권주가의 한 소절을 부르다가, 둘 다 부르기 지치면 가락에 맞춰 멋들어지게 휘파람을 불며 걸어갔지요. 그렇게 노스 브리지에 이르렀는데, 이 다리는 이 지점에서 에든버러의 구시가지와 신시가지를 잇고 있습니다. 여기서 그분은 잠시 멈춰 서서, 차곡차곡 포개진 채 멀리서 그토록 높이 반짝이는, 기이하고 불규칙하게 무리 지은 불빛들을 바라보았습니다. 그 불빛들은 마치 별처럼 보였지요. 한쪽으로는 성벽에서, 다른 쪽으로는 캘턴 힐에서 반짝이며, 마치 진짜로 공중에 떠 있는 성들을 환히 밝히는 듯했습니다. 그동안 그림처럼 아름다운 옛 시가지는 그 아래 음울함과 어둠 속에서 무겁게 잠들어 있었습니다. 그 왕궁과 홀리루드 예배당은, 제 삼촌의 한 친구가 늘 하던 말마따나, 밤낮으로 늙은 아서즈 시트가 지키고 있었지요. 그 늙은 봉우리는 오래도록 지켜봐 온 그 고대 도시 위로, 무뚝뚝하고 시커멓게 솟아올라, 마치 무뚝뚝한 정령처럼 우뚝 서 있었습니다. 말씀드리자면, 신사 여러분, 제 삼촌은 여기서 잠시 멈춰 주위를 둘러보았고, 그러고는 비록 달이 기울고 있었지만 조금 개어진 날씨에 한마디 찬사를 보낸 뒤, 다시 전처럼 당당하게 걸어 나아갔습니다. 큰 위엄을 갖추고 길 한복판을 지키며, 마치 누구든 그 자리의 소유권을 두고 다투려는 자가 나타나기를 몹시 바라는 듯한 표정으로요. 그런데 마침 그 점을 다투려는 자는 아무도 없었지요. 그래서 그분은 조끼 주머니에 엄지손가락을 찔러 넣은 채, 어린 양처럼 걸어갔습니다."]], [["‘When my uncle reached the end of Leith Walk, he had to cross a pretty large piece of waste ground which separated him from a short street which he had to turn down to go direct to his lodging. Now, in this piece of waste ground, there was, at that time, an enclosure belonging to some wheelwright who contracted with the Post Office for the purchase of old, worn-out mail coaches; and my uncle, being very fond of coaches, old, young, or middle-aged, all at once took it into his head to step out of his road for no other purpose than to peep between the palings at these mails--about a dozen of which he remembered to have seen, crowded together in a very forlorn and dismantled state, inside. My uncle was a very enthusiastic, emphatic sort of person, gentlemen; so, finding that he could not obtain a good peep between the palings he got over them, and sitting himself quietly down on an old axle-tree, began to contemplate the mail coaches with a deal of gravity.", "\"제 삼촌이 리스 워크의 끝에 이르렀을 때, 그분은 꽤 넓은 빈터를 가로질러야 했습니다. 그 빈터는 그분이 곧장 숙소로 가려면 꺾어 들어가야 하는 짧은 거리와 그분 사이를 갈라놓고 있었지요. 그런데 이 빈터에는 그 무렵 어느 수레바퀴 목수의 울타리 친 마당이 하나 있었습니다. 그 목수는 낡고 닳아 빠진 우편 마차를 사들이는 계약을 우체국과 맺고 있었지요. 그리고 마차라면 낡았든 새것이든 중년이든 무척이나 좋아하셨던 제 삼촌은, 갑자기 이 우편 마차들을 울타리 틈으로 들여다보겠다는 일념으로 가던 길에서 벗어나야겠다는 생각이 들었습니다. 그분은 그 안에 열두어 대쯤의 마차가 아주 처량하게 해체된 상태로 빽빽이 모여 있는 것을 본 기억이 있었거든요. 제 삼촌은 아주 열정적이고 적극적인 부류의 사람이었습니다, 신사 여러분. 그래서 울타리 틈으로는 제대로 들여다볼 수가 없다는 것을 알게 되자, 그분은 울타리를 넘어가 낡은 차축 위에 조용히 앉아서는, 그 우편 마차들을 자못 진지하게 바라보기 시작했습니다."]], [["‘There might be a dozen of them, or there might be more--my uncle was never quite certain on this point, and being a man of very scrupulous veracity about numbers, didn’t like to say--but there they stood, all huddled together in the most desolate condition imaginable.", "\"마차는 열두 대쯤 되었을 수도 있고, 더 많았을 수도 있습니다—제 삼촌은 이 점에 대해서는 결코 확신하지 못했고, 숫자에 관해서는 무척 양심적으로 정확을 기하는 분이었던지라 함부로 말씀하기를 꺼리셨지요—아무튼 마차들은 거기, 상상할 수 있는 가장 황량한 꼴로 한데 옹기종기 모여 서 있었습니다. 문짝은 경첩에서 뜯겨 나가 치워져 있었고, 안감은 벗겨져 여기저기 녹슨 못 하나에 조각만 너덜너덜 매달려 있었습니다."], ["The doors had been torn from their hinges and removed; the linings had been stripped off, only a shred hanging here and there by a rusty nail; the lamps were gone, the poles had long since vanished, the ironwork was rusty, the paint was worn away; the wind whistled through the chinks in the bare woodwork; and the rain, which had collected on the roofs, fell, drop by drop, into the insides with a hollow and melancholy sound.", "등불은 사라졌고, 끌채는 진작에 없어졌으며, 쇠붙이는 녹슬었고, 칠은 다 벗겨졌습니다. 바람은 헐벗은 목재의 틈새로 휘파람 소리를 냈고, 지붕에 고였던 빗물은 텅 빈 듯 우울한 소리를 내며 한 방울 한 방울 마차 안으로 떨어졌습니다."], ["They were the decaying skeletons of departed mails, and in that lonely place, at that time of night, they looked chill and dismal.", "그것들은 떠나간 우편 마차들의 썩어 가는 해골이었고, 그 외딴곳에서, 그 밤 시각에, 그것들은 으스스하고 음산해 보였습니다."]], [["‘My uncle rested his head upon his hands, and thought of the busy, bustling people who had rattled about, years before, in the old coaches, and were now as silent and changed; he thought of the numbers of people to whom one of these crazy, mouldering vehicles had borne, night after night, for many years, and through all weathers, the anxiously expected intelligence, the eagerly looked-for remittance, the promised assurance of health and safety, the sudden announcement of sickness and death.", "\"제 삼촌은 두 손에 머리를 괴고, 여러 해 전 그 낡은 마차들 속에서 덜컹거리며 분주히 오가던 바쁜 사람들을, 그리고 이제는 그 마차들처럼 말없이 변해 버린 그들을 생각했습니다. 그분은 이 낡아 가고 곰팡이 슨 마차들 가운데 한 대가 여러 해 동안 밤마다, 온갖 날씨를 뚫고, 수많은 사람들에게 애타게 기다리던 소식을, 간절히 바라던 송금을, 약속된 건강과 안전에 대한 보증을, 또 갑작스러운 병과 죽음의 통지를 실어 날랐던 것을 생각했습니다."], ["The merchant, the lover, the wife, the widow, the mother, the school-boy, the very child who tottered to the door at the postman’s knock--how had they all looked forward to the arrival of the old coach.", "상인, 연인, 아내, 과부, 어머니, 학생, 그리고 우편배달부의 노크 소리에 비틀비틀 문가로 걸어 나오던 바로 그 어린아이까지—그들 모두가 얼마나 그 낡은 마차의 도착을 손꼽아 기다렸던가요."], ["And where were they all now?", "그런데 그들은 지금 모두 어디에 있을까요?"]], [["‘Gentlemen, my uncle used to say that he thought all this at the time, but I rather suspect he learned it out of some book afterwards, for he distinctly stated that he fell into a kind of doze, as he sat on the old axle-tree looking at the decayed mail coaches, and that he was suddenly awakened by some deep church bell striking two.", "\"신사 여러분, 제 삼촌은 자기가 그때 이 모든 것을 생각했다고 말씀하시곤 했지만, 저는 그분이 나중에 어떤 책에서 그것을 배운 게 아닌가 하는 의심이 좀 듭니다. 왜냐하면 그분은 낡은 차축 위에 앉아 썩어 가는 우편 마차를 바라보다가 일종의 선잠에 빠져들었고, 어느 깊은 교회 종이 두 시를 치는 소리에 불현듯 잠에서 깨어났다고 분명히 말씀하셨거든요."], ["Now, my uncle was never a fast thinker, and if he had thought all these things, I am quite certain it would have taken him till full half-past two o’clock at the very least.", "그런데 제 삼촌은 결코 생각이 빠른 분이 아니었습니다. 만약 그분이 이 모든 것을 정말로 생각하셨다면, 저는 적어도 두 시 반은 꽉 채워 걸렸으리라 확신합니다."], ["I am, therefore, decidedly of opinion, gentlemen, that my uncle fell into a kind of doze, without having thought about anything at all.", "그러므로 저는, 신사 여러분, 제 삼촌이 아무것도 생각하지 않은 채 일종의 선잠에 빠져들었다는 쪽으로 단연 의견이 기웁니다."]], [["‘Be this as it may, a church bell struck two.", "\"어찌 되었든, 교회 종이 두 시를 쳤습니다."], ["My uncle woke, rubbed his eyes, and jumped up in astonishment.", "제 삼촌은 잠에서 깨어, 눈을 비비고, 깜짝 놀라 벌떡 일어났습니다."]], [["‘In one instant, after the clock struck two, the whole of this deserted and quiet spot had become a scene of most extraordinary life and animation.", "\"시계가 두 시를 친 직후 그 순간, 이 버려지고 고요하던 곳 전체가 더없이 비상한 생기와 활기의 무대로 변해 있었습니다. 우편 마차의 문은 경첩에 달려 있었고, 안감은 새로 갈려 있었으며, 쇠붙이는 새것이나 다름없었고, 칠은 새로 입혀졌으며, 등불은 켜져 있었습니다."], ["The mail coach doors were on their hinges, the lining was replaced, the ironwork was as good as new, the paint was restored, the lamps were alight; cushions and greatcoats were on every coach-box, porters were thrusting parcels into every boot, guards were stowing away letter-bags, hostlers were dashing pails of water against the renovated wheels; numbers of men were pushing about, fixing poles into every coach; passengers arrived, portmanteaus were handed up, horses were put to; in short, it was perfectly clear that every mail there, was to be off directly.", "마부석마다 방석과 두꺼운 외투가 놓여 있었고, 짐꾼들은 짐칸마다 소포를 밀어 넣고 있었으며, 차장들은 우편 행낭을 챙겨 넣고 있었고, 마부 조수들은 새로 단장한 바퀴에 물통의 물을 끼얹고 있었습니다. 수많은 사람이 분주히 오가며 마차마다 끌채를 끼워 맞추고 있었고, 승객들이 도착했으며, 여행 가방들이 위로 건네지고, 말들이 매여졌습니다."], ["Gentlemen, my uncle opened his eyes so wide at all this, that, to the very last moment of his life, he used to wonder how it fell out that he had ever been able to shut ‘em again.", "요컨대, 거기 있는 모든 우편 마차가 곧 떠날 참이라는 것이 더없이 분명했습니다. 신사 여러분, 제 삼촌은 이 모든 것에 어찌나 눈을 크게 떴던지, 죽는 날까지도 어떻게 그가 그 눈을 다시 감을 수 있었는지 의아해하시곤 했습니다."]], [["‘“Now then!”", "\"'자, 그럼!'"], ["said a voice, as my uncle felt a hand on his shoulder, “you’re booked for one inside.", "제 삼촌이 어깨에 손이 닿는 것을 느꼈을 때 한 목소리가 말했습니다. '당신은 안쪽 자리 하나에 예약되어 있소."], ["You’d better get in.”", "어서 타는 게 좋겠소.'"]], [["‘“I booked!”", "\"'내가 예약을 했다고!'"], ["said my uncle, turning round.", "제 삼촌이 돌아서며 말했습니다."]], [["‘“Yes, certainly.”", "\"'그렇소, 틀림없이.'"]], [["‘My uncle, gentlemen, could say nothing, he was so very much astonished.", "\"신사 여러분, 제 삼촌은 너무나 놀란 나머지 아무 말도 할 수 없었습니다."], ["The queerest thing of all was that although there was such a crowd of persons, and although fresh faces were pouring in, every moment, there was no telling where they came from.", "그 가운데서도 가장 기이한 것은, 그토록 많은 사람이 북적이고 매 순간 새로운 얼굴들이 밀려들었는데도, 도대체 그들이 어디서 왔는지 알 수가 없다는 점이었습니다."], ["They seemed to start up, in some strange manner, from the ground, or the air, and disappear in the same way.", "그들은 어떤 기이한 방식으로 땅에서, 혹은 공기에서 불쑥 솟아났다가, 같은 방식으로 사라지는 듯했습니다."], ["When a porter had put his luggage in the coach, and received his fare, he turned round and was gone; and before my uncle had well begun to wonder what had become of him, half a dozen fresh ones started up, and staggered along under the weight of parcels, which seemed big enough to crush them.", "짐꾼 하나가 짐을 마차에 실어 주고 삯을 받고 나면, 그는 돌아서서 사라져 버렸지요. 그리고 제 삼촌이 미처 그가 어디로 갔는지 의아해하기도 전에, 여섯 명쯤의 새로운 짐꾼이 불쑥 솟아나, 그들을 짓눌러 버릴 만큼 커다란 소포의 무게에 휘청거리며 걸어왔습니다."], ["The passengers were all dressed so oddly too!", "승객들도 모두 어찌나 괴상하게 차려입었던지요!"], ["Large, broad-skirted laced coats, with great cuffs and no collars; and wigs, gentlemen--great formal wigs with a tie behind.", "큼지막한 소맷부리에 옷깃은 없고, 자락이 넓게 퍼진 레이스 달린 큰 코트를 입었고, 가발을—신사 여러분, 뒤에 끈을 묶은 크고 격식 차린 가발을 쓰고 있었습니다."], ["My uncle could make nothing of it.", "제 삼촌은 도무지 영문을 알 수가 없었지요."]], [["‘“Now, are you going to get in?”", "\"'자, 탈 거요, 안 탈 거요?'"], ["said the person who had addressed my uncle before.", "앞서 제 삼촌에게 말을 걸었던 사람이 말했습니다."], ["He was dressed as a mail guard, with a wig on his head and most enormous cuffs to his coat, and had a lantern in one hand, and a huge blunderbuss in the other, which he was going to stow away in his little arm-chest.", "그는 우편 마차 차장 차림이었는데, 머리에 가발을 쓰고 코트에는 엄청나게 큰 소맷부리를 달았으며, 한 손에는 등불을, 다른 손에는 거대한 나팔총을 들고 있었습니다. 그는 그 총을 자기 작은 팔걸이 상자에 챙겨 넣으려던 참이었지요."], ["“are you going to get in, Jack Martin?”", "'탈 거요, 안 탈 거요, 잭 마틴?'"], ["said the guard, holding the lantern to my uncle’s face.", "차장이 제 삼촌의 얼굴에 등불을 들이대며 말했습니다."]], [["‘“Hollo!”", "\"'이봐!'"], ["said my uncle, falling back a step or two.", "제 삼촌이 한두 걸음 물러서며 말했습니다."], ["“That’s familiar!”", "'말버릇이 너무 무람없군!'"]], [["‘“It’s so on the way-bill,”", "\"'운송 명부에 그렇게 적혀 있소.'"], ["said the guard.", "차장이 말했습니다."]], [["‘“Isn’t there a ‘Mister’ before it?”", "\"'그 앞에 '미스터'가 안 붙어 있나?'"], ["said my uncle.", "제 삼촌이 말했습니다."], ["For he felt, gentlemen, that for a guard he didn’t know, to call him Jack Martin, was a liberty which the Post Office wouldn’t have sanctioned if they had known it.", "신사 여러분, 그분은 일면식도 없는 차장이 자기를 잭 마틴이라고 부르는 것은, 우체국이 그 사실을 알았더라면 결코 허용하지 않았을 무례라고 느끼셨던 거지요."]], [["‘“No, there is not,”", "\"'아니, 안 붙어 있소.'"], ["rejoined the guard coolly.", "차장이 냉랭하게 대꾸했습니다."]], [["‘“Is the fare paid?”", "\"'삯은 치러졌나?'"], ["inquired my uncle.", "제 삼촌이 물었습니다."]], [["‘“Of course it is,”", "\"'물론 치러졌소.'"], ["rejoined the guard.", "차장이 대꾸했습니다."]], [["‘“It is, is it?”", "\"'치러졌다, 이 말이지?'"], ["said my uncle.", "제 삼촌이 말했습니다."], ["“Then here goes!", "'그럼 탑시다!"], ["Which coach?”", "어느 마차요?'"]], [["‘“This,”", "\"'이거요.'"], ["said the guard, pointing to an old-fashioned Edinburgh and London mail, which had the steps down and the door open.", "차장이 발판이 내려지고 문이 열린 구식 에든버러-런던 우편 마차를 가리키며 말했습니다."], ["“Stop!", "'잠깐!"], ["Here are the other passengers.", "다른 승객들이 왔소."], ["Let them get in first.”", "저들이 먼저 타게 하시오.'"]], [["‘As the guard spoke, there all at once appeared, right in front of my uncle, a young gentleman in a powdered wig, and a sky-blue coat trimmed with silver, made very full and broad in the skirts, which were lined with buckram.", "\"차장이 말하는 사이, 제 삼촌 바로 앞에 별안간 한 젊은 신사가 나타났습니다. 분을 바른 가발을 쓰고, 은빛 장식을 두른 하늘색 코트를 입었는데, 자락은 아주 풍성하고 넓게 만들어졌고 안쪽에는 빳빳한 심을 댄 천을 대었습니다."], ["Tiggin and Welps were in the printed calico and waistcoat piece line, gentlemen, so my uncle knew all the materials at once.", "티긴 앤드 웰프스는 무늬 옥양목과 조끼 옷감을 취급하는 곳이었지요, 신사 여러분. 그래서 제 삼촌은 그 옷감들을 한눈에 다 알아보았습니다."], ["He wore knee breeches, and a kind of leggings rolled up over his silk stockings, and shoes with buckles; he had ruffles at his wrists, a three-cornered hat on his head, and a long taper sword by his side.", "그는 무릎까지 오는 반바지를 입었고, 비단 양말 위로 일종의 각반을 둘둘 말아 올렸으며, 버클이 달린 구두를 신고 있었습니다. 손목에는 주름 장식을 달았고, 머리에는 삼각모를 썼으며, 옆구리에는 길고 가느다란 검을 차고 있었습니다."], ["The flaps of his waist-coat came half-way down his thighs, and the ends of his cravat reached to his waist.", "조끼 자락은 허벅지 중간까지 내려왔고, 크라바트 끝은 허리까지 닿았지요."], ["He stalked gravely to the coach door, pulled off his hat, and held it above his head at arm’s length, cocking his little finger in the air at the same time, as some affected people do, when they take a cup of tea.", "그는 점잔을 빼며 마차 문 쪽으로 성큼성큼 걸어가, 모자를 벗어 팔을 쭉 뻗어 머리 위로 들어 올렸는데, 그와 동시에 새끼손가락을 공중에 까딱 세웠습니다. 어떤 점잔 빼는 사람들이 차를 한 잔 들 때 그러듯이요."], ["Then he drew his feet together, and made a low, grave bow, and then put out his left hand.", "그러고는 두 발을 모으고, 낮고 정중하게 절을 한 다음, 왼손을 내밀었습니다."], ["My uncle was just going to step forward, and shake it heartily, when he perceived that these attentions were directed, not towards him, but to a young lady who just then appeared at the foot of the steps, attired in an old-fashioned green velvet dress with a long waist and stomacher.", "제 삼촌은 막 앞으로 나서서 그 손을 힘차게 잡으려던 참이었는데, 그 정중한 예가 자기가 아니라 바로 그때 발판 아래에 나타난 한 젊은 숙녀를 향한 것임을 알아차렸습니다. 그 숙녀는 허리가 길고 가슴 장식을 댄 구식 초록색 벨벳 드레스를 입고 있었습니다."], ["She had no bonnet on her head, gentlemen, which was muffled in a black silk hood, but she looked round for an instant as she prepared to get into the coach, and such a beautiful face as she disclosed, my uncle had never seen--not even in a picture.", "신사 여러분, 그녀는 머리에 보닛을 쓰지 않았고 대신 검은 비단 두건으로 머리를 감쌌는데, 마차에 오르려고 채비하면서 잠깐 주위를 둘러보았습니다. 그때 드러난 그토록 아름다운 얼굴을 제 삼촌은 한 번도 본 적이 없었지요—심지어 그림에서조차도요."], ["She got into the coach, holding up her dress with one hand; and as my uncle always said with a round oath, when he told the story, he wouldn’t have believed it possible that legs and feet could have been brought to such a state of perfection unless he had seen them with his own eyes.", "그녀는 한 손으로 드레스 자락을 추켜올린 채 마차에 올랐습니다. 그런데 제 삼촌이 이 이야기를 하실 때마다 큼지막한 욕을 곁들여 늘 하시던 말씀이지만, 그분은 자기 두 눈으로 직접 보지 않았더라면 다리와 발이 그토록 완벽한 경지에 이를 수 있다는 것을 도무지 믿지 못했을 거랍니다."]], [["‘But, in this one glimpse of the beautiful face, my uncle saw that the young lady cast an imploring look upon him, and that she appeared terrified and distressed.", "\"하지만 그 아름다운 얼굴을 한 번 흘끗 보는 사이에, 제 삼촌은 그 젊은 숙녀가 자기에게 애원하는 눈길을 던지는 것을, 그리고 그녀가 겁에 질려 괴로워하는 듯이 보이는 것을 알아차렸습니다."], ["He noticed, too, that the young fellow in the powdered wig, notwithstanding his show of gallantry, which was all very fine and grand, clasped her tight by the wrist when she got in, and followed himself immediately afterwards.", "또한 그분은, 분을 바른 가발을 쓴 젊은 사내가, 겉으로는 매우 멋지고 거창한 정중함을 내보이면서도, 그녀가 탈 때 그녀의 손목을 꽉 움켜잡고는, 곧바로 자기도 뒤따라 올라타는 것을 눈여겨보았습니다."], ["An uncommonly ill-looking fellow, in a close brown wig, and a plum-coloured suit, wearing a very large sword, and boots up to his hips, belonged to the party; and when he sat himself down next to the young lady, who shrank into a corner at his approach, my uncle was confirmed in his original impression that something dark and mysterious was going forward, or, as he always said himself, that “there was a screw loose somewhere.”", "일행 중에는 또 한 사람, 유난히 험상궂게 생긴 사내가 있었는데, 바싹 붙는 갈색 가발을 쓰고 자줏빛 옷을 입었으며, 아주 큰 검을 차고 엉덩이까지 올라오는 장화를 신고 있었습니다. 그가 젊은 숙녀 옆에 앉자—그녀는 그가 다가오자 구석으로 움츠러들었지요—제 삼촌은 무언가 어둡고 수상쩍은 일이 벌어지고 있다는, 혹은 그분 자신이 늘 하시던 말마따나 '어딘가 나사가 하나 빠져 있다'는 처음의 인상을 굳히게 되었습니다."], ["It’s quite surprising how quickly he made up his mind to help the lady at any peril, if she needed any help.", "그분이 만약 그 숙녀가 도움을 필요로 한다면 어떤 위험을 무릅쓰고라도 그녀를 돕겠다고 얼마나 빨리 마음을 정했는지는 정말이지 놀라울 정도입니다."]], [["‘“Death and lightning!”", "\"'죽음과 번개로다!'"], ["exclaimed the young gentleman, laying his hand upon his sword as my uncle entered the coach.", "제 삼촌이 마차에 오를 때 젊은 신사가 검에 손을 얹으며 외쳤습니다."]], [["‘“Blood and thunder!”", "\"'피와 천둥이다!'"], ["roared the other gentleman.", "다른 신사가 으르렁거렸습니다."], ["With this, he whipped his sword out, and made a lunge at my uncle without further ceremony.", "그러고는 검을 휙 뽑아 들더니, 더 이상 격식도 차리지 않고 제 삼촌을 향해 찔러 들어왔습니다."], ["My uncle had no weapon about him, but with great dexterity he snatched the ill-looking gentleman’s three-cornered hat from his head, and, receiving the point of his sword right through the crown, squeezed the sides together, and held it tight.", "제 삼촌은 몸에 무기 하나 지니고 있지 않았지만, 굉장한 솜씨로 그 험상궂은 신사의 삼각모를 머리에서 낚아채, 검 끝이 모자 꼭대기를 곧장 꿰뚫고 들어오자, 양쪽을 한데 쥐어짜 단단히 붙들었습니다."]], [["‘“Pink him behind!”", "\"'뒤에서 찔러 버려!'"], ["cried the ill-looking gentleman to his companion, as he struggled to regain his sword.", "험상궂은 신사가 검을 되찾으려 안간힘을 쓰며 동료에게 소리쳤습니다."]], [["‘“He had better not,”", "\"'안 하는 게 좋을걸.'"], ["cried my uncle, displaying the heel of one of his shoes, in a threatening manner.", "제 삼촌이 위협하듯 한쪽 구두 뒤축을 들어 보이며 외쳤습니다."], ["“I’ll kick his brains out, if he has any--, or fracture his skull if he hasn’t.”", "'머리통에 골이 있거든 그 골을 걷어차 버리겠다—없거든 두개골을 박살 내 주지.'"], ["Exerting all his strength, at this moment, my uncle wrenched the ill-looking man’s sword from his grasp, and flung it clean out of the coach window, upon which the younger gentleman vociferated, “Death and lightning!”", "바로 이 순간, 제 삼촌은 온 힘을 다해 그 험상궂은 사내의 검을 그의 손아귀에서 비틀어 빼앗아, 마차 창밖으로 멀찍이 내던졌습니다. 그러자 젊은 신사가 다시 '죽음과 번개로다!'"], ["again, and laid his hand upon the hilt of his sword, in a very fierce manner, but didn’t draw it.", "하고 고래고래 외치며, 아주 사납게 자기 검 자루에 손을 얹었지만, 검을 뽑지는 않았습니다."], ["Perhaps, gentlemen, as my uncle used to say with a smile, perhaps he was afraid of alarming the lady.", "어쩌면, 신사 여러분, 제 삼촌이 미소를 지으며 늘 하시던 말씀처럼, 어쩌면 그는 숙녀를 놀라게 할까 봐 두려웠던 것이겠지요."]], [["‘“Now, gentlemen,”", "\"'자, 신사 여러분.' 제 삼촌이 침착하게 자리에 앉으며 말했습니다."], ["said my uncle, taking his seat deliberately, “I don’t want to have any death, with or without lightning, in a lady’s presence, and we have had quite blood and thundering enough for one journey; so, if you please, we’ll sit in our places like quiet insides.", "'나는 숙녀가 계신 자리에서 죽음이든 뭐든, 번개가 따르든 안 따르든, 그런 걸 벌이고 싶지 않소. 그리고 우리는 이번 여정 하나치고는 피와 천둥을 충분히 겪었소."], ["Here, guard, pick up that gentleman’s carving-knife.”", "그러니 괜찮다면 우리 모두 얌전한 안쪽 승객답게 제자리에 앉읍시다. 이봐요, 차장, 저 신사의 식칼이나 좀 주워 주시오.'"]], [["‘As quickly as my uncle said the words, the guard appeared at the coach window, with the gentleman’s sword in his hand.", "\"제 삼촌이 그 말을 하기가 무섭게, 차장이 그 신사의 검을 손에 들고 마차 창가에 나타났습니다."], ["He held up his lantern, and looked earnestly in my uncle’s face, as he handed it in, when, by its light, my uncle saw, to his great surprise, that an immense crowd of mail-coach guards swarmed round the window, every one of whom had his eyes earnestly fixed upon him too.", "그는 검을 안으로 건네면서 등불을 치켜들고 제 삼촌의 얼굴을 진지하게 들여다보았는데, 그 불빛으로 제 삼촌은 크게 놀라며 보았습니다. 어마어마한 수의 우편 마차 차장들이 창문 둘레에 떼 지어 몰려 있었고, 그들 하나하나가 역시 진지한 눈으로 자기를 응시하고 있었던 것이지요."], ["He had never seen such a sea of white faces, red bodies, and earnest eyes, in all his born days.", "그분은 태어나서 그토록 많은 하얀 얼굴과 붉은 몸뚱이와 진지한 눈동자의 바다를 본 적이 없었습니다."]], [["‘“This is the strangest sort of thing I ever had anything to do with,”", "\"'이건 내가 이제껏 관여해 본 것 중에 가장 기이한 일이로군.'"], ["thought my uncle; “allow me to return you your hat, sir.”", "제 삼촌은 속으로 생각했습니다. '모자를 돌려 드리지요, 선생.'"]], [["‘The ill-looking gentleman received his three-cornered hat in silence, looked at the hole in the middle with an inquiring air, and finally stuck it on the top of his wig with a solemnity the effect of which was a trifle impaired by his sneezing violently at the moment, and jerking it off again.", "\"험상궂은 신사는 말없이 자기 삼각모를 받아, 한가운데 뚫린 구멍을 미심쩍은 표정으로 바라보다가, 마침내 자못 엄숙하게 그것을 자기 가발 꼭대기에 눌러썼습니다. 그런데 그 순간 그가 격렬하게 재채기를 하며 모자를 다시 홱 떨어뜨리는 바람에, 그 엄숙함의 효과가 약간 손상되고 말았지요."]], [["‘“All right!”", "\"'다 됐소!'"], ["cried the guard with the lantern, mounting into his little seat behind.", "등불을 든 차장이 뒤쪽 자기 작은 자리에 올라타며 외쳤습니다."], ["Away they went.", "그들은 출발했습니다."], ["My uncle peeped out of the coach window as they emerged from the yard, and observed that the other mails, with coachmen, guards, horses, and passengers, complete, were driving round and round in circles, at a slow trot of about five miles an hour.", "제 삼촌은 마당을 빠져나오면서 마차 창밖을 내다보았는데, 다른 우편 마차들이 마부와 차장과 말과 승객까지 완비한 채, 시속 5마일쯤의 느린 속보로 원을 그리며 빙글빙글 돌고 있는 것을 보았습니다."], ["My uncle burned with indignation, gentlemen.", "제 삼촌은 분노로 들끓었지요, 신사 여러분."], ["As a commercial man, he felt that the mail-bags were not to be trifled with, and he resolved to memorialise the Post Office on the subject, the very instant he reached London.", "상업에 종사하는 사람으로서 그분은 우편 행낭을 가지고 장난쳐서는 안 된다고 느꼈고, 런던에 도착하는 바로 그 순간 이 문제에 관해 우체국에 진정서를 내기로 결심했습니다."]], [["‘At present, however, his thoughts were occupied with the young lady who sat in the farthest corner of the coach, with her face muffled closely in her hood; the gentleman with the sky-blue coat sitting opposite to her; the other man in the plum-coloured suit, by her side; and both watching her intently.", "\"하지만 지금 당장은, 그분의 생각이 마차의 가장 먼 구석에 두건으로 얼굴을 바싹 감싸고 앉아 있는 젊은 숙녀에게 쏠려 있었습니다. 하늘색 코트를 입은 신사는 그녀 맞은편에 앉아 있었고, 자줏빛 옷을 입은 다른 사내는 그녀 옆에 앉아 있었으며, 둘 다 그녀를 뚫어지게 지켜보고 있었습니다."], ["If she so much as rustled the folds of her hood, he could hear the ill-looking man clap his hand upon his sword, and could tell by the other’s breathing (it was so dark he couldn’t see his face) that he was looking as big as if he were going to devour her at a mouthful.", "그녀가 두건 자락을 조금이라도 바스락거리면, 험상궂은 사내가 검에 손을 탁 얹는 소리가 들렸고, 다른 사내의 숨소리로 보아(너무 어두워서 그 얼굴은 볼 수 없었지요) 그가 마치 그녀를 한입에 집어삼킬 듯이 험악한 표정을 짓고 있다는 것을 알 수 있었습니다."], ["This roused my uncle more and more, and he resolved, come what might, to see the end of it.", "이것이 제 삼촌을 점점 더 격앙시켰고, 그분은 무슨 일이 있어도 이 일의 결말을 보고야 말겠다고 결심했습니다."], ["He had a great admiration for bright eyes, and sweet faces, and pretty legs and feet; in short, he was fond of the whole sex.", "그분은 빛나는 눈과 어여쁜 얼굴과 예쁜 다리와 발을 무척 흠모했습니다. 요컨대 그분은 그 성(性) 전체를 좋아하셨지요."], ["It runs in our family, gentleman--so am I.", "이건 우리 집안 내력입니다, 신사 여러분—저도 그러니까요."]], [["‘Many were the devices which my uncle practised, to attract the lady’s attention, or at all events, to engage the mysterious gentlemen in conversation.", "\"제 삼촌은 그 숙녀의 주의를 끌거나, 적어도 그 수상쩍은 신사들을 대화에 끌어들이려고 갖가지 수단을 부려 보았습니다. 모두 헛수고였지요."], ["They were all in vain; the gentlemen wouldn’t talk, and the lady didn’t dare.", "신사들은 말을 하려 들지 않았고, 숙녀는 감히 말을 하지 못했습니다."], ["He thrust his head out of the coach window at intervals, and bawled out to know why they didn’t go faster.", "그분은 이따금 마차 창밖으로 머리를 내밀고, 왜 더 빨리 가지 않느냐고 고래고래 소리쳤습니다."], ["But he called till he was hoarse; nobody paid the least attention to him.", "하지만 목이 쉬도록 불러 댔어도, 아무도 그분에게 조금도 주의를 기울이지 않았습니다."], ["He leaned back in the coach, and thought of the beautiful face, and the feet and legs.", "그분은 마차에 등을 기대고 그 아름다운 얼굴과 발과 다리를 생각했습니다."], ["This answered better; it whiled away the time, and kept him from wondering where he was going, and how it was that he found himself in such an odd situation.", "이쪽이 더 나았지요. 그것은 시간을 때워 주었고, 자기가 어디로 가는 건지, 어쩌다 이런 괴상한 처지에 놓이게 되었는지 의아해하지 않게 해 주었습니다."], ["Not that this would have worried him much, anyway--he was a mighty free and easy, roving, devil-may-care sort of person, was my uncle, gentlemen.", "하기야 그런 것이 어차피 그분을 그리 괴롭히지도 않았겠지만요—제 삼촌은 더없이 자유롭고 느긋하며, 떠돌기 좋아하고, 될 대로 되라는 식의 사람이었으니까요, 신사 여러분."]], [["‘All of a sudden the coach stopped.", "\"별안간 마차가 멈춰 섰습니다."], ["“Hollo!”", "'이봐!'"], ["said my uncle, “what’s in the wind now?”", "제 삼촌이 말했습니다. '이번엔 또 무슨 바람이 분 거요?'"]], [["‘“Alight here,”", "\"'여기서 내리시오.'"], ["said the guard, letting down the steps.", "차장이 발판을 내리며 말했습니다."]], [["‘“Here!”", "\"'여기서!'"], ["cried my uncle.", "제 삼촌이 외쳤습니다."]], [["‘“Here,”", "\"'여기서요.'"], ["rejoined the guard.", "차장이 대꾸했습니다."]], [["‘“I’ll do nothing of the sort,”", "\"'난 그런 짓은 하지 않겠소.'"], ["said my uncle.", "제 삼촌이 말했습니다."]], [["‘“Very well, then stop where you are,”", "\"'좋소, 그럼 그 자리에 그대로 있으시오.'"], ["said the guard.", "차장이 말했습니다."]], [["‘“I will,”", "\"'그러겠소.'"], ["said my uncle.", "제 삼촌이 말했습니다."]], [["‘“Do,”", "\"'그러시구려.'"], ["said the guard.", "차장이 말했습니다."]], [["‘The passengers had regarded this colloquy with great attention, and, finding that my uncle was determined not to alight, the younger man squeezed past him, to hand the lady out.", "\"승객들은 이 대화를 매우 주의 깊게 지켜보고 있었는데, 제 삼촌이 내리지 않기로 작정했다는 것을 알자, 젊은 사내가 그를 비집고 지나가 숙녀를 부축해 내렸습니다."], ["At this moment, the ill-looking man was inspecting the hole in the crown of his three-cornered hat.", "그때 험상궂은 사내는 자기 삼각모 꼭대기에 뚫린 구멍을 살피고 있었지요."], ["As the young lady brushed past, she dropped one of her gloves into my uncle’s hand, and softly whispered, with her lips so close to his face that he felt her warm breath on his nose, the single word “Help!”", "젊은 숙녀가 스쳐 지나가면서, 제 삼촌의 손에 자기 장갑 한 짝을 떨어뜨리고는, 그 입술을 그의 얼굴에 어찌나 가까이 댔던지 그가 자기 코에 그녀의 따뜻한 숨결을 느낄 만큼 가까이서, '도와주세요!'라는 단 한마디를 가만히 속삭였습니다."], ["Gentlemen, my uncle leaped out of the coach at once, with such violence that it rocked on the springs again.", "신사 여러분, 제 삼촌은 당장 마차에서 펄쩍 뛰어내렸는데, 어찌나 힘차게 뛰어내렸던지 마차가 다시 용수철 위에서 흔들거렸습니다."]], [["‘“Oh!", "\"'오!"], ["you’ve thought better of it, have you?”", "마음을 고쳐먹으셨구려, 응?'"], ["said the guard, when he saw my uncle standing on the ground.", "제 삼촌이 땅 위에 서 있는 것을 본 차장이 말했습니다."]], [["‘My uncle looked at the guard for a few seconds, in some doubt whether it wouldn’t be better to wrench his blunderbuss from him, fire it in the face of the man with the big sword, knock the rest of the company over the head with the stock, snatch up the young lady, and go off in the smoke.", "\"제 삼촌은 몇 초 동안 차장을 바라보며, 차라리 그에게서 나팔총을 비틀어 빼앗아, 큰 검을 든 사내의 얼굴에 그것을 쏘아 버리고, 개머리판으로 나머지 일행의 머리를 후려친 다음, 젊은 숙녀를 낚아채 연기 속으로 달아나 버리는 게 낫지 않을까 잠시 망설였습니다."], ["On second thoughts, however, he abandoned this plan, as being a shade too melodramatic in the execution, and followed the two mysterious men, who, keeping the lady between them, were now entering an old house in front of which the coach had stopped.", "그러나 다시 생각해 보니, 그것은 실행에 옮기기에는 약간 지나치게 멜로드라마 같다는 생각이 들어 그 계획을 단념하고, 두 수상쩍은 사내를 뒤따랐습니다. 그들은 숙녀를 가운데 둔 채, 마차가 멈춰 선 그 앞의 어느 낡은 집으로 들어서고 있었지요."], ["They turned into the passage, and my uncle followed.", "그들이 통로로 꺾어 들자, 제 삼촌도 뒤따랐습니다."]], [["‘Of all the ruinous and desolate places my uncle had ever beheld, this was the most so.", "\"제 삼촌이 이제껏 본 그 모든 폐허가 되고 황량한 곳들 가운데, 이곳이 가장 심했습니다."], ["It looked as if it had once been a large house of entertainment; but the roof had fallen in, in many places, and the stairs were steep, rugged, and broken.", "그것은 한때 큰 유흥 시설이었던 것처럼 보였지만, 지붕은 여러 군데가 내려앉았고, 계단은 가파르고 울퉁불퉁하며 부서져 있었습니다."], ["There was a huge fireplace in the room into which they walked, and the chimney was blackened with smoke; but no warm blaze lighted it up now.", "그들이 걸어 들어간 방에는 거대한 벽난로가 있었고, 굴뚝은 그을음으로 시커멨습니다. 하지만 이제는 어떤 따뜻한 불꽃도 그것을 밝혀 주지 않았지요."], ["The white feathery dust of burned wood was still strewed over the hearth, but the stove was cold, and all was dark and gloomy.", "타다 남은 나무의 하얀 깃털 같은 재가 아직 화덕 위에 흩어져 있었지만, 난로는 차가웠고, 모든 것이 어둡고 음울했습니다."]], [["‘“Well,”", "\"'허허,'"], ["said my uncle, as he looked about him, “a mail travelling at the rate of six miles and a half an hour, and stopping for an indefinite time at such a hole as this, is rather an irregular sort of proceeding, I fancy.", "제 삼촌이 주위를 둘러보며 말했습니다. '시속 6마일 반으로 달리는 우편 마차가 이런 누추한 굴에서 기약 없이 멈춰 서다니, 이건 꽤나 규칙에 어긋나는 처사로군."], ["This shall be made known.", "이 일은 알려져야겠어."], ["I’ll write to the papers.”", "내가 신문에다 투고하겠소.'"]], [["‘My uncle said this in a pretty loud voice, and in an open, unreserved sort of manner, with the view of engaging the two strangers in conversation if he could.", "\"제 삼촌은 가능하다면 두 낯선 자를 대화에 끌어들일 속셈으로, 꽤 큰 목소리로, 거리낌 없이 탁 트인 태도로 이 말을 했습니다."], ["But, neither of them took any more notice of him than whispering to each other, and scowling at him as they did so.", "하지만 그들 중 누구도 그분에게 그 이상의 주의를 기울이지 않았고, 그저 서로 속닥거리며 그러는 동안 그분을 노려보기만 했습니다."], ["The lady was at the farther end of the room, and once she ventured to wave her hand, as if beseeching my uncle’s assistance.", "숙녀는 방의 가장 먼 끝에 있었는데, 한번은 마치 제 삼촌의 도움을 간청하듯 손을 흔들어 보이는 모험을 감행했습니다."]], [["‘At length the two strangers advanced a little, and the conversation began in earnest.", "\"마침내 두 낯선 자가 조금 다가왔고, 대화가 본격적으로 시작되었습니다."]], [["‘“You don’t know this is a private room, I suppose, fellow?”", "\"'여기가 사사로운 방이라는 걸 모르는 모양이군, 이 작자야?'"], ["said the gentleman in sky-blue.", "하늘색 옷을 입은 신사가 말했습니다."]], [["‘“No, I do not, fellow,”", "\"'모르오, 이 작자야.'"], ["rejoined my uncle.", "제 삼촌이 대꾸했습니다."], ["“Only, if this is a private room specially ordered for the occasion, I should think the public room must be a very comfortable one;”", "'다만, 이게 이번 일을 위해 특별히 마련한 사사로운 방이라면, 공용 방이라는 데는 어지간히 안락한 곳이겠다는 생각이 드는군.'"], ["with this, my uncle sat himself down in a high-backed chair, and took such an accurate measure of the gentleman, with his eyes, that Tiggin and Welps could have supplied him with printed calico for a suit, and not an inch too much or too little, from that estimate alone.", "그러고 나서 제 삼촌은 등받이가 높은 의자에 앉아, 두 눈으로 그 신사의 치수를 어찌나 정확히 재었던지, 티긴 앤드 웰프스는 그 어림짐작 하나만으로도 그에게 한 치도 많거나 적지 않게 옷 한 벌 분량의 무늬 옥양목을 댈 수 있었을 겁니다."]], [["‘“Quit this room,”", "\"'이 방에서 나가시지.'"], ["said both men together, grasping their swords.", "두 사내가 검을 움켜쥐며 동시에 말했습니다."]], [["‘“Eh?”", "\"'엥?'"], ["said my uncle, not at all appearing to comprehend their meaning.", "제 삼촌이 그들의 말뜻을 도무지 못 알아듣는 시늉을 하며 말했습니다."]], [["‘“Quit the room, or you are a dead man,”", "\"'이 방에서 나가, 안 그러면 넌 죽은 목숨이다.'"], ["said the ill-looking fellow with the large sword, drawing it at the same time and flourishing it in the air.", "큰 검을 든 험상궂은 사내가, 동시에 검을 뽑아 공중에 휘두르며 말했습니다."]], [["‘“Down with him!”", "\"'저놈을 쓰러뜨려라!'"], ["cried the gentleman in sky-blue, drawing his sword also, and falling back two or three yards.", "하늘색 옷을 입은 신사가 역시 검을 뽑아 들고 두세 야드 뒤로 물러서며 외쳤습니다."], ["“Down with him!”", "'저놈을 쓰러뜨려!'"], ["The lady gave a loud scream.", "숙녀가 크게 비명을 질렀습니다."]], [["‘Now, my uncle was always remarkable for great boldness, and great presence of mind.", "\"그런데 제 삼촌은 늘 대단한 대담함과 대단한 침착함으로 유명한 분이었습니다."], ["All the time that he had appeared so indifferent to what was going on, he had been looking slily about for some missile or weapon of defence, and at the very instant when the swords were drawn, he espied, standing in the chimney-corner, an old basket-hilted rapier in a rusty scabbard.", "그분이 무슨 일이 벌어지든 그토록 무심한 척하던 내내, 사실은 방어용으로 쓸 무슨 던질 만한 것이나 무기가 없나 슬그머니 둘러보고 있었던 것이지요. 그리고 검이 뽑힌 바로 그 순간, 그분은 굴뚝 모퉁이에 녹슨 칼집에 든 낡은 바구니 손잡이 레이피어 한 자루가 세워져 있는 것을 발견했습니다."], ["At one bound, my uncle caught it in his hand, drew it, flourished it gallantly above his head, called aloud to the lady to keep out of the way, hurled the chair at the man in sky-blue, and the scabbard at the man in plum-colour, and taking advantage of the confusion, fell upon them both, pell-mell.", "제 삼촌은 단번에 그것을 손에 움켜쥐고, 칼을 뽑아, 늠름하게 머리 위로 휘두르고는, 숙녀에게 비키라고 큰 소리로 외친 다음, 의자를 하늘색 옷의 사내에게, 칼집을 자줏빛 옷의 사내에게 내던지고, 그 혼란을 틈타 두 사내 모두에게 마구잡이로 덤벼들었습니다. * 레이피어: 가늘고 긴 양날 찌르기용 검."]], [["‘Gentlemen, there is an old story--none the worse for being true-- regarding a fine young Irish gentleman, who being asked if he could play the fiddle, replied he had no doubt he could, but he couldn’t exactly say, for certain, because he had never tried.", "\"신사 여러분, 한 멋진 아일랜드 청년에 관한 옛이야기가 하나 있습니다—사실이라고 해서 못할 것도 없는 이야기지요. 누군가 그에게 바이올린을 켤 줄 아느냐고 묻자, 그는 분명 켤 수 있을 거라고는 생각하지만, 한 번도 켜 본 적이 없어서 확실히는 말 못 하겠다고 대답했답니다."], ["This is not inapplicable to my uncle and his fencing.", "이 이야기는 제 삼촌과 그분의 검술에 들어맞지 않는 바가 아닙니다."], ["He had never had a sword in his hand before, except once when he played Richard the Third at a private theatre, upon which occasion it was arranged with Richmond that he was to be run through, from behind, without showing fight at all.", "그분은 그때까지 한 번 말고는 손에 검을 쥐어 본 적이 없었거든요. 그 한 번이란 어느 사설 극장에서 리처드 3세를 연기했을 때였는데, 그때는 그분이 전혀 싸우는 시늉도 없이 뒤에서 찔리는 것으로 리치먼드와 미리 짜 놓았던 터였지요."], ["But here he was, cutting and slashing with two experienced swordsman, thrusting, and guarding, and poking, and slicing, and acquitting himself in the most manful and dexterous manner possible, although up to that time he had never been aware that he had the least notion of the science.", "그런데 지금 그분은 두 노련한 검객을 상대로 베고 후려치고, 찌르고 막고, 쑤시고 가르며, 가능한 한 가장 사내답고 능란한 솜씨로 자기 몫을 해내고 있었습니다. 그때까지 그분은 자기에게 그 기술에 대한 소양이 조금이라도 있다는 것을 전혀 알지 못했는데도 말이지요."], ["It only shows how true the old saying is, that a man never knows what he can do till he tries, gentlemen.", "이것은 사람이란 한번 해 보기 전에는 자기가 무엇을 할 수 있는지 결코 모른다는 옛말이 얼마나 참된 것인지를 보여 줄 뿐입니다, 신사 여러분."]], [["‘The noise of the combat was terrific; each of the three combatants swearing like troopers, and their swords clashing with as much noise as if all the knives and steels in Newport market were rattling together, at the same time.", "\"싸움의 소음은 무시무시했습니다. 세 명의 싸움꾼은 저마다 기병처럼 욕설을 퍼부었고, 그들의 검은 마치 뉴포트 시장의 모든 칼과 강철이 동시에 한데 부딪쳐 덜그럭거리는 것만큼이나 큰 소리를 내며 맞부딪쳤습니다."], ["When it was at its very height, the lady (to encourage my uncle most probably) withdrew her hood entirely from her face, and disclosed a countenance of such dazzling beauty, that he would have fought against fifty men, to win one smile from it and die.", "그것이 한창 절정에 이르렀을 때, 숙녀는(아마도 제 삼촌을 격려하기 위해서였겠지요) 두건을 얼굴에서 완전히 걷어 냈고, 그토록 눈부시게 아름다운 용모를 드러냈으니, 그분은 그 얼굴에서 미소 하나를 얻을 수만 있다면 쉰 명의 사내를 상대로라도 싸우다 죽었을 겁니다."], ["He had done wonders before, but now he began to powder away like a raving mad giant.", "그분은 이미 놀라운 일을 해내고 있었지만, 이제는 미쳐 날뛰는 거인처럼 마구 휘둘러 대기 시작했습니다."]], [["‘At this very moment, the gentleman in sky-blue turning round, and seeing the young lady with her face uncovered, vented an exclamation of rage and jealousy, and, turning his weapon against her beautiful bosom, pointed a thrust at her heart, which caused my uncle to utter a cry of apprehension that made the building ring.", "\"바로 이 순간, 하늘색 옷을 입은 신사가 돌아서서 젊은 숙녀가 얼굴을 드러낸 것을 보고는, 분노와 질투의 외침을 토해 내며, 자기 무기를 그녀의 아름다운 가슴을 향해 돌려, 그녀의 심장을 겨누어 찔러 들어갔습니다. 그 바람에 제 삼촌은 건물이 울리도록 우려의 비명을 내질렀지요."], ["The lady stepped lightly aside, and snatching the young man’s sword from his hand, before he had recovered his balance, drove him to the wall, and running it through him, and the panelling, up to the very hilt, pinned him there, hard and fast.", "숙녀는 가볍게 옆으로 비켜서서, 그가 균형을 되찾기 전에 젊은 사내의 손에서 검을 낚아채, 그를 벽 쪽으로 몰아붙이고는, 칼을 그의 몸과 벽 판자를 자루 끝까지 꿰뚫도록 찔러 넣어, 그를 거기에 단단히 못 박아 버렸습니다."], ["It was a splendid example.", "그것은 훌륭한 본보기였습니다."], ["My uncle, with a loud shout of triumph, and a strength that was irresistible, made his adversary retreat in the same direction, and plunging the old rapier into the very centre of a large red flower in the pattern of his waistcoat, nailed him beside his friend; there they both stood, gentlemen, jerking their arms and legs about in agony, like the toy-shop figures that are moved by a piece of pack-thread.", "제 삼촌은 승리의 함성을 크게 내지르며, 거역할 수 없는 힘으로 자기 상대를 같은 방향으로 후퇴시켜, 그 낡은 레이피어를 그자 조끼 무늬에 있는 커다란 붉은 꽃의 한복판에 푹 찔러 넣어, 그를 그 친구 옆에 못 박아 버렸습니다. 거기서 두 사내는, 신사 여러분, 노끈 한 가닥으로 움직이는 장난감 가게의 인형들처럼, 고통 속에 팔다리를 버둥거리며 서 있었지요."], ["My uncle always said, afterwards, that this was one of the surest means he knew of, for disposing of an enemy; but it was liable to one objection on the ground of expense, inasmuch as it involved the loss of a sword for every man disabled.", "제 삼촌은 나중에 늘 말씀하시기를, 이것이 그분이 아는 적을 처치하는 가장 확실한 방법 가운데 하나라고 하셨습니다. 다만 비용 면에서 한 가지 흠이 있었으니, 무력화시키는 사람 하나당 검 한 자루를 잃는 셈이었기 때문이지요."]], [["‘“The mail, the mail!”", "\"'마차예요, 마차!'"], ["cried the lady, running up to my uncle and throwing her beautiful arms round his neck; “we may yet escape.”", "숙녀가 제 삼촌에게 달려와 그 아름다운 두 팔로 그의 목을 끌어안으며 외쳤습니다. '우리 아직 달아날 수 있어요.'"]], [["‘“May!”", "\"'있다고요!'"], ["cried my uncle; “why, my dear, there’s nobody else to kill, is there?”", "제 삼촌이 외쳤습니다. '아니, 그런데 여보, 이제 더 죽일 사람이 아무도 없잖소, 그렇지 않소?'"], ["My uncle was rather disappointed, gentlemen, for he thought a little quiet bit of love-making would be agreeable after the slaughtering, if it were only to change the subject.", "제 삼촌은 다소 실망하셨지요, 신사 여러분. 그분은 학살이 끝난 뒤에는 화제라도 좀 바꿀 겸, 조용한 사랑 놀음이 좀 있으면 좋겠다고 생각하셨거든요."]], [["‘“We have not an instant to lose here,”", "\"'여기서 한순간도 지체할 수 없어요.'"], ["said the young lady.", "젊은 숙녀가 말했습니다."], ["“He (pointing to the young gentleman in sky-blue) is the only son of the powerful Marquess of Filletoville.”", "'저 사람은(하늘색 옷을 입은 젊은 신사를 가리키며) 막강한 필레토빌 후작의 외아들이에요.'"]], [["‘“Well then, my dear, I’m afraid he’ll never come to the title,”", "\"'그렇다면, 여보, 그자는 영영 작위를 잇지 못할까 봐 걱정이구려.'"], ["said my uncle, looking coolly at the young gentleman as he stood fixed up against the wall, in the cockchafer fashion that I have described.", "제 삼촌이, 제가 앞서 묘사한 그 풍뎅이 같은 모양새로 벽에 박힌 채 서 있는 젊은 신사를 냉랭하게 바라보며 말했습니다."], ["“You have cut off the entail, my love.”", "'당신이 한정 상속을 끊어 버렸으니까, 내 사랑.'"]], [["‘“I have been torn from my home and my friends by these villains,”", "\"'저는 저 악당들에게 제 집과 친구들에게서 끌려왔어요.'"], ["said the young lady, her features glowing with indignation.", "젊은 숙녀가, 분노로 이목구비를 붉히며 말했습니다."], ["“That wretch would have married me by violence in another hour.”", "'저 비열한 자가 한 시간만 더 있으면 폭력으로 저와 결혼하려 했을 거예요.'"]], [["‘“Confound his impudence!”", "\"'저런 뻔뻔한 놈을 봤나!'"], ["said my uncle, bestowing a very contemptuous look on the dying heir of Filletoville.", "제 삼촌이 죽어 가는 필레토빌의 상속자에게 자못 경멸 어린 눈길을 던지며 말했습니다."]], [["‘“As you may guess from what you have seen,”", "\"'보신 것으로 짐작하시겠지만,'"], ["said the young lady, “the party were prepared to murder me if I appealed to any one for assistance.", "젊은 숙녀가 말했습니다. '저 일당은 제가 누구에게든 도움을 청하면 저를 죽일 작정이었어요."], ["If their accomplices find us here, we are lost.", "저들의 공범들이 우리를 여기서 발견하면, 우린 끝장이에요."], ["Two minutes hence may be too late.", "지금부터 이 분 후면 너무 늦을지도 몰라요."], ["The mail!”", "마차로!'"], ["With these words, overpowered by her feelings, and the exertion of sticking the young Marquess of Filletoville, she sank into my uncle’s arms.", "이 말과 함께, 그녀는 북받치는 감정과 젊은 필레토빌 후작을 찌르느라 들인 힘에 압도되어, 제 삼촌의 두 팔 안으로 쓰러졌습니다."], ["My uncle caught her up, and bore her to the house door.", "제 삼촌은 그녀를 받아 안아 집 문 쪽으로 데려갔습니다."], ["There stood the mail, with four long-tailed, flowing-maned, black horses, ready harnessed; but no coachman, no guard, no hostler even, at the horses’ heads.", "거기에는 마차가, 꼬리가 길고 갈기가 휘날리는 네 마리의 검은 말이 이미 마구를 갖춘 채, 서 있었습니다. 하지만 마부도 없고, 차장도 없고, 말머리 곁에는 마부 조수조차 없었습니다."]], [["‘Gentlemen, I hope I do no injustice to my uncle’s memory, when I express my opinion, that although he was a bachelor, he had held some ladies in his arms before this time; I believe, indeed, that he had rather a habit of kissing barmaids; and I know, that in one or two instances, he had been seen by credible witnesses, to hug a landlady in a very perceptible manner.", "\"신사 여러분, 제 삼촌이 비록 독신이긴 했지만 이때 이전에도 몇몇 숙녀를 품에 안아 본 적이 있다는 제 의견을 밝히더라도, 그분의 명예에 누를 끼치는 것은 아니리라 생각합니다. 실은 그분이 술집 아가씨들에게 입맞추는 버릇이 꽤 있었다고 저는 믿습니다."], ["I mention the circumstance, to show what a very uncommon sort of person this beautiful young lady must have been, to have affected my uncle in the way she did; he used to say, that as her long dark hair trailed over his arm, and her beautiful dark eyes fixed themselves upon his face when she recovered, he felt so strange and nervous that his legs trembled beneath him.", "그리고 한두 차례는 그분이 어느 여관 안주인을 아주 눈에 띄게 끌어안는 것을 믿을 만한 증인들이 보았다는 것도 압니다. 제가 이런 정황을 언급하는 까닭은, 이 아름다운 젊은 숙녀가 제 삼촌을 그토록 사로잡았으니 얼마나 보기 드문 부류의 사람이었음이 틀림없는지를 보여 드리기 위해서입니다."], ["But who can look in a sweet, soft pair of dark eyes, without feeling queer?", "그분이 늘 하시던 말씀이, 그녀의 길고 검은 머리카락이 자기 팔 위로 늘어지고, 그녀가 정신을 차렸을 때 그 아름답고 검은 두 눈이 자기 얼굴에 못 박히자, 자기는 어찌나 이상하고 떨렸던지 다리가 후들거렸다는 겁니다. 하지만 누가 그 달콤하고 부드러운 검은 두 눈을 들여다보면서 기분이 묘해지지 않을 수 있겠습니까?"], ["I can’t, gentlemen.", "저는 못 합니다, 신사 여러분."], ["I am afraid to look at some eyes I know, and that’s the truth of it.", "저는 제가 아는 어떤 눈들은 차마 들여다보기가 두렵습니다. 그게 사실입니다."]], [["‘“You will never leave me,”", "\"'당신은 절대 저를 떠나지 않으실 거죠.'"], ["murmured the young lady.", "젊은 숙녀가 속삭였습니다."]], [["‘“Never,”", "\"'절대로.'"], ["said my uncle.", "제 삼촌이 말했습니다."], ["And he meant it too.", "그리고 그분은 진심으로 그럴 작정이었지요."]], [["‘“My dear preserver!”", "\"'제 소중한 구원자여!'"], ["exclaimed the young lady.", "젊은 숙녀가 외쳤습니다."], ["“My dear, kind, brave preserver!”", "'제 소중하고, 친절하고, 용감한 구원자여!'"]], [["‘“Don’t,”", "\"'그러지 마시오.'"], ["said my uncle, interrupting her.", "제 삼촌이 그녀의 말을 가로막으며 말했습니다."]], [["‘“‘Why?”", "\"'왜요?'"], ["inquired the young lady.", "젊은 숙녀가 물었습니다."]], [["‘“Because your mouth looks so beautiful when you speak,”", "\"'당신이 말할 때면 그 입이 너무도 아름다워 보여서 말이오.'"], ["rejoined my uncle, “that I’m afraid I shall be rude enough to kiss it.”", "제 삼촌이 대꾸했습니다. '그만 내가 무례하게도 거기에 입을 맞춰 버릴까 봐 두렵소.'"]], [["‘The young lady put up her hand as if to caution my uncle not to do so, and said--No, she didn’t say anything--she smiled.", "\"젊은 숙녀는 제 삼촌에게 그러지 말라고 주의를 주기라도 하듯 손을 들어 올리고는—아니, 그녀는 아무 말도 하지 않았습니다—미소 지었습니다."], ["When you are looking at a pair of the most delicious lips in the world, and see them gently break into a roguish smile--if you are very near them, and nobody else by--you cannot better testify your admiration of their beautiful form and colour than by kissing them at once.", "세상에서 가장 감미로운 한 쌍의 입술을 바라보고 있을 때, 그 입술이 부드럽게 장난기 어린 미소로 살며시 벌어지는 것을 본다면—그것도 아주 가까이 있고 곁에 아무도 없다면—그 아름다운 모양과 빛깔에 대한 흠모를 곧장 입맞춤하는 것보다 더 잘 증명할 길은 없는 법이지요."], ["My uncle did so, and I honour him for it.", "제 삼촌은 그렇게 했고, 저는 그 일로 그분을 떳떳이 여깁니다."]], [["‘“Hark!”", "\"'쉿!'"], ["cried the young lady, starting.", "젊은 숙녀가 흠칫 놀라며 외쳤습니다."], ["“The noise of wheels, and horses!”", "'바퀴 소리예요, 그리고 말들이!'"]], [["‘“So it is,”", "\"'정말 그렇군.'"], ["said my uncle, listening.", "제 삼촌이 귀를 기울이며 말했습니다."], ["He had a good ear for wheels, and the trampling of hoofs; but there appeared to be so many horses and carriages rattling towards them, from a distance, that it was impossible to form a guess at their number.", "그분은 바퀴 소리와 말발굽 소리에 밝은 귀를 지니고 있었지요. 하지만 멀리서 어찌나 많은 말과 마차가 그들을 향해 덜컹거리며 다가오는 것 같던지, 그 수를 짐작하기란 불가능했습니다."], ["The sound was like that of fifty brakes, with six blood cattle in each.", "그 소리는 마치 짐마차 쉰 대가, 각각에 혈통 좋은 소 여섯 마리씩을 매단 것 같았지요."]], [["‘“We are pursued!”", "\"'우린 쫓기고 있어요!'"], ["cried the young lady, clasping her hands.", "젊은 숙녀가 두 손을 맞잡으며 외쳤습니다."], ["“We are pursued.", "'우린 쫓기고 있어요."], ["I have no hope but in you!”", "제겐 당신밖에는 희망이 없어요!'"]], [["‘There was such an expression of terror in her beautiful face, that my uncle made up his mind at once.", "\"그녀의 아름다운 얼굴에는 그토록 공포에 질린 표정이 어려 있어서, 제 삼촌은 단번에 마음을 정했습니다."], ["He lifted her into the coach, told her not to be frightened, pressed his lips to hers once more, and then advising her to draw up the window to keep the cold air out, mounted to the box.", "그분은 그녀를 마차 안으로 들어 올리고, 겁먹지 말라고 말한 뒤, 다시 한번 그녀의 입술에 자기 입술을 댄 다음, 찬 공기가 들어오지 않도록 창문을 올리라고 일러 주고는, 마부석으로 올라갔습니다."]], [["‘“Stay, love,”", "\"'잠깐만요, 여보.'"], ["cried the young lady.", "젊은 숙녀가 외쳤습니다."]], [["‘“What’s the matter?”", "\"'무슨 일이오?'"], ["said my uncle, from the coach-box.", "제 삼촌이 마부석에서 말했습니다."]], [["‘“I want to speak to you,”", "\"'당신에게 할 말이 있어요.'"], ["said the young lady; “only a word.", "젊은 숙녀가 말했습니다. '딱 한마디만요."], ["Only one word, dearest.”", "단 한마디만요, 가장 사랑하는 분.'"]], [["‘“Must I get down?”", "\"'내가 내려가야 하오?'"], ["inquired my uncle.", "제 삼촌이 물었습니다."], ["The lady made no answer, but she smiled again.", "숙녀는 아무 대답도 하지 않았지만, 다시 미소 지었습니다."], ["Such a smile, gentlemen!", "그 미소라니요, 신사 여러분!"], ["It beat the other one, all to nothing.", "앞서의 그것을 완전히 무색하게 만들 정도였지요."], ["My uncle descended from his perch in a twinkling.", "제 삼촌은 눈 깜짝할 사이에 자기 자리에서 내려왔습니다."]], [["‘“What is it, my dear?”", "\"'무슨 일이오, 여보?'"], ["said my uncle, looking in at the coach window.", "제 삼촌이 마차 창 안을 들여다보며 말했습니다."], ["The lady happened to bend forward at the same time, and my uncle thought she looked more beautiful than she had done yet.", "숙녀가 마침 그와 동시에 몸을 앞으로 숙였는데, 제 삼촌은 그녀가 지금껏보다 한층 더 아름다워 보인다고 생각했습니다."], ["He was very close to her just then, gentlemen, so he really ought to know.", "바로 그때 그분은 그녀에게 아주 가까이 있었지요, 신사 여러분. 그러니 그분이야말로 정말로 잘 알았을 겁니다."]], [["‘“What is it, my dear?”", "\"'무슨 일이오, 여보?'"], ["said my uncle.", "제 삼촌이 말했습니다."]], [["‘“Will you never love any one but me--never marry any one beside?”", "\"'당신은 저 말고 다른 누구도 절대 사랑하지 않으시겠죠—저 말고 다른 누구와도 절대 결혼하지 않으시겠죠?'"], ["said the young lady.", "젊은 숙녀가 말했습니다."]], [["‘My uncle swore a great oath that he never would marry anybody else, and the young lady drew in her head, and pulled up the window.", "\"제 삼촌은 자기는 절대 다른 누구와도 결혼하지 않겠다고 큼지막한 맹세를 했고, 젊은 숙녀는 머리를 안으로 들이고 창문을 올렸습니다."], ["He jumped upon the box, squared his elbows, adjusted the ribands, seized the whip which lay on the roof, gave one flick to the off leader, and away went the four long-tailed, flowing-maned black horses, at fifteen good English miles an hour, with the old mail-coach behind them.", "그분은 마부석으로 펄쩍 뛰어올라, 양 팔꿈치를 가지런히 펴고, 고삐를 매만지고, 지붕에 놓여 있던 채찍을 움켜쥐고는, 바깥쪽 선두마에게 한 번 휙 채찍질을 했습니다. 그러자 꼬리가 길고 갈기가 휘날리는 네 마리 검은 말이, 그 뒤로 낡은 우편 마차를 매단 채, 시속 15마일이라는 어엿한 영국식 속도로 내달렸습니다."], ["Whew!", "휘익!"], ["How they tore along!", "그것들이 어떻게 질주했던지요!"]], [["‘The noise behind grew louder.", "\"뒤쪽의 소음이 점점 커졌습니다."], ["The faster the old mail went, the faster came the pursuers--men, horses, dogs, were leagued in the pursuit.", "낡은 우편 마차가 빨리 갈수록, 추격자들도 그만큼 빨리 따라왔습니다—사람과 말과 개가 추격에 한 패가 되었지요."], ["The noise was frightful, but, above all, rose the voice of the young lady, urging my uncle on, and shrieking, “Faster!", "그 소음은 무시무시했지만, 무엇보다 젊은 숙녀의 목소리가 그 위로 높이 솟아올라, 제 삼촌을 다그치며 '더 빨리! 더 빨리!'"], ["Faster!”", "하고 비명을 질렀습니다."]], [["‘They whirled past the dark trees, as feathers would be swept before a hurricane.", "\"그들은 마치 깃털이 허리케인 앞에 휩쓸리듯 어두운 나무들을 휙휙 지나쳤습니다. 집과 대문과 교회와 건초더미와 온갖 종류의 사물들을, 그들은 갑자기 풀려난 으르렁대는 물살 같은 속도와 소음으로 쏜살같이 지나쳤습니다."], ["Houses, gates, churches, haystacks, objects of every kind they shot by, with a velocity and noise like roaring waters suddenly let loose.", "하지만 그래도 추격의 소음은 점점 커져만 갔고, 그래도 제 삼촌은 젊은 숙녀가 미친 듯이 '더 빨리! 더 빨리!'"], ["But still the noise of pursuit grew louder, and still my uncle could hear the young lady wildly screaming, “Faster! Faster!”", "하고 비명 지르는 것을 들을 수 있었습니다."]], [["‘My uncle plied whip and rein, and the horses flew onward till they were white with foam; and yet the noise behind increased; and yet the young lady cried, “Faster!", "\"제 삼촌은 채찍과 고삐를 부지런히 놀렸고, 말들은 거품으로 하얘질 때까지 앞으로 날아갔습니다. 그래도 뒤쪽의 소음은 더해 갔고, 그래도 젊은 숙녀는 '더 빨리!"], ["Faster!”", "더 빨리!'"], ["My uncle gave a loud stamp on the boot in the energy of the moment, and--found that it was gray morning, and he was sitting in the wheelwright’s yard, on the box of an old Edinburgh mail, shivering with the cold and wet and stamping his feet to warm them!", "하고 외쳤습니다. 제 삼촌은 그 순간의 격정에 차서 마차 짐칸을 쾅 하고 세게 굴렀는데—그러자 어느덧 잿빛 아침이었고, 그분은 수레바퀴 목수의 마당에서, 낡은 에든버러 우편 마차의 마부석에 앉아, 추위와 습기에 덜덜 떨며 발을 따뜻하게 하려고 동동 구르고 있는 것이었습니다!"], ["He got down, and looked eagerly inside for the beautiful young lady.", "그분은 내려가서, 그 아름다운 젊은 숙녀를 찾으려고 안쪽을 열심히 들여다보았습니다."], ["Alas!", "아아!"], ["There was neither door nor seat to the coach.", "마차에는 문도 좌석도 없었습니다."], ["It was a mere shell.", "그것은 그저 텅 빈 껍데기였지요."]], [["‘Of course, my uncle knew very well that there was some mystery in the matter, and that everything had passed exactly as he used to relate it.", "\"물론 제 삼촌은 이 일에 무언가 신비로운 것이 있다는 것을, 그리고 모든 일이 그분이 늘 이야기하시던 그대로 정확히 일어났다는 것을 아주 잘 알고 계셨습니다."], ["He remained staunch to the great oath he had sworn to the beautiful young lady, refusing several eligible landladies on her account, and dying a bachelor at last.", "그분은 그 아름다운 젊은 숙녀에게 맹세한 그 큼지막한 맹세에 변함없이 충실하여, 그녀를 생각해 마땅한 여관 안주인 여럿을 마다하고, 끝내 독신으로 돌아가셨습니다."], ["He always said what a curious thing it was that he should have found out, by such a mere accident as his clambering over the palings, that the ghosts of mail-coaches and horses, guards, coachmen, and passengers, were in the habit of making journeys regularly every night.", "그분은 늘 말씀하시기를, 그저 울타리를 기어 넘는 것 같은 순전한 우연으로, 우편 마차와 말과 차장과 마부와 승객의 유령들이 밤마다 규칙적으로 여행을 다니는 버릇이 있다는 것을 알아냈으니 참으로 묘한 일이라고 하셨습니다."], ["He used to add, that he believed he was the only living person who had ever been taken as a passenger on one of these excursions.", "그분은 덧붙여 말씀하시기를, 자기야말로 이런 야간 행차들 가운데 한 번에 승객으로 태워진 유일한 산 사람이리라 믿는다고 하셨지요."], ["And I think he was right, gentlemen--at least I never heard of any other.’", "그리고 저는 그분이 옳았다고 생각합니다, 신사 여러분—적어도 저는 그런 사람이 또 있다는 이야기는 들어 본 적이 없으니까요.\""]], [["‘I wonder what these ghosts of mail-coaches carry in their bags,’ said the landlord, who had listened to the whole story with profound attention.", "\"그 우편 마차 유령들은 행낭 속에 무엇을 싣고 다니는지 궁금하군요.\" 그 이야기 전체를 깊은 주의를 기울여 듣고 있던 여관 주인이 말했다. \"물론 사신(死信)들이지요.\" 외판원이 말했다."]], [["‘The dead letters, of course,’ said the bagman.", "\"오, 아! 그렇고말고.\" 여관 주인이 대꾸했다. \"그건 미처 생각 못 했군요.\""]], [["‘Oh, ah! To be sure,’ rejoined the landlord. ‘I never thought of that.’", "* 사신(死信): 수취인 불명 등으로 배달되지 못한 우편물."]], [["CHAPTER L. HOW MR. PICKWICK SPED UPON HIS MISSION, AND HOW HE WAS REINFORCED IN THE OUTSET BY A MOST UNEXPECTED AUXILIARY\nThe horses were put to, punctually at a quarter before nine next morning, and Mr. Pickwick and Sam Weller having each taken his seat, the one inside and the other out, the postillion was duly directed to repair in the first instance to Mr. Bob Sawyer’s house, for the purpose of taking up Mr. Benjamin Allen.", "이튿날 아침, 정확히 여덟 시 사십오 분에 말이 매여졌고, 픽윅 씨와 샘 웰러가 각각 자리를 잡되 한 사람은 안에, 다른 한 사람은 바깥에 앉자, 마부에게는 우선 보브 소여 씨의 집으로 가서 벤저민 앨런 씨를 태우라는 지시가 정식으로 내려졌다."]], [["It was with feelings of no small astonishment, when the carriage drew up before the door with the red lamp, and the very legible inscription of ‘Sawyer, late Nockemorf,’ that Mr.", "마차가 붉은 등이 달린 문 앞에, 그리고 ‘소여, 전 노케모프’라는 매우 또렷한 글귀가 적힌 그 문 앞에 멈춰 섰을 때, 픽윅 씨가 마차 창밖으로 머리를 쑥 내밀고 회색 제복을 입은 소년이 분주하게 덧문을 닫는 모습을 본 것은 적잖은 놀라움이었다."], ["Pickwick saw, on popping his head out of the coach window, the boy in the gray livery very busily employed in putting up the shutters--the which, being an unusual and an unbusinesslike proceeding at that hour of the morning, at once suggested to his mind two inferences: the one, that some good friend and patient of Mr.", "아침 이 시각에 그런 일은 흔치 않고 또 영업답지 않은 처사인지라, 그것은 곧 그의 마음속에 두 가지 추론을 불러일으켰다."], ["Bob Sawyer’s was dead; the other, that Mr. Bob Sawyer himself was bankrupt.", "하나는 보브 소여 씨의 어떤 좋은 친구이자 환자가 죽었으리라는 것이고, 다른 하나는 보브 소여 씨 자신이 파산했으리라는 것이었다."]], [["‘What is the matter?’ said Mr.", "“무슨 일이냐?”"], ["Pickwick to the boy.", "픽윅 씨가 소년에게 물었다."]], [["‘Nothing’s the matter, Sir,’ replied the boy, expanding his mouth to the whole breadth of his countenance.", "“아무 일도 없어요, 선생님.” 소년이 얼굴 전체 너비만큼 입을 벌리며 대답했다."]], [["‘All right, all right!’ cried Bob Sawyer, suddenly appearing at the door, with a small leathern knapsack, limp and dirty, in one hand, and a rough coat and shawl thrown over the other arm.", "“좋아, 좋아!” 보브 소여가 한 손에는 축 늘어지고 더러운 작은 가죽 배낭을, 다른 팔에는 거친 외투와 숄을 걸친 채 갑자기 문간에 나타나며 외쳤다."], ["‘I’m going, old fellow.’", "“나도 가네, 친구.”"]], [["‘You!’ exclaimed Mr.", "“자네가!”"], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 소리쳤다."]], [["‘Yes,’ replied Bob Sawyer, ‘and a regular expedition we’ll make of it.", "“그렇다네.” 보브 소여가 대답했다."], ["Here, Sam! Look out!’ Thus briefly bespeaking Mr.", "“아주 제대로 된 원정으로 만들어 보세나."], ["Weller’s attention, Mr.", "여기, 샘! 받게!”"], ["Bob Sawyer jerked the leathern knapsack into the dickey, where it was immediately stowed away, under the seat, by Sam, who regarded the proceeding with great admiration. This done, Mr.", "이렇게 짧게 웰러 씨의 주의를 청한 보브 소여 씨는 가죽 배낭을 마부석 뒤 좌석으로 휙 던졌고, 샘은 그 일을 크게 감탄하며 지켜보다가 곧장 좌석 밑에 챙겨 넣었다."], ["Bob Sawyer, with the assistance of the boy, forcibly worked himself into the rough coat, which was a few sizes too small for him, and then advancing to the coach window, thrust in his head, and laughed boisterously.", "이 일이 끝나자 보브 소여 씨는 소년의 도움을 받아 몇 치수 작은 거친 외투 속으로 힘껏 몸을 밀어 넣고는, 마차 창으로 다가가 머리를 들이밀며 요란하게 웃었다."]], [["‘What a start it is, isn’t it?’ cried Bob, wiping the tears out of his eyes, with one of the cuffs of the rough coat.", "“이것 참 깜짝 출발 아닌가, 안 그래?” 보브가 거친 외투 소맷부리 한쪽으로 눈물을 닦으며 외쳤다."]], [["‘My dear Sir,’ said Mr.", "“여보게,”"], ["Pickwick, with some embarrassment, ‘I had no idea of your accompanying us.’", "픽윅 씨가 다소 당황하여 말했다. “자네가 우리와 동행하리라곤 생각도 못 했네.”"]], [["‘No, that’s just the very thing,’ replied Bob, seizing Mr.", "“그렇지, 바로 그게 핵심이라네.”"], ["Pickwick by the lappel of his coat.", "보브가 픽윅 씨의 외투 깃을 움켜쥐며 대꾸했다."], ["‘That’s the joke.’", "“그게 농담이지.”"]], [["‘Oh, that’s the joke, is it?’ said Mr.", "“오, 그게 농담이란 말이지?”"], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Of course,’ replied Bob.", "“물론이지.” 보브가 대답했다."], ["‘It’s the whole point of the thing, you know-- that, and leaving the business to take care of itself, as it seems to have made up its mind not to take care of me.’ With this explanation of the phenomenon of the shutters, Mr.", "“그게 전부 핵심이라네—그것, 그리고 영업을 제 알아서 돌아가게 내버려 두는 것 말일세. 영업이 나를 돌볼 마음이 없는 모양이니 말이야.”"], ["Bob Sawyer pointed to the shop, and relapsed into an ecstasy of mirth.", "덧문 현상에 대한 이런 설명과 함께, 보브 소여 씨는 가게를 가리키더니 다시 환희에 찬 웃음으로 빠져들었다."]], [["‘Bless me, you are surely not mad enough to think of leaving your patients without anybody to attend them!’ remonstrated Mr.", "“이런, 자네 설마 아무도 돌볼 사람 없이 환자들을 버려두려는 미친 생각을 하는 건 아니겠지!”"], ["Pickwick in a very serious tone.", "픽윅 씨가 매우 진지한 어조로 항의했다."]], [["‘Why not?’ asked Bob, in reply.", "“왜 안 되나?” 보브가 되물었다."], ["‘I shall save by it, you know.", "“그렇게 하면 절약이 된다네, 알다시피. 그자들 누구도 돈을 안 내거든."], ["None of them ever pay.", "게다가,” 보브가 은밀한 속삭임으로 목소리를 낮추며 말했다."], ["Besides,’ said Bob, lowering his voice to a confidential whisper, ‘they will be all the better for it; for, being nearly out of drugs, and not able to increase my account just now, I should have been obliged to give them calomel all round, and it would have been certain to have disagreed with some of them.", "“그렇게 하는 편이 그들에게도 훨씬 나아. 약이 거의 다 떨어졌고 지금 당장은 외상을 늘릴 수도 없으니, 결국 모두에게 감홍을 처방해야 했을 텐데, 그러면 그중 몇몇은 분명 탈이 났을 테니까."], ["So it’s all for the best.’", "그러니 모든 게 잘된 일이지.”"]], [["There was a philosophy and a strength of reasoning about this reply, which Mr. Pickwick was not prepared for.", "이 대답에는 픽윅 씨가 미처 예상치 못한 철학과 추론의 힘이 깃들어 있었다."], ["He paused a few moments, and added, less firmly than before--", "그는 잠시 말을 멈추었다가, 아까보다 덜 단호하게 덧붙였다—"]], [["‘But this chaise, my young friend, will only hold two; and I am pledged to Mr.", "“하지만 이 마차는, 여보게 젊은 친구, 두 사람밖에 못 태우네."], ["Allen.’", "게다가 나는 앨런 씨에게 약속을 했네.”"]], [["‘Don’t think of me for a minute,’ replied Bob.", "“저는 조금도 신경 쓰지 마십시오.” 보브가 대답했다."], ["‘I’ve arranged it all; Sam and I will share the dickey between us.", "“다 마련해 두었습니다. 샘과 제가 마부석을 나눠 앉으면 됩니다."], ["Look here.", "보십시오."], ["This little bill is to be wafered on the shop door: “Sawyer, late Nockemorf. Inquire of Mrs.", "이 작은 안내문은 가게 문에 붙일 겁니다."], ["Cripps over the way.” Mrs.", "‘소여, 전 노케모프."], ["Cripps is my boy’s mother. “Mr.", "건너편 크립스 부인에게 문의하시오.’ 크립스 부인은 제 소년의 어머니입니다."], ["Sawyer’s very sorry,” says Mrs.", "‘소여 씨가 몹시 죄송해하십니다.’ 크립스 부인이 말하겠지요."], ["Cripps, “couldn’t help it--fetched away early this morning to a consultation of the very first surgeons in the country--couldn’t do without him--would have him at any price-- tremendous operation.”", "‘어쩔 수 없는 일이었답니다—오늘 아침 일찍이 이 나라 최고 외과 의사들의 진료 회의에 불려 가셨답니다—그분 없이는 안 된다고—어떤 값을 치르더라도 모셔 가겠다고—엄청난 수술이랍니다.’ 사실은,”"], ["The fact is,’ said Bob, in conclusion, ‘it’ll do me more good than otherwise, I expect.", "보브가 결론지으며 말했다. “이게 오히려 제게 득이 될 거라고 봅니다."], ["If it gets into one of the local papers, it will be the making of me.", "지방 신문 어딘가에 실리기라도 하면, 그게 제 출세를 만들어 줄 테죠."], ["Here’s Ben; now then, jump in!’", "자, 벤이 왔습니다. 어서, 타게!”"]], [["With these hurried words, Mr. Bob Sawyer pushed the postboy on one side, jerked his friend into the vehicle, slammed the door, put up the steps, wafered the bill on the street door, locked it, put the key in his pocket, jumped into the dickey, gave the word for starting, and did the whole with such extraordinary precipitation, that before Mr. Pickwick had well begun to consider whether Mr. Bob Sawyer ought to go or not, they were rolling away, with Mr. Bob Sawyer thoroughly established as part and parcel of the equipage.", "이렇게 서두른 말과 함께 보브 소여 씨는 마부 소년을 한쪽으로 밀치고, 친구를 마차 안으로 홱 밀어 넣고, 문을 쾅 닫고, 발판을 올리고, 거리 쪽 문에 안내문을 붙이고, 문을 잠그고, 열쇠를 주머니에 넣고, 마부석으로 뛰어올라 출발 신호를 내렸으니, 그 모든 일을 어찌나 비상한 신속함으로 해치웠던지, 픽윅 씨가 보브 소여 씨가 가야 할지 말아야 할지를 제대로 따져 보기도 전에, 일행은 보브 소여 씨를 마차의 떼려야 뗄 수 없는 일부로 완전히 자리잡힌 채로 굴러가고 있었다."]], [["So long as their progress was confined to the streets of Bristol, the facetious Bob kept his professional green spectacles on, and conducted himself with becoming steadiness and gravity of demeanour; merely giving utterance to divers verbal witticisms for the exclusive behoof and entertainment of Mr. Samuel Weller.", "브리스틀 거리를 벗어나지 않는 동안에는, 익살꾼 보브도 직업용 녹색 안경을 쓴 채 점잖고 진중한 태도로 처신했고, 그저 새뮤얼 웰러 씨만을 위한 갖가지 말장난을 늘어놓을 뿐이었다."], ["But when they emerged on the open road, he threw off his green spectacles and his gravity together, and performed a great variety of practical jokes, which were calculated to attract the attention of the passersby, and to render the carriage and those it contained objects of more than ordinary curiosity; the least conspicuous among these feats being a most vociferous imitation of a key-bugle, and the ostentatious display of a crimson silk pocket- handkerchief attached to a walking-stick, which was occasionally waved in the air with various gestures indicative of supremacy and defiance.", "그러나 탁 트인 길로 나서자, 그는 녹색 안경과 진중함을 한꺼번에 벗어 던지고는, 행인들의 이목을 끌고 마차와 그 안의 사람들을 보통 이상의 호기심거리로 만들기에 딱 알맞은 갖가지 짓궂은 장난을 펼쳤다. 그 묘기들 가운데 가장 덜 눈에 띄는 것이라야, 매우 큰 소리로 키뷰글을 흉내 내는 것과, 지팡이에 매단 진홍빛 비단 손수건을 과시하듯 내보이는 것으로, 그 손수건은 이따금 우월함과 도전을 나타내는 갖가지 몸짓과 함께 공중에 휘둘러졌다."]], [["‘I wonder,’ said Mr.", "“궁금하군.”"], ["Pickwick, stopping in the midst of a most sedate conversation with Ben Allen, bearing reference to the numerous good qualities of Mr.", "윈클 씨와 그 누이의 수많은 미덕에 관한 매우 차분한 이야기를 벤 앨런과 나누던 중에 말을 멈추며 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Winkle and his sister--‘I wonder what all the people we pass, can see in us to make them stare so.’", "“우리가 지나치는 사람들이 우리에게서 대체 무얼 보길래 저렇게들 빤히 쳐다보는지 궁금하군.”"]], [["‘It’s a neat turn-out,’ replied Ben Allen, with something of pride in his tone.", "“멋진 차림새 아닙니까.” 벤 앨런이 어딘지 자랑스러운 어조로 대답했다."], ["‘They’re not used to see this sort of thing, every day, I dare say.’", "“저들은 이런 걸 매일 보는 데 익숙지 않을 테죠, 아마.”"]], [["‘Possibly,’ replied Mr.", "“그럴 수도 있지.”"], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 대답했다."], ["‘It may be so.", "“그럴지도 모르지."], ["Perhaps it is.’", "아마 그렇겠지.”"]], [["Mr. Pickwick might very probably have reasoned himself into the belief that it really was, had he not, just then happening to look out of the coach window, observed that the looks of the passengers betokened anything but respectful astonishment, and that various telegraphic communications appeared to be passing between them and some persons outside the vehicle, whereupon it occurred to him that these demonstrations might be, in some remote degree, referable to the humorous deportment of Mr.", "픽윅 씨는 정말로 그러하다는 믿음에 스스로를 설득시켰을지도 모른다."], ["Robert Sawyer.", "다만 바로 그때 마차 창밖을 내다보다가, 행인들의 표정이 공손한 놀라움과는 거리가 멀다는 것을, 그리고 그들과 마차 바깥의 어떤 사람들 사이에 갖가지 신호 같은 교신이 오가는 듯하다는 것을 알아채지 않았더라면 말이다. 그제야 그는 이 소동이 어느 정도 멀게나마 로버트 소여 씨의 익살스러운 처신에서 비롯된 것일지도 모른다는 데에 생각이 미쳤다."]], [["‘I hope,’ said Mr.", "“부디 우리의 들뜬 친구가 저 뒤 마부석에서 무슨 어처구니없는 짓을 벌이고 있지 않기를 바라네.”"], ["Pickwick, ‘that our volatile friend is committing no absurdities in that dickey behind.’", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Oh dear, no,’ replied Ben Allen.", "“오, 천만에요, 절대로요.”"], ["‘Except when he’s elevated, Bob’s the quietest creature breathing.’", "벤 앨런이 대답했다. “취했을 때만 빼면, 보브는 세상에서 가장 조용한 녀석입니다.”"]], [["Here a prolonged imitation of a key-bugle broke upon the ear, succeeded by cheers and screams, all of which evidently proceeded from the throat and lungs of the quietest creature breathing, or in plainer designation, of Mr. Bob Sawyer himself.", "이때 길게 끄는 키뷰글 흉내 소리가 귀를 때렸고, 그 뒤를 환호와 비명이 이었으니, 그 모든 것은 분명 세상에서 가장 조용한 녀석, 더 쉽게 말하자면 보브 소여 씨 본인의 목구멍과 폐에서 터져 나온 것이었다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick and Mr. Ben Allen looked expressively at each other, and the former gentleman taking off his hat, and leaning out of the coach window until nearly the whole of his waistcoat was outside it, was at length enabled to catch a glimpse of his facetious friend.", "픽윅 씨와 벤 앨런 씨는 의미심장하게 서로를 마주 보았고, 픽윅 씨는 모자를 벗고 조끼가 거의 다 밖으로 나올 만큼 마차 창밖으로 몸을 내밀어, 마침내 그 익살스러운 친구를 한눈에 볼 수 있었다."]], [["Mr. Bob Sawyer was seated, not in the dickey, but on the roof of the chaise, with his legs as far asunder as they would conveniently go, wearing Mr.", "보브 소여 씨는 마부석이 아니라 마차 지붕 위에 앉아 있었으니, 두 다리를 편하게 벌릴 수 있는 데까지 한껏 벌리고, 새뮤얼 웰러 씨의 모자를 머리 한쪽으로 비스듬히 쓰고, 한 손에는 어마어마하게 큰 샌드위치를, 다른 손에는 제법 큼직한 휴대용 술병을 받쳐 든 채, 둘 다에 강렬한 흥취로 매달려 있었다."], ["Samuel Weller’s hat on one side of his head, and bearing, in one hand, a most enormous sandwich, while, in the other, he supported a goodly-sized case-bottle, to both of which he applied himself with intense relish, varying the monotony of the occupation by an occasional howl, or the interchange of some lively badinage with any passing stranger.", "그 단조로움은 이따금 내지르는 고함이나, 지나가는 낯선 이와 주고받는 활기찬 농담으로 변주되었다."], ["The crimson flag was carefully tied in an erect position to the rail of the dickey; and Mr. Samuel Weller, decorated with Bob Sawyer’s hat, was seated in the centre thereof, discussing a twin sandwich, with an animated countenance, the expression of which betokened his entire and perfect approval of the whole arrangement.", "진홍빛 깃발은 마부석 난간에 똑바로 선 채 정성스레 매여 있었고, 보브 소여의 모자로 치장한 새뮤얼 웰러 씨는 그 한가운데 앉아 쌍둥이 같은 샌드위치를 먹어 치우며, 이 모든 조처를 온전히, 완벽하게 승인한다는 뜻을 드러내는 생기 넘치는 표정을 짓고 있었다."]], [["This was enough to irritate a gentleman with Mr. Pickwick’s sense of propriety, but it was not the whole extent of the aggravation, for a stage-coach full, inside and out, was meeting them at the moment, and the astonishment of the passengers was very palpably evinced.", "이것만으로도 픽윅 씨처럼 예의범절을 중시하는 신사를 화나게 하기에 충분했지만, 그것이 화의 전부는 아니었다."], ["The congratulations of an Irish family, too, who were keeping up with the chaise, and begging all the time, were of rather a boisterous description, especially those of its male head, who appeared to consider the display as part and parcel of some political or other procession of triumph.", "마침 그 순간 안팎으로 손님이 가득 찬 역마차 한 대가 그들과 마주쳤고, 그 승객들의 놀라움은 매우 또렷이 드러났던 것이다. 게다가 마차를 따라오며 내내 구걸하던 아일랜드인 가족의 환호도 자못 떠들썩한 것이었으니, 특히 그 집안의 남자 가장은 이 광경을 무슨 정치적인, 혹은 그 밖의 개선 행렬의 일부쯤으로 여기는 듯했다."]], [["‘Mr. Sawyer!’ cried Mr.", "“소여 씨!”"], ["Pickwick, in a state of great excitement, ‘Mr.", "픽윅 씨가 몹시 흥분한 상태로 외쳤다."], ["Sawyer, Sir!’", "“소여 씨, 이보게!”"]], [["‘Hollo!’ responded that gentleman, looking over the side of the chaise with all the coolness in life.", "“여보세요!” 그 신사는 세상 더없이 태연하게 마차 옆으로 몸을 내밀며 대꾸했다."]], [["‘Are you mad, sir?’ demanded Mr.", "“자네 미쳤나?”"], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 따져 물었다."]], [["‘Not a bit of it,’ replied Bob; ‘only cheerful.’", "“조금도 아니올시다.” 보브가 대답했다. “그저 유쾌할 뿐이죠.”"]], [["‘Cheerful, sir!’ ejaculated Mr.", "“유쾌하다고!” 픽윅 씨가 소리쳤다."], ["Pickwick. ‘Take down that scandalous red handkerchief, I beg.", "“그 망측한 붉은 손수건을 내리게, 제발."], ["I insist, Sir.", "명령일세."], ["Sam, take it down.’", "샘, 그걸 내리게.”"]], [["Before Sam could interpose, Mr. Bob Sawyer gracefully struck his colours, and having put them in his pocket, nodded in a courteous manner to Mr. Pickwick, wiped the mouth of the case-bottle, and applied it to his own, thereby informing him, without any unnecessary waste of words, that he devoted that draught to wishing him all manner of happiness and prosperity. Having done this, Bob replaced the cork with great care, and looking benignantly down on Mr. Pickwick, took a large bite out of the sandwich, and smiled.", "샘이 끼어들 새도 없이, 보브 소여 씨는 우아하게 그의 깃발을 내려 주머니에 넣고는, 픽윅 씨에게 정중하게 고개를 끄덕이고, 술병 주둥이를 닦아 제 입에 갖다 댐으로써, 군말 없이, 그 한 모금을 픽윅 씨의 온갖 행복과 번영을 비는 데 바친다는 뜻을 전했다. 이렇게 하고 나서 보브는 마개를 매우 조심스럽게 도로 막고, 픽윅 씨를 인자하게 내려다보며 샌드위치를 큼직하게 한 입 베어 물고 미소 지었다."]], [["‘Come,’ said Mr.", "“자,”"], ["Pickwick, whose momentary anger was not quite proof against Bob’s immovable self-possession, ‘pray let us have no more of this absurdity.’", "순간의 노여움이 보브의 흔들림 없는 침착함을 당해 내지 못한 픽윅 씨가 말했다. “부디 이런 어처구니없는 짓은 더 이상 하지 말게.”"]], [["‘No, no,’ replied Bob, once more exchanging hats with Mr.", "“네, 네.” 보브가 다시 한 번 웰러 씨와 모자를 바꿔 쓰며 대답했다."], ["Weller; ‘I didn’t mean to do it, only I got so enlivened with the ride that I couldn’t help it.’", "“그러려던 건 아니었습니다. 다만 마차 타는 게 어찌나 신이 나던지 어쩔 수 없었던 거죠.”"]], [["‘Think of the look of the thing,’ expostulated Mr.", "“이게 어떻게 보일지 생각해 보게.” 픽윅 씨가 타일렀다."], ["Pickwick; ‘have some regard to appearances.’", "“체면을 좀 생각하란 말일세.”"]], [["‘Oh, certainly,’ said Bob, ‘it’s not the sort of thing at all.", "“오, 그럼요.” 보브가 말했다."], ["All over, governor.’", "“전혀 그럴 만한 일이 아니죠. 다 끝났습니다, 단장님.”"]], [["Satisfied with this assurance, Mr. Pickwick once more drew his head into the chaise and pulled up the glass; but he had scarcely resumed the conversation which Mr.", "이 다짐에 만족한 픽윅 씨는 다시 한 번 마차 안으로 머리를 들이고 유리창을 올렸다."], ["Bob Sawyer had interrupted, when he was somewhat startled by the apparition of a small dark body, of an oblong form, on the outside of the window, which gave sundry taps against it, as if impatient of admission.", "그러나 보브 소여 씨가 중단시켰던 이야기를 채 다시 시작하기도 전에, 창밖에 나타난 길쭉한 모양의 작고 검은 물체에 다소 흠칫 놀라고 말았으니, 그것은 마치 들여보내 달라고 안달이라도 난 듯 창문을 톡톡 두드려 댔다."]], [["‘What’s this?’ exclaimed Mr.", "“이게 뭔가?”"], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 소리쳤다."]], [["‘It looks like a case-bottle;’ remarked Ben Allen, eyeing the object in question through his spectacles with some interest; ‘I rather think it belongs to Bob.’", "“휴대용 술병 같은데요.” 벤 앨런이 안경 너머로 자못 흥미롭게 그 물체를 살피며 말했다. “아무래도 보브의 것 같습니다.”"]], [["The impression was perfectly accurate; for Mr.", "그 짐작은 완벽하게 정확했다."], ["Bob Sawyer, having attached the case-bottle to the end of the walking-stick, was battering the window with it, in token of his wish, that his friends inside would partake of its contents, in all good-fellowship and harmony.", "보브 소여 씨가 휴대용 술병을 지팡이 끝에 매달아, 안에 탄 친구들이 우애와 화목 속에서 그 내용물을 함께 들기를 바라는 표시로 창문을 두드려 대고 있었던 것이다."]], [["‘What’s to be done?’ said Mr.", "“어찌하면 좋을까?”"], ["Pickwick, looking at the bottle.", "픽윅 씨가 술병을 바라보며 말했다."], ["‘This proceeding is more absurd than the other.’", "“이 짓은 아까 것보다 더 어처구니없군.”"]], [["‘I think it would be best to take it in,’ replied Mr.", "“받아들이는 게 제일 좋을 것 같습니다.” 벤 앨런 씨가 대답했다."], ["Ben Allen; ‘it would serve him right to take it in and keep it, wouldn’t it?’", "“받아서 그냥 가지고 있으면 그자에게 딱 좋은 응징이 되지 않겠습니까?”"]], [["‘It would,’ said Mr.", "“그렇겠군.”"], ["Pickwick; ‘shall I?’", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. “그럴까?”"]], [["‘I think it the most proper course we could possibly adopt,’ replied Ben.", "“우리가 취할 수 있는 가장 적절한 방책이라고 생각합니다.” 벤이 대답했다."]], [["This advice quite coinciding with his own opinion, Mr.", "이 조언이 자신의 생각과 완전히 일치하는지라, 픽윅 씨는 가만히 창문을 내리고 지팡이에서 술병을 떼어 냈다."], ["Pickwick gently let down the window and disengaged the bottle from the stick; upon which the latter was drawn up, and Mr. Bob Sawyer was heard to laugh heartily.", "그러자 지팡이는 위로 끌려 올라갔고, 보브 소여 씨가 유쾌하게 웃는 소리가 들려왔다."]], [["‘What a merry dog it is!’ said Mr.", "“참으로 명랑한 친구로군!”"], ["Pickwick, looking round at his companion, with the bottle in his hand.", "픽윅 씨가 손에 술병을 든 채 동행을 돌아보며 말했다."]], [["‘He is,’ said Mr.", "“그렇습죠.”"], ["Allen.", "앨런 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘You cannot possibly be angry with him,’ remarked Mr.", "“도저히 저 친구에게 화를 낼 수가 없군.”"], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Quite out of the question,’ observed Benjamin Allen.", "“화를 낸다는 건 어림도 없는 일이지요.” 벤저민 앨런이 말했다."]], [["During this short interchange of sentiments, Mr. Pickwick had, in an abstracted mood, uncorked the bottle.", "이렇게 짧게 소회를 주고받는 동안, 픽윅 씨는 무심결에 술병 마개를 따고 있었다."]], [["‘What is it?’ inquired Ben Allen carelessly.", "“그게 뭡니까?” 벤 앨런이 무심히 물었다."]], [["‘I don’t know,’ replied Mr.", "“모르겠네.”"], ["Pickwick, with equal carelessness.", "픽윅 씨도 똑같이 무심하게 대답했다."], ["‘It smells, I think, like milk-punch.’", "“냄새로 보아선, 밀크펀치 같군.”"]], [["Oh, indeed?’ said Ben.", "“오, 그렇습니까?” 벤이 말했다."]], [["‘I think so,’ rejoined Mr.", "“그런 것 같네.”"], ["Pickwick, very properly guarding himself against the possibility of stating an untruth; ‘mind, I could not undertake to say certainly, without tasting it.’", "픽윅 씨가 거짓을 말할 가능성을 매우 적절하게 경계하며 덧붙였다. “명심하게, 맛을 보지 않고서야 확실히 말할 수는 없네.”"]], [["‘You had better do so,’ said Ben; ‘we may as well know what it is.’", "“맛을 보시는 편이 낫겠습니다.” 벤이 말했다. “그게 뭔지 알아 두는 것도 나쁘지 않죠.”"]], [["‘Do you think so?’ replied Mr.", "“그렇게 생각하나?” 픽윅 씨가 대답했다."], ["Pickwick. ‘Well; if you are curious to know, of course I have no objection.’", "“좋아, 자네가 그리 알고 싶다면 나야 반대할 까닭이 없지.”"]], [["Ever willing to sacrifice his own feelings to the wishes of his friend, Mr. Pickwick at once took a pretty long taste.", "언제나 자신의 감정을 친구의 바람에 기꺼이 희생하는 픽윅 씨는, 곧장 제법 길게 한 모금을 맛보았다."]], [["‘What is it?’ inquired Ben Allen, interrupting him with some impatience.", "“그게 뭡니까?” 벤 앨런이 다소 안달하며 그를 가로막고 물었다."]], [["‘Curious,’ said Mr.", "“묘하군.”"], ["Pickwick, smacking his lips, ‘I hardly know, now.", "픽윅 씨가 입맛을 다시며 말했다. “지금도 잘 모르겠네."], ["Oh, yes!’ said Mr.", "오, 그렇지!”"], ["Pickwick, after a second taste.", "두 번째로 맛본 뒤에 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["‘It is punch.’", "“이건 펀치일세, 정말로.”"]], [["Mr. Ben Allen looked at Mr. Pickwick; Mr. Pickwick looked at Mr. Ben Allen; Mr. Ben Allen smiled; Mr. Pickwick did not.", "벤 앨런 씨가 픽윅 씨를 바라보았고, 픽윅 씨가 벤 앨런 씨를 바라보았다. 벤 앨런 씨는 미소 지었지만, 픽윅 씨는 그러지 않았다."]], [["‘It would serve him right,’ said the last-named gentleman, with some severity--‘it would serve him right to drink it every drop.’", "“그자에게 딱 좋은 응징이 될 걸세.” 끝에 말한 그 신사가 다소 엄하게 말했다. “한 방울도 남김없이 마셔 버리는 게 그자에게 딱 좋은 응징이겠지.”"]], [["‘The very thing that occurred to me,’ said Ben Allen.", "“저도 바로 그 생각을 했습니다.” 벤 앨런이 말했다."]], [["‘Is it, indeed?’ rejoined Mr.", "“정말 그런가?”"], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 대꾸했다."], ["‘Then here’s his health!’ With these words, that excellent person took a most energetic pull at the bottle, and handed it to Ben Allen, who was not slow to imitate his example.", "“그렇다면 그자의 건강을 위하여!” 이 말과 함께 그 훌륭한 인물은 병을 매우 힘차게 들이켜고는 벤 앨런에게 건넸으니, 벤 또한 그 본보기를 따르는 데 굼뜨지 않았다."], ["The smiles became mutual, and the milk-punch was gradually and cheerfully disposed of.", "미소는 서로에게 번졌고, 밀크펀치는 차츰차츰 유쾌하게 비워져 갔다."]], [["‘After all,’ said Mr.", "“어쨌든 말이야,” 마지막 한 방울을 비우며 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick, as he drained the last drop, ‘his pranks are really very amusing; very entertaining indeed.’", "“저 친구의 장난은 정말이지 무척 재미있어. 정말로 흥겹단 말일세.”"]], [["‘You may say that,’ rejoined Mr.", "“그렇게 말씀하셔도 됩니다.”"], ["Ben Allen. In proof of Bob Sawyer’s being one of the funniest fellows alive, he proceeded to entertain Mr.", "벤 앨런 씨가 대꾸했다."], ["Pickwick with a long and circumstantial account how that gentleman once drank himself into a fever and got his head shaved; the relation of which pleasant and agreeable history was only stopped by the stoppage of the chaise at the Bell at Berkeley Heath, to change horses.", "보브 소여가 살아 있는 사람 중 가장 우스운 녀석이라는 증거로, 그는 픽윅 씨에게 그 신사가 한번은 술을 마시다가 열병이 나서 머리를 박박 밀었던 일을 길고 자세하게 들려주기 시작했다. 이 유쾌하고 흐뭇한 이야기는 마차가 버클리 히스의 벨 여관에 말을 갈아매려고 멈춰 서면서야 비로소 중단되었다."]], [["‘I say!", "“이보세요!"], ["We’re going to dine here, aren’t we?’ said Bob, looking in at the window.", "여기서 저녁을 들 거죠, 그렇죠?” 보브가 창 안을 들여다보며 말했다."]], [["‘Dine!’ said Mr.", "“저녁이라니!”"], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["‘Why, we have only come nineteen miles, and have eighty-seven and a half to go.’", "“겨우 십구 마일밖에 안 왔고, 아직 팔십칠 마일하고 반이나 더 가야 하는데.”"]], [["‘Just the reason why we should take something to enable us to bear up against the fatigue,’ remonstrated Mr.", "“그러니까 더더욱 이 피로를 견뎌 낼 무언가를 들어 둬야지요.”"], ["Bob Sawyer.", "보브 소여 씨가 항변했다."]], [["‘Oh, it’s quite impossible to dine at half-past eleven o’clock in the day,’ replied Mr.", "“오, 낮 열한 시 반에 저녁을 든다는 건 도무지 말도 안 되네.”"], ["Pickwick, looking at his watch.", "픽윅 씨가 자기 시계를 보며 대답했다."]], [["‘So it is,’ rejoined Bob, ‘lunch is the very thing.", "“그렇긴 하군요.” 보브가 대꾸했다."], ["Hollo, you sir!", "“점심이 딱이겠네요."], ["Lunch for three, directly; and keep the horses back for a quarter of an hour.", "여보시오, 거기! 셋이 먹을 점심을 당장."], ["Tell them to put everything they have cold, on the table, and some bottled ale, and let us taste your very best Madeira.’ Issuing these orders with monstrous importance and bustle, Mr.", "그리고 말은 십오 분만 묶어 두시오. 차게 둔 건 죄다 식탁에 올리고, 병맥주도 좀 내오고, 댁의 최고급 마데이라도 맛보게 해 주시오.”"], ["Bob Sawyer at once hurried into the house to superintend the arrangements; in less than five minutes he returned and declared them to be excellent.", "어마어마한 거드름과 소동을 떨며 이런 주문을 내린 보브 소여 씨는, 곧장 안으로 서둘러 들어가 준비를 감독했고, 오 분도 채 안 되어 돌아와서는 모든 게 훌륭하다고 단언했다."]], [["The quality of the lunch fully justified the eulogium which Bob had pronounced, and very great justice was done to it, not only by that gentleman, but Mr. Ben Allen and Mr. Pickwick also. Under the auspices of the three, the bottled ale and the Madeira were promptly disposed of; and when (the horses being once more put to) they resumed their seats, with the case-bottle full of the best substitute for milk-punch that could be procured on so short a notice, the key-bugle sounded, and the red flag waved, without the slightest opposition on Mr. Pickwick’s part.", "점심의 질은 보브가 늘어놓은 찬사를 충분히 입증했고, 그 신사뿐 아니라 벤 앨런 씨와 픽윅 씨도 점심에 한껏 정당한 대접을 베풀었다. 셋의 후원 아래 병맥주와 마데이라는 즉각 비워졌고, (말이 다시 매여) 그들이, 그토록 촉박한 통고로 구할 수 있는 최고의 밀크펀치 대용품으로 가득 채운 휴대용 술병을 들고 다시 자리에 앉자, 키뷰글이 울리고 붉은 깃발이 휘날렸으되, 픽윅 씨 쪽에서는 조금의 반대도 없었다."]], [["At the Hop Pole at Tewkesbury, they stopped to dine; upon which occasion there was more bottled ale, with some more Madeira, and some port besides; and here the case-bottle was replenished for the fourth time.", "튜크스베리의 홉 폴 여관에서 그들은 저녁을 들려고 멈췄다. 이때에는 병맥주가 더 나왔고, 마데이라도 더, 그리고 포트와인까지 곁들였다."], ["Under the influence of these combined stimulants, Mr. Pickwick and Mr.", "그리고 여기서 휴대용 술병은 네 번째로 다시 채워졌다."], ["Ben Allen fell fast asleep for thirty miles, while Bob and Mr. Weller sang duets in the dickey.", "이 합쳐진 자극제들의 영향 아래, 픽윅 씨와 벤 앨런 씨는 삼십 마일 동안 곤히 잠들었고, 그동안 보브와 웰러 씨는 마부석에서 이중창을 불렀다."]], [["It was quite dark when Mr. Pickwick roused himself sufficiently to look out of the window.", "픽윅 씨가 정신을 충분히 차려 창밖을 내다보았을 때는 사방이 완전히 어두워져 있었다."], ["The straggling cottages by the road-side, the dingy hue of every object visible, the murky atmosphere, the paths of cinders and brick-dust, the deep-red glow of furnace fires in the distance, the volumes of dense smoke issuing heavily forth from high toppling chimneys, blackening and obscuring everything around; the glare of distant lights, the ponderous wagons which toiled along the road, laden with clashing rods of iron, or piled with heavy goods--all betokened their rapid approach to the great working town of Birmingham.", "길가에 띄엄띄엄 흩어진 오두막들, 눈에 보이는 모든 사물의 거무튀튀한 빛깔, 음울한 대기, 석탄재와 벽돌 가루가 깔린 길, 저 멀리 용광로 불의 짙붉은 빛, 우뚝 솟은 높다란 굴뚝에서 묵직하게 뿜어 나와 주위 온 사물을 검게 물들이고 흐릿하게 가리는 자욱한 연기 무더기, 멀리서 번득이는 불빛들, 부딪치며 덜그럭대는 쇠막대를 싣거나 무거운 화물을 산더미처럼 쌓고 길을 따라 힘겹게 나아가는 육중한 짐마차들—이 모든 것이 그들이 거대한 공업 도시 버밍엄에 빠르게 다가가고 있음을 알려 주었다."]], [["As they rattled through the narrow thoroughfares leading to the heart of the turmoil, the sights and sounds of earnest occupation struck more forcibly on the senses.", "소동의 심장부로 이어지는 좁은 길들을 덜컹거리며 지나갈 때, 진지한 노동의 광경과 소리가 더욱 강렬하게 감각을 두드렸다."], ["The streets were thronged with working people.", "거리는 일하는 사람들로 북적였다."], ["The hum of labour resounded from every house; lights gleamed from the long casement windows in the attic storeys, and the whirl of wheels and noise of machinery shook the trembling walls.", "노동의 윙윙거림이 집집마다 울려 퍼졌고, 다락층의 길쭉한 여닫이창에서 불빛이 새어 나왔으며, 바퀴 도는 소리와 기계 돌아가는 소음이 떨리는 벽을 뒤흔들었다."], ["The fires, whose lurid, sullen light had been visible for miles, blazed fiercely up, in the great works and factories of the town.", "그 붉고 음침한 빛이 몇 마일 밖에서도 보이던 그 불들은, 이 도시의 거대한 공장과 작업장에서 사납게 타올랐다."], ["The din of hammers, the rushing of steam, and the dead heavy clanking of engines, was the harsh music which arose from every quarter.", "망치 두드리는 소리, 증기 뿜는 소리, 그리고 기관의 둔하고 묵직한 쩔그렁 소리가 사방에서 일어나는 거친 음악이었다."]], [["The postboy was driving briskly through the open streets, and past the handsome and well-lighted shops that intervene between the outskirts of the town and the Old Royal Hotel, before Mr. Pickwick had begun to consider the very difficult and delicate nature of the commission which had carried him thither.", "마부 소년이 탁 트인 거리를 지나, 도시 변두리와 올드 로열 호텔 사이에 늘어선 멋지고 환하게 불 밝힌 가게들을 지나 활기차게 마차를 몰고 가는 동안에도, 픽윅 씨는 자신을 그곳까지 이끌어 온 임무가 얼마나 어렵고 미묘한 성질의 것인지를 미처 헤아리기 시작하지 못하고 있었다."]], [["The delicate nature of this commission, and the difficulty of executing it in a satisfactory manner, were by no means lessened by the voluntary companionship of Mr. Bob Sawyer.", "이 임무의 미묘한 성질과, 그것을 만족스럽게 수행하기 어려운 점은, 보브 소여 씨의 자발적인 동행으로 인해 결코 줄어들지 않았다."], ["Truth to tell, Mr. Pickwick felt that his presence on the occasion, however considerate and gratifying, was by no means an honour he would willingly have sought; in fact, he would cheerfully have given a reasonable sum of money to have had Mr.", "사실대로 말하자면, 픽윅 씨는 이런 자리에서 그가 함께 있는 것이, 아무리 사려 깊고 흐뭇한 일이라 해도, 결코 자신이 기꺼이 구했을 만한 영예가 아니라고 느꼈다."], ["Bob Sawyer removed to any place at not less than fifty miles’ distance, without delay.", "실은, 보브 소여 씨를 지체 없이 적어도 오십 마일은 떨어진 어딘가로 치워 버릴 수만 있다면, 그는 기꺼이 상당한 액수의 돈이라도 내놓았을 것이다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick had never held any personal communication with Mr.", "픽윅 씨는 노 윈클 씨와 한 번도 직접 만나 본 적이 없었으나, 한두 번 편지로 서신을 주고받으며 그 아들의 도덕적 품성과 행실에 관한 문의에 만족스러운 답을 보낸 적은 있었다."], ["Winkle, senior, although he had once or twice corresponded with him by letter, and returned satisfactory answers to his inquiries concerning the moral character and behaviour of his son; he felt nervously sensible that to wait upon him, for the first time, attended by Bob Sawyer and Ben Allen, both slightly fuddled, was not the most ingenious and likely means that could have been hit upon to prepossess him in his favour.", "그가 처음 그를 찾아뵙는 자리에, 약간씩 거나하게 취한 보브 소여와 벤 앨런을 거느리고 가는 것이, 자신에게 호의를 품게 만들기에 결코 가장 기발하거나 가망 있는 방편이 아니라는 사실이 그는 신경 쓰여 견딜 수 없었다."]], [["‘However,’ said Mr.", "“그렇지만,”"], ["Pickwick, endeavouring to reassure himself, ‘I must do the best I can.", "픽윅 씨가 스스로를 다잡으려 애쓰며 말했다. “나는 할 수 있는 한 최선을 다해야 한다."], ["I must see him to-night, for I faithfully promised to do so.", "오늘 밤 그를 만나야 해, 그렇게 하겠다고 굳게 약속했으니까."], ["If they persist in accompanying me, I must make the interview as brief as possible, and be content that, for their own sakes, they will not expose themselves.’", "저들이 굳이 따라오겠다면, 면담을 되도록 짧게 끝내고, 저들도 제 체면을 위해서 추태를 부리지는 않으리라는 데 만족할 수밖에.”"]], [["As he comforted himself with these reflections, the chaise stopped at the door of the Old Royal. Ben Allen having been partially awakened from a stupendous sleep, and dragged out by the collar by Mr. Samuel Weller, Mr. Pickwick was enabled to alight. They were shown to a comfortable apartment, and Mr. Pickwick at once propounded a question to the waiter concerning the whereabout of Mr. Winkle’s residence.", "이런 생각으로 마음을 달래고 있을 때, 마차가 올드 로열 호텔 문 앞에 멈췄다. 엄청난 잠에서 어느 정도 깨어난 벤 앨런이 새뮤얼 웰러 씨에게 멱살을 잡혀 끌려 나오자, 픽윅 씨도 비로소 내릴 수 있었다. 그들은 아늑한 방으로 안내되었고, 픽윅 씨는 즉시 급사에게 윈클 씨의 거처가 어디인지를 물었다."]], [["‘Close by, Sir,’ said the waiter, ‘not above five hundred yards, Sir.", "“바로 근처입니다, 선생님.” 급사가 말했다."], ["Mr. Winkle is a wharfinger, Sir, at the canal, sir.", "“오백 야드도 채 안 됩니다, 선생님."], ["Private residence is not--oh dear, no, sir, not five hundred yards, sir.’ Here the waiter blew a candle out, and made a feint of lighting it again, in order to afford Mr.", "윈클 씨는 운하의 부두 관리인이십니다, 선생님. 사저는 아니—오, 천만에요, 선생님, 오백 야드도 안 됩니다, 선생님.”"], ["Pickwick an opportunity of asking any further questions, if he felt so disposed.", "여기서 급사는 촛불 하나를 끄고는, 픽윅 씨가 그럴 마음이 있다면 더 물어볼 기회를 주려고, 다시 불을 붙이는 시늉을 했다."]], [["‘Take anything now, Sir?’ said the waiter, lighting the candle in desperation at Mr.", "“지금 무얼 좀 드시겠습니까, 선생님?” 픽윅 씨의 침묵에 절박해진 급사가 촛불을 켜며 말했다."], ["Pickwick’s silence.", "“차나 커피요, 선생님?"], ["‘Tea or coffee, Sir?", "저녁 식사요, 선생님?”"], ["Dinner, sir?’", "“지금은 아무것도 안 하겠네.”"]], [["‘Nothing now.’\n‘Very good, sir.", "“알겠습니다, 선생님."], ["Like to order supper, Sir?’", "야식을 주문하시겠습니까, 선생님?”"]], [["‘Not just now.’", "“지금은 아닐세.”"]], [["‘Very good, Sir.’ Here, he walked slowly to the door, and then stopping short, turned round and said, with great suavity--", "“알겠습니다, 선생님.” 여기서 그는 천천히 문 쪽으로 걸어가다가, 문득 멈춰 서서 돌아보며 매우 상냥하게 말했다—"]], [["‘Shall I send the chambermaid, gentlemen?’", "“객실 하녀를 보내 드릴까요, 신사분들?”"]], [["‘You may if you please,’ replied Mr.", "“원한다면 그러게나.”"], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘If you please, sir.’", "“선생님께서 원하신다면요.”"]], [["‘And bring some soda-water,’ said Bob Sawyer.", "“그리고 소다수를 좀 가져오게.” 보브 소여가 말했다."]], [["‘Soda-water, Sir!", "“소다수요, 선생님!"], ["Yes, Sir.’ With his mind apparently relieved from an overwhelming weight, by having at last got an order for something, the waiter imperceptibly melted away.", "네, 선생님.” 마침내 무엇이든 주문을 하나 받았다는 사실에 짓누르던 무거운 짐을 덜어 낸 듯한 마음으로, 급사는 어느새 소리 없이 녹아 사라졌다."], ["Waiters never walk or run.", "급사들은 결코 걷거나 뛰는 법이 없다."], ["They have a peculiar and mysterious power of skimming out of rooms, which other mortals possess not.", "그들에게는 다른 인간에게는 없는, 방에서 스르르 미끄러져 나가는 독특하고 신비로운 능력이 있다."]], [["Some slight symptoms of vitality having been awakened in Mr. Ben Allen by the soda-water, he suffered himself to be prevailed upon to wash his face and hands, and to submit to be brushed by Sam.", "소다수 덕분에 벤 앨런 씨에게 약간의 생기가 되살아나자, 그는 얼굴과 손을 씻고 샘에게 옷을 솔질받는 일에 마지못해 응했다."], ["Mr. Pickwick and Bob Sawyer having also repaired the disorder which the journey had made in their apparel, the three started forth, arm in arm, to Mr.", "픽윅 씨와 보브 소여도 여행으로 흐트러진 옷차림을 추스른 뒤, 셋은 팔짱을 끼고 윈클 씨 댁으로 출발했다."], ["Winkle’s; Bob Sawyer impregnating the atmosphere with tobacco smoke as he walked along.", "보브 소여는 걸어가는 내내 담배 연기로 대기를 물들였다."]], [["About a quarter of a mile off, in a quiet, substantial-looking street, stood an old red brick house with three steps before the door, and a brass plate upon it, bearing, in fat Roman capitals, the words, ‘Mr.", "사 분의 일 마일쯤 떨어진, 조용하고 견실해 보이는 거리에, 문 앞에 세 단의 계단이 있고 그 위에 ‘윈클 씨’라는 글자가 두툼한 로마자 대문자로 새겨진 놋쇠 명패가 붙은 낡은 붉은 벽돌집이 서 있었다."], ["Winkle.’ The steps were very white, and the bricks were very red, and the house was very clean; and here stood Mr. Pickwick, Mr.", "계단은 무척 희고, 벽돌은 무척 붉고, 집은 무척 깨끗했다."], ["Benjamin Allen, and Mr. Bob Sawyer, as the clock struck ten.", "시계가 열 시를 칠 때, 픽윅 씨와 벤저민 앨런 씨와 보브 소여 씨가 바로 여기에 와 섰다."]], [["A smart servant-girl answered the knock, and started on beholding the three strangers.", "말쑥한 하녀가 노크에 응답하더니, 낯선 사람 셋을 보고 흠칫 놀랐다."]], [["‘Is Mr. Winkle at home, my dear?’ inquired Mr.", "“윈클 씨 계신가, 아가씨?”"], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘He is just going to supper, Sir,’ replied the girl.", "“막 저녁 식사를 하려던 참이세요, 선생님.” 하녀가 대답했다."]], [["‘Give him that card if you please,’ rejoined Mr.", "“이 명함을 그분께 전해 주겠나.”"], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 대꾸했다."], ["‘Say I am sorry to trouble him at so late an hour; but I am anxious to see him to- night, and have only just arrived.’", "“이렇게 늦은 시각에 폐를 끼쳐 죄송하다고 말씀드려 주게. 다만 오늘 밤 꼭 뵙고 싶고, 방금 막 도착했다고 말일세.”"]], [["The girl looked timidly at Mr. Bob Sawyer, who was expressing his admiration of her personal charms by a variety of wonderful grimaces; and casting an eye at the hats and greatcoats which hung in the passage, called another girl to mind the door while she went upstairs.", "하녀는 갖가지 놀라운 우스꽝스러운 표정으로 자신의 미모에 대한 감탄을 표현하고 있는 보브 소여 씨를 겁먹은 듯 쳐다보았다."], ["The sentinel was speedily relieved; for the girl returned immediately, and begging pardon of the gentlemen for leaving them in the street, ushered them into a floor-clothed back parlour, half office and half dressing room, in which the principal useful and ornamental articles of furniture were a desk, a wash-hand stand and shaving-glass, a boot-rack and boot- jack, a high stool, four chairs, a table, and an old eight-day clock.", "그러고는 복도에 걸린 모자와 외투들에 눈길을 던지더니, 자기가 위층에 올라가는 동안 문을 지키라고 다른 하녀를 불렀다. 그 보초는 곧 임무에서 풀려났으니, 하녀가 즉시 돌아와 신사분들을 거리에 세워 둔 일을 사과하고는, 그들을 바닥에 천을 깐 안쪽 응접실로 안내했던 것이다."], ["Over the mantelpiece were the sunken doors of an iron safe, while a couple of hanging shelves for books, an almanac, and several files of dusty papers, decorated the walls.", "그곳은 절반은 사무실, 절반은 화장실 격으로, 쓸모 있고 보기 좋은 가구라야 책상 하나, 세면대와 면도용 거울, 부츠걸이와 부츠 빼는 기구, 높은 걸상 하나, 의자 넷, 탁자 하나, 그리고 여드레 태엽을 감는 낡은 괘종시계 하나가 주된 것이었다. 벽난로 선반 위에는 철제 금고의 움푹 들어간 문짝들이 있었고, 책을 얹는 매달린 선반 한 쌍, 달력 하나, 그리고 먼지 쌓인 서류 묶음 몇 개가 벽을 장식하고 있었다."]], [["‘Very sorry to leave you standing at the door, Sir,’ said the girl, lighting a lamp, and addressing Mr.", "“문 앞에 세워 두어서 정말 죄송합니다, 선생님.” 하녀가 등불을 켜며 매혹적인 미소로 픽윅 씨에게 말했다."], ["Pickwick with a winning smile, ‘but you was quite strangers to me; and we have such a many trampers that only come to see what they can lay their hands on, that really--’", "“하지만 선생님들이 제겐 전혀 낯선 분들이었던 데다, 그저 손댈 만한 게 뭐 없나 보러 오는 떠돌이가 워낙 많아서, 정말이지—”"]], [["‘There is not the least occasion for any apology, my dear,’ said Mr.", "“조금도 사과할 필요 없다네, 아가씨.”"], ["Pickwick good-humouredly.", "픽윅 씨가 너그러이 말했다."]], [["‘Not the slightest, my love,’ said Bob Sawyer, playfully stretching forth his arms, and skipping from side to side, as if to prevent the young lady’s leaving the room.", "“추호도 그럴 필요 없소, 내 사랑.” 보브 소여가 마치 아가씨가 방을 나가지 못하게 막으려는 듯, 장난스레 두 팔을 쭉 뻗고 이쪽저쪽으로 깡충거리며 말했다."]], [["The young lady was not at all softened by these allurements, for she at once expressed her opinion, that Mr.", "이 유혹에 아가씨는 조금도 누그러지지 않았다."], ["Bob Sawyer was an ‘odous creetur;’ and, on his becoming rather more pressing in his attentions, imprinted her fair fingers upon his face, and bounced out of the room with many expressions of aversion and contempt.", "그도 그럴 것이 그녀는 곧장 보브 소여 씨가 ‘망측한 인간’이라는 자기 의견을 밝혔고, 그가 좀 더 들이대며 수작을 걸자, 자기 고운 손가락을 그의 얼굴에 찍어 누르고는, 혐오와 경멸의 표현을 잔뜩 쏟아 내며 방에서 튕겨 나가 버렸다."]], [["Deprived of the young lady’s society, Mr. Bob Sawyer proceeded to divert himself by peeping into the desk, looking into all the table drawers, feigning to pick the lock of the iron safe, turning the almanac with its face to the wall, trying on the boots of Mr. Winkle, senior, over his own, and making several other humorous experiments upon the furniture, all of which afforded Mr. Pickwick unspeakable horror and agony, and yielded Mr. Bob Sawyer proportionate delight.", "아가씨와의 교제를 빼앗긴 보브 소여 씨는, 책상 안을 들여다보고, 탁자 서랍을 죄다 뒤지고, 철제 금고의 자물쇠를 따는 시늉을 하고, 달력을 벽 쪽으로 돌려놓고, 노 윈클 씨의 부츠를 자기 부츠 위에 신어 보고, 그 밖에도 가구를 가지고 여러 익살스러운 실험을 하며 시간을 보냈으니, 이 모든 것이 픽윅 씨에게는 이루 말할 수 없는 공포와 고통을 안겨 주었고, 보브 소여 씨에게는 그에 걸맞은 기쁨을 안겨 주었다."]], [["At length the door opened, and a little old gentleman in a snuff- coloured suit, with a head and face the precise counterpart of those belonging to Mr.", "마침내 문이 열리고, 코담배 빛깔의 옷을 입은 자그마한 노신사가 들어왔으니, 그 머리와 얼굴은 다소 대머리인 점만 빼면 젊은 윈클 씨의 것과 정확히 똑같았다."], ["Winkle, junior, excepting that he was rather bald, trotted into the room with Mr. Pickwick’s card in one hand, and a silver candlestick in the other.", "그는 한 손에 픽윅 씨의 명함을, 다른 손에 은촛대를 들고 종종걸음으로 방에 들어왔다."]], [["‘Mr. Pickwick, sir, how do you do?’ said Winkle the elder, putting down the candlestick and proffering his hand.", "“픽윅 씨, 안녕하십니까?”"], ["‘Hope I see you well, sir.", "노 윈클이 촛대를 내려놓고 손을 내밀며 말했다."], ["Glad to see you. Be seated, Mr.", "“건강하신 모습이길 바랍니다, 선생."], ["Pickwick, I beg, Sir.", "뵙게 되어 반갑습니다."], ["This gentleman is--’", "앉으시지요, 픽윅 씨, 부디 앉으십시오. 이 신사분은—”"]], [["‘My friend, Mr.", "“제 친구 소여 씨입니다.”"], ["Sawyer,’ interposed Mr.", "픽윅 씨가 끼어들었다."], ["Pickwick, ‘your son’s friend.’", "“댁의 아드님 친구이지요.”"]], [["‘Oh,’ said Mr.", "“오,”"], ["Winkle the elder, looking rather grimly at Bob.", "노 윈클 씨가 다소 험상궂게 보브를 바라보며 말했다."], ["‘I hope you are well, sir.’", "“건강하시길 바랍니다, 선생.”"]], [["‘Right as a trivet, sir,’ replied Bob Sawyer.", "“삼발이처럼 멀쩡합니다, 선생.” 보브 소여가 대답했다."]], [["‘This other gentleman,’ cried Mr.", "“이쪽 다른 신사분은,”"], ["Pickwick, ‘is, as you will see when you have read the letter with which I am intrusted, a very near relative, or I should rather say a very particular friend of your son’s.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. “제가 맡아 온 편지를 읽어 보시면 아시겠지만, 댁의 아드님과 매우 가까운 친척, 아니 차라리 매우 각별한 친구라 해야겠지요."], ["His name is Allen.’", "성함은 앨런입니다.”"]], [["‘That gentleman?’ inquired Mr.", "“저 신사분 말이오?”"], ["Winkle, pointing with the card towards Ben Allen, who had fallen asleep in an attitude which left nothing of him visible but his spine and his coat collar.", "윈클 씨가 명함으로 벤 앨런을 가리키며 물었다. 벤 앨런은 등뼈와 외투 깃 말고는 아무것도 보이지 않는 자세로 잠들어 있었다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick was on the point of replying to the question, and reciting Mr. Benjamin Allen’s name and honourable distinctions at full length, when the sprightly Mr. Bob Sawyer, with a view of rousing his friend to a sense of his situation, inflicted a startling pinch upon the fleshly part of his arm, which caused him to jump up with a shriek. Suddenly aware that he was in the presence of a stranger, Mr. Ben Allen advanced and, shaking Mr. Winkle most affectionately by both hands for about five minutes, murmured, in some half-intelligible fragments of sentences, the great delight he felt in seeing him, and a hospitable inquiry whether he felt disposed to take anything after his walk, or would prefer waiting ‘till dinner-time;’ which done, he sat down and gazed about him with a petrified stare, as if he had not the remotest idea where he was, which indeed he had not.", "픽윅 씨가 막 그 물음에 답하며 벤저민 앨런 씨의 이름과 명예로운 칭호들을 빠짐없이 늘어놓으려던 참에, 활기찬 보브 소여 씨가 친구에게 제 처지를 일깨워 줄 요량으로 그의 팔 살집을 깜짝 놀랄 만큼 세게 꼬집었고, 그 바람에 벤은 비명을 지르며 펄쩍 뛰어올랐다. 자신이 낯선 사람 앞에 있다는 것을 문득 깨달은 벤 앨런 씨는 앞으로 나서서, 윈클 씨의 두 손을 약 오 분 동안 더없이 다정하게 흔들며, 반쯤만 알아들을 수 있는 토막 문장들로, 그를 뵙게 되어 크게 기쁘다는 말과, 산책 뒤에 무얼 좀 들 마음이 있으신지, 아니면 ‘저녁 식사 때까지’ 기다리시는 편이 좋으신지를 묻는 환대의 말을 중얼거렸다. 그러고는 자리에 앉아 자기가 대체 어디 있는지 전혀 모르겠다는 듯 굳어 버린 눈으로 주위를 두리번거렸으니, 실제로 그는 정말 몰랐던 것이다."]], [["All this was most embarrassing to Mr. Pickwick, the more especially as Mr. Winkle, senior, evinced palpable astonishment at the eccentric--not to say extraordinary--behaviour of his two companions. To bring the matter to an issue at once, he drew a letter from his pocket, and presenting it to Mr. Winkle, senior, said--", "이 모든 것이 픽윅 씨에게는 더없이 거북한 일이었으니, 더더욱 노 윈클 씨가 두 동행의 별난—아니, 기이하다 할 만한—행동에 또렷이 놀라움을 드러냈기 때문이었다. 이 문제를 단번에 매듭짓고자, 그는 주머니에서 편지를 꺼내 노 윈클 씨에게 건네며 말했다—"]], [["‘This letter, Sir, is from your son.", "“이 편지는, 선생, 댁의 아드님이 보낸 것입니다."], ["You will see, by its contents, that on your favourable and fatherly consideration of it, depend his future happiness and welfare.", "그 내용을 보시면 아시겠지만, 그에 대한 선생의 호의적이고 자애로운 헤아림에 그 아이의 앞날의 행복과 안녕이 달려 있습니다."], ["Will you oblige me by giving it the calmest and coolest perusal, and by discussing the subject afterwards with me, in the tone and spirit in which alone it ought to be discussed?", "부디 그것을 더없이 차분하고 냉정하게 읽어 주시고, 그 뒤에 마땅히 그래야 할 어조와 정신으로 저와 그 문제를 의논해 주시겠습니까?"], ["You may judge of the importance of your decision to your son, and his intense anxiety upon the subject, by my waiting upon you, without any previous warning, at so late an hour; and,’ added Mr.", "선생의 결정이 아드님에게 얼마나 중요한지, 그리고 그 아이가 이 문제로 얼마나 애를 태우고 있는지는, 제가 사전에 아무 기별도 없이 이렇게 늦은 시각에 선생을 찾아뵙는 것만 보아도 짐작하실 수 있을 것입니다. 그리고,”"], ["Pickwick, glancing slightly at his two companions--‘and under such unfavourable circumstances.’", "픽윅 씨가 두 동행을 슬쩍 곁눈질하며 덧붙였다. “이렇게 불리한 상황에서 말입니다.”"]], [["With this prelude, Mr. Pickwick placed four closely-written sides of extra superfine wire-wove penitence in the hands of the astounded Mr.", "이런 서두와 함께, 픽윅 씨는 극상의 고급 직조지에 빽빽이 써 내려간 참회의 글 넉 장을 깜짝 놀란 노 윈클 씨의 손에 쥐여 주었다."], ["Winkle, senior. Then reseating himself in his chair, he watched his looks and manner: anxiously, it is true, but with the open front of a gentleman who feels he has taken no part which he need excuse or palliate.", "그러고는 다시 의자에 앉아 그의 표정과 태도를 살폈으니, 과연 마음 졸이기는 했으나, 변명하거나 얼버무릴 어떤 짓도 하지 않았다고 느끼는 신사의 떳떳한 낯빛으로였다."]], [["The old wharfinger turned the letter over, looked at the front, back, and sides, made a microscopic examination of the fat little boy on the seal, raised his eyes to Mr.", "늙은 부두 관리인은 편지를 이리저리 뒤집어, 앞면과 뒷면과 옆면을 살펴보고, 봉인에 찍힌 통통한 어린 소년을 현미경이라도 들이대듯 들여다본 다음, 눈을 들어 픽윅 씨의 얼굴을 바라보았다."], ["Pickwick’s face, and then, seating himself on the high stool, and drawing the lamp closer to him, broke the wax, unfolded the epistle, and lifting it to the light, prepared to read.", "그러고는 높은 걸상에 앉아 등불을 자기 쪽으로 더 가까이 끌어당기고, 봉랍을 뜯어 편지를 펼치고는, 그것을 불빛에 비추어 읽을 채비를 했다."]], [["Just at this moment, Mr. Bob Sawyer, whose wit had lain dormant for some minutes, placed his hands on his knees, and made a face after the portraits of the late Mr. Grimaldi, as clown. It so happened that Mr. Winkle, senior, instead of being deeply engaged in reading the letter, as Mr. Bob Sawyer thought, chanced to be looking over the top of it at no less a person than Mr. Bob Sawyer himself; rightly conjecturing that the face aforesaid was made in ridicule and derision of his own person, he fixed his eyes on Bob with such expressive sternness, that the late Mr. Grimaldi’s lineaments gradually resolved themselves into a very fine expression of humility and confusion.", "바로 이 순간, 몇 분간 잠잠하던 보브 소여 씨의 익살기가 발동하여, 그는 두 손을 무릎에 얹고는 고인이 된 그리말디 씨가 어릿광대로 분한 초상화를 본떠 우스꽝스러운 표정을 지었다. 그런데 공교롭게도 노 윈클 씨는 보브 소여 씨가 생각한 것처럼 편지 읽기에 깊이 빠져 있던 것이 아니라, 마침 편지 위쪽 너머로 다름 아닌 바로 그 보브 소여 씨 본인을 바라보고 있던 참이었다. 앞서 말한 그 표정이 자기 자신을 조롱하고 비웃으려 지어진 것임을 정확히 짐작한 그는, 어찌나 의미심장하게 엄한 눈길을 보브에게 박았던지, 고인이 된 그리말디 씨의 이목구비는 차츰차츰 매우 그럴듯한 겸손과 당황의 표정으로 풀려 갔다."]], [["‘Did you speak, Sir?’ inquired Mr.", "“무어라 하셨소, 선생?”"], ["Winkle, senior, after an awful silence.", "끔찍한 침묵 끝에 노 윈클 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘No, sir,’ replied Bob, With no remains of the clown about him, save and except the extreme redness of his cheeks.", "“아닙니다, 선생.” 보브가 대답했다. 그에게서 어릿광대의 흔적이라곤, 두 뺨이 유난히 붉어진 것 말고는 남아 있지 않았다."]], [["‘You are sure you did not, sir?’ said Mr.", "“정말 아무 말 안 하신 게 분명하오, 선생?”"], ["Winkle, senior.", "노 윈클 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Oh dear, yes, sir, quite,’ replied Bob.", "“오, 그럼요, 선생, 전혀 안 했습니다.” 보브가 대답했다."]], [["‘I thought you did, Sir,’ replied the old gentleman, with indignant emphasis.", "“하신 줄 알았소, 선생.” 노신사가 분개한 어조로 힘주어 대꾸했다."], ["‘Perhaps you looked at me, sir?’", "“혹 나를 빤히 쳐다보신 거요, 선생?”"]], [["‘Oh, no!", "“오, 아닙니다!"], ["sir, not at all,’ replied Bob, with extreme civility.", "선생, 천만에요.” 보브가 더없이 정중하게 대답했다."]], [["‘I am very glad to hear it, sir,’ said Mr. Winkle, senior.", "“그 말을 들으니 매우 기쁘오, 선생.”"], ["Having frowned upon the abashed Bob with great magnificence, the old gentleman again brought the letter to the light, and began to read it seriously.", "노 윈클 씨가 말했다. 무안해진 보브에게 자못 위풍당당하게 눈살을 찌푸린 노신사는, 다시 편지를 불빛에 가져가 진지하게 읽기 시작했다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick eyed him intently as he turned from the bottom line of the first page to the top line of the second, and from the bottom of the second to the top of the third, and from the bottom of the third to the top of the fourth; but not the slightest alteration of countenance afforded a clue to the feelings with which he received the announcement of his son’s marriage, which Mr.", "픽윅 씨는 그가 첫 장 맨 아랫줄에서 둘째 장 맨 윗줄로, 둘째 장 맨 아래에서 셋째 장 맨 위로, 셋째 장 맨 아래에서 넷째 장 맨 위로 옮겨 가는 동안 그를 뚫어지게 지켜보았다."], ["Pickwick knew was in the very first half-dozen lines.", "그러나 아들의 결혼 소식을 받아 든 그 심정을 알아챌 단서가 될 만한 표정 변화는 조금도 없었으니, 픽윅 씨는 그 소식이 맨 첫머리 대여섯 줄 안에 들어 있음을 알고 있었다."]], [["He read the letter to the last word, folded it again with all the carefulness and precision of a man of business, and, just when Mr.", "그는 편지를 마지막 한 마디까지 다 읽고는, 사업가다운 온갖 꼼꼼함과 정확함으로 다시 접었다."], ["Pickwick expected some great outbreak of feeling, dipped a pen in the ink-stand, and said, as quietly as if he were speaking on the most ordinary counting-house topic--", "그러고는 픽윅 씨가 무슨 큰 감정의 폭발을 예상하던 바로 그때, 잉크병에 펜을 적시며, 더없이 평범한 회계실 화제라도 말하듯 차분하게 말했다—"]], [["‘What is Nathaniel’s address, Mr. Pickwick?’", "“너새니얼의 주소가 어찌 되오, 픽윅 씨?”"]], [["‘The George and Vulture, at present,’ replied that gentleman.", "“현재로선 조지 앤드 벌처입니다.” 픽윅 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘George and Vulture.", "“조지 앤드 벌처."], ["Where is that?’", "그게 어디요?”"]], [["‘George Yard, Lombard Street.’", "“롬바드 가, 조지 야드입니다.” “시내요?”"]], [["‘In the city?’", "“그렇습니다.”"]], [["‘Yes.’\nThe old gentleman methodically indorsed the address on the back of the letter; and then, placing it in the desk, which he locked, said, as he got off the stool and put the bunch of keys in his pocket--", "노신사는 그 주소를 편지 뒷면에 차근차근 적어 넣고는, 그것을 책상에 넣고 잠근 다음, 걸상에서 내려와 열쇠 꾸러미를 주머니에 넣으며 말했다—"]], [["‘I suppose there is nothing else which need detain us, Mr. Pickwick?’", "“이제 우리를 더 붙들어 둘 다른 일은 없으리라 생각하오, 픽윅 씨?”"]], [["‘Nothing else, my dear Sir!’ observed that warm-hearted person in indignant amazement.", "“다른 일이 없다니요, 선생!” 그 다정한 인물이 분개한 경악 속에 말했다."], ["‘Nothing else!", "“다른 일이 없다니요!"], ["Have you no opinion to express on this momentous event in our young friend’s life?", "우리 젊은 친구의 인생에서 이토록 중대한 사건을 두고 표명하실 의견이 하나도 없단 말입니까?"], ["No assurance to convey to him, through me, of the continuance of your affection and protection?", "저를 통해 그 아이에게 전할, 변함없는 애정과 보호의 다짐도 하나 없단 말입니까?"], ["Nothing to say which will cheer and sustain him, and the anxious girl who looks to him for comfort and support?", "그 아이와, 또 위로와 지지를 구하며 그를 바라보고 있는 그 애타는 처녀를 격려하고 떠받쳐 줄 한마디 말도 없단 말입니까?"], ["My dear Sir, consider.’", "여보시오, 선생, 생각해 보십시오.”"]], [["‘I will consider,’ replied the old gentleman.", "“생각해 보겠소.” 노신사가 대답했다."], ["‘I have nothing to say just now.", "“지금 당장은 할 말이 없소."], ["I am a man of business, Mr.", "나는 사업가요, 픽윅 씨."], ["Pickwick. I never commit myself hastily in any affair, and from what I see of this, I by no means like the appearance of it.", "나는 어떤 일에서도 결코 성급하게 자신을 내맡기지 않으며, 내가 본 바로는 이 일의 모양새가 도무지 마음에 들지 않소."], ["A thousand pounds is not much, Mr. Pickwick.’", "천 파운드는 큰돈이 아니오, 픽윅 씨.”"]], [["‘You’re very right, Sir,’ interposed Ben Allen, just awake enough to know that he had spent his thousand pounds without the smallest difficulty.", "“지당하신 말씀입니다, 선생.” 자기 천 파운드를 아무 어려움 없이 다 써 버렸다는 것쯤은 알아챌 만큼 막 깨어난 벤 앨런이 끼어들었다."], ["‘You’re an intelligent man.", "“선생은 지혜로운 분이십니다."], ["Bob, he’s a very knowing fellow this.’", "보브, 이분은 아주 사리에 밝은 양반이야.”"]], [["‘I am very happy to find that you do me the justice to make the admission, sir,’ said Mr.", "“그 점을 인정해 주는 공정함을 베푸시니 매우 기쁘구려, 선생.”"], ["Winkle, senior, looking contemptuously at Ben Allen, who was shaking his head profoundly.", "노 윈클 씨가, 심오하게 고개를 끄덕이고 있는 벤 앨런을 경멸하듯 바라보며 말했다."], ["‘The fact is, Mr. Pickwick, that when I gave my son a roving license for a year or so, to see something of men and manners (which he has done under your auspices), so that he might not enter life a mere boarding-school milk-sop to be gulled by everybody, I never bargained for this.", "“사실인즉, 픽윅 씨, 내가 아들에게 한 해 남짓 떠돌 면허를 주어 세상 사람과 풍속을 좀 보게 한 것은 (그 아이가 선생의 후원 아래 그리해 온 것이지요), 그 아이가 누구에게나 속아 넘어가는 한낱 기숙학교 물러 터진 풋내기로 인생에 들어서지 않게 하려던 것이지, 이런 일을 흥정한 것은 결코 아니었소."], ["He knows that very well, so if I withdraw my countenance from him on this account, he has no call to be surprised.", "그 아이도 그것을 잘 알고 있으니, 이 일로 내가 그에게서 후원을 거두어들인다 한들 놀랄 까닭이 없소."], ["He shall hear from me, Mr. Pickwick.", "그 아이는 내 소식을 듣게 될 것이오, 픽윅 씨."], ["Good- night, sir.--Margaret, open the door.’", "안녕히 가시오, 선생.—마거릿, 문을 열어라.”"]], [["All this time, Bob Sawyer had been nudging Mr.", "이 내내 보브 소여는 벤 앨런 씨를 쿡쿡 찔러 옳은 편에서 무어라도 말하게 부추기고 있었다."], ["Ben Allen to say something on the right side; Ben accordingly now burst, without the slightest preliminary notice, into a brief but impassioned piece of eloquence.", "그리하여 벤은 이제 아무런 사전 예고도 없이, 짧지만 격정적인 웅변 한 토막을 터뜨렸다."]], [["‘Sir,’ said Mr.", "“선생,”"], ["Ben Allen, staring at the old gentleman, out of a pair of very dim and languid eyes, and working his right arm vehemently up and down, ‘you--you ought to be ashamed of yourself.’", "벤 앨런 씨가 자못 흐리멍덩하고 나른한 두 눈으로 노신사를 노려보며, 오른팔을 사납게 위아래로 휘저으며 말했다. “당신—당신은 스스로 부끄러운 줄 알아야 하오.”"]], [["‘As the lady’s brother, of course you are an excellent judge of the question,’ retorted Mr.", "“그 숙녀의 오라비이시니, 물론 이 문제에 대해서는 탁월한 심판관이시겠지요.”"], ["Winkle, senior.", "노 윈클 씨가 받아쳤다."], ["‘There; that’s enough.", "“자, 그만하면 됐소."], ["Pray say no more, Mr. Pickwick.", "부디 더는 말씀 마시오, 픽윅 씨."], ["Good-night, gentlemen!’", "안녕히들 가시오, 신사분들!”"]], [["With these words the old gentleman took up the candle-stick and opening the room door, politely motioned towards the passage.", "이 말과 함께 노신사는 촛대를 집어 들고 방문을 열어, 정중하게 복도 쪽을 가리켰다."]], [["‘You will regret this, Sir,’ said Mr.", "“후회하실 겁니다, 선생.”"], ["Pickwick, setting his teeth close together to keep down his choler; for he felt how important the effect might prove to his young friend.", "픽윅 씨가 치미는 노여움을 억누르려 이를 악물며 말했으니, 그 결과가 젊은 친구에게 얼마나 중대한 것일지 절감했기 때문이었다."]], [["‘I am at present of a different opinion,’ calmly replied Mr.", "“나는 현재로선 생각이 다르오.” 노 윈클 씨가 차분하게 대답했다."], ["Winkle, senior. ‘Once again, gentlemen, I wish you a good-night.’", "“다시 한 번, 신사분들, 안녕히 가시기를 비오.”"]], [["Mr. Pickwick walked with angry strides into the street.", "픽윅 씨는 성난 큰 걸음으로 거리로 걸어 나갔다."], ["Mr. Bob Sawyer, completely quelled by the decision of the old gentleman’s manner, took the same course.", "노신사의 단호한 태도에 완전히 기가 꺾인 보브 소여 씨도 같은 길을 따랐다."], ["Mr. Ben Allen’s hat rolled down the steps immediately afterwards, and Mr.", "곧이어 벤 앨런 씨의 모자가 계단 아래로 굴러떨어졌고, 그 뒤를 곧장 벤 앨런 씨의 몸뚱이가 따랐다."], ["Ben Allen’s body followed it directly. The whole party went silent and supperless to bed; and Mr.", "일행은 모두 말없이, 저녁도 거른 채 잠자리에 들었다."], ["Pickwick thought, just before he fell asleep, that if he had known Mr. Winkle, senior, had been quite so much of a man of business, it was extremely probable he might never have waited upon him, on such an errand.", "그리고 픽윅 씨는 잠들기 직전, 노 윈클 씨가 그토록 철저한 사업가인 줄 미리 알았더라면, 그런 용건으로 그를 찾아뵙는 일은 결코 없었으리라는 것이 십중팔구라고 생각했다."]], [["CHAPTER LI. IN WHICH MR.", "픽윅 씨의 눈앞에 여덟 시에 밝아 온 아침은 그의 기분을 북돋우거나, 사절의 임무가 뜻밖의 결과로 끝난 데서 비롯된 침울함을 덜어 줄 만한 것이 전혀 못 되었다."], ["PICKWICK ENCOUNTERS AN OLD ACQUAINTANCE--TO WHICH FORTUNATE CIRCUMSTANCE THE READER IS MAINLY INDEBTED FOR MATTER OF THRILLING INTEREST HEREIN SET DOWN, CONCERNING TWO GREAT PUBLIC MEN OF MIGHT AND POWER\nThe morning which broke upon Mr.", "하늘은 어둡고 음울했고, 공기는 축축하고 쌀쌀했으며, 거리는 젖어 질척거렸다."], ["Pickwick’s sight at eight o’clock, was not at all calculated to elevate his spirits, or to lessen the depression which the unlooked-for result of his embassy inspired. The sky was dark and gloomy, the air was damp and raw, the streets were wet and sloppy.", "연기는 솟아오를 용기조차 없는 듯 굴뚝 꼭대기 위에 굼뜨게 매달려 있었고, 비는 쏟아질 기운마저 없는 듯 느릿느릿하고 끈질기게 내렸다."], ["The smoke hung sluggishly above the chimney-tops as if it lacked the courage to rise, and the rain came slowly and doggedly down, as if it had not even the spirit to pour. A game-cock in the stableyard, deprived of every spark of his accustomed animation, balanced himself dismally on one leg in a corner; a donkey, moping with drooping head under the narrow roof of an outhouse, appeared from his meditative and miserable countenance to be contemplating suicide.", "마구간 마당의 싸움닭은 평소의 활기를 한 톨도 남김없이 빼앗긴 채 구석에서 처량하게 한 다리로 몸을 가누고 있었고, 헛간의 좁은 지붕 아래에서 고개를 떨군 채 멍하니 서 있는 당나귀는 그 사색에 잠긴 비참한 표정으로 보아 자살을 궁리하고 있는 것만 같았다."], ["In the street, umbrellas were the only things to be seen, and the clicking of pattens and splashing of rain-drops were the only sounds to be heard.", "거리에서 보이는 것이라고는 우산뿐이었고, 들리는 소리라고는 나막신 굽이 또각거리는 소리와 빗방울이 튀는 소리뿐이었다."]], [["The breakfast was interrupted by very little conversation; even Mr.", "아침 식사는 대화가 거의 없는 가운데 진행되었다. 보브 소여 씨조차 날씨와 전날의 흥분이 미친 영향을 느꼈다."], ["Bob Sawyer felt the influence of the weather, and the previous day’s excitement.", "그 자신의 표현력 넘치는 말로 하자면, 그는 \"뻗어 버린\" 상태였다."], ["In his own expressive language he was ‘floored.’ So was Mr. Ben Allen.", "벤 앨런 씨도 마찬가지였다."], ["So was Mr. Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨도 마찬가지였다."]], [["In protracted expectation of the weather clearing up, the last evening paper from London was read and re-read with an intensity of interest only known in cases of extreme destitution; every inch of the carpet was walked over with similar perseverance; the windows were looked out of, often enough to justify the imposition of an additional duty upon them; all kinds of topics of conversation were started, and failed; and at length Mr.", "날씨가 갤까 하는 기대를 길게 끌면서, 런던에서 온 지난 저녁 신문을 극도의 궁핍한 처지에서나 알 법한 강렬한 관심을 가지고 읽고 또 읽었다. 카펫의 한 치 한 치를 그와 비슷한 끈기로 밟고 다녔으며, 창문에는 추가 세금을 매길 만한 정당한 구실이 될 만큼 자주 내다보았다."], ["Pickwick, when noon had arrived, without a change for the better, rang the bell resolutely, and ordered out the chaise.", "온갖 종류의 대화 화제가 시작되었다가 실패했다. 마침내 정오가 되어도 나아질 기미가 없자, 픽윅 씨는 단호히 종을 울려 마차를 대령시키라고 명했다."]], [["Although the roads were miry, and the drizzling rain came down harder than it had done yet, and although the mud and wet splashed in at the open windows of the carriage to such an extent that the discomfort was almost as great to the pair of insides as to the pair of outsides, still there was something in the motion, and the sense of being up and doing, which was so infinitely superior to being pent in a dull room, looking at the dull rain dripping into a dull street, that they all agreed, on starting, that the change was a great improvement, and wondered how they could possibly have delayed making it as long as they had done.", "길은 진창이었고, 보슬보슬 내리던 비는 지금까지보다 더 거세게 쏟아졌으며, 진흙과 빗물이 열린 마차 창문으로 어찌나 많이 튀어 들어오는지 안에 탄 두 사람의 불편함이 밖에 탄 두 사람의 그것에 거의 맞먹을 지경이었다. 그럼에도 그 움직임, 그리고 자리를 떨치고 일어나 무언가를 하고 있다는 느낌에는 무언가가 있어서, 칙칙한 방에 갇혀 칙칙한 비가 칙칙한 거리로 떨어지는 것을 바라보는 것보다 한없이 나았다. 그래서 일행은 출발하면서 이 변화가 대단한 개선이라는 데 모두 동의했고, 어째서 여태껏 이렇게 오래 미룰 수 있었던 것인지 의아해했다."]], [["When they stopped to change at Coventry, the steam ascended from the horses in such clouds as wholly to obscure the hostler, whose voice was however heard to declare from the mist, that he expected the first gold medal from the Humane Society on their next distribution of rewards, for taking the postboy’s hat off; the water descending from the brim of which, the invisible gentleman declared, must have drowned him (the postboy), but for his great presence of mind in tearing it promptly from his head, and drying the gasping man’s countenance with a wisp of straw.", "코번트리에서 말을 바꾸려고 멈췄을 때, 말들에서 김이 어찌나 구름처럼 피어오르는지 마구간지기의 모습이 온통 가려져 보이지 않았다. 다만 안개 속에서 그의 목소리가 들려왔는데, 자기는 다음번 인도주의협회 포상 때 첫 금메달을 기대한다는 것이었다. 우편마차 마부의 모자를 벗겨 주었기 때문이라고 했다. 그 보이지 않는 신사가 단언하기를, 모자챙에서 흘러내리던 물 때문에 마부는 (그 마부 말이다) 익사할 뻔했으나, 자기가 침착하게 그 모자를 재빨리 머리에서 잡아채어, 헐떡거리는 그 사내의 얼굴을 짚단 한 줌으로 닦아 준 덕분에 살았다는 것이었다."]], [["‘This is pleasant,’ said Bob Sawyer, turning up his coat collar, and pulling the shawl over his mouth to concentrate the fumes of a glass of brandy just swallowed.", "\"이거 기분 좋군.\" 보브 소여가 외투 깃을 세우고, 방금 삼킨 브랜디 한 잔의 김을 모으려고 숄을 입까지 끌어올리며 말했다."]], [["‘Wery,’ replied Sam composedly.", "\"그렇구먼유.\" 샘이 차분하게 대답했다."]], [["‘You don’t seem to mind it,’ observed Bob.", "\"자넨 별로 신경 쓰지 않는 모양이군.\" 보브가 말했다."]], [["‘Vy, I don’t exactly see no good my mindin’ on it ‘ud do, sir,’ replied Sam.", "\"아, 그게유, 제가 신경 쓴다고 무슨 좋은 일이 생길 것 같지가 않아서유, 나리.\" 샘이 대답했다."]], [["‘That’s an unanswerable reason, anyhow,’ said Bob.", "\"하여간 그건 반박할 수 없는 이유로군.\" 보브가 말했다."]], [["‘Yes, sir,’ rejoined Mr.", "\"맞습니다, 나리.\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 받았다."], ["‘Wotever is, is right, as the young nobleman sweetly remarked wen they put him down in the pension list ‘cos his mother’s uncle’s vife’s grandfather vunce lit the king’s pipe vith a portable tinder-box.’", "\"있는 그대로가 옳은 거지유, 어느 젊은 귀족 양반이 다정하게 말했듯이 말입니다. 그 양반 어머니의 삼촌의 부인의 할아버지가 옛날에 휴대용 부싯깃통으로 임금님 담뱃대에 불을 붙여 드린 적이 있다는 이유로 연금 명단에 그 양반을 올려 줬을 때 말이지유.\""]], [["Not a bad notion that, Sam,’ said Mr.", "\"그거 나쁘지 않은 생각이군, 샘.\""], ["Bob Sawyer approvingly.", "보브 소여 씨가 흡족하게 말했다."]], [["‘Just wot the young nobleman said ev’ry quarter-day arterwards for the rest of his life,’ replied Mr.", "\"그 젊은 귀족 양반이 그 후로 평생 분기 결산일마다 했던 말이 바로 그거였지유.\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘Wos you ever called in,’ inquired Sam, glancing at the driver, after a short silence, and lowering his voice to a mysterious whisper--‘wos you ever called in, when you wos ‘prentice to a sawbones, to wisit a postboy.’", "\"나리는 말입니다.\" 잠시 침묵한 뒤 샘이 마부를 흘끗 보고는, 목소리를 신비스러운 속삭임으로 낮추며 물었다. \"나리가 의사 양반 밑에서 견습 시절에, 우편마차 마부 진찰하러 불려 간 적 있으셨어유?\""]], [["‘I don’t remember that I ever was,’ replied Bob Sawyer.", "\"그런 적은 없었던 것 같은데.\" 보브 소여가 대답했다."]], [["‘You never see a postboy in that ‘ere hospital as you walked (as they says o’ the ghosts), did you?’ demanded Sam.", "\"그 병원에서 (유령에 대해 말할 때처럼) 어슬렁대는 우편마차 마부를 본 적도 없으시지유?\" 샘이 물었다."]], [["‘No,’ replied Bob Sawyer.", "\"없어.\" 보브 소여가 대답했다."], ["‘I don’t think I ever did.’", "\"그런 적 없는 것 같아.\""]], [["‘Never know’d a churchyard were there wos a postboy’s tombstone, or see a dead postboy, did you?’ inquired Sam, pursuing his catechism.", "\"우편마차 마부의 묘비가 있는 묘지를 아신 적도 없고, 죽은 마부를 보신 적도 없으시지유?\" 샘이 문답을 이어 가며 물었다."]], [["‘No,’ rejoined Bob, ‘I never did.’", "\"없어.\" 보브가 받았다. \"그런 적 없어.\""]], [["‘No!’ rejoined Sam triumphantly.", "\"없지유!\" 샘이 의기양양하게 받았다."], ["‘Nor never vill; and there’s another thing that no man never see, and that’s a dead donkey.", "\"앞으로도 없으실 겁니다. 그리고 아무도 본 적 없는 게 또 하나 있는데, 그게 바로 죽은 당나귀입니다."], ["No man never see a dead donkey ‘cept the gen’l’m’n in the black silk smalls as know’d the young ‘ooman as kep’ a goat; and that wos a French donkey, so wery likely he warn’t wun o’ the reg’lar breed.’", "죽은 당나귀를 본 사람은 아무도 없어유, 검은 비단 반바지를 입고 염소 치는 젊은 아가씨를 알던 그 신사 양반 빼고는유. 그것도 프랑스 당나귀였으니, 십중팔구 정통 혈통은 아니었을 겁니다.\""]], [["‘Well, what has that got to do with the postboys?’ asked Bob Sawyer.", "\"그래, 그게 우편마차 마부랑 무슨 상관인데?\" 보브 소여가 물었다."]], [["‘This here,’ replied Sam. ‘Without goin’ so far as to as-sert, as some wery sensible people do, that postboys and donkeys is both immortal, wot I say is this: that wenever they feels theirselves gettin’ stiff and past their work, they just rides off together, wun postboy to a pair in the usual way; wot becomes on ‘em nobody knows, but it’s wery probable as they starts avay to take their pleasure in some other vorld, for there ain’t a man alive as ever see either a donkey or a postboy a- takin’ his pleasure in this!’", "\"이런 겁니다.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"아주 분별 있는 사람들이 그러는 것처럼 우편마차 마부랑 당나귀가 둘 다 불사신이라고까지 주장하지는 않겠지만, 제가 말하고 싶은 건 이겁니다. 그것들은 자기가 뻣뻣해지고 일에서 한물갔다 싶으면 둘이 함께 떠나 버린다, 이겁니다. 늘 그렇듯이 한 쌍에 마부 하나꼴로 말이지유. 그것들이 어떻게 되는지는 아무도 모르지만, 십중팔구 다른 세상에서 즐기려고 떠나는 거지유. 왜냐하면 이 세상에서 당나귀든 우편마차 마부든 즐기는 꼴을 본 사람은 살아 있는 한 아무도 없으니까유!\""]], [["Expatiating upon this learned and remarkable theory, and citing many curious statistical and other facts in its support, Sam Weller beguiled the time until they reached Dunchurch, where a dry postboy and fresh horses were procured; the next stage was Daventry, and the next Towcester; and at the end of each stage it rained harder than it had done at the beginning.", "이 박식하고 주목할 만한 이론을 길게 늘어놓으며, 그것을 뒷받침할 진기한 통계며 여러 사실을 인용하면서, 샘 웰러는 던처치에 닿을 때까지 시간을 잘도 보냈다. 그곳에서 마른 마부와 싱싱한 말들을 구했다. 다음 역참은 데번트리였고, 그다음은 토스터였다. 그리고 각 역참 끝에 다다를 때마다 비는 처음 출발할 때보다 더 거세게 내렸다."]], [["‘I say,’ remonstrated Bob Sawyer, looking in at the coach window, as they pulled up before the door of the Saracen’s Head, Towcester, ‘this won’t do, you know.’", "\"이봐요.\" 토스터의 새러슨스 헤드 여관 문 앞에 마차를 세웠을 때, 보브 소여가 마차 창문 안을 들여다보며 항의했다. \"이래선 안 되겠어요, 아시겠어요.\""]], [["‘Bless me!’ said Mr.", "\"이런!\""], ["Pickwick, just awakening from a nap, ‘I’m afraid you’re wet.’", "막 선잠에서 깨어난 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"젖으신 모양이군요.\""]], [["‘Oh, you are, are you?’ returned Bob.", "\"아, 그러시군요, 그렇죠?\" 보브가 받았다."], ["‘Yes, I am, a little that way, Uncomfortably damp, perhaps.’", "\"예, 좀 그렇긴 합니다. 어쩌면 불편할 만큼 축축할지도요.\""]], [["Bob did look dampish, inasmuch as the rain was streaming from his neck, elbows, cuffs, skirts, and knees; and his whole apparel shone so with the wet, that it might have been mistaken for a full suit of prepared oilskin.", "보브는 정말 축축해 보였으니, 목이며 팔꿈치며 소맷부리며 옷자락이며 무릎에서 비가 줄줄 흘러내렸고, 옷차림 전체가 젖어 어찌나 번들거리는지 무두질한 방수포 한 벌로 오해받을 만했다."]], [["‘I am rather wet,’ said Bob, giving himself a shake and casting a little hydraulic shower around, like a Newfoundland dog just emerged from the water.", "\"제가 좀 젖긴 했네요.\" 보브가 몸을 한 번 부르르 떨어, 막 물에서 나온 뉴펀들랜드 개처럼 자그마한 물줄기를 사방으로 흩뿌리며 말했다."]], [["‘I think it’s quite impossible to go on to-night,’ interposed Ben.", "\"오늘 밤에 더 가는 건 도저히 불가능하다고 봐요.\" 벤이 끼어들었다."]], [["‘Out of the question, sir,’ remarked Sam Weller, coming to assist in the conference; ‘it’s a cruelty to animals, sir, to ask ‘em to do it.", "\"말도 안 되지유, 나리.\" 샘 웰러가 의논에 거들려고 다가오며 한마디 했다."], ["There’s beds here, sir,’ said Sam, addressing his master, ‘everything clean and comfortable.", "\"짐승들한테 그걸 시키는 건 학대입니다요, 나리. 여기 침대도 있고유, 나리,\""], ["Wery good little dinner, sir, they can get ready in half an hour--pair of fowls, sir, and a weal cutlet; French beans, ‘taturs, tart, and tidiness.", "주인을 향해 샘이 말했다. \"다 깨끗하고 편안합니다."], ["You’d better stop vere you are, sir, if I might recommend.", "아주 괜찮은 자그마한 저녁 식사를 삼십 분이면 준비해 준다는데유, 나리. 닭 한 쌍에 송아지 커틀릿, 강낭콩에 감자, 타르트에 깔끔함까지지유."], ["Take adwice, sir, as the doctor said.’", "제가 권해도 된다면, 나리, 그냥 여기 머무시는 게 좋겠습니다. 의사 양반 말마따나 충고를 받아들이시지유, 나리.\""]], [["The host of the Saracen’s Head opportunely appeared at this moment, to confirm Mr.", "마침 이 순간 새러슨스 헤드의 주인이 때맞춰 나타나, 이 여관의 숙박 시설에 관한 웰러 씨의 말을 확인해 주었다."], ["Weller’s statement relative to the accommodations of the establishment, and to back his entreaties with a variety of dismal conjectures regarding the state of the roads, the doubt of fresh horses being to be had at the next stage, the dead certainty of its raining all night, the equally mortal certainty of its clearing up in the morning, and other topics of inducement familiar to innkeepers.", "그리고 길 사정에 관한 갖가지 음울한 추측, 다음 역참에서 싱싱한 말을 구할 수 있을지 의심스럽다는 것, 밤새 비가 올 게 확실하다는 것, 아침이면 갤 게 그만큼 확실하다는 것, 그 밖에 여관 주인들이 흔히 쓰는 유인책을 동원하여 그의 간청을 거들었다."]], [["‘Well,’ said Mr. Pickwick; ‘but I must send a letter to London by some conveyance, so that it may be delivered the very first thing in the morning, or I must go forwards at all hazards.’", "\"좋소.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"하지만 무슨 수를 써서든 런던으로 편지를 한 통 보내야겠소. 내일 아침 제일 먼저 배달되도록 말이오. 그게 아니면 무슨 위험을 무릅쓰고서라도 앞으로 가야 하오.\""]], [["The landlord smiled his delight.", "여관 주인은 기쁨에 차 미소 지었다."], ["Nothing could be easier than for the gentleman to inclose a letter in a sheet of brown paper, and send it on, either by the mail or the night coach from Birmingham.", "그 신사분이 편지를 갈색 종이로 싸서, 버밍엄에서 오는 우편마차나 야간 마차 편에 부치는 것보다 더 쉬운 일은 없다는 것이었다."], ["If the gentleman were particularly anxious to have it left as soon as possible, he might write outside, ‘To be delivered immediately,’ which was sure to be attended to; or ‘Pay the bearer half-a-crown extra for instant delivery,’ which was surer still.", "신사분이 되도록 빨리 전달되기를 특별히 원하신다면, 겉면에 \"즉시 배달 요망\"이라고 쓰면 되는데, 그러면 틀림없이 처리될 것이고, 또는 \"즉시 배달 시 배달부에게 반 크라운 추가 지급\"이라고 쓰면 더더욱 확실하다고 했다."]], [["‘Very well,’ said Mr.", "\"아주 좋소.\""], ["Pickwick, ‘then we will stop here.’", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"그럼 여기서 머물겠소.\""]], [["‘Lights in the Sun, John; make up the fire; the gentlemen are wet!’ cried the landlord.", "\"선룸에 불 켜라, 존. 난로 지피고."], ["‘This way, gentlemen; don’t trouble yourselves about the postboy now, sir.", "신사분들 젖으셨다!\" 여관 주인이 외쳤다."], ["I’ll send him to you when you ring for him, sir.", "\"이쪽으로 오시지요, 신사분들. 마부는 지금 신경 쓰지 마십시오, 나리."], ["Now, John, the candles.’", "부르시면 보내 드리겠습니다, 나리. 자, 존, 촛불 가져와.\""]], [["The candles were brought, the fire was stirred up, and a fresh log of wood thrown on.", "촛불을 가져오고, 난로를 헤집어 새 장작 한 토막을 던져 넣었다."], ["In ten minutes’ time, a waiter was laying the cloth for dinner, the curtains were drawn, the fire was blazing brightly, and everything looked (as everything always does, in all decent English inns) as if the travellers had been expected, and their comforts prepared, for days beforehand.", "십 분쯤 지나자 종업원 하나가 저녁 식탁보를 깔고, 커튼을 치고, 난로가 활활 타올라, 모든 것이 (점잖은 영국 여관이라면 어디서나 늘 그렇듯이) 마치 여러 날 전부터 여행객들을 기다리며 그들의 편의를 준비해 둔 것처럼 보였다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick sat down at a side table, and hastily indited a note to Mr. Winkle, merely informing him that he was detained by stress of weather, but would certainly be in London next day; until when he deferred any account of his proceedings. This note was hastily made into a parcel, and despatched to the bar per Mr. Samuel Weller.", "픽윅 씨는 한쪽 곁상에 앉아 윈클 씨에게 보내는 쪽지를 서둘러 적었는데, 단지 자기가 날씨 탓에 발이 묶였으나 내일은 틀림없이 런던에 도착할 것이라고만 알렸을 뿐, 그때까지는 자신이 한 일에 관한 어떤 보고도 미뤄 두었다. 이 쪽지는 부랴부랴 꾸러미로 만들어져, 새뮤얼 웰러 씨를 통해 카운터로 보내졌다."]], [["Sam left it with the landlady, and was returning to pull his master’s boots off, after drying himself by the kitchen fire, when glancing casually through a half-opened door, he was arrested by the sight of a gentleman with a sandy head who had a large bundle of newspapers lying on the table before him, and was perusing the leading article of one with a settled sneer which curled up his nose and all other features into a majestic expression of haughty contempt.", "샘은 그것을 여주인에게 맡기고, 부엌 난롯불에 몸을 말린 뒤 주인의 장화를 벗겨 드리려고 돌아가던 참이었다. 그때 반쯤 열린 문 안을 무심코 흘끗 보다가, 모래빛 머리를 한 어떤 신사의 모습에 발길을 멈추었다. 그 신사는 앞에 놓인 탁자 위에 신문 한 무더기를 쌓아 두고, 그중 한 신문의 사설을 읽고 있었는데, 코며 다른 모든 이목구비를 한껏 치켜올려 오만한 경멸이 깃든 위풍당당한 표정을 짓는, 고정된 비웃음을 띠고 있었다."]], [["‘Hollo!’ said Sam, ‘I ought to know that ‘ere head and them features; the eyeglass, too, and the broad-brimmed tile! Eatansvill to vit, or I’m a Roman.’", "\"아니!\" 샘이 말했다. \"저 머리랑 저 얼굴 생김새는 내가 알 만한데. 저 외알 안경이랑, 챙 넓은 모자까지! 이턴스윌이라 이거지, 아니면 내가 로마 사람이게.\""]], [["Sam was taken with a troublesome cough, at once, for the purpose of attracting the gentleman’s attention; the gentleman starting at the sound, raised his head and his eyeglass, and disclosed to view the profound and thoughtful features of Mr.", "샘은 곧장 성가신 기침병이 든 척했으니, 그 신사의 주의를 끌기 위함이었다."], ["Pott, of the Eatanswill Gazette.", "그 소리에 놀란 신사는 고개와 외알 안경을 들어 올렸고, 이턴스윌 가제트의 깊고 사색적인 얼굴, 곧 포트 씨의 얼굴을 드러내 보였다."]], [["‘Beggin’ your pardon, sir,’ said Sam, advancing with a bow, ‘my master’s here, Mr.", "\"실례합니다요, 나리.\" 샘이 절을 하며 다가가 말했다."], ["Pott.’", "\"제 주인이 여기 와 계십니다, 포트 씨.\""]], [["‘Hush!", "\"쉿!"], ["hush!’ cried Pott, drawing Sam into the room, and closing the door, with a countenance of mysterious dread and apprehension.", "쉿!\" 포트가 신비스러운 공포와 불안의 표정을 지으며 샘을 방 안으로 끌어들이고는 문을 닫으며 외쳤다."]], [["‘Wot’s the matter, Sir?’ inquired Sam, looking vacantly about him.", "\"무슨 일이십니까요, 나리?\" 샘이 멍하니 주위를 둘러보며 물었다."]], [["‘Not a whisper of my name,’ replied Pott; ‘this is a buff neighbourhood.", "\"내 이름은 한 마디도 속삭이지 말게.\" 포트가 대답했다."], ["If the excited and irritable populace knew I was here, I should be torn to pieces.’", "\"여긴 버프파 동네야. 흥분 잘하고 성질 급한 군중이 내가 여기 있는 걸 알면, 난 갈가리 찢기고 말 걸세.\""]], [["‘No!", "\"그래유!"], ["Vould you, sir?’ inquired Sam.", "그러시겠어유, 나리?\" 샘이 물었다."]], [["‘I should be the victim of their fury,’ replied Pott.", "\"난 그들의 분노의 희생양이 될 거야.\" 포트가 대답했다."], ["‘Now young man, what of your master?’", "\"자, 젊은이, 자네 주인은 무슨 일인가?\""]], [["‘He’s a-stopping here to-night on his vay to town, with a couple of friends,’ replied Sam.", "\"두 분 친구와 함께 도시로 가시는 길에 오늘 밤 여기 묵으십니다요.\" 샘이 대답했다."]], [["‘Is Mr.", "\"윈클 씨도 그중 한 사람인가?\""], ["Winkle one of them?’ inquired Pott, with a slight frown.", "포트가 살짝 인상을 찌푸리며 물었다."]], [["‘No, Sir. Mr.", "\"아닙니다요, 나리."], ["Vinkle stops at home now,’ rejoined Sam.", "윈클 씨는 이제 집에 계세유.\" 샘이 받았다."], ["‘He’s married.’", "\"결혼하셨거든유.\""]], [["‘Married!’ exclaimed Pott, with frightful vehemence.", "\"결혼!\" 포트가 무시무시한 격정으로 외쳤다."], ["He stopped, smiled darkly, and added, in a low, vindictive tone, ‘It serves him right!’", "그는 말을 멈추고 음침하게 미소 지으며, 낮고 앙심에 찬 어조로 덧붙였다. \"그 친구한테는 그게 딱 어울리는 꼴이지!\""]], [["Having given vent to this cruel ebullition of deadly malice and cold- blooded triumph over a fallen enemy, Mr. Pott inquired whether Mr.", "쓰러진 적에 대한 이 잔인한 악의의 분출과 냉혹한 승리감을 토해 낸 뒤, 포트 씨는 픽윅 씨의 친구들이 \"블루파\"인지 물었다."], ["Pickwick’s friends were ‘blue?’ Receiving a most satisfactory answer in the affirmative from Sam, who knew as much about the matter as Pott himself, he consented to accompany him to Mr.", "그 문제에 대해 포트 자신만큼이나 잘 아는 샘에게서 더없이 만족스러운 긍정의 대답을 듣고, 그는 샘을 따라 픽윅 씨의 방으로 가는 데 동의했다."], ["Pickwick’s room, where a hearty welcome awaited him, and an agreement to club their dinners together was at once made and ratified.", "그곳에서는 진심 어린 환영이 그를 기다리고 있었고, 저녁 식사를 함께 하기로 하는 합의가 즉석에서 이루어지고 확정되었다."]], [["‘And how are matters going on in Eatanswill?’ inquired Mr.", "\"그래, 이턴스윌은 사정이 어떻게 돌아가고 있소?\""], ["Pickwick, when Pott had taken a seat near the fire, and the whole party had got their wet boots off, and dry slippers on.", "포트가 난로 가까이 자리를 잡고, 일행 모두가 젖은 장화를 벗고 마른 슬리퍼를 신은 뒤, 픽윅 씨가 물었다."], ["‘Is the Independent still in being?’", "\"인디펜던트는 아직도 명맥을 잇고 있소?\""]], [["‘The Independent, sir,’ replied Pott, ‘is still dragging on a wretched and lingering career. Abhorred and despised by even the few who are cognisant of its miserable and disgraceful existence, stifled by the very filth it so profusely scatters, rendered deaf and blind by the exhalations of its own slime, the obscene journal, happily unconscious of its degraded state, is rapidly sinking beneath that treacherous mud which, while it seems to give it a firm standing with the low and debased classes of society, is nevertheless rising above its detested head, and will speedily engulf it for ever.’", "\"인디펜던트 말입니까, 나리.\" 포트가 대답했다. \"그건 여전히 비참하고 질질 끄는 명줄을 끌고 있습니다. 그 비참하고 수치스러운 존재를 아는 몇 안 되는 자들에게마저 혐오와 멸시를 받고, 제가 그토록 흥청망청 뿌려 대는 그 오물에 질식하고, 제 진창에서 나는 독기에 눈멀고 귀먹어, 그 외설스러운 신문은 다행히도 제 비천한 처지를 의식하지 못한 채, 그 음흉한 진흙 밑으로 빠르게 가라앉고 있지요. 그 진흙은 사회의 천하고 타락한 계층 사이에서 그것에게 굳건한 발판을 주는 것처럼 보이지만, 그럼에도 그 가증스러운 머리 위로 차오르고 있어 머지않아 그것을 영영 삼켜 버릴 것입니다.\""]], [["Having delivered this manifesto (which formed a portion of his last week’s leader) with vehement articulation, the editor paused to take breath, and looked majestically at Bob Sawyer.", "(그가 지난주 사설의 일부였던) 이 선언문을 격렬한 어조로 토해 낸 뒤, 편집장은 숨을 고르려고 잠시 멈추고는 위풍당당하게 보브 소여를 바라보았다."]], [["‘You are a young man, sir,’ said Pott.", "\"자네는 젊은이로군.\" 포트가 말했다."]], [["Mr. Bob Sawyer nodded.", "보브 소여 씨가 고개를 끄덕였다."]], [["‘So are you, sir,’ said Pott, addressing Mr.", "\"자네도 마찬가지고.\""], ["Ben Allen.", "벤 앨런 씨를 향해 포트가 말했다."]], [["Ben admitted the soft impeachment.", "벤이 그 부드러운 지적을 인정했다."]], [["‘And are both deeply imbued with those blue principles, which, so long as I live, I have pledged myself to the people of these kingdoms to support and to maintain?’ suggested Pott.", "\"그리고 두 사람 다, 내가 살아 있는 한 이 왕국의 국민들에게 지지하고 지켜 나가겠노라 맹세한 그 블루파의 신조에 깊이 물들어 있겠지?\" 포트가 넌지시 물었다."]], [["‘Why, I don’t exactly know about that,’ replied Bob Sawyer.", "\"글쎄요, 그건 정확히 잘 모르겠는데요.\" 보브 소여가 대답했다."], ["‘I am--’", "\"저는—\""]], [["‘Not buff, Mr.", "\"버프파는 아니지요, 픽윅 씨.\""], ["Pickwick,’ interrupted Pott, drawing back his chair, ‘your friend is not buff, sir?’", "포트가 의자를 뒤로 빼며 끼어들었다. \"당신 친구가 버프파는 아니겠지요, 나리?\""]], [["‘No, no,’ rejoined Bob, ‘I’m a kind of plaid at present; a compound of all sorts of colours.’", "\"아니, 아닙니다.\" 보브가 받았다. \"전 지금 일종의 격자무늬랄까요, 온갖 색이 뒤섞인 거랄까요.\""]], [["‘A waverer,’ said Pott solemnly, ‘a waverer.", "\"줏대 없는 사람이로군.\" 포트가 엄숙하게 말했다."], ["I should like to show you a series of eight articles, Sir, that have appeared in the Eatanswill Gazette.", "\"줏대 없는 사람. 자네에게 이턴스윌 가제트에 실린 여덟 편의 연재 기사를 보여 주고 싶군, 나리."], ["I think I may venture to say that you would not be long in establishing your opinions on a firm and solid blue basis, sir.’", "감히 말하건대, 자네는 머지않아 자네 견해를 굳건하고 견실한 블루파의 토대 위에 세우게 될 거라고 보네, 나리.\""]], [["I dare say I should turn very blue, long before I got to the end of them,’ responded Bob.", "\"그 끝까지 다 읽기도 훨씬 전에 제가 새파랗게 질려 버릴 것 같은데요.\" 보브가 응수했다."]], [["Mr. Pott looked dubiously at Bob Sawyer for some seconds, and, turning to Mr. Pickwick, said--", "* 블루(blue)에는 '블루파'라는 뜻과 '우울하다·새파랗다'는 뜻이 함께 있어 말장난이 된다."]], [["‘You have seen the literary articles which have appeared at intervals in the Eatanswill Gazette in the course of the last three months, and which have excited such general--I may say such universal--attention and admiration?’", "포트 씨는 몇 초 동안 보브 소여를 미심쩍게 바라보다가, 픽윅 씨에게 고개를 돌리고 말했다. \"지난 석 달간 이턴스윌 가제트에 간간이 실려, 그토록 널리—아니, 보편적이라 해도 좋겠습니다만—주목과 찬사를 불러일으킨 그 문예 기사들을 보셨겠지요?\""]], [["‘Why,’ replied Mr.", "\"글쎄요.\""], ["Pickwick, slightly embarrassed by the question, ‘the fact is, I have been so much engaged in other ways, that I really have not had an opportunity of perusing them.’", "그 질문에 약간 당황하여 픽윅 씨가 대답했다. \"실은, 제가 다른 일들에 워낙 매여 있었던 탓에, 정말이지 그것을 읽어 볼 기회가 없었습니다.\""]], [["‘You should do so, Sir,’ said Pott, with a severe countenance.", "\"꼭 읽으셔야 합니다, 나리.\" 포트가 근엄한 얼굴로 말했다."]], [["‘I will,’ said Mr.", "\"읽겠습니다.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘They appeared in the form of a copious review of a work on Chinese metaphysics, Sir,’ said Pott.", "\"그것은 중국 형이상학을 다룬 어떤 저작에 대한 방대한 서평의 형식으로 실렸지요, 나리.\" 포트가 말했다."]], [["‘Oh,’ observed Mr.", "\"아.\" 픽윅 씨가 한마디 했다."], ["Pickwick; ‘from your pen, I hope?’", "\"당신 펜에서 나온 것이길 바랍니다만?\""]], [["‘From the pen of my critic, Sir,’ rejoined Pott, with dignity.", "\"제 평론가의 펜에서 나온 것입니다, 나리.\" 포트가 위엄 있게 받았다."]], [["‘An abstruse subject, I should conceive,’ said Mr.", "\"꽤 난해한 주제일 텐데요.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Very, Sir,’ responded Pott, looking intensely sage.", "\"아주 그렇지요, 나리.\" 포트가 한껏 현명한 표정을 지으며 응답했다."], ["‘He crammed for it, to use a technical but expressive term; he read up for the subject, at my desire, in the “Encyclopaedia Britannica.”’", "\"그는, 전문적이지만 적절한 표현을 쓰자면, 그걸 위해 '벼락공부'를 했습니다. 제 청에 따라 '브리태니커 백과사전'에서 그 주제를 미리 읽어 두었지요.\""]], [["‘Indeed!’ said Mr.", "\"그렇군요!\""], ["Pickwick; ‘I was not aware that that valuable work contained any information respecting Chinese metaphysics.’", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"그 귀중한 저작에 중국 형이상학에 관한 정보가 들어 있는 줄은 몰랐습니다.\""]], [["‘He read, Sir,’ rejoined Pott, laying his hand on Mr.", "\"그는 말입니다, 나리.\""], ["Pickwick’s knee, and looking round with a smile of intellectual superiority--‘he read for metaphysics under the letter M, and for China under the letter C, and combined his information, Sir!’", "포트가 픽윅 씨의 무릎에 손을 얹고는 지적 우월감이 깃든 미소를 띠며 둘러보고 받았다. \"그는 형이상학(Metaphysics)은 M 항목에서, 중국(China)은 C 항목에서 읽고, 그 정보를 합친 겁니다, 나리!\""]], [["Mr. Pott’s features assumed so much additional grandeur at the recollection of the power and research displayed in the learned effusions in question, that some minutes elapsed before Mr.", "문제의 그 박식한 글에 발휘된 역량과 연구의 기억에 포트 씨의 얼굴이 어찌나 한층 위풍당당해졌는지, 픽윅 씨가 대화를 다시 이어 갈 엄두를 내기까지 몇 분이 흘렀다."], ["Pickwick felt emboldened to renew the conversation; at length, as the editor’s countenance gradually relaxed into its customary expression of moral supremacy, he ventured to resume the discourse by asking--", "마침내 편집장의 얼굴이 차츰 평소의 도덕적 우월감 어린 표정으로 풀어지자, 픽윅 씨는 이렇게 물으며 용기를 내어 대화를 재개했다."]], [["‘Is it fair to inquire what great object has brought you so far from home?’", "\"이렇게 멀리까지 집을 떠나오시게 한 위대한 목적이 무엇인지 여쭤도 되겠습니까?\""]], [["‘That object which actuates and animates me in all my gigantic labours, Sir,’ replied Pott, with a calm smile: ‘my country’s good.’", "\"제 모든 거대한 노고에서 저를 움직이고 분발케 하는 그 목적이지요, 나리.\" 포트가 차분한 미소를 띠며 대답했다. \"나라의 안녕 말입니다.\""]], [["I supposed it was some public mission,’ observed Mr.", "\"무슨 공적 사명인가 보다고 짐작했습니다.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 한마디 했다."]], [["‘Yes, Sir,’ resumed Pott, ‘it is.’ Here, bending towards Mr. Pickwick, he whispered in a deep, hollow voice, ‘A Buff ball, Sir, will take place in Birmingham to-morrow evening.’", "\"그렇습니다, 나리.\" 포트가 다시 말을 이었다. \"바로 그렇지요.\" 여기서 그는 픽윅 씨 쪽으로 몸을 숙여, 깊고 공허한 목소리로 속삭였다. \"내일 저녁 버밍엄에서 버프파 무도회가 열립니다, 나리.\""]], [["‘God bless me!’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"맙소사!\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 외쳤다."]], [["‘Yes, Sir, and supper,’ added Pott.", "\"그렇습니다, 나리, 게다가 만찬까지 곁들여서요.\""], ["‘You don’t say so!’ ejaculated Mr. Pickwick.", "포트가 덧붙였다."]], [["Pott nodded portentously.", "\"설마요!\" 픽윅 씨가 내뱉었다."]], [["Now, although Mr.", "포트가 불길하게 고개를 끄덕였다."], ["Pickwick feigned to stand aghast at this disclosure, he was so little versed in local politics that he was unable to form an adequate comprehension of the importance of the dire conspiracy it referred to; observing which, Mr.", "그런데 픽윅 씨는 이 폭로에 경악한 척했지만, 사실 지역 정치에 워낙 어두웠던 터라 그것이 가리키는 끔찍한 음모의 중대성을 제대로 헤아릴 수가 없었다."], ["Pott, drawing forth the last number of the Eatanswill Gazette, and referring to the same, delivered himself of the following paragraph:--", "이를 알아챈 포트 씨는 이턴스윌 가제트 최근 호를 꺼내 그것을 참조하며 다음 문단을 읊었다."]], [["# HOLE-AND-CORNER BUFFERY.", "# HOLE-AND-CORNER BUFFERY."]], [["‘A reptile contemporary has recently sweltered forth his black venom in the vain and hopeless attempt of sullying the fair name of our distinguished and excellent representative, the Honourable Mr.", "은밀한 구석에서 벌이는 버프파의 책동."], ["Slumkey-- that Slumkey whom we, long before he gained his present noble and exalted position, predicted would one day be, as he now is, at once his country’s brightest honour, and her proudest boast: alike her bold defender and her honest pride--our reptile contemporary, we say, has made himself merry, at the expense of a superbly embossed plated coal- scuttle, which has been presented to that glorious man by his enraptured constituents, and towards the purchase of which, the nameless wretch insinuates, the Honourable Mr.", "\"파충류 같은 어느 동업지가 최근 시커먼 독기를 뿜어내며, 우리의 걸출하고 훌륭한 대표이신 존경하는 슬럼키 씨—그분이 지금의 고귀하고 드높은 지위를 얻기 훨씬 전부터 우리가, 언젠가는 지금처럼 나라의 가장 빛나는 영예이자 가장 자랑스러운 자긍심이 되리라고, 그 대담한 수호자이자 정직한 긍지가 되리라고 예언했던 바로 그 슬럼키 씨—의 깨끗한 이름을 더럽히려는 헛되고 가망 없는 시도를 했다. 우리가 말하는 이 파충류 같은 동업지는, 그 영광스러운 인물에게 황홀해진 유권자들이 헌정한, 더없이 훌륭하게 양각 무늬를 넣은 도금 석탄통을 두고 비아냥거렸다."], ["Slumkey himself contributed, through a confidential friend of his butler’s, more than three-fourths of the whole sum subscribed.", "그 이름 없는 비열한은, 존경하는 슬럼키 씨 본인이 자기 집사의 한 막역한 친구를 통해, 모금된 전체 금액의 사분의 삼이 넘는 돈을 그 석탄통 구입에 보탰다고 넌지시 흘렸다."], ["Why, does not the crawling creature see, that even if this be the fact, the Honourable Mr. Slumkey only appears in a still more amiable and radiant light than before, if that be possible?", "아니, 그 기어 다니는 족속은 보지 못하는가, 설령 그것이 사실이라 한들, 존경하는 슬럼키 씨는 (그게 가능하다면) 전보다 한층 더 자애롭고 빛나는 모습으로 보일 따름이라는 것을?"], ["Does not even his obtuseness perceive that this amiable and touching desire to carry out the wishes of the constituent body, must for ever endear him to the hearts and souls of such of his fellow townsmen as are not worse than swine; or, in other words, who are not as debased as our contemporary himself?", "그 둔감한 머리로도 알아채지 못하는가, 유권자단의 바람을 받들고자 하는 이 자애롭고 가슴 뭉클한 열망이야말로, 돼지만도 못하지 않은—다시 말해 우리 동업지 자신만큼 타락하지 않은—그의 동향인들의 마음과 영혼에 그를 영원히 사랑받게 만들 것임을?"], ["But such is the wretched trickery of hole-and- corner Buffery!", "그러나 은밀한 구석에서 벌이는 버프파의 책동이란 이토록 비참한 속임수다!"], ["These are not its only artifices.", "이것이 그들의 유일한 술책도 아니다."], ["Treason is abroad.", "반역이 횡행하고 있다."], ["We boldly state, now that we are goaded to the disclosure, and we throw ourselves on the country and its constables for protection--we boldly state that secret preparations are at this moment in progress for a Buff ball; which is to be held in a Buff town, in the very heart and centre of a Buff population; which is to be conducted by a Buff master of the ceremonies; which is to be attended by four ultra Buff members of Parliament, and the admission to which, is to be by Buff tickets!", "우리는 폭로하라는 자극을 받은 지금, 대담하게 진술하노니, 그리고 보호를 구하여 이 나라와 그 경관들에게 우리 자신을 내맡기노니—우리는 대담하게 진술하노라, 바로 이 순간 버프파 무도회를 위한 은밀한 준비가 진행 중이라는 것을. 그것은 버프파의 도시에서, 버프파 인구의 한복판에서 열릴 것이며, 버프파 무도회 진행자가 주관하고, 네 명의 극단적 버프파 의원이 참석하며, 입장은 버프파 입장권으로만 가능하다!"], ["Does our fiendish contemporary wince?", "우리의 악마 같은 동업지여, 움찔하는가?"], ["Let him writhe, in impotent malice, as we pen the words, We will be there.’", "우리가 이 글을 쓰는 동안 무력한 악의에 차서 몸부림치게 하라, 우리는 그곳에 갈 것이다.\""]], [["‘There, Sir,’ said Pott, folding up the paper quite exhausted, ‘that is the state of the case!’", "\"보십시오, 나리.\" 포트가 완전히 진이 빠져 신문을 접으며 말했다. \"이게 사태의 전모입니다!\""]], [["The landlord and waiter entering at the moment with dinner, caused Mr.", "마침 이때 여관 주인과 종업원이 저녁 식사를 들고 들어오는 바람에, 포트 씨는 자기 목숨이 픽윅 씨의 손에 달려 있고 그의 비밀 엄수에 의존하고 있다는 표시로 손가락을 입술에 갖다 댔다."], ["Pott to lay his finger on his lips, in token that he considered his life in Mr. Pickwick’s hands, and depended on his secrecy.", "이턴스윌 가제트 인용문을 읽는 동안과 그에 뒤이은 토론 동안 불경스럽게도 잠들어 있던 보브 소여 씨와 벤저민 앨런 씨는, 귀에 대고 속삭인 마법의 단어 \"저녁 식사\"라는 한마디에 깨어났다."], ["Messrs. Bob Sawyer and Benjamin Allen, who had irreverently fallen asleep during the reading of the quotation from the Eatanswill Gazette, and the discussion which followed it, were roused by the mere whispering of the talismanic word ‘Dinner’ in their ears; and to dinner they went with good digestion waiting on appetite, and health on both, and a waiter on all three.", "그리고 그들은 식욕에 따르는 좋은 소화력을 갖추고, 둘 다에 따르는 건강을 갖추고, 셋 모두에 따르는 종업원을 갖추고서 저녁 식탁으로 나아갔다."]], [["In the course of the dinner and the sitting which succeeded it, Mr. Pott descending, for a few moments, to domestic topics, informed Mr.", "저녁 식사와 그에 이은 자리가 이어지는 동안, 포트 씨는 잠시 가정사로 화제를 낮추어, 이턴스윌의 공기가 자기 부인에게 맞지 않아, 부인이 잃었던 건강과 기운을 되찾고자 여러 유행하는 온천지를 두루 여행하는 중이라고 픽윅 씨에게 알렸다."], ["Pickwick that the air of Eatanswill not agreeing with his lady, she was then engaged in making a tour of different fashionable watering-places with a view to the recovery of her wonted health and spirits; this was a delicate veiling of the fact that Mrs.", "이는 다음과 같은 사실을 우아하게 가린 것이었다."], ["Pott, acting upon her often- repeated threat of separation, had, in virtue of an arrangement negotiated by her brother, the lieutenant, and concluded by Mr. Pott, permanently retired with the faithful bodyguard upon one moiety or half part of the annual income and profits arising from the editorship and sale of the Eatanswill Gazette.", "포트 부인은 그동안 거듭 위협해 온 별거를, 그녀의 오빠인 중위가 협상하고 포트 씨가 매듭지은 합의에 따라, 충직한 호위병을 거느린 채, 이턴스윌 가제트의 편집과 판매에서 나오는 연간 수입과 이익의 절반에 해당하는 몫을 받기로 하고 영구히 떠나 버렸던 것이다."]], [["While the great Mr. Pott was dwelling upon this and other matters, enlivening the conversation from time to time with various extracts from his own lucubrations, a stern stranger, calling from the window of a stage-coach, outward bound, which halted at the inn to deliver packages, requested to know whether if he stopped short on his journey and remained there for the night, he could be furnished with the necessary accommodation of a bed and bedstead.", "위대한 포트 씨가 이런저런 일들을 늘어놓으며, 이따금 자신의 노작에서 뽑은 갖가지 발췌문으로 대화에 활기를 더하고 있는 동안, 짐 꾸러미를 부려 놓으려고 여관 앞에 멈춰 선, 외곽으로 향하는 역마차 창문에서 어떤 근엄한 낯선 사람이 부르며, 만일 자기가 여정을 중도에 끊고 여기서 하룻밤 묵는다면 침대와 침대틀이라는 꼭 필요한 숙박 편의를 제공받을 수 있겠느냐고 물었다."]], [["‘Certainly, sir,’ replied the landlord.", "\"물론입니다, 나리.\" 여관 주인이 대답했다."]], [["‘I can, can I?’ inquired the stranger, who seemed habitually suspicious in look and manner.", "\"그럴 수 있다, 그 말이지?\" 외모며 태도가 습관적으로 의심에 차 보이는 그 낯선 사람이 물었다."]], [["‘No doubt of it, Sir,’ replied the landlord.", "\"의심의 여지가 없습니다, 나리.\" 여관 주인이 대답했다."]], [["‘Good,’ said the stranger.", "\"좋소.\" 낯선 사람이 말했다."], ["‘Coachman, I get down here.", "\"마부, 나 여기서 내리겠소."], ["Guard, my carpet- bag!’", "차장, 내 융단 가방!\""]], [["Bidding the other passengers good-night, in a rather snappish manner, the stranger alighted.", "다른 승객들에게 다소 퉁명스러운 투로 잘 자라는 인사를 건네고, 낯선 사람은 내렸다. 그는 약간 키가 작은 신사로, 호저나 구두솔 양식으로 깎은 아주 뻣뻣한 검은 머리카락이 머리 전체에 곧추서 빳빳하게 솟아 있었다."], ["He was a shortish gentleman, with very stiff black hair cut in the porcupine or blacking-brush style, and standing stiff and straight all over his head; his aspect was pompous and threatening; his manner was peremptory; his eyes were sharp and restless; and his whole bearing bespoke a feeling of great confidence in himself, and a consciousness of immeasurable superiority over all other people.", "그 인상은 거만하고 위협적이었으며, 태도는 단호했고, 두 눈은 날카롭고 잠시도 가만있질 못했다. 그리고 그 거동 전체가 자기 자신에 대한 대단한 자신감과, 다른 모든 사람들에 대한 헤아릴 수 없는 우월감의 의식을 드러내 보였다."]], [["This gentleman was shown into the room originally assigned to the patriotic Mr.", "이 신사는 애초에 애국자 포트 씨에게 배정되었던 방으로 안내되었다."], ["Pott; and the waiter remarked, in dumb astonishment at the singular coincidence, that he had no sooner lighted the candles than the gentleman, diving into his hat, drew forth a newspaper, and began to read it with the very same expression of indignant scorn, which, upon the majestic features of Pott, had paralysed his energies an hour before.", "그런데 종업원은, 그 기묘한 우연의 일치에 말없이 놀라며, 그가 촛불을 켜자마자 신사가 모자 속으로 손을 집어넣어 신문 한 부를 꺼내더니, 한 시간 전 포트의 위풍당당한 얼굴에 떠올라 그의 기운을 마비시켰던 것과 똑같은 분노 어린 경멸의 표정을 지으며 그것을 읽기 시작했다고 말했다."], ["The man observed too, that, whereas Mr. Pott’s scorn had been roused by a newspaper headed the Eatanswill Independent, this gentleman’s withering contempt was awakened by a newspaper entitled the Eatanswill Gazette.", "종업원은 또 이런 점도 알아챘으니, 포트 씨의 경멸은 이턴스윌 인디펜던트라는 제호의 신문에 의해 촉발된 반면, 이 신사의 신랄한 경멸은 이턴스윌 가제트라는 제목의 신문에 의해 일깨워졌다는 것이었다."]], [["‘Send the landlord,’ said the stranger.", "\"여관 주인을 보내게.\" 낯선 사람이 말했다."]], [["‘Yes, sir,’ rejoined the waiter.", "\"예, 나리.\" 종업원이 받았다."]], [["The landlord was sent, and came.", "여관 주인이 불려 왔다."]], [["‘Are you the landlord?’ inquired the gentleman.", "\"당신이 여관 주인이오?\" 신사가 물었다."]], [["‘I am sir,’ replied the landlord.", "\"그렇습니다, 나리.\" 여관 주인이 대답했다."]], [["‘Do you know me?’ demanded the gentleman.", "\"날 아시오?\" 신사가 따져 물었다."]], [["‘I have not had that pleasure, Sir,’ rejoined the landlord.", "\"그런 영광을 누린 적은 없습니다, 나리.\" 여관 주인이 받았다."]], [["‘My name is Slurk,’ said the gentleman.", "\"내 이름은 슬러크요.\" 신사가 말했다."]], [["The landlord slightly inclined his head.", "여관 주인은 살짝 고개를 숙였다."]], [["‘Slurk, sir,’ repeated the gentleman haughtily.", "\"슬러크요, 나리.\" 신사가 거만하게 되풀이했다."], ["‘Do you know me now, man?’", "\"이제 날 알겠소, 이 사람아?\""]], [["The landlord scratched his head, looked at the ceiling, and at the stranger, and smiled feebly.", "여관 주인은 머리를 긁적이며 천장을 보고, 낯선 사람을 보고, 힘없이 미소 지었다."]], [["‘Do you know me, man?’ inquired the stranger angrily.", "\"날 알겠소, 이 사람아?\" 낯선 사람이 성을 내며 물었다."]], [["The landlord made a strong effort, and at length replied, ‘Well, Sir, I do not know you.’", "여관 주인은 안간힘을 쓰다가 마침내 대답했다. \"글쎄요, 나리, 저는 당신을 모르겠습니다.\""]], [["‘Great Heaven!’ said the stranger, dashing his clenched fist upon the table.", "\"맙소사!\" 낯선 사람이 꽉 쥔 주먹을 탁자에 내리치며 말했다."], ["‘And this is popularity!’", "\"이것이 바로 인기로구나!\""]], [["The landlord took a step or two towards the door; the stranger fixing his eyes upon him, resumed.", "여관 주인이 문 쪽으로 한두 걸음 옮기자, 낯선 사람은 그에게 눈길을 박고 다시 말을 이었다."]], [["‘This,’ said the stranger--‘this is gratitude for years of labour and study in behalf of the masses.", "\"이것이,\" 낯선 사람이 말했다."], ["I alight wet and weary; no enthusiastic crowds press forward to greet their champion; the church bells are silent; the very name elicits no responsive feeling in their torpid bosoms.", "\"이것이 대중을 위해 수년간 바친 노고와 연구에 대한 보답이로구나. 나는 젖고 지친 몸으로 내렸건만, 자기들의 투사를 맞으려 앞다투어 몰려드는 열광적인 군중은 없고, 교회 종소리도 잠잠하며, 그 이름조차 그들의 무딘 가슴에 아무런 응답의 감정을 불러일으키지 못하는구나."], ["It is enough,’ said the agitated Mr.", "이거야말로,\" 격앙된 슬러크 씨가 이리저리 거닐며 말했다."], ["Slurk, pacing to and fro, ‘to curdle the ink in one’s pen, and induce one to abandon their cause for ever.’", "\"사람의 펜 속 잉크를 응고시키고, 그들의 대의를 영영 저버리게 만들 만한 일이로다.\""]], [["‘Did you say brandy-and-water, Sir?’ said the landlord, venturing a hint.", "\"브랜디에 물을 타 드릴까요, 나리?\" 여관 주인이 슬쩍 운을 떼며 말했다."]], [["‘Rum,’ said Mr.", "\"럼이오.\""], ["Slurk, turning fiercely upon him.", "슬러크 씨가 사납게 돌아서며 말했다."], ["‘Have you got a fire anywhere?’", "\"어디 불 피워 놓은 데 있소?\""]], [["‘We can light one directly, Sir,’ said the landlord.", "\"곧바로 하나 지필 수 있습니다, 나리.\" 여관 주인이 말했다."]], [["‘Which will throw out no heat until it is bed-time,’ interrupted Mr.", "\"그건 잠자리에 들 시간이나 돼야 열기를 내겠지.\" 슬러크 씨가 가로막았다."], ["Slurk. ‘Is there anybody in the kitchen?’", "\"부엌엔 누구 있소?\""]], [["Not a soul.", "아무도 없었다."], ["There was a beautiful fire.", "부엌에는 아름다운 불이 타고 있었다."], ["Everybody had gone, and the house door was closed for the night.", "다들 가 버렸고, 여관 문도 밤이라 닫혀 있었다."]], [["‘I will drink my rum-and-water,’ said Mr.", "\"내 럼앤드워터는,\" 슬러크 씨가 말했다."], ["Slurk, ‘by the kitchen fire.’ So, gathering up his hat and newspaper, he stalked solemnly behind the landlord to that humble apartment, and throwing himself on a settle by the fireside, resumed his countenance of scorn, and began to read and drink in silent dignity.", "\"부엌 난롯가에서 마시겠소.\" 그리하여 모자와 신문을 챙겨 들고, 그는 여관 주인을 뒤따라 그 초라한 방으로 엄숙하게 성큼성큼 걸어가, 난롯가의 긴 의자에 몸을 던지고는 다시 경멸의 표정을 지으며 묵묵히 위엄을 갖추고 읽고 마시기 시작했다."]], [["Now, some demon of discord, flying over the Saracen’s Head at that moment, on casting down his eyes in mere idle curiosity, happened to behold Slurk established comfortably by the kitchen fire, and Pott slightly elevated with wine in another room; upon which the malicious demon, darting down into the last-mentioned apartment with inconceivable rapidity, passed at once into the head of Mr.", "그런데 그때 불화의 악마 하나가 새러슨스 헤드 위를 날아가다가, 그저 한가한 호기심에 눈을 아래로 던졌는데, 마침 슬러크가 부엌 난롯가에 편안히 자리 잡고 있고, 포트가 다른 방에서 술기운에 살짝 들떠 있는 것을 보게 되었다."], ["Bob Sawyer, and prompted him for his (the demon’s) own evil purpose to speak as follows:--", "그러자 그 심술궂은 악마는 상상도 못 할 속도로 후자의 방으로 쏜살같이 내려와, 곧바로 보브 소여 씨의 머릿속으로 들어가, 자신의(그 악마의) 사악한 목적을 위해 그가 다음과 같이 말하도록 부추겼다."]], [["‘I say, we’ve let the fire out.", "\"이봐요, 우리 불이 다 꺼졌어요."], ["It’s uncommonly cold after the rain, isn’t it?’", "비 온 뒤라 유난히 춥군요, 안 그래요?\""]], [["‘It really is,’ replied Mr.", "\"정말 그렇습니다.\""], ["Pickwick, shivering.", "픽윅 씨가 몸을 떨며 대답했다."]], [["‘It wouldn’t be a bad notion to have a cigar by the kitchen fire, would it?’ said Bob Sawyer, still prompted by the demon aforesaid.", "\"부엌 난롯가에서 시가나 한 대 피우는 것도 나쁘지 않겠는데요, 안 그래요?\" 보브 소여가, 여전히 앞서 말한 악마에게 부추김을 받아 말했다."]], [["‘It would be particularly comfortable, I think,’ replied Mr.", "\"그거 아주 편안할 것 같군요.\""], ["Pickwick. ‘Mr.", "픽윅 씨가 대답했다."], ["Pott, what do you say?’", "\"포트 씨, 어떻게 생각하시오?\""]], [["Mr.", "포트 씨가 선뜻 동의했다."], ["Pott yielded a ready assent; and all four travellers, each with his glass in his hand, at once betook themselves to the kitchen, with Sam Weller heading the procession to show them the way.", "그리하여 네 여행객은 저마다 손에 잔을 들고 곧장 부엌으로 향했고, 샘 웰러가 길을 안내하며 행렬의 선두에 섰다."]], [["The stranger was still reading; he looked up and started.", "낯선 사람은 여전히 읽고 있다가, 고개를 들고 흠칫했다."], ["Mr. Pott started.", "포트 씨도 흠칫했다."]], [["‘What’s the matter?’ whispered Mr.", "\"무슨 일이오?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 속삭였다."]], [["‘That reptile!’ replied Pott.", "\"저 파충류!\" 포트가 대답했다."]], [["‘What reptile?’ said Mr.", "\"무슨 파충류요?\""], ["Pickwick, looking about him for fear he should tread on some overgrown black beetle, or dropsical spider.", "픽윅 씨가, 혹시 웃자란 검은 딱정벌레나 수종에 걸린 거미를 밟을까 두려워 주위를 둘러보며 말했다."]], [["‘That reptile,’ whispered Pott, catching Mr.", "\"저 파충류 말입니다.\""], ["Pickwick by the arm, and pointing towards the stranger.", "포트가 픽윅 씨의 팔을 붙잡고 낯선 사람 쪽을 가리키며 속삭였다."], ["‘That reptile Slurk, of the Independent!’", "\"인디펜던트의 저 파충류 슬러크!\""]], [["‘Perhaps we had better retire,’ whispered Mr.", "\"우리가 물러나는 게 낫지 않을까요.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 속삭였다."]], [["‘Never, Sir,’ rejoined Pott, pot-valiant in a double sense--‘never.’ With these words, Mr.", "\"절대 안 됩니다, 나리.\" 포트가 두 가지 의미로 술기운에 용감해져서 받았다."], ["Pott took up his position on an opposite settle, and selecting one from a little bundle of newspapers, began to read against his enemy.", "\"절대로.\" 이 말과 함께 포트 씨는 맞은편 긴 의자에 자리를 잡고, 자그마한 신문 뭉치에서 하나를 골라 자기 적수에 맞서 읽기 시작했다."]], [["Mr.", "* 술기운에 용감해진다는 표현은 영어의 'pot-valiant'를 옮긴 것으로, 포트(Pott)의 이름과도 겹쳐 말장난이 된다."], ["Pott, of course read the Independent, and Mr.", "포트 씨는 물론 인디펜던트를 읽었고, 슬러크 씨는 물론 가제트를 읽었다. 그리고 두 신사는 각각 상대방의 글에 대한 경멸을 신랄한 웃음과 비아냥거리는 코웃음으로 또렷이 드러냈다."], ["Slurk, of course, read the Gazette; and each gentleman audibly expressed his contempt at the other’s compositions by bitter laughs and sarcastic sniffs; whence they proceeded to more open expressions of opinion, such as ‘absurd,’ ‘wretched,’ ‘atrocity,’ ‘humbug,’ ‘knavery’, ‘dirt,’ ‘filth,’ ‘slime,’ ‘ditch-water,’ and other critical remarks of the like nature.", "거기서 그들은 한층 더 노골적인 의견 표명으로 나아갔으니, 이를테면 \"터무니없군\", \"한심하군\", \"잔학무도해\", \"허튼소리야\", \"협잡질\", \"오물\", \"쓰레기\", \"진창\", \"도랑물\" 따위, 그 밖에 그와 비슷한 성격의 평론적 언급들이었다."]], [["Both Mr. Bob Sawyer and Mr. Ben Allen had beheld these symptoms of rivalry and hatred, with a degree of delight which imparted great additional relish to the cigars at which they were puffing most vigorously. The moment they began to flag, the mischievous Mr. Bob Sawyer, addressing Slurk with great politeness, said--", "보브 소여 씨와 벤 앨런 씨는 이 경쟁과 증오의 징후를 어찌나 흐뭇하게 지켜보았는지, 그 덕에 그들이 한껏 힘차게 뻐끔거리던 시가에 큰 풍미가 더해졌다. 그 기세가 수그러들기 시작하는 순간, 짓궂은 보브 소여 씨가 슬러크에게 아주 정중하게 말을 건넸다."]], [["‘Will you allow me to look at your paper, Sir, when you have quite done with it?’", "\"나리, 그 신문 다 보시거든 제가 좀 봐도 되겠습니까?\""]], [["‘You will find very little to repay you for your trouble in this contemptible thing, sir,’ replied Slurk, bestowing a Satanic frown on Pott.", "\"이 하찮은 물건에서는 수고한 보람을 거둘 만한 게 거의 없을 겁니다, 나리.\" 슬러크가 포트에게 악마 같은 인상을 쓰며 대답했다."]], [["‘You shall have this presently,’ said Pott, looking up, pale with rage, and quivering in his speech, from the same cause.", "\"이건 곧 보시게 해 드리지요.\" 포트가 같은 이유로 분노에 새파래진 채, 떨리는 목소리로 고개를 들며 말했다."], ["‘Ha!", "\"하!"], ["ha!", "하!"], ["you will be amused with this fellow’s audacity.’", "이 작자의 뻔뻔함에는 재미있어하시게 될 겁니다.\""]], [["Terrible emphasis was laid upon ‘thing’ and ‘fellow’; and the faces of both editors began to glow with defiance.", "\"물건\"과 \"작자\"에는 무시무시한 강조가 실렸고, 두 편집장의 얼굴은 도전으로 이글거리기 시작했다."]], [["‘The ribaldry of this miserable man is despicably disgusting,’ said Pott, pretending to address Bob Sawyer, and scowling upon Slurk.", "\"이 비참한 자의 상스러운 욕설은 경멸스러울 만큼 역겹군요.\" 포트가 보브 소여에게 말을 거는 척하면서 슬러크를 노려보며 말했다."]], [["Here, Mr. Slurk laughed very heartily, and folding up the paper so as to get at a fresh column conveniently, said, that the blockhead really amused him.", "여기서 슬러크 씨가 아주 호탕하게 웃으며, 새 단을 편하게 읽을 수 있도록 신문을 접고는, 그 멍청이가 정말이지 자기를 즐겁게 한다고 말했다."]], [["‘What an impudent blunderer this fellow is,’ said Pott, turning from pink to crimson.", "\"이 작자, 어찌나 뻔뻔한 얼간이인지.\" 포트가 분홍빛에서 진홍빛으로 변하며 말했다."]], [["‘Did you ever read any of this man’s foolery, Sir?’ inquired Slurk of Bob Sawyer.", "\"이 사람이 쓴 어리석은 글을 읽어 본 적 있으십니까, 나리?\" 슬러크가 보브 소여에게 물었다."]], [["‘Never,’ replied Bob; ‘is it very bad?’", "\"전혀요.\" 보브가 대답했다. \"그렇게 형편없습니까?\""]], [["‘Oh, shocking!", "\"오, 끔찍하지요!"], ["shocking!’ rejoined Slurk.", "끔찍해요!\" 슬러크가 받았다."]], [["‘Really!", "\"정말이지!"], ["Dear me, this is too atrocious!’ exclaimed Pott, at this juncture; still feigning to be absorbed in his reading.", "이런, 이건 너무 잔학무도하군!\" 바로 이 대목에서 포트가, 여전히 자기 신문에 몰두한 척하며 외쳤다."]], [["‘If you can wade through a few sentences of malice, meanness, falsehood, perjury, treachery, and cant,’ said Slurk, handing the paper to Bob, ‘you will, perhaps, be somewhat repaid by a laugh at the style of this ungrammatical twaddler.’", "\"악의와 비열함과 거짓과 위증과 배신과 위선으로 가득한 몇 문장을 헤쳐 나갈 수 있으시다면,\" 슬러크가 보브에게 신문을 건네며 말했다. \"이 문법도 모르는 허튼소리꾼의 문체를 비웃는 것으로 다소간 보답을 받으실지도 모르겠군요.\""]], [["‘What’s that you said, Sir?’ inquired Mr.", "\"방금 뭐라고 하셨소, 나리?\""], ["Pott, looking up, trembling all over with passion.", "포트 씨가 격분으로 온몸을 부들부들 떨며 고개를 들고 물었다."]], [["‘What’s that to you, sir?’ replied Slurk.", "\"당신과 무슨 상관이오, 나리?\" 슬러크가 대답했다."]], [["‘Ungrammatical twaddler, was it, sir?’ said Pott.", "\"문법도 모르는 허튼소리꾼이라고 했소, 나리?\" 포트가 말했다."]], [["‘Yes, sir, it was,’ replied Slurk; ‘and blue bore, Sir, if you like that better; ha! ha!’", "\"그렇소, 나리, 그랬소.\" 슬러크가 대답했다. \"그게 더 마음에 드신다면, '블루파 멧돼지'라고 하지요, 나리. 하! 하!\""]], [["Mr.", "* '블루 보어(blue bore)'는 '블루파의 따분한 자'라는 뜻과 '파란 멧돼지'라는 뜻을 함께 노린 조롱이다."], ["Pott retorted not a word at this jocose insult, but deliberately folded up his copy of the Independent, flattened it carefully down, crushed it beneath his boot, spat upon it with great ceremony, and flung it into the fire.", "포트 씨는 이 익살스러운 모욕에 한마디도 응수하지 않고, 천천히 자기 인디펜던트 신문을 접어, 조심스럽게 납작하게 펴고, 장화 밑에 짓밟고, 거창한 격식을 차려 침을 뱉고는, 그것을 불 속에 던져 버렸다."]], [["‘There, sir,’ said Pott, retreating from the stove, ‘and that’s the way I would serve the viper who produces it, if I were not, fortunately for him, restrained by the laws of my country.’", "\"보시오, 나리.\" 포트가 난로에서 물러서며 말했다. \"다행히 그자에게는 다행스럽게도 내가 우리나라 법에 매여 있지 않다면, 그것을 만들어 내는 그 독사를 바로 이렇게 처리할 거요.\""]], [["‘Serve him so, sir!’ cried Slurk, starting up.", "\"그자를 그렇게 처리하시오, 나리!\" 슬러크가 벌떡 일어서며 외쳤다."], ["‘Those laws shall never be appealed to by him, sir, in such a case.", "\"이런 경우라면 그자는 결코 그 법에 호소하지 않을 거요, 나리."], ["Serve him so, sir!’", "그자를 그렇게 처리하시오, 나리!\""]], [["‘Hear!", "\"옳소!"], ["hear!’ said Bob Sawyer.", "옳소!\" 보브 소여가 말했다."]], [["‘Nothing can be fairer,’ observed Mr.", "\"더없이 공정한 말씀이오.\""], ["Ben Allen.", "벤 앨런 씨가 한마디 했다."]], [["‘Serve him so, sir!’ reiterated Slurk, in a loud voice.", "\"그자를 그렇게 처리하시오, 나리!\" 슬러크가 큰 소리로 거듭 외쳤다."]], [["Mr. Pott darted a look of contempt, which might have withered an anchor.", "포트 씨가 경멸의 눈길을 쏘아붙였는데, 그것은 닻이라도 시들게 할 만했다."]], [["‘Serve him so, sir!’ reiterated Slurk, in a louder voice than before.", "\"그자를 그렇게 처리하시오, 나리!\" 슬러크가 아까보다 더 큰 소리로 거듭 외쳤다."]], [["‘I will not, sir,’ rejoined Pott.", "\"그러지 않겠소, 나리.\" 포트가 받았다."]], [["‘Oh, you won’t, won’t you, sir?’ said Mr.", "\"오, 그러지 않겠다, 그러지 않겠다 이거지요, 나리?\""], ["Slurk, in a taunting manner; ‘you hear this, gentlemen!", "슬러크 씨가 비아냥거리는 투로 말했다. \"여러분, 이 말 들으셨지요!"], ["He won’t; not that he’s afraid--, oh, no!", "이 사람은 안 하겠다는군요. 겁이 나서가 아니라—오, 천만에!"], ["he won’t.", "안 하겠다는 거예요."], ["Ha!", "하!"], ["ha!’", "하!\""]], [["‘I consider you, sir,’ said Mr.", "\"나는 당신을, 나리,\""], ["Pott, moved by this sarcasm, ‘I consider you a viper.", "이 빈정거림에 발끈한 포트 씨가 말했다."], ["I look upon you, sir, as a man who has placed himself beyond the pale of society, by his most audacious, disgraceful, and abominable public conduct.", "\"나는 당신을 독사로 봅니다. 나는 당신을, 나리, 더없이 뻔뻔하고 수치스럽고 가증스러운 공적 행위로 스스로를 사회의 울타리 밖에 내던진 인간으로 봅니다."], ["I view you, sir, personally and politically, in no other light than as a most unparalleled and unmitigated viper.’", "나는 당신을, 나리, 사적으로나 정치적으로나 오로지 더없이 유례없고 에누리 없는 독사로밖에 보지 않습니다.\""]], [["The indignant Independent did not wait to hear the end of this personal denunciation; for, catching up his carpet-bag, which was well stuffed with movables, he swung it in the air as Pott turned away, and, letting it fall with a circular sweep on his head, just at that particular angle of the bag where a good thick hairbrush happened to be packed, caused a sharp crash to be heard throughout the kitchen, and brought him at once to the ground.", "분개한 인디펜던트는 이 인신공격성 규탄의 끝을 들으려고 기다리지 않았다. 옮길 만한 물건들로 가득 채운 융단 가방을 집어 들어, 포트가 돌아서는 순간 공중에서 휘둘러, 그것을 빙 돌리는 동작으로 그의 머리 위에 떨어뜨렸기 때문이다. 마침 두툼한 머리솔이 들어 있던 바로 그 가방 모서리가 떨어지면서 부엌 전체에 날카로운 충돌음이 울려 퍼졌고, 포트는 단번에 바닥에 나뒹굴었다."]], [["‘Gentlemen,’ cried Mr.", "\"여러분.\""], ["Pickwick, as Pott started up and seized the fire- shovel--‘gentlemen!", "포트가 벌떡 일어나 부삽을 움켜쥐자 픽윅 씨가 외쳤다. \"여러분!"], ["Consider, for Heaven’s sake--help--Sam--here--pray, gentlemen--interfere, somebody.’", "제발 좀 생각해 보시오—도와줘—샘—이리 와—제발, 여러분—누가 좀 말려 주시오.\""]], [["Uttering these incoherent exclamations, Mr. Pickwick rushed between the infuriated combatants just in time to receive the carpet-bag on one side of his body, and the fire-shovel on the other. Whether the representatives of the public feeling of Eatanswill were blinded by animosity, or (being both acute reasoners) saw the advantage of having a third party between them to bear all the blows, certain it is that they paid not the slightest attention to Mr. Pickwick, but defying each other with great spirit, plied the carpet-bag and the fire-shovel most fearlessly. Mr. Pickwick would unquestionably have suffered severely for his humane interference, if Mr. Weller, attracted by his master’s cries, had not rushed in at the moment, and, snatching up a meal-sack, effectually stopped the conflict by drawing it over the head and shoulders of the mighty Pott, and clasping him tight round the shoulders.", "이런 두서없는 외침을 내뱉으며, 픽윅 씨는 격분한 두 싸움꾼 사이로 돌진했는데, 마침 융단 가방을 한쪽 옆구리에, 부삽을 다른 쪽 옆구리에 받기에 딱 알맞은 때였다. 이턴스윌 여론을 대표하는 이 두 사람이 적개심에 눈이 멀었던 것인지, 아니면 (둘 다 예리한 논리가였던 만큼) 모든 타격을 대신 받아 줄 제삼자를 사이에 두는 이점을 알아챈 것인지는 모르겠으나, 분명한 것은 그들이 픽윅 씨에게는 조금도 주의를 기울이지 않은 채, 대단한 기세로 서로에게 맞서며 융단 가방과 부삽을 더없이 겁 없이 휘둘러 댔다는 사실이다. 주인의 외침에 이끌려 웰러 씨가 그 순간 뛰어 들어와 곡식 자루를 낚아채, 힘센 포트의 머리와 어깨 위로 그것을 뒤집어씌워 어깨를 단단히 끌어안고서 효과적으로 싸움을 멈추지 않았더라면, 픽윅 씨는 그 인도적인 개입의 대가로 틀림없이 심하게 다쳤을 것이다."]], [["‘Take away that ‘ere bag from the t’other madman,’ said Sam to Ben Allen and Bob Sawyer, who had done nothing but dodge round the group, each with a tortoise-shell lancet in his hand, ready to bleed the first man stunned.", "\"저 다른 미치광이한테서 저 가방을 뺏어요.\" 샘이 벤 앨런과 보브 소여에게 말했다."], ["‘Give it up, you wretched little creetur, or I’ll smother you in it.’", "그 둘은 거북 등딱지 란셋을 저마다 손에 들고, 처음 기절하는 사람의 피를 뽑을 채비를 한 채 무리 주위를 빙빙 돌며 피하기만 하고 있었던 것이다. \"내놔, 이 가엾고 쪼끄만 작자야, 안 그러면 그 안에다 처박아 질식시켜 버릴 테니까.\""]], [["Awed by these threats, and quite out of breath, the Independent suffered himself to be disarmed; and Mr.", "이 위협에 겁먹고, 또 완전히 숨이 차서, 인디펜던트는 순순히 무장 해제당했다."], ["Weller, removing the extinguisher from Pott, set him free with a caution.", "그리고 웰러 씨는 포트에게서 그 덮개를 벗겨 내고, 한마디 주의를 주며 그를 풀어 주었다."]], [["‘You take yourselves off to bed quietly,’ said Sam, ‘or I’ll put you both in it, and let you fight it out vith the mouth tied, as I vould a dozen sich, if they played these games. And you have the goodness to come this here way, sir, if you please.’", "\"두 분은 조용히 자러 들어가세유.\" 샘이 말했다. \"안 그러면 두 분 다 거기 처넣고, 입을 묶은 채로 결판을 내게 할 테니까유. 이런 짓거리를 한다면 이런 작자 열둘이라도 그렇게 해 버릴 겁니다. 그리고 나리, 괜찮으시다면 이쪽으로 와 주시겠어유.\""]], [["Thus addressing his master, Sam took him by the arm, and led him off, while the rival editors were severally removed to their beds by the landlord, under the inspection of Mr.", "이렇게 주인에게 말을 건네며, 샘은 그의 팔을 잡고 데리고 나갔다."], ["Bob Sawyer and Mr. Benjamin Allen; breathing, as they went away, many sanguinary threats, and making vague appointments for mortal combat next day.", "그동안 경쟁 관계의 두 편집장은 보브 소여 씨와 벤저민 앨런 씨의 감독하에 여관 주인에 의해 각각 침대로 옮겨졌는데, 가면서도 피비린내 나는 위협을 잔뜩 내뱉고, 이튿날 목숨을 건 결투를 하자며 막연한 약속을 잡았다."], ["When they came to think it over, however, it occurred to them that they could do it much better in print, so they recommenced deadly hostilities without delay; and all Eatanswill rung with their boldness--on paper.", "그러나 곰곰이 생각해 보고 나니, 인쇄물로 하면 훨씬 더 잘할 수 있겠다는 데 생각이 미쳤고, 그래서 그들은 지체 없이 죽기 살기 식 적대를 재개했다. 그리하여 이턴스윌 전역이 그들의 대담함으로—지면상에서—떠들썩하게 울려 퍼졌다."]], [["They had taken themselves off in separate coaches, early next morning, before the other travellers were stirring; and the weather having now cleared up, the chaise companions once more turned their faces to London.", "그들은 다른 여행객들이 채 움직이기도 전, 이튿날 아침 일찍 각자 다른 마차를 타고 떠나 버렸다. 그리고 이제 날씨가 개었으므로, 마차에 함께 탄 일행은 다시 한번 런던으로 얼굴을 향했다."]], [["CHAPTER LII. INVOLVING A SERIOUS CHANGE IN THE WELLER FAMILY, AND THE UNTIMELY DOWNFALL OF MR. STIGGINS\nConsidering it a matter of delicacy to abstain from introducing either Bob Sawyer or Ben Allen to the young couple, until they were fully prepared to expect them, and wishing to spare Arabella’s feelings as much as possible, Mr. Pickwick proposed that he and Sam should alight in the neighbourhood of the George and Vulture, and that the two young men should for the present take up their quarters elsewhere. To this they very readily agreed, and the proposition was accordingly acted upon; Mr. Ben Allen and Mr. Bob Sawyer betaking themselves to a sequestered pot- shop on the remotest confines of the Borough, behind the bar door of which their names had in other days very often appeared at the head of long and complex calculations worked in white chalk.", "젊은 부부가 두 사람을 맞을 마음의 준비가 충분히 될 때까지 보브 소여나 벤 앨런을 그들에게 소개하지 않는 것이 도리에 맞는 일이라 여기고, 또 가능한 한 아라벨라의 감정을 배려하고자 한 픽윅 씨는, 자기와 샘은 조지 앤드 벌처 근처에서 내리고 두 청년은 당분간 다른 곳에 묵는 것이 좋겠다고 제안했다. 두 사람은 이에 흔쾌히 동의하여 제안은 그대로 실행되었으니, 벤 앨런 씨와 보브 소여 씨는 자치구의 가장 외진 변두리에 있는 어느 한적한 술집으로 물러갔다. 그 술집 카운터 문 뒤에는 지난날 그들의 이름이 흰 분필로 길고 복잡하게 적힌 외상 계산서의 머리에 무던히도 자주 올라 있곤 했었다."]], [["‘Dear me, Mr.", "\"어머나, 웰러 씨.\""], ["Weller,’ said the pretty housemaid, meeting Sam at the door.", "문간에서 샘을 맞으며 예쁜 하녀가 말했다."]], [["‘Dear me I vish it vos, my dear,’ replied Sam, dropping behind, to let his master get out of hearing.", "\"어머나라, 정말 그랬으면 좋겠구려, 내 사랑.\" 샘은 주인이 들리지 않을 만큼 멀어지도록 뒤로 처지면서 대꾸했다."], ["‘Wot a sweet-lookin’ creetur you are, Mary!’", "\"당신은 어쩌면 그렇게 사랑스럽게 생겼소, 메리!\""]], [["‘Lor’, Mr. Weller, what nonsense you do talk!’ said Mary.", "\"어머, 웰러 씨, 무슨 그런 실없는 말씀을 하세요!\""], ["‘Oh! don’t, Mr.", "메리가 말했다."], ["Weller.’", "\"어머! 그러지 마세요, 웰러 씨.\""]], [["‘Don’t what, my dear?’ said Sam.", "\"뭘 그러지 말라는 거요, 내 사랑?\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["‘Why, that,’ replied the pretty housemaid.", "\"아니, 그거 말이에요.\" 예쁜 하녀가 대꾸했다."], ["‘Lor, do get along with you.’ Thus admonishing him, the pretty housemaid pushed Sam against the wall, declaring that he had tumbled her cap, and put her hair quite out of curl.", "\"어머, 저리 좀 비키세요.\" 이렇게 그를 나무라면서 예쁜 하녀는 샘을 벽 쪽으로 밀치며, 그가 자기 모자를 헝클어뜨리고 머리의 곱슬을 다 풀어 놓았다고 항변했다."]], [["‘And prevented what I was going to say, besides,’ added Mary.", "\"게다가 제가 하려던 말까지 못 하게 했잖아요.\" 메리가 덧붙였다."], ["‘There’s a letter been waiting here for you four days; you hadn’t gone away, half an hour, when it came; and more than that, it’s got “immediate,”", "\"여기 나흘이나 당신을 기다리던 편지가 있어요. 당신이 떠난 지 반 시간도 안 돼서 왔지 뭐예요."], ["on the outside.’", "게다가 겉봉에 '급함'이라고 적혀 있다고요.\""]], [["‘Vere is it, my love?’ inquired Sam.", "\"어디 있소, 내 사랑?\" 샘이 물었다."]], [["‘I took care of it, for you, or I dare say it would have been lost long before this,’ replied Mary. ‘There, take it; it’s more than you deserve.’", "\"제가 잘 챙겨 뒀어요. 안 그랬으면 진작에 없어졌을 거예요.\" 메리가 대꾸했다. \"자, 받으세요. 당신한테는 과분하지만요.\""]], [["With these words, after many pretty little coquettish doubts and fears, and wishes that she might not have lost it, Mary produced the letter from behind the nicest little muslin tucker possible, and handed it to Sam, who thereupon kissed it with much gallantry and devotion.", "이렇게 말하면서, 혹시 잃어버린 건 아닐까 하는 갖가지 귀엽고 새침한 의심과 걱정과 바람을 늘어놓은 끝에, 메리는 더없이 곱고 자그마한 모슬린 가슴 장식 뒤에서 편지를 꺼내 샘에게 건넸고, 샘은 그에 무척이나 정중하고 헌신적인 태도로 그 편지에 입을 맞추었다."]], [["‘My goodness me!’ said Mary, adjusting the tucker, and feigning unconsciousness, ‘you seem to have grown very fond of it all at once.’", "\"어머, 세상에!\" 가슴 장식을 매만지며 짐짓 모르는 척하면서 메리가 말했다. \"갑자기 그 편지를 무척이나 아끼시게 됐나 봐요.\""]], [["To this Mr.", "이에 웰러 씨는 그저 윙크 하나로 답했을 뿐인데, 그 깊디깊은 뜻이라는 것은 어떤 묘사로도 그 희미한 기색조차 전할 수 없는 것이었다."], ["Weller only replied by a wink, the intense meaning of which no description could convey the faintest idea of; and, sitting himself down beside Mary on a window-seat, opened the letter and glanced at the contents.", "그러고는 창가 자리에 메리 곁에 앉아 편지를 펴서 그 내용을 훑어보았다."]], [["‘Hollo!’ exclaimed Sam, ‘wot’s all this?’", "\"아니!\" 샘이 외쳤다. \"이게 다 뭐람?\""]], [["‘Nothing the matter, I hope?’ said Mary, peeping over his shoulder.", "\"무슨 일 생긴 건 아니겠죠?\" 메리가 어깨 너머로 들여다보며 말했다."]], [["‘Bless them eyes o’ yourn!’ said Sam, looking up.", "\"그 눈 좀 보게나!\" 샘이 고개를 들며 말했다."]], [["‘Never mind my eyes; you had much better read your letter,’ said the pretty housemaid; and as she said so, she made the eyes twinkle with such slyness and beauty that they were perfectly irresistible.", "\"제 눈은 신경 쓰지 마시고, 편지나 읽으시는 게 훨씬 낫겠어요.\" 예쁜 하녀가 말했다. 그러면서 어찌나 교활하고 어여쁘게 눈을 반짝거리는지, 도무지 견딜 수가 없을 정도였다. 샘은 입맞춤으로 기운을 차린 뒤, 다음과 같이 읽어 내려갔다."]], [["Sam refreshed himself with a kiss, and read as follows:--", "\"그랜비 후작 여관, 도킹 인근, 수요일. 사랑하는 새믈에게,"]], [["‘I am wery sorry to have the pleasure of being a Bear of ill news your Mother in law cort cold consekens of imprudently settin too long on the damp grass in the rain a hearin of a shepherd who warnt able to leave off till late at night owen to his having vound his-self up vith brandy and vater and not being able to stop his-self till he got a little sober which took a many hours to do the doctor says that if she’d svallo’d varm brandy and vater artervards insted of afore she mightn’t have been no vus her veels wos immedetly greased and everythink done to set her agoin as could be inwented your father had hopes as she vould have vorked round as usual but just as she wos a turnen the corner my boy she took the wrong road and vent down hill vith a welocity you never see and notvithstandin that the drag wos put on drectly by the medikel man it wornt of no use at all for she paid the last pike at twenty minutes afore six o’clock yesterday evenin havin done the jouney wery much under the reglar time vich praps was partly owen to her haven taken in wery little luggage by the vay your father says that if you vill come and see me Sammy he vill take it as a wery great favor for I am wery lonely Samivel N. B. he vill have it spelt that vay vich I say ant right and as there is sich a many things to settle he is sure your guvner wont object of course he vill not Sammy for I knows him better so he sends his dooty in which I join and am Samivel infernally yours", "너의 새어머이가 빗속에 축축한 풀밭에 너무 오래 분별없이 앉아 어떤 목자의 설교를 듣다가 감기가 든 까닭에 나쁜 소식을 전하는 곰이 되는 기쁨을 누리게 되어 무척 안타깝구나 그 목자란 작자가 브랜디와 물로 제 몸에 태엽을 감아 놓는 바람에 밤늦도록 그칠 줄을 몰랏고 어느 정도 술이 깰 때까지 제 입을 멈출 수가 업서서 그러느라 여러 시간이 걸렷단다 의사 말이 만일 너의 새어머이가 그 전이 아니라 그 후에 따뜻한 브랜디 물을 들이켯더면 더 나뻐지지 안앗을 거라더구나 즉시 바퀴에 기름을 치고 다시 굴러가게 하느라 생각해낼 수 잇는 온갖 일을 다 햇단다 너의 아버이는 늘 하던 대로 회복하기를 바랏지마는 모퉁이를 돌려는 찰나에 얘야 그만 길을 잘못 들어 너는 본 적도 업는 속도로 비탈을 내려가 버렷고 의사 양반이 곧바로 제동을 걸엇건마는 아무 소용이 업서서 어제 저녁 여섯시 이십분 전에 마지막 요금소를 지나고 말앗단다 정해진 시간보다 훨씬 일찍 여정을 마첫스니 이는 아마도 도중에 짐을 아주 조금밖에 싣지 안앗던 까닭이리라 너의 아버이가 말하기를 새미야 네가 와서 나를 본다면 무척 큰 호의로 여기겟다더구나 나는 무척 외롭단다 새미블 추신 아버이가 굳이 그 철자로 쓰라 하는데 나는 그게 옳지 안타고 말한다 그리고 정리할 일이 하도 만흐니 네 주인 양반도 분명 반대하지 안을 거란다 물론 반대하지 안켓지 새미 나는 그분을 더 잘 아니까 그러니 그분이 안부를 전하시고 나도 거기 더하여 새미블 지긋지긋하게 너를 사랑하는"]], [["‘Wot a incomprehensible letter,’ said Sam; ‘who’s to know wot it means, vith all this he-ing and I-ing! It ain’t my father’s writin’, ‘cept this here signater in print letters; that’s his.’", "토니 웰러로부터.\" \"참 알아먹기 힘든 편지로군.\" 샘이 말했다. \"이 '그가' '내가' 하는 통에 누가 무슨 뜻인지 알겠어! 이 인쇄체로 박은 서명만 빼고는 아버지 글씨가 아냐. 이건 아버지가 쓴 게 맞고.\""]], [["‘Perhaps he got somebody to write it for him, and signed it himself afterwards,’ said the pretty housemaid.", "\"누구한테 대신 써 달라고 하시고는 서명만 직접 하셨나 봐요.\" 예쁜 하녀가 말했다."]], [["‘Stop a minit,’ replied Sam, running over the letter again, and pausing here and there, to reflect, as he did so.", "\"잠깐만.\" 샘은 다시 편지를 훑어보며 이따금 멈춰 곰곰이 생각하면서 대꾸했다."], ["‘You’ve hit it.", "\"당신이 맞혔어."], ["The gen’l’m’n as wrote it wos a-tellin’ all about the misfortun’ in a proper vay, and then my father comes a-lookin’ over him, and complicates the whole concern by puttin’ his oar in.", "이걸 쓴 양반은 그 불행한 일을 제대로 차근차근 다 적어 가던 참인데, 그때 아버지가 들여다보면서 끼어들어 가지고 일을 죄다 뒤죽박죽으로 만든 거야."], ["That’s just the wery sort o’ thing he’d do.", "딱 아버지가 할 만한 짓이지."], ["You’re right, Mary, my dear.’", "당신 말이 맞아, 메리 내 사랑.\""]], [["Having satisfied himself on this point, Sam read the letter all over, once more, and, appearing to form a clear notion of its contents for the first time, ejaculated thoughtfully, as he folded it up--", "이 점에 대해 확신을 얻은 샘은 편지를 다시 한 번 처음부터 끝까지 읽었고, 비로소 그 내용을 또렷이 파악한 듯 편지를 접으며 생각에 잠긴 채 내뱉었다."]], [["‘And so the poor creetur’s dead!", "\"그래서 가엾은 양반이 죽었단 말이지!"], ["I’m sorry for it.", "안됐구먼."], ["She warn’t a bad- disposed ‘ooman, if them shepherds had let her alone.", "그 목자들만 가만 내버려 뒀더라면 나쁜 마음씨를 가진 여자는 아니었는데."], ["I’m wery sorry for it.’", "참 안됐어.\""]], [["Mr. Weller uttered these words in so serious a manner, that the pretty housemaid cast down her eyes and looked very grave.", "웰러 씨가 이 말을 어찌나 진지하게 내뱉는지, 예쁜 하녀는 눈을 내리깔고 무척 엄숙한 표정을 지었다."]], [["‘Hows’ever,’ said Sam, putting the letter in his pocket with a gentle sigh, ‘it wos to be--and wos, as the old lady said arter she’d married the footman.", "\"하지만,\" 샘은 가벼운 한숨과 함께 편지를 주머니에 넣으며 말했다."], ["Can’t be helped now, can it, Mary?’", "\"이렇게 될 일이었고—그렇게 됐지, 하인이랑 결혼하고 나서 노부인이 한 말처럼 말이야. 이제 와서 어쩔 수 없는 일이지, 안 그래, 메리?\""]], [["Mary shook her head, and sighed too.", "메리는 고개를 가로젓고 자기도 한숨을 쉬었다."]], [["‘I must apply to the hemperor for leave of absence,’ said Sam.", "\"황제 폐하께 휴가를 청해야겠어.\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["Mary sighed again--the letter was so very affecting.", "메리가 다시 한숨을 쉬었다—편지가 어찌나 가슴 아픈지."]], [["‘Good-bye!’ said Sam.", "\"잘 있어!\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["‘Good-bye,’ rejoined the pretty housemaid, turning her head away.", "\"잘 가요.\" 예쁜 하녀가 고개를 돌리며 받아 말했다."]], [["‘Well, shake hands, won’t you?’ said Sam.", "\"이봐, 악수는 하고 가야지, 안 그래?\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["The pretty housemaid put out a hand which, although it was a housemaid’s, was a very small one, and rose to go.", "예쁜 하녀가 손을 내밀었는데, 하녀의 손이라고는 하나 무척 작고 고운 손이었다. 그러고는 일어나서 가려 했다."]], [["‘I shan’t be wery long avay,’ said Sam.", "\"오래 떠나 있진 않을 거야.\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["‘You’re always away,’ said Mary, giving her head the slightest possible toss in the air.", "\"당신은 늘 떠나 있잖아요.\" 메리가 고개를 살짝 까딱 치켜들며 말했다."], ["‘You no sooner come, Mr. Weller, than you go again.’", "\"웰러 씨, 당신은 오기가 무섭게 또 떠나 버린다고요.\""]], [["Mr. Weller drew the household beauty closer to him, and entered upon a whispering conversation, which had not proceeded far, when she turned her face round and condescended to look at him again.", "웰러 씨는 이 집안의 미인을 자기 쪽으로 더 가까이 끌어당기고는 속닥속닥 대화를 시작했는데, 얼마 진행되지도 않아 그녀는 얼굴을 돌려 그를 너그러이 다시 바라보았다."], ["When they parted, it was somehow or other indispensably necessary for her to go to her room, and arrange the cap and curls before she could think of presenting herself to her mistress; which preparatory ceremony she went off to perform, bestowing many nods and smiles on Sam over the banisters as she tripped upstairs.", "두 사람이 헤어질 무렵, 어찌 된 영문인지 그녀는 안주인 앞에 나설 생각을 하기에 앞서 반드시 자기 방에 가서 모자와 곱슬머리를 다시 매만져야만 했다. 그 준비 의식을 치르러 가면서 그녀는 계단을 종종걸음으로 올라가며 난간 너머로 샘에게 수없이 고갯짓과 미소를 보냈다."]], [["‘I shan’t be avay more than a day, or two, Sir, at the furthest,’ said Sam, when he had communicated to Mr.", "\"길어야 하루나 이틀이면 됩니다요, 나리.\""], ["Pickwick the intelligence of his father’s loss.", "아버지가 상을 당했다는 소식을 픽윅 씨에게 전한 뒤 샘이 말했다."]], [["‘As long as may be necessary, Sam,’ replied Mr.", "\"필요한 만큼 얼마든지 있어도 좋네, 샘.\""], ["Pickwick, ‘you have my full permission to remain.’\nSam bowed.", "픽윅 씨가 대답했다. \"내가 전적으로 허락할 테니 머물러 있게.\""]], [["‘You will tell your father, Sam, that if I can be of any assistance to him in his present situation, I shall be most willing and ready to lend him any aid in my power,’ said Mr.", "샘이 고개를 숙여 인사했다. \"자네 아버지께 전하게, 샘."], ["Pickwick.", "지금 같은 처지에 내가 무엇이든 도울 일이 있다면, 내 힘이 닿는 한 어떤 도움이든 기꺼이 흔쾌히 베풀겠노라고 말일세.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Thank’ee, sir,’ rejoined Sam.", "\"고맙습니다요, 나리.\" 샘이 받아 말했다."], ["‘I’ll mention it, sir.’", "\"그렇게 전하겠습니다요, 나리.\""]], [["And with some expressions of mutual good-will and interest, master and man separated.", "그리고 서로의 호의와 관심을 담은 몇 마디를 나눈 뒤, 주인과 하인은 헤어졌다."]], [["It was just seven o’clock when Samuel Weller, alighting from the box of a stage-coach which passed through Dorking, stood within a few hundred yards of the Marquis of Granby.", "새뮤얼 웰러가 도킹을 지나는 역마차의 마부석에서 내려 그랜비 후작 여관에서 몇백 야드도 안 되는 곳에 섰을 때는 막 일곱 시였다. 춥고 음울한 저녁이었다."], ["It was a cold, dull evening; the little street looked dreary and dismal; and the mahogany countenance of the noble and gallant marquis seemed to wear a more sad and melancholy expression than it was wont to do, as it swung to and fro, creaking mournfully in the wind.", "작은 거리는 황량하고 쓸쓸해 보였고, 그 고귀하고 늠름한 후작의 마호가니 얼굴은 바람에 구슬프게 삐걱대며 이리저리 흔들리는 동안 평소보다 한층 더 슬프고 우울한 표정을 띤 듯했다. 차양은 내려져 있었고 덧창은 반쯤 닫혀 있었다."], ["The blinds were pulled down, and the shutters partly closed; of the knot of loungers that usually collected about the door, not one was to be seen; the place was silent and desolate.", "평소 문간에 모여 있던 한가한 무리는 한 사람도 보이지 않았다. 그곳은 적막하고 황량했다."]], [["Seeing nobody of whom he could ask any preliminary questions, Sam walked softly in, and glancing round, he quickly recognised his parent in the distance.", "미리 무언가 물어볼 만한 사람이 아무도 보이지 않자, 샘은 살그머니 안으로 걸어 들어가 주위를 둘러보다가 이내 저만치에서 아버지를 알아보았다."]], [["The widower was seated at a small round table in the little room behind the bar, smoking a pipe, with his eyes intently fixed upon the fire.", "홀아비가 된 그는 카운터 뒤편의 작은 방에서 둥근 탁자 앞에 앉아 파이프를 피우며 두 눈을 불에 골똘히 박고 있었다."], ["The funeral had evidently taken place that day, for attached to his hat, which he still retained on his head, was a hatband measuring about a yard and a half in length, which hung over the top rail of the chair and streamed negligently down.", "장례식은 분명 그날 치러진 모양이었으니, 그가 여태 머리에 쓰고 있는 모자에는 한 야드 반쯤 되는 길이의 상장(喪章) 띠가 달려 의자 등받이 위로 넘겨져 무심하게 늘어져 있었다."], ["Mr. Weller was in a very abstracted and contemplative mood.", "웰러 씨는 무척 넋이 나간 듯 사색에 잠긴 기분이었다."], ["Notwithstanding that Sam called him by name several times, he still continued to smoke with the same fixed and quiet countenance, and was only roused ultimately by his son’s placing the palm of his hand on his shoulder.", "샘이 몇 번이나 이름을 불렀건만 그는 여전히 그 한결같이 고요한 표정으로 담배만 피워 댔고, 마침내 아들이 그의 어깨에 손바닥을 얹고 나서야 비로소 정신을 차렸다."]], [["‘Sammy,’ said Mr.", "\"새미야,\""], ["Weller, ‘you’re welcome.’", "웰러 씨가 말했다. \"어서 오너라.\""]], [["‘I’ve been a-callin’ to you half a dozen times,’ said Sam, hanging his hat on a peg, ‘but you didn’t hear me.’", "\"여섯 번이나 불렀는데,\" 샘이 못걸이에 모자를 걸며 말했다. \"못 들으셨어요.\""]], [["‘No, Sammy,’ replied Mr.", "\"못 들었다, 새미야.\""], ["Weller, again looking thoughtfully at the fire.", "웰러 씨가 다시 생각에 잠긴 채 불을 바라보며 대답했다."], ["‘I was in a referee, Sammy.’", "\"내가 영중(靈中)에 빠져 있었지, 새미야.\""]], [["‘Wot about?’ inquired Sam, drawing his chair up to the fire.", "\"무슨 영중요?\" 샘이 의자를 불 쪽으로 당기며 물었다."]], [["‘In a referee, Sammy,’ replied the elder Mr.", "\"영중이지, 새미야.\" 웰러 영감이 대답했다."], ["Weller, ‘regarding her, Samivel.’ Here Mr.", "\"그 사람 생각하느라, 새미블.\""], ["Weller jerked his head in the direction of Dorking churchyard, in mute explanation that his words referred to the late Mrs. Weller.", "여기서 웰러 씨는 그 말이 고인이 된 웰러 부인을 가리키는 것임을 무언으로 설명하듯 도킹 교회 묘지 쪽으로 고개를 까딱했다."]], [["‘I wos a-thinkin’, Sammy,’ said Mr.", "\"내가 생각하고 있었단다, 새미야.\""], ["Weller, eyeing his son, with great earnestness, over his pipe, as if to assure him that however extraordinary and incredible the declaration might appear, it was nevertheless calmly and deliberately uttered.", "웰러 씨가 파이프 너머로 아들을 무척 진지하게 바라보며 말했는데, 마치 이 말이 아무리 별나고 믿기 어렵게 들릴지라도 차분하고 신중하게 한 말임을 일러 주려는 듯했다."], ["‘I wos a-thinkin’, Sammy, that upon the whole I wos wery sorry she wos gone.’", "\"내가 생각하고 있었어, 새미야, 대체로 그 사람이 떠난 게 무척 안타깝다고 말이다.\""]], [["‘Vell, and so you ought to be,’ replied Sam.", "\"그야, 안타까워하셔야죠.\" 샘이 대답했다."]], [["Mr. Weller nodded his acquiescence in the sentiment, and again fastening his eyes on the fire, shrouded himself in a cloud, and mused deeply.", "웰러 씨는 그 말에 동의하듯 고개를 끄덕이고는, 다시 두 눈을 불에 박고 연기 구름 속에 몸을 감추며 깊은 상념에 잠겼다."]], [["‘Those wos wery sensible observations as she made, Sammy,’ said Mr.", "\"그 사람이 한 말이 무척 사리에 닿는 소리였지, 새미야.\""], ["Weller, driving the smoke away with his hand, after a long silence.", "한참 침묵을 지키던 웰러 씨가 손으로 연기를 휘저으며 말했다."]], [["‘Wot observations?’ inquired Sam.", "\"무슨 말요?\" 샘이 물었다."]], [["‘Them as she made, arter she was took ill,’ replied the old gentleman.", "\"그 사람이 병들고 나서 한 말 말이다.\" 노인이 대답했다."]], [["‘Wot was they?’", "\"무슨 말이었는데요?\""]], [["‘Somethin’ to this here effect.", "\"대충 이런 뜻이었지."], ["“Veller,”", "'여보,'"], ["she says, “I’m afeered I’ve not done by you quite wot I ought to have done; you’re a wery kind- hearted man, and I might ha’ made your home more comfortabler.", "그 사람이 말하더구나, '내가 당신한테 마땅히 했어야 할 만큼 제대로 못 해 준 것 같아 마음이 쓰여요. 당신은 무척 마음씨 고운 사람인데, 내가 우리 집을 더 편안하게 만들 수도 있었을 텐데."], ["I begin to see now,”", "이제야 알겠어요,'"], ["she says, “ven it’s too late, that if a married ‘ooman vishes to be religious, she should begin vith dischargin’ her dooties at home, and makin’ them as is about her cheerful and happy, and that vile she goes to church, or chapel, or wot not, at all proper times, she should be wery careful not to con-wert this sort o’ thing into a excuse for idleness or self-indulgence.", "그 사람이 말하길, '너무 늦어 버렸지만, 결혼한 여자가 신앙심을 가지려면 우선 집안에서 제 본분을 다하고 곁에 있는 사람들을 즐겁고 행복하게 해 주는 데서부터 시작해야 하고, 적당한 때에 교회든 예배당이든 어디든 가더라도 이런 일을 게으름이나 제 욕심의 핑계로 둔갑시키지 않도록 무척 조심해야 한다는 걸요. 나는 그렇게 해 버렸어요,'"], ["I have done this,”", "그 사람이 말하길, '나보다 그런 짓을 더 한 작자들한테 시간이며 재물을 다 허비해 버렸지요. 하지만 여보, 내가 떠나고 나면 그 사람들을 알기 전의 나를, 본디 천성 그대로의 나를 떠올려 주기를 바라요.'"], ["she says, “and I’ve vasted time and substance on them as has done it more than me; but I hope ven I’m gone, Veller, that you’ll think on me as I wos afore I know’d them people, and as I raly wos by natur.”", "'수전,' 내가 말했다—나는 이 말에 그만 목이 콱 메었다, 새미블."], ["‘“Susan,”", "그건 부인하지 않으마, 얘야—'수전,' 내가 말했지, '당신은 통틀어서 나한테 무척 좋은 아내였소."], ["says I--I wos took up wery short by this, Samivel; I von’t deny it, my boy--“Susan,”", "그런 말은 일절 하지 마오. 마음 단단히 먹어요, 여보."], ["I says, “you’ve been a wery good vife to me, altogether; don’t say nothin’ at all about it; keep a good heart, my dear; and you’ll live to see me punch that ‘ere Stiggins’s head yet.”", "그러면 당신은 살아서 내가 저 스티긴스란 작자 머리통을 후려치는 꼴을 보게 될 거요.' 그 사람이 이 말에 웃더구나, 새미블.\""], ["She smiled at this, Samivel,’ said the old gentleman, stifling a sigh with his pipe, ‘but she died arter all!’", "노인이 파이프로 한숨을 짓누르며 말했다. \"그래도 결국 죽고 말았지!\""]], [["‘Vell,’ said Sam, venturing to offer a little homely consolation, after the lapse of three or four minutes, consumed by the old gentleman in slowly shaking his head from side to side, and solemnly smoking, ‘vell, gov’nor, ve must all come to it, one day or another.’", "\"뭐,\" 노인이 천천히 고개를 좌우로 가로젓고 엄숙하게 담배를 피우며 보낸 서너 분이 흐른 뒤, 샘이 소박한 위로를 한마디 건네 보며 말했다. \"뭐, 아버지, 우리도 다 언젠가는 그리 되는 거잖아요.\""]], [["‘So we must, Sammy,’ said Mr.", "\"그렇고말고, 새미야.\""], ["Weller the elder.", "웰러 영감이 말했다."]], [["‘There’s a Providence in it all,’ said Sam.", "\"그게 다 섭리지요.\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["‘O’ course there is,’ replied his father, with a nod of grave approval.", "\"물론 그렇지.\" 그의 아버지가 진지하게 동의하듯 고개를 끄덕이며 대답했다."], ["‘Wot ‘ud become of the undertakers vithout it, Sammy?’", "\"그게 없으면 장의사들은 뭐가 되겠냐, 새미야?\""]], [["Lost in the immense field of conjecture opened by this reflection, the elder Mr. Weller laid his pipe on the table, and stirred the fire with a meditative visage.", "이 생각이 열어 놓은 무한한 추측의 들판에서 길을 잃은 웰러 영감은 파이프를 탁자에 내려놓고 사색에 잠긴 얼굴로 불을 쑤셨다."]], [["While the old gentleman was thus engaged, a very buxom-looking cook, dressed in mourning, who had been bustling about, in the bar, glided into the room, and bestowing many smirks of recognition upon Sam, silently stationed herself at the back of his father’s chair, and announced her presence by a slight cough, the which, being disregarded, was followed by a louder one.", "노인이 이러고 있는 사이, 상복을 차려입은 무척 풍만하게 생긴 요리사 여인이, 그때껏 카운터에서 부산을 떨고 있다가, 슬며시 방 안으로 미끄러져 들어와 샘에게 알은체하는 웃음을 잔뜩 흘리고는 조용히 그 아버지의 의자 뒤에 자리를 잡고서 가벼운 기침으로 자기가 왔음을 알렸다. 그 기침이 무시당하자 더 큰 기침이 뒤따랐다."]], [["‘Hollo!’ said the elder Mr.", "\"이런!\""], ["Weller, dropping the poker as he looked round, and hastily drew his chair away.", "웰러 영감이 둘러보다가 부지깽이를 떨어뜨리고 황급히 의자를 빼며 말했다."], ["‘Wot’s the matter now?’", "\"또 무슨 일이오?\""]], [["‘Have a cup of tea, there’s a good soul,’ replied the buxom female coaxingly.", "\"차 한잔 드세요, 그래야 착하시죠.\" 풍만한 여인이 어르듯 대답했다."]], [["‘I von’t,’ replied Mr.", "\"안 마시오.\""], ["Weller, in a somewhat boisterous manner.", "웰러 씨가 다소 거칠게 대답했다."], ["‘I’ll see you--’ Mr.", "\"그쪽 얼굴이나—\""], ["Weller hastily checked himself, and added in a low tone, ‘furder fust.’", "웰러 씨는 황급히 말을 멈추고 낮은 소리로 덧붙였다. \"다신 안 볼 테요.\""]], [["‘Oh, dear, dear!", "\"어머나, 저런, 저런!"], ["How adwersity does change people!’ said the lady, looking upwards.", "역경이란 게 사람을 어찌나 변하게 하는지!\" 여인이 위를 올려다보며 말했다."]], [["‘It’s the only thing ‘twixt this and the doctor as shall change my condition,’ muttered Mr.", "\"내 처지를 바꿔 놓을 거라곤 여기서 의사한테까지 가는 일밖에 없소.\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 중얼거렸다."]], [["‘I really never saw a man so cross,’ said the buxom female.", "\"이렇게 심술궂은 사람은 정말이지 처음 봐요.\" 풍만한 여인이 말했다."]], [["‘Never mind.", "\"상관없소. 다 내 좋자고 하는 거니까."], ["It’s all for my own good; vich is the reflection vith vich the penitent school-boy comforted his feelin’s ven they flogged him,’ rejoined the old gentleman.", "매를 맞을 때 뉘우치는 학동이 제 심정을 달랜다는 게 바로 이런 생각이지.\" 노인이 받아 말했다."]], [["The buxom female shook her head with a compassionate and sympathising air; and, appealing to Sam, inquired whether his father really ought not to make an effort to keep up, and not give way to that lowness of spirits.", "풍만한 여인은 가엾고 측은해하는 기색으로 고개를 가로젓고는, 샘에게 호소하며 그의 아버지가 정말이지 기운을 차리려고 애써야 하지 않겠느냐고, 저렇게 의기소침에 빠져들면 안 되는 것 아니냐고 물었다."]], [["‘You see, Mr.", "\"있잖아요, 새뮤얼 씨,\" 풍만한 여인이 말했다."], ["Samuel,’ said the buxom female, ‘as I was telling him yesterday, he will feel lonely, he can’t expect but what he should, sir, but he should keep up a good heart, because, dear me, I’m sure we all pity his loss, and are ready to do anything for him; and there’s no situation in life so bad, Mr.", "\"제가 어제 그분께 말씀드렸듯이, 외로우신 거야 당연하지요, 어쩔 수 없는 일이고요, 하지만 마음을 굳게 먹으셔야지요. 어머나, 우리 모두 그분이 상을 당한 걸 안타까워하고 무엇이든 해 드리려는 마음인걸요."], ["Samuel, that it can’t be mended.", "그리고 새뮤얼 씨, 세상에 아무리 나쁜 처지라도 나아질 수 없는 처지란 없는 법이에요. 그건 제 남편이 죽었을 때 어느 무척 훌륭한 분이 제게 해 주신 말씀이지요.\""], ["Which is what a very worthy person said to me when my husband died.’ Here the speaker, putting her hand before her mouth, coughed again, and looked affectionately at the elder Mr. Weller.", "여기서 여인은 손을 입에 갖다 대고 다시 기침을 하고는 웰러 영감을 다정하게 바라보았다."]], [["‘As I don’t rekvire any o’ your conversation just now, mum, vill you have the goodness to re-tire?’ inquired Mr.", "\"지금 당장 당신 얘기 따위는 필요 없으니, 그만 물러나 주시는 친절을 베풀어 주시겠소, 부인?\""], ["Weller, in a grave and steady voice.", "웰러 씨가 진중하고 차분한 목소리로 물었다."]], [["‘Well, Mr.", "\"어머나, 웰러 씨,\""], ["Weller,’ said the buxom female, ‘I’m sure I only spoke to you out of kindness.’", "풍만한 여인이 말했다. \"저는 그저 친절한 마음에서 말씀드린 거예요.\""]], [["‘Wery likely, mum,’ replied Mr.", "\"그렇겠지요, 부인.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다."], ["Weller. ‘Samivel, show the lady out, and shut the door after her.’", "\"새미블, 이 부인을 모셔다 드리고 나가시거든 문을 닫아라.\""]], [["This hint was not lost upon the buxom female; for she at once left the room, and slammed the door behind her, upon which Mr.", "이 암시가 풍만한 여인에게 헛되이 들리지는 않았으니, 그녀는 즉시 방을 나가며 등 뒤로 문을 쾅 닫았다."], ["Weller, senior, falling back in his chair in a violent perspiration, said--", "그러자 웰러 영감은 진땀을 줄줄 흘리며 의자에 털썩 기대어 말했다."]], [["‘Sammy, if I wos to stop here alone vun week--only vun week, my boy-- that ‘ere ‘ooman ‘ud marry me by force and wiolence afore it was over.’", "\"새미야, 내가 여기 혼자 일주일만—단 일주일만, 얘야—머물러 있다간, 저 여편네가 그 안에 힘으로 우격다짐으로 나랑 결혼해 버릴 게다.\""]], [["‘Wot!", "\"아니!"], ["is she so wery fond on you?’ inquired Sam.", "그렇게나 아버지를 좋아한답니까?\" 샘이 물었다."]], [["‘Fond!’ replied his father.", "\"좋아하다마다!\" 그의 아버지가 대답했다."], ["‘I can’t keep her avay from me.", "\"도무지 떼어 놓을 수가 없어."], ["If I was locked up in a fireproof chest vith a patent Brahmin, she’d find means to get at me, Sammy.’", "내가 특허 자물쇠 달린 내화 금고 속에 갇혀 있어도, 저 여자는 기어이 나한테 다가올 방도를 찾아낼 게다, 새미야.\""]], [["‘Wot a thing it is to be so sought arter!’ observed Sam, smiling.", "\"그렇게나 사람 좋아함을 받는다는 게 어떤 일인지!\" 샘이 웃으며 한마디 했다."]], [["‘I don’t take no pride out on it, Sammy,’ replied Mr.", "\"난 거기서 자랑거리라곤 하나도 못 느낀다, 새미야.\" 웰러 씨가 사납게 불을 쑤시며 대답했다."], ["Weller, poking the fire vehemently, ‘it’s a horrid sitiwation.", "\"끔찍한 처지야. 그것 때문에 내가 멀쩡한 내 집에서 쫓겨나다시피 했지."], ["I’m actiwally drove out o’ house and home by it.", "네 가엾은 새어머이 몸에서 숨이 채 빠져나가기도 전에, 어떤 노파는 잼 한 단지를 보내오고, 또 다른 노파는 젤리 한 단지를 보내오고, 또 다른 여편네는 캐모마일 차를 큰 주전자로 한가득 끓여서 제 손으로 직접 들고 오더구나.\""], ["The breath was scarcely out o’ your poor mother- in-law’s body, ven vun old ‘ooman sends me a pot o’ jam, and another a pot o’ jelly, and another brews a blessed large jug o’ camomile-tea, vich she brings in vith her own hands.’ Mr.", "웰러 씨는 진절머리 나는 기색으로 말을 멈추고는 주위를 둘러보며 속삭여 덧붙였다. \"다들 과부였다, 새미야, 죄다 과부였어."], ["Weller paused with an aspect of intense disgust, and looking round, added in a whisper, ‘They wos all widders, Sammy, all on ‘em, ‘cept the camomile-tea vun, as wos a single young lady o’ fifty-three.’", "캐모마일 차 끓여 온 여편네만 빼고. 그건 쉰셋 먹은 미혼 처녀였지.\""]], [["Sam gave a comical look in reply, and the old gentleman having broken an obstinate lump of coal, with a countenance expressive of as much earnestness and malice as if it had been the head of one of the widows last-mentioned, said:", "샘이 익살스러운 표정으로 답하자, 노인은 마지막에 언급한 과부들 중 하나의 머리통이라도 되는 양 진지함과 악의가 가득 어린 얼굴로 고집스러운 석탄 덩이를 깨뜨리고는 말했다."]], [["‘In short, Sammy, I feel that I ain’t safe anyveres but on the box.’", "\"한마디로, 새미야, 난 마부석 위 말고는 어디서도 안전하지가 않다는 걸 느낀다.\""]], [["‘How are you safer there than anyveres else?’ interrupted Sam.", "\"거기가 다른 데보다 어째서 더 안전한데요?\" 샘이 끼어들었다."]], [["‘’Cos a coachman’s a privileged indiwidual,’ replied Mr.", "\"마부란 특권을 누리는 사람이니까.\""], ["Weller, looking fixedly at his son.", "웰러 씨가 아들을 빤히 바라보며 대답했다."], ["‘’Cos a coachman may do vithout suspicion wot other men may not; ‘cos a coachman may be on the wery amicablest terms with eighty mile o’ females, and yet nobody think that he ever means to marry any vun among ‘em.", "\"마부는 다른 사내들이 의심받을 일을 의심 안 받고 할 수 있으니까. 마부는 팔십 마일에 걸친 여자들과 아주 다정한 사이로 지내도, 그중 누구랑 결혼할 마음이라고 생각하는 사람이 아무도 없으니까."], ["And wot other man can say the same, Sammy?’", "다른 어떤 사내가 그런 말을 할 수 있겠냐, 새미야?\""]], [["‘Vell, there’s somethin’ in that,’ said Sam.", "\"그야, 일리가 있네요.\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["‘If your gov’nor had been a coachman,’ reasoned Mr.", "\"네 주인 양반이 마부였더라면,\""], ["Weller, ‘do you s’pose as that ‘ere jury ‘ud ever ha’ conwicted him, s’posin’ it possible as the matter could ha’ gone to that extremity?", "웰러 씨가 따져 말했다. \"설령 그 일이 그 지경까지 갈 수 있었다 쳐도, 그 배심원들이 과연 유죄 판결을 내렸겠냐?"], ["They dustn’t ha’ done it.’", "감히 그러지 못했을 게다.\""]], [["‘Wy not?’ said Sam, rather disparagingly.", "\"왜 못 해요?\" 샘이 다소 깔보듯 말했다."]], [["‘Wy not!’ rejoined Mr.", "\"왜 못 하냐고!\""], ["Weller; ‘’cos it ‘ud ha’ gone agin their consciences.", "웰러 씨가 받아 말했다. \"그건 양심에 거슬리는 일이 됐을 테니까."], ["A reg’lar coachman’s a sort o’ con-nectin’ link betwixt singleness and matrimony, and every practicable man knows it.’", "제대로 된 마부란 독신과 결혼 사이를 잇는 일종의 연결 고리거든. 분별 있는 사람이라면 누구나 그걸 안다.\""]], [["‘Wot!", "\"그러니까!"], ["You mean, they’re gen’ral favorites, and nobody takes adwantage on ‘em, p’raps?’ said Sam.", "마부들이 다들 인기를 누리고, 아무도 그들을 함부로 이용해 먹지 않는다, 뭐 그런 뜻이에요?\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["His father nodded.", "그의 아버지가 고개를 끄덕였다."]], [["‘How it ever come to that ‘ere pass,’ resumed the parent Weller, ‘I can’t say.", "\"어쩌다가 그 지경에까지 이르렀는지는,\" 웰러 영감이 말을 이었다."], ["Wy it is that long-stage coachmen possess such insiniwations, and is alvays looked up to--a-dored I may say--by ev’ry young ‘ooman in ev’ry town he vurks through, I don’t know.", "\"나도 말 못 한다. 어째서 장거리 마부들이 그런 매력을 지녀서, 거쳐 가는 마을마다 젊은 여자란 여자는 죄다 그들을 우러러보는지—숭배한다고까지 할 수 있지—나도 모른다."], ["I only know that so it is.", "그저 그렇다는 것만 안다."], ["It’s a regulation of natur--a dispensary, as your poor mother-in-law used to say.’", "그건 천성의 규율이지—네 가엾은 새어머이 말마따나 제약(製藥)이라고나 할까.\""]], [["‘A dispensation,’ said Sam, correcting the old gentleman.", "\"섭리(攝理)요.\" 샘이 노인의 말을 바로잡았다."]], [["‘Wery good, Samivel, a dispensation if you like it better,’ returned Mr. Weller; ‘I call it a dispensary, and it’s always writ up so, at the places vere they gives you physic for nothin’ in your own bottles; that’s all.’", "\"좋아, 새미블, 네 마음에 더 들면 섭리라고 하자꾸나.\" 웰러 씨가 받아 말했다. \"난 제약이라고 부른다. 네 빈 병에 약을 공짜로 담아 주는 곳마다 늘 그렇게 써 붙여 놨거든. 그뿐이다.\""]], [["With these words, Mr. Weller refilled and relighted his pipe, and once more summoning up a meditative expression of countenance, continued as follows--", "이렇게 말한 뒤 웰러 씨는 파이프에 다시 담배를 채워 불을 붙이고, 또 한 번 사색에 잠긴 표정을 불러일으키며 다음과 같이 말을 이었다."]], [["‘Therefore, my boy, as I do not see the adwisability o’ stoppin here to be married vether I vant to or not, and as at the same time I do not vish to separate myself from them interestin’ members o’ society altogether, I have come to the determination o’ driving the Safety, and puttin’ up vunce more at the Bell Savage, vich is my nat’ral born element, Sammy.’", "\"그러니까, 얘야, 내가 결혼할 마음이 있건 없건 여기 머물러 결혼이나 당하고 있는 게 현명한 일이라고는 보이지 않고, 그렇다고 동시에 그 흥미로운 사회 구성원들과 영영 인연을 끊고 싶지도 않으니, 나는 세이프티 마차를 몰고 다시 한 번 벨 새비지 여관에 묵기로 결심했단다. 그게 내 타고난 천성에 맞는 자리거든, 새미야.\""]], [["‘And wot’s to become o’ the bis’ness?’ inquired Sam.", "\"그럼 이 가게는 어떻게 되는 거예요?\" 샘이 물었다."]], [["‘The bis’ness, Samivel,’ replied the old gentleman, ‘good-vill, stock, and fixters, vill be sold by private contract; and out o’ the money, two hundred pound, agreeable to a rekvest o’ your mother-in-law’s to me, a little afore she died, vill be invested in your name in--What do you call them things agin?’", "\"가게는, 새미블,\" 노인이 대답했다. \"영업권이며 물건이며 비품이며 죄다 개인 간 매매로 팔 게다. 그래서 들어온 돈 중에서 이백 파운드는, 네 새어머이가 죽기 조금 전에 나한테 부탁한 대로, 네 이름으로—그 뭐라더라, 그게?\""]], [["‘Wot things?’ inquired Sam.", "\"그게 뭐요?\" 샘이 물었다."]], [["‘Them things as is always a-goin’ up and down, in the city.’", "\"시내에서 늘 오르락내리락하는 그것 말이다.\""]], [["‘Omnibuses?’ suggested Sam.", "\"합승마차요?\" 샘이 넘겨짚었다."]], [["‘Nonsense,’ replied Mr. Weller.", "\"무슨 헛소리냐.\""], ["‘Them things as is alvays a- fluctooatin’, and gettin’ theirselves inwolved somehow or another vith the national debt, and the chequers bill; and all that.’", "웰러 씨가 대답했다. \"늘 등락을 거듭하고, 어찌어찌해서 국채니 재무 어음이니 하는 것들에 얽혀 들어가는 그것 말이다.\""]], [["‘Oh!", "\"아!"], ["the funds,’ said Sam.", "공채(公債)요.\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["‘Ah!’ rejoined Mr.", "\"옳지!\" 웰러 씨가 받아 말했다."], ["Weller, ‘the funs; two hundred pounds o’ the money is to be inwested for you, Samivel, in the funs; four and a half per cent.", "\"공채지. 그 돈 중에서 이백 파운드를 네 이름으로 공채에 넣을 거다, 새미블."], ["reduced counsels, Sammy.’", "사 푼 오 리짜리 감리 공채에 말이다, 새미야.\""]], [["‘Wery kind o’ the old lady to think o’ me,’ said Sam, ‘and I’m wery much obliged to her.’", "\"저를 생각해 주시다니 새어머이도 참 인정 많은 분이셨네요.\" 샘이 말했다. \"정말 감사하지요.\""]], [["‘The rest will be inwested in my name,’ continued the elder Mr.", "\"나머지는 내 이름으로 넣어 둘 거다.\" 웰러 영감이 말을 이었다."], ["Weller; ‘and wen I’m took off the road, it’ll come to you, so take care you don’t spend it all at vunst, my boy, and mind that no widder gets a inklin’ o’ your fortun’, or you’re done.’", "\"내가 마차 일에서 손을 떼면 그게 너한테로 갈 테니, 한꺼번에 다 써 버리지 않게 조심하고, 어떤 과부도 네 재산 낌새를 채지 못하게 명심해라, 안 그러면 넌 끝장이다.\""]], [["Having delivered this warning, Mr.", "이 경고를 마치고 웰러 씨는 한결 평온해진 얼굴로 다시 파이프를 물었다."], ["Weller resumed his pipe with a more serene countenance; the disclosure of these matters appearing to have eased his mind considerably.", "이런 일들을 털어놓고 나니 마음이 한결 가벼워진 모양이었다."]], [["‘Somebody’s a-tappin’ at the door,’ said Sam.", "\"누가 문을 두드리는데요.\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["‘Let ‘em tap,’ replied his father, with dignity.", "\"두드리라고 해라.\" 그의 아버지가 점잖게 대답했다."]], [["Sam acted upon the direction.", "샘은 그 지시대로 했다."], ["There was another tap, and another, and then a long row of taps; upon which Sam inquired why the tapper was not admitted.", "또 한 번 두드리는 소리가 났고, 또 한 번, 그러고는 길게 줄지어 두드리는 소리가 났다. 그러자 샘이 어째서 두드리는 사람을 들이지 않느냐고 물었다."]], [["‘Hush,’ whispered Mr.", "\"쉿,\" 웰러 씨가 겁먹은 표정으로 속삭였다."], ["Weller, with apprehensive looks, ‘don’t take no notice on ‘em, Sammy, it’s vun o’ the widders, p’raps.’", "\"신경 쓰지 마라, 새미야. 아마 과부들 중 하나일 게다.\""]], [["No notice being taken of the taps, the unseen visitor, after a short lapse, ventured to open the door and peep in.", "두드리는 소리에 아무런 반응이 없자, 보이지 않던 방문객은 잠시 뒤 큰맘 먹고 문을 열어 안을 들여다보았다."], ["It was no female head that was thrust in at the partially-opened door, but the long black locks and red face of Mr. Stiggins.", "반쯤 열린 문틈으로 쑥 들이민 것은 여자의 머리가 아니라, 길고 검은 머리타래에 붉은 얼굴을 한 스티긴스 씨였다."], ["Mr. Weller’s pipe fell from his hands.", "웰러 씨의 손에서 파이프가 떨어졌다."]], [["The reverend gentleman gradually opened the door by almost imperceptible degrees, until the aperture was just wide enough to admit of the passage of his lank body, when he glided into the room and closed it after him, with great care and gentleness.", "목사 양반은 자기 빼빼 마른 몸뚱이가 겨우 빠져나갈 만큼 틈이 벌어질 때까지 거의 알아챌 수 없을 만큼 조금씩 문을 열더니, 방 안으로 슬며시 미끄러져 들어와 무척이나 조심스럽고 부드럽게 등 뒤로 문을 닫았다."], ["Turning towards Sam, and raising his hands and eyes in token of the unspeakable sorrow with which he regarded the calamity that had befallen the family, he carried the high-backed chair to his old corner by the fire, and, seating himself on the very edge, drew forth a brown pocket-handkerchief, and applied the same to his optics.", "샘 쪽으로 돌아서서, 이 집안에 닥친 재앙을 바라보는 말할 수 없는 슬픔의 표시로 두 손과 두 눈을 치켜든 그는, 등받이 높은 의자를 불가의 자기 옛 구석 자리로 옮겨다 놓고 그 맨 끝에 걸터앉아, 갈색 손수건을 꺼내 자기 두 눈에 갖다 댔다."]], [["While this was going forward, the elder Mr. Weller sat back in his chair, with his eyes wide open, his hands planted on his knees, and his whole countenance expressive of absorbing and overwhelming astonishment.", "이러는 동안 웰러 영감은 두 눈을 활짝 뜨고 두 손을 무릎에 짚은 채 의자에 등을 기대고 앉아 있었는데, 그 얼굴 전체에는 사로잡힐 듯 압도적인 경악이 어려 있었다."], ["Sam sat opposite him in perfect silence, waiting, with eager curiosity, for the termination of the scene.", "샘은 그 맞은편에 완전한 침묵 속에 앉아, 이 장면이 어떻게 끝날지 호기심에 가득 차 기다렸다."]], [["Mr. Stiggins kept the brown pocket-handkerchief before his eyes for some minutes, moaning decently meanwhile, and then, mastering his feelings by a strong effort, put it in his pocket and buttoned it up.", "스티긴스 씨는 몇 분 동안 갈색 손수건을 두 눈에 댄 채 그동안 점잖게 신음 소리를 내더니, 이윽고 안간힘을 써 감정을 다스리고는 손수건을 주머니에 넣고 단추를 채웠다."], ["After this, he stirred the fire; after that, he rubbed his hands and looked at Sam.", "그런 다음 그는 불을 쑤셨고, 그 다음에는 두 손을 비비며 샘을 바라보았다."]], [["‘Oh, my young friend,’ said Mr.", "\"오, 나의 젊은 친구여.\""], ["Stiggins, breaking the silence, in a very low voice, ‘here’s a sorrowful affliction!’", "스티긴스 씨가 무척 낮은 목소리로 침묵을 깨며 말했다. \"이 무슨 슬픈 고난이란 말이오!\""]], [["Sam nodded very slightly.", "샘이 아주 살짝 고개를 끄덕였다."]], [["‘For the man of wrath, too!’ added Mr.", "\"게다가 저 분노의 사내에게도 말이오!\""], ["Stiggins; ‘it makes a vessel’s heart bleed!’", "스티긴스 씨가 덧붙였다. \"그릇의 마음이 피를 흘릴 일이로다!\""]], [["Mr. Weller was overheard by his son to murmur something relative to making a vessel’s nose bleed; but Mr. Stiggins heard him not.", "웰러 씨가 그릇의 코에서 피가 나게 해 주겠다는 식의 무언가를 중얼거리는 것을 아들이 엿들었으나, 스티긴스 씨는 듣지 못했다."]], [["‘Do you know, young man,’ whispered Mr.", "\"젊은이, 혹시 아시오?\""], ["Stiggins, drawing his chair closer to Sam, ‘whether she has left Emanuel anything?’", "스티긴스 씨가 의자를 샘 쪽으로 더 가까이 끌어당기며 속삭였다. \"그분이 에마누엘에게 무언가 남기셨는지?\""]], [["‘Who’s he?’ inquired Sam.", "\"그게 누군데요?\" 샘이 물었다."]], [["‘The chapel,’ replied Mr.", "\"예배당 말이오.\" 스티긴스 씨가 대답했다."], ["Stiggins; ‘our chapel; our fold, Mr. Samuel.’", "\"우리 예배당, 우리 양 우리 말이오, 새뮤얼 씨.\""]], [["‘She hasn’t left the fold nothin’, nor the shepherd nothin’, nor the animals nothin’,’ said Sam decisively; ‘nor the dogs neither.’", "\"우리에도 아무것도 안 남겼고, 목자에게도 아무것도 안 남겼고, 짐승들에게도 아무것도 안 남겼어요.\" 샘이 단호하게 말했다. \"개들한테도요.\""]], [["Mr. Stiggins looked slily at Sam; glanced at the old gentleman, who was sitting with his eyes closed, as if asleep; and drawing his chair still nearer, said--", "스티긴스 씨는 샘을 슬쩍 곁눈질하고는, 잠든 듯 눈을 감고 앉아 있는 노인을 흘긋 보더니, 의자를 한층 더 가까이 끌어당기며 말했다."]], [["‘Nothing for me, Mr. Samuel?’", "\"나한테는 아무것도 없소, 새뮤얼 씨?\""]], [["Sam shook his head.", "샘이 고개를 가로저었다."]], [["‘I think there’s something,’ said Stiggins, turning as pale as he could turn.", "\"무언가 있을 텐데.\" 스티긴스가 핼쑥해질 수 있는 만큼 핼쑥해지며 말했다."], ["‘Consider, Mr.", "\"잘 생각해 보시오, 새뮤얼 씨."], ["Samuel; no little token?’", "작은 정표 하나도 없소?\""]], [["‘Not so much as the vorth o’ that ‘ere old umberella o’ yourn,’ replied Sam.", "\"그쪽 그 낡은 우산 값어치만큼도 없어요.\" 샘이 대답했다."]], [["‘Perhaps,’ said Mr.", "\"어쩌면,\" 스티긴스 씨가 잠시 깊이 생각하더니 머뭇거리며 말했다."], ["Stiggins hesitatingly, after a few moments’ deep thought, ‘perhaps she recommended me to the care of the man of wrath, Mr. Samuel?’", "\"어쩌면 그분이 나를 저 분노의 사내가 돌봐 주기를 부탁하셨을지도 모르오, 새뮤얼 씨?\""]], [["‘I think that’s wery likely, from what he said,’ rejoined Sam; ‘he wos a-speakin’ about you, jist now.’", "\"그분이 한 말로 보아 그럴 가능성이 꽤 높아 보이네요.\" 샘이 받아 말했다. \"방금 그쪽 얘기를 하고 계셨거든요.\""]], [["‘Was he, though?’ exclaimed Stiggins, brightening up.", "\"정말이오?\" 스티긴스가 환해지며 외쳤다."], ["‘Ah!", "\"아!"], ["He’s changed, I dare say.", "그분이 변하셨나 보오, 틀림없이."], ["We might live very comfortably together now, Mr. Samuel, eh?", "이제 우리 둘이 아주 편안하게 함께 지낼 수도 있겠소, 새뮤얼 씨, 응?"], ["I could take care of his property when you are away--good care, you see.’", "당신이 떠나 있는 동안 내가 그분의 재산을 돌봐 드릴 수 있을 테고—잘 돌봐 드리는 거지, 알겠소?\""]], [["Heaving a long-drawn sigh, Mr. Stiggins paused for a response.", "길게 끄는 한숨을 내쉬며 스티긴스 씨는 대답을 기다리며 말을 멈추었다."], ["Sam nodded, and Mr. Weller the elder gave vent to an extraordinary sound, which, being neither a groan, nor a grunt, nor a gasp, nor a growl, seemed to partake in some degree of the character of all four.", "샘이 고개를 끄덕이자, 웰러 영감이 기이한 소리를 내뱉었는데, 그것은 신음도 아니고, 끙 하는 소리도 아니고, 헐떡임도 아니고, 으르렁거림도 아니면서, 어느 정도는 그 네 가지 성질을 다 띤 듯한 소리였다."]], [["Mr. Stiggins, encouraged by this sound, which he understood to betoken remorse or repentance, looked about him, rubbed his hands, wept, smiled, wept again, and then, walking softly across the room to a well- remembered shelf in one corner, took down a tumbler, and with great deliberation put four lumps of sugar in it.", "이 소리에 용기를 얻은 스티긴스 씨는, 그것이 회한이나 뉘우침을 나타내는 것이라 여기고는 주위를 둘러보고, 두 손을 비비고, 울고, 웃고, 다시 울더니, 살그머니 방을 가로질러 한구석의 익히 기억하던 선반으로 가서 텀블러 하나를 내려다가 무척 신중하게 그 안에 각설탕 네 덩이를 넣었다."], ["Having got thus far, he looked about him again, and sighed grievously; with that, he walked softly into the bar, and presently returning with the tumbler half full of pine-apple rum, advanced to the kettle which was singing gaily on the hob, mixed his grog, stirred it, sipped it, sat down, and taking a long and hearty pull at the rum-and-water, stopped for breath.", "여기까지 한 다음 그는 다시 한 번 주위를 둘러보고 비통하게 한숨을 쉬었다. 그러고는 살며시 카운터로 걸어 들어갔다가 이내 텀블러에 파인애플 럼을 반쯤 채워 돌아와, 화로 선반에서 흥겹게 노래하던 주전자 쪽으로 다가가 자기 그로그주를 타고, 휘젓고, 한 모금 맛보고, 자리에 앉아, 럼과 물을 길고도 흡족하게 들이켠 뒤, 숨을 돌리려 멈추었다."]], [["The elder Mr. Weller, who still continued to make various strange and uncouth attempts to appear asleep, offered not a single word during these proceedings; but when Stiggins stopped for breath, he darted upon him, and snatching the tumbler from his hand, threw the remainder of the rum-and-water in his face, and the glass itself into the grate.", "여전히 잠든 척하려고 갖가지 기묘하고 어설픈 시도를 거듭하던 웰러 영감은 이 모든 과정 동안 단 한 마디도 하지 않았다."], ["Then, seizing the reverend gentleman firmly by the collar, he suddenly fell to kicking him most furiously, accompanying every application of his top- boot to Mr.", "그러나 스티긴스가 숨을 돌리려 멈추자, 그는 그에게 달려들어 손에서 텀블러를 낚아채 남은 럼과 물을 그의 얼굴에 끼얹고 잔 자체는 난로 속으로 내던졌다."], ["Stiggins’s person, with sundry violent and incoherent anathemas upon his limbs, eyes, and body.", "그러고는 목사 양반의 멱살을 단단히 움켜쥐고 별안간 무지막지하게 발길질을 퍼붓기 시작했으니, 그의 장화가 스티긴스 씨의 몸에 한 번 닿을 때마다 그의 사지며 두 눈이며 몸뚱이에 대고 갖은 사납고 두서없는 저주를 곁들였다."]], [["‘Sammy,’ said Mr.", "\"새미야,\" 웰러 씨가 말했다."], ["Weller, ‘put my hat on tight for me.’", "\"내 모자 좀 단단히 씌워 다오.\""]], [["Sam dutifully adjusted the hat with the long hatband more firmly on his father’s head, and the old gentleman, resuming his kicking with greater agility than before, tumbled with Mr. Stiggins through the bar, and through the passage, out at the front door, and so into the street--the kicking continuing the whole way, and increasing in vehemence, rather than diminishing, every time the top-boot was lifted.", "샘은 효성스럽게 긴 상장 띠가 달린 모자를 아버지의 머리에 한층 단단히 고쳐 씌웠고, 노인은 전보다 한층 민첩하게 발길질을 다시 시작하며 스티긴스 씨와 함께 카운터를 지나, 복도를 지나, 정문 밖으로, 그렇게 거리로 굴러나갔다—발길질은 그 내내 이어졌고, 장화를 들어 올릴 때마다 줄어들기는커녕 오히려 그 기세가 더해 갔다."]], [["It was a beautiful and exhilarating sight to see the red-nosed man writhing in Mr. Weller’s grasp, and his whole frame quivering with anguish as kick followed kick in rapid succession; it was a still more exciting spectacle to behold Mr.", "발길질이 빠르게 잇따르는 가운데 빨간 코의 사내가 웰러 씨의 손아귀에서 몸부림치며 온몸을 고통에 떠는 광경은 아름답고도 흥겨운 구경거리였다."], ["Weller, after a powerful struggle, immersing Mr. Stiggins’s head in a horse-trough full of water, and holding it there, until he was half suffocated.", "한바탕 격렬한 몸싸움 끝에 웰러 씨가 스티긴스 씨의 머리를 물이 가득한 말구유에 처박고 그가 반쯤 질식할 때까지 거기 붙들고 있는 광경은 한층 더 짜릿한 장관이었다."]], [["‘There!’ said Mr.", "\"옜다!\""], ["Weller, throwing all his energy into one most complicated kick, as he at length permitted Mr.", "웰러 씨가 마침내 스티긴스 씨에게 구유에서 머리를 빼게 허락하며 가장 복잡한 발길질 한 방에 온 힘을 쏟아부으며 말했다."], ["Stiggins to withdraw his head from the trough, ‘send any vun o’ them lazy shepherds here, and I’ll pound him to a jelly first, and drownd him artervards!", "\"저 게으른 목자란 작자들 아무나 여기 보내 봐라, 먼저 곤죽이 되도록 두들겨 패고 나서 물에 처박아 죽여 버릴 테니!"], ["Sammy, help me in, and fill me a small glass of brandy.", "새미야, 나 좀 부축해서 들여보내 주고, 브랜디 한 잔 작은 걸로 따라 다오."], ["I’m out o’ breath, my boy.’", "숨이 차는구나, 얘야.\""]], [["CHAPTER LIII. COMPRISING THE FINAL EXIT OF MR. JINGLE AND JOB TROTTER, WITH A GREAT MORNING OF BUSINESS IN GRAY’S INN SQUARE--CONCLUDING WITH A DOUBLE KNOCK AT MR. PERKER’S DOOR\nWhen Arabella, after some gentle preparation and many assurances that there was not the least occasion for being low-spirited, was at length made acquainted by Mr. Pickwick with the unsatisfactory result of his visit to Birmingham, she burst into tears, and sobbing aloud, lamented in moving terms that she should have been the unhappy cause of any estrangement between a father and his son.", "아라벨라는 픽윅 씨가 부드러운 운을 떼고 풀 죽을 일이 조금도 없다고 거듭 다독인 끝에 그의 버밍엄 방문이 만족스럽지 못한 결과로 끝났다는 것을 마침내 알게 되자, 왈칵 눈물을 쏟으며 소리 내어 흐느꼈고, 자기가 아버지와 아들 사이를 갈라놓은 불행한 원인이 되었다며 가슴 아픈 말로 한탄했다."]], [["‘My dear girl,’ said Mr.", "\"아가야,\""], ["Pickwick kindly, ‘it is no fault of yours.", "픽윅 씨가 다정하게 말했다."], ["It was impossible to foresee that the old gentleman would be so strongly prepossessed against his son’s marriage, you know.", "\"그건 네 잘못이 아니다. 그 노신사가 아들의 결혼에 그토록 강하게 반대하고 있을 줄 누가 미리 알 수 있었겠느냐, 안 그러냐."], ["I am sure,’ added Mr.", "분명히 말하건대,\""], ["Pickwick, glancing at her pretty face, ‘he can have very little idea of the pleasure he denies himself.’", "픽윅 씨는 그녀의 어여쁜 얼굴을 바라보며 덧붙였다. \"그는 자기가 스스로에게서 빼앗고 있는 기쁨이 어떤 것인지 거의 짐작도 못 하고 있을 게다.\""]], [["‘Oh, my dear Mr.", "\"오, 픽윅 선생님,\""], ["Pickwick,’ said Arabella, ‘what shall we do, if he continues to be angry with us?’", "아라벨라가 말했다. \"그분이 계속 저희에게 화를 내신다면 저희는 어떻게 해야 할까요?\""]], [["‘Why, wait patiently, my dear, until he thinks better of it,’ replied Mr.", "\"이런, 그분이 마음을 고쳐먹을 때까지 참고 기다리는 거지, 아가야.\""], ["Pickwick cheerfully.", "픽윅 씨가 쾌활하게 대답했다."]], [["‘But, dear Mr.", "\"하지만 픽윅 선생님, 시아버님께서 도움을 거두신다면 너새니얼은 어떻게 되는 거죠?\""], ["Pickwick, what is to become of Nathaniel if his father withdraws his assistance?’ urged Arabella.", "아라벨라가 채근했다."]], [["‘In that case, my love,’ rejoined Mr.", "\"그렇게 된다면, 얘야,\""], ["Pickwick, ‘I will venture to prophesy that he will find some other friend who will not be backward in helping him to start in the world.’", "픽윅 씨가 받아 말했다. \"감히 예언하건대, 그를 도와 세상에 첫발을 내딛게 하는 데 주저하지 않을 다른 친구를 찾게 될 게다.\""]], [["The significance of this reply was not so well disguised by Mr. Pickwick but that Arabella understood it.", "이 대답에 담긴 뜻을 픽윅 씨가 그리 잘 감추지는 못한 터라 아라벨라는 그것을 알아차렸다."], ["So, throwing her arms round his neck, and kissing him affectionately, she sobbed louder than before.", "그래서 그녀는 두 팔로 그의 목을 끌어안고 다정하게 입을 맞추며 전보다 더 크게 흐느꼈다."]], [["‘Come, come,’ said Mr.", "\"자, 자,\" 픽윅 씨가 그녀의 손을 잡으며 말했다."], ["Pickwick taking her hand, ‘we will wait here a few days longer, and see whether he writes or takes any other notice of your husband’s communication.", "\"여기서 며칠만 더 기다려 보자꾸나. 그분이 편지를 쓰는지, 아니면 네 남편이 보낸 소식에 어떤 다른 응답이라도 하는지 두고 보자."], ["If not, I have thought of half a dozen plans, any one of which would make you happy at once.", "그렇지 않으면, 너를 당장 행복하게 해 줄 방법을 여섯 가지쯤 생각해 두었으니 그중 어느 것이든 쓰면 된다."], ["There, my dear, there!’", "자, 아가야, 자!\""]], [["With these words, Mr. Pickwick gently pressed Arabella’s hand, and bade her dry her eyes, and not distress her husband.", "이렇게 말하며 픽윅 씨는 아라벨라의 손을 가만히 쥐었고, 눈물을 닦고 남편을 괴롭히지 말라고 일렀다."], ["Upon which, Arabella, who was one of the best little creatures alive, put her handkerchief in her reticule, and by the time Mr. Winkle joined them, exhibited in full lustre the same beaming smiles and sparkling eyes that had originally captivated him.", "그러자 세상에서 가장 사랑스러운 작은 사람 중 하나인 아라벨라는 손수건을 손가방에 넣었고, 윈클 씨가 그들에게 합류했을 무렵에는 애초에 그를 사로잡았던 바로 그 환한 미소와 반짝이는 눈빛을 온전한 광채로 드러내 보였다."]], [["‘This is a distressing predicament for these young people,’ thought Mr.", "\"이 젊은이들에게는 참으로 난처한 곤경이로구나.\""], ["Pickwick, as he dressed himself next morning.", "이튿날 아침 옷을 차려입으며 픽윅 씨는 생각했다."], ["‘I’ll walk up to Perker’s, and consult him about the matter.’", "\"퍼커한테 가서 이 일을 상의해 봐야겠다.\""]], [["As Mr. Pickwick was further prompted to betake himself to Gray’s Inn Square by an anxious desire to come to a pecuniary settlement with the kind-hearted little attorney without further delay, he made a hurried breakfast, and executed his intention so speedily, that ten o’clock had not struck when he reached Gray’s Inn.", "게다가 픽윅 씨는 그 마음씨 고운 작은 변호사와 더 미루지 않고 금전 문제를 정산하고 싶다는 조바심에 더욱 그레이즈 인 광장으로 발길을 재촉하게 되어, 아침을 서둘러 먹고는 그 작정을 어찌나 빨리 실행에 옮겼던지, 그레이즈 인에 도착했을 때는 아직 열 시도 치지 않았다."]], [["It still wanted ten minutes to the hour when he had ascended the staircase on which Perker’s chambers were.", "퍼커의 사무실이 있는 계단을 다 올라갔을 때만 해도 아직 정각까지 십 분이 남아 있었다."], ["The clerks had not arrived yet, and he beguiled the time by looking out of the staircase window.", "사무원들은 아직 도착하지 않았고, 그는 계단 창밖을 내다보며 시간을 보냈다."]], [["The healthy light of a fine October morning made even the dingy old houses brighten up a little; some of the dusty windows actually looking almost cheerful as the sun’s rays gleamed upon them. Clerk after clerk hastened into the square by one or other of the entrances, and looking up at the Hall clock, accelerated or decreased his rate of walking according to the time at which his office hours nominally commenced; the half-past nine o’clock people suddenly becoming very brisk, and the ten o’clock gentlemen falling into a pace of most aristocratic slowness. The clock struck ten, and clerks poured in faster than ever, each one in a greater perspiration than his predecessor. The noise of unlocking and opening doors echoed and re-echoed on every side; heads appeared as if by magic in every window; the porters took up their stations for the day; the slipshod laundresses hurried off; the postman ran from house to house; and the whole legal hive was in a bustle.", "화창한 시월 아침의 건강한 햇살은 칙칙하고 낡은 집들조차 조금은 환하게 밝혀 주었다. 먼지 낀 창문 몇몇은 햇빛이 그 위에서 반짝일 때 정말이지 거의 명랑해 보일 정도였다. 사무원들이 잇따라 이쪽저쪽 입구로 광장에 황급히 들어서서는 회관 시계를 올려다보며, 명목상 자기 근무 시간이 시작되는 때에 맞추어 걸음을 빨리하기도 하고 늦추기도 했다. 아홉 시 반인 사람들은 갑자기 무척 부산해졌고, 열 시인 신사들은 더없이 귀족적인 느릿함의 보조로 접어들었다. 시계가 열 시를 쳤고, 사무원들은 그 어느 때보다 빠르게 밀려들었는데, 저마다 앞사람보다 더 땀을 뻘뻘 흘리고 있었다. 문을 따고 여는 소리가 사방에서 메아리치고 또 메아리쳤다. 마치 마법이라도 부린 듯 창마다 사람 머리가 나타났다. 수위들은 그날의 자기 자리를 잡았고, 슬리퍼를 질질 끄는 세탁부들은 부랴부랴 떠났으며, 우편배달부는 이 집 저 집 뛰어다녔다. 법조계라는 벌집 전체가 분주했다."]], [["‘You’re early, Mr.", "\"일찍 오셨군요, 픽윅 선생님.\""], ["Pickwick,’ said a voice behind him.", "그의 등 뒤에서 한 목소리가 말했다."]], [["‘Ah, Mr.", "\"아, 로튼 씨.\""], ["Lowten,’ replied that gentleman, looking round, and recognising his old acquaintance.", "그 신사는 돌아보며 옛 지인을 알아보고 대답했다."]], [["‘Precious warm walking, isn’t it?’ said Lowten, drawing a Bramah key from his pocket, with a small plug therein, to keep the dust out.", "\"걷기에 무지하게 더운 날씨죠, 안 그래요?\" 로튼은 먼지가 들어가지 않게 작은 마개를 끼운 브라마 열쇠를 주머니에서 꺼내며 말했다."]], [["‘You appear to feel it so,’ rejoined Mr.", "\"그렇게 느끼시는 모양이군요.\""], ["Pickwick, smiling at the clerk, who was literally red-hot.", "픽윅 씨는 말 그대로 벌겋게 달아오른 사무원을 보고 미소 지으며 받아 말했다."]], [["‘I’ve come along, rather, I can tell you,’ replied Lowten.", "\"꽤나 빨리 걸어왔거든요, 정말이지.\" 로튼이 대답했다."], ["‘It went the half hour as I came through the Polygon.", "\"폴리곤을 지나올 때 반 시를 쳤어요."], ["I’m here before him, though, so I don’t mind.’", "그래도 제가 그분보다 먼저 와 있으니 상관없죠.\""]], [["Comforting himself with this reflection, Mr.", "이런 생각으로 스스로를 위안하며 로튼 씨는 문 열쇠에서 마개를 빼냈다."], ["Lowten extracted the plug from the door-key; having opened the door, replugged and repocketed his Bramah, and picked up the letters which the postman had dropped through the box, he ushered Mr.", "문을 열고 나서 브라마 열쇠에 다시 마개를 끼워 주머니에 넣은 다음, 우편배달부가 우편함 구멍으로 떨어뜨린 편지들을 집어 들고는 픽윅 씨를 사무실로 안내했다."], ["Pickwick into the office. Here, in the twinkling of an eye, he divested himself of his coat, put on a threadbare garment, which he took out of a desk, hung up his hat, pulled forth a few sheets of cartridge and blotting-paper in alternate layers, and, sticking a pen behind his ear, rubbed his hands with an air of great satisfaction.", "여기서 그는 눈 깜짝할 사이에 외투를 벗고, 책상에서 꺼낸 올이 다 드러난 옷을 걸치고, 모자를 걸고, 두꺼운 종이와 압지를 번갈아 겹친 몇 장을 끄집어내고는, 펜 한 자루를 귀 뒤에 꽂은 채 매우 흡족한 기색으로 두 손을 비볐다."]], [["‘There, you see, Mr.", "\"자, 보세요, 픽윅 선생님.\""], ["Pickwick,’ he said, ‘now I’m complete.", "그가 말했다. \"이제 만반의 준비가 됐습니다."], ["I’ve got my office coat on, and my pad out, and let him come as soon as he likes.", "사무실 외투도 걸쳤고, 받침판도 꺼내 놨으니, 그분이 오고 싶을 때 오시면 되죠."], ["You haven’t got a pinch of snuff about you, have you?’", "혹시 코담배 한 줌 가지고 계시진 않죠?\""]], [["‘No, I have not,’ replied Mr.", "\"아니, 없는데요.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘I’m sorry for it,’ said Lowten.", "\"그것 참 아쉽군요.\" 로튼이 말했다."], ["‘Never mind.", "\"괜찮습니다."], ["I’ll run out presently, and get a bottle of soda.", "이따 잠깐 나가서 소다수나 한 병 사 오죠."], ["Don’t I look rather queer about the eyes, Mr. Pickwick?’", "저, 제 눈 좀 이상해 보이지 않나요, 픽윅 선생님?\""]], [["The individual appealed to, surveyed Mr. Lowten’s eyes from a distance, and expressed his opinion that no unusual queerness was perceptible in those features.", "질문을 받은 당사자는 멀찍이서 로튼 씨의 두 눈을 살펴보고는, 그 이목구비에 별다른 이상은 보이지 않는다는 의견을 밝혔다."]], [["‘I’m glad of it,’ said Lowten.", "\"그것 다행이네요.\" 로튼이 말했다."], ["‘We were keeping it up pretty tolerably at the Stump last night, and I’m rather out of sorts this morning.", "\"어젯밤 스텀프에서 꽤나 흥청거렸거든요. 그래서 오늘 아침엔 컨디션이 영 별로예요."], ["Perker’s been about that business of yours, by the bye.’", "그건 그렇고, 퍼커 씨가 선생님 그 일을 봐 주셨답니다.\""]], [["‘What business?’ inquired Mr.", "\"무슨 일이요?\""], ["Pickwick. ‘Mrs.", "픽윅 씨가 물었다."], ["Bardell’s costs?’", "\"바델 부인의 소송 비용 말입니까?\""]], [["‘No, I don’t mean that,’ replied Mr.", "\"아니요, 그게 아니라.\" 로튼 씨가 대답했다."], ["Lowten.", "\"선생님 부탁으로 어음 할인업자한테 파운드당 십 실링을 쳐서 빚을 갚아 준 그 손님 말입니다."], ["‘About getting that customer that we paid the ten shillings in the pound to the bill- discounter for, on your account--to get him out of the Fleet, you know-- about getting him to Demerara.’", "그를 플리트에서 빼내 주는 일이요, 아시죠. 그를 데메라라로 보내는 그 일 말입니다.\""]], [["‘Oh, Mr. Jingle,’ said Mr.", "\"아, 징글 씨요.\""], ["Pickwick hastily.", "픽윅 씨가 다급히 말했다."], ["‘Yes.", "\"그래요."], ["Well?’", "그래서요?\""]], [["‘Well, it’s all arranged,’ said Lowten, mending his pen.", "\"그래서, 다 정리됐습니다.\" 로튼이 펜촉을 다듬으며 말했다."], ["‘The agent at Liverpool said he had been obliged to you many times when you were in business, and he would be glad to take him on your recommendation.’", "\"리버풀 대리인이 말하길, 선생님이 사업하실 때 여러 번 신세를 졌으니 선생님 추천이라면 기꺼이 그를 받아들이겠답니다.\""]], [["‘That’s well,’ said Mr.", "\"그것 잘됐군요.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["‘I am delighted to hear it.’", "\"그 소식을 들으니 기쁩니다.\""]], [["‘But I say,’ resumed Lowten, scraping the back of the pen preparatory to making a fresh split, ‘what a soft chap that other is!’", "\"근데 말이죠,\" 로튼이 펜촉을 새로 가르려고 뒷면을 긁으며 말을 이었다. \"그 다른 친구는 정말 물러 터진 작자더군요!\""]], [["‘Which other?’", "\"어느 다른 친구요?\""]], [["‘Why, that servant, or friend, or whatever he is; you know, Trotter.’", "\"아니, 그 하인인지 친구인지 뭔지 하는 사람 말입니다. 아시잖아요, 트로터요.\""]], [["‘Ah!’ said Mr.", "\"아!\""], ["Pickwick, with a smile.", "픽윅 씨가 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["‘I always thought him the reverse.’", "\"나는 늘 그를 그 반대라고 생각했는데요.\""]], [["‘Well, and so did I, from what little I saw of him,’ replied Lowten, ‘it only shows how one may be deceived.", "\"글쎄요, 저도 잠깐 본 바로는 그랬는데요.\" 로튼이 대답했다."], ["What do you think of his going to Demerara, too?’", "\"사람이 얼마나 속을 수 있는지를 보여 줄 뿐이죠. 글쎄, 그 사람도 데메라라로 간다는 거 어떻게 생각하세요?\""]], [["‘What!", "\"뭐라고요!"], ["And giving up what was offered him here!’ exclaimed Mr.", "여기서 제안받은 자리를 마다하고 말입니까!\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 외쳤다."]], [["‘Treating Perker’s offer of eighteen bob a week, and a rise if he behaved himself, like dirt,’ replied Lowten.", "\"퍼커 씨가 주당 십팔 실링에, 처신만 잘하면 올려 주겠다고 한 제안을 똥처럼 취급했지 뭡니까.\" 로튼이 대답했다."], ["‘He said he must go along with the other one, and so they persuaded Perker to write again, and they’ve got him something on the same estate; not near so good, Perker says, as a convict would get in New South Wales, if he appeared at his trial in a new suit of clothes.’", "\"그가 말하길 그 다른 사람과 꼭 함께 가야겠다는 겁니다. 그래서 그들이 퍼커 씨를 졸라 다시 편지를 쓰게 했고, 같은 농장에 그 사람 일자리를 하나 마련해 주긴 했는데, 퍼커 씨 말로는 죄수가 새 옷을 차려입고 재판에 나타나서 뉴사우스웨일스에서 얻는 자리만도 훨씬 못한 거랍니다.\""]], [["‘Foolish fellow,’ said Mr.", "\"어리석은 친구로군.\""], ["Pickwick, with glistening eyes.", "픽윅 씨가 눈을 반짝이며 말했다."], ["‘Foolish fellow.’", "\"어리석은 친구야.\""]], [["‘Oh, it’s worse than foolish; it’s downright sneaking, you know,’ replied Lowten, nibbing the pen with a contemptuous face. ‘He says that he’s the only friend he ever had, and he’s attached to him, and all that. Friendship’s a very good thing in its way--we are all very friendly and comfortable at the Stump, for instance, over our grog, where every man pays for himself; but damn hurting yourself for anybody else, you know! No man should have more than two attachments--the first, to number one, and the second to the ladies; that’s what I say--ha! ha!’ Mr. Lowten concluded with a loud laugh, half in jocularity, and half in derision, which was prematurely cut short by the sound of Perker’s footsteps on the stairs, at the first approach of which, he vaulted on his stool with an agility most remarkable, and wrote intensely.", "\"오, 어리석은 정도가 아니죠. 그건 정말 비굴한 짓이라니까요, 아시죠.\" 로튼이 경멸 어린 표정으로 펜촉을 다듬으며 대답했다. \"그 사람이 자기한테 있었던 유일한 친구라느니, 정이 들었다느니 어쩌고 하더군요. 우정이란 게 그 나름으로는 아주 좋은 거죠. 이를테면 우리도 스텀프에서 그로그주를 앞에 놓고 다들 우애 좋고 편안하게 지냅니다. 거기선 다들 제 몫은 제가 내지만 말이죠. 하지만 빌어먹을, 남을 위해 자기를 해치다니, 원! 사람이라면 애착은 두 가지 이상 두면 안 되는 겁니다. 첫째는 제일인자, 곧 자기 자신한테, 둘째는 숙녀분들한테. 제 말이 그겁니다. 하하!\" 로튼 씨는 반은 익살, 반은 조롱이 섞인 큰 웃음으로 말을 맺었는데, 그 웃음은 계단을 오르는 퍼커의 발소리에 미처 끝나기도 전에 뚝 끊겼다. 그 소리가 처음 다가오자마자 그는 놀랄 만큼 날렵하게 의자 위로 훌쩍 올라앉아 맹렬히 글을 쓰기 시작했다."]], [["The greeting between Mr.", "픽윅 씨와 그의 법률 고문 사이의 인사는 따뜻하고 정겨웠다."], ["Pickwick and his professional adviser was warm and cordial; the client was scarcely ensconced in the attorney’s arm- chair, however, when a knock was heard at the door, and a voice inquired whether Mr. Perker was within.", "그러나 의뢰인이 변호사의 안락의자에 채 자리 잡기도 전에 문을 두드리는 소리가 들리더니, 퍼커 씨가 안에 계시냐고 묻는 목소리가 났다."]], [["‘Hark!’ said Perker, ‘that’s one of our vagabond friends--Jingle himself, my dear Sir. Will you see him?’", "\"들어 보세요!\" 퍼커가 말했다. \"우리의 그 떠돌이 친구로군요. 바로 징글입니다, 선생님. 만나 보시겠습니까?\""]], [["‘What do you think?’ inquired Mr.", "\"어떻게 생각하세요?\""], ["Pickwick, hesitating.", "픽윅 씨가 머뭇거리며 물었다."]], [["‘Yes, I think you had better.", "\"네, 만나 보시는 게 좋겠어요."], ["Here, you Sir, what’s your name, walk in, will you?’", "거기, 선생, 이름이 뭐든 간에, 들어오시겠소?\""]], [["In compliance with this unceremonious invitation, Jingle and Job walked into the room, but, seeing Mr. Pickwick, stopped short in some confusion.", "이 격식 없는 초대에 따라 징글과 잡이 방으로 걸어 들어왔으나, 픽윅 씨를 보고는 다소 당황하여 우뚝 멈춰 섰다."]], [["‘Well,’ said Perker, ‘don’t you know that gentleman?’", "\"이런,\" 퍼커가 말했다. \"저 신사분을 모르시오?\""]], [["‘Good reason to,’ replied Mr.", "\"잘 알다마다요.\""], ["Jingle, stepping forward. ‘Mr.", "징글이 앞으로 나서며 대답했다."], ["Pickwick-- deepest obligations--life preserver--made a man of me--you shall never repent it, Sir.’", "\"픽윅 씨, 더없는 은혜를, 생명의 은인을, 저를 사람으로 만들어 주신 분을, 절대 후회하실 일 없게 하겠습니다, 선생님.\""]], [["‘I am happy to hear you say so,’ said Mr.", "\"그렇게 말해 주니 기쁘군요.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick. ‘You look much better.’", "\"한결 좋아 보입니다.\""]], [["‘Thanks to you, sir--great change--Majesty’s Fleet--unwholesome place-- very,’ said Jingle, shaking his head.", "\"선생님 덕분이지요. 엄청난 변화입니다."], ["He was decently and cleanly dressed, and so was Job, who stood bolt upright behind him, staring at Mr.", "폐하의 플리트, 건강에 안 좋은 곳, 아주.\" 징글이 고개를 저으며 말했다."], ["Pickwick with a visage of iron.", "그는 단정하고 깔끔하게 차려입고 있었고, 그의 뒤에 꼿꼿이 서서 쇠처럼 굳은 얼굴로 픽윅 씨를 응시하고 있는 잡도 마찬가지였다."]], [["‘When do they go to Liverpool?’ inquired Mr.", "\"저들이 리버풀로 떠나는 게 언제죠?\""], ["Pickwick, half aside to Perker.", "픽윅 씨가 퍼커에게 반쯤 곁눈질하며 물었다."]], [["‘This evening, Sir, at seven o’clock,’ said Job, taking one step forward.", "\"오늘 저녁입니다, 선생님. 일곱 시에요.\""], ["‘By the heavy coach from the city, Sir.’", "잡이 한 걸음 앞으로 나서며 말했다. \"시내에서 출발하는 짐마차 편으로요, 선생님.\""]], [["‘Are your places taken?’", "\"좌석은 예약했고?\""]], [["‘They are, sir,’ replied Job.", "\"네, 했습니다, 선생님.\" 잡이 대답했다."]], [["‘You have fully made up your mind to go?’", "\"떠나기로 완전히 마음을 굳혔나?\""]], [["‘I have sir,’ answered Job.", "\"네, 그렇습니다, 선생님.\" 잡이 답했다."]], [["‘With regard to such an outfit as was indispensable for Jingle,’ said Perker, addressing Mr.", "\"징글에게 꼭 필요한 채비 말씀인데요,\" 퍼커가 픽윅 씨에게 큰 소리로 말했다."], ["Pickwick aloud. ‘I have taken upon myself to make an arrangement for the deduction of a small sum from his quarterly salary, which, being made only for one year, and regularly remitted, will provide for that expense.", "\"제가 나서서 그의 분기별 봉급에서 소액을 공제하도록 조처를 해 두었습니다."], ["I entirely disapprove of your doing anything for him, my dear sir, which is not dependent on his own exertions and good conduct.’", "단 일 년만 떼고 꼬박꼬박 송금하면 그 비용을 충당할 수 있습니다. 그의 노력과 선행에 달려 있지 않은 일은 그를 위해 무엇 하나라도 하시는 것을 저는 전적으로 반대합니다, 선생님.\""]], [["‘Certainly,’ interposed Jingle, with great firmness.", "\"물론입니다.\" 징글이 아주 단호하게 끼어들었다."], ["‘Clear head--man of the world--quite right--perfectly.’", "\"명석한 머리, 세상 물정에 밝은 분, 지당하신 말씀, 완벽합니다.\""]], [["‘By compounding with his creditor, releasing his clothes from the pawnbroker’s, relieving him in prison, and paying for his passage,’ continued Perker, without noticing Jingle’s observation, ‘you have already lost upwards of fifty pounds.’", "\"채권자와 화해하고, 전당포에서 그의 옷을 되찾고, 감옥에서 그를 구해 내고, 뱃삯까지 치르느라,\" 퍼커가 징글의 말에 아랑곳없이 계속했다. \"선생님은 이미 오십 파운드 넘게 잃으셨습니다.\""]], [["‘Not lost,’ said Jingle hastily, ‘Pay it all--stick to business--cash up--every farthing.", "\"잃은 게 아니죠.\" 징글이 다급히 말했다."], ["Yellow fever, perhaps--can’t help that--if not--’ Here Mr.", "\"전부 갚습니다, 사업에 매진해서, 현금으로 갚습니다, 한 푼도 빠짐없이."], ["Jingle paused, and striking the crown of his hat with great violence, passed his hand over his eyes, and sat down.", "황열병에 걸릴지도 모르지만, 그건 어쩔 수 없죠, 만약 안 걸리면.\" 여기서 징글 씨는 말을 멈추고, 모자 꼭대기를 세게 후려치고는, 손으로 두 눈을 훔치고 자리에 앉았다."]], [["‘He means to say,’ said Job, advancing a few paces, ‘that if he is not carried off by the fever, he will pay the money back again.", "\"저 사람 말은요,\" 잡이 몇 걸음 다가서며 말했다."], ["If he lives, he will, Mr.", "\"열병으로 죽지만 않으면 그 돈을 도로 갚겠다는 겁니다."], ["Pickwick. I will see it done.", "살아만 있으면 갚을 겁니다, 픽윅 씨."], ["I know he will, Sir,’ said Job, with energy.", "제가 반드시 그렇게 되도록 지켜보겠습니다. 저는 그가 갚으리란 걸 압니다, 선생님.\""], ["‘I could undertake to swear it.’", "잡이 힘주어 말했다. \"맹세코 장담할 수 있습니다.\""]], [["‘Well, well,’ said Mr.", "\"좋네, 좋아.\""], ["Pickwick, who had been bestowing a score or two of frowns upon Perker, to stop his summary of benefits conferred, which the little attorney obstinately disregarded, ‘you must be careful not to play any more desperate cricket matches, Mr.", "픽윅 씨는 그동안 베푼 은혜를 일일이 요약하는 것을 그만두라고 퍼커에게 스무 번도 넘게 눈살을 찌푸려 보였으나 그 작은 변호사가 고집스레 무시한 터라 이렇게 말했다. \"이제 다시는 그런 무모한 크리켓 시합을 하거나 토머스 블레이조 경과 다시 사귀는 일은 조심해야 하네, 징글 씨."], ["Jingle, or to renew your acquaintance with Sir Thomas Blazo, and I have little doubt of your preserving your health.’", "그러면 건강을 지키는 데 별 어려움은 없으리라 보네.\""]], [["Mr. Jingle smiled at this sally, but looked rather foolish notwithstanding; so Mr.", "징글 씨는 이 농담에 미소를 지었으나, 그래도 다소 멋쩍은 표정이었다."], ["Pickwick changed the subject by saying--", "그래서 픽윅 씨는 화제를 바꾸어 말했다."]], [["‘You don’t happen to know, do you, what has become of another friend of yours--a more humble one, whom I saw at Rochester?’", "\"혹시 자네의 또 다른 친구, 좀 더 처지가 딱한 친구 말인데, 내가 로체스터에서 본 그 사람이 어떻게 되었는지 알고 있나?\""]], [["‘Dismal Jemmy?’ inquired Jingle.", "\"음울한 제미 말입니까?\" 징글이 물었다."]], [["‘Yes.’", "\"그래.\""]], [["Jingle shook his head.", "징글이 고개를 저었다."]], [["‘Clever rascal--queer fellow, hoaxing genius--Job’s brother.’", "\"영리한 악당, 별난 녀석, 사람 골리는 천재, 잡의 형입니다.\""]], [["‘Job’s brother!’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"잡의 형!\" 픽윅 씨가 외쳤다."], ["Pickwick. ‘Well, now I look at him closely, there is a likeness.’", "\"허, 이제 자세히 보니 정말 닮은 데가 있군.\""]], [["‘We were always considered like each other, Sir,’ said Job, with a cunning look just lurking in the corners of his eyes, ‘only I was really of a serious nature, and he never was.", "\"저희는 늘 서로 닮았다는 말을 들었습니다, 선생님.\" 잡이 눈가에 살짝 교활한 기색을 드리운 채 말했다."], ["He emigrated to America, Sir, in consequence of being too much sought after here, to be comfortable; and has never been heard of since.’", "\"다만 저는 정말로 진지한 천성이었고 그는 한 번도 그런 적이 없었지요. 그는 여기서 너무 많이 쫓겨 다녀 편할 수가 없게 된 통에 미국으로 이민을 갔고, 그 뒤로는 소식이 끊겼습니다, 선생님.\""]], [["‘That accounts for my not having received the “page from the romance of real life,” which he promised me one morning when he appeared to be contemplating suicide on Rochester Bridge, I suppose,’ said Mr.", "\"그렇다면 그가 로체스터 다리에서 자살을 궁리하는 듯 보이던 어느 아침에 내게 약속했던 '실제 인생이라는 소설의 한 페이지'를 내가 끝내 받지 못한 까닭이 설명되는군.\""], ["Pickwick, smiling.", "픽윅 씨가 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["‘I need not inquire whether his dismal behaviour was natural or assumed.’", "\"그의 음울한 행동이 천성이었는지 꾸민 것이었는지 굳이 물어볼 필요는 없겠어.\""]], [["‘He could assume anything, Sir,’ said Job.", "\"그는 무엇이든 꾸며 낼 수 있었습니다, 선생님.\" 잡이 말했다."], ["‘You may consider yourself very fortunate in having escaped him so easily.", "\"그를 그토록 쉽게 피하신 것을 큰 행운으로 여기셔도 됩니다. 가까이 지냈더라면 그는 저보다도 더 위험한 지인이 되었을 겁니다.\""], ["On intimate terms he would have been even a more dangerous acquaintance than--’ Job looked at Jingle, hesitated, and finally added, ‘than--than-myself even.’", "잡은 징글을 바라보며 머뭇거리다가, 마침내 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"저보다도, 저보다도 말입니다.\""]], [["‘A hopeful family yours, Mr.", "\"가망 넘치는 집안이로군요, 트로터 씨.\""], ["Trotter,’ said Perker, sealing a letter which he had just finished writing.", "퍼커가 막 다 쓴 편지를 봉하며 말했다."]], [["‘Yes, Sir,’ replied Job.", "\"네, 그렇습니다, 선생님.\" 잡이 대답했다."], ["‘Very much so.’", "\"매우 그렇지요.\""]], [["‘Well,’ said the little man, laughing, ‘I hope you are going to disgrace it.", "\"흠,\" 작은 남자가 웃으며 말했다."], ["Deliver this letter to the agent when you reach Liverpool, and let me advise you, gentlemen, not to be too knowing in the West Indies.", "\"당신이 그 집안의 명예를 더럽혀 주기를 바라겠소. 리버풀에 도착하면 이 편지를 대리인에게 전하시오."], ["If you throw away this chance, you will both richly deserve to be hanged, as I sincerely trust you will be.", "그리고 두 분에게 충고하건대, 서인도제도에서 너무 약게 굴지 마시오. 이 기회를 내던진다면 두 분 다 교수형감이 마땅하니, 진심으로 그리되기를 바라오."], ["And now you had better leave Mr.", "자, 이제 픽윅 씨와 나만 있게 해 주는 게 좋겠소."], ["Pickwick and me alone, for we have other matters to talk over, and time is precious.’ As Perker said this, he looked towards the door, with an evident desire to render the leave-taking as brief as possible.", "우린 의논할 다른 일이 있고 시간이 귀하니 말이오.\" 퍼커는 이렇게 말하며, 작별을 되도록 짧게 끝내고 싶다는 기색이 역력한 채 문 쪽을 바라보았다."]], [["It was brief enough on Mr. Jingle’s part.", "징글 씨 쪽에서는 작별이 충분히 짧았다."], ["He thanked the little attorney in a few hurried words for the kindness and promptitude with which he had rendered his assistance, and, turning to his benefactor, stood for a few seconds as if irresolute what to say or how to act.", "그는 도움을 베풀어 준 친절과 신속함에 대해 몇 마디 다급한 말로 작은 변호사에게 감사하고는, 자기 은인 쪽으로 돌아서서, 무슨 말을 해야 할지 어떻게 행동해야 할지 결정하지 못한 듯 잠시 서 있었다."], ["Job Trotter relieved his perplexity; for, with a humble and grateful bow to Mr. Pickwick, he took his friend gently by the arm, and led him away.", "잡 트로터가 그의 난처함을 덜어 주었으니, 픽윅 씨에게 겸손하고 감사에 찬 절을 올리고는 친구의 팔을 부드럽게 잡아 데리고 나갔다."]], [["‘A worthy couple!’ said Perker, as the door closed behind them.", "\"훌륭한 한 쌍이로군요!\" 그들 뒤로 문이 닫히자 퍼커가 말했다."]], [["‘I hope they may become so,’ replied Mr.", "\"그들이 그리되기를 바랍니다.\""], ["Pickwick. ‘What do you think?", "픽윅 씨가 대답했다."], ["Is there any chance of their permanent reformation?’", "\"어떻게 생각하세요? 저들이 영영 개과천선할 가망이 있을까요?\""]], [["Perker shrugged his shoulders doubtfully, but observing Mr. Pickwick’s anxious and disappointed look, rejoined--", "퍼커는 미심쩍은 듯 어깨를 으쓱했으나, 픽윅 씨의 근심스럽고 실망한 표정을 보고는 이렇게 받아 말했다."]], [["‘Of course there is a chance.", "\"물론 가망은 있지요."], ["I hope it may prove a good one.", "좋은 쪽으로 판명되기를 바랍니다."], ["They are unquestionably penitent now; but then, you know, they have the recollection of very recent suffering fresh upon them.", "저들은 지금 틀림없이 뉘우치고 있습니다. 하지만 아시다시피, 저들에게는 아주 최근에 겪은 고난의 기억이 생생하게 남아 있으니까요."], ["What they may become, when that fades away, is a problem that neither you nor I can solve.", "그 기억이 희미해질 때 저들이 어떻게 될지는 선생님도 저도 풀 수 없는 문제입니다."], ["However, my dear Sir,’ added Perker, laying his hand on Mr.", "하지만 선생님,\" 퍼커가 픽윅 씨의 어깨에 손을 얹으며 덧붙였다."], ["Pickwick’s shoulder, ‘your object is equally honourable, whatever the result is.", "\"결과야 어떻든 선생님의 의도는 한결같이 훌륭합니다."], ["Whether that species of benevolence which is so very cautious and long-sighted that it is seldom exercised at all, lest its owner should be imposed upon, and so wounded in his self-love, be real charity or a worldly counterfeit, I leave to wiser heads than mine to determine.", "너무도 신중하고 멀리 내다보는 나머지, 베푸는 이가 속아 넘어가 자존심에 상처를 입을까 봐 좀처럼 실행되지 않는 그런 종류의 자선이 참된 자비인지 아니면 세속적인 가짜인지는, 저보다 더 현명한 사람들의 판단에 맡기겠습니다."], ["But if those two fellows were to commit a burglary to-morrow, my opinion of this action would be equally high.’", "하지만 저 두 친구가 내일 강도질을 저지른다 해도, 이 행위에 대한 제 평가는 변함없이 높을 것입니다.\""]], [["With these remarks, which were delivered in a much more animated and earnest manner than is usual in legal gentlemen, Perker drew his chair to his desk, and listened to Mr. Pickwick’s recital of old Mr. Winkle’s obstinacy.", "법조계 신사들에게서 흔히 볼 수 있는 것보다 훨씬 더 활기차고 진지한 태도로 전해진 이런 말과 함께, 퍼커는 의자를 책상으로 끌어당기고는 늙은 윈클 씨의 고집에 관한 픽윅 씨의 이야기에 귀를 기울였다."]], [["‘Give him a week,’ said Perker, nodding his head prophetically.", "\"일주일 말미를 주세요.\" 퍼커가 예언하듯 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다."]], [["‘Do you think he will come round?’ inquired Mr.", "\"그가 마음을 돌릴 거라고 생각하세요?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘I think he will,’ rejoined Perker.", "\"그러리라 봅니다.\" 퍼커가 받아 말했다."], ["‘If not, we must try the young lady’s persuasion; and that is what anybody but you would have done at first.’", "\"그렇지 않으면 그 젊은 숙녀의 설득을 시도해야지요. 선생님이 아닌 다른 사람이라면 누구든 처음부터 그렇게 했을 일입니다.\""]], [["Mr. Perker was taking a pinch of snuff with various grotesque contractions of countenance, eulogistic of the persuasive powers appertaining unto young ladies, when the murmur of inquiry and answer was heard in the outer office, and Lowten tapped at the door.", "퍼커 씨가 젊은 숙녀들에게 딸린 설득력을 찬미하느라 갖가지 기괴하게 얼굴을 찡그려 가며 코담배를 한 줌 들이마시고 있을 때, 바깥 사무실에서 묻고 답하는 웅성거림이 들리더니 로튼이 문을 두드렸다."]], [["‘Come in!’ cried the little man.", "\"들어오게!\" 작은 남자가 외쳤다."]], [["The clerk came in, and shut the door after him, with great mystery.", "사무원이 들어와 무척 비밀스러운 태도로 등 뒤의 문을 닫았다."]], [["‘What’s the matter?’ inquired Perker.", "\"무슨 일인가?\" 퍼커가 물었다."]], [["‘You’re wanted, Sir.’", "\"손님이 찾으십니다, 선생님.\""]], [["‘Who wants me?’", "\"누가 날 찾는데?\""]], [["Lowten looked at Mr. Pickwick, and coughed.", "로튼이 픽윅 씨를 흘끗 보고는 기침을 했다."]], [["‘Who wants me?", "\"누가 날 찾느냐니까?"], ["Can’t you speak, Mr. Lowten?’", "말을 못 하겠나, 로튼 씨?\""]], [["‘Why, sir,’ replied Lowten, ‘it’s Dodson; and Fogg is with him.’", "\"저, 선생님,\" 로튼이 대답했다. \"도드슨입니다. 포그도 함께 와 있고요.\""]], [["‘Bless my life!’ said the little man, looking at his watch, ‘I appointed them to be here at half-past eleven, to settle that matter of yours, Pickwick. I gave them an undertaking on which they sent down your discharge; it’s very awkward, my dear Sir; what will you do? Would you like to step into the next room?’", "\"이런 맙소사!\" 작은 남자가 회중시계를 보며 말했다. \"그자들한테 열한 시 반에 여기로 오라고 약속해 두었지. 자네 그 일을 매듭짓기로 말일세, 픽윅. 내가 각서를 한 장 써 주니 그자들이 자네 석방 서류를 내려보냈거든. 참 거북하게 됐군요, 선생님. 어떻게 하시겠습니까? 옆방으로 가 계시겠어요?\""]], [["The next room being the identical room in which Messrs. Dodson & Fogg were, Mr.", "그 옆방이라는 게 바로 도드슨 씨와 포그 씨가 있는 그 방인지라, 픽윅 씨는 자기가 있던 자리에 그대로 있겠다고 대답했다."], ["Pickwick replied that he would remain where he was: the more especially as Messrs. Dodson & Fogg ought to be ashamed to look him in the face, instead of his being ashamed to see them.", "더구나 자기가 그들을 보기 부끄러워할 게 아니라, 도드슨 씨와 포그 씨야말로 자기 얼굴을 똑바로 보기 부끄러워해야 마땅하다는 것이었다."], ["Which latter circumstance he begged Mr. Perker to note, with a glowing countenance and many marks of indignation.", "픽윅 씨는 이 후자의 사정을 잘 새겨 두라고, 벌겋게 달아오른 얼굴로 분개의 기색을 잔뜩 드러내며 퍼커 씨에게 당부했다."]], [["‘Very well, my dear Sir, very well,’ replied Perker, ‘I can only say that if you expect either Dodson or Fogg to exhibit any symptom of shame or confusion at having to look you, or anybody else, in the face, you are the most sanguine man in your expectations that I ever met with.", "\"좋습니다, 선생님, 아주 좋아요.\" 퍼커가 대답했다."], ["Show them in, Mr.", "\"다만 한 가지 말씀드릴 수 있는 건, 도드슨이든 포그든 선생님이나 그 누구의 얼굴을 마주하면서 부끄러움이나 당혹스러움의 기미라도 보이리라 기대하신다면, 제가 만나 본 사람 중 가장 낙천적인 분이시라는 겁니다."], ["Lowten.’", "모셔 들이게, 로튼 씨.\""]], [["Mr. Lowten disappeared with a grin, and immediately returned ushering in the firm, in due form of precedence--Dodson first, and Fogg afterwards.", "로튼 씨는 씩 웃으며 사라졌다가, 곧 격식에 따른 순서대로 그 회사를 안내해 들어왔으니, 도드슨이 먼저요 포그가 그 뒤였다."]], [["‘You have seen Mr.", "\"픽윅 씨를 만나 보셨지요?\""], ["Pickwick, I believe?’ said Perker to Dodson, inclining his pen in the direction where that gentleman was seated.", "퍼커가 그 신사가 앉아 있는 방향으로 펜을 기울이며 도드슨에게 말했다."]], [["‘How do you do, Mr.", "\"안녕하십니까, 픽윅 씨?\""], ["Pickwick?’ said Dodson, in a loud voice.", "도드슨이 큰 소리로 말했다."]], [["‘Dear me,’ cried Fogg, ‘how do you do, Mr.", "\"이런, 세상에,\" 포그가 외쳤다."], ["Pickwick?", "\"안녕하십니까, 픽윅 씨?"], ["I hope you are well, Sir.", "평안하시기를 바랍니다, 선생님."], ["I thought I knew the face,’ said Fogg, drawing up a chair, and looking round him with a smile.", "어쩐지 낯이 익다 했지요.\" 포그는 의자를 끌어당기고 미소를 지으며 주위를 둘러보며 말했다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick bent his head very slightly, in answer to these salutations, and, seeing Fogg pull a bundle of papers from his coat pocket, rose and walked to the window.", "픽윅 씨는 이 인사에 답하여 고개를 아주 살짝 숙였을 뿐, 포그가 외투 주머니에서 서류 뭉치를 꺼내는 것을 보고는 일어나 창가로 걸어갔다."]], [["‘There’s no occasion for Mr. Pickwick to move, Mr.", "\"픽윅 씨께서 자리를 옮기실 필요는 없습니다, 퍼커 씨.\""], ["Perker,’ said Fogg, untying the red tape which encircled the little bundle, and smiling again more sweetly than before. ‘Mr.", "포그가 그 작은 뭉치를 두른 빨간 끈을 풀고 전보다 더 달콤하게 다시 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["Pickwick is pretty well acquainted with these proceedings.", "\"픽윅 씨께서는 이 절차를 꽤 잘 아십니다."], ["There are no secrets between us, I think.", "우리 사이엔 비밀이랄 게 없잖습니까. 헤!"], ["He!", "헤!"], ["he! he!’", "헤!\""]], [["‘Not many, I think,’ said Dodson.", "\"별로 없지요.\" 도드슨이 말했다."], ["‘Ha! ha!", "\"하하하!\""], ["ha!’ Then both the partners laughed together--pleasantly and cheerfully, as men who are going to receive money often do.", "그러고 나서 두 동업자는 함께 웃었으니, 돈을 받으러 가는 사람들이 흔히 그러하듯 유쾌하고 명랑하게 웃었다."]], [["‘We shall make Mr.", "\"엿본 값은 픽윅 씨께서 치르시도록 해야겠군요.\""], ["Pickwick pay for peeping,’ said Fogg, with considerable native humour, as he unfolded his papers.", "포그가 서류를 펼치며 제법 타고난 익살을 부려 말했다."], ["‘The amount of the taxed costs is one hundred and thirty-three, six, four, Mr. Perker.’", "\"사정된 소송 비용 총액은 백삼십삼 파운드 육 실링 사 펜스입니다, 퍼커 씨.\""]], [["There was a great comparing of papers, and turning over of leaves, by Fogg and Perker, after this statement of profit and loss.", "이 손익 명세가 나온 뒤로 포그와 퍼커는 서류를 대조하고 책장을 넘기느라 한참을 보냈다."], ["Meanwhile, Dodson said, in an affable manner, to Mr. Pickwick--", "그러는 동안 도드슨이 픽윅 씨에게 상냥한 투로 말을 걸었다."]], [["‘I don’t think you are looking quite so stout as when I had the pleasure of seeing you last, Mr. Pickwick.’", "\"지난번 뵙는 기쁨을 누렸을 때만큼 건장해 보이시진 않는 것 같군요, 픽윅 씨.\""]], [["‘Possibly not, Sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"아마 그렇겠죠, 선생님.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다."], ["Pickwick, who had been flashing forth looks of fierce indignation, without producing the smallest effect on either of the sharp practitioners; ‘I believe I am not, Sir.", "그는 그 두 약삭빠른 모사꾼 어느 쪽에도 손톱만큼의 효과조차 내지 못한 채 사나운 분개의 눈길을 번뜩이고 있던 참이었다. \"그렇지 못할 겁니다, 선생님."], ["I have been persecuted and annoyed by scoundrels of late, Sir.’", "요즘 들어 악당들에게 시달리고 시끄럽게 부대껴 왔으니까요, 선생님.\""]], [["Perker coughed violently, and asked Mr. Pickwick whether he wouldn’t like to look at the morning paper.", "퍼커가 심하게 기침을 하고는 픽윅 씨에게 조간신문이라도 보시지 않겠냐고 물었다."], ["To which inquiry Mr. Pickwick returned a most decided negative.", "그 질문에 픽윅 씨는 더없이 단호하게 싫다고 답했다."]], [["‘True,’ said Dodson, ‘I dare say you have been annoyed in the Fleet; there are some odd gentry there.", "\"맞습니다,\" 도드슨이 말했다."], ["Whereabouts were your apartments, Mr.", "\"플리트에서 시달리셨겠지요. 거긴 별난 작자들이 좀 있으니까요."], ["Pickwick?’", "선생님 방은 어디쯤이었습니까, 픽윅 씨?\""]], [["‘My one room,’ replied that much-injured gentleman, ‘was on the coffee- room flight.’", "\"내 방은,\" 그 크게 상처 입은 신사가 대답했다. \"휴게실 계단 쪽에 있었소.\""]], [["‘Oh, indeed!’ said Dodson.", "\"오, 그래요!\""], ["‘I believe that is a very pleasant part of the establishment.’", "도드슨이 말했다. \"거긴 그 시설에서 아주 쾌적한 구역이라던데요.\""]], [["‘Very,’ replied Mr.", "\"매우 그렇지요.\""], ["Pickwick drily.", "픽윅 씨가 무뚝뚝하게 대답했다."]], [["There was a coolness about all this, which, to a gentleman of an excitable temperament, had, under the circumstances, rather an exasperating tendency. Mr.", "이 모든 일에는 일종의 냉랭함이 감돌았으니, 흥분하기 쉬운 기질의 신사에게는 이런 상황에서 다분히 화를 돋우는 데가 있었다."], ["Pickwick restrained his wrath by gigantic efforts; but when Perker wrote a cheque for the whole amount, and Fogg deposited it in a small pocket-book, with a triumphant smile playing over his pimply features, which communicated itself likewise to the stern countenance of Dodson, he felt the blood in his cheeks tingling with indignation.", "픽윅 씨는 어마어마한 노력으로 분노를 억눌렀다. 그러나 퍼커가 전액에 해당하는 수표를 써 주고, 포그가 여드름투성이 얼굴에 의기양양한 미소를 띤 채—그 미소는 도드슨의 근엄한 얼굴에도 옮아갔다—그것을 작은 수첩에 챙겨 넣자, 그는 두 뺨의 피가 분노로 따끔거리는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["‘Now, Mr.", "\"자, 도드슨 씨,\""], ["Dodson,’ said Fogg, putting up the pocket-book and drawing on his gloves, ‘I am at your service.’", "포그가 수첩을 집어넣고 장갑을 끼며 말했다. \"준비됐습니다.\""]], [["‘Very good,’ said Dodson, rising; ‘I am quite ready.’", "\"좋아요,\" 도드슨이 일어서며 말했다. \"나도 다 됐습니다.\""]], [["‘I am very happy,’ said Fogg, softened by the cheque, ‘to have had the pleasure of making Mr.", "\"참으로 기쁩니다,\" 수표로 마음이 누그러진 포그가 말했다."], ["Pickwick’s acquaintance. I hope you don’t think quite so ill of us, Mr.", "\"픽윅 씨와 알게 되는 기쁨을 누리게 되어서요."], ["Pickwick, as when we first had the pleasure of seeing you.’", "처음 뵙는 기쁨을 누렸을 때만큼 저희를 나쁘게 여기시진 않으시기를 바랍니다, 픽윅 씨.\""]], [["‘I hope not,’ said Dodson, with the high tone of calumniated virtue.", "\"그러시지 않기를 바랍니다.\""], ["‘Mr.", "도드슨이 중상모략당한 미덕의 고상한 어조로 말했다."], ["Pickwick now knows us better, I trust; whatever your opinion of gentlemen of our profession may be, I beg to assure you, sir, that I bear no ill-will or vindictive feeling towards you for the sentiments you thought proper to express in our office in Freeman’s Court, Cornhill, on the occasion to which my partner has referred.’", "\"픽윅 씨께서 이제 저희를 더 잘 아시게 되었으리라 믿습니다. 저희 직업에 종사하는 사람들을 어떻게 여기시든, 제 동업자가 언급한 그 일이 있던 날 콘힐 프리먼스 코트의 저희 사무실에서 선생님께서 표하시기에 합당하다 여기신 그 견해에 대해, 저는 선생님께 아무런 악의나 앙심도 품고 있지 않음을 분명히 말씀드립니다.\""]], [["‘Oh, no, no; nor I,’ said Fogg, in a most forgiving manner.", "\"오, 그럼요, 그럼요. 저도 마찬가지입니다.\" 포그가 더없이 너그러운 투로 말했다."]], [["‘Our conduct, Sir,’ said Dodson, ‘will speak for itself, and justify itself, I hope, upon every occasion.", "\"저희의 행실은, 선생님,\" 도드슨이 말했다."], ["We have been in the profession some years, Mr.", "\"어떤 경우에든 스스로 말해 주고 스스로를 정당화하리라 믿습니다."], ["Pickwick, and have been honoured with the confidence of many excellent clients.", "저희는 이 직업에 몇 해를 몸담아 왔습니다, 픽윅 씨. 그리고 수많은 훌륭한 의뢰인들의 신임을 받는 영예를 누려 왔지요."], ["I wish you good-morning, Sir.’", "그럼 안녕히 계십시오, 선생님.\""]], [["‘Good-morning, Mr.", "\"안녕히 계십시오, 픽윅 씨.\""], ["Pickwick,’ said Fogg.", "포그가 말했다."], ["So saying, he put his umbrella under his arm, drew off his right glove, and extended the hand of reconciliation to that most indignant gentleman; who, thereupon, thrust his hands beneath his coat tails, and eyed the attorney with looks of scornful amazement.", "이렇게 말하며 그는 우산을 겨드랑이에 끼고, 오른쪽 장갑을 벗어, 그 더없이 분개한 신사에게 화해의 손을 내밀었다. 그러자 그 신사는 두 손을 외투 자락 밑으로 쑤셔 넣고는 경멸 어린 경악의 눈초리로 그 변호사를 노려보았다."]], [["‘Lowten!’ cried Perker, at this moment.", "\"로튼!\" 바로 이때 퍼커가 외쳤다."], ["‘Open the door.’", "\"문 열어 드리게.\""]], [["‘Wait one instant,’ said Mr.", "\"잠깐만 기다리세요.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick. ‘Perker, I will speak.’", "\"퍼커, 나는 한마디 해야겠소.\""]], [["‘My dear Sir, pray let the matter rest where it is,’ said the little attorney, who had been in a state of nervous apprehension during the whole interview; ‘Mr.", "\"선생님, 부디 그냥 이쯤에서 덮어 두십시오.\" 면담 내내 안절부절못하던 작은 변호사가 말했다."], ["Pickwick, I beg--’", "\"픽윅 씨, 제발—\""]], [["‘I will not be put down, Sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"나는 입 다물고 있지 않겠소, 선생.\""], ["Pickwick hastily. ‘Mr.", "픽윅 씨가 급히 대꾸했다."], ["Dodson, you have addressed some remarks to me.’", "\"도드슨 씨, 당신은 내게 몇 마디 말을 건넸소.\""]], [["Dodson turned round, bent his head meekly, and smiled.", "도드슨이 돌아서서 온순하게 고개를 숙이고는 미소 지었다."]], [["‘Some remarks to me,’ repeated Mr.", "\"내게 몇 마디 말을 건넸소.\""], ["Pickwick, almost breathless; ‘and your partner has tendered me his hand, and you have both assumed a tone of forgiveness and high-mindedness, which is an extent of impudence that I was not prepared for, even in you.’", "픽윅 씨가 거의 숨이 차도록 되풀이했다. \"그리고 당신 동업자는 내게 손을 내밀었고, 두 사람 다 용서와 고결함의 어조를 취했으니, 그것은 당신네한테서조차 미처 예상치 못한 정도의 뻔뻔함이오.\""]], [["‘What, sir!’ exclaimed Dodson.", "\"뭐라고요, 선생!\" 도드슨이 외쳤다."]], [["‘What, sir!’ reiterated Fogg.", "\"뭐라고요, 선생!\" 포그가 따라 외쳤다."]], [["‘Do you know that I have been the victim of your plots and conspiracies?’ continued Mr.", "\"내가 당신네 음모와 계략의 희생자였다는 걸 알기나 하오?\" 픽윅 씨가 계속했다."], ["Pickwick. ‘Do you know that I am the man whom you have been imprisoning and robbing?", "\"내가 바로 당신네가 가두고 등쳐 먹은 그 사람이라는 걸 아오?"], ["Do you know that you were the attorneys for the plaintiff, in Bardell and Pickwick?’", "당신네가 바델 대 픽윅 사건에서 원고 측 변호인이었다는 걸 아오?\""]], [["‘Yes, sir, we do know it,’ replied Dodson.", "\"네, 선생, 알고 있습니다.\" 도드슨이 대답했다."]], [["‘Of course we know it, Sir,’ rejoined Fogg, slapping his pocket--perhaps by accident.", "\"물론 알지요, 선생.\" 포그가 받아 말하며 자기 주머니를 툭 쳤으니, 어쩌면 무심결이었으리라."]], [["‘I see that you recollect it with satisfaction,’ said Mr.", "\"당신네가 그걸 흡족하게 기억하고 있다는 게 보이는군.\""], ["Pickwick, attempting to call up a sneer for the first time in his life, and failing most signally in so doing.", "픽윅 씨가 평생 처음으로 비웃음을 지어 보려 애썼으나 더없이 명백하게 실패하며 말했다."], ["‘Although I have long been anxious to tell you, in plain terms, what my opinion of you is, I should have let even this opportunity pass, in deference to my friend Perker’s wishes, but for the unwarrantable tone you have assumed, and your insolent familiarity.", "\"당신네에 대한 내 생각이 어떤지를 단도직입적으로 말해 주고 싶은 마음이 오래전부터 굴뚝같았으나, 친구 퍼커의 뜻을 존중하여 이 기회마저도 그냥 지나쳤을 거요. 당신네가 취한 그 가당찮은 어조와 그 무례한 허물없음만 아니었다면 말이오."], ["I say insolent familiarity, sir,’ said Mr.", "무례한 허물없음이라고 했소, 선생.\""], ["Pickwick, turning upon Fogg with a fierceness of gesture which caused that person to retreat towards the door with great expedition.", "픽윅 씨가 어찌나 사납게 몸짓하며 포그 쪽으로 돌아섰던지, 그자는 황급히 문 쪽으로 물러났다."]], [["‘Take care, Sir,’ said Dodson, who, though he was the biggest man of the party, had prudently entrenched himself behind Fogg, and was speaking over his head with a very pale face.", "\"조심하시오, 선생.\" 도드슨이 말했다."], ["‘Let him assault you, Mr.", "그는 일행 중 가장 덩치가 컸으나 슬그머니 포그 뒤로 몸을 사리고는, 새파랗게 질린 얼굴로 그의 머리 너머로 말하고 있었다."], ["Fogg; don’t return it on any account.’", "\"그가 당신을 폭행하게 두시오, 포그 씨. 무슨 일이 있어도 되받아치지는 마시오.\""]], [["‘No, no, I won’t return it,’ said Fogg, falling back a little more as he spoke; to the evident relief of his partner, who by these means was gradually getting into the outer office.", "\"네, 네, 되받아치지 않겠습니다.\" 포그가 말하면서 조금 더 뒤로 물러났으니, 그 덕에 차츰 바깥 사무실로 빠져나가게 된 동업자는 안도하는 기색이 역력했다."]], [["‘You are,’ continued Mr.", "\"당신네는,\""], ["Pickwick, resuming the thread of his discourse- -’you are a well-matched pair of mean, rascally, pettifogging robbers.’", "픽윅 씨가 다시 말의 실마리를 이어 갔다. \"당신네는 천하고 악랄하며 비열한 짓을 일삼는 강도, 잘 어울리는 한 쌍이오.\""]], [["‘Well,’ interposed Perker, ‘is that all?’", "\"자,\" 퍼커가 끼어들었다. \"그게 전부입니까?\""]], [["‘It is all summed up in that,’ rejoined Mr.", "\"그 한마디에 다 들어 있소.\" 픽윅 씨가 받아 말했다."], ["Pickwick; ‘they are mean, rascally, pettifogging robbers.’", "\"저들은 천하고 악랄하며 비열한 짓을 일삼는 강도들이오.\""]], [["‘There!’ said Perker, in a most conciliatory tone.", "\"자, 됐습니다!\" 퍼커가 더없이 달래는 투로 말했다."], ["‘My dear sirs, he has said all he has to say.", "\"선생님들, 이분이 하실 말씀은 다 하셨습니다."], ["Now pray go.", "이제 어서 가시지요."], ["Lowten, is that door open?’", "로튼, 그 문 열려 있나?\""]], [["Mr. Lowten, with a distant giggle, replied in the affirmative.", "로튼 씨가 멀찍이서 킬킬거리며 그렇다고 대답했다."]], [["‘There, there--good-morning--good-morning--now pray, my dear sirs--Mr.", "\"자, 자, 안녕히 가십시오, 안녕히, 자 부디, 선생님들, 로튼 씨, 문!\" 작은 남자가 마다하지 않는 도드슨과 포그를 사무실 밖으로 떠밀며 외쳤다."], ["Lowten, the door!’ cried the little man, pushing Dodson & Fogg, nothing loath, out of the office; ‘this way, my dear sirs--now pray don’t prolong this--Dear me--Mr. Lowten--the door, sir--why don’t you attend?’", "\"이쪽입니다, 선생님들, 부디 이 일을 더 길게 끌지 마시고, 이런 참, 로튼 씨, 문 말이오, 선생, 왜 거기 신경을 안 쓰나?\""]], [["‘If there’s law in England, sir,’ said Dodson, looking towards Mr.", "\"영국에 법이라는 게 있다면, 선생,\" 도드슨이 모자를 쓰며 픽윅 씨 쪽을 바라보고 말했다."], ["Pickwick, as he put on his hat, ‘you shall smart for this.’", "\"당신은 이 일로 호되게 대가를 치를 거요.\""]], [["‘You are a couple of mean--’", "\"당신네는 천하고—\""]], [["‘Remember, sir, you pay dearly for this,’ said Fogg.", "\"기억하시오, 선생, 이 값을 비싸게 치르게 될 거요.\" 포그가 말했다."]], [["‘--Rascally, pettifogging robbers!’ continued Mr.", "\"악랄하며 비열한 짓을 일삼는 강도들이오!\""], ["Pickwick, taking not the least notice of the threats that were addressed to him.", "픽윅 씨가 자기에게 던져진 위협에는 눈곱만큼도 아랑곳하지 않고 계속했다."]], [["‘Robbers!’ cried Mr.", "\"강도들!\""], ["Pickwick, running to the stair-head, as the two attorneys descended.", "픽윅 씨가 두 변호사가 내려가는 동안 계단 꼭대기로 달려가며 외쳤다."]], [["‘Robbers!’ shouted Mr.", "\"강도들!\""], ["Pickwick, breaking from Lowten and Perker, and thrusting his head out of the staircase window.", "픽윅 씨가 로튼과 퍼커를 뿌리치고 계단 창밖으로 머리를 내밀며 고함쳤다."]], [["When Mr.", "픽윅 씨가 다시 머리를 들여놓았을 때, 그의 얼굴은 미소 띤 평온한 표정이었다."], ["Pickwick drew in his head again, his countenance was smiling and placid; and, walking quietly back into the office, he declared that he had now removed a great weight from his mind, and that he felt perfectly comfortable and happy.", "그리고 조용히 사무실로 걸어 들어와, 이제 마음에서 큰 짐을 덜어 냈으며, 더없이 후련하고 행복하다고 선언했다."]], [["Perker said nothing at all until he had emptied his snuff-box, and sent Lowten out to fill it, when he was seized with a fit of laughing, which lasted five minutes; at the expiration of which time he said that he supposed he ought to be very angry, but he couldn’t think of the business seriously yet--when he could, he would be.", "퍼커는 코담배갑을 다 비우고 로튼을 내보내 채워 오게 할 때까지 아무 말도 하지 않다가, 별안간 웃음보가 터져 오 분 동안이나 웃어 댔다. 그 시간이 다하자 그는 자기가 무척 화를 내야 마땅하겠지만 아직은 그 일을 진지하게 생각할 수가 없다고, 진지하게 생각할 수 있게 되면 그러겠노라고 말했다."]], [["‘Well, now,’ said Mr.", "\"자, 그럼,\""], ["Pickwick, ‘let me have a settlement with you.’", "픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"당신과 정산을 좀 합시다.\""]], [["Of the same kind as the last?’ inquired Perker, with another laugh.", "\"방금 그것과 같은 종류로요?\" 퍼커가 또 한 번 웃으며 물었다."]], [["‘Not exactly,’ rejoined Mr.", "\"그건 아니고요.\""], ["Pickwick, drawing out his pocket-book, and shaking the little man heartily by the hand, ‘I only mean a pecuniary settlement.", "픽윅 씨가 수첩을 꺼내고 작은 남자의 손을 진심으로 정답게 흔들며 받아 말했다. \"내 말은 그저 금전상의 정산을 뜻한 거요."], ["You have done me many acts of kindness that I can never repay, and have no wish to repay, for I prefer continuing the obligation.’", "당신은 내가 결코 갚을 수 없는, 그리고 갚고 싶지도 않은 친절을 여러 번 베풀어 주었소. 나는 그 은혜를 계속 진 채로 있는 편이 더 좋으니 말이오.\""]], [["With this preface, the two friends dived into some very complicated accounts and vouchers, which, having been duly displayed and gone through by Perker, were at once discharged by Mr.", "이런 머리말과 함께, 두 친구는 무척 복잡한 계산서와 영수증 더미 속으로 파고들었다."], ["Pickwick with many professions of esteem and friendship.", "퍼커가 그것들을 차근차근 펼쳐 보이며 일일이 검토한 뒤, 픽윅 씨는 존경과 우정의 마음을 거듭 표하며 그 자리에서 모두 결제했다."]], [["They had no sooner arrived at this point, than a most violent and startling knocking was heard at the door; it was not an ordinary double- knock, but a constant and uninterrupted succession of the loudest single raps, as if the knocker were endowed with the perpetual motion, or the person outside had forgotten to leave off.", "그들이 이 단계에 막 다다랐을 때, 더없이 거세고 깜짝 놀랄 만한 문 두드리는 소리가 들렸다. 그것은 흔히 들리는 똑똑 두 번 두드리는 소리가 아니라, 마치 노커에 영구 운동의 힘이 깃들었거나 밖에 선 사람이 멈출 줄을 잊어버린 듯, 더없이 요란하게 한 번씩 끊임없이 줄기차게 두드려 대는 소리였다."]], [["‘Dear me, what’s that?’ exclaimed Perker, starting.", "\"이런 세상에, 저게 뭡니까?\" 퍼커가 깜짝 놀라며 외쳤다."]], [["‘I think it is a knock at the door,’ said Mr.", "\"문을 두드리는 소리 같은데요.\""], ["Pickwick, as if there could be the smallest doubt of the fact.", "픽윅 씨가, 마치 그 사실에 조금이라도 의심의 여지가 있다는 듯 말했다."]], [["The knocker made a more energetic reply than words could have yielded, for it continued to hammer with surprising force and noise, without a moment’s cessation.", "노커는 말보다 더 힘찬 대답을 해 보였으니, 한순간도 멈추지 않고 놀라운 힘과 소음으로 계속 쾅쾅 두들겨 댔던 것이다."]], [["‘Dear me!’ said Perker, ringing his bell, ‘we shall alarm the inn.", "\"이런 참!\" 퍼커가 종을 울리며 말했다."], ["Mr.", "\"온 여관을 놀라게 하겠군."], ["Lowten, don’t you hear a knock?’", "로튼 씨, 노크 소리 안 들리나?\""]], [["‘I’ll answer the door in one moment, Sir,’ replied the clerk.", "\"곧 문 열어 드리겠습니다, 선생님.\" 사무원이 대답했다."]], [["The knocker appeared to hear the response, and to assert that it was quite impossible he could wait so long.", "노커는 그 대답을 듣기라도 한 듯, 그렇게 오래 기다리기란 도무지 불가능하다고 우기는 것 같았다."], ["It made a stupendous uproar.", "그것은 어마어마한 소동을 일으켰다."]], [["‘It’s quite dreadful,’ said Mr.", "\"정말 끔찍하군요.\""], ["Pickwick, stopping his ears.", "픽윅 씨가 두 귀를 틀어막으며 말했다."]], [["‘Make haste, Mr.", "\"서두르게, 로튼 씨.\""], ["Lowten,’ Perker called out; ‘we shall have the panels beaten in.’", "퍼커가 큰 소리로 불렀다. \"문짝이 부서지겠어.\""]], [["Mr. Lowten, who was washing his hands in a dark closet, hurried to the door, and turning the handle, beheld the appearance which is described in the next chapter.", "어두운 골방에서 손을 씻고 있던 로튼 씨가 부랴부랴 문으로 가서 손잡이를 돌리자, 다음 장에 묘사될 그 광경이 눈앞에 나타났다."]], [["CHAPTER LIV. CONTAINING SOME PARTICULARS RELATIVE TO THE DOUBLE KNOCK, AND OTHER MATTERS: AMONG WHICH CERTAIN INTERESTING DISCLOSURES RELATIVE TO MR.", "깜짝 놀란 사무원의 눈앞에 나타난 것은 한 소년이었다—놀랄 만큼 뚱뚱한 소년이었다—종복 차림을 하고서, 문 앞 매트 위에 똑바로 서서, 마치 잠든 듯 눈을 감고 있었다."], ["SNODGRASS AND A YOUNG LADY ARE BY NO MEANS IRRELEVANT TO THIS HISTORY\nThe object that presented itself to the eyes of the astonished clerk, was a boy--a wonderfully fat boy--habited as a serving lad, standing upright on the mat, with his eyes closed as if in sleep.", "여행하는 유랑 마차 안에서든 밖에서든 그렇게 뚱뚱한 소년은 본 적이 없었다."], ["He had never seen such a fat boy, in or out of a travelling caravan; and this, coupled with the calmness and repose of his appearance, so very different from what was reasonably to have been expected of the inflicter of such knocks, smote him with wonder.", "게다가 그 침착하고 평온한 겉모습은, 그런 노크를 해 댄 자에게서 으레 기대할 법한 것과는 너무도 달라서, 그는 경이감에 사로잡혔다."]], [["‘What’s the matter?’ inquired the clerk.", "\"무슨 일이냐?\" 사무원이 물었다."]], [["The extraordinary boy replied not a word; but he nodded once, and seemed, to the clerk’s imagination, to snore feebly.", "그 기이한 소년은 한마디도 대답하지 않았다. 다만 한 번 고개를 끄덕였고, 사무원의 상상에는, 희미하게 코를 고는 듯했다."]], [["‘Where do you come from?’ inquired the clerk.", "\"어디서 왔느냐?\" 사무원이 물었다."]], [["The boy made no sign.", "소년은 아무런 기척이 없었다."], ["He breathed heavily, but in all other respects was motionless.", "숨을 무겁게 쉬었지만, 다른 모든 점에서는 미동도 없었다."]], [["The clerk repeated the question thrice, and receiving no answer, prepared to shut the door, when the boy suddenly opened his eyes, winked several times, sneezed once, and raised his hand as if to repeat the knocking.", "사무원은 세 번이나 같은 질문을 되풀이했으나 답이 없자, 문을 닫으려고 했다. 그러자 소년이 갑자기 눈을 뜨더니, 몇 번 눈을 깜박이고, 한 번 재채기를 하고, 다시 노크를 하려는 듯 손을 들어 올렸다."], ["Finding the door open, he stared about him with astonishment, and at length fixed his eyes on Mr. Lowten’s face.", "문이 열려 있는 것을 발견한 소년은 놀란 표정으로 주위를 두리번거리다가, 마침내 로튼 씨의 얼굴에 시선을 멈췄다."]], [["‘What the devil do you knock in that way for?’ inquired the clerk angrily.", "\"대체 왜 그렇게 노크를 해 대는 거냐?\" 사무원이 화를 내며 물었다."]], [["‘Which way?’ said the boy, in a slow and sleepy voice.", "\"어떻게요?\" 소년이 느리고 졸린 목소리로 말했다."]], [["‘Why, like forty hackney-coachmen,’ replied the clerk.", "\"아니, 마흔 명의 삯마차꾼처럼 말이다.\" 사무원이 대답했다."]], [["‘Because master said, I wasn’t to leave off knocking till they opened the door, for fear I should go to sleep,’ said the boy.", "\"주인 나리가 그러셨거든요, 문 열 때까지 노크를 멈추지 말라고요, 안 그러면 제가 잠들까 봐서요.\" 소년이 말했다."]], [["‘Well,’ said the clerk, ‘what message have you brought?’", "\"그래,\" 사무원이 말했다. \"무슨 전갈을 가져왔느냐?\""]], [["‘He’s downstairs,’ rejoined the boy.", "\"아래층에 계셔요.\" 소년이 대답했다."]], [["‘Who?’", "\"누가?\""]], [["‘Master.", "\"주인 나리요."], ["He wants to know whether you’re at home.’", "댁에 계신지 알고 싶어 하셔요.\""]], [["Mr. Lowten bethought himself, at this juncture, of looking out of the window.", "이 대목에서 로튼 씨는 창밖을 내다볼 생각이 났다."], ["Seeing an open carriage with a hearty old gentleman in it, looking up very anxiously, he ventured to beckon him; on which, the old gentleman jumped out directly.", "뚜껑 없는 마차 안에 건장한 노신사가 타고서 몹시 초조하게 위를 올려다보고 있는 것을 보고, 그는 큰맘 먹고 손짓을 했다. 그러자 노신사가 곧장 마차에서 뛰어내렸다."]], [["‘That’s your master in the carriage, I suppose?’ said Lowten.", "\"마차에 탄 저분이 네 주인이겠지?\" 로튼이 말했다."]], [["The boy nodded.", "소년이 고개를 끄덕였다."]], [["All further inquiries were superseded by the appearance of old Wardle, who, running upstairs and just recognising Lowten, passed at once into Mr.", "더 이상의 질문은 와들 영감의 등장으로 가로막혔다."], ["Perker’s room.", "그는 계단을 뛰어 올라와 로튼을 겨우 알아보고는 곧장 퍼커 씨의 방으로 들어갔다."]], [["‘Pickwick!’ said the old gentleman.", "\"픽윅!\""], ["‘Your hand, my boy!", "노신사가 말했다."], ["Why have I never heard until the day before yesterday of your suffering yourself to be cooped up in jail?", "\"손 좀 줘 보게, 이 사람아! 어째서 자네가 감옥에 갇혀 지냈다는 걸 그저께서야 듣게 된 건가?"], ["And why did you let him do it, Perker?’", "그리고 자넨 어째서 저 사람이 그러도록 내버려 둔 건가, 퍼커?\""]], [["‘I couldn’t help it, my dear Sir,’ replied Perker, with a smile and a pinch of snuff; ‘you know how obstinate he is?’", "\"어쩔 수가 없었습니다, 선생님.\" 퍼커가 미소를 지으며 코담배 한 자밤을 집으며 대답했다. \"이분이 얼마나 고집이 센지 아시잖습니까?\""]], [["‘Of course I do; of course I do,’ replied the old gentleman.", "\"물론 알지, 물론 알고말고.\" 노신사가 대답했다."], ["‘I am heartily glad to see him, notwithstanding.", "\"그래도 이 사람을 보니 진심으로 반갑네."], ["I will not lose sight of him again, in a hurry.’", "다시는 이 사람을 함부로 놓치지 않을 걸세.\""]], [["With these words, Wardle shook Mr.", "이 말과 함께 와들은 다시 한 번 픽윅 씨의 손을 잡고 흔들었고, 퍼커에게도 똑같이 한 뒤, 안락의자에 몸을 던졌다."], ["Pickwick’s hand once more, and, having done the same by Perker, threw himself into an arm-chair, his jolly red face shining again with smiles and health.", "그의 명랑한 붉은 얼굴이 미소와 건강함으로 다시 빛나고 있었다."]], [["‘Well!’ said Wardle.", "\"자!\" 와들이 말했다."], ["‘Here are pretty goings on--a pinch of your snuff, Perker, my boy--never were such times, eh?’", "\"참 볼만한 일들이 벌어지고 있군—코담배 한 자밤 주게, 퍼커—이런 시절은 일찍이 없었어, 안 그런가?\""]], [["‘What do you mean?’ inquired Mr.", "\"무슨 말씀이십니까?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘Mean!’ replied Wardle.", "\"무슨 말이냐고!\" 와들이 대답했다."], ["‘Why, I think the girls are all running mad; that’s no news, you’ll say?", "\"아니, 내 생각엔 처녀들이 죄다 미쳐 돌아가는 것 같단 말이야. 그게 새로울 것 없는 소리라고 하겠지?"], ["Perhaps it’s not; but it’s true, for all that.’", "하긴 그럴지도 모르지. 하지만 그래도 그게 사실이라네.\""]], [["‘You have not come up to London, of all places in the world, to tell us that, my dear Sir, have you?’ inquired Perker.", "\"세상 어느 곳을 다 두고서 그 말씀을 하시러 런던까지 올라오신 건 아니겠지요, 선생님?\" 퍼커가 물었다."]], [["‘No, not altogether,’ replied Wardle; ‘though it was the main cause of my coming.", "\"아니, 꼭 그것만은 아니지.\" 와들이 대답했다."], ["How’s Arabella?’", "\"그래도 그게 내가 올라온 주된 까닭이긴 해. 아라벨라는 어떤가?\""]], [["‘Very well,’ replied Mr.", "\"아주 잘 지냅니다.\""], ["Pickwick, ‘and will be delighted to see you, I am sure.’", "픽윅 씨가 대답했다. \"선생님을 뵈면 분명 무척 기뻐할 겁니다.\""]], [["‘Black-eyed little jilt!’ replied Wardle.", "\"검은 눈동자의 앙큼한 것 같으니!\""], ["‘I had a great idea of marrying her myself, one of these odd days.", "와들이 대답했다. \"나도 언젠가 그 애와 결혼해 볼까 하는 생각을 꽤나 했었지."], ["But I am glad of it too, very glad.’", "하지만 이렇게 된 것도 기뻐, 아주 기쁘다네.\""]], [["‘How did the intelligence reach you?’ asked Mr.", "\"그 소식이 어떻게 선생님께 닿았습니까?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘Oh, it came to my girls, of course,’ replied Wardle.", "\"오, 물론 우리 집 딸들한테 왔지.\" 와들이 대답했다."], ["‘Arabella wrote, the day before yesterday, to say she had made a stolen match without her husband’s father’s consent, and so you had gone down to get it when his refusing it couldn’t prevent the match, and all the rest of it.", "\"아라벨라가 그저께 편지를 보냈더군, 남편 아버지의 동의 없이 몰래 결혼을 했고, 그래서 자네가 그 동의를 받아 내러 내려갔다고 말이야, 그자가 거절해도 결혼을 막을 수 없게 된 마당에 말일세, 그 밖의 사연들도 전부. 나는 우리 딸들에게 뭔가 진지한 말을 해 줄 아주 좋은 기회라고 생각했지."], ["I thought it a very good time to say something serious to my girls; so I said what a dreadful thing it was that children should marry without their parents’ consent, and so forth; but, bless your hearts, I couldn’t make the least impression upon them.", "그래서 자식이 부모 동의 없이 결혼하는 게 얼마나 끔찍한 일인지 어쩌고저쩌고 말했다네. 그런데 이런, 그 애들에겐 손톱만큼도 감명을 줄 수가 없었어."], ["They thought it such a much more dreadful thing that there should have been a wedding without bridesmaids, that I might as well have preached to Joe himself.’", "그 애들은 들러리도 없이 결혼식이 치러졌다는 게 훨씬 더 끔찍한 일이라고 여기더군. 차라리 조 녀석한테 설교하는 편이 나았을 거야.\""]], [["Here the old gentleman stopped to laugh; and having done so to his heart’s content, presently resumed--", "여기서 노신사는 웃느라 잠시 말을 멈췄다. 실컷 웃고 난 뒤 곧 다시 말을 이었다—"]], [["‘But this is not the best of it, it seems.", "\"하지만 이게 다가 아닌 모양이야."], ["This is only half the love- making and plotting that have been going forward.", "이건 그동안 벌어진 연애와 음모의 절반에 지나지 않거든."], ["We have been walking on mines for the last six months, and they’re sprung at last.’", "우린 지난 여섯 달 동안 지뢰밭을 걷고 있었던 거고, 그게 마침내 터져 버린 게지.\""]], [["‘What do you mean?’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"무슨 말씀이십니까?\""], ["Pickwick, turning pale; ‘no other secret marriage, I hope?’", "픽윅 씨가 창백해지며 소리쳤다. \"또 다른 비밀 결혼은 아니겠지요, 제발?\""]], [["‘No, no,’ replied old Wardle; ‘not so bad as that; no.’", "\"아니, 아니야.\" 와들 영감이 대답했다. \"그렇게까지 나쁘진 않아. 아니야.\""]], [["‘What then?’ inquired Mr.", "\"그럼 뭡니까?\" 픽윅 씨가 물었다."], ["Pickwick; ‘am I interested in it?’", "\"제가 거기 얽혀 있습니까?\""]], [["‘Shall I answer that question, Perker?’ said Wardle.", "\"그 질문에 대답해도 되겠나, 퍼커?\" 와들이 말했다."]], [["‘If you don’t commit yourself by doing so, my dear Sir.’", "\"그렇게 하셔도 책잡힐 일이 없으시다면야, 선생님.\""]], [["‘Well then, you are,’ said Wardle.", "\"그럼 좋아, 자넨 얽혀 있다네.\" 와들이 말했다."]], [["‘How?’ asked Mr.", "\"어떻게요?\""], ["Pickwick anxiously.", "픽윅 씨가 초조하게 물었다."], ["‘In what way?’", "\"어떤 식으로 말입니까?\""]], [["‘Really,’ replied Wardle, ‘you’re such a fiery sort of a young fellow that I am almost afraid to tell you; but, however, if Perker will sit between us to prevent mischief, I’ll venture.’", "\"솔직히,\" 와들이 대답했다. \"자넨 워낙 불같은 젊은이라서 말하기가 거의 두려울 지경이야. 하지만 어쨌든 퍼커가 우리 사이에 앉아 사고를 막아 준다면, 한번 해 보지.\""]], [["Having closed the room door, and fortified himself with another application to Perker’s snuff-box, the old gentleman proceeded with his great disclosure in these words--", "방문을 닫고 퍼커의 코담배갑에 다시 한 번 손을 대어 기운을 차린 노신사는, 이런 말로 그 중대한 폭로를 시작했다—"]], [["‘The fact is, that my daughter Bella--Bella, who married young Trundle, you know.’", "\"실은 말이야, 내 딸 벨라가—벨라, 그 왜 트런들 청년과 결혼한 그 애 말일세.\""]], [["‘Yes, yes, we know,’ said Mr.", "\"예, 예, 압니다.\""], ["Pickwick impatiently.", "픽윅 씨가 조바심을 내며 말했다."]], [["‘Don’t alarm me at the very beginning.", "\"시작부터 나를 놀라게 하지 말게나."], ["My daughter Bella--Emily having gone to bed with a headache after she had read Arabella’s letter to me-- sat herself down by my side the other evening, and began to talk over this marriage affair.", "내 딸 벨라가—에밀리는 내게 아라벨라의 편지를 읽어 준 뒤 두통이 나서 잠자리에 들었고—요전 저녁에 내 곁에 앉더니 이 결혼 이야기를 꺼내기 시작했어. '저기, 아빠,'"], ["“Well, pa,” she says, “what do you think of it?”", "그 애가 말하길, '어떻게 생각하세요?'"], ["“Why, my dear,”", "'글쎄, 얘야,'"], ["I said, “I suppose it’s all very well; I hope it’s for the best.”", "내가 말했지, '다 괜찮은 일이겠지. 잘된 일이길 바란다.'"], ["I answered in this way because I was sitting before the fire at the time, drinking my grog rather thoughtfully, and I knew my throwing in an undecided word now and then, would induce her to continue talking.", "내가 이렇게 대답한 건, 그때 마침 난롯가에 앉아 꽤 골똘히 생각하며 그로그를 마시고 있던 참이라, 이따금 이렇게 어정쩡한 말을 던져 주면 그 애가 이야기를 계속하리라는 걸 알았기 때문이지. 우리 집 두 딸은 다 제 어미를 빼다 박았어."], ["Both my girls are pictures of their dear mother, and as I grow old I like to sit with only them by me; for their voices and looks carry me back to the happiest period of my life, and make me, for the moment, as young as I used to be then, though not quite so light-hearted.", "나도 나이가 들수록 그 애들만 곁에 두고 앉아 있는 게 좋아졌다네. 그 애들의 목소리와 모습이 나를 내 인생에서 가장 행복했던 시절로 데려다주고, 그 순간만큼은 나를 그 시절처럼 젊게 만들어 주거든, 그만큼 마음이 가볍진 않더라도 말일세."], ["“It’s quite a marriage of affection, pa,”", "'정말 애정으로 맺어진 결혼이에요, 아빠.'"], ["said Bella, after a short silence.", "잠시 침묵하더니 벨라가 말했어."], ["“Yes, my dear,”", "'그래, 얘야,'"], ["said I, “but such marriages do not always turn out the happiest.”’", "내가 말했지, '하지만 그런 결혼이 늘 가장 행복한 결말을 맺는 건 아니란다.'\""]], [["‘I question that, mind!’ interposed Mr.", "\"그건 저로선 의문입니다, 명심하세요!\""], ["Pickwick warmly.", "픽윅 씨가 열을 내며 끼어들었다."]], [["‘Very good,’ responded Wardle, ‘question anything you like when it’s your turn to speak, but don’t interrupt me.’", "\"좋아, 좋아.\" 와들이 응수했다. \"자네 차례가 되면 뭐든 의문을 제기하게, 다만 내 말을 끊진 말게.\""]], [["‘I beg your pardon,’ said Mr.", "\"죄송합니다.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Granted,’ replied Wardle.", "\"용서하지.\" 와들이 대답했다."], ["‘“I am sorry to hear you express your opinion against marriages of affection, pa,”", "\"'애정으로 맺어진 결혼에 반대하는 의견을 말씀하시니 마음이 아파요, 아빠.'"], ["said Bella, colouring a little.", "벨라가 약간 얼굴을 붉히며 말했어."], ["“I was wrong; I ought not to have said so, my dear, either,”", "'내가 틀렸다. 그런 말을 하지 말았어야 했어, 얘야.'"], ["said I, patting her cheek as kindly as a rough old fellow like me could pat it, “for your mother’s was one, and so was yours.”", "내가 말했지, 나 같은 거친 늙은이가 할 수 있는 한 다정하게 그 애의 뺨을 토닥이면서 말이야, '네 어미도 그런 결혼이었고, 너도 그랬으니까.'"], ["“It’s not that I meant, pa,”", "'제가 말하려는 건 그게 아니에요, 아빠.'"], ["said Bella.", "벨라가 말했어."], ["“The fact is, pa, I wanted to speak to you about Emily.”’", "'실은요, 아빠, 에밀리에 관해서 말씀드리고 싶었어요.'\""]], [["Mr. Pickwick started.", "픽윅 씨가 흠칫했다."]], [["‘What’s the matter now?’ inquired Wardle, stopping in his narrative.", "\"이번엔 또 무슨 일인가?\" 와들이 이야기를 멈추며 물었다."]], [["‘Nothing,’ replied Mr.", "\"아무것도 아닙니다.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 대답했다."], ["‘Pray go on.’", "\"어서 계속하십시오.\""]], [["‘I never could spin out a story,’ said Wardle abruptly.", "\"난 도무지 이야기를 길게 끌 줄을 몰라.\" 와들이 퉁명스럽게 말했다."], ["‘It must come out, sooner or later, and it’ll save us all a great deal of time if it comes at once.", "\"어차피 조만간 나올 일이니, 한꺼번에 나오는 편이 우리 모두 시간을 크게 아끼는 거지. 그러니 요컨대 이런 거야."], ["The long and the short of it is, then, that Bella at last mustered up courage to tell me that Emily was very unhappy; that she and your young friend Snodgrass had been in constant correspondence and communication ever since last Christmas; that she had very dutifully made up her mind to run away with him, in laudable imitation of her old friend and school-fellow; but that having some compunctions of conscience on the subject, inasmuch as I had always been rather kindly disposed to both of them, they had thought it better in the first instance to pay me the compliment of asking whether I would have any objection to their being married in the usual matter-of-fact manner.", "벨라가 마침내 용기를 내어 내게 말하길, 에밀리가 몹시 불행해한다는 거야. 지난 크리스마스 이후로 그 애와 자네의 젊은 친구 스노드그래스가 끊임없이 편지를 주고받고 소식을 나누어 왔다는 거지."], ["There now, Mr.", "그 애가 제 오랜 친구이자 학교 동무를 본받아 그자와 함께 도망칠 마음을 아주 효녀답게 먹었다는 거야."], ["Pickwick, if you can make it convenient to reduce your eyes to their usual size again, and to let me hear what you think we ought to do, I shall feel rather obliged to you!’", "다만 그 일에 양심의 가책이 좀 있었다는데, 내가 그 둘 모두에게 늘 제법 호의를 품어 왔던 터라, 그래서 우선은 나한테 예의를 갖춰 그 둘이 흔한 평범한 방식으로 결혼하는 데 내가 반대할 마음이 있는지 물어보는 편이 낫겠다고 둘이서 생각했다는 거지. 자, 픽윅, 자네가 그 눈을 다시 본래 크기로 줄여 주고, 우리가 어떻게 해야 한다고 생각하는지 좀 들려준다면, 내 자네한테 꽤나 고마워하겠네!\""]], [["The testy manner in which the hearty old gentleman uttered this last sentence was not wholly unwarranted; for Mr.", "이 건장한 노신사가 마지막 문장을 내뱉을 때의 그 짜증스러운 태도는 전혀 까닭 없는 것은 아니었다."], ["Pickwick’s face had settled down into an expression of blank amazement and perplexity, quite curious to behold.", "픽윅 씨의 얼굴이 멍한 경악과 당혹의 표정으로 굳어져, 보기에 자못 신기할 지경이었으니 말이다."]], [["‘Snodgrass!--since last Christmas!’ were the first broken words that issued from the lips of the confounded gentleman.", "\"스노드그래스가!—지난 크리스마스 이후로!\" 어안이 벙벙해진 신사의 입에서 나온 첫 토막말이 그것이었다."]], [["‘Since last Christmas,’ replied Wardle; ‘that’s plain enough, and very bad spectacles we must have worn, not to have discovered it before.’", "\"지난 크리스마스 이후로지.\" 와들이 대답했다. \"그건 아주 분명해. 진작 알아채지 못했으니 우리가 어지간히 형편없는 안경을 끼고 있었던 게 틀림없어.\""]], [["‘I don’t understand it,’ said Mr.", "\"이해가 안 됩니다.\""], ["Pickwick, ruminating; ‘I cannot really understand it.’", "픽윅 씨가 곰곰이 생각하며 말했다. \"정말이지 이해할 수가 없군요.\""]], [["‘It’s easy enough to understand it,’ replied the choleric old gentleman.", "\"이해하기야 충분히 쉽지.\" 성마른 노신사가 대답했다."], ["‘If you had been a younger man, you would have been in the secret long ago; and besides,’ added Wardle, after a moment’s hesitation, ‘the truth is, that, knowing nothing of this matter, I have rather pressed Emily for four or five months past, to receive favourably (if she could; I would never attempt to force a girl’s inclinations) the addresses of a young gentleman down in our neighbourhood.", "\"자네가 더 젊은 사람이었다면, 진작에 그 비밀을 알았을 거야. 게다가,\""], ["I have no doubt that, girl- like, to enhance her own value and increase the ardour of Mr.", "와들이 잠시 머뭇거리다 덧붙였다. \"사실은 말이야, 내가 이 일을 전혀 모르고서, 지난 너덧 달 동안 에밀리에게 우리 동네 어느 젊은 신사의 구애를 호의적으로 받아 주라고 (받아 줄 수 있다면 말일세."], ["Snodgrass, she has represented this matter in very glowing colours, and that they have both arrived at the conclusion that they are a terribly-persecuted pair of unfortunates, and have no resource but clandestine matrimony, or charcoal.", "난 결코 처녀의 마음을 억지로 강요할 생각은 없으니까) 제법 채근했거든. 의심할 바 없이, 그 애가 처녀답게, 제 몸값을 높이고 스노드그래스 씨의 열정을 부추기려고, 이 일을 아주 화려한 색깔로 부풀려 이야기했을 거고, 그래서 둘이 함께 자기들이 끔찍하게 박해받는 가련한 한 쌍이라는 결론에 이르렀을 게야, 비밀 결혼 아니면 숯불 말고는 달리 방도가 없다는 식으로 말이지."], ["Now the question is, what’s to be done?’", "자, 이제 문제는, 어떻게 해야 하느냐는 걸세.\""]], [["‘What have you done?’ inquired Mr.", "\"선생님은 어떻게 하셨습니까?\""], ["Pickwick. ‘I!’", "픽윅 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘I mean what did you do when your married daughter told you this?’", "\"나 말인가!\" \"제 말은, 결혼한 따님이 이 이야기를 했을 때 선생님은 어떻게 하셨냐는 겁니다.\""]], [["‘Oh, I made a fool of myself of course,’ rejoined Wardle.", "\"오, 물론 바보짓을 했지.\" 와들이 응수했다."]], [["‘Just so,’ interposed Perker, who had accompanied this dialogue with sundry twitchings of his watch-chain, vindictive rubbings of his nose, and other symptoms of impatience. ‘That’s very natural; but how?’", "\"바로 그렇습니다.\" 퍼커가 끼어들었다. 그는 이 대화 내내 회중시계 줄을 만지작거리고, 분풀이하듯 코를 문지르고, 그 밖의 초조한 기색을 보여 왔던 터였다. \"지극히 자연스러운 일이지요. 한데 어떻게 하셨습니까?\""]], [["‘I went into a great passion and frightened my mother into a fit,’ said Wardle.", "\"내가 잔뜩 성을 내는 바람에 내 어머니가 놀라 발작을 일으키셨지.\" 와들이 말했다."]], [["‘That was judicious,’ remarked Perker; ‘and what else?’", "\"현명하셨군요.\" 퍼커가 한마디 했다. \"그리고 또요?\""]], [["‘I fretted and fumed all next day, and raised a great disturbance,’ rejoined the old gentleman.", "\"이튿날 온종일 끙끙 앓고 씩씩대면서 한바탕 큰 소란을 피웠지.\" 노신사가 응수했다."], ["‘At last I got tired of rendering myself unpleasant and making everybody miserable; so I hired a carriage at Muggleton, and, putting my own horses in it, came up to town, under pretence of bringing Emily to see Arabella.’", "\"마침내 나도 나 자신을 불쾌하게 만들고 모두를 비참하게 만드는 데 지쳐 버렸어. 그래서 머글턴에서 마차를 빌리고, 거기에 내 말들을 매어, 에밀리를 데리고 아라벨라를 보러 간다는 핑계로 도시로 올라온 거야.\""]], [["‘Miss Wardle is with you, then?’ said Mr.", "\"그럼 와들 양도 함께 오셨군요?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘To be sure she is,’ replied Wardle.", "\"그렇고말고.\" 와들이 대답했다."], ["‘She is at Osborne’s Hotel in the Adelphi at this moment, unless your enterprising friend has run away with her since I came out this morning.’", "\"지금 이 순간 애덜파이의 오즈번 호텔에 있어, 자네의 그 진취적인 친구가 내가 오늘 아침 나온 사이에 데리고 도망치지 않았다면 말일세.\""]], [["‘You are reconciled then?’ said Perker.", "\"그럼 화해는 하셨습니까?\" 퍼커가 말했다."]], [["‘Not a bit of it,’ answered Wardle; ‘she has been crying and moping ever since, except last night, between tea and supper, when she made a great parade of writing a letter that I pretended to take no notice of.’", "\"전혀 아니야.\" 와들이 답했다. \"그 애는 그 뒤로 줄곧 울고 시무룩하게 지냈어. 어젯밤 차와 저녁 식사 사이에, 내가 짐짓 못 본 척했던 편지 한 통을 거창하게 써 댈 때만 빼고는 말이야.\""]], [["‘You want my advice in this matter, I suppose?’ said Perker, looking from the musing face of Mr.", "\"이 일에 제 조언을 바라시는 거겠지요?\""], ["Pickwick to the eager countenance of Wardle, and taking several consecutive pinches of his favourite stimulant.", "퍼커가, 픽윅 씨의 골똘한 얼굴과 와들의 간절한 표정을 번갈아 보고서, 자기가 좋아하는 그 자극제를 연거푸 몇 자밤 들이켜며 말했다."]], [["‘I suppose so,’ said Wardle, looking at Mr.", "\"그런 셈이네.\""], ["Pickwick.", "와들이 픽윅 씨를 바라보며 말했다."]], [["‘Certainly,’ replied that gentleman.", "\"물론입니다.\" 그 신사가 대답했다."]], [["‘Well then,’ said Perker, rising and pushing his chair back, ‘my advice is, that you both walk away together, or ride away, or get away by some means or other, for I’m tired of you, and just talk this matter over between you. If you have not settled it by the next time I see you, I’ll tell you what to do.’", "\"그럼 좋습니다.\" 퍼커가 일어나 의자를 뒤로 밀며 말했다. \"제 조언은, 두 분이 함께 걸어 나가시든, 마차를 타고 나가시든, 어떤 수단으로든 자리를 뜨시라는 겁니다. 저는 두 분께 지쳤으니까요. 그리고 이 일을 두 분끼리 의논해 보십시오. 다음번에 제가 두 분을 뵐 때까지 매듭을 짓지 못하시면, 그때 제가 어떻게 해야 할지 말씀드리겠습니다.\""]], [["‘This is satisfactory,’ said Wardle, hardly knowing whether to smile or be offended.", "\"이거 만족스럽군요.\" 와들이, 웃어야 할지 기분이 상해야 할지 도통 모르겠다는 투로 말했다."]], [["‘Pooh, pooh, my dear Sir,’ returned Perker.", "\"천만에요, 선생님.\" 퍼커가 응수했다."], ["‘I know you both a great deal better than you know yourselves.", "\"전 두 분 자신보다 두 분을 훨씬 잘 압니다."], ["You have settled it already, to all intents and purposes.’", "두 분은 사실상 이미 매듭을 다 지으신 겁니다.\""]], [["Thus expressing himself, the little gentleman poked his snuff-box first into the chest of Mr. Pickwick, and then into the waistcoat of Mr.", "이렇게 말하면서 자그마한 신사는 코담배갑을 먼저 픽윅 씨의 가슴팍에, 다음엔 와들 씨의 조끼에 쿡 찔러 댔고, 그 바람에 셋 모두 웃음을 터뜨렸다."], ["Wardle, upon which they all three laughed, especially the two last-named gentlemen, who at once shook hands again, without any obvious or particular reason.", "특히 뒤에 언급된 두 신사가 더했으니, 그들은 별다른 뚜렷한 이유도 없이 곧장 다시 손을 맞잡았다."]], [["‘You dine with me to-day,’ said Wardle to Perker, as he showed them out.", "\"오늘 나와 저녁을 함께 드세.\" 와들이 그들을 배웅하며 퍼커에게 말했다."]], [["‘Can’t promise, my dear Sir, can’t promise,’ replied Perker.", "\"약속은 못 드립니다, 선생님, 약속은 못 드려요.\""], ["‘I’ll look in, in the evening, at all events.’", "퍼커가 대답했다. \"어쨌든 저녁에 들러 보겠습니다.\""]], [["‘I shall expect you at five,’ said Wardle.", "\"다섯 시에 기다리겠네.\" 와들이 말했다."], ["‘Now, Joe!’ And Joe having been at length awakened, the two friends departed in Mr.", "\"자, 조!\" 그리고 조가 마침내 깨워지자, 두 친구는 와들 씨의 마차에 올라 떠났다."], ["Wardle’s carriage, which in common humanity had a dickey behind for the fat boy, who, if there had been a footboard instead, would have rolled off and killed himself in his very first nap.", "그 마차에는 인정상 뚱뚱한 소년을 위해 뒤쪽에 좌석이 달려 있었는데, 만약 거기에 발판만 달려 있었더라면 소년은 첫 낮잠을 자다가 굴러떨어져 죽고 말았을 것이다."]], [["Driving to the George and Vulture, they found that Arabella and her maid had sent for a hackney-coach immediately on the receipt of a short note from Emily announcing her arrival in town, and had proceeded straight to the Adelphi.", "조지 앤드 벌처로 마차를 몰고 가 보니, 아라벨라와 그 하녀는 에밀리가 도시에 도착했음을 알리는 짧은 쪽지를 받자마자 삯마차를 불러서 곧장 애덜파이로 떠났다는 것이었다."], ["As Wardle had business to transact in the city, they sent the carriage and the fat boy to his hotel, with the information that he and Mr. Pickwick would return together to dinner at five o’clock.", "와들은 시내에서 볼일이 있었으므로, 마차와 뚱뚱한 소년을 그의 호텔로 보내며, 그와 픽윅 씨가 다섯 시에 함께 저녁을 먹으러 돌아올 것이라고 전하게 했다."]], [["Charged with this message, the fat boy returned, slumbering as peaceably in his dickey, over the stones, as if it had been a down bed on watch springs.", "이 전갈을 맡은 뚱뚱한 소년은, 마치 용수철 위에 깔린 깃털 침대라도 되는 양, 돌길 위를 평온하게 뒷좌석에서 졸며 돌아왔다."], ["By some extraordinary miracle he awoke of his own accord, when the coach stopped, and giving himself a good shake to stir up his faculties, went upstairs to execute his commission.", "무슨 기이한 기적인지, 마차가 멈추자 소년은 제풀에 잠에서 깨어났고, 정신을 차리려고 몸을 한 번 호되게 흔든 뒤 임무를 수행하러 위층으로 올라갔다."]], [["Now, whether the shake had jumbled the fat boy’s faculties together, instead of arranging them in proper order, or had roused such a quantity of new ideas within him as to render him oblivious of ordinary forms and ceremonies, or (which is also possible) had proved unsuccessful in preventing his falling asleep as he ascended the stairs, it is an undoubted fact that he walked into the sitting-room without previously knocking at the door; and so beheld a gentleman with his arms clasping his young mistress’s waist, sitting very lovingly by her side on a sofa, while Arabella and her pretty handmaid feigned to be absorbed in looking out of a window at the other end of the room.", "그런데 그 흔들기가 뚱뚱한 소년의 정신을 제대로 정돈해 주기는커녕 죄다 뒤죽박죽으로 섞어 놓았는지, 아니면 그 안에 새로운 생각을 잔뜩 일깨워 그가 평소의 격식과 예절을 까맣게 잊게 만들었는지, 아니면 (이 또한 있을 법한 일인데) 계단을 오르는 동안 그가 잠드는 것을 막는 데 실패했는지는 모르겠으나, 한 가지 분명한 사실은 그가 먼저 문을 두드리지도 않고 거실로 걸어 들어갔다는 것이다. 그리하여 그는 한 신사가 두 팔로 자기의 젊은 여주인의 허리를 감싸 안고 소파에서 그녀 곁에 아주 다정하게 앉아 있는 광경을 목격했다."], ["At the sight of this phenomenon, the fat boy uttered an interjection, the ladies a scream, and the gentleman an oath, almost simultaneously.", "한편 아라벨라와 그 어여쁜 하녀는 방 저쪽 끝 창밖을 내다보는 데 골몰한 척하고 있었다. 이 진풍경을 보고서, 뚱뚱한 소년은 감탄사를, 숙녀들은 비명을, 그리고 신사는 욕설을, 거의 동시에 내뱉었다."]], [["‘Wretched creature, what do you want here?’ said the gentleman, who it is needless to say was Mr.", "\"한심한 녀석 같으니, 여기 무슨 볼일이냐?\""], ["Snodgrass.", "신사가 말했는데, 그가 스노드그래스 씨임은 굳이 말할 필요도 없겠다."]], [["To this the fat boy, considerably terrified, briefly responded, ‘Missis.’", "이에 대해 뚱뚱한 소년은 잔뜩 겁을 먹은 채로 짧게 대답했다. \"마님이요.\""]], [["‘What do you want me for,’ inquired Emily, turning her head aside, ‘you stupid creature?’", "\"무슨 일로 나를 찾는 거냐,\" 에밀리가 고개를 옆으로 돌리며 물었다. \"이 멍청한 녀석아?\""]], [["‘Master and Mr.", "\"주인 나리하고 픽윅 씨가 다섯 시에 여기서 저녁을 드시러 오신답니다.\""], ["Pickwick is a-going to dine here at five,’ replied the fat boy.", "뚱뚱한 소년이 대답했다."]], [["‘Leave the room!’ said Mr.", "\"방에서 나가!\""], ["Snodgrass, glaring upon the bewildered youth.", "스노드그래스 씨가 어쩔 줄 모르는 소년을 노려보며 말했다."]], [["‘No, no, no,’ added Emily hastily.", "\"안 돼요, 안 돼요, 안 돼.\" 에밀리가 황급히 덧붙였다."], ["‘Bella, dear, advise me.’", "\"벨라, 얘, 어떻게 하면 좋을지 좀 알려 줘.\""]], [["Upon this, Emily and Mr.", "이에 에밀리와 스노드그래스 씨, 그리고 아라벨라와 메리가 한쪽 구석에 모여, 몇 분 동안 속삭이며 진지하게 의논했다."], ["Snodgrass, and Arabella and Mary, crowded into a corner, and conversed earnestly in whispers for some minutes, during which the fat boy dozed.", "그동안 뚱뚱한 소년은 꾸벅꾸벅 졸았다."]], [["‘Joe,’ said Arabella, at length, looking round with a most bewitching smile, ‘how do you do, Joe?’", "\"조.\" 마침내 아라벨라가 더없이 매혹적인 미소를 띠며 돌아보고 말했다. \"잘 지냈니, 조?\""]], [["‘Joe,’ said Emily, ‘you’re a very good boy; I won’t forget you, Joe.’", "\"조.\" 에밀리가 말했다. \"넌 참 착한 아이야. 널 잊지 않으마, 조.\""]], [["‘Joe,’ said Mr.", "\"조.\""], ["Snodgrass, advancing to the astonished youth, and seizing his hand, ‘I didn’t know you before.", "스노드그래스 씨가 어리둥절해하는 소년에게 다가가 그 손을 붙잡으며 말했다. \"내가 전엔 널 몰라봤구나."], ["There’s five shillings for you, Joe!”", "자, 5실링이다, 조!\""]], [["‘I’ll owe you five, Joe,’ said Arabella, ‘for old acquaintance sake, you know;’ and another most captivating smile was bestowed upon the corpulent intruder.", "\"난 5실링을 빚지마, 조.\" 아라벨라가 말했다. \"오랜 정을 봐서 말이야, 알겠지?\" 그러고는 또 한 번 더없이 사람을 사로잡는 미소를 그 살집 좋은 침입자에게 베풀었다."]], [["The fat boy’s perception being slow, he looked rather puzzled at first to account for this sudden prepossession in his favour, and stared about him in a very alarming manner.", "뚱뚱한 소년은 머리 회전이 느려서, 처음엔 자기에게 갑자기 쏟아지는 이 호의를 어떻게 받아들여야 할지 몰라 어리둥절한 표정을 짓고는, 자못 섬뜩한 태도로 주위를 두리번거렸다. 마침내 그 넓적한 얼굴이 그에 걸맞게 넓적한 헤벌쭉한 웃음의 기미를 보이기 시작했다."], ["At length his broad face began to show symptoms of a grin of proportionately broad dimensions; and then, thrusting half-a-crown into each of his pockets, and a hand and wrist after it, he burst into a horse laugh: being for the first and only time in his existence.", "그러더니 양쪽 주머니에 반 크라운씩을 쑤셔 넣고, 그 뒤로 손과 손목까지 따라 집어넣고는, 말처럼 큰 소리로 껄껄 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 그것은 그의 생애에서 처음이자 유일한 일이었다."]], [["‘He understands us, I see,’ said Arabella.", "\"이 애가 우리 뜻을 알아들었나 봐요.\" 아라벨라가 말했다."]], [["‘He had better have something to eat, immediately,’ remarked Emily.", "\"당장 뭘 좀 먹이는 게 좋겠어요.\" 에밀리가 한마디 했다."]], [["The fat boy almost laughed again when he heard this suggestion.", "뚱뚱한 소년은 이 제안을 듣고 또다시 웃을 뻔했다."], ["Mary, after a little more whispering, tripped forth from the group and said--", "메리는 조금 더 속삭이고 나서 무리에서 종종걸음으로 나오며 말했다—"]], [["‘I am going to dine with you to-day, sir, if you have no objection.’", "\"오늘 너랑 같이 저녁을 먹을 거란다, 괜찮다면 말이야.\""]], [["‘This way,’ said the fat boy eagerly.", "\"이쪽이에요.\" 뚱뚱한 소년이 신이 나서 말했다."], ["‘There is such a jolly meat-pie!’", "\"정말 끝내주는 고기 파이가 있거든요!\""]], [["With these words, the fat boy led the way downstairs; his pretty companion captivating all the waiters and angering all the chambermaids as she followed him to the eating-room.", "이 말과 함께 뚱뚱한 소년이 앞장서서 아래층으로 내려갔다. 그의 어여쁜 동행은 식당으로 그를 따라가면서 종업원들을 죄다 사로잡고 객실 하녀들을 죄다 화나게 했다."]], [["There was the meat-pie of which the youth had spoken so feelingly, and there were, moreover, a steak, and a dish of potatoes, and a pot of porter.", "소년이 그토록 감개무량하게 말하던 그 고기 파이가 거기 있었고, 게다가 스테이크 한 접시와 감자 요리 한 접시, 그리고 포터 한 병이 있었다."]], [["‘Sit down,’ said the fat boy.", "\"앉아요.\" 뚱뚱한 소년이 말했다."], ["‘Oh, my eye, how prime!", "\"와, 이거 죽이네!"], ["I am so hungry.’", "난 정말 배고파요.\""]], [["Having apostrophised his eye, in a species of rapture, five or six times, the youth took the head of the little table, and Mary seated herself at the bottom.", "황홀경에 빠진 양 다섯 번인가 여섯 번 \"와\" 하고 감탄사를 내뱉고 나서, 소년은 작은 식탁의 상석에 앉았고, 메리는 말석에 자리를 잡았다."]], [["‘Will you have some of this?’ said the fat boy, plunging into the pie up to the very ferules of the knife and fork.", "\"이거 좀 들래요?\" 뚱뚱한 소년이 나이프와 포크의 자루 끝까지 푹 파이 속에 박아 넣으며 말했다."]], [["‘A little, if you please,’ replied Mary.", "\"조금만, 괜찮다면.\" 메리가 대답했다."]], [["The fat boy assisted Mary to a little, and himself to a great deal, and was just going to begin eating when he suddenly laid down his knife and fork, leaned forward in his chair, and letting his hands, with the knife and fork in them, fall on his knees, said, very slowly--", "뚱뚱한 소년은 메리에게는 조금, 자기에게는 잔뜩 덜어 주고서, 막 먹기 시작하려다가, 갑자기 나이프와 포크를 내려놓고 의자에서 몸을 앞으로 숙이더니, 나이프와 포크를 쥔 두 손을 무릎 위에 떨구고는, 아주 느릿느릿 말했다—"]], [["‘I say!", "\"있잖아요!"], ["How nice you look!’", "정말 예쁘네요!\""]], [["This was said in an admiring manner, and was, so far, gratifying; but still there was enough of the cannibal in the young gentleman’s eyes to render the compliment a double one.", "이 말은 감탄하는 투로 한 것이었으니, 거기까지는 듣기 좋은 소리였다. 그러나 이 젊은 신사의 눈에는 식인종 같은 기색이 여전히 충분히 남아 있어서, 그 찬사를 이중의 의미로 만들고 말았다."]], [["‘Dear me, Joseph,’ said Mary, affecting to blush, ‘what do you mean?’", "\"어머나, 조지프.\" 메리가 얼굴을 붉히는 시늉을 하며 말했다. \"그게 무슨 소리야?\""]], [["The fat boy, gradually recovering his former position, replied with a heavy sigh, and, remaining thoughtful for a few moments, drank a long draught of the porter.", "뚱뚱한 소년은 차츰 본래 자세를 되찾으며, 무거운 한숨으로 답하고는, 몇 순간 생각에 잠겨 있다가, 포터를 길게 한 모금 들이켰다."], ["Having achieved this feat, he sighed again, and applied himself assiduously to the pie.", "이 위업을 이루고 난 뒤 다시 한숨을 쉬고는, 부지런히 파이에 매달렸다."]], [["‘What a nice young lady Miss Emily is!’ said Mary, after a long silence.", "\"에밀리 아가씨는 참 고운 분이셔!\" 메리가 한참 침묵하다가 말했다."]], [["The fat boy had by this time finished the pie.", "이때쯤 뚱뚱한 소년은 파이를 다 먹어 치운 뒤였다."], ["He fixed his eyes on Mary, and replied--", "그는 메리에게 시선을 고정하고 대답했다—"]], [["‘I knows a nicerer.’", "\"난 더 고운 사람도 알아요.\""]], [["‘Indeed!’ said Mary.", "\"어머, 정말!\" 메리가 말했다."]], [["‘Yes, indeed!’ replied the fat boy, with unwonted vivacity.", "\"그럼요, 정말이고말고요!\" 뚱뚱한 소년이 평소답지 않은 활기를 띠며 대답했다."]], [["‘What’s her name?’ inquired Mary.", "\"그 사람 이름이 뭔데?\" 메리가 물었다."]], [["‘What’s yours?’", "\"누나 이름은 뭐예요?\""]], [["‘Mary.’", "\"메리야.\""]], [["‘So’s hers,’ said the fat boy.", "\"그 사람도 그래요.\" 뚱뚱한 소년이 말했다."], ["‘You’re her.’ The boy grinned to add point to the compliment, and put his eyes into something between a squint and a cast, which there is reason to believe he intended for an ogle.", "\"누나가 바로 그 사람이에요.\" 소년은 그 찬사에 쐐기를 박으려고 히죽 웃으며, 두 눈을 사팔뜨기와 곁눈질의 중간쯤으로 만들었는데, 그것은 추파를 던지려던 것이라 짐작할 만한 이유가 있다."]], [["‘You mustn’t talk to me in that way,’ said Mary; ‘you don’t mean it.’", "\"그런 식으로 나한테 말하면 안 돼.\" 메리가 말했다. \"진심도 아니면서.\""]], [["‘Don’t I, though?’ replied the fat boy.", "\"진심이 아니라고요?\" 뚱뚱한 소년이 대답했다."], ["‘I say?’", "\"있잖아요?\""]], [["‘Well?’", "\"왜?\""]], [["‘Are you going to come here regular?’", "\"여기 계속 오실 거예요?\""]], [["‘No,’ rejoined Mary, shaking her head, ‘I’m going away again to-night.", "\"아니.\" 메리가 고개를 저으며 응수했다."], ["Why?’", "\"오늘 밤 다시 떠날 거야. 왜?\""]], [["‘Oh,’ said the fat boy, in a tone of strong feeling; ‘how we should have enjoyed ourselves at meals, if you had been!’", "\"오.\" 뚱뚱한 소년이 격한 감정을 담아 말했다. \"누나가 계셨더라면 식사 때마다 우리 얼마나 즐거웠을까요!\""]], [["‘I might come here sometimes, perhaps, to see you,’ said Mary, plaiting the table-cloth in assumed coyness, ‘if you would do me a favour.’", "\"가끔은 여기 와서 너를 볼 수도 있겠지, 어쩌면.\" 메리가 짐짓 수줍은 척하며 식탁보를 접으며 말했다. \"네가 내 부탁 하나만 들어준다면 말이야.\""]], [["The fat boy looked from the pie-dish to the steak, as if he thought a favour must be in a manner connected with something to eat; and then took out one of the half-crowns and glanced at it nervously.", "뚱뚱한 소년은 부탁이라는 것이 으레 먹을 것과 어떤 식으로든 관계가 있다고 여기는 듯 파이 접시에서 스테이크로 시선을 옮겼다. 그러더니 반 크라운 하나를 꺼내 초조하게 그것을 흘끔 보았다."]], [["‘Don’t you understand me?’ said Mary, looking slily in his fat face.", "\"내 말 못 알아듣겠어?\" 메리가 그의 뚱뚱한 얼굴을 짓궂게 들여다보며 말했다."]], [["Again he looked at the half-crown, and said faintly, ‘No.’", "소년은 또다시 반 크라운을 바라보고는 힘없이 말했다. \"네.\""]], [["‘The ladies want you not to say anything to the old gentleman about the young gentleman having been upstairs; and I want you too.’", "\"숙녀분들이 네가 그 젊은 신사분이 위층에 와 계셨던 걸 영감님께 아무 말도 하지 않기를 바라셔. 나도 그러길 바라고.\""]], [["‘Is that all?’ said the fat boy, evidently very much relieved, as he pocketed the half-crown again.", "\"그게 다예요?\" 뚱뚱한 소년이 반 크라운을 다시 주머니에 넣으며, 분명 크게 안심한 표정으로 말했다."], ["‘Of course I ain’t a-going to.’", "\"당연히 말 안 할 거예요.\""]], [["‘You see,’ said Mary, ‘Mr.", "\"있잖아,\" 메리가 말했다."], ["Snodgrass is very fond of Miss Emily, and Miss Emily’s very fond of him, and if you were to tell about it, the old gentleman would carry you all away miles into the country, where you’d see nobody.’", "\"스노드그래스 씨는 에밀리 아가씨를 무척 좋아하시고, 에밀리 아가씨도 그분을 무척 좋아하셔. 만약 네가 그 얘기를 해 버리면, 영감님이 너희를 모두 시골 멀리 데려가서, 거기선 아무도 못 보게 될 거야.\""]], [["‘No, no, I won’t tell,’ said the fat boy stoutly.", "\"아니, 아니, 말 안 할게요.\" 뚱뚱한 소년이 단호하게 말했다."]], [["‘That’s a dear,’ said Mary.", "\"착하기도 해라.\" 메리가 말했다."], ["‘Now it’s time I went upstairs, and got my lady ready for dinner.’", "\"이제 위층에 올라가서 아가씨 저녁 식사 채비를 해 드릴 시간이네.\""]], [["‘Don’t go yet,’ urged the fat boy.", "\"아직 가지 마요.\" 뚱뚱한 소년이 졸랐다."]], [["‘I must,’ replied Mary.", "\"가야 해.\" 메리가 대답했다."], ["‘Good-bye, for the present.’", "\"그럼 또 봐, 이만.\""]], [["The fat boy, with elephantine playfulness, stretched out his arms to ravish a kiss; but as it required no great agility to elude him, his fair enslaver had vanished before he closed them again; upon which the apathetic youth ate a pound or so of steak with a sentimental countenance, and fell fast asleep.", "뚱뚱한 소년은 코끼리 같은 장난기로 두 팔을 뻗어 입맞춤을 빼앗으려 했다. 그러나 그를 피하는 데에는 그리 대단한 날렵함이 필요치 않았으므로, 그가 다시 두 팔을 오므리기도 전에 그 어여쁜 매혹자는 사라져 버리고 없었다. 그러자 무덤덤한 소년은 감상적인 표정으로 스테이크를 한 파운드쯤 먹고서, 깊이 곯아떨어졌다."]], [["There was so much to say upstairs, and there were so many plans to concert for elopement and matrimony in the event of old Wardle continuing to be cruel, that it wanted only half an hour of dinner when Mr. Snodgrass took his final adieu.", "위층에서는 할 말이 어찌나 많은지, 그리고 와들 영감이 계속 모질게 군다면 도망치고 결혼하기 위해 짜야 할 계획이 어찌나 많은지, 스노드그래스 씨가 마지막 작별을 고했을 때는 저녁 식사까지 겨우 반 시간밖에 남지 않았다."], ["The ladies ran to Emily’s bedroom to dress, and the lover, taking up his hat, walked out of the room. He had scarcely got outside the door, when he heard Wardle’s voice talking loudly, and looking over the banisters beheld him, followed by some other gentlemen, coming straight upstairs.", "숙녀들은 옷을 갈아입으러 에밀리의 침실로 달려갔고, 연인은 모자를 집어 들고 방을 나섰다."], ["Knowing nothing of the house, Mr. Snodgrass in his confusion stepped hastily back into the room he had just quitted, and passing thence into an inner apartment (Mr.", "그가 문밖으로 채 나서기도 전에, 와들이 큰 소리로 떠드는 목소리가 들려왔다."], ["Wardle’s bedchamber), closed the door softly, just as the persons he had caught a glimpse of entered the sitting-room. These were Mr.", "난간 너머로 내려다보니, 다른 몇몇 신사를 거느린 그가 곧장 위층으로 올라오고 있는 것이 보였다."], ["Wardle, Mr. Pickwick, Mr.", "이 집을 전혀 모르는 스노드그래스 씨는 당황한 나머지 황급히 방금 나온 방으로 도로 들어갔고, 거기서 다시 안쪽 방(와들 씨의 침실)으로 들어가, 자기가 얼핏 본 사람들이 거실로 들어서는 바로 그 순간 살그머니 문을 닫았다."], ["Nathaniel Winkle, and Mr. Benjamin Allen, whom he had no difficulty in recognising by their voices.", "그들은 와들 씨, 픽윅 씨, 너새니얼 윈클 씨, 그리고 벤저민 앨런 씨였는데, 그는 그들의 목소리로 어렵지 않게 그들을 알아볼 수 있었다."]], [["‘Very lucky I had the presence of mind to avoid them,’ thought Mr.", "'그들을 피할 침착함을 발휘했으니 천만다행이군.'"], ["Snodgrass with a smile, and walking on tiptoe to another door near the bedside; ‘this opens into the same passage, and I can walk quietly and comfortably away.’", "스노드그래스 씨는 미소를 지으며 생각하고는, 침대 옆 또 다른 문으로 발끝을 세워 걸어갔다. '이 문은 같은 복도로 통하니, 조용히 편안하게 빠져나갈 수 있겠어.'"]], [["There was only one obstacle to his walking quietly and comfortably away, which was that the door was locked and the key gone.", "그가 조용하고 편안하게 빠져나가는 데에는 단 하나의 장애물이 있었으니, 문이 잠겨 있고 열쇠가 없어졌다는 것이었다."]], [["‘Let us have some of your best wine to-day, waiter,’ said old Wardle, rubbing his hands.", "\"오늘은 자네네 가장 좋은 포도주를 좀 내오게, 종업원.\" 와들 영감이 두 손을 비비며 말했다."]], [["‘You shall have some of the very best, sir,’ replied the waiter.", "\"아주 최상품으로 내드리겠습니다, 손님.\" 종업원이 대답했다."]], [["‘Let the ladies know we have come in.’", "\"숙녀분들께 우리가 들어왔다고 알려 주게.\""]], [["‘Yes, Sir.’", "\"예, 손님.\""]], [["Devoutly and ardently did Mr.", "스노드그래스 씨는 숙녀들이 자기가 들어와 있음을 알아 줄 수 있기를 경건하고도 간절하게 바랐다."], ["Snodgrass wish that the ladies could know he had come in.", "그는 한 번은 큰맘 먹고 열쇠 구멍에 대고 \"종업원!\" 하고 속삭여 보았지만, 엉뚱한 종업원이 자기를 구하러 올지도 모른다는 가능성이 머릿속에 번뜩 떠올랐다."], ["He ventured once to whisper, ‘Waiter!’ through the keyhole, but the probability of the wrong waiter coming to his relief, flashed upon his mind, together with a sense of the strong resemblance between his own situation and that in which another gentleman had been recently found in a neighbouring hotel (an account of whose misfortunes had appeared under the head of ‘Police’ in that morning’s paper), he sat himself on a portmanteau, and trembled violently.", "동시에 자기 처지가, 최근 이웃 호텔에서 또 다른 신사가 발견되었던 상황(그 불운에 관한 기사가 그날 아침 신문의 '경찰' 면에 실린 바 있었다)과 너무도 흡사하다는 생각이 들자, 그는 여행 가방 위에 걸터앉아 심하게 떨었다."]], [["‘We won’t wait a minute for Perker,’ said Wardle, looking at his watch; ‘he is always exact.", "\"퍼커는 잠시도 기다리지 말지.\" 와들이 시계를 보며 말했다."], ["He will be here, in time, if he means to come; and if he does not, it’s of no use waiting.", "\"그 친구는 늘 정확하거든. 올 생각이면 시간 맞춰 올 테고, 안 올 거면 기다려 봐야 소용없지."], ["Ha!", "아!"], ["Arabella!’", "아라벨라!\""]], [["‘My sister!’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"내 동생!\""], ["Benjamin Allen, folding her in a most romantic embrace.", "벤저민 앨런 씨가 더없이 낭만적인 포옹으로 그녀를 끌어안으며 외쳤다."]], [["‘Oh, Ben, dear, how you do smell of tobacco,’ said Arabella, rather overcome by this mark of affection.", "\"어머, 벤 오빠, 담배 냄새가 어쩜 이렇게 진동해.\" 아라벨라가 이 애정 표시에 다소 질려서 말했다."]], [["‘Do I?’ said Mr.", "\"내가?\""], ["Benjamin Allen.", "벤저민 앨런 씨가 말했다."], ["‘Do I, Bella?", "\"내가 그래, 벨라?"], ["Well, perhaps I do.’", "음, 그럴지도 모르지.\""]], [["Perhaps he did, having just left a pleasant little smoking-party of twelve medical students, in a small back parlour with a large fire.", "그럴지도 몰랐다. 그는 방금 큼직한 난로가 있는 작은 뒷방에서 의대생 열두 명이 모인 정겨운 자그마한 흡연 모임을 막 빠져나온 참이었으니 말이다."]], [["‘But I am delighted to see you,’ said Mr.", "\"하지만 널 보니 정말 기쁘다.\" 벤 앨런 씨가 말했다."], ["Ben Allen. ‘Bless you, Bella!’", "\"잘 지냈지, 벨라!\""]], [["‘There,’ said Arabella, bending forward to kiss her brother; ‘don’t take hold of me again, Ben, dear, because you tumble me so.’", "\"자, 됐어요.\" 아라벨라가 오빠에게 입을 맞추려 몸을 앞으로 기울이며 말했다. \"다시 날 붙잡지 마, 벤 오빠, 자꾸 날 헝클어뜨리니까.\""]], [["At this point of the reconciliation, Mr. Ben Allen allowed his feelings and the cigars and porter to overcome him, and looked round upon the beholders with damp spectacles.", "화해의 이 대목에서, 벤 앨런 씨는 감정과 시가와 포터에 압도되어, 안경에 김이 서린 채로 둘러선 사람들을 바라보았다."]], [["‘Is nothing to be said to me?’ cried Wardle, with open arms.", "\"내겐 할 말이 없는 거냐?\" 와들이 두 팔을 벌리며 소리쳤다."]], [["‘A great deal,’ whispered Arabella, as she received the old gentleman’s hearty caress and congratulation.", "\"아주 많죠.\" 아라벨라가, 노신사의 다정한 포옹과 축하를 받으며 속삭였다."], ["‘You are a hard-hearted, unfeeling, cruel monster.’", "\"당신은 매정하고 무정하고 잔인한 괴물이에요.\""]], [["‘You are a little rebel,’ replied Wardle, in the same tone, ‘and I am afraid I shall be obliged to forbid you the house. People like you, who get married in spite of everybody, ought not to be let loose on society. But come!’ added the old gentleman aloud, ‘here’s the dinner; you shall sit by me. Joe; why, damn the boy, he’s awake!’", "\"넌 자그마한 반역자로구나.\" 와들이 같은 투로 대꾸했다. \"아무래도 너를 이 집에 못 들어오게 해야겠어. 너처럼 모두의 반대를 무릅쓰고 결혼하는 사람들은 세상에 풀어놓아선 안 되는 법이야. 하지만 자!\" 노신사가 큰 소리로 덧붙였다. \"여기 저녁 식사가 왔구나. 넌 내 곁에 앉거라. 조. 아니, 이 녀석 좀 보게, 깨어 있잖아!\""]], [["To the great distress of his master, the fat boy was indeed in a state of remarkable vigilance, his eyes being wide open, and looking as if they intended to remain so. There was an alacrity in his manner, too, which was equally unaccountable; every time his eyes met those of Emily or Arabella, he smirked and grinned; once, Wardle could have sworn, he saw him wink.", "주인을 크게 당혹스럽게 한 일이지만, 뚱뚱한 소년은 정말로 눈에 띄게 또렷한 상태였다. 두 눈을 활짝 뜨고서, 계속 그렇게 뜨고 있을 작정인 듯이 보였다. 게다가 그의 태도에는 역시 영문 모를 민첩함이 있었다. 그의 눈이 에밀리나 아라벨라의 눈과 마주칠 때마다, 그는 능글맞게 웃고 헤벌쭉거렸다. 한 번은, 와들이 단언컨대, 그가 윙크하는 것을 본 듯도 했다."]], [["This alteration in the fat boy’s demeanour originated in his increased sense of his own importance, and the dignity he acquired from having been taken into the confidence of the young ladies; and the smirks, and grins, and winks were so many condescending assurances that they might depend upon his fidelity.", "뚱뚱한 소년의 이 달라진 거동은, 자신의 중요성에 대한 한층 높아진 자각과, 젊은 숙녀들의 비밀을 함께 나누게 됨으로써 얻은 위엄에서 비롯된 것이었다. 그 능글맞은 웃음과 헤벌쭉거림과 윙크는, 그들이 자기의 충성심을 믿어도 좋다는 갖가지 생색내는 듯한 확언이었다."], ["As these tokens were rather calculated to awaken suspicion than allay it, and were somewhat embarrassing besides, they were occasionally answered by a frown or shake of the head from Arabella, which the fat boy, considering as hints to be on his guard, expressed his perfect understanding of, by smirking, grinning, and winking, with redoubled assiduity.", "그러나 이런 표시들은 의심을 가라앉히기보다는 오히려 불러일으키기 십상이었고, 게다가 다소 난처하기도 했으므로, 이따금 아라벨라가 눈살을 찌푸리거나 고개를 흔드는 것으로 응답했다. 뚱뚱한 소년은 이를 조심하라는 암시로 여겨, 그것을 완벽히 알아들었다는 뜻을 곱절로 부지런히 능글맞게 웃고 헤벌쭉거리고 윙크하는 것으로 표현했다."]], [["‘Joe,’ said Mr.", "\"조.\""], ["Wardle, after an unsuccessful search in all his pockets, ‘is my snuff-box on the sofa?’", "와들 씨가 주머니란 주머니를 다 뒤지고도 허탕을 친 뒤에 말했다. \"내 코담배갑이 소파 위에 있느냐?\""]], [["‘No, sir,’ replied the fat boy.", "\"없는데요, 나리.\" 뚱뚱한 소년이 대답했다."]], [["‘Oh, I recollect; I left it on my dressing-table this morning,’ said Wardle.", "\"아, 생각났다. 오늘 아침 화장대 위에 두고 왔지.\""], ["‘Run into the next room and fetch it.’", "와들이 말했다. \"옆방에 가서 가져오너라.\""]], [["The fat boy went into the next room; and, having been absent about a minute, returned with the snuff-box, and the palest face that ever a fat boy wore.", "뚱뚱한 소년은 옆방으로 들어갔다. 그리고 한 일 분쯤 자리를 비웠다가, 코담배갑과 함께, 뚱뚱한 소년으로서 일찍이 지어 본 적 없는 더없이 창백한 얼굴을 하고 돌아왔다."]], [["‘What’s the matter with the boy?’ exclaimed Wardle.", "\"이 녀석한테 무슨 일이 있는 거냐?\" 와들이 소리쳤다."]], [["‘Nothen’s the matter with me,’ replied Joe nervously.", "\"아무 일도 없는데요.\" 조가 초조하게 대답했다."]], [["‘Have you been seeing any spirits?’ inquired the old gentleman.", "\"귀신이라도 본 게냐?\" 노신사가 물었다."]], [["‘Or taking any?’ added Ben Allen.", "\"아니면 술이라도 마신 게냐?\" 벤 앨런이 덧붙였다."]], [["‘I think you’re right,’ whispered Wardle across the table.", "\"자네 말이 맞는 것 같네.\" 와들이 식탁 너머로 속삭였다."], ["‘He is intoxicated, I’m sure.’", "\"이 녀석 취했어, 틀림없어.\""]], [["Ben Allen replied that he thought he was; and, as that gentleman had seen a vast deal of the disease in question, Wardle was confirmed in an impression which had been hovering about his mind for half an hour, and at once arrived at the conclusion that the fat boy was drunk.", "벤 앨런은 자기도 그런 것 같다고 대답했다. 그리고 그 신사가 바로 그 병을 숱하게 보아 온 터라, 와들은 반 시간 전부터 머릿속을 맴돌던 인상에 확신을 얻어, 곧장 뚱뚱한 소년이 취했다는 결론에 이르렀다."]], [["‘Just keep your eye upon him for a few minutes,’ murmured Wardle.", "\"몇 분만 저 녀석을 잘 지켜보게.\" 와들이 중얼거렸다."], ["‘We shall soon find out whether he is or not.’", "\"취했는지 아닌지 곧 알게 될 테니.\""]], [["The unfortunate youth had only interchanged a dozen words with Mr.", "이 불운한 소년은 스노드그래스 씨와 고작 열 마디쯤 주고받았을 뿐이었다."], ["Snodgrass, that gentleman having implored him to make a private appeal to some friend to release him, and then pushed him out with the snuff- box, lest his prolonged absence should lead to a discovery.", "그 신사가 소년에게 어느 친구에게든 은밀히 부탁하여 자기를 풀어 달라고 간청한 다음, 자리를 오래 비우면 발각될까 봐 코담배갑을 들려 그를 밀어낸 것이었다."], ["He ruminated a little with a most disturbed expression of face, and left the room in search of Mary.", "소년은 더없이 심란한 표정으로 잠깐 골똘히 생각하다가, 메리를 찾으러 방을 나섰다."]], [["But Mary had gone home after dressing her mistress, and the fat boy came back again more disturbed than before.", "그러나 메리는 여주인의 단장을 마친 뒤 집으로 돌아간 뒤였고, 뚱뚱한 소년은 전보다 더 심란해져서 다시 돌아왔다."]], [["Wardle and Mr. Ben Allen exchanged glances.", "와들과 벤 앨런 씨가 서로 눈길을 주고받았다."]], [["‘Joe!’ said Wardle.", "\"조!\" 와들이 말했다."]], [["‘Yes, sir.’", "\"예, 나리.\""]], [["‘What did you go away for?’", "\"왜 나갔던 거냐?\""]], [["The fat boy looked hopelessly in the face of everybody at table, and stammered out that he didn’t know.", "뚱뚱한 소년은 식탁에 둘러앉은 모두의 얼굴을 막막한 심정으로 바라보다가, 자기도 모르겠다고 더듬거렸다."]], [["‘Oh,’ said Wardle, ‘you don’t know, eh?", "\"오.\" 와들이 말했다."], ["Take this cheese to Mr.", "\"모르겠다, 그거냐?"], ["Pickwick.’", "이 치즈를 픽윅 씨께 갖다 드려라.\""]], [["Now, Mr. Pickwick being in the very best health and spirits, had been making himself perfectly delightful all dinner-time, and was at this moment engaged in an energetic conversation with Emily and Mr.", "그런데 픽윅 씨는 더없이 좋은 건강과 기분 상태여서, 저녁 식사 내내 자신을 완벽하게 유쾌한 사람으로 만들고 있었다."], ["Winkle; bowing his head, courteously, in the emphasis of his discourse, gently waving his left hand to lend force to his observations, and all glowing with placid smiles.", "마침 이 순간에는 에밀리, 윈클 씨와 더불어 활기찬 대화에 열중하던 참이었다. 자기 이야기에 힘을 줄 때면 정중하게 고개를 숙이고, 자신의 견해에 무게를 싣고자 왼손을 부드럽게 흔들며, 온통 잔잔한 미소로 빛나고 있었다."], ["He took a piece of cheese from the plate, and was on the point of turning round to renew the conversation, when the fat boy, stooping so as to bring his head on a level with that of Mr.", "그는 접시에서 치즈 한 조각을 집어 들고, 막 몸을 돌려 대화를 다시 이어 가려던 참이었다."], ["Pickwick, pointed with his thumb over his shoulder, and made the most horrible and hideous face that was ever seen out of a Christmas pantomime.", "그때 뚱뚱한 소년이 자기 머리가 픽윅 씨의 머리와 같은 높이가 되도록 몸을 숙이고는, 엄지손가락으로 자기 어깨 너머를 가리키며, 크리스마스 무언극에서도 본 적 없는 가장 끔찍하고 흉측한 표정을 지어 보였다."]], [["‘Dear me!’ said Mr. Pickwick, starting, ‘what a very--Eh?’ He stopped, for the fat boy had drawn himself up, and was, or pretended to be, fast asleep.", "\"아니 이런!\" 픽윅 씨가 흠칫하며 말했다. \"이 무슨 참으로—어?\" 그는 말을 멈췄다. 뚱뚱한 소년이 몸을 똑바로 세우고는, 깊이 잠들었거나 잠든 척하고 있었기 때문이다."]], [["‘What’s the matter?’ inquired Wardle.", "\"무슨 일입니까?\" 와들이 물었다."]], [["‘This is such an extremely singular lad!’ replied Mr.", "\"이 아이는 정말이지 지극히 별난 녀석이군요!\""], ["Pickwick, looking uneasily at the boy.", "픽윅 씨가 불안하게 소년을 바라보며 대답했다."], ["‘It seems an odd thing to say, but upon my word I am afraid that, at times, he is a little deranged.’", "\"이런 말을 하기는 좀 이상하지만, 정말이지 저는 이 아이가 때때로 약간 정신이 온전치 않은 게 아닌가 걱정됩니다.\""]], [["‘Oh! Mr.", "\"어머!"], ["Pickwick, pray don’t say so,’ cried Emily and Arabella, both at once.", "픽윅 씨, 제발 그런 말씀 마세요.\" 에밀리와 아라벨라가 동시에 소리쳤다."]], [["‘I am not certain, of course,’ said Mr.", "\"물론 확실치는 않습니다만,\""], ["Pickwick, amidst profound silence and looks of general dismay; ‘but his manner to me this moment really was very alarming.", "픽윅 씨가 깊은 침묵과 모두의 당혹스러운 표정 가운데서 말했다. \"방금 저를 대하는 이 아이의 태도가 정말 무척 섬뜩했습니다."], ["Oh!’ ejaculated Mr.", "아얏!\""], ["Pickwick, suddenly jumping up with a short scream.", "픽윅 씨가 짧은 비명과 함께 갑자기 벌떡 일어서며 외쳤다."], ["‘I beg your pardon, ladies, but at that moment he ran some sharp instrument into my leg.", "\"용서하십시오, 숙녀분들. 하지만 방금 이 아이가 무슨 뾰족한 도구로 제 다리를 찔렀습니다."], ["Really, he is not safe.’", "정말이지 이 아이는 위험합니다.\""]], [["‘He’s drunk,’ roared old Wardle passionately.", "\"이 녀석 취했어.\" 와들 영감이 격하게 고함쳤다."], ["‘Ring the bell!", "\"종을 울려라!"], ["Call the waiters!", "종업원들을 불러!"], ["He’s drunk.’", "이 녀석 취했다고.\""]], [["‘I ain’t,’ said the fat boy, falling on his knees as his master seized him by the collar.", "\"안 취했어요.\" 뚱뚱한 소년이, 주인이 멱살을 잡자 무릎을 꿇으며 말했다."], ["‘I ain’t drunk.’", "\"전 안 취했어요.\""]], [["‘Then you’re mad; that’s worse.", "\"그럼 미친 게로구나. 그게 더 나쁘지."], ["Call the waiters,’ said the old gentleman.", "종업원들을 불러라.\" 노신사가 말했다."]], [["‘I ain’t mad; I’m sensible,’ rejoined the fat boy, beginning to cry.", "\"전 안 미쳤어요. 제정신이라고요.\" 뚱뚱한 소년이 울먹이기 시작하며 응수했다."]], [["‘Then, what the devil did you run sharp instruments into Mr.", "\"그럼 도대체 왜 픽윅 씨 다리에 뾰족한 도구를 찔러 댔단 말이냐?\""], ["Pickwick’s legs for?’ inquired Wardle angrily.", "와들이 화를 내며 물었다."]], [["‘He wouldn’t look at me,’ replied the boy.", "\"저분이 절 안 봐 주셔서요.\" 소년이 대답했다."], ["‘I wanted to speak to him.’", "\"저분께 말씀드리고 싶었거든요.\""]], [["‘What did you want to say?’ asked half a dozen voices at once.", "\"무슨 말을 하고 싶었던 거냐?\" 대여섯 사람의 목소리가 한꺼번에 물었다."]], [["The fat boy gasped, looked at the bedroom door, gasped again, and wiped two tears away with the knuckle of each of his forefingers.", "뚱뚱한 소년은 숨을 헐떡이며 침실 문을 바라보고, 다시 헐떡이고는, 양 검지의 손가락 마디로 눈물 두 방울을 닦았다."]], [["‘What did you want to say?’ demanded Wardle, shaking him.", "\"무슨 말을 하고 싶었던 거냐?\" 와들이 그를 흔들며 다그쳤다."]], [["‘Stop!’ said Mr.", "\"잠깐!\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick; ‘allow me.", "\"제게 맡기십시오."], ["What did you wish to communicate to me, my poor boy?’", "내게 무슨 이야기를 전하고 싶었니, 가엾은 아이야?\""]], [["‘I want to whisper to you,’ replied the fat boy.", "\"속삭여서 말씀드리고 싶어요.\" 뚱뚱한 소년이 대답했다."]], [["‘You want to bite his ear off, I suppose,’ said Wardle. ‘Don’t come near him; he’s vicious; ring the bell, and let him be taken downstairs.’", "\"이분 귀를 물어뜯고 싶다는 거겠지.\" 와들이 말했다. \"저 녀석 가까이 가지 마십시오. 사나운 녀석이니까. 종을 울려서 아래층으로 끌고 가게 합시다.\""]], [["Just as Mr.", "윈클 씨가 막 종 줄을 손에 쥐려던 바로 그 순간, 그 손길이 일제히 터진 경악의 외침에 멈춰졌다."], ["Winkle caught the bell-rope in his hand, it was arrested by a general expression of astonishment; the captive lover, his face burning with confusion, suddenly walked in from the bedroom, and made a comprehensive bow to the company.", "갇혀 있던 연인이, 당혹감으로 얼굴이 벌겋게 달아오른 채로, 침실에서 불쑥 걸어 나와 일동에게 두루 절을 한 것이다."]], [["‘Hollo!’ cried Wardle, releasing the fat boy’s collar, and staggering back.", "\"이런!\" 와들이 뚱뚱한 소년의 멱살을 놓고 비틀거리며 뒤로 물러나며 외쳤다."], ["‘What’s this?’", "\"이게 무슨 일인가?\""]], [["‘I have been concealed in the next room, sir, since you returned,’ explained Mr.", "\"선생님이 돌아오신 뒤로 옆방에 숨어 있었습니다.\""], ["Snodgrass.", "스노드그래스 씨가 해명했다."]], [["‘Emily, my girl,’ said Wardle reproachfully, ‘I detest meanness and deceit; this is unjustifiable and indelicate in the highest degree. I don’t deserve this at your hands, Emily, indeed!’", "\"에밀리, 얘야.\" 와들이 나무라듯 말했다. \"난 비열함과 기만을 몹시 싫어한다. 이건 도무지 변명의 여지가 없고, 더없이 점잖지 못한 짓이야. 난 네게서 이런 일을 당할 사람이 아니야, 에밀리, 정말이지!\""]], [["‘Dear papa,’ said Emily, ‘Arabella knows--everybody here knows--Joe knows--that I was no party to this concealment.", "\"아빠.\" 에밀리가 말했다."], ["Augustus, for Heaven’s sake, explain it!’", "\"아라벨라도 알아요—여기 있는 모두가 알아요—조도 알고요—이 숨긴 일에 제가 가담하지 않았다는 걸요. 오거스터스, 제발 좀 설명해 줘요!\""]], [["Mr. Snodgrass, who had only waited for a hearing, at once recounted how he had been placed in his then distressing predicament; how the fear of giving rise to domestic dissensions had alone prompted him to avoid Mr.", "그저 발언할 기회만 기다리고 있던 스노드그래스 씨는, 곧장 자신이 어떻게 그 곤혹스러운 처지에 놓이게 되었는지를 죽 이야기했다."], ["Wardle on his entrance; how he merely meant to depart by another door, but, finding it locked, had been compelled to stay against his will.", "집안의 불화를 일으킬까 하는 두려움 하나 때문에 와들 씨가 들어올 때 그를 피했던 것이며, 그저 다른 문으로 나가려 했는데 그 문이 잠겨 있는 바람에 본의 아니게 머물 수밖에 없었던 것이라고."], ["It was a painful situation to be placed in; but he now regretted it the less, inasmuch as it afforded him an opportunity of acknowledging, before their mutual friends, that he loved Mr.", "처하기에 괴로운 상황이긴 했으나, 지금은 그것을 그리 후회하지 않는다고 했다. 왜냐하면 그것이 그에게, 서로의 친구들 앞에서, 자신이 와들 씨의 따님을 깊고 진실하게 사랑한다는 것을 고백할 기회를 주었기 때문이라고."], ["Wardle’s daughter deeply and sincerely; that he was proud to avow that the feeling was mutual; and that if thousands of miles were placed between them, or oceans rolled their waters, he could never for an instant forget those happy days, when first--et cetera, et cetera.", "그 마음이 서로 통하는 것임을 자랑스럽게 밝히는 바이며, 설령 수천 마일이 두 사람 사이에 가로놓이거나 대양이 그 물결을 굽이치게 한다 해도, 처음으로—기타 등등, 기타 등등 했던 그 행복한 나날들을 한순간도 결코 잊을 수 없으리라고."]], [["Having delivered himself to this effect, Mr. Snodgrass bowed again, looked into the crown of his hat, and stepped towards the door.", "이런 취지로 자기 심정을 토로하고 나서, 스노드그래스 씨는 다시 절을 하고, 모자의 꼭대기 속을 들여다보고는, 문 쪽으로 걸음을 옮겼다."]], [["‘Stop!’ shouted Wardle.", "\"멈추게!\" 와들이 외쳤다."], ["‘Why, in the name of all that’s--’", "\"아니, 도대체 무엇의 이름으로—\""]], [["‘Inflammable,’ mildly suggested Mr.", "\"불같은 것의.\""], ["Pickwick, who thought something worse was coming.", "픽윅 씨가, 무언가 더 험한 말이 나올까 봐 부드럽게 거들어 주었다."]], [["‘Well--that’s inflammable,’ said Wardle, adopting the substitute; ‘couldn’t you say all this to me in the first instance?’", "\"음—불같은 것의 이름으로 말일세.\" 와들이 그 대용어를 받아들이며 말했다. \"이 모든 걸 애초에 나한테 말할 수는 없었나?\""]], [["‘Or confide in me?’ added Mr.", "\"아니면 제게 털어놓든지요?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 덧붙였다."]], [["‘Dear, dear,’ said Arabella, taking up the defence, ‘what is the use of asking all that now, especially when you know you had set your covetous old heart on a richer son-in-law, and are so wild and fierce besides, that everybody is afraid of you, except me? Shake hands with him, and order him some dinner, for goodness gracious’ sake, for he looks half starved; and pray have your wine up at once, for you’ll not be tolerable until you have taken two bottles at least.’", "\"어머, 어머.\" 아라벨라가 변호에 나서며 말했다. \"지금 와서 그런 걸 다 따져 무슨 소용이에요, 더구나 당신의 그 욕심 많은 늙은 마음이 더 부유한 사위에게 가 있었던 걸 알면서, 게다가 어찌나 거칠고 사나운지 저 빼고는 모두가 무서워하는 분이면서요? 이분과 악수하고, 저녁이라도 좀 시켜 주세요, 제발 좀요, 이분 반쯤 굶주린 얼굴이잖아요. 그리고 포도주도 어서 올려 오라고 하세요, 적어도 두 병은 드셔야 봐 줄 만해지실 테니까요.\""]], [["The worthy old gentleman pulled Arabella’s ear, kissed her without the smallest scruple, kissed his daughter also with great affection, and shook Mr. Snodgrass warmly by the hand.", "이 갸륵한 노신사는 아라벨라의 귀를 잡아당기고, 조금의 거리낌도 없이 그녀에게 입을 맞추고, 제 딸에게도 더없는 애정으로 입을 맞춘 뒤, 스노드그래스 씨의 손을 따뜻하게 잡고 흔들었다."]], [["‘She is right on one point at all events,’ said the old gentleman cheerfully.", "\"적어도 한 가지 점에서는 저 애 말이 옳아.\" 노신사가 유쾌하게 말했다."], ["‘Ring for the wine!’", "\"포도주를 가져오라고 종을 울려라!\""]], [["The wine came, and Perker came upstairs at the same moment. Mr.", "포도주가 왔고, 바로 그 순간 퍼커가 위층으로 올라왔다."], ["Snodgrass had dinner at a side table, and, when he had despatched it, drew his chair next Emily, without the smallest opposition on the old gentleman’s part.", "스노드그래스 씨는 곁상에서 저녁을 들었고, 다 먹고 나자 노신사 쪽의 조금의 반대도 없이 의자를 에밀리 곁으로 끌어다 놓았다."]], [["The evening was excellent. Little Mr. Perker came out wonderfully, told various comic stories, and sang a serious song which was almost as funny as the anecdotes. Arabella was very charming, Mr. Wardle very jovial, Mr. Pickwick very harmonious, Mr. Ben Allen very uproarious, the lovers very silent, Mr. Winkle very talkative, and all of them very happy.", "저녁 시간은 더없이 훌륭했다. 자그마한 퍼커 씨는 놀랍도록 흥이 올라, 갖가지 우스운 이야기를 들려주고, 그 일화들 못지않게 우스운 진지한 노래 한 곡을 불렀다. 아라벨라는 무척 사랑스러웠고, 와들 씨는 무척 유쾌했고, 픽윅 씨는 무척 조화로웠고, 벤 앨런 씨는 무척 떠들썩했고, 연인들은 무척 말이 없었고, 윈클 씨는 무척 수다스러웠으며, 그들 모두는 무척 행복했다."]], [["CHAPTER LV. MR.", "\"새뮤얼,\""], ["SOLOMON PELL, ASSISTED BY A SELECT COMMITTEE OF COACHMEN, ARRANGES THE AFFAIRS OF THE ELDER MR. WELLER\nSamivel,’ said Mr.", "장례식 다음 날 아침, 웰러 씨가 아들에게 말을 건넸다."], ["Weller, accosting his son on the morning after the funeral, ‘I’ve found it, Sammy.", "\"그걸 찾았다, 새미."], ["I thought it wos there.’", "거기 있을 줄 알았어.\""]], [["‘Thought wot wos there?’ inquired Sam.", "\"뭐가 거기 있을 줄 알았다고요?\" 샘이 물었다."]], [["‘Your mother-in-law’s vill, Sammy,’ replied Mr. Weller.", "\"네 새어머니의 유언장 말이다, 새미,\""], ["‘In wirtue o’ vich, them arrangements is to be made as I told you on, last night, respectin’ the funs.’", "웰러 씨가 대답했다. \"그게 있어야 어젯밤에 내가 너한테 말한 그 자금 처리 일을 손볼 수 있는 거 아니냐.\""]], [["‘Wot, didn’t she tell you were it wos?’ inquired Sam.", "\"아니, 그게 어디 있는지 새어머니가 말 안 해 줬어요?\" 샘이 물었다."]], [["‘Not a bit on it, Sammy,’ replied Mr.", "\"한마디도 없었다, 새미,\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다."], ["Weller. ‘We wos a adjestin’ our little differences, and I wos a-cheerin’ her spirits and bearin’ her up, so that I forgot to ask anythin’ about it.", "\"우리는 우리끼리 작은 다툼을 풀고 있었고, 나는 그 사람 기운을 북돋아 주고 받쳐 주느라 그것에 대해 물어볼 생각조차 못 했지."], ["I don’t know as I should ha’ done it, indeed, if I had remembered it,’ added Mr.", "사실 기억했더라도 물어봤을지는 모르겠다만,\" 웰러 씨가 덧붙였다."], ["Weller, ‘for it’s a rum sort o’ thing, Sammy, to go a-hankerin’ arter anybody’s property, ven you’re assistin’ ‘em in illness.", "\"새미, 누군가 병들어 도와주는 마당에 그 재산을 탐내고 돌아다니는 건 영 고약한 짓이거든."], ["It’s like helping an outside passenger up, ven he’s been pitched off a coach, and puttin’ your hand in his pocket, vile you ask him, vith a sigh, how he finds his-self, Sammy.’", "마차에서 떨어진 바깥 승객을 일으켜 세워 주는 척하면서 한숨 쉬며 '괜찮으시오?' 하고 묻는 동안 그자 주머니에 손을 집어넣는 거나 마찬가지지, 새미.\""]], [["With this figurative illustration of his meaning, Mr. Weller unclasped his pocket-book, and drew forth a dirty sheet of letter-paper, on which were inscribed various characters crowded together in remarkable confusion.", "이렇게 자기 뜻을 비유적으로 설명한 뒤, 웰러 씨는 수첩을 펼쳐 더러운 편지지 한 장을 끄집어냈는데, 거기에는 갖가지 글자가 놀랄 만큼 어지럽게 뒤엉켜 빽빽하게 적혀 있었다."]], [["‘This here is the dockyment, Sammy,’ said Mr.", "\"이게 바로 그 문서다, 새미,\" 웰러 씨가 말했다."], ["Weller. ‘I found it in the little black tea-pot, on the top shelf o’ the bar closet.", "\"술집 벽장 맨 위 선반에 있던 작은 검은 찻주전자 속에서 찾았다."], ["She used to keep bank-notes there, ‘fore she vos married, Samivel.", "결혼하기 전엔 거기다 지폐를 넣어 두곤 했지, 새뮤얼. 계산하려고 뚜껑 여는 걸 내가 여러 번, 여러 번 봤다."], ["I’ve seen her take the lid off, to pay a bill, many and many a time.", "가엾은 사람, 집 안의 찻주전자란 찻주전자를 죄다 유언장으로 채워 놨어도 조금도 불편하지 않았을 게다."], ["Poor creetur, she might ha’ filled all the tea-pots in the house vith vills, and not have inconwenienced herself neither, for she took wery little of anythin’ in that vay lately, ‘cept on the temperance nights, ven they just laid a foundation o’ tea to put the spirits atop on!’", "요즘 들어선 그쪽 방면으로 뭐 그리 많이 마시지도 않았으니까, 금주의 밤만 빼고 말이지. 그날만은 차로 기초를 깔고 그 위에 독한 술을 부어 올렸거든!\""]], [["‘What does it say?’ inquired Sam.", "\"뭐라고 적혀 있는데요?\" 샘이 물었다."]], [["‘Jist vot I told you, my boy,’ rejoined his parent.", "\"내가 너한테 말한 그대로다, 얘야,\" 그의 아버지가 대답했다."], ["‘Two hundred pound vurth o’ reduced counsels to my son-in-law, Samivel, and all the rest o’ my property, of ev’ry kind and description votsoever, to my husband, Mr. Tony Veller, who I appint as my sole eggzekiter.’", "\"'감액 공채 이백 파운드어치를 내 사위 새뮤얼에게, 그 밖의 온갖 종류와 명목의 내 모든 재산은 내 남편 토니 웰러 씨에게 남기며, 그를 나의 유일한 유언 집행인으로 임명한다.'\""]], [["‘That’s all, is it?’ said Sam.", "\"그게 전부예요?\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["‘That’s all,’ replied Mr. Weller.", "\"그게 전부다,\""], ["‘And I s’pose as it’s all right and satisfactory to you and me as is the only parties interested, ve may as vell put this bit o’ paper into the fire.’", "웰러 씨가 대답했다. \"그래서 말인데, 이해 당사자가 너하고 나뿐이고 다 옳고 만족스러우니, 이 종잇조각은 불 속에 던져 버려도 무방할 게다.\""]], [["‘Wot are you a-doin’ on, you lunatic?’ said Sam, snatching the paper away, as his parent, in all innocence, stirred the fire preparatory to suiting the action to the word.", "\"미친 영감님, 지금 뭐 하시는 거예요?\" 샘이 종이를 낚아채며 말했다."], ["‘You’re a nice eggzekiter, you are.’", "그의 아버지가 천연덕스럽게 말한 대로 행하려고 불을 휘젓고 있던 참이었다. \"유언 집행인 한번 훌륭하시네요, 정말.\""]], [["‘Vy not?’ inquired Mr.", "\"왜 안 되는데?\""], ["Weller, looking sternly round, with the poker in his hand.", "웰러 씨가 부지깽이를 손에 든 채 엄하게 둘러보며 물었다."]], [["‘Vy not?’ exclaimed Sam.", "\"왜 안 되냐고요?\""], ["‘’Cos it must be proved, and probated, and swore to, and all manner o’ formalities.’", "샘이 외쳤다. \"검인을 받고, 증명하고, 선서하고, 온갖 격식을 다 거쳐야 하니까요.\""]], [["‘You don’t mean that?’ said Mr.", "\"그게 정말이냐?\""], ["Weller, laying down the poker.", "웰러 씨가 부지깽이를 내려놓으며 말했다."]], [["Sam buttoned the will carefully in a side pocket; intimating by a look, meanwhile, that he did mean it, and very seriously too.", "샘은 유언장을 옆주머니에 조심스레 단추까지 채워 넣었다. 그러면서 눈빛으로, 자기 말이 진심이며 그것도 아주 진지한 진심임을 내비쳤다."]], [["‘Then I’ll tell you wot it is,’ said Mr.", "\"그렇다면 내 말해 두마,\""], ["Weller, after a short meditation, ‘this is a case for that ‘ere confidential pal o’ the Chancellorship’s.", "잠시 생각에 잠겼다가 웰러 씨가 말했다. \"이건 그 대법관님과 친한 양반한테 맡길 일이다."], ["Pell must look into this, Sammy.", "펠이 이걸 살펴봐야 해, 새미."], ["He’s the man for a difficult question at law.", "그 사람이 까다로운 법률 문제엔 적임자지."], ["Ve’ll have this here brought afore the Solvent Court, directly, Samivel.’", "이걸 곧장 파산 법원에 올려야겠다, 새뮤얼.\""]], [["‘I never did see such a addle-headed old creetur!’ exclaimed Sam irritably; ‘Old Baileys, and Solvent Courts, and alleybis, and ev’ry species o’ gammon alvays a-runnin’ through his brain.", "\"이렇게 머리가 멍청한 영감은 또 처음 보네!\" 샘이 짜증을 내며 외쳤다."], ["You’d better get your out o’ door clothes on, and come to town about this bisness, than stand a-preachin’ there about wot you don’t understand nothin’ on.’", "\"올드 베일리니, 파산 법원이니, 현장 부재 증명이니, 온갖 헛소리가 늘 머릿속을 휘젓고 다닌다니까. 자기가 아무것도 모르는 일을 가지고 거기 서서 설교하느니, 외출복이나 챙겨 입고 이 일로 시내에 가는 게 낫겠어요.\""]], [["‘Wery good, Sammy,’ replied Mr.", "\"아주 좋다, 새미,\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다."], ["Weller, ‘I’m quite agreeable to anythin’ as vill hexpedite business, Sammy.", "\"일을 신속하게 처리하는 일이라면 나는 뭐든 찬성이다, 새미. 하지만 이거 하난 명심해라, 얘야."], ["But mind this here, my boy, nobody but Pell--nobody but Pell as a legal adwiser.’", "펠 말고는 아무도 안 돼. 법률 자문은 펠 말고는 누구도 안 돼.\""]], [["‘I don’t want anybody else,’ replied Sam.", "\"저도 다른 사람은 필요 없어요,\" 샘이 대답했다."], ["‘Now, are you a-comin’?’", "\"자, 이제 가시겠어요?\""]], [["‘Vait a minit, Sammy,’ replied Mr.", "\"잠깐 기다려라, 새미,\""], ["Weller, who, having tied his shawl with the aid of a small glass that hung in the window, was now, by dint of the most wonderful exertions, struggling into his upper garments.", "웰러 씨가 대답했다. 창가에 걸린 작은 거울의 도움을 받아 목도리를 매고 난 그는, 이제 놀라울 만큼 안간힘을 쓰며 윗옷 속으로 몸을 비집어 넣느라 씨름하고 있었다."], ["‘Vait a minit’ Sammy; ven you grow as old as your father, you von’t get into your veskit quite as easy as you do now, my boy.’", "\"잠깐만 기다려라, 새미. 네가 네 아비만큼 나이가 들면, 지금처럼 조끼에 쉽게 들어가지지가 않는단다, 얘야.\""]], [["‘If I couldn’t get into it easier than that, I’m blessed if I’d vear vun at all,’ rejoined his son.", "\"그렇게 들어가는 것보다 더 쉽게 안 들어간다면, 차라리 조끼 따위 아예 안 입고 말겠어요,\" 그의 아들이 대꾸했다."]], [["‘You think so now,’ said Mr.", "\"지금이야 그렇게 생각하겠지,\""], ["Weller, with the gravity of age, ‘but you’ll find that as you get vider, you’ll get viser.", "웰러 씨가 노인다운 진중함으로 말했다. \"허나 몸이 넓어질수록 지혜도 넓어진다는 걸 알게 될 게다."], ["Vidth and visdom, Sammy, alvays grows together.’", "넓이와 지혜는 말이다, 새미, 늘 함께 자라는 법이야.\""]], [["As Mr. Weller delivered this infallible maxim--the result of many years’ personal experience and observation--he contrived, by a dexterous twist of his body, to get the bottom button of his coat to perform its office.", "웰러 씨가 여러 해에 걸친 몸소의 경험과 관찰의 결실인 이 틀림없는 격언을 늘어놓는 동안, 그는 몸을 능숙하게 비틀어 마침내 외투의 맨 아래 단추가 제 구실을 하도록 만들었다."], ["Having paused a few seconds to recover breath, he brushed his hat with his elbow, and declared himself ready.", "몇 초간 숨을 고른 뒤, 그는 팔꿈치로 모자를 쓱쓱 닦고는 준비가 됐다고 선언했다."]], [["‘As four heads is better than two, Sammy,’ said Mr.", "\"머리 넷이 둘보다 나으니까, 새미,\" 그들이 이륜마차를 타고 런던 가도를 달리는 동안 웰러 씨가 말했다."], ["Weller, as they drove along the London Road in the chaise-cart, ‘and as all this here property is a wery great temptation to a legal gen’l’m’n, ve’ll take a couple o’ friends o’ mine vith us, as’ll be wery soon down upon him if he comes anythin’ irreg’lar; two o’ them as saw you to the Fleet that day.", "\"또 이 재산이란 게 법률 양반한테는 엄청난 유혹이니까, 내 친구 두엇을 데려가자꾸나. 그자가 조금이라도 수상한 짓을 하면 곧장 덮칠 친구들이지."], ["They’re the wery best judges,’ added Mr.", "그날 너를 플리트까지 바래다준 그 두 사람 말이다. 그 친구들이야말로 최고의 감정가들이거든,\""], ["Weller, in a half-whisper- -’the wery best judges of a horse, you ever know’d.’", "웰러 씨가 반쯤 속삭이며 덧붙였다. \"네가 아는 한 가장 뛰어난 말 감정가들이지.\""]], [["‘And of a lawyer too?’ inquired Sam.", "\"변호사 감정도요?\" 샘이 물었다."]], [["‘The man as can form a ackerate judgment of a animal, can form a ackerate judgment of anythin’,’ replied his father, so dogmatically, that Sam did not attempt to controvert the position.", "\"짐승을 정확하게 판단할 줄 아는 사람은 뭐든 정확하게 판단할 줄 아는 법이다,\" 그의 아버지가 어찌나 단정적으로 대답하던지, 샘은 그 주장을 반박하려 들지 않았다."]], [["In pursuance of this notable resolution, the services of the mottled- faced gentleman and of two other very fat coachmen--selected by Mr.", "이 훌륭한 결심을 좇아, 얼룩진 얼굴의 신사와 다른 살집 좋은 마부 둘—아마도 웰러 씨가 그들의 넓이와, 따라서 거기서 비롯되는 지혜를 보고 골랐을 것이다—의 협력을 요청했다."], ["Weller, probably, with a view to their width and consequent wisdom--were put into requisition; and this assistance having been secured, the party proceeded to the public-house in Portugal Street, whence a messenger was despatched to the Insolvent Court over the way, requiring Mr. Solomon Pell’s immediate attendance.", "이 지원을 확보한 일행은 포르투갈 가의 선술집으로 향했고, 거기서 길 건너 파산 법원으로 심부름꾼을 보내 솔로몬 펠 씨가 즉시 와 줄 것을 청했다."]], [["The messenger fortunately found Mr.", "심부름꾼은 다행히도 펠 씨가 법정 안에 있는 것을 찾아냈다."], ["Solomon Pell in court, regaling himself, business being rather slack, with a cold collation of an Abernethy biscuit and a saveloy.", "일감이 다소 한산한 터라, 그는 애버네시 비스킷과 소시지로 된 간단한 찬 음식으로 요기를 하고 있었다."], ["The message was no sooner whispered in his ear than he thrust them in his pocket among various professional documents, and hurried over the way with such alacrity that he reached the parlour before the messenger had even emancipated himself from the court.", "그 전갈이 귓속에 속삭여지자마자 그는 그것들을 갖가지 직무용 서류 사이에 끼워 주머니에 쑤셔 넣고는, 어찌나 날쌔게 길을 건너왔는지 심부름꾼이 법정에서 채 빠져나오기도 전에 응접실에 닿았다."]], [["‘Gentlemen,’ said Mr.", "\"여러분,\""], ["Pell, touching his hat, ‘my service to you all.", "펠 씨가 모자에 손을 대며 말했다."], ["I don’t say it to flatter you, gentlemen, but there are not five other men in the world, that I’d have come out of that court for, to-day.’", "\"모두에게 인사 올립니다. 아첨하려고 하는 말은 아닙니다만, 여러분, 오늘 제가 저 법정에서 나올 만한 사람은 세상에 다섯 명도 안 됩니다.\""]], [["‘So busy, eh?’ said Sam.", "\"그렇게 바쁘셨소, 응?\" 샘이 말했다."]], [["‘Busy!’ replied Pell; ‘I’m completely sewn up, as my friend the late Lord Chancellor many a time used to say to me, gentlemen, when he came out from hearing appeals in the House of Lords. Poor fellow; he was very susceptible to fatigue; he used to feel those appeals uncommonly. I actually thought more than once that he’d have sunk under ‘em; I did, indeed.’", "\"바쁘냐고요!\" 펠 씨가 대답했다. \"완전히 녹초가 되었지요. 고인이 되신 제 친구 대법관님께서 상원에서 항소심을 듣고 나오실 때마다 저한테 여러 번 하시던 말씀입니다, 여러분. 가엾은 양반, 피로에 무척 약하셨지요. 그 항소심들을 유독 힘들어하셨습니다. 저는 솔직히 한두 번 이상, 그분이 그 일에 짓눌려 쓰러지실 거라고 생각했습니다. 정말이지요.\""]], [["Here Mr. Pell shook his head and paused; on which, the elder Mr. Weller, nudging his neighbour, as begging him to mark the attorney’s high connections, asked whether the duties in question produced any permanent ill effects on the constitution of his noble friend.", "여기서 펠 씨가 고개를 젓고 말을 멈추자, 웰러 영감은 이웃한 친구를 슬쩍 찔러 그 변호사의 높은 인맥을 눈여겨보라는 듯이 하고는, 그 직무가 그분 고귀한 친구의 체질에 어떤 영구적인 해를 끼쳤는지 물었다."]], [["‘I don’t think he ever quite recovered them,’ replied Pell; ‘in fact I’m sure he never did. “Pell,” he used to say to me many a time, “how the blazes you can stand the head-work you do, is a mystery to me.”--“Well,” I used to answer, “I hardly know how I do it, upon my life.”--“Pell,” he’d add, sighing, and looking at me with a little envy--friendly envy, you know, gentlemen, mere friendly envy; I never minded it--“Pell, you’re a wonder; a wonder.” Ah! you’d have liked him very much if you had known him, gentlemen. Bring me three-penn’orth of rum, my dear.’", "\"그분이 거기서 완전히 회복하신 적은 없는 것 같습니다,\" 펠 씨가 대답했다. \"아니, 결코 회복하지 못하셨다고 확신합니다. '펠,' 그분이 저한테 여러 번 말씀하셨지요. '자네가 그 골치 아픈 머리 일을 어떻게 견뎌 내는지 나로선 도무지 알 수가 없네.' '글쎄요,' 제가 대답하곤 했습니다. '제가 어떻게 해내는지 저도 잘 모르겠습니다, 정말로.' '펠,' 그분은 한숨을 쉬며, 약간의 부러움—친근한 부러움이지요, 여러분, 그저 친근한 부러움 말입니다. 저는 한 번도 개의치 않았습니다만—그런 눈으로 저를 보시며 덧붙이셨지요. '펠, 자네는 경이야. 경이로운 사람이야.' 아! 여러분이 그분을 아셨더라면 무척 좋아하셨을 겁니다, 여러분. 럼주 삼 펜스어치만 가져다주게, 아가씨.\""]], [["Addressing this latter remark to the waitress, in a tone of subdued grief, Mr.", "이 마지막 말을 웨이트리스에게 가라앉은 슬픔의 어조로 건넨 뒤, 펠 씨는 한숨을 내쉬며 자기 구두와 천장을 바라보았다."], ["Pell sighed, looked at his shoes and the ceiling; and, the rum having by that time arrived, drank it up.", "그러는 사이 럼주가 도착하자 그것을 들이켰다."]], [["‘However,’ said Pell, drawing a chair to the table, ‘a professional man has no right to think of his private friendships when his legal assistance is wanted.", "\"하지만,\" 펠 씨가 의자를 탁자 쪽으로 끌어당기며 말했다."], ["By the bye, gentlemen, since I saw you here before, we have had to weep over a very melancholy occurrence.’", "\"법조인이란 법률적 도움이 필요한 마당에 사사로운 친분을 생각해서는 안 되는 법이지요. 그건 그렇고, 여러분, 지난번 여기서 뵌 뒤로 우리는 참으로 애통한 일을 두고 눈물을 흘려야 했지요.\""]], [["Mr. Pell drew out a pocket-handkerchief, when he came to the word weep, but he made no further use of it than to wipe away a slight tinge of rum which hung upon his upper lip.", "펠 씨는 '눈물'이라는 말에 이르자 손수건을 꺼냈으나, 그것을 윗입술에 묻은 약간의 럼주 자국을 닦는 데밖에 더 쓰지는 않았다."]], [["‘I saw it in the ADVERTISER, Mr.", "\"광고지에서 봤습니다, 웰러 씨,\""], ["Weller,’ continued Pell.", "펠 씨가 말을 이었다."], ["‘Bless my soul, not more than fifty-two!", "\"맙소사, 겨우 쉰둘이라니!"], ["Dear me--only think.’", "저런—생각 좀 해 보십시오.\""]], [["These indications of a musing spirit were addressed to the mottled-faced man, whose eyes Mr.", "이 사색에 잠긴 듯한 표현은 얼룩진 얼굴의 사내를 향한 것이었는데, 펠 씨가 우연히 그의 눈을 마주친 까닭이었다."], ["Pell had accidentally caught; on which, the mottled- faced man, whose apprehension of matters in general was of a foggy nature, moved uneasily in his seat, and opined that, indeed, so far as that went, there was no saying how things was brought about; which observation, involving one of those subtle propositions which it is difficult to encounter in argument, was controverted by nobody.", "그러자 매사에 대한 이해가 안개처럼 흐릿한 그 얼룩진 얼굴의 사내는 자리에서 거북하게 몸을 움직이더니, 정말이지 그 점에 관한 한 일이 어떻게 되는 건지는 누구도 알 수 없는 노릇이라는 의견을 내놓았다. 이 발언은 논쟁에서 맞서기 어려운 그 미묘한 명제들 가운데 하나를 담고 있던 터라, 아무도 반박하지 않았다."]], [["‘I have heard it remarked that she was a very fine woman, Mr.", "\"고인이 무척 미인이셨다는 이야기를 들었습니다, 웰러 씨,\""], ["Weller,’ said Pell, in a sympathising manner.", "펠 씨가 동정 어린 태도로 말했다."]], [["‘Yes, sir, she wos,’ replied the elder Mr.", "\"그렇소, 그랬지요,\" 웰러 영감이 대답했다."], ["Weller, not much relishing this mode of discussing the subject, and yet thinking that the attorney, from his long intimacy with the late Lord Chancellor, must know best on all matters of polite breeding.", "이런 식으로 그 화제를 논하는 것이 그리 달갑지는 않았으나, 그래도 그 변호사가 고인이 된 대법관과 오래 친분을 쌓은 사람이니 점잖은 예법에 관한 모든 일에서 가장 잘 알 거라고 여겼다."], ["‘She wos a wery fine ‘ooman, sir, ven I first know’d her.", "\"내가 처음 알았을 적엔 무척 고운 여자였소."], ["She wos a widder, sir, at that time.’", "그땐 과부였지요, 선생.\""]], [["‘Now, it’s curious,’ said Pell, looking round with a sorrowful smile; ‘Mrs.", "\"그것참 묘하군요,\" 펠 씨가 서글픈 미소를 지으며 둘러보았다."], ["Pell was a widow.’", "\"펠 부인도 과부였지요.\""]], [["‘That’s very extraordinary,’ said the mottled-faced man.", "\"그것참 신기한 일이네요,\" 얼룩진 얼굴의 사내가 말했다."]], [["‘Well, it is a curious coincidence,’ said Pell.", "\"그래요, 묘한 우연이지요,\" 펠 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Not at all,’ gruffly remarked the elder Mr.", "\"전혀 안 그렇소,\" 웰러 영감이 무뚝뚝하게 한마디 했다."], ["Weller. ‘More widders is married than single wimin.’", "\"처녀보다 과부가 더 많이 시집가니까.\""]], [["‘Very good, very good,’ said Pell, ‘you’re quite right, Mr.", "\"좋습니다, 좋습니다,\" 펠 씨가 말했다."], ["Weller. Mrs.", "\"지당하신 말씀입니다, 웰러 씨."], ["Pell was a very elegant and accomplished woman; her manners were the theme of universal admiration in our neighbourhood.", "펠 부인은 무척 우아하고 교양 있는 여자였지요. 그 사람의 몸가짐은 우리 동네에서 누구나 칭송하던 화제였습니다."], ["I was proud to see that woman dance; there was something so firm and dignified, and yet natural, in her motion.", "저는 그 사람이 춤추는 걸 보는 게 자랑스러웠지요. 그 동작에는 어딘가 그토록 단단하고 기품 있으면서도 자연스러운 데가 있었습니다."], ["Her cutting, gentlemen, was simplicity itself.", "그 사람의 스텝은 말이지요, 여러분, 단순함 그 자체였습니다."], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["well, well!", "그래요, 그래요!"], ["Excuse my asking the question, Mr.", "이런 걸 여쭤서 죄송합니다만, 새뮤얼 씨,\""], ["Samuel,’ continued the attorney in a lower voice, ‘was your mother-in-law tall?’", "변호사가 목소리를 낮추어 말을 이었다. \"댁의 새어머니는 키가 크셨습니까?\""]], [["‘Not wery,’ replied Sam.", "\"별로 안 컸소,\" 샘이 대답했다."]], [["‘Mrs. Pell was a tall figure,’ said Pell, ‘a splendid woman, with a noble shape, and a nose, gentlemen, formed to command and be majestic.", "\"펠 부인은 키가 컸지요,\""], ["She was very much attached to me--very much--highly connected, too.", "펠 씨가 말했다. \"고귀한 몸매에, 명령하고 위엄을 떨치도록 빚어진 코를 가진, 멋진 여자였습니다, 여러분."], ["Her mother’s brother, gentlemen, failed for eight hundred pounds, as a law stationer.’", "그 사람은 저를 무척 따랐지요—무척이나—집안도 훌륭했고요. 그 사람 어머니의 오라비는 말이지요, 여러분, 법률 문구상을 하다가 팔백 파운드 빚을 지고 망했답니다.\""]], [["‘Vell,’ said Mr.", "\"자,\""], ["Weller, who had grown rather restless during this discussion, ‘vith regard to bis’ness.’", "이 논의가 이어지는 동안 다소 안절부절못하던 웰러 씨가 말했다. \"이제 일 얘기로 갑시다.\""]], [["The word was music to Pell’s ears.", "그 말은 펠 씨의 귀에 음악과도 같았다."], ["He had been revolving in his mind whether any business was to be transacted, or whether he had been merely invited to partake of a glass of brandy-and-water, or a bowl of punch, or any similar professional compliment, and now the doubt was set at rest without his appearing at all eager for its solution.", "그는 정말로 처리할 일거리가 있는 건지, 아니면 그저 브랜디에 물 탄 술 한 잔이나 펀치 한 사발, 혹은 그와 비슷한 직무상의 호의를 함께하자고 초대받은 것뿐인지 머릿속으로 곱씹던 참이었는데, 이제 그 의문이, 그가 굳이 답을 갈망하는 기색을 전혀 내비치지 않고도 풀린 것이었다."], ["His eyes glistened as he laid his hat on the table, and said--", "그는 모자를 탁자에 내려놓으며, 눈을 반짝이며 말했다."]], [["‘What is the business upon which--um?", "\"무슨 일로—흠?"], ["Either of these gentlemen wish to go through the court?", "이분들 가운데 누가 법원을 거쳐야 할 일이 있으신지요? 우리에겐 체포가 필요합니다."], ["We require an arrest; a friendly arrest will do, you know; we are all friends here, I suppose?’", "형식적인 체포면 됩니다, 아시지요. 여기 다들 친구들이지 않습니까, 그렇지요?\""]], [["‘Give me the dockyment, Sammy,’ said Mr.", "\"그 문서를 이리 다오, 새미,\""], ["Weller, taking the will from his son, who appeared to enjoy the interview amazingly.", "웰러 씨가 아들에게서 유언장을 받아 들며 말했다. 아들은 이 면담을 무척이나 즐기는 듯 보였다."], ["‘Wot we rekvire, sir, is a probe o’ this here.’", "\"우리가 필요한 건 말이오, 선생, 이것의 시험이오.\""]], [["‘Probate, my dear Sir, probate,’ said Pell.", "\"검인입니다, 선생, 검인이지요,\" 펠 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Well, sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"글쎄, 선생,\" 웰러 씨가 날카롭게 대꾸했다."], ["Weller sharply, ‘probe and probe it, is wery much the same; if you don’t understand wot I mean, sir, I des-say I can find them as does.’", "\"시험이든 검인이든 거기서 거기지. 내가 무슨 말 하는지 못 알아들으시겠다면, 알아듣는 사람을 내 찾아낼 수 있을 거요.\""]], [["‘No offence, I hope, Mr.", "\"기분 상하게 할 생각은 없었습니다, 웰러 씨,\""], ["Weller,’ said Pell meekly.", "펠 씨가 온순하게 말했다."], ["‘You are the executor, I see,’ he added, casting his eyes over the paper.", "\"보아하니 당신이 유언 집행인이시군요,\" 그가 종이를 훑어보며 덧붙였다."]], [["‘I am, sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"그렇소, 선생,\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘These other gentlemen, I presume, are legatees, are they?’ inquired Pell, with a congratulatory smile.", "\"여기 다른 분들은, 제 짐작에, 유산 수령인이신가요?\" 펠 씨가 축하하는 듯한 미소를 띠며 물었다."]], [["‘Sammy is a leg-at-ease,’ replied Mr.", "\"새미가 다리 편한 수령인이오,\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다."], ["Weller; ‘these other gen’l’m’n is friends o’ mine, just come to see fair; a kind of umpires.’", "\"여기 다른 양반들은 내 친구들인데, 그저 공정하게 진행되나 보러 온 거요. 일종의 심판이라고나 할까.\""]], [["‘Oh!’ said Pell, ‘very good.", "\"오!\" 펠 씨가 말했다."], ["I have no objections, I’m sure.", "\"아주 좋습니다. 저야 물론 이의 없습니다."], ["I shall want a matter of five pound of you before I begin, ha!", "시작하기 전에 댁에게서 오 파운드쯤 받아야겠군요, 하!"], ["ha!", "하!"], ["ha!’", "하!\""]], [["It being decided by the committee that the five pound might be advanced, Mr. Weller produced that sum; after which, a long consultation about nothing particular took place, in the course whereof Mr.", "위원회에서 그 오 파운드를 선불해도 되겠다고 결정하자, 웰러 씨가 그 금액을 내놓았다."], ["Pell demonstrated to the perfect satisfaction of the gentlemen who saw fair, that unless the management of the business had been intrusted to him, it must all have gone wrong, for reasons not clearly made out, but no doubt sufficient. This important point being despatched, Mr.", "그러고 나서 별것도 아닌 일을 두고 긴 협의가 이어졌는데, 그 과정에서 펠 씨는, 이 일의 처리가 자신에게 맡겨지지 않았더라면 무슨 까닭에선지—명확히 밝혀지지는 않았으나 분명 충분한 까닭에서—죄다 어긋나 버렸을 것임을, 공정한 진행을 지켜보던 신사들이 완전히 만족할 만큼 입증해 보였다."], ["Pell refreshed himself with three chops, and liquids both malt and spirituous, at the expense of the estate; and then they all went away to Doctors’ Commons.", "이 중대한 점이 처리되자, 펠 씨는 유산의 비용으로 돼지갈비 세 점과 맥아주와 독주를 곁들여 원기를 회복했다. 그런 뒤 일행은 모두 닥터스 코먼스로 떠났다."]], [["The next day there was another visit to Doctors’ Commons, and a great to-do with an attesting hostler, who, being inebriated, declined swearing anything but profane oaths, to the great scandal of a proctor and surrogate.", "이튿날 닥터스 코먼스를 또 한 차례 방문했고, 증인으로 나선 마부 때문에 한바탕 소동이 벌어졌다. 그자는 취한 상태라 불경한 욕설 외에는 어떤 선서도 하지 않으려 들어, 대리인과 대리 판사를 크게 곤혹스럽게 만들었다."], ["Next week, there were more visits to Doctors’ Commons, and there was a visit to the Legacy Duty Office besides, and there were treaties entered into, for the disposal of the lease and business, and ratifications of the same, and inventories to be made out, and lunches to be taken, and dinners to be eaten, and so many profitable things to be done, and such a mass of papers accumulated that Mr. Solomon Pell, and the boy, and the blue bag to boot, all got so stout that scarcely anybody would have known them for the same man, boy, and bag, that had loitered about Portugal Street, a few days before.", "그다음 주에는 닥터스 코먼스를 더 여러 번 방문했고, 그 밖에 유산세 사무소에도 한 차례 들렀으며, 임차권과 영업권 처분을 위한 계약이 체결되고 그것이 비준되었으며, 재산 목록이 작성되어야 했고, 점심을 들고, 저녁을 먹고, 이득이 될 만한 갖가지 일들을 처리해야 했으며, 또 어찌나 많은 서류가 쌓였는지, 솔로몬 펠 씨와 사환과 거기에 푸른 가방까지 죄다 어찌나 살이 올랐던지, 며칠 전 포르투갈 가를 어슬렁거리던 그 사람, 그 소년, 그 가방인 줄을 누구도 거의 알아보지 못할 지경이었다."]], [["At length all these weighty matters being arranged, a day was fixed for selling out and transferring the stock, and of waiting with that view upon Wilkins Flasher, Esquire, stock-broker, of somewhere near the bank, who had been recommended by Mr.", "마침내 이 모든 막중한 일들이 정리되자, 공채를 매각하고 자산을 이전하는 날이 정해졌고, 그 일을 위해 윌킨스 플래셔라는 신사를 찾아가기로 했다."], ["Solomon Pell for the purpose.", "그는 은행 근처 어딘가에 있는 증권 중개인으로, 솔로몬 펠 씨가 그 일에 추천한 사람이었다."]], [["It was a kind of festive occasion, and the parties were attired accordingly. Mr.", "그것은 일종의 축제 같은 행사였고, 일행은 그에 걸맞게 차려입었다."], ["Weller’s tops were newly cleaned, and his dress was arranged with peculiar care; the mottled-faced gentleman wore at his button-hole a full-sized dahlia with several leaves; and the coats of his two friends were adorned with nosegays of laurel and other evergreens.", "웰러 씨의 장화는 새로 닦였고, 옷차림은 각별히 신경 써서 갖추어졌다. 얼룩진 얼굴의 신사는 단춧구멍에 잎이 여러 장 달린 큼지막한 달리아 한 송이를 꽂았고, 그의 두 친구의 외투에는 월계수와 그 밖의 상록수 꽃다발이 장식되어 있었다."], ["All three were habited in strict holiday costume; that is to say, they were wrapped up to the chins, and wore as many clothes as possible, which is, and has been, a stage-coachman’s idea of full dress ever since stage-coaches were invented.", "세 사람 모두 엄격한 명절 차림을 하고 있었으니, 다시 말해 턱까지 칭칭 싸매고 옷이란 옷은 되도록 많이 껴입었는데, 이는 역마차가 발명된 이래로 마부들이 정장이라고 여겨 온, 또 여전히 여기는 그것이었다."]], [["Mr.", "펠 씨는 정해진 시각에 늘 만나던 장소에서 기다리고 있었다."], ["Pell was waiting at the usual place of meeting at the appointed time; even he wore a pair of gloves and a clean shirt, much frayed at the collar and wristbands by frequent washings.", "그조차도 장갑을 끼고 깨끗한 셔츠를 입고 있었는데, 그 셔츠는 잦은 세탁으로 깃과 소맷동이 몹시 닳아 있었다."]], [["‘A quarter to two,’ said Pell, looking at the parlour clock.", "\"두 시 십오 분 전이군요,\" 펠 씨가 응접실 시계를 보며 말했다."], ["‘If we are with Mr. Flasher at a quarter past, we shall just hit the best time.’", "\"십오 분에 플래셔 씨 댁에 닿으면 마침 딱 좋은 때를 맞추는 셈입니다.\""]], [["‘What should you say to a drop o’ beer, gen’l’m’n?’ suggested the mottled-faced man.", "\"여러분, 맥주 한잔 어떻겠소?\" 얼룩진 얼굴의 사내가 제안했다."]], [["‘And a little bit o’ cold beef,’ said the second coachman.", "\"찬 쇠고기도 조금 곁들이고,\" 두 번째 마부가 말했다."]], [["‘Or a oyster,’ added the third, who was a hoarse gentleman, supported by very round legs.", "\"아니면 굴이라도,\" 세 번째 사내가 덧붙였다. 그는 목소리가 쉰 신사로, 몹시 둥근 다리로 몸을 받치고 있었다."]], [["‘Hear, hear!’ said Pell; ‘to congratulate Mr.", "\"옳소, 옳소!\" 펠 씨가 말했다."], ["Weller, on his coming into possession of his property, eh?", "\"웰러 씨가 재산을 손에 넣으신 걸 축하하는 뜻으로, 응? 하!"], ["Ha! ha!’", "하!\""]], [["‘I’m quite agreeable, gen’l’m’n,’ answered Mr.", "\"나야 얼마든지 찬성이오, 여러분,\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다."], ["Weller. ‘Sammy, pull the bell.’", "\"새미, 종을 울려라.\""]], [["Sammy complied; and the porter, cold beef, and oysters being promptly produced, the lunch was done ample justice to.", "새미가 그대로 따랐고, 흑맥주와 찬 쇠고기와 굴이 즉시 차려지자 점심은 충분히 대접받았다."], ["Where everybody took so active a part, it is almost invidious to make a distinction; but if one individual evinced greater powers than another, it was the coachman with the hoarse voice, who took an imperial pint of vinegar with his oysters, without betraying the least emotion.", "모두가 그토록 적극적으로 가담한 마당에 우열을 가리는 것은 거의 부당한 일이겠으나, 굳이 누가 남보다 더 큰 역량을 보였다고 한다면, 그것은 목소리가 쉰 마부였으니, 그는 굴에 식초를 한 임페리얼 파인트나 곁들이면서도 조금의 동요도 내비치지 않았다."]], [["‘Mr. Pell, Sir,’ said the elder Mr.", "\"펠 씨,\""], ["Weller, stirring a glass of brandy- and-water, of which one was placed before every gentleman when the oyster shells were removed--‘Mr.", "굴 껍데기가 치워지자 신사마다 앞에 놓인 브랜디에 물 탄 술 한 잔을 휘저으며 웰러 영감이 말했다."], ["Pell, Sir, it wos my intention to have proposed the funs on this occasion, but Samivel has vispered to me--’", "\"펠 씨, 이 자리에서 자금 얘기를 꺼내려 했소만, 새뮤얼이 나한테 귀띔하기를—\""]], [["Here Mr.", "여기서, 잔잔한 미소를 지으며 잠자코 굴을 먹고 있던 새뮤얼 웰러 씨가 아주 우렁찬 목소리로 \"옳소!\""], ["Samuel Weller, who had silently eaten his oysters with tranquil smiles, cried, ‘Hear!’ in a very loud voice.", "하고 외쳤다."]], [["‘--Has vispered to me,’ resumed his father, ‘that it vould be better to dewote the liquor to vishin’ you success and prosperity, and thankin’ you for the manner in which you’ve brought this here business through.", "\"—나한테 귀띔하기를,\" 그의 아버지가 말을 이었다."], ["Here’s your health, sir.’", "\"이 술은 차라리 당신의 성공과 번영을 비는 데, 그리고 이 일을 이렇게 잘 처리해 준 것에 감사하는 데 바치는 게 낫겠다더군. 당신의 건강을 위하여, 선생.\""]], [["‘Hold hard there,’ interposed the mottled-faced gentleman, with sudden energy; ‘your eyes on me, gen’l’m’n!’", "\"잠깐 거기 멈추시오,\" 얼룩진 얼굴의 신사가 별안간 기운차게 끼어들었다. \"여러분, 나를 보시오!\""]], [["Saying this, the mottled-faced gentleman rose, as did the other gentlemen.", "이렇게 말하며 얼룩진 얼굴의 신사가 일어서자, 다른 신사들도 일어섰다."], ["The mottled-faced gentleman reviewed the company, and slowly lifted his hand, upon which every man (including him of the mottled countenance) drew a long breath, and lifted his tumbler to his lips.", "얼룩진 얼굴의 신사는 좌중을 둘러보고는 천천히 손을 들어 올렸고, 그러자 모든 사람이(얼룩진 얼굴의 그자까지 포함하여) 길게 숨을 들이쉬며 잔을 입술로 가져갔다."], ["In one instant, the mottled-faced gentleman depressed his hand again, and every glass was set down empty.", "순식간에 얼룩진 얼굴의 신사가 손을 다시 내리자, 잔이란 잔은 모두 빈 채로 내려놓였다."], ["It is impossible to describe the thrilling effect produced by this striking ceremony.", "이 인상적인 의식이 자아낸 짜릿한 효과는 이루 말로 다 할 수 없다."], ["At once dignified, solemn, and impressive, it combined every element of grandeur.", "동시에 위엄 있고, 엄숙하고, 감명 깊은 그것은 장엄함의 온갖 요소를 두루 갖추고 있었다."]], [["‘Well, gentlemen,’ said Mr.", "\"자, 여러분,\" 펠 씨가 말했다."], ["Pell, ‘all I can say is, that such marks of confidence must be very gratifying to a professional man.", "\"제가 드릴 수 있는 말씀은, 이런 신임의 표시는 법조인에게 무척이나 흐뭇한 일이라는 것뿐입니다."], ["I don’t wish to say anything that might appear egotistical, gentlemen, but I’m very glad, for your own sakes, that you came to me; that’s all.", "자만하는 것처럼 들릴 소리는 하고 싶지 않습니다, 여러분, 하지만 여러분 자신을 위해서라도 저를 찾아오신 게 정말 다행입니다. 그게 다입니다."], ["If you had gone to any low member of the profession, it’s my firm conviction, and I assure you of it as a fact, that you would have found yourselves in Queer Street before this.", "만일 이 직종의 하찮은 자에게 가셨더라면, 이건 제 굳은 확신이고 또 사실로서 장담드립니다만, 여러분은 지금쯤 곤경에 빠지셨을 겁니다."], ["I could have wished my noble friend had been alive to have seen my management of this case.", "저는 제 고귀한 친구분이 살아 계셔서 제가 이 사건을 처리하는 솜씨를 보셨더라면 좋았을 거라고 생각합니다."], ["I don’t say it out of pride, but I think--However, gentlemen, I won’t trouble you with that.", "자랑삼아 하는 말은 아닙니다만, 제 생각엔—하지만 여러분, 그 얘기로 폐를 끼치지는 않겠습니다."], ["I’m generally to be found here, gentlemen, but if I’m not here, or over the way, that’s my address.", "저는 대개 여기서 만나실 수 있습니다, 여러분, 그래도 여기나 길 건너에 없거든, 그게 제 주소입니다."], ["You’ll find my terms very cheap and reasonable, and no man attends more to his clients than I do, and I hope I know a little of my profession besides.", "제 보수는 무척 싸고 합당하다는 걸 아시게 될 겁니다. 또 저만큼 의뢰인에게 마음을 쓰는 사람도 없고요."], ["If you have any opportunity of recommending me to any of your friends, gentlemen, I shall be very much obliged to you, and so will they too, when they come to know me.", "그 밖에도 제 직무에 대해서는 제법 알고 있다고 자부합니다. 혹 여러분의 벗들 가운데 누구에게라도 저를 추천해 주실 기회가 있다면, 여러분, 저는 무척 감사하겠고, 그분들도 저를 알게 되면 마찬가지로 고마워할 겁니다."], ["Your healths, gentlemen.’", "여러분의 건강을 위하여.\""]], [["With this expression of his feelings, Mr. Solomon Pell laid three small written cards before Mr. Weller’s friends, and, looking at the clock again, feared it was time to be walking. Upon this hint Mr. Weller settled the bill, and, issuing forth, the executor, legatee, attorney, and umpires, directed their steps towards the city.", "이렇게 자기 심정을 토로한 뒤, 솔로몬 펠 씨는 글씨가 적힌 작은 명함 석 장을 웰러 씨의 친구들 앞에 내려놓고는, 다시 시계를 보더니 그만 걸음을 옮길 때가 된 듯하다고 했다. 이 암시에 따라 웰러 씨가 셈을 치렀고, 밖으로 나선 유언 집행인과 유산 수령인과 변호사와 심판들은 시내 쪽으로 발걸음을 향했다."]], [["The office of Wilkins Flasher, Esquire, of the Stock Exchange, was in a first floor up a court behind the Bank of England; the house of Wilkins Flasher, Esquire, was at Brixton, Surrey; the horse and stanhope of Wilkins Flasher, Esquire, were at an adjacent livery stable; the groom of Wilkins Flasher, Esquire, was on his way to the West End to deliver some game; the clerk of Wilkins Flasher, Esquire, had gone to his dinner; and so Wilkins Flasher, Esquire, himself, cried, ‘Come in,’ when Mr. Pell and his companions knocked at the counting-house door.", "증권 거래소의 윌킨스 플래셔 신사의 사무소는 잉글랜드 은행 뒤편 골목 안 어느 건물 이 층에 있었다. 윌킨스 플래셔 신사의 집은 서리 주 브릭스턴에 있었다. 윌킨스 플래셔 신사의 말과 이륜마차는 가까운 대여 마구간에 있었다. 윌킨스 플래셔 신사의 마부는 사냥감 고기를 배달하러 웨스트 엔드로 가는 길이었다. 윌킨스 플래셔 신사의 사무원은 점심을 먹으러 갔다. 그래서 펠 씨와 그 일행이 사무소 문을 두드렸을 때, 윌킨스 플래셔 신사 본인이 \"들어오시오\"라고 외쳤다."]], [["‘Good-morning, Sir,’ said Pell, bowing obsequiously.", "\"안녕하십니까, 선생,\" 펠 씨가 굽실거리며 인사했다."], ["‘We want to make a little transfer, if you please.’", "\"괜찮으시다면 자산 이전을 좀 하고 싶습니다.\""]], [["‘Oh, just come in, will you?’ said Mr.", "\"오, 어서 들어오시지요?\""], ["Flasher.", "플래셔 씨가 말했다."], ["‘Sit down a minute; I’ll attend to you directly.’", "\"잠깐 앉으세요. 곧 봐 드리겠습니다.\""]], [["‘Thank you, Sir,’ said Pell, ‘there’s no hurry.", "\"고맙습니다, 선생,\" 펠 씨가 말했다."], ["Take a chair, Mr.", "\"급할 것 없습니다."], ["Weller.’", "의자에 앉으시지요, 웰러 씨.\""]], [["Mr. Weller took a chair, and Sam took a box, and the umpires took what they could get, and looked at the almanac and one or two papers which were wafered against the wall, with as much open-eyed reverence as if they had been the finest efforts of the old masters.", "웰러 씨는 의자에 앉았고, 샘은 상자에 걸터앉았으며, 심판들은 앉을 만한 데를 골라 앉아서는, 벽에 풀로 붙여 놓은 달력과 종이 한두 장을 마치 옛 거장들의 더없이 훌륭한 역작이라도 되는 양 눈을 휘둥그레 뜨고 경건하게 바라보았다."]], [["‘Well, I’ll bet you half a dozen of claret on it; come!’ said Wilkins Flasher, Esquire, resuming the conversation to which Mr.", "\"좋아, 클라레 여섯 병 걸겠어. 어때!\""], ["Pell’s entrance had caused a momentary interruption.", "윌킨스 플래셔 신사가 말했다. 펠 씨의 등장으로 잠시 끊겼던 대화를 다시 이어 가는 참이었다."]], [["This was addressed to a very smart young gentleman who wore his hat on his right whisker, and was lounging over the desk, killing flies with a ruler.", "이 말은 무척 말쑥한 젊은 신사에게 건넨 것이었는데, 그자는 모자를 오른쪽 구레나룻에 얹은 채 책상에 늘어져서 자로 파리를 때려잡고 있었다."], ["Wilkins Flasher, Esquire, was balancing himself on two legs of an office stool, spearing a wafer-box with a penknife, which he dropped every now and then with great dexterity into the very centre of a small red wafer that was stuck outside.", "윌킨스 플래셔 신사는 사무용 걸상의 두 다리로 몸을 가누며, 작은 칼로 풀 상자를 콕콕 찌르고 있었는데, 이따금 그 칼을 떨어뜨려 바깥에 붙은 작은 빨간 봉인지의 한가운데에 정확히 꽂아 넣는 솜씨가 여간 능숙하지 않았다."], ["Both gentlemen had very open waistcoats and very rolling collars, and very small boots, and very big rings, and very little watches, and very large guard-chains, and symmetrical inexpressibles, and scented pocket-handkerchiefs.", "두 신사 모두 조끼를 무척 헤쳐 입었고, 깃을 잔뜩 늘어뜨렸으며, 무척 작은 부츠에, 무척 큼지막한 반지에, 무척 자그마한 시계에, 무척 큼직한 시곗줄에, 균형 잡힌 바지에, 향수 뿌린 손수건까지 갖추고 있었다."]], [["‘I never bet half a dozen!’ said the other gentleman.", "\"난 여섯 병으로는 안 걸어!\" 다른 신사가 말했다."], ["‘I’ll take a dozen.’", "\"열두 병으로 받지.\""]], [["‘Done, Simmery, done!’ said Wilkins Flasher, Esquire.", "\"좋아, 시머리, 좋아!\" 윌킨스 플래셔 신사가 말했다."]], [["‘P.", "\"잊지 마, 무를 수 없는 거야,\""], ["P., mind,’ observed the other.", "상대가 일렀다."]], [["‘Of course,’ replied Wilkins Flasher, Esquire.", "\"물론이지,\" 윌킨스 플래셔 신사가 대답했다."], ["Wilkins Flasher, Esquire, entered it in a little book, with a gold pencil-case, and the other gentleman entered it also, in another little book with another gold pencil-case.", "윌킨스 플래셔 신사는 금 연필갑을 꺼내 작은 수첩에 그것을 적었고, 다른 신사도 또 다른 금 연필갑을 꺼내 또 다른 작은 수첩에 그것을 적었다."]], [["‘I see there’s a notice up this morning about Boffer,’ observed Mr.", "\"오늘 아침에 보퍼 건으로 공고가 났던데,\" 시머리 씨가 말했다."], ["Simmery. ‘Poor devil, he’s expelled the house!’", "\"불쌍한 녀석, 거래소에서 제명됐어!\""]], [["‘I’ll bet you ten guineas to five, he cuts his throat,’ said Wilkins Flasher, Esquire.", "\"열 기니 대 다섯 기니로 걸지. 그자가 제 목을 그을 거라고,\" 윌킨스 플래셔 신사가 말했다."]], [["‘Done,’ replied Mr.", "\"좋아,\""], ["Simmery.", "시머리 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘Stop!", "\"잠깐!"], ["I bar,’ said Wilkins Flasher, Esquire, thoughtfully.", "그건 안 돼,\" 윌킨스 플래셔 신사가 곰곰이 말했다."], ["‘Perhaps he may hang himself.’", "\"어쩌면 목을 맬지도 모르잖아.\""]], [["‘Very good,’ rejoined Mr.", "\"아주 좋아,\""], ["Simmery, pulling out the gold pencil-case again.", "시머리 씨가 다시 금 연필갑을 꺼내며 대꾸했다."], ["‘I’ve no objection to take you that way.", "\"그쪽으로 받아도 난 이의 없어."], ["Say, makes away with himself.’", "그럼 '스스로 목숨을 끊는다'로 하지.\""]], [["‘Kills himself, in fact,’ said Wilkins Flasher, Esquire.", "\"요컨대 자살한다는 거지,\" 윌킨스 플래셔 신사가 말했다."]], [["‘Just so,’ replied Mr.", "\"바로 그거야,\""], ["Simmery, putting it down.", "시머리 씨가 그것을 적으며 대답했다."], ["‘“Flasher--ten guineas to five, Boffer kills himself.”", "\"'플래셔—열 기니 대 다섯 기니, 보퍼가 자살한다.'"], ["Within what time shall we say?’", "기한은 얼마로 할까?\""]], [["‘A fortnight?’ suggested Wilkins Flasher, Esquire.", "\"이 주일?\" 윌킨스 플래셔 신사가 제안했다."]], [["‘Con-found it, no,’ rejoined Mr.", "\"제기랄, 안 돼,\""], ["Simmery, stopping for an instant to smash a fly with the ruler.", "시머리 씨가 잠시 멈추고 자로 파리를 때려잡으며 대꾸했다."], ["‘Say a week.’", "\"일주일로 하지.\""]], [["‘Split the difference,’ said Wilkins Flasher, Esquire.", "\"중간으로 하자,\" 윌킨스 플래셔 신사가 말했다."], ["‘Make it ten days.’", "\"열흘로 해.\""]], [["‘Well; ten days,’ rejoined Mr.", "\"좋아, 열흘이야,\""], ["Simmery.", "시머리 씨가 대꾸했다."]], [["So it was entered down on the little books that Boffer was to kill himself within ten days, or Wilkins Flasher, Esquire, was to hand over to Frank Simmery, Esquire, the sum of ten guineas; and that if Boffer did kill himself within that time, Frank Simmery, Esquire, would pay to Wilkins Flasher, Esquire, five guineas, instead.", "그리하여 작은 수첩들에 이렇게 적혔다. 보퍼가 열흘 안에 자살하든가, 아니면 윌킨스 플래셔 신사가 프랭크 시머리 신사에게 열 기니를 건네야 한다고. 그리고 보퍼가 그 기한 안에 정말로 자살하면, 그 대신 프랭크 시머리 신사가 윌킨스 플래셔 신사에게 다섯 기니를 치러야 한다고."]], [["‘I’m very sorry he has failed,’ said Wilkins Flasher, Esquire.", "\"그자가 망한 건 참 안됐어,\" 윌킨스 플래셔 신사가 말했다."], ["‘Capital dinners he gave.’", "\"기막힌 만찬을 베풀었었는데.\""]], [["‘Fine port he had too,’ remarked Mr. Simmery.", "\"포트와인도 훌륭한 게 있었지,\""], ["‘We are going to send our butler to the sale to-morrow, to pick up some of that sixty-four.’", "시머리 씨가 한마디 했다. \"우리 집은 내일 그 집사 사람을 경매에 보내서 그 육사년산을 좀 사 올 참이야.\""]], [["‘The devil you are!’ said Wilkins Flasher, Esquire.", "\"설마 그래?\" 윌킨스 플래셔 신사가 말했다."], ["‘My man’s going too.", "\"우리 사람도 갈 거야."], ["Five guineas my man outbids your man.’", "우리 사람이 자네 사람보다 높이 부르는 데 다섯 기니 걸지.\""]], [["‘Done.’", "\"좋아.\""]], [["Another entry was made in the little books, with the gold pencil-cases; and Mr.", "작은 수첩들에 금 연필갑으로 또 한 줄이 적혔다."], ["Simmery, having by this time killed all the flies and taken all the bets, strolled away to the Stock Exchange to see what was going forward.", "그러는 사이 파리를 죄다 잡고 내기도 죄다 건 시머리 씨는, 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있나 보러 증권 거래소로 어슬렁어슬렁 떠났다."]], [["Wilkins Flasher, Esquire, now condescended to receive Mr. Solomon Pell’s instructions, and having filled up some printed forms, requested the party to follow him to the bank, which they did: Mr.", "이제 윌킨스 플래셔 신사는 솔로몬 펠 씨의 지시를 받는 데로 너그러이 마음을 돌려, 인쇄된 양식 몇 장을 작성하고는 일행더러 자기를 따라 은행으로 가자고 청했고, 일행은 그대로 따랐다."], ["Weller and his three friends staring at all they beheld in unbounded astonishment, and Sam encountering everything with a coolness which nothing could disturb.", "웰러 씨와 그의 세 친구는 눈에 보이는 모든 것을 끝없는 경탄에 차서 멀거니 바라보았고, 샘은 무엇에도 흔들리지 않는 침착함으로 매사에 맞섰다."]], [["Crossing a courtyard which was all noise and bustle, and passing a couple of porters who seemed dressed to match the red fire engine which was wheeled away into a corner, they passed into an office where their business was to be transacted, and where Pell and Mr.", "온통 시끄럽고 부산한 안뜰을 가로지르고, 한쪽 구석으로 밀어 둔 빨간 소방차에 맞춰 차려입은 듯한 짐꾼 두 사람을 지나, 그들은 업무를 처리할 사무실로 들어섰다."], ["Flasher left them standing for a few moments, while they went upstairs into the Will Office.", "거기서 펠 씨와 플래셔 씨는 그들을 잠시 세워 둔 채, 위층 유언 사무소로 올라갔다."]], [["‘Wot place is this here?’ whispered the mottled-faced gentleman to the elder Mr.", "\"여긴 무슨 곳이오?\""], ["Weller.", "얼룩진 얼굴의 신사가 웰러 영감에게 속삭였다."]], [["‘Counsel’s Office,’ replied the executor in a whisper.", "\"공채 사무소요,\" 유언 집행인이 속삭여 대답했다."]], [["‘Wot are them gen’l’men a-settin’ behind the counters?’ asked the hoarse coachman.", "\"저 계산대 뒤에 앉아 있는 양반들은 뭐요?\" 목소리 쉰 마부가 물었다."]], [["‘Reduced counsels, I s’pose,’ replied Mr.", "\"감액 공채들인가 보오,\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다."], ["Weller. ‘Ain’t they the reduced counsels, Samivel?’", "\"저게 그 감액 공채들 아니냐, 새뮤얼?\""]], [["‘Wy, you don’t suppose the reduced counsels is alive, do you?’ inquired Sam, with some disdain.", "\"아니, 설마 감액 공채가 살아 있는 거라고 생각하는 건 아니죠?\" 샘이 다소 경멸하듯 물었다."]], [["‘How should I know?’ retorted Mr.", "\"내가 어찌 알겠냐?\""], ["Weller; ‘I thought they looked wery like it.", "웰러 씨가 받아쳤다. \"꼭 그래 보이길래 그랬지."], ["Wot are they, then?’", "그럼 저게 뭐냐?\""]], [["‘Clerks,’ replied Sam.", "\"사무원들이에요,\" 샘이 대답했다."]], [["‘Wot are they all a-eatin’ ham sangwidges for?’ inquired his father.", "\"근데 다들 왜 햄 샌드위치를 먹고 있는 거냐?\" 그의 아버지가 물었다."]], [["‘’Cos it’s in their dooty, I suppose,’ replied Sam, ‘it’s a part o’ the system; they’re alvays a-doin’ it here, all day long!’", "\"그게 직무라서겠죠,\" 샘이 대답했다. \"제도의 일부예요. 여기선 늘 그러고 있다니까요, 온종일!\""]], [["Mr.", "웰러 씨와 그의 친구들이 이 나라의 화폐 제도와 연관된 이 기묘한 규정을 미처 곱씹어 볼 겨를도 없이, 펠 씨와 윌킨스 플래셔 신사가 다시 합류하여 그들을 계산대의 한쪽으로 데려갔는데, 그 위쪽에는 큼지막한 'W'"], ["Weller and his friends had scarcely had a moment to reflect upon this singular regulation as connected with the monetary system of the country, when they were rejoined by Pell and Wilkins Flasher, Esquire, who led them to a part of the counter above which was a round blackboard with a large ‘W.’ on it.", "자가 적힌 둥근 흑판이 걸려 있었다."]], [["‘Wot’s that for, Sir?’ inquired Mr.", "\"그건 뭐 하는 거요, 선생?\""], ["Weller, directing Pell’s attention to the target in question.", "웰러 씨가 펠 씨의 주의를 그 표적으로 돌리며 물었다."]], [["‘The first letter of the name of the deceased,’ replied Pell.", "\"고인의 이름 첫 글자입니다,\" 펠 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘I say,’ said Mr.", "\"이보시오,\""], ["Weller, turning round to the umpires, there’s somethin’ wrong here.", "웰러 씨가 심판들을 돌아보며 말했다. \"여기 뭔가 잘못됐소."], ["We’s our letter--this won’t do.’", "우린 우리 글자라야지—이건 안 돼.\""]], [["The referees at once gave it as their decided opinion that the business could not be legally proceeded with, under the letter W., and in all probability it would have stood over for one day at least, had it not been for the prompt, though, at first sight, undutiful behaviour of Sam, who, seizing his father by the skirt of the coat, dragged him to the counter, and pinned him there, until he had affixed his signature to a couple of instruments; which, from Mr.", "심판들은 곧장, W 자 아래에서는 이 일을 합법적으로 진행할 수 없다는 것이 자신들의 단호한 의견이라고 내놓았고, 그러니 십중팔구 그 일은 적어도 하루는 미루어졌을 것이다. 샘의 신속한—언뜻 보기엔 불효막심한—행동만 아니었더라면 말이다."], ["Weller’s habit of printing, was a work of so much labour and time, that the officiating clerk peeled and ate three Ribstone pippins while it was performing.", "샘은 아버지의 외투 자락을 움켜쥐고 계산대로 끌고 가, 두 가지 서류에 서명을 마칠 때까지 거기에 붙박아 두었다. 웰러 씨가 글자를 인쇄하듯 한 자 한 자 또박또박 쓰는 버릇이 있는 탓에 그 일이 어찌나 품과 시간이 드는 작업이었던지, 그것이 행해지는 동안 담당 사무원이 립스턴 피핀 사과 세 개를 까먹을 정도였다."]], [["As the elder Mr. Weller insisted on selling out his portion forthwith, they proceeded from the bank to the gate of the Stock Exchange, to which Wilkins Flasher, Esquire, after a short absence, returned with a cheque on Smith, Payne, & Smith, for five hundred and thirty pounds; that being the money to which Mr.", "웰러 영감이 자기 몫을 즉시 매각하겠다고 고집을 부린 터라, 그들은 은행에서 증권 거래소 정문으로 옮겨 갔고, 윌킨스 플래셔 신사가 잠시 자리를 비웠다가 스미스, 페인 앤드 스미스 앞으로 발행된 오백삼십 파운드짜리 수표를 들고 돌아왔다."], ["Weller, at the market price of the day, was entitled, in consideration of the balance of the second Mrs. Weller’s funded savings.", "그것은 둘째 웰러 부인의 공채 저축 잔액에 대한 대가로, 그날의 시장 가격에 따라 웰러 씨가 받을 권리가 있는 돈이었다."], ["Sam’s two hundred pounds stood transferred to his name, and Wilkins Flasher, Esquire, having been paid his commission, dropped the money carelessly into his coat pocket, and lounged back to his office.", "샘의 이백 파운드는 그의 명의로 이전되었고, 윌킨스 플래셔 신사는 수수료를 지급받자 그 돈을 무심히 외투 주머니에 떨어뜨려 넣고는 어슬렁어슬렁 자기 사무소로 돌아갔다."]], [["Mr. Weller was at first obstinately determined on cashing the cheque in nothing but sovereigns; but it being represented by the umpires that by so doing he must incur the expense of a small sack to carry them home in, he consented to receive the amount in five-pound notes.", "웰러 씨는 처음에는 그 수표를 소버린 금화로만 바꾸겠다고 완강하게 고집했으나, 그렇게 하면 그것을 집까지 나르려고 작은 자루를 사야 하는 비용이 든다는 점을 심판들이 일러 주자, 오 파운드짜리 지폐로 그 금액을 받는 데 동의했다."]], [["‘My son,’ said Mr.", "\"내 아들,\" 그들이 은행 건물에서 나오며 웰러 씨가 말했다."], ["Weller, as they came out of the banking-house--‘my son and me has a wery partickler engagement this arternoon, and I should like to have this here bis’ness settled out of hand, so let’s jest go straight avay someveres, vere ve can hordit the accounts.’", "\"내 아들과 내가 오늘 오후에 아주 각별한 약속이 있는데, 이 일은 후딱 마무리 짓고 싶구려. 그러니 어디 곧장 가서 계산을 따져 볼 만한 데로 갑시다.\""]], [["A quiet room was soon found, and the accounts were produced and audited.", "곧 조용한 방을 찾아내, 장부를 꺼내 검토했다."], ["Mr. Pell’s bill was taxed by Sam, and some charges were disallowed by the umpires; but, notwithstanding Mr.", "펠 씨의 청구서는 샘이 따져 깎았고, 몇몇 비용은 심판들이 인정하지 않았다."], ["Pell’s declaration, accompanied with many solemn asseverations that they were really too hard upon him, it was by very many degrees the best professional job he had ever had, and one on which he boarded, lodged, and washed, for six months afterwards.", "그러나 펠 씨가 정말이지 자기에게 너무 가혹하다고 여러 번 엄숙히 단언하며 항변했음에도, 그것은 그가 여태껏 맡은 일 가운데 단연 가장 짭짤한 일거리였고, 그 덕에 그는 이후로 여섯 달이나 먹고, 자고, 빨래까지 해결했다."]], [["The umpires having partaken of a dram, shook hands and departed, as they had to drive out of town that night. Mr.", "심판들은 독주를 한 잔씩 들이켜고는 악수를 나눈 뒤 떠났으니, 그날 밤 안으로 시 외곽으로 마차를 몰아 나가야 했기 때문이다."], ["Solomon Pell, finding that nothing more was going forward, either in the eating or drinking way, took a friendly leave, and Sam and his father were left alone.", "솔로몬 펠 씨는 더는 먹거나 마실 일이 벌어지지 않으리란 걸 알아채고는 다정하게 작별을 고했고, 샘과 그의 아버지만 남게 되었다."]], [["‘There!’ said Mr.", "\"자!\""], ["Weller, thrusting his pocket-book in his side pocket.", "웰러 씨가 수첩을 옆주머니에 쑤셔 넣으며 말했다."], ["‘Vith the bills for the lease, and that, there’s eleven hundred and eighty pound here.", "\"임차권 대금이랑 뭐랑 합쳐서 여기 천백팔십 파운드가 있다."], ["Now, Samivel, my boy, turn the horses’ heads to the George and Wulter!’", "자, 새뮤얼, 얘야, 말 머리를 조지 앤드 벌처로 돌려라!\""]], [["CHAPTER LVI. AN IMPORTANT CONFERENCE TAKES PLACE BETWEEN MR.", "픽윅 씨는 홀로 앉아 여러 가지 일을 곰곰이 생각하고 있었는데, 다른 여러 고려사항 가운데서도, 지금 자리잡지 못한 처지가 그에게 끊임없는 안타까움과 근심의 씨앗이 되고 있는 그 젊은 부부를 어떻게 하면 가장 잘 챙겨 줄 수 있을까 궁리하던 참이었다."], ["PICKWICK AND SAMUEL WELLER, AT WHICH HIS PARENT ASSISTS--AN OLD GENTLEMAN IN A SNUFF-COLOURED SUIT ARRIVES UNEXPECTEDLY\nMr. Pickwick was sitting alone, musing over many things, and thinking among other considerations how he could best provide for the young couple whose present unsettled condition was matter of constant regret and anxiety to him, when Mary stepped lightly into the room, and, advancing to the table, said, rather hastily--", "그때 메리가 가벼운 걸음으로 방에 들어와 탁자 쪽으로 다가오며 다소 다급하게 말했다—"]], [["‘Oh, if you please, Sir, Samuel is downstairs, and he says may his father see you?’", "\"아, 죄송하지만 나으리, 새뮤얼이 아래층에 와 있는데요, 자기 아버지가 나으리를 좀 뵈어도 되겠느냐고 묻습니다.\""]], [["‘Surely,’ replied Mr.", "\"물론이지.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘Thank you, Sir,’ said Mary, tripping towards the door again.", "\"고맙습니다, 나으리.\" 메리가 다시 종종걸음으로 문 쪽으로 향하며 말했다."]], [["‘Sam has not been here long, has he?’ inquired Mr.", "\"새뮤얼이 온 지 오래되지는 않았지?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘Oh, no, Sir,’ replied Mary eagerly.", "\"아, 아니에요, 나으리.\""], ["‘He has only just come home.", "메리가 열심히 대답했다."], ["He is not going to ask you for any more leave, Sir, he says.’", "\"방금 막 돌아왔어요. 더는 휴가를 청하지 않을 거라고 하던데요, 나으리.\""]], [["Mary might have been conscious that she had communicated this last intelligence with more warmth than seemed actually necessary, or she might have observed the good-humoured smile with which Mr.", "메리는 이 마지막 소식을 실제로 필요해 보이는 것보다 더 따뜻하게 전했다는 것을 스스로 의식하고 있었는지도 모른다. 아니면 말을 마쳤을 때 픽윅 씨가 자기를 바라보던 그 다정한 미소를 알아챘는지도 모른다."], ["Pickwick regarded her, when she had finished speaking. She certainly held down her head, and examined the corner of a very smart little apron, with more closeness than there appeared any absolute occasion for.", "어쨌든 그녀는 분명히 고개를 숙이고서, 굳이 그럴 까닭이 있어 보이지도 않는데도 아주 깔끔한 작은 앞치마의 한 귀퉁이를 필요 이상으로 꼼꼼히 들여다보았다."]], [["‘Tell them they can come up at once, by all means,’ said Mr.", "\"어서 올라오라고, 꼭 그렇게 전해 주게.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["Mary, apparently much relieved, hurried away with her message.", "메리는 짐짓 크게 안도한 듯, 그 전갈을 들고 서둘러 나갔다."]], [["Mr.", "픽윅 씨는 방 안을 두세 번 오르내렸다."], ["Pickwick took two or three turns up and down the room; and, rubbing his chin with his left hand as he did so, appeared lost in thought.", "그러면서 왼손으로 턱을 문지르며 생각에 잠긴 듯했다."]], [["‘Well, well,’ said Mr.", "\"음, 음.\""], ["Pickwick, at length in a kind but somewhat melancholy tone, ‘it is the best way in which I could reward him for his attachment and fidelity; let it be so, in Heaven’s name.", "픽윅 씨가 마침내 다정하나 다소 우울한 어조로 말했다. \"그의 충직함과 헌신에 보답할 수 있는 가장 좋은 길이지."], ["It is the fate of a lonely old man, that those about him should form new and different attachments and leave him.", "하느님의 이름으로, 그렇게 되게 하자. 곁에 있는 사람들이 새롭고 다른 인연을 맺고 자기를 떠나는 것, 그것이 외로운 늙은이의 운명이지."], ["I have no right to expect that it should be otherwise with me.", "나라고 달리되리라 기대할 권리는 없어."], ["No, no,’ added Mr.", "아니, 아니지.\""], ["Pickwick more cheerfully, ‘it would be selfish and ungrateful.", "픽윅 씨가 좀 더 쾌활하게 덧붙였다. \"그건 이기적이고 배은망덕한 일일 거야."], ["I ought to be happy to have an opportunity of providing for him so well.", "그를 이토록 잘 자리잡게 해 줄 기회를 얻었으니 기뻐해야 마땅하지. 나는 기쁘다."], ["I am. Of course I am.’", "물론 기쁘고말고.\""]], [["Mr. Pickwick had been so absorbed in these reflections, that a knock at the door was three or four times repeated before he heard it.", "픽윅 씨는 이런 생각에 너무도 깊이 빠져 있던 터라, 문 두드리는 소리가 서너 번 거듭되고서야 비로소 들었다."], ["Hastily seating himself, and calling up his accustomed pleasant looks, he gave the required permission, and Sam Weller entered, followed by his father.", "황급히 자리에 앉아 평소의 유쾌한 표정을 불러 모은 다음, 들어오라는 허락을 내렸다. 그러자 샘 웰러가 들어왔고, 그 뒤를 아버지가 따라 들어왔다."]], [["‘Glad to see you back again, Sam,’ said Mr.", "\"돌아온 걸 보니 반갑네, 샘.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["‘How do you do, Mr. Weller?’", "\"안녕하시오, 웰러 씨?\""]], [["‘Wery hearty, thank’ee, sir,’ replied the widower; ‘hope I see you well, sir.’", "\"아주 정정합니다요, 고맙습니다, 나으리.\" 홀아비가 대답했다. \"나으리도 평안하시지요?\""]], [["‘Quite, I thank you,’ replied Mr.", "\"아주 좋소, 고맙소.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘I wanted to have a little bit o’ conwersation with you, sir,’ said Mr.", "\"나으리, 좀 말씀을 나누고 싶은 게 있어서요.\" 웰러 씨가 말했다."], ["Weller, ‘if you could spare me five minits or so, sir.’", "\"오 분쯤만 시간을 내주실 수 있다면 말입니다요, 나으리.\""]], [["‘Certainly,’ replied Mr.", "\"물론이오.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다."], ["Pickwick. ‘Sam, give your father a chair.’", "\"샘, 아버지께 의자를 갖다 드리게.\""]], [["‘Thank’ee, Samivel, I’ve got a cheer here,’ said Mr.", "\"고맙다, 새뮤얼, 여기 의자 하나 챙겼다.\" 웰러 씨가 말하면서 의자 하나를 앞으로 끌어왔다."], ["Weller, bringing one forward as he spoke; ‘uncommon fine day it’s been, sir,’ added the old gentleman, laying his hat on the floor as he sat himself down.", "\"참으로 날씨가 좋은 날이었습니다, 나으리.\" 노신사가 자리에 앉으며 모자를 바닥에 내려놓고 덧붙였다."]], [["‘Remarkably so, indeed,’ replied Mr.", "\"정말이지 놀랍도록 좋았소.\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다."], ["Pickwick. ‘Very seasonable.’", "\"아주 철에 맞는 날씨였소.\""]], [["‘Seasonablest veather I ever see, sir,’ rejoined Mr.", "\"이제껏 본 중에 가장 철에 맞는 날씨였습죠, 나으리.\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 맞장구쳤다."], ["Here, the old gentleman was seized with a violent fit of coughing, which, being terminated, he nodded his head and winked and made several supplicatory and threatening gestures to his son, all of which Sam Weller steadily abstained from seeing.", "여기서 노신사는 격렬한 기침 발작에 사로잡혔는데, 그것이 끝나자 고개를 끄덕이고 눈을 찡긋하며 아들에게 애원하는 듯도 하고 으르는 듯도 한 몸짓을 여러 번 해 보였다. 그러나 샘 웰러는 그 모든 것을 한사코 못 본 척하였다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick, perceiving that there was some embarrassment on the old gentleman’s part, affected to be engaged in cutting the leaves of a book that lay beside him, and waited patiently until Mr. Weller should arrive at the object of his visit.", "픽윅 씨는 노신사 쪽에 어떤 거북함이 있다는 것을 알아채고, 곁에 놓인 책의 책장을 가르는 일에 골몰한 척하면서, 웰러 씨가 방문의 용건에 다다를 때까지 끈기 있게 기다렸다."]], [["‘I never see sich a aggrawatin’ boy as you are, Samivel,’ said Mr.", "\"너처럼 사람 속을 뒤집는 녀석은 처음 본다, 새뮤얼.\""], ["Weller, looking indignantly at his son; ‘never in all my born days.’", "웰러 씨가 아들을 분하게 노려보며 말했다. \"내 평생 처음이야.\""]], [["‘What is he doing, Mr.", "\"이 사람이 뭘 어쨌소, 웰러 씨?\""], ["Weller?’ inquired Mr. Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘He von’t begin, sir,’ rejoined Mr.", "\"이놈이 운을 안 떼지 뭡니까, 나으리.\" 웰러 씨가 받아 말했다."], ["Weller; ‘he knows I ain’t ekal to ex-pressin’ myself ven there’s anythin’ partickler to be done, and yet he’ll stand and see me a-settin’ here taking up your walable time, and makin’ a reg’lar spectacle o’ myself, rayther than help me out vith a syllable.", "\"뭔가 특별히 처리할 일이 있을 때 내가 말주변이 없다는 걸 뻔히 알면서도, 나으리의 귀중한 시간을 잡아먹으며 내가 여기 이렇게 앉아 꼴사납게 구경거리가 되도록 그냥 서서 보고만 있단 말입니다. 한마디라도 거들어 줄 생각은 안 하고요."], ["It ain’t filial conduct, Samivel,’ said Mr.", "그건 자식 된 도리가 아닙니다, 새뮤얼.\""], ["Weller, wiping his forehead; ‘wery far from it.’", "웰러 씨가 이마를 닦으며 말했다. \"도리하고는 한참 멀어.\""]], [["‘You said you’d speak,’ replied Sam; ‘how should I know you wos done up at the wery beginnin’?’", "\"아버지가 직접 말하겠다 했잖아요.\" 샘이 대답했다. \"운도 떼기 전에 이미 막힐 줄을 내가 어떻게 알아요?\""]], [["‘You might ha’ seen I warn’t able to start,’ rejoined his father; ‘I’m on the wrong side of the road, and backin’ into the palin’s, and all manner of unpleasantness, and yet you von’t put out a hand to help me.", "\"내가 출발도 못 하는 걸 봤으면 알았어야지.\" 아버지가 응수했다."], ["I’m ashamed on you, Samivel.’", "\"길 한복판도 아니고 엉뚱한 쪽에 들어서서 울타리에 들이박고 온갖 험한 꼴을 다 당하고 있는데도, 손 하나 내밀어 거들어 줄 생각을 안 해. 너 때문에 내가 부끄럽다, 새뮤얼.\""]], [["‘The fact is, Sir,’ said Sam, with a slight bow, ‘the gov’nor’s been a- drawin’ his money.’", "\"사실인즉, 나으리.\" 샘이 가볍게 고개를 숙이며 말했다. \"아버지가 자기 돈을 인출하셨거든요.\""]], [["‘Wery good, Samivel, wery good,’ said Mr.", "\"좋다, 새뮤얼, 좋아.\""], ["Weller, nodding his head with a satisfied air, ‘I didn’t mean to speak harsh to you, Sammy.", "웰러 씨가 흡족한 기색으로 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다. \"너한테 모질게 말하려던 건 아니다, 새미."], ["Wery good.", "좋아."], ["That’s the vay to begin.", "그렇게 시작하는 거다."], ["Come to the pint at once.", "곧장 본론으로 들어가야지."], ["Wery good indeed, Samivel.’", "아주 좋다, 새뮤얼.\""]], [["Mr. Weller nodded his head an extraordinary number of times, in the excess of his gratification, and waited in a listening attitude for Sam to resume his statement.", "웰러 씨는 만족이 넘쳐 유난히 여러 번 고개를 끄덕이고는, 샘이 진술을 이어 가기를 귀 기울이는 자세로 기다렸다."]], [["‘You may sit down, Sam,’ said Mr.", "\"샘, 자네는 앉아도 좋네.\""], ["Pickwick, apprehending that the interview was likely to prove rather longer than he had expected.", "이 면담이 예상보다 다소 길어질 듯하다는 것을 알아챈 픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["Sam bowed again and sat down; his father looking round, he continued--", "샘은 다시 고개를 숙이고 앉았다. 아버지가 둘레를 살피자, 그가 말을 이었다—"]], [["‘The gov’nor, sir, has drawn out five hundred and thirty pound.’", "\"아버지가요, 나으리, 오백삼십 파운드를 인출하셨어요.\""]], [["‘Reduced counsels,’ interposed Mr.", "\"감채 공채.\""], ["Weller, senior, in an undertone.", "웰러 영감이 나직한 목소리로 끼어들었다."]], [["‘It don’t much matter vether it’s reduced counsels, or wot not,’ said Sam; ‘five hundred and thirty pounds is the sum, ain’t it?’", "\"감채 공채든 뭐든 그건 별로 중요하지 않아요.\" 샘이 말했다. \"오백삼십 파운드, 그게 금액 맞죠?\""]], [["‘All right, Samivel,’ replied Mr.", "\"맞다, 새뮤얼.\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘To vich sum, he has added for the house and bisness--’", "\"거기에 집과 사업을 합쳐서—\""]], [["‘Lease, good-vill, stock, and fixters,’ interposed Mr.", "\"임차권, 영업권, 재고, 비품.\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 끼어들었다."]], [["‘As much as makes it,’ continued Sam, ‘altogether, eleven hundred and eighty pound.’", "\"다 합치면,\" 샘이 말을 이었다. \"통틀어 천백팔십 파운드가 됩니다.\""]], [["‘Indeed!’ said Mr.", "\"그래요!\""], ["Pickwick. ‘I am delighted to hear it.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["I congratulate you, Mr.", "\"그 말을 들으니 기쁘오."], ["Weller, on having done so well.’", "웰러 씨, 그토록 잘되셨다니 축하하오.\""]], [["‘Vait a minit, Sir,’ said Mr.", "\"잠깐만요, 나으리.\""], ["Weller, raising his hand in a deprecatory manner.", "웰러 씨가 만류하는 몸짓으로 손을 들며 말했다."], ["‘Get on, Samivel.’", "\"계속해라, 새뮤얼.\""]], [["‘This here money,’ said Sam, with a little hesitation, ‘he’s anxious to put someveres, vere he knows it’ll be safe, and I’m wery anxious too, for if he keeps it, he’ll go a-lendin’ it to somebody, or inwestin’ property in horses, or droppin’ his pocket-book down an airy, or makin’ a Egyptian mummy of his-self in some vay or another.’", "\"이 돈을요,\" 샘이 약간 머뭇거리며 말했다. \"아버지는 안전한 곳에다 두고 싶어 안달이세요. 저도 무척 그러길 바라고요. 아버지가 그냥 가지고 계시면, 누군가한테 빌려주든가, 말에다 재산을 처박든가, 지하 통풍구에 지갑을 떨어뜨리든가, 아니면 어떻게든 자기 자신을 이집트 미라로 만들어 버리실 게 뻔하니까요.\""]], [["‘Wery good, Samivel,’ observed Mr.", "\"아주 좋다, 새뮤얼.\""], ["Weller, in as complacent a manner as if Sam had been passing the highest eulogiums on his prudence and foresight.", "웰러 씨가, 마치 샘이 자기의 신중함과 선견지명에 최고의 찬사를 바치기라도 한 듯이 흐뭇한 태도로 한마디 했다."], ["‘Wery good.’", "\"아주 좋아.\""]], [["‘For vich reasons,’ continued Sam, plucking nervously at the brim of his hat--‘for vich reasons, he’s drawn it out to-day, and come here vith me to say, leastvays to offer, or in other vords--’", "\"그런 까닭에,\" 샘이 모자 챙을 초조하게 잡아 뜯으며 말을 이었다. \"그런 까닭에 아버지가 오늘 이걸 인출하셨고, 저와 함께 이리로 와서 말씀드리려는 거예요, 적어도 청하시려는, 그러니까 다시 말하면—\""]], [["‘To say this here,’ said the elder Mr.", "\"그러니까 이걸 말하려는 거다.\""], ["Weller impatiently, ‘that it ain’t o’ no use to me.", "웰러 영감이 참다못해 말했다. \"나한테는 이게 아무 쓸모가 없습니다."], ["I’m a-goin’ to vork a coach reg’lar, and ha’n’t got noveres to keep it in, unless I vos to pay the guard for takin’ care on it, or to put it in vun o’ the coach pockets, vich ‘ud be a temptation to the insides.", "나는 마차를 정기로 몰러 갈 참인데, 이걸 둘 데가 아무 데도 없어요. 차장한테 돈을 줘서 맡기든가, 아니면 마차 주머니에 넣어 두든가 해야 할 텐데, 그러면 안에 탄 손님들한테 유혹거리가 되지 않겠습니까."], ["If you’ll take care on it for me, sir, I shall be wery much obliged to you.", "나으리가 저를 위해 이걸 맡아 주신다면, 정말 크게 감사하겠습니다. 어쩌면,\""], ["P’raps,’ said Mr. Weller, walking up to Mr.", "웰러 씨가 픽윅 씨에게 다가가 귀에 대고 속삭였다."], ["Pickwick and whispering in his ear--‘p’raps it’ll go a little vay towards the expenses o’ that ‘ere conwiction.", "\"어쩌면 그 유죄판결 비용에 조금이나마 보탬이 될지도 모르고요."], ["All I say is, just you keep it till I ask you for it again.’ With these words, Mr.", "제 말은 그저, 제가 다시 달라고 할 때까지 맡아만 주십사 하는 겁니다.\""], ["Weller placed the pocket-book in Mr. Pickwick’s hands, caught up his hat, and ran out of the room with a celerity scarcely to be expected from so corpulent a subject.", "이 말과 함께 웰러 씨는 지갑을 픽윅 씨의 손에 쥐여 주고, 모자를 집어 들더니, 저렇게 뚱뚱한 사람한테서는 좀처럼 기대하기 어려운 날렵함으로 방을 뛰쳐나갔다."]], [["‘Stop him, Sam!’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"붙들게, 샘!\""], ["Pickwick earnestly.", "픽윅 씨가 간절히 외쳤다."], ["‘Overtake him; bring him back instantly!", "\"따라가게. 당장 데려와!"], ["Mr. Weller--here--come back!’", "웰러 씨—여기—돌아오시오!\""]], [["Sam saw that his master’s injunctions were not to be disobeyed; and, catching his father by the arm as he was descending the stairs, dragged him back by main force.", "샘은 주인의 분부를 거역할 수 없다는 것을 알았다. 그래서 계단을 내려가던 아버지의 팔을 붙들어, 완력으로 끌고 도로 올라왔다."]], [["‘My good friend,’ said Mr.", "\"여보시오, 친구.\""], ["Pickwick, taking the old man by the hand, ‘your honest confidence overpowers me.’", "픽윅 씨가 노인의 손을 잡으며 말했다. \"당신의 그 정직한 신뢰가 나를 압도하는구려.\""]], [["‘I don’t see no occasion for nothin’ o’ the kind, Sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"그런 일에는 아무 까닭도 없지 않습니까, 나으리.\""], ["Weller obstinately.", "웰러 씨가 고집스럽게 대답했다."]], [["‘I assure you, my good friend, I have more money than I can ever need; far more than a man at my age can ever live to spend,’ said Mr.", "\"정말이지, 친구, 나는 평생 다 못 쓸 만큼 돈이 많소. 내 나이의 사람이 살아 있는 동안 도저히 다 쓸 수 없을 만큼이오.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘No man knows how much he can spend, till he tries,’ observed Mr.", "\"써 보기 전엔 자기가 얼마나 쓸 수 있는지 아무도 모릅니다.\""], ["Weller.", "웰러 씨가 한마디 했다."]], [["‘Perhaps not,’ replied Mr.", "\"그럴지도 모르지.\""], ["Pickwick; ‘but as I have no intention of trying any such experiments, I am not likely to come to want.", "픽윅 씨가 대답했다. \"하지만 나는 그런 실험을 해 볼 생각이 전혀 없으니, 궁핍에 빠질 일도 없을 거요."], ["I must beg you to take this back, Mr.", "이걸 도로 가져가시오, 웰러 씨."], ["Weller.’", "부디 부탁이오.\""]], [["Wery well,’ said Mr.", "\"좋습니다요.\""], ["Weller, with a discontented look.", "웰러 씨가 못마땅한 표정으로 말했다. \"내 말 잘 들어라, 새미."], ["‘Mark my vords, Sammy, I’ll do somethin’ desperate vith this here property; somethin’ desperate!’", "이 재산으로 뭔가 큰일을 저지르고 말 테다. 큰일을!\""]], [["‘You’d better not,’ replied Sam.", "\"그러지 않는 게 좋을 텐데요.\" 샘이 대답했다."]], [["Mr. Weller reflected for a short time, and then, buttoning up his coat with great determination, said--", "웰러 씨는 잠시 생각에 잠겼다가, 단단히 결심한 듯 외투 단추를 채우며 말했다—"]], [["‘I’ll keep a pike.’", "\"통행료 징수소를 차리겠다.\""]], [["‘Wot!’ exclaimed Sam.", "\"뭐라고요!\" 샘이 외쳤다."]], [["‘A pike!’ rejoined Mr.", "\"통행료 징수소!\""], ["Weller, through his set teeth; ‘I’ll keep a pike.", "웰러 씨가 앙다문 잇새로 받아 말했다. \"통행료 징수소를 차리겠다."], ["Say good-bye to your father, Samivel.", "아버지한테 작별 인사나 해라, 새뮤얼."], ["I dewote the remainder of my days to a pike.’", "남은 평생을 통행료 징수소에 바치겠다.\""]], [["This threat was such an awful one, and Mr. Weller, besides appearing fully resolved to carry it into execution, seemed so deeply mortified by Mr. Pickwick’s refusal, that that gentleman, after a short reflection, said--", "이 협박이 너무도 끔찍한 것인 데다, 웰러 씨가 정말로 그것을 실행에 옮기리라 단단히 작정한 듯 보였고, 게다가 픽윅 씨의 거절에 몹시 마음이 상한 듯 보였기에, 픽윅 씨는 잠시 생각한 끝에 이렇게 말했다—"]], [["‘Well, well, Mr. Weller, I will keep your money.", "\"음, 음, 웰러 씨, 그럼 당신의 돈을 맡아 두겠소."], ["I can do more good with it, perhaps, than you can.’", "어쩌면 당신보다 내가 이것으로 더 좋은 일을 할 수 있을지도 모르겠고.\""]], [["‘Just the wery thing, to be sure,’ said Mr.", "\"바로 그겁니다, 암요.\" 웰러 씨가 환해지며 말했다."], ["Weller, brightening up; ‘o’ course you can, sir.’", "\"당연히 그러실 수 있고말굽쇼, 나으리.\""]], [["‘Say no more about it,’ said Mr.", "\"그 얘긴 더는 하지 맙시다.\""], ["Pickwick, locking the pocket-book in his desk; ‘I am heartily obliged to you, my good friend.", "픽윅 씨가 지갑을 책상에 넣고 잠그며 말했다. \"진심으로 고맙소, 친구."], ["Now sit down again.", "자, 이제 다시 앉으시오."], ["I want to ask your advice.’", "당신의 조언을 좀 구하고 싶소.\""]], [["The internal laughter occasioned by the triumphant success of his visit, which had convulsed not only Mr. Weller’s face, but his arms, legs, and body also, during the locking up of the pocket-book, suddenly gave place to the most dignified gravity as he heard these words.", "방문이 의기양양한 성공으로 끝난 데서 비롯된 속웃음이, 지갑을 잠그는 동안 웰러 씨의 얼굴뿐 아니라 팔과 다리와 온몸까지 들썩이게 했는데, 이 말을 듣는 순간 그것은 더없이 위엄 있는 근엄함으로 돌변하였다."]], [["‘Wait outside a few minutes, Sam, will you?’ said Mr.", "\"샘, 잠깐만 밖에 나가 있어 주겠나?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["Sam immediately withdrew.", "샘은 즉시 물러갔다."]], [["Mr. Weller looked uncommonly wise and very much amazed, when Mr. Pickwick opened the discourse by saying--", "픽윅 씨가 이렇게 말문을 열자, 웰러 씨는 유난히 영리해 보이면서도 몹시 놀란 표정을 지었다—"]], [["‘You are not an advocate for matrimony, I think, Mr. Weller?’", "\"웰러 씨, 당신은 결혼을 찬성하는 편은 아니지 싶은데, 그렇소?\""]], [["Mr. Weller shook his head.", "웰러 씨는 고개를 가로저었다."], ["He was wholly unable to speak; vague thoughts of some wicked widow having been successful in her designs on Mr.", "그는 도무지 말을 할 수가 없었다."], ["Pickwick, choked his utterance.", "어떤 사악한 과부가 픽윅 씨에게 흉계를 꾸며 성공을 거두었다는 막연한 생각이 그의 목구멍을 틀어막았다."]], [["‘Did you happen to see a young girl downstairs when you came in just now with your son?’ inquired Mr.", "\"방금 아들과 함께 들어올 때, 혹시 아래층에서 젊은 처녀를 보지 못했소?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘Yes.", "\"봤습니다."], ["I see a young gal,’ replied Mr.", "젊은 처자를 봤습죠.\""], ["Weller shortly.", "웰러 씨가 짧게 대답했다."]], [["‘What did you think of her, now?", "\"그래, 그녀를 어떻게 보았소?"], ["Candidly, Mr. Weller, what did you think of her?’", "솔직하게, 웰러 씨, 그녀를 어떻게 생각하오?\""]], [["‘I thought she wos wery plump, and vell made,’ said Mr.", "\"통통하고 몸매가 잘 빠졌다 싶었습니다.\""], ["Weller, with a critical air.", "웰러 씨가 품평하는 투로 말했다."]], [["‘So she is,’ said Mr.", "\"바로 그렇소.\""], ["Pickwick, ‘so she is.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["What did you think of her manners, from what you saw of her?’", "\"정말 그렇소. 당신이 본 바로는, 그녀의 태도는 어떻게 생각했소?\""]], [["‘Wery pleasant,’ rejoined Mr.", "\"아주 상냥하더군요.\" 웰러 씨가 대답했다."], ["Weller. ‘Wery pleasant and comformable.’", "\"아주 상냥하고 만족스러웠습죠.\""]], [["The precise meaning which Mr. Weller attached to this last-mentioned adjective, did not appear; but, as it was evident from the tone in which he used it that it was a favourable expression, Mr. Pickwick was as well satisfied as if he had been thoroughly enlightened on the subject.", "웰러 씨가 이 마지막 형용사에 정확히 어떤 뜻을 담았는지는 드러나지 않았으나, 그가 그 말을 쓴 어조로 보아 그것이 호의적인 표현임이 분명했으므로, 픽윅 씨는 마치 그 점에 관해 속속들이 깨우침을 얻은 것처럼 흡족해하였다."]], [["‘I take a great interest in her, Mr.", "\"나는 그녀에게 큰 관심을 두고 있소, 웰러 씨.\""], ["Weller,’ said Mr. Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["Mr. Weller coughed.", "웰러 씨가 헛기침을 했다."]], [["‘I mean an interest in her doing well,’ resumed Mr.", "\"내 말은, 그녀가 잘되기를 바라는 관심이오.\""], ["Pickwick; ‘a desire that she may be comfortable and prosperous.", "픽윅 씨가 말을 이었다. \"그녀가 편안하고 넉넉하게 살았으면 하는 바람이지."], ["You understand?’", "알겠소?\""]], [["‘Wery clearly,’ replied Mr.", "\"아주 분명히 알겠습니다.\""], ["Weller, who understood nothing yet.", "아직 아무것도 알아듣지 못한 웰러 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘That young person,’ said Mr.", "\"그 젊은 처녀가,\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick, ‘is attached to your son.’", "\"당신의 아들을 마음에 두고 있소.\""]], [["‘To Samivel Veller!’ exclaimed the parent.", "\"새뮤얼 웰러를요!\" 그 아비가 외쳤다."]], [["‘Yes,’ said Mr.", "\"그렇소.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘It’s nat’ral,’ said Mr.", "\"자연스러운 일이지요.\""], ["Weller, after some consideration, ‘nat’ral, but rayther alarmin’.", "웰러 씨가 잠시 생각하더니 말했다. \"자연스럽긴 하나, 좀 불안한 일입니다."], ["Sammy must be careful.’", "새미가 조심해야겠어요.\""]], [["‘How do you mean?’ inquired Mr.", "\"무슨 뜻이오?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘Wery careful that he don’t say nothin’ to her,’ responded Mr. Weller.", "\"그 처자한테 아무 말도 하지 않도록 아주 조심해야 한다는 거죠.\""], ["‘Wery careful that he ain’t led avay, in a innocent moment, to say anythin’ as may lead to a conwiction for breach.", "웰러 씨가 대답했다. \"순진한 순간에 끌려가서, 혼인 약속 파기 소송으로 유죄판결을 받게 할 만한 말을 입 밖에 내지 않도록 아주 조심해야 합니다."], ["You’re never safe vith ‘em, Mr.", "그것들이 일단 누구한테 흉계를 품으면, 절대 안심할 수가 없거든요, 픽윅 씨."], ["Pickwick, ven they vunce has designs on you; there’s no knowin’ vere to have ‘em; and vile you’re a-considering of it, they have you.", "어디서 어떻게 잡아야 할지 알 수가 없어요. 이쪽에서 이리저리 궁리하는 동안에, 그것들이 벌써 이쪽을 낚아채 버린단 말입니다."], ["I wos married fust, that vay myself, Sir, and Sammy wos the consekens o’ the manoover.’", "저도 처음에 바로 그런 식으로 장가를 들었습죠, 나으리. 새미가 바로 그 술수의 결과물이고요.\""]], [["‘You give me no great encouragement to conclude what I have to say,’ observed Mr.", "\"내가 하려는 말의 결론을 내리는 데 그리 큰 격려가 되지는 않는구려.\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick, ‘but I had better do so at once.", "\"그래도 차라리 단번에 말하는 게 낫겠소."], ["This young person is not only attached to your son, Mr. Weller, but your son is attached to her.’", "이 젊은 처녀가 당신 아들을 마음에 둔 것만이 아니라, 당신 아들 또한 그녀를 마음에 두고 있소, 웰러 씨.\""]], [["‘Vell,’ said Mr.", "\"허허,\""], ["Weller, ‘this here’s a pretty sort o’ thing to come to a father’s ears, this is!’", "웰러 씨가 말했다. \"이거 아비 귀에 들어오기엔 참으로 묘한 종류의 일이로군요, 이거!\""]], [["‘I have observed them on several occasions,’ said Mr.", "\"나는 여러 차례 그 둘을 눈여겨보았소.\""], ["Pickwick, making no comment on Mr.", "픽윅 씨가 웰러 씨의 마지막 말에는 아무런 토를 달지 않고 말했다."], ["Weller’s last remark; ‘and entertain no doubt at all about it.", "\"그래서 그 점에 대해 조금도 의심하지 않소."], ["Supposing I were desirous of establishing them comfortably as man and wife in some little business or situation, where they might hope to obtain a decent living, what should you think of it, Mr. Weller?’", "만약 내가 그 둘을 부부로 맺어 주고, 어떤 작은 사업이나 자리에서 편안히 자리잡게 하여, 그들이 그럭저럭 괜찮은 살림을 꾸려 갈 희망을 가지게 해 주고 싶다면, 당신은 그것을 어떻게 생각하겠소, 웰러 씨?\""]], [["At first, Mr. Weller received with wry faces a proposition involving the marriage of anybody in whom he took an interest; but, as Mr.", "처음에는 웰러 씨가, 자기가 관심을 두는 사람이 누구든 그 결혼이 걸린 제안에는 떨떠름한 표정으로 응했다."], ["Pickwick argued the point with him, and laid great stress on the fact that Mary was not a widow, he gradually became more tractable. Mr.", "그러나 픽윅 씨가 그 점을 두고 그를 설득하면서, 메리가 과부가 아니라는 사실을 크게 강조하자, 그는 차츰 다루기 쉬워졌다."], ["Pickwick had great influence over him, and he had been much struck with Mary’s appearance; having, in fact, bestowed several very unfatherly winks upon her, already.", "픽윅 씨는 그에게 큰 영향력을 가지고 있었고, 그 자신도 메리의 자태에 적잖이 마음이 끌렸던 터였다."], ["At length he said that it was not for him to oppose Mr.", "사실 그는 이미 그녀에게 도무지 아비답지 못한 눈짓을 여러 번 던진 바 있었다."], ["Pickwick’s inclination, and that he would be very happy to yield to his advice; upon which, Mr.", "마침내 그는, 픽윅 씨의 뜻을 거스를 처지가 못 되며, 그의 조언에 기꺼이 따르겠다고 말했다."], ["Pickwick joyfully took him at his word, and called Sam back into the room.", "이에 픽윅 씨는 기쁘게 그 말을 받아들이고, 샘을 다시 방으로 불러들였다."]], [["‘Sam,’ said Mr.", "\"샘,\""], ["Pickwick, clearing his throat, ‘your father and I have been having some conversation about you.’", "픽윅 씨가 헛기침을 하며 말했다. \"자네 아버지와 내가 자네에 관해 이야기를 좀 나누었네.\""]], [["‘About you, Samivel,’ said Mr.", "\"네 얘기를 말이다, 새뮤얼.\""], ["Weller, in a patronising and impressive voice.", "웰러 씨가 거들먹거리는, 짐짓 무게 잡힌 목소리로 말했다."]], [["‘I am not so blind, Sam, as not to have seen, a long time since, that you entertain something more than a friendly feeling towards Mrs.", "\"샘, 나는 자네가 윈클 부인의 하녀에게 단순한 친근감 이상의 무언가를 품고 있다는 걸 진작부터 알아차리지 못할 만큼 눈이 어둡지는 않다네.\""], ["Winkle’s maid,’ said Mr. Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘You hear this, Samivel?’ said Mr.", "\"들었지, 새뮤얼?\""], ["Weller, in the same judicial form of speech as before.", "웰러 씨가 아까와 똑같은 재판관 같은 말투로 말했다."]], [["‘I hope, Sir,’ said Sam, addressing his master, ‘I hope there’s no harm in a young man takin’ notice of a young ‘ooman as is undeniably good- looking and well-conducted.’", "\"바라건대, 나리,\" 샘이 주인을 향해 말했다. \"젊은 남자가 누가 봐도 예쁘고 행실 바른 젊은 처자에게 마음을 둔다고 해서 거기에 잘못이 있을 거라고는 생각지 않습니다.\""]], [["‘Certainly not,’ said Mr.", "\"그야 당연하지.\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Not by no means,’ acquiesced Mr.", "\"천만에, 절대 아니지.\""], ["Weller, affably but magisterially.", "웰러 씨가 상냥하면서도 위엄 있게 동의했다."]], [["‘So far from thinking there is anything wrong in conduct so natural,’ resumed Mr.", "\"그처럼 자연스러운 행동에 무언가 잘못이 있다고 생각하기는커녕,\""], ["Pickwick, ‘it is my wish to assist and promote your wishes in this respect.", "픽윅 씨가 다시 말을 이었다. \"나는 이 점에서 자네의 바람을 돕고 북돋아 주고 싶다네."], ["With this view, I have had a little conversation with your father; and finding that he is of my opinion--’", "그런 뜻에서 자네 아버지와 잠깐 이야기를 나누었고, 그분도 나와 같은 생각이라는 걸 알게 되었으니—\""]], [["‘The lady not bein’ a widder,’ interposed Mr.", "\"그 처자가 과부가 아니라는 점에서 말입니다.\""], ["Weller in explanation.", "웰러 씨가 설명을 덧붙이며 끼어들었다."]], [["‘The lady not being a widow,’ said Mr.", "\"그 처자가 과부가 아니라는 점에서.\""], ["Pickwick, smiling.", "픽윅 씨가 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["‘I wish to free you from the restraint which your present position imposes upon you, and to mark my sense of your fidelity and many excellent qualities, by enabling you to marry this girl at once, and to earn an independent livelihood for yourself and family.", "\"나는 자네가 지금 처지 때문에 받고 있는 속박에서 벗어나게 해 주고 싶고, 또 자네의 충실함과 여러 훌륭한 자질에 대한 내 마음을 표하고 싶다네. 그러기 위해 자네가 당장 이 처자와 결혼하여 자네 자신과 가족을 위한 독립된 생계를 꾸릴 수 있도록 해 주려는 것이지."], ["I shall be proud, Sam,’ said Mr.", "샘,\""], ["Pickwick, whose voice had faltered a little hitherto, but now resumed its customary tone, ‘proud and happy to make your future prospects in life my grateful and peculiar care.’", "지금까지 조금 떨리던 픽윅 씨의 목소리가 이제 다시 평소의 어조를 되찾았다. \"자네 앞날의 인생 설계를 내가 고맙고도 각별한 마음으로 보살피는 것이 나로서는 자랑스럽고도 행복한 일일세.\""]], [["There was a profound silence for a short time, and then Sam said, in a low, husky sort of voice, but firmly withal--", "잠깐 깊은 침묵이 흘렀고, 이윽고 샘이 낮고 쉰 듯하지만 단호한 목소리로 말했다."]], [["‘I’m very much obliged to you for your goodness, Sir, as is only like yourself; but it can’t be done.’", "\"베풀어 주시는 친절에 정말 감사드립니다, 나리. 나리다운 일이지요. 하지만 그건 안 될 일입니다.\""]], [["‘Can’t be done!’ ejaculated Mr.", "\"안 될 일이라니!\""], ["Pickwick in astonishment.", "픽윅 씨가 놀라서 외쳤다."]], [["‘Samivel!’ said Mr.", "\"새뮤얼!\""], ["Weller, with dignity.", "웰러 씨가 위엄을 갖추어 말했다."]], [["‘I say it can’t be done,’ repeated Sam in a louder key.", "\"안 될 일이라고 했습니다.\" 샘이 좀 더 큰 소리로 되풀이했다."], ["‘Wot’s to become of you, Sir?’", "\"나리는 어떻게 되시겠습니까?\""]], [["‘My good fellow,’ replied Mr.", "\"여보게,\" 픽윅 씨가 대답했다."], ["Pickwick, ‘the recent changes among my friends will alter my mode of life in future, entirely; besides, I am growing older, and want repose and quiet.", "\"내 친구들 사이에 최근에 일어난 변화들로 해서 앞으로 내 생활 방식은 완전히 달라질 걸세. 게다가 나도 나이를 먹어 가니 휴식과 안정이 필요하다네."], ["My rambles, Sam, are over.’", "샘, 내 떠돌이 시절은 끝났어.\""]], [["‘How do I know that ‘ere, sir?’ argued Sam. ‘You think so now! S’pose you wos to change your mind, vich is not unlikely, for you’ve the spirit o’ five-and-twenty in you still, what ‘ud become on you vithout me? It can’t be done, Sir, it can’t be done.’", "\"그걸 제가 어떻게 압니까, 나리?\" 샘이 따지듯 말했다. \"지금은 그렇게 생각하시지요! 하지만 마음이 바뀌실 수도 있지 않습니까. 그럴 법한 일이지요. 나리께는 아직도 스물다섯 살짜리 기운이 남아 있으니까요. 그럴 때 제가 곁에 없으면 어떻게 되시겠습니까? 안 될 일입니다, 나리. 안 될 일입니다.\""]], [["‘Wery good, Samivel, there’s a good deal in that,’ said Mr.", "\"옳거니, 새뮤얼, 그 말에 일리가 있다.\""], ["Weller encouragingly.", "웰러 씨가 부추기듯 말했다."]], [["‘I speak after long deliberation, Sam, and with the certainty that I shall keep my word,’ said Mr.", "\"샘, 나는 오래 숙고한 끝에 말하는 것이고, 내 말을 반드시 지키리라는 확신을 가지고 말하는 걸세.\""], ["Pickwick, shaking his head.", "픽윅 씨가 고개를 저으며 말했다."], ["‘New scenes have closed upon me; my rambles are at an end.’", "\"이제 새로운 국면들이 내 앞에 펼쳐졌고, 내 떠돌이 시절은 막을 내렸다네.\""]], [["‘Wery good,’ rejoined Sam.", "\"좋습니다.\" 샘이 대꾸했다."], ["‘Then, that’s the wery best reason wy you should alvays have somebody by you as understands you, to keep you up and make you comfortable.", "\"그렇다면 그것이야말로 나리를 이해하고, 떠받쳐 드리고, 편안하게 해 드릴 누군가를 늘 곁에 두셔야 할 가장 확실한 이유입니다. 더 세련된 친구가 필요하시면 그것도 좋습니다, 그런 사람을 두십시오."], ["If you vant a more polished sort o’ feller, vell and good, have him; but vages or no vages, notice or no notice, board or no board, lodgin’ or no lodgin’, Sam Veller, as you took from the old inn in the Borough, sticks by you, come what may; and let ev’rythin’ and ev’rybody do their wery fiercest, nothin’ shall ever perwent it!’", "하지만 봉급이 있건 없건, 통고가 있건 없건, 식사가 나오건 안 나오건, 잠자리가 있건 없건, 나리께서 자치구의 그 낡은 여관에서 데려오신 샘 웰러는 무슨 일이 닥쳐도 나리 곁을 지킬 겁니다. 모든 일과 모든 사람이 제아무리 사납게 막아선들, 그 무엇도 결코 그걸 막지 못할 겁니다!\""]], [["At the close of this declaration, which Sam made with great emotion, the elder Mr. Weller rose from his chair, and, forgetting all considerations of time, place, or propriety, waved his hat above his head, and gave three vehement cheers.", "샘이 큰 감정을 담아 이렇게 선언을 마치자, 아버지 웰러 씨가 의자에서 벌떡 일어나, 때와 장소와 예의에 대한 일체의 고려를 잊은 채 모자를 머리 위로 휘두르며 세 번 우렁차게 환호했다."]], [["‘My good fellow,’ said Mr. Pickwick, when Mr.", "\"여보게,\""], ["Weller had sat down again, rather abashed at his own enthusiasm, ‘you are bound to consider the young woman also.’", "자신의 열의에 다소 머쓱해진 웰러 씨가 다시 자리에 앉았을 때 픽윅 씨가 말했다. \"자네는 그 처자도 생각해야 하지 않겠나.\""]], [["‘I do consider the young ‘ooman, Sir,’ said Sam.", "\"그 처자도 생각하고 있습니다, 나리.\""], ["‘I have considered the young ‘ooman.", "샘이 말했다. \"그 처자를 생각해 보았지요."], ["I’ve spoke to her.", "그 애와 이야기도 나누었습니다."], ["I’ve told her how I’m sitivated; she’s ready to vait till I’m ready, and I believe she vill.", "제 처지가 어떤지 다 말해 주었고요. 그 애는 제가 준비될 때까지 기다리겠다고 합니다."], ["If she don’t, she’s not the young ‘ooman I take her for, and I give her up vith readiness.", "그렇게 할 거라고 저는 믿습니다. 만약 그러지 않는다면 그건 제가 생각하던 그 처자가 아닌 거고, 그땐 미련 없이 단념하겠습니다."], ["You’ve know’d me afore, Sir.", "나리는 예전부터 저를 잘 아시지 않습니까."], ["My mind’s made up, and nothin’ can ever alter it.’", "제 마음은 정해졌고, 그 무엇도 결코 바꾸지 못합니다.\""]], [["Who could combat this resolution?", "이 결심을 누가 꺾을 수 있겠는가?"], ["Not Mr. Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨는 아니었다."], ["He derived, at that moment, more pride and luxury of feeling from the disinterested attachment of his humble friends, than ten thousand protestations from the greatest men living could have awakened in his heart.", "그는 그 순간, 세상에서 가장 위대한 사람들이 만 번을 다짐한다 한들 그의 마음에 불러일으키지 못했을 만큼의 긍지와 더없이 흐뭇한 감정을, 이 소박한 벗들의 사심 없는 애정에서 길어 올렸다."]], [["While this conversation was passing in Mr. Pickwick’s room, a little old gentleman in a suit of snuff-coloured clothes, followed by a porter carrying a small portmanteau, presented himself below; and, after securing a bed for the night, inquired of the waiter whether one Mrs.", "이런 대화가 픽윅 씨의 방에서 오가는 동안, 코담배 빛깔의 옷을 차려입은 자그마한 노신사가 작은 여행 가방을 든 짐꾼을 뒤에 거느리고 아래층에 나타났다."], ["Winkle was staying there, to which question the waiter of course responded in the affirmative.", "그는 하룻밤 묵을 침대를 잡고 나서 웨이터에게 윈클 부인이라는 분이 여기 묵고 있느냐고 물었고, 이 물음에 웨이터는 물론 그렇다고 대답했다."]], [["‘Is she alone?’ inquired the old gentleman.", "\"혼자 있소?\" 노신사가 물었다."]], [["‘I believe she is, Sir,’ replied the waiter; ‘I can call her own maid, Sir, if you--’", "\"그런 줄로 압니다, 손님.\" 웨이터가 대답했다. \"원하시면 부인의 하녀를 불러 드릴—\""]], [["‘No, I don’t want her,’ said the old gentleman quickly.", "\"아니, 그 사람은 필요 없소.\" 노신사가 얼른 말했다."], ["‘Show me to her room without announcing me.’", "\"내가 왔다는 것을 알리지 말고 그 방으로 안내하시오.\""]], [["‘Eh, Sir?’ said the waiter.", "\"네, 손님?\" 웨이터가 말했다."]], [["‘Are you deaf?’ inquired the little old gentleman.", "\"귀가 먹었소?\" 자그마한 노신사가 물었다."]], [["‘No, sir.’", "\"아닙니다, 손님.\""]], [["‘Then listen, if you please.", "\"그럼 잘 들으시오."], ["Can you hear me now?’", "이제 내 말 들리오?\""]], [["‘Yes, Sir.’", "\"네, 손님.\""]], [["‘That’s well.", "\"좋소."], ["Show me to Mrs. Winkle’s room, without announcing me.’", "내가 왔다는 것을 알리지 말고 윈클 부인의 방으로 안내하시오.\""]], [["As the little old gentleman uttered this command, he slipped five shillings into the waiter’s hand, and looked steadily at him.", "자그마한 노신사는 이렇게 명령하면서 웨이터의 손에 슬며시 오 실링을 쥐여 주고는 그를 빤히 쳐다보았다."]], [["‘Really, sir,’ said the waiter, ‘I don’t know, sir, whether--’", "\"하지만 손님,\" 웨이터가 말했다. \"잘 모르겠습니다, 손님, 그게—\""]], [["‘Ah!", "\"아!"], ["you’ll do it, I see,’ said the little old gentleman.", "자네가 해 줄 걸세, 보아하니.\" 자그마한 노신사가 말했다."], ["‘You had better do it at once.", "\"지금 당장 하는 게 좋아."], ["It will save time.’", "시간을 아끼게 될 테니.\""]], [["There was something so very cool and collected in the gentleman’s manner, that the waiter put the five shillings in his pocket, and led him upstairs without another word.", "신사의 태도에 어찌나 침착하고 흔들림 없는 데가 있던지, 웨이터는 오 실링을 호주머니에 넣고 더는 한마디도 없이 그를 위층으로 안내했다."]], [["‘This is the room, is it?’ said the gentleman.", "\"이 방이지, 그렇지?\" 신사가 말했다."], ["‘You may go.’", "\"이제 가도 좋네.\""]], [["The waiter complied, wondering much who the gentleman could be, and what he wanted; the little old gentleman, waiting till he was out of sight, tapped at the door.", "웨이터는 이 신사가 도대체 누구이며 무엇을 원하는지 몹시 궁금해하면서 시키는 대로 했다. 자그마한 노신사는 그가 시야에서 사라질 때까지 기다렸다가 문을 두드렸다."]], [["‘Come in,’ said Arabella.", "\"들어오세요.\" 아라벨라가 말했다."]], [["‘Um, a pretty voice, at any rate,’ murmured the little old gentleman; ‘but that’s nothing.’ As he said this, he opened the door and walked in. Arabella, who was sitting at work, rose on beholding a stranger--a little confused--but by no means ungracefully so.", "\"음, 어쨌든 목소리는 곱군.\" 자그마한 노신사가 중얼거렸다. \"하지만 그건 아무것도 아니지.\" 이렇게 말하면서 그는 문을 열고 안으로 들어갔다. 바느질을 하며 앉아 있던 아라벨라는 낯선 사람을 보고 일어섰다—조금 당황한 기색이었으나 결코 볼품없이 그러지는 않았다."]], [["‘Pray don’t rise, ma’am,’ said the unknown, walking in, and closing the door after him.", "\"부디 일어나지 마시오, 부인.\" 낯선 사람이 안으로 들어와 뒤로 문을 닫으며 말했다."], ["‘Mrs. Winkle, I believe?’", "\"윈클 부인이시지요?\""]], [["Arabella inclined her head.", "아라벨라가 고개를 숙여 인사했다."]], [["‘Mrs.", "\"버밍엄의 노인의 아들과 결혼한 너새니얼 윈클 부인 맞소?\""], ["Nathaniel Winkle, who married the son of the old man at Birmingham?’ said the stranger, eyeing Arabella with visible curiosity.", "낯선 사람이 눈에 띄는 호기심으로 아라벨라를 살피며 말했다."]], [["Again Arabella inclined her head, and looked uneasily round, as if uncertain whether to call for assistance.", "아라벨라가 다시 고개를 숙여 인사하고는, 도움을 청해야 할지 어쩔지 몰라 하는 듯 불안하게 주위를 둘러보았다."]], [["‘I surprise you, I see, ma’am,’ said the old gentleman.", "\"내가 부인을 놀라게 한 모양이군.\" 노신사가 말했다."]], [["‘Rather, I confess,’ replied Arabella, wondering more and more.", "\"솔직히, 조금은요.\" 점점 더 의아해하며 아라벨라가 대답했다."]], [["‘I’ll take a chair, if you’ll allow me, ma’am,’ said the stranger.", "\"실례지만 의자에 좀 앉겠소, 부인.\" 낯선 사람이 말했다."]], [["He took one; and drawing a spectacle-case from his pocket, leisurely pulled out a pair of spectacles, which he adjusted on his nose.", "그는 의자 하나를 끌어다 앉고는, 호주머니에서 안경집을 꺼내 천천히 안경을 끄집어내 코에 걸쳤다."]], [["‘You don’t know me, ma’am?’ he said, looking so intently at Arabella that she began to feel alarmed.", "\"나를 모르겠소, 부인?\" 그가 아라벨라를 어찌나 빤히 바라보던지 그녀가 겁이 나기 시작할 정도로 말했다."]], [["‘No, sir,’ she replied timidly.", "\"네, 손님.\" 그녀가 머뭇거리며 대답했다."]], [["‘No,’ said the gentleman, nursing his left leg; ‘I don’t know how you should.", "\"그렇지.\" 노신사가 왼쪽 다리를 어루만지며 말했다."], ["You know my name, though, ma’am.’", "\"부인이 나를 알 리가 없지. 하지만 부인은 내 이름은 알 거요.\""]], [["‘Do I?’ said Arabella, trembling, though she scarcely knew why.", "\"제가요?\" 아라벨라가, 왜인지도 잘 모른 채 떨면서 말했다."], ["‘May I ask what it is?’", "\"여쭤봐도 될까요, 어떤 이름이신지?\""]], [["‘Presently, ma’am, presently,’ said the stranger, not having yet removed his eyes from her countenance.", "\"곧 알게 되오, 부인, 곧.\" 낯선 사람이 그녀의 얼굴에서 아직 눈을 떼지 않은 채 말했다."], ["‘You have been recently married, ma’am?’", "\"최근에 결혼하셨소, 부인?\""]], [["‘I have,’ replied Arabella, in a scarcely audible tone, laying aside her work, and becoming greatly agitated as a thought, that had occurred to her before, struck more forcibly upon her mind.", "\"네.\" 아라벨라가 들릴락 말락 한 목소리로 대답하며 바느질감을 내려놓았다. 앞서 한 번 떠올랐던 생각이 더 강하게 마음을 치고 들어오자 그녀는 몹시 동요했다."]], [["‘Without having represented to your husband the propriety of first consulting his father, on whom he is dependent, I think?’ said the stranger.", "\"부인의 남편이 의지하고 있는 그의 아버지와 먼저 상의하는 것이 옳다는 점을, 부인은 남편에게 깨우쳐 주지도 않은 채 말이오?\" 낯선 사람이 말했다."]], [["Arabella applied her handkerchief to her eyes.", "아라벨라가 손수건을 눈가에 갖다 댔다."]], [["‘Without an endeavour, even, to ascertain, by some indirect appeal, what were the old man’s sentiments on a point in which he would naturally feel much interested?’ said the stranger.", "\"그 노인이 당연히 큰 관심을 가질 만한 일에 관해 그가 어떤 생각을 품고 있는지, 어떤 간접적인 방법으로 슬쩍 떠보아 알아내려는 시도조차 없이 말이오?\" 낯선 사람이 말했다."]], [["‘I cannot deny it, Sir,’ said Arabella.", "\"부인할 수 없습니다, 손님.\" 아라벨라가 말했다."]], [["‘And without having sufficient property of your own to afford your husband any permanent assistance in exchange for the worldly advantages which you knew he would have gained if he had married agreeably to his father’s wishes?’ said the old gentleman.", "\"또 남편이 아버지의 뜻에 맞게 결혼했더라면 얻었을 세속적 이득과 맞바꿀 만큼, 그에게 지속적인 도움을 줄 변변한 재산도 부인 자신에게 없으면서 말이오?\" 노신사가 말했다."], ["‘This is what boys and girls call disinterested affection, till they have boys and girls of their own, and then they see it in a rougher and very different light!’", "\"이런 걸 두고 사내아이들과 계집아이들은 사심 없는 애정이라고 부르지. 그러다가 제 자식들을 두게 되면, 그때 가서는 훨씬 거칠고 사뭇 다른 빛깔로 그것을 보게 되는 법이오!\""]], [["Arabella’s tears flowed fast, as she pleaded in extenuation that she was young and inexperienced; that her attachment had alone induced her to take the step to which she had resorted; and that she had been deprived of the counsel and guidance of her parents almost from infancy.", "아라벨라는 눈물을 줄줄 흘리며 변명 삼아 호소하기를, 자신은 어리고 세상 물정에 어두웠으며, 오로지 그 애정 하나 때문에 자신이 택한 그런 행동을 하게 되었고, 거의 어릴 적부터 부모의 조언과 인도를 받지 못한 채 자랐다고 했다."]], [["‘It was wrong,’ said the old gentleman in a milder tone, ‘very wrong.", "\"잘못한 일이오.\" 노신사가 한결 누그러진 어조로 말했다."], ["It was romantic, unbusinesslike, foolish.’", "\"아주 잘못한 일이지. 낭만적이고, 분별없고, 어리석은 일이었소.\""]], [["‘It was my fault; all my fault, Sir,’ replied poor Arabella, weeping.", "\"제 잘못입니다. 모두 제 잘못이에요, 손님.\" 가엾은 아라벨라가 흐느끼며 대답했다."]], [["‘Nonsense,’ said the old gentleman; ‘it was not your fault that he fell in love with you, I suppose?", "\"무슨 소리.\" 노신사가 말했다."], ["Yes it was, though,’ said the old gentleman, looking rather slily at Arabella.", "\"그가 부인에게 반한 게 부인 잘못은 아니지 않소? 아니, 따지고 보면 그것도 그렇구먼.\""], ["‘It was your fault.", "노신사가 아라벨라를 다소 짓궂게 바라보며 말했다."], ["He couldn’t help it.’", "\"그것도 부인 잘못이지. 그 녀석으로서는 어쩔 수가 없었을 테니까.\""]], [["This little compliment, or the little gentleman’s odd way of paying it, or his altered manner--so much kinder than it was, at first--or all three together, forced a smile from Arabella in the midst of her tears.", "이 자그마한 칭찬이, 혹은 자그마한 신사가 그것을 건네는 별난 방식이, 혹은 처음과는 사뭇 다르게 한층 다정해진 그의 태도가, 아니면 이 셋이 다 합쳐져서, 아라벨라는 눈물 가운데서도 그만 미소를 짓고 말았다."]], [["‘Where’s your husband?’ inquired the old gentleman, abruptly; stopping a smile which was just coming over his own face.", "\"부인의 남편은 어디 있소?\" 노신사가, 막 제 얼굴에 번지려던 미소를 거두며 불쑥 물었다."]], [["‘I expect him every instant, sir,’ said Arabella.", "\"이제 곧 돌아올 거예요, 손님.\" 아라벨라가 말했다."], ["‘I persuaded him to take a walk this morning.", "\"제가 권해서 오늘 아침 산책을 나갔어요."], ["He is very low and wretched at not having heard from his father.’", "아버지에게서 아무 소식이 없어 그이는 몹시 기가 죽고 비참해하고 있답니다.\""]], [["‘Low, is he?’ said the old gentlemen.", "\"기가 죽었다고?\" 노신사가 말했다."], ["‘Serve him right!’", "\"고소하군!\""]], [["‘He feels it on my account, I am afraid,’ said Arabella; ‘and indeed, Sir, I feel it deeply on his.", "\"제 탓이라고 여겨 그러는 것 같아요.\" 아라벨라가 말했다."], ["I have been the sole means of bringing him to his present condition.’", "\"그리고 정말이지, 손님, 저도 그이 때문에 마음이 너무나 아픕니다. 그이를 지금 이 지경으로 몰고 온 것이 오로지 저 하나 때문이니까요.\""]], [["‘Don’t mind it on his account, my dear,’ said the old gentleman.", "\"그 녀석 때문이라면 마음 쓰지 마시오, 부인.\" 노신사가 말했다."], ["‘It serves him right.", "\"고소한 일이지."], ["I am glad of it--actually glad of it, as far as he is concerned.’", "나는 그게 기쁘오—그 녀석에 관한 한, 정말로 기쁘다오.\""]], [["The words were scarcely out of the old gentleman’s lips, when footsteps were heard ascending the stairs, which he and Arabella seemed both to recognise at the same moment.", "이 말이 노신사의 입에서 채 떨어지기도 전에 계단을 올라오는 발소리가 들렸는데, 그와 아라벨라는 같은 순간에 그 소리를 알아챈 듯했다."], ["The little gentleman turned pale; and, making a strong effort to appear composed, stood up, as Mr.", "자그마한 신사는 얼굴이 창백해졌고, 침착해 보이려고 애써 안간힘을 쓰며 자리에서 일어섰다."], ["Winkle entered the room.", "그때 윈클 씨가 방으로 들어왔다."]], [["‘Father!’ cried Mr.", "\"아버지!\""], ["Winkle, recoiling in amazement.", "윈클 씨가 놀라서 움찔 뒤로 물러서며 외쳤다."]], [["‘Yes, sir,’ replied the little old gentleman.", "\"그래, 이놈아.\" 자그마한 노신사가 대답했다."], ["‘Well, Sir, what have you got to say to me?’", "\"자, 이놈아, 나한테 할 말이 뭐냐?\""]], [["Mr. Winkle remained silent.", "윈클 씨는 잠자코 있었다."]], [["‘You are ashamed of yourself, I hope, Sir?’ said the old gentleman.", "\"부끄러운 줄 알겠지, 응?\" 노신사가 말했다."]], [["Still Mr. Winkle said nothing.", "여전히 윈클 씨는 아무 말도 하지 않았다."]], [["‘Are you ashamed of yourself, Sir, or are you not?’ inquired the old gentleman.", "\"부끄러운 줄 아느냐, 모르느냐?\" 노신사가 물었다."]], [["‘No, Sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"아닙니다, 아버지.\""], ["Winkle, drawing Arabella’s arm through his.", "윈클 씨가 아라벨라의 팔을 자기 팔에 끼며 대답했다."], ["‘I am not ashamed of myself, or of my wife either.’", "\"저는 제 자신이 부끄럽지도 않고, 제 아내가 부끄럽지도 않습니다.\""]], [["‘Upon my word!’ cried the old gentleman ironically.", "\"허, 그것참!\" 노신사가 비꼬듯 외쳤다."]], [["‘I am very sorry to have done anything which has lessened your affection for me, Sir,’ said Mr.", "\"아버지를 향한 정을 깎아내릴 일을 한 것은 정말로 죄송합니다, 아버지.\" 윈클 씨가 말했다."], ["Winkle; ‘but I will say, at the same time, that I have no reason to be ashamed of having this lady for my wife, nor you of having her for a daughter.’", "\"하지만 동시에 이 말씀은 드려야겠습니다. 저는 이 사람을 아내로 맞은 것을 부끄러워할 이유가 없으며, 아버지께서도 이 사람을 며느리로 두신 것을 부끄러워하실 이유가 없습니다.\""]], [["‘Give me your hand, Nat,’ said the old gentleman, in an altered voice.", "\"네 손을 다오, 냇.\" 노신사가 달라진 목소리로 말했다."], ["‘Kiss me, my love.", "\"내게 입맞춤을 해 다오, 얘야."], ["You are a very charming little daughter-in-law after all!’", "따지고 보니 너는 아주 사랑스러운 며느리로구나!\""]], [["In a few minutes’ time Mr. Winkle went in search of Mr. Pickwick, and returning with that gentleman, presented him to his father, whereupon they shook hands for five minutes incessantly.", "몇 분 뒤 윈클 씨가 픽윅 씨를 찾으러 나갔다가 그 신사와 함께 돌아와 자기 아버지에게 인사시켰고, 그리하여 그들은 오 분 동안이나 쉼 없이 악수를 나누었다."]], [["‘Mr. Pickwick, I thank you most heartily for all your kindness to my son,’ said old Mr.", "\"픽윅 씨, 내 아들에게 베풀어 주신 온갖 친절에 진심으로 감사드립니다.\""], ["Winkle, in a bluff, straightforward way.", "윈클 노인이 무뚝뚝하면서도 솔직한 투로 말했다."], ["‘I am a hasty fellow, and when I saw you last, I was vexed and taken by surprise.", "\"나는 성미가 급한 사람이라, 지난번에 당신을 보았을 때는 화가 난 데다 갑작스러운 일에 당황했었지요."], ["I have judged for myself now, and am more than satisfied.", "이제 내 눈으로 직접 판단했고, 더할 나위 없이 만족스럽습니다."], ["Shall I make any more apologies, Mr. Pickwick?’", "사과를 더 드려야겠습니까, 픽윅 씨?\""]], [["‘Not one,’ replied that gentleman.", "\"한마디도 필요 없습니다.\" 그 신사가 대답했다."], ["‘You have done the only thing wanting to complete my happiness.’", "\"당신은 내 행복을 완성하는 데 빠져 있던 단 한 가지 일을 해 주셨으니까요.\""]], [["Hereupon there was another shaking of hands for five minutes longer, accompanied by a great number of complimentary speeches, which, besides being complimentary, had the additional and very novel recommendation of being sincere.", "이리하여 다시 오 분 동안 더 악수가 이어졌고, 거기에 더해 수많은 칭찬의 말이 오갔는데, 이 말들은 칭찬일 뿐 아니라 진심에서 우러났다는, 매우 새삼스러운 미덕까지 갖추고 있었다."]], [["Sam had dutifully seen his father to the Belle Sauvage, when, on returning, he encountered the fat boy in the court, who had been charged with the delivery of a note from Emily Wardle.", "샘은 도리를 다해 아버지를 벨 소바주까지 바래다주었는데, 돌아오는 길에 안마당에서 그 뚱뚱한 소년과 마주쳤다. 그 아이는 에밀리 워들이 보낸 쪽지를 전하는 심부름을 맡고 있었다."]], [["‘I say,’ said Joe, who was unusually loquacious, ‘what a pretty girl Mary is, isn’t she?", "\"있잖아요,\" 평소답지 않게 수다스러워진 조가 말했다."], ["I am so fond of her, I am!’", "\"메리는 참 예쁜 처자지요, 안 그래요? 나는 그 애가 어찌나 좋은지 몰라요, 정말로요!\""]], [["Mr. Weller made no verbal remark in reply; but eyeing the fat boy for a moment, quite transfixed at his presumption, led him by the collar to the corner, and dismissed him with a harmless but ceremonious kick.", "웰러 씨는 말로 대꾸하지 않았다."], ["After which, he walked home, whistling.", "다만 잠시 그 뚱뚱한 소년을 노려보며 그 주제넘음에 그만 어이가 없어 멍해졌다가, 멱살을 잡아 구석으로 끌고 가서는 해롭지는 않으나 격식을 차린 발길질 한 방으로 그를 내쫓았다. 그러고 나서 그는 휘파람을 불며 집으로 걸어갔다."]], [["CHAPTER LVII. IN WHICH THE PICKWICK CLUB IS FINALLY DISSOLVED, AND EVERYTHING CONCLUDED TO THE SATISFACTION OF EVERYBODY\nFor a whole week after the happy arrival of Mr. Winkle from Birmingham, Mr. Pickwick and Sam Weller were from home all day long, only returning just in time for dinner, and then wearing an air of mystery and importance quite foreign to their natures. It was evident that very grave and eventful proceedings were on foot; but various surmises were afloat, respecting their precise character. Some (among whom was Mr. Tupman) were disposed to think that Mr. Pickwick contemplated a matrimonial alliance; but this idea the ladies most strenuously repudiated. Others rather inclined to the belief that he had projected some distant tour, and was at present occupied in effecting the preliminary arrangements; but this again was stoutly denied by Sam himself, who had unequivocally stated, when cross-examined by Mary, that no new journeys were to be undertaken. At length, when the brains of the whole party had been racked for six long days, by unavailing speculation, it was unanimously resolved that Mr. Pickwick should be called upon to explain his conduct, and to state distinctly why he had thus absented himself from the society of his admiring friends.", "윈클 씨가 버밍엄에서 무사히 돌아온 뒤로 꼬박 한 주 동안, 픽윅 씨와 샘 웰러는 온종일 집을 비웠다가 저녁 식사 시간에 딱 맞춰 돌아올 뿐이었고, 그럴 때면 그들의 천성과는 영 어울리지 않는 비밀스럽고 거창한 분위기를 풍겼다. 무언가 매우 중대하고 파란만장한 일이 벌어지고 있음은 분명했으나, 그 일의 정확한 성격에 관해서는 갖가지 추측이 떠돌았다. 어떤 이들(그 가운데 터프먼 씨가 있었다)은 픽윅 씨가 혼인 동맹을 꾀하고 있다고 생각하는 쪽이었으나, 이 견해는 부인들이 가장 강력히 부인했다. 다른 이들은 그가 어떤 먼 여행을 계획하고 지금 그 사전 준비를 진행하고 있다는 쪽으로 기울었으나, 이 또한 샘 자신이 강하게 부정했으니, 메리가 캐물었을 때 그는 새로운 여행 따위는 결코 떠나지 않는다고 분명히 잘라 말했던 것이다. 마침내 온 일행의 머리가 엿새라는 긴 날 동안 부질없는 억측으로 쥐어짜인 끝에, 픽윅 씨에게 그의 행동을 해명할 것을 요구하고, 어찌하여 그를 흠모하는 친구들의 모임에서 이렇게 자취를 감추었는지 분명히 밝히도록 하자는 데에 만장일치로 의견이 모아졌다."]], [["With this view, Mr. Wardle invited the full circle to dinner at the Adelphi; and the decanters having been thrice sent round, opened the business.", "이런 뜻에서 워들 씨는 일행 전원을 애덜파이로 초대해 만찬을 베풀었고, 술병이 세 차례 돌고 나자 본론을 꺼냈다."]], [["‘We are all anxious to know,’ said the old gentleman, ‘what we have done to offend you, and to induce you to desert us and devote yourself to these solitary walks.’", "\"우리는 모두 알고 싶소.\" 노신사가 말했다. \"우리가 대체 무슨 잘못을 저질러 당신을 노하게 하고, 우리를 저버린 채 이런 외로운 산책에만 몰두하게 만들었는지 말이오.\""]], [["‘Are you?’ said Mr.", "\"그렇소?\""], ["Pickwick.", "픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["‘It is singular enough that I had intended to volunteer a full explanation this very day; so, if you will give me another glass of wine, I will satisfy your curiosity.’", "\"참으로 묘하게도, 나는 바로 오늘 자청해서 전부 해명하려던 참이었소. 그러니 포도주를 한 잔 더 주신다면, 여러분의 궁금증을 풀어 드리리다.\""]], [["The decanters passed from hand to hand with unwonted briskness, and Mr. Pickwick, looking round on the faces of his friends with a cheerful smile, proceeded--", "술병이 여느 때와 달리 활기차게 이 손에서 저 손으로 건너갔고, 픽윅 씨는 친구들의 얼굴을 둘러보며 흐뭇한 미소를 띤 채 말을 이었다."]], [["‘All the changes that have taken place among us,’ said Mr.", "\"우리 사이에 일어난 모든 변화는,\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick, ‘I mean the marriage that has taken place, and the marriage that WILL take place, with the changes they involve, rendered it necessary for me to think, soberly and at once, upon my future plans.", "\"이미 이루어진 혼인과, 앞으로 이루어질 혼인을 두고 하는 말이오만, 그에 따르는 변화들과 더불어, 나로 하여금 내 앞날의 계획을 차분하게, 그리고 곧장 생각하지 않을 수 없게 만들었소."], ["I determined on retiring to some quiet, pretty neighbourhood in the vicinity of London; I saw a house which exactly suited my fancy; I have taken it and furnished it.", "나는 런던 근교의 조용하고 아담한 동네로 물러나기로 마음먹었소. 내 마음에 꼭 드는 집을 보았기에, 그것을 사들이고 가구도 들여놓았소."], ["It is fully prepared for my reception, and I intend entering upon it at once, trusting that I may yet live to spend many quiet years in peaceful retirement, cheered through life by the society of my friends, and followed in death by their affectionate remembrance.’", "이제 나를 맞이할 채비가 다 되었으니, 곧장 그리로 들어갈 작정이오. 평화로운 은거 속에서 조용한 세월을 여러 해 더 보낼 수 있기를, 살아생전엔 친구들과의 교제로 위로받고, 죽은 뒤엔 그들의 다정한 추억으로 기림을 받기를 바라면서 말이오.\""]], [["Here Mr. Pickwick paused, and a low murmur ran round the table.", "여기서 픽윅 씨가 말을 멈추자, 식탁을 따라 나직한 웅성거림이 돌았다."]], [["‘The house I have taken,’ said Mr.", "\"내가 마련한 집은,\" 픽윅 씨가 말했다."], ["Pickwick, ‘is at Dulwich.", "\"덜위치에 있소."], ["It has a large garden, and is situated in one of the most pleasant spots near London.", "널찍한 정원이 딸려 있고, 런던 근방에서 가장 쾌적한 곳 가운데 하나에 자리 잡고 있소."], ["It has been fitted up with every attention to substantial comfort; perhaps to a little elegance besides; but of that you shall judge for yourselves.", "실질적인 안락함을 빠짐없이 갖추도록 손을 보았고, 어쩌면 거기에 약간의 우아함까지 더했을지도 모르오. 허나 그것은 여러분이 직접 판단하시오."], ["Sam accompanies me there.", "샘이 거기까지 나를 따라가오."], ["I have engaged, on Perker’s representation, a housekeeper--a very old one--and such other servants as she thinks I shall require.", "퍼커의 추천을 받아 가정부를 한 사람 들였는데, 아주 나이 든 사람이오. 그리고 그녀가 내게 필요하리라고 여기는 그 밖의 하인들도 들였소."], ["I propose to consecrate this little retreat, by having a ceremony in which I take a great interest, performed there.", "나는 이 작은 안식처를, 내가 큰 관심을 두고 있는 어떤 의식을 그곳에서 치름으로써 봉헌하고자 하오."], ["I wish, if my friend Wardle entertains no objection, that his daughter should be married from my new house, on the day I take possession of it.", "내 친구 워들이 반대하지 않는다면, 그의 따님이 내가 집에 들어가는 그날 내 새집에서 혼례를 올렸으면 하오. 젊은이들의 행복이란,\""], ["The happiness of young people,’ said Mr.", "픽윅 씨가 다소 감정에 북받쳐 말했다."], ["Pickwick, a little moved, ‘has ever been the chief pleasure of my life.", "\"언제나 내 인생의 가장 큰 기쁨이었소."], ["It will warm my heart to witness the happiness of those friends who are dearest to me, beneath my own roof.’", "내게 가장 소중한 친구들의 행복을 내 지붕 아래서 지켜보는 일은 내 마음을 따뜻하게 데워 줄 것이오.\""]], [["Mr.", "픽윅 씨가 다시 말을 멈추었다."], ["Pickwick paused again: Emily and Arabella sobbed audibly.", "에밀리와 아라벨라는 소리 내어 흐느꼈다."]], [["‘I have communicated, both personally and by letter, with the club,’ resumed Mr.", "\"나는 직접 만나서도, 또 편지로도 클럽에 알렸소.\""], ["Pickwick, ‘acquainting them with my intention.", "픽윅 씨가 말을 이었다. \"내 뜻을 전했지요."], ["During our long absence, it has suffered much from internal dissentions; and the withdrawal of my name, coupled with this and other circumstances, has occasioned its dissolution.", "우리가 오래 자리를 비운 동안, 클럽은 내부의 불화로 큰 어려움을 겪었소. 거기에 내 이름이 빠지게 된 일까지 더해지고, 또 다른 사정들과 겹치면서 클럽은 해산하게 되었소."], ["The Pickwick Club exists no longer.", "픽윅 클럽은 이제 더 이상 존재하지 않소."]], [["‘I shall never regret,’ said Mr.", "\"나는 결코 후회하지 않을 것이오.\""], ["Pickwick in a low voice, ‘I shall never regret having devoted the greater part of two years to mixing with different varieties and shades of human character, frivolous as my pursuit of novelty may have appeared to many.", "픽윅 씨가 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"비록 새로운 것을 좇는 내 행각이 많은 이들에게 부질없어 보였을지언정, 2년의 대부분을 인간 성품의 갖가지 부류와 갖가지 결을 두루 겪는 데 바친 것을 나는 결코 후회하지 않을 것이오."], ["Nearly the whole of my previous life having been devoted to business and the pursuit of wealth, numerous scenes of which I had no previous conception have dawned upon me--I hope to the enlargement of my mind, and the improvement of my understanding.", "내 이전 삶의 거의 전부가 사업과 부의 추구에 바쳐졌던 터라, 일찍이 짐작조차 못 했던 수많은 장면들이 내 앞에 펼쳐졌소. 바라건대 그것이 내 마음을 넓히고 내 식견을 길러 주었기를 바라오."], ["If I have done but little good, I trust I have done less harm, and that none of my adventures will be other than a source of amusing and pleasant recollection to me in the decline of life.", "내가 한 선행이 보잘것없다 할지라도, 끼친 해악은 그보다 더 적기를, 그리고 내 모험들 가운데 어느 것도 인생의 황혼기에 내게 즐겁고 유쾌한 추억의 샘이 아닌 것이 없기를 바라오."], ["God bless you all!’", "여러분 모두에게 신의 축복이 함께하기를!\""]], [["With these words, Mr. Pickwick filled and drained a bumper with a trembling hand; and his eyes moistened as his friends rose with one accord, and pledged him from their hearts.", "이 말과 함께 픽윅 씨는 떨리는 손으로 잔을 가득 채워 단숨에 비웠고, 친구들이 한마음으로 자리에서 일어나 진심으로 그를 위해 축배를 들자 그의 두 눈이 촉촉이 젖었다."]], [["There were few preparatory arrangements to be made for the marriage of Mr. Snodgrass.", "스노드그래스 씨의 혼인을 위해 사전에 해 둘 준비는 별로 없었다."], ["As he had neither father nor mother, and had been in his minority a ward of Mr. Pickwick’s, that gentleman was perfectly well acquainted with his possessions and prospects.", "그에게는 아버지도 어머니도 없었고, 미성년 시절엔 픽윅 씨의 피후견인이었던 터라, 그 신사는 그의 재산과 앞날의 전망을 속속들이 잘 알고 있었다."], ["His account of both was quite satisfactory to Wardle--as almost any other account would have been, for the good old gentleman was overflowing with hilarity and kindness--and a handsome portion having been bestowed upon Emily, the marriage was fixed to take place on the fourth day from that time--the suddenness of which preparations reduced three dressmakers and a tailor to the extreme verge of insanity.", "두 가지에 대한 그의 설명은 워들을 흡족하게 했으니 — 사실 거의 어떤 설명이라도 그러했을 터인데, 그 선량한 노신사는 유쾌함과 친절로 넘쳐흐르고 있었기 때문이다 — 에밀리에게 두둑한 지참금이 주어지자, 혼례는 그날로부터 나흘째 되는 날에 치르기로 정해졌다. 이렇듯 갑작스러운 준비 탓에 양재사 셋과 재단사 하나가 거의 실성하기 직전까지 내몰렸다."]], [["Getting post-horses to the carriage, old Wardle started off, next day, to bring his mother back to town.", "이튿날, 워들 영감은 마차에 역마를 매어 어머니를 모시고 런던으로 돌아오려고 길을 나섰다."], ["Communicating his intelligence to the old lady with characteristic impetuosity, she instantly fainted away; but being promptly revived, ordered the brocaded silk gown to be packed up forthwith, and proceeded to relate some circumstances of a similar nature attending the marriage of the eldest daughter of Lady Tollimglower, deceased, which occupied three hours in the recital, and were not half finished at last.", "그가 특유의 성급함으로 노부인에게 그 소식을 전하자, 그녀는 당장 까무러쳤다. 허나 곧바로 정신을 차리고는 비단 능직 가운을 즉시 꾸리라 분부했고, 이어서 고인이 된 톨림글라워 부인의 맏딸 혼사에 따랐던 비슷한 종류의 사정들을 늘어놓기 시작했는데, 이를 이야기하는 데 세 시간이 걸렸으면서도 끝내 절반도 마치지 못했다."]], [["Mrs. Trundle had to be informed of all the mighty preparations that were making in London; and, being in a delicate state of health, was informed thereof through Mr.", "런던에서 이루어지고 있는 그 대단한 준비들을 트런들 부인에게도 알려야 했는데, 그녀가 몸 상태가 미묘한 처지였던 터라 그 소식이 그녀에게 너무 벅찰까 염려하여 트런들 씨를 통해 전했다."], ["Trundle, lest the news should be too much for her; but it was not too much for her, inasmuch as she at once wrote off to Muggleton, to order a new cap and a black satin gown, and moreover avowed her determination of being present at the ceremony.", "허나 그녀에게 그 소식은 결코 벅차지 않았으니, 그녀는 즉시 머글턴으로 편지를 띄워 새 모자와 검은 공단 가운을 주문했을 뿐만 아니라, 그 의식에 참석하겠다는 결심까지 밝혔던 것이다."], ["Hereupon, Mr. Trundle called in the doctor, and the doctor said Mrs.", "이에 트런들 씨가 의사를 불러왔고, 의사는 트런들 부인이 자기 몸 상태가 어떤지 가장 잘 알 터라고 말했다."], ["Trundle ought to know best how she felt herself, to which Mrs.", "그러자 트런들 부인은 자신이 충분히 감당할 만하며 가기로 마음을 굳혔다고 답했다."], ["Trundle replied that she felt herself quite equal to it, and that she had made up her mind to go; upon which the doctor, who was a wise and discreet doctor, and knew what was good for himself, as well as for other people, said that perhaps if Mrs. Trundle stopped at home, she might hurt herself more by fretting, than by going, so perhaps she had better go.", "이에 현명하고 신중한 의사인지라, 다른 사람들에게 무엇이 좋은지뿐 아니라 자기 자신에게 무엇이 좋은지도 잘 아는 그 의사는, 어쩌면 트런들 부인이 집에 머무르면 가는 것보다 애를 태워 몸을 더 상하게 할지도 모르니, 차라리 가는 편이 나을 것 같다고 말했다."], ["And she did go; the doctor with great attention sending in half a dozen of medicine, to be drunk upon the road.", "그리하여 그녀는 갔다. 의사는 세심한 배려로 길에서 마시도록 약 여섯 병을 보내 주었다."]], [["In addition to these points of distraction, Wardle was intrusted with two small letters to two small young ladies who were to act as bridesmaids; upon the receipt of which, the two young ladies were driven to despair by having no ‘things’ ready for so important an occasion, and no time to make them in--a circumstance which appeared to afford the two worthy papas of the two small young ladies rather a feeling of satisfaction than otherwise.", "이러한 분주한 일들에 더하여, 워들은 신부 들러리를 맡기로 한 두 명의 어린 아가씨에게 보낼 짤막한 편지 두 통을 맡았다. 그 편지를 받자 두 어린 아가씨는, 그토록 중대한 행사에 갖춰 입을 \"옷가지\"가 하나도 준비되어 있지 않고 만들 시간도 없다는 사실에 절망에 빠졌다."], ["However, old frocks were trimmed, and new bonnets made, and the young ladies looked as well as could possibly have been expected of them.", "한데 이 사정은 두 어린 아가씨의 두 훌륭한 아버지에게는 오히려 불만보다는 흡족함을 안겨 주는 듯했다. 어쨌든 낡은 드레스에 장식을 달고 새 보닛을 만들었으며, 두 아가씨는 누가 보아도 기대할 수 있는 만큼 곱게 차려입었다."], ["And as they cried at the subsequent ceremony in the proper places, and trembled at the right times, they acquitted themselves to the admiration of all beholders.", "그리고 뒤이은 의식에서 적절한 대목마다 울고, 알맞은 순간마다 떨었기에, 그들은 보는 이 모두의 감탄을 자아낼 만큼 제 몫을 훌륭히 해냈다."]], [["How the two poor relations ever reached London--whether they walked, or got behind coaches, or procured lifts in wagons, or carried each other by turns--is uncertain; but there they were, before Wardle; and the very first people that knocked at the door of Mr.", "그 두 가난한 친척이 대체 어떻게 런던에 다다랐는지 — 걸어왔는지, 마차 뒤꽁무니에 매달려 왔는지, 짐마차에 얻어 탔는지, 아니면 번갈아 서로 업고 왔는지 — 는 알 수 없다."], ["Pickwick’s house, on the bridal morning, were the two poor relations, all smiles and shirt collar.", "허나 어쨌든 그들은 워들보다 먼저 와 있었으며, 혼례날 아침 픽윅 씨 집의 문을 맨 처음 두드린 이가 바로 그 두 가난한 친척이었으니, 온통 웃음과 빳빳한 셔츠 깃 차림이었다."]], [["They were welcomed heartily though, for riches or poverty had no influence on Mr.", "그래도 그들은 진심으로 환대받았으니, 부유함이나 가난함 따위는 픽윅 씨에게 아무 영향도 끼치지 못했기 때문이다."], ["Pickwick; the new servants were all alacrity and readiness; Sam was in a most unrivalled state of high spirits and excitement; Mary was glowing with beauty and smart ribands.", "새 하인들은 모두 민첩하고 빈틈없었고, 샘은 더할 나위 없이 들뜨고 신이 난 상태였으며, 메리는 아름다움과 산뜻한 리본으로 환히 빛나고 있었다."]], [["The bridegroom, who had been staying at the house for two or three days previous, sallied forth gallantly to Dulwich Church to meet the bride, attended by Mr. Pickwick, Ben Allen, Bob Sawyer, and Mr.", "신랑은 이삼 일 전부터 그 집에 머물고 있었는데, 픽윅 씨와 벤 앨런, 보브 소여, 터프먼 씨를 거느리고 신부를 맞으러 덜위치 교회로 늠름하게 나섰다."], ["Tupman; with Sam Weller outside, having at his button-hole a white favour, the gift of his lady-love, and clad in a new and gorgeous suit of livery invented for the occasion.", "샘 웰러는 마차 밖에 자리 잡았는데, 단춧구멍에는 연인이 선물한 흰 장식을 꽂고, 이 행사를 위해 새로 고안한 화려한 제복을 차려입은 모습이었다."], ["They were met by the Wardles, and the Winkles, and the bride and bridesmaids, and the Trundles; and the ceremony having been performed, the coaches rattled back to Mr.", "그들은 워들 일가와 윈클 일가, 신부와 들러리들, 그리고 트런들 일가와 만났다."], ["Pickwick’s to breakfast, where little Mr. Perker already awaited them.", "의식이 끝나자 마차들은 덜컹거리며 픽윅 씨 집으로 되돌아가 조찬을 들었는데, 그곳에는 자그마한 퍼커 씨가 이미 그들을 기다리고 있었다."]], [["Here, all the light clouds of the more solemn part of the proceedings passed away; every face shone forth joyously; and nothing was to be heard but congratulations and commendations.", "여기서, 그 엄숙한 절차의 부분에 드리웠던 옅은 구름이 모두 걷혔다. 모든 얼굴이 즐겁게 빛났고, 들리는 것이라고는 축하와 칭찬의 말뿐이었다."], ["Everything was so beautiful!", "모든 것이 어찌나 아름답던지!"], ["The lawn in front, the garden behind, the miniature conservatory, the dining-room, the drawing-room, the bedrooms, the smoking-room, and, above all, the study, with its pictures and easy- chairs, and odd cabinets, and queer tables, and books out of number, with a large cheerful window opening upon a pleasant lawn and commanding a pretty landscape, dotted here and there with little houses almost hidden by the trees; and then the curtains, and the carpets, and the chairs, and the sofas!", "앞쪽의 잔디밭, 뒤쪽의 정원, 자그마한 온실, 식당, 응접실, 침실들, 흡연실, 그리고 무엇보다도 서재 — 그림들과 안락의자들, 진기한 장식장들, 별난 탁자들, 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 책들이 있고, 쾌적한 잔디밭으로 열려 나무에 거의 가려진 작은 집들이 여기저기 점점이 박힌 아름다운 풍경을 굽어보는 크고 밝은 창문이 있는 서재. 그리고 또 커튼들, 양탄자들, 의자들, 소파들이라니!"], ["Everything was so beautiful, so compact, so neat, and in such exquisite taste, said everybody, that there really was no deciding what to admire most.", "모든 것이 어찌나 아름답고, 어찌나 알차고, 어찌나 정갈하며, 또 어찌나 빼어난 안목으로 꾸며졌던지, 무엇을 가장 감탄해야 할지 정말이지 도무지 정할 수 없다고 모두가 입을 모았다."]], [["And in the midst of all this, stood Mr.", "그리고 이 모든 것의 한가운데에 픽윅 씨가 서 있었으니, 그의 얼굴은 미소로 환히 빛나 어떤 남자도, 여자도, 아이도 그 미소를 거역할 수 없을 정도였다. 그 자신이 일행 가운데 가장 행복한 사람이었다."], ["Pickwick, his countenance lighted up with smiles, which the heart of no man, woman, or child, could resist: himself the happiest of the group: shaking hands, over and over again, with the same people, and when his own hands were not so employed, rubbing them with pleasure: turning round in a different direction at every fresh expression of gratification or curiosity, and inspiring everybody with his looks of gladness and delight.", "같은 사람들과 몇 번이고 거듭 악수를 나누었고, 그 두 손이 악수에 쓰이지 않을 때면 즐거움에 겨워 두 손을 비벼 댔다. 기쁨이나 호기심이 새로이 표현될 때마다 그쪽으로 몸을 돌렸으며, 환희와 기쁨이 어린 그 표정으로 모두에게 흥을 불어넣었다."]], [["Breakfast is announced.", "조찬이 차려졌음을 알린다. 픽윅 씨는 노부인(톨림글라워 부인을 주제로 자못 열변을 토하던)을 긴 식탁의 상석으로 안내한다."], ["Mr.", "워들은 말석에 앉는다."], ["Pickwick leads the old lady (who has been very eloquent on the subject of Lady Tollimglower) to the top of a long table; Wardle takes the bottom; the friends arrange themselves on either side; Sam takes his station behind his master’s chair; the laughter and talking cease; Mr.", "친구들은 양쪽으로 자리를 잡는다. 샘은 주인의 의자 뒤에 자리를 잡는다."], ["Pickwick, having said grace, pauses for an instant and looks round him.", "웃음소리와 이야기 소리가 멎는다. 픽윅 씨는 감사 기도를 드리고 나서 잠시 멈추어 주위를 둘러본다."], ["As he does so, the tears roll down his cheeks, in the fullness of his joy.", "그러는 동안, 벅차오르는 기쁨에 그의 두 뺨 위로 눈물이 흘러내린다."]], [["Let us leave our old friend in one of those moments of unmixed happiness, of which, if we seek them, there are ever some, to cheer our transitory existence here.", "이렇듯 한 점 티 없는 행복의 순간에 우리의 옛 친구를 남겨 두기로 하자. 그런 순간이란, 우리가 찾으려 들기만 하면 이 덧없는 삶을 위로해 줄 것이 언제나 얼마쯤은 있는 법이다."], ["There are dark shadows on the earth, but its lights are stronger in the contrast.", "이 땅 위에는 어두운 그림자들이 드리워 있지만, 그 대비 덕분에 빛은 한층 더 강렬해진다."], ["Some men, like bats or owls, have better eyes for the darkness than for the light.", "어떤 이들은 박쥐나 올빼미처럼 빛보다 어둠을 더 잘 보는 눈을 지녔다."], ["We, who have no such optical powers, are better pleased to take our last parting look at the visionary companions of many solitary hours, when the brief sunshine of the world is blazing full upon them.", "그런 시력을 지니지 못한 우리는, 세상의 짧은 햇살이 그들 위에 환히 타오를 때, 여러 외로운 시간을 함께해 온 환상 속 벗들에게 마지막 작별의 눈길을 보내는 편이 더 기껍다."]], [["It is the fate of most men who mingle with the world, and attain even the prime of life, to make many real friends, and lose them in the course of nature.", "세상과 어울려 인생의 한창때에 이르기까지 한 사람은, 많은 진실한 벗을 사귀었다가 자연의 흐름 속에서 그들을 잃는 것이 대부분의 운명이다."], ["It is the fate of all authors or chroniclers to create imaginary friends, and lose them in the course of art.", "모든 작가나 연대기 기록자는 상상 속의 벗들을 지어냈다가 예술의 흐름 속에서 그들을 잃는 것이 그 운명이다."], ["Nor is this the full extent of their misfortunes; for they are required to furnish an account of them besides.", "그러나 이것이 그들 불운의 전부는 아니니, 그들은 그 벗들에 관한 결말까지 마련해야 하는 요구를 받기 때문이다."]], [["In compliance with this custom--unquestionably a bad one--we subjoin a few biographical words, in relation to the party at Mr. Pickwick’s assembled.", "이 관습에 따라 — 의심할 바 없이 좋지 못한 관습이지만 — 우리는 픽윅 씨 집에 모였던 일행에 관하여 몇 마디 전기적인 글을 덧붙이고자 한다."]], [["Mr. and Mrs. Winkle, being fully received into favour by the old gentleman, were shortly afterwards installed in a newly-built house, not half a mile from Mr. Pickwick’s. Mr. Winkle, being engaged in the city as agent or town correspondent of his father, exchanged his old costume for the ordinary dress of Englishmen, and presented all the external appearance of a civilised Christian ever afterwards.", "윈클 씨 부부는 노신사에게서 완전히 호감을 되찾아, 머지않아 픽윅 씨 집에서 반 마일도 채 떨어지지 않은 새로 지은 집에 자리를 잡았다. 윈클 씨는 도시에서 아버지의 대리인 겸 시내 통신원으로 일하게 되었고, 옛 복장을 영국인들의 평범한 옷차림으로 바꾸었으며, 그 후로 줄곧 교양 있는 기독교인의 외양을 빠짐없이 갖추었다."]], [["Mr. and Mrs.", "스노드그래스 씨 부부는 딩글리 델에 정착하여 작은 농장을 사들여 일구었는데, 이는 수익보다는 소일거리를 위한 것이었다."], ["Snodgrass settled at Dingley Dell, where they purchased and cultivated a small farm, more for occupation than profit. Mr.", "스노드그래스 씨는 이따금 넋을 놓고 우울에 잠기곤 하는 까닭에, 오늘날까지도 친구와 지인들 사이에서 대단한 시인으로 평판이 자자하다."], ["Snodgrass, being occasionally abstracted and melancholy, is to this day reputed a great poet among his friends and acquaintance, although we do not find that he has ever written anything to encourage the belief.", "비록 그가 그러한 믿음을 뒷받침할 만한 글을 단 한 줄이라도 쓴 적이 있는지는 우리로서는 알 수 없지만 말이다."], ["There are many celebrated characters, literary, philosophical, and otherwise, who hold a high reputation on a similar tenure.", "문학이며 철학이며 그 밖의 분야에서 이와 비슷한 근거로 높은 명성을 누리는 저명한 인물들은 수두룩하다."]], [["Mr. Tupman, when his friends married, and Mr.", "터프먼 씨는 친구들이 혼인하고 픽윅 씨가 정착하자, 리치먼드에 거처를 마련하여 그 후로 줄곧 그곳에서 살았다."], ["Pickwick settled, took lodgings at Richmond, where he has ever since resided. He walks constantly on the terrace during the summer months, with a youthful and jaunty air, which has rendered him the admiration of the numerous elderly ladies of single condition, who reside in the vicinity.", "그는 여름철이면 늘 산책로를 거니는데, 젊은이다운 활기차고 경쾌한 분위기를 풍기는 탓에, 인근에 사는 수많은 독신의 나이 든 부인들로부터 흠모를 받는다."], ["He has never proposed again.", "그는 다시는 청혼하지 않았다."]], [["Mr. Bob Sawyer, having previously passed through the Gazette, passed over to Bengal, accompanied by Mr.", "보브 소여 씨는 앞서 파산 공보에 이름을 올린 뒤 벵골로 건너갔는데, 벤저민 앨런 씨가 동행했다."], ["Benjamin Allen; both gentlemen having received surgical appointments from the East India Company.", "두 신사 모두 동인도 회사로부터 외과의 직책을 받았던 것이다."], ["They each had the yellow fever fourteen times, and then resolved to try a little abstinence; since which period, they have been doing well. Mrs.", "그들은 각각 황열병을 열네 번씩 앓고 나서, 약간의 절제를 시도해 보기로 마음먹었으며, 그 후로는 잘 지내고 있다."], ["Bardell let lodgings to many conversable single gentlemen, with great profit, but never brought any more actions for breach of promise of marriage.", "바델 부인은 말동무가 될 만한 여러 독신 신사들에게 하숙을 놓아 큰 이득을 보았으나, 다시는 혼인 약속 위반으로 소송을 걸지 않았다."], ["Her attorneys, Messrs. Dodson & Fogg, continue in business, from which they realise a large income, and in which they are universally considered among the sharpest of the sharp.", "그녀의 변호인인 도드슨 앤드 포그 양반들은 여전히 영업을 이어 가고 있으며, 그로써 막대한 수입을 거두어들이고 있고, 또한 약삭빠른 자들 가운데서도 가장 약삭빠른 축으로 누구에게나 여겨지고 있다."]], [["Sam Weller kept his word, and remained unmarried, for two years.", "샘 웰러는 약속을 지켜 2년 동안 미혼으로 지냈다."], ["The old housekeeper dying at the end of that time, Mr. Pickwick promoted Mary to the situation, on condition of her marrying Mr.", "그 기간이 끝날 무렵 나이 든 가정부가 세상을 떠나자, 픽윅 씨는 메리를 그 자리에 앉히되 즉시 웰러 씨와 혼인할 것을 조건으로 삼았고, 그녀는 군소리 없이 그리했다."], ["Weller at once, which she did without a murmur. From the circumstance of two sturdy little boys having been repeatedly seen at the gate of the back garden, there is reason to suppose that Sam has some family.", "뒤뜰 정원의 문간에서 튼실한 사내아이 둘이 거듭 눈에 띄었다는 사정으로 미루어 보아, 샘에게 식구가 좀 생긴 것이라고 짐작할 만한 근거가 있다."]], [["The elder Mr. Weller drove a coach for twelve months, but being afflicted with the gout, was compelled to retire.", "웰러 영감은 열두 달 동안 마차를 몰았으나, 통풍에 시달린 탓에 일을 그만둘 수밖에 없었다."], ["The contents of the pocket-book had been so well invested for him, however, by Mr.", "허나 픽윅 씨가 그를 위해 그 지갑의 내용물을 어찌나 잘 투자해 두었던지, 그는 두둑한 노후 자금으로 은퇴할 수 있었다."], ["Pickwick, that he had a handsome independence to retire on, upon which he still lives at an excellent public-house near Shooter’s Hill, where he is quite reverenced as an oracle, boasting very much of his intimacy with Mr.", "그는 그 돈으로 슈터스 힐 근처의 훌륭한 선술집에서 여전히 살고 있는데, 그곳에서는 마치 신탁을 내리는 현자처럼 받들어지고 있다."], ["Pickwick, and retaining a most unconquerable aversion to widows.", "그는 픽윅 씨와의 친분을 무던히도 자랑하며, 과부들에 대해서는 도무지 누를 길 없는 혐오감을 지금껏 간직하고 있다."]], [["Mr. Pickwick himself continued to reside in his new house, employing his leisure hours in arranging the memoranda which he afterwards presented to the secretary of the once famous club, or in hearing Sam Weller read aloud, with such remarks as suggested themselves to his mind, which never failed to afford Mr.", "픽윅 씨 자신은 새집에서 계속 살면서, 한가한 시간이면 훗날 그 한때 이름났던 클럽의 간사에게 넘긴 비망록들을 정리하거나, 샘 웰러가 소리 내어 책 읽는 것을 들으며 떠오르는 대로 이런저런 논평을 곁들이곤 했는데, 그것은 어김없이 픽윅 씨에게 크나큰 즐거움을 안겨 주었다."], ["Pickwick great amusement. He was much troubled at first, by the numerous applications made to him by Mr.", "그는 처음에는 스노드그래스 씨와 윈클 씨, 트런들 씨가 저마다 제 아이의 대부가 되어 달라며 거듭 청해 오는 통에 무척 시달렸으나, 이제는 거기에 익숙해져 으레 그러려니 하며 그 역할을 맡고 있다."], ["Snodgrass, Mr. Winkle, and Mr.", "그는 징글 씨에게 베푼 너그러움을 후회할 일은 한 번도 없었으니, 징글이라는 사람과 잡 트로터 둘 다 때가 지나면서 사회의 떳떳한 일원이 되었기 때문이다."], ["Trundle, to act as godfather to their offspring; but he has become used to it now, and officiates as a matter of course. He never had occasion to regret his bounty to Mr.", "다만 그들은 옛 소굴과 유혹의 무대로 되돌아가는 일만큼은 한결같이 단호히 거부해 왔다."], ["Jingle; for both that person and Job Trotter became, in time, worthy members of society, although they have always steadily objected to return to the scenes of their old haunts and temptations. Mr.", "픽윅 씨는 이제 몸이 다소 쇠약해졌지만, 예전의 그 젊은이다운 기상은 여전히 고스란히 간직하고 있어서, 날씨 좋은 날이면 덜위치 미술관의 그림들을 감상하거나 쾌적한 인근을 거니는 모습이 지금도 자주 눈에 띈다."], ["Pickwick is somewhat infirm now; but he retains all his former juvenility of spirit, and may still be frequently seen, contemplating the pictures in the Dulwich Gallery, or enjoying a walk about the pleasant neighbourhood on a fine day. He is known by all the poor people about, who never fail to take their hats off, as he passes, with great respect.", "그는 인근의 가난한 사람들 모두에게 알려져 있으며, 그들은 그가 지나갈 때면 어김없이 깊은 존경을 담아 모자를 벗어 보인다."], ["The children idolise him, and so indeed does the whole neighbourhood.", "아이들은 그를 우상처럼 떠받들며, 실로 온 동네가 그러하다."], ["Every year he repairs to a large family merry-making at Mr. Wardle’s; on this, as on all other occasions, he is invariably attended by the faithful Sam, between whom and his master there exists a steady and reciprocal attachment which nothing but death will terminate.", "해마다 그는 워들 씨 댁에서 열리는 대가족의 흥겨운 잔치에 찾아가는데, 이때에도, 그리고 다른 모든 경우에도, 그는 변함없이 충직한 샘을 거느리니, 주인과 그 사이에는 오직 죽음만이 끝맺을 수 있는 한결같고 서로를 향한 애정이 깃들어 있다."]]];
